/* The heavy half of the ww2 cards — GENERATED, never hand-edited.
 *
 * abstract / sources / why / quote / wiki / image / places, for the cards whose ids begin `ww2-`. None of it is
 * read until a reader REVEALS a card in this collection, so it is fetched then (bundle
 * `cardExtra:ww2`) rather than downloaded by every visitor before they can flip one.
 *
 * IT STAGES ONTO A QUEUE rather than assigning, for the reason glossary-extra.js does: app.js
 * snapshots PRISTINE_CARDS at boot, which is BEFORE this file lands, so a plain assignment would
 * leave the editor's revert baseline empty and Revert would DELETE a shipped abstract rather than
 * restore it. cardExtraIngest drains the queue and re-seeds that baseline.
 *
 * An id data.js does not carry is not resurrected — the light half decides what a card is. */
(function () {
  var CARD_EXTRA = {
    "ww2-001": {"questions":["Germany's pursuit of living space in the east and Japan's of an empire in East Asia gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two separate beginnings, in different years and different continents.","Around 60 million people died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them civilians, and the estimates still vary by millions between one authority and another."],"abstract":"The <b>Second World War</b> was the largest armed conflict in history, fought between the Axis powers led by Germany, Italy and Japan and a coalition that came to include Britain, France, the Soviet Union, China and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had no single beginning: Japan invaded China proper on 7 July 1937, opening the war in Asia, while Germany's attack on Poland on 1 September 1939 opened it in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France, having guaranteed Poland's borders that spring, declared war on 3 September, and the Soviet Union and the United States were both brought in during 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany fought for what its leadership called living space in eastern Europe, and Japan for rule and influence throughout East Asia and the Pacific; neither aim could be had without conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting closed in Europe with the unconditional German surrender of 7 and 9 May 1945, and in Asia with Japan's formal surrender on 2 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its origins are conventionally traced back past the 1930s to the peace settlement of 1919 and 1920, which dismantled four empires and redrew the map of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That settlement also barred Austria from union with Germany, a prohibition the Austrian-born Adolf Hitler and his National Socialist party made an active aim to overturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler set out the eastern war he intended to the German army's most senior officers on 3 February 1933, 4 days after his appointment as <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Chancellor_of_Germany\">chancellor</span>, with a war against Poland as its precondition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan's road ran through the seizure of Manchuria in September 1931 and the clash at the Marco Polo Bridge in July 1937, after which the United States shifted by stages from aid for China to confrontation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cost is the one figure no account omits: something near 60 million dead, most of them civilians, and national totals are still published as ranges differing by millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War II: In Depth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-depth. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War II Dates and Timeline,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 15 November 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War I: Treaties and Reparations,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-i-treaties-and-reparations. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Zweite Weltkrieg,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1937–1945</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, \"Research Starters: Worldwide Deaths in World War II,\" https://www.nationalww2museum.org/students-teachers/student-resources/research-starters/research-starters-worldwide-deaths-world-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender%2C_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg/1920px-Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender%2C_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg","title":"The Japanese surrender, 2 September 1945","desc":"Japanese Foreign Minister Mamoru Shigemitsu signs the Instrument of Surrender aboard the USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay, the last of the war's surrenders and the formal end of the fighting.","credit":"Photograph by Lieutenant Stephen E. Korpanty, US Army Signal Corps; restored by Adam Cuerden. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender,_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg","alt":"A man in a dark morning coat and striped trousers leans over a cloth-covered table on a warship's deck to sign a document, watched by an American general and ranks of Allied officers."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the war have no single starting date?","a":"Because Japan invaded China proper on 7 July 1937, opening the war in Asia, while Germany’s attack on Poland on 1 September 1939 opened it in Europe, and the Soviet Union and United States entered only in 1941."},{"q":"Why could neither Axis aim be pursued without conquest?","a":"Because Germany fought for what its leadership called living space in eastern Europe and Japan for rule throughout East Asia and the Pacific, and both meant taking territory other states held."},{"q":"Why are the casualty figures still published as ranges?","a":"Because the total is near 60 million, most of them civilians, and national totals differ by millions between the counts that are made."}],"wiki":"World War II","places":["adm0:arm","adm0:aus","adm0:aze","adm0:bel","adm0:bih","adm0:blr","adm0:bra","adm0:can","adm0:chn","adm0:deu","adm0:est","adm0:fra","adm0:gbr","adm0:geo","adm0:grc","adm0:hrv","adm0:hun","adm0:ind","adm0:ita","adm0:jpn","adm0:kaz","adm0:kgz","adm0:ltu","adm0:lux","adm0:lva","adm0:mda","adm0:mex","adm0:mkd","adm0:mne","adm0:mng","adm0:nld","adm0:nor","adm0:nzl","adm0:phl","adm0:pol","adm0:rus","adm0:srb","adm0:svn","adm0:tjk","adm0:tkm","adm0:ukr","adm0:usa","adm0:uzb","adm0:zaf","pol:british_raj","pol:ccp","pol:front_allied","pol:front_allied_occupied","pol:front_axis","pol:front_axis_occupied","pol:hungary_1918","pol:italy","pol:japan","pol:mongolia_pr","pol:roc","pol:south_africa","pol:ussr","pol:yugoslavia"]},
    "ww2-002": {"questions":["Germany gave up 5,000 guns, its submarines and the left bank of the Rhine under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran for 30 days at a time and left the blockade in place.","A civilian secretary of state signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a republic 2 days old, on behalf of the same high command that had demanded it in September."],"abstract":"The <b>Armistice of 11 November 1918</b> ended the fighting of the First World War: the guns of the Western Front fell silent at eleven that morning, after more than 4 years of continuous war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German high command demanded it, not the politicians who signed it: on 29 September 1918 the Supreme Army Command told the government its position was militarily hopeless and required negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A parliamentary government was formed under Prince Max von Baden, and the October reforms of 28 October turned a constitutional monarchy into a parliamentary one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Revolution then overtook the negotiations: the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Kingdom_of_Bavaria\">Bavarian monarchy</span> fell on 7 November, and on 9 November Max von Baden announced the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Wilhelm_II\">Kaiser</span>'s abdication without authorisation, handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert, and Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Reichstag_(German_Empire)\">Reichstag</span> that afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Matthias Erzberger, a civilian secretary of state, signed at Compiègne at five o'clock French time for both the Supreme Command and the new government, opposite Foch and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Rosslyn_Wemyss\">Wemyss</span> for the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> An armistice suspends fighting rather than concluding a war: this one ran for 30 days with an option to extend, and either side could denounce it on 48 hours' notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its terms disarmed Germany where it stood: 5,000 guns, 25,000 machine guns and 1,700 aircraft surrendered, the submarines given up and the principal warships interned, the left bank of the Rhine evacuated and Allied bridgeheads planted at Mainz, Koblenz and Cologne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Germany renounced the treaties of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Brest-Litovsk\">Brest-Litovsk</span> and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Bucharest_(1918)\">Bucharest</span>, and the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Blockade_of_Germany_(1914–1919)\">Allied naval blockade</span> stayed in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What no armistice could supply was the experience of defeat: the fighting had never reached German soil and German armies still held large tracts of eastern Europe, which left room for conspiracy theories blaming the collapse on the home front rather than on the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those theories became a weapon against the men who had signed, and a right-wing terrorist group murdered Erzberger in 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Australian War Memorial, \"Remembrance Day,\" https://www.awm.gov.au/commemoration/remembrance. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Erste Weltkrieg,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/erster-weltkrieg. [Open access]","Boris Barth, \"Stab-in-the-back Myth,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/stab-in-the-back-myth/. [Open access]","Alexander Gallus, \"Revolutions (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-germany/. [Open access]","Christopher Dowe, \"Erzberger, Matthias,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/erzberger-matthias/. [Open access]","\"The Special Representative in France House to the Secretary of State, Paris, November 11, 1918,\" document 420 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918, Supplement 1, The World War, Volume I</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1933), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1918Supp01v01/d420. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Foch_train.jpg/1920px-Foch_train.jpg","title":"The carriage at Compiègne","desc":"Wagons-Lits dining car No. 2419 D, in which the negotiations were held and the armistice signed at five in the morning on 11 November 1918.","credit":"US Army Signal Corps, from the US Army Military History Institute collection. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foch_train.jpg","alt":"A dark railway dining car lettered Dining-Car and numbered 2419 D stands at a platform beside a brick building, with a second carriage coupled behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the army rather than the politicians demand it?","a":"Because on 29 September 1918 the Supreme Army Command told the government its position was militarily hopeless and required negotiations, and a parliamentary government was formed to conduct them."},{"q":"Why is an armistice not the same as a peace?","a":"Because it suspends fighting rather than concluding a war: this one ran 30 days with an option to extend, and either side could denounce it on 48 hours’ notice."},{"q":"Why did the terms leave room for a legend of betrayal?","a":"Because the fighting had never reached German soil and German armies still held large tracts of eastern Europe, so no armistice could supply the experience of defeat."}],"wiki":"Armistice of 11 November 1918","places":["pl:q180871"]},
    "ww2-003": {"questions":["Neither Germany nor Bolshevik Russia was invited to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the German delegation reached Paris only at the end of April, to be handed a finished draft.","Poincaré opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at three in the afternoon on 18 January 1919, the anniversary, by accident, of the German Empire's proclamation at Versailles."],"abstract":"The <b>Paris Peace Conference</b> was the gathering at which the victors of the First World War wrote the peace, running from 18 January 1919 to 21 January 1920, a year and 3 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raymond Poincaré opened it at three in the afternoon in the Peace Rooms of the French foreign ministry, before delegates of the allied and associated belligerents and of states that had broken off relations with the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The date fell on the anniversary of the proclamation of the German Empire in the Hall of Mirrors in 1871, which was not deliberate — the Italian delegates had arrived late — though it gave the opening speech its theme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thirty-two countries were represented, but the plenary met only nine times and the smaller powers had a formal part in it and no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Business began in a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Council_of_Ten_(Paris_Peace_Conference)\">Council of Ten</span> — the heads of government and foreign ministers of the United States, Britain, France and Italy, with two Japanese representatives — which met seventy-two times and set up fifty-eight subcommittees without reaching conclusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From March 1919 it split: a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Council_of_Four_(Paris_Peace_Conference)\">Council of Four</span> of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Woodrow_Wilson\">Wilson</span>, Lloyd George, Clemenceau and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Vittorio_Emanuele_Orlando\">Orlando</span> took the decisions, while their foreign ministers formed a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Council_of_Five_(Paris_Peace_Conference)\">Council of Five</span> and did the detailed work on Europe's frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The conference had opened with no agreed agenda, so its expert commissions drafted maximum demands on the assumption that the Germans would be negotiated with afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not: the victors, convinced of German guilt and afraid that argument would divide them, refused to meet the German delegation, which was invited only at the end of April and handed a finished draft on 7 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bolshevik Russia was not invited at all, its government unrecognised, and the defeated Central Powers were excluded with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Five treaties came out of the conference — <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Versailles\">Versailles</span>, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Saint-Germain-en-Laye_(1919)\">Saint-Germain</span>, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Neuilly-sur-Seine\">Neuilly</span>, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Trianon\">Trianon</span> and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Sèvres\">Sèvres</span> — and a sixth, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Lausanne\">Lausanne</span> in 1923, replaced Sèvres after the only real negotiation with a former enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","\"Protocol No. 1, Session of January 18, 1919,\" document 3 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d3. [Open access]","\"Organization of the Conference, October 1, 1919,\" document 2 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d2. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Big_four.jpg/1920px-Big_four.jpg","title":"The Council of Four, 27 May 1919","desc":"Lloyd George and Orlando talking at the left, Clemenceau at the centre and Wilson at the right — the four heads of government who took the conference's decisions from March 1919.","credit":"Photograph by Edward N. Jackson, US Army Signal Corps. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_four.jpg","alt":"Four elderly men in dark suits stand on the stone steps of a building; two on the left are talking, one gesturing, while the other two stand apart looking towards them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the smaller powers count for so little?","a":"Because the plenary met only nine times, and business was done first in a Council of Ten and then in a Council of Four that took the decisions."},{"q":"Why were the German delegates never negotiated with?","a":"Because the victors, convinced of German guilt and afraid that argument would divide them, refused to meet them and handed over a finished draft on 7 May."},{"q":"Why did the expert commissions draft maximum demands?","a":"Because the conference opened with no agreed agenda and they worked on the assumption that the Germans would be negotiated with afterwards."}],"wiki":"Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)"},
    "ww2-004": {"questions":["Hermann Müller and Johannes Bell signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Hall of Mirrors on 28 June 1919, a cabinet having resigned rather than put its name to it.","Running to 440 articles in fifteen parts and opening with the Covenant of the League of Nations, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came into force on 10 January 1920."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Versailles</b> was the peace imposed on Germany at the end of the First World War, running to 440 articles in fifteen parts and signed on 28 June 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German delegation received the draft on 7 May and was allowed to answer only in writing, an exchange of notes that changed little; the speech its foreign minister made at the handover so angered the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Allies_of_World_War_I\">Allies</span> that they sharpened the war-guilt wording instead of softening it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Faced with an ultimatum, the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Weimar_National_Assembly\">National Assembly</span> approved signature only just before it expired, the cabinet resigned over the question, and Brockdorff-Rantzau would not sign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hermann Müller and Johannes Bell went instead, and in the Galerie des Glaces at three in the afternoon, with Clemenceau presiding, the Germans signed first and the Allied and Associated powers after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was ratified by the National Assembly on 9 July and came into force on 10 January 1920, once France, Britain and Italy had ratified too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Part I was the Covenant of the League of Nations, which the defeated could not at first join, and Part XIII created the International Labour Organization on the argument that peace could rest only on social justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The military clauses were built to leave Germany unable to attack anyone: an army of 100,000 men including 4,000 officers, conscription abolished, submarines and any air force forbidden, the navy cut to six battleships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No fortifications, troops or manoeuvres were allowed on the left bank of the Rhine or within 50 kilometres (30 miles) east of it, and a breach was to count as a hostile act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Germany lost about an eighth of its territory and a tenth of its people, its colonies passing to the victors as League mandates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nobody in Germany accepted it: most called it a dictated peace of shame, no political camp granted it legitimacy, and the national right made the fight against the fetters of Versailles a question of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","\"Protocol No. 2, Plenary Session of June 28, 1919,\" document 12 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d12. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part V, arts. 160, 173, 181, 191 and 198, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partv.asp. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part III, arts. 42–44, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Weimarer Republik,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Treaty_of_Versailles_Signing%2C_Hall_of_Mirrors.jpg/1920px-Treaty_of_Versailles_Signing%2C_Hall_of_Mirrors.jpg","title":"The signing in the Hall of Mirrors, 28 June 1919","desc":"The delegations and spectators packed into the Galerie des Glaces at Versailles for the signature, in the room where the German Empire had been proclaimed in 1871.","credit":"Photograph by Helen Johns Kirtland and Lucian Swift Kirtland, US National Archives. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Versailles_Signing,_Hall_of_Mirrors.jpg","alt":"A long mirrored and vaulted gallery crowded from end to end with hundreds of men in dark suits, standing and seated around tables in the middle distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war-guilt wording end up harder rather than softer?","a":"Because the speech the German foreign minister made at the handover so angered the Allies that they sharpened it instead."},{"q":"Why were the military clauses shaped as they were?","a":"Because they were built to leave Germany unable to attack anyone: 100,000 men, no conscription, no submarines or air force, and no fortifications or troops within 50 kilometres east of the Rhine."},{"q":"Why did no German political camp grant it legitimacy?","a":"Because most called it a dictated peace of shame, the country having lost about an eighth of its territory and a tenth of its people without negotiating any of it."}],"wiki":"Treaty of Versailles"},
    "ww2-005": {"questions":["Reparations needed a legal foundation, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made Germany accept responsibility for the loss and damage its aggression had caused, and the next article conceded that its resources could not cover the whole.","The clause blamed for humiliating Germany is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the treaty's actual moral charge is in Part VII, which arraigns Wilhelm II for an offence against international morality."],"abstract":"<b>Article 231</b> opens the reparations part of the Treaty of Versailles, and in it the Allied and Associated Governments affirm and Germany accepts responsibility for the loss and damage caused by a war imposed through German and allied aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became known in Germany as the war guilt clause, although the word guilt appears nowhere in it, nor anywhere else in Part VIII.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its work was legal rather than moral: it established liability, and the article immediately after it concedes that German resources were not adequate to make complete reparation for all such loss and damage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That wording settled an argument among the victors over whether Germany owed only for the illegal invasion of Belgium and the occupied French departments, or for the whole cost of the war down to the pensions of veterans and their dependants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sharper accusation the Germans actually received came in the covering note of 16 June 1919, which is not part of the treaty and appears nowhere in its text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The treaty's genuine moral charge sits in Part VII, whose article 227 publicly arraigns Wilhelm II for a supreme offence against international morality and the sanctity of treaties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German foreign minister's speech at the handover of the draft so angered the Allies that they hardened the responsibility wording rather than softening it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No political camp in Germany granted the clause any legitimacy, revising the treaty became the fixed aim of Weimar foreign policy, and the struggle against it was the one domestic theme that united every social and political group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most Germans rejected the settlement as a dictated peace of shame, and the national right made the fight against the fetters of Versailles a question of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the Allies the clause was the foundation of a bill; for Germans it was a verdict passed upon them, and it was as a verdict that they refused it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part VIII, arts. 231–232, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partviii.asp. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part VII, art. 227, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partvii.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Weimarer Republik,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/28._Juni_1919%2C_Weimar_Markt%2C_Stra%C3%9Fenbahn_Weimar_h%C3%A4lt_wegen_der_Demo_gegen_den_Versailler_Vertrag.jpg/1280px-28._Juni_1919%2C_Weimar_Markt%2C_Stra%C3%9Fenbahn_Weimar_h%C3%A4lt_wegen_der_Demo_gegen_den_Versailler_Vertrag.jpg","title":"Weimar, 28 June 1919","desc":"The market square filled by a demonstration against the treaty on the day it was signed, with a tram halted in the middle of the crowd.","credit":"Stadtmuseum Weimar. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:28._Juni_1919,_Weimar_Markt,_Straßenbahn_Weimar_hält_wegen_der_Demo_gegen_den_Versailler_Vertrag.jpg","alt":"A town square packed shoulder to shoulder with a crowd in hats, seen from above between tall old buildings, with a tram stopped among them."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name it acquired in Germany misleading?","a":"Because the word guilt appears nowhere in the article or in the rest of Part VIII: its work was legal, establishing liability for loss and damage."},{"q":"Why did the wording have to settle an argument among the victors?","a":"Because they disagreed over whether Germany owed only for the illegal invasion of Belgium and occupied France, or for the whole cost of the war down to veterans’ pensions."},{"q":"Why did Germans read it as a verdict rather than a bill?","a":"Because for the Allies it was the foundation of a claim, and it was as a moral judgement passed upon them that no political camp in Germany granted it legitimacy."}],"wiki":"Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles"},
    "ww2-006": {"questions":["Two billion gold marks a year plus 26% of the value of exports was the schedule for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Germany asked for a postponement within 7 months.","Paid partly in coal and impossible to transfer abroad without an export surplus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also created demand in a depression and brought work to German industry."],"abstract":"<b>Reparations</b> were the compensation Germany was required to make for the damage of the First World War, and the Treaty of Versailles deliberately named no figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Article 233 handed that to an Inter-Allied Reparation Commission, which was to give Germany a hearing, report the amount on or before 1 May 1921, and draw up a schedule discharging the whole obligation within 30 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 28 April 1921 the Commission notified the German government that the damage for which it was responsible came to 132 billion gold marks, about 32 billion dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A week later it issued the Schedule of Payments: a fixed annuity of 2 billion gold marks and a variable one worth 26% of the value of German exports, with 1 billion due inside 25 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of it was taken in kind, coal to France and Belgium above all, and the rest in foreign currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Germany asked for a partial postponement on 14 December 1921, and the Commission's Cannes decision of January 1922 replaced the instalments with payments of 31 million gold marks every 10 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difficulty was the transfer as much as the size: paying abroad required an export surplus, and where the money was raised instead on international capital markets the real economic transfer approached nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Allied tariffs made that surplus harder to earn and gave the German government an incentive to devalue in order to undercut them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against all that, the obligation to deliver coal kept output high in the Ruhr and the Saar, and the payments created demand in a post-war depression that brought orders and work to German industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Payment in practice was poor and only small amounts were transferred during the 1920s, which is part of why the economic legacy of the treaty is still discussed as much through myth as through figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part VIII, arts. 231–233, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partviii.asp. [Open access]","Reparation Commission, <i>Statement of Germany's Obligations Under the Heading of Reparations, etc., at April 30th, 1922</i> (London: His Majesty's Stationery Office, 1922), Introduction, https://archive.org/details/reparationpapers00repa. [Open access]","Marcel Boldorf, \"Post-war Economies (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/post-war-economies-germany/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/%28De_g._%C3%A0_d._les_repr%C3%A9sentants_allemands_de_la_Kriegslasten_Kommission%29_Michaelis%2C_Fischer%2C_von_Oertzen%2C_29-12-21_%28%C3%A0_l%27h%C3%B4tel%29_Astoria_-_btv1b530743143.jpg/1280px-%28De_g._%C3%A0_d._les_repr%C3%A9sentants_allemands_de_la_Kriegslasten_Kommission%29_Michaelis%2C_Fischer%2C_von_Oertzen%2C_29-12-21_%28%C3%A0_l%27h%C3%B4tel%29_Astoria_-_btv1b530743143.jpg","title":"The German war-burdens delegation in Paris, 29 December 1921","desc":"Michaelis, Fischer and von Oertzen of the Kriegslasten-Kommission on the stairs of the Hôtel Astoria, a fortnight after Germany asked for the schedule to be postponed.","credit":"Agence Rol, Bibliothèque nationale de France. Public domain, via Gallica. https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b530743143","alt":"Three men in dark suits and striped trousers stand on the curving marble staircase of a hotel lobby, one holding a briefcase and one a folded paper."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the treaty name no sum?","a":"Because it handed the figure to an Inter-Allied Reparation Commission, which was to hear Germany and report the amount by 1 May 1921 with a schedule over 30 years."},{"q":"Why was transferring the money harder than raising it?","a":"Because paying abroad required an export surplus, and where the money was raised on international capital markets instead the real economic transfer approached nothing."},{"q":"Why did Allied tariffs work against the Allies’ own aim?","a":"Because they made that export surplus harder to earn and gave the German government an incentive to devalue in order to undercut them."}],"wiki":"World War I reparations"},
    "ww2-007": {"questions":["Lloyd George fought at the conference to limit German losses in Upper Silesia and on the fringes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and won a plebiscite for the one and little else.","Danzig lay outside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was made a Free City under League protection instead, inside the Polish customs frontier and with its port at Poland's use."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish Corridor</b> was the strip of former German territory awarded to Poland at Versailles to give it access to the Baltic, and it separated East Prussia from the rest of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came out of a wider settlement: article 87 recognised Poland's complete independence and renounced Germany's title to the land, and Wilson's thirteenth point had already promised an independent Poland with access to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the west, Alsace-Lorraine was restored to France as from the date of the armistice, and Eupen and Malmedy went to Belgium, where inhabitants were given 6 months to register a wish to remain German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Saar coal mines were ceded to France as compensation for the mines wrecked in the north of France, while the territory itself passed to the League of Nations as trustee for 15 years, after which its people would choose their sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Danzig, which the corridor did not include, was made a Free City under the League's protection, inside the Polish customs frontier and with its port at Poland's unrestricted use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Elsewhere the treaty put the question to the inhabitants: the Danish frontier was to be fixed in conformity with the wishes of the population, and Upper Silesia was to vote on whether to join Germany or Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Memel was renounced to the Allied powers and went in the end to Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Article 80 bound Germany to respect Austrian independence as inalienable except with the consent of the League Council, closing off any union with the rump Austrian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Altogether Germany gave up roughly 70,000 square kilometres (27,000 square miles) and more than 6.5 million people, and with them half its iron ore supply and a quarter of its hard coal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lloyd George had pressed at the conference to limit the German losses in Upper Silesia and on the fringes of the corridor, and secured the Silesian plebiscite and little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part III, arts. 34, 45, 49, 51, 80, 87, 88, 99, 102, 104 and 109, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","Piotr Szlanta, \"Poland,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/poland/. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Polish_Corridor.PNG/500px-Polish_Corridor.PNG","title":"The Polish Corridor and Danzig","desc":"The strip of former German territory that gave Poland access to the Baltic, with the Free City of Danzig at its mouth and East Prussia separated from the rest of Germany to its east.","credit":"Map by Space Cadet at English Wikipedia, via Wikimedia Commons. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_Corridor.PNG","alt":"A map of the Baltic coast in which Germany is green and Poland grey: a grey corridor labelled CORRIDOR reaches the sea between two green blocks, with an orange Danzig at its mouth and Germany East Prussia to the east."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a strip of German territory given to Poland at all?","a":"Because Wilson’s thirteenth point had promised an independent Poland with access to the sea, and the treaty renounced Germany’s title to the land."},{"q":"Why were some frontiers put to a vote and others not?","a":"Because the Danish frontier was to follow the wishes of the population and Upper Silesia was to choose between Germany and Poland, while other transfers were simply awarded."},{"q":"Why did the territorial losses matter economically as well?","a":"Because with roughly 70,000 square kilometres and 6.5 million people went half of Germany’s iron ore supply and a quarter of its hard coal."}],"wiki":"Polish Corridor"},
    "ww2-008": {"questions":["Not one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the national entity self-determination had promised, each holding minorities it feared and none content with its frontiers.","Czechoslovakia inherited three million German speakers and quarrelled with Poland over Teschen, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not combine against a revived Germany or Russia."],"abstract":"The <b>successor states</b> of Central and Eastern Europe were the countries that took over territory and international responsibilities from the German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman empires when those collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Czechoslovakia, Austria and Hungary, together with the Soviet Union, filled the political vacuum the collapse left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settlement consolidated the Balkans and fragmented Eastern Europe: the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, renamed Yugoslavia in 1929, grew from Serbia's 4.6 million people to 13.6 million, and Romania's population more than doubled to 16 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Austria became independent reluctantly, its 8 million people mostly in or near a Vienna stripped of the empire that had been its reason for existing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hungary lost two-thirds of its pre-war territory and 58% of its population, a third of the Magyars among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Czechoslovakia was assembled from Bohemia, Moravia and Slovakia, owing much to the exile campaigns of Tomáš Masaryk and Eduard Beneš, and it was given three million German speakers, allocated to it against self-determination and in favour of defensible frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polish independence had rested on the improbable defeat of all three empires that had partitioned the country, and after the 1920 war with Russia it fixed a frontier far east of the line the conference recommended, leaving only 69% of its people Polish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None was the national entity the principle promised: centuries of war, migration and intermarriage had left an ethnic patchwork, so each held minorities it resented and feared, and all were dissatisfied with their frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peacemakers wrote a system of minority protection into the settlement, partly to encourage assimilation and partly to deny neighbouring kin-states a pretext, then handed responsibility to the League of Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Czechoslovakia and Poland fell out at once over the coalfield of Teschen, and that inability to combine was the region's standing weakness should Germany or Russia revive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","<i>Vienna Convention on Succession of States in respect of Treaties</i>, 23 August 1978, art. 2, United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1946, p. 3, https://legal.un.org/ilc/texts/instruments/english/conventions/3_2_1978.pdf. [Open access]","Piotr Szlanta, \"Poland,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/poland/. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part III, arts. 80–86, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War I: Treaties and Reparations,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-i-treaties-and-reparations. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Partition_of_Austria-Hungary_1919_map.jpg/1920px-Partition_of_Austria-Hungary_1919_map.jpg","title":"The partition of Austria-Hungary, 1919","desc":"A map published in The Times History of the War showing the boundaries of the successor states as defined in the peace treaties, with the pre-war frontiers of Austria-Hungary marked as a dotted line.","credit":"The Times History of the War, vol. 21 (1919), via the Internet Archive and Wikimedia Commons. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partition_of_Austria-Hungary_1919_map.jpg","alt":"A black and white map of central Europe in 1919, with Austria, Hungary, Czecho-Slovakia, Yugoslavia, Poland and Rumania outlined in heavy new borders across the dotted pre-war frontier of Austria-Hungary."},"why":[{"q":"Why could none of them be the nation-state the principle promised?","a":"Because centuries of war, migration and intermarriage had left an ethnic patchwork, so each held minorities it resented and feared and all were dissatisfied with their frontiers."},{"q":"Why was minority protection written into the settlement?","a":"Because it was meant partly to encourage assimilation and partly to deny neighbouring kin-states a pretext, with responsibility handed to the League of Nations."},{"q":"Why was the region’s weakness its inability to combine?","a":"Because Czechoslovakia and Poland fell out at once over the coalfield of Teschen, leaving them divided should Germany or Russia revive."}],"wiki":"Successor state","places":["pol:austria_1918","pol:czechoslovakia","pol:estonia_1918","pol:finland","pol:hungary_1918","pol:latvia_1918","pol:lithuania_1918","pol:poland_1918"]},
    "ww2-009": {"questions":["Count Albert Apponyi asked the Supreme Council for plebiscites in the disputed lands and was refused, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was handed to Hungary with its frontiers essentially unchanged.","Interwar governments made the revision of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the object of Hungarian foreign policy, which drew the country towards the Axis and won back half the lost lands between 1938 and 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Trianon</b> was the peace imposed on Hungary at the end of the First World War, signed at the Grand Trianon Palace in the park at Versailles on 4 June 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Austria-Hungary came apart in the autumn of 1918, and while the Hungarian government waited for the conference to settle the frontiers by negotiation, Czechoslovak, Romanian and Serbian troops occupied territory far beyond the armistice lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A communist government took power in March 1919, fought and lost a war with Romania, and Romanian troops entered Budapest that August, so Hungary reached the conference with no army and no leverage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its delegation was not invited to Paris until December 1919 and received terms drafted without it; Count Albert Apponyi was allowed one speech to the Supreme Council, asked for plebiscites, and was refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two delegates were sent to sign, and the treaty ran to fourteen parts, opening with the Covenant of the League of Nations and describing the new borders in cartographic detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hungary was left with 92,963 of the 282,870 square kilometres (35,893 of 109,217 square miles) the kingdom had held outside Croatia, and with 7.6 million of its 18.3 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romania took the largest share, Czechoslovakia and the new Serb-Croat-Slovene Kingdom most of the rest, and Austria received the western strip that became the Burgenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 3.2 million people who had given Hungarian as their mother tongue in the 1910 census now lived outside the state, and more than 400,000 refugees crossed into it between 1918 and 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army was capped at 35,000 men, reparations were charged, and a covering letter from Alexandre Millerand hinted at frontier adjustments that came to nothing beyond a plebiscite at Sopron in 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every interwar government worked for revision, and the treaty is still felt in Hungary as a national wound<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> — which is why the country aligned with the powers that wanted the settlement broken, and recovered about half the lost territory between 1938 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Miklós Zeidler, \"Trianon, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/trianon-treaty-of/. [Open access]","Tamás Révész, \"Post-war Turmoil and Violence (Hungary),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2020), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/post-war-turmoil-and-violence-hungary/. [Open access]","<i>Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Hungary</i>, 4 June 1920, Parts I–IV, World War I Document Archive, Harold B. Lee Library, Brigham Young University, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Treaty_of_Trianon. [Open access]","Peter Ferwagner, \"Centenary (Hungary),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/centenary-hungary/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Holocaust in Hungary,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hungary-before-the-german-occupation. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Le_comte_Apponyi%2C_chef_de_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_hongroise%2C_arrive_au_quai_d%27Orsay_pour_la_remise_du_trait%C3%A9_de_paix.jpg/1920px-Le_comte_Apponyi%2C_chef_de_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_hongroise%2C_arrive_au_quai_d%27Orsay_pour_la_remise_du_trait%C3%A9_de_paix.jpg","title":"Count Apponyi arrives for the peace terms, 15 January 1920","desc":"Count Albert Apponyi, head of the Hungarian delegation, arriving at the Quai d'Orsay in Paris on the day the conditions of peace were handed over. He addressed the Supreme Council the following morning and was refused.","credit":"Agence Rol, Bibliothèque nationale de France. Public domain, via Gallica. https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b53036032b","alt":"A black and white press photograph of an elderly bearded man in a top hat and long overcoat standing at the foot of stone steps beside a motor car, watched by French officers in uniform."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Hungary reach the conference with no leverage?","a":"Because a communist government had taken power and lost a war with Romania, whose troops entered Budapest that August, so the country arrived with no army."},{"q":"Why did waiting for negotiation cost it territory?","a":"Because while its government waited for the conference to settle the frontiers, Czechoslovak, Romanian and Serbian troops occupied territory far beyond the armistice lines."},{"q":"Why did the treaty shape Hungarian policy for 20 years?","a":"Because about 3.2 million Hungarian speakers were left outside the state, so every interwar government worked for revision and the country aligned with the powers that wanted the settlement broken."}],"wiki":"Treaty of Trianon"},
    "ww2-010": {"questions":["Signed on 24 July 1923, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> returned eastern Thrace, Anatolia and Izmir to Turkey and abolished the capitulations that had given foreign powers privileges inside the country.","A convention compelling Orthodox Christians and Muslims to move between the two countries was given the force of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which outlasted every other treaty made after the First World War."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b>, signed on 24 July 1923, was the settlement between Turkey and the Allied powers, and the only one of the post-war treaties that was genuinely negotiated rather than imposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ottoman Empire had been left until last: its terms were drafted in London and San Remo in the spring of 1920 and signed at Sèvres that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sèvres recognised an independent Armenia, restricted the Turkish army, put the Straits under international control, gave France and Italy spheres in Anatolia, and handed Greece most of Thrace and the government of Izmir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sultan signed under duress, but the nationalist movement Mustafa Kemal was building at Ankara rejected it, and the treaty was never ratified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kemal’s forces halted the Greek advance at Sakarya in 1921 and drove it back through 1922, ending in a massacre at Izmir and a stand-off with British troops at Chanak; the Ankara assembly abolished the sultanate that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Negotiations opened at Lausanne on 20 November 1922 and, after a break, closed in July 1923, with George Curzon and later the high commissioner at Constantinople, Rumbold, leading for Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new treaty returned eastern Thrace, Anatolia, Izmir and some Aegean islands to Turkey, and abolished the capitulations, the financial and legal privileges foreign powers had long enjoyed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armenia went unmentioned, its independence having been destroyed in December 1920, and the Arab provinces stayed outside the new state as British and French mandates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate convention signed in January 1923 compelled an exchange of populations between Greece and Turkey, and article 142 gave it the force of the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The republic was declared on 29 October 1923, and Lausanne has proved the longest-lasting of the settlements, the only one both sides had bargained over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","Yücel Yanıkdağ, \"Ottoman Empire/Middle East,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/ottoman-empiremiddle-east/. [Open access]","<i>Treaty of Peace with Turkey Signed at Lausanne</i>, 24 July 1923, arts. 28 and 142, in <i>The Treaties of Peace 1919-1923</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Carnegie Endowment for International Peace, 1924), World War I Document Archive, Brigham Young University, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Treaty_of_Lausanne. [Open access]","<i>The Peace Treaty of Sèvres</i>, 10 August 1920, World War I Document Archive, Harold B. Lee Library, Brigham Young University, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Peace_Treaty_of_S%C3%A8vres. [Open access]","Loukianos Hassiotis, \"Greece,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/greece/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Lausanne_-_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_turque_-_Ismet_Pacha_-_btv1b90539548.jpg/1920px-Lausanne_-_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_turque_-_Ismet_Pacha_-_btv1b90539548.jpg","title":"The Turkish delegation at Lausanne, 1922","desc":"İsmet Pasha and his colleagues outside their hotel during the conference. Turkey came to Lausanne as the winner of a war rather than as a defeated power, and the terms were bargained over for eight months.","credit":"Agence Meurisse, Bibliothèque nationale de France. Public domain, via Gallica. https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b90539548","alt":"A black and white press photograph of four men in dark overcoats and hats standing on a pavement outside a stone hotel entrance, one of them holding a cane."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the first Ottoman settlement never ratified?","a":"Because the sultan signed Sèvres under duress and the nationalist movement Mustafa Kemal was building at Ankara rejected it."},{"q":"Why could Turkey negotiate where the other defeated powers could not?","a":"Because its forces had halted the Greek advance at Sakarya and driven it back, ending in a stand-off with British troops at Chanak."},{"q":"Why has this treaty lasted where the others did not?","a":"Because it is the only one both sides had bargained over rather than received, which is what the negotiation at Lausanne produced."}],"wiki":"Treaty of Lausanne"},
    "ww2-011": {"questions":["Graded A, B or C by how far its people were held to have developed, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> obliged its holder to report every year to a permanent commission in Geneva.","Of all the territories placed under a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only Iraq and Lebanon became independent before 1945, and the rest passed to the United Nations as trust territories."],"abstract":"A <b>League of Nations mandate</b> was a former German or Ottoman territory placed under the administration of a victorious power, which governed it on the League’s behalf rather than owning it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea came from a proposal by Jan Smuts and answered Woodrow Wilson’s wish to end the war without annexations, and it was written into article 22 of the Covenant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That article held the well-being of peoples “not yet able to stand by themselves under the strenuous conditions of the modern world” to be a sacred trust of civilisation, and gave their tutelage to advanced nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mandates were graded A, B and C by how far their inhabitants were judged to have developed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The A mandates, former Ottoman provinces, were provisionally recognised as independent nations needing advice; the B mandates in central Africa were administered outright under conditions barring the slave trade; and the C mandates, South West Africa and the Pacific islands, were governed as parts of the holder’s own territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Britain and France took most of them, with smaller shares to Japan, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand, and none at all to Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each holder had to report every year to a Permanent Mandates Commission, which met in Geneva from 1921 and advised the Council on whether the terms were being kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system was meant to end as its territories grew able to stand alone, but before 1945 only Iraq, in 1932, and Lebanon, in 1943, reached independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It both undermined and underpinned colonial rule: it constrained annexation and created an obligation to report, while dressing continued European government in new legal clothes at the moment self-determination had been promised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations Charter brought the remaining mandates into its trusteeship system, and the one that stayed outside, South West Africa, was ruled by South Africa until the International Court declared that presence illegal in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, 28 June 1919, art. 22, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","Anita Ziegerhofer, \"League of Nations,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/league-of-nations/. [Open access]","Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","<i>Charter of the United Nations</i>, 26 June 1945, arts. 75–77, United Nations, https://www.un.org/en/about-us/un-charter/full-text. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, \"Legal Consequences for States of the Continued Presence of South Africa in Namibia (South West Africa) notwithstanding Security Council Resolution 276 (1970),\" advisory opinion of 21 June 1971, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/53. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the system invented at all?","a":"Because it answered Wilson’s wish to end the war without annexations, holding the well-being of the peoples concerned to be a sacred trust of civilisation rather than a possession."},{"q":"Why were the territories graded into three classes?","a":"Because the grades ranked how far their inhabitants were judged able to stand alone, so an A mandate was provisionally recognised as a nation while a C mandate was governed as part of the holder’s own territory."},{"q":"Why is the system said to have both restrained and dressed up colonial rule?","a":"Because it constrained annexation and created an obligation to report, while giving continued European government new legal clothes at the moment self-determination had been promised."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Article_22_of_Covenant_of_the_League_of_Nations%2C_highlighting_the_three_mandate_classes.jpg/500px-Article_22_of_Covenant_of_the_League_of_Nations%2C_highlighting_the_three_mandate_classes.jpg","title":"Article 22 of the Covenant of the League of Nations","desc":"The three classes of mandate as Article 22 set them out, boxed in red, blue and yellow","alt":"A page of printed text headed Article 22, three of whose paragraphs are outlined in red, blue and yellow boxes","credit":"Allied Nations of World War I, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Article_22_of_Covenant_of_the_League_of_Nations,_highlighting_the_three_mandate_classes.jpg"},"wiki":"League of Nations mandate"},
    "ww2-012": {"questions":["Every decision of its Assembly and Council had to be unanimous, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imposed sanctions only once, against Italy over Abyssinia, and to no effect.","Japan left in 1933 over Manchuria and Germany the same year, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also ran the Saar, resettled refugees, and its technical work outlasted it in bodies the United Nations inherited."],"abstract":"The <b>League of Nations</b> was the first permanent international organisation founded to keep the peace, created by a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Covenant_of_the_League_of_Nations\">Covenant</span> of 26 articles that formed Part I of the Treaty of Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It came into existence on 10 January 1920, and 41 states opened its first <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Assembly_of_the_League_of_Nations\">Assembly</span> at Geneva that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its members renounced secret diplomacy, undertook to reduce their armaments, and pledged to respect one another’s territorial integrity and political independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Assembly gave every member one vote, a Council of great and lesser powers met at least once a year, and a permanent <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Secretariat_of_the_League_of_Nations\">Secretariat</span> ran the business; decisions in both bodies had to be unanimous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 63 states belonged at one time or another and never more than 60 at once, and the United States, whose president had proposed the thing, never joined at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Article 16 declared that a member going to war in defiance of the Covenant had made war on every other member, but sanctions were left to each state’s own judgement and needed a unanimous Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League settled the Åland islands in 1920, Upper Silesia in 1921 and a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Incident_at_Petrich\">Greek-Bulgarian border war</span> in 1925, and of twenty-four disputes brought to its Council between 1921 and 1936 it resolved five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It administered the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Territory_of_the_Saar_Basin\">Saar</span>, supervised Danzig and the minority treaties, and oversaw the mandates; its commissions worked on refugees, the opium trade, slavery and public health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan walked out in 1933 rather than accept the Council’s finding on Manchuria, Germany left the same year and Italy in 1937, and article 16 was invoked exactly once, against Italy over Abyssinia, without effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League was wound up in 1946, its court giving way to the International Court of Justice, and its technical bodies and its idea of collective security passing to the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Anita Ziegerhofer, \"League of Nations,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/league-of-nations/. [Open access]","<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, 28 June 1919, arts. 5, 8, 10 and 16, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United Nations Office at Geneva, \"The League of Nations,\" UN Library and Archives Geneva, https://www.ungeneva.org/en/library-archives/league-of-nations. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"The League of Nations, 1920,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/league. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, \"History,\" https://www.icj-cij.org/history. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/League_of_nations_at_Salle_de_la_Reformation%2C_Geneva.jpg/1920px-League_of_nations_at_Salle_de_la_Reformation%2C_Geneva.jpg","title":"The Assembly in session at Geneva, 1920","desc":"Delegates at their desks on the floor of the Salle de la Réformation in Geneva, where the League's Assembly met from 1920, with the public and the press crowding the gallery above.","credit":"Photograph by Alfred Frankl, 1920, Fridtjof Nansen picture archive, National Library of Norway, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:League_of_nations_at_Salle_de_la_Reformation,_Geneva.jpg","alt":"A crowded hall seen from above, rows of men in dark suits seated at long desks covered in papers, and a packed gallery of spectators along the balcony."},"why":[{"q":"Why was collective security so hard to enforce?","a":"Because although article 16 declared war on one member war on all, sanctions were left to each state’s own judgement and needed a unanimous Council."},{"q":"Why did unanimity weaken the organisation?","a":"Because decisions in both the Assembly and the Council had to be unanimous, so any state with an interest in the outcome could stop a finding against it."},{"q":"Why did members leaving matter more than members joining?","a":"Because Japan walked out rather than accept the finding on Manchuria, Germany left the same year and Italy in 1937, so the states most likely to be judged simply removed themselves."}],"wiki":"League of Nations"},
    "ww2-013": {"questions":["Republicans feared that Article X of the Covenant bound America to defend other members’ borders, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pressed reservations that Wilson, half-paralysed by a stroke, refused to accept.","9 months after the Senate’s refusal Harding won the presidency on a platform against the League, and the country <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had kept out of it made its own peace with Germany in 1921."],"abstract":"<b>Henry Cabot Lodge</b> was the Republican Senate majority leader and chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee who led the opposition that kept the United States out of the League of Nations its own president had proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wilson had attached the Covenant to the Treaty of Versailles as its first part, believing that an effective League would in time soften whatever was unjust in the peace terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He returned to Washington in February 1919 to find opposition already building in Congress and the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objection that mattered was not the treaty’s harshness towards Germany but Article X of the Covenant, in which members undertook to preserve one another’s territorial integrity and political independence against external aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Opponents feared losing control of American foreign policy, transferring the power to declare war from Congress to the League’s council, and inviting scrutiny of the interventions the United States was used to making in Central America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A majority of the Senate was for the treaty, but ratification needed two-thirds, and the opposition split into irreconcilables who would take it on no terms and reservationists who would take it with amendments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reservations asserted Congress’s right to declare war, a right to withdraw from the League, and international recognition of the Monroe Doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wilson took his case to the country in a long speaking tour, collapsed, and suffered a crippling stroke on 27 September 1919; from his sickroom he refused every reservation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In March 1920 the treaty was defeated by forty-nine votes to thirty-five, and 9 months later Warren Harding won the presidency on a platform against the League.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Congress ended the war by joint resolution in July 1921 and the Treaty of Berlin restored relations with Germany that August, so the organisation built to keep the peace began without the strongest power on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, \"The League of Nations, 1920,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/league. [Open access]","Jennifer D. Keene, \"United States of America,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/united-states-of-america/. [Open access]","Mark E. Benbow, \"Wilson, Woodrow,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/wilson-woodrow/. [Open access]","<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, 28 June 1919, art. 10, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Germany,\" <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/germany. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Henry_Cabot_Lodge.jpg/960px-Henry_Cabot_Lodge.jpg","title":"Henry Cabot Lodge","desc":"A studio portrait from the Senate years by the Washington firm Harris and Ewing. Lodge chaired the Foreign Relations Committee and led the opposition that kept the United States out of the League of Nations.","credit":"Harris and Ewing, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Cabot_Lodge.jpg","alt":"A black and white studio portrait of an elderly man with a full white beard and moustache and swept-back hair, in a dark suit and tie, looking towards the camera."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the opposition fasten on Article X?","a":"Because members undertook to preserve one another’s territorial integrity, which opponents feared would transfer the power to declare war from Congress to the League’s council."},{"q":"Why did a Senate majority for the treaty not ratify it?","a":"Because ratification needed two-thirds, and the opposition split into irreconcilables who would take it on no terms and reservationists who would take it with amendments."},{"q":"Why did Wilson’s own conduct help defeat it?","a":"Because he took his case to the country, collapsed and suffered a crippling stroke, and from his sickroom refused every reservation."}],"wiki":"Henry Cabot Lodge"},
    "ww2-014": {"questions":["Testifying to a parliamentary committee in November 1919, Hindenburg said the army had been stabbed from behind, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried his authority into every campaign of the nationalist right.","German Jews had served in the army out of proportion to their numbers, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was believed anyway, and it helped make the republic seem the creature of traitors."],"abstract":"The <b>stab-in-the-back myth</b>, in German the <i>Dolchstoßlegende</i>, was the false claim that the German army of 1918 was never beaten in the field but was betrayed from behind by revolutionaries, socialists and Jews at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany was in fact beaten: the Allied counteroffensive that began in July 1918 broke the spring offensives, and by the end of September Ludendorff himself admitted the war was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the army command that asked the government to seek an armistice, and Ludendorff put the blame for the defeat on the politicians in Berlin as he did so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ordinary Germans found the surrender hard to credit, because no fighting had reached German soil and German troops still stood deep in occupied Europe, while the collapse of the home front was visible daily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wartime propaganda had allowed no doubt of victory to the last, so the request for an armistice in October 1918 arrived as a shock demanding an explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Several versions circulated at once, blaming parliament, the socialists, the trade unions or the Jews, and the Pan-German League decided in 1918 to place the whole blame on the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legend gained its authority in November 1919, when Hindenburg told a parliamentary committee of inquiry that the army had been stabbed from behind, citing an English officer for the phrase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was entirely untrue — German Jews had served loyally and out of proportion to their numbers — but it was believed, and it made the republic look like the creature of traitors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German National People’s Party campaigned on it and gained ground in 1924, and the National Socialists took it up, while Ludendorff joined Hitler’s Munich putsch in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hindenburg was elected president in 1925 and appointed Hitler chancellor on 30 January 1933, by which time the myth had helped break off any argument between the republic’s defenders and its enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Boris Barth, \"Stab-in-the-back Myth,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/stab-in-the-back-myth/. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Dolchstoßlegende,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/dolchstosslegende.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitism in History: World War I,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism-in-history-world-war-i. [Open access]","Anna von der Goltz, \"Hindenburg, Paul von,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/hindenburg-paul-von/. [Open access]","Roger Chickering, \"Ludendorff, Erich,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/ludendorff-erich/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/1920_poster_12000_Jewish_soldiers_KIA_for_the_fatherland.jpg/500px-1920_poster_12000_Jewish_soldiers_KIA_for_the_fatherland.jpg","title":"An die deutschen Mütter, 1920","desc":"A leaflet answering the myth in its own time. Issued by the Reich Association of Jewish Front-Line Soldiers, it reads “To German mothers! 12,000 Jewish soldiers fell for the fatherland on the field of honour”, and asks German women not to let a Jewish mother be mocked in her grief.","credit":"Reichsbund jüdischer Frontsoldaten, 1920. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1920_poster_12000_Jewish_soldiers_KIA_for_the_fatherland.jpg","alt":"A black and white leaflet in which a woman in mourning sits beside a soldier’s grave marked with an Iron Cross, the grave inscribed in German with the words twelve thousand Jewish soldiers fell for the fatherland on the field of honour."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the defeat seem to need explaining at all?","a":"Because no fighting had reached German soil and German troops still stood deep in occupied Europe, while wartime propaganda had allowed no doubt of victory to the last."},{"q":"Why is the charge inverted as well as false?","a":"Because it was the army command that asked the government to seek an armistice, Ludendorff blaming the politicians in Berlin as he did so."},{"q":"Why did Hindenburg’s testimony matter so much?","a":"Because it gave the legend authority: telling a parliamentary committee that the army had been stabbed from behind made the republic look like the creature of traitors."}],"wiki":"Stab-in-the-back myth"},
    "ww2-015": {"questions":["Seats in the Reichstag of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were shared out in proportion to the vote, and article 48 let the president suspend basic rights and govern without it.","Sixteen governments came and went in the 14 years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which enfranchised women, set an 8-hour day, and ended when Hindenburg appointed Hitler chancellor."],"abstract":"The <b>Weimar Republic</b> is the name given to the German state between the fall of the empire in 1918 and the Nazi seizure of power in 1933, after the town in Thuringia where its constituent assembly met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The republic was proclaimed in Berlin on 9 November 1918, and the assembly gathered at Weimar rather than in a capital still torn by street fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A draft written largely by the constitutional lawyer Hugo Preuß was approved by 262 votes to 75 on 31 July 1919, signed by President Friedrich Ebert on 11 August and in force 3 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the first democratic constitution in German history: political authority came from the people, men and women over twenty voted by universal secret suffrage, and women were made equal before the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Reichstag was elected by proportional representation, and the president by the whole people for a 7-year term, with command of the armed forces and the power to dissolve parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Article 48 allowed the president, when public order was seriously disturbed, to suspend basic rights and take whatever measures he thought necessary, if need be with the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proportional representation gave small parties seats and a voice that no German had had before, but it also made majorities hard to assemble and later helped extremist parties into the chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sixteen governments held office in 14 years; the republic was fought by armed extremists of left and right in its early years, found relative calm from 1924, and lost it in the slump that began in 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also expanded unemployment insurance, established bargaining between workers and employers, and set an 8-hour working day, while Berlin became one of the most socially liberal cities in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended on 30 January 1933, when Hindenburg, who had been elected president in 1925 with the backing of the right, appointed Adolf Hitler chancellor of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Weimar Republic,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-weimar-republic. [Open access]","\"The Weimar Constitution (August 11, 1919),\" arts. 1, 22, 41, 43, 48 and 109, in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, German Historical Institute, Washington, DC, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/the-weimar-constitution-august-11-1919. [Open access]","Erik Jensen with Insa Kummer, \"Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, 2nd ed., German Historical Institute, Washington, DC, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Weimarer Republik,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik.html. [Open access]","Anna von der Goltz, \"Hindenburg, Paul von,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/hindenburg-paul-von/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-08215%2C_Berlin%2C_Verfassungsfeier_im_Stadion.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-08215%2C_Berlin%2C_Verfassungsfeier_im_Stadion.jpg","title":"Constitution Day in Berlin, 11 August 1929","desc":"The republic marking the tenth anniversary of its constitution. Young men in tabards bearing the Reich eagle carry an enormous black, red and gold flag across a stadium field — the colours the constitution gave the republic, against the black, white and red of the empire.","credit":"Georg Pahl, Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-08215. CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-08215,_Berlin,_Verfassungsfeier_im_Stadion.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph looking down on a stadium field where about twenty young men in tabards marked with an eagle carry a very large flag stretched flat between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the assembly meet in a small Thuringian town?","a":"Because the capital was still torn by street fighting, so the constituent assembly gathered at Weimar instead of in Berlin."},{"q":"Why did proportional representation cut both ways?","a":"Because it gave small parties seats and a voice no German had had before, and also made majorities hard to assemble and later helped extremist parties into the chamber."},{"q":"Why was Article 48 so consequential?","a":"Because it let the president suspend basic rights and take whatever measures he thought necessary when public order was seriously disturbed, if need be with the army."}],"wiki":"Weimar Republic","places":["pol:weimar"]},
    "ww2-016": {"questions":["Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from the Reichstag to forestall Liebknecht’s free socialist one, and that double announcement showed how divided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> already was.","Ebert’s bargain with the army let the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> end in a parliamentary republic rather than a council dictatorship, at the cost of Freikorps killings and a permanently split left."],"abstract":"The <b>German Revolution</b> of 1918 and 1919 destroyed the monarchy, replaced it with a parliamentary republic, and left the German left permanently divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began at the end of October 1918, when the naval command ordered the fleet out for a last battle although armistice talks had already opened, and war-weary sailors at Wilhelmshaven and Kiel mutinied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The revolt spread within days from sailors to workers, and by 10 November councils of workers and soldiers had taken over the municipal government of practically every large German city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first throne to fall was Bavaria’s, where a republic was proclaimed at Munich on 7 November under the Independent Social Democrat Kurt Eisner; the other princes went within days, and almost no blood was shed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 9 November Berlin struck, and Chancellor Max von Baden announced the Kaiser’s abdication on his own authority and handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert, both unconstitutionally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That afternoon Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from the Reichstag to forestall Karl Liebknecht’s free socialist republic, and the double proclamation showed how far the movement had already split.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next day the Social Democrats and Independents formed a Council of People’s Deputies, and General Wilhelm Groener promised the army’s support against the radical left in return for the officer corps keeping its autonomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In December a congress of the councils voted overwhelmingly for elections to a national assembly, which were held on 19 January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A rising in Berlin from 5 to 12 January, named after the Spartacists though they did little to direct it, was crushed, and Liebknecht and Rosa Luxemburg were murdered on the night of 15 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly 1,200 people died in further Berlin fighting that March, the Munich council republic was put down in April, and many workers never forgave the Social Democrats for the alliance that did it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Revolution von 1918/19,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/revolution.html. [in German] [Open access]","\"The Spread of the 1918 Revolution,\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, German Historical Institute, Washington, DC, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/the-spread-of-the-1918-revolution. [Open access]","Alexander Gallus, \"Revolutions (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-germany/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Weimar Republic,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-weimar-republic. [Open access]","Ingrid Sharp, \"Luxemburg, Rosa,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/luxemburg-rosa/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-810%2C_Berlin%2C_Brandenburger_Tor%2C_Novemberrevolution.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-810%2C_Berlin%2C_Brandenburger_Tor%2C_Novemberrevolution.jpg","title":"Revolutionaries at the Brandenburg Gate, 9 November 1918","desc":"An open car of armed soldiers with a machine gun on the roof and a flag streaming behind drives through the gate on the day the Kaiser's abdication was announced and the republic proclaimed, with crowds watching from the pavement.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-B0527-0001-810 / unknown author / CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-810,_Berlin,_Brandenburger_Tor,_Novemberrevolution.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of an open motor car crowded with armed soldiers, a machine gun mounted above the windscreen and a flag flying, driving through the columns of the Brandenburg Gate past onlookers."},"why":[{"q":"Why did sailors mutiny in the last days of the war?","a":"Because the naval command ordered the fleet out for a last battle although armistice talks had already opened."},{"q":"Why did two republics get proclaimed on one afternoon?","a":"Because Scheidemann proclaimed one from the Reichstag to forestall Liebknecht’s free socialist republic, which showed how far the movement had already split."},{"q":"Why did many workers never forgive the Social Democrats?","a":"Because Groener promised the army’s support against the radical left in return for the officer corps keeping its autonomy, and that alliance put the risings down."}],"wiki":"German revolution of 1918–1919"},
    "ww2-017": {"questions":["A general strike and the refusal of the civil service to take orders broke the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within 4 days, without a shot fired by the republic’s own soldiers.","Wearing swastikas on their helmets, the men who mounted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were sent weeks later to crush workers in the Ruhr who had risen against them."],"abstract":"The <b>Kapp Putsch</b> was an attempt by army officers and Freikorps troops to overthrow the Weimar Republic in March 1920, and the first serious challenge to it from the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 29 February 1920, following an instruction from the Inter-Allied Military Control Commission, the defence minister Gustav Noske ordered the disbandment of Hermann Ehrhardt’s marine brigade of about 6,000 men and the Loewenfeld Freikorps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Walther von Lüttwitz, the senior commander of the provisional Reichswehr, refused, and early on 13 March the Ehrhardt brigade occupied the government quarter of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The men marched with swastikas painted on their steel helmets, and Wolfgang Kapp, a co-founder of the wartime German Fatherland Party, made himself chancellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The army would not act against them, so the cabinet, the chancellor Gustav Bauer and President Ebert left the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From 15 March about twelve million workers stopped work in the largest strike movement in German history, cutting Berlin off from news and transport and shutting its gas, water and power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The putschists could not restart the utilities and gave up on 17 March; what also broke them was the ministerial bureaucracy’s refusal to carry out Kapp’s orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Saxony, Thuringia and above all the Ruhr the left tried to carry the strike on into a workers’ revolution, and the government sent Freikorps against them, the Ehrhardt brigade among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political murder followed: Matthias Erzberger in 1921 and, on 24 June 1922, the foreign minister Walther Rathenau, shot in Berlin by members of the far-right Organisation Consul after nationalist newspapers had all but called for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The republic survived every one of these attacks, but it had learned that its own army would put down the left and stand aside for the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Burkhard Asmuss, \"Der Lüttwitz-Kapp-Putsch 1920,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/luettwitz-kapp-putsch-1920. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Generalstreik 1920,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/generalstreik-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Weimar Republic,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-weimar-republic. [Open access]","Vera Hierholzer, \"Die Ermordung Walter Rathenaus,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/ermordung-rathenaus-1922. [Open access]","Alexander Gallus, \"Revolutions (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-germany/. [Open access]","Levke Harders, \"Wolfgang Kapp 1858–1922,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/wolfgang-kapp. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Bonn_Marktplatz_Demonstration_Kapp-Putsch_1920.jpg/1280px-Bonn_Marktplatz_Demonstration_Kapp-Putsch_1920.jpg","title":"Demonstration against the Kapp Putsch, Bonn, 15 March 1920","desc":"A crowd fills the market square in Bonn on 15 March 1920, the first day of the general strike called against the putsch, photographed from an upper window of the old town hall.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Stadtarchiv Bonn. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonn_Marktplatz_Demonstration_Kapp-Putsch_1920.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd of men in hats and overcoats packing a paved market square, seen from above, with shop fronts and a large arched doorway along one side."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the government have to leave Berlin?","a":"Because the army would not act against the putschists who had occupied the government quarter, so the cabinet and the president went rather than be taken."},{"q":"Why did the attempt collapse without fighting?","a":"Because about twelve million workers stopped work, cutting Berlin’s gas, water and power, and the ministerial bureaucracy refused to carry out the new chancellor’s orders."},{"q":"Why was the episode a lesson about the republic’s own army?","a":"Because it had learned that its army would put down the left and stand aside for the right, the same Freikorps then being sent against the workers in the Ruhr."}],"wiki":"Kapp Putsch"},
    "ww2-018": {"questions":["Miners and officials were told not to obey the occupiers, and the state printed money to pay them, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in a currency collapse rather than in coal.","The government that had urged passive resistance during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called it off in September 1923, by which time only four of the district’s 89 blast furnaces were still alight."],"abstract":"The <b>Occupation of the Ruhr</b> was the entry of French and Belgian troops into Germany’s coal and steel district in January 1923, to take reparations by force after the Allied Reparations Commission found German deliveries in arrears at the end of 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shortfall was a small one, and the French prime minister Raymond Poincaré wanted more from the occupation than coal: his aim was to revise the Treaty of Versailles in France’s favour and push Germany’s western frontier eastward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 60,000 soldiers, five French divisions with some Belgian units among them, crossed into the district on 11 January and at once imposed a state of emergency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government of Wilhelm Cuno could not resist militarily and did not try; it called instead for passive resistance, and forbade officials to carry out the occupiers’ orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The population held together, and the occupation authorities expelled between 120,000 and 150,000 people from the Ruhr and from the Rhineland into unoccupied Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Active resistance grew beside it, with nationalists and communists carrying out sabotage and bombings together, and saboteurs who were executed, Albert Leo Schlageter among them, were made martyrs at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Paying the strikers meant printing money, and the presses that funded the resistance drove the mark’s collapse into hyperinflation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cost was plain on the ground: a British correspondent walking through Essen in late September found four of the district’s 89 blast furnaces still working, the rest cold and most fallen in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gustav Stresemann, chancellor since 13 August, called off passive resistance on 26 September 1923 to save the currency, and was denounced for it by nationalists and separatists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The troops stayed until 1925 and went only after the Dawes Plan rescheduled reparations and provided for their evacuation, so the resistance had cost Germany its currency without shortening the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Ruhrbesetzung,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/ruhrbesetzung-1923. [Open access]","\"Reichstag Hearing on the Occupation of the Ruhr (January 17, 1923),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:document-4341. [Open access]","Morgan Philips Price, \"On the End of Passive Resistance in the Ruhr (September 25, 1923),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:document-4420. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","\"The Occupation of the Rhineland after the First World War,\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:map-5018. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Inflation,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/inflation. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/French_enter_Essen.jpg/500px-French_enter_Essen.jpg","title":"French cavalry entering Essen, 1923","desc":"A column of French cavalry in steel helmets rides down a cobbled street in Essen past a corner cafe, in the first weeks of the occupation.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_enter_Essen.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of mounted soldiers in steel helmets riding in a broad column down a wet cobbled street, with shop fronts and a motor car on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a small shortfall answered with an occupation?","a":"Because Poincaré wanted more than coal: his aim was to revise the Treaty of Versailles in France’s favour and push Germany’s western frontier eastward."},{"q":"Why did passive resistance wreck the currency?","a":"Because paying the strikers meant printing money, and the presses that funded the resistance drove the mark’s collapse into hyperinflation."},{"q":"Why did calling the resistance off not end the occupation?","a":"Because the troops stayed until 1925 and went only after the Dawes Plan rescheduled reparations, so the resistance cost Germany its currency without shortening the occupation."}],"wiki":"Occupation of the Ruhr"},
    "ww2-019": {"questions":["Paying strikers in the occupied Ruhr with freshly printed notes turned a bad currency into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the Reich’s whole war debt came to just over sixteen pfennigs.","By the autumn of 1923, 133 outside firms were printing notes for the Reich day and night, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had reached a note for 100 trillion marks."],"abstract":"The <b>German hyperinflation</b> was the destruction of the mark’s value in 1922 and 1923, at the end of which the new currency was fixed at one Rentenmark to a trillion paper marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began in the way the First World War was paid for: Germany borrowed rather than taxed, and of 164 billion marks of war costs only 10 billion came from levies and taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The money in circulation had already grown fivefold by 1918, and pensions for the wounded and the bereaved, the return to peacetime production and a reparations bill of 132 billion gold marks kept the deficits growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1922 the Reich’s debt service stood at 126% of its revenue, and the murder of Walther Rathenau in June shook what confidence in the republic was left at home and abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French and Belgian entry into the Ruhr in January 1923 turned decline into catastrophe, because the government answered it with passive resistance and paid the strikers with printed money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the autumn of 1923 as many as 133 outside firms and 1,783 presses were printing notes for the Reich day and night, and in November the Reichsbank issued a note for 100 trillion marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prices moved faster than the presses: a pound of rice cost 80,000 marks one day and 160,000 the next, wages were often paid daily, and a restaurant bill could double during the meal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Real wages fell to about 40% of their pre-war level and savings built over generations were wiped out; a British correspondent wrote in October that Germany’s condition beggared description.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There were winners, since industry borrowed in good money and repaid in worthless money, and the state’s entire war debt of 164 billion marks came to just over sixteen pfennigs at the changeover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That changeover came on 15 November 1923, when the Rentenmark was issued against a mortgage on German farmland and industry, and the Reichsmark replaced it in August 1924 under the Dawes Plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Inflation,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/inflation. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Währungsreform 1923,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/waehrungsreform-1923. [in German] [Open access]","Friedrich Kroner, \"Overwrought Nerves\" (1923), <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/friedrich-kroner-overwrought-nerves-1923. [Open access]","Morgan Philips Price, \"On Hyperinflation and its Effects (October 18, 1923),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/morgan-philips-price-on-hyperinflation-and-its-effects-october-18-1923. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","Morgan Philips Price, “On the End of Passive Resistance in the Ruhr (September 25, 1923),” German History in Documents and Images, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/morgan-philips-price-on-the-end-of-passive-resistance-in-the-ruhr-1923. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/In_a_Berlin_Bank_LCCN2014716642.jpg/1280px-In_a_Berlin_Bank_LCCN2014716642.jpg","title":"In a Berlin bank, 1923","desc":"A clerk reaches up into a wall of banknote bundles stacked from floor to ceiling in a Berlin bank, photographed for an American news agency in 1923.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, ggbain.36493. No known restrictions on publication, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:In_a_Berlin_Bank_LCCN2014716642.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a man in a dark suit standing beside stacks of banded banknotes piled from the floor to the ceiling and filling most of the room, reaching up to the top of one stack."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the collapse begin with how the war was financed?","a":"Because Germany borrowed rather than taxed — of 164 billion marks of war costs only 10 billion came from levies and taxes — so the money in circulation had already grown fivefold by 1918."},{"q":"Why did prices outrun even the printing presses?","a":"Because a pound of rice could cost 80,000 marks one day and 160,000 the next, so wages were often paid daily and a restaurant bill could double during the meal."},{"q":"Why did some parties gain from the destruction?","a":"Because industry borrowed in good money and repaid in worthless money, and the state’s whole war debt of 164 billion marks came to just over sixteen pfennigs at the changeover."}],"wiki":"Hyperinflation in the Weimar Republic"},
    "ww2-020": {"questions":["Half the German National People’s Party voted for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in August 1924 despite calling it a surrender of sovereignty, because France had promised to leave the Ruhr in return.","American loans made under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> paid German reparations, which paid French and British war debts, which were owed back to the United States."],"abstract":"The <b>Dawes Plan</b> was the settlement of 1924 that rescheduled German reparations, ended the occupation of the Ruhr and brought American money into the German economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It followed the collapse of the old arrangement: Germany had fallen behind on the payments set by the London Schedule of 1921, France and Belgium had occupied the Ruhr, and the mark had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Allies now feared that Germany could not pay at all, and Washington feared that France and Britain would then default on the war loans they owed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A committee of experts chaired by Charles G. Dawes, a Chicago banker who was then the first director of the American budget bureau, reported on 9 April 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its principle was that Germany should pay only what its economy could bear, so the plan fixed neither a total sum nor a date by which the debt would be discharged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Annual payments were to begin at one billion gold marks and rise to 2.5 billion after 5 years, and the Reichsbahn and the Reichsbank were put under international control as security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It came with money: a loan of 800 million marks to steady the Reichsbank, floated on the American market by J. P. Morgan and oversubscribed at once, after which American banks went on lending for 4 more years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That produced a circle in which American loans paid German reparations, which paid French and British war debts, which were owed back to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German right attacked the plan as a surrender of sovereignty, but half the deputies of the German National People’s Party voted for it on 29 August 1924, because France had promised in return to leave the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The money and the calm began a recovery in which German output grew by half between 1924 and 1929, and Dawes shared the Nobel Peace Prize for 1925 with Austen Chamberlain, both of them receiving it a year later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Dawes-Plan,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/dawesplan. [Open access]","\"The Dawes Plan (1924),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/the-dawes-plan-1924. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","\"The Nobel Peace Prize 1925,\" <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1925/summary/. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Ruhrbesetzung,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/ruhrbesetzung-1923. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Allied_Experts_in_Berlin_-_Flora_-_Sir_R._Kindersley_-_McFadyean_-_Pirelli_-_Allix_-_Parmentier_-_Dawes_-_Young_-_Houtart_-_Sir_J._Stamp_-_Francqui_LCCN2014716971.jpg/1280px-Allied_Experts_in_Berlin_-_Flora_-_Sir_R._Kindersley_-_McFadyean_-_Pirelli_-_Allix_-_Parmentier_-_Dawes_-_Young_-_Houtart_-_Sir_J._Stamp_-_Francqui_LCCN2014716971.jpg","title":"The Allied experts in Berlin","desc":"Eleven of the Allied experts round a table in Berlin, their names written along the top and foot of the negative: Flora, Kindersley, McFadyean, Pirelli, Allix, Parmentier, Dawes, Young, Houtart, Stamp and Francqui. No date was recorded on the caption card.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, ggbain.36823. No known restrictions on publication, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_Experts_in_Berlin_-_Flora_-_Sir_R._Kindersley_-_McFadyean_-_Pirelli_-_Allix_-_Parmentier_-_Dawes_-_Young_-_Houtart_-_Sir_J._Stamp_-_Francqui_LCCN2014716971.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of eleven men in dark suits seated and standing behind a long table with papers in front of them, names handwritten across the top and bottom of the negative."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States care about a European settlement?","a":"Because Washington feared that if Germany could not pay, France and Britain would default on the war loans they owed it."},{"q":"Why did the plan fix no total and no end date?","a":"Because its principle was that Germany should pay only what its economy could bear, so the annuities were set and the final sum was not."},{"q":"Why did half the nationalist deputies vote for it?","a":"Because France had promised in return to leave the Ruhr, which was worth more to them than the sovereignty the plan gave up."}],"wiki":"Dawes Plan"},
    "ww2-021": {"questions":["Two foreign ministers shared a Nobel Peace Prize for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which took Germany out of isolation and into the League of Nations the following September.","The western guarantee given by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted a decade, until German troops marched back into the demilitarised Rhineland in March 1936 in breach of it."],"abstract":"The <b>Locarno Treaties</b> were the agreements concluded at Locarno in Switzerland on 16 October 1925, by which Germany accepted its western frontier and Europe’s statesmen believed the war had at last been closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conference had opened on 5 October, with the German chancellor Hans Luther and the foreign minister Gustav Stresemann meeting the leading statesmen of France, Britain, Italy, Belgium, Poland and Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the main treaty Germany, France and Belgium guaranteed the frontiers between them as fixed at Versailles, along with the demilitarised zone of the Rhineland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain and Italy signed as guarantors, undertaking to come to the help of whichever side a breach was committed against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four arbitration treaties went with it, binding Germany to settle disputes with France, Belgium, Poland and Czechoslovakia before an international commission rather than by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What Germany did not do was recognise its eastern frontier: Stresemann took the western guarantee while expressly keeping open the revision of the border with Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That asymmetry was the point for each side, since France gained a British guarantee and an end to isolation, Britain gained peaceful change at little cost, and Germany gained a step back towards great-power standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British foreign secretary Austen Chamberlain called Locarno the real dividing line between the years of war and the years of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German isolation was over: the country joined the League of Nations on 10 September 1926, and Briand and Stresemann shared the Nobel Peace Prize for 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The western guarantee held for a decade, until about 30,000 German soldiers marched into the demilitarised Rhineland on 7 March 1936 in breach of both Locarno and Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Burkhard Asmuss and Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Konferenz von Locarno 1925,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/locarno. [in German] [Open access]","\"Treaty of Mutual Guarantee between Germany, Belgium, France, Great Britain and Italy; October 16, 1925,\" <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/locarno_001.asp. [Open access]","\"Negotations between Gustav Stresemann, Austen Chamberlain, and Aristide Briand in Locarno (October 1925),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/negotations-between-gustav-stresemann-joseph-austen-chamberlain-and-aristide-briand-in-locarno-october-1925. [Open access]","\"The Nobel Peace Prize 1926,\" <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1926/summary/. [Open access]","\"Wehrmacht Units Occupy the Demilitarized Rhineland Zone: The First Troops March Into Koblenz (March 1936),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/wehrmacht-units-occupy-the-demilitarized-rhineland-zone-the-first-troops-march-into-koblenz-march-1936. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Conf%C3%A9rence_de_Locarno_-_de_gauche_%C3%A0_droite_Luther%2C_Stressmann%2C_Von_Schubert%2C_Kiep_-_btv1b9024864s.jpg/1280px-Conf%C3%A9rence_de_Locarno_-_de_gauche_%C3%A0_droite_Luther%2C_Stressmann%2C_Von_Schubert%2C_Kiep_-_btv1b9024864s.jpg","title":"The German delegation at Locarno, October 1925","desc":"Five men of the German delegation standing under a pergola in a garden at Locarno. The agency caption names four of them, among them Chancellor Hans Luther and Foreign Minister Gustav Stresemann, and the library dates the print to 6 October 1925.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, Bibliotheque nationale de France, Meurisse 27136 A. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conf%C3%A9rence_de_Locarno_-_de_gauche_%C3%A0_droite_Luther%2C_Stressmann%2C_Von_Schubert%2C_Kiep_-_btv1b9024864s.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of five men in suits standing on a lawn under a wooden pergola covered with climbing plants, garden chairs and a bench behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany guarantee one frontier and not the other?","a":"Because Stresemann took the western guarantee while expressly keeping open the revision of the border with Poland, so the arbitration treaties in the east promised only procedure."},{"q":"Why did each side think it had gained?","a":"Because France gained a British guarantee and an end to isolation, Britain gained peaceful change at little cost, and Germany gained a step back towards great-power standing."},{"q":"Why is the western guarantee dated to a decade?","a":"Because it held until about 30,000 German soldiers marched into the demilitarised Rhineland on 7 March 1936, in breach of both Locarno and Versailles."}],"wiki":"Locarno Treaties"},
    "ww2-022": {"questions":["Only wars of aggression were covered by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so a state could sign it in 1928 without giving up anything it expected to need.","When Japan invaded Manchuria in 1931, appeals under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> failed, and the treaty that had outlawed war turned out to have no machinery behind it."],"abstract":"The <b>Kellogg-Briand Pact</b> was a treaty signed in Paris on 27 August 1928 by which the signatories renounced war as an instrument of national policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Until the First World War a sovereign state could make war at its own discretion, and the League of Nations had limited that right without making a war of aggression unlawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pact began as a French proposal: in an open letter of April 1927 the foreign minister Aristide Briand offered the United States a bilateral agreement to outlaw war between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> President Coolidge and his secretary of state Frank B. Kellogg were wary, fearing a bilateral pact would read as an alliance obliging America to defend France, and proposed instead that the two invite every nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wider version was popular, partly because only wars of aggression were covered and self-defence was not, so a state could sign without giving up anything it expected to need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The treaty has two substantive articles: the parties condemn recourse to war and renounce it as an instrument of national policy, and agree to settle all disputes between them by peaceful means alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fifteen states signed at Paris, among them Germany, whose delegation under the already gravely ill Stresemann had pressed for a treaty, and by 1929 sixty-three states had joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American Senate ratified it by 85 votes to 1, but only after recording that it neither limited the right of self-defence nor obliged the United States to act against a signatory who broke it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was tested in 1931, when Japan invaded Manchuria after an explosion on a railway near Mukden, and the United States sat in on League of Nations council meetings for the first time to press for enforcement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those appeals failed, and with no enforcement machinery and no agreed definition of self-defence the pact did little to prevent the war it was written against, though Kellogg received the Nobel Peace Prize for it in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]","\"Kellogg-Briand Pact 1928,\" <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/kbpact.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Briand-Kellogg-Pakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/briandkellog. [Open access]","\"The Nobel Peace Prize 1929,\" <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1929/summary/. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/President_Coolidge_signs_Kellogg_Treaty._President_Coolidge_today_formally_signed_the_document_of_ratification_of_the_Kellogg_treaty_before_a_distinguished_gathering_in_the_east_room_of_the_LCCN2016889136.jpg/1280px-President_Coolidge_signs_Kellogg_Treaty._President_Coolidge_today_formally_signed_the_document_of_ratification_of_the_Kellogg_treaty_before_a_distinguished_gathering_in_the_east_room_of_the_LCCN2016889136.jpg","title":"Coolidge signs the American ratification, 1929","desc":"President Calvin Coolidge signing the American instrument of ratification in the East Room of the White House in 1929, watched by Vice President Charles Dawes, Secretary of State Frank B. Kellogg and members of the Cabinet and the Senate.","credit":"National Photo Company Collection, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, LCCN 2016889136. No known restrictions on publication, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Coolidge_signs_Kellogg_Treaty._President_Coolidge_today_formally_signed_the_document_of_ratification_of_the_Kellogg_treaty_before_a_distinguished_gathering_in_the_east_room_of_the_LCCN2016889136.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a man signing a document at a long table in a grand room with two chandeliers, some thirty men in dark suits seated and standing behind him."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States want the pact widened?","a":"Because a bilateral agreement with France would read as an alliance obliging America to defend her, so Washington proposed that the two invite every nation instead."},{"q":"Why was so sweeping a treaty easy to sign?","a":"Because only wars of aggression were covered and self-defence was not, so a state could sign without giving up anything it expected to need."},{"q":"Why did it fail its first test?","a":"Because it had no enforcement machinery and no agreed definition of self-defence, so the appeals over Manchuria came to nothing."}],"wiki":"Kellogg–Briand Pact"},
    "ww2-023": {"questions":["Cinema and spectator sport became mass entertainments on the American model during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted from the end of the hyperinflation to the crash of 1929.","The unemployment insurance passed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was calculated for 1.4 million claimants, and the government fell in 1930 when the figure passed five million."],"abstract":"The <b>Golden Twenties</b> is the name given to Germany’s brief recovery between the end of the hyperinflation in 1923 and the crash of 1929, and it is a label its own users qualify, since misery was general among working families through the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A stable currency and the foreign loans that came with the Dawes Plan started a real upswing, and German output grew by half between 1924 and 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Industry was reorganised and rationalised on American lines, and by the end of 1928 the modernised plant employed four million more workers than before the war within the same borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wages followed: the standard of living rose appreciably, and for much of the working class it reached or passed the pre-war level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In July 1927 the Reichstag passed by an overwhelming majority the first national unemployment insurance in Germany, giving workers and salaried employees a legal right to benefit,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and employer and employee each paid 3% of the wage into the fund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Benefit ran for 26 weeks at between a quarter and three-quarters of the wage the claimant had been earning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The culture is what the name remembers: by the middle of the decade about two million people went to the cinema every day, and from 1923 radio carried big sporting events and concerts to a mass audience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans argued about all of it under the catchword Americanism, which stood for industrial, economic and cultural modernity and rested as much on borrowed images as on any knowledge of America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The recovery was bought on credit, and the Reichsbank’s foreign commissioner said so in December 1928: the price was an annual interest burden abroad of well over five hundred million reichsmarks, and part of the borrowing was not productive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The insurance had never been planned for more than 700,000 unemployed at a time; by February 1930 the figure had passed five million, and the governing coalition broke over how to pay for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Alltagsleben,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/alltagsleben. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Dawes-Plan,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/dawesplan. [Open access]","“Report of the Commissioner of the Reichsbank (December 10, 1928),” German History in Documents and Images, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/report-of-the-commissioner-of-the-reichsbank-december-10-1928. [Open access]","“Reichstag Debate on the Unemployment Insurance Act (1927),” German History in Documents and Images, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/reichstag-debate-on-the-unemployment-insurance-act-1927. [Open access]","Helene Simon, “Unemployment” (April 1929), German History in Documents and Images, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/helene-simon-unemployment-april-1929. [Open access]","“Sign on a Barbershop Announcing Special Prices for the Unemployed (1927),” German History in Documents and Images, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:image-4099. [Open access]","Rudolf Kayser, “Americanism” (1925), German History in Documents and Images, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/rudolf-kayser-americanism-1925. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Berlin%2C_das_neu_erbaute_Capitol%2C_Portal_mit_viel_Verkehr_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._001524.jpg/1280px-Berlin%2C_das_neu_erbaute_Capitol%2C_Portal_mit_viel_Verkehr_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._001524.jpg","title":"The Capitol cinema in Berlin, April 1927","desc":"The newly built Capitol cinema advertising the latest Douglas Fairbanks film, with motor cars drawn up along the kerb and tram rails in the foreground.","credit":"Sammlung Willy Pragher, Landesarchiv Baden-Wuerttemberg, Staatsarchiv Freiburg W 134 Nr. 001524. CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin%2C_das_neu_erbaute_Capitol%2C_Portal_mit_viel_Verkehr_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._001524.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a large cinema front lettered CAPITOL, with a painted film hoarding above the entrance, a row of open motor cars parked along the pavement and tram rails in the road."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name qualified by the people who use it?","a":"Because society remained deeply divided and want was widespread among working families living at the edge of subsistence throughout the same years."},{"q":"Why was the recovery fragile?","a":"Because it was bought on credit: the Reichsbank’s foreign commissioner put the price at an annual interest burden abroad of well over five hundred million reichsmarks, part of it not productively invested."},{"q":"Why did the unemployment insurance break the government?","a":"Because the fund had been calculated for at most 1.4 million unemployed, and by February 1930 the figure had passed five million."}],"wiki":"Golden Twenties"},
    "ww2-024": {"questions":["The United States would not attend the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it refused to deal with the Russian government, which it had not recognised since breaking off relations in 1917.","Fearing they would be left out of whatever the Allies agreed with Moscow, the German delegates to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went 30 kilometres (20 miles) up the coast and made their own deal."],"abstract":"The <b>Genoa Conference</b> was an international economic conference of some thirty countries held in Italy in April 1922, called above all to bring Soviet Russia back into the European trading system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia had left the war separately at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, and the Allies had answered the revolution with troops, Japanese, American and other forces occupying parts of northern Russia, Ukraine and Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Bolshevik government had repudiated the Tsar’s debts, ignored existing treaties and seized foreign property, and the United States broke off relations in December 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There had been one chance of a settlement: in March 1919 an American attaché sent to Moscow during the Paris peace conference came back with a Bolshevik offer of an armistice, an end to the blockade and a promise to honour Russia’s debts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Allied leaders let the deadline pass without answering, and that failure delayed American recognition of Soviet Russia for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Genoa was the British prime minister’s attempt 3 years later, and the United States refused to attend, in part because it would not deal with the Russian government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German delegation went with modest expectations and a fear of being shut out of the important business, and of any Allied agreement with Russia leaving Germany out in the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the two outcasts dealt with each other: on 16 April, at Rapallo about 30 kilometres (20 miles) along the coast, Germany and Soviet Russia restored relations and waived their war claims against one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Allied powers were surprised and angry, and feared for the whole Versailles order, above all for the existence of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The larger settlement Genoa had been called to make never came, and the United States did not recognise the Soviet Union until 16 November 1933, after almost 16 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Bullitt Mission to Soviet Russia, 1919,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/bullitt-mission. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Recognition of the Soviet Union, 1933,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/ussr. [Open access]","\"Cabinet Consultation with the President Regarding the Agenda for Genoa (April 5, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/cabinet-consultation-with-the-president-regarding-the-agenda-for-genoa-april-5-1922. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Vertrag von Rapallo,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/vertrag-von-rapallo. [Open access]","\"German Cabinet Meeting on Diplomatic Effects of the Treaty of Rapallo (April 18, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/german-cabinet-meeting-on-diplomatic-effects-of-the-treaty-of-rapallo-april-18-1922. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Conf%C3%A9rence_de_G%C3%AAnes_-_arriv%C3%A9e_de_M._Lloyd_Georges_aux_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9s_de_M._Pacta_et_de_M._Schlanger_-_btv1b9038062q.jpg/1280px-Conf%C3%A9rence_de_G%C3%AAnes_-_arriv%C3%A9e_de_M._Lloyd_Georges_aux_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9s_de_M._Pacta_et_de_M._Schlanger_-_btv1b9038062q.jpg","title":"Lloyd George arriving at Genoa, April 1922","desc":"The British prime minister, hat in hand at the centre, arriving with other delegates past a line of Italian police. The library dates the press print to 9 April 1922.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, Bibliotheque nationale de France, Meurisse 96567. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conf%C3%A9rence_de_G%C3%AAnes_-_arriv%C3%A9e_de_M._Lloyd_Georges_aux_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9s_de_M._Pacta_et_de_M._Schlanger_-_btv1b9038062q.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of about twenty men in dark overcoats standing in a hall, several holding hats, with uniformed policemen in helmets ranged along the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Soviet Russia outside the trading system at all?","a":"Because the Bolshevik government had repudiated the Tsar’s debts, ignored existing treaties and seized foreign property, and the Allies had answered the revolution with troops."},{"q":"Why did the two outcasts deal with each other?","a":"Because the German delegation feared being shut out of the important business, and that an Allied agreement with Russia would leave Germany in the cold."},{"q":"Why did the agreement alarm the Allied powers?","a":"Because they feared for the whole Versailles order, above all for the existence of Poland, which lay between the two states that had just settled with each other."}],"wiki":"Genoa Economic and Financial Conference (1922)"},
    "ww2-025": {"questions":["The Allied powers feared the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed to another partition of Poland, and the German minister who signed it was murdered by nationalist officers 10 weeks later.","Believing they were being shut out of the conference down the coast, the German delegates negotiated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> separately with Soviet Russia in a nearby town."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Rapallo</b> was the agreement of 16 April 1922 by which Germany and Soviet Russia restored diplomatic relations, the two most isolated states in Europe recognising one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was signed at Rapallo, about 30 kilometres (20 miles) from Genoa, while the great economic conference there was still sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each side gave up its claims on the other: both renounced the costs and damages of the war, and Germany dropped its claim to German property the Soviet state had nationalised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That disposed of Article 116 of the Treaty of Versailles, under which Russia would have been entitled to German reparations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German foreign minister Walther Rathenau signed for Germany and Georgy Chicherin for Russia, and the treaty also provided for trade between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Germany’s motive was fear as much as ambition, its delegates at Genoa believing they were being shut out of the main business and that any Allied agreement with Russia would leave Germany out in the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Allied powers were surprised and angry, and feared for the Versailles order itself, above all that the two might arrange another partition of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The leadership of the Reichswehr under Hans von Seeckt did want joint action against Poland to recover the eastern frontier of 1914, but no such alliance followed from the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rathenau thought it necessary and knew what it would cost, writing days later that it could not have been done without inflicting wounds and that the storm was not yet over; ultra-nationalist officers murdered him on 24 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The line held all the same, and after Locarno unsettled Moscow the Berlin Treaty of April 1926 promised each side neutrality if the other were attacked or sanctioned by a third power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"German-Russian Treaty of Rapallo (April 16, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/german-russian-treaty-of-rapallo-april-16-1922. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Vertrag von Rapallo,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/vertrag-von-rapallo. [Open access]","\"German Cabinet Meeting on Diplomatic Effects of the Treaty of Rapallo (April 18, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/german-cabinet-meeting-on-diplomatic-effects-of-the-treaty-of-rapallo-april-18-1922. [Open access]","\"Foreign Minister Walter Rathenau after Signing the Treaty of Rapallo (April 16, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/foreign-minister-walter-rathenau-after-signing-the-treaty-of-rapallo-april-16-1922. [Open access]","\"Friendship Treaty between Germany and the Soviet Union (Berlin Treaty) (April 24, 1926),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/friendship-treaty-between-germany-and-the-soviet-union-berlin-treaty-april-24-1926. [Open access]","\"German-Russian Agreement; April 16, 1922 (Treaty of Rapallo),\" <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/rapallo_001.asp. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Joseph_Wirth.jpg/960px-Joseph_Wirth.jpg","title":"German and Soviet negotiators at Rapallo, 1922","desc":"Chancellor Joseph Wirth talking on a sunlit terrace with the Soviet delegates Leonid Krasin, Georgy Chicherin and Adolf Joffe, one of them carrying a briefcase.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Archiv der sozialen Demokratie der Friedrich-Ebert-Stiftung. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Wirth.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of five men in dark suits standing and talking on a paved terrace with a balustrade and palm leaves behind them, a bentwood stool between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did mutual renunciation suit both sides?","a":"Because both dropped the costs and damages of the war and Germany its claim to nationalised property, which also disposed of the article entitling Russia to German reparations."},{"q":"Why did Germany sign at all?","a":"Because its delegates at Genoa believed they were being shut out of the main business and that any Allied agreement with Russia would leave Germany in the cold."},{"q":"Why did the signer expect to pay for it?","a":"Because Rathenau wrote days later that it could not have been done without inflicting wounds and that the storm was not yet over; he was murdered on 24 June."}],"wiki":"Treaty of Rapallo (1922)"},
    "ww2-026": {"questions":["Cruisers were left unlimited by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1921 and 1922, so a new building race began at once and the powers were back at the table within 5 years.","The Nine-Power Treaty signed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised to respect China’s territorial integrity but gave nobody any way to enforce it, as Japan showed in Manchuria."],"abstract":"The <b>Washington Naval Conference</b> was a meeting of nine powers held from 12 November 1921 to 6 February 1922 to limit navies and settle the rivalries of the western Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American secretary of state Charles Evans Hughes invited Britain, Japan, France and Italy to talk about naval reductions, and Belgium, China, Portugal and the Netherlands to talk about the Far East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Five-Power Treaty was the centrepiece, fixing capital-ship tonnage at 500,000 tons each for the United States and Britain, 300,000 for Japan and 175,000 each for France and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had asked for ten to ten to seven and the American navy for ten to ten to five; the conference settled on five to five to three, and required the powers to stop building capital ships and scrap old ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article XIX froze the fortification of Pacific bases, which alarmed American officers who thought it left Hawaii, Guam and the Philippines exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Four-Power Treaty replaced the Anglo-Japanese alliance of 1902 with a promise to consult, removing the risk that Britain would be bound to join Japan against the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nine-Power Treaty made the American Open Door policy international, its signatories promising to respect the territorial integrity of China, but it recognised Japanese dominance in Manchuria and provided no way to enforce anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whole classes of warship had been left unlimited, so a cruiser race began at once and the powers returned to the table in 1927 and 1930, when the limits were extended and set to expire in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The good feeling did not last: the American Immigration Act of 1924 excluded Japanese immigrants, which Japan took as a breach of the Gentlemen’s Agreement and protested against, and relations worsened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japan took Manchuria in 1931 and attacked Shanghai in 1932, and Washington answered that breach of the Nine-Power Treaty by declaring itself no longer bound by the naval limits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","Joseph V. Fuller, ed., <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1922</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1938), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1922v01. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The London Naval Conference, 1930,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/london-naval-conf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Immigration Act of 1924 (The Johnson-Reed Act),\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/immigration-act. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg/1280px-Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg","title":"The signing of the naval treaty, February 1922","desc":"The delegations ranged round a great U-shaped table under the flags of the signatory powers, with spectators watching from the balconies, at the signing in February 1922.","credit":"Unknown photographer. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph looking down on a large hall where about eighty men in dark suits sit and stand around a U-shaped table, national flags and palms along the wall behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the treaty replacing the Anglo-Japanese alliance matter?","a":"Because it removed the risk that Britain would be bound to join Japan against the United States, substituting a promise to consult."},{"q":"Why did a cruiser race begin immediately?","a":"Because whole classes of warship had been left unlimited, so the powers had to return to the table in 1927 and 1930."},{"q":"Why did the Nine-Power Treaty guarantee so little?","a":"Because although its signatories promised to respect China’s territorial integrity, it recognised Japanese dominance in Manchuria and provided no way to enforce anything."}],"wiki":"Washington Naval Conference"},
    "ww2-027": {"questions":["Article 8 of the League Covenant promised to cut arms to the lowest point consistent with safety, a pledge the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent 3 years at Geneva trying to redeem.","Herbert Hoover complained in May 1932 of the ineffectiveness of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose deadlock between French security and German equality outlasted the plan he put forward that June."],"abstract":"The <b>General Disarmament Conference</b>, formally the Conference for the Reduction and Limitation of Armaments, opened at Geneva on 2 February 1932 as the League of Nations’ one attempt to disarm the world by treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its root was Article 8 of the League Covenant, which held that peace required \"the reduction of national armaments to the lowest point consistent with national safety\" and made the League responsible for planning it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Treaty of Versailles pointed the same way: its military clauses opened by saying Germany accepted them \"in order to render possible the initiation of a general limitation of the armaments of all nations\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany read that as a promise, and cited the 100,000-man army the treaty imposed as proof of a bargain the victors had never kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The naval powers had meanwhile tried separately, capping capital ships at Washington in 1922, failing to extend the caps to cruisers at Geneva in 1927, and patching the gap at London in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The 1927 talks collapsed over how cruisers should be counted, after which the United States authorised fifteen new cruisers and a carrier, joining the arms race it had met to stop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The general conference stalled between French demands for security and German demands for equality, and in May 1932 Herbert Hoover told his cabinet of \"the ineffectiveness of accomplishment at the disarmament Conference\", the world then spending five billion dollars a year on arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His plan of 22 June 1932 revived them briefly, and Germany, which had left, returned that December on a five-power declaration granting equality of rights within a system providing security for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The concession bought 10 months: on 14 October 1933 Germany withdrew from both the conference and the League, and the powers spent 1934 bargaining in parallel rather than together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Japan denounced the Washington treaty that year, the naval ceilings of 1922 and 1930 were due to expire in 1936, and the conference’s last work was an American-sponsored treaty on the arms trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Gustave A. Nuermberger et al., eds., <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1932, General, Volume I</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1932v01. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" article 8, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","\"The Versailles Treaty, June 28, 1919: Part V, Military, Naval and Air Clauses,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partv.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Geneva Naval Conference, 1927,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/geneva. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The London Naval Conference, 1930,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/london-naval-conf. [Open access]","\"President Hoover to the Secretary of State, Washington, May 24, 1932,\" document 121 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1932, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1932v01/d121. [Open access]","\"The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great Britain, Washington, October 14, 1933,\" document 205 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1933, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933v01/d205. [Open access]","Rogers P. Churchill et al., eds., <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1934, General, Volume I</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1934v01. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Gen%C3%A8ve%2C_2-2-32%2C_ouverture_de_la_conf%C3%A9rence_du_d%C3%A9sarmement_-_btv1b532520413.jpg/960px-Gen%C3%A8ve%2C_2-2-32%2C_ouverture_de_la_conf%C3%A9rence_du_d%C3%A9sarmement_-_btv1b532520413.jpg","title":"The opening session at Geneva, 2 February 1932","desc":"Delegates fill the hall of the Batiment electoral in Geneva on the opening day of the Conference for the Reduction and Limitation of Armaments. A press photograph by the Agence Rol, held by the Bibliotheque nationale de France.","credit":"Agence Rol, via Gallica and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gen%C3%A8ve%2C_2-2-32%2C_ouverture_de_la_conf%C3%A9rence_du_d%C3%A9sarmement_-_btv1b532520413.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a crowded assembly hall, delegates in dark suits seated at tiered desks covered with papers, with a packed public gallery above."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany treat its own disarmament as a bargain?","a":"Because the military clauses of Versailles opened by saying Germany accepted them to render possible a general limitation of the armaments of all nations."},{"q":"Why did the 1927 naval talks make matters worse?","a":"Because they collapsed over how cruisers should be counted, after which the United States authorised fifteen new cruisers and a carrier, joining the race it had met to stop."},{"q":"Why did the concession of equality buy so little?","a":"Because it bought 10 months: Germany returned in December 1932 on a five-power declaration and withdrew from both the conference and the League on 14 October 1933."}],"wiki":"Conference for the Reduction and Limitation of Armaments"},
    "ww2-028": {"questions":["Real wages had fallen and the cost of living stood at 268 against 100 in 1913 when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried Italian workers into the streets and onto the land.","Giovanni Giolitti’s refusal to clear the occupied steelworks by force in September 1920 came at the height of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cost the liberal state the confidence of property."],"abstract":"The <b>Biennio Rosso</b>, ‘the red two years’ of 1919 and 1920, was the wave of strikes, land seizures and factory occupations that swept Italy after the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy had entered that war on 24 May 1915, 10 months after it began, against the wishes of the Socialist Party and much of the Catholic world, so the country fought already divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cost was severe: excess deaths between 1915 and 1918 reached about 1,250,000, of which some 400,000 came from the fighting itself, and a tenth of the men mobilised died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state paid by borrowing and by printing, and paper money in circulation rose from 2,783 million lire in 1913 to 18,552 million in 1919, with the cost-of-living index at 268 against 100 in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wartime contracts had meanwhile built a small, tightly concentrated industrial bloc around FIAT, Ansaldo, Ilva and Montecatini, while real wages fell for labourers and clerks alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When peace came, protest resumed where the war had interrupted it, in forms the conflict itself had changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second half of 1919 brought an unprecedented wave of rural strikes and land occupations, the summer had already seen violent protests in the cities against the cost of living, and the spring of 1920 brought mass industrial strikes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1920 steelworkers occupied the factories, an act whose symbolic weight exceeded anything it won, and Giovanni Giolitti refused the loud demands that he restore the owners’ property by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The political ground had already moved, the 1919 election being the first fought under proportional representation and the parliament it returned sitting from 1 December 1919 to 7 April 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The liberal leaders would deal with neither of the two new mass parties, the Socialists and the Popular Party founded in January 1919, and the paralysis that followed opened the way for Mussolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","Elena Papadia, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10896. [Open access]","Pierluigi Scolè, \"War Losses (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2025), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11650/1.1. [Open access]","Fabio Degli Esposti, \"War Finance (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10722. [Open access]","Roberto Bianchi, \"Social Conflict and Control, Protest and Repression (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10367. [Open access]","Camera dei deputati, \"XXV Legislatura del Regno d’Italia,\" <i>Portale storico della Camera dei deputati</i>, https://storia.camera.it/legislature/leg-regno-XXV. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Biennio_Rosso.jpg/960px-Biennio_Rosso.jpg","title":"Workers holding an occupied factory, 1920","desc":"Armed workers photographed behind a hammer-and-sickle banner at a factory they had taken over during the occupations of 1920. The photographer is unrecorded; the print reproduced here is the one held by Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Unknown photographer, 1920, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biennio_Rosso.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of about twenty men in caps and hats, several holding rifles, standing and sitting behind a large banner painted with a hammer and sickle inside a wreath."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Italy come out of the war already divided?","a":"Because it had entered 10 months late against the wishes of the Socialist Party and much of the Catholic world, so the intervention itself was contested."},{"q":"Why did prices matter as much as politics?","a":"Because paper money rose from 2,783 million lire in 1913 to 18,552 million in 1919, with the cost-of-living index at 268, while real wages fell for labourers and clerks alike."},{"q":"Why did the factory occupations settle nothing?","a":"Because their symbolic weight exceeded anything they won, and Giolitti refused the demands that he restore the owners’ property by force."}],"wiki":"Biennio Rosso"},
    "ww2-029": {"questions":["The Pact of London had promised Italy the Alps, Trieste and Dalmatia but left Fiume to Croatia, and the cry of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned that gap into a national grievance.","When Orlando left the Council of Four in April 1919 and did not sit again until 7 May, the phrase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was already at work on what Italy felt it had been denied."],"abstract":"The <b>mutilated victory</b> was the nationalist charge that Italy, having won the war at enormous cost, had been cheated of its reward at the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Paris_Peace_Conference_(1919–1920)\">Paris Peace Conference</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy had joined the Allies in 1915 on written conditions: the frontier of the Alps, the ports of Trieste and Pola, and much of the Dalmatian coast with many of its islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That bargain, the Pact of London, would move over 200,000 German-speaking Tyrolese and 750,000 Southern Slavs under Italian rule, and it left Fiume to Croatia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States, which had entered as an Associated Power, held that it was not bound by agreements the Allies made before it joined, and Woodrow Wilson opposed the Italian claims on the Adriatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy was meanwhile asking more than the Pact had promised, its proposals of 23 April 1919 demanding Fiume under Italian sovereignty as well as the islands and a mandate over Zara and Sebenico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That day Wilson went over the Italian government’s head with a public appeal to the Italian people, defending it to the other leaders as a friendly message setting out the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Clemenceau and Lloyd George answered Orlando with a memorandum asking what valid reason there was for adding \"this little city on the Croatian coast\" to Italy \"in the teeth of the Treaty\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Italian delegation then left: Orlando sat with the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Paris_Peace_Conference_(1919–1920)\">Council of Four</span> on 24 April and not again until 7 May, by which time the German treaty was almost done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Neither the demand that the Pact of London be honoured in full nor the claim to Fiume had much hope of being heard, and the expected scaling down of Italian claims let the radical right foster a devastating sense of victimhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gabriele d’Annunzio seized Fiume with several thousand volunteers in September 1919 and was forced out only in December 1920, and the agitation over the Adriatic gave militants a way to imagine an answer from outside parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","\"Fiume and the Peace Settlement,\" appendix I to \"Notes of a Meeting Held at Mr. Lloyd George’s Residence, Paris, April 24, 1919, 4 p.m.,\" document 19 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d19. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]","\"Notes of a Meeting Held at President Wilson’s House, Paris, April 23, 1919, 11 a.m.,\" document 16 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d16. [Open access]","\"Notes of a Meeting Held at President Wilson’s House, Paris, April 24, 1919, 11 a.m.,\" document 18 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d18. [Open access]","\"Notes of a Meeting Held at President Wilson’s House, Paris, May 7, 1919, 11 a.m.,\" document 48 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d48. [Open access]","Vanda Wilcox, \"D’Annunzio, Gabriele,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11260. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Council_of_Four_of_the_Peace_Conference._Mr._Lloyd_George%2C_Signor_Orlando%2C_M._Clemenceau%2C_President_Woodrow_Wilson...._-_NARA_-_530791.tif/1280px-Council_of_Four_of_the_Peace_Conference._Mr._Lloyd_George%2C_Signor_Orlando%2C_M._Clemenceau%2C_President_Woodrow_Wilson...._-_NARA_-_530791.tif.jpg","title":"The Council of Four in Paris, 27 May 1919","desc":"Lloyd George and Orlando talking on the left, Clemenceau and Wilson on the right, outside the Hotel Crillon. Orlando had walked out of the conference in late April and returned on 7 May. A United States Signal Corps photograph held by the National Archives as record 530791.","credit":"U.S. Signal Corps, National Archives and Records Administration 530791, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_of_Four_of_the_Peace_Conference._Mr._Lloyd_George%2C_Signor_Orlando%2C_M._Clemenceau%2C_President_Woodrow_Wilson...._-_NARA_-_530791.tif","alt":"Black and white photograph of four elderly men in dark overcoats on the steps of a building, two of them in conversation on the left and two standing apart on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States hold itself free of the Pact of London?","a":"Because it had entered as an Associated Power and held that it was not bound by agreements the Allies made before it joined."},{"q":"Why did the Italian claim weaken as it grew?","a":"Because Italy asked more than the Pact had promised, demanding Fiume under Italian sovereignty as well, which the Pact had left to Croatia."},{"q":"Why did the disappointment matter more than the terms?","a":"Because the expected scaling down of Italian claims let the radical right foster a devastating sense of victimhood, and the seizure of Fiume showed an answer from outside parliament."}],"wiki":"Mutilated victory"},
    "ww2-030": {"questions":["Article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles settled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Japan’s favour, handing over the Tsingtao railway and the German rights in a Chinese province.","Secret wartime promises from Britain, France, Italy and Russia, bought with Japanese naval help in the Mediterranean, had largely decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the conference opened."],"abstract":"The <b>Shandong question</b>, written Shantung in the diplomacy of the day, was the argument at the Paris Peace Conference over what should become of Germany’s holdings in the Chinese province of Shandong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had joined the Allies in 1914, taken the German leasehold at Kiaochow, and then carried supplies, warships and loans for them while its own economy boomed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It pressed China directly as well: a treaty of 1915 arising from the Twenty-One Demands gave Japan 99-year leases in south Manchuria, and a second bound China to accept whatever Japan settled with Germany over Shandong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese officials and civilians moved into the province’s railways, administration and industries, and the Japanese population of its capital, Jinan, rose from 450 before the war to 25,000 by its end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, France, Italy and Russia had meanwhile promised in secret wartime treaties to back Japan’s claims there in return for Japanese naval help in the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Washington saw the danger early, a telegram of January 1919 warning that everything the conference might do for China would be futile if Japan’s special right in Shandong were acknowledged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States had entered the war as an Associated Power and held itself unbound by agreements the Allies made before it joined, but those agreements still bound the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles duly made Germany renounce, in favour of Japan, its rights and privileges in Kiaochow and Shandong, the Tsingtao-Tsinanfu railway among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan left Paris one of the Big Five, a founding member of the League of Nations with one of the four permanent seats on its governing body, and mandatory, under the class C terms of the Covenant, for the German island groups of the western Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In China the news that Jiaozhou would not be given back brought students into the streets in the May Fourth Movement, and Japan restored Chinese sovereignty there only in 1922, after the Washington Conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Rustin Gates, \"War Aims and War Aims Discussions (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11442. [Open access]","\"The Acting Secretary of State to the Commission to Negotiate Peace,\" received January 5, 1919, document 439 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv02/d439. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]","\"The Versailles Treaty, June 28, 1919: Part IV, German Rights and Interests Outside Germany,\" article 156, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiv.asp. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" article 22, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Japanese_delegation_at_the_Paris_Peace_Conference_1919.jpg/960px-Japanese_delegation_at_the_Paris_Peace_Conference_1919.jpg","title":"The Japanese delegation at Paris, 1919","desc":"Plenipotentiaries, secretaries and military officers of the Japanese delegation to the peace conference, photographed in a courtyard. The print was copied at the Japanese Foreign Ministry archives; the photographer of the 1919 original is unrecorded.","credit":"Unknown photographer, 1919, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_delegation_at_the_Paris_Peace_Conference_1919.jpg","alt":"Black and white group photograph of about sixty men in three rows outside a building, most in dark suits and several in military uniform, the front row seated."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the conference not simply return the province to China?","a":"Because Britain, France, Italy and Russia had promised in secret wartime treaties to back Japan’s claims there in return for Japanese naval help in the Mediterranean."},{"q":"Why had China’s own position been undermined in advance?","a":"Because a treaty of 1915 arising from the Twenty-One Demands bound China to accept whatever Japan settled with Germany over Shandong."},{"q":"Why did the decision matter inside China?","a":"Because the news that Jiaozhou would not be given back brought students into the streets in the May Fourth Movement."}]},
    "ww2-031": {"questions":["Baron Makino told the plenary session of April 1919 of his government’s poignant regret when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was left out of the Covenant, and promised Japan would press the principle again.","Making no distinction in law or in fact on account of race or nationality was the wording of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the League of Nations Commission declined twice in 1919."],"abstract":"The <b>racial equality proposal</b> was Japan’s attempt at the Paris Peace Conference to write a principle of equal treatment for the nationals of member states into the Covenant of the League of Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its original form, read into the record by Baron Makino Nobuaki, had the parties agree to accord all aliens from member states \"equal and just treatment in every respect, making no distinction, either in law or in fact, on account of their race or nationality\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Makino argued that a man asked to defend the League with his life would naturally demand to stand on an equal footing with the people he was defending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League of Nations Commission did not adopt it, and when the draft Covenant went to a plenary session on 14 February 1919 without it, Japan reserved the right to raise the question again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had reason to expect a hearing, being one of the Big Five at Paris, a founder of the League and one of four powers holding a permanent seat on its governing body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 11 April Makino tried a weaker version, a phrase in the Preamble endorsing the equality of nations and the just treatment of their nationals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It won what he called a clear majority of the commission and was still ruled not adopted, for want of unanimity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 8 days later the American commissioners in Paris discussed a British-drafted resolution meant to satisfy the Japanese claim \"without exactly stating that recognition in so many words\", and the American Secretary of State doubted it should be inserted at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the plenary of 28 April Makino read the original amendment back into the record and declared his government’s \"poignant regret\", saying Japan would go on pressing the principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Covenant went out with no such clause, and in 1924 the United States barred Japanese immigration outright, breaking the Gentlemen’s Agreement of 1907 and giving great offence in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Preliminary Peace Conference, Protocol No. 5, Plenary Session of April 28, 1919,\" document 7 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d7. [Open access]","Rustin Gates, \"War Aims and War Aims Discussions (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11442. [Open access]","\"Minutes of the Daily Meetings of the Commissioners Plenipotentiary, Saturday, April 19th, 1919,\" document 53 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume XI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv11/d53. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Immigration Act of 1924 (The Johnson-Reed Act),\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/immigration-act. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Japanese_peace_delegates_in_1919_with_Makino_Nobuaki.jpg/960px-Japanese_peace_delegates_in_1919_with_Makino_Nobuaki.jpg","title":"The Japanese plenipotentiaries at Paris, 1919","desc":"Seated, from the left: Baron Makino Nobuaki, who put the amendment; Marquis Saionji Kinmochi; and Viscount Chinda Sutemi. Standing behind them are Ijuin Hikokichi and Matsui Keishiro, the ambassadors to Italy and France.","credit":"Bain News Service, via the Library of Congress and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_peace_delegates_in_1919_with_Makino_Nobuaki.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of five men in formal dress in a panelled room, three seated on a sofa and two standing behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan connect the principle to military service?","a":"Because Makino argued that a man asked to defend the League with his life would naturally demand to stand on an equal footing with the people he was defending."},{"q":"Why did a proposal with a clear majority fail?","a":"Because it was ruled not adopted for want of unanimity, even in the weaker form that asked only for a phrase in the Preamble."},{"q":"Why did the refusal have consequences beyond Paris?","a":"Because in 1924 the United States barred Japanese immigration outright, breaking the Gentlemen’s Agreement of 1907 and giving great offence in Japan."}],"wiki":"Racial Equality Proposal"},
    "ww2-032": {"questions":["Britain left the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1931 and the United States in 1933, and by the middle of that year every European debtor but Finland had defaulted on its American loans.","Fixing a currency’s value in gold and letting money move at settled rates, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the frame in which reparations, war debts and American loans all had to circulate."],"abstract":"The <b>gold standard</b> fixed a currency’s value in gold and let money move between countries at settled rates, and rebuilding it was the central project of the interwar international economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system had been suspended to pay for the First World War, and the United States, the European states and Japan spent the 1920s working to restore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it had to carry was new: Germany owed reparations set in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks, about $31.5 billion, and the Allies owed the United States more than $10 billion lent during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington refused to cancel those debts or to let them be linked to reparations, and in 1922 created a commission to negotiate repayment with the seventeen countries that had borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain answered the same year with the Balfour Note, saying it would seek from its allies and from Germany only as much as it owed the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Dawes Plan of 1924 turned the tangle into a circle: American banks lent Germany enough to pay reparations to France and Britain, which used the money to service their war debts to America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing in that circle earned dollars, and American tariffs saw to it that little else did, the Fordney-McCumber Act of 1922 raising rates and making it harder for Europeans to export to the United States and so earn what they owed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German cabinet, preparing for the Genoa conference in April 1922, was already discussing debt relief, a working community of central banks and a transition to the gold standard as the way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When American lending dried up after 1928 the circle stopped: Hoover obtained a one-year moratorium on all debt and reparation payments in 1931, and at Lausanne in 1932 the European powers agreed to cancel their claims on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain left the gold standard in 1931 and the United States in 1933, and by the middle of that year every European debtor except Finland had defaulted on its American loans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]","\"Cabinet Consultation with the President Regarding the Agenda for Genoa (April 5, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/cabinet-consultation-with-the-president-regarding-the-agenda-for-genoa-april-5-1922. [Open access]","Gustave A. Nuermberger et al., eds., <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1932, General, Volume I</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1932v01. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Lausanne%2C_16-6-1932_%28conf%C3%A9rence%2C_M._Herriot_au_centre%29_-_btv1b53256433g.jpg/960px-Lausanne%2C_16-6-1932_%28conf%C3%A9rence%2C_M._Herriot_au_centre%29_-_btv1b53256433g.jpg","title":"Edouard Herriot at Lausanne, 16 June 1932","desc":"The French prime minister, centre, among delegates and reporters on the opening day of the Lausanne conference, at which the European powers agreed to give up their reparation claims on Germany. A press photograph by the Agence Rol, held by the Bibliotheque nationale de France.","credit":"Agence Rol, via Gallica and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lausanne%2C_16-6-1932_%28conf%C3%A9rence%2C_M._Herriot_au_centre%29_-_btv1b53256433g.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a crowd of men in suits outdoors, one in the middle holding a cigarette and looking towards the camera, roofs and a tower behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the payments form a circle rather than a flow?","a":"Because American banks lent Germany enough to pay reparations to France and Britain, which used the money to service their war debts to America."},{"q":"Why could nothing in that circle earn dollars?","a":"Because American tariffs saw to it that little else did, the Fordney-McCumber Act raising rates and making it harder for Europeans to export to the United States."},{"q":"Why did the system stop when American lending dried up?","a":"Because the circle depended on new loans: within 3 years there was a moratorium on all payments, the European powers cancelled their claims on Germany, and every debtor but Finland defaulted."}],"wiki":"Gold standard"},
    "ww2-033": {"questions":["Buying shares on a tenth down with the stock as collateral was ordinary practice before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why so many small holdings vanished when prices fell.","The Federal Reserve Bank of New York bought securities and lent freely through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and was accused by the Board of exceeding its authority for doing so."],"abstract":"The <b>Wall Street crash</b> of October 1929 ended a bull market in which the Dow Jones Industrial Average had risen six-fold since 1921, to a peak of 381.17 on 3 September 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brokerage houses, investment trusts and margin accounts had let ordinary people buy shares on credit, putting down a tenth of the price and pledging the stock itself as collateral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Federal Reserve was sceptical, holding that its founding act did not contemplate using its resources to extend speculative credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How to act divided it: <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Federal_Reserve_Board\">the Board</span> wanted reserve banks to refuse credit to members lending to speculators, while <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"George_L._Harrison\">George Harrison</span> in <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"New_York_City\">New York</span> wanted a higher discount rate, and got 6% in August 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because currencies were tied to gold, that tightening pushed foreign central banks to raise their own rates, and the international economy slowed before Wall Street did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> American money had been holding Europe up too, propping the German economy with bank loans until 1928, and when the lending stopped Germany foundered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bankers tried to steady the market in October by buying blocks of shares at high prices in public, and failed; on <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Black_Monday_(1929)\">Black Monday</span>, 28 October, the Dow fell nearly 13%, on Black Tuesday nearly 12 more, and by mid-November it had lost almost half its value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Federal Reserve Bank of New York bought government securities, lent freely and cut its discount rate, which other reserve banks thought beyond its authority and which is now credited with keeping the city’s banks open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crash did not make the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Great_Depression\">Depression</span>: its effect faded within months and by the autumn of 1930 recovery looked close, and it was the banking panics of 1930 and 1931, and the crises after them, that turned a sharp recession into a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tariffs rose as the slump deepened, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Herbert_Hoover\">Hoover</span> having pledged farm protection in 1928 and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States_Congress\">Congress</span> passing the Tariff Act of 1930; the Dow closed at 41.22 on 8 July 1932, 89% below its peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gary Richardson, Alejandro Komai, Michael Gou and Daniel Park, \"Stock Market Crash of 1929,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1929. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","Gary Richardson, \"The Great Depression,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Crowds_gathering_outside_New_York_Stock_Exchange.jpg/960px-Crowds_gathering_outside_New_York_Stock_Exchange.jpg","title":"Wall Street, 24 October 1929","desc":"The street outside the New York Stock Exchange filled with people during the heavy trading of 24 October, four days before the worst fall. A policeman stands on the steps at the right; Trinity Church closes the view.","credit":"Associated Press, 24 October 1929, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain in the United States. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowds_gathering_outside_New_York_Stock_Exchange.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph looking down a narrow city street packed with hundreds of men in hats, tall stone buildings on both sides and a church spire in the distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why did buying on margin make the fall so steep?","a":"Because ordinary people had bought shares on credit, putting down a tenth of the price and pledging the stock itself as collateral, so falling prices called in the loans."},{"q":"Why did an American rate rise slow the world economy?","a":"Because currencies were tied to gold, so the tightening pushed foreign central banks to raise their own rates, and the international economy slowed before Wall Street did."},{"q":"Why is the crash not the cause of the Depression?","a":"Because its effect faded within months and by the autumn of 1930 recovery looked close; it was the banking panics of 1930 and 1931 that turned a sharp recession into a decade."}],"wiki":"Wall Street crash of 1929"},
    "ww2-034": {"questions":["Deflation was as much a part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as unemployment, the American money supply falling by nearly 30% between 1930 and 1933 and average prices falling with it.","Leaving the gold standard, as Britain did in 1931 and the United States in 1933, was one of the retreats by which governments tried to escape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its deflation."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Depression</b> was the longest and deepest downturn in the history of the United States and of the modern industrial economy, beginning in August 1929 and ending during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not one event but a series: a stock market crash in 1929, regional banking panics in 1930 and 1931, and national and international financial crises from 1931 to 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The worst of it was monetary, the American <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Money_supply\">money supply</span> falling by nearly 30% between the autumn of 1930 and the winter of 1933, with average prices falling by as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That deflation raised the real weight of every debt, distorted decisions, cut consumption, drove unemployment up and pushed banks, firms and households into bankruptcy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Federal Reserve did not stop it, partly because its leaders disagreed: some held that a central bank should lend to solvent banks under run, others that lending should contract when the economy did, and a few that troubled institutions should be left to fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Defending the gold standard argued for the same tightening, and because currencies were tied to gold, American rate rises pushed recessions onto other countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain left the standard in 1931 and the United States in 1933, and the London Economic Conference of that year broke up without agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trade was strangled too: the Tariff Act of 1930 entrenched American protection, and world trade fell by about two-thirds between 1929 and 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American lending abroad had already stopped, and by the middle of 1933 every European debtor except Finland had defaulted on its war loans to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bottom came in March 1933, when the American banking system collapsed and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Franklin_D._Roosevelt\">Roosevelt</span> declared a national banking holiday; reform followed, then a second recession in 1937, and full recovery only with the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gary Richardson, \"The Great Depression,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","Gary Richardson, Alejandro Komai, Michael Gou and Daniel Park, \"Stock Market Crash of 1929,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1929. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Unemployed_men_queued_outside_a_depression_soup_kitchen_opened_in_Chicago_by_Al_Capone%2C_02-1931_-_NARA_-_541927.jpg/960px-Unemployed_men_queued_outside_a_depression_soup_kitchen_opened_in_Chicago_by_Al_Capone%2C_02-1931_-_NARA_-_541927.jpg","title":"A soup kitchen queue in Chicago, February 1931","desc":"Men waiting on a wet pavement under a sign reading Free Soup Coffee and Doughnuts for the Unemployed. The kitchen was opened by Al Capone. Held by the National Archives as record 541927.","credit":"Unknown photographer, February 1931, National Archives and Records Administration 541927, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unemployed_men_queued_outside_a_depression_soup_kitchen_opened_in_Chicago_by_Al_Capone%2C_02-1931_-_NARA_-_541927.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a long queue of men in overcoats and caps on a wet city pavement beside a storefront whose sign offers free soup, coffee and doughnuts for the unemployed."},"why":[{"q":"Why did falling prices make the slump worse?","a":"Because deflation raised the real weight of every debt, cut consumption and pushed banks, firms and households into bankruptcy as the money supply fell by nearly 30%."},{"q":"Why did the Federal Reserve not stop it?","a":"Because its leaders disagreed: some held a central bank should lend to solvent banks under run, others that lending should contract with the economy, and a few that troubled institutions should fail."},{"q":"Why did defending gold deepen the trouble abroad?","a":"Because the standard argued for the same tightening, and with currencies tied to gold American rate rises pushed recessions onto other countries."}],"wiki":"Great Depression"},
    "ww2-035": {"questions":["When the last majority coalition fell in March 1930 over half a percentage point of unemployment insurance, government under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the ordinary way Germany was run.","Brüning imposed his austerity against the will of the parliamentary majority through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Hindenburg saw Hitler’s appointment as a continuation of the same policy."],"abstract":"<b>Article 48</b> of the Weimar constitution let the president govern by emergency decree, and after 1930 that became the ordinary way Germany was run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 27 March 1930 the coalition led by the Social Democrat Hermann Müller broke up over whether to raise unemployment-insurance contributions by half a percentage point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parties could not agree even in the face of a world economic crisis that had begun in the United States and spread around the globe, and President Paul von Hindenburg refused Müller the use of the article.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since late 1929 Hindenburg and his advisers had been planning a government that would rest on his confidence rather than on a majority in parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heinrich Brüning of the Centre Party had agreed to lead such a cabinet before Müller fell, was appointed chancellor on 29 March, and power began to shift from the Reichstag to the president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Brüning drove an austerity programme through by emergency decree against the will of the parliamentary majority, raising taxes and cutting spending, salaries, pensions and insurance benefits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The crisis it answered was severe: protectionism spread on both sides of the Atlantic, German exports fell from 13.5 to 5.7 billion Reichsmarks, industrial production by about 40%, and unemployment rose from 1.3 million to more than six million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Real incomes fell by a third, and the economic crisis became a crisis of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The National Socialists and the Communists gained at the Reichstag elections of September 1930 and July 1932 while the liberal Democratic Party sank into insignificance, and Brüning’s successors Franz von Papen and Kurt von Schleicher had less parliamentary support than he had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hindenburg understood the appointment of Adolf Hitler as chancellor in 1933 as a continuation of that policy of presidential cabinets, and across the Atlantic the Depression was driving the United States further into isolation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Rebekka von Mallinckrodt, \"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette. [Open access]","Oliver Schweinoch and Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Weltwirtschaftskrise,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/industrie/wirtschaftskrise. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","Gary Richardson, \"The Great Depression,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Erich_Salomon_Kabinett_Br%C3%BCning_1930.jpg/960px-Erich_Salomon_Kabinett_Br%C3%BCning_1930.jpg","title":"The Bruning cabinet meeting, 1930","desc":"Ministers of the first Bruning cabinet at a table out of doors, photographed candidly by Erich Salomon. Commons names Kurt Wachsmann, Erich Zweigert, Martin Schiele, Joseph Wirth, Hermann Dietrich, Heinrich Bruning and Hermann Puender among the sitters.","credit":"Erich Salomon, 1930, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erich_Salomon_Kabinett_Br%C3%BCning_1930.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of about ten men in suits seated around a long table on grass under trees, several reading papers, white garden chairs in the foreground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a coalition break over half a percentage point?","a":"Because the parties could not agree on unemployment-insurance contributions even in the face of a world economic crisis, and the president refused the chancellor the emergency article."},{"q":"Why was the refusal not simply a constitutional scruple?","a":"Because since late 1929 Hindenburg and his advisers had been planning a government resting on his confidence rather than on a majority in parliament."},{"q":"Why did governing by decree shift power permanently?","a":"Because Brüning drove austerity through against the will of the parliamentary majority, and his successors had less parliamentary support than he had."}],"wiki":"Article 48 of the Weimar Constitution"},
    "ww2-036": {"questions":["A doctrine published under Mussolini’s name held the state to be an absolute beside which individuals count only in relation to it, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> describing itself.","Unlike Nazism, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Italy did not make antisemitism central until 1938, when laws against Jews were passed partly under pressure from Germany."],"abstract":"<b>Fascism</b> was the movement that took power in Italy in 1922 and gave its name to a family of authoritarian nationalist movements between the wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It grew out of the First World War, during which ideas from right and left, corporatist and syndicalist alike, mixed into a new political culture called national radicalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That culture fed fascism, while the wartime quarrel between neutralists and interventionists over joining the war grew more violent after 1918 and kept the divided liberals from coming together again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gabriele d’Annunzio’s seizure of Fiume in September 1919 and the proto-fascist regency he proclaimed there supplied much of the movement’s style before Mussolini held power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In October 1922 King Victor Emmanuel III appointed Mussolini prime minister, and over the next 7 years the Fascists built a one-party dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It was never quite absolute: the monarchy stayed independent and remained in theory commander-in-chief, and Mussolini’s leadership was nominally subject to the party's supreme body, the Gran Consiglio del Fascismo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In its own account, published under Mussolini’s name, fascism \"conceives of the State as an absolute, in comparison with which all individuals or groups are relative\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same text rejects pacifism as cowardice, holds that war alone brings human energy to its highest tension, and calls democracy a regime that gives people the illusion of sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It announced the twentieth century as \"a century of authority\", against the individualism it identified with liberalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antisemitism was not central to Italian fascism as it was to Nazism: Jews could join the party until 1938, and the antisemitic laws of that year came partly under pressure from Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","Vanda Wilcox, \"D’Annunzio, Gabriele,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11260. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","Elena Papadia, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10896. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/MVSN_in_piazza_di_Siena.jpg/1280px-MVSN_in_piazza_di_Siena.jpg","title":"Fascist militia in the piazza di Siena, Rome, 1936","desc":"Ranks of the Voluntary Militia for National Security drawn up in the Villa Borghese, photographed for L'Illustrazione Italiana of 15 March 1936.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, L'Illustrazione Italiana, 15 March 1936. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MVSN_in_piazza_di_Siena.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of several hundred uniformed militiamen standing in ranks on grass among umbrella pines, most with the right arm raised in salute, while a small group of officers walks along the front of the line."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war leave the ground it grew on?","a":"Because the wartime split between neutralists and interventionists persisted afterwards in a more violent form, keeping the fragmented liberal camp from reassembling."},{"q":"Why was the dictatorship never quite absolute?","a":"Because the monarchy stayed independent and remained in theory commander-in-chief, and the leadership was nominally subject to the party’s supreme body."},{"q":"Why is Italian fascism distinguished from Nazism on antisemitism?","a":"Because Jews could join the party until 1938, and the antisemitic laws of that year came partly under pressure from Nazi Germany."}],"wiki":"Fascism"},
    "ww2-037": {"questions":["The Acerbo Law and a decree of December 1925 turned the government <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> headed into a dictatorship answerable to the king alone rather than to the Italian parliament.","Dismissed by the king in July 1943 and shot by partisans 2 years later, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had governed Italy for 21 years and taken it into the war beside Germany."],"abstract":"<b>Benito Mussolini</b> founded the Italian Fascist movement and governed Italy from 1922 until 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born in 1883 at Dovia di Predappio to a blacksmith who named him after the Mexican revolutionary Benito Juárez, and he began his political life as a socialist, editing the party newspaper <i>Avanti!</i> from 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war broke him from the socialists: after an editorial demanding Italian intervention he resigned the paper, and when his own interventionist <i>Il Popolo d’Italia</i> appeared the party expelled him, its funding by industrialists and the French concealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He served 17 months with the Bersaglieri until a training explosion in February 1917 had him evacuated, and he returned to his paper demanding war until victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement he inaugurated in March 1919 won only 4,800 votes that November and revived when the strikes of the <i>biennio rosso</i> made its squads useful to the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its style came out of the war, the squads taking over the symbols and practices of the shock troops and demanding submission to a charismatic leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The king, looking to him for order and stabilisation, appointed him prime minister on 30 October 1922 after some 30,000 Fascists converged on Rome while he waited in Milan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Acerbo Law of 1923 handed the largest party two-thirds of the seats, and a decree of December 1925 made the head of government answerable to the king alone rather than to parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He signed the Pact of Steel with Hitler in May 1939 and took Italy into the war in June 1940; defeat in North Africa and the landing in Sicily brought a vote of no confidence on 25 July 1943, and the king dismissed and arrested him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German paratroopers freed him to head a puppet Italian state at Salò, and in April 1945 communist partisans caught him near the Swiss border and shot him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Benito Mussolini,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/benito-mussolini-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","Stefano Marcuzzi, \"Victor Emmanuel III, King of Italy,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10977. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Mussolini_mezzobusto.jpg/500px-Mussolini_mezzobusto.jpg","title":"Benito Mussolini in uniform","desc":"A portrait photograph of Mussolini in uniform with his arms folded, as reproduced in the second edition of Adolf Dresler's Mussolini als Journalist in 1939.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Public domain, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mussolini_mezzobusto.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a bald, heavy-set man in a dark military-style tunic with a shoulder strap and badge, standing with his arms folded and a stern expression."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war break him from the socialists?","a":"Because after an editorial demanding Italian intervention he resigned the party newspaper, and when his own interventionist paper appeared the party expelled him."},{"q":"Why did a movement with 4,800 votes revive?","a":"Because the strikes of the biennio rosso made its squads useful to the authorities, so official toleration gave it a place the ballot had not."},{"q":"Why did the king appoint him?","a":"Because he looked to him for order and stabilisation while some 30,000 Fascists converged on Rome and Mussolini himself waited in Milan."}],"wiki":"Benito Mussolini"},
    "ww2-038": {"questions":["Blackshirt columns converged on the capital on the night of 27 and 28 October 1922 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Mussolini himself waited in Milan for the king’s invitation.","Fascist Italy counted its own era from 28 October 1922, the date of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and required that year to appear beside the Gregorian date in public documents."],"abstract":"The <b>March on Rome</b> was the insurrection of late October 1922 by which the Fascists took power in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mussolini had announced at the end of September that the march would be made, and the party congress held at Naples between 24 and 26 October in effect settled its stages, with the four men later called the quadrumvirs — Balbo, Bianchi, De Bono and De Vecchi — in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the threat lay 2 years of squad violence spread across the country, which official toleration had done much to make possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the night of 27 and 28 October the columns moved, and some 30,000 armed Fascists converged on the capital while Mussolini himself stayed in Milan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prime Minister Luigi Facta ordered martial law, and Victor Emmanuel III refused to countersign the decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The king, who looked to Mussolini as the man likeliest to restore order, invited him instead to form a government and appointed him prime minister on 30 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He came down from Milan by train, and the blackshirts paraded through Rome, ending on 31 October in front of the king and the new prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rising was therefore both a threat of force and a handover made by the crown within the constitution, which is why an older account could call it little more than a train journey answering the sovereign’s invitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More recent work stresses the long preparation and the squad violence of those days, and treats the march as the key to the authoritarian structure that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regime made it a founding myth, counting its own era from 28 October 1922, requiring that year to stand beside the Gregorian date in public acts, and sponsoring an official film of the events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gianmarco Mancosu, \"A Noi! La marcia su Roma tra politica e propaganda,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 53, no. 1 (2023), http://www.studistorici.com/2023/03/29/mancosu_numero_53/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Benito Mussolini,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/benito-mussolini-1. [Open access]","Stefano Marcuzzi, \"Victor Emmanuel III, King of Italy,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10977. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Benito_Mussolini_marching_into_Rome.jpg/1280px-Benito_Mussolini_marching_into_Rome.jpg","title":"Fascists at the Victor Emmanuel monument, Rome, 1922","desc":"A press photograph of the columns filling the piazza below the Victor Emmanuel monument, printed in The Outlook of 29 November 1922.","credit":"Wide World Photos, published in The Outlook 132, no. 13 (29 November 1922), 575. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_marching_into_Rome.jpg","alt":"A grainy black-and-white photograph looking down on a Roman piazza, dense columns of people filling the streets and the steps below the colonnaded Victor Emmanuel monument, part of it still under scaffolding."},"why":[{"q":"Why did martial law never take effect?","a":"Because the prime minister ordered it and the king refused to countersign the decree, inviting Mussolini to form a government instead."},{"q":"Why can it be described both as a coup and as a handover?","a":"Because it was a threat of force answered by an appointment made by the crown within the constitution, which is why an older account called it little more than a train journey."},{"q":"Why did the regime make so much of the date?","a":"Because it counted its own era from 28 October 1922, required that year to stand beside the Gregorian date in public acts, and sponsored an official film of the events."}],"wiki":"March on Rome"},
    "ww2-039": {"questions":["A consolidated public security law of 1926, one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, followed an attempt on Mussolini’s life at Bologna and punished those who left the country secretly for political reasons.","Mussolini’s slogan that everything was within the state and nothing against it was given legal form by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which contemporaries saw as ending one regime and beginning another."],"abstract":"The <b>leggi fascistissime</b> were the laws of 1925 to 1927 that turned a government the Fascists led into a Fascist state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their purpose was to realise Mussolini’s slogan that everything was within the state, nothing outside it and nothing against it, and contemporaries understood them as the end of one regime and the beginning of another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were meant to build a new constitutional order rather than simply to remove opponents and put party men in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A consolidated public security law was approved in 1926 in the days after an attempt on Mussolini’s life at Bologna on 31 October, and it gave prefects wide discretion over any association held to be acting against the national interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same law extended police confinement to political offences and raised the penalties for leaving the country secretly for political reasons, and a Special Tribunal for the Defence of the State was trying political cases by 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Over the 7 years from 1922 the Fascists established and consolidated a one-party dictatorship on these foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was never quite absolute: the monarchy stayed independent of the party and remained in theory commander-in-chief, and Mussolini’s leadership was nominally subject to the party’s supreme body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the account published under his name the state leaves the individual a margin of liberty, but the state alone decides how much of that liberty is useful and how much may be taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regime dressed the new order in the memory of the war, its militia taking over the symbols and practices of the wartime shock troops and its ceremonies resting on the cult of the fallen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What was built glorified violence, and it took Italy into a series of wars and finally into a second world conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Francesco Altavilla, \"Il processo di Savona,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 14, no. 2 (2013), http://www.studistorici.com/2013/08/29/altavilla_numero_14/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Benito_Mussolini_alla_Camera_dei_Deputati_%2810%29.png/1280px-Benito_Mussolini_alla_Camera_dei_Deputati_%2810%29.png","title":"The Italian Chamber of Deputies in session in the 1920s","desc":"The chamber that passed the laws of 1925 to 1927, seen from the public gallery with the government benches on the left.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 1920s. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_alla_Camera_dei_Deputati_%2810%29.png","alt":"A black-and-white photograph looking down into a parliamentary chamber, deputies seated at curved tiers of desks, a long bench of ministers along one wall and four men at a small table on the floor of the house."},"why":[{"q":"Why were these laws more than a purge of opponents?","a":"Because they were meant to build a new constitutional order rather than simply to remove opponents and put party men in their place."},{"q":"Why did an assassination attempt matter to the legislation?","a":"Because the consolidated public security law was approved in the days after the attempt at Bologna, giving prefects wide discretion over any association held to act against the national interest."},{"q":"Why is the liberty the state left not really liberty?","a":"Because the state alone decides how much of that margin is useful and how much may be taken away."}]},
    "ww2-040": {"questions":["The Carta del Lavoro of 1927 set out the principles of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the order in which people, syndicates and corporations were meant to form one hierarchy under the state.","Corporatism entered fascism through the jurist Alfredo Rocco, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it produced claimed descent from the guilds of medieval Italy while banning strikes under penal sanction."],"abstract":"The <b>corporate state</b> was the economic and constitutional order fascist Italy built in the 1920s and 1930s, and one of the defining features of that state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Corporatism entered fascism through the jurist Alfredo Rocco and became the keystone of its economic architecture, offered as a third way between liberal and communist economies and as an answer to the slump of the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It claimed descent from the guilds of medieval Italy, a lineage that hovers between real history and invented tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first fascist programme had already fused revolutionary socialism, syndicalism and extreme nationalism, and corporativist and syndicalist strands ran together in the political culture the movement grew out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two laws of 1926 gave the system its legal form: free trade unions were dissolved and replaced by syndicates authorised and controlled by the state, employers’ organisations were placed under the same tutelage, and a ministry of corporations was created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Strikes and lockouts were forbidden under penal sanction, and collective labour disputes went instead to a labour magistracy that claimed to protect both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Carta del Lavoro of 1927, drafted by Rocco and Carlo Costamagna, set out the principles the order claimed to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Consiglio Nazionale delle Corporazioni coordinated the syndicates, its powers widened by further legislation in 1930 and 1932, and the liberal chamber of deputies was at last abolished and replaced by an assembly of the fasci and the corporations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People, syndicates, corporations and state formed a single hierarchy, which the regime itself called totalitarian in the sense that no free space was left outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account published under Mussolini’s name says as much, claiming that the state reaches every aspect of national life through its corporative institutions and frames all the economic forces of the nation within them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Mario G. Losano, \"Un modello italiano per l’economia nel Brasile di Getúlio Vargas: la «Carta del Lavoro» del 1927,\" <i>Rechtsgeschichte – Legal History</i> 20 (2012): 274–305, http://data.rg.mpg.de/rechtsgeschichte/rg20_274losano.pdf. [in Italian] [Open access]","Roberta Ferrari, \"Corporativismo fascista e New Deal statunitense. Pianificare tra Stato e business,\" <i>Laboratoire italien</i> 32 (2024), https://doi.org/10.4000/122lg. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/1927_edition_of_the_Carta_del_Lavoro_commented_by_Giuseppe_Bottai.png/1280px-1927_edition_of_the_Carta_del_Lavoro_commented_by_Giuseppe_Bottai.png","title":"The Carta del Lavoro, Rome, 1927","desc":"The title page of the 1927 Rome edition of the Charter of Labour with Giuseppe Bottai's commentary, dated in the Fascist era as year V.","credit":"Anonymous, Edizioni del Diritto del Lavoro, Rome, 1927. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1927_edition_of_the_Carta_del_Lavoro_commented_by_Giuseppe_Bottai.png","alt":"The title page of a pamphlet, the words La Carta del Lavoro printed large in red between two black rules, with a line below saying it is illustrated by Giuseppe Bottai and an imprint reading Rome, MCMXXVII, Anno V."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the system offered as a third way?","a":"Because it was presented as lying between liberal and communist economies and as an answer to the slump of the 1930s."},{"q":"Why did banning strikes require a new tribunal?","a":"Because with strikes and lockouts forbidden under penal sanction, collective disputes went instead to a labour magistracy that claimed to protect both sides."},{"q":"Why did the regime itself call the order totalitarian?","a":"Because people, syndicates, corporations and state formed a single hierarchy in the sense that no free space was left outside it."}],"wiki":"Corporate statism"},
    "ww2-041": {"questions":["10 seconds of a face in half shadow, in the official film of the march on Rome, gave the cult of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the lasting images of its iconography.","Italian propaganda abroad cultivated foreign admirers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the first German life of him was published in Munich in 1924, 9 years before Germany had a leader of its own."],"abstract":"The title <b>Duce</b> was the centre of the personal cult that fascist Italy built around Mussolini, and that cult was one of the regime’s chief instruments for manufacturing consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to it as a journalist: the war-diary extracts he published in his own paper already presented him as a Nietzschean superman among submissive comrades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cinema was treated from the beginning as a fundamental instrument of consent, and the official film of the march on Rome gave the cult one of its lasting images, 10 seconds of his face in half shadow, staring the audience down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Istituto Luce carried that work on, and the regime’s founding myth was re-articulated as its needs changed, above all around the African empire proclaimed in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Discipline was the object: the movement demanded complete submission to the party hierarchy and above all to the charismatic leader at the head of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state described in Mussolini’s own name enforces order, discipline and obedience to the moral code of the country, and holds up the memory of past leaders as an example for future generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of the ritual came out of the war, the militia taking over the symbols of the wartime shock troops and the commemoration of the fallen supplying the regime with a liturgy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cult was exported as well as home-grown: Italian propaganda services abroad cultivated foreign admirers, and the first German life of Mussolini was published in Munich in 1924 by a young journalist of the Nazi party’s press office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1933 Germany had a leader of its own, and what German admirers were allowed to say about a second leader sent by providence changed with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is largely what the regime chose to show, since the images of the cult were made, kept and remade by the propaganda apparatus that produced them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gianmarco Mancosu, \"A Noi! La marcia su Roma tra politica e propaganda,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 53, no. 1 (2023), http://www.studistorici.com/2023/03/29/mancosu_numero_53/. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","Claire Lorenzelli, \"Un Mussolini munichois ? Construction et utilisation du culte du « Duce » dans différents cercles de sociabilité bavarois (1922-1943),\" <i>Laboratoire italien</i> 30 (2023), https://doi.org/10.4000/laboratoireitalien.10074. [in French] [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Dichiarazione_di_guerra_a_Piazza_Venezia_%2810_giugno_1940%29.jpg/1280px-Dichiarazione_di_guerra_a_Piazza_Venezia_%2810_giugno_1940%29.jpg","title":"The crowd in Piazza Venezia, 10 June 1940","desc":"The square below the balcony of the Palazzo Venezia during the speech announcing Italy's declaration of war on France and Britain.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 10 June 1940, Corriere della Sera. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dichiarazione_di_guerra_a_Piazza_Venezia_%2810_giugno_1940%29.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph taken from above of a Roman square filled edge to edge with a dense crowd, banners held up among them, beside the long plain facade of a palace with a small balcony above its doorway."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the cult treated as an instrument rather than a flourish?","a":"Because it was one of the regime’s chief means of manufacturing consent, cinema being treated from the beginning as a fundamental instrument of it."},{"q":"Why was the leader’s image tied to the memory of the war?","a":"Because the militia took over the symbols of the wartime shock troops and the commemoration of the fallen supplied the regime with a liturgy."},{"q":"Why is the surviving evidence hard to read straight?","a":"Because what survives is largely what the regime chose to show, the images having been made, kept and remade by the propaganda apparatus that produced them."}],"wiki":"Duce"},
    "ww2-042": {"questions":["The Hoare-Laval plan of December 1935 would have ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ethiopia’s expense, and is now read as an early form of the appeasement of the later 1930s.","Defeat at Maychew and the fall of Addis Ababa decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which sanctions were wound up and Italy moved away from Britain and France towards Germany."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Italo-Ethiopian War</b> was the invasion of Ethiopia by fascist Italy in 1935 and the conquest that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 2 October 1935 the emperor telegraphed the League of Nations that Italian troops had crossed the frontier south of Mount Mussa Ali and were establishing themselves on Ethiopian soil in preparation for an extensive attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ethiopia was a member of the League, and the organisation’s helplessness before an attack on one of its own members did lasting damage to its authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Britain and France mattered more to each other and to the Italian government than Ethiopia did, and the plan their ministers put forward in December 1935 would have settled the war at Ethiopian expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Resigning over the outcry it caused, the British foreign secretary told the Commons he had been obsessed above all with preventing a European war and an isolated one with Italy, and that Anglo-French solidarity was essential.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Italy had ratified the Geneva Protocol against chemical weapons in 1928, and Mussolini’s own directive of 30 December 1934 nonetheless called for absolute superiority in artillery and gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When an Ethiopian counter-offensive checked the advance, Badoglio had mustard-gas bombs dropped on the Takkaze on 22 December 1935 and Graziani did the same on the southern front 2 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The emperor protested to Geneva at once and asked for an impartial enquiry, but neither Britain nor France would indict Mussolini while Germany rearmed, and the Council passed the question to the International Red Cross, which declined it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Defeat at Maychew on 31 March 1936 and the Italian entry into Addis Ababa on 5 May ended the fighting, and sanctions were wound up that summer so the great powers could have normal relations with Italy again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Italy abandoned the agreements of early 1935 and moved towards Germany, working the new African empire into the regime’s founding myth, while far beyond Africa the war was read racially and Ethiopia stood for sovereignty to many African-Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["The Consul at Geneva (Gilbert) to the Secretary of State, 2 October 1935, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1935, General, the Near East and Africa</i>, vol. 1, doc. 543, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1935v01/d543. [Open access]","Sir Samuel Hoare, \"Personal Explanation,\" <i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 307 (19 December 1935), cols. 2007–17, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1935/dec/19/personal-explanation. [Open access]","Christian Carnevale, \"Proteste inascoltate: l’uso dei gas durante la guerra d’Etiopia e l’apatia della comunità internazionale,\" <i>Nuova Antologia Militare</i> 4, no. 16 (2023): 439–464, https://doi.org/10.36158/978889295793014. [in Italian] [Open access]","Gianmarco Mancosu, \"A Noi! La marcia su Roma tra politica e propaganda,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 53, no. 1 (2023), http://www.studistorici.com/2023/03/29/mancosu_numero_53/. [Open access]","T. P. Nesterova, \"France and Attempt to Resolve the Italo-Ethiopian Conflict in Late 1935: the Hoare–Laval Plan,\" <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i>, no. 8 (2020): 398–411, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2020-8-398-411. [Open access]","T. P. Nesterova, \"France, Italy and Diplomatic End of Italo-Ethiopian War in League of Nations (May—July 1936),\" <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i>, no. 8 (2021): 400–413, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2021-8-400-413. [Open access]","Edward O. Erhagbe and Ehimika A. Ifidon, \"African-Americans and the Italo–Ethiopian Crisis, 1935–1936: The Practical Dimension of Pan-Africanism,\" <i>Aethiopica</i> 11 (2008): 68–84, https://doi.org/10.15460/aethiopica.11.1.187. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Abyssinian-soldiers-1936-142348340618.jpg/1280px-Abyssinian-soldiers-1936-142348340618.jpg","title":"Ethiopian horsemen, 1936","desc":"Mounted troops of the Ethiopian army photographed during the war with Italy.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 1936, Ahlen and Akerlund. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abyssinian-soldiers-1936-142348340618.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a close-packed troop of horsemen on grey horses, rifles slung across their backs and rows of pennants on tall staffs rising behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the attack damage the League so badly?","a":"Because Ethiopia was a member, and the organisation’s helplessness before an attack on one of its own members did lasting damage to its authority."},{"q":"Why did Britain and France prefer a bargain at Ethiopia’s expense?","a":"Because they mattered more to each other and to the Italian government than Ethiopia did; resigning over the outcry, the British foreign secretary told the Commons he had been obsessed above all with preventing a European war and with avoiding an isolated one with Italy."},{"q":"Why did Ethiopia’s protests about the gas never produce an enquiry?","a":"Because neither Britain nor France would indict Mussolini while Germany was rearming, and the Council passed the question to the International Red Cross, which declined it, so the protests had no consequence at all."}],"wiki":"Second Italo-Ethiopian War","places":["adm0:eth","adm0:ita","city:1159151549","pol:ethiopia","pol:italian_africa","pol:italy"]},
    "ww2-043": {"questions":["The doctrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided humanity into races with inherited traits, put Germans at the top of that hierarchy, and demanded living space for them in eastern Europe.","Presenting itself as the heir of the trench soldiers, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> condemned parliament as the source of national weakness and demanded submission to a charismatic leader."],"abstract":"<b>Nazism</b>, short for the <b>National Socialism</b> of Hitler’s party, grew out of a small antisemitic and völkisch splinter group founded in Munich in January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler joined it that autumn, and at a meeting of two thousand people in February 1920 the German Workers’ Party was renamed the National Socialist German Workers’ Party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The programme published on 24 February 1920 demanded the union of all Germans in a greater Germany and the invalidation of the peace treaties of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Versailles\">Versailles</span> and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Treaty_of_Saint-Germain-en-Laye_(1919)\">Saint-Germain</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It confined citizenship to those of German blood and stated that no Jew could belong to the nation, so racial exclusion stood in the party’s first published aims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same document demanded land to feed the people, a national army, a press closed to non-Germans and a strong central power, and its authors promised to pursue those points ruthlessly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The movement presented itself as the heir of the trench soldiers, taking the wartime shock troops as the model for a new political soldier and dressing its militia in their symbols and practices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It condemned democracy and parliament as the source of national weakness, branded the parties of the left as anti-national, and demanded submission to the hierarchy and above all to a charismatic leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once Germany had such a leader of its own in 1933, his cult set the terms even for those abroad who admired a different one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its doctrine held that humanity was divided into races whose traits were inherited, that some races were superior to others, and that Germans as ‘Aryans’ stood at the top as a master race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From that came the demand for ‘living space’ in eastern Europe, the plan to remove or dominate the people already there, and the persecution that ended in the murder of six million Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Claire Lorenzelli, \"Un Mussolini munichois ? Construction et utilisation du culte du « Duce » dans différents cercles de sociabilité bavarois (1922-1943),\" <i>Laboratoire italien</i> 30 (2023), https://doi.org/10.4000/laboratoireitalien.10074. [in French] [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/1920-02-24_25-points-program_NSDAP_page1.jpg/1280px-1920-02-24_25-points-program_NSDAP_page1.jpg","title":"The party programme of 24 February 1920","desc":"The first page of the printed twenty-five point programme, its opening demands set in two columns of blackletter.","credit":"Printed programme of the NSDAP, 24 February 1920. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1920-02-24_25-points-program_NSDAP_page1.jpg","alt":"A printed sheet headed Programm der National-Sozialistischen Deutschen Arbeiter-Partei in heavy blackletter type, with numbered demands running down two columns beneath it."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the 1920 programme matter to what followed?","a":"Because it confined citizenship to those of German blood and stated that no Jew could belong to the nation, so racial exclusion stood in the party’s first published aims."},{"q":"Why did the movement present itself as the heir of the trenches?","a":"Because it took the wartime shock troops as the model for a new political soldier and dressed its militia in their symbols and practices."},{"q":"Why did the racial doctrine require territory?","a":"Because holding Germans to be a master race produced the demand for living space in eastern Europe and the plan to remove or dominate the people already there."}],"wiki":"Nazism"},
    "ww2-044": {"questions":["Partially blinded by gas in 1918 and drawn into politics through an army information office, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made his first antisemitic speeches in Munich in August 1919.","A trial for treason in 1924 gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a platform against parliamentary democracy, and the prison term that followed gave him the time to write his book."],"abstract":"<b>Adolf Hitler</b> was the Austrian-born leader of the Nazi party and, from January 1933, the dictator of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in 1889 in the Upper Austrian border town of Braunau am Inn, he lived in Vienna from February 1908 until May 1913, drifting and supporting himself by painting watercolours and sketches, and the war gave his life its direction when he left for Munich and joined the German army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was wounded twice, decorated, and partially blinded by mustard gas near Ypres in October 1918; the armistice reached him in a military hospital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in Munich he worked for an army information office that gathered intelligence on civilian parties and gave the troops anti-communist instruction, and in August 1919, as a course instructor, he made his first violent antisemitic speeches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the party that October, helped write the 1920 programme of racist antisemitism and expansionist nationalism, and by 1921 was its absolute leader, with membership at 55,000 and more than 4,000 men in its militia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The attempt to seize power in Bavaria in November 1923 failed, and he turned his trial for treason into a platform against parliamentary democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Convicted, he served less than a year, and used the imprisonment to begin the book that set out his racial and expansionist programme, eventually published as <i><span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Mein_Kampf\">Mein Kampf</span></i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After his release he rebuilt and reunified the party and chose elections instead of force, campaigning for rearmament and sovereignty and against Versailles, communism and what he called Jewish influence in German life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement he led demanded submission to the party hierarchy and above all to a charismatic leader, which is what he made of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His party took 2.6% of the vote in 1928 and 37.3% in July 1932, and he was appointed chancellor by the president rather than voted into office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He killed himself with his wife Eva in the bunker beneath the Reich Chancellery in Berlin on 30 April 1945, as Allied forces moved into the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Daniel Wosnitzka, “Adolf Hitler 1889–1945,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 1998), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/adolf-hitler. [in German] [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Hitler_portrait_crop.jpg/1280px-Hitler_portrait_crop.jpg","title":"Adolf Hitler, 1938","desc":"An official portrait taken in 1938 by Heinrich Hoffmann, his personal photographer, when Hitler was Führer and Reich Chancellor.","credit":"Heinrich Hoffmann, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H1216-0500-002, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitler_portrait_crop.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white half-length photograph of a man with a short moustache and side-parted hair, in a double-breasted party tunic with an Iron Cross and a swastika armband, staring at the camera."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a failed coup help rather than finish him?","a":"Because he turned his trial for treason into a platform against parliamentary democracy, served less than a year, and used the imprisonment to begin the book setting out his programme."},{"q":"Why did he choose elections after 1924?","a":"Because force had failed in Bavaria, so he rebuilt the party and campaigned instead for rearmament and sovereignty and against Versailles and communism."},{"q":"Why is it significant that he was appointed rather than elected?","a":"Because his party took 37.3% in July 1932 and never a majority, so the chancellorship came from the president rather than from a vote."}],"wiki":"Adolf Hitler"},
    "ww2-045": {"questions":["Several German states banned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1922 as a danger to the republic, and it was dissolved outright after the failed Bavarian coup of 1923.","With about 55,000 members by 1923, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still took only 2.6% of the national vote in May 1928, while the republic was stable."],"abstract":"The <b>NSDAP</b>, the National Socialist German Workers’ Party, began as the German Workers’ Party founded in Munich on 5 January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of many small antisemitic and völkisch groups in a city that had become the centre of right-wing agitation against the Weimar Republic, and it was taken at first for an unimportant debating circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler joined in the autumn of 1919 and drew audiences to it, and on 24 February 1920 the party took its new name at a meeting of two thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its published programme of that day demanded a greater Germany and the invalidation of the Versailles treaty, confined citizenship to those of German blood, and was anti-democratic and anti-communist alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of 1921 the party had a paramilitary wing, the Sturmabteilung, which fought its street battles and supplied its parades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its language was extreme enough that several German states banned it in 1922 as a danger to the Weimar Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took the wartime shock troops as its model of a political soldier, and its own militia borrowed their symbols and practices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The party had about 55,000 members and more than 4,000 men in its militia by 1923, when its leaders tried to seize power in Bavaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Banned and dissolved after that failure, it was permitted again in 1925, rebuilt under one leader, given new formations in the SS that year and the Hitler Youth in the next, and organised from 1928 into a structure matching the electoral districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so it stayed marginal while the Weimar Republic was stable, taking between 1.6 and 2.5% in the state elections of 1926 and 1927 and 2.6% nationally in May 1928, when it had about a hundred thousand members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-04651A%2C_Preu%C3%9Fen%2C_Wahlplakat_Nationalsozialisten.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-04651A%2C_Preu%C3%9Fen%2C_Wahlplakat_Nationalsozialisten.jpg","title":"A party election poster in Prussia, 1932","desc":"Passers-by reading a Nazi election poster pasted round an advertising column, from the presidential campaign of 1932.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-04651A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-04651A,_Preu%C3%9Fen,_Wahlplakat_Nationalsozialisten.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of five people in coats and caps standing with their backs to the camera, reading a large blackletter poster wrapped around a cylindrical advertising column on a street corner."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Munich where such a party could grow?","a":"Because the city had become the centre of right-wing agitation against the Weimar Republic, so one small antisemitic and völkisch group among many had an audience to draw on."},{"q":"Why did some German states ban it as early as 1922?","a":"Because its language was extreme enough to be judged a danger to the republic, and it already had a paramilitary wing fighting its street battles."},{"q":"Why did it stay marginal for most of the 1920s?","a":"Because the Weimar Republic was stable: it took between 1.6 and 2.5% in state elections of 1926 and 1927 and 2.6% nationally in May 1928."}],"wiki":"Nazi Party"},
    "ww2-046": {"questions":["The trial that followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made Hitler famous in nationalist circles, and the prison term it brought gave him the leisure to write his book.","Bavarian authorities banned the party, its militia and its newspaper after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collapsed, and it was permitted again only in 1925."],"abstract":"The <b>Beer Hall Putsch</b> was the attempt by Hitler and other Nazi leaders to seize power in Bavaria on 8 and 9 November 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan was to take the Bavarian state first and then march on Berlin to overthrow the government in Berlin, which the party had refused to compete with at the ballot box.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had about 55,000 members at the time, and its leaders had decided that power was to be taken by force rather than sought through the elections they despised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rising collapsed quickly against the Bavarian police, and Hitler was arrested and tried for high treason before a Munich court along with the other ringleaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He used the trial as a stage against parliamentary democracy and for xenophobic nationalism, and it carried his name into the German and international press and made him famous in nationalist circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The court sentenced him to 5 years, of which he served less than 9 months, a leniency that says much about where the sympathies of the Bavarian bench lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He began writing in Landsberg prison the book that became his best-known work, hoping it would earn money and spread his views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Bavarian authorities answered the attempt by banning and dissolving the party, its militia and its newspaper, so that the movement lost at once its organisation and its voice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party that had grown out of a small Munich splinter group of January 1919 was thus broken within 4 years of taking its name, and its leader was in prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When it was allowed again the strategy changed rather than the doctrine, and the movement kept its contempt for parliament and its demand for a leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Adolf_Hitler_m.fl._i_M%C3%BCnchen%2C_1924.jpg/1280px-Adolf_Hitler_m.fl._i_M%C3%BCnchen%2C_1924.jpg","title":"The defendants at Munich, 1924","desc":"The accused photographed at the treason trial that followed the putsch, their names lettered along the foot of the print.","credit":"Photograph by Heinrich Hoffmann, 1924. Bundesarchiv, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolf_Hitler_m.fl._i_M%C3%BCnchen,_1924.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of nine men standing in a line outside a building, most in uniform with swords and medals, two in civilian overcoats, with their surnames handwritten across the bottom of the image."},"why":[{"q":"Why take Bavaria first rather than Berlin?","a":"Because the plan was to seize the state and then march on the national government, which the party had refused to compete with at the ballot box."},{"q":"Why did the sentence say something about the court?","a":"Because he served less than 9 months of 5 years, a leniency that indicates where the sympathies of the Bavarian bench lay."},{"q":"Why did losing the party hurt more than losing the coup?","a":"Because the authorities banned and dissolved the party, its militia and its newspaper, so the movement lost at once its organisation and its voice."}],"wiki":"Beer Hall Putsch"},
    "ww2-047": {"questions":["Sales of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose with the party’s vote, reaching almost 230,000 copies by the end of 1932 and more than twelve million printed by the end of 1944.","Bavaria held the copyright of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the war and used it to prevent reprinting in Germany until that copyright expired at the end of 2015."],"abstract":"<b><i>Mein Kampf</i></b>, meaning <i>My Struggle</i>, is the book Hitler began writing in Landsberg prison in 1924 after his conviction for high treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had intended to call it \"<i>Four and a Half Years Against Lies, Stupidity and Cowardice</i>\", until his publisher, Max Amann, suggested the much shorter title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Nazi_Party\">The party</span>’s own publishing house issued the first volume in 1925 and the second in the following year, and in the summer of 1928 he wrote a further book on foreign policy that was never published and was found only in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it he set out a racial and social Darwinist view of history, dictatorship at home, military expansion, and the seizure of living space in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That living space was to be taken in eastern Europe, whose peoples were to be removed, dominated or killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The book was not an immediate success: the first edition of 10,000 copies largely sold out and a second was printed, but sales slackened quickly afterwards while the party remained small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They rose with the party’s fortunes, and by the end of 1932, after it had become the largest party in parliament, almost 230,000 copies had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler’s appointment as <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Chancellor_of_Germany\">chancellor</span> in January 1933 made the author a multi-millionaire, with more than 850,000 copies sold in that year alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 12 million copies had been printed by the end of 1944, most of them after 1939, in editions for newlyweds, in braille, and in translations into more than a dozen languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the war the Allies withdrew it from circulation with the rest of Nazi propaganda, and the copyright passed to Bavaria, which used it to prevent German reprinting until it expired at the end of 2015 and a critical edition appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Lebensraum,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensraum. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Mein_Kampf_dust_jacket.jpeg/1280px-Mein_Kampf_dust_jacket.jpeg","title":"The dust jacket of the second volume","desc":"The jacket of the volume subtitled The National Socialist Movement, issued by the party's own publishing house in Munich.","credit":"Dust jacket, Franz Eher Nachfolger, Munich, 1926-1927, New York Public Library. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mein_Kampf_dust_jacket.jpeg","alt":"A book jacket with the title set large in red outlined letters on a cream ground, the author's name in white on black bands above and below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the book sell so poorly at first?","a":"Because the party remained small: the first edition of ten thousand largely sold out and a second was printed, and sales slackened quickly after that."},{"q":"Why did sales track politics rather than the other way round?","a":"Because almost 230,000 copies had gone by the end of 1932, after the party became the largest in parliament, and more than 850,000 in 1933 alone."},{"q":"Why could Germany prevent reprinting after the war?","a":"Because the copyright passed to Bavaria, which used it to block German reprints until it expired at the end of 2015."}],"wiki":"Mein Kampf"},
    "ww2-048": {"questions":["Drawing on trench comradeship and older völkisch ideas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was offered as the answer to every social division of the Weimar Republic.","Since the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was defined by race, the belonging it offered required the exclusion of everyone the party counted as outside the German people."],"abstract":"<b>Volksgemeinschaft</b>, ‘the people’s community’, was the social promise at the centre of Nazi ideology, and the thing the movement offered Germans in exchange for their liberties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It drew on older völkisch ideas of community and on the comradeship men remembered, or believed they remembered, from the trenches of the First World War, where rank and class were held to have counted for nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nazis propagated it as the solution to every political and social antagonism of the Weimar Republic, at a time when those antagonisms had brought the state repeatedly to a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The community was defined by race and modelled on an idealised picture of the ancient Germans, and it was meant to cancel differences of birth, occupation, wealth and education among those counted as members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who counted was settled by descent: the party’s own programme confined membership of the nation to those of German blood and excluded Jews by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The doctrine behind it divided humanity into races whose traits were held to be inherited, ranked those races against one another, and placed Germans at the top of the ranking as a supposed master race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unity of that kind required an enemy, and the movement branded the parties of the left as anti-national and democracy itself as the source of weakness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also required obedience, and the party demanded complete submission to its hierarchy and above all to the leader at its head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the seizure of power the promise took on a central function in establishing the new dictatorship, since a community defined in this way had no place for parties, unions or private loyalties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it offered ordinary Germans was belonging; what it required was the exclusion, and finally the destruction, of those it defined as outside the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["\"Volksgemeinschaft,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/innenpolitik/volksgemeinschaft.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-020-23%2C_N%C3%BCrnberg%2C_WHW-Werbetafel.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-020-23%2C_N%C3%BCrnberg%2C_WHW-Werbetafel.jpg","title":"A Winter Relief hoarding in Nuremberg","desc":"A street board calling for sacrifice to the Winter Relief campaign, one of the ways the community was made a daily obligation.","credit":"Photograph by Kurt Grimm. Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1984-020-23, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-020-23,_N%C3%BCrnberg,_WHW-Werbetafel.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a large blackletter poster on a wooden frame at a street corner, a medieval round tower behind it and a man walking past."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the promise attractive in the Weimar years?","a":"Because it was offered as the solution to every political and social antagonism of the republic, at a time when those antagonisms had brought the state repeatedly to a standstill."},{"q":"Why did the community need an enemy?","a":"Because a unity of that kind was defined by exclusion, so the movement branded the parties of the left as anti-national and democracy itself as the source of weakness."},{"q":"Why did the idea serve the dictatorship after 1933?","a":"Because a community defined by race and obedience had no place for parties, unions or private loyalties."}],"wiki":"Volksgemeinschaft"},
    "ww2-049": {"questions":["Older religious hostility was recast as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once theories of race and eugenics gave the prejudice the appearance of science.","Written into the party programme of 1920, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced the demand that Jews be excluded from the nation and finally the murder of six million."],"abstract":"<b>Racial antisemitism</b> is the false claim that Jews are a biologically separate, inferior or parasitic race, and it was a central component of Nazi ideology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hostility to Jews in Europe was far older, reaching back to antiquity and early Christianity, and Christianity played a central part in spreading it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1900 antisemitic stereotypes, misconceptions and myths were well established and widely accepted in Germany and other European societies, which is what later made the Holocaust possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The racial form of it dates from the late nineteenth century, when theories of race and eugenics were given the appearance of science and old religious hostility was recast as a claim about biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political movements built on it demanded the official exclusion of Jews from economic, social and political life, and some demanded their forced emigration; scapegoating of this kind returns in times of upheaval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The most infamous of the conspiracy theories appeared around 1900 in a fabricated publication purporting to reveal a Jewish plot to rule the world, one of a family of lies that cast Jews as hidden masters of press and finance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nazi party drew on that inheritance and wrote it into its first programme, which confined the nation to those of German blood and excluded Jews by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler made his first violent antisemitic speeches in Munich in 1919 and set the argument down on paper a month later, urging that Jews be removed from Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His book promoted the same view as one of the components of the movement’s doctrine, alongside its racial hierarchy and its foreign policy of conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The doctrine falsely claimed that Jews were an inferior race and an existential threat, and it led to the murder of six million of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Boycot_of_Jewish_shops_april_1_1933.jpeg/1280px-Boycot_of_Jewish_shops_april_1_1933.jpeg","title":"The boycott of 1 April 1933","desc":"Uniformed men outside a shop in Germany during the boycott, beside a placard denouncing its Jewish owners.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 1 April 1933. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boycot_of_Jewish_shops_april_1_1933.jpeg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a shopfront with a large handwritten placard on a post beside it, a uniformed man standing in front of the window and passers-by at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the racial form dated to the late nineteenth century?","a":"Because theories of race and eugenics were then given the appearance of science, and an older religious hostility was recast as a claim about biology."},{"q":"Why did the older prejudice matter to what came after?","a":"Because by 1900 antisemitic stereotypes and myths were well established and widely accepted across European societies, which is what later made the Holocaust possible."},{"q":"Why do such claims resurface in particular periods?","a":"Because scapegoating of this kind returns in times of upheaval, when movements demand the exclusion of a named group from economic, social and political life."}],"wiki":"Racial antisemitism"},
    "ww2-050": {"questions":["The demand for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was inseparable from racial doctrine, since the land was to be taken on the claim that Germans were a master race.","Germany had held ground as far east as Minsk in the First World War, and the loss of it sharpened the later demand for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the same region."],"abstract":"<b>Lebensraum</b>, or ‘living space’, was the demand that Germany take land in the east to feed and settle its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word was coined in 1901 by the geographer Friedrich Ratzel, who held that a nation had to be self-sufficient in resources and territory to be safe from outside threats, and who pointed to the British and French empires as the model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and others misapplied Darwin to states, arguing that nations struggle for resources and that the fittest expand into other people’s territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans had looked east for such room long before the Nazis, since the German colonisation of the Middle Ages, and by the twentieth century many wrote of eastern lands as rich country wasted on peoples they called inferior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German armies did take that ground in the First World War, reaching Minsk and running a military dictatorship there, and lost it again in defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler, who had served in that war, insisted Germany must never again be beaten by a shortage of resources, and spoke of the ores of the Urals and the farmland of Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His book made the seizure of living space in the east one of the party’s central aims, beside its antisemitism and its racial view of the world, and he returned to the theme in the second book he left unpublished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The demand was inseparable from that racial doctrine: the land was to be taken because Germans were held to be a master race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people already living there were to be removed, dominated or murdered, and the occupation of eastern Europe was carried out with extreme brutality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The movement that made this demand had condemned democracy as weakness and promised instead a nation disciplined enough, and ruthless enough, to take whatever it decided it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Lebensraum,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensraum. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Greater_German_Reich_in_1942.png/1280px-Greater_German_Reich_in_1942.png","title":"Europe under German control in 1942","desc":"A modern map of the extent of German rule and occupation at its furthest, the demand for living space as it was finally acted on.","credit":"Map by the Wikimedia contributor Mackay 86, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_German_Reich_in_1942.png","alt":"A colour map of Europe with Germany and its annexed territory in dark red, occupied and allied countries in orange, and the Italian sphere in green."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the demand argued from biology?","a":"Because its authors misapplied Darwin to states, holding that nations struggle for resources and that the fittest expand into other people’s territory."},{"q":"Why was the east the direction chosen?","a":"Because Germans had looked there since the medieval colonisation, German armies had held that ground in the First World War and lost it, and its farmland and ores were coveted."},{"q":"Why was the demand inseparable from the racial doctrine?","a":"Because the land was to be taken on the ground that Germans were a master race, and the people living there were to be removed, dominated or murdered."}],"wiki":"Lebensraum"},
    "ww2-051": {"questions":["The blond and blue-eyed ideal of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fitted many Germans badly, and the regime never persecuted anyone for the colour of their hair or eyes.","A national community open to every member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was promised, on the understanding that Jews, Black people and Romani people were excluded from it as outsiders."],"abstract":"The <b>Aryan race</b> was the invented category at the top of the Nazi racial order, and the standard by which everyone else in Germany was measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doctrine held that humanity was divided into races whose traits passed unchanged from one generation to the next, and that those traits fixed a people’s looks, intelligence, creativity and strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Races so defined were then ranked against one another, and those with the supposedly better traits were said to dominate the rest, which turned an invented biology into a claim about who ought to rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans were identified as Aryans and placed at the top of that ranking, which is why the regime’s propaganda called them a master race and taught schoolchildren to think of themselves in those terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type was idealised as blond, blue-eyed, tall and athletic, though many Germans the regime counted as Aryan looked nothing like it, and hair and eye colour were never the ground of persecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state built on the doctrine promised a community open to all Aryan Germans whatever their class, region or religion, and excluded Jews, Black people and Romani people from it as racial outsiders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claim had been written into the party’s first programme, which allowed membership of the nation only to those of German blood and named Jews as excluded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its antisemitic half was an older prejudice restated as biology: a hatred with roots in antiquity and in early Christianity, recast in the late 19th century as a claim about descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The movement that carried the doctrine also condemned democracy as weakness and branded the parties of the left as enemies of the nation, so that the racial argument and the political one worked as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What followed was not an argument but a policy, since the ranking was used to justify first exclusion from the community, then expulsion from public life, and in the end mass murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Volk_und_Rasse_Heft_7_Heuert_Juli_1935_illustrierte_Monatsschrift._People_and_Race_Nazi_Germany_Third_Reich_propaganda_magazine_cover_photo_Reichsarbeitsdienst_RAD_Labour_Service_members_%28Holocaust_exhibition_HL-senteret_Oslo_2021_B.jpg/1280px-Volk_und_Rasse_Heft_7_Heuert_Juli_1935_illustrierte_Monatsschrift._People_and_Race_Nazi_Germany_Third_Reich_propaganda_magazine_cover_photo_Reichsarbeitsdienst_RAD_Labour_Service_members_%28Holocaust_exhibition_HL-senteret_Oslo_2021_B.jpg","title":"Volk und Rasse, July 1935","desc":"The cover of the German race-propaganda monthly Volk und Rasse for July 1935, showing two labour service men in profile as the idealised racial type. Photographed in a Holocaust exhibition at the HL-senteret, Oslo.","credit":"Photograph by Wolfmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volk_und_Rasse_Heft_7_Heuert_Juli_1935_illustrierte_Monatsschrift._People_and_Race_Nazi_Germany_Third_Reich_propaganda_magazine_cover_photo_Reichsarbeitsdienst_RAD_Labour_Service_members_%28Holocaust_exhibition_HL-senteret_Oslo_2021_B.jpg","alt":"Magazine cover in Fraktur type showing two young men in uniform caps in profile, one wearing a swastika armband."},"why":[{"q":"Why does ranking races turn biology into politics?","a":"Because races with supposedly better traits were said to dominate the rest, which converts an invented biology into a claim about who ought to rule."},{"q":"Why was the idealised appearance not the test?","a":"Because many Germans the regime counted as Aryan looked nothing like the blond, blue-eyed type, and hair and eye colour were never the ground of persecution."},{"q":"Why did the racial and the political arguments work as one?","a":"Because the same movement condemned democracy as weakness and branded the left as enemies of the nation, so exclusion by race and by politics ran together."}],"wiki":"Aryan race"},
    "ww2-052": {"questions":["Called early after a coalition broke over unemployment insurance, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to build a majority that would keep the left out of office for good.","The party that won its breakthrough in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had rebuilt itself since 1925 as a national organisation combining modern campaigning with street violence."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichstag election of 1930</b> was the Nazi electoral breakthrough, and the moment a fringe party became a force in German politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until then the movement had been marginal, taking between 1.6 and 2.5% in state elections and 2.6% nationally in May 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governing coalition had broken in March 1930 over half a percentage point on unemployment insurance contributions, which showed that the parties could not agree even in a slump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three middle-class parties then invoked the emergency provisions of the constitution to call an early election, hoping to build a majority that would keep the Social Democrats and the left out of government for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The manoeuvre failed, and in September 1930 the Nazis took nearly a fifth of the vote, drawing in new, unemployed and alienated voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The party had been rebuilt after 1925 into a national organisation whose structure matched the electoral districts, and its campaigning combined modern technique with street violence for which the leadership could deny responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its youth and its distance from every government since 1918 were themselves advantages in a country tired of the parties in office, and it could promise what none of them had delivered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1930 governments ruled by presidential decree rather than by parliamentary consent, which made each election less a choice of government than a test of strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The movement’s programme had not changed with its fortunes: contempt for parliament, a demand for authority, hostility to the left, and the promise of a nation restored to strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> 2 years later, in the elections of July 1932, the party took 37.3% of the vote and became the largest in the Reichstag, though still short of a majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","\"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette.html. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10313%2C_Reichstagswahl%2C_Propagandawagen_des_Zentrums.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10313%2C_Reichstagswahl%2C_Propagandawagen_des_Zentrums.jpg","title":"Campaigning for the September 1930 election","desc":"A Centre Party propaganda lorry on a Berlin street in August 1930, at the opening of the campaign for the Reichstag election of 14 September.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-10313, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10313,_Reichstagswahl,_Propagandawagen_des_Zentrums.jpg","alt":"A lorry carrying a large banner reading Wählt Zentrum, surrounded by cyclists with placards on a crowded street."},"why":[{"q":"Why was an early election called at all?","a":"Because three middle-class parties invoked the emergency provisions hoping to build a majority that would keep the Social Democrats and the left out of government for good."},{"q":"Why did the party’s inexperience help it?","a":"Because its youth and its distance from every government since 1918 were advantages in a country tired of the parties in office, and it could promise what none of them had delivered."},{"q":"Why did elections after 1930 decide less than they seemed to?","a":"Because governments ruled by presidential decree rather than by parliamentary consent, so each election was less a choice of government than a test of strength."}]},
    "ww2-053": {"questions":["Because Prussia was Germany’s democratic anchor, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> removed the strongest institutional obstacle standing in the way of a national takeover.","Carried out while cabinets already governed by decree rather than by parliament, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came 6 months before the chancellorship changed hands."],"abstract":"The <b>Preußenschlag</b>, ‘the Prussian coup’ of 20 July 1932, ended the last serious share the Social Democrats had in governing the Weimar Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By emergency decree the president deposed the Prussian government of Otto Braun, who had led the largest German state since 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Braun’s ministry had lost its parliamentary majority at the state election of 24 April 1932 and was still in office only to keep the administration running.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chancellor, Franz von Papen, was appointed Reich commissioner in Prussia, and a new interior minister, the mayor of Essen, ran what its enemies called the red fortress in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prussia was two-thirds of Germany and its democratic anchor, so taking its government by decree removed the strongest institutional obstacle in the way of a national takeover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Weimar state had already stopped governing itself in the ordinary way, since from 1930 its cabinets ruled by presidential decree rather than by the consent of a parliamentary majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That habit began in March 1930, when the last broad coalition collapsed over half a percentage point in unemployment insurance contributions and the parties proved unable to compromise in a slump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each new election then made the parliament less workable rather than more, and by July 1932 the Nazis were the largest party in a chamber that could no longer produce a government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The movement that profited most had never hidden that it meant to be rid of the parliamentary system altogether, and said so in every campaign it fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> 6 months after Prussia was taken by decree, the chancellorship itself was handed to the leader of that movement by the same president who had signed the order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Preußenschlag 1932,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/preussenschlag.html. [Open access]","\"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-13680%2C_Berlin%2C_Verordnung_%C3%BCber_Ausnahmezustand.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-13680%2C_Berlin%2C_Verordnung_%C3%BCber_Ausnahmezustand.jpg","title":"The state of emergency posted in Berlin","desc":"Berliners reading the Reich President's decree of July 1932 declaring a state of emergency over Berlin and the province of Brandenburg, posted on an advertising column.","credit":"Photograph by Georg Pahl. Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-13680, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-13680,_Berlin,_Verordnung_%C3%BCber_Ausnahmezustand.jpg","alt":"People standing at an advertising column reading a printed official notice pasted among commercial posters."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Prussia the state worth taking by decree?","a":"Because it was two-thirds of Germany and its democratic anchor, so its government was the strongest institutional obstacle to a national takeover."},{"q":"Why was the government there vulnerable?","a":"Because it had lost its parliamentary majority at the state election of April 1932 and remained in office only to keep the administration running."},{"q":"Why did each election make the parliament less workable?","a":"Because cabinets had ruled by presidential decree since 1930, and by July 1932 the largest party in the chamber was one that meant to be rid of the system altogether."}],"wiki":"1932 Prussian coup d'état"},
    "ww2-054": {"questions":["Conservatives who arranged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> believed they could contain the new chancellor in a coalition, and found within months that the arrangement ran the other way.","Because Germany had been governed by decree since 1930, the office handed over in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> already carried powers no chancellor of the 1920s had used."],"abstract":"The <b>Machtergreifung</b>, ‘the seizure of power’, is the name given to Hitler’s appointment as chancellor on 30 January 1933 and the months in which a democracy became a dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was not voted into office: the president, Paul von Hindenburg, appointed him at the head of a coalition in which conservatives believed they could contain him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party behind him was the largest in the Reichstag after July 1932, but it had never won a majority and its vote had fallen back in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany had been governed by presidential decree since 1930, so the office he was given already carried powers no chancellor of the 1920s had used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In less than 6 months Germany was transformed from a democratic state into a one-party dictatorship built on racist and authoritarian principles, which is the whole point of the term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The steps were quick and legal in form: a decree suspending civil rights, an election fought under it, and a law transferring the power to legislate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Political opponents, above all communists and social democrats, were intimidated, arrested and in many cases killed, while the Nazi campaign went on unhindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement had said for more than a decade that it meant to be rid of parliament and of the parties, so what it did in office surprised nobody who had listened to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The conservatives who thought they were using it found within months that the arrangement ran the other way, and their own parties were dissolved with the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the middle of July the Nazi party was the only one left in Germany, the others outlawed or dissolved under pressure, and the parliament had become a chamber for acclamation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","\"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-15347%2C_Berlin%2C_Ernennung_Adolf_Hitlers_zum_Reichskanzler.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-15347%2C_Berlin%2C_Ernennung_Adolf_Hitlers_zum_Reichskanzler.jpg","title":"Berlin, 30 January 1933","desc":"A crowd in the street outside the Reich chancellery on the day Hitler was appointed chancellor, seen from an upper window.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-15347, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-15347,_Berlin,_Ernennung_Adolf_Hitlers_zum_Reichskanzler.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd in hats fills a street around a parked open car, photographed from above."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the office he received matter as much as the appointment?","a":"Because Germany had been governed by presidential decree since 1930, so the chancellorship already carried powers no chancellor of the 1920s had used."},{"q":"Why were the conservatives wrong about containing him?","a":"Because within months the arrangement ran the other way, and their own parties were dissolved with the rest."},{"q":"Why did what followed surprise nobody who had been listening?","a":"Because the movement had said for more than a decade that it meant to be rid of parliament and of the parties."}]},
    "ww2-055": {"questions":["Thousands of communists and social democrats were arrested under the decree issued the day after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the governing party campaigned unhindered.","Even with the emergency powers taken after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Nazis won only 43.9% on 5 March and still needed a coalition partner."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichstag fire</b> of 27 February 1933 burned the German parliament building 4 weeks after Hitler became chancellor, and gave him the occasion to be rid of his opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next day the president was persuaded to issue a decree for the protection of the people and state, made under the emergency article of the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It suspended the articles of the constitution guaranteeing personal liberty, free expression of opinion including the freedom of the press, the rights of assembly and association, and the privacy of post, telegraph and telephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It permitted house searches, confiscations and restrictions on property beyond the ordinary legal limits, and let the Reich take over the powers of any state that failed to restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thousands of communists and social democrats were arrested in the days that followed, their meetings banned and their newspapers closed, while the National Socialist election campaign went on unhindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The decree was never lifted, so the rights it suspended stayed suspended for 12 years and Germany became a police state without further legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so the party did not win the election of 5 March: with 43.9% it still needed the German National People’s Party as a coalition partner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the decree had done was to make the campaign one-sided, since the opposition could not meet, print or speak while its own leaders were in custody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the clearest case of the pattern the seizure of power followed, which was to use the state’s own emergency machinery to destroy German democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The movement had promised for years to be done with parliamentary government altogether, and the burnt chamber served it as an emblem of the danger it claimed to be saving the country from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Decree of the Reich President for the Protection of the People and State (Reichstag Fire Decree) (February 28, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/decree-of-the-reich-president-for-the-protection-of-the-people-and-state-reichstag-fire-decree-february-28-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Firemen_work_on_the_burning_Reichstag_Building_in_February%2C_1933%2C_after_fire_broke_out_simultaneously_at_20_places...._-_NARA_-_535790.tif/1280px-Firemen_work_on_the_burning_Reichstag_Building_in_February%2C_1933%2C_after_fire_broke_out_simultaneously_at_20_places...._-_NARA_-_535790.tif.jpg","title":"The Reichstag burning, February 1933","desc":"The German parliament building alight on the night of 27 February 1933, with the words Dem deutschen Volke on the portico and smoke pouring from the dome.","credit":"US National Archives and Records Administration, 535790. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firemen_work_on_the_burning_Reichstag_Building_in_February,_1933,_after_fire_broke_out_simultaneously_at_20_places...._-_NARA_-_535790.tif","alt":"A large classical building at night with flames and smoke rising from its dome and light showing through the windows."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the decree more consequential than the fire?","a":"Because it suspended personal liberty, free expression, assembly, association and the privacy of the post, and it was never lifted, so the rights stayed suspended for 12 years."},{"q":"Why did a one-sided campaign still not produce a majority?","a":"Because with 43.9% the party still needed the German National People’s Party as a coalition partner."},{"q":"Why is this the clearest case of the pattern?","a":"Because it used the state’s own emergency machinery to destroy German democracy, rather than acting against the constitution from outside it."}],"wiki":"Reichstag fire"},
    "ww2-056": {"questions":["The two-thirds majority the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required was manufactured by excluding the communist deputies, arresting others, and manipulating the rules of the chamber.","Once the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in force the Reichstag lost its reason for existing and met only to applaud decisions the government had already taken."],"abstract":"The <b>Enabling Act</b> of 23 March 1933 transferred the power to make law from the German parliament to Hitler’s cabinet, and so ended parliamentary government in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Laws made under it could depart from the constitution itself, which meant that the government could legislate against the document it had sworn to uphold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The parliament that passed it had been elected 3 weeks earlier under the emergency decree that followed the Reichstag fire, with the opposition’s press and meetings banned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Nazis had taken 43.9% at that election and governed in coalition, so the two-thirds majority the act required had to be manufactured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was manufactured by arresting and intimidating elected deputies, by excluding the communist members altogether, and by manipulating the rules of the chamber so that absent deputies counted as present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> With the act in force the Reichstag lost its reason for existing and became a body that met to applaud decisions already taken elsewhere, a rubber stamp for the chancellor’s dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government used its new power at once, dissolving the state parliaments and appointing governors who answered to the Reich interior ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A law of 7 April then purged the civil service of Jews and of political opponents, requiring proof of ancestry from everyone in public employment, and the professions followed within months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the middle of July every other party had been outlawed or had dissolved itself, and Germany was a one-party state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement that did all this had told the electorate for years that it despised parliament and the parties in it, and the act was that promise kept in a single afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Decree of the Reich President for the Protection of the People and State (Reichstag Fire Decree) (February 28, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/decree-of-the-reich-president-for-the-protection-of-the-people-and-state-reichstag-fire-decree-february-28-1933. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Enabling_act_of_1933_printed_in_the_Reich_Law_Gazette.jpg/500px-Enabling_act_of_1933_printed_in_the_Reich_Law_Gazette.jpg","title":"The Enabling Act in the Reich Law Gazette","desc":"The Law to Remedy the Distress of the People and the Reich as printed in the Reichsgesetzblatt of 24 March 1933, with its five articles and the signatures of Hindenburg, Hitler and three ministers.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enabling_act_of_1933_printed_in_the_Reich_Law_Gazette.jpg","alt":"A printed page of German law in Fraktur type headed Reichsgesetzblatt, dated 24 March 1933."},"why":[{"q":"Why was this act more than a delegation of power?","a":"Because laws made under it could depart from the constitution itself, so the government could legislate against the document it had sworn to uphold."},{"q":"Why did the required majority have to be manufactured?","a":"Because the party had taken 43.9% and governed in coalition, so deputies were arrested and intimidated, the communists excluded, and absentees counted as present."},{"q":"Why did the parliament lose its reason for existing?","a":"Because with the power to legislate elsewhere it became a body that met to applaud decisions already taken."}],"wiki":"Enabling act"},
    "ww2-057": {"questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the state governments were dissolved, the civil service purged of Jews and opponents, and the trade unions abolished within a few months of 1933.","Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the schools as well, a law of 1934 left the party’s own youth organisation as the only lawful one in Germany."],"abstract":"<b>Gleichschaltung</b>, a German word meaning ‘coordination’ or ‘synchronisation’, is the name the regime gave to the process of remaking every German institution to serve its own ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political, social and cultural life were rearranged one field at a time, with the object of turning the country into a single-party state under Hitler and his party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Enabling Act of March 1933 supplied the instrument, since the cabinet could now legislate without consulting the parliament and against the constitution if it chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two further laws dissolved the existing state governments and put a Reich governor over each, reporting to the interior minister Wilhelm Frick rather than to any local assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The civil service was purged on 7 April 1933 by a law excluding Jews and political opponents, which reached judges, teachers, university professors and lawyers as well as every rank of administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Trade unions were abolished in May 1933 and their members enrolled with their employers in a single labour organisation controlled by the party, while farmers were gathered into a Reich Food Estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September the propaganda minister created a Reich Culture Chamber over literature, music, theatre, radio, film, the fine arts and the press, so that only its members could go on working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sports teams, music groups and craft associations that had belonged to parties or unions were disbanded, and a law of 1934 left the Hitler Youth as the only lawful youth organisation in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attempt was made, with less success, on the Christian denominations and their own youth groups, but by mid-July 1933 a law had declared the party the only one permitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of the work came from below rather than above, as Germans coordinated their own associations unbidden, and the movement that had promised a nation without parties or private loyalties had one within 6 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service (April 7, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/law-for-the-restoration-of-the-professional-civil-service-april-7-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Der_Weg_des_%22gleichgeschalteten%22_Staatsb%C3%BCrgers.jpg/1280px-Der_Weg_des_%22gleichgeschalteten%22_Staatsb%C3%BCrgers.jpg","title":"The path of the coordinated citizen","desc":"A propaganda chart setting out the life of a German under the regime, from the Jungvolk and the League of German Girls through labour service and military service into the German Labour Front.","credit":"Photograph by Maddriver371, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Weg_des_%22gleichgeschalteten%22_Staatsb%C3%BCrgers.jpg","alt":"A diagram with two horizontal arrows of marching figures, one for women and one for men, labelled by age and by organisation."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the process move field by field so quickly?","a":"Because the Enabling Act let the cabinet legislate without the parliament and against the constitution, so each institution could be remade by law."},{"q":"Why did a culture chamber control who could work?","a":"Because only its members could go on working in literature, music, theatre, radio, film, the fine arts and the press, so membership became a licence to practise."},{"q":"Why is it wrong to picture the process as purely imposed?","a":"Because much of the work came from below, as Germans coordinated their own associations unbidden."}],"wiki":"Gleichschaltung"},
    "ww2-058": {"questions":["Wages and contracts were not settled by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, but by trustees for labour whom the Reich labour ministry appointed.","Propaganda presented the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the end of class conflict, while workers lost every institution through which they had pressed their own interests."],"abstract":"The <b>German Labour Front</b> was the single organisation into which German workers and their employers were herded after the free trade unions were destroyed in May 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The slump and years of mass unemployment had already worn down the labour movement’s cohesion and morale and left the unions badly destabilised before the new government touched them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a few months of that government taking office the workers had lost every institution that had traditionally represented them, their unions dissolved and the Social Democratic and Communist parties banned between May and July 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new body joined employers and employees in one association attached to the party, under the party leader Robert Ley, and essentially every worker in every sector of the economy belonged to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It did not in fact settle wages, tariffs or contracts, which were decided by trustees for labour appointed by the Reich labour ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where the old unions had put workers’ rights first, the new one preached national economic goals above personal well-being, and farmers were gathered separately into a Reich Food Estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Propaganda presented the arrangement as the end of class difference and conflict and the foundation of a national community marked by harmony and solidarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What workers had actually lost were the organs that had carried their interests, along with the right to form organisations of their own and the right to strike, so that control over working conditions passed to employers and trustees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Front also ran the leisure programme Strength through Joy, which offered subsidised holidays, theatre and concert tickets, music and art classes and sport, and so reached into workers’ free time as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same coordination was applied to professional and cultural bodies, and by the middle of July 1933 the party was the only one permitted, so that a movement which had denounced the left absorbed the labour movement rather than argued with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Appeal of the German Workers’ Front after the Dissolution of the Free Trade Unions (May 2, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/appeal-of-the-german-workers-front-after-the-dissolution-of-the-free-trade-unions-then-as-now-we-remain-comrades-may-2-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/FHM-282370_Deutsche_Arbeitsfront_member_paramilitary_uniform_DAF_swastika_flag_Nazi_Germany_propaganda_photo_%28Frihedsmuseet_Nationalmuseet_Denmark%29_No_known_rights.jpg/1280px-FHM-282370_Deutsche_Arbeitsfront_member_paramilitary_uniform_DAF_swastika_flag_Nazi_Germany_propaganda_photo_%28Frihedsmuseet_Nationalmuseet_Denmark%29_No_known_rights.jpg","title":"A German Labour Front man and the Front's flag","desc":"A propaganda photograph of a member of the German Labour Front in uniform beside the organisation's flag, which set a swastika inside a cogwheel.","credit":"Frihedsmuseet, Nationalmuseet, Denmark, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FHM-282370_Deutsche_Arbeitsfront_member_paramilitary_uniform_DAF_swastika_flag_Nazi_Germany_propaganda_photo_%28Frihedsmuseet_Nationalmuseet_Denmark%29_No_known_rights.jpg","alt":"A uniformed man standing on a roof edge beside a flagpole flying a flag with a swastika inside a cogwheel."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the unions already weak when they were destroyed?","a":"Because the slump and years of mass unemployment had worn down the labour movement’s cohesion and morale and left them badly destabilised beforehand."},{"q":"Why was the new body not a union in any sense?","a":"Because it did not settle wages, tariffs or contracts, which were decided by trustees for labour appointed by the Reich labour ministry."},{"q":"Why did it run holidays and concerts as well?","a":"Because the leisure programme reached into workers’ free time, so the organisation covered what the old unions had not as well as replacing what they had."}],"wiki":"German Labour Front"},
    "ww2-059": {"questions":["Rumours of a planned coup were spread before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that the killings could be presented to Germans as the defence of the state against a second revolution.","With the militia leadership dead after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the elite guard rose in its place and took control of the German police."],"abstract":"The <b>Night of the Long Knives</b> was the purge of the party’s own paramilitary leadership at the end of June 1934, carried out by shooting rather than by any legal process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rumours of a coup planned by the militia had been spread beforehand on Hitler’s instructions, so that the operation, codenamed Hummingbird, could be presented afterwards as a defence of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The day after the purge the propaganda minister announced that Ernst Röhm and the former chancellor Kurt von Schleicher had been plotting a second revolution, which the party had thwarted and so saved the country from chaos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party press dwelt on the private life of the militia chief and his circle, and told readers that they no longer presented a moral danger to the German people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 July a law on measures of state self-defence declared the murders lawful after the event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The party newspaper of that day carried pledges of loyalty from across the country, a message from the president thanking the leader for saving Germany from grave danger, and a report of the impression the cleansing action had made abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The militia had been the movement’s street army since 1921 and had grown far larger than the regular forces, whose officers wanted it cut down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With its leadership dead the elite guard rose in its place and took control of the police, which turned the state into a police state in earnest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The killings settled that authority ran downward from one man and was not to be questioned, which was the principle the regime now called its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hindenburg died the following month, and the offices of president and chancellor were merged in the man the purge had confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Völkischer Beobachter Justifies the Purge in Response to the ‘Röhm Putsch’ (July 3, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/the-front-page-of-the-voelkischer-beobachter-justifies-the-purge-in-response-to-the-so-called-roehm-putsch-july-3-1934. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14886%2C_Kurt_Daluege%2C_Heinrich_Himmler%2C_Ernst_R%C3%B6hm.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14886%2C_Kurt_Daluege%2C_Heinrich_Himmler%2C_Ernst_R%C3%B6hm.jpg","title":"Daluege, Himmler and Röhm, 1933","desc":"The police chief Kurt Daluege, the head of the elite guard Heinrich Himmler and the militia chief Ernst Röhm together in August 1933, less than a year before the purge in which Röhm was shot.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-14886, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14886,_Kurt_Daluege,_Heinrich_Himmler,_Ernst_R%C3%B6hm.jpg","alt":"Three uniformed men standing close together outdoors, the nearest wearing an Iron Cross."},"why":[{"q":"Why were rumours of a coup spread in advance?","a":"Because it let the operation be presented afterwards as a defence of the state rather than as a settling of accounts within the party."},{"q":"Why did a law have to be passed after the killings?","a":"Because they had been carried out by shooting rather than by any legal process, so a law of 3 July declared them lawful after the event."},{"q":"Why did the purge change the shape of the state?","a":"Because with the militia’s leadership dead the elite guard rose in its place and took control of the police, which made the state a police state in earnest."}],"wiki":"Night of the Long Knives"},
    "ww2-060": {"questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the armed forces swore their oath of loyalty to Hitler personally, after the two highest offices were merged on the president’s death in August 1934.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been practised inside the movement for years before it became the working rule of the German state after 1933."],"abstract":"The <b>Führerprinzip</b>, ‘the leader principle’, held that authority flowed downward from Hitler through government, party, economy and family, and was to be obeyed without question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a metaphor but the working rule of the state, since the leader’s will became the foundation of all legislation once the parliament had given up its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parliament had done so in March 1933, when it transferred the making of laws to the cabinet, and it thereafter met only to applaud what had been decided elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Hindenburg died in August 1934 the offices of president and chancellor were merged and Hitler took the title of leader and Reich chancellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The armed forces then swore their oath of loyalty to him personally in that capacity, rather than to the constitution or the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> As chancellor his power was still limited by the laws of the German state; as leader it was held to be unlimited and identical with the destiny of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Party officials, Hitler Youth members and labour service leaders had already sworn oaths of their own in February 1934, read out in Munich and broadcast across the country so that the apparatus was sworn to one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The principle had been practised in the movement long before it was practised in the state, since the party had demanded submission to a charismatic leader from its early years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had also been advertised: the party told voters for a decade that it wanted authority rather than debate, and once in office it kept that promise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the principle produced was a state in which no office had a standing of its own, and every institution answered upward to a single will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-16108A%2C_Vereidigung_von_Reichswehr-Soldaten_auf_Hitler.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-16108A%2C_Vereidigung_von_Reichswehr-Soldaten_auf_Hitler.jpg","title":"The army's oath, August 1934","desc":"Soldiers of the Reichswehr swearing the oath of loyalty to Hitler personally in August 1934, after the offices of president and chancellor were combined.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-16108A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-16108A,_Vereidigung_von_Reichswehr-Soldaten_auf_Hitler.jpg","alt":"A rank of helmeted soldiers in greatcoats standing with their right hands raised."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the principle a working rule rather than a slogan?","a":"Because the leader’s will became the foundation of all legislation once the parliament had transferred the making of laws to the cabinet."},{"q":"Why did the two titles differ in what they permitted?","a":"Because as chancellor his power was still limited by the laws of the German state, while as leader it was held to be unlimited and identical with the destiny of the nation."},{"q":"Why did the oaths matter as much as the offices?","a":"Because the armed forces swore loyalty to him personally rather than to the constitution or the state, as party and youth officials had already done."}],"wiki":"Führerprinzip"},
    "ww2-061": {"questions":["Candidates for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were admitted only after tests of ancestry and political reliability, and its members needed permission to marry.","For shooting the party militia’s leadership in June 1934 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made an independent organisation answerable to Hitler alone."],"abstract":"The <b>Schutzstaffel</b>, ‘the protection squads’, began in 1925 as a small bodyguard for Hitler and ended as the organisation that ran the police and the camps of the German state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was at first subordinate to the party militia, and its duties were guarding leaders and meetings and selling subscriptions to the party newspaper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In January 1929, when it numbered 280 men, Heinrich Himmler was made its Reich leader, and he rebuilt it by admitting candidates only on tests of ancestry and political reliability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An intelligence branch followed in 1931, which from August 1934 was the only body allowed to gather political intelligence in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An office set up under a marriage decree came the same year to vet the ancestry of members’ brides, and the expertise it built was later used to decide who counted as German in occupied Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It had more than 52,000 members when the party took power, and for shooting the militia’s leadership in June 1934 it was made an independent organisation answerable to Hitler alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chief had already been given the Prussian political police that April, which let him gather the whole political police apparatus of Germany into one hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1936 he was chief of every police force in the country, and he told the Academy of German Law that law was a matter of higher principles rather than of codes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organisation ran the concentration camps, whose guards it trained and whose regime it set, and it grew into something like a state inside the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The political police it absorbed was joined to its own intelligence service in September 1939 in a single Reich Security Main Office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","\"Hermann Göring, Chief of the State Secret Police Office, Names Heinrich Himmler Deputy Chief and Inspector of the Prussian Political Police (April 20, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hermann-goering-chief-of-the-state-secret-police-office-names-heinrich-himmler-deputy-chief-and-inspector-of-the-prussian-political-police-april-20-1934. [Open access]","\"Himmler Speech at the Academy of German Law (October 11, 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/himmler-speech-at-the-academy-of-german-law-october-11-1936. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gestapo,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gestapo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17312%2C_Berlin-Lichterfelde%2C_Hitler_bei_Leibstandarte.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17312%2C_Berlin-Lichterfelde%2C_Hitler_bei_Leibstandarte.jpg","title":"Hitler reviews his guard regiment, December 1935","desc":"Hitler inspecting the SS bodyguard regiment at the former cadet school in Berlin-Lichterfelde, ten years after the organisation began as a small escort.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-17312, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17312,_Berlin-Lichterfelde,_Hitler_bei_Leibstandarte.jpg","alt":"Steel-helmeted troops with shouldered rifles marching past a saluting figure on snow-covered ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a bodyguard of 280 men end up running Germany's police?","a":"Because Himmler took over in January 1929 and rebuilt it on tests of ancestry and political reliability, then was given the Prussian political police in April 1934, which let him gather the whole political police apparatus of Germany into one hand."},{"q":"Why was it made independent of the party militia in June 1934?","a":"It was rewarded for shooting the militia's own leadership. Having killed its parent organisation for Hitler, it was detached from it and made answerable to Hitler alone."},{"q":"Why did an office set up to vet brides matter beyond marriage?","a":"Vetting members' brides under a marriage decree built expertise in judging ancestry, and that expertise was later used to decide who counted as German in occupied Europe."}],"wiki":"Schutzstaffel"},
    "ww2-062": {"questions":["Too small to watch a whole nation, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> depended on denunciations offered by neighbours and colleagues out of belief, spite or self-interest.","The emergency decree of February 1933 freed the police who became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to open mail, tap telephones and search houses without any warrant."],"abstract":"The <b>Gestapo</b>, short for ‘the secret state police’, was the political police of Nazi Germany, and the instrument through which the regime hunted the people it counted as enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political policing was not new: the Weimar Republic had political police in each of its states, but they answered to state and local governments and were bound by a constitution guaranteeing privacy and free speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emergency decree that followed the Reichstag fire suspended those protections, so that the police could open mail, listen to telephone calls and search houses without a warrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Centralising the force took several years, and it was only by the end of 1936 that a single political police under Himmler covered the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That summer it was joined with the criminal police in a security police under Reinhard Heydrich, who also ran the party’s own intelligence service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its most feared power was protective custody, which allowed it to send a person straight to a concentration camp with no lawyer, no appeal and no trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A law of December 1934 made criticism of the party or the regime a crime, so that telling a joke about Hitler could bring an arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its agents were mostly trained detectives who had served before 1933, joined by ideologues brought in from the intelligence service with no police training at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was too small to watch a nation and did not try, relying instead on denunciations from neighbours, colleagues and relatives, offered out of belief, spite or self-interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1939 it became one office of a new Reich Security Main Office under the elite guard, and the state it had helped build no longer troubled to call its power lawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gestapo,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gestapo. [Open access]","\"Hermann Göring, Chief of the State Secret Police Office, Names Heinrich Himmler Deputy Chief and Inspector of the Prussian Political Police (April 20, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hermann-goering-chief-of-the-state-secret-police-office-names-heinrich-himmler-deputy-chief-and-inspector-of-the-prussian-political-police-april-20-1934. [Open access]","\"Himmler Speech at the Academy of German Law (October 11, 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/himmler-speech-at-the-academy-of-german-law-october-11-1936. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183_R97512%2C_Berlin%2C_Geheimes_Staatspolizeihauptamt.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183_R97512%2C_Berlin%2C_Geheimes_Staatspolizeihauptamt.jpg","title":"The Secret State Police headquarters, Berlin","desc":"The building at 8 Prinz-Albrecht-Strasse that housed the Gestapo's main office, photographed before the war.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183 R97512, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183_R97512,_Berlin,_Geheimes_Staatspolizeihauptamt.jpg","alt":"A large stone office building on a corner behind railings, with a car parked on the empty street."},"why":[{"q":"Why was political policing itself not the novelty here?","a":"The Weimar Republic already had political police in each of its states. What changed was that they answered to state and local governments and were bound by a constitution guaranteeing privacy and free speech, and those protections were suspended."},{"q":"Why did the emergency decree after the Reichstag fire matter so much?","a":"It suspended the constitutional protections, so the police could open mail, listen to telephone calls and search houses without a warrant. The machinery already existed; the decree removed what restrained it."},{"q":"Why did a force too small to watch a nation still terrify one?","a":"It did not try to watch everybody, relying instead on denunciations from neighbours, colleagues and relatives offered out of belief, spite or self-interest. Anyone might inform, so the surveillance was supplied by the population."}],"wiki":"Gestapo"},
    "ww2-063": {"questions":["Anyone could be sent to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the political police under protective custody, with no charge, no trial and no appeal against the order.","Punishment by humiliation, violence and forced labour, not reform, was the purpose of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the beginning, and deterrence of everyone outside."],"abstract":"The first Nazi <b>concentration camp</b> system was improvised in the spring of 1933 to hold the thousands of people arrested as political opponents after the party took power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The militia and the police set the earliest camps up locally, all over Germany, wherever a disused factory, castle or barracks could be turned to the purpose, and there were many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dachau, on the grounds of an abandoned munitions works near Munich, opened in March 1933 and took its first prisoners on the twenty-second of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Himmler, then police president of Munich, described it publicly as the first camp for political prisoners, and it was built for five thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its inmates at first were Communists, Social Democrats, trade unionists and other opponents of the new government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The purpose was never reform but punishment by daily humiliation, arbitrary violence and forced labour, and beyond that the deterrence of everybody outside the wire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In October 1933 Dachau’s commandant Theodor Eicke issued regulations prescribing brutal punishments for the smallest offence, and when he was made inspector of the whole system he imposed them everywhere and made his camp the training school for its guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the early local camps were closed and replaced by a smaller number run centrally by the elite guard, whose death’s head units supplied the guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The political police could send anyone into this system by protective custody, without charge or trial, which is what made the camps a standing threat rather than a punishment for crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dachau was the only camp opened in 1933 that lasted to 1945, and it became the model on which the rest were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dachau,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dachau. [Open access]","\"Employment of Prisoners at the Oranienburg Protective Custody Camp near Berlin (April 1, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/employment-of-prisoners-at-the-oranienburg-protective-custody-camp-near-berlin-april-1-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gestapo,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gestapo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Bundesarchiv_Bild_152-01-14%2C_Dachau%2C_Konzentrationslager_1933.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_152-01-14%2C_Dachau%2C_Konzentrationslager_1933.jpg","title":"Prisoners at Dachau, May 1933","desc":"Inmates eating in the mess hall of the Dachau protective-custody camp two months after it opened, in a photograph taken for the regime.","credit":"Photograph by Friedrich Franz Bauer. Bundesarchiv, Bild 152-01-14, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_152-01-14,_Dachau,_Konzentrationslager_1933.jpg","alt":"Rows of men in light uniforms sitting at long trestle tables eating from bowls in a barrack hall."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the earliest camps improvised and scattered across Germany?","a":"The militia and the police set them up locally in the spring of 1933 to hold the thousands arrested after the party took power, using whatever disused factory, castle or barracks could be turned to the purpose."},{"q":"Why did Dachau become the model for the whole system?","a":"Its commandant Theodor Eicke issued regulations in October 1933 prescribing brutal punishments for the smallest offence, and when he was made inspector of the whole system he imposed them everywhere and made his camp the training school for its guards."},{"q":"Why were the camps a standing threat rather than a punishment for crimes?","a":"The political police could send anyone into them by protective custody, without charge or trial. The purpose was never reform but punishment and, beyond that, the deterrence of everybody outside the wire."}],"wiki":"Concentration camp"},
    "ww2-064": {"questions":["The cheap receiver mass-produced under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to bring the poorest German households within reach of the regime’s broadcasts.","Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> presented each new restriction as the government restoring order, much of it aimed at public passivity rather than at enthusiasm."],"abstract":"<b>Nazi propaganda</b> was the regime’s effort to carry its message through every channel a modern state could reach, and it worked on Germans first in a democracy and then in a dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda was set up in March 1933 under Joseph Goebbels, with art, music, theatre, film, books, radio, schoolbooks and the press in its charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its minister told the German press that his object was the spiritual mobilisation of the population, and that achieving it would amount to a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He treated radio as the most important instrument he had, dissolved the independent broadcasters and had a cheap receiver mass-produced so that the poorest households could be reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The images it used were not invented: the stereotypes of Jews it printed were already familiar to the audience it was aimed at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Campaigns were timed to prepare the ground for measures rather than to explain them afterwards, running hard in 1935 before the race laws of September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same was done in 1938 before the wave of economic laws that followed the November pogrom, so that each new restriction met a public already prepared for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of it aimed at passivity rather than enthusiasm, presenting each measure against Jews as the government stepping in to restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Abroad it argued that German demands for territory were reasonable, and at home it dwelt on the treatment of German minorities in the states that had gained land in 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the invasion of the Soviet Union it fused communism and Jewry into one enemy, and that theme may have helped Germans and their auxiliaries fight to the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Propaganda,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-propaganda. [Open access]","\"Joseph Goebbels, Two Speeches on the Tasks of the Reich Ministry for Popular Enlightenment and Propaganda (March 15, March 25, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/joseph-goebbels-two-speeches-on-the-tasks-of-the-reich-ministry-for-popular-enlightenment-and-propaganda-march-15-march-25-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nuremberg Race Laws,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nuremberg-race-laws. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Volksempf%C3%A4nger_VE_301_Dyn%2C_NG-2009-110.jpg/1280px-Volksempf%C3%A4nger_VE_301_Dyn%2C_NG-2009-110.jpg","title":"The People's Receiver","desc":"The cheap radio set mass-produced from 1933 so that the poorest German households could be reached by the ministry's broadcasts.","credit":"Rijksmuseum, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volksempf%C3%A4nger_VE_301_Dyn,_NG-2009-110.jpg","alt":"A small bakelite radio with a fabric-covered speaker, a tuning dial and four knobs."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels treat radio as his most important instrument?","a":"It reached into the home directly, so he dissolved the independent broadcasters and had a cheap receiver mass-produced in order that even the poorest households could be reached."},{"q":"Why were campaigns run before new measures rather than after them?","a":"They were meant to prepare the ground rather than explain a decision afterwards, running hard in 1935 before the race laws of September and again in 1938 before the economic laws that followed the November pogrom, so each restriction met a prepared public."},{"q":"Why aim at passivity rather than at enthusiasm?","a":"Presenting each measure against Jews as the government stepping in to restore order asked for acquiescence rather than participation, which is a far lower bar and enough for the measures to proceed unopposed."}],"wiki":"Propaganda in Nazi Germany"},
    "ww2-065": {"questions":["The nationwide anti-Jewish riot of November 1938 was coordinated by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Hitler’s approval and sold to the world as spontaneous popular anger.","Rejected for war service and holding a doctorate in German literature, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose through the party as the organiser of its Berlin branch."],"abstract":"<b>Joseph Goebbels</b> ran the propaganda of the Nazi movement and then of the German state, and was among the most radical antisemites in its leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in the Rhineland in 1897 to a strict Catholic family, he was rejected for war service because a club foot hindered his walking, and took a doctorate in German literature at <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Heidelberg_University\">Heidelberg</span> in 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the party in 1924, edited one of its papers, and in 1926 was made its chief in Greater Berlin, where he built its organisation from almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ran his own Berlin newspaper from 1927, under the motto that it spoke for the oppressed against the exploiters, entered the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Reichstag_(Weimar_Republic)\">Reichstag</span> in 1928, and became the party’s head of propaganda in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Berlin he marched the militia into working-class districts held by the left, and made heroes of those killed in the street fighting that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A song named after one such man became the party’s anthem, the movement claimed seventy fallen in the first 8 months of 1932 alone, and its Berlin militia grew from 450 men in 1926 to some 32,000 by January 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Made Reich minister for propaganda in 1933, he was charged with bringing the press, art, film, music and literature into line with the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He dissolved the independent broadcasters, put a cheap receiver into ordinary homes, and told editors that his aim was the spiritual mobilisation of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In November 1938 he coordinated the nationwide anti-Jewish riot with Hitler’s approval, while presenting it to the world as spontaneous popular anger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He kept both his party and his ministerial offices to the end, an unconditional follower of a leader whose authority he had spent 20 years advertising, and on 1 May 1945, in Hitler's headquarters bunker in Berlin, he and his wife Magda took their own lives after arranging the poisoning of their six children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Propaganda,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-propaganda. [Open access]","\"Joseph Goebbels, Two Speeches on the Tasks of the Reich Ministry for Popular Enlightenment and Propaganda (March 15, March 25, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/joseph-goebbels-two-speeches-on-the-tasks-of-the-reich-ministry-for-popular-enlightenment-and-propaganda-march-15-march-25-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","Susanne Eckelmann, “Joseph Goebbels 1897–1945,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2023), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/joseph-goebbels. [in German] [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17049%2C_Joseph_Goebbels_spricht.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17049%2C_Joseph_Goebbels_spricht.jpg","title":"Goebbels speaking, August 1934","desc":"The Reich propaganda minister in mid-speech, photographed in Berlin in August 1934.","credit":"Photograph by Georg Pahl. Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-17049, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17049,_Joseph_Goebbels_spricht.jpg","alt":"A man in uniform and peaked cap shouting with his right index finger raised."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels make heroes of men killed in street fighting?","a":"Marching the militia into working-class districts held by the left produced casualties, and the movement claimed seventy fallen in the first 8 months of 1932 alone. A song named after one of them became the party's anthem, so the dead served as recruitment."},{"q":"Why was a doctorate in German literature useful to a propaganda minister?","a":"His ministry was charged with bringing the press, art, film, music and literature into line with the regime, so the culture he was remaking was the one he had been trained to study."},{"q":"Why present the November 1938 riot as spontaneous popular anger?","a":"He had coordinated it nationwide with Hitler's approval, and calling it spontaneous let the regime disclaim responsibility while presenting the violence as what Germans already felt rather than what the party had ordered."}],"wiki":"Joseph Goebbels"},
    "ww2-066": {"questions":["The students of 1933 fed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with works by Jewish authors, pacifist writing that criticised war, and books praising socialism or communism.","Officials of the party attended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without having organised it, which made plain to everyone that the students acted with the regime’s approval."],"abstract":"The Nazi <b>book burning</b> of May 1933 destroyed tens of thousands of volumes in bonfires in more than twenty university towns and cities across Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not organised by the government: the students of the national union of student bodies arranged it under the name of an action against the un-German spirit, and the party’s own student group already controlled that union and its chapters at universities across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local and national officials attended all the same, and their presence made plain that the students had the regime’s approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was burnt was whatever the students called un-German: works by Jewish authors, pacifist writing that criticised war, and books praising socialism or communism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the burning in Berlin on 10 May a banner read that German students were marching against the un-German spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Remarque’s novel of the Western Front was a favourite target, condemned for the pacifism of its account of the trenches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German students had been among the movement’s earliest supporters, and secular middle-class student clubs had long been ultra-nationalist and antisemitic, many of them hostile to the Weimar Republic since the end of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The burnings were neither the first nor the last: a party branch in Kaiserslautern had burnt Remarque in March, the youth organisation staged its own at schools, and more followed into the autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stood at the head of a wider effort to remake German culture, which within months put every writer, musician and artist under a state chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The propaganda ministry founded that spring took charge of that effort, and the man who ran it had himself taken a doctorate in German literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Book Burning,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning. [Open access]","\"Book Burnings Across Germany (1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/book-burnings-across-germany-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Propaganda,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-propaganda. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597%2C_Berlin%2C_Opernplatz%2C_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597%2C_Berlin%2C_Opernplatz%2C_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg","title":"The bonfire on the Opernplatz, May 1933","desc":"Students and onlookers saluting round the burning heap of books on the Opernplatz in Berlin during the burnings of May 1933.","credit":"Photograph by Georg Pahl. Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-14597, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597,_Berlin,_Opernplatz,_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg","alt":"A night crowd with arms raised in salute around a wide bonfire of scattered books and papers."},"why":[{"q":"Why did students rather than the government organise the burnings?","a":"The national union of student bodies arranged them under the name of an action against the un-German spirit, and the party's own student group already controlled that union and its chapters, so the party acted through the students."},{"q":"Why did officials attend an event they had not organised?","a":"Their presence made plain that the students had the regime's approval, which turned a student action into a public statement of government policy without the government having to order it."},{"q":"Why were German students among the movement's earliest supporters?","a":"Secular middle-class student clubs had long been ultra-nationalist and antisemitic, many of them hostile to the Weimar Republic since the end of the First World War, so the ground was prepared well before 1933."}],"wiki":"Book burning"},
    "ww2-067": {"questions":["Because private clubs and associations adopted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of their own accord, a man could be expelled from a choir or a sports club by a rule nobody had imposed.","The race laws of 1935 rebuilt the exclusions made under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on descent rather than religion, and gave them one legal definition to work from."],"abstract":"The <b>Aryan Paragraph</b> was the clause that excluded Jews from an office, a profession or an association, and it was the device by which Germany was closed to its Jews before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany’s Jews numbered about 525,000 in 1933, under 1% of the population, and most of them were middle-class townspeople who considered themselves German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first coordinated action against them was a boycott on 1 April 1933, when Germans were told not to shop in Jewish businesses or visit Jewish doctors and lawyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 6 days later a law on the civil service excluded Jews and the politically unreliable from public employment, and it carried the first such clause in national law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached every rank of administration and judges, teachers, university professors and lawyers with it, and the formula was then copied outwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That April a quota capped Jewish pupils and students, decrees cut Jewish work in medicine and law, and by 1934 Jewish actors could not appear on stage or screen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than four hundred restrictions followed in 6 years, some national and many issued by state, regional and town officials acting on their own initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Private associations adopted the clause voluntarily, so that a man could be expelled from a choir or a sports club by a rule nobody had ordered him to be expelled under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The race laws of 1935 rebuilt the whole scheme on descent rather than religion, and gave the exclusions a single legal definition to work from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1938 Jews were being registered, renamed and stamped, and after the November pogrom they were barred from schools, cinemas, theatres and sports grounds outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Boycott of Jewish Businesses,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-boycott-of-jewish-businesses. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jews in Prewar Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jews-in-prewar-germany. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nuremberg Race Laws,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nuremberg-race-laws. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Book Burning,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Judenboykott_1933-04-01_Berlin_Anti-Semitic_Anti-Jewish_Nazi_boycott_of_Jewish_businesses_shops_in_Germany_SA_brownshirt_Poster_warning_sign_window_display_lace_and_crochet_shop_Kauft_nicht_bei_Juden_No_known_copyright.jpg/1280px-Judenboykott_1933-04-01_Berlin_Anti-Semitic_Anti-Jewish_Nazi_boycott_of_Jewish_businesses_shops_in_Germany_SA_brownshirt_Poster_warning_sign_window_display_lace_and_crochet_shop_Kauft_nicht_bei_Juden_No_known_copyright.jpg","title":"The boycott of 1 April 1933","desc":"A militia man posted outside a Berlin lace shop under placards in German and English telling Germans to buy only at German shops.","credit":"Photograph attributed to Georg Pahl. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judenboykott_1933-04-01_Berlin_Anti-Semitic_Anti-Jewish_Nazi_boycott_of_Jewish_businesses_shops_in_Germany_SA_brownshirt_Poster_warning_sign_window_display_lace_and_crochet_shop_Kauft_nicht_bei_Juden_No_known_copyright.jpg","alt":"A uniformed man standing before a shop window hung with lace, beside large printed placards."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the clause spread so far beyond the civil service law?","a":"The formula proved copyable: a quota capped Jewish pupils that same April, decrees cut Jewish work in medicine and law, and more than four hundred restrictions followed in 6 years, many issued by state, regional and town officials on their own initiative."},{"q":"Why did private associations adopt a clause nobody had ordered?","a":"They took it up voluntarily, so a man could be expelled from a choir or a sports club under a rule the state had never imposed. Exclusion spread by imitation as well as by decree."},{"q":"Why did the 1935 race laws change what the paragraph rested on?","a":"They rebuilt the whole scheme on descent rather than religion, which gave the scattered exclusions a single legal definition to work from instead of leaving each office to decide who was Jewish."}],"wiki":"Aryan paragraph"},
    "ww2-068": {"questions":["A person with three or four Jewish grandparents was a Jew under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a definition that rested on the religious records of the grandparents.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made citizenship a condition of many professions and courses, they were used to push Jews out of offices and universities."],"abstract":"The <b>Nuremberg Laws</b> of 15 September 1935 were two measures announced at the party’s rally in Nuremberg that made the exclusion of Jews from German life a matter of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, a citizenship law, reserved citizenship to those of German or related blood, which made good a promise the party had printed in its programme in 1920, so that Jews became subjects with no political rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second, the law for the protection of German blood and honour, forbade marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans on pain of severe punishment, and was meant to isolate them socially as well as racially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also barred Jewish households from employing German women under 45 as servants, and barred Jews from flying the new national flag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither law said who a Jew was, and the whole scheme needed that question answered before it could be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A regulation of 14 November supplied the answer: a person with three or four Jewish grandparents was a Jew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grandparent counted as Jewish if they had belonged to the Jewish religious community, so the racial law rested in the end on church and synagogue records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those with one or two such grandparents were put in a middle category, neither German nor Jewish in the law’s terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans were then required to prove their ancestry with baptism certificates, community registers and gravestones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the definition settled, the regime used it to push Jews out of professions, offices and courses of study that citizenship had been made to require: judges could no longer cite commentaries by Jewish authors, Jewish officers were expelled from the army, and Jewish students were barred from doctoral examinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nuremberg Race Laws,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nuremberg-race-laws. [Open access]","\"The Reich Citizenship Law (September 15, 1935) and the First Regulation to the Reich Citizenship Law (November 14, 1935),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/the-reich-citizenship-law-september-15-1935-and-the-first-regulation-to-the-reich-citizenship-law-november-14-1935. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jews in Prewar Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jews-in-prewar-germany. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Book Burning,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg/1280px-Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg","title":"The official chart of the race laws","desc":"The government's own chart of 1935 setting out the categories the laws created, from German-blooded through two grades of mixed descent to Jew, by the religion of the grandparents.","credit":"German government chart, 1935. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg","alt":"A printed chart headed Die Nürnberger Gesetze with five columns of white, half-shaded and black circles representing grandparents."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the two laws need a further regulation before they could work?","a":"Neither said who a Jew was, and the whole scheme depended on that question. A regulation of 14 November supplied the answer: three or four Jewish grandparents made a person a Jew."},{"q":"Why did a racial law end up resting on church and synagogue records?","a":"A grandparent counted as Jewish if they had belonged to the Jewish religious community, so ancestry could only be established from religious registers. Germans were then required to prove theirs with baptism certificates and gravestones."},{"q":"Why reserve citizenship to those of German or related blood?","a":"It made good a promise the party had printed in its programme in 1920, and turning Jews into subjects with no political rights supplied the legal footing for pushing them out of professions and offices that citizenship was made to require."}],"wiki":"Nuremberg Laws"},
    "ww2-069": {"questions":["About 26,000 Jewish men were imprisoned in concentration camps after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for no reason at all beyond the fact that they were Jews.","The Jewish community was fined a billion marks in an atonement payment after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on top of everything the rioters had destroyed."],"abstract":"<b>Kristallnacht</b>, ‘the night of broken glass’, was the anti-Jewish riot that ran through Germany, Austria and the annexed Sudetenland on 9 and 10 November 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 1,400 <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Synagogue\">synagogues</span> were burnt, thousands of Jewish businesses and homes were wrecked and looted, and Jews were humiliated, assaulted and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The riot was meant to look like a spontaneous outburst of popular anger, and was in fact coordinated by the propaganda minister with Hitler’s approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its pretext was the shooting of a German diplomat in Paris on 7 November by a 17-year-old whose parents were among 17,000 Polish Jews the police had expelled 10 days before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The expelled had been left stranded in the strip of land between the two frontiers, where they lived in wretched conditions for weeks, and news of their plight is what moved him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The perpetrators were party officials, the militia, the elite guard and the youth organisation, joined by ordinary Germans who stole from the wrecked houses and made a public show of humiliating their Jewish neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The police then imprisoned about 26,000 Jewish men in concentration camps for no reason but that they were Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost 11,000 of them were sent to <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Dachau_concentration_camp\">Dachau</span>, and most were released within weeks or months, many after proving they had arranged to emigrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The regime then fined the Jewish community a billion marks in what it called an atonement payment, and followed it with a barrage of laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German officials called it an action; Jews called it a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pogrom\">pogrom</span>, the Russian word for the anti-Jewish violence of the previous century, and the fiction of spontaneity did not survive contact with the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]","\"American Consul Samuel Honaker’s Description of Antisemitic Persecution and of Kristallnacht and its Aftereffects in the Stuttgart Region (November 1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/american-consul-samuel-honakers-description-of-antisemitic-persecution-and-kristallnacht-and-its-aftereffects-in-the-stuttgart-region-november-12-and-november-15-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dachau,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dachau. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/The_day_after_Kristallnacht.jpg/1280px-The_day_after_Kristallnacht.jpg","title":"The morning after, 10 November 1938","desc":"Passers-by in a German street looking away from a Jewish-owned shop whose window was smashed during the night.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_day_after_Kristallnacht.jpg","alt":"A shop front with a large jagged hole in its plate-glass window, with several people walking past."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the riot need to look spontaneous?","a":"It was coordinated by the propaganda minister with Hitler's approval, and calling it popular anger let the regime present the violence as what Germans felt rather than what it had ordered. The fiction did not survive contact with the world."},{"q":"Why did a shooting in Paris serve as the pretext?","a":"The 17-year-old who shot the diplomat had parents among 17,000 Polish Jews the police had expelled 10 days before and left stranded between the frontiers, so the deed the regime used as its excuse was a response to its own expulsion."},{"q":"Why fine the victims a billion marks after the riot?","a":"The regime called it an atonement payment, which made the community pay for the destruction of its own property and turned a night of violence into a transfer of wealth backed by a barrage of laws."}],"wiki":"Kristallnacht"},
    "ww2-070": {"questions":["Roosevelt called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but sent a businessman friend rather than a senior official, which set the weight of the meeting before it opened.","The German government answered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with satisfaction that its critics abroad would not open their own doors to the refugees."],"abstract":"The <b>Evian Conference</b> of July 1938 brought delegates from 32 countries to a resort on the French shore of Lake Geneva to discuss the refugees fleeing Nazi persecution, and it settled almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German policy at that stage was to make Jewish life impossible rather than to kill, so that Jews would leave, and by 1938 about 250,000 had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leaving meant finding a country that would take you, and that was the difficulty the conference had been called to address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American president called it but sent a businessman friend rather than a senior official, which set its weight before it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over 9 days delegate after delegate expressed sympathy for the refugees and then explained why his own country could admit no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Only the Dominican Republic offered to take a substantial number, and the meeting’s one lasting product was an intergovernmental committee that would go on working at the problem without the power to solve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German government replied with satisfaction that foreign states criticised its treatment of Jews and would not open their own doors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American quotas fixed in 1924 stayed in place, defended by people who feared competition for work in a depression and by officials who were themselves antisemitic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bill to admit 20,000 refugee children failed in the American legislature the following year, and a liner carrying nearly a thousand refugees was turned away from Cuba and then from the United States in 1939, and had to sail back to Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 4 months after the conference the pogrom of November showed the world exactly what the refugees were fleeing, and the doors stayed shut all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Evian Conference,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-evian-conference. [Open access]","\"American Consul Samuel Honaker’s Description of Antisemitic Persecution and of Kristallnacht and its Aftereffects in the Stuttgart Region (November 1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/american-consul-samuel-honakers-description-of-antisemitic-persecution-and-kristallnacht-and-its-aftereffects-in-the-stuttgart-region-november-12-and-november-15-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Voyage of the St. Louis,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/voyage-of-the-st-louis-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jews in Prewar Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jews-in-prewar-germany. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Myron_Taylor_addresses_the_%C3%89vian_Conference.jpg/1280px-Myron_Taylor_addresses_the_%C3%89vian_Conference.jpg","title":"The American delegate speaks at Évian, July 1938","desc":"Myron Taylor addressing the international conference on refugees at Évian-les-Bains, with the other delegations seated around him.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myron_Taylor_addresses_the_%C3%89vian_Conference.jpg","alt":"A man in a dark suit standing to read from papers at a long table, with seated delegates around him."},"why":[{"q":"Why was finding a country to take them the whole difficulty?","a":"German policy at that stage was to make Jewish life impossible rather than to kill, so that Jews would leave. Leaving required somewhere to go, and about 250,000 had already gone by 1938."},{"q":"Why did sending a businessman rather than an official matter?","a":"The American president had called the conference and then sent a friend from business instead of a senior official, which set its weight before it opened and signalled that no country need expect pressure to raise its limits."},{"q":"Why did the German government welcome the outcome?","a":"It replied with satisfaction that foreign states criticised its treatment of Jews and would not open their own doors, which let it answer criticism by pointing at the refusals of its critics."}],"wiki":"Évian Conference"},
    "ww2-071": {"questions":["Britain, France, Belgium and the Netherlands took in the passengers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but three of those four countries were overrun by Germany the next year.","10 months after the Évian conference had declined to raise any refugee limits, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to carry its passengers back across the Atlantic."],"abstract":"The <b>Saint Louis</b> was the German liner that carried almost a thousand Jewish refugees out of Hamburg in May 1939 and was made to bring them back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She sailed from Hamburg on 13 May 1939 with Cuba as her destination, carrying passengers who held landing permits bought in good faith and who expected to wait there for American visas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before she arrived the Cuban government revoked those permits, and she lay off Havana for days while a handful of passengers were let ashore and the rest were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States refused them as well, and in June she turned back across the Atlantic towards the continent they had paid to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, France, Belgium and the Netherlands then agreed between them to divide the stranded passengers up and take them in, which is why the voyage is remembered as a rescue at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That looked like a rescue and was only half of one, since three of those four countries were overrun by German forces the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the passengers put ashore in western Europe were caught there when the German army arrived and were murdered in the killing programme that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The voyage happened 10 months after 32 governments had met at Évian and declined, almost without exception, to raise their limits on refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the passengers were fleeing was already plain: a country where Jews had been stripped of citizenship, robbed of their businesses and imprisoned in camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ship became the standing illustration of a refugee crisis in which the danger was obvious, the escape route existed and had been paid for, and the doors were shut anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Voyage of the St. Louis,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/voyage-of-the-st-louis-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Evian Conference,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-evian-conference. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Euthanasia Program,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82-%D0%9B%D1%83%D0%B8%D1%81%C2%BB_%282%29.jpg/1280px-%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82-%D0%9B%D1%83%D0%B8%D1%81%C2%BB_%282%29.jpg","title":"Passengers on the Saint Louis, 1939","desc":"A group of the ship's Jewish passengers photographed on deck during the voyage, beside a lifebuoy carrying her name.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82-%D0%9B%D1%83%D0%B8%D1%81%C2%BB_%282%29.jpg","alt":"Eleven men and women in summer clothes posed on a ship's deck behind a lifebuoy marked St. Louis."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the voyage remembered as only half a rescue?","a":"Britain, France, Belgium and the Netherlands divided the stranded passengers between them, but three of those four countries were overrun the following year, and many of those put ashore in western Europe were caught there and murdered."},{"q":"Why did the passengers hold permits that turned out to be worthless?","a":"They had bought Cuban landing permits in good faith and expected to wait there for American visas, but the Cuban government revoked the permits before the ship arrived and the United States then refused them as well."},{"q":"Why does this voyage stand for the wider refugee crisis?","a":"The danger was already plain, the escape route existed and had been paid for, and the doors were shut anyway, 10 months after 32 governments had met at Evian and declined almost without exception to raise their limits."}],"wiki":"St. Louis"},
    "ww2-072": {"questions":["Six gassing installations built under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed more than seventy thousand people between January 1940 and August 1941, by the programme’s own count.","A bishop’s sermon in August 1941 led Hitler to halt the gassings of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the killing of disabled children never stopped at all."],"abstract":"<b>Aktion T4</b> was the secret programme by which German doctors and nurses killed institutionalised patients with disabilities, and it was the regime’s first industrial killing of its own citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name came from the address of its coordinating office in Berlin, Tiergartenstrasse 4, and it was run out of Hitler’s private chancellery rather than through any ministry, which kept it clear of the ordinary machinery of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the doctrine that some lives were unworthy of life, a claim about genetic and financial burden that eugenicists had been making for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of August 1939 required physicians, nurses and midwives to report every newborn and child under three showing signs of severe disability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parents were then encouraged to admit such children to special clinics, which were killing wards where staff used overdoses of medicine or starvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler signed a secret authorisation that autumn to protect the doctors, and had it backdated to 1 September 1939 so that it would look like a wartime measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six gassing installations were then built for adult patients at Bernburg, Brandenburg, Grafeneck, Hadamar, Hartheim and Sonnenstein, and by the programme’s own reckoning they killed 70,273 people between January 1940 and August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Records were falsified to give natural causes in every case, and it still became an open secret, protested privately and from pulpits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the bishop of Münster denounced the killings in a sermon on 3 August 1941, Hitler ordered the gassings halted, though the killing of children never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It resumed the next year by injection and starvation and ran to the end of the war, and the men and the methods went on to the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Euthanasia Program,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Persecution of Roma (Gypsies) in Prewar Germany, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-roma-gypsies-in-prewar-germany-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Bus_Hartheim_Foto_Niedernhart_Prozess.jpg/960px-Bus_Hartheim_Foto_Niedernhart_Prozess.jpg","title":"A grey bus at Hartheim, about 1940","desc":"One of the buses run by a bogus ambulance company that carried patients to the killing centre at Hartheim, its windows painted over, photographed with its driver.","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bus_Hartheim_Foto_Niedernhart_Prozess.jpg","alt":"A single-decker bus of the 1930s parked outside a large building, a man in uniform standing beside the door."},"why":[{"q":"Why was it run from Hitler's private chancellery rather than a ministry?","a":"That kept it clear of the ordinary machinery of the state, so the killing needed no law, no ministry's approval and no paper trail through the administration that would have made it visible."},{"q":"Why was Hitler's authorisation backdated to 1 September 1939?","a":"Backdating it to the day the war began made a programme of killing German citizens look like a wartime measure, while the document itself existed only to protect the doctors who carried it out."},{"q":"Why did halting the gassings in 1941 not end the killing?","a":"Hitler ordered the gassings stopped after a bishop denounced them publicly, but the killing of children never stopped, it resumed the next year by injection and starvation, and the men and methods went on to the camps."}],"wiki":"Aktion T4"},
    "ww2-073": {"questions":["About 100,000 arrests were made under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the Nazi years, and roughly half of them ended in a conviction and a prison sentence.","Although the regime rewrote and enforced it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not a Nazi invention and had been part of the German criminal code since 1871."],"abstract":"<b>Paragraph 175</b> was the clause of the German criminal code that made sexual relations between men a crime, and it was the chief instrument of the regime’s campaign against gay men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a Nazi invention: it had entered the imperial code in 1871 and stayed in German law in one form or another until 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Courts under the empire and the Weimar Republic had read it narrowly, requiring proof of acts resembling intercourse, which made convictions difficult to obtain and kept the numbers low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regime rewrote it in 1935 in wider and harsher terms, and convictions rose sharply once the new wording was in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The police raided the bars, watched the places where gay men met, and closed down their associations and their newspapers, dismantling communities that had grown up in Germany before 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> About 100,000 arrests were made under the statute during the 12 years of the regime, and roughly half of them, some 53,000, ended in a conviction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of those convicted served fixed prison terms, but some were sent on to concentration camps at the end of their sentences, for no fixed period at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other groups were persecuted by the same machinery: Roma were registered by a research office on racial hygiene from 1936, made subject to the race laws, and rounded up into municipal camps in city after city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the Berlin Games of 1936 the police cleared the Roma of the city into a field at Marzahn beside a cemetery and a sewage dump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the camps prisoners wore coloured markings by category, and the men held as asocials, among them a great many Roma, wore a black triangle sewn to the uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Paragraph 175 and the Nazi Campaign against Homosexuality,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/paragraph-175-and-the-nazi-campaign-against-homosexuality. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Persecution of Homosexuals in the Third Reich,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-homosexuals-in-the-third-reich. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Persecution of Roma (Gypsies) in Prewar Germany, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-roma-gypsies-in-prewar-germany-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Euthanasia Program,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-051-07%2C_Tafel_mit_KZ-Kennzeichen_%28Winkel%29_retouched.jpg/960px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-051-07%2C_Tafel_mit_KZ-Kennzeichen_%28Winkel%29_retouched.jpg","title":"The camp marking chart, 1936","desc":"The official chart of prisoner markings, showing the pink triangle for men held under Paragraph 175 and the black triangle for those classed as asocial.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1993-051-07, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-051-07,_Tafel_mit_KZ-Kennzeichen_%28Winkel%29_retouched.jpg","alt":"A hand-painted chart of coloured triangles in rows, with a striped prisoner's jacket and trousers drawn at the foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why did convictions rise sharply after 1935 under an old law?","a":"The clause had been in the code since 1871, but courts had read it narrowly, requiring proof of acts resembling intercourse. The regime rewrote it in wider and harsher terms, so the same statute suddenly convicted far more men."},{"q":"Why did the police close bars and newspapers as well as prosecute?","a":"Prosecution reached individuals; raiding the meeting places and shutting the associations and papers dismantled the communities that had grown up in Germany before 1933, which no number of convictions on its own would have done."},{"q":"Why did some men stay imprisoned after serving their sentences?","a":"Most of those convicted served fixed prison terms, but some were sent on to concentration camps at the end of them, for no fixed period at all, so the courts handed the police a supply of prisoners the courts had not sentenced."}],"wiki":"Paragraph 175"},
    "ww2-074": {"questions":["Bonhoeffer and Niemöller belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but neither was typical of a movement mostly concerned with keeping the state out of church affairs.","When a protest was read from the pulpits of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1935, the authorities briefly arrested more than seven hundred pastors."],"abstract":"The <b>Confessing Church</b> was the movement within German Protestantism that refused to let the state dictate the church’s doctrine, and it emerged in opposition to a pro-Nazi faction within its own body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of Germany’s sixty million people were Christian in 1933, about forty million Protestant and twenty million Catholic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A movement of German Christians had grown up in the 1920s that embraced the racial and nationalist parts of the new ideology and wanted a single Reich Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its opponents answered with the Barmen declaration, which held that the church owed its allegiance to God and scripture rather than to any earthly leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both groups stayed inside the German Evangelical Church, so what followed was a struggle for control of one body rather than a schism, and it was fought over doctrine and appointments as much as over politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The regime answered dissent forcefully: when a protest was read from Confessing pulpits in March 1935, more than seven hundred pastors were briefly arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two most famous members were Dietrich Bonhoeffer, executed for joining the conspiracy against Hitler, and Martin Niemöller, who spent 7 years in the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither man was typical, and for most of its members the struggle was an internal church affair about keeping the state out of doctrine rather than a fight against the regime itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Catholic Church had meanwhile signed a concordat with the government in July 1933, trading withdrawal from politics for guarantees the regime did not intend to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When those guarantees were broken the pope answered in 1937 with an encyclical read from German pulpits, written in German rather than Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Reich Concordat between the Holy See and the German Reich (July 20, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/reich-concordat-between-the-holy-see-and-the-german-reich-july-20-1933. [Open access]","\"With Deep Anxiety (Mit brennender Sorge): Papal Encyclical on the Situation of the Catholic Church in the German Reich (March 14, 1937),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/with-deep-anxiety-mit-brennender-sorge-papal-encyclical-on-the-situation-of-the-catholic-church-in-the-german-reich-march-14-1937. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dietrich Bonhoeffer,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dietrich-bonhoeffer. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Freie_Evangelische_Synode_1934_%2827818347276%29.jpg/1280px-Freie_Evangelische_Synode_1934_%2827818347276%29.jpg","title":"The free synod at Barmen, January 1934","desc":"Delegates in conversation at the free evangelical synod held at Barmen on 3 and 4 January 1934, one of the meetings out of which the Confessing Church came.","credit":"Archiv der Evangelischen Kirche im Rheinland. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freie_Evangelische_Synode_1934_%2827818347276%29.jpg","alt":"Two men in suits seated close together in conversation in a plain room."},"why":[{"q":"Why was this a struggle for control rather than a schism?","a":"Both the German Christians and their opponents stayed inside the German Evangelical Church, so what followed was fought over doctrine and appointments within one body rather than by leaving it."},{"q":"Why are Bonhoeffer and Niemoller misleading as examples of it?","a":"Neither man was typical. For most members the struggle was an internal church affair about keeping the state out of doctrine rather than a fight against the regime, which is what the two most famous cases suggest."},{"q":"Why did the Catholic Church end up protesting from German pulpits in 1937?","a":"It had signed a concordat in July 1933 trading withdrawal from politics for guarantees the regime did not intend to keep, and when those were broken the pope answered with an encyclical written in German rather than Latin."}],"wiki":"Confessing Church"},
    "ww2-075": {"questions":["A credit scheme run through a bogus company hid the cost of the rearmament that followed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, paying for about half of all weapons bought to 1936.","Within a year of reintroducing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Germany sent about 30,000 soldiers into the demilitarised Rhineland, and neither France nor Britain acted."],"abstract":"German rearmament began in earnest with the reintroduction of <b>conscription</b> on 16 March 1935, which tore up the military clauses of the Versailles treaty in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The announcement was followed the next day by Heroes’ Remembrance Day, a mourning day the regime had renamed to celebrate the glory of the fallen rather than to avoid another war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The open break with the treaty’s military clauses brought sharp criticism abroad, and the government moved at once to divide the powers that might otherwise have answered it together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain was offered a naval treaty in June 1935 holding the German fleet to 35% of its own and German submarines to 45%, and it accepted without consulting its allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In March 1936 about 30,000 soldiers marched into the demilitarised Rhineland, breaking both the Versailles treaty and the Locarno pact, which Germany had signed of its own free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That was the riskiest step so far and could have been stopped, but France would not act without Britain and Britain saw no international border crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The money came through the Reichsbank, whose president devised a credit scheme of bills drawn on a bogus company, which kept the scale of arms spending out of the published accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those bills paid for about half of all weapons bought between 1934 and 1936, and foreign trade was brought under state control to serve the same end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In August 1936 Hitler set the whole country a deadline in a confidential memorandum, and it was expressed in two sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army was to be fit for deployment within 4 years and the economy fit for war within 4 years, which is what rearmament had been for all along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Heroes’ Remembrance Day in Berlin (March 17, 1935),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/heroes-remembrance-day-in-berlin-march-17-1935. [Open access]","\"Joachim von Ribbentrop after the Conclusion of the German-British Naval Treaty in London (June 18, 1935),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/joachim-von-ribbentrop-after-the-conclusion-of-the-german-british-naval-treaty-in-london-june-18-1935. [Open access]","\"Wehrmacht Units Occupy the Demilitarized Rhineland Zone: The First Troops March into Koblenz (March 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/wehrmacht-units-occupy-the-demilitarized-rhineland-zone-the-first-troops-march-into-koblenz-march-1936. [Open access]","\"Hitler with Reichsbank President Hjalmar Schacht (May 5, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-with-reichsbank-president-hjalmar-schacht-may-5-1934. [Open access]","\"Hitler’s Confidential Memo on Autarky (August 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-confidential-memo-on-autarky-august-1936. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25647%2C_Berlin%2C_Vereidigung_junger_Rekruten.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25647%2C_Berlin%2C_Vereidigung_junger_Rekruten.jpg","title":"Swearing in the first conscripts, November 1935","desc":"Recruits of the first class called up under the conscription law of March 1935 taking their oath in Berlin on 7 November of that year.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H25647, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25647,_Berlin,_Vereidigung_junger_Rekruten.jpg","alt":"Ranks of young soldiers in greatcoats and helmets standing with right hands raised in a city square."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany offer Britain a naval treaty in June 1935?","a":"Conscription had torn up the treaty's military clauses in public and brought sharp criticism abroad, so the government moved to divide the powers that might have answered together. Britain accepted without consulting its allies."},{"q":"Why was the Rhineland march the riskiest step so far?","a":"About 30,000 soldiers broke both Versailles and the Locarno pact Germany had signed freely, and it could have been stopped. France would not act without Britain, and Britain saw no international border crossed."},{"q":"Why were arms paid for with bills drawn on a bogus company?","a":"The scheme kept the scale of arms spending out of the published accounts while paying for about half of all weapons bought between 1934 and 1936, so foreign observers could not read the rearmament off the books."}],"wiki":"Conscription"},
    "ww2-076": {"questions":["Foreign trade came under almost complete state control while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was economics minister, with imports held down to food and the raw materials rearmament needed.","After criticising the pace and the financing of rearmament, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost influence to Göring’s 4-year plan and was dismissed from the economics ministry."],"abstract":"<b>Hjalmar Schacht</b> financed the first years of German rearmament, and his career marks the point at which the regime stopped caring what its bankers thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As president of the Reichsbank he devised a credit system of bills drawn on a metallurgical research company that existed only on paper, set up in April 1933 for exactly that purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those bills paid for about half of all weapons purchases between 1934 and 1936 while keeping the spending out of the published accounts, where foreign observers might have read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He became minister of economics on 3 August 1934, the day after the president’s death, and used the office to drive the country towards self-sufficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German foreign trade came under almost complete state control, with imports held down to foodstuffs and the raw materials rearmament needed, and exports of manufactures pushed hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His influence began to fall away steadily from 1936, the year a 4-year plan was instituted under Hermann Göring and the whole economy was pointed openly at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plan aimed to centralise the mobilisation of labour, restrict imports, control wages and prices, and synthesise the raw materials Germany lacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also reached into ordinary households, telling Germans to salvage waste and consume less as a duty owed to the racial community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Schacht criticised the pace of rearmament and the way it was being financed, warning that the spending could not go on, and Hitler dismissed him from the economics ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had never held an economic doctrine of its own despite the word socialist in its name, and it left industry in private hands so long as industry produced what a coming war would need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Hitler with Reichsbank President Hjalmar Schacht (May 5, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-with-reichsbank-president-hjalmar-schacht-may-5-1934. [Open access]","\"Hitler’s Confidential Memo on Autarky (August 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-confidential-memo-on-autarky-august-1936. [Open access]","\"Propaganda Poster for the Four-Year Plan (1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/propaganda-poster-for-the-four-year-plan-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Hjalmar_Schacht.jpg/500px-Hjalmar_Schacht.jpg","title":"Hjalmar Schacht","desc":"The banker who ran the Reichsbank and, from August 1934, the economics ministry, and who financed the first phase of German rearmament.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hjalmar_Schacht.jpg","alt":"A studio portrait of a man in a high collar and rimless glasses, seated with one arm over a chair back."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Schacht's influence fall away from 1936?","a":"That was the year a 4-year plan was instituted under Goring and the economy was pointed openly at war, which took the direction of it out of his hands and made his caution about the pace an obstacle."},{"q":"Why did a party calling itself socialist leave industry in private hands?","a":"It had never held an economic doctrine of its own despite the word in its name, and private ownership was no obstacle so long as industry produced what a coming war would need."},{"q":"Why did state control of foreign trade serve rearmament?","a":"Imports were held down to foodstuffs and the raw materials rearmament needed while exports of manufactures were pushed hard, which drove the country towards self-sufficiency and freed foreign currency for the things arms production could not do without."}],"wiki":"Hjalmar Schacht"},
    "ww2-077": {"questions":["Autarky was the central aim of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that Germany could fight a war without depending on trade for its fuel or its food.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needed households as well as factories, Germans were told to salvage waste as a duty owed to the racial community."],"abstract":"The <b>Four-Year Plan</b> was the programme begun in October 1936 that turned the German economy openly towards a war the government intended to start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its charter was a confidential memorandum Hitler had written that August, which reviewed Germany’s shortages of raw materials and then set two tasks in two sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army was to be fit for deployment within 4 years, and the economy fit for war within 4 years; those were the two tasks, stated in that order and without qualification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hermann Göring was given charge of it, over the head of the economics minister who had financed rearmament so far and whose influence dated from that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plan centralised the mobilisation of labour, restricted imports, imposed controls on wages and prices, and took charge of allocating the raw materials the country did have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its central aim was autarky, so that Germany could fight without depending on trade for fuel or food, which meant synthesising in factories what could not be bought or mined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That could not be done by decree alone, so ordinary Germans were told to salvage waste and to curb what they consumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The posters that told them so framed the effort as a duty owed to the racial community rather than as an economic measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Göring also used the plan’s machinery to systematise the exploitation and expropriation of Jewish property, which was folded into the same drive for resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had no economic doctrine of its own to apply, and this was the shape its economics finally took: private industry left in private hands and directed at a war of conquest in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Hitler’s Confidential Memo on Autarky (August 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-confidential-memo-on-autarky-august-1936. [Open access]","\"Propaganda Poster for the Four-Year Plan (1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/propaganda-poster-for-the-four-year-plan-1938. [Open access]","\"Hitler with Reichsbank President Hjalmar Schacht (May 5, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-with-reichsbank-president-hjalmar-schacht-may-5-1934. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/G%C3%B6ring_Reichsautobahn_1936.jpg/1280px-G%C3%B6ring_Reichsautobahn_1936.jpg","title":"Göring in 1936","desc":"Hermann Göring, who was given charge of the Four-Year Plan that October, saluted by men of the German Labour Front at a motorway opening in April 1936.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B6ring_Reichsautobahn_1936.jpg","alt":"A uniformed man waving from an open car as ranks of men in working uniform salute beside the road."},"why":[{"q":"Why was autarky the plan's central aim?","a":"So that Germany could fight without depending on trade for fuel or food, which meant synthesising in factories what could not be bought or mined. The point was to make a war of conquest possible rather than to enrich the country."},{"q":"Why give charge of it to Goring rather than the economics minister?","a":"Schacht had financed rearmament so far and doubted the pace it was being pushed at, so putting Goring over his head moved the direction of the economy to somebody who would not argue about how much a coming war should cost."},{"q":"Why frame salvaging waste as a duty to the racial community?","a":"Autarky could not be achieved by decree alone and needed ordinary households to consume less, and an appeal to belonging asked for that as an obligation rather than admitting the shortages the memorandum had described."}],"wiki":"Four Year Plan"},
    "ww2-078": {"questions":["Open to Aryan Germans of every class, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was closed by definition to Jews, Roma and Black Germans, who were named as outsiders to it.","Appeals to salvage waste and consume less were made in the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as a duty owed to the community rather than an economic necessity."],"abstract":"<b>Volksgemeinschaft</b>, ‘the people’s community’, was the regime’s promise of a German society that transcended the divisions of class and religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was offered in place of the party politics of the Weimar Republic, which the movement had spent a decade denouncing as a system that set Germans against one another for the benefit of nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The promise was open to Aryan Germans of every class, region and religion, and closed by definition to Jews, Roma and Black Germans, who were named as outsiders to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its price was individual freedom, since the same programme that promised belonging abolished the associations through which Germans had until then pursued interests of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trade unions went first in May 1933, then the professional and cultural bodies, until almost no organised group in Germany stood outside the party’s reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What replaced them was membership of the community itself, expressed through the party’s own organisations, from the youth movement to the labour front, rather than through anything a person had chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The idea did practical work as well as ideological work, and appeals for material sacrifice were made in its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germans were told to salvage waste and to consume less because they owed it to the community, rather than because the economy required it of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It reached the churches too, where a faction of German Protestants took up its racial and nationalist language and pressed for a single national church built along the same lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The community it described never existed as advertised, but the belonging it offered was real enough to many Germans that they judged it worth the freedoms it cost them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Propaganda Poster for the Four-Year Plan (1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/propaganda-poster-for-the-four-year-plan-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Hitler Youth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hitler-youth-2. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/%22Tretet_ein_in_die_Deutsche_Volksgemeinschaft%21%22_-_LABW_-_Hauptstaatsarchiv_Stuttgart_%29J_151_Nr_2712%29.jpeg/1280px-%22Tretet_ein_in_die_Deutsche_Volksgemeinschaft%21%22_-_LABW_-_Hauptstaatsarchiv_Stuttgart_%29J_151_Nr_2712%29.jpeg","title":"Join the German people's community","desc":"A printed appeal in heavy blackletter type inviting Germans to enter the people's community, from the Stuttgart state archive.","credit":"Landesarchiv Baden-Württemberg, Hauptstaatsarchiv Stuttgart, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Tretet_ein_in_die_Deutsche_Volksgemeinschaft%21%22_-_LABW_-_Hauptstaatsarchiv_Stuttgart_%29J_151_Nr_2712%29.jpeg","alt":"A wide printed sheet reading Tretet ein in die Deutsche Volksgemeinschaft in bold German blackletter."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the promise of belonging require dissolving Germans' own associations?","a":"The same programme that offered community abolished the trade unions, professional bodies and cultural groups through which Germans had pursued interests of their own, so belonging replaced choosing rather than sitting beside it."},{"q":"Why was the idea useful for asking Germans to consume less?","a":"It let material sacrifice be demanded as a debt owed to the community rather than explained as something the economy required, which put the request beyond argument about whether the economy really required it."},{"q":"Why did the community have to be closed as well as open?","a":"It was offered to Aryan Germans of every class, region and religion, and Jews, Roma and Black Germans were named as outsiders. The exclusion was what gave the promised unity its content, since a community open to everyone would have described nothing."}],"wiki":"Volksgemeinschaft"},
    "ww2-079": {"questions":["From about 100,000 members in January 1933 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew to 5.4 million by 1937, roughly two thirds of all Germans aged ten to eighteen.","Boys in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were trained in increasingly military ways while the girls’ branch prepared its members to be wives and mothers."],"abstract":"The <b>Hitler Youth</b> was the party’s youth organisation, with a branch for boys of that name and a League of German Girls beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It had about 100,000 members when the party took power in January 1933 and more than two million by the end of that same year, roughly a third of German children of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany had been full of youth movements since the turn of the century, political, religious and social, and the party’s youth leader had nearly all of them dissolved in 1933 and their members drawn into his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Membership reached 5.4 million by 1937, about two thirds of all Germans aged between ten and eighteen, drawn in by enthusiasm, by pressure from other children and by coercion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law of December 1936 made membership compulsory and turned the organisation from a party body into a state one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Physical training for boys took increasingly paramilitary forms, with an emphasis on strength of will, obedience and the unconditional performance of duty rather than on games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Girls were prepared instead for the future the regime intended for them, as wives and mothers of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1940 the organisation held 7.2 million young Germans, some 82% of them, and in the last months of the war boys in their early teens were put into civil defence and the home guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every young German went willingly, and some avoided it altogether: the groups who met to listen to banned swing music kept out of it and passed messages to student dissidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For most of them, though, it was the ordinary shape of growing up, and it was where the party reached children before anyone else could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Hitler Youth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hitler-youth-2. [Open access]","\"Law on the Hitler Youth (December 1, 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/law-on-the-hitler-youth-december-1-1936. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Listening to Swing Music (1937),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/listening-to-swing-music-1937. [Open access]","Tessa Sauerwein, “Hitler Youth (HJ), 1926–1945,” <i>Historisches Lexikon Bayerns</i>, https://www.historisches-lexikon-bayerns.de/Lexikon/EN:Hitler_Youth_(HJ),_1926-1945. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Bundesarchiv_Bild_133-043%2C_Hitlerjugend%2C_Zeltlager.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_133-043%2C_Hitlerjugend%2C_Zeltlager.jpg","title":"A Hitler Youth camp, about 1933","desc":"Boys crowded into the mouth of a bell tent at a Hitler Youth camp, with a leader standing beside it.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 133-043, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_133-043,_Hitlerjugend,_Zeltlager.jpg","alt":"A dozen laughing boys packed into the open flap of a large bell tent, one older boy in uniform standing at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why did membership multiply twentyfold during 1933?","a":"Germany had been full of youth movements since the turn of the century, and the party's youth leader had nearly all of them dissolved that year and their members drawn into his own, so growth came from absorbing rivals as much as from recruitment."},{"q":"Why did a law in December 1936 change what the organisation was?","a":"It made membership compulsory and turned a party body into a state one, so belonging stopped being a political choice and became something a German child was required by law to do."},{"q":"Why were boys and girls trained so differently?","a":"Boys' physical training took increasingly paramilitary forms, stressing strength of will, obedience and unconditional duty rather than games, while girls were prepared for the future the regime intended for them, as wives and mothers of the nation."}],"wiki":"Hitler Youth"},
    "ww2-080": {"questions":["Because a person could love the music and still accept the regime, historians argue about whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amounted to resistance at all.","Some clubs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused the compulsory youth organisation and passed messages to the student group that printed leaflets in Munich."],"abstract":"The <b>Swing Kids</b> were young Germans who went on listening to jazz and swing after the regime condemned the music, and they are the awkward case in any account of dissent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jazz had come to Germany with American goods and American money in the 1920s, under the arrangements that followed the war, and it had taken hold among the young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1933 the regime called it degenerate, tying it to Black American culture and to what it described as a Jewish vice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Young Germans went on dancing to it anyway, in clubs and at private parties, wearing long hair and English clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether that was resistance is a real question rather than a rhetorical one, since a person could love the music, frequent the clubs and still believe everything the regime taught about race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some of the clubs did become political over time, and their members refused to join the party’s youth organisation, membership of which the law had by then made compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One such network kept up a line of communication with the student group in Munich that printed and distributed leaflets against the regime and was executed for doing so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That group is the reason the question matters, because it shows how far the distance between non-conformity and opposition could be crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most dissent before the war never crossed it: the churches argued mainly about their own affairs, and a protest read from Confessing pulpits in 1935 brought some seven hundred arrests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The few who went further, among them a theologian later executed for joining the plot against Hitler, were exceptions in a country that had mostly acquiesced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Listening to Swing Music (1937),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/listening-to-swing-music-1937. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"White Rose,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/white-rose. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Hitler Youth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hitler-youth-2. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dietrich Bonhoeffer,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dietrich-bonhoeffer. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is calling them resistance a real question rather than a rhetorical one?","a":"A person could love the music, frequent the clubs and still believe everything the regime taught about race, so listening to condemned music was non-conformity and not by itself opposition to anything the regime was doing."},{"q":"Why did the regime condemn jazz as degenerate at all?","a":"It tied the music to Black American culture and to what it described as a Jewish vice, so a form that had reached Germany with American goods in the 1920s became a racial matter rather than a question of taste."},{"q":"Why does the Munich student group matter to their story?","a":"One swing network kept up a line of communication with the students who printed leaflets against the regime and were executed for it, which shows how far the distance between non-conformity and opposition could be crossed, and that most dissent never crossed it."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Swing_tanzen_verboten_Humboldt_Forum.jpg/1920px-Swing_tanzen_verboten_Humboldt_Forum.jpg","title":"Swing Kids","desc":"\"Swing tanzen verboten\", Illustration in Ausstellung im Humboldt Forum.","credit":"ChickSR, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swing_tanzen_verboten_Humboldt_Forum.jpg","alt":"Swing tanzen verboten Humboldt Forum"},"wiki":"Swingjugend"},
    "ww2-081": {"questions":["Taiwan was taken from China in 1895 and Korea annexed in 1910 by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had abolished its feudal order only a generation earlier.","In 1914 barely 2.6% of the population could vote in the Imperial Diet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where former samurai from two domains still made the decisions."],"abstract":"The <b>Empire of Japan</b> was the state founded on the Meiji Constitution of 1889 and dissolved in 1947, and it is the Japan that fought the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founders overthrew the feudal order in 1868 and built the institutions of a modern country at speed, adding compulsory education in 1872, a cabinet in 1885 and a civil service examination in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The constitution promulgated on 11 February 1889 gave the country an elected lower house within an Imperial Diet, and the Diet first met the following year, after a long campaign by the Freedom and People’s Rights Movement had pressed the government into conceding both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also vested every executive, legislative and judicial power in the emperor and made him commander-in-chief, which mattered enormously later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Voting was narrow at first, and in 1914 only 1.5 million men paid the ten yen in annual tax that the franchise required, about 2.6% of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Real decisions rested for decades with a small group of former samurai from the Satsuma and Chōshū domains who had led the reforms, stood close to the throne, and were known as the genrō or elder statesmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The empire grew outward as it modernised, taking Taiwan from China in 1895, defeating Russia in 1905 and annexing Korea in 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Treaty revision ran alongside that expansion, ending extraterritoriality in 1899 and recovering full control of the tariff in 1911.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States and Japan were on cordial terms at the turn of the century, and an American president mediated the peace that ended the Russian war, though relations soured badly over immigration in the 2 decades afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The structure the constitution created, with an emperor above politics and a military answering to him rather than to the cabinet, is what the 1930s crisis broke open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, \"Evolution of the Meiji State,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha2/index.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10434. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japanese-American Relations at the Turn of the Century, 1900–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/japanese-relations. [Open access]","\"Treaty Regarding the Annexation of Korea to the Empire of Japan,\" Seoul, 22 August 1910, in <i>\"The World and Japan\" Database</i>, Institute for Advanced Studies on Asia, University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/pw/19100822.T1E.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Kenpohapu-chikanobu.jpg/1280px-Kenpohapu-chikanobu.jpg","title":"The promulgation of the Meiji Constitution, 1889","desc":"A woodblock triptych of the ceremony of 11 February 1889 at which the emperor handed the constitution to his prime minister before the assembled court and government.","credit":"Toyohara Chikanobu, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenpohapu-chikanobu.jpg","alt":"Colour woodblock print of a crowded palace hall, the emperor seated at right beneath red drapery while ranks of officials in gold-braided uniforms stand and bow."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1889 constitution's structure matter so much in the 1930s?","a":"It vested every executive, legislative and judicial power in the emperor and made him commander-in-chief, so the military answered to him rather than to the cabinet. That arrangement is what the crisis of the 1930s broke open."},{"q":"Why did an elected lower house not mean broad participation?","a":"The franchise required paying ten yen in annual tax, so in 1914 only 1.5 million men could vote, about 2.6% of the population, and real decisions rested with a small group of elder statesmen close to the throne."},{"q":"Why did the country modernise and expand at the same time?","a":"The institutions and the empire were one project: compulsory education, a cabinet and a civil service came in the 1870s and 1880s, and Taiwan, victory over Russia and Korea followed, alongside treaty revision that ended extraterritoriality and recovered the tariff."}],"wiki":"Empire of Japan","places":["pol:japan"]},
    "ww2-082": {"questions":["Russia was defeated in 1905 and its lease on the Liaotung Peninsula passed into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had been forced to give that same peninsula back 10 years earlier.","The Twenty-One Demands presented to China in 1915 were the most sweeping rights ever extracted by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and China did not forget them."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese colonial empire</b> was the overseas territory Japan gathered between 1895 and 1919, and it was the base from which the country moved into Manchuria in 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan defeated China in 1894 and 1895, took Taiwan, and drove Chinese power out of Korea, only to be pressed by Russia, France and Germany into handing back the Liaotung Peninsula it had won, in exchange for a larger indemnity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia then leased that same peninsula for itself and moved troops into Manchuria, and the collision that followed became the Russo-Japanese War of 1904 and 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Treaty of Portsmouth, mediated by Theodore Roosevelt, gave Japan the southern half of Sakhalin and a free hand in Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korea was made a protectorate in 1905 and annexed outright on 22 August 1910, by a treaty in which its emperor ceded all rights of sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The First World War extended the reach further, as the navy took the German island groups of the western Pacific in 1914 and the army stormed the German fortress at Qingdao that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Twenty-One Demands of 1915 were the most sweeping set of rights any power had extracted from China, and they were remembered in China long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 50,000 Japanese troops were poured into the Russian Far East by December 1918, in an intervention the Japanese public greeted coldly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of this was unusual among the powers of the day, and the United States had built its own China policy on demanding equal commercial access rather than territory, a position it announced in a set of notes to the other powers in 1899 and 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What changed after 1918 was the mood, and the Washington Conference of 1921 and 1922 tied Japan into naval limits and a treaty guaranteeing Chinese integrity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, \"Russo-Japanese War,\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10050. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Treaty of Portsmouth and the Russo-Japanese War, 1904–1905,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/portsmouth-treaty. [Open access]","\"Treaty Regarding the Annexation of Korea to the Empire of Japan,\" Seoul, 22 August 1910, in <i>\"The World and Japan\" Database</i>, Institute for Advanced Studies on Asia, University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/pw/19100822.T1E.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Secretary of State John Hay and the Open Door in China, 1899–1900,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/hay-and-china. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Empire_of_Japan_%281930%29_territories.svg/1280px-Empire_of_Japan_%281930%29_territories.svg.png","title":"The Japanese empire in 1930","desc":"A globe showing Japan in dark green with its colonies of Korea, Taiwan, Karafuto and the Kuril Islands in lighter green, and the South Seas Mandate outlined to the south-east.","credit":"Colohisto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_of_Japan_%281930%29_territories.svg","alt":"Orthographic globe centred on East Asia with the Japanese home islands in dark green, Korea, Taiwan and Karafuto in pale green, and a dotted boundary over the western Pacific."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Liaotung Peninsula lead to war with Russia?","a":"Japan won it from China and was pressed by Russia, France and Germany into handing it back, whereupon Russia leased the same peninsula for itself and moved troops into Manchuria. The collision that followed became the war of 1904."},{"q":"Why were the Twenty-One Demands remembered in China so long afterwards?","a":"They were the most sweeping set of rights any power had extracted from China, which made them a standing grievance rather than one more episode among the concessions the powers had been taking."},{"q":"Why does it matter that none of this was unusual among the powers?","a":"The other powers were doing the same, the United States building its China policy on demanding equal commercial access instead. What changed after 1918 was the mood, and the Washington Conference tied Japan into naval limits and a guarantee of Chinese integrity."}],"wiki":"Japanese colonial empire"},
    "ww2-083": {"questions":["A declaration of independence read out across Korea on 1 March 1919 was suppressed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which afterwards eased censorship without loosening its grip.","The slogan naisen ittai, meaning Japan and Korea as one body, was pressed on Koreans by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the late 1930s to make them serve the war."],"abstract":"The <b>Government-General of Korea</b> was the colonial administration that governed the Korean peninsula for Japan from 1910 until the empire surrendered in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had taken charge of Korean foreign relations on 17 November 1905, and the American legation in Seoul closed 11 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protectorate that followed was a colony in all but name, and its Residency-General built a press-control apparatus through a security act and a press act in 1907, textbook regulations in 1908 and a publication act in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The annexation treaty signed at Seoul on 22 August 1910 had the Korean emperor make a complete and permanent cession of all rights of sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first decade of direct rule was run by a military gendarmerie whose harshness in the countryside was notorious even to foreign observers otherwise sympathetic to Japanese administration, and the Korean police force it replaced was dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 1 March 1919 a declaration of independence was read aloud in towns across the country, and 33 signatories handed themselves in to the police in Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The demonstrations were unarmed and enormous, spreading from Seoul across the whole country within hours, and the colonial authorities put them down with a violence that foreign witnesses photographed and recorded in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan answered with what it called cultural rule, easing press restrictions and replacing gendarmes with civil police, while leaving the structure of the colony untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the late 1930s the policy hardened again into assimilation, summed up in the slogan naisen ittai, meaning Japan and Korea as one body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That campaign was meant to turn Koreans into imperial subjects who would work and fight for the war, and it reached into schooling, language, names and family life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Treaty Regarding the Annexation of Korea to the Empire of Japan,\" Seoul, 22 August 1910, in <i>\"The World and Japan\" Database</i>, Institute for Advanced Studies on Asia, University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/pw/19100822.T1E.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Korea,\" https://history.state.gov/countries/korea. [Open access]","Soyoung Kim, \"Textbook Inspection and Censorship in Korea during the Protectorate Period: A Study of Inspection Copies of Textbooks Compiled by the Young Korean Academy,\" <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 2 (2016): 79–119, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2016.08.21.2.79. [Open access]","F. A. McKenzie, <i>Korea’s Fight for Freedom</i> (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1920), https://archive.org/details/koreasfightforfr00mckerich. [Open access]","AhRan Ellie Bae, \"Helen Kim as New Woman and Collaborator: A Comprehensive Assessment of Korean Collaboration under Japanese Colonial Rule,\" <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 1 (2017): 107–37, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2017.22.1.107. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Japanese_General_Government_Building.jpg/1280px-Japanese_General_Government_Building.jpg","title":"The Government-General building in Seoul, 1929","desc":"The domed headquarters of the colonial administration, built inside the grounds of the Gyeongbokgung palace and finished in 1926.","credit":"Mondaen Bangtaero, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_General_Government_Building.jpg","alt":"Sepia photograph of a large domed neoclassical building in stone, seen across an empty forecourt with hills behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the 1905 protectorate a colony in all but name?","a":"Japan had taken charge of Korean foreign relations, the American legation closed 11 days later, and the Residency-General built a press-control apparatus through security, press, textbook and publication acts before annexation was ever signed."},{"q":"Why did Japan switch to cultural rule after March 1919?","a":"Unarmed demonstrations spread across the whole country within hours and were put down with a violence foreign witnesses photographed and recorded, so the press restrictions were eased and gendarmes replaced with civil police while the colony's structure stayed untouched."},{"q":"Why did assimilation reach into names and family life from the late 1930s?","a":"The campaign summed up as Japan and Korea as one body was meant to turn Koreans into imperial subjects who would work and fight for the war, which required more of them than obedience to an administration."}],"wiki":"Governor-General of Chōsen"},
    "ww2-084": {"questions":["Party cabinets governing Japan under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut military spending from 65 to about 30% of the budget, which the Imperial Army never forgave.","The assassination of Prime Minister Inukai Tsuyoshi in May 1932 ended <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no fully party-based cabinet governed Japan again until after the war."],"abstract":"<b>Taishō democracy</b> is the name given to the 2 decades after 1912 in which elected parties, not oligarchs, came closest to governing Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A coalition of parties toppled an oligarchic cabinet for the first time in February 1913, in what is remembered as the Taishō Political Crisis, after the army had brought down its predecessor by withdrawing its minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The First World War then transformed the country economically, and the rice riots of 1918 brought two million people onto the streets and shook the leadership badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan got its first genuine party cabinet in September 1918 under Hara Takashi, and party cabinets held office almost continuously from 1924 to 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those governments joined the international disarmament movement, entering the League of Nations at its founding in 1919, signing the Kellogg-Briand Pact in 1928, and taking part in the Washington, Geneva and London naval conferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Universal Manhood Suffrage Law passed the Imperial Diet in March 1925 and dropped the tax qualification that had limited the vote for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first election held under it, in February 1928, returned the two big parties almost level with each other, despite a government campaign of moving prefectural governors and police chiefs about beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same session passed the Peace Preservation Law, so the widening of the franchise arrived alongside a new instrument for policing opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Military spending was cut from 65% of the budget in the years to 1922 to about 30% by 1932, which the army resented deeply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The end came quickly after 1931: the cabinet could not restrain the army in Manchuria, and the murder of Prime Minister Inukai Tsuyoshi in May 1932 closed the era of party government until after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, \"Taisho Democracy,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha3/index.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10434. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Adoption of Universal Manhood Suffrage Law and Peace Preservation Law,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha3/description13.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"First Election with Universal Manhood Suffrage,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha3/description14.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"5.15 Incident of 1932,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description02.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Burning_of_the_Okayama_Seimai%2C_1918_rice_riots.jpg/1280px-Burning_of_the_Okayama_Seimai%2C_1918_rice_riots.jpg","title":"The rice riots at Okayama, 1918","desc":"A crowd stands in front of the smoking ruin of a rice-milling business during the disturbances that brought two million people onto the streets that summer.","credit":"Asahi Shimbun, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burning_of_the_Okayama_Seimai,_1918_rice_riots.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a large crowd in straw hats before a burnt-out brick building still smoking behind a low wall."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the army resent the party governments so deeply?","a":"Military spending was cut from 65% of the budget in the years to 1922 to about 30% by 1932, and those cabinets had joined the disarmament movement, entering the League and signing the naval treaties."},{"q":"Why did the widening of the vote arrive with a law policing opinion?","a":"The same Diet session that dropped the tax qualification in March 1925 passed the Peace Preservation Law, so the franchise was extended and a new instrument for controlling what the newly enfranchised might say was created together."},{"q":"Why did the era end so quickly after 1931?","a":"The cabinet could not restrain the army in Manchuria, which exposed how little the elected government controlled, and the murder of Prime Minister Inukai in May 1932 closed party government until after the war."}],"wiki":"Taishō Democracy"},
    "ww2-085": {"questions":["The Meiji Constitution made the emperor commander-in-chief, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered to the throne rather than to the ministers who were supposed to govern it.","Between 1934 and 1936 the feud between the Imperial Way and Control factions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced a purge, a murdered general and an attempted coup."],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Japanese Army</b> was Japan’s land force from 1873 to 1945, and its constitutional position let it make and unmake governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> National conscription created it, and by 1877 it had put down the last samurai resistance to the new state, while drilling young men from every class and region into something like a common sense of nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tokyo gave it an autonomous General Staff in 1878, and the Imperial Rescript to Soldiers and Sailors of 1882 tied it directly to the emperor rather than to ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Meiji Constitution then named the emperor commander-in-chief, and two ordinances of 1900 required that the army and navy ministers be serving officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last rule was the lever, because a cabinet cannot be formed without a full complement of ministers, so refusing to supply one could bring any government down or stop it being made at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was used at once, when the war minister resigned in December 1912 over the army’s demand for two more divisions and brought the cabinet down with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule was abolished in 1913, restored in 1936, and used the following January to prevent Ugaki Kazushige from forming a government at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ugaki wrote that it caused him great regret to have an imperial order obstructed by the emperor’s own army, and the report of his complaint was banned from publication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The army was also divided against itself, and the feud between its Imperial Way and Control factions produced a purge, an assassination and an attempted coup between 1934 and 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against all this the parties were weakening, having cut military budgets through the 1920s and then lost their claim to govern once they proved unable to restrain the army in Manchuria after 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Stillbirth of the UGAKI Cabinet,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description08.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Army Factional Infighting,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description05.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10434. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/The_3rd_Infantry_Regiment_of_Imperial_Japanese_Army_departing_from_Aoyama_Station.jpg/1280px-The_3rd_Infantry_Regiment_of_Imperial_Japanese_Army_departing_from_Aoyama_Station.jpg","title":"An infantry battalion leaving Tokyo, 1894","desc":"The first battalion of the 3rd Regiment drawn up beside its troop train at Aoyama station on 26 September 1894, on its way to the war with China.","credit":"Akiyoshi Zentarō, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_3rd_Infantry_Regiment_of_Imperial_Japanese_Army_departing_from_Aoyama_Station.jpg","alt":"Faded photograph of a long column of soldiers with packs standing beside a line of railway carriages on open ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did requiring serving officers as ministers give the army so much power?","a":"A cabinet cannot be formed without a full complement of ministers, so refusing to supply one could bring any government down or stop it being made at all, as happened in December 1912 and again to Ugaki in January 1937."},{"q":"Why did the army answer to the emperor rather than to ministers?","a":"It was given an autonomous General Staff in 1878, the Rescript of 1882 tied it directly to the emperor, and the constitution then named him commander-in-chief, so the chain of command bypassed the cabinet entirely."},{"q":"Why could Ugaki's complaint not be published?","a":"He had written that it caused him great regret to have an imperial order obstructed by the emperor's own army, which named the contradiction at the centre of the constitutional arrangement, so the report of it was banned."}],"wiki":"Imperial Japanese Army"},
    "ww2-086": {"questions":["Araki Sadao and Masaki Jinzaburō were the senior patrons of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held that corrupt advisers stood between the emperor and his people.","A lieutenant colonel of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> murdered Nagata Tetsuzan in 1935, after the rival Control Faction forced one of its patrons out of his army post."],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Way Faction</b> was the radical current in the Japanese army that wanted a violent purification of the state around the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gathered around General Araki Sadao and General Masaki Jinzaburō, and it was opposed inside the army by the more technocratic Control Faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its junior officers held that politicians, financiers and court advisers had come between the emperor and his people, and that only direct action would sweep them away and restore his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In November 1934 several of them, with five military academy cadets, were arrested for plotting a coup and insisted they had been framed by their rivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Masaki was forced out of his post in July 1935, a lieutenant colonel of the faction answered by murdering Nagata Tetsuzan, the leading figure of the Control Faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same politics were at work in the campaign of 1935 against the theory of the emperor as an organ of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That theory had underpinned constitutional government for a generation, and nationalist groups, much of the army and a party seeking to topple the cabinet joined in denouncing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government banned the constitutional scholar Minobe Tatsukichi’s books in April 1935 and ordered corrections printed, which showed how far an academic argument had become a political weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The faction’s attempt came on 26 February 1936, when its officers seized the government quarter of central Tokyo and killed a former prime minister, the finance minister and an army inspector general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At first events seemed to run their way, but the emperor declared firmly that the revolt must be suppressed and it collapsed within 3 days, after which the ringleaders and the two thinkers who had shaped them were sentenced to death and the faction was purged out of the army, leaving the military’s voice in politics stronger than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, \"Army Factional Infighting,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description05.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"The 2.26 Incident of 1936,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description07.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Dispute over the Emperor as an Organ of Government Theory,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description04.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"5.15 Incident of 1932,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description02.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Rebel_troops_in_February_26_Incident.JPG/960px-Rebel_troops_in_February_26_Incident.JPG","title":"Rebel officers and men, 26 February 1936","desc":"A lieutenant of the rising instructs his company in the courtyard of the unfinished Diet building on the morning the rebels seized central Tokyo.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebel_troops_in_February_26_Incident.JPG","alt":"Sepia photograph of an officer in a long greatcoat addressing several dozen soldiers in caps and coats crowded together in the snow."},"why":[{"q":"Why did junior officers think direct action was the remedy?","a":"They held that politicians, financiers and court advisers had come between the emperor and his people, so on their reading the problem was an obstruction to be swept away rather than a policy to be argued about."},{"q":"Why was an academic theory of the emperor attacked in 1935?","a":"The organ theory had underpinned constitutional government for a generation, so nationalist groups, much of the army and a party seeking to topple the cabinet joined in denouncing it, and the government banned Minobe's books."},{"q":"Why did the failed coup leave the military stronger in politics?","a":"The emperor declared firmly that the revolt must be suppressed, and it collapsed in 3 days. The ringleaders were sentenced to death and the faction purged, which removed the army's internal rival and left one voice speaking for it."}],"wiki":"Imperial Way Faction"},
    "ww2-087": {"questions":["The League commission that examined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> concluded that the damage was too slight to justify military action and that the response was not legitimate self-defence.","Tokyo declared a policy of non-expansion after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and could not enforce it, which destroyed the cabinet and ended conciliatory diplomacy towards China."],"abstract":"The <b>Mukden Incident</b> was the staged explosion of 18 September 1931 that Japan used as its reason for occupying Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A short stretch of the South Manchuria Railway near Liutiao Lake, on the outskirts of Mukden, now Shenyang, was blown up between 10:00 PM and 10:30 PM.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line belonged to Japan, and Japanese officers blamed Chinese nationalists for the blast, then attacked the barracks north of the city and captured the walled city itself within hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commission the League of Nations sent to investigate found that the damage did not prevent the punctual arrival of the next train and was not itself enough to justify military action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its verdict was that the night’s operations could not be regarded as measures of legitimate self-defence, though it allowed that officers on the spot may have believed they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The charge has always been that the bomb was laid by officers of the Kwantung Army, the Japanese force stationed along the railway, in order to manufacture the pretext they wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cabinet in Tokyo announced a policy of non-expansion at once, and the army in Manchuria simply carried on expanding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That failure destroyed the second Wakatsuki cabinet and ended Foreign Minister Shidehara Kijūrō’s attempt at conciliatory diplomacy, after an effort to form a cross-party government of cooperation backfired into political turmoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also revived an army whose budgets and standing had been shrinking for a decade under party government, and which had been forced to disband four complete infantry divisions in 1925.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The incident is usually taken as the opening of the 15 years of war that ran from Manchuria in 1931 through the invasion of China to Japan’s defeat in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Japanese_soldiers_near_Mukden%2C_October_1931.jpg/1280px-Japanese_soldiers_near_Mukden%2C_October_1931.jpg","title":"Japanese soldiers at Mukden, October 1931","desc":"Five infantrymen with rifles and steel helmets outside a shuttered shopfront in the city weeks after the railway explosion.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_soldiers_near_Mukden,_October_1931.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of five helmeted soldiers with rifles resting outside a closed Chinese shopfront in bright sunlight."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the League's commission reject the self-defence claim?","a":"It found that the damage did not prevent the punctual arrival of the next train and was not itself enough to justify military action, though it allowed that officers on the spot may have believed they were defending themselves."},{"q":"Why did Tokyo's policy of non-expansion change nothing?","a":"The cabinet announced it at once and the army in Manchuria simply carried on expanding, which destroyed the Wakatsuki cabinet and ended Shidehara's attempt at conciliatory diplomacy by showing the government could not command its own forces."},{"q":"Why did the incident revive the army's standing at home?","a":"Its budgets and position had been shrinking for a decade under party government, to the point of disbanding four infantry divisions in 1925, and a swift conquest restored what a decade of cuts had taken."}],"wiki":"Mukden incident"},
    "ww2-088": {"questions":["About 10,000 soldiers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were posted in the leased territory at the tip of the Liaotung Peninsula, under rights Japan had held since 1905.","Ishiwara Kanji of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that Manchuria was the crucial strategic stronghold of Japan’s destiny, chiefly as a barrier against Soviet expansion."],"abstract":"The <b>Kwantung Army</b> was the Japanese force stationed in the leased territory at the tip of the Liaotung Peninsula and along the South Manchuria Railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan had held those rights since its victory over Russia in 1905 and kept about 10,000 soldiers there, with a treaty claim to raise the number to 15,000 if it judged that necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its officers engineered the explosion of September 1931 and then overran Manchuria within a few months against an untrained Chinese army that offered next to no resistance, before consolidating their hold on a resource-rich region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ishiwara Kanji, one of the officers behind the plan, had argued that spring that Manchuria was the most crucial strategic stronghold for Japan’s national destiny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He valued it chiefly as a barrier that would hinder Russian expansion eastward, and only secondarily for its farmland and its coal and iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The League’s commission recorded plainly that a large area of indisputably Chinese territory had been seized and occupied by Japanese armed forces without any declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manchuria was as large as France and Germany together, and its 400,000 foreign residents included about 230,000 Japanese living along the railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army installed Japanese advisers over Chinese officials, and in some districts tax collectors had to submit their books daily to the Japanese gendarmerie and obtain its approval before spending public money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cabinet in Tokyo could not stop any of it, and the episode showed how little control the civil government had over its own forces abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same force went on to make policy for itself for another decade, and the habit of soldiers in the field presenting their government with accomplished facts became one of the standing features of Japanese expansion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","Jaeik Ahn, \"Examining the Manchukuo Issue in the Context of the Asia-Pacific War’s Outbreak,\" <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 23, no. 1 (2025): 99–121, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2025.04.23.1.99. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Guerre_de_Mandchourie_-_compagnie_japonaise_dans_le_plaine_glac%C3%A9e_-_btv1b90486889.jpg/1280px-Guerre_de_Mandchourie_-_compagnie_japonaise_dans_le_plaine_glac%C3%A9e_-_btv1b90486889.jpg","title":"A Japanese company in Manchuria, 1932","desc":"Infantry with a regimental flag drawn up on a frozen plain during the winter campaign that completed the occupation.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerre_de_Mandchourie_-_compagnie_japonaise_dans_le_plaine_glac%C3%A9e_-_btv1b90486889.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a long line of soldiers in padded winter coats standing on broken ice, an officer on horseback at the left."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Manchuria valued chiefly as a barrier rather than for its resources?","a":"Ishiwara had argued that spring that it was the most crucial strategic stronghold for Japan's national destiny, valuing it as a barrier to Russian expansion eastward and only secondarily for its farmland, coal and iron."},{"q":"Why could 10,000 troops overrun a region the size of France and Germany?","a":"The Chinese army opposing them was untrained and offered next to no resistance, so the force stationed along the railway took the whole region within a few months and then consolidated its hold."},{"q":"Why did this episode become a standing feature of Japanese expansion?","a":"The cabinet in Tokyo could not stop any of it, so the same force went on making policy for itself for another decade, and soldiers in the field presenting their government with accomplished facts became the pattern."}],"wiki":"Kwantung Army"},
    "ww2-089": {"questions":["A League commission concluded that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not have been formed without Japanese troops and officials, and was therefore no genuine independence movement.","Japan was among the very few governments to recognise <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it was still pressing for wider recognition on the eve of the Pacific war."],"abstract":"<b>Manchukuo</b> was the state proclaimed in Manchuria in March 1932 and run in fact by the Japanese army that had just conquered the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A declaration of independence was published on 18 February 1932, signed by the governors of four provinces and by Mongol princes, and it claimed that these men had been chosen by the people to fulfil their wish for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commander of the Kwantung Army gave a dinner that same night for the heads of the new state and assured them of his assistance if they needed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An organic law promulgated in March vested all executive power in a regent who could overrule the state's legislative chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regent was Puyi, the last Qing emperor of China, whom the League’s commission interviewed at Changchun during its enquiry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The commission also met the government’s Japanese officials and advisers, and found that most of the delegations it received had been presented to it by the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its finding was that the presence of Japanese troops and the activity of Japanese officials were the two things without which the state could not have been formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It therefore held that the regime had not been called into existence by a genuine and spontaneous independence movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan recognised it in 1932, one of very few governments in the world to do so, and spent the next decade trying to have that recognition accepted more widely, above all by China itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The refusal of other governments to recognise it was still an obstacle in the negotiations with the United States on the eve of the Pacific war, and Japanese diplomats read the American demands as a proposal to undo it altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Jaeik Ahn, \"Examining the Manchukuo Issue in the Context of the Asia-Pacific War’s Outbreak,\" <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 23, no. 1 (2025): 99–121, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2025.04.23.1.99. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Emperor_Hirohito_Handshakes_Emperor_Puyi_of_Manchukuo_26_June_1940.png/960px-Emperor_Hirohito_Handshakes_Emperor_Puyi_of_Manchukuo_26_June_1940.png","title":"Puyi received in Tokyo, 26 June 1940","desc":"The regent, by then styled emperor of Manchukuo, is greeted on the platform at Tokyo Station during a state visit meant to display the new state's standing.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Hirohito_Handshakes_Emperor_Puyi_of_Manchukuo_26_June_1940.png","alt":"Black and white photograph of two uniformed men shaking hands on a railway platform, watched by officials in coats and a naval officer in white."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the commission reject the claim of spontaneous independence?","a":"It found that the presence of Japanese troops and the activity of Japanese officials were the two things without which the state could not have been formed, and that most of the delegations it received had been presented to it by the authorities."},{"q":"Why did an organic law give the regent power over the legislature?","a":"Vesting all executive power in a regent who could overrule the legislative chamber meant one office decided everything, and that office was held by Puyi, whom the army had installed and could direct."},{"q":"Why did recognition of it still matter a decade later?","a":"Japan was one of very few governments to recognise it and spent 10 years trying to have that recognition accepted more widely. The refusal of others was still an obstacle in the 1941 negotiations with the United States."}],"wiki":"Manchukuo"},
    "ww2-090": {"questions":["Rather than restoring the position of 1931, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposed an autonomous Manchuria under Chinese sovereignty with Japanese rights recognised by new treaties.","The Japanese delegation walked out of the League Assembly when it adopted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in February 1933, and Japan gave notice of withdrawal a month later."],"abstract":"The <b>Lytton Report</b> was the League of Nations commission’s account of the Manchurian crisis, signed on 1 October 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League had asked it, in December 1931, to examine the issues between China and Japan, including their causes and their state at the time of the enquiry, and to suggest a way forward that could reconcile the interests of both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commission spent about 6 weeks in Manchuria, interviewing the Kwantung Army’s commander, Japanese consular officials and the regent of the new state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It found that the railway explosion of September 1931 had not justified the attack, and that the operations that followed were not legitimate self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It found equally that the new state owed its existence to Japanese troops and officials rather than to any spontaneous movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It did not simply order a return to the position before September 1931, which it thought impossible, and it said so plainly rather than pretending the previous arrangement could be restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead it proposed an autonomous administration for the three provinces under Chinese sovereignty, with Japanese rights recognised and new treaties to settle the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ten principles asked that any solution suit both countries, respect Soviet interests, and conform to the Covenant, the Kellogg-Briand Pact and the Nine-Power Treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> China and Japan were both League members, and the report insisted that each was entitled to the same consideration, on the ground that a solution benefiting only one of them would be no gain for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Assembly adopted the report in February 1933 the Japanese delegation walked out, and Japan gave notice of withdrawal from the League the following month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Thomas W. Burkman, \"League of Nations and Japan,\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11571. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Lytton_Commission_in_Shanghai.jpg/1280px-Lytton_Commission_in_Shanghai.jpg","title":"The League commission arrives in Shanghai, 1932","desc":"The members of the commission of enquiry photographed on arrival beneath a banner naming them as representatives of the League of Nations.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lytton_Commission_in_Shanghai.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of about fifteen men in overcoats and hats standing in a row at night beneath a painted banner."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the report refuse to order a return to the position before 1931?","a":"It thought restoring the previous arrangement impossible and said so plainly rather than pretending otherwise, proposing instead an autonomous administration for the three provinces under Chinese sovereignty with Japanese rights recognised."},{"q":"Why did it insist both countries were entitled to equal consideration?","a":"Both were League members, and the commission held that a solution benefiting only one of them would be no gain for peace, so its principles asked that any settlement suit both and respect Soviet interests too."},{"q":"Why did adopting it end Japan's membership of the League?","a":"The report found the attack was not legitimate self-defence and the new state not a genuine independence movement, so when the Assembly adopted it in February 1933 the delegation walked out and notice of withdrawal followed the next month."}],"wiki":"Lytton Report"},
    "ww2-091": {"questions":["Under Article 1 of the Covenant the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took 2 years to complete, since a member could leave only after giving that much notice.","Tokyo justified the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by claiming a special position in China and a responsibility for order in East Asia that it would not share."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese withdrawal from the League of Nations</b> was announced in February 1933 and completed 2 years later, and it was the first time a great power walked out of the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League had sent a commission of enquiry to Manchuria, and its report of October 1932 found that the Japanese attack of the previous September had not been legitimate self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also held that the new state of Manchukuo owed its existence to Japanese troops and officials rather than to any movement of the local people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Assembly adopted the report on 24 February 1933 the Japanese delegation walked out of the chamber and never came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cabinet decided on 17 March to give notice, did so 10 days later, and under Article 1 of the Covenant a member could leave only after 2 years’ notice, so the withdrawal took effect in March 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese government explained itself by saying that Japan had a special position in China and that its views need not agree with those of other nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It would keep peace and order in East Asia on its own responsibility, and would oppose any attempt by China to seek foreign support in resisting it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The League had no way to enforce its finding, and its members had no appetite for economic sanctions in the middle of a worldwide depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both countries had also signed the Kellogg-Briand Pact of 1928 renouncing war as an instrument of policy, and appeals based on it proved equally ineffective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The departure left Japan free of the one body that had judged it, and its diplomacy after 1933 was increasingly conducted outside the framework the last war had built, in growing isolation from the other great powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" 1919, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Plenipotentiary_Matsuoka_Yosuke_circa_1933.jpg/500px-Plenipotentiary_Matsuoka_Yosuke_circa_1933.jpg","title":"Matsuoka Yōsuke, chief delegate at Geneva","desc":"Matsuoka led the Japanese delegation that walked out of the League Assembly in February 1933, and was later foreign minister.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plenipotentiary_Matsuoka_Yosuke_circa_1933.jpg","alt":"Black and white portrait of a moustached man in a dark three-piece suit seated with his hands on his knees."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the withdrawal take 2 years to complete?","a":"Under Article 1 of the Covenant a member could leave only after 2 years' notice. The cabinet decided on 17 March 1933 and gave notice 10 days later, so the departure took effect in March 1935."},{"q":"Why did the League's finding change nothing on the ground?","a":"It had no way to enforce the finding, and its members had no appetite for economic sanctions in the middle of a worldwide depression, so a judgement was all the organisation could produce."},{"q":"Why did Japan claim its views need not agree with other nations'?","a":"The government explained that Japan had a special position in China and would keep peace and order in East Asia on its own responsibility, opposing any Chinese attempt to seek foreign support in resisting it."}]},
    "ww2-092": {"questions":["The emperor’s own insistence that the revolt be crushed ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the purge that followed left the army’s rival faction in command.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the work of officers who wanted a government whose policies would suit the army’s plans for expansion on the Asian continent."],"abstract":"The <b>February 26 Incident</b> was the attempted coup of 1936 in which young Japanese army officers seized central Tokyo and murdered members of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 22 officers and 1,400 men revolted in the early hours of 26 February and terrorised the capital for 3.5 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They took the prime minister’s residence, the Diet building, the war and home ministries, the police headquarters and the General Staff building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They killed the finance minister, the Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal and an army inspector general, and tried to kill the prime minister and the grand chamberlain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rebels belonged to the army’s radical Imperial Way current, which held that corrupt ministers and court advisers stood between the emperor and his people, and they wanted a government whose policies would suit the army’s plans for the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> For a time the rising seemed to be succeeding, and senior officers sympathetic to it drafted a statement urging the men back to their units rather than condemning them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The emperor then declared plainly that the revolt must be suppressed, and it collapsed on 29 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The punishment was severe: the ringleaders were sentenced to death, along with the two writers held to have shaped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The purge that followed removed the Imperial Way faction from the army and left its rivals in charge of a service that now frightened every politician in Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came a year after the campaign against the theory of the emperor as an organ of the state, which had already destroyed the constitutional argument for party government, and no party cabinet governed Japan again before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"The 2.26 Incident of 1936,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description07.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Army Factional Infighting,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description05.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Dispute over the Emperor as an Organ of Government Theory,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description04.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/February_26_Incident%2C_in_Police_Department%2C_01.jpg/1280px-February_26_Incident%2C_in_Police_Department%2C_01.jpg","title":"Rebel troops in the snow, 27 February 1936","desc":"Soldiers of the rising crowd the courtyard of the Metropolitan Police headquarters in Tokyo, their rifles stacked in the snow.","credit":"Kōyō Ishikawa, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:February_26_Incident,_in_Police_Department,_01.jpg","alt":"Aerial black and white photograph of hundreds of soldiers in greatcoats standing about a snow-covered courtyard among stacked rifles."},"why":[{"q":"Why did senior officers draft a statement urging the men back rather than condemning them?","a":"For a time the rising seemed to be succeeding and some of them were sympathetic to it, so the army's first instinct was to bring the rebels in quietly rather than to treat them as enemies of the state."},{"q":"Why did the emperor's declaration end it in 3 days?","a":"The rebels' whole claim was that they acted for an emperor kept from his people by corrupt ministers, so his plain statement that the revolt must be suppressed removed the only authority they had appealed to."},{"q":"Why did suppressing a coup leave the army more powerful?","a":"The purge removed the Imperial Way faction and left its rivals in charge of a service that now frightened every politician in Tokyo, and no party cabinet governed Japan again before the war."}],"wiki":"February 26 incident"},
    "ww2-099": {"questions":["Chiang Kai-shek’s detention at Xi’an in December 1936 set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in motion, and his release produced an outburst of nationalism across China.","A manifesto from Shaanxi announced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in September 1937, abolishing the soviet governments and putting the Red Army under the national command."],"abstract":"The <b>Second United Front</b> was the alliance of 1937 between the Chinese Nationalist government and the Chinese Communist Party against the Japanese invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two sides had fought a civil war for a decade, and that division was one of the reasons foreign governments doubted whether a country so split against itself could be helped at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The turn came in December 1936, when Chiang Kai-shek was detained at Xi’an by one of his own commanders from the 12th to the 25th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His release on Christmas Day set off what the American ambassador in China described as a spontaneous outburst of nationalism across the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Negotiations between the national government and the Communists in the north-west were already being reported by American diplomats in the spring of 1937, months before the fighting began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Communist commander Zhu De was telling foreign audiences by then that a proposal of a national front against Japan had brought China’s 20-year civil war to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The formal announcement came in September 1937, in a manifesto that the Communist central committee issued from Shaanxi and that the American consul general at Hankou reported home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It declared support for Sun Yat-sen’s three principles, cancelled the movements to overthrow Nationalist political power including the forcible confiscation of land, and abolished the existing soviet governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Red Army was reorganised as part of the National Revolutionary Army under the government’s military command and awaited orders for the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had spent years trying to detach northern China from the central government with anti-communist slogans, and its invasion produced exactly the unity those slogans had been meant to prevent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, 24 September 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d609. [Open access]","\"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, 12 January 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1936, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1936v04/d460. [Open access]","\"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, 2 June 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d85. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Chiang_Kai-shek_after_Xi%27an_incident.jpg/500px-Chiang_Kai-shek_after_Xi%27an_incident.jpg","title":"Chiang Kai-shek returns to Nanjing, December 1936","desc":"Chiang and Soong Mei-ling are met at the airfield after his release at Xi’an, the crisis that opened the way to the united front.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Kai-shek_after_Xi%27an_incident.jpg","alt":"Grainy black and white photograph of a crowd of officials and officers in overcoats greeting a couple at an airfield."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the two sides ally after a decade of civil war?","a":"Chiang's detention at Xi'an in December 1936 and his release on Christmas Day set off what the American ambassador described as a spontaneous outburst of nationalism across the whole country, and negotiations were being reported by the spring of 1937."},{"q":"Why did the Communists cancel their own programme in the manifesto?","a":"They declared support for Sun Yat-sen's three principles, abandoned the movements to overthrow Nationalist power including forcible confiscation of land, and abolished the soviet governments, which was the price of the Red Army serving under the government's command."},{"q":"Why did Japan's own policy help produce the alliance?","a":"It had spent years trying to detach northern China from the central government with anti-communist slogans, and its invasion produced exactly the unity between Nationalists and Communists those slogans had been meant to prevent."}],"wiki":"Second United Front"},
    "ww2-093": {"questions":["A postwar tribunal called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the cornerstone of the whole edifice of Japanese preparation for war, though it began in the service ministries.","The Conference of Five Ministers approved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 7 August 1936, 6 months after a rising by young officers had silenced the political parties."],"abstract":"The <b>Basis of National Policy</b> was the decision of August 1936 in which the Japanese cabinet committed the country to expansion in two directions at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began in the service ministries rather than in the cabinet as a whole, when the war and navy ministers agreed a draft between themselves on 30 June 1936 and laid it before their three colleagues the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their text said Japan should realise the spirit of the imperial way by a consistent policy of overseas expansion and correct the aggressive policies of the great powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Conference of Five Ministers approved the plan on 7 August and put its decisions into a signed statement 4 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agreed policy was to secure a firm position on the Asian continent while advancing into and developing the South Seas, and to complete Japan’s armament so that it could do both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That meant preparing against the Soviet Union in the north and against Britain and the United States in the south, which is a programme for two wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judgment of the postwar tribunal called it the cornerstone of the whole edifice of Japanese preparation for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came 6 months after the February rising had frightened the political parties into silence and made the army’s wishes very difficult to refuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same cabinet restored the rule that the war and navy ministers must be officers on the active list, which handed the army a veto over the formation of any government at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within a year Japan was at war in China, which absorbed the resources the northern half of the plan required, and the southern half was pursued from 1940 onwards instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Stillbirth of the UGAKI Cabinet,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description08.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"The 2.26 Incident of 1936,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description07.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/K%C5%8Dki_Hirota_Cabinet_19360309.jpg/960px-K%C5%8Dki_Hirota_Cabinet_19360309.jpg","title":"The Hirota cabinet, March 1936","desc":"Prime Minister Hirota Kōki and his ministers, the government whose Conference of Five Ministers approved the policy five months later.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C5%8Dki_Hirota_Cabinet_19360309.jpg","alt":"Black and white group photograph of about fifteen men in formal dress and uniform on a staircase, with Japanese calligraphy overwritten across it."},"why":[{"q":"Why is committing to both directions a programme for two wars?","a":"Securing a firm position on the Asian continent meant preparing against the Soviet Union in the north, while advancing into the South Seas meant preparing against Britain and the United States, so the plan named two sets of enemies at once."},{"q":"Why did the cabinet find the army's wishes hard to refuse in 1936?","a":"The decision came 6 months after the February rising had frightened the political parties into silence, and the same cabinet restored the rule that the service ministers be serving officers, which handed the army a veto over forming any government."},{"q":"Why was only the southern half of the plan pursued after 1937?","a":"Within a year Japan was at war in China, which absorbed the resources the northern half required, so the advance southward was taken up from 1940 while the preparation against the Soviet Union went unfunded."}]},
    "ww2-094": {"questions":["Planners fixed the ultimate reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1940 as everything between eastern India and Burma on one side and Australia and New Zealand on the other.","The territories taken under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were governed for Japan’s benefit rather than freed, which is why the phrase is remembered bitterly across the region."],"abstract":"The <b>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</b> was the name Japan gave from 1940 to the bloc of Asian territories it meant to take from the Western empires and lead itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It replaced the earlier talk of a new order in East Asia, and the change of phrase followed the German victories in Europe in the summer of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Britain, France and the Netherlands in eclipse, Japanese leaders decided to seize their possessions in East and Southeast Asia and the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Army, navy and foreign ministry representatives agreed on 16 July 1940 that the ultimate goal ran from eastern India and Burma to Australia and New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The immediate objective was narrower and still enormous, covering Hong Kong, French Indochina, Thailand, Malaya and the Netherlands East Indies with their oil, rubber and tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The announced purpose was to drive the Western imperial powers out of Asia, and that promise was made to peoples who had lived under those powers for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The actual purpose was to secure the raw materials Japan was importing from the West, so that it would no longer depend on suppliers who could cut it off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States was the main source of the oil, steel and iron the Japanese military used, and it was tightening its restrictions through 1940 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order it set out to overturn had been built at Washington in 1921, where the powers agreed naval limits and a treaty guaranteeing the integrity of China, on the older American principle of equal commercial access rather than territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territories taken under it after 1941 were governed for Japan’s benefit rather than freed, which is why the slogan is remembered bitterly across the region, though the empires it attacked never regained their old authority either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Secretary of State John Hay and the Open Door in China, 1899–1900,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/hay-and-china. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Japanese_Empire_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg/1280px-Japanese_Empire_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg.png","title":"The Japanese empire at its greatest extent","desc":"A globe showing the territory Japan held or occupied in 1942, the reach the Co-Prosperity Sphere had been announced to justify.","credit":"Emok, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Empire_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg","alt":"Orthographic globe centred on East Asia with Japan in dark green and a large swathe of Southeast Asia and the western Pacific in lighter green."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the phrase change in the summer of 1940?","a":"The German victories in Europe left Britain, France and the Netherlands in eclipse, so Japanese leaders decided to seize their possessions in East and Southeast Asia and needed a name for a bloc rather than for a new order in East Asia."},{"q":"Why did the announced purpose differ from the actual one?","a":"The promise to drive the Western powers out of Asia was made to peoples who had lived under them for generations, while the aim was to secure the raw materials Japan was importing from suppliers who could cut it off."},{"q":"Why is the slogan remembered bitterly across the region?","a":"The territories taken after 1941 were governed for Japan's benefit rather than freed, so the promise of liberation was answered with another occupation, though the empires it attacked never regained their old authority either."}],"wiki":"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"},
    "ww2-095": {"questions":["Within a day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> large Kwantung Army units were converging from Manchuria, which a postwar tribunal read as proof that the war was chosen.","American opinion swung firmly behind China after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, having been divided while the country was split between Nationalists and Communists."],"abstract":"The <b>Marco Polo Bridge Incident</b> was the clash of 7 July 1937 outside Beijing that grew within weeks into a full war between Japan and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese garrison troops held an unusual night exercise at Lukouchiao, an old stone crossing on the road south out of the city, and then reported that one of their soldiers was missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They demanded to enter the walled town of Wanping to search for him, and the fighting began while their complaint was still being negotiated between the two sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese issued an ultimatum for the town’s surrender on the following afternoon, took substantial casualties in the battle that followed, and proposed a truce themselves on 10 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The incident might have been regarded as closed at that point, and the postwar tribunal found plainly that this was not what the Japanese army intended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Within a day of the first shots, large units of the Kwantung Army were converging on the scene from Manchuria, and reinforcements followed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese officials told an American diplomat in Nanjing the next morning that the clash was clearly premeditated, and their foreign ministry had already protested to the Japanese embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tribunal read the way a local skirmish was allowed to become a full offensive as evidence that the Army General Staff had chosen war with China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had been working to detach the five provinces of northern China from the central government for 2 years, building up local self-government there and using anti-communist slogans to justify it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The date is the usual starting point for the war in Asia, and the government in Tokyo was no better able to restrain its army in 1937 than it had been in 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","\"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, 9 July 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d107. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Thomas_Child_-_Marco_Polo_Bridge_%28Lugou_Qiao%29%2C_Peking%2C_1877_NA01-75.jpg/960px-Thomas_Child_-_Marco_Polo_Bridge_%28Lugou_Qiao%29%2C_Peking%2C_1877_NA01-75.jpg","title":"The Marco Polo Bridge, photographed in 1877","desc":"The stone bridge at Lugouqiao south of Beijing, 60 years before the night exercise that turned into a war.","credit":"Thomas Child, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Child_-_Marco_Polo_Bridge_%28Lugou_Qiao%29,_Peking,_1877_NA01-75.jpg","alt":"Sepia photograph of a long many-arched stone bridge with a balustrade of carved posts, crossing a wide shallow riverbed."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the tribunal find the army had chosen war rather than stumbled into it?","a":"The Japanese proposed a truce themselves on 10 July and the incident might have been closed there, but within a day of the first shots large Kwantung Army units were converging from Manchuria with reinforcements behind them."},{"q":"Why did Chinese officials call the clash premeditated at once?","a":"Japan had been working for 2 years to detach the five northern provinces from the central government, building up local self-government and using anti-communist slogans, so a night exercise outside Beijing fitted a pattern already visible."},{"q":"Why does the incident echo the Manchurian one of 1931?","a":"The government in Tokyo was no better able to restrain its army in 1937 than it had been in 1931, so a local action by officers in the field again decided national policy."}],"wiki":"Marco Polo Bridge incident"},
    "ww2-096": {"questions":["Japan held the cities and railways but never the countryside during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a campaign meant to be short became a drain on everything it had.","The Nationalists and Communists were forced into an alliance by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Communist party announced in September 1937."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Sino-Japanese War</b> ran from July 1937 to Japan’s surrender in 1945 and was the largest war fought in Asia in the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a clash at the Marco Polo Bridge, which Chinese officials told American diplomats at once had been premeditated, and out of 6 years of Japanese encroachment in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japanese forces took Beijing in July 1937, fought a 3-month battle for Shanghai from August, and reached the Chinese capital at Nanjing in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chinese government refused the peace terms passed to it through the German ambassador in November and December 1937, and moved its capital inland rather than surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither side declared war on the other, which is why it was called an incident for years and why the neutrality laws of other countries never came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war forced the Nationalists and the Communists into an alliance, and the Communist party announced in September 1937 that it would cooperate with the whole nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan held the cities and the railways and could not hold the countryside, and the campaign it expected to finish quickly became a drain on everything it had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States was still the main supplier of the oil, steel and iron the Japanese military needed, and it began restricting their sale as the war dragged on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pressure was one of the reasons Japan turned south in 1940 and 1941 towards the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia, while the same years drew the United States into the quarrel through credits to China and then Lend-Lease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war merged into the wider conflict after the attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941, and Chinese resistance held down a large part of the Japanese army until the surrender in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","\"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, 24 September 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d609. [Open access]","\"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, 9 July 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d107. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Japanese_Occupation_of_China_1940.svg/1280px-Japanese_Occupation_of_China_1940.svg.png","title":"Japanese-occupied China in 1940","desc":"The area under Japanese control three years into the war, running along the coast and the railways and leaving the interior to the Chinese government.","credit":"Emilfaro, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Occupation_of_China_1940.svg","alt":"Map of China with the eastern provinces and Manchuria shaded pink for Japanese control and the western interior left pale."},"why":[{"q":"Why did neither side declare war on the other?","a":"It was called an incident for years, which kept the neutrality laws of other countries from coming into force and so left China able to buy arms and Japan able to buy American oil and steel."},{"q":"Why did a war Japan expected to finish quickly become a drain?","a":"It held the cities and the railways and could not hold the countryside, and the Chinese government moved its capital inland rather than accept the peace terms passed through the German ambassador in late 1937."},{"q":"Why did the war in China push Japan southward?","a":"The United States was the main supplier of the oil, steel and iron its military needed and began restricting their sale as the fighting dragged on, which made the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia the alternative."}],"wiki":"Second Sino-Japanese War","places":["adm0:chn","adm0:jpn","pol:ccp","pol:japan","pol:roc"]},
    "ww2-097": {"questions":["Chiang Kai-shek committed his best divisions to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than yield the city, in a deliberate choice to fight where foreigners could see it.","The retreating Chinese armies fell back up the Yangtze after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Japanese forces followed them to the capital within weeks."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Shanghai</b> was the 3-month fight for China’s largest city in the autumn of 1937, and it turned a northern incident into a national war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting followed the killing of a Japanese naval officer named Oyama at a city airfield, which gave Japan the reason it wanted for landing troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shanghai was where the foreign powers had their concessions and their newspapers, and the American consul general had warned a week beforehand that a clash there was a real danger and that the foreign concessions would not be spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chiang Kai-shek committed his best divisions rather than yield the city, which was a deliberate choice to fight where foreigners could see it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese held on for about 3 months against naval gunfire from the river and attack from the air before their line finally gave way in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> China appealed to the League of Nations while the battle was still going on, and the powers met at Brussels that November under the treaty they had signed in 1921 to guarantee its integrity, without agreeing to do anything effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain and the United States each offered their good offices and were refused, and the Japanese army preferred that Germany should be asked to act as go-between instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cost to China was enormous, since the divisions lost at Shanghai were the trained and German-equipped core of an army it could not quickly replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The retreating Chinese armies fell back up the Yangtze towards the capital at Nanjing, and the Japanese army followed them there within a matter of weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the battle bought was time and the attention of the world, and the length of the Chinese resistance that autumn is the reason foreign opinion turned as it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","\"The Consul General at Shanghai (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, 7 August 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d377. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Japanese_Special_Naval_Landing_Forces_in_Battle_of_Shanghai_1937.jpg/960px-Japanese_Special_Naval_Landing_Forces_in_Battle_of_Shanghai_1937.jpg","title":"Japanese naval landing troops at Shanghai, 1937","desc":"Special Naval Landing Forces in gas masks behind a machine gun in the rubble of the city during the autumn battle.","credit":"Ministry of the Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Special_Naval_Landing_Forces_in_Battle_of_Shanghai_1937.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a dozen helmeted soldiers in gas masks crouched behind a machine gun among broken bricks."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Chiang commit his best divisions to a city he might lose?","a":"Shanghai was where the foreign powers had their concessions and their newspapers, so fighting there was a deliberate choice to resist where foreigners could see it rather than to hold ground he could defend more cheaply."},{"q":"Why was the cost of the battle so hard for China to bear?","a":"The divisions lost at Shanghai were the trained and German-equipped core of an army it could not quickly replace, so a 3-month defence spent the best troops China had at the start of an 8-year war."},{"q":"Why did the powers meeting at Brussels achieve nothing?","a":"They met under the 1921 treaty guaranteeing Chinese integrity but agreed on nothing effective, Britain and the United States each had their good offices refused, and the Japanese army preferred Germany as go-between."}],"wiki":"Battle of Shanghai"},
    "ww2-098": {"questions":["Burial societies counted more than 155,000 bodies after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them with their hands tied behind their backs.","Foreigners who stayed through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> organised a safety zone for refugees, and the missionaries among them kept letters, diaries and photographs."],"abstract":"The <b>Nanjing Massacre</b> was the mass killing, rape and looting carried out by Japanese forces in the Chinese capital after it fell on 13 December 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese demand for surrender was ignored and the city was bombarded, and an American naval officer on the river reported 4 days before it fell that the gates were shut, fires were burning on both banks and the bombing had been intensive all day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The postwar tribunal found that prisoners of war were killed in groups by machine-gun fire along the bank of the Yangtze within 72 hours of surrendering, more than 30,000 of them, without even a pretence of trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its estimate was that over 200,000 civilians and prisoners were murdered in the city and its surroundings in the first 6 weeks of the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burial societies and other organisations counted more than 155,000 bodies, most of them with their hands tied behind their backs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunal put the number of rapes in the first month at approximately 20,000, and found that women and girls of every age were attacked throughout the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soldiers stopped unarmed civilians in the road and shot them, looted shops and warehouses and then burned them, and roughly a third of the city was destroyed by fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners who stayed in the city organised a safety zone for refugees, and a Japanese embassy official warned them that the army meant to make it bad for Nanjing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An international committee ran a safety zone that was still feeding about 250,000 refugees in January, when the Japanese army stopped it selling rice and flour of its own accord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American missionaries who stayed kept letters, diaries, reports and photographs that are now an archive at Yale, and opinion in the United States hardened as the news came out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Yale Divinity Library, \"Nanking Massacre Project,\" Yale University Library, https://web.library.yale.edu/divinity/nanking. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","\"The Commander of the United States Yangtze Patrol (Marquart) to the Chief of Naval Operations, 9 December 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v04/d485. [Open access]","\"The Third Secretary of Embassy in China (Allison) to the Secretary of State, 22 January 1938\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d54. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Minnie_Vautrin_helped_the_refugees_in_1937.jpg/1280px-Minnie_Vautrin_helped_the_refugees_in_1937.jpg","title":"Refugees in Nanjing, 1937","desc":"A column of women and children moving through the city under a flag, among those sheltered by the foreign-run safety zone.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minnie_Vautrin_helped_the_refugees_in_1937.jpg","alt":"Sepia photograph of a long file of women in winter coats walking down a narrow street behind a white flag."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the scale of the killing documented as precisely as it is?","a":"Burial societies and other organisations counted more than 155,000 bodies, most with their hands tied behind their backs, and the postwar tribunal put the total at over 200,000 civilians and prisoners in the first 6 weeks."},{"q":"Why did foreigners in the city organise a safety zone?","a":"An international committee ran it for refugees and was still feeding about 250,000 of them in January, when the Japanese army stopped it selling rice and flour of its own accord."},{"q":"Why did American opinion harden as a result?","a":"The missionaries who stayed kept letters, diaries, reports and photographs, now an archive at Yale, so the news came out with a documentary record behind it rather than as a distant rumour."}],"wiki":"Nanjing Massacre"},
    "ww2-100": {"questions":["The largest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at Nomonhan in May 1939 and ended that September with the defeat of the Japanese forces engaged.","A postwar tribunal could not establish whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were ordered from Tokyo or begun by the army in the field on its own account."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet-Japanese border conflicts</b> were the undeclared battles fought along the frontiers of Manchukuo, Mongolia and the Soviet Far East between 1932 and 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia had been the army’s first strategic enemy in the national defence plan of 1907, displaced by the United States only in the revision of 1923, and planners kept up their preparations for a Soviet war once Manchuria gave the two countries a common border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In July 1938 Japanese guards were reinforced near Lake Khasan and a diplomat was sent to Moscow to demand that Soviet troops withdraw from a hill of strategic value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war minister and the chief of the General Staff tried to get the emperor’s approval for an attack, misrepresenting the situation to him to obtain it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From January 1939 Japanese armed detachments carried out raids across the Outer Mongolian border on several occasions, as part of a plan to bring that country within Japan’s sphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The largest action began at Nomonhan in May 1939, when Kwantung Army units attacked Soviet forces on the Manchukuoan frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew into a campaign of considerable magnitude and ended in September 1939 with the defeat of the Japanese forces that had been engaged in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal that examined it later could find no evidence of whether the attack was ordered from Tokyo or begun by the army in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That question runs through the whole period, since an army that had staged its own pretext for war in 1931 was very hard for any government in Tokyo to restrain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The defeat weighed heavily in the argument that followed about whether Japan should strike north against the Soviet Union or south towards the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Khalkhin_Gol_Soviet_tanks_1939.jpg/960px-Khalkhin_Gol_Soviet_tanks_1939.jpg","title":"Soviet tanks at Khalkhin Gol, 1939","desc":"Camouflaged Soviet armour on the Mongolian steppe during the summer campaign that ended in the defeat of the Japanese force.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalkhin_Gol_Soviet_tanks_1939.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of five light tanks draped with cut brush standing in long grass on open rolling ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan keep preparing for a war with the Soviet Union?","a":"Russia had been the army's first strategic enemy in the defence plan of 1907, displaced by the United States only in 1923, and taking Manchuria gave the two countries a common border to plan across."},{"q":"Why could the tribunal not say who ordered the Nomonhan attack?","a":"It found no evidence of whether the attack was ordered from Tokyo or begun by the army in the field, which is the question running through the whole period for a force that had staged its own pretext in 1931."},{"q":"Why did the defeat matter to Japan's later choices?","a":"It weighed heavily in the argument about whether to strike north against the Soviet Union or south towards the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia, and the northern option lost."}],"wiki":"Soviet–Japanese border conflicts","places":["adm0:jpn","adm0:mng","adm0:rus","pol:japan","pol:mongolia_pr","pol:ussr"]},
    "ww2-101": {"questions":["A tribunal later held that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amounted to an aggressive war rather than the border clash the defence described, the fighting having run 4 months.","Japan and the Soviet Union agreed the boundary they had fought over in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only in June 1940, and a neutrality pact followed the next April."],"abstract":"The <b>Battles of Khalkhin Gol</b> were the undeclared campaign fought from May to September 1939 on the eastern boundary of Outer Mongolia, where it adjoins the Manchurian province of Heilungkiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Outer Mongolia mattered to Japanese planners because it flanks the Trans-Siberian Railway, the line joining Soviet territory in Europe to Soviet territory in the Far East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chief of staff of the Kwantung Army argued in 1936 that extending Japanese and Manchukuoan power there might remove Soviet influence from the whole region without fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet Union had already answered that danger with a mutual assistance agreement with the Mongolian People's Republic, under which its troops were stationed in a number of Mongolian towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hostilities opened on 11 May 1939 when several hundred Japanese reconnaissance troops attacked the Mongolian border guards, and the further small attacks that followed were each repulsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both sides brought up support, and from 28 May the fighting resumed on a large scale with aircraft, artillery and tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese case was that the affair was a boundary dispute, the frontier being the Khalkhin Gol river itself, where the Mongolian side placed it some 20 kilometres (12 miles) further east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The struggle spread across a front of 50 to 60 kilometres (31 to 37 miles), and the Tokyo tribunal put Japanese killed, wounded and captured above 50,000 against Mongolian and Soviet losses above 9,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kwantung Army announced the failure of its frontier war on 5 September, days after the German invasion of Poland, and a new cabinet in Tokyo had the fighting stopped within a week and the boundary itself agreed the following June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A neutrality pact between the two states followed in April 1941, and by then Japan's advance had turned south towards the oil and rubber of the European empires in Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), 49,396–49,404, https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","I. A. Degtev, \"From the Khalkhin Gol Events to the Neutrality Pact: Relations Between the USSR and Japan in the Reports of Soviet Diplomats in Tokyo (1939–1941),\" <i>Russian Japanology Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2025): 73–92, https://doi.org/10.55105/2658-6444-2025-1-73-92. [Open access]","\"Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Pact, April 13, 1941,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s1.asp. [Open access]","Rina Na and Shuo Zhang, \"A Review of Chinese Academic Research on the Strategic Role of People's Republic of Mongolia During World War II,\" <i>Mongolian Journal of International Affairs</i> 26 (2025): 125–46, https://doi.org/10.5564/mjia.v26i1.4450. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/The_Kwantung_Army_in_Mongolia_1939.png","title":"Japanese infantry on the Mongolian steppe, July 1939","desc":"A column of the 23rd Division of the Imperial Japanese Army on the march across open grassland during the summer fighting.","credit":"Japanese press photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Kwantung_Army_in_Mongolia_1939.png","alt":"A tinted photograph of a long column of soldiers in field caps, carrying rifles and packs, marching away across flat treeless grassland."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Japanese planners care about Outer Mongolia?","a":"It flanks the Trans-Siberian Railway, the line joining Soviet territory in Europe to Soviet territory in the Far East, and the Kwantung Army's chief of staff argued in 1936 that holding it might remove Soviet influence from the region without a fight."},{"q":"Why was the border-dispute defence rejected?","a":"The fighting ran 4 months across a front of 50 to 60 kilometres and drew in aircraft, artillery and tanks, which the tribunal held made it an aggressive war rather than a clash between border guards."},{"q":"Why did the outcome matter beyond the frontier?","a":"The army's case for striking north at the Soviet Union lost its force, the two states settled the boundary and then signed a neutrality pact, and Japan's expansion turned south towards Southeast Asia instead."}],"wiki":"Battles of Khalkhin Gol","places":["pl:q993299"]},
    "ww2-102": {"questions":["Money set aside in March 1940 for a war against the Soviet Union was switched to industry, an early move in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that ended at Pearl Harbor.","German victories in western Europe left the Dutch and French colonies of Asia without protectors, which is what made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look possible to ministers in Tokyo."],"abstract":"The <b>Southern Advance</b> was the strategy that took Japan into Southeast Asia rather than into Siberia, and it settled an argument the country's services had been having for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army had built its plans around a northern war and its foreign minister still regarded the Soviet Union as Japan's foremost enemy in 1939, while the navy's interest lay in the resources of the South Seas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That assumption collapsed in August of that year, when Germany signed a non-aggression pact with Moscow and the cabinet in Tokyo fell as a result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The defeat at Nomonhan the following month took away the remaining argument that the army could win a northern war quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan's ambassadors in Berlin and Rome then worked with the German foreign minister for a reconciliation with the Soviet Union, so that the three Axis states could turn on the Western powers alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their purpose, the Tokyo tribunal found, was to clear the way for Japan's advance to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1940 money and materials allotted to military preparations against the Soviet Union were partly diverted to industry meant to end Japanese dependence on American supply, and the Overseas Ministry drew up plans for a move into Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German attack on the Netherlands that May put the Netherlands East Indies in question, and the Japanese foreign minister at once invited Berlin to say where it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tripartite Pact of September 1940 had Germany and Italy recognise Japanese leadership in a new order in greater East Asia, and a neutrality pact with the Soviet Union followed in April 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Agreements with Vichy France then let Japanese forces into Indochina, and the embargo with which the United States answered left Tokyo choosing between withdrawal from China and war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), 48,870–48,919, https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","\"Three-Power Pact Between Germany, Italy, and Japan, Signed at Berlin, September 27, 1940,\" arts. 1–2, in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/triparti.asp. [Open access]","\"Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Pact, April 13, 1941,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s1.asp. [Open access]","I. A. Degtev, \"From the Khalkhin Gol Events to the Neutrality Pact: Relations Between the USSR and Japan in the Reports of Soviet Diplomats in Tokyo (1939–1941),\" <i>Russian Japanology Review</i> 8, no. 1 (2025): 73–92, https://doi.org/10.55105/2658-6444-2025-1-73-92. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the northern strategy lose its case in 1939?","a":"Germany's non-aggression pact with Moscow destroyed the assumption behind it and brought down the cabinet, and the defeat at Nomonhan the following month removed the argument that the army could win such a war quickly."},{"q":"Why did Japan's ambassadors work for a Soviet reconciliation?","a":"So that Germany, Italy and Japan could direct their efforts against the Western powers alone, which the Tokyo tribunal found was meant to clear the way for Japan's advance to the south."},{"q":"Why did German victories in Europe matter in Tokyo?","a":"A defeated colonial power could not defend its Asian possessions, and the attack on the Netherlands in May 1940 put the Netherlands East Indies in question at once."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Japanese_advance_to_Lang_Son1940.jpg","title":"Japanese troops advancing on Lang Son, September 1940","desc":"Soldiers riding lorries with regimental and national flags along a road in northern Indochina, in the first move of the advance south.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_advance_to_Lang_Son1940.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a line of open lorries packed with helmeted soldiers carrying flags, on a road beside a river with hills behind."}},
    "ww2-103": {"questions":["Italy joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a year after Germany and Japan had signed it, and its published text never once named the country it was really aimed at.","A secret protocol annexed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> barred either signatory from making agreements with Moscow, which Tokyo believed Berlin had broken in August 1939."],"abstract":"The <b>Anti-Comintern Pact</b> was the agreement of 25 November 1936 by which Japan and Germany undertook to act together against the Communist International.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its three short articles bound them to keep each other informed of that body's activities, to confer on the necessary measures of defence and carry them out in close co-operation, and to invite other states whose internal peace was menaced to join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text ran for 5 years and named no country, although it was directed at the Soviet Union, with which Japan was then in hostilities on the Manchurian frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The approach had begun in October 1935 in informal talks opened by the Japanese military attaché in Berlin with the approval of the army general staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ambassador took the negotiations over the following spring and initialled the text with the German foreign minister on 23 October; the Privy Council ratified it a month later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Germany had wanted a general military alliance and the Japanese general staff had favoured one, but opinion at home was not ready for it and the pact was presented instead as a purely defensive measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A secret protocol went with it, committing each side to neutrality if the Soviet Union attacked the other without provocation and to make no agreement with Moscow that ran against the spirit of the pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy joined on 6 November 1937, a year after it and Germany had pledged a common foreign policy in the treaty of friendship that became known as the Rome-Berlin Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pact belonged to a run of moves by which the German foreign ministry sought the diplomatic initiative, alongside withdrawal from the League of Nations, open rearmament and the naval agreement with Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its limits showed in August 1939, when Germany made its own pact with Moscow: Tokyo treated that as a breach, and the protest it drafted was not delivered until the invasion of Poland was finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Anti-Comintern Pact: German-Japanese Agreement and Supplementary Protocol, Signed at Berlin, November 25, 1936,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tri1.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Alliance in World War II,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-alliance-in-world-war-ii. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), 48,562–48,563, 48,737–48,738, 48,875–48,876, https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Foreign Policy, 1933–1945,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-foreign-policy-1933-1945. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Antikominternpakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/antikomintern. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the published text name no country?","a":"It was framed as a defence against the Communist International rather than against a state, which let Tokyo present it to the Privy Council as purely defensive at a time when Japanese opinion was not ready for an alliance with Germany."},{"q":"Why did Germany not get the alliance it wanted?","a":"Public opinion in Japan was not yet prepared for a general military alliance, so the cabinet's contractual powers were limited and the two states signed an agreement about the Communist International instead."},{"q":"Why did Tokyo treat the 1939 pact with Moscow as a breach?","a":"The secret protocol bound each signatory to make no agreement with the Soviet Union that ran against the spirit of the pact, and Germany's non-aggression treaty of August 1939 did exactly that."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Japan_Germany_Anti_Commintern_Pact_25_November_1936.jpg/1280px-Japan_Germany_Anti_Commintern_Pact_25_November_1936.jpg","title":"The signature page of the pact, Berlin, 25 November 1936","desc":"The Japanese copy of the agreement, with the closing article, the date in the eleventh year of Showa and the two ambassadors' seals.","credit":"World Imaging, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_Germany_Anti_Commintern_Pact_25_November_1936.jpg","alt":"Photograph of an open document written in vertical Japanese script, with two signatures and two red wax seals hanging from ribbons."},"wiki":"Anti-Comintern Pact","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-104": {"questions":["The third article of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised mutual assistance if a signatory were attacked by a power not yet involved in the European war or the Chinese conflict.","Hungary, Romania, Slovakia and Bulgaria each acceded to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between November 1940 and March 1941, and that is how they became Axis powers."],"abstract":"The <b>Tripartite Pact</b> was the treaty signed in Berlin on 27 September 1940 by which Germany, Italy and Japan formalised the alliance that made them the Axis powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its preamble held it a condition of any lasting peace that all nations be given each its own proper place, and set the three to co-operate in their efforts in greater East Asia and in Europe respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan recognised the leadership of Germany and Italy in establishing a new order in Europe, and the two European states recognised Japan's in greater East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third article bound all three to assist one another with political, economic and military means should any of them be attacked by a power not then involved in the European war or the Chinese conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fifth article said the terms did not affect the political status existing between each signatory and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Negotiations had begun in the middle of 1938 and were fruitless for a year and a half, because Germany wanted an alliance against all its potential enemies where Japan wanted the Anti-Comintern Pact developed against the Soviet Union alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What settled it was the state of both: the German government needed allies for the campaign it was already planning, and Japan was isolated by its war in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both hoped the sight of the alliance would keep the United States out of the war and bring Britain to terms, and in Washington it did the opposite, linking the conflicts in Europe and Asia and making China a potential ally against fascism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany then pressed the states of southeastern Europe to accede, offering economic aid, military protection and territory, and Hungary joined on 20 November 1940, Romania 3 days later, Slovakia the day after that and Bulgaria on 1 March 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Croatia followed, so that five more European states were bound to the alliance, and every one of Germany's European allies took some part in the murder of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Three-Power Pact Between Germany, Italy, and Japan, Signed at Berlin, September 27, 1940,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/triparti.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Alliance in World War II,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-alliance-in-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), 49,344–49,346, https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Dreimächtepakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/der-dreimaechtepakt. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the negotiations take 2 years to succeed?","a":"Germany wanted a military alliance directed against all its potential enemies, while Japan wanted the Anti-Comintern Pact developed into one aimed principally at the Soviet Union, and neither would take the other's version."},{"q":"Why did the third article matter more than the rest?","a":"By promising help against a power not yet involved in either war it was aimed at the United States, which is why the pact linked the European and Asian conflicts instead of keeping them apart."},{"q":"Why did four more states accede within 6 months?","a":"Germany offered them economic aid, military protection and territory while preparing to attack the Soviet Union, and needed transit rights, raw materials and the cooperation of the states along its route."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Signing_ceremony_for_the_Axis_Powers_Tripartite_Pact.jpg/1280px-Signing_ceremony_for_the_Axis_Powers_Tripartite_Pact.jpg","title":"The signing ceremony in Berlin, 27 September 1940","desc":"German, Italian and Japanese officials at the table and standing behind it as the treaty is signed at the Reich Chancellery.","credit":"Heinrich Hoffmann, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signing_ceremony_for_the_Axis_Powers_Tripartite_Pact.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of three men seated at a long table with papers, and two rows of uniformed and suited officials standing behind them."},"wiki":"Tripartite Pact","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-105": {"questions":["The fear that a League of Nations would freeze the world dominance of Europe and America gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> much of its force in Japan after the First World War.","The Co-Prosperity Sphere announced in 1940 dressed conquest in the language of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, promising to drive the Western empires out while securing Asian resources for Japan."],"abstract":"<b>Pan-Asianism</b> was the claim that the peoples of Asia shared a common interest against European and American dominance and that Japan should lead them out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese thinkers of the later 19th century worked the European sense of a competitive world into an older universalism, and the fusion produced a powerful ideology of expansion aimed at East and Southeast Asia and the South Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grievances behind it were real: European states had made Japan give up part of its winnings from the war with China in 1895, and Japanese opinion feared that an international organisation would restrain rising nations and sustain the dominance of the war's victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A future prime minister warned in a popular monthly that unless the peace conference recognised racial equality and opened all colonies to free trade, Japan might one day be compelled to break loose from its confinement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan joined the League of Nations all the same and worked inside it for a decade, sending some of its ablest diplomats and jurists to Geneva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By July 1939 the war and navy ministers were telling the press that interference by third powers in the building of a new order should be stamped out, and that Japan would never abandon making East Asia for East Asiatics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere announced the next year declared an intention to drive the Western imperialist nations out of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its purpose was to build Japanese wealth so the country need not depend on Western supply rather than to free the long-subject peoples, and carrying it out meant conquest and no withdrawal from China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tripartite Pact gave the claim the form of a treaty, with Germany and Italy recognising Japanese leadership in a new order in greater East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tribunal at Tokyo called the sphere a euphemism for hegemony, and the wartime plans for it took in the Soviet Far East, Outer Mongolia and half the Trans-Siberian Railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Harald Kleinschmidt, \"The Ideological Background of Japanese Expansionism, C. 1900,\" <i>Studia Historiae Oeconomicae</i> 39, no. 1 (2021): 33–54, https://doi.org/10.2478/sho-2021-0002. [Open access]","Thomas W. Burkman, \"League of Nations and Japan,\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11571. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","\"Three-Power Pact Between Germany, Italy, and Japan, Signed at Berlin, September 27, 1940,\" art. 2, in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/triparti.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), 49,294–49,296, 49,358–49,360, https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the doctrine take hold after the First World War?","a":"European states had forced Japan to give up part of its winnings from the war with China in 1895, and Japanese opinion feared that a League of Nations would restrain rising powers and preserve the dominance of the war's European and American victors."},{"q":"Why is the Co-Prosperity Sphere not evidence of liberation?","a":"The project was meant to build Japanese economic and material wealth so the country need not depend on Western supply, it required a campaign of military conquest and rule, and it did not include leaving China."},{"q":"Why does the plan for Siberia undercut the whole claim?","a":"A doctrine of Asian solidarity against European empire ended in wartime plans to annex the Maritime Province, take Outer Mongolia and share control of the Trans-Siberian Railway with Germany, which the tribunal read as hegemony under another name."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/1943_Tokyo_conference.jpg/1280px-1943_Tokyo_conference.jpg","title":"The Greater East Asia Conference, Tokyo, November 1943","desc":"Delegates of the governments Japan had installed or allied with across occupied Asia, assembled for the group photograph.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1943_Tokyo_conference.jpg","alt":"Black and white group photograph of about fifty men in suits, uniforms and robes, ranged on the steps of a colonnaded building."},"wiki":"Pan-Asianism"},
    "ww2-106": {"questions":["Article 16 of the Covenant gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its teeth, requiring every member to sever trade and financial relations with a state that went to war in breach of its promises.","Manchuria in 1931 and Abyssinia in 1935 each showed what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was worth once a great power was the aggressor and the others judged their interests to lie elsewhere."],"abstract":"<b>Collective security</b> is the principle that an aggressor against any one state should be treated as an aggressor against all the rest, and the League of Nations was the first attempt to build a system on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Members bound themselves by joining to settle their disputes peacefully, renounced secret diplomacy, undertook to reduce their armaments and pledged to respect the territorial integrity and political independence of every other member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Article 16 of the Covenant supplied the sanction: a member that went to war in disregard of its promises was deemed to have committed an act of war against all the others, who undertook at once to sever every trade and financial relation with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same article made it the Council's duty to recommend what military, naval or air forces the members should contribute, but only to recommend, so the decision to use force stayed with each government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Economic pressure had been discussed before 1914 as a way of enforcing arbitration awards, and the Covenant turned it into an instrument of deterrence in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In practice the machinery worked where the dispute was small and failed whenever a great power was the aggressor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Council met first over the Japanese seizure of Manchuria, commissioned an enquiry, adopted its report, and then watched the Japanese delegation walk out while the conquest stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States, which had never joined, could offer only a declaration that it would not recognise what force had won, and that changed nothing on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The League did impose economic sanctions on Italy for invading Abyssinia, and the chief result was to drive Rome towards Berlin rather than to save Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The organisation never became universal either, since the United States stayed out and much of the world remained under colonial rule, so a principle that depended on everyone acting together was worked by a membership that was never everyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, arts. 10–17, in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","Gordan Struić, \"Collective Security and Its Mechanisms in the Covenant of the League of Nations,\" <i>Pravni vjesnik</i> 33, no. 3–4 (2017): 145–66, https://doi.org/10.25234/pv/5041. [Open access]","Cristine Koehler Zanella, \"The Road Taken: the Institutionalization of Economic Sanctions as an Instrument at Service of International Collective Security,\" <i>Carta Internacional</i> 11, no. 2 (2016): 53–77, https://doi.org/10.21530/ci.v11n2.2016.483. [Open access]","United Nations Office at Geneva, \"The League of Nations,\" UN Library and Archives Geneva, https://www.ungeneva.org/en/library-archives/league-of-nations. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Article 16 not stop a determined aggressor?","a":"It made the trade and financial boycott automatic in words, but on force it only required the Council to recommend what each member should contribute, so the decision to act stayed with governments weighing their own interests."},{"q":"Why did the Manchurian case damage the whole idea?","a":"The League enquired, reported and adopted the report, and Japan simply walked out with the conquest intact, which showed that the machinery could establish who the aggressor was and then do nothing about it."},{"q":"Why did the League's membership matter to the principle?","a":"The idea depends on an aggressor facing everyone else at once, and the organisation never became universal: the United States never joined and much of the world was still under colonial rule."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Volkerenbond%2CVolkenbond_organisatie_voor_Internationale_Samenwerking_opgericht_op_25_januari_1919%2C_SFA022825171.jpg/1280px-Volkerenbond%2CVolkenbond_organisatie_voor_Internationale_Samenwerking_opgericht_op_25_januari_1919%2C_SFA022825171.jpg","title":"A session of the League of Nations","desc":"Delegates at their desks around a canopied rostrum, in the chamber where an aggressor was to be faced by every other member at once.","credit":"Spaarnestad photo archive, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volkerenbond,Volkenbond_organisatie_voor_Internationale_Samenwerking_opgericht_op_25_januari_1919,_SFA022825171.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph looking down into a panelled chamber where men in dark suits sit at tiered desks beside a raised rostrum under a canopy."},"wiki":"Collective security","places":["city:1159151505"]},
    "ww2-107": {"questions":["Non-recognition was all the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered, since a world depression had killed support for economic sanctions and few thought American interests in Manchuria worth a war.","After Japanese forces attacked Shanghai the secretary of state widened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by declaring that the United States no longer held itself bound by the naval limitation agreements."],"abstract":"The <b>Stimson Doctrine</b> was the American declaration of 1932 that the United States would recognise no agreement between Japan and China which violated its rights or the treaties to which it subscribed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It answered the crisis that began on 18 September 1931, when an explosion destroyed a stretch of railway near Mukden and the Japanese, who owned the line, blamed Chinese nationalists and invaded Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within months the Japanese army had overrun a region whose Chinese defenders were untrained and had set about consolidating its hold on the resources there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American policy in the Far East rested on the Open Door for equal commercial access, on the territorial integrity of China and on cooperation with the other interested powers, and the seizure challenged all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Few in Washington thought American interests there worth intervention, and a world depression left little support for economic sanctions, though the Covenant made a boycott of a covenant-breaking state the duty of every member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States sat in on League of Nations council meetings for the first time, trying to have the Kellogg-Briand Pact enforced against a state that had signed it, and the appeals proved ineffective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That pact had outlawed war as an instrument of national policy and asked its parties to settle disputes peacefully, but it provided no means of enforcement and never defined self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Non-recognition was what remained, and it proved ineffectual: Japanese forces attacked Shanghai in 1932, and the secretary of state answered that his country no longer held itself bound by the naval limitation agreements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League's own commission divided the blame between Chinese nationalism and Japanese militarism while refusing to recognise Manchukuo, whose creation it held to breach the territorial integrity of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the report was adopted in 1933 the Japanese delegation walked out for good, ending a decade in which Japan had worked inside the League and sent some of its ablest diplomats and jurists to Geneva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]","Thomas W. Burkman, \"League of Nations and Japan,\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11571. [Open access]","<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, art. 16, in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","Cristine Koehler Zanella, \"The Road Taken: the Institutionalization of Economic Sanctions as an Instrument at Service of International Collective Security,\" <i>Carta Internacional</i> 11, no. 2 (2016): 53–77, https://doi.org/10.21530/ci.v11n2.2016.483. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Washington answer with non-recognition rather than force?","a":"Few American officials thought their interests in Manchuria profound enough to make intervention desirable, and the world depression had left almost no support for economic sanctions either, so a refusal to acknowledge the result was what remained."},{"q":"Why could the Kellogg-Briand Pact not be used against Japan?","a":"The pact outlawed war and asked its parties to settle disputes peacefully but provided no means of enforcement and never defined self-defence, so appeals under it against a signatory produced nothing."},{"q":"Why did the doctrine make no difference in Manchuria?","a":"Japan already held the territory and went on expanding, attacking Shanghai the same year, so a declaration that the outcome would not be recognised cost the occupying power nothing it valued."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Henry_Stimson%2C_Harris_%26_Ewing_bw_photo_portrait%2C_1929.jpg/1280px-Henry_Stimson%2C_Harris_%26_Ewing_bw_photo_portrait%2C_1929.jpg","title":"Henry Stimson, secretary of state, 1929","desc":"The studio portrait taken in the year he took the office he held through the Manchurian crisis.","credit":"Harris & Ewing, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Stimson,_Harris_%26_Ewing_bw_photo_portrait,_1929.jpg","alt":"Black and white studio portrait of a moustached man in a tweed suit and patterned tie, turned three-quarters against a dark background."},"wiki":"Stimson Doctrine"},
    "ww2-108": {"questions":["Members of the League argued through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about whether an oil embargo would end the war quickly or provoke armed resistance from Italy, and never imposed one.","Italy came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> estranged from Britain and France and closer to Germany, and within a year the two had pledged a common foreign policy."],"abstract":"The <b>Abyssinia Crisis</b> was the test the League of Nations failed when Italy invaded Ethiopia, and no other episode did as much to discredit collective security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Covenant left no doubt what members were meant to do: a state that went to war in disregard of its promises had committed an act of war against all the rest, who undertook to sever every trade and financial relation with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The League did act, and more than fifty of its members joined in economic sanctions against a great power, which was further than the organisation had ever gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the sanctions left out was oil, and members argued for months over whether an embargo on it would bring the war to a quick end or provoke Italian attack on whichever country applied it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> British ministers wanted an assurance from the other members that they would be defended if Italy singled them out, and while they looked for it the oil sanction stayed suspended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the sanctions, Britain and France were negotiating terms that would have handed Italy much of the territory it had invaded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The terms leaked in December 1935, the foreign secretary who had agreed them resigned, and the leader of the opposition described them in the Commons as the surrender to an aggressor of half an empire in exchange for a corridor for camels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The prime minister told the House that the cabinet had endorsed its colleague's work before it had time to study the documents, took the responsibility on himself and his colleagues, and declared the proposals completely dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy finished its conquest all the same, and the crisis ended its alignment with Britain and France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 25 October 1936 Rome and Berlin entered a treaty of friendship pledging a common foreign policy, and a fortnight later Germany signed the Anti-Comintern Pact with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Alliance in World War II,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-alliance-in-world-war-ii. [Open access]","<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, art. 16, in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"League of Nations and Abyssinia,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 December 1935, vol. 307, cols. 2017–127, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1935/dec/19/league-of-nations-and-abyssinia. [Open access]","Gordan Struić, \"Collective Security and Its Mechanisms in the Covenant of the League of Nations,\" <i>Pravni vjesnik</i> 33, no. 3–4 (2017): 145–66, https://doi.org/10.25234/pv/5041. [Open access]","Cristine Koehler Zanella, \"The Road Taken: the Institutionalization of Economic Sanctions as an Instrument at Service of International Collective Security,\" <i>Carta Internacional</i> 11, no. 2 (2016): 53–77, https://doi.org/10.21530/ci.v11n2.2016.483. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was oil kept out of the sanctions?","a":"Members feared an oil embargo would provoke Italy into attacking whichever country applied it, and Britain wanted assurances from the rest of the League that it would be defended before taking that risk, so the measure stayed suspended."},{"q":"Why did the leaked terms cause such damage?","a":"Two of the League's leading members had been offering an aggressor much of the territory it had invaded while sanctions against that aggressor were in force, which contradicted the policy the sanctions were meant to serve."},{"q":"Why did the crisis push Italy towards Germany?","a":"The sanctions and the collapse of its alignment with Britain and France left Italy looking elsewhere, and within a year it had pledged a common foreign policy with Berlin in the treaty of friendship known as the Rome-Berlin Axis."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Emperor_Haile_Selassie_League_of_Nations_speech.png","title":"Haile Selassie addresses the League of Nations, 1936","desc":"The Ethiopian emperor speaking from the rostrum at Geneva after the conquest of his country.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Haile_Selassie_League_of_Nations_speech.png","alt":"Black and white photograph of a bearded man in a dark cloak standing at a lit rostrum, with seated delegates and a stenographer below him."},"wiki":"Abyssinia Crisis","places":["city:1159151505"]},
    "ww2-109": {"questions":["News of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaked before the cabinet had studied the documents, and the outcry that followed cost the British foreign secretary his office within 10 days.","One purpose of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was to get negotiations started before an oil embargo came before the League 5 days later, which its author said he could not otherwise postpone."],"abstract":"The <b>Hoare-Laval Pact</b> was the plan agreed in Paris in December 1935 by the British foreign secretary and the French prime minister for ending the Italian war in Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It had three parts, international supervision, exchanges of territory and openings for Italian economic expansion, which were the principles a League committee had already put forward and the emperor had accepted under the threat of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy was to keep part of the Tigre province it occupied but evacuate other conquered territory including the sacred town of Aksum, and to receive a strip of Danakil and Ogaden that was entirely desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ethiopia was to have an outlet to the sea at Assab with a wide corridor in full sovereignty, and nothing was discussed that would have stopped it building a railway there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A southern zone was set aside for Italian economic expansion, the part that met the fiercest criticism, with the League left a free hand to fill that chapter in as it wished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hurry, on the foreign secretary's own account, was that an oil embargo was due before the League within 5 days, and he would not ask for a postponement unless negotiations had actually begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He held the talks in an atmosphere of threatened war, in which almost every member state opposed military action and no member but Britain had taken any military precautions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The terms leaked before the cabinet had studied the documents, ministers endorsed them rather than repudiate an absent colleague, and the prime minister said afterwards that he ought to have fetched him home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The outcry was violent enough that the foreign secretary resigned on 19 December, and the opposition leader called the plan the surrender to an aggressor of half an empire in exchange for a corridor for camels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The prime minister took the responsibility on the whole government and declared the proposals completely dead, but the League's two leading members had been shown ready to buy off the aggressor they were sanctioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Personal Explanation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 December 1935, vol. 307, cols. 2007–17, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1935/dec/19/personal-explanation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"League of Nations and Abyssinia,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 December 1935, vol. 307, cols. 2017–127, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1935/dec/19/league-of-nations-and-abyssinia. [Open access]","T. P. Nesterova, \"France and Attempt to Resolve the Italo-Ethiopian Conflict in Late 1935: the Hoare–Laval Plan,\" <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i>, no. 8 (2020): 398–411, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2020-8-398-411. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Alliance in World War II,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-alliance-in-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Gordan Struić, \"Collective Security and Its Mechanisms in the Covenant of the League of Nations,\" <i>Pravni vjesnik</i> 33, no. 3–4 (2017): 145–66, https://doi.org/10.25234/pv/5041. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the plan put together in such haste?","a":"An oil embargo was due before the League within 5 days, and the foreign secretary did not think he could ask for it to be postponed unless he could show that peace negotiations had actually begun."},{"q":"Why did the cabinet endorse terms it disliked?","a":"The terms leaked before ministers had studied the documents, so they had to decide at once whether to back an absent colleague or repudiate him, and the prime minister said his instinct was to stand by the man who was not there to explain himself."},{"q":"Why did the affair damage the League rather than just a minister?","a":"Its two leading members were shown to be negotiating away much of the victim's territory while sanctions against the aggressor were in force, which is not a policy the sanctions could survive being seen beside."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Samuel_Hoare_%28CNews%29_-_btv1b532048524.jpg/1280px-Samuel_Hoare_%28CNews%29_-_btv1b532048524.jpg","title":"Samuel Hoare, British foreign secretary","desc":"A press photograph of the minister whose agreement with the French prime minister cost him his office within ten days.","credit":"Agence Central News, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Hoare_%28CNews%29_-_btv1b532048524.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a man in a hat and dark suit walking along a pavement carrying a briefcase and a rolled umbrella."},"wiki":"Hoare–Laval Pact","places":["city:1159151613"]},
    "ww2-110": {"questions":["France did not march against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it lacked British support, and Italy had already given assurances that it would take no part in international action.","Berlin justified the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by calling a French treaty with Moscow a breach of Locarno, and ignored the article of that treaty which provided for such a dispute."],"abstract":"The <b>Remilitarisation of the Rhineland</b> was the German army's entry on 7 March 1936 into the zone along the western frontier that the peace of 1919 had ordered kept clear of troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soldiers crossed the Rhine bridges and set up garrisons at Aachen, Trier and Saarbrücken, and occupying the strip of 50 kilometres (31 miles) broke both the Treaty of Versailles and the Treaty of Locarno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The zone had begun as a French demand to separate the Rhineland provinces from Germany, given up in exchange for a 15-year occupation, permanent demilitarisation and a guarantee of French security from Britain and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That guarantee never came, since the American Senate did not ratify and British ratification depended on it, so what remained were Articles 42 and 43, which carried no time limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The zone then appeared in Germany's own opening proposals at Locarno in 1925, where Britain and Italy guaranteed it and undertook to help at once any party against whom a flagrant breach was directed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Berlin justified the move by calling the mutual assistance pact France had made with the Soviet Union in 1935 a breach of Locarno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The third article of that treaty provided for exactly such a dispute and France offered to go to the Hague Court, but the German government decided for itself that the incompatibility voided the whole agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The step was risky, since a military answer from the Western powers would have forced the troops straight back, but Italy had promised to take no part in international action and France would not move without Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed was sharp criticism and condemnation before the League Council, which met the Locarno powers in London while the British foreign secretary told the Commons he would not be the first to go back on a British signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler offered non-aggression pacts and a return to the League and was not taken up, and the affair confirmed him in the belief that Europe would tolerate his expansion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Einmarsch ins Rheinland 1936,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/einmarsch-ins-rheinland-1936. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"European Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 26 March 1936, vol. 310, cols. 1435–549, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1936/mar/26/european-situation. [Open access]","<i>Treaty of Mutual Guarantee Between Germany, Belgium, France, Great Britain and Italy, Locarno, October 16, 1925</i>, arts. 1–4, in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/locarno_001.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Foreign Policy, 1933–1945,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-foreign-policy-1933-1945. [Open access]","Gordan Struić, \"Collective Security and Its Mechanisms in the Covenant of the League of Nations,\" <i>Pravni vjesnik</i> 33, no. 3–4 (2017): 145–66, https://doi.org/10.25234/pv/5041. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the demilitarised zone there in the first place?","a":"France had wanted the Rhineland provinces separated from Germany altogether and gave that up in exchange for a 15-year occupation, permanent demilitarisation, and an Anglo-American guarantee of French security which in the end never arrived."},{"q":"Why did France not send troops in reply?","a":"It would not act without British support, which it did not have, and Italy had already given assurances that it would take no part in any international action against Germany."},{"q":"Why did the legal argument about Locarno not hold?","a":"The treaty's third article provided for settling exactly this kind of dispute and France offered to take the question to the Hague Court, but Germany decided for itself that the Franco-Soviet pact voided the whole agreement."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/German_troops_march_into_K%C3%B6ln%2C_1936.jpg/1280px-German_troops_march_into_K%C3%B6ln%2C_1936.jpg","title":"German troops entering Cologne, March 1936","desc":"Horse-drawn artillery passing the cathedral before a crowd, on the day the army returned to the demilitarised zone.","credit":"CaiusCobbe, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_troops_march_into_K%C3%B6ln,_1936.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of soldiers with horse-drawn guns crossing a tramlined square in front of a gothic cathedral, watched by a dense crowd."},"wiki":"Remilitarisation of the Rhineland","places":["city:1159148451"]},
    "ww2-112": {"questions":["Two units of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went into the defence of Madrid in November 1936 after only a few weeks of training, and their losses were heavy.","The League of Nations asked for all foreign units to leave Spain, and the Republic sent the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> home in October 1938 while Germany and Italy stayed."],"abstract":"The <b>International Brigades</b> were the volunteer formations raised from September 1936 to fight for the Spanish Republic, and they were much the largest body of foreigners in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recruitment was organised by the Communist International, which had the French Communist Party sign volunteers up in Paris; it was done covertly, because the French government had publicly declared its non-intervention in the conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Five brigades of foreigners were ready in France by the autumn of 1936 and crossed the Pyrenees or went by sea, and four mixed brigades of Spaniards and foreigners were formed beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The numbers are given differently by different accounts: one puts them above 40,000 from more than forty nations, another at 35,000 to 40,000 from over fifty countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The French were the largest contingent at about 10,000, with roughly 5,000 each of Germans, Italians and Poles, and volunteers came from antifascist conviction, from persecution at home, and also from unemployment and a taste for adventure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two brigades were put into the defence of Madrid in November 1936 after a few weeks of training, and the Brigades went on to fight in central Spain, Aragon, Andalusia and at the Ebro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They kept a large measure of independence until 1937, when heavy losses had to be made up with Spanish soldiers and they passed under the Spanish general staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their record has been reconsidered rather than simply repeated: one such account concludes that, for all the courage and self-sacrifice and their frequent use as shock troops, what they gave the Republic's army was moral rather than militarily decisive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the autumn of 1938 the League of Nations called for all non-Spanish units to leave, and the Republic sent the Brigades home in October while the Axis powers ignored the demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 2,000 who could not go home stayed to fight in Catalonia, crossed into France in February 1939 and were interned; many of the Germans among them were later handed to the Gestapo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Internationalen Brigaden,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/internationale-brigaden. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Spanish Civil War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/spanish-civil-war. [Open access]","Michael Alpert, \"Una trompeta lejana. Las Brigadas Internacionales en la guerra de España: una reconsideración sesenta años después,\" <i>Espacio, Tiempo y Forma. Serie V, Historia Contemporánea</i>, no. 12 (1999), https://revistas.uned.es/index.php/ETFV/article/view/2980. [in Spanish] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Spain,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 29 October 1936, vol. 316, cols. 39–152, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1936/oct/29/spain. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Spanische Bürgerkrieg,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/spanischerbk. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was recruitment carried on covertly in Paris?","a":"The French government had publicly declared non-intervention in the Spanish conflict, so signing volunteers up openly would have contradicted its own stated policy; the Comintern used the French Communist Party to do it out of sight."},{"q":"Why did the Brigades lose their independence in 1937?","a":"Their losses had been heavy enough that the units could only be kept up to strength with Spanish soldiers, and once they were largely Spanish they were placed under the Spanish general staff."},{"q":"Why did the Republic send the Brigades home in 1938?","a":"The League of Nations had called for all non-Spanish units to withdraw so that the war could be contained as an internal Spanish matter, and the Republic complied even though the Axis powers did not."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Last_parade_of_the_Abraham_Lincoln_Brigade_1938.jpg","title":"The last parade of the Lincoln battalion, 1938","desc":"American volunteers of the Fifteenth International Brigade drawn up on parade in Spain shortly before the foreign units were sent home.","credit":"Veterans of the Abraham Lincoln Brigade, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Last_parade_of_the_Abraham_Lincoln_Brigade_1938.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of ranks of men in dark uniforms standing at attention in an open field, with officers walking along the front rank."},"wiki":"International Brigades","places":["city:1159151503"]},
    "ww2-113": {"questions":["The number of people killed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still disputed, with accounts ranging from a couple of hundred dead to around two thousand.","Condor Legion aircrew carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while Germany described its airmen in Spain as volunteers, and the raid became the war's emblem of civilian suffering."],"abstract":"The <b>bombing of Guernica</b> on 26 April 1937 destroyed a Basque market town in a raid of about 3 hours, and became the most notorious single act of the Spanish Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The aircraft were German and Italian: the Condor Legion had arrived in Spain the previous August, officially made up of German volunteers, and by November 1936 had a hundred aircraft and 5,000 men under its commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler and Mussolini explained their intervention as a fight against Bolshevism, and the war also gave them a chance to test new weapons, above all for the air force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How many people died is genuinely disputed and the figures given by reputable institutions differ by an order of magnitude, one putting the dead at 200 civilians or more and another at around 2,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town mattered to the Basques beyond its size, as the seat of the oak under which their old liberties had been sworn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The raid did more damage to the rebels' case abroad than to their enemies' armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Destroying a population widely regarded as the most Catholic in Spain exposed the claim that a military rebellion was a religious crusade, and it moved writers and citizens abroad who had until then held back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pablo Picasso's painting, made the same year, put the suffering of civilians in front of the world, while German propaganda and magazines such as the air force's own built a myth of the Condor Legion at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gernika has since become the Basque Country's foremost site of memory, a symbol first of Basque liberty and then, through the painting, of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That meaning has proved volatile, because a symbol taken up in opposed political arguments can be made to carry contradictory messages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Spanish Civil War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/spanish-civil-war. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Legion Condor,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/legion-condor. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Spanische Bürgerkrieg,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/spanischerbk. [in German] [Open access]","André Bénit, \"Avril 1937. La Légion Condor dévaste le Pays basque,\" <i>Alternative Francophone</i> 1, no. 5 (2012): 26–40, https://doi.org/10.29173/af17065. [in French] [Open access]","Ludger Mees, \"Guernica/Gernika como símbolo,\" <i>Historia Contemporánea</i>, no. 35 (2007), https://doi.org/10.1387/hc.4107. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the death toll still disputed?","a":"The figures published by reputable institutions differ by an order of magnitude, one giving 200 civilians or more and another around 2,000, and the raid was reported from the start inside a propaganda argument about who was responsible."},{"q":"Why did the raid damage the Nationalist case abroad?","a":"The rebels presented their rising as a religious crusade, and destroying a town whose people were widely regarded as the most Catholic in Spain contradicted that claim in front of foreign opinion."},{"q":"Why has the meaning of Gernika kept changing?","a":"It became a site of memory rather than only a place, standing first for old Basque liberties and later, through Picasso's painting, for peace, and such symbols are taken up by opposed political arguments and made to say contradictory things."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/El_poblado_de_Guernica_en_ruinas_tras_el_bombardeo.jpg","title":"Gernika after the raid, 1937","desc":"The town seen from above after the attack, with whole streets reduced to roofless shells and hills rising behind.","credit":"Anonymous, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_poblado_de_Guernica_en_ruinas_tras_el_bombardeo.jpg","alt":"Black and white aerial view of a small town in which almost every building has lost its roof and stands as bare walls, with wooded hills beyond."},"wiki":"Bombing of Guernica","places":["pl:q189848"]},
    "ww2-115": {"questions":["Intervening together in Spain brought Germany and Italy into their first real co-operation, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was announced within months of it.","Italy had guaranteed Austrian independence until sanctions over Ethiopia left it isolated, after which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced its old alignment with Britain and France."],"abstract":"The <b>Rome-Berlin Axis</b> was the alignment of Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy announced at the end of 1936, and the word became the name for the whole wartime alliance that grew out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states had been rivals rather than partners for most of the previous 3 years, chiefly over Austria: Mussolini guaranteed Austrian independence and in 1934 moved troops to the Brenner to make the point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What changed was Ethiopia: the League of Nations imposed economic sanctions on Italy for the invasion, Britain and France turned away from Rome, and the political isolation that followed made Mussolini look elsewhere for partners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In January 1936 he told the German ambassador that he no longer objected to Austria becoming a German satellite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Intervening together in Spain from the summer of 1936 gave the two states their first real co-operation, in weapons deliveries and then in troops sent to fight for Franco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 25 October 1936 Germany and Italy signed a secret agreement to pursue a common foreign policy, and a week later Mussolini spoke in Milan of an axis between Rome and Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy joined the Anti-Comintern Pact on 6 November 1937, which had bound Germany and Japan against the Communist International the year before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Until 1939 the axis meant a shared anti-communism and a mutual tolerance of each other's expansion rather than a treaty of alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That came on 22 May 1939 with the Pact of Steel, which provided for military co-operation and mutual support, and which Mussolini named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tripartite Pact of 27 September 1940 added Tokyo, and by then the word covered every state fighting alongside Germany, from Hungary and Romania to Bulgaria, Slovakia and Croatia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Alliance in World War II,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-alliance-in-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Achse Berlin-Rom,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/achse-berlin-rom. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die deutsch-italienischen Beziehungen,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/die-deutsch-italienischen-beziehungen. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Spanish Civil War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/spanish-civil-war. [Open access]","\"Anti-Comintern Pact: German-Japanese Agreement and Supplementary Protocol, Signed at Berlin, November 25, 1936,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tri1.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Italy stop defending Austrian independence?","a":"Sanctions over the invasion of Ethiopia left Italy isolated and ended its alignment with Britain and France, so in January 1936 Mussolini told the German ambassador he no longer objected to Austria becoming a German satellite."},{"q":"Why is the alignment named after a speech rather than a treaty?","a":"The agreement of 25 October 1936 was secret; what the public heard was Mussolini speaking in Milan a week later about an axis running between Rome and Berlin, and the phrase stuck."},{"q":"Why was the Axis not an alliance until 1939?","a":"Until then it meant a shared anti-communism and each state tolerating the other's expansion. Only the Pact of Steel of May 1939 committed them to military co-operation and mutual support."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1990-0412-515%2C_Berlin%2C_Kundgebung_mit_Mussolini.jpg","title":"Mussolini's rally in Berlin, 1937","desc":"A speaker's tribune set against enormous banners on the Maifeld, where Mussolini addressed a mass audience during his German state visit.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1990-0412-515, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1990-0412-515,_Berlin,_Kundgebung_mit_Mussolini.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a stone tribune flanked by uniformed ranks under vast hanging banners bearing an eagle and swastika."},"wiki":"Axis powers","places":["city:1159151593"]},
    "ww2-116": {"questions":["A rigged plebiscite held a month after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reported that 99.73% of Austrians approved, with Jews and Roma barred from voting.","No foreign government intervened over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the other powers' acceptance of it was a significant act of appeasement that let expansion continue unchecked."],"abstract":"The <b>Anschluss</b> was the annexation of Austria by Nazi Germany on 11–13 March 1938, and the first act of territorial expansion the regime carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Union of the two states was forbidden by the treaties of Versailles and Saint-Germain, because the victors expected a joined Germany and Austria to be too powerful, and support for it stayed strong on both sides of the border between the wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Austrian Nazis had tried before: a putsch on 25 July 1934 killed the chancellor Engelbert Dollfuss, and Hitler withdrew his support from the plotters only when Mussolini moved Italian troops to the Brenner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italy's guarantee was what changed, for once sanctions over Ethiopia had left it isolated Mussolini told the German ambassador in January 1936 that he no longer objected to Austria becoming a German satellite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In November 1937 Hitler told his commanders that overthrowing Austria and Czechoslovakia together was the first objective, since it would shorten Germany's frontiers and free forces for use elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 12 February 1938 he dictated an agreement to the Austrian chancellor Kurt von Schuschnigg that lifted the ban on the Austrian Nazis, brought them into government and handed them the interior ministry with its control of the police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Schuschnigg called a plebiscite on independence for 13 March; Hitler forced him to cancel it, an ultimatum forced his resignation on 11 March, and German troops crossed the next day against no resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The laws joining the two states were issued on 13 March, and a plebiscite on 10 April officially returned 99.73% in Austria, with neither Jews nor Roma allowed to vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Terror followed at once: between 12 and 22 March there were 1,742 recorded arrests in Austria and 96 suicides in Vienna, and for Social Democrats, Communists and Jews flight was often the only escape left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No foreign government intervened, and that acceptance was a significant act of appeasement which allowed Hitler to continue; before March was out he had decided to break up Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Territorial Aggression: The Anschluss,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-territorial-aggression-the-anschluss. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der „Anschluss“ Österreichs 1938,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/anschluss-oesterreich-1938. [Open access]","\"Hossbach Memorandum, November 10, 1937,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/hossbach.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Prewar Expansion,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-prewar-expansion. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die deutsch-italienischen Beziehungen,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/die-deutsch-italienischen-beziehungen. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had the union been forbidden after the First World War?","a":"The victorious powers expected that a Germany joined to Austria would be too large and too powerful, so the treaties of Versailles and Saint-Germain expressly ruled the union out."},{"q":"Why did Italy stop protecting Austrian independence?","a":"Sanctions over the invasion of Ethiopia left Italy isolated, and in January 1936 Mussolini told the German ambassador that he no longer objected to Austria becoming a German satellite, which removed the one guarantee that had held Hitler back in 1934."},{"q":"Why did the plebiscite return such a figure?","a":"It was held a month after the annexation, under a government the Nazis already controlled, and Jews and Roma were barred from voting, so the 99.73% measures the conditions of the vote rather than opinion."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1987-0922-503%2C_Wien%2C_Einmarsch_deutscher_Truppen%2C_Sp%C3%A4hpanzer.jpg","title":"German troops entering Vienna, March 1938","desc":"An armoured car moving down a Vienna street between crowds, some of them raising their arms, during the German entry into Austria.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1987-0922-503, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1987-0922-503,_Wien,_Einmarsch_deutscher_Truppen,_Sp%C3%A4hpanzer.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of an armoured car driving along a wide city street lined with tall buildings and dense crowds of onlookers."},"wiki":"Anschluss","places":["city:1159151563"]},
    "ww2-117": {"questions":["The Sudeten German Party was told to make demands Prague could never accept, and its Karlsbad Programme of April 1938 asked for autonomy in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amounting to a state within a state.","Ceding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost Czechoslovakia every fortification facing Germany, cut its main railways and roads, and handed over 690,000 Czechs along with the German speakers."],"abstract":"The <b>Sudetenland</b> was the rim of Bohemia and Moravia along the German and Austrian frontier, home to about three million German speakers, and the ground on which Hitler brought Europe to the edge of war in 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its Germans had been at a disadvantage since Czechoslovakia was founded on 28 October 1918, and unemployment among them ran above the national average through the years of the depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Konrad Henlein's Sudeten German Party took more than two thirds of the German vote in the elections of 1935, the largest share won by any party in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its programme was for a state reorganised along national lines, in which each nationality would form a legal body with its own registry, its own institutions and a spokesman entitled to act for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 28 March 1938 Hitler instructed Henlein always to put demands to the Czechoslovak government that it could not possibly grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Karlsbad Programme of 24 April did exactly that, and the crisis it produced gave Hitler his reason to demand that the region be handed over outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British policy was to keep the peace by concession, and the government disapproved of Czechoslovakia's treatment of its minorities enough to negotiate about frontiers in central Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler claimed that 300 Sudeten Germans had been killed, which was not the case, and used it to move German troops up to the Czech border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territory ceded in October covered about 22,500 km² (8,700 sq mi), took in 690,000 Czechs, and cut the country's main road and rail links.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every fortification facing Germany lay inside it, some 400,000 Czechs left their homes, and Gestapo units following the army arrested thousands with the help of the Sudeten German Free Corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Das Münchner Abkommen 1938,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/muenchner-abkommen-1938. [in German] [Open access]","Oskar Mulej, \"Territorial and Non-territorial Aspects in the Autonomist Proposals of the Sudeten German Party, 1937–38,\" <i>Nationalities Papers</i> 50, no. 5 (2022): 942–62, https://doi.org/10.1017/nps.2021.61. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Chamberlain and Hitler 1938,\" Education: Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/chamberlain-and-hitler/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Prewar Expansion,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-prewar-expansion. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Besetzung des Sudetengebietes 1938,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/besetzung-des-sudetengebietes-1938. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Henlein told to make impossible demands?","a":"Hitler wanted the nationality question left unsettled so that the crisis would sharpen. A programme the Czechoslovak government could not grant kept the grievance alive and gave him his reason to demand the territory itself."},{"q":"Why would the party's programme have unmade the state?","a":"It would have turned each nationality into a legal body with its own registry, institutions and spokesman, so that the nationalities rather than the citizens carried political will, which is not how Czechoslovakia was constituted."},{"q":"Why was losing the region so damaging militarily?","a":"Every fortification and bunker facing Germany stood inside the ceded belt, and the new frontier cut the country's main roads and railways, so what was left was open to attack."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H13158%2C_Anschluss_sudetendeutscher_Gebiete.jpg","title":"German troops entering the Sudetenland, October 1938","desc":"Soldiers walking arm in arm with civilians along a street hung with swastika flags during the occupation of the ceded districts.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H13158, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H13158,_Anschluss_sudetendeutscher_Gebiete.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of soldiers and civilians walking together down a street beneath large hanging flags, with a crowd behind them."},"wiki":"Sudetenland","places":["pl:q384544"]},
    "ww2-118": {"questions":["Britain and France guaranteed what was left of Czechoslovakia under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and did nothing when German troops occupied Prague less than 6 months later.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> provided for plebiscites in disputed districts, but an international commission simply fixed the new frontier on 7 October with no Czechoslovak representative present."],"abstract":"The <b>Munich Agreement</b> was concluded by Germany, the United Kingdom, France and Italy in the early hours of 30 September 1938, and gave Germany the Sudetenland without a shot being fired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Neither Czechoslovakia nor its ally the Soviet Union was invited, and the Czechoslovak government, treated as an object of the negotiation rather than a party to it, had to submit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The text laid down a timetable: evacuation was to begin on 1 October and be complete by the 10th, in five stages, with no existing installations destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An international commission of the four powers and Czechoslovakia was to settle the conditions, determine the areas in which plebiscites would be held, and fix the final frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France guaranteed the remaining state against unprovoked aggression, and Germany was to give a guarantee of its own once the Polish and Hungarian minority questions had been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Chamberlain defended the agreement in the Commons by setting it against the ultimatum Hitler had handed him at Godesberg a week before, which had allowed no commission, no defined plebiscite areas and no guarantee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commission fixed the frontier on 7 October with no Czechoslovak representative present, and the last zone was occupied on the 10th as the text provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler had wanted the war the agreement prevented, but the popularity it won him at home wrecked the plans of the army officers who had meant to remove him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blame for Munich is usually laid on Chamberlain alone, and one account argues that the position of the French government and the strength of British feeling against another war belong in the reckoning too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 15 March 1939 German forces occupied Bohemia and Moravia, Slovakia became a German client state, and the guarantee given at Munich was never invoked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Munich Pact, September 29, 1938,\" in <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/munich1.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Prime Minister's Statement,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 3 October 1938, vol. 339, cols. 40–162, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/oct/03/prime-ministers-statement. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Das Münchner Abkommen 1938,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/muenchner-abkommen-1938. [in German] [Open access]","Taras Tkachuk, \"The Munich Agreement of 1938: the Approaches of the British Politics and Diplomacy,\" <i>European Historical Studies</i>, no. 10 (2018): 253–74, https://doi.org/10.17721/2524-048X.2018.10.253-274. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Prewar Expansion,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-prewar-expansion. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Czechoslovakia not at the conference?","a":"The four powers treated the Sudeten question as theirs to settle, so the country whose territory was being transferred was handled as an object of the negotiation rather than a party to it, and had to accept what was agreed."},{"q":"Why did Chamberlain think the agreement an improvement?","a":"He compared it with the ultimatum handed to him at Godesberg a week earlier, which had set no commission, left the plebiscite areas undefined and offered no guarantee, where the agreement provided for all three."},{"q":"Why did the agreement leave Hitler dissatisfied?","a":"He had wanted the war it prevented. What it gave him instead was popularity at home for having avoided one, which also destroyed the plans of army officers who had intended to remove him."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R69173%2C_M%C3%BCnchener_Abkommen%2C_Staatschefs.jpg","title":"The four leaders at Munich, 1938","desc":"The heads of the four governments and their delegations standing together in the conference room before the agreement was signed.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-R69173, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R69173,_M%C3%BCnchener_Abkommen,_Staatschefs.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a line of men in suits and uniforms standing in a panelled room, with a table and papers in front of them."},"wiki":"Munich Agreement","places":["city:1159151357"]},
    "ww2-119": {"questions":["The policy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reflected the losses of the First World War, an economy in slump, and doubts about whether the dominions would fight again.","Most of Britain's press, business leaders and Conservative politicians supported <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is usually judged a failure because it did not prevent the war."],"abstract":"<b>Appeasement</b> is the strategy of making concessions to an aggressive foreign power in order to avoid war, and the word attaches above all to British policy towards Nazi Germany in the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In popular memory it belongs to Neville Chamberlain, prime minister from 1937 to 1940, but it had been the policy of Ramsay MacDonald and Stanley Baldwin before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its reasons were not naivety alone: the First World War had left Britain psychologically, economically and militarily unready for another, the depression had driven unemployment up, and feeling against war was widespread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain also depended on an empire whose support in a second war was less certain than it had been in the first, and feared that war, or defeat, would cost it the colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most powerful parts of British society were behind the policy, including business leaders, the royal family, the BBC, <i>The Times</i> and most Conservative leaders, with Winston Churchill the notable exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> British governments imposed no penalty when Germany left the League of Nations, rearmed or remilitarised the Rhineland, preferring to negotiate, as in the naval agreement of 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government believed that Germany had genuine grievances left by the Treaty of Versailles, and that meeting them would leave Hitler satisfied and less demanding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chamberlain told the Commons after Munich that the declaration he had signed with Hitler recorded a wish that the two peoples should never go to war with one another again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was at the same time approving large increases in armament spending, having been advised in secret that Czechoslovakia was lost and that war was bound to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Appeasement is usually judged a failure because it did not prevent the war, though the argument continues over how far Britain's circumstances left its governments any other course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The British Policy of Appeasement toward Hitler and Nazi Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/neville-chamberlain. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Chamberlain and Hitler 1938,\" Education: Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/chamberlain-and-hitler/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Prime Minister's Statement,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 3 October 1938, vol. 339, cols. 40–162, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/oct/03/prime-ministers-statement. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Das Münchner Abkommen 1938,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/muenchner-abkommen-1938. [in German] [Open access]","Taras Tkachuk, \"The Munich Agreement of 1938: the Approaches of the British Politics and Diplomacy,\" <i>European Historical Studies</i>, no. 10 (2018): 253–74, https://doi.org/10.17721/2524-048X.2018.10.253-274. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did British governments think concession would work?","a":"They believed Germany had genuine grievances left by the Treaty of Versailles, and that once those were met Hitler would be satisfied and become less demanding rather than more."},{"q":"Why was the policy so widely supported at the time?","a":"The First World War had left Britain unready for another, the depression had driven unemployment up, and antiwar feeling ran through the press, business, the royal family and most of the Conservative leadership."},{"q":"Why did Britain rearm while still appeasing?","a":"The two ran together. Chamberlain was approving large increases in armament spending at the same time as he was signing at Munich, having been told in secret that Czechoslovakia was lost and that war would come anyway."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/MunichAgreement.jpg","title":"Chamberlain at Heston aerodrome, 1938","desc":"The British prime minister speaking to a crowd beside his aircraft on returning from the Munich conference.","credit":"Ministry of Information official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MunichAgreement.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a man in a dark overcoat and hat holding up a sheet of paper before a dense crowd of hats and raised arms, with an aircraft behind."},"wiki":"Appeasement"},
    "ww2-120": {"questions":["Between July and November 1940 the polemic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went through 26 impressions, and its verdict on appeasement has shaped popular memory ever since.","Written in the weeks after Dunkirk, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> demanded that the men who let the walls fall into ruin should retire of their own volition."],"abstract":"<b><i>Guilty Men</i></b> is a polemic published in London in July 1940 under the pseudonym Cato, and it fixed the verdict on appeasement that most people in Britain have held ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens with a crowd bursting into the French Convention in 1793 to demand a dozen guilty men, and then names fifteen of its own, with Chamberlain, Simon, Hoare, MacDonald, Baldwin and Halifax at the head of the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its case is that the men who had governed Britain through the 1930s left the country disarmed, and that the army rescued from the beaches of Dunkirk was the bill for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ends by demanding that they retire of their own volition, so that those repairing the breaches in the walls are not burdened with the men who let the walls fall into ruin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The book sold on an extraordinary scale, going through 26 impressions between July and November 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The charge had been made in Parliament first, by Duff Cooper, who resigned from the cabinet over Munich and explained his reasons to the Commons on 3 October 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chamberlain's government fell after the debate of 7 and 8 May 1940 on the campaign in Norway, in which its majority collapsed to 81, with 281 votes against 200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Written in the weeks after Dunkirk, the book is an argument for a change of government rather than a history, and it names men rather than weighing the circumstances they governed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later work has pressed the other way, arguing that the blame should not fall on Chamberlain alone, and that French policy and the strength of British feeling against another war belong in the account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What is not disputed is the outcome, since appeasement did not prevent the war, which is why it remains the standard against which a policy of concession is judged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Cato, <i>Guilty Men</i> (London: Victor Gollancz, 1940), preface, 123–25, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.5205. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Personal Explanation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 3 October 1938, vol. 339, cols. 29–40, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/oct/03/personal-explanation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1251–366, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/08/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","Taras Tkachuk, \"The Munich Agreement of 1938: the Approaches of the British Politics and Diplomacy,\" <i>European Historical Studies</i>, no. 10 (2018): 253–74, https://doi.org/10.17721/2524-048X.2018.10.253-274. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The British Policy of Appeasement toward Hitler and Nazi Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/neville-chamberlain. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the book appear when it did?","a":"It was written in the weeks after Dunkirk, when the army had just been rescued from the beaches and Chamberlain's government had fallen, and it was arguing that the ministers responsible should leave public life at once."},{"q":"Why has its verdict lasted so long?","a":"It reached an enormous readership, 26 impressions in 5 months, and it put the case as a list of named men, which is easier to remember than an argument about the circumstances they were governing in."},{"q":"Why do later accounts resist it?","a":"The book names individuals rather than weighing what constrained them, and later work argues that the position of the French government and the strength of British opinion against another war have to be counted alongside Chamberlain's own decisions."}],"wiki":"Guilty Men","places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-111": {"questions":["About half a million people died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and roughly two hundred thousand of them were killed away from the fighting, in executions and reprisals.","Britain and France answered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a policy of non-intervention that left the recognised government unable to buy arms while its enemies were supplied openly."],"abstract":"The <b>Spanish Civil War</b> began on 17 July 1936, when generals in Spanish Morocco launched a long-prepared revolt against the Popular Front government of the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rising only half succeeded: it carried rural districts where the right was strong and was beaten down in cities with strong traditions of the left, so what was meant as a coup became a front line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the coup failed the Republican state's machinery of repression collapsed with it, and in Catalonia and Valencia workers' committees took over companies and land until the balance shifted back after May 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both sides appealed abroad within days of the rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler agreed on 25 July to arm the rebels, wanting to prevent a Spain aligned with France and the Soviet Union, to secure Spanish raw materials, and to try out his new air force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Britain and France answered with non-intervention, and more than two dozen states signed an agreement in August 1936 that Germany, Italy and the Soviet Union then broke openly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Italy supplied some 75,000 troops and Germany about 20,000 men by rotation through the Condor Legion, while Soviet tanks, aircraft and advisers went to the Republic and tens of thousands of volunteers came to fight for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Killing away from the front matched the fighting in scale, because combatants on both sides set out to eradicate their ideological opponents rather than merely defeat them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of roughly 500,000 dead, about 200,000 died in systematic killings, mob violence or torture; almost 7,000 clergy were killed in the Republican zone, and the Nationalists executed some 100,000 people during the war and about 50,000 after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Franco won in the spring of 1939, half a million Republicans fled into France, and Spain stayed formally neutral in the wider war that began 5 months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Spanish Civil War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/spanish-civil-war. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Spanische Bürgerkrieg,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/spanischerbk. [in German] [Open access]","Albert Balcells, \"Collectivisations in Catalonia and the Region of Valencia during the Spanish Civil War, 1936–1939,\" <i>Catalan Historical Review</i>, no. 10 (2017): 77–92, https://revistes.iec.cat/index.php/CHR/article/view/143735. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Spain,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 29 October 1936, vol. 316, cols. 39–152, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1936/oct/29/spain. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Foreign Policy, 1933–1945,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-foreign-policy-1933-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rising become a civil war rather than a coup?","a":"It succeeded only in part. The rebels took rural districts where the right was strong but were defeated in cities with strong traditions of the left, so instead of a change of government the country was left divided along a front line."},{"q":"Why did non-intervention work against the Republic?","a":"The Republic was the recognised government and could have bought arms legally, but the agreement stopped that, while Germany and Italy broke the same agreement openly and kept supplying the rebels."},{"q":"Why were so many killed away from the fighting?","a":"Both sides treated the war as a chance to destroy their ideological opponents rather than simply to win, so roughly 200,000 of the 500,000 dead were killed in systematic killings, mob violence or torture."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Forces_of_the_Spanish_Government_Crossing_the_Ebro_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Republican troops crossing the Ebro, 1938","desc":"Soldiers of the Spanish Republic filing across a pontoon bridge during the Ebro offensive, the Republic's last large attack of the war.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forces_of_the_Spanish_Government_Crossing_the_Ebro_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a long single file of soldiers crossing a low pontoon bridge over a wide river, with more troops waiting on the far bank."},"wiki":"Spanish Civil War","places":["city:1159151503"]},
    "ww2-114": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first sat on 9 September 1936 with every European state represented but Portugal, which joined a little later after talks at Geneva.","Because the Republic was the recognised government of Spain, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> denied it arms it could otherwise have bought, which some historians argue helped Franco."],"abstract":"The <b>Non-Intervention Committee</b> was the body set up in London in 1936 to supervise an agreement by the European powers to send no arms or men to either side in the Spanish Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The initiative was French, and the British foreign secretary told the Commons that his government had at once accepted both the proposal for a committee and the French suggestion that it should sit in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scheme rested on notes exchanged between London and Paris in the middle of August, which were only to come into force once Germany, Italy, Portugal and the Soviet Union had agreed as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The committee first met on 9 September 1936 with every European country represented except Portugal, which joined shortly afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than two dozen states signed, among them France, Britain, Italy, Germany and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The last three broke the agreement openly, Germany and Italy supplying the rebels and the Soviet Union the Republic, and the foreign secretary read the House a long list of documented violations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The committee could ask questions and count ships, and it could not stop anything; a control scheme with observers was set up and produced very little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recruitment for the Republic went on regardless, organised covertly in Paris precisely because France had declared its non-intervention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The policy fell hardest on the side it did not name, since the Republic was the recognised government of Spain and would otherwise have been able to buy arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some historians argue that the arrangement suited Franco, who could get weapons on credit from his allies, while the Republic had to pay hard currency for often outdated equipment and smuggle it past the embargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Spain,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 29 October 1936, vol. 316, cols. 39–152, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1936/oct/29/spain. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Spanish Civil War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/spanish-civil-war. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Internationalen Brigaden,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/internationale-brigaden. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Spanische Bürgerkrieg,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/spanischerbk. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Foreign Policy, 1933–1945,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-foreign-policy-1933-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the agreement need Germany, Italy and the Soviet Union?","a":"The Anglo-French notes of August 1936 were only to take effect once those governments and Portugal had agreed, because an embargo that the states most likely to supply arms had not joined would have restrained nobody."},{"q":"Why could the committee not enforce its own agreement?","a":"It had no power beyond asking its members questions and running a control scheme of observers, so when Germany, Italy and the Soviet Union broke the agreement openly there was nothing it could do about it."},{"q":"Why did non-intervention favour the rebels?","a":"The Republic was the recognised government and could otherwise have bought arms legally, whereas the rebels received them on credit from Germany and Italy in spite of the embargo."}],"places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-121": {"questions":["A Birmingham manufacturer who reached the Commons at 49 and the Treasury within 5 years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resigned in May 1940 after the failure of the Norwegian campaign.","It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who told the Commons on the last day of March 1939 that Britain would at once support Poland against any threat to its independence."],"abstract":"<b>Neville Chamberlain</b> was prime minister of the United Kingdom from 1937 to 1940, and no name is more firmly attached to the policy of appeasement or to its collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was born in Edgbaston, Birmingham, on 18 March 1869, the younger son of the Victorian statesman Joseph Chamberlain and half-brother of Austen, who became chancellor of the exchequer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sent out at 21 to run a 20,000-acre estate in the Bahamas, a venture that eventually failed, he came home to make his name as a Birmingham manufacturer and was lord mayor of the city in 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He entered the Commons only in 1918, at the age of 49, and rose quickly: postmaster general in 1922, then minister of health, and chancellor of the exchequer within 5 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His Local Government Act of 1929 reorganised the poor law and local finance, and from the Treasury he carried the protective tariff his father had spent a career arguing for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> As prime minister from May 1937 he took foreign policy into his own hands, flying to Germany three times in September 1938 and returning from Munich with a declaration that the two peoples would never go to war again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German troops entered Prague in March 1939, and 2 days later he announced the end of appeasement in a speech at Birmingham, following it with guarantees to Poland, Greece, Romania and Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first of those was the promise, given to the Commons on the last day of March, of all the support in Britain's power should any action clearly threaten Polish independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Germany invaded Poland he sent the ultimatum that expired at eleven on the morning of 3 September, and told the House at noon that the country was at war with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same short speech he said that everything he had worked for, hoped for and believed in during his public life had crashed into ruins; he resigned on 10 May 1940 and died that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Government of the United Kingdom, \"Neville Chamberlain,\" Past Prime Ministers, https://www.gov.uk/government/history/past-prime-ministers/neville-chamberlain. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Arthur Neville Chamberlain 1869-1940,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/arthur-neville-chamberlain. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"European Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 31 March 1939, vol. 345, cols. 2415-20, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/mar/31/european-situation-1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Prime Minister's Announcement,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 3 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 291-302, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/03/prime-ministers-announcement. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The British Policy of Appeasement toward Hitler and Nazi Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/neville-chamberlain. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is appeasement remembered as his policy rather than his party's?","a":"Because he conducted it in person. He flew to Germany three times in a fortnight, negotiated with Hitler face to face and brought back a declaration with his own signature on it, so when the policy failed the failure had a name and a face attached to it."},{"q":"Why did the occupation of Prague change British policy so fast?","a":"Everything taken before it could be argued to be German. Prague was not, so the claim that Hitler wanted only to gather Germans into the Reich could no longer be made, and within a fortnight Britain was handing out guarantees it had refused to give for 20 years."},{"q":"Why did he leave office in 1940 and not after Munich?","a":"Munich was popular at the time, and the guarantees that followed the fall of Prague were his own policy, so the change of course cost him nothing. What finished him was the war itself: after the Norwegian expedition failed he lost the confidence of his own party over his conduct of it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Neville-Chamberlain.jpg","title":"Neville Chamberlain","desc":"A studio portrait of the British prime minister, taken while he was chancellor of the exchequer.","credit":"Bassano Ltd, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neville-Chamberlain.jpg","alt":"Black and white studio portrait of an elderly man in a wing collar and dark suit, with a moustache and receding hair."},"wiki":"Neville Chamberlain","places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-122": {"questions":["Slovakia declared its independence on 14 March 1939 and the rest of the country was proclaimed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 2 days later, its president kept in office and its army dissolved.","Czech factories, weapons and skilled workers passed to German war production when the country was reorganised as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, beyond the reach of Allied bombers until late in the war."],"abstract":"The <b>Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia</b> was the German territory made out of the Czech lands in March 1939, and the first of Hitler's seizures to take a country that was not German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Munich Agreement had already stripped Czechoslovakia of the Sudetenland and with it every fortified position on its frontier, and Hungary took further districts under the First Vienna Award.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Berlin then worked on Czech and Slovak antagonism until the Slovak diet declared independence on 14 March, leaving the rump state with no reason to hold together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> President Emil Hácha was summoned to Berlin that night and offered a choice between occupation with the bombing of Prague and placing the fate of the Czech people in Hitler's hands; he signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German troops crossed at six the next morning, and in London the prime minister told the Commons that afternoon that the Czechs had been ordered not to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The protectorate proclaimed on 16 March covered about 49,000 square kilometres (19,000 square miles) and some 7.5 million people, of whom about 250,000 Germans were given Reich citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It kept a Czech government and Hácha as state president, but a Reich Protector answerable to Hitler alone could strike down its laws at will, and Konstantin von Neurath held the office first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Czechoslovak army was dissolved and its weapons went into Wehrmacht stocks, to be used against Poland within 6 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Czech industry, a skilled workforce and a position out of range of Allied bombers made the territory valuable to German war production for most of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No great power recognised the annexation, and the United States refused throughout the war to accept that the Czechoslovak Republic had ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"'Zerschlagung der Rest-Tschechei' 1939,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/zerschlagung-der-rest-tschechei-1939. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"'Reichsprotektorat Böhmen und Mähren',\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/reichsprotektorat-boehmen-und-maehren. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Czecho-Slovakia,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 15 March 1939, vol. 345, cols. 435-555, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/mar/15/czecho-slovakia-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Czechoslovakia,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/czechoslovakia. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Occupation of Czechoslovakia by Germany, March 15, 1939; refusal of the United States to recognize extinction of the Czechoslovak Republic,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/ch2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Munich make the occupation easier?","a":"The Sudetenland held the frontier fortifications, so once it had gone the Czech lands had no defensible border left. A state that cannot be defended is one whose government can be summoned to Berlin and told what will happen if it refuses."},{"q":"Why keep a Czech president and government at all?","a":"The point was to run the country cheaply. An autonomous administration under a Reich Protector who could annul anything it did left the appearance of self-government while every decision that mattered stayed German, and the industry kept working."},{"q":"Why did Britain and France not act on the Munich guarantee?","a":"They had promised to guarantee what was left of Czechoslovakia, and in March 1939 they confined themselves to condemning what had happened. The state they had guaranteed had, on the German account, dissolved itself, and neither government was willing to go to war over it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-2004-1202-505%2C_Prag%2C_Burg%2C_Besuch_Adolf_Hitler.jpg","title":"German troops at Prague Castle, 15 March 1939","desc":"Soldiers drawn up inside the castle gates on the day the occupation of the Czech lands began.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-2004-1202-505, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-2004-1202-505,_Prag,_Burg,_Besuch_Adolf_Hitler.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of uniformed soldiers standing in ranks in a wet courtyard beside ornamental iron gates, with officers walking past them."},"wiki":"Protectorate of Bohemia and Moravia","places":["city:1159151359"]},
    "ww2-123": {"questions":["Hitler answered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 28 April 1939 by denouncing both the naval agreement with Britain and the non-aggression pact he had signed with Poland 5 years earlier.","Britain's obligations to Poland were cast into formal shape by the treaty of 25 August 1939, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that took Britain into war a week later."],"abstract":"The <b>Anglo-Polish alliance</b> was the commitment under which Britain went to war with Germany on 3 September 1939, and the only guarantee of the 1930s that was ever honoured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began as a unilateral promise, given to the Commons on 31 March 1939, that Britain would at once lend Poland all the support in its power against any action clearly threatening Polish independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French government authorised the same statement to be made on its behalf, so the pledge bound both western powers from the first afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a reversal: 2 years of settling German claims by negotiation had ended a fortnight earlier in Prague, and Britain now promised to fight for a country it could not reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler treated the guarantee as a hostile act and on 28 April denounced both the Anglo-German naval agreement and the German-Polish non-aggression pact of 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That pact had been Hitler's own work, signed for 10 years in 1934 to weaken the French alliance system, and it had held the quarrel over Danzig and the corridor in check for 5 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Through the summer the two governments negotiated a permanent treaty, and the Agreement of Mutual Assistance was signed in London on 25 August, 2 days after Germany and the Soviet Union signed their own pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The prime minister told the Commons 4 days later that the obligations to Poland had been cast into formal shape by that agreement and would be carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Berlin read the Moscow pact as decisive, Hitler writing to Mussolini that it had produced an entirely new situation in world politics and was the greatest possible gain for the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The alliance did not save Poland, which was overrun in 5 weeks while no western offensive came, but it did settle that the German attack would begin a European war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"European Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 31 March 1939, vol. 345, cols. 2415-20, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/mar/31/european-situation-1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 29 August 1939, vol. 351, cols. 111-23, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/aug/29/international-situation. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der deutsch-polnische Nichtangriffspakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/der-deutsch-polnische-nichtangriffspakt. [Open access]","\"Letter from Hitler to Mussolini, August 25, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns057.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why guarantee Poland when Britain could not defend it?","a":"The guarantee was not a plan to save Polish territory but a warning about the next move: a statement that the next act of force would mean a European war rather than another conference. Its purpose was deterrence, and it failed as deterrence."},{"q":"Why did Hitler denounce the 1934 pact rather than ignore it?","a":"That pact had been signed to keep Poland out of a French alliance system, and the British guarantee put Poland inside a western one instead. The agreement had done its work and now stood in the way of the attack being prepared."},{"q":"Why did the Moscow pact not break the alliance?","a":"Berlin expected it to, on the reasoning that Britain and France would not fight for a country the Soviet Union had abandoned. The British answer was that the guarantee had been given before any Russian agreement was discussed and did not depend on one."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/People_of_Warsaw_under_GB_Embassy_3.09.1939.jpg","title":"Anglo-Polish alliance","desc":"People of Warsaw in happy demonstration (\"Long live England!\") under British Embassy in Warsaw just after British declaration of state of war with Nazi Germany.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People_of_Warsaw_under_GB_Embassy_3.09.1939.jpg","alt":"Crowds outside the British embassy in Warsaw on 3 September 1939, banners raised"},"wiki":"Anglo-Polish alliance","places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-124": {"questions":["Britain and France answered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within a week by guaranteeing Greece and Romania, the second and third such pledges given that spring.","A provisional council sitting under occupation offered the crown to the king of Italy after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, joining the two states in a personal union."],"abstract":"The <b>Italian invasion of Albania</b> began on Good Friday, 7 April 1939, and ended within days in the extinction of a state that had been an Italian client for over a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italy had financed Albania's roads, ports and administration since the 1920s, and after the conquest of Abyssinia had left it isolated in Europe it looked to the Adriatic and to German friendship for its next move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through the spring Rome pressed King Zog for control of Albanian ports, airfields and ministries, and he refused terms he judged incompatible with sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italian warships appeared off the coast on 6 April, troops disembarked at dawn the next day at four ports, and accounts of how much resistance was offered differ sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zog left Tirana during the night with Queen Geraldine and their son, born 2 days earlier, and reached Greek territory on 8 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Italian troops entered the capital that morning, leaflets were dropped calling on Albanians to welcome them, and the foreign minister flew in to see the country taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> London had been told on 7 April that Italy fully intended to respect Albanian independence, and Parliament was recalled from the Easter recess to hear an account of what had happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A provisional council then offered the crown to Victor Emmanuel III, an offer the prime minister called a farce made by a people under occupation by a huge foreign force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reply of the western powers was not to act in Albania but to guarantee Greece and Romania, extending to the Balkans the policy begun with Poland a week before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 4 months later, in Moscow, Stalin asked whether so small and mountainous a country was of any use to Italy, and was told it mattered for strategic reasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"European Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 13 April 1939, vol. 346, cols. 5-140, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/apr/13/european-situation. [Open access]","Peter Tase, \"Italy and Albania: The political and economic alliance and the Italian invasion of 1939,\" <i>Academicus International Scientific Journal</i>, no. 6 (2012): 62-70, https://doi.org/10.7336/academicus.2012.06.06. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, April 8, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v02/d339. [Open access]","\"Memorandum of a Conversation Held on the Night of August 23d to 24th, Between the Reich Foreign Minister and Herren Stalin and Molotov,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns053.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die deutsch-italienischen Beziehungen,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/die-deutsch-italienischen-beziehungen. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why invade a country Italy already controlled?","a":"Influence had limits: Zog could still refuse terms, as he did that spring over the ports, the airfields and the ministries. Occupation replaced a client who could say no with a personal union that could not, and it did so in the month Germany took Prague."},{"q":"Why did Britain and France respond in Greece and Romania rather than Albania?","a":"Albania was gone within 2 days and neither power could reach it. What they could do was tell Italy where the next move would meet resistance, so the guarantee already given to Poland was extended to the two Balkan states most obviously at risk."},{"q":"Why did so little resistance materialise?","a":"The neighbouring states would not arm the Albanians, the resistance that formed had no organisation behind it, and the king left before the fighting began. Italian money had also been reaching local leaders directly for years, which had hollowed out the government's authority."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Italian_troops_in_Durr%C3%ABs.jpg","title":"Italian troops in Durrës, April 1939","desc":"Soldiers and vehicles in a street of the port where the landings began on 7 April.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_troops_in_Durr%C3%ABs.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a column of soldiers marching down a wide street past parked cars and low buildings."},"wiki":"Italian invasion of Albania","places":["city:1159144543"]},
    "ww2-125": {"questions":["3 months after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was signed, Mussolini wrote to Hitler that Italy could not take the initiative in a European war given the state of its preparations.","Ciano and Ribbentrop put their names to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Hitler's presence, turning the Axis proclaimed in 1936 into a formal military alliance."],"abstract":"The <b>Pact of Steel</b> was the treaty of friendship and alliance signed by Germany and Italy in Berlin on 22 May 1939, the last of the agreements that prepared the attack on Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the culmination of 3 years of drawing together that had begun with the Axis of 1936 and deepened in the joint intervention in the Spanish Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Italy had isolated itself internationally by annexing Abyssinia, and wanted to keep up an aggressive foreign policy for which it now hoped for German backing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pact provided for close military co-operation and for mutual support in the event of a war of aggression, which is what made it unlike most alliances of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Italian foreign minister Galeazzo Ciano and the German foreign minister Joachim von Ribbentrop signed it in the new Reich Chancellery with Hitler standing beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> For Hitler its value was that it committed a hesitant partner to the war he was planning, and so helped make the invasion of Poland possible 3 months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome had told Berlin more than once, before the pact was signed and after it, that Italian war preparations were far from complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 25 August Hitler wrote to inform Mussolini of the pact just concluded with the Soviet Union and of his intentions towards Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mussolini replied the same day that Italy would give every political and economic help if the conflict stayed local, but that if Poland's allies attacked he had better not take the initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two powers did fight together later, in the Balkans and in North Africa, where the co-ordination the treaty promised was conspicuously lacking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Stahlpakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/der-stahlpakt. [Open access]","German Historical Institute, \"The 'Pact of Steel': The Signing of the German-Italian Military Alliance in the New Reich Chancellery (May 22, 1939),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/ghdi:image-1998. [Open access]","\"Letter from Mussolini to Hitler, August 25, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns058.asp. [Open access]","\"Letter from Hitler to Mussolini, August 25, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns057.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die deutsch-italienischen Beziehungen,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/die-deutsch-italienischen-beziehungen. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Italy sign an alliance it was not ready to honour?","a":"Isolation after Abyssinia had left it with no other great-power friend, and it wanted German backing for expansion of its own. Rome also assumed the obligation would not be called in soon, having told Berlin more than once that Italian preparations were far from complete."},{"q":"Why does the absence of a defensive limit matter?","a":"Most alliances promise help only if a partner is attacked. This one promised help in a war of aggression as well, so Germany could begin a war of its choosing and call on Italy, which is precisely what Hitler wanted from it."},{"q":"Why did the alliance fail its first test?","a":"Because the war came years before Italy was ready for one. When Hitler asked for support in August 1939 Mussolini answered that Italy would help politically and economically if the conflict stayed local, but had better not take the initiative if Poland's allies attacked."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Patto-acciaio.jpg","title":"The signing of the Pact of Steel, 22 May 1939","desc":"The German and Italian delegations at the table in the new Reich Chancellery as the treaty is signed.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patto-acciaio.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of two men signing documents at a long table while some twenty men in uniform and evening dress stand watching behind them."},"wiki":"Pact of Steel","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-126": {"questions":["A British and French military mission travelled to Moscow in August 1939, the last stage of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and came away with nothing agreed at all.","The Soviet government's public account said it had turned to Germany because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had reached an impasse, and not the other way round."],"abstract":"The <b>Anglo-French-Soviet negotiations</b> were the attempt, running from April to August 1939, to bind the three powers into a pact of mutual assistance against Germany, and their failure cleared the way for the pact Moscow signed instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They opened in the middle of April, a fortnight after the British guarantee to Poland, and they never recovered from the slowness with which London approached them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sticking points were which small states the three would guarantee, whether a state overturned from within rather than invaded was covered, and above all whether Soviet armies could enter Poland and Romania to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the end of July Moscow proposed that military conversations begin at once, and the two western governments agreed and named their delegations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British mission was led by Admiral Sir Reginald Drax with an air marshal and a major-general, and it was sent before any political agreement had been reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The prime minister called that an almost unprecedented step, taken to show trust and in the belief that the political questions would then present no insuperable difficulty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Moscow the talks turned on passage, and the Polish government would not agree under any conditions to Soviet troops entering its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet commander of the talks told a Moscow newspaper afterwards that they had broken down on that refusal, and that the pact with Germany followed the impasse rather than causing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stalin and Molotov, talking to the German foreign minister that same week, said the British mission had never told them what it really wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether the western powers were too slow and too suspicious, or whether Moscow was bargaining with both sides and had already decided, is still the central question about the summer of 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"Anglo-French-Soviet negotiations attempting to reach an agreement for mutual assistance, April 15-August 25, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/ch6. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Anglo-French-Russian Conversations,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 31 July 1939, vol. 350, cols. 1928-30, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/jul/31/anglo-french-russian-conversations. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 27, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d302. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 24 August 1939, vol. 351, cols. 2-63, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/aug/24/international-situation. [Open access]","\"Memorandum of a Conversation Held on the Night of August 23d to 24th, Between the Reich Foreign Minister and Herren Stalin and Molotov,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns053.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the question of passage matter so much?","a":"The Soviet Union shared no border with Germany in 1939, so its armies could only fight one by crossing Poland or Romania. Without that permission a Soviet guarantee to those countries was a promise it had no way of keeping."},{"q":"Why would Poland not give the permission?","a":"Warsaw would not agree under any conditions, and the Soviet commander said so publicly when the talks collapsed. A Polish government that had spent 20 years between two powerful neighbours did not distinguish easily between an army admitted to help and an army admitted."},{"q":"Why send soldiers to Moscow before agreeing the politics?","a":"It was meant as a demonstration of good faith, and London said so: the hope was that military talks would carry the political ones. Moscow read the same sequence as evidence that Britain was not serious about a treaty."}],"places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ww2-127": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was announced while a British and French military mission was still in Moscow, and the British prime minister told the Commons it had come as an unpleasant surprise.","German forces invaded Poland 9 days after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was signed, their eastern flank secured by the state that had been the presumed enemy."],"abstract":"The <b>Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact</b> was the treaty of non-aggression between Germany and the Soviet Union, dated 23 August 1939 and signed in Moscow that night after months of secret contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its articles obliged each side to attack the other in no circumstances, to give no support to a third power that attacked the other, to consult, and to join no grouping of powers aimed at the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ran for 10 years, renewable for five more unless denounced a year in advance, and came into force the moment it was signed rather than on ratification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing in the published text was surprising in itself; what was extraordinary was who had signed it, since each state had spent 6 years describing the other as its principal enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German foreign minister flew to Moscow to conclude it, and the Soviet head of government signed as commissar for foreign affairs, in Stalin's presence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The talk that followed the signature ranged over Japan, Italy, Turkey, Britain and France, and Stalin proposed a toast to Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In London the announcement arrived while a British military mission was still in Moscow, and the prime minister told the Commons that it had come as a very unpleasant surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For Germany the pact removed the risk of a war on two fronts and made the attack on Poland possible 9 days later; it also opened a trade in Soviet raw materials for German machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the Soviet Union it bought time and territory, since a secret protocol signed the same night divided the lands between the two states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It lasted 22 months, and ended when Germany invaded the Soviet Union on 22 June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Treaty of Nonaggression Between Germany and the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, August 23, 1939,\" Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/nonagres.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Hitler-Stalin-Pakt 1939,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/hitler-stalin-pakt-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German-Soviet Pact,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-soviet-pact. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 24 August 1939, vol. 351, cols. 2-63, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/aug/24/international-situation. [Open access]","\"Memorandum of a Conversation Held on the Night of August 23d to 24th, Between the Reich Foreign Minister and Herren Stalin and Molotov,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns053.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did two declared enemies sign a treaty at all?","a":"Each got what it most wanted at that moment. Germany got a free hand in Poland without a second front; the Soviet Union got out of a war it was being asked to join on other people's terms, and gained territory and time to prepare."},{"q":"Why did it change the diplomatic situation so completely?","a":"Every western calculation had rested on Germany having to fear a war on two fronts. Once the Soviet Union was neutral, the threat that a guarantee to Poland was supposed to carry lost most of its force, and Berlin said so openly."},{"q":"Why does the published text tell only half the story?","a":"The treaty itself is an ordinary non-aggression pact and reads like one. What made it a partition of eastern Europe was the secret protocol signed with it the same night, which was not published and which the reader of the treaty could not know about."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H27337%2C_Moskau%2C_Stalin_und_Ribbentrop_im_Kreml.jpg","title":"Stalin and Ribbentrop in the Kremlin, August 1939","desc":"The Soviet leader and the German foreign minister shaking hands after the treaty was concluded.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H27337, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H27337,_Moskau,_Stalin_und_Ribbentrop_im_Kreml.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of two men in a panelled room shaking hands and looking at one another, one in a plain tunic and the other in a dark suit."},"wiki":"Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact","places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ww2-128": {"questions":["Estonia, Latvia and Finland were placed in the Soviet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by a protocol that was kept secret and that both governments long refused to acknowledge.","A month later the line between the German and the Soviet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was redrawn, Lithuania passing to Moscow in exchange for territory in central Poland."],"abstract":"A <b>sphere of influence</b> is a region one power agrees to leave to another, and the secret additional protocol signed in Moscow on 23 August 1939 drew such a boundary across eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first clause put the northern frontier of Lithuania on the line, so that Finland, Estonia and Latvia lay on the Soviet side and Lithuania on the German one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its second divided Poland approximately along the rivers Narew, Vistula and San, and left open whether an independent Polish state should survive at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its third recorded Soviet interest in Bessarabia and a declaration of complete German political disinterest in the area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The document was two pages long, was to be treated by both parties as strictly secret, and was signed by the same two foreign ministers as the treaty it was attached to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Within 6 weeks both powers had occupied their portions of Poland, and on 28 September a further secret protocol moved Lithuania into the Soviet sphere in exchange for Polish territory east of the Vistula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet Union then acted on the rest of what it had been allotted, attacking Finland in November 1939 and annexing the three Baltic states in the summer of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Germany for its part annexed western Poland outright and governed the centre as occupied territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The public treaty said nothing of any of this, and the protocol became known to the world only because American and British armies captured the German foreign ministry's archives in 1945 and published what they found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The phrase itself is older than 1939 and describes an arrangement that stops short of annexation, though in this case the distinction lasted a matter of weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["\"Secret Additional Protocol, August 23, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/addsepro.asp. [Open access]","\"Secret Supplementary Protocol, September 28, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/sesupp.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Hitler-Stalin-Pakt 1939,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/hitler-stalin-pakt-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German-Soviet Pact,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-soviet-pact. [Open access]","Raymond James Sontag and James Stuart Beddie, eds., \"Preface,\" <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941: Documents from the Archives of the German Foreign Office</i> (Washington: Department of State, 1948), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/nsprefac.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the protocol kept secret when the treaty was published?","a":"The treaty could be defended as a normal act of state; the protocol could not. It disposed of the territory of six countries that were not party to it, at a moment when both signatories were still telling those countries that their independence was safe."},{"q":"Why draw a line rather than simply agree to co-operate?","a":"Because both intended to move, and each wanted to know in advance where the other would stop. A named boundary along three rivers meant the two armies could occupy Poland in the same month without meeting as enemies."},{"q":"Why revise the line a month later?","a":"The occupation had gone differently from the plan, and the two governments traded. Germany gave up Lithuania, which the August line had assigned to it, and took in exchange the Polish territory between the Vistula and the Bug."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1990-1028-500%2C_Dtsch.-Sowjet._Grenz-_u._Freundschaftsvertrag.jpg","title":"Signing the German-Soviet boundary treaty, 28 September 1939","desc":"The treaty that redrew the line agreed a month earlier being signed in Moscow, with Molotov and Stalin standing behind.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1990-1028-500, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1990-1028-500,_Dtsch.-Sowjet._Grenz-_u._Freundschaftsvertrag.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a seated man signing a document at a desk while two men in dark clothing stand behind him watching."},"wiki":"Sphere of influence"},
    "ww2-129": {"questions":["Identified with the League of Nations and with agreements against Germany, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in the middle of negotiations with a British delegation when Molotov replaced him.","A German letter of 25 August 1939 dated the Kremlin's new willingness to deal with Berlin from the departure of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 3 months earlier."],"abstract":"<b>Maxim Litvinov</b> was the Soviet people's commissar for foreign affairs whose removal in May 1939 is usually taken as the moment Soviet policy turned away from the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was the figure through whom the outside world dealt with Moscow: it was to him that Roosevelt wrote in November 1933 to say that the United States recognised the Soviet government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was identified with the League of Nations and with the search for agreements against Germany, and in the spring of 1939 he was conducting the negotiations with the British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 3 May a decree of the Presidium appointed Molotov foreign commissar; the Soviet press announced the appointment with great fanfare and put the dismissal on the last page as a short item under Chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German chargé reported the surprise it caused, noting that Litvinov had received the British ambassador on 2 May and had stood beside Stalin at the May Day parade the day before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He read it as a sudden decision of Stalin's, connected with disagreement in the Kremlin about the negotiations and with Stalin's mistrust of the capitalist world, and remarked, in the manner of the time, that the new commissar was not a Jew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomats elsewhere could not agree what it meant: the Soviet ambassador in Paris insisted nothing had changed, Poland's foreign minister expected a change of method rather than direction, and the French feared a signal of displeasure with London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Berlin the Soviet chargé pressed the German foreign office to say whether the change would alter its attitude, and stressed how much the new man's personality would matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler, writing to Mussolini on 25 August, dated the Kremlin's readiness to reorient its relations with Germany from the departure of Litvinov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether the dismissal decided the course of that summer or merely registered a decision already taken is argued about still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"The German Chargé in the Soviet Union (Tippelskirch) to the German Foreign Office: Telegram, May 4, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns002.asp. [Open access]","\"Foreign Office Memorandum, May 5, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns003.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, May 4, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d249. [Open access]","\"Letter from Hitler to Mussolini, August 25, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns057.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR),\" <i>A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/soviet-union. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a change of commissar read as a change of policy?","a":"Because of who he was. He was the man the west negotiated with, the face of the League policy and of the search for agreements against Germany, so replacing him in the middle of the British talks was itself a statement, whatever Moscow said."},{"q":"Why did observers disagree so sharply about what it meant?","a":"Nobody outside the Kremlin knew whether it signalled a new direction or a harder bargaining method. The Soviet ambassador in Paris said nothing had changed, Warsaw expected new methods and the same aims, and Paris read it as displeasure with London."},{"q":"Why did Berlin pay such close attention to it?","a":"Germany had been putting out careful feelers towards Moscow since April, and needed to know whether they were worth pursuing. Within days its diplomats were asking what the change meant, and by August Hitler was dating the whole rapprochement from it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/RIAN_archive_409024_Soviet_diplomat_Maxim_Litvinov.jpg","title":"Maxim Litvinov","desc":"The Soviet foreign commissar photographed at a conference table in front of a wall map.","credit":"Vladimir Grebnev / RIA Novosti archive, image #409024, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_409024_Soviet_diplomat_Maxim_Litvinov.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a heavy-set man in spectacles and a dark tunic seated at a table, with a large map on the wall behind him."},"wiki":"Maxim Litvinov","places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ww2-130": {"questions":["German demands for the return of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and for a road and railway across the Polish corridor were the stated occasion of the crisis that ended in war.","A League high commissioner lived in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to settle its disputes with Poland, an office worth little once its senate was run by the local Nazi party."],"abstract":"The <b>Free City of Danzig</b> was a small self-governing state on the Baltic, created by the Treaty of Versailles in 1919 out of a largely German port and the district around it, tied administratively to Poland and belonging to neither state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty placed it under the protection of the League of Nations, guaranteed its constitution and provided for a high commissioner resident in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That commissioner was to deal in the first instance with every difference arising between Poland and the Free City, which was the arrangement's central weakness and its central purpose at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Danzig lay inside the Polish customs frontier, Poland controlled the Vistula and the railways within its territory, and Poland conducted its foreign relations and protected its citizens abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans ordinarily resident there lost German nationality and became citizens of the Free City, an arrangement Germany resented from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The local Nazi party dominated the city senate from 1933, so the League's guarantees increasingly protected a government whose own purpose was to have the city returned to Germany, and Jewish shops there were daubed with antisemitic slogans by 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From October 1938 Berlin pressed Poland to agree to the return of Danzig, to an extraterritorial road and railway across the corridor to East Prussia, and to join the Anti-Comintern Pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Poland refused, and the British guarantee of 31 March 1939 was followed on 28 April by Hitler's denunciation of the German-Polish non-aggression pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through that summer the Commons repeatedly asked whether an attempt to change the city's status, from outside or from within, would count as aggression covered by the pledge to Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany annexed Danzig when it invaded Poland in September 1939, and the city has been Polish Gdańsk since 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Treaty of Versailles, Part III, Section XI: Danzig, Articles 100-108,\" Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Danzig,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/danzig. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Danzig (British Position),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 10 July 1939, vol. 349, cols. 1786-90, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/jul/10/danzig-british-position. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der deutsch-polnische Nichtangriffspakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/der-deutsch-polnische-nichtangriffspakt. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Increasing German pressure on Poland, April 15-August 21, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/ch5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the city not simply given to Poland or left to Germany?","a":"It was overwhelmingly German in population and indispensable to Poland as a port, so either answer denied one of the two something it regarded as essential. The Free City was an attempt to satisfy both, and it satisfied neither."},{"q":"Why did the League's protection count for so little?","a":"Its instrument was a high commissioner whose job was to arbitrate disputes with Poland. That works while both sides want an arbitrator; from 1933 the city's own government wanted annexation instead, and no arbitration could produce that."},{"q":"Why did the quarrel over one city become a European war?","a":"Danzig was the demand, but the road and railway across the corridor came with it, and behind both stood the question of whether Poland would become a German satellite. Britain had guaranteed that it would not, and both sides knew it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H27915%2C_Danzig%2C_Enfernen_eines_polnischen_Hoheitszeichens.jpg","title":"A Polish emblem taken down in Danzig, 1939","desc":"Men removing the Polish state emblem from a building in the Free City as German control was imposed.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H27915, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H27915,_Danzig,_Enfernen_eines_polnischen_Hoheitszeichens.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of two men on a pavement prising a metal emblem off a wall beside a doorway covered in printed notices."},"wiki":"Free City of Danzig","places":["city:1159149707"]},
    "ww2-131": {"questions":["A poll in 1937 found 73% in favour of a national vote before any declaration of war, one measure of how deep <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran in the years before Pearl Harbor.","Senators of both parties invoked the first president's warning against permanent alliances when they blocked attempts to act abroad, and their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shaped a decade of policy."],"abstract":"<b>Isolationism</b> was the American conviction, dominant between the wars, that the United States should stay out of European and Asian conflicts and out of the alliances that produce them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a retreat from the world: through the same years the country went on expanding its trade and defending its interests in Latin America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its advocates reached back to the Farewell Address of 1796, which urged Americans to have as little political connection as possible with foreign nations and to steer clear of permanent alliances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The First World War gave the case its force, and the charge that bankers and arms makers had pushed the country into it was pressed by a senator's report and by two popular books of the mid-1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A retired Marine Corps general put it at its bluntest in 1935, defining a racket as something that is not what it seems to most people and reckoning that the last war had made at least 21,000 new millionaires in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Congress had already refused to join the League of Nations, and in the 1930s it wrote the argument into law as a series of Neutrality Acts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pact signed at Paris in 1928 seemed to make more than that unnecessary, since its three short articles renounced war as an instrument of national policy and provided for no enforcement of any kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The movement belonged to no single party: its leaders in the Senate were progressives from California, Idaho and Wisconsin, and it drew in business owners and peace activists alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1938 an Indiana congressman came close to writing it into the Constitution, with an amendment requiring a national referendum before any declaration of war except after an attack on American soil; a poll the year before had found 73% in favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Support fell away as the war spread, and the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor settled the question: the House voted for war 388 to 1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, \"American Isolationism in the 1930s,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/american-isolationism. [Open access]","United States House of Representatives, \"Supporting a National Vote to Declare War,\" History, Art &amp; Archives, https://history.house.gov/HouseRecord/Detail/25769819821. [Open access]","George Washington, \"Washington's Farewell Address 1796,\" The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/washing.asp. [Open access]","Smedley D. Butler, <i>War Is a Racket</i> (New York: Round Table Press, 1935), https://archive.org/details/WarIsARacket. [Open access]","\"Kellogg-Briand Pact 1928,\" The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/kbpact.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the First World War make Americans less willing to fight another?","a":"Because the case for having entered it looked thinner afterwards. A Senate inquiry and two widely read books argued that bankers and munitions makers had drawn the country in for profit, so the casualties came to seem a price paid for somebody else's business rather than for a national interest."},{"q":"Why is it wrong to describe isolationism as cutting the country off?","a":"Because trade and hemispheric influence went on growing throughout. What isolationists refused was political and military entanglement in Europe and Asia — alliances, collective security and war — not commerce, and the United States was economically engaged the whole time."},{"q":"Why did the amendment for a war referendum attract so much support?","a":"It followed from the argument that ordinary people, who would be conscripted to fight, had been manoeuvred into the last war by interests that would not. Putting the decision to a national vote promised to take it away from those interests, and 73% of those polled in 1937 approved."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/UncleSamShouldveStayedHome.jpg","title":"Some people think I should have stayed t'hum","desc":"A 1919 cartoon of Uncle Sam picking his way out of a barbed-wire field labelled European Politics, umbrella marked Monroe Doctrine in one hand and a bag marked Prestige in the other, with bricks labelled Japan and Italy flying past.","credit":"Harry Grant Dart in Life magazine, 26 June 1919, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UncleSamShouldveStayedHome.jpg","alt":"Cartoon of a gaunt Uncle Sam stepping over barbed wire in a smoky landscape, carrying a furled umbrella and a travelling bag while labelled bricks fly at him."},"wiki":"Isolationism","places":["city:1159151573"]},
    "ww2-132": {"questions":["A cash-and-carry clause let a belligerent buy anything but weapons if it paid at once and carried the goods away itself, the one concession the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made to the White House.","In November 1939 Congress lifted the arms embargo and put all trade with warring states on cash-and-carry terms, the last of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before Lend-Lease."],"abstract":"The <b>Neutrality Acts</b> were a series of laws passed between 1935 and 1939 by which Congress tried to keep the United States out of another war by fixing the terms of American neutrality in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, approved on 31 August 1935, forbade the export of arms to states at war, required arms makers to register and hold an export licence, and warned Americans that they travelled in a war zone at their own risk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It set up a National Munitions Control Board of five cabinet secretaries to run the licensing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The president signed it while warning that no legislature can foresee every future situation, and that provisions this inflexible might have the opposite effect from the one intended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Congress renewed the embargo in February 1936 with a ban on loans to belligerents; an act continued in force on 1 May 1937 barred Americans from belligerent ships and covered civil wars too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That act carried the one concession the White House had asked for, cash-and-carry, which let a belligerent buy anything but arms if it paid at once and shipped the goods itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oil and other raw materials were not implements of war, so the clause was worth most to whoever had the hard currency and the merchant fleet, which meant Britain and France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After war broke out the president asked Congress to repeal the arms embargo, saying he regretted that Congress had passed the 1935 act and regretted equally that he had signed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The act of November 1939 lifted the embargo, put all trade with belligerents on cash-and-carry terms, kept the ban on loans and shut American ships out of the combat zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cash-and-carry served only a buyer with dollars, and Britain soon lacked them; with Lend-Lease agreed, the House repealed the ban on arming merchant ships in October 1941 and the Senate lifted the combat-zone bar the next month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, \"The Neutrality Acts, 1930s,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/neutrality-acts. [Open access]","United States Congress, \"Joint Resolution Providing for the Prohibition of the Export of Arms, Ammunition, and Implements of War to Belligerent Countries,\" Public Resolution No. 67, 49 Stat. 1081, 31 August 1935, U.S. Government Publishing Office, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-49/pdf/STATUTE-49-Pg1081.pdf. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Statement on Neutrality Legislation,\" 31 August 1935, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-neutrality-legislation. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Message to Congress Urging Repeal of the Embargo Provisions of the Neutrality Law,\" 21 September 1939, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/message-congress-urging-repeal-the-embargo-provisions-the-neutrality-law. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Lend-Lease and Military Aid to the Allies in the Early Years of World War II,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/lend-lease. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why legislate neutrality in advance rather than decide case by case?","a":"Because the argument of the 1930s was that the last war had been entered by drift — loans, arms sales and shipping had entangled the country before anyone chose. Fixing the rules ahead of time was meant to remove the discretion, and the choices, that had produced that drift."},{"q":"Why did cash-and-carry favour Britain and France in particular?","a":"Because it required both hard currency and shipping. A buyer had to pay immediately and carry the goods away in its own vessels, and of the likely belligerents only Britain and France had the dollars and the merchant fleets, so a rule that looked even-handed was not."},{"q":"Why did the president come to regret a law he had signed himself?","a":"Because it treated aggressor and victim alike. He had warned at the signing that inflexible provisions might drag the country into war rather than keep it out, and by 1939 an embargo that denied arms to the states resisting Germany was working against the policy he wanted."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Neutrality_Act_of_1939_LCCN2016877743.jpg/1920px-Neutrality_Act_of_1939_LCCN2016877743.jpg","title":"The last page of the Neutrality Act of 1939","desc":"The signatures of the Speaker and the Vice President, with the president's approval written beneath them at 12.04 p.m. on 4 November 1939.","credit":"Harris and Ewing, Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neutrality_Act_of_1939_LCCN2016877743.jpg","alt":"Photograph of the closing page of a printed joint resolution, with three handwritten signatures and a dated line of approval at the foot."},"places":["city:1159151573"]},
    "ww2-133": {"questions":["At a press conference the next day the president refused to amplify the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, told reporters that sanctions were out of the window, and said they would have to invent a clue themselves.","Naming no aggressor anywhere in its text, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was followed by a conference at Brussels that Japan twice declined to attend."],"abstract":"The <b>Quarantine Speech</b> was an address given by the American president at Chicago on 5 October 1937, and the first public sign that he wanted his country to act against aggression abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He told the crowd that a reign of terror and international lawlessness had reached a stage where the very foundations of civilisation were threatened, with civilians killed by bombs from the air and ships sunk without notice in what was called peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 90% of the world's people wanted to live in peace, he said, and were being put at risk by the other ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sentence that gave the speech its name compared this to disease: when an epidemic begins to spread, a community approves and joins in a quarantine of the patients to protect its own health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He named no country, no dictator and no war anywhere in the text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Behind it lay the war in China, begun at the Marco Polo Bridge that July, and an American opinion swinging towards the Chinese as Japanese forces moved down the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Asked at a press conference the next day whether he cared to amplify the remark, the president answered simply no.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pressed on whether he meant economic sanctions, he called the word a terrible one, said sanctions were out of the window and that a quarantine was not one because sanctions are coercive, and told reporters they would have to invent a clue for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Conferences, he added, were out of the window too; yet the one thing the speech produced was a conference, which met at Brussels in November under the Nine Power Treaty and which the Japanese government twice refused to join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Americans were not yet willing to risk their lives and livelihoods for peace abroad, and the country stayed behind its neutrality laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"October 5, 1937: Quarantine Speech,\" Presidential Speeches, Miller Center, University of Virginia, https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/october-5-1937-quarantine-speech. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Press Conference,\" 6 October 1937, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/press-conference-19. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"American Isolationism in the 1930s,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/american-isolationism. [Open access]","Norman H. Davis to Shigeru Yoshida, Paris, 3 December 1937, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, document 228, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v04/d228. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937-41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the speech name no aggressor?","a":"Because naming one would have been a commitment. The president was testing how far opinion would let him go, and a general argument about lawlessness could be applauded by people who would have refused any specific step against Japan, Germany or Italy."},{"q":"Why did he refuse to explain what a quarantine was?","a":"Because he had no programme, and said so: it was an attitude rather than a plan. Defining it would have forced him to say whether he meant economic sanctions, which he had explicitly ruled out, or something that looked like abandoning neutrality."},{"q":"Why did the Brussels Conference achieve nothing?","a":"Because the state it was called to restrain would not come. Japan declined both invitations, holding that the war was a matter between itself and China, and the treaty powers had no means of acting that did not require the agreement of the country they were meeting about."}],"wiki":"Quarantine Speech","places":["city:1159151491"]},
    "ww2-134": {"questions":["About 140 km (87 miles) of fortification cost more than twice the sum first voted, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deliberately stopped short of the Belgian border.","A German general told the Nuremberg tribunal that his army could not have broken through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at its strongest points between the Rhine and Luxembourg."],"abstract":"The <b>Maginot Line</b> was the belt of fortifications France built along its frontier with Germany in the 1930s, and the concrete form of a doctrine that expected the next war to be fought as the last had ended, on a continuous front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its enabling law was voted on 14 January 1930 at a cost of 3,000 million francs, after a decade in which French planning had turned away from the offensive schemes of the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Junior officers objected that the money should go instead to tanks and mobile divisions, Charles de Gaulle among them, and were brushed aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1935 the works had cost 7,000 million francs, more than twice the original budget, for about 140 km (87 miles) of fortification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stopped short of the 400 km (250 miles) of Belgian frontier on purpose, since fortifying that would have told Belgium that France did not intend to defend it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The line was not meant to win a war but to buy time, holding an attack with few men while France mobilised and pushing any German advance into Belgium, where it could be fought in the open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany answered with the Westwall, which in contrast to the elaborate French works was a system of smaller bunkers, tank traps and obstacles distributed in depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 31 August 1939 Hitler's first war directive ordered that the German western frontier was not to be crossed without his express permission and that the army there was to hold the Westwall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The French did not attack either, and a German general later told the Nuremberg tribunal that if Germany did not collapse in 1939 it was only because some 110 French and British divisions in the west were held completely inactive against 23 German ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same witness explained why the offensive of May 1940 went round rather than through: heavy artillery to break the strongest sectors was lacking, and a slow siege would have left the German rear open to an Anglo-French thrust through Belgium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ioannis Salavrakos, \"An Economic Analysis of the Fall of France in June 1940 Based on the Theory of New Institutional Economics,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 19, no. 4 (2019): 40-75, https://doi.org/10.55016/ad1dxt54. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 25, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-255-nsia. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, \"Directive No. 1 for the Conduct of the War,\" 31 August 1939, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/wardir1.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 4 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-04-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]","Robert Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939-1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1-18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did France not fortify the Belgian frontier as well?","a":"Because a wall there would have been a message. Fortifying the line with Belgium would have told the Belgians that France meant to fight west of them rather than for them, so the gap was a political choice, not an oversight."},{"q":"Why call it a doctrine rather than just a wall?","a":"Because the fortifications only make sense inside a plan. They were meant to hold the German frontier cheaply while France mobilised, and to channel any attack into Belgium where a battle could be fought in the open — the works were one part of a way of fighting."},{"q":"Why did the Germans go around it in 1940 rather than through?","a":"Because they judged they could not break it. Their operations chief said the heavy artillery for the strongest sectors was lacking, and that a long siege would have left their rear open to a British and French advance through Belgium and Holland."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Maginot_Line_Fort_%28165456755%29.jpg","title":"A gallery inside a Maginot Line fort","desc":"The works were mostly underground: a lit tunnel with its narrow-gauge track in the floor and cable runs along the wall, linking blocks buried under the hillside.","credit":"Thomas Roessler, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maginot_Line_Fort_%28165456755%29.jpg","alt":"A long straight concrete tunnel lit by a single lamp, with a narrow railway track set into the floor and bundles of cables running along the right-hand wall."},"wiki":"Maginot Line","places":["pl:q22640"]},
    "ww2-135": {"questions":["The Opposition moved in March 1935 that the policy was at variance with the spirit of the League of Nations, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was argued over in those terms for years.","An agreement with Germany in June 1935 fixing the two fleets at 35 to 100 belongs to the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as much as any warship laid down."],"abstract":"<b>British rearmament</b> in the 1930s was the slow and bitterly contested rebuilding of forces run down since 1918, and it began in earnest with the defence white paper of 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commons debate on that paper, on 11 March, shows how unwelcome the change was: the Opposition moved that the policy was completely at variance with the spirit in which the League of Nations had been created and would end in an arms race and in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Members complained that a Parliament which had opened on disarmament had come down first to limitation and then to rearmament, and one put the sum already spent on armaments at 1,500 million pounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government's case rested on what was happening abroad, since Germany was rearming and Japan had left the League.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That same March Germany announced an air force and reintroduced conscription, planning a peacetime army of 36 divisions and 580,000 men that was to be fit for war by 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Britain's first answer was to negotiate rather than only to build: an agreement signed in London on 18 June 1935 set the German fleet at 35% of the British, with parity allowed in submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It breached the Versailles terms and broke the Stresa front, and was defended in London as a success for appeasement and as insurance against a naval race like the one before 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Building competed with everything else: when South Africa asked in 1936 for 15-inch guns to defend Cape Town, Britain's own programme made it unwilling to part with guns of that calibre, and it lent a monitor instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Compulsion came only in 1939, when the Commons debated on 4 May a bill to call up men of 20 and 21 for 6 months of continuous training as militiamen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 310,000 men were of that age and perhaps 200,000 would be available; the scheme was attacked in the House as taking a steam-hammer to crack a nut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Defence,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 March 1935, vol. 299, cols. 35-174, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1935/mar/11/defence. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Military Training Bill,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 May 1939, vol. 346, cols. 2095-2209, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/may/04/military-training-bill. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Wehrpflicht 1935,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/wehrpflicht-1935. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Das deutsch-britische Flottenabkommen,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/das-deutsch-britische-flottenabkommen. [Open access]","Deon Visser, \"Anglo-South African Relations and the Erebus Scheme, 1936-1939,\" <i>Scientia Militaria: South African Journal of Military Studies</i> 35, no. 1 (2011), https://doi.org/10.5787/35-1-30. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was rearmament so unpopular in 1935?","a":"Because it read as an admission that collective security had failed. The Opposition argued in the Commons that building arms was at variance with the spirit of the League of Nations and would produce the very competition it was meant to guard against."},{"q":"Why sign a naval agreement with Germany while rearming against her?","a":"Because limitation was thought cheaper than competition. Fixing the German fleet at 35% of the British promised to prevent a race like the one before 1914 — at the price of accepting a breach of Versailles and breaking the front Britain had just joined at Stresa."},{"q":"Why did conscription come only in 1939?","a":"Because it was politically the hardest step of all. Compulsory service was treated as a Continental practice foreign to Britain, and even in May 1939 the bill to train men of 20 and 21 for 6 months was attacked in the Commons as wildly disproportionate."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Prince_of_Wales-1.jpg","title":"British rearmament","desc":"Prince of Wales-1.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_of_Wales-1.jpg","alt":"The battleship HMS Prince of Wales under way at sea"},"wiki":"British rearmament before World War II","places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-136": {"questions":["A poor state on the European periphery needed wars that were short and lively, and out of that need came <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ‘the war of movement’ at the operational level.","Mission tactics left the means to the officer on the spot, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be fought by divisions and corps rather than directed from the top."],"abstract":"<b>Bewegungskrieg</b>, ‘the war of movement’, is the name the Prussians gave to a way of fighting that runs through German military thinking from the seventeenth century to the Third Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its origin was a strategic problem rather than a theory: a small and impoverished state on the European periphery could not survive a long war, and so had to fight wars that were, in the phrase of its rulers, short and lively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it stressed was manoeuvre at the operational level, not a faster march rate or a nimbler platoon but the movement of large units such as divisions, corps and armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end it aimed at was the Kesselschlacht, the cauldron battle, in which an enemy was hemmed in on all sides and then destroyed in a series of concentric operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its command style, usually called Auftragstaktik today, gave a subordinate a mission and left the means of achieving it to the officer on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The tradition survived defeat inside an army the Versailles treaty had cut to 100,000 men, of whom 4,000 were officers, with its general staff disbanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those functions were carried on under another name by the Truppenamt, and the treaty's bans were evaded by building weapons and training crews in the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When conscription returned in March 1935 the Reichswehr was renamed the Wehrmacht and set to grow to 36 divisions and 580,000 men, fit for war by 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By that year the peacetime army counted 36 infantry and motorised divisions in Germany proper, three more in Austria and the Sudeten districts, five Panzer divisions, four light divisions and three mountain divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The idea was not universal: French planning in the same years rested on firepower and a set-piece defensive battle, and treated the tank as a support arm for the infantry rather than a weapon of decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939-1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1-18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 8-20, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-255-nsia. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Wehrpflicht 1935,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/wehrpflicht-1935. [Open access]","United States War Department, <i>Handbook on German Military Forces</i>, TM-E 30-451 (Washington, D.C.: War Department, 1945), chap. 1, https://archive.org/details/TME-30-4511945-nsia. [Open access]","Ioannis Salavrakos, \"An Economic Analysis of the Fall of France in June 1940 Based on the Theory of New Institutional Economics,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 19, no. 4 (2019): 40-75, https://doi.org/10.55016/ad1dxt54. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a poor state develop a doctrine of movement?","a":"Because it could not afford the alternative. Prussia lacked the population and money to win a war of attrition, so its armies had to seek a quick decision — and a doctrine built to end wars fast is what poverty on an exposed frontier produced."},{"q":"Why is the operational level the important one here?","a":"Because it is the level at which the doctrine works. Bewegungskrieg was not about individual soldiers moving faster but about swinging divisions, corps and whole armies around an enemy, so its unit of thought is the formation rather than the tactic."},{"q":"Why did leaving the means to subordinates suit this way of war?","a":"Because movement outruns orders. If formations are to encircle an enemy who is also moving, decisions have to be made where the information is, so a commander set the mission and let the officer present choose how to accomplish it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Liebenwerdaer_Heide_1939_Wehrmacht_%C3%9Cbung.jpg/1920px-Liebenwerdaer_Heide_1939_Wehrmacht_%C3%9Cbung.jpg","title":"A German army exercise on the Liebenwerda heath, 1939","desc":"Engineers laying a corduroy road of felled trunks so that a horse-drawn wagon can cross soft ground, the practical side of a doctrine built on movement.","credit":"Photograph BB, family archive, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liebenwerdaer_Heide_1939_Wehrmacht_%C3%9Cbung.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of soldiers in steel helmets beside a two-horse wagon driving onto a track of logs laid across sandy ground in a pine wood."},"wiki":"Maneuver warfare","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-137": {"questions":["Most of the tanks in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1939 were the light Mark I and Mark II, against only 20 Mark III and 24 Mark IV apiece.","Beside them stood four light divisions, small motorised formations with one tank battalion each, which the army meant to convert into a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apiece in the autumn of 1939."],"abstract":"A <b>Panzer division</b> was the German armoured formation of the late 1930s: a tank brigade or regiment together with motorised infantry, a motorised artillery regiment of 105 mm howitzers, and reconnaissance, antitank, engineer and signal battalions, all under one commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its authorised strength was about 12,000 officers and men, and it carried roughly 300 tanks of the four types then in service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of those were very light: the Mark I was a two-man tankette with two machine guns, and the Mark II a three-man vehicle mounting a 20 mm gun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four of the divisions that faced Poland were each to have 124 Mark I and 138 Mark II against only 20 Mark III and 24 Mark IV, and even those numbers were not reached everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside them stood four light divisions, small motorised formations with one tank battalion apiece, which were to become Panzer divisions in the autumn of 1939 as equipment allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What mattered was less the machines than their concentration: about three-quarters of the German tank force was gathered into armoured divisions, the remainder kept as general headquarters regiments for close infantry support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Poland had done the opposite, forming only one armoured brigade out of some 600 tanks and dispersing the rest among corps and army tank battalions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> France, which had more tanks than Germany, also treated the tank as a support arm for the infantry rather than a weapon of decision, and had built almost none at all in 1934 and 1935 before reaching 1,059 in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Concentration was what the German operational tradition asked for, since a war of movement is fought by whole formations swinging round an enemy rather than by escorting an infantry advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The five Panzer and four light divisions took the field together in September 1939, and the lessons the army drew from Poland were applied in the campaigns that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 28-31, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-255-nsia. [Open access]","Robert Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939-1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1-18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","Ioannis Salavrakos, \"An Economic Analysis of the Fall of France in June 1940 Based on the Theory of New Institutional Economics,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 19, no. 4 (2019): 40-75, https://doi.org/10.55016/ad1dxt54. [Open access]","United States War Department, <i>Handbook on German Military Forces</i>, TM-E 30-451 (Washington, D.C.: War Department, 1945), chap. 1, https://archive.org/details/TME-30-4511945-nsia. [Open access]","United States War Department, General Staff, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, September 1 to October 5, 1939</i> (Washington, D.C.: War Department, 1942), https://archive.org/details/TheGermanCampaignInPoland-nsia. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did concentrating tanks matter more than the tanks themselves?","a":"Because a tank scattered among infantry battalions can only help an infantry attack. Gathered into a division with its own infantry, guns and engineers, armour can be sent somewhere on its own — and it was that choice, not better machines, that separated the German army from its neighbours in 1939."},{"q":"Why give a tank division its own infantry and artillery?","a":"Because tanks alone cannot hold ground or reduce a defended position. Putting motorised infantry, howitzers, engineers and signals in the same formation meant the whole force moved at the tanks' pace instead of waiting for the rest of the army to catch up."},{"q":"Why were German tanks in 1939 so light if the doctrine was so ambitious?","a":"Because production had not caught up with the plan. Most vehicles were the Mark I tankette and the Mark II with its 20 mm gun, with only a few dozen Mark III and Mark IV in each division, and even those figures were not met everywhere."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/German_tanks_of_the_7th_Panzer_Division_roll_through_a_French_town%2C_May-June_1940_%28242-EAPC-3-275.35%29.jpg/1920px-German_tanks_of_the_7th_Panzer_Division_roll_through_a_French_town%2C_May-June_1940_%28242-EAPC-3-275.35%29.jpg","title":"Tanks of the 7th Panzer Division in France, 1940","desc":"A column of light tanks of the kind that made up most of a Panzer division's strength, driving in single file through a French town.","credit":"United States National Archives and Records Administration, photo 242-EAPC-3-275/35, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_tanks_of_the_7th_Panzer_Division_roll_through_a_French_town,_May-June_1940_%28242-EAPC-3-275.35%29.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph looking down a straight road in a small town, with four small tracked vehicles driving nose to tail towards the camera, a commander standing in each turret."},"wiki":"Panzer division (Wehrmacht)","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-138": {"questions":["For <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the air arm was so essentially offensive that using it defensively doomed it to absurdity, and the only sound course was to strike first and hardest.","Bombs of explosive, incendiary and poison gas were what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> expected to end a war quickly by wrecking whole areas of population."],"abstract":"<b>Giulio Douhet</b> was an Italian officer, born in 1869, whose <i>The Command of the Air</i> appeared in 1921 under the auspices of the Ministry of War and remains the most uncompromising statement of interwar air power theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To have command of the air, he wrote, is to be in a position to prevent the enemy from flying while retaining the ability to fly oneself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Command of the air meant victory; to be beaten in the air meant defeat and the acceptance of whatever terms the enemy chose to impose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held the air arm to be so essentially offensive that using it defensively doomed it to absurdity, since a defending force might be the stronger and still be left waiting on the attacker’s initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The weapons he expected were explosive, incendiary and poison-gas bombs mixed as the target required, which he thought could wreck large areas of population and their transit lines in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Politicians took the argument up: the Commons was told in November 1932 that the man in the street should realise no power on earth could protect him from being bombed, because the bomber would always get through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The only defence was said to be offence, which meant killing more women and children more quickly than the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fear shaped the decade, and in Chicago in 1937 the American president opened his warning about international lawlessness with civilians murdered by bombs from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Spain was a testing ground rather than a proof: the Legion Condor had 100 aircraft and 5,000 men there by November 1936, and the bombing of Guernica in April 1937 was condemned across the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The air force that went to war in 1939 was not built on the theory, its tasks in Poland being to destroy the Polish air force, disrupt mobilisation and give the army close support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Giulio Douhet, <i>The Command of the Air</i>, trans. Dino Ferrari (Washington, D.C.: Office of Air Force History, 1983), https://archive.org/details/TheCommandOfTheAir. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"International Affairs,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 10 November 1932, vol. 270, cols. 525-641, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1932/nov/10/international-affairs. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 16-17, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-255-nsia. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Legion Condor,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/legion-condor. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"October 5, 1937: Quarantine Speech,\" Presidential Speeches, Miller Center, University of Virginia, https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/october-5-1937-quarantine-speech. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the theory conclude that defence was pointless?","a":"Because it treated the air as a medium with no front line. If an attacker can fly anywhere, a defender must be strong everywhere at once, so a force kept back to defend is larger than the attacker and still cannot choose where the blow falls."},{"q":"Why did politicians repeat the argument so readily in the 1930s?","a":"Because it was frightening and simple, and nobody had yet tested it. Told that the bomber would always get through and that the only defence was to bomb first, governments could see no protection for their populations short of matching the threat."},{"q":"Why did the German air force of 1939 not follow it?","a":"Because it was built to serve a war of movement on the ground. Its tasks in Poland were to destroy the enemy air force, break up mobilisation and support the army directly — work that assumes a land campaign rather than a war decided from the air alone."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Scuola_di_guerra_aerea%2C_palazzina_di_comando%2C_interno%2C_sala_degli_stemmi%2C_busto_di_giulio_douhet.jpg/1920px-Scuola_di_guerra_aerea%2C_palazzina_di_comando%2C_interno%2C_sala_degli_stemmi%2C_busto_di_giulio_douhet.jpg","title":"A bust of Douhet at the Air War School, Florence","desc":"The bronze stands in the hall of arms of the command building; no free photograph of him at a usable size has been found.","credit":"Francesco Bini, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scuola_di_guerra_aerea,_palazzina_di_comando,_interno,_sala_degli_stemmi,_busto_di_giulio_douhet.jpg","alt":"A dark bronze portrait head of a middle-aged man with a clipped moustache and swept-back hair, mounted on a green marble plinth against a white wall."},"wiki":"Giulio Douhet","places":["city:1159151593"]},
    "ww2-139": {"questions":["With an active strength of about 730,000 men and 1,100,000 in reserve, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could raise 51 reserve divisions, every one of them infantry.","France had more artillery and Poland more horse cavalry, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the army that had concentrated its tanks rather than spreading them among the infantry."],"abstract":"The <b>Wehrmacht</b> was the German armed force created in March 1935, when the Reichswehr was renamed and conscription returned in defiance of the Versailles treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plan was a peacetime army of 36 divisions and 580,000 men, fit for war by 1939, with service of one year and then of two from August 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the outbreak of war the active army stood at about 730,000 men and the reserve at about 1,100,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine of its divisions were armoured or light, five Panzer and four light, while the 51 reserve divisions it could raise on mobilisation were all infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mobilisation meant requisitioning motor vehicles and horses to meet the extensive requirements of those reserve infantry divisions and their supporting units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Polish army it attacked was authorised 30 infantry divisions, 14 horse cavalry brigades, one mechanised cavalry brigade and two air divisions, on a peacetime ceiling of 280,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poland had about 600 tanks and had formed only one armoured brigade from them, most of the rest being dispersed among corps and army tank battalions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> France, once mobilised, counted 2,680,000 men in 75 infantry divisions, 13 fortress divisions, five light cavalry divisions and 40 tank battalions, and had more artillery than Germany, 11,200 guns against 7,710.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain had no comparable army at all and was still debating, on 4 May 1939, a bill to call up men of 20 and 21 for 6 months of training, of whom perhaps 200,000 would be available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The difference lay less in numbers than in where they stood: a German general testified after the war that about 110 French and British divisions in the west were held completely inactive against 23 German ones while Poland was being overrun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 22-31, 51-53, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-255-nsia. [Open access]","Ioannis Salavrakos, \"An Economic Analysis of the Fall of France in June 1940 Based on the Theory of New Institutional Economics,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 19, no. 4 (2019): 40-75, https://doi.org/10.55016/ad1dxt54. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Military Training Bill,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 May 1939, vol. 346, cols. 2095-2209, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/may/04/military-training-bill. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 4 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-04-46.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Wehrpflicht 1935,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/wehrpflicht-1935. [Open access]","United States War Department, General Staff, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, September 1 to October 5, 1939</i> (Washington, D.C.: War Department, 1942), https://archive.org/details/TheGermanCampaignInPoland-nsia. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the picture of a fully mechanised German army wrong?","a":"Because most of it walked. Nine divisions were armoured or light and every one of the 51 reserve divisions was infantry, and mobilisation required requisitioning horses in quantity to move those divisions and their supply units."},{"q":"Why did more French artillery and tanks not decide the matter?","a":"Because equipment counts for less than how it is organised and used. France had 11,200 guns to Germany's 7,710 and plenty of tanks, but the tanks were spread in battalions to support infantry, so they were never concentrated where a decision could be forced."},{"q":"Why does the inactivity of the western armies in 1939 matter so much?","a":"Because it turned a risk into a free hand. Germany left only 23 divisions facing about 110 French and British ones while the rest overran Poland, and a German general said afterwards that this alone was why the country did not collapse that year."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/40/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-318-0071-16A%2C_Polen%2C_bespannte_Einheiten_bei_Fluss%C3%BCberquerung.jpg","title":"Horse-drawn units crossing a river in Poland, 1939","desc":"Most of the German army moved like this: a wagon team crossing a pontoon bridge while more transport waits on the far bank.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-318-0071-16A, photograph Koll, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-318-0071-16A,_Polen,_bespannte_Einheiten_bei_Fluss%C3%BCberquerung.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a two-horse gun team coming off a low pontoon bridge onto a sandy bank, with a long column of wagons still crossing behind it."},"wiki":"Wehrmacht","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-140": {"questions":["The claim at its centre was that Germany's future depended on solving a need for space, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the deadline for acting at 1943 to 1945.","Written up 5 days after the meeting and never a verbatim transcript, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was disputed at Nuremberg on those grounds and has been ever since."],"abstract":"The <b>Hossbach Memorandum</b> is the record of a conference held in the Reich Chancellery on the afternoon of 5 November 1937, written up 5 days afterwards by the officer who took the notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler told the war minister, the three service chiefs and the foreign minister that he wished his exposition to be regarded, in the event of his death, as his last will and testament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument was about space: some 85 million Germans were packed into a habitable area he called too narrow, and no answer lay in self-sufficiency or in world trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Living space had to be sought in Europe rather than in colonies, and nowhere was empty, since the attacker always comes up against a possessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He put a date on it, saying that if Germany did not act by 1943 to 1945 any year might bring a food crisis for want of foreign exchange, and that this would be a turning point for the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two of the contingencies he described were the overthrow of Austria and of Czechoslovakia, which is why the document was read at Nuremberg as evidence of a plan for aggressive war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The defence did not deny its authenticity, but pointed out that it does not purport to be a verbatim transcript and was dated 5 days after the meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Goering testified that he had not believed Hitler really meant to attack either country, and that the conference was meant to press the army's commander-in-chief to speed rearmament up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What followed was not the sequence the memorandum sketched: Austria went in March 1938, the Sudetenland at Munich, the rest of Czechoslovakia in March 1939, and on 31 August a directive fixed the attack on Poland for 4:45 AM.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether it shows a programme or a mood is the hinge of every argument about inevitability; what is certain is that the war Germany got was not the short one its way of fighting was built to win.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["\"Minutes of a Conference in the Reich Chancellery, Berlin, November 5, 1937,\" The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/hossbach.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Nazi Regime in Germany,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judnazi.asp. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 4-7, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-255-nsia. [Open access]","Robert Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939-1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1-18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, \"Directive No. 1 for the Conduct of the War,\" 31 August 1939, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/wardir1.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the document matter more than what was said at the meeting?","a":"Because it is the only record. The conference was secret and left no other minute, so every argument about whether Hitler had a timetable for war rests on what one officer wrote down 5 days later from memory."},{"q":"Why did the defence at Nuremberg attack its form rather than its contents?","a":"Because denying the contents was impossible. The paper was plainly authentic, so the case had to be that it was not verbatim, was written up late, and recorded a way of pressing generals to rearm faster rather than a decision to go to war."},{"q":"Why does the sequence of 1938 and 1939 complicate the case for a plan?","a":"Because events did not follow the memorandum. It sketched contingencies involving France, Italy and a war in the Mediterranean, whereas Austria, the Sudetenland, Prague and Poland came in an order and by means the paper does not describe."}],"wiki":"Hossbach Memorandum","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-141": {"questions":["German troops crossed the Polish frontier at 4:45 AM on 1 September 1939, executing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the plan named in Berlin's own files.","The Nuremberg judges traced the war's origin to an armed forces directive naming <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, issued 5 months before any shot was fired near Danzig."],"abstract":"<b>Fall Weiss</b>, or ‘Case White’, was the German armed forces' plan for a war against Poland, and the name under which the invasion of September 1939 was prepared and fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Part II of the annual directive for the armed forces, issued on 3 April 1939, carried that title and required preparations to be complete so that the attack could be launched at any time from 1 September onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its mission was stated plainly: should Poland's attitude require a solution by force, the Wehrmacht was to destroy the Polish armed forces by surprise attack, and mobilisation would be withheld until the last moment to preserve secrecy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The navy was to neutralise the Polish fleet and blockade the ports, the Luftwaffe to destroy the Polish air force and disrupt Polish mobilisation, while a minimum force held the West Wall against any French move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A special annex of 21 April settled that there would be no declaration of war at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler had told his commanders in May that the quarrel over Danzig was not the reason for the attack, and that there was no question of sparing Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Directive No. 1 for the Conduct of the War, signed on 31 August, fixed the date at 1 September and the time at 4:45 AM; the United States consulate in Danzig was informed that day that a state of war had existed since exactly that hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Roughly sixty divisions and 1.5 million men went in from East Prussia, Silesia and Slovakia, with more than 2,000 tanks and some 1,300 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The plan worked faster than anyone had expected: the Polish field armies were broken in about 3 weeks, Warsaw capitulated after 27 days, and the last organised resistance ended on 5 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The victory cost the German army 8,082 dead, 27,278 wounded and 5,029 missing, and it left Hitler convinced that his own judgement was better than his generals'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggression Against Poland,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judpolan.asp. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, \"Directive No. 1 for the Conduct of the War,\" 31 August 1939, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/wardir1.asp. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 39-42, 120-21, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]","War Department, \"The German Campaign in Poland, September 1 to October 5, 1939,\" <i>Digests and Lessons of Recent Military Operations</i> (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1942), 1, 30, https://archive.org/details/TheGermanCampaignInPoland. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Danzig (Kuykendall) to the Secretary of State, September 1, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d403. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the plan forbid a declaration of war?","a":"Surprise was the whole of it. The directive required that the Polish armed forces be destroyed by surprise attack and that mobilisation be withheld until the last moment, so a declaration would have given away both the timing and the intention."},{"q":"Why did the plan leave only a minimum force in the west?","a":"Everything was staked on speed. The Wehrmacht's panzer, motorised and light divisions went east, leaving infantry on the West Wall, on the calculation that Poland could be beaten before France and Britain could act."},{"q":"Why does the campaign's speed matter beyond Poland?","a":"It changed Hitler. The victory came faster than his generals had expected, and it left him convinced that his own strategic judgement was better than theirs, which shaped every argument over the war's direction afterwards."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Map.5.jpg","title":"Deployment of the Wehrmacht, 1 September 1939","desc":"A US Army map of the German concentration against Poland, with the forces left facing France marked on the western frontier.","credit":"US Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AMap.5.jpg","alt":"A printed map of central Europe with red loops marking German army groups massed along the Polish frontier and a smaller group facing France, and a red box below giving the German high command's organisation."},"wiki":"Fall Weiss","places":["pl:q517081"]},
    "ww2-142": {"questions":["A condemned prisoner was killed and left in Polish uniform at the station door, the grimmest detail of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a participant later described it under oath.","Hitler had promised his commanders a propagandist cause for starting the war, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 31 August 1939 was the one he used."],"abstract":"The <b>Gleiwitz incident</b> was a faked Polish attack on a German radio station at Gleiwitz in Upper Silesia on the evening of 31 August 1939, staged by the SS so that Germany could present its invasion of Poland as retaliation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It belonged to a wider set of frontier provocations known inside the German services as Undertaking Himmler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the middle of August the Abwehr, the military intelligence service, was asked to supply Polish uniforms, identity cards and equipment for an operation of that name, a demand its officers found both mysterious and suspicious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler had already told his commanders on 22 August that he would give a propagandist cause for starting the war, never mind whether it was plausible, because the victor would not be asked afterwards whether he had told the truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The incident was therefore not an improvisation but the last step of a plan 3 weeks old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alfred Naujocks, an officer of the SD, swore at Nuremberg that Heydrich had ordered him on or about 10 August to simulate an attack on the station and make it appear that the attackers were Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He waited at Gleiwitz a fortnight; at noon on 31 August the code word came, and that evening his party seized the station, broadcast a short speech in Polish over an emergency transmitter and fired some pistol shots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A man supplied by the Gestapo, referred to by the code name Canned Goods, was drugged, shot and laid at the entrance so that a body in civilian clothes would be found there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other provocations were prepared along the frontier on the same pattern, with condemned criminals to be dressed in Polish uniforms and left dead at the scene for the press to photograph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first Wehrmacht communiqué duly spoke of attacks by Polish units on German territory, and Hitler's proclamation that morning declared that force would be met with force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggression Against Poland,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judpolan.asp. [Open access]","\"Testimony of Erwin Lahousen, 30 November 1945,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals Before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/11-30-45.asp. [Open access]","\"Affidavit of Alfred Helmut Naujocks, 20 November 1945 (Document 2751-PS),\" read into the record 20 December 1945, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals Before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/12-20-45.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, September 1, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d402. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why stage an attack at all, if the invasion was already decided?","a":"The pretext was for audiences rather than for the decision. Heydrich said proof of Polish attacks was needed for the foreign press as well as for German propaganda, so the story had to look like evidence, not like an announcement."},{"q":"Why was a prisoner's body part of the plan?","a":"A raid with no casualties would have looked like theatre. A dead man in civilian clothes at the station door supplied the physical evidence a police report and press photographs could be built on."},{"q":"Why did the Abwehr officers find the uniform request suspicious?","a":"Because Himmler's name was attached to it. Polish uniforms had no ordinary intelligence use, and an operation carrying that name told them the SS was preparing something they were not to be told about."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Gliwice%2C_radiostacja_-_Sender_Gleiwitz_01.jpg","title":"The radio tower at Gliwice","desc":"The wooden transmitter mast of the station the SS attacked on 31 August 1939, still standing outside the town.","credit":"Kamil Czaiński, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AGliwice%2C_radiostacja_-_Sender_Gleiwitz_01.jpg","alt":"A tall latticed wooden radio mast on a green lawn under a blue sky, with a low brick building at its foot."},"wiki":"Gleiwitz incident","places":["pl:q105084"]},
    "ww2-143": {"questions":["Industry, coal and the best farmland lay in the west, so the Polish high command chose a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covering everything rather than a shorter line in the interior.","American observers judged Poland's defeat hastened by a decision to spread its divisions in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> far in advance of the country's traditional river positions."],"abstract":"A <b>cordon defence</b> spreads an army evenly along a frontier so as to cover every part of it, rather than concentrating in depth behind a shorter line and keeping strong reserves to counter-attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poland adopted one in 1939, and the army that held it was broken in about 18 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The peacetime army was authorised thirty infantry divisions, several mountain brigades, fourteen horse cavalry brigades, one mechanised brigade and two air divisions, within a personnel ceiling of 280,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On mobilisation 1.5 million reservists of the classes 1898 to 1915 could be called up, with a further 560,000 older men available for security and rear-area work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Equipment was the weakness: thirteen separate tank battalions in the whole army, and little more than 300 aircraft, most of them destroyed in the first days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The deployment covered the entire border, in forces named for the districts they defended — Narew, Modlin, Pomorze, Poznań, Łódź, Kraków and the Carpathians — with a general reserve of twelve divisions about Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Economics and politics drove the choice, because the mining and industrial districts of the west could not be given up without a fight and the effect of surrendering them untouched was thought unbearable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geography made it worse, since East Prussia to the north and Slovakia to the south left Poland a ready-made salient that invited attack on both flanks at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American study of the campaign written in 1942 set out the consequences plainly: mobilisation was not completed in time, German strength was underestimated, and a major percentage of the army was strung out far in advance of the country's traditional river positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 14 September the Polish foreign minister was telling an American diplomat that almost all Polish war industry had been destroyed, and that without an immediate Allied attack this might be the end of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 52-58, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","War Department, \"The German Campaign in Poland, September 1 to October 5, 1939,\" <i>Digests and Lessons of Recent Military Operations</i> (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1942), 31, https://archive.org/details/TheGermanCampaignInPoland. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Rumania (Gunther) to the Secretary of State, September 16, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d436. [Open access]","Robert Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939-1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1-18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Poland not simply defend behind its rivers?","a":"Because the ground in front of them was the country's wealth. The mining and industrial districts and the best farmland lay west of the Vistula, and a plan that gave them up without a fight was thought politically and economically impossible."},{"q":"Why does a cordon fail against a mechanised attacker?","a":"It is equally thin everywhere. An attacker concentrating armour on one stretch breaks through against local odds, and there is no depth behind the line and no reserve near enough to seal the gap."},{"q":"Why did Poland's shape make the deployment worse?","a":"East Prussia lay to the north and Slovakia to the south, so the country was already a salient. Covering the whole frontier meant presenting flanks on two sides to an enemy planning a double envelopment."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Warsaw_Cavalry_of_Poland_1939.jpg","title":"Polish cavalry in Warsaw, August 1939","desc":"A cavalry regiment riding through the capital weeks before the invasion; the army had eleven such brigades in the field.","credit":"Benjamin Gasul, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWarsaw_Cavalry_of_Poland_1939.jpg","alt":"A colour photograph of a long line of mounted soldiers in brown uniforms riding down a tree-lined city street, with a boy running along the pavement beside them."}},
    "ww2-144": {"questions":["German intelligence expected five Polish divisions near Kutno and met twelve, a miscalculation that turned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the crisis of the September campaign.","A German communiqué of 21 September claimed 170,000 prisoners from the pocket closed at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of the largest surrenders in military history."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Bzura</b>, fought west of Warsaw between 9 and 19 September 1939, was the largest engagement of the Polish campaign and the only one in which the German advance was seriously checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Polish armies falling back from Poznań and the Corridor, most of whose divisions had scarcely been engaged, found the flank of the German Eighth Army marching past them towards the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German headquarters believed the Polish forces around Kutno and along the river amounted to five infantry divisions and two cavalry brigades; they were in fact twelve divisions and the two brigades, more than double the Eighth Army's strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Polish attack went in on 9 September, and the critical days were the 11th and 12th, when Polish divisions took Sochaczew and won footholds on the right bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost the whole left wing of the Tenth Army had to be pulled off the approaches to Warsaw and thrown into the fighting, leaving only a screen before the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That was the battle's real result: German troops had reached the outskirts of Warsaw on 8 and 9 September, and the diversion bought the capital time it used to dig in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 15 September the German armies closed in from every direction at once — from the south, from the Warsaw side, and from the north bank of the Vistula, which barred any withdrawal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A bridgehead across the Bzura was established by 16 September and the Poles spent their remaining strength in attempts to break out eastwards; Kutno fell the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isolated units began surrendering on the 17th and the last gave up their arms on the 19th, a German communiqué claiming 170,000 prisoners 2 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> While the pocket was being crushed Poland's foreign minister warned an American diplomat that without an immediate Allied attack this might be the end of Poland, and the field armies were broken within about 18 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 101-6, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","War Department, \"The German Campaign in Poland, September 1 to October 5, 1939,\" <i>Digests and Lessons of Recent Military Operations</i> (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1942), 22-29, https://archive.org/details/TheGermanCampaignInPoland. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Rumania (Gunther) to the Secretary of State, September 16, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d436. [Open access]","Robert Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939-1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1-18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Poznań divisions still fresh in the second week?","a":"The German attack had gone around them. The main thrusts came from East Prussia, Silesia and Slovakia against the flanks, so the armies in the western salient were bypassed and fell back with their strength intact."},{"q":"Why did the German intelligence error matter so much?","a":"The Eighth Army was marching past a force it thought half its own size and was in fact twice it. Its flank was exposed to an attack nobody had planned for, and the whole army group had to be redirected to contain it."},{"q":"Why is the battle counted a Polish defeat despite its success?","a":"The attack could disrupt the German plan but not escape it. The Poles had no reserves to exploit the gain, and the same concentration that made the blow possible put twelve divisions inside a pocket the Germans then closed."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Bitwa_nad_Bzur%C4%85_%282-149%29.jpg","title":"Wrecked Polish equipment on the bank of the Bzura","desc":"Guns, wagons and dead horses at the river after the pocket west of Warsaw was closed.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABitwa_nad_Bzur%C4%85_%282-149%29.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a wide flat riverbank strewn with overturned wagons, field guns and dead horses, with a line of trees behind."},"wiki":"Battle of the Bzura","places":["pl:q250485"]},
    "ww2-145": {"questions":["German commanders were instructed not to let civilians leave, because hunger inside the city would hasten the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that began in mid-September 1939.","About a fifth of the city's houses were destroyed and three fifths damaged during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended a week before German troops formally entered."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Warsaw</b> ran from the second week of September 1939 until the Polish garrison capitulated at the end of the month, and it was the last act of the campaign in central Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German armour reached the outskirts on 8 and 9 September and was thrown back, a repulse that taught the German army to pass around cities and leave their reduction to the infantry coming up behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Leaflets were dropped proposing negotiations for the evacuation of civilians and the diplomatic colony, and a truce was briefly arranged in the Praga sector on 16 September, but the Polish commander refused to receive the German emissary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bombardment resumed in earnest on 18 September and the Luftwaffe attacked the waterworks and other utilities from the air on the 20th, 3 weeks after Roosevelt had appealed to every government about to fight not to bomb civilian populations or unfortified cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prisoners reported the garrison to be largely regular troops, with ammunition in ample supply and morale good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler watched the shelling of Praga from a church tower on 22 September and approved an assault on the half of the city west of the Vistula, which would also drive the population eastwards and relieve the Germans of feeding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack opened on 26 September behind a heavy air and artillery bombardment, and the German commander had been instructed not to let civilians out beforehand, because the worsening food situation would hasten the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fighting stopped at 2:00 PM on 27 September, and the Polish forces in the city formally surrendered the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Drinking water had been cut off for 5 days, more than 16,000 of the garrison were wounded, the public utilities were wrecked and the city was close to starvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American study of the campaign reckoned that a fifth of all houses had been destroyed and about three fifths hit at least once; German troops did not formally occupy the city until 5 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 109-13, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","War Department, \"The German Campaign in Poland, September 1 to October 5, 1939,\" <i>Digests and Lessons of Recent Military Operations</i> (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1942), 22, 30, https://archive.org/details/TheGermanCampaignInPoland. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"An Appeal to Great Britain, France, Italy, Germany, and Poland to Refrain from Air Bombing of Civilians,\" 1 September 1939, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/node/209957. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Warsaw,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were civilians kept inside the city rather than driven out?","a":"Because hunger was a weapon. The German commander was told not to let the population leave before the assault, on the reasoning that the food a large civilian population needed would bring the garrison to terms sooner."},{"q":"Why did the first German attack on Warsaw fail?","a":"Armour was sent against a defended city. Tanks in streets lose the speed and space that make them dangerous, and the lesson the Germans drew was to bypass cities and leave them to the infantry behind."},{"q":"Why did the garrison give in when it still had ammunition?","a":"The city ran out of everything else. Water had been cut for 5 days, the utilities were wrecked, over 16,000 of the defenders were wounded and food was nearly gone, so the limit was the population's endurance rather than the army's."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Warsaw_1939_Polish_POWs.jpg","title":"The Warsaw garrison marching into captivity","desc":"Polish soldiers file out of the city past damaged buildings after the capitulation at the end of September 1939.","credit":"Press agency photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWarsaw_1939_Polish_POWs.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a dense column of soldiers in greatcoats walking along a wide street, watched by civilians, with gutted buildings behind them."},"wiki":"Siege of Warsaw (1939)","places":["city:1159151299"]},
    "ww2-146": {"questions":["Berlin pressed Moscow for a date through September and rejected the pretext of protecting Ukrainians and Belorussians, which Molotov used anyway when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came.","Britain's guarantee to Poland covered only a German attack, so London declared no war on Moscow after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 17 September 1939."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet invasion of Poland</b> began at six in the morning on 17 September 1939, 16 days after the German attack and while the Polish army was still in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the secret protocol of the Nazi-Soviet pact being executed, the two governments having divided the country into spheres of interest a month before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ribbentrop had cabled Moscow as early as 3 September suggesting that the Red Army move in; Molotov answered that the time was not yet right, while committing his government to intervene when the moment suited both powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 15 September Berlin was pressing hard, asking the Soviet government to fix a day and an hour so that the two armies could coordinate and no political vacuum could arise in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same cable objected to the reason Moscow proposed to give — that the Red Army was protecting Ukrainians and Belorussians threatened by the Germans — as contrary to German intentions and certain to expose the two states to the world as enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Stalin received the German ambassador at two in the morning on 17 September and announced that the Red Army would cross at six along the whole line from Polotsk to Kamenets-Podolsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He asked that German aircraft stay west of the Białystok–Brest-Litovsk–Lwów line, and read out the note to be handed to the Polish ambassador that night, altering the passages the Germans found unacceptable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy in Moscow reported the same morning that Soviet troops had entered eastern Poland under the guise of restoring order and protecting the minorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Warsaw protested that the crossing was a flagrant violation of its treaties, but in London the Foreign Office concluded that no question arose of Britain declaring war on the Soviet Union under the Anglo-Polish pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Red Army destroyed or captured the organised Polish forces it met, and offered the Germans armed assistance where it seemed necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Reich Foreign Minister to the German Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Schulenburg), September 15, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns072.asp. [Open access]","\"The German Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Schulenburg) to the German Foreign Office, September 17, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns074.asp. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 122-27, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, September 17, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d437. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, September 17, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d440. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, September 18, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d450. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow wait 16 days?","a":"It was not ready and it wanted Poland beaten first. Preparations for a movement on that scale were incomplete, and the Soviet government refused to move until the Polish capital was on the point of falling and the outcome was no longer in doubt."},{"q":"Why did Berlin object to the protection-of-minorities pretext?","a":"It named Germany as the threat. A Soviet announcement that the Red Army was shielding Ukrainians and Belorussians from the advancing Germans would have presented the two allies to the world as enemies, which is exactly what Ribbentrop said."},{"q":"Why did Britain not declare war on the Soviet Union?","a":"The guarantee did not cover it. The Anglo-Polish agreement obliged Britain against a German attack, and the Foreign Office concluded that no question of war with Moscow arose under it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Desfile_conjunto_nazi-sovi%C3%A9tico_en_Brest-Livosk%2C_Polonia%2C_22_de_septiembre_de_1939_-_36115519274_Nazi-Soviet_parade_Brest-Livosk_Poland_1939-09-22.jpg","title":"German and Soviet troops at Brest-Litovsk, 22 September 1939","desc":"A Soviet tank passes German soldiers and their motorcycles in the town the two armies handed over between them.","credit":"Contando Estrelas, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ADesfile_conjunto_nazi-sovi%C3%A9tico_en_Brest-Livosk%2C_Polonia%2C_22_de_septiembre_de_1939_-_36115519274_Nazi-Soviet_parade_Brest-Livosk_Poland_1939-09-22.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a small tank driving along a cobbled street past a parked car and a row of soldiers standing with motorcycles under trees."},"wiki":"Soviet invasion of Poland"},
    "ww2-147": {"questions":["A secret protocol to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved Lithuania into the Soviet sphere in exchange for the Lublin province and parts of the Warsaw province.","Both governments undertook in a second secret protocol to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to suppress any Polish agitation in their territories and to tell each other how."],"abstract":"The <b>German-Soviet Frontier Treaty</b>, signed in Moscow on 28 September 1939 under the title Boundary and Friendship Treaty, fixed the partition of Poland between Germany and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its preamble declared it the two governments' exclusive task, after the collapse of the former Polish state, to re-establish peace and order in those territories and to assure the peoples living there a peaceful life in keeping with their national character.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article I set the boundary of their respective national interests along a line marked on an attached map; Article II called it definitive and rejected any interference by third powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article III divided the administration, Germany reorganising public life west of the line and the Soviet Union east of it, and Article V made the treaty effective on signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result is generally called the fourth partition of Poland, after those of the eighteenth century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Two secret supplementary protocols were signed with it, the first amending the August agreement so that Lithuania passed to the Soviet sphere while the province of Lublin and parts of the province of Warsaw passed to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second bound each party to tolerate no Polish agitation affecting the other's territory, to suppress any beginnings of it and to keep the other informed of the measures taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That clause is the treaty's clearest single sentence: the two powers undertook to police Polish national feeling for one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A declaration published the same day announced that the problems arising from the collapse of the Polish state had been definitively settled, and called on Britain and France to end the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Should that effort fail, it added, the fact would demonstrate that Britain and France were responsible for continuing the war, whereupon the two governments would consult on the necessary measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"German-Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty, September 28, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/gsbound.asp. [Open access]","\"Secret Supplementary Protocol, September 28, 1939\" [Lithuania and the Lublin province], in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/sesupp.asp. [Open access]","\"Secret Supplementary Protocol, September 28, 1939\" [Polish agitation], in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/sesupp1.asp. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 126-28, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","\"Declaration of the Government of the German Reich and the Government of the U.S.S.R. of September 28, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/dec939.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the two powers revise the line agreed in August?","a":"Because the campaign had changed what each wanted. Germany took the Polish heartland west of the new line, and in exchange the Soviet Union gained Lithuania, which the August protocol had left in the German sphere."},{"q":"Why did the treaty speak of the \"former Polish state\"?","a":"The phrasing did the political work. Calling Poland collapsed rather than conquered let both governments present the partition as filling a vacuum, and denied any Polish authority the standing to object."},{"q":"Why include a clause about Polish agitation?","a":"Neither occupier wanted the other's half to become a refuge. A Polish movement operating across the line would have threatened both, so each promised to suppress it at home and report what it had done."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/MolotovRibbentropStalin.jpg","title":"Molotov signs the treaty in Moscow, 28 September 1939","desc":"Ribbentrop and Stalin stand behind the Soviet foreign minister as he signs the boundary and friendship treaty.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AMolotovRibbentropStalin.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a seated man signing a document at a table while a group of men in suits and uniform, including a moustached figure at the right, stand watching behind him."},"wiki":"German–Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty","places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ww2-148": {"questions":["Chamberlain told the Commons that no undertaking had been received by the time stipulated, words that amounted to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the ultimatum's own terms.","Paris wanted both ultimatums presented before noon to expire that evening, but London moved first, so the two allies issued a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 6 hours apart."],"abstract":"A <b>declaration of war</b> is a state's formal announcement that it regards itself as at war, and the Hague convention of 1907 requires that hostilities begin only after previous and explicit warning, in the form either of a reasoned declaration or of an ultimatum carrying a conditional declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France had guaranteed Poland in the spring of 1939, and when Germany attacked they sent notes on 1 September demanding that all aggressive action be suspended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No reply came, and at nine on the morning of 3 September the British ambassador handed the German foreign minister a final note: unless satisfactory assurances reached London by eleven, a state of war would exist between the two countries from that hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chamberlain read the note to the Commons and added that no such undertaking had been received, and that consequently the country was at war with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He told the nation by radio the same morning that everything he had worked for in public life had crashed into ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> London and Paris had agreed that both ambassadors would present ultimatums before noon, to take effect that evening; the French believed the British moved early because of the mood of the House of Commons the night before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The French note was delivered at midday and named 5:00 PM as the hour from which France would fulfil its agreement with Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany rejected the ultimatum outright, replying that the German government and people refused to receive, accept or fulfil demands of that nature, and blaming the British cabinet for having frustrated a peaceful revision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Daladier told the French people that the responsibility for the blood being shed fell entirely on Hitler's government, which had chosen war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The forms were observed on both sides, and the war that followed them lasted almost 6 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["\"Convention (III) Relative to the Opening of Hostilities, The Hague, 18 October 1907,\" Articles 1-2, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague03.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Prime Minister's Announcement,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 3 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 291-302, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/03/prime-ministers-announcement. [Open access]","Neville Chamberlain, \"Radio Address, September 3, 1939,\" The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gb3.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, September 3, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d420. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, September 3, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d421. [Open access]","\"Communication from the German Government to the British Government, September 3, 1939,\" The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gp3.asp. [Open access]","Edouard Daladier, \"Statement of September 3, 1939,\" The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/fr3.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why an ultimatum rather than a straight declaration?","a":"The Hague convention allowed either, and an ultimatum left a way out. It named a condition Germany could still meet, so the responsibility for the war rested on the refusal rather than on the declaration."},{"q":"Why did the two allies declare war 6 hours apart?","a":"They had agreed to act together before noon, and London went early. French diplomats put it down to the anger of the House of Commons the previous night, which had left the government no room to wait."},{"q":"Why did Germany refuse the British note in those terms?","a":"Accepting would have meant withdrawing from Poland. Berlin's reply refused the form as well as the substance, rejecting demands in the nature of ultimata and casting the British cabinet as the party that had blocked peaceful revision."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/PARIS-SOIR-3-09-1939.jpg","title":"Paris-Soir announces the declarations of war","desc":"The front page of 3 September 1939 gives both hours: Britain from eleven that morning, France from five that afternoon.","credit":"Collective work, Paris-Soir, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3APARIS-SOIR-3-09-1939.jpg","alt":"The front page of a French newspaper with the banner headline LA GUERRE EST DECLAREE above two lines naming the hours at which Britain and France entered the war."},"wiki":"Declaration of war","places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-149": {"questions":["Chamberlain refused to bomb targets in Germany for fear of retaliation, one reason the winter of 1939 became known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a campaign.","Hitler's first directive told his troops in the west that responsibility for opening hostilities must rest on the Allies, which helped produce the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of that autumn and winter."],"abstract":"The <b>Phoney War</b> is the English name for the months between the Allied declarations of war on 3 September 1939 and the German offensive in the west the following spring, when the western front stayed almost silent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was in large part a policy rather than an accident, and on the German side an explicit one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Directive No. 1 for the Conduct of the War, signed on 31 August 1939, laid down that in the west the responsibility for opening hostilities should rest squarely on England and France, that insignificant frontier violations were to be met by purely local action, and that the German frontier was not to be crossed without Hitler's express permission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German forces there were to conserve their strength so that the operations against Poland could be finished, and orders to go over to the attack were reserved to Hitler himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German command knew the risk it ran in throwing almost everything eastwards, and began moving divisions and headquarters back to the West Wall before the fighting in Poland was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On the Allied side the caution was argued out at the top: Daladier proposed on 12 September that British aircraft bomb military objectives in Germany, including the Rhine bridges, and Chamberlain flatly refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He did not wish to provoke attacks on British aircraft factories then turning out machines at speed, and he was worried about the effect of bombing on opinion in the United States; he also thought Poland lost in any case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> French troops did attack in the Saar, and Daladier told the American ambassador that he was satisfied in the highest degree by their success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The view from Poland was different, its officials reporting that Britain and France seemed to be staging more of a demonstration than a serious attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the time the Polish campaign ended the moment had passed, and the Allies faced a fait accompli on a western frontier the Germans had reinforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Elisabeth du Reau, \"The Foreign Policy of France and the Franco-Soviet Relations in the First Months of the 'Phoney War' (September 1939 - March 1940),\" <i>MGIMO Review of International Relations</i>, no. 4 (2009): 204-10, https://www.vestnik.mgimo.ru/jour/article/view/2736. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, \"Directive No. 1 for the Conduct of the War,\" 31 August 1939, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/wardir1.asp. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 130-31, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, September 13, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d434. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Rumania (Gunther) to the Secretary of State, September 16, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d436. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany hold back in the west at all?","a":"Almost everything was in Poland. The directive told the western forces to conserve their strength until the eastern operation was finished, and to leave the blame for opening hostilities to the Allies."},{"q":"Why did Chamberlain refuse to bomb German targets?","a":"He feared what would come back. Bombing would invite attacks on the aircraft factories then building Britain's fighter strength, and he was anxious about how it would look to opinion in the United States."},{"q":"Why did the quiet matter once Poland had fallen?","a":"The chance did not return. Germany moved divisions back to the West Wall while the Polish campaign was still running, so an Allied offensive that might have told in September faced a reinforced frontier by October."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/8inchHowitzerCamouflagedLaquielle20October1939.jpg","title":"A British howitzer camouflaged in France, October 1939","desc":"An eight-inch gun hidden with straw beside a farm building during the months when the western front stayed quiet.","credit":"Keating G (Lt), War Office official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A8inchHowitzerCamouflagedLaquielle20October1939.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a large artillery piece draped in loose straw beside a thatched farm building, with two soldiers standing by it."},"wiki":"Phoney War","places":["city:1159140613"]},
    "ww2-150": {"questions":["Conquered Poland was split in two: the western strip taken directly into the Reich as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the remainder ruled as a colony.","Military government under the army lasted barely 8 weeks before Hitler handed the occupation to civilians, keeping the annexed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Reich's own ministries."],"abstract":"A <b>Reichsgau</b> was an administrative district of Nazi Germany under the Reich Ministry of the Interior, and the form in which the western provinces of conquered Poland were taken directly into Germany in the autumn of 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The frontier treaty signed in Moscow on 28 September had made the reorganisation of public administration west of the agreed line a German responsibility, and Berlin chose to divide what it held into two quite different things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A secret protocol signed the same day bound each power to suppress any Polish agitation affecting the other's territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three military government commands had followed the armies in, with headquarters at Danzig, Poznań and Kraków; on 25 September Hitler ordered a fuller organisation under Rundstedt, who held both the troops and the administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> West Prussia and the Poznań area were annexed on 8 October, effective from 1 November, together with a strip of Silesia that Poland had gained after the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two military districts were formed in the annexed areas and transferred to the Replacement Army, the measure of how completely the ground was now treated as German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The remainder of German-occupied Poland was placed under a civilian governor general by a decree of 12 October, made public on the 19th and effective from 26 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hans Frank, a party lawyer, was appointed to it, and the choice was deliberate: Hitler was convinced that the army would not carry through the destruction of the Polish nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brauchitsch brought the army's protest to Hitler on 17 October and was told it would be relieved of administering the occupied territories altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The annexed lands were West Prussia, Poznań, Upper Silesia and the former Free City of Danzig, leaving Warsaw, Kraków, Radom and Lublin outside the Reich; what civilian rule meant showed at once in Frank's decree of 23 November, requiring every Jew in his jurisdiction to wear a white armband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 126-28, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of Poland, Fall 1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-poland-fall-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Warsaw,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","\"German-Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty, September 28, 1939,\" Article III, in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/gsbound.asp. [Open access]","\"Secret Supplementary Protocol, September 28, 1939\" [Polish agitation], in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/sesupp1.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why annex part of Poland outright instead of occupying all of it?","a":"The western provinces were claimed as German ground. Making them Reichsgaue put them under the Reich's own ministries and its military districts, which is a claim of permanence rather than of conquest."},{"q":"Why did Hitler take the occupation away from the army?","a":"He did not trust it to do what he intended. He believed the army would not carry through the destruction of the Polish nation, so he handed the General Government to a party lawyer answerable to himself."},{"q":"Why did the division into two zones matter to Poles?","a":"It decided which set of rules they lived under. In the annexed districts they were a population to be removed or Germanised; in the General Government they were a labour force under a separate administration with no pretence of rights."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Expulsion_of_Poles_by_Nazi-German_occupants_%28village_near_Ciechocinek%29.jpg","title":"Poles assembled for expulsion from a village near Ciechocinek","desc":"Villagers gathered outside their houses in the annexed territory, waiting to be moved out to make room for German settlers.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AExpulsion_of_Poles_by_Nazi-German_occupants_%28village_near_Ciechocinek%29.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of a large crowd of villagers with bundles standing in a field beside a wooden cottage and trees."},"wiki":"Reichsgau","places":["city:1159139065"]},
    "ww2-151": {"questions":["Hans Frank governed from the Wawel castle at Kraków, and the four districts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held about a third of pre-war Poland's people.","Nuremberg heard that the occupation policy of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on destroying Poland as a nation and stripping it for the German war effort."],"abstract":"The <b>General Government</b> was the German civil administration imposed on the part of occupied Poland that was not annexed outright to the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the campaign ended the Germans annexed the western provinces as Reichsgaue, and Hitler directed on 12 October 1939 that the remainder be governed by civilians rather than by the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took that step because he was convinced the army would not carry through the destruction of the Polish nation he intended, and the decree was published on 19 October to take effect a week later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governor was Hans Frank, a party lawyer who had served as chief civil administrator behind the front and who answered to Hitler alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ruled from the Wawel, the castle of the Polish kings at Kraków, over four districts seated at Kraków, Warsaw, Radom and Lublin; a fifth, Galicia, was added after the invasion of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Polish government in exile reckoned the territory at about 95,000 square kilometres (37,000 square miles) with some 11.5 million inhabitants, roughly a third of pre-war Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its first official name spoke of the occupied Polish territories, but in August 1940 that phrase was dropped and only the General Government remained, which Polish observers read as a quiet annexation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Frank set out his purpose in October 1939 in words the Nuremberg tribunal later quoted: Poland was to be treated like a colony and the Poles made the slaves of a greater German empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had been directed to reduce the Polish economy to the minimum needed for bare existence, and the tribunal found his rule a reign of terror backed by summary police courts and mass shootings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Treblinka and Majdanek were built in this territory, which had held between two and a half and three and a half million Jews; in January 1944 Frank put the number still alive at 103,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 127-28, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: Frank,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judfrank.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes Against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Polish Ministry of Information, <i>The Black Book of Poland</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1942), 12-13, 162, https://archive.org/details/TheBlackBookOfPoland. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Administration of Poland,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-administration-of-poland. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler take occupied Poland out of the army's hands?","a":"He was convinced the army would not carry through the destruction of the Polish nation he intended, so on 12 October 1939 he ordered a civil administration under a governor answering to him alone."},{"q":"Why does the change of name in August 1940 matter?","a":"The first title spoke of the occupied Polish territories, which acknowledged an occupation; dropping that phrase left only the General Government, and Polish observers read the change as annexation by stealth."},{"q":"Why was the General Government not simply annexed like the western provinces?","a":"It was meant to hold the Polish and Jewish population cleared out of the annexed Reichsgaue and to serve as a reservoir of labour, so it was governed as a dependency rather than incorporated."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Occupation_of_Poland_1939_%28b%26w%29.png/1920px-Occupation_of_Poland_1939_%28b%26w%29.png","title":"Occupied Poland, 1939 to 1941","desc":"The partition of Poland after the campaign of 1939: the western provinces annexed to the Reich, the General Government in the centre and south, and the Soviet zone to the east.","credit":"Map by Lonio17, black and white version by Poeticbent, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Occupation_of_Poland_1939_(b%26w).png","alt":"A black and white map of Poland showing the territory annexed by Germany, the area labelled General Government, and the Soviet zone beyond it."},"wiki":"General Government","places":["city:1159149709"]},
    "ww2-152": {"questions":["The Gestapo told the scholars gathered in the university hall that Kraków had been a bastion of Polonism, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took 174 of them away.","Seventeen of the men taken in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died, and the survivors came home to a country where Polish higher education had been closed down altogether."],"abstract":"<b>Sonderaktion Krakau</b> was the German operation of 6 November 1939 that arrested the teaching staff of the Jagiellonian University and the mining school in Kraków.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that the Polish educated classes had been marked down for extermination as early as September 1939, so the operation was an early instance of a settled policy rather than a local excess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> According to the wartime dossier compiled by the Polish government in exile, the professors were invited by the occupation authorities to a lecture on the attitude of the German authorities to science and teaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When they had gathered in the university hall the head of the Gestapo in Kraków told them that they were all under arrest, giving as his reasons that they meant to resume lectures, that they had gone on working in their institutes, and that the university had been a bastion of Polonism for 500 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of 174 professors and assistants taken, seven who were very old or seriously ill were released and the rest were held in Kraków, then moved to Breslau with five students who had been in the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After about 3 weeks the prisoners were sent on to the concentration camp at Sachsenhausen, north of Berlin, where Polish clergy and other Poles were already held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same account describes unheated huts through the hard winter of 1939 to 1940, roll-calls in thin clothing, systematic beatings and the withholding of medical care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seventeen of the men died, fourteen in the camp and three after returning to Kraków.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who came back found Polish higher and secondary education closed down across the General Government, and some of them, the chemist Tadeusz Estreicher among them, went on to teach in the clandestine university that Poles maintained through the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the dossier was published by a belligerent government in 1942, its figures are best read beside the tribunal's later findings, which they match in substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polish Ministry of Information, <i>The Black Book of Poland</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1942), 83-84, 446-52, https://archive.org/details/TheBlackBookOfPoland. [Open access]","Alicja Rafalska-Łasocha, \"Professor Tadeusz Estreicher (1871-1952), chemist, cryogenic scientist, historian of science, art lover, humanist, translator and patriot,\" <i>Studia Historiae Scientiarum</i> 20 (2021): 167-90, https://ojs.ejournals.eu/SHS/article/view/7532. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes Against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Administration of Poland,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-administration-of-poland. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: Frank,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judfrank.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the professors invited to a lecture rather than simply arrested?","a":"The invitation brought the whole teaching body into one hall at one hour, so a single operation could take nearly all of them at once; the announced subject, German policy towards science and teaching, gave them a reason to attend."},{"q":"Why did the Gestapo give the university's age as a reason for the arrests?","a":"The stated grounds were that Kraków had been a bastion of Polonism for 500 years, which makes the target the institution's role in Polish national life rather than anything its staff had done."},{"q":"Why should the Black Book's account be read with care?","a":"It was published in 1942 by the Polish government in exile, a belligerent making a case against an enemy, so its figures are best checked against the Nuremberg findings, which agree with it in substance."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Krakow%2C_Uniwersytet_Jagiellonski%2C_Collegium_Novum._ca_1900_%2871416528%29.jpg/1920px-Krakow%2C_Uniwersytet_Jagiellonski%2C_Collegium_Novum._ca_1900_%2871416528%29.jpg","title":"Collegium Novum, Kraków","desc":"The main building of the Jagiellonian University, photographed around 1900. Its hall was where the university's teaching staff were called to a lecture on 6 November 1939 and arrested.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krakow,_Uniwersytet_Jagiellonski,_Collegium_Novum._ca_1900_(71416528).jpg","alt":"A large neo-Gothic university building of pale stone with arched windows and a broad forecourt, in a photograph from about 1900."},"wiki":"Sonderaktion Krakau","places":["city:1159149709"]},
    "ww2-153": {"questions":["Thousands of Poles thought likely to resist German rule were shot under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them the political and cultural figures buried in the graves at Palmiry.","Carried out by the Security Police and the SD with little pretence of legal procedure, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1940 aimed at what Frank called the leading representatives of the Polish intelligentsia."],"abstract":"The <b>AB-Aktion</b> was the mass killing of Polish political, professional and cultural leaders carried out in the General Government from the spring of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is an abbreviation of the German for ‘extraordinary pacification operation’, and the Nuremberg tribunal called it a brutal action run by the Security Police and the SD with only a partial pretence of legal procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The killing of Polish elites was not new in 1940: the tribunal found that the intelligentsia had been marked down for extermination as early as September 1939, and public executions in town squares were a feature of the first weeks of occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What made the spring of 1940 different was the cover the campaign in the West provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a police conference on 30 May 1940 Hans Frank said he was taking advantage of the offensive that had diverted the attention of the world from Poland in order to liquidate thousands of Poles likely to resist German domination, first of all the leading representatives of the Polish intelligentsia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The victims were teachers, priests, lawyers, officers, landowners, local officials and political activists, taken from prisons and from lists drawn up in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many were shot in woods outside the towns rather than in public; the graves at Palmiry, north of Warsaw, hold several thousand people, among them prominent figures of Polish political and cultural life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The wider machinery of terror ran alongside it, with summary police courts ordering the shooting of groups of twenty to two hundred and hostages killed in reprisal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Frank's own cynicism about the scale is recorded in the judgment, where he is quoted joking that Poland had not forest enough to make the paper for posters announcing every seven Poles shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reliable totals are hard to fix, because much of the killing was unrecorded and the surviving evidence is the occupier's own paperwork and the reports smuggled out by the Polish underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: Frank,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judfrank.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes Against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Polish Victims,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","Polish Ministry of Information, <i>The Black Book of Poland</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1942), 7, 33, https://archive.org/details/TheBlackBookOfPoland. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Administration of Poland,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-administration-of-poland. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the killing of Polish leaders intensify in May 1940 rather than earlier?","a":"Frank said at a police conference on 30 May 1940 that the German offensive in the West had turned the world's attention away from Poland, and that this was the moment to liquidate thousands of Poles likely to resist German rule."},{"q":"Why were teachers, priests and lawyers picked out?","a":"The aim was to destroy Poland as a nation rather than to punish particular acts, so the targets were the people who carried its institutional and cultural life and around whom resistance might form."},{"q":"Why is the AB-Aktion's death toll uncertain?","a":"Much of the killing was done away from witnesses and left no record, so the evidence is the occupier's own paperwork, the graves found afterwards and the reports smuggled out by the Polish underground."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Policjanci_niemieccy_prowadz%C4%85_Polak%C3%B3w_na_rozstrzelanie_w_Palmirach_%2821-206-2%29.jpg/1920px-Policjanci_niemieccy_prowadz%C4%85_Polak%C3%B3w_na_rozstrzelanie_w_Palmirach_%2821-206-2%29.jpg","title":"Poles led to execution at Palmiry","desc":"German police lead a group of Polish women through the forest near Palmiry to a mass grave, 1940. The woman in the foreground was afterwards identified as Janina Skalska.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Policjanci_niemieccy_prowadz%C4%85_Polak%C3%B3w_na_rozstrzelanie_w_Palmirach_(21-206-2).jpg","alt":"A group of women in coats walking away from the camera through a pine forest, with uniformed men behind them."},"wiki":"Aktion AB","places":["pl:q3117935"]},
    "ww2-154": {"questions":["Moscow declared in November 1939 that the 13.5 million Polish citizens in its zone were now Soviet citizens, one of the first steps in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the region.","Political, judicial, economic and cultural life was remade on the Soviet model, a programme of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the NKVD enforced with arrests and deportations."],"abstract":"<b>Sovietisation</b> is the remaking of a society's institutions on the Soviet model, and it is the name historians give to what was done to eastern Poland between 1939 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Red Army crossed the frontier at dawn on 17 September 1939, the American embassy in Moscow reporting that it came under the guise of restoring order and protecting the Ukrainian and White Russian minorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moscow's further argument was that the Polish state had collapsed and could no longer guarantee its citizens' security, a pretext the British Foreign Office expected to take exception to while declining to treat the invasion as a cause of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Polish army offered no organised resistance to it, and around 250,000 soldiers, border guards, police officers, prison guards and officials were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The USSR annexed the territory and in November 1939 declared that the 13.5 million Polish citizens living there were henceforth Soviet citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The programme that followed was designed, in the words of one study of it, to destroy the political, judicial, social, economic, national and cultural foundations of the annexed lands and to impose Soviet order in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deportation was part of that design rather than a by-product of it, prepared by decisions worked out in the Politburo and executed by the NKVD.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same study notes that the Soviet authorities moved faster and harder here than in the Baltic states annexed the following summer, with mass shootings of officers, policemen and prisoners that had no Baltic counterpart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Soviet newspapers defended the seizure on the ground that most of the population was Ukrainian and Belorussian and that holding the region was of great defensive value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The arrangement lasted until June 1941, when the German invasion of the Soviet Union carried the whole of Poland under German rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["European Court of Human Rights, <i>Janowiec and Others v. Russia</i>, applications nos. 55508/07 and 29520/09, Grand Chamber judgment of 21 October 2013, paras. 14-16, https://hudoc.echr.coe.int/eng?i=001-127684. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, September 17, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d437. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, September 18, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d450. [Open access]","Nataliia Lebedeva, \"Deportations from Poland and the Baltic States to the USSR in 1939-1941: Common Features and Specific Traits,\" <i>Lithuanian Historical Studies</i> 7, no. 1 (2002): 95-112, https://doi.org/10.30965/25386565-00701005. [Paywalled]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 4, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d255. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"German Administration of Poland,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-administration-of-poland. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain not declare war on the Soviet Union in September 1939?","a":"The Anglo-Polish pact was not read as bringing it about automatically, the Poles did not ask for it, and London confined itself to a formal protest against the invasion and the pretext given for it."},{"q":"Why did Moscow claim to be protecting Ukrainians and Belorussians?","a":"It gave the invasion a defensive and ethnic justification instead of a territorial one, and it fitted the later argument that most of the population of the region was Ukrainian and Belorussian rather than Polish."},{"q":"Why were the deportations part of Sovietisation rather than a separate policy?","a":"They were planned in the Politburo alongside the other measures and aimed at the same end, removing the people around whom the old political, social and cultural order was built so that Soviet institutions could replace it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Wyzwolony_Bia%C5%82ystok_newspaper_3_11_1939.jpg/1920px-Wyzwolony_Bia%C5%82ystok_newspaper_3_11_1939.jpg","title":"Wyzwolony Białystok, 3 November 1939","desc":"The front page of a Soviet-sponsored newspaper in occupied Białystok, reporting the resolutions of the People's Assembly and the session of the Supreme Soviet that took the territory into the USSR.","credit":"Published by the Temporary Government of the Białystok Region, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wyzwolony_Bia%C5%82ystok_newspaper_3_11_1939.jpg","alt":"The front page of a Polish-language newspaper dated 3 November 1939, headed Wyzwolony Bialystok, with columns of dense type."},"wiki":"Sovietization"},
    "ww2-155": {"questions":["Germany announced the discovery of mass graves in a forest near Smolensk in 1943, and Moscow blamed the Germans for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for nearly 50 years.","A KGB note of 1959 proposing to destroy the files put the number shot in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the related executions at 21,857."],"abstract":"The <b>Katyn massacre</b> was the shooting of Polish prisoners of war and civilian officials by the Soviet security police in the spring of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lavrentiy Beria proposed it to Stalin in early March, arguing that the prisoners were enemies of Soviet power beyond reform, and counted 14,736 officers and policemen in the camps and 10,685 more Poles in the prisons of the western districts of Ukraine and Belorussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 5 March 1940 the Politburo ordered that their cases be settled by a special procedure ending in shooting, without the prisoners being summoned or the charges disclosed; the decision carries the signatures of Stalin, Voroshilov, Mikoyan, Molotov, Kalinin and Kaganovich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The killings were done in April and May 1940 at several places, the men from Kozelsk in the Katyn Forest near Smolensk, those from Starobelsk in the Kharkov prison and the policemen from Ostashkov in the Kalinin prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A KGB note of 1959 recommending that the files be destroyed put the total shot at 21,857, of whom 4,421 died at Katyn itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> German forces found the Katyn graves in 1943 and an international commission of forensic experts concluded that the Soviet authorities were responsible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moscow answered with a commission of its own, which announced in January 1944 that the Germans had done it in the autumn of 1941, and the Soviet prosecutors at Nuremberg put that charge into the indictment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over 2 days in July 1946 the tribunal heard three witnesses for each side and then said nothing about Katyn in its judgment, and a United States congressional committee took evidence pointing the other way in 1951 and 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet Union admitted responsibility only in April 1990, when Gorbachev handed the documents to the Polish president and the official news agency named Beria and his subordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Russian State Duma went further in November 2010, calling the massacre an arbitrary act of the totalitarian state carried out on Stalin's direct orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["European Court of Human Rights, <i>Janowiec and Others v. Russia</i>, applications nos. 55508/07 and 29520/09, Grand Chamber judgment of 21 October 2013, paras. 15-24, 38, 73, https://hudoc.echr.coe.int/eng?i=001-127684. [Open access]","\"Indictment: Count Three (War Crimes),\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals Before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/count3.asp. [Open access]","United States House of Representatives, <i>The Katyn Forest Massacre: Hearings Before the Select Committee to Conduct an Investigation of the Facts, Evidence and Circumstances of the Katyn Forest Massacre</i>, 82nd Congress, part 7 (Washington, D.C.: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1952), 2254, https://archive.org/details/katynforestmassa07unit. [Open access]","\"Proceedings of 1 July 1946\" [the Katyn evidence], <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals Before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-01-46.asp. [Open access]","\"Proceedings of 2 July 1946\" [the Katyn evidence, continued], <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals Before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-02-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet leadership order the prisoners shot rather than held?","a":"Beria's proposal described them as enemies of Soviet power who would go on working against it, and they were the officer corps, police and officials of the Polish state whose institutions the annexation was meant to destroy."},{"q":"Why did the case at Nuremberg not settle who was responsible?","a":"The Soviet prosecution put its own version into the indictment and objected to oral evidence; the tribunal heard witnesses for both sides and then made no finding about Katyn at all in its judgment."},{"q":"Why did the truth take until 1990 to be admitted?","a":"The Soviet Union had built its case on a commission of its own making and staked the charge at Nuremberg on it, and a KGB proposal of 1959 shows the files were kept sealed precisely so that the official version could not be contradicted."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/The_Katyn_Massacre%2C_1940_HU106203.jpg","title":"The international commission at Katyn, 1943","desc":"Members of the forensic commission brought to the Katyn graves in April 1943 examining personal effects taken from the bodies of the murdered officers.","credit":"German official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Katyn_Massacre,_1940_HU106203.jpg","alt":"Several men in overcoats and hats leaning over a long table spread with papers and small objects, in a bare room with tall windows."},"wiki":"Katyn massacre","places":["pl:q4217543"]},
    "ww2-156": {"questions":["Beria headed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which deported Polish families to the far north, to Siberia and to Central Asia, each operation approved by the Politburo in advance.","An amnesty in August 1941 was supposed to free every Polish citizen the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held, and the Polish embassy in Moscow found most were still in labour camps."],"abstract":"The <b>NKVD</b>, the People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs, was the Soviet Union's security police and the instrument of its rule in the lands taken from Poland in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It held the officers, border guards, policemen and officials rounded up after the invasion in special camps at Kozelsk, Starobelsk and Ostashkov, and it carried out the shootings the Politburo ordered in March 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alongside the killings it ran the deportations, which one study describes as prepared by decisions carefully worked out in the Politburo and as an integral part of the programme of imposing Soviet order on the annexed territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three waves went east from the former Polish provinces during 1940, and a fourth followed in May and June 1941, this time taking in Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Bessarabia and northern Bukovina as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole families were moved at a few hours' notice to the far north, to Siberia and to Central Asia, and put to compulsory labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The German invasion of the Soviet Union in June 1941 changed Moscow's view of the Poles overnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An agreement signed in London on 30 July 1941 restored diplomatic relations, declared the German-Soviet treaties on Polish territory void and allowed a Polish army to be raised on Soviet soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A decree of August 1941 then amnestied Polish citizens held as prisoners of war or on other grounds, and Moscow maintained afterwards that it had been applied in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Polish embassy reported otherwise: most deportees were still in compulsory labour camps, free rail travel was refused to all but those released from prison, and about 100,000 people were being directed to Uzbekistan for heavy work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How many Poles were deported in all is still argued over, because the Soviet records were closed for 50 years and the Polish figures were gathered in exile from the deportees themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["European Court of Human Rights, <i>Janowiec and Others v. Russia</i>, applications nos. 55508/07 and 29520/09, Grand Chamber judgment of 21 October 2013, paras. 16-19, https://hudoc.echr.coe.int/eng?i=001-127684. [Open access]","Nataliia Lebedeva, \"Deportations from Poland and the Baltic States to the USSR in 1939-1941: Common Features and Specific Traits,\" <i>Lithuanian Historical Studies</i> 7, no. 1 (2002): 95-112, https://doi.org/10.30965/25386565-00701005. [Paywalled]","A. Gurjanovas, \"Exiles to the far Regions of the USSR in May-June, 1941,\" <i>Genocidas ir Rezistencija</i> 2, no. 2 (1997): 56-65, https://www.journals.vu.lt/gr/en/article/view/40514. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum by the Polish Ambassador (Ciechanowski),\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d275. [Open access]","United States House of Representatives, <i>The Katyn Forest Massacre: Hearings Before the Select Committee to Conduct an Investigation of the Facts, Evidence and Circumstances of the Katyn Forest Massacre</i>, 82nd Congress, part 7 (Washington, D.C.: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1952), 2254, https://archive.org/details/katynforestmassa07unit. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant) to the Secretary of State, July 30, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d253. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the deportations planned in the Politburo rather than left to the police?","a":"They were a political programme, not a security response: each operation was worked out in advance at the top of the party as part of imposing Soviet order on the annexed lands, and the NKVD executed what was decided."},{"q":"Why did Moscow amnesty Polish citizens in August 1941?","a":"Germany had invaded the Soviet Union in June, so the Poles were now potential allies; an agreement of 30 July restored relations and allowed a Polish army to be raised in the USSR, which required the prisoners to be released."},{"q":"Why is the number of people deported still disputed?","a":"The Soviet archives on the operations stayed closed for 50 years, so the figures used in the West were built up in exile from the deportees' own accounts, and the two bodies of evidence have never fully agreed."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/NKVD_troika_execution_order_for_Samson_Dadiani.jpg","title":"An NKVD troika's execution order","desc":"A protocol of an NKVD troika in the Georgian SSR, dated 10 December 1937, sentencing a man to be shot. Boards of this kind passed sentence in the absence of the accused and without a trial.","credit":"Archive of the Ministry of Internal Affairs of Georgia, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NKVD_troika_execution_order_for_Samson_Dadiani.jpg","alt":"A typed Russian-language document on yellowed paper, headed Protocol, with a handwritten resolution and signatures in ink across the right-hand column."},"wiki":"NKVD","places":["pl:q176761"]},
    "ww2-157": {"questions":["An agreement with Moscow in July 1941 let the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raise an army on Soviet soil, but the two broke off relations again in 1943 over Katyn.","The United States kept an ambassador to it from 1939 until 1945, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> published the wartime dossiers that set out its case against the occupation."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish government-in-exile</b> was the continuation abroad of the Polish state after its territory had been divided between Germany and the Soviet Union in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American ambassador left Warsaw on 5 September 1939 and followed the government to France, where it sat until the French collapse the following summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When France fell in June 1940 the government moved to London, and the American embassy went with it; the same ambassador was accredited to the exiled governments of Belgium, Czechoslovakia, Greece, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway and Yugoslavia, all of them by then in the same city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in August 1940 that the Czechs, Poles, Norwegians, Dutch and Belgians were still in the field and were recognised by Britain and the United States as the lawful governments of their states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Ministry of Information published dossiers such as the volume of 1942 that set out, in the government's own words and figures, the case against the German occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its hardest relationship was with Moscow, which had annexed half the country and held hundreds of thousands of its citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An agreement signed in London on 30 July 1941 declared the German-Soviet treaties on Polish territory void, restored diplomatic relations and provided for a Polish army to be formed in the USSR under a commander the Polish government named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It settled nothing about the frontier, and when General Sikorski said publicly that the 1939 border could not be questioned, the Soviet press answered that the treaty of Riga was not unchangeable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In April 1943 the government asked the International Red Cross to investigate the graves at Katyn, and Molotov replied that it had stabbed the Soviet Union in the back, whereupon Moscow broke off relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain and America went on recognising it to the end of the war in Europe, and the American mission to it in London was wound up on 5 July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,\" https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1132-274, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/20/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant) to the Secretary of State, July 30, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d253. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 4, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d255. [Open access]","United States House of Representatives, <i>The Katyn Forest Massacre: Hearings Before the Select Committee to Conduct an Investigation of the Facts, Evidence and Circumstances of the Katyn Forest Massacre</i>, 82nd Congress, part 7 (Washington, D.C.: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1952), 2253-54, https://archive.org/details/katynforestmassa07unit. [Open access]","Polish Ministry of Information, <i>The Black Book of Poland</i> (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1942), https://archive.org/details/TheBlackBookOfPoland. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Polish government move twice in under a year?","a":"It left Poland when the country was overrun in September 1939 and settled in France; when France collapsed in June 1940 it had to move again, and London became the seat of almost every exiled government in Europe."},{"q":"Why did the agreement of July 1941 not settle Polish-Soviet relations?","a":"It voided the German-Soviet treaties on Polish territory but said nothing about where the frontier would run, and Moscow made clear at once that it did not regard the 1921 border as fixed."},{"q":"Why was the Katyn request to the Red Cross such a serious step?","a":"It asked an independent body to examine a crime Moscow had blamed on Germany, so Moscow treated it as taking the enemy's side and used it as the ground for breaking off relations altogether."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Wsp%C3%B3lne_posiedzenie_rz%C4%85du_RP_i_Rady_Narodowej_z_udzia%C5%82em_prezydenta_RP_w_Londynie_%2821-19-5%29.jpg/1920px-Wsp%C3%B3lne_posiedzenie_rz%C4%85du_RP_i_Rady_Narodowej_z_udzia%C5%82em_prezydenta_RP_w_Londynie_%2821-19-5%29.jpg","title":"The Polish government and National Council in London","desc":"A joint sitting of the Polish government in exile and its National Council, held in London between 1940 and 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wsp%C3%B3lne_posiedzenie_rz%C4%85du_RP_i_Rady_Narodowej_z_udzia%C5%82em_prezydenta_RP_w_Londynie_(21-19-5).jpg","alt":"Rows of men in dark suits seated in a panelled room, listening; a few women among them."},"wiki":"Polish government-in-exile","places":["city:1159151577"]},
    "ww2-158": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in August 1940 that the Poles were still in the field, sword in hand, and the pilots of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were by then flying in the Royal Air Force.","A second body of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was raised in the Soviet Union from 1941 under General Anders, out of prisoners the amnesty had released."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish Armed Forces in the West</b> were the army, navy and air force rebuilt abroad after Poland's defeat and placed under the exiled government in Paris and then in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 800,000 or so men who had served in Poland's defence the Germans claimed 694,000 prisoners, and the rest were killed, taken by the Russians, hidden at home or fled into Romania and Hungary; it was from the last of these that the new formations were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The French collapse in June 1940 drove them across the Channel with the government they served, and Britain became the base of the whole effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that August that the Poles, like the Czechs, Norwegians, Dutch and Belgians, were still in the field, sword in hand, and were recognised by Britain and the United States as lawful governments of their states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same debate heard that Polish airmen had fought splendidly in France and were fighting well over Britain, one of them having shot down three German aircraft in a single action and then saved his squadron leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A second and stranger body of Polish troops was raised on the other side of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The agreement of 30 July 1941 provided for a Polish army to be formed in the Soviet Union under a commander named by the Polish government, subordinated in operations to the Soviet high command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> General Władysław Anders, who was to command it, told the American ambassador on 16 August that the military convention had been signed that morning, that the released prisoners would assemble between Saratov and Stalingrad, and that he hoped for about 60,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His recruits came out of the camps the amnesty had opened, and the Polish embassy reported that most deportees were still held and destitute, which limited what the army could draw on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Anders said the released men's morale was good and their hatred of the Germans stronger than their feelings about their captors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,\" https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1132-274, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/20/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant) to the Secretary of State, July 30, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d253. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 17, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d258. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum by the Polish Ambassador (Ciechanowski),\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d275. [Open access]","United States House of Representatives, <i>The Katyn Forest Massacre: Hearings Before the Select Committee to Conduct an Investigation of the Facts, Evidence and Circumstances of the Katyn Forest Massacre</i>, 82nd Congress, part 7 (Washington, D.C.: U.S. Government Printing Office, 1952), 2254, https://archive.org/details/katynforestmassa07unit. [Open access]","Robert M. Kennedy, <i>The German Campaign in Poland, 1939</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-255 (Washington, D.C.: Department of the Army, 1956), 133, https://archive.org/details/kennedy-robert-m-the-german-campaign-in-poland-1939-1956. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Polish units rebuilt in France rather than kept in Poland?","a":"Poland itself was occupied and divided, so the only ground on which its forces could be reconstituted was that of an ally; men escaped through Romania, Hungary and the Baltic to reach it."},{"q":"Why did a Polish army come to be raised inside the Soviet Union?","a":"The German invasion of June 1941 made Moscow and the Polish government co-belligerents, and their agreement of 30 July provided for a Polish army on Soviet soil under a Polish commander."},{"q":"Why could Anders not say how many men he would get?","a":"His recruits were deportees and prisoners scattered across the USSR whose release depended on Soviet cooperation, and the figures Soviet officials gave him contradicted one another."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/The_Polish_Army_in_the_Norwegian_Campaign%2C_1940_HU93734.jpg/1920px-The_Polish_Army_in_the_Norwegian_Campaign%2C_1940_HU93734.jpg","title":"Polish troops in the Norwegian campaign, 1940","desc":"Men of the Polish Independent Podhale Rifles Brigade crowded on the deck of a Norwegian coastal steamer during the campaign in Norway, April 1940.","credit":"Polish official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Polish_Army_in_the_Norwegian_Campaign,_1940_HU93734.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in helmets and packs standing shoulder to shoulder across the whole deck of a small ship."},"wiki":"Polish Armed Forces in the West","places":["city:1159149575"]},
    "ww2-159": {"questions":["Moscow denounced its non-aggression pact over a frontier incident Finland offered to investigate jointly, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed 2 days later.","Finland's government resigned on the first night of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to make way for one the Soviet Union might be willing to negotiate with."],"abstract":"The <b>Winter War</b> was the Soviet Union's war against Finland, fought from 30 November 1939 until a ceasefire the following March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Moscow had spent 1939 pressing Finland for islands in the Gulf of Finland and ground on the Karelian Isthmus, and the Finns had answered with counter-offers in Soviet Karelia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The immediate pretext was a frontier incident; Helsinki replied on 29 November that Finland had not injured Soviet territorial integrity and proposed that the two countries' frontier commissioners investigate it together under a convention of 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same note observed that the Soviet denunciation of the non-aggression pact had not been warranted, and that only ordinary frontier guards stood on the Finnish side of the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack came the next morning, artillery opening at 7:00 AM near Terijoki and north of Lake Ladoga where Soviet troops crossed, with Petsamo and the Finnish part of the Fisherman's Peninsula occupied and bombs dropped on Helsinki's Malmi airfield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Nine Soviet bombers attacked the fortresses in the Bay of Helsinki at twenty past ten, and the American minister reported that no attack had been made from the Finnish side anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That night the Finnish government resigned, after a unanimous vote of confidence in the Diet, in order to make way for a government with which the Soviet Union would negotiate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow's answer was to recognise no Finnish government at all: it recognised the so-called Kuusinen government instead, a body of Finnish communists set up under Soviet protection, and concluded a formal treaty with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 14 December 1939 the Council of the League of Nations passed a resolution on the attack which the British prime minister afterwards cited as the ground of Britain's obligation to help Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Finland held out through the winter against much larger forces, and the peace it made in March cost it territory and drove it towards Germany for the supplies and support it wanted against the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Finland (Schoenfeld) to the Secretary of State, November 29, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d1110. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Finland (Schoenfeld) to the Secretary of State, November 30, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d1112. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Finland (Schoenfeld) to the Secretary of State, November 30, 1939, 10:40 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d1113. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston), November 30, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d1116. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, December 3, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v01/d1133. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Finland,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 March 1940, vol. 358, cols. 1802-3, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/mar/19/finland. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Finland,\" https://history.state.gov/countries/finland. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Finland propose a joint investigation of the frontier incident?","a":"It denied having attacked at all and pointed out that only ordinary frontier guards stood on its side, so an inspection by both countries' frontier commissioners under their 1928 convention would have settled the question."},{"q":"Why did the Finnish government resign on the first night of the war?","a":"Moscow had refused to deal with it, so the Diet gave it a vote of confidence and it stood down anyway in the hope that a new government would be one the Soviet Union was willing to negotiate with."},{"q":"Why did recognising the Kuusinen government matter?","a":"It let Moscow claim there was already a lawful Finnish government asking for Soviet help, which removed any need to negotiate with the government in Helsinki and cast the invasion as assistance rather than attack."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/A_Finnish_Maxim_M-32_machine_gun_nest_during_the_Winter_War.jpg/1920px-A_Finnish_Maxim_M-32_machine_gun_nest_during_the_Winter_War.jpg","title":"A Finnish machine-gun position, February 1940","desc":"A Finnish Maxim M-32 machine-gun nest about five kilometres (three miles) north of Lemetti, photographed on 21 February 1940, with the Soviet line a hundred metres away.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Finnish_Maxim_M-32_machine_gun_nest_during_the_Winter_War.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in white snow capes crouched in a dug-out among snow-covered trees behind a machine gun."},"wiki":"Winter War","places":["adm0:fin","adm0:rus","pl:q209010","pol:finland","pol:ussr"]},
    "ww2-160": {"questions":["Finland gave up the Karelian Isthmus with Viborg and leased Hanko under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, having been told that after a war no territory would be offered in exchange.","Britain and France had offered troops if Finland asked for them, but Helsinki took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead and Chamberlain told the Commons that aggression had succeeded."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Peace Treaty</b> ended the Winter War on terms Moscow dictated and Finland accepted rather than fight on alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 8 March 1940 Molotov set out the Soviet conditions to the American ambassador: a long lease on the Hanko peninsula and the islands around it, the Karelian Isthmus including Viborg and its bay, and the north-western shore of Lake Ladoga with Sortavala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked what Finland would get in return, he laughed and said that after a war they could not give any territory in exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Finnish delegates were given the same terms in the Kremlin the following night, with the new frontier traced in detail from the Bay of Viborg north-east to the old border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The one point still being argued was the power station at Imatra, which served the whole of southern Finland, and a German diplomat in Moscow thought no other concession likely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A way out was on offer: the French had instructed their chargé in Moscow to tell the Finnish delegation that if Finland fought on, Britain and France would send whatever was needed, troops included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chamberlain told the Commons afterwards that the Finns had been informed as early as 25 February that men as well as material would come if they appealed for them, and that they had decided not to appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 12 March the delegates were instructed to accept, and the Soviet government refused an armistice until the terms were signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The treaty was announced that night, to be ratified within 3 days, with hostilities ending at noon on 13 March and Soviet troops taking up the new frontier 2 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Commons received the news with sympathy for the Finns and, in the Opposition leader's words, deep regret at the success of aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, March 8, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d277. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, March 9, 1940, 9 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d279. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, March 9, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d278. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, March 12, 1940, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d284. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, March 12, 1940, 1 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d283. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Prime Minister's Statement,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 13 March 1940, vol. 358, cols. 1161-5, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/mar/13/prime-ministers-statement. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, March 13, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d286. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Finland accept terms it had rejected before the war?","a":"It had fought for 3 months against far larger forces and the Soviet demands had grown rather than softened; the alternative was to fight on in the hope of Allied help that would have to be asked for and might arrive too late."},{"q":"Why did Finland not appeal for Anglo-French troops?","a":"The offer turned on a formal Finnish appeal that never came; Chamberlain told the Commons it had always been understood that the Finnish government must decide what suited its own interests in the light of all it knew, and it decided not to ask."},{"q":"Why did Moscow refuse an armistice before the treaty was signed?","a":"Fighting was its remaining pressure on the Finnish delegation, and stopping it first would have let Helsinki reopen terms it had just been forced to accept."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Evacuation_at_the_end_of_Winter_War.jpg/1920px-Evacuation_at_the_end_of_Winter_War.jpg","title":"Karelians leaving the ceded territory, March 1940","desc":"Civilians being evacuated at Tyrjä-Parikkala on 15 March 1940, two days after the fighting stopped, as the districts ceded to the Soviet Union were emptied.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evacuation_at_the_end_of_Winter_War.jpg","alt":"Children in thick hats and coats, some carrying bundles and a baby, crowded together in the open in winter."},"wiki":"Moscow Peace Treaty","places":["pl:q14657"]},
    "ww2-191": {"questions":["Pétain's broadcast of 17 June 1940 asking for terms spoke of the destitute refugees of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who were then crossing the roads of France in their millions.","Belgian families who had fled the Germans in 1914 took to the roads again in May 1940, crossing into France and joining the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that soon swept south towards the Loire."],"abstract":"The <b>Exode</b> was the mass flight of civilians across France in May and June 1940, as the German armies broke through in the north and drove south towards Paris and the Loire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People began leaving their homes on 10 May, the day the offensive opened, and at the peak some eight million French, Belgian, Dutch and other refugees were on the move, though no firm count was ever made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French defence ministry's own account counts eight million civilians in flight by mid-June, a stream that merged with the disintegrating French army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thousands of Belgians had crossed into France since May, many repeating the flight of 1914, and on 26 May the American ambassador reported a stream of refugees pouring into south-western France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paris emptied after 10 June, when government officials were ordered out and millions of others left of their own accord, and some of the worst jams and deaths came at the Loire crossings in mid-June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mayors often set off the departure, most prefects left too, and the refugees complained almost with one voice that the public authorities had abandoned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 14 June Roosevelt told Churchill that he had asked Congress for fifty million dollars for food and clothing for civilian refugees in France, and that the Senate had agreed unanimously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pétain's broadcast of 17 June, announcing that France must stop fighting, spoke of the unhappy refugees crossing the roads in extreme destitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The armistice signed on 22 June bound the French government to carry out the return of the population to the occupied zone in agreement with the German authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In August Pétain still told Roosevelt that millions of refugees waited to go home, and his regime used the sense of abandonment to justify replacing the Third Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Robert O. Paxton, \"Inside the Panic,\" review of <i>Fleeing Hitler: France 1940</i>, by Hanna Diamond, <i>The New York Review of Books</i>, 22 November 2007, https://www.nybooks.com/articles/2007/11/22/inside-the-panic/.","Nadine Bonnefoi, \"Aux origines de la France Libre. L'Appel du 18 juin,\" <i>Les chemins de la mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/france-libre/aux. [in French] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, May 26, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d123. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy), June 14, 1940, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d216. [Open access]","\"Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,\" in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chief of State of France (Pétain) to President Roosevelt, August 27, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d608. [Open access]","Simon Catros and Bernard Wilkin, \"Sur les chemins de l'exode : Les réfugiés belges dans l'Eure, 1940,\" <i>Histoire, économie et société</i> 41, no. 1 (2022): 57–73, https://www.revues.armand-colin.com/histoire/histoire-economie-societe/histoire-economie-societe-12022/chemins-lexode-refugies-belges-leure-1940. [in French] [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many people flee instead of staying at home?","a":"Memory and fear drove them. Folk memories of German violence against civilians in 1914, of near starvation in the occupied north and of men deported to work for the German war effort all pushed families onto the roads, and the bombing of Rotterdam on 14 May taught city-dwellers to expect the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many Belgians were repeating a flight they had already made in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the exodus matter politically?","a":"Because the French blamed their own state for it. Refugees complained almost unanimously that mayors, prefects and even firemen had left them to fend for themselves, and the new regime at Vichy exploited that impression of abandonment to build its case against the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pétain spoke to the refugees directly in the broadcast that asked for terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the number of refugees never be exact?","a":"Because nobody could count them. At the peak no firm count could be made, and movement ran both ways: some people returned within days while others stayed displaced for the rest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure therefore survives as a round estimate, eight million in both the review cited here and the French defence ministry's account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1971-083-01%2C_Frankreich%2C_franz%C3%B6sische_Fl%C3%BCchtlinge.jpg","title":"Refugees near Gien, 19 June 1940","desc":"Refugee carts loaded high with belongings near Gien on the Loire on 19 June 1940, in a photograph from the German side now held by the Bundesarchiv.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1971-083-01, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1971-083-01,_Frankreich,_französische_Flüchtlinge.jpg","alt":"A woman in an apron leads a horse pulling a cart hung with buckets past a car piled high with luggage on a country road."}},
    "ww2-192": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons that he had read Article 8 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with grief and amazement, because it placed the French fleet under German and Italian control fully armed.","General Huntziger signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the forest of Compiègne at 6:50 PM, but it took effect only once France had also concluded an armistice with Italy."],"abstract":"The <b>Armistice of 22 June 1940</b> was the agreement under which France stopped fighting Germany, requested by Marshal Pétain on 17 June, the day after he replaced Paul Reynaud as head of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler had the terms presented in the forest of Compiègne, in the railway carriage in which Germany had signed the armistice of November 1918, brought back from its museum for the purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A preamble read by Keitel said the German demands were meant to prevent a resumption of the fighting, to secure the war against Britain and to prepare a peace that would right the wrong done to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> General Huntziger and General Keitel signed at 6:50 PM, but the agreement took effect only 6 hours after France had also concluded an armistice with Italy, on 25 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The terms were milder than the French had feared: the empire was left untouched and France was allowed an army of 100,000 men to keep internal order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> France north and west of a line from Geneva to the Spanish border was occupied, and there the French government was to support the occupier's regulations through its own administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government could sit in the unoccupied zone, France paid for the occupation troops, and French prisoners of war stayed in German camps until a peace was concluded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article 8 made the fleet gather in designated ports to be disarmed under German or Italian control, while Germany solemnly declared that it did not mean to use the ships in its war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 25 June that he had read that article with grief and amazement, since the ships would pass under enemy control fully armed and the armistice could be voided on any pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A French government handout replied that it had never thought of ceding the fleet and had secured a system of controlled surveillance that kept a reduced fleet active to protect the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["\"Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,\" in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Heath) to the Secretary of State, June 21, 1940, 8 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d231. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Heath) to the Secretary of State, June 22, 1940, 11 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d233. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Deputy Ambassador in France (Biddle) to the Secretary of State, June 26, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d237. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 25 June 1940, vol. 362, cols. 301–7, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/25/war-situation. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Waffenstillstand in Compiègne 1940,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/waffenstillstand-in-compiegne-1940. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"France,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the armistice signed at Compiègne?","a":"Because the place carried a message. Hitler chose the spot in the forest where the armistice of 11 November 1918 had been signed, had the old wooden saloon car brought back from the museum and invited the world press to watch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German preamble spoke of eradicating the memory of 1918 now that France had been defeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the terms milder than the French expected?","a":"Because Hitler wanted France out of the war and Britain alone. Leaving the empire untouched gave the French government little reason to fight on from its colonies, and it signalled to Britain that Germany did not seek to destroy France, though Churchill did not answer with peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Keitel's preamble named the war against England as one purpose of the demands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fleet clause alarm the British?","a":"Because the ships would sit fully armed in ports under German and Italian control. Churchill pointed out that the German promise exempted ships needed for coast surveillance and minesweeping, and that Germany could void the whole armistice at any time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1982-089-18%2C_Waffenstillstand_von_Compi%C3%A8gne%2C_Unterh%C3%A4ndler.jpg","title":"Keitel hands over the armistice terms, Compiègne 1940","desc":"General Keitel presenting the German armistice terms to the French delegation in the forest of Compiègne in June 1940.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1982-089-18, Heinrich Hoffmann, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1982-089-18,_Waffenstillstand_von_Compiègne,_Unterhändler.jpg","alt":"German officers on one side of a table in a railway carriage hand papers across to French officers in képis on the other."},"wiki":"Armistice of 22 June 1940"},
    "ww2-193": {"questions":["In October 1940 the government of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issued its first Statut des Juifs, which excluded Jews from public office, the army and many professions.","After Pétain met Hitler at Montoire in October 1940, the leaders of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> announced agreement on the principle of collaboration for the reconstruction of peace in Europe."],"abstract":"<b>Vichy France</b> is the name given to the French State led by Marshal Pétain after the defeat of 1940, after the town in the Auvergne where its government settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The armistice let the French government choose its seat in unoccupied territory, and although Vichy's measures applied in theory to all of France, it governed on its own only in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 10 July 1940 parliament, meeting at Vichy, gave Pétain's government full powers to draw up a new constitution, though the one the regime drafted was never promulgated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pétain became head of the French State with near-absolute powers, and the motto 'work, family, country' replaced 'liberty, equality, fraternity'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Officially neutral, the regime collaborated closely with Germany, and in November 1942 German troops occupied the southern zone as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After Pétain met Hitler at Montoire in October 1940, the official communiqué announced agreement 'on the principle of collaboration for the reconstruction of peace in Europe'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pierre Laval, the leading figure in that policy, told American journalists in November that when he left office in 1936 he had chosen fascism over bolshevism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In October 1940 Vichy issued the first <i>Statut des Juifs</i>, which with a second law of June 1941 excluded Jews from public life, the civil service, the army and many professions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It created a General Commissariat for Jewish Affairs in March 1941, and in July 1942 French police under René Bousquet arrested 13,000 Jews in Paris and held them in the Vélodrome d'Hiver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most Jews deported from France were murdered at Auschwitz, and it is unlikely the Germans could have deported so many without the help of French police and administrators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"France,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","\"Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,\" in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","Assemblée nationale, \"1940-1958 : d'une République à l'autre,\" <i>La République et le suffrage universel</i>, https://www2.assemblee-nationale.fr/decouvrir-l-assemblee/histoire/le-suffrage-universel/la-republique-et-le-suffrage-universel/1940-1958-d-une-republique-a-l-autre. [in French] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, July 5, 1940, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d538. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in France (Matthews) to the Secretary of State, October 26, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d474. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in France (Matthews) to the Secretary of State, November 15, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d480. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, \"Henri Philippe Pétain 1856–1951,\" <i>LeMO-Biografien, Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/henri-philippe-petain. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Waffenstillstand in Compiègne 1940,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/waffenstillstand-in-compiegne-1940. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vichy hand over foreign Jews first?","a":"It hoped to buy more independence and to shield French-born Jews. Vichy officials met German quotas with foreign and stateless Jews while being less willing to give up French citizens, but the bargain trapped them into filling every quota the Germans demanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the autumn of 1942 some 42,000 Jews had passed through the Drancy camp, many of them foreign or stateless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British attack on the fleet help the collaborators?","a":"Because it turned French anger against Britain. The American ambassador reported that the attack greatly strengthened those who wanted complete cooperation with Germany and Italy and left the advocates of cooperation with Britain and the United States in despair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Vichy govern only part of France?","a":"Because the armistice gave Germany all the rights of an occupying power north and west of the demarcation line, and bound the French administration there to carry out German regulations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only in the unoccupied zone could the new government act on its own, and after November 1942 not even there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25217%2C_Henry_Philippe_Petain_und_Adolf_Hitler.jpg","title":"Pétain and Hitler at Montoire, 24 October 1940","desc":"Hitler greeting Marshal Pétain at Montoire-sur-le-Loir on 24 October 1940, the meeting after which Vichy announced the principle of collaboration.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H25217, Heinrich Hoffmann, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25217,_Henry_Philippe_Petain_und_Adolf_Hitler.jpg","alt":"An elderly French officer in a gold-embroidered képi shakes hands with Hitler while uniformed German officers look on."},"wiki":"Vichy France"},
    "ww2-194": {"questions":["Condemned to death for treason in August 1945, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had his sentence commuted to life imprisonment by de Gaulle because of his age and his service in the First World War.","Meeting Hitler at Montoire in October 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> accepted the principle of collaboration, though he had told the American ambassador that only Hitler's defeat could free France."],"abstract":"<b>Philippe Pétain</b> was the French marshal who asked Germany for an armistice in June 1940 and then led the Vichy regime as head of the French State until 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in 1856 into a farming family at Cauchy-à-la-Tour, he made his name in the First World War, when his defence of Verdun in 1916 made him 'the hero of Verdun' across France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1917 he took command when large parts of the army stood close to mutiny, held the front together with discipline and better supply, and in 1918 was made a marshal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As inspector-general of the army in the 1920s he pressed for the Maginot Line against younger officers, among them de Gaulle, who wanted mobile, motorised warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was ambassador in Spain when the war began, and in May 1940 Paul Reynaud brought the 84-year-old marshal into the government as his deputy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Reynaud fell on 16 June, Churchill told the Commons, a new government was formed under Pétain for the prime purpose of seeking an armistice; in July Pétain protested to Roosevelt that the British attack on the French fleet was a hateful aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At midday on 17 June Pétain told the French by radio that they must stop fighting, offering France 'the gift of my person' to ease its misfortune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In October he agreed with Hitler at Montoire on 'the principle of collaboration', although in July he had told the American ambassador that only Hitler's defeat by another power could restore France's independence, and in August he complained of England's brutal selfishness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> His regime issued the anti-Jewish statutes of October 1940 and June 1941 and handed foreign Jews to the Germans for deportation in the hope of shielding French ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In August 1945 he was condemned to death for treason, but de Gaulle commuted the sentence to life imprisonment because of his age and his First World War service, and he died in 1951.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Manfred Wichmann, \"Henri Philippe Pétain 1856–1951,\" <i>LeMO-Biografien, Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/henri-philippe-petain. [in German] [Open access]","Nadine Bonnefoi, \"Aux origines de la France Libre. L'Appel du 18 juin,\" <i>Les chemins de la mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/france-libre/aux. [in French] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 25 June 1940, vol. 362, cols. 301–7, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/25/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, July 5, 1940, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d538. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in France (Matthews) to the Secretary of State, October 26, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d474. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"France,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The President of the French Council of Ministers (Pétain) to President Roosevelt, July 4, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d537. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in France (Murphy) to the Secretary of State, August 7, 1940, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d464. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a defeated France turn to an old soldier?","a":"Because of his name. Reynaud brought him into the government in May 1940, when the breakthrough had shaken confidence in the cabinet, and to the whole country he was still the hero of Verdun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His broadcast of 17 June leaned on that standing, invoking the army and the veterans he had once commanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Pétain break with Britain in July 1940?","a":"Because of the attack on the fleet at Mers-el-Kébir. He wrote to Roosevelt the next day that British ships had fired on French warships lying at anchor and called it a hateful aggression that nothing could justify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His government then broke off relations with London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was his death sentence not carried out?","a":"De Gaulle commuted it to life imprisonment because of Pétain's service in the First World War and his great age; he was 89 at the trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Philippe_P%C3%A9tain_1941_Portrait_photograph.jpg","title":"Official portrait, 1941","desc":"An official colour portrait photograph of the head of the French State, printed by the Draeger works in 1941.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Imprimerie Draeger, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippe_Pétain_1941_Portrait_photograph.jpg","alt":"An elderly man with a white moustache in a gold-embroidered red képi and a military tunic, in a colour portrait."},"wiki":"Philippe Pétain"},
    "ww2-195": {"questions":["Equatorial Africa and Cameroon had rallied to de Gaulle in late August 1940, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a month later showed that French West Africa would stay loyal to Vichy.","French posters headed 'Let us always remember' paired Mers-el-Kébir with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the September 1940 clash in which British ships backed a Free French expedition."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Dakar</b> was the failed attempt by Free French and British forces in September 1940 to bring French West Africa over to de Gaulle, and it left the French empire split between Vichy and Free France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1940 France ruled more than 12 million square kilometres (4.6 million square miles) on four continents, nine-tenths of it in Africa, which made the colonies a prize for both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In June the officers of French Equatorial Africa were nearly unanimous for fighting on, and its governor-general spoke of a solid bloc of African colonies continuing the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By late July the mood had turned: Vichy named Pierre Boisson High Commissioner at Dakar, and the new governor-general at Brazzaville promised to carry out the armistice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 26 and 29 August Chad, Cameroon and the rest of Equatorial Africa nonetheless declared for de Gaulle, and the governor-general fled across the river to the Belgian Congo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> West Africa and its great port of Dakar stayed loyal to Vichy, and the expedition that arrived on 23 September failed to rally it in 3 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that the operation was primarily French and that neither he nor de Gaulle had wanted a long or bloody conflict with the Vichy French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He blamed the failure on three cruisers and three destroyers that had passed the Straits of Gibraltar unchallenged, carrying bitter Vichy partisans to man the shore batteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The batteries, reinforced by the guns of the damaged battleship Richelieu, fought a stiff action in which two Vichy submarines were sunk and a British battleship and cruiser were damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> French posters paired Dakar with Mers-el-Kébir as wrongs to remember, and in December a British colonial governor was still calling it the Dakar affair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Musée de l'Ordre de la Libération, <i>De Gaulle et la France libre</i>, dossier pédagogique (professeur) (Paris: Ordre de la Libération, n.d.), https://www.ordredelaliberation.fr/sites/default/files/media/fichers/Dossier%20p%C3%A9dagogique%20%28professeur%29%20-%20De%20Gaulle%20et%20la%20France%20libre_1.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Leopoldville (Mallon) to the Secretary of State, June 25, 1940, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d725. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Leopoldville (Mallon) to the Secretary of State, July 26, 1940, 8 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d726. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Leopoldville (Mallon) to the Secretary of State, August 28, 1940, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d730. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 261–352, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]","Victoria and Albert Museum, \"Souvenons nous toujours,\" poster, France, c. 1940, accession no. E.1297-2004, <i>V&amp;A Explore the Collections</i>, https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O100869/. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Lagos (Jester) to the Secretary of State, December 11, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d742. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did de Gaulle need territory in Africa?","a":"Because a movement in exile needed ground of its own. The rallying of Equatorial Africa and Cameroon gave him a base of sovereignty and legitimacy, a continuous bloc from the Atlantic to the Red Sea, and troops; Brazzaville became the seat of Free France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the expedition fail, by Churchill's account?","a":"Because Dakar had been reinforced before it arrived. Through accidents and errors that became the subject of disciplinary action, the Vichy ships were not stopped at Gibraltar, and their partisans stiffened a garrison that de Gaulle had believed would come over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain keep backing de Gaulle after the failure?","a":"Churchill told the Commons that de Gaulle had been right to think most Frenchmen in Dakar favoured Free France, that his judgement had been extremely sure-footed, and that the government had no intention of abandoning his cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Dakar_24_September_1940_8.jpg","title":"Bombardment of Dakar harbour, 24 September 1940","desc":"A British battleship's salvo falling short during the bombardment of Dakar harbour on 24 September 1940, photographed by a sailor with the fleet.","credit":"Frederick Milthorp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dakar_24_September_1940_8.jpg","alt":"Warships on a grey sea, with columns of spray and smoke rising along the horizon."},"wiki":"Battle of Dakar"},
    "ww2-196": {"questions":["Recognised by Churchill on 28 June 1940, the movement called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved its headquarters to Carlton Gardens that July and gained an African capital at Brazzaville in August.","The phrase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first used on the air by de Gaulle on 22 June 1940, 4 days after the broadcast that the movement took as its founding act."],"abstract":"<b>Free France</b> was the political and military organisation that de Gaulle built in London from June 1940 to keep France in the war against the Axis and to take the place of Pétain's regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founding act was his broadcast of 18 June, and on 22 June, in a second appeal whose recording survives, he used the expression 'France libre' on the air for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill officially recognised him as leader of the Free French on 28 June, when, in the words of the Order of Liberation's museum, he was an isolated man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its headquarters moved from Saint Stephen's House to Carlton Gardens at the end of July, and an agreement with Churchill on 7 August let it acquire the attributes of a state step by step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Paid for by Britain, it began with derisory means, and even at its height in 1943 it counted only about 60,000 men and women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The armistice ordered the French government to forbid its citizens to fight for any state still at war with Germany, and said that those who did would be treated as insurgents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Few answered at first: a handful of officers such as Koenig and Leclerc, a vice-admiral, two generals, two deputies, the jurist René Cassin and some hundreds of unknown men and women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 4 July that French troops in Britain would be sent home except those who had volunteered to follow de Gaulle in the forces of liberation he led.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Free French navy was formed on 1 July, and in Africa, where British officers had urged the colonies to fight on since June, Equatorial Africa declared for de Gaulle in August after a bloodless coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Washington kept its distance, telling its consul at Léopoldville to avoid raising any question of principle about relations with the de Gaulle committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Musée de l'Ordre de la Libération, <i>De Gaulle et la France libre</i>, dossier pédagogique (professeur) (Paris: Ordre de la Libération, n.d.), https://www.ordredelaliberation.fr/sites/default/files/media/fichers/Dossier%20p%C3%A9dagogique%20%28professeur%29%20-%20De%20Gaulle%20et%20la%20France%20libre_1.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Nadine Bonnefoi, \"Aux origines de la France Libre. L'Appel du 18 juin,\" <i>Les chemins de la mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/france-libre/aux. [in French] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"French Fleet,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 July 1940, vol. 362, cols. 1043–51, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/04/french-fleet. [Open access]","\"Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,\" in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Leopoldville (Mallon) to the Secretary of State, August 30, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d731. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Consul at Leopoldville (Mallon), September 7, 1940, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d732. [Open access]","UNESCO, \"The Appeal of 18 June 1940,\" <i>Memory of the World Register</i>, registered 2005, https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/appeal-18-june-1940. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Lagos (Jester) to the Secretary of State, June 25, 1940, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d724. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so few French people join at first?","a":"Because hardly anyone heard the call. In the chaos of the exodus and the defeat few people could listen to the broadcasts from London, and fewer still could act on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The appeal of 18 June was little heard, while Pétain's broadcast reached the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did de Gaulle give Free France the trappings of a state?","a":"Because he claimed to represent France at war, not to lead a band of exiles. From August 1940 Free France gave itself armed forces, laws, a decoration and a currency, and the Order of Liberation, created at Brazzaville in November, answered Pétain's power to award the Legion of Honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was joining Free France dangerous for a Frenchman?","a":"Because the armistice made it so. Article 10 bound the French government to stop its citizens fighting for Germany's enemies and told German troops to treat any who did as insurgents rather than soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Allied_Soldiers_Like_London_and_London_Likes_Them-_Overseas_Troops_in_England%2C_1940_D1722.jpg","title":"Free French servicemen in London, 1940","desc":"Free French soldiers and sailors buying the French-language newspaper France from a London newsagent in 1940, a Ministry of Information photograph.","credit":"Ministry of Information Photo Division, Imperial War Museums D 1722, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_Soldiers_Like_London_and_London_Likes_Them-_Overseas_Troops_in_England,_1940_D1722.jpg","alt":"Men in French naval and army uniform, steel helmets slung at their sides, reading newspapers outside a London newsagent."},"wiki":"Free France"},
    "ww2-197": {"questions":["Wounded and captured in 1916, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried five times to escape and later argued in a book of 1934 for a professional army built around armoured forces.","Appointed under-secretary of state for war by Reynaud on 6 June 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left for London when the new government sought an armistice and was condemned to death in his absence."],"abstract":"<b>Charles de Gaulle</b> was the French general who refused the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Armistice_of_22_June_1940\">armistice of 1940</span>, led Free France from London, headed the provisional government at the Liberation and founded the Fifth Republic, which he governed as <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"President_of_France\">president</span> from 1959 to 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in Lille in 1890 and trained at Saint-Cyr, he was wounded several times in the First World War, captured in 1916 and tried five times to escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between the wars he made his name as a military theorist, arguing in <i>Vers l'armée de métier</i> of 1934 for a corps of armoured forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In May 1940 he commanded the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"4th_Armored_Division_(France,_1940)\">4th Armoured Division</span> as a colonel, and on 6 June Paul Reynaud made him under-secretary of state for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the government sought an armistice he left for London, and on 18 June he called over the BBC on French soldiers in Britain to join him, since France still had a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"French_colonial_empire\">vast empire</span>; the manuscript of that appeal is on UNESCO's Memory of the World register.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> On 4 July Churchill told the Commons that French volunteers would follow de Gaulle, and that summer a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Vichy_France\">Vichy</span> court sentenced him to death in his absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Battle_of_Dakar\">Dakar</span> Churchill still praised his extremely sure-footed judgement, and though an American consul feared that failure in Gabon would hurt his prestige, he went on to rally the empire's territories, unite with the Resistance inside France and place France among the victorious Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He entered liberated Paris on 26 August 1944 as president of the provisional government, but resigned in January 1946, at odds with the constituent assembly over the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recalled in the Algerian crisis of May 1958, he had a constitution with a strong executive approved by referendum, was elected president that December and accepted Algerian independence through the Évian accords of 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As president he pursued national independence, taking France out of NATO's integrated command and having the president elected by universal suffrage, and he resigned after losing <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"1969_French_constitutional_referendum\">a referendum</span> in April 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Présidence de la République, \"Charles de Gaulle, 1959–1969,\" <i>Les anciens présidents de la République</i>, Élysée, https://www.elysee.fr/charles-de-gaulle. [in French] [Open access]","Dorlis Blume, Regina Haunhorst and Irmgard Zündorf, \"Biografie Charles de Gaulle,\" <i>LeMO-Biografien, Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, 4 March 2016, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/biografie/charles-de-gaulle. [in German] [Open access]","Musée de l'Ordre de la Libération, <i>De Gaulle et la France libre</i>, dossier pédagogique (professeur) (Paris: Ordre de la Libération, n.d.), https://www.ordredelaliberation.fr/sites/default/files/media/fichers/Dossier%20p%C3%A9dagogique%20%28professeur%29%20-%20De%20Gaulle%20et%20la%20France%20libre_1.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Nadine Bonnefoi, \"Aux origines de la France Libre. L'Appel du 18 juin,\" <i>Les chemins de la mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/france-libre/aux. [in French] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"France,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 261–352, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]","UNESCO, \"The Appeal of 18 June 1940,\" <i>Memory of the World Register</i>, registered 2005, https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/appeal-18-june-1940. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"French Fleet,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 July 1940, vol. 362, cols. 1043–51, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/04/french-fleet. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul at Leopoldville (Mallon) to the Secretary of State, October 31, 1940, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d734. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did de Gaulle go to London instead of staying in France?","a":"Because he would not accept the armistice. He came back from a mission to London on 16 June to find the government seeking terms and left again at once to carry on the war; he had been in London to work on a plan to unite the French and British governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his famous appeal reach so few people?","a":"It went out once on the BBC on the evening of 18 June and was not recorded, while Pétain's broadcast of the previous day had reached the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A nearly complete text reached the press still free in southern France, but most people first met his words in the poster of August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill defend him after the failure at Dakar?","a":"Churchill told the Commons that de Gaulle had been right about French feeling in Dakar, that his conduct in perplexing circumstances had raised British opinion of him, and that Britain would not abandon his cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Charles_de_Gaulle_micro_BBC_Londres.jpg","title":"At the BBC microphone in London","desc":"The leader of the Free French speaking into a BBC microphone in London, in a Keystone photograph taken some time between 1940 and 1943.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Keystone-France, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_de_Gaulle_micro_BBC_Londres.jpg","alt":"An officer in uniform seated before an old studio microphone against a dark background."},"wiki":"Charles de Gaulle","places":["city:1159147227"]},
    "ww2-198": {"questions":["Nearly 1,300 French sailors died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Pétain protested to Roosevelt as a hateful aggression against warships lying at anchor near Oran.","Churchill left the judgement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Parliament, the nation, the United States and history, after British battleships had opened fire on the French fleet in Algeria."],"abstract":"The <b>Attack on Mers-el-Kébir</b> was the British bombardment on 3 July 1940 of the French fleet at its base beside Oran in Algeria, carried out to stop the ships falling into German hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article 8 of the armistice required the French fleet to gather in designated ports and be disarmed under German or Italian control, against a German promise not to use it in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that Britain had offered to release France from its treaty obligations on one condition, that the fleet sail for British ports first, and that this had not been done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early on 3 July French ships in British harbours were boarded, and Admiral Gensoul at Mers-el-Kébir was told to join the British, sail with reduced crews to a British or West Indian port, or sink his ships within 6 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When he refused, Admiral Somerville's squadron opened fire at 5:53 PM, sinking a battleship and driving a battle cruiser ashore, while one battle cruiser escaped towards Toulon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The French dead are usually put at 1,297 sailors, the figure a French deputy cited in a National Assembly question in 2006, while a museum catalogue gives around 1,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Pétain protested to Roosevelt the next day that the British had fired on warships at anchor that could not defend themselves, and called the attack a hateful aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His government broke off diplomatic relations with Britain, and the American ambassador reported that the attack had greatly strengthened those who wanted complete cooperation with Germany and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Germans and Italians were quick to exploit the anti-British feeling, lifting the armistice clauses on the French fleet and air force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill left the judgement of the action to Parliament, the nation, the United States and history, and French posters afterwards urged the French always to remember it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"French Fleet,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 July 1940, vol. 362, cols. 1043–51, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/04/french-fleet. [Open access]","\"Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,\" in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The President of the French Council of Ministers (Pétain) to President Roosevelt, July 4, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d537. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in France (Murphy) to the Secretary of State, July 18, 1940, 9 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d542. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, July 5, 1940, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d538. [Open access]","Assemblée nationale, Question écrite no. 108560 de M. Christian Ménard, \"Lieux de mémoire,\" <i>Journal officiel</i>, 31 October 2006, 11188, réponse 30 January 2007, 1043, https://questions.assemblee-nationale.fr/dyn/12/questions/QANR5L12QE108560.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Victoria and Albert Museum, \"Souvenons nous toujours,\" poster, France, c. 1940, accession no. E.1297-2004, <i>V&amp;A Explore the Collections</i>, https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O100869/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain fire on the fleet of its former ally?","a":"Because it believed the armistice put that fleet within Germany's reach. Article 8 left the ships armed in ports under German or Italian control with only a German promise to protect them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that the whole cabinet agreed, without hesitation, that no other decision was possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack help Germany in France?","a":"It turned French anger against Britain. The American ambassador found the cabinet's reaction violent in the extreme, reported that the collaborators had been strengthened and noted that Germany and Italy at once relaxed the armistice clauses on the fleet and air force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll given as a range?","a":"Because the figures differ by source. The 1,297 sailors in a French deputy's question to the National Assembly is the usual number, while a museum catalogue speaks of around 1,200; on the day itself Churchill could only say that French losses must have been heavy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Cuirass%C3%A9_Bretagne_03-07-1940.jpg","title":"The battleship Bretagne, 3 July 1940","desc":"The French battleship Bretagne at Mers-el-Kébir on 3 July 1940, the ship that blew up and sank in the British bombardment.","credit":"Jacques Mulard, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuirassé_Bretagne_03-07-1940.jpg","alt":"A warship half hidden by dense black smoke beside a tall white column of spray thrown up by a shell."},"wiki":"Attack on Mers-el-Kébir"},
    "ww2-199": {"questions":["Forced labourers held in four camps on Alderney died in their hundreds during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a British government review of 2024 putting the likely toll at between 641 and 1,027.","Islanders switched to the Reichsmark, continental time and driving on the right under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when the German forces began surrendering on 9 May 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>German occupation of the Channel Islands</b> lasted from the end of June 1940 to May 1945 and made Jersey, Guernsey, Alderney and Sark the only British territory Germany occupied in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the fall of France the British government decided to demilitarise the islands, withdrawing its troops to use them elsewhere rather than defend islands so close to the French coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 25,000 people left before the Germans arrived, 22,656 of them through the official evacuation, out of a population the Home Secretary put at 92,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of Alderney left, with around half of Guernsey and a fifth of Jersey, and since the demilitarisation had not been announced the German attack came with bombing that killed 44 islanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Lords a Jerseyman protested that demilitarisation was a near relative of abandonment, breaking ties with the Crown that were centuries old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By September the Home Secretary still had no news of the islanders to give the Commons, and attempts to learn anything through the Red Cross had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The occupiers brought in the Reichsmark, driving on the right and continental time, banned radios, and punished rule-breakers with prison or deportation, as the Guernsey journalist Frank Falla found when he was deported for his part in an underground newsletter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A few Jews who had been refused entry to Britain as enemy aliens had to stay, were registered and stripped of their property and businesses, and some were deported to concentration camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On Alderney the Germans built four camps for forced and slave labourers, and a review published by the British government in 2024 put those sent there at 7,608 to 7,812 and the likely deaths at 641 to 1,027, while finding no evidence that Alderney had been a 'mini-Auschwitz'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill announced on 8 May 1945 that 'our dear Channel Islands' would be free the next day, and the German forces began surrendering on 9 May, still kept as Liberation Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Channel Islands (Evacuation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 July 1940, vol. 362, cols. 1006–7, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/04/channel-islands-evacuation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"The Channel Islands,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 July 1940, vol. 116, cols. 835–54, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1940/jul/09/the-channel-islands. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Channel Islands,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 5 September 1940, vol. 365, col. 3, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/sep/05/channel-islands. [Open access]","Holocaust Memorial Day Trust, \"30 June 1940: Nazi occupation of the Channel Islands begins,\" https://www.hmd.org.uk/resource/30-june-1940-nazi-occupation-of-the-channel-islands-begins/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office, \"Review into Alderney camps determines truth around death toll during Nazi occupation,\" press release, 22 May 2024, https://www.gov.uk/government/news/review-into-alderney-camps-determines-truth-around-death-toll-during-nazi-occupation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"Victory in Europe,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1945, vol. 136, cols. 159–60, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1945/may/08/victory-in-europe. [Open access]","Frank Falla Archive, \"About the Archive,\" https://www.frankfallaarchive.org/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain not defend the islands?","a":"Because after the fall of France they lay a few miles off an enemy coast, and Churchill reluctantly decided to withdraw the troops and use them elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A member asked in the Commons whether it would not have been better to evacuate everyone and defend the islands; the Home Secretary would only say that was another matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were islanders killed when the Germans arrived?","a":"Because Britain had kept the demilitarisation secret, so the Germans attacked as if the islands were defended, and their bombing killed 44 people on Jersey and Guernsey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Alderney death toll need a new review?","a":"Because the figure was disputed. The count made after liberation was 389, while some claims ran to many thousands; the 2024 review set out to dispel conspiracy theories and gave a likely range of 641 to 1,027, with 1,134 as the most it thought possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-228-0326-34A%2C_Guernsey_-_Jersey%2C_Deutsche_Soldaten.jpg","title":"German soldiers in Saint Helier, Jersey, 1941","desc":"German soldiers in King Street, Saint Helier, during the occupation, photographed by a Wehrmacht propaganda company in August 1941.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-228-0326-34A, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-228-0326-34A,_Guernsey_-_Jersey,_Deutsche_Soldaten.jpg","alt":"German soldiers standing with a bicycle on a street corner outside a shop signed Edwards, Charing Cross Bazaar."},"wiki":"German occupation of the Channel Islands"},
    "ww2-200": {"questions":["The generals blamed parliament, the troops, the English and the fifth column, everyone but themselves, according to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an eyewitness account of 1940 published only in 1946.","A fuel officer of the French First Army wrote <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1940, and it appeared in 1946, 2 years after the Germans had shot him as a leader of the resistance around Lyon."],"abstract":"<b><i>Strange Defeat</i></b>, in French <i>L'Étrange Défaite</i>, is the account of the fall of France written in 1940 by Marc Bloch, a historian of the Middle Ages who had served in the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bloch was 53 when war came and asked to fight, and as the officer responsible for the First Army's fuel supply he saw an edifice he had thought solid prove rotten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He wrote the text at Guéret-Fougères in the Creuse between July and September 1940, and the manuscript was buried in a garden until after the Liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excluded from his post by the <i>Statut des Juifs</i>, he joined the resistance movement Franc-Tireur, was arrested by the Gestapo in Lyon in March 1944 and was shot with 29 others on 16 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It appeared in 1946 with a foreword by Georges Altman of Franc-Tireur, who called it the most lucid explanation and indictment of 1940 yet published in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Bloch began from what he heard around him: asked whose fault the defeat was, the generals answered the parliamentary regime, the troops, the English and the fifth column, everyone but themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own verdict was that whatever the deeper causes, the direct cause was the incapacity of the high command, whose errors were fundamentally those of a human group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rival explanations were already in the air: Pétain blamed an enemy superior in numbers and arms, a French government handout set 55 French divisions against 140 German ones, and de Gaulle blamed a bad military system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vichy put two former prime ministers, Daladier and Blum, on trial as those chiefly responsible for the defeat, but the proceedings were later halted because they were unpopular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Whether the civilian exodus contributed to the defeat is still debated, though a judgement shared by prominent French historians holds that the battle was already lost, and lost by the generals rather than by citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Marc Bloch, <i>L'Étrange Défaite : Témoignage écrit en 1940</i>, with Georges Altman's foreword to the original edition of 1946, electronic edition (Ebooks libres et gratuits, 2009), Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/l-etrange-defaite-fr-marc-bloch-1946. [in French] [Open access]","Laurent Douzou, \"Marc Bloch,\" <i>Les Chemins de la Mémoire</i>, no. 234 (March 2013), Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/personnalites/personnalites-lettre-lettre/marc-bloch. [in French] [Open access]","Bibliothèque nationale de France, \"Marc Bloch, L'Étrange défaite, 1940,\" <i>Médiathèque</i>, https://www.bnf.fr/fr/mediatheque/marc-bloch-letrange-defaite-1940. [in French] [Open access]","Robert O. Paxton, \"Inside the Panic,\" review of <i>Fleeing Hitler: France 1940</i>, by Hanna Diamond, <i>The New York Review of Books</i>, 22 November 2007, https://www.nybooks.com/articles/2007/11/22/inside-the-panic/.","Nadine Bonnefoi, \"Aux origines de la France Libre. L'Appel du 18 juin,\" <i>Les chemins de la mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/france-libre/aux. [in French] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Deputy Ambassador in France (Biddle) to the Secretary of State, June 26, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d237. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, \"Henri Philippe Pétain 1856–1951,\" <i>LeMO-Biografien, Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/henri-philippe-petain. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Bloch blame the high command rather than the soldiers?","a":"Because a commander answers for everything done under his orders. Bloch recalled Joffre saying that he did not know whether he had won the Marne, but that if it had been lost it would have been lost by him, and he held the generals of 1940 to the same rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Vichy prefer to blame the Republic?","a":"Because blaming the Republic justified replacing it. The regime tried Daladier and Blum as the men chiefly responsible for the defeat, and it exploited the refugees' sense of having been abandoned by the authorities to build support for its own authoritarian order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the book appear only in 1946?","a":"Because it could not be published under the occupation. The manuscript was hidden in a garden, its author was a Jew excluded from his post and later a resistance leader, and he was shot in June 1944; Franc-Tireur published it after the Liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Marc_Bloch_%281886-1944%29.jpg","title":"Identity photograph of the author, 1930s","desc":"An identity photograph of the historian who wrote the book, taken in the 1930s.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marc_Bloch_(1886-1944).jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders photograph of a balding man with round spectacles and a moustache, wearing a suit and striped tie."},"wiki":"Strange Defeat"},
    "ww2-181": {"questions":["Jodl told the Nuremberg tribunal that during the Polish campaign some 110 divisions of the British and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had stood idle in the west against only 23 German ones.","Churchill told the Commons on 4 June 1940 that the great <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been cast back and disturbed by the onrush of a few thousand armoured vehicles."],"abstract":"The <b>French Army</b> was the land force of the Third Republic, and in 1940 it met the German invasion of the West with far more artillery than its enemy and slightly more tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After mobilisation in 1939 it counted about 2,680,000 men, organised in 75 infantry divisions, 13 fortress divisions, 5 light cavalry divisions and 40 tank battalions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its doctrine had turned defensive in the 1930s, when the memory of a costly victory in 1918 led the government to build the Maginot Line rather than the tank and mobile divisions that junior officers such as Charles de Gaulle urged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tanks were treated as a support arm for the infantry rather than a weapon of attack, and between 1923 and 1939 only 0.15 per cent of the military budget went on telecommunications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the Polish campaign of 1939, the German general Alfred Jodl later told the Nuremberg tribunal, about 110 French and British divisions in the west had been held inactive against 23 German ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> When the attack came, the defence minister telephoned the prime minister at six in the morning of 15 May to say that the troops near Sedan could not hold out against the masses of tanks and aircraft launched against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Behind the breach, the British official history records, retreating units, refugees and reinforcements crossed one another's paths until orders no longer reached those who were to carry them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 18 May the prime minister, Paul Reynaud, was telling the American ambassador that France was inferior to Germany in both soldiers and material, and on 4 June Marshal Pétain spoke to him of a threefold German superiority in manpower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 4 June that the great French Army had been very largely cast back and disturbed by the onrush of a few thousand armoured vehicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The campaign ended with that army destroyed and an estimated two million French, British, Dutch and Belgian soldiers taken prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Ioannis-Dionysios Salavrakos, “An Economic Analysis of the Fall of France in June 1940 Based on the Theory of New Institutional Economics,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 19, no. 4 (2019): 51–55, 64, https://doi.org/10.55016/ad1dxt54. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 4 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-04-46.asp. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 2, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 318–19, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 4 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, May 18, 1940—10 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 189, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d189. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, May 15, 1940—noon,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 180, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d180. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, June 4, 1940—4 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 202, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d202. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did France fortify its frontier instead of building armoured divisions?","a":"Because its doctrine had become defensive. The costly victory of 1918 weighed on the general staff, the Maginot Line was voted in 1930 and far overran its budget, and the junior officers who wanted the money spent on tank and mobile divisions, de Gaulle among them, were pushed aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did more guns and tanks not save the army?","a":"Because numbers were not the whole of it. Its tanks were spread among the infantry rather than massed for attack, almost nothing had been spent on communications, and once the front broke at Sedan orders stopped reaching the units meant to carry them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Jodl dwell on the idle divisions of 1939?","a":"Because it served his defence at Nuremberg. He argued that Germany could crush Poland alone but could never have withstood a concentrated attack in the west, and that it escaped collapse in 1939 only because the French and British divisions there did nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/French-troops-have-destroyed-a-bridge-over-Saar-391767937365.jpg","title":"French troops at a demolished bridge, November 1939","desc":"A press photograph of 8 November 1939 showing a bridge near the Saar front blown up by French troops during the first winter of the war.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Åhlén & Åkerlund, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French-troops-have-destroyed-a-bridge-over-Saar-391767937365.jpg","alt":"The wreck of a bridge lying in a river below damaged houses and bare trees."},"wiki":"French Army"},
    "ww2-182": {"questions":["A directive from General <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 19 May 1940 told Georges that it was all a question of hours, but Weygand took over the supreme command the next day.","Holding that a supreme commander's role was advisory once battle was joined, General <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took no further part in the northern campaign after ordering the advance to the Dyle."],"abstract":"<b>Maurice Gamelin</b> was the French general under whose supreme command the Allied armies in France fought until he was replaced in the middle of the German offensive of May 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With Maxime Weygand he had led the French Army through the 1930s, from January 1938 he was also Chief of the Staff of National Defence, and in September 1939 he went to London to discuss the front the British Expeditionary Force would hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In October 1939 he described to the British chief of the imperial general staff an attack through the Ardennes and across the Meuse that was almost exactly the one the Germans later made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet his final conclusion, the British official history records, was that an attack in force through the Ardennes was impracticable, and the comparatively weak French Ninth Army stood opposite it as the hinge of the Allied advance into Belgium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also sent the French Seventh Army on an unsupported advance into Holland, which wasted a valuable reserve and did little to delay the Dutch defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Once he had ordered the advance to the Dyle, he took no further part in the northern campaign, treating the battle as the business of General Georges and the role of a supreme commander as purely advisory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the night of 15 May he was telephoning the defence minister with news that proved true: the line south of Namur had collapsed and German motorised units were pouring through the gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 19 May he handed Georges a directive that disclaimed any wish to interfere, proposed striking at the rear of the German armour and ended that it was all a question of hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The prime minister, Paul Reynaud, had already decided to replace him, Weygand took over on 20 May, and no fresh action was taken on Gamelin's directive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 4 June only that Weygand had assumed command in place of Gamelin once the force of the German breakthrough was realised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 316–19, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Ioannis-Dionysios Salavrakos, “An Economic Analysis of the Fall of France in June 1940 Based on the Theory of New Institutional Economics,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 19, no. 4 (2019): 50–51, 70, https://doi.org/10.55016/ad1dxt54. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, May 16, 1940—11 a.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 153, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d153. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 113–14, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 4 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","General the Viscount Gort, “Despatches on the Operations of the British Expeditionary Force, France and Belgium, 1939–40,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 35305, 17 October 1941, 5900, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/35305/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Gamelin's dispositions help the German breakthrough?","a":"Because the strength was in the wrong places. The weak Ninth Army formed the hinge of the advance opposite the Ardennes, where the main blow fell, while the Seventh Army, which could have been the reserve, was spent in Holland to no useful end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his last directive change nothing?","a":"Because it came too late and too timidly. It opened by saying he did not wish to interfere, he was replaced the next day, and Weygand spent his first days studying the situation before issuing his own order on 22 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the British official history still defend the advance to the Dyle?","a":"Because Belgium had been promised help and the advance kept it fighting. It took the Germans 18 days to break Belgian resistance, and staying on the French frontier would not obviously have saved the northern armies from being cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Maurice_Gamelin.jpg","title":"General Maurice Gamelin, about 1939–1940","desc":"A French press photograph of the commander-in-chief of the French Army in the uniform of a general, taken around the time of the Phoney War.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Gamelin.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of an elderly officer with a moustache, wearing a peaked cap with oak-leaf embroidery and rows of medal ribbons."},"wiki":"Maurice Gamelin"},
    "ww2-183": {"questions":["Seizing the bridges at Moerdijk, Dordrecht and Rotterdam by parachute was Göring's plan for opening a back door in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was over in 5 days.","The Nuremberg tribunal found no evidence that Britain and France had planned to march on the Ruhr, and judged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> plainly an act of aggressive war."],"abstract":"The <b>German invasion of the Netherlands</b> began on 10 May 1940 and was over in 5 days, for on 14 May the commander of the Dutch army ordered his troops to cease fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler had told his commanders in May 1939 that Dutch and Belgian air bases must be occupied and declarations of neutrality ignored, while assuring the Netherlands that its neutrality would be respected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the day of the attack German ambassadors handed over a memorandum claiming that Britain and France, with Dutch and Belgian consent, were preparing to march through the Low Countries against the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found no evidence for that claim and judged the invasion entirely without justification and plainly an act of aggressive war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shortly after four in the morning anti-aircraft and machine-gun fire broke out in The Hague, and the Dutch Foreign Office told the American minister that the country considered itself at war and had appealed to France and Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Within 2 hours the defence ministry reported attacks along the whole eastern frontier, bombs on every airport and parachute troops trying to land near Delft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Göring told the tribunal that he had proposed dropping a parachute division behind the Dutch forces to seize the bridges at Moerdijk, Dordrecht and Rotterdam, opening a back door into the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Airborne troops covered by heavy bombing and followed by tanks got behind defences built to resist a frontal attack, and the French Seventh Army, sent north to help, could not change the outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Queen Wilhelmina reached England on 13 May, and her government told Washington that although the army had laid down its arms in most of the country, the Netherlands remained at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The German high command announced on 14 May that after the capitulation of Rotterdam the Dutch command had given up purposeless resistance, though fighting continued in Zeeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, “The Invasion of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxemburg,” in <i>Judgment of the International Military Tribunal</i>, 1 October 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judbelg.asp. [Open access]","“The Minister in the Netherlands (Gordon) to the Secretary of State, The Hague, May 10, 1940—5 a.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 133, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d133. [Open access]","“The Minister in the Netherlands (Gordon) to the Secretary of State, The Hague, May 10, 1940—6 a.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 134, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d134. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 9, proceedings of 15 March 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-15-46.asp. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 40, 49–50, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","“The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, Berlin, May 14, 1940—midnight,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 151, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d151. [Open access]","“The Netherlands Minister (Loudon) to the Secretary of State, Washington, May 16, 1940,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 156, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d156. [Open access]","“The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy), Washington, May 18, 1940—7 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 157, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d157. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany attack a neutral country?","a":"Because its planners wanted Dutch and Belgian air bases and feared for the Ruhr. Hitler told his commanders in November 1939 that the Ruhr was Germany's Achilles heel, that breaking Dutch and Belgian neutrality was meaningless, and that no one would question it once Germany had won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Dutch defences fail so quickly?","a":"Because the attack came from behind as well as in front. Parachute troops seized the great bridges in the rear while bombers struck the airfields, so positions built to meet a frontal attack were turned before the army could use them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government insist the Netherlands was still at war?","a":"Because only the army in the home country had laid down its arms. The queen and her ministers were in London, and the navy and the forces in unoccupied territory were to go on fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1998-043-06A%2C_Maasbr%C3%BCcke_bei_Rotterdam.jpg","title":"The Moerdijk bridges, 13 May 1940","desc":"A German photograph of the bridge over the Hollands Diep at Moerdijk, one of the crossings seized by German parachute troops in the first days of the invasion; the archive's caption wrongly calls it a Maas bridge at Rotterdam.","credit":"Jäger, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1998-043-06A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1998-043-06A,_Maasbr%C3%BCcke_bei_Rotterdam.jpg","alt":"The roadway of a long steel girder bridge seen from one end, with vehicles in the distance."},"wiki":"German invasion of the Netherlands"},
    "ww2-184": {"questions":["Red flares turned two German bomber groups back, Göring claimed, but the first group dropped its bombs and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the old centre of the city burning.","About 800 people died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the city archive's estimate, and its research has now put names to 705 of the civilian victims of 14 May 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Rotterdam Blitz</b> was the German bombing of the centre of Rotterdam in the early afternoon of 14 May 1940, the fifth day of the invasion of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German parachute troops had seized the bridges over the river in the city, but the few who had crossed to the north bank were surrounded, under heavy fire and without artillery of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Göring later told the Nuremberg tribunal that he had promised his parachutists bomber support and ordered one unit of at most 36 aircraft to bomb the Dutch positions north of the bridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Surrender talks for the city were already under way, and the British prosecutor put it to Kesselring that written terms had been handed to a Dutch officer 35 minutes before the bombing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German troops on the ground fired red flares, and by Göring's account the first group of bombers missed their meaning and bombed, while the two groups that followed turned back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Fire then spread through the old city, and Göring blamed burning fats and oils and the city's fire brigade, rather than the bombs, for most of the destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The prosecution argued that the aim had been to terrorise the Dutch into surrender, while Kesselring insisted that the attack served only the tactical needs of the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of the victims have ranged from 800 to 1,400; the city archive puts the dead at about 800, nearly half of them unnamed for years, and has now identified 705 civilian victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That night the German high command announced that after the capitulation of Rotterdam the Dutch command had ordered its troops to stop fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British official history calls the raid an overwhelming demonstration of German air power that left most of the business heart of the city in ruins, and records that German commanders later expected the same air force to close the ports of Dunkirk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 9, proceedings of 13 March 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-13-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 9, proceedings of 15 March 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-15-46.asp. [Open access]","Stadsarchief Rotterdam, “De doden van 14 mei,” https://stadsarchief.rotterdam.nl/de-doden-van-14-mei. [in Dutch] [Open access]","“The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, Berlin, May 14, 1940—midnight,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 151, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d151. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 49, 347, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Campaign in France, 10 May 1940 – 30 October 1940</i>, trans. Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (US) (1947), 2, https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","Stadsarchief Rotterdam, “Slachtoffers bombardement,” https://stadsarchief.rotterdam.nl/slachtoffers-bombardement. [in Dutch] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a city in surrender talks bombed at all?","a":"That is still the question. Göring and Kesselring said the call for bombers came from trapped parachutists and that there was no direct radio link to stop the aircraft; the prosecution answered that the attack could easily have been called off and was meant to terrorise the Dutch into giving up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the destruction so great?","a":"Because fire did more than the bombs. Göring himself said that stone and concrete buildings were left standing while the older houses burned, and that the flames were fed by large stocks of fats and oils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll still uncertain?","a":"Because for years nearly half of the dead had no names. The city archive gives about 800 deaths as an estimate, and only recent research has identified 705 civilian victims by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Bombardement_Rotterdam%2C_Bestanddeelnr_120-0455.jpg","title":"Rotterdam after the bombing, May 1940","desc":"A photograph from the Dutch government information service of the ruined centre of Rotterdam after the German air attack of 14 May 1940.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Anefo, Nationaal Archief, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bombardement_Rotterdam,_Bestanddeelnr_120-0455.jpg","alt":"Gutted shells of buildings and heaps of rubble in a city street, with groups of people working among the ruins."},"wiki":"German bombing of Rotterdam"},
    "ww2-185": {"questions":["An emissary sent by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the German lines on the evening of 27 May 1940 to ask for armistice terms and was told only unconditional surrender would do.","Churchill told the Commons on 4 June that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had surrendered his army without consulting his ministers, exposing the whole flank and line of retreat of the Allies."],"abstract":"<b>Leopold III</b> was King of the Belgians from 1934, and as commander of the Belgian army he surrendered it to Germany on 28 May 1940 against the decision of his government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The son of Albert I, he had steered Belgium from 1936 towards a policy of independence and then neutrality as the threat of a new European war grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Germany invaded on 10 May he appealed to President Roosevelt, declaring that Belgium, brutally attacked by a country bound to it by the most solemn engagements, would defend herself with all her strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He directed the military operations himself, and it took the Germans 18 days to break Belgian resistance on the left of the Allied line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the evening of 27 May a Belgian emissary crossed the German lines to ask what terms an armistice would carry, and the German reply was unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Belgian army ceased to resist at four the next morning, leaving a gap of 20 miles between the British left and the coast near Nieuport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That afternoon the prime minister, Hubert Pierlot, broadcast from France that the king had opened separate negotiations against the unanimous decision of his government, and that the act had no legal value because no royal act was valid without a minister's signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Belgian envoys in Washington said the king was now a prisoner who could not exercise his rights, and that his excuse was an army without rations and almost without ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill first asked the Commons to suspend judgment, but on 4 June he condemned a surrender made without prior consultation, without the advice of ministers and on the king's own personal act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Leopold stayed in occupied Belgium, was taken to Germany in 1944, and after years of the crisis known as the 'Royal Question' passed his powers to his son Baudouin in 1950 and abdicated in July 1951.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“The King of the Belgians (Leopold III) to President Roosevelt, Brussels, May 10, 1940,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 142, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d142. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 199–201, 317, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 28 May 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 421–22, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/28/war-situation. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 4 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, May 28, 1940,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 166, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d166. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Secretary of State (Berle), Washington, May 28, 1940,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 167, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d167. [Open access]","Université de Liège, “Leopold III (1901–1983),” <i>Reflexions</i> (2007), https://www.reflexions.uliege.be/cms/c_15144/en/leopold-iii-1901-1983. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government say the surrender had no legal force?","a":"Because under the Belgian constitution every power came from the nation and no act of the king took effect without a minister's countersignature. The ministers had voted unanimously against capitulation, so Pierlot declared the act void and the government the only legal authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the surrender alarm the British so much?","a":"Because the Belgian army had been guarding the eastern flank of the retreat to the sea. When it stopped fighting, the British had to cover a flank of more than 30 miles at the shortest notice to keep the road to Dunkirk open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Leopold's conduct become a crisis after the war?","a":"Because the surrender, his decision to stay in occupied Belgium and his wartime remarriage divided the country. A 1950 referendum went his way overall but split Flanders from Wallonia and Brussels, and the unrest that followed led him to hand power to his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/L%C3%A9opold_III-1940-revue-01.jpg","title":"The King of the Belgians reviewing tanks, May 1940","desc":"Leopold III, with the Belgian war minister General Denis at his left, reviewing a column of tanks, as published in <i>L'Illustration</i> of 18 May 1940.","credit":"Photographer unknown, <i>L'Illustration</i>, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9opold_III-1940-revue-01.jpg","alt":"A tall officer in a trench coat walking along a road beside another officer, with a light tank and soldiers behind them."},"wiki":"Leopold III of Belgium"},
    "ww2-186": {"questions":["Halder noted on 22 May 1940 that the armoured drive on Calais had been halted by Army Group A until the situation created by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was clear.","Gort ordered Frankforce to pull back on the evening of 23 May, 2 days after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because German pressure from the Lorette ridge was hemming it in."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Arras</b> was the British attack south of Arras on 21 May 1940 against the flank of the German armoured thrust that had cut the Allied armies in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That thrust, Churchill told the Commons, had severed all communications between the northern armies and the main French forces and shorn its way up the coast towards Boulogne and Calais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Gort's order to Major-General Franklyn on 20 May was only to support the Arras garrison and block the roads south of the town, but at his headquarters it came to be seen as the first stage of a larger Anglo-French counter-attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attacking force was in the end not two divisions but two battalions, with 58 Mark I and 16 Mark II infantry tanks, many in urgent need of overhaul after long road marches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gort's despatch records that a French light mechanised division co-operated on the flank, though less widely than Franklyn had hoped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Attacking at two in the afternoon, the two columns took several villages and some 400 prisoners, ran into the German 7th Armoured Division and the SS Totenkopf Division, and were ordered back that evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 7th Armoured Division's war diary admitted the loss of nine medium and several light tanks and 378 men, and its commander, Rommel, described a very heavy battle against hundreds of enemy tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Halder recorded on 22 May that the armoured drive on Calais had been halted until the situation at Arras was clear, and on 23 May that Kleist felt unable to go on while the crisis there lasted, with tank losses as high as 50 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Frankforce held its ground until the evening of 23 May, when Gort, seeing it dangerously hemmed in, ordered it to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history concludes that the attacks around Arras underlined for Rundstedt the need to close up his mobile formations, one of the reasons for the halt he ordered on the canal line that evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 87–97, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","General the Viscount Gort, “Despatches on the Operations of the British Expeditionary Force, France and Belgium, 1939–40,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 35305, 17 October 1941, 5917–18, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/35305/data.pdf. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Campaign in France, 10 May 1940 – 30 October 1940</i>, trans. Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (US) (1947), 24–32, https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 138, 347–48, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 4 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, May 18, 1940—10 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 189, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d189. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was so small a force sent?","a":"Because there was nothing else to send. Gort's divisions were committed on the main front, and what began as an order to support the Arras garrison grew in others' minds into a counter-attack without ever being given more troops or new orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack worry the German command more than its size deserved?","a":"Because heavy British tanks shrugged off the German anti-tank guns, and Rommel's division believed it faced hundreds of tanks. Kleist reported tank losses of up to half and felt he could not go on while Arras remained unresolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Frankforce have to give up Arras?","a":"Because the tanks were wearing out and German forces were working round its right flank from the high ground of the Lorette ridge. With only two roads left for escape, Gort withdrew it eastwards on the night of 23 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/IWM-F-2141-Matilda.jpg","title":"A 4th Royal Tank Regiment Matilda in France, January 1940","desc":"The driver of a Mark I infantry tank of the 4th Royal Tank Regiment, one of the two tank battalions in the attack at Arras, photographed by a War Office official photographer.","credit":"L. A. Puttnam, War Office official photographer, Imperial War Museum F 2141, public domain (Crown copyright expired), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IWM-F-2141-Matilda.jpg","alt":"A soldier in a balaclava sitting at the open driver's hatch of a small armoured tank with a name painted on its front."},"wiki":"Battle of Arras (1940)"},
    "ww2-187": {"questions":["Pétain complained to the American ambassador that during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the British had insisted on taking their own army off first while French divisions held the line.","Churchill warned the Commons that wars are not won by evacuations, even as he reported that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had carried over 335,000 men to England."],"abstract":"The <b>Dunkirk evacuation</b>, code-named Operation Dynamo, took British and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Allies_of_World_War_II\">Allied</span> troops off the port and beaches of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Dunkirk\">Dunkirk</span> between 26 May and 4 June 1940, the last port left to the armies trapped in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Admiralty_(United_Kingdom)\">Admiralty</span> ordered Dynamo to begin at 6.57 in the evening of 26 May, hoping to lift up to 45,000 men of the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"British_Expeditionary_Force_(World_War_II)\">British Expeditionary Force</span> in 2 days before enemy action ended it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill had told the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"House_of_Commons_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Commons</span> to prepare for hard and heavy tidings, and later admitted he had feared announcing the greatest military disaster in British history, with perhaps 20,000 or 30,000 men saved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German leaders, like the British Admiralty, thought no large evacuation from open beaches possible, and only on 26 May did Hitler allow the advance on Dunkirk to stop it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Warships and civilian craft worked from the harbour and from 10 miles of beach to the east, and about a quarter of the troops were carried in ships manned by the Merchant Navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the operation ended on 4 June, the British official history counts 338,226 men evacuated, nearly 100,000 of them from the beaches and almost 140,000 of them French and other Allied soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill gave the figure of over 335,000 men carried by nearly 1,000 ships of all kinds, and warned that wars are not won by evacuations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The price was heavy, with 228 ships lost, 177 RAF aircraft destroyed or damaged and nearly all the army's guns and transport left in France, while Kesselring later said bad weather had grounded his aircraft for about 2 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> French troops held the western part of the bridgehead almost to the end and covered the last 2 days of embarkation, and to Frenchmen, the official history notes, Dunkirk meant something different.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 4 June Marshal Pétain told the American ambassador that the British had insisted on taking their own army off first while French divisions held the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bertram H. Ramsay, “The Evacuation of the Allied Armies from Dunkirk and Neighbouring Beaches,” despatch of 18 June 1940, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38017, 17 July 1947, 3295–96, 3299, 3311, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38017/data.pdf. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 246–48, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 4 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Campaign in France, 10 May 1940 – 30 October 1940</i>, trans. Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (US) (1947), 36–37, https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, June 4, 1940—4 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 202, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d202. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 2, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 28 May 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 421–22, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/28/war-situation. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 347, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 9, proceedings of 13 March 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-13-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many more men get away than anyone planned for?","a":"Because the perimeter held for 9 days instead of two and the weather was kind. The fighting troops kept the enemy off while the navy brought home not 45,000 men but more than a third of a million, about 100,000 of them lifted straight off the beaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the French remember it differently?","a":"Because for many of their soldiers it meant leaving France rather than going home, and because French troops held the bridgehead while others embarked. Pétain, within days, was accusing the British of saving their own army first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill refuse to call it a victory?","a":"Because the army had escaped but its equipment had not. Nearly 1,000 guns and all the transport and armoured vehicles of the army in the north were lost, and he reminded the House that wars are not won by evacuations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Dunkirk_26-29_May_1940_NYP68075.jpg","title":"Troops waiting on the beach at Dunkirk, May 1940","desc":"British soldiers queue in long lines across the sands for the boats, between 26 and 29 May 1940.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Imperial War Museum NYP 68075, public domain (Crown copyright expired), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunkirk_26-29_May_1940_NYP68075.jpg","alt":"Long winding lines of soldiers stretching across a wide sandy beach towards the sea, seen from the dunes."},"wiki":"Dunkirk evacuation","places":["pl:q911972"]},
    "ww2-188": {"questions":["Divisional commanders took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Hitler's own interference because Rundstedt reissued his decision of 23 May under the words 'by the Führer's orders'.","Halder complained that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stopped the armoured left wing dead in its tracks and left the finishing off of the encircled enemy to the air force."],"abstract":"The <b>halt order</b> stopped the German armoured divisions on the canal line between Gravelines and Lens from 24 to 26 May 1940, after they had won bridgeheads across it at Aire, Saint-Omer and Watten, while the Allied armies fell back towards Dunkirk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It began as a decision of Rundstedt, commanding Army Group A, whose Fourth Army ordered on the evening of 23 May that the armoured groups of Kleist and Hoth would halt next day and close up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rundstedt judged that the trapped Allies would be finished within days, while his armour had suffered heavily and would soon be needed for the offensive to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler visited Rundstedt on 24 May, agreed entirely with the halt, insisted that the armour be conserved, and countermanded Brauchitsch's order placing it under Army Group B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rundstedt then reissued the order in Hitler's name, and divisional commanders who saw only the words 'by the Führer's orders' later cited it as proof of Hitler's interference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At army headquarters Halder wrote that the armoured left wing, with no enemy before it, had been stopped dead in its tracks on the Führer's direct orders, leaving the encircled enemy to the air force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 25 May Hitler left the decision to Army Group A, which declined to move, and it was Hitler who on 26 May authorised the advance on Dunkirk to prevent further evacuations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind the pause lay the belief that the Luftwaffe could close the evacuation ports, as it had wrecked Warsaw and Rotterdam, and no German leader thought a large evacuation from open beaches possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kesselring, who commanded an air fleet over Dunkirk, told the Nuremberg tribunal that bad weather had grounded his aircraft for about 2 days, though the British admiral in charge of the evacuation found the weather extremely good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The British official history doubts that a different order would have changed the outcome, and Churchill credited the defenders of Calais with drawing off two armoured divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 138–39, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 347–51, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Campaign in France, 10 May 1940 – 30 October 1940</i>, trans. Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (US) (1947), 33–37, https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 9, proceedings of 13 March 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-13-46.asp. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons debate, 4 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","General the Viscount Gort, “Despatches on the Operations of the British Expeditionary Force, France and Belgium, 1939–40,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 35305, 17 October 1941, 5921–22, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/35305/data.pdf. [Open access]","Bertram H. Ramsay, “The Evacuation of the Allied Armies from Dunkirk and Neighbouring Beaches,” despatch of 18 June 1940, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38017, 17 July 1947, 3295–96, 3299, 3311, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38017/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rundstedt want to halt his armour?","a":"Because he thought the battle in the north was already won and his tanks were worn and scattered. He had five armies spread over a huge area, French attacks threatening his southern flank and an offensive across the Somme to come, so closing up on the canal seemed the prudent course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Hitler blamed for it?","a":"Because the order went out in his name. Rundstedt reissued his own decision as 'by the Führer's orders', and after the war generals who had seen only those words, and who wanted to clear themselves of letting the British escape, laid the whole decision on Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans not expect the evacuation to succeed?","a":"Because they trusted the air force to close the ports, as it had shown it could at Warsaw and Rotterdam, and no one believed a large army could be taken off open beaches. Kesselring later said bad weather had grounded his aircraft for about 2 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-189": {"questions":["Ciano declared war on France and Britain on 10 June 1940, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ordered only on 19 June, over Badoglio’s objections, and stalled at the first French fortification.","Starace reported that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had sent men to a useless death 2 days before the armistice signed at the Villa Incisa on 24 June 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Italian invasion of France</b> was the offensive Mussolini launched in the Alps in June 1940, after declaring war on France and Britain while the French armies were already collapsing before the Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Through the spring Roosevelt had urged Mussolini to keep the war from spreading to the Mediterranean, offering to pass Italy's claims on to the British and French governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 1 June Ciano told the American ambassador that the decision to enter the war had already been taken and that Mussolini meant to honour his alliance with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French ambassador in Rome was told that Mussolini had no interest in receiving French territory by peaceful negotiation, because he had decided to make war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the afternoon of 10 June Ciano received the French and British ambassadors in turn and declared that Italy considered itself at war with both countries from 11 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In Paris Reynaud remarked on what admirable people the Italians were to stab France in the back at such a moment, and Roosevelt declared the same day that the hand that held the dagger had struck it into the back of its neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In London Attlee told the Commons that Mussolini was playing the jackal, hoping for scraps from another beast's kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mussolini ordered the attack in the Alps on 19 June over the objections of his chief of staff, Badoglio, and by Ciano's diary the troops had still not advanced on 21 June beyond the first French fortification that resisted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An armistice was signed at the Villa Incisa outside Rome at 7:15 PM on 24 June, and the Franco-German armistice, which waited on it, took effect early the next morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Starace came back from the front to report, in Ciano's words, the total unpreparedness of the army and men sent to a useless death 2 days before the armistice with the methods of 20 years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“The Ambassador in Italy (Phillips) to the Secretary of State, Rome, June 1, 1940—1 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 831, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d831. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, June 3, 1940—9 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 834, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d834. [Open access]","“The Italian Ambassador (Colonna) to the Secretary of State, Washington, June 11, 1940,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 835, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d835. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, June 10, 1940—2 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 207, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d207. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Address at University of Virginia,” 10 June 1940, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-university-virginia. [Open access]","Clement Attlee, “Italy's Decision,” House of Commons debate, 11 June 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, vol. 361, cols. 1163–69, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/11/italys-decision. [Open access]","Galeazzo Ciano, <i>The Ciano Diaries, 1939–1943</i>, ed. Hugh Gibson (Doubleday, 1946; repr. Simon Publications, 2001), 263–70, https://archive.org/details/GibsonHughTheCianoDiaries19391943TheCompleteUnabridgedDiariesOfCountGaleazzoCian. [Open access]","“The Chargé in Germany (Heath) to the Secretary of State, Berlin, June 24, 1940—11 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 236, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d236. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What reasons were given for Italy's entry into the war?","a":"Mussolini told Washington that he was bound by his alliance with Germany and wanted his freedom of action, and he refused any peaceful settlement of his claims on France. In London Attlee called it the argument of the jackal, scenting scraps from another beast's kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Alpine offensive achieve so little?","a":"Because it was launched in a hurry and badly prepared. Badoglio opposed it, a general was overheard saying he was absolutely unready to attack, and Starace reported a total lack of offensive means and of capable senior officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Mussolini want the attack before the armistice?","a":"Because he wanted to reach the armistice after a victory of his own forces. Ciano records his bitterness that the war had been won by Hitler without real Italian participation, which left him little say over the terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Mussolini_DOW_10_June_1940.jpg","title":"The declaration of war from the Palazzo Venezia, 10 June 1940","desc":"A press photograph of Mussolini on the balcony of the Palazzo Venezia in Rome as he announced Italy's declaration of war on France and Britain.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in Italy, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mussolini_DOW_10_June_1940.jpg","alt":"A man in a dark uniform and cap speaking from a stone balcony with carved balusters."},"wiki":"Italian invasion of France"},
    "ww2-190": {"questions":["The 1907 Hague rule that undefended towns may not be attacked or bombarded is what a government relies on when it declares a capital an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than fight for it.","Bullitt reported that the German occupation passed without disorder or loss of life, after Paris had been made an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ahead of the entry of 14 June 1940."],"abstract":"An <b>open city</b> is a town that its defenders give up without a fight, relying on the rule of the 1907 Hague regulations that towns, villages and buildings which are undefended may not be attacked or bombarded by any means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same regulations required an attacking commander to do all in his power to warn the authorities before a bombardment, and forbade the pillage even of a town taken by assault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In June 1940 the declaration was made for Paris, as German tanks crossed the Seine at two points and the battle moved towards the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 10 June most government offices and foreign missions had left for western France, although Reynaud still promised to fight in front of Paris and behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roosevelt advised his ambassador, William C. Bullitt, to follow the government if other envoys did, but left the choice to him and asked him to do what he could, as a red-blooded American, to save human life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 13 June Bullitt's message reached Berlin by way of Bern: Paris had been declared an open city, General Héring was withdrawing the army that had defended it, and General Dentz remained in command with only gendarmes and police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Police, gendarmes and firemen stayed at their posts to protect lives and property, and Bullitt, who remained with part of his staff, offered to help the city change hands without loss of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German state secretary, Weizsäcker, received the message coldly, objecting that it came neither from the American government nor from the French one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> German troops entered Paris on 14 June, the day the French government moved on to Bordeaux and Weygand told a British general that organised resistance by the French armies as a whole was at an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next day the German military authorities passed on Bullitt's report that the occupation of Paris had taken place entirely without disorder or loss of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex: Regulations Concerning the Laws and Customs of War on Land, The Hague, 18 October 1907, arts. 25–28, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), 298, https://archive.org/details/WarInFranceAndFlanderswithMaps. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, June 10, 1940—7 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 499, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d499. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in France (Bullitt) to the Secretary of State, Paris, June 10, 1940—2 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 207, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d207. [Open access]","“The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in France (Bullitt), Washington, June 11, 1940—9 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 500, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d500. [Open access]","“The Chargé in Germany (Heath) to the Secretary of State, Berlin, June 13, 1940—4 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 501, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d501. [Open access]","“The Chargé in Germany (Heath) to the Secretary of State, Berlin, June 13, 1940—9 p.m.,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General and Europe, Volume II</i>, document 503, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d503. [Open access]","“The Chargé in Germany (Heath) to the Secretary of State, Berlin, June 15, 1940,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, document 221, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d221. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why declare a city open instead of defending it?","a":"Because an undefended town may not lawfully be attacked or bombarded. Giving up the defence of Paris was a way to spare its people and buildings once the army could no longer hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the message go through the American embassy in Berlin?","a":"Because Bullitt had no radio or cable link with the United States and the French government had left. He telephoned the American legation in Bern, which passed the message to the embassy in Berlin for the German government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Weizsäcker receive it so coldly?","a":"Because it was not an official communication. He objected that it came neither from Washington nor from the French government, and questioned how an ambassador could speak for the diplomatic corps or for 'the government of the city'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-126-0350-26A%2C_Paris%2C_Einmarsch%2C_Parade_deutscher_Truppen.jpg","title":"German troops parade in Paris, June 1940","desc":"Mounted German soldiers riding through an empty Paris street during the parade after the city was occupied in June 1940, photographed by a German propaganda-company photographer.","credit":"Heinz Fremke, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-126-0350-26A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-126-0350-26A,_Paris,_Einmarsch,_Parade_deutscher_Truppen.jpg","alt":"Helmeted soldiers on horseback riding in loose formation down a broad tree-lined street between tall apartment buildings."},"wiki":"Open city"},
    "ww2-161": {"questions":["Molotov told the German ambassador that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was needed to end British and French intrigue, naming Dekanozov, Vyshinsky and Zhdanov as the emissaries sent to form new governments.","Elections without opposition candidates, held in July 1940 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, produced assemblies that asked to join the Soviet Union, and the Supreme Soviet admitted Lithuania on 3 August."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet occupation of the Baltic states</b> was the seizure of Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia by the Soviet Union in June 1940 and their incorporation into it as Soviet republics that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The way had been opened by the secret protocol to the Nazi-Soviet pact of August 1939, which drew the boundary between the German and Soviet spheres of influence at the northern border of Lithuania, leaving Estonia and Latvia on the Soviet side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second secret protocol, signed in Moscow on 28 September 1939, moved Lithuania itself into the Soviet sphere in exchange for the province of Lublin and part of the province of Warsaw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lithuania then signed a pact of mutual assistance with Moscow on 10 October 1939, which also transferred to it the city of Vilno, now Vilnius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In June 1940 Moscow accused Lithuania of having joined Latvia and Estonia since then in a military alliance aimed against the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Lithuania accepted a Soviet ultimatum on the morning of 15 June, and Soviet armoured divisions crossed its border at several points that afternoon, with demands for a new government, the trial of two security chiefs and larger garrisons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ultimatums to Estonia and Latvia followed the next day and were accepted at once, again demanding new governments and the entry of more Soviet forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Molotov told the German ambassador that it had become necessary to end British and French intrigue in the region, and named Dekanozov, Vyshinsky and Zhdanov as the special emissaries sent to form the new governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the Estonian elections of July no opposition candidates stood, and the official figures gave a turnout of 81½ per cent, with 93 per cent of the ballots marked for the new government's candidates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 3 August the Supreme Soviet admitted Lithuania as a constituent republic at the request of its new parliament, and Latvia and Estonia were admitted in the days that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["\"Secret Additional Protocol, August 23, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/addsepro.asp. [Open access]","\"Secret Supplementary Protocol, September 28, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/sesupp.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, June 15, 1940, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d364. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Lithuania (Norem) to the Secretary of State, June 15, 1940, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d363. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, June 17, 1940, 2 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d370. [Open access]","\"The German Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Schulenburg) to the German Foreign Office, June 18, 1940,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns145.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Estonia (Leonard) to the Secretary of State, July 17, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d400. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, August 4, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d421. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany raise no objection to the Soviet move?","a":"Because it had already conceded the region in secret: the protocol of August 1939 put Estonia and Latvia on the Soviet side of the line, and the protocol of September 1939 added Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Molotov told the German ambassador of the action only after it had begun, having opened the same conversation with the warmest congratulations on the splendid success of the German armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the July elections settle nothing?","a":"Because there was nothing to choose between. In Estonia no opposition stood, so every one of the new government's candidates in the 80 districts was elected, and the same week the American chargé was told in confidence that annexation was imminent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"How quickly did independence end?","a":"In 7 weeks. The first ultimatum was accepted on 15 June and Soviet armour crossed into Lithuania that afternoon; on 3 August the Supreme Soviet passed the law admitting Lithuania, and Latvia and Estonia followed within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Riga_1940_Soviet_Army.jpg","title":"A Soviet tank in Riga, 1940","desc":"A Soviet BT tank and a lorry carrying soldiers cross the centre of Riga in 1940, watched by crowds, in the year the Red Army entered Latvia.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riga_1940_Soviet_Army.jpg","alt":"Seen from above, a light tank and a lorry full of soldiers cross a cobbled square with tram lines, watched by dense crowds on either side."},"wiki":"Soviet occupation of the Baltic states (1940)"},
    "ww2-162": {"questions":["Bukovina had never belonged even to Tsarist Russia, the German ambassador objected, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its northern part with Czernowitz as the last missing piece of a unified Ukraine.","Laws passed in Moscow on 2 August 1940 divided the territory taken in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between the Ukrainian republic and a new Moldavian Soviet Socialist Republic."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet occupation of Bessarabia and Northern Bukovina</b> was the Soviet Union's seizure of two provinces of eastern Romania at the end of June 1940, forced by ultimatum and carried out without a battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Bessarabia had passed from Russia to Romania in 1918, and the note that opened the crisis declared that the Soviet Union had never reconciled itself to the loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its claim had been recorded in the secret protocol of August 1939, in which the Soviet side called attention to its interest in Bessarabia and Germany declared itself politically disinterested in those areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Northern Bukovina was a new demand, and when the German ambassador objected that it had never belonged even to Tsarist Russia, Molotov answered that it was the last missing part of a unified Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany cared because Romania was supplying it with very large amounts of raw materials, which its ambassador warned a crisis there would endanger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 23 June Molotov told the ambassador that the Bessarabian question brooked no further delay and that the Soviet Union would use force if Romania refused a peaceful settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After German objections he limited the Bukovina claim to its northern part and the city of Czernowitz, and refused outright to return the Romanian gold reserve held in Moscow since the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The note handed to the Romanian minister at ten on the night of 26 June demanded Bessarabia together with the part of Bukovina whose people, it said, were related to Soviet Ukraine by history, language and nationality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ribbentrop had his minister in Bucharest advise Romania to yield, and on 28 June, after a second ultimatum, it accepted terms giving its army 4 days to withdraw while the Red Army occupied Kishinev, Czernowitz and Akkerman that same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Laws passed in Moscow on 2 August gave northern Bukovina and three Bessarabian districts to the Ukrainian republic and made the rest of Bessarabia part of a new Moldavian Soviet Socialist Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["\"Secret Additional Protocol, August 23, 1939,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/addsepro.asp. [Open access]","\"The German Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Schulenburg) to the German Foreign Office, June 23, 1940,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns146.asp. [Open access]","\"The German Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Schulenburg) to the German Foreign Office, June 26, 1940, 12:59 a.m.,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns151.asp. [Open access]","\"The German Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Schulenburg) to the German Foreign Office, June 26, 1940,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns153.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Rumania (Gunther) to the Secretary of State, June 27, 1940, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d502. [Open access]","\"The Reich Foreign Minister to the German Foreign Office, June 27, 1940,\" in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/ns155.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, June 28, 1940, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d506. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, August 3, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d522. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Romania give way without fighting?","a":"Because its most likely protector told it to. Ribbentrop instructed the German minister in Bucharest that, to avoid war between Romania and the Soviet Union, Germany could only advise Romania to yield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Romania accepted the second ultimatum on 28 June, in the words the Soviet broadcast reported, to avoid bloodshed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany accept a Soviet move into Romania?","a":"Because the secret protocol of August 1939 had already recorded German political disinterest in Bessarabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its worry was economic: the ambassador told Molotov that Romania was supplying very large amounts of essential military and civilian raw materials, and that trouble there would encroach on German interests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was northern Bukovina added to the demand?","a":"Molotov called Bukovina the last missing part of a unified Ukraine, to be settled together with Bessarabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cut back under German objection to the northern part and Czernowitz, the new line still gave the Soviet Union a direct railway from Bessarabia through Czernowitz to Lemberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Soviet_occupation_of_Bessarabia_and_Northern_Bukovina_53.jpg","title":"Red Army men in a Bessarabian village, 1940","desc":"A Soviet press photograph from the occupation of Bessarabia: according to its original caption, a Red Army pilot who had brought fresh newspapers to a village talks with peasants.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soviet_occupation_of_Bessarabia_and_Northern_Bukovina_53.jpg","alt":"A man in a flying helmet holding papers, surrounded by villagers in caps and headscarves, beside a biplane on open ground."},"wiki":"Soviet occupation of Bessarabia and Northern Bukovina"},
    "ww2-163": {"questions":["Mines were laid in Norwegian waters on 8 April 1940 under Operation Wilfred, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept an infantry brigade ready to follow to Narvik if Germany answered by landing in Norway.","Captured papers on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were produced at Nuremberg to argue that Germany had only forestalled an Allied landing, a defence the Tribunal rejected because the German plans came first."],"abstract":"<b>Plan R 4</b> was the British military plan of spring 1940 to land small forces at Narvik, Trondheim, Bergen and Stavanger if Germany moved against Norway, paired with a naval operation called Wilfred to mine Norwegian waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in the winter, when the Allies wanted to stop the Swedish iron ore that reached Germany through Narvik and down the sheltered channel along the Norwegian coast known as the Leads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 5 February the Supreme War Council approved a larger scheme to send troops towards Finland through Narvik and the Swedish orefields, but Finland's surrender in mid-March ended it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chamberlain later told the Commons that the troops held ready were to occupy Norwegian ports only if invited by the Norwegian government or after Norwegian neutrality had already been violated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 5 April Britain and France warned Norway and Sweden that the Allies could no longer acquiesce in Germany obtaining vital war resources and facilities from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> As finally drawn up, the plan laid minefields off the Vestfjord and Stadland and assigned a brigade to Narvik and five battalions to Bergen, Trondheim and Stavanger, to sail when the Germans set foot on Norwegian soil or clearly intended to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British ambassador told Washington that the mining would probably take place early on Monday 8 April, and the minefield off Narvik was laid at daylight that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Germany invaded Norway and Denmark the next day, and Chamberlain told the Commons that the Allies had never contemplated occupying Scandinavian territory so long as Germany did not attack it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Nuremberg the defence cited captured Allied papers to plead self-defence, and the Tribunal accepted that the plan was definite, with a convoy due to sail on 5 April, later postponed to 8 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It still ruled that the German plans had been prepared long before, and that Germany had occupied Norway to gain bases against Britain and France rather than to forestall an imminent Allied landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 2, \"Norway and the War: British and German Military Plans\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-2.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"German Invasion of Norway and Denmark: Allies' Decision,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1940, vol. 359, cols. 507-14, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/apr/09/allies-decision. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Norway (Situation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 2 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 906-13, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/02/norway-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The British Ambassador (Lothian) to the Secretary of State, April 6, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d53. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The British Ambassador (Lothian) to the Secretary of State, April 7, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d55. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"The Aggression against Denmark and Norway,\" <i>Judgment of the International Military Tribunal</i>, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddenma.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"The War at Sea,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 April 1940, vol. 359, cols. 733-64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/apr/11/the-war-at-sea. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the troops to land only after a German move?","a":"Because the plan rested on Norwegian consent. Chamberlain told the Commons that the forces could occupy Norwegian ports only on invitation or after Germany had violated Norwegian neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official history adds that each force was equipped as lightly as possible and expected at worst sporadic Norwegian resistance, not Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Allies care so much about Narvik?","a":"Because Swedish iron ore went through it to Germany, especially in winter when the Baltic port of Luleå was frozen, and then travelled south inside Norwegian territorial waters, out of reach of the blockade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chamberlain called those waters a long communication trench for German supplies of iron ore and contraband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg Tribunal reject the German claim of a preventive war?","a":"Because the dates ran the wrong way. Raeder's memorandum on gaining bases in Norway dated from October 1939 and said nothing about forestalling the Allies, and German naval staff entries of late March did not expect a British landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Allied plans, the Tribunal said, were not the cause of the invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Plan R 4"},
    "ww2-164": {"questions":["To keep <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> secret and fast, its first wave of 8,850 men sailed in warships, while equipment went ahead in merchant ships posing as ordinary traffic.","Jodl recorded on 2 April 1940 that Hitler had ordered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to be carried out on 9 April, once his commanders confirmed that all preparations were complete."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Weserübung</b>, the ‘Weser Exercise’, was the German plan for the simultaneous occupation of Denmark and Norway, carried out on 9 April 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raeder first raised the value of Norwegian bases with Hitler in October 1939, and in December the Norwegian fascist leader Vidkun Quisling was brought to Berlin with a plan for a coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study under the armed forces high command was authorised that month, and an order of 27 January 1940 turned this 'Study North' into detailed planning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler charged General von Falkenhorst with the preparations on 21 February, days after the Altmark incident, which the British official history judges the effective trigger of the decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His directive of 1 March gave its aims as preventing British encroachment on Scandinavia and the Baltic, guaranteeing the ore base in Sweden and giving the navy and air force a wider start line against Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the German navy was heavily outnumbered, the plan rested on secrecy, speed and deception, and its first wave of 8,850 men sailed in warships while equipment went ahead in merchant ships posing as ordinary traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jodl recorded on 2 April that Hitler had ordered the operation carried out on 9 April, and the first ships of the leading echelon began to move early the next morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German troops crossed the Danish frontier at daybreak on 9 April, and German leaflets distributed in Denmark claimed that the occupation was protecting Danish neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Berlin radio announced that the army had taken over the protection of Denmark and Norway, and a note handed to the Norwegian minister said the landings had no aggressive aims and were forestalling an Allied invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg Tribunal later rejected that defence and held that the invasions of both countries were acts of aggressive war, planned long before the Allied plans relied on to excuse them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, \"The Aggression against Denmark and Norway,\" <i>Judgment of the International Military Tribunal</i>, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddenma.asp. [Open access]","\"Document 1809-PS: Diary of General Jodl,\" in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1809-ps.asp. [Open access]","T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 2, \"Norway and the War: British and German Military Plans\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-2.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, April 9, 1940, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d61. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, April 9, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d62. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Denmark (Atherton) to the Secretary of State, April 9, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d59. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"German Invasion of Norway and Denmark: Allies' Decision,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1940, vol. 359, cols. 507-14, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/apr/09/allies-decision. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany attack Denmark as well as Norway?","a":"The British official history says the Danish operation was designed chiefly to make the attack on Norway easier, and Hitler's directive required the Danish crossing and the Norwegian landings to happen together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg Tribunal noted that no defendant ever claimed any other belligerent planned to occupy Denmark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the troops travel in warships?","a":"Because six divisions would normally have needed more than half 1 million tons of escorted transports, an armada Germany could not muster and which would have announced its purpose long before it reached Norway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Warships were fast and did not proclaim their destination, though their load handicapped them in any naval fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the date left open for so long?","a":"Jodl recorded in mid-March that Hitler had not yet decided what reason to give for the operation, and that peace between Finland and the Soviet Union had removed any political basis for occupying Norway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fixed 9 April only on 2 April, when his commanders confirmed that the preparations were complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Weser%C3%BCbung_Kaupisch_Flugblatt_1.jpg/1920px-Weser%C3%BCbung_Kaupisch_Flugblatt_1.jpg","title":"A German leaflet dropped over Denmark, 9 April 1940","desc":"One of the leaflets addressed to the soldiers and people of Denmark on the morning of the invasion, claiming that Germany had come to protect the country from Britain and France.","credit":"Jens Rost, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weser%C3%BCbung_Kaupisch_Flugblatt_1.jpg","alt":"A printed sheet with a large heading and several paragraphs of Danish text beneath it."},"wiki":"Operation Weserübung"},
    "ww2-165": {"questions":["British and French troops landed at Namsos and Åndalsnes to take Trondheim during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but German air superiority forced them out of central Norway by early May.","King Haakon refused to accept a government under Quisling early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended in June when the Norwegian army stopped fighting and the King left for Britain."],"abstract":"The <b>Norwegian campaign</b> was the fighting that followed the German invasion of Norway on 9 April 1940 and lasted until the Norwegian army ceased fighting in June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Germans took Bergen, Trondheim, Stavanger and Narvik in the first hours, while at Oscarsborg the guns of an old fortress sank the new cruiser Blücher with the loss of about 1,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At five that morning the German minister demanded that Norway accept military occupation, and the foreign minister, Halvdan Koht, replied that Norway must resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Next morning's Oslo papers carried Quisling's proclamation that his party had taken power because the lawful government had ordered mobilisation and fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Germany demanded that this government be accepted, King Haakon said he would abdicate rather than be party to it, his ministers followed him, and the Germans were told that resistance would continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> British troops landed at Namsos, north of Trondheim, and Åndalsnes, to the south, from 16 April, with French mountain troops following, but the only good airfields were already in German hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A direct naval assault on Trondheim planned for 25 April was dropped, and Churchill told the Commons that the 12,000 men withdrawn from central Norway under heavy bombing were fewer than a division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fighting went on in the north into June, but on 9 June the Swedish foreign minister reported that the Norwegian army had ceased fighting because the Allies had withdrawn their help, and that the King and government were on their way to England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From London the King told his people that he would have preferred to stay but had gone to carry on the work of liberating the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The British official history counts 1,869 British, about 530 French and Polish and 1,335 Norwegian casualties on land, against a German General Staff figure of 5,296.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 3, \"9th April: The German Plan in Action\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-3.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The First Secretary of Legation in Norway (Cox) to the Secretary of State, April 9, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d64. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Norway (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, April 10, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d69. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Norway (Situation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 2 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 906-13, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/02/norway-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1251-366, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/08/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Sweden (Sterling) to the Secretary of State, June 9, 1940, 11 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d94. [Open access]","T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 15, \"The Campaign in Retrospect\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-15.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Norwegian Minister (Morgenstierne) to the Secretary of State, June 17, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d98. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the sinking of the Blücher matter so much?","a":"Because it carried most of the staff meant to take over Oslo, and the seaborne advance on the capital was held up for more than a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oslo was therefore not firmly in German hands on the vital morning, and the government had time to leave and organise resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the Allies not hold central Norway?","a":"Because the airfields were already in German hands, so fighters could not protect the troops, and bombing of the small ports at Namsos and Åndalsnes stopped artillery and reinforcements from being landed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the same time the German thrust north from Oslo grew far stronger than expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the King refuse the German terms?","a":"Because by then the German demands included accepting an unconstitutional government under Quisling, and the King said he would abdicate rather than be party to such a breach of constitutional principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His ministers followed his lead at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Senkingen_av_Bl%C3%BCcher_%285655145774%29.jpg","title":"The Oslofjord after the Blücher went down, 9 April 1940","desc":"Wreckage and men in the water off Drøbak after the German cruiser Blücher was sunk by the guns and torpedoes of Oscarsborg fortress on the first morning of the invasion of Norway; from the National Archives of Norway.","credit":"Riksarkivet (National Archives of Norway), no known copyright restrictions, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Senkingen_av_Bl%C3%BCcher_(5655145774).jpg","alt":"A stretch of calm water scattered with dark specks of debris and swimmers, below a wooded, snow-flecked shore."},"wiki":"Norwegian campaign"},
    "ww2-166": {"questions":["The battleship Warspite led nine destroyers up the fjord on 13 April 1940 in the second naval action of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, destroying what remained of the German force there.","Norwegian, French and Polish troops retook the town on 28 May at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only for the Allies to withdraw from northern Norway in early June."],"abstract":"The <b>Battles of Narvik</b> were the fighting for the northern Norwegian ore port between April and June 1940: two naval actions in its fjords, followed by a land campaign that retook the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Narvik fell on 9 April to a German force brought north in ten modern destroyers, a heavy risk, since the slightest hitch in their turn round could leave them bottled up by British ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler had wanted naval forces kept at Narvik after the landing, Jodl recorded, but Raeder rejected the idea, and the plan sent the destroyers home as soon as they could refuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At dawn on 10 April Captain Warburton-Lee, told by the Admiralty that he must be the sole judge of whether to attack, took his lighter H-class destroyers into the harbour, where torpedoes killed the German commodore and sank two destroyers, and six merchant ships were destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the way out the flotilla was caught between two German groups, the Hardy was beached and the Hunter sunk, and Warburton-Lee died of his wounds; his Victoria Cross was the first of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Churchill told the Commons that half the combatant vessels on each side had been knocked out, and that the ammunition ship Rauenfels had been blown up as the British withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 13 April the battleship Warspite led nine destroyers up the fjord, its aircraft sank a submarine, and the attack, Chamberlain said, completely destroyed the enemy's naval forces at the port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill admitted afterwards that no big ship had gone in with the first attack because the only one available was a battle-cruiser, and Britain had only three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After weeks of fighting on land, Narvik was entered on 28 May by a Norwegian battalion, in an operation in which French and Polish troops also fought, at a cost of about 150 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The success was short-lived, for the Allies withdrew from Narvik in early June and the remnant of the Norwegian army in the north surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 4, \"British Countermeasures by Sea and Air\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-4.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"The War at Sea,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 April 1940, vol. 359, cols. 733-64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/apr/11/the-war-at-sea. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Norway (Situation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 2 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 906-13, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/02/norway-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1251-366, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/08/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 13, \"The Capture of Narvik\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-13.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Sweden (Sterling) to the Secretary of State, June 15, 1940, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d97. [Open access]","\"Document 1809-PS: Diary of General Jodl,\" in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1809-ps.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Narvik worth so much fighting?","a":"Because of the iron ore shipped from it. Churchill told the Commons that his eye had always been fixed on Narvik, a port that might lead to some decisive achievement in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British official history notes that it was the ore traffic which had made Narvik a centre of the world's interest for so many months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the first attack go in without a big ship?","a":"Because the only one available was a battle-cruiser, Britain had only three, and losing one would have badly upset the balance of the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the second attack the Warspite was sent in, accepting the risk of minefields, submarines and torpedoes from destroyers hidden in the side fjords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the German destroyers still in harbour on 10 April?","a":"Because refuelling was slow. Only one tanker had arrived, and filling each pair of destroyers took between 7 and 8 hours, so the flotilla had not yet left for home when the British attacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/The_Second_British_Naval_Action_Off_Narvik._13_April_1940._A21.jpg/1920px-The_Second_British_Naval_Action_Off_Narvik._13_April_1940._A21.jpg","title":"A German destroyer burning at Narvik, 13 April 1940","desc":"A German destroyer abandoned and on fire east of Narvik harbour during the second British naval action; she drifted until the next morning and then sank.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Second_British_Naval_Action_Off_Narvik._13_April_1940._A21.jpg","alt":"A warship on fire and trailing smoke in a fjord below snow-covered mountains."},"wiki":"Battles of Narvik"},
    "ww2-167": {"questions":["The government won the division that closed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by 281 votes to 200, yet Chamberlain resigned within days and Churchill formed an all-party coalition.","Lloyd George told Chamberlain during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to sacrifice the seals of office, after the Prime Minister had called on his friends to support him in the lobby."],"abstract":"The <b>Norway Debate</b> was the 2-day Commons debate on the conduct of the war on 7 and 8 May 1940, held after the failure in Norway, which brought down Neville Chamberlain's government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 2 May Chamberlain had told the House that British troops had been withdrawn from Åndalsnes, since German local air superiority made it impossible to land the artillery and tanks they needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Opening the debate, he asked members not to form hasty opinions on a campaign that was not yet finished, and defended his remark of 5 April that Hitler had missed the bus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The phrase was thrown back at him from the benches, and the polemic <i>Guilty Men</i>, published later in 1940, printed it in capitals in its indictment of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Leo Amery, a Conservative, ended his speech by turning on the government the words Cromwell had used to dismiss the Long Parliament, 'In the name of God, go'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the second day Herbert Morrison announced that Labour would divide the House, and Chamberlain answered that he had friends in the House and called on them to support him in the lobby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lloyd George replied that the Prime Minister had appealed for sacrifice and should give an example himself by sacrificing the seals of office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill, the First Lord of the Admiralty, closed for the government, and the House divided on the adjournment, with 281 votes for the government and 200 against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unable to form a national government himself, Chamberlain resigned after the failure of the British effort in Norway, and Churchill was chosen to succeed him at the head of an all-party coalition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 13 May that he had received the King's commission on the Friday evening and formed a War Cabinet of five, and the House approved the new government by 381 votes to none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Norway (Situation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 2 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 906-13, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/02/norway-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 7 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1073-196, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/07/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1251-366, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/08/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","Government of the United Kingdom, \"Neville Chamberlain,\" Past Prime Ministers, https://www.gov.uk/government/history/past-prime-ministers/neville-chamberlain. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"His Majesty's Government,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 13 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1501-25, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/13/his-majestys-government-1. [Open access]","Cato, <i>Guilty Men</i> (London: Victor Gollancz, 1940), chap. 23, \"Missing the Bus,\" Digital Library of India scan, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.5205. [Open access]","Government of the United Kingdom, \"Sir Winston Churchill,\" Past Prime Ministers, https://www.gov.uk/government/history/past-prime-ministers/winston-churchill. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a government that won the vote fall?","a":"Because the debate showed that it had lost the support it needed. One member who had once resigned from Chamberlain's government said he would vote against it, with the deepest reluctance, because in war no one could take neither side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chamberlain resigned soon after, unable to form a national government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the appeal to his friends backfire?","a":"Because it made a question of national leadership personal. Lloyd George said it was not a question of who the Prime Minister's friends were but a far bigger issue, and Labour's closing speaker complained that Chamberlain had jumped up mostly on the basis of his friendships in the House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill gain from a defeat he had helped to direct?","a":"Morrison noted that the First Lord bore considerable responsibility for the operations, but Lloyd George reminded the House that Churchill had been right about German strength when the Prime Minister of the day said his facts were not true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 13 May he took office at the head of a government of all parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Norway Debate"},
    "ww2-168": {"questions":["'I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat,' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told the Commons on 13 May 1940, 3 days after receiving the King's commission to form a government.","Lloyd George admitted in the Norway Debate that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been right about German rearmament in the years when the Prime Minister of the day called his facts untrue."],"abstract":"<b>Winston Churchill</b> was the British statesman who became <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Prime_Minister_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Prime Minister</span> in May 1940 and led the wartime government until 1945; after the Conservative victory of 1951 he served a second term as Prime Minister until poor health made him resign in 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born on 30 November 1874 at Blenheim Palace, he served as a soldier and part-time journalist before entering <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Parliament_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Parliament</span> as a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Conservative_Party_(UK)\">Conservative</span> in 1900 and crossing to the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Liberal_Party_(UK)\">Liberals</span> in 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As First Lord of the Admiralty in the First World War he was blamed for the Gallipoli campaign and resigned, and as Chancellor of the Exchequer from 1924 he returned Britain to the gold standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Out of office for most of the 1930s, he wrote, made speeches and warned against the appeasement of Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His writing, from campaign reports of the 1890s to a four-volume history of the First World War, won him the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Made First Lord of the Admiralty again on the outbreak of war in 1939, he told the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Cabinet_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Cabinet</span> on 18 October, after a U-boat sank the battleship <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"HMS_Royal_Oak_(08)\">Royal Oak</span> inside Scapa Flow, that the anchorage was quite unfit as a base for the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> He repeatedly pressed his colleagues to mine the inshore route along the Norwegian coast, and the operation finally mounted in April 1940 was christened <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Operation_Wilfred\">Wilfred</span> by him, as being 'minor and innocent'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In April <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Neville_Chamberlain\">Chamberlain</span> made him chairman of the Cabinet's Military Co-ordinating Committee and then authorised him to give guidance and direction to the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Chiefs_of_Staff_Committee\">Chiefs of Staff</span>, while he reported the naval war to the Commons himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Norway Debate he defended the campaign for the government, while Lloyd George told the House that the First Lord had been right about German rearmament when the Prime Minister of the day said his facts were not true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Receiving <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"George_VI\">the King</span>'s commission on the evening of 10 May, he formed a government of all parties and told the Commons 3 days later that he had nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Nobel Foundation, \"Winston Churchill: Biographical,\" The Nobel Prize in Literature 1953, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/literature/1953/churchill/biographical/. [Open access]","Government of the United Kingdom, \"Sir Winston Churchill,\" Past Prime Ministers, https://www.gov.uk/government/history/past-prime-ministers/winston-churchill. [Open access]","T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 2, \"Norway and the War: British and German Military Plans\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-2.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"The War at Sea,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 April 1940, vol. 359, cols. 733-64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/apr/11/the-war-at-sea. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1251-366, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/08/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"His Majesty's Government,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 13 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1501-25, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/13/his-majestys-government-1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Conduct of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 7 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1073-196, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/07/conduct-of-the-war. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939-1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. 6, \"The Sea Approaches and Coastal Waters, 3rd September-31st December, 1939,\" 91-110, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-I/UK-RN-I-6.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939-1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. 5, \"Opening Moves in Home Waters, 3rd September-31st December, 1939,\" 63-90, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-I/UK-RN-I-5.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Churchill want to mine Norwegian waters?","a":"Because German imports of Swedish iron ore came south from Narvik along Norway's sheltered coastal channel, where the blockade could not reach them, and mines would force the ships out to sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He pressed the Cabinet within a month of the outbreak of war and again in December 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the failure in Norway not ruin him?","a":"Labour pointed out that he bore considerable responsibility for the operations, but the debate turned on Chamberlain's whole conduct of the war, and Lloyd George reminded the House that Churchill had been right about German strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Chamberlain resigned, Churchill was chosen to lead an all-party coalition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he promise only 'blood, toil, tears and sweat'?","a":"Because he took office, as he told the House, in the preliminary stage of one of the greatest battles in history, with fighting in Norway and Holland, and he offered many long months of struggle and suffering rather than quick success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Winston_Churchill_As_Prime_Minister_1940-1945_MH26392.jpg/1920px-Winston_Churchill_As_Prime_Minister_1940-1945_MH26392.jpg","title":"Churchill in the Cabinet Room","desc":"Winston Churchill at his seat in the Cabinet Room at 10 Downing Street, London, during his wartime premiership.","credit":"British government photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winston_Churchill_As_Prime_Minister_1940-1945_MH26392.jpg","alt":"An older, heavy-set man in a dark suit and spotted bow tie, a cigar in his hand, seated at a desk in a room with bookshelves and pillars."},"wiki":"Winston Churchill"},
    "ww2-169": {"questions":["Hitler was displeased that the men aboard had offered no resistance in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Jodl recorded that he was pressing energetically for the preparations against Norway.","Norway's view in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a German state ship could pass through its waters unsearched was, Chamberlain told the Commons, at variance with international law as Britain understood it."],"abstract":"The <b>Altmark incident</b> was the boarding of the German naval supply ship Altmark by a British destroyer in Norwegian territorial waters on 16 February 1940 and the release of the British seamen held aboard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Altmark had served the pocket battleship Admiral Graf Spee in the South Atlantic, refuelling her and taking on board the crews of the merchant ships she sank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Graf Spee was caught off the River Plate, the Altmark made for Germany with her prisoners, using Norwegian territorial waters for hundreds of miles to escape capture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Norwegian officers stopped her twice but, by the Norwegian account, accepted her papers as a warship flying the German state flag, and her captain refused an inspection that was then not pressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Hague Convention of 1907 on naval neutrality, the mere passage of belligerent warships through a neutral's waters did not affect its neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The destroyer Cossack entered Jøssingfjord, south of Stavanger, and its boarding party freed 299 officers and men from British merchant ships sunk by the Graf Spee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before acting, the British commander proposed taking the Altmark to Bergen under joint escort for investigation and invited the Norwegian officer to join the boarding party, and both offers were refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Norway protested bitterly at the breach of its waters, and its minister in Washington later cited the case as part of British pressure on Norwegian neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official history judges that the incident produced the effective German decision to invade Norway, since it showed Britain ready in some circumstances to infringe Norwegian neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jodl recorded on 19 February that Hitler was displeased that the Graf Spee's crew on the Altmark had offered no resistance, and that he was pressing energetically for the preparations against Norway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"German Ship \"Altmark\": Rescue of British Prisoners,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 February 1940, vol. 357, cols. 1161-4, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/feb/20/rescue-of-british-prisoners. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939-1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. 7, \"Ocean Warfare, 3rd September-31st December, 1939,\" 111-21, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-I/UK-RN-I-7.html. [Open access]","T. K. Derry, <i>The Campaign in Norway</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 2, \"Norway and the War: British and German Military Plans\", HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Norway/UK-NWE-Norway-2.html. [Open access]","\"Document 1809-PS: Diary of General Jodl,\" in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1809-ps.asp. [Open access]","Convention (XIII) concerning the Rights and Duties of Neutral Powers in Naval War, The Hague, 18 October 1907, art. 10, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague13.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Secretary of State (Berle), March 28, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d50. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Norwegians not find the prisoners?","a":"By the Norwegian foreign minister's own account they never searched the ship. They treated her as a warship under the German state flag, examined her papers and let her go, and a later request to inspect her was dropped when her captain refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain feel entitled to act inside neutral waters?","a":"Chamberlain argued that Norway had shown complete indifference to German use of its waters, and that a doctrine letting a warship use them for hundreds of miles to carry prisoners to Germany was at variance with international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hague Convention itself said only that mere passage did not affect a neutral's neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the incident matter beyond the rescue?","a":"Because it hardened both sides' plans for Norway. The British found in it fresh justification for acting against the Narvik ore traffic, and Hitler pressed his own preparations energetically within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Altmark_in_J%C3%B8ssingfjord.jpg","title":"The Altmark in Jøssingfjord, February 1940","desc":"An RAF reconnaissance photograph of the German supply ship Altmark moored in Jøssingfjord, taken shortly before HMS Cossack's boarding party went aboard on 16 February 1940.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altmark_in_J%C3%B8ssingfjord.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a ship moored in a narrow inlet among steep, snow-streaked rocky hills."},"wiki":"Altmark incident"},
    "ww2-170": {"questions":["Captain Langsdorff scuttled his ship off Montevideo on 17 December 1939 rather than face the forces he wrongly believed were waiting after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 4 days earlier.","The 21 American republics protested that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been fought inside the security zone they declared at Panama in October 1939, a protest Britain answered with reservations."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the River Plate</b> was the action of 13 December 1939 off the estuary between Uruguay and Argentina in which three British cruisers fought the German pocket battleship Admiral Graf Spee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Graf Spee had raided Allied trade in the South Atlantic since late September, sinking nine ships of some 50,000 tons without the loss of a single British life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commodore Harwood, who had concentrated the Exeter, Ajax and Achilles off the estuary the day before, attacked at once in two divisions, so that the enemy had either to split her fire or leave one adversary unengaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Graf Spee's six 280-millimetre (11-inch) guns soon crippled the Exeter and later knocked out both after turrets of the Ajax, before the German ship broke away westwards and made for Montevideo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chamberlain told the Commons the next day that the German minister in Uruguay had admitted 36 dead and 60 wounded, and called his claim that the British had used mustard gas entirely without foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Uruguay allowed 72 hours for repairs, and the German minister refused to promise that the ship would either leave by the deadline or accept internment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Captain Langsdorff believed that a strong force including the Ark Royal and Renown was waiting outside, although only three cruisers were, and Berlin approved scuttling in preference to internment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the evening of 17 December the ship sailed down the river and was blown up about 11 kilometres (7 miles) off shore, both her magazines exploding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her crew were carried to Buenos Aires, where Argentina interned 1,040 men, Uruguay interned the steamer Tacoma that had taken them off as an auxiliary warship, and Langsdorff shot himself 3 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 21 American republics protested that the battle had been fought inside the security zone proclaimed at Panama, and Britain replied that it had understood the zone was to rest on the belligerents' consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939-1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. 7, \"Ocean Warfare, 3rd September-31st December, 1939,\" 111-21, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-I/UK-RN-I-7.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"The War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 14 December 1939, vol. 355, cols. 1337-49, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/dec/14/the-war. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Uruguay (Wilson) to the Secretary of State, December 16, 1939, 10 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The American Republics, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v05/d133. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Uruguay (Wilson) to the Secretary of State, received December 17, 1939, 6:30 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The American Republics, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v05/d138. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Uruguay (Wilson) to the Secretary of State, December 18, 1939, 5 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The American Republics, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v05/d140. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Panamanian Minister for Foreign Affairs (Garay) to the Secretary of State, January 26, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d737. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in Argentina (Armour) to the Secretary of State, December 19, 1939, 10 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The American Republics, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v05/d141. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Uruguayan Minister (Richling) to the Secretary of State, January 3, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d730. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Graf Spee go into Montevideo instead of making for the open sea?","a":"Because she needed repairs. Her captain obtained a 72-hour extension of the permitted stay to make them, and the German minister insisted to Uruguay's foreign minister that more time still was needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Langsdorff scuttle his ship rather than fight his way out?","a":"Because he believed, wrongly, that the Ark Royal and Renown were already waiting, and he asked Berlin whether he should scuttle or accept internment if a breakout meant certain destruction. Raeder and Hitler agreed that scuttling was preferable to internment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the American republics protest?","a":"Because the action was fought inside the maritime security zone that the 21 republics had declared at Panama in October 1939 to keep the war away from their shores, and they sent a joint communication to the belligerents about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/The_German_pocket_battleship_ADMIRAL_GRAF_SPEE_in_flames_after_being_scuttled_off_Montevideo%2C_Uruguay%2C_after_the_Battle_of_the_River_Plate%2C_17_December_1939._A6.jpg","title":"The Admiral Graf Spee burning off Montevideo","desc":"The German pocket battleship Admiral Graf Spee in flames after being scuttled off Montevideo on 17 December 1939, four days after the battle.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_German_pocket_battleship_ADMIRAL_GRAF_SPEE_in_flames_after_being_scuttled_off_Montevideo,_Uruguay,_after_the_Battle_of_the_River_Plate,_17_December_1939._A6.jpg","alt":"A large warship burning, with a towering column of black smoke rising above it over open water."},"wiki":"Battle of the River Plate"},
    "ww2-171": {"questions":["The Allies were not outnumbered in tanks, holding by one count about 4,340 on the continent against 3,863, yet they lost the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because the German armour was concentrated.","On 18 May 1940 the French premier warned the American ambassador that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might end in the absolute defeat of France and Britain within 2 months."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of France</b> was the German campaign of May and June 1940 against France, Britain and the Low Countries, in which Germany conquered Holland in 5 days, Belgium in eighteen and France in 46.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decision to attack in the west was Hitler’s, taken once Poland had fallen, and his generals opposed it at the time on the ground that it would pay better to let the Allies attack first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the three attacking army groups, the one facing Luxembourg and the Ardennes held 44 divisions, seven of them armoured, with a reserve of 45 divisions behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In tanks the sides were close, and one calculation gives Britain and France 4,340 on the continent against 3,863 for Germany, most of the Allied machines being better armed and armoured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What told was concentration, since seven of the ten panzer divisions went through the Ardennes together while the French spread 36 tank battalions evenly along their borders in support of the infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 18 May the French premier told the American ambassador that armies which had moved at about 6 kilometres (4 miles) an hour in the last war now moved at about 50 kilometres (30 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The German armour broke through to the coast, the Allied armies in Belgium were cut off, and more than 300,000 British and French troops were taken off the beaches near Dunkirk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Paris fell on 14 June, and on 18 June Churchill told the Commons that what General Weygand had called the Battle of France was over and that the battle of Britain was about to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The armistice signed on 22 June took effect only once France had also agreed terms with Italy, and it kept French soldiers in German camps as prisoners of war until a peace was concluded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Germany took an estimated two million French, British, Dutch and Belgian prisoners, yet more than half 1 million Allied soldiers got away to fight again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 3, 35–45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-3.html. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 84–85, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “German Invasion of Western Europe, May 1940,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, accessed 29 October 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-invasion-of-western-europe-may-1940. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1–3, https://jmss.org/article/download/58008/43655. [Open access]","“Franco-German Armistice: June 25, 1940,” text of the armistice agreement signed at Compiègne on 22 June 1940, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 18 June 1940, vol. 362, cc. 51–64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/18/war-situation. [Open access]","William C. Bullitt to the Secretary of State, Paris, 18 May 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 189, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d189. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies lose although they had as many tanks?","a":"Because numbers were not what decided it. Most Allied tanks were better armed and armoured than the German ones, but the French spread their tank battalions along the whole frontier in support of the infantry, while the Germans put seven of their ten panzer divisions through the Ardennes together and struck a thin defence at one of its weakest points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German generals oppose attacking in the west?","a":"Because they thought waiting was the better bet. The army general staff and the leading generals argued in the autumn of 1939 that it would pay better to let the Allies take the offensive, and the decision to attack was Hitler’s; the British official history judges that, on purely military grounds, the offensive when it came proved well timed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the armistice depend on Italy?","a":"Because its last article said so. The agreement signed at Compiègne was to take effect only when the French government had also reached an agreement with Italy on ending hostilities, and fighting was to stop 6 hours after Italy told Germany that it had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-126-0347-09A%2C_Paris%2C_Deutsche_Truppen_am_Arc_de_Triomphe.jpg","title":"German troops at the Arc de Triomphe, Paris, 1940","desc":"A German propaganda-company photograph of infantry marching past the Arc de Triomphe after Paris fell in June 1940.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-126-0347-09A / Gutjahr, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-126-0347-09A,_Paris,_Deutsche_Truppen_am_Arc_de_Triomphe.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a column of helmeted soldiers in field grey marching across a cobbled square beneath a huge triumphal arch carved with relief sculpture."},"wiki":"Battle of France"},
    "ww2-172": {"questions":["Orders found in a German aircraft that force-landed in Belgium in January 1940 gave the Allies an early version of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the plan for the western offensive.","The Maginot Line’s strongest sectors could not be broken without heavy artillery, a German general told the Nuremberg tribunal, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent the attack round it."],"abstract":"<b>Fall Gelb</b>, ‘Case Yellow’, was the German plan for the offensive in the west, drawn up after the fall of Poland and issued in five versions before it was carried out on 10 May 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler announced his decision to attack on 27 September 1939 and ordered preparations finished by 12 November, over the doubts of the army’s commander-in-chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A directive followed on 9 October, and the general staff issued the first version on 19 October, aiming to defeat as much of the Allied armies as possible and to win ground in the Low Countries and northern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weight lay in the north, where the armoured divisions were to cross central Belgium, so in January 1940 the plan still followed closely the pattern the Allies had forecast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neutral states lay in its path, and the Nuremberg tribunal later judged the invasion of the Netherlands, Belgium and Luxembourg plainly an act of aggressive war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The attack was fixed for November and then postponed again and again for weather and transport, and when a German aircraft force-landed in Belgium on 10 January the Allies recovered its orders largely readable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Meanwhile Army Group A, under Rundstedt with Manstein as his chief of staff, argued repeatedly that the main effort belonged on the southern wing, so that the Allies could be cut off rather than pushed back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler had been drawn to Sedan on his own account, and after Manstein put the army group’s case to him on 17 February, the final version of 24 February moved the whole weight south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jodl later told the tribunal that the plan went round the Maginot Line because heavy artillery for its strongest sectors was lacking and a long assault would have exposed the German rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In May seven of the ten panzer divisions went through the Ardennes on a front of about 70 kilometres (43 miles), and the drive to the coast that followed is often called the Sichelschnitt, the sickle cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 2, 21–24, 31–32, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-2.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 9 August 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-09-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Invasion of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxemburg,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judbelg.asp. [Open access]","James V. Koch, review of <i>The Blitzkrieg Legend: The 1940 Campaign in the West</i>, by Karl-Heinz Frieser, and <i>Thunder in the East: The Nazi-Soviet War 1941–1945</i>, by Evan Mawdsley, H-German, H-Net Reviews, June 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=11928. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the plan rewritten so many times?","a":"Because the early versions satisfied almost nobody. Hitler had wanted to use his forces south of Liège and forced a first change at the end of October; Army Group A then pressed for the main effort on the southern wing; and the repeated postponements for weather gave time for war games in February that pointed the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the captured papers of January not ruin the offensive?","a":"Because they described a plan that was then dropped. The British official history notes that the plan was not changed at once after the forced landing, but in February Hitler, as Jodl recorded, argued that the papers had only confirmed the enemy’s belief that Germany wanted the Channel coast, and that Sedan was where the main thrust was not expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack go through neutral countries at all?","a":"Jodl told the tribunal that the Maginot Line could not be broken at its strongest points for lack of heavy artillery, and that the Ruhr was Germany’s weak spot if the Allies came through Belgium and Holland first. The tribunal found no evidence for the claim that the Allies had planned to occupy those countries, and judged the invasion aggressive war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/1939-1940-battle_of_france-plan-evolution.png","title":"The evolution of Plan Yellow, October 1939 to January 1940","desc":"Four maps from the United States Military Academy’s atlas of the Second World War compare the German army’s plans of 19 and 29 October 1939, the alternative put forward by Army Group A, and the plan as it stood in mid-January 1940.","credit":"From the atlas of the Department of History, United States Military Academy, author unrecorded, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1939-1940-battle_of_france-plan-evolution.png","alt":"Four coloured maps of the Low Countries and northern France, each with red arrows for German thrusts and blue lines for expected Allied moves, the arrows shifting from Belgium in the first maps towards Sedan and Luxembourg in another."},"wiki":"Manstein plan"},
    "ww2-173": {"questions":["A shorter front with the Escaut and the French frontier defences behind it was the case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, despite a 7-day advance over roads never reconnoitred.","Ordered into execution by General Gamelin on the morning of 10 May 1940, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved the strongest Allied armies away from the point where the German armour struck."],"abstract":"The <b>Dyle Plan</b>, known to the Allied staffs as Plan D, was the French scheme for meeting a German invasion of Belgium by moving the best Allied armies forward to the River Dyle, east of Brussels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French High Command settled it in November 1939 as one of two choices, the other, Plan E, being a shorter move to the River Scheldt, known to the British as the Escaut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dyle line was shorter, could be held in greater depth with rivers and the French frontier defences behind it, and kept more of Belgium out of German hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cost was an advance of about 100 kilometres (60 miles) for the British over roads never reconnoitred, a move of 7 days that would work only if the Belgians held the Germans off meanwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Belgium was neutral, so the Allies could not enter until it was invaded, and the Nuremberg tribunal found that their planning for the Low Countries was meant to defend them against a German attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Every discussion rested on the assumption that the main German blow would fall on central Belgium, and the records show no study of what would follow if it fell farther south while the armies moved east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> General Gamelin added a variant of his own, sending the French Seventh Army of six infantry divisions and a light mechanised division forward to the mouth of the Scheldt to link the Belgians with the Dutch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 10 May Gamelin ordered the immediate execution of Plan D, and at one in the afternoon British armoured cars crossed into Belgium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the new front from Sedan to Antwerp, the strongest Allied forces, with almost all the French motor transport and modern tanks, were massed between Namur and Antwerp, where the attack was expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German weight came through the Ardennes instead, and on 18 May the French premier said the enemy’s immediate aim was to cut off the army in the Antwerp region, one of France’s best.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 2, 21–24, 31–32, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-2.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 3, 35–45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-3.html. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 58, 84, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Invasion of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxemburg,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judbelg.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “German Invasion of Western Europe, May 1940,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, accessed 29 October 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-invasion-of-western-europe-may-1940. [Open access]","William C. Bullitt to the Secretary of State, Paris, 18 May 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 189, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d189. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why go forward to the Dyle rather than wait on the French frontier?","a":"Because the forward line looked better once reached. It was shorter to hold, it had the Dendre, the Escaut and the prepared French frontier defences behind it for depth, and it left more of Belgium in Allied hands, though the move would take 7 days and depended on the Belgians holding meanwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the plan not be worked out with the Belgians?","a":"Because Belgium was neutral. Its high command wanted to know exactly what the Allies intended but would not give its own defence plans or allow reconnaissance, so contact ran through military attachés and a few British officers visited the Dyle line unofficially in plain clothes; Germany, meanwhile, claimed at Nuremberg that it had feared an Allied thrust through Belgium into the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the plan leave the Allies exposed?","a":"Because it had no alternative built into it. The planning assumed the main blow in central Belgium and no defence was prepared against a breakthrough farther south, so the mobile forces rushed north while the German penetration through Luxembourg towards Sedan cut the hinge between them and the Maginot Line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/1940-Fall_Gelb.jpg","title":"Opposing forces and plans in the west, May 1940","desc":"A map from the United States Military Academy’s atlas of the war: blue arrows show the Allied armies swinging forward from the French frontier to a line along the Dyle and on into Holland, red arrows the German thrusts.","credit":"Department of History, United States Military Academy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1940-Fall_Gelb.jpg","alt":"A coloured map of the Low Countries and northern France with blue arrows sweeping north-east from the French border to a line east of Brussels and red dashed arrows for attacks from Germany."},"wiki":"French war planning 1920–1940"},
    "ww2-174": {"questions":["Of 71 Allied bombers sent against the German crossing points on 14 May 1940, more than half were lost trying to reverse the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the armour could pour through.","Two French reservist divisions with almost no regular officers bore the brunt of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, towards which Guderian’s corps had been aimed since a German order of November 1939."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Sedan</b> was the German crossing of the River Meuse at and around Sedan on 13 May 1940 and the fighting that widened the bridgehead, the breakthrough that decided the campaign in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sedan had been a German objective since November 1939, when an order gave Guderian’s corps the task of seizing the west bank of the Meuse there by surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1940 Hitler argued that the armour should be concentrated towards Sedan, where the enemy did not expect the main thrust, and Jodl called the route a tactical secret path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French had judged the Ardennes impracticable for tanks, and the two divisions that bore the brunt were reservists with almost no regular officers and no experience of the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German armour reached the river on the night of 12 May, and infantry in rubber boats made small bridgeheads before a full attack went in the next afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That attack was prepared not by artillery but by dive-bombing that Guderian had rehearsed before the campaign, and infantry and engineers crossed under the fire of tanks, guns and aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A sergeant of the 10th Panzer Division captured key bunkers across the river on 13 May, and the French failure to counter-attack the bridgehead in time proved fatal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 14 May British and French bombers attacked the bridges, and of 71 aircraft sent that afternoon 56 per cent were lost, though the German corps war diary complained of constant air attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 15 May the French told the American ambassador that the greatest battle in history was being fought near Sedan, that, as Daladier warned, the troops could not hold out against the masses of tanks and aircraft, and that the wastage of aircraft there was enormous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill later told the Commons that once the defences at Sedan and on the Meuse were broken only a rapid retreat could have saved the northern armies, around which the German eruption swept like a sharp scythe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 3, 40–45, 53–56, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-3.html. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","James V. Koch, review of <i>The Blitzkrieg Legend: The 1940 Campaign in the West</i>, by Karl-Heinz Frieser, and <i>Thunder in the East: The Nazi-Soviet War 1941–1945</i>, by Evan Mawdsley, H-German, H-Net Reviews, June 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=11928. [Open access]","William C. Bullitt to the Secretary of State, Paris, 15 May 1940, noon, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 180, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d180. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 4 June 1940, vol. 361, cc. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","William C. Bullitt to the Secretary of State, Paris, 15 May 1940, noon, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 179, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d179. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the French defence at the river give way so fast?","a":"Because the troops there were the weakest in the line and were still settling in. The sector was held mostly by Series B reservist divisions with almost no regular officers and no experience of the enemy, and the British official history quotes a French account that the battle began before staff and troops were familiar with their new positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did aircraft take the place of artillery in the attack?","a":"Because guns and shells could not be brought through the Ardennes in time. The Germans relied on air support to limit their need for artillery and ammunition on the move, and the dive-bombing that Guderian had rehearsed broke the defenders’ nerve, in a French historian’s phrase weakening the reactions necessary for battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Allied bombing of the bridges fail?","a":"Because the bombers were too few and too exposed. Of 109 Battles and Blenheims that attacked the Sedan area on 14 May, 45 were lost; the German war diary admits delays to bridging, but the official history judges the effect too small to change the course of the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1978-062-24%2C_Floing%2C_Pontonbr%C3%BCcke_%C3%BCber_die_Maas.jpg","title":"A German pontoon bridge over the Meuse at Floing, near Sedan, 14 May 1940","desc":"Men of the 1st Panzer Regiment cross the river on a pontoon bridge with French prisoners of war; an ambulance half-track waits on a ferry and tanks stand on the far bank.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1978-062-24 / o.Ang., CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1978-062-24,_Floing,_Pontonbrücke_über_die_Maas.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of soldiers in steel helmets and long coats crowding a narrow pontoon bridge across a river, a motorcycle in front and a vehicle marked with red crosses beside them, with two tanks on the far bank."},"wiki":"Battle of Sedan (1940)"},
    "ww2-175": {"questions":["Combat engineers repaired a poor road network as the columns moved, which is how seven of the ten panzer divisions crossed the wooded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a front of about 70 kilometres.","Gamelin had once described to the British an attack through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sweeping south of the Meuse, almost exactly as it came, before he settled on the opposite view."],"abstract":"The <b>Ardennes</b> is an upland of old, hard rock in south-eastern Belgium, the western end of the Rhenish massif, and its forest reaches on into northern Luxembourg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The massif has been slowly lifted over millions of years, and rivers such as the Ourthe, the largest Ardennes tributary of the Meuse, have cut valleys up to 150 metres (490 feet) deep into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is wooded country, called the Ardennes Forest in many accounts, and in 1940 it was crossed by a poor road network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The French high command held it impracticable for tanks and unsuitable for deploying any large armoured force, and covered it with only a thin screen of horsed cavalry and light tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet in October 1939 Gamelin had described to the British chief of staff an attack through the Ardennes, sweeping south of the Meuse, almost exactly as it later came, before settling on the opposite view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The German plan turned on that assumption, and in May 1940 seven of the ten panzer divisions, with five motorised divisions behind them, went through the Ardennes on a front of about 70 kilometres (43 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The roads held only because combat engineers repaired them as the columns moved, and anti-aircraft guns travelling with the columns decimated the Allied air attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The defenders lost time in hours as well as days, as when a critical bridge over the Semois was left undefended, and by 14 May the Germans were out of the Ardennes and across the Meuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A German museum biography of Guderian describes his corps’ drive through the Ardennes as a risky advance that the terrain had made seem hardly possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> 4 years later the Allies again failed to foresee the scale of a German offensive there, the counter-offensive of December 1944, which Jodl said had been prepared at four conferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Gilles Rixhon et al., “Plio-Quaternary Landscape Evolution in the Uplifted Ardennes: New Insights from 26Al/10Be Data from Cave-Deposited Alluvium (Meuse Catchment, E Belgium),” <i>Geomorphology</i> (2020): 107424, uncorrected proof, ORBi, Université de Liège, https://orbi.uliege.be/bitstream/2268/253676/1/proof_GEOMOR_107424.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “German Invasion of Western Europe, May 1940,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, accessed 29 October 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-invasion-of-western-europe-may-1940. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 3, 35–45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-3.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 23, 316–18, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-23.html. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 84–86, 119, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","James V. Koch, review of <i>The Blitzkrieg Legend: The 1940 Campaign in the West</i>, by Karl-Heinz Frieser, and <i>Thunder in the East: The Nazi-Soviet War 1941–1945</i>, by Evan Mawdsley, H-German, H-Net Reviews, June 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=11928. [Open access]","Alexander Mühle and Arnulf Scriba, “Heinz Guderian 1888–1954,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/heinz-guderian. [Open access] [in German]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the French think the Ardennes would stop tanks?","a":"Because the ground looked like an obstacle: wooded hills cut by deep river valleys and crossed by few good roads. The British official history concludes that the theory was simply mistaken, since with careful planning and good organisation the country offered no serious hindrance to a rapid advance by large forces, armour included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the weak French troops opposite the Ardennes matter so much?","a":"Because the plan only made sense if no big attack came there. The official history notes that placing the weaker Ninth Army opposite the Ardennes could be justified only on the theory that an attack in strength through it was impracticable, and the German blow fell exactly there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German columns get through so quickly?","a":"Because every arm had trained to work together. Engineers kept the poor roads open, anti-aircraft guns in the columns beat off air attacks, and reconnaissance units led the advance through country where there was little resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1980-004-32%2C_Heinz_Guderian_in_Bouillon%2C_Frankreich.jpg","title":"German officers at Bouillon on the Semois, 12 May 1940","desc":"General Heinz Guderian with officers at Bouillon, a small town in a deep valley of the Belgian uplands, two days into the offensive.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1980-004-32 / o.Ang., CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1980-004-32,_Heinz_Guderian_in_Bouillon,_Frankreich.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of German officers in peaked caps and long greatcoats, some in black tank uniforms, standing beside a stone balustrade in a small-town street."},"wiki":"Ardennes"},
    "ww2-176": {"questions":["Images of paratroops, tanks and screaming Stukas made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> famous in 1940, yet an American army survey argues they hid how much it owed to training in combined arms.","Spread by <i>Time</i> and <i>Life</i> magazines rather than coined by German officers, the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the name for Germany’s short campaigns of 1939 to 1941."],"abstract":"<b>Blitzkrieg</b>, ‘lightning war’, is the name given to Germany’s way of winning its first campaigns of the Second World War quickly, by massing tanks, aircraft and artillery on a narrow front, breaking through and encircling the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany used it against Poland in 1939, against Denmark, Norway, the Low Countries and France in 1940, and against Yugoslavia and Greece in 1941, but it could not reach Britain across the Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word was no German invention, for it had circulated in military writing in the 1930s for any rapid and decisive victory, and it gained wide currency in the West through <i>Time</i> and <i>Life</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the German army had was less a new doctrine than an old Prussian habit of short wars of movement adapted to new machines, with tanks, motorised infantry, artillery and engineers trained to work together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even so, most senior German commanders saw mechanised forces as a useful specialist tool until France fell, and only then did the army wholeheartedly accept mechanised blitzkrieg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> To those on the receiving end in May 1940 the speed was the shock, and on 18 May the French premier told the American ambassador that conquests could now be carried out with incredible speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> 2 days earlier the American ambassador in London had warned Washington that the Allied collapse might come like a stroke of lightning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 4 June Churchill told the Commons that the great French army had been cast back and disturbed by the onrush of a few thousand armoured vehicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Images of paratroops, tanks and screaming Stukas hid how much the victory owed to combined-arms training, and to quieter novelties such as supplying the armoured spearheads with fuel by air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Against the Soviet Union the method failed, for German forces reached the gates of Moscow in 1941 but could not win, and Germany was drawn into the long war it had meant to avoid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Blitzkrieg (Lightning War),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, accessed 29 October 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/blitzkrieg-lightning-war. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 5–6, https://jmss.org/article/download/58008/43655. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 41–42, 53–55, 84–86, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","William C. Bullitt to the Secretary of State, Paris, 18 May 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 189, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d189. [Open access]","Joseph P. Kennedy to the Secretary of State, London, 16 May 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 184, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d184. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 4 June 1940, vol. 361, cc. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","David Yelton, review of <i>The German Way of War: From the Thirty Years’ War to the Third Reich</i>, by Robert M. Citino, H-German, H-Net Reviews, October 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=12431. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany want its wars to be short?","a":"Because a long war was the one it expected to lose. Germany’s strategy early in the war was to avoid a drawn-out struggle by beating its opponents one at a time in short campaigns, and the habit was older than the tank: Prussia, small and poor, had long sought wars that were short and lively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a deep breakthrough unnerve the defenders so much?","a":"Because they had no drill for it. The French command was built for methodical, set-piece battles, and when strong enemy forces suddenly appeared in the rear it lacked any procedure for moving units quickly to meet them, so command broke down and no solid front could be rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the method not finish Britain?","a":"Because tanks could not cross the sea. Britain was protected from a German ground attack by the English Channel and the Royal Navy, and Germany turned east in June 1941 with Britain still fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-383-0338-03%2C_Frankreich%2C_Piloten_bei_Lagebesprechung_vor_Ju_87.jpg","title":"Dive-bomber crews at a briefing in France, May 1940","desc":"Luftwaffe aircrew study a map in front of a Junkers Ju 87 ‘Stuka’ near Arras, the aircraft whose shriek became part of the popular image of lightning war.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-383-0338-03 / Stift, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-383-0338-03,_Frankreich,_Piloten_bei_Lagebesprechung_vor_Ju_87.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of six airmen in flying suits and caps gathered round a map on a folding table in front of a single-engined aircraft with a large propeller and a bomb under its wing."},"wiki":"Blitzkrieg"},
    "ww2-177": {"questions":["French slowness, German luck and generals who disobeyed orders, not a planned doctrine, won the campaign of 1940 according to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which treats the famous label as hindsight.","A western plan issued in five versions between October 1939 and February 1940 is part of the case made by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against a German doctrine of lightning war."],"abstract":"The <b>Blitzkrieg legend</b> is the name for the argument that Germany never had a doctrine or plan of lightning war, and that the word was a label pinned on victories won largely by improvisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands against the familiar account, in which blitzkrieg was a deliberate strategy of short campaigns built on tanks, aircraft and artillery massed on a narrow front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The word was not coined by the German army, for it can be found in military writing before 1939 for any rapid and complete victory, and it first gained wide currency in the West in <i>Time</i> and <i>Life</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest known printed German use, in a military weekly of October 1938, reported that the French manoeuvres of that year had tested the strategic surprise attack, also called Blitzkrieg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German officers writing in 1940 still put the word in quotation marks and called it a buzzword, and though the marks had gone by 1941 the term was never given a precise technical sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The record of the campaign in the west points the same way, since the generals opposed attacking in the autumn of 1939 and the plan went through five versions before taking its final form in February 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jodl told the Nuremberg tribunal, answering the charge of planning aggressive war, that everything had had to be improvised and that nothing had been ready except the plan of attack against Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Most senior German commanders treated mechanised forces as a useful but specialised tool, and the army accepted mechanised blitzkrieg wholeheartedly only after the fall of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the revisionist reading, the speed of 1940 came from French slowness, German luck at critical moments and field commanders such as Guderian and Rommel who disobeyed their orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A related view treats blitzkrieg as the old Prussian habit of short, aggressive wars of movement fitted with new machines, so that what was new in 1940 was the equipment rather than the idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 5–6, https://jmss.org/article/download/58008/43655. [Open access]","James V. Koch, review of <i>The Blitzkrieg Legend: The 1940 Campaign in the West</i>, by Karl-Heinz Frieser, and <i>Thunder in the East: The Nazi-Soviet War 1941–1945</i>, by Evan Mawdsley, H-German, H-Net Reviews, June 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=11928. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 41–42, 53, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","David Yelton, review of <i>The German Way of War: From the Thirty Years’ War to the Third Reich</i>, by Robert M. Citino, H-German, H-Net Reviews, October 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=12431. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Blitzkrieg (Lightning War),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, accessed 29 October 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/blitzkrieg-lightning-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the quotation marks of 1940 matter?","a":"Because they show how German officers themselves treated the word. Writers in the army’s own weekly in the summer and autumn of 1940 used it only inside quotation marks, one calling it a buzzword, which suggests a borrowed catchword rather than the name of a doctrine they had been taught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a doctrine seem obvious to people at the time?","a":"Because the results looked like one. The speed of the victory over France seemed to validate the idea of blitzkrieg in Germany and abroad even when its details were not understood, and the images of paratroops, tanks and dive-bombers hid the plainer story of combined-arms training.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should Jodl’s testimony be read with care?","a":"Because he gave it in his own defence. He was answering the charge that he had helped prepare aggressive war, and his claim that nothing but the plan against Poland had been ready served that defence, so it supports the improvisation argument only as the statement of an interested witness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1981-060-03%2C_Frankreich%2C_Infanterie_auf_dem_Marsch.jpg","title":"German infantry on the march in France, 1940","desc":"Infantry on foot, some pushing bicycles, on a tree-lined country road in France: the army behind the armoured spearheads.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1981-060-03 / Bauer, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1981-060-03,_Frankreich,_Infanterie_auf_dem_Marsch.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of helmeted soldiers with rifles and packs walking along a tree-lined country road, some wheeling bicycles."}},
    "ww2-178": {"questions":["Made chief of the general staff the day after the July 1944 bomb plot, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sat on the army court of honour that expelled officers linked to the plotters.","Having arranged dive-bomber support in exercises before the campaign, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the Meuse in May 1940 and then pushed on harder than his orders allowed."],"abstract":"<b>Heinz Guderian</b> was a German general, born at Kulm in West Prussia in 1888, the most influential German advocate of armoured warfare, who led the corps that broke through at Sedan in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A signals officer in the First World War, he insisted on a radio in every armoured vehicle, an advantage over the French, who often had radios only in command tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1929 he had decided that what was needed was a new formation of all arms built round the tank, with anti-tank guns, engineers and motorised infantry in the team.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He trained with tanks in the Soviet Union while the Versailles treaty forbade them at home, took command of the 2nd Panzer Division on its formation in 1935, and set out his ideas in <i>Achtung, Panzer!</i> in 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other branches resisted the new arm, and he was shunted aside as chief of mobile troops, with little control over the light and motorised divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In November 1939 his corps was given the task of seizing the west bank of the Meuse at Sedan by surprise, and in late May it held the 1st, 2nd and 10th Panzer Divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before the campaign he arranged accurate support from dive-bombers, and on 13 May it arrived while a superior’s request for high-level bombing did not, so the crossing went in behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then pushed on harder than his orders allowed, and accounts of the campaign count such disobedience by field commanders among the causes of its speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Dismissed for an unauthorised withdrawal during the Soviet winter offensive of December 1941, he returned in 1943 as inspector-general of armoured troops and became chief of the general staff the day after the bomb plot of 20 July 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the army’s court of honour he helped expel officers linked to the plot, so that the People’s Court could try them as civilians, and in 1943 he had accepted from Hitler an estate worth 1.24 million Reichsmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Mühle and Arnulf Scriba, “Heinz Guderian 1888–1954,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/heinz-guderian. [Open access] [in German]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 53–55, 85–86, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), app. 3, “German Force Engaged,” 401–3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-III.html. [Open access]","James V. Koch, review of <i>The Blitzkrieg Legend: The 1940 Campaign in the West</i>, by Karl-Heinz Frieser, and <i>Thunder in the East: The Nazi-Soviet War 1941–1945</i>, by Evan Mawdsley, H-German, H-Net Reviews, June 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=11928. [Open access]","David Yelton, review of <i>The German Way of War: From the Thirty Years’ War to the Third Reich</i>, by Robert M. Citino, H-German, H-Net Reviews, October 2006, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=12431. [Open access]","Robert M. Citino, “WWII Book Review: Guderian,” review of <i>Guderian: Panzer Pioneer or Myth Maker?</i>, by Russell Hart, HistoryNet, accessed 29 October 2026, https://www.historynet.com/wwii-book-review-guderian/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he insist on a radio in every tank?","a":"Because his early career had been in signals. Serving with radio units supporting the cavalry in 1914 taught him what wireless could do for command, and a radio in each vehicle let small units be manoeuvred quickly, where armies that relied on hand signals and flags could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his part in the court of honour matter so much?","a":"Because expulsion stripped the accused of the army’s protection. Once the court had thrown officers linked to the 20 July plot out of the Wehrmacht, the People’s Court under Roland Freisler could try them as civilians; the same museum biography notes that he also kept some officers, such as Hans Speidel, from being expelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should his own account of 1940 be read with care?","a":"Because the story has largely been told by its participants. The drive to the coast has often been narrated by men such as Guderian and Manstein, and the British official history warns that German generals writing in captivity credited the victories to professional skill and the failures to Hitler, which contemporary documents do not bear out; a recent biography calls his own memoir self-serving and distorted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-769-0229-10A%2C_Frankreich%2C_Guderian%2C_%22Enigma%22.jpg","title":"In a radio command vehicle in France, May 1940","desc":"The general stands in his armoured command half-track while a radio operator in front works an Enigma cipher machine, the wireless command he had argued for.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-769-0229-10A / Borchert, Erich (Eric), CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-769-0229-10A,_Frankreich,_Guderian,_\"Enigma\".jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of an officer in a peaked cap standing in an open armoured vehicle, with men in headphones and a cipher machine in the foreground."},"wiki":"Heinz Guderian"},
    "ww2-179": {"questions":["Charged under the Royal Warrant of 1945 and tried by a British military court in Hamburg in 1949, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sentenced to 18 years but released in May 1953.","Shown at Nuremberg an order of November 1941 from his own headquarters calling for the extermination of the Jewish-Bolshevist system, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> said that he did not recollect it."],"abstract":"<b>Erich von Manstein</b> was a German field marshal, born Fritz Erich von Lewinski in Berlin in 1887 and adopted as a baby by his aunt and her husband, whose name he took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A general staff officer, he became deputy to the chief of the general staff, Ludwig Beck, in 1936 and was moved to Silesia against his will in the Blomberg–Fritsch crisis of 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1939 he became chief of staff of Army Group A under Gerd von Rundstedt, the army group facing Luxembourg and the Ardennes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He went on to command an army in the Crimea and army groups in southern Russia, and Hitler removed him from command in March 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Nuremberg in 1946 he testified for the generals, telling the tribunal that the chief of the general staff could not be called Hitler’s decisive adviser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His name is tied to the plan of 1940 because he drafted the memoranda in which Army Group A argued, against the army’s commander-in-chief, that the main weight of the offensive belonged on the southern wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Posted away to an infantry corps, he set out those views to Hitler after a dinner on 17 February 1940, and the next day the army leadership proposed the shift of armour that the final plan made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Credit was later claimed by Hitler, by the general staff and by Manstein himself, and the British official history gives it chiefly to Rundstedt, though how much he owed to Manstein cannot be known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Nuremberg the prosecution put to him an order of 20 November 1941 issued from his headquarters, which declared that the Jewish-Bolshevist system must be exterminated once and for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain made an exception to its decision to start no new war crimes trials, served charges on him in May 1949 under the Royal Warrant of June 1945, and its military court in Hamburg sentenced him in December to 18 years, but he was released in May 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Mühle, Oliver Schweinoch and Arnulf Scriba, “Erich von Manstein 1887–1973,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/erich-von-manstein. [Open access] [in German]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), supplement, “The Planning and Conduct of the German Campaign,” 333–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-Supplement.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 9 August 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-09-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 10 August 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-10-46.asp. [Open access]","Emanuel Shinwell, “Field-Marshal von Manstein (Trial),” House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 31 May 1949, vol. 465, cc. 1894–95, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1949/may/31/field-marshal-von-manstein-trial. [Open access]","Michael Stewart, “Field-Marshal von Manstein (Trial),” House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 5 July 1949, vol. 466, cc. 1949–50, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1949/jul/05/field-marshal-von-manstein-trial. [Open access]","Earl Ferrers, “Alleged War Criminals: Trials,” House of Lords, <i>Hansard</i>, 24 April 1991, vol. 528, cc. 268–70, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1991/apr/24/alleged-war-criminals-trials. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the credit for the 1940 plan disputed?","a":"Because three parties claimed it. Hitler, the general staff and Manstein all did; the British official history, working from contemporary German documents, holds that the strategic idea was Rundstedt’s, that the final plan was drawn up by the army general staff, and that how much Rundstedt owed to his chief of staff cannot be known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his dinner with Hitler matter?","a":"Because the army leadership had kept Army Group A’s case from Hitler. Brauchitsch had refused to forward Rundstedt’s memorandum, so the evening of 17 February may have been the first time Hitler heard the proposal set out, and the following day the army leaders themselves proposed moving the armour south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his trial cause argument in Parliament?","a":"Because some members thought it unfair. They pressed for him to have British counsel and claimed the Geneva Convention required a field marshal to be tried by his equals; the government answered that the defence had competent German counsel and that the convention’s rules on trials covered only offences committed in captivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H01758%2C_Erich_v._Manstein.jpg","title":"Official portrait","desc":"A studio portrait from the archive of the wartime German news-photo agency, in general officer’s uniform and peaked cap.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H01758 / o.Ang., CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H01758,_Erich_v._Manstein.jpg","alt":"Black and white studio portrait of a grey-haired German officer in a peaked cap and uniform, with embroidered collar patches and a ribbon in his buttonhole."},"wiki":"Erich von Manstein"},
    "ww2-180": {"questions":["On the eve of the Meuse crossing the commander of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked for high-level attacks by medium bombers, a request the Luftwaffe failed to meet in time.","Guderian’s corps of three armoured divisions was grouped with others under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a command above the corps, as the German armour drove for the Channel in May 1940."],"abstract":"<b>Panzer Group Kleist</b> was the mass of armour that General Ewald von Kleist commanded in the German offensive of May 1940, a grouping of armoured corps under one commander that led Army Group A through the Ardennes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An armoured corps of the day normally consisted of two armoured divisions and one motorised division, though the grouping changed continually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 24 May the corps under Kleist and Hoth included Guderian’s XIX Corps with the 1st, 2nd and 10th Armoured Divisions and Reinhardt’s XXXXI Corps with the 6th and 8th and an SS motorised division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany had ten panzer divisions in the west, and seven of them, with five motorised divisions close behind, went through the Ardennes on a front of about 70 kilometres (43 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their tanks were no better than the Allies’, most French machines being better armed and armoured, so what counted was concentration and the training of every arm to work with the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On the eve of the Meuse crossing Kleist asked for high-level bombing that would have endangered his own troops, a request the Luftwaffe did not meet in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once across, the columns advanced with armoured reconnaissance battalions up to a day’s march ahead and slower units strung out behind, which left them open to a flank attack such as the British one at Arras on 21 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The five armoured divisions leading the advance on 20 May had counted on 10 May, by a German return, seventeen tank battalions with more than 900 guns and over 2,000 machine guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Paris on 15 May the French premier reported Daladier’s warning that the troops could not hold out against the masses of tanks and aircraft being launched at them near Sedan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 4 June that eight or nine armoured divisions of about 400 vehicles each had cut the armies of the north off from the main French forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), app. 3, “German Force Engaged,” 401–3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-III.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>The War in France and Flanders, 1939–1940</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1953), chap. 5, 76–77, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-NWE-Flanders/UK-NWE-Flanders-5.html. [Open access]","Jonathan M. House, <i>Toward Combined Arms Warfare: A Survey of 20th-Century Tactics, Doctrine, and Organization</i>, Research Survey 2 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1984), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA528264. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 4 June 1940, vol. 361, cc. 787–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 2–3, https://jmss.org/article/download/58008/43655. [Open access]","William C. Bullitt to the Secretary of State, Paris, 15 May 1940, noon, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General</i>, vol. 1, ed. Matilda F. Axton and Shirley L. Phillips (Washington, D.C.: Government Printing Office, 1959), doc. 180, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d180. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why put several armoured corps under one commander?","a":"Because the point was to mass the armour at the decisive place. The Germans concentrated their mechanised forces into a few large masses at critical points, unlike the French, who spread their tank battalions along the frontier; the British official history notes that the grouping of the corps nonetheless changed continually during the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the German columns vulnerable after the breakthrough?","a":"Because they advanced in long columns behind a reconnaissance screen. At Arras on 21 May the British struck the flank of Rommel’s division, and only the improvised use of 88 mm anti-aircraft guns and 105 mm howitzers against tanks stopped the heavy British infantry tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the panzer divisions win with tanks that were no better?","a":"Because the victory was one of doctrine more than hardware. Almost all German armour was inferior to the Allies’, but it was concentrated, and its divisions had headquarters trained to combine tanks, infantry, artillery and engineers, where French armoured divisions were tank-heavy and short of practised infantry support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1986-0210-503%2C_General_Ewald_von_Kleist.jpg","title":"The panzer group’s commander, 1940","desc":"A portrait of General Ewald von Kleist taken in 1940, the year he commanded the armoured group that led the drive through the Ardennes.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1986-0210-503 / Hartmann, Fritz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1986-0210-503,_General_Ewald_von_Kleist.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a seated German general in a peaked cap and uniform with a cross at his throat, resting his gloved hands on the hilt of a sword."},"wiki":"XXII Army Corps (Wehrmacht)"},
    "ww2-241": {"questions":["Italy's declaration of war in June 1940 turned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into an active front, with over 215,000 Italian troops in Libya facing the small British garrison of Egypt.","Only from Malta could British ships and aircraft threaten the Axis sea route to North Africa, the lifeline on which the war in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to depend."],"abstract":"The <b>Mediterranean and Middle East theatre</b> was the British name for the war around the Mediterranean, run by a Middle East Command that answered at first for Egypt, Palestine, the Sudan, Cyprus and British Somaliland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That command began in August 1939 as a planning staff of five officers, and its garrisons then included no complete formation and only 64 field guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its character changed on 10 June 1940, when Count Ciano told the British ambassador in Rome that Italy would be at war with the United Kingdom from one minute past midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Attlee told the Commons that Italy would feel the blockade, since British possession of both ends of the Mediterranean became a real restraint in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wavell estimated that over 215,000 Italian troops stood in Libya and over 200,000 in Italian East Africa, so that Egypt, the Sudan, Kenya and British Somaliland were all at once open to attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The collapse of France that month removed the French fleet and armies in North Africa and Syria on which British planning had relied, and a minister told the Lords that the position changed in a night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of all the places left to the British, only Malta allowed naval and air forces to offer a permanent threat to the short sea route between Italy and its armies in North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By April 1941 Churchill could tell the Commons that forces once outnumbered four or five to one had taken more Italian prisoners than there were British troops in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The arrival of German forces, in Libya from February 1941 and in the Balkans that spring, turned a war against Italy into a longer contest in which supply by sea weighed on every campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German Historical Museum notes that in the summer of 1942 about two thirds of Axis supplies for North Africa were sunk by forces based on Malta, and that the fighting in Africa ended at Tunis on 13 May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Archibald P. Wavell, \"Operations in the Middle East from August, 1939 to November, 1940,\" despatch of 10 December 1940, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37609, 13 June 1946, 2997–3004, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37609/supplement/2997. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Italy's Decision,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 June 1940, vol. 361, cols. 1163–69, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/11/italys-decision. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. VI, 109–24, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-6.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"Operations in Northern Africa,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 December 1940, vol. 118, cols. 179–82, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1940/dec/19/operations-in-northern-africa. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Afrikafeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/afrikafeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. III, 43–59, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Italy's entry turn the region into a theatre of war?","a":"Because Italian armies in Libya and East Africa, over 400,000 men by Wavell's estimate, bordered Egypt, the Sudan, Kenya and British Somaliland, all of them guarded by small British garrisons."},{"q":"Why did the fall of France matter so much here?","a":"Because British planning had counted on the French fleet and on French armies in North Africa and Syria, and with France out of the war Britain faced Italy in the region alone."},{"q":"Why was Malta so important to the whole theatre?","a":"Because it was the only base from which British ships and aircraft could keep threatening the short sea route that supplied the Axis armies in North Africa."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Radius_of_action_of_Allied_aircraft_from_Malta_in_relation_to_Axis_shipping_routes%2C_Summer_and_Autumn%2C_1941.jpg","title":"Malta and the Axis sea routes, 1941","desc":"A map from the British official history showing the Axis shipping routes from Italy and Greece to Libya and the circles of range of aircraft based on Malta in the summer and autumn of 1941.","credit":"Map from I. S. O. Playfair, The Mediterranean and Middle East, vol. 2 (HMSO, 1956), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radius_of_action_of_Allied_aircraft_from_Malta_in_relation_to_Axis_shipping_routes,_Summer_and_Autumn,_1941.jpg","alt":"A map of the central Mediterranean with Malta at the centre of concentric circles and blue lines tracing shipping routes from Italy and Greece to the Libyan coast."},"wiki":"Mediterranean and Middle East theatre of World War II"},
    "ww2-242": {"questions":["Badoglio opposed the attack that opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, warning that the British fleet would seize Greek bases from which to bomb the Romanian oilfields.","By January 1941 fourteen Greek divisions faced nineteen Italian ones inside Albania, a stalemate in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that helped bring the German invasion of Greece that April."],"abstract":"The <b>Greco-Italian War</b> began on 28 October 1940, when Italian forces in Albania invaded Greece, and it became part of a wider German campaign the following April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mussolini decided on 13 October, knowing the Germans would disapprove, and approved a plan whose author promised a swift operation against little resistance, with Epirus the first objective and Athens the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marshal Badoglio opposed the move, and an American diplomat in Rome reported his objection that the British fleet would seize Greek bases from which to bomb the Romanian oilfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At three in the morning of 28 October the Italian minister handed the Greek premier an ultimatum expiring at six, demanding strategic points that, when asked, he could not name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> General Metaxas rejected it, and a Liberal member told the Commons a week later that the Greeks, a small people of seven million against a mighty empire, had been true to their ancient traditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Italian plan rested on the assumption that there would be almost no resistance, and the resolute Greek defence came as a complete surprise to its authors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 22 November the Greeks had taken Koritsa and Leskovik and thrown the invaders almost entirely out of Epirus, carrying the war onto Albanian soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An informant told the American embassy in Rome that the original plan had counted on landings on Corfu and Cephalonia to end Greek resistance in 8 days, and that storms had prevented them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When January 1941 opened, fourteen Greek divisions faced nineteen Italian ones on a front of about 160 kilometres (100 miles), some 30 to 50 kilometres (20 to 30 miles) inside the Albanian border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler had written to Mussolini that nothing could be done in the Balkans before March, and the Nuremberg tribunal later traced how the Italian failure led on to the German invasion of 6 April 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XII, 223–34, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-12.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Italy (Reed) to the Secretary of State, October 21, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d422. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Greece (MacVeagh) to the Secretary of State, October 28, 1940, 5 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d428. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Italy (Reed) to the Secretary of State, December 3, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d472. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 5 November 1940, vol. 365, cols. 1205–310, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/nov/05/war-situation. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>Greece, Crete and Syria</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), chap. 1, 1–6, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417347. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggression against Yugoslavia and Greece,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judyugo.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Mussolini attack Greece without German help?","a":"Because the Greek plan did not depend on Germany, and he expected a swift operation with little resistance, taking Epirus first and then marching on Athens."},{"q":"Why did the Italian invasion fail so quickly?","a":"Because the plan assumed almost no resistance, the planned landings on Corfu and Cephalonia were prevented by storms, and the Greeks counter-attacked hard enough to carry the war into Albania."},{"q":"Why did the war draw Germany into the Balkans?","a":"Because the Italian failure left a stalemate in Albania and a British presence in Greece, and the Nuremberg tribunal traced how this led to the German invasion of April 1941."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Lot_11618-9_%2824545900475%29.jpg","title":"A Greek gun crew in Albania, 1940–41","desc":"Greek artillerymen at work with a light gun and a range-finding telescope on snowy ground during the campaign in Albania, in a photograph distributed by the American Office of War Information.","credit":"Office of War Information photograph, National Museum of the U.S. Navy, Lot 11618-9, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lot_11618-9_(24545900475).jpg","alt":"Five soldiers in helmets and greatcoats crouched in snow around a small wheeled gun, one looking through a tall binocular telescope on a tripod."},"wiki":"Greco-Italian War"},
    "ww2-243": {"questions":["Only a small Greek detachment held the mountains between the two divisions on either side of the range when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began with the Italian invasion of 28 October 1940.","Defeat in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led Mussolini to replace Visconti Prasca with General Soddu, keeping the beaten commander in the field so as not to advertise the failure."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Pindus</b> was the fighting of late October and early November 1940 in the Pindus mountains of north-western Greece, where the Italian invasion was first stopped and thrown back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek government had avoided anything that might provoke Italy, so only three divisions and part of a fourth had been mobilised when the war began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of them stood close to the Albanian frontier, one on each side of the Pindus range, while in the mountains themselves there was only a small detachment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General mobilisation was declared on the morning of 28 October, and the Greek chief of staff told the American military attaché that the opposing forces had been in contact, with artillery firing, since the ultimatum expired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greek army had no tanks and was short of anti-aircraft and anti-tank weapons and of transport, while the Italians brought an armoured division whose presence made the Greeks avoid open ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the mountains the Italian 3rd Alpine Division, the Julia, pushed to within about 19 kilometres (12 miles) of the Metsovon pass before it was counter-attacked by troops even better suited to that country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 7 November the Julia was in full retreat, by the next day the Italian offensive had collapsed, and the Greeks went on to take the offensive themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The British military attaché reported in early November that the Italians had nowhere made more than preliminary contact with the main Greek positions, though poor communications in the Pindus sector had caused early confusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mussolini kept the defeated commander, Visconti Prasca, in the field so as not to publicise the failure, but replaced him in command with General Soddu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An informant in Rome later blamed storms that had prevented landings meant to support the advance through the passes, while Churchill said simply that the Greeks had hurled back the invaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XII, 226–34, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-12.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Greece (MacVeagh) to the Secretary of State, October 28, 1940, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d429. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Greece (MacVeagh) to the Secretary of State, November 4, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d450. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Italy (Reed) to the Secretary of State, December 3, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d472. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>Greece, Crete and Syria</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), chap. 1, 1–6, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417347. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so few Greek troops ready when the invasion began?","a":"Because the Greek government avoided any action likely to provoke Italy, and so had mobilised only three divisions and part of a fourth before the attack."},{"q":"Why did the Julia division's advance end in retreat?","a":"Because after pushing close to the Metsovon pass it was counter-attacked by Greek troops even better suited to mountain country, and by 7 November it was in full retreat."},{"q":"Why did Mussolini not sack Visconti Prasca outright?","a":"Because removing him would have publicised the Italian failure, so he stayed in the field while General Soddu took over the command."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Italian_Invasion_1940_in_Pindus_Epirus.svg/960px-Italian_Invasion_1940_in_Pindus_Epirus.svg.png","title":"The Italian invasion, 28 October – 18 November 1940","desc":"A modern map of the Italian thrusts from Albania into Epirus and the Pindus mountains, marking the furthest Italian advance of 4–5 November and the front of 18 November as the Greek counter-attacks began.","credit":"Map by Alexikoua, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_Invasion_1940_in_Pindus_Epirus.svg","alt":"A relief map of the Albanian–Greek border with red arrows pushing into Greece, blue arrows pushing back, and a hatched band showing how far the invaders got."},"wiki":"Battle of Pindus"},
    "ww2-244": {"questions":["Heavy Matilda tanks whose presence the Italians never suspected led the assault on Nibeiwa camp at the start of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, achieving complete tactical surprise.","Within 2 months <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had carried a force of never more than two divisions some 800 kilometres west and taken about 130,000 Italian prisoners."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Compass</b> was the British offensive in the Western Desert that opened on 9 December 1940 as an attack on Italian camps and grew into a pursuit that ended 10 weeks later at Beda Fomm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wavell judged by October that he need not wait for Marshal Graziani to resume his advance, and planned a short, swift blow against fortified camps strung some 80 kilometres (50 miles) from Maktila to Sofafi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> General O'Connor proposed to strike first at the centre group of camps through a gap about 24 kilometres (15 miles) wide between Nibeiwa and Rabia, attacking each camp from the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Surprise was the keynote, so only a handful of commanders were told of the plan, while two hidden supply dumps were stocked about 65 kilometres (40 miles) west of Matruh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Nibeiwa tactical surprise was complete, for the assault came at an unexpected moment from an unexpected direction and was led by heavy Matilda tanks whose presence the Italians had not suspected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Wavell reported that this first phase, which he called the Battle of Sidi Barrani, destroyed the greater part of five divisions and took over 38,000 prisoners and 400 guns for 133 British dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A minister told the Lords on 19 December that what began as a large-scale raid had been exploited with great élan over about 225 kilometres (140 miles) from railhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 6th Australian Division then took Bardia, where Wavell counted 45,000 prisoners, and Tobruk, where nearly 30,000 more were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 7 February a force never larger than two divisions had advanced about 800 kilometres (500 miles) and taken some 130,000 prisoners, at a cost of 500 killed, 1,373 wounded and 55 missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill admitted in April that Britain would have been content to halt at Tobruk, and the scale of the defeat led Mussolini to ask Germany for help, so that German troops landed at Tripoli on 11 February 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XIV, 257–75, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-14.html. [Open access]","Archibald P. Wavell, \"Operations in the Western Desert from December 7th, 1940, to February 7th, 1941,\" despatch of 21 June 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37628, 26 June 1946, 3261–70, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37628/supplement/3261. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"Operations in Northern Africa,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 December 1940, vol. 118, cols. 179–82, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1940/dec/19/operations-in-northern-africa. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>To Benghazi</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1961), chap. 6, 131–42, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417334. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XIX, 351–69, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-19.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Afrikafeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/afrikafeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Wavell attack before the Italians moved again?","a":"Because he judged the Italian camps around Sidi Barrani to be widely separated and badly placed, and thought a short, swift blow could succeed despite British inferiority in numbers."},{"q":"Why was the attack on Nibeiwa such a surprise?","a":"Because the plan was kept secret from all but a few commanders, the assault came from the rear at an unexpected moment, and it was led by heavy Matilda tanks the Italians did not know were there."},{"q":"Why did the British advance not simply stop after Sidi Barrani?","a":"Because the Italian collapse made it possible to gain ground easily and cheaply, so the raid was exploited into a pursuit through Bardia and Tobruk and across Cyrenaica."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Italian_soldiers_taken_prisoner_during_Operation_Compass.jpg","title":"Italian prisoners after Bardia, January 1941","desc":"A column of Italian soldiers captured in the assault on Bardia marches to a British base on 6 January 1941, photographed by the Army Film and Photographic Unit.","credit":"Photograph by Capt. G. Keating, No. 1 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum E 1579, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_soldiers_taken_prisoner_during_Operation_Compass.jpg","alt":"A dense column of men in greatcoats and caps walking towards the camera across flat, empty desert under a pale sky."},"wiki":"Operation Compass"},
    "ww2-245": {"questions":["A small force on wheels astride the main road, led by the 2nd Rifle Brigade, held off column after column of Italian tanks and lorries in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in February 1941.","General Bergonzoli surrendered unconditionally after a final attempt by about 30 tanks failed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Tenth Army's commander had been mortally wounded."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Beda Fomm</b> was fought on 5–7 February 1941 on the coast road south of Benghazi, where British armour cut off the Italian Tenth Army as it retreated from Cyrenaica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Air reports on 2 and 3 February showed that the Italians were abandoning Cyrenaica, and General O'Connor decided not to wait for two fresh tank regiments due a week later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sent the 7th Armoured Division across about 240 kilometres (150 miles) of unreconnoitred desert from Mechili by way of Msus, with supplies so close-run that it was kept going only just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meanwhile the 6th Australian Division pressed along the roads over the Jebel Akhdar and reached Benghazi before nightfall on 6 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The air commander reported that the interception, made about 50 kilometres (30 miles) south of Soluch on 5 February, completely surprised the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A small column on wheels under Lieutenant-Colonel Combe reached the main road near Beda Fomm about midday on 5 February, and the 2nd Rifle Brigade established itself astride it that afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Through 6 February column after column of Italian tanks and lorries tried to break through, while the 4th Armoured Brigade attacked them from the flank around a low hillock the British called the Pimple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shortly after dawn on 7 February a final attempt by about 30 tanks failed and General Bergonzoli surrendered unconditionally, the army commander General Tellera having been mortally wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British counts differ: Wavell put the losses of this final phase at about 20,000 men, 120 tanks and 190 guns, while the official history counted some 25,000 prisoners and more than 100 medium tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill admitted in April that Britain would have been content to halt at Tobruk, while the threatened loss of Libya led Mussolini to ask Germany for help and German troops landed at Tripoli on 11 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XIX, 354–62, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-19.html. [Open access]","Archibald P. Wavell, \"Operations in the Western Desert from December 7th, 1940, to February 7th, 1941,\" despatch of 21 June 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37628, 26 June 1946, 3261–70, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37628/supplement/3261. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>To Benghazi</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1961), chap. 11, 263–72, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417339. [Open access]","Arthur Longmore, \"Air Operations in the Middle East from January 1st, 1941, to May 3rd, 1941,\" despatch of 24 November 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37729, 19 September 1946, 4673–84, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37729/supplement/4673. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Afrikafeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/afrikafeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did O'Connor send his tanks across the desert instead of along the coast?","a":"Because the Italians were withdrawing along the coast road, and a dash across the desert by way of Msus could reach the road south of Benghazi ahead of them and cut off their retreat."},{"q":"Why could such a small blocking force hold the road?","a":"Because the Italian columns arrived piecemeal and their attacks, though pressed with vigour, lacked co-ordination, while the 4th Armoured Brigade kept striking them from the flank."},{"q":"Why do the figures for the Italian losses differ?","a":"Because Wavell's despatch and the British official history counted differently, the first giving about 20,000 men for the final phase and the second about 25,000 prisoners."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Tobruk_Agedabia_road2.jpg","title":"Northern Cyrenaica and its roads","desc":"A modern map of northern Cyrenaica showing the coast road through Benghazi and the desert tracks by Mechili, Msus and Antelat along which British armour moved to cut the road south of Benghazi.","credit":"Map by Maher27777, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tobruk_Agedabia_road2.jpg","alt":"A map of the bulge of eastern Libya marking Benghazi, Msus, Mechili, Antelat, Ajedabia, Derna and Tobruk, with the coast road and desert tracks drawn between them."},"wiki":"Battle of Beda Fomm"},
    "ww2-246": {"questions":["Wavell's first aim in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was limited, to secure his flanks at Kassala and Kismayu and to make the Italian position impossible by fomenting the patriot rising in Ethiopia.","The surrender of Gondar on 27 November 1941, with 10,000 Italian and 12,000 colonial prisoners, closed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had begun with Italian gains in the Sudan and Somaliland."],"abstract":"The <b>East African campaign</b> was the British and Commonwealth conquest in 1940–41 of Italian East Africa, made up of Eritrea, Italian Somaliland and the Ethiopian empire Italy had conquered before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Italy entered the war Wavell estimated over 200,000 Italian troops in East Africa, and in the first months they took Kassala and frontier posts in the Sudan and overran British Somaliland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wavell's own aim at first was limited, to secure his flanks at Kassala and Kismayu and to make the Italian position impossible by fomenting the patriot rising inside Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The campaign therefore grew out of events rather than a single design, an improvisation after the British fashion of war, as Wavell later called it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eden told the Commons in February 1941 that Britain would welcome the reappearance of an independent Ethiopian state, recognised Haile Selassie's claim to the throne and had no territorial ambitions in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> From the Sudan General Platt pursued the Italians from Kassala in January and stormed Keren in March after hard fighting that, Churchill told the Commons, cost about 4,000 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From Kenya General Cunningham, supported by the South African Air Force, took Kismayu and Mogadishu in February and then pursued the Italians for over 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) to Addis Ababa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Gojjam the Ethiopian patriots and a small force under Colonel Wingate worked eastwards ahead of the Emperor, who entered his capital on 5 May 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Duke of Aosta capitulated at Amba Alagi in May, and a peer who had spent most of his life with the Indian Army told the Lords that troops from practically every dominion and African colony of the empire had taken part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The last Italian stronghold, Gondar, fell on 27 November 1941 with 10,000 Italian and 12,000 colonial prisoners, completing the downfall of Italian rule in East Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XXI, 391–406, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-21.html. [Open access]","Archibald P. Wavell, \"Operations in East Africa, November, 1940–July, 1941,\" despatch of 21 May 1942, <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37645, 10 July 1946, 3527–30, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37645/page/3527. [Open access]","Archibald P. Wavell, \"Operations in the Middle East from August, 1939 to November, 1940,\" despatch of 10 December 1940, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37609, 13 June 1946, 2997–3004, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37609/supplement/2997. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. XVI, 303–21, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-16.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"East African Campaigns,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 May 1941, vol. 119, cols. 297–311, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1941/may/28/east-african-campaigns. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Abyssinia (Status),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 February 1941, vol. 368, cols. 804, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/feb/04/abyssinia-status. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Wavell at first plan only limited attacks?","a":"Because he wanted to secure his flanks at Kassala and Kismayu and to rely on the patriot rising in Ethiopia to make the Italian position impossible, while sending troops north where they were needed."},{"q":"Why did the Emperor's return matter to British policy?","a":"Because Britain had declared that it would welcome an independent Ethiopian state, recognised Haile Selassie's claim to the throne and disclaimed any territorial ambitions in the country."},{"q":"Why did so many different peoples fight in this campaign?","a":"Because, as a peer told the Lords, troops came from practically every dominion and African colony of the empire, fighting alongside the South African Air Force and the Ethiopian patriots."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/Haile_Selassie%2C_Emperor_of_Abyssinia%2C_with_Brigadier_Daniel_Arthur_Sandford_%28left%29_and_Colonel_Wingate_%28right%29_in_Dambacha_Fort%2C_after_it_had_been_captured%2C_15_April_1941._E2462.jpg","title":"Haile Selassie at Dambacha Fort, April 1941","desc":"The Emperor Haile Selassie with Brigadier Daniel Arthur Sandford, left, and Colonel Wingate, right, in Dambacha Fort in Gojjam, after its capture on 15 April 1941.","credit":"British official photograph, Imperial War Museum E 2462, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haile_Selassie,_Emperor_of_Abyssinia,_with_Brigadier_Daniel_Arthur_Sandford_(left)_and_Colonel_Wingate_(right)_in_Dambacha_Fort,_after_it_had_been_captured,_15_April_1941._E2462.jpg","alt":"A seated bearded man in uniform at a table spread with a map, flanked by a tall officer in shorts and a bearded man in a sweater pointing at the map."},"wiki":"East African campaign (World War II)"},
    "ww2-247": {"questions":["Photographs taken by Glenn Martin aircraft from Malta showed the berths, balloons and nets that shaped the plan for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which a hangar fire had postponed from Trafalgar Day.","Churchill told the Commons that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> affected decisively the balance of naval power in the Mediterranean, and every Italian battleship able to steam soon left for the west coast."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Taranto</b> was the night attack of 11–12 November 1940 in which torpedo aircraft from the carrier Illustrious struck the Italian battle fleet at anchor in its own base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Taranto, in the heel of Italy, sheltered all six Italian battleships by October 1940, and from there the fleet was well placed to control the central Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American diplomat in Rome had reported in October that ships and parachutists were gathering at Taranto and Bari for an attack on Corfu, part of the war Italy was opening against Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Admiral Cunningham wrote that a torpedo attack on the anchorage by the Fleet Air Arm had been considered for many months, long before Italy entered the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made it possible was photographic reconnaissance by Royal Air Force Glenn Martins flying from Malta, which showed the battleships' berths and the balloons and nets protecting them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A hangar fire aboard Illustrious ruled out the first date, Trafalgar Day on 21 October, and the attack went in 3 weeks later with 21 Swordfish instead of the 30 intended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aircraft flew off about 270 kilometres (170 miles) from the target in two waves an hour apart, flares lighting the battleships from the east while torpedo aircraft came down as low as 9 metres (30 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eleven torpedoes and the loss of two aircraft put half the Italian battle fleet out of action, and in February 1941 the Admiralty told the Commons that one battleship had apparently been abandoned as a total loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons the next day that the result affected decisively the balance of naval power in the Mediterranean, and Cunningham called it an example of economy of force probably unsurpassed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 12 November every Italian warship able to steam left Taranto for safer ports on the west coast of Italy, and Italian admirals were afterwards warned to avoid damage to what remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew B. Cunningham, \"Fleet Air Arm Operations against Taranto on 11th November, 1940,\" despatch of 16 January 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38023, 24 July 1947, 3471–75, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38023/supplement/3471. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Fleet Air Arm Success,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 13 November 1940, vol. 365, cols. 1712–14, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/nov/13/fleet-air-arm-success. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XII, 234–38, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-12.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. XVI, 299–314, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-16.html. [Open access]","G. Hermon Gill, <i>Royal Australian Navy, 1939–1942</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 2, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 5, 231–36, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417446. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Damaged Italian Fleet, Taranto (Awards),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 26 February 1941, vol. 369, cols. 502–3, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/feb/26/damaged-italian-fleettaranto-awards. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Italy (Reed) to the Secretary of State, October 22, 1940, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d424. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the attack delayed until November?","a":"Because a fire in Illustrious' hangar ruled out the first date, Trafalgar Day on 21 October, so the attack went in 3 weeks later and with fewer aircraft than the 30 intended."},{"q":"Why did reconnaissance from Malta matter so much?","a":"Because the plan depended on knowing exactly where the battleships lay, and the photographs also revealed the balloons and nets that changed the method of attack."},{"q":"Why did the attack change how the Italian fleet behaved?","a":"Because the fleet left Taranto for ports on the west coast of Italy, and its admirals were afterwards told to avoid damage to the ships that remained."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Taranto_1940_%283%29.JPG","title":"Taranto from the air, November 1940","desc":"An aerial photograph of Taranto taken after the attack, from an album now in the Australian War Memorial, whose caption points out an Italian battleship down by the bows and beached at the far right.","credit":"Photograph from the album of C. Oliver, Australian War Memorial, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taranto_1940_(3).JPG","alt":"A grainy black-and-white aerial view of a curving shoreline, with a town and fields below and a few ships on the dark water above."},"wiki":"Battle of Taranto"},
    "ww2-248": {"questions":["An Italian raid on the convoys carrying British troops to Greece, made under German pressure, ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with three heavy cruisers and two destroyers sunk.","Cunningham wrote that the later operations off Greece and Crete were conducted under the cover of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which deterred the Italian fleet from interfering again."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Cape Matapan</b> was fought on 28–29 March 1941 south of Greece between the British Mediterranean Fleet and an Italian force, and it ended with three Italian heavy cruisers and two destroyers sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Italian sortie, decided on after German pressure, was a raid on Allied shipping south of Crete and in the Aegean by one battleship, eight cruisers and thirteen destroyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its obvious targets were the convoys of Operation Lustre, which between 4 March and 24 April carried some 58,000 troops with their transport and stores to Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Admiral Cunningham took three battleships and the carrier Formidable out of Alexandria after dark on 27 March, after a flying boat had sighted Italian cruisers steering towards Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Next morning the battleship Vittorio Veneto nearly trapped a squadron of British cruisers, which escaped when torpedo aircraft from Formidable attacked her, while Blenheims from Greece flew 24 sorties against the Italian ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In the afternoon a torpedo hit slowed the Vittorio Veneto, and at dusk another aircraft stopped the heavy cruiser Pola, whose loss of electrical power put all her turrets out of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cunningham chose to risk a night action, and when the cruisers Zara and Fiume came back to help the Pola his battleships, closing on the stopped ship found by the Valiant's radar, wrecked them at about 3,700 metres (4,000 yards).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Italians had no radar and kept their heavy guns unmanned at night, and they lost about 2,400 officers and men, while Cunningham reported one aircraft lost and no other damage or casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Vittorio Veneto escaped, but Churchill told the Commons that the battle had decisively broken Italian naval power in the Mediterranean, and in the Lords it was compared with Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its lasting result, following Taranto, was to deter the Italian fleet from interfering in the operations off Greece and Crete, which Cunningham wrote were conducted under its cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew B. Cunningham, \"Battle of Matapan,\" despatch of 11 November 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38031, 31 July 1947, 3591–605, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38031/supplement/3591. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. IV, 61–69, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-4.html. [Open access]","G. Hermon Gill, <i>Royal Australian Navy, 1939–1942</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 2, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 8, 309–17, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417449. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"The Mediterranean Victory,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 1 April 1941, vol. 118, cols. 928–30, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1941/apr/01/the-mediterranean-victory. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]","Arthur Longmore, \"Air Operations in the Middle East from January 1st, 1941, to May 3rd, 1941,\" despatch of 24 November 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37729, 19 September 1946, 4673–84, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37729/supplement/4673. [Open access]","Andrew B. Cunningham, \"Transportation of the Army to Greece and Evacuation of the Army from Greece, 1941,\" despatch of 11 December 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38293, 19 May 1948, 3041–42, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38293/supplement/3041. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Italian fleet put to sea at all?","a":"Because the Germans had pressed for an attack on Allied communications, and the convoys carrying troops and stores to Greece were exposed south of Crete and in the Aegean."},{"q":"Why did a night action favour the British?","a":"Because the British had found the stopped Pola by radar, while the Italians had no radar and kept their heavy guns unmanned at night, so their cruisers were caught unprepared."},{"q":"Why did the battle matter beyond the ships sunk?","a":"Because, following Taranto, it deterred the Italian fleet from interfering with the operations off Greece and Crete that followed in April and May 1941."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Bolzano_under_air_torpedo_attack.jpg","title":"The cruiser Bolzano under air attack, 28 March 1941","desc":"The Italian heavy cruiser Bolzano photographed from a Fleet Air Arm aircraft during the torpedo attacks of 28 March; the British official history notes the splash of a torpedo entering the water.","credit":"Royal Navy photograph, photographer unknown, reproduced in the British official history (HMSO, 1956), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bolzano_under_air_torpedo_attack.jpg","alt":"A warship at speed on a grey sea, seen between the struts of an aircraft, with a small plane low above it and a white splash in the foreground."},"wiki":"Battle of Cape Matapan"},
    "ww2-249": {"questions":["Only 20 minutes' flying time from Sicily, the island was bombed on 114 occasions by March 1941, early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Luftwaffe had made far heavier from January 1941.","Britain promised a free gift of £10,000,000 in November 1942 to rebuild an island whose people had endured the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, attacks of unprecedented length and severity."],"abstract":"The <b>Siege of Malta</b> was the long Axis air and sea assault on the British island colony of Malta, which began with an Italian raid on the morning of 11 June 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island lay barely 20 minutes' flying time from the airfields of Sicily and is smaller than the Isle of Wight, so its air defence had no depth in any direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When ten Italian aircraft first bombed the dockyard and Hal Far airfield, only 34 of the 112 heavy and 8 of the 60 light anti-aircraft guns approved a year earlier were in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By early March 1941 the Air Ministry counted 114 occasions on which Malta had been bombed, and the minister praised the courage and high morale of its civilian population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 2 months later the government would not disclose service casualties and said that particulars of civilian casualties were not available in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Malta mattered because after the fall of France it was the only place from which British naval and air forces could offer a permanent threat to the sea route between Italy and North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reconnaissance aircraft from the island made the Fleet Air Arm's attack on Taranto possible, and submarines and aircraft based there sank Axis ships carrying supplies to Libya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For that reason the Luftwaffe began violent attacks in January 1941 to neutralise the island while German troops crossed to Tripoli, and Hitler had a plan for its capture prepared, then shelved it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German Historical Museum notes that in the summer of 1942 about two thirds of German and Italian supplies for North Africa were lost to submarines and aircraft operating from Malta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In November 1942 the British government promised Malta a free gift of £10,000,000 for rebuilding, in recognition of its people's gallantry under attacks of unprecedented length and severity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. VI, 109–24, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-6.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. III, 43–59, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-3.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Malta (Air Raids),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 March 1941, vol. 369, cols. 1145–46, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/mar/11/malta-air-raids. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Malta War Damage (British Government's Gift),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 10 November 1942, vol. 383, cols. 2260–61, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/nov/10/malta-war-damage-british-governments-gift. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Afrikafeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/afrikafeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]","Andrew B. Cunningham, \"Fleet Air Arm Operations against Taranto on 11th November, 1940,\" despatch of 16 January 1941, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38023, 24 July 1947, 3471–75, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38023/supplement/3471. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Malta (Air-Raids),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1941, vol. 371, cols. 982–83, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/may/08/malta-air-raids. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Malta so hard to defend from the air?","a":"Because it lay barely 20 minutes' flying time from Sicily and was too small to give the defence any depth, while its guns and fighters fell far short of the approved scale."},{"q":"Why did the Germans attack Malta so heavily in 1941?","a":"Because the island's ships and aircraft threatened the sea route to North Africa, and the Luftwaffe was sent to neutralise it while German troops crossed to Tripoli."},{"q":"Why did Britain promise money to rebuild Malta?","a":"Because the repairs were beyond the island's own resources and the government wanted to recognise its people's gallantry under attacks of unprecedented length and severity."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/BombDamageMalta.jpg","title":"Kingsway, Valletta, April 1942","desc":"Service personnel and civilians clearing debris from Kingsway, the principal street of Valletta, after heavy bombing in April 1942, photographed by a Royal Navy official photographer.","credit":"Photograph by Lt J. E. Russell, Royal Navy official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BombDamageMalta.jpg","alt":"A street lined with shattered stone buildings, heaps of rubble on both sides, and groups of people and a lorry moving along the cleared middle."},"wiki":"Siege of Malta (World War II)"},
    "ww2-250": {"questions":["Bulgaria's parliament approved the entry of German troops by 140 votes to 20 in March 1941, opening the way for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Greece and Yugoslavia.","Churchill described German forces in Bulgaria growing from a few handfuls of tourists to 25 divisions before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck Greece and Yugoslavia on 6 April 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Balkan campaign</b> was Germany's military intervention in south-eastern Europe in the spring of 1941, which ended with the conquest of Yugoslavia and Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By then Germany was already master of Romania and Hungary, both of which had joined the Tripartite Pact in the autumn of 1940, and German troops flowed through Hungary into Romania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Italian failure against Greece changed German plans, and on 12 November 1940 Hitler ordered preparations to occupy the Greek mainland north of the Aegean, entering if necessary through Bulgaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His directive of 13 December for Operation Marita explained the danger of British air bases in Greece to Italy and to the Romanian oilfields, on which Germany depended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American diplomat in Bucharest reported in January 1941 that probably well over 100,000 German troops were already in Romania, many moved south to bridgeheads on the Danube facing Bulgaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Bulgaria signed the Tripartite Pact on 1 March 1941, and its prime minister told parliament that German troops would enter the country, a decision approved by 140 votes to 20 without debate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill described the German force in Bulgaria growing from a few handfuls of tourists to 25 divisions, while British hopes of a united Balkan front failed when Belgrade refused even to receive Eden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The coup in Belgrade at the end of March made Hitler resolve to destroy Yugoslavia as well, and on 6 April German forces invaded both Yugoslavia and Greece without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the German command the British landing in Greece threatened the southern flank of the coming attack on the Soviet Union, and the German Historical Museum puts German losses in both countries at over 12,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler told his people that the attack was needed because British forces in Greece meant to extend the war to the Balkans, but the Nuremberg tribunal concluded that aggression against both countries had long been contemplated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggression against Yugoslavia and Greece,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judyugo.asp. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>Greece, Crete and Syria</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), chap. 1, 1–6, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417347. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Balkanfeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 7 October 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/balkanfeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Middle East Victories,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 April 1941, vol. 370, cols. 1587–605, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/09/middle-east-victories. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Rumania (Gunther) to the Secretary of State, January 7, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d285. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Bulgaria (Earle) to the Secretary of State, March 2, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d306. [Open access]","G. Hermon Gill, <i>Royal Australian Navy, 1939–1942</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 2, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 8, 309–17, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417449. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany turn to the Balkans in late 1940?","a":"Because the Italian attack on Greece had failed and Britain was helping the Greeks, and British air bases in Greece would threaten Italy and the Romanian oilfields on which Germany depended."},{"q":"Why did Bulgaria matter so much to the German plan?","a":"Because German troops gathered in Romania had to cross the Danube and pass through Bulgaria to reach northern Greece, and Bulgaria's accession to the Tripartite Pact opened that road."},{"q":"Why did the campaign widen to include Yugoslavia?","a":"Because the coup in Belgrade at the end of March overthrew the government that had joined the pact, and Hitler resolved to destroy Yugoslavia along with Greece."}],"wiki":"Balkans campaign (World War II)"},
    "ww2-291": {"questions":["Hitler chose Koch to govern the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at a conference on 16 July 1941, saying Ukraine would be the most important district for the next 3 years.","Galicia went to the General Government and Transnistria to Romania, leaving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> some 339,275 square kilometres with about 17 million people under German civil rule."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichskommissariat Ukraine</b> was the German civil administration that ruled most of occupied Ukraine from 1 September 1941, with its capital not at Kiev but at Rivne in Volhynia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler's decree of 17 July 1941 appointed Alfred Rosenberg Reich Minister for the Occupied Eastern Territories and divided the conquered land into Reich commissariats, general regions and districts, each under a German commissar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was one of four commissariats Rosenberg planned, and its counterpart in the Baltic lands was to go to Gauleiter Hinrich Lohse, whom Rosenberg had already approached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler kept much of Ukraine out of it, giving Galicia to the General Government and northern Bukovina and Transnistria to Romania, so that it finally covered 339,275 square kilometres (131,000 square miles) with about 17 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rival powers answering to Berlin worked inside it, from Himmler's police and Göring's economic staff to Sauckel's recruiters of labour for Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The man who ran it was Erich Koch, Gauleiter of East Prussia, whom Hitler preferred at the July conference because Ukraine would be the most important district for the next 3 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rosenberg warned at that meeting that Koch might soon refuse to obey his instructions, and in practice Koch, well connected to Bormann, neutralised most of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Kiev on 5 March 1943 Koch told party members that they were a master race and that the lowliest German worker was a thousand times more valuable than the local population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under his rule food was cut, schooling stopped at four grades, and an order to security officers in Ukraine, quoted at Nuremberg, allowed villages to be burned and whole populations handed over by force for labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg judgment held Rosenberg responsible for setting up the administration that carried out these policies, and the commissariat was formally dissolved on 10 November 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Ihor Kamenetsky and Myroslav Yurkevich, \"Reichskommissariat Ukraine,\" <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies; originally <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 4, 1993), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CR%5CE%5CReichskommissariatUkraine.htm. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, \"Decree of the Fuehrer concerning the administration of the newly-occupied Eastern territories,\" 17 July 1941, Document 1997-PS, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1997-ps.asp. [Open access]","Martin Bormann, memorandum for the record of the conference at the Führer's headquarters, 16 July 1941, Document L-221, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 7 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 1086–93, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag07unit. [Open access]","Army Group B, Military Government, report on the speech of Reichskommissar Erich Koch in Kiev on 5 March 1943, 1 April 1943, Document 1130-PS, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 797–98, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag03unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: Rosenberg,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judrosen.asp. [Open access]","Vasyl Markus, \"Koch, Erich,\" <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies; originally <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 2, 1988), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CK%5CO%5CKochErich.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the capital placed at Rivne rather than Kiev?","a":"The encyclopedia of the Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies explains the choice as part of a wider policy: Hitler refused to sanction a Ukrainian government and cut territories out of the commissariat to weaken Ukrainian hopes of independence, and the old capital was passed over in favour of a town in Volhynia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rosenberg have so little control over Koch?","a":"Hitler appointed Koch over Rosenberg's own preference, Göring told the July conference that appointees should work independently, and Koch's good connections with Bormann let him neutralise most of the minister's instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German rule push Ukrainians towards resistance?","a":"Food supplies were cut, schools and cultural bodies closed, and labour was taken by force; in Volhynia, Koch's own area, his methods prompted the formation of the Ukrainian Insurgent Army in 1942, and Soviet partisans opposed him too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1969-067-01%2C_Kiew%2C_Alfred_Meyer%2C_Erich_Koch%2C_Alfred_Rosenberg.jpg","title":"Rosenberg's visit to Kiev, June 1942","desc":"Party and SS officials during Reichsleiter Alfred Rosenberg's visit to Kiev in June 1942; the archive names Rosenberg, Erich Koch and Alfred Meyer among them.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv Bild 146-1969-067-01, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1969-067-01,_Kiew,_Alfred_Meyer,_Erich_Koch,_Alfred_Rosenberg.jpg","alt":"A group of men in peaked caps and party and SS uniforms; the man at the centre wears a long double-breasted greatcoat."},"wiki":"Reichskommissariat Ukraine"},
    "ww2-292": {"questions":["Some 11,000 track demolitions and 9,000 derailed trains were counted in 1943, the year the railway war of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached its height behind the Eastern Front.","An American army study of 1956 judged that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hurt the Wehrmacht but never decisively, while judging that they would have made any lasting German administration extremely difficult."],"abstract":"<b>Soviet partisans</b> were the irregular fighters who waged war behind the German lines in the occupied Soviet Union from 1941 to 1944, living in hidden camps in the forests and marshes of Belorussia, Ukraine and Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 3 July 1941 Stalin told the country by radio that partisan units and sabotage groups must be formed in occupied areas to blow up bridges and roads and make conditions unbearable for the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first bands were made up mostly of soldiers cut off by the encirclements and young Communists, and a Central Staff of the Partisan Movement was set up in Moscow on 30 May 1942 to bring them under control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their numbers are known only roughly, rising from about 30,000 in January 1942 to about 200,000 in summer 1943, with perhaps 400,000 to 500,000 Soviet citizens taking part over the whole war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German officers writing after the war called the movement the Soviet second front behind the German lines, a kind of blockade that small forces could carry out against the supply lines to the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Their sharpest weapon was the war on the railways, and in 1943 alone some 11,000 track demolitions and 9,000 derailed trains were counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A German war historian swore at Nuremberg that a monthly report recorded 800 to 1,000 attacks on the railways in the Russian area, costing 200 to 300 locomotives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German answer was terror: Reichenau complained in October 1941 that partisans were still being taken prisoner, and Keitel's order of 16 September spoke of fifty or a hundred lives for every German killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Ukraine, where cover was scarce outside the north-east and support was thin, the movement became strong only in mid-1943 and grew rapidly as the Red Army advanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American army history of 1956 judged that the bands hurt the Wehrmacht but never decisively, though 100,000 and more partisans backed by a hostile population would have made a lasting German administration extremely difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Edgar M. Howell, <i>The Soviet Partisan Movement, 1941–1944</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-244 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1956), 45–46, 66, 209–11, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-244. [Open access]","Thomas Jander, \"Der Partisanenkrieg in der Sowjetunion,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/partisanenkrieg-im-osten. [in German] [Open access]","Andrij Makuch, Lev Shankovsky and Yevhen Stakhiv, \"Soviet partisans in Ukraine, 1941–5,\" <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies; originally <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 5, 1993), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CS%5CO%5CSovietpartisansinUkraine1941hD75.htm. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","<i>Rear Area Security in Russia: The Soviet Second Front Behind the German Lines</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-240 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-240. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4, proceedings of 7 January 1946, affidavit of Wilhelm Scheidt (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]","Walter von Reichenau, \"Conduct of Troops in Eastern Territories,\" Army Command 6, 10 October 1941, forwarded by the High Command of the Army on 28 October 1941, Document D-411, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 7 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 51–53, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag07unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the partisans concentrate in forests and marshes?","a":"They could not survive in open country against German troops, so the bands that were not broken up gathered in the Pripyat Marshes, the woods of Belorussia and the trackless land south of Lake Ilmen, where terrain protected their bases and made German counter-action difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the railways their main target?","a":"The German armies were supplied by rail across huge distances, so every demolition, derailed train and lost locomotive weakened the front; German officers described the method as a blockade that small forces could carry out against the enemy's lifeline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did civilians suffer from both sides?","a":"Partisans often took food and clothing by force from villagers already struggling to survive, while German sweeps against them burned villages and killed suspects and bystanders alike, so the rural population was caught between two violent forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Pakhomov%27s_partizan_brigade_November_1941.jpg/1280px-Pakhomov%27s_partizan_brigade_November_1941.jpg","title":"Partisans near Moscow, November 1941","desc":"The partisan detachment of the Solnechnogorsk district under Aleksei Pakhomov, photographed in the Moscow region on 24–25 November 1941.","credit":"Photograph by M. Bachurin, Main Archival Directorate of Moscow (Mos.ru), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pakhomov's_partizan_brigade_November_1941.jpg","alt":"A line of men in fur hats and winter coats holding rifles, standing in the snow in front of bare trees and a hut."},"wiki":"Soviet partisans"},
    "ww2-293": {"questions":["In autumn 1942 the German army proposed freeing some 180,000 soldiers from the rear by having <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recruited among Russian prisoners take over service and supply jobs behind the lines.","Of the 1,790,138 men the German army had on the Eastern Front on 1 October 1944, about 150,000 were <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Soviet auxiliaries serving in German units."],"abstract":"<b>Hilfswillige</b>, ‘willing helpers’, shortened by German soldiers to Hiwis, were Soviet citizens, mostly prisoners of war, who served with German units on the Eastern Front in non-combatant jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They came from a captive population that German policy treated with deadly neglect, for of some 5.7 million Soviet soldiers taken prisoner about 3.3 million were dead by the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The museum account reckons that the German army released about one million Soviet prisoners as auxiliaries of the army and the SS, a total for auxiliaries of every kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study written for the US Army by former German generals says that many volunteers from the camps replaced soldiers taken out of supply and service units, and that in general they proved quite dependable and useful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same authors rated the indigenous combat units far lower, and an army history of the partisan war lists Hiwis among the mixed forces in the rear, beside native security battalions and local volunteer units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By autumn 1942 the German army was short of men, and its Organisational Branch proposed freeing some 180,000 soldiers from the rear by having Hiwis recruited among Russian prisoners take over service and supply work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The auxiliaries went where the army went, and on 18 December 1942 the encircled Sixth Army at Stalingrad reported a ration strength of 246,000, including 13,000 Romanians, 19,300 Russian auxiliaries and 6,000 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the lines, local Hilfswillige also served beside police, SS and army troops in the hunting detachments sent against the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The numbers stayed large as the German army shrank, and on 1 October 1944 its strength on the Eastern Front stood at 1,790,138, of whom about 150,000 were Hiwis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For a captured Red Army soldier the choice was made against camps in which, by the museum's account, 2 million Soviet prisoners had died within 8 months of the invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 532, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 20, 69, 412, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]","<i>Military Improvisations During the Russian Campaign</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 79, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","Edgar M. Howell, <i>The Soviet Partisan Movement, 1941–1944</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-244 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1956), 148, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-244. [Open access]","Thomas Jander, \"Der Partisanenkrieg in der Sowjetunion,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/partisanenkrieg-im-osten. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Military and the Holocaust,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-military-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Soviet prisoners volunteer to serve the Germans?","a":"The sources point to the camps themselves: German policy left Soviet prisoners without shelter, food or care, and about 3.3 million of them died, so service as an auxiliary was a way out of conditions in which millions were dying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German army want them?","a":"It was running short of men, and every auxiliary who drove, carried or cooked behind the lines released a German soldier for the front; in autumn 1942 the army planned to free some 180,000 men in this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were they kept mostly out of combat?","a":"The former German generals who wrote about the campaign judged indigenous combat units far below German standards and said front-line troops usually objected to them, while volunteers in supply and service jobs were found quite dependable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-004-3632-24%2C_Russland%2C_einheimische_Freiwillige_der_Wehrmacht.jpg","title":"Soviet volunteers of the Wehrmacht, 1942","desc":"A German propaganda company photograph of January 1942, captioned by the archive as Soviet volunteers of the Wehrmacht, decorated with assault badges, with their relatives.","credit":"Photograph by Elle, Bundesarchiv Bild 101I-004-3632-24, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-004-3632-24,_Russland,_einheimische_Freiwillige_der_Wehrmacht.jpg","alt":"Two young men in belted tunics and caps, with round badges on their chests, stand among older villagers, men in caps and women in headscarves."},"wiki":"Hiwi (volunteer)"},
    "ww2-294": {"questions":["Hitler called Reichenau's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> excellent, and on 28 October 1941 the army high command sent it to other commands asking for similar instructions.","Questioned at Nuremberg about Reichenau's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Manstein said he had rejected it, until shown his own order calling for the extermination of the Jewish-Bolshevik system."],"abstract":"The <b>Severity Order</b> was the instruction on the conduct of troops in the eastern territories that Field Marshal Walter von Reichenau, commanding the Sixth Army, issued on 10 October 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It told soldiers that the essential aim of the war against the 'Jewish-Bolshevik system' was the complete destruction of its means of power, and that a soldier in the East was not only a fighter but the bearer of a ruthless national ideology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The soldier must therefore understand the necessity of 'a severe but just revenge on subhuman Jewry', the order said, while partisans and 'unnatural women' were not to be treated as prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feeding civilians and prisoners from army kitchens was condemned as misunderstood humanity, and neither historic nor artistic considerations were to count in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It set the soldier two tasks, the complete annihilation of the Bolshevik doctrine and armed forces and the pitiless extermination of foreign treachery and cruelty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 28 October the High Command of the Army sent copies to the other commands, saying that Hitler had called the order excellent and asking for similar instructions where none had been issued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It added to the criminal orders issued before the invasion, which had freed soldiers from prosecution for crimes in the Soviet Union and demanded ruthless action against agitators, saboteurs and Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It came less than a fortnight after the massacre at Babi Yar outside Kiev, where 33,771 Jews were shot in 2 days after the city's German military commander met SS officials to arrange the killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Nuremberg in August 1946 Erich von Manstein said he had turned the order down and knew no commander who gave it weight, until he was shown his own order of 20 November 1941 calling for the Jewish-Bolshevik system to be exterminated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reichenau took over Army Group South from Rundstedt in the command changes of that winter, and the museum counts such orders among those that made the eastern war one of racial annihilation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Walter von Reichenau, \"Conduct of Troops in Eastern Territories,\" Army Command 6, 10 October 1941, forwarded by the High Command of the Army on 28 October 1941, Document D-411, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 7 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 51–53, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag07unit. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Military and the Holocaust,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-military-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Army and the Racial Nature of the War against the Soviet Union,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-army-and-the-racial-nature-of-the-war-against-the-soviet-union. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 10 August 1946, cross-examination of Erich von Manstein (Nuremberg, 1948), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-10-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Winterschlacht 1941/42,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winterschlacht-194142. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kiev and Babi Yar,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kiev-and-babi-yar. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the High Command spread one general's order to the whole army?","a":"Hitler had described it as excellent, and the High Command of the Army forwarded it on his authority, asking other commanders to issue instructions on the same lines if they had not already done so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the order attack feeding civilians and prisoners?","a":"It treated food from army kitchens as part of the army's own hard-won supply and called giving it away a misunderstood humanitarian act, which fitted a war in which German planners accepted that millions of Soviet people would starve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the order matter in arguments about the army's guilt?","a":"It shows a senior field commander, and Hitler's approval of him, tying the army's war to revenge on Jews; Manstein's denial at Nuremberg collapsed when a similar order of his own was produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B05284%2C_Walter_v._Reichenau.jpg","title":"Walter von Reichenau","desc":"Field Marshal Walter von Reichenau, commander of the German Sixth Army when he issued the order, in a portrait the archive dates to 1941.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B05284, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B05284,_Walter_v._Reichenau.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of a grey-haired officer in uniform with oak-leaf collar patches, a monocle and an Iron Cross at his throat."},"wiki":"Severity Order"},
    "ww2-295": {"questions":["Editors of the studies former German generals wrote for the US Army kept the authors' prejudices, and such accounts helped spread the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after 1945.","In March 1997 a Bundestag deputy attacked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the lie that only the SS was to blame and that the army had fought a clean campaign in the East."],"abstract":"The <b>clean Wehrmacht myth</b> is the belief, widespread after 1945, that the German armed forces fought a conventional war in the Soviet Union and had no part in the Holocaust or the regime's other crimes, which were laid at the door of the SS.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Holocaust Memorial Museum in Washington traces it to Nuremberg, where the SS was declared a criminal organisation and the military was not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal did refuse to declare the General Staff and High Command criminal, but only because some 130 officers holding certain posts were not a 'group' in the meaning of its charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same passage it called them a ruthless military caste and a disgrace to the honourable profession of arms who should be tried where the facts warranted it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fourteen senior commanders were indicted in the American High Command Case, and of the thirteen who reached judgment in October 1948 two received life sentences and nine terms of 3 to 20 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The myth grew from the generals' own accounts, and in August 1946 Erich von Manstein denied that commanders had issued instructions supporting the killing of Jews, until shown such an order with his own signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many former officers then wrote studies for the US Army, and the editors of one said they had tried to keep the point of view, the expressions and even the prejudices of the authors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Another, on the partisan war, spoke of a natural Slav inclination to fight from ambush, and its American editors left such passages unchanged as the author's prejudices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The first great public challenge came with the Hamburg exhibition on the crimes of the Wehrmacht from 1941 to 1944, which opened in March 1995 and travelled to 33 German and Austrian cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Bundestag debated it in March 1997, a Green deputy said the aim was to end the lie that the SS alone was to blame and that the Wehrmacht had fought a clean campaign in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Military and the Holocaust,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-military-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Accused Organisations,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judorg.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 10 August 1946, cross-examination of Erich von Manstein (Nuremberg, 1948), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-10-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings, Case #12: The High Command Case,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings-case-12-the-high-command-case. [Open access]","<i>Military Improvisations During the Russian Campaign</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), preface, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","Deutscher Bundestag, <i>Plenarprotokoll 13/163</i>, 13 March 1997, debate on the exhibition \"Vernichtungskrieg. Verbrechen der Wehrmacht 1941 bis 1944,\" 14708–09, https://dserver.bundestag.de/btp/13/13163.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Rear Area Security in Russia: The Soviet Second Front Behind the German Lines</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-240 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), v, 1, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-240. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nuremberg seem to clear the army?","a":"The tribunal declared the SS criminal but not the General Staff and High Command, on the technical ground that the officers were not a group under its charter, even while saying many of them had taken part in war crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the generals' version of the war last so long?","a":"The United States wanted German military expertise for the Cold War, and former generals wrote accounts for American use that concealed the army's part in the Holocaust, while prosecutions in postwar Germany were almost non-existent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the 1995 exhibition so controversial?","a":"It showed in stark detail how deeply the army was involved in the crimes of the war, which struck at a belief held by much of the public, and by 1997 it had become the subject of a full debate in the Bundestag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Neonaziaufmarsch_in_Muenchen.jpg","title":"Marching against the exhibition, Munich 2002","desc":"Opponents of the Wehrmacht exhibition marching in Munich in October 2002 carry posters reading 'Glory and honour to the German soldier!'","credit":"Photograph by Andreas Bohnenstengel, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neonaziaufmarsch_in_Muenchen.jpg","alt":"Young men in dark jackets hold up identical posters of a helmeted soldier above the words 'Ruhm und Ehre dem deutschen Soldaten!'"},"wiki":"Myth of the clean Wehrmacht"},
    "ww2-296": {"questions":["Telephoning Bock at midnight on 16 December 1941, Hitler announced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the words that there was only one correct decision, not to go a step back.","An American army history judged that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may have kept the German armies from disintegrating in winter 1941, but also hardened Hitler's faith in his own judgement."],"abstract":"The <b>stand-fast order</b> was Hitler's command of December 1941 that the German armies before Moscow were to hold their positions with fanatical resistance instead of retreating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The offensive on Moscow had failed at the start of the month, with troops not equipped for temperatures around −40 °C (−40 °F) and frostbite casualties more than double the losses in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Red Army struck first from Kalinin on 5 December and then, under Zhukov, against the armies standing directly before the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One German study records that the troops north-west of Moscow held out until 5 December and that the first retreat order of the war was given the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler's orders of 15 December left commanders some freedom to withdraw, but local retreats in the middle of the month lost so much heavy equipment that he changed his methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At midnight on 16 December he told Field Marshal von Bock by telephone that there was only one correct decision, 'not to go a step back'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order sent to Army Group Centre on 18 December demanded that commanders intervene in person 'to compel the troops to fanatical resistance in their positions', even where the enemy had broken through on the flanks or in the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Brauchitsch resigned as commander-in-chief of the army on 19 December and Hitler took the post himself, while Guderian and Hoepner, who had pulled back against the order, were dismissed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An army history written from the German records judges that the armies, caught without prepared positions or adequate winter clothing, might well have disintegrated had the command conceded a retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It adds that the crisis left Hitler more confident of his own military judgement, and that the rest of the war was dominated by a conflict between his rigidity and the professionals' principles of flexibility and mobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 98–101, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 81–82, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 14, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Winterschlacht 1941/42,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winterschlacht-194142. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Angriff auf Moskau 1941,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Effects of Climate on Combat in European Russia</i>, German Report Series, CMH Pub 104-6, reprint of Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-291 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1952), 4, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-291. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler forbid a general retreat?","a":"Local withdrawals in mid-December had cost heavy weapons and equipment, and he argued that only holding every position could gain time to bring up reinforcements from Germany and the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some generals defy it?","a":"Commanders at the front doubted they could hold at all; Kluge predicted the line would not stand whatever the orders, and Guderian and Hoepner withdrew their troops and were dismissed for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the order matter for the rest of the war?","a":"Because the armies stood and survived, Hitler concluded that his judgement had been right, and the conflict between his rigid orders to hold and his commanders' wish for flexibility shaped German conduct of the war from then on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Wojska_niemieckie_na_froncie_moskiewskim_%282-878%29.jpg/1280px-Wojska_niemieckie_na_froncie_moskiewskim_%282-878%29.jpg","title":"German troops on the Moscow front, December 1941","desc":"German infantry with an assault gun and an armoured car entering a village on the Moscow front in December 1941.","credit":"Photograph by Bohmer, National Digital Archives (Poland), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wojska_niemieckie_na_froncie_moskiewskim_(2-878).jpg","alt":"Soldiers in greatcoats and whitewashed helmets walk along a snowy village street beside a low tracked assault gun, with wooden houses and bare trees behind."}},
    "ww2-297": {"questions":["In the spring <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only the light, high-wheeled Panje cart could move for weeks, riding through the deep mud on the still frozen ground beneath.","Former German generals admitted that neither their commanders nor their intelligence services had grasped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the handbook issued to the troops in 1941 barely mentioned it."],"abstract":"<b>Rasputitsa</b>, ‘the season of bad roads’, is the period in spring and autumn when thaw and rain turn the unsurfaced roads and open country of European Russia into deep mud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A German study of the eastern climate puts the autumn muddy season at about 4 weeks from early October and the spring one at 4 to 6 weeks, ending only when the ground has thawed enough to absorb the melted snow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In spring the thaw worked downwards over ground frozen to a depth of about 2.5 metres (8 or 9 feet), and for 3 weeks or more nothing but peasant carts could move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The light, high-wheeled Panje cart could ride through deep mud on the frozen layer beneath, and its wooden body let it be used almost as a boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Red Army designed its vehicles for these conditions and avoided large operations in the mud, and the thaw of March 1942 brought operations on both sides to a halt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> German planners had not allowed for it, and former German officers admitted that neither the commanders nor the intelligence agencies grasped its implications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The autumn rains began on 7 October 1941, just after the encirclements at Vyazma and Bryansk, and for one panzer division leading the drive on Moscow the mud brought the blitzkrieg to a sudden end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In late October some 6,000 supply trucks loaded with ammunition, rations and fuel piled up on the road between Smolensk and Vyazma, as the approaches to emergency bridges kept sinking into the mud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Slowed and confined to the roads, the Germans met the Red Army on nearly equal terms for almost the first time, and the wider tracks of the T-34 kept it more buoyant in the mud than German tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Zhukov used the pause to strengthen the defences of Moscow with the help of civilians, and the army's own planning study concluded that the change in ground conditions was one of the main reasons the city was not taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Effects of Climate on Combat in European Russia</i>, German Report Series, CMH Pub 104-6, reprint of Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-291 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1952), 29–32, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-291. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 40, 177–78, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 79–81, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Angriff auf Moskau 1941,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Military Improvisations During the Russian Campaign</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 3–4, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","<i>Terrain Factors in the Russian Campaign</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-290 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 10–13, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-290. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 14, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the mud hurt the attacker more than the defender?","a":"An attacker had to move and keep the initiative, while a defender in prepared positions could shift reserves along established communications; the German study says the attacker in mud was confined to local actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Panje cart succeed where trucks failed?","a":"Its high wheels and light weight let it plough through deep mud while riding on the frozen ground below, whereas heavy trucks broke through road surfaces and sank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Germans surprised by something every peasant knew?","a":"They expected to destroy the Red Army quickly, and by their own officers' account neither the commanders nor the intelligence services had understood the muddy season, which the troops' handbook barely mentioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-289-1091-26%2C_Russland%2C_Pferdegespann_im_Schlamm.jpg","title":"Horses sunk in the mud, March 1942","desc":"A German horse team sunk in deep mud in the central sector near Kursk in March 1942, as the spring thaw set in.","credit":"Photograph by Dinstühler, Bundesarchiv Bild 101I-289-1091-26, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-289-1091-26,_Russland,_Pferdegespann_im_Schlamm.jpg","alt":"Horses harnessed to a cart have sunk to their bellies in churned mud on a flat, waterlogged plain while soldiers in greatcoats stand around them."},"wiki":"Rasputitsa"},
    "ww2-298": {"questions":["North-west of Moscow the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1941–1942 brought a January mean of −36 °C (−32 °F) and, on 26 January, the lowest reading of the whole campaign.","A year later the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for once failed the Red Army at Rzhev, where the Germans were better equipped and the weather was milder than in 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Russian Winter</b>, sometimes personified as 'General Winter', is the long, severe winter of European Russia, which German officers who fought there called an ally of the Russian Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> South of the Arctic Circle it sets in suddenly and lasts 5 to 6 months, and the winter of 1941–1942 was the most severe of the whole campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> North-west of Moscow the mean temperature in January 1942 was −36 °C (−32 °F), and on 26 January the same area recorded the campaign's lowest reading, −53 °C (−63 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rifles would not fire, machine guns iced up, recoil liquid froze in the guns, and narrow-tracked German tanks could not move through the snow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One German army counted 14,236 frostbite cases among its 96,535 casualties between January and March 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The planning study written for the US Army by former German officers says the effect of the weather was no secret and that the leaders should not have been surprised by low temperatures in winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Expecting the campaign to end before the bad weather, the command had ordered winter clothing only for the 60 divisions meant to stay in Russia as an occupation force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proper winter clothing reached the front only in early spring 1942, so troops improvised with blankets, sheepskins and the clothing of the dead, and an appeal for furs was launched in Germany shortly before Christmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jews across eastern Europe were forced to give up their warm clothing for the unprepared soldiers that winter, while frostbite casualties ran at more than twice the losses in combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Winter was not invincible: a year later 'General Winter' for once failed the Russians at Rzhev, and Soviet historians held up the summer offensive of 1943 as proof that the Red Army did not need its help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Effects of Climate on Combat in European Russia</i>, German Report Series, CMH Pub 104-6, reprint of Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-291 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1952), 3–6, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-291. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 89, 101, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","<i>Military Improvisations During the Russian Campaign</i>, German Report Series, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 65–66, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Angriff auf Moskau 1941,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Military and the Holocaust,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-military-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 107, 141, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 513–14, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the German troops so badly clothed for the cold?","a":"Hitler and his advisers were sure the campaign would end before the bad weather, so winter clothing was ordered only for the 60 divisions meant to occupy Russia afterwards, and the proper issue arrived only in spring 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 'General Winter' a misleading explanation for German defeat?","a":"The German officers' own study says the weather was no secret and should not have surprised anyone, and a year later the Germans were better equipped and the winter failed the Russians; Soviet histories pointed to the summer of 1943 as victory without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was cold so dangerous to weapons as well as men?","a":"Extreme cold jammed rifle bolts, iced up machine guns and froze the recoil liquid in artillery, while narrow German tank tracks could not cope with deep snow, so firepower fell just as the Soviet attacks came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/General_Winter.jpg/1280px-General_Winter.jpg","title":"'General Winter', 1916","desc":"A French magazine cover of 9 January 1916 shows 'General Winter' as a giant Russian figure looming over a frozen battlefield of the First World War.","credit":"Illustration by Louis Bombled, <i>Le Petit Journal</i>, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Winter.jpg","alt":"A giant white-bearded figure in a fur hat and red coat crouches over a snowy battlefield strewn with fallen soldiers, horses and abandoned guns."},"wiki":"Russian Winter"},
    "ww2-299": {"questions":["Plans for the opening phase of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell into Soviet hands on 19 June 1942 when a German staff officer's plane strayed across the front and landed behind the lines.","Paulus told the Nuremberg tribunal that before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Hitler declared at Poltava that if he did not get the oil of Maikop and Grozny he must end the war."],"abstract":"<b>Case Blue</b>, in German Fall Blau, was the German summer offensive of 1942 in southern Russia, aimed at the Don bend and the oil of the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler conceived it himself and dictated the final text of Directive No. 41, issued on 5 April 1942, changing the code name from Siegfried to Blau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The directive told Army Group Centre to hold and Army Group North to take Leningrad, while every available force went south to destroy the enemy in the Don bend, seize the Caucasus oil and cross the mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalingrad was to be taken or at least brought within range of German artillery, and the long flank along the Don was to be held by allied armies with German divisions behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back from Moscow, Churchill told the Commons on 8 September that Finnish, Hungarian, Romanian and Italian troops more than made up for the German divisions kept in the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Molotov had warned Roosevelt on 30 May that if no second front drew German divisions away, Hitler might win the foodstuffs of Ukraine and the oil wells of the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> 2 days later, Friedrich Paulus told the Nuremberg tribunal, Hitler declared at Poltava, 'If I do not get the oil of Maikop and Grosny, then I must end this war.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 19 June Major Joachim Reichel flew over the front carrying plans for the first phase and came down behind Soviet lines, but Stalin dismissed the captured papers as a trumped-up piece of intelligence work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offensive opened on 28 June along a front of some 800 kilometres (500 miles) between Kursk and Taganrog, and the speed of the advance as the Soviets fell back behind the Don persuaded Hitler that the Red Army was finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Surviving the winter had strengthened his confidence in his own judgement, and his directive of 23 July split the offensive into simultaneous drives on Stalingrad and the Caucasus, overstretching a front of more than 2,000 kilometres (1,200 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 121–24, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 330–33, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 14, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die deutsche Sommeroffensive 1942,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1942. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 11 February 1946, testimony of Friedrich Paulus (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-11-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conference Held at the White House, by Mr. Samuel H. Cross, Interpreter, May 30, 1942,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d471. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 September 1942, vol. 383, cols. 82–110, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/sep/08/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler aim at the Caucasus rather than Moscow in 1942?","a":"After the failure before Moscow the German leadership prepared for a long war, and the oil of the Caucasus was meant to fuel it; Hitler told his commanders that without the oil of Maikop and Grozny he would have to end the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the lost plans not ruin the offensive?","a":"Stalin refused to believe them, calling the documents a trumped-up piece of work by intelligence, and ordered his own attack towards Orel instead, so the German blow still fell on unprepared defenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the quick advance of July prove dangerous?","a":"The Soviets were withdrawing rather than being encircled, but Hitler read the speed as proof the Red Army was beaten and split his forces between Stalingrad and the Caucasus, stretching a front of more than 2,000 kilometres with too few reserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Przeprawa_wojsk_niemieckich_przez_Don_%282-747%29.jpg/1280px-Przeprawa_wojsk_niemieckich_przez_Don_%282-747%29.jpg","title":"Crossing the Don, July 1942","desc":"A German motorcycle column crosses the Don on a wooden bridge in July 1942, with vehicles and supplies abandoned by Soviet troops in the background.","credit":"Photograph by Dieck, National Digital Archives (Poland), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Przeprawa_wojsk_niemieckich_przez_Don_(2-747).jpg","alt":"Helmeted soldiers push motorcycles with sidecars across a narrow plank bridge, with a jumble of trucks and wreckage on the far bank."},"wiki":"Case Blue"},
    "ww2-300": {"questions":["When <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Maikop on 9 August 1942, First Panzer Army found the oil installations above ground wrecked, though the wells themselves were not on fire.","Hitler let Field Marshal List resign on 10 September 1942 and took over Army Group A himself after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stalled short of Grozny and the Black Sea coast."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Edelweiss</b> was the German drive into the Caucasus in the summer and autumn of 1942, carried out by Army Group A under Field Marshal Wilhelm List to seize the oil fields of Maikop, Grozny and Baku.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was set out in Directive No. 45 of 23 July, which sent Army Group A south from Rostov while Army Group B advanced on Stalingrad, two drives at once instead of one after the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its targets lay far off, for in straight lines Maikop was about 290 kilometres (180 miles) from Rostov, Grozny about 640 kilometres (400 miles) and Baku about 1,130 kilometres (700 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 25 July the army group had 435 serviceable tanks for eight spearhead divisions, and fuel was so short that one corps could not move at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the mountains supplies could be carried only by mule, largely by mounted Cossack units fighting on the German side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> First Panzer Army reached Maikop on 9 August and found the oil installations above ground wrecked, though the wells were not on fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 21 August mountain troops raised a German flag on Elbrus, at 5,633 metres (18,481 feet) the highest peak of the Caucasus, a symbolic feat that hid a strategic disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 8 September that a new British command in Persia and Iraq around the Tenth Army might eventually support the Russian left flank and would in any case defend Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The advance stalled in early September, spread across some 450 kilometres (280 miles), and after List and Jodl advised against pushing the mountain corps on, List resigned on 10 September and Hitler took command himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Visiting in December, an American envoy found the whole civilian population of Ordzhonikidze digging defences and Soviet officers sure of their superiority, and the retreat from the Caucasus began that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 152–55, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 358–78, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Vorstoß in den Kaukasus 1942,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vorstoss-in-den-kaukasus-1942. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die deutsche Sommeroffensive 1942,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1942. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Iran (Dreyfus) to the Secretary of State, December 29, 1942,\" report of General Hurley to President Roosevelt, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d562. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 8 September 1942, vol. 383, cols. 82–110, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/sep/08/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans want the Caucasus so badly?","a":"Its oil fields at Maikop, Grozny and Baku were the ultimate objectives of the summer, valued both for the German war effort and for the expected effect of their loss on the Soviet ability to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the drive run out of strength?","a":"Directive No. 45 split the German effort between Stalingrad and the Caucasus, tanks and fuel were short, supplies in the mountains had to go by mule, and Soviet resistance stiffened as the front stretched over some 450 kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was List removed?","a":"List and Jodl advised against continuing the mountain corps' advance, which clashed with Hitler's wishes; Jodl's deputy later suggested that Hitler sacrificed subordinates to protect himself, and Hitler blamed List for the failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Czo%C5%82gi_niemieckie_w_szyku_bojowym_w_dolinie_g%C3%B3rskiej_na_Kaukazie_%282-821%29.jpg/1280px-Czo%C5%82gi_niemieckie_w_szyku_bojowym_w_dolinie_g%C3%B3rskiej_na_Kaukazie_%282-821%29.jpg","title":"German tanks in the Caucasus, September 1942","desc":"German tanks deployed for battle in a mountain valley in the Caucasus in September 1942, with infantry positions in the foreground.","credit":"Photographer unknown, National Digital Archives (Poland), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Czo%C5%82gi_niemieckie_w_szyku_bojowym_w_dolinie_g%C3%B3rskiej_na_Kaukazie_(2-821).jpg","alt":"Dozens of tanks spread across a broad valley floor below bare hills, seen from a grassy slope where soldiers lie behind a machine gun."}},
    "ww2-261": {"questions":["A week after TASS had dismissed rumours of war as clumsy propaganda, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent three German army groups across the Soviet frontier at three in the morning on 22 June 1941.","The Nuremberg tribunal called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> plain aggression without the shadow of legal excuse, finding it impossible to believe that Germany had only been forestalling a Soviet attack."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Barbarossa</b> was the code name for the German invasion of the Soviet Union, launched on 22 June 1941 and the largest German military operation of the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler signed the directive bearing that name on 18 December 1940, ordering the armed forces to crush Soviet Russia in a lightning campaign even before the war with Britain was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army committed 3,050,000 men in 148 divisions, three-quarters of its field strength, and Finland and Romania added some 650,000 more between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three army groups attacked on a front from the Baltic to the Carpathians, the northern one driving through the Baltic states towards Leningrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler had told his generals on 30 March that this would be a war of extermination, and mobile killing units of the SS and police followed the armies to murder Jews and Communist officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Surprise was almost complete, for only a week earlier the Soviet news agency TASS had dismissed rumours of a German attack as propaganda concocted by hostile forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The attack began at three in the morning, and by daylight the Luftwaffe was destroying Soviet aircraft on the ground while the army seized every bridge on the border rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1 December the Germans claimed 3,006,867 prisoners, yet by the end of July they had already realised that the bulk of the Red Army had not been destroyed in western Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal later called the invasion plain aggression, finding it impossible to believe that the claim of a forestalled Soviet attack had ever been honestly held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Soviet counter-offensive before Moscow on 6 December ended a campaign Hitler had wanted finished within 5 months, and the war in the East became the main event of the whole conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of the Soviet Union, June 1941,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 11 June 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-the-soviet-union-june-1941. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 1–6, 20–25, 44, 88–90, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1987), 3–7, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Überfall auf die Sowjetunion,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ueberfall-auf-die-sowjetunion-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggressive War against the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsov.asp. [Open access]","Robert M. Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1–18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 10, <i>The High Command Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), 949–50, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, June 14, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d151. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Soviet Union taken so completely by surprise?","a":"Moscow had publicly denied the danger: a week before the attack TASS called the rumours of war hostile propaganda. When the blow came at three in the morning, aircraft were caught on their airfields and the border bridges were taken before the defence was organised."},{"q":"Why did the huge haul of prisoners not bring victory in 1941?","a":"The plan depended on destroying the main Soviet forces in western Russia, and by the end of July the Germans knew they had not done so. Three million prisoners were taken, but enough of the Red Army survived to stop the advance before Moscow in December."},{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg tribunal reject the claim of a preventive war?","a":"It found that the claim of a forestalled Soviet attack could never have been honestly held, and it called the invasion plain aggression, launched without warning and without the shadow of legal excuse."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/German_troops_crossing_the_Soviet_border.jpg/1280px-German_troops_crossing_the_Soviet_border.jpg","title":"German troops at the Soviet frontier, 22 June 1941","desc":"German infantry and a horse-drawn infantry gun passing Soviet border marker IV/95 on the first day of the invasion, with smoke rising from the plain beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Johannes Hähle, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_troops_crossing_the_Soviet_border.jpg","alt":"Helmeted soldiers with packs walk past a striped wooden border post beside a horse-drawn cart and a small gun, smoke rising from a fire on a flat plain ahead."},"wiki":"Operation Barbarossa"},
    "ww2-262": {"questions":["Hitler changed the draft of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 17 December 1940 so that Leningrad and the Baltic came first and the drive on Moscow had to wait until they had fallen.","Aiming at a final line from the Volga to Archangel, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned months of German staff planning, begun with the Marcks plan of August 1940, into Operation Barbarossa."],"abstract":"<b>Directive No. 21</b> was the order Hitler signed on 18 December 1940 under the cover name Operation Barbarossa, the first operational order for the invasion of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It opened by declaring that the German armed forces must prepare to crush Soviet Russia in a lightning campaign even before the war against Britain had ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Preparations not already under way were to be finished by 15 May 1941, and great caution was demanded so that the intention to attack would not be recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its overall plan was to destroy the mass of the Red Army in western Russia by deep armoured thrusts and to stop intact units from withdrawing into the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The final objective was a line from the Volga to Archangel, from which the Luftwaffe could reach the last Soviet industrial region in the Urals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The directive drew on months of staff work, beginning with the plan General Erich Marcks submitted on 5 August 1940, which made Moscow the principal objective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Friedrich Paulus, who took the work over in September, testified at Nuremberg that it was finished in early November and tested in two war games before the directive was issued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler then changed the draft on 17 December, giving first priority to Leningrad and the Baltic and letting the drive on Moscow continue only after they were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Simultaneous drives on both objectives were to be considered only in the event of an unexpectedly rapid collapse of Soviet resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler told his generals on 30 March 1941 that the goals in Russia were to crush the armed forces and break up the state, and the attack, delayed about 5 weeks by the Yugoslav campaign, began on 22 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 3–13, 20–25, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 407–9, document 446-PS, https://archive.org/details/NaziConspiracyAndAggression. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, testimony of Friedrich Paulus, 11 February 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 7 (Nuremberg, 1947), 254–56, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-11-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggressive War against the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsov.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of the Soviet Union, June 1941,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 11 June 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-the-soviet-union-june-1941. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1987), 3–7, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 10, <i>The High Command Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), 949–50, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the directive insist that preparations stay unobserved?","a":"Its plan depended on catching the Red Army close to the frontier and destroying it there before intact units could escape into the interior, and that required the attack to come as a surprise."},{"q":"What did Hitler change in the draft on 17 December 1940?","a":"He made Leningrad and the destruction of Soviet forces in the Baltic the first priority, so that the advance on Moscow, the principal objective of the Marcks plan, would continue only after those goals were reached."},{"q":"Why did the attack begin in June rather than in May?","a":"Paulus testified that Hitler's decision at the end of March to attack Yugoslavia postponed the date by about 5 weeks, so the invasion that had been prepared for mid-May began on 22 June."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Marcks_Plan_for_Operation_Barbarossa.jpg","title":"The Marcks plan, August 1940","desc":"The plan General Erich Marcks submitted on 5 August 1940, as mapped in the US Army's 1955 study: arrows in four phases converging on Moscow, with a second thrust south of the Pripyat Marshes towards Kiev.","credit":"US Department of the Army, 1955, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcks_Plan_for_Operation_Barbarossa.jpg","alt":"A map of eastern Europe with red, orange and pink arrows running from German-held Poland and East Prussia towards Moscow and Kiev."}},
    "ww2-263": {"questions":["Field Marshal von Bock complained that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let practically every soldier shoot, from the front or from behind, any Russian he took or claimed to take for a partisan.","Cross-examined at Nuremberg about whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had assured German soldiers impunity for lawless acts against Soviet civilians, Keitel answered that it had, within certain limits."],"abstract":"The <b>Barbarossa decree</b> was the order on military jurisdiction that Hitler issued on 13 May 1941 for the coming war, signed by Keitel and sent out by the armed forces high command the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arguing that the courts martial were too short-staffed for so vast a theatre, it removed crimes by enemy civilians from their competence and ordered the troops to dispose of guerrillas ruthlessly, fighting or in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A suspect was to be brought before an officer, who would decide whether he was to be shot, and an officer of at least battalion rank could order collective measures against the locality from which an attack had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its second part made it no longer obligatory to prosecute soldiers for offences against civilians, and reminded judges that the collapse of 1918 had been caused above all by Bolshevik influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler had prepared his generals for it on 30 March 1941, calling the coming campaign a war of extermination and the struggle against Communism no job for military courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Historians now see it as the pseudo-legal basis of German violence against Soviet civilians, and perhaps no other order did as much to radicalise the war in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Field Marshal von Bock complained that it gave practically every soldier the right to shoot any Russian he took, or claimed to take, for a partisan, from the front or from behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its demand that any resistance, even passive resistance, be met by shooting led to whole villages being burned and the rural population of entire districts shot in reprisal for partisan attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recent estimates put the victims of German anti-partisan operations and reprisals in the Soviet Union at half a million people or more, and soldiers who refused to take part were a vanishing minority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cross-examined at Nuremberg, Keitel admitted signing it on Hitler's order, and when asked whether it had assured German soldiers impunity he replied that it had, within certain limits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 871–75, document C-50, https://archive.org/details/NaziConspiracyAndAggression. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 10, <i>The High Command Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), 949–50, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X.pdf. [Open access]","Felix Römer, \"„Im alten Deutschland wäre solcher Befehl nicht möglich gewesen“: Rezeption, Adaption und Umsetzung des Kriegsgerichtsbarkeitserlasses im Ostheer 1941/42,\" <i>Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte</i> 56, no. 1 (2008): 53–99, https://www.ifz-muenchen.de/heftarchiv/2008_1_3_roemer.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Christian Hartmann, \"Verbrecherischer Krieg – verbrecherische Wehrmacht? Überlegungen zur Struktur des deutschen Ostheeres 1941–1944,\" <i>Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte</i> 52, no. 1 (2004): 1–75, https://www.ifz-muenchen.de/heftarchiv/2004_1_1_hartmann.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Army and the Racial Nature of the War against the Soviet Union,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-army-and-the-racial-nature-of-the-war-against-the-soviet-union. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, cross-examination of Wilhelm Keitel, 5 April 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 10 (Nuremberg, 1947), 613–14, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-05-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the decree take Soviet civilians away from the military courts?","a":"The decree itself argued that courts martial were too short-staffed for so vast a theatre and that only ruthless action by the troops would work, and Hitler had already told his generals that the struggle against Communism was no job for military courts."},{"q":"Why was prosecuting soldiers for crimes against civilians made optional?","a":"The decree told judges to remember that the collapse of Germany in 1918 had been caused above all by Bolshevik influence, so an offence against a Soviet civilian was to reach a court martial only when discipline or security required it."},{"q":"Why do historians give this dry legal order so much weight?","a":"It handed the decision over life and death to junior officers and soldiers, which is why Bock said any Russian could be shot as a supposed partisan, and the reprisals it licensed are reckoned to have cost half a million lives or more."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Barbarossa_Decree_C-50.jpg","title":"The Barbarossa decree, first page","desc":"The first page of the decree of 13 May 1941 on military jurisdiction in the Barbarossa area, document C-50 at Nuremberg, with its opening clauses on offences by enemy civilians.","credit":"German armed forces high command, 1941, public domain as a German official work, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barbarossa_Decree_C-50.jpg","alt":"A typed German document headed Führerhauptquartier, 13 May 1941, with handwritten notes across the top and the marking C-50 in the margin."},"wiki":"Barbarossa decree"},
    "ww2-264": {"questions":["Konrad Meyer's memorandum of 28 May 1942 for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the cost of settling German marches and strongpoints in the conquered East at 66.6 billion Reichsmarks.","Himmler commissioned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Reich Commissioner for the Strengthening of German Nationhood, intending eastern Europe up to the Urals to be settled by Germans once its inhabitants were driven out."],"abstract":"<b>Generalplan Ost</b>, the General Plan East, was the SS planning for the German colonisation of conquered eastern Europe and the removal of most of the people who lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The idea and the name date from 1941, perhaps 1940, and the impulse came from Himmler in his role as Reich Commissioner for the Strengthening of German Nationhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The agrarian scientist Konrad Meyer delivered a first version on 15 July 1941 and a larger memorandum on 28 May 1942 that priced the settlement at 66.6 billion Reichsmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A version drawn up in the Reich Security Main Office reckoned on removing 31 million of the 45 million non-Germans in the area and settling about 10 million Germans over 30 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those expelled were to go to western Siberia, though even an official of the Eastern Ministry who approved the aim doubted that a removal on this scale could be carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The plan shows that the war in the East was meant to clear land for German settlement, and the Nuremberg tribunal found that crimes there were part of a design to expel and annihilate whole populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Himmler wrote in July 1942 that the task was not to Germanise the East in the old sense but to see that only people of purely Germanic blood lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The SS general Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski testified that before the invasion Himmler had spoken of the purpose of the Russian campaign as decimating the Slav population by 30 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As late as February 1943 a presentation to staff of the Reich Security Main Office described a colonised zone of 700,000 square kilometres (270,000 square miles), its edge running along the Dnieper through Kiev to the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Few Germans showed any wish to settle there, and the German historian who edited its key documents in 1958 concluded that what stopped it was the turn of the war, not a return to reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Helmut Heiber, \"Der Generalplan Ost,\" <i>Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte</i> 6, no. 3 (1958): 281–325, https://www.ifz-muenchen.de/heftarchiv/1958_3_5_heiber.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Czesław Madajczyk, \"Principes généraux et caractère des transferts de population opérés par les Nazis,\" <i>Studia Historiae Oeconomicae</i> 8 (1973): 3–34, https://doi.org/10.14746/sho.1973.08.1.001. [in French] [Open access]","Linde Apel, \"Der Generalplan Ost,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/generalplan-ost. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, testimony of Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski, 7 January 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Lebensraum,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensraum. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: The Aggressive War against the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsov.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the plan said to be for 'German nationhood' rather than conquest alone?","a":"Its purpose was settlement: conquered land was to be emptied of most of its people and filled with Germans, which Himmler put as making sure that only people of purely Germanic blood lived in the East."},{"q":"Why did officials who approved its aims still doubt it?","a":"The scale was enormous, with 31 million people to be moved to western Siberia, and a critic in the Eastern Ministry doubted that such a removal could ever be carried out, while few Germans wanted to go and settle."},{"q":"Why was the plan never carried out in full?","a":"The military situation turned against Germany, and the historian who edited its key documents in 1958 concluded that it was the turn of the war, not any return to reason, that stopped it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/Wysiedlanie-Zamojszczyzna.jpg","title":"Expulsions in the Zamość region, December 1942","desc":"Polish villagers being driven from their farms by the SS in the Zamość region of occupied Poland in December 1942, during deportations of more than 100,000 people begun as the plan took shape.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wysiedlanie-Zamojszczyzna.jpg","alt":"Men and women in winter clothes with bundles and baskets stand crowded outside thatched cottages, watched by an SS officer and armed guards."},"wiki":"Generalplan Ost"},
    "ww2-265": {"questions":["Guidelines of 23 May 1941 for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposed sealing off the Soviet black-earth south, leaving the forest zone with Moscow and Leningrad to face the most serious famine.","Herbert Backe, who ran German food rationing, turned food policy into starvation policy in the occupied Soviet lands through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that German planners drew up in 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Hunger Plan</b> is the name historians give to the German planning of 1941 to feed the army and the Reich from Soviet food while millions of Soviet citizens starved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 2 May 1941 a meeting of state secretaries recorded that the war could be continued only if all the armed forces were fed from Russia in the third year of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same memorandum stated that there was no doubt many millions of people would starve to death if what Germany needed was taken out of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agricultural guidelines for the Economic Staff East, dated 23 May 1941, spelled out how: the grain surplus of the black-earth south would be cut off from the forest zone of the north, Moscow and Leningrad included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their authors wrote that the people there, above all in the towns, would face the most serious famine and would have to be diverted into Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The policy was tied to the home front, because Hitler and the planners remembered the food riots of the First World War, a war they believed had been lost on the home front, and meant to keep German rations and morale up at any price.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herbert Backe, the official in charge of German food rationing, was involved in the planning, and under his direction food policy became starvation policy in the occupied Soviet lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Other guidelines issued before the invasion said that many tens of millions of people in the territory would become superfluous and would have to die or migrate to Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal quoted the aim of feeding the armies from Soviet territory even if many millions starved, and Rosenberg's declaration of 20 June 1941 that Germany had no obligation to feed the Russian people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soviet prisoners of war became the first victims of this policy of mass starvation, many of them given at most 700 calories a day, so that by the winter of 1941 starvation and disease were killing them in vast numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 378, document 2718-PS, https://archive.org/details/NaziConspiracyAndAggression. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 7 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 295–302, document EC-126, https://archive.org/details/NaziConspiracyAndAggression. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Army and the Racial Nature of the War against the Soviet Union,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-army-and-the-racial-nature-of-the-war-against-the-soviet-union. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Lebensraum,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensraum. [Open access]","Gesine Gerhard, \"Food and Genocide: Nazi Agrarian Politics in the Occupied Territories of the Soviet Union,\" <i>Contemporary European History</i> 18, no. 1 (2009): 45–65, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960777308004827. [Paywalled]","Gesine Gerhard, \"The Hungerplan,\" in <i>Nazi Hunger Politics: A History of Food in the Third Reich</i> (Lanham, MD: Rowman & Littlefield, 2015), 85–102, https://doi.org/10.5771/9781442227255-85. [Paywalled]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Treatment of Soviet POWs: Starvation, Disease, and Shootings, June 1941–January 1942,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-treatment-of-soviet-pows-starvation-disease-and-shootings-june-1941january-1942. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were German planners ready to accept millions of deaths?","a":"They had decided that the army must be fed from Russia and that German rations must not fall, because they blamed hunger on the home front for the defeat of 1918, so Soviet lives were the cost they chose to impose."},{"q":"Why were the northern forest zone and its cities singled out?","a":"The food surplus came from the black-earth south; sealing that region off would release grain for Germany, while the deficit north, with Moscow and Leningrad, would be left without supplies and its people expected to starve or flee to Siberia."},{"q":"How did the Nuremberg tribunal deal with the plan?","a":"It quoted the intention to feed the German armies from Soviet territory even if many millions starved, together with Rosenberg's statement that Germany had no obligation to feed the Russian people, as evidence of premeditated plunder."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J02034%2C_Herbert_Backe_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Herbert Backe, 1942","desc":"Herbert Backe, the state secretary in charge of German food rationing, photographed at his desk in June 1942.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-J02034, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J02034,_Herbert_Backe_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A man in round spectacles and a dark uniform with collar insignia sits at a desk with papers spread in front of him."},"wiki":"Hunger Plan"},
    "ww2-266": {"questions":["Hitler suspended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 6 May 1942 after commanders reported that Soviet soldiers fought harder once they learned that captured commissars were being shot.","A supplement to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issued by Brauchitsch asked that captured commissars be set apart and killed inconspicuously, on an officer's order, outside the battle zone proper."],"abstract":"The <b>Commissar Order</b> was the German instruction that captured Soviet political commissars were to be shot, issued by the armed forces high command on 6 June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Political commissars had been reintroduced into the Red Army in May 1937, in part to control the commander and to supervise the political education of the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The order called them the originators of Asiatic barbaric methods of fighting and laid down that, if captured in combat or while resisting, they must on principle be shot immediately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those taken were to be separated from other prisoners on the battlefield, denied the protection of international law and then liquidated, while commissars seized in the rear went to the Einsatzgruppen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A supplement from the army commander-in-chief, Brauchitsch, on 8 June asked that the killings be done inconspicuously, outside the battle zone proper, on an officer's order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It put into writing what Hitler had told his generals on 30 March 1941, that this was a war of extermination in which commissars and the Communist intelligentsia were to be destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The German forces generally complied in the summer and autumn of 1941, and one panzer group reported that the summary killings were no problem for the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No firm total is known, but one estimate puts the commissars killed in the area of Army Group Centre alone at between 3,000 and 5,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From late summer 1941 commanders asked for it to be withdrawn, partly because Soviet soldiers who learned of the shootings resisted harder, and Hitler suspended it on 6 May 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal cited the destruction of the commissars as a war crime that was the result of direct orders circulated through the highest official channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 10, <i>The High Command Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), 949–50, 1054–57, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Commissar Order,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/commissar-order. [Open access]","Christian Hartmann, \"Verbrecherischer Krieg – verbrecherische Wehrmacht? Überlegungen zur Struktur des deutschen Ostheeres 1941–1944,\" <i>Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte</i> 52, no. 1 (2004): 1–75, https://www.ifz-muenchen.de/heftarchiv/2004_1_1_hartmann.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Überfall auf die Sowjetunion,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ueberfall-auf-die-sowjetunion-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Henderson) to the Secretary of State, February 18, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d451. [Open access]","Felix Römer, \"„Im alten Deutschland wäre solcher Befehl nicht möglich gewesen“: Rezeption, Adaption und Umsetzung des Kriegsgerichtsbarkeitserlasses im Ostheer 1941/42,\" <i>Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte</i> 56, no. 1 (2008): 53–99, https://www.ifz-muenchen.de/heftarchiv/2008_1_3_roemer.pdf. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the commissars singled out?","a":"They were Communist Party officials who watched over the commander and the political education of the troops, and the order cast them as the originators of barbaric methods, echoing Hitler's call for the extermination of Bolshevist commissars."},{"q":"Why was the order withdrawn in 1942?","a":"Commanders had asked for it to go since late summer 1941, partly because Soviet soldiers who learned that commissars were being shot fought harder, and Hitler suspended it in May 1942."},{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg tribunal treat these killings differently from a guard's brutality?","a":"They did not arise from individual excess but from direct orders issued through the highest official channels, so responsibility ran to the top of the command."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Kommissarbefehl_001.jpg","title":"The covering letter of 6 June 1941","desc":"The high command's letter of 6 June 1941 sending the guidelines for the treatment of political commissars to the commanders, with its distribution list of twenty copies.","credit":"German armed forces high command, 1941, public domain as a German official work, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kommissarbefehl_001.jpg","alt":"A typed German letter dated 6.6.1941, signed in ink, with a distribution list down the left side and handwritten notes."},"wiki":"Commissar Order"},
    "ww2-267": {"questions":["Twenty new mechanised corps begun in early 1941 were far from fully equipped, and even the newest tanks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried no radios when the Germans invaded.","German staff officers formed a low opinion of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after its poor showing against Finland in the winter of 1939–40, and saw the purges of 1937 as a sign of weakness."],"abstract":"The <b>Red Army</b> was the land army of the Soviet Union, and in June 1941 it was in a state of flux, expanding and modernising at a faster rate since war broke out in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Voroshilov, the defence commissar, claimed in March 1939 that its strength had slightly more than doubled in 5 years and that its political workers had grown from 15,000 to 34,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Foreign military attachés in Moscow agreed in 1938 that the purge and the return of political commissars had damaged its fighting efficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The purges of 1937 and 1938 had removed most of its senior commanders, and its poor showing against Finland in the winter of 1939–40 left German staff officers with a low opinion of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In numbers it may have been the best-equipped army in the world, with 24,000 tanks by Stalin's account, though a German briefing of March 1941 judged its air force very large but mostly outmoded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In June 1941 it had 4.7 million soldiers, but only about half were stationed in the western Soviet Union and the Polish territories taken in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its new T-34 and KV tanks outclassed any German model, yet only 1,225 and 639 had been built, and of the twenty mechanised corps begun in early 1941 few were fully equipped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radios were scarce, even the newest tanks carried none, and the artillery was pulled by ordinary farm tractors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Khrushchev later charged that Moscow had forbidden defensive works at the border for fear of provoking Germany and had at first ordered that German fire not be returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the first day much of the Soviet air force was destroyed on the ground and millions of soldiers were soon encircled, yet a German staff study had warned that the Russian soldier was not to be underestimated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1987), 10–12, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 14–15, 42–43, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Überfall auf die Sowjetunion,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ueberfall-auf-die-sowjetunion-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]","Nikita S. Khrushchev, <i>The Crimes of the Stalin Era: Special Report to the 20th Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union</i>, annotated by Boris I. Nicolaevsky (New York: The New Leader, 1956), S36–S39, https://archive.org/details/stalin2_202007. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Henderson) to the Secretary of State, February 18, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d451. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, March 16, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d575. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 10, <i>The High Command Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), 949, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-X.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of the Soviet Union, June 1941,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 11 June 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-the-soviet-union-june-1941. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the army in flux in June 1941?","a":"It had been expanding since 1939 and was trying to act on the lessons of its poor war against Finland, and new formations such as the twenty mechanised corps begun that spring were still far from complete."},{"q":"Why did so many tanks not make it a stronger army?","a":"Most were obsolete, fewer than 1,900 of the two new types had been built, radios were lacking even in the newest tanks, and the artillery depended on farm tractors to move."},{"q":"Why were the border troops so badly prepared?","a":"According to Khrushchev, Moscow forbade defensive works at the frontier for fear of provoking Germany and at first even ordered that German fire not be returned."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-209-0091-11%2C_Russland-Nord%2C_russischer_Panzer_KW-2.jpg","title":"A Soviet KV-2 tank, June 1941","desc":"A disabled Soviet KV-2 heavy tank at the roadside in the northern sector of the front, examined by German soldiers in June 1941.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-209-0091-11, photograph by Nägele, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-209-0091-11,_Russland-Nord,_russischer_Panzer_KW-2.jpg","alt":"A huge boxy tank with a tall turret and short gun sits tilted at the edge of a dirt road, one soldier on top and another watching."},"wiki":"Red Army"},
    "ww2-268": {"questions":["Soviet accounts say that on 15 June 1941 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> radioed from Tokyo that the war would begin on 22 June, one of many warnings Moscow received that spring.","Ozaki Hotsumi, recruited in Shanghai around 1930, belonged to the Tokyo spy ring of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose richest source of information was the German embassy itself."],"abstract":"<b>Richard Sorge</b> was a German Communist who spied for the Soviet Union in Tokyo, where he worked from 1933 as correspondent of the <i>Frankfurter Zeitung</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> According to the Japanese police record he had joined the Soviet Communist Party in 1925, and in Japan he built a ring that included a German radio operator, a Yugoslav and Ozaki Hotsumi, recruited in Shanghai around 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His richest source was the German embassy, where he enjoyed confidence and respect, and he passed what he learned there to Moscow by radio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In May 1941 talk at the embassy about Hess's flight to Britain convinced him that a German attack on the Soviet Union was inevitable and even imminent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 20 June a German officer passing through Tokyo told him that the main effort would be against Moscow, and the military attaché put the divisions massed on the frontier at 175.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 15 June, according to Soviet accounts, he had already radioed that the war would begin on 22 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His was one of many warnings: Churchill had cautioned Stalin through his ambassador in April, and the Soviet attaché in Berlin reported in May a plan to invade on 14 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> American diplomats heard in early June that Germany would invade within a fortnight, and on 19 June a German informant in Moscow advised that American women and children be sent away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yet on 13 June Moscow had authorised TASS to call the rumours of a German attack propaganda clumsily concocted by forces hostile to both countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Khrushchev said in 1956 that Stalin ordered such reports to be disbelieved so as not to provoke war, though Soviet histories later claimed the TASS statement was a probe and the frontier was readied from 14 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States House of Representatives, Committee on Un-American Activities, <i>Hearings on American Aspects of the Richard Sorge Spy Case</i>, 82nd Cong., 1st sess., 9, 22 and 23 August 1951 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), 1173–74, 1196–98, https://archive.org/details/hearingsonameric00unit. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1987), 22–23, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Morris) to the Secretary of State, June 8, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d150. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, June 19, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d152. [Open access]","Nikita S. Khrushchev, <i>The Crimes of the Stalin Era: Special Report to the 20th Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union</i>, annotated by Boris I. Nicolaevsky (New York: The New Leader, 1956), S36–S39, https://archive.org/details/stalin2_202007. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of the Soviet Union, June 1941,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 11 June 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-the-soviet-union-june-1941. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, June 14, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d151. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the German embassy such a good source for a Soviet spy?","a":"Sorge posed as a German journalist and enjoyed the confidence of the ambassador and the military attaché, so he heard what Berlin was telling its own diplomats, including the number of divisions massed on the Soviet frontier."},{"q":"Why did Moscow not act on so many warnings?","a":"Khrushchev said that Stalin ordered such information to be disbelieved for fear of provoking a war, and a week before the attack TASS publicly dismissed the rumours as hostile propaganda."},{"q":"Why is the story of the ignored warnings still argued over?","a":"Khrushchev told it in 1956 to discredit Stalin, while later Soviet histories claimed the TASS statement was only a probe and that the frontier districts were put on a war footing in the week before the attack."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1985-1003-020%2C_Richard_Sorge.jpg","title":"Richard Sorge, about 1940","desc":"Richard Sorge photographed around 1940, in a picture that had been kept by his family.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1985-1003-020, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1985-1003-020,_Richard_Sorge.jpg","alt":"A dark-haired man in a jacket, waistcoat and tie sits outdoors in front of a house with tall windows, looking down to one side."},"wiki":"Richard Sorge","places":["city:1159151609"]},
    "ww2-269": {"questions":["Khrushchev blamed the repressions that followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the disasters of 1941, saying they had almost wiped out commanders who had fought in Spain and the Far East.","The American embassy admitted it knew nothing of the evidence on which the marshal and his colleagues were condemned in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, tried in secret and shot within a day."],"abstract":"The <b>Tukhachevsky affair</b> was the secret trial and execution in June 1937 of Marshal Mikhail Tukhachevsky and seven other senior commanders of the Red Army for treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pravda announced on 11 June that the eight, among them Yakir and Uborevich, were charged with spying for a foreign power unfriendly to the Soviet Union and with preparing the Red Army's defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The case was heard the same day at a closed session of the Supreme Court under a law of 1934 that allowed no counsel and required death sentences to be carried out at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The executions were announced on 13 June, and the American embassy admitted that it knew nothing of the evidence on which the officers had been condemned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Khrushchev's secret speech of 1956 put the annihilation of commanders that followed down to Stalin's suspiciousness and to slanderous accusations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The purge ran on through 1938 and, by a Soviet count, removed all the military district and corps commanders, nearly all those of divisions and brigades, and half the regimental commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It cut the officer corps of all ranks by one fifth, and the navy and the air force were hit just as hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Foreign military attachés in Moscow agreed by early 1938 that the purge and the return of political commissars had harmed the army's fighting efficiency, differing only on how long recovery would take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Voroshilov insisted in March 1939 that the fighting at Lake Khasan gave the lie to talk of weakness, but German planners took the purges as evidence of Soviet vulnerability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Khrushchev blamed the repression for the defeats of 1941, saying it had almost wiped out the commanders with experience of Spain and the Far East and taught soldiers to unmask their superiors as hidden enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Henderson) to the Secretary of State, June 11, 1937,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d343. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Henderson) to the Secretary of State, June 13, 1937,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d345. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Henderson) to the Secretary of State, February 18, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d451. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, March 16, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933-39/d575. [Open access]","Nikita S. Khrushchev, <i>The Crimes of the Stalin Era: Special Report to the 20th Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union</i>, annotated by Boris I. Nicolaevsky (New York: The New Leader, 1956), S36–S39, https://archive.org/details/stalin2_202007. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1987), 10–11, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the defendants not defend themselves?","a":"They were tried at a closed session under a law of 1934 that provided no counsel for either side and required death sentences to be carried out immediately, and they were shot the day after the trial."},{"q":"Why did foreign observers think the Red Army had been weakened?","a":"The purge removed nearly all of its senior commanders and a fifth of its officers, and attachés in Moscow agreed that it, together with the return of political commissars, had harmed the army's fighting efficiency."},{"q":"Why did Khrushchev link the purge to the defeats of 1941?","a":"He argued that it had almost wiped out commanders with combat experience in Spain and the Far East and had undermined discipline by teaching soldiers to denounce their superiors as hidden enemies."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Mikhail_Tukhachevsky.jpg","title":"Mikhail Tukhachevsky","desc":"Marshal Mikhail Tukhachevsky in uniform, in a portrait made before his arrest and execution in 1937.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Tukhachevsky.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of a man with swept-back hair in a military tunic with a star on each collar tab and medals on his chest."},"wiki":"Case of the Trotskyist Anti-Soviet Military Organization"},
    "ww2-270": {"questions":["German claims of 665,000 prisoners at Kiev and 3,006,867 in all by December 1941 measured the success of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the first months of Barbarossa.","Sealing off one immense pocket after another at Białystok, Minsk and Smolensk, the panzer groups of 1941 fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that German doctrine held up as the ideal end of a war of movement."],"abstract":"A <b>Kesselschlacht</b>, literally a ‘cauldron battle’, is a battle of encirclement, in which an enemy is hemmed in on all sides and destroyed in concentric operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the desired end of the Prussian and German war of movement, which sought to strike the mass of the enemy army a rapid, annihilating blow, ideally from the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Directive No. 21 built the invasion of the Soviet Union around it, ordering that the Red Army in western Russia be destroyed by deep armoured thrusts before intact units could withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panzer groups closed the rings, as the Second and Third did when they linked up near Minsk, and the slower armies then had to mop up the trapped forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Germans had used the method in Poland and France, and in 1941 they applied it to the Soviet Union on a far larger scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By 29 June two pockets east of Białystok and east of Minsk were closed, and they would yield over three hundred thousand prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the pocket east of Kiev was cleared in September the Germans took 665,000 prisoners, and the encirclements at Vyazma and Bryansk in October yielded hundreds of thousands more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1 December 1941 the Germans claimed 3,006,867 prisoners, men who had often been cut off from supplies and reinforcements and had little choice but to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most did not survive: of some 5.7 million Soviet soldiers who fell into German hands during the war, about 3.3 million were dead by its end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yet the cauldrons did not end the war, for by the end of July the Germans knew the bulk of the Red Army had escaped destruction in western Russia, and reducing the pockets cost time the Germans needed on the road to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Robert M. Citino, \"The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,\" <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 1–18, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 22–25, 44, 74, 79, 88–90, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1987), 27–37, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Überfall auf die Sowjetunion,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ueberfall-auf-die-sowjetunion-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Invasion of the Soviet Union, June 1941,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 11 June 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/invasion-of-the-soviet-union-june-1941. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did German doctrine prize encirclement so highly?","a":"A numerically weaker army had to win quickly, and trapping the enemy on all sides and destroying him in concentric operations promised a decisive, annihilating victory instead of a long war of attrition."},{"q":"Why did so many Soviet soldiers surrender in the pockets?","a":"Once the rings closed they were cut off from supplies and reinforcements and had little choice, and by December the Germans claimed more than three million prisoners."},{"q":"Why did the cauldron battles of 1941 not win the war?","a":"Enough of the Red Army escaped destruction in western Russia to keep fighting, as the Germans had realised by the end of July, and reducing the pockets cost them time and strength on the road to Moscow."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-L28726%2C_Sowjetische_Kriegsgefangene_bei_Smolensk.jpg","title":"Soviet prisoners from the Smolensk pocket, August 1941","desc":"Soviet prisoners in a German transit camp near Smolensk in August 1941; the original caption counts some 16,000 men in the camp, with more arriving every day from the great pocket at Smolensk.","credit":"Markwardt (Propagandakompanie), Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-L28726, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-L28726,_Sowjetische_Kriegsgefangene_bei_Smolensk.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white photograph of a dense crowd of men in caps and field uniforms packed into a yard, more of them sitting along the ridge of a low wooden roof behind."},"wiki":"Encirclement"},
    "ww2-201": {"questions":["Telling the Commons that the Battle of France was over and the battle of Britain about to begin, Churchill delivered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> speech at Westminster on 18 June 1940.","Even five divisions would need 200 to 250 ships, Churchill argued in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> speech, an armada the Navy could find and attack long before it reached the coast."],"abstract":"<b>Their finest hour</b> is the name given to the speech Winston Churchill made to the House of Commons on 18 June 1940, when he told members that what General Weygand had called the Battle of France was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 4 June, reporting the evacuation from Dunkirk, he had promised that Britain would defend its island 'if necessary for years, if necessary alone'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new speech was a balance sheet of that resolve: over a million and a quarter men under arms, half a million Local Defence Volunteers, and Dominion troops arriving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill argued that even five divisions would need 200 to 250 ships to cross the sea, an armada the Navy could find and attack long before it reached the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The great question, he said, was whether Britain could break Hitler's air weapon, and he ended by asking his listeners so to bear themselves that men would still say, 'This was their finest hour.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the public account lay a private one, for the Chiefs of Staff had considered in late May what Britain should do if France collapsed, and their paper on strategy in the near future reached the War Cabinet on 27 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> They judged that Britain could fight on only if the Luftwaffe was denied air superiority, since otherwise its sea lanes and war industries would be threatened and an invasion might follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The American ambassador in London, Joseph Kennedy, reported on 12 June that British preparedness was appallingly weak and that Britain would hold on in the hope that the United States would come in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lord Lothian passed on reports in May that Hitler and Mussolini would offer peace terms as an ultimatum once Paris fell, and Churchill wrote to Roosevelt on 11 June that Hitler, baffled of quick results, would turn upon Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In July a British aide-mémoire warned Washington that almost all Europe's resources were now at the Axis powers' disposal and that Britain could not pay cash for American supplies indefinitely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 18 June 1940, vol. 362, cols. 51-64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/18/war-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 June 1940, vol. 361, cols. 787-98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conversation, by the Secretary of State, July 5, 1940, with annexed British Aide-Mémoire of July 3, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d38. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, June 12, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d31. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), May 21, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d24. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The British Prime Minister (Churchill) to President Roosevelt, June 11, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d47. [Open access]","Ian Gooderson, \"A Triumph of Delusion over Despair: British Strategic Readjustment, June 1940 – June 1941,\" King's College London, 16 July 2021, https://www.kcl.ac.uk/a-triumph-of-delusion-over-despair-british-strategic-readjustment-june-1940-june-1941. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Confidential Annex to WM (40) 141: Western Front; Norway; British Strategy in the Near Future,\" 27 May 1940, CAB 65/13/22, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C9024484/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Churchill think a German invasion by sea could be defeated?","a":"Because even a small invading army needed a great many ships: he reckoned that five lightly equipped divisions would require 200 to 250 vessels, which the Navy, helped by air reconnaissance and minefields, could find and attack before they landed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the air battle matter most in the British calculation?","a":"Because the Chiefs of Staff concluded in late May that Britain could carry on only if the Luftwaffe was denied air superiority, and Churchill told the Commons that the great question was whether Britain could break Hitler's air weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain need the United States so badly in the summer of 1940?","a":"Because almost all the industry of Europe was now in Axis hands, British factories faced bombing and imports faced blockade, and the British government told Washington in July that it could not keep paying cash for American arms and supplies indefinitely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-202": {"questions":["Nine War Cabinet meetings in 3 days, with Reynaud's visit on the 26th and a suggested approach to Mussolini on the 28th, made up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in London.","Whether Churchill seriously weighed asking Hitler's terms is still argued from the minutes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which some read as hypotheticals raised only to be rejected."],"abstract":"The <b>May 1940 war cabinet crisis</b> was the argument inside Britain's War Cabinet, over the last days of May 1940, about whether to ask Mussolini to find out what peace terms Hitler would offer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its setting was the encirclement of the Allied armies in the north, and the American ambassador, who saw Lord Halifax on the night of 23 May, reported that the situation according to those who knew was very, very grim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 26 May the War Cabinet's confidential record dealt with the visit to London of the French premier, Reynaud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Halifax, the Foreign Secretary, wanted Mussolini to ascertain Hitler's terms, while Churchill told the War Cabinet that day that Britain could never accept a peace achieved under German domination of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The War Cabinet met nine times in 3 days, an unusual intensity, and on 28 May the agenda of its confidential annex was a suggested direct approach to Signor Mussolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 28 May Churchill also met the outer Cabinet, the ministers outside the War Cabinet, and the government resolved to fight on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That same day, announcing the Belgian surrender, he told the Commons that nothing which might happen in the battle could relieve Britain of its duty to defend the world cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 4 June he promised that Britain would fight on the beaches and never surrender, and that if the island were subjugated the Empire overseas would carry on the struggle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How close Britain came to talks is still disputed: one reading of the minutes is that Churchill seriously considered asking Hitler's terms, but a reviewer answered that the two men were debating hypotheticals and that Churchill's words read plainly as arguments against seeking terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another view holds that Churchill was shaping events all along, using a prime minister's control over the Cabinet's agenda and over which papers reached it and when.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["The National Archives (UK), \"Confidential Annex to WM (40) 140: France, M. Reynaud's Visit,\" 26 May 1940, CAB 65/13/21, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C9024483/. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Confidential Annex to WM (40) 145: Italy, Suggested Direct Approach to Signor Mussolini,\" 28 May 1940, CAB 65/13/24, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C9024486/. [Open access]","Michael McMenamin, \"Books, Arts, &amp; Curiosities – Outfoxing the Holy Fox,\" review of <i>Darkest Hour: How Churchill Brought England Back from the Brink</i>, by Anthony McCarten, <i>Finest Hour</i> 178, International Churchill Society, https://winstonchurchill.org/publications/finest-hour/finest-hour-178/books-arts-curiosities-outfoxing-the-holy-fox/. [Open access]","Michael McMenamin, \"Cabinet Government in the War Crisis of May 1940,\" review of <i>Cabinet's Finest Hour: The Hidden Agenda of May 1940</i>, by David Owen, The Churchill Project, Hillsdale College, 26 March 2018, https://winstonchurchill.hillsdale.edu/?p=5754. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 May 1940, vol. 361, cols. 421-2, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/28/war-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 June 1940, vol. 361, cols. 787-98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation. [Open access]","Ian Gooderson, \"A Triumph of Delusion over Despair: British Strategic Readjustment, June 1940 – June 1941,\" King's College London, 16 July 2021, https://www.kcl.ac.uk/a-triumph-of-delusion-over-despair-british-strategic-readjustment-june-1940-june-1941. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, May 24, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d25. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Halifax want to approach Mussolini?","a":"Because he hoped Mussolini could find out what terms Hitler would offer, at a moment when the Allied armies in the north were cut off and the American ambassador, after seeing Halifax, reported the situation as very grim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill reject a peace sought through Mussolini?","a":"Because, as he told the War Cabinet on 26 May, Britain could never accept a peace achieved under a German domination of Europe, and on 28 May he told the Commons that nothing in the battle could relieve Britain of its duty to the world cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still disagree about the crisis?","a":"Because the minutes record Halifax and Churchill debating what Germany might offer and what might be accepted, which one writer reads as Churchill seriously considering talks and a reviewer reads as arguments against them, while another view sees Churchill steering the outcome through his control of the agenda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"1940 British war cabinet crisis"},
    "ww2-203": {"questions":["Churchill wanted no bargain between friends in danger, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traded Caribbean leases for the warships Britain needed after eleven destroyers were sunk or damaged in 10 days.","Roosevelt's cabinet feared Congress would block a gift, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was framed as hemisphere defence, with the Bermuda and Newfoundland sites granted freely and without consideration."],"abstract":"The <b>Destroyers-for-bases deal</b> was the agreement of 2 September 1940 by which the United States transferred fifty old destroyers to Britain in return for the right to build naval and air bases on British territory in the western Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bases in Newfoundland and Bermuda were granted freely and without consideration, while those in the Bahamas, Jamaica, Saint Lucia, Trinidad, Antigua and British Guiana were given in exchange for the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All were leased for 99 years free of rent, and the destroyers were of the type generally referred to as 1,200 tons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill had pressed for them in a message of 31 July, listing eleven destroyers sunk or damaged in 10 days and asking for fifty or sixty of the oldest American destroyers at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exchange let Roosevelt present the transfer as essential to the security of the Western Hemisphere rather than as a gift, which the public and Congress were likely to oppose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Roosevelt's cabinet agreed on 2 August that the survival of the British Isles might depend on the destroyers, but also that legislation asked for without preparation would meet defeat or endless delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His terms of 13 August asked for an assurance that if British waters became untenable the fleet would be sent to other parts of the Empire rather than surrendered or sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill disliked the language of a bargain, writing on 22 August that the two countries were friends in danger and that Britain would offer the bases even if the destroyers never came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> 2 days earlier he had told the Commons that the British Empire and the United States would have to be somewhat mixed up together, a process that, like the Mississippi, just kept rolling along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The deal marked the start, in September 1940, of significant American military aid to Britain, more than a year before the United States entered the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, July 31, 1940, transmitting a message from the Former Naval Person,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d50. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum by President Roosevelt, August 2, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d51. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy), August 13, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d56. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Kennedy) to the Secretary of State, August 22, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d61. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The British Ambassador (Lothian) to the Secretary of State, September 2, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d65. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the British Ambassador (Lothian), September 2, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d66. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1132-274, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/20/war-situation. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, \"Lend-Lease and Military Aid to the Allies in the Early Years of World War II,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/lend-lease. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain need old American destroyers in 1940?","a":"Because, as Churchill told Roosevelt on 31 July, Germany held the whole French coast for U-boat and air attacks, eleven British destroyers had been sunk or damaged in 10 days, and new construction would not close the gap for 3 or 4 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the deal framed as an exchange for bases?","a":"Because Roosevelt's cabinet thought a plain request to Congress would fail, and leases on British territory let him justify the transfer as essential to the security of the Western Hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt ask for an assurance about the British fleet?","a":"Because his cabinet believed a public assurance that the fleet would never fall into German hands would greatly lessen opposition in Congress, so his terms asked that the ships be sent to other parts of the Empire rather than surrendered or sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Wickes_class_destroyers_before_transfer_to_the_UK_1940.JPG/1280px-Wickes_class_destroyers_before_transfer_to_the_UK_1940.JPG","title":"American destroyers awaiting transfer to Britain, 1940","desc":"Sailors inspect depth charges on a quayside in 1940, with the American destroyers USS Buchanan and USS Crowninshield, hull numbers 131 and 134, moored behind them before their transfer to the Royal Navy.","credit":"United States Office of War Information photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wickes_class_destroyers_before_transfer_to_the_UK_1940.JPG","alt":"Sailors in white and dark caps bending over a rack of drum-shaped depth charges on a dock, with grey warships numbered 131 and 134 moored behind an American flag."},"wiki":"Destroyers-for-bases deal"},
    "ww2-204": {"questions":["Churchill warned in December 1940 that Britain could soon no longer pay cash, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered by letting the president lend or lease war material to nations vital to American defence.","Instead of repayment in dollars, Article VII of the 1942 agreement under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bound Britain to work with the United States against discrimination in world trade after the war."],"abstract":"<b>Lend-Lease</b> was the American programme, created by an Act of Congress that became law on 11 March 1941, which let the president supply war material to any country whose defence he deemed vital to the defence of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its occasion was a British crisis of payment, for Churchill warned Roosevelt on 7 December 1940 that the moment approached when Britain could no longer pay cash for shipping and other supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 10 days later Roosevelt told the press he wanted to get rid of the silly, foolish old dollar sign, and compared the plan to lending a garden hose to a neighbour whose house was on fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a broadcast on 29 December he argued that if Britain went down the Axis powers would control Europe, Asia, Africa, Australasia and the high seas, leaving all the Americas living at the point of a gun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bill let the president sell, transfer, exchange, lease or lend defence articles on whatever terms he thought satisfactory, with the benefit to the United States taking any direct or indirect form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Churchill told the Commons on 12 March 1941 that the Americans had in effect written a new Magna Carta, pledging their overwhelming industrial and financial strength to the defeat of Nazism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Repayment was deliberately left vague, and the Anglo-American agreement of February 1942 promised that the final terms would not burden commerce and would include action against discriminatory treatment in international trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That article turned the debt into a commitment to a more open world economy after the war, the consideration Washington wanted in place of dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The programme soon reached beyond Britain, and a mutual aid agreement with the Soviet Union followed in June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> By the State Department historians' count the United States signed Lend-Lease agreements with more than 30 countries and dispensed some $50 billion in aid, which Churchill later called the most unsordid act one nation had done for another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The British Prime Minister (Churchill) to President Roosevelt, December 7, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d20. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Press Conference,\" 17 December 1940, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/press-conference-3. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Fireside Chat,\" 29 December 1940, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-9. [Open access]","National Archives (United States), \"Lend-Lease Act (1941),\" <i>Milestone Documents</i>, with transcript of H.R. 1776, Lend Lease Bill, 10 January 1941, Records of the U.S. House of Representatives, Record Group 233, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/lend-lease-act. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"United States Lease-Lend Bill,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 12 March 1941, vol. 369, cols. 1291-2, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/mar/12/united-states-lease-lend-bill. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, \"Lend-Lease and Military Aid to the Allies in the Early Years of World War II,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/lend-lease. [Open access]","\"Anglo-American Mutual Aid Agreement, February 23, 1942,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/angam42.asp. [Open access]","\"Mutual Aid Agreement Between the United States and the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, June 11, 1942,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/amsov42.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Britain not simply keep buying American arms?","a":"Because by December 1940 its orders in the United States many times exceeded its remaining exchange resources, and Churchill warned that paying cash to the end would leave Britain stripped to the bone after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt use the garden-hose comparison?","a":"Because he wanted to get rid of the dollar sign: munitions would be lent and returned if intact or replaced if destroyed, so the debt would be a gentleman's obligation to repay in kind rather than a dollar loan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the United States ask for in return?","a":"Mainly a promise about the post-war economy: Article VII of the 1942 agreement bound Britain to joint action against discriminatory treatment in international commerce and for lower tariffs, the consideration Washington wanted instead of dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/American-built_155_mm_howitzers_shipped_to_England_as_lend-lease_reach_an_ordnance_depot_on_their_way_to_action._-_NARA_-_196322.jpg/1280px-American-built_155_mm_howitzers_shipped_to_England_as_lend-lease_reach_an_ordnance_depot_on_their_way_to_action._-_NARA_-_196322.jpg","title":"Lend-Lease howitzers in England","desc":"American-built 155 mm howitzers sent to Britain under Lend-Lease reach an ordnance depot in England, about 1941.","credit":"Photographer unknown, National Archives and Records Administration, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American-built_155_mm_howitzers_shipped_to_England_as_lend-lease_reach_an_ordnance_depot_on_their_way_to_action._-_NARA_-_196322.jpg","alt":"A long hall filled with rows of large wheeled artillery guns, their shields stencilled with the words danger air brakes, and two workers standing among them."},"wiki":"Lend-Lease"},
    "ww2-205": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in September 1941 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not alter British policy for India and Burma, since it aimed chiefly at nations under the Nazi yoke.","Imperial preference nearly stalled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until the words 'with due respect for their existing obligations' were added to its fourth point on access to trade and raw materials."],"abstract":"The <b>Atlantic Charter</b> was the joint declaration of eight common principles issued by Roosevelt and Churchill on 14 August 1941, after their meeting off Newfoundland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two leaders met on 9 and 10 August aboard the USS Augusta in Placentia Bay to discuss their war aims and to outline a post-war international system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their countries, the declaration said, sought no aggrandisement, wanted no territorial changes against the freely expressed wishes of the peoples concerned, and respected the right of all peoples to choose their form of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It looked to the final destruction of the Nazi tyranny and to a peace in which all men might live free from fear and want, and Roosevelt told Congress that its principles necessarily included freedom of religion and of information.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither leader got all he wanted, for Roosevelt hoped the charter would encourage Americans to back intervention, while Churchill had come chiefly to get the Americans into the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The hardest bargaining was over trade: when Churchill read a draft promising access to markets without discrimination, he asked at once whether it was meant to apply to the Ottawa agreements on imperial preference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He said he could not accept it without consulting the Dominions, and the final text added the phrase 'with due respect for their existing obligations'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in London on 9 September, Churchill told the Commons that the declaration did not qualify British policy on self-government in India, Burma or other parts of the Empire, being aimed primarily at the nations of Europe under the Nazi yoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 24 September the Allied governments in London, the Soviet Union among them, declared their adherence to its principles, and the Netherlands insisted that the trade reservation must not perpetuate existing obligations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 1 January 1942 the signatories of the Declaration by United Nations subscribed to its programme, and the Anglo-American aid agreement of the following month bound both countries to its economic objectives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["\"Atlantic Charter, August 14, 1941,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/atlantic.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, \"The Atlantic Conference &amp; Charter, 1941,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/atlantic-conf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), August 11, 1941,\" The Atlantic Conference documents, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/at08.asp. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"President Roosevelt's Message to Congress on the Atlantic Charter, August 21, 1941,\" The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/atcmess.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 September 1941, vol. 374, cols. 67-156, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/sep/09/war-situation. [Open access]","\"Inter-Allied Council Statement on the Principles of the Atlantic Charter, September 24, 1941,\" The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/interall.asp. [Open access]","\"Declaration by the United Nations, January 1, 1942,\" <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy 1941-1949</i>, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade03.asp. [Open access]","\"Anglo-American Mutual Aid Agreement, February 23, 1942,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/angam42.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Churchill hesitate over the fourth point?","a":"Because the draft promised access to markets without discrimination, which Welles confirmed was aimed at the Ottawa agreements on imperial preference; Churchill said he could not agree without the Dominions, and the words about existing obligations were added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt want the declaration?","a":"Because he hoped it would encourage Americans to back intervention on the Allied side, and he wanted Britain to affirm publicly that it had made no secret treaties about territory, as the Allies had during the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill exclude India and Burma?","a":"Because, he told the Commons, the meeting had in mind chiefly the restoration of sovereignty to the nations of Europe under Nazi rule, while British policy on self-government in the Empire was covered by separate declarations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Prince_of_Wales-5.jpg/1280px-Prince_of_Wales-5.jpg","title":"Roosevelt and Churchill in Placentia Bay, August 1941","desc":"Roosevelt and Churchill seated on the after deck of HMS Prince of Wales during a church service on 10 August 1941, with their senior commanders standing behind them.","credit":"United States Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_of_Wales-5.jpg","alt":"Two men seated in front, one in a dark suit and one in a dark naval coat, with naval and army officers in uniform standing behind them on a ship's deck."},"wiki":"Atlantic Charter"},
    "ww2-206": {"questions":["Roosevelt told reporters in May 1941 that he himself led the movement whose name the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had grabbed, urging instead the slogan of America first through aid to Britain.","Charles Lindbergh spoke for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Madison Square Garden and Fort Wayne, but after a speech in September 1941 attacking Jews he was called America's number one Nazi."],"abstract":"The <b>America First Committee</b> was an American organisation that campaigned against intervention in the European war in 1940 and 1941, until the bombing of Pearl Harbor brought the United States into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a group of law students who were concerned about being drawn into the war, among them Robert Douglas Stuart Jr., whom the Hoover Institution names as its founder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stuart recalled that the movement began on the campuses before he realised that a student group alone could not make itself effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its national organiser, Page Hufty, ran a speakers bureau that arranged 126 public addresses and rallies in 32 states, alongside newsletters, advertisements, mail campaigns and liaison with Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rallies drew national figures, and one at Madison Square Garden in 1941 put Senator Burton K. Wheeler, Charles Lindbergh, Kathleen Norris and Norman Thomas on the same platform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Lindbergh, the aviator, was already speaking against war in 1940, telling a meeting sponsored by the Yale University America First Committee on 30 October that American involvement would be a disaster for both America and Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He joined the committee in 1941 and toured the country, but after a speech of 11 September attacking Jews he was widely considered, in one newspaper's phrase, America's number one Nazi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He kept speaking, and on 5 October addressed some 3,000 people at an America First rally in Fort Wayne, his first appearance since the controversial address at Des Moines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Roosevelt contested the very name, telling reporters in May 1941 that if anyone led the America First movement it was the president, and that the true slogan was America first through aid to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Stuart remembered that when Pearl Harbor came the members all closed shop, and he went straight into the army as an artillery officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Jean McElwee Cannon, \"Hoover Acquires the Mann Randolph Page and John Archbold Hufty America First Committee Collection,\" Hoover Institution Library &amp; Archives, 4 September 2024, https://www.hoover.org/news/hoover-acquires-page-hufty-america-first-committee-collection. [Open access]","Robert Douglas Stuart Jr., interview by Charles Stuart Kennedy, 14 March 1991, Foreign Affairs Oral History Project, Association for Diplomatic Studies and Training, Library of Congress, https://www.loc.gov/item/mfdipbib001143/. [Open access]","\"Senator Burton K. Wheeler, Charles A. Lindbergh, Kathleen Norris, and Norman Thomas on Speakers' Platform at a Rally of the America First Committee at Madison Square Garden, New York,\" Associated Press photograph, 1941, New York World-Telegram &amp; Sun Collection, Library of Congress, https://www.loc.gov/item/94506130/. [Open access]","\"Lindbergh Warns That War Means Disaster, New Haven, Conn.,\" ACME Newspictures photograph, 30 October 1940, New York World-Telegram and the Sun Newspaper Photograph Collection, Library of Congress, https://www.loc.gov/item/2012647135/. [Open access]","\"Lindbergh Speaks Again, Fort Wayne, Ind.,\" ACME Newspictures photograph, 5 October 1941, New York World-Telegram and the Sun Newspaper Photograph Collection, Library of Congress, https://www.loc.gov/item/2012647136/. [Open access]","Wayne E. Arnold, review of <i>Behold, America: A History of America First and the American Dream</i>, by Sarah Churchwell, <i>European Journal of American Studies</i>, Reviews 2020-3, https://journals.openedition.org/ejas/16324. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Press Conference,\" 23 May 1941, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/press-conference-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did students start the committee?","a":"Because, as Stuart recalled, a group of them at law school feared being drawn into the European war, since everything they had learned suggested the earlier American commitment might have been unwise; he soon saw that only a wider organisation could be effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt object to the committee's name?","a":"Because he argued that aid to Britain served America first, since Britain was holding the line and doing practically all the fighting, and he said the other side had grabbed off something that did not belong to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Lindbergh become a liability?","a":"Because after he attacked Jews in a speech on 11 September 1941 he became widely regarded, in one newspaper's words, as America's number one Nazi, and the press described his next rally as his first since a controversial address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"America First Committee"},
    "ww2-207": {"questions":["The air force as artillery, the navy as engineers and mid-August as the deadline for readiness: Hitler's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laid down how a crossing of the Channel was to be prepared.","Before any crossing, Hitler's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required the Royal Air Force to be so far overcome that it could no longer seriously attack, a condition Germany never met in 1940."],"abstract":"<b>Directive No. 16</b> was Hitler's order of 16 July 1940 to prepare a landing operation against England and, if necessary, to carry it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened by observing that England, despite her militarily hopeless situation, still showed no sign of willingness to come to terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The landing was to be a surprise crossing on a broad front from about Ramsgate to west of the Isle of Wight, with the air force acting as artillery and the navy as engineers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Preparations were to be complete by mid-August, and the project was given the code name Seeloewe, or Sea Lion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed a conference at the Berghof on 13 July, where the army's chief of staff, Franz Halder, presented the plan and an order went out for invasion preparations to start at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its conditions show how much depended on others, for the RAF had to be so far overcome that it showed no considerable aggressive force, and mine-free channels and flanking minefields had to be laid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admiral Raeder later testified that the navy and the army always insisted that air superiority must be on their side, and that the landing could not take place later than the beginning of October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill had already identified the weak point, telling the Commons that even five divisions would need 200 to 250 ships, which the Navy could find and attack before they reached land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Berlin the American chargé reported in August that rumours of imminent action against the British Isles kept accumulating, with the date moved from one weekend to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the war Alfred Jodl told the Nuremberg tribunal that the invasion of England was one of the operational plans drafted in every detail but never carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 442-PS: General Order No. 16 on the Preparation of a Landing Operation against England, 16 July 1940,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 399-403, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag03unit. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 4, entry for 13 July 1940, 115-16, United States Army translation, https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 18 June 1940, vol. 362, cols. 51-64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/18/war-situation. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document C-66: Memorandum for Admiral Assmann, Signed Raeder, 10 January 1944,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 886-87, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 14, proceedings of 18 May 1946, testimony of Erich Raeder, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-18-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, August 2, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d40. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, testimony of Alfred Jodl, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler order invasion preparations in July 1940?","a":"Because, in the directive's own words, England still showed no sign of coming to terms despite what he called her militarily hopeless situation, and he wanted to eliminate the English homeland as a base for the war against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the plan depend on the Luftwaffe?","a":"Because the directive required the RAF to be so far overcome that it could not seriously attack the crossing, and Raeder testified that air superiority was a condition both the navy and the army insisted on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British think an invasion could be beaten?","a":"Because Churchill calculated that even five lightly equipped divisions would need 200 to 250 ships, an armada that air reconnaissance would spot and the Navy could attack long before it landed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1970-084-11%2C_Fecamp%2C_%C3%9Cbung_zum_Unternehmen_%22Seel%C3%B6we%22.jpg","title":"Invasion exercise at Fécamp, October 1940","desc":"German mountain troops wait on the quay at Fécamp to board camouflaged fishing boats in an exercise for the planned landing in England, photographed on 9 October 1940.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1970-084-11, photograph by Hecht, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1970-084-11,_Fecamp,_%C3%9Cbung_zum_Unternehmen_%22Seel%C3%B6we%22.jpg","alt":"A line of helmeted soldiers with packs standing on a quay beside fishing boats painted with dark camouflage stripes, masts and rigging above them."}},
    "ww2-208": {"questions":["In February 1941 German orders presented the build-up against the Soviet Union as a diversion from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the invasion of England that troops were to believe was still coming.","Weather limited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a landing no later than the beginning of October 1940, Raeder testified, after which the crossing would have to wait until May."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Sea Lion</b> was the German plan of 1940 to invade Britain, given its code name, Seeloewe, in Hitler's order of 16 July to prepare a landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its stated aim was to eliminate the English homeland as a base for continuing the war against Germany and, if necessary, to occupy it completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Through July, August and September the German navy was heavily occupied with preparations for the landing, Admiral Raeder later told the Nuremberg tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both the navy and the army insisted that air superiority was a precondition, and Raeder believed Hitler himself recognised control of the air as essential to the operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The weather set a deadline, because a landing could not be made later than the beginning of October and would otherwise have to wait for May of the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Air superiority could not be won to the extent needed, and so the landing was postponed until the spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler's directive of 12 November still kept the option open, ordering the services to improve the basis for taking up Seeloewe again in the spring of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By early December, however, the economic staff's history recorded instructions that there was for the time being no question of an invasion, only a siege of England, and that the preparations should merely be concluded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The plan then served as cover, for an order of February 1941 directed that the deployment against the Soviet Union be presented as a diversion from the invasion of England, while German troops were kept believing it was still being prepared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Alfred Jodl later counted the invasion of England among the operations drafted in every detail and never carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 442-PS: General Order No. 16 on the Preparation of a Landing Operation against England, 16 July 1940,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 399-403, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag03unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 14, proceedings of 18 May 1946, testimony of Erich Raeder, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-18-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 14, proceedings of 17 May 1946, testimony of Erich Raeder, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-17-46.asp. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document C-66: Memorandum for Admiral Assmann, Signed Raeder, 10 January 1944,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 886-87, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document C-147: Directive No. 18, 12 November 1940,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 957-60, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 2353-PS: Extracts from General Thomas's Basic Facts for a History of German War and Armament Economy,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 1082-83, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag04unit. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document C-33: Operations Barbarossa and Marita, German Naval War Staff War Diary, February 1941,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 845-48, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, testimony of Alfred Jodl, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was air superiority essential to Sea Lion?","a":"Because the crossing had to be protected from British air and naval attack; the navy and army both insisted on air superiority, and Raeder believed Hitler recognised control of the air as the essential prerequisite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the operation postponed rather than cancelled in 1940?","a":"Because the weather ruled out a landing after early October, and when air superiority had not been won the landing was put off to the spring; a directive of November 1940 still told the services to improve the basis for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany keep up the appearance of invasion in 1941?","a":"Because an order of February 1941 used it to hide the build-up against the Soviet Union, which was to be presented as a deception diversion from the invasion of England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101II-MN-1369-10A%2C_Wilhelmshaven%2C_Prahme_f%C3%BCr_%22Unternehmen_Seel%C3%B6we%22.jpg","title":"Barges gathered for Sea Lion, 1940","desc":"River barges collected in the harbour at Wilhelmshaven in 1940 for the planned invasion of England, photographed by a German navy propaganda company.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101II-MN-1369-10A, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101II-MN-1369-10A,_Wilhelmshaven,_Prahme_f%C3%BCr_%22Unternehmen_Seel%C3%B6we%22.jpg","alt":"Rows of flat river barges and small tugs moored side by side along a stone quay, with one man standing on the quay in the foreground."},"wiki":"Operation Sea Lion"},
    "ww2-209": {"questions":["Goering first told planners to prepare for a long war only on 6 November 1940, a late date that fits the idea of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built for quick campaigns.","Critics of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> point to steep investment in war industry from 1939 to 1941 and to falling productivity, blaming poor planning rather than a design for short wars."],"abstract":"The <b>Blitzkrieg economy</b> is the thesis that Nazi Germany geared its war industry to short, sharp campaigns rather than a long war, and so left its economy only partly mobilised until the defeat before Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its starting point was the finding of the American bombing survey in 1945 that until the defeat at Moscow German industry was incompletely mobilised and Germany did not foresee the need for full economic mobilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German arms production in 1940 and 1941 was generally below Britain's, much of industry ran on a single shift, and relatively few German women were drawn into factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Albert Speer took over armaments in early 1942, production rose threefold, which the survey read as a sign of how undermobilised the earlier years had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its classic form the thesis held that Germany lacked the manpower and raw materials for a prolonged war and organised production to supply quick campaigns, a strategy that also allowed a prosperous civilian economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After the fall of France, production priorities shifted from land weapons to aircraft for the Luftwaffe, and between September and December 1940 from aircraft to submarines in order to beat Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the regime, the economic staff's history recorded that only on 6 November 1940 did Goering first say Germany should prepare for a long war, and that in early December the invasion of England gave way to a siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Armaments output sagged in the autumn of 1940, with ammunition and aircraft production declining sharply, and the fall was halted only in January 1941, when preparations against Russia began on a modest scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Critics reply that investment in war industry rose steeply from 1939 to 1941 while labour productivity fell, so that the shortfall came from poor planning and structural weakness rather than any Blitzkrieg design.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A 2009 review summarised the standard account as German production stagnating until Speer's economic miracle of 1942 to 1944, which Allied bombing then cut short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1945), 1-2, https://archive.org/details/101634748.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","Burton H. Klein, <i>Germany's Economic Preparations for War</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1959), 27, 78, 189, 235-36, https://archive.org/details/germanys-economic-preparations-for-war-by-burton-h.-klein-1959. [Open access]","Ioannis-Dionysios Salavrakos, \"A Re-assessment of the German Armaments Production during World War II,\" <i>Scientia Militaria: South African Journal of Military Studies</i> 44, no. 2 (2016): 113-45, https://doi.org/10.5787/44-2-1178. [Open access]","R. J. Overy, \"Hitler's War and the German Economy: A Reinterpretation,\" in <i>War and Economy in the Third Reich</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1994), 233-56, https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780198202905.003.0009. [Paywalled]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 2353-PS: Extracts from General Thomas's Basic Facts for a History of German War and Armament Economy,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 1082-83, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag04unit. [Open access]","Robert J. Gordon, review of <i>The Wages of Destruction: The Making and Breaking of the Nazi Economy</i>, by Adam Tooze, <i>Journal of Economic History</i> 69, no. 1 (2009): 312-16, https://gordon.economics.northwestern.edu/files/2021/04/Tooze.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1945 survey think Germany was undermobilised?","a":"Because German arms output in 1940 and 1941 was generally below Britain's, much of industry worked one shift, few women were recruited, and production tripled under Speer after 1942, which suggested how much slack there had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might a short-war strategy need less economic effort?","a":"Because if each enemy could be beaten before the democracies intervened, Germany needed only immediate superiority rather than massive preparations, and could keep its civilian economy prosperous in the meantime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do critics reject the idea?","a":"Because investment in war industry rose steeply between 1939 and 1941 while productivity fell, which points to poor planning, industrial limits and badly made policy rather than a deliberate choice of short wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-210": {"questions":["Whatever came of the political talks with Moscow, preparations for the East already ordered verbally were to continue, Hitler's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stated on 12 November 1940.","Spain was to be brought into the war and Gibraltar seized under Hitler's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which also kept alive the option of a landing in England in the spring of 1941."],"abstract":"<b>Directive No. 18</b> was Hitler's order of 12 November 1940 setting out preparatory measures for the conduct of the war in the near future, a list of the options he still kept open after the fall of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plan for Spain, code-named Felix, aimed to bring Spain into the war, seize Gibraltar and close the straits so as to drive the British from the western Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army was to prepare to occupy the Greek mainland north of the Aegean from Bulgaria, so that German aircraft could strike British air bases threatening the Romanian oilfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Russia it said that whatever came of the political talks just begun, all the preparations for the East already ordered verbally would continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On England it kept open the chance of taking up the landing, Seeloewe, again in the spring of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The choice behind it went back to the summer, when Hitler offered peace proposals to Britain in a Reichstag speech on 19 July, while German officials insisted that Britain should sue for peace and blamed American encouragement for its resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> 6 days earlier Halder had recorded that Hitler was greatly puzzled by Britain's persisting unwillingness to make peace and saw the answer in Britain's hope on Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler told Raeder in August that troop movements to the East were camouflage for the invasion of England, but in September admitted intentions against Russia, and on 26 September the admiral argued against any such undertaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Raeder later reasoned that Hitler, fearing that control of the air over the Channel could not be won that autumn, considered as early as August and September whether an Eastern campaign was feasible first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American bombing survey dated the decision to attack Russia to the autumn of 1940, and a 1959 study argued that one reason for giving up the invasion was that a quick campaign in the East would need no massive economic effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document C-147: Directive No. 18, 12 November 1940,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 957-60, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 4, entry for 13 July 1940, 115-16, United States Army translation, https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 14, proceedings of 17 May 1946, testimony of Erich Raeder, The Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-17-46.asp. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document C-66: Memorandum for Admiral Assmann, Signed Raeder, 10 January 1944,\" in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 886-87, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1945), 1-2, https://archive.org/details/101634748.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Kirk) to the Secretary of State, August 2, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The British Commonwealth, The Soviet Union, The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v03/d40. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, July 20, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v01/d649. [Open access]","Burton H. Klein, <i>Germany's Economic Preparations for War</i> (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1959), 27, 78, 189, 235-36, https://archive.org/details/germanys-economic-preparations-for-war-by-burton-h.-klein-1959. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the directive plan to seize Gibraltar?","a":"Because closing the straits would drive the British out of the western Mediterranean, the object of the planned intervention in Spain, code-named Felix, which also aimed to stop Britain establishing itself elsewhere on the Iberian Peninsula or the Atlantic islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler think Britain refused to make peace?","a":"Because, as Halder recorded after the Berghof conference of 13 July, he saw the answer in Britain's hope on Russia, and expected to have to compel Britain by force, which he disliked because its defeat would benefit Japan and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the East come to outweigh the invasion of England?","a":"Because control of the air over the Channel could not be won in the autumn of 1940, and a quick campaign against Russia looked achievable without a massive economic effort; the bombing survey dates the decision to that autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-211": {"questions":["German claims of 1,921 British aircraft destroyed in July and August, against a true loss of 558, show how much the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought in a fog of overstatement.","The German Air Staff allowed 4 days to crush the fighters south of London, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in October with Fighter Command still in being."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Britain</b> was the air campaign of 1940 in which the Luftwaffe set out to destroy or paralyse RAF Fighter Command, without which a German invasion of the British Isles could not be attempted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill named it before it started, telling the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"House_of_Commons_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Commons</span> on 18 June that the Battle of France was over and that he expected the battle of Britain was about to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Luftwaffe\">German Air Staff</span> planned with remarkable confidence, allowing 4 days to overcome the fighter defences south of a line from London to Gloucester and 4 weeks to beat the RAF as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Hugh_Dowding\">Dowding</span> later chose 10 July as the opening, the day of the first really big German formation sent to bring his fighters to battle, while the German account dates the main offensive from 13 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Air Ministry account published in the war ran its great days to 31 October, when the Luftwaffe had virtually abandoned daylight attacks for night raiding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> On 20 August, with the fighting at its height, Churchill told the House that never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both sides overstated what they achieved, and on 5 September he reported that German claims for July and August added up to 1,921 British aircraft destroyed, against an actual loss of 558.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In September the bombers were turned on London, a change Göring told the Nuremberg tribunal he had not thought of ultimate value, since he knew from the first war that Londoners could take a great deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The German historical museum counts 2,265 German aircraft lost in the air war over Britain, and notes that British fighter output of about 470 a month was double the German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fighter Command lost nearly 450 men killed, and the official historian judged the battle won by a margin whose narrowness was clear only to those who had studied all its phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 18 June 1940, vol. 362, cols. 51–64, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/18/war-situation. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4543–57, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4543. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. IX, 160–61, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-9.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Luftschlacht um England 1940/41,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftschlacht-um-england-194041. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1132–274, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/20/war-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 5 September 1940, vol. 365, cols. 28–48, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/sep/05/war-situation. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XV, 233–50, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-15.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 9, testimony of Hermann Göring, 15 March 1946, 339–40, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-15-46.asp. [Open access]","Air Ministry, <i>The Battle of Britain, August–October 1940: An Air Ministry Account of the Great Days from 8th August–31st October 1940</i> (London: HMSO for the Ministry of Information), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/ministry-of-information-the-battle-of-britain-1941. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany have to beat Fighter Command before any invasion?","a":"Because an invasion could not be attempted while the RAF's fighters were intact. The whole campaign was aimed at destroying or paralysing Fighter Command, and the German Air Staff expected to overcome the fighters in the south within 4 days."},{"q":"Why did the battle's numbers mislead people at the time?","a":"Because both sides overstated their successes. German claims for July and August came to 1,921 British aircraft destroyed when the real loss was 558, and the true balance only emerged later."},{"q":"Why did Göring doubt the turn against London?","a":"Because he did not think bombing the capital would decide anything. He told the Nuremberg tribunal that he knew from the first war that Londoners could take a great deal, so the change had little ultimate value."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/British_and_German_aircraft_a_dog_fight.jpg","title":"Vapour trails after a dogfight, September 1940","desc":"The pattern of condensation trails left by British and German aircraft after a dogfight, photographed on 18 September 1940, during the daylight battle.","credit":"Photograph by Mr Puttnam, War Office official photographer, public domain, Imperial War Museum, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_and_German_aircraft_a_dog_fight.jpg","alt":"A dark sky criss-crossed by dozens of curving white vapour trails, with banks of cloud at the edges of the frame."},"wiki":"Battle of Britain"},
    "ww2-212": {"questions":["In 1940 the groups of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were run from Bentley Priory, where raids were identified before group and sector commanders chose which fighters went up to meet them.","Renamed Air Defence of Great Britain in November 1943, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took back its old name in October 1944 while it was fighting the German flying bombs."],"abstract":"<b>RAF Fighter Command</b> was the part of the Royal Air Force responsible for defending the United Kingdom from air attack, formed on 14 July 1936 out of the older Air Defence of Great Britain organisation, with its headquarters at Bentley Priory in Stanmore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first commander, Air Chief Marshal Sir Hugh Dowding, began with a single fighter group of eight stations and eleven squadrons in south-east England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At various times it also controlled Balloon Command, the Observer Corps, the chain of radar stations and some units of Anti-Aircraft Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Renamed Air Defence of Great Britain in November 1943, it took back its old name in October 1944, and in 1944 and 1945 it helped stop the flying-bomb attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1967 the government announced that Bomber and Fighter Commands would become Strike Command, the fighters forming No. 11 Group, and a farewell parade at Bentley Priory in April 1968 marked the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In September 1939 Dowding reminded the Air Ministry that its own estimate for home defence was 52 squadrons, while he had the equivalent of only 34.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 9 July 1940 he had 58 squadrons in various states of efficiency, and 1,253 pilots against an establishment of 1,450.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A new No. 10 Group took over the south-west that July, freeing No. 11 Group for London and the south-east, while Nos. 12 and 13 Groups lay further north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dowding judged it impossible to run the fighting from his own headquarters, so the command identified raids, group commanders chose the sector and the strength to send, and sector commanders directed the fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The plan was for Hurricanes to attack the bombers while Spitfires dealt with the German fighters, though the pre-war habit of tight formation flying hampered the pilots for much of the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["The National Archives (UK), \"Air Ministry: Fighter Command: Registered Files,\" series AIR 16, administrative history, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C2071. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. II, 36, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-2.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. IX, 149–52, 162, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-9.html. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4544, 4547–48, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4544. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Bomber, Fighter and Transport Commands,\" written answer, <i>Hansard</i>, 16 June 1967, vol. 748, cols. 154–55W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written-answers/1967/jun/16/bomber-fighter-and-transport-commands. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Disbandment of Fighter Command (Ceremonies),\" written answer, <i>Hansard</i>, 1 April 1968, vol. 762, col. 23W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written-answers/1968/apr/01/disbandment-of-fighter-command-ceremonies. [Open access]","Royal Air Force Museum, \"The New Tactics,\" <i>History of the Battle of Britain</i>, online exhibition, https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/online-exhibitions/history-of-the-battle-of-britain/the-new-tactics/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Dowding hand the fighting down to groups and sectors?","a":"Because he judged it impossible to control the battle from his own headquarters. The command identified raids, group commanders chose which sector and how many fighters to send, and sector commanders directed the fighters onto the enemy."},{"q":"Why was No. 10 Group formed in July 1940?","a":"Because the south-west needed a fighter group of its own. When the new group took over that region it freed No. 11 Group to concentrate on the defence of London and the south-east."},{"q":"Why was Dowding anxious about his strength in 1939?","a":"Because he had far fewer squadrons than the Air Ministry itself thought necessary. Its estimate for home defence was 52 squadrons, while he had the equivalent of only 34."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Royal_Air_Force_1939-1945-_Fighter_Command_CH442.jpg","title":"The King at Hornchurch, June 1940","desc":"Flying Officer John Allen of No. 54 Squadron receives the Distinguished Flying Cross from King George VI at Hornchurch on 27 June 1940, with Fighter Command's chief, Sir Hugh Dowding, among the officers watching.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, Imperial War Museum CH 442, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Air_Force_1939-1945-_Fighter_Command_CH442.jpg","alt":"An officer faces a young pilot beside a small draped table on an airfield, watched by a line of officers, with ranks of airmen and a large hangar behind."},"wiki":"RAF Fighter Command"},
    "ww2-213": {"questions":["A sector controller watching the enemy's track and his own fighters' positions on one table, fixed by radio direction-finding, was the working end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Battle of Britain.","Before the war a 30 per cent interception rate was thought satisfactory, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of radar, observers and operations rooms was credited with far higher rates in 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Dowding system</b> was the network of radar stations, observers, telephone lines and operations rooms through which RAF Fighter Command found German raids and sent fighters to meet them in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its backbone was a chain of large radar stations about 50 kilometres (30 miles) apart, which could see aircraft at an average of about 130 kilometres (80 miles) but nothing flying below 300 metres (1,000 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once a raid had crossed the coast the Observer Corps was the only means of tracking it, and its plots passed through observer centres to the sector and group tables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every radar plot went first to a Filter Room at the command's headquarters at Bentley Priory, where surplus information was weeded out and friend told from foe before tracks went by direct line to the operations tables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The work began late in 1936, when the Air Staff and Fighter Command devised a system of fighter control to use the new stations and appointed the signals officer R. G. Hart, whom Dowding credited with the Filter Room's details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In a sector operations room the controller saw the enemy's track beside that of his own fighters, which were fixed by direction-finding on their radio signals for 15 seconds in every minute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With both before him, an Air Ministry account explained during the war, interception became in theory a fairly simple mathematical problem, and he talked the pilots onto the enemy by radio telephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before the war 30 per cent interception had been thought satisfactory, but the same account claimed rates of 75, 90 and sometimes 100 per cent in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The warning let fighters be sent up to intercept even through thick cloud, and the German historical museum calls British radar a revolutionary early-warning system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its weak point was the sector stations, and Dowding and Park feared the system might break down if bombing forced them onto emergency operations rooms, while Post Office engineers and army signallers kept repairing the lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4546–48, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4546. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. II, 39, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-2.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XV, 235, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-15.html. [Open access]","Air Ministry, <i>The Battle of Britain, August–October 1940: An Air Ministry Account of the Great Days from 8th August–31st October 1940</i> (London: HMSO for the Ministry of Information), 7–8, https://archive.org/details/ministry-of-information-the-battle-of-britain-1941. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Luftwaffe,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftwaffe. [in German] [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Air Ministry: Fighter Command: Registered Files,\" series AIR 16, administrative history, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C2071. [Open access]","National Records of Scotland, \"Our Records: Sir Robert Watson-Watt (1892–1973),\" ScotlandsPeople, https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/news-and-articles/our-records-sir-robert-watson-watt-1892-1973. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did every radar plot pass through a Filter Room?","a":"Because raw plots were confusing. Surplus information had to be weeded out and friendly aircraft told apart from enemy ones before the tracks went by direct line to the command, group and sector tables."},{"q":"Why did the system need the Observer Corps as well as radar?","a":"Because the radar stations could not see aircraft flying below about 300 metres, and they could not follow a raid inland. Once it had crossed the coast, the observers were the only means of tracking it."},{"q":"Why were the sector stations the system's weak point?","a":"Because the controllers who directed the fighters worked in the sector operations rooms. Dowding and Park feared the system might break down if bombing forced them onto emergency rooms, and repair crews kept mending the lines."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Interior_of_RAF_Fighter_Command%27s_Sector_%27G%27_Operations_Room_at_Duxford%2C_Cambridgeshire%2C_September_1940._CH1401.jpg","title":"A sector operations room, Duxford, September 1940","desc":"Inside the Sector 'G' operations room at Duxford in Cambridgeshire in September 1940, with the call signs of the squadrons it controlled on the wall behind.","credit":"Photograph by Stanley Arthur Devon, public domain, Imperial War Museum CH 1401, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Interior_of_RAF_Fighter_Command%27s_Sector_%27G%27_Operations_Room_at_Duxford,_Cambridgeshire,_September_1940._CH1401.jpg","alt":"Men in RAF uniform at raised desks with telephones and headsets in a crowded room, a map pinned to the wall and a young airman seated at a table in front."},"wiki":"Dowding system"},
    "ww2-214": {"questions":["Tall masts on high ground could not be hidden, so the stations of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were given the misleading name of Radio Direction Finding stations before the word radar came in.","A Heyford bomber's echoes picked up near Daventry in February 1935 led to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose coastal stations could not see aircraft flying below about 300 metres."],"abstract":"<b>Chain Home</b> was the line of radar stations built along the British coast from the mid-1930s, beginning with five stations north and south of the Thames Estuary and planned as a chain of twenty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began in January 1935, when Robert Watson-Watt of the National Physical Laboratory was asked whether radio waves could harm aircraft, said they could not, and suggested they could detect them by their echoes instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 26 February a Heyford bomber flying through the beam of the transmitter at Daventry gave clear echoes up to about 13 kilometres (8 miles) away, and Dowding, then in charge of air research, found more money for the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The masts, about 75 metres (250 feet) tall on high ground, could not be hidden, so the stations were given the misleading name of Radio Direction Finding stations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bawdsey was the first, twenty stations were ready by 1939, and at various times the chain came under Fighter Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In 1940 Dowding described the stations as about 50 kilometres (30 miles) apart, seeing aircraft at an average of about 130 kilometres (80 miles) and in the best conditions nearly 320 kilometres (200 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their serious limitation was that they could not see aircraft below about 300 metres (1,000 feet), so smaller low-level stations were added to the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 12 August the Luftwaffe attacked six of the stations, and while five suffered no lasting damage, Ventnor on the Isle of Wight was out of action until a replacement opened at Bembridge on the 23rd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not knowing that Ventnor had been knocked out, Göring was too hasty in discouraging further attacks on the radar stations, a chance the official historian thought the Germans had wasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dowding judged that its warnings could have been had by no other means, a German museum calls British radar revolutionary, and Watson-Watt received £50,000 in 1952 for initiating it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. II, 36–39, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-2.html. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4546, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4546. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XII, 184, 191, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-12.html. [Open access]","National Records of Scotland, \"Our Records: Sir Robert Watson-Watt (1892–1973),\" ScotlandsPeople, https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/news-and-articles/our-records-sir-robert-watson-watt-1892-1973. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Luftwaffe,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftwaffe. [in German] [Open access]","Bawdsey Radar Trust, \"The Daventry Experiment,\" https://www.bawdseyradar.org.uk/the-daventry-experiment/. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Air Ministry: Fighter Command: Registered Files,\" series AIR 16, administrative history, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C2071. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the stations called Radio Direction Finding stations?","a":"Because they could not be hidden. Their tall masts stood on high ground near the sea, so a misleading name was used to discourage questions about what they really did."},{"q":"Why did the chain need extra low-level stations?","a":"Because the main stations could not see aircraft flying below about 300 metres. Smaller stations were added to the line to cover that gap."},{"q":"Why did the attack on Ventnor not cripple the defences?","a":"Because the Germans did not follow it up. Göring, unaware that the station had been knocked out, discouraged further attacks on radar stations, and a replacement opened at Bembridge 11 days later."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/The_360ft_transmitter_towers_at_Bawdsey_Chain_Home_radar_station%2C_Suffolk%2C_May_1945._CH15337.jpg","title":"Chain Home towers at Bawdsey, 1945","desc":"The transmitter towers of the Chain Home station at Bawdsey in Suffolk, about 110 metres (360 feet) tall, photographed in May 1945.","credit":"Photograph by Flight Lieutenant Goodchild, Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, Imperial War Museum CH 15337, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_360ft_transmitter_towers_at_Bawdsey_Chain_Home_radar_station,_Suffolk,_May_1945._CH15337.jpg","alt":"Two tall steel lattice towers seen from below against a cloudy sky, with platforms at intervals and thin wires strung between them."},"wiki":"Chain Home"},
    "ww2-215": {"questions":["Created a baron in 1943, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave his retirement to spiritualism and to campaigns against cruelty to animals after leading Fighter Command through 1940.","Replaced at Fighter Command by Air Marshal Douglas on 25 November 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had defended his own view of strategy against the Air Staff for 4 years."],"abstract":"<b>Hugh Dowding</b> was the RAF officer who led Fighter Command from its creation in July 1936 until November 1940, and so commanded Britain's fighter defences through the Battle of Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Born in Moffat in Scotland in 1882 and educated at Winchester and the Royal Military Academy at Woolwich, he served as a gunner abroad and joined the Royal Flying Corps when the First World War began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As the Air Ministry's member for research in 1935 he asked for proof that aircraft would reflect radio waves, and after the Daventry test he backed radar with steps the official historian rated among the most decisive in British history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was due to retire in June 1939 but was asked to stay on because of the tense international situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Created a baron in July 1943, he gave much of his later life to spiritualism and to campaigns against cruelty to animals, making his maiden speech in the Lords on slaughterhouses in 1948, and he died in February 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1940 he watched his squadrons being drained away to France, seeing his resources slipping away like sand in an hour-glass, until he asked to put his case in person to the War Cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> To his inexpressible relief the Cabinet decided to send no more fighters to France except to cover the final evacuation, a decision he called one of the great turning points of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He recorded that Churchill spoke with affectionate raillery of him and his 'Chicks', and that every one of them was needed before the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His stubborn defence of his own view of strategy had often brought him into conflict with the Air Staff, and on 25 November 1940 Air Marshal Douglas replaced him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official historian concluded that, whatever rank posterity gave him, he would surely be remembered as one who had deserved well of his country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Melvyn Willin, \"Hugh Dowding,\" <i>Psi Encyclopedia</i> (London: Society for Psychical Research, 2024), https://psi-encyclopedia.spr.ac.uk/articles/hugh-dowding/. [Open access]","UK Parliament, \"Mr Hugh Dowding,\" <i>Hansard 1803–2005</i>, people index, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/people/mr-hugh-dowding. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, \"Slaughter of Animals,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 18 March 1948, vol. 154, cols. 989–1009, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1948/mar/18/slaughter-of-animals. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. II, 36–37, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-2.html. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4545, 4555, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4545. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XVII, 265–66, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-17.html. [Open access]","Moffat Museum, \"Military Service 1939–1941,\" <i>Dowding: The Man</i>, exhibition, https://moffatmuseum.co.uk/?p=457. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Dowding fight to stop fighters going to France?","a":"Because he was watching the force meant to defend Britain disappear. He saw his resources slipping away like sand in an hour-glass, and he thought the Cabinet's decision to stop the flow one of the great turning points of the war."},{"q":"Why does the official history link him to radar?","a":"Because in 1935, as the Air Ministry's member for research, he asked for proof that aircraft reflected radio waves, and after the Daventry test he backed the work with steps the historian rated among the most decisive in British history."},{"q":"Why was he replaced in November 1940?","a":"Because his stubborn defence of his own view of strategy had often brought him into conflict with the Air Staff. Air Marshal Douglas took over Fighter Command on 25 November."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Hugh_Dowding.jpg","title":"Air Chief Marshal Sir Hugh Dowding","desc":"Dowding in an official portrait by a Ministry of Information photographer; the exact date of the photograph is not recorded.","credit":"Ministry of Information official photographer, public domain, Imperial War Museum D 1417, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hugh_Dowding.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of a moustached officer in an RAF peaked cap with a braided peak, pilot's wings and medal ribbons."},"wiki":"Hugh Dowding"},
    "ww2-216": {"questions":["Announced by Hitler in February 1935 with some 800 combat aircraft, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found in 1940 that its Bf 110 fighters and Ju 87 dive-bombers could not survive over England.","Göring told the Nuremberg tribunal that destroying the RAF had mattered most to him as commander-in-chief of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the air battle over England in 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Luftwaffe</b> was the air force of Nazi Germany, built up in secret after the Treaty of Versailles had banned German military aviation and announced by Hitler in February 1935 with some 800 combat aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the third branch of the Wehrmacht beside the army and navy, under Hermann Göring as commander-in-chief, and it also controlled the anti-aircraft artillery and the paratroops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1939 it had about 400,000 men and more than 4,000 modern aircraft in four air fleets, and its close support of the army helped bring quick victories in Poland and France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allied aircraft production outstripped Germany's as the war went on, and by 1944 it lacked above all well-trained pilots, while Allied command of the air left Germany unable to stop the landings in Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 430,000 members of Luftwaffe units had died by the surrender of 8 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For the attack on Britain, Luftflotten 2 and 3 under Kesselring and Sperrle held some 1,130 long-range bombers, 320 dive-bombers and about 1,050 fighters, while Luftflotte 5 in Norway added about 130 bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kesselring later told the Nuremberg tribunal that he had urged an invasion straight after Dunkirk, and Göring that the elimination of the enemy air force had been of decisive importance to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Weaknesses soon showed, for the twin-engined Bf 110 could not hold its own against the nimbler British fighters and the Ju 87 dive-bomber suffered so heavily that it was withdrawn from the fight over Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By September, Dowding wrote, escorting fighters flew inside and below the bombers and in a stack up to 9,000 metres (30,000 feet), like looking up the escalator at Piccadilly Circus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A German museum faults Göring's wholly mistaken picture of British defences and production, and the crews' claims ran far ahead of reality, reaching 1,921 British aircraft for July and August against 558 actually lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Luftwaffe,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftwaffe. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Luftschlacht um England 1940/41,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftschlacht-um-england-194041. [in German] [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. IX, 160–61, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-9.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 9, testimony of Albert Kesselring, 13 March 1946, 206, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-13-46.asp. [Open access]","Royal Air Force Museum, \"The New Tactics,\" <i>History of the Battle of Britain</i>, online exhibition, https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/online-exhibitions/history-of-the-battle-of-britain/the-new-tactics/. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4551, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4551. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 5 September 1940, vol. 365, cols. 28–48, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/sep/05/war-situation. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 9, testimony of Hermann Göring, 15 March 1946, 340, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-15-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Luftwaffe built in secret at first?","a":"Because the Treaty of Versailles had banned Germany from having military aircraft. Its existence was only announced by Hitler in February 1935, by which time it had some 800 combat aircraft."},{"q":"Why did the Bf 110 and the Ju 87 fail over Britain?","a":"Because they met fast single-engined fighters. The twin-engined Bf 110 could not hold its own against the nimbler British fighters, and the Ju 87 dive-bomber lost so heavily that it was withdrawn from the fight."},{"q":"Why did German commanders misread the battle?","a":"Because they started from a mistaken picture of British defences and production, and their crews' claims ran far ahead of reality, reaching 1,921 British aircraft destroyed in July and August when 558 had been lost."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-058-1784A-14%2C_Frankreich%2C_Jagdflugzeuge_Me_109_auf_Feldflugplatz.jpg","title":"Messerschmitt Bf 109s on a French airfield, August 1940","desc":"Messerschmitt Bf 109 E fighters of Jagdgeschwader 51 on a field airfield in France in August 1940, the month the Luftwaffe threw its full weight against Britain; the men sit on the grass beside them.","credit":"Erhardt Eckert, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-058-1784A-14, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-058-1784A-14,_Frankreich,_Jagdflugzeuge_Me_109_auf_Feldflugplatz.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white photograph of a single-engined fighter with a large three-bladed propeller parked on grass, a group of men sitting and lying beside it, a lorry behind and more fighters in a row in the distance."},"wiki":"Luftwaffe"},
    "ww2-217": {"questions":["German planners called 13 August 1940 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the start of the assault proper on the RAF after 4 weeks of preliminary raids on shipping and the coast.","Unescorted bombers wrecked the airfield at Eastchurch on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but it belonged to Coastal Command rather than the fighters and was fit for use again within 10 hours."],"abstract":"<b>Adlertag</b>, or ‘Eagle Day’, was the German name for 13 August 1940, the day the Luftwaffe opened its main offensive against the RAF in the Battle of Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seen from Germany the preliminary phase had begun in mid-July and the assault proper came 4 weeks later, while the British reckon the battle from 10 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler had ordered the Luftwaffe to destroy the enemy air force as soon as possible and suggested a start on 5 August, but poor weather forecasts led Göring to put it off to the 11th and then to the 13th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aim, in a German museum's summary, was to win the air superiority an invasion needed through heavy attacks on naval forces, arms factories, air defences and RAF bases in southern England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An Air Ministry account published during the war dated the offensive from 8 August instead, a date Dowding also saw grounds for as the first attack in force on targets on land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The 13th began badly for the attackers, since dull weather prompted a last-minute postponement to the afternoon that reached some units too late to be obeyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unescorted bombers of Luftflotte 2 hit the Coastal Command airfield at Eastchurch, which was fit for use again within 10 hours, while formations bound for Farnborough and Odiham missed, and 88 dive-bombers turned back with their mission unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ju 87 dive-bomber's losses over England later grew so heavy that it had to be withdrawn from the fight against Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In all the Luftwaffe flew 1,485 sorties that day and lost 45 aircraft, against Fighter Command's loss of 13 aircraft but only seven pilots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official historian found that the day had not produced a single successful attack on a Fighter Command station and had brought the German air force the worst rebuff it had yet received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XII, 183–88, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-12.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. X, 164–65, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-10.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Luftschlacht um England 1940/41,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftschlacht-um-england-194041. [in German] [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4544, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4544. [Open access]","Air Ministry, <i>The Battle of Britain, August–October 1940: An Air Ministry Account of the Great Days from 8th August–31st October 1940</i> (London: HMSO for the Ministry of Information), 9–10, https://archive.org/details/ministry-of-information-the-battle-of-britain-1941. [Open access]","Royal Air Force Museum, \"The New Tactics,\" <i>History of the Battle of Britain</i>, online exhibition, https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/research/online-exhibitions/history-of-the-battle-of-britain/the-new-tactics/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Eagle Day delayed from early August?","a":"Because of the weather. Hitler had suggested starting on 5 August, but poor forecasts led Göring to postpone first to the 11th and then to the 13th, and even then a last-minute delay reached some units too late."},{"q":"Why did the heavy raid on Eastchurch matter so little?","a":"Because Eastchurch belonged to Coastal Command, not to the fighters the offensive was meant to destroy, and it was fit for use again within 10 hours."},{"q":"Why do British and German accounts start the battle on different days?","a":"Because each side measured from a different event. The British reckon from 10 July and a wartime Air Ministry account from 8 August, while German planners saw Adlertag as the start of the assault proper."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/The_Battle_of_Britain_1940_CH2064.jpg","title":"A shot-down Ju 87 dive-bomber, 1940","desc":"Spitfire pilots beside the wreckage of a Junkers Ju 87 Stuka that, according to the Imperial War Museum's caption, they shot down as it attacked a Channel convoy in 1940.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, Imperial War Museum CH 2064, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Britain_1940_CH2064.jpg","alt":"Four smiling airmen in life jackets stand beside the overturned, wrecked fuselage of an aircraft on rough ground."},"wiki":"Adlertag"},
    "ww2-218": {"questions":["Replaced at Uxbridge by Leigh-Mallory in December 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on to command the air defence of Malta from July 1942 with a plan of forward interception.","Dowding praised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for adjusting his tactics to each new German move while 11 Group bore the brunt of the attacks on its sector stations in 1940."],"abstract":"<b>Keith Park</b> was the RAF officer who commanded No. 11 Group, the fighter group defending London and the south-east, through the Battle of Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the First World War he had served as a lieutenant in the Royal Field Artillery, as his officer's file in the National Archives records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dowding paid a very sincere tribute to the way Park adjusted his tactics to each new German move, and noted that in the heaviest fighting he acted from day to day on his own initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 3 weeks after Douglas replaced Dowding, Leigh-Mallory, the champion of big formations, took Park's place at Uxbridge, for victory had not silenced criticism of his methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In July 1942 he succeeded Lloyd in command of the RAF on Malta, where his forward interception plan sent Spitfires head-on at the bombers, and in 1945 he took up another overseas command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 19 August 1940 Park ordered that most squadrons sent against a raid should go for the bombers, that sector stations be guarded when threatened, and that pilots fight over their own territory where possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 31 August to 6 September Fighter Command lost 161 aircraft and the Luftwaffe 189, and the official historian called it the most critical week of the battle for Dowding and Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The brunt fell on a score of single-seater squadrons and some 400 pilots of No. 11 Group, while five forward airfields and six of its seven sector stations were badly damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Park wrote that if the enemy had kept up his attacks on Biggin Hill and the neighbouring sectors, the fighter defences of London would have been in a perilous state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He found that newly arrived squadrons often took the heaviest losses, and he sent squadrons up in pairs because assembling bigger formations cost time he did not have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XIII, 203–17, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-13.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XVII, 266, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-17.html. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4551, 4554, 4556, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4551. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>The Air Battle of Malta: The Official Account of the R.A.F. in Malta, June 1940 to November 1942</i> (London: HMSO, 1944), https://archive.org/details/air-battle-malta. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Lieutenant Keith Rodney Park, Royal Field Artillery,\" WO 339/41919, 1915–1921, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C1095813. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), \"Assumption of Command by Air Marshal Sir Keith Park,\" AIR 23/2318, 1945, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C750210. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Park insist on guarding his sector stations?","a":"Because the defence of London depended on them. He wrote that if the enemy had kept attacking Biggin Hill and the neighbouring sectors, London's fighter defences would have been in a perilous state."},{"q":"Why did he send squadrons up in pairs rather than big wings?","a":"Because assembling bigger formations cost time he did not have, while a pair of squadrons could be sent up quickly against each raid."},{"q":"Why was he moved from No. 11 Group in December 1940?","a":"Because victory had not silenced criticism of his methods. Leigh-Mallory, the champion of big formations, took his place at Uxbridge soon after Douglas replaced Dowding."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Keith_Park_and_his_Hurricane_OK1%2C_1941.jpg","title":"Keith Park with his Hurricane","desc":"Park in flying clothing beside his Hurricane, coded OK1, in a print he signed and dated 15 September 1941.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Air Force Museum of New Zealand, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keith_Park_and_his_Hurricane_OK1,_1941.jpg","alt":"A man in a long flying coat, leather helmet and goggles stands beside a fighter's fuselage marked with a roundel and large letters."},"wiki":"Keith Park"},
    "ww2-219": {"questions":["Made Reichsmarschall on 19 July 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> soon lost prestige when the Luftwaffe he had commanded since 1935 failed to defeat the RAF over England.","A First World War fighter ace who took over Richthofen's old wing in 1918, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took cyanide at Nuremberg in October 1946, hours before his execution."],"abstract":"<b>Hermann Göring</b> was the Nazi leader who commanded the Luftwaffe from 1935, and whom the Nuremberg tribunal judged the moving force for aggressive war second only to Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in Rosenheim in Bavaria in 1893, he was a celebrated fighter pilot in the First World War, winning the Pour le Mérite and taking over Richthofen's old wing in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He met Hitler in 1922 and joined the Nazi party, and after 1933 he controlled the Prussian police and helped set up the first concentration camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1936 he took charge of the Four Year Plan to prepare the economy for war, and on 19 July 1940 he was made Reichsmarschall, though failure in the battle over England soon cost him prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Found guilty on all four counts at Nuremberg, he took cyanide in his cell on 15 October 1946, hours before he was to be hanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 19 August 1940 Göring reserved to himself the right to order raids on Liverpool and London, while his air fleets kept up the attack on Fighter Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After RAF bombers struck Berlin on the night of 25 August, Hitler declared on 4 September that London would be bombed in reprisal, and the German Air Ministry had issued its orders for the new phase 2 days earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> London was first cleared as a target on the night of 5 September, and on the 7th more than 300 bombers with some 600 fighters attacked the docks, killing about a thousand Londoners that day and night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that the attacks were meant to terrorise London into submission, and that four-fifths of the 2,000 civilians killed in the first half of September had died there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Nuremberg Göring said he had not thought the reprisal raids of ultimate value, since destroying the RAF mattered more to him, while Kesselring agreed that political targets had been ordered from a certain date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Susanne Eckelmann, \"Hermann Göring,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/hermann-goering. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: Goering,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judgoeri.asp. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XIII, 203, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-13.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XV, 234–40, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-15.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 9, testimony of Hermann Göring, 15 March 1946, 339–40, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-15-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 9, testimony of Albert Kesselring, 13 March 1946, 206, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-13-46.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 17 September 1940, vol. 365, cols. 121–38, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/sep/17/war-situation. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Luftschlacht um England 1940/41,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftschlacht-um-england-194041. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did London become the Luftwaffe's main target in September 1940?","a":"Because after RAF bombers struck Berlin on the night of 25 August, Hitler declared that London would be bombed in reprisal. The German Air Ministry had already issued orders for the new phase, and the great daylight raid came on 7 September."},{"q":"Why did Göring say the raids on London had little value?","a":"Because, as he told the Nuremberg tribunal, destroying the RAF mattered more to him than bombing the capital, so he did not think the reprisal raids of ultimate value."},{"q":"Why did the tribunal rank him second only to Hitler?","a":"Because it judged him the moving force for aggressive war after Hitler himself, and found him guilty on all four counts of the indictment."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-343-0678-13%2C_Westfront%2C_Hermann_G%C3%B6ring_mit_Luftwaffen-Offizieren.jpg","title":"Göring with Luftwaffe officers, autumn 1940","desc":"Göring, in a greatcoat with the Knight's Cross at his collar, among senior Luftwaffe officers outside a large building in Belgium or France in September or October 1940, according to the German Federal Archives.","credit":"Photograph by Dreesen, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-343-0678-13, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-343-0678-13,_Westfront,_Hermann_G%C3%B6ring_mit_Luftwaffen-Offizieren.jpg","alt":"A heavy-set officer in a peaked cap and long coat with a cross at his throat stands in a courtyard as other officers in caps talk beside him."},"wiki":"Hermann Göring"},
    "ww2-220": {"questions":["In 1942 Churchill declined to make 15 September an official Air Trafalgar Day, yet the date lived on as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, honouring the fighter pilots' victory of 1940.","A bomb in the grounds of Buckingham Palace and two great raids on London by about 220 bombers marked the Sunday later remembered as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Britain every September."],"abstract":"<b>Battle of Britain Day</b> is 15 September, kept in Britain each year to mark the Battle of Britain, the date chosen largely because about 180 German aircraft were once thought to have been shot down on that day in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1942 an MP asked Churchill to have the day named Air Trafalgar Day or something similar, and he declined at that stage of the war, while not wishing to discourage spontaneous commemoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In October 1945 another member asked for a Thanksgiving Day to Fighter Command in perpetuity, and the Prime Minister replied that it would be considered together with a national day of remembrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An Air Ministry account published during the war called Sunday 15 September one of 'the great days', when 500 German aircraft fought a running battle from Hammersmith to Dungeness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 2 days afterwards Churchill told the Commons that it was the most brilliant and fruitful action yet fought by RAF fighters, and that after searching inquiries he believed the figures were not exaggerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The German plan sent about 220 bombers of Luftflotte 2 against London in two waves, with a gap between them that let British squadrons refuel and rearm, and without the usual feints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Park met the first wave with squadrons in pairs while No. 12 Group sent five squadrons south in a single formation, and a bomb fell in the grounds of Buckingham Palace but failed to explode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dowding called the third phase the most critical stage of the battle, writing that on this day the guns and fighters together accounted for 185 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In fact Fighter Command lost 26 aircraft and the Luftwaffe about 60, while a German museum gives 56 lost out of 1,700 aircraft sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The official historian still ranked it among the most important days, for if 15 August showed the German command that air supremacy would not come quickly, 15 September went far to convince it that it would not come at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. XV, 242–45, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-15.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 17 September 1940, vol. 365, cols. 121–38, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/sep/17/war-situation. [Open access]","Air Ministry, <i>The Battle of Britain, August–October 1940: An Air Ministry Account of the Great Days from 8th August–31st October 1940</i> (London: HMSO for the Ministry of Information), 17, 20, https://archive.org/details/ministry-of-information-the-battle-of-britain-1941. [Open access]","Hugh Dowding, \"The Battle of Britain,\" despatch of 20 August 1941, <i>Supplement to the London Gazette</i>, no. 37719, 11 September 1946, 4557, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37719/supplement/4557. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Luftschlacht um England 1940/41,\" LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftschlacht-um-england-194041. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Battle of Britain (Commemoration),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 29 September 1942, vol. 383, col. 672, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/sep/29/battle-of-britain-commemoration. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Royal Air Force Victories (Commemoration),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 15 October 1945, vol. 414, col. 686, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/oct/15/royal-air-force-victories-commemoration. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was 15 September chosen for the commemoration?","a":"Because about 180 German aircraft were at one time thought to have been shot down that day. The real figure was about 60, but the day still went far to convince the German command that it would not win air supremacy."},{"q":"Why did the German attack on 15 September go badly?","a":"Because the plan split the raid on London into two waves with a gap that let British squadrons land, refuel and rearm before the second, and it left out the usual feints."},{"q":"Why did Churchill refuse an official Air Trafalgar Day in 1942?","a":"Because he did not want to make such a recommendation at that stage of the war, though the government did not wish to discourage spontaneous commemorations of the anniversary."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Air_Chief_Marshal_Lord_Dowding_taking_the_salute_with_Air_Marshal_Sir_Trafford_Leigh_Mallory%2C_Air_Officer_Commanding-in-Chief_Fighter_Command%2C_at_a_parade_on_the_third_anniversary_of_the_Battle_of_Britain_at_Fighter_Com_CH11054.jpg","title":"Battle of Britain anniversary, Bentley Priory, 1943","desc":"Lord Dowding takes the salute beside Air Marshal Sir Trafford Leigh-Mallory at the parade for the third anniversary of the Battle of Britain at Fighter Command headquarters on 15 September 1943.","credit":"Photograph by George Woodbine, public domain, Imperial War Museum CH 11054, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Air_Chief_Marshal_Lord_Dowding_taking_the_salute_with_Air_Marshal_Sir_Trafford_Leigh_Mallory,_Air_Officer_Commanding-in-Chief_Fighter_Command,_at_a_parade_on_the_third_anniversary_of_the_Battle_of_Britain_at_Fighter_Com_CH11054.jpg","alt":"Two RAF officers stand on a rug on a lawn, one saluting, with a band and a newsreel camera on a car behind them."},"wiki":"Battle of Britain Day"},
    "ww2-281": {"questions":["Anastas Mikoyan later wrote that Stalin placed the entire responsibility on him when he became first deputy chairman of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 26 June 1941.","Defence plants had the highest priority when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent more than 1,360 large factories east in some 3 months of 1941, 455 of them to the Urals."],"abstract":"The <b>Evacuation Council</b> was the body the Soviet leadership created on 24 June 1941, 2 days into the German invasion, to decide what should be moved east and where.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first chairman was Lazar Kaganovich, the railways commissar, and Anastas Mikoyan, made first deputy chairman on 26 June, later wrote that Stalin had placed the entire responsibility on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 16 July the State Defence Committee reorganised the council under Nikolai Shvernik, with Aleksei Kosygin and Mikhail Pervukhin as his deputies, because Kaganovich was overloaded with transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No such body had been planned before the war, a gap one historian explains by Stalin's fear that planning for retreat might encourage defeatism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Defence plants and their workers had the highest priority, and industrial evacuees received larger rations, better housing and better medical care than the women, children and old people moved with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Counts differ: Nikolai Voznesensky, head of the State Planning Commission, wrote that more than 1,360 large, mainly war, plants went east in some 3 months of 1941, 455 of them to the Urals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A modern estimate counts 1,523 large defence enterprises evacuated in 1941 and 1942, while Mikoyan gave 2,593 enterprises moved wholly or partly by rail over the whole war, with more than 10 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Moscow and Donets industrial regions shut down to evacuate in October 1941, and Voznesensky called the last 2 months of that year the hardest, with plants stopped at their old sites and not yet working at the new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 13 November 1941 the people's commissar of the chemical industry ordered the builders of Factory No. 34 to restore evacuated production faster and to report every 5 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A postwar study written from German records noted the Germans' surprise at the Russians' ability to move war plants and their failure to cut the railways to the Urals, and a German museum counts the transfer among the reasons the war turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["A. I. Mikoyan, “On the Evacuation Council,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (1989), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-009 (4 August 1989), 16–22, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335521. [Open access]","Kristen Edwards, “Wartime Evacuation of Soviet Civilians, 1941–1943,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/wartime-evacuation/. [Open access]","N. A. Voznesensky, <i>Soviet Economy during the Second World War</i> (New York: International Publishers, 1949), 36–38, https://archive.org/details/economy_202103. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 39–40, 135–136, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 88–92, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","M. F. Denisov, letter to the head of construction of Factory No. 34 on speeding the restoration of evacuated production, 13 November 1941, United State Archive of Chelyabinsk Oblast, f. R-1359, op. 1, d. 14, l. 85, Electronic Library of Historical Documents, https://docs.historyrussia.org/ru/nodes/299943-pismo-narkoma-himicheskoy-promyshlennosti-sssr-m-f-denisova-nachalniku-stroitelstva-zavoda-locale-nil-34-ob-uskorenii-rabot-po-vosstanovleniyu-evakuirovannyh-proizvodstv-13-noyabrya-1941-g [in Russian]. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Überfall auf die Sowjetunion 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ueberfall-auf-die-sowjetunion-1941 [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union need a new body to run the evacuation?","a":"Because nothing of the kind had been planned before the war; a historian of the evacuation links that gap to Stalin's fear that planning for retreat would encourage defeatism, so the council had to be created 2 days after the invasion began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the counts of evacuated factories disagree?","a":"Because they count different things over different periods: Voznesensky gave more than 1,360 large plants for some 3 months of 1941, a modern estimate gives 1,523 large defence enterprises for 1941 and 1942, and Mikoyan gave 2,593 enterprises moved wholly or partly by rail across the whole war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the last months of 1941 the worst for Soviet industry?","a":"Because evacuated plants had stopped producing at their old sites but had not yet started at the new ones, while the Moscow and Donets regions had shut down to evacuate in October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Letter_from_Mikhail_Denisov_to_the_head_of_construction_of_Factory_No._34%2C_1941.jpg","title":"Restarting an evacuated plant, November 1941","desc":"A typed order of 13 November 1941 from the people's commissar of the chemical industry, telling the builders of Factory No. 34 to speed the restoration of evacuated production and report every five days.","credit":"People's Commissariat of the Chemical Industry of the USSR, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Letter_from_Mikhail_Denisov_to_the_head_of_construction_of_Factory_No._34,_1941.jpg","alt":"A typed Russian document under a Soviet coat of arms, stamped 'declassified', with handwritten notes in the margins and a large signature at the foot."}},
    "ww2-282": {"questions":["Hitler's order of 8 December 1941 halting all major operations in the East came 3 days after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had begun north of the Soviet capital.","Churchill told the Commons in January 1942 that the Russian front had begun to advance, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> having pushed German armies back more than 200 kilometres in places."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow counter-offensive</b> was the Soviet attack of December 1941 that threw the German armies back from the approaches to the capital, the first time in the war that the Wehrmacht had been forced into a large retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Georgy Zhukov sent the Stavka a plan for it on 30 November, conceived not against an enemy already halted but as a near-to-last move in a battle that might still turn against the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin kept the decision on when to commit the reserve armies gathered east of Moscow in his own hands, and was sparing in releasing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kalinin Front attacked on 5 December about 150 kilometres (90 miles) north of Moscow, and Zhukov's West Front struck Army Group Centre the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cold worked for the attackers: on the night of 4 December the temperature fell to −32 °C (−25 °F), German tanks would not start the next morning, and frostbite came to cost the Wehrmacht more men than enemy fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 8 December Hitler ordered a halt to all major offensive operations in the East, blaming the surprisingly early winter, and his order of 16 December to hold with fanatical resistance could not stop retreats of more than 200 kilometres (120 miles) in places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became clear even to Hitler that the war would now last far longer than planned, and he dismissed Brauchitsch and took command of the army himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stalin then raised his sights: on 5 January 1942 the Politburo and the Stavka approved a general offensive along almost the whole front, designed, an American official history judges, not to leave a single German army untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In London, Churchill told the Commons that the Russian front had not only stood unbroken but had begun the advance in a magnificent counter-attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The victory brought Zhukov sudden fame, and a Soviet general later wrote that Stalin disliked his rising popularity and that Beria's staff resumed gathering compromising material on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 61–66, 137–142, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Winterschlacht 1941/42,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winterschlacht-194142 [in German]. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 91–93, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Forced Labor: Soviet POWs, January 1942 through May 1945,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/forced-labor-soviet-pows-january-1942-through-may-1945. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Angriff auf Moskau 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941 [in German]. [Open access]","N. G. Pavlenko, “Reflections on the Fate of a Military Leader (Notes of a Military Historian),” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 12 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-006 (8 May 1989), 18–24, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335401. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Zhukov's plan a gamble when he proposed it?","a":"Because the Germans had not yet stopped; the plan was conceived as a near-to-last move in a battle that might still turn against the Russians, and Stalin was slow to release the reserve armies it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German army suffer so badly in December 1941?","a":"Because it met the attack in extreme cold, with temperatures near −32 °C (−25 °F), tanks that would not start and frostbite cases that came to exceed losses to enemy fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the counter-offensive grow in January 1942?","a":"Because Stalin judged the Germans to be in confusion after their setback at Moscow and badly prepared for winter, and on 5 January the Politburo and the Stavka approved a general offensive meant to leave no German army untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Horsemen_of_1st_Guard_Cavalry_Corps_entering_Odoev.jpg","title":"Soviet cavalry entering Odoyev, December 1941","desc":"Horsemen of General Pavel Belov's 1st Guards Cavalry Corps ride into Odoyev in the Tula region on 22 December 1941, past a captured German 37 mm anti-tank gun.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horsemen_of_1st_Guard_Cavalry_Corps_entering_Odoev.jpg","alt":"Riders in winter coats and fur hats move along a snowy town street, with a small wheeled anti-tank gun in the foreground."}},
    "ww2-283": {"questions":["Having signed the Allied declaration in Berlin on 5 June 1945 beside Eisenhower and Montgomery, Marshal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sent the next year to command the Odessa military district.","Removed unexpectedly as Soviet defence minister on 26 October 1957, Marshal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been Stalin's deputy as supreme commander during the Second World War."],"abstract":"<b>Georgy Zhukov</b> was the Soviet commander most often sent where the war was being decided, a conscripted private of the Imperial Russian Army in the First World War who rose to be Stalin's deputy as supreme commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His career before 1939 was spent in the cavalry and the armoured forces that grew out of it, and he may have survived Stalin's purge of the army through the patronage of the old cavalry leaders Voroshilov, Budenny and Timoshenko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made his name at Khalkhin Gol in Mongolia, where his attack of 20 August 1939 encircled the Japanese force within 3 days and the pocket was destroyed by 31 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Zhukov himself believed that the urgent summons to Khalkhin Gol had saved him from arrest, as a Soviet military historian who knew him later recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Germany invaded he sat on the Stavka from its first day, and an American official history notes that he was almost always away from Moscow, as a Stavka representative or in a major field command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In October 1941 he used the pause that the autumn mud forced on the Germans to strengthen the defences around Moscow with the help of the city's civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> He then sent the Stavka the plan for the December counter-offensive, and his West Front struck Army Group Centre on 6 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 5 June 1945 he signed in Berlin, beside Eisenhower, Montgomery and de Lattre de Tassigny, the declaration by which the four Allies assumed supreme authority over Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Stalin soon turned on him: after a meeting in 1946 that heard testimony extracted from arrested officers about a supposed military conspiracy, Zhukov was sent to command the Odessa and then the Urals military districts, a disgrace that lasted until Stalin's death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He returned to become defence minister, only to be removed on 26 October 1957 at the apparent peak of his prestige, and was partly rehabilitated after Khrushchev fell in 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Vladislav A. Goncharov, “Marshal Zhukov: Warrior, Commander, Citizen” (Strategy Research Project, US Army War College, Carlisle Barracks, PA, 1999), vii–viii, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA382914. [Open access]","Clayton B. Kyker, “The Genesis of an Operational Commander: Georgi K. Zhukov at Khalkin Gol” (paper, Naval War College, Newport, RI, 1998), 1–10, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA348831. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 8–10, 24–31, 61–62, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Winterschlacht 1941/42,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winterschlacht-194142 [in German]. [Open access]","N. G. Pavlenko, “Reflections on the Fate of a Military Leader (Notes of a Military Historian),” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 12 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-006 (8 May 1989), 18–24, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335401. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, Office of Current Intelligence, “Party-Military Relations in the USSR and the Fall of Marshal Zhukov,” Soviet Staff Study, OCI No. 0077/59 (1959), 1, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-0001408626. [Open access]","“Declaration Regarding the Defeat of Germany and the Assumption of Supreme Authority by Allied Powers; June 5, 1945,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger01.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Angriff auf Moskau 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941 [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Zhukov so rarely in Moscow during the war?","a":"Because Stalin used him as a representative of the Stavka and as a front commander, sending him to the sectors that mattered most, from Leningrad and Moscow onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin push Zhukov aside after the victory?","a":"Because his popularity, already rising after Moscow, troubled Stalin, and in 1946 extracted testimony about a supposed conspiracy was used against him before he was sent to the Odessa and Urals districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Khalkhin Gol matter to his later career?","a":"Because it was his first great encirclement, a short, decisive battle in August 1939, and Zhukov believed the summons there had also spared him arrest in the purge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Georgy_Zhukov.jpg","title":"Marshal Georgy Zhukov","desc":"Zhukov in a postwar portrait as Marshal of the Soviet Union, with four Hero of the Soviet Union stars above his rows of ribbons.","credit":"Ministry of Defence of the Russian Federation (mil.ru), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgy_Zhukov.jpg","alt":"A smiling older man in a peaked cap with a red band and a grey uniform, four gold stars and many medal ribbons on his chest."},"wiki":"Georgy Zhukov"},
    "ww2-284": {"questions":["Compulsory military training for every male Soviet citizen aged 16 to 50 was ordered from October 1941 by a decree of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Stalin chaired.","Molotov, Voroshilov, Malenkov and Beria sat with Stalin on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the war cabinet formally above the Stavka in the Soviet chain of command."],"abstract":"The <b>State Defence Committee</b>, known by its Russian initials as the GKO, was the war cabinet in which the Soviet leadership concentrated the whole of state power on 30 June 1941, 8 days after the German invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founding decision named Stalin chairman, Molotov his deputy, and Voroshilov, Malenkov and Beria as members, and obliged every citizen and every party, Soviet, Komsomol and military body to carry out its decisions without question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mikoyan, Voznesensky and Kaganovich were added to it in February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American official history calls it the war cabinet the pre-war plans had envisaged and the highest organ of wartime government, whose decrees had the force of law in both military and civilian affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Stavka, the military high command, was formally subordinate to it, but the committee dealt mainly with the non-military side of the war, and neither body had any authority independent of Stalin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A Soviet general wrote in 1988 that mobilising people and material resources for the armed forces was one of its most important functions, and that it ordered the training of reservists on 16 July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1 October 1941 a decree of the committee made military training compulsory, alongside their jobs, for every male citizen aged 16 to 50, on a course of 110 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 16 July 1941 it reorganised the Evacuation Council, which moved factories east, and in mid-October it decreed the evacuation of the government from Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mikoyan recalled that when the decree on evacuating the capital was adopted, Stalin proposed that the Politburo and committee members leave that same day while he followed the next morning, though in the end the inner group's departure was abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Railways came under its hand as well: from February 1942 a Transport Committee worked under it, and Ivan Kovalev, later a member, recalled being told by Stalin in July 1941 to draft a committee decision to get military freight to the fronts on time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the USSR, Central Committee of the VKP(b) and Council of People's Commissars, “Obrazovanie Gosudarstvennogo Komiteta Oborony,” decision of 30 June 1941, <i>Vedomosti Verkhovnogo Soveta SSSR</i>, 1941, no. 31, Istoricheskie Materialy, https://istmat.org/node/24298 [in Russian]. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 28–31, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","P. T. Kunitskiy, “Restoration of the Broken Strategic Defensive Front in 1941,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 7 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-001 (12 January 1989), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335328. [Open access]","State Defence Committee, “O vseobshchem obyazatelnom obuchenii voennomu delu grazhdan SSSR,” <i>Izvestiya</i>, no. 221, 19 September 1941, Istoricheskie Materialy, https://istmat.org/node/24305 [in Russian]. [Open access]","A. I. Mikoyan, “On the Evacuation Council,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (1989), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-009 (4 August 1989), 16–22, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335521. [Open access]","N. Ye. Medvedev, “By Strong-Willed Decisions...,” interview with I. V. Kovalev, <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 12 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-006 (8 May 1989), 24–31, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335401. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet leadership create a new war cabinet in June 1941?","a":"Because the emergency after the invasion demanded that all power be concentrated in one body; the pre-war plans had foreseen such a war cabinet, and on 30 June it was given the whole of state power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the committee concern itself with factories and railways rather than battles?","a":"Because the Stavka handled operations while the committee dealt mainly with the non-military side of the war, mobilising people and resources, moving industry east and keeping freight moving to the fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could neither the committee nor the Stavka act on its own?","a":"Because Stalin chaired both, and an American official history concludes that neither had any authority independent of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%93%D0%9A%D0%9E_%E2%84%96_979%D1%81%D1%81_%D0%BE%D1%82_30.11.1941_%D0%B3.jpg","title":"A decree of the committee, 30 November 1941","desc":"Decree No. 979ss of 30 November 1941, ordering anti-aircraft guns for the icebreakers working in the White Sea, signed in red by Stalin as chairman and endorsed by other members.","credit":"State Defence Committee of the USSR, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Постановление_ГКО_№_979сс_от_30.11.1941_г.jpg","alt":"A typed one-page Russian decree with a large red signature at the foot, several handwritten signatures and a 'declassified' stamp."},"wiki":"State Defense Committee"},
    "ww2-285": {"questions":["Zhukov and Vasilevsky were often sent to the fronts as representatives of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Soviet supreme headquarters that kept the strategic reserves in Stalin's hands.","Order 227 of July 1942 required army commanders who allowed unauthorised retreats to be sent to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for court-martial, under Stalin's direct authority."],"abstract":"The <b>Stavka</b> was the general headquarters of the Soviet armed forces in the Second World War, named and modelled on the Stavka, or staff, of the imperial Russian army in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pre-war plans had foreseen such a headquarters, but none existed when Germany invaded, and it was formed on 23 June 1941 out of the Main Military Council, with Marshal Timoshenko as chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first members included Stalin, Molotov, Zhukov, Voroshilov, Budenny and Admiral Kuznetsov, and Zhukov later said there had been two commanders-in-chief, Timoshenko by law and Stalin in fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 10 July it became the Stavka of the Supreme Command under Stalin, with Marshal Shaposhnikov added, and in August, once Stalin had become supreme commander-in-chief, the Stavka of the Supreme High Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American official history judges that it had no authority independent of Stalin, who seldom convened it in full and used its members as personal advisers and the General Staff as his planning agency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its chief instrument in the field was the representative, a member or officer such as Zhukov sent to coordinate the fronts in a crucial sector, a practice that gave it effective control where it mattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also held the strategic reserves: in July 1941 it hurriedly moved the 16th Army to the Western Front, and the decision on when to commit the reserve armies before Moscow was reserved to it, which meant to Stalin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin's Order No. 227 of July 1942 required army commanders who let their troops retreat without authority to be sent to the Stavka for court-martial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Soviet officer's tally credits the forces under its direction with 51 strategic operations and more than 250 operations at front level in the course of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its standing was jealously guarded, and a Soviet military historian records that late in 1945 Stalin accused Zhukov of claiming the victories for himself and playing down the role of the Stavka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 8–10, 24–31, 61–62, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","P. T. Kunitskiy, “Restoration of the Broken Strategic Defensive Front in 1941,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 7 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-001 (12 January 1989), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335328. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 30–36, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Vladislav A. Goncharov, “Marshal Zhukov: Warrior, Commander, Citizen” (Strategy Research Project, US Army War College, Carlisle Barracks, PA, 1999), vii–viii, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA382914. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order No. 227: Not One Step Back,” 28 July 1942, in <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/nazi-tide-stops/no-one-steps-back/. [Open access]","N. G. Pavlenko, “Reflections on the Fate of a Military Leader (Notes of a Military Historian),” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 12 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-006 (8 May 1989), 18–24, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335401. [Open access]","N. Ye. Medvedev, “By Strong-Willed Decisions...,” interview with I. V. Kovalev, <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 12 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-006 (8 May 1989), 24–31, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335401. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Stavka created only after the invasion?","a":"Because although pre-war plans foresaw a general headquarters on the imperial model, none existed on 22 June 1941, and it had to be formed the next day from the Main Military Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Stavka send representatives to the fronts?","a":"Because it gave the headquarters effective control in crucial sectors and a level of command able to turn strategic guidance into operations, with men such as Zhukov coordinating several fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Stavka hard to separate from Stalin?","a":"Because he chaired it from July 1941, seldom met it in full, used its members as personal advisers and kept decisions such as the commitment of reserves in his own hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Boris_Shaposhnikov_02.jpg","title":"Marshal Boris Shaposhnikov","desc":"Boris Shaposhnikov, the Soviet staff officer added to the Stavka on 10 July 1941, in a portrait from the 1940s.","credit":"Ministry of Defence of the Russian Federation (mil.ru), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_Shaposhnikov_02.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white head-and-shoulders portrait of a man with swept-back hair in a dark uniform with embroidered collar, a star at the throat and medals."},"wiki":"Stavka"},
    "ww2-286": {"questions":["About two million volunteers in some 60 people's militia divisions joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1941, alongside reservists called up by decree of the Supreme Soviet.","Stalin told Harry Hopkins in July 1941 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would give him 350 divisions under arms by the spring campaign of 1942."],"abstract":"<b>Soviet mobilisation</b> in 1941 was the calling to arms of reservists and volunteers after the German invasion, by which the Red Army filled new formations to replace the armies it was losing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the war began the Red Army had about 4.7 million soldiers, only about half of them stationed in the western regions where the blow fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A decree of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet on 22 June 1941 declared mobilisation in fourteen military districts of men liable for service born from 1905 to 1918, with 23 June as its first day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1 July some 5.3 million men had been called up, and in August reservists born from 1890 to 1904 and young men born in 1923 followed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Volunteers came too: by the autumn about 60 people's militia divisions had been raised from some two million volunteers, and around 400,000 people served in local fighter battalions during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The State Defence Committee ordered the training of reservists in July and, from 1 October 1941, compulsory military training for every male citizen aged 16 to 50, on a course of 110 hours alongside his job.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stalin told Harry Hopkins on 31 July 1941 that he had 240 divisions at the front and 20 in reserve, and could mobilise 350 divisions by the spring campaign of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American official history reckons from scattered Soviet figures that 291 divisions were sent from the Stavka reserves before 1 December 1941, and that at least 182 divisions, or 39 per cent of all those committed, had been lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mobilisation produced men faster than officers able to command large formations, and what became of the militia divisions, the same history adds, is impossible to determine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German intelligence on 1 December 1941 found the mobilisation of so many Soviet divisions less surprising than the quantity of tanks and guns they brought, which exceeded anything known before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the USSR, “O mobilizatsii voennoobyazannykh,” decree of 22 June 1941, <i>Vedomosti Verkhovnogo Soveta SSSR</i>, 1941, no. 29, Istoricheskie Materialy, https://istmat.org/node/24303 [in Russian]. [Open access]","P. T. Kunitskiy, “Restoration of the Broken Strategic Defensive Front in 1941,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 7 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-001 (12 January 1989), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA335328. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 28–31, 46–49, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","State Defence Committee, “O vseobshchem obyazatelnom obuchenii voennomu delu grazhdan SSSR,” <i>Izvestiya</i>, no. 221, 19 September 1941, Istoricheskie Materialy, https://istmat.org/node/24305 [in Russian]. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum by Mr. Harry L. Hopkins, Personal Representative of President Roosevelt, Moscow, July 31, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, 805–807, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d752. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 91, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Überfall auf die Sowjetunion 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ueberfall-auf-die-sowjetunion-1941 [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Red Army survive the losses of 1941?","a":"Because mobilisation kept filling new formations: 5.3 million men were called up by 1 July, older and younger age groups followed in August, and 291 divisions were sent from the reserves before December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were people's militia divisions raised as well?","a":"Because volunteers in the threatened areas were organised alongside the call-up, about two million of them in some 60 militia divisions by the autumn of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was compulsory training ordered in September 1941?","a":"Because the army needed trained reserves, so the State Defence Committee required every male citizen aged 16 to 50 to take 110 hours of military training without leaving his work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/RIAN_archive_662757_New_recruits_during_mobilization.jpg","title":"New recruits in Moscow, 1941","desc":"Men called up for service gather outside barracks in Moscow in 1941; the archive dates the photograph variously to 23 June and to August of that year.","credit":"Anatoliy Garanin, RIA Novosti archive, image #662757, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_662757_New_recruits_during_mobilization.jpg","alt":"Men in civilian caps and jackets walk in a loose column along a cobbled street between brick buildings, while others stand in a crowd."}},
    "ww2-287": {"questions":["Remembered for its demand of 'not a step back', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered each Soviet army to form three to five blocking detachments against panic and unauthorised retreat.","A Soviet journal printed the full text of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only in 1988, at its readers' request, although the order had been read aloud to every company in 1942."],"abstract":"<b>Order No. 227</b> was the order Stalin signed as people's commissar of defence on 28 July 1942, as German armies drove towards Stalingrad, remembered by its demand of 'not a step back'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It told the army that its resources were not limitless: with Ukraine, Belorussia, the Baltic and the Donbas lost, the country had fewer people, factories, grain and metal, and no longer outnumbered the Germans in manpower or grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It accused units of the Southern Front of abandoning Rostov without serious resistance or orders, and said the people were losing faith in the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commanders, commissars and political workers who retreated from their positions without orders were to be treated as traitors of the motherland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Citing the harsh measures the Germans had used to restore discipline after their winter retreat, it ordered every front to form penal battalions of 800 men and every army three to five blocking detachments of up to 200, to shoot panic-mongers on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The order was to be read aloud in every company, battery and squadron, and on 29 July the army's political chief demanded reports on its explanation twice a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was not published at the time: Soviet accounts quoted only parts of it, the full text survived in captured German records, and a Soviet journal printed it complete only in 1988, at its readers' request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A general who first read it near Voronezh wrote that the troops took it as justice against panic-mongers and that he knew of no blocking detachment firing on its own men in his sector.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its slogan became part of the defence of Stalingrad, where, in one account, the defenders held on with their backs to the Volga, mindful of Order No. 227.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By August 1944 a front's political chief reported that most blocking detachments were guarding staffs and communications rather than doing what the order required, and proposed disbanding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Joseph Stalin, “Text of Stalin's Order 227 ‘Not a Step to the Rear!’,” order of the People's Commissar of Defence No. 227, 28 July 1942, <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 45–47, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order No. 227: Not One Step Back,” 28 July 1942, in <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/nazi-tide-stops/no-one-steps-back/. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 360–363, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","P. N. Lashchenko, “Dictated by Severe Necessity,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 47–49, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","A. Shcherbakov, order of 29 July 1942 to the chiefs of the political directorates of the fronts and districts, <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 49, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","Lewis Siegelbaum, “The Battle of Stalingrad,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/nazi-tide-stops/. [Open access]","A. Lobachev, report of 25 August 1944 to the chief of the Main Political Administration of the Red Army, “On Shortcomings in the Activities of the Front Blocking Detachments,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 49–50, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin issue the order in July 1942?","a":"Because the German summer offensive was driving towards Stalingrad and the Caucasus, Rostov had been given up without serious resistance, and the order argued that further retreat would leave the country without people, grain and factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the order point to the German army as a model?","a":"Because it claimed that penal units and special detachments behind unsteady divisions had restored German discipline after the winter retreat, and asked whether the Red Army should not learn from its enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the full text not published until 1988?","a":"Because Soviet accounts gave only its patriotic passages; the complete order survived in captured German records and a Soviet journal printed it only at its readers' request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Order_227_page_1.jpg","title":"Order No. 227, first page","desc":"The first of seven typed pages of Order No. 227, dated Moscow, 28 July 1942, and marked 'not for publication' at the top right.","credit":"I. Stalin, People's Commissariat of Defence of the USSR, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Order_227_page_1.jpg","alt":"A typed Russian document headed with the word Prikaz in large letters, the number 227 and the date 28 July 1942."},"wiki":"Order No. 227"},
    "ww2-288": {"questions":["A wound received even on the first day returned a soldier from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to his unit with his former rank, a veteran Soviet general wrote in 1988.","Created by Order 227 in July 1942 alongside the blocking detachments, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took middle and senior commanders found guilty of cowardice or of breaking discipline."],"abstract":"<b>Shtrafbat</b>, the Russian shortening of ‘penal battalion’, names the punishment units that Stalin's Order No. 227 of 28 July 1942 created in the Red Army, alongside blocking detachments posted behind unreliable troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each front was to form one to three penal battalions of 800 men for middle and senior commanders and political workers guilty of cowardice or instability, and each army five to ten penal companies of 150 to 200 for soldiers and junior commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They were to serve on the most difficult sectors, so that their men could atone for their crimes against the motherland with their blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each army was also to form three to five well-armed blocking detachments of up to 200 men, to stand behind unreliable divisions and shoot panic-mongers and cowards on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The version of the order found in captured German records gives a penal battalion 500 men, and an American official history notes that the NKVD's own troops had often been formed into blocking detachments with powers of summary judgment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A term in a penal unit ran from 1 to 3 months, and a veteran general wrote in 1988 that a wound, even on the first day, returned the soldier to his unit with his former rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He insisted that the units were led by regular officers rather than convicts and that service in them left no criminal record, testimony written in defence of an order he had served under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The blocking detachments drifted from their purpose: in August 1944 the political chief of the Third Baltic Front reported that most he inspected were guarding staffs and communications or serving as cooks, clerks and tailors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He proposed reorganising or disbanding them as having lost their importance, and on 29 October 1944 Stalin ordered the separate blocking detachments disbanded by 13 November and their men sent to reinforce rifle divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That order dealt with the blocking detachments alone and did not mention the penal battalions and companies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Joseph Stalin, “Text of Stalin's Order 227 ‘Not a Step to the Rear!’,” order of the People's Commissar of Defence No. 227, 28 July 1942, <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 45–47, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","P. N. Lashchenko, “Dictated by Severe Necessity,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 47–49, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","A. Lobachev, report of 25 August 1944 to the chief of the Main Political Administration of the Red Army, “On Shortcomings in the Activities of the Front Blocking Detachments,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 49–50, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order Disbanning Blocking Detachments,” order of the People's Commissar of Defence No. 0349, 29 October 1944, <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 8 (1988), translated in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, JPRS-UMJ-89-002 (13 February 1989), 50, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-002. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 10–12, 361–363, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","“Stalin: Order No. 227 – Not One Step Back (July 28, 1942),” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, translated from Robert H. McNeal, ed., <i>Resolutions and Decisions of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union</i> (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1974), https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/nazi-tide-stops/no-one-steps-back/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were penal units sent to the hardest sectors?","a":"Because Order No. 227 meant them as a way for men guilty of cowardice or instability to atone for their crimes against the motherland with their blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the blocking detachments disbanded in 1944?","a":"Because a front's political chief found that most were guarding staffs and communications or doing administrative jobs, and proposed ending them; Stalin ordered them disbanded and their men sent to rifle divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should the 1988 account by a veteran general be read with care?","a":"Because it was written in defence of the order by a man who had served under it, insisting on the fairness of the penal units and doubting that blocking detachments fired on their own troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/57/Order_227_page_5.jpg","title":"Order No. 227, page 5","desc":"The fifth page of Order No. 227 of 28 July 1942, where the order sets out the forming of one to three penal battalions of 800 men within each front.","credit":"I. Stalin, People's Commissariat of Defence of the USSR, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Order_227_page_5.jpg","alt":"A typed page in Russian numbered 5 at the top, with numbered and lettered paragraphs of instructions."},"wiki":"Shtrafbat"},
    "ww2-289": {"questions":["Regulations of 8 September 1941 calling the use of arms against captives legal were cited at Nuremberg as part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Eastern Front.","By February 1942 some two million captured Red Army soldiers were dead, the worst of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falling in the first winter of starvation and epidemics."],"abstract":"The <b>German mistreatment of Soviet prisoners of war</b> killed, by the estimate of the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, some 3.3 million of the roughly 5.7 million Red Army soldiers who fell into German hands, about 57 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 231,000 British and American prisoners Germany held, about 8,300, or 3.6 per cent, died in captivity, a contrast the museum attributes to deliberate policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany claimed it owed Soviet prisoners nothing because the Soviet Union had not ratified the Geneva Convention of 1929, although general international law also protected prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moscow had told the United States on 8 August 1941 that it would observe the Hague Convention of 1907 and other rules of war, but only so far as Germany observed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In November the deputy foreign commissar told the Swedish minister, who represented Soviet interests in Germany, that the Soviet Union could have no dealings with a state given to such barbarous practices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The first winter was the worst: about 3.35 million Soviet soldiers were captured in 1941, and some two million of those taken by February 1942 were dead from starvation, exposure, disease or shooting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many received at most 700 calories a day, almost 5,000 died each day in October 1941, and in December a German general reported some 2,500 deaths a day in the camps of his territory alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From July 1941 security police teams screened the camps for Jews, communists and others judged intolerable, who were handed to the SS and shot, with estimates of the victims ranging from at least 140,000 to 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Regulations of 8 September 1941 declared the use of arms against Soviet prisoners as a rule legal, and the Nuremberg tribunal rejected the defence that the Soviet Union's absence from the Geneva Convention excused what followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Figures still differ: the American museum counts about 930,000 Soviet prisoners still in German custody in January 1945, while a German museum puts those who survived in Germany to the end at around 630,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Treatment of Soviet POWs: Starvation, Disease, and Shootings, June 1941–January 1942,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-treatment-of-soviet-pows-starvation-disease-and-shootings-june-1941january-1942. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “The Law Relating to War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Murder and Ill-Treatment of Prisoners of War,” Judgment, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 9, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, 1005–1006, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d962. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Thurston) to the Secretary of State, November 21, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, 1013–1014, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d971. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Kriegsgefangenschaft,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 18 September 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsgefangenschaft [in German]. [Open access]","Alfred Rosenberg, “About Discussions with the Fuehrer on 14 December 1941,” Document 1517-PS, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1517-ps.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many Soviet prisoners die compared with British and American ones?","a":"Because their deaths were the result of deliberate policy: they were starved on rations as low as 700 calories a day and screened for shooting, while British and American prisoners were treated far better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany cite the Geneva Convention?","a":"Because the Soviet Union had not ratified it, Germany claimed no duty of humane care; the Nuremberg tribunal rejected that defence, since general international law also protected prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for Soviet prisoners differ?","a":"Because the surviving sources are incomplete and measure different things; one museum counts about 930,000 still in custody in January 1945, another around 630,000 surviving in Germany at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B08478%2C_Sowjetische_Gefangene_bei_Balta.jpg","title":"Soviet prisoners near Balta, August 1941","desc":"A long column of Soviet prisoners taken in the fighting around Balta in Ukraine is marched to a collecting camp in August 1941, watched by a German soldier.","credit":"Brunnengräber, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-B08478, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B08478,_Sowjetische_Gefangene_bei_Balta.jpg","alt":"A German soldier with a canteen walks along a dirt road beside a long column of men in worn uniforms stretching into the distance across flat country."},"wiki":"German atrocities committed against Soviet prisoners of war"},
    "ww2-290": {"questions":["Rosenberg complained in December 1941 that Erich Koch ruled Ukraine as if he answered to Hitler directly, bypassing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Rosenberg headed in Berlin.","The Nuremberg tribunal found that the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> directed that the Hague rules of land warfare did not apply in the conquered East."],"abstract":"The <b>Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories</b> was the German ministry set up by Hitler's decree of 17 July 1941 to govern conquered Soviet lands through civilian officials once the fighting had passed on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler appointed the party ideologist Alfred Rosenberg as Reich minister, with his office in Berlin, and gave him the civil administration of the new territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territories were to be divided into Reich Commissariats, general regions and districts, and Hitler kept for himself the appointment of the Reich commissars and commissars-general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its authority was limited from the start: the commanders of the armed forces kept their sovereign rights, and the powers of the Four-Year Plan and of Himmler as chief of the SS and police were left outside the decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In December 1941 Rosenberg complained to Hitler that the Reich commissar for Ukraine, Erich Koch, behaved as if he dealt with Hitler directly and ruled without Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Nuremberg tribunal found that Rosenberg helped to shape the policies of Germanisation, exploitation, forced labour and the extermination of Jews and opponents in the East, and set up the administration that carried them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He directed that the Hague rules of land warfare did not apply in the occupied Eastern territories, and had said before the invasion that feeding the German people stood at the top of Germany's claims on the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of his subordinates summed up the policy in August 1942: the Slavs were to work for Germany, and in so far as they were not needed, they might die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ministry's own liaison officer with Army Group South reported on 5 October 1941 that, after the fire in Kiev, the city's Jews had been liquidated on 29 and 30 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A US Army study written after the war counted the failure to frame a workable policy in the occupied territories among the reasons resistance grew, and Rosenberg was executed at Nuremberg on 16 October 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Adolf Hitler, “Decree of the Fuehrer Concerning the Administration of the Newly-Occupied Eastern Territories,” 17 July 1941, Document 1997-PS, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1997-ps.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Rosenberg,” Judgment, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judrosen.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “The Law Relating to War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” Judgment, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Alfred Rosenberg, “About Discussions with the Fuehrer on 14 December 1941,” Document 1517-PS, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1517-ps.asp. [Open access]","Daniel Wosnitzka, “Alfred Rosenberg,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/alfred-rosenberg [in German]. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 88–89, https://archive.org/details/da-pam-20-261a-the-german-campaign-in-russia-planning-and-operations-1940-1942-1955. [Open access]","Captain Dr. Koch, report no. 10 of the deputy of the Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories with Army Group South, 5 October 1941, Document 053-PS, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/053-ps.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the ministry weaker than its title suggests?","a":"Because Hitler's decree left the armed forces their sovereign rights, kept the powers of the Four-Year Plan and of Himmler outside it, and reserved the appointment of the top commissars to Hitler himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rosenberg quarrel with Erich Koch?","a":"Because Koch, the Reich commissar for Ukraine, gave the impression that he dealt with Hitler directly and ruled without Berlin, which undercut the minister's authority over him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Rosenberg condemned at Nuremberg?","a":"Because the tribunal found that he helped shape the policies of exploitation, forced labour and the extermination of Jews in the East and built the administration that carried them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B05926%2C_Pressekonferenz%2C_A._Meyer%2C_A._Rosenberg%2C_W._Weiss.jpg","title":"Alfred Rosenberg at a press conference, November 1941","desc":"Alfred Rosenberg, Reich Minister for the Occupied Eastern Territories, standing at a press conference dated 19 November 1941, with his permanent deputy Alfred Meyer seated to his right and the press chief Wilhelm Weiss to his left.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-B05926, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B05926,_Pressekonferenz,_A._Meyer,_A._Rosenberg,_W._Weiss.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white photograph of a man in a pinstriped suit standing to speak at a long table, a uniformed man and a man in a dark suit seated either side of him, and large maps pinned to the walls behind."},"wiki":"Reich Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories"},
    "ww2-271": {"questions":["An order to leave the salient came only on 25 June, too late for the Soviet armies trapped in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose front commander was later court-martialled and shot.","German counts put the prisoners taken in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at between 290,000 and over 325,000 when the fighting in its pockets ended on 9 July 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Białystok–Minsk</b> was the first great encirclement of the German invasion of the Soviet Union, fought by Army Group Centre against the Soviet Western Front in the summer of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its plan was a double pincer, with Hoth's panzer group driving east north of the Białystok salient, Guderian's south of it, and infantry marching behind to close an inner ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end of June the two arms had closed one pocket east of Białystok and a larger one around Minsk, holding four Soviet armies with 43 divisions between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Minsk fell on 28 June, when Hoth's tanks broke into the city, and the Soviet news bureau said nothing of its loss that day or after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fighting in the pockets ended on 9 July, and German counts of the prisoners taken run from 290,000 to more than 325,000, with some 2,500 tanks captured or destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Moscow's orders made the trap worse: on the evening of 22 June the fronts were told to attack, and only on 25 June was the Western Front ordered out of the salient, when its armies were already half surrounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Halder noted on 24 June that Hitler feared the ring around Białystok was not tight enough, and on 3 July he judged the enemy there annihilated and wrote that the campaign had probably been won in 2 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The army drew a more cautious lesson, that a ring drawn too wide could not be held tight enough to stop a breakout, and its losses already exceeded those of the whole war before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The front's commander, General Pavlov, was recalled to Moscow with his chief of staff and his political deputy, and all three were court-martialled and shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the front the occupiers set up a ghetto in Minsk in late July, and mass killings of its inmates began in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 44–47, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 27–30, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Kesselschlachten bei Bialystok und Minsk 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-bialystok-1941. [in German] [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Halder War Journal</i>, entries of 24 June and 3 July 1941, trans. Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","Mikhail Yu. Myagkov, “The Catastrophe of 1941 in Belorussia,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/katastrofa-1941-goda-v-belorussii. [in Russian] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Minsk,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/minsk. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Ostfront,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ostfront. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the order to leave the Białystok salient come to nothing?","a":"Because it came too late: on the evening of 22 June the fronts had been told to attack, and by the time the high command ordered the Western Front out of the salient on 25 June its armies were already half surrounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Halder think by 3 July that the campaign was as good as won?","a":"Because the Soviet forces caught in the Białystok pocket looked annihilated to him, and on that basis he wrote that the campaign against the Soviet Union had probably been won in the space of 2 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What lesson did the German army take from the size of the pockets?","a":"That a ring of encirclement drawn too wide could not be held strongly enough to stop a breakout, a worry Hitler had already raised on 24 June when he feared the ring around Białystok was not tight enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1982-077-11,_Russland,_Minsk,_gefangene_sowjetische_Soldaten.jpg","title":"Soviet prisoners in Minsk, July 1941","desc":"Captured Soviet soldiers marched along a street in Minsk under German guard on 2 July 1941, while the pockets west of the city were still being cleared.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1982-077-11, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1982-077-11,_Russland,_Minsk,_gefangene_sowjetische_Soldaten.jpg","alt":"A long column of men in uniform walking along a cobbled street past low wooden houses, a wrecked car in the foreground and a soldier with a rifle walking beside them."},"wiki":"Battle of Białystok–Minsk"},
    "ww2-272": {"questions":["Hitler's Directive 33, issued while the encircled armies were still fighting in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, turned the panzer groups away from Moscow and started a month of argument with his generals.","The American ambassador reported that the evacuation announced after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had finally convinced the people of Moscow how serious the military situation had become."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Smolensk</b> of 1941 was the fighting of July and early August in which Army Group Centre broke the Soviet line on the upper Dnieper and Dvina and encircled large forces around Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two rivers flank the Vitebsk Gate, a gap about 80 kilometres (50 miles) wide that offered the most defensible line west of the capital, and the Soviet command had moved four reserve armies up to the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The panzer groups attacked on 10 July, Hoth's north of the gap and Guderian's south of it, and after heavy street fighting one of Guderian's corps took Smolensk on 16 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pocket east of the city was cleared on 5 August, and the Germans counted more than 300,000 prisoners and some 3,000 tanks captured or destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soviet accounts hold that the 16th and 20th Armies inside the ring got back across the Dnieper with their main forces, helped by counter-attacks such as General Kachalov's from Roslavl, which drew ten German divisions against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The ring did not hold, and on 25 July Keitel told Army Group Centre that air reconnaissance had for days seen Soviet columns escaping through a gap on its eastern side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler had sketched the sequel on 8 July, with Hoth free to help Army Group North once the battle at Smolensk was won, and Directive 33 of 19 July turned both panzer groups to the flanks and left Moscow to the infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His generals pressed for a direct thrust on the capital instead, and through a month of argument Army Group Centre stood on the line it had reached east of Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Moscow the American ambassador reported that the announcement of the city's evacuation had finally convinced the public of the seriousness of the situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The battle also reshaped the Soviet command, for Stalin made himself people's commissar of defence on 19 July, and 2 days later German bombers made their first raid on Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 48–57, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 29–35, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht bei Smolensk 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-smolensk-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 17, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d638. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, July 21, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d629. [Open access]","Roman V. Strelets, “‘Our Fighting These Days Has Utterly Deranged the Enemy Attack’: The Specific Features of Advance on Smolensk by the Operational Group of Lieutenant General V. Ya. Kachalov in July 1941,” <i>Military Historical Journal</i>, no. 8 (2021), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/boyami-etih-dney-my-sovershenno-rasstroili-nastuplenie-protivnika-osobennosti-nastupleniya-operativnoy-gruppy-general-leytenanta-v. [in Russian] [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Halder War Journal</i>, entries of 8 July 1941, trans. Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Smolensk pocket not hold all the Soviet troops inside it?","a":"Because a gap stayed open on its eastern side, through which German air reconnaissance watched Soviet columns escaping for days, and counter-attacks from outside the ring, such as Kachalov's from Roslavl, pinned down German divisions that might have closed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Army Group Centre stop east of Smolensk after the battle?","a":"Because Hitler's Directive 33 sent its panzer groups to the north and south rather than on to Moscow, and the month-long argument with generals who wanted a direct thrust on the capital left it standing on the line it had reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the battle change the way the Soviet war was run?","a":"Stalin took the post of people's commissar of defence on 19 July, putting the war's direction formally in his own hands, while in Moscow the news of Smolensk's evacuation finally convinced the public that the situation was serious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-137-1032-14A,_Russland,_brennendes_Dorf,_deutsche_Kavallerie.jpg","title":"Army Group Centre's advance, 16 July 1941","desc":"German cavalrymen ride through a burning village in the central sector of the front on 16 July 1941, the day Smolensk fell.","credit":"Photograph by Rudolf Kessler, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-137-1032-14A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-137-1032-14A,_Russland,_brennendes_Dorf,_deutsche_Kavallerie.jpg","alt":"Two horsemen riding down a dusty village street between wooden fences while houses burn behind them under thick smoke."},"wiki":"Battle of Smolensk (1941)"},
    "ww2-273": {"questions":["Dozens of Jews were beaten to death before onlookers in the Lietūkis garage yard on 27 June, one of the most notorious scenes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Lithuania's interwar capital.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began on 25 June 1941, the day a German field command was set up in the city that refused to recognise Lithuania's provisional government."],"abstract":"The <b>Kaunas pogrom</b> was the wave of killings of Jews in Kaunas, Lithuania's interwar capital, in the last days of June 1941, in the gap between the Red Army's retreat and the setting up of German rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lithuania had been under Soviet rule since June 1940, and that year of arrests and deportations sharpened an antisemitic myth that blamed the Jews as a whole for communism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As the Soviets withdrew on 23 June, armed Lithuanians calling themselves partisans, many from the Lithuanian Activist Front, began to humiliate, rob, arrest and murder Jews in the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German troops took Kaunas on 24 June and set up a field command the next day, which recognised neither Lithuania's provisional government nor its declaration of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With the arrival of the security police chief Stahlecker and his Einsatzgruppe A the violence became organised, and on 25 and 26 June 'partisans' and students killed more than 800 people in the old Jewish quarter of Slobodka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 27 June Lithuanian, and possibly some German, perpetrators beat dozens of Jews to death in the garage yard of the Lietūkis cooperative in the city centre while a crowd looked on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One Lithuanian historian puts the dead of the pogroms at several thousand, and the Jews murdered in Kaunas before the ghetto was sealed on 15 August at about 8,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 30 June the killing moved to the Seventh Fort, an old fortification under joint German and Lithuanian control, where about 5,000 Jewish men were shot by 7 July, many by men of a new Lithuanian battalion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study of those battalions finds that a sense of duty and fear moved all the men who took part in the shootings, while hatred, cruelty, drink and greed drove only some of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The pogrom thus opened the destruction of Lithuania's Jews, about 250,000 people in 1941, of whom German units and their Lithuanian auxiliaries had killed most by the end of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Holocaust in Kovno,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kovno. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lithuania,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lithuania. [Open access]","Arūnas Bubnys, “The Kaunas Ghetto (1941–1944),” <i>Genocidas ir rezistencija</i> 2, no. 16 (2004): 7–40, https://doi.org/10.61903/GR.2004.201. [in Lithuanian] [Open access]","Alfredas Rukšėnas, “Holocaust in Lithuania: Anti-Jewish Pogroms in Kaunas on 25–29 June, 1941,” <i>Genocidas ir rezistencija</i> 2, no. 50 (2021): 37–79, https://doi.org/10.61903/GR.2021.202. [in Lithuanian] [Open access]","Alfredas Rukšėnas, “Motives of the Soldiers of Kaunas Self-Defence Battalions for Participation in the Killing of Jews and Other Groups of People during the Nazi Occupation,” <i>Genocidas ir rezistencija</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.61903/GR.2011.202. [in Lithuanian] [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Halder War Journal</i>, entries of 24 June 1941, trans. Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the killing start before the Germans took control of Kaunas?","a":"Because the Red Army's withdrawal on 23 June left a vacuum, and armed Lithuanians calling themselves partisans used it to humiliate, rob, arrest and murder Jews before German troops arrived on 24 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What changed when Einsatzgruppe A reached the city?","a":"The violence became organised: with Stahlecker's security police in Kaunas, armed Lithuanian squads carried out the large-scale pogrom in Slobodka on 25 and 26 June, killing more than 800 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the pogrom lead to in Kaunas itself?","a":"To mass shootings at the Seventh Fort, where about 5,000 Jewish men were killed by 7 July, and to a ghetto sealed on 15 August, by which time about 8,000 of the city's Jews may already have been murdered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Paminklas_holokausto_aukoms_prie_Liet%C5%ABkio_gara%C5%BE%C5%B3.JPG","title":"Memorial at the Lietūkis garage, Kaunas","desc":"The stone that marks the site of the Lietūkis garage in Kaunas, where Jews were beaten to death on 27 June 1941, photographed in 2012.","credit":"Photograph by Vilensija, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paminklas_holokausto_aukoms_prie_Liet%C5%ABkio_gara%C5%BE%C5%B3.JPG","alt":"A dark stone slab carved with inscriptions standing among pine branches beside a paved yard, with white flowers and small stones laid at its foot."},"wiki":"Kaunas pogrom"},
    "ww2-274": {"questions":["Hitler's order of 21 August put the Crimea and the Donets ahead of Moscow and led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Germans counted 665,000 prisoners by late September 1941.","A captured Soviet general called the German delay before Moscow caused by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a second 'miracle of the Marne', since 6 weeks had been lost in the centre."],"abstract":"The <b>First Battle of Kiev</b> was the encirclement and destruction of the Soviet Southwestern Front east of the Ukrainian capital in September 1941, by Army Group South and forces turned south from Army Group Centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By early August the German advances had wrapped halfway round the Soviet armies at Kiev, and the American ambassador in Moscow saw that a drive through Gomel aimed to cut the city off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stalin would not give Kiev up, removing Zhukov as chief of the General Staff when he proposed it, and on 11 September he refused Budenny's request to withdraw and replaced him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guderian's panzer group struck south from 25 August and Kleist's north from a bridgehead at Kremenchug, and they met at Lokhvitsa in mid-September, with German forces across the Dnieper in strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kiev fell on 19 September and the pocket was cleared by the 26th; the Germans counted 665,000 prisoners, a Soviet history gives the front 677,085 men at the start and 150,541 at the end, and a Russian survey puts the September captives at about 450,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The battle grew out of Hitler's order of 21 August, which declared that the capture of Moscow was not the most important objective and put the Crimea, the Donets Basin and the encirclement of Leningrad first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His generals had wanted a direct thrust on the capital, and Guderian failed in a last attempt to change his mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cost was time, for six precious weeks had been lost in the centre and Guderian's group had to march 640 kilometres (400 miles) before it could rejoin Army Group Centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Soviet general captured in 1942 told the Germans that the Soviet command had seen their failure to press on to Moscow that autumn as a second 'miracle of the Marne'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the captured city, after Soviet mines wrecked the German headquarters and the main street, SS and police units shot 33,771 Jews at Babyn Yar on 29 and 30 September, by their own report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 65–77, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 32–34, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht bei Kiew 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-kiew-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, August 23, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d639. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, September 13, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d202. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings at Babyn Yar (Babi Yar),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kiev-and-babi-yar. [Open access]","Mikhail Yu. Myagkov, “The Catastrophe of 1941 in Belorussia,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/katastrofa-1941-goda-v-belorussii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many Soviet soldiers trapped east of Kiev?","a":"Because Stalin refused to give the city up, removing Zhukov when he proposed abandoning it and refusing Budenny's request to withdraw on 11 September, so the armies stayed in place while the German pincers closed behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler send Guderian south instead of towards Moscow?","a":"Because his order of 21 August held that Moscow was not the most important objective and gave priority to the Crimea, the Donets Basin and the encirclement of Leningrad, overruling generals who wanted a direct thrust on the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a German victory look like a Soviet reprieve?","a":"Because it cost the Germans 6 weeks in the centre and a 640-kilometre march for Guderian's group, a delay that a captured Soviet general said his commanders saw as a second 'miracle of the Marne'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B12190,_Kiew,_Brand_in_der_Hauptstra%C3%9Fe.jpg","title":"Kiev's main street on fire, September 1941","desc":"Smoke and rubble on the main street of Kiev in September 1941; in the week after the city fell, mines left by retreating Soviet forces wrecked much of the centre.","credit":"Photograph by Kraayvanger, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-B12190, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B12190,_Kiew,_Brand_in_der_Hauptstra%C3%9Fe.jpg","alt":"A wide city street filled with billowing smoke and debris, tall buildings on either side and tram wires strung overhead."},"wiki":"Battle of Kiev (1941)"},
    "ww2-275": {"questions":["Cut off in Courland when Soviet armies reached the coast in October 1944, Germany's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was renamed after that Latvian province in January 1945.","Hitler forbade Leeb's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to storm Leningrad once Schlüsselburg had fallen, because he wanted to avoid street fighting and the burden of feeding the city's millions."],"abstract":"<b>Army Group North</b> was the German army group that invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941 under Field Marshal von Leeb, with the capture of Leningrad as its strategic objective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It advanced with the Sixteenth and Eighteenth Armies and the Fourth Panzer Group, and within 9 days it had crossed the Dvina at Dvinsk and Jēkabpils while Soviet forces in Lithuania and Latvia fought delaying actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From September 1941 it held the siege lines round Leningrad, until an offensive that the Leningrad and Volkhov Fronts opened on 14 January 1944 drove it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Driven back through the Baltic states, it was cut off in Courland, the westernmost province of Latvia, when Soviet armies reached the sea south of Memel in October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 26 January 1945 it was renamed Army Group Courland, and its old name passed to what had been Army Group Centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In July 1941 its panzer corps split, one making for Novgorod and the other going round Lake Peipus towards Narva, because the direct road through Luga crossed ground unsuited to tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Halder planned on 3 July for the advance to cut Leningrad off between the Gulf of Finland and Lake Ladoga, and 5 days later Hitler declared his firm decision to level Leningrad and Moscow so as not to have to feed their people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pause before the Luga gave the defenders time to build a line there with civilian labour, and the Germans broke through on its flank near Shimsk only on 15 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The army group took Schlüsselburg on Lake Ladoga on 8 September, cutting Leningrad's last land link, but Hitler forbade an assault on the city to avoid street fighting and the burden of feeding its millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its drive to join the Finns east of the lake reached Tikhvin in November, but no gains could be made beyond it, and the army group settled into the siege it would hold until January 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 44–58, 74–87, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 32–45, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Halder War Journal</i>, entries of 3 July and 8 July 1941, trans. Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","Natalya Yu. Shpirina, “How Saint Petersburg Found Itself in the Siege: Forming of the Outer Front of the Blockade in the Battle for Leningrad,” <i>Izvestia of the Herzen Russian State Pedagogical University</i>, no. 110 (2009), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/kak-leningrad-okazalsya-v-blokade-formirovanie-vneshnego-fronta-blokady-v-bitve-za-leningrad-iyul-sentyabr-1941-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikita A. Lomagin, “‘To Comrade Stalin, People’s Commissar of Defense’: Reports by K. Voroshilov, Commander-in-Chief of Leningrad Front,” <i>Modern History of Russia</i> 9, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/narodnomu-komissaru-oborony-tovarischu-stalinu-doneseniya-komanduyuschego-leningradskim-frontom-k-e-voroshilova. [in Russian] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 251–53, 407–9, 426, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the panzer corps heading for Leningrad split up in July?","a":"Because the direct road to Leningrad through Luga crossed ground unsuited to tanks, so one corps went round Lake Peipus towards Narva while the other made for Novgorod, against the army high command's wish to keep them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the army group not storm Leningrad after taking Schlüsselburg?","a":"Because Hitler forbade it, wanting to avoid the heavy casualties of street fighting and to escape any responsibility for feeding the city's millions, in line with his July decision to level the city rather than feed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the delay before the Luga help the defenders?","a":"It gave Leningrad weeks in which to build a defensive line on the Luga with civilian labour, so that the Germans broke through on its flank near Shimsk only in mid-August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-212-0214-08A,_Russland-Nord,_v._Leeb_u.a._beim_Kartenstudium.jpg","title":"Leeb and Hoepner at the map table, September 1941","desc":"Field Marshal von Leeb, second from left, and General Hoepner of the Fourth Panzer Group, third from left, studying a map on the northern front in September 1941.","credit":"Photograph by Hansen, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-212-0214-08A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-212-0214-08A,_Russland-Nord,_v._Leeb_u.a._beim_Kartenstudium.jpg","alt":"Four officers in grey uniforms lean over a large map spread across a table in a bright room, one tracing a line with his finger."},"wiki":"Army Group North"},
    "ww2-276": {"questions":["Begun when Schlüsselburg fell in September 1941 and ended on 27 January 1944, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted close to 900 days and killed hundreds of thousands of civilians.","Hitler forbade storming the city during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because he wished to avoid street fighting and refused responsibility for feeding its millions of inhabitants."],"abstract":"The <b>Siege of Leningrad</b> was the blockade of the Soviet Union's second city, which German troops cut off from all its land links in early September 1941 by taking Schlüsselburg on Lake Ladoga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German divisions closed the ring from the south and east while Finnish troops held the north, and by 13 September a British general in Moscow believed the city completely surrounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For close to 900 days the city was shelled and bombed almost without pause, defended by the Red Army behind fortifications that nearly half a million inhabitants helped to build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of a population of about 2.9 million, including half a million children, some 630,000 died of hunger-related causes by one count, most in the winter of 1941–42, while the German Historical Museum puts the dead at between 800,000 and a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The blockade was ended only by the Red Army's winter offensive of 1943–44, on 27 January 1944, and Leningrad became a 'hero city', a symbol of Soviet determination to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The siege was a choice, for Hitler had told his generals on 8 July that he meant to level Leningrad and Moscow so as not to have to feed their populations through the winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the ring closed he expressly forbade a direct attack, wishing to avoid the losses of house-to-house fighting and refusing responsibility for feeding the city's millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A naval staff directive of 22 September stated that the Führer had decided to wipe Petersburg from the face of the earth, and an order signed by Jodl on 7 October ruled that a surrender was not to be accepted even if offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same order said that no German soldier was to enter the city, and that fire was to force back anyone who tried to leave it through the German lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Finns, whose staff said in September that their troops would not join the siege, stopped near the old border on the Karelian Isthmus, about 16 kilometres (10 miles) north of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 74–78, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 35, 40, 135–37, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 8, proceedings of 22 February 1946 (Nuremberg, 1947), 113, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-22-46.asp. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Halder War Journal</i>, entries of 8 July 1941, trans. Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Minister in Finland (Schoenfeld) to the Secretary of State, September 9, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d77. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, September 13, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d202. [Open access]","Pär Sparén et al., “Long Term Mortality after Severe Starvation during the Siege of Leningrad: Prospective Cohort Study,” <i>BMJ</i> 328, no. 7430 (2004): 11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC313894/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans besiege Leningrad rather than storm it?","a":"Because Hitler wanted the city destroyed without paying for it: he forbade a direct attack to avoid the losses of house-to-house fighting, and refused to take on the burden of feeding its millions, having already said in July that he meant to level it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were civilians not allowed to escape through the German lines?","a":"Because the order of 7 October ruled out any surrender and told the troops to force back with fire anyone trying to leave the city through their lines, so that the population stayed inside the ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Finns not close in on the city from the north?","a":"Their staff told the American military attaché in September that Finnish troops would not take part in the siege, and they stopped near the old frontier on the Karelian Isthmus about 16 kilometres north of Leningrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Anti_aircraft_Leningrad_1941.JPG","title":"Anti-aircraft guns by Saint Isaac's, November 1941","desc":"Anti-aircraft guns firing beside Saint Isaac's Cathedral in Leningrad on 24 November 1941, in the first winter of the siege.","credit":"Photograph by Boris Kudoyarov, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anti_aircraft_Leningrad_1941.JPG","alt":"A gun crew crouched beside a tall barrel pointed at the sky, white bursts of smoke around them and the dark dome of a cathedral behind."},"wiki":"Siege of Leningrad"},
    "ww2-277": {"questions":["Some 3,000 tonnes of flour and 2,500 tonnes of sugar burned in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the city's single food depot, on the day the German ring around Leningrad closed.","Party leaders later blamed the fire at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the hunger of 1941–42, though the famine owed far more to the trickle of supplies reaching the blockaded city that autumn."],"abstract":"The <b>Badayev warehouses</b> were the single food depot of Leningrad, the Soviet Union's second city, and their burning in a German air raid on 8 September 1941 became the symbol of the famine that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that day, as the ring round the city closed, German aircraft dropped more than 6,300 incendiary bombs and started 178 or 183 fires by different official counts, the largest of them at the warehouses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 40 storerooms burned with some 3,000 tonnes of flour and 2,500 tonnes of sugar, and up to 1,000 tonnes of burnt flour and 900 tonnes of burnt sugar were later reprocessed for food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Party leaders afterwards blamed the fire for the food catastrophe, and in the minds of Leningraders it marked the beginning of the hunger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet the warehouses held only a few days' food at pre-war rations, since the city had lived from daily deliveries, and the stocks that burned amounted to 1 to 3 days' supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The famine came from the blockade itself: in the first 20 days of October only 3,650 tonnes of flour reached the city against the 13,713 tonnes needed, and by mid-December even the oil cake, bran and mill dust were gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The bread ration was cut again and again, and from 20 November it stood at its lowest, 250 grams a day for manual workers and 125 grams for everyone else, about 460 calories if received in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People ate cats, dogs and rats, bark and sawdust, and in frosts of −40 °C (−40 °F) the bodies of the dead piled up at the edges of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deaths recorded by the security police rose from 9,183 in November to 52,612 in December and 96,751 in January 1942, against about 3,500 a month before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Leningrad archpriest told the Nuremberg tribunal that burials at his cemetery church reached several thousand a day, and that on 7 February 1942 piles of bodies surrounded the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yulia Z. Kantor, “‘The Streets Became Avenues of the Dead’: To the Issue of Mortality in Leningrad in the First Half of the Siege (According to the Documents of the FSB Archive for St Petersburg and Leningrad Region),” <i>Petersburg Historical Journal</i>, no. 4 (20) (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ulitsy-stali-prospektami-mertvyh-k-voprosu-o-smertnosti-v-leningrade-v-pervoe-polugodie-blokady-po-dokumentam-arhiva-ufsb-po-spb-i-lo. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikita A. Lomagin, “‘To Comrade Stalin, People’s Commissar of Defense’: Reports by K. Voroshilov, Commander-in-Chief of Leningrad Front,” <i>Modern History of Russia</i> 9, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/narodnomu-komissaru-oborony-tovarischu-stalinu-doneseniya-komanduyuschego-leningradskim-frontom-k-e-voroshilova. [in Russian] [Open access]","Pär Sparén et al., “Long Term Mortality after Severe Starvation during the Siege of Leningrad: Prospective Cohort Study,” <i>BMJ</i> 328, no. 7430 (2004): 11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC313894/. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 8, proceedings of 27 February 1946 (Nuremberg, 1947), 333–35, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-27-46.asp. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 135–37, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fire at the warehouses become the symbol of the famine?","a":"Because it happened on the day the ring closed and was the largest of the fires started in that raid, so Leningraders came to see it as the start of the hunger, and party leaders afterwards blamed it for the food catastrophe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the fire not have caused the famine on its own?","a":"Because the warehouses held only a few days' food at pre-war rations, the city having lived from daily deliveries; the flour and sugar that burned came to 1 to 3 days' supply, and some of it was reprocessed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What actually drove the death toll up that winter?","a":"The blockade's starvation of supply: far less flour arrived than was needed, the stocks ran out in mid-December, and from 20 November most people received 125 grams of bread a day, so recorded deaths rose to 96,751 in January 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/57/RIAN_archive_216_The_Volkovo_cemetery.jpg","title":"Burying the dead at the Volkovo cemetery","desc":"Three men burying victims of the siege at the Volkovo cemetery in Leningrad; the RIA Novosti archive dates the photograph to 1 October 1942.","credit":"Photograph by Boris Kudoyarov, RIA Novosti archive, image 216, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_216_The_Volkovo_cemetery.jpg","alt":"Bodies wrapped in cloth lie on wet ground in the foreground while three men in coats stand beside a handcart, a fence and barrage balloons behind them."},"wiki":"Siege of Leningrad"},
    "ww2-278": {"questions":["German aircraft and artillery struck at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the ice in the winter of 1941–42 but could not stop its traffic of food, troops and evacuees.","In the 1942 navigation season boats and barges of the Ladoga flotilla carried some 503,000 people out of Leningrad along the summer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, across the lake to the east."],"abstract":"The <b>Road of Life</b> was the route across Lake Ladoga that linked besieged Leningrad with the rest of the Soviet Union, by boat in the navigation season and over the ice in winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Once Schlüsselburg fell in September 1941 every land route into the city was cut, and apart from a small airlift the lake was its only link with the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In winter it ran as Military Automobile Road 101, whose details were kept secret, with walkers and carts due to start across the ice on 22 November 1941 and motor traffic on 25 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By February 1942 the tracks laid on the ice, dozens of parallel roads joined by crossings, ran to 1,770 kilometres (1,100 miles) in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the 1942 navigation season the Ladoga flotilla carried about 1,008,000 tonnes of cargo, 775,000 of them into Leningrad, and some 789,000 passengers, 503,000 of them out of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The first weeks on the ice were perilous, for a 2-ton truck could carry only 90 to 135 kilograms (200 to 300 pounds) of extra load, and many broke through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While the Germans held the railway through Tikhvin, which they took in November, supplies had to reach the shore by a long road detour, and after its recapture the first train passed through only on 1 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city needed 1,000 tonnes of provisions a day, but even a good day's haul in December fell short of that, a third of it inedible, and its own stocks had run out in mid-December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Luftwaffe was ordered to strike the ice road but could spare few aircraft, though raids on 28 and 29 December killed or wounded about 200 people, and German artillery shelled it without stopping the traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the same ice hundreds of thousands were evacuated, and from 22 January to 15 April 1942 a column of 80 Leningrad and Moscow buses carried people along a 55-kilometre (34-mile) stretch of the route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 35, 135–37, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","United States Department of the Army, <i>Effects of Climate on Combat in European Russia</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-291 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1952), 17, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-104-6-effects-of-climate-on-combat-in-european-russia-1952. [Open access]","Vladimir L. Pyankevich and Aleksandr N. Chistikov, “Along the Lake on Foot: The Evacuation of the Population from Leningrad in Late November and Early December, 1941,” <i>Modern History of Russia</i> 9, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/peshkom-po-ozeru-evakuatsiya-naseleniya-iz-leningrada-v-kontse-noyabrya-nachale-dekabrya-1941-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vyacheslav A. Mosunov, “Enemy Air Raids on the ‘Road of Life’ and Planning the Operations to Destroy It in November 1941 – January 1942,” <i>Petersburg Historical Journal</i>, no. 3 (27) (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/udary-aviatsii-protivnika-po-doroge-zhizni-i-planirovanie-operatsiy-po-ee-unichtozheniyu-v-noyabre-1941-yanvare-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Pavel S. Chekalev-Demidovsky, “The Road of Life, or the Ice Road Winter Transport Route: Evacuating People from Leningrad, the Winter of 1941–1942,” <i>Bulletin of Ryazan State University</i>, no. 4 (65) (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/avtobusnye-kolonny-na-ledovoy-trasse-evakuatsiya-naseleniya-leningrada-zimoy-1941-1942-godov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]","Sergei V. Fedulov, Natalya N. Mizirkina and Vladimir V. Sokolov, “The Summer Road of Life of Blockaded Leningrad, 1942,” <i>Military Historical Journal</i>, no. 8 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/letnyaya-doroga-zhizni-blokadnogo-leningrada-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Nikita D. Prigodich, “The Food Supply of Besieged Leningrad and the Evacuation of the Population by Using Aviation in the Autumn of 1941,” <i>RUDN Journal of Russian History</i> 19, no. 2 (2020): 320–29, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8674-2020-19-2-320-329. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the ice road so dangerous in its first weeks?","a":"Because the ice was still thin: a 2-ton truck could carry only a few hundred pounds of extra load, and many broke through, while German aircraft and artillery attacked the route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fall of Tikhvin make supply across the lake harder?","a":"Because it put the railway through Tikhvin in German hands, so supplies had to reach the shore by a long road detour, and even after its recapture the first train passed through only on 1 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the road not end the hunger in the first winter?","a":"Because it never brought the 1,000 tonnes of provisions the city needed each day, a third of what came was inedible, and Leningrad's own stocks had already run out in mid-December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/LeningradIceRoad.jpg","title":"The ice road from the air","desc":"A German reconnaissance photograph of a section of the ice road across Lake Ladoga, about 60 kilometres (37 miles) east of Leningrad, taken in one of the siege winters.","credit":"Unknown Luftwaffe photographer, public domain in the United States, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LeningradIceRoad.jpg","alt":"A grainy aerial photograph of a white frozen surface crossed diagonally by a thin straight track, with handwritten German labels and arrows."},"wiki":"Road of Life"},
    "ww2-279": {"questions":["The Reich press chief declared the eastern campaign decided in October 1941, but mud and then frost brought <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a halt short of Moscow by 5 December.","The German spearhead stopped at Krasnaya Polyana in early December, and frostbite casualties outnumbered battle casualties as the offensive codenamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to an end."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Typhoon</b> was the German offensive against Moscow in the autumn of 1941, ordered by Hitler's Directive 35 of 6 September and carried out by Army Group Centre under Field Marshal von Bock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It opened with a double envelopment, Guderian's armour attacking on 30 September and the other two panzer groups on 2 October, and within a week six Soviet armies were trapped west of Vyazma and most of the Bryansk Front near Bryansk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Germans counted 663,000 prisoners from Vyazma and about 100,000 from Bryansk, Halder called the performance 'downright classical', and a Russian survey puts the Soviet captives of both battles at about 600,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Then on 7 October the autumn rains began, and the advance became a struggle through mud in which motorised units often needed an hour to cover a single kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The attack resumed on 15 November once the ground froze, but by 5 December it had ground to a halt, its spearhead stopped at Krasnaya Polyana about 40 kilometres (25 miles) from Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> German confidence peaked in early October, when Hitler opened the offensive with an appeal announcing the decisive battle and the Reich press chief told foreign journalists that the eastern campaign was decided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Muscovites learned of the danger only on 8 October, from a communiqué about intense battles near Vyazma and Bryansk, and the American ambassador reported the news as a great shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the mud Soviet T-34 tanks, whose wide tracks kept them afloat, could block a column until heavy guns came up, and by one German account only one tank in ten came through the muddy season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Before the final push the high command weighed exhausted troops, a supply system that could bring forward only the most urgent rations, ammunition and fuel, and no winter clothing before January, and chose to risk it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When it stopped, frostbite casualties outnumbered those from enemy action, and on 6 December, as fresh Soviet troops attacked, the first German retreat order of the war was given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 75–90, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 34–46, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Doppelschlacht bei Wjasma und Brjansk 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-wjasma-1941. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Angriff auf Moskau 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, October 11, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d864. [Open access]","United States Department of the Army, <i>Effects of Climate on Combat in European Russia</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-291 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1952), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-104-6-effects-of-climate-on-combat-in-european-russia-1952. [Open access]","Mikhail Yu. Myagkov, “The Catastrophe of 1941 in Belorussia,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 3 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/katastrofa-1941-goda-v-belorussii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans think Moscow was theirs in early October?","a":"Because the double envelopment at Vyazma and Bryansk had trapped several Soviet armies in a week and yielded hundreds of thousands of prisoners, so Hitler announced the decisive battle and his press chief called the campaign decided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the mud matter so much to the German advance?","a":"Because it slowed motorised units to an hour a kilometre and favoured the Soviet T-34s, whose wide tracks kept them afloat, while by one German account only one tank in ten survived the muddy season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the final push of November fail?","a":"Because it was launched by exhausted troops on a supply system that could bring forward only the most urgent rations, ammunition and fuel and no winter clothing, so frostbite cost more men than the enemy and the attack stalled short of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-2008-0317,_Russland,_Bergung_eines_Verwundeten.jpg","title":"Before Moscow, November 1941","desc":"German soldiers tend a wounded comrade in the snow before Moscow in November 1941, with houses burning in a village behind them.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-2008-0317, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-2008-0317,_Russland,_Bergung_eines_Verwundeten.jpg","alt":"Two soldiers in steel helmets kneel over a man lying in a snowy field while other soldiers walk past and smoke rises from houses in the distance."},"wiki":"Battle of Moscow"},
    "ww2-280": {"questions":["Stalin reviewed troops on Red Square on 7 November 1941 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and many of the soldiers marched from the parade straight to the front line.","Civilians, mostly women, dug three semicircular defence lines round the capital during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while workers' militia battalions prepared to man them against Army Group Centre."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Moscow</b> was the struggle for the Soviet capital from October 1941 into the winter, in which the German attack on the city was stopped and then thrown back by a Soviet counter-offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler had decided in July to level Moscow rather than feed its people, and an order of 12 October forbade German soldiers to enter the city or to accept its surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> German bombers had raided the city since the night of 21 July, but by November seven rings of anti-aircraft guns made it almost invulnerable from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German armour came closest in early December, when one panzer group was stopped at Krasnaya Polyana, about 40 kilometres (25 miles) out, and a panzer division stood some 14 kilometres (9 miles) from the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the first week of December the Red Army opened a counter-offensive that drove the Germans back in the winter battles of 1941–42.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The crisis inside the city came in mid-October, when news of the German advance to Vyazma came as a great shock to Muscovites and on 14 October German troops broke through the Mozhaysk line and took Kalinin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 15 October Molotov told the British and American ambassadors that the government and the diplomatic corps must leave that day for Kuibyshev, assuring them that the fight for Moscow would continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The loss of Kalinin set off panic and looting, and on 19 October the State Defence Committee put Moscow under a state of siege, while Stalin stayed in the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Civilians, mostly women, dug three semicircular defence lines round the capital and workers' militia battalions prepared to man them, while Zhukov used the pause forced by the mud to strengthen the defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 7 November, the anniversary of the revolution, Stalin reviewed troops on Red Square, most of whom went straight on to the front, calling on them to follow Russia's national heroes from Alexander Nevsky to Kutuzov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, October 15, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d865. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, October 11, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d864. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, July 21, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d629. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 37–42, https://archive.org/details/MoscowToStalingradDecisionInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Angriff auf Moskau 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941. [in German] [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 81–88, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","United States Department of the Army, <i>Effects of Climate on Combat in European Russia</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-291 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1952), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-104-6-effects-of-climate-on-combat-in-european-russia-1952. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Halder War Journal</i>, entries of 8 July 1941, trans. Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet government leave Moscow in mid-October?","a":"Because on 14 October the Germans broke through the Mozhaysk line and took Kalinin, and it looked as though their advance might not be checked, so Molotov sent the government and the diplomatic corps to Kuibyshev the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin stage a parade on Red Square on 7 November?","a":"To mark the anniversary of the revolution in a city under threat, sending the troops straight from the square to the front and calling on them to follow Russia's national heroes from Alexander Nevsky to Kutuzov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans not plan to take the city by storm?","a":"Because Hitler meant to level Moscow rather than feed its people, and his order of 12 October forbade German soldiers to enter the city or to accept its surrender even if it were offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/RIAN_archive_604273_Barricades_on_city_streets.jpg","title":"Barricades in a Moscow street, October 1941","desc":"Anti-tank obstacles of crossed steel beams and a wall of timber across a Moscow street in October 1941, the month the city was put under a state of siege.","credit":"Photograph by Arkady Shaikhet, RIA Novosti archive, image 604273, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_604273_Barricades_on_city_streets.jpg","alt":"Rows of crossed steel girders and a wall of upright timber blocks stretched across a wet city street in front of a large apartment building."},"wiki":"Battle of Moscow"},
    "ww2-221": {"questions":["Some 2,937 airmen from fifteen countries, Poles, New Zealanders and Canadians among them, qualified for the clasp that marks them out as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the summer of 1940.","Fighter Command began July 1940 197 pilots short of establishment, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who held the line lost 449 aircrew killed in battle by the end of October."],"abstract":"The <b>Few</b> is the name given to the airmen of RAF Fighter Command who fought the Battle of Britain in 1940, taken from Winston Churchill's speech to the House of Commons on 20 August that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speaking at the height of the battle, he said the gratitude of every home went out to the British airmen turning the tide of world war, for never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His very next sentence turned from the fighter pilots to the bomber crews flying night after night into Germany, so the tribute was wider at first than the name it later gave to Fighter Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Air Ministry's official account of the battle opened with that scene in a crowded House and quoted the words in full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The phrase travelled further still, and a Polish writer's wartime book about No. 303 Squadron quoted it before adding that they were few indeed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Membership of the Few is defined by a clasp, awarded to 2,937 British and Allied airmen who flew at least one authorised sortie with an accredited unit of Fighter Command between 10 July and 31 October 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They came from fifteen countries, among them 2,342 from Great Britain, 145 from Poland, 127 from New Zealand, 112 from Canada and 88 from Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Numbers were the command's weakness from the start, for it was 197 pilots short of establishment on 8 July, and between 8 and 18 August another 94 pilots were killed or missing and 60 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly 300 fighter pilots had been lost over France and the Low Countries, and the gap was patched with 58 Fleet Air Arm pilots and new Polish and Czechoslovak squadrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official history counts 449 of its aircrew killed in battle, while claims ran ahead of the truth, as on 18 August, when 125 German aircraft were recorded destroyed against an actual loss of about 69.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 20 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1166–67, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/20/war-situation. [Open access]","Air Ministry, <i>The Battle of Britain, August–October 1940: An Air Ministry Account of the Great Days from 8th August–31st October 1940</i> (London: HMSO for the Ministry of Information, 1942), https://archive.org/details/ministry-of-information-the-battle-of-britain-1941. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 200, 493, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/index.html. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 156, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Battle of Britain London Monument, “The Airmen,” https://bbm.org.uk/the-airmen/. [Open access]","Royal Air Force Museum, “Poles and Czechoslovaks in the Battle of Britain,” RAF Museum blog, 21 September 2020, https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/blog/poles-and-czechoslovaks-in-the-battle-of-britain/. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), “The Battle of Britain's Hardest Day,” https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/explore-by-time-period/second-world-war/the-battle-of-britains-hardest-day/. [Open access]","Arkady Fiedler, <i>Squadron 303: The Story of the Polish Fighter Squadron with the R.A.F.</i> (New York: Roy, 1943), 23–25, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.5670. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the name end up meaning Fighter Command's airmen alone?","a":"Because Churchill spoke at the height of the daylight battle, and the Air Ministry's official account of the battle opened with the scene in the Commons and quoted the line in full, which tied it to the fighter fight. Yet his very next sentence had praised the bomber crews flying night after night into Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were so many of the Few not British?","a":"Because Fighter Command was short of pilots from the start, 197 below establishment in July, after losing nearly 300 fighter pilots over France. Polish and Czechoslovak squadrons and Fleet Air Arm pilots helped fill the gap, and the clasp list counts men from fifteen countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are wartime victory claims treated with caution?","a":"Because claims made in combat ran well ahead of real losses. On 18 August Fighter Command recorded 125 German aircraft destroyed, but the actual German loss that day was about 69.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Dowding_and_The_Few.jpg","title":"Dowding with Battle of Britain pilots, September 1942","desc":"Sir Hugh Dowding, in civilian clothes, with RAF pilots of the Battle of Britain and a woman officer outside the Air Ministry in London at the battle's second anniversary in September 1942.","credit":"Photographer not credited, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dowding_and_The_Few.jpg","alt":"A row of young men in RAF uniforms and caps with an older man in a dark suit and bowler hat and a woman in uniform, standing in front of a stone building."},"wiki":"The Few"},
    "ww2-222": {"questions":["A Czech sergeant flying as a guest of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Josef František, was credited with 17 victories before he died in a flying accident on 8 October 1940.","Wartime accounts credited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with 126 German aircraft in 42 days of the 1940 battle, a figure later revised downwards to about 79."],"abstract":"<b>No. 303 Squadron</b> was a Polish fighter squadron of the Royal Air Force, one of four squadrons, with Nos. 302, 310 and 312, flown in the Battle of Britain by airmen of the defeated Polish and Czechoslovak air forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By August 1940 some 8,400 Polish airmen were in Britain, many having escaped Poland in 1939 and France in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dowding at first doubted their morale and preferred national squadrons, and the Air Ministry agreed to form one Czechoslovak and three Polish squadrons at once, though at half the usual strength in pilots and aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Allied Forces Act of August 1940 gave the forces of the exiled governments legal standing in Britain, although in practice the Poles stayed under RAF operational control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each had an RAF commander and British flight commanders with Polish understudies, and 303's Hurricanes had their guns set to converge at about 180 metres (200 yards), half the standard distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 30 August, during a training flight near Northolt, Pilot Officer Ludwik Paszkiewicz broke formation to shoot down a Messerschmitt Bf 110, and the squadron was declared operational the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the 6 weeks that followed it claimed 126 enemy aircraft, the highest score of any unit in Fighter Command, though the figure has since been revised to about 79.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Polish account published in 1943 divided the 126 into 93 for the Poles, 16 for the three British members and 17 for its Czech guest Josef František, killed in a flying accident on 8 October, and dated the squadron's battle to the 43 days from 30 August to 11 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The 145 Polish pilots were only about five per cent of Fighter Command's strength, but they came in the critical weeks from late August when pilots were scarcest, and 29 were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 15 September one pilot in five in action was Polish, and Dowding later wrote that without the Polish squadrons he hesitated to say the outcome of the battle would have been the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Royal Air Force Museum, “Poles and Czechoslovaks in the Battle of Britain,” RAF Museum blog, 21 September 2020, https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/blog/poles-and-czechoslovaks-in-the-battle-of-britain/. [Open access]","Kris Hendrix, “Polish No. 303 Squadron and the Hurricane 80K,” RAF Museum blog, 3 June 2020, https://www.rafmuseum.org.uk/blog/polish-no-303-squadron-and-the-hurricane-80k/. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 156, 183, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 200, 493, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/index.html. [Open access]","Arkady Fiedler, <i>Squadron 303: The Story of the Polish Fighter Squadron with the R.A.F.</i> (New York: Roy, 1943), 23–25, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.5670. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Allied Forces Bill [Lords],” <i>Hansard</i>, 21 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1350–90, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/21/allied-forces-bill-lords. [Open access]","Battle of Britain London Monument, “The Airmen,” https://bbm.org.uk/the-airmen/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Poles first kept in squadrons of their own?","a":"Because Dowding doubted the morale of airmen who had been defeated twice, in Poland and in France, and preferred to keep them together in national units. The Air Ministry formed the new squadrons at once but at half the usual strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the squadron's score changed since 1940?","a":"Because the wartime total of 126 victories, the highest in Fighter Command, rested on claims made at the time, and it has since been revised downwards to about 79.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Polish pilots matter more than their numbers suggest?","a":"Because they arrived in the critical weeks from late August, when Fighter Command was shortest of pilots. Though only about five per cent of its strength, on 15 September one pilot in five in action was Polish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Pilots_of_No._303_%28Polish%29_Squadron_RAF_with_one_of_their_Hawker_Hurricanes%2C_October_1940._CH1535.jpg/1280px-Pilots_of_No._303_%28Polish%29_Squadron_RAF_with_one_of_their_Hawker_Hurricanes%2C_October_1940._CH1535.jpg","title":"Pilots of No. 303 Squadron, October 1940","desc":"Polish pilots of the squadron with their Canadian flight commander, John Kent, walking away from a Hawker Hurricane in October 1940, photographed for the Air Ministry.","credit":"Photograph by Stanley Arthur Devon, Air Ministry official photographer, Imperial War Museums CH 1535, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pilots_of_No._303_(Polish)_Squadron_RAF_with_one_of_their_Hawker_Hurricanes,_October_1940._CH1535.jpg","alt":"A group of airmen in flying jackets and life vests walk across grass towards the camera, smiling, with a fighter aircraft parked behind them."},"wiki":"No. 303 Squadron RAF"},
    "ww2-223": {"questions":["A shelter census taken in London in November 1940 found only 40 per cent of people in any official shelter during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of the rest staying in their own homes.","The first 2 months of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, September and October 1940, brought 27,091 civilians killed and wounded, the highest toll of any period of the war in Britain."],"abstract":"The <b>Blitz</b> was the German bombing offensive against Britain's cities, mostly by night, that opened with the attack on London on 7 September 1940 and ran into May 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew from the Luftwaffe's lesson that mass raids by day were costly while big night raids could be made at little cost, and in September alone about 5,300 tons of high explosive were aimed at London on 24 nights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From then until the middle of November London was attacked by an average of about 160 bombers a night, with a single night's respite on 3 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 14 November, beginning with Coventry, the bombers turned to industrial cities and ports, among them Birmingham, Liverpool, Glasgow, Plymouth, Bristol, Southampton, Hull and Belfast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Night fighters, airborne radar and gun-laying sets were still arriving for most of the period, so the defences brought down few bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A month into the raids Churchill told the Commons that the killed and seriously wounded had fallen week by week from over 6,000 in the first week to under 3,000 in the fourth, and he credited the shelters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The first 2 months were nonetheless the worst of the whole war for civilian casualties, with 27,091 people killed and wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Most Londoners did not go to public shelters, for a census in early November 1940 found 9 per cent in public shelters, 4 per cent in the Tubes and 27 per cent in household shelters, so most stayed in their own homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Tube stations, at first closed to shelterers, were opened under the weight of numbers, and in November the government authorised eight deep shelters, which were not ready until after the Blitz had ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Over the whole war bombing killed 51,509 civilians in the United Kingdom, and the official verdict was that the night offensive failed to halt production or to break the national will to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 251–58, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-16.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 260–81, 528, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-17.html. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 210, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 392, 508, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","Sarah Castagnetti, “The Bethnal Green Tube Shelter Disaster,” The National Archives (UK), https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/the-bethnal-green-tube-shelter-disaster/. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>Front Line 1940–41: The Official Story of the Civil Defence of Britain</i> (London: HMSO, 1942), 67–68, https://archive.org/details/frontline1940. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “The Psychology of Protecting the UK Public against External Threat: COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 7, no. 11 (2020): 991–96, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7834303/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 290–91, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Luftwaffe bomb mostly at night?","a":"Because it had learnt that mass attacks in daylight brought heavy losses, while big night raids could be made at little cost. In September it aimed about 5,300 tons of high explosive at London on 24 nights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were so few bombers shot down?","a":"Because the tools of night defence were still arriving. Night fighters, airborne radar and gun-laying sets were scarce or troublesome for most of the Blitz, so the guns and fighters could do little against bombers they could not see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did casualties fall after the first weeks of raids?","a":"Churchill told the Commons that weekly casualties fell from over 6,000 to under 3,000 in the first month, and he gave the credit to the shelters. Even so, September and October 1940 remained the worst months of the war for civilian casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Heinkel_He_111_over_Wapping%2C_East_London.jpg/1280px-Heinkel_He_111_over_Wapping%2C_East_London.jpg","title":"A Heinkel He 111 over Wapping, 7 September 1940","desc":"A German bomber over the bend of the Thames and the docks of East London at the start of the evening raid of 7 September 1940, the first night of the Blitz, photographed from another German aircraft.","credit":"German air force photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinkel_He_111_over_Wapping,_East_London.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a city cut by a looping river lined with docks, with the dark shape of a twin-engined aircraft in the upper left."},"wiki":"The Blitz"},
    "ww2-224": {"questions":["Planners who believed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> assumed 600,000 killed in 60 days of air attack, yet bombing killed 51,509 civilians in Britain over the whole war.","The Blitz tested the belief that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and that bombing could break a nation, and the official history judged it never brought the enemy near a decisive stroke."],"abstract":"The <b>bomber will always get through</b> was the warning Stanley Baldwin gave the House of Commons on 10 November 1932, that no power on earth could protect the man in the street from being bombed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any town within reach of an aerodrome could be bombed in the first 5 minutes of a war, he argued, and the only defence was offence, which meant killing more women and children more quickly than the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He put the coming test in terms of nerve, asking whose morale would be shattered quickest by that first bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Planners turned the fear into numbers, and in 1937 the Committee of Imperial Defence assumed 60 days of attack with 600,000 killed and 1,200,000 injured, while psychiatric casualties were expected to outnumber physical ones by three to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The theory of a knock-out blow aimed at London shaped the early plans for evacuation, shelters and emergency services, and a chapter of the official civil defence history takes its title from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Blitz of 1940 and 1941 put the prophecy to the test, and in one sense it held, for London was bombed nightly for 10 weeks and the night defences brought down few attackers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What failed was the predicted result, since a month into the raids Churchill could tell the Commons that each ton of bombs was killing well under one person, a deadliness he put at less than a tenth of that of 1914–1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over the whole war bombing killed 51,509 civilians in the United Kingdom, fewer than a tenth of the deaths once assumed for the first 60 days alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The deep-shelter mentality that planners had feared proved rare, and most people preferred to sit out the raids at home rather than go underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The official history concluded that the night Blitz never brought the enemy within sight of a decisive stroke, and the government's own account declared that there was to be no knock-out blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “International Affairs,” <i>Hansard</i>, 10 November 1932, vol. 270, col. 632, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1932/nov/10/international-affairs. [Open access]","Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), 9–20, https://archive.org/details/problems-of-social-policy. [Open access]","Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 281, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), xvii, 256, 528, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/index.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 290–91, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “The Psychology of Protecting the UK Public against External Threat: COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 7, no. 11 (2020): 991–96, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7834303/. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>Front Line 1940–41: The Official Story of the Civil Defence of Britain</i> (London: HMSO, 1942), https://archive.org/details/frontline1940. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Baldwin think there was no defence against bombers?","a":"Because he argued that any town within reach of an aerodrome could be bombed in the first 5 minutes of a war, so the only defence was offence, which meant killing more women and children more quickly than the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the pre-war casualty forecasts so high?","a":"Because planning was dominated by the fear of a knock-out blow aimed at London. In 1937 the Committee of Imperial Defence assumed 60 days of attack with 600,000 killed and 1,200,000 injured, and psychiatric casualties were expected to outnumber physical ones by three to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombing kill far fewer people than predicted?","a":"Churchill credited the shelters, telling the Commons in October 1940 that each ton of bombs was killing less than one person, under a tenth of the rate in the First World War. Over the whole war 51,509 civilians died in bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The bomber will always get through"},
    "ww2-225": {"questions":["The Germans coined the verb 'coventrieren' after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which destroyed the city's medieval cathedral and about 40 hectares (100 acres) of its centre.","A month after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a War Cabinet decision led to an RAF raid in the same manner, code-named Abigail, which fell on Mannheim on 16 December 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Coventry Blitz</b> was the German air raid on Coventry on the night of 14–15 November 1940, the attack that opened the Luftwaffe's campaign against Britain's industrial cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Coventry, a city of about 250,000, had grown into a major centre of the engineering, motor and radio industries, and its aero-engine and machine-tool works made it a target of national importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bright moonlight helped the attackers, and the raid was led by Kampfgruppe 100, which used a radio-beam system called X-Gerät to find the city and mark it with incendiaries for the bombers behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crews were briefed to hit named works such as the Standard Motor Company and the Alvis aero-engine factory, but the fires spread through the medieval centre and destroyed the cathedral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Accounts of the force differ, for one official history counts about 450 aircraft over the city and another 437 operating, while a third puts it at some 330.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In one night 554 people were killed and 865 seriously injured, and about 40 hectares (100 acres) of the city centre were destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Telephones failed early, broken water mains hampered the firefighters and every railway line out of the city was blocked, and for a time local government was paralysed by the shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Observers for Mass-Observation reported open signs of hysteria, terror and neurosis, a picture at odds with the official judgement that the people were stunned but their morale was not broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Production was interrupted for about 2 months, yet half the staff of the Standard works were back on 16 November, and the German failure to return in strength let the city recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Germans coined the verb 'coventrieren' from the raid, and a month later a War Cabinet decision led to an RAF raid in the same manner on Mannheim, code-named Abigail, on 16 December 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 261–66, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-17.html. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 210–12, 235, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 404–05, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), 304–05, https://archive.org/details/problems-of-social-policy. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared: Mental Health Outcomes in the UK,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 8, no. 8 (2021): 708–16, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9756958/. [Open access]","Coventry Cathedral, “Our History,” https://www.coventrycathedral.org.uk/our-story/history. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>Front Line 1940–41: The Official Story of the Civil Defence of Britain</i> (London: HMSO, 1942), https://archive.org/details/frontline1940. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Coventry chosen as a target?","a":"Because it had grown into a major centre of the engineering, motor and radio industries, and its aero-engine and machine-tool works mattered to the whole war effort. Crews were briefed to hit named factories such as the Standard and Alvis works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so much of the city centre burn?","a":"Because the pathfinders of Kampfgruppe 100 marked the city with incendiaries by moonlight and the main force bombed the fires, while broken water mains and failed telephones crippled the firefighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Coventry recover faster than expected?","a":"Because the Germans did not come back in strength the following nights, and repairs began at once. Half the staff of the Standard works were back at work on 16 November, and production was interrupted for about 2 months rather than for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/A_wrecked_bus_stands_among_a_scene_of_devastation_in_the_centre_of_Coventry_after_the_major_Luftwaffe_air_raid_on_the_night_of_14-15_November_1940._H5593.jpg/1280px-A_wrecked_bus_stands_among_a_scene_of_devastation_in_the_centre_of_Coventry_after_the_major_Luftwaffe_air_raid_on_the_night_of_14-15_November_1940._H5593.jpg","title":"The centre of Coventry after the raid of 14–15 November 1940","desc":"A wrecked bus among the rubble in the centre of Coventry after the raid, with firemen still playing a hose on the smoking ruins.","credit":"Photograph by Mr Taylor, War Office official photographer, Imperial War Museums H 5593, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_wrecked_bus_stands_among_a_scene_of_devastation_in_the_centre_of_Coventry_after_the_major_Luftwaffe_air_raid_on_the_night_of_14-15_November_1940._H5593.jpg","alt":"The twisted metal frame of a burnt-out bus beside heaps of bricks, with a fireman aiming a hose at smoking rubble and gutted buildings behind."},"wiki":"Coventry Blitz"},
    "ww2-226": {"questions":["By the eve of war in 1939 the volunteers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> numbered over 1,500,000 men and women, organised locally and still far from fully trained or equipped.","Ministers preferred the name Civil Defence to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the 1939 Act for its better psychological value, though the old services were not formally renamed until September 1941."],"abstract":"<b>Air Raid Precautions</b>, usually shortened to ARP, was the British system for protecting civilians from air attack, built up by central government and local councils from 1935 and later known as Civil Defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Home Office's first circular on the subject, issued on 9 July 1935 and sold to the public for twopence, invited councils and employers to create ARP machinery and asked the public to volunteer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Air Raid Precautions Act of 1937, in force from 1 January 1938, required the larger councils to submit ARP schemes, and the Home Secretary told the Commons that 200,000 men and women had already volunteered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his plan an air raid warden would be responsible for the safety of a street or block of houses, setting local services in motion once a raid was detected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early effort went heavily into gas, with masks promised for the whole population, and by December 1938 the minister in charge was answering criticism that gas had been given undue attention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> A household card of the war shows what ARP meant in a street, listing the warden's post and first-aid post, the siren signals, the blackout rules and how to tackle an incendiary bomb with sand, water and a stirrup pump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the eve of war the voluntary ARP services had grown to over 1,500,000 men and women, locally organised and still far from fully trained or equipped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ministers chose the name Civil Defence for the 1939 Act for its better psychological value, but the ARP General Services kept their old name until September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Blitz the wardens, rescue parties and first-aid workers were in the thick of it, and the government's official story of civil defence was titled simply <i>Front Line</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many kept working through the raids at great risk to their lives, and in November 1940 the government said records were being kept for a roll of honour of those killed on duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 57, 106, 193, 286, 301, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Air-Raid Precautions Bill,” <i>Hansard</i>, 15 November 1937, vol. 329, cols. 42–45, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1937/nov/15/air-raid-precautions-bill. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), “The Home Front in the Second World War,” teaching resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/home-front-second-world-war/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Air-Raid Precautions Workers (Roll of Honour),” <i>Hansard</i>, 5 November 1940, vol. 365, col. 1193, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/nov/05/air-raid-precautions-workers-roll-of. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “The Psychology of Protecting the UK Public against External Threat: COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 7, no. 11 (2020): 991–96, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7834303/. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>Front Line 1940–41: The Official Story of the Civil Defence of Britain</i> (London: HMSO, 1942), https://archive.org/details/frontline1940. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Air-Raid Shelters,” <i>Hansard</i>, 21 December 1938, vol. 342, cols. 2880–81, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/dec/21/air-raid-shelters. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the early ARP effort criticised?","a":"Because it went heavily into gas, with masks promised for the whole population, and by December 1938 the minister in charge was answering criticism that gas had been given undue attention while high explosive and fire still needed work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did ARP depend on local councils?","a":"Because the 1935 circular invited councils and employers to build the machinery, and the 1937 Act then made the larger councils submit schemes. The services that grew from it were organised locally rather than run from London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was ARP renamed Civil Defence?","a":"Because ministers thought Civil Defence had better psychological value and used it for the title of the 1939 Act. The change was slow, and the ARP General Services kept their old name until September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Tea_and_buns_are_supplied_by_local_Air_Raid_Precautions_%28ARP%29_workers_to_fellow_ARP_workers_and_civilians_in_this_basement_shelter_in_South_East_London_during_1940._D1623.jpg/1280px-Tea_and_buns_are_supplied_by_local_Air_Raid_Precautions_%28ARP%29_workers_to_fellow_ARP_workers_and_civilians_in_this_basement_shelter_in_South_East_London_during_1940._D1623.jpg","title":"ARP workers in a South London shelter, 1940","desc":"Air Raid Precautions workers serving tea and buns to fellow ARP workers and civilians in a basement shelter in South East London in 1940.","credit":"Ministry of Information Photo Division photographer, Imperial War Museums D 1623, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tea_and_buns_are_supplied_by_local_Air_Raid_Precautions_(ARP)_workers_to_fellow_ARP_workers_and_civilians_in_this_basement_shelter_in_South_East_London_during_1940._D1623.jpg","alt":"Men and women in steel helmets crowd round a large urn in a low basement room while a young man in a helmet holds a tray of cups."},"wiki":"Air Raid Precautions"},
    "ww2-227": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in October 1940 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> justly deserved its fame, crediting shelters for a death toll of under one person per ton of bombs.","Flooding was the chief practical defect of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in January 1941 a minister told the Commons the government was well aware of the problem."],"abstract":"The <b>Anderson shelter</b> was a prefabricated steel garden shelter issued to British households from February 1939, a design commissioned by Sir John Anderson, the minister then in charge of civil defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anderson told the Commons in December 1938 that shelters proof against a direct hit were impracticable, and that the aim was protection against splinters, blast and falling debris in or close to people's own homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made of fourteen corrugated steel sheets weighing about 400 kilograms (8 hundredweight) in all, sunk about 60 centimetres (2 feet) into the ground and covered with earth, and it held four people, or six at a pinch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manual workers insured under the health insurance scheme, and others earning no more than £250 a year, received one free, and the government undertook to supply some 2.5 million families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over 2,300,000 had been distributed by 1940, and in November 1940 the Commons heard of a feeling of injustice among people just above the income limit who could not get one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In practice a properly sited and covered shelter usually survived a 50-kilogram bomb falling 2 metres (6 feet) away or a 250-kilogram bomb 6 metres (20 feet) away, its occupants suffering no more than shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its chief practical defect was flooding, and in January 1941 a minister told the Commons that the government was well aware of the problem of the flooded Andersons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In inner London in November 1940 about 27 per cent of people slept in household shelters such as the Anderson, while most of the rest stayed in their own homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill credited the shelters, among which he said the Anderson justly deserved its fame, for a death rate of under one person per ton of bombs, less than a tenth of that of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The government's account of the Blitz spoke of about a million back-garden Andersons in London and of one street where nearly 800 were made homeless but only three killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Air-Raid Shelters,” <i>Hansard</i>, 21 December 1938, vol. 342, cols. 2880–84, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/dec/21/air-raid-shelters. [Open access]","Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 187–89, 196, 504–05, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","Sarah Castagnetti, “The Bethnal Green Tube Shelter Disaster,” The National Archives (UK), https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/the-bethnal-green-tube-shelter-disaster/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 290–91, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Anderson Shelters,” <i>Hansard</i>, 22 January 1941, vol. 368, cols. 175–76, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/jan/22/anderson-shelters. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “The Psychology of Protecting the UK Public against External Threat: COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 7, no. 11 (2020): 991–96, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7834303/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Anderson Shelters,” <i>Hansard</i>, 5 November 1940, vol. 365, cols. 1195–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/nov/05/anderson-shelters. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>Front Line 1940–41: The Official Story of the Civil Defence of Britain</i> (London: HMSO, 1942), 65, https://archive.org/details/frontline1940. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the shelter not made bomb-proof?","a":"Because Anderson told the Commons that shelters proof against a direct hit were impracticable. The aim instead was protection against splinters, blast and falling debris, close to people's own homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some people resent the scheme?","a":"Because the shelters were free only to insured manual workers and others earning no more than £250 a year. In November 1940 the Commons heard of a feeling of injustice among people just above the income limit who could not get one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill praise the Anderson?","a":"Because he credited the shelters for a bombing death rate he put at less than a tenth of that of the First World War, and he singled out the Anderson as justly deserving its fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/An_Anderson_shelter_remains_intact_amidst_destruction_in_Latham_Street%2C_Poplar%2C_London_during_1941._D5949.jpg/1280px-An_Anderson_shelter_remains_intact_amidst_destruction_in_Latham_Street%2C_Poplar%2C_London_during_1941._D5949.jpg","title":"An Anderson shelter in Poplar, 1941","desc":"An Anderson shelter still standing amid the wreckage of houses in Latham Street, Poplar, in 1941, after a land mine fell a few yards away; the three people inside were not hurt.","credit":"Ministry of Information Photo Division photographer, Imperial War Museums D 5949, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Anderson_shelter_remains_intact_amidst_destruction_in_Latham_Street,_Poplar,_London_during_1941._D5949.jpg","alt":"A small arched hut of corrugated metal with a dark doorway, half buried in broken bricks and timber in front of the shells of terraced houses."}},
    "ww2-228": {"questions":["By the end of 1939 over 900,000 mothers and children had drifted home from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, leaving only about 570,000 official evacuees in the reception areas.","A Ministry of Health poster showed Hitler's ghost whispering 'Take them back!' to a mother, urging parents to leave children placed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the safer areas."],"abstract":"The <b>Government Evacuation Scheme</b> was the official British plan to move mothers, young children and schoolchildren from cities expected to be bombed into safer reception areas, where householders took them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Planners saw it, with shelters, as one of the two methods of protection that overshadowed all others in importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country was zoned into evacuation, neutral and reception areas, and in September 1939 the Minister of Health refused to add more districts to the evacuation list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the early hours of 1 September 1939 the scheme began to operate, and in 3 days it moved 1,473,000 people, most of them before war was declared and without a single accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Labour member called it the most remarkable movement of the civil population ever recorded in history, while admitting there had been misfits and misadventures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Alongside it some 2,000,000 people evacuated themselves privately, a movement the Health Departments did not measure until the fifth year of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Complaints in the Commons about verminous children were met by the minister's reply that there had been no time for weeks of preparation, though he admitted there were lessons to be learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With no bombs falling, over 900,000 mothers and children had returned home by the end of 1939, leaving about 570,000 official evacuees in the reception areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Posters pleaded with mothers not to bring children back, one showing the ghostly figure of Hitler whispering 'Take them back!', and a second scheme planned for 670,000 schoolchildren met a very poor response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When some children were sent overseas in 1940 the Home Secretary promised a balance between the well-to-do and those sent under the government scheme, and by December peers complained that London was still far from cleared of children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), 11, 101–02, 139, 243, https://archive.org/details/problems-of-social-policy. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Evacuation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 14 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 802–03, 877–79, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/14/evacuation. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), “The Home Front in the Second World War,” teaching resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/home-front-second-world-war/. [Open access]","Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 187, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Children's Overseas Evacuation Scheme,” <i>Hansard</i>, 1 August 1940, vol. 363, col. 1399, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/aug/01/childrens-overseas-evacua-tion-scheme. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, “Evacuation Problems,” <i>Hansard</i>, 10 December 1940, vol. 118, cols. 74–90, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1940/dec/10/evacuation-problems. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Evacuation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 13 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 627–28, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/13/evacuation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many evacuees go home within months?","a":"Because the expected bombing did not come. With no raids on the cities, over 900,000 mothers and children had returned by the end of 1939, leaving about 570,000 official evacuees in the reception areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government plead with mothers not to bring children back?","a":"Because the scheme only worked if children stayed in the safer areas, and the drift home was emptying them. One poster showed the ghostly figure of Hitler whispering 'Take them back!' to a mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the arrival of city children cause such complaint?","a":"Because members raised the condition of some children, verminous among them, and the minister replied that a movement of this size at such short notice had left no time for weeks of preparation. He admitted there were lessons to be learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/The_Civilian_Evacuation_Scheme_in_Britain_during_the_Second_World_War_HU36238.jpg","title":"East End children setting off as evacuees","desc":"Young children from the East End of London, carrying their belongings and gas masks, setting off on their journey to safer areas under the government's evacuation scheme.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Imperial War Museums HU 36238, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Civilian_Evacuation_Scheme_in_Britain_during_the_Second_World_War_HU36238.jpg","alt":"A crowd of small boys and girls with bundles and bags slung over their shoulders walk along a cobbled street past shops, watched by adults."},"wiki":"Evacuations of civilians in the United Kingdom during the Second World War"},
    "ww2-229": {"questions":["Mass-Observation reported hysteria, terror and neurosis in Coventry after its raid, evidence that sits uneasily with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promoted by wartime propaganda and postwar politicians.","Churchill told the Commons in October 1940 that all classes had suffered evenly, as they would desire to do, a note of shared sacrifice central to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the bombing years."],"abstract":"The <b>Blitz spirit</b> is the picture of Britain under the bombing of 1940 and 1941 as a nation united, cheerful and unbroken, a picture promoted by official propaganda during the war and by politicians after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A month into the raids Churchill told the Commons that all classes had suffered evenly, as they would desire to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The official history of air defence wrote of dogged humour and a new sense of fellowship between all classes, and judged that the bombing left people more than ever determined to see the war through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government's account of civil defence went further, reporting no panic, no white faces in the streets and only twenty-three neurotics admitted to hospital in all London in October 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That account also corrected an image of the time, pointing out that most Londoners slept at home or in household shelters rather than in the crowded public shelters that drew so much publicity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Yet Mass-Observation, commissioned by the government to survey morale in bombed towns, reported open signs of hysteria, terror and neurosis in Coventry, where local government itself was for a time paralysed by shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Hull a government study found that 6 months after the raids 4 per cent of men and 14 per cent of women had severe neuroses, and in several cities people trekked out each night to the countryside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Conditions in the big shelters were squalid at first, without beds, proper sanitation or food, and some shelterers were drunk, noisy or verminous, though behaviour was on the whole good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fear could still turn deadly, as at the Bethnal Green Tube shelter on 3 March 1943, where alarm at unfamiliar explosions was followed by a fall on the stairs in poor light that killed 173 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A recent review argues that this myth of a nation untroubled by bombing fostered resolve and American support in 1940 and 1941 but later helped to sideline psychiatry in planning the National Health Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 257–58, 281, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-16.html. [Open access]","Ministry of Information, <i>Front Line 1940–41: The Official Story of the Civil Defence of Britain</i> (London: HMSO, 1942), 65–70, https://archive.org/details/frontline1940. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared: Mental Health Outcomes in the UK,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 8, no. 8 (2021): 708–16, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9756958/. [Open access]","Edgar Jones, “The Psychology of Protecting the UK Public against External Threat: COVID-19 and the Blitz Compared,” <i>The Lancet Psychiatry</i> 7, no. 11 (2020): 991–96, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7834303/. [Open access]","Terence H. O’Brien, <i>Civil Defence</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 508–09, https://archive.org/details/civil-defence-obrien. [Open access]","Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), 304–05, https://archive.org/details/problems-of-social-policy. [Open access]","Sarah Castagnetti, “The Bethnal Green Tube Shelter Disaster,” The National Archives (UK), https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/the-bethnal-green-tube-shelter-disaster/. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, col. 290, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government stress calm and unity in 1940?","a":"Because, as a recent review argues, the picture of a nation untroubled by bombing had a positive use in 1940 and 1941, when official propaganda promoted London as a symbol of resilience, fostering resolve and American support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians doubt the official picture?","a":"Because evidence gathered at the time was more mixed. Mass-Observation reported hysteria, terror and neurosis in Coventry, and a study in Hull found severe neuroses in 4 per cent of men and 14 per cent of women 6 months after the raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Bethnal Green matter to the argument?","a":"Because it shows that fear could still turn deadly late in the war. On 3 March 1943, after alarm at unfamiliar explosions, people fell on the stairs of the Tube shelter in poor light and 173 were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Shelter_Photographs_Taken_in_London_by_Bill_Brandt%2C_November_1940_D1564.jpg","title":"A sing-along in a London shelter, November 1940","desc":"An ARP warden plays the piano for a sing-along in an air raid shelter beneath a religious institution in London, one of a series of shelter photographs taken by Bill Brandt for the Ministry of Information.","credit":"Photograph by Bill Brandt, Ministry of Information Photo Division, Imperial War Museums D 1564, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shelter_Photographs_Taken_in_London_by_Bill_Brandt,_November_1940_D1564.jpg","alt":"A man in uniform plays an upright piano in a timber-propped basement while rows of seated men and women in coats and hats look on."}},
    "ww2-230": {"questions":["Set up on the first day of war in September 1939, Chamberlain's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had nine members, five of them burdened with heavy departmental duties.","Churchill told the Commons in May 1941 that adding one service minister to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would mean adding all three, against the principle of a small non-departmental body."],"abstract":"The <b>War Cabinet</b> was the small body of senior ministers that directed Britain's war effort from the first day of the Second World War, taking the place of the normal, much larger Cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Chamberlain had promised the Cabinet on 1 September 1939 a War Cabinet on the model of 1916–19, the only practical course in a great war, and it met on the first day of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It had nine members, among them Simon at the Exchequer, Halifax at the Foreign Office, Hore-Belisha at the War Office and Churchill as First Lord of the Admiralty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writers between the wars had praised Lloyd George's War Cabinet of five or six members free of departmental duties, so a body in which five of nine ran departments was criticised as a departure from true principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> With other ministers and chiefs of staff called in, its meetings were usually about fifteen strong, half the size of a pre-war Cabinet, and it authorised some sixty committees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> When Churchill became prime minister he formed a War Cabinet of five, himself as Prime Minister and Minister of Defence with Chamberlain, Attlee, Halifax and Greenwood, its list dated 11 May 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He told the Commons 2 days later that it represented, with the Opposition Liberals, the unity of the nation, and that the three party leaders would serve either in it or in high executive office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked in May 1941 to bring in the Liberal leader, he replied that adding one service minister would mean adding all three, against the principle of a small body of non-departmental ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much business passed to committees, above all the Lord President's Committee, the chief focus of civil government by 1941, while great decisions stayed its own, such as the RAF's raid in the Coventry manner in December 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Reconstructed after the disasters in the Far East, by February 1942 it had seven members, three without departments, with Attlee as Deputy Prime Minister and the Minister of Labour among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Cabinet,” <i>Hansard</i>, 3 September 1939, vol. 351, col. 287, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/03/war-cabinet. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Cabinet,” <i>Hansard</i>, 13 May 1940, vol. 360, col. 1497, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/13/war-cabinet. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “His Majesty's Government,” <i>Hansard</i>, 13 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1501–25, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/13/his-majestys-government-1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Cabinet,” <i>Hansard</i>, 15 May 1941, vol. 371, cols. 1254–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/may/15/war-cabinet. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Ministerial Changes,” <i>Hansard</i>, 24 February 1942, vol. 378, cols. 27–176, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/24/ministerial-changes. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 89–93, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-3.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 216–20, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-8.html. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 234–35, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a War Cabinet created at all?","a":"Because a small supreme body on the model of Lloyd George's War Cabinet of 1916–19 was seen as the only practical course in a great war. Chamberlain promised one on 1 September 1939, and it met on the first day of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Chamberlain's War Cabinet criticised?","a":"Because five of its nine members ran departments, while the interwar ideal was a War Cabinet of five or six members free of departmental duties. Critics called it a departure from true principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill keep his War Cabinet so small?","a":"Because he held to the principle of a small body of non-departmental ministers. Asked to bring in the Liberal leader, he replied that adding one service minister would mean adding all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Churchill_Coalition_Government_-_24_October_1941.jpg/1280px-Churchill_Coalition_Government_-_24_October_1941.jpg","title":"Churchill and his ministers, 24 October 1941","desc":"Churchill seated at the centre of the front row among ministers of his coalition, with Attlee, Eden, Beaverbrook and Bevin to one side and Anderson, Greenwood and Kingsley Wood to the other.","credit":"Photograph by Vandyk, Imperial War Museums, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Churchill_Coalition_Government_-_24_October_1941.jpg","alt":"Fifteen men in dark suits in two rows, eight seated on chairs on a lawn and seven standing behind them in front of a hedge."},"wiki":"War cabinet"},
    "ww2-251": {"questions":["Hitler told his commanders that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had made Yugoslavia an uncertain factor, and ordered the country destroyed militarily and as a national unit without any ultimatum.","The American minister reported wild enthusiasm in Belgrade after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended Prince Paul's regency, with demonstrations outside his legation and the old ministers under arrest."],"abstract":"The <b>Yugoslav coup d'état</b> of 27 March 1941 overthrew the regency of Prince Paul 2 days after his government had signed Yugoslavia's adherence to the Tripartite Pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> At the Belvedere Palace in Vienna the prime minister, Dragiša Cvetković, had declared that his country was joining the forces building a new order in south-eastern Europe, and the Axis promised in return not to demand passage for its troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Early on 27 March, officers led by General Simović, a former commander of the air force, seized power, and a manifesto in the name of the 17-year-old King Peter II announced that he had assumed power and that the regents had resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Belgrade greeted the news with wild enthusiasm and continuous demonstrations, and the regents and leading ministers were reported to be under arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history judged the coup an expression, mainly by the Serbs, of the choice between subjection and resistance rather than a national rising, while a German museum account calls it a putsch engineered by Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Simović told the American minister that his government would neither denounce nor ratify the pact, which it regarded as its predecessor's work, but would resist any move on Salonika by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The young king told the American minister that Prince Paul had kept him ignorant of the negotiations over the pact, and that on the night of the coup he had been held prisoner by General Kosić, who would not let him see Simović.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Berlin that same day Hitler told his commanders that Yugoslavia was an uncertain factor for the coming attack on Greece and still more for the later attack on the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Without waiting for any declaration of loyalty, he resolved to destroy Yugoslavia militarily and as a national unit, with no ultimatum and with unmerciful harshness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The new foreign minister assured the German minister that Yugoslavia wanted to keep friendly relations, but the attack came 10 days later, on 6 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Germany (Morris) to the Secretary of State, March 25, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d962. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Yugoslavia (Lane) to the Secretary of State, March 27, 1941, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d963. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Yugoslavia (Lane) to the Secretary of State, March 28, 1941, midnight,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d967. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Yugoslavia (Lane) to the Secretary of State, March 31, 1941, midnight,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d971. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 4, 72–73, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-4.html. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 1746-PS: Conference of Hitler and the German High Command on the Situation in Yugoslavia, March 27, 1941,\" <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1746-ps.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Balkanfeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/balkanfeldzug-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 21–22, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the new government not simply repudiate the Tripartite Pact?","a":"Because it did not want to provoke Germany or Italy. Simović said his government would neither denounce nor ratify the pact, treating it as the work of its predecessor rather than an obligation of its own."},{"q":"Why did Hitler decide to attack without waiting for assurances?","a":"Because he judged that Yugoslavia could not be relied on during his campaigns against Greece and the Soviet Union, and he would not trust any assurance the new government gave; he ordered its destruction with no inquiries and no ultimatum."},{"q":"Why did the British official history call the coup a Serb choice rather than a national rising?","a":"Because it judged that the officers spoke mainly for the Serbs, who chose resistance over subjection, rather than for the whole country."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Demonstrations_in_Belgrade_1941.jpg/1920px-Demonstrations_in_Belgrade_1941.jpg","title":"Belgrade, 27 March 1941","desc":"Crowds fill a Belgrade street on the day of the coup, some holding flags, as the American minister reported continuous demonstrations across the city.","credit":"Photographer unknown, from the Enciklopedija Leksikografskog zavoda (Zagreb, 1967), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demonstrations_in_Belgrade_1941.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd of men and women in hats and coats packs a city street lined with tall buildings, with flags held up above their heads."},"wiki":"Yugoslav coup d'état"},
    "ww2-252": {"questions":["Hitler promised the Adriatic coast to Italy, the Banat to Hungary and Macedonia to Bulgaria to win their help in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that he ordered on 27 March 1941.","12 days of fighting in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the German army 151 killed, 392 wounded and 15 missing, against some 254,000 prisoners taken."],"abstract":"The <b>invasion of Yugoslavia</b> began on 6 April 1941, when German, Italian, Hungarian and Bulgarian forces attacked the kingdom, which surrendered 11 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler had decided on it on 27 March, the day of the Belgrade coup, ordering the country destroyed militarily and as a national unit and promising its neighbours the Adriatic coast, the Banat and Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan drawn up the next day called for a concentric attack to destroy the Yugoslav forces and dissolve the state, with Croat independence movements to be favoured and Croat territory spared from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south German armour reached Skopje by the evening of 7 April, and the quick defeat of the southern Yugoslav army opened the road into Greece through Monastir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The judges at Nuremberg found that the attack came without warning and so swiftly that there had been no time even to stage the usual incidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> It opened, as Hitler's plan required, with an air attack on Belgrade at seven on the morning of Sunday 6 April, although the government had announced days before that the capital would be an open town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American minister reported that the bombing was unquestionably indiscriminate, that the worst damage lay in crowded shopping and residential quarters, and that many dead were still lying in the streets 2 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With its high command cut off from the field, the Yugoslav army broke up, a government representative asked for a ceasefire on 14 April, and the unconditional surrender signed on 17 April took effect at noon the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> German losses in the 12 days were 151 killed, 392 wounded and 15 missing, while some 254,000 prisoners were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cordell Hull called it a barbaric invasion and an attempt to annihilate a country by brute force, and promised that American supplies would be sent to the Yugoslavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 1746-PS: Conference of Hitler and the German High Command on the Situation in Yugoslavia, March 27, 1941,\" <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1746-ps.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Minister in Yugoslavia (Lane), April 6, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d972. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in Hungary (Travers) to the Secretary of State, April 12, 1941, 10 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d974. [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 49–50, 63–64, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"The Aggression against Yugoslavia and Greece,\" <i>Judgment of the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judyugo.asp. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 5, 85, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-5.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the attack begin with the bombing of Belgrade?","a":"Because Hitler's plan required it, and the raid cut the Yugoslav high command off from its armies in the field, so that the army broke up within days."},{"q":"Why did Hungary, Bulgaria and Italy join the attack?","a":"Because Hitler promised each of them Yugoslav territory: the Adriatic coast to Italy, the Banat to Hungary and Macedonia to Bulgaria."},{"q":"Why were Croat areas to be spared from the air?","a":"Because the German plan aimed to win the Croats over by favouring their independence movements and treating them as friends of the Axis, so that the Yugoslav state would come apart from within."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Bundesarchiv_Bild_141-1005%2C_Belgrad%2C_Zerst%C3%B6rungen.jpg","title":"Destruction in Belgrade, 1941","desc":"A tram lies wrecked among the rubble of collapsed buildings in Belgrade in 1941, while civilians and a soldier look on.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 141-1005, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_141-1005,_Belgrad,_Zerst%C3%B6rungen.jpg","alt":"A street of damaged buildings with a wrecked tram tipped over among heaps of rubble and timber; several men in coats and hats and a soldier in a greatcoat stand looking at it."},"wiki":"Invasion of Yugoslavia"},
    "ww2-253": {"questions":["Falling back from the Aliakmon line to Thermopylae and then to the beaches, Anzac troops lost the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as German motorcyclists entered Athens on 27 April.","Members of the Commons debated a motion approving help to Greece after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the British lost about 12,000 men from all causes."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Greece</b> was the German conquest of mainland Greece in April 1941, an operation code-named Marita that Hitler ordered in a directive of 13 December 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its purpose, the directive said, was to foil a British attempt to build air bases behind a Balkan front, which would threaten Italy and the Romanian oilfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1941 Field Marshal List agreed with the Bulgarian general staff that German armoured and motorised divisions would cross Bulgaria to attack Greece, with every care taken over secrecy and surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> British troops began landing in Greece on 3 March to help the Greeks against Italy, and about 58,000 British and Dominion service personnel were carried there over some 6 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 12 April the Australian and New Zealand divisions in the line were joined as an Anzac Corps, fighting side by side again 26 years after Gallipoli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Germans attacked on 6 April, entered Salonika on the 9th and came through Monastir, turning the Allied line on the Aliakmon and forcing a retreat to Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 21 April the surrender of the Greek armies facing the Italians in Epirus was accepted, and by then the British had already decided to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Of the 50,732 men lifted from the beaches at the end of April an uncertain number were Greeks and Yugoslavs, and British losses from all causes came to about 12,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German motorcyclists entered Athens on the morning of 27 April, and the US Army study of the campaign put German losses at about 1,100 killed and 4,000 wounded and missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Commons debated a motion approving the policy of sending help to Greece, one member defended the expedition as a debt of honour arising from the guarantee to Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, \"The Aggression against Yugoslavia and Greece,\" <i>Judgment of the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judyugo.asp. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 1746-PS: Conference between Field Marshal List and Representatives of the Bulgarian General Staff, February 8, 1941,\" <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1746-ps.asp. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 5, 85–104, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-5.html. [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 111–12, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>Greece, Crete and Syria</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, Army, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), 70, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.242863. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 7 May 1941, vol. 371, cols. 867–950, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/may/07/war-situation. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Balkanfeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/balkanfeldzug-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler decide to invade Greece?","a":"Because a British presence behind a Balkan front, with air bases in Greece, would threaten both Italy and the Romanian oilfields."},{"q":"Why did the British decide to leave Greece so soon?","a":"Because the German advance through Monastir turned every line they tried to hold, and the Greek armies in Epirus surrendered on 21 April, leaving the expeditionary force alone."},{"q":"Why did one member call the expedition a debt of honour?","a":"Because Britain had given Greece a guarantee, and he argued that it obliged the government to send help once Greece was attacked."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-164-0389-23A%2C_Athen%2C_Hissen_der_Hakenkreuzflagge.jpg","title":"The German war flag raised on the Acropolis","desc":"German soldiers hoist the Reich war flag on the Acropolis in front of the Parthenon, in a propaganda photograph that the German Federal Archives dates to May 1941.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-164-0389-23A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-164-0389-23A,_Athen,_Hissen_der_Hakenkreuzflagge.jpg","alt":"Helmeted soldiers stand saluting as a large flag bearing a swastika and an iron cross is raised on a pole, with the columns of the Parthenon behind them."},"wiki":"German invasion of Greece"},
    "ww2-254": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons he took full responsibility for the choice to fight the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without effective air support rather than let the Germans occupy the island unopposed.","Decrypted German signals warned General Freyberg of the airborne plan weeks before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but he still divided his New Zealanders between Maleme airfield and the coast."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Crete</b> was fought between 20 May and 1 June 1941, when German airborne troops in the operation code-named Merkur took the island from its British, Dominion and Greek defenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After intense bombing, Churchill told the Commons on the first afternoon, about 1,500 enemy troops had landed by glider, parachute and troop-carrier in the Canea and Maleme area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Decrypted German signals had shown since late April that Crete was the target, and on 6 May they gave the assault plan in detail, beginning with parachute landings on the three airfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> General Freyberg nevertheless split his New Zealand forces between Maleme airfield and the coast, against a seaborne attack that intelligence had made the lesser danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Germans held Maleme after the first days and flew in mountain troops, and on 27 May the evacuation was ordered, mostly from the small beach at Sphakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The British official history counted nearly 1,800 British and Dominion soldiers killed and about 12,000 taken prisoner, while the Royal Navy lost three cruisers and six destroyers and had 1,828 men killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German reports gave losses of between 3,986 and 6,453 men, and some 350 aircraft, more than half of them troop carriers, were lost or damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commander of the German airborne corps complained afterwards that his intelligence officers had put the defenders at about a third of their real strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons he took full responsibility for fighting without effective air support, since the alternative was to let the Germans occupy the island unopposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Privately he counted Crete a defeat, and so many German parachutists had been lost that too few remained for large airborne operations when the invasion of the Soviet Union began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 7, 131–47, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-7.html. [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 140–41, 149–51, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Crete (Enemy Attack),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 May 1941, vol. 371, cols. 1496–97, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/may/20/crete-enemy-attack. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Defence of Crete,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 10 June 1941, vol. 372, cols. 63–164, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/jun/10/defence-of-crete. [Open access]","Miriam F. Perlberg, \"Intelligence Lessons Learned from the Battle for Crete, May 1941\" (paper, Naval War College, Newport, RI, 1992), 10–13, DTIC ADA250269, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA250269. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>Greece, Crete and Syria</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, Army, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), 284, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.242863. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Balkanfeldzug,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/balkanfeldzug-1941.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in the United Kingdom (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, June 2, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d736. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the warning from decrypted signals not save Maleme?","a":"Because Freyberg could not share the source with his staff and still feared a landing from the sea, so he spread his New Zealanders between the airfield and the coast instead of concentrating them where the Germans came."},{"q":"Why did Britain fight for Crete without air cover?","a":"Because, as Churchill put it, the only alternative was to let the Germans occupy the island without opposition, and the commanders believed there was a good chance of winning even so."},{"q":"Why did the victory cost Germany so much?","a":"Because the parachute troops landed among prepared defenders whose strength had been badly underestimated, and heavy losses of men and transport aircraft left too few trained parachutists for the start of the war in the east."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Bundesarchiv_Bild_141-0864%2C_Kreta%2C_Landung_von_Fallschirmj%C3%A4gern.jpg","title":"Parachute landings on Crete, May 1941","desc":"German parachutists come down over a hillside on Crete while men already on the ground cross a field of grain below, May 1941.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 141-0864, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_141-0864,_Kreta,_Landung_von_Fallschirmj%C3%A4gern.jpg","alt":"Dozens of parachutes drift down over a low ridge; in the foreground a helmeted soldier crouches in a field of tall grain beside a dropped bundle, and another man walks towards him."},"wiki":"Battle of Crete"},
    "ww2-255": {"questions":["The exiled government in London protested to Washington against each stage of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Sumner Welles answered by condemning the invasion and mutilation of Yugoslavia.","Hitler promised the Adriatic coast, the Banat and Macedonia to his allies on 27 March 1941, anticipating the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed the kingdom's surrender."],"abstract":"The <b>partition of Yugoslavia</b> was the division of the defeated kingdom among the Axis powers and their allies after its surrender in April 1941, drawn so as to exploit its ethnic tensions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany annexed northern and eastern Slovenia, occupied the Banat with its large German minority, and set up a military administration in Serbia under a puppet government of General Milan Nedić.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy annexed the rest of Slovenia, occupied the Adriatic coast including Montenegro, and attached Kosovo to Albania, which it already held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hungary annexed the Bačka and Baranja, and Bulgaria occupied Macedonia and the small district of Pirot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler had sketched much of this on 27 March, when he promised the Adriatic coast to Italy, the Banat to Hungary and Macedonia to Bulgaria in return for their help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Ustaša proclaimed a so-called Independent State of Croatia, which annexed Bosnia and Herzegovina but was divided into German and Italian zones, and agreements signed in Rome on 18 May ceded much of the Dalmatian coast and islands to Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its borders were set by Germany and Italy, and Hungary annexed the Međimurje district from it in December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The exiled government in London protested to Washington at each step, and Sumner Welles replied with the indignation of his government at the invasion and mutilation of Yugoslavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Further notes condemned the division of Slovenia between Germany and Italy as a breach of the rule that an occupier may not change the status of territory before a peace settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In July Bulgaria created new districts of Skopje and Bitola and began recruiting soldiers there, which the exiled government protested as a fresh attempt to dismember the Yugoslav state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 1746-PS: Conference of Hitler and the German High Command on the Situation in Yugoslavia, March 27, 1941,\" <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1746-ps.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Yugoslav Minister (Fotitch) to the Secretary of State, May 24, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d978. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Yugoslav Minister (Fotitch), May 28, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d979. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Yugoslav Minister (Fotitch), September 25, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d981. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Yugoslav Minister (Fotitch) to the Secretary of State, November 4, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d982. [Open access]","Jasenovac Memorial Site, \"Uspostava NDH,\" Javna ustanova Spomen-područje Jasenovac, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=9404. [in Croatian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the partition follow ethnic lines?","a":"Because the occupiers used the kingdom's ethnic tensions to fix their new borders; Germany, for example, occupied the Banat, which had a large German minority."},{"q":"Why did the exiled government call the annexations illegal?","a":"Because, it argued, an occupying power may not change the legal status of territory it holds in war before a peace settlement, so every annexation was void."},{"q":"Why had Hungary and Bulgaria been promised land in advance?","a":"Because Hitler wanted their armies in the attack, and on 27 March he offered the Banat to Hungary and Macedonia to Bulgaria to make the war popular there."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Map_of_the_Axis_occupation_of_Yugoslavia.svg/1280px-Map_of_the_Axis_occupation_of_Yugoslavia.svg.png","title":"Yugoslavia divided","desc":"The kingdom's 1939 frontier with the areas annexed or occupied by Germany, Italy, Hungary, Bulgaria and Albania, the Independent State of Croatia and occupied Serbia, as the borders stood in 1942.","credit":"Map by Amitchell125, after Jozo Tomasevich (1975), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Axis_occupation_of_Yugoslavia.svg","alt":"A map of the former Yugoslavia hatched and shaded in red, orange, brown, green and blue to show the areas taken by Germany, Italy, Hungary, Bulgaria and Albania, with Croatia and Serbia labelled in the middle."}},
    "ww2-256": {"questions":["Between 77,000 and 99,000 people were murdered at Jasenovac, the largest camp complex of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Serbs, Jews, Roma and Croat opponents of the regime.","The exiled Yugoslav government told Washington that the so-called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been imposed under the protection of an occupying army and was void in international law."],"abstract":"The <b>Independent State of Croatia</b> was proclaimed in Zagreb on 10 April 1941, while the German invasion of Yugoslavia was still under way, by the fascist and separatist Ustaša movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its leader, Ante Pavelić, had gone into exile in 1929, and the movement he built up abroad carried out terrorist acts that included the assassination of King Alexander at Marseilles in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Membership grew from about 2,500 in April 1941 to more than 100,000 by May, and power was concentrated in Pavelić's hands, though it depended wholly on the policies of Hitler and Mussolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The state included Bosnia and Herzegovina and covered 102,725 square kilometres (39,662 square miles) with some 6.6 million people, but Germany and Italy divided it into zones and Italy annexed much of the Dalmatian coast and almost all its islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The exiled government protested to Washington that it had been imposed under the protection of an army of occupation and was void in international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One of the regime's basic aims, the memorial site at Jasenovac states, was genocide against the Serbs, Jews and Roma within its borders, and its militia burned whole Serbian villages and killed their people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum puts the number of Serbs killed by the Croatian authorities at between 320,000 and 340,000, and estimates that more than 30,000 Croatian Jews were killed in Croatia or at Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The largest of its camps was the Jasenovac complex on the Sava, where between 77,000 and 99,000 people were murdered between 1941 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The museum gives its figures as ranges because many documents were destroyed and the subject has long been distorted by ethnic and ideological conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Croat leaders who had joined the Belgrade government only on 3 April left it for Zagreb when the new state was proclaimed, ending a show of national unity that had lasted exactly 7 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Yugoslav Minister (Fotitch) to the Secretary of State, May 12, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d977. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Yugoslav Minister (Fotitch) to the Secretary of State, May 24, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v02/d978. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jasenovac,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jasenovac. [Open access]","Jasenovac Memorial Site, \"Uspostava NDH,\" Javna ustanova Spomen-područje Jasenovac, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=9404. [in Croatian] [Open access]","Jasenovac Memorial Site, \"Ustaše,\" Javna ustanova Spomen-područje Jasenovac, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=5110. [in Croatian] [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 24–25, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the exiled government call the state void?","a":"Because it had been set up under the protection of an occupying army, and an occupation in wartime cannot change the legal status of the territory it holds."},{"q":"Why are the death tolls given as ranges?","a":"Because many records were destroyed, others were long closed to independent scholars, and the subject has been distorted by ethnic and ideological conflict, so historians work from census data and surviving archives."},{"q":"Why was the state independent only in name?","a":"Because the Ustaše owed their power wholly to Hitler and Mussolini, Germany and Italy fixed its borders and stationed troops in their own zones, and Italy annexed much of its coast."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Independent_State_of_Croatia_1941-43.png","title":"The Independent State of Croatia, 1941–1943","desc":"The state's borders in 1941–1943, with the line dividing the German and Italian occupation zones and the coastal districts annexed by Italy.","credit":"Map by DIREKTOR, after a map by PANONIAN, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Independent_State_of_Croatia_1941-43.png","alt":"A map in which a large dark pink area stretches from Zagreb to Mostar, crossed by a grey line separating German and Italian zones, with Italian-held strips along the Adriatic coast."},"wiki":"Independent State of Croatia"},
    "ww2-257": {"questions":["Only thirty or forty German aircraft, flown in through Vichy-held Syria, reached Iraq during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the British invasion of Syria and Lebanon followed in June 1941.","Rashid Ali and his supporters crossed into Iran on 30 May, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and anti-Jewish rioting broke out in Baghdad as the Regent returned."],"abstract":"The <b>Anglo-Iraqi War</b> was the brief campaign of May 1941 in which British forces put down the pro-Axis government that Rashid Ali al-Gailani and four army leaders had set up in Baghdad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the morning of 2 April the pro-British Regent fled to the American legation disguised in women's dress, and the American minister drove him to the air base at Habbaniya hidden under a rug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain held bases at Habbaniya and Shaibah under a treaty of 1930, and from 18 April it landed troops at Basra, arguing that the treaty allowed them passage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before dawn on 30 April Iraqi troops occupied the plateau that overlooks the Habbaniya airfield and warned that they would attack if any aircraft took off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The training school's aircraft struck first on 2 May, and at dawn on 6 May the Iraqis were found to have left part of the plateau, abandoning large quantities of arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Germany sent aid through Vichy-held Syria, where the French released war material, but the Iraqis were told to hold out alone for a fortnight, and Churchill later said that only thirty or forty German aircraft arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fallujah was taken on 19 May, and on 30 May Rashid Ali and his chief supporters crossed into Iran, after which an armistice was signed outside Baghdad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Regent returned on 1 June, rioters killed between 150 and 180 Jews in 2 days of violence that became known as the Farhud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German use of Syria's airfields helped to persuade Britain to invade Syria and Lebanon with Free French troops on 8 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After 5 weeks of fighting the Vichy authorities signed a convention at Acre on 14 July, and more than 37,000 French soldiers and civilians were afterwards repatriated by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Minister Resident in Iraq (Knabenshue) to the Secretary of State, April 2, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d498. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister Resident in Iraq (Knabenshue) to the Secretary of State, April 30, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d523. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 9, 181–94, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-9.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 10, 206–21, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-10.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Farhud,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-farhud. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 September 1941, vol. 374, cols. 67–156, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/sep/09/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British strike first at Habbaniya?","a":"Because the Iraqi army held the plateau overlooking the airfield and had cut the base off by land, so every day of waiting let the besiegers grow stronger."},{"q":"Why did German help to Iraq achieve so little?","a":"Because it had to come through Vichy-held Syria, the Iraqis were told to hold out alone for a fortnight, and only thirty or forty German aircraft ever arrived."},{"q":"Why did the war in Iraq lead to the invasion of Syria?","a":"Because German aircraft had used Syria's airfields on their way to Iraq, showing that the Vichy authorities there would let the Germans in."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/BritsLookingOnBaghdad1941.jpg","title":"British troops above Baghdad, June 1941","desc":"Soldiers in sun helmets look out over the Tigris and the city of Baghdad on 11 June 1941, ten days after the Regent's return.","credit":"Lieutenant A. R. Tanner, No. 1 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museums E 3464, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BritsLookingOnBaghdad1941.jpg","alt":"A group of soldiers in pith helmets and khaki shirts, rifles slung, stand on high ground looking across a wide river towards a bridge, domes and minarets."},"wiki":"Anglo-Iraqi War"},
    "ww2-258": {"questions":["Reza Shah appealed to Roosevelt to stop the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, writing that Russian and British forces had crossed his borders without notice and bombed open cities.","Britain and the Soviet Union justified the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the danger from German residents in the country and the need to keep open the shortest route to Russia."],"abstract":"The <b>Anglo-Soviet invasion of Iran</b> began on 25 August 1941, when about 40,000 Soviet troops entered from the north and about 19,000 British troops, mostly in Indian brigades, from the west and south, half of the British making straight for the oilfields around Ahwaz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Britain and the Soviet Union had demanded the expulsion of the Germans in Iran, whom Churchill called a fifth column that might seize the oilfields and close the shortest route to Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A joint note of 17 August drew an unsatisfactory reply, and General Wavell was ordered to take the oilfields at Ahwaz and near Khanaqin and then, with Soviet help, the country's communications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the south the British met little opposition and captured all but one of the German and Italian merchant ships in the port of Bandar Shahpur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Reza Shah protested to the envoys that the Germans seemed to want all of Europe and now the Russians and British wanted Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He appealed to Roosevelt that Russian and British forces had crossed his borders without notice and bombed open and undefended cities, and asked him to end these acts of aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 28 August the new prime minister told the Majlis that Iranian forces had been ordered to cease all resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 16 September Reza Shah abdicated in favour of his son, Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, and left the country, and British and Soviet troops entered Tehran the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Both governments agreed to the young shah's succession only on condition of his future good conduct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The terms of the occupation, later written into a treaty of alliance in January 1942, gave Britain and the Soviet Union control of Iran's railways, roads and ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 September 1941, vol. 374, cols. 67–156, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/sep/09/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Iran (Dreyfus) to the Secretary of State, August 25, 1941, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d399. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Shah of Iran (Reza Shah Pahlavi) to President Roosevelt, August 25, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d401. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Iran (Dreyfus) to the Secretary of State, August 28, 1941, 10 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d419. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Minister in Iran (Dreyfus) to the Secretary of State, September 19, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d454. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 13, 251–52, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-13.html. [Open access]","T. H. Vail Motter, <i>The Persian Corridor and Aid to Russia</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Middle East Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1952), 9–11, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub8-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain and the Soviet Union invade a neutral country?","a":"Because they feared that the Germans in Iran would seize the oilfields and close the shortest route by which supplies could reach Russia, and Iran had not expelled them when asked."},{"q":"Why did Reza Shah appeal to Roosevelt?","a":"Because he hoped the president would press the invaders to halt, protesting that they had crossed his borders without notice and bombed open and undefended cities."},{"q":"Why did the occupying powers let his son succeed?","a":"Because both governments were willing to accept him, though only on condition of his future good conduct."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/The_British_Army_in_the_Middle_East_1941_E5327.jpg","title":"Guarding an Iranian refinery, September 1941","desc":"Two Indian riflemen guard an oil refinery in Iran on 4 September 1941, ten days after British and Indian troops crossed the frontier to secure the oilfields.","credit":"Captain G. Keating, No. 1 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museums E 5327, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_British_Army_in_the_Middle_East_1941_E5327.jpg","alt":"Two soldiers in sun helmets, shorts and packs, rifles on their shoulders, stand with their backs to the camera facing a tall tower of pipes and girders and a chimney."},"wiki":"Anglo-Soviet invasion of Iran"},
    "ww2-259": {"questions":["The 1943 declaration of the Big Three at Tehran thanked Iran for helping to move supplies to the Soviet Union, the traffic that ran through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until 1945.","Churchill called the route from the Gulf to the Caspian the surest and shortest way to reach Russia, and as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it carried American aid until 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Persian Corridor</b> was the supply route that ran from the ports at the head of the Persian Gulf across Iran to the Soviet Union, one of five routes by which lend-lease aid reached the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in September 1941 that the route across Persia ran some 800 kilometres (500 miles) from the Gulf to the Caspian and was the surest and shortest way to reach Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Anglo-Soviet occupation of August 1941 put Britain in the south and the Soviet Union in the north in firm control of Iran's railways, roads and ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iran's railway had little capacity and the country was very difficult, and the British official history judges that the route began to deliver large quantities only when American help arrived on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In November 1941 the American War Department planned to ship aircraft to Basra for assembly and delivery to the Russians, and an American assembly plant at Abadan was agreed at the end of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> By late 1942 the United States had become a full partner of the British in the corridor, which rose to second place among the five routes and carried nearly a quarter of the lend-lease tonnage sent to the Soviet Union from the Western Hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 1942 and 1945 British and American agencies delivered about 5.2 million tonnes (5,149,376 long tons) to the Soviets, of which the Americans accounted for about 4.5 million tonnes (4,417,243 long tons).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official American history cites an estimate that by United States Army standards these deliveries were enough to keep sixty combat divisions in the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It calls the operation an experiment in cooperation without precedent, in which one rival occupier of Iran, helped by the United States, supplied the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Tehran conference in December 1943 the three Allied leaders thanked Iran for its help, particularly in moving supplies from overseas to the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["T. H. Vail Motter, <i>The Persian Corridor and Aid to Russia</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Middle East Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1952), 4–6, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub8-1. [Open access]","T. H. Vail Motter, <i>The Persian Corridor and Aid to Russia</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Middle East Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1952), 9–11, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub8-1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 9 September 1941, vol. 374, cols. 67–156, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/sep/09/war-situation. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 13, 252, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-13.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of War (Stimson) to the Secretary of State, November 24, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d479. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Minister in Iran (Dreyfus), December 30, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The British Commonwealth; The Near East and Africa, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v03/d487. [Open access]","\"Declaration of the Three Powers Regarding Iran, December 1, 1943,\" in \"The Tehran Conference, November 28–December 1, 1943,\" Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tehran.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies need a route through Iran at all?","a":"Because it was the surest and shortest way to get supplies to Russia overland from warm water, at a time when the Soviet Union was fighting for its life."},{"q":"Why did the corridor deliver little at first?","a":"Because Iran's railway had little capacity and the mountains and deserts were very difficult, and large deliveries began only when American help arrived on a large scale."},{"q":"Why was the arrangement unusual?","a":"Because Britain and the Soviet Union, old rivals for control of Iran, both occupied it, and one of them, with American help, supplied the other across a country that neither ruled."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Near_East_Iran_-_truck_convoy_of_US_supplies_for_USSR_-_NARA_-_195340.jpg/1920px-Near_East_Iran_-_truck_convoy_of_US_supplies_for_USSR_-_NARA_-_195340.jpg","title":"A supply convoy in Iran, June 1943","desc":"Iranian women sit by the roadside as a halted convoy of American lorries carrying supplies for the Soviet Union stretches into the distance, 5 June 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, US National Archives 195340, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_East_Iran_-_truck_convoy_of_US_supplies_for_USSR_-_NARA_-_195340.jpg","alt":"Three women in dark wraps sit on grass below a raised road where a long line of canvas-covered lorries stands nose to tail, with other figures walking beside them."},"wiki":"Persian Corridor"},
    "ww2-260": {"questions":["An army order of 7 April 1941 explained that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required the invasion of the Soviet Union to be postponed by 4 to 6 weeks, with a new target date of 22 June.","A US Army study concluded that only part of the delay to Barbarossa came from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a late spring left Polish and Russian river valleys flooded into June."],"abstract":"<b>Operation 25</b> was the German name for the campaign against Yugoslavia, whose instructions Hitler laid down in Directive No. 25 on the evening of the Belgrade coup of 27 March 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler's Directive No. 21 of December 1940 had called for all preparations for the invasion of the Soviet Union to be complete by 15 May 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the conference of 27 March he said that the overthrow in Belgrade would have been far more serious had it come during the Russian campaign, and that the start of that campaign would now have to be postponed by up to 4 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Divisions and staffs already moving to the east were diverted to the Balkans, and the armoured units would need at least 3 weeks of refitting in Germany afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 7 April an army order explained that Operation 25 required the invasion of the Soviet Union to be postponed by 4 to 6 weeks, and on 30 April Hitler fixed the new date as 22 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Field Marshal Paulus told the Nuremberg tribunal that the decision to attack Yugoslavia had postponed the invasion by about 5 weeks, to the second half of June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The US Army's study of the Balkan campaigns argued that only part of the delay came from the Balkans, since a late spring left the river valleys of Poland and western Russia flooded into early June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also found that all but three of the nine diverted divisions had been replaced from reserves by the time the invasion began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German writers, it reported, accepted a loss of about 3 weeks but thought it minor beside the weeks wasted in the summer of 1941 by Hitler's quarrel with his generals over strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Authors of other nations, it added, stressed the Yugoslav revolt and the British intervention in Greece and Crete as the cause of the delay and played down the floods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, \"Document 1746-PS: Conference of Hitler and the German High Command on the Situation in Yugoslavia, March 27, 1941,\" <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1746-ps.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"The Aggressive War against the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics,\" <i>Judgment of the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1946), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsov.asp. [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 150–53, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 37, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Friedrich Paulus, testimony of 11 February 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 7 (Nuremberg, 1947), 255, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-11-46.asp. [Open access]","US Department of the Army, <i>The German Campaigns in the Balkans (Spring 1941)</i>, DA Pamphlet 20-260 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1953), 22–24, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-260. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Yugoslav coup affect the timetable for the war against the Soviet Union?","a":"Because divisions and staffs already moving east had to be diverted to the Balkans, and the armoured units then needed weeks of refitting in Germany before they could take part."},{"q":"Why do historians doubt that the Balkans alone delayed Barbarossa?","a":"Because a late spring left the river valleys of Poland and western Russia flooded into June, so an attack on 15 May may not have been possible anyway."},{"q":"Why did German writers think the lost weeks mattered little?","a":"Because they believed the 1941 offensive failed mainly through the summer quarrel between Hitler and his generals over strategy, which cost more time than the Balkan campaigns."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Marsza%C5%82ek_Walther_von_Brauchitsch_podczas_zwiedzania_Akropolu_%282-494%29.jpg/1280px-Marsza%C5%82ek_Walther_von_Brauchitsch_podczas_zwiedzania_Akropolu_%282-494%29.jpg","title":"Brauchitsch on the Acropolis, May 1941","desc":"Field Marshal Walther von Brauchitsch, commander of the German army, whose order of 7 April postponed the invasion of the Soviet Union, tours the Acropolis with officers in May 1941.","credit":"Photograph by Schlickum, Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe 2-494, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marsza%C5%82ek_Walther_von_Brauchitsch_podczas_zwiedzania_Akropolu_(2-494).jpg","alt":"Five German officers in peaked caps and long boots walk across rough ground in front of the tall columns of a ruined Greek temple."},"wiki":"Invasion of Yugoslavia"},
    "ww2-231": {"questions":["Critics in January 1942 complained that holding the office of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as well as the premiership let one man dominate the Chiefs of Staff, but the Commons backed him by 464 votes to one.","Ismay's small staff, the military wing of the War Cabinet secretariat, served the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who in 1941 chaired only 44 of the 462 meetings of the Chiefs of Staff."],"abstract":"The <b>Minister of Defence</b> was the title Winston Churchill created and took for himself on becoming prime minister in May 1940, so that one man both led the government and supervised the conduct of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He later told the Commons that he had sought the King's permission to assume the title because a wartime prime minister could not be separated from the general supervision of the war and the final responsibility for its result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No Ministry of Defence was set up: the three service departments stayed autonomous, and his small staff under General Ismay was the military wing of the War Cabinet secretariat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The old Military Co-ordination Committee became the Defence Committee, meeting in two panels for operations and for supply, and the Minister of Defence chaired both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Supreme authority still lay with the War Cabinet, under whose authority, Sir Stafford Cripps told the Commons in 1942, the Minister of Defence always acted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The arrangement was born in the crisis of the German offensive in the west, and on 13 May 1940, the day Churchill offered nothing but blood, toil, tears and sweat, the Commons backed his new government by 381 votes to none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As war leader he reached into the war economy too, insisting in autumn 1940 on more armoured divisions and in spring 1941 fixing a ceiling on the army's size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The dual office came under attack after the disasters in the Far East, when members argued in January 1942 that one man dominated the Chiefs of Staff and that nobody could call the Minister of Defence to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The House nevertheless voted its confidence in the government by 464 votes to one on 29 January 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill replied in February that the war was run from day to day by the Chiefs of Staff, and that in 1941 he had chaired only 44 of their 462 meetings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["J. R. M. Butler, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1939–June 1941</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 180–81, 247–49, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-2. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"His Majesty's Government,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 13 May 1940, vol. 360, cols. 1501–25, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/13/his-majestys-government-1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Ministerial Changes,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 24 February 1942, vol. 378, cols. 27–176, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/24/ministerial-changes. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Motion of Confidence in His Majesty's Government,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 29 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 927–1019, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/29/motion-of-confidence-in-his-majestys. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 209–23, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-8.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 25 February 1942, vol. 378, cols. 230–322, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/25/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Churchill not create a Ministry of Defence?","a":"Because he saw no need for one: as prime minister he could deal directly with the three service departments, which stayed autonomous, and his small staff came from the War Cabinet secretariat under General Ismay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did members of the Commons attack the dual office in January 1942?","a":"Because after the disasters in the Far East critics argued that one man dominated the Chiefs of Staff and that nobody stood above the Minister of Defence to question him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill tell the Commons how few Chiefs of Staff meetings he chaired?","a":"To show that the professional chiefs, not he, ran the war from day to day: in 1941 he presided at only 44 of their 462 meetings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Winston_Churchill_with_his_Chiefs_of_Staff_at_10_Downing_Street%2C_7_May_1945._Seated_are_Sir_Charles_Portal%3B_Sir_Alan_Brooke%3B_Sir_Andrew_Cunningham._Standing_are_Major_General_L_C_Hollis_and_General_Sir_Hastings_H41826.jpg","title":"Churchill with the Chiefs of Staff, 7 May 1945","desc":"Churchill seated among the three Chiefs of Staff in the garden of 10 Downing Street, with General Ismay, his chief of staff as Minister of Defence, and General Hollis standing behind.","credit":"Photograph by William George Horton, War Office official photographer, Imperial War Museum H 41826, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winston_Churchill_with_his_Chiefs_of_Staff_at_10_Downing_Street,_7_May_1945._Seated_are_Sir_Charles_Portal;_Sir_Alan_Brooke;_Sir_Andrew_Cunningham._Standing_are_Major_General_L_C_Hollis_and_General_Sir_Hastings_H41826.jpg","alt":"Six men posed in a walled garden: four seated in a row, two in uniform and one in naval dress beside a civilian in a dark suit and bow tie, with two army officers standing behind."},"wiki":"Minister of Defence (United Kingdom)"},
    "ww2-232": {"questions":["Scottish listeners complained in 1940 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hard to receive, and the Minister of Information blamed transmitters switched off for security reasons set by the Air Ministry.","Chamberlain's statement of 3 September 1939 that the country was at war went out on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and for millions of listeners it marked the start of the war."],"abstract":"The <b>BBC Home Service</b> was the single national radio service that replaced the BBC's National and Regional Programmes in the first days of September 1939, on the eve of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change was a matter of national security, because planners feared that the signals of the BBC's powerful transmitters would guide enemy aircraft to their targets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The engineers' answer was to synchronise the transmitters, and that need lay behind the creation of one service in place of two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The price was patchy reception: when an MP complained in November 1940 that the Home Service was hard to hear in Scotland, the Minister of Information said transmitters had to go off the air from time to time for security reasons set by the Air Ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chamberlain's statement on 3 September 1939 that the country was at war went out on the Home Service, and for millions of Britons it marked the start of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In wartime the BBC worked under close supervision: its board of governors was cut to speed decisions, and political broadcasts were arranged subject to the advice and control of the Ministry of Information.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The minister told the Commons in July 1940 that the BBC surveyed its audience day by day and that its talks were heard by very large audiences and widely approved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A few broadcasters became stars, among them J. B. Priestley, whose Postscript talks were a sensation, and Churchill, though his broadcasts were fewer than people remember and some famous ones were reports of speeches in Parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The official history of British strategy judged that Churchill's broadcasts rallied the nation and were the most effective weapons of Allied propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Corporation also kept off the air people who had campaigned against the war effort, a policy the minister defended in March 1941 on the ground that speaking on the BBC was a privilege, not a right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David Hendy, \"‘This Country Is at War’,\" <i>The BBC and World War Two</i>, History of the BBC, https://www.bbc.co.uk/historyofthebbc/100-voices/ww2/country-at-war. [Open access]","David Hendy, \"Icons,\" <i>The BBC and World War Two</i>, History of the BBC, https://www.bbc.co.uk/historyofthebbc/100-voices/ww2/icons. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Broadcast Programmes (Scotland),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 20 November 1940, vol. 365, col. 1968, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/nov/20/broadcast-programmes-scotland. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Broadcast Talks,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 17 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 201–2, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/17/broadcast-talks. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"British Broadcasting Corporation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 May 1940, vol. 361, col. 412, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/28/british-broadcasting-corporation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Broadcasting Policy (Political Opinions),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 12 March 1941, vol. 369, cols. 1269–71, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/mar/12/broadcasting-policy-political-opinions. [Open access]","J. R. M. Butler, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1939–June 1941</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 247, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the BBC replace its two services with one?","a":"Because military planners feared that the signals of its powerful transmitters would guide enemy aircraft, and synchronising the transmitters to prevent that meant a single Home Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Home Service hard to hear in parts of Scotland?","a":"Because transmitters had to go off the air from time to time, in whole or in part, for security reasons decided by the Air Ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Minister of Information defend keeping some people off the air?","a":"He argued that speaking on the BBC was a privilege and not a right, and that those who had campaigned against the national war effort could not expect it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"BBC Home Service"},
    "ww2-233": {"questions":["The Statute of Westminster of 1931 named Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, the Irish Free State and Newfoundland, each a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose consent British laws now required.","Canada waited a week for its Parliament before declaring war on 10 September 1939, a week of formal neutrality that marked it as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> free to choose its own course."],"abstract":"A <b>Dominion</b> was one of the self-governing members of the British Commonwealth, and the Statute of Westminster of 1931 named six: Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, the Irish Free State and Newfoundland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The statute laid down that no law of the British Parliament would extend to a Dominion except at its request and with its consent, and it let Dominion parliaments repeal or amend British acts as they applied to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Australia, New Zealand and Newfoundland were exempted from its key sections until their own parliaments chose to adopt them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1914 a British declaration of war had bound the whole Empire, but by 1939 the Dominions were sovereign states, completely free to choose their own course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain's ultimatum to Germany on 3 September 1939 was issued on its own responsibility, although the Dominion governments had been kept informed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Australia and New Zealand declared war without waiting for their parliaments, and Menzies announced by radio that because Britain had declared war, Australia was also at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He held that the King could not be at war and at peace at the same time, calling the idea a metaphysical notion that eluded him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Canada waited for its Parliament, where only four members spoke against taking part, and declared war on 10 September after a week of formal neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In South Africa the Nationalists wanted the country kept out of European wars, but the House of Assembly rejected Hertzog's neutrality by 80 votes to 67 and the Union entered the war on 6 September under Smuts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Eire, nominally still in the Commonwealth under its constitution of 1937, alone declared neutrality and kept diplomatic relations with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, <i>Statute of Westminster 1931</i>, 22 & 23 Geo. 5 c. 4, as enacted, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo5/22-23/4/enacted. [Open access]","J. R. M. Butler, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1939–June 1941</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 2–4, 43–44, 275, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-2. [Open access]","Paul Hasluck, <i>The Government and the People, 1939–1941</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 4, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1952), 152–55, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417687. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>Arms, Men and Governments: The War Policies of Canada, 1939–1945</i> (Ottawa: Queen's Printer, 1970), 8–11, https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/forces/D2-5569E.pdf. [Open access]","Canadian War Museum, \"Canada Goes to War, 1939,\" <i>A Chronology of Canadian Military History</i>, https://www.warmuseum.ca/cwm/exhibitions/chrono/1931goes_to_e.html. [Open access]","David Katz, \"A Case of Arrested Development: The Historiography Relating to South Africa’s Participation in the Second World War,\" <i>Scientia Militaria</i> 40, no. 3 (2012): 280–317, https://doi.org/10.5787/40-3-1034. [Open access]","Ian van der Waag, \"The Union Defence Force between the Two World Wars, 1919–1940,\" <i>Scientia Militaria</i> 30, no. 2 (2000): 183–219, https://doi.org/10.5787/30-2-174. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Canada not go to war on the same day as Britain?","a":"Because as a Dominion it was free to choose: the government left the decision to Parliament, which approved taking part, and Canada declared war on 10 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Menzies declare war without asking Parliament first?","a":"Because he held that the King could not be at war and at peace at once, so Britain's declaration meant that Australia was at war as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did South Africa enter the war only after a vote?","a":"Because its prime minister, Hertzog, wanted neutrality; the House of Assembly rejected it by 80 votes to 67, and Smuts led the Union into the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/W.S._Monroe%2C_J.G._Coates%2C_Stanley_Bruce%2C_Gen._B._Hertzog%2C_W.T._Cosgrave_-_H.M._the_King_with_Imperial_Conference_delegates%2C_1926_LCCN2014713172.jpg","title":"The King with Imperial Conference delegates, 1926","desc":"George V with the leaders of the Dominions at the Imperial Conference of 1926, among them Stanley Bruce of Australia, J. G. Coates of New Zealand, J. B. M. Hertzog of South Africa and W. T. Cosgrave of the Irish Free State.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress, no known restrictions, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W.S._Monroe,_J.G._Coates,_Stanley_Bruce,_Gen._B._Hertzog,_W.T._Cosgrave_-_H.M._the_King_with_Imperial_Conference_delegates,_1926_LCCN2014713172.jpg","alt":"Eight men in white tie and evening dress, three seated in front of a patterned curtain and five standing behind, with names printed along the top and bottom edges."},"wiki":"Dominion"},
    "ww2-234": {"questions":["English Canadians mocked the home-defence conscripts raised under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as 'zombies', and only in November 1944 were 16,000 of them ordered overseas.","A National Registration of everyone aged 16 or over in August 1940 prepared the first call-up of single men aged 21 to 24 under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for 30 days' training."],"abstract":"The <b>National Resources Mobilization Act</b> was the Canadian law of 21 June 1940 that allowed the government to call men up for compulsory military service at home while forbidding compulsory service overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was drafted after the fall of France, when the Cabinet War Committee agreed on 17 June that compulsory service for home defence was desirable, and it became law 4 days after Pétain asked for an armistice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It let the cabinet require people to place themselves, their services and their property at the disposal of the Crown for the defence of Canada, but not to serve outside Canada and its territorial waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A National Registration of everyone aged 16 or over followed on 19–21 August 1940, recording each person's occupation so that workers could be placed in industries vital to the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first call-up, in September 1940, was for 29,750 single men aged 21 to 24 to train for 30 days from 9 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The law kept the pledge given when Canada went to war that only volunteers would serve overseas, so the army came to hold two kinds of soldier: general-service volunteers and conscripts for home defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From April 1941 the conscripts were kept on duty for the rest of the war, and English Canadians who resented them called them 'zombies'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the plebiscite of 27 April 1942, 2,945,514 Canadians voted to release the government from its pledge and 1,643,006 voted against, but Quebec voted 993,663 to 376,188 against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After heavy infantry losses in Normandy and Italy, the government in November 1944 authorised sending 16,000 of the home-defence conscripts overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In all 12,908 went overseas, 2,463 reached units in the field and 69 were killed, so Canada's war overseas remained almost entirely a volunteer effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["C. P. Stacey, <i>Arms, Men and Governments: The War Policies of Canada, 1939–1945</i> (Ottawa: Queen's Printer, 1970), 32–33, 398–402, 474–80, https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/forces/D2-5569E.pdf. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>Six Years of War: The Army in Canada, Britain and the Pacific</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War, vol. 1 (Ottawa: Queen's Printer, 1955), 82, 118–21, https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/forces/D2-501E.pdf. [Open access]","Canadian War Museum, \"Conscription, 1939–1945,\" <i>A Chronology of Canadian Military History</i>, https://www.warmuseum.ca/cwm/exhibitions/chrono/1931conscription_e.html. [Open access]","Canadian War Museum, \"Politics and Government: Conscription,\" <i>Democracy at War: Canadian Newspapers and the Second World War</i>, https://www.warmuseum.ca/cwm/exhibitions/newspapers/canadawar/conscription_e.html. [Open access]","Canada, Department of National War Services, <i>Classification of Occupations: National Registration, 1940</i> (Ottawa: King's Printer, 1940), introduction, https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2017/statcan/12-506/CS12-506B-1940-eng.pdf. [Open access]","Canadian War Museum, \"Canada Goes to War, 1939,\" <i>A Chronology of Canadian Military History</i>, https://www.warmuseum.ca/cwm/exhibitions/chrono/1931goes_to_e.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the act forbid sending conscripts overseas?","a":"Because when Canada went to war the government had promised that only volunteers would serve overseas, and the act was written to keep that pledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Quebec's vote in 1942 matter so much?","a":"Because although Canadians as a whole voted to release the government from its pledge, Quebec voted 993,663 to 376,188 against, dividing the country along the lines of language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were conscripts finally sent overseas in 1944?","a":"Because heavy losses among front-line infantry in Normandy and Italy left units short of reinforcements that volunteers alone could not supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/William_Lyon_Mackenzie_King_voting_on_the_introduction_of_conscription_for_overseas_military_service_-_1942_-_MIKAN_3193315.jpg","title":"Mackenzie King votes in the plebiscite, 27 April 1942","desc":"The Canadian prime minister casting his ballot in Ottawa in the plebiscite that asked to release his government from its pledge against conscription for overseas service.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Library and Archives Canada, MIKAN 3193315, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Lyon_Mackenzie_King_voting_on_the_introduction_of_conscription_for_overseas_military_service_-_1942_-_MIKAN_3193315.jpg","alt":"An elderly man in a suit drops a ballot into a metal box marked Dominion of Canada while a woman in a hat and an older man sit at a table with papers."},"wiki":"National Resources Mobilization Act"},
    "ww2-235": {"questions":["Churchill's promise in August 1940 to cut losses in the Mediterranean if Japan invaded Australia or New Zealand kept Australian planners loyal to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after France fell.","The 10-week campaign that ended in February 1942 shocked Australia and New Zealand, which since 1937 had built their defence plans on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its promised fleet."],"abstract":"The <b>Singapore strategy</b> was Britain's plan between the wars to defend its interests in the East by building a naval base at Singapore and sending the main fleet there from European waters when danger arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1918 Britain judged that it could not keep strong fleets in both hemispheres, so it held the fleet in Europe, ready to reinforce the Far East when required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Australia and New Zealand built their defence on that promise, and in 1937 and again in November 1939 they were told that Singapore would never be allowed to fall and that the Mediterranean would take second place to their security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meanwhile both sent their first troops to the Middle East, and a joint Australian and New Zealand convoy reached the Suez Canal on 12 February 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The fall of France and Italy's entry into the war then removed the French fleet on which Britain had relied in the Mediterranean, and sending a strong fleet east became impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Australia's naval chief told the War Cabinet in June 1940 that the defence of northern Australia hinged on a battle fleet based at Singapore, without which the situation was radically changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In August 1940 Churchill assured both governments that if Japan invaded either on a large scale, Britain would cut its losses in the Mediterranean and come to their aid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Relying on that assurance, Australia's chiefs of staff still judged Singapore the dominant factor in their country's defence, though by early 1941 they thought its relief depended essentially on American naval help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In January 1942 Churchill admitted to the Commons that Britain had never had the power to defend the Far East effectively while fighting Germany and Italy, and Australia demanded a voice in the War Cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Singapore fell in February 1942 after a campaign of only 10 weeks, and for Australia and New Zealand the loss of the bastion on which they had depended came as a shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 1–4, 33–34, 472–73, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]","J. R. M. Butler, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1939–June 1941</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 333–34, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-2. [Open access]","Paul Hasluck, <i>The Government and the People, 1939–1941</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 4, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1952), 221–25, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417688. [Open access]","Paul Hasluck, <i>The Government and the People, 1939–1941</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 4, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1952), 325–27, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417690. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Ministerial Changes,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 24 February 1942, vol. 378, cols. 27–176, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/24/ministerial-changes. [Open access]","Gavin Long, <i>To Benghazi</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1952), 69–71, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417332. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Britain not send its fleet east in 1940?","a":"Because the fall of France removed the French fleet that had been counted on to hold the Mediterranean, so a British fleet had to stay there to watch the Italian navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Australia's chiefs keep faith with the strategy after France fell?","a":"Because Churchill had assured them, on the authority of the Cabinet, that Britain would cut its losses in the Mediterranean and come to their aid if Japan invaded on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Australian planners look to the United States by 1941?","a":"Because they judged that, as things stood, the relief of Singapore depended essentially on American naval assistance rather than on a British fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Arrival_in_Palestine_of_Mr._Antony_%28i.e.%2C_Anthony%29_Eden._Eden_inspecting_A.I.F._%28i.e.%2C_Australian_Imperial_Forces%29_troops_LOC_matpc.20150.jpg","title":"Australian troops in Palestine, February 1940","desc":"Anthony Eden, then Secretary for the Dominions, inspecting men of the Second Australian Imperial Force soon after the first contingent reached the Middle East.","credit":"Matson Photograph Collection, Library of Congress, no known restrictions, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrival_in_Palestine_of_Mr._Antony_(i.e.,_Anthony)_Eden._Eden_inspecting_A.I.F._(i.e.,_Australian_Imperial_Forces)_troops_LOC_matpc.20150.jpg","alt":"A line of soldiers in slouch hats and shorts stands on dusty ground while a tall man in a dark civilian suit walks past them with uniformed officers."},"wiki":"Singapore strategy"},
    "ww2-236": {"questions":["At Sidi Rezegh in November 1941 and at Tobruk in June 1942 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suffered two of the greatest defeats in the history of South African arms.","Bound only to defend the Union, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went to war in 1939 after the House of Assembly rejected Hertzog's neutrality by 80 votes to 67."],"abstract":"The <b>Union Defence Force</b> was the military force of the Union of South Africa, created by the Defence Act of 1912 with a small permanent force, a citizen force of volunteers and a citizen reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1939 it was bound only to serve in defence of the Union, and for some time it was uncertain how far beyond South Africa that duty reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nationalists wanted it built up to protect South Africa's neutrality rather than to fight in European wars, while Smuts argued that the country's first line of defence lay outside its borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> South Africa entered the war on 6 September 1939 only after the House of Assembly rejected Hertzog's neutrality by 80 votes to 67 and Smuts became prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Union was almost totally unprepared, and Smuts had to rebuild the force almost from scratch before Italy entered the war in June 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The legal limit was met with a new attestation form on which volunteers agreed to serve anywhere in Africa, and Kenya, facing Italian East Africa, became the Union's wartime frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of November 1940 about 27,000 of the 77,000 British troops in East Africa were South Africans, and the 1st South African Division had arrived under Major-General George Brink.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moving north to the Western Desert, the force suffered two of the greatest defeats of South African arms, the first being the loss of its 5th Brigade at Sidi Rezegh in November 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In June 1942 the whole 2nd South African Division went into captivity with the Tobruk garrison, about 25,000 men lost in a single day that the War Cabinet had not expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In all about a quarter of 1 million white men were mobilised, together with several hundred thousand women and black South Africans for the auxiliary services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["J. R. M. Butler, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1939–June 1941</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 3, 44, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-2. [Open access]","Ian van der Waag, \"The Union Defence Force between the Two World Wars, 1919–1940,\" <i>Scientia Militaria</i> 30, no. 2 (2000): 183–219, https://doi.org/10.5787/30-2-174. [Open access]","David Katz, \"A Case of Arrested Development: The Historiography Relating to South Africa’s Participation in the Second World War,\" <i>Scientia Militaria</i> 40, no. 3 (2012): 280–317, https://doi.org/10.5787/40-3-1034. [Open access]","D. Conradie, \"South Africa at War, 1912–1982,\" <i>Scientia Militaria</i> 12, no. 2 (1982), https://doi.org/10.5787/12-2-629. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 406–7, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-22.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Central Direction of the War,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 2 July 1942, vol. 381, cols. 527–611, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jul/02/central-direction-of-the-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did South African volunteers sign a new attestation form?","a":"Because the force was bound only to defend the Union, so service further afield needed men who had agreed in writing to serve anywhere in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Kenya the first theatre for South African troops?","a":"Because the threat was Italian expansion in East Africa, and Smuts treated Kenya as South Africa's wartime frontier and its first line of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the surrender at Tobruk shock London?","a":"Because a garrison of about 25,000 men, the whole 2nd South African Division among them, fell in a single day, which neither the War Cabinet nor the Chiefs of Staff had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Union Defence Force (South Africa)"},
    "ww2-237": {"questions":["Chamberlain calculated that Britain needed 20,000 pilots and 30,000 other aircrew a year, so he proposed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Canada, Australia and New Zealand in September 1939.","By March 1945 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had turned out 131,553 aircrew, more than half of them Canadians, from training sites that numbered 231 at their peak."],"abstract":"The <b>British Commonwealth Air Training Plan</b> was the scheme under which Britain, Canada, Australia and New Zealand trained aircrew together, with most advanced training in Canada; in Australia it was called the Empire Air Training Scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chamberlain proposed it to the Dominions on 26 September 1939, calculating that Britain's enlarged air force would need 20,000 pilots and 30,000 other aircrew a year, more than twice what Britain could train.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Canada was the obvious centre because it lay closest to Britain and the war zone, could build military aircraft and was near the aircraft factories of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Air Minister announced the plan to the Commons on 10 October 1939, promising that its graduates would join either Dominion squadrons or the Royal Air Force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A British mission under Lord Riverdale went to Ottawa to settle details with missions from Australia and New Zealand, and Australia dropped a plan to send six squadrons abroad so as to make its largest contribution to the scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> After 7 weeks of bargaining over cost and command, the four-party agreement was signed on 17 December 1939, with the Royal Canadian Air Force in executive command of the plan in Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Canada insisted that its graduates should serve in Canadian squadrons in the field, and the quarrel over that point ran on to the eve of the signing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the divisible cost of advanced training, Canada carried about 81 per cent, Australia 11 per cent and New Zealand 8 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At its peak the plan ran 231 training sites, with more than 10,000 aircraft and over 100,000 military personnel to run it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When it closed in March 1945 it had produced 131,553 aircrew: 72,835 for the RCAF, 42,110 for the RAF, 9,606 for the RAAF and 7,002 for the RNZAF.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["C. P. Stacey, <i>Arms, Men and Governments: The War Policies of Canada, 1939–1945</i> (Ottawa: Queen's Printer, 1970), 17–29, 53–54, https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2009/forces/D2-5569E.pdf. [Open access]","Douglas Gillison, <i>Royal Australian Air Force, 1939–1942</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1962), 79–88, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417583. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 72–73, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RAF-I/UK-RAF-I-3.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War in the Air,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 10 October 1939, vol. 352, cols. 173–86, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/oct/10/war-in-the-air. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Air Training, Canada (Mission),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 11 October 1939, vol. 352, cols. 310–11, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/oct/11/air-training-canada-mission. [Open access]","Canadian War Museum, \"The British Commonwealth Air Training Plan, 1939–1945,\" <i>A Chronology of Canadian Military History</i>, https://www.warmuseum.ca/cwm/exhibitions/chrono/1931air_training_plan_e.html. [Open access]","Paul Hasluck, <i>The Government and the People, 1939–1941</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 4, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1952), 165–66, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417687. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was advanced training concentrated in Canada?","a":"Because Canada was the Dominion closest to Britain and the war zone, could build military aircraft and lay near the aircraft factories of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Canada bargain so hard over squadrons?","a":"Because Canadian opinion would demand that its airmen be seen fighting in Canadian units, and Mackenzie King made the point a condition of signing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain need the Dominions to train its aircrew?","a":"Because the planned air force needed more than twice Britain's own training capacity, given its limited space and exposure to air attack, and Britain also lacked the men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Instructor_and_student%2C_No._2_Service_Flying_Training_School%2C_with_North_American_Harvard_II_aircraft%2C_Uplands%2C_Ont.jpg","title":"Harvard trainers at Uplands, Ontario, July 1941","desc":"An instructor and a pupil of No. 2 Service Flying Training School, Royal Canadian Air Force, beside a line of North American Harvard II advanced trainers.","credit":"Photograph by Nicholas Morant, Library and Archives Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Instructor_and_student,_No._2_Service_Flying_Training_School,_with_North_American_Harvard_II_aircraft,_Uplands,_Ont.jpg","alt":"Two airmen in flying kit with parachute packs talk on an airfield beside a long row of parked single-engined training aircraft."},"wiki":"British Commonwealth Air Training Plan"},
    "ww2-238": {"questions":["Believed to resist malaria well, battalions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 11th East African Division were sent down the Kabaw Valley towards the Chindwin in August 1944.","Volunteers officered by men seconded from British regiments, the six battalions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> formed two brigades in East Africa before Italy entered the war."],"abstract":"The <b>King's African Rifles</b> were the regiment of Britain's East African colonies, and before war with Italy they formed two brigades: three battalions from Kenya and Uganda and three from Tanganyika and Nyasaland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like the other African colonial forces they were almost entirely infantry in peacetime, with men enlisted voluntarily and officers and senior non-commissioned officers seconded from British regiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Italy entered the war in June 1940 the Inspector-General of the African Colonial Forces, Major-General Dickinson, took command of the East Africa Force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> London expected most colonial recruits to serve within their own territories at first, relieving British troops of garrison duty there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pay, allowances and pensions for colonial troops varied from colony to colony, and the East and West African forces were the only colonial forces that still allowed corporal punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> A battalion of the regiment helped defend British Somaliland in August 1940, counter-attacking towards the Mirgo pass and joining the rearguard at Barkasan during the withdrawal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of November 1940 about 33,000 East Africans were serving among the 77,000 British troops in East Africa, the 2nd King's African Rifles among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In August 1944 the 11th East African Division, built round battalions of the regiment, pursued the retreating Japanese down the Kabaw Valley towards the Chindwin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was chosen for that notoriously malarial valley because East Africans were believed to have a high resistance to malaria, and aircraft sprayed DDT over the areas it held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By March 1945 the Allied land forces in South-East Asia included 44,988 East Africans, and a rehabilitation centre for East African soldiers, fitting artificial limbs among other work, had opened in Nairobi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 171–79, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-9.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 406–7, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-22.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Coloured Men (Recruitment),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 21 May 1940, vol. 361, cols. 6–7, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/21/coloured-men-recruitment. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Colonial Troops (Pay, Allowances and Pensions),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 14 July 1943, vol. 391, cols. 182–83, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/jul/14/colonial-troops-pay-allowances-and. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Colonial Troops (Corporal Punishment),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 18 April 1944, vol. 399, cols. 11–12, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/apr/18/colonial-troops-corporal-punishment. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"African Troops (Rehabilitation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, written answers, 7 February 1945, vol. 407, col. 2077W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1945/feb/07/african-troops-rehabilitation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regiment's officers come from British regiments?","a":"Because the African colonial forces enlisted African volunteers as soldiers while their officers and senior non-commissioned officers were seconded from British regiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the 11th East African Division sent into the Kabaw Valley?","a":"Because the valley was notoriously malarial and East Africans were believed to have a high resistance to malaria, backed up by spraying DDT from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did members of Parliament press ministers about African soldiers' conditions?","a":"Because pay and pensions for colonial troops varied from colony to colony, and only the East and West African forces still allowed corporal punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Soldiers_of_the_11th_East_African_Division_crossing_the_River_Chindwin_by_ferry_before_moving_towards_the_village_of_Shwegyin%2C_Burma%2C_December_1944._SE923.jpg","title":"East African troops crossing the Chindwin, December 1944","desc":"Askaris of the 11th East African Division crowded on a ferry across the River Chindwin before moving towards the village of Shwegyin in Burma.","credit":"No 9 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum SE 923, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soldiers_of_the_11th_East_African_Division_crossing_the_River_Chindwin_by_ferry_before_moving_towards_the_village_of_Shwegyin,_Burma,_December_1944._SE923.jpg","alt":"Dozens of African soldiers in bush hats with rifles and packs sit packed together on a flat river craft, some smiling at the camera, with a boat alongside."},"wiki":"King's African Rifles"},
    "ww2-239": {"questions":["Churchill worried that announcing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would revive West Indian fears of transfer to the United States, so he asked Roosevelt to deny any claim to sovereignty.","Under the auspices of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, West Indian Conferences from 1944 brought two delegates from each Caribbean territory to discuss food prices, fisheries and post-war production."],"abstract":"The <b>Anglo-American Caribbean Commission</b> was a joint British and American body created in March 1942 to strengthen social and economic cooperation between the two countries' territories and bases in the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had six members, three named by the president of the United States and three by the British government, and it was to advise both on labour, agriculture, housing, health, education, welfare and finance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two governments took an equal part, with Charles Taussig and Sir Frank Stockdale as joint chairmen, though the United States paid nothing towards development and welfare in the British West Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Talks on economic collaboration had begun earlier, and in February 1942 the Colonial Office said that America's entry into the war had slowed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commission came as American bases were being built in the British islands, and MPs asked in January 1942 why black Americans were said to be refused visas to work there while white Americans were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Churchill feared that the announcement would revive anxiety in many islands about a transfer to the United States, and in January 1942 he reminded Roosevelt of a promise to deny any such aim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the commission was announced on 9 March 1942, Roosevelt duly stated that the United States did not seek sovereignty over the islands on which its bases stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war made the colonies militarily important to the Allies, and in October 1942 their local forces passed from the Colonial Office to the War Office, which grouped them in 1943 into North and South Caribbean Forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In January 1944 the two governments agreed a regular system of West Indian Conferences under the commission, with two representatives from each territory, one of them normally an unofficial member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their agenda covered supplies for development and welfare, the prices of local foodstuffs, food production after the war and fisheries research, and Stockdale chaired the first conference, whose recommendations London then weighed with Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Announcement of the Anglo-American Caribbean Commission,\" March 9, 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/announcement-the-anglo-american-caribbean-commission. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Prime Minister Churchill to President Roosevelt, January 14, 1942,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d258. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Caribbean Colonies,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 4 February 1942, vol. 377, col. 1176, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/04/caribbean-colonies. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Caribbean Commission,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 27 January 1943, vol. 386, cols. 494–95, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/jan/27/caribbean-commission. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Caribbean Conferences,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 January 1944, vol. 396, cols. 181–82, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jan/19/caribbean-conferences. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"West Indian Conference,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 21 June 1944, vol. 401, col. 180, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jun/21/west-indian-conference. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"West Indian Bases (Colour Bar),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 695–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/28/west-indian-bases-colour-bar. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"West Indies (Local Forces),\" <i>Hansard</i>, written answers, 23 November 1943, vol. 393, cols. 1458–59W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1943/nov/23/west-indies-local-forces. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Churchill want a statement from Roosevelt?","a":"Because anxiety persisted in many islands that they might be handed to the United States, and announcing the commission was likely to revive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the West Indian Conferences created?","a":"To associate public opinion in the colonies more directly with the commission's work, through advisory meetings of two representatives from every territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the United States pay for welfare in the British islands?","a":"No: although both governments took an equal part in the commission, the Colonial Secretary told the Commons in 1943 that the United States did not contribute to development and welfare in the British West Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Caribbean Commission"},
    "ww2-240": {"questions":["Britons who surrendered aluminium utensils in July 1940 were answering an appeal that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> insisted did not mean its aircraft programme lacked metal.","Lord Beaverbrook's informal circle of business advisers, led by a Ford executive, ran the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1940, amid bitter rivalry with an Air Ministry whose 'air marshals' he distrusted."],"abstract":"The <b>Ministry of Aircraft Production</b> was the British department set up in May 1940, as Churchill formed his government, to take aircraft production away from the Air Ministry and drive it under Lord Beaverbrook.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It grew out of the Air Ministry's production department at Harrogate, whose output in the early months of 1940 was already rising fast and running ahead of programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beaverbrook thought the men he called 'air marshals' unsuited to running production, and he ran the ministry through an informal group of business advisers headed by Mr Hennessy of the Ford Motor Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations with the Air Ministry were bitter, each side resenting the other's claims and methods, and the prime minister was often called in to mediate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Output was secret: in June 1940 the ministry's parliamentary secretary told the Commons that it would not be in the public interest to disclose the figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1940 the ministry agreed that until the end of September all effort would go into five types already in quantity production, the Wellington, Whitley, Blenheim, Hurricane and Spitfire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To speed output, technical staff at the factories were given wider power to release equipment not exactly to specification, and stocks of spares were turned into new aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deliveries of new fighters rose from 256 in April to 467 in September, more than enough to cover losses, and Fighter Command ended the Battle of Britain with more aircraft than it had at the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The public joined in through an appeal for aluminium utensils, which the ministry insisted in July 1940 did not mean it lacked metal for its programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The spurt borrowed from the future and unsettled later output, and by the summer of 1941, when a front line of 4,000 bombers was being planned, Beaverbrook had moved to the Ministry of Supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), 115–25, 136–38, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-4.html. [Open access]","Denis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 151–53, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RAF-I/UK-RAF-I-6.html. [Open access]","J. R. M. Butler, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1939–June 1941</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 251–54, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-2. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 209–23, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-8.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Aircraft Production,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 5 June 1940, vol. 361, cols. 829–30, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/05/aircraft-production. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Aircraft Production,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 19 June 1940, vol. 362, cols. 143–44, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/19/aircraft-production. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Aluminium,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 17 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 192–94, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/17/aluminium. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the ministry concentrate on only five aircraft types?","a":"To get the greatest number of machines into the air at once, which meant giving overriding priority to the types already in quantity production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the 1940 spurt cause trouble later?","a":"Because stocks of materials and components and reserves of capacity were used up at once, sacrificing future output to the needs of the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the official historian of the RAF qualify Beaverbrook's credit?","a":"Because output had already risen in April and May before the ministry existed, and the gains of the summer were the achievement of the whole aircraft industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Minister of Aircraft Production"},
    "ww2-371": {"questions":["General Antonov told the Yalta conference that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been brought forward to mid-January because of the German attack in the Ardennes, despite fog that grounded aircraft.","Soldiers of the 60th Army reached the gates of Auschwitz on 27 January 1945 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as Konev's First Ukrainian Front swept through Upper Silesia towards the Oder."],"abstract":"The <b>Vistula–Oder offensive</b> was the Red Army's winter attack of January 1945, in which the First Belorussian and First Ukrainian Fronts broke out of their bridgeheads on the Vistula and drove across Poland to the Oder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Stavka planned it as the opening of a campaign to end the war, allowing about 15 days for the breakthrough and a further 30 for a drive on to Berlin and the Elbe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the front the railways of eastern Poland had been converted to the Russian gauge, and the First Belorussian Front alone stockpiled 2.5 million shells in its Magnuszew bridgehead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Konev's artillery opened on the Baranow bridgehead before dawn on 12 January, Zhukov attacked out of the Magnuszew and Pulawy bridgeheads 2 days later, and Warsaw fell on 17 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Konev's front crossed the German border east of Breslau on 21 January, took the Upper Silesian industrial region almost undamaged, and its soldiers opened the gates of Auschwitz on 27 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Speed was the point: in 18 days, General Antonov told the Yalta conference, Soviet troops had advanced up to 500 kilometres (310 miles), an average of 25 to 30 kilometres a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He said the attack had been ordered for mid-January without waiting for better weather, because of the Allies' difficulties after the German offensive in the Ardennes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 18 January that Marshal Stalin was very punctual and would rather be before his time than late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By 31 January the First Belorussian Front stood on the Oder north of Küstrin, about 40 miles (65 kilometres) from Berlin, before Stalin halted Zhukov and Konev to secure their flanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The losses are contested: Antonov claimed about 400,000 German soldiers killed, wounded or captured, German army records counted 660,000 German casualties in January and February, and German intelligence put Soviet casualties in January at 680,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 416–27, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 428, 439–40, 456, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet-German War 1941–1945: Myths and Realities; A Survey Essay</i>, paper presented at the Strom Thurmond Institute of Government and Public Affairs, Clemson University, 11 October 2001, https://archive.org/details/the-soviet-german-war-1941-1945-myths-and-realities-david-m-glantz-1001. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, Yalta, February 4, 1945, 5 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 581–83, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d330. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Day of Liberation,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/liberation/day-of-liberation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation and Foreign Policy,” <i>Hansard</i>, 18 January 1945, vol. 407, cols. 376–493, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/jan/18/war-situation-and-foreign-policy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the offensive launched in fog that grounded the Soviet air force?","a":"Because, as Antonov told the Yalta conference, the High Command chose not to wait for better weather once the German attack in the Ardennes had put the Western Allies in difficulty. An American official history adds that it did Stalin no harm to arrive at Yalta with his armies on the move and Poland in his hands."},{"q":"Why did the German front in Poland collapse so quickly?","a":"The divisions facing the Baranow bridgehead held a chain of strongpoints with roughly one man for every 15 yards. Hitler rejected his generals' plan to pull back before the barrage and sent his main reserve, the Sixth SS Panzer Army, to Hungary rather than to the Vistula."},{"q":"Why did the Red Army stop on the Oder instead of driving on to Berlin?","a":"Stalin worried about the long open flank between Zhukov's and Rokossovsky's fronts and about German forces in Pomerania and Silesia, so on 2 February he ordered a halt. Konev and Rokossovsky later argued that Berlin could have been taken in February."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%8F%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%83%D1%8E_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%83_%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B7_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%9E%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80_%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D1%81_%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D1%80%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%BC_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC.jpg","title":"Soviet sappers bridging the Oder, January 1945","desc":"Soviet engineers building a pontoon crossing over the Oder beside a wrecked bridge on 22 January 1945, during the Vistula–Oder offensive.","credit":"Photograph by Semyon Alperin, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%8F%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%83%D1%8E_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D1%83_%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B7_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%9E%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%80_%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D1%81_%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D1%80%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%BC_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in greatcoats and caps heave timber and steel beams in misty water beneath the broken span of a large bridge."},"wiki":"Vistula–Oder offensive"},
    "ww2-372": {"questions":["Kolberg, the subject of Goebbels' most expensive film, held out until 18 March 1945 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which also cost Germany the ports of Gdynia and Danzig.","General Antonov warned at Yalta that the Germans would ship their Courland divisions to hold the Baltic flank, which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> then cleared between Stettin and Danzig."],"abstract":"The <b>East Pomeranian offensive</b> was the Soviet campaign of February to April 1945 that cleared German forces from Pomerania and West Prussia, the Baltic flank north of the Red Army's bridgeheads on the Oder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the January advance the Stavka gave Rokossovsky's Second Belorussian Front the task of occupying West Prussia and Pomerania from Danzig to Stettin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Facing it was Army Group Vistula under Heinrich Himmler, which the German Historical Museum describes as hastily formed, mainly from training, police and Volkssturm units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rokossovsky's first push from 10 February made slow progress through woods and swamps softened by the thaw, and he halted it on 19 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antonov had told the Yalta conference that the Germans would try to defend Pomerania with divisions shipped from Courland, and the Soviet command came to see the region as a threat to the road to Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That threat seemed real on 16 February, when an improvised German force struck south from Stargard in Operation Solstice, which an American army history calls one of the war's closest approaches to a planned fiasco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gained at most a few miles before stalling in mud and minefields, yet on 17 February the Stavka dropped its plan to go straight for Berlin and turned Zhukov north to help Rokossovsky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhukov struck on 1 March and his tanks reached the coast on 4 March, splitting the German armies and cutting off Kolberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kolberg, whose stand against Napoleon in 1807 Goebbels had made into the most expensive film of the Nazi era, held out until 18 March while about 80,000 inhabitants and refugees left by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gdynia fell on 28 March and Danzig 2 days later, but clearing the flank had taken about 6 weeks in which Berlin seems to have dropped out of the Stavka's sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 439–48, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 456–60, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet-German War 1941–1945: Myths and Realities; A Survey Essay</i>, paper presented at the Strom Thurmond Institute of Government and Public Affairs, Clemson University, 11 October 2001, https://archive.org/details/the-soviet-german-war-1941-1945-myths-and-realities-david-m-glantz-1001. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, Yalta, February 4, 1945, 5 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 581–83, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d330. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Verena Keilen, “Film ‘Kolberg’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kunst-und-kultur/film-kolberg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a failed German counter-attack matter so much?","a":"An American official history argues that Operation Solstice, though it gained only a few miles, hit the most fragile part of the Soviet plan. On 17 February the Stavka dropped the idea of driving straight on Berlin and turned Zhukov's front north against Army Group Vistula."},{"q":"Why was Himmler a poor choice to command Army Group Vistula?","a":"He arrived with no staff, no communications and almost no troops, and soon moved his headquarters behind the Oder, well away from the front. Guderian forced him to accept Wenck as a minder for the offensive, and he gave up the command in March."},{"q":"Why did Kolberg carry special meaning in 1945?","a":"Goebbels had commissioned the film Kolberg, about the town's defence against Napoleon, as a call to hold out, and it opened on 30 January 1945. Weeks later the real town was cut off and fell on 18 March after about 80,000 people had been taken off by sea."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Za%C5%9Blubiny_z_morzem_-_5-ta_Brygada_Artylerii_Ci%C4%99%C5%BCkiej.jpg","title":"Polish soldiers on the beach at Kołobrzeg, March 1945","desc":"Soldiers of the 5th Heavy Artillery Brigade of the Polish army taking part in the ceremony of 'marriage to the sea' at Kołobrzeg, the former Kolberg, after the town fell in March 1945.","credit":"Photograph probably by Tadeusz Delawski, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Za%C5%9Blubiny_z_morzem_-_5-ta_Brygada_Artylerii_Ci%C4%99%C5%BCkiej.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in long coats stand at the edge of a flat, wet beach, one holding a flag on a pole, with a harbour pier on the horizon."},"wiki":"East Pomeranian offensive"},
    "ww2-373": {"questions":["The sinking of the liner Wilhelm Gustloff north of the Stolpe Bank on 30 January 1945 became the most notorious loss of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by sea across the Baltic.","By mid-February 1945 about 1,300,000 of the province's 2,300,000 people had left in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them walking to Danzig along the Frische Nehrung and through the Vistula delta."],"abstract":"The <b>evacuation of East Prussia</b> was the flight of German civilians and soldiers from Germany's easternmost province, overland and across the Baltic, as the Red Army closed in during the winter and spring of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Treks of refugees had been moving west since October 1944, after crimes committed by Red Army soldiers against German civilians became known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Soviet offensive cut the province off in late January 1945, an American army history writes, the treks for the first time were made up of Germans and were propelled by sheer terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commander of the Fourth Army, Friedrich Hossbach, planned on 24 January to break out to the west with his whole army, accepting that the civilians would have to stay behind, and was soon replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By mid-February about 1,300,000 of the province's 2,300,000 people had left, some by boat but most on foot to Danzig along the Frische Nehrung and through the Vistula delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The sea route was not at first a priority, for on 22 January Hitler agreed with Admiral Dönitz that scarce coal must go to military transport and not to evacuating refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dönitz ordered oil-fired vessels and the cruiser Emden to carry refugees where they could be spared, and by 28 January 62,000 people from East and West Prussia had been taken west by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German Historical Museum counts nearly 800 warships and merchant ships gathered by the navy, which by May had carried about 1.5 million civilians and 500,000 soldiers from East Prussia, Pomerania and Courland to Denmark and Schleswig-Holstein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Losses were heavy and remain uncertain: about 80 ships were sunk, among them the Wilhelm Gustloff with roughly 4,100 people aboard by one officer's later count, and no passenger lists could be kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dönitz told the Nuremberg tribunal in 1946 that the navy's surface fleet had been concentrated in the Baltic to carry troops, wounded and above all refugees, and that he fought on in the East in May to bring them west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 424–32, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-1945. [in German] [Open access]","“Fuehrer Conferences on Naval Affairs, 1945,” in <i>Brassey’s Naval Annual 1948</i>, ed. H. G. Thursfield (New York: Macmillan, 1948), 422–80, https://archive.org/details/brasseys-naval-annual-1948-ocr. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, proceedings of 9 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-09-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, proceedings of 10 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-10-46.asp. [Open access]","Gerhard Hynitzsch, “Die letzten Tage der ‘Cap Arcona’,” <i>Deutsches Schiffahrtsarchiv</i> 22 (1999): 189–98, https://ww2.dsm.museum/DSA/DSA22_1999_189198_Hynitzsch.pdf. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler at first refuse coal for refugee ships?","a":"Dönitz reported that only 3 weeks' coal was left for sea transport, which was needed to bring divisions from Courland and Norway. Hitler decided that the remaining coal must be kept for military operations, and the navy was told to use only oil-burning ships that could be spared."},{"q":"Why was the crossing of the Baltic so dangerous?","a":"Soviet submarines could operate almost undisturbed, Dönitz complained after the Wilhelm Gustloff was sunk, because there were no German aircraft left to hunt them and too few escorts to do more than guard the convoys directly."},{"q":"Why are the death tolls of the sinkings still uncertain?","a":"Ships were loaded in haste with refugees and wounded, and there was no time to draw up passenger lists, so the number of people aboard any one of them, and the number drowned, can only be estimated."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1990-001-30%2C_Fl%C3%BCchtlingstreck.jpg","title":"A refugee trek on the ice of the Curonian Lagoon, January 1945","desc":"East Prussian refugees with horse-drawn wagons crossing the frozen Curonian Lagoon in January 1945, with a dead horse in the foreground.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1990-001-30, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1990-001-30,_Fl%C3%BCchtlingstreck.jpg","alt":"A line of horse-drawn carts crosses a vast snowy plain of ice under a white sky, past the dark body of a horse lying in the foreground."},"wiki":"Evacuation of East Prussia"},
    "ww2-374": {"questions":["Taboo for decades in East Germany, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1945 is now misused by neo-Nazis, whose so-called funeral marches claim the town's dead for their own ends.","Few of the suicides that swept eastern Germany at the end of the war took as many lives as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the exact number of the dead is still unknown."],"abstract":"The <b>mass suicide in Demmin</b> was the death of hundreds of the small Pomeranian town's people by their own hand as the Red Army arrived in the spring of 1945, one of many such suicides in eastern Germany in the last weeks of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet soldiers entering Germany had been prepared with a new theme of vengeance, spread in meetings, slogans, roadside signs and leaflets written by prominent authors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Political officers told stories of German crimes against Russian women and children, so that each soldier would feel he had a personal score to settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American army history describes the Soviet vengeance on German civilians as swift, personal and merciless, with rape, arson, pillage and murder accompanying the march into eastern Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German Historical Museum notes that those overtaken by Soviet troops faced murder, deportation and rape, and that rape and robbery were everyday events in the eastern regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In the spring of 1945 Demmin, a small town in what is now Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania, became the scene of a mass suicide as hundreds of its inhabitants took their own lives while the Red Army advanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parents killed their children and then themselves, and whole families drowned together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mass suicides took place across Germany in the last days of the war, above all in the east, but few places saw as many people kill themselves as Demmin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In East Germany the subject was taboo for decades, and the exact number of the dead is still not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deaths are now also claimed by neo-Nazis, who use a so-called funeral march through the town to bend its memory to their own purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, “Über Leben in Demmin,” bpb Mediathek, https://www.bpb.de/mediathek/290178/ueber-leben-in-demmin. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 420, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 424, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Germans in the east so afraid of the Red Army's arrival?","a":"Soviet troops had been taught that they were coming for vengeance, and the treks of refugees heading west since the autumn of 1944 carried news of murder, deportation and rape among those the front overtook."},{"q":"Why is the number of dead still unknown?","a":"For decades the subject could not be discussed openly in East Germany, so no count was made while witnesses were young, and the exact figure has never been established."},{"q":"Why is remembering Demmin so contentious?","a":"Neo-Nazis use the deaths for marches that present Germans only as victims, while the American history the card cites warns that weighing German crimes in the Soviet Union against Soviet crimes in Germany is profitless: both fell on people who were mostly innocent."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Demmin_Findling_Freitote_1945_Friedhof.JPG","title":"The memorial stone in Demmin cemetery","desc":"A boulder in the cemetery at Demmin with a plaque recording that hundreds of known and unknown victims of the Demmin tragedy of May 1945 lie there in a mass grave and single graves, photographed in 2017.","credit":"Photograph by Wikswat, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demmin_Findling_Freitote_1945_Friedhof.JPG","alt":"A large grey boulder with a brass plaque, set on bare earth and fallen leaves, with red flowers planted along its base."},"wiki":"Demmin mass suicide"},
    "ww2-375": {"questions":["A Soviet tank detachment drove through Elbing with its headlights on during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and reached the Baltic coast on 23 January 1945, cutting off Army Group Centre.","Stalin had told Churchill and Roosevelt at Tehran that he wanted Königsberg as an ice-free port, a city his armies took in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Potsdam then assigned to the Soviet Union."],"abstract":"The <b>East Prussian offensive</b> was the Soviet campaign of January to April 1945 that destroyed the German army group holding East Prussia and ended with the capture of Königsberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Antonov described it at Yalta as a subsidiary attack meant to draw German strength from the centre, with Chernyakhovsky's forces advancing on Königsberg and Rokossovsky's cutting East Prussia off along the Vistula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Third Belorussian Front opened in earnest on 13 January at Stallupönen and Pillkallen, and the defenders held at first in fog that kept Soviet aircraft and armour out of play.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler read this as near-victory and took Panzer Corps Grossdeutschland away to Army Group A, and on 18 January the Second Army's front broke near Mława.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the night of 23 January a Soviet tank detachment drove through Elbing with its headlights on and reached the coast, isolating the German army group from the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Stalin had long wanted this ground: at Tehran in 1943 he said he would accept the Curzon Line in Poland if the Soviet Union received northern East Prussia with Tilsit and Königsberg, an ice-free port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler refused withdrawals, dismissed the army group commander Reinhardt for ordering one, and told his successor Rendulic to hold Königsberg and what was left of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chernyakhovsky was killed in the February fighting, and Vasilevsky took command of a battle for what the Russians called the cradle of German militarism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vasilevsky threw four armies at Königsberg on 6 April and the city surrendered on 9 April, after which Hitler had its commandant, Otto Lasch, condemned to death in absentia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Potsdam that summer the Allies agreed in principle to the eventual transfer of Königsberg and the area around it to the Soviet Union, subject to expert examination of the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 428–32, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 444–45, 470, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet-German War 1941–1945: Myths and Realities; A Survey Essay</i>, paper presented at the Strom Thurmond Institute of Government and Public Affairs, Clemson University, 11 October 2001, https://archive.org/details/the-soviet-german-war-1941-1945-myths-and-realities-david-m-glantz-1001. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, Yalta, February 4, 1945, 5 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 581–83, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d330. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Bohlen Minutes, Tripartite Political Meeting, December 1, 1943, 6 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, 596–604, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d379. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-1945. [in German] [Open access]","“Protocol of the Proceedings of the Berlin Conference, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin care so much about Königsberg?","a":"At Tehran he said that northern East Prussia would give the Soviet Union an ice-free port and a small piece of German territory that he felt it deserved, and he made it the price of accepting the Curzon Line. The Potsdam conference agreed to the transfer in principle."},{"q":"Why did the German defence collapse after a promising start?","a":"Hitler took Panzer Corps Grossdeutschland away when the front seemed to be holding, refused to let the Fourth Army pull back, and so left the army group's flank open to the dash through Elbing to the sea."},{"q":"Why did the fighting drag on until April?","a":"Heavy snow followed by a sudden thaw snarled Soviet supply, two Soviet commands got in each other's way in a small area, and a German spoiling attack in February reopened a link to Königsberg."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/%D0%90%D0%B2%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%90._%D0%90%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%8E_%D1%8E%D0%B3%D0%BE-%D0%B2%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B5_%D0%9A%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B3%D1%81%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B0.jpg","title":"Soviet soldiers fighting south-east of Königsberg, February 1945","desc":"Soviet submachine gunners of Lieutenant A. Asonychev's unit fighting for a village south-east of Königsberg on 20 February 1945, during the East Prussian offensive.","credit":"Photograph by Leonid Dorensky, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%90%D0%B2%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%90._%D0%90%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%8E_%D1%8E%D0%B3%D0%BE-%D0%B2%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B5_%D0%9A%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B3%D1%81%D0%B1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"Soldiers with guns advance over heaps of rubble towards a burning, roofless building whose wall reads Hotel Schloss."},"wiki":"East Prussian offensive"},
    "ww2-376": {"questions":["In a secret agreement signed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Stalin promised to enter the war against Japan within 3 months of Germany's surrender in return for southern Sakhalin and the Kuril Islands.","Roosevelt told Congress on 1 March 1945 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ought to spell the end of spheres of influence and balances of power, expedients that had always failed."],"abstract":"The <b>Yalta Conference</b> was the meeting of Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin in the Crimea from 4 to 11 February 1945, the second summit of the Big Three after Tehran, held at Stalin's invitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came as the Red Army stood on the Oder, and General Antonov opened the military talks by describing an advance of up to 500 kilometres (310 miles) in 18 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The leaders agreed that each power would occupy a zone of Germany under a central Control Commission in Berlin, with a zone for France formed out of the British and American zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They wrote the possible dismemberment of Germany into the surrender terms, and passed a Soviet proposal of 20 billion dollars in reparations, half for the Soviet Union, to a commission in Moscow over British objections to naming any figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They also settled the voting formula for the Security Council and called the founding conference of the United Nations for 25 April 1945 in San Francisco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On Poland, Churchill told the others that for Britain, which had gone to war to protect Poland, it was a matter of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stalin replied that for Russia it was a matter of honour and of security, because Poland had been the corridor through which Germany had attacked twice in 30 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The settlement put Poland's eastern frontier on the Curzon Line and called for the Soviet-backed provisional government to be reorganised on a broader democratic basis and pledged to free and unfettered elections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A secret agreement promised Soviet entry into the war against Japan 2 or 3 months after Germany's surrender, in return for southern Sakhalin, the Kuril Islands and rights in Manchuria, and was published only in February 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt told Congress the conference should spell the end of spheres of influence and unilateral action, and Churchill told the Commons he knew of no government that kept its obligations more solidly than the Soviet one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “Communiqué Issued at the End of the Conference: Report of the Crimea Conference, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 968–75, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d500. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Protocol of the Proceedings of the Crimea Conference, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 975–82, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d501. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Agreement Regarding Entry of the Soviet Union Into the War Against Japan, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 984, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d503. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Bohlen Minutes, Third Plenary Meeting, February 6, 1945, 4 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 667–71, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d352. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, Yalta, February 4, 1945, 5 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 581–83, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d330. [Open access]","Christina Hoor, “Konferenz von Jalta,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/konferenz-von-jalta. [in German] [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Address to Congress on the Yalta Conference,” 1 March 1945, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-congress-the-yalta-conference. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Crimea Conference,” <i>Hansard</i>, 27 February 1945, vol. 408, cols. 1267–345, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/feb/27/crimea-conference. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Churchill and Roosevelt accept so much of what Stalin wanted?","a":"The Red Army's winter offensive had already put Poland and Germany's eastern provinces in Soviet hands, and the German Historical Museum suggests that this, with Roosevelt's failing health, probably made the Western leaders readier to compromise."},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt want the Soviet Union in the war against Japan?","a":"Securing Soviet entry into the Pacific war was one of his main aims, and Stalin agreed to declare war on Japan within about 3 months of Germany's defeat in return for territory in the Far East, set down in a secret agreement."},{"q":"Why did France get a zone of Germany without being invited to Yalta?","a":"Stalin agreed only reluctantly, on condition that the French zone was carved out of the areas planned for the Americans and British, so that the Soviet zone was left untouched."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Yalta_summit_1945_with_Churchill%2C_Roosevelt%2C_Stalin.jpg/1920px-Yalta_summit_1945_with_Churchill%2C_Roosevelt%2C_Stalin.jpg","title":"The Big Three at Yalta, February 1945","desc":"Churchill, Roosevelt and Stalin seated together at the Yalta Conference on 9 February 1945, with their foreign ministers and military chiefs standing behind them.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yalta_summit_1945_with_Churchill,_Roosevelt,_Stalin.jpg","alt":"Three older men sit side by side on a terrace, one in a greatcoat with a cigar, one in a dark cape and one in a military uniform, with officers in caps behind them."},"wiki":"Yalta Conference"},
    "ww2-377": {"questions":["Drawn in 1919 to mark the eastern limit of indisputably Polish territory, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its name a year later from Lord Curzon, who put it to both warring sides.","Roosevelt told Congress that the Poles would be compensated in the north and west for what they lost by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since the people east of it were mainly Belorussian and Ukrainian."],"abstract":"The <b>Curzon Line</b> was a proposed boundary between Poland and Russia, drawn after the First World War, running from Dünaburg through Brest to Przemyśl, and adopted at Yalta in 1945 as the basis of Poland's eastern frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Anthony Eden told the Commons that it was drawn in 1919 by the Commission on Polish Affairs of the Supreme Council in Paris, to mark the eastern limit of territory that was indisputably Polish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became linked with Lord Curzon only a year later, Eden said, when the proposal was put to both sides in an attempt to end the Polish-Soviet fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The frontier of the interwar years lay farther east, and at Tehran in 1943 Molotov claimed that the Soviet border of 1939, which Eden called the Ribbentrop–Molotov Line, was the Curzon Line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stalin offered there to accept the Curzon Line if the Soviet Union received northern East Prussia with Königsberg, and marked his proposal in red pencil on an American map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Yalta Roosevelt asked whether Lwów and its oil might be left to Poland, and Churchill, who had backed the line in Parliament, said such a gesture would be admired as an act of magnanimity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stalin answered that Curzon and Clemenceau, not the Russians, had fixed the line, and that he could not return to Moscow less Russian than they had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The protocol of 11 February declared that the eastern frontier should follow the Curzon Line with digressions of 5 to 8 kilometres in Poland's favour, while Poland would gain substantial territory in the north and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roosevelt defended this to Congress on the ground that the people east of the line were mainly Belorussian and Ukrainian, and Churchill told the Commons he backed it as the fairest division possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Poland's western frontier was left to a peace conference, and at Potsdam the former German lands east of the Oder and western Neisse were placed under Polish administration until that final settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Poland,” <i>Hansard</i>, 15 December 1944, vol. 406, cols. 1478–578, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/dec/15/poland. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Bohlen Minutes, Tripartite Political Meeting, December 1, 1943, 6 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, 596–604, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d379. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Bohlen Minutes, Third Plenary Meeting, February 6, 1945, 4 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 667–71, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d352. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Protocol of the Proceedings of the Crimea Conference, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 975–82, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d501. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Crimea Conference,” <i>Hansard</i>, 27 February 1945, vol. 408, cols. 1267–345, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/feb/27/crimea-conference. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Address to Congress on the Yalta Conference,” 1 March 1945, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-congress-the-yalta-conference. [Open access]","Christina Hoor, “Konferenz von Jalta,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/konferenz-von-jalta. [in German] [Open access]","“Protocol of the Proceedings of the Berlin Conference, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British government back a line the Polish government in London rejected?","a":"Churchill told the Commons that the line had been drawn in 1919 by an expert commission at a time when Russia had few friends among the Allies, and that after Russia's sufferings he thought it the fairest division possible, not a surrender to force."},{"q":"Why could Stalin argue that the line was not a Russian invention?","a":"It had been drawn at the Paris peace conference, where the Russians were not represented, and at Yalta he reminded the others that Curzon and Clemenceau had fixed it against Russia's will."},{"q":"Why was Poland promised German land in the west?","a":"The Yalta protocol balanced the loss of the eastern lands with substantial accessions of territory in the north and west, and Roosevelt presented it to Congress as compensation for what Poland gave up east of the line."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Curzon_line_en.svg/1024px-Curzon_line_en.svg.png","title":"Poland's frontiers and the Curzon Line","desc":"A modern map showing the Curzon Line, the Polish-Soviet frontier of 1945–47, the eastern lands annexed by the Soviet Union in 1939 and the German territories placed under Poland in 1945.","credit":"Map by radek.s, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curzon_line_en.svg","alt":"A map of Poland with a blue line running north to south down its eastern side, grey areas to the east and pink areas along the western and northern edges."},"wiki":"Curzon Line","places":["city:1159150351"]},
    "ww2-378": {"questions":["The Republican platform of 1952 promised to repudiate secret understandings such as those of Yalta, giving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argument a place in American party politics.","Speaking at Riga in 2005, President Bush placed Yalta in the unjust tradition of Munich and the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact, a late official echo of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> charge."],"abstract":"<b>Western betrayal</b> is the charge that Britain and the United States abandoned allies in central and eastern Europe to the Soviet Union, made above all about the Yalta agreements of February 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Yalta the three powers put Poland's eastern frontier on the Curzon Line and agreed that the Soviet-backed provisional government would be broadened and pledged to free elections, rather than restoring the government in exile in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a Declaration on Liberated Europe they also promised interim governments broadly representative of all democratic elements and the earliest possible free elections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics doubted from the start that such promises could be enforced, while Churchill told the Commons he declined to discuss Russian good faith and knew of no government that kept its obligations more solidly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A further agreement on Soviet entry into the war against Japan, which handed over the Kuril Islands and southern Sakhalin, stayed secret until February 1946, and secret understandings became a theme of later attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The first parliamentary protest came on 28 February 1945, when the member for Penryn and Falmouth moved an amendment regretting the transfer of an ally's territory to another power contrary to treaty and to the Atlantic Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called the settlement the fifth partition of Poland, and the amendment was defeated by 396 votes to 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the United States the charge became a weapon against Roosevelt's record, and the Republican platform of 1952 called Tehran, Yalta and Potsdam the scenes of tragic blunders and promised to repudiate secret understandings that aided Communist enslavement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Books such as Felix Wittmer's <i>The Yalta Betrayal</i> of 1953 argued that the pact itself betrayed American principles, and President Eisenhower told Congress that year that he would seek a resolution rejecting any such secret commitment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The charge outlived the Cold War, and in 2005 President George W. Bush said at Riga that the agreement at Yalta followed in the unjust tradition of Munich and the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Crimea Conference,” <i>Hansard</i>, 28 February 1945, vol. 408, cols. 1416–519, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/feb/28/crimea-conference. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Crimea Conference,” <i>Hansard</i>, 27 February 1945, vol. 408, cols. 1267–345, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/feb/27/crimea-conference. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Protocol of the Proceedings of the Crimea Conference, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 975–82, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d501. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Agreement Regarding Entry of the Soviet Union Into the War Against Japan, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 984, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d503. [Open access]","“Republican Party Platform of 1952,” 7 July 1952, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/republican-party-platform-1952. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, “Annual Message to the Congress on the State of the Union,” 2 February 1953, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/annual-message-the-congress-the-state-the-union-16. [Open access]","Felix Wittmer, <i>The Yalta Betrayal: Data on the Decline and Fall of Franklin Delano Roosevelt</i> (Caldwell, ID: Caxton Printers, 1953), https://archive.org/details/yaltabetrayaldat010842mbp. [Open access]","George W. Bush, “Remarks in Riga,” 7 May 2005, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-riga. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did critics call Yalta a betrayal rather than a defeat?","a":"Because Britain had gone to war over the defence of Poland, and the protesting MPs argued that agreeing to hand Polish territory to another power broke treaty obligations and the Atlantic Charter's promise that peoples would choose their own government."},{"q":"Why did Churchill defend the agreement so strongly?","a":"He told the Commons that he believed the Soviet leaders wished to live in honourable friendship with the Western democracies, that their word was their bond, and that a schism between East and West would be a disaster for mankind."},{"q":"Why did Yalta become an issue in American party politics?","a":"After 1945 Republicans used it against the Democratic record, the 1952 platform promised to repudiate secret understandings such as those of Yalta, and Eisenhower repeated the pledge in his first State of the Union message."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Livadiya_Conference.JPG","title":"The conference table at Livadia","desc":"The round table in the Livadia Palace near Yalta at which the agreements of February 1945 were negotiated, photographed in 2002 with framed pictures of the conference on the wall.","credit":"Photograph by Podvalov, GPL 2.0 or later, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livadiya_Conference.JPG","alt":"A round table with a white cloth, small flags and wooden chairs in a white hall with arches, while visitors look on behind a rope."},"wiki":"Western betrayal"},
    "ww2-379": {"questions":["Fighting in the Austrian capital went on until the afternoon of 13 April 1945, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only 3 days before the Red Army began its assault on Berlin.","Karl Renner offered his help to the Soviet command after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Austria, and on 27 April 1945 three parties proclaimed the democratic republic restored."],"abstract":"The <b>Vienna offensive</b> was the Soviet campaign of 16 March to 15 April 1945 in which the Second and Third Ukrainian Fronts drove German forces out of western Hungary and took Vienna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It followed Operation Spring Awakening, the last German offensive of the war, launched on 6 March near Lake Balaton by the Sixth SS Panzer Army to protect oil fields that Hitler ranked above almost anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Third Ukrainian Front had built deep belts of mines and anti-tank defences, and the German attack bogged down in mud and snow after gaining nearly 20 miles (30 kilometres) at best.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 16 March the Soviet counter-offensive struck between Lake Velence and Bicske, aimed through Székesfehérvár and Sopron towards Vienna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antonov had told the Yalta conference that the Germans would probably defend the direction of Vienna more strongly, drawing troops from Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The German line gave way within days, and one army commander reported that his soldiers no longer wanted to be the last to die in a war they thought lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet tanks crossed the Austrian border on 30 March, and by 7 April Soviet troops were in Vienna as far as the Gürtel and on the Danube west of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting in the city went on until the afternoon of 13 April, but without any attempt by the German command to make Vienna another Budapest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Allies had declared at Moscow in 1943 that Austria, the first free country to fall victim to Hitlerite aggression, would be liberated and its annexation in 1938 treated as null and void.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Karl Renner, a former chancellor, offered the Soviet command his help in restoring an independent Austria, and on 27 April three parties proclaimed the democratic republic restored in the spirit of the 1920 constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 450–54, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 454–56, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet-German War 1941–1945: Myths and Realities; A Survey Essay</i>, paper presented at the Strom Thurmond Institute of Government and Public Affairs, Clemson University, 11 October 2001, https://archive.org/details/the-soviet-german-war-1941-1945-myths-and-realities-david-m-glantz-1001. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, Yalta, February 4, 1945, 5 p.m.,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, 581–83, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d330. [Open access]","“Declaration on Austria,” in the Moscow Conference declarations of October 1943, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kennan) to the Secretary of State, Moscow, April 26, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d65. [Open access]","Bundeskanzleramt der Republik Österreich, “April des Jahres 1945,” <i>Gang der Geschichte</i>, https://www.bundeskanzleramt.gv.at/bundeskanzleramt/besuchen-sie-uns/gang-der-geschichte/april_1945.html. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler send his last armoured reserve to Hungary rather than to the Oder?","a":"He ranked the Hungarian oil fields as the most vital area of the whole Eastern Front, and Guderian complained that for Hitler the words oil fields were spelled in capitals."},{"q":"Why did Vienna escape the fate of Budapest?","a":"The new army group commander, Rendulic, refused to treat the city as a place for a last stand, dismissed Skorzeny's reports of disintegration, and the battle ended on 13 April without a siege like the one that destroyed Budapest."},{"q":"Why did Austria's new government lean on the Moscow Declaration?","a":"The declaration of independence of 27 April borrowed its wording and started from Austria's status as a victim of Hitler, and the Austrian chancellery notes that self-critical questions about shared responsibility found no place in it then."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%85_%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2_%D0%A2-34-85_%D0%B2_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%A8%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%85%D0%B0%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8B.jpg","title":"Soviet tanks in Schwechat near Vienna, April 1945","desc":"A column of Soviet T-34-85 tanks with soldiers riding on them in the town of Schwechat on the edge of Vienna in April 1945.","credit":"Photograph by Yevgeny Khaldei, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%85_%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2_%D0%A2-34-85_%D0%B2_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%A8%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%85%D0%B0%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B5_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%8B.jpg","alt":"Tanks carrying soldiers roll down a cobbled street between two-storey houses, with a church tower in the distance."},"wiki":"Vienna offensive"},
    "ww2-380": {"questions":["Konev's front raced ahead while Zhukov's was held for days in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last great German defence on the road from the Oder to Berlin.","A memorial raised in 1972 to glorify the Red Army's victory stands above the Oderbruch where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost tens of thousands of Soviet, Polish and German lives."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Seelow Heights</b> was the opening battle of the Soviet Berlin operation in April 1945, fought for the ridge on the western edge of the Oderbruch about 70 kilometres (43 miles) east of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since the end of January the Soviet fronts had held bridgeheads on the west bank of the Oder near Küstrin and Frankfurt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Zhukov's First Belorussian Front massed 3,155 tanks and self-propelled guns and 16,934 artillery pieces against a German Ninth Army with 512 armoured vehicles and 344 pieces of regular artillery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main force, packed into a front of 20 miles (32 kilometres) between Wriezen and Seelow, had to cross the marshy valley floor of the Oder and the Alte Oder and then take the heights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The memorial site puts the defenders at 200,000 men against 900,000 attackers, with the First Polish Army fighting alongside the Soviet troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The assault began before dawn on 16 April behind a huge barrage, but the searchlights meant to blind the Germans failed, and in the mud, smoke and darkness the waves of infantry piled up on each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Apparently on Stalin's orders two tank armies were thrown in the same day, adding to the tangle, and by nightfall the attackers were still in front of the German main line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only on the third and fourth days did Soviet armour break through west of Wriezen and beyond Seelow, while Konev's front to the south advanced far faster and was rewarded by Stalin with a share in the taking of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Losses are disputed: the memorial site gives over 35,000 Soviet and 12,000 German dead for 16 to 20 April, while an American historian counts 361,367 Soviet and Polish soldiers fallen in the whole Berlin operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The memorial built in 1972 glorified the Red Army's victory, and only after 1990 could an exhibition begin to treat the battle without ideological prescriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 470–72, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 473–76, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet-German War 1941–1945: Myths and Realities; A Survey Essay</i>, paper presented at the Strom Thurmond Institute of Government and Public Affairs, Clemson University, 11 October 2001, https://archive.org/details/the-soviet-german-war-1941-1945-myths-and-realities-david-m-glantz-1001. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, “Gedenkstätte Seelower Höhen,” Erinnerungsorte, https://www.bpb.de/themen/holocaust/erinnerungsorte/503115/gedenkstaette-seelower-hoehen/. [in German] [Open access]","Stiftung Denkmal für die ermordeten Juden Europas, “Seelow Heights Memorial,” <i>Memorial Museums: Portal to Sites of Remembrance in Europe</i>, https://www.memorialmuseums.org/memorialmuseum/seelow-heights-memorial?locale=en. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-1945. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Zhukov attack straight into the strongest German position?","a":"The Soviet plan put its main weight on Berlin to seize the planned Soviet occupation zone quickly, and the direct road from the Küstrin bridgehead led over the Seelow Heights, the largest natural obstacle on the way to the capital."},{"q":"Why did the searchlights fail?","a":"In the mud, smoke and darkness of the opening barrage they did not blind the defenders as intended, and the memorial site says they turned against the Red Army, whose infantry suffered heavy losses."},{"q":"Why has the memorial's message changed?","a":"It was built in 1972 to glorify the Red Army's victory, and only after the fall of the Berlin Wall could a new exhibition of 1995 also remember prisoners, forced labourers and civilians and discuss how memory of the war has changed."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Gedenkst%C3%A4tte_Seelower_H%C3%B6hen_Blick_ins_Oderbruch.JPG","title":"The Oderbruch from the Seelow Heights","desc":"The view east from the memorial on the Seelow Heights across the flat valley of the Oderbruch, over which the Soviet assault came in April 1945.","credit":"Photograph by Assenmacher, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gedenkst%C3%A4tte_Seelower_H%C3%B6hen_Blick_ins_Oderbruch.JPG","alt":"Trees in the foreground frame a wide, flat green plain stretching to a distant horizon under a grey sky."},"wiki":"Battle of the Seelow Heights"},
    "ww2-351": {"questions":["SS and police units that included the Dirlewanger and Kaminski brigades crushed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, massacring tens of thousands of civilians in the Wola district during the first week of August 1944.","3 days after Soviet radio told Warsaw that the hour of action had come, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, yet the Vistula bridges and the suburb of Praga stayed in German hands throughout."],"abstract":"The <b>Warsaw Uprising</b> was the revolt of the Polish Home Army against the German occupiers of Warsaw, launched at five in the afternoon of 1 August 1944 and ended by capitulation on 2 October after 63 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of Operation Tempest, the Home Army's plan for local risings ahead of the advancing Red Army, which at first left the capital out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Tadeusz Bór-Komorowski gave the order on 31 July, after Soviet forces appeared on the east bank of the Vistula, expecting a fight of a few days that would free the city before the Soviets entered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aim was political as much as military, since the government-in-exile in London wanted to keep Poland from falling wholly under Soviet control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 29 July Soviet radio had broadcast an appeal from the Union of Polish Patriots telling Warsaw that the hour of action had already arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> About 45,000 Home Army fighters took part, but only a quarter of them had weapons, and the four Vistula bridges and the suburb of Praga on the east bank stayed in German hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Himmler gave the suppression to SS and police units under Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski, among them the Kaminski and Dirlewanger brigades, whose atrocities were reported even to Guderian and Jodl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The counts of the massacre in the district of Wola between 5 and 7 August differ: about 40,000 people in the reckoning of the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, and more than 50,000 in that of the German Historical Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 5 October that despite all the efforts of the Soviet Army the German positions on the Vistula could not be taken and relief could not come in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The total dead are counted differently too, at more than 180,000 by the Holocaust Museum, about 180,000, mostly civilians, by the German Historical Museum, and perhaps 250,000 by the official history of the US Army Air Forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Warsaw Uprising,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-warsaw-polish-uprising. [Open access]","Martin Borkowski-Saruhan, \"Der Warschauer Aufstand 1944,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/warschauer-aufstand-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 340–41, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 344–45, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, August 17, 1944, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d1263. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Warsaw Epic (Tribute to Polish Nation),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 5 October 1944, vol. 403, cols. 1139–40, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/oct/05/warsaw-epic-tribute-to-polish-nation. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 315–16, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), proceedings of 7 January 1946, 466–67, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Home Army rise in Warsaw at the end of July 1944?","a":"Because Soviet forces had appeared on the east bank of the Vistula, and Bór-Komorowski expected a fight of a few days that would free the city before they entered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aim was also political, since the government in London wanted to keep Poland from falling wholly under Soviet control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moscow radio had itself called on Warsaw to rise on 29 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the insurgents never link up with the Red Army?","a":"Because the four Vistula bridges and Praga on the east bank stayed German, and the Soviet tank army that had come close to the city shifted its effort south to a bridgehead at Magnuszew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that the German positions on the Vistula could not be taken in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the death tolls for the rising differ so widely?","a":"Because different institutions have counted the dead and published different totals: more than 180,000 for the Holocaust Museum, about 180,000 for the German Historical Museum and perhaps 250,000 for the US Army Air Forces history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Even the Wola massacre is put at about 40,000 by one and more than 50,000 by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/AK-soldiers_Parasol_Regiment_Warsaw_Uprising_1944.jpg","title":"Home Army soldiers at Gęsiówka, 5 August 1944","desc":"A patrol of the Zośka battalion in captured German uniforms and with captured German weapons, photographed on the fifth day of the rising at the Gęsiówka camp; the man on the right was killed later that day.","credit":"Photograph by Juliusz Bogdan Deczkowski, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AK-soldiers_Parasol_Regiment_Warsaw_Uprising_1944.jpg","alt":"Three young men in camouflage smocks and steel helmets stand in a street holding rifles and a submachine gun, white armbands on their sleeves."},"wiki":"Warsaw Uprising"},
    "ww2-352": {"questions":["Moscow's refusal in August 1944 to let American bombers land on Soviet airfields after their drops crippled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a refusal Harriman blamed on ruthless political considerations.","Of 1,284 containers dropped by 107 B-17s on 18 September 1944, the only American mission of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as few as 130 may have reached Polish hands."],"abstract":"The <b>Warsaw airlift</b> was the attempt by Allied air forces to keep the Warsaw Uprising supplied by parachute during August and September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Polish, British, South African and other crews flew Liberators and Halifaxes from airfields in southern Italy, a round trip of about 12 hours and 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first flights left on 4 August and the last on the night of 21 to 22 September, and over the city the crews had to come down to about 100 metres (330 feet) through searchlights and anti-aircraft fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Heavy bombers based in England could not fly to Warsaw and back, and the round trip from Italy was very hard, so the Americans planned to fly on and land at their shuttle bases in Soviet territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When that mission at last flew on 18 September, 107 B-17s dropped 1,284 containers, but it was later known that only 288, or possibly only 130, reached Polish hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The shuttle needed Soviet consent, and on 15 August Vyshinsky told the American ambassador that Moscow could not go along with it, calling the rising a purely adventuristic affair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Averell Harriman cabled that night that for the first time since coming to Moscow he was gravely concerned, judging the refusal to rest not on operational difficulties but on ruthless political considerations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet Government kept its airfields closed to American supply planes until 10 September and began dropping supplies on the city itself on 13 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 26 September that the Soviet armies had sent supplies and air cover once their operational plans permitted, and that the American force had flown on to Soviet bases under Soviet escort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A second American mission was never cleared, with Stalin apparently refusing permission on 2 October, the day the Home Army's representatives signed the capitulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, August 15, 1944, 8 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d1260. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, August 15, 1944, 11 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d1261. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 315–16, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 344–45, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Warsaw (Allied Assistance),\" <i>Hansard</i>, 26 September 1944, vol. 403, cols. 25–27, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/26/warsaw-allied-assistance. [Open access]","Michał Tomasz Wójciuk, \"Warsaw Airlift: Incredible Stories That Really Happened during Warsaw Rising,\" Warsaw Rising Museum, https://www.1944.pl/en/article/warsaw-airlift-incredible-stories-that-really,5323.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Americans need Soviet airfields for a drop on Warsaw?","a":"Because heavy bombers from England could not fly to Warsaw and back, and the round trip from Italy was very difficult, so the plan was to fly on and land at the shuttle bases in Soviet territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Harriman therefore asked Molotov for immediate Soviet approval of the mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Harriman think the Soviet refusal was political?","a":"Because Vyshinsky's letter did not plead difficulties: it called the rising a purely adventuristic affair that the Soviet Government would not lend its hand to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harriman concluded that the refusal rested not on operational problems but on ruthless political considerations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so little of the American drop reach the Poles?","a":"Because by 18 September the areas the insurgents held had shrunk too far for accurate drops from high altitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of 1,284 containers, only 288 or possibly only 130 fell into Polish hands, and the Germans collected the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/AGAD_Grupa_powsta%C5%84c%C3%B3w_podejmuje_zrzut_ze_spadochronu.jpg","title":"Insurgents gathering an air drop, Warsaw 1944","desc":"A group of insurgents in a Warsaw street hauling in a parachute that has come down with supplies, photographed in August or September 1944.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Central Archives of Historical Records in Warsaw, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGAD_Grupa_powsta%C5%84c%C3%B3w_podejmuje_zrzut_ze_spadochronu.jpg","alt":"A large white parachute billows over a city street as several figures in coats and helmets grab at it in front of tall apartment buildings."},"wiki":"Warsaw airlift"},
    "ww2-353": {"questions":["Stalin told Churchill in July 1944 that he could not treat the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the government of Poland, but that it might become the kernel of a provisional one.","On the last day of 1944 the Polish National Council turned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a provisional government, and the Soviet Union recognised it within a week."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish Committee of National Liberation</b> was the provisional authority that Polish communists set up under Soviet sponsorship in July 1944 to govern the territory the Red Army was taking from the Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A decree of the Polish National Council dated 21 July created it, and its manifesto, issued in Chełm the next day, announced a land reform, state custody of German-held property and an alliance with the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The manifesto proposed to settle the eastern frontier on the principle of Polish lands to Poland and Ukrainian, Belarusian and Lithuanian lands to the Soviet republics, and the committee soon backed the Curzon Line that Moscow wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its chairman was the socialist Edward Osóbka-Morawski, and its members included Wanda Wasilewska and Michał Rola-Żymierski, who was named commander of the combined Polish forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The committee soon moved its seat from Chełm to Lublin, which is why it became known as the Lublin Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> On 23 July Stalin told Churchill that the Soviet Union would not set up its own administration in Poland and had therefore made contact with the committee, which it could not treat as a government but which might become the kernel of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 2 days later Moscow announced that it would conclude with the committee an agreement on relations between the Soviet command and the Polish administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The committee thus stood as a rival to the government in London, and Churchill told the Commons in December that its formation was a complication that would not have arisen had the London Poles taken British advice earlier in the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He warned that as the Red Army advanced, the area the Lublin Committee administered would grow and its contacts with the Soviet Government would become more intimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 31 December 1944 the National Council turned the committee into the Provisional Polish Government, which the Soviet Union recognised on 5 January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Chairman of the Council of People's Commissars of the Soviet Union (Stalin) to the British Prime Minister (Churchill), July 23, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d1299. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, July 24, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d1300. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, July 26, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d1301. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 340–41, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Warsaw Uprising,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-warsaw-polish-uprising. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Poland,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 15 December 1944, vol. 406, cols. 1480–82, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/dec/15/poland. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"President Roosevelt to the British Prime Minister (Churchill), 4 January 1945,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945, Europe, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v05/d104. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin say the Soviet Union needed the committee at all?","a":"Because, he told Churchill, the Soviet Union did not want to set up its own administration in Poland, and he claimed to have found no other force able to create a Polish one, dismissing the London-led underground as ephemeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moscow then announced an agreement with the committee on relations between the Soviet command and the Polish administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the committee matter so much for the frontier question?","a":"Because its manifesto proposed to divide the borderlands by nationality and it soon backed the Curzon Line Moscow wanted, the main point dividing the Soviet Union from the London government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill named the frontier and the relation with the Lublin Committee as the two crucial issues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the committee turned into a government at the end of 1944?","a":"Because Moscow was ready to recognise it: Stalin told Roosevelt that on 27 December the Soviet Presidium had already informed the Poles it would recognise a provisional government as soon as one was formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The National Council made the change on 31 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/PKWN_manifest.jpg","title":"Reading the committee's manifesto, July 1944","desc":"A man reads a posted copy of the manifesto of the Polish Committee of National Liberation, dated 22 July 1944; the photograph became a symbol much reproduced in communist propaganda.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PKWN_manifest.jpg","alt":"A bearded man in a flat cap and a heavy coat, a bundle on his back, stands reading a large printed poster pasted to a wooden wall."},"wiki":"Polish Committee of National Liberation"},
    "ww2-354": {"questions":["Malinovsky's and Tolbukhin's fronts, coordinated by Timoshenko, launched the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in August 1944, and Romanian divisions covering Iași abandoned their positions without a fight on its first day.","The Soviet official history counts 18 of 25 German divisions surrounded and destroyed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last encircled pockets in Bessarabia and Moldavia were wiped out by 4 September 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Jassy–Kishinev offensive</b> was the Soviet attack of August 1944 that broke the German and Romanian front in Bessarabia and Moldavia, named after the towns now called Iași and Chișinău.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Malinovsky's Second and Tolbukhin's Third Ukrainian Fronts, with Marshal Timoshenko coordinating for the Stavka, opened it on 20 August with barrages on two narrow sectors, north-west of Iași and south of Tiraspol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the Soviet figures given in the American official history, the two fronts had 929,000 men and an advantage of nearly two to one in troops and better than three to one in tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet official history in turn credits Army Group South Ukraine with more than 900,000 men, logistical units included, behind defences up to 80 kilometres (50 miles) deep in the Iași sector.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two Romanian divisions covering Iași abandoned their positions without a fight on the first day, and by the next evening five more had fallen apart completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The pincers closed behind the German Sixth Army, which Tolbukhin's troops ringed when they took Cahul on 26 August, while the coup in Bucharest on 23 August turned Romania against its ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 25 August Moscow promised that Romanian armies joining the Red Army against the Germans would keep their arms, but said that fighting on Romanian soil would end only when the German armies there were liquidated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German army group commander soon reported that not a single complete division of the Sixth Army had escaped, only headquarters and service troops with some 5,000 vehicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet account counts 18 of Army Group South Ukraine's 25 German divisions surrounded and destroyed, the last pockets wiped out by 4 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The armistice signed in Moscow on 12 September restored the Soviet-Romanian frontier of 1940 and put at least twelve Romanian infantry divisions under the Allied, in practice Soviet, High Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 349–52, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 352–56, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 136–38, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 148–49, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, August 25, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, Europe, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d209. [Open access]","\"The Armistice Agreement with Rumania; September 12, 1944,\" Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/rumania.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Romanian front collapse so quickly?","a":"Because the German army group had been stripped of eleven divisions since June, nearly a third of its strength, while Romanians held 160 of the 392 miles of its front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its staff had worried about Romania changing sides rather than asking how much will to fight the Romanian army still had, and whole divisions gave way at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the strength figures for the battle seem not to fit together?","a":"Because both come from Soviet sources counting in different ways: the attackers are put at 929,000 men with a two-to-one edge in troops, yet the defenders at more than 900,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The larger count of the defenders includes their logistical units and the Romanian armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow promise to let Romanian armies keep their weapons?","a":"Because it wanted them fighting the Germans at once: the statement of 25 August offered to leave armed any Romanian force that joined the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The armistice then required at least twelve Romanian infantry divisions to fight under Soviet direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-244-2321-34%2C_Ostfront-S%C3%BCd%2C_Panzer_V_%28Panther%29.jpg","title":"A German Panther in the retreat from Romania, August 1944","desc":"A German propaganda-company photograph of a Panther tank and its crew during the retreat from Romania towards Hungary in August 1944, with an abandoned vehicle in the field behind.","credit":"Photograph by Waidelich, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-244-2321-34, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-244-2321-34,_Ostfront-S%C3%BCd,_Panzer_V_(Panther).jpg","alt":"A long-barrelled tank stands in a trampled field of tall stalks under a wide grey sky, with men in caps standing on its rear deck."},"wiki":"Second Jassy–Kishinev offensive"},
    "ww2-355": {"questions":["Hitler ordered the Luftwaffe to bomb the royal palace in Bucharest after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an attack that only hardened Romanian feeling against Germany and preceded a declaration of war on 25 August 1944.","Remembered in Soviet histories as an anti-fascist uprising led by the Communist Party, the overthrow of Antonescu in August 1944 is usually called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the monarch in whose palace he was arrested."],"abstract":"<b>King Michael's Coup</b> was the overthrow of Marshal Ion Antonescu on 23 August 1944, when the dictator was arrested at the royal palace and Romania left the war on Germany's side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Antonescu's personal authority was the only basis of the German alliance, and the German army group believed the country stayed in the war only because its fear of the Russians still slightly exceeded its desire for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had rejected armistice terms offered in Cairo by the Americans, British and Soviets in the spring, and a milder Soviet offer made in secret in Sweden, unable to bring himself to take the plunge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Soviet offensive of 20 August broke the front, he was called to an audience with the king on the afternoon of 23 August and arrested with his ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new government, formed from the National Liberal, National Peasant, Communist and Social Democratic parties, announced that night that it had accepted the armistice terms of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The coup is told two ways: German records have the king dismissing Antonescu at an audience, while the Soviet official history calls it a communist-led anti-fascist uprising in which the royal guard handed him to an armed communist detachment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That history adds that the king and his courtiers had hoped to limit the action to a change of government and keep the masses out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler ordered the putsch smashed and the king arrested, but German troops marching on Bucharest were stopped in the suburbs, and the bombing of the palace and government buildings on 24 August united the country against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Romania declared war on Germany on 25 August, and the armistice signed in Moscow on 12 September dated its withdrawal from the war from four in the morning of 24 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 28 September that the satellites were passing from alliance with Germany not to neutrality but to war, and praised the restraint of the Soviet terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 349–51, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 352–55, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Declaration of New Rumanian Government in Broadcast on August 23, 1944, Over Radio Romania,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, Europe, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d206. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 144–46, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","\"The Armistice Agreement with Rumania; September 12, 1944,\" Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/rumania.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 September 1944, vol. 403, cols. 487–89, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had Antonescu not left the war before August 1944?","a":"Because he could not bring himself to accept terms: he rejected the three Allies' offer in Cairo and a milder secret Soviet offer in Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even after the front broke he insisted that every piece of ground be held, saying the fate of the whole Romanian people was being decided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are there two versions of who made the coup?","a":"Because the American official history follows German records of the palace audience, while the Soviet official history credits a communist-led uprising in which the royal guard handed Antonescu to communist fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even that history quotes Gheorghiu-Dej saying the decisive factor was the breakthrough of the front and the fast Soviet advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German counter-stroke in Bucharest fail?","a":"Because not a single Romanian general would side with the Germans, and the 6,000 troops sent against the capital were stopped in the suburbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bombing of the palace that Hitler ordered then gave the government its reason for an open breach and united national feeling against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Ej%C3%A9rcitoSovi%C3%A9ticoEnBucarest1944.jpg","title":"Soviet troops arriving in Bucharest, 30 August 1944","desc":"Residents of Bucharest shake hands with Soviet soldiers as the Red Army enters the city on 30 August 1944, a week after the coup that took Romania out of the Axis.","credit":"Photographer unknown, archive of the Institute of History of the Romanian Workers' Party, via the Fototeca online a comunismului românesc, free licence, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ej%C3%A9rcitoSovi%C3%A9ticoEnBucarest1944.jpg","alt":"Smiling men in caps crowd round two soldiers in field caps who are shaking hands in the foreground."},"wiki":"1944 Romanian coup d'état"},
    "ww2-356": {"questions":["Insurgents who seized the war ministry in Sofia and arrested the cabinet in the small hours of 9 September 1944 carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that made Kimon Georgiev prime minister.","Bulgaria had never fought the Soviet Union before September 1944, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed Moscow's sudden declaration of war ended its alliance with Germany within days."],"abstract":"The <b>1944 Bulgarian coup d'état</b> was the seizure of power in Sofia on 9 September 1944 by the Fatherland Front, which replaced the government of Konstantin Muraviev and turned Bulgaria against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bulgaria was allied to Germany but had never been at war with the Soviet Union, and in August 1944 it was looking desperately for a way to keep the Red Army off its soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After Romania changed sides Sofia declared its neutrality, but Moscow claimed that a neutral Bulgaria would become a refuge for the retreating Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 5 September the Soviet Union broke off relations and declared war, and Molotov handed the note to the Bulgarian minister in Moscow that evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Muraviev cabinet asked for an armistice and broke with Germany, but Moscow refused to consider the request, citing a contradiction between what it told the Soviet chargé in Sofia and what it said in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Soviet troops crossed the border on 8 September, and that night Moscow radio announced that Muraviev could not guarantee the armistice, after which, an American consul reported, his government resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Soviet account, insurgents and army units loyal to the Fatherland Front took the war ministry in the small hours of 9 September and arrested the ministers in session there, under the direction of the Bulgarian Workers' Party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The new government under Kimon Georgiev, with Damyan Velchev as war minister and four communists among its members, removed the regents and declared war on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 28 September that the Soviet declaration had been enough to make Bulgaria turn its armies on the Germans, but that it must work its passage before Britain granted it any special status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The armistice signed in Moscow on 28 October required Bulgaria to withdraw from Greek and Yugoslav territory and to place forces under the general direction of the Allied, in practice Soviet, High Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, September 5, 1944, 7 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d325. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, September 9, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d340. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Istanbul (Berry) to the Secretary of State, September 11, 1944, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d348. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 175–76, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 355, 369–71, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","\"The Armistice Agreement with Bulgaria; October 28, 1944,\" Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/bulgaria.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 September 1944, vol. 403, cols. 487–89, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union declare war on a country that had never fought it?","a":"Because, Moscow said, Bulgaria had kept up relations with Germany and was sheltering Germans on its soil, letting them build a new base of resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A neutral Bulgaria, in the Soviet argument, would have been a refuge for the retreating German armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill called the intervention startling and effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Muraviev government fall even after it asked for an armistice?","a":"Because Moscow said it told the Soviet chargé in Sofia that it had broken with Germany while announcing in public only that it had asked for an armistice, and that this contradiction bred mistrust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once Moscow radio declared that it could not guarantee the terms, the government resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain refuse Bulgaria any special status?","a":"Because Churchill held Bulgaria responsible for cruelty against Greece and Yugoslavia while its own soil had been spared, and said it must work its passage for a long time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The armistice duly ordered its forces and officials out of Greek and Yugoslav territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/9-septemvri_Soviets.jpg","title":"Soviet troops in Sofia, September 1944","desc":"Soviet soldiers in a jeep decked with flowers are greeted by crowds in Sofia in September 1944, a week after the Fatherland Front took power.","credit":"Photographer unknown, National Museum of Military History, Bulgaria, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9-septemvri_Soviets.jpg","alt":"Soldiers ride an open jeep covered in flowers along a city street lined with onlookers, while young women run up holding bouquets."},"wiki":"1944 Bulgarian coup d'état"},
    "ww2-357": {"questions":["Tito went to Moscow in September 1944 to agree the terms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, under which Soviet troops would enter Yugoslavia and leave once their military tasks were done.","The German group cut off south-east of Belgrade in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried for days to reach the Sava, and substantially fewer than the 12,000 first recorded actually escaped."],"abstract":"The <b>Belgrade offensive</b> was the joint campaign of the Red Army and Tito's Partisans that drove the Germans out of eastern Serbia and took the Yugoslav capital in October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was agreed in Moscow at the end of September, when Tito arrived to settle how Soviet troops would enter Yugoslavia and fight alongside his People's Liberation Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet Government declared that its troops would leave Yugoslavia once their military tasks were done and that civil administration where they were stationed would stay in Yugoslav hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Just before, Tito had ordered that British and American officers attached to his forces go no farther than corps headquarters, which an American adviser in Caserta reported was generally read as a wish to keep Western witnesses away from civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In early October the Soviet Fifty-seventh Army crossed from Bulgaria, one of its mechanised corps cut the railway into Belgrade from the south on 8 October, and Bulgarian troops attacked towards Niš.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By noon on 13 October Soviet armour was deploying 10 kilometres (6 miles) south of the city, and on the night of the 14th Soviet troops and Partisans entered Belgrade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Soviet account the Yugoslavs had asked to enter their capital first, so men of the 1st Proletarian Division rode in on Soviet tanks and the two armies came in together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A German group cut off south-east of the city, which the Soviet history puts at 20,000 men, tried for days to break out to the Sava, and the German army group's journal recorded 12,000 crossing before noting that the real number was substantially smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kalemegdan fortress fell on 20 October, and the Soviet history claims 15,000 enemy soldiers killed and 9,000 taken prisoner in the street fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within a week Tito was in Belgrade putting his agreement with the royal government's prime minister, Ivan Šubašić, before the Anti-Fascist Council, a reconciliation Churchill said Britain and the Soviet Union were urging jointly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 373–77, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 192–93, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 203–06, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Report by Major Charles W. Thayer of the Independent American Military Mission to Marshal Tito, 4 November 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, Europe, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d1312. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 27 October 1944, vol. 404, cols. 492–93, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/oct/27/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The United States Political Adviser on the Staff of the Supreme Allied Commander, Mediterranean Theater (Kirk), to the Secretary of State, September 24, 1944, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, Europe, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d1306. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Soviet troops need Tito's agreement to enter Yugoslavia?","a":"Because Tito led the forces that held much of the country and meant to govern it: in Moscow the two sides settled the Soviet entry, joint operations and Soviet supplies, and Moscow promised to withdraw and leave civil administration to Yugoslavs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the same moment Tito was limiting where British and American officers could go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the numbers of Germans killed and escaped so uncertain?","a":"Because the two sides' records disagree: the Soviet history counts 20,000 Germans sealed off, 15,000 killed and 9,000 captured in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German army group journal first recorded 12,000 men crossing the Sava, then admitted the real number was substantially smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did taking Belgrade matter politically?","a":"Because it gave Tito his capital: by 27 October he was there putting his agreement with Šubašić on a single government before the Anti-Fascist Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that Britain and Russia were acting jointly towards both Šubašić and Tito.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Borci_3._bataljona_1._proleterske_brigade_u_Beogradu%2C_21._oktobra_1944.jpg","title":"Partisans in Belgrade, 21 October 1944","desc":"Fighters of the 3rd Battalion of the 1st Proletarian Brigade march through Belgrade on 21 October 1944, the day after the last German stronghold in the city fell.","credit":"Photographer unknown, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borci_3._bataljona_1._proleterske_brigade_u_Beogradu,_21._oktobra_1944.jpg","alt":"A column of young soldiers in greatcoats and side caps, rifles slung, walks along a cobbled city street."},"wiki":"Belgrade offensive"},
    "ww2-358": {"questions":["Judging that only Soviet forces were placed to help, the Combined Chiefs of Staff set aside the Czechoslovak exile government's appeal for air supply to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in central Slovakia in 1944.","The Soviet offensive through the Dukla Pass was meant to reach the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but it stalled in the Carpathians and the Germans took Banská Bystrica on 28 October 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Slovak National Uprising</b> was the armed revolt against the Tiso regime and the German army that broke out in Slovakia at the end of August 1944 and was crushed by the end of October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was prepared by the underground Slovak National Council together with officers of the Slovak army, who drew up its final plan at a meeting on 20 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The council sent two envoys to Moscow in early August to coordinate the rising with the Soviet command, and its plans treated Soviet help as decisive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Partisans seized towns such as Ružomberok and Zvolen in the last week of August, and on 29 August the local actions became a national rising centred on Banská Bystrica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its strength is described very differently: the Soviet history counts 65,000 to 70,000 Slovak troops stationed in the country, while the American official history speaks of a one-division Slovak army going over to the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Czechoslovak government in London asked Washington for help on 31 August, saying that Moscow had been told a rising was possible but not its exact date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Combined Chiefs of Staff judged supply by American or British aircraft not reasonably feasible, since geography left only the Soviet forces in a position to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Soviet East Carpathian offensive meant to reach the insurgents took the Dukla Pass with the Czechoslovak 1st Corps on 6 October, but stalled 15 to 20 kilometres (9 to 12 miles) beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 24 October the London government feared the rising might be liquidated, and on 28 October the Germans took Banská Bystrica and General Rudolf Viest ordered the insurgent army to disband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From September German units deported about 12,600 Slovak Jews, most of them to Auschwitz and Theresienstadt, and after the fall the remaining fighters withdrew into the mountains as partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 297–301, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 307–10, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 358–59, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé to the Czechoslovak Government in Exile (Schoenfeld) to the Secretary of State, August 31, 1944, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d462. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé to the Czechoslovak Government in Exile (Schoenfeld) to the Secretary of State, October 24, 1944, 8 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d463. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé to the Czechoslovak Government in Exile (Schoenfeld), at London, November 18, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d464. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Holocaust in Slovakia,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-in-slovakia. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rising break out before the Red Army arrived?","a":"Because its plan provided for a rising if Germany tried to occupy Slovakia, and the occupation was beginning in the last days of August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Holocaust Museum dates the outbreak to the moment Soviet troops entered neighbouring Subcarpathian Rus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Masaryk said the timing had been left to the military leaders inside the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Western Allies send so little help?","a":"Because their chiefs of staff judged that supply by American or British aircraft was not reasonably feasible and that only Soviet forces were placed to give it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A second appeal in October listed mortars, machine guns and anti-tank weapons, but by the time it was answered the rising had collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rising's defeat matter so much for Slovakia's Jews?","a":"Because German security police units came in with the army to round up the remaining Jews: about 12,600 were deported from September onwards and a few thousand more were killed in hiding or fighting with the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Perhaps half of those deported survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Nastupeni_povstalci.jpg","title":"An insurgent artillery battery, autumn 1944","desc":"The men and commander of the 18th anti-aircraft artillery battery, a Slovak army unit that fought in the Slovak National Uprising, drawn up for inspection in the autumn of 1944.","credit":"Photograph from the archive of Pavel Pelech, free licence with attribution, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nastupeni_povstalci.jpg","alt":"A long rank of soldiers in steel helmets presents arms on open ground while an officer at the front salutes another officer facing them."},"wiki":"Slovak National Uprising"},
    "ww2-359": {"questions":["Arrow Cross militias shot thousands of Jews into the Danube during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Soviet troops freed the city's two ghettos in mid-January 1945.","By the American official history's count, fewer than 700 of nearly 30,000 defenders reached German lines when the garrison broke out of Buda on 11 February 1945, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 2 days later."],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Budapest</b> was the Soviet encirclement and capture of the Hungarian capital, closed on 26 December 1944 and ended on 13 February 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To Hitler the city had become a symbol as Stalingrad once had been, and he ruled that it be held even if that meant drawing strength from the Ardennes offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The garrison of four German and two Hungarian divisions under SS-Obergruppenführer Karl Pfeffer-Wildenbruch was trapped with a population normally of over a million, for whom almost nothing had been prepared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet history counts more than 188,000 German and Hungarian troops inside the ring, a figure far above the six divisions of the German account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also says that two Soviet envoys bringing surrender terms were killed, and that the garrison then had to be destroyed by storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pest, on the east bank, fell on 18 January after 23 days of street fighting, and three German relief attacks in January failed to break through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inside the city Arrow Cross militias, who shunned combat, shot as many as 20,000 Jews on the banks of the Danube between December and the end of January, by the reckoning of the US Holocaust Memorial Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Soviet troops reached the international ghetto on 16 January and the main ghetto on 17 and 18 January, freeing about 119,000 Jews in Budapest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On the night of 11 February the Buda garrison tried to break out, and the counts differ: the American official history has fewer than 700 of close to 30,000 reaching the German lines, while the Soviet history says more than 15,000 got through a corridor before being destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hungary's provisional government had already signed an armistice in Moscow on 20 January, declaring war on Germany while the battle for its capital went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 384–86, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 432, 448–49, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 263–64, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","A. A. Grechko, ed., <i>Liberation Mission of the Soviet Armed Forces in the Second World War</i>, trans. David Fidlon (Moscow: Progress Publishers, 1975), 265–66, https://archive.org/details/a.-a.-grechko-ed.-liberation-mission-of-the-soviet-armed-forces-in-the-second-wo. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Budapest,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/budapest. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Holocaust in Hungary,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-holocaust-in-hungary. [Open access]","\"Armistice Agreement with Hungary; January 20, 1945,\" Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/hungary.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans not abandon Budapest?","a":"Because Hitler had made it a symbol, as Stalingrad had been, and said losing it would halve the effect of his victory in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the army group objected to a siege among a million starving people, Guderian called the question immaterial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler himself directed the attempts at relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the size and fate of the garrison disputed?","a":"Because the two sides counted differently: the Soviet history puts more than 188,000 troops inside the ring and has over 15,000 breaking out before being destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American official history, using German records, names six divisions and says fewer than 700 of close to 30,000 reached German lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the city's Jews in such danger during the siege?","a":"Because the Arrow Cross regime installed in October herded them into ghettos and its militias shot as many as 20,000 into the Danube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Soviet troops freed the two ghettos in mid-January, and about 119,000 Jews in Budapest survived to be liberated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Budapest%2C_a_L%C3%A1nch%C3%ADd_%C3%A9s_a_romos_Kir%C3%A1lyi_Palota._Fortepan_16247.jpg/1920px-Budapest%2C_a_L%C3%A1nch%C3%ADd_%C3%A9s_a_romos_Kir%C3%A1lyi_Palota._Fortepan_16247.jpg","title":"The Chain Bridge and the Royal Palace, 1945","desc":"The Chain Bridge across the Danube, blown up by the retreating Germans, and the ruined Royal Palace on Castle Hill in Buda, photographed in 1945.","credit":"Photograph by Márton Kurutz, Fortepan 16247, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Budapest,_a_L%C3%A1nch%C3%ADd_%C3%A9s_a_romos_Kir%C3%A1lyi_Palota._Fortepan_16247.jpg","alt":"Two stone bridge towers stand in a wide river with their chains and deck fallen into the water, and a long damaged palace on a hill behind."},"wiki":"Siege of Budapest"},
    "ww2-360": {"questions":["Told that Finland could not fight on for more than 3 months, its parliament approved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the morning it was signed in September 1944.","Mannerheim, made president in August 1944, renounced Ryti's agreement with Germany and opened the talks that ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, under which Finland owed reparations of 300 million dollars in goods."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Armistice</b> was the agreement signed in Moscow on 19 September 1944 that took Finland out of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Finland had fought beside Germany since 1941, and in the summer of 1944 President Risto Ryti had signed an agreement with Germany that Mannerheim later called a contract made in a desperate hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fall of Pskov and Narva in July threatened the sea routes on which Finland depended for food and military supplies, and at a secret meeting on 28 July its leaders decided that Ryti should resign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parliament made Marshal Carl Gustaf Mannerheim president on 4 August, and when Keitel visited on 17 August Mannerheim told him the agreement was void with Ryti's resignation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 25 August Finland asked whether Moscow would receive a delegation, and the reply demanded a break with Germany and the departure of all German troops by 15 September, or their disarmament by the Finns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Parliament accepted on 2 September and Mannerheim proposed a ceasefire for the talks; the Finns stopped firing at seven in the morning of 4 September, while the Soviet side went on for another day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The terms, presented a week after the delegation arrived, restored the 1940 frontier, gave the Soviet Union the whole Pechenga region and a 50-year lease on Porkkala within artillery range of Helsinki, and set reparations at 300 million dollars in goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the morning of 19 September Parliament approved them after being told that Finland could not continue the war for more than another 3 months, and the armistice was signed shortly before noon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vyshinsky told the American ambassador that the only substantial changes were 2.5 months instead of two to put the army on a peacetime footing and 6 years instead of five to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 28 September that the Finnish and Romanian terms bore the imprint of the Soviet will yet showed restraint, and that Finns and Germans were already fighting each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 387–89, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968; repr. 2002), 390–91, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, August 26, 1944, 7 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d551. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, September 4, 1944, noon,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d553. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State, September 19, 1944, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944, The British Commonwealth and Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d560. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 September 1944, vol. 403, cols. 487–89, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Finland seek terms when its army had just won a battle?","a":"Because the Soviet advance along the Baltic coast threatened the sea routes that brought its food and weapons, and Mannerheim told Keitel that Finland could not endure a second bloodletting like the 60,000 casualties of the summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> News that Romania had sued for an armistice added urgency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow insist that the German troops leave Finland?","a":"Because a Finland that kept German forces would still be a base against the Soviet Union, so the preliminary terms required their departure by 15 September or their disarmament by the Finns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that Finns and Germans were already fighting and the Germans were burning villages as they went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the reparations lower than first demanded?","a":"Because the earlier demand of 600 million dollars had been cut, partly after a Finnish campaign using Swedish economists and partly to meet American and British objections, though the Finns later said the price basis nearly doubled the real value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The final text allowed 6 years to pay instead of five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/End_of_Soviet_Finland_war_1944.jpg","title":"Ceasefire talks on the Ilomantsi front, 5 September 1944","desc":"Finnish officers and a soldier carrying a white flag meet Soviet officers on the road on the Ilomantsi front on 5 September 1944, at local talks held as the fighting stopped.","credit":"Photograph by the Finnish Army, SA-kuva, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:End_of_Soviet_Finland_war_1944.jpg","alt":"On a forest road, soldiers in field caps stand facing a group of officers further along, one of them holding a white flag on a pole."},"wiki":"Moscow Armistice"},
    "ww2-331": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the victors a free hand but no licence to behave in a barbarous manner towards the German people.","Signed at Reims on 7 May 1945, the German act of military surrender fulfilled the Allied demand for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first proclaimed at Casablanca more than 2 years earlier."],"abstract":"<b>Unconditional surrender</b> was the Allied war aim, announced at Casablanca in January 1943, that the Axis powers would be fought until they gave themselves up without any agreed terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Roosevelt announced it to reporters on the lawn of his villa on 24 January, saying that the elimination of German, Japanese and Italian war power meant the unconditional surrender of Germany, Italy and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It did not mean the destruction of their populations, he said, but the destruction of the philosophies in those countries which were based on conquest and the subjugation of other peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reminded the press of the Civil War general nicknamed 'Unconditional Surrender' Grant and suggested they might call the gathering the 'unconditional surrender' meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two leaders had discussed the formula beforehand, and when Churchill hesitated over applying it to Italy the British War Cabinet urged that Italy should not be exempted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its first trial came with Italy's surrender in September 1943, an inconclusive test because German troops still held most of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Moscow in October 1943 the United States, Britain, the Soviet Union and China pledged together to fight on until the Axis powers laid down their arms on the basis of unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that it meant the victors would have a free hand, not that they were entitled to behave in a barbarous manner or to blot Germany out from among the nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> British and American intelligence staffs judged that winter that German propaganda was presenting the policy as a plan to exterminate the nation, and some planners asked whether it should be restated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It held all the same, and at Reims on 7 May 1945 Jodl signed for the German High Command an act surrendering unconditionally to the Western Allies and simultaneously to the Soviet High Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"Transcript of Press Conference, Casablanca, January 24, 1943,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d395. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Draft Statement to the Press, Casablanca, January 20–22?, 1943,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, 834–36, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d448. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 37–41, 428–30, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1943-1944. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 22 February 1944, vol. 397, col. 699, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","\"The Moscow Conference; October 1943: Joint Four-Nation Declaration,\" from <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","\"Act of Military Surrender Signed at Rheims at 0241 on the 7th Day of May, 1945,\" from <i>Treaties and Other International Agreements of the United States of America, 1776–1949</i>, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gs3.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, \"The Casablanca Conference, 1943,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/casablanca. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Roosevelt choose a slogan rather than set out peace terms?","a":"The Army's official history reads it as a simple formula that rallied the Allies and told friend and foe that this time there would be no negotiated peace and no escape clauses like those of the Fourteen Points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It may also have reassured the Russians, disappointed that no second front had yet opened, that the Western powers meant to fight to the finish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill gave the Commons the same reason: no German argument like the one after the last war, that Germany had surrendered on the strength of Wilson's Fourteen Points, would be admitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Italy a poor test of the policy?","a":"When Italy surrendered in September 1943 the Germans still controlled most of the country and many Italian forces, so the new Italian government could not make its own orders to surrender obeyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy then declared war on Germany, and some of the harsher surrender terms were held in abeyance while it showed its good faith by fighting on the Allied side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some Allied staffs want the formula restated in 1944?","a":"German propaganda presented it as proof that the Allies meant to exterminate the German nation and enslave its people, and with no Allied statement contradicting this it was stiffening the will to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill had already tried to answer the charge, telling the Commons that unconditional surrender did not mean the German people would be enslaved or destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The formula was never formally changed, and the German act of surrender of May 1945 was unconditional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/GenUSGrant.jpg","title":"Ulysses S. Grant, the 'Unconditional Surrender' general","desc":"A Civil War portrait of Ulysses S. Grant, the Union general whose nickname, 'Unconditional Surrender' Grant, Roosevelt recalled to reporters at Casablanca on 24 January 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division (cwpbh.01058), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GenUSGrant.jpg","alt":"A bearded man in a dark double-breasted uniform with star-marked shoulder straps, seated and looking off to one side."},"wiki":"Unconditional surrender"},
    "ww2-332": {"questions":["Held in the Soviet legation because the American one lay outside the city walls, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fixed May 1944 for the cross-Channel invasion with a Soviet offensive timed alongside it.","Churchill recalled the three matches he had used to show how Poland might shift westward, and at the same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Stalin promised war on Japan once Germany was beaten."],"abstract":"The <b>Tehran Conference</b> was the first meeting of Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin together, held in the Iranian capital from 28 November to 1 December 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill had written to Stalin in September that he would come anywhere at any risk for such a meeting, and accepted Tehran though he would have preferred Cyprus or Khartoum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Because the American legation lay outside the city walls, Stalin offered Roosevelt the security of the Soviet compound, and the three plenary sessions were held in the Soviet legation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the first session Roosevelt said they sat around the table for the first time as a family, and Stalin, after a brief reply, said 'Now let us get down to business.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The leaders' public declaration spoke of concerted plans for the destruction of the German forces and ended with the words 'We leave here, friends in fact, in spirit and in purpose.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its military conclusions recorded that Overlord, the cross-Channel invasion, would be launched in May 1944 with a landing in southern France, and that Soviet forces would attack at about the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin put Soviet weight behind that plan against Churchill's case for more operations in the eastern Mediterranean, and said the Soviet Union would join the war against Japan once Germany was beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On Poland, Churchill recalled the three matches he had used one evening to show a possible solution, the leaders examined maps of the Curzon Line, and he proposed compensation in the west up to the Oder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt had told Stalin privately that with six to seven million Polish-American voters and an election in 1944 he could take no public part in any such arrangement for the time being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Home again, Roosevelt said he had 'got along fine' with Stalin, while Churchill told the Commons in February that the two had agreed Poland should be compensated at Germany's expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Tehran Conference, November 28–December 1, 1943,\" from <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tehran.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Bohlen Minutes, December 1, 1943, 3:20 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, 594–95, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d378. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Bohlen Minutes, December 1, 1943, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, 596–604, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d379. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 356–61, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1943-1944. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, \"The Tehran Conference, 1943,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/tehran-conf. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Fireside Chat,\" December 24, 1943, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 22 February 1944, vol. 397, cols. 697–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the USSR, <i>Stalin's Correspondence with Churchill, Attlee, Roosevelt and Truman, 1941–45</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1958), 159–60, https://archive.org/details/stalinscorrespon010909mbp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the three leaders meet in Tehran?","a":"Stalin wanted it there, and Churchill, who had told him he would come anywhere at any risk for a meeting of the three, deferred to his wishes although he would have preferred Cyprus or Khartoum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Iran also mattered to the alliance in its own right: the leaders thanked it in a separate declaration for helping to carry supplies to the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin back the cross-Channel invasion over Mediterranean operations?","a":"He argued that Soviet experience showed the value of converging pincer operations, such as simultaneous landings in northern and southern France, and he regarded other Mediterranean operations as diversions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The military conclusions duly recorded Overlord for May 1944, together with an operation against southern France and a Soviet offensive at about the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt want nothing about Poland made public?","a":"He told Stalin that he might have to run again in 1944 and did not wish to lose the votes of six to seven million Americans of Polish extraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He said he personally favoured moving Poland's borders westward, but could not take part publicly in any such arrangement at that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill, by contrast, set out the idea of compensation at Germany's expense to the House of Commons in February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Teheran_conference-1943.jpg/1280px-Teheran_conference-1943.jpg","title":"Stalin, Roosevelt and Churchill at Tehran","desc":"From left, Joseph Stalin, Franklin D. Roosevelt and Winston Churchill on the portico of the Soviet embassy during the Tehran Conference, between 28 November and 1 December 1943.","credit":"U.S. Signal Corps photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teheran_conference-1943.jpg","alt":"Three men seated side by side in armchairs on a terrace before arched windows: one in a military tunic and cap, one in a dark suit, one in a uniform."},"wiki":"Tehran Conference"},
    "ww2-333": {"questions":["Stalin's warning to Churchill in June 1943 that Soviet confidence in its allies was under severe stress marked a low point for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the second front was postponed again.","Calling it the anti-Hitlerite coalition, Stalin insisted in November 1943 that relations within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had grown stronger, contrary to the enemy's hopes, as he hailed the Moscow Conference."],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Alliance</b> was the wartime coalition of Britain, the Soviet Union and the United States against Germany, which took shape in 1941 and 1942 as each power was drawn into the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The US Army's official history calls it a war marriage of expediency, held together by common danger while each partner looked at the European war through a separate pair of spectacles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, in that reading, sought a return to the balance of power in Europe, while the Soviet Union, suspicious of friend and foe alike, never lost sight of strengthening its position in eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inner web of the alliance was the close relationship between Britain and the United States, and the Soviet Union, fighting on one front far from its partners, stood apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin, who called it the anti-Hitlerite coalition, told a Moscow audience in November 1943 that relations among the Allies had grown stronger, contrary to the expectations of the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The sharpest strain was the second front, which Stalin complained in June 1943 had been postponed from 1942 to 1943 and now again to the spring of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He protested that the decision had been taken without Soviet participation and warned Churchill that Soviet confidence in its allies was being subjected to severe stress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poland was the other wound, since Britain had gone to war in fulfilment of its guarantee to Poland while Moscow held to the frontier of 1939 and accused the exile government's agents of killing partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Even so, the Moscow conference of October 1943 pledged the major Allies to continue their united action into the peace, and at Tehran the three leaders declared that they left friends in fact, in spirit and in purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that he felt sympathy for both the Poles and the Russians, and that Russia's demand for reassurance about its western frontiers was not beyond what was reasonable or just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1943-1944. [Open access]","Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the USSR, <i>Stalin's Correspondence with Churchill, Attlee, Roosevelt and Truman, 1941–45</i> (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1958), 131–38, https://archive.org/details/stalinscorrespon010909mbp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"War and International Situation,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 22 February 1944, vol. 397, cols. 697–98, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Speech at Celebration Meeting of the Moscow Soviet of Working People's Deputies and Moscow Party and Public Organisations,\" 6 November 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/11/06s.htm. [Open access]","\"The Moscow Conference; October 1943: Joint Four-Nation Declaration,\" from <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Bohlen Minutes, December 1, 1943, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, 596–604, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d379. [Open access]","\"The Tehran Conference, November 28–December 1, 1943,\" from <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tehran.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the partners disagree so often?","a":"Each came to the war with different traditions, policies, interests, geography and resources, so that, in the words of the official history, each looked at the European war through a separate pair of spectacles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain thought in terms of the balance of power and its Mediterranean route to the empire, while the Soviet Union sought security and a stronger position in eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the second front cause such bitterness?","a":"The Soviet Union had been fighting the main German forces for 2 years and had been told the invasion of western Europe would come in 1942 and then in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stalin wrote that the latest postponement left the Soviet army to do the job almost single-handed, and that it had been decided without any Soviet participation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Poland so hard for Britain in particular?","a":"Churchill reminded the Commons that Britain had declared war in fulfilment of its guarantee to Poland, yet he also accepted that Russia had a right to reassurance on its western frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Tehran the Soviet side held to the 1939 line and said it would welcome relations only with a Polish government that stopped the killing of partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/The_Prime_Minister_in_behalf_of_King_George_VI_of_Great_Britain%2C_presents_The_Sword_of_Stalingrad_to_Stalin%2C_for_the..._-_NARA_-_195332.jpg","title":"The Sword of Stalingrad presented at Tehran","desc":"The presentation of the Sword of Stalingrad, King George VI's gift to the citizens of Stalingrad, in the Soviet embassy at Tehran on or about 29 November 1943.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, National Archives and Records Administration (195332), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Prime_Minister_in_behalf_of_King_George_VI_of_Great_Britain,_presents_The_Sword_of_Stalingrad_to_Stalin,_for_the..._-_NARA_-_195332.jpg","alt":"In a room lit by a large chandelier and crowded with officers and diplomats, a man in uniform holds a long sword upright beside its open case on a table."}},
    "ww2-334": {"questions":["Litvinov balked at 'freedom of religion' but accepted 'religious freedom' once Roosevelt reversed the words, the president told his cabinet after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was signed on New Year's Day 1942.","Subscribing to the Atlantic Charter and open to any nation helping in the struggle against Hitlerism, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was later joined by Mexico and, early in 1945, Turkey and Egypt."],"abstract":"The <b>Declaration by United Nations</b> was the joint statement, signed in Washington on 1 January 1942, in which the governments at war with the Axis bound themselves together and set out what they were fighting for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 26 signatories subscribed to the purposes and principles of the Atlantic Charter, the statement of common principles that Roosevelt and Churchill had issued in August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They declared complete victory essential to defend life, liberty, independence and religious freedom, and to preserve human rights and justice in their own lands as well as in other lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each pledged its full military or economic resources against the members of the Tripartite Pact with which it was at war, and promised not to make a separate armistice or peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stayed open to other nations rendering material help in the struggle against Hitlerism, and adherents ran from Mexico in June 1942 to Turkey, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Lebanon and Syria early in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The title was Roosevelt's, proposed on the morning of the signing, and the words 'or authorities' in the closing clause were dropped from the signed text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His adviser Harry Hopkins urged him to get religious freedom into the document, to list as many small countries as possible, and to leave out the Free French for the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Soviet ambassador Maxim Litvinov objected to 'freedom of religion' but consented once Roosevelt turned the words round to 'religious freedom', as the president later told his cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hopkins could not understand British hesitation over including India, and India duly signed through Girja Shankar Bajpai among the original signatories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The declaration became the reference point for later Allied pledges, and the four-power declaration at Moscow in October 1943 cited it when promising to fight on until unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["\"Declaration by the United Nations, January 1, 1942,\" from <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade03.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Declaration by United Nations, January First, 1942,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, General; the British Commonwealth; the Far East, Volume I</i>, 25–26, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v01/d18. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Draft Declaration of Allied Unity, January 1, 1942,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, 376–77, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d228. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Editorial Note\" on the Roosevelt–Litvinov luncheon of December 27, 1941, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d66. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum by the President's Special Assistant (Hopkins), December 27, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d216. [Open access]","\"The Moscow Conference; October 1943: Joint Four-Nation Declaration,\" from <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","\"Atlantic Charter, August 14, 1941,\" Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/atlantic.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the declaration rest on the Atlantic Charter?","a":"The Charter already set out a common program of purposes and principles, so adopting it let 26 governments state their aims in a single clause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hopkins thought the bare reference was not enough and wanted a fuller restatement of aims for human freedom, justice and security for all peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the pledge worded only against enemies each signatory was fighting?","a":"Hopkins warned that the draft wording of the first paragraph would be very difficult for the Soviet Union to subscribe to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The signed text bound each government only against those members of the Tripartite Pact with which it was actually at war, while every signatory promised to make no separate armistice or peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a wartime alliance text mention religious freedom?","a":"Roosevelt, prompted by Hopkins, pressed hard to have it included, and Hopkins urged him to raise it with Litvinov in person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Litvinov objected to 'freedom of religion', and Roosevelt won his consent by turning the phrase round to 'religious freedom'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/%22Declaration_by_the_United_Nations%22_-_DPLA_-_2eb656b0bb2c1b4263dfd432f6b5be44_%28page_2%29.jpg/1920px-%22Declaration_by_the_United_Nations%22_-_DPLA_-_2eb656b0bb2c1b4263dfd432f6b5be44_%28page_2%29.jpg","title":"A facsimile of the Declaration by United Nations","desc":"A wartime facsimile of the Declaration by United Nations from the Office of War Information, with the typed text at left and the signatures of the original signatories and later adherents up to 1945.","credit":"Office of War Information, National Archives and Records Administration, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Declaration_by_the_United_Nations%22_-_DPLA_-_2eb656b0bb2c1b4263dfd432f6b5be44_(page_2).jpg","alt":"A long, wide sheet headed Declaration by United Nations, with a column of typed text on the left and rows of handwritten signatures and dates filling the rest."},"wiki":"Declaration by United Nations"},
    "ww2-335": {"questions":["Naming Stalingrad as the decline of the German army and Kursk as its disaster, Stalin called 1943 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the war in his speech for the revolution's anniversary.","Stalin credited Allied blows in North Africa, the Mediterranean and southern Italy with easing the summer campaign of 1943, the year of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though they were not yet a second front."],"abstract":"The <b>radical turning point</b> is the phrase, in the official English translation, that Stalin used for the year of war from November 1942 to November 1943, when the Eastern Front swung decisively in the Soviet Union's favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his speech on the eve of the revolution's 26th anniversary, on 6 November 1943, he headed the first part of his review 'A Year Marking a Radical Turn in the Course of the War'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a turning point, he said, above all because the Red Army had for the first time in the war carried through a big summer offensive against the German troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalingrad, in his words, had signified the decline of the German-fascist army, while the battle of Kursk had confronted it with disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He claimed a turn on the home front and in the whole world war as well, with Italy out of the fascist bloc and Germany's other allies anxious to find a way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The US Army's official history frames the summer of 1943 around two questions: whether Germany could again make a strong bid for victory, and whether the Soviet army could win without its old ally, winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Operation Citadel at Kursk answered the first, it says, and in the following 2.5 months the Soviet army erased the last doubt about the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stalin's order of 5 August, marking the recapture of Orel and Belgorod, declared dispelled the German myth that Soviet troops could not wage a successful offensive in summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By November the Red Army had pushed the southern front back about 250 kilometres (155 miles), crossed the Dnieper and retaken Kiev, and the German army was afterwards capable only of defensive stopping actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stalin also allowed that Allied operations in North Africa, the Mediterranean and southern Italy and the bombing of German industry had eased the Soviet summer campaign, though they were not yet a second front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["J. V. Stalin, \"Speech at Celebration Meeting of the Moscow Soviet of Working People's Deputies and Moscow Party and Public Organisations,\" 6 November 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/11/06s.htm. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 143, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Order of the Day Addressed to Colonel-Generals Popov, Sokolovsky, and Koniev, and Army-Generals Rokossovsky and Vatutin,\" 5 August 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/08/05.htm. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1943,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1943. [in German] [Open access]","Christian Cunningham, \"Why Fight On? The German Decision to Close the Kursk Salient\" (strategy research project, U.S. Army War College, 2004), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA423367. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin single out the summer offensive?","a":"Until 1943 the Red Army had won its great successes in winter, and Stalin stressed that for the first time it had carried through a big summer offensive against the German troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His order of 5 August said the recapture of Orel and Belgorod dispelled the German myth that Soviet troops could not attack successfully in summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin rank Kursk alongside Stalingrad?","a":"He said the defeat of the German troops on the Soviet front had been predetermined by those two battles: Stalingrad signified the decline of the German army, while Kursk confronted it with disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kursk, he added, was the last German attempt at a big summer offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the phrase belong to 1943 rather than to Stalingrad alone?","a":"The official US Army history makes the same point from the other side: Citadel showed that Germany could no longer make a strong bid for victory, and the summer and autumn showed the Soviet army could win without winter's help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Kursk the Germans lost the strategic initiative and could only fight defensive stopping actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/%D0%93%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%9C%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8F%D0%BD_%D0%92.%D0%90._%D0%B8_%D0%B5%D1%84%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%90._%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B6%D0%B0%D1%8E%D1%82_%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8_%D0%B8%D0%BC._%D0%94%D0%B7%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B2_%D0%A5%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5_23_%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B3%D1%83%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0_1943_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0.jpg","title":"A flag raised over liberated Kharkov","desc":"Guards Major V. A. Muradyan and Lance Corporal A. Tsarev raise a flag on Dzerzhinsky Square in Kharkov on 23 August 1943, the day the city was retaken.","credit":"Photograph by Dmitry Chernov, TASS, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%93%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%9C%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8F%D0%BD_%D0%92.%D0%90._%D0%B8_%D0%B5%D1%84%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%90._%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B6%D0%B0%D1%8E%D1%82_%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8_%D0%B8%D0%BC._%D0%94%D0%B7%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B2_%D0%A5%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8C%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5_23_%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B3%D1%83%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0_1943_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"Two Soviet soldiers in peaked caps on a high vantage point, one waving a large flag, with tall modernist office blocks behind them."}},
    "ww2-336": {"questions":["Fought around a Soviet-held bulge about 150 kilometres (90 miles) long between Orel and Belgorod, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began on 5 July 1943 and ended with the initiative passing for good to the Red Army.","Moscow fired 12 salvoes from 120 guns on 5 August 1943 to mark the recapture of Orel and Belgorod, exactly a month after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had opened with a German offensive."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kursk</b> was the clash of July and August 1943 around the Kursk salient, a Soviet-held bulge in the front about 150 kilometres (90 miles) long, where a German offensive met a prepared Soviet defence and then a Soviet counter-offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 5 July German armies struck from the Orel area in the north and the Belgorod area in the south, aiming to pinch off the bulge and encircle the Soviet forces inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The attack came as no surprise and ran into defences many lines deep, so that Model's Ninth Army advanced only about 10 kilometres (6 miles) and Hoth's Fourth Panzer Army just over 30 (19 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 12 July Soviet fronts opened Operation Kutuzov against the Orel salient behind the northern pincer, and on 13 July Hitler called off the offensive, citing the danger at Orel and the Allied landing in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The southern counterstroke, Operation Rumyantsev, followed, Orel and Belgorod were retaken on 5 August to a Moscow salute of 12 salvoes from 120 guns, and Kharkov fell at the end of the month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Counts of the forces differ widely, depending on whose figures are used and which phase and sectors are included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The German Historical Museum gives about 800,000 German soldiers with over 2,000 tanks against about 1.35 million Red Army men with just under 4,000 tanks and assault guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 1998 US Army data study, built from German and Soviet archives, counts 900,000 Germans with 2,700 tanks and assault guns against 1,337,000 Soviet troops with 3,306.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The US Army's 1986 study of the Soviet defence puts the tanks and self-propelled guns of the two Soviet fronts inside the bulge alone at about 5,300, with more than 1,600 more in the reserve Steppe Front behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin called Kursk the last German attempt at a big summer offensive, and after it, in the German Historical Museum's words, the initiative passed for good to the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["David M. Glantz, <i>Soviet Defensive Tactics at Kursk, July 1943</i>, CSI Report No. 11 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1986), 26, https://archive.org/details/soviet-defensive-tactics-at-kursk-russia-july-1943-david-m-glantz-1986. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 124–42, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Schlacht bei Kursk 1943,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 25 August 2022, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-kursk-1943. [in German] [Open access]","Walter J. Bauman, <i>Kursk Operation Simulation and Validation Exercise – Phase II (KOSAVE II)</i>, Study Report CAA-SR-98-7 (Bethesda, MD: U.S. Army Concepts Analysis Agency, 1998), 2-2–2-3, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA360311. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Speech at Celebration Meeting of the Moscow Soviet of Working People's Deputies and Moscow Party and Public Organisations,\" 6 November 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/11/06s.htm. [Open access]","Kerry K. Pierce, \"Kursk: A Study in Operational Art\" (monograph, School of Advanced Military Studies, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1987), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA184964. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1943,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1943. [in German] [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Order of the Day Addressed to Colonel-Generals Popov, Sokolovsky, and Koniev, and Army-Generals Rokossovsky and Vatutin,\" 5 August 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/08/05.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the German attack achieve no surprise?","a":"The plan, a thrust south from Orel and north from Belgorod, was logical to a fault and had not the slightest chance of surprise, and it was postponed for months while the Russians dug in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By June the armies faced a fortified front miles deep, backed by waves of reserves, the opposite of the conditions first expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for the battle disagree so much?","a":"Each count rests on different records and draws its line round the battle differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The German Historical Museum, a 1998 US Army data study built from both armies' archives, and the US Army's 1986 study of the Soviet defence each give different totals for men and tanks, and none should be quoted as the single true figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler stop the offensive on 13 July?","a":"The Orel salient behind his northern army was in danger, and the Allies had landed in Sicily on 10 July, so troops would have to be taken from the East to defend Italy and the Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manstein protested that the battle in the south was just reaching its turning point, but Kluge said Ninth Army could not advance again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/%D0%A0%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%97%D0%B8%D0%A1-3_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8C_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5.jpg/1280px-%D0%A0%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%97%D0%B8%D0%A1-3_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8C_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5.jpg","title":"A Soviet ZiS-3 gun in action at Kursk","desc":"A camouflaged Soviet 76-millimetre ZiS-3 divisional gun firing on the Orel–Kursk direction of the Kursk bulge, photographed on 6 July 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Pavel Troshkin, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A0%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%83%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%97%D0%B8%D0%A1-3_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8C_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5.jpg","alt":"A gun crew in field caps crouch around a field gun hidden among bushes, one holding a shell, while an officer in the foreground watches through binoculars."},"wiki":"Battle of Kursk"},
    "ww2-337": {"questions":["Hitler's April order said victory at Kursk must be a beacon seen around the world, but <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was postponed again and again while he waited for Panther tanks and Ferdinand assault guns.","Jodl told Wehrmacht propaganda to present <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a counter-offensive, building an alibi in advance in case the German attack on the Kursk bulge failed."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Citadel</b> was the German offensive of July 1943 against the Kursk salient, meant to cut off the bulge with two converging thrusts from Orel and Belgorod and destroy the Soviet armies inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hitler first ordered it in Operations Order 5 of 13 March, as part of a series of limited offensives to steady the front after the winter defeats and strike before the Russians did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two attacking armies made up the entire German strategic reserve, which could not be quickly replaced if it were lost or tied down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Operations Order 6 in April declared that the victory at Kursk must have the effect of a beacon seen around the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The start, first meant for early May, slipped again and again, chiefly because Hitler wanted the new Panther tanks and Ferdinand assault guns in quantity and Model needed time to prepare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At a conference in Munich on 4 May, Model argued that his tanks could not stand up to the new Soviet anti-tank weapons, while Manstein, Kluge and Zeitzler warned that delay would help the Russians more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guderian and Speer opposed the attack altogether because of the tank losses it would cost, and a week later Hitler admitted to Guderian that the thought of it gave him butterflies in his stomach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fixed 5 July only late in June, by when the armies faced a fortified front miles deep, and Jodl told propaganda to present the operation as a counter-offensive in case it failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the north alone over 1,200 tanks and self-propelled guns were assigned, about 900 of them immediately ready, to strike on a front of less than 30 kilometres (19 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler called the offensive off on 13 July, though fighting in the south went on for days, and the German Historical Museum calls it the Wehrmacht's last great offensive on the Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 124–42, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Kerry K. Pierce, \"Kursk: A Study in Operational Art\" (monograph, School of Advanced Military Studies, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1987), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA184964. [Open access]","Christian Cunningham, \"Why Fight On? The German Decision to Close the Kursk Salient\" (strategy research project, U.S. Army War College, 2004), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA423367. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Schlacht bei Kursk 1943,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 25 August 2022, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-kursk-1943. [in German] [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>Soviet Defensive Tactics at Kursk, July 1943</i>, CSI Report No. 11 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1986), 25, https://archive.org/details/soviet-defensive-tactics-at-kursk-russia-july-1943-david-m-glantz-1986. [Open access]","Walter J. Bauman, <i>Kursk Operation Simulation and Validation Exercise – Phase II (KOSAVE II)</i>, Study Report CAA-SR-98-7 (Bethesda, MD: U.S. Army Concepts Analysis Agency, 1998), 2-2–2-3, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA360311. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Order of the Day Addressed to Colonel-Generals Popov, Sokolovsky, and Koniev, and Army-Generals Rokossovsky and Vatutin,\" 5 August 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/08/05.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler keep postponing the attack?","a":"He wanted the new Panther tanks and Ferdinand assault guns in quantity, after Model reported that his heaviest tanks could not stand up to the new Soviet anti-tank weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Model's Ninth Army also needed more time to gather the men, supplies and equipment for the northern pincer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> And Hitler had doubts of his own: the two armies were the whole strategic reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was surprise impossible?","a":"The plan, a straight thrust south from Orel and north from Belgorod, was logical to a fault, and every week of delay let the Russians dig in more deeply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the time it began the attackers had to grind through a fortified front miles deep and backed by waves of reserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler cancel it on 13 July?","a":"Soviet attacks were threatening the Orel salient behind the northern army, and the Allies had landed in Sicily 3 days earlier, so he needed troops for Italy and the Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The southern attack went on for some days more, but its territorial gains were soon given up as the Soviet counter-offensive developed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-022-2924-31%2C_Russland%2C_Unternehmen_%22Zitadelle%22%2C_Infanterie.jpg","title":"German infantry during the preparations for Citadel","desc":"German infantry passing through a destroyed village, filed by the Bundesarchiv under Unternehmen Zitadelle and dated 21 June 1943, two weeks before the offensive began.","credit":"Photograph by Kipper, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-022-2924-31, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-022-2924-31%2C_Russland%2C_Unternehmen_%22Zitadelle%22%2C_Infanterie.jpg","alt":"Four German soldiers with helmets, packs and equipment walk past ruined walls on a dusty track, behind a wooden barrier strung with barbed wire."},"wiki":"Operation Citadel"},
    "ww2-338": {"questions":["Created as a reserve behind the Kursk bulge in the spring of 1943 and named on 9 July, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held more than 1,600 tanks behind defences many belts deep.","Troops of the Voronezh Front and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> retook Belgorod on 5 August 1943, a month after the German offensive against the Kursk salient had opened."],"abstract":"The <b>Steppe Front</b> was the Soviet strategic reserve that stood behind the Kursk salient in the summer of 1943, the deepest layer of a defence built many lines deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Having decided to receive the German attack and then counter-attack, the Soviet command formed a special reserve behind the bulge in the spring, which became the Steppe Military District and on 9 July the Steppe Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Commanded by Ivan Konev, it held several combined-arms armies, the 5th Guards Tank Army and further tank, mechanised and cavalry corps, with more than 1,600 tanks by one count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its task, in the US Army's 1986 study of the defence, was to ensure that no German operational breakthrough occurred and to supply the strength for the planned Soviet counter-attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German intelligence failed to detect it before the battle, and with it much of the Soviet strategic reserve gathered behind the salient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In front of it the Central and Voronezh Fronts each built three army defensive belts with two more front belts behind them, and the Central Front alone dug nearly 5,000 kilometres (3,000 miles) of trenches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Engineers laid minefields to densities of 1,700 anti-personnel and 1,500 anti-tank mines per kilometre of front, thickest in and before the first defensive position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south, a US Army data study counts eight Soviet defensive lines, seven of which the German attack breached before it stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the Voronezh Front's reserves ran out, the 5th Guards Army and 5th Guards Tank Army were sent forward, and Konev protested violently against this piecemeal use of the front he commanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its remaining armies joined the counter-offensive, and on 5 August troops of the Steppe and Voronezh Fronts took Belgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["David M. Glantz, <i>Soviet Defensive Tactics at Kursk, July 1943</i>, CSI Report No. 11 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1986), 19, 26, https://archive.org/details/soviet-defensive-tactics-at-kursk-russia-july-1943-david-m-glantz-1986. [Open access]","Charles L. Crow, \"An Operational Level Analysis of Soviet Armored Formations in the Deliberate Defense in the Battle of Kursk, 1943\" (master's thesis, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1985), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA164605. [Open access]","Christian Cunningham, \"Why Fight On? The German Decision to Close the Kursk Salient\" (strategy research project, U.S. Army War College, 2004), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA423367. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 133–37, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Walter J. Bauman, <i>Kursk Operation Simulation and Validation Exercise – Phase II (KOSAVE II)</i>, Study Report CAA-SR-98-7 (Bethesda, MD: U.S. Army Concepts Analysis Agency, 1998), 2-3, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA360311. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Order of the Day Addressed to Colonel-Generals Popov, Sokolovsky, and Koniev, and Army-Generals Rokossovsky and Vatutin,\" 5 August 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/08/05.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why keep a whole front in reserve rather than in the line?","a":"The Soviet plan was to absorb the German blow and then counter-attack, so a strong reserve had to stop any breakthrough first and then provide the striking force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Konev was told by Stalin that it might be used defensively if necessary, and drew up two counter-attack plans for that case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German intelligence miss it?","a":"Elaborate Soviet deception hid much of the build-up, and the Germans badly underestimated the forces in front of them, a 2004 Army War College study notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It quotes the finding that German intelligence failed to detect ten armies behind the salient, and so knew nothing of much of the strategic reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the defence built so deep?","a":"In 1941 and 1942 German armour had broken shallow Soviet lines, and the Red Army had spent months learning techniques to counter it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Kursk the attacking tanks had to fight through belt after belt of trenches, mines and anti-tank guns that sapped their strength before they met the Soviet reserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%A0%D0%9A%D0%9A%D0%90_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%82_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5_%D0%B2_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D1%83%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%8F%D1%85.jpg/1280px-%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%A0%D0%9A%D0%9A%D0%90_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%82_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5_%D0%B2_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D1%83%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%8F%D1%85.jpg","title":"A Soviet sapper laying mines at Kursk","desc":"A Red Army sapper laying a wooden-cased anti-tank mine at night in front of the German lines on the Orel–Kursk direction, July 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Kazimir Lishko, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%A0%D0%9A%D0%9A%D0%90_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%82_%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5_%D0%B2_%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D1%83%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%8F%D1%85.jpg","alt":"A helmeted soldier crouches in long grass at night, placing a box-shaped mine, with a bright light in the dark sky behind him."},"wiki":"Steppe Front"},
    "ww2-339": {"questions":["Soviet-based accounts claim over 350 German tanks lost in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while unit records compiled in the 1990s show only a handful destroyed against 144 Soviet tanks that day.","Army Detachment Kempf's panzers crossed the Northern Donets before dawn on 12 July but were held off, so the SS fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone against the 5th Guards Tank Army."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Prokhorovka</b> was the tank battle of 12 July 1943 near the village of Prokhorovka, on the southern face of the Kursk salient, where a Soviet counter-attack met the II SS Panzer Corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a week of fighting through the Soviet defences, the Fourth Panzer Army had swung its spearhead north-east towards Prokhorovka once the road to Oboyan was blocked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There it ran into Lieutenant General Rotmistrov's reinforced 5th Guards Tank Army, which had force-marched about 400 kilometres (250 miles) from the reserve to assemble north-east of the village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Further south Army Detachment Kempf's III Panzer Corps seized a bridgehead over the Northern Donets before dawn to join the SS, but was held off and never reached the battlefield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Soviet attack went in after a 15-minute barrage, and the tanks closed at speed, since at close range the heavier German guns and armour counted for less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The numbers engaged and lost are fiercely disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Accounts drawn from the Soviet record, followed in a 1995 US Army staff college thesis, set 850 Soviet tanks against about 600 German, about 100 of them Tigers, and claim over 350 German tanks lost by nightfall, 70 of them Tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A 1987 staff college monograph gives the same starting strengths but puts the losses at around 300 tanks on each side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kursk Data Base, compiled in the 1990s from German and Soviet unit records, instead records 144 tanks of the three Soviet tank corps destroyed near Prokhorovka that day against only a handful of German tanks, though many more on both sides were damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whatever the count, the drive on Prokhorovka was the high-water mark of the German offensive, and on 13 July Hitler called the whole operation off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Walter J. Bauman, <i>Kursk Operation Simulation and Validation Exercise – Phase II (KOSAVE II)</i>, Study Report CAA-SR-98-7 (Bethesda, MD: U.S. Army Concepts Analysis Agency, 1998), 2-2–2-3, 8-5, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA360311. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>Soviet Defensive Tactics at Kursk, July 1943</i>, CSI Report No. 11 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1986), https://archive.org/details/soviet-defensive-tactics-at-kursk-russia-july-1943-david-m-glantz-1986. [Open access]","Kerry K. Pierce, \"Kursk: A Study in Operational Art\" (monograph, School of Advanced Military Studies, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1987), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA184964. [Open access]","Enrique Ramos, \"Analysis and Significance of the Battle of Kursk in July 1943\" (master's thesis, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1995), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA299270. [Open access]","Christian Cunningham, \"Why Fight On? The German Decision to Close the Kursk Salient\" (strategy research project, U.S. Army War College, 2004), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA423367. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 137–38, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Charles L. Crow, \"An Operational Level Analysis of Soviet Armored Formations in the Deliberate Defense in the Battle of Kursk, 1943\" (master's thesis, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1985), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA164605. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the reported tank losses differ so much?","a":"Early accounts rested on Soviet claims, repeated in Western studies such as a 1995 staff college thesis that gives over 350 German tanks lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Kursk Data Base was built in the 1990s from both armies' unit records and counts destroyed and damaged tanks separately, which gives a very different picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet tanks try to fight at close range?","a":"At short range the size of the guns and the strength of the armour meant less, and only speed and numbers counted, so Rotmistrov ordered his units to close with the enemy as fast as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In such a fight the Tiger could not make full use of its heavier armour and firepower against the faster T-34.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kempf's failure to arrive matter?","a":"Without his three panzer divisions the SS faced the Soviet tank army alone, and the battle did not go as Hoth and Manstein had planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His corps had crossed the Northern Donets before dawn on 12 July but could not reach the battlefield in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Soviet_troops_and_T-34_tanks_counterattacking_Kursk_Voronezh_Front_July_1943.jpg/1280px-Soviet_troops_and_T-34_tanks_counterattacking_Kursk_Voronezh_Front_July_1943.jpg","title":"Soviet infantry and T-34s counter-attacking","desc":"Soviet infantry advancing behind T-34 tanks through smoke, described on Commons as Voronezh Front troops counter-attacking at Prokhorovka on 12 July 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Ministry of Defence of the Russian Federation (mil.ru), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soviet_troops_and_T-34_tanks_counterattacking_Kursk_Voronezh_Front_July_1943.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in helmets with rifles and submachine guns run forward through tall grass behind two tanks, with black smoke billowing ahead of them."},"wiki":"Battle of Prokhorovka"},
    "ww2-340": {"questions":["Model's armies fell back into the Hagen position by 18 August after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke into the Orel salient, and Orel itself was liberated on 5 August 1943.","Stalin's order of 5 August 1943 hailed the capture of Orel in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the end of the German myth that Soviet troops could not win a summer offensive."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Kutuzov</b> was the Soviet offensive against the German-held Orel salient, north of Kursk, launched on 12 July 1943 while the German attack on the Kursk bulge was still under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troops of the Western, Bryansk and Central Fronts struck the Second Panzer Army, which held a front of about 270 kilometres (170 miles) with 14 divisions and could not stop quick, deep penetrations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before noon Kluge had to send the divisions meant for the Ninth Army's attack on Kursk to the Second Panzer Army instead, and that evening to take two panzer divisions from Model's assault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler gave Model command of both armies, and Model, a specialist in stubborn defence, was allowed from 22 July to conduct an elastic defence through heavy rain and under constant Soviet air attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Germans were back in the Hagen position across the base of the salient by 18 August, having lost the equivalent of 14 divisions but kept a coherent front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Orel itself fell on 5 August, the same day as Belgorod, and Stalin's order of the day said the German myth that Soviet troops could not attack successfully in summer had been dispelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moscow saluted the victory with 12 salvoes from 120 guns, and the divisions that first broke into the two towns took the towns' names as titles of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stalin later called the battle of Kursk the last German attempt at a big summer offensive, ending in a counter-offensive that turned into the Red Army's summer offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After it the German army was capable only of defensive stopping actions in the East, a 2004 US Army War College study concludes, and the German Historical Museum says the initiative passed for good to the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The museum counts about 8,400 Soviet tanks against 2,300 German in the summer offensive that followed, which by November had pushed the southern front about 250 kilometres (155 miles) west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Kerry K. Pierce, \"Kursk: A Study in Operational Art\" (monograph, School of Advanced Military Studies, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1987), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA184964. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 136–42, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Order of the Day Addressed to Colonel-Generals Popov, Sokolovsky, and Koniev, and Army-Generals Rokossovsky and Vatutin,\" 5 August 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/08/05.htm. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, \"Speech at Celebration Meeting of the Moscow Soviet of Working People's Deputies and Moscow Party and Public Organisations,\" 6 November 1943, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1943/11/06s.htm. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Schlacht bei Kursk 1943,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 25 August 2022, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-bei-kursk-1943. [in German] [Open access]","Christian Cunningham, \"Why Fight On? The German Decision to Close the Kursk Salient\" (strategy research project, U.S. Army War College, 2004), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA423367. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1943,\" <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1943. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the attack on Orel end the German offensive in the north?","a":"The Orel salient was the base behind the German Ninth Army, and once Soviet troops broke into it Kluge had to divert reserves and take panzer divisions and assault guns from the attack on Kursk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Germans had only five reserve divisions in the whole area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans escape encirclement at Orel?","a":"Model fought an elastic defence from fortifications built up over 2 years, and heavy rain, traffic jams and the friction of moving huge formations kept many Soviet troops from the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The withdrawal to the Hagen position was a major tactical achievement, though nobody knew how long the line would hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow fire a salute for Orel and Belgorod?","a":"Stalin's order said that the two towns had been retaken exactly one month after the German offensive began, and that this dispelled the myth that Soviet troops could not wage a successful summer offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He ordered 12 salvoes from 120 guns and gave the leading divisions the towns' names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%80%D1%8B%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%B4%D1%8B%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8B_%D0%B8%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5.jpg/1280px-%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%80%D1%8B%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%B4%D1%8B%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8B_%D0%B8%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5.jpg","title":"A Red Army soldier attacking through smoke near Orel","desc":"A Red Army infantryman advancing behind a smoke screen during the Orel offensive, photographed on 20 July 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Israel Ozersky, RIA Novosti archive, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%80%D1%8B%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%BC_%D0%B4%D1%8B%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8B_%D0%B8%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%83%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B4%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B5.jpg","alt":"Seen from behind, a soldier with a rifle and fixed bayonet strides through wildflowers into thick white smoke."},"wiki":"Operation Kutuzov","places":["city:1159148111"]},
    "ww2-301": {"questions":["Soviet historians date the start of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to 17 July 1942, when two newly created armies met the German advance in the great bend of the Don.","Churchill told the Commons in February 1943 that the hand which planned the attack leading to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had brought the German armies the greatest disaster in their military history."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Stalingrad</b> was the struggle for the industrial city on the lower Volga that began in the summer of 1942 and ended with the destruction of the German Sixth Army in February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet historians date its start to 17 July 1942, when two newly created Soviet armies met the German advance in the great bend of the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Don_(river)\">Don</span>, about 160 kilometres (100 miles) west of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler's directive of 23 July sent one army group south into the Caucasus while the other was to take Stalingrad, with the Luftwaffe assisting by the timely destruction of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From mid-September the fighting inside the city was contested block by block and building by building, and the defenders formed special storm groups for it, the first of their kind in the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 19 November the Soviet counter-offensive broke through the Romanian armies on the flanks, and on 23 November the ring closed around the Sixth Army west of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> How many died is still disputed, and the figures differ by hundreds of thousands according to who counts and over which dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Soviet figures put German losses in the pocket at 147,200 killed and wounded and over 91,000 captured, while the US Army's official history judged them somewhat over 200,000 and noted that the exact total was apparently never determined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Friedrich_Paulus\">Paulus</span> told the Nuremberg tribunal in 1946 that more than 100,000 of his soldiers had died of hunger, cold and snow, and of the prisoners only about 6,000 ever returned to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The official Russian count of Soviet losses for the whole battle is 1,129,619, of whom 478,741 were irrecoverable losses, the dead and the missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that the Germans had suffered the greatest disaster in all their military history, and the German Historical Museum dates to the battle the moment the initiative passed to the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 38–46, 53–58, 79–80, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 359–60, 393–95, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","V. P. Trut, “Osobennosti taktiki vedeniia boevykh deistvii sovetskikh voisk v period Stalingradskoi bitvy” [Features of Soviet tactics in the Battle of Stalingrad], <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriia 4: Istoriia. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniia</i> 23, no. 1 (2018): 13–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-taktiki-vedeniya-boevyh-deystviy-sovetskih-voysk-v-period-stalingradskoy-bitvy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the USSR, <i>Stalin's Correspondence with Churchill, Attlee, Roosevelt and Truman, 1941–45</i> (Moscow, 1957; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1958), 77, https://archive.org/details/stalinscorrespon010909mbp. [Open access]","N. A. Bolotov and V. N. Popov, “Pobeda pod Stalingradom i ee tsena” [The victory at Stalingrad and its price], <i>Via in tempore. Istoriia. Politologiia</i> 9, no. 1 (2009): 120–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pobeda-pod-stalingradom-i-ee-tsena. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","Joel S. A. Hayward, “Stalingrad: An Examination of Hitler's Decision to Airlift,” <i>Airpower Journal</i> 11, no. 1 (Spring 1997): 21, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA529777. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 12 February 1946, cross-examination of Friedrich Paulus, 299, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-12-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 386 (11 February 1943), “War Situation,” cols. 1453–531, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/feb/11/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the figures for the dead at Stalingrad differ so widely?","a":"Because each count measures something different over different dates. The official Russian figure of 1,129,619 Soviet casualties covers the whole battle, while a later recount of the fronts on the Stalingrad axis reaches 1,347,214.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the German side, Soviet figures give 147,200 killed and wounded and over 91,000 captured, and the US Army history says the exact total was apparently never determined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler refuse to let the Sixth Army withdraw?","a":"Because he had publicly staked his personal prestige on the city, and giving it up meant, in his own words at a December conference, sacrificing the whole sense of the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Paulus reported in January that further fighting was beyond human possibility, the answer he received was that capitulation was impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Soviet historians start the battle in July rather than September?","a":"Because 17 July 1942 was the day two newly created Soviet armies first met the German advance in the bend of the Don, and the date fits closely with Hitler's recasting of the offensive, which left the Sixth Army to drive on Stalingrad alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R74190%2C_Russland%2C_Kesselschlacht_Stalingrad.jpg","title":"A Soviet storm group in the ruins, 1942","desc":"Red Army soldiers of a storm group fighting their way through a ruined building in Stalingrad in 1942, the kind of small-unit combat the city battle was fought in.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv Bild 183-R74190, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R74190,_Russland,_Kesselschlacht_Stalingrad.jpg","alt":"Soldiers with rifles scrambling over heaps of loose bricks inside a shattered brick building, one climbing a broken wall."},"wiki":"Battle of Stalingrad","places":["city:1159150697"]},
    "ww2-302": {"questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Luftwaffe was to assist by the timely destruction of Stalingrad, while Army Group A went south into the Caucasus as Operation Edelweiss.","German tanks reached the Volga north of Stalingrad on 23 August 1942 in the opening stage of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last stage was to have been a thrust to Astrakhan."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Fischreiher</b> was the code name, meaning ‘heron’, that Hitler's Directive 45 of 23 July 1942 gave to Army Group B's share of the German summer offensive in southern Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The directive told the army group to hold the line of the Don, advance on Stalingrad, destroy the Soviet forces gathering there and block the land bridge between the Don and the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Motorised units were then to drive down the lower Volga to Astrakhan, while the Luftwaffe was to destroy the city and disrupt shipping on the river by sowing mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same directive sent Army Group A into the Caucasus as Operation Edelweiss, a split along two lines at right angles that the official US Army history calls the cardinal tactical sin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strain showed at once, because half of the Sixth Army's daily supply tonnage went to the Caucasus drive and by 28 July Paulus was almost on the defensive for lack of fuel and ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Sixth Army destroyed the Soviet bridgehead west of Kalach between 7 and 11 August, taking about 50,000 prisoners, but it reached the Don crossings some 2 weeks later than planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 23 August the XIV Panzer Corps broke out of its Don bridgehead behind about 1,000 tons of bombs and covered about 58 kilometres (36 miles) in little more than 12 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tanks came out on the Volga north of the city at Latoshinka, Akatovka and Rynok, bringing the tractor works under fire, while almost the whole of the 4th Air Fleet struck the residential districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> To keep its toehold on the river the corps had to form a hedgehog cut off from the army, and a secure corridor to it was opened only on 2 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The final stage, the advance to Astrakhan, was still a prospect that Hoth and Weichs discussed on 12 September, the eve of the first German assault on the city centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 359–60, 383–85, 393–94, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 152–57, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 38–42, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Friedrich Paulus 1890–1957,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2014), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/friedrich-paulus. [in German] [Open access]","S. Ia. Lavrenov, “Rol' 62-i armii v otrazhenii pervogo shturma Stalingrada, 13–26 sentiabria 1942 g.” [The role of the 62nd Army in repelling the first assault on Stalingrad], <i>Obozrevatel' – Observer</i>, no. 2 (2023): 90–101, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-62-y-armii-v-otrazhenii-pervogo-shturma-stalingrada-13-26-sentyabrya-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Directive 45 slow the advance on Stalingrad?","a":"Because the two army groups now had to share one usable railway and the motor transport from a single railhead. Half of the Sixth Army's daily supply tonnage went to the Caucasus drive, so Paulus had to pull back his spearhead near Kalach on 26 July and was almost on the defensive 2 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Astrakhan part of the plan?","a":"Because the directive aimed to block the principal branch of the Volga and secure the German flank along the lower river. Hitler was reverting to an earlier staff concept, which held that a drive across the Caucasus could only be risked once that flank was safe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the panzer corps on the Volga in danger after 23 August?","a":"Because its thrust was so narrow that it had to break contact with the rest of the army and form an all-round defence. Paulus could not spare infantry to open a corridor, and on 24 August Soviet armies attacked his long northern flank from their bridgeheads on the Don.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B28114%2C_Russland%2C_Kampf_um_Stalingrad%2C_Vormarsch.jpg","title":"A Volga ferry landing, August 1942","desc":"German army vehicles at a captured ferry landing on the Volga in August 1942, with a burning ferry drifting downstream in midstream, as the German press caption described it.","credit":"Photograph by Herber, Propagandakompanie, Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B28114, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B28114,_Russland,_Kampf_um_Stalingrad,_Vormarsch.jpg","alt":"Two lorries and an armoured car on a wooden jetty over a wide flat river, with smoke rising from a boat far out on the water."},"wiki":"Operation Fischreiher","places":["city:1159150697"]},
    "ww2-303": {"questions":["The first Soviet plant to build tractors, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had become the main producer of the T-34 medium tank by 1942 and kept sending tanks to the front under fire.","The last Germans to surrender at Stalingrad, about 33,000 men of the XI Corps, held a perimeter anchored on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the north of the city until 2 February 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Stalingrad Tractor Factory</b> was the great engineering works in the northern districts of Stalingrad that opened in 1930 as the first Soviet plant built to make tractors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the war it was turned over to building the T-34 medium tank, and it relied on 182 supplying factories, 122 of which were themselves being evacuated eastwards in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By different counts it turned out between 250 and nearly 300 tanks a month, and until September 1942 it was the main producer of the type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When German tanks reached the Volga at Rynok on 23 August, the works came under artillery fire, yet between then and 1 September it sent 119 tanks, 24 tractors and 55 tank engines to the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By then the front line ran only 500 metres (550 yards) from its workshops, and many of its workers took up arms to defend the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In October Paulus resolved to take the rest of Stalingrad piece by piece from north to south, beginning with the tractor factory and the brickworks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 14 October, from a forward command post west of the factory, he sent in the 14th Panzer Division, whose tanks were inside the works by ten in the morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By dawn the next day a panzer grenadier regiment had reached the Volga beyond it, cutting the 62nd Army's bridgehead in two, and the Sixth Army later called the attack its one complete success in the north of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By mid-November the Germans held about 90 per cent of Stalingrad, but the ruined factory became one of the last German positions when the Soviet ring tightened in January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 33,000 men of the XI Corps held a perimeter around the tractor works for 2 days after Paulus surrendered, sending their last message on the morning of 2 February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["E. Iu. Bolotova and D. I. Lozin, “Promyshlennost' Stalingrada – frontu, 1941–1943 gg.” [The industry of Stalingrad for the front, 1941–1943], <i>Kaspiiskii region: politika, ekonomika, kul'tura</i>, no. 3 (2023): 89–98, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/promyshlennost-stalingrada-frontu-1941-1943-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. V. Vidiashev, “Oboronnaia promyshlennost' Nizhnego Povolzh'ia v gody Velikoi Otechestvennoi voiny” [The defence industry of the Lower Volga in the Great Patriotic War], <i>Promyshlennost': ekonomika, upravlenie, tekhnologii</i>, no. 19 (2007): 134–36, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/oboronnaya-promyshlennost-nizhnego-povolzhya-v-gody-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","S. Ia. Lavrenov, “Rol' 62-i armii v otrazhenii pervogo shturma Stalingrada, 13–26 sentiabria 1942 g.” [The role of the 62nd Army in repelling the first assault on Stalingrad], <i>Obozrevatel' – Observer</i>, no. 2 (2023): 90–101, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-62-y-armii-v-otrazhenii-pervogo-shturma-stalingrada-13-26-sentyabrya-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 459–61, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 38, 46, 79, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the factory matter so much to both sides?","a":"Because until September 1942 it was the main source of the T-34, the Soviet medium tank, turning out about 250 a month by one count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even under artillery fire it sent 119 tanks to the front between 23 August and 1 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Paulus attack the factory district first in October?","a":"Because he no longer had the strength for a single sweep through the city. His plan was to take what remained piece by piece from north to south, starting with the tractor factory and the brickworks, with engineers blasting corridors through whole blocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans in the north hold out after Paulus surrendered?","a":"Because Soviet attacks on 26 January had split the pocket in two, leaving the XI Corps in a separate perimeter around the tractor works. Hitler then ordered the corps to fight to the last man, and it held out for another 48 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B22437%2C_Sowjetunion%2C_Luftaufnahme_Stalingrad.jpg","title":"The tractor factory district from the air","desc":"A German aerial photograph of the tractor factory and the workers' housing around it in the north of Stalingrad, dated 1941 in the archive's record, with the Volga along the bottom.","credit":"Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B22437, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B22437,_Sowjetunion,_Luftaufnahme_Stalingrad.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white aerial view of long factory halls and rail sidings, with rows of apartment blocks around them and a river at the bottom edge."},"wiki":"Volgograd Tractor Plant"},
    "ww2-304": {"questions":["Telling his men to close right up to the German line so that enemy aircraft dared not strike them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the 62nd Army through the street fighting in Stalingrad.","Once chief military adviser to Chiang Kai-shek, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> heard the German general Hans Krebs report Hitler's death at a forward command post in Berlin on 1 May 1945."],"abstract":"<b>Vasily Chuikov</b> was the Soviet general who commanded the 62nd Army in the defence of Stalingrad and in 1945 led the Eighth Guards Army into Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born near Moscow in 1900, he trained as a metalworker, joined the Red Fleet in 1917 and then the Red Army, and studied at the Frunze Military Academy in the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He first went to China as a military adviser in 1927 and returned in 1940–42 as Soviet military attaché and chief military adviser to Chiang Kai-shek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He commanded an army in the occupation of eastern Poland in 1939 and in the war against Finland, and in July 1942 he was acting commander of the 64th Army west of Stalingrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the war he headed the Soviet military administration in Germany from 1949, became a Marshal of the Soviet Union in 1955, and was buried at Volgograd in 1982 as he had asked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Chuikov took over the 62nd Army on 12 September 1942 from a commander who had doubted that it could hold the city, and he swore to defend Stalingrad or die in the attempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His army was cut off from the fronts to its north and south and outnumbered several times over, and on 17 September he reported that its reserves were exhausted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His command post moved from a dugout on Mamayev Kurgan to a bunker by the Tsaritsa River and then north towards the Red October works, and in October German troops dug in about 270 metres (300 yards) from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He had his units close right up to the German line, because the enemy would not call in aircraft where its own men were so near, and the defenders fought in small storm groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 1 May 1945 the German general Hans Krebs was brought to Chuikov's forward command post in Berlin to report Hitler's death and seek an armistice, which the Soviet side refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Dorlis Blume and Irmgard Zündorf, “Biografie Wassilij Tschuikow,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Bonn: Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, 2016), https://www.hdg.de/lemo/biografie/wassilij-tschuikow. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 43–46, 493, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","A. L. Verchenko, “Sovetsko-kitaiskoe vzaimodeistvie v gody iapono-kitaiskoi voiny, 1931–1945 gg., i sokhranenie istoricheskoi pamiati” [Soviet-Chinese cooperation in the Sino-Japanese War and the preservation of historical memory], <i>Kitai v mirovoi i regional'noi politike. Istoriia i sovremennost'</i>, no. 25 (2020): 382–99, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sovetsko-kitayskoe-vzaimodeystvie-v-gody-yapono-kitayskoy-voyny-1931-1945-gg-i-sohranenie-istoricheskoy-pamyati. [in Russian] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 393–95, 461, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","S. Ia. Lavrenov, “Rol' 62-i armii v otrazhenii pervogo shturma Stalingrada, 13–26 sentiabria 1942 g.” [The role of the 62nd Army in repelling the first assault on Stalingrad], <i>Obozrevatel' – Observer</i>, no. 2 (2023): 90–101, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rol-62-y-armii-v-otrazhenii-pervogo-shturma-stalingrada-13-26-sentyabrya-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","A. V. Isaev, “Bor'ba za initsiativu v tsentre Stalingrada. 19–26 sentiabria 1942 g.” [The struggle for the initiative in the centre of Stalingrad, 19–26 September 1942], <i>Izvestiia Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo pedagogicheskogo universiteta</i>, no. 3 (2015): 225–31, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/borba-za-initsiativu-v-tsentre-stalingrada-19-26-sentyabrya-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. P. Trut, “Osobennosti taktiki vedeniia boevykh deistvii sovetskikh voisk v period Stalingradskoi bitvy” [Features of Soviet tactics in the Battle of Stalingrad], <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriia 4: Istoriia. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniia</i> 23, no. 1 (2018): 13–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-taktiki-vedeniya-boevyh-deystviy-sovetskih-voysk-v-period-stalingradskoy-bitvy. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Chuikov keep his front line so close to the Germans?","a":"Because the Luftwaffe and the German artillery were causing heavy losses, and he noticed that where the two front lines converged the enemy would not use aircraft for fear of hitting its own troops. Close combat in the ruins therefore took away one of the German advantages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the 62nd Army so hard to reinforce?","a":"Because it was pinned against the Volga, cut off from the fronts on either side, and everything had to come across the river. By 17 September Chuikov was already reporting that his reserves were exhausted, and in November drifting ice made every crossing slow and dangerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Chuikov's headquarters keep moving?","a":"Because the German advance kept reaching it. On 17 September two German corps met on the Tsaritsa less than 1.6 kilometres (a mile) from his bunker, and he moved north that night; in October German troops dug in about 270 metres (300 yards) from his post in the cliff above the Volga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%BA%D1%82_62-%D0%B9_%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%B0_%D0%9D.%D0%98._%D0%9A%D1%80%D1%8B%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%2C_%D0%92.%D0%98._%D0%A7%D1%83%D0%B9%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%2C_%D0%9A.%D0%90._%D0%93%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%2C_%D0%90.%D0%98._%D0%A0%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D1%86%D0%B5%D0%B2._%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%B1%D1%80%D1%8C_1942_%D0%B3.jpg","title":"The 62nd Army's command post, December 1942","desc":"Chuikov, second from left, with his chief of staff Nikolai Krylov, the army's political member Kuzma Gurov and the divisional commander Alexander Rodimtsev, over a map in Stalingrad in December 1942.","credit":"Photograph by Leonid Leonidov, TASS, from the Ministry of Defence of the Russian Federation, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%BA%D1%82_62-%D0%B9_%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%87%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA_%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%B0_%D0%9D.%D0%98._%D0%9A%D1%80%D1%8B%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2,_%D0%92.%D0%98._%D0%A7%D1%83%D0%B9%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2,_%D0%9A.%D0%90._%D0%93%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2,_%D0%90.%D0%98._%D0%A0%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D1%86%D0%B5%D0%B2._%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%B1%D1%80%D1%8C_1942_%D0%B3.jpg","alt":"Four men in military tunics gathered round a table covered with a map in a low room lit by a hanging lamp, one of them writing."},"wiki":"Vasily Chuikov","places":["city:1159150697"]},
    "ww2-305": {"questions":["Stalin told Churchill on 20 November 1942 that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had pierced the German front along 22 kilometres (14 miles) in the north-west and 12 kilometres (7 miles) in the south.","The tank and cavalry corps of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met at Sovetsky near Kalach on 23 November 1942, closing a ring around the German Sixth Army 4 days after the offensive began."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Uranus</b> was the Soviet counter-offensive that opened on 19 November 1942 and within 4 days encircled the German Sixth Army, by one German count about 250,000 Germans and over 30,000 Romanians and Soviet auxiliaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By Zhukov's account, he and Vasilevsky conceived it in the Kremlin on 12 and 13 September, and late that month Stalin wrote his approval on their signed map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plan was to hold the Sixth Army in the city, smash the Romanian armies on either flank and send armour round behind it to meet at Kalach on the Don.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet strength in the offensive zone was slightly over 1 million men with about 900 tanks, and Soviet estimates made the two sides nearly equal in troops, which the official US Army history doubts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Russian staff history credits the whole counter-offensive, which ran on to 2 February 1943, with enemy losses of 800,000 men, about 180,000 of them prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Everything depended on surprise, so front commanders learned of the plan only in mid-October, the fronts went over to the defensive, and villages near the front were ringed with trenches for German air reconnaissance to see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Fifth Tank Army moved about 800 kilometres (500 miles) in 3 weeks of night marches, and 22 bridges were thrown into the Serafimovich bridgehead, five of them dummies to draw German fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As late as 6 November German army intelligence expected the main Soviet blow against Army Group Centre, and only on 12 November did it warn of an attack on the Romanian Third Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After an artillery preparation of about an hour and a half in heavy snow, the Romanian line broke by early afternoon on 19 November, while Hitler was resting at the Berghof in Bavaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stalin told Churchill the next day that the front had been pierced along 22 kilometres (14 miles) and 12 kilometres (7 miles), and on 23 November the two pincers met at Sovetsky near Kalach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 48–58, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 442–46, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","I. P. Makar, “Ot ‘Urana’ do ‘Kol'tsa’: Opyt deiatel'nosti organov strategicheskogo rukovodstva pri podgotovke i v khode Stalingradskoi nastupatel'noi operatsii” [From ‘Uranus’ to ‘Ring’: the work of the strategic command in the Stalingrad offensive], <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 1 (2008): 9–10, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/ot-urana-do-koltsa-opyt-deyatelnosti-organov-strategicheskogo-rukovodstva-pri-podgotovke-i-v-hode-stalingradskoy-nastupatelnoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Bruce R. Pirnie, <i>Soviet Deception Operations in World War II</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1985), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA165980. [Open access]","Joel S. A. Hayward, “Stalingrad: An Examination of Hitler's Decision to Airlift,” <i>Airpower Journal</i> 11, no. 1 (Spring 1997): 23, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA529777. [Open access]","Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the USSR, <i>Stalin's Correspondence with Churchill, Attlee, Roosevelt and Truman, 1941–45</i> (Moscow, 1957; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1958), 77, https://archive.org/details/stalinscorrespon010909mbp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the offensive strike the Romanian armies rather than the Germans?","a":"Because the Romanians on the Sixth Army's flanks were neither trained nor equipped for the Eastern Front. Hoth had already reported that moderate fire was enough to make them fall back, and in November they had no anti-tank weapons heavier than 47 mm against the Soviet armour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German intelligence miss the build-up?","a":"Because the deception was thorough. Units arrived at night under radio silence and in formations smaller than an army, reserves were held 200 miles away at Saratov, and German intelligence was already convinced that the main blow would fall on Army Group Centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the plan a gamble?","a":"Because it assumed that for 2 months the Sixth Army would neither take Stalingrad nor go over to the defensive, and that once surrounded it would not know how to get out. The official US Army history calls Uranus a highly speculative venture beside the parallel Operation Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Kalac_Novembre_1942.jpg","title":"Soviet troops advancing, November 1942","desc":"Soviet infantry and T-34 tanks advancing across snow-covered ground during the November 1942 counter-offensive, captioned on Commons as the advance on Kalach.","credit":"Soviet photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalac_Novembre_1942.jpg","alt":"Soldiers running forward over snowy ground beside two tanks, with the roofless shell of a large building and smoke behind them."},"wiki":"Operation Uranus"},
    "ww2-306": {"questions":["Formed as the 10th Army at the secret mobilisation of August 1939, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fought in Poland and France before Hitler sent it to take Stalingrad in 1942.","Rebuilt after its destruction at Stalingrad, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was surrounded again in Romania in August 1944, and its army group reported that not one complete division had escaped."],"abstract":"The <b>German Sixth Army</b> was the field army that Hitler sent to take Stalingrad in 1942 and that was encircled and destroyed there in the winter of 1942–43.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was formed at the secret mobilisation of August 1939 as the 10th Army, fought in Poland, and went into Belgium and France in 1940 under its new number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Friedrich Paulus took command in January 1942 near Kharkov, and its defeat of the Soviet offensive there in May made him a figure of German war propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the summer it advanced across the Don steppe to the Volga, and in the pocket Paulus had 20 German infantry, armoured and motorised divisions and two Romanian divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rebuilt after Stalingrad, the army was surrounded and destroyed a second time in Romania in August 1944, when not a single complete division escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the ring closed on 23 November 1942, Paulus asked for freedom to break out to the south-west, saying that his five corps commanders agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler instead ordered it to hold, named it a fortress and promised supply by air, while the commander of the air fleet reported that he had 298 transports and needed 500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How many men were inside is itself uncertain, since the German Historical Museum gives about 250,000 Germans and over 30,000 Romanians and Soviet auxiliaries, but its biography of Paulus gives 220,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet side first believed that only 80,000 to 90,000 were trapped, and the commander of the 16th Air Army later wrote that the true figure, more than 330,000, was established only in mid-January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hitler was already talking of rebuilding the army from its survivors before the end, and at the surrender Soviet figures counted over 91,000 prisoners, among them 24 generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 57–59, 79–80, 353–55, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Friedrich Paulus 1890–1957,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2014), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/friedrich-paulus. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 12 February 1946, cross-examination of Friedrich Paulus, 303, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-12-46.asp. [Open access]","Joel S. A. Hayward, “Stalingrad: An Examination of Hitler's Decision to Airlift,” <i>Airpower Journal</i> 11, no. 1 (Spring 1997): 28–29, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA529777. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 478–79, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","A. B. Oleinik, “‘Unichtozhenie transportnykh samoletov protivnika schitat' osnovnoi zadachei’” [‘Destruction of enemy transport aircraft to be considered the main task’], <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (2024): 16–23, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/unichtozhenie-transportnyh-samolyotov-protivnika-schitat-osnovnoy-zadachey. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the corps commanders want to break out at once?","a":"Because they believed supplies would decide the issue and that air supply was a straw to grasp at. On 23 November only thirty transport aircraft were at hand, and the corps commanders met at Gumrak on 22 November and agreed to gather all their strength for a breakout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the size of the trapped army uncertain?","a":"Because the sources disagree by more than 100,000 men. One German museum account gives about 250,000 Germans and over 30,000 others, its biography of Paulus gives 220,000, and a Soviet air commander wrote that his side first thought 80,000 to 90,000 were trapped and only later established more than 330,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"What became of the army's name after Stalingrad?","a":"Hitler wanted the Sixth Army reconstituted quickly from as many survivors as could be found, and a new army under that number fought on until August 1944, when it was surrounded in Romania and lost all its divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-E0406-0022-010%2C_Kolonne_deutscher_Kriegsgefangener.jpg","title":"Prisoners from the Sixth Army, February 1943","desc":"A column of German soldiers taken prisoner at Stalingrad marching to a prisoner-of-war camp in February 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv Bild 183-E0406-0022-010, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-E0406-0022-010,_Kolonne_deutscher_Kriegsgefangener.jpg","alt":"A long line of men wrapped in coats and blankets trudging along a snowy track that stretches away into a white landscape."}},
    "ww2-307": {"questions":["Soviet tanks overran Tatsinskaya on 24 December 1942, one of the main bases of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though claims for the German aircraft destroyed there run from 300 to 431.","The loss of Pitomnik on 12 January 1943 and of Gumrak 9 days later left parachute drops as the only remaining form of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the Sixth Army's pocket."],"abstract":"The <b>Stalingrad airlift</b> was the Luftwaffe's attempt to keep the encircled German Sixth Army supplied from the air between late November 1942 and its surrender in early February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler's decision to hold the city rested on two assumptions, that a relief force could be assembled and that the army could be kept fighting by air until it arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first assurance came on 20 November from the Luftwaffe chief of staff, Hans Jeschonnek, who pointed to the hundred thousand men supplied in the Demyansk pocket the previous winter, and Göring soon gave his own guarantee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The air commanders on the spot, Richthofen and Fiebig, warned at once that an army could not be supplied from the air, and Richthofen reported that he had 298 transports and needed 500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Estimates of what the army needed ran from the Sixth Army's first figure of 750 tons a day down to the 300 to 400 tons that the German Historical Museum gives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> By the US Army history's count, deliveries averaged about 90 tons a day between 1 and 23 December and about 120 in the first 3 weeks of January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent Russian study puts the average over the whole operation at about 138 tons a day from 4,691 successful sorties, with a peak of 326 tons on 7 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Soviet air forces set up zones of blockade from the German bases to the pocket itself, and by the same study the Luftwaffe lost 285 transport aircraft in action between 30 November and 3 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 24 December Soviet tanks overran Tatsinskaya, a main airlift base, though Soviet claims for the aircraft destroyed there range from 300 to 431 and are doubted by Russian historians themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The loss of Pitomnik airfield on 12 January and of Gumrak around 21 January left airdrops as the army's only supply, after about 30,000 wounded had been flown out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 59–62, 76–78, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Joel S. A. Hayward, “Stalingrad: An Examination of Hitler's Decision to Airlift,” <i>Airpower Journal</i> 11, no. 1 (Spring 1997): 23–30, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA529777. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 478–79, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","A. B. Oleinik, “‘Unichtozhenie transportnykh samoletov protivnika schitat' osnovnoi zadachei’” [‘Destruction of enemy transport aircraft to be considered the main task’], <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (2024): 16–23, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/unichtozhenie-transportnyh-samolyotov-protivnika-schitat-osnovnoy-zadachey. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. N. Tomilin, “Tatsinskii tankovyi reid i ego rol' v razgrome okruzhennoi gruppirovki nemetsko-fashistskikh voisk pod Stalingradom” [The Tatsinskaya tank raid and its part in destroying the encircled German forces at Stalingrad], <i>Via in tempore. Istoriia. Politologiia</i> 31, no. 15 (2014): 152–59, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tatsinskiy-tankovyy-reyd-i-ego-rol-v-razgrome-okruzhennoy-gruppirovki-nemetsko-fashistskih-voysk-pod-stalingradom. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the comparison with the Demyansk pocket misleading?","a":"Because Demyansk held about 100,000 men who needed some 300 tons a day, which took almost 500 Junkers transports, against a Soviet air force that was barely present. At Stalingrad there were almost three times as many men and the Soviet air forces were far stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the Soviet blockade not stop the flights altogether?","a":"Because units assigned only to hunting transports were constantly diverted to other tasks, there was no effective defence at night, when the Germans increasingly flew, and the anti-aircraft guns could not reach aircraft flying high. The Luftwaffe kept up a minimum of supply almost to the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the airlift fall so far short even of its own targets?","a":"Because in late November the Luftwaffe was fighting a second campaign in North Africa, to which 400 combat aircraft had been sent from the east. The aircraft that remained had to fly across enemy territory and contested airspace, in uncertain weather and without adequate ground support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4._%D0%A3_%D1%81%D0%B1%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0.jpg","title":"A downed German transport, 1943","desc":"A Soviet officer examining the wreck of a German Junkers Ju 52 transport aircraft shot down near Stalingrad, photographed in 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Sergey Strunnikov, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4._%D0%A3_%D1%81%D0%B1%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"The torn-open fuselage of an aircraft marked with a cross lying in the snow, with a man in a greatcoat standing among the wreckage."}},
    "ww2-308": {"questions":["Told after reporting that his army's suffering had passed human endurance that capitulation was impossible, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept the Sixth Army fighting at Stalingrad until the end of January 1943.","Called as a witness for the Soviet prosecution at Nuremberg in February 1946, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told the tribunal that more than 100,000 of his soldiers had died of hunger and cold."],"abstract":"<b>Friedrich Paulus</b> was the German general who commanded the Sixth Army at Stalingrad and, on 31 January 1943, became the first German field marshal ever taken prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in 1890 at Breitenau near Kassel, the son of an auditor, he was refused entry to the imperial navy and joined the Prussian army as an officer cadet in 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost his whole career was spent on the staff, and in September 1940 he became deputy to the chief of the army general staff and worked on the plan for invading the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Sixth Army, which he took over in January 1942, was his first command in the field, and the official US Army history judged that he fought the summer campaign well by professional standards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An air-power historian describes him as a cautious commander who lacked the strength of character to put dissenting views to Hitler, who took his silence as support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Around 20 January 1943 he reported that further fighting was beyond human possibility, and he was told that capitulation was impossible and that the army must do its historic duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler promoted him to field marshal on 30 January, a gesture the German Historical Museum reads as a demand to fight to the end, since no German field marshal had ever surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That night the Soviet 38th Motorised Rifle Brigade surrounded the Univermag department store on Red Square, in whose basement his headquarters lay, and the next morning he went into captivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As a prisoner he joined appeals by the National Committee for a Free Germany for the Wehrmacht to surrender after the 20 July plot, and in 1946 he testified at Nuremberg for the prosecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Released in 1953, he settled in Dresden in East Germany and died there on 1 February 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 56–57, 79, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Friedrich Paulus 1890–1957,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2014), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/friedrich-paulus. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 11 February 1946, testimony of Friedrich Paulus, 252–53, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-11-46.asp. [Open access]","Joel S. A. Hayward, “Stalingrad: An Examination of Hitler's Decision to Airlift,” <i>Airpower Journal</i> 11, no. 1 (Spring 1997): 25–28, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA529777. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 12 February 1946, cross-examination of Friedrich Paulus, 283–84, 287, 299, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-12-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","V. G. Makarov, “‘On na menia proizvel vpechatlenie zatravlennogo zveria…’ Rasskazyvaiut ochevidtsy pleneniia general-fel'dmarshala Pauliusa i ego generalov” [‘He struck me as a hunted beast…’: eyewitnesses describe the capture of Field Marshal Paulus and his generals], <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (2018): 25–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/on-na-menya-proizvyol-vpechatlenie-zatravlennogo-zverya-rasskazyvayut-ochevidtsy-pleneniya-general-feldmarshala-paulyusa-i-ego. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler promote Paulus on the eve of the surrender?","a":"Because no German field marshal had ever capitulated, and the promotion was meant to push Paulus to fight on to a hero's death. When Paulus surrendered anyway, Hitler remarked that he had turned round on the threshold of immortality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Paulus not break out against Hitler's orders?","a":"Because, as he told the flak general who urged it, Hitler had ordered him to stand fast and a breakout with the means available would probably end in disaster. His chief of staff argued the same, and Paulus would not contradict the order on his own authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Paulus explain at Nuremberg why he had fought on?","a":"He said that when he took command he had not seen the full extent of the crime of the war, and that he did his duty as a soldier; he added that when he reported the army's misery in January, the Supreme Command answered that capitulation was impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BB-%D1%84%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%B4%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B0_%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%83%D0%BB%D1%8E%D1%81%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8A%D1%8E%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B8%D1%80%D1%83%D1%8E%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B1_64-%D0%B9_%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8._31.01.1943_%D0%B3.jpg","title":"Paulus taken to the 64th Army's headquarters","desc":"Field Marshal Friedrich Paulus, right, being escorted as a prisoner to the headquarters of the Soviet 64th Army on 31 January 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BB-%D1%84%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%B4%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B0_%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%83%D0%BB%D1%8E%D1%81%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%8A%D1%8E%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B8%D1%80%D1%83%D1%8E%D1%82_%D0%B2_%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%B1_64-%D0%B9_%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8._31.01.1943_%D0%B3.jpg","alt":"A tall officer in a long greatcoat and peaked cap walking past wooden huts in the snow, with a soldier in a fur hat beside him."},"wiki":"Friedrich Paulus"},
    "ww2-309": {"questions":["Manstein's relief attempt code-named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to meet a breakout code-named Donnerschlag, which Hitler refused to approve while the Sixth Army still held Stalingrad.","German officers recalled that during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> they could see signal flares rising from the Stalingrad pocket at night across the last 48 kilometres (30 miles) of steppe."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Winter Storm</b>, in German Wintergewitter, was the attempt by Manstein's Army Group Don in December 1942 to break through to the Sixth Army encircled at Stalingrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prepared from 1 December, it gave the main effort to the LVII Panzer Corps striking north-east from Kotelnikovo, with a secondary thrust from the Don–Chir bridgehead towards Kalach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet attacks on the Chir tied down the corps meant for the secondary thrust, and by 9 December the operation had shrunk to two divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sixth Army was to gather its armour on the south-western edge of the pocket, ready both to strike towards the relief force and to break out, while still holding its other fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler insisted on 12 December that Stalingrad could not be given up under any circumstances, since that would sacrifice the whole sense of the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The 6th Panzer Division, with about 200 tanks and assault guns by its own officers' account, broke through the Soviet advance divisions and crossed the Aksay River on the third day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After days of tank fighting around Verkhne-Kumsky, the corps reached the Mishkova River on 19 December, about 56 kilometres (35 miles) from the pocket, and the German Historical Museum puts the closest approach at 48 kilometres (30 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Manstein then urged a breakout code-named Donnerschlag, but Hitler would not approve it, and the Sixth Army's chief of staff reported fuel for only about 19 kilometres (12 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 23 December the 6th Panzer Division was pulled out for the Chir front, where a Soviet offensive on the middle Don, launched on 16 December, had torn a gap and reached Tatsinskaya by 24 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A captured German officer later told Soviet interviewers that his men had held out because Hitler promised a relief from Kotelnikovo that would break the ring from both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 61–65, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 479–83, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>Russian Combat Methods in World War II</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-230 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1950), 70–74, https://archive.org/details/Dapam20-230. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","V. N. Tomilin, “Tatsinskii tankovyi reid i ego rol' v razgrome okruzhennoi gruppirovki nemetsko-fashistskikh voisk pod Stalingradom” [The Tatsinskaya tank raid and its part in destroying the encircled German forces at Stalingrad], <i>Via in tempore. Istoriia. Politologiia</i> 31, no. 15 (2014): 152–59, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tatsinskiy-tankovyy-reyd-i-ego-rol-v-razgrome-okruzhennoy-gruppirovki-nemetsko-fashistskih-voysk-pod-stalingradom. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. G. Makarov, “‘On na menia proizvel vpechatlenie zatravlennogo zveria…’ Rasskazyvaiut ochevidtsy pleneniia general-fel'dmarshala Pauliusa i ego generalov” [‘He struck me as a hunted beast…’: eyewitnesses describe the capture of Field Marshal Paulus and his generals], <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (2018): 25–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/on-na-menya-proizvyol-vpechatlenie-zatravlennogo-zverya-rasskazyvayut-ochevidtsy-pleneniya-general-feldmarshala-paulyusa-i-ego. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had the relief force shrunk before it even set off?","a":"Because Soviet attacks along the Chir forced Manstein to commit the divisions meant for the secondary thrust, the divisions for the main thrust arrived slowly, and the high command allowed the air force field divisions to be used for defence only. By 9 December only two divisions were left for the attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Sixth Army not break out to meet the relief?","a":"Because Hitler refused to approve the breakout, insisting that the army stay until firm contact was made. Neither Manstein nor Paulus could legally order it without him, and Paulus's chief of staff regarded giving up the pocket as an act of desperation to be avoided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the 6th Panzer Division withdrawn on 23 December?","a":"Because the Soviet offensive on the middle Don, begun on 16 December, had broken the front behind Army Group Don and was heading for its airfields and supply bases. The division was sent north of the lower Don to bolster the collapsing Chir front, and the two weak panzer divisions left could not go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Winter Storm"},
    "ww2-310": {"questions":["Zhukov said he proposed the scheme behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 29 November 1942: to split the Stalingrad pocket from west to east and then destroy its two halves in turn.","During <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Germans lost Pitomnik, the better of their two airstrips inside the Stalingrad pocket, on 12 January 1943, which ended their fighter cover over the encircled army."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Koltso</b>, from the Russian for ‘ring’, was the offensive of January 1943 in which the Soviet Don Front destroyed the German Sixth Army in the Stalingrad pocket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stalin and Vasilevsky named it on 4 December 1942, with the aim of splitting the pocket from west to east and wiping out the two halves in turn, a scheme Zhukov said he had proposed on 29 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was postponed when the German relief attack in December drew away the Second Guards Army that was to have carried the main thrust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The task fell to Rokossovsky's Don Front, with Voronov as the Stavka's representative, and Soviet figures give the front 281,000 men and 250 tanks in early January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The final push, first set for 6 January, began on 10 January after the Sixth Army had rejected surrender ultimatums on the two preceding days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pitomnik, the better of the two airstrips, fell on 12 January, and after Gumrak was lost around 21 January only airdrops could reach the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 22 January Paulus radioed that rations were exhausted and over 12,000 wounded lay untended, and the reply, as before, was that surrender was out of the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 62nd Army took Mamayev Kurgan on 25 or 26 January after 135 days of fighting for the hill, and on 26 January tanks of the 21st Army linked up from the west and split the pocket in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the night of 31 January the 38th Motorised Rifle Brigade surrounded the Univermag department store, whose basement held Sixth Army headquarters, and Paulus surrendered in the southern pocket that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The XI Corps in the northern pocket around the tractor works gave up on 2 February, a day after Churchill had congratulated Stalin on the end of the German Sixth Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 76–80, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 484–85, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-12-moscow-to-stalingrad-decision-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke-and-magna-bauer. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 12 February 1946, cross-examination of Friedrich Paulus, 287, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-12-46.asp. [Open access]","V. P. Trut, “Osobennosti taktiki vedeniia boevykh deistvii sovetskikh voisk v period Stalingradskoi bitvy” [Features of Soviet tactics in the Battle of Stalingrad], <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriia 4: Istoriia. Regionovedenie. Mezhdunarodnye otnosheniia</i> 23, no. 1 (2018): 13–25, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-taktiki-vedeniya-boevyh-deystviy-sovetskih-voysk-v-period-stalingradskoy-bitvy. [in Russian] [Open access]","V. G. Makarov, “‘On na menia proizvel vpechatlenie zatravlennogo zveria…’ Rasskazyvaiut ochevidtsy pleneniia general-fel'dmarshala Pauliusa i ego generalov” [‘He struck me as a hunted beast…’: eyewitnesses describe the capture of Field Marshal Paulus and his generals], <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 3 (2018): 25–34, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/on-na-menya-proizvyol-vpechatlenie-zatravlennogo-zverya-rasskazyvayut-ochevidtsy-pleneniya-general-feldmarshala-paulyusa-i-ego. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Stalingrad,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-stalingrad-194243. [in German] [Open access]","Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the USSR, <i>Stalin's Correspondence with Churchill, Attlee, Roosevelt and Truman, 1941–45</i> (Moscow, 1957; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1958), 91, https://archive.org/details/stalinscorrespon010909mbp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the operation postponed in December?","a":"Because Manstein's relief attack from Kotelnikovo began on 12 December, and the Second Guards Army, which was to have led the thrust through the pocket, was sent to the Stalingrad Front to meet it instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the first days disappoint the Soviet command?","a":"Because the attack lost momentum after a few days. The Soviet account blamed an intelligence error that underestimated German strength, and five of Rokossovsky's seven armies had been fighting since midsummer and were at least 150,000 men below strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans in the north surrender 2 days after Paulus?","a":"Because the link-up of 26 January had left the XI Corps in a separate pocket around the tractor works, and on 1 February Hitler ordered it to fight to the last man. It sent its last message on the morning of 2 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B22531%2C_Stalingrad%2C_Ruine_des_Warenhauses.jpg","title":"The ruined Univermag department store","desc":"The burnt-out department store on Red Square in Stalingrad, in whose basement the Sixth Army's headquarters lay at the end, photographed under a German flag in October 1942.","credit":"Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B22531, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B22531,_Stalingrad,_Ruine_des_Warenhauses.jpg","alt":"The gutted rounded corner of a large building with a swastika flag hanging from it, overlooking a square full of rubble and burnt-out blocks."},"wiki":"Operation Koltso"},
    "ww2-361": {"questions":["Hitler said he needed the Baltic coast as a bargaining counter when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke through towards Riga, and a later German army study blamed his hold order for losing a whole army group.","Bagramyan's armies struck west from Šiauliai on 5 October during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, reaching the sea on either side of Memel and cutting the German forces in Latvia off by land."],"abstract":"The <b>Baltic offensive</b> was the Soviet autumn campaign of 1944 that drove Army Group North out of Estonia and most of Latvia and pressed what was left of it against the sea in Courland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By mid-September the German front north of East Prussia had shrunk to a winding coastal strip about 110 to 130 kilometres (70 to 80 miles) wide and more than 640 kilometres (400 miles) long, pinched near Riga to under 32 kilometres (20 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marshal Vasilevskiy coordinated three Baltic fronts converging on Riga, while the Leningrad Front moved an army south of Tartu to strike behind the German forces holding the Narva line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack opened on 14 September, and by the next day the army group commander, Schörner, was asking leave to evacuate Estonia while there was still time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler resisted, arguing that the Soviet Union had peace feelers out and that he needed the Baltic territory to bargain with, before giving a conditional yes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> As Estonia was abandoned the Germans also emptied their labour camps there, shipping some Jewish prisoners to Stutthof and driving many thousands on death marches along the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In September 1944 the Soviet Union once again annexed Estonia as one of its republics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Instead of splitting the army group, the September fighting squeezed it into a tight knot around Riga, so the Stavka shifted its weight south-west and on 5 October Bagramyan's front struck from Šiauliai towards Memel, reaching the coast on both sides of the port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The last convoy left Riga on 10 October, by the time of the city's liberation almost all of its Jews had been killed, and on 21 October Hitler ordered the army group to go over to the defensive in Courland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill had warned the Commons in September that large German armies might be cut off in the Baltic States, and a post-war German army study judged that a whole army group was kept there, out of the defence of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 403–406, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 407–409, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>Military Improvisations during the Russian Campaign</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 36–37, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Estonia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/estonia. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Riga,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/riga. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War and International Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 28 September 1944, vol. 403, cols. 421–604, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the September attacks fail to cut Army Group North in two?","a":"Because the German withdrawal from Estonia went faster than expected: Schörner revised his plan on 17 September, the corps on the Narva front marched to Pärnu by the 20th, and reinforcements arrived south of Riga just in time to stop a breakthrough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Instead of splitting, the army group was compressed into a much shorter front around Riga, which made a Soviet breakthrough there both harder and less rewarding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler hesitate to evacuate the Baltic States?","a":"He argued that the troops on the outer flank could not get away in any case, that the Soviet Union had peace feelers out and the Baltic territory was needed to bargain with, and that the navy would lose its Baltic training waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He agreed only conditionally, after being told the withdrawal could still be cancelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the offensive matter beyond the Baltic?","a":"Because it left a whole army group shut up in Courland while the Red Army pressed on East Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in September that large German armies might be cut off in the Baltic States, and a post-war German study for the US Army concluded that the army group's orders to hold kept its best troops out of the defence of Germany itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/Red_Army_soldiers_in_Riga._October_1944.jpg","title":"Soviet troops in Riga, October 1944","desc":"Soldiers of the Red Army's 130th Latvian Rifle Corps on Brīvības Boulevard in Riga in mid-October 1944, days after the city was taken.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Army_soldiers_in_Riga._October_1944.jpg","alt":"A column of soldiers in greatcoats and side caps, rifles slung, marching towards the camera along a tree-lined city boulevard with a tall monument behind them."},"wiki":"Baltic offensive"},
    "ww2-362": {"questions":["More than 4,000 young soldiers were trapped in Ternopol because the town had been made a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that could not be given up, and only 53 of them reached German lines.","Ninth Army's war diary called the orders creating each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> particularly dangerous on 22 June 1944, the eve of the Soviet offensive that overran Orsha and Mogilev."],"abstract":"A <b>Fester Platz</b>, or ‘fortified place’, was a town or city that Hitler ordered to be held to the last man as the German armies fell back across the Soviet Union in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He created the category in Führer Order 11 on 8 March 1944, for towns astride the lines of communication that were, in his words, to perform the same functions as forts did in times past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each had a commandant answerable to the army group commander and removable only with Hitler's consent, with a corps commander's authority, which included the power to impose the death penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler designated 26 cities and larger towns on still-occupied Soviet territory, among them Ternopol, Proskurov, Kovel', Brody, Vinnitsa and Pervomaysk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A post-war German study for the US Army described commandants given absolute powers under a special oath who often had to pick their garrisons off the streets from men passing through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ternopol showed the cost: over 4,000 men, most of them 18-year-olds, were encircled there from 21 March, the garrison was not allowed to break out until a relief attempt had stalled, and when it finally did, 53 men reached the German line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the SS general commanding Kovel' reported that he was surrounded and wanted to get out, Himmler telegraphed back that he had been sent to Kovel' to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Soviet summer offensive Ninth Army wrote that it considered the orders establishing fortified places particularly dangerous, and at Mogilev nothing had been heard from the garrison for a day when Hitler finally let it go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Commanders who retreated against such hold orders paid for it, as Manstein and Kleist were dismissed at the end of March after Hitler accused them of lacking staying power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German army's own post-war verdict was that these tactics, championed by Hitler in person, obstructed all operational freedom and devoured the very substance of the army, and a companion study warned that holding strong points must never become routine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 277–289, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 316–333, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>Military Improvisations during the Russian Campaign</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 36–37, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>German Defense Tactics against Russian Break-throughs</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-233, Historical Study (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 34–35, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-233. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Frühjahrsoffensive 1944,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/fruehjahrsoffensive-1944 [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler create fortified places?","a":"The US Army's history links the order to Hitler's annoyance at losing Nevel and Rovno and to a growing readiness to try any measure, however desperate, that might slow the Soviet offensives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Towns on the roads and railways were to act like forts of the past, holding out behind the enemy's spearheads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the army regard the orders as so dangerous?","a":"Because a garrison that could not withdraw was simply lost once the front moved past it, as Ternopol showed when 53 men out of more than 4,000 got back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ninth Army wrote before the summer offensive that rigid defence and fortified places tied it to tactics it could not accept, and the post-war German study concluded that they devoured the army's substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the garrisons often so weak?","a":"Because a town was usually designated at the last minute rather than prepared over years, so the commandant had to stop whatever soldiers, guns and vehicles were passing through and press them into the defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A companion study added that strong points worked only as an emergency measure, never as a standard procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Prigionieri_a_Tarnopol.png","title":"German prisoners at Ternopol, April 1944","desc":"German soldiers taken prisoner when the Red Army captured Ternopol in April 1944, after the garrison of the fortified place had been encircled for nearly four weeks.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prigionieri_a_Tarnopol.png","alt":"A dense crowd of soldiers in German caps and tunics, many raising their hands, packed together in the open with trees behind them."}},
    "ww2-363": {"questions":["Operation Valkyrie, drafted to put down unrest by forced labourers, became the machinery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but its orders reached many military districts late and alongside contrary telexes.","Churchill told the Commons that the highest personalities in the Reich were murdering one another after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, during which more than 1,200 security men were briefly arrested in Paris."],"abstract":"The <b>20 July plot</b> was the attempt by German officers and their civilian allies to kill Hitler at his East Prussian headquarters on 20 July 1944 and take power in Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its machinery was Operation Valkyrie, Army plans for putting down uprisings by forced labourers or unrest at home, which the conspirators meant to use to seize the military districts once Hitler was dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Orders prepared in advance gave executive power to Field Marshal von Witzleben as the new commander-in-chief, with SS troops to be disarmed and the concentration camps occupied by the Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beck was to be head of state and Goerdeler chancellor, and Gisevius, who came secretly to Berlin for the day, later named Witzleben, Olbricht, Hoepner and the city commandant von Hase among the generals involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plotters wanted an immediate end to the war and its crimes and a return to the rule of law, but since early 1943 the Allies had insisted on unconditional surrender, which limited their options.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Stauffenberg's bomb went off in a briefing hut at the Wolf's Lair, but Hitler survived with light injuries, and once that was known the rising was already failing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Olbricht and Mertz von Quirnheim set Valkyrie going only at about four in the afternoon, many districts received the orders late and alongside contrary telexes from headquarters, and the plotters never reached the radio stations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Paris the military commander, Carl Heinrich von Stülpnagel, had more than 1,200 members of the security apparatus arrested, until Field Marshal von Kluge refused to join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shortly before midnight Colonel General Fromm ordered Stauffenberg, Olbricht, Mertz von Quirnheim and Haeften shot in the courtyard of the Bendler Block, while Beck, handed a pistol, asked for another when his first shot failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hitler at first spoke of a very small clique of ambitious officers, and Churchill told the Commons on 2 August that the highest personalities in the German Reich were murdering one another, or trying to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["German Resistance Memorial Center, “The Attempted Coup of July 20, 1944,” Topics 11, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/11-the-attempted-coup-of-july-20-1944. [Open access]","German Resistance Memorial Center, “Aims of the Attempted Coup,” Topics 10, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/10-aims-of-the-attempted-coup. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Das Attentat auf Adolf Hitler vom 20. Juli 1944,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/attentat-vom-20-juli [in German]. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Der Widerstand in der Wehrmacht,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, last changed 12 July 2019, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/militaerischer-widerstand [in German]. [Open access]","Hans Rothfels, <i>The German Opposition to Hitler: An Appraisal</i>, trans. Lawrence Wilson, new rev. ed. (Chicago: Henry Regnery, 1962), 72–80, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.58053. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 12, proceedings of 25 April 1946, testimony of Hans Bernd Gisevius, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-25-46.asp. [Open access]","Hans Bernd Gisevius, <i>To the Bitter End</i>, trans. Richard and Clara Winston (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1947), 570–573, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.167391. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 August 1944, vol. 402, cols. 1459–568, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the plotters use Operation Valkyrie?","a":"Because it already existed as a legitimate Army plan for suppressing unrest at home, so its orders could move troops and take over the military districts without first revealing a coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Orders drafted in advance under Witzleben's and Fromm's names were kept sealed by Olbricht for safe keeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the coup collapse within hours?","a":"Hitler survived the bomb, and once that was known the rising was already failing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Valkyrie was started late, many military districts received the conspirators' orders at the same time as contrary telexes from headquarters, and without the radio stations the plotters could not appeal to the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the plotters go ahead when Germany was losing anyway?","a":"They wanted to end the war and its crimes at once and to show that another Germany existed, even though the Allied demand for unconditional surrender since early 1943 limited what they could achieve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They hoped to prevent a national catastrophe and restore the rule of law, with those responsible for crimes tried under due process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1972-025-10%2C_Hitler-Attentat%2C_20._Juli_1944.jpg","title":"The wrecked briefing hut at the Wolf's Lair","desc":"Hermann Göring, Martin Bormann and other officials inspecting the briefing hut at Hitler's headquarters near Rastenburg in East Prussia after the bomb of 20 July 1944.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1972-025-10, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1972-025-10,_Hitler-Attentat,_20._Juli_1944.jpg","alt":"A group of men in long coats and uniforms standing in a room with a collapsed ceiling, broken timbers and debris covering the floor."},"wiki":"20 July plot"},
    "ww2-364": {"questions":["Twice in July 1944 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put off the attempt because Göring and Himmler were absent, and on the 20th he had to plant the bomb himself.","Shot in the Bendler Block courtyard on Fromm's orders, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had flown back to Berlin believing Hitler dead and spent the evening trying to rally the military districts."],"abstract":"<b>Claus von Stauffenberg</b> was the German army officer who planted the bomb at Hitler's headquarters on 20 July 1944 and directed the attempted coup in Berlin that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in 1907 at Jettingen in Bavaria into a Catholic noble family, he and his brother Berthold were drawn as boys into the circle of the poet Stefan George, whom he revered all his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He joined the 17th Cavalry Regiment at Bamberg in 1926, spoke for Hitler against Hindenburg in the presidential election of 1932, and served as a staff officer in Poland and France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1943 a low-flying air attack in North Africa cost him an eye, his right hand and two fingers of his left hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The memorial to the German resistance writes that only during the war did he recognise the criminal nature of Nazi policy, freeing himself more slowly than his fellow conspirators from the spell of Hitler's victories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the autumn of 1943 Olbricht brought him into the General Army Office in Berlin, and Gisevius told the Nuremberg tribunal that Stauffenberg took over Oster's place in all the conspiracy's activities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because only he had access to Hitler's headquarters, the conspiracy's chief of staff had to commit the deed himself, and he postponed it twice when Göring and Himmler were absent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 20 July he flew back to Berlin believing Hitler dead, tried all evening to win over the military districts, and was shot that night in the courtyard of the Bendler Block on Fromm's orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gisevius, who was in Berlin that day, later portrayed him as a soldier to the core who looked towards reconciliation with Russia and whom only military disaster had shocked into opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A historian of the resistance called that portrait radically false, arguing that Stauffenberg shared the Kreisau circle's rejection of every totalitarian system, whatever its political colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["German Resistance Memorial Center, “Claus Schenk Graf von Stauffenberg,” Biographies, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/biographies/index-of-persons/biographie/view-bio/claus-schenk-graf-von-stauffenberg/. [Open access]","Susanne Eckelmann, “Claus Schenk Graf von Stauffenberg 1907–1944,” LeMO Biografie, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/claus-stauffenberg [in German]. [Open access]","Hans Bernd Gisevius, <i>To the Bitter End</i>, trans. Richard and Clara Winston (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1947), 483–485, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.167391. [Open access]","Hans Rothfels, <i>The German Opposition to Hitler: An Appraisal</i>, trans. Lawrence Wilson, new rev. ed. (Chicago: Henry Regnery, 1962), 123–124, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.58053. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 12, proceedings of 25 April 1946, testimony of Hans Bernd Gisevius, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-25-46.asp. [Open access]","Gedenkstätte Plötzensee, “July 20, 1944,” German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gedenkstaette-ploetzensee.de/en/executions-in-ploetzensee-prison/july-20-1944. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Das Attentat auf Adolf Hitler vom 20. Juli 1944,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/attentat-vom-20-juli [in German]. [Open access]","Hans Rothfels, <i>The German Opposition to Hitler: An Appraisal</i>, trans. Lawrence Wilson, new rev. ed. (Chicago: Henry Regnery, 1962), 76–78, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.58053. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stauffenberg plant the bomb himself despite his injuries?","a":"Because after his appointment he was the only conspirator with access to Hitler's headquarters, so the man who was meant to direct the coup from Berlin also had to carry the bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The memorial notes that he decided this in early July 1944 despite his severe injury and his key role in Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he postpone the attempt twice in July?","a":"The conspirators wanted to remove Göring and Himmler together with Hitler, and both times they were absent, so he held back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Earlier attempts by others had also failed, which is why he finally resolved to act in person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do accounts of his motives differ so sharply?","a":"Gisevius, writing as a participant, described a passionate soldier driven by military disaster and drawn towards Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A historian of the resistance rejected this as a deep misunderstanding, holding that Stauffenberg shared the Kreisau circle's opposition to every totalitarian system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The German Historical Museum notes that the mass murders as well as incompetent military leadership drew him into the resistance in 1942 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-079-02%2C_F%C3%BChrerhauptquartier%2C_Stauffenberg%2C_Hitler%2C_Keitel.jpg","title":"Stauffenberg at the Wolf's Lair, 15 July 1944","desc":"Stauffenberg, standing at attention on the left, at Hitler's headquarters near Rastenburg on 15 July 1944, as Hitler greets an officer and Wilhelm Keitel waits on the right.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1984-079-02, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-079-02,_F%C3%BChrerhauptquartier,_Stauffenberg,_Hitler,_Keitel.jpg","alt":"On a woodland path a tall young officer stands stiffly at the left while a man in a peaked cap shakes hands with another officer and an older officer holding a folder looks on."},"wiki":"Claus von Stauffenberg"},
    "ww2-365": {"questions":["Once senior operations officer of Army Group Centre, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued in the summer of 1944 that the attempt on Hitler must be made at any cost, whatever its chance of practical success.","Hearing on 21 July 1944 that the coup in Berlin had failed, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed himself at the front near Ostrów, and the Gestapo later exhumed and burned his body."],"abstract":"<b>Henning von Tresckow</b> was the staff officer of Army Group Centre who led the military conspiracy against Hitler on the Eastern Front and organised several attempts on his life before 20 July 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in Magdeburg in 1901 into a Prussian officer's family, he served as a volunteer in 1917 and 1918, and like many officers he welcomed the Nazi takeover in 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The memorial to the German resistance dates his turn to resolute opposition to the November pogroms of 1938, while the German Historical Museum stresses his rejection of the war in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As senior operations officer of Army Group Centre from 1941 he was well informed about the crimes of the anti-partisan war and of the Einsatzgruppen, and he drew a group of resolute anti-Nazis onto his staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gisevius told the Nuremberg tribunal that Tresckow protested to his superiors against the Commissar Order as soon as it was issued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Unable to win over the army group commander, Kluge, Tresckow and his fellow officer Fabian von Schlabrendorff decided to kill Hitler with a bomb on his aircraft, so that his death would look like an air crash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 13 March 1943, as Hitler left Smolensk, Schlabrendorff set the fuse and handed an officer in Hitler's party a parcel disguised as brandy, but the detonator failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The next day Schlabrendorff flew to Hitler's headquarters and swapped the bomb for a real parcel before anyone discovered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the summer of 1944 Tresckow urged that the attempt be made at any cost, because what mattered was no longer the practical object but that the resistance should have risked the decisive throw before the world and history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When he heard that the coup had failed he killed himself at the front near Ostrów on 21 July, and the Gestapo later exhumed his body from the family estate and burned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["German Resistance Memorial Center, “Henning von Tresckow,” Biographies, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/biographies/index-of-persons/biographie/view-bio/henning-von-tresckow/. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Henning von Tresckow 1901–1944,” LeMO Biografie, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/henning-von-tresckow [in German]. [Open access]","Fabian von Schlabrendorff, <i>Offiziere gegen Hitler</i>, ed. Gero v. S. Gaevernitz (Zürich: Europa Verlag, 1946), 72–80, https://archive.org/details/offiziere-gegen-hitler [in German]. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 12, proceedings of 25 April 1946, testimony of Hans Bernd Gisevius, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-25-46.asp. [Open access]","Hans Rothfels, <i>The German Opposition to Hitler: An Appraisal</i>, trans. Lawrence Wilson, new rev. ed. (Chicago: Henry Regnery, 1962), 74–79, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.58053. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Der Widerstand in der Wehrmacht,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, last changed 12 July 2019, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/militaerischer-widerstand [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Tresckow want Hitler's death to look like an accident?","a":"Because Hitler still had many followers in 1943, and the plotters feared that an open assassination would rouse them against the coup that was meant to follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A bomb on the aircraft would let the army take over after what seemed an air crash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the conspirators act without Kluge?","a":"Kluge, their army group commander, recognised the need but lacked the will to act, arguing that neither the people nor the soldiers would understand such a deed at that moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unable to win over a senior commander, Tresckow decided to act on his own responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Manstein and Guderian also refused to join him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he insist on an attempt in 1944 when success seemed unlikely?","a":"He argued that the practical object no longer mattered: the German resistance had to show the world and history that it had risked the decisive throw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German Historical Museum notes that this pressed Stauffenberg to go ahead with the bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1976-130-53%2C_Henning_v._Tresckow.jpg","title":"Henning von Tresckow, 1944","desc":"Henning von Tresckow photographed in 1944, the year he was promoted to major general.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1976-130-53, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1976-130-53,_Henning_v._Tresckow.jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders portrait of a bald officer in a German army tunic with embroidered collar patches and an eagle on the breast."},"wiki":"Henning von Tresckow"},
    "ww2-366": {"questions":["Presided over from 1942 by Roland Freisler, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed more than 5,600 death sentences for political offences between 1934 and 1945, many of them carried out at Plötzensee.","Filmed proceedings at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> caught Freisler mocking Hoepner, one of more than a hundred conspirators of 20 July sentenced to death in over 55 trials."],"abstract":"The <b>People's Court</b> was the Nazi regime's tribunal for political offences, which between 1934 and 1945 sentenced more than 5,600 people to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of those it condemned came from Germany, Austria and the occupied Czech lands, and more than 1,400 of them were killed in Plötzensee prison in Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roland Freisler presided from 1942, and the memorial at Plötzensee describes how he humiliated and mocked defendants, interpreted the law arbitrarily and justified death sentences only in the briefest form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After 20 July 1944 an army court of honour expelled the officers involved from the Wehrmacht on Hitler's command, so that they could be tried by the People's Court rather than the Reich Court Martial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 55 trials followed, ending in over a hundred death sentences, and from October 1944 people who had merely supported the plotters or helped them escape were condemned as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first trial, of Field Marshal von Witzleben, Hoepner, Stieff, Yorck and four other officers on 7 and 8 August, ended in death sentences for all eight, who were hanged at Plötzensee the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler had ordered that the conspirators be hanged like cattle within 2 hours of sentence, and the defendants could not choose their own lawyers or see their indictments until shortly before trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The proceedings were filmed, and one exchange has Freisler badgering Hoepner, who had insisted he was no swine, to say which zoological category he did belong to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The special commission set up to hunt the plotters grew to more than 400 officials, and thousands were arrested in the following months, including former deputies of the Communist, Social Democratic and Centre parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some defendants used the dock to state their convictions: Yorck told the court that the essential issue was the state's totalitarian claim on the citizen, and Moltke wrote from prison that he was to die for his thoughts alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Gedenkstätte Plötzensee, “July 20, 1944,” German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gedenkstaette-ploetzensee.de/en/executions-in-ploetzensee-prison/july-20-1944. [Open access]","Gedenkstätte Plötzensee, “Unjust Justice System,” German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gedenkstaette-ploetzensee.de/en/unjust-justice-system. [Open access]","Gedenkstätte Plötzensee, “Reign of Judicial Terror,” German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gedenkstaette-ploetzensee.de/en/executions-in-ploetzensee-prison/reign-of-judicial-terror. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Das Attentat auf Adolf Hitler vom 20. Juli 1944,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/attentat-vom-20-juli [in German]. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Der Widerstand in der Wehrmacht,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, last changed 12 July 2019, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/militaerischer-widerstand [in German]. [Open access]","Hans Bernd Gisevius, <i>To the Bitter End</i>, trans. Richard and Clara Winston (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1947), 570–572, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.167391. [Open access]","Hans Rothfels, <i>The German Opposition to Hitler: An Appraisal</i>, trans. Lawrence Wilson, new rev. ed. (Chicago: Henry Regnery, 1962), 113–114, 122–123, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.58053. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the officers expelled from the army before trial?","a":"Because as serving officers they would have come before the Reich Court Martial; an army court of honour, acting on Hitler's command, expelled them so that the People's Court could try them instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its presiding judge, Freisler, held most of the 20 July trials himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Freisler's conduct of the trials so well known?","a":"The government-controlled press reported the first trial in detail, printing passages of the proceedings word for word, and the court scenes were filmed as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The surviving film, photographs and recordings now give an impression of Freisler's hate-filled conduct of the proceedings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the persecution reach far beyond the plotters themselves?","a":"The investigation soon showed that the conspiracy extended well beyond the army, and the special commission hunting the plotters grew to more than 400 officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From October 1944 the court also sentenced people who had only supported or sheltered those involved, and in all 89 people linked to the resistance of 20 July were killed at Plötzensee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Bundesarchiv_Bild_151-12-16%2C_Volksgerichtshof%2C_Erwin_v._Witzleben.jpg","title":"Erwin von Witzleben before the People's Court","desc":"Field Marshal Erwin von Witzleben standing before the People's Court in Berlin in August 1944, at the first trial of the 20 July conspirators.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 151-12-16, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_151-12-16,_Volksgerichtshof,_Erwin_v._Witzleben.jpg","alt":"A grey-haired man in a plain jacket stands with his hands clasped in front of a large swastika emblem, with a policeman in a spiked helmet seated below him."},"wiki":"People's Court (Germany)"},
    "ww2-367": {"questions":["An armband reading 'Deutscher Volkssturm Wehrmacht' was often the only uniform of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose men trained on Sundays and were meant to fight only near their own homes.","About 15,000 men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped hold encircled Breslau until May 1945, and its battalions joined SS and Hitler Youth units in the last defence of Berlin."],"abstract":"The <b>Volkssturm</b> was the German home guard formed in the autumn of 1944 to put every man between 16 and 60 who was not yet fighting into the defence of the homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a Nazi Party body: recruitment of a planned six million men was the task of Martin Bormann as head of the Party Chancellery, while military command lay with Heinrich Himmler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Gauleiter were made its commanders in their own regions, alongside their other late-war duties of evacuating civilians and preparing the destruction of vital installations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Men were to wear Army uniform if any could be found, otherwise party uniform or civilian clothes, and an American handbook of March 1945 noted the armband reading 'Deutscher Volkssturm Wehrmacht' and weapons limited to small arms, machine guns and bazookas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were meant to stay at their jobs, drill in their spare time, usually on Sundays, and be called out only when the enemy threatened their own district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A post-war German study for the US Army judged the scheme doomed from the outset, because the Wehrmacht could spare no weapons, clothing or instructors for hundreds of thousands of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its proper task, the same study argued, was building and guarding road blocks, and sending whole battalions far from home on the Eastern Front ran against its original purpose and could only fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some units broke up or surrendered on contact, but in the East many fought with the courage of despair, and about 15,000 Volkssturm men helped hold encircled Breslau until May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of the six reserve battalions in Berlin's defence plan were Volkssturm, and in the last days much of the capital was held by Volkssturm, SS and Hitler Youth formations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In mid-April 1945 Speer drafted a speech declaring Volkssturm service against the enemy voluntary, and the German Historical Museum puts its dead in the tens of thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Der Volkssturm,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/volkssturm [in German]. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 413, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 463, 481, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","War Department, <i>Handbook on German Military Forces</i>, TM-E 30-451 (Washington, DC: War Department, 15 March 1945), II-96, https://archive.org/details/TME-30-4511945. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>Military Improvisations during the Russian Campaign</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-201, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1951), 93–95, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-202. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 2 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 29, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag02unit. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, “Speer Document 019: Speer’s Speech in Hamburg on 16 April 1945,” <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, Supplement B (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1948), 942–43, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyagunit_0. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Volkssturm run by the Party rather than the army?","a":"It was conceived as a Party improvisation, recruited through Bormann's Party Chancellery and probably kept apart from the Wehrmacht on purpose, coming under army control only when it was committed to battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Gauleiter commanded it in their own regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it achieve so little in battle?","a":"Its men differed widely in age and training, drilled only on Sundays, and could be issued little beyond rifles, pistols and grenades, because the army had nothing to spare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The German Historical Museum judges its military value low, though in the East many units fought with the courage of despair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many Volkssturm men fight hard in the East?","a":"Years of hate propaganda against 'Bolshevik subhumans' and the savagery of the German occupiers had bred a panic fear of the Red Army's revenge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 15,000 Volkssturm men helped defend encircled Breslau for months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1974-120-20A%2C_Alter_Mann_mit_Armbinde_%22Deutscher_Volkssturm%22.jpg","title":"A Volkssturm recruit, autumn 1944","desc":"A propaganda photograph of a 55-year-old textile merchant wearing the Volkssturm armband as the first battalions assembled in the autumn of 1944.","credit":"Photograph by Falkowski, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1974-120-20A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1974-120-20A,_Alter_Mann_mit_Armbinde_%22Deutscher_Volkssturm%22.jpg","alt":"A grey-haired man in a long leather coat, holding a rifle and a hat, wears a white armband with an eagle emblem while other men gather in the street behind him."},"wiki":"Volkssturm","places":["city:1159139051"]},
    "ww2-368": {"questions":["The Nuremberg tribunal found that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knew his labour demands would be met by foreign workers under compulsion, but credited his late sabotage of Hitler's scorched-earth orders.","Hitler's former architect, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told the Nuremberg court that German munitions output peaked in August 1944 and aircraft production a month later, despite heavy bombing."],"abstract":"<b>Albert Speer</b> was Hitler's architect who became Reich Minister for Armaments and Munitions in February 1942 and ran the German war economy until the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He joined the Nazi Party in the early 1930s, staged the Nuremberg rallies with dramatic lighting, and as general building inspector planned the rebuilding of Berlin as the capital 'Germania'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler appointed him on 15 February 1942 after Fritz Todt was killed in an air crash, and in September 1943 his offices were merged into the Reich Ministry for Armaments and War Production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The US Strategic Bombing Survey credited him with rationalising production and removing the worst inefficiencies, and found that armament output rose threefold under his direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It added that the rise said less about the efficiency of dictatorship than about how far German industry had been undermobilised in the early years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that he introduced assembly-line methods, and that production peaked in August 1944 for munitions, September for aircraft and December for ordnance and the new U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Through his combined efforts with Guderian, tank output rose to 988 in May 1943, 300 of them the new Panthers, though it probably still fell well short of Soviet production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> His output rested on forced labour: the tribunal found that he passed his labour needs to Sauckel knowing they would be met by foreign workers under compulsion, and the Holocaust Museum writes that he used millions of forced labourers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the last months he opposed Hitler's scorched-earth orders with a counter-decree of September 1944, and he told the tribunal that in February 1945 he had planned to kill Hitler with poison gas through the bunker's ventilation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Convicted of war crimes and crimes against humanity, he was sentenced to 20 years, and under cross-examination he admitted that reports of secret wonder weapons had been exaggerated to keep people fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Speer,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judspeer.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 19 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-19-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 20 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 21 June 1946, cross-examination of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Gabriel Eikenberg, “Albert Speer 1905–1981,” LeMO Biografie, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 5 February 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/albert-speer [in German]. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Albert Speer,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 28 November 2005, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/albert-speer. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 119–121, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could German output rise so steeply after 1942?","a":"The bombing survey found that Germany had been slow to mobilise, clinging to the idea of a short war, so there was much unused capacity for Speer to bring into production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speer himself pointed to assembly-line methods and wider programmes for scarce materials and parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he convicted although he ran no deportations himself?","a":"The tribunal held that he set the numbers of workers his industries needed and passed them to Sauckel, knowing the demands would be met by foreign labourers under compulsion, and that he urged the programme's extension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also used concentration camp labour in industries under his control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tribunal take his opposition to Hitler into account?","a":"It recognised in mitigation that his blocked industries kept many workers at home, and that near the end he had the courage to tell Hitler the war was lost and to sabotage the destruction of production facilities at personal risk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He received 20 years rather than a death sentence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1981-052-06A%2C_Albert_Speer_spricht_in_Munitionsfabrik.jpg","title":"Speer in a bomb-damaged munitions works, 1944","desc":"Albert Speer in a munitions factory damaged by an air raid in May 1944, where he came to thank the workers for restarting production unexpectedly quickly.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1981-052-06A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1981-052-06A,_Albert_Speer_spricht_in_Munitionsfabrik.jpg","alt":"A man in a suit raises his arm in the Nazi salute among a crowd of factory workers, many of them saluting too, inside a large industrial hall."},"wiki":"Albert Speer","places":["city:1159151529"]},
    "ww2-369": {"questions":["Deported in their millions from the occupied Soviet territories, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> received lower rations and wages than workers from western Europe and were often persecuted again after returning home.","An instruction of August 1942 to supply Speer with a further million Russian labourers fed the deportation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the regime's term for civilian workers from the occupied Soviet Union."],"abstract":"<b>Ostarbeiter</b>, ‘Eastern workers’, was the Nazi term for civilian workers taken from the occupied territories of the Soviet Union to labour in the German Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An early recruitment of volunteers soon gave way to forcible deportation, and the totals differ: the Forced Labor 1939–1945 archive gives 2.1 million in one place and more than three million in another, while the US Holocaust Memorial Museum puts Soviet citizens deported in 1942 to 1944 at nearly three million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 20 February 1942 Himmler issued the Eastern Workers' Decrees, which subjected them to a discriminatory special law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They had to wear a badge marked 'OST', were usually housed in special camps, and were treated far worse than forced labourers from other countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their food, pay and housing lay far below those of workers from northern and western Europe, whose rations roughly matched those of Germans, and even suspected sexual relations with Germans could bring punishment up to execution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Nuremberg tribunal described man-hunts in streets, cinemas and churches, and quoted an order to SD officers in Ukraine that when a village had to be burned, its whole population would be handed over by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At a conference in August 1942 Sauckel was instructed to supply Speer with a further million Russian labourers for the armament industry by the end of October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In March 1944 Sauckel himself said that of five million foreign workers who had arrived in Germany, not even 200,000 had come voluntarily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Without the forced labourers, prisoners of war and camp inmates, the German Historical Museum concludes, the Reich could not have carried on the war beyond 1942 at the latest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After liberation many former Eastern workers met discrimination in the Soviet Union and were persecuted for supposed collaboration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Freie Universität Berlin and Stiftung “Erinnerung, Verantwortung und Zukunft,” “Forced Labor – Terms,” <i>Forced Labor 1939–1945: Memory and History</i>, https://www.zwangsarbeit-archiv.de/en/zwangsarbeit/zwangsarbeit/zwangsarbeit-begriffe/index.html. [Open access]","Freie Universität Berlin and Stiftung “Erinnerung, Verantwortung und Zukunft,” “Events and Topics,” <i>Forced Labor 1939–1945: Memory and History</i>, https://www.zwangsarbeit-archiv.de/en/zwangsarbeit/ereignisse/index.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Forced Labor: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/forced-labor-an-overview. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Speer,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judspeer.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Zwangsarbeit,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 August 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft/zwangsarbeit [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Eastern workers treated worse than other foreign labourers?","a":"Because Nazi racial doctrine ranked Slavs as particularly inferior, and the lower a group stood in that hierarchy, the worse its conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Western Europeans received rations close to those of Germans, while most Eastern workers suffered hunger and abuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did recruitment turn from volunteers to force?","a":"Because volunteers were too few for German demands, and quotas set by Sauckel were filled by conscription where appeals failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sauckel himself admitted in 1944 that not even 200,000 of five million foreign workers had come voluntarily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did their suffering not end with liberation?","a":"Many who returned to the Soviet Union were treated with suspicion, discriminated against and persecuted for supposed collaboration with the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of the war millions of non-German displaced persons remained in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J10842%2C_Gruppe_von_Ostarbeitern_vor_Fahrt_nach_Deutschland.jpg","title":"Eastern workers before departure, 1943","desc":"A German propaganda photograph from July 1943, captioned as volunteers and their relatives listening to the mayor before leaving for work in Germany.","credit":"Photograph by Kurrasch, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-J10842, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J10842,_Gruppe_von_Ostarbeitern_vor_Fahrt_nach_Deutschland.jpg","alt":"A large crowd of men, women and children, many of the women in headscarves, standing packed together in a town square."},"wiki":"Ostarbeiter"},
    "ww2-370": {"questions":["Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that rumours of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were spread to keep Germans fighting in late 1944, when oil output had collapsed and Hitler could only fight for time.","Churchill told the Commons in November 1944 that the German announcement of a new rocket, the latest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the regime's propaganda, reflected its desperate need to encourage its people."],"abstract":"<b>Wunderwaffe</b>, ‘wonder weapon’, was the Nazi propaganda name for the new weapons that were supposed to turn the war in Germany's favour at the last moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1943 the regime announced 'retaliation weapons' as its answer to Allied bombing and spread the belief that the Wehrmacht had technologically superior systems that could still bring a decisive turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The V-1 flying bomb was first fired at London on 13 June 1944, but only about a quarter of those launched reached their target, and the brief euphoria it caused soon turned to scepticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The V-2 rocket followed in September, and Churchill told the Commons in November that the German announcement of it reflected the regime's desperate need to give its people some encouragement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Jet fighters began to come off the lines in October 1944, yet Speer testified that bombing so slowed mass production of the new weapons that they could not be used in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By the end of 1944 Hitler's strategy was bankrupt: he had staked his last reserves on the offensive in the West and could only fight for time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German oil consumption had exceeded production since May 1944, and by December, according to Speer, the fuel shortage had reached catastrophic proportions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Armoured vehicle output still reached its wartime peak in December, but heavy bombing of the Ruhr that month cut steel production to about half the September level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that Hitler deceived his people with rumours of a miracle weapon to keep up resistance, and that troops he visited at the front believed new weapons would bring victory without soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Such slogans could not hide the senselessness of the fighting or the coming defeat, the German Historical Museum concludes, though Speer believed this faith explained why so many Germans gave their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Johannes Leicht, “Die ‘Wunderwaffen’ V1 und V2,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 9 November 2023, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/die-wunderwaffen-v1-und-v2 [in German]. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 20 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 21 June 1946, cross-examination of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 411–413, https://archive.org/details/cmh-pub-30-5-1-stalingrad-to-berlin-the-german-defeat-in-the-east-by-earl-ziemke. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “German Long-Range Rockets,” <i>Hansard</i>, 10 November 1944, vol. 404, cols. 1653–4, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/nov/10/german-long-range-rockets. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Volkssturm,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/volkssturm [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime promise wonder weapons?","a":"To keep up civilian morale and the fighting spirit of the troops under Allied bombing, by persuading them that Germany still held a technological means of turning the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speer testified that the hope was deliberately fed to keep up resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the new weapons fail to change the war?","a":"The V-1 mostly missed or was shot down, with only about a quarter reaching its target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speer said bombing delayed mass production of jets and other new weapons until it was too late, and from May 1944 the attacks on fuel plants starved what was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Speer's testimony on this point matter?","a":"As armaments minister he knew what was actually being built, and he agreed under cross-examination that reports of a new and secret weapon had been exaggerated to keep the people in the war, mostly in its last phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also said he had protested to Hitler and Goebbels against deceiving the people in this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Bundesarchiv_Bild_141-2738%2C_Unterirdische_Produktion_von_Me_262.jpg","title":"Underground Me 262 production, 1945","desc":"Messerschmitt Me 262 jet fighters being built in the underground REIMAHG armaments works near Kahla in Thuringia in 1945.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 141-2738, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_141-2738,_Unterirdische_Produktion_von_Me_262.jpg","alt":"The tail and rear fuselage of an aircraft surrounded by ladders and work stands inside a rough-walled tunnel."},"wiki":"Wunderwaffe"},
    "ww2-311": {"questions":["Made by English craftsmen to a design King George VI approved, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> honoured a victory that Churchill called the greatest disaster in German military history.","Stalin passed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Roosevelt after accepting it for the city's people, as the Allies honoured the battle that Stalin said began the decline of the German army."],"abstract":"The <b>Sword of Stalingrad</b> was the sword of honour that King George VI had made for the people of Stalingrad, presented to Stalin by Churchill at the Tehran Conference on 29 November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its blade bore the inscription 'To the steel-hearted citizens of Stalingrad, a gift from King George VI as a token of the homage of the British people', engraved in Russian and English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The King chose and approved the design himself, and it was made by English craftsmen whose families had made swords for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ceremony took place in the conference room of the Soviet embassy in Tehran, with a guard of honour of Soviet and British soldiers and a band playing both national anthems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin accepted it for the citizens of Stalingrad and passed it to Roosevelt, who called it a very fine gift and praised the people of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was one of several Allied tributes, for Roosevelt had hailed the 162 days of epic battle and later sent the city a scroll saying its victory had been the turning point of the war of the Allied nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in February 1943 that Stalingrad had brought upon the German armies the greatest disaster they had ever suffered in all their military history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stalin's own verdict, in November 1943, was that Stalingrad marked the beginning of the decline of the German fascist army, from which the Germans never recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Russian historian adds that the defeat of Germany's allies that winter cracked the Axis bloc, setting Italy, Romania, Hungary and Finland looking for a way out of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American official history sets Stalingrad beside Guadalcanal and the North African landings as the start of the Axis retreat on all fronts, after which the war would be fought on other terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “Log of the Trip,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, 466–67, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d353. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 February 1943, vol. 386, cols. 1453–531, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/feb/11/war-situation. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Congratulations to Marshal Stalin on the Russian Victory at Stalingrad,” 4 February 1943, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/congratulations-marshal-stalin-the-russian-victory-stalingrad. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Speech at Celebration Meeting, November 6, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 112–31, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/USSR/GPW/index.html. [Open access]","Mikhail Yu. Myagkov, “The Battle of Stalingrad in the Context of Modern History,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stalingradskaya-bitva-v-kontekste-sovremennoy-istorii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 79–80, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-4.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 118, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a British king send a sword to a Soviet city?","a":"Because British leaders hailed Stalingrad as a decisive defeat for the common enemy. Churchill had told the Commons that it brought the German armies the greatest disaster in their history, and the sword was presented, as its inscription said, as a token of the homage of the British people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the sword handed over at Tehran rather than in Stalingrad?","a":"Because Churchill, Roosevelt and Stalin were together at Tehran, and the presentation could be made before all three delegations. The ceremony was held in the Soviet embassy just before a plenary session, with a guard of honour and both national anthems, and was filmed and photographed afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Stalingrad so often called the turning point of the war?","a":"Because after it, as an American official history puts it, the war in the East would be fought on other terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Stalin said the Germans never recovered from it, Roosevelt's scroll called it the turning point, and an American official history counts it with Guadalcanal and North Africa as the start of the Axis retreat on all fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/%D0%9C%D0%B5%D1%87_%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%9C%D1%83%D0%B7%D0%B5%D0%B5-%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B5_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B1%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B0%C2%BB.jpeg","title":"The Sword of Stalingrad on display","desc":"The two-handed sword and its decorated scabbard in a glass case at the Stalingrad Battle museum-reserve in Volgograd, photographed in May 2014.","credit":"Photograph by Patriot k, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%B5%D1%87_%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%9C%D1%83%D0%B7%D0%B5%D0%B5-%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B5_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B1%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B2%D0%B0%C2%BB.jpeg","alt":"A long straight sword with a gilded hilt and a decorated scabbard mounted diagonally against a red background in a display case."},"wiki":"Sword of Stalingrad"},
    "ww2-312": {"questions":["The Motherland Calls, the tallest statue in the world when it was raised, stands on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> above a hall where 34 mosaic banners carry 7,200 names of the fallen.","Boris Polevoy accused Vuchetich of gigantomania over the memorial on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Chuikov defended a project that took from 1958 to 1967 to complete."],"abstract":"<b>Mamayev Kurgan</b> is the hill in the centre of Volgograd, the former Stalingrad, on which stands the memorial ensemble 'To the Heroes of the Battle of Stalingrad', opened on 15 October 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Marked on military maps as Height 102, it is an ancient burial mound about 90 metres (300 feet) high that divided the city in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1942 the German Sixth Army drove past it into the city, and the fighting at the hill and the railway station became so heavy that it was hard to tell who was attacking and who defending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two regiments of Rodimtsev's 13th Guards Division took the hill after crossing the Volga, and fighting for it swung back and forth until January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The memorial was built between 1958 and 1967 by a team under the sculptor Yevgeny Vuchetich, who said the idea came from veterans asking why their own soil had no monument like his in Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its central figure, The Motherland Calls, was the tallest statue in the world when it was made, 85 metres (279 feet) high with a sword of 33 metres (108 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Visitors climb past a statue of a soldier who stood to the death and ruined walls to the Square of Heroes, the Hall of Military Glory and the Square of Sorrow, a route laid out to lead from heroism through mourning to grandeur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the hall 34 lowered mosaic banners carry 7,200 names, and at the opening Brezhnev called the battle gigantic both in scale and in its effect on the whole later course of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The design was fiercely argued over: the writer Boris Polevoy accused Vuchetich of gigantomania and of wanting to dress the hill in granite costing tens of millions of roubles, while Chuikov defended the project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Russian study of its symbolic politics notes that readings of the monument stress that people of every nationality gave their lives and that the Motherland mourns all her children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 389–90, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Decision/USA-EF-Decision-19.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 41–42, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-3.html. [Open access]","Aleksey D. Popov, “The Creation of the Memorial Complex ‘To the Heroes of the Battle of Stalingrad’ on Mamayev Kurgan: Historical Memory, Art and Soviet Monumental Propaganda,” <i>Science Journal of Volgograd State University. History, Area Studies, International Relations</i> 28, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sozdanie-memorialnogo-kompleksa-geroyam-stalingradskoy-bitvy-na-mamaevom-kurgane-istoricheskaya-pamyat-iskusstvo-i-sovetskaya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Oleg V. Ryabov, “The Monument ‘The Motherland Calls!’ in Symbolic Politics: Contours of a Problem Field,” <i>Labyrinth: Journal of Social and Humanitarian Research</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/monument-rodina-mat-zovet-v-simvolicheskoy-politike-kontury-problemnogo-polya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dmitry V. Vasiliev and Nikita A. Mazaev, “The Monument-Ensemble on Mamayev Kurgan as an Experience of Rethinking Commemorative Projects and Practices,” <i>Tomsk State University Journal of Cultural Studies and Art History</i>, no. 49 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pamyatnik-ansambl-na-mamaevom-kurgane-kak-opyt-pereosmysleniya-proektov-i-praktik-kommemoratsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir I. Popkov et al., “‘And Then Mamayev Kurgan Spoke…’: On the 50th Anniversary of the Opening of the Monument-Ensemble ‘To the Heroes of the Battle of Stalingrad’,” <i>Izvestia of the Volgograd State Pedagogical University</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/i-vot-on-zagovoril-mamaev-kurgan-k-50-letiyu-otkrytiya-pamyatnika-ansamblya-geroyam-stalingradskoy-bitvy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Mikhail Yu. Myagkov, “The Battle of Stalingrad in the Context of Modern History,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stalingradskaya-bitva-v-kontekste-sovremennoy-istorii. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the hill fought over so fiercely in 1942?","a":"Because it was the highest point in a narrow city stretched along the river. At about 90 metres (300 feet) it divided Stalingrad in two and dominated much of the city, and the fighting for it swung back and forth until January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the memorial provoke public argument?","a":"Because its scale and cost divided people who had known the battle. Polevoy, a former war correspondent at Stalingrad, wanted a monument that was strict, manly and simple, and writers such as Nekrasov and Simonov and General Rodimtsev took his side, while Chuikov backed Vuchetich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the memorial laid out as a route rather than a single statue?","a":"Because Vuchetich wanted to commemorate mass heroism, which he thought a figure on a pedestal could not convey. A sequence of squares, walls and halls, visited one after another, was meant to build a particular emotional state before the visitor reached the main statue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/RIAN_archive_41580_Mamai_Hill..jpg","title":"Victory Day on Mamayev Kurgan, 1982","desc":"A soldier's mother weeping for her son, killed in the Battle of Stalingrad, at the Hall of Military Glory on Mamayev Kurgan on 9 May 1982.","credit":"Photograph by Perventsev, RIA Novosti archive, image 41580, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_41580_Mamai_Hill..jpg","alt":"An elderly woman in a patterned headscarf wiping her eyes with a handkerchief, with a young boy beside her in a crowd."},"wiki":"Mamayev Kurgan"},
    "ww2-313": {"questions":["Kleist took 400,000 men into the Kuban bridgehead in early 1943, drawing out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until Hitler ordered the Gotenkopf evacuated that September and the Soviets reached Taman.","Stalin's order of 25 January 1943 counted Mozdok, Nalchik, Pyatigorsk and Stavropol among the towns freed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though Army Group A itself escaped encirclement."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Caucasus</b> is the name Soviet and Russian historians give to the fighting of July 1942 to October 1943 in which the Red Army first held and then drove back the German Army Group A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its prize was oil, and Paulus told the Nuremberg tribunal that Hitler had declared at Poltava on 1 June 1942, 'If I do not get the oil of Maikop and Grosny, then I must end this war.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once Stalingrad was encircled, Army Group A had to pull back, sending First Panzer Army north through Rostov while Kleist took 400,000 men into the Kuban bridgehead the Germans called the Gotenkopf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet blow against the army group itself stayed close to a token one, since transport troubles and cautious leadership kept the Transcaucasus Front from closing the vast encirclement the Stavka had planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, Stalin's order of 25 January 1943 counted Mozdok, Nalchik, Pyatigorsk, Stavropol and Armavir among the towns liberated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The divisions brought out of the mountains had grown slow in months of static warfare, and Manstein complained that they asked for a long period of grace before they would move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler held on to the bridgehead through the summer, postponing its evacuation on 14 August for fear of the effect on Germany's allies and on neutral Turkey, and on 3 September ordered it given up, leaving the Soviets 'an uninhabitable desert'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The battle ended with the Novorossiysk–Taman offensive of 10 September to 9 October 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its cost to the Germans is disputed: Soviet-era figures counted over 100,000 lost in 1942 and 275,000 killed in 1943, an American historian puts all Axis losses at 184,000, and a Russian study of German records finds at least 315,000 killed, captured, wounded and sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The standard Russian statistical study gives Soviet losses for the whole battle as 344,390 killed and missing and 605,122 wounded, sick and frostbitten, and one recent estimate puts the liberation of 1943 alone at about 520,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 81–86, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-5.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 155–63, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-8.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 7, Fifty-Sixth Day, 11 February 1946, testimony of Friedrich Paulus, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-11-46.asp. [Open access]","Ilya V. Kiselev, “The Human Losses of the German Troops in the Battle for the Caucasus, 1942–1943,” <i>Humanities and Law Research</i>, no. 1 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lyudskie-poteri-germanskih-voysk-v-hode-bitvy-za-kavkaz-1942-1943-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ilya V. Kiselev, “The Price of Liberation: The Human Losses of the Soviet Troops in the Offensive in the North Caucasus in 1943,” <i>Humanities and Law Research</i>, no. 1 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tsena-osvobozhdeniya-lyudskie-poteri-sovetskih-voysk-v-hode-nastupleniya-na-severnom-kavkaze-v-1943-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Timothy A. Wray, <i>Standing Fast: German Defensive Doctrine on the Russian Front during World War II, Prewar to March 1943</i>, Research Survey 5 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1986), 156–58, https://archive.org/details/csi-research-survey-no.-5-standing-fast-german-defensive-doctrine-on-the-russian. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day of the Supreme Commander-in-Chief, January 25, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 88–89, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/USSR/GPW/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans leave so many men in the Kuban instead of bringing them out?","a":"Because by late January it was too late to take the whole army group out through Rostov, and Hitler wanted to keep a foothold that threatened the Caucasus, later putting off its evacuation for fear of the effect on his allies and on Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 400,000 men in the bridgehead could do little more than keep up the fiction of that threat, and 100,000 were later flown out without their weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Red Army not trap Army Group A in the Caucasus?","a":"Because the offensive against the army group itself never became more than a token one. The Stavka seems to have planned a gigantic encirclement completed from the south and east, but organisational and transport problems and cautious leadership on the Transcaucasus Front kept it from taking the initiative on any large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for German losses in the battle differ so widely?","a":"Because they come from different kinds of record. Soviet totals rest on wartime front reports of enemy killed, while a recent Russian count works from German staff returns and includes the wounded and sick, while an American historian, adding up German figures period by period, reaches a far lower total of 184,000 for all the Axis forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/RIAN_archive_66145_Village_fighting.jpg","title":"Soviet troops fighting for a village, Caucasian front","desc":"Soviet soldiers with rifles and a machine gun in action among the buildings of a village on the Caucasian front, photographed by Max Alpert and dated 1942 in the RIA Novosti archive.","credit":"Photograph by Max Alpert, RIA Novosti archive, image 66145, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_66145_Village_fighting.jpg","alt":"Soldiers lying and crouching behind a wooden cart and bushes, aiming weapons towards a thatched building in bright sunlight."},"wiki":"Battle of the Caucasus"},
    "ww2-314": {"questions":["German transport aircraft supplying the Stalingrad pocket lost their base at Tatsinskaya on Christmas Eve 1942, when the 24th Tank Corps arrived during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after 6 days behind the German lines.","Second Guards Army was diverted to stop Manstein's relief attack in December 1942, so the Stavka halved the depth of Saturn and launched the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead along the middle Don."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Little Saturn</b> was the Soviet offensive on the middle Don of 16–30 December 1942, called the Middle Don operation in Russian, in which the South-Western and Voronezh Fronts broke the Italian Eighth Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began as a far larger plan, Saturn, approved by Stalin on 2 December, to reach Rostov and so cut off both the Fourth Panzer Army and Army Group A in the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Manstein's attempt to relieve Stalingrad opened on 12 December, Second Guards Army was sent to meet it, and the Stavka turned both arms of the plan inward towards Tatsinskaya and Morozovsk, halving its depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three Soviet armies broke into the Italian line on 16 and 17 December and within 4 days had torn a gap 160 kilometres (100 miles) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bad weather grounded the Voronezh Front's aircraft until midday at the start, but the two air armies flew more than 2,000 sorties in the first 5 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The 24th Tank Corps was sent through the enemy's rear for Tatsinskaya, and after covering about 350 kilometres (220 miles) in 6 days it stormed the airfield on the night of 24 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tatsinskaya and Morozovsk were the bases for the airlift to the trapped German Sixth Army, and losing the forward airfields spoiled every prospect of renewing the relief attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manstein had to take a panzer division from the relief force to guard them, and an American official history concludes that he had in effect said farewell to Sixth Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin's order of 25 January 1943 named Kantemirovka, Morozovsky and Millerovo among the towns liberated, and the most distinguished units took names such as Tatsinskaya along with Guards status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Russian historian has argued that the full Saturn would have trapped the troops Manstein instead brought out through Rostov, who went on to defeat the Red Army in the Donbass and at Kharkov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 483–93, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Decision/USA-EF-Decision-23.html. [Open access]","Timothy A. Wray, <i>Standing Fast: German Defensive Doctrine on the Russian Front during World War II, Prewar to March 1943</i>, Research Survey 5 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1986), 151–52, https://archive.org/details/csi-research-survey-no.-5-standing-fast-german-defensive-doctrine-on-the-russian. [Open access]","Utash B. Ochirov, “Operation Big Saturn: The Plan and the Possibilities of Its Realisation,” <i>Science Journal of Volgograd State University. History, Area Studies, International Relations</i>, no. 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/operatsiya-bolshoy-saturn-plan-i-vozmozhnosti-realizatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vasily D. Kurdyukov, “Stalingrad on the Middle Don,” <i>Eurasian Forum</i>, no. 1 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stalingrad-na-srednem-donu. [in Russian] [Open access]","Viktor A. Shamray, “The Combat Operations of the 2nd Air Army of the Voronezh Front in the Middle Don Operation, Little Saturn, in December 1942,” <i>Eurasian Forum</i>, no. 1 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/boevye-deystviya-2-y-vozdushnoy-armii-voronezhskogo-fronta-v-srednedonskoy-operatsii-malyy-saturn-v-dekabre-1942-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day of the Supreme Commander-in-Chief, January 25, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 88–89, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/USSR/GPW/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the original Saturn plan reduced?","a":"Because Manstein's relief attack towards Stalingrad began on 12 December, and the reserves meant for the deeper blow were needed to stop it. Second Guards Army went south to meet the German thrust, and the Stavka cut the plan so that the two arms of the offensive turned inward to meet near Tatsinskaya and Morozovsk instead of driving on Rostov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet commanders later disagreed about when that decision was taken and who proposed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Italian sector give way so quickly?","a":"Because the allied armies on the Don were the weakest part of a long, exposed flank, and the Germans had little behind them. Within 4 days of the first attacks three Soviet armies had opened a gap 160 kilometres (100 miles) wide, and the weak 27th Panzer Division posted behind the Italians could not close it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack on Tatsinskaya matter for the Germans trapped at Stalingrad?","a":"Because Tatsinskaya and Morozovsk were the air bases on which Sixth Army's supply by air depended. Losing them, and having to send armour from the relief force to protect them, ended any real hope of reaching the pocket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3d/Tacinskaja_1942.jpg","title":"The airfield at Tatsinskaya, December 1942","desc":"Wrecked German aircraft and a Soviet T-34 tank in the snow at Tatsinskaya, the air base for the Stalingrad airlift that the 24th Tank Corps overran on 24 December 1942.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tacinskaja_1942.jpg","alt":"A snowy field with the wreckage of several aircraft, one with a cross on its fuselage, and a tank on the right."},"wiki":"Operation Little Saturn"},
    "ww2-315": {"questions":["Czechoslovak volunteers under Ludvík Svoboda fought their first action at Sokolovo in March 1943 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their company commander Otakar Jaroš died leading a counter-attack against German tanks.","Stalin claimed on 1 May 1943 that the Germans had tried to arrange a 'German Stalingrad' in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and failed, yet the Soviet front had been pushed back to the Donets."],"abstract":"The <b>Third Battle of Kharkov</b> was the German counteroffensive of February and March 1943 in which Manstein's Army Group South threw back the Soviet advance that had followed Stalingrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet drive into the Donbass had opened on 29 January, and on 16 February the Red Army entered Kharkov after the SS Panzer Corps abandoned it against Hitler's orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 23 February Stalin told the army it was attacking on a front 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) long, and days earlier Soviet tanks had stood 58 kilometres (36 miles) from Manstein's headquarters at Zaporozhye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 19 February two panzer armies struck the flank of the overstretched spearheads, while the Fourth Air Force flew about 1,000 sorties a day against 350 in January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> SS divisions re-entered Kharkov on 11 March against orders, cleared it in 3 days of street fighting, and took Belgorod on 18 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The counterstroke shattered the Soviet First Guards Army, and the Germans counted 615 destroyed tanks, though thousands of Soviet troops walked out of a trap the panzer divisions had too little infantry to close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Russian historian has argued that the troops Manstein brought out through Rostov were the ones who then defeated the Soviet offensives in the Donbass and at Kharkov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By late March the southern front stood almost where the German summer offensive of 1942 had begun, with a large Soviet bulge west of Kursk, and an American official history calls the battle the last German victory in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Sokolovo the 1st Czechoslovak Independent Battalion, the first Allied unit on the Soviet-German front, fought its first battle on 8 March, and its company commander Otakar Jaroš was made a Hero of the Soviet Union after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stalin claimed on 1 May that the Germans had brought over thirty new divisions from the West to arrange a German Stalingrad at Kharkov, while the German record shows seven fresh divisions ordered east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 81–97, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-5.html. [Open access]","Timothy A. Wray, <i>Standing Fast: German Defensive Doctrine on the Russian Front during World War II, Prewar to March 1943</i>, Research Survey 5 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1986), 154–64, https://archive.org/details/csi-research-survey-no.-5-standing-fast-german-defensive-doctrine-on-the-russian. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day of the Supreme Commander-in-Chief No. 195, May 1, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 100–107, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/USSR/GPW/index.html. [Open access]","Larisa N. Yampolskaya, “The First Independent Czechoslovak Battalion in the Battles for Sokolovo, 8–13 March 1943, in the Context of the Third Battle for Kharkov,” <i>Pskov Military-Historical Bulletin</i>, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/pervyy-otdelnyy-chehoslovatskiy-batalon-v-boyah-za-s-sokolovo-8-13-marta-1943-g-v-kontekste-tretiey-bitvy-za-harkov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Utash B. Ochirov, “Operation Big Saturn: The Plan and the Possibilities of Its Realisation,” <i>Science Journal of Volgograd State University. History, Area Studies, International Relations</i>, no. 4 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/operatsiya-bolshoy-saturn-plan-i-vozmozhnosti-realizatsii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vitaly A. Beldyugin, “The Operations of the Troops of the South-Western Front in the Donbass in February 1943,” <i>Cultural Landscape of Regions</i> 5, no. 2 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/deystviya-voysk-yugo-zapadnogo-fronta-na-donbasse-v-fevrale-1943-goda. [in Russian] [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day of the Supreme Commander-in-Chief No. 95, February 23, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 91–99, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/USSR/GPW/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Germans counterattack so soon after the disaster at Stalingrad?","a":"Because the Soviet advance had outrun its strength while the Germans had kept theirs. Army Group South had retreated without excessive loss and had a fresh striking force in the SS Panzer Corps, and seven more divisions were ordered from the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet spearheads, by contrast, had followed the line of least resistance, and casualties, breakdowns and lengthening supply lines had worn them down by the time the blow fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the city fall to the Soviets in mid-February despite Hitler's order?","a":"Because the order could not be carried out. Lanz, told to hold Kharkov and counterattack at the same time, had only three divisions fit for combat against four Soviet armies, and the city was not fortified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the corridor out shrank to little more than 1.6 kilometres (one mile), the SS Panzer Corps withdrew against orders, and Hitler sacked Lanz on 20 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans take so few prisoners in their victory?","a":"Because the panzer divisions that did the damage had too little infantry of their own to seal off the battlefield. They destroyed tanks and guns in large numbers, but thousands of Soviet soldiers simply walked out of the trap before it could be closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J22454%2C_Charkow%2C_Sch%C3%BCtzenpanzerwagen_der_SS.jpg","title":"Waffen-SS troops in Kharkov, March 1943","desc":"A German propaganda-company photograph of an armoured half-track and SS infantry on Sumskaya Street in Kharkov, taken in March 1943 when the SS Panzer Corps retook the city.","credit":"Photograph by Zschäkel, Bundesarchiv Bild 183-J22454, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J22454,_Charkow,_Sch%C3%BCtzenpanzerwagen_der_SS.jpg","alt":"A camouflaged half-track armoured vehicle with soldiers walking beside it on a wet city street lined with damaged buildings, and a tank on the right."},"wiki":"Third Battle of Kharkov"},
    "ww2-316": {"questions":["Revived from the imperial army of Peter I, the title of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marked the elite of a rebuilt Red Army, whose rifle regiments had an extra submachine-gun company.","Whole armies could join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Eleventh Guards Army opened the Orel offensive of July 1943 with 3,000 guns and heavy mortars, almost twice an army's issue at Stalingrad."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet Guards</b> were the units of the Red Army and Navy given the title 'Guards' for distinguished service, an honour revived from the Russian imperial guard that Peter I had founded in 1687.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rocket-artillery units received the name on formation in the summer of 1941, but the Guards date their birth to 18 September 1941, when the 100th, 127th, 153rd and 161st Rifle Divisions became the 1st to 4th Guards for their fighting near Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same order gave them special Guards banners and raised their pay, by half for officers and double for soldiers, rates later paid in every unit that won the title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of 21 May 1942 added the word 'Guards' to the rank of every member of such a unit and created a Guards badge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American intelligence noted that Guards formations were upgraded in men and equipment, a Guards rifle regiment having an extra submachine-gun company and a replacement pool twice the usual size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The title was one strand of a wider rebuilding in 1942, when tank corps returned, tank armies were formed on the German model, and the army took on the old trappings of rank, etiquette, decorations and Russian military tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 9 October 1942 single command was restored and the commissars reduced to deputies for political work, and on 6 January 1943 shoulder boards, the emblem of the old army, came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1943 the Guards formed an elite within an army that otherwise neglected its infantry, and they were first to receive more anti-tank weapons and the new Sudayev submachine gun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whole armies bore the title, and Eleventh Guards Army opened the Orel offensive of July 1943 with 3,000 guns and heavy mortars, almost double the standard issue at Stalingrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stalin held them up as the model, ordering every arm in February 1944 to beat the enemy 'as our valiant Guardsmen are beating him'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Dmitry V. Shunyakov, “The Development of the Soviet System of Awards and Incentives for Servicemen in 1941–1945,” <i>Military Historical Journal</i>, no. 4 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/razvitie-sovetskoy-nagradnoy-sistemy-i-pooschreniy-voennosluzhaschih-v-1941-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Evgeny G. Guzhva, “The Revival of Moral Traditions in the Red Army during the Great Patriotic War,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 3 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vozrozhdenie-nravstvennyh-traditsiy-v-krasnoy-armii-v-gody-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","United States War Department, <i>Handbook on U.S.S.R. Military Forces</i>, Technical Manual TM 30-430 (Washington, DC: War Department, 1945–46), https://archive.org/details/tm-30-430-handbook-on-u.-s.-s.-r.-military-forces-1946. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 34–36, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-2.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 146–47, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-8.html. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day of the Supreme Commander-in-Chief No. 16, February 23, 1944,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 138–46, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/USSR/GPW/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a revolutionary army revive a title from the tsars?","a":"Because the disasters of 1941 and 1942 convinced the leadership that old military traditions gave armies staying power in the field. The Guards were restored as one of the first steps in reviving forgotten traditions, followed by decorations, strict rank distinctions and, in January 1943, shoulder boards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Guards title matter to the soldiers who received it?","a":"Because it brought tangible rewards as well as honour. Officers in Guards units drew one and a half times the normal pay and soldiers double, every member carried the word 'Guards' in his rank, and the unit received a special banner and badge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Guards units given better equipment than others?","a":"Because the title marked units that had fought best or been given especially hazardous tasks. Guards armies were upgraded in personnel and equipment, and Guards rifle units had extra submachine guns and larger reserves of replacements, while the ordinary infantry received the poorest recruits and little training.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/%D0%92%D1%80%D1%83%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%93%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B0_16_%D0%93%D0%B2.%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BF_02_%D0%BE%D0%BA%D1%82%D1%8F%D0%B1%D1%80%D1%8F_1942.jpg","title":"Guards badges presented, 2 October 1942","desc":"Soldiers of the 16th Guards Mortar Regiment drawn up in a forest clearing for the presentation of the Guards badge on 2 October 1942, a photograph kept in the unit's war diary.","credit":"Central Archive of the Russian Ministry of Defence, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D1%80%D1%83%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D0%93%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B0_16_%D0%93%D0%B2.%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BF_02_%D0%BE%D0%BA%D1%82%D1%8F%D0%B1%D1%80%D1%8F_1942.jpg","alt":"A long line of soldiers in caps and belted tunics standing at attention among tall trees."},"wiki":"Guards unit (Soviet Union)"},
    "ww2-317": {"questions":["From August 1942 the medium T-34 was assembled on a conveyor in the old tractor shops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which turned three-quarters of its capacity over to the new tank.","Isaak Zaltsman ran <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before taking charge of the whole Soviet tank industry, and his dismissal in 1949 later fed a legend of the wronged 'tank king'."],"abstract":"<b>Tankograd</b>, ‘tank city’, was the unofficial wartime name of the Chelyabinsk Kirov Plant in the southern Urals, the giant tank works formed on the site of the Chelyabinsk Tractor Plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even before the war the new tractor plant had been told to build the heavy KV tank, as a second source beside the Kirov works in Leningrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 12 September 1941 the State Defence Committee decided to send the Kirov works to Chelyabinsk instead of Nizhny Tagil, and parts of it merged with the tractor plant, the Kharkov diesel works and other factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Isaak Zaltsman, director of the Kirov works, led its move east and the building of the Urals heavy-tank combine, and in June 1942 he took charge of the whole tank industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its designers, led by Zhozef Kotin, gave the front the heavy KV and later IS tanks, and the plant also turned out diesel engines by the tens of thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> From August 1942 it also assembled the medium T-34 on a conveyor in the old tractor shops, copying mass-production methods from Nizhny Tagil, and three-quarters of its capacity went over to the new tank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its armoured hulls came largely from Plant 200 in the same city and from Uralmash in Sverdlovsk, so even Tankograd depended on factories in its own region and beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counts of its output differ, one study crediting it with over 19,000 tanks and self-propelled guns and another listing 3,681 KVs, 5,094 T-34s and 3,362 IS tanks between July 1941 and April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Urals as a whole sent 49,700 medium and heavy tanks and self-propelled guns to the front, 70 per cent of all those built in the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Zaltsman's drive for quantity was later blamed for delaying more powerful designs, and after his dismissal in 1949 his name went unmentioned for decades until a legend of the wronged 'tank king' grew up around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Vasily V. Zapariy, “The Formation and Development of the Territorial Production Complex for Heavy Tanks in Chelyabinsk, 1940–1945,” <i>Economic History</i> 13, no. 4 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-i-razvitie-territorialno-proizvodstvennogo-kompleksa-po-vypusku-tyazhelyh-tankov-v-chelyabinske-v-1940-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksey N. Fedorov, “The ‘Tank King’ and the ‘Disgraced General’: The Birth of the Legend of Isaak Zaltsman,” <i>Modern History of Russia</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tankovyy-korol-i-opalnyy-general-rozhdenie-legendy-ob-isaake-zaltsmane. [in Russian] [Open access]","Andrey V. Lobanov, “The Tank-Building Industry of the USSR in 1941–1945,” <i>Military Historical Journal</i>, no. 6 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tankostroitelnaya-promyshlennost-sssr-v-1941-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arseny Yu. Ermolov, “The Evacuation of the Soviet Tank Industry in 1941: A Unique Historical Phenomenon or a Large-Scale Failure?,” <i>RUDN Journal of Russian History</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evakuatsiya-predpriyatiy-sovetskoy-tankovoy-promyshlennosti-v-1941-g-unikalnoe-istoricheskoe-yavlenie-ili-masshtabnaya-neudacha. [in Russian] [Open access]","Andrey N. Speransky, “Tank Production in the Urals in the 20th Century as a Factor in the Development of the Military-Industrial Complex of the USSR,” <i>Bulletin of the South Ural State University. Social Sciences and the Humanities</i> 13, no. 2 (2013), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tankovoe-proizvodstvo-na-urale-v-hh-veke-kak-faktor-razvitiya-voenno-promyshlennogo-kompleksa-sssr. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vladimir V. Zapariy, Vasily V. Zapariy and Zhang Guangxiang, “The Formation of Proto-Clusters in the Tank Industry of the Urals in 1941–1945,” <i>Economy of Region</i> 13, no. 3 (2017), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/formirovanie-protoklasterov-v-tankovoy-promyshlennosti-urala-v-1941-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Kirov works sent to Chelyabinsk rather than Nizhny Tagil?","a":"Because the plan changed when the Kharkov tank factories also had to be evacuated. Nizhny Tagil was first chosen for the Kirov works, but on 12 September 1941 the State Defence Committee sent it to the Chelyabinsk Tractor Plant instead, leaving the Urals wagon works at Nizhny Tagil for Kharkov's Plant 183.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a heavy-tank works switch most of its effort to the T-34?","a":"Because the front needed medium tanks above all, and a decree of 28 July 1942 brought every factory able to assemble the T-34 into its production. The tank was simple enough to build in the former tractor shops on tractor-style conveyors, while the KV stayed in the new tank buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do figures for Tankograd's output disagree?","a":"Because they count different things. One total includes self-propelled guns built on tank chassis and reaches more than 19,000 machines, while another lists only the KV, T-34 and IS tanks themselves, which come to just over 12,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/RIAN_archive_634312_New_military_equipment_arriving_at_Dubosekovo_sidetrack.jpg","title":"New IS-2 heavy tanks arrive by rail, 1945","desc":"Whitewashed IS-2 heavy tanks, a type built at Chelyabinsk, on railway flatcars after delivery to the front in Eastern Europe in the winter of 1945, photographed by Alexander Mokletsov.","credit":"Photograph by Alexander Mokletsov, RIA Novosti archive, image 634312, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_634312_New_military_equipment_arriving_at_Dubosekovo_sidetrack.jpg","alt":"A line of large white-painted tanks with long guns standing on railway flatcars, with soldiers in winter coats beside the track."},"wiki":"Chelyabinsk Tractor Plant"},
    "ww2-318": {"questions":["Kharkov's Plant 183 moved to Nizhny Tagil in the autumn of 1941 to keep the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in production, and its merger with the wagon works there made a giant tank centre.","German anti-tank guns at Rostov in 1941 were literally rolled flat by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose sloped and hardened armour pushed Germany to fit heavier guns to its own tanks."],"abstract":"The <b>T-34</b> was the Soviet medium tank of the Second World War, put into mass production on the eve of the war alongside the heavy KV and armed at first with a 76-millimetre gun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It came from the Kharkov locomotive works, whose tank shop became Plant 183, and before the invasion the Stalingrad Tractor Plant was also ordered to build it, with hulls and turrets from a nearby shipyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its engine was the V-2 diesel, whose long development was formally finished only in 1939 and which powered both the T-34 and the KV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its armour was steel hardened to a high degree and set at a slope, which gave the tank exceptionally strong protection against shells when the war began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1941 German tank guns made little impression on it, and at Rostov one German division had its anti-tank guns literally rolled flat by T-34s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When Kharkov was threatened, Plant 183 moved to the railway-wagon works at Nizhny Tagil in the Urals, taking 5,234 workers with it and creating one of the largest tank-building centres in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To build fast with few skilled welders, the factories turned to automatic welding of the hull, and the T-34's hardened plates tended to crack under it, weakening their resistance to heavy shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Soviet figures claimed over 24,000 tanks built in 1942, 66 per cent of them T-34s, and by mid-1943 the armoured forces were largely equipped with it, the lend-lease models being thought too light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two T-34s sent to the United States in 1942 were examined at the Aberdeen proving ground, where American engineers put the poor air cleaner first among its faults and the test tank finally failed through its engine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Germans answered by giving their new tanks heavier, high-velocity guns able to duel the T-34 and by refitting older tanks with larger cannon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["N. N. Melnikov, “The V-2 Diesel Engine: The Difficult Path to Creating a Soviet Tank Engine,” <i>Technologos</i>, no. 2 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/dizel-motor-v-2-trudnyy-put-sozdaniya-sovetskogo-tankovogo-dvigatelya. [in Russian] [Open access]","Andrey V. Lobanov, “The Tank-Building Industry of the USSR in 1941–1945,” <i>Military Historical Journal</i>, no. 6 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tankostroitelnaya-promyshlennost-sssr-v-1941-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arseny Yu. Ermolov, “The Evacuation of the Soviet Tank Industry in 1941: A Unique Historical Phenomenon or a Large-Scale Failure?,” <i>RUDN Journal of Russian History</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/evakuatsiya-predpriyatiy-sovetskoy-tankovoy-promyshlennosti-v-1941-g-unikalnoe-istoricheskoe-yavlenie-ili-masshtabnaya-neudacha. [in Russian] [Open access]","Vasily V. Zapariy, “The Creation and Development of Technologies for the Automatic Welding of Armour in the USSR, 1939–1945,” <i>History and Modern Perspectives</i>, no. 2 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sozdanie-i-razvitie-tehnologiy-avtomaticheskoy-svarki-broni-v-sssr-1939-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Timothy A. Wray, <i>Standing Fast: German Defensive Doctrine on the Russian Front during World War II, Prewar to March 1943</i>, Research Survey 5 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1986), 27, 54–55, 103, https://archive.org/details/csi-research-survey-no.-5-standing-fast-german-defensive-doctrine-on-the-russian. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 34, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-2.html. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 146, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Defeat/USA-EF-Defeat-8.html. [Open access]","Andrey V. Grebenyuk, “The Red Army on the Eve of the Great Patriotic War,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO University</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/rkka-nakanune-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the T-34 come as such a shock to the Germans in 1941?","a":"Because its sloped armour of high-hardness steel was far stronger than German tank and anti-tank guns had been built to defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1941 Soviet counterattacks that included T-34s or KVs were a serious problem for the panzer units, since both tanks were virtually invulnerable to fire from German tanks, and the panzer divisions came to lean on their attached 88-millimetre anti-aircraft guns, turned against ground targets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the T-34 welded by machine instead of by hand?","a":"Because there were not enough skilled welders for the numbers wanted. Automatic welding raised output sharply, but on the T-34's hard armour plate it caused cracks, and dropping the careful machining of parts made the problem worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviets keep building the T-34 rather than relying on Allied tanks?","a":"Because the Red Army regarded the American and British lend-lease tanks it received as too light. By the summer of 1943 its tank corps and tank armies were largely equipped with the Soviet-designed T-34.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/RIAN_archive_613694_Red_Army_men_are_on_offensive_near_Bryansk.jpg","title":"T-34s and infantry near Bryansk, September 1943","desc":"T-34 tanks of the 10th Guards Tank Brigade advancing with Soviet infantry near Karachev during the Bryansk offensive, photographed by Ivan Shagin on 16 September 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Ivan Shagin, RIA Novosti archive, image 613694, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_613694_Red_Army_men_are_on_offensive_near_Bryansk.jpg","alt":"Two tanks moving across open grassland under a dark clouded sky, with soldiers carrying rifles running alongside them."},"wiki":"T-34"},
    "ww2-319": {"questions":["By the end of 1941 the United States had shipped only 204 of the 600 aircraft promised under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as shortages of shipping and escorts held back deliveries to Russia.","Roosevelt followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with an offer of a billion dollars in interest-free Lend-Lease credit on 30 October 1941, and formal eligibility for the Soviet Union followed in November."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Protocol</b>, also called the First Protocol, was the agreement signed as of 1 October 1941 that fixed what Britain and the United States would supply to the Soviet Union until the end of June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The missions under Averell Harriman and Lord Beaverbrook reached Moscow on 28 September and settled the terms in meetings with Stalin and his officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The memorandum signed by Molotov, Beaverbrook and Harriman promised 400 aircraft and 500 tanks a month, with 5,000 jeeps and a long list of raw materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Harriman reported that the Soviets hoped to import about 500,000 tons a month, and that Archangel, normally frozen from December to June, was a vulnerable port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 30 October Roosevelt offered a billion dollars of interest-free Lend-Lease credit, repayable from 5 years after the war, and Lend-Lease was formally extended to the Soviet Union on 7 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Delivery fell far short at first, and by the end of 1941 the United States had sent 204 aircraft instead of 600 and 182 tanks instead of 750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shipping was the bottleneck, and once the northern convoys were suspended in the summer of 1942, neither the Persian Gulf nor the Pacific route could yet make up the difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Russian historian calculates that the protocol's deliveries were only 9.4 per cent of all wartime aid, yet made up about 14 per cent of the Red Army's tanks and 10 per cent of its aircraft to the end of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Over the whole war 47 per cent of the aid from the Western Hemisphere went by the Pacific route to Siberia, where only cargo classed as non-military could be carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its weight is still argued, for Voznesensky's study of 1948, read in advance by Stalin, put Allied industrial deliveries at only about 4 per cent of Soviet output, while later estimates came out two or three times higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["W. Averell Harriman, “The President’s Special Representative (Harriman) to President Roosevelt, October 29, 1941,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d798. [Open access]","W. Averell Harriman, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Steinhardt) to the Secretary of State, October 3, 1941,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d789. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “President Roosevelt to the Chairman of the Council of People’s Commissars of the Soviet Union (Stalin), October 30, 1941,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, General, The Soviet Union, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v01/d799. [Open access]","Aleksandr Yu. Komarkov, “Lend-Lease for the USSR in the First Year of the Great Patriotic War: Features, Problems, Results,” <i>Society. Environment. Development (Terra Humana)</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lend-liz-dlya-sssr-v-pervyy-god-velikoy-otechestvennoy-osobennosti-problemy-itogi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 557–58, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]","T. H. Vail Motter, <i>The Persian Corridor and Aid to Russia</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Middle East Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1952), 432–33, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub8-1. [Open access]","Sergey M. Monin, “The Second World War: Myths about Lend-Lease,” <i>People and Texts: Historical Almanac</i>, no. 12 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vtoraya-mirovaya-voyna-mify-o-lend-lize. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies fall so far behind on what the protocol promised?","a":"Because production and shipping both lagged behind the promises. The United States entered the war late and expanded its production slowly, so only 204 of 600 aircraft and 182 of 750 tanks had gone by the end of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Arctic convoys were stopped in the summer of 1942, the other routes were not yet ready to take their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt offer credit rather than ask for payment?","a":"Because the Soviet Union could not pay for supplies on the scale agreed in Moscow. Roosevelt proposed up to a billion dollars under the Lend-Lease Act, with no interest and no repayment until 5 years after the war, so that money would not hold up deliveries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do estimates of the importance of Allied aid to the Soviet Union differ so much?","a":"Because they measure different things. Voznesensky's 4 per cent compared only industrial deliveries with Soviet output, and was often repeated as if it were the share of all aid, though Voznesensky himself noted a fivefold rise in raw materials imported from the Allies, and later estimates came out two or three times higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Measured against the Red Army's stock of tanks and aircraft in the first 18 months, the share was larger, about 14 and 10 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Kroger_canned_pork_for_lend-lease_shipment_to_the_USSR.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Kroger_canned_pork_for_lend-lease_shipment_to_the_USSR.tif.jpg","title":"Canned pork for the Soviet Union, 1943","desc":"Tins of stewed pork labelled in Russian, packed in Cincinnati for Lend-Lease shipment to the Soviet Union, photographed in June 1943 for the United States Office of War Information.","credit":"Photograph by Howard R. Hollem, Office of War Information, Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kroger_canned_pork_for_lend-lease_shipment_to_the_USSR.tif","alt":"A row of food tins with Russian labels behind two metal trays holding chunks of cooked meat and a lump of fat."},"wiki":"Moscow Protocol"},
    "ww2-320": {"questions":["Hitler ordered the sea route past Norway interdicted in March 1942, and by that summer losses forced Britain to suspend the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while Stalin waited for promised supplies.","Though film and memoir made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most famous road for Allied aid to Russia, more cargo actually travelled by the Pacific route and the Persian Gulf."],"abstract":"The <b>Arctic convoys</b> were the Allied merchant convoys that carried war supplies round the north of Norway to Murmansk and Archangel from August 1941, the shortest sea route from the West to the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first, codenamed Dervish, reached Archangel late that summer, and from PQ-1, which sailed from Iceland on 29 September, outbound convoys carried PQ numbers and returning ones QP until November 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1941 seven convoys of 54 ships delivered 154,000 tons, 43 per cent of all the cargo sent to the Soviet Union that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From American ports the run was about 7,200 kilometres (4,500 miles), 3 weeks' sailing and 5 weeks in convoy, and in summer the ships passed between the ice edge and Bear Island, in winter south of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British Admiralty ran the escort, and the Soviet Northern Fleet joined it for the last stretch into Murmansk, Archangel or Molotovsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> At first the Germans paid little attention, and by mid-March 1942 twelve PQ convoys had arrived with the loss of one merchant ship out of 103, until Hitler ordered the route cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bombers, U-boats and surface ships based in Norway then made the voyage a gauntlet, and after PQ-17 lost 22 of its 33 ships the British suspended the convoys in July and August 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A September convoy lost 13 of its 40 cargo ships, the October and November sailings were cancelled for the landings in North Africa, and Churchill told Roosevelt he could not reconcile having no more PQs until 1943 with conscience or interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill had already admitted to the Commons that shipping had suffered heavy losses in operations such as the Russian and Malta convoys, and of 1,822 British aircraft sent under the First Protocol, 288 went down at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over the whole war the northern route carried 3,964,231 long tons from the Western Hemisphere, about 22.6 per cent, behind the Pacific route and the Persian Gulf, so the best-known road to Russia was not the busiest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 233–37, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Decision/USA-EF-Decision-11.html. [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 557–58, 583–84, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]","T. H. Vail Motter, <i>The Persian Corridor and Aid to Russia</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Middle East Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1952), 432–33, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub8-1. [Open access]","M. N. Suprun, “The Significance of the Northern Convoys in the Organisation of Military-Economic Cooperation between the USSR and the Allies, 1941–1945,” <i>Administrative Consulting</i>, no. 2 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/znachenie-severnyh-konvoev-v-organizatsii-voenno-ekonomicheskogo-sotrudnichestva-sssr-i-soyuznikov-v-1941-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandr Yu. Komarkov, “Lend-Lease for the USSR in the First Year of the Great Patriotic War: Features, Problems, Results,” <i>Society. Environment. Development (Terra Humana)</i>, no. 1 (2012), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lend-liz-dlya-sssr-v-pervyy-god-velikoy-otechestvennoy-osobennosti-problemy-itogi. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey M. Monin, “The Second World War: Myths about Lend-Lease,” <i>People and Texts: Historical Almanac</i>, no. 12 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vtoraya-mirovaya-voyna-mify-o-lend-lize. [in Russian] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 September 1942, vol. 383, cols. 82–110, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/sep/08/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British suspend the convoys in the summer of 1942?","a":"Because the losses had become more than the escorts could prevent. Hitler's order of March 1942 brought heavy ships, U-boats and torpedo-bombers to Norway, and in the long days of the Arctic summer the convoys were within reach of German air bases for much of the voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When PQ-17 lost 22 of its 33 ships, the convoys were stopped for July and August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the route so valuable despite the danger?","a":"Because it was the shortest way from the West to the Soviet Union, and the rail haul from the northern ports to the front and the industrial centres was short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first months of the war it carried a larger share of aid than any other route, 43 per cent of the cargo sent in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Arctic route often thought to have been the main one?","a":"Because novels, films and the memoirs of the men who sailed in the convoys made it the best known to the public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In tonnage it came third: the Pacific route to Siberian ports carried nearly half of all aid from the Western Hemisphere and the Persian Gulf slightly more than the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/The_Royal_Navy_during_the_Second_World_War_A12271.jpg","title":"An ammunition ship explodes in a Russian convoy","desc":"A Royal Navy official photograph, taken from the deck of an aircraft carrier, of an ammunition ship blowing up among the merchant ships of a convoy bound for Russia.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, Imperial War Museums A 12271, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Royal_Navy_during_the_Second_World_War_A12271.jpg","alt":"A huge white cloud of smoke rising from the sea among a long line of distant ships, with a dark shadow in the foreground."},"wiki":"Arctic convoys of World War II"},
    "ww2-321": {"questions":["Marina Raskova's aviation group trained three women's regiments in 1941, but only the night bombers later known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed all-female under a single commander throughout the war.","Given the Guards title in February 1943 for its fighting over Stalingrad and the Kuban, the regiment known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flew about 24,000 sorties by Soviet count before reaching Berlin."],"abstract":"The <b>Night Witches</b> were the women of the 588th Night Bomber Aviation Regiment, later the 46th Guards, the best known of the women's air units the Soviet Union sent into combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1941 the record-breaking pilot Marina Raskova was given command of an all-female aviation group, number 122, which trained the pilots, navigators, mechanics and ground crews of three regiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The night bombers were the only one of the three to remain all-female throughout the war, and they kept a single commander, Yevdokiya Bershanskaya, a pre-war instructor in civil aviation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The name was a German nickname rather than a Russian one, and the regiment's own title became the 46th Guards Taman Night Light Bomber Regiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were part of a far wider mobilisation, for a Soviet historian writing in 1985 counted over 100,000 young women who joined the air defence forces under Komsomol sponsorship alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The regiment flew the U-2, a wooden open-cockpit biplane in production since 1927 and renamed the Po-2 in 1944, which could carry up to 300 kilograms (660 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Formed in February 1942, it reached the front by 27 May as part of the 218th Air Division, began its campaign in the North Caucasus and ended it at Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crews flew five to ten sorties a night, often gliding onto their targets with the engine cut, without parachutes and, for most of the war, with no weapons but their bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fighting over Stalingrad and the Kuban won it the Guards title in February 1943, and on the night of 23 June 1944 it flew on the 2nd Belorussian Front at the opening of the summer offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By Soviet count the regiment flew about 24,000 sorties, and in March 1945 the <i>Illustrated London News</i> showed its pilots to British readers as fully combatant members of the Red Air Force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Kristal L. M. Alfonso, <i>Femme Fatale: An Examination of the Role of Women in Combat and the Policy Implications for Future American Military Operations</i>, Drew Paper 5 (Maxwell Air Force Base, AL: Air University Press, 2009), 29–32, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA550428. [Open access]","A. G. Pervov and V. N. Guskov, “Night Operations of Frontal Aviation,” <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 6 (1988): 28–33, trans. in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, no. 6 (June 1988), 18–21, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA346422. [Open access]","Beth A. Myers, “Soviet and American Airwomen during World War II: A Comparison of Their Formation, Treatment and Dismissal” (MA thesis, University of Utah, 2003), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA416572. [Open access]","V. Murmantseva, “The Soviet Woman and Her Contribution to the War Effort in Military Service and Civilian Labor,” <i>Kommunist vooruzhennykh sil</i>, no. 5 (1985): 20–24, trans. in <i>JPRS Report: USSR Military Affairs</i>, JPRS-UMA-85-044 (30 July 1985), 16–18, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA354521. [Open access]","M. N. Kozhevnikov, <i>The Command and Staff of the Soviet Army Air Force in the Great Patriotic War, 1941–1945: A Soviet View</i> (Moscow: Nauka, 1977; trans. and published under the auspices of the United States Air Force), 157, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA130405. [Open access]","“Fully Combatant Members of the Red Air Force: Russian Women Pilots,” <i>Illustrated London News</i> 206, no. 5526 (17 March 1945): 297–98, https://archive.org/details/sim_illustrated-london-news_1945-03-17_206_5526. [Open access]","Ella Winter, “Congress of Heroines,” <i>Soviet Russia Today</i> 13, no. 6 (October 1944): 9, 32, https://archive.org/details/sim_update-ussr_1944-10_13_6. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regiment fly an obsolete biplane rather than modern bombers?","a":"Because the U-2 was the standard machine of Soviet night light bomber aviation: it was simple to operate, cheap to build and could carry up to 300 kilograms, and dozens of night bomber regiments were formed with it in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its pilots wanted faster aircraft, and one view is that the regiment kept the outdated type partly because it was all-female and its crews were seen as little girls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the night raids matter more than the weight of bombs they dropped?","a":"Because their effect on the enemy's rest was greater than the damage they did. Crews flew five to ten sorties a night close to the front, and German accounts admitted that the unpredictable raids cut the troops' short rest and hampered supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How could the regiment stay all-female when it lost crews?","a":"It trained its own replacements. The U-2 had dual controls, so navigators learned to fly on the way home from missions, mechanics trained as navigators and armourers as mechanics, and new volunteers entered as armourers and worked their way up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/%D0%93%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BA_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BA-%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2_46-%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%9D%D0%91%D0%90%D0%9F_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%A3-2.jpg/1280px-%D0%93%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BA_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BA-%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2_46-%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%9D%D0%91%D0%90%D0%9F_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%A3-2.jpg","title":"Pilots of the 46th Guards regiment, 1943","desc":"Women of the 46th Guards Night Bomber Regiment grouped in front of one of their U-2 biplanes, photographed for TASS at Gelendzhik on 15 September 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Yevgeny Khaldei, TASS, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%93%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BA_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B5%D0%BA-%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2_46-%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B3%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%9D%D0%91%D0%90%D0%9F_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4_%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%A3-2.jpg","alt":"About twenty young women in flying helmets and heavy coats standing close together on bare ground in front of a biplane."},"wiki":"Night Witches"},
    "ww2-322": {"questions":["A statement from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> published on 16 April 1943 denied the German accusation over the Katyn graves and claimed the Polish officers had fallen into German hands.","On the second anniversary of the invasion the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claimed 6,400,000 Germans and their allies killed or captured against 4,200,000 Soviet dead and missing, and blamed the missing second front."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet Information Bureau</b>, known as the Sovinformburo, was the agency that spoke for the Soviet government about the war, issuing the official communiqués on the fighting and briefing foreign correspondents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Solomon Lozovsky announced it to the foreign press on 28 June 1941, with the Politburo member Alexander Shcherbakov as director and himself as assistant, and promised communiqués twice a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Correspondents cabled the noon and midnight communiqués abroad as they came, and one American reporter judged them accurate on the general position though slow in admitting losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lozovsky's press conferences mixed denials of German claims with jokes at the expense of Goebbels, and another correspondent found his information growing less reliable as the Germans advanced in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its figures were weapons too: on the second anniversary of the invasion it put German and allied losses at 6,400,000 killed or captured and Soviet losses at 4,200,000 killed and missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> By 1943 the bureau also voiced Moscow's quarrels with its allies, and the same anniversary statement blamed German successes partly on the absence of a second front in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A special communiqué of 7 August 1943 claimed that Red Army victories had chained the main German forces to the east and made conditions still more favourable for an Allied landing on the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When German radio announced the Katyn graves in April 1943, a Sovinformburo statement published on 16 April denied the charge and said the Polish prisoners had fallen into German hands near Smolensk in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 3 days later <i>Pravda</i> accused circles in the Polish government in London of collaborating with Hitler, and the Tass agency said the editorial reflected the view of leading Soviet circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bureau's denial was false: the officers had been shot by the Soviet security police in 1940, as the Soviet government admitted under Gorbachev after decades of denial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Henry C. Cassidy, <i>Moscow Dateline, 1941–1943</i> (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1943), 58–60, https://archive.org/details/MoscowDateline-nsia. [Open access]","Wallace Carroll, <i>We're in This with Russia</i> (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1942), 87, https://archive.org/details/WereInThisWithRussia-nsia. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Second Secretary of Embassy in the Soviet Union (Page) to the Secretary of State, April 19, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d282. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Standley) to the Secretary of State, June 24, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d414. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Standley) to the Secretary of State, August 10, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d429. [Open access]","James von Geldern, “Katyn Forest Massacre,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/katyn-forest-massacre/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did foreign correspondents rely on the bureau's communiqués?","a":"Because they had little else. The Soviet papers often left out the names of places and the dates of actions, and the twice-daily communiqué was the one official account of the fighting; one reporter thought it accurate on the general position, though it never told everything and was slow to admit losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bureau keep raising the second front in 1943?","a":"Because Moscow used its military statements to press its allies. Its review of June 1943 blamed German successes partly on the missing second front of 1942, and its August communiqué argued that Soviet victories had made an Allied landing easier than ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Katyn denial matter beyond the Soviet Union?","a":"Because it set the line that Pravda and Tass followed within days, turning a German accusation into a charge that the Polish government in London was collaborating with Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The denial was false, and the Soviet state kept it up until it admitted the crime under Gorbachev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/%D0%90%D0%B3%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80-%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%91%D1%82_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B1%D1%8E%D1%80%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BC_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B5.jpg","title":"Reading the communiqué to the enemy, Stalingrad","desc":"A Soviet agitator in the ruins of Stalingrad reads the latest Sovinformburo report through a megaphone, in German, to enemy soldiers holding a building, December 1942.","credit":"Photograph by Leonid Leonidov, TASS, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%90%D0%B3%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%80-%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%91%D1%82_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B1%D1%8E%D1%80%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BC_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%B2_%D0%A1%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B5.jpg","alt":"A man in a padded jacket and fur hat shouting through a large cone-shaped megaphone towards a ruined brick building in the snow."},"wiki":"Soviet Information Bureau"},
    "ww2-323": {"questions":["Tactics makes the steps from which operational leaps are assembled and strategy points out the path, wrote Svechin of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the purged Red Army had to relearn in battle.","The Stalingrad counter-offensive of November 1942, one link in a chain of planned Stavka offensives, gave the Red Army its first large success in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its purged theorists had developed."],"abstract":"<b>Operational art</b> is the level of military thought between strategy and tactics, governing how a series of battles is prepared and fought as one operation towards a goal that strategy has set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet theorists began to fill this gap in the early 1920s, and in lectures of 1923 and 1924 the former imperial staff officer Aleksandr Svechin named it and gave it the definition that went into his book <i>Strategy</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Svechin put the relationship in an image: tactics makes up the steps from which operational leaps are assembled, and strategy points out the path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea grew out of the ferment of pre-1914 Russian military thought in which Svechin had matured, and some suspect he borrowed the concept from an older officer, A. V. Gerua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It long outlived its Soviet origins, and the United States Army first defined operational art in its 1986 field manual as the use of forces to reach strategic goals through campaigns and major operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The men who built the theory did not see it tested, for the purges that began in 1937 liquidated most of the Red Army's theoreticians and senior commanders, and Svechin himself died in 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Red Army relearned the art in combat, and the counter-offensive at Stalingrad on 19 November 1942, which opened what Soviet historians call the second period of the war, was its first successful large-scale operational manoeuvre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Stavka planned it as one link in a chain, with Uranus at Stalingrad to be followed by Saturn towards Rostov, while Mars and Jupiter were to destroy the German forces east of Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The execution was uneven: Mars failed at a cost put at over 300,000 casualties, and armour losses in the tank and mechanised corps ran above half, chiefly from logistical and mechanical causes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lessons were absorbed during 1943, and the rules and principles that had emerged by 1942 were adjusted that year and perfected in the great offensives of 1944 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce W. Menning, “Operational Art's Origins,” in <i>Historical Perspectives of the Operational Art</i>, ed. Michael D. Krause and R. Cody Phillips (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 2005), 8, 15, https://archive.org/details/historicalperspe00mich. [Open access]","Bruce W. Menning, “The Imperial Russian Legacy of Operational Art, 1878–1914,” in <i>Historical Perspectives of the Operational Art</i>, ed. Michael D. Krause and R. Cody Phillips (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 2005), 208–9, https://archive.org/details/historicalperspe00mich. [Open access]","Jacob W. Kipp, <i>Mass, Mobility, and the Red Army's Road to Operational Art, 1918–1936</i> (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Soviet Army Studies Office, 1987), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA195053. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, “Soviet Operational Art since 1936: The Triumph of Maneuver War,” in <i>Historical Perspectives of the Operational Art</i>, ed. Michael D. Krause and R. Cody Phillips (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 2005), 247–53, https://archive.org/details/historicalperspe00mich. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Great Patriotic War and the Maturation of Soviet Operational Art, 1941–1945</i> (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Soviet Army Studies Office, 1987), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA194152. [Open access]","James R. Howard, “The Roots of Soviet Victory: The Application of Operational Art on the Eastern Front, 1942–1943” (MMAS thesis, US Army Command and General Staff College, 2003), 31–32, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA416926. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Soviet thinkers need a level between strategy and tactics?","a":"Because the wars of 1914 to 1920 had shown that single battles rarely decided anything and that decision came from whole chains of related operations, shaped by railways, roads and supply. Older terms such as grand tactics were used without rigour, so Svechin proposed a separate category with its own discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Red Army have to relearn operational art during the war?","a":"Because the purges from 1937 removed most of the theorists and senior commanders who understood it, so their ideas fell into disuse or disrepute just before the war, and the army had to recover them through costly trial and error in 1941 and 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Stalingrad treated as a turning point in Soviet operational art?","a":"Because it was the first large Soviet operation in which mobile corps broke through, drove deep and closed a ring around a whole German army, and it was planned as part of a chain of offensives. Its flaws in command and supply then shaped the reforms of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Svechin_AA_1923.jpg","title":"Aleksandr Svechin","desc":"Aleksandr Svechin, the former imperial staff officer who named and defined operational art in his lectures of 1923 and 1924, in a portrait taken before 1923.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svechin_AA_1923.jpg","alt":"A balding man in a dark double-breasted tunic seated at a desk, looking to one side, one hand resting on the desk."},"wiki":"Operational level of war"},
    "ww2-324": {"questions":["Supervised in person by Lavrenti Beria from Grozny, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> removed 478,479 Chechens and Ingush in its first week, according to his own cable to Stalin.","Soviet officials recorded that 14.6 to 23.7 per cent of the people expelled in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died between 1944 and 1948, figures that leave out deaths on the journey itself."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Lentil</b> was the code name for the deportation of the Chechen and Ingush peoples from their homeland in the North Caucasus to Kazakhstan and Central Asia, begun in February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was one of a series in which the NKVD, the Soviet security police, deported about a million people of the North Caucasus and Crimea in 1943 and 1944, whole peoples charged with treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moscow accused the Chechens and Ingush of mass collaboration, although the Germans had held only the north-west of their republic, for about 12 weeks, and many Chechen men served in the Red Army or with the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lavrenti Beria, head of the NKVD, came to Grozny on 17 February to direct it, and reported that 19,000 special troops and up to 100,000 more NKVD officers and men took part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> 12 years later Khrushchev told a party congress that no person of common sense could grasp how whole nations, women and children included, could be made responsible for hostile acts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 23 February 1944 the men of each village were called to gatherings for Red Army Day, surrounded by soldiers with automatic weapons and read the expulsion decree, while their families were rounded up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Families had about half an hour to gather belongings before trucks took them to the railway and cattle cars carried them east on journeys of 20 to 30 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beria cabled Stalin that 478,479 Chechens and Ingush had been deported in the first week, but the documents disagree, and one study gives 350,000 to 400,000 Chechens and 91,250 Ingush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The toll is disputed: Soviet officials recorded that 14.6 to 23.7 per cent of the deportees died in 1944–1948, a demographic study puts deaths near 30 per cent by 1952, and Chechen historians speak of half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those judged unfit to travel were shot, about 700 people of the mountain village of Khaibakh are said to have been burned alive in a stable, and the exiles were not allowed home until 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aurélie Campana, “The Massive Deportation of the Chechen People: How and Why Chechens Were Deported,” <i>Mass Violence and Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 5 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-massive-deportation-of-the-chechen-people-how-and-why-chechens-were-deported/. [Open access]","Aurélie Campana, “The Soviet Massive Deportations: A Chronology,” <i>Mass Violence and Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 5 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-soviet-massive-deportations-a-chronology/. [Open access]","Ariel Cohen, <i>Russia's Counterinsurgency in North Caucasus: Performance and Consequences</i> (Carlisle, PA: Strategic Studies Institute, US Army War College, 2014), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA597804. [Open access]","James von Geldern, “Mass Deportation of National Minorities,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/deportation-of-minorities/. [Open access]","Lavrentii Beria, “To the State Defense Committee, March 7, 1944,” trans. James von Geldern, <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/deportation-of-minorities/deportation-of-minorities-texts/beria-reports-to-the-defense-committee/. [Open access]","Nikita Khrushchev, “On the Cult of Personality and Its Consequences,” speech to the Twentieth Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union, 24–25 February 1956, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/archive/khrushchev/1956/02/24.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime claim the Chechens and Ingush had to be deported?","a":"It accused them, like the other deported peoples, of mass collaboration with the German invaders. The charge was a pretext: the Germans had held only a corner of the republic for a few weeks, and official documents of 1943 and 1944 praised the local part in resisting them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the numbers of deportees differ so much?","a":"Because the official documents themselves disagree on how many were moved and on how many Chechens there were. Beria reported 478,479 in the first week, while later cables give other totals for all the peoples deported from the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are deaths in exile so hard to count?","a":"Because the deportees were left out of every census for 13 years, and the NKVD's own reports cover only the special settlements, not the expulsion and the journey. Estimates therefore range from the officials' 14.6 to 23.7 per cent to the half claimed by Chechen historians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Operation_Lentil_%28Caucasus%29.svg/1280px-Operation_Lentil_%28Caucasus%29.svg.png","title":"The route of the deportation","desc":"A map of the deportation of February 1944, with arrows running from the Chechen and Ingush homeland in the North Caucasus to Kazakhstan and Central Asia.","credit":"Map by Takhirgeran Umar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Operation_Lentil_%28Caucasus%29.svg","alt":"A map of the Soviet Union shaded red, with white arrows curving east from the Caucasus into Kazakhstan and Central Asia."},"wiki":"Operation Lentil (Caucasus)"},
    "ww2-325": {"questions":["Molotov assured the British ambassador in May 1943 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bore no resemblance to the French or Czech national committees and was purely an internal Soviet matter.","A Polish division raised in the Soviet Union under the aegis of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left for the front in the late summer of 1943, over the protests of the government in London."],"abstract":"The <b>Union of Polish Patriots</b> was a body of Poles in the Soviet Union, chaired by Wanda Wasilewska, which Moscow promoted in 1943 as a rival to the Polish government in exile in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Wasilewska edited its paper <i>Wolna Polska</i>, ‘Free Poland’, and was reputed to be the wife of Korneichuk, newly appointed as a Soviet deputy commissar for foreign affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind it lay the frontier, for the Treaty of Riga of 1921 had recognised Polish rule over borderlands that the Soviet Union annexed in 1939 and meant to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 16 January 1943 Moscow declared that everyone living in those districts in November 1939 was a Soviet citizen, cancelling its earlier readiness to treat ethnic Poles from there as Polish citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Sikorski told Roosevelt that the Soviet aim was a communist Polish government that would offer Poland to Moscow as a satellite, and dismissed the so-called patriots as unknown in Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The union came into the open as relations broke, when on 28 April 1943 <i>Izvestiya</i> printed Wasilewska's article declaring that the Polish patriots were against the government of General Sikorski.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It accused the London government of being controlled by Hitlerite elements and asked for Polish units to be formed on Soviet soil to fight shoulder to shoulder with the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Questioned by the British ambassador in May, Molotov denied that the union resembled the Czech or French national committees and insisted that its work was a purely internal Soviet matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In late August Moscow announced that the first division formed under the union's aegis was going to the front, and the London government protested against Polish formations raised without its knowledge or consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By October the All-Slav Committee in Moscow was telling the First Polish Corps of General Berling that it fell to them to lay the foundation of a new Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “The Ambassador to the Polish Government in Exile (Biddle) to the Secretary of State, January 28, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d238. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Standley) to the Secretary of State, April 28, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d297. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Polish Prime Minister (Sikorski) to President Roosevelt, May 4, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d306. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Standley) to the Secretary of State, May 23, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d319. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Polish Ambassador (Ciechanowski) to the Secretary of State,” 1943, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d344. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Hamilton) to the Secretary of State, October 22, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d452. [Open access]","Piotr H. Kosicki, “The Katyń Massacres of 1940,” <i>Mass Violence and Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 8 September 2008, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-katyn-massacres-of-1940/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow insist the union was only an internal matter?","a":"Because recognising it as a rival authority would have openly challenged a government its allies still recognised. Molotov therefore denied any likeness to the French or Czech committees, while treating the Poles of the annexed east as Soviet citizens whom the new Polish units could recruit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the London government object to the Polish division?","a":"Because it considered itself the only authority entitled to decide on the lives of Polish citizens, and saw units raised without its consent as an infringement of Polish sovereignty and of the Polish-Soviet agreements of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was citizenship so bitter an issue?","a":"Because it decided the frontier in advance. By declaring everyone in the annexed districts a Soviet citizen, Moscow treated land that Poland claimed under the Treaty of Riga as already Soviet, and Sikorski saw the decree as part of a campaign against Poland's independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Polish_1st_Infantry_Division_%281943%29.jpg","title":"Polish soldiers leave for the front, 1943","desc":"Soldiers of the Polish division formed in the Soviet Union in 1943 crowd a railway wagon as they leave for the front that summer.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_1st_Infantry_Division_%281943%29.jpg","alt":"Smiling soldiers in helmets crowded into the open doorway of a wooden railway wagon, some of them waving."},"wiki":"Union of Polish Patriots"},
    "ww2-326": {"questions":["Stalin dismissed the German inquiry as a farce staged under Hitler's management, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had concluded that the officers in the Katyn Forest were killed in the spring of 1940.","Two signatories of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Markov and Hájek, later distanced themselves from its findings behind the iron curtain, while five others told an American committee in 1952 that they had signed freely."],"abstract":"The <b>Katyn Commission</b> was the international panel of forensic experts that Germany assembled in April 1943 to examine the mass graves of Polish officers its troops had found in the Katyn Forest near Smolensk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German radio announced the find on 13 April, and a news-service release next day said that Polish officers shot by the Soviet security police in spring 1940 had been exhumed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The panel drew leading doctors from 12 European countries, neutral Switzerland among them, and they examined the graves on 29 and 30 April, each free to perform his own autopsies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 30 April all twelve signed a protocol concluding that the men had been killed in the spring of 1940, when the area was under Soviet control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American diplomat in Bern judged that Germany had known of the bodies for months and had timed its campaign to divide the Allies and weaken Western support for the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Polish government in London had already asked the International Red Cross to investigate, setting out how some 8,300 officers from three Soviet camps had vanished in the spring of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stalin called the German inquiry an investigation farce staged under Hitler's management, and used the Polish appeal as his reason for breaking off relations with the London government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In January 1944 a Soviet commission led by Nikolai Burdenko reported that the victims had died in late 1941 under German occupation, and Soviet prosecutors brought its findings to Nuremberg, where the charge was dropped for lack of evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two members of the 1943 panel, Dr Markov and Professor František Hájek, later distanced themselves from its protocol under Soviet rule, while five others told a committee of the American Congress in 1952 that every member had signed freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The counts still differ: the Germans reported 4,123 bodies at Katyn and Polish figures 4,243, while Soviet records released after Moscow admitted the crime give 4,421 or 4,443 shot there, out of about 21,857 Poles killed in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Congress, House of Representatives, Select Committee on the Katyn Forest Massacre, <i>The Katyn Forest Massacre: Final Report</i>, 82nd Cong., 2nd sess., H. Rept. 2505 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1952), 1–2, 31–33, https://archive.org/details/KatynForestMassacreFinalReport. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Minister in Switzerland (Harrison) to the Secretary of State, April 15, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d277. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Communiqué of the Minister of National Defense of the Polish Government in Exile, at London, April 17, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d278. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Minister in Switzerland (Harrison) to the Secretary of State, April 19, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d283. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Secretary of State to President Roosevelt, April 25, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d290. [Open access]","Piotr H. Kosicki, “The Katyń Massacres of 1940,” <i>Mass Violence and Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 8 September 2008, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-katyn-massacres-of-1940/. [Open access]","James von Geldern, “Katyn Forest Massacre,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/katyn-forest-massacre/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany invite foreign experts to Katyn?","a":"Because a finding by outsiders would carry more weight than German propaganda alone. An American diplomat in Bern saw the campaign as aimed at setting the London Poles against Moscow and at weakening Western support for the Soviet Union just before the spring fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the London Poles ask for the Red Cross rather than accept the German findings?","a":"Because they knew German propaganda lied, yet thousands of their officers had vanished from Soviet camps in 1940 and repeated questions to Stalin and his ministers had brought no answer, so they asked for a proper international body to verify the facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the commission's verdict stay contested for decades?","a":"Because Moscow answered it with the Burdenko report blaming the Germans, kept that version at Nuremberg, and denied the crime until it admitted it under Gorbachev; in the Cold War the question became a quarrel between the blocs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/Witnesses_of_the_massacre%2C_Parfemon_Kiselev_a_local_resident_of_Katyn_area%2C_listening_to_Dr_Ferenc_Ors%C3%B3s.jpg","title":"A witness questioned at Katyn, 30 April 1943","desc":"Parfien Kisielew, a local resident who gave evidence about the killings, with Dr Ferenc Orsós, a member of the commission, at Katyn on 30 April 1943.","credit":"Photograph by a German official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Witnesses_of_the_massacre%2C_Parfemon_Kiselev_a_local_resident_of_Katyn_area%2C_listening_to_Dr_Ferenc_Ors%C3%B3s.jpg","alt":"An old bearded man in a flat cap speaking while a man in a hat writes notes, with men in hats and coats crowded round."},"wiki":"Katyn Commission"},
    "ww2-327": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in July 1943 that the agreement <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had made with Stalin on 30 July 1941 was an outstanding example of his political wisdom.","Killed with his daughter when his aircraft crashed at Gibraltar in July 1943, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had led the Polish government in exile as prime minister and commander-in-chief since 1939."],"abstract":"<b>Władysław Sikorski</b> was the Polish general who led his country's government in exile as prime minister and commander-in-chief from the catastrophe of 1939 until his death in July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Poland's defeat he raised a Polish army of over 80,000 men in France, and when France fell he began again in Britain, building forces there and in the Middle East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His agreement with Stalin of 30 July 1941, followed by a military agreement in August, allowed a Polish army to be formed in the Soviet Union, and Churchill called it an outstanding example of his political wisdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Moscow on 3 December 1941 he handed Stalin a list of 3,845 missing Polish officers, and Stalin assured him that all Polish officers had been freed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1943 he was in Washington, pressing for credits to fly parachutists into occupied Poland and pleading the case for Poland's frontiers and future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When the Germans announced the Katyn graves in April 1943, his government asked the International Red Cross to investigate, and Stalin answered by breaking relations with what he called the Sikorski government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sikorski told the British that his government had acted without knowing what line the Germans would take, and under British pressure he agreed not to press the Red Cross request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill warned Stalin that Sikorski was in danger of being overthrown by Poles who thought he had not stood up for them against the Soviets, and that his successor would be worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Roosevelt told Stalin that Sikorski had in no way acted with Hitler but had made a mistake in taking the matter to the Red Cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sikorski called the break the climax of a long Soviet campaign against Poland, and 2 months later he and his daughter were killed when his aircraft crashed at Gibraltar on the night of 4 July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “General Sikorski,” <i>Hansard</i>, 6 July 1943, vol. 390, cols. 1946–50, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/jul/06/general-sikorski. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Communiqué of the Minister of National Defense of the Polish Government in Exile, at London, April 17, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d278. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), January 4, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d234. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Secretary of State to President Roosevelt, April 25, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d290. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The British Prime Minister (Churchill) to President Roosevelt, April 25, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d291. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “President Roosevelt to the Chairman of the Council of People's Commissars of the Soviet Union (Stalin), April 26, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d293. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Polish Prime Minister (Sikorski) to President Roosevelt, May 4, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d306. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sikorski's government appeal to the Red Cross over Katyn?","a":"Because many Poles in Britain and the Middle East had relatives or comrades among the missing officers, and the Soviet government had never answered repeated questions about them, so it could not ignore the German news and wanted an impartial body to check it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill and Roosevelt try to soften Stalin's break with Sikorski?","a":"Because a public rupture threatened the alliance. Churchill feared Sikorski would fall and be replaced by someone worse, and Roosevelt pointed to the millions of Poles in the United States, many in the armed forces, who would take a complete break badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Sikorski see the break as more than a reaction to Katyn?","a":"Because Moscow had been stripping deported Poles of their citizenship since 1941, had sponsored a committee of Polish patriots and a Polish communist detachment, and had demanded the 1939 partition line as the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Sikorski_in_Gibraltar.jpg","title":"Sikorski at Gibraltar, July 1943","desc":"General Sikorski, pointing, with British and Polish officers on his last visit to Gibraltar, shortly before the crash of 4 July 1943.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sikorski_in_Gibraltar.jpg","alt":"Officers in peaked caps standing on a stone parapet above the sea, one in the centre pointing out across the water."},"wiki":"Władysław Sikorski"},
    "ww2-328": {"questions":["Tying up some 250,000 tons of shipping and every landing craft in Britain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was an operation the British Chiefs of Staff said they could not recommend if they were free agents.","The plan Marshall took to London in April 1942 paired a full invasion called Roundup for 1943 with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a contingent cross-Channel landing in case of emergency that year."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Sledgehammer</b> was the Allied plan for an emergency landing in northern France in 1942, a contingent operation to be mounted if a crisis on the Eastern Front or a German weakening in the west demanded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It formed part of the plan General Marshall took to London in April 1942, in which a build-up of American forces in Britain called Bolero was to lead to a full invasion, Roundup, in the spring of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sledgehammer and Roundup were British code names, and the idea outlived 1942, for at Casablanca Churchill still thought of any cross-Channel move in 1943 in terms of a Sledgehammer operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Washington on 30 May Molotov called the second front a predominantly political problem, and Roosevelt authorised him to tell Stalin that the United States expected a second front that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The communiqué of 11 June said that full understanding had been reached on the urgent tasks of creating a second front in Europe in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The British War Cabinet had resolved the same day that there should be no major landing on the continent that year unless the Allies meant to stay, and that Sledgehammer would go ahead only with a good prospect of success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mounting it would tie up some 250,000 tons of shipping and every landing craft in Britain, and the British Chiefs of Staff concluded that as free agents they could not recommend it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 8 July London told Washington that it would not mount Sledgehammer and hoped the Americans would agree to invade North Africa instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moscow treated the promise as settled, and in late June Molotov warned the British and American ambassadors of the cruel disillusionment that would follow any failure to redeem Anglo-Saxon promises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Churchill told Stalin in August that a second front in Europe in 1942 was impossible, Stalin answered in writing that the refusal dealt a moral blow to Soviet public opinion and prejudiced the plans of the Soviet command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Maurice Matloff and Edwin M. Snell, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1941–1942</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 184–91, 266–68, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1941-1942. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 23, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1943-1944. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of Conference Held at the White House, by Mr. Samuel H. Cross, Interpreter, May 30, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d471. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Press Release Issued by the White House, June 11, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d483. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Standley) to the Secretary of State, June 22, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d489. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant) to the Secretary of State, July 10, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d501. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The President of the Council of People's Commissars of the Soviet Union (Stalin) to Prime Minister Churchill and Mr. Averell Harriman, August 13, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d516. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British refuse to launch Sledgehammer in 1942?","a":"Because the odds against a successful landing that year were heavy and the cost of preparing it was great: it would have absorbed shipping and every landing craft, halted amphibious training and slowed preparations for 1943, all for an operation the British did not mean to launch unless they could stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Moscow believe a second front had been promised?","a":"Because Roosevelt let Molotov tell Stalin that a second front was expected that year, and the public communiqué spoke of full understanding on creating one in 1942. Soviet officials and newspapers then treated it as a solemn obligation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin think 1942 was the best year for a second front?","a":"He argued that almost all the German army, and its best troops, were in the east, leaving only small and inferior forces in western Europe, and that nobody knew whether conditions in 1943 would be as favourable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Franklin_D._Roosevelt%2C_Vyacheslav_Molotov.jpg","title":"Roosevelt and Molotov, 1 June 1942","desc":"Roosevelt and the Soviet foreign minister Molotov in the White House study on 1 June 1942, during the visit in which a second front in 1942 was discussed.","credit":"United States National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franklin_D._Roosevelt%2C_Vyacheslav_Molotov.jpg","alt":"A man in a white suit seated at a desk, turned towards a man in a dark suit and glasses who sits beside a large wall map."},"wiki":"Operation Sledgehammer"},
    "ww2-329": {"questions":["The Four-Nation Declaration signed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of October 1943 recognised the need for a general international organisation open to all peace-loving states, large and small.","Austria's annexation was declared null and void and a European Advisory Commission was set up in London by the foreign ministers who met at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the autumn of 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Moscow Conference</b> of October 1943 was the meeting of the American, British and Soviet foreign ministers, Cordell Hull, Anthony Eden and Vyacheslav Molotov, held in the Soviet capital from 19 to 30 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was meant to be exploratory, preparing the way for a meeting of the three heads of government, and it was the first time in the war that British and American staff officers discussed strategic plans face to face with Soviet military representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ministers met twelve times in full session, with Molotov in the chair after Hull and Eden persuaded him to preside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their Four-Nation Declaration, signed for China as well after American insistence, pledged that united action would continue after the war and recognised the need for a general international organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also declared Austria's annexation of 1938 null and void, set out democratic principles for Italy, and warned that Germans guilty of atrocities would be sent back to be judged where their crimes were done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Soviet delegation put forward a single item of its own, measures to shorten the war against Germany, and would discuss nothing else until it had been explored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Molotov asked in writing whether the statement Roosevelt and Churchill had made in June, that Anglo-American forces would invade northern France in the spring of 1944, remained in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Generals Deane and Ismay reaffirmed the spring landing but would not give the exact date that Marshal Voroshilov pressed for, and the Western ministers turned down Soviet proposals to push Turkey into the war and to seek Swedish air bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conference set up a European Advisory Commission in London to make joint recommendations on questions the three governments referred to it, and an Advisory Council for Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eden told the Commons that its results had exceeded his hopes, and Roosevelt called it a tremendous success, as much for its spirit as for its agreements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 291–301, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1943-1944. [Open access]","“The Moscow Conference, October 1943,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Protocol, Signed at Moscow, November 1, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d684. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Protocol, Signed at Moscow, November 1, 1943 (Most Secret Protocol),” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d685. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Moscow Conference,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 November 1943, vol. 393, cols. 1323–54, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/nov/11/moscow-conference. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Excerpts from the Press Conference,” 29 October 1943, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/excerpts-from-the-press-conference-26. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet delegation insist on discussing the war first?","a":"Because Moscow's overriding concern was a landing in northern France that would draw German forces from the east. It asked whether the spring 1944 date still held, and proposed bringing Turkey into the war and gaining Swedish air bases, all to shorten the war against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Four-Nation Declaration seen as a step towards the United Nations?","a":"Because it committed the great powers to keep acting together after the fighting and recognised the need for a general international organisation, based on the sovereign equality of peace-loving states, to keep the peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the conference create a European Advisory Commission?","a":"To give the three governments a standing body in London where questions arising as the war went on could be studied together, so that agreed plans could be made in advance instead of being improvised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%B8%D1%8F_1943_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0.jpg","title":"The round table in Moscow, October 1943","desc":"The American, British and Soviet delegations at the round table of the Moscow Conference in October 1943, with Molotov in the chair.","credit":"Photograph by Mikhail Kalashnikov, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%84%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%B8%D1%8F_1943_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"A large formal room with a chandelier and a big painting, where men in suits and uniforms sit round a circular table covered with papers."},"wiki":"Moscow Conference (1943)"},
    "ww2-330": {"questions":["Marshall warned at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that every diversion from the main plot acted as a suction pump, but the chiefs chose Sicily rather than France for the next blow.","A directive issued at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 21 January 1943 set British and American bombers the progressive destruction of Germany's military, industrial and economic system and the undermining of its morale."],"abstract":"The <b>Casablanca Conference</b> was the meeting of Roosevelt and Churchill and their Combined Chiefs of Staff near Casablanca in French Morocco from 14 to 24 January 1943, 2 months after the Allied landings in North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stalin was invited but stayed away because, the communiqué explained, he was directing a great offensive himself as commander-in-chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For 10 days the combined staffs met two or three times a day, and Churchill told the Commons he had never known so prolonged a professional examination of the whole war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The conference is best remembered for Roosevelt's announcement to the press on its last day that peace required the unconditional surrender of Germany, Italy and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It also brought together the French generals Henri Giraud and Charles de Gaulle, who were photographed with Roosevelt and Churchill on the lawn behind the President's villa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> General Marshall made a last stand for a cross-Channel invasion in 1943, warning that every diversion from the main plot acted as a suction pump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Sir Alan Brooke answered for the British that no large landing in France was possible before late summer, and that the Allies should instead threaten Germany all round the Mediterranean and try to knock Italy out of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The chiefs agreed that defeating the U-boat must remain a first charge on Allied resources, and they ordered Eisenhower to invade Sicily in the favourable moon of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A directive of 21 January set the British and American bomber forces the progressive destruction of the German military, industrial and economic system and the undermining of German morale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt and Churchill then told Stalin that these operations, together with his own powerful offensive, might well bring Germany to her knees in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “The Communiqué, 24 January 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d452. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum by the Combined Chiefs of Staff, C.C.S. 155/1, January 19, 1943: Conduct of the War in 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d408. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum by the Combined Chiefs of Staff, C.C.S. 166/1/D, January 21, 1943: The Bomber Offensive from the United Kingdom,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d412. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Directive to the Commander in Chief, Allied Expeditionary Force in North Africa (Eisenhower), C.C.S. 171/2/D, January 23, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d417. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “President Roosevelt and Prime Minister Churchill to Premier Stalin, January 25, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d423. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Transcript of Press Conference, Casablanca, January 24, 1943,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d395. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 21–25, https://archive.org/details/StrategicPlanningForCoalitionWarfare1943-1944. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 February 1943, vol. 386, cols. 1453–531, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/feb/11/war-situation. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Roosevelt–Churchill Meeting with de Gaulle and Giraud, about Noon, January 24, 1943,” editorial note, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d394. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Marshall give up his case for a landing in France in 1943?","a":"Because the shipping saved by opening the Mediterranean, the safety of the sea route there and the veteran troops already in North Africa outweighed it. Using those troops on the spot would not strain Atlantic shipping, which the U-boats made the major consideration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Sicily chosen as the next objective?","a":"Because taking it promised to make the Mediterranean route more secure, divert German pressure from the Russian front and intensify pressure on Italy, and the American delegation judged it more profitable than Sardinia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt and Churchill send Stalin a report at once?","a":"Because he had been unable to come and was bearing the weight of the land war, and their stated aim was to draw German land and air forces away from the Russian front while sending the greatest possible flow of supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Franklin_D._Roosevelt%2C_Churchill%2C_Giraud%2C_and_DeGaulle_in_Casablanca_-_NARA_-_196990.jpg/1280px-Franklin_D._Roosevelt%2C_Churchill%2C_Giraud%2C_and_DeGaulle_in_Casablanca_-_NARA_-_196990.jpg","title":"Giraud, Roosevelt, de Gaulle and Churchill","desc":"General Henri Giraud, President Roosevelt, General Charles de Gaulle and Prime Minister Churchill on the lawn of the President's villa at Casablanca on 24 January 1943.","credit":"United States National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franklin_D._Roosevelt%2C_Churchill%2C_Giraud%2C_and_DeGaulle_in_Casablanca_-_NARA_-_196990.jpg","alt":"Four men seated in a row on garden chairs on a lawn: two in uniform, one in a light suit and one in a dark suit and hat."},"wiki":"Casablanca Conference"},
    "ww2-391": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in August 1944 that the Russian armies had torn the guts out of the German army in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fought from June 1941 to May 1945.","German losses had reached almost 410,000 men by the end of August 1941, 10 weeks into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Western writers more often simply call the Eastern Front."],"abstract":"The <b>German-Soviet War</b> was the war between Germany with its allies and the Soviet Union, from the invasion of 22 June 1941 to the German surrender of May 1945, on what Western writers call the Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German army entered it with an average of some 3.78 million men in the Russian theatre and had lost almost 410,000 of them, killed, wounded or missing, by the end of August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet strength then grew far faster, and by German estimates of July 1943 the Red Army numbered 5,755,000 men, a 10-month gain three times the German one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older Cold War view explained the outcome by Soviet numbers and Hitler's blunders, while more recent work stresses how the Red Army learned to fight and how even an inefficient Stalinist economy outproduced Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One American study of the war's memory counts some 8 million Red Army soldiers dead and 22 million civilians killed by German forces and killing squads, as its own estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Inside the German high command this was a standing joke, and in April 1944 Jodl had to rebut the quip that 53 per cent of the army fought in Russia while the rest waited in the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Western Allies said as much in public, and on 2 August 1944 Churchill told the Commons that the Russian armies had done the main work in tearing the guts out of the German army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Tehran in December 1943 Stalin had undertaken to attack in the East at about the time of the cross-Channel invasion, so that Germany could not move troops west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The great Soviet offensive of summer 1944 destroyed Army Group Centre with the help of thousands of Lend-Lease trucks, a reminder that the Soviet effort was itself partly supplied from the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most historians now give the Red Army the leading and possibly the principal role in defeating the Wehrmacht, while stressing that it paid with losses no Western democracy would have accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, March 1955), https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 2 August 1944, vol. 402, cc. 1459–568, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]","“The Tehran Conference, November 28–December 1, 1943: Military Conclusions,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tehran.asp. [Open access]","Mark J. Conversino, review of <i>The Red Army and the Second World War</i>, by Alexander Hill, H-War, H-Net Reviews, September 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=55197. [Open access]","Victoria Paige, review of <i>Stalinism at War: The Soviet Union in World War II</i>, by Mark Edele, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=60007. [Open access]","David Wildermuth, review of <i>The Myth of the Eastern Front: The Nazi-Soviet War in American Popular Culture</i>, by Ronald Smelser and Edward J. Davies II, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=38439. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Eastern Front weigh so heavily in Germany's war?","a":"Because most of the German army was there, and in April 1944 Jodl had to rebut the joke that 53 per cent of it fought in Russia while the rest sat in the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The losses began at once: almost 410,000 men had been killed, wounded or gone missing by the end of August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin promise an offensive timed with the invasion of France?","a":"Because at Tehran the Allies agreed that a Soviet attack at the same moment would stop Germany shifting forces from the Eastern Front to the Western.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill later told the Commons that the Russian armies had done the main work of breaking the German army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still argue about how the war was won?","a":"Because the older view explained the result by Soviet numbers and Hitler's mistakes, while later work credits the Red Army's growing skill and an economy that outproduced Germany despite its inefficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Eastern_Front_1943-08_to_1944-12.png","title":"The Soviet advance, August 1943 to December 1944","desc":"A map of the Eastern Front showing in coloured bands how far the Red Army pushed the German line west between 1 August 1943 and 31 December 1944.","credit":"Drawn by Gdr, modified by Zocky and others, GFDL, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Front_1943-08_to_1944-12.png","alt":"A coloured map of eastern Europe from the Baltic to the Black Sea, with bands of yellow, orange, purple, blue and green marking successive Soviet gains and black arrows pointing west towards Germany."},"wiki":"Eastern Front (World War II)"},
    "ww2-392": {"questions":["Shielded by the US Army from a German court, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the former generals whose studies of the Eastern Front taught Americans how the Red Army fought.","A 1955 American study of the invasion of Russia named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chief of staff of the German army from 1938 to 1942, first among the generals whose monographs it used."],"abstract":"<b>Franz Halder</b> was chief of the general staff of the German army from 1 September 1938 until Hitler dismissed him on 24 September 1942, and so stood at the centre of its planning from Poland to the invasion of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Throughout those years he kept shorthand notebooks of conferences, reports and staff talks, which American prosecutors later had transcribed and entered as evidence in the trial of Wilhelm von Leeb and other generals at Nuremberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under 30 March 1941 the notebooks record Hitler telling his generals that the war in the East would be a clash of two ideologies and a war of extermination, in which commissars were to be treated as criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1946 the International Military Tribunal declined to declare the general staff and high command a criminal organisation, while calling its members a disgrace to the honourable profession of arms and a ruthless military caste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A leading study of German operational thinking has since levelled withering criticism at Halder and at the system that guided the German army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> After the war Halder became the central figure of the US Army's Operational History (German) Section, leading a control group of former generals who wrote accounts of the Eastern Front for the Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their manuscripts became official publications, and a 1950 Department of the Army pamphlet on Russian combat methods admitted that it rested mainly on the recollections of a small group of former German commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same pamphlet called the Russian soldier semi-Asiatic, strange and contradictory, carrying wartime language into an American Cold War manual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 1955 army study of the invasion of Russia named Halder first among the former generals whose monographs it used, beside captured German military records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians of the myth of the Eastern Front argue that such accounts helped persuade Americans that the Wehrmacht had fought a clean war lost by Hitler, and that the US Army even shielded Halder from prosecution in a German court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, prefatory note and vol. 6, entry for 30 March 1941 (Nuremberg: Evidence Division, Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany, 1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Accused Organisations,” 30 September–1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judorg.asp. [Open access]","United States Army, European Command, Historical Division, <i>Russian Combat Methods in World War II</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-230 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, November 1950), https://archive.org/details/Dapam20-230. [Open access]","George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations, 1940–1942</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, March 1955), https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","David Wildermuth, review of <i>The Myth of the Eastern Front: The Nazi-Soviet War in American Popular Culture</i>, by Ronald Smelser and Edward J. Davies II, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=38439. [Open access]","Robert Kirchubel, review of <i>The Myth and Reality of German Warfare: Operational Thinking from Moltke the Elder to Heusinger</i>, by Gerhard Paul Gross, H-War, H-Net Reviews, July 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=49015. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the US Army employ a former German chief of staff?","a":"Because in the early Cold War American soldiers wanted to know how the Red Army fought, and the German generals knew it better than anyone in the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Halder's group supplied accounts of combat and orders of battle, and some became official army pamphlets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the generals' accounts shape Western memory of the war?","a":"Because they told a convenient story in which Hitler's decisions lost the war and the army fought honourably, the root of the myth of a clean Wehrmacht.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal had refused to declare the high command a criminal organisation, though it called its members a disgrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Halder's own journal undercut that story?","a":"Because it records Hitler telling the generals in March 1941 that the war in the East would be a war of extermination and that commissars were to be dealt with as criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1970-052-08%2C_Franz_Halder.jpg","title":"Franz Halder in 1938","desc":"A signed studio portrait of Franz Halder from 1938, the year he became chief of the general staff of the German army.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1970-052-08, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1970-052-08,_Franz_Halder.jpg","alt":"Black-and-white portrait of a grey-haired officer with a close-cropped head, round wire spectacles and a small moustache, in uniform with embroidered collar patches and medal ribbons, a signature across the lower right."},"wiki":"Franz Halder"},
    "ww2-393": {"questions":["Stalin broadcast on the evening of 9 May 1945 that the great day of victory had come, and that date is still kept as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Russia every spring.","Vladimir Putin called the Soviet triumph sacred in his 2005 speech for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the holiday at the heart of Russia's official memory of the Great Patriotic War."],"abstract":"<b>Victory Day</b> is the holiday of 9 May on which the Soviet Union, and Russia after it, celebrates the defeat of Germany in what both call the Great Patriotic War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It follows from the surrender signed in Berlin on 8 May 1945 and in force from midnight, and that evening Stalin broadcast that the great day of victory over Germany had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ended with eternal glory to the heroes who had fallen for the freedom of the motherland, joining mourning to triumph in a way the day has kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The soldiers themselves were not honoured in kind at first, for most of the privileges granted to veterans were abolished in 1947 and 1948 to save money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By one historian's count about 34.5 million people served in the Soviet forces during the war and roughly a fifth of them died, yet the veterans became an organised movement only decades later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From the end of 1947 the day itself was a working day, marked by lectures and wreath-laying, until in 1965 it became a day off again and the twentieth anniversary was celebrated on a grand scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Leningrad that year a festive commission planted a Green Belt of Glory, ran a star relay and opened new monuments, the start of the commemoration associated with the Brezhnev years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Soviet collapse the victory became again a keystone of national identity, and in his speech for the sixtieth anniversary in 2005 Vladimir Putin called it sacred, a triumph of good over evil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official story leaves out the Hitler-Stalin pact, Katyn and the annexation of the Baltic states, and a historian in Moscow dates active state intervention in the politics of memory to no later than 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A study of Russian everyday politics finds the cult of victory running through life from childhood, and its reviewer thinks it helped win support for the annexation of Crimea, while in Central Asia the holiday has been reshaped by new national histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["J. V. Stalin, “Victory Speech,” broadcast from Moscow, 9 May 1945, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1945/05/09v.htm. [Open access]","А. В. Ломакин, «Возвращенный праздник: празднование 20-летия Великой Победы в Ленинграде 1965 г.», <i>Ученые записки Новгородского государственного университета</i>, no. 4 (59) (2025): 877–87, https://doi.org/10.34680/2411-7951.2025.4(59).877-887. [in Russian] [Open access]","Franziska Exeler, review of <i>Soviet Veterans of the Second World War: A Popular Movement in an Authoritarian Society, 1941–1991</i>, by Mark Edele, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, January 2010, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=29476. [Open access]","Ivo Mijnssen, “The Victory Myth and Russia’s Identity,” <i>Russian Analytical Digest</i>, no. 72 (9 February 2010): 6–9, https://css.ethz.ch/content/dam/ethz/special-interest/gess/cis/center-for-securities-studies/pdfs/RAD-72.pdf. [Open access]","Alexey Miller, “The Politics of the Past in Russia,” <i>Russian Analytical Digest</i>, no. 72 (9 February 2010): 2–4, https://css.ethz.ch/content/dam/ethz/special-interest/gess/cis/center-for-securities-studies/pdfs/RAD-72.pdf. [Open access]","Jacob Lassin, review of <i>Everyday Foreign Policy: Performing and Consuming the Russian Nation after Crimea</i>, by Elizaveta Gaufman, H-War, H-Net Reviews, April 2025, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=61563. [Open access]","Т. Н. Золотова, И. А. Селезнева и К. Ю. Смирных, «Одна на всех: праздник День Победы в динамике исторической памяти о Великой Отечественной войне в центральноазиатских республиках», <i>Традиционная культура</i> 26, no. 2 (2025): 11–30, https://doi.org/10.26158/tk.2025.26.2.001. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Victory Day held on 9 May rather than 8 May?","a":"Because the act signed in Berlin on 8 May came into force at midnight that night, and Stalin announced the victory to the Soviet people in a broadcast on the evening of 9 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was 1965 a turning point for the holiday?","a":"Because 9 May became a day off again that year after almost 2 decades as a working day, so the twentieth anniversary could be marked on a large scale with relays, planting and new monuments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the victory matter so much in Russia today?","a":"Because the state treats it as a keystone of national identity and of Russia's standing in the former Soviet space, so other versions of the war are treated as attacks on the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A reviewer of one study argues that this cult of victory helped prepare opinion for the annexation of Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/%D0%9C%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D1%8E%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%A0._%D0%AF._%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%89%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8_%D0%B2_%D0%9C%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B5.jpg","title":"The Victory Day parade of 1965","desc":"Marshal Rodion Malinovsky, the Soviet defence minister, takes the parade on Red Square on 9 May 1965, the twentieth anniversary and the first Victory Day in years to be a day off.","credit":"Photographer not recorded, Ministry of Defence of the Russian Federation (mil.ru), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Маршал_Советского_Союза_Р._Я._Малиновский_принимает_парад_на_Красной_площади_в_Москве.jpg","alt":"A marshal in dress uniform covered in medals stands saluting in an open-topped car as it drives across Red Square, with rows of soldiers, crowds and a huge painted banner on a building behind him."},"wiki":"Victory Day (9 May)"},
    "ww2-394": {"questions":["Field Marshal Alexander warned London that enforcing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would mean handcuffs, locked railway carriages and almost certain death for Soviet citizens who refused to go home.","Moscow's officers complained that the Western Allies broke the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by handing over returning Soviet citizens with one day's food instead of the three agreed."],"abstract":"The <b>Yalta repatriation agreement</b> was the accord signed in the Crimea on 11 February 1945 under which the United States and the Soviet Union undertook to return each other's citizens freed by their armies, beside a similar British-Soviet arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its nine articles required that liberated prisoners and civilians be kept apart from enemy prisoners of war, held in camps open to their own country's repatriation officers, fed and clothed, and handed over without delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing in the text mentioned force, yet at Yalta the three leaders agreed that all Allied citizens were to be subject to compulsory repatriation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Western Allies refused to apply it to people from the lands Moscow had annexed after September 1939, the western parts of Belarus, Ukraine and Moldova and the Baltic states, a gap through which many escaped return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 4.2 million forced labourers and prisoners of war went back to the Soviet Union, while Moscow still claimed some 450,000 lost citizens who stayed in the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> British commanders found forced return hard to stomach, and Field Marshal Alexander warned London that it meant handcuffs, locked railway carriages and almost certain death for those sent back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government held to the policy, and when Soviet officers demanded that a camp commander in Styria load some 1,500 former prisoners for Vienna, only 15 volunteered to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet officials had grievances of their own, complaining that the Allies handed over returnees with a day's food instead of the three agreed and left many camps short of clothing and shoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The returnees themselves arrived uneasy, and one miner back from Britain told officials at the Odessa checkpoint that the British and Americans had warned that all repatriates were sent to Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The displaced who stayed in the West soon became prizes in the propaganda contest of the early Cold War, recast in American policy from victims of Nazism into victims of communism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “Agreement Between the United States and the Soviet Union Concerning Liberated Prisoners of War and Civilians, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d504. [Open access]","Olena Naumenko, “From the Yalta Agreements to the Refusal of Involuntary Repatriation: The Evolution of the Western Vision on the Soviet Forced Displacement (1944–1945),” <i>The Ethnology Notebooks</i>, no. 1 (163) (2022): 183–91, https://doi.org/10.15407/nz2022.01.183. [Open access]","А. Ф. Бичехвост, «Взаимоотношения с союзниками по антигитлеровской коалиции в процессе репатриации советских граждан: трудности и противоречия (1944–1945)», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 24, no. 2 (2024): 181–87, https://doi.org/10.18500/1819-4907-2024-24-2-181-187. [in Russian] [Open access]","Glennys Young, review of <i>Return to the Motherland: Displaced Soviets in WWII and the Cold War</i>, by Seth Bernstein, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, December 2023, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=59189. [Open access]","Alisa Kuzmina, review of <i>Lost Souls: Soviet Displaced Persons and the Birth of the Cold War</i>, by Sheila Fitzpatrick, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, May 2025, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=61628. [Open access]","«Проект записки Отдела пропаганды и агитации ЦК ВКП(б) Г. М. Маленкову “О ходе репатриации, об устройстве на работу репатриированных советских граждан и об организации политической работы с ними”, 26 июля 1945 г.», in <i>Советская жизнь, 1945–1953</i> (Moscow: ROSSPEN, 2003), 368–77, Istoricheskie materialy, https://istmat.org/node/18438. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the agreement end in forced repatriation?","a":"Because the leaders at Yalta took it to mean that every Allied citizen must be sent home, whether or not the person wished to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The text itself spoke only of separating, caring for and handing over the liberated without delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some Soviet citizens escape return?","a":"Because the Western Allies would not send back people from territories annexed after September 1939, and some British officers quietly obstructed the policy, so many registered as non-Soviet or simply stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were returnees afraid to go home?","a":"Because they had heard in the West that repatriates were sent to Siberia, and at Soviet checkpoints they were held under guard without knowing where they would be sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Russian_displaced_persons_and_former_prisoners_of_war_about_to_leave_transit_camps_near_Hamburg_for_the_Russian_zone_of_occupation_from_where_they_will_be_repatriated_to_the_Soviet_Union%2C_31_May_1945._BU7034.jpg","title":"Soviet citizens leaving Hamburg for home, May 1945","desc":"Soviet displaced persons and former prisoners of war about to leave the transit camps near Hamburg for the Soviet zone, from where they were to be repatriated, on 31 May 1945.","credit":"Sergeant Chitham, No. 5 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum BU 7034, public domain (UK Crown copyright expired), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russian_displaced_persons_and_former_prisoners_of_war_about_to_leave_transit_camps_near_Hamburg_for_the_Russian_zone_of_occupation_from_where_they_will_be_repatriated_to_the_Soviet_Union,_31_May_1945._BU7034.jpg","alt":"A group of men and boys in caps and worn jackets crowd the open doorway of a wooden railway wagon, one in the middle playing an accordion, some looking at the camera without smiling."}},
    "ww2-395": {"questions":["Organised as NKVD special camps on 6 January 1942, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> screened Red Army men who had been captured or encircled by the enemy before their fate was decided.","The department running the Soviet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was disbanded on 22 January 1946, and its work of checking returning prisoners and civilians passed to the Gulag."],"abstract":"<b>Filtration camps</b> were the NKVD camps and checkpoints through which the Soviet state screened its own soldiers and civilians returning from captivity, encirclement or forced labour before deciding what to do with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system began in the war's first winter, when special NKVD camps were organised on 6 January 1942 to check Red Army servicemen who had been prisoners or had been encircled by the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The camps gained a department of their own in August 1944, renamed on 20 February 1945 the Department of Verification and Filtration Camps and wound up on 22 January 1946, when the Gulag took over its work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Returnees from the West entered the system at collection camps abroad, set up under agreements such as the one signed at Yalta, where lists giving each person's name, birth year and home address were drawn up for the trains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two copies travelled with each train to the commander of a reserve unit or the head of an NKVD verification and filtration point, where the repatriates underwent what officials called state verification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> By July 1945 such points stood in 19 border towns, where returnees were usually held 7 to 10 days, sometimes fifteen, for a medical examination, delousing and a check of identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most were then allowed to go home, but a party report of that month found them anxious and guarded, and one miner back from Britain said the point treated them like camp inmates under guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Suspicion fell hardest on men, who were more likely to face a long verification and labour mobilisation, and surveillance and interrogation of returnees could go on for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was nonetheless no formal order sending all repatriates to the Gulag, and research in Soviet archives has overturned the older belief that nearly all of them were arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Former prisoners of war still carried the stigma of having fallen into German hands, and stood near the bottom of the veterans' hierarchy, facing discrimination and sometimes outright repression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["«Указатель переименования и хронологических рамок структурных подразделений», in <i>Лубянка. ВЧК–ОГПУ–НКВД–НКГБ–МГБ–МВД–КГБ, 1917–1960: Справочник</i> (Moscow: Mezhdunarodnyi fond «Demokratiia», 1997), Istoricheskie materialy, https://istmat.org/node/39000. [in Russian] [Open access]","«Проект записки Отдела пропаганды и агитации ЦК ВКП(б) Г. М. Маленкову “О ходе репатриации, об устройстве на работу репатриированных советских граждан и об организации политической работы с ними”, 26 июля 1945 г.», in <i>Советская жизнь, 1945–1953</i> (Moscow: ROSSPEN, 2003), 368–77, Istoricheskie materialy, https://istmat.org/node/18438. [in Russian] [Open access]","А. Ф. Бичехвост, «Репатриация советских граждан железнодорожным транспортом (1944–1945)», <i>Известия Саратовского университета. Новая серия. Серия: История. Международные отношения</i> 22, no. 3 (2022): 298–306, https://doi.org/10.18500/1819-4907-2022-22-3-298-306. [in Russian] [Open access]","Glennys Young, review of <i>Return to the Motherland: Displaced Soviets in WWII and the Cold War</i>, by Seth Bernstein, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, December 2023, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=59189. [Open access]","Franziska Exeler, review of <i>Soviet Veterans of the Second World War: A Popular Movement in an Authoritarian Society, 1941–1991</i>, by Mark Edele, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, January 2010, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=29476. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Agreement Between the United States and the Soviet Union Concerning Liberated Prisoners of War and Civilians, February 11, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d504. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet state screen its own returning citizens?","a":"Because it treated anyone who had been in enemy hands as a possible traitor, and wanted both to punish real collaborators and to put returnees back to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The checks began in 1942 with soldiers who had been captured or encircled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were most repatriates allowed home after the checks?","a":"Because the points were meant to sort people rather than imprison them all: after a week or two most were permitted to return home, and no general order sent repatriates to the Gulag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did returnees fear the filtration points?","a":"Because they were kept under guard without knowing where they would be sent, and party workers found them guarded and worried about whether they would keep their rights as citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Frigjort_krigsfange_fra_arbeidsleir_i_Saltdal.jpg","title":"A freed Soviet prisoner in Norway, 1945","desc":"A liberated Russian prisoner of war from the Bjørnelva camp in Saltdal, northern Norway, photographed after the German surrender of May 1945; the picture comes from the papers of the doctor who then ran the prisoner-of-war office in Bodø.","credit":"Riksarkivet (National Archives of Norway), CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frigjort_krigsfange_fra_arbeidsleir_i_Saltdal.jpg","alt":"Close-up portrait of a gaunt young man with cropped hair, hollow cheeks and prominent collarbones, wearing a loose V-necked jumper against a dark background."},"wiki":"NKVD filtration camps","places":["city:1159149325"]},
    "ww2-396": {"questions":["Zhukov's order of 10 June 1945 allowing political parties was among the first acts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and within weeks the eastern zone's parties formed one anti-fascist bloc.","Reparations removals carried out by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Potsdam terms may have cost eastern Germany a third of its industrial base before the occupation ended."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet Military Administration in Germany</b>, known by its Russian initials as SVAG, governed the Soviet zone of occupation from its headquarters at Karlshorst in Berlin from the summer of 1945 until the founding of the German Democratic Republic in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its authority rested on the declaration of 5 June 1945, signed in Berlin by Zhukov and the other Allied commanders, by which the four powers assumed supreme authority over a defeated Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Potsdam the Allies confirmed that each commander-in-chief exercised that authority in his own zone, and all four jointly through the Control Council for matters affecting Germany as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Moscow arrived without a detailed plan, and in the first weeks local Red Army commanders, with no clear lines of authority, ruled the zone more or less arbitrarily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even after SVAG was set up, tensions between Moscow and Karlshorst and among Soviet agencies hampered it, while it tried to control even minute details of German administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Zhukov's order of 10 June 1945 authorised political parties, and within weeks Communists, Social Democrats, Liberal Democrats and Christian Democrats had been drawn into a single anti-fascist bloc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Potsdam let the Soviet Union take its reparations from its own zone, and the removals, with land reform and expropriation, cost eastern Germany perhaps a third of its industrial base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The NKVD ran its own operation, holding 122,671 people in special camps where as many as 43,889 died, according to the main archival history of the zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rape by Soviet soldiers, put by one German estimate that this history supports at up to two million victims, denied the occupiers and the Communists they backed any legitimacy among Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1947 and 1948 SVAG and the Socialist Unity Party had turned towards a Sovietised zone, and American efforts to contain it made eastern Germany a front line of the early Cold War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Steven P. Remy, review of <i>The Russians in Germany: A History of the Soviet Zone of Occupation, 1945–1949</i>, by Norman M. Naimark, H-German, H-Net Reviews, May 1996, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=420. [Open access]","“Declaration Regarding the Defeat of Germany and the Assumption of Supreme Authority by Allied Powers, June 5, 1945,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger01.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The United States Political Adviser for Germany (Murphy) to the Secretary of State, Frankfurt, 6 June 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d276. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The United States Political Adviser for Germany (Murphy) to the Secretary of State, Frankfurt, August 8, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d767. [Open access]","“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–1949</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","Christopher A. Reith, review of <i>Between Containment and Rollback: The United States and the Cold War in Germany</i>, by Christian F. Ostermann, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=58936. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet zone pay its reparations from its own territory?","a":"Because the Potsdam conference settled that Soviet claims would be met by removals from the Soviet zone and from German assets abroad, with a share of equipment from the western zones on top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the occupation fail to win Germans over?","a":"Because Red Army violence, the removals and the secret police camps made the occupiers hated, and the Communist party they sponsored came to be seen as a tool of the Kremlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the zone's politics move towards Sovietisation?","a":"Because SVAG and Walter Ulbricht distrusted spontaneous movements, and economic hardship, the unpopularity of the occupiers and the deepening East-West split led them in 1947 and 1948 to abandon a German road to socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-S90338%2C_Berlin%2C_DDR-Gr%C3%BCndung%2C_Regierung_bei_Tschuikow.jpg","title":"The handover at Karlshorst, November 1949","desc":"General Vasily Chuikov greets Walter Ulbricht at Berlin-Karlshorst on 11 November 1949, when the Soviet Military Administration was dissolved and its functions passed to the new East German government; Otto Grotewohl stands second from left.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-S90338, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-S90338,_Berlin,_DDR-Gründung,_Regierung_bei_Tschuikow.jpg","alt":"In a crowded room a Soviet general in uniform shakes hands with a balding civilian in a dark suit, while other civilians and officers in Soviet uniforms stand watching."},"wiki":"Soviet Military Administration in Germany"},
    "ww2-397": {"questions":["The uranium miner Wismut, employing more than 200,000 people by 1950, was organised as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of the companies through which Moscow owned industry in eastern Germany.","Taking a firm's land, buildings, machinery and stocks but not its cash or debts, each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Soviet zone worked for the Soviet Union as reparations."],"abstract":"A <b>Sowjetische Aktiengesellschaft</b>, or ‘Soviet joint-stock company’, was one of the corporations through which the Soviet Union took over major firms in its zone of Germany and ran them as part of its reparations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Potsdam had settled that Soviet claims would be met by removals from the Soviet zone and from German assets abroad, and the Western powers renounced any claim to shares of German enterprises in the eastern zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A list obtained by American intelligence in December 1946 named Soviet corporations for machinery, electrical works, fuel, potash, chemicals, transport machinery, and the photographic and cinema industry, among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Province of Saxony each firm passed by an individual order of the chief of the provincial administration, the first numbered 142 on 22 July 1946, and by 1 November the numbering had reached 222.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Russians took the land, buildings, machinery, stocks and raw materials but not cash or debts, so the Lindner wagon works at Ammendorf, valued at 14 million Reichsmarks, joined the Soviet corporation for transport machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The companies stood beside a wider programme of dismantling, which carried whole plants east until it was halted in 1948 without either disarming Germany completely or giving Soviet reconstruction the help expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By one estimate removals and current production together met the Soviet goal of ten billion dollars in reparations by 1950, at the cost of perhaps a third of eastern Germany's industrial base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Among them was the uranium miner Wismut, which employed more than 200,000 people in 1950 and by 1952 had become the biggest uranium producer in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1950 it was shipping more than 1,200 tonnes of the metal a year to the Soviet Union, nearly 60 per cent of the Eastern bloc's output and indispensable to the Soviet nuclear programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Several were later handed back, and on 1 January 1954 a Soviet-German commission under the mining minister Fritz Selbmann was to place works such as the steel plant at Thale under German control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–1949</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Group, “Soviet Corporations,” intelligence report, Germany, Russian zone, 27 December 1946, CIA-RDP82-00457R000200320003-5, CIA Reading Room, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00457r000200320003-5. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “SAG Enterprises To Be Turned Over to the Ministry of Mining and Smelting,” information report, East Germany, 27 January 1954, CIA-RDP80-00810A003200290008-7, CIA Reading Room, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp80-00810a003200290008-7. [Open access]","Lutz Prieß, review of <i>Sowjetische Demontagen in Deutschland 1944–1949: Hintergründe, Ziele und Wirkungen</i>, ed. Rainer Karlsch and Jochen Laufer, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, January 2003, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=17197. [in German] [Open access]","Matthias Uhl, review of <i>Uranbergbau im Kalten Krieg: Die Wismut im sowjetischen Atomkomplex</i>, ed. Rudolf Boch and Rainer Karlsch, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, March 2013, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=38668. [in German] [Open access]","Steven P. Remy, review of <i>The Russians in Germany: A History of the Soviet Zone of Occupation, 1945–1949</i>, by Norman M. Naimark, H-German, H-Net Reviews, May 1996, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=420. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Moscow run factories in Germany instead of moving them all east?","a":"Because dismantled plants often failed to give Soviet reconstruction the help expected once moved, while firms left in place could keep producing for the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Potsdam had already given Moscow its reparations from its own zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Wismut so important to the Soviet Union?","a":"Because after 1945 the Soviet Union had neither the atomic bomb nor enough uranium, and the ore mined by Wismut in the Erzgebirge and Thuringia became indispensable to its nuclear weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did a German firm become Soviet property?","a":"By a numbered order of the head of the provincial administration, after which an expert valued its land, buildings, machinery and stocks and a notarised deed transferred them, leaving cash and debts with the old firm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-26796-0006%2C_Gustav_H%C3%BCbner.jpg","title":"A Wismut brigade leader, 1954","desc":"Gustav Hübner, a brigade leader of the German-Soviet Wismut company, photographed on 8 October 1954 when he received the East German National Prize for introducing fast mechanised tunnelling.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-26796-0006, photograph by Schmidt, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-26796-0006,_Gustav_Hübner.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of a young man with swept-back hair in a dark miner's tunic with crossed-hammer collar badges, a striped tie and a row of medals and badges on his chest."}},
    "ww2-398": {"questions":["Marshal Malinovsky chaired the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Romania, whose statutes allowed its American and British members a staff of only five people each.","The Potsdam conference took the Soviet proposals for Hungary as the model for revising each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Romania, Bulgaria and Hungary once the war in Europe had ended."],"abstract":"An <b>Allied Control Commission</b> was the body set up under an armistice of 1944 or 1945 to enforce its terms on a former ally of Germany until peace was made, and in Romania, Bulgaria and Hungary the commission was run by the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Romanian armistice, signed in Moscow on 12 September 1944, placed its commission under the general direction and orders of the Allied, in fact Soviet, High Command acting for all the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moscow named Marshal Malinovsky its chairman, and the statutes sent to the American embassy allowed the American and British representatives a staff of only five people each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bulgarian and Hungarian armistices of 28 October 1944 and 20 January 1945 followed the same pattern, with a Soviet chairman and American and British representatives taking part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The commissions oversaw the return of looted property, the use of industry and transport and the demobilisation of the defeated armies, which gave their chairmen wide powers in the defeated countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> When the war in Europe ended, the Soviet government told its partners in Sofia that its chairman would now consult them at least once every 10 days and discuss major directives with them first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Potsdam conference accepted the Soviet proposals for Hungary as the basis for revising all three commissions, now that hostilities had ceased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In August 1945 the Soviet deputy chairman in Romania sent the Americans new regulations stating that the chairman would be a representative of the Soviet armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Western members could now travel freely, but only after telling the commission their time and route in advance, and directives were still issued by the chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commissions thus gave legal form to the Red Army's presence in south-eastern Europe, with the Soviet High Command in the chair and its Western allies taking part as junior members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“The Armistice Agreement with Rumania; September 12, 1944,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/rumania.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State,” September 1944, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944, Europe, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d253. [Open access]","“The Armistice Agreement with Bulgaria; October 28, 1944,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/bulgaria.asp. [Open access]","“Armistice Agreement with Hungary; January 20, 1945,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/hungary.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Representative on the Allied Control Commission for Bulgaria (Crane) to the Joint Chiefs of Staff,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945, Volume I</i>, no. 309, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d309. [Open access]","“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–1949</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chief of the United States Military Representation on the Allied Control Commission for Rumania (Schuyler) to the War Department,” August 1945, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, Europe, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v05/d426. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union chair the commissions in these countries?","a":"Because the armistices were concluded by the Allied, in practice Soviet, High Command whose armies held the countries, and the texts put the commissions under its general direction and orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Americans unhappy with the arrangement?","a":"Because the statutes made their representatives members of a Soviet-run body with a staff of five, and the ambassador expected to seek separate political representation or direct contact with the Romanian government instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What changed when the war in Europe ended?","a":"The Soviet government promised regular consultation and prior discussion of major directives, and Potsdam adopted its Hungarian proposals for all three commissions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The chairman remained a Soviet officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/IICCR_C057_Red_Army_Bucharest.jpg","title":"The Red Army enters Bucharest, August 1944","desc":"Soviet troops ride into Bucharest along Bulevardul Carol on 30 August 1944, two weeks before the armistice that set up the Allied Control Commission in the Romanian capital.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Romanian National Archives, Fototeca online a comunismului românesc photo C057, FOCR licence, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IICCR_C057_Red_Army_Bucharest.jpg","alt":"Soviet soldiers in forage caps, one with medals, crowd the back and cab of an open truck beneath a large furled regimental banner, in front of a tall city building."},"wiki":"Allied Commission"},
    "ww2-399": {"questions":["2 weeks after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of February 1946, George Kennan sent his long telegram tracing the Kremlin's neurotic view of the world to a traditional Russian sense of insecurity.","Calling for three or more new 5-year plans to make the country safe against all contingencies, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the eve of the 1946 Supreme Soviet elections alarmed American officials."],"abstract":"<b>Stalin's election speech</b> was the address he gave to the voters of his Moscow district on 9 February 1946, before the elections to the Supreme Soviet, and his first full statement of what the war just won had meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He told them the war had been no accident but the inevitable result of capitalism's uneven development, which drove the worse-provided powers to seize a new share by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He added that the Second World War had been anti-fascist and liberating from the start, which the American chargé in Moscow noted as a departure from the line that it had been imperialist before 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victory, Stalin argued, proved the Soviet system a better form of society than any other and justified the 5-year plans and collectivisation that had gone before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He promised an end to rationing but called for three or more new 5-year plans to lift output to 50 million tons of pig iron and 60 million of steel a year, against all contingencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It came after a war in which the Red Army had not only saved the Soviet Union but driven into the heart of Europe, where Soviet power would hold sway for the next 45 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Washington the director of European affairs called it the most important and authoritative guide to postwar Soviet policy, and another official urged that Soviet propaganda be diligently countered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> George Kennan, reporting the election speeches, noted that they boasted of the enormous international authority the Soviet Union now enjoyed while placing little reliance on the new United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 February his long telegram explained that at the bottom of the Kremlin's neurotic view of world affairs lay a traditional and instinctive Russian sense of insecurity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The quarrels that followed, over Soviet removals and party politics in eastern Germany and over the displaced people Moscow claimed as its own, are where historians now find the birth of the Cold War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["J. V. Stalin, “Origin and Character of the Second World War,” from a speech to the voters of his district, 9 February 1946, in <i>For Peaceful Coexistence: Post War Interviews</i> (New York: International Publishers, 1951), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1946/02/09.htm. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kennan) to the Secretary of State, Moscow, February 12, 1946,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946, Eastern Europe, The Soviet Union, Volume VI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v06/d474. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kennan) to the Secretary of State, Moscow, February 22, 1946,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946, Eastern Europe, The Soviet Union, Volume VI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v06/d475. [Open access]","Mark J. Conversino, review of <i>The Red Army and the Second World War</i>, by Alexander Hill, H-War, H-Net Reviews, September 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=55197. [Open access]","Christopher A. Reith, review of <i>Between Containment and Rollback: The United States and the Cold War in Germany</i>, by Christian F. Ostermann, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=58936. [Open access]","Alisa Kuzmina, review of <i>Lost Souls: Soviet Displaced Persons and the Birth of the Cold War</i>, by Sheila Fitzpatrick, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, May 2025, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=61628. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did American officials take the speech so seriously?","a":"Because it came from Stalin himself and set out the party line for the years ahead, and the State Department's director of European affairs judged it the most authoritative guide to postwar Soviet policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin present the war as proof of the Soviet system?","a":"Because victory let him justify the 5-year plans and collectivisation as the preparation that had made it possible, and call for new plans on the same pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Red Army had indeed saved the country and carried Soviet power into Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kennan answer with a long telegram?","a":"Because he believed the Kremlin's hostility arose from inner Russian needs and a traditional sense of insecurity rather than from anything the West had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Bulletin_at_the_Elections_to_the_Supreme_Soviet_of_the_USSR_%281946%29.jpg","title":"A ballot for the Supreme Soviet election, 1946","desc":"A ballot paper for the elections of 10 February 1946 to the Supreme Soviet in Stalingrad's urban district number 286, the day after Stalin's speech, bearing the name of a single candidate.","credit":"Stalingrad urban district electoral commission, public domain (official document), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bulletin_at_the_Elections_to_the_Supreme_Soviet_of_the_USSR_(1946).jpg","alt":"A printed Russian ballot paper headed with the words election ballot and the date 10 February 1946, with one candidate's name in the left column and the bodies that nominated him on the right."}},
    "ww2-400": {"questions":["Washington long held that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be fixed only by a peace treaty, and the two-plus-four treaty of 1990 gave the last word on the frontier between Germany and Poland.","Churchill told the Commons in December 1944 that moving Poland west to the frontier later drawn along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would mean the total expulsion of the Germans living there."],"abstract":"The <b>Oder–Neisse line</b> is the frontier between Germany and Poland drawn by the victors in 1945, running from the Baltic just west of Swinemünde, today's Świnoujście, along the Oder and then the western Neisse to the Czechoslovak border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Yalta the Allies agreed that Poland's eastern frontier would follow the Curzon Line and that Poland must receive substantial accessions of territory in the north and west, the extent to await a peace conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Allied declaration of 5 June 1945 still spoke of Germany within its frontiers of 31 December 1937, the reference point for later German talk of the lost territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Potsdam the three powers placed the former German lands east of the line, with southern East Prussia and Danzig, under Polish administration, while the final delimitation was again left to the peace settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill had told the Commons in December 1944 that the plan meant the total expulsion of the Germans from the lands Poland would gain, which he called the most satisfactory and lasting method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The line followed the Red Army, whose winter offensive of 1945 carried Soviet troops more than 400 kilometres (250 miles) without a pause to the Oder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was among the least negotiated results of the war, for American officials balked at the size of the transfer, the Allies fudged it at Potsdam, and American diplomats still proposed corrections in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For decades the border was valid only by default, with Washington insisting that it could be fixed only in a peace treaty and West German governments speaking of the borders of 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The treaties of Willy Brandt's Eastern policy between 1969 and 1972 accepted its inviolability, and the two-plus-four treaty of 1990 gave the last word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind it stood the verdict of the Eastern Front, which had carried the Red Army into the heart of Europe, where Soviet power would hold sway for the next 45 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–1949</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","“The Yalta Conference: Protocol of Proceedings of Crimea Conference,” February 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","William G. Gray, review of <i>The Oder-Neisse Line: The United States, Poland, and Germany in the Cold War</i>, by Debra J. Allen, H-German, H-Net Reviews, January 2004, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=8618. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Poland,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 15 December 1944, vol. 406, cc. 1478–578, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/dec/15/poland. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Mark J. Conversino, review of <i>The Red Army and the Second World War</i>, by Alexander Hill, H-War, H-Net Reviews, September 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=55197. [Open access]","“Declaration Regarding the Defeat of Germany and the Assumption of Supreme Authority by Allied Powers, June 5, 1945,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger01.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Poland gain German land in the west?","a":"Because the Allies agreed at Yalta that Poland's eastern frontier would follow the Curzon Line, with the lands beyond going to the Soviet Union, and that Poland must be compensated with territory in the north and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the border remain provisional for so long?","a":"Because the Potsdam text left the final delimitation to a peace settlement that never came, and Washington, wary of West German politics, kept saying the border could be fixed only in a peace treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill favour expelling the Germans?","a":"Because he believed a clean sweep, with no mixture of populations, would avoid endless trouble of the kind Alsace-Lorraine had caused, and thought large transfers more possible in modern conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Oder-Neisse_line_between_Germany_and_Poland.jpg","title":"The Oder–Neisse frontier today","desc":"A relief map of the German-Polish border along the Oder and the Neisse from the Baltic to the Czech border, with the shift west at the northern end that leaves Szczecin in Poland; the labels are in German.","credit":"Christoph Lingg, map from demis.nl, CC BY-SA 2.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oder-Neisse_line_between_Germany_and_Poland.jpg","alt":"A map with Germany shaded green on the left and Poland grey-blue on the right, divided by a dark blue river line labelled Oder running south from the Baltic and a paler line labelled Neisse continuing to the Czech Republic."},"wiki":"Oder–Neisse line"},
    "ww2-381": {"questions":["Some 2.5 million Soviet soldiers with more than 6,000 tanks were massed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against about a million Germans on the Oder front who had barely 800 tanks.","The American official history called the street fighting of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hardly more than a contested mop-up, since the encirclement outside the city had already decided it."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Berlin</b> was the last great Soviet offensive of the war in Europe, opened on 16 April 1945 by Zhukov’s 1st Belorussian Front on the Oder and Konev’s 1st Ukrainian Front on the Neisse, and ended with the surrender of the city on 2 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> For it the Soviet command massed some 2.5 million soldiers, over 6,000 tanks and 7,500 aircraft against about a million Germans on the Oder front with barely 800 tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Zhukov’s attack began in darkness under searchlights meant to blind the defenders, but his infantry piled up in the mud and smoke below the Seelow Heights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 25 April the two fronts met north-west of Potsdam and closed the ring around the capital, on the same day that American and Soviet troops made contact on the Elbe at Torgau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside the ring were barely 100,000 defenders, over 40,000 of them Volkssturm militia, and about two million civilians sheltering in cellars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Stalin had assured Eisenhower that Berlin had lost its former strategic significance and would receive only secondary forces, even as his armies redeployed in haste for an offensive aimed at it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soon afterwards he wrote to Roosevelt that his commanders were sure the Western Allies had made a secret deal with the Germans to let them advance east almost without resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the city the fighting from the S-Bahn ring inwards was, in the American official history’s judgement, hardly more than a contested mop-up, because the battle had been decided outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Göring, Himmler, Ribbentrop and Dönitz left the capital while Hitler stayed in his bunker, where on 23 April Speer heard him rage at a telegram from Göring and say it did not matter who negotiated the capitulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After Hitler’s suicide on 30 April a request for an armistice was refused, the garrison laid down its arms on 2 May, and on the night of 8 to 9 May the Wehrmacht’s unconditional surrender was signed at the Soviet headquarters in Karlshorst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 468–99, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chairman of the Council of People’s Commissars of the Soviet Union (Stalin) to President Roosevelt, April 3, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d532. [Open access]","H. R. Trevor-Roper, <i>The Last Days of Hitler</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1947), 194–95, 209–11, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.85969. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 21 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]","Deutscher Bundestag, “Gregor Gaida: Reichstagsfiale,” <i>Artothek: Die Kunstsammlung des Deutschen Bundestages</i>, https://www.bundestag.de/besuche/kunst/artothek/gaida-gregor-1012598. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Museum Berlin-Karlshorst, “Historischer Ort,” https://www.museum-karlshorst.de/ueber-uns/historischer-ort/. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin tell Eisenhower that Berlin no longer mattered?","a":"Because he did not trust his allies to leave the city to him. While he was telling Eisenhower that Berlin had lost its strategic significance, his armies were redeploying for an offensive aimed at it, and within days he was accusing the Western Allies of a secret deal with the Germans to open the way east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the fighting inside the city called a mop-up?","a":"Because the decisive fighting happened outside it. Once the Soviet fronts closed their ring on 25 April and cut off the German armies on the Oder, the defenders inside, barely 100,000 men and many of them militia, could only delay the end, and the official history found that the promised fortress had never come into existence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fighting not stop when Hitler died?","a":"Because those left in his bunker tried to use his death to bargain. They sent word to the Soviet command and asked for an armistice so that the successor government could meet in Berlin, but the reply demanded unconditional surrender, and only on 2 May did the garrison lay down its arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-E0406-0022-012%2C_Sowjetische_Artillerie_vor_Berlin.jpg","title":"Soviet artillery before Berlin, April 1945","desc":"Soviet field guns drawn up in open country during the offensive on Berlin that began on 16 April 1945, a picture from the wartime archive of the East German news agency.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-E0406-0022-012, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-E0406-0022-012,_Sowjetische_Artillerie_vor_Berlin.jpg","alt":"A long row of field guns in a flat field with their barrels raised, soldiers in greatcoats and boxes of ammunition around them."},"wiki":"Battle of Berlin"},
    "ww2-382": {"questions":["Reporting on 29 April that the Russians would reach the Chancellery by 1 May, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told Hitler it was now or never for the garrison to break out of the encircled city.","A plaque in Tempelhof records the house where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, commander of the Berlin defence area, signed the order for the German troops in the city to stop fighting on 2 May 1945."],"abstract":"<b>Helmuth Weidling</b> was the German general who commanded the defence of Berlin in its last 10 days and who surrendered the city to the Red Army on 2 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A General der Artillerie in command of the LVI Panzer Corps, he was ordered by Hitler on 23 April to take over the city’s eastern and south-eastern defences with his troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler then made him defence commandant of all Berlin, only a day after he had intended to have him shot, and Weidling answered that he would rather they had shot him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he inherited was a garrison of barely 100,000 men, more than 40,000 of them Volkssturm militia, in a city of some two million exhausted civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beside his own corps the sectors were held by Volkssturm, SS and Hitler Youth formations, the great flak towers stood like concrete battleships, powerful yet helpless, and he found that his predecessors had tried to command through the public telephone system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the night of 28 April Weidling brought Hitler a plan to break out of the encircled city, and Hitler listened before declaring that it was better for him to stay where he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the conference of the following night he reported that the Russians had advanced almost to the Air Ministry and would reach the Chancellery by 1 May at the latest, and said that now or never the troops might break through the ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Told after Hitler’s suicide that the Führer had committed hara-kiri, he went back to his command post in disgust and released his soldiers from their oaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early on 2 May he crossed the lines and, at the command post of the Soviet 8th Guards Army in Tempelhof, signed the order to the German troops in Berlin to stop fighting at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A memorial plaque on that house now records that for Berlin his order meant the end of the war, although it was another 2 days before the fighting died out completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 480–94, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","H. R. Trevor-Roper, <i>The Last Days of Hitler</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1947), 143, 194–95, 212–13, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.85969. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Deutscher Bundestag, “Gregor Gaida: Reichstagsfiale,” <i>Artothek: Die Kunstsammlung des Deutschen Bundestages</i>, https://www.bundestag.de/besuche/kunst/artothek/gaida-gregor-1012598. [in German] [Open access]","Bezirksamt Tempelhof-Schöneberg von Berlin, “Gartenstadt Neu-Tempelhof: Kiezspaziergang vom 16.05.2015,” Berlin.de, https://www.berlin.de/ba-tempelhof-schoeneberg/ueber-den-bezirk/spaziergaenge/2015/kiezspaziergang_05_2015-315782.php. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Weidling say he would rather have been shot?","a":"Because he understood what he was being given. Hitler had wanted him shot only the day before, and the command he now received was a city held by barely 100,000 men, many of them militia, with no proper means of command, in a battle that had already been lost outside its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler refuse the breakout Weidling proposed?","a":"Because he had decided to stay in Berlin himself. He listened to the plan on the night of 28 April and declared that it was better to remain where he was, and when Weidling pressed again the next night that it was now or never, Hitler replied that a breakout was impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his order matter if the war went on for another week?","a":"Because it ended the fighting for the city itself. Signed at a Soviet command post in Tempelhof on 2 May, it told every German unit in Berlin to stop at once, and the house’s memorial plaque records that for Berlin this meant the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/%D0%93%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%93%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BC%D1%83%D1%82_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%B9%D0%B4%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3_-_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%93%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%8E%D1%89%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%91%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0%2C_2_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%81%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%81%D1%8F_%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD_%D0%B2%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B5_%D1%81_%D1%87%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%BE.jpg","title":"Weidling and his staff after the surrender, 2 May 1945","desc":"General Helmuth Weidling with members of his staff, photographed on the day they surrendered to the Red Army in Berlin.","credit":"Photograph by Gleb Samsonov, <i>Izvestia</i>, from the Russian Ministry of Defence website, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%93%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%93%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BC%D1%83%D1%82_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%B9%D0%B4%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3_-_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%87%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%93%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%8E%D1%89%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%91%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0%2C_2_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%81%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%81%D1%8F_%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD_%D0%B2%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B5_%D1%81_%D1%87%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B8_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%BE.jpg","alt":"Four German officers in field uniform stand unsmiling in a row in front of a dark doorway."},"wiki":"Helmuth Weidling"},
    "ww2-383": {"questions":["Speer flew into Berlin on 23 April 1945 and was with Hitler in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when Bormann brought him a telegram in which Göring proposed to take over power.","The roar of diesel ventilation sealed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> off from the sound of the battle, although shells landing close by shook its rooms and filled them with fumes."],"abstract":"The <b>Führerbunker</b> was the air-raid shelter beneath the garden of the old Reich Chancellery in Berlin where Hitler spent the last weeks of the war and where he killed himself on 30 April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stairs led down from the Chancellery to an upper bunker of twelve tiny rooms, including servants’ quarters and Hitler’s vegetarian kitchen, and from there a curved stair descended to his own deeper bunker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That lower part held eighteen small and cramped rooms off a central passage, with a conference room for the daily situation meetings, a private suite for Hitler and Eva Braun, and four flights of concrete steps up to an emergency exit in the garden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American official history pictures him there prematurely aged and palsied, buried under about 6 metres (20 feet) of earth and concrete out of sight and hearing of the destruction rolling in on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The roar of the diesel-driven ventilation gave almost perfect isolation, though shells landing close by shook the bunker and filled it with fumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On his birthday, 20 April, Hitler received the Nazi leadership in the bunker for the last time, and in the following days most of them left the city while Goebbels and Bormann stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Speer flew back in on 23 April and was there when Bormann brought a telegram from Göring, at which Hitler called him corrupt and a drug addict and then said that he might negotiate the capitulation all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Until 27 April he kept up the regular situation conferences, though his practical concern had narrowed to orders such as a guard in case a Russian tank dug him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After his suicide his body and Eva Braun’s were carried up the emergency stairs, laid a few feet from the porch, soaked in petrol and set alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bunker’s last occupants then broke up in suicide or flight, and a month later Stalin told an American envoy that the talk of funerals and burials struck him as very dubious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["H. R. Trevor-Roper, <i>The Last Days of Hitler</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1947), 106–09, 199–203, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.85969. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 481, 490–92, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 21 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 26 June 1946, testimony of Hans Fritzsche, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-26-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum by the Assistant to the Secretary of State (Bohlen), May 26, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 1, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d24. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler stay in the bunker when the others left Berlin?","a":"Because he had made Berlin the last stand of his war. The official history describes him narrowing the whole conflict down to the city, and while Göring, Himmler, Ribbentrop, Dönitz and Speer chose to leave, he stayed underground with Goebbels and Bormann still hoping for a turn of fate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did life in the bunker feel cut off from the battle?","a":"Because it was built to be. Several metres of earth and concrete and the constant noise of the ventilation shut out most sight and sound, so that the daily conferences went on as if armies could still be moved, until shells close by shook the rooms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the bodies burned in the garden rather than hidden?","a":"Because the emergency exit opened straight onto the garden and there was nowhere else to go. Petrol was fetched, the bodies were carried up the four flights of stairs, laid near the porch and set alight while Soviet shells fell around the mourners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-V04744%2C_Berlin%2C_Garten_der_zerst%C3%B6rte_Reichskanzlei.jpg","title":"The bunker’s garden exit, July 1947","desc":"The garden of the ruined Reich Chancellery in July 1947, with the entrance block of the Führerbunker on the left and a conical concrete shelter for the guard in the middle, before the site was blown up.","credit":"Photographer unknown, ADN-Zentralbild, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-V04744, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-V04744,_Berlin,_Garten_der_zerstörte_Reichskanzlei.jpg","alt":"A squat concrete block and a cone-roofed concrete tower stand in a weedy yard in front of a gutted building."},"wiki":"Führerbunker"},
    "ww2-384": {"questions":["Asked by reporters on 2 May 1945, Truman confirmed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the best authority possible, though he admitted that nobody yet knew how it had happened.","Stalin told Harry Hopkins in May 1945 that he doubted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had happened at all, suspecting an escape by submarine to Japan."],"abstract":"The <b>death of Adolf Hitler</b> came by suicide in his bunker beneath the Reich Chancellery on the afternoon of 30 April 1945, with Soviet troops a few hundred metres away storming the Reichstag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The night before he had married Eva Braun and dictated his personal and political testaments, naming Grand Admiral Dönitz as his successor at the head of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had just been told that the last attempt to relieve Berlin, by General Wenck’s Twelfth Army, had stopped short and could not go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After a farewell to his staff he and Eva Braun withdrew to their suite, where a single shot was heard, and they were found dead on a sofa, he having shot himself and she having taken poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their bodies were carried up to the garden, soaked in petrol and set alight, and his driver later told the Nuremberg tribunal that he had carried out Eva Braun and seen Hitler’s body wrapped in a blanket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> How the world learned of it was managed, for Bormann first told Dönitz only that he was the successor and kept back the fact of the death for a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Dönitz announced it on the evening of 1 May, he said that Hitler had fallen that afternoon fighting at the head of his troops, and both statements were untrue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 2 May Truman told reporters he had it on the best authority possible that Hitler was dead, but that how he died was not yet known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stalin was less sure, telling an American envoy in Moscow late in May that in his opinion Hitler was not dead but hiding, perhaps gone by submarine to Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Nuremberg his driver put the hour between 2:00 and 3:00 PM where others said 3:30 PM, and Hans Fritzsche, who had stayed in Berlin, said only that Hitler and his entourage had taken the way of suicide or fled toward the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["H. R. Trevor-Roper, <i>The Last Days of Hitler</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1947), 199–203, 211–12, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.85969. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 491–94, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 3 July 1946, testimony of Erich Kempka, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-03-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “The President’s News Conference,” 2 May 1945, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/the-presidents-news-conference-651. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum by the Assistant to the Secretary of State (Bohlen), May 26, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 1, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d24. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 26 June 1946, testimony of Hans Fritzsche, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-26-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Dönitz say Hitler died fighting?","a":"Because a death in battle suited the regime better than a suicide in a shelter. Dönitz had never been told how Hitler died, so any account he gave was speculation, and he chose the heroic one, broadcast on 1 May with Wagner and Bruckner in the background.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin doubt that Hitler was dead?","a":"Because nothing he had seen settled it. He told Harry Hopkins that Soviet doctors thought they had identified Goebbels’s body, but that the talk of funerals and burials struck him as very dubious, and that Hitler, Bormann and others might have escaped by submarine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the death kept back for a day?","a":"Because Bormann wanted to keep his own position. He told Dönitz that he was the successor without saying that Hitler was dead, treating the news as a card he was not yet ready to play while he tried to negotiate with the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/American_soldiers_read_of_Hitler%27s_death_in_an_%22extra%22_edition_of_%22Stars_and_Stripes.%22.jpg","title":"Reading the news, 2 May 1945","desc":"Four American soldiers read an extra edition of the army newspaper <i>Stars and Stripes</i> announcing Hitler’s death.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps, National Archives 111-SC-204386, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_soldiers_read_of_Hitler%27s_death_in_an_%22extra%22_edition_of_%22Stars_and_Stripes.%22.jpg","alt":"Four soldiers in helmets and caps sit side by side, each holding up a newspaper whose front page reads HITLER DEAD."},"wiki":"Death of Adolf Hitler"},
    "ww2-385": {"questions":["A looted wristwatch was retouched from the soldier’s arm in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and clouds of smoke were later added to the picture to heighten its drama.","Taken on 2 May 1945, 2 days after Red Army soldiers first hoisted a red flag on the building, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the Soviet image of the victory over Nazi Germany."],"abstract":"<b>Raising a Flag over the Reichstag</b> is the photograph Yevgeny Khaldei took on the roof of the Reichstag in Berlin early on 2 May 1945, showing a Red Army soldier fixing the Soviet flag above the ruined city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Khaldei, born in 1917 into a Jewish family in Donetsk in Ukraine, had worked for the TASS news agency since 1936 and followed the Red Army to Berlin as a war photographer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his own account a young soldier offered to climb onto the roof with the red flag Khaldei carried, they found a long pole, and he used a whole film of 36 exposures to get something of Berlin into the picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He flew to Moscow that night and the picture was published at once, becoming the image of the victory in every Soviet newspaper and newsreel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The building had been a charred ruin since 1933 and meant little in Nazi Germany itself, but to the Russians it was the symbol of the Third Reich and the end point of their war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> It was not the first flag on the building, for Red Army soldiers had hoisted a red flag on its roof on 30 April after fighting French Waffen-SS volunteers of the Charlemagne unit who defended it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the street fighting each Soviet objective had a number and a red flag marked its capture, and several groups carried flags towards the Reichstag to make sure that one of them arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The building was not finally taken until 2 May, and the newsreel scenes of the flag-raising shown in many documentaries were restaged in the days that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Khaldei had re-enacted the moment and then retouched his picture, removing a looted wristwatch from the soldier’s arm, and later added clouds of smoke to heighten the drama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked again and again whether it was faked or staged, he always gave the same answer: that it was a good photo, and next question, please.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Deutscher Bundestag, “Gregor Gaida: Reichstagsfiale,” <i>Artothek: Die Kunstsammlung des Deutschen Bundestages</i>, https://www.bundestag.de/besuche/kunst/artothek/gaida-gregor-1012598. [in German] [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Rotarmisten mit sowjetischer Fahne auf dem Reichstag,” photograph by Jewgeni Chaldej, inv. no. F 60/2052, <i>LeMO</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/bestand/objekt/rotarmisten-auf-dem-reichstag-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Ernst Volland, “»Drei Juden haben Berlin erobert«,” <i>Jüdische Allgemeine</i>, 28 March 2011, https://www.juedische-allgemeine.de/kultur/drei-juden-haben-berlin-erobert. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 488, 492, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Deutscher Bundestag, “Das Reichstagsgebäude und seine historischen Orte,” https://www.bundestag.de/besuche/architektur/reichstag/geschichte/orte. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Deutscher Bundestag, “Geschichte des Gebäudes,” <i>Das Reichstagsgebäude</i>, https://www.bundestag.de/besuche/architektur/reichstag/geschichte. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the moment have to be staged?","a":"Because the real capture could not be photographed as it happened. Several groups had carried flags towards the building and the fighting for it went on until 2 May, so Khaldei arrived that morning with a flag of his own and re-enacted the scene on the roof with a young soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a wristwatch removed from the picture?","a":"Because the watches on the soldier’s wrist were loot. A hero of the victory could not be shown wearing plundered property, so Khaldei retouched the photograph before it went round the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Reichstag matter so much to the Red Army?","a":"Because it had become the goal of the whole war in Soviet propaganda. Posters first showed the Brandenburg Gate as the target and then the Reichstag, a building that had been a ruin since 1933 but stood, for the Russians, as the symbol of the Third Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Raising_a_flag_over_the_Reichstag.jpg","title":"Raising a Flag over the Reichstag, 2 May 1945","desc":"The published version of Khaldei’s photograph, smoke included, as it appeared in the Soviet magazine <i>Ogoniok</i> on 13 May 1945.","credit":"Photograph by Yevgeny Khaldei, TASS, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raising_a_flag_over_the_Reichstag.jpg","alt":"A soldier crouching on a high stone parapet fixes a large hammer-and-sickle flag above a ruined city, with two more figures on the roof behind him and smoke over the streets."},"wiki":"Raising a Flag over the Reichstag"},
    "ww2-386": {"questions":["A long posting in Moscow before the war may explain why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, acting chief of the army’s general staff, was sent to negotiate with the Red Army after Hitler’s suicide.","Returning to the bunker at about noon on 1 May, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought back a Soviet demand for capitulation that Goebbels refused, so the city’s surrender was left to its commandant."],"abstract":"<b>Hans Krebs</b> was the German general who, as acting Chief of Staff of the Army High Command, crossed the Soviet lines in Berlin after Hitler’s suicide to seek an armistice, opening the surrender of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler gave him the post in March 1945 when he sent Guderian on sick leave, and Krebs was known for unquenchable optimism and a chameleon-like ability to adopt his superiors’ views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Captured generals called him able but time-serving, and his long service in Moscow before the war may explain why he was chosen for the last mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He stayed with Hitler in the bunker through the battle and took part in the conference of 30 April that planned an approach to the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At midnight he set out with a letter from Goebbels and Bormann that announced Hitler’s death and authorised him to negotiate an armistice or truce pending the decision of the new head of state, Admiral Dönitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was taken to the command post of Chuikov’s 8th Guards Army, where a front commander later arrived, probably with instructions from Moscow, to give him the answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When he returned to the bunker at about noon on 1 May the reply demanded capitulation and the delivery of everyone in the bunker, and Goebbels insisted that in keeping with Hitler’s wishes there would be none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hans Fritzsche of the propaganda ministry then offered the city’s surrender to Zhukov, and General Burgdorf came to shoot him for it under Hitler’s order, but the capitulation went ahead under the military commander’s signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At 5:00 AM on 2 May the defence commandant crossed the lines and in the Tempelhof house that served Chuikov as a command post signed the order to the German troops in Berlin to stop fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Krebs and Burgdorf had said that they meant to shoot themselves and stayed behind in the Chancellery, and Stalin was still telling an American envoy weeks later that he thought Krebs had probably escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 464–65, 480, 493–94, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","H. R. Trevor-Roper, <i>The Last Days of Hitler</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1947), 110, 209–11, 215, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.85969. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 26 June 1946, testimony of Hans Fritzsche, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-26-46.asp. [Open access]","Bezirksamt Tempelhof-Schöneberg von Berlin, “Gartenstadt Neu-Tempelhof: Kiezspaziergang vom 16.05.2015,” Berlin.de, https://www.berlin.de/ba-tempelhof-schoeneberg/ueber-den-bezirk/spaziergaenge/2015/kiezspaziergang_05_2015-315782.php. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Schlacht um Berlin 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 May 2020, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/schlacht-um-berlin-1945. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum by the Assistant to the Secretary of State (Bohlen), May 26, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 1, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d24. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels and Bormann send Krebs to the Russians?","a":"Because they hoped the Soviet command would recognise them as the government Hitler’s will had named. Krebs had served in Moscow before the war, and his letter asked only for an armistice or truce until Dönitz decided, not for surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Krebs’s mission fail?","a":"Because the Soviet side would not bargain. The answer he brought back at noon on 1 May demanded capitulation and the handing over of everyone in the bunker, and Goebbels, who meant to follow Hitler in death, refused it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin think Krebs might still be alive?","a":"Because the bodies had not settled the question for him. He told Harry Hopkins late in May that the talk of funerals and burials struck him as very dubious and that Bormann, Goebbels, Hitler and probably Krebs had escaped and were in hiding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Goebbels_letter_to_the_Soviet_Supreme_Command_%281%29.jpg","title":"Zhukov reports Krebs’s mission, 1 May 1945","desc":"The first page of Zhukov’s message to Stalin of 1 May 1945, reporting that General Krebs had come to the sector of the 8th Guards Army and handed Chuikov a statement from Goebbels and Bormann announcing Hitler’s suicide.","credit":"Soviet document, via ria.ru, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goebbels_letter_to_the_Soviet_Supreme_Command_(1).jpg","alt":"A typed Russian document headed with an address to Stalin, containing a quoted statement that ends with the names Goebbels and Bormann."},"wiki":"Hans Krebs (Wehrmacht general)"},
    "ww2-387": {"questions":["Keitel, Friedeburg and Stumpff put their names to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before Zhukov and Tedder at Karlshorst, just after midnight on a document dated 8 May 1945.","Calling itself a purely military act, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left Germany’s political fate to the declaration of 5 June 1945, by which the four Allied commands assumed supreme authority."],"abstract":"The <b>German Instrument of Surrender</b> was the act by which the German High Command surrendered all German forces unconditionally to the Western Allies and the Soviet Union, signed at Reims on 7 May 1945 and again in Berlin on 8 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It covered all forces on land, at sea and in the air under German control, which were to cease operations at 23.01 Central European Time on 8 May and stay where they stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No ship or aircraft was to be scuttled or damaged, and any failure to comply would bring whatever punitive action the Allied commands thought fit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The act called itself a military surrender, without prejudice to any general instrument that the United Nations might later impose on Germany and its armed forces as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That came on 5 June 1945, when the four Allied commands declared that they assumed supreme authority, since no central government was left to answer for Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Dönitz sent Jodl to Eisenhower’s headquarters at Reims hoping for a phased capitulation, and American officers judged that the Germans mainly wanted to save as many troops as possible from the Russians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Eisenhower insisted on an immediate and total surrender, and at 2:41 AM on 7 May Jodl signed it, with General Bedell Smith, the Soviet General Susloparov and the French General Sevez signing as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stalin would accept a general surrender only if it was signed before the Red Army’s supreme commander, so it was repeated at the Soviet headquarters in Berlin-Karlshorst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There Keitel for the High Command and the army, Friedeburg for the navy and Stumpff for the air force signed before Zhukov and Air Chief Marshal Tedder, with de Lattre de Tassigny and Spaatz as witnesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Arguments over the text and missing lines in the Russian version held it up until after midnight, so the document dated 8 May was signed early on 9 May, in a hall that survives as the heart of a museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Act of Military Surrender Signed at Rheims at 0241 on the 7th Day of May, 1945,” 59 Stat. 1857, in Charles I. Bevans, comp., <i>Treaties and Other International Agreements of the United States of America 1776–1949</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gs3.asp. [Open access]","“Act of Military Surrender Signed at Berlin on the 8th Day of May, 1945,” 59 Stat. 1857, in Charles I. Bevans, comp., <i>Treaties and Other International Agreements of the United States of America 1776–1949</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gs11.asp. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 496–98, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Museum Berlin-Karlshorst, “Historischer Ort,” https://www.museum-karlshorst.de/ueber-uns/historischer-ort/. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, “Victory in Europe,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1945, vol. 136, cols. 159–60, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1945/may/08/victory-in-europe. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in France (Caffery) to the Secretary of State, May 6, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d565. [Open access]","“Declaration Regarding the Defeat of Germany and the Assumption of Supreme Authority by Allied Powers; June 5, 1945,” Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger01.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the surrender signed twice?","a":"Because Stalin would not accept a surrender signed in a Western headquarters with only one Soviet general, Susloparov, signing for his High Command. He insisted that the German commanders sign again before the Red Army’s supreme commander, which gave the Soviet contribution its due and put the heads of all three German services on the document.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Berlin document bear a date it was not signed on?","a":"Because the ceremony ran late. It had been meant for the afternoon of 8 May, but arguments over changes to the text and missing lines in the Russian version delayed the signatures until shortly after midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the act call itself only a military surrender?","a":"Because it dealt with armed forces, not with the German state. Its text left room for a general instrument to supersede it, and that role fell to the Allied declaration of 5 June, which assumed supreme authority over a country with no government left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Field_Marshall_Keitel_signs_German_surrender_terms_in_Berlin_8_May_1945.jpg","title":"Keitel signs in Berlin, 8 May 1945","desc":"Field Marshal Wilhelm Keitel signing the surrender at the Soviet headquarters in Karlshorst, his marshal’s baton and cap on the table in front of him.","credit":"Photograph by Lt. Moore, U.S. Army, National Archives 111-SC-206292, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Field_Marshall_Keitel_signs_German_surrender_terms_in_Berlin_8_May_1945.jpg","alt":"An elderly officer seated at a table signs papers with a pen, a baton and a peaked cap lying beside him while men stand behind."},"wiki":"German Instrument of Surrender"},
    "ww2-388": {"questions":["Truman broadcast on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the flags of freedom flew over all Europe, but warned his listeners that the victory was only half won while Japan still fought.","Because Stalin’s request for a delay reached the White House too late, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell on 8 May in the West while Moscow announced its victory the following evening."],"abstract":"<b>Victory in Europe Day</b> was 8 May 1945, the day Britain, the United States and their allies celebrated the end of the war against Germany, while the Soviet Union marked its own victory a day later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The surrender Jodl had signed at Reims ordered all German forces to cease active operations at 23.01 Central European Time on 8 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Churchill’s statement to Parliament that afternoon announced that hostilities would end officially at one minute after midnight, and that this should not stop the country celebrating that day and the next as Victory-in-Europe Days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Washington Truman broadcast that the flags of freedom flew over all Europe, warned that the victory was only half won while Japan fought on, and proclaimed Sunday 13 May a day of prayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In defeated Germany the formal end drew little interest, since most Germans had seen the war end when Allied troops occupied their home towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The three leaders had agreed to announce the surrender together at nine in the morning Washington time on 8 May, but Churchill wanted to speak at once, and Admiral Leahy complained that the situation on the announcement was terribly confused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stalin asked Truman to wait, writing that German resistance in the east was not slackening and that an early announcement might mislead the Soviet public, and he proposed seven in the evening, Moscow time, on 9 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His message reached the White House at one in the morning of 8 May, by which time preparations had gone too far, and Truman made his announcement that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The American chargé in Moscow, George Kennan, had already noticed how quietly the Soviet press treated the German surrenders in the West, and thought the Kremlin wished to wait until it could credit the end chiefly to the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The final act was signed at Karlshorst in the early hours of 9 May, and that evening Stalin broadcast from Moscow that the great day of victory over Germany had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Lords, “Victory in Europe,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 May 1945, vol. 136, cols. 159–60, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1945/may/08/victory-in-europe. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Broadcast to the American People Announcing the Surrender of Germany,” 8 May 1945, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/broadcast-the-american-people-announcing-the-surrender-germany. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Proclamation by the President of the United States of America, May 8, 1945,” Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gs8.asp. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, “Victory Speech,” broadcast from Moscow, 9 May 1945, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1945/05/09v.htm. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of Telephone Conversation, by the Acting Secretary of State, May 7, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d566. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Marshal Stalin to President Truman, May 7, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d567. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kennan) to the Secretary of State, May 7, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission, Austria, Germany</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d568. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","“Act of Military Surrender Signed at Rheims at 0241 on the 7th Day of May, 1945,” 59 Stat. 1857, in Charles I. Bevans, comp., <i>Treaties and Other International Agreements of the United States of America 1776–1949</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gs3.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Russia mark the victory a day later than the West?","a":"Because Stalin did not want to announce the surrender before it had taken effect on his front. He asked Truman to wait until the evening of 9 May, the Berlin act was signed only after midnight, and he broadcast the victory that evening, so the Soviet date became 9 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the announcement so confused?","a":"Because the three governments were trying to speak at the same moment. They had agreed on nine in the morning Washington time, but Churchill pressed to announce at once, Stalin wanted to wait, and Admiral Leahy told the State Department that nobody had any control over de Gaulle either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Truman sound so sober on a day of celebration?","a":"Because the war was only half over for the United States. He spoke of the terrible price paid to rid the world of Hitler, of the homes in mourning, and of the Japanese divisions still to be defeated, and his watchword for the months ahead was work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Winston_Churchill_waves_to_crowds_in_Whitehall_in_London_as_they_celebrate_VE_Day%2C_8_May_1945._H41849.jpg","title":"Churchill in Whitehall on VE Day","desc":"Churchill gives the V sign to the crowds from the balcony of the Ministry of Health in Whitehall on 8 May 1945, the day he broadcast the end of the war with Germany.","credit":"Photograph by Major W. G. Horton, War Office official photographer, Imperial War Museum H 41849, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winston_Churchill_waves_to_crowds_in_Whitehall_in_London_as_they_celebrate_VE_Day,_8_May_1945._H41849.jpg","alt":"From a balcony a man raises two fingers towards a street packed with a vast crowd, two men in suits beside him."},"wiki":"Victory in Europe Day"},
    "ww2-389": {"questions":["Created by decree in November 1942 with Shvernik as president and Burdenko, Tolstoy and Lysenko among its members, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> registered German crimes and damage on occupied Soviet soil.","A report of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> blaming the Katyn killings on the Germans went before the Nuremberg tribunal, which heard witnesses on it and then left Katyn out of its judgment."],"abstract":"The <b>Extraordinary State Commission</b> was the body the Soviet Union created in November 1942 to record the crimes of the German invaders and their allies on occupied Soviet territory and to reckon the damage they had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its decree charged it with registering the murder of peaceful citizens and the abduction of Soviet people into German slavery, as well as the destruction of homes, collective farms, factories, railways, museums, schools and places of worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members, with Nikolai Shvernik as president, included the surgeon Burdenko, the writer Alexei Tolstoy, the historian Tarle, the agronomist Lysenko and Nikolai, Metropolitan of Kiev and Galich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its local commissions questioned eyewitnesses and compiled victim lists, protocols, photographs and maps of the sites of atrocities and graves, records now held by the State Archive of the Russian Federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Selected files on crimes against persons, including many lists of Holocaust victims, have been copied for the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, where they fill close to 700,000 digital images.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Nuremberg the Soviet chief prosecutor quoted its totals for the occupied lands: 1,710 towns and more than 70,000 villages destroyed in whole or part, over 6 million buildings burned and some 25 million people left homeless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He added 31,850 industrial establishments destroyed, 98,000 collective farms looted, 84,000 schools and colleges ruined, and damage estimated at 679,000 million roubles at the government prices of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 2 weeks later its general conclusions were read into the record as Exhibit USSR-35, presented in place of the separate documents on which they rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its special commission on Katyn, under Burdenko, announced in January 1944 that the Germans had shot the Polish officers, and the tribunal heard witnesses on the charge in July 1946 but left Katyn out of its judgment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1990 the Soviet news agency itself acknowledged, on the basis of newly opened archives, that Beria, Merkulov and their subordinates bore direct responsibility, so on Katyn the commission’s verdict had been false.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the USSR, “Decree on the Formation of an Extraordinary State Committee for the Ascertaining and Investigation of Crimes Committed by the German-Fascist Invaders and Their Associates,” November 1942, <i>Information Bulletin</i>, Embassy of the USSR, 10 November 1942, ibiblio, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/policy/1942/421101a.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 8 February 1946, opening statement of R. A. Rudenko, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-08-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 8, proceedings of 22 February 1946, Soviet prosecution, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-22-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Extraordinary State Commission to Investigate German-Fascist Crimes Committed on Soviet Territory from the USSR,” RG-22.002M, collections catalogue, https://collections.ushmm.org/search/catalog/irn501971. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 1 July 1946, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-01-46.asp. [Open access]","European Court of Human Rights, Grand Chamber, <i>Janowiec and Others v. Russia</i>, applications nos. 55508/07 and 29520/09, judgment of 21 October 2013, HUDOC, https://hudoc.echr.coe.int/eng?i=001-127684. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union count its losses in the middle of the war?","a":"Because it meant to make Germany pay and to punish the guilty. The decree of 1942 set the commission to establish the compensation owed to citizens and to the state, and to identify wherever possible the individual German criminals so that they could be prosecuted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must its findings be read with care?","a":"Because it was an arm of a state with crimes of its own to hide. Its Katyn report blamed the Germans for a massacre that Soviet authorities themselves admitted in 1990 had been carried out on the orders of Beria and his subordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians of the Holocaust use its records?","a":"Because they hold evidence gathered close to the events. Local commissions took eyewitness statements and drew up lists of victims and maps of graves, and the files copied for the Holocaust Memorial Museum include many name lists of Jewish victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Site_of_Janowska_Nazi_Camp_-_Lviv%2C_1944.jpg","title":"Investigating a mass grave at Janowska, 1944","desc":"Soviet investigators of the Extraordinary State Commission at the site of the Janowska camp and its mass graves in Lviv in 1944.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Soviet archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Site_of_Janowska_Nazi_Camp_-_Lviv,_1944.jpg","alt":"Soldiers and civilians stand among dug-up trenches on a hillside, with bodies laid out on the ground."},"wiki":"Extraordinary State Commission","places":["city:1159151585"]},
    "ww2-390": {"questions":["Demographers reckon the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as excess deaths, subtracting from those alive in mid-1941 who did not survive the war the people who would have died anyway.","Endorsed at a Russian parliamentary hearing in 2017, a figure of 42 million for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was examined and found without foundation, leaving the older estimate standing."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet war dead</b> of the Second World War numbered, on the estimate most widely accepted, 26 to 27 million people, about one in eight of the pre-war population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They include soldiers killed in battle and civilians murdered under occupation, but also millions whose lives were cut short by hunger, disease and exhaustion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among them were some 3.3 million of the roughly 5.7 million Soviet soldiers taken prisoner by the Germans, who died in captivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stalin told <i>Pravda</i> in 1946 that the Soviet Union had lost about seven million people, a figure conveniently just below German losses, and Khrushchev later raised it to 20 million, another round number rather than a count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only under Gorbachev was a full audit commissioned from the Soviet statistical agency, whose demographers put the loss at 26 to 27 million in work published in 1990 and 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Their method counts excess deaths: those alive in mid-1941 who did not survive the war, less those who would have died anyway, plus the excess among wartime babies, about 26.6 million in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sum rests on censuses 20 years apart, in 1939 and 1959, on imperfect wartime registration and on assumptions about emigration, and the method gives a lower bound that may understate real war deaths by about 1.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Military deaths are counted separately, and the 8.7 million found by a Russian military commission in 1993 compare with the more than 12 million estimated in the American official history of the Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Russian demographer has argued that the commission undercounted the prisoners who died in German hands by half or more, while other writers in Russia have offered totals from about 20 million to 42 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The figure of 42 million was endorsed at a Russian parliamentary hearing in 2017, and close examination of its sources found it without foundation, leaving 26 to 27 million as the best figure on existing knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Harrison, “Counting the Soviet Union’s War Dead: Still 26–27 Million,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 71, no. 6 (2019): 1036–47, accepted manuscript, https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/98747/. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, “Interview to ‘Pravda’ Correspondent Concerning Mr. Winston Churchill’s Speech at Fulton,” March 1946, in <i>J. V. Stalin on Post-War International Relations</i> (London: Soviet News, 1947), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1946/03/x01.htm. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Counting Soviet Deaths in the Great Patriotic War: Comment,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 55, no. 6 (2003): 939–44, postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/eas03a_postprint.pdf. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series, CMH Pub 30-5-1 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 500, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Sergei Maksudov, “Otsenka voennykh poter’: poiski istiny, nevezhestvo ili predvziatost’?” <i>Demograficheskoe obozrenie</i> 4, no. 3 (2017): 141–52, https://demreview.hse.ru/article/download/7320/8192. [in Russian] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin give so low a figure?","a":"Because he did not wish to disclose the real scale of the losses. He announced about seven million, a round number just below German losses, while still insisting that Soviet losses were several times those of Britain and the United States together; the true number stayed secret until the archives opened at the end of the 1980s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the dead not simply be added up?","a":"Because most of them were never recorded one by one. Deaths from hunger, disease and exhaustion cannot be told apart from peacetime deaths, so the war’s toll has to be estimated as the excess over what would have happened without it, using censuses taken 20 years apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the estimate of 42 million rejected?","a":"Because the extra deaths had to come from somewhere in the population accounts. It needed millions more people alive in 1941 or far fewer normal deaths, and its key figure for the 1941 population turned out to be a planning document read the wrong way, so the earlier estimate of 26 to 27 million stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/0112Cb._Piskarevskoye_Memorial_Cemetery._Mass_graves_of_1942-1943.jpg","title":"Mass graves of 1942–1943, Piskaryovskoye","desc":"A grass-covered mass grave of the siege years at the Piskaryovskoye Memorial Cemetery in Saint Petersburg, marked by a stone with a star and its year, photographed in May 2023.","credit":"Photograph by Aleksandrovy AG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0112Cb._Piskarevskoye_Memorial_Cemetery._Mass_graves_of_1942-1943.jpg","alt":"A long low grassy mound edged with stone, a small fir tree and a stone marker with a star in front, birch trees behind."},"wiki":"World War II casualties of the Soviet Union"},
    "ww2-341": {"questions":["Only in the north, behind the Narva River and Lake Peipus, had the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually begun to look like a fortified line, with thousands of bunkers built by late 1943.","Manstein told the Nuremberg tribunal that Hitler had forbidden fortifying the Dnieper, so the southern half of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was barely begun when his armies reached the river in 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Panther–Wotan line</b> was the defensive line Hitler ordered on 12 August 1943, to run from the Kerch peninsula along the Dnieper and then north past Gomel, Vitebsk and Pskov to the Gulf of Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mussolini and the army chief of staff Zeitzler had proposed such an East Wall that spring, but Hitler had refused, claiming that a line in the rear would undermine the troops’ will to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since the words East Wall might prove dangerous for a line that did not yet exist, the army soon gave it two code names, Wotan in the south and Panther in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Wotan sector ran along the Molochnaya River, covering Nikopol and the railway through Melitopol that supplied the German forces in the Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Panther sector lay behind the Narva River and Lakes Peipus and Pskov, and there, unlike most of the line, building had really begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Soviet advance turned the line into a race: on 8 September, at Zaporozhye, Hitler approved in principle a withdrawal to the Wotan position, and the retreat to the Dnieper began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Manstein later testified at Nuremberg that the Dnieper had not been fortified because Hitler had forbidden it, and that the work had only just begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The last units of Army Group Centre entered the Panther position on 2 October, after the armies in the south and centre had been driven back an average of about 240 kilometres (150 miles) in 2.5 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Red Army did not stop there: by November it held the western bank of the Dnieper between Gomel and Zaporozhye, and it had broken the Wotan sector at Melitopol on 23 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only in the north did the line serve its purpose, when Army Group North fell back to it early in 1944 and held the front from Narva to Pskov until the summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 121–22, 153–55, 164–72, 248–49, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1943,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1943. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 10 August 1946, testimony of Erich von Manstein, 612–13, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-10-46.asp. [Open access]","Vladimir S. Milbakh and Viktor A. Chernukhin, “The Artillery of the Red Army in the Leningrad–Novgorod Strategic Offensive Operation, 14 January – 1 March 1944,” <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-artilleriya-bila-po-samym-transheyam-liniyam-svyazi-my-byli-lisheny-upravleniya-i-nesli-bolshie-poteri. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey Ya. Lavrenov, “The Liberation of Crimea: The Third Strategic Blow of the Red Army,” <i>Obozrevatel – Observer</i>, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osvobozhdenie-kryma-tretiy-strategicheskiy-udar-krasnoy-armii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1943/44,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-194344. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had Hitler refused to build an East Wall before August 1943?","a":"Because he believed a fortified line in the rear would undermine the troops’ will to fight, and he still hoped to advance east again once Soviet strength declined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the army give the line two harmless code names?","a":"Because calling the southern half an East Wall was thought psychologically dangerous when it might have to be occupied before any work on it could begin; Wotan and Panther gave nothing away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Panther sector hold when the Wotan sector did not?","a":"Because in the north the line had natural obstacles, the Narva River and the lakes, and real building had begun there in 1943, while along the Dnieper and the Molochnaya the work had barely started when the retreating armies arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Hitlerdnieper.jpg","title":"German gunners on the Dnieper, 1943","desc":"A German gun crew digging in on the bank of the Dnieper during the fighting on the lower river in 1943, a photograph now in the State Archive of the Russian Federation.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitlerdnieper.jpg","alt":"German soldiers in steel helmets dig in around a gun on a scrubby riverbank, with a wide river and a low far shore behind them."},"wiki":"Panther–Wotan line"},
    "ww2-342": {"questions":["Offered the title Hero of the Soviet Union for crossing under fire, about 2,000 soldiers or more won it in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1943, more than in any other operation.","Manstein told the Nuremberg tribunal that if the Soviet armies could not be halted at the river the war was lost, which is what made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> decisive in the autumn of 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Dnieper</b> was the series of Soviet offensives of August to December 1943 that freed Ukraine east of the river and won bridgeheads on its western bank from Gomel to Zaporozhye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One Russian count gives the Soviet side 2,633,000 men against 1,240,000 Germans and their allies, and four times as many guns, though the tanks were nearly equal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The river was largely unfortified, and Manstein later testified that Hitler had forbidden work on it, though unless the Soviet armies were halted there the war was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Stavka ordered its fronts to cross on the run, and between 22 and 30 September they reached the river on a front of 750 kilometres (470 miles) and seized 23 bridgeheads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stalin boasted on 6 November that four great rivers had been forced in three summer months, and in February 1944 he claimed the German defences broken from Zhlobin to Kherson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Near Bukrin, south-east of Kiev, four soldiers crossed in a rowboat after dark on 22 September, and by daylight their company held the top of the high western bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind them the infantry crossed on whatever would float, timbers, fuel drums, wooden doors and straw wrapped in ponchos, while engineers built causeways for the heavy equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To widen the Bukrin bridgehead the Stavka dropped paratroops on the night of 24 September, but only 4,575 were dropped, they were scattered far and wide, and more than 60 per cent of the force never returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The title Hero of the Soviet Union, offered for the crossings, was awarded on an unprecedented scale: about 2,000 times by the US Army’s count and 2,438 times by a Russian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The price was heavy: in the Chernigov–Poltava operation alone, one of the opening phases, Soviet losses came to 428,000 men, 103,000 of them killed, missing or captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 165–72, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet Airborne Experience</i>, Research Survey 4 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1984), 91–108, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA153124. [Open access]","Sergey M. Monin, “Turning Point in the Great Patriotic War,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO-Universiteta</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/korennoy-perelom-v-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyne. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1943,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1943. [in German] [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Speech Delivered at the Joint Celebration Meeting, November 6, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 113–14, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 10 August 1946, testimony of Erich von Manstein, 612–13, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-10-46.asp. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day No. 16, February 23, 1944,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 139, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Stavka order its armies to cross the Dnieper on the run?","a":"Because it feared that if the Germans were given time to dig in on the far bank the front might settle into a stalemate, so speed mattered more than preparation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the airborne drop at Bukrin go so badly?","a":"Because fuel shortages and confusion at the airfields left about 30 per cent of the force behind, the drop was made at night and scattered the paratroopers widely, and German troops had just moved into the landing area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union hand out so many Hero of the Soviet Union titles for this battle?","a":"Because the title, with its pension and public honour, was offered in advance to men who distinguished themselves in crossing the river under fire, as a way of keeping the armies moving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%8E%D1%89%D0%B8%D1%85_%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B7_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%94%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80.jpg","title":"Red Army units crossing the Dnieper, 1943","desc":"Soldiers ferrying a field gun across the Dnieper on a raft on the Kiev axis in 1943, photographed by Yefim Kopyt.","credit":"Photograph by Yefim Kopyt, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B2%D1%83%D1%8E%D1%89%D0%B8%D1%85_%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D1%87%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B7_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%94%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BF%D1%80.jpg","alt":"A line of soldiers stands on a low wooden raft on a broad river, pushing a small field gun on wheels towards the bank."},"wiki":"Battle of the Dnieper"},
    "ww2-343": {"questions":["The killings at Babi Yar were stopped only days before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended German rule in the Ukrainian capital on 6 November 1943, after more than 2 years of occupation.","Twice in October 1943 the armies in the Bukrin bridgehead failed to break out, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was launched instead from Lyutezh, north of the city, in early November."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Battle of Kiev</b> was the Soviet offensive of November 1943 that retook the capital of Soviet Ukraine, today’s Kyiv, which German troops had held since September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Soviet reckoning it was the Kiev strategic offensive of 3 to 13 November, followed by a defensive battle until 22 December in which Manstein retook Zhitomir but could not reach the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the autumn’s Dnieper crossings, which had left Soviet bridgeheads at Bukrin to the south of the city and at Lyutezh and Yasnogorodka to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kiev was the third largest Soviet city, and Stalin hailed its capture in his order of the day on 7 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before leaving, the Germans had made about 300 prisoners from the nearby Syrets camp dig up and burn the bodies at Babi Yar, the ravine where Kiev’s Jews had been shot in September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Twice in October armies attacking out of the Bukrin bridgehead failed, because they lacked the bridging to bring heavy artillery across and could not see far enough to direct its fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Stavka then ordered Vatutin to shift the Third Guards Tank Army north to Lyutezh, a secret night march that crossed the Dnieper twice while the troops left at Bukrin feigned an attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 3 November, after a massive bombardment, six rifle divisions and a tank corps broke the German line around Lyutezh, and within 2 days the front there had collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the night of 5 November the fighting swept through the streets of Kiev, and the next morning the last Germans retreated south, the killings at Babi Yar having been halted only days before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within a week the offensive had pushed about 150 kilometres (93 miles) west to Zhitomir, and on 24 December the Soviet winter offensive opened from the Kiev area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 184–86, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Sergey M. Monin, “Turning Point in the Great Patriotic War,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO-Universiteta</i>, no. 2 (2010), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/korennoy-perelom-v-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyne. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1943,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1943. [in German] [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Order of the Day No. 309, November 7, 1943,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 133, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Kiev and Babi Yar,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kiev-and-babi-yar. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1943/44,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-194344. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the breakout attempts from the Bukrin bridgehead fail?","a":"Because the Soviet armies there lacked the bridging material to bring heavy artillery across, and the steep banks limited observation so badly that guns on the eastern bank could not fire accurately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the tank army moved to Lyutezh in secret?","a":"Because surprise was the point: the German defence north of the city proved weaker, and the march was made by night while the troops left at Bukrin kept up a feigned attack to hold the Germans’ attention there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans dig up the graves at Babi Yar in 1943?","a":"Because the Red Army was approaching, and they wanted to hide the mass murders, so prisoners from the Syrets camp were forced to exhume and burn the bodies; most were then killed, but a few escaped and testified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Kiev_Kreschatik_after_liberation_November_1943.jpg","title":"Soviet troops on the Khreshchatyk, November 1943","desc":"Soviet infantry marching along Kiev’s main street, the Khreshchatyk, after the city was retaken in November 1943, photographed by Arkady Shaikhet.","credit":"Photograph by Arkady Shaikhet, public domain in Ukraine, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kiev_Kreschatik_after_liberation_November_1943.jpg","alt":"A column of soldiers with rifles and greatcoats walks along a wet street past the gutted shells of tall buildings and heaps of rubble."},"wiki":"Battle of Kiev (1943)","places":["city:1159151495"]},
    "ww2-344": {"questions":["Stalin’s broadcast of 3 July 1941 demanded that not one engine, railway car or pound of grain be left to the enemy, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> order for every forced retreat of the Red Army.","Allied tribunals often acquitted German generals over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during retreats, holding that a commander under arduous conditions must be judged on the situation as it appeared to him."],"abstract":"A <b>scorched earth</b> policy destroys whatever an enemy could use, from crops and livestock to shelter, transport and industry, in ground an army is abandoning, and on the Eastern Front both sides practised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Hague Regulations of 1907 already forbade destroying or seizing enemy property unless such destruction was imperatively demanded by the necessities of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 July 1941 Stalin ordered by radio that in a forced retreat not a single engine, railway car, pound of grain or gallon of fuel be left to the enemy, and that whatever could not be removed be destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg charter listed devastation not justified by military necessity among war crimes, and the judgment found that German forces had wantonly destroyed towns and villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In later trials of German generals the plea of necessity often succeeded, because a commander retreating under arduous conditions was judged on the situation as it appeared to him at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The German form was first carried out on a large scale in March 1943, when Army Group Centre left the Rzhev salient in Operation Büffel, a plan in which laying waste the abandoned ground was a central part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 3 September 1943, ordering his troops out of the Kuban, Hitler directed that the civilians be taken to the Crimea and the Russians left an uninhabitable desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Manstein testified at Nuremberg that during the retreat behind the Dnieper planned destruction was carried out on Hitler’s orders to make the land east of the river useless to the Russians, and he defended it as necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It fell short of the orders: the Germans took 268,000 tons of grain and 488,000 cattle and horses across the river, but left about 1.66 million tons of grain and nearly 3 million animals behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of about 600,000 civilians herded west in caravans, the Germans later estimated that some 280,000 crossed the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex, The Hague, 18 October 1907, Regulations, art. 23(g), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Radio Address, July 3, 1941,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 14, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 164, 171–72, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 20, proceedings of 10 August 1946, testimony of Erich von Manstein, 612–13, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/08-10-46.asp. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 12, <i>The German High Command Trial</i> (London: HMSO, 1949), 93–94, 123–25, https://archive.org/details/LawReportsOfWarTrialCriminals. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Hostages Trial</i> (London: HMSO, 1949), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/LawReportsOfWarTrialCriminals. [Open access]","Dmitry E. Komarov, “Scorched Earth Tactics in Action: The Retreat of the Nazi Invaders from the Rzhev–Vyazma Salient in March 1943,” <i>Nauka. Obshchestvo. Oborona</i> 11, no. 2 (2023), https://doi.org/10.24412/2311-1763-2023-2-13-13. [in Russian] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” Nuremberg, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was scorched earth not simply forbidden by the laws of war?","a":"Because the Hague Regulations allowed the destruction of enemy property where the necessities of war imperatively demanded it, and a retreating army could argue that denying the enemy food, shelter and transport was exactly such a necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the generals’ tribunals so often accept the plea of necessity?","a":"Because they held that a commander in retreat, his troops in danger of being cut off, had to decide quickly, so he was judged on what he could know at the time, and proving that a particular act of devastation went beyond necessity needed detailed operational evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German destruction east of the Dnieper fall short of the orders?","a":"Because the economic staffs had neither the men nor the equipment to destroy or remove everything, and many inhabitants sabotaged the evacuation to save their own property, so most of the grain and cattle were left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/%D0%92%D1%8B%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%B2%D1%88%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B6%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D1%81_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0.jpg","title":"On the ashes of a home, Baryshivka, September 1943","desc":"A woman and child sitting beside their bundles on the burnt site of their home in Baryshivka, near Kiev, on the day Soviet troops retook the town during the advance to the Dnieper, photographed by Kazimir Lishko.","credit":"Photograph by Kazimir Lishko, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D1%8B%D0%B6%D0%B8%D0%B2%D1%88%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%B6%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D1%81_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"A woman holding a small child sits on the ground beside a heap of bundles, in front of a burnt-out village where only brick chimneys still stand."},"wiki":"Scorched earth"},
    "ww2-345": {"questions":["On 27 January 1944 Leningrad fired a salute for its complete liberation from the blockade, won by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against siege lines Army Group North had held for 2 years.","Hitler withheld the order for Operation Blau after Lindemann said his army could hold, so Army Group North met the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the positions it had held since 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Leningrad–Novgorod offensive</b> was the Soviet operation of 14 January to 1 March 1944 that ended the siege of Leningrad and drove the German Army Group North back from the city towards the Baltic states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Leningrad, Volkhov and Second Baltic Fronts took part, and one Russian count gives them more than 1 million men against 741,000 in Army Group North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Germans had spent 2 years fortifying the approaches to the city, where batteries of 170 and 240 millimetre guns, ranging up to 31 kilometres (19 miles), had been firing on Leningrad itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since September 1943 the army group had planned a withdrawal to the Panther position farther west under the code name Blau, moving 250,000 of the 900,000 civilians in the zone into Latvia and Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler held back the order after the Eighteenth Army’s commander, Lindemann, said his troops could weather an attack, and the army group’s chief of staff warned that it was marching to disaster with its eyes open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 14 January the Second Shock Army attacked out of the Oranienbaum bridgehead west of the city while the Forty-second Army struck from the Pulkovo Heights, and the Volkhov Front attacked near Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two armies from Leningrad met near Russko-Vysotskoye after 6 days of fighting, and on 20 January the Volkhov Front took Novgorod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pushkin and Slutsk fell on 24 January and Krasnogvardeysk 2 days later, and on the evening of 27 January Leningrad fired a salute to its front for the city’s complete liberation from the blockade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The siege had lasted about 900 days, and the German Historical Museum puts the number of inhabitants who died in it at between 800,000 and a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By March the Germans were back on the Panther line between Narva and Pskov, where the front held until the summer, and Stalin later counted the offensive as the first of the year’s ten crushing blows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 248–56, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Vladimir S. Milbakh and Viktor A. Chernukhin, “The Artillery of the Red Army in the Leningrad–Novgorod Strategic Offensive Operation, 14 January – 1 March 1944,” <i>Voenno-istoricheskii zhurnal</i>, no. 2 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/russkaya-artilleriya-bila-po-samym-transheyam-liniyam-svyazi-my-byli-lisheny-upravleniya-i-nesli-bolshie-poteri. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Winteroffensive 1943/44,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/winteroffensive-194344. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Speech Delivered at the Joint Celebration Meeting, November 6, 1944,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 156–58, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]","Olga V. Koshina, “The Ten Stalinist Blows,” <i>Gumanitarii: aktualnye problemy gumanitarnoi nauki i obrazovaniya</i>, no. 3 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/desyat-stalinskih-udarov. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Army Group North not withdraw to the Panther position before the attack?","a":"Because Hitler withheld the order after the Eighteenth Army’s commander said his corps and divisions were confident they could weather the attack, so the army group stayed in positions it had too few troops to hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack come from two directions at once?","a":"Because striking from the Oranienbaum bridgehead and from the Pulkovo Heights on converging lines was meant to cut off the German force between them and open a breach wide enough to break the whole defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 27 January remembered in Leningrad?","a":"Because on that evening the city fired a salute to the Leningrad Front for its complete liberation from the blockade, after about 900 days in which hundreds of thousands of its inhabitants had died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%84%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B3_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B4_%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8C%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B9._19_%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8F_1944_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0.jpg","title":"Red flag on Voronya Gora, 19 January 1944","desc":"A Soviet soldier raising a red flag on Voronya Gora, the hill near Krasnoye Selo from which German siege guns had overlooked Leningrad, on 19 January 1944.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%84%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B3_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B4_%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%8C%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B9._19_%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8F_1944_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"A smiling soldier in a fur-collared coat and fur hat holds up a large flag on a pole above a snowy landscape."},"wiki":"Leningrad–Novgorod offensive"},
    "ww2-346": {"questions":["Antonescu urged Hitler in March 1944 to evacuate the seven Romanian divisions on the peninsula, weeks before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke through from the Sivash and Perekop in April.","Of 64,700 men still in Sevastopol in May 1944, 26,700 were left on the beaches of Cape Khersones at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that destroyed the Seventeenth Army."],"abstract":"The <b>Crimean offensive</b> was the Soviet operation of 8 April to 12 May 1944 in which the Fourth Ukrainian Front and the Independent Coastal Army retook the Crimea and destroyed the German Seventeenth Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army had been cut off since the Red Army reached the Perekop isthmus and the Sivash in the autumn of 1943, and its commanders and Zeitzler wanted it taken out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler kept it there, raising its German strength from one division to five beside seven Romanian ones, and one Russian estimate puts the whole force at more than 195,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Antonescu pressed Hitler in person on 22 March to let the Romanian troops go, and later wrote a letter so blunt that Zeitzler kept it from Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin later counted the campaign as the third of his ten crushing blows of 1944, the one that threw the Germans into the Black Sea and freed the Crimea and Odessa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The offensive opened from the north on 8 April, the Romanian 10th Division holding half the Sivash bridgehead line collapsed on the second day, and Simferopol fell on 13 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler ordered Sevastopol held to keep Turkey quiet until he had beaten off the Anglo-American invasion, and dismissed the army’s commander, Jaenecke, for doubting it could be done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The main assault came on 7 May behind Balaklava, Soviet troops held the Sapun Heights by nightfall, and Sevastopol was taken on 9 May, at a cost for the whole operation of 17,754 Soviet dead and 67,065 wounded by the Russian count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The evacuation from Cape Khersones became what the US Army’s history calls a tragic fiasco, with 26,700 of the 64,700 men still at Sevastopol left on the beach, though the German Historical Museum says 150,000 reached Constanța in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two Soviet security officials reported on 11 May that Sevastopol was almost entirely destroyed and that about 15,000 people remained, most of them living in dugouts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 291–95, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Frühjahrsoffensive 1944,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/fruehjahrsoffensive-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Tatyana P. Malyutina, “The Liberation of Crimea: The Retreat of the Romanian Mountain Corps under the Onslaught of Soviet Troops in April 1944,” <i>Vestnik Tomskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta</i>, no. 483 (2022): 130–38, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osvobozhdenie-kryma-otstuplenie-rumynskogo-gornogo-korpusa-pod-natiskom-sovetskih-voysk-v-aprele-1944-g. [in Russian] [Open access]","Olga V. Koshina, “The Ten Stalinist Blows,” <i>Gumanitarii: aktualnye problemy gumanitarnoi nauki i obrazovaniya</i>, no. 3 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/desyat-stalinskih-udarov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Sergey Ya. Lavrenov, “The Liberation of Crimea: The Third Strategic Blow of the Red Army,” <i>Obozrevatel – Observer</i>, no. 1 (2025), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osvobozhdenie-kryma-tretiy-strategicheskiy-udar-krasnoy-armii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Speech Delivered at the Joint Celebration Meeting, November 6, 1944,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 156–58, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler refuse to evacuate the Crimea in time?","a":"Because he wanted Sevastopol held for the weeks he thought he needed to keep Turkey quiet until he had beaten off the Anglo-American invasion, and the navy and air force had given him a rosy picture of supplying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Antonescu want the Romanian troops out?","a":"Because he thought it nonsense to hold a distant beachhead while Romania itself was in danger, and he had pressed Hitler in person in March to agree to their evacuation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were so many men left on the beaches at the end?","a":"Because convoys sent to take them off Cape Khersones turned back empty or loaded only a fraction of what they could carry, the navy claiming that smoke hid the shore, while the army insisted the ships could have found their way in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%B2_%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%2C_1944_%D0%B3.jpg","title":"Street fighting in Sevastopol, 9 May 1944","desc":"Soldiers of the Fourth Ukrainian Front advancing through ruins during the street fighting in Sevastopol on the day the city fell, photographed by Aleksandr Brodsky for TASS.","credit":"Photograph by Aleksandr Brodsky, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%B8_%D0%B2%D0%BE_%D0%B2%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D1%85_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%B2_%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%2C_1944_%D0%B3.jpg","alt":"Soldiers with rifles clamber over heaps of broken masonry between ruined stone buildings, one of them waving the others forward."},"wiki":"Crimean offensive"},
    "ww2-347": {"questions":["German intelligence dismissed the massing of four Soviet fronts in Belorussia as apparently a deception, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of June 1944 struck where the German commanders least expected it.","Churchill told the Commons in August 1944 that the Russian armies had done the main work in tearing the guts out of the German army, weeks after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had retaken Minsk."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Bagration</b> was the Soviet offensive against German Army Group Centre in Belorussia, begun on 22 June 1944 according to the German records and on 23 June in Soviet accounts, and ended on 29 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was named after General Pyotr Bagration, one of the heroes of Russia’s war against Napoleon in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Four fronts, the First Baltic and the First, Second and Third Belorussian, were to break the German strongholds at Vitebsk, Orsha, Mogilev and Bobruisk and then close on Minsk from the north-east and south-east to trap the Fourth Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The US Army’s history puts the Soviet troops in the attack zone at 1.2 million against an army group of a little over 700,000, while the German Historical Museum gives it about 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Tehran the Western Allies had promised their landing in France for about the start of June, and Stalin had promised that the whole Russian army would be thrown into general battle in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Germans expected the main blow farther south and dismissed the build-up opposite Army Group Centre as apparently a deception, a belief a Soviet deception plan worked to keep alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On the night of 19 June partisans planted more than 5,000 mines on the roads and railways behind two German armies, and on 22 June the attack began around Vitebsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ninth Army was trapped at Bobruisk with about 70,000 men, Soviet troops took Minsk on 3 July, and in 12 days the army group lost 25 divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Losses are disputed: Soviet records give 178,507 dead and missing and 587,308 wounded and sick, one Russian writer triples the dead, and German losses are put at about 350,000 by the German Historical Museum and 480,000 to 550,000 in a Russian military journal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 2 August that the Russian armies had done the main work in tearing the guts out of the German army, and Stalin counted the offensive as the fifth of his ten blows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 313–25, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Vladimir V. Litvinenko, “Operation Bagration: The Human Losses of the Red Army and the Wehrmacht, Myths and Reality,” <i>Voennaya mysl</i>, no. 5 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/operatsiya-bagration-lyudskie-poteri-krasnoy-armii-i-vermahta-mify-i-realnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1944,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1944. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 August 1944, vol. 402, cols. 1474–75, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]","Petr V. Petriy, “The Spiritual Sources of Operation Bagration,” <i>Nauka. Kultura. Obshchestvo</i>, no. 4 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/duhovnye-istoki-operatsii-bagration. [in Russian] [Open access]","Terje Bruoygard, “Operational Art in Theory and War: A Comparison of Soviet Theory and the Red Army’s Conduct in Operation BAGRATION 1944” (master’s thesis, US Marine Corps Command and Staff College, 2013), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA601678. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Speech Delivered at the Joint Celebration Meeting, November 6, 1944,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 156–58, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans fail to see the offensive coming?","a":"Because they had convinced themselves the main blow would fall farther south where their reserves stood ready, and they read the build-up in Belorussia as a deception while the Soviet side worked to make them believe exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do estimates of the losses differ so widely?","a":"Because the two armies counted losses differently, the German 10-day reports left many losses out, and writers disagree over whether official Soviet figures can be trusted, so estimates range from one critic’s tripling of the Soviet dead to widely different German totals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the timing of the offensive matter to the Western Allies?","a":"Because at Tehran Stalin had promised that the whole Russian army would be thrown into battle in the East to match the landing in France, so the offensive pinned German forces that might otherwise have gone west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/158th_Rifle_Division_Vitebsk_June_1944.jpg","title":"Fighting at Vitebsk station, June 1944","desc":"Soldiers of the 881st Rifle Regiment of the Soviet 158th Rifle Division fighting near the railway station at Vitebsk in the opening days of the offensive in Belorussia, June 1944.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Ministry of Defence of the Russian Federation, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:158th_Rifle_Division_Vitebsk_June_1944.jpg","alt":"Soldiers crouch behind a small wheeled machine gun among the steel pylons of a wrecked railway station while others run forward across the tracks."},"wiki":"Operation Bagration"},
    "ww2-348": {"questions":["Stripped of 88 per cent of its tanks a month earlier, Busch’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost 25 divisions in 12 days when the Soviet summer offensive of 1944 broke over Belorussia.","On 17 July 1944, 57,600 prisoners from the shattered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were marched through the streets of Moscow, which water trucks then washed down behind them."],"abstract":"<b>Army Group Centre</b> was the German army group that invaded the Soviet Union north of the Pripyat Marshes in June 1941 under Field Marshal Fedor von Bock, with 51 divisions, nine of them armoured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its road led through Minsk and Smolensk towards Moscow, but Hitler made the drive wait while he turned to the Ukraine, and the offensive Bock opened on 30 September stalled at the capital in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It then held the salient around Rzhev and Vyazma until Hitler agreed on 6 February 1943 to give it up, and the withdrawal, Operation Büffel, was carried out as a scorched-earth operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In June 1944, under Field Marshal Ernst Busch, it still held a great bulge around Vitebsk, Orsha, Mogilev and Bobruisk, which Hitler refused to let it shorten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A month earlier it had handed its LVI Panzer Corps to the army group to the south, giving up 6 per cent of its front but 88 per cent of its tanks, and Busch surrendered the corps without protest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> When the Soviet offensive struck on 22 June its armies were bound by orders to hold fortified places and every foot of ground, and the Ninth Army’s commander wrote that he faced the battle with bitterness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its three armies were soon encircled, Walter Model replaced Busch at the end of June, and in 12 days the army group lost 25 divisions, the Fourth Army alone 130,000 of its 165,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Russian military study counts 60 German divisions smashed in the offensive as a whole, 22 of them wiped out, and the German Historical Museum judges that the army group had practically ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 17 July 57,600 of its captured men, 19 generals among them, were marched through Moscow, and water trucks washed the streets behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The name outlived the force: on 26 January 1945 what remained of it was renamed Army Group North, and the title Army Group Centre passed to the former Army Group A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["George E. Blau, <i>The German Campaign in Russia: Planning and Operations (1940–1942)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-261a (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 22, 38–39, 79–80, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-261A. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Angriff auf Moskau 1941,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/angriff-auf-moskau-1941. [in German] [Open access]","Dmitry E. Komarov, “Scorched Earth Tactics in Action: The Retreat of the Nazi Invaders from the Rzhev–Vyazma Salient in March 1943,” <i>Nauka. Obshchestvo. Oborona</i> 11, no. 2 (2023), https://doi.org/10.24412/2311-1763-2023-2-13-13. [in Russian] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 314–17, 324–25, 426, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1944,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Vladimir V. Litvinenko, “Operation Bagration: The Human Losses of the Red Army and the Wehrmacht, Myths and Reality,” <i>Voennaya mysl</i>, no. 5 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/operatsiya-bagration-lyudskie-poteri-krasnoy-armii-i-vermahta-mify-i-realnost. [in Russian] [Open access]","Aleksandr L. Kuzminykh, “The March of the Defeated: Preparation, Organisation and Symbolic Meaning of the Escorting of German Prisoners of War through Moscow on 17 July 1944,” <i>Penitentsiarnaya nauka</i> 19, no. 2 (2025): 118–24, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/marsh-pobezhdennyh-podgotovka-organizatsiya-i-simvolicheskoe-znachenie-konvoirovaniya-nemetskih-voennoplennyh-po-moskve-17-iyulya. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the army group so weak when the 1944 offensive came?","a":"Because the German command expected the main Soviet blow farther south and had moved its armour there, so when LVI Panzer Corps left it lost 88 per cent of its tanks while still holding the longest front of any army group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did its armies stand and fight instead of falling back?","a":"Because Hitler’s orders bound them to hold fortified places and every foot of ground, and Busch passed those orders on without question, refusing his army commanders’ requests to withdraw until it was too late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the prisoners marched through Moscow?","a":"Because the Soviet government wanted a public demonstration of its victory, and the NKVD planned a route through the city’s main streets for some 57,600 prisoners, with the captured generals marching in a column of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/194407_abandoned_german_vehicles_belarus.jpg","title":"German Ninth Army vehicles near Bobruisk, June 1944","desc":"Abandoned and wrecked vehicles of the German Ninth Army on a road near Bobruisk at the end of June 1944, photographed for the Soviet 16th Air Army to record its attacks.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:194407_abandoned_german_vehicles_belarus.jpg","alt":"Dozens of wrecked and abandoned lorries and cars lie scattered across a sandy clearing beside a pine forest, with fuel drums in the foreground."},"wiki":"Army Group Centre"},
    "ww2-349": {"questions":["Many of the officers who designed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were killed in Stalin’s purges, yet their ideas returned to guide the Red Army’s great offensives of 1943 to 1945.","A first echelon broke the tactical defences and a mobile group of tanks drove through the gap into the enemy’s rear, the pattern of the Soviet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked out in the 1930s."],"abstract":"The <b>deep operation</b> was the Red Army’s theory of the offensive, worked out between the late 1920s and 1936, in which a front struck the whole depth of the enemy’s defences at once and drove mobile forces into his rear to encircle him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of the theory of deep battle, the same idea at the tactical level, set out at the end of the 1920s in the writings of Tukhachevsky, Triandafillov, Yegorov, Isserson and others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Triandafillov argued that a decisive result could come only from a large encirclement, made possible by a series of consecutive operations following one after another in time and space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Provisional Field Regulations of 1936 wrote the principle down: modern weapons made possible the simultaneous destruction of the enemy at all echelons, and an enemy who was attacked must be surrounded and completely destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An attacking army was organised in echelons, a first to break the tactical defences and a second, or a mobile group, to drive through the gap into the enemy’s rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The theory was nearly lost as soon as it was written down, because many of the men who devised it died in the purges of the late 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The disasters of 1941 seemed to bury it, but the commanders of 1943 to 1945 revived the concepts of their predecessors and used them to defeat the German army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fullest application was the offensive against Army Group Centre in June 1944, whose design of deception, breakthrough at four strongholds, encirclement at Minsk and pursuit matched the pre-war theory closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There the Soviet commanders massed infantry and artillery on narrow sectors and kept their tanks out of sight until a gap opened, then sent them straight through without regard for their flanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Writing in 1978, a Soviet military journal still called the theory the most important achievement of Soviet military science in the years before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["People’s Commissariat of Defence of the USSR, <i>Provisional Field Regulations for the Red Army, 1936</i>, art. 9, trans. Foreign Broadcast Information Service, JPRS-UMA-86-031 (12 June 1986), 5, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA361873. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>The Soviet Airborne Experience</i>, Research Survey 4 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1984), 1–3, 13–14, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA153124. [Open access]","L. I. Voloshin, “Theory of the Operation in Depth and Trends in Its Development,” <i>Voyennaya mysl</i>, no. 8 (1978): 14–26, trans. Foreign Broadcast Information Service (1979), CIA Reading Room CIA-RDP05S00365R000200210001-0, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp05s00365r000200210001-0. [Open access]","Terje Bruoygard, “Operational Art in Theory and War: A Comparison of Soviet Theory and the Red Army’s Conduct in Operation BAGRATION 1944” (master’s thesis, US Marine Corps Command and Staff College, 2013), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA601678. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 321, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","United States Department of the Army, <i>The Soviet Army: Operations and Tactics</i>, Field Manual 100-2-1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1984), 2-5 – 2-6, https://archive.org/details/fm100-2-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Soviet theorists want to strike the whole depth of the defence at once?","a":"Because new weapons, tanks, aircraft and long-range artillery made it possible, and hitting the enemy’s rear at the same time as his front line would cut his communications and leave his commanders unable to restore a coordinated defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the theory nearly forgotten by 1941?","a":"Because the 1936 regulations appeared just months before the purges, in which many of their authors were killed or imprisoned, so the ideas lacked champions in the army’s training and education when war came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the 1944 Belorussian offensive treated as the theory’s proof?","a":"Because its design followed the pre-war ideas closely, from deception and simultaneous breakthroughs to a great encirclement and relentless pursuit, and the new mechanised and air forces delivered what the theory had promised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Mikhail_Tukhachevsky_circa_1935.jpg","title":"Mikhail Tukhachevsky, about 1935","desc":"Mikhail Tukhachevsky, one of the Soviet theorists of deep battle and the operation in depth, photographed about 1935, two years before he was shot in the purges.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in the United States, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Tukhachevsky_circa_1935.jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders portrait of a heavy-set man with swept-back hair in a military tunic with large collar patches bearing stars."},"wiki":"Deep operation"},
    "ww2-350": {"questions":["About 3,000 Home Army soldiers helped the Red Army clear Lvov during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1944, only to be ordered to disarm and disband the day after the city fell.","Konev’s tank armies crossed the Vistula at Baranów and held a bridgehead west of Sandomierz by late August 1944, the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that broke Army Group North Ukraine."],"abstract":"The <b>Lvov–Sandomierz offensive</b> was the attack by Konev’s First Ukrainian Front from 13 July to 29 August 1944 that broke the German Army Group North Ukraine, took Lvov and carried the Red Army across the Vistula into southern Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came as the offensive in Belorussia drew German reserves north, and it struck an army group that had lost three panzer and two infantry divisions outright in the weeks before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Konev had ten armies, three of them tank armies, and aimed his thrusts at Rava Russkaya and Lvov in the centre and at Stanislav in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farther north the neighbouring First Belorussian Front took Lublin on 24 July, a day after reaching the Majdanek extermination camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stalin later counted the offensive as the sixth of his ten blows of 1944, which freed western Ukraine and won a bridgehead across the Vistula west of Sandomierz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The armoured spearheads met on 18 July and encircled the German XIII Corps around Brody, five German divisions and the SS Division Galicia, of whose 30,000 men no more than 5,000 escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Lvov about 3,000 soldiers of the Polish underground Home Army fought beside the Soviet troops from 23 to 27 July, seizing the citadel, the main post office and the town hall, and the city was cleared on 27 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next day the Soviet side demanded that the Home Army units lay down their arms and disband, which they did on 28 July, and on the night of 3 August their commanders were arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Near the city Soviet forces found the shoes of victims at the Janowska camp, to which, or to Belzec, the last Jews of the Lvov ghetto had been sent when the Germans destroyed it in June 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Konev’s tank armies began crossing the Vistula at Baranów at the end of July, beat off repeated counter-attacks, took Sandomierz on 18 August and held a bridgehead about 75 kilometres (47 miles) wide and 60 kilometres (37 miles) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, United States Army, 1968), 329–33, 340–44, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Valery B. Makovsky, “The Lvov–Sandomierz Offensive Operation: A View after Seventy Years,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO-Universiteta</i>, no. 1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/lvovsko-sandomirskaya-nastupatelnaya-operatsiya-vzglyad-cherez-70-let. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dariusz Rogut, “The Attitude of Soviet Security Organs to the Home Army (July 1944 – January 1945),” <i>Historia provinciae</i> 4, no. 4 (2020): 1303–51, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/the-attitude-of-soviet-security-organs-to-the-home-army-july-1944-january-1945. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lvov,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lvov. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin, “Speech Delivered at the Joint Celebration Meeting, November 6, 1944,” in <i>On the Great Patriotic War of the Soviet Union</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1946), 156–58, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.65349. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die sowjetische Sommeroffensive 1944,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sommeroffensive-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Army Group North Ukraine weaker than it looked in July 1944?","a":"Because the disaster in Belorussia forced the German command to send reserves north, and the army group had already lost three panzer and two infantry divisions outright and received untested divisions in exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Home Army fight for Lvov alongside the Red Army?","a":"Because under Operation Tempest its units were to fight the retreating Germans, and its commander hoped to go on fighting as a division of their own, loyal to the Polish government in London but under Red Army command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Vistula bridgehead at Sandomierz matter?","a":"Because it gave the Red Army a large foothold on the western bank of Poland’s great river, which the Germans attacked repeatedly in August without being able to destroy it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/%D0%93%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%91%D1%83%D1%88%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%82_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9._%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BE%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0_%D0%9B%D1%8C%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B2.jpg","title":"Street fighting on the edge of Lvov, July 1944","desc":"Guardsmen of Major Aleksei Bushmakin’s motor-rifle battalion of the 16th Guards Mechanised Brigade fighting in the outskirts of Lvov in July 1944, photographed by Dmitry Chernov for TASS.","credit":"Photograph by Dmitry Chernov, public domain in Russia, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%93%D0%B2%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%B9%D1%86%D1%8B_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0_%D0%91%D1%83%D1%88%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%B4%D1%83%D1%82_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%87%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9._%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BE%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0_%D0%9B%D1%8C%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B2.jpg","alt":"A soldier in a steel helmet aims a sub-machine gun from a doorway beside an iron gate while another crouches behind an overturned chair in the foreground."},"wiki":"Lvov-Sandomierz Offensive","places":["city:1159149323"]},
    "ww2-461": {"questions":["Built to keep the Japanese armies in Burma supplied once the sea route round Malaya became unsafe, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was driven through the monsoon of 1943 with hand tools.","The Tokyo tribunal found that 16,000 of 46,000 prisoners died on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 18 months, and that at least 60,000 Asian labourers died beside them."],"abstract":"The <b>Burma Railway</b> was the line of some 415 kilometres (260 miles) that the Japanese army drove through jungle and mountains between Thailand and Burma in 1942 and 1943 to supply its forces in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It ran from Bampong, where it left the railway from Bangkok, to Thanbyuzayat, where it joined the existing line north to Moulmein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early in 1942 Imperial General Headquarters decided, on the advice of Tojo, to build the link with prisoners of war and set November 1943 for its completion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The labour force was about 60,000 Allied prisoners of war and some 200,000 Asian labourers, the romusha, who built the track with hand tools through the monsoon of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Work began at each end, and the two halves met near Konkoita in Thailand, about 273 kilometres (170 miles) from Bampong, on 16 October 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The hurry was strategic, for the sea route through the Straits of Malacca had become unreliable after Midway, and the armies in Burma needed an overland supply route as they planned to invade India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In February 1943, as Allied attacks on shipping grew, the date for completion was brought forward by 4 months, and prisoners were told that the line had to be finished at all costs, whatever the loss of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prisoners' dead are still counted differently: the Tokyo tribunal found that 16,000 of 46,000 died, the Australian War Memorial gives at least 2,815 Australians and over 11,000 other Allied prisoners, and one recent estimate is around 12,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The toll among the Asian labourers was heavier and is less certain, with the tribunal finding that at least 60,000 of 150,000 died, the Memorial suggesting perhaps 75,000 and one scholarly estimate putting it at possibly 100,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In January 1944 Britain's foreign secretary told the Commons that thousands of prisoners in Siam were building a railway without adequate shelter, food or medical care, and once it opened Allied bombers attacked its bridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chapter VIII, \"Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)\" (Tokyo, 1948), 1048–56, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – The Burma–Thailand Railway,\" online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan/burmathai. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Australian Prisoners of War: Second World War – Prisoners of the Japanese, Burma-Thailand Railway,\" research guide, https://www.awm.gov.au/research/guide/pow-ww2-burma-thai. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Distances between Camps on the Burma-Thailand Railway,\" Encyclopedia, last updated 20 January 2020, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/pow/camp_distance. [Open access]","Joan Beaumont and Andrea Witcomb, \"The Thai-Burma Railway,\" in <i>The Pacific War: Aftermaths, Remembrance and Culture</i>, ed. Christina Twomey and Ernest Koh (Abingdon: Routledge, 2015), 67–87, abstract at ANU Open Research, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/items/3f678a79-e5d4-4baa-81cd-1e454c2c1937. [Paywalled]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, \"Japanese Treatment,\" <i>Hansard</i>, 28 January 1944, vol. 396, cols. 1029–35, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jan/28/japanese-treatment. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 470, 512–13, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan need a railway between Thailand and Burma at all?","a":"Its armies in Burma were supplied by sea round Malaya, and once that route became unreliable an overland line from the Bangkok railway to Thanbyuzayat and Moulmein offered a safer way to keep them fed and armed for the planned move on India."},{"q":"Why are the death tolls on the railway given so differently?","a":"They come from different bodies counting in different ways: the Tokyo tribunal drew on the evidence before it, including Japanese figures, the Australian War Memorial counts Allied prisoners, and for the Asian labourers every source gives only an estimate."},{"q":"Why did deaths rise so steeply during 1943?","a":"The deadline was shortened by 4 months just as the monsoon broke, so the work was driven harder in camps that often had no roofs, little food and almost no medicine; by Japanese figures prisoner deaths in Thailand rose from 54 in November 1942 to 800 in August 1943."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Burma_Thailand_Railway_Konkoita_Bridge_Building_1943.jpg","title":"Bridge building at Konkoita, 1943","desc":"Men building a timber bridge at Konkoita in 1943, near the point where the two halves of the line were joined in October; the photograph was taken by a Japanese photographer and is held by the Australian War Memorial.","credit":"Unknown Japanese photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burma_Thailand_Railway_Konkoita_Bridge_Building_1943.jpg","alt":"A long low timber trestle in the foreground and, behind it, a line of men carrying heavy logs across a second wooden structure at the edge of the jungle."},"wiki":"Burma Railway"},
    "ww2-462": {"questions":["None of the 16 bombers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was lost to enemy action, yet all were lost when their crews crash-landed or baled out over China in darkness and rain.","Japanese admirals still arguing over how far to push their defensive perimeter found their case for Midway strengthened by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 2 days after the operation was agreed."],"abstract":"The <b>Doolittle Raid</b> was the attack of 18 April 1942 in which 16 army B-25 bombers, flown off the aircraft carrier Hornet, bombed Tokyo, Yokohama, Kobe and Nagoya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was conceived in January 1942, apparently with President Roosevelt playing a part, as a stroke at Japan's capital that would lift American morale after Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its leader, James Doolittle, wrote that the object was to bomb Japan's industrial centres and that the damage was hoped to be both material and psychological.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The crews trained to lift heavily loaded bombers off a short run, and on the Hornet the first plane had only about 142 metres (467 feet) of clear deck in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bombers were meant to fly on some 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) to airfields in eastern China, which Chiang Kai-shek agreed to only reluctantly and without being told the details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the morning of the raid a Japanese patrol boat, one of the many small picket craft Nimitz afterwards noted far out at sea, sighted the task force, and Halsey launched the bombers some 10 hours early and about 1,300 kilometres (800 miles) from Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All 16 got off the plunging deck in under an hour, and they met little opposition because the Japanese had not expected a raid from such a distance until the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of the 16, 15 bombed targets in Japan, but over China every plane crash-landed or was abandoned in darkness and rain, one crew was interned in the Soviet Union, and three captured airmen were executed by the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese soon advanced on the Chinese airfields the raiders had been meant to use, and kept four army fighter groups at home that were needed in the Solomons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The raid came 2 days after Japan's leaders had agreed to attack Midway, and it swept away the remaining objections to pushing the defensive line as far east as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), chapter 12, \"Drawing the Battle Line in the Pacific,\" 437–44, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-12.html. [Open access]","James H. Doolittle, \"General Doolittle's Report on Japanese Raid, April 18, 1942,\" Headquarters of the Army Air Forces, 9 July 1942, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/rep/Doolittle/Report.html. [Open access]","Commander-in-Chief, United States Pacific Fleet, \"Report of Action in Connection with the Bombing of Tokyo on April 18, 1942,\" to Commander-in-Chief, United States Fleet, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/rep/Doolittle/CinCPac-Tokyo.html. [Open access]","Marc A. Mitscher, \"Report of Action, April 18, 1942, with Notable Events Prior and Subsequent Thereto,\" USS <i>Hornet</i>, 28 April 1942, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/ships/logs/CV/cv8-Tokyo.html. [Open access]","Frank O. Hough, Verle E. Ludwig and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Pearl Harbor to Guadalcanal</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 1 (Washington: Historical Branch, U.S. Marine Corps, 1958), part V, chapter 1, \"Setting the Stage: Early Naval Operations,\" 205–13, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/I/USMC-I-V-1.html. [Open access]","Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part two, \"The Battles for Midway and the Aleutians,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartTwo.html. [Open access]","Henry F. Schorreck, \"The Role of COMINT in the Battle of Midway,\" SRH-230 (Fort Meade, MD: National Security Agency), HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-Midway.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the bombers to fly on to China instead of returning to the carrier?","a":"Trials with B-25s off Norfolk, Virginia, showed that taking off from a carrier would be fairly easy but landing back on one extremely difficult, so the plan became a take-off east of Tokyo and a flight west to airfields in eastern China."},{"q":"Why was the raid launched so far from Japan?","a":"Japanese picket boats sighted the task force early on 18 April, and rather than risk the carriers to bombers that might come out against them, Halsey sent the B-25s off at once, accepting that most could not reach the Chinese airfields."},{"q":"Why did so small a raid matter strategically?","a":"It did little material damage, but it showed Japanese commanders that their homeland could be reached, made them keep fighters at home, and strengthened Yamamoto's argument for taking Midway and pushing the perimeter east."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/B-25B_taking_off_USS_Hornet_18Apr1942.jpeg","title":"A B-25 leaves the Hornet, 18 April 1942","desc":"One of the 16 army B-25B Mitchell bombers taking off from the carrier USS Hornet on the morning of the raid, after the task force had been sighted and the launch brought forward.","credit":"U.S. Army Air Forces photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B-25B_taking_off_USS_Hornet_18Apr1942.jpeg","alt":"A twin-engined bomber climbing away low over the sea just beyond the bow of an aircraft carrier, seen from the carrier's deck."},"wiki":"Doolittle Raid"},
    "ww2-463": {"questions":["Surface ships never sighted or fired on one another in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was fought entirely by carrier aircraft striking at the enemy's carriers at almost the same moment.","The Lexington was lost in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the damage to the Shokaku and the loss of the Zuikaku's aircrew kept both carriers out of the Midway operation."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Coral Sea</b> was the carrier battle of 4 to 8 May 1942 in which American and Australian forces turned back a Japanese seaborne invasion of Port Moresby in New Guinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Port Moresby was to be the southern outpost of Japan's defensive perimeter, denying the Allies a base within air range of Rabaul and dominating northern Australia and New Guinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese plans were uncovered by signals intelligence, and the Allies sent a force built round the carriers Yorktown and Lexington, with the Australian cruisers Australia and Hobart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 7 May American aircraft found and sank the light carrier Shoho, while Japanese carrier planes sank the destroyer Sims and crippled the oiler Neosho.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 8 May the main forces found each other and struck almost at once: the Shokaku took six bomb hits, both American carriers were hit, and the burning Lexington had to be abandoned and sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The fight was new in kind, the first major engagement in naval history decided without surface ships exchanging a shot, purely an air action between carrier groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tactically it was close to a draw, for the Americans lost more tonnage and suffered the heavier damage, while the Japanese lost more aircraft, 43 from all causes against 33.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strategically it was Japan's first major setback: the invasion force retired, the landing was put off until July, and after Midway the plan to take Port Moresby by sea was abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also shaped the next battle, because the damaged Shokaku and the Zuikaku, which had lost most of its aircraft and crews, were kept out of the Midway operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Nimitz judged that Fletcher had used the intelligence supplied to him with consummate skill, while an air force history admitted that army bombers, lacking any coordination with the navy, had dropped bombs on friendly ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>The Battle of the Coral Sea, May 4 to May 8, 1942</i>, Combat Narratives (Washington: Office of Naval Intelligence), 1–2, 46–47, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/USN-CN-Coral/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter 4, \"The Battle of the Coral Sea,\" 51–52, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-4.html. [Open access]","Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part one, \"The Battle of the Coral Sea,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartOne.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), chapter 12, \"Drawing the Battle Line in the Pacific,\" 444–49, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-12.html. [Open access]","Frank O. Hough, Verle E. Ludwig and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Pearl Harbor to Guadalcanal</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 1 (Washington: Historical Branch, U.S. Marine Corps, 1958), part V, chapter 1, \"Setting the Stage: Early Naval Operations,\" 205–13, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/I/USMC-I-V-1.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Coral Sea,\" Encyclopedia, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/coral_sea. [Open access]","Henry F. Schorreck, \"The Role of COMINT in the Battle of Midway,\" SRH-230 (Fort Meade, MD: National Security Agency), HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-Midway.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan want Port Moresby?","a":"It was to be the southern anchor of the defensive perimeter: held by Japan it would deny the Allies an air base within reach of Rabaul and threaten northern Australia and the sea routes round New Guinea."},{"q":"Why did the carriers fight without the surface ships ever meeting?","a":"Each side's aircraft could strike from far beyond gun range, so both commanders sought to cripple the other's carriers from the air; weather and poor scouting meant days of searching, and when contact came both sides launched at almost the same time."},{"q":"Why is a battle in which the Americans lost more counted as a strategic success?","a":"The invasion convoy turned back, Port Moresby was never attacked from the sea, and two of Japan's best carriers were missing from Midway a month later, which cut Yamamoto's air striking power there by a third."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/A_heavy_explosion_on_board_USS_Lexington_%28CV-2%29_blows_an_aircraft_over_her_side%2C_8_May_1942_%2880-G-7413%29.jpg","title":"The Lexington explodes, 8 May 1942","desc":"A heavy explosion aboard the carrier USS Lexington late on 8 May, probably from torpedo warheads stowed in the hangar, blows an aircraft over her side; the destroyer Hammann backs away with survivors.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, Naval History and Heritage Command, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_heavy_explosion_on_board_USS_Lexington_(CV-2)_blows_an_aircraft_over_her_side,_8_May_1942_(80-G-7413).jpg","alt":"A large carrier seen from a distance with a tall column of smoke and debris rising from its deck, and a smaller warship nearby."},"wiki":"Battle of the Coral Sea"},
    "ww2-464": {"questions":["Yamamoto expected the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to draw out the American carriers, assuming they were still far away after the Coral Sea, and planned to occupy the atoll on 6 June.","Every torpedo plane of the Hornet's squadron was shot down attacking without fighter cover in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the attacks drew the Japanese fighters onto themselves minutes before the dive bombers came."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Midway</b> was the naval and air battle of 3 to 7 June 1942 in which an American force of three carriers, with aircraft based on Midway itself, defeated a Japanese attempt to seize the atoll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yamamoto meant the operation to draw out and destroy the American carriers, and the Combined Fleet committed over 200 ships, eight carriers among them, to it and to a linked thrust at the Aleutians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Midway was to be occupied on 6 June, for the Japanese knew that all available American carrier strength had been in the distant Coral Sea only 3 weeks before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nagumo, commanding the four carriers of the striking force, recorded before the battle that the enemy was not aware of the plan and that no powerful carrier unit was believed to be near.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His force had sailed a third weaker than Yamamoto wanted, since the two carriers damaged or stripped of aircrew in the Coral Sea had stayed behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> On 4 June the Japanese struck Midway, and while Nagumo's reserve aircraft were being changed from torpedoes to bombs for a second strike, an American force was reported within reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American torpedo squadrons attacked first and without fighter cover, and were almost wiped out: the Hornet's lost every plane, while the Enterprise lost ten of 14 and the Yorktown ten of 12.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shortly after 10:00 AM dive bombers from the Enterprise and Yorktown hit Akagi, Kaga and Soryu, and a counterstrike from the surviving Hiryu crippled the Yorktown before Hiryu was itself found and bombed that afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yamamoto then abandoned the operation, and in the days that followed American aircraft sank the cruiser Mikuma while a Japanese submarine sank the crippled Yorktown on 7 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the count of the National WWII Museum Japan lost about 3,057 men, four carriers, a cruiser and hundreds of aircraft, and the United States about 362 men, a carrier, a destroyer and 144 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter 5, \"The Battle of Midway,\" 57–60, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-5.html. [Open access]","Chuichi Nagumo, \"CINC First Air Fleet Detailed Battle Report No. 6,\" translated in \"The Japanese Story of the Battle of Midway,\" <i>ONI Review</i> 2, no. 5 (May 1947), HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/IJN/rep/Midway/Nagumo/index.html. [Open access]","Chester W. Nimitz, \"Battle of Midway,\" action report, Commander-in-Chief, United States Pacific Fleet, to Commander-in-Chief, United States Fleet, CinCPac file A16 01849, 1942, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/rep/Midway/Midway-CinCPac.html. [Open access]","Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>The Battle of Midway, June 3–6, 1942</i>, Combat Narratives (Washington: Office of Naval Intelligence), 1–2, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/USN-CN-Midway/USN-CN-Midway-1.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), chapter 12, \"Drawing the Battle Line in the Pacific,\" 450–53, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-12.html. [Open access]","Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part two, \"The Battles for Midway and the Aleutians,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartTwo.html. [Open access]","Henry F. Schorreck, \"The Role of COMINT in the Battle of Midway,\" SRH-230 (Fort Meade, MD: National Security Agency), HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-Midway.html. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, \"The Battle of Midway,\" article, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/battle-midway. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yamamoto want Midway?","a":"He believed Japan had to fight a decisive battle with the American fleet before American shipbuilding outstripped Japan's, and an attack on Midway, a sentry for Hawaii, was the bait most likely to bring the carriers out."},{"q":"Why were the Japanese carriers caught with aircraft on deck?","a":"Nagumo had ordered his reserve aircraft rearmed with bombs for a second strike on Midway, then learned of American ships and decided to wait for his returning strike before launching, so the carriers were refuelling and rearming when the dive bombers arrived."},{"q":"Why did the doomed torpedo attacks still matter?","a":"The Japanese concentrated their fighters and anti-aircraft fire on the torpedo planes, so the dive bombers that arrived a few minutes later met far less opposition; Nimitz called the torpedo squadrons a sacrifice that enabled the dive bombers to make their attack."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/B5Ns_attacking_USS_Yorktown_%28CV-5%29_at_Midway_1942.jpg","title":"Torpedo planes attack the Yorktown, 4 June 1942","desc":"Nakajima B5N torpedo planes from the carrier Hiryu fly through heavy anti-aircraft fire as they attack USS Yorktown in the afternoon of 4 June; the nearest has already dropped its torpedo.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph 80-G-11639, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B5Ns_attacking_USS_Yorktown_(CV-5)_at_Midway_1942.jpg","alt":"Bursts of anti-aircraft fire dotting the sky over a calm sea, with several small aircraft flying low just above the water."},"wiki":"Battle of Midway"},
    "ww2-465": {"questions":["A plain-language signal that Midway's water plant had failed was followed by a Japanese report that AF was short of water, a ruse remembered as the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Hawaii.","By mid-April 1942 Japanese naval messages were being intercepted, decrypted, translated and passed on by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within 6 hours of their transmission, almost all of them in one system, JN 25."],"abstract":"<b>Station HYPO</b> was the US Navy's radio intelligence centre at Pearl Harbor, which intercepted, broke and reported Japanese naval messages for the commander of the Pacific Fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was administratively part of the 14th Naval District but worked almost entirely for the fleet, and was run by Joseph Rochefort, both a Japanese linguist and a cryptanalyst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rochefort worked closely with Edwin Layton, the fleet intelligence officer, who briefed Nimitz every day and had orders to interrupt him at any time with urgent intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After Pearl Harbor its reports lost their sharpness for several weeks, a change one of its officers later put down to guilt over the surprise of 7 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By mid-April 1942, working mainly on the one system JN 25, it was intercepting, decrypting, translating and passing on messages within 6 hours of their transmission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By mid-May the decoders knew a blow was coming against Midway and the Aleutians, but the code letters AF, which Rochefort and Layton read as Midway, left room for doubt, and Nimitz at first thought Oahu the likelier target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By their later account they proposed a ruse in which Midway reported a breakdown of its water plant in plain language, after which a Japanese intelligence report said that AF was short of water, though no direct record of the episode has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 8 May HYPO had already identified a new striking force under Nagumo built round four carriers, and on 25 May its recovery of the date cipher let Rochefort predict the Aleutians attack for 3 June and Midway for 4 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nimitz based his final timetable on those dates despite objections from his own staff, and the Japanese striking force appeared off Midway on schedule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He said afterwards that without early information of the Japanese movement, and with his carriers caught dispersed, the battle would have ended far differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part one, \"The Battle of the Coral Sea,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartOne.html. [Open access]","Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part two, \"The Battles for Midway and the Aleutians,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartTwo.html. [Open access]","Henry F. Schorreck, \"The Role of COMINT in the Battle of Midway,\" SRH-230 (Fort Meade, MD: National Security Agency), HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-Midway.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), chapter 12, \"Drawing the Battle Line in the Pacific,\" 450–51, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-12.html. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, \"The Battle of Midway,\" article, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/battle-midway. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did one radio unit in Hawaii matter so much?","a":"It sat beside the fleet commander, and Rochefort and Layton worked so closely that what HYPO learned reached Nimitz within hours, letting him place carriers he could not afford to lose where the enemy was going to be."},{"q":"Why was the meaning of AF disputed?","a":"The Japanese used code letters for places, and only some had been identified; Midway fitted the pattern of letters near Hawaii, but Nimitz himself at first thought Oahu the likely target and did not rule out the American west coast."},{"q":"Why do historians treat the water ruse with caution?","a":"Both Rochefort and Layton described it, and a Japanese report that AF was short of water was recorded, but the message ordering the ruse has never been found, so its date and its exact part in the decision remain unproved."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Rochefort_WWII_naval_intel.jpg","title":"Joseph Rochefort","desc":"Joseph Rochefort, the officer in charge of the Pearl Harbor radio intelligence unit, photographed as a lieutenant commander; he was awarded the Distinguished Service Medal for Midway only after his death.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph NH 84826, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rochefort_WWII_naval_intel.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white head-and-shoulders portrait of a naval officer in uniform with a peaked cap."},"wiki":"Station HYPO"},
    "ww2-466": {"questions":["Losing four carriers, about 250 aircraft and some 100 of its first-line pilots at Midway left the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unable to lead further Japanese conquests, the bombing survey concluded.","American codebreakers followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a distinct communications unit and on 6 June 1942 noticed that its flag had moved from the Akagi to the cruiser Nagara."],"abstract":"The <b>First Air Fleet</b> was the Imperial Japanese Navy's carrier striking force, commanded by Chuichi Nagumo, which carried out the attack on Pearl Harbor and conducted the whole of the Japanese air attack at Midway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For Pearl Harbor it brought together six carriers, Akagi, Kaga, Soryu, Hiryu, Shokaku and Zuikaku, under Nagumo's direct command, screened by a light cruiser and destroyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its reports and orders appear under several names, among them Striking Force, Mobile Force and First Attack Force, but it was one organisation built round the fleet carriers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American radio analysts tracked it as a single communications entity, and in May 1942 they correctly identified a new striking force under Nagumo built round four of its carriers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Midway those four, Akagi and Kaga of the 1st Carrier Division and Soryu and Hiryu of the 2nd, formed the striking force that approached the atoll from the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The significance of Midway lies in what happened to this force, for all four carriers were lost, together with about 250 aircraft and some 100 first-line pilots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bombing survey concluded that this deprived Japan of the striking force with which it had made its conquests and had planned to blunt American counterattacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japan converted battleships and seaplane tenders into carriers, but the effort fell short, and from then on the balance of power in the Pacific shifted steadily towards the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American army history adds that the navy never replaced its carrier losses, so that Japanese land operations afterwards suffered a chronic shortage of naval and air support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nagumo's own report admitted the loss of all four carriers and the failure to occupy Midway, and the National WWII Museum puts Japan's dead in the battle at about 3,057.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter 2, \"The Japanese Attack on Pearl Harbor, Planning and Execution,\" 13–20, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-2.html. [Open access]","Chuichi Nagumo, \"CINC First Air Fleet Detailed Battle Report No. 6,\" translated in \"The Japanese Story of the Battle of Midway,\" <i>ONI Review</i> 2, no. 5 (May 1947), HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/IJN/rep/Midway/Nagumo/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter 5, \"The Battle of Midway,\" 57–60, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-5.html. [Open access]","Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part two, \"The Battles for Midway and the Aleutians,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartTwo.html. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Papua, 23 July 1942–23 January 1943</i>, CMH Pub 72-7 (Washington: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), 5–8, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-Papua/index.html. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, \"The Battle of Midway,\" article, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/battle-midway. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a single carrier force so important to Japan?","a":"Massing the fleet carriers under one command let Japan strike the American fleet at Pearl Harbor, an attack meant to keep it out of action while the southern conquests went ahead, and at Midway the same force carried the whole Japanese air attack."},{"q":"Why could Japan not simply build the force again?","a":"It converted battleships and seaplane tenders into carriers, but the bombing survey judged the effort insufficient, and an American army history notes that the navy never replaced its carrier losses, leaving later land campaigns short of naval and air support."},{"q":"Why did American codebreakers watch this force so closely?","a":"It was the force that had struck Pearl Harbor, and its signals made it a recognisable unit; when analysts in Hawaii identified a new striking force under Nagumo in May 1942, the early warning gave the Americans a major advantage before Midway."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Hiryu_burning_on_5_June_1942_%28NH_73064%29.jpg","title":"The Hiryu burning, 5 June 1942","desc":"The carrier Hiryu, last of the four carriers of the striking force, burning shortly after sunrise on 5 June 1942, photographed from an aircraft of the carrier Hosho; part of the forward lift has been thrown upright by an explosion.","credit":"Photograph by Ensign Kiyoshi Oniwa, Imperial Japanese Navy, Naval History and Heritage Command, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Hiryu_burning_on_5_June_1942_(NH_73064).jpg","alt":"An aerial view of an aircraft carrier at sea, its flight deck buckled and smoke rising from the forward part of the ship."},"wiki":"1st Air Fleet"},
    "ww2-467": {"questions":["About 13,000 Japanese soldiers and sailors were taken off by destroyers on 3 nights of February 1943, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which some 9,000 of their comrades had died of disease.","Ordered on 18 September 1942 to pull back from the Kokoda Track, the Japanese in Papua were made to wait on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Imperial General Headquarters now put above everything else."],"abstract":"The <b>Guadalcanal campaign</b> was the 6-month struggle for the island of Guadalcanal in the southern Solomons, from the American landings of 7 August 1942 to the Japanese evacuation in February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese had staked out an airstrip on the plains of the Lunga River in June 1942 and began grading a runway in July, and American planners feared that aircraft based there would cut the sea lanes to Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 7 August the reinforced 1st Marine Division landed at Tulagi and Guadalcanal, opening the American counteroffensive in the Pacific and capturing the partly built airstrip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Marines named it Henderson Field after a hero of Midway, and holding it became the object of extremely bitter fighting on land, at sea and in the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 2 nights after the landing a Japanese cruiser force surprised the Allied screen off Savo Island and sank four heavy cruisers, among them HMAS Canberra, which lost 84 of her crew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> For months afterwards the Japanese ran troops onto the island at night in fast destroyers, the Tokyo Express, but their attacks on the airfield failed one after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The struggle drained strength from New Guinea, where on 18 September the force on the Kokoda Track was ordered back because everything was to be subordinated to retaking Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After their great convoy was destroyed in November the Japanese never again sent transports to the island, and destroyers took off about 13,000 survivors between 1 and 8 February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 60,000 American soldiers and Marines served there, of whom some 1,600 were killed and 4,245 wounded, while of more than 36,000 Japanese over 14,800 were killed or missing and 9,000 died of disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese army records counted 21,600 deaths from battle and disease, an American estimate of the time put Japanese casualties at 28,580, and Japan also lost over 600 aircraft with their pilots and the initiative it had held since Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter 7, \"The Solomons Campaign,\" 104–26, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-7.html. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>Guadalcanal: The First Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1949), chapter XV, \"Final Operations on Guadalcanal,\" 336–50, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Guadalcanal/USA-P-Guadalcanal-15.html. [Open access]","Frank O. Hough, Verle E. Ludwig and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Pearl Harbor to Guadalcanal</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 1 (Washington: Historical Branch, U.S. Marine Corps, 1958), part VI, chapter 1, \"Background and Preparations,\" 235–38, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/I/USMC-I-VI-1.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Sinking of HMAS Canberra in Battle of Savo Island,\" Encyclopedia, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/ww2_navy/savo. [Open access]","Elizabeth Collins, \"The Navy at Guadalcanal: Series of Naval Engagements Ends 75 Years Ago,\" Defense Media Activity, 30 November 2017, DVIDS, https://www.dvidshub.net/news/260499/navy-guadalcanal-series-naval-engagements-ends-75-years-ago. [Open access]","Samuel Milner, <i>Victory in Papua</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chapter VI, \"The Japanese Offensive Collapses,\" 95–99, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Papua/USA-P-Papua-6.html. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Papua, 23 July 1942–23 January 1943</i>, CMH Pub 72-7 (Washington: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), 5–8, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-Papua/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did one half-built airstrip matter so much?","a":"Aircraft based there could threaten the sea lanes between the United States and Australia, and once the Americans held it as Henderson Field, Japanese commanders later admitted that American air superiority from the island left their army unable to move."},{"q":"Why did Guadalcanal affect the fighting in New Guinea?","a":"Japan treated the Solomons and New Guinea as parts of one campaign but could not supply both, so it reinforced Guadalcanal at New Guinea's expense and ordered its troops on the Kokoda Track back to the beachhead at Buna."},{"q":"Why did so many Japanese survive to be evacuated?","a":"The destroyers came in on three dark nights and the Americans did not realise at once that the enemy was leaving; after the war Japanese commanders said the Americans had moved too slowly and that a resolute attack at Cape Esperance would have destroyed their army."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Aerial_view_of_Henderson_Field%2C_Guadalcanal%2C_on_22_August_1942_%2880-G-12216%29.jpg","title":"Henderson Field, 22 August 1942","desc":"The airfield on Guadalcanal photographed from the air on 22 August 1942, two weeks after the Marines captured it; Grumman Wildcat fighters stand on the field and the buildings south of the runway were meant as workshops.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph 80-G-12216, Naval History and Heritage Command, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Henderson_Field,_Guadalcanal,_on_22_August_1942_(80-G-12216).jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a long cleared airstrip cut through palm groves and grassland, with small aircraft and low buildings beside it."},"wiki":"Guadalcanal campaign"},
    "ww2-468": {"questions":["The battleship Kirishima was left unnavigable by radar-directed gunfire from the Washington on the night of 14 November in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 2 nights after the Hiei was crippled.","Seven heavily laden transports were sunk or gutted on 14 November and the rest beached at Tassafaronga in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which Japan never again sent transports to the island."],"abstract":"The <b>Naval Battle of Guadalcanal</b> was the series of night surface actions and air attacks of 12 to 15 November 1942 in which American forces turned back the largest Japanese attempt to reinforce Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A convoy of 11 or 12 transports carrying about 10,000 troops of the 38th Division, with artillery and supplies, were to land after a bombardment had put Henderson Field out of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Early on 13 November Callaghan's cruisers and destroyers met a force led by the battleships Hiei and Kirishima near Savo Island in a confused action at close range in which ships of both sides fired on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Callaghan and Scott were both killed and the cruisers Atlanta and Juneau and four destroyers were sunk, but the bombardment was prevented and the crippled Hiei was scuttled after a day of air attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The five Sullivan brothers were aboard the Juneau when her magazine exploded, and only ten of her crew were eventually pulled from the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 14 November aircraft from Henderson Field and the carrier Enterprise caught the transports in the Slot and sank or gutted seven of them, though destroyers rescued some 5,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That night the battleships Washington and South Dakota met a Japanese force under Kondo, and the Washington's radar-directed fire left the Kirishima unnavigable, so that her captain ordered her scuttled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The four transports that reached the island beached themselves near Tassafaronga and were burned out the next day, and it was believed unlikely that any large number of men or supplies got ashore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The navy's wartime account counted nine American ships sunk against 26 Japanese by Pacific Fleet reckoning, but noted that only two battleships, a heavy cruiser, five destroyers and ten transports or cargo ships were certainly lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Afterwards the Japanese never again sent transports to Guadalcanal and relied on the destroyers of the Tokyo Express alone, and the same account judged that the decisive action of the campaign had been fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>Battle of Guadalcanal, 11–15 November 1942</i>, Combat Narratives (Washington, 1944; repr. Naval Historical Center), iii–iv, 83–84, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/USN-CN-Guadalcanal/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter 7, \"The Solomons Campaign,\" 123–26, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-7.html. [Open access]","Frank O. Hough, Verle E. Ludwig and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Pearl Harbor to Guadalcanal</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 1 (Washington: Historical Branch, U.S. Marine Corps, 1958), part VI, chapter 8, \"Critical November,\" 352–54, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/I/USMC-I-VI-8.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), chapter 2, \"The Battle for Guadalcanal,\" 57–59, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-2.html. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>Guadalcanal: The First Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1949), chapter XV, \"Final Operations on Guadalcanal,\" 336–50, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Guadalcanal/USA-P-Guadalcanal-15.html. [Open access]","Elizabeth Collins, \"The Navy at Guadalcanal: Series of Naval Engagements Ends 75 Years Ago,\" Defense Media Activity, 30 November 2017, DVIDS, https://www.dvidshub.net/news/260499/navy-guadalcanal-series-naval-engagements-ends-75-years-ago. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese risk a convoy of slow transports?","a":"The fast destroyers of the Tokyo Express could not load or unload tanks and heavy artillery, and without such weapons their troops had failed to break the American perimeter in October, so a convoy of large transports was assembled to carry them."},{"q":"Why did American cruisers take on battleships?","a":"Although far weaker in gun power, they were the only ships at hand that could stop the bombardment of Henderson Field and the landing that was to follow, so they went out towards Savo Island to turn the Japanese back."},{"q":"Why were the Japanese losses overstated at the time?","a":"Much of the fighting was at night and at close range, where reports from different ships disagreed; the navy's own account set the Pacific Fleet's figure of 26 ships sunk beside a smaller list of losses it regarded as certain."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Japanese_transports_Hirokawa_Maru_and_Kinugawa_Maru_beached_and_burning_on_Guadalcanal%2C_15_November_1942_%2880-G-67248%29.jpg","title":"Transports burning at Tassafaronga, 15 November 1942","desc":"The Japanese transports Hirokawa Maru and Kinugawa Maru beached and burning on the coast of Guadalcanal on 15 November 1942, at the end of the failed attempt to land reinforcements.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph 80-G-67248, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_transports_Hirokawa_Maru_and_Kinugawa_Maru_beached_and_burning_on_Guadalcanal,_15_November_1942_(80-G-67248).jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a coastline where two ships lie against the shore, with thick white smoke drifting inland over the trees."},"wiki":"Naval Battle of Guadalcanal"},
    "ww2-469": {"questions":["Japanese submarines carried only some 3,500 tons of cargo in 95 trips during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the Allies moved more than 200,000 tons forward in September 1943 alone.","An American army history counted 110,000 Japanese dead from battle, disease and starvation between Wau and Sansapor in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with another 30,000 cut off and left behind."],"abstract":"The <b>New Guinea campaign</b> was the Allied war against Japan on New Guinea, from the Japanese landings at Lae and Salamaua in March 1942 to the capture of Sansapor near the island's western end on 30 July 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The island stretches some 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles), and with almost no roads through its jungle and mountains both sides moved by sea along the coast and relied on air power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1942 the push on Port Moresby was turned back in the Coral Sea, on the Kokoda Track and at Milne Bay, where Japanese troops were forced to evacuate for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Papuan fighting ended at Buna and Sanananda in January 1943, costing about 13,000 Japanese dead and 8,500 Allied battle casualties, 5,698 of them Australian, with 27,000 cases of malaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Japan treated the Solomons as its more important front, and its commanders reinforced Guadalcanal at New Guinea's expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In March 1943 Allied aircraft destroyed a convoy carrying about 5,000 troops to Lae in the Bismarck Sea, after which the Japanese could reach the north coast only by barge, small craft and submarine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In September 1943 sea landings and a parachute drop at Nadzab forced the Japanese out of Lae, and in April 1944 MacArthur bypassed Hansa Bay and Wewak to land at Hollandia and Aitape, trapping 40,000 Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before Hollandia the Allies took more than 24,000 battle casualties, about 70 per cent Australian, to advance some 480 kilometres (300 miles) in 20 months, but then 9,500 carried them 2,100 kilometres (1,300 miles) in 100 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese losses were far greater: an American army history counts 110,000 dead from battle, disease and starvation between Wau and Sansapor, and an Australian naval study notes claims that combat caused only 3 per cent of Japan's deaths there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Supply decided it, for Japanese submarines carried some 3,500 tons of cargo to New Guinea in 95 trips over 10 months, while in September 1943 alone the Allies moved over 200,000 tons forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Edward J. Drea, <i>New Guinea, 24 January 1943–31 December 1944</i>, CMH Pub 72-9 (Washington: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1993), 3, 10, 30–31, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-NewGuinea/index.html. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Papua, 23 July 1942–23 January 1943</i>, CMH Pub 72-7 (Washington: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), 5–8, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-Papua/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), chapter VIII, \"The New Guinea Campaign,\" 172–75, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-8.html. [Open access]","David Stevens, \"The Naval Campaigns for New Guinea,\" <i>Journal of the Australian War Memorial</i>, no. 34, paras. 22, 29–30, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/journal/j34/stevens. [Open access]","<i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i> (Washington: Department of the Army, 1966), chapter VI, \"The Westward Drive along New Guinea,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-6.html. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>Guadalcanal: The First Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1949), chapter XV, \"Final Operations on Guadalcanal,\" 336–50, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Guadalcanal/USA-P-Guadalcanal-15.html. [Open access]","<i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i> (Washington: Department of the Army, 1966), chapter IV, \"Clearing of Papua,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-4.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did both sides fight along the coast rather than inland?","a":"New Guinea's jungle and mountains had almost no roads, and none were built that mattered militarily, so armies moved by sea from one coastal point to the next and air power and air transport counted for more than in most theatres."},{"q":"Why did most Japanese soldiers on the island die without fighting?","a":"Once the Allies controlled the sea and air off the coast, Japanese garrisons were in effect blockaded, and illness and malnutrition killed far more men than combat; a Japanese survivor of Buna said they lost because they could not supply their troops."},{"q":"Why did the advance speed up so sharply in 1944?","a":"Amphibious landings let the Allies bypass strongly held points and come ashore where the enemy was weak, and air superiority let the navy carry the troops forward safely, so each leap was longer and cost fewer lives than the frontal attacks of 1942 and 1943."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Allied_amphibious_forces_off_Hollandia%2C_New_Guinea%2C_22_April_1944.jpg","title":"Off Hollandia, 22 April 1944","desc":"Allied landing ships and transports gathered in Humboldt Bay off Hollandia on 22 April 1944, the day of the landings that bypassed the Japanese bases at Hansa Bay and Wewak.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph from the USS Bunker Hill cruise book, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_amphibious_forces_off_Hollandia,_New_Guinea,_22_April_1944.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a wide bay crowded with ships close to a hilly, forested shore."},"wiki":"New Guinea campaign"},
    "ww2-470": {"questions":["Many Papuan carriers remembered as Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had in fact been conscripted under an order of June 1942, nearly 5,000 of them in August alone.","Ordered back on 18 September 1942 because everything was to be subordinated to retaking Guadalcanal, Horii's starving troops ended their advance in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within 50 kilometres of Port Moresby."],"abstract":"The <b>Kokoda Track campaign</b> was the fighting of July to November 1942 on the mountain path between Port Moresby and Kokoda in Papua, where Australian troops halted and drove back a Japanese overland advance on Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Short of carriers after Midway, the Japanese decided to take Port Moresby by marching from Buna across the Owen Stanley Range, which rises to about 4,000 metres (13,000 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A force under Tomitaro Horii landed near Buna on 22 July and pushed inland through Kokoda, a village with an airfield some 80 kilometres (50 miles) from Buna and more than 160 kilometres (100 miles) from Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reinforced in August to about 8,000 army and 3,450 naval troops, Horii's men pushed the Australians back and took Ioribaiwa on 16 September, while the Australians held the Imita Range a day's march behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both names have been used since the war: Track, from the language of the bush, used by veterans and the official history, and Trail, adopted by the army as a battle honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> By then the Japanese had outrun their supplies, as Allied aircraft kept breaking the bridge at Wairopi, and by 17 September there was not a grain of rice left on the ridge for issue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Setbacks at Milne Bay and on Guadalcanal had already brought orders to halt, and on 18 September Horii was told to withdraw to Buna because everything was to be subordinated to retaking Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Australian 7th Division advanced from late September, retook Kokoda and its airfield on 2 November, and broke the Japanese at Oivi and Gorari, turning their withdrawal into a rout across the Kumusi River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Allied effort depended on Papuan carriers, whose popular image as willing Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels hides the fact that many were conscripted under an order of June 1942, 4,947 in August alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Australian War Memorial calls the campaign arguably Australia's most significant of the war, and the Papuan fighting cost the Allies 8,500 battle casualties, 5,698 of them Australian, against about 13,000 Japanese dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charles R. Anderson, <i>Papua, 23 July 1942–23 January 1943</i>, CMH Pub 72-7 (Washington: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), 5–8, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-Papua/index.html. [Open access]","Samuel Milner, <i>Victory in Papua</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chapter VI, \"The Japanese Offensive Collapses,\" 95–99, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Papua/USA-P-Papua-6.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Kokoda,\" Encyclopedia, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/kokoda. [Open access]","Emma Rogerson, \"The “Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels”: Looking beyond the Myth,\" Summer Vacation Scholarship paper (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 2012), 11–12, https://www.awm.gov.au/sites/default/files/svss_2012_rogerson_paper.pdf. [Open access]","<i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i> (Washington: Department of the Army, 1966), chapter IV, \"Clearing of Papua,\" HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-4.html. [Open access]","Samuel Milner, <i>Victory in Papua</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chapter VII, \"The Advance on the Beachhead,\" 119–20, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Papua/USA-P-Papua-7.html. [Open access]","Edward J. Drea, <i>New Guinea, 24 January 1943–31 December 1944</i>, CMH Pub 72-9 (Washington: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1993), 3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-NewGuinea/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese try to cross the mountains on foot?","a":"The carrier losses at Midway made another seaborne landing at Port Moresby too risky, so they chose an overland advance from Buna through Kokoda, across some of the most forbidding terrain in the world."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese stop at Ioribaiwa when Port Moresby was so close?","a":"Their supply line over the mountains had collapsed under air attack and the front-line troops were out of rice, and Tokyo, unable to win on Guadalcanal and in Papua at once, ordered Horii to halt and then to fall back to the coast."},{"q":"Why is the image of the Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels questioned?","a":"It presents the carriers as willing volunteers, but wartime records show that many were conscripted under a military order, recruited with propaganda and sometimes force, and that numbers fell away through illness and desertion as the campaign went on."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Australian_39th_Battalion_after_the_Kokoda_Track_campaign_1942_%28AWM_013289%29.jpg","title":"The 39th Battalion, September 1942","desc":"Men of the 39th Battalion, a militia unit, on parade on 6 September 1942 after weeks of fighting on the Kokoda Track; their ragged clothing shows what the campaign had cost them.","credit":"Photograph held by the Australian War Memorial, copyright status recorded as clear, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_39th_Battalion_after_the_Kokoda_Track_campaign_1942_(AWM_013289).jpg","alt":"A line of thin, unshaven soldiers in worn uniforms and slouch hats leaning on rifles in front of a thatched hut, with an officer standing facing them."},"wiki":"Kokoda Track campaign"},
    "ww2-401": {"questions":["Japanese staff officers in November 1941 folded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the coming war against the United States, Britain and the Netherlands, naming Chiang among the enemies.","Washington spoke of an undeclared war where Tokyo spoke of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Roosevelt left the Neutrality Act unapplied, barring only government-owned ships from carrying arms."],"abstract":"The <b>China Incident</b> was the name Japan's government and high command gave to the war they fought in China from July 1937, and Tokyo's policy papers kept to it year after year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 13 July 1937 the Supreme Command adopted a 'Policy for the Treatment of the North China Incident', promising to localise the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1 October the government had adopted an 'Outline Regarding the Settlement of the China Incident', which looked for a speedy end through armed force combined with timely diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Washington called it Japan's undeclared war on China, and on 14 September 1937 Roosevelt left the Neutrality Act unapplied, barring only government-owned ships from carrying arms to either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No declaration ever came, and in June 1938 the war minister Itagaki told a news agency that none was needed, while warning that the army must be ready to fight on perhaps for 10 more years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The name promised a short affair: Japan hoped the battle for Xuzhou would destroy China's main armies, and then planned to present the capture of Hankow as the end of the military phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> China's Nationalist government described its own strategy as trading space for time, and by its count Japan had to raise its divisions in China from 25 before Nanjing fell to 31 for the Wuhan campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A historian at Japan's National Institute for Defense Studies judges that taking Wuhan and Guangzhou brought the army almost to the limit of what it could mobilise, while Chiang Kai-shek fought on from Chongqing and the war settled into deadlock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Preparing for war with the United States, Britain and the Netherlands, Japanese staff officers folded the incident into it, and the plan adopted on 15 November 1941 named 'Chiang' beside those three powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After December 1941 the China front became a secondary one for Japan, and its armies there turned mainly to holding the occupied areas and containing Nationalist counter-offensives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chap. 5, \"Japanese Aggression Against China,\" 685–741, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-5a.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, <i>Peace and War: United States Foreign Policy, 1931–1941</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1943), 46–48, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Dip/PaW/PaW-7.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, July 19, 1938, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d219. [Open access]","Noriaki Yashiro, \"Japanese Strategy in the Second Phase of the Pacific War,\" in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, 2009 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 79–80, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/07.pdf. [Open access]","Nobu Iwatani, \"The Japanese Counterinsurgency Operations in Mainland China,\" in <i>Irregular Warfare in Historical Perspectives</i>, 2017 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 59, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2017/06_iwatani.pdf. [Open access]","Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 350–51, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan call a war of this size an incident?","a":"Its leaders expected a short campaign and kept hoping that one more victory, at Xuzhou or at Hankow, would make China give way. No formal declaration was made, and in June 1938 the war minister told the press that none was needed even as he warned of perhaps 10 more years of fighting."},{"q":"Why did the United States not apply its Neutrality Act?","a":"With no war declared by either side, Roosevelt chose in September 1937 to leave the question where it stood. He stopped only government-owned ships from carrying arms to China or Japan, and warned that other American ships doing so sailed at their own risk."},{"q":"Why did the name last after the war had stalled?","a":"It stayed in Tokyo's papers through the deadlock that followed the fall of Wuhan, until planners in November 1941 folded the incident into a wider war against the United States, Britain, the Netherlands and Chiang's China."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/China_Incident_War_Medal_01.JPG","title":"Japan's China Incident War Medal","desc":"The campaign medal Japan issued for service in the war in China, which carried the official name of the conflict rather than calling it a war.","credit":"Photograph by Uaauaa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Incident_War_Medal_01.JPG","alt":"A bronze medal with a sunburst and chrysanthemum design hanging from a clasp with Japanese characters and a ribbon striped in red, yellow, blue and purple."},"wiki":"Marco Polo Bridge incident"},
    "ww2-402": {"questions":["The American ambassador found China's leaders unusually self-confident in early 1937, at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after Japanese-directed irregulars had been driven out of Suiyuan.","Chiang Kai-shek spent much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> trying to crush the Communists while opposing Japan only by diplomacy, until Zhang Xueliang's men kidnapped him in December 1936."],"abstract":"The <b>Nanjing decade</b> was the period in which China's Nationalist government ruled from Nanjing, declared the national capital in April 1927, until the Japanese invasion of 1937 drove it inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In those years the country gave the appearance of coalescing steadily into one state, as finances improved, manufactures grew and Chiang Kai-shek brought one warlord after another to heel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1937 the one major dissident force left was the Communists, compressed into the far north-west after the Long March had cut their army from over 300,000 men to about 10,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chiang's preferred course was to crush them while opposing Japan by diplomacy alone, until a group led by Zhang Xueliang kidnapped him in December 1936 and made him agree to a United Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At its plenary session of February 1937, from which several provincial leaders stayed away, the Kuomintang set terms for the Communists, including the abolition of the Red Army, and promised to negotiate with Japan without giving up sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The American ambassador found the government in a new mood of security and self-reliance, convinced that Japan wished to avoid major operations and heartened by the repulse of Japanese-directed irregulars in Suiyuan in November 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet he judged that the Tangku Truce of 1933 had cost China military jurisdiction over some 13,000 square kilometres (5,000 square miles) of Hebei, the ground on which the East Hebei regime and the smuggling of Japanese goods rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For its army the government had turned to Germany, and by 1937 a German military mission had brought about 30 divisions loyal to Chiang to a standard of efficiency never before known in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American officer called these the 'Generalissimo's Own', some 300,000 men led by graduates of the Whampoa and Central academies, while many provincial troops were badly led and preyed on the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German-trained troops fought in the defence of the lower Yangtze, but by 1939 Japan held the railways, the seaports and the key cities of China, Nanjing among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 50–51, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 4–6, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-1.html. [Open access]","Nobu Iwatani, \"The Japanese Counterinsurgency Operations in Mainland China,\" in <i>Irregular Warfare in Historical Perspectives</i>, 2017 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 60–61, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2017/06_iwatani.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, February 23, 1937, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d25. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, No. 392, March 22, 1937,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d46. [Open access]","Evans Fordyce Carlson, <i>The Chinese Army: Its Organization and Military Efficiency</i> (New York: Institute of Pacific Relations, 1940), 29–30, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.503348. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Chiang put the Communists before Japan?","a":"His new government did not have the resources to deal with both at once, and he preferred to crush the Communists while opposing Japan by diplomacy alone. Chinese opinion, outraged by Japan, did not approve, and his kidnapping in December 1936 forced the United Front on him."},{"q":"Why were Chinese leaders more confident in early 1937?","a":"They had come to believe that Japan wanted to avoid a major war in China, Japanese demands had been settled one by one rather than as a package, and Japanese-directed irregulars had been driven out of Suiyuan in November 1936."},{"q":"Why did the German-trained divisions not save the coast and cities?","a":"They were about 30 divisions in a much larger and uneven army. They fought hard on the lower Yangtze, but Japan still took the railways, ports and key cities, and the government fell back into the interior."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Chiang_Kai-shek%2C_Yu_Ta-wei%2C_and_Alexander_von_Falkenhausen_inspecting_imported_German-made_weapons.png","title":"Chiang Kai-shek inspecting German weapons, before 1938","desc":"Chiang Kai-shek with Yu Ta-wei, head of the ordnance department, and Alexander von Falkenhausen, chief of the German military advisers, inspecting weapons imported from Germany.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Kai-shek,_Yu_Ta-wei,_and_Alexander_von_Falkenhausen_inspecting_imported_German-made_weapons.png","alt":"A group of officers and men in civilian suits stand around a large gun on an open hillside, with soldiers lined up behind them."},"wiki":"Nanjing decade"},
    "ww2-403": {"questions":["Germany recalled the military advisers who had trained about 30 divisions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in May 1938, telling them their service was out of line with German neutrality.","Chinese Communist troops became part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in September 1937, reorganised as the Eighth Route Army under commanders who swore allegiance to Chiang Kai-shek."],"abstract":"The <b>National Revolutionary Army</b> was the army of China's Nationalist government, which met Japan's invasion from 1937 under the command of Chiang Kai-shek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chiang held supreme command of land, naval and air forces as chairman of the National Military Council, and in 1943 the government said it was clothing the nation's 6,000,000 troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seeking a modern army, the Nationalists had turned to Germany, Italy and Russia for advice and arms, and a German military mission had raised about 30 divisions loyal to Chiang to a standard never before known in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the United Front even the Communist forces of the north-west were reorganised as government troops, the Eighth Route Army, whose commanders swore allegiance and obedience to Chiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By early 1938 the army had withdrawn from North China, keeping only some troops in southern Shanxi and leaving a vacuum that the Japanese, holding only railways and cities, could not fill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> American officers in 1941 saw a coalition of commanders' forces rather than a unified national army, in which the German-trained and better provincial divisions, perhaps 40 in all, were mediocre by Western standards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They counted about 3,819,000 men under arms in 246 front-line and 70 rear divisions, and judged that only Chiang's personal troops, about 30 divisions, were loyal to him rather than to their war-area commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A division was treated as its commander's property, so a general who lost a third of his men lost a third of his power, and commanders were slow to spend men or equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany told its advisers in May 1938 that serving China was out of line with its neutrality, and the American ambassador expected their departure to depress the Chinese, whom they had served well and loyally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the army abandoned Wuhan in October 1938, the National Military Council explained that it attached no importance to holding any particular place, and that its aim was to prolong the war and preserve China's capacity to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 5, 32–35, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-1.html. [Open access]","Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 321–24, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, September 13, 1937, 8 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d586. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, May 26, 1938, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d170. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in Germany (Wilson) to the Secretary of State, June 2, 1938, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d177. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, October 25, 1938, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d330. [Open access]","Nobu Iwatani, \"The Japanese Counterinsurgency Operations in Mainland China,\" in <i>Irregular Warfare in Historical Perspectives</i>, 2017 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 59, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2017/06_iwatani.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Chinese commanders reluctant to risk their divisions?","a":"A division was its commander's military and political asset, and no replacements of men or equipment were coming. American observers believed that a commander who lost a third of his men lost a third of his power and income, so most kept their units out of costly fights."},{"q":"Why did Germany withdraw its advisers in 1938?","a":"Berlin said that once the fighting had grown into large-scale war, service with the Chinese army was out of line with German neutrality. The American ambassador in Hankow believed that Japanese pressure lay behind the move."},{"q":"Why did the army give up Wuhan without a last stand?","a":"Its command said that the strategy was a prolonged war in which no single place mattered. Holding on would have let the Japanese trap and destroy its main forces, while withdrawing kept them to fight on and protected the move of men and resources inland."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/NH_77779.jpg","title":"A Nationalist soldier, November 1937","desc":"A soldier of China's Nationalist army with a Mauser rifle, photographed on 26 November 1937 and kept in the collection of the United States Naval History and Heritage Command.","credit":"Naval History and Heritage Command NH 77779, courtesy of Vice Admiral Morton L. Deyo, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NH_77779.jpg","alt":"A young soldier in a German-style steel helmet, a cotton uniform with shorts and puttees, holding a rifle across his body on a city street."},"wiki":"National Revolutionary Army"},
    "ww2-404": {"questions":["China's Communist forces in the north-west became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in September 1937, with Zhu De in command and Peng Dehuai as his deputy under the National Military Council.","Base areas around the Wutai and Taihang mountains were built by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from late 1937, while Japanese garrisons in North China held little more than railways and cities."],"abstract":"The <b>Eighth Route Army</b> was the name under which China's Communist forces in the north-west were reorganised as government troops in 1937, with Zhu De as commander and Peng Dehuai as his deputy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party's declaration of 22 September 1937 placed the former Red Army under the National Military Council, and the force was later renamed the 18th Group Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nationalist officials insisted this was no popular front, saying the Communists had given up propaganda and class struggle and that their commanders had sworn obedience to Chiang Kai-shek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American Marine officer who studied it found an army in which leadership rested on merit, leaders and fighters ate and dressed alike, and political commissars shared authority with commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its first rule, he reported, was to carry out the anti-Japanese patriotic principles, and its third was not to take the smallest thing from the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Mao Zedong insisted on independent guerrilla warfare in the mountains, and the party adopted his view rather than the frontal battles beside the Nationalists that many of its leaders had first planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In October and November 1937 the army began building base areas around the Wutai and Taihang mountains, and a year later a Japanese drive took Wutai and drove one of its headquarters away with the loss of ammunition and supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Foreigners who toured the guerrilla areas of Hebei and Shanxi in 1938 found them growing in strength and wrecking railways, though observers were surprised at how little they achieved against Japanese outposts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Japanese army at first dismissed the Communists as bandits, and only after large-scale sabotage in the summer of 1940 did it come to treat them as a real threat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the party's own later count the Eighth Route and New Fourth armies grew from just over 40,000 men in 1937 to 1 million by 1945, and a Japanese defence study puts the Communist forces at 500,000 by the end of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, September 13, 1937, 8 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d586. [Open access]","Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 50–51, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","Mao Tse-tung, \"Problems of Strategy in Guerrilla War Against Japan,\" May 1938, with the editors' introductory note and notes 2–3, in <i>Selected Works of Mao Tse-tung</i>, vol. 2, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/mao/selected-works/volume-2/mswv2_08.htm. [Open access]","Nobu Iwatani, \"The Japanese Counterinsurgency Operations in Mainland China,\" in <i>Irregular Warfare in Historical Perspectives</i>, 2017 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 59–62, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2017/06_iwatani.pdf. [Open access]","Evans Fordyce Carlson, <i>The Chinese Army: Its Organization and Military Efficiency</i> (New York: Institute of Pacific Relations, 1940), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.503348. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The First Secretary of Embassy in China (Salisbury) to the Secretary of State, August 23, 1938, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d254. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Lockhart) to the Secretary of State, October 27, 1938, 1 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d343. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nationalist officials deny this was a popular front?","a":"They presented the change as the Communists submerging themselves in the national army, giving up propaganda and class struggle, with their commanders sworn to obey Chiang, rather than as a partnership between two parties."},{"q":"Why did the army fight mainly as guerrillas?","a":"It was poorly equipped, and Mao argued that independent guerrilla warfare in the mountains would let it survive and grow while China fought a protracted war. The party adopted his view, and the army built base areas behind the Japanese lines."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese take so long to see the danger?","a":"Their army at first regarded the Communists as bandits who would soon disappear. Only after large-scale sabotage in the summer of 1940 did it treat them as a real threat and turn its security campaigns against them."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Zhu_De_Evans_Carlson_1937.jpg","title":"Zhu De with Evans Carlson, December 1937","desc":"Zhu De, commander of the Communist forces, with the United States Marine officer Evans Carlson at the Eighth Route Army's field headquarters in Shanxi province.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhu_De_Evans_Carlson_1937.jpg","alt":"Two men stand side by side in a sunny courtyard, one in a padded cotton uniform and cap, the other in a heavy coat, checked scarf, fur hat and riding boots."},"wiki":"Eighth Route Army"},
    "ww2-405": {"questions":["Tsinghua, Peking and Nankai universities moved first to Changsha and then to Kunming to keep teaching in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where they formed the National Southwest Associated University.","Trenched roads, dismantled railways and barricaded passes gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a buffer up to 160 kilometres deep after the fall of Wuhan, as a long stalemate settled over the front."],"abstract":"<b>Free China</b> was the wartime name for the parts of China still held by the Nationalist government, mainly in the west and south-west, after Japan had taken the seaports, the railways and the key cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As Nanjing was threatened in November 1937 the government told the American embassy that its seat was now Chongqing, though the Foreign Office would work from Hankow for the time being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the war modern industry had been concentrated in a few coastal cities, and the region later called Free China had not a single blast furnace and only 17,000 of China's 5,000,000 spindles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 639 private factories moved inland with more than 116,000 tons of equipment and 12,164 skilled workers, 254 of them to Sichuan, and 70 per cent had resumed work by 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Universities followed: Tsinghua, Peking and Nankai moved to Changsha and then to Kunming, where they became the National Southwest Associated University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the army gave up Wuhan in October 1938, the National Military Council said the real point of defending it had been to cover the transfer of men and material from the south-east and centre to the north-west and south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind the new front the Chinese trenched roads, dismantled railways, removed bridges and barricaded mountain passes to create a buffer 80 to 160 kilometres (50 to 100 miles) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Yellow River flood of 1938 also served as a barrier, cutting Luoyang and Zhengzhou off from the war zone and, in the words of one Chinese study, laying a foundation for a long war in the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Links with the outside world were thin, and after the fall of Canton and Hankow the road from the Soviet Union through Xinjiang and Xi'an was one of the few channels of supply left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A stalemate settled over the front, broken by Japanese forays and two abortive Chinese offensives in 1939, while Nationalist China sought closer ties with Britain, France, the Soviet Union and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 369–70, 436–38, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, November 18, 1937, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d758. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, October 25, 1938, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d330. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 5–6, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-1.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Lockhart) to the Secretary of State, October 27, 1938, 1 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d343. [Open access]","Hucheng Li, Bingbing Wang, Jun Yan, Baorui Qiu and Fei Zhuo, \"Environmental Impact of Huayuankou Dike Breach in 1938,\" in <i>Proceedings of the 2015 4th International Conference on Sustainable Energy and Environmental Engineering</i> (Paris: Atlantis Press, 2016), 1105–8, https://doi.org/10.2991/icseee-15.2016.196. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Wuhan defended if it was to be given up?","a":"The Nationalist command said that its real purpose was to buy time to move men, factories and resources to the west, not to keep the three cities. Fighting to the end there would have let the Japanese corner and destroy its main forces."},{"q":"Why did factories have to move so far?","a":"Modern industry had been concentrated in coastal cities such as Shanghai, Canton and Tianjin, which lay in Japan's path, while the interior was farming country with almost no industry, no blast furnace and few spindles."},{"q":"Why was Free China so hard to supply?","a":"Japan held the seaports and the railways, so after Canton and Hankow fell supplies had to come by a few long overland routes, such as the road from the Soviet Union through Xinjiang and Xi'an."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/The_Gate%2C_National_Southwestern_Associated_University%2C_1938.jpg","title":"Gate of the National Southwest Associated University, 1938","desc":"The gate of the university formed in Kunming by Tsinghua, Peking and Nankai universities after they had fled the Japanese advance, one of the institutions that moved into the unoccupied interior.","credit":"National Southwestern Associated University, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Gate,_National_Southwestern_Associated_University,_1938.jpg","alt":"A plain gateway of two square pillars with a signboard of Chinese characters across the top, opening onto a dirt road and low buildings."},"wiki":"Free China (Second Sino-Japanese War)"},
    "ww2-406": {"questions":["Japanese army planners in 1942 wanted to knock down the government in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by invading Sichuan, but the failure at Guadalcanal ended preparations for the planned offensive.","The National Government announced the temporary removal of the capital to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 20 November 1937, and the city's population had more than doubled from its 1927 figure by 1939."],"abstract":"<b>Chongqing</b> is the city at the meeting of the Yangtze and Jialing rivers in south-west China that became the wartime capital when the National Government announced the temporary removal of the capital there on 20 November 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American embassy had been told 2 days earlier that the seat of government was definitely Chongqing, where Lin Sen, the head of state, already was, while the Foreign Office would work from Hankow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city's population, 208,294 in 1927, had risen to about 550,000 by 1939, even as evacuations were ordered to thin it out against air raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made a special municipality under the Executive Yuan on 5 May 1939 and proclaimed an auxiliary capital on 6 September 1940, when its municipal area was tripled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the fall of Canton and Hankow in October 1938 it was the seat of Chiang Kai-shek's wartime government, shielded by a belt of wrecked roads, railways and bridges in front of the western mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A study from Japan's National Institute for Defense Studies describes the Japanese army, unable to finish the war by force while Chiang resisted from Chongqing, turning to political manoeuvring instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The best-known manoeuvre came on 18 December 1938, when Wang Jingwei, vice-chairman of the Kuomintang, left Chongqing in a departure arranged in advance with Japanese officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The heavy raids of May 1939 showed how exposed the city was, yet the American chargé reported that he had not heard any suggestion that the temporary capital be moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1942 the Japanese army still argued for knocking down the 'Chongqing government' before turning on Britain, and began preparing an invasion of Sichuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That autumn, after the failure to retake Guadalcanal, Imperial General Headquarters told the emperor that it would stop preparations for the offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 792–95, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, November 18, 1937, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d758. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 6, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-1.html. [Open access]","Nobu Iwatani, \"The Japanese Counterinsurgency Operations in Mainland China,\" in <i>Irregular Warfare in Historical Perspectives</i>, 2017 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 59, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2017/06_iwatani.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chap. 5, \"Japanese Aggression Against China,\" 738–39, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-5a.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, May 5, 1939, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East; The Near East and Africa, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v04/d378. [Open access]","Noriaki Yashiro, \"Japanese Strategy in the Second Phase of the Pacific War,\" in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, 2009 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 89–92, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/07.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government settle so far up the Yangtze?","a":"Chongqing had long been the key to Sichuan and the provinces further west, and its wartime mayor called it the most proper place for a wartime capital. Behind mountains and a belt of wrecked roads and railways, it lay beyond the reach of Japan's armies."},{"q":"Why did Japan turn to politics to end the war?","a":"After Wuhan and Guangzhou its army was close to the limit of what Japan could mobilise, and Chiang would not yield. Tokyo therefore tried to undermine the Chongqing government by political means, of which Wang Jingwei's defection was the most important."},{"q":"Why was the invasion of Sichuan planned and then dropped?","a":"In 1942 the army argued that knocking out Chongqing should come before an attack on Britain. The failure to retake Guadalcanal changed the war in the Pacific, and that autumn preparations for the offensive were stopped."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/%E4%BA%9C%E7%B4%B0%E4%BA%9C%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%B3_14_066_%22%E9%87%8D%E6%85%B6_%EF%BC%88%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E7%9C%81%EF%BC%89%22.jpg/1280px-%E4%BA%9C%E7%B4%B0%E4%BA%9C%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%B3_14_066_%22%E9%87%8D%E6%85%B6_%EF%BC%88%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E7%9C%81%EF%BC%89%22.jpg","title":"Chongqing on the Yangtze","desc":"Part of Chongqing's riverfront, photographed for the Japanese pictorial series <i>Ajia taikan</i>, whose caption described the city as built at the meeting of the Yangtze and the Jialing.","credit":"Photograph from <i>Ajia taikan</i>, published by the Kokuryūkai, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%9C%E7%B4%B0%E4%BA%9C%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%B3_14_066_%22%E9%87%8D%E6%85%B6_%EF%BC%88%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E7%9C%81%EF%BC%89%22.jpg","alt":"A wide river with a crowded built-up shore in the middle distance and hills behind, and a large house with a tiled roof in the foreground among trees."},"wiki":"Chongqing"},
    "ww2-407": {"questions":["Chongqing's air-defence headquarters counted 193 raids and 5,553 aircraft between January 1938 and September 1941 in its record of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which burned out much of the downtown.","An American diplomat found no justification for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 3 and 4 May 1939, whose fires swept business and residential districts that he believed were unguarded."],"abstract":"The <b>Bombing of Chongqing</b> was the Japanese air campaign against China's wartime capital, which the city's air-defence headquarters counted as 193 raids between 30 January 1938 and 1 September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its record put 5,553 aircraft over the city in that time, dropping 11,181 demolition and 1,800 incendiary bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first great raids came on 3 and 4 May 1939, and the American chargé reported large areas burned, more than 100,000 people said to have fled, and Chinese casualties of probably 1,000 to 2,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan's naval authorities said their target on 4 May had been the headquarters of the Military Council, and that heavy anti-aircraft fire near the foreign legations had forced other planes to attack the guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American embassy saw no justification for bombing extensive business and residence areas in the centre of the city, which as far as it could tell were unguarded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In June 1940 Ambassador Johnson concluded that General Itagaki had set out to demolish the city, scattering light and heavy demolition bombs through its business and residential areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also described a leaflet said to have been dropped from Japanese planes that showed Chongqing in flames, its streets strewn with dead, above a Nanjing at peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Incendiary bombs on 19 and 20 August 1940 gutted the downtown business section, and the Chinese afterwards rebuilt it with wider roads and simpler single-storey buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After a bomb fell close to the American gunboat Tutuila in July 1941, Tokyo called it an accident pure and simple and told Washington, in confidence, that it had decided to suspend all bombing over the city area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Within days the city endured unusually heavy and prolonged raids, with 18 alarms totalling close to 70 hours between 8 and 13 August, though the area opposite the American chancery and gunboat was spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 792–93, 818–19, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, May 5, 1939, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East; The Near East and Africa, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v04/d378. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Shanghai (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, May 5, 1939, 4 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East; The Near East and Africa, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v04/d379. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chargé in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, May 16, 1939, 8 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East; The Near East and Africa, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v04/d384. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, June 13, 1940, 10 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d966. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conversation, by the Acting Secretary of State (Welles), July 31, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d970. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, August 11, 1941, 6 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d972. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan bomb a city far beyond its armies' reach?","a":"Its armies were held far down the Yangtze, so bombing was the way to strike the capital itself; Ambassador Johnson concluded in 1940 that the aim was to demolish the city, and Japanese leaflets set its ruin beside a Nanjing at peace."},{"q":"Why did American protests make little headway at first?","a":"Japan's airmen said Chinese guns stood close to foreign property, and the embassy's own military attaché judged that legitimate targets were scattered through the city, which weakened any protest even as the embassy insisted the burned districts were unguarded."},{"q":"Why did the Tutuila affair matter?","a":"A near miss on an American warship drew a direct inquiry from Washington and led Tokyo to promise, in confidence, to stop bombing the city area. The heavy raids that followed within days showed how little that promise restrained its forces."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Casualties_of_a_mass_panic_-_Chungking%2C_China.jpg/1280px-Casualties_of_a_mass_panic_-_Chungking%2C_China.jpg","title":"After the air-raid shelter crush, Chongqing, June 1941","desc":"Victims of the crush at a public air-raid tunnel during the Japanese raid of 5 June 1941, when people trying to get back into the shelter were trampled or suffocated, photographed by Carl Mydans.","credit":"Photograph by Carl Mydans, National Archives and Records Administration 535558, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casualties_of_a_mass_panic_-_Chungking,_China.jpg","alt":"Bodies of men, women and children lying across a long flight of stone steps."},"wiki":"Bombing of Chongqing"},
    "ww2-408": {"questions":["Japanese columns converging on Xuzhou turned east along a branch line toward a walled canal village, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed left Isogai's 10th Division badly mauled.","To wipe out the humiliation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Japan combed northern China for about ten divisions and closed on Xuzhou, but the Chinese armies slipped away before the city fell."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Taierzhuang</b> was fought in the spring of 1938 for a walled village where the Grand Canal crosses a branch railway north-east of Xuzhou, and it ended in what China's government called the first severe blow its poorly equipped army had dealt the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese columns were converging on Xuzhou, the junction of the Tianjin–Pukou and Longhai railways, and one force turned east along the branch line to take Taierzhuang as a base for the attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its spearhead, a brigade of Isogai's 10th Division, collided with General Sun Lianzhong's troops just as they reached the town, while Tang Enbo's corps to the north joined an enveloping movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 3 April the main body of the division had been drawn in, and the defenders held the town against more than 60 field guns, 10 heavy guns and 30 to 40 tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 8 April a coordinated counter-offensive drove the Japanese north toward Yixian, and foreign observers reported the 10th Division badly mauled and disorganised, though Japanese officers denied it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Captain Evans Carlson, an American Marine who watched 3 days of the fighting, told his ambassador that the Chinese had won a well-deserved victory, the first defeat Japanese troops had suffered in the field in modern times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Hankow the mood shifted from pessimism to dogged optimism, resting on hopes of wearing Japan down rather than of driving it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the Japanese the defeat was a humiliation to be wiped out, and they combed the north to mass about ten divisions on the Xuzhou front, against which China assembled more than 400,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan hoped the battle for Xuzhou would destroy China's main armies, but the Chinese withdrew with their field forces intact before the city fell on 19 May, denying the Japanese the 'Tannenberg' they had confidently spoken of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tokyo then warned its public that taking Xuzhou would not by itself end Chinese resistance, and the army turned to a new drive on Hankow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 350–51, 354–56, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","Evans Fordyce Carlson, <i>The Chinese Army: Its Organization and Military Efficiency</i> (New York: Institute of Pacific Relations, 1940), 28–29, 70, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.503348. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Shanghai (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, April 11, 1938, 5 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d145. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, April 19, 1938, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d149. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Secretary of State, May 21, 1938, 11 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d166. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chap. 5, \"Japanese Aggression Against China,\" 722–23, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-5a.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a small walled village matter so much?","a":"It stood where the Grand Canal crosses the branch railway that the Japanese meant to use as a base against Xuzhou, the junction of two of China's main railways, so holding it blocked one arm of the converging attack."},{"q":"Why did the victory change the mood in Hankow?","a":"It showed that with good tactics the Chinese could inflict heavy losses despite poorer equipment. Observers in Hankow found a dogged optimism, though it rested on hopes of wearing Japan down rather than of driving it out."},{"q":"Why did Japan's reply at Xuzhou fail to end the war?","a":"Japan massed some ten divisions to destroy China's main armies, but the Chinese withdrew with their field forces intact before Xuzhou fell, and Tokyo itself warned the public that resistance would go on."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Battle_of_Taierzhuang.jpg","title":"Street fighting in Taierzhuang, 1938","desc":"Chinese soldiers fighting their way into Taierzhuang in 1938, in the street battles for the town.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Taierzhuang.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in helmets crouch and run along a rubble-strewn street beside a ruined brick building with a row of wooden posts."},"wiki":"Battle of Taierzhuang"},
    "ww2-409": {"questions":["China's wartime handbook said only that a dike gave way under fire, but the American ambassador privately put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> down to Chinese troops breaching the dikes to block the Japanese.","A postwar Nationalist survey counted 893,303 dead and 3,911,354 displaced in Henan, Anhui and Jiangsu from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose waters were not turned back until 1947."],"abstract":"The <b>1938 Yellow River flood</b> was the inundation that followed when Chinese Nationalist troops broke the river's southern dike at Huayuankou, north of Zhengzhou, on 9 June 1938 to stop the Japanese advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two attempts at Zhaokou on 4–6 June had failed, and the breach at Huayuankou was dug with the Japanese less than 50 kilometres (30 miles) away and only minimal warning given, lest the enemy speed up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water spread over more than 60 counties of Henan, Anhui and Jiangsu, in a belt about 400 kilometres (250 miles) long and 30 to 80 kilometres (20 to 50 miles) wide, and reached the sea by way of the Huai River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The river was not returned to its old course until 1947, and the flooding went on year after year in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How many died is still argued over, and the fullest count, a Nationalist survey of 1948 on which Micah Muscolino draws, gives 893,303 deaths and 3,911,354 people displaced in the three provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> China's government at the time said the dike had given way under fire during a battle in central Henan, and its official chronology recorded only that the dikes were damaged as a result of hostilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American ambassador in Hankow reported privately in July that the Chinese had blocked the advance on Zhengzhou by breaching the Yellow River dikes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Japanese spokesman admitted on 15 June that the flood had completely checked the advance on Zhengzhou and would probably force the abandonment of a push south toward Hankow along the Ping–Han railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japanese troops were shifted east and south to join the advance up the Yangtze instead, so the drive on Wuhan came along the river rather than overland from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nationalist leaders judged that national survival outweighed damage they knew the floods would cause, while scarce relief resources were drained away to the war and recovery was held back for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Micah Muscolino, \"Yellow River Flood, 1938–47,\" DisasterHistory.org, https://disasterhistory.org/yellow-river-flood-1938-47. [Open access]","Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 5–6, and \"Chronology of Major Events,\" entry for June 11, 1938, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, July 19, 1938, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d219. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The First Secretary of Embassy in China (Salisbury) to the Secretary of State, June 15, 1938, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d186. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The First Secretary of Embassy in China (Salisbury) to the Secretary of State, June 28, 1938, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d195. [Open access]","Hucheng Li, Bingbing Wang, Jun Yan, Baorui Qiu and Fei Zhuo, \"Environmental Impact of Huayuankou Dike Breach in 1938,\" in <i>Proceedings of the 2015 4th International Conference on Sustainable Energy and Environmental Engineering</i> (Paris: Atlantis Press, 2016), 1105–8, https://doi.org/10.2991/icseee-15.2016.196. [Open access]","Sravani Biswas, review of <i>The Ecology of War in China: Henan Province, the Yellow River, and Beyond, 1938–1950</i>, by Micah S. Muscolino, H-War, H-Net Reviews, January 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=43345. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Nationalists break the dike?","a":"Xuzhou had fallen and the Japanese were closing on Zhengzhou, the junction of the railways to Wuhan. Cutting the Longhai line with floodwater was meant to halt them before Wuhan fell, and leaders judged that national survival outweighed the damage they knew it would cause."},{"q":"Why did so little warning reach people downstream?","a":"Commanders feared that news of the plan would reach the Japanese and lead them to speed up their advance, so only minimal public warning was given before the water was let through."},{"q":"Why did the flood not save Wuhan?","a":"It stopped the advance from the north along the railway, but Japan moved troops east and south to attack up the Yangtze instead, and Wuhan fell in October 1938, though only after the government had withdrawn into the interior."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/1938_flood_rescue.jpg","title":"Rescue work in the flooded area, June 1938","desc":"A Japanese news-agency photograph of 23 June 1938, issued with a caption presenting Japanese troops rescuing Chinese civilians in the Yellow River flood area.","credit":"Domei News photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1938_flood_rescue.jpg","alt":"Small boats with people aboard on a wide stretch of muddy water, with a line of trees on the far bank."},"wiki":"1938 Yellow River flood"},
    "ww2-410": {"questions":["Japan meant the capture of Hankow to mark the end of the war's military phase, but in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Chinese pulled their main force out before the cities fell in October 1938.","Tokyo announced in November 1938 that the National Government had been reduced to a local regime after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Japan the cities of Hankow, Wuchang and Hanyang."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Wuhan</b> was the Japanese campaign of 1938 against the three cities of Wuhan on the middle Yangtze, where Hankow had become the base of China's resistance after the loss of Nanjing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened on 12 June with an attack on Anqing and ended on 25 October, when the Chinese evacuated Hankow and Wuchang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese advanced in columns along both banks of the Yangtze and north of the Dabie Mountains toward Xinyang, with gunboats and cruisers on the river and gas used at the Matang barrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> China put 148 divisions with 1,005,000 men into the battle, against a Japanese force that numbered at most ten and a half divisions, about 230,000 men, at any one time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cholera and malaria struck both armies, at one point 60 per cent of the Chinese army was sick, and after Soviet aircraft went home in August the Japanese had the air to themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese meant the capture of Hankow to stand before their people as the end of the military phase of the war, the American ambassador reported in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Chinese command instead withdrew its main force before the cities fell, and its spokesman said the real point of the defence had been to cover the transfer of men and material to the north-west and south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nationalist China claimed more than 200,000 Japanese casualties in the 4.5 months, and said Japan had needed 31 divisions in China for the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 November, with Canton also taken, Tokyo declared that the National Government had been reduced to a local regime and announced its aim of a New Order in East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A historian at Japan's National Institute for Defense Studies judges that taking Wuhan and Guangzhou brought the army almost to the limit of what it could mobilise, and the war then settled into deadlock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Chinese Ministry of Information, comp., <i>China Handbook, 1937–1943: A Comprehensive Survey of Major Developments in China in Six Years of War</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1943), 351, 356–57, https://archive.org/details/chinahandbook19300chin. [Open access]","Evans Fordyce Carlson, <i>The Chinese Army: Its Organization and Military Efficiency</i> (New York: Institute of Pacific Relations, 1940), 71–73, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.503348. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, July 19, 1938, 3 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d219. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, October 22, 1938, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d318. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, October 25, 1938, 2 p.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d330. [Open access]","Nobu Iwatani, \"The Japanese Counterinsurgency Operations in Mainland China,\" in <i>Irregular Warfare in Historical Perspectives</i>, 2017 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 59, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2017/06_iwatani.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chap. 5, \"Japanese Aggression Against China,\" 730–33, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-5a.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did China not fight to the last for Wuhan?","a":"Its command said it attached no importance to holding any one place. The defence was meant to protect the move of men and resources inland, and standing to the end would have let the Japanese trap and destroy its main armies."},{"q":"Why did air power favour Japan in the battle?","a":"Soviet aircraft left in August and Chinese aviation was weak, so Japanese bombers had the air to themselves. Their bombing was more accurate than earlier in the war, and it was used in mass to blast a way through stubborn resistance."},{"q":"Why did taking Wuhan not end the war?","a":"The Chinese government and much of its army withdrew to the west, and Japan's army was near the limit of what it could mobilise, so instead of a surrender the war settled into deadlock."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Wuhan_german_divs.jpg","title":"German-trained Chinese troops, 1938","desc":"Soldiers of one of the Nationalist army's German-trained divisions, in German-pattern steel helmets, before the Battle of Wuhan.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wuhan_german_divs.jpg","alt":"Rows of soldiers in coal-scuttle steel helmets and belted uniforms stand packed together in formation."},"wiki":"Battle of Wuhan","places":["pl:q2038907"]},
    "ww2-411": {"questions":["After Wuhan fell, Tokyo chose to hold what it had and besiege the rest, and the first large test of that choice was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> south of the Yangtze that autumn.","The American ambassador believed the capture of Hunan's capital was meant to launch Wang Jingwei's government, so the Japanese setback in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read as a blow to that plan."],"abstract":"The <b>First Battle of Changsha</b> was the Japanese drive towards the capital of Hunan in September and October 1939, which the Japanese army called the Kiangsi-Hunan Operation and the Chinese counted as the first battle for the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Wuhan and Canton fell in October 1938, Imperial General Headquarters judged that a large, prolonged war against Chiang Kai-shek was not needed and that a new pro-Japanese regime should be built up instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plan was to secure the occupied territory, wear down the remaining Chinese forces by a strong prolonged siege, and keep one army at Wuhan to intimidate the Chinese in Hubei, Hunan and Jiangxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1939 the 11th Army resolved to destroy the main strength of the Chinese 9th War Area and crush its fighting spirit, while Europe was distracted and a new central government was being prepared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About four Japanese divisions attacked from mid-September, and in Washington a State Department adviser said that Japan was concentrating on a puppet government at Nanjing and a new drive around Changsha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The columns pushed south towards Changsha, then turned back in early October and were north of the Xinqiang River again by 9 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese record claims heavy losses on about ten of the 30 Chinese divisions in the area, but admits that breaking the will of a force then put at 50 divisions was still far off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American ambassador reported on 17 October that the expedition had suffered a severe reverse, and that taking Changsha had been meant to mark the setting up of a unified government under Wang Jingwei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Beijing a Chinese informant told the American embassy that the defeat north of Changsha had weakened the generals promoting the new government and might delay its formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By December the French ambassador in Chongqing traced Chinese optimism partly to the victory at Changsha, and said the Chinese were confident of repeating it against the Japanese force that had taken Nanning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Japanese Monograph No. 70, <i>China Area Operations Record, July 1937–November 1941</i> (Tokyo: Office of the Military History Officer, Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East, 1957), 27–31, 54–56, HyperWar, http://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-70/JM-70.pdf. [Open access]","Japanese Monograph No. 179, <i>Central China Area Operations Record, 1937–1941</i> (Tokyo: Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East and Eighth United States Army Rear, n.d.), 135–55, HyperWar, http://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-179/JM-179.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum of Conversation, by the Adviser on Political Relations (Hornbeck), September 27, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d244. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Lockhart) to the Secretary of State, October 10, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d254. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, October 17, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d257. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, December 27, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d687. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese turn back short of Changsha?","a":"Their own record says the aim was to destroy Chinese forces and break their spirit, not to hold ground; only about four divisions could be spared while the rest of the 11th Army guarded the Yangtze, and after the sweep they returned to their start line."},{"q":"Why did both sides claim success?","a":"The Japanese counted Chinese divisions mauled and ground covered; the Chinese, and foreign diplomats in Chongqing, saw an enemy that had come within reach of Changsha and gone back again, which looked like a repulse."},{"q":"Why did the battle matter for Wang Jingwei?","a":"The American ambassador believed a capture of Changsha had been meant to launch Wang's government in triumph; without it the plan lost momentum, and his regime was not established at Nanjing until 30 March 1940."}],"wiki":"Battle of Changsha (1939)"},
    "ww2-412": {"questions":["Washington's note of 30 December 1938 called it highly paradoxical that equal treatment for Americans in China should depend on accepting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Tokyo proclaimed.","Prime Minister Konoe's statement of 22 December 1938 named recognition of Manchukuo and Japanese garrisons as the minimum China must accept to join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Japan was building."],"abstract":"The <b>New Order in East Asia</b> was the aim that Japan's government proclaimed on 3 November 1938, a bloc of Japan, Manchukuo and China joined by mutual aid in political, economic and cultural fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It claimed that with Canton and the three cities of Wuhan taken, the Kuomintang government was a mere local regime, and that Japan would not lay down its arms until it was crushed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It offered a place even to that government if it gave up its anti-Japanese and pro-communist policy and remade its personnel, and it called the new order the ultimate purpose of the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a radio speech that morning Prime Minister Konoe Fumimaro said Japan sought China's cooperation rather than its conquest, and the development rather than the ruin of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 December he set out the terms: China was to recognise Manchukuo, sign an anti-Comintern agreement, accept Japanese troops at specified points and give Japan facilities for resources in North China and Inner Mongolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 16 December 1938 the cabinet created the Asia Development Board to run occupied China, headed by the prime minister with the war, navy, finance and foreign ministers as vice-presidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal found that the board was to guide the political, economic and cultural development of China, while two national policy companies founded in April 1938 financed development in the North and the Centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Foreign Minister Arita told the American ambassador on 18 November that applying the principles of the past to the new conditions in East Asia would not help, and that outsiders could join reconstruction if they understood this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Washington replied on 30 December that Japan was making equal treatment of Americans in China conditional on accepting a new order of its own design, which it called highly paradoxical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It said that the United States could not assent to a régime set up for the special purposes of a third country that would deprive Americans of their long-established rights of equal opportunity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"Statement by the Japanese Government, November 3, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v01/d329. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Radio Speech by the Japanese Prime Minister (Prince Konoye), November 3, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v01/d330. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Statement by the Japanese Prime Minister (Prince Konoye), December 22, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v01/d332. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chapter IV, pp. 306–09, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-4b.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The American Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs (Arita), December 30, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v01/d617. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs (Arita) to the American Ambassador in Japan (Grew), November 18, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v01/d608. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan announce a new aim after taking Wuhan?","a":"The fall of Canton and Wuhan had not ended the war, so Tokyo recast the campaign as the building of a new order and invited Chinese who would cooperate, even a reformed Kuomintang, to join it."},{"q":"Why did the United States object to the new order?","a":"It came with Japanese control of currency, trade and resources in occupied China, which shut out equal opportunity, and Washington held that treaties could be changed only by agreement, not by one side's declaration."},{"q":"Why did Konoe insist that China was not being conquered?","a":"His government wanted Chinese partners to run the occupied areas and a peace on Japanese terms, and presenting the war as cooperation with a reborn China made that offer easier to accept."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Fumimaro_Konoe_Cabinet_19370604.jpg/1920px-Fumimaro_Konoe_Cabinet_19370604.jpg","title":"Konoe's first cabinet, June 1937","desc":"Prince Konoe Fumimaro's first cabinet, formed in June 1937, which issued the statements of 1938 setting out Japan's aims in China.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fumimaro_Konoe_Cabinet_19370604.jpg","alt":"Two rows of men in formal morning dress standing on a flight of steps, posed for a group photograph."}},
    "ww2-413": {"questions":["A relief league chaired by Madame Sun Yat-sen reported in 1943 that the drives later remembered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had levelled hundreds of North China villages since April 1941.","Japanese army records describe only mopping-up operations against guerrillas, while the Chinese Communist Party named the same campaigns the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after their three commands to the troops."],"abstract":"The <b>Three Alls policy</b>, ‘burn all, kill all, steal all’ in one rendering, is the name given to the Japanese army's campaigns of devastation against Communist-held areas of North China in the early 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Japanese it appears as sankō sakusen, the three-all operations, and the label was later taken up by the Chinese Communist Party, while Japan's own records speak of mopping-up operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official Japanese account of 1940 says Communist guerrillas raided garrisons across the occupied territory and wrecked railways, roads and telegraph lines, so the army spread out its forces and mopped up strenuously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A relief league chaired by Madame Sun Yat-sen reported in 1943 that great Japanese mopping-up campaigns had begun in April 1941 and had levelled hundreds of villages, killing tens of thousands of farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It added that large districts had been left devastated, with few inhabitants and no food reserves, and that people were flooding the roads in search of food and work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Tokyo tribunal recorded the pattern without using the name, finding that in August 1941 a detachment in Manchukuo captured the village of Si-Tu-Ti in Jehol, killed the members of more than 300 families and burned it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same judgment traced the methods back to the first months of the war, when soldiers killed 24 people at the Hebei village of Tung Wang Chia and burned about two-thirds of its homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A chronology of Japanese mass violence adds that in February 1942, during the anti-communist campaign, a Japanese brigade used mustard gas for 8 days against Communist forces in Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1940 a source close to Chiang Kai-shek said the Communists were mainly building bases, mobilising people and resisting mopping-up campaigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By early 1945 Communist commanders told an American diplomat that Japan, short of troops for the Pacific, had thinned its strong points and blockhouses on the North China plain and their own operations had grown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Arnaud Doglia, \"Japanese Mass Violence and Its Victims in the Fifteen Years War (1931–45),\" <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 October 2011, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/japanese-mass-violence-and-its-victims-in-the-fifteen-years-war-1931-45/. [Open access]","China Defence League, <i>In Guerrilla China: Report of the China Defence League</i> (Chungking: China Defence League, 1943), 36, https://archive.org/details/in-guerrilla-china. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chapter VIII, pp. 1018–21, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Japanese Monograph No. 70, <i>China Area Operations Record, July 1937–November 1941</i> (Tokyo: Office of the Military History Officer, Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East, 1957), 38–39, HyperWar, http://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-70/JM-70.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, October 24, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d457. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Memorandum by the Second Secretary of Embassy in China (Ludden), February 1945,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, The Far East, China, Volume VII</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v07/d156. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan turn on the villages rather than the guerrilla armies?","a":"Its own record admits the guerrillas could not be pinned down and struck at railways and garrisons across the occupied zone; the villages that fed and sheltered them were within reach of Japanese columns."},{"q":"Why is the name itself treated with care?","a":"It is the label the Chinese Communist Party used for the campaigns, while Japanese army records call them mopping-up operations, so the name carries one side's account even though the destruction is recorded in other evidence."},{"q":"Why is no single death toll given for the campaigns?","a":"The open sources used here give no agreed figure; contemporary reports speak of tens of thousands killed in particular drives, and the Tokyo judgment lists individual massacres rather than a total."}],"wiki":"Three Alls policy"},
    "ww2-414": {"questions":["Japan signed a treaty on 30 November 1940 with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> allowing Japanese garrisons in North China, and officials in Chongqing were pleased by its anti-communist clause.","Inaugurated at a Nanjing ceremony that paid homage to Sun Yat-sen, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised a new order in East Asia and a national army to absorb the guerrilla units."],"abstract":"The <b>Wang Jingwei regime</b> was the National Government that Japan set up at Nanjing on 30 March 1940 under Wang Jingwei, formerly vice-president of the Kuomintang, to rule occupied China in place of Chongqing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wang had broken with Chiang Kai-shek after the fall of Wuhan, answered a declaration by Konoe and fled to Indochina, where he went on calling for peace with Japan on an anti-communist basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Five Ministers' Conference in Tokyo decided on 6 June 1939, while he was still in Japan, on a policy for a new central government, and Japan lent him 40 million yen to build it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the inauguration Wang read a programme that promised a new order in East Asia, cooperation against the Communist International and a national defence army to absorb the guerrilla units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Provisional Government in Beijing and the Reformed Government in Nanjing were merged into it, and on 30 November 1940 Japan signed a treaty with it under which Japanese troops would be stationed in North China and Inner Mongolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For the Japanese army the regime was meant to be a way out of the war, and the American ambassador believed the failed drive on Changsha in 1939 had been intended to mark its launch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Washington told Tokyo in November 1940 that the regime lacked genuine Chinese support and served Japan's special purposes, and that recognising it would only make a settlement harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Chongqing, Japan's recognition of the regime had little visible effect, and new American credits were taken partly as a reply to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Chinese officials were even delighted that the treaty openly provided for the suppression of communism, which they thought would help prevent a deal between Japan and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Japanese army's own history concedes that Chongqing fought on, and that Imperial General Headquarters had to accept that peace could not come through the new government and prepare for a long war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chapter V, pp. 743–51, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-5a.html. [Open access]","Japanese Monograph No. 70, <i>China Area Operations Record, July 1937–November 1941</i> (Tokyo: Office of the Military History Officer, Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East, 1957), 80–81, HyperWar, http://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-70/JM-70.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, October 17, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d257. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The First Secretary of Embassy in China (Stanton) to the Secretary of State, March 30, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d349. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan (Grew), November 27, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d474. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, December 4, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d481. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, December 7, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d486. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan want a Chinese government at Nanjing?","a":"It hoped that a Chinese regime cooperating with Japan would make the occupied areas governable and split the resistance, so that the war could end without fighting Chongqing to a finish."},{"q":"Why did the regime fail to end the war?","a":"Chongqing kept fighting, and the United States and others refused to treat the regime as China's government, so it never drew the support that would have made further resistance look pointless."},{"q":"Why did Chongqing welcome the treaty's anti-communist clause?","a":"Its officials thought Japan's open pledge to suppress communism would block any bargain between Japan and the Soviet Union made at China's expense."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/Wang_Jingwei_Regime_3rd_anniversary_parade.JPG","title":"Third-anniversary parade, about 1943","desc":"A parade marking the third anniversary of the Wang Jingwei regime, about 1943, photographed for a Japanese book on the new China.","credit":"Photograph by Katsumi Ikeda, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wang_Jingwei_Regime_3rd_anniversary_parade.JPG","alt":"Columns of helmeted soldiers with rifles on their shoulders march past an officer saluting from a raised platform, with crowds and flags behind."},"wiki":"Wang Jingwei regime"},
    "ww2-415": {"questions":["The Federal Reserve Bank of China issued the only legal tender in occupied North China under the authority of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose notes Japan's foreign minister defended to Washington.","Wang Kemin chaired both the New People's Association and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Beijing administration absorbed into Wang Jingwei's regime at Nanjing in March 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Provisional Government of the Republic of China</b> was the Chinese administration that the Japanese army inaugurated in Beijing on 14 December 1937, the day after Nanjing fell, to govern occupied North China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its head was Wang Kemin, a retired senior official whom Japanese officers persuaded to come north from Hong Kong, travelling by way of Shanghai and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese foreign ministry records produced at the Tokyo trial show that the North China army planned to make this regime the central government of China in the future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local peace maintenance committees in the Beijing and Tianjin areas joined it, and the East Hebei regime was merged into it on 30 June 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lasted until March 1940, when it was absorbed into Wang Jingwei's government at Nanjing, after Japanese generals had argued over whether North China should keep wide autonomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Wang Kemin also chaired the New People's Association, created on the orders of the Japanese army to explain the regime's policies to the people, with a Japanese as its vice-chairman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Federal Reserve Bank opened in March 1938 with mainly Japanese directing staff, and the currency it issued became the only legal tender in North China as the yen bloc was extended there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan's foreign minister defended the bank's notes to the United States in November 1938 as the legal currency required by the Provisional Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By October 1939 the American embassy in Beijing judged that Japan was applying in North China exactly the methods it had used in Manchuria, with Japanese residents and development companies multiplying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Japanese army's own history concedes that the regimes it sponsored had no military or financial power and leaders who could not be relied on, so that their administration was unsatisfactory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chapter V, pp. 713–16, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-5a.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chapter IV, pp. 233–35, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-4a.html. [Open access]","Japanese Monograph No. 70, <i>China Area Operations Record, July 1937–November 1941</i> (Tokyo: Office of the Military History Officer, Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East, 1957), 27, 80, HyperWar, http://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-70/JM-70.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs (Arita) to the American Ambassador in Japan (Grew), November 18, 1938,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v01/d608. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Lockhart) to the Secretary of State, October 27, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d271. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Lockhart) to the Secretary of State, October 10, 1939,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1939, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1939v03/d254. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan set up a government in Beijing so early in the war?","a":"The North China army wanted a Chinese authority to run the conquered provinces and hoped to make it the core of a future central government that would cooperate with Japan."},{"q":"Why was a new currency so important to the regime?","a":"A bank issuing the only legal tender let Japan steer credit and foreign exchange and pull North China's economy into the yen bloc, to the benefit of Japanese industry."},{"q":"Why did the regime achieve so little?","a":"By the Japanese army's own account the regimes it sponsored had no military or financial power and leaders who could not be relied on, so their government remained weak."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/%E4%B8%AD%E5%8D%8E%E6%B0%91%E5%9B%BD%E4%B8%B4%E6%97%B6%E6%94%BF%E5%BA%9C%E6%88%90%E7%AB%8B.jpg","title":"The regime's name board, 14 December 1937","desc":"Two men fix the name board of the Provisional Government of the Republic of China at its founding in Beijing on 14 December 1937.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%AD%E5%8D%8E%E6%B0%91%E5%9B%BD%E4%B8%B4%E6%97%B6%E6%94%BF%E5%BA%9C%E6%88%90%E7%AB%8B.jpg","alt":"Two men in dark uniforms and caps fix a tall vertical sign with Chinese characters beside a studded gate."},"wiki":"Provisional Government of the Republic of China (1937–1940)"},
    "ww2-416": {"questions":["Students of the Bethune Medical College served with mobile medical units at the front during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, according to a relief report published in Chongqing in 1943.","Even as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tore up Japanese railways in Hebei and Shanxi, a source close to Chiang Kai-shek claimed the Communists had barely fought the Japanese in 18 months."],"abstract":"The <b>Hundred Regiments Offensive</b> was the Communist attack of 1940 on the railways, communications and mines that held the Japanese occupation of North China together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan's official account says that in late August Zhu De, commander of the 18th Army Group, ordered his forces across North China to strike at railways and industrial areas simultaneously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the night of 20 August they attacked the Shijiazhuang–Yangqu railway and the northern part of the Datong–Fenglingdu line, blasting track and communications, and destroyed the Jingxing coal mine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The garrisons could not be reinforced because the lines were cut, but they held out, and the Japanese mopped up until they counted the operation complete on 3 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A relief league's report published in Chongqing in 1943 records students of the Bethune Medical College serving with mobile medical units at the front during what it called the Hundred Regiments Campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese monograph claims that heavy losses forced the Communists back to guerrilla warfare, after which they offered very little resistance in the area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet in October 1940 a source close to Chiang Kai-shek told the American ambassador it was generally conceded, even among local Communists, that they had done little fighting against the Japanese in 18 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In November the Communist commanders admitted that their armies numbered some 500,000 men, far more than the 45,000 the government paid for, and lived on the anti-Japanese governments they had organised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In January 1941 Chongqing's spokesman complained that a chance to destroy four Japanese divisions in Shanxi had been lost because the Communists ignored orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> American observers who later crossed the Communist areas concluded that the Japanese held only a thin skeleton of North China and that the countryside behind their lines was controlled by forces on the Allied side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Japanese Monograph No. 70, <i>China Area Operations Record, July 1937–November 1941</i> (Tokyo: Office of the Military History Officer, Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East, 1957), 38–44, HyperWar, http://ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-70/JM-70.pdf. [Open access]","China Defence League, <i>In Guerrilla China: Report of the China Defence League</i> (Chungking: China Defence League, 1943), 28, https://archive.org/details/in-guerrilla-china. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, October 24, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d457. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, November 29, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d476. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, January 17, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d514. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Report by the Second Secretary of Embassy in China (Service), March 17, 1945,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, The Far East, China, Volume VII</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v07/d203. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Communists attack railways and mines?","a":"The Japanese held North China through a thin network of lines, roads and garrisons; cutting them isolated the garrisons and struck at the coal and industry Japan was exploiting."},{"q":"Why does Japan's record treat it as a Communist defeat?","a":"The garrisons held, the mopping-up went on until December, and the monograph claims that heavy Communist losses pushed them back to guerrilla warfare."},{"q":"Why did Chiang's circle play the offensive down?","a":"Relations with the Communists were worsening over the New Fourth Army and Communist expansion, and Chongqing argued that the Communists were growing their armies and bases rather than fighting Japan."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Hundred_Regiments_Offensive_1940.jpg","title":"Eighth Route Army at Niangzi Pass, 1940","desc":"Eighth Route Army soldiers at Niangzi Pass in 1940, a photograph first published in a Yan'an newspaper.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hundred_Regiments_Offensive_1940.jpg","alt":"Soldiers climb a scrubby hillside beside a stone watchtower, one of them carrying a flag."},"wiki":"Hundred Regiments Offensive"},
    "ww2-417": {"questions":["Chiang Kai-shek called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a matter of military discipline, while his spokesman admitted that 25 of the best government divisions were watching the Communists rather than Japan.","Ye Ting, captured on 12 January 1941 with about 7,000 of his men, was the most senior prisoner of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fought between nominal allies in southern Anhui."],"abstract":"The <b>New Fourth Army incident</b> was the destruction in January 1941 of the Communist New Fourth Army's units south of the Yangtze by Nationalist troops in southern Anhui, while the two parties were nominally allied against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chongqing had ordered the Communist forces in Anhui and Jiangsu to move north of the Yangtze by the end of December 1940 and north of the Yellow River by the end of January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army's commander, Ye Ting, signalled that he would move but asked first for arrears of pay, winter uniforms, ammunition and pensions for the wounded and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> According to the Communist chief of staff in Chongqing, about 10,000 men of the remaining units were surrounded and attacked on 6 January by 80,000 government troops near Maolin as they marched north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government spokesman said that on 12 January some 7,000 Communist troops had been disarmed and that Ye Ting and other senior officers had been captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Military Council's version, sent to Chinese embassies abroad, was that the army had defied orders and turned south to waylay the government's 40th Division, and that it had been abolished as a unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The spokesman Hollington Tong told the American embassy that relations had reached a showdown and that 25 of the government's best divisions were watching the Communists instead of fighting Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chiang Kai-shek told a memorial service on 27 January that the action was purely a matter of military discipline and blamed Japanese sources for talk of civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Communist leaders in Chongqing kept up their contacts with the government, and their paper printed nothing on the incident beyond a short poem of lament by Zhou Enlai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Zhou was reported to hold that China could not be plunged into civil war at that time, and informed observers judged that relations were unlikely to be damaged beyond repair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, December 16, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d494. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, January 16, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d513. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, January 17, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d514. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Chinese Ministry for Foreign Affairs to the Chinese Embassy, January 19, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d515. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, January 29, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d522. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, January 25, 1941,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1941, The Far East, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d520. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Chongqing order the New Fourth Army north?","a":"The government wanted the Communist forces out of central China, where they were expanding near the Yangtze and its communications with the sea, and moved into the north where they could be contained."},{"q":"Why do the two accounts of the clash differ so much?","a":"The Communists said their column was surrounded on the march by a far larger force; the government said the column had left its route to attack a government division and was punished for disobedience."},{"q":"Why did neither side break the alliance after the clash?","a":"Both still faced Japan, and Zhou Enlai and Chiang Kai-shek alike insisted that China could not afford a civil war at that moment."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/1941N4A.jpg","title":"New Fourth Army soldiers, 1941","desc":"Soldiers of the New Fourth Army in 1941, sitting among stacked rifles and machine guns.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1941N4A.jpg","alt":"Rows of seated soldiers in caps behind piles of rifles and several machine guns."},"wiki":"New Fourth Army Incident"},
    "ww2-418": {"questions":["In 1944 the Communist representatives in Chongqing demanded the lifting of the blockade and legal recognition for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as conditions of a settlement with the government.","A relief report estimated that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with fewer than 2 million people, had resettled more than 100,000 refugees on public land in 4 years of war."],"abstract":"The <b>Shaan-Gan-Ning Border Region</b>, the Shensi–Kansu–Ninghsia Border Area of wartime spelling, was the principal rear base of the Chinese Communists, governed from Yan'an on the barren plateau of northern Shaanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chongqing never fully accepted it, and in November 1940 the Nationalist chief of staff offered a special area of 18 counties in North Shaanxi under a delegate the Communists would recommend, but only if they obeyed orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the Communists' own account in 1944, a People's Political Council elected every 4 years chose the region's chairman and government committee, with county and municipal councils elected every 3 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American embassy warned that this picture might be idealised, while noting that reports from every available source agreed that elections had in fact been held in the Communist areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A relief report of 1943 estimated that the region, with fewer than 2 million people, had taken in more than 100,000 refugees in 4 years, giving them land, seed and 2 years free of tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From 1940 the region lived under a blockade that cut off outside funds and stopped staff crossing its boundary, conditions shared by its hospitals and cooperatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In February 1944 the Communists named the lifting of the blockade and legal recognition of their border regions among their four main demands on the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Service, reporting from Yan'an in August 1944, found that the blockade had compelled the Communists to develop production to the point of self-sufficiency, helped by two good harvests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The production campaign improved the people's diet and health and brought the first cotton grown in the region, though equipment and technicians stayed short and quality suffered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Service judged that the result was to make the Communists more effective, either in the war against Japan or in resisting pressure from the Kuomintang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, November 6, 1940,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d458. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, February 23, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944, China, Volume VI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d291. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, February 18, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944, China, Volume VI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d283. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, July 6, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944, China, Volume VI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d376. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, August 29, 1944,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944, China, Volume VI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d420. [Open access]","China Defence League, <i>In Guerrilla China: Report of the China Defence League</i> (Chungking: China Defence League, 1943), 47–48, https://archive.org/details/in-guerrilla-china. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government blockade the region?","a":"Relations between the Kuomintang and the Communists had turned into armed rivalry, and Chongqing used the blockade to contain the Communists' main base and deny it outside supplies and money."},{"q":"Why did the blockade end up strengthening the region?","a":"Cut off from outside help, the Communists organised a production campaign that brought self-sufficiency, better food and new crops, which raised morale and stability in the base."},{"q":"Why did the Communists stress their elections in 1944?","a":"Elected councils were a point at issue in their negotiations with the government, and presenting their regime as democratic helped win the sympathy of American observers."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/%E9%99%95%E7%94%98%E5%AE%81%E8%BE%B9%E5%8C%BA%E9%93%B6%E8%A1%8C%E4%BA%94%E5%85%83%E7%BA%B8%E5%B8%81.jpg/1920px-%E9%99%95%E7%94%98%E5%AE%81%E8%BE%B9%E5%8C%BA%E9%93%B6%E8%A1%8C%E4%BA%94%E5%85%83%E7%BA%B8%E5%B8%81.jpg","title":"A Border Region Bank note","desc":"A five-yuan note of the Shaan-Gan-Ning Border Region Bank, now in a museum of Chinese finance and taxation.","credit":"Photograph by 三猎, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%99%95%E7%94%98%E5%AE%81%E8%BE%B9%E5%8C%BA%E9%93%B6%E8%A1%8C%E4%BA%94%E5%85%83%E7%BA%B8%E5%B8%81.jpg","alt":"A worn blue banknote with a city gate in the centre between two large characters."},"wiki":"Yan'an Soviet"},
    "ww2-419": {"questions":["Kawashima Kiyoshi told Soviet interrogators that at least 600 prisoners a year died in the experiments of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose expeditions also spread germs near Ningbo and Changde.","Ishii Shirō escaped trial, apparently by handing his results to the United States, so no one answered at Tokyo for the germ warfare of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in occupied China."],"abstract":"<b>Unit 731</b> was the Japanese army's biological warfare organisation, officially the Kwantung Army Epidemic Prevention and Water Supply Unit, built up by the army surgeon Ishii Shirō from 1932 and given that name in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main complex at Pingfang outside Harbin grew to about 3,000 staff, among them doctors and microbiologists recruited from Japan's leading universities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plague was its chief interest, but it also worked on smallpox, botulism, brucellosis, cholera and dysentery, and with Unit 100 cultivated anthrax and glanders for use against animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one part of a network, and in 1939 further units opened at Nanjing, Beijing and Canton, giving the army biological warfare centres in the north, centre and south of occupied China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soil taken from the Pingfang site in recent years yielded a living strain of anthrax, which the Chinese scientists who found it believe did not come from a local natural source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The number killed is disputed, for historians cited in a 2025 review put the prisoners who died at Pingfang at about 3,000, while one chronology estimates 5,000 to 6,000 Chinese murdered across the network's facilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kawashima Kiyoshi, the unit's production chief, told Soviet interrogators that no fewer than about 600 prisoners a year died from deliberate infection, and Soviet experts judged his account consistent with bacteriology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He also testified that the unit tested plague-flea bombs on prisoners tied to stakes at Anta in 1941, and sent expeditions to use germs against Chinese troops near Ningbo in 1940 and Changde in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ishii escaped prosecution, apparently in exchange for giving the United States the details of his experiments, and no perpetrator of biological warfare was tried at the Tokyo tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese government has still not formally recognised the unit's work, a silence that a Japanese physician argued in 2020 should end before Japan conducts new research on deadly pathogens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Walter E. Grunden and Olli H. Tuovinen, \"Legacy and Impact of the 1925 Geneva Protocol: One Hundred Years of Treaties and Debates on Chemical and Biological Weapons,\" <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 16 (2025), https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2025.1685967. [Open access]","<i>Materials on the Trial of Former Servicemen of the Japanese Army Charged with Manufacturing and Employing Bacteriological Weapons</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1950), 57–58, https://archive.org/details/MaterialsOnTheTrialOfFormerServicemen. [Open access]","Arnaud Doglia, \"Japanese Mass Violence and Its Victims in the Fifteen Years War (1931–45),\" <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 October 2011, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/japanese-mass-violence-and-its-victims-in-the-fifteen-years-war-1931-45/. [Open access]","Yarong Wu, Yuan Yuan, Bing Yuan, Jiaxin Li, Jinglin Wang and Yujun Cui, \"Bacteriologic and Genomic Investigation of <i>Bacillus anthracis</i> Isolated from World War II Site, China,\" <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 30, no. 12 (2024), https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3012.231520. [Open access]","Yasuharu Tokuda, \"Japan's Lack of Accountability for Conducting Research on Deadly Pathogens,\" <i>Journal of General and Family Medicine</i> 21, no. 1 (2020), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6942930/. [Open access]","Gong Wenjing, Yue Rongyuan and Zhao Shijian, \"Khabarovskii protsess i nakazanie prestupnikov voenno-biologicheskoi programmy Iaponii,\" <i>Vestnik Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta. Istoriia</i> 70, no. 3 (2025): 673–700, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/habarovskiy-protsess-i-nakazanie-prestupnikov-voenno-biologicheskoy-programmy-yaponii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Edward Drea, \"Introduction,\" in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 6–7, https://www.archives.gov/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan put its germ warfare centre in Manchuria?","a":"In occupied Manchukuo, under the Kwantung Army, the army could hold prisoners and run human experiments that could not be done in the home islands, far from public view."},{"q":"Why do estimates of the unit's victims vary so much?","a":"The experiments ran across a network of sites, records were destroyed, and the estimates rest on different kinds of evidence, from trial testimony to later research, covering different places."},{"q":"Why did the United States protect Ishii?","a":"American officials wanted the data from human experiments that no American laboratory could perform, and an immunity deal secured it at the price of leaving the crimes unpunished."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Unit_731_-_Complex.jpg","title":"The Unit 731 compound at Pingfang","desc":"An aerial view of the Unit 731 compound at Pingfang, a photograph from the unit's own bulletin.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unit_731_-_Complex.jpg","alt":"A grainy aerial view of a large square building around a courtyard, surrounded by lower buildings on flat open ground."},"wiki":"Unit 731"},
    "ww2-420": {"questions":["Dismissed in the West as a Stalinist show trial, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1949 nonetheless left published proceedings that remained the main source on Japanese germ warfare for decades.","Moscow held back its germ-warfare evidence from the Tokyo tribunal in 1947 and used it 2 years later at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Soviet Far East."],"abstract":"The <b>Khabarovsk war crimes trial</b> was the Soviet military tribunal that tried 12 former Japanese servicemen in December 1949 for preparing and using bacteriological weapons, the only court to try members of Unit 731.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Military Tribunal of the Primorye Military Area heard the case in open session in Khabarovsk from 25 to 30 December, with eight Soviet defence lawyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The accused ranged from General Yamada Otozō, commander of the Kwantung Army from 1944, to two medical trainees, and six had served in Unit 731, two in Unit 100 and four elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The verdict found that Unit 731 had caused a plague epidemic near Ningbo in 1940 by dropping infected fleas from aircraft, sent a similar expedition to Changde in 1941 and spread germs in China again in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many in the West dismissed the verdicts and the evidence together as another Stalinist show trial, yet the published proceedings remained for decades the main source on Japanese germ warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Soviet Union had first gathered this evidence for the Tokyo tribunal, but in September 1947 a government commission decided not to present it, citing American hostility and the risk that prisoner witnesses would retract.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All 12 were convicted and sent to labour camps for terms of two to 25 years, Yamada, Kawashima Kiyoshi of Unit 731 and two others receiving the maximum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet archives opened since show that the sentences were drafted in advance and approved by the Politburo on 17 December, 8 days before the trial began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ishii Shirō and other leaders were never tried, because the United States granted them immunity in exchange for their research data, and no one answered for biological warfare at the Tokyo tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chemical warfare, which the Japanese army used more than 2,000 times in China by one estimate, went unprosecuted too, lest an inquiry expose the bargain with Ishii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Materials on the Trial of Former Servicemen of the Japanese Army Charged with Manufacturing and Employing Bacteriological Weapons</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1950), 57–58, 621–34, https://archive.org/details/MaterialsOnTheTrialOfFormerServicemen. [Open access]","Gong Wenjing, Yue Rongyuan and Zhao Shijian, \"Khabarovskii protsess i nakazanie prestupnikov voenno-biologicheskoi programmy Iaponii,\" <i>Vestnik Sankt-Peterburgskogo universiteta. Istoriia</i> 70, no. 3 (2025): 673–700, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/habarovskiy-protsess-i-nakazanie-prestupnikov-voenno-biologicheskoy-programmy-yaponii. [in Russian] [Open access]","Andrei M. Egorov and Natalia P. Nikitina, \"Tokiiskii i Khabarovskii protsessy v kontekste problemy bakteriologicheskoi voiny,\" <i>Metamorfozy istorii</i>, no. 26 (2022), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/tokiyskiy-i-habarovskiy-protsessy-v-kontekste-problemy-bakteriologicheskoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Walter E. Grunden and Olli H. Tuovinen, \"Legacy and Impact of the 1925 Geneva Protocol: One Hundred Years of Treaties and Debates on Chemical and Biological Weapons,\" <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 16 (2025), https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2025.1685967. [Open access]","Yasuharu Tokuda, \"Japan's Lack of Accountability for Conducting Research on Deadly Pathogens,\" <i>Journal of General and Family Medicine</i> 21, no. 1 (2020), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6942930/. [Open access]","Edward Drea, \"Introduction,\" in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 6–7, https://www.archives.gov/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union hold a trial of its own?","a":"Its evidence had been kept out of the Tokyo tribunal and the United States had shielded Ishii, and in the early Cold War an open trial of Japanese germ warfare suited Moscow politically as well as legally."},{"q":"Why was the trial distrusted in the West?","a":"It was staged by Stalin's government, the sentences were settled before it opened, and much of the evidence came from prisoners' testimony, so many observers treated it as propaganda."},{"q":"Why does the trial still matter?","a":"Its proceedings preserved testimony about human experiments and attacks on Chinese towns while the main organisers went free, and for decades researchers had little else to work from."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Otozo.Yamada.1949.jpg","title":"Yamada Otozō on trial, 1949","desc":"General Yamada Otozō, former commander of the Kwantung Army, at the trial in Khabarovsk in December 1949.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otozo.Yamada.1949.jpg","alt":"A courtroom with uniformed guards, a man standing to speak into microphones and officials seated at tables."},"wiki":"Khabarovsk war crimes trials"},
    "ww2-431": {"questions":["Gas shells reported at Chienteh and Kinhwa and railway towns plundered and then destroyed in the withdrawal marked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Japan launched in Chekiang in 1942.","Imperial General Headquarters ordered on 30 April 1942 the operation later called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, meant to annihilate Chinese forces near Chuchow and destroy the air bases that threatened Japan."],"abstract":"The <b>Zhejiang-Jiangxi campaign</b> was the Japanese offensive of the summer of 1942 through the east China provinces then spelt Chekiang and Kiangsi, aimed at the airfields from which American bombers might reach Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its trigger was the Doolittle raid of 18 April 1942, when 16 B-25 bombers flown off the carrier Hornet struck Tokyo and other cities and then tried to reach fields around Chuchow in Chekiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In darkness and rain the bombers crash-landed or were abandoned, and almost all the airmen who parachuted were recovered by Chinese people and carried to safety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chiang Kai-shek had feared a violent Japanese reaction to the planes landing in China, and to the end he refused to let the field at Chuchow be used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Orders issued by Imperial General Headquarters on 30 April told the army in China to annihilate the enemy forces in the area and destroy its principal air bases, and 53 Japanese battalions drove into the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The cost fell on the people who had helped the airmen, and the American official history records that the Japanese did what ingenuity and malice suggested to persuade the peasants that sheltering Americans was unwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 1 June China's foreign minister handed the State Department a telegram saying the attackers had used gas shells at Chienteh and Kinhwa and dropped gas bombs from aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In July the American embassy passed on Chinese reports of systematic plundering and extortion in the towns along the railway, and an American naval observer said a Chinese commander had been ordered to withdraw without fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the Japanese pulled back in August they destroyed towns in their wake along the Chekiang-Kiangsi railway, while Chinese troops retook Wenchow and long stretches of the line behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Chinese reported that about 60,000 Japanese troops then returned to north China, and the loss of the Chekiang bases ended an American plan to bomb Japan with China-based heavy bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell’s Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 162–63, 184, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-5.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 438–42, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-12.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), June 1, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d73. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, July 23, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d107. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, August 21, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d121. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in China (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, August 29, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d126. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Chiang Kai-shek oppose letting the raiders land in China?","a":"He expected the Japanese to strike back at the ground where the bombers came down, and he wanted time to place his troops around Chuchow first; the raid went ahead without his full knowledge, and the reprisal he feared followed within weeks."},{"q":"Why did Japan answer one small raid with a whole campaign?","a":"The orders of 30 April show how sensitive Tokyo was to any threat of bombing from China: the army was told to annihilate the forces in the area and destroy the main air bases, so that the airfields could not be used again."},{"q":"Why did the campaign end an American bombing plan?","a":"The HALPRO plan to bomb Japan with China-based B-24 bombers depended on the Chekiang fields; once those bases were lost, and with too few transport aircraft, Stilwell judged the effort impossible and the bombers were held in Egypt."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Soldiers_Zhejiang_Campaign_1942.jpg","title":"Japanese soldiers in Chekiang, May 1942","desc":"Men of the Japanese 13th Army in action near Jinhua in Chekiang province on 30 May 1942, during the offensive launched after the Doolittle raid.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soldiers_Zhejiang_Campaign_1942.jpg","alt":"Three Japanese soldiers in steel helmets lying in grass among young pine trees, one aiming a short tube-shaped grenade launcher."},"wiki":"Zhejiang-Jiangxi campaign"},
    "ww2-432": {"questions":["Japan answered through Father Jacquinot in Shanghai that it could not grant the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but would try to respect the district as far as military necessity allowed.","Danish, German, British and American residents proposed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in November 1937, promising to keep out soldiers, military offices and every armed man but police carrying pistols."],"abstract":"The <b>Nanking Safety Zone</b> was a refuge for civilians inside the walled city of Nanking, proposed in November 1937 by an international committee of Danish, German, British and American residents as the Japanese army approached the Chinese capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The committee undertook to keep the zone free of military establishments, of armed men other than civil police with pistols and of passing soldiers, and to inspect it to see that these promises were kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It chose an area in the western part of the city, which it noted the Japanese air force had carefully respected in its bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan replied through Father Jacquinot in Shanghai that it could not grant the zone, but that its forces would try to respect the district as far as was consistent with military necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The committee went ahead, marking the boundaries with flags bearing a red cross inside a circle, and asked both sides not to attack the area while refugees pressed into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> As the Japanese closed in, the committee's German chairman, Rabe, also proposed a 3-day truce under which Chinese troops would leave the walled city and the Japanese would not attack it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> More than half of the city's inhabitants had fled before Nanking fell on 13 December, and Chinese soldiers who stayed behind threw away their arms and uniforms and sought refuge in the zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The zone did not prevent the atrocities that followed, which the Tokyo tribunal described in its judgment, and the committee's secretary filed two protests a day for the first 6 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In January 1938 an American diplomat reported that some 200,000 Chinese refugees were said to be within the walls, most of them in the safety zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later that month the chairman's own account put the refugees at about 250,000, and complained that the army had stopped the committee's rice sales while it was feeding 50,000 people a day free of charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, November 22, 1937,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d776. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Second Secretary of Embassy in China (Atcheson) to the Secretary of State, December 2, 1937,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d822. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Second Secretary of Embassy in China (Atcheson) to the Secretary of State, December 6, 1937,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d853. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Second Secretary of Embassy in China (Atcheson) to the Secretary of State, December 9, 1937,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d872. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chapter VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1010–16, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Third Secretary of Embassy in China (Allison) to the Secretary of State, January 10, 1938,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v04/d286. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Third Secretary of Embassy in China (Allison) to the Secretary of State, January 22, 1938,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d54. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the committee choose the western part of the city?","a":"That sector had been carefully respected by Japanese bombers, so it already looked like the safest ground, and it could hold the thousands of civilians who had nowhere else to go once the fighting reached Nanking."},{"q":"Why did Japan refuse to grant the zone formally?","a":"Its reply kept a free hand: it would not accept responsibility if Chinese forces misused the area, and promised only to respect the district as far as military necessity allowed."},{"q":"Why did food become the committee's main problem in January 1938?","a":"The army stopped its rice sales and released very little grain for civilians, while refusing to let the committee bring in stocks it had bought outside the walls, so it was feeding 50,000 people a day from shrinking supplies."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Refugee_shelters_established_by_the_International_Committee_for_the_Nanjing_Safety_Zone.jpg","title":"A refugee shelter of the Nanking Safety Zone","desc":"The gateway of one of the refugee shelters run by the International Committee for the Nanking Safety Zone, with a banner naming the committee and the shelter.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in China, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Refugee_shelters_established_by_the_International_Committee_for_the_Nanjing_Safety_Zone.jpg","alt":"A street gateway with a tall vertical banner of Chinese characters hanging beside it and a few people in dark coats walking past."},"wiki":"Nanking Safety Zone"},
    "ww2-433": {"questions":["Acquitted of war crimes by a court in Shanghai in January 1949, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left for Japan while Communist broadcasts were demanding that the Nationalists rearrest him and hand him over.","A delegate at the Tangku truce talks of 1933 and later commander in north China, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led over 1 million Japanese troops south of the Great Wall by the summer of 1945."],"abstract":"<b>Yasuji Okamura</b> was the Japanese general who commanded the China Expeditionary Army at the end of the war and formally surrendered Japan's forces in China at Nanking on 9 September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He had long dealt with Chinese governments, and American diplomats named him among the principal Japanese delegates at the truce talks that opened at Tangku on 30 May 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As vice chief of staff to General Muto he told a Japanese journalist that the truce had been accompanied by oral agreements on withdrawing Chinese troops from north China and reopening rail traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1944 he was the former commander of the North China Area Army, and in late August of that year he was given a new headquarters, the 6th Area Army, to finish the Ichigo offensive in east China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He later succeeded Field Marshal Hata in the supreme command, and the American official history notes that both men knew the intricacies of Chinese politics well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 1945 his army held the eastern third of China with its seaports and main railways, with 25 infantry divisions, an armoured division and 22 independent brigades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Over 1 million men stood under his command south of the Great Wall by that summer, and in the spring he had sent about 60,000 of them against the American air base at Chihchiang, ignoring orders from Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In late May Imperial General Headquarters ordered him to shorten his lines and withdraw the main body of his troops to the central and northern provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the surrender the North China Area Army obeyed a directive from Chungking to surrender only to representatives of Chiang Kai-shek, and its troops held off Communist attacks while they waited to be relieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A court in Shanghai acquitted him of war crimes on 26 January 1949, and Communist broadcasts called the verdict a plot and ordered the Nationalists to rearrest him, but he had already left for Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, May 31, 1933,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1933, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933v03/d390. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, June 9, 1933,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1933, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933v03/d400. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell’s Command Problems</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1956), 405–10, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Command/USA-CBI-Command-11.html. [Open access]","Theresa L. Kraus, <i>China Offensive</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-39 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 6, 14, 17, 21, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-ChinaO/index.html. [Open access]","Benis M. Frank and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Victory and Occupation</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1968), 529, 532, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/V/USMC-V-V-1.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in China (Stuart) to the Secretary of State, February 1, 1949,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1949, The Far East: China, Volume VIII</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1949v08/d121. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japanese troops in north China go on guarding cities after the surrender?","a":"Chungking ordered them to surrender only to Chiang Kai-shek's representatives, and until Nationalist troops arrived they were the only force able to stop the Communists seizing the cities and railways, so they kept their discipline and stayed on guard."},{"q":"Why was Okamura given a new command in August 1944?","a":"To finish the Ichigo offensive in east China: the 6th Area Army took over the armies from Hankow to Canton, leaving Hata free to prepare against American landings on the China coast."},{"q":"Why did the Communists object so strongly to his acquittal?","a":"Their broadcasts called it a scheme to regroup Nationalist forces and even bring Japanese reactionaries to fight the Chinese people, which reflected deep suspicion of Japanese and American policy in Japan."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Tanggu_Truce.jpg","title":"The Tangku truce talks, 1933","desc":"Japanese and Chinese officers at the table for the signing of the Tangku truce on 31 May 1933, the talks at which Okamura was one of the principal Japanese delegates.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanggu_Truce.jpg","alt":"Officers in uniform seated on both sides of a long table with papers and a vase of flowers, more men standing behind them against a wall."},"wiki":"Yasuji Okamura"},
    "ww2-434": {"questions":["Truman said the Marines of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were in north China to disarm and evacuate the Japanese, but by guarding ports and railways for the Nationalists they took a side.","Named in a staff study of 13 August 1945, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent about 50,000 Marines to Tientsin and Tsingtao to stand in for Nationalist troops who could not yet reach north China."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Beleaguer</b> was the landing of the United States Marines' III Amphibious Corps in north China in the autumn of 1945, to help the Nationalists reoccupy the region and send its Japanese troops home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name comes from a joint staff study of 13 August 1945, and the occupation plan of Admiral Nimitz's headquarters published the next day aimed the corps at Tientsin and Tsingtao rather than Shanghai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Wedemeyer's orders were to help the Nationalists occupy key areas, receive the Japanese surrender and repatriate Japanese troops, while avoiding any part in a fratricidal conflict in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chiang Kai-shek's armies had no transport able to move large bodies of men over long distances, and in north China the Japanese garrison was the only force that could stop the Communists seizing the cities and railways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peak strength of the corps in China stayed close to 50,000 men, drawn mainly from the 1st and 6th Marine Divisions and the 1st Marine Aircraft Wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war had left two armed Chinese camps each holding back most of its army for a civil war, and Wedemeyer warned in August that the Communists might begin it once Japan surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Marines found about 116,000 Japanese regulars in their area, and on 6 October some 50,000 of them surrendered at Tientsin to General Rockey, who signed in the name of Chiang Kai-shek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese were so cooperative that most arrangements for their care were left to them, while Communist roadblocks and an attack on a Marine patrol showed where the danger now lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Truman declared on 15 December 1945 that the Marines were in north China to disarm and evacuate the Japanese, and that American support would not extend to military intervention in Chinese internal strife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet moving Nationalist troops could be read as giving them an advantage over the Communists, and the Marine Corps' own history judged that the entanglement had an incalculable effect on American policy for a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Benis M. Frank and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Victory and Occupation</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1968), 529–35, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/V/USMC-V-V-1.html. [Open access]","Benis M. Frank and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Victory and Occupation</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1968), 542–58, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/V/USMC-V-V-2.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Time Runs Out in CBI</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 393–96, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Time/USA-CBI-Time-12.html. [Open access]","Theresa L. Kraus, <i>China Offensive</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-39 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 3–4, 20–21, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-ChinaO/index.html. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Statement by the President: United States Policy Toward China,” December 15, 1945, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-united-states-policy-toward-china-0. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Marines sent to north China rather than Shanghai?","a":"Nationalist troops could be flown into Shanghai and Nanking by American planes, but a long gap would pass before they could reach the north in strength, so the Marines were to stand in for them there."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese in north China cooperate so readily?","a":"They had been ordered to surrender only to Chiang Kai-shek's representatives and held their discipline, and the Marine history records them as so cooperative that most arrangements for their own care were left to them."},{"q":"Why did Wedemeyer's directive seem contradictory?","a":"It told him to keep supporting the Nationalists and to help move their troops to key areas, but also not to support them in a fratricidal war, and moving those troops could itself be read as taking sides."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Japanese_Surrender%2C_Tientsin%2C_China%2C_October_1945_-_43150265625.jpg","title":"The Japanese surrender at Tientsin, October 1945","desc":"The surrender ceremony at Tientsin in October 1945, with ranks of Marines drawn up behind the signing table and Japanese officers standing in line across the square.","credit":"Official USMC photograph, Archives Branch, Marine Corps History Division, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Surrender,_Tientsin,_China,_October_1945_-_43150265625.jpg","alt":"Seen from above, a draped table with two armchairs in a sunlit square, a photographer beside it, a line of officers on the left and rows of soldiers on the right."},"wiki":"Operation Beleaguer"},
    "ww2-435": {"questions":["Because so large a force had to stay with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Japan could spare only 11 of its 51 divisions for the conquest of Southeast Asia in December 1941.","With over 1 million men south of the Great Wall in the summer of 1945, the undefeated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the largest part of Japan's army still intact when the war ended."],"abstract":"The <b>China Expeditionary Army</b> was the Japanese army command that ran the war in China, holding the occupied east of the country with its seaports and main railways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Japan planned its attack on the West in 1941, a large force had to be left in Manchuria and an even larger one in China, so that of the army's 51 divisions only 11 could be given to the Southern Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1942 Imperial General Headquarters used it to punish east China after the Doolittle raid, ordering it to destroy the air bases from which Japan might be bombed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By late 1943 it had about 620,000 men in some 32 division equivalents, and five infantry divisions left China for the Pacific islands from that December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1944 Field Marshal Hata commanded 24 divisions in China, two of which, the 26th and 62nd, were then sent to the Philippines and Okinawa for the decisive battle Tokyo expected there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The war in China was also the issue on which peace with the United States broke down, since Tojo refused in October 1941 to accept the withdrawal of troops from China that a settlement would require.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American note of 26 November 1941 asked Japan to withdraw all its military, naval, air and police forces from China and Indochina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> To the end the army stayed largely intact: by the summer of 1945 its commander had over 1 million men south of the Great Wall, and most of Japan's 2 million soldiers on the Asian mainland were in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Allied planners had never treated China as a decisive theatre, and the American army's campaign history calls it a sideshow whose fate was settled by battles fought elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet every division held in China was one Japan could not send against the Americans, which is why American planners hoped a rebuilt Chinese army could tie these armies down and keep them from going home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 92–112, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-4.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 113–30, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-5.html. [Open access]","Mark D. Sherry, <i>China Defensive</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-38 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 5, 15, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-ChinaD/index.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell’s Command Problems</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1956), 405–07, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Command/USA-CBI-Command-11.html. [Open access]","Theresa L. Kraus, <i>China Offensive</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-39 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 3, 6, 17, 22–23, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-C-ChinaO/index.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell’s Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 163, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-5.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Document Handed by the Secretary of State to the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) on November 26, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d409. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Japan send so few divisions south in 1941?","a":"A large force had to stay in Manchuria and an even larger one in China, with further garrisons for Korea, Formosa, Indochina and the home islands, which left only 11 of the army's 51 divisions for the Southern Army."},{"q":"Why did the army in China matter to the talks with Washington?","a":"Withdrawal from China was the point on which agreement failed: Tojo would not accept it in October 1941, and the American note of 26 November asked for the withdrawal of all Japanese forces from China and Indochina."},{"q":"Why was the China theatre called a sideshow?","a":"The Allies never regarded it as decisive and could not send ground forces there, offering air and logistic help instead, and the war was ended by the Pacific battles, so the army's campaign history judges that China's fate was settled elsewhere."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Hata_Syunroku_get_off_a_plane.jpg","title":"Shunroku Hata beside his aircraft","desc":"Shunroku Hata, who as a field marshal commanded the China Expeditionary Army's 24 divisions in 1944, saluting beside an aircraft on an open airfield.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hata_Syunroku_get_off_a_plane.jpg","alt":"An officer in a peaked cap, tunic and riding boots salutes beside the door of a high-winged aircraft, a soldier in a helmet standing at the door."},"wiki":"China Expeditionary Army"},
    "ww2-436": {"questions":["Japan's chief ministers and service chiefs met before the emperor in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 2 July 1941 and resolved not to be deterred by the risk of war with Britain and America.","Decisions hammered out at Liaison Conferences were formalised in an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as on 6 September 1941, when Japan fixed its minimum demands and resolved to prepare for war."],"abstract":"An <b>Imperial Conference</b> was a meeting of Japan's chief ministers and the heads of the army and navy held in the presence of the emperor, and in 1941 it sanctioned each step towards war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Policy was worked out beforehand at Liaison Conferences between the cabinet and the high command, and the Imperial Conference then formalised what they had agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 2 July 1941, after Germany invaded the Soviet Union, a conference decided that Japan would keep to the Tripartite Pact and push south, and would not be deterred by the possibility of war with Britain and America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conference of 6 September resolved to go on negotiating with the United States while preparing for war, and fixed the minimum demands Japan would make and the maximum concessions it would grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Japanese historian argues that the American oil embargo left no choice at that meeting but to harden the resolve for war, and an American historian calls the need for Dutch East Indies oil the main reason Japan went to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Under Tojo, prime minister from October, the conference of 5 November confirmed that course, set a deadline for the talks and approved Japan's last two offers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 29 November the senior statesmen heard Konoye ask why Japan could not go on with broken economic relations but without war, and Tojo answered that the end of such a course would be gradual impoverishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Imperial Conference of 1 December, with Tojo presiding, then decided for war, its record stating that the negotiations had failed and that Japan would open hostilities against the United States, Britain and the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kido noted in his diary that the conference met at two in the afternoon that day, and the opening of the war was then fixed for 8 December, Japanese time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 6 days later Japan's ambassadors handed Washington a memorandum declaring that it was impossible to reach an agreement through further negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 92–112, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-4.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 113–30, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-5.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chapter VII, “The Pacific War,” 955–76, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","Kiyoshi Aizawa, “Japanese Strategy in the First Phase of the Pacific War,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War: The 2009 International Forum on War History, Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 33–43, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/04.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum Handed by the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) to the Secretary of State at 2:20 P.M. on December 7, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d420. [Open access]","Williamson Murray, “Allied Strategy in the First Phase of the Pacific War: Pearl Harbor and the U.S. Reaction,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War: The 2009 International Forum on War History, Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 44–56, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/05.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the conference sanction decisions rather than make them?","a":"The bargaining between the cabinet and the high command took place first at Liaison Conferences; the Imperial Conference, held before the emperor, gave their agreement its formal and final authority."},{"q":"Why did the oil embargo weigh so heavily in September 1941?","a":"Japan's reserves would shrink every month without new supplies, and the navy reckoned it had enough oil to keep the fleet at war for only about a year and a half, so delay itself seemed to close off the choice of war."},{"q":"Why did Tojo reject Konoye's idea of living with broken economic relations?","a":"He told the senior statesmen on 29 November that such a course would end in gradual impoverishment, a slow decline he judged worse than the risks of a war with the United States and Britain."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Gozen-kaigi_11_January_1938.jpg","title":"An Imperial Conference, January 1938","desc":"The Imperial Conference of 11 January 1938, held during the war with China, with the emperor seated at the head of the room, naval officers on one side and army officers on the other.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gozen-kaigi_11_January_1938.jpg","alt":"A panelled hall lit by chandeliers, with a figure seated before a screen at the far end and men in uniform behind two long brocade-covered tables."},"wiki":"Imperial Conference"},
    "ww2-437": {"questions":["Roosevelt offered on 24 July 1941 to guarantee Japan equal access to Indochina's food and raw materials, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went ahead the next day and the assets freeze followed.","Planned in Tokyo as a step short of war, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read in Washington as a search for bases for further conquest and answered within days by a freeze of Japanese assets."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese occupation of southern French Indochina</b> in July 1941 gave Japan air and naval bases from which it could launch attacks on Singapore, the Philippines and the Dutch East Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan already held the north under agreements with Vichy France, which had given it the military occupation of Tonkin and the use of air bases and military facilities there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Imperial Conference of 2 July 1941 ordered that plans for Thailand and Indochina be carried out at once, and Japan served Vichy with what was in effect an ultimatum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal found that Vichy, telling Germany it had no alternative but to give in to violence, accepted the demands, and that 40,000 troops sailed on 24 July to occupy the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The agreement provided for eight air bases near Saigon and naval bases at Saigon and Cam Ranh Bay, and it was signed on 29 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Washington read the move as a step towards conquest, and the acting secretary of state said on 24 July that Japan could only want bases there for further and more obvious movements of conquest in adjacent areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt offered that day to guarantee Japan equal access to the food and raw materials of Indochina if the colony were neutralised, but nothing came of it, and Japanese troops moved into the south the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tokyo told the American ambassador on 25 July that Japan had no intention of making the south a base for armed advance, and warned that a freeze of its assets or an oil embargo would stir up anger among the Japanese people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The freeze came on 26 July, and a Japanese historian notes that Tokyo, which had judged the move to be short of war, had not expected so strong a response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The south then became a bargaining counter, and Japan's last offer of 20 November proposed moving its troops from southern to northern Indochina in return for trade and American oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 92–112, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-4.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chapter VII, “The Pacific War,” 926–31, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Press Release Issued by the Department of State on July 24, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d230. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Japanese Minister for Foreign Affairs (Toyoda) to the American Ambassador in Japan (Grew), July 25, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d232. [Open access]","Kiyoshi Aizawa, “Japanese Strategy in the First Phase of the Pacific War,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War: The 2009 International Forum on War History, Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 33–43, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/04.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Draft Proposal Handed by the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) to the Secretary of State on November 20, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d403. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the move alarm the United States so much?","a":"Bases around Saigon and at Cam Ranh Bay put Japanese aircraft and warships within reach of Singapore, the Philippines and the Dutch East Indies, and Washington could see no defensive reason for them, only preparation for further conquest."},{"q":"Why did Japan expect to get away with it?","a":"It treated the occupation as a step short of war, carried out by agreement with a Vichy government in no position to resist, and did not foresee how strongly the United States and Britain would respond."},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt's neutralisation offer fail?","a":"Japan was already committed by the Imperial Conference of 2 July to carry out its plans for Indochina at once, and its troops were moving into the south the day after the offer was made."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Japanese_troops_entering_Saigon_in_1941.jpg","title":"Japanese troops entering Saigon, 1941","desc":"A Japanese column of horses and carts entering Saigon in 1941, watched by crowds along the street, as the occupation of southern Indochina began.","credit":"Japanese Army photograph, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_troops_entering_Saigon_in_1941.jpg","alt":"Soldiers on horseback and covered carts moving down a wide city street lined with tall colonial buildings and onlookers dressed in white."}},
    "ww2-438": {"questions":["American planners objected that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might drive Japan to seize the oil it needed, since the assets freeze it imposed amounted to a de facto oil embargo.","At Chiang Kai-shek's own request, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> extended the freezing of assets to China as well as Japan, so that licences could be run to strengthen the Chinese government's trade."],"abstract":"<b>Executive Order 8832</b>, signed by President Roosevelt on 26 July 1941, froze Japanese assets in the United States and became, in effect, the American oil embargo against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Legally it was a short amendment, adding China and Japan to an order of April 1940 under which the assets of various European countries had already been frozen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The White House explained that the measure brought every financial, import and export transaction involving Japanese interests under government control, with criminal penalties for violations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> China was included at Chiang Kai-shek's own request, so that the licensing system could be run to strengthen the Chinese government's trade and exchange position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earlier American restrictions had already cut off scrap iron and steel, copper and other metals, and the Tokyo tribunal's judgment records that by 20 June 1941 petroleum exports were banned except to Britain and South America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Because Japan no longer had the dollars to buy the materials of war, the freeze set up a de facto oil embargo, and American military planners objected that it might drive Japan to war to gain the oil it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Admiral Stark opposed a total embargo at the time, favouring a partial one that would leave Japan enough for essential peacetime needs but none for military purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The navy chief of staff, Admiral Nagano, told the emperor that Japan's oil would keep the fleet at war for only a year and a half, and the head of the Planning Board warned that the blockade would bring collapse within 2 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Japanese historian concludes that the complete embargo met the conditions Japan had already set for a war of self-defence, which left its leaders no choice but to harden their resolve for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In November Hull told the Japanese envoys that public feeling on oil was so acute that he might almost be lynched if he let it go freely to Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “Press Release Issued at Poughkeepsie, New York, by the White House on July 25, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d184. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Executive Order No. 8832, Signed by President Roosevelt, July 26, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d185. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 92–112, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-4.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chapter VII, “The Pacific War,” 848–49, 934, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of a Conversation, November 26, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d407. [Open access]","Kiyoshi Aizawa, “Japanese Strategy in the First Phase of the Pacific War,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War: The 2009 International Forum on War History, Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 33–43, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/04.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a freeze of assets work as an oil embargo?","a":"Every transaction with Japanese interests now needed a government licence and Japan's dollars in the United States were frozen, so it no longer had the money to buy oil and the other materials of war."},{"q":"Why did American planners oppose the freeze?","a":"They feared it would push Japan into war to take the oil it needed, endangering American interests in the Atlantic, where the strategy of the time meant to concentrate against Germany."},{"q":"Why did the embargo push Japan towards war rather than compromise?","a":"Its reserves could only shrink: Nagano reckoned the fleet could fight for about a year and a half, and the Planning Board warned of collapse within 2 years, so waiting looked like losing the chance to fight at all."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Teiichi_Suzuki.jpg","title":"Teiichi Suzuki, head of the Planning Board","desc":"Teiichi Suzuki, president of Japan's Planning Board in 1941, who warned after the freeze that the blockade would exhaust Japan within two years; photographed during the Tokyo war crimes trial in 1947.","credit":"US Army photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teiichi_Suzuki.jpg","alt":"Half-length portrait of a thin man with swept-back grey hair in a light double-breasted suit and a spotted tie, standing before a curtain."}},
    "ww2-439": {"questions":["Kurusu warned that Tokyo would likely throw up its hands at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which demanded that Japan support no regime in China but the National Government at Chungking.","Rather than a temporary modus vivendi, Washington answered Japan's Proposal B with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which a Liaison Conference in Tokyo summarily rejected the next day."],"abstract":"The <b>Hull note</b> was the American proposal that Secretary of State Cordell Hull handed to the Japanese envoys Nomura and Kurusu on 26 November 1941, marked strictly confidential, tentative and without commitment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titled an outline of a proposed basis for agreement, it opened with a draft declaration of principles, among them the inviolability of every nation's territory and sovereignty and equality of commercial opportunity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its practical steps asked Japan to withdraw all military, naval, air and police forces from China and Indochina, and to support no government in China other than the National Government at Chungking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In return the United States would lift its freeze on Japanese funds, negotiate a trade agreement and share the cost of stabilising the dollar-yen rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An oral statement handed over with it called the plan one practical example of a broad settlement, meant to bridge the gap between the American draft of 21 June and the Japanese draft of 25 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It answered Japan's Proposal B of 20 November, which had offered to move troops from southern to northern Indochina in exchange for a return to normal trade and a required quantity of American oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A temporary modus vivendi had been drafted instead, but Hull judged its small prospects not worth the risk to Chinese morale, and on 26 November the president agreed to drop it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kurusu, reading the note, said his government would be likely to throw up its hands, and that he did not see how it could consider the paragraphs on withdrawal and on recognising Chiang Kai-shek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Liaison Conference rejected it the next day, and the Tokyo tribunal found that it brought Japan's leaders face to face with principles they had never been prepared to apply, at a moment when their preparations for war were complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Japan's final memorandum of 7 December complained that the American proposal totally ignored Japanese claims and declared further negotiation impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, “Document Handed by the Secretary of State to the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) on November 26, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d409. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Oral Statement Handed by the Secretary of State to the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) on November 26, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d408. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of a Conversation, November 26, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d407. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Draft Proposal Handed by the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) to the Secretary of State on November 20, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d403. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 113–30, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-5.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chapter VII, “The Pacific War,” 969–73, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum Handed by the Japanese Ambassador (Nomura) to the Secretary of State at 2:20 P.M. on December 7, 1941,” <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Japan, 1931–1941, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d420. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Washington drop the modus vivendi?","a":"Hull thought the slight chance that Japan would accept it was not worth the risk of lowering Chinese morale and resistance and opening the way to appeasement, and Britain, China, the Netherlands and Australia had all reacted against it."},{"q":"Why could Japan not accept the note's terms on China?","a":"They meant withdrawing every soldier from China and abandoning the Nanking regime of Wang Ching-wei for Chiang Kai-shek, which Kurusu said he did not see how his government could consider."},{"q":"Why did the note not stop the attack?","a":"Japan's preparations for war were already complete and its deadline was days away; the Tokyo tribunal found that its leaders had never been prepared to apply the principles the note set out."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Hull%2C_Nomura_and_Kurusu_on_7_December_1941.jpg","title":"Hull with Nomura and Kurusu, November 1941","desc":"Cordell Hull walking with Ambassador Kichisaburō Nomura, left, and the special envoy Saburō Kurusu, right, on their way to meet President Roosevelt on 17 November 1941, nine days before the note was handed over.","credit":"Mainichi Shinbun photograph, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hull,_Nomura_and_Kurusu_on_7_December_1941.jpg","alt":"Three men in long overcoats and hats walking side by side along a path, the tall man in the middle flanked by two shorter men in glasses."},"wiki":"Hull note"},
    "ww2-440": {"questions":["Opposing the alliance with Germany as vice minister of the navy, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later forced his Pearl Harbor plan on a doubtful Naval General Staff by threatening to resign.","Convinced that Japan must win or lose on the first day, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pressed for the Midway operation in 1942 and ordered its abandonment after all four carriers of the striking force were lost."],"abstract":"<b>Isoroku Yamamoto</b> was the commander-in-chief of Japan's Combined Fleet from the end of August 1939 and the author of the plan to open the Pacific war with a carrier strike on Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As vice minister of the navy he had opposed the alliance with Germany and Italy, because he feared it would lead to war with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a champion of naval aviation, having headed the navy's aviation headquarters in the 1930s, in a service whose core doctrine was still the battleship with giant guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the London Naval Conference of 1930 he wrote that a navy forced into inferiority should first use air attacks to deliver heavy blows to the superior American fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In January 1941 a preliminary study of a Pearl Harbor operation was ordered, and the American strategic bombing survey later recorded that the attack was conceived and proposed by him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Many in the Naval General Staff thought the plan too risky, and only his argument that the southern advance needed the destruction of the American fleet, his prestige and his threat to resign carried it in October 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He told the navy minister early in 1941 that Japan must be determined to win or lose the war on the first day, but a Japanese historian concludes that even he never drew up a plan for victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 22 November he ordered the carrier force to leave Tankan Bay on the 26th, and on 1 December he gave final approval to the plan of attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Pearl Harbor he pressed for the Midway operation over the objections of the Naval General Staff, and when all four carriers of the striking force were lost there in June 1942 he ordered the operation abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 18 April 1943 codebreakers' knowledge of his itinerary let 16 American P-38 fighters intercept his aircraft over Bougainville, and he was found dead in the wreck of his plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Kiyoshi Aizawa, “Japanese Strategy in the First Phase of the Pacific War,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War: The 2009 International Forum on War History, Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 33–43, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/04.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 13–15, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-2.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 92–112, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-4.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, November 1948), chapter VII, “The Pacific War,” 955–76, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","Noriaki Yashiro, “Japanese Strategy in the Second Phase of the Pacific War,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War: The 2009 International Forum on War History, Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 79–112, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/07.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 59, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Campaigns/USSBS-PTO-5.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 212–14, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yamamoto want to strike Pearl Harbor first?","a":"He believed the southern advance depended on destroying the American fleet at the outset, with a blow heavy enough to break the morale of the US Navy and the American people, rather than waiting for the stronger shipbuilder to choose the moment of battle."},{"q":"Why did the Naval General Staff resist his plan?","a":"Many thought it too risky: the American fleet might not be in port on the day, and the long voyage to Hawaii raised the danger of discovery, while the staff's own plans looked to a fleet battle fought nearer Japan."},{"q":"Why was it possible to kill him in 1943?","a":"American decoders had the precise itinerary of his inspection trip and he was known for punctuality, so long-range P-38 fighters could fly a hidden route of about 700 kilometres (435 miles) to meet his aircraft over Bougainville."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/H78628_Isoroku_Yamamoto.jpg","title":"Captain Yamamoto in Washington, 1926","desc":"Isoroku Yamamoto, left, then Japanese naval attaché in the United States, with the Secretary of the Navy Curtis Wilbur, Captain Kiyoshi Hasegawa and Admiral Edward Eberle at the Navy Department on 17 February 1926.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H78628_Isoroku_Yamamoto.jpg","alt":"Four men seated on a leather sofa, two Japanese officers in dress uniform with swords and two Americans in suits, a ship model on the wall behind."},"wiki":"Isoroku Yamamoto"},
    "ww2-441": {"questions":["Landings at Kota Bharu in northern Malaya began shortly before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did, so the blow at Hawaii opened a Japanese offensive stretching across a quarter of the globe.","Because it concentrated on warships and aircraft, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the base's workshops, power plant and main fuel store standing, and sunk battleships could later be raised."],"abstract":"The <b>attack on Pearl Harbor</b> was the Japanese carrier raid of 7 December 1941 on the United States Pacific Fleet and the airfields of Oahu in the Territory of Hawaii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At 7:55 AM, Hawaiian time, more than 300 aircraft from six carriers began the attack, and in less than 2 hours American forces suffered 3,435 casualties and lost or saw badly damaged 188 aircraft, eight battleships, three light cruisers and four other vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The striking force had sailed from Hitokappu Bay in the Kuriles on 26 November, ready to turn back if the Washington talks succeeded, and withdrew undetected after losing 29 aircraft and five midget submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan's reply to the latest American proposals, saying only that further negotiation was useless, reached <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States_Secretary_of_State\">Secretary of State</span> Cordell Hull at 2:20 PM in Washington, after the bombing had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The congressional inquiry called it the greatest military and naval disaster in the nation's history, and Churchill told the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"House_of_Commons_of_the_United_Kingdom\">Commons</span> it was a cold-blooded, calculated and treacherous attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Pearl Harbor was the eastern flank of a far wider offensive, and the landings at <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Malayan_campaign\">Kota Bharu</span> in <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"British_Malaya\">Malaya</span> began shortly before the first bombs fell on Oahu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Within a day Japan had also struck Hong Kong, Guam, the Philippines, <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Wake_Island\">Wake</span> and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Midway_Atoll\">Midway</span>, a list <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Franklin_D._Roosevelt\">Roosevelt</span> read out when he asked <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States_Congress\">Congress</span> to declare war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Isoroku_Yamamoto\">Yamamoto</span> had pressed the raid on a doubtful <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Imperial_Japanese_Navy_General_Staff\">Naval General Staff</span>, arguing that an intact American fleet at Hawaii could break into the western Pacific while the army seized the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The raiders concentrated on warships and aircraft and left the workshops, power plant and main fuel store standing, so damaged ships could be raised, and the carriers <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"USS_Enterprise_(CV-6)\">Enterprise</span> and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"USS_Lexington_(CV-2)\">Lexington</span> were at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"National_Archives_and_Records_Administration\">National Archives</span> judges that the attack instantly united Americans behind a massive mobilisation for war, and the Australian official history that Japan had turned against itself a people with far greater resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, <i>Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack: Report of the Joint Committee</i>, 79th Cong., 2nd sess., S. Doc. 244 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), xi–xii, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/pha/congress/part_0.html. [Open access]","Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, <i>Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack: Report of the Joint Committee</i>, 79th Cong., 2nd sess., S. Doc. 244 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), pt. 2, “The Japanese Attack and Its Aftermath,” 53–66, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/pha/congress/part_2.html. [Open access]","Military History Section, Headquarters, Army Forces Far East, <i>Pearl Harbor Operations: General Outline of Orders and Plans</i>, Japanese Monograph No. 97 (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 1–25, http://ibiblio.org/pha/monos/097/index.html. [Open access]","“Japanese Note to the United States, December 7, 1941,” <i>Department of State Bulletin</i> 5, no. 129 (13 December 1941), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/p3.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 December 1941, vol. 376, cols. 1686–1700, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/11/war-situation. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 7, “Widespread Onslaught,” 122–33, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070100. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Joint Session of the Congress, Monday, December 8, 1941: Address by the President of the United States,” in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec03.asp. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 6–8, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Joint Address to Congress Leading to a Declaration of War Against Japan (1941),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/joint-address-to-congress-declaration-of-war-against-japan. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the raiders strike on a Sunday morning?","a":"Because the planners expected the Pacific Fleet, following its usual routine during manoeuvres, to come into Pearl Harbor on Friday and leave on Monday, so Sunday offered the best chance of finding the battleships at anchor."},{"q":"Why did Japan's note arrive after the attack had begun?","a":"It was timed to be handed over only shortly before the raid so as not to give away the surprise, and when it was delivered at 2:20 in Washington it announced the end of negotiations rather than a declaration of war."},{"q":"Why did the damage at Pearl Harbor prove less lasting than it first looked?","a":"The workshops, power plant and fuel store were left largely intact and the fleet's carriers were at sea, so the base kept working, most damaged ships could be raised and repaired, and the carriers were untouched."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Akagi_Pearl_Harbor_Second_Wave_Prep.jpg/1920px-Akagi_Pearl_Harbor_Second_Wave_Prep.jpg","title":"Aircraft on the deck of Akagi, 7 December 1941","desc":"Aircraft prepare to take off from the carrier Akagi, the flagship of the striking force, for the second wave of the attack on Pearl Harbor.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Imperial Japanese Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akagi_Pearl_Harbor_Second_Wave_Prep.jpg","alt":"Rows of single-engined aircraft with spinning propellers on a carrier deck, with deck crew in white crouching beside them."},"wiki":"Attack on Pearl Harbor"},
    "ww2-442": {"questions":["Yamamoto threatened to resign before the Naval General Staff accepted his Hawaii plan, which then went to the fleet commanders as part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that set out the whole war.","In the east the American fleet will be destroyed, ran the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which also fixed that the date for opening hostilities would come later in an Imperial General Headquarters order."],"abstract":"The <b>Combined Fleet Operation Order No. 1</b> was the Japanese navy's master order for opening the war in the Pacific, issued by Admiral Isoroku Yamamoto on 5 November 1941 and titled preparations for war and commencement of hostilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was an 89-page volume covering every phase of the navy's preparations, and the inquiry of the American Congress treated the day it was issued as the day the plan for Pearl Harbor was complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order said that an approximate date for opening operations would be announced by a later order, while the exact moment war began would be given in an order of Imperial General Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its statement of aims for the east was that the American fleet would be destroyed, its lines of supply to the Orient cut, and victories exploited to break the enemy's will to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only the American Pacific Fleet and the air power on Oahu were seen as serious obstacles, so the striking force was built round six carriers carrying about 360 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The plan behind the order went back to January 1941, when Yamamoto asked Rear Admiral Takijiro Onishi to study whether a surprise attack on Hawaii was feasible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A section of the Naval General Staff opposed it as too risky, arguing that dividing the fleet could wreck the southern invasions and that a force spotted on the way might be destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> War games at the Naval War College in Tokyo from 10 to 13 September included a secret session on the attack, and a landing on Oahu was ruled out because there were not enough transports for it and the southern drive together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only after Yamamoto threatened to resign did the Chief of the Naval General Staff, Osami Nagano, approve the plan on 20 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Secrecy shaped the order itself: the missions of the submarine force and the carrier force were left blank in the printed text, and the carrier crews learned their target only after the order of 2 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, <i>Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack: Report of the Joint Committee</i>, 79th Cong., 2nd sess., S. Doc. 244 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), pt. 2, “The Japanese Attack and Its Aftermath,” 53–57, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/pha/congress/part_2.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 6–8, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Military History Section, Headquarters, Army Forces Far East, <i>Pearl Harbor Operations: General Outline of Orders and Plans</i>, Japanese Monograph No. 97 (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 1–25, http://ibiblio.org/pha/monos/097/index.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 105–9, https://archive.org/details/StrategyAndCommandTheFirstTwoYears. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 6, “Awaiting the First Blow,” 109–11, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070099. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 59–61, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were parts of the order left blank in print?","a":"To keep the Hawaii operation secret even inside the navy; the missing passages were told only to a small circle of senior officers and staff, and army leaders seem not to have heard of the plan until November."},{"q":"Why did the Naval General Staff resist Yamamoto's plan?","a":"Its planners thought the seizure of the southern resource areas came first, feared that sending most of the carriers east would weaken the southern invasions, and worried that a force detected on the long voyage might be lost."},{"q":"Why was no landing on Oahu planned?","a":"There were not enough transports for both the southern invasions and an Oahu landing, preparations could not be made in time, and a slow convoy would have made it unlikely that the force could approach undetected."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Zuikaku_Hitokappu_Bay_November_1941.jpg/1920px-Zuikaku_Hitokappu_Bay_November_1941.jpg","title":"Zuikaku at Hitokappu Bay, November 1941","desc":"The carrier Zuikaku at anchor in Hitokappu Bay in the Kurile Islands, where the striking force assembled before sailing for Hawaii on 26 November 1941.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Imperial Japanese Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuikaku_Hitokappu_Bay_November_1941.jpg","alt":"A grey aircraft carrier lying at anchor in a wide, calm bay beneath low hills."}},
    "ww2-443": {"questions":["A bomb set off the forward magazine of Arizona, killing 1,177 men, and Oklahoma capsized with hundreds trapped inside her, the worst losses along <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that morning.","West Virginia and California were sunk at their moorings on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but raised and rebuilt, and West Virginia was present in Tokyo Bay at the Japanese surrender."],"abstract":"<b>Battleship Row</b> was the line of moorings along the south-east shore of Ford Island in Pearl Harbor where the battleships of the United States Pacific Fleet lay on the morning of 7 December 1941, when the fleet was devastated in less than 2 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Seven of the fleet's eight battleships were there, most in pairs: Nevada astern of Arizona, Arizona with the repair ship Vestal beside her, Tennessee inside West Virginia, Maryland inside Oklahoma, and California.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eighth, the flagship Pennsylvania, was in dry dock across the harbour, where she was bombed and strafed and lost 31 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The heavy ships were the raiders' main targets, and the torpedo planes, which did the most damage, came in low from the south-east and hit every outboard battleship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wooden fins fitted to the torpedoes kept them from diving into the mud of the shallow harbour, which had been the obstacle the Japanese planners expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Arizona was the worst loss: a bomb set off her forward magazine, the explosion killed 1,177 sailors and marines, and the ship was never raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oklahoma took torpedo after torpedo and capsized, and the counts of how many differ, from at least four in the navy's account to Congress to eight in the National Park Service's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She lost 429 men, and only 32 of those trapped inside her hull could be cut out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> West Virginia and California were sunk at their moorings but raised and rebuilt, Nevada was run aground rather than block the channel, and Maryland was back in service by June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roosevelt told Congress the next day only that the attack had caused severe damage and that very many American lives had been lost, and the committee of Congress later listed eight battleships among the ships sunk or badly damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, <i>Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack: Report of the Joint Committee</i>, 79th Cong., 2nd sess., S. Doc. 244 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), pt. 2, “The Japanese Attack and Its Aftermath,” 55–65, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/pha/congress/part_2.html. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Battleship Row,” Pearl Harbor National Memorial, https://www.nps.gov/perl/learn/historyculture/battleship-row.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “USS Arizona,” Pearl Harbor National Memorial, https://www.nps.gov/perl/learn/historyculture/uss-arizona.htm. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 7, “Widespread Onslaught,” 122–33, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070100. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Joint Session of the Congress, Monday, December 8, 1941: Address by the President of the United States,” in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec03.asp. [Open access]","Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, <i>Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack: Report of the Joint Committee</i>, 79th Cong., 2nd sess., S. Doc. 244 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), xi–xii, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/pha/congress/part_0.html. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Joint Address to Congress Leading to a Declaration of War Against Japan (1941),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/joint-address-to-congress-declaration-of-war-against-japan. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the outboard battleships suffer most?","a":"Torpedoes could reach only the ships on the open-water side, so every outboard battleship was hit by one or more torpedoes, while the inboard ships, Tennessee and Maryland, were damaged mainly by bombs."},{"q":"Why did shallow water not save the fleet?","a":"The planners expected the narrow, shallow harbour to defeat ordinary aerial torpedoes, so they fitted stabilising fins and had the torpedoes launched from very low height, which let them run straight instead of burying themselves in the mud."},{"q":"Why was Arizona never raised?","a":"The explosion of her forward magazine damaged her too badly to salvage, so she still rests on the bottom with more than 900 of her dead inside, and the memorial completed over her in 1961 honours everyone killed in the attack."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Pearl_Harbor_Japanese_recon_photo_of_battleship_row_80G30551.jpg/1920px-Pearl_Harbor_Japanese_recon_photo_of_battleship_row_80G30551.jpg","title":"Battleship Row from a Japanese aircraft, 7 December 1941","desc":"A vertical view taken from a Japanese aircraft during the horizontal bombing attack, showing Nevada, Arizona with Vestal outboard, Tennessee with West Virginia, Maryland with Oklahoma, and part of the oiler Neosho.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Imperial Japanese Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pearl_Harbor_Japanese_recon_photo_of_battleship_row_80G30551.jpg","alt":"An aerial photograph looking straight down on a row of paired warships beside a shoreline, with oil slicks and ripples spreading across the water."},"wiki":"Battleship Row"},
    "ww2-444": {"questions":["Soon after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Senate voted for war with Japan by 82 to none, and the House by 388 to one, Jeannette Rankin casting the only vote against.","Roosevelt dictated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to his secretary Grace Tully on the evening of the attack, choosing a short appeal over the full account of Japanese deceit that Cordell Hull wanted."],"abstract":"The <b>Day of Infamy speech</b> was the address Franklin D. Roosevelt gave to a joint session of Congress on 8 December 1941, the day after the attack on Pearl Harbor, asking it to declare that a state of war had existed with Japan since the attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It opened with the words 'Yesterday, December 7, 1941, a date which will live in infamy', and the President was in the chamber for only 10 minutes, from 12:29 to 12:39.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had dictated the draft to his secretary Grace Tully at about 5:00 PM, choosing a short, plain appeal rather than the full account of Japanese deceit that Secretary of State Cordell Hull had urged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His most important correction was to the first line, which in the typed draft read 'a date which will live in world history'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The speech stressed that Japan had kept negotiating while it prepared the attack, and that the distance from Japan to Hawaii showed the raid had been planned days or even weeks before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Congress acted at once: the Senate passed the war resolution by 82 votes to none, and the House by 388 votes to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The one vote against was cast by Jeannette Rankin of Montana, a pacifist who had first been elected to the House in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The resolution declared formally the state of war it said Japan had thrust upon the United States, and directed the President to use the entire naval and military forces of the country to bring the conflict to a successful end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roosevelt signed it at 4:00 PM, and the next evening he told the country by radio that 'We are now in this war' and that Germany and Italy already considered themselves at war with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In London on 11 December Churchill told the Commons that the United States had declared war on its assailants, that Britain had done the same, and that much of the Western Hemisphere was following.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, “Joint Address to Congress Leading to a Declaration of War Against Japan (1941),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/joint-address-to-congress-declaration-of-war-against-japan. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Joint Session of the Congress, Monday, December 8, 1941: Address by the President of the United States,” in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec03.asp. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Address to Congress Requesting a Declaration of War with Japan,” 8 December 1941, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-congress-requesting-declaration-war-with-japan. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Proceedings in the Senate,” 8 and 11 December 1941, in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec04.asp. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Proceedings in the House of Representatives,” 8 and 11 December 1941, in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec05.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. House of Representatives, “RANKIN, Jeannette,” History, Art & Archives, https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/R/RANKIN,-Jeannette-(R000055)/. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Radio Address of the President, Broadcast from the White House, December 9, 1941,” in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec06.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 December 1941, vol. 376, cols. 1686–1700, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/11/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the speech so short?","a":"Roosevelt chose a brief, uncomplicated appeal to the American people over the long recital of Japanese treachery that Hull wanted, so the case rested on a few plain facts that needed no argument."},{"q":"Why does the speech list Malaya, Hong Kong, Guam, the Philippines, Wake and Midway?","a":"Japan had struck all of them within a day, and listing them one by one showed that Pearl Harbor was part of a planned offensive across the Pacific rather than a single raid."},{"q":"Why did Congress declare war on Japan only, and not on Germany?","a":"Roosevelt asked only for war with Japan, which had attacked; Germany and Italy were dealt with 3 days later, after they declared war on the United States themselves."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/FDR_addressing_Congress_on_the_Declaration_of_War%2C_December_8%2C_1941.jpg/1920px-FDR_addressing_Congress_on_the_Declaration_of_War%2C_December_8%2C_1941.jpg","title":"Roosevelt addresses Congress, 8 December 1941","desc":"President Franklin D. Roosevelt speaking to the joint session of Congress on 8 December 1941, from the Office of War Information collection.","credit":"Office of War Information, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FDR_addressing_Congress_on_the_Declaration_of_War,_December_8,_1941.jpg","alt":"A man at a lectern crowded with microphones, with officials seated behind him on a raised dais."},"wiki":"Day of Infamy speech"},
    "ww2-445": {"questions":["Under the Tripartite Pact Germany was bound to help Japan only if Japan was attacked, so nothing in the treaty required the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed Pearl Harbor by 4 days.","Roosevelt called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the long known and the long expected, and the Senate answered it with a war resolution passed by 88 votes to none."],"abstract":"The <b>German declaration of war on the United States</b> was made on 11 December 1941, 4 days after the attack on Pearl Harbor, in a note from Foreign Minister Joachim von Ribbentrop handed to the American chargé d'affaires in Berlin and in a speech by Hitler to the Reichstag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The note accused the United States of breaking every rule of neutrality and of open military aggression, naming the destroyers Greer, Kearny and Reuben James as ships that had fired on German submarines according to plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It concluded that Roosevelt's government had virtually created a state of war, and that Germany too now considered itself at war with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Washington the German chargé d'affaires, Hans Thomsen, handed a copy to the State Department at 9:30 AM and asked for arrangements for his embassy to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler read the Reichstag a new agreement with Italy and Japan by which none of the three would make an armistice or peace with the United States or Britain without full mutual understanding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> No treaty obliged Germany to act, since under the Tripartite Pact of September 1940 Germany and Italy had promised to help Japan only if Japan was attacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that Ribbentrop had nonetheless told the Japanese ambassador on 28 November that Germany would join a war between Japan and the United States immediately, and that Germany and Italy had agreed a few days later to support Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Italy declared war the same day, and Roosevelt asked Congress to recognise a state of war with both, calling what had happened the long known and the long expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Senate voted for war with Germany by 88 votes to none, and the House by 393 with one member answering present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill had told the Commons the same day that Japan must have counted on German and Italian support, and that the United States would very likely face the open hostility of all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Joachim von Ribbentrop, note to the American chargé d’affaires in Berlin, 11 December 1941, with the State Department’s account of its delivery in Washington, <i>Department of State Bulletin</i>, 13 December 1941, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gerdec41.asp. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, speech to the Reichstag, 11 December 1941, as recorded by the Monitoring Service of the British Broadcasting Corporation, ibiblio World War II Resources, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/policy/1941/1941-12-11b.html. [Open access]","“Pact Between the Axis Powers Barring a Separate Peace with the United States or Great Britain, December 11, 1941,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tripart.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Against the United States,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judunite.asp. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Proceedings in the Senate,” 8 and 11 December 1941, in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec04.asp. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Proceedings in the House of Representatives,” 8 and 11 December 1941, in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec05.asp. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 December 1941, vol. 376, cols. 1686–1700, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/11/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Germany not obliged to declare war?","a":"The Tripartite Pact was defensive: Germany and Italy were bound to help Japan only if Japan was attacked, and at Pearl Harbor Japan was the attacker."},{"q":"Why did the German note name American destroyers?","a":"It rested Germany's case on the claim that the United States was already fighting at sea, so that Germany was only recognising a state of war that Roosevelt had virtually created."},{"q":"Why did the three Axis powers sign a new agreement the same day?","a":"It bound Germany, Italy and Japan not to make a separate armistice or peace with the United States or Britain, so each could be sure the others would not leave the war without it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1987-0703-507%2C_Berlin%2C_Reichstagssitzung%2C_Rede_Adolf_Hitler.jpg/1920px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1987-0703-507%2C_Berlin%2C_Reichstagssitzung%2C_Rede_Adolf_Hitler.jpg","title":"Hitler's Reichstag speech, 11 December 1941","desc":"Hitler speaking to the Reichstag in the Kroll Opera House on 11 December 1941, when he announced war with the United States; Ribbentrop sits on the government bench beside the rostrum.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-1987-0703-507, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-1987-0703-507,_Berlin,_Reichstagssitzung,_Rede_Adolf_Hitler.jpg","alt":"A man in uniform speaking at a rostrum beneath a huge eagle emblem, with rows of uniformed men seated on benches below him."},"wiki":"German declaration of war on the United States"},
    "ww2-446": {"questions":["Roosevelt called U-boats the rattlesnakes of the Atlantic in a broadcast about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after a submarine fired two torpedoes at a destroyer carrying mail to Iceland.","Hitler had ordered his navy to fire only in self-defence, Raeder told the Nuremberg court, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the case of the Kearny were where that order was tested."],"abstract":"The <b>Greer incident</b> was the encounter of 4 September 1941 in which a German submarine fired on the American destroyer Greer south-east of Greenland, 3 months before the United States entered the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roosevelt told the country in a broadcast on 11 September that the Greer had been carrying mail to Iceland under the American flag when the submarine fired a torpedo at her and later a second, without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called German submarines and raiders the rattlesnakes of the Atlantic and warned that from then on Axis warships entering waters vital to American defence did so at their own peril.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German account differed, and Hitler later told the Reichstag that the Greer had been operating with British aircraft against German submarines when it happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same speech he listed American patrols in the western Atlantic, the landing in Iceland and the depth-charging of a submarine by the destroyer Kearny in October as steps by which Roosevelt had driven towards war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler had ordered his navy not to open fire of its own accord but only in self-defence, Grand Admiral Erich Raeder testified at Nuremberg, and the Greer and the Kearny were the cases in which that order was put to the test.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Germany declared war on 11 December, Ribbentrop's note named the Greer first among the destroyers that, it said, had fired on German submarines according to plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The note called this proof that the United States had virtually created a state of war, and Hitler added that Roosevelt had ordered his navy to attack and sink German and Italian ships everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that German policy had shifted during 1941 from keeping America out of the war to encouraging Japan in a course almost certain to bring it in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Roosevelt for his part told Americans on 9 December that Germany and Italy considered themselves at war with the United States already, whatever formal declaration they made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” 11 September 1941, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-11. [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, speech to the Reichstag, 11 December 1941, as recorded by the Monitoring Service of the British Broadcasting Corporation, ibiblio World War II Resources, http://www.ibiblio.org/pha/policy/1941/1941-12-11b.html. [Open access]","Joachim von Ribbentrop, note to the American chargé d’affaires in Berlin, 11 December 1941, with the State Department’s account of its delivery in Washington, <i>Department of State Bulletin</i>, 13 December 1941, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gerdec41.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 14, proceedings of 20 May 1946, testimony of Erich Raeder, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-20-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Against the United States,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judunite.asp. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Radio Address of the President, Broadcast from the White House, December 9, 1941,” in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec06.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Roosevelt make so much of the Greer?","a":"It let him present a German attack on an American warship on legitimate business as piracy, and so justify warning that Axis warships in waters vital to American defence would be in danger from then on."},{"q":"Why does the German account of the encounter differ?","a":"Hitler claimed that the Greer had been hunting the submarine together with British aircraft, which turned the American story of an unprovoked attack into one of American aggression."},{"q":"Why did Germany still cite the Greer in December?","a":"Its declaration of war rested on the claim that the United States was already fighting at sea, and the Greer was the first and best known of the clashes it could point to."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/USS_Greer%3B0514502.jpg","title":"The destroyer USS Greer","desc":"The United States Navy destroyer Greer, which was fired on by a German submarine in September 1941.","credit":"United States Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USS_Greer;0514502.jpg","alt":"A long, low warship with four funnels and the number 145 painted on her bow, steaming across choppy water."}},
    "ww2-447": {"questions":["Only 11 of Japan's 51 divisions could be spared for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because most of the army was tied down in China or facing the Soviet Union in Manchuria and Korea.","The oil of Borneo and Sumatra was the prize of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and plans were laid to seize Palembang by airborne assault before its wells could be wrecked."],"abstract":"The <b>Southern Operation</b> was the Japanese plan, and the offensive that carried it out from December 1941, for seizing the American, British and Dutch possessions of South-East Asia and the western Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its object was the resources of the south, above all oil, and plans were laid to seize the Borneo oilfields early and Palembang by airborne assault before the wells could be wrecked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened with simultaneous blows at Pearl Harbor, the Philippines and Malaya, to be followed by Borneo, Celebes, Sumatra and Java, and finally by pacification and, if possible, Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Imperial General Headquarters set no fixed limits but reckoned that the Philippines would take about 50 days, Malaya 100 and the Netherlands East Indies 150.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only 11 of the army's 51 divisions and about 700 aircraft could be spared, because 28 divisions were committed in China and 13 stood in Manchuria and Korea against the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Southern Army under Count Terauchi was ordered mobilised on 6 November 1941, with four armies for the Philippines, Thailand and Burma, Malaya, and the islands from Borneo to Java, and Malaya was given four divisions including the Guards, more than any other target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost the whole carrier strength of the Combined Fleet went to the attack on Pearl Harbor, so the army fighters covering the Malaya landings had to be treated as wholly expendable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the first day landings went ahead at Kota Bharu, Singora and Pattani while Hong Kong, Guam, the Philippines and Wake were struck, and 2 days later naval aircraft sank Prince of Wales and Repulse off Malaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A defensive perimeter was then to run from the Kuriles through Wake, the Marshall and Gilbert Islands, New Guinea, Timor, Java and Malaya to the border of Burma and India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plans show no intention of defeating the United States: Japan meant to fight a war of limited objectives and then negotiate a favourable peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 59–61, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 105–9, https://archive.org/details/StrategyAndCommandTheFirstTwoYears. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 6, “Awaiting the First Blow,” 109–11, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070099. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 74–75, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 7, “Widespread Onslaught,” 122–33, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070100. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “Joint Session of the Congress, Monday, December 8, 1941: Address by the President of the United States,” in <i>Declarations of a State of War with Japan, Germany, and Italy</i>, S. Doc. 148, 77th Cong., 1st sess., Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/dec03.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Southern Operation need the attack on Pearl Harbor?","a":"The planners believed the southern drive could succeed only if the American Pacific Fleet was destroyed or neutralised, so that it could not break in while Japanese forces were spread across the region."},{"q":"Why could so few divisions be used in the south?","a":"The war in China held 28 divisions and the defence against the Soviet Union another 13, leaving only 11 divisions and about 700 aircraft for the conquest of the whole region."},{"q":"Why did the plan end with a defensive perimeter?","a":"Japan did not expect to defeat the United States; it meant to hold the conquered resource areas behind a fortified ring and wait for a negotiated peace on favourable terms."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Southern_Operations_December_1941-May_1942.jpg","title":"Map of the Southern Operations, December 1941 – May 1942","desc":"A map from the Japanese side of the war showing the routes of the armies and fleets in the Southern Operations, with the operations of December to February, March and April to May drawn in different colours.","credit":"Reports of General MacArthur, vol. 2, United States Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Operations_December_1941-May_1942.jpg","alt":"A coloured map of South-East Asia and the western Pacific with black, red and green arrows sweeping south from China, Indochina and Formosa towards Malaya, Borneo, Java and New Guinea."}},
    "ww2-448": {"questions":["Told before sailing that no fighter cover could be given off Singora, Admiral Tom Phillips took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> north anyway and lost both his capital ships to aircraft from Indochina.","Churchill could remember no naval blow so heavy or so painful as the loss of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose battleship and battlecruiser were sunk by bombers and torpedo bombers off Malaya."],"abstract":"<b>Force Z</b> was the British squadron of the battleship Prince of Wales, the battlecruiser Repulse and four destroyers that sailed from Singapore on 8 December 1941 to attack the Japanese landings in southern Thailand and northern Malaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two capital ships had been sent east with great misgivings on the Admiralty's part, and on 1 December the Admiralty, worried about their exposed position, suggested moving them away from Singapore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Admiral Sir Tom Phillips judged it unacceptable to keep a powerful naval force idle at Singapore while the army and air force were hard pressed, and hoped to strike the transports off Singora and Kota Bharu at dawn on 10 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before sailing he was told that fighter protection off Singora could not be provided, and only a reconnaissance to the north was promised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three Japanese reconnaissance aircraft sighted the force on the evening of 9 December, and Phillips turned back, judging that surprise had been lost and that heavy air attack must be expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A signal reporting landings at Kuantan drew him to the coast instead, where all was quiet, while Japanese submarines had already reported the squadron, as post-war interrogations showed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 11 in the morning of 10 December a search aircraft found the ships, and high-level bombers and torpedo bombers from bases in Indochina attacked them in waves for about an hour and a half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Repulse, hit by five torpedoes and a bomb, sank first, and Prince of Wales, hit by four or possibly five torpedoes and a bomb, capsized some three-quarters of an hour later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Phillips and Captain J. C. Leach went down with the flagship, Repulse alone lost 27 officers and 486 men, and the Australian official history puts the navy's total loss at 845 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill broke the news to the Commons that day and the next said he could remember no naval blow so heavy or so painful, while Japan's own record says the attack crippled the British Far Eastern Fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Layton, “Loss of H.M. Ships Prince of Wales and Repulse,” despatch to the Admiralty, December 1941, with the narrative of operations of Force Z and the reports of Captain W. G. Tennant, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38214, 26 February 1948, 1237–44, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38214/page/1237/data.pdf. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 8, “Invasion of Malaya,” 141–52, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070101. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “British Naval Losses,” <i>Hansard</i>, 10 December 1941, vol. 376, col. 1501, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/10/british-naval-losses. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 December 1941, vol. 376, cols. 1686–1700, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/11/war-situation. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 74–75, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 7, “Widespread Onslaught,” 122–33, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070100. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Phillips sail without fighter cover?","a":"He thought keeping a powerful fleet idle at Singapore while the army and air force were under attack was unacceptable, and he counted on surprise and the speed of his heavy ships instead of air cover."},{"q":"Why did Phillips think his heavy ships could take the risk?","a":"He believed that any Japanese aircraft he met would not be carrying anti-ship bombs or torpedoes, and that on the way back he would face only hastily organised long-range bombers from Indochina."},{"q":"Why was the loss so serious for Britain?","a":"With both ships gone there was no prospect that British naval action could seriously hinder the Japanese landings in Malaya, and the value of the Singapore naval base had virtually vanished."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Japanese_high-level_bombing_attack_on_HMS_Prince_of_Wales_and_HMS_Repulse_on_10_December_1941_%28NH_60566%29.jpg/1280px-Japanese_high-level_bombing_attack_on_HMS_Prince_of_Wales_and_HMS_Repulse_on_10_December_1941_%28NH_60566%29.jpg","title":"Force Z under attack, 10 December 1941","desc":"Taken from a Japanese aircraft during the first high-level bombing attack: Repulse, near the bottom, has just been hit by one bomb and near-missed by others, and Prince of Wales, near the top, is making heavy smoke.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Imperial Japanese Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_high-level_bombing_attack_on_HMS_Prince_of_Wales_and_HMS_Repulse_on_10_December_1941_(NH_60566).jpg","alt":"An aerial photograph of two warships far below on open sea, one ringed by white splashes from falling bombs."},"wiki":"Force Z"},
    "ww2-449": {"questions":["Moving on civilian bicycles seized in the country and landing behind the defenders from the sea, Yamashita's troops in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the Johore Strait within 2 months.","Percival thought Japan had used at least 150,000 men in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the Australian official history put the whole of Yamashita's army at about 125,000."],"abstract":"The <b>Malayan campaign</b> was the Japanese conquest of the Malay Peninsula and Singapore, fought between the first landings on 8 December 1941 and the British surrender on 15 February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan's planners gave Malaya the fastest transports and expected to take it in about 100 days, with Singapore to be taken from the landward side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yamashita's 25th Army landed at Singora and Pattani in southern Thailand and at Kota Bharu in northern Malaya, where Australian aircraft drove the first wave back before it landed again the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British plan to forestall such landings by moving into southern Thailand, Operation Matador, was cancelled only after a fatal delay, and the 11th Indian Division poised for it was caught on the wrong foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The air force had 141 serviceable aircraft against a target of 336, and within 48 hours the equivalent of three bomber squadrons and one fighter squadron had been lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Jitra on 11 and 12 December an advance guard of the Japanese 5th Division drove the 11th Indian Division from its position, at a cost to the attackers of 27 killed and 83 wounded by their own records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Percival judged that the enemy relied on outflanking movements and infiltration by small parties, moved on civilian bicycles seized in the country, and crossed rivers, swamps and jungle faster than anyone had thought possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Slim River in January the 11th Indian Division ceased for a time to exist as a fighting formation, and Percival blamed the utter weariness of troops who had fought and moved day and night for a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The numbers are disputed: Percival estimated at least 150,000 Japanese against a British total of a little over 125,000, while the Australian official history gives Yamashita's army as 125,400 men, nearly 37,000 of them line-of-communication troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Japan's own record the drive went smoothly despite sporadic resistance, and by late January every unit had reached the Johore Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["A. E. Percival, “Operations of Malaya Command, from 8th December, 1941 to 15th February, 1942,” despatch to the Secretary of State for War, April 1946, Second Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38215, 26 February 1948, 1245–1346, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38215/supplement/1245/data.pdf. [Open access]","Robert Brooke-Popham, “Operations in the Far East, from 17th October 1940 to 27th December 1941,” despatch, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38183, 22 January 1948, 535–76, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38183/supplement/535/data.pdf. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 7, “Widespread Onslaught,” 122–33, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070100. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 8, “Invasion of Malaya,” 141–52, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070101. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 74–75, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 105–9, https://archive.org/details/StrategyAndCommandTheFirstTwoYears. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 59–61, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese advance so fast?","a":"They had command of the air within days and of the sea soon after, moved light on bicycles, and used outflanking marches and landings behind the defenders, who had to fall back to avoid being cut off."},{"q":"Why was Operation Matador never carried out?","a":"It meant entering neutral Thailand, which London had warned would almost certainly mean war with Japan, so the commander-in-chief held back until the Japanese convoys had already reached the coast."},{"q":"Why do the figures for the two armies differ so much?","a":"Percival estimated Japanese strength by multiplying the divisions he believed had been used by a notional 20,000 men each, while the Australian history gives a count of Yamashita's army itself, with its line-of-communication troops included."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Japanese_bicycle_troops_crossing_a_river.%2C_Malayan_campaign%2C_1942.jpg/1920px-Japanese_bicycle_troops_crossing_a_river.%2C_Malayan_campaign%2C_1942.jpg","title":"Japanese bicycle troops crossing a river in Malaya","desc":"Japanese soldiers wading a river with their bicycles beside a demolished bridge during the Malayan campaign.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_bicycle_troops_crossing_a_river.,_Malayan_campaign,_1942.jpg","alt":"A column of heavily laden soldiers wading waist-deep across a river, carrying bicycles, beside a steel bridge with a broken span."},"wiki":"Malayan campaign"},
    "ww2-450": {"questions":["Japanese barges crossed the Johore Strait on the night of 8 February 1942 against the Australians on the north-western shore, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came just one week later.","London had told its commanders that the security of the naval base came before every other consideration, and the Australian official history called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the greatest disasters in British history."],"abstract":"The <b>fall of Singapore</b> was the surrender of the British, Indian, Australian and local forces on Singapore Island to Lieutenant-General Tomoyuki Yamashita's 25th Army on 15 February 1942, a week after the Japanese crossed the Johore Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Singapore mattered for its naval base, and in December London told the commander-in-chief that the base's security was the vital issue, with which nothing else must compete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Japan's plan was to take the fortress from the landward side through Malaya, a conquest expected to need about 100 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The landings began soon after 10:30 PM on 8 February against the Australians holding the north-western shore, where one machine-gun officer counted some 20 landing craft carrying about 25 men each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 13 February the Japanese were within about 4.5 kilometres (5,000 yards) of the seafront, bringing the whole city within artillery range, and Wavell ordered Percival to fight on house to house if necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the morning of 15 February Percival's commanders agreed that a counter-attack was impracticable, and with the water supply guaranteed for perhaps 24 hours more he decided to capitulate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He met Yamashita that afternoon at the Ford motor factory near Bukit Timah village and was given no copy of the terms, which he later recorded from memory as the unconditional surrender of all forces in the Singapore area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting stopped at 8:30 PM, and Japanese accounts suggest the attackers had expected days more fighting and had fired most of their shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Figures differ: Percival put the British force at about 85,000 and the Japanese on the island and in south Malaya at no fewer than 100,000, while the Australian history gives Japanese losses in taking the island as 1,714 killed and 3,378 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same history counts more than 130,000 defenders taken prisoner in the 70-day campaign and calls the fall one of the greatest disasters in British history, and Churchill refused the Commons any recriminations over so lamentable a misfortune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["A. E. Percival, “Operations of Malaya Command, from 8th December, 1941 to 15th February, 1942,” despatch to the Secretary of State for War, April 1946, Second Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38215, 26 February 1948, 1245–1346, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38215/supplement/1245/data.pdf. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 17, “Cease Fire,” 371–83, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070109. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 15, “Defence of Western Area,” https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070107. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 74–75, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Fall of Singapore,” <i>Hansard</i>, 17 February 1942, vol. 377, cols. 1674–87, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/17/fall-of-singapore. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 105–9, https://archive.org/details/StrategyAndCommandTheFirstTwoYears. [Open access]","Robert Brooke-Popham, “Operations in the Far East, from 17th October 1940 to 27th December 1941,” despatch, Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38183, 22 January 1948, 535–76, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38183/supplement/535/data.pdf. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, compiled from Japanese Demobilization Bureaux records (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint, CMH Pub 13-1, 1994), 59–61, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Percival surrender while his men still had ammunition?","a":"The water supply was breaking down faster than it could be repaired, military food stocks were down to a few days, and his commanders judged a counter-attack impracticable, so further resistance seemed likely to bring disaster on a crowded civilian city."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese not know how near the defenders were to collapse?","a":"Their own artillery was running short and the defenders' fire had been heavy, so Yamashita's chief of staff expected days more of fighting and was surprised when a flag of truce arrived."},{"q":"Why was the loss of Singapore so heavy a blow for Britain?","a":"The naval base had been the centre of British defence in the Far East, and the campaign that ended there put more than 130,000 men into captivity and, in the Australian history's words, dealt a heavy blow to the prestige of British arms."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Conference_on_the_surrender_of_the_British_forces%2C_Singapore%2C_1942.jpg/1920px-Conference_on_the_surrender_of_the_British_forces%2C_Singapore%2C_1942.jpg","title":"The surrender conference at Singapore, 15 February 1942","desc":"Japanese officers facing British officers across a table at the conference on the surrender of the British forces in Singapore, February 1942.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Japanese army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conference_on_the_surrender_of_the_British_forces,_Singapore,_1942.jpg","alt":"Japanese officers in uniform seated and standing behind a table, with British officers in shirtsleeves seen from behind in the foreground."},"wiki":"Fall of Singapore"},
    "ww2-421": {"questions":["A War Ministry report of September 1942 counted 400 military brothels, 280 of them in China, holding the women the army called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and recruited mostly through private agents.","The Tokyo tribunal found that Japanese troops in Guilin lured women with promises of factory work and then forced them to serve as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the town was abandoned in July 1945."],"abstract":"<b>Comfort women</b>, ianfu in Japanese, were the women held in comfort stations, the military brothels of the Japanese armed forces, where by Tokyo's own admission in 1993 they lived in misery under coercion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A government study found that the stations were opened at the military's request and that the army licensed and equipped them and set their rules, hours and tariffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Private agents acting for the military did most of the recruiting, often taking women against their will through coaxing and coercion, sometimes with officials or soldiers directly involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stations were confirmed from China and the Philippines to Burma, New Guinea and French Indochina, and the women came from Japan, the Korean Peninsula, China, Taiwan, the Philippines, Indonesia and the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No document gives a total, so every figure is an estimate: Hata Ikuhiko's have ranged from 20,000 to 90,000, Yoshimi Yoshiaki's from 45,000 to 200,000, and Su Zhiliang's from 360,000 to 410,000, the last counting women abused outside the stations as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The system grew out of the war in China, where a station was opened for Japanese naval troops at Shanghai in 1932 and the number multiplied after full-scale war broke out in 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Okamura Yasuji, a senior staff officer at Shanghai in 1932, later called himself the initiator, saying that after rapes by soldiers he had asked the governor of Nagasaki to send women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Officials gave three reasons for the stations: to stop rapes of Chinese women from feeding anti-Japanese feeling, to keep venereal disease from weakening the army, and to stop secrets leaking through contact with local women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A War Ministry report of September 1942 counted 400 such facilities, 280 of them in north, central and south China, and Chinese women were among those confirmed to have been held in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal found that troops occupying Guilin recruited women for supposed factory work and forced them into prostitution, but the trials otherwise treated such abuse only as general inhumane treatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Japanese government, “On the Issue of Wartime ‘Comfort Women’,” 4 August 1993, reproduced by the Asian Women's Fund, https://www.awf.or.jp/e6/statement-03.html. [Open access]","Yohei Kono, “Statement by Chief Cabinet Secretary Yohei Kono on the Result of the Study on the Issue of ‘Comfort Women’,” 4 August 1993, reproduced by the Asian Women's Fund, https://www.awf.or.jp/e6/statement-02.html. [Open access]","Asian Women's Fund, “Who Were the Comfort Women? The Establishment of Comfort Stations,” <i>Digital Museum: The Comfort Women Issue and the Asian Women's Fund</i>, https://www.awf.or.jp/e1/facts-01.html. [Open access]","Asian Women's Fund, “Number of Comfort Stations and Comfort Women,” <i>Digital Museum: The Comfort Women Issue and the Asian Women's Fund</i>, https://www.awf.or.jp/e1/facts-07.html. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 40–41, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, ch. 8, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1021–22, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Pingfan Zhang and Cheng Fang, “‘We Resolve Our Own Sorrows’: Screening Comfort Women in Chinese Documentary Films,” <i>Humanities and Social Sciences Communications</i> 11 (2024): article 139, https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02652-y. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese army want brothels of its own?","a":"Because its own officers feared what their soldiers' conduct was costing. Internal documents gave three reasons for opening the stations: rapes of local women were turning occupied populations against Japan, venereal disease was taking men out of the ranks, and contact with local women might let military secrets leak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the number of women still disputed?","a":"Because there is no document with comprehensive data from which a total could be worked out. Every estimate starts from an assumed number of soldiers for each woman and an assumed rate at which women were replaced, so published figures differ more than tenfold, from about 20,000 to 410,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the issue become public only in the 1990s?","a":"Because the postwar trials had not pursued it as a crime in its own right and survivors had stayed silent. The first public testimony of a Korean survivor, and documents showing direct military involvement, pushed the Japanese government into the study that ended in its apology of August 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Chinese_girl_from_one_of_the_Japanese_Army%27s_%27comfort_battalions%27.jpg/1280px-Chinese_girl_from_one_of_the_Japanese_Army%27s_%27comfort_battalions%27.jpg","title":"A Chinese woman from a ‘comfort battalion’, 1945","desc":"A Chinese woman from one of the Japanese army's comfort battalions waits to be questioned by a Royal Air Force officer at a camp in Rangoon on 8 August 1945.","credit":"Photograph by Sergeant A D Titmuss, No. 9 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_girl_from_one_of_the_Japanese_Army's_'comfort_battalions'.jpg","alt":"A young woman in a light dress sits barefoot on a low wooden frame, talking with a crouching officer in a peaked cap and shorts."},"wiki":"Comfort women"},
    "ww2-422": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons on 18 July 1940 that Burma would stop arms, petrol and lorries going up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for 3 months, then let the agreement lapse that October.","Chiang told the American ambassador in October 1940 that closing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had cut off both American and Soviet supplies and that reopening it had begun to lift morale."],"abstract":"The <b>Burma Road</b> was the mountain highway that carried supplies from the railhead at Lashio in British Burma into south-west China, and in 1940 it became the Nationalist government's sole supply link with the outside world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its value was as much symbolic as practical, for maladministration and corruption had cut an already low capacity, and by 1941 American observers called it the worst bottleneck in aid to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 24 June 1940 Japan asked Britain to stop war material reaching China through Burma and Hong Kong, warning that the traffic was seriously harming Anglo-Japanese relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese government urged Washington to declare that American goods must pass freely and to threaten Japan with an embargo on scrap iron and petrol, so as to stiffen the British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cordell Hull told the press that a highway running through countries not at war was a different matter from a blockade at sea, and that if Japan wanted the route closed it would have to blockade it itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 18 July Churchill told the Commons that Burma would suspend for 3 months the transit of arms, ammunition, petrol, lorries and railway material, because Britain was itself engaged in a life and death struggle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called it a temporary arrangement to gain time for a just settlement, and on 8 October announced that as no settlement had come the agreement would run until 17 October and not be renewed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chiang Kai-shek told the American ambassador that the closure had stopped American goods and promised Soviet supplies alike and sapped popular morale, which the reopening had already begun to restore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fall of Rangoon in March 1942 cut off the flow of supplies by land, leaving only an air route over the mountains from India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The first convoy over a new road from Ledo in India left on 12 January 1945 and entered Kunming on 4 February, after which Chiang suggested that the route be renamed the Stilwell Road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Transit of War Material to China,” <i>Hansard</i>, 18 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 399–401, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/18/transit-of-war-material-to-china. [Open access]","Chinese Ministry for Foreign Affairs to the Chinese Embassy in Washington, 10 July 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1940</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 60, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d60. [Open access]","Cordell Hull to Joseph C. Grew, 17 July 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1940</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 69, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d69. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 8 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 261–352, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/08/war-situation. [Open access]","Nelson T. Johnson to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 20 October 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1940</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 720, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v04/d720. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 8, 44, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-1.html. [Open access]","Frank H. Heck, “Airline to China,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 7, <i>Services around the World</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1958), 114, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VII/AAF-VII-5.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Time Runs Out in CBI</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1959), 140–41, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Time/USA-CBI-Time-4.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain agree to close the road in 1940?","a":"Because it could not risk war with Japan while fighting for its life in Europe. Churchill told the Commons that the government had obligations to China but could not ignore that Britain was in a life and death struggle, and that time and a relief of tension were needed before any fair settlement could come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the United States refuse to accept the closure?","a":"Because it rejected Japan's right to close a road through neutral territory. Hull drew a line between a belligerent's lawful blockade at sea and a highway running through countries not at war, while the Chinese urged Washington to back the British with the threat of an embargo on Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the road less useful than its fame suggested?","a":"Because it was never an efficient highway. It was not built for all weathers, maladministration and corruption had cut its capacity, and by 1941 American representatives judged it the worst logistical bottleneck in aid to China, valued above all as China's last tie with the outside world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/China-bound_convoy_travels_Burma_Road.jpg","title":"A China-bound convoy on the Burma Road","desc":"Lorries of a supply convoy climbing the Burma Road towards China, in a photograph used by the US Army Center of Military History in its account of the China campaigns of 1942 to 1945.","credit":"U.S. Army Military History Institute, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China-bound_convoy_travels_Burma_Road.jpg","alt":"A line of heavy lorries and smaller vehicles grinds up a rough dirt road cut into a wooded hillside."},"wiki":"Burma Road"},
    "ww2-423": {"questions":["Between June and December 1943 the India-China Wing lost 168 crew members in accidents, most of them on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> route between the airfields of Assam and Kunming.","After the Bay of Bengal landing was cancelled in December 1943, Roosevelt offered Chiang all available air transport for carrying supplies over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to his armies instead."],"abstract":"The <b>Hump</b> was the airmen's name for the mountains between Assam in India and Yunnan in China, and for the air route across them that became China's lifeline after the fall of Rangoon in March 1942 cut the road from Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Chabua in the Brahmaputra valley to Kunming was some 800 kilometres (500 miles), over ridges of 4,300 to 4,900 metres (14,000 to 16,000 feet), and the main ridge, between the Salween and Mekong rivers, gave the whole route its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Monsoon rains of around 5,000 millimetres (200 inches) a season and Japanese fighters made it a route of surpassing danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In January 1942 T. V. Soong told Roosevelt that 100 transports could fly 12,000 tons a month into China, and Roosevelt assured Chiang Kai-shek that the supply route via India could be kept open by air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The start was meagre, for in July 1942 American military aircraft carried only 85 tons, and the Chinese national airline, flying freight between Assam and Kunming under an army contract, about 221.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its tonnage fuelled the theatre's quarrels, for Claire Chennault believed air power supplied over it could beat Japan in China, while Joseph Stilwell, backed by Marshall, distrusted the airlift and wanted a land route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the Bay of Bengal landing promised at Cairo was given up after Tehran, Roosevelt offered Chiang instead the concentration of all air transport on carrying supplies over the Hump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Air Transport Command the eastbound lift rose from 2,916 tons in July 1943 to 18,975 in July 1944 and a peak of 71,042 net tons in July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The price was high: between June and December 1943 the India-China Wing had 155 major aircraft accidents, 135 of them on the Hump route, and 168 crew members were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the crisis of late 1944 Chennault's air force received 13,578 tons in November alone, and the lift dwarfed the trickle over the Ledo Road until the Hump was officially closed in November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Frank H. Heck, “Airline to China,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 7, <i>Services around the World</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1958), 114–51, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VII/AAF-VII-5.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 77–78, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-2.html. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt to Chiang Kai-shek, Cairo, 5 December 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1943, China</i>, doc. 139, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943China/d139. [Open access]","J. Franklin Ray Jr., memorandum to Alger Hiss, 7 July 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1943, China</i>, doc. 609, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943China/d609. [Open access]","Herbert Weaver and Lee Bowen, “Problems of Command,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 435–36, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-13.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, “Foreword,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/index.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Time Runs Out in CBI</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1959), 12, 25, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Time/USA-CBI-Time-1.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Hump so dangerous to fly?","a":"Because of the land, the weather and the enemy together. Aircraft climbing out of a valley barely above sea level had to cross ridges of 4,300 to 4,900 metres, the monsoon brought enormous rainfall for months on end, and Japanese fighters were active over the route in the winters of 1942 to 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hump tonnage cause so many quarrels?","a":"Because every ton flown to one purpose was denied to another. Chennault wanted it for an air offensive he believed could beat Japan in China, while Stilwell and Marshall wanted to reopen a land route with Chinese armies, and later the B-29 bases and the Chinese forces all competed for the same lift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the airlift grow so much in 1944 and 1945?","a":"Because more and larger aircraft, new airfields and round-the-clock flying under the Air Transport Command turned a trickle into a flow. Monthly deliveries rose from under 3,000 tons in July 1943 to over 71,000 in July 1945, far more than the Ledo Road ever carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/231227-F-IO108-005_A_C-46_flies_over_the_Hump.jpg/1280px-231227-F-IO108-005_A_C-46_flies_over_the_Hump.jpg","title":"A C-46 Commando over the Hump, 1944","desc":"A Curtiss C-46 Commando, the transport considered the workhorse of the Hump, flying over snow-covered peaks in 1944.","credit":"U.S. Army Air Forces photograph, National Museum of the United States Air Force, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:231227-F-IO108-005_A_C-46_flies_over_the_Hump.jpg","alt":"A dark twin-engined transport plane with a star on its fuselage flies above jagged snowy mountains."},"wiki":"The Hump"},
    "ww2-424": {"questions":["Chennault claimed that in 10 weeks over Rangoon the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed 217 Japanese aircraft with never more than 20 serviceable fighters, for six pilots killed or captured.","When the contracts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran out on 4 July 1942, only five pilots chose to stay in China and join the Army Air Forces' new task force."],"abstract":"The <b>Flying Tigers</b> was the popular name of the American Volunteer Group, a fighter unit of American pilots and ground crews that flew for China under Claire Chennault from December 1941 to July 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chennault, a retired Air Corps officer advising the Chinese air force since 1937, sold the plan in Washington as a way to protect the Burma Road while China's air force was rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A contract signed with T. V. Soong on 15 April 1941 set it up outside lend-lease, and 101 pilots, 63 from the Navy and 38 from the Army, took one-year contracts once officers were assured they would lose no seniority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hired through a private aircraft company and paid with Chinese money, they were nonetheless, as a report passed to the State Department admitted, tacitly sponsored by Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Near Toungoo in Burma they trained on 100 P-40B fighters released from a Swedish order, in Chennault's tactics of fighting in pairs and refusing turning combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They first fought over Kunming on 20 December 1941, and one squadron helped the RAF defend Rangoon until the port fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chennault's figures for the 10 weeks at Rangoon credited his pilots, who never had more than 20 serviceable fighters, with 217 enemy aircraft destroyed and 43 probables for six pilots killed or captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American newspapers made them famous as the Flying Tigers, a name Chennault never understood, and their shark-mouthed noses copied a picture of RAF fighters in the Libyan desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In April and May 1942 they strafed Japanese columns on the roads to Lashio and the Salween, helping to halt the advance into Yunnan, though an American general complained that most volunteers were now moved by mercenary motives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The group was disbanded on 4 July 1942, when only five of its pilots chose to stay in China with the Army Air Forces, and the official history credits it with almost 300 enemy aircraft destroyed for fewer than 50 planes and 9 pilots lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weaver, “Commitments to China,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 486–89, 504–06, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-14.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 17–19, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-1.html. [Open access]","Claire Lee Chennault, <i>Way of a Fighter: The Memoirs of Claire Lee Chennault</i>, ed. Robert Hotz (New York: G. P. Putnam's Sons, 1949), 130–36, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.506335. [Open access]","Lauchlin Currie to Stanley K. Hornbeck, enclosing report no. 32, 1 November 1941, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1941</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 782, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d782. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, 3 July 1942, transmitting J. Franklin Ray's summary of Chungking interviews, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 94, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d94. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 143, 147–48, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-4.html. [Open access]","Austin C. Brady to the Secretary of State, Rangoon, 21 November 1941, transmitting a message from Claire Chennault for Lauchlin Currie, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1941</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 803, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v05/d803. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How could American officers fight for China before Pearl Harbor?","a":"Because Washington wanted China defended without going to war itself. The volunteers were hired by a private company and paid with Chinese money, officers could resign without losing seniority, and American warships escorted their ship past the Japanese mandated islands although the escort was contrary to the neutrality laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the P-40 do well against nimbler Japanese fighters?","a":"Because Chennault's tactics played to its strengths. Pilots flew in pairs, dived on the enemy at high speed, made one quick pass and broke away, using the P-40's ruggedness, firepower and diving speed while refusing the turning fights for which the lighter Japanese aircraft were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Chennault want Chinese pilots organised the same way?","a":"Because he blamed the system rather than the airmen. In November 1941 he told Currie that some 350 competent Chinese pilots could form effective groups if they were taken out of the Aeronautical Commission's control and given the same status as the American volunteers, apart from pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Hells_Angels%2C_Flying_Tigers_1942.jpg/1280px-Hells_Angels%2C_Flying_Tigers_1942.jpg","title":"The Hell's Angels squadron in flight, 1942","desc":"Shark-mouthed P-40 fighters of the 3rd Squadron of the American Volunteer Group, the Hell's Angels, photographed in flight by the pilot R. T. Smith on 28 May 1942.","credit":"Photograph by R. T. Smith, San Diego Air and Space Museum Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hells_Angels,_Flying_Tigers_1942.jpg","alt":"Four fighter planes with painted shark mouths fly in a stepped line against a pale sky, the nearest numbered 68."},"wiki":"Flying Tigers"},
    "ww2-425": {"questions":["Claire Chennault argued that air power could beat Japan in China, but <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, backed by Marshall, distrusted the airlift and pressed for a ground campaign to reopen Burma.","After the surprise seizure of its airstrip on 17 May 1944, Myitkyina held out against the Chinese and American troops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until it was declared secure on 3 August."],"abstract":"<b>Joseph Stilwell</b> was the American general whose strategy in the China-Burma-India theatre rested on reopening a land route to China with Chinese armies trained and led by himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the Japanese drove his Chinese divisions out of Burma in 1942, Chiang Kai-shek gave him command of the Chinese Army in India on 2 July, and the British offered Ramgarh in Bihar as its training centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ramgarh Training Center was activated on 26 August 1942, and during the summer and autumn some 45,000 Chinese troops were gathered there to be armed with lend-lease weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 10,000 soldiers who had escaped from Burma were in India by October and 20,000 more were to be flown in, though the American embassy doubted the Chinese army was ready to help retake Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Yunnan a second force expected to reach 30 divisions was to come under his command, but Chinese commanders disputed his choice of units and the amount of artillery he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> His plan was bitterly opposed by the airman Claire Chennault, who believed air power could do more for less, while Stilwell, strongly supported by George Marshall, had no faith in an airlift and pressed on with a ground offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 31 October 1943 his Chinese divisions began the drive south from the Brahmaputra valley towards Mogaung and Myitkyina, the key to the north Burma route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 17 May 1944 American volunteers of the force codenamed Galahad and a Chinese regiment took Myitkyina's airstrip by complete surprise, but the town held out as its garrison grew from about 500 to more than 3,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Myitkyina was declared secure on 3 August, 2 days after the Japanese commander Mizukami killed himself, at a cost of 972 Chinese and 272 Americans killed and 980 Americans sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His harsh criticism of Chiang's will to fight dominated accounts of the Nationalist war for decades, a view that a historian drawing on Chiang's diaries argues cannot stand up to the archives opened since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 192–94, 212–13, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-6.html. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Command Problems</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1956), 224–26, 252–54, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Command/USA-CBI-Command-6.html. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, 29 October 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 149, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d149. [Open access]","William P. Ludden to Clarence E. Gauss, Kunming, 16 December 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 157, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d157. [Open access]","Herbert Weaver and Lee Bowen, “Problems of Command,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 435–36, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-13.html. [Open access]","Lee Bowen, “Allied Offensive in Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 494–517, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]","Yung-fa Chen, “Chiang Kai-shek and the Japanese Ichigo Offensive, 1944,” in <i>Chiang Kai-shek and His Time: New Historical and Historiographical Perspectives</i>, ed. Laura De Giorgi and Guido Samarani (Venice: Edizioni Ca' Foscari, 2017), 37, https://edizionicafoscari.unive.it/media/pdf/books/978-88-6969-127-0/978-88-6969-127-0-ch-02.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why train Chinese soldiers in India rather than in China?","a":"Because the weapons and food were there. Lend-lease ordnance landed at Karachi could be sent straight to Ramgarh for training, the embassy reported thousands of Chinese soldiers already in India, and Chiang had already charged Stilwell with feeding and caring for the troops who escaped from Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stilwell and Chennault quarrel?","a":"Because they wanted the same scarce supplies for opposite strategies. Chennault thought a modest air force supplied by an expanded airlift could strike the Japanese hard, while Stilwell, backed by Marshall, doubted any airlift could sustain China and wanted the tonnage for a ground offensive to reopen the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Myitkyina take 11 weeks to capture?","a":"Because the surprise of 17 May was not followed up. Untried Chinese troops attacking the town on 20 May became confused, fired on their own men and fell back, the Japanese reinforced the garrison to over 3,000 in strong positions, and the besiegers had no land line of supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/SC_190517_-_Lt._Gen._Joseph_W._Stilwell_with_Chinese_General_Sun_Li-jen_%28left%29_and_an_unidentified_Chinese_general_in_northern_Burma%2C_discussing_a_tactical_situation_over_a_map._29_March%2C_1944.jpg/1280px-SC_190517_-_Lt._Gen._Joseph_W._Stilwell_with_Chinese_General_Sun_Li-jen_%28left%29_and_an_unidentified_Chinese_general_in_northern_Burma%2C_discussing_a_tactical_situation_over_a_map._29_March%2C_1944.jpg","title":"Stilwell with Sun Li-jen in northern Burma, 1944","desc":"Lieutenant General Joseph Stilwell, centre, with the Chinese general Sun Li-jen, left, and another Chinese general discussing the tactical situation over a map in northern Burma on 29 March 1944.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph SC 190517, U.S. National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SC_190517_-_Lt._Gen._Joseph_W._Stilwell_with_Chinese_General_Sun_Li-jen_(left)_and_an_unidentified_Chinese_general_in_northern_Burma,_discussing_a_tactical_situation_over_a_map._29_March,_1944.jpg","alt":"Three men in field shirts, one wearing glasses, lean together over a map beside a vehicle among trees."},"wiki":"Joseph Stilwell","places":["city:1159147861"]},
    "ww2-426": {"questions":["Announced on the eve of China's national day in October 1942, the talks that produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved Chiang to cable Roosevelt that nothing could do more for Chinese morale.","Hull wanted jurisdiction over American soldiers settled by a separate exchange of notes so that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would keep no vestige of the old system of foreign privileges."],"abstract":"The <b>Sino-American Treaty for the Relinquishment of Extraterritorial Rights in China</b> was signed in Washington on 11 January 1943 by Secretary of State Cordell Hull and the Chinese ambassador, Wei Tao-ming, to abolish the extraterritorial system under which Americans in China had been exempt from Chinese jurisdiction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Roosevelt sent it to the Senate on 1 February with a supplementary exchange of notes, describing its two aims as the abolition of that system and the regulation of related matters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Washington had long been pledged to give the rights up as quickly as circumstances allowed, most recently in letters exchanged with the Chinese foreign minister in May 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain too had promised in July 1940, in the same statement that closed the Burma Road, to negotiate the abolition of extraterritorial rights and the return of concessions once peace came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In May 1942 Hull complained that Madame Chiang's newspaper articles condemning extraterritoriality gave the United States no credit for its liberal policy, and asked the embassy whether to act now or wait for an opportune moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 9 October 1942, the eve of China's national day, London and Washington issued simultaneous statements that they would negotiate the immediate relinquishment of their rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chiang told a mass meeting that ending the unequal treaties fulfilled one of Sun Yat-sen's aims, and cabled Roosevelt that the gesture would do more for his people's morale than anything else could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hull handed the ambassador a draft on 24 October, calling the step a practical manifestation of American foreign policy and asking that the text stay confidential while it was discussed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A British minister told the Commons that Britain's parallel agreement would cover only extraterritorial rights in Chinese territory and not Hong Kong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Abolition raised the practical question of who would try American soldiers stationed in China, and Hull had it settled by a separate exchange of notes so that the treaty kept no vestige of the old system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Transit of War Material to China,” <i>Hansard</i>, 18 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 399–401, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/18/transit-of-war-material-to-china. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “China (Extra-Territorial Rights),” <i>Hansard</i>, 14 October 1942, vol. 383, cols. 1602–3, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/oct/14/china-extra-territorial-rights. [Open access]","Chiang Kai-shek to Franklin D. Roosevelt, transmitted by Clarence E. Gauss, 12 October 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 242, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d242. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, 12 October 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 243, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d243. [Open access]","Cordell Hull to John Carter Vincent, 6 May 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 216, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d216. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Transmittal to Congress of a Treaty for the Relinquishment of Extraterritorial Rights in China,” 1 February 1943, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/transmittal-congress-treaty-for-the-relinquishment-extraterritorial-rights-china. [Open access]","Cordell Hull to Clarence E. Gauss, 4 January 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1943, China</i>, doc. 624, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943China/d624. [Open access]","Maxwell M. Hamilton, memorandum of conversation, 24 October 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 257, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d257. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had the United States not given up the rights sooner?","a":"Because it had always tied the step to circumstances. It was pledged to give the rights up as rapidly as conditions allowed, and as late as May 1942 Hull was still asking the embassy whether to act at once or hold the matter until an opportune occasion arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Chiang treat the announcement as a victory?","a":"Because it answered a long-standing nationalist demand. He told a mass meeting that abolishing the unequal treaties fulfilled one of Sun Yat-sen's aims, and wrote to Roosevelt that China had at last gained equality among the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the treaty raise the question of American soldiers?","a":"Because once Americans fell under Chinese law, someone had to decide who would try troops stationed in China. Washington believed it could claim jurisdiction anyway, but kept the issue out of the treaty and settled it by notes, so the treaty itself retained nothing of the old privileges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Hull-Dawming.jpg","title":"Cordell Hull and Wei Tao-ming exchange ratifications","desc":"Secretary of State Cordell Hull and the Chinese ambassador Wei Tao-ming at the State Department exchanging ratifications of the treaty, which came into force at the moment of the exchange.","credit":"Office of War Information photograph, Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hull-Dawming.jpg","alt":"Two men in dark suits shake hands across a desk with papers on it, in front of tall windows with half-drawn blinds."},"wiki":"Treaty between the United States and China for the Relinquishment of Extraterritorial Rights in China"},
    "ww2-427": {"questions":["A Chinese record of a dinner during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has Roosevelt telling Chiang that China should take its place as one of the Big Four and recover Taiwan after the war.","The amphibious landing in the Bay of Bengal that Chiang pressed for at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dropped within days, once Roosevelt and Churchill had returned from meeting Stalin at Tehran."],"abstract":"The <b>Cairo Conference</b> brought Roosevelt, Churchill and Chiang Kai-shek together in North Africa in late November 1943 to settle the Allies' aims against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text, agreed on 26 November and released by the White House on 1 December, declared that the three Allies coveted no gain for themselves and would fight on until Japan surrendered unconditionally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan was to be stripped of the Pacific islands it had seized since 1914, Manchuria, Formosa and the Pescadores were to be restored to China, and Korea was in due course to become free and independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No American record of Roosevelt's dinner with Chiang on 23 November has been found, and the account later published is a summary from the Chinese government's own files.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In it Roosevelt says China should take its place as one of the Big Four, the two agree that the north-eastern provinces, Taiwan and the Pescadores must return to China, and Chiang proposes joint trusteeship for the Ryukyus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The military bargain was harder, for Chiang's price for committing his armies in Burma was a large amphibious operation in the Bay of Bengal, and he also asked for a loan of a billion dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Chinese insisted too on an official rate of 20 of their dollars to one American when the black market paid 240, which turned the B-29 airfields at Chengdu into a vast expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After Tehran, where the Allies planned a great landing in Europe for late spring, Roosevelt told Churchill on 5 December that landing craft could not be spared for the Bay of Bengal and offered Chiang a smaller campaign or delay until November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chiang replied on 23 December that he would delay his offensive, but warned that relegating China to the background had caused serious misgivings and that Burma was a matter of life and death for China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Americans greeted the communiqué with jubilation, although Reuters had released most of the story nearly 24 hours early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“Final Text of the Communiqué,” 26 November 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, doc. 343, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d343. [Open access]","“Chinese Summary Record,” Roosevelt–Chiang dinner meeting, 23 November 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, doc. 259, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d259. [Open access]","Editors of <i>Foreign Relations of the United States</i>, “Editorial Note,” Roosevelt–Chiang dinner meeting, 23 November 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, doc. 258, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d258. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt to Winston Churchill, Cairo, 5 December 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, doc. 490, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d490. [Open access]","Chiang Kai-shek to Franklin D. Roosevelt, Chungking, 23 December 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, doc. 529, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d529. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Command Problems</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1956), 77, 82, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Command/USA-CBI-Command-2.html. [Open access]","Stephen T. Early to Harry L. Hopkins, 2 December 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i>, doc. 350, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d350. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Chinese record of the dinner matter so much?","a":"Because no American record of the talk was ever made, so the summary the Chinese government kept is the fullest account of what Roosevelt and Chiang said to each other at Cairo. The American editors printed it in the official volume for exactly that reason, noting how little authoritative information otherwise exists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Bay of Bengal landing cancelled?","a":"Because Tehran committed the Allies to grand operations in Europe in the late spring of 1944, which needed so many heavy landing craft that none could be spared for a simultaneous landing in the Bay of Bengal. Roosevelt judged that ending the war with Germany early would help China most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did money become an issue at Cairo?","a":"Because the Chinese asked for a billion-dollar loan and wanted American spending converted at an official rate of 20 to 1 when the black market paid 240 to 1. At that rate the B-29 airfields at Chengdu alone would cost the United States a huge sum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/CairoConferenceParticipants.jpg","title":"Chiang, Roosevelt and Churchill at Cairo","desc":"Chiang Kai-shek, Franklin Roosevelt, Winston Churchill and Madame Chiang seated in front of their generals and admirals at Cairo on 25 November 1943.","credit":"US Army photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CairoConferenceParticipants.jpg","alt":"Four seated figures, one man in a white suit and a woman in a light coat, with a row of uniformed officers standing behind them beneath a pergola."},"wiki":"Cairo Conference"},
    "ww2-428": {"questions":["Peasants told visiting American journalists that they could live on bark and peanut shells but not the grain taxes, at the height of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the front-line province of Henan.","Stripped of their grain by their own army during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Henan villagers disarmed thousands of retreating Nationalist soldiers when the Japanese attacked in April 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Chinese famine of 1942–1943</b> struck Henan, a front-line province of north China fought over since 1938, after drought ruined its harvests in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Food production fell to about half the level of earlier years, and across Nationalist, Japanese and Communist territory around a million people died of starvation and related causes while perhaps three million fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That toll follows a statistical reconstruction of a little under a million, against a commonly cited three million and the estimate of two to three million dead made by the journalists Theodore White and Annalee Jacoby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> War made the drought deadly, for Henan's battered countryside had to feed Nationalist armies of over 700,000 men, while some 100,000 Japanese and collaborationist troops lived off grain purchases and raids in the occupied two-fifths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1941 the Nationalist land tax was collected in grain, and in the summer of 1942 at least a sixth of the wheat harvest was taken from the villages even as the drought withered the autumn crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> White and Jacoby met a district officer ordered to deliver some 180,000 kilograms (400,000 pounds) of grain from a harvest of about 160,000 kilograms (350,000 pounds), and peasants who said they could bear the famine but not the taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American diplomat noted in March 1943 that the government's relief was almost all money and credit, with no food moving into a province whose northern part, beyond the Yellow River, the Japanese largely held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In occupied districts, one collaborator later said, the occupiers had moved by 1942 from taking the eggs to eating the eggs and starving the chickens, seizing grain for their own survival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even after the good harvest of 1943 the troops crowded into Henan went on plundering the people, carrying off livestock that relief committees had bought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Japanese attacked in April 1944, peasants stripped by their own army turned on the retreating Nationalist troops, and White and Jacoby reported an estimate that 50,000 soldiers were disarmed in a few weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Baker, “The Slow, the Quick and the Dead: Environment, Politics and Temporality in the Henan Famine, 1942–43,” <i>International Review of Environmental History</i> 4, no. 2 (2018): 93–110, https://doi.org/10.22459/IREH.04.02.2018.07. [Open access]","Theodore H. White and Annalee Jacoby, <i>Thunder Out of China</i> (New York: William Sloane Associates, 1946), 166–78, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.188120. [Open access]","John Carter Vincent to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 30 March 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1943, China</i>, doc. 173, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943China/d173. [Open access]","George Atcheson Jr. to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 30 June 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1943, China</i>, doc. 205, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943China/d205. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 339–40, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-9.html. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 15 June 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 6, <i>China</i>, doc. 93, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d93. [Open access]","Yung-fa Chen, “Chiang Kai-shek and the Japanese Ichigo Offensive, 1944,” in <i>Chiang Kai-shek and His Time: New Historical and Historiographical Perspectives</i>, ed. Laura De Giorgi and Guido Samarani (Venice: Edizioni Ca' Foscari, 2017), 46, https://edizionicafoscari.unive.it/media/pdf/books/978-88-6969-127-0/978-88-6969-127-0-ch-02.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a drought become one of the war's worst famines?","a":"Because the war had already drained the province and the state kept taking grain. Armies on both sides of the front lived off Henan's villages, the Nationalist land tax was collected in kind, and requisitioning and soaring prices struck suddenly just as the harvests failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was grain not moved into Henan in time?","a":"Because the government sent paper rather than food. Neighbouring Shaanxi had ample stores, but ordering them into Henan would have upset the balance of power the regime depended on, a commander in Hubei refused to release grain, and an American diplomat found no actual food being moved at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Allies learn of the famine so slowly?","a":"Because it was hidden behind censorship and rumour. In Chungking the story arrived as fragments of gossip from people who had heard from someone who had been there, and American officers found that reports of famine and of a peasant rising in Henan only seeped through the Chinese censorship in the spring of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Agsphoto_11239_full.jpg/1280px-Agsphoto_11239_full.jpg","title":"A famine victim in Henan, 1943","desc":"A man crazed with hunger gnaws the bark of a tree during the Henan famine, photographed by Harrison Forman, who travelled to the province with Theodore White in early 1943.","credit":"Photograph by Harrison Forman, American Geographical Society Library, University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agsphoto_11239_full.jpg","alt":"A thin man in padded clothes crouches on stone paving with his face pressed against the trunk of a tree."},"wiki":"Chinese famine of 1942–1943"},
    "ww2-429": {"questions":["Fang Xianjue's 10th Army held Hengyang until 8 August 1944, long after observers in Chungking had written it off, during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Japanese drive through Hunan and Guangxi.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> overran his bases at Guilin and Liuzhou in 1944, Chennault's air force flew on from fields east of the Japanese corridor supplied across enemy lines."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Ichi-Go</b> was the Japanese army's great offensive of 1944 in China, launched to take the railways running from north China towards Indochina and to destroy the American air bases near them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Imperial General Headquarters, aware that American B-29s could bomb Japan from Guilin and Liuzhou, ordered the China Expeditionary Army on 24 January 1944 to destroy the important enemy bases in south-west China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its aims were the airfields, a land route to the Southern Army through French Indochina at a time when Japan's sea routes were insecure, and the overthrow of the Chungking government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Imperial headquarters considered mobilising 400,000 to 500,000 men, and the American air history judged the summer drive larger by at least 100,000 than any offensive force Japan had used in China before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Henan, where the campaign opened in April, the Chinese offered practically no resistance outside a few fortified places, and fleeing troops were often attacked and disarmed by peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The defeats spread gloom in Chungking, where the American ambassador reported in June that officials doubted their armies could stand and expected Guilin, Kunming and Chongqing to be threatened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Foreign observers expected Hengyang to be given up at once, yet it held until 8 August, when the 10th Army commander Fang Xianjue and four division commanders surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 20 July the Japanese counted 19,286 of their own men killed, wounded or sick in the drive through Hunan, and kept no record of losses after that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eastern airfields that had been the key to Claire Chennault's success were isolated and overrun, and his air force fought on from fields east of the Japanese corridor supplied by transports flying across enemy lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In December a Japanese division pushed into Guizhou and took Tushan, on the road to Guiyang, and the offensive had by then captured more than 10,000 tons of Chinese munitions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Japanese Research Division, Office of the Military History Officer, Headquarters, United States Army Forces Far East, <i>Army Operations in China, January 1944–August 1945</i>, Japanese Monograph No. 72 (Tokyo, 1956), 16–18, 89–90, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-72/JM-72.PDF. [Open access]","Yung-fa Chen, “Chiang Kai-shek and the Japanese Ichigo Offensive, 1944,” in <i>Chiang Kai-shek and His Time: New Historical and Historiographical Perspectives</i>, ed. Laura De Giorgi and Guido Samarani (Venice: Edizioni Ca' Foscari, 2017), 38, 46, https://edizionicafoscari.unive.it/media/pdf/books/978-88-6969-127-0/978-88-6969-127-0-ch-02.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, “Foreword,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/index.html. [Open access]","Herbert Weaver, “Fourteenth Air Force Operations, January 1943–June 1944,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 541–45, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-16.html. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 15 June 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 6, <i>China</i>, doc. 93, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d93. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 27 June 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 6, <i>China</i>, doc. 99, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d99. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Time Runs Out in CBI</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1959), 142, 170–71, 175, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Time/USA-CBI-Time-5.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan need a land route through China in 1944?","a":"Because its sea communications with the south were no longer safe. A railway corridor from north China to French Indochina would link Japan's armies in China with the Southern Army by land, and the drives of 1944 gave it exactly that line of communication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Chinese armies in Henan collapse so quickly?","a":"Because they were in no state to fight and had alienated the people around them. Except in a few fortified places they offered practically no resistance, and the American ambassador reported that peasants long repressed by the troops had turned on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the offensive matter to American strategy?","a":"Because it took away the bases from which American aircraft struck Japan's shipping and threatened Japan itself. Tokyo ordered the campaign knowing that B-29s could reach the home islands from Guilin and Liuzhou, and the drives overran the eastern fields on which Chennault's hopes of a decisive air campaign had rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Evacuaci%C3%B3nDeLiuchowNoviembre1944.jpg/1280px-Evacuaci%C3%B3nDeLiuchowNoviembre1944.jpg","title":"Evacuating Liuchow, November 1944","desc":"Soldiers, civilians, carts and lorries crowd a road out of Liuchow, now Liuzhou, as the Japanese advance closed in on the city and its American air base in November 1944.","credit":"U.S. Army photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evacuaci%C3%B3nDeLiuchowNoviembre1944.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd of people in wide hats with carts, bundles and lorries packed along a road between bare trees."},"wiki":"Operation Ichi-Go"},
    "ww2-430": {"questions":["At Cairo in 1943 the Chinese insisted on an official rate of 20 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the American dollar, while black-market dealers in Chungking were paying 240.","In 1944 the Americans proposed 100 and the Chinese held out for 30 in talks over how many <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each dollar should buy to pay for American bases in China."],"abstract":"The <b>fabi</b> was the paper legal tender of the Nationalist government, created by a decree of 3 November 1935 that made the notes of three government banks the only lawful money and took silver out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Holders of silver dollars and bullion had to exchange them for the notes, and anyone found illegally holding silver could be punished under the law on treason against the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1937 war spending pushed the currency down, and in 1940 it fell so fast that a stabilisation fund of 5 million pounds was used up within months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second fund backed by American and British credit failed in 1941, forcing Chiang to centralise the land tax at the cost of strained relations with the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In February 1942 Chiang thanked Roosevelt for a loan of 500 million dollars, saying it would serve military needs and also redeem legal tender notes, control the currency and stabilise prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> By mid-1943 the American adviser to the finance ministry reckoned that retail prices in Chungking stood near 100 times their level before 1937 and were rising 7 or 8 per cent a month, with Kunming nearer 200 times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He expected prices to double every 8 to 10 months if the trend held, and warned that undernourished troops and officers drawn into trade were undermining the war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The exchange rate became a quarrel between allies, for at Cairo the Chinese insisted on an official rate of 20 to the American dollar when the black market paid 240.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American soldiers and then civilian agencies took to selling dollars on the black market, and in 1944 the governments haggled over a rate for army spending, the Americans proposing 100 to 1 and the Chinese 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Meanwhile the Chinese advanced 1,000 million in their currency every month for American needs, while American forces, cut off by the blockade, bought their food and building materials locally as prices outran the exchange rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Edwin S. Cunningham to the Secretary of State, Shanghai, 4 November 1935, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1935</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 613, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1935v03/d613. [Open access]","Yung-fa Chen, “Chiang Kai-shek and the Japanese Ichigo Offensive, 1944,” in <i>Chiang Kai-shek and His Time: New Historical and Historiographical Perspectives</i>, ed. Laura De Giorgi and Guido Samarani (Venice: Edizioni Ca' Foscari, 2017), 37, https://edizionicafoscari.unive.it/media/pdf/books/978-88-6969-127-0/978-88-6969-127-0-ch-02.pdf. [Open access]","George Atcheson Jr. to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 31 August 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1943, China</i>, doc. 369, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943China/d369. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Command Problems</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1956), 77, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Command/USA-CBI-Command-2.html. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 28 March 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 6, <i>China</i>, doc. 669, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d669. [Open access]","Clarence E. Gauss to the Secretary of State, Chungking, 11 January 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 6, <i>China</i>, doc. 612, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v06/d612. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Time Runs Out in CBI</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1959), 12, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Time/USA-CBI-Time-1.html. [Open access]","T. V. Soong to Cordell Hull, 16 February 1942, enclosing Chiang Kai-shek to Franklin D. Roosevelt, 15 February 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942, China</i>, doc. 354, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942China/d354. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did runaway inflation not bring Free China down at once?","a":"Because most Chinese lived off the land rather than on wages. American and British observers agreed that inflation alone would not cause an early collapse, since the great majority of the population was agricultural, and judged that the outlook depended more on the crops than on anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the exchange rate matter to the American war effort?","a":"Because almost everything the Americans used in China apart from fuel and munitions had to be bought there in Chinese money. At the official rate every project, from airfields to food for the troops, cost many times its real value, which made the monetary cost of operating in China a strategic problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Chiang resist changing the official rate?","a":"Because a public devaluation would have meant a loss of face that could not be forced without a showdown. American negotiators in 1944 therefore proposed paying a lump sum in dollars every 3 months with no mention of a rate at all, so that the Generalissimo's face would be saved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/1000_Yuan%2C_Central_Bank_of_China_%281945%29_07.jpg","title":"A 1,000-yuan note of the Central Bank of China, 1945","desc":"A thousand-yuan note of the Central Bank of China dated 1945 and bearing the portrait of Sun Yat-sen, one of the large denominations issued late in the war.","credit":"Central Bank of China, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1000_Yuan,_Central_Bank_of_China_(1945)_07.jpg","alt":"A purple banknote with a portrait of a moustached man in the centre, ornate rosettes, Chinese characters and the serial number 024742."},"wiki":"Chinese National Currency"},
    "ww2-451": {"questions":["Roosevelt told Americans in February 1942 that the plan had always been to retire into the peninsula, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted until Edward King surrendered his 78,000 men.","A Japanese campaign meant to take about 50 days took 5 months, and its longest phase was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the jungle peninsula west of Manila Bay."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Bataan</b> was the siege of the American and Filipino army that withdrew into the Bataan peninsula, across the bay from Manila, after the Japanese invaded Luzon in December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roosevelt told Americans in February 1942 that for 40 years the plan for a full-scale Japanese attack had been a delaying action ending in a slow retirement into Bataan and Corregidor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese 14th Army's first offensive against the peninsula failed, and between January and early March it lost almost 7,000 men killed and wounded, with 10,000 to 12,000 more down with disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its commander, Masaharu Homma, said at his trial after the war that at that moment the defenders could have walked to Manila without meeting much resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Japanese had expected the whole Philippine campaign to take about 50 days, and it took 5 months and some 192,000 army and navy personnel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The defenders' worst enemy was hunger, since soldiers who needed about 4,000 calories a day received roughly 2,000 in January, 1,500 in February and 1,000 in March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 2,800 carabao, the water buffalo of Philippine farms, were slaughtered for meat, and malaria spread quickly once the quinine ran low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the end of March a surgeon found signs of malnutrition in 65 per cent of one Philippine Scout regiment, and the final Japanese offensive opened on 3 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 9 April Major General Edward King surrendered his 78,000 men unconditionally, having asked in vain that they be treated as prisoners of war under the Geneva Convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The surgeon of the Luzon Force called it a defeat due to disease and starvation rather than to military conditions, and on 28 April Roosevelt told the nation that it paid tribute to the men who had held out so long on Bataan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["General Staff of General MacArthur, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, CMH Pub 13-1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint 1994), 103–19, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” 23 February 1942, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-6. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>The Fall of the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. 19, 347–52, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-PI/USA-P-PI-19.html. [Open access]","Mary Ellen Condon-Rall and Albert E. Cowdrey, <i>Medical Service in the War Against Japan</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1998), 34–36, https://archive.org/details/MedicalServiceInTheWarAgainstJapan. [Open access]","Alvin P. Stauffer, <i>The Quartermaster Corps: Operations in the War Against Japan</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1956), 31–32, https://archive.org/details/quartermastercor00stau_1. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>The Fall of the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. 26, 454–67, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-PI/USA-P-PI-26.html. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” 28 April 1942, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the defenders retreat into Bataan instead of fighting for Manila?","a":"The prewar plan for a full Japanese attack was a delaying action ending in a slow retirement into Bataan and Corregidor, holding the mouth of Manila Bay while the United States built up the strength to win a war of attrition against Japan itself."},{"q":"Why did hunger decide the battle more than the fighting did?","a":"The peninsula's stocks could not be replaced through the blockade, so the daily ration fell to about a quarter of what fighting men needed; malnutrition, malaria and dysentery wore the army down until it could not hold a line when the final offensive came."},{"q":"Why did the long defence matter to Japan?","a":"A campaign planned for about 50 days took 5 months, and the 14th Army suffered thousands of battle and sickness casualties, so Japan had to commit about 192,000 army and navy personnel, considerably more than its initial allotment."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/American-and-Filipino-soldiers-in-action-on-Bataan-Peninsula-142355137601.jpg","title":"Defenders on Bataan, 1942","desc":"Members of an American and Filipino anti-tank company behind a camouflaged barricade on the Bataan peninsula during the siege of early 1942.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American-and-Filipino-soldiers-in-action-on-Bataan-Peninsula-142355137601.jpg","alt":"Two soldiers in steel helmets crouch in a shallow trench behind a heap of cut branches, a third aims a rifle from the right."},"wiki":"Battle of Bataan"},
    "ww2-452": {"questions":["The Tokyo tribunal put the deaths at about 8,000, while an American army medical history counts some 650 Americans and 5,000 to 10,000 Filipinos lost on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in April 1942.","In February 1946 the Supreme Court declined to intervene in the trial of Masaharu Homma, whose 14th Army had driven the prisoners on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, over two dissents."],"abstract":"The <b>Bataan Death March</b> was the forced movement on foot of the American and Filipino soldiers who surrendered on the Bataan peninsula in April 1942, from its southern tip north to San Fernando in Pampanga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribunal in Tokyo found that the prisoners marched in intense heat for 9 days along a highway it measured at 120 kilometres (75 miles), with little or no food or water for the first 5 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who fell out were shot or bayoneted, and at San Fernando the survivors were crammed into railway freight cars, where many died, for Camp O'Donnell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American army medical history adds that the boxcars carried them about 40 kilometres (25 miles), after which they walked the last 6 kilometres (3.5 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 9,000 Americans and 45,000 Filipinos reached O'Donnell, a half-built training camp, and the tribunal found that at least 27,500 prisoners died there between April and December 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The men had lived for weeks on less than half the normal ration, and the quartermasters spared about 38,000 litres (10,000 gallons) of petrol in the hope that it would move the prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> General King had also saved vehicles for the sick and exhausted, but the Japanese would not discuss terms or the use of the trucks, and in all about 83,000 men were taken prisoner on Bataan and Corregidor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How many died on the march is disputed: the tribunal put the deaths at approximately 8,000 Americans and Filipinos, while the army medical history counts about 650 Americans and between 5,000 and 10,000 Filipinos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked about the mistreatment of American prisoners in January 1944, Secretary of State Cordell Hull said that all the demons available would be needed to describe the tortures inflicted on Americans and Filipinos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Masaharu Homma, whose 14th Army held the prisoners, was tried by an American military commission in Manila, and two Supreme Court justices dissented over its haste when the court declined to intervene in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1043–46, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>The Fall of the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. 26, 454–67, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-PI/USA-P-PI-26.html. [Open access]","Mary Ellen Condon-Rall and Albert E. Cowdrey, <i>Medical Service in the War Against Japan</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1998), 365, https://archive.org/details/MedicalServiceInTheWarAgainstJapan. [Open access]","Alvin P. Stauffer, <i>The Quartermaster Corps: Operations in the War Against Japan</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1956), 31–32, https://archive.org/details/quartermastercor00stau_1. [Open access]","General Staff of General MacArthur, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, CMH Pub 13-1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint 1994), 119, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Japanese Atrocities: Statement by the Secretary of State,” <i>Department of State Bulletin</i> 10, no. 240 (29 January 1944): 115, https://archive.org/details/sim_department-of-state-bulletin_1944-01-29_10_240. [Open access]","Supreme Court of the United States, <i>In re Homma</i>, 327 U.S. 759 (1946), Murphy, J., dissenting, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/327/759. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>The Fall of the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. 21, 367–89, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-PI/USA-P-PI-21.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many prisoners already dying when the march began?","a":"They had fought for 3 months on a ration that never reached even half the normal issue, with malaria and dysentery spreading through the peninsula, so thousands began the march starved and sick and could not keep the pace their guards demanded."},{"q":"Why do the death tolls for the march differ so widely?","a":"No full record was kept, and the counts rest on different evidence: the tribunal gave one approximate figure from the testimony before it, while the army's medical history gives a range built from medical reports, so each figure should be read with its source."},{"q":"Why did two justices object to Homma's trial while accepting that atrocities had happened?","a":"Frank Murphy wrote that neither clearer proof of guilt nor the atrocities of Japanese troops could excuse the undue haste of the trial or a directive admitting coerced confessions, and that hasty, revengeful action was not the American way."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Burial_detail_at_Camp_O%27Donnell_after_Bataan_Death_March.jpg","title":"Carrying the fallen, May 1942","desc":"A captured Japanese photograph of American prisoners using improvised litters to carry comrades who had collapsed from lack of food and water after the march from Bataan.","credit":"Photographer unknown, captured Japanese photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burial_detail_at_Camp_O%27Donnell_after_Bataan_Death_March.jpg","alt":"Men walk in a long file along a dusty road, several pairs carrying slumped figures in blankets slung between poles."},"wiki":"Bataan Death March"},
    "ww2-453": {"questions":["Three Japanese forces struck south through Borneo, Celebes and Sumatra before converging on Java, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was settled once the Allied striking force was destroyed in the Java Sea.","Planners in Tokyo meant to call on the islanders to declare independence and hand over the oil wells intact during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which broke the Malay Barrier by March 1942."],"abstract":"The <b>Dutch East Indies campaign</b> was the Japanese conquest of the Netherlands' Asian colony, the islands of present-day Indonesia, in the first 3 months of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan's plan was a three-pronged advance, with a western force aimed at Palembang in southern Sumatra, a central force taking the oil ports of Tarakan and Balikpapan in Borneo, and an eastern force moving through Celebes, Amboina and Timor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three were then to combine for the invasion of Java, landing near Batavia and near Sourabaya, with the land forces under the 16th Army of Lieutenant General Imamura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Allies defended the islands through a short-lived American, British, Dutch and Australian command under General Wavell, known as ABDACOM, which also took in Burma and Malaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its naval striking force met the Japanese covering the Java invasion on 27 February, and the Dutch cruisers De Ruyter and Java and the destroyers Kortenaer, Electra and Jupiter were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Oil was the prize, and Tokyo's planners had decided as early as 1940 to capture Singapore first so that the wells of the Indies would not be destroyed before they could be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> During the siege of Singapore the inhabitants of the Indies were to be called on to declare their independence, seize the oil wells and hand them over intact to the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By late February parachute troops had dropped on Palembang and its refineries, and Wavell's command was being wound up, with Wavell himself leaving for India on 25 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At nine in the morning on 8 March the Dutch commander-in-chief broadcast that organised resistance in Java would end, and that afternoon at Kalidjati the governor-general and General ter Poorten agreed to the capitulation of all troops in the Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tokyo had already allotted Java and Sumatra to army military administration and Celebes, Dutch Borneo and the eastern islands to the navy, and by 28 March the whole of Sumatra was occupied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chaps. 18, 25–26, 291–92, 415–49, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. 7, 153–80, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-7.html. [Open access]","Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>The Java Sea Campaign</i>, Combat Narratives (Washington, DC: Publication Branch, Office of Naval Intelligence, 1943), 50–81, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/USN-CN-Java/USN-CN-JavaSea-16.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VII, “The Pacific War,” 863–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 54–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. 11, 240–63, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-11.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan need the Indies so badly?","a":"Japan had gone to war chiefly for the raw materials of the southern area, above all oil, and the wells and refineries of Borneo and Sumatra were the richest within reach, so it planned the southern offensive to capture them before they could be wrecked."},{"q":"Why could the Allied fleet not stop the invasion of Java?","a":"Its ships came from four navies with little joint training and no common plan, had no air reconnaissance or fighter cover, and were losing the fuel they depended on as the oilfields fell, so the striking force fought at a disadvantage and was destroyed."},{"q":"Why did British and American troops in Java surrender with the Dutch?","a":"The Dutch commander expected all Allied forces to lay down their arms after his broadcast of 8 March; the British commanders judged that their small force had no prospect of fighting on effectively, obeyed, and signed the formal surrender at Bandoeng on 12 March."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Japanse_invasie_op_Java_TMnr_10001990.jpg","title":"Japanese troops landing on Java, 1942","desc":"Japanese soldiers haul a lorry through the shallows onto a beach during the invasion of Java in March 1942, with more trucks already ashore beneath the palms.","credit":"Photographer unknown, Wereldmuseum Amsterdam (Tropenmuseum) collection, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Japanse_invasie_op_Java_TMnr_10001990.jpg","alt":"A long line of soldiers pulling on a rope drag a truck through breaking surf towards a beach lined with palm trees."},"wiki":"Dutch East Indies campaign"},
    "ww2-454": {"questions":["Two Canadian battalions sent out for garrison duty only fought their first battle of the war in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended at the Peninsula Hotel on Christmas Day 1941.","Japanese troops bayoneted the wounded in the hospital at Saint Stephen's College during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Anthony Eden compared the atrocities to the Nanking massacre of 1937."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Hong Kong</b> was the Japanese conquest of the British colony on the south China coast, fought from 8 to 25 December 1941 against a garrison that Churchill later said had been reinforced enough to hold the island for a long time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two Canadian battalions, the Royal Rifles of Canada and the Winnipeg Grenadiers, had arrived in November and brought it to nearly 12,000 combatants, though London and Ottawa understood them to be meant for garrison duty only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the first morning 36 Japanese bombers destroyed all five Royal Air Force aircraft at Kai Tak while the Japanese infantry crossed the frontier river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The loss of the Shing Mun Redoubt made the Gin Drinkers Line untenable, the defenders were pushed off the mainland within days, and on the night of 18 December the Japanese crossed to the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill signalled on 21 December that every day of resistance helped the Allied cause, and on Christmas Day the governor, Sir Mark Young, surrendered the colony without conditions at the Peninsula Hotel in Kowloon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The British official history puts British battle casualties at about 4,440, probably too low, and the total loss with the capitulation at 11,848 combatants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese official figures were 675 killed and 2,079 wounded, though other, unofficial evidence suggested heavier losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the 1,975 Canadians sent in 1941, the Canadian War Museum counts 290 killed in action and nearly 300 more who died in captivity, and Veterans Affairs Canada puts those who never came home at more than 550.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tribunal in Tokyo found that during the capture Japanese troops entered the military hospital at Saint Stephen's College, bayoneted the sick and wounded in their beds, and raped and murdered nurses there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In March 1942 Anthony Eden told the Commons that 50 officers and men had been bound and bayoneted to death and the survivors herded into a camp without sanitation, and he called the Japanese claim to a code of chivalry a nauseating hypocrisy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. 8, 119–26, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. 9, 127–51, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]","Veterans Affairs Canada, “The Defence of Hong Kong,” https://www.veterans.gc.ca/en/remembrance/military-history/second-world-war/defence-hong-kong. [Open access]","Canadian War Museum, “The Battle for Hong Kong,” <i>Canada and the Second World War</i>, https://www.warmuseum.ca/cwm/exhibitions/chrono/1931hong_kong_e.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1036–37, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Hong Kong (Japanese Barbarities),” <i>Hansard</i>, 10 March 1942, vol. 378, cols. 930–3, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/mar/10/hong-kong-japanese-barbarities. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain reinforce a colony it could hardly hope to hold?","a":"The reinforcement was meant to deter Japan, and London was advised that a strengthened garrison could hold the island for a long time; the two Canadian battalions arrived only weeks before the attack, without their carriers and lorries and without the training they needed."},{"q":"Why was the mainland lost so quickly?","a":"The Gin Drinkers Line was thinly held, the key Shing Mun Redoubt fell within 2 days, and the Japanese showed unexpected skill at night fighting, so the commander judged the line could not be held and pulled his troops back across the harbour."},{"q":"Why did Churchill urge resistance when the defence was hopeless?","a":"His message of 21 December asked that the enemy be made to expend the utmost life and equipment, arguing that every day of resistance helped the Allied cause everywhere; the official history sets this against Japanese losses of 2,754 killed and wounded by their own count."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/1941_Hong_Kong_Battle_-_Japanese_troops_in_North_Point.png","title":"Japanese troops in North Point, 1941","desc":"Japanese infantry advancing through North Point on Hong Kong Island on 19 December 1941, the day after the landings on the island.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1941_Hong_Kong_Battle_-_Japanese_troops_in_North_Point.png","alt":"Three soldiers in helmets and heavy packs crouch and run along a street beside a wall, one gripping a rifle."},"wiki":"Battle of Hong Kong"},
    "ww2-455": {"questions":["On 8 March 1942 a Japanese regiment marched into Rangoon and found it deserted, the turning point in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that cut China off from its only seaport for Allied supplies.","Some 134 transports carried two more divisions from Singapore to Rangoon in March and April 1942, giving the 15th Army the strength to finish the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the north."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese conquest of Burma</b> was the campaign of January to May 1942 in which the 15th Army drove the British out of Burma and closed the land route that supplied China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan wanted Burma to protect the north-western flank of its new southern empire and for its oil and rice, and above all because the only route by which China could be kept supplied ran through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 15th Army entered Bangkok on 8 December 1941 and made Siam its base, and its 55th Division crossed the frontier about 20 January and took Moulmein on the 30th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army General Staff had not expected to afford the operation so early, but the southern campaigns went so well that the Southern Army was ordered in mid-January to go ahead without waiting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the Allied side Burma was placed in the joint American, British, Dutch and Australian command under Wavell, alongside Malaya and the Netherlands Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The decisive moment was Rangoon, which the 215th Regiment entered about midday on 8 March 1942 to find unoccupied and deserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its loss cut China off from the only seaport through which it could be supplied, deprived the Allies of Burma's rice, oil, timber and minerals, and left the British army fighting with its back to its former base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Japanese could now reinforce by sea, and between 19 March and 28 April some 134 transports carried the 56th and 18th Divisions from Singapore to Rangoon without loss, while a naval force occupied the Andaman Islands on 23 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Toungoo fell on 30 March and Prome during the same month, and by 29 April the 15th Army held Lashio, cutting the Burma Road, before entering Mandalay on 1 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese planners had also intended before the war to use independence movements in Burma to ease their advance, and the beaten British and Chinese armies retreated to India and towards the Chinese frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 67–69, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India’s Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), chaps. 1–2, 1–2, 21–23, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India’s Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), chap. 5, 99–100, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","Military History Section, Headquarters Army Forces Far East, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 79: Burma and Andaman Invasion Naval Operations, Mar 42 – Apr 42</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, n.d.), http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/pdfs/JM-79_Burma%20and%20Andaman%20Invasion/JM-79.htm. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VII, “The Pacific War,” 863–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. 7, 153–80, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan invade Burma at all?","a":"Burma guarded the north-western flank of the southern area Japan had just conquered, offered oil and rice, and carried the only route by which China could be supplied, so taking it tightened the blockade of the Nationalist government."},{"q":"Why did the fall of Rangoon decide the campaign?","a":"Rangoon was Burma's only real seaport and the base behind the army; once it was gone the defenders could be supplied only by a trickle of air transport, while the Japanese could bring whole divisions in by sea from Singapore."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese seize the Andaman Islands?","a":"The islands flanked the sea route to Rangoon and offered air and flying-boat bases, so holding them protected the convoys carrying the 15th Army's reinforcements and supplies."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Japanese_troops_in_Burma.jpg","title":"Japanese troops in Burma, 1942","desc":"Japanese soldiers in Burma in 1942, assembled before a colossal reclining Buddha, in a photograph taken by a Domei news agency cameraman.","credit":"Domei Tsushinsha photographer, Mainichi Newspapers, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_troops_in_Burma.jpg","alt":"A line of soldiers with packs and rifles stands at the foot of an enormous carved head of a reclining Buddha."},"wiki":"Japanese invasion of Burma","places":["city:1159151477"]},
    "ww2-456": {"questions":["Japan's army planners refused a navy demand to invade the port, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran to 64 raids by November 1943, was never the prelude to a landing.","Australia's wartime inquiry counted 243 dead and the War Memorial now gives 252, a gap that shows how hard it is to fix the toll of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1942."],"abstract":"The <b>Bombing of Darwin</b> was the series of Japanese air raids on the port in Australia's Northern Territory that began with two heavy attacks on 19 February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darwin had been built up as a base for the defence of the Dutch East Indies, and on the morning of the raid its harbour was crowded with a convoy that had just turned back from Timor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first wave came from Vice Admiral Nagumo's four fleet carriers, which flew off 152 bombers and torpedo bombers and 36 fighters, and a second raid by land-based bombers from Kendari and Ambon hit the airfield before midday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States Navy's narrative records that the destroyer Peary was hit five times and sank with all but 52 of her crew, while the town was strafed and set alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history counts 11 ships lost and 23 aircraft destroyed, and the town was evacuated by nightfall in fear of an invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The death toll has never been settled: the inquiry under Mr Justice Lowe listed 228 dead in Darwin and 15 more nearby, the air force official history gave 238, and the Australian War Memorial now gives 252.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been called 'Australia's Pearl Harbor', and with reason, for the carriers, their escorts, the commanding admiral, the air attack leader and the chief planner were the same as at Oahu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The raid was not the prelude to invasion that many feared, because the Japanese army refused a navy demand to take Port Darwin, judging that operations against Australia could not be supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United States Navy, which had been using Darwin as a base, abandoned it after the attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The War Memorial counts 64 raids on Darwin, the last on 12 November 1943, among 97 air attacks on northern Australia, which tied down anti-aircraft guns and air units that would otherwise have gone forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Australian War Memorial, “Bombing of Darwin,” https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/E84294. [Open access]","Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>The Java Sea Campaign</i>, Combat Narratives (Washington, DC: Publication Branch, Office of Naval Intelligence, 1943), 36–40, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/USN-CN-Java/USN-CN-JavaSea-11.html. [Open access]","John C. Hartley, <i>The Japanese Attack on Darwin, 19 February 1942: A Case Study in Surprise at the Operational Level</i> (Carlisle Barracks, PA: US Army War College, 1988), 1–3, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA193977. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 425–26, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]","General Staff of General MacArthur, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, CMH Pub 13-1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint 1994), 131–32, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 82–83, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. 9, 198–224, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-9.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Darwin caught by surprise?","a":"Its defences were thin and the carrier aircraft arrived without warning from an unexpected direction, catching ten American fighters as they refuelled or circled the harbour, so the attackers could bomb ships and docks almost unopposed."},{"q":"Why did Japan bomb Darwin rather than invade it?","a":"The Japanese navy wanted the port taken because the Americans could use it against New Guinea and the Bismarcks, but the army refused, judging that operations against Australia could not be supplied with the shipping and divisions available; bombing denied the base without a landing."},{"q":"Why do the figures for the dead differ?","a":"The inquiry of 1942 counted deaths in Darwin separately from those on ships and an aircraft nearby, the air force history broke its total down by place, and the War Memorial's figure counts service personnel and civilians together, so each total depends on who is included."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Darwin_raid_%28AWM_044603%29.jpg","title":"Darwin Harbour under attack, 19 February 1942","desc":"An aerial view of ships burning in Darwin Harbour, photographed by the Japanese task force during the first raid.","credit":"Japanese photographer, Australian War Memorial 044603, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darwin_raid_%28AWM_044603%29.jpg","alt":"A grainy aerial view of a coastline with columns of dark smoke drifting across the water and shore."},"wiki":"Bombing of Darwin"},
    "ww2-457": {"questions":["Japan's planners drew a new line from western New Guinea through the Carolines to the Marianas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, leaving Rabaul and the Solomons as an outpost line outside it.","Some 300,000 Japanese soldiers in the Southeast Area were left to fight delaying actions once the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pulled the main line back to the Banda Sea and the Marianas."],"abstract":"The <b>Absolute National Defence Sphere</b> was the reduced perimeter that Japan's leaders resolved in September 1943 to hold at all costs, accepting that the conquests of 1942 could no longer all be defended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Outline of the Future War Direction Policy, adopted at the Imperial Conference of 30 September, named the areas to be held as the Kuriles, the Bonins, the central and western Inner South Seas, and western New Guinea, the Sunda Islands and Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same document ordered that sea communications within the sphere be safeguarded throughout the war and that a strategic posture to meet the Allied offensive be ready by about the middle of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The perimeter ran from western New Guinea through the Carolines to the Marianas, to be strongly manned and fortified as a bastion behind which forces could gather for counterblows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The army section of Imperial General Headquarters had pressed for such a line for some time and was ready to leave Rabaul, by then almost in the front line, outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The decision rested on a sober estimate: the planners put Allied front-line strength facing Japan at about 2,400 aircraft and 23 divisions, with some 6,000 aircraft and 70 to 80 divisions in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan's own weaknesses were aircraft and shipping, for 445 vessels of about 1,754,000 tons had been sunk in less than 2 years and the 55,000 aircraft wanted for 1944 were far beyond production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A British official history adds that the planners saw no hope of holding the existing front in the South-East Pacific, given the disparity between Japanese and American resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Operations outside the line were henceforth to be delaying actions, and some 300,000 men were eventually isolated at Rabaul and elsewhere in the Southeast Area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Army officers later complained that the navy weakened the Marianas and Carolines by sending reinforcements forward to the Gilberts and Marshalls, so that the new policy was never properly carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. 27, 543–58, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-27.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), app. Q, 655–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-Q.html. [Open access]","General Staff of General MacArthur, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1, CMH Pub 13-1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966; facsimile reprint 1994), 225–26, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 140–46, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan shorten its perimeter in 1943?","a":"Defeats at Midway, Guadalcanal and in Papua, and heavy losses of ships and pilots, had left too little strength to hold the outer line in the Solomons and New Guinea, so the planners chose to trade space for time and build up aircraft behind a shorter line."},{"q":"Why was Rabaul left outside the line?","a":"It was by then almost in the front line, with Allied air power dominant around it, so the army preferred to draw the perimeter west of it and use its garrison to delay the Allies as an outpost rather than reinforce it as the main bastion."},{"q":"Why did the policy fail in practice?","a":"It depended on aircraft and shipping that Japan could not produce in time, and many reinforcements meant for the Marianas and Carolines were sunk by submarines or sent forward to the Marshalls and Gilberts, weakening the very line that was to be held."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Japan%27s_military_expansion%2C_1943.jpg","title":"Japan's empire, February 1943","desc":"An American newspaper map published on 14 February 1943, showing Japan and its conquests in red across East Asia and the western Pacific at the time the tide was turning.","credit":"Map by William Wisner, <i>Chicago Sunday Tribune</i>, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan%27s_military_expansion%2C_1943.jpg","alt":"A coloured newspaper map of Asia and the Pacific with Japanese-held territory in red and a dashed line around the empire."}},
    "ww2-458": {"questions":["Printed in dollars for Malaya and Borneo, guilders for the Indies and pesos for the Philippines, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let Japan's occupying armies pay their way on the spot.","After the surrender each liberated territory had to deal separately with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the inflation that had stripped much of its value, before its economy could recover."],"abstract":"<b>Japanese invasion money</b> was the military currency that Japan printed before the Pacific War for the territories its armies expected to occupy in the south, and issued there during the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal in Tokyo found that the War and Finance Ministries began preparing it as early as January 1941, and that the notes were deposited with the Bank of Japan to be drawn by the army as it occupied enemy territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made in dollars for Malaya, Borneo and Thailand, in guilders for the Netherlands East Indies and in pesos for the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cabinet Printing Bureau was still producing occupation currency in September 1941, while negotiations with the United States went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judges treated this as evidence that both ministries already contemplated Japanese armies occupying those territories long before the war began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The money served an occupation policy built on self-sufficiency, for military administrations were to restore order, take rapid control of resources vital to defence and secure the self-sustenance of the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Resources the forces needed locally were as a rule to be allocated on the spot, while key defence materials were to be fed into the materials plan of Imperial General Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From November 1942 a Ministry of Greater East Asia oversaw political affairs in the occupied areas, and in 1943 Tokyo resolved to win the confidence of the peoples of Greater East Asia and guide them into supporting its war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Keeping political and economic control of the major countries of the Co-Prosperity Sphere, whose raw materials were essential to Japan's war effort, was among the tasks the high command set its forces in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the surrender inflation had robbed the occupation money and the surviving pre-war currencies of much of their value, and in Malaya the rising cost of living had undermined the whole structure of wages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VII, “The Pacific War,” 877–80, 943–44, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1112–14, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 54–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 241, 279, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), app. Q, 655–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-Q.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan print occupation money before the war began?","a":"Its armies were meant to live off the territories they took, paying for local labour, food and materials on the spot, so currency for each target area had to be ready when the troops landed."},{"q":"Why did the occupation currencies lose their value?","a":"Food and consumer goods ran short across the occupied territories while the money in circulation kept growing, so it bought less and less; by 1945 both the Japanese issues and the old currencies still in use had lost much of their value."},{"q":"Why was the post-war clean-up handled territory by territory?","a":"Japan had issued special currencies in some places while old ones circulated in others, so each territory needed its own arrangements to restore enough stability for recovery, and Allied personnel had to be protected from being paid in devalued money."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Japanese_One_Gulden_note-_Occupation_currency_Dutch_East_Indies-_now_Indonesia.jpg","title":"A Japanese one-gulden note","desc":"A one-gulden note issued by the Japanese government for the occupied Netherlands East Indies, inscribed in Dutch, photographed at the US Army Museum of Hawaii.","credit":"Photograph by Mak, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_One_Gulden_note-_Occupation_currency_Dutch_East_Indies-_now_Indonesia.jpg","alt":"An orange-brown banknote with the words De Japansche Regeering and Een Gulden between two large numeral ones and fruit motifs."},"wiki":"Japanese invasion money"},
    "ww2-459": {"questions":["Burma had declared independence in August 1943 and the Philippines in October, so both new governments could send their heads to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Tokyo that November.","Washington called José Laurel's government in Manila a puppet, but Laurel sat beside Tojo at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which presented Japan's empire as a partnership of Asian nations."],"abstract":"The <b>Greater East Asia Conference</b> was the meeting in Tokyo on 5 and 6 November 1943 at which Japan gathered the leaders of the governments it had created or allied with across Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan's own record lists those attending as the leaders of Japan, Manchukuo, the Nanking government of China, the Philippines, Thailand and Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan's planners had long meant to use Asian nationalism as a weapon, and in 1940 they decided that independence movements would be used in French Indochina, Burma and Malaya to ease their advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the occupation plan of 1941 told the armies to win the confidence of local people precisely so as to prevent untimely independence movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The balance shifted as the war turned, and on 31 May 1943 an Imperial Council approved a plan whose main point was to accelerate the independence of the Philippines and Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Burma declared independence on 1 August 1943 and signed an alliance with Japan the same day, and the Philippines declared independence on 14 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Subhas Chandra Bose, brought from Germany by submarine, set up a Provisional Government of Free India in Singapore with himself as its head and as commander of the Indian National Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war policy adopted that September promised that Japan would win the confidence of the peoples of Greater East Asia and guide them into supporting its war effort, and would help India achieve independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States treated these governments as Japanese creations, and in November 1943 the State Department referred to José Laurel as 'President' of the puppet government set up by Japan in the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A British official history notes that Japan's aim in going to war had been a Co-Prosperity Sphere under its own control, whose raw materials were essential to its war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 147–48, 365–69, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), app. Q, 655–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-Q.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VII, “The Pacific War,” 863–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India’s Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 383–84, 431–32, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Note From the Spanish Government Regarding Message of Spanish Foreign Minister to the Philippine Puppet Government,” <i>Department of State Bulletin</i> 9, no. 229 (13 November 1943), https://archive.org/details/sim_department-of-state-bulletin_1943-11-13_9_229. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan grant independence to Burma and the Philippines in 1943?","a":"As the war turned against it, Japan sought the confidence and cooperation of the occupied peoples in its war effort, and in May 1943 its leaders decided to accelerate independence; Burma and the Philippines each signed an alliance with Japan once declared independent."},{"q":"Why did the Allies dismiss the conference?","a":"The United States regarded the new governments as Japanese creations, and its State Department openly described the government in Manila as a puppet set up by Japan rather than a sovereign state that could speak for Filipinos."},{"q":"Why did Bose attend although India was not occupied?","a":"Japan backed his Provisional Government of Free India and the Indian National Army as a means of undermining British rule in India, and its war policy of 1943 pledged to help India achieve independence."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Subhas_Chandra_1943_Tokyo.jpg","title":"A Tokyo rally, November 1943","desc":"Subhas Chandra Bose addressing a gathering in Tokyo during the Greater East Asia Conference in November 1943, beneath a banner proclaiming Greater East Asian unity.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Subhas_Chandra_1943_Tokyo.jpg","alt":"A speaker stands at a microphone on a raised platform under a large banner of Japanese characters, with rows of seated officials below."},"wiki":"Greater East Asia Conference"},
    "ww2-460": {"questions":["Built before the war for about 650 civilian prisoners on Singapore's eastern peninsula, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave its name to the whole camp complex where Allied soldiers were held from 1942.","A Union Jack hidden through the war in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was raised at the Singapore surrender ceremony in September 1945, after liberated prisoners had begun leaving for home."],"abstract":"<b>Changi Prison</b> was Singapore's civilian gaol on the Changi peninsula, and its name came to stand for the prisoner-of-war and internee camps that occupied some 25 square kilometres around it from 1942 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Singapore capitulated on 15 February 1942 more than 50,000 British and Australian and 35,000 Indian troops became prisoners, and 2 days later the British and Australians marched out to the Changi area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the whole Malayan campaign Britain and the Commonwealth lost 138,708 men, more than 130,000 of them as prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For most of the war the soldiers lived in the barracks of the old British base, Selarang, Roberts and Kitchener, while the gaol itself held civilians, part of some 130,000 Allied civilians the Japanese interned across Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The prisoners ran their own camp, with gardens, concert parties, a hospital and even a university, but from 1942 large working parties were sent away to Japan, Borneo and the Burma-Siam railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In September 1942 about 15,400 men who refused to sign a promise not to escape were crowded into Selarang barracks square for 3 days until their commanders agreed to sign under duress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan had told the Allies in January 1942 that, although it had not ratified the Geneva convention on prisoners, it would apply its provisions to them 'mutatis mutandis', with the necessary changes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In March 1942 Anthony Eden told the Commons that London had no information at all about its prisoners in Malaya, and the Red Cross was not recognised in the southern occupied territories until August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In May 1944 the prisoners were moved into and around the gaol, built for 650 civilian inmates, and 11,700 were crammed into less than a quarter of a square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal in Tokyo compared the fate of all British and American prisoners: 4 per cent of those held by Germany and Italy died in captivity, against 27 per cent of the 132,134 held by Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Australian War Memorial, “Changi,” Encyclopedia, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/pow/changi. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), appendix 30, 532–36, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 473, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1001–02, 1096–1101, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Civilian Internees,” Encyclopedia, https://www.awm.gov.au/encyclopedia/pow/ww2/civilian_internees. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Hong Kong (Japanese Barbarities),” <i>Hansard</i>, 10 March 1942, vol. 378, cols. 930–3, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/mar/10/hong-kong-japanese-barbarities. [Open access]","National Heritage Board, Singapore, “The Site,” Changi Chapel and Museum, https://www.heritage.sg/changichapelmuseum/about-us/the-site. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Changi remembered so darkly when it was not the worst camp?","a":"It was the starting point for most Australian and British prisoners, the place from which working parties left for the Burma-Siam railway and Borneo, and the overcrowding in and around the gaol from 1944 fixed it in memory, although camps such as those on the railway were far deadlier."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese demand promises not to escape?","a":"Japanese regulations required prisoners to swear not to escape and treated those who refused as intending to, so in 1942 thousands at Selarang were confined without proper food or sanitation until their officers signed under duress."},{"q":"Why were prisoners moved into the gaol in 1944?","a":"The Japanese were building an airfield in the Changi area and wanted the barracks round it for their air units, so the prisoners, then more than 11,000, were concentrated in a gaol built for 650 while the civilian internees were moved to Sime Road."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Allied_prisoners_of_war_after_the_liberation_of_Changi_Prison%2C_Singapore_-_c._1945.jpg","title":"Liberated prisoners in Changi Gaol, 1945","desc":"Allied prisoners of war in a corridor of Changi Prison, Singapore, after the camp was liberated in September 1945.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_prisoners_of_war_after_the_liberation_of_Changi_Prison%2C_Singapore_-_c._1945.jpg","alt":"Thin men sit and lie on bedding along a long prison corridor lined with open cell doors and hanging clothes."},"wiki":"Changi Prison"},
    "ww2-471": {"questions":["At Casablanca in January 1943 Admiral King revived the Central Pacific route of the prewar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to answer where the Allies should go once Rabaul had fallen.","Governor Leonard Wood called it a fatal error to plan on losing the Philippines, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> approved in 1924 still told the garrison to hold Manila Bay until the fleet arrived."],"abstract":"<b>War Plan Orange</b> was the American plan for a war against Japan alone, named, like the other colour plans of its day, after the code name of the probable enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its principles were settled by 1913: Japan would strike first at the Philippines, and the Battle Fleet would cross the Pacific by way of Pearl Harbor and Guam to relieve a garrison expected to hold out for 3 or 4 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Joint Board approved the first joint version in 1924, conceiving the war as offensive and essentially naval, with Japan to be isolated by cutting its sea routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army's part was to hold Manila Bay until the fleet arrived, though some planners thought the islands indefensible, and the final revision decided that the garrison would have to manage without reinforcement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japan's own prewar proposal of November 1941 mirrored the idea from the other side, expecting to intercept the American fleet as it came into the western Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The plan presumed a war with Japan alone, and by 1941 it had been folded into a global strategy that put Germany first, while Pearl Harbor sank the battleships on which an early drive into the mandated islands depended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A generation of officers had learned their trade on its assumptions, and at Casablanca in January 1943 Admiral King revived its Central Pacific route for the advance after Rabaul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Addressing the Combined Chiefs in May 1943, King recalled 30 or 40 years of studies premised on supporting or recovering the Philippines and named the Marianas as the key to the Japanese lines of communication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The strategic plan for the defeat of Japan prepared for that conference made the Central Pacific the main effort and the South and Southwest Pacific a subsidiary one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians still argue over the result, one view holding that the Navy's drive on the Marianas won the war while MacArthur's advance kept the old plan's Philippine objective alive at real cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. I, \"The Beginnings of Pacific Strategy,\" 21–44, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-1.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), part I, chap. 1, \"Early Plans for a War with Japan,\" 3–7, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-I-1.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl and Edmund G. Love, <i>Seizure of the Gilberts and Marshalls</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), chap. I, \"The Strategic Background,\" 1–17, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Gilberts/USA-P-Gilberts-1.html. [Open access]","Noriaki Yashiro, “Japanese Strategy in the Second Phase of the Pacific War: Consequences of Operation Strategies in Main Battlefield, the Pacific Ocean,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 79–112, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/07.pdf. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXII, \"The Revival of ORANGE,\" 434–453, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-22.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, May 21, 1943, 10:30 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Conferences at Washington and Quebec, 1943</i>, document 57, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943/d57. [Open access]","Phillips O’Brien, “American Strategy in the Pacific after Midway: From Parity to Supremacy,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 113–126, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/08.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the plan centre on Manila Bay rather than on Japan itself?","a":"The Philippines were the American possession Japan was expected to attack first, and Manila Bay was the only base west of Pearl Harbor big enough for the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The planners therefore made holding the bay the Army's mission, so that the fleet would find a friendly harbour when it arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How Japan would finally be beaten was left vaguer, as pressure made progressively more severe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the plan out of date by December 1941?","a":"It assumed a war between the United States and Japan alone, which no longer matched a world of Axis alliances and American support for Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The strategy that replaced it put Germany first, and the losses at Pearl Harbor removed the battleships an early advance into the mandated islands would have needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did King choose the Central Pacific route in 1943?","a":"He argued that the Philippines could be taken by a flank approach, whereas the Indies would need a costly frontal attack, and that the Marianas lay across Japan's lines of communication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The route was also shorter and familiar to naval officers from their war college studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Fort_Drum_Battery_Marshall_Firing_1935_%281%29.jpg","title":"Fort Drum in Manila Bay at gunnery practice, 1935","desc":"Battery Marshall of Fort Drum, the concrete fort built on El Fraile Island at the mouth of Manila Bay, firing during annual target practice on 6 August 1935.","credit":"U.S. Army photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Drum_Battery_Marshall_Firing_1935_%281%29.jpg","alt":"The concrete fort of Fort Drum standing in the sea during a firing exercise by one of its batteries."},"wiki":"War Plan Orange"},
    "ww2-472": {"questions":["Taking lightly held airfield sites while strong garrisons such as Rabaul and Manokwari were cut off, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1943–44 let MacArthur move his bomber line forward without frontal assaults.","Cut off from supply except by the occasional submarine, the garrisons stranded by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost their aircraft to air attack and ran out of food on islands the Japanese had expected to defend."],"abstract":"<b>Leapfrogging</b> was the Allied method in the Pacific of jumping past strong Japanese garrisons to seize weakly held ground beyond them, leaving them, in a phrase of the time, to wither on the vine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its reach was set by aircraft range: in the South and Southwest Pacific each jump went only as far as land-based fighters could cover, because MacArthur had no carriers and Halsey had them only briefly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It needed a margin of strength the Allies lacked until 1943, and the first amphibious bypass came that May, when the capture of Attu led the Japanese to abandon Kiska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That July Halsey, wary of another slugging match after Munda, proposed to bypass Kolombangara and let it die on the vine, landing instead on lightly held Vella Lavella on 15 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Central Pacific Nimitz struck at Kwajalein early in 1944 while leaving Wotje and Maloelap behind, and the later decision to bypass Truk freed seasoned troops for a sudden blow at the Marianas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The biggest prize left behind was Rabaul, which the Combined Chiefs at Quebec in August 1943 decided to neutralise rather than capture, over MacArthur's insistence that it be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> MacArthur himself described his method as massive strokes against main objectives only, the very opposite of island hopping, which he equated with slow frontal pressure and extravagant losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His forces applied it along the New Guinea coast, jumping past Hansa Bay to Hollandia in April 1944 and announcing after the Sansapor landing of 30 July that the 15,000-strong garrison of Manokwari was by-passed and useless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bypassing depended on knowing where the enemy was weak, and decrypted Japanese signals did much to show it until the fighting reached the Philippines, where MacArthur chose to assault the main islands instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> For the men left behind the method was grim, since bypassed garrisons cut off from supply lost their aircraft and ran out of food, and on some islands Japanese ate Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["John Miller Jr., <i>CARTWHEEL: The Reduction of Rabaul</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), chap. X, \"After Munda,\" 165–188, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Rabaul/USA-P-Rabaul-10.html. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>CARTWHEEL: The Reduction of Rabaul</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), chap. XII, \"The Invasion of Bougainville,\" 222–250, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Rabaul/USA-P-Rabaul-12.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, Reports of General MacArthur, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966), chap. V, \"Up From Papua,\" 100–133, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-5.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, Reports of General MacArthur, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966), chap. VI, \"The Westward Drive Along New Guinea,\" 134–165, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-6.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXIX, \"Prospects for the Future,\" 584–606, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-29.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), part III, chap. 1, \"FLINTLOCK Plans and Preparations,\" 117–141, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-III-1.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), part III, chap. 6, \"The Fruits of Victory,\" 220–230, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-III-6.html. [Open access]","John Ferris, “Politics as Strategy: The United States and the End of the Pacific War, 1944–45,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 147–166, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/10.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 10–12, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Allies not leapfrog from the start of the war?","a":"Bypassing a garrison safely needs a clear superiority in strength, which the Allies did not have until 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south each jump was also limited by the range of land-based fighters, since MacArthur had no carriers of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did MacArthur reject the term island hopping?","a":"He used it for the opposite of his own plan: pushing the enemy back island by island by direct frontal pressure, with heavy casualties and slow progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His method was to take only key points that were lightly defended and suitable for airfields, leaving the strong garrisons to be neutralised from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Rabaul neutralised rather than captured?","a":"Planners in Washington argued in 1943 that isolating Rabaul would save a great deal of force for other operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> MacArthur wanted its harbour as a naval base, but the Combined Chiefs at Quebec decided that it should be neutralised, not captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/CartwheelAreaMap.jpg/1920px-CartwheelAreaMap.jpg","title":"The CARTWHEEL area, 1943–44","desc":"A map of New Guinea, New Britain and the Solomon Islands, where the 1943–44 advance on Rabaul ended with the base bypassed and isolated, drawn for the US Army official history.","credit":"Map by F. Temple for the US Army official history, 1959, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CartwheelAreaMap.jpg","alt":"A map of eastern New Guinea, New Britain and the Solomon Islands, the area of the 1943–44 advance on Rabaul."},"wiki":"Leapfrogging (strategy)"},
    "ww2-473": {"questions":["Striding ashore near Palo on Leyte in October 1944, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told the Filipinos by portable radio that he had returned, with President Osmeña at his side.","As Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> accepted Japan's surrender in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945, his name heading those of Nimitz and eight other nations' representatives."],"abstract":"<b>Douglas MacArthur</b> was the American general who commanded in the Philippines when Japan attacked and then led the Southwest Pacific Area, the Allied theatre that took in Australia, New Guinea, the Philippines and most of the Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington judged the Philippine garrison doomed but MacArthur too valuable to lose, and on 22 February 1942 the president ordered him to Australia to take command of a new theatre there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He left Corregidor by PT boat on the night of 11 March with his family and staff, 21 people in all, and after an angry wait for proper aircraft on Mindanao reached Darwin by B-17 on the 17th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As he left he spoke the words that became famous, 'I shall return', convinced that the counteroffensive could only be organised from Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Navy would never have entrusted its fleet to an Army officer, so the directives of 30 March 1942 made him commander of the Southwest Pacific Area while Nimitz took the rest of the ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His aim never wavered from the Philippines, and in 1943 he pressed to capture Rabaul until the Combined Chiefs decided at Quebec that it would be neutralised and bypassed instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His advance along New Guinea rested on bypassing strong garrisons for weakly held bases, guided by decrypted Japanese signals, and it brought the date of the Philippine landings forward by 5 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 20 October 1944 he came ashore near Palo on Leyte behind the assault waves and announced by portable radio, with President Osmeña beside him, that he had returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Once in the Philippines he refused to bypass most of the islands and assaulted the main ones, treating their conquest as an aim rather than a means, a choice some historians now judge to have cost lives needlessly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 2 September 1945 he accepted Japan's surrender in Tokyo Bay as Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, the authority to which the instrument subjected the emperor and the Japanese government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. VIII, \"The Philippines,\" 181–197, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-8.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XI, \"Organization and Command of the Pacific,\" 240–263, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-11.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, Reports of General MacArthur, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966), chap. II, \"Establishment of the Southwest Pacific Area,\" 28–44, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-2.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, Reports of General MacArthur, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966), chap. VIII, \"The Leyte Operation,\" 196–241, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-8.html. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>CARTWHEEL: The Reduction of Rabaul</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), chap. XII, \"The Invasion of Bougainville,\" 222–250, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Rabaul/USA-P-Rabaul-12.html. [Open access]","John Ferris, “Politics as Strategy: The United States and the End of the Pacific War, 1944–45,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 147–166, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/10.pdf. [Open access]","Phillips O’Brien, “American Strategy in the Pacific after Midway: From Parity to Supremacy,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 113–126, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/08.pdf. [Open access]","\"Instrument of Surrender, 2 September 1945,\" Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j4.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was MacArthur ordered out of the Philippines?","a":"Washington believed the garrison could not be saved and that MacArthur was too valuable to lose with it, so the president ordered him to Australia to lead a new command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He accepted after his staff argued he could do more for the garrison from there, and became convinced the counteroffensive had to be built in Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Pacific split between MacArthur and Nimitz?","a":"No single commander was acceptable to both services: MacArthur had the support of the president, the Army and the Australians but not the confidence of the Navy, which would not hand its fleet to an Army officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nimitz was then far junior, so two commands were created instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does his reputation divide historians?","a":"His bypassing campaign in New Guinea was fast and economical, resting on good intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His insistence on retaking the main Philippine islands, though, has been criticised as costly and secondary to the Navy's drive on the Marianas, which some argue did more to defeat Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/MacArthur_and_Sutherland_s265357.jpg/1920px-MacArthur_and_Sutherland_s265357.jpg","title":"MacArthur in the Corregidor tunnel, March 1942","desc":"General Douglas MacArthur with his chief of staff, Major General Richard Sutherland, in the headquarters tunnel on Corregidor on 1 March 1942, ten days before he left for Australia.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacArthur_and_Sutherland_s265357.jpg","alt":"General MacArthur with his chief of staff in the headquarters tunnel on Corregidor."},"wiki":"Douglas MacArthur"},
    "ww2-474": {"questions":["Answering questions put at the Nuremberg tribunal's request in 1946, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stated that the Chief of Naval Operations had ordered unrestricted submarine warfare against Japan on 7 December 1941.","A former submarine officer who had forecast in 1912 that the torpedo-armed submarine would help decide fleet actions, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later credited the submarines with holding the line while the fleet recovered."],"abstract":"<b>Chester W. Nimitz</b> was the American admiral who commanded the Pacific Fleet from December 1941 to the end of the war, and was Chief of Naval Operations by 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A rear admiral when Pearl Harbor was attacked, he was jumped two grades and appointed on 17 December to replace Kimmel, who was relieved while the disaster was investigated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Pacific was divided on 30 March 1942 he became Commander in Chief, Pacific Ocean Areas, an ocean command larger in extent than MacArthur's, holding the Central and North Pacific directly and the South Pacific through an admiral under him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a letter to King in December 1942 he summed up the lessons of the first year and was already raising the idea of bypassing enemy positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under his direction amphibious forces advanced almost 3,700 kilometres (2,000 nautical miles) between November 1943 and August 1944, from Tarawa to within aerial striking distance of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A submarine commander in his youth, he had written as a lieutenant in 1912 that the improving torpedo and submarine would play a large part in deciding fleet actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1946, answering questions put at the Nuremberg tribunal's request for Dönitz's defence, he stated that the Chief of Naval Operations had ordered unrestricted submarine warfare against Japan on 7 December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He said that in the declared zone American submarines had attacked merchant ships without warning from that day, sparing only hospital ships and vessels on safe-conduct voyages for humanitarian purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the war he wrote that it was the submarines that had held the lines while the fleet replaced its losses, and Truman's citation of October 1945 credited him with Saipan, Leyte Gulf, Iwo Jima and Okinawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 2 September 1945 he signed the instrument of surrender in Tokyo Bay as the United States representative, beneath MacArthur as Supreme Commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 2 July 1946, interrogatory of Fleet Admiral Chester W. Nimitz, exhibit Doenitz-100, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-02-46.asp. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. VI, \"The First Weeks of War, 7–26 December,\" 131–153, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-6.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XI, \"Organization and Command of the Pacific,\" 240–263, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-11.html. [Open access]","Phillips O’Brien, “American Strategy in the Pacific after Midway: From Parity to Supremacy,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 113–126, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/08.pdf. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), part I, chap. 1, \"Early Plans for a War with Japan,\" 3–7, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-I-1.html. [Open access]","Naval History Division, <i>United States Submarine Losses, World War II</i>, reissue with an appendix of Axis submarine losses (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1963), dedication, foreword and preface, iii–iv, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/SubLosses/index.html. [Open access]","\"Instrument of Surrender, 2 September 1945,\" Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j4.asp. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, \"Citation Accompanying the Distinguished Service Medal Presented to Admiral Chester W. Nimitz,\" 5 October 1945, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/node/230676. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a rear admiral given the Pacific Fleet?","a":"After Pearl Harbor the Navy relieved Kimmel so that the officers responsible for the base's future security would not also be caught up in the investigation, and Nimitz was jumped two grades to replace him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was then still junior and not yet famous, which also shaped how the Pacific commands were divided in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his Nuremberg answers matter to Dönitz?","a":"Dönitz was charged with breaking the rules of submarine warfare, and Nimitz confirmed that American submarines had attacked Japanese merchant ships without warning from the first day of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal cited those answers when it decided not to base Dönitz's sentence on his breaches of the submarine rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he credit the submarines so highly?","a":"In the first months of the war the surface fleet was rebuilding after Pearl Harbor, and the submarines were the force that kept attacking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He had served in submarines himself and had foreseen in 1912 how dangerous an offensive weapon they would become.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/Admiral_Chester_W._Nimitz%2C_USN%2C_signs_the_Japanese_surrender_document_aboard_USS_Missouri_%28BB-63%29_in_Tokyo_Bay_on_2_September_1945.jpg","title":"Nimitz signs the surrender, Tokyo Bay, 2 September 1945","desc":"Fleet Admiral Chester W. Nimitz signing the Japanese instrument of surrender for the United States aboard the battleship USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Admiral_Chester_W._Nimitz%2C_USN%2C_signs_the_Japanese_surrender_document_aboard_USS_Missouri_%28BB-63%29_in_Tokyo_Bay_on_2_September_1945.jpg","alt":"Fleet Admiral Nimitz seated at a table signing a document, with officers in uniform around him."},"wiki":"Chester W. Nimitz"},
    "ww2-475": {"questions":["Off Guadalcanal between August and November 1942 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost two fleet carriers, four heavy cruisers and 12 destroyers in the hardest naval fighting of its war.","Truman's citation of October 1945 credited the commander of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Saipan, Leyte Gulf, Iwo Jima and Okinawa and with placing its ships in Tokyo Bay for the surrender."],"abstract":"The <b>United States Pacific Fleet</b> was the American navy's main force in the war against Japan, and when the Japanese struck Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941 all of it except its three carriers and their escort was in port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its commander, Admiral Kimmel, was relieved on 17 December and replaced by Nimitz, who held the command until May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On taking office at the end of December, Admiral King gave the fleet two missions: first to hold the Hawaii–Midway line and the communications with the Pacific coast, then the line of communications to Australia and New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its carriers, absent from Pearl Harbor during the attack, raided Japanese-held islands early in 1942, and its submarines sank 300,000 tons of Japanese shipping in the first 4 months of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> King later likened the naval war to a boxing match in four phases, running from the defensive to the offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The hardest fighting came early: between 8 August and 15 November 1942 the navy lost off Guadalcanal two of the five fleet carriers it lost in the whole war, four of its seven heavy cruisers and 12 destroyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not until the Essex-class carrier Bunker Hill was completed in May 1943 had it made up those carrier losses, and not until January 1944 did it have one more fleet carrier in service than on the day of Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the end naval aircraft and submarines did most of the damage: of 1.3 million tons of Japanese carriers, battleships, cruisers and destroyers sunk, the bombing survey credited about 625,000 to Navy and Marine planes and 375,000 to submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Truman's citation of October 1945 credited the fleet's commander with the invasion of Saipan, a decisive defeat of the Japanese fleet, the victory at Leyte Gulf and the assaults on Iwo Jima and Okinawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The same citation ended with the fleet's ships in Tokyo Bay for the formal capitulation of the Japanese Empire, signed there on 2 September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. VI, \"The First Weeks of War, 7–26 December,\" 131–153, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-6.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, \"Combined Chiefs of Staff Minutes, May 21, 1943, 10:30 a.m.,\" <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Conferences at Washington and Quebec, 1943</i>, document 57, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943/d57. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXII, \"The Revival of ORANGE,\" 434–453, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-22.html. [Open access]","Phillips O’Brien, “American Strategy in the Pacific after Midway: From Parity to Supremacy,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 113–126, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/08.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 2 July 1946, interrogatory of Fleet Admiral Chester W. Nimitz, exhibit Doenitz-100, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-02-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 10–12, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, \"Citation Accompanying the Distinguished Service Medal Presented to Admiral Chester W. Nimitz,\" 5 October 1945, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/node/230676. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fleet stand on the defensive in early 1942?","a":"Pearl Harbor had put the battle force out of action, and King's first orders were to hold the Hawaii–Midway line and the route to Australia rather than to advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fleet still struck where it could, with carrier raids and a submarine campaign that sank 300,000 tons of shipping in 4 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Guadalcanal so costly for the fleet?","a":"It was fighting a still powerful Japanese navy without overall superiority, and the Japanese were then better at night fighting and had better torpedoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within 3 months there were four major naval battles there, and the losses in carriers, cruisers and destroyers were the heaviest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the balance change from 1943?","a":"New Essex-class carriers and aircraft began to arrive in numbers, making up the 1942 losses by May 1943 and allowing far more ambitious operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end of the war naval aircraft and submarines had sunk most of the Japanese fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Aerail_view_of_Pearl_Harbor_on_3_May_1940.jpg/1920px-Aerail_view_of_Pearl_Harbor_on_3_May_1940.jpg","title":"The fleet at Pearl Harbor, May 1940","desc":"The US fleet at its moorings in Pearl Harbor on 3 May 1940, with eight battleships and the carrier Yorktown lying alongside Ford Island in the centre of the harbour.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerail_view_of_Pearl_Harbor_on_3_May_1940.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a harbour with an island in the middle, lines of large grey warships moored along its shore."},"wiki":"United States Pacific Fleet"},
    "ww2-476": {"questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck Truk in February 1944, sinking more than 186,000 tons of shipping, the Japanese judged the lagoon unfit as a fleet anchorage and the Combined Fleet fell back.","Lent to MacArthur for the Hollandia landings of April 1944, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added some 800 aircraft to the Allied air forces and gave the Southwest Pacific its first carrier-based close air support."],"abstract":"The <b>Fast Carrier Task Force</b> was the striking force of the Pacific Fleet's fast carriers from early 1944, sailing as Task Force 58 in several carrier task groups, each with its own screen of supporting warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built on the new Essex-class carriers: the first entered service in December 1942, and by January 1944 eight had joined the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first great operation came in the Marshalls, where four groups with 12 carriers, able to put about 700 aircraft against an estimated 130 Japanese, opened the attack on 29 January 1944, 2 days before the landings at Kwajalein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan, having lost its trained carrier air groups in 1942 and 1943, kept its own carriers out of battle until 1944 and then lost seven of them in that year's engagements without appreciable result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Further south every advance was limited by the range of land-based fighters, because MacArthur had no carriers and Halsey had them only briefly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its reach showed at Truk on 16 and 17 February 1944, when a 2-day carrier attack destroyed 26 merchant ships, six combatant ships and 270 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 186,000 tons of shipping went down, including 52,000 tons of tankers whose loss soon hampered the Japanese fleet's movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese officers later said that Truk became unsuitable as a fleet anchorage and that the Combined Fleet fell back, while Nimitz dropped a planned five-division assault, leaving the base, in a Marine history's phrase, a rattlesnake without fangs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In April the force was lent to MacArthur for the landings at Hollandia, its planes striking the airfields there on the 21st, the day before the troops went ashore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its presence added some 800 aircraft to the Allied air forces engaged and gave the Southwest Pacific its first carrier-based close air support in an amphibious landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 178–180, 191–194, 202–203, 378–380, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 10–12, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Phillips O’Brien, “American Strategy in the Pacific after Midway: From Parity to Supremacy,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, International Forum on War History 2009 (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 113–126, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/08.pdf. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Southwest Pacific Area, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, Reports of General MacArthur, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1966), chap. VI, \"The Westward Drive Along New Guinea,\" 134–165, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/RptsMacA/I/RptsI-6.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), part III, chap. 6, \"The Fruits of Victory,\" 220–230, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-III-6.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXIX, \"Prospects for the Future,\" 584–606, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-29.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the carrier force strike so far from its bases?","a":"Carrier aircraft went wherever the ships could sail, so the force was not tied to the range of land-based fighters that limited every advance in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its raids reached as far as Palau in the western Carolines and Hollandia in New Guinea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Truk raid matter so much?","a":"It sank merchant shipping and tankers that Japan could not spare, and the loss of tankers soon cut the Japanese navy's supply of oil and its mobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also showed that Truk did not need to be invaded at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the force lent to MacArthur for Hollandia?","a":"Hollandia lay beyond the cover of his land-based fighters, and the carriers could protect the landings against the Japanese fleet while bombarding and supporting the troops from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their strikes on the Hollandia airfields came the day before the landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Murderers_row_at_Ulithi_Atoll_-_US_Third_fleet_carriers_at_anchor_on_8_December_1944_%2880-G-294131%29.jpg/1920px-Murderers_row_at_Ulithi_Atoll_-_US_Third_fleet_carriers_at_anchor_on_8_December_1944_%2880-G-294131%29.jpg","title":"Fast carriers at anchor in Ulithi, December 1944","desc":"Essex-class carriers of the US Third Fleet at anchor in Ulithi Atoll on 8 December 1944, during a break from operations off the Philippines; the line was nicknamed Murderers' Row.","credit":"Photograph from a USS Ticonderoga aircraft, U.S. Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murderers_row_at_Ulithi_Atoll_-_US_Third_fleet_carriers_at_anchor_on_8_December_1944_%2880-G-294131%29.jpg","alt":"A line of large aircraft carriers at anchor one behind another, seen from the air."},"wiki":"Fast Carrier Task Force"},
    "ww2-477": {"questions":["Partly because Nimitz swore the United States had waged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Pacific from its first day of war, the Nuremberg tribunal did not base Dönitz's sentence on submarine breaches.","American submarines sank about 4.8 million tons of Japanese merchant shipping in their campaign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, at a cost of 52 boats and some 3,500 officers and men."],"abstract":"<b>Unrestricted submarine warfare</b> is the sinking of merchant ships without warning and without providing for their crews, which the London Protocol of 1936 forbade: a submarine that could not rescue survivors was to let the ship pass unharmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 7 December 1941, the day of Pearl Harbor, the American Chief of Naval Operations nevertheless ordered unrestricted submarine and air warfare against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nimitz later justified the order by the Japanese attacks of that day on American bases, ships and nationals without warning or declaration of war, and denied that any later order rested on reprisal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stated that throughout the declared Pacific theatre submarines attacked merchant ships without warning, sparing hospital ships and vessels on safe-conduct voyages, and counted fishing boats and every other non-combatant vessel as merchantmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rescue was limited: submarines did not pick up survivors when that added undue hazard, though they often handed over rubber boats or provisions and in numerous cases took prisoners by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The campaign began at once, the Pacific Fleet's submarines being in one admiral's later words the first major naval offensive of the war, and in the first 4 months they sank 300,000 tons of Japanese shipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By the postwar count of the Joint Army–Navy Assessment Committee, American submarines sank 540,192 tons of Japanese warships and 4,779,902 tons of merchant shipping, 54.6 per cent of all Japanese vessel losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bombing survey, counting ships sunk or put out of action, gave the submarines 54.7 per cent and noted that weak Japanese anti-submarine measures had helped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The price was 52 submarines with 374 officers and 3,131 men, 16 and 13 per cent of the officers and men on operations, while Japan believed at the end that it had sunk 468.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Nuremberg, Nimitz's answers, read into the record on 2 July 1946, helped persuade the tribunal not to base Dönitz's sentence on his breaches of the submarine rules, though it still found those breaches proved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 17, proceedings of 2 July 1946, interrogatory of Fleet Admiral Chester W. Nimitz, exhibit Doenitz-100, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/07-02-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, \"Judgment: Doenitz,\" <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 10–12, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Joint Army-Navy Assessment Committee, <i>Japanese Naval and Merchant Shipping Losses During World War II by All Causes</i> (Washington, DC, February 1947), appendix, \"Japanese Shipping Lost by United States Submarines,\" HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/IJN/JANAC-Losses/JANAC-Losses-6.html. [Open access]","Naval History Division, <i>United States Submarine Losses, World War II</i>, reissue with an appendix of Axis submarine losses (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1963), introduction, 1–4, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/SubLosses/SS_losses-Intro.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl and Edmund G. Love, <i>Seizure of the Gilberts and Marshalls</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), chap. I, \"The Strategic Background,\" 1–17, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Gilberts/USA-P-Gilberts-1.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXII, \"The Revival of ORANGE,\" 434–453, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-22.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States order unrestricted warfare at once?","a":"Nimitz said the order of 7 December 1941 was justified by Japan's attacks that day on American bases, ships and nationals without warning or a declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese shipping was also a target immediately within reach, since submarines could act at long range inside the Japanese perimeter from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did submarines seldom rescue survivors?","a":"Nimitz said they had little room for passengers and that survivors often refused to come aboard, so picking up many was unsafe; rubber boats or provisions were frequently given instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 1936 rules allowed no such excuse: a submarine that could not rescue was not to sink the ship at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tribunal not punish Dönitz for his submarine war?","a":"It found that he had broken the 1936 Protocol, but noted that the British had ordered ships sunk on sight in the Skagerrak and that Nimitz confirmed American unrestricted warfare in the Pacific from the first day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In view of both, it did not assess his sentence on those breaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Japanese_Cargo_Ship_Sinking.jpg","title":"The Nittsu Maru sinking, March 1943","desc":"The Japanese cargo ship Nittsu Maru sinking in the Yellow Sea on 23 March 1943, photographed through the periscope of the submarine USS Wahoo, which had torpedoed her.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Cargo_Ship_Sinking.jpg","alt":"A grainy periscope view, crossed by range markings, of a ship rearing up out of the sea amid smoke."},"wiki":"Unrestricted submarine warfare"},
    "ww2-478": {"questions":["Drawing on captured documents, prisoners' reports and Japanese shipowners' lists, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reported in 1947 that 2,346 Japanese merchant vessels of 500 tons or more had been sunk.","The bombing survey expected its own shipping figures to differ little from the final evaluation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which credited submarines with 1,113 of the merchant ships sunk by American forces."],"abstract":"The <b>Joint Army–Navy Assessment Committee</b> was the American body set up in January 1943, by agreement between the Army's Chief of Staff and the Navy's Commander in Chief, to assess enemy naval and merchant shipping losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had four Navy and three Army members, the Army Air Forces among them, and studied reports of loss or damage from all causes, taking the findings of a separate anti-submarine committee without further review.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sources ranged from prisoners' reports, captured documents and photographic intelligence to the bombing survey's reports and Japanese navy ministry and shipowners' lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its final report, dated 3 February 1947, listed every Japanese naval vessel known or believed lost and every merchant vessel of 500 gross tons or more, with assessments agreed unanimously by its members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That threshold mattered because Japan's merchant fleet was its lifeline: at the start of the war its ocean-going steel ships of over 500 tons came to some 6 million tons, nearly 4.1 million of them taken by the army and navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> By the committee's count, 2,346 Japanese merchant vessels totalling 8,618,109 tons were sunk in the war, together with 686 naval vessels of 1,965,646 tons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American forces accounted for 2,117 of the merchant ships, and submarines alone for 1,113 of them, 4,779,902 tons, against 359 credited to carrier aircraft and 241 to mines laid by Army Air Forces planes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bombing survey, publishing in July 1946 before the committee had finished, said it believed its own figures would not differ significantly from the committee's final evaluation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wartime claims could be wildly wrong: at the end of hostilities the Japanese produced figures showing 468 American submarines sunk, when only 48 had been lost in combat operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both counts also left something out, since land-based aircraft sank large numbers of barges and vessels under 500 tons that appear in neither tabulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Joint Army-Navy Assessment Committee, <i>Japanese Naval and Merchant Shipping Losses During World War II by All Causes</i> (Washington, DC, February 1947), foreword, ii, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/IJN/JANAC-Losses/index.html. [Open access]","Joint Army-Navy Assessment Committee, <i>Japanese Naval and Merchant Shipping Losses During World War II by All Causes</i> (Washington, DC, February 1947), \"Summaries of Japanese Shipping Losses,\" tables I–II, vi–vii, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/IJN/JANAC-Losses/JANAC-Losses-2.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 10–12, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Transportation Division, <i>The War Against Japanese Transportation, 1941–1945</i> (Washington, DC, May 1947), chap. 1, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/waragainstjapane54unit. [Open access]","Naval History Division, <i>United States Submarine Losses, World War II</i>, reissue with an appendix of Axis submarine losses (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1963), introduction, 1–4, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/SubLosses/SS_losses-Intro.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army and Navy set up a joint committee?","a":"Losses were reported by every service and from every cause, and the joint directive gave a single committee the task of studying and evaluating all of those reports together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members came from the Navy, the Army and the Army Air Forces, so each service's claims were weighed together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the committee need Japanese records?","a":"Claims made in wartime could be badly inflated: the Japanese, for their part, believed they had sunk 468 American submarines when 48 were lost in combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lists compiled by the Japanese navy ministry and shipowners were among the sources the committee set beside American and Allied reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do its totals understate the destruction?","a":"It counted only merchant vessels of 500 gross tons or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land-based aircraft in particular sank many barges and small craft below that size, which no tabulation of this kind includes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/80-G-28884.jpg","title":"The Heiyo Maru sinking, January 1943","desc":"The Japanese transport Heiyo Maru afire and sinking after being torpedoed by the submarine USS Whale in the central Pacific on 17 January 1943, photographed through the submarine's periscope.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:80-G-28884.jpg","alt":"A periscope view of a burning ship low in the water, with smoke rising from it."},"wiki":"Joint Army–Navy Assessment Committee"},
    "ww2-479": {"questions":["Some 34,000 American and Canadian troops landed on Kiska in August 1943 to end the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only to find that the Japanese garrison had slipped away through the fog weeks before.","Attu's garrison was almost wiped out in a last counterattack at the end of May 1943, the main battle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which the Japanese had seized two islands of Alaska."],"abstract":"The <b>Aleutian Islands campaign</b> was the fighting of June 1942 to August 1943 for the island chain that runs west from Alaska towards Japan, begun when Japanese carrier planes struck Dutch Harbor and Japanese forces seized Kiska and Attu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The northern attack was subsidiary to the thrust at Midway, and one account has Yamamoto agreeing to occupy only the two westernmost islands after the carrier disaster there, perhaps to save something from the defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Navy codebreakers had read the plan and predicted the attack for 3 June, but Admiral Theobald, commanding in the north, distrusted the intelligence as a possible ruse and placed his main force some 640 kilometres (400 miles) south of Kodiak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Command in the theatre was split between the Navy and the Army's Alaska Defense Command, and quarrels among the senior officers left Washington in grave doubt that they would cooperate well enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Weather was a second enemy, and in the critical period of the Attu operation it allowed air bombardment and support on only 9 of 20 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Kiska was the first objective in planning, but after the Americans occupied Adak on 30 August 1942 and Amchitka the following January, the first landing came on Attu on 11 May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fighting lasted almost 3 weeks and ended with a Japanese counterattack on 29 May that overran American positions before nearly all the attackers were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japanese figures later put their army dead on Attu at 2,351, and the loss of the island led them to abandon Kiska, taking its garrison out in fog on 29 July aboard two light cruisers and ten destroyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 15 August a force of 34,426 troops, 5,300 of them Canadian, landed on Kiska under combat conditions and found it empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A later Naval War College study called the campaign a lesson in how not to conduct a joint operation, 14 months long and costly because the commanders never achieved unity of command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i>, United States Cryptologic History, series IV, vol. 5 (1993; repr., Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2017), 47–54, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/priceless_advantage-nsa. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXI, \"The North Pacific and the Soviet Union,\" 419–433, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-21.html. [Open access]","Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>The Aleutians Campaign, June 1942–August 1943</i>, Combat Narratives (1945; repr., Washington, DC: Naval Historical Center, 1993), chap. 11, \"The Invasion of Attu, May 1943,\" 78–116, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/Aleutians/USN-CN-Aleutians-11.html. [Open access]","Office of Naval Intelligence, <i>The Aleutians Campaign, June 1942–August 1943</i>, Combat Narratives (1945; repr., Washington, DC: Naval Historical Center, 1993), chap. 13, \"The Invasion of Kiska, 15 August 1943,\" 123–128, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/Aleutians/USN-CN-Aleutians-13.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 77–90, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>CARTWHEEL: The Reduction of Rabaul</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), chap. X, \"After Munda,\" 165–188, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Rabaul/USA-P-Rabaul-10.html. [Open access]","Charles B. Breslin, \"World War II in the Aleutians: The Fundamentals of Joint Campaigns\" (paper, Department of Operations, Naval War College, Newport, RI, 18 June 1994), DTIC ADA279617, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA279617. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese go to the Aleutians at all?","a":"The attack was planned as a subsidiary part of the Midway operation, with a small carrier force striking Dutch Harbor while other forces seized islands to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After Midway, one account suggests, the occupation of Kiska and Attu went ahead partly to save something from the defeat and let Japanese newspapers report American territory taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the campaign so slow?","a":"Weather and distance kept aircraft grounded for much of the time, and there were few places fit for bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Command was divided between services whose commanders quarrelled, and one later study blamed the 14-month length of the campaign on that lack of unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Kiska empty when the Allies landed?","a":"After losing Attu the Japanese decided to evacuate Kiska rather than defend it, and on 29 July light cruisers and destroyers used the summer fog to slip in and take the garrison off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Allied landing on 15 August met no opposition, and patrols found signs of an evacuation 1 or 2 weeks old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Attu_landing_craft_on_beach_1943.jpg/1920px-Attu_landing_craft_on_beach_1943.jpg","title":"Landing craft at Massacre Bay, Attu, May 1943","desc":"American soldiers of the southern landing force on the beach at Massacre Bay, Attu, on 11 May 1943, with landing craft from the attack transport USS Heywood in the foreground.","credit":"U.S. Office of War Information photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attu_landing_craft_on_beach_1943.jpg","alt":"Landing craft drawn up on a beach in the foreground, with American soldiers on the shore beyond."},"wiki":"Aleutian Islands campaign"},
    "ww2-480": {"questions":["Only 146 of Betio's garrison of 4,836 were taken alive in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Marines won in 76 hours on an island too small for a beachhead.","The casualty list from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, more than 3,000 Marines killed, wounded or missing in a few days, shocked the American public at the end of 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Tarawa</b> was the American capture in November 1943 of Betio, the key island of Tarawa atoll in the Gilberts and the only one with an airfield, in the first offensive of the Central Pacific drive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The 2d Marine Division, earlier earmarked for an assault on Rabaul, was moved from New Zealand to the Central Pacific for the operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Betio bristled with guns in emplacements of steel, concrete and coconut logs, built over about 9 months by a garrison whose combat strength ran above 3,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only amphibian tractors could cross the fringing reef, and the division had about 125 of them, enough for the first three waves, after asking for 100 of the newest model and receiving 50.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Makin, attacked the same day by an Army regimental combat team, fell by the evening of the second day against a garrison estimated at 600 to 800 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> On the morning of 20 November the transports lay too far south, and the tractors fell some 40 minutes behind schedule against choppy seas, a head wind and a receding tide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bombardment lifted too long before the first wave arrived, and the Marines who followed in boats had to ride or wade ashore across the reef under fire they could not return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 76 hours the division had killed or captured the whole garrison, taking only 146 prisoners from 4,836 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marine losses were 3,301 killed, wounded and missing in the first official count of 1947 and 3,318 in the audited figures of 1952, the dead numbering about 990 in the first and 1,085 in the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The casualty list shocked the American public, though the Marines' own history judged that Betio had proved their amphibious doctrine essentially sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James R. Stockman, <i>The Battle for Tarawa</i>, Marines in World War II Historical Monograph (Washington, DC: Historical Section, Division of Public Information, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1947), \"Conclusion,\" 66–69, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Tarawa/USMC-M-Tarawa-7.html. [Open access]","James R. Stockman, <i>The Battle for Tarawa</i>, Marines in World War II Historical Monograph (Washington, DC: Historical Section, Division of Public Information, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1947), appendices B and C, \"Marine Casualties\" and \"Japanese Casualties,\" 72–73, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Tarawa/USMC-M-Tarawa-B.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl and Edmund G. Love, <i>Seizure of the Gilberts and Marshalls</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), chap. IX, \"The Capture of Tarawa,\" 127–156, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Gilberts/USA-P-Gilberts-9.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), part II, chap. 5, \"The Importance of GALVANIC,\" 103–113, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-II-5.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), appendix H, \"Marine Casualties,\" HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-H.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XXVIII, \"The Execution of Strategy: Pacific Operations, August–December 1943,\" 559–583, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-28.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 191–192, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","John Miller Jr., <i>CARTWHEEL: The Reduction of Rabaul</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), chap. XII, \"The Invasion of Bougainville,\" 222–250, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Rabaul/USA-P-Rabaul-12.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Betio so hard to take?","a":"The island was so small that there was no room for a beachhead, and every approach was covered by guns in protected emplacements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A coral reef stopped everything but amphibian tractors, and the division had too few of them for more than the first three waves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombardment not do more?","a":"The shelling and air strikes destroyed buildings and defences above ground, but the gap between the end of the bombardment and the arrival of the first wave was too long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Unexpected currents, slow boats and the depth of water at the reef caused the delay, and the defenders recovered in time to meet the following waves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the casualty figures differ?","a":"The first official history of 1947 used figures supplied that year, while the Marine Corps history of 1966 used figures audited to 1952 and notes that many who died of wounds also appear among the wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both put the total above 3,300, against a Japanese garrison almost entirely destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Tarawa_aerial_view_24_Nov_1943.jpg/1920px-Tarawa_aerial_view_24_Nov_1943.jpg","title":"Betio from the air, 24 November 1943","desc":"Betio, the main island of Tarawa atoll, seen from a Dauntless dive bomber of USS Essex flying at about 150 metres (500 feet) on 24 November 1943, looking north towards the last pocket of Japanese resistance.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarawa_aerial_view_24_Nov_1943.jpg","alt":"A low aerial view of the flat, battered island of Betio, with two anti-aircraft guns visible in the lower left."},"wiki":"Battle of Tarawa"},
    "ww2-481": {"questions":["Because the atolls fell so quickly in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Truk was left to wither, its earmarked divisions went to Saipan instead, and the Marianas landing came months earlier than planned.","An amphibious headquarters ship, rocket-firing landing craft and underwater demolition teams all proved themselves in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which set the pattern for the later island landings."],"abstract":"The <b>Gilbert and Marshall Islands campaign</b> was the opening thrust of the American drive across the Central Pacific, prepared from August 1943 and fought from November 1943 to February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Admiral Nimitz's method was to seize one chain of islands as the base for the next, so landings on Tarawa and Makin in the Gilberts, set for 20 November 1943, came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Army's 27th Infantry Division took Makin against light resistance, but the 2nd Marine Division captured fortified Tarawa only at one of the heaviest casualty rates of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lessons of Tarawa shaped the Marshalls landings: heavier and longer bombardment, carefully chosen targets, and the first Navy underwater demolition teams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japan had decided not to commit its fleet to these islands, and by mid-February the threat of the American carriers had driven that fleet from Truk to Palau and home waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> For the Marshalls, Nimitz proposed to bypass the eastern atolls of Wotje and Maloelap and go straight for Kwajalein, against the advice of Spruance, Turner and Holland Smith, who feared for the supply line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Japanese staff officer later recalled that few on the Combined Fleet staff had thought the Americans would go right to the heart of the group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Landings began on 31 January 1944, Roi-Namur was taken by noon on 2 February and Kwajalein Island by 4 February, and Eniwetok was seized from 17 to 23 February, 3 months ahead of schedule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counts of the cost differ: the bombing survey gave 716 American casualties for Eniwetok, while the Army's campaign history lists 262 killed, 757 wounded and 77 missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Truk was then bypassed, and the divisions held back for it began training for the Marianas, whose invasion was brought forward to June 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Philip A. Crowl and Edmund G. Love, <i>Seizure of the Gilberts and Marshalls</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 167-69, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Gilberts/USA-P-Gilberts-11.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl and Edmund G. Love, <i>Seizure of the Gilberts and Marshalls</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 370-74, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Gilberts/USA-P-Gilberts-21.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 190-95, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","Burton Wright III, <i>Eastern Mandates</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-23 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), 7-8, 24-26, https://archive.org/details/EasternMandates. [Open access]","John C. Chapin, <i>Breaking the Outer Ring: Marine Landings in the Marshall Islands</i>, Marines in World War II Commemorative Series (Washington, DC: Marine Corps Historical Center, 1994), 1-4, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-C-Marshalls/index.html. [Open access]","Henry I. Shaw Jr., Bernard C. Nalty and Edwin T. Turnbladh, <i>Central Pacific Drive</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1966), 220, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/III/USMC-III-III-6.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nimitz go straight for Kwajalein instead of the outer atolls?","a":"American carrier and air strength was great enough to strike boldly at the centre of the group, while the Japanese had reinforced Mille, Wotje and Maloelap, where they expected the blow. Spruance warned that shipping bound for Kwajalein would be exposed, but the dangers his commanders feared never materialised."},{"q":"Why could Truk be bypassed after the Marshalls fell?","a":"The carrier raid of 17 and 18 February showed it to be far weaker than planners had believed, the Japanese fleet had already withdrawn from it, and aircraft from the newly seized Marshalls bases could keep it neutralised without an assault."},{"q":"Why did the Marshalls matter more than the Gilberts?","a":"They broke the line of outposts guarding Japan's inner defences, and the speed of the advance robbed the Japanese of the time they needed to fortify the islands nearer home; troops saved there were free for the Marianas months earlier than planned."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Infantrymen_advance_aided_by_medium_tank_on_Kwajalein._1_February%2C_1944._%2849350350947%29.jpg/1280px-Infantrymen_advance_aided_by_medium_tank_on_Kwajalein._1_February%2C_1944._%2849350350947%29.jpg","title":"Infantry and a tank on Kwajalein, 1 February 1944","desc":"American infantrymen advancing with the support of a medium tank on Kwajalein Island on the second day of the landings, with smoke from burning positions behind them.","credit":"U.S. Army photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Infantrymen_advance_aided_by_medium_tank_on_Kwajalein._1_February%2C_1944._%2849350350947%29.jpg","alt":"A medium tank moves across churned ground among broken palm trees while soldiers walk beside it under a sky dark with smoke."},"wiki":"Gilbert and Marshall Islands campaign"},
    "ww2-482": {"questions":["Hundreds of civilians leapt from the cliffs of Marpi Point after victory in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was announced on 9 July 1944, and estimates of the civilian dead still vary widely.","The airfields won in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put Japan's home islands within reach of the B-29, and the first mission against Tokyo flew from the island on 24 November 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Saipan</b>, fought from 15 June to 9 July 1944, was the American conquest of the second largest of the Mariana Islands and the first break in Japan's inner ring of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island was a major Japanese base and sugar-growing centre, home to about 20,000 Japanese civilians and some 4,000 Chamorros, and its garrison of around 30,000 was roughly twice the American estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 8,000 troops landed in the first half hour on 15 June, supported by nearly 150 armoured amphibian tractors, and almost 20,000 were ashore by evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The drive north past Mount Tapotchau was slow and bloody, and the Marines named its killing grounds Death Valley and Purple Heart Ridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before dawn on 7 July the remnants of the garrison struck two Army battalions in what the Marine Corps monograph calls the most devastating banzai attack of the war, after General Saito had ordered it and killed himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Admiral Turner declared the island secured on 9 July, and shortly afterwards hundreds of civilians leapt from the cliffs of Marpi Point, some parents casting their children ahead of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Interpreters coaxed many people out of the caves, but an American inquiry that questioned about 150 Japanese and Chamorro civilians found none who said soldiers had stopped them surrendering, while noting that those who might have said so were dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How many civilians died is not known: the Marine monograph gives no figure, while the National Park Service, starting from about 26,000 civilians and 18,000 interned, infers about 8,000 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Army's history counts 14,111 American casualties among the 71,034 men of the landing force, about a fifth of the combat troops, while almost the whole Japanese garrison was wiped out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The defeat could not be hidden from the Japanese public, Tojo's cabinet fell within days, and Japanese leaders later said the war was lost once the B-29s could reach Japan from the Marianas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Philip A. Crowl, <i>Campaign in the Marianas</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 256-66, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Marianas/USA-P-Marianas-12.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl, <i>Campaign in the Marianas</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 443-45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Marianas/USA-P-Marianas-21.html. [Open access]","Carl W. Hoffman, <i>Saipan: The Beginning of the End</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1950), 244-45 and n. 74, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Saipan/USMC-M-Saipan-6.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 209-12, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Battle of Saipan,\" American Memorial Park, https://www.nps.gov/amme/learn/historyculture/battle-of-saipan.htm. [Open access]","John C. Chapin, <i>Breaching the Marianas: The Battle for Saipan</i>, Marines in World War II Commemorative Series (Washington, DC: Marine Corps Historical Center, 1994), 36, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-C-Saipan/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 21, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many civilians on Saipan kill themselves?","a":"The code of death before surrender, which Saito's last order invoked by quoting the army's code of battle ethics against being taken alive, prevailed among soldiers and civilians alike despite American appeals. Some civilians were also held back by soldiers, though an American inquiry could not confirm threats from the survivors it questioned."},{"q":"Why did the loss of Saipan matter so much to Japan?","a":"It cracked the inner defence line and put the home islands within range of the B-29s, which began flying against Tokyo from Saipan in November 1944. Prince Higashikuni later said the war was lost when the Marianas were taken, and Tojo's cabinet fell almost at once."},{"q":"Why was the fighting harder than the Americans expected?","a":"The garrison was roughly twice the size intelligence had estimated, the mountainous interior around Mount Tapotchau favoured the defence, and the caves sheltered more civilians than soldiers, which made clearing them slow and dangerous."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/A_member_of_a_Marine_patrol_on_Saipan_found_this_family_of_Japs_hiding_in_a_hillside_cave._The_mother%2C_four_children_an_-_NARA_-_532380.jpg/1280px-A_member_of_a_Marine_patrol_on_Saipan_found_this_family_of_Japs_hiding_in_a_hillside_cave._The_mother%2C_four_children_an_-_NARA_-_532380.jpg","title":"A Marine officer at a family's cave, 21 June 1944","desc":"A Marine intelligence and Japanese-language officer persuading a mother, her four children and their dog to leave a hillside cave on Saipan and go to the internment camp.","credit":"Photograph by Corporal Angus Robertson, U.S. Marine Corps, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_member_of_a_Marine_patrol_on_Saipan_found_this_family_of_Japs_hiding_in_a_hillside_cave._The_mother%2C_four_children_an_-_NARA_-_532380.jpg","alt":"A Marine in a helmet crouches at the mouth of a cave, talking to a thin woman holding a baby with small children beside her."},"wiki":"Battle of Saipan"},
    "ww2-483": {"questions":["American submarines sank the carriers Taiho and Shokaku during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the next evening scores of returning American planes ran out of fuel or crashed landing in the dark.","Admiral Toyoda's A-Go plan sought a decisive fleet action, but in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Spruance kept his carriers close to Saipan, fearing that the Japanese might slip round him at night."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Philippine Sea</b>, fought on 19 and 20 June 1944 west of the Marianas, was the carrier battle in which the Japanese fleet tried and failed to break the American invasion of Saipan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admiral Toyoda's Operation A-Go sought a decisive fleet action, but his planners expected the blow to fall on the Palaus, and fighting at Biak had drawn off much of their land-based air strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Admiral Ozawa brought 9 carriers with about 430 aircraft against the 15 carriers and 891 planes of Admiral Spruance's Fifth Fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Spruance held Task Force 58 close to Saipan rather than steaming west to strike first, fearing an end run that never in fact came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 19 June the Japanese raids were broken up far from the carriers, only the battleship South Dakota taking a direct bomb hit, and American submarines sank the carriers Taiho and Shokaku the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The slaughter is counted differently: the bombing survey credited American fighters with about 366 aircraft, the Army's official history says Ozawa lost 330 of his 430 carrier planes, and a later Army study gives 385 of 545 launched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Late on 20 June a strike of 216 American aircraft sank the carrier Hiyo and two oilers, but on the long flight back in darkness only 116 landed safely and about 80 were lost in ditchings and deck crashes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Captain Fuchida of the Combined Fleet staff later said the Japanese had used up practically all of their air strength in the operation, and the survey summed up the loss as three carrier divisions' air groups virtually annihilated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The defeat was kept from the Japanese public, but it made the loss of the Marianas almost certain and led to the resignation of the Navy Minister, a prelude to the fall of Tojo's cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japanese naval officers began to study suicide tactics more seriously after the battle, and one carrier division commander volunteered to organise special-attack units, though no action was taken then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 209-15, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, \"Interrogation Nav. No. 9: Vice Admiral Kurita Takeo,\" in <i>Interrogations of Japanese Officials</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 32-33, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/IJO/IJO-9.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl, <i>Campaign in the Marianas</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 119-22, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Marianas/USA-P-Marianas-7.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 292-96, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 563, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Western Pacific</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-29 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), 12-13, https://archive.org/details/WesternPacific. [Open access]","Carl W. Hoffman, <i>Saipan: The Beginning of the End</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1950), 259-60, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Saipan/USMC-M-Saipan-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Spruance not race west to strike the Japanese fleet first?","a":"His first duty was to cover the landing at Saipan, and he feared that Ozawa might pass round him under cover of darkness and fall on the transports. He overruled Mitscher's protest and turned east for the night, giving up the chance of an early blow."},{"q":"Why were Japanese aircraft losses so heavy on 19 June?","a":"The raids were poorly coordinated and came in piecemeal through the day, American fighters met them 80 to 100 kilometres (50 to 60 miles) out with the advantage of height, and the Japanese planes, lighter and less well armed, were cut down before most could reach the carriers."},{"q":"Why did the battle matter far beyond Saipan?","a":"It finished Japanese carrier aviation as a naval force, doomed the garrisons in the Marianas by giving the Americans command of the surrounding sea and air, and shook the government in Tokyo, where the Navy Minister's resignation began Tojo's fall."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Zuikaku_and_two_destroyers_under_attack_on_20_June_1944_%2880-G-238025%29.jpg/1280px-Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Zuikaku_and_two_destroyers_under_attack_on_20_June_1944_%2880-G-238025%29.jpg","title":"Zuikaku under attack, 20 June 1944","desc":"The Japanese carrier Zuikaku, centre, and two destroyers manoeuvring under attack by American carrier aircraft late on 20 June 1944; Zuikaku was hit by several bombs but survived.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, Naval History and Heritage Command, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_aircraft_carrier_Zuikaku_and_two_destroyers_under_attack_on_20_June_1944_%2880-G-238025%29.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a large ship turning in a tight circle amid bomb splashes, with two smaller ships leaving curving wakes nearby."},"wiki":"Battle of the Philippine Sea"},
    "ww2-484": {"questions":["Already premier and war minister, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also made himself chief of the Army General Staff in February 1944, a step some officers likened to usurping imperial prerogatives.","The Tokyo tribunal held that the shocking state of prisoners of war in 1944, when the cabinet of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell, proved he had taken no proper steps to care for them."],"abstract":"<b>Hideki Tojo</b> was the army general who led Japan's government from October 1941 to July 1944 and took the country into war with the United States and Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had been chief of staff of the Kwantung Army from 1937, vice-minister of war from 1938 and minister of war from July 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The bombing survey described him as the leader and symbol of the military clique that took Japan to war without clear aims or any idea of how it would end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1944, after the American landings in the Marshalls and the raid on Truk, he added the post of chief of the Army General Staff to the premiership and the war ministry, a move some officers criticised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Defeats had been hidden from the public or passed off as withdrawals, but the fall of Saipan in July 1944 could not be kept from the Japanese people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The senior statesmen, former premiers with no constitutional office, had been working against him since 1943, and Admiral Okada sent word that it would be best for him to resign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The loss of Saipan gave them their chance: his attempt to reshuffle the cabinet failed, the Navy Minister resigned with their help, and Tojo found he no longer had the emperor's support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 18 July 1944, the day the fall of Saipan was announced, he resigned with his whole cabinet and gave up the army staff post as well, ending 33 months in office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He had told the nation that Japan faced an unprecedented crisis, and Japanese leaders later testified that the war was lost once B-29s could fly against Japan from the Marianas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the war the Tokyo tribunal found him guilty of planning and waging aggressive war and held him responsible for the neglect of prisoners, plain from their condition when his cabinet fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), <i>Japan's Struggle to End the War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 1-3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/JapansStruggle/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 21, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 257, 304, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperations. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 669-70, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl, <i>Campaign in the Marianas</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 443-45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Marianas/USA-P-Marianas-21.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, 1948), chap. 10, 1205-08, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-10.html. [Open access]","Carl W. Hoffman, <i>Saipan: The Beginning of the End</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1950), 259-60, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Saipan/USMC-M-Saipan-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Tojo fall only after Saipan, though critics had worked against him for months?","a":"The senior statesmen had been quietly discrediting his government since 1943, but they needed an opening. The loss of Saipan provided it: a cabinet reshuffle failed, the Navy Minister resigned, and without the emperor's support his position collapsed within days."},{"q":"Why was his taking command of the Army General Staff criticised?","a":"Holding the premiership, the war ministry and the army's top command at once joined political and military authority in one man, and some army and navy officers saw it as reminiscent of the usurpation of imperial prerogatives."},{"q":"Why did his fall not bring an early peace?","a":"His successor Kuniaki Koiso was a retired general, and the army agreed to join the new cabinet only on condition that the war be prosecuted vigorously. Even so, the change broke the grip of Tojo's clique and began the slow move towards surrender."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Tojo_Hideki.jpg","title":"Hideki Tojo in uniform","desc":"Portrait of Hideki Tojo in army uniform wearing his decorations, from a Japanese collection of photographs of prime ministers.","credit":"Photographer unknown, National Diet Library, public domain in Japan, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tojo_Hideki.jpg","alt":"An older man with round glasses and a moustache, in a high-collared military tunic covered with star-shaped medals, holding a cigarette."},"wiki":"Hideki Tojo","places":["city:1159151609"]},
    "ww2-485": {"questions":["Japanese defenders in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fought from a defence in depth set back from the beaches, the new approach Imperial General Headquarters had ordered in July 1944 after failed beach defences.","Only Admiral Halsey had argued that the Palaus could safely be bypassed, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the fight for Angaur cost about 9,800 American battle casualties."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Peleliu</b> was the American capture of an island in the southern Palaus, begun by the 1st Marine Division on 15 September 1944 and finished by the Army's 81st Infantry Division on 27 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nimitz had cut the Palau operation down to Angaur, Peleliu and Ngesebus, whose airfields were meant to support the coming invasion of the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The defence was directed by Colonel Kunio Nakagawa, whose name appeared on every important captured order, while the senior officer on the island, General Murai, stayed in the background.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In July 1944 Imperial General Headquarters had ordered new tactics: a main line of resistance far enough inland to escape the bombardment, and a defence in depth designed to wear the attackers down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On Peleliu the defenders dug into the coral ridges and caves of the Umurbrogol, and for the first time in the Marines' Pacific experience they made no futile banzai charges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When General Murai proposed an all-out attack in November, General Inoue forbade it, saying that Saipan had been lost quickly through vain banzai charges and that it was easy to die but difficult to live on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Marine commander had expected the operation to last 4 days, but it went on for almost 2.5 months, and Nakagawa and Murai killed themselves on the night of 24 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Palaus about 13,600 Japanese were killed, over 11,000 of them on Peleliu, and some 400 prisoners were taken, mostly Korean and Okinawan troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American battle casualties came to around 9,800, about 6,525 of them in the reinforced 1st Marine Division, and the Army's history called it one of the bloodiest battles of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Halsey alone had thought the Palaus could be bypassed, and with hindsight the Army's history judged he was probably right, though Peleliu and Angaur did prove useful bases for the Philippine campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Ross Smith, <i>The Approach to the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 452-53, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Approach/USA-P-Approach-19.html. [Open access]","Robert Ross Smith, <i>The Approach to the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 571-75, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Approach/USA-P-Approach-24.html. [Open access]","F. O. Hough, <i>The Assault on Peleliu</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1950), 179-80, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Peleliu/USMC-M-Peleliu-9.html. [Open access]","F. O. Hough, <i>The Assault on Peleliu</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1950), 18, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Peleliu/USMC-M-Peleliu-2.html. [Open access]","George W. Garand and Truman R. Strobridge, <i>Western Pacific Operations</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1971), 70-71, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/IV/USMC-IV-III-1.html. [Open access]","George W. Garand and Truman R. Strobridge, <i>Western Pacific Operations</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1971), 262-63, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/IV/USMC-IV-III-8.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 275, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Peleliu so costly to take?","a":"The garrison had dug a defence in depth into rugged coral ridges and caves, the preliminary naval gunfire could not destroy positions of that kind, and the defenders fought to the death instead of wasting themselves in banzai charges, so each cave had to be taken in turn."},{"q":"Why did the Japanese commanders avoid banzai charges on Peleliu?","a":"Imperial General Headquarters had concluded that beach defence and mass counterattacks only fed troops to American firepower, and General Inoue told his officers that Saipan had fallen quickly because of vain banzai attacks; delay itself was the aim."},{"q":"Why is the value of taking Peleliu still questioned?","a":"The Angaur bomber field was not ready until after the Leyte landing, and its first mission against the Philippines flew only in November. Halsey had urged bypassing the Palaus, though Nimitz, MacArthur and the Joint Chiefs believed with what they then knew that the islands had to be taken."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Amphibious_tractors_go_to_work_on_the_caves_that_still_have_some_Jap_snipers_holding_out_in_them._They_are_in_the_hills_of_Peleliu._October%2C_1944._%2853397132027%29.jpg/1280px-Amphibious_tractors_go_to_work_on_the_caves_that_still_have_some_Jap_snipers_holding_out_in_them._They_are_in_the_hills_of_Peleliu._October%2C_1944._%2853397132027%29.jpg","title":"Attacking caves on Peleliu, October 1944","desc":"Amphibious tractors working against caves still held by Japanese snipers in the ridges of Peleliu, October 1944.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphibious_tractors_go_to_work_on_the_caves_that_still_have_some_Jap_snipers_holding_out_in_them._They_are_in_the_hills_of_Peleliu._October%2C_1944._%2853397132027%29.jpg","alt":"Soldiers move through shattered tree stumps below a steep, smoke-streaked limestone ridge with dark cave openings."},"wiki":"Battle of Peleliu"},
    "ww2-486": {"questions":["Four American divisions came ashore at Lingayen Gulf on 9 January 1945 against little resistance, opening the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that was still unfinished when Japan surrendered in August.","The Shobu Group of about 152,000 men tied down major parts of three American divisions in the northern mountains until the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Luzon</b> was the American reconquest of the island of Luzon in the Philippines, opened by the landing at Lingayen Gulf on 9 January 1945 and still unfinished when Japan surrendered in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its aims were the prompt seizure of the Central Plains and the Manila area, the destruction of the main Japanese forces and the closing of the northern entrance to the South China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By mid-December 1944 General Yamashita had concluded that, with no air reinforcements coming, his only course was a static defence that would delay the conquest of Luzon as long as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave up the Central Plains and Manila Bay region and concentrated his forces in three mountain strongholds, the largest being the Shobu Group of about 152,000 men in the north under his own command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The approach was the worst of it for the fleet: suicide aircraft hit 16 ships on 6 January in Lingayen Gulf alone, 10 of them seriously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The landing itself met little opposition, as four divisions went ashore on a front of about 19 kilometres (12 miles) and all the assault waves reached the beach within 10 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yamashita had chosen not to defend the southern shores of the gulf, and the bombing survey judged that the Americans fought an enemy disorganised, badly supplied and isolated beyond hope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Manila and the Central Plains were secure by early March, but the Shobu Group kept the better part of three American divisions busy in the northern mountains until the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Excluding Leyte, the fighting on Luzon and the southern islands cost the Sixth and Eighth Armies almost 47,000 battle casualties, 10,380 of them killed, and Sixth Army suffered over 93,400 non-battle casualties by 30 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese lost nearly all of their 230,000 troops on Luzon apart from those who gave up at the end, and Yamashita and about 50,500 of his men surrendered only after the war ended, at Baguio on 3 September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 88-95, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-5.html. [Open access]","Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 651-52, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-33.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 758, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 288-89, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","Dale Andrade, <i>Luzon</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-28 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1996), 28-30, https://archive.org/details/Luzon. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 258-59, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurTheCampaigns. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Yamashita not defend the beaches or Manila?","a":"He had no air support coming, his armoured division would be immobile under American air power, and his troops were understrength and short of supplies, so he chose a static defence in the mountains that could cost the Americans time and lives instead of a battle he would lose on the plains."},{"q":"Why did the campaign drag on until the end of the war?","a":"The Shobu Group held rugged mountain country in northern Luzon, and once the strategically important areas were taken only limited forces were needed to keep it on the defensive, so it was left to be worn down slowly by fighting, starvation and disease."},{"q":"Why were American non-battle casualties so high on Luzon?","a":"The divisions were tired after earlier campaigns and understrength, replacements barely covered battle losses, and the troops met new diseases, an enervating climate and hot days and cold, wet nights in the mountains, all of which filled the hospitals."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Coast_Guard_landing_barges_at_Lingayen_Gulf.jpg/1280px-Coast_Guard_landing_barges_at_Lingayen_Gulf.jpg","title":"Landing barges in Lingayen Gulf, 9 January 1945","desc":"A line of Coast Guard-manned landing barges carrying the first wave of troops to the Luzon beaches after the naval bombardment of 9 January 1945.","credit":"U.S. Coast Guard photograph, National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coast_Guard_landing_barges_at_Lingayen_Gulf.jpg","alt":"Landing craft packed with helmeted soldiers throw up spray as they speed in a line across rough dark water."},"wiki":"Battle of Luzon"},
    "ww2-487": {"questions":["With the entrance only 72 kilometres (45 miles) away, Kurita turned his ships north in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a decision Combined Fleet headquarters received with disappointment and indignation.","Admiral Toyoda sent the whole Combined Fleet into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because, as he later said, a fleet cut off from southern fuel would be useless even if it survived."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Leyte Gulf</b> was the series of naval and air actions of 23 to 26 October 1944 in which the Japanese Navy committed almost its whole remaining strength against the American landing on Leyte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Sho plan, battleship forces were to break into the gulf and destroy the invasion shipping, while a carrier force with few aircraft lured the American carriers away to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Admiral Toyoda later explained that without the fleet the Philippines could not be held, and that a fleet cut off from its southern fuel would be useless anyway, so the gamble had to be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two American fleets were present, Kinkaid's Seventh under MacArthur and Halsey's Third under Nimitz, and Halsey's orders made destroying the enemy fleet his primary task if the chance arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 24 October carrier aircraft sank the giant battleship Musashi, and that night Halsey took his carriers and battleships north after the decoy, leaving San Bernardino Strait unguarded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Before dawn on 25 October the southern Japanese force was destroyed in Surigao Strait, and to the north all four of Ozawa's carriers were sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kurita's main force came through the strait unopposed and fell on a group of escort carriers off Samar, sinking an escort carrier, two destroyers and a destroyer escort but losing three heavy cruisers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shortly after midday, with the entrance of the gulf only 72 kilometres (45 miles) away, Kurita turned north, believing the transports had probably gone and that he could do more good in the open sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Combined Fleet headquarters met his decision with disappointment and indignation, and by 26 October Japan had lost 4 carriers, 3 battleships, 10 cruisers and 9 destroyers, and what remained of the fleet was reduced to virtual impotence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> American ships in the gulf also suffered the first organised kamikaze attacks, and the Army Air Forces' history judged that American courage and Japanese blunders had saved the beachhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 280-86, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, \"Interrogation Nav. No. 9: Vice Admiral Kurita Takeo,\" in <i>Interrogations of Japanese Officials</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 32, 44-45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/IJO/IJO-9.html. [Open access]","M. Hamlin Cannon, <i>Leyte: The Return to the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 87-91, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Return/USA-P-Return-6.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 397-99, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Leyte</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-27 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), 16-17, https://archive.org/details/Leyte. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl, <i>Campaign in the Marianas</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 443-45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Marianas/USA-P-Marianas-21.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), vii-viii, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kurita turn away when he was so close to Leyte Gulf?","a":"He had no news of the other Japanese forces, his schedule was hours late, and intercepted messages suggested American air attacks were gathering. He told interrogators that in the narrow gulf he could not manoeuvre and would achieve nothing, while in the open sea he might still be useful."},{"q":"Why did Halsey leave San Bernardino Strait unguarded?","a":"He did not know that the northern carriers had almost no aircraft, he believed Kurita's force had been badly damaged on 24 October, and his orders made the destruction of the enemy fleet his primary task whenever the chance arose."},{"q":"Why is the battle seen as the end of the Japanese Navy as a fighting force?","a":"It lost most of its remaining surface strength in 3 days, and the survivors were so scattered and disorganised that carrier aircraft and submarines picked them off in the weeks that followed, until 68 combatant ships had been lost defending the Philippines."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/80-G-272555_Battle_of_the_Sibuyan_Sea%2C_24_October_1944.jpg/1280px-80-G-272555_Battle_of_the_Sibuyan_Sea%2C_24_October_1944.jpg","title":"The cruiser Haguro under air attack, 24 October 1944","desc":"The Japanese heavy cruiser Haguro firing at attacking American carrier aircraft during the Sibuyan Sea action on 24 October 1944.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:80-G-272555_Battle_of_the_Sibuyan_Sea%2C_24_October_1944.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a long warship at speed, smoke bursting from its guns and a white wake streaming behind it."},"wiki":"Battle of Leyte Gulf"},
    "ww2-488": {"questions":["From October 1944 to the end of the Okinawa campaign Japanese pilots flew 2,550 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> missions, of which 475 scored hits or damaging near misses but none sank a fleet carrier.","The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 'divine wind', recalled the typhoon that wrecked a 13th-century Mongol invasion fleet, and Japanese commanders called such operations tokko, or special attack."],"abstract":"<b>Kamikaze</b> was the name for Japan's organised suicide attacks of 1944 and 1945, above all aircraft deliberately crashed into Allied ships, which the Japanese forces called tokko, ‘special attack’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word, meaning ‘divine wind’, recalled the 13th-century typhoon that scattered and sank a Mongol invasion fleet off southern Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Front-line airmen had talked of crash tactics since 1943, the Army section of Imperial General Headquarters began informal discussions in May 1944, and by the summer the Army was recruiting volunteers for special-attack training.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By then, the bombing survey concluded, Japanese air commanders knew they could not match American air power anywhere, and their pilots' willingness to die was the one asset left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A pilot who meant to crash needed little skill to hit a ship if he got past the fighters and the guns, and enough simultaneous attackers would ensure that some did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Vice Admiral Onishi ordered the first units formed on 19 October 1944, and naval pilots of the 201st Air Group made the first attack on 25 October during the Battle of Leyte Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Onishi's chief of staff, Captain Inoguchi, told American interrogators that such attacks were never ordered and that every pilot volunteered, but the Japanese record compiled for MacArthur's staff says the High Command was forced to sanction and then actively push them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Suicide aircraft struck the ships off Leyte and Mindoro, where the cruiser Nashville lost more than 130 men on 13 December, and the kamikaze became the sole hope of the Japanese air forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From October 1944 to the end of the Okinawa campaign the survey counted 2,550 kamikaze missions, of which 475 hit or damaged their targets, sinking about 45 vessels but nothing larger than an escort carrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The menace mounted until May 1945, and for over a month B-29s were diverted from Japanese cities to bomb the Kyushu airfields from which most of the attacks came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, \"Interrogation Nav. No. 12: Captain Inoguchi Rikihei,\" in <i>Interrogations of Japanese Officials</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 60-61, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/IJO/IJO-12.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 10, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 561-66, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","M. Hamlin Cannon, <i>Leyte: The Return to the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 87-91, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Return/USA-P-Return-6.html. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Leyte</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-27 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), 16-17, https://archive.org/details/Leyte. [Open access]","Dale Andrade, <i>Luzon</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-28 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1996), 5-6, https://archive.org/details/Luzon. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), xi, xx, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/index.html. [Open access]","Philip A. Crowl and Edmund G. Love, <i>Seizure of the Gilberts and Marshalls</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 370-74, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Gilberts/USA-P-Gilberts-21.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan turn to suicide attacks in late 1944?","a":"Its air forces had lost their skilled pilots, replacements were poorly trained, and conventional bombing had proved futile against American fighters and anti-aircraft fire. Crash attack promised the greatest results for the least cost in planes and pilots, and needed little instruction."},{"q":"Why did kamikaze attacks fall short of what Japan expected?","a":"The Japanese were misled by their own inflated claims of heavy ships sunk and ignored their technicians' advice that a heavier explosive charge was needed to sink large ships, so although many warships were damaged, no ship larger than an escort carrier was sunk."},{"q":"Why is it hard to say how voluntary the attacks were?","a":"Captain Inoguchi insisted after the war that no one ordered them and that men volunteered, but the Japanese record compiled for MacArthur's staff shows the Army asking its air schools for lists of volunteers from mid-1944 and the High Command finally pushing the formation of special-attack units."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Japanese_Mitsubishi_A6M_kamikaze_plane_hits_USS_Suwannee_%28CVE-27%29_on_25_October_1944_%28NH_71524%29.jpg/1280px-Japanese_Mitsubishi_A6M_kamikaze_plane_hits_USS_Suwannee_%28CVE-27%29_on_25_October_1944_%28NH_71524%29.jpg","title":"A kamikaze hits USS Suwannee, 25 October 1944","desc":"A Japanese Zero suicide plane striking the American escort carrier Suwannee on 25 October 1944, photographed from USS Petrof Bay; a Hellcat that chased it is pulling out above.","credit":"U.S. Navy photograph, Naval History and Heritage Command, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Mitsubishi_A6M_kamikaze_plane_hits_USS_Suwannee_%28CVE-27%29_on_25_October_1944_%28NH_71524%29.jpg","alt":"An aircraft carrier at sea with a burst of thick smoke erupting from its stern, while small aircraft and flak bursts dot the sky."},"wiki":"Kamikaze"},
    "ww2-489": {"questions":["MacArthur announced the imminent recapture of the city on 4 February 1945, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted until 3 March and left an estimated 100,000 Filipino civilians dead.","Artillery, not bombing, breached the old walls of Intramuros during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because so many women and children were trapped inside the Spanish fortress."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Manila</b> was the month of street fighting, from 3 February to 3 March 1945, in which American and Filipino forces retook the Philippine capital from its Japanese garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seizing Manila was a chief aim of the Luzon invasion, but General Yamashita had not meant to defend it, reasoning that its million people could not be fed, its buildings burned easily and its flat ground needed great strength to hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rear Admiral Iwabuchi, whose Manila Naval Defense Force grew to nearly 16,000 sailors, chose to fight instead, and from 3 February the local army command gave him full authority over every army unit in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At dawn on 3 February a flying column of the 1st Cavalry Division raced into the northern suburbs and freed some 4,000 American and Allied internees at Santo Tomas University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> MacArthur announced the city's imminent recapture on 4 February while his staff planned a victory parade, but the battle had barely begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It became a series of house-to-house struggles, and although MacArthur placed strict limits on artillery and air support to protect the city and its people, massive devastation could not be avoided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japanese troops demolished buildings as they withdrew, and a captured order of the Manila naval defence command told its men to gather Filipinos in one place before killing them, to save ammunition and labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bombing of the walled city of Intramuros was ruled out because many women and children were trapped inside, and after a broadcast plea to surrender or release them went unanswered it was breached by artillery and infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Army's history counts 1,010 Americans killed and 5,565 wounded in the city, and about 16,000 Japanese killed in and around it, at least 12,500 of them from Iwabuchi's force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It records an estimated 100,000 Filipino civilian dead and describes the city as a shambles, its 400-year-old walled centre nearly razed and its government quarter wiped out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 305-07, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-16.html. [Open access]","Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 240-42, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-13.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 490-99, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 271-75, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurTheCampaigns. [Open access]","Dale Andrade, <i>Luzon</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-28 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1996), 12-16, https://archive.org/details/Luzon. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, 1948), chap. 8, 1040-41, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 288, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Manila defended against Yamashita's wishes?","a":"Disagreement and confusion between the army and navy commands in the city produced a series of compromises. Admiral Okochi had strengthened the naval defences on his own initiative, and the control he handed to the army was so limited that Iwabuchi's force could fight its own battle."},{"q":"Why did so many civilians die in the battle?","a":"Hundreds of thousands of people were caught in a month of house-to-house fighting that destroyed much of the city despite American limits on artillery and air support, and Japanese forces also massacred Filipinos, some under orders captured afterwards."},{"q":"Why did the Americans not bomb Intramuros?","a":"Many civilians, mostly women and children, were trapped inside the old walled city, so bombing it flat was ruled out; when the Japanese ignored a broadcast appeal to surrender or let the civilians go, artillery opened breaches for an infantry assault instead."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/ManilaIntramuros.jpg/1280px-ManilaIntramuros.jpg","title":"American troops in the Walled City, 27 February 1945","desc":"U.S. troops moving through rubble beside a disabled vehicle during the fighting in Intramuros, the Walled City of Manila.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ManilaIntramuros.jpg","alt":"Two soldiers with rifles pick their way through broken masonry past a wrecked tracked vehicle, ruined buildings rising behind them."},"wiki":"Battle of Manila (1945)"},
    "ww2-490": {"questions":["By the war's end more than 2,000 crippled B-29s had made emergency landings on the airfields won in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began with landings on 19 February 1945.","American casualties exceeded Japanese losses in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where only 216 prisoners had been taken by 26 March 1945 from a garrison twice the size American intelligence expected."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Iwo Jima</b> was the American conquest of a small volcanic island about 1,080 kilometres (670 miles) south of Tokyo, fought from 19 February to 26 March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island is less than 8 kilometres (5 miles) long and covers under 21 square kilometres (8 square miles), with the extinct volcano Mount Suribachi at its southern tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Americans wanted it as an emergency field for crippled B-29s, a base for escort fighters and air-sea rescue, and to end the warnings and raids it gave Japan against the bombers in the Marianas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> General Kuribayashi abandoned the doctrine of counterattacking at the beach and of costly banzai charges, ordering mutually supporting positions in depth to be held to the death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His men dug more than 18 kilometres (11 miles) of tunnels, and American intelligence, judging that the island's rainwater could support only 12,000 or 13,000 men, underestimated the garrison by half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Marines landed on 19 February, and despite the long bombardment the defenders had to be dug out of their underground positions and killed one by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Organised resistance was declared at an end on 16 March, but fighting went on in the north until 26 March, and Kuribayashi is thought to have taken his own life during the last night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Marine Corps' official history notes that American casualties in this battle exceeded those of the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The bombing survey gave about 21,000 American casualties, while a later Marine Corps account counts 24,053 Marine and Navy casualties with 6,140 dead, against some 22,000 Japanese killed and only 216 prisoners by 26 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the war's end, by the survey's count about 2,400 and by the Marine account 2,251 B-29s had made emergency landings on the island, and the air force history suggested the crews saved might balance the lives lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Whitman S. Bartley, <i>Iwo Jima: Amphibious Epic</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1954), 4-11, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-IwoJima/USMC-M-IwoJima-1.html. [Open access]","Whitman S. Bartley, <i>Iwo Jima: Amphibious Epic</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1954), 193, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-IwoJima/USMC-M-IwoJima-9.html. [Open access]","Joseph H. Alexander, <i>Closing In: Marines in the Seizure of Iwo Jima</i>, Marines in World War II Commemorative Series (Washington, DC: Marine Corps Historical Center, 1994), 2-6, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-C-Iwo/index.html. [Open access]","Joseph H. Alexander, <i>Closing In: Marines in the Seizure of Iwo Jima</i>, Marines in World War II Commemorative Series (Washington, DC: Marine Corps Historical Center, 1994), 45-49, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-C-Iwo/index.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey (Pacific), Naval Analysis Division, <i>The Campaigns of the Pacific War</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), 320-21, https://archive.org/details/campaignsofpacif46unit. [Open access]","George W. Garand and Truman R. Strobridge, <i>Western Pacific Operations</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1971), 712-13, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/IV/USMC-IV-VI-12.html. [Open access]","George W. Garand and Truman R. Strobridge, <i>Western Pacific Operations</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1971), 444-56, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/IV/USMC-IV-VI-1.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), xviii-xix, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States want Iwo Jima so badly?","a":"It lay on the B-29s' route to Honshu, its radar and fighters warned and harried the bombers, and its aircraft raided the Marianas bases. In American hands it could become an emergency landing field, a base for escort fighters and a station for air-sea rescue."},{"q":"Why was Iwo Jima so costly to capture?","a":"Kuribayashi built his defence underground in depth and refused to waste men in banzai charges, so naval gunfire and air bombardment left most positions intact and direct air support had limited effect; the troops had to clear the defenders individually."},{"q":"Why do the casualty figures for Iwo Jima differ?","a":"They count different things: the bombing survey gave an approximate total of American killed, wounded and missing for the whole campaign, while the Marine account counts the Marines and attached Navy personnel of the assault units, which it calls the highest single-action losses in the Corps' history."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/%22Iwo%27s_Gibraltar%22%2C_Iwo_Jima%2C_1945_%2848298967666%29.jpg/1280px-%22Iwo%27s_Gibraltar%22%2C_Iwo_Jima%2C_1945_%2848298967666%29.jpg","title":"Mount Suribachi from the first airfield, 1945","desc":"Marines and equipment on Motoyama airfield number one, dwarfed by Mount Suribachi, from whose cave positions the defenders fired on the beachhead.","credit":"Official U.S. Marine Corps photograph, Marine Corps History Division, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Iwo%27s_Gibraltar%22%2C_Iwo_Jima%2C_1945_%2848298967666%29.jpg","alt":"A low, steep-sided volcanic mountain rises behind a flat expanse of dark sand where a field gun and a few men are dug in."},"wiki":"Battle of Iwo Jima"},
    "ww2-491": {"questions":["Because a smaller flag had already flown over the crater that morning, critics later claimed that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been posed, a charge its photographer always denied.","Marine Corps investigations in 1946 and much later changed the names attached to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, putting Harold Schultz and Harold Keller where John Bradley and Rene Gagnon had been."],"abstract":"<b>Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima</b> is the photograph Joe Rosenthal of the Associated Press took on 23 February 1945 of men hoisting a large American flag on Mount Suribachi, at the southern tip of Iwo Jima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After 4 days of fighting, a patrol of the 28th Marines under First Lieutenant Harold Schrier reached the summit and at 10.20 raised a small flag, about 137 by 71 centimetres (54 by 28 inches), on a length of iron pipe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Watching from the beach, Navy Secretary James Forrestal said that the flag meant a Marine Corps for the next 500 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thought too small to be seen from below, it was replaced by a larger flag from the landing ship LST 779, and Rosenthal followed the second patrol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 3 hours after the first raising, five Marines and a Navy corpsman pushed the new flag upright, and Rosenthal, by his own account, swung his camera round and caught the moment almost by accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His film reached the United States before the Marine photographs of the first flag, and the picture was soon on millions of Seventh War Loan posters and on a three-cent stamp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among his 18 photographs that day was a posed group round the flag, and when his editor asked whether the flag-raising picture was posed he said yes, thinking of that one, which started a lasting rumour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The names given in 1945 were wrong twice over: Harlon Block was identified in 1946, and the Marine Corps later concluded that Harold Schultz and Harold Keller, not John Bradley and Rene Gagnon, were in the picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The photograph won a Pulitzer Prize and became the model for the Marine Corps War Memorial, dedicated by Eisenhower in 1954, and the flag it shows is displayed at the National Museum of the Marine Corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The battle went on until 26 March and cost the Americans 5,931 dead and 17,372 wounded, while only 216 prisoners were taken from a garrison of about 21,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Whitman S. Bartley, <i>Iwo Jima: Amphibious Epic</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1954), 74–76, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-IwoJima/USMC-M-IwoJima-4.html. [Open access]","George W. Garand and Truman R. Strobridge, <i>Western Pacific Operations</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1971), 540–42, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/IV/USMC-IV-VI-5.html. [Open access]","Joseph H. Alexander, <i>Closing In: Marines in the Seizure of Iwo Jima</i>, Marines in World War II Commemorative Series (Washington, DC: History and Museums Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1994), 25–26, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-C-Iwo/index.html. [Open access]","Bernard C. Nalty and Danny J. Crawford, <i>The United States Marines on Iwo Jima: The Battle and the Flag Raisings</i> (Washington, DC: History and Museums Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1995), 8–11, https://archive.org/details/TheUnitedStatesMarinesOnIwoJimaTheBattleAndTheFlagRaisings. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History of the Marine Corps War Memorial,” George Washington Memorial Parkway, last updated 1 May 2023, https://www.nps.gov/gwmp/learn/historyculture/usmcwarmemorial.htm. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “World War II Photos,” item 156, “Flag raising on Iwo Jima,” 80-G-413988, https://www.archives.gov/research/military/ww2/photos. [Open access]","National Museum of the Marine Corps, “World War II Gallery,” https://www.usmcmuseum.com/wwii.html. [Open access]","Edwin H. Simmons, “The Island Campaign,” in <i>Pearl to V-J Day: World War II in the Pacific</i>, ed. Jacob Neufeld, William T. Y’Blood and Mary Lee Jefferson (Washington, DC: Air Force History and Museums Program, 2000), 25–44, https://archive.org/details/PearlToVJDay. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a second flag go up on Suribachi at all?","a":"The first flag, raised by Schrier’s patrol at 10.20, was thought too small to be seen from the beaches below, so a larger one was brought from LST 779 and raised about 3 hours later; Rosenthal photographed this second raising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did people come to believe the picture was staged?","a":"Rosenthal also took a posed group shot round the flag, and when his editor asked whether the flag-raising picture was posed he answered yes, thinking of that one; he and surviving witnesses insisted the famous picture was unrigged, saying a posed shot would have shown the men’s faces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the identities of the flag raisers keep changing?","a":"The survivors were rushed home for a bond tour without studying the picture closely; Gagnon’s first list named Henry Hansen, corrected to Harlon Block in 1946, and the Marine Corps later concluded that Harold Schultz and Harold Keller stood where Bradley and Gagnon had been placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger.jpeg/1920px-Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger.jpeg","title":"Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima, 23 February 1945","desc":"Joe Rosenthal’s photograph of the second, larger flag being pushed upright on the summit of Mount Suribachi.","credit":"Photograph by Joe Rosenthal, Associated Press, public domain (copyright not renewed), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima%2C_larger.jpeg","alt":"Six men in helmets and fatigues straining together to push a long flagpole upright on rubble-strewn high ground, the flag beginning to unfurl against a cloudy sky."},"wiki":"Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima"},
    "ww2-492": {"questions":["Admiral Leahy told Truman on 18 June 1945 that troops in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had lost 35 per cent in casualties, a ratio he applied to the planned invasion of Kyushu.","Islanders call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the typhoon of steel, and the prefectural museum counts some 200,000 dead, soldiers and civilians together, from about 3 months of fighting."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Okinawa</b> was the American conquest of the largest of the Ryukyu Islands, fought on land, at sea and in the air from April to June 1945 under the plan called Operation Iceberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Okinawa lay within striking distance of Kyushu, and nearly half a million people lived there, people who did not then regard themselves as Japanese, according to a Marine historian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> General Ushijima Mitsuru’s 32nd Army of about 77,000 men, with naval troops and some 20,000 Okinawan conscripts, chose not to fight at the beaches but to hold ridge lines in the south around Shuri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> So on 1 April more than 16,000 troops were ashore within an hour and 60,000 by nightfall, and the real fighting began only when the Tenth Army reached the Shuri defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Off the island, kamikaze aircraft sank or damaged hundreds of ships, and the battleship <i>Yamato</i>, which sailed on 6 April with fuel for a one-way voyage, was sunk by carrier aircraft before it reached the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Organised resistance ended on 22 June, after Ushijima and his chief of staff killed themselves near Mabuni, and the campaign was declared over on 2 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official American count was 12,520 killed or missing and 36,631 wounded, with 36 ships sunk and 368 damaged, the heaviest cost of any campaign against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 110,000 Japanese soldiers died and 7,401 surrendered, while Tenth Army intelligence reckoned that some 42,000 civilians had also been killed; Okinawan counts put the island’s own dead at about 120,000 of more than 200,000 on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Within a week of the landings the Koiso cabinet fell, and in Washington on 18 June Leahy took Okinawa’s 35 per cent casualty rate as the yardstick for invading Kyushu, while Truman hoped to avoid an Okinawa from one end of Japan to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Okinawans remember the battle as the typhoon of steel, 3 months in which shelling and bombing changed the island’s whole landscape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Roy E. Appleman, James M. Burns, Russell A. Gugeler and John Stevens, <i>Okinawa: The Last Battle</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), 1–2, 68–70, 467–74, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Okinawa/USA-P-Okinawa-18.html. [Open access]","Charles S. Nichols Jr. and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Okinawa: Victory in the Pacific</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1955), 55–57, 258–60, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Okinawa/USMC-M-Okinawa-11.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 10–11, 15–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Minutes of Meeting Held at the White House on Monday, 18 June 1945 at 1530,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 1, doc. 598, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d598. [Open access]","Okinawa Prefectural Peace Memorial Museum, “鉄の暴風―住民の見た沖縄戦,” https://www.peace-museum.okinawa.jp/heiwagakusyu/okinawasen_ni_tsuite/1/index.html. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Himeyuri Peace Museum, “ひめゆり学徒隊の沖縄戦,” https://www.himeyuri.or.jp/himeyuri/study_war/. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Edwin H. Simmons, “The Island Campaign,” in <i>Pearl to V-J Day: World War II in the Pacific</i>, ed. Jacob Neufeld, William T. Y’Blood and Mary Lee Jefferson (Washington, DC: Air Force History and Museums Program, 2000), 25–44, https://archive.org/details/PearlToVJDay. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the landings on 1 April so easy?","a":"Ushijima had decided not to defend the shoreline against overwhelming American firepower and held his main strength in deep positions along the ridges in the south, so the Tenth Army walked ashore and met the real defence only at Shuri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Okinawa weigh so heavily on plans to invade Japan?","a":"Its casualties were the highest of any Pacific campaign, and on 18 June Leahy proposed applying Okinawa’s 35 per cent loss rate to the troops planned for Kyushu; Truman said he hoped to prevent an Okinawa from one end of Japan to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the civilian death figures so far apart?","a":"They were counted in different ways by different people: American intelligence inferred about 42,000 civilian dead from the excess of bodies over the expected garrison, while Okinawan reckonings count about 120,000 islanders, conscripts included, among more than 200,000 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/1945-4-25_Battle_of_Mt._Tanodake_%28Tanyu%29.jpg","title":"Marines near Mount Tanodake, 25 April 1945","desc":"A group of Marines gathered among stripped trees during the fighting on the Motobu Peninsula in northern Okinawa.","credit":"US Marine Corps photograph, Okinawa Prefectural Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1945-4-25_Battle_of_Mt._Tanodake_%28Tanyu%29.jpg","alt":"Helmeted soldiers, some with foliage tucked into their helmets, crowded together among bare, shell-damaged trees."},"wiki":"Battle of Okinawa"},
    "ww2-493": {"questions":["An order given on the night of 18 June 1945 disbanded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, turning them out of the hospital caves into the American encirclement, and most of their dead died afterwards.","The cenotaph to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands over the Ihara Third Surgical Cave, where some 80 people died when American troops attacked it early on 19 June 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Himeyuri students</b> were the girls and teachers of two Naha schools, the women’s department of the Okinawa Normal School and the First Prefectural Girls’ High School, mobilised as nurses in the Battle of Okinawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Short of men, the Japanese army drew pupils from all 21 of the island’s secondary and normal schools, girls mostly as nurses and boys as runners, carriers and repair parties, and over 2,000 pupils died in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mobilisation of girls had no legal basis; it was settled between the army and the prefecture, and the schools complied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the night of 23 March 1945, 222 students and 18 teachers left for the Okinawa Army Hospital at Haebaru, a hillside of nearly 40 caves fitted with crude bunks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There they emptied bedpans, fetched water and food under fire and buried the dead, living on one rice ball a day by the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Shuri fell the hospital was ordered south on 25 May, and on the night of 18 June the student corps was disbanded and its members sent out of the caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 136 mobilised students and teachers who died, 117 died or went missing after that order, which left them inside the American encirclement, and some 80 people died when the Ihara Third Surgical Cave was attacked the next morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taught that capture was shameful and told that Americans tore men apart and abused and killed women, many tried to die when surrounded, and some used grenades on themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were part of a far larger civilian catastrophe whose size depends on who counts it: American intelligence estimated about 42,000 civilians killed, while Okinawan reckonings put the island’s dead at about 120,000 of some 200,000 on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A cenotaph rose over the Ihara cave in 1946, and survivors opened the Himeyuri Peace Museum beside it in 1989, where 227 names are now remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Himeyuri Peace Museum, “ひめゆり学徒隊の沖縄戦,” https://www.himeyuri.or.jp/himeyuri/study_war/. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Himeyuri Peace Museum, “Frequently Asked Questions,” https://www.himeyuri.or.jp/en/qa-2/. [Open access]","古賀徳子, “沖縄戦の次世代への継承：ひめゆり平和祈念資料館の取り組み,” <i>生活協同組合研究</i> 535 (2020): 34–41, https://doi.org/10.57538/consumercoopstudies.535.0_34. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Roy E. Appleman, James M. Burns, Russell A. Gugeler and John Stevens, <i>Okinawa: The Last Battle</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), 57–58, 467–68, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Okinawa/USA-P-Okinawa-18.html. [Open access]","Okinawa Prefectural Peace Memorial Museum, “鉄の暴風―住民の見た沖縄戦,” https://www.peace-museum.okinawa.jp/heiwagakusyu/okinawasen_ni_tsuite/1/index.html. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Charles S. Nichols Jr. and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Okinawa: Victory in the Pacific</i> (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1955), 55–57, 258–60, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/USMC-M-Okinawa/USMC-M-Okinawa-11.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did most of the Himeyuri dead die after 18 June?","a":"The disbandment order turned the students out of the hospital caves just as American forces closed on the southern tip, so they were left among the shelling with nowhere to go; 117 of the 136 dead died or vanished after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did surrounded students try to kill themselves?","a":"They had been taught that dying for the country was natural and that being taken prisoner was disgraceful, and propaganda told them Americans would tear men apart and abuse and kill women; only when they saw that prisoners were not killed did survivors grieve for friends who had died days earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Okinawa’s civilian death tolls so different?","a":"American intelligence worked backwards from the bodies it counted and reckoned about 42,000 civilian dead, while Okinawan counts of the island’s own people, including conscripts, reach about 120,000; in the last 2 weeks of June alone, American troops saw 80,000 civilians, a third to a half of them wounded, come out of the caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Okinawa_Itoman_Himeyuri_Cenotaph_hdsr_VLux5_01.jpg/1920px-Okinawa_Itoman_Himeyuri_Cenotaph_hdsr_VLux5_01.jpg","title":"The Himeyuri Cenotaph at Itoman","desc":"The memorial with its lily relief stands above the mouth of the Ihara Third Surgical Cave in southern Okinawa.","credit":"Photograph by Hyppolyte de Saint-Rambert, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okinawa_Itoman_Himeyuri_Cenotaph_hdsr_VLux5_01.jpg","alt":"A white rectangular monument with a carved lily on one side, standing behind a fenced, plant-fringed hole in the rocky ground."},"wiki":"Himeyuri students"},
    "ww2-494": {"questions":["The Strategic Bombing Survey reckoned that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed about 330,000 people and left some 30 per cent of Japan’s urban population homeless in 9 months.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> switched to night fire raids in March 1945, the share of Japanese who expected defeat rose from 19 per cent to 68 per cent before the surrender."],"abstract":"The <b>air raids on Japan</b> were the American bombing of the Japanese home islands, carried out chiefly by B-29 Superfortresses of the Twentieth Air Force from June 1944 until the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raids from China, beginning against Yawata on 15 June 1944, dropped only about 800 tons on Japan and achieved little, and the campaign began in earnest when B-29s from the Marianas first struck Tokyo on 24 November 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those first attacks bombed aircraft-engine plants from about 9,100 metres (30,000 feet), and fewer than 10 per cent of the bombs fell in the target areas, though the threat forced a hasty dispersal of the industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arnold replaced the commander, Haywood Hansell, with Curtis LeMay, who took over on 20 January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the night of 9 to 10 March LeMay sent the bombers in low at night with incendiaries, and the fire raids burned out about 41 square kilometres (16 square miles) of Tokyo and within days the same tactics were used on Nagoya, Osaka and Kobe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In all B-29s dropped about 147,000 tons on Japan, roughly 104,000 of them on 66 urban areas, where some 40 per cent of the built-up area was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Strategic Bombing Survey estimated about 806,000 civilian casualties, about 330,000 of them deaths, a figure that includes the dead of the two atomic bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ordinary Japanese felt the bombing more than any battle: those who thought Japan could not win rose from 19 per cent in March 1945 to 68 per cent just before the surrender, and over half blamed the air raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Survey itself judged that blockade alone would have cut Japanese output by 40 to 50 per cent and that the war’s outcome was decided at sea and on the islands, though it called the bombing the main factor in the timing of surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians remain divided on whether the fire raids were a necessary shortcut to victory or a campaign against civilians, a debate their commander dismissed in his memoirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 10–11, 15–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 115, 566–67, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-18.html. [Open access]","William M. Leary, “The Strategic Air War against Japan,” in <i>Pearl to V-J Day: World War II in the Pacific</i>, ed. Jacob Neufeld, William T. Y’Blood and Mary Lee Jefferson (Washington, DC: Air Force History and Museums Program, 2000), 113–29, https://archive.org/details/PearlToVJDay. [Open access]","Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, “東京都における戦災の状況（東京）,” https://www.soumu.go.jp/main_sosiki/daijinkanbou/sensai/situation/state/kanto_11.html. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Seth Paridon, “Hellfire on Earth: Operation MEETINGHOUSE,” The National WWII Museum, 8 March 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/hellfire-earth-operation-meetinghouse. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Morale Division, <i>The Effects of Strategic Bombing on Japanese Morale</i> (Washington, DC, 1947), 106–10, https://archive.org/details/effectsofstrateg00unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the early B-29 raids achieve so little?","a":"From China the bombers could reach few targets and dropped only about 800 tons; from the Marianas they bombed from around 30,000 feet, where fewer than one bomb in ten fell in the target area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the campaign switch to burning cities?","a":"Precision raids on aircraft plants kept failing, while Japanese defences were weak at night and low altitude allowed far heavier bomb loads; LeMay ordered low-level night incendiary raids, and Tokyo, Nagoya, Osaka and Kobe were burned within 10 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians still argue about these raids?","a":"Supporters follow the Survey, which judged the bombing decisive for the timing of surrender, while critics see an assault on civilians; LeMay himself wrote that he had not worried about the morality of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Firebombing_of_Toyama.jpg/1920px-Firebombing_of_Toyama.jpg","title":"Toyama burning, 1 August 1945","desc":"An American aerial photograph of Toyama under incendiary attack, one of the smaller cities burned in the summer of 1945.","credit":"US Army Air Forces photograph, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firebombing_of_Toyama.jpg","alt":"A dark aerial view of a city with bright patches of fire and billowing smoke spreading across it."},"wiki":"Air raids on Japan"},
    "ww2-495": {"questions":["Police records counted 83,793 dead after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while later estimates run higher, and the Strategic Bombing Survey put the raid’s casualties at about 185,000.","Curtis LeMay sent his bombers in at night at low altitude, mostly without ammunition, for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, telling Arnold of the plan only the day before the mission."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Meetinghouse</b> was the American incendiary raid on Tokyo on the night of 9 to 10 March 1945, a raid that no later attack on a Japanese city equalled in destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some 334 B-29s took off from Guam, Saipan and Tinian on the evening of 9 March, and about 279 reached the target, a crowded district of eastern Tokyo full of small workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They came in at between 1,500 and 2,800 metres (4,900 and 9,200 feet), most without ammunition for their guns, and pathfinders marked the area with napalm bombs a few minutes after midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A strong wind drove the fires together, and within half an hour the fire chief judged the situation out of control; 95 fire engines and 125 firemen were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By morning about 41 square kilometres (15.8 square miles) had burned, including 63 per cent of Tokyo’s commercial area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Police records listed 267,171 buildings destroyed, 1,008,005 people homeless and 83,793 dead, and the Japanese government still gives about 84,000 dead from that police history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other estimates run higher: an American museum historian writes of about 110,000 dead, and the Strategic Bombing Survey counted about 185,000 casualties, dead and injured together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many died trying to run through the flames or sheltering by water, and Tokyo’s own civil defence, built on neighbourhood bucket teams and hand pumps, could not cope with a raid of that weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The raid cost 14 bombers, and within 10 days the same tactics had burned large parts of Nagoya, Osaka and Kobe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For LeMay it vindicated the change from daylight precision bombing; for historians who have taken issue with his view it marked a campaign aimed at cities and the civilians in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]","Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, “東京都における戦災の状況（東京）,” https://www.soumu.go.jp/main_sosiki/daijinkanbou/sensai/situation/state/kanto_11.html. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Seth Paridon, “Hellfire on Earth: Operation MEETINGHOUSE,” The National WWII Museum, 8 March 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/hellfire-earth-operation-meetinghouse. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 10–11, 15–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Civilian Defense Division, <i>Final Report Covering Air-Raid Protection and Allied Subjects in Japan</i> (Washington, DC, 1947), 29–31, https://archive.org/details/finalreportcover00unit. [Open access]","William M. Leary, “The Strategic Air War against Japan,” in <i>Pearl to V-J Day: World War II in the Pacific</i>, ed. Jacob Neufeld, William T. Y’Blood and Mary Lee Jefferson (Washington, DC: Air Force History and Museums Program, 2000), 113–29, https://archive.org/details/PearlToVJDay. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the raid so much deadlier than earlier ones?","a":"It struck a densely built wooden district at night from low altitude with incendiaries, in a strong wind; the fires merged within half an hour, overwhelmed the fire service and trapped people who tried to escape through them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the death tolls differ so much?","a":"Police counted 83,793 bodies, the figure Japan’s government still cites at about 84,000, but bodies were lost in the fires and rivers; others estimate about 110,000 dead, and the Survey gave about 185,000 dead and injured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did LeMay send the bombers in low and largely unarmed?","a":"He judged Japanese flak and night fighters weak, and low altitude saved fuel and engine strain so each aircraft could carry about 6 tons of bombs; leaving out ammunition added still more bomb weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Bombing_of_Tokyo%2C_Photo_by_Ishikawa%2C_01.jpg/1920px-Bombing_of_Tokyo%2C_Photo_by_Ishikawa%2C_01.jpg","title":"Tokyo after the March 1945 raid","desc":"A man hauls a laden cart through the ruins of a burned-out Tokyo street; the photographer, Ishikawa Kōyō, recorded the city after the raids.","credit":"Photograph by Ishikawa Kōyō, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bombing_of_Tokyo%2C_Photo_by_Ishikawa%2C_01.jpg","alt":"A man pulling a two-wheeled cart piled with belongings along a street of rubble, with burnt tree trunks and bicycles nearby."},"wiki":"Bombing of Tokyo (10 March 1945)"},
    "ww2-496": {"questions":["After commanding XX Bomber Command in India and China, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took over the Marianas bombers and ordered the night incendiary raid on Tokyo of 9 to 10 March 1945.","The general who led Strategic Air Command for almost 9 years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran for vice-president beside George Wallace in 1968 and later said he despised Wallace’s policies."],"abstract":"<b>Curtis LeMay</b> was the American air commander who directed the fire-bombing of Japan’s cities in 1945 and went on to build Strategic Air Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Born in Columbus, Ohio, on 15 November 1906, he made his name as a bomber commander in Europe before taking over XX Bomber Command in India and China on 29 August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1945 Arnold decided to replace Haywood Hansell in the Marianas, and LeMay took command of XXI Bomber Command on 20 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At first his daylight precision raids did no better: an eighth attack on the Nakajima engine works at Musashino on 4 March again failed, and most bombs fell on the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> LeMay then decided, on his own authority and with Arnold told only the day before, to send the B-29s over Tokyo at night, low, with incendiaries and largely without ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The raid of 9 to 10 March burned out about 41 square kilometres (15.8 square miles) of Tokyo, and the official toll was 83,793 dead, a figure Japan’s government still cites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He sent the fire raids on to Nagoya, Osaka and Kobe within 10 days, with losses of under 1 per cent of crews, and the official air force history called him one of the greatest operational air commanders of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his 1965 memoirs he wrote that he had not worried about the morality of the bombing, and historians have increasingly disputed that view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He commanded Strategic Air Command from 1948 to 1957, opposed giving missiles priority over bombers, and became Air Force chief of staff before retiring in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1968 he ran as George Wallace’s vice-presidential candidate, a decision an admiring profile calls a fatal error for his reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 115, 566–67, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-18.html. [Open access]","William M. Leary, “The Strategic Air War against Japan,” in <i>Pearl to V-J Day: World War II in the Pacific</i>, ed. Jacob Neufeld, William T. Y’Blood and Mary Lee Jefferson (Washington, DC: Air Force History and Museums Program, 2000), 113–29, https://archive.org/details/PearlToVJDay. [Open access]","Walter J. Boyne, “LeMay,” <i>Air Force Magazine</i>, 1 March 1998, https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/0398lemay/. [Open access]","Lawrence J. Spinetta, “White vs. LeMay: The Battle Over Ballistic Missiles,” <i>Air Force Magazine</i>, 1 January 2013, https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/0113lemay/. [Open access]","Daniel L. Haulman, “3 Airmen Who Sought—But Never Won—the Presidency,” <i>Air & Space Forces Magazine</i>, 30 November 2023, https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/3-airmen-who-sought-but-never-won-the-presidency/. [Open access]","Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, “東京都における戦災の状況（東京）,” https://www.soumu.go.jp/main_sosiki/daijinkanbou/sensai/situation/state/kanto_11.html. [in Japanese] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was LeMay sent to the Marianas?","a":"Hansell’s precision campaign against the aircraft industry was producing poor results, Arnold wanted a commander who would get results from the B-29, and LeMay had a record as an aggressive and effective group commander in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his change of tactics work?","a":"Japanese night fighters and flak were weak at low altitude by night, so the bombers could fly singly, carry about 6 tons each and saturate cities of wooden houses with incendiaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his record so disputed?","a":"His admirers credit him with shortening the war and later deterring the Soviet Union, while historians have increasingly questioned his blunt view that the morality of burning cities was not his concern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Curtis_E._LeMay_and_Roger_Ramey.webp","title":"LeMay and Ramey, January 1945","desc":"Major General Curtis LeMay, left, with Brigadier General Roger Ramey shortly before LeMay left to take command of XXI Bomber Command.","credit":"US Army Air Forces photograph, National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curtis_E._LeMay_and_Roger_Ramey.webp","alt":"Two officers in uniform seated at a desk looking over papers, one with a pipe, a flag in the background."},"wiki":"Curtis LeMay"},
    "ww2-497": {"questions":["On 11 September 1940 the Home Ministry advised every neighbourhood in Japan to form a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for mutual aid, and the groups soon handled rationing and air-raid duties too.","In 1943 the government chose each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the last link for Imperial Rule Assistance Association propaganda, but leaders swamped by rationing work passed little of it on."],"abstract":"<b>Tonarigumi</b> were Japan’s neighbourhood associations, small groups of adjoining households that the wartime state used for mutual aid, rationing, savings drives, propaganda and civil defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Japanese writer of 1943 traced them to the five-family groups of the Tokugawa period and dated their modern form to Tokyo after the earthquake of 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mayor of Tokyo proposed a city-wide network in 1939, and on 11 September 1940 the Home Ministry advised every neighbourhood in the country to form one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each group of 10 to 20 households met monthly under an elected leader, with one member of every family named for air-raid duty, and living in the area was enough to make membership compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japanese writers in wartime presented them as voluntary bodies of neighbourly cooperation, while American investigators after the war stressed that residents had no choice and that the police used the groups to watch every household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From about 1938 the prefectures turned the groups into agencies of air defence, so that in a raid the nearest neighbours were to fight fires and give first aid before the fire brigade arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were meant to have two-man hand pumps, but materials ran short and many relied on buckets, fire beaters and crocks of standing water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alongside them the authorities moved children out of the cities, and in June 1945 some 142,000 Tokyo schoolchildren were still evacuated, while about 55,000 houses had been pulled down for firebreaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Against the great fire raids such defence was hopeless: on the night of 9 to 10 March 1945 Tokyo’s fire chief judged the fires out of control within half an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1943 the government also made the groups the outlet for Imperial Rule Assistance Association propaganda, but their leaders, swamped by rationing and other duties, passed it on with little effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Shigeto Hozumi, “The Tonari-gumi of Japan,” <i>Contemporary Japan</i> 12, no. 8 (August 1943): 984–87, https://archive.org/details/contemporary-japan-1943.8. [Open access]","Kennosuke Sato, “How the Tonari-gumi Operates,” <i>Contemporary Japan</i> 13, nos. 7–9 (July–September 1944): 778–79, https://archive.org/details/contemporary-japan-1944.7-9. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Civilian Defense Division, <i>Final Report Covering Air-Raid Protection and Allied Subjects in Japan</i> (Washington, DC, 1947), 29–31, https://archive.org/details/finalreportcover00unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Morale Division, <i>The Effects of Strategic Bombing on Japanese Morale</i> (Washington, DC, 1947), 106–10, https://archive.org/details/effectsofstrateg00unit. [Open access]","Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, “東京都における戦災の状況（東京）,” https://www.soumu.go.jp/main_sosiki/daijinkanbou/sensai/situation/state/kanto_11.html. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the state turn neighbourhood groups into air-raid defence?","a":"They already reached every household, could put a leader and helpers on the scene of a fire within minutes, and gave a channel for passing training and equipment down from the city and prefecture to each home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do wartime and post-war accounts of them differ?","a":"Japanese writers in 1943 and 1944 described the groups as voluntary and neighbourly, while the American survey after the war found that residence made membership compulsory and that the police used them to control and scrutinise people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did they fail against the fire raids?","a":"They had buckets and few pumps, and raids of saturation weight started more fires at once than any neighbourhood could fight; in Tokyo on 10 March the fires were out of control within half an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Bucket_brigade_in_fire_drill_of_Tonarigumi%2C_The_neighborhood_mutual-aid_association%2C_1941_Japan.jpg","title":"A tonarigumi fire drill, 1941","desc":"Members of a neighbourhood association pass buckets along a narrow street during a fire drill.","credit":"Photograph by Umemoto Tadao, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucket_brigade_in_fire_drill_of_Tonarigumi%2C_The_neighborhood_mutual-aid_association%2C_1941_Japan.jpg","alt":"People in aprons and headscarves standing in a line along a narrow lane between wooden houses, passing buckets towards a water barrel."},"wiki":"Tonarigumi"},
    "ww2-498": {"questions":["Tojo held Japan’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> himself alongside the premiership and the war ministry, while its Air Ordnance Bureau tried to take aircraft production out of the services’ hands.","An American survey judged that Japan’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hamstrung by army and navy officers in its own offices and became little more than a bureau for civilian goods."],"abstract":"The <b>Ministry of Munitions</b> was the Japanese ministry set up on 1 November 1943 to direct the war economy as a whole and, above all, the production of aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Until then plans were drawn up by a planning board and carried out by separate ministries and by the army and navy, whose clashing demands produced what an American survey called a chaotic situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the defeats of late 1942 Tojo won emergency economic powers, approved on 17 March 1943, and in November the new ministry took control of industry from half of the old Ministry of Commerce and Industry, with a Total Mobilization Bureau in place of the Cabinet Planning Board.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under a Munitions Company Act, vital firms became designated munitions companies under its direction, and the big business houses kept their influence through that act and through the men chosen to run the ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tojo himself served as minister of munitions, as well as prime minister and war minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its Air Ordnance Bureau was to bring planning, equipment and materials for aircraft under one head, but the army and navy kept their own supervisors in the factories and often kept their plans from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A production drive still demanded 2.16 times the September 1943 output of planes and engines by June 1944, and aircraft output reached its highest levels that spring and summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Engines became the bottleneck from April 1944, and the spring push stripped stocks of parts and raw materials that could not be replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From November 1944 the B-29s went after the engine works, though their eighth attack on Nakajima’s Musashino plant, on 4 March 1945, was another failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ministry never broke the deadlock between the services’ demands and civilian needs, and in the American survey’s verdict it became little more than an office handling goods for industries such as machine tools and chemicals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Over-all Economic Effects Division, <i>The Effects of Strategic Bombing on Japan’s War Economy</i> (Washington, DC, 1946), 21–23, https://archive.org/details/effectsofstrateg53unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Aircraft Division, <i>The Japanese Aircraft Industry</i> (Washington, DC, 1947), https://archive.org/details/the-japanese-aircraft-industry-ussbs. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]","William M. Leary, “The Strategic Air War against Japan,” in <i>Pearl to V-J Day: World War II in the Pacific</i>, ed. Jacob Neufeld, William T. Y’Blood and Mary Lee Jefferson (Washington, DC: Air Force History and Museums Program, 2000), 113–29, https://archive.org/details/PearlToVJDay. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i> (Tokyo, 1948), 952–53, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a Ministry of Munitions created in 1943?","a":"Rivalry between the army and navy over materials, tools and labour threatened the expansion of aircraft production, and the separate ministries had no single centre of authority, so the government tried to put production under one ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the ministry fail to take control of production?","a":"The army and navy kept their own supervisors in the factories, placed officers in the ministry who were moved on frequently, and would not cut their demands for materials, so its materials plan was practically nullified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did aircraft output fall after 1944 despite the ministry?","a":"Engine supply fell short from April 1944, the spring production drive used up stocks that could not be replaced, and from late 1944 the B-29s attacked the engine works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Open_the_gunjusho_1943.png","title":"The new ministry’s signboard","desc":"A signboard reading 軍需省, Ministry of Munitions, is put up at the ministry’s opening, as shown in the government’s photographic weekly.","credit":"Cabinet Information Bureau, <i>Shashin Shūhō</i> no. 297, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Open_the_gunjusho_1943.png","alt":"A man in a suit fixing a tall wooden signboard with large Japanese characters beside a doorway."},"wiki":"Ministry of Munitions (Japan)"},
    "ww2-499": {"questions":["After Leyte Gulf, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 26 October 1944 told listeners of attacks on American transports and warships, when in fact the Combined Fleet had been shattered.","A president of the Board of Information admitted that each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay outside his board’s control, and that some of these war bulletins were simply not true."],"abstract":"An <b>Imperial General Headquarters Announcement</b> was an official war communiqué of Japan’s supreme command, read out on the radio and printed by newspapers that were required to carry service releases exactly as given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Board of Information, created in 1940 to coordinate government publicity, had no control over these announcements and simply had to pass them on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its president Akabane later told American interviewers that the board transmitted them as true, that sometimes they were not, and that this was very embarrassing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rivalry between the services made things worse, since each gave the board false estimates of its chances, which may explain the run of decisive battles announced from Leyte to Okinawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Defeats were withheld from the public or disguised as strategic withdrawals, and the commanders themselves were misled by their own inflated claims of heavy ships sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> When Saipan fell, the announcement of 18 July 1944 said that the garrison had died to a man and that the Japanese residents had largely shared its fate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The announcement of 26 October, after Leyte Gulf, spoke of air and naval attacks on the American fleet when the Combined Fleet had been destroyed, and one young listener who secretly heard American broadcasts could not tell which version was true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Propaganda of the same years told civilians that Americans would tear men apart and abuse and kill women, and surviving Himeyuri students said it made them prefer death to capture, some even using grenades on themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Radio Tokyo called the fire raid of 10 March 1945 ‘slaughter bombing’ and compared the destruction with Nero’s burning of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Saipan’s fall could not be hidden, and as defeats and air raids mounted Americans found growing distrust of government news, with 68 per cent of Japanese expecting defeat just before the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Morale Division, <i>The Effects of Strategic Bombing on Japanese Morale</i> (Washington, DC, 1947), 106–10, https://archive.org/details/effectsofstrateg00unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 10–11, 15–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","大森淳郎, “シリーズ 戦争とラジオ〈第7回〉敗戦への道：負け戦はどう伝えられたのか,” <i>放送研究と調査</i> 71, no. 3 (2021): 58–89, https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/bunken/71/3/71_58/_pdf/-char/ja. [in Japanese] [Open access]","古賀徳子, “沖縄戦の次世代への継承：ひめゆり平和祈念資料館の取り組み,” <i>生活協同組合研究</i> 535 (2020): 34–41, https://doi.org/10.57538/consumercoopstudies.535.0_34. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 608–23, 631–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]","Himeyuri Peace Museum, “ひめゆり学徒隊の沖縄戦,” https://www.himeyuri.or.jp/himeyuri/study_war/. [in Japanese] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Board of Information not correct these bulletins?","a":"The announcements came from the supreme command, outside the board’s province; it lacked a voice in high policy and had to accept the army and navy statements and try to make them look consistent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bulletins exaggerate successes?","a":"Each service, to save face, gave false estimates of its prospects, and pilots’ claims of heavy ships sunk were believed even by commanders, so inflated results reached the public as fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did people stop believing them?","a":"The fall of Saipan could not be concealed, air raids crossed the whole country unopposed, and some listeners secretly heard American broadcasts that told a different story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Dai-hon-ei_Happyou_to_annouce_Japanese_military_victory_in_the_battle_of_Midway.png","title":"Announcing the results of Midway, June 1942","desc":"The navy’s spokesman Captain Hiraide Hideo reads Imperial General Headquarters’ version of the Battle of Midway to the press.","credit":"Photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dai-hon-ei_Happyou_to_annouce_Japanese_military_victory_in_the_battle_of_Midway.png","alt":"A uniformed officer speaking into a microphone at a crowded table of men taking notes, others standing behind."}},
    "ww2-500": {"questions":["Language schools, shrine visits and favours for families who took Japanese names were part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which aimed to make colonial subjects think and act as Japanese.","Koreans swore an oath that Japan and Korea were one body in mass meetings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the Cairo Declaration of 1943 spoke of the enslavement of Korea’s people."],"abstract":"The <b>Kominka movement</b>, from the Japanese for ‘making imperial subjects’, was the campaign of the war years to turn the peoples of Japan’s colonies, Korea and Taiwan, into loyal Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Korea officials proclaimed Japan and Korea to be one body, and Koreans swore that oath in Japanese at mass meetings while being urged to volunteer for the war in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Taiwan an American wartime handbook described a programme of language schools, youth clubs and village societies, and favours granted to families who took Japanese ways or names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1939 there were over 6,000 Japanese-language schools in Taiwan, and official statistics claimed that 45.59 per cent of Taiwanese understood Japanese, though the handbook doubted that most had a working knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same handbook called the programme inconsistent, since it asked people to think and act as Japanese while keeping them subservient in economic, political and social life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The war gave the movement a military purpose: the army introduced special volunteers in Taiwan in April 1942, and youth leagues, reorganised under the Imperial Subjects’ Public Service Association in 1943, pressed young men to enlist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the first intake 425,921 Taiwanese applied and 1,020 were admitted, and the next year 601,147 applied for 1,030 places, figures the authorities presented as proof of loyalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Korea conscription had begun by 1944 to drain men from the villages, and Koreans also served as labourers with Japanese garrisons, 600 of them in the Kerama Islands off Okinawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Allies saw it differently: at Cairo in 1943 they declared Taiwan would return to China and that, mindful of the enslavement of the Korean people, Korea would in due course be free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Japanese historian has noted that because Korea was a colony rather than an enemy, its wartime story, from name changes to forced recruitment, long drew less attention than the war with China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew J. Grajdanzev, <i>Modern Korea</i> (New York: Institute of Pacific Relations, 1944), 5, 69–70, 121, 282–83, https://archive.org/details/modernkorea031283mbp. [Open access]","Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, <i>Civil Affairs Handbook: Taiwan (Formosa), Taichu Province</i>, OPNAV 13-26 (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 1944), 132–33, https://archive.org/details/OPNAV13-26. [Open access]","宮崎聖子, “日本植民地下の台湾における青年団：1940-43年の制度面を中心に,” <i>日本の教育史学</i> 50 (2007): 110–22, https://doi.org/10.15062/kyouikushigaku.50.0_110. [in Japanese] [Open access]","Fumitaka Kurosawa, “Reconsideration of Post-War Understanding of Modern Japanese History: An Examination of War and Colonial Rule of Imperial Japan,” in <i>2012 International Forum on War History: Proceedings</i> (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 39–40, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2012/03.pdf. [Open access]","“Cairo Communiqué, December 1, 1943,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/cairo.asp. [Open access]","Roy E. Appleman, James M. Burns, Russell A. Gugeler and John Stevens, <i>Okinawa: The Last Battle</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), 57–58, 467–68, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Okinawa/USA-P-Okinawa-18.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan push assimilation so hard during the war?","a":"The war needed colonial manpower: the army opened special volunteer schemes in Taiwan from 1942, and youth leagues and schools prepared young men to serve as soldiers while language and shrine programmes were to make them loyal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should huge volunteer numbers be read with caution?","a":"The authorities publicised the 425,921 applications of 1942 as proof of loyalty, but the youth leagues that recruited them were organisations for controlling young people and pressing them to enlist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the movement self-contradictory?","a":"A wartime American study noted that it asked Taiwanese to think and act as Japanese while keeping them in a subservient economic, political and social position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Japanese_Name_Change_Bulletin_of_Taikyu_Court.jpg/1920px-Japanese_Name_Change_Bulletin_of_Taikyu_Court.jpg","title":"A name-change notice, Taikyu, 1940","desc":"A notice of the Taikyu (Daegu) district court in Korea, dated 10 August 1940, urging families to register Japanese-style surnames before the deadline.","credit":"Taikyu District Court, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Name_Change_Bulletin_of_Taikyu_Court.jpg","alt":"A printed poster in red and black Japanese and Korean script with bold headings at either side."}},
    "ww2-541": {"questions":["Begun on the first day of the war with the sinking of the liner Athenia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted until the German surrender in May 1945 and cost both sides heavily.","German reckoning put the cost of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 32,000 submariners from 781 U-boats, while the U-boats and Italian submarines sank 2,828 merchant ships."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Atlantic</b> was the struggle between German U-boats, aircraft and raiders and the Allied navies and air forces over the shipping on which Britain depended, fought above all in the Western Approaches west of Britain and Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began at once: on 3 September 1939, the day war was declared, U-30 sank the liner Athenia without warning against Hitler's orders, and Germany never admitted the sinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill, as First Lord of the Admiralty, told the Commons that month that Britain had replied with convoy, the arming of merchant ships and a hunt for the U-boats, though convoy was no complete defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German navy's aim was to cut the sea route from America to Britain, and its war on the convoys caused heavy British losses and temporary supply crises in 1940 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 6 March 1941 Churchill issued his Battle of the Atlantic directive, ordering that the U-boat at sea be hunted and the Focke-Wulf bombers attacked in the air and in their nests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The campaign ran for the whole European war, and it turned in May 1943, when Dönitz broke off the convoy battle in the North Atlantic on 24 May after losing more than 30 U-boats in 3 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Between 1939 and 1945 Germany commissioned 1,162 U-boats, and the British official history counts 784 of them lost, most of those sunk at sea by British or British-controlled forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German figures given by the American official historian put the cost at 32,000 submariners from 781 boats, while a German museum gives just under 27,000 crewmen killed, so the toll is a range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the other side the U-boats and Italian submarines sank 2,828 Allied and neutral merchant ships of 14,687,231 tons, and the British merchant navy alone lost 29,994 men to enemy action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Nuremberg the tribunal called the U-boat arm the principal part of the German fleet and found that the real damage to the enemy had been done almost entirely by its submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 91, 103, 363–64, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, part 2, <i>1st June 1944–14th August 1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1961), 304–5, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-2. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 362–63, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “U-Boat Warfare,” statement by the First Lord of the Admiralty, Winston Churchill, <i>Hansard</i>, 26 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 1239–46, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/26/u-boat-warfare. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Doenitz,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1946), 82–83, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was most of the battle fought west of Britain and Ireland?","a":"Because so much of Britain's shipping came and went through those focal waters that the enemy concentrated his attack there, which is why the official history places the struggle largely in the Western Approaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany fight its naval war mainly with submarines?","a":"Because the U-boat arm was the principal part of its fleet: the Nuremberg tribunal found that the surface ships made only a few raids, and the German museum account makes the cutting of Britain's sea routes the navy's main task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the German losses given as a range?","a":"Because the counts differ: the German figures reported by the American official historian give 32,000 submariners from 781 boats, while the Deutsches Historisches Museum gives just under 27,000 dead from about 780 boats sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/An_aerial_view_of_a_convoy_in_the_Atlantic%2C_1941._Two_escorts_can_be_seen_in_the_foreground._C2647.jpg","title":"An Atlantic convoy, 1941","desc":"An Atlantic convoy seen from the air in 1941, with two escorts in the foreground.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_aerial_view_of_a_convoy_in_the_Atlantic,_1941._Two_escorts_can_be_seen_in_the_foreground._C2647.jpg","alt":"Rows of merchant ships spread across a grey sea seen from above, with two smaller warships nearer the camera."},"wiki":"Battle of the Atlantic"},
    "ww2-542": {"questions":["Short of escorts when the U-boats reached American waters in 1942, the US Navy delayed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its own coast and moved ships between anchorages by day instead.","Revived in 1917 after a century out of favour, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was restarted by the Admiralty within days of the outbreak of war in September 1939."],"abstract":"The <b>convoy system</b> sailed merchant ships in organised groups under naval escort, a convoy being defined in the British official history as one or more merchant ships protected by one or more warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The practice was centuries old, fell out of favour after the Napoleonic Wars and was revived in 1917 and 1918, when it did more than anything else to defeat German unrestricted submarine warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In September 1939 the Admiralty started it at once: east coast convoys began on 6 September, outward ocean convoys on the 7th, and the first Halifax convoy, HX 1, sailed on the 16th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ships faster than 15 knots or slower than nine sailed alone and made easy targets, while of 5,756 ships convoyed by the end of 1939 only four were sunk by submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 26 September that convoy was a good and well-tried defence but not a complete one, and that a steady proportion of losses must be expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The old objection was delay, since a convoy moves at its slowest ship's speed, but the official history judged that losses among independents were so much higher that wider convoy would have saved tonnage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dönitz testified that from the start he ordered his U-boats to attack all merchant ships in convoy, and that his pack attacks were a tactic against formations to which the prize rules could not apply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the U-boats reached the American coast in 1942 there were too few escorts, so from 1 April ships moved between anchorages by day, and an interlocking convoy system began only in late August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In March 1943 the Admiralty feared that convoy, by then the linchpin of British maritime strategy, could no longer be regarded as an effective defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What saved it, the official history says, was the arrival of support groups, escort carriers and very long range aircraft, and on the Gibraltar route 2,200 ships crossed from May to December 1943 with only one lost to U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. VI, “The Sea Approaches and Coastal Waters, 3rd September–31st December, 1939,” 91–95, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-I/UK-RN-I-6.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. XIV, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Triumph of the Escorts, 1st January–31st May, 1943,” 367–68, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “U-Boat Warfare,” statement by the First Lord of the Admiralty, Winston Churchill, <i>Hansard</i>, 26 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 1239–46, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/26/u-boat-warfare. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 17–18, 254–55, 260, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 13, proceedings of 8 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, 248–50, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-08-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Doenitz,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1946), 55–56, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did convoy cost less shipping time than its critics feared?","a":"Because losses among independently routed ships were so much higher that, by the official history's reckoning, wider convoy would have saved tonnage, and independent ships diverted round danger often made slower passages anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were convoys on the American coast started so late?","a":"Because there were not enough escorts, and a convoy without adequate protection was judged worse than none, so from 1 April 1942 ships moved by day between anchorages until the interlocking system began at the end of August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Admiralty doubt convoy in March 1943?","a":"Because that month's losses were so heavy that it feared convoy could no longer be regarded as an effective defence; the support groups, escort carriers and long-range aircraft that arrived next turned the tables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/A_convoy_conference_in_progress%2C_August_1942._A11796.jpg","title":"A convoy conference, August 1942","desc":"Masters and naval officers at a convoy conference before sailing, August 1942.","credit":"R. G. G. Coote, Royal Navy official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_convoy_conference_in_progress,_August_1942._A11796.jpg","alt":"A room of seated men in merchant and naval uniforms listening to an officer briefing them at the front."}},
    "ww2-543": {"questions":["Fitted after the losses of 1943 with the Schnorkel mast copied from captured Dutch boats, a German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could charge its batteries at periscope depth instead of surfacing under Allied radar.","Only about 30 to 40 boats were operational in 1939, Dönitz testified, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arm could at first inflict mere pinpricks on a great naval power like Britain."],"abstract":"The <b>U-boat</b> was the German navy's submarine, and in the Second World War it was the main weapon of Germany's attempt to cut the sea routes carrying supplies and war materials to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany began building a modern U-boat arm in 1936 and had 57 boats under Karl Dönitz in 1939, few in number, the Nuremberg tribunal judged, but fully prepared for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Dönitz told the Nuremberg tribunal that only about 30 to 40 were operational, enough for mere pinpricks against a great naval power, and that the U-boat war really began in 1942, when boats ordered at the outbreak were ready.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The workhorse was the Type VIIC, which American naval intelligence credited with 17.7 knots on the surface but only 7.6 submerged, five torpedo tubes and 14 torpedoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it was so slow underwater, such a boat closed a convoy on the surface after dark, fired its torpedoes and dived only to escape the counter-attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That dependence on the surface grew fatal once Allied aircraft carried radar, and only after the heavy losses of April and May 1943 did Germany take up the Schnorkel, a mast that let a boat charge its batteries at periscope depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The device had been found in captured Dutch submarines in 1940, and by mid-1944 about 30 operational boats had it, though it slowed them underwater and made their commanders more defensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dönitz pinned his hopes on the Type XXI, a streamlined boat with far larger batteries and short bursts of 17 knots submerged, but bombing, shortages and defects left so few ready that it never changed the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1943 Hitler gave U-boat building priority over big ships, and between 1939 and 1945 Germany commissioned 1,162 U-boats, of which 784 were lost, by the British official count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> German figures cited by the American official historian give 32,000 submariners lost from 781 boats, while the Deutsches Historisches Museum puts the dead at just under 27,000, most lost after May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 25–26, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 338–39, 362–63, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, part 1, <i>1st June 1943–31st May 1944</i> (London: HMSO, 1960), 17–18, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-1. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, part 2, <i>1st June 1944–14th August 1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1961), 304–5, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-2. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 13, proceedings of 8 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, 248–50, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-08-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Doenitz,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did U-boats attack on the surface rather than submerged?","a":"Because a Type VIIC was more than twice as fast on the surface as underwater, so it closed a convoy surfaced at night, fired, and dived only to escape the counter-attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Schnorkel adopted so late?","a":"Because the Germans attached little importance to the device they found in captured Dutch submarines in 1940 until radar-equipped aircraft made surfacing deadly and the losses of April and May 1943 forced the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the U-boat war start slowly?","a":"Because, as Dönitz testified, Germany had only 30 to 40 operational boats in 1939 and the large building programme began only when war came, so the boats then ordered were not ready until 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/U-210_PA-037443.jpg","title":"U-210 under attack, 6 August 1942","desc":"The Type VIIC submarine U-210 running at full speed on the surface while trying to escape the Canadian destroyer HMCS Assiniboine, 6 August 1942.","credit":"G. E. Salter, Canadian Department of National Defence, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U-210_PA-037443.jpg","alt":"A low grey submarine with a conning tower cutting through the sea on the surface, seen from a pursuing ship."},"wiki":"U-boat"},
    "ww2-544": {"questions":["The Nuremberg tribunal found <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guilty of war crimes for ordering neutral ships sunk without warning in operational zones, yet did not assess his 10-year sentence on those breaches.","Before the war <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> estimated that 300 U-boats would be needed for decisive results against a world-wide convoy system, yet he began the war with a few dozen operational boats."],"abstract":"<b>Karl Dönitz</b> was the German admiral who rebuilt and commanded the U-boat arm, led the German navy from January 1943 and succeeded Hitler as head of state in the last days of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born near Berlin on 16 September 1891, he joined the navy in 1910, went into submarines in 1916 and was captured by the British in October 1918 as commander of a U-boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1935 he rebuilt the U-boat arm, and as its commander from 1936 he trained it in night attacks on the surface and in attacks by groups of boats, the tactic later called the wolf pack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the war he had estimated that 300 U-boats would be needed for decisive results if Britain adopted convoy, yet in 1939 he had 57 boats, of which only about 30 to 40 were operational.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His principle, he said, was to be as strong as possible in the right place at the right time, with his boats directed by radio from his headquarters at Lorient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 30 January 1943 Hitler made him Commander-in-Chief of the navy in place of Raeder, and the tribunal heard that the two conferred on naval problems about 120 times during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After losing more than 30 U-boats in the first 3 weeks of May 1943 he broke off the convoy battle in the North Atlantic on 24 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Named in Hitler's testament, he took power on 1 May 1945, ordered the war in the east to go on, and from Flensburg managed a staged surrender until British forces arrested his government on 23 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal acquitted him of conspiracy and convicted him of waging aggressive war and of war crimes, among them sinking neutral ships without warning in operational zones, though it did not assess his sentence on those breaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sentenced to 10 years, he left Spandau prison in 1956, wrote memoirs that painted him as a soldier apart from the regime, and died on 24 December 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Manfred Wichmann, “Karl Dönitz 1891–1980,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/karl-doenitz. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Doenitz,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 13, proceedings of 8 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, 248–50, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-08-46.asp. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 131, 354–56, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 25–26, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tribunal not punish him for his submarine warfare?","a":"Because, in view of a British order of May 1940 to sink all ships at sight in the Skagerrak and of Admiral Nimitz's statement that the United States waged unrestricted submarine warfare in the Pacific from its first day of war, it did not assess his sentence on those breaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he favour attacks by groups of U-boats?","a":"Because, as he testified, submarines had always operated singly while every other kind of warship fought together, and a formation such as a convoy could only be attacked collectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler choose him to lead the navy in 1943?","a":"Because the U-boat arm was the principal part of the German fleet; the tribunal noted that Raeder had recommended him and that Hitler too knew submarine warfare was the essential part of Germany's naval war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1976-127-06A%2C_Karl_D%C3%B6nitz.jpg","title":"Karl Dönitz, 1943","desc":"Karl Dönitz in uniform, photographed in April 1943, three months after he became Commander-in-Chief of the German navy.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1976-127-06A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1976-127-06A,_Karl_Dönitz.jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders portrait of a thin-faced man in a dark naval uniform with a peaked cap and a cross at his collar."},"wiki":"Karl Dönitz"},
    "ww2-545": {"questions":["Dönitz told the Nuremberg tribunal that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tactic merely ended the principle of individual action, making U-boats fight collectively against convoys like any other warships.","The constant wireless reports a shadowing boat sent were the weakness of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since direction-finding stations and escorts could fix the sender's position and drive it off."],"abstract":"The <b>wolfpack</b>, in German Rudeltaktik, was the U-boat tactic of concentrating several submarines against one convoy and attacking it together, usually on the surface at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dönitz told the Nuremberg tribunal that the submarines of all navies had so far operated singly, and that the tactic simply broke with that principle so that U-boats fought collectively like other warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had trained the U-boat arm in group attacks and night surface attacks from 1936, and in January 1940 he tried to send two more boats to join one that had found a French convoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The method needed a convoy's position and route to be fixed at U-boat headquarters at Lorient, after which the nearest boat shadowed it and homed the rest of the group on to it by wireless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Groups were usually of about eight or nine boats, sometimes up to twenty, and once gathered they closed on the surface after dark, fired, dived to escape and returned on successive nights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Packs were introduced gradually between October 1940 and March 1941, and in attacks on convoys SC 7 and HX 79 that October seventeen and fourteen ships were sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tactic caught the British unprepared, because their asdic, built to find a submerged submarine, could not detect a surfaced one, and their escort groups had been broken up by the losses of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its weakness lay in its method, for the shadower could be driven off or sunk, and the frequent wireless signals it sent could be located by direction-finding stations and by suitably equipped escorts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Accounts of the control differ: an American official history has the boats closely directed by radio from Lorient, a German museum says the commanders kept full freedom of action, and the British history finds the first attempts too rigidly controlled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By May 1943 a pack meeting a well-escorted convoy faced carrier aircraft and support groups, and Dönitz's operations officer was not surprised when three of seven boats were lost against convoy HX 237.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 13, proceedings of 8 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, 248–50, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-08-46.asp. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 131, 350, 354–56, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 25–26, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Karl Dönitz 1891–1980,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/karl-doenitz. [in German] [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 76–79, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. XIV, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Triumph of the Escorts, 1st January–31st May, 1943,” 366, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Dönitz need packs rather than single boats?","a":"Because a convoy gathered its ships and escorts in one place, and Dönitz argued that such a formation could only be attacked collectively; the American history adds that the tactic answered good British sound gear and Germany's lack of air reconnaissance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the British unprepared for it?","a":"Because inter-war thinking assumed a submerged enemy: the asdic could not find a surfaced U-boat, and the losses of 1940 had broken the escort flotillas into groups of ships unfamiliar with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did pack attacks fail in May 1943?","a":"Because convoys now had escort carriers and support groups; against HX 237 the carrier's aircraft and the escorts sank three of a pack of seven, a loss that Dönitz's operations officer said did not surprise him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101II-MW-4260-37%2C_Lorient%2C_U-Boote_U-123_und_U-201_auslaufend.jpg","title":"U-123 and U-201 leaving Lorient, 1941","desc":"Two U-boats, U-123 and U-201, putting to sea from Lorient, the base from which Dönitz directed the packs, on 8 June 1941.","credit":"Kramer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101II-MW-4260-37, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101II-MW-4260-37,_Lorient,_U-Boote_U-123_und_U-201_auslaufend.jpg","alt":"Two submarines with crews on deck moving out of a harbour one behind the other."},"wiki":"Wolfpack (naval tactic)"},
    "ww2-546": {"questions":["Prien, Kretschmer and Endras won their fame as aces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1940, attacking lone ships, stragglers and weakly escorted convoys from new bases in western France.","The U-boats' slaughter off the American coast in 1942 was their second such period, recalling the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed the fall of France and the move to Biscay bases."],"abstract":"The <b>First Happy Time</b> was the name U-boat commanders later gave to the months from July to October 1940, when they sank Allied shipping at a rate they would enjoy only once more, off the American coast in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It followed the fall of France, and by August Brest, Lorient and La Pallice were in use as U-boat bases from which boats could cruise as far as 25° West, beyond British escorts and aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 17 August Hitler declared a total blockade of the British Isles and warned that neutral ships would be sunk at sight, and a German museum account links that unrestricted campaign to heavy British losses and supply shortages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dönitz still had few boats, and he later testified that in one month he had only two at sea, so that at first the U-boats could deliver only pinpricks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their victims were mostly ships sailing alone, weakly escorted convoys and stragglers, since outward convoys dispersed between 15° and 17° West and many ships still sailed independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In these 4 months, by the British official count, U-boats sank 144 unescorted and 73 escorted ships for the loss of only six boats, two of them in attacks on convoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the time of the aces, Prien, Kretschmer, Endras, Frauenheim and others, and against U-boats attacking on the surface at night the escorts' asdic was of little use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 70 per cent of sinkings came in night attacks by surfaced U-boats, and in October convoys SC 7 and HX 79 lost seventeen and fourteen ships as several boats began to attack together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American official history describes the U-boats moving out into the mid-Atlantic with this new technique late in 1940, when convoy HX 90 lost ten ships to attacks on 1 and 2 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The age of the lone aces ended in March 1941, when convoy escorts sank Prien's U-47 and Schepke's U-100 and captured Kretschmer from U-99.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 348–51, 354–55, 365, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. IV, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Campaign in American Waters, 1st January–31st July, 1942,” 94–96, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-4.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 24–26, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 13, proceedings of 8 May 1946, testimony of Karl Dönitz, 248–50, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-08-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the U-boats so successful in these months?","a":"Because from the Biscay ports they could reach far beyond British escorts and aircraft, and they found plenty of easy targets in ships sailing alone, stragglers and weakly escorted convoys that dispersed in mid-ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few U-boats achieve so much?","a":"Because the targets, not the boats, were plentiful: Dönitz testified that in one month he had only two boats at sea, and the British official history counts only six U-boats lost in 4 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the period end?","a":"Because convoys gained stronger escorts and the pack replaced the lone ace; in March 1941 convoy escorts killed Prien and Schepke and captured Kretschmer, and Britain's supply routes were never cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-2006-1130-500%2C_Kapit%C3%A4nleutnant_G%C3%BCnther_Prien.jpg","title":"Günther Prien, 1940","desc":"The U-boat commander Günther Prien of U-47, one of the aces of 1940, photographed that year.","credit":"Annelise Schulze, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-2006-1130-500, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-2006-1130-500,_Kapitänleutnant_Günther_Prien.jpg","alt":"A portrait of a young naval officer in a peaked cap and dark uniform with a cross at his collar."},"wiki":"First Happy Time"},
    "ww2-547": {"questions":["In January 1943 some 37 U-boats waited along the edge of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the convoys sailing between North America and Britain had no air cover.","At its first meeting in November 1942 the Anti-U-boat Warfare Committee named filling the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the first need, requiring some 40 long-range radar-fitted aircraft."],"abstract":"The <b>mid-Atlantic gap</b> was the stretch of the North Atlantic convoy routes beyond the reach of land-based aircraft, which the British called the air gap or Greenland air gap and the Americans the 'black pit'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1941 there was also a gap in surface escort, between the point where Canadian escorts left a convoy and the point where British escorts joined it, and there in April a pack sank ten of the 22 ships of convoy SC 26.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Escort groups based at Hvalfjordur in Iceland filled that surface gap from April 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At its first meeting on 4 November 1942 Churchill's Anti-U-boat Warfare Committee named filling the air gap in mid-Atlantic as the first need, for which about 40 long-range radar-fitted aircraft were required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The only suitable aircraft was the American Liberator, since the comparable British Lancaster was fitted for bombing, and deliveries were disappointing because the Americans claimed most Liberators for themselves, the Pacific included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the end of January 1943, 37 U-boats waited on the limits of the Greenland air gap, and in February Coastal Command still had only one squadron of very long range Liberators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In March, on the advice of the Atlantic Convoy Conference, the Combined Chiefs of Staff undertook to provide very long range aircraft by 1 July to cover the critical leg of the route east of Newfoundland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Such aircraft were Liberators with armour and turrets removed and bigger fuel tanks added, able to stay 4 hours on station about 1,850 kilometres (1,000 nautical miles) from base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American operations analysts found that the few very long range aircraft available from March 1943 proved extraordinarily effective, and that after 17 May no ships were sunk in the North Atlantic for some time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet a 'black pit' remained around the Azores, covered only by escort carriers until Portugal granted Britain the use of Lagens Field there in October 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. III, “The Priority of Maritime Air Operations, 1942,” 88–89, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-3.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. XIV, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Triumph of the Escorts, 1st January–31st May, 1943,” 355–56, 362–63, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 42–45, 66–67, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The Antisubmarine Command,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 377–411, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-12.html. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1946), 82–83, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 56–58, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the gap not be closed sooner?","a":"Because the only suitable aircraft was the Liberator: the British Lancaster was fitted for bombing Germany, and deliveries of Liberators were disappointing because the Americans claimed most of them for their own needs, including the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the U-boats gather at its edge?","a":"Because convoys inside it had no air escort, and by the end of January 1943, 37 U-boats were waiting on the limits of the Greenland air gap for the convoys to enter it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Azores matter?","a":"Because the 'black pit' around them lay beyond land-based aircraft and was covered only by escort carriers until Portugal granted Britain the use of Lagens Field in October 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Consolidated_Liberator_-_The_Battle_of_the_Atlantic_1939-1945_CA122.jpg","title":"A Coastal Command Liberator off the Azores","desc":"A Liberator of No. 220 Squadron, Royal Air Force, flying from Lagens in the Azores, one of the bases that closed the last of the air gap.","credit":"B. J. Daventry, Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Consolidated_Liberator_-_The_Battle_of_the_Atlantic_1939-1945_CA122.jpg","alt":"A four-engined bomber with twin tail fins in flight over the sea near a coastline."},"wiki":"Mid-Atlantic gap"},
    "ww2-548": {"questions":["USS Bogue, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built on a merchant hull, first sailed with North Atlantic convoys in March 1943, alongside the British Biter, Archer and Dasher.","Between May and December 1943, 2,200 ships crossed between the United States and Gibraltar with only one lost to U-boats, guarded by groups each built round an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and destroyers."],"abstract":"An <b>escort carrier</b> was a small, slow aircraft carrier, usually built on a merchant hull, carrying about two dozen aircraft at up to 18.5 knots to protect convoys and hunt submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first was HMS Audacity, a captured German merchant ship, the Hannover, fitted with a flight deck and in service from June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sent to the Gibraltar convoys in September with six American Martlet fighters, she did such good work that Dönitz made her destruction the primary object of his U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With convoy HG 76 in December 1941 her aircraft and the escort under F. J. Walker helped destroy five U-boats for the loss of two merchant ships, though Audacity herself was torpedoed on the 21st.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lesson, in the words of the American official history, was to skip the stopgaps and build escort carriers, and the United States converted merchant hulls both for its own navy and, under Lend-Lease, for the Royal Navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The type reached the North Atlantic convoy routes in March 1943, when USS Bogue sailed with convoy SC 123 and the British Biter, Archer and Dasher joined the Western Approaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their arrival coincided with that of the support groups, and the British official history counts escort carriers, support groups and very long range aircraft as what turned the tables on the U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> With convoy HX 237 in May 1943 the Biter's Swordfish flushed U-89 for the escorts to sink, and Dönitz's operations officer said he was not surprised to lose three boats of seven against a convoy protected by an escort carrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American groups of a carrier and about four destroyers then guarded the convoys between the United States and Gibraltar, on which 2,200 ships crossed from May to December 1943 with only one lost to U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their aircraft, able to surprise U-boats on the surface, attacked in 60 of 68 sightings studied, and about 40 per cent of those attacks sank the boat, according to US Navy operations analysts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 476–79, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. XIV, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Triumph of the Escorts, 1st January–31st May, 1943,” 366–68, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 37–41, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 76–79, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1946), 55–56, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were escort carriers built on merchant hulls?","a":"Because hulls already under construction could be converted quickly; the United States turned C-3 freighter hulls into escort carriers from 1941, after Audacity showed, in the American historian's words, that the stopgaps should be skipped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did U-boat commanders fear them?","a":"Because their aircraft could surprise a U-boat on the surface far from land; the American analysts found that 60 of 68 sightings led to an attack and that about 40 per cent of attacks sank the boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Dönitz make Audacity a target?","a":"Because her fighters shot down Focke-Wulf reconnaissance aircraft and helped the escorts against U-boats, so that the Germans regarded her presence as the greatest danger to their boats on the Gibraltar route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/HMS_AUDACITY%2C_1941._FL1204.jpg","title":"HMS Audacity, 1941","desc":"HMS Audacity, the first escort carrier, under way in coastal waters in 1941.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HMS_AUDACITY,_1941._FL1204.jpg","alt":"A merchant ship with a flat, empty flight deck running its whole length, under way near a coast."},"wiki":"Escort carrier"},
    "ww2-549": {"questions":["Under Blackett, operational research became a recognised part of British <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its scientists studying the results achieved by both sides and recommending tactical changes.","Churchill claimed in 1939 that modern <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let one or two destroyers pursue a U-boat that would once have needed a flotilla of 15 or 20 vessels for a whole day."],"abstract":"<b>Anti-submarine warfare</b> is the finding and destroying of submarines and the protection of shipping against them, and against the U-boats it combined escorts, aircraft, new weapons and scientific analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in September 1939 that hunting one U-boat had once taken a flotilla of 15 or 20 vessels a whole day, while now one or two destroyers could keep up a relentless pursuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The escort's basic weapon was the depth charge, but the attacking ship lost contact as it ran in over the target, a blind time of 45 to 90 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hedgehog, coming into production at the end of 1941, answered that by throwing 24 contact-fused bombs in a ring about 230 metres (250 yards) ahead of the ship, cutting the blind time to 15 to 20 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American operational data later showed Hedgehog attacks to be more successful than depth-charge attacks made in the same incidents, a superiority the analysts judged to be inherent in the weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By mid-1942, the British official history notes, the Allies had short-wave radar, high-frequency direction finding, the Leigh Light and more powerful air depth charges, yet U-boats were still completed faster than they were sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first squadron of Leigh Light aircraft was formed in March 1942, and its first 'dark night' attack took the enemy completely by surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Above all the official history singles out the centimetric radar set, which returned the initiative in attack by night or in low visibility first to the surface escorts and soon to Coastal Command's aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Scientists also studied the results achieved by both sides, and under P. M. S. Blackett operational research became a recognised element in planning British moves and disposing forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1943 American army airmen argued that only offensive tactics against U-boats at sea and in their bases could reduce the fleet, and the Navy's own operations research group likewise criticised tying army aircraft to convoy escort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1946), 21–22, 124–25, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. IV, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Campaign in American Waters, 1st January–31st July, 1942,” 107–8, 112, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-4.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. VIII, “The Battle of the Atlantic. The Second Campaign on the Convoy Routes, 1st August–31st December, 1942,” 208–9, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-8.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The Antisubmarine Command,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 377–411, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-12.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “U-Boat Warfare,” statement by the First Lord of the Admiralty, Winston Churchill, <i>Hansard</i>, 26 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 1239–46, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/26/u-boat-warfare. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “U-Boot-Krieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/u-boot-krieg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the depth charge alone not enough?","a":"Because the attacking ship lost contact with the submerged boat during its final run, a blind time of 45 to 90 seconds, and its sound gear gave no depth; Hedgehog's ahead-thrown, contact-fused bombs cut the blind time to 15 to 20 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did centimetric radar matter so much?","a":"Because it let escorts and then aircraft find surfaced U-boats at night and in poor visibility, which the official history says returned the initiative in attack to the British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were scientists brought into the battle?","a":"Because their study of the results achieved by both sides produced strategic and tactical recommendations, and by mid-1942 operational research had become a recognised part of planning in the Admiralty and Western Approaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Hedgehog_on_HMS_Westcott_WWII_IWM_A_30999.jpg","title":"Hedgehog on HMS Westcott","desc":"A Hedgehog anti-submarine mortar on the forecastle of the British destroyer HMS Westcott.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hedgehog_on_HMS_Westcott_WWII_IWM_A_30999.jpg","alt":"Rows of finned bombs mounted on spigots in a frame on the deck at the bow of a warship."},"wiki":"Anti-submarine warfare"},
    "ww2-550": {"questions":["In 1937 the Naval Staff, trusting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to find any submerged boat, reported that the submarine should never again present the problem Britain had faced in 1917.","Officially said to stand for a committee of 1917 that no archive has found, the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first appears in a report of July 1918 on experimental work at Parkeston Quay."],"abstract":"<b>ASDIC</b> was the Royal Navy's name for the echo-ranging equipment with which a ship detected a submerged submarine, a technique for which the Americans coined the word sonar in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An alternating current applied to a quartz crystal beneath the ship made it vibrate and send out a pulse of sound, and an echo from an obstacle returned to the same crystal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave the bearing and range of a submerged target but not its depth, so depth charges had to be dropped in large patterns set to explode at varying depths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The British official history derives the name from the initials of an Allied Submarine Detection Investigation Committee of 1917, and in December 1939 the Admiralty gave Oxford University Press much the same explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No such committee has been found in the records, however, and the word first appears in a report on experimental work at Parkeston Quay of 6 July 1918, with another explanation tying it to the Admiralty's Anti-Submarine Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Work on it advanced greatly from 1927 at the Admiralty's establishment at Portland, and in 1937 the Naval Staff reported that the submarine should never again present the problem of 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill echoed that confidence in September 1939, telling the Commons that one or two destroyers could now keep up a relentless pursuit of a U-boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same confidence may have helped persuade the Admiralty to risk fleet carriers on submarine hunts, and on 17 September 1939 U-29 sank HMS Courageous with her captain and 518 of her company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It worked well against a submerged boat but had an effective range of some 1,400 metres (1,500 yards) and could not detect a surfaced U-boat, the weakness that Dönitz's night surface attacks exploited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Late in the war, snorkel-fitted U-boats lying on the bottom in shallow coastal water were hard to find by echo-ranging, because wrecks and rocks returned confusing echoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 34–35, 354–55, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","Willem Hackmann, “Sonar, Wireless Telegraphy and the Royal Navy: Scientific Development in a Military Context, 1890–1939,” in <i>Science, Politics and the Public Good</i>, ed. Nicolaas A. Rupke (London: Palgrave Macmillan, 1988), 90–118, n. 1, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-349-09514-8_5. [Paywalled]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “U-Boat Warfare,” statement by the First Lord of the Admiralty, Winston Churchill, <i>Hansard</i>, 26 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 1239–46, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/26/u-boat-warfare. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 24–26, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, 1946), 21–22, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. VI, “The Sea Approaches and Coastal Waters, 3rd September–31st December, 1939,” 105–6, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-I/UK-RN-I-6.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 338–39, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did ASDIC fail against the attacks of 1940?","a":"Because it had been designed to find a submerged submarine and could not detect one on the surface, so when U-boats attacked surfaced at night the escorts had to fall back on sighting them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the origin of the name uncertain?","a":"Because the committee the Admiralty named in 1939 has never been identified in the records; the word appears suddenly in July 1918, and a naval staff report of 1924 linked it to the Anti-Submarine Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did inter-war confidence prove costly early in the war?","a":"Because faith in asdic-fitted destroyers helped persuade the Admiralty to use fleet carriers for submarine hunting, and U-29 sank HMS Courageous on 17 September 1939 while two of her escorts were away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/ASDIC_hut%2C_Merseyside_Maritime_Museum_%281%29.jpg","title":"A reconstructed ASDIC hut","desc":"A reconstruction of the ASDIC hut of a British destroyer at the start of the Second World War, at the Merseyside Maritime Museum in Liverpool.","credit":"Reptonix, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ASDIC_hut,_Merseyside_Maritime_Museum_(1).jpg","alt":"A cramped compartment with a dial-faced instrument console, a seat and headphones for the operator."}},
    "ww2-581": {"questions":["Roosevelt publicly called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only a temporary expedient on 17 November 1942, after Churchill warned that it looked like making terms with local quislings.","Clark's threat to hold the admiral incommunicado won a cease-fire on 10 November 1942, the first step in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that left a Vichy commander governing French North Africa."],"abstract":"The <b>Darlan deal</b> was the arrangement of November 1942 by which the Allied command accepted Admiral Jean François Darlan, commander-in-chief of the Vichy armed forces, as the head of French North Africa in return for French help against the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darlan happened to be in Algiers when the Torch landings began, and on 10 November General Mark Clark, Eisenhower's deputy, told him to order a cease-fire at once or be held incommunicado.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shortly before noon Darlan signed, in Marshal Pétain's name, orders for the French forces to stop fighting, and Morocco obeyed in time to save Casablanca from a destructive attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pétain publicly disavowed him, but there is reason to believe that he withdrew the disavowal within hours through a private cypher held only by Darlan and the navy minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 13 November the French leaders agreed that Darlan would be High Commissioner, that Giraud would command the ground and air forces, and that Noguès and Châtel would keep Morocco and Algeria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The news caused an uproar in Britain and the United States, where Darlan had the reputation of a collaborator, and de Gaulle's Free French regarded him as a traitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill wrote to Roosevelt on 17 November that the deal could only be a temporary expedient justifiable solely by the stress of battle, warning against seeming ready to make terms with local Quislings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Roosevelt used those words in public the same day, adding that no permanent arrangement should be made with Darlan and that the deal had saved lives and time for the attack on Tunis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The detailed Clark–Darlan Agreement was signed on 22 November, and the word protocol was dropped from its title to avoid the appearance of diplomatic recognition of Darlan's regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement ended on 24 December 1942, when Darlan was assassinated in Algiers by a young man whom the French quickly tried and shot, and Giraud succeeded him as High Commissioner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 262–71, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-14.html. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 357–58, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-18.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair and C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 161–62, 190, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Statement on Temporary Political Arrangements in Africa,\" 17 November 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-temporary-political-arrangements-africa. [Open access]","Memorandum of conversation by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), 18 November 1942, enclosing Churchill to Roosevelt, 17 November 1942, document 446 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Historian), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d446. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Tunisia, 17 November 1942–13 May 1943</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-12 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 3–5, 12, https://archive.org/details/TunisiaCmh72-12. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies deal with Darlan rather than Giraud?","a":"French officers in North Africa felt bound by their oath to Pétain, Darlan spoke with the Marshal's authority while Giraud looked like a dissident, and the broadcast appeal in Giraud's name had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eisenhower told London and Washington that without Darlan a quick conquest of Tunisia was impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill and Roosevelt call it a temporary expedient?","a":"Feeling against Darlan's record ran deep, and Churchill feared the political injury of seeming to make terms with local quislings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Roosevelt answered the critics by saying that no permanent arrangement would be made and that it applied to the local situation only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the deal give the Allies in the field?","a":"It stopped the fighting in Morocco and Algeria, put French troops into the fight in Tunisia and brought French West Africa over to the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eisenhower called Darlan the source of all their practical help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/USA-MTO-NWA-p266.jpeg","title":"Negotiations at Algiers, 13 November 1942","desc":"Eisenhower, Darlan, Clark and Robert Murphy at Algiers on the day the French leaders reached their working agreement.","credit":"U.S. Army photograph, National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USA-MTO-NWA-p266.jpeg","alt":"Four men standing in a row in front of palm leaves: an American general in uniform, an older man in a dark suit and hat, a tall officer in uniform, and a man in a suit."},"wiki":"Clark-Darlan Accords"},
    "ww2-582": {"questions":["By the British count 238,243 unwounded prisoners were held on 25 May 1943 at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the American official history estimated about 275,000.","Defeats at Kasserine and Fondouk taught American divisions with only 4 days of earlier combat hard lessons during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fought between November 1942 and May 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Tunisian campaign</b> was the fighting for Tunisia between November 1942 and 13 May 1943, in which American, British and French forces from Algeria and the British Eighth Army from Libya destroyed the last Axis armies in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the Torch landings both sides rushed in troops, and an Axis airlift that began on 12 November would bring over 15,000 men, while ships landed 176 tanks at Tunis and Bizerte during November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Allied run for Tunis was stopped short of the city in December, and Darlan's assassination on 24 December added a political crisis to the military setback.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In February 1943 Axis attacks broke through the American positions at Sidi Bou Zid and the Kasserine Pass, and the Allies reorganised under Alexander's new 18th Army Group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Eighth Army came up on the southern flank, Allied fighters shot down 50 to 70 of about 100 Axis transport aircraft on 18 April alone by American estimates, and British and American troops entered Tunis and Bizerta on 7 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 13 May Alexander signalled Churchill that the Allies were masters of the North African shores, and Attlee told the Commons that North Africa would now be a forward base for further attacks on the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> How many prisoners were taken is uncertain: the British count of unwounded prisoners held on 25 May was 238,243, while the American official history estimated about 275,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Allied battle casualties came to about 70,000 killed, wounded and missing, some 18,000 of them American, 36,000 British and 16,000 French, by the preliminary figures the American history prints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the United States Army, which began with only 4 days of combat experience, it was a hard school, with notable failures at Kasserine Pass and Fondouk before its divisions improved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American campaign study judged that the defeat amounted to the mortal wounding of Italy as an Axis partner, and the Allies turned at once to preparing their next landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 644–66, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-34.html. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 675, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-35.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair and C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 269, 452–60, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Tunisia, 17 November 1942–13 May 1943</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-12 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 3–5, 12, 17–18, 26–27, https://archive.org/details/TunisiaCmh72-12. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, \"The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August, 1942, to 13th May, 1943,\" despatch of 23 May 1947, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 884, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/supplement/884/data.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 191, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-6.html. [Open access]","Clement Attlee, \"North African Campaign (Victorious Ending),\" House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 13 May 1943, vol. 389, cols. 795–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/may/13/north-african-campaign-victorious-ending. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies fail to take Tunis in 1942?","a":"The Axis moved faster: an airlift and convoys poured troops, tanks and supplies into Tunis and Bizerte, Axis submarines worked between Sicily and Tunisia with little to fear, and the Allied run for Tunis was stopped in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the prisoner figures differ?","a":"No satisfactory Axis count survives, so the totals come from different Allied tallies: 238,243 unwounded prisoners held on 25 May, an 18th Army Group reckoning of 244,500, and an American estimate of about 275,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the campaign mean for the American army?","a":"It met well-trained, battle-tested enemy units for the first time and suffered months of setbacks with high casualties, but it ended with five full divisions in the field, four of them now experienced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/American_soldiers_passing_reviewing_stand%2C_Allied_victory_parade_Tunis%2C_Tunisia%2C_1943_%2826973965494%29.jpg","title":"The Allied victory parade in Tunis, 1943","desc":"American soldiers pass the reviewing stand on Avenue Gambetta in the victory parade that followed the Axis surrender.","credit":"Office of War Information photograph, Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_soldiers_passing_reviewing_stand,_Allied_victory_parade_Tunis,_Tunisia,_1943_(26973965494).jpg","alt":"Columns of American soldiers in helmets marching along a broad city avenue past a reviewing stand."},"wiki":"Tunisian campaign"},
    "ww2-583": {"questions":["Rommel complained that the Italian high command sent him towards Le Kef instead of Tébessa during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first major clash between American and German troops in Tunisia.","On 22 February 1943, with British armour holding the road south of Thala, Rommel told Kesselring that pressing on in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no chance of success."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kasserine Pass</b> was the Axis offensive of February 1943 in central Tunisia in which German and Italian forces under Rommel drove the American II Corps back through the passes of the Western Dorsal, its first major battle against German troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It began on 14 February, when about a hundred German tanks came out of the Faïd pass and attacked the dispersed 1st Armored Division at Sidi Bou Zid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 4 days of defeats II Corps lost 2,546 men missing, 103 tanks and 280 vehicles, and gave up Gafsa, Sbeitla, Fériana and the airfield at Thélepte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pass itself, shaped from the air like a crude capital X, was one of three main gaps through the range, and with Allied aircraft grounded by a sirocco Rommel's troops broke through it on 20 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Italian high command ordered the thrust north towards Le Kef rather than the deeper drive on the American base at Tébessa that Rommel wanted, a change his papers call appallingly short-sighted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The advance was stopped on 21 and 22 February at Sbiba, below Djebel el Hamra on the Tébessa road and south of Thala, where a British armoured brigade lost fifteen tanks in a delaying action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the afternoon of 22 February Rommel told Kesselring that further attacks had no chance of success, and that evening the Italian high command ordered the offensive broken off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Allies did not recognise the withdrawal for more than a day, and the pass was back in their hands by the evening of 25 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Axis records claimed 4,026 prisoners for 16 to 24 February and gave German casualties as only 989, while Italian casualties were not recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexander judged that Rommel had over-exploited an initial success, and American troops drew lessons about German tank tactics and the weakness of their own light tanks and antitank guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 438–58, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-23.html. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 459–84, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-24.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair and C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 289–303, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Tunisia, 17 November 1942–13 May 1943</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-12 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 16–18, https://archive.org/details/TunisiaCmh72-12. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, \"The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August, 1942, to 13th May, 1943,\" despatch of 23 May 1947, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 868–70, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/supplement/868/data.pdf. [Open access]","Erwin Rommel, <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, ed. B. H. Liddell Hart, trans. Paul Findlay (1953), 401–8, a memoir compiled from Rommel's own notes, https://archive.org/details/THEROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 156–58, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-5.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the American positions collapse so quickly?","a":"The 1st Armored Division was spread thin when about a hundred German tanks attacked at Sidi Bou Zid, and an ineffective counterattack and the capture of some 1,400 men forced a major withdrawal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rommel break off the attack?","a":"His thrusts below Djebel el Hamra and at Thala were making no progress, fuel and ammunition were short, rain had made the ground unfit for mobile operations, and he wanted to strike the Eighth Army in the south instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What changed on the Allied side after the battle?","a":"A new 18th Army Group under Alexander tightened control over the three national commands, Patton took over II Corps in early March, and American soldiers studied German tactics and the faults of their own weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/SC_170095_-_Italian_tank_destroyed_by_Allied_gun._Kasserine_Pass%2C_Tunisia._24_February%2C_1943.jpg","title":"A wrecked Italian tank at the Kasserine Pass","desc":"An Italian tank knocked out by Allied fire in the Kasserine Pass, photographed on 24 February 1943 as the Axis forces pulled back.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SC_170095_-_Italian_tank_destroyed_by_Allied_gun._Kasserine_Pass,_Tunisia._24_February,_1943.jpg","alt":"A destroyed tank lying on rough, scrubby ground in a valley."},"wiki":"Battle of Kasserine Pass"},
    "ww2-584": {"questions":["Some 442 guns on a front of about 2.7 kilometres (3,000 yards) opened <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> early on 6 May 1943, and Allied aircraft flew 1,958 sorties in direct support that day.","Within a week of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> breaking through to Tunis, von Arnim and Messe had both surrendered, and Alexander claimed that only 663 Axis soldiers escaped from Africa."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Strike</b> was the final Allied offensive in Tunisia, settled by Alexander and Anderson on 30 April 1943 to break through to Tunis on a narrow front and finish the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main blow fell to the British 9th Corps under Horrocks, whose two infantry divisions were to open a gap for the 6th and 7th Armoured Divisions to dash through before the Axis could man the inner defences of Tunis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack opened at three in the morning of 6 May with some 442 guns on a front of about 2.7 kilometres (3,000 yards), and the air forces flew 1,958 sorties that day in direct support, a figure never before approached in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same day Army Group Africa reported that most of the 15th Panzer Division must be deemed destroyed and that the road to Tunis would be open on 7 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the afternoon of 7 May patrols of the 7th Armoured Division reached the centre of Tunis, and American troops entered Bizerta the same afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Admiral Cunningham's Operation Retribution sealed the sea, his signal ordering the ships to sink, burn and destroy and to let nothing pass, and in the event very few Axis troops tried to escape that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The forces cut off north of Tunis accepted unconditional surrender on 9 May, and von Arnim, commanding Army Group Africa, was taken at his headquarters on 12 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The newly promoted Marshal Messe surrendered the First Italian Army on 13 May, and Alexander reported to Churchill that the Allies were masters of the North African shores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The number of prisoners is uncertain: the British count of unwounded prisoners on 25 May was 238,243, the 18th Army Group reckoned 244,500 and the American history about 275,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexander wrote that only 663 men escaped, and the American campaign study counted the defeat as the mortal wounding of Italy as an Axis partner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair and C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 446–53, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair and C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 423–24, 457–60, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 644–66, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-34.html. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, \"The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August, 1942, to 13th May, 1943,\" despatch of 23 May 1947, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 883–84, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/supplement/883/data.pdf. [Open access]","Charles R. Anderson, <i>Tunisia, 17 November 1942–13 May 1943</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-12 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 26–27, https://archive.org/details/TunisiaCmh72-12. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the attack made on a narrow front?","a":"Alexander wanted a breakthrough rather than a broad push: the armour was to pass through on the day the infantry attacked, deny the enemy time to form an anti-tank screen and cut off the troops north of Tunis from the Cap Bon peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few Axis troops escape by sea?","a":"The Axis high command had decided to fight on with no regard for evacuation, and from 9 May Allied destroyers, coastal craft and aircraft patrolled the Tunisian coast under Operation Retribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the prisoner totals so different?","a":"No satisfactory Axis count of the losses was found, so the figures come from Allied tallies made in different ways: unwounded prisoners held on 25 May, an army group reckoning from 20 March, and an American estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/SC_171624_-_Unloading_German_prisoners_arriving_at_one_of_the_P.O.W._camps_in_Mateur%2C_Tunisia%2C_North_Africa._10_May%2C_1943._%2852273182179%29.jpg","title":"German prisoners at Mateur, 10 May 1943","desc":"German prisoners climb down from a truck at a prisoner-of-war camp near Mateur as the Axis armies in Tunisia surrendered.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps photograph, National Archives, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SC_171624_-_Unloading_German_prisoners_arriving_at_one_of_the_P.O.W._camps_in_Mateur,_Tunisia,_North_Africa._10_May,_1943._(52273182179).jpg","alt":"Soldiers in caps and field uniforms unloading kit from the back of a truck in an open field, watched by guards."},"wiki":"Operation Strike"},
    "ww2-585": {"questions":["Decided at Casablanca in January 1943, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended on 17 August when American troops entered Messina just hours after the last Axis soldiers had crossed the strait.","Tens of thousands of German and Italian troops escaped across the Strait of Messina at the end of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which no coordinated Allied air and naval plan had been made to prevent."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Husky</b> was the Allied invasion of Sicily, fought from 10 July to 17 August 1943 by the American Seventh Army and the British Eighth Army under Alexander's Fifteenth Army Group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the Casablanca conference in January 1943 Churchill, Roosevelt and the Combined Chiefs of Staff decided to take the island once Africa was cleared, as a base against southern Europe and to open the Mediterranean to Allied shipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plan was recast in the spring so that both armies landed together in the south-east of the island, accepting the risk of supplying them over the beaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the night of 9 to 10 July an armada of 2,590 vessels by one American count, some 3,000 ships and craft in Roosevelt's round figures, began landing more than seven divisions along a front of about 160 kilometres (100 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened with the first large-scale Allied airborne operations of the war, and over the next 38 days half 1 million Allied soldiers, sailors and airmen fought for the island, the first piece of the Axis homeland to fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Germans fought a delaying withdrawal towards Messina and from 11 August ran a full-scale evacuation across the strait that Allied air and naval forces failed to stop, though bombers struck the beaches and boats round the clock in its last 3 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> American troops entered Messina on the morning of 17 August, just hours after the last Axis soldiers had boarded ship for the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How many got away is disputed: the American official history counts 39,569 Germans and 70,000 to 75,000 Italians, while a German record cited in the British history puts the Germans at about 60,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American history puts Allied losses at under 20,000, 7,402 American and 11,843 British, against 12,000 Germans dead or captured and 147,000 Italians dead, wounded or captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the campaign Mussolini was toppled from power, and the Allies quickly followed their victory by invading the Italian mainland in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 88, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-5.html. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 410–17, 431, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-21.html. [Open access]","C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn, H. L. Davies and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Campaign in Sicily 1943 and the Campaign in Italy, 3rd September 1943 to 31st March 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1973), 182–83, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-5. [Open access]","Andrew J. Birtle, <i>Sicily, 9 July–17 August 1943</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-16 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 3–4, 24–27, https://archive.org/details/Sicily. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, \"The Conquest of Sicily from 10th July, 1943 to 17th August, 1943,\" despatch of 9 October 1946, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38205, 12 February 1948, 1009, 1012, 1022, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38205/supplement/1009/data.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 446, 472–73, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-14.html. [Open access]","Admiralty, Naval Staff, <i>Battle Summary No. 35: The Invasion of Sicily, Operation \"Husky\"</i>, B.R. 1736 (27) (Admiralty, 1946), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/Battlesummaryno35royalnavy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies choose Sicily?","a":"Taking it would drive Axis air and naval forces from their island base, open the Mediterranean sea lanes to Allied shipping and give a base for operations against southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many Axis troops escape?","a":"The Allies had massed their landings at the most predictable site, never seriously considered a bolder stroke at Messina or Calabria, and made no coordinated air, land and sea plan to close the strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the escape figures disagree?","a":"They come from different records: the American history gives 39,569 Germans, a German high command entry of 18 August about 60,000, and the Italian figures run from 70,000 to 75,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Troops_from_51st_Highland_Division_unloading_stores_from_tank_landing_craft_on_the_opening_day_of_the_Allied_invasion_of_Sicily%2C_10_July_1943._A17916.jpg","title":"Landing stores in Sicily, 10 July 1943","desc":"Men of the British 51st Highland Division unload stores from tank landing craft on the first day of the invasion.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, Imperial War Museum A 17916, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troops_from_51st_Highland_Division_unloading_stores_from_tank_landing_craft_on_the_opening_day_of_the_Allied_invasion_of_Sicily,_10_July_1943._A17916.jpg","alt":"Soldiers carrying boxes and equipment off beached landing craft onto a sandy shore."},"wiki":"Allied invasion of Sicily"},
    "ww2-586": {"questions":["An act of 1928 placed the succession to the Italian throne and the powers of the crown in the custody of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which also kept a list of Mussolini's possible successors.","Having not met for more than 3 years, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was summoned for 24 July 1943 after Mussolini came back from meeting Hitler at Feltre in the Dolomites."],"abstract":"The <b>Grand Council of Fascism</b> was the supreme council of Mussolini's regime, set up on 11 January 1923 as the governing body of the Fascist party and made a constitutional organ of the Italian state by an act of 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The act gave it the right to review constitutional questions, to propose legislation, to sanction international treaties and to keep a list of possible successors to Mussolini as head of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also placed the powers and prerogatives of the crown and the succession to the throne in the council's custody, which American analysts in 1943 saw as a blow to the monarchy's prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On paper it was the regime's highest body, yet by July 1943 it had not met for more than 3 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Party leaders unhappy with the conduct of the war asked Mussolini on 16 July to summon it, and after his meeting with Hitler at Feltre he called it for 24 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Dino Grandi drew up a motion asking that the war be separated from politics and that command of the armed forces return to the crown, in effect a move to unseat Mussolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 28 members met at five in the afternoon of 24 July, and after almost 9 hours of debate 19 of them voted for the motion at about three in the morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That afternoon the king told Mussolini that Badoglio would replace him, and carabinieri took the fallen dictator away in an ambulance as a prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American official history calls the meeting a sideshow that gave the king a constitutional occasion for a dismissal already planned, and Badoglio's government dissolved the Fascist party and on 27 July abolished the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that the keystone of the Fascist arch had crumbled, and at Verona in January 1944 Mussolini's new republic tried the members who had voted against him and shot five of them, Ciano among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 263–69, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-14.html. [Open access]","C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn, H. L. Davies and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Campaign in Sicily 1943 and the Campaign in Italy, 3rd September 1943 to 31st March 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1973), 206–7, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-5. [Open access]","Leo Pasvolsky to the Secretary of State, 18 August 1943, with enclosed Department of State memoranda on Italy, document 368 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Conferences at Washington and Quebec, 1943</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Historian), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943/d368. [Open access]","Office of Strategic Services, Research and Analysis Branch, <i>The Italian Social Republic (The Fascist Puppet Government): Description</i> (Washington, DC, 15 March 1944), sections I–II, https://archive.org/details/per_oss-state-department-intelligence-and-research_the-italian-social-republic_1944-03-15_9. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, \"Italy and the War,\" House of Commons, <i>Hansard</i>, 27 July 1943, vol. 391, cols. 1397–402, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/jul/27/italy-and-the-war. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 281, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-15.html. [Open access]","Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, \"Gran consiglio del fascismo,\" <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Treccani, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/gran-consiglio-del-fascismo_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, \"Repubblica sociale italiana,\" <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Treccani, 2011), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/repubblica-sociale-italiana_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the vote matter if the king had already decided?","a":"The king and his advisers were already planning Mussolini's removal, and the council's vote gave them a constitutional crisis to serve as the occasion for dismissing him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did members who were not rebels vote for the motion?","a":"Some signed in the belief that it would only force Mussolini to give up the military command he had held since the war began, while Grandi hoped the king would replace him with a triumvirate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the monarchy bound up with the council?","a":"The 1928 act put the crown's powers and the succession to the throne in the council's custody, which American analysts thought had badly undermined the king's prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/Votazione_odg_Grandi.jpg","title":"The roll of the vote on Grandi's motion","desc":"A Fascist party list of the Grand Council's members, marked by hand with how each voted on Dino Grandi's motion of 24 to 25 July 1943.","credit":"National Fascist Party document, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Votazione_odg_Grandi.jpg","alt":"A typed list of names and offices headed P.N.F. Roma, with handwritten marks beside each name and handwritten totals in the corner."},"wiki":"Grand Council of Fascism"},
    "ww2-587": {"questions":["Twelve short articles required Italy to hand over its fleet and aircraft and surrender Corsica and all its territory as Allied bases under the terms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> signed in Sicily.","Political, economic and financial conditions were left out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and imposed later in the Instrument of Surrender that Badoglio signed at Malta on 29 September 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Armistice of Cassibile</b> was the military armistice between Italy and the Allies signed on 3 September 1943 at Alexander's headquarters at Cassibile in Sicily, by which Italy stopped fighting on the Axis side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Giuseppe Castellano signed it for Marshal Badoglio, head of the Italian government, and Eisenhower for the Allies, with Harold Macmillan and Robert Murphy present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its twelve short articles demanded an immediate end to Italian hostilities, the transfer of the fleet and aircraft, and the surrender of Corsica and all Italian territory for use as Allied bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last article left political, economic and financial conditions for later, and Badoglio and Eisenhower signed these long terms at Malta on 29 September as the Instrument of Surrender of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Castellano had first met Eisenhower's emissaries in Lisbon in August, and the Italian leaders agreed to the terms on 1 September after asking in vain that the armistice follow a landing of at least fifteen Allied divisions north of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The armistice was kept secret until 1830 on 8 September, when Eisenhower broadcast it from Radio Algiers on the eve of the main landing at Salerno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That day a planned American airborne drop near Rome was called off after General Maxwell Taylor, sent secretly into the city, reported that the Italians now said they could not secure the airfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Badoglio read his own announcement on Radio Rome an hour late, at 1945, giving the Italian people no warning of German reprisals and no guidance for the future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Early next morning the king, Badoglio and the service chiefs left Rome for the Adriatic coast, while German units moved quickly to disarm their former allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The armistice and the instrument that supplemented it remained in force until the peace treaty of February 1947 took effect on 15 September 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Armistice with Italy; September 3, 1943,\" military armistice signed at Fairfield Camp, Sicily, 61 Stat. 2740, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/italy01.asp. [Open access]","\"Armistice with Italy: Instrument of Surrender; September 29, 1943,\" signed at Malta, 61 Stat. 2742, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/italy03.asp. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 498–508, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-26.html. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 510–19, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-27.html. [Open access]","C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn, H. L. Davies and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Campaign in Sicily 1943 and the Campaign in Italy, 3rd September 1943 to 31st March 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1973), 214–18, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-5. [Open access]","Kenneth V. Smith, <i>Naples-Foggia, 9 September 1943–21 January 1944</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-17 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 6, https://archive.org/details/Naples-Foggia. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the armistice kept secret for 5 days?","a":"It was to be announced together with the Allied landing on the mainland, which came 6 hours after the two broadcasts, and with an airborne landing near Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the airborne drop on Rome cancelled?","a":"Italian commanders told General Taylor in Rome that the Germans had reinforced around the city, that their own troops lacked fuel and ammunition, and that they could not secure the airfields for American paratroops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the announcement throw Italy into confusion?","a":"Badoglio's broadcast gave no hint that Germany had become the enemy or what the army should do, and the king and government left Rome before dawn the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Armistice_1943_Castellano_Eisenhower.jpg","title":"Castellano and Eisenhower at Cassibile","desc":"General Giuseppe Castellano, in civilian clothes, shakes hands with Eisenhower after the signing, with General Walter Bedell Smith looking on.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armistice_1943_Castellano_Eisenhower.jpg","alt":"A man in a civilian suit shaking hands with an American general in uniform, while another officer watches."},"wiki":"Armistice of Cassibile"},
    "ww2-588": {"questions":["Orders issued on 28 July 1943, 3 days after Mussolini's fall, brought German plans for northern Italy and the Balkans together as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ready for an Italian change of sides.","About 800,000 Italian soldiers were captured, mostly without a fight, in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and some 650,000 of them were taken to the Reich and the occupied territories."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Achse</b> was the German plan, carried out from 8 September 1943, to disarm the Italian armed forces and take control of Italy and the Italian-held zones in Yugoslavia, Greece and France if Italy left the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of earlier plans to move German divisions into northern Italy and take over Italian sectors in the Balkans, and the orders that combined them under the name Achse went out on 28 July, 3 days after Mussolini's fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German units were to disarm Italian soldiers except those who stayed loyal, occupy the main ports of northern Italy and take control of the frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Through August the Wehrmacht reinforced its troops in Italy while both governments dissimulated, and the executive order was given on 8 September, the day the armistice was announced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German commanders acted within 6.5 hours and, helped by their preparations and by Italian confusion, disarmed the Italian army without the serious fighting they had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two German mobile divisions closed on Rome, and on 10 September the Italian commanders there accepted German terms while the king and Badoglio landed at Brindisi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The main fleet sailed to surrender to the Allies, and German aircraft sank the battleship Roma, with her commander, Admiral Carlo Bergamini, and most of the crew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Balkans, Greece and the Aegean more than 600,000 Italian soldiers were almost all dissolved by 15 September, and on Cephalonia the British official history records the massacre of General Gandin and 4,000 of his men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Berlin documentation centre on Nazi forced labour puts the Italian soldiers captured, mostly without a fight, at about 800,000, of whom some 650,000 were taken to the Reich and the occupied territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany classed them as military internees rather than prisoners of war, so that they could be used as forced labour in armaments production without regard to international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn, H. L. Davies and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Campaign in Sicily 1943 and the Campaign in Italy, 3rd September 1943 to 31st March 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1973), 189–90, 543, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-5. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 469–73, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-24.html. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 524–35, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-28.html. [Open access]","Dokumentationszentrum NS-Zwangsarbeit, \"Besetzung und Gefangennahme,\" in <i>Zwischen allen Stühlen: Die Geschichte der italienischen Militärinternierten 1943–1945</i>, online exhibition, Stiftung Topographie des Terrors, https://www.ns-zwangsarbeit.de/italienische-militaerinternierte/themen/besetzung-und-gefangennahme. [in German] [Open access]","Dokumentationszentrum NS-Zwangsarbeit, \"Italienische Militärinternierte,\" introduction to the permanent exhibition <i>Zwischen allen Stühlen</i>, Stiftung Topographie des Terrors, https://www.ns-zwangsarbeit.de/italienische-militaerinternierte. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans succeed so easily?","a":"They had planned since late July and reinforced their troops during August, while the Italian army was left without clear orders to resist once the king and the government fled Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were captured Italians called internees?","a":"Calling them military internees rather than prisoners of war let Germany put them to forced labour in the armaments industry without regard to international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the Italian fleet?","a":"It sailed to surrender to the Allies; German aircraft sank the Roma with her commander and most of the crew, and the rest met British battleships on 10 September and was escorted to Bizerte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J15358%2C_Bozen%2C_entwaffneten_Badoglio-Einheiten_marschieren_durch_die_Stadt.jpg","title":"Disarmed Italian soldiers in Bolzano, 1943","desc":"Italian soldiers, disarmed by the Germans after the armistice, are marched through the streets of Bolzano under German guard; the German propaganda caption called them Badoglio units.","credit":"Fred Rieder, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-J15358, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J15358,_Bozen,_entwaffneten_Badoglio-Einheiten_marschieren_durch_die_Stadt.jpg","alt":"A column of Italian soldiers in greatcoats and caps walking down a town street, with an armed German soldier in a helmet beside them."},"wiki":"Operation Achse"},
    "ww2-589": {"questions":["Freed from the Gran Sasso by German glider troops in September 1943, Mussolini headed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose main offices stood on the western shore of Lake Garda near Salò.","By 1945 Marshal Graziani, defence minister of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, commanded an army group on the Ligurian coast made up mainly of German divisions."],"abstract":"The <b>Italian Social Republic</b> was the Fascist state that Mussolini headed in German-occupied northern and central Italy from the autumn of 1943 until the German collapse in Italy in the spring of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> German troops freed Mussolini from his prison on the Gran Sasso on 12 September 1943 and flew him to Vienna that evening, and he went on to Germany to discuss a new Fascist government with Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A cabinet meeting of 27 September declared the new Fascist Republican State in office, the party congress at Verona in November adopted the Verona Manifesto as its programme, and the name Italian Social Republic came into use on 1 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> No constitution was ever produced, and its ministries were scattered among ten towns of northern Italy, with the main offices on the western shore of Lake Garda near Salò.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German governors and Gauleiters ran the frontier provinces of Venezia Giulia and Alto Adige, and an American study of March 1944 found that the decisive voice in all major matters rested with the Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same study judged that the regime's radical social slogans could not hide that it rested solely on force, faced with strikes, guerrilla warfare and clandestine opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Verona Manifesto declared those of the Jewish race to be foreigners and enemy nationals for the duration of the war, and German police deported 4,733 Jews from transit camps in northern Italy to Auschwitz-Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its regular army proved hard to rebuild, the Fascist militia, absorbed into a Republican National Guard, became one of its chief weapons, and the Italian soldiers held in Germany were now declared military internees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By 1945 its defence minister, Marshal Rodolfo Graziani, commanded an army group on the Ligurian coast with three German divisions and one Italian division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the last days of April Graziani's army was cut off south of the Po while he took refuge with the SS on Lake Como, and the German surrender in Italy took effect on 2 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Office of Strategic Services, Research and Analysis Branch, <i>The Italian Social Republic (The Fascist Puppet Government): Description</i> (Washington, DC, 15 March 1944), summary, sections I–III and appendix, https://archive.org/details/per_oss-state-department-intelligence-and-research_the-italian-social-republic_1944-03-15_9. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 536–38, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-28.html. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1977), 420, 505–8, 519–20, 542, https://archive.org/details/CassinoToTheAlps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn, H. L. Davies and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Campaign in Sicily 1943 and the Campaign in Italy, 3rd September 1943 to 31st March 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1973), 216, 379–80, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-5. [Open access]","Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, \"Repubblica sociale italiana,\" <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Treccani, 2011), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/repubblica-sociale-italiana_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Dokumentationszentrum NS-Zwangsarbeit, \"Italienische Militärinternierte,\" introduction to the permanent exhibition <i>Zwischen allen Stühlen</i>, Stiftung Topographie des Terrors, https://www.ns-zwangsarbeit.de/italienische-militaerinternierte. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the republic often called a puppet state?","a":"American analysts found that the Germans held the decisive voice in all major matters, and German military governors and Gauleiters ran its north-eastern frontier provinces outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it call itself a social republic?","a":"Its leaders proclaimed radical political and social aims in the Verona Manifesto and the state's name, which American analysts read as an attempt to disguise a regime that rested on force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the republic treat Italy's Jews?","a":"Its programme declared Jews foreigners and enemy nationals, and German police, sometimes aided by Fascist militia, rounded Jews up in the cities and deported 4,733 from transit camps in northern Italy to Auschwitz-Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Italian_Social_Republic_late_1943_map.png","title":"The Italian Social Republic, late 1943","desc":"A modern map of the area claimed by the Italian Social Republic at the end of 1943, from the Alps to just south of Rome, with Salò marked on Lake Garda.","credit":"PANONIAN and R-41, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_Social_Republic_late_1943_map.png","alt":"A map of northern and central Italy shaded yellow and labelled Italian Social Republic, with Switzerland and Germany to the north and the Kingdom of Italy to the south-east."},"wiki":"Italian Social Republic"},
    "ww2-590": {"questions":["The two Allied armies suffered 312,000 casualties and the Germans 434,646 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, according to the figures of the American official history.","Meant to eliminate Italy and wear down German forces before the cross-Channel attack, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> repeatedly lost divisions to northwest Europe, southern France and Greece."],"abstract":"The <b>Italian campaign</b> was the Allied war on the Italian mainland from the landings of September 1943 to the German surrender in Italy on 2 May 1945, which the American official history calls the longest sustained Allied campaign of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Allied plan it followed Sicily as the third step of a Mediterranean strategy, meant to eliminate Italy from the war and then to contain and wear down German forces before the main attack across the Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British Eighth Army crossed the Strait of Messina on 3 September 1943, the Fifth Army landed at Salerno on 9 September, and the Germans moved at once to defend the whole peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They then held a series of fortified lines across Italy, the strongest of them the Gustav Line anchored on Monte Cassino and the Garigliano and Rapido rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the Anzio landing and three costly assaults on Monte Cassino, the offensive of May 1944 opened the way to Rome on 4 June, and the Allies were held again in the northern Apennines that winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Counting both sides, about a million men served in Italy at one time or another, and Allied strength ranged from 400,000 to 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the American official history's count the two Allied armies suffered 312,000 casualties, while German casualties totalled 434,646, including 48,067 killed and 214,048 missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexander wrote that after Sicily the theatre's operations became preparatory and subsidiary to the invasion from Britain, and divisions were repeatedly taken away, leaving enough troops to hold the Germans but not to destroy them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American campaign study finds the heavy investment of 1944 hard to justify, since it was often not apparent whether the Allies or the Germans were doing the tying down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another answers that the Allied offensives kept the Germans from substantially reinforcing other fronts, and the final offensive of April 1945 broke into the Po valley and ended in the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1977), 541–42, https://archive.org/details/CassinoToTheAlps. [Open access]","Kenneth V. Smith, <i>Naples-Foggia, 9 September 1943–21 January 1944</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-17 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 6–12, https://archive.org/details/Naples-Foggia. [Open access]","Clayton D. Laurie, <i>Rome-Arno, 22 January–9 September 1944</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-20 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 3–4, 23–30, https://archive.org/details/Rome-Arno. [Open access]","Dwight D. Oland, <i>North Apennines, 10 September 1944–4 April 1945</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-34 (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/NorthApennines. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 1, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Sicily/USA-MTO-Sicily-1.html. [Open access]","C. J. C. Molony, with F. C. Flynn, H. L. Davies and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Campaign in Sicily 1943 and the Campaign in Italy, 3rd September 1943 to 31st March 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1973), 380, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-5. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, \"The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,\" despatch submitted in April 1947, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2879, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies fight in Italy at all?","a":"The Mediterranean plan was to invade the mainland after Sicily, eliminate Italy as a belligerent and win air bases around Rome, then contain and wear down German forces before the cross-Channel invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the advance so slow?","a":"Rugged, compartmented terrain and bad weather favoured the defence, the Germans held one fortified line after another, and the Allies lacked powerful mobile reserves to exploit local successes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is its value still argued over?","a":"It cost the Allies 312,000 casualties, and even American campaign studies doubt who was tying down whom, though they grant that the Germans could not shift substantial forces from Italy to other fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/The_Campaign_in_Italy%2C_September-december_1943-_the_Allied_Advance_To_the_Gustav_Line_NA7712.jpg","title":"A night barrage on the Volturno, October 1943","desc":"A British self-propelled 25-pounder, mounted on a wrecked German aircraft for extra elevation, fires in the barrage before the Volturno crossing.","credit":"Sergeant Mott, No. 2 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum NA 7712, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Campaign_in_Italy,_September-december_1943-_the_Allied_Advance_To_the_Gustav_Line_NA7712.jpg","alt":"A gun firing at night, its muzzle flash lighting up the gun crew and the wreckage it stands on."},"wiki":"Italian campaign (World War II)"},
    "ww2-551": {"questions":["Shore stations from Hartland Point to Bermuda used <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to send cross-bearings to London and Washington, where U-boats were plotted and convoys steered away from them.","From 1942 escorts carried sets for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the masthead, so a U-boat reporting a convoy could be fixed by the very ships it was shadowing."],"abstract":"<b>High-frequency direction finding</b>, called HF/DF or ‘Huff-Duff’ by sailors, was the technique of taking bearings on short-wave radio transmissions to fix the position of the transmitter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing in the message had to be read, because the mere fact that a U-boat commander was transmitting was enough to locate him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bearings from shore stations were plotted in London and Washington and sent to escorts in a secret daily bulletin, so that convoys could be turned away from the packs they revealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fixes were seldom accurate to better than 80 to 160 kilometres (50 to 100 miles), but that was close enough to order an evasive change of course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American naval history of the work dates its own first bearings on U-boats to January 1941, with seven stations, and says that by the end of the war fixes drew on more than forty American, British and Canadian stations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The weakness of shore stations was delay, so in 1942 sets were fitted in the escorts themselves, and the British official history says they were standard equipment by the end of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In June 1942 bearings taken by a Canadian warship with one convoy led to the sighting of two and possibly three surfaced U-boats, and the best practice was to fit several escorts so that they could cross-bear on boats close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The U-boats were exposed because Dönitz ran his packs by radio from ashore, and a boat that sighted a convoy reported it and then sent homing signals to bring the others in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Raeder told Hitler in May 1941 that the British had a far-reaching and excellent direction-finding network, yet the British official history found that no counter to the method was devised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> British codebreakers noted that the Germans squeezed U-boat signals into short coded forms partly to escape the direction finders, and the burst transmitter tried in 1944 was never fixed by the Allied stations at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 105–6, 226–28, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), 107, 112, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-4.html. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War</i>, vol. 1 (London: HMSO, 1979), 333–34, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 4, <i>Technical Intelligence from Allied Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-025, ch. 8, “U-Boat Communications and U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-025/SRH025-8.html. [Open access]","C. H. O’D. Alexander, “Cryptographic History of Work on the German Naval Enigma,” c. 1945, The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/1, 43, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/gne/gne-000.htm. [Open access]","“Fuehrer Conferences on Naval Affairs, 1939–1945,” in <i>Brassey’s Naval Annual 1948</i>, ed. H. G. Thursfield (New York: Macmillan, 1948), 196, https://archive.org/details/brasseys-naval-annual-1948-ocr. [Open access]","Karl Doenitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i>, trans. Division of Naval Intelligence (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 15–16, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a U-boat be found without its message being read?","a":"Because a direction finder measures only where a signal comes from, and two or more bearings crossing on a chart fix the transmitter whatever it says.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shore stations could therefore plot U-boats and warn convoys even while the German codes were unreadable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Dönitz’s methods make HF/DF so effective?","a":"Because his packs were directed from ashore by wireless, and the boat that found a convoy had to report it and send homing signals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every one of those transmissions offered a bearing, and escorts with their own sets could steer straight for the boat that sent it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a shipborne set better than shore stations?","a":"Because a shore fix arrived late and was often 80 to 160 kilometres (50 to 100 miles) out, while an escort’s own set gave an immediate bearing on a U-boat close to the convoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British fitted their escorts in 1942 and made the set standard by the end of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Radar_and_Electronic_Warfare_1939-1945_A31092.jpg","title":"A destroyer’s HF/DF aerial","desc":"A high-frequency direction-finding aerial fitted on the foremast of a Royal Navy destroyer.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, Imperial War Museum A 31092, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radar_and_Electronic_Warfare_1939-1945_A31092.jpg","alt":"A lattice of thin wires forming a cage-like aerial on top of a ship’s mast against a pale sky."},"wiki":"High-frequency direction finding"},
    "ww2-552": {"questions":["A weather-report codebook seized from U-559 in the Mediterranean in October 1942 let the cryptanalysts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> break back into the U-boat traffic within weeks.","Captures from the trawler Krebs, the weather ship München and U-110 in 1941 gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Bletchley Park the settings to read German naval signals almost currently."],"abstract":"<b>Hut 8</b> was the section of the Government Code and Cypher School at Bletchley Park that attacked the German navy’s Enigma cipher, including the keys used by the U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own wartime history describes a long research period in which papers captured off Narvik in April 1940 showed how the naval indicator system worked, though regular breaking still failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In August 1940 Birch of the naval section wrote that Turing, the hut’s first head, and Twinn were like people waiting for a miracle, and the hut held that only a captured month of keys would open the traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1941 spare rotors seized from the trawler Krebs in March supplied the naval wheels Bletchley lacked, and material from the weather ship München, U-110 and the Lauenburg let the cipher be read almost currently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American history suggests that the fall in sinkings that summer owed much to the evasive routing these decrypts made possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 1 February 1942 the U-boat key, called Shark in the hut, moved to a four-rotor machine, and apart from 2 days no U-boat traffic was read until 7 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Alexander, author of its cryptographic history, reckoned that a Shark day would take 50 to 100 times as long as an army or air force key on the three-rotor bombes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The way back in came when the U-boats’ short weather codebook was captured from U-559 in the Mediterranean in October 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Weather reports were sent with the machine working as a three-rotor Enigma, so the book gave cribs that broke most Shark days until 10 March 1943, when a short-signal codebook taken at the same time replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German navy feared compromise at times but blamed its losses on Allied radar and direction finding, and its countermeasures guarded against capture rather than codebreaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["C. H. O’D. Alexander, “Cryptographic History of Work on the German Naval Enigma,” c. 1945, The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/1, 35–49, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/gne/gne-000.htm. [Open access]","A. P. Mahon, “The History of Hut Eight, 1939–1945,” The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/2, 22–24, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/hut8/hut8-000.htm. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War</i>, vol. 1 (London: HMSO, 1979), 337–38, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 1, <i>Allied Communication Intelligence, December 1942–May 1945</i>, SRH-009, ch. 1, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-009/index.html. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 3, <i>German Naval Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-024, 83–85, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-024/SRH024-7.html. [Open access]","Jürgen Rohwer, “Radio Intelligence in the Battle of the Atlantic,” in <i>The Intelligence Revolution: A Historical Perspective</i>, ed. Walter T. Hitchcock (Washington, DC: Office of Air Force History, 1991), 81–82, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA433346. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did captures matter so much to Hut 8?","a":"Because the naval Enigma was used with procedures the hut could not reconstruct from intercepts alone in 1940, and its own history says a pinch of a month’s keys was the best hope of getting regular breaking started.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The captures of 1941 supplied keys and tables that let the traffic be read almost currently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the four-rotor Shark key so hard to break?","a":"Because a fourth rotor multiplied the work of the bombes, the machines that tested possible settings; Alexander reckoned an average Shark day would take 50 to 100 times as long as an army or air force key, which the existing bombes could not afford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did weather reports help break Shark?","a":"Because U-boats sent short weather signals with the machine working as a three-rotor Enigma, and the same readings were rebroadcast in a weaker cipher that the British could read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the codebook captured from U-559, the two could be matched and used as cribs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Hut_8%2C_Bletchley_Park.jpg","title":"Hut 8 at Bletchley Park","desc":"The restored wooden hut at Bletchley Park in which the naval Enigma section worked, photographed in 2017.","credit":"Bibeyjj, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hut_8%2C_Bletchley_Park.jpg","alt":"A low single-storey wooden hut with a felt roof and a row of windows, standing on a lawn among trees."},"wiki":"Hut 8"},
    "ww2-553": {"questions":["The slower of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was overhauled by the faster one in mid-ocean, so the two formed a single mass of shipping that the wolfpacks attacked for 3 nights.","German naval intelligence probably read a diversion order sent in an Allied naval cipher, which helped the U-boats find the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossing eastwards in March 1943."],"abstract":"<b>Convoys HX 229 and SC 122</b> were two eastbound Atlantic convoys bound for Britain whose combined battle in March 1943 was one of the biggest convoy actions of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The U-boat command concentrated about forty boats from three groups, Raubgraf, Stürmer and Dränger, across their route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American studies of the intercepts conclude that the attack was made possible by last-minute shifts in the U-boat lines, probably helped by a diversion order to HX 229 sent in an Allied naval cipher the Germans were reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From 16 March the U-boats sank twelve of the forty ships of HX 229 in 3 days, while SC 122, about 190 kilometres (120 miles) ahead, was found too, and the two convoys merged into one mass of shipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Air cover arrived on 17 March, and by 20 March the growing air escort forced the attackers to give up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The British official history counts 21 ships of 141,000 tons lost for one U-boat sunk, while the U-boats claimed 32 ships, and the American naval history gives nine ships from SC 122 and eleven from HX 229.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history called it a serious disaster to the Allied cause, and the commodore of HX 229 reported that apart from U-boat attacks the voyage was fairly average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came in the worst month of the campaign, when the American history counts 85 ships sunk in all areas in the first 20 days of March, 68 per cent of the tonnage in convoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With an average of 116 U-boats operating each day in the North Atlantic, evasive routing had stopped working, because a convoy that avoided one pack ran into another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dönitz later wrote that the convoy battles of March 1943 were the peak of the U-boats’ success, although days before this one the British had lost the weather codebook through which they read the U-boat key and had to break it another way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), 364–66, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 343–44, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 1, <i>Allied Communication Intelligence, December 1942–May 1945</i>, SRH-009, ch. 3, 40, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-009/SRH009-3.html. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 3, <i>German Naval Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-024, 37–41, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-024/SRH024-4.html. [Open access]","Karl Doenitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i>, trans. Division of Naval Intelligence (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 19–20, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","C. H. O’D. Alexander, “Cryptographic History of Work on the German Naval Enigma,” c. 1945, The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/1, 48–49, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/gne/gne-000.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many U-boats find these two convoys?","a":"Because about forty boats had been spread in lines across the route, and American studies conclude that German intelligence had probably read a diversion order sent to HX 229 in an Allied cipher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once one boat made contact, its reports brought the others in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for ships sunk differ?","a":"Because the counts were made differently: the British official history gives 21 ships of 141,000 tons, the American naval history gives nine from SC 122 and eleven from HX 229, and the U-boats themselves claimed 32 ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack end on 20 March?","a":"Because air cover reached the convoys on 17 March and grew until it forced the U-boats to give up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aircraft overhead made it too dangerous for boats to stay on the surface near the convoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f8/Convoy_in_Rough_Weather.jpg","title":"Convoy in Rough Weather","desc":"A wartime painting by Rowley Murphy of a Canadian destroyer escorting a merchant convoy through heavy North Atlantic seas.","credit":"Rowley Walter Murphy, Canadian War Museum, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Convoy_in_Rough_Weather.jpg","alt":"A painting of a camouflaged warship pitching through grey waves, with merchant ships in line behind it under a stormy sky."}},
    "ww2-554": {"questions":["Reporting to Hitler on 31 May 1943, Dönitz blamed the losses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on more aircraft and a new location device that let planes surprise U-boats at night.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Germans pressed a programme of new submarines with Speer, because U-boats were being sunk faster than they could be built."],"abstract":"<b>Black May</b> is the name given to May 1943, the month in which the U-boat offensive against the North Atlantic convoys collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 1 May about sixty U-boats waited in four groups at the edge of the air gap south of Greenland, but the convoys now had support groups, escort carriers and very long range aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a series of convoy battles that began with ONS 5, the U-boats lost heavily for few sinkings, and by 22 May they had lost 33 boats and the command decided to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The toll for the whole month was 41, and an American count of boats lost in attacks on the convoys between America and Britain rose from 4 in March and 8 in April to 18 in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The collapse surprised the U-boat command, because exaggerated claims by its commanders had hidden a falling rate of success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dönitz told Hitler on 31 May that monthly losses had risen from about 14 boats to 36 or 37, some 30 per cent of the boats at sea, and that this was too high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He blamed the increase in enemy aircraft and above all a new location device that let planes find surfaced U-boats at night and in poor visibility, and he moved his boats to the area west of the Azores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 23 and 24 May he told his boats why they were leaving and called the move temporary, though an American history notes that retreat from the main convoy lanes was the sign of defeat in the U-boats’ own traffic, and the British official history judged that the convoy escorts at sea and in the air had won a decisive victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Codebreaking also counted, because from 10 March to the end of June the British read 90 of the 112 days of the U-boat key.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American naval history describes U-boats being sunk faster than they could be built after Black May, and Dönitz turned to new types of submarine with Speer’s ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), 367–77, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 60, 244–45, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","“Fuehrer Conferences on Naval Affairs, 1939–1945,” in <i>Brassey’s Naval Annual 1948</i>, ed. H. G. Thursfield (New York: Macmillan, 1948), 331–32, https://archive.org/details/brasseys-naval-annual-1948-ocr. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 2, <i>U-Boat Operations</i>, SRH-008, ch. 4, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-008/SRH008-4.html. [Open access]","C. H. O’D. Alexander, “Cryptographic History of Work on the German Naval Enigma,” c. 1945, The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/1, 49, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/gne/gne-000.htm. [Open access]","Karl Doenitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i>, trans. Division of Naval Intelligence (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 22–23, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 1, <i>Allied Communication Intelligence, December 1942–May 1945</i>, SRH-009, ch. 3, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-009/SRH009-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the U-boats suddenly lose so many boats?","a":"Because the convoys now had support groups, escort carriers and long-range aircraft, and aircraft with a new location device could find surfaced U-boats at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dönitz told Hitler that most losses happened in transit or waiting, away from the convoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Dönitz call the withdrawal temporary?","a":"Because he hoped new weapons and a radar warning set would let the boats resume convoy attacks, and he told Hitler he intended to go back to the North Atlantic at the new moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his messages to the boats he promised better weapons soon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did codebreaking matter in May 1943?","a":"Because the British were reading most days of the U-boat key in these months, 90 of 112 between 10 March and the end of June, which helped route convoys and support groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/U-boat_survivors_clambering_aboard_HMS_Escapade_May_1943.jpg","title":"U-boat survivors, May 1943","desc":"Survivors from a sunken U-boat climb aboard the British destroyer HMS Escapade in May 1943.","credit":"HMS Escapade Canteen Committee, Imperial War Museum A 17012, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U-boat_survivors_clambering_aboard_HMS_Escapade_May_1943.jpg","alt":"Men in the water and on a scrambling net climbing up the side of a warship, with others still swimming nearby."},"wiki":"Black May (World War II)"},
    "ww2-555": {"questions":["More than a hundred boats were completed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> design before the surrender, yet only U-2511 sailed on a war patrol, and that only in the last days of fighting.","Very large batteries and a hull shaped for running underwater gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a burst of about 17 knots submerged, though it still needed a snorkel to recharge."],"abstract":"The <b>Type XXI submarine</b> was the large ‘electro-boat’ that Germany designed after the defeats of 1943 so that its U-boats could fight underwater rather than on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A streamlined boat of about 1,500 tons with diesel-electric engines and very large batteries, it could make 17.5 knots submerged for short distances but needed a snorkel to run its diesels underwater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dönitz saw it as the answer to radar-equipped aircraft, which had made the old tactic of attacking convoys on the surface impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Speer’s armaments ministry gave O. H. Merker, an expert in motor-car production, charge of building it by mass production, with hull sections made in dispersed factories and assembled at the yards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dönitz ordered 290 to be built by 1 March 1945, and the first, U-2501, was launched on 20 April 1944, Hitler’s birthday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hurry showed, for the first boats were so defective that months of work were needed before they could go to sea, and British air-laid mines disrupted crew training in the Baltic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mass assembly also concentrated the risk, and Dönitz warned Hitler in May 1944 that thirty to forty parts of one kind were built at once in one yard, so a single raid could cost as many boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A bombproof bunker at Farge near Bremen, meant to assemble 14 boats a month, was hit by heavy bombs in March 1945 and never began production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The counts differ: the American naval history gives 119 boats completed, while the British official history says that by May 1945 twelve had finished working up beside the operational U-2511, with 91 more on trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> U-2511 sailed on the only war patrol of the type in the last days of the war, in April by British and German accounts and on 3 May by an American study of the intercepts, and it was still outbound at the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 60–62, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Karl Doenitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i>, trans. Division of Naval Intelligence (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 23, 29–30, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, part 2 (London: HMSO, 1961), 141, 294–302, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-2. [Open access]","“Fuehrer Conferences on Naval Affairs, 1939–1945,” in <i>Brassey’s Naval Annual 1948</i>, ed. H. G. Thursfield (New York: Macmillan, 1948), 391–92, https://archive.org/details/brasseys-naval-annual-1948-ocr. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Munitions Division, <i>Submarine Assembly Shelter, Farge, Germany</i>, 2nd ed. (Washington, DC, 1947), 1–3, https://archive.org/details/ussbs-99-submarine-assembly-shelter-at-farge. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 2, <i>U-Boat Operations</i>, SRH-008, 162–63, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-008/SRH008-12.html. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 1, <i>Allied Communication Intelligence, December 1942–May 1945</i>, SRH-009, 59–60, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-009/SRH009-5.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany need a submarine that was fast underwater?","a":"Because by 1943 aircraft with radar could find U-boats on the surface by day and night, and the old type could only move slowly when submerged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dönitz wanted a boat as handy underwater as the old ones had been on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was it built in prefabricated sections?","a":"Because sections could be made quickly in widely dispersed factories, which gave better protection against bombing and raised the capacity of the yards almost fourfold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sections were then joined at assembly yards, and a bombproof assembly bunker was planned at Farge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few ever reach the enemy?","a":"Because the first boats were full of defects, mines disrupted training in the Baltic, and the bombing of transport and assembly yards caused month after month of delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An American study notes that the type had failed its final tests by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Typ_XXI_-_Wilhelm_Bauer_-_Bremerhaven_01.jpg","title":"The Type XXI boat Wilhelm Bauer","desc":"The preserved Type XXI submarine Wilhelm Bauer, formerly U-2540, at the museum harbour in Bremerhaven, Germany.","credit":"Llez, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Typ_XXI_-_Wilhelm_Bauer_-_Bremerhaven_01.jpg","alt":"The streamlined bow of a grey submarine moored in a harbour, seen from the front, with a tall smooth conning tower behind."},"wiki":"Type XXI submarine"},
    "ww2-556": {"questions":["In 1941 King Haakon VII told the staff of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that seeing them helped him understand his position as the world’s largest shipowner, for it managed Norway’s requisitioned fleet.","Almost 90 per cent of the Norwegian government’s revenue in exile came from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which earned freight by chartering the country’s requisitioned ships to the Allies."],"abstract":"<b>Nortraship</b>, the Norwegian Shipping and Trade Mission, was the body through which Norway’s exiled government ran the country’s merchant fleet for the Allies from 1940 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the outbreak of war Norway had the fourth largest merchant fleet in the world, of about 4.8 million gross tons, and its tankers made up about a fifth of the world’s tanker tonnage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In November 1939 a still neutral Norway placed some 150 tankers at British disposal under a tonnage agreement that was only initialled, so that it would look like a gentlemen’s agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Germany invaded on 9 April 1940, more than a thousand ships with about 30,000 seamen were outside home waters, and later that month the government requisitioned every ship over 500 gross tons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masters ignored German broadcasts ordering them into neutral or German-held ports, helped by British promises of insurance and cash drawn up with a leading Norwegian shipowner, and in January 1941 five more ships broke out of Gothenburg in Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Nortraship took its name from its telegram address and opened in London in April 1940, with a second head office in New York that managed about a third of the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the largest shipping company in the world, and in 1941 King Haakon VII told its staff that seeing them helped him understand his position as the world’s largest shipowner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its earnings paid for the government in exile, which received almost £80 million from Nortraship between July 1940 and June 1945, nearly 90 per cent of its revenue, and the British history notes that the ships were that government’s sole asset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cost fell on the crews, and Norwegian estimates of the seamen killed range from about 3,000 to 3,700, and of the ships lost from about 500 to more than 700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the war a secret fund built from the difference between Norwegian and British wages became a long grievance, and the Storting granted the seamen 155 million kroner only in 1972.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Stig Tenold, “The Second World War,” in <i>Norwegian Shipping in the 20th Century</i> (Cham: Palgrave Macmillan, 2019), 133–45, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-95639-8_5. [Open access]","Frode Rabbevåg, “Nortraship,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Nortraship. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Tor Wisting, “Krigsseiler,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/krigsseiler. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Jordan Siemianowski, “Behind the Scenes of Norway’s Role in the Second World War: The Norwegian-British Tonnage Agreement from 11th November 1939,” <i>Folia Scandinavica Posnaniensia</i> 11 (2010): 45–58, https://presstoarch.amu.edu.pl/index.php/fsp/article/view/3613. [Open access]","C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i> (London: HMSO, 1955), 93–94, 98–99, https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), 392–93, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Norway’s ships not go home after the invasion?","a":"Because the government requisitioned them and the masters refused German orders to make for neutral or German-held ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British offered insurance, cash and instructions to masters who sailed to Allied ports, on advice from a Norwegian shipowner in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fleet matter so much to the exiled government?","a":"Because it was the government’s one great asset after 1940: freight earnings gave it almost £80 million, nearly 90 per cent of its revenue, and a way to show that Norway still existed as a nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for losses differ?","a":"Because Norwegian reference works and historians count differently: one article gives about 3,000 seamen and 500 ships lost, another 3,700 seamen, and a shipping historian more than 700 ships and around 3,700 sailors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Nortraships_office_in_London%2C_Tanker_Departement_1944.jpg","title":"Nortraship’s tanker department, 1944","desc":"Staff of Nortraship’s tanker department at work in its London office on 20 March 1944.","credit":"Unknown photographer, National Archives of Norway, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nortraships_office_in_London%2C_Tanker_Departement_1944.jpg","alt":"Five people in an office with desks, papers and telephones, two women and three men, looking towards the camera."},"wiki":"Nortraship"},
    "ww2-557": {"questions":["The first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Patrick Henry, was launched at Baltimore on 27 September 1941, and by December 1943 the average time to build one had fallen to 39 days.","Hulls that cracked with explosive suddenness in cold weather, often from hatch corners, made the welded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the main subject of a wartime naval inquiry into fractures."],"abstract":"The <b>Liberty ship</b> was the standard emergency cargo ship of the United States Maritime Commission, the EC2 type of 10,500 tons deadweight, which made up more than half of the welded merchant ships the Commission built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was adapted from a British tramp-steamer design, with an all-welded hull and a 2,500-horsepower triple-expansion steam engine that gave about 11 knots loaded and was easy for inexperienced engineers to run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in an order for sixty cargo ships that Britain placed with the Kaiser and Todd interests in December 1940, which the British history of war production calls the first contract for Liberty ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first Liberty, the Patrick Henry, was launched at Baltimore on 27 September 1941 and delivered on the last day of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its slowness meant it needed escorts, and Dönitz, waging a war on tonnage, counted a Liberty returning empty from Africa as good a target as a loaded one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Building times fell steeply, from an average of 235 days from keel to delivery for the first ships to 122 days by June 1942 and 55 days by that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commission reported a low of 39.2 days in December 1943, and some yards built ships in 2 weeks or less as demonstrations of prefabrication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By mid-1945 it counted 2,587 regular Liberty cargo ships delivered for the United States, while a naval board listed 2,710 ships of the EC2 family in service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Welding brought a hazard, for hulls cracked with explosive suddenness, above all in winter, and in Liberty ships a quarter of all fractures began at the corners of hatches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One Liberty, the Stephen Hopkins, armed with a single 4-inch gun, fought the German raider Stier in the South Atlantic in September 1942, and both ships sank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 294, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","United States Maritime Commission, <i>Report to Congress for the Period Ended June 30, 1942</i> (Washington, DC, 1943), 9–10, https://rosap.ntl.bts.gov/view/dot/77222/dot_77222_DS1.pdf. [Open access]","United States Maritime Commission, <i>Report to Congress for the Period Ended June 30, 1945</i> (Washington, DC, 1946), 1–3, https://www.fmc.gov/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/REPORT_TO_CONGRESS_1945.pdf. [Open access]","Board of Investigation Convened by Order of the Secretary of the Navy, <i>Final Report on the Design and Methods of Construction of Welded Steel Merchant Vessels</i>, 15 July 1946 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), 4–5, 9, 20, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_AD0636332. [Open access]","M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i> (London: HMSO, 1952), 230–31, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-5.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), 266, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 58–59, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was such a slow, old-fashioned ship chosen?","a":"Because it could be built fast and run by inexperienced crews: its triple-expansion engine was simple to build and operate, while a faster ship would have taken far longer to produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its welded hull suited mass production in new yards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did building times fall so fast?","a":"Because the Commission treated the ship as a mass-production assembly project in eighteen new yards, and prefabrication and experience cut the average from 235 days to 55 within a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The low point was 39.2 days in December 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some Liberty ships crack?","a":"Because their all-welded hulls behaved differently from riveted ones, and fractures started at points of stress such as hatch corners, especially in cold weather; a naval board investigated the failures and found a quarter began at hatch corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/NH_75817_%22Liberty%22_ship_RICHMOND_MUMFORD_PEARSON.jpg","title":"Launch of a Liberty ship, 1942","desc":"The Liberty ship Richmond Mumford Pearson is launched at the Delta Shipbuilding Company, New Orleans, on 27 October 1942.","credit":"US Navy, Naval History and Heritage Command NH 75817, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NH_75817_%22Liberty%22_ship_RICHMOND_MUMFORD_PEARSON.jpg","alt":"A newly built cargo ship sliding stern first down a slipway into the water, with crowds and cranes on the shore."},"wiki":"Liberty ship"},
    "ww2-558": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread eighteen new shipyards along the American coasts to avoid the congestion that had slowed shipbuilding in the First World War.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> assembled ships from prefabricated sections, the German U-boat builders copied Henry Kaiser’s method for their new submarines."],"abstract":"The <b>Emergency Shipbuilding Program</b> was the United States Maritime Commission’s wartime drive to build merchant ships in great numbers, begun in 1941 because its long-range ship types took too long to build for the emergency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was run as mass production, with ships of simplified design assembled in eighteen new yards spread along the coasts to avoid the congestion of the First World War’s great yards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early in 1942 the President directed the Commission to build 8 million deadweight tons of shipping that year and 16 million in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The yards delivered 760 ships in 1942 and 1,949 of about 19.3 million deadweight tons in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Henry Kaiser’s organisation for prefabricated shipbuilding, set going in the spring of 1941, made his method so well known that German U-boat builders later copied it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In 1944 the Liberty programme was curtailed, with no new contracts after April, and production moved to the faster, turbine-driven Victory ship, the first delivered on 29 February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of June 1945 the Commission had overseen the delivery of 6,469 vessels of all types since 1937, including 1,215 small craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Speed came from all-welded hulls, and a naval board found that 970 of the 4,694 welded ships it studied had suffered fractures, and that eight had been lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain had agreed to concentrate on warships and leave most merchant building to the Americans, and a British note of late 1942 said its operations rested on the belief that it could rely on American shipbuilding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The British official history concluded that even after American yards had overtaken the losses of merchant ships, it was the supply of specialised landing craft that set the scale of Allied offensives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Maritime Commission, <i>Report to Congress for the Period Ended June 30, 1942</i> (Washington, DC, 1943), 9–10, https://rosap.ntl.bts.gov/view/dot/77222/dot_77222_DS1.pdf. [Open access]","United States Maritime Commission, <i>Report to Congress for the Period Ended June 30, 1945</i> (Washington, DC, 1946), 1–6, https://www.fmc.gov/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/REPORT_TO_CONGRESS_1945.pdf. [Open access]","M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i> (London: HMSO, 1952), 230–31, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-5.html. [Open access]","Board of Investigation Convened by Order of the Secretary of the Navy, <i>Final Report on the Design and Methods of Construction of Welded Steel Merchant Vessels</i>, 15 July 1946 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), 9, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_AD0636332. [Open access]","C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i> (London: HMSO, 1955), 363–64, https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 61, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, part 2 (London: HMSO, 1961), 401–2, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States need an emergency programme?","a":"Because the Commission’s long-range ship types took too long to build, while war demanded far more shipping than the merchant fleet could supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The answer was a simplified ship built by mass production in new yards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain depend on American yards?","a":"Because Britain had agreed to put its limited resources into warships and leave most merchant construction to the United States, so its own fleet kept shrinking while the American one grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were welded hulls a problem?","a":"Because welding sped construction but welded hulls fractured with explosive suddenness in some conditions, and a naval board found that 970 of 4,694 welded ships had suffered fractures, eight being lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Skilled_women_workers_helped_build_SS_George_Washington_Carver.jpg","title":"Women scalers at a Kaiser yard, 1943","desc":"Two women scalers at work on the Liberty ship George Washington Carver at the Kaiser shipyards in Richmond, California, in April 1943.","credit":"E. F. Joseph, Office of War Information, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skilled_women_workers_helped_build_SS_George_Washington_Carver.jpg","alt":"Two women in work clothes and caps holding scaling tools beside the steel side of a ship under construction."},"wiki":"Emergency Shipbuilding Program"},
    "ww2-559": {"questions":["Disguised as a Dutch merchant ship, the German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Kormoran drew the Australian cruiser Sydney to close range in November 1941, and both ships were lost.","By the British count each German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried four identities, and together they sank or captured 98 ships of about 593,000 tons in 1940 and 1941."],"abstract":"An <b>auxiliary cruiser</b> was a merchant ship converted into a warship, and Germany’s auxiliary cruisers of the Second World War were disguised raiders sent to sink and capture Allied shipping on distant oceans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German navy planned to convert twenty-six ships, and nine reached the open sea, each with a merchant name, a warship name, a ship number and a letter given by the British Admiralty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Atlantis, for example, was Ship 16 and Raider C and had been the motor ship Goldenfels of a Bremen line, and captures by such raiders gave German naval intelligence British code material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hidden guns, torpedoes and mines let them pass as harmless freighters, and supply ships and prizes kept them at sea for remarkable periods, the Orion for 510 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their orders said that war on merchant shipping was the task and that combat even with weaker warships was not to be sought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the British count they sank or captured 98 ships of about 593,000 tons in 1940 and 1941, more than the German warship raiders achieved in the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raeder told Hitler in May 1941 that Ship 33, the Pinguin, had sunk or taken about 120,000 tons, three Antarctic whale factory ships among them, before a British cruiser sank her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 19 November 1941 the Kormoran, posing as a Dutch ship, drew the Australian cruiser Sydney within about 1,800 metres (2,000 yards) and opened fire, and none of the Sydney’s 645 men survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kormoran, burning, was scuttled, and 315 of her crew of 400 survived, so the only accounts of the battle come from the German side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1942 the raiders broke out down the Channel under heavy escort, but their returns kept falling, and Dönitz later blamed British radar for the loss of the raiders outside home waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), 278–80, 541, 548–50, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i> (London: HMSO, 1956), 164, 266, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]","“Fuehrer Conferences on Naval Affairs, 1939–1945,” in <i>Brassey’s Naval Annual 1948</i>, ed. H. G. Thursfield (New York: Macmillan, 1948), 196–97, https://archive.org/details/brasseys-naval-annual-1948-ocr. [Open access]","Karl Doenitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i>, trans. Division of Naval Intelligence (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 13, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “HMAS Sydney II and the Kormoran,” Encyclopedia, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/hmas_sydney. [Open access]","Jürgen Rohwer, “Radio Intelligence in the Battle of the Atlantic,” in <i>The Intelligence Revolution: A Historical Perspective</i>, ed. Walter T. Hitchcock (Washington, DC: Office of Air Force History, 1991), 79, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA433346. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the raiders avoid fights with warships?","a":"Because their orders made war on merchant shipping the task, and even slight damage could cut their effectiveness and their endurance far from any friendly port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kormoran fought the Sydney only after her disguise was about to be exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a disguised raider so hard to catch?","a":"Because it looked like an ordinary freighter, and a warship could only be sure by asking shore authorities where every genuine Allied merchant ship was, a plot that took years to perfect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the raiders become less successful?","a":"Because the spread of convoy, the hunting down of their supply ships and better plotting of Allied shipping left fewer victims, and the British official figures show sinkings falling to six ships in the second half of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1969-117-48%2C_Hilfskreuzer_Kormoran.jpg","title":"The auxiliary cruiser Kormoran","desc":"The German auxiliary cruiser Kormoran, a converted freighter, photographed at sea from a German U-boat.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1969-117-48, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1969-117-48%2C_Hilfskreuzer_Kormoran.jpg","alt":"A plain merchant-looking ship with one funnel and cargo masts steaming on a calm grey sea."}},
    "ww2-560": {"questions":["In the Denmark Strait on 24 May 1941 the battlecruiser Hood blew up, leaving three survivors, during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the German navy’s great sortie into the Atlantic.","A torpedo from a Swordfish that jammed the Bismarck’s rudder on the evening of 26 May 1941 doomed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and let British battleships catch her the next morning."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Rheinübung</b>, ‘Rhine Exercise’, was the sortie of the new German battleship Bismarck and the heavy cruiser Prinz Eugen into the Atlantic in May 1941, planned as a 3-month raid on Allied merchant shipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The orders were to attack enemy supply traffic north of the equator for as long as the situation allowed, and tankers and supply ships were stationed at sea beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ships left Norway on 21 May, were reported gone from Bergen by a British aircraft on 22 May, and were sighted by the cruiser Suffolk in the Denmark Strait on the evening of 23 May, after which her radar kept them in view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early on 24 May the battlecruiser Hood and the battleship Prince of Wales engaged them, and the Hood blew up within minutes, leaving three survivors from a crew of more than 1,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A hit from the Prince of Wales cost the Bismarck fuel, so Admiral Lütjens detached the Prinz Eugen and turned for a French port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The British lost touch early on 25 May, and a Coastal Command Catalina found the battleship again in the morning of 26 May, about 1,110 kilometres (690 miles) from Brest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official intelligence history adds that an air force Enigma decrypt had shown she was heading for France, though it judges that the searches would probably have found her anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That evening Swordfish torpedo bombers from the carrier Ark Royal jammed her rudder, which Tovey called the immediate and principal cause of her being brought to action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 27 May the King George V and Rodney wrecked her with gunfire, the cruiser Dorsetshire torpedoed her, and she sank with 110 survivors picked up by British ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dönitz called the loss a grave one and thought ship-borne radar had decided it, while captured papers soon let the British destroy the supply ships prepared for the sortie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), 395–418, 542, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","John C. Tovey, “Sinking of the German Battleship Bismarck on 27th May, 1941,” despatch of July 1941, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38098, 16 October 1947, 4847–58, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38098/supplement/4847/data.pdf. [Open access]","“Fuehrer Conferences on Naval Affairs, 1939–1945,” in <i>Brassey’s Naval Annual 1948</i>, ed. H. G. Thursfield (New York: Macmillan, 1948), 195–220, https://archive.org/details/brasseys-naval-annual-1948-ocr. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War</i>, vol. 1 (London: HMSO, 1979), 343–45, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Karl Doenitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i>, trans. Division of Naval Intelligence (Washington, DC: Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 13, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","Jürgen Rohwer, “Radio Intelligence in the Battle of the Atlantic,” in <i>The Intelligence Revolution: A Historical Perspective</i>, ed. Walter T. Hitchcock (Washington, DC: Office of Air Force History, 1991), 82, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA433346. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>History of United States Naval Operations in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i> (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 62–65, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Hood sink so quickly?","a":"Because a shell hit set off an enormous explosion between her after funnel and mainmast, and she sank in 3 or 4 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history says the exact cause will never be established and compares it with the battlecruisers lost at Jutland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler question the operation afterwards?","a":"Because he asked why the Bismarck had not attacked the damaged Prince of Wales again after sinking the Hood, even at the risk of loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Raeder replied that she had re-engaged to cover the Prinz Eugen’s escape and that the British ship had kept out of range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the United States watch the sortie closely?","a":"Because no American ship then in service could match the Bismarck, and the American naval history says the battle stressed the strategic importance of Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Her near escape showed what a single fast battleship could threaten on the Atlantic routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-055-13%2C_Schlachtschiff_Bismarck%2C_Seegefecht.jpg","title":"Bismarck in action, 24 May 1941","desc":"The Bismarck fires on the Prince of Wales during the battle in the Denmark Strait, photographed from the Prinz Eugen.","credit":"Lagemann, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1984-055-13, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-055-13%2C_Schlachtschiff_Bismarck%2C_Seegefecht.jpg","alt":"A large warship in the distance on a grey sea, with a bright muzzle flash and dark smoke from its forward guns."},"wiki":"Operation Rheinübung"},
    "ww2-511": {"questions":["Opening with a retreat of nearly 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) in 1942 and ending with Rangoon reoccupied without a fight in May 1945, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought between India and China.","Churchill told the Commons in August 1944 that more than 250,000 British and Imperial troops were committed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along a front of some 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles)."],"abstract":"The <b>Burma campaign</b> was the war fought for Burma and the Indian frontier between Japan and the forces of the British Empire, China and the United States, from the Japanese invasion of January 1942 to the end of the war in August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its first 5 months the Japanese drove the Allied armies out, and the British and Indian forces fell back nearly 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) to India at a cost of 13,463 casualties against 4,597 Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next 2 years brought a failed offensive in the coastal Arakan and the first long-range raids behind Japanese lines, and in 1944 the defeat of a Japanese attempt to invade India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in August 1944 that more than 250,000 British and Imperial troops were committed to the advance, along a front of some 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1945 the Fourteenth Army drove south through central Burma, and Rangoon, held by the Japanese for 3 years and 2 months, was reoccupied without fighting on 3 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Allies never fully agreed what the campaign was for, and a study of Allied strategy in 1942 describes bitter discord between the British, American and Chinese governments over their priorities in Burma from the outset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the Americans the theatre existed to keep China in the war, and their air force's history judged that the final drive to Rangoon bore little relation to that primary mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same history records that the joint Anglo-American air command in South-East Asia was dissolved after the campaign, in belated recognition that the two allies' interests differed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The theatre also stood low in both allies' priorities, lying at the far end of the supply line from America and well down Britain's list throughout 1942 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountbatten thought it imperative to destroy the impression that South-East Asia was the “Forgotten Front”, and in August 1944 Churchill told the Commons that the 14th Army must not be forgotten because of larger events nearer home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["National Army Museum, “The Far East Campaign,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/far-east-campaign. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India's Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 209–10, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 August 1944, vol. 402, cols. 1462–63, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 397, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","Brian Farrell, “By the Seat of the Pants? Allied Strategy and the Japanese Onslaught in Southeast Asia, December 1941–May 1942,” in <i>Strategy in the Pacific War</i>, 2009 International Forum on War History: Proceedings (Tokyo: National Institute for Defense Studies), 73, https://www.nids.mod.go.jp/english/event/forum/pdf/2009/06.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, “Foreword,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/index.html. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 16–17, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies disagree about what the campaign was for?","a":"Because each wanted something different from it. A study of Allied strategy in 1942 finds bitter discord between the British, American and Chinese governments over their priorities in Burma from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the Americans the point was to keep China in the war, and their air force's own history later judged that the drive to Rangoon bore little relation to that mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British, meanwhile, ranked Asia well below other theatres in 1942 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Burma called a forgotten front?","a":"Because it was far from home and low in every ally's priorities. It lay at the extreme end of the supply line from America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mountbatten found a widespread impression that South-East Asia was the “Forgotten Front” and set out to destroy it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In August 1944 Churchill reminded the Commons that the 14th Army must not be forgotten because of the larger events nearer home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the opening retreat matter so much?","a":"Because it set the terms for everything after. The British and Indian forces were driven nearly 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) back to India and lost 13,463 men against 4,597 Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Without adequate resources a major counter-offensive was out of the question, and the first attempt, in the Arakan, failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only in 1945 did it retake Rangoon, which the Japanese had held for 3 years and 2 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Douglas_DC-3_-_British_Army_in_Burma_1944_SE588.jpg","title":"Unloading an air-supply aircraft, Burma, 1944","desc":"Troops of the Royal Indian Army Service Corps unload an American C-47 transport at an airstrip in the Pinwe area on 21 November 1944; air supply carried much of the campaign's weight.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums (SE 588), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Douglas_DC-3_-_British_Army_in_Burma_1944_SE588.jpg","alt":"A twin-engined transport aircraft marked COM-CAR stands on a dry airstrip with its cargo door open while men pass loads down to a military truck parked alongside."},"wiki":"Burma campaign"},
    "ww2-512": {"questions":["Before the Chindwin crossing at Shwegyin the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned the tanks and lorries it could not take up the narrow track, and its last units reached Tamu as the monsoon broke.","On 15 April 1942 the commander of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered the Yenangyaung oilfields destroyed, and the Chinese 38th Division attacked to free his trapped 1st Burma Division on the Pin Chaung."],"abstract":"<b>Burma Corps</b>, usually shortened to Burcorps, was the corps headquarters formed in March 1942 to command the British, Indian and Burmese divisions retreating before the Japanese 15th Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lieutenant-General William Slim flew in from India on 19 March to take command of the 1st Burma Division, the 17th Indian Division and the 7th Armoured Brigade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander had asked India for a corps commander and staff, but for lack of air transport most of the headquarters and its signals had to be found from the troops already in Burma, and it was never fully staffed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the loss of Rangoon its task was to hold the Irrawaddy valley and the oilfields at Yenangyaung, while Chinese armies under Stilwell held the Sittang valley to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 15 April Slim ordered the oilfields destroyed, and the Chinese 38th Division attacked to free the 1st Burma Division from a Japanese roadblock on the Pin Chaung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The corps then fell back to the Chindwin, and on 10 May its rearguard left the Shwegyin basin, burning the tanks, lorries and stores it could not carry, so that the army reached India without most of its equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its last units marched up the malarial Kabaw Valley and reached Tamu between 14 and 19 May, as the first heavy rains of the monsoon began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official history calls it a retreat of nearly 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) in 3.5 months, the longest ever made by a British army, while the National Army Museum puts the distance at about 1,400 kilometres (900 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The losses are counted differently too: the British history gives 13,463 British, Indian and Burmese casualties against 4,597 Japanese, while the American history says some 12,000 exhausted survivors reached India after 13,000 casualties, 9,000 of them missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wavell judged Slim and his divisional commanders good and resolute, and Mountbatten later called the campaign a military defeat but not a major disaster, since the army came out more or less intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India's Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 5, 147–48, 167–73, 209–10, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","Archibald P. Wavell, “Operations in Burma from 15th December, 1941, to 20th May, 1942,” with the reports of T. J. Hutton and Harold Alexander, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38228, 11 March 1948, 1674, 1698, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38228/page/1698/data.pdf. [Open access]","Charles F. Romanus and Riley Sunderland, <i>Stilwell's Mission to China</i>, United States Army in World War II: China-Burma-India Theater (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), 125–26, 139–40, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-CBI-Mission/USA-CBI-Mission-4.html. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “The Far East Campaign,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/far-east-campaign. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “William Slim: The Soldiers' Soldier,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/william-slim. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 16, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a corps headquarters created in the middle of a retreat?","a":"Because Alexander, now nominally in command of the Chinese armies too, could not also handle the divisions on the Irrawaddy himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He asked India for a corps commander, and Slim arrived on 19 March 1942 to take over the 1st Burma Division, the 17th Indian Division and the 7th Armoured Brigade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Air transport was so short that the staff and signals had to be found from troops already in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the corps destroy the Yenangyaung oilfields?","a":"Because they were Burma's main source of oil and must not fall to the Japanese intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slim held back the order as long as possible, since the Allied forces still needed the petrol, and gave it only on 15 April as Japanese troops closed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The burning came just before a Japanese roadblock trapped the 1st Burma Division north of the fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the distance and the losses of the retreat given so differently?","a":"Because different bodies measured them in different ways. The British official history speaks of nearly 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) and 13,463 casualties, including Burmese units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American history counts some 13,000 casualties, 9,000 of them missing, and 12,000 survivors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The National Army Museum gives the distance as about 1,400 kilometres (900 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/British_troops_destroy_equipment_and_machinery_at_the_Yenangyaung_oilfields_in_Burma_before_retreating%2C_16_April_1942._IND989.jpg","title":"Demolitions at Yenangyaung, 16 April 1942","desc":"British troops wreck equipment and machinery at the Yenangyaung oilfields before the retreat continued north.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums (IND 989), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_troops_destroy_equipment_and_machinery_at_the_Yenangyaung_oilfields_in_Burma_before_retreating,_16_April_1942._IND989.jpg","alt":"Huge clouds of black smoke and flame billow up behind wrecked machinery and pipework half hidden by scrub."},"wiki":"Burma Corps"},
    "ww2-513": {"questions":["British doctors traced every malaria outbreak among troops on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to men who had not swallowed their daily tablet, and found that some officers doubted the drug worked at all.","As daily <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tablets and forward treatment units took hold, the ratio of sick to wounded in South-East Asia fell from 120 to one in 1943 to ten to one in 1945."],"abstract":"<b>Mepacrine</b>, sold in the United States as Atabrine and also known as quinacrine, was a synthetic drug against malaria whose daily tablet became the main defence of the Allied armies in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German chemists first made it in 1930, part of a search for substitutes for quinine that began when Germany was cut off from the drug in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Japan overran the Netherlands East Indies, the Allies lost the principal source of quinine, solved the secret of making mepacrine and put it into mass production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Experiments in Australia in 1943 showed that one tablet of 100 milligrams a day prevented attacks of malaria, curing the falciparum form and holding the vivax form in check until the drug was stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it suppressed the disease rather than preventing infection, it worked only if every man took his tablet every day, which made malaria a matter of military discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The need was plain in Burma, where during the monsoon of 1943 the Eastern Army admitted 120 sick men to hospital for every wounded one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Malaria Forward Treatment Units close behind the fighting divisions cut the time a malaria case spent away from his unit from months to weeks, and by mid-1944 the Fourteenth Army was taking twelve million tablets a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mountbatten's figures show the annual malaria rate falling from 84 per cent of army strength in 1943 to 13 per cent by 1945, and the ratio of sick to wounded from 120 to one to ten to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British medical history found that every outbreak it could trace in units on mepacrine came from men failing to take their tablets, and that some senior officers did not believe the drug worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Disease never vanished: between June and November 1944 one corps lost 47,098 men to sickness, 20,430 of them to malaria and other fevers, against 3,289 battle casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Medical Department, United States Army, <i>Preventive Medicine in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Communicable Diseases: Malaria</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1963), 6–7, https://archive.org/details/1278003RX5.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 5, <i>Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 123–25, 239–40, 647, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-5. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 34–35, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 16, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 460–61, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","George J. Giffard, “Operations in Burma and North-East India from 16th November, 1943 to 22nd June, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39171, 19 March 1951, 1369, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39171/supplement/1369/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies need a synthetic drug at all?","a":"Because quinine, the only effective remedy for over 300 years, came mainly from the Netherlands East Indies, and Japan's conquest cut the Allies off from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mepacrine, first made in Germany in 1930, could be manufactured anywhere once its secret was solved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Burma, where malaria was rife, it became the daily defence of whole armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did taking the tablet become a matter of discipline?","a":"Because the drug kept malaria in check only while it was taken every day; it did not stop infection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British medical history found that outbreaks in units on mepacrine came from men not taking their tablets, and that some senior officers doubted it worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Commanders therefore had to enforce it, as they enforced any other order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was disease such a threat in Burma?","a":"Because the troops lived and fought in malarial jungle and through the monsoon. In 1943 the Eastern Army admitted 120 sick to hospital for every wounded man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even in 1944 one corps lost 47,098 men to sickness against 3,289 battle casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mountbatten's figures show the malaria rate falling from 84 per cent of strength in 1943 to 13 per cent by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/The_Royal_Naval_School_of_Malaria_and_Hygiene_Control%2C_Nr_Colombo%2C_Ceylon%2C_December_1944_A28178.jpg","title":"Anti-malaria work in Ceylon, December 1944","desc":"At the Royal Naval School of Malaria and Hygiene Control near Colombo, a petty officer holds aside brushwood while a hidden pool is sprayed with DDT; drugs such as mepacrine were one part of the wider campaign against malaria in South-East Asia Command.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, Imperial War Museums (A 28178), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Royal_Naval_School_of_Malaria_and_Hygiene_Control,_Nr_Colombo,_Ceylon,_December_1944_A28178.jpg","alt":"A sailor in a peaked cap and shorts bends to pull aside dry branches while a young man with a sprayer tank on his back aims its nozzle at the ground, with palm trees behind."},"wiki":"Mepacrine"},
    "ww2-514": {"questions":["Of about 3,000 men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who crossed into Burma in February 1943, some 2,182 returned 4 months later, having marched at least 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) behind Japanese lines.","Gliders landed the first men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at a clearing code-named Broadway on 5 March 1944, beginning a fly-in that carried over 9,000 people and 1,300 animals."],"abstract":"The <b>Chindits</b> were the long-range penetration brigades raised in India under Orde Wingate to fight behind Japanese lines in Burma, moving on foot and supplied from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wavell approved one such brigade in 1942 under the cover name 77th Indian Infantry Brigade, organised in eight columns that each carried an RAF section with wireless to call for supply drops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name came from the chinthe, the half-lion, half-eagle beast whose statues guard Burmese pagodas, which Wingate chose as an emblem of cooperation between ground and air forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1943 about 3,000 men crossed the Chindwin, cut the railway and pressed east of the Irrawaddy, and about 2,182 came back 4 months later after marching at least 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1944 a much larger Special Force was flown in by glider and transport aircraft, and the British count gives about 400 men landed at Broadway on the first night while the American count gives 539.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> By 11 March, by American figures, 9,052 people and 1,359 animals had been flown in, and the columns lived entirely on air supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The National Army Museum credits them with raising morale and drawing Japanese forces from the main battles, and Churchill praised their support for Stilwell's advance in northern Burma in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Disease was a constant enemy: an officer of 1943 recorded that 29 of his 30 men caught malaria after recrossing the Chindwin, and by July 1944 most men in two of its brigades had had at least three attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history judged that the first expedition had little strategic value but proved that troops could be supplied by air, and that it showed Mutaguchi and other Japanese commanders the hills could be crossed, helping lead them to disaster in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of Special Force it concluded that the results did not justify the resources taken from the 14th Army, while Mountbatten held that Wingate's operations had shown well-trained troops to be superior to the Japanese in the jungle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India's Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 243–45, 324, 327–29, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 181, 412, 446, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","Lee Bowen, “Allied Offensive in Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 493–94, 503–506, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “The Far East Campaign,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/far-east-campaign. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 17, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 August 1944, vol. 402, col. 1462, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 5, <i>Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 239–40, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Chindits depend on aircraft?","a":"Because they marched far beyond any road. Each column carried an RAF section with wireless to call for drops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1944 the whole of Special Force was flown in by glider and transport aircraft, 9,052 people and 1,359 animals by 11 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official history thought proving that troops could live on air supply was the first expedition's most valuable lesson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is their value still argued over?","a":"Because their costs were high and their gains hard to measure. The British official history judged that Special Force's results were not worth the resources taken from the 14th Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The National Army Museum stresses the morale they raised and the Japanese forces they drew away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mountbatten saw them as proof that trained troops could beat the Japanese in the jungle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the first expedition matter to the Japanese?","a":"Because it changed their thinking. Japanese commanders, Mutaguchi among them, saw that troops could cross the hills along the frontier, and their defensive policy gave way to an offensive one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official history calls that change the expedition's outstanding outcome and says it eventually led the Japanese to disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Chindit_column%2C_Operation_Longcloth.jpg","title":"A Chindit column crossing a river, 1943","desc":"Men and mules of a Chindit column cross a river in Burma during the first expedition of 1943.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chindit_column,_Operation_Longcloth.jpg","alt":"Soldiers with heavy packs and rifles wait on a sandy bank while others lead laden mules across a wide, shallow river."},"wiki":"Chindits"},
    "ww2-515": {"questions":["Churchill took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Quebec conference in August 1943, where the Allies agreed to a much larger long-range penetration force to fight behind Japanese lines under his command.","Churchill told the Commons that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, killed when his bomber crashed west of Imphal in March 1944, had been a man of genius who might well have become a man of destiny."],"abstract":"<b>Orde Wingate</b> was a British officer who led irregular forces in Palestine and Ethiopia and then created the Chindits, the long-range penetration brigades that fought behind Japanese lines in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wavell chose him in 1940 to help organise the Ethiopian Patriots against the Italians, because of an impression formed in Palestine in 1939 that he might lead unorthodox enterprises well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early in 1941 he commanded Gideon Force, which harried the Italian garrisons of Gojjam and cleared the way for the Emperor Haile Selassie, who entered his capital on 5 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wavell sent for him again in 1942, and in India he argued that regular troops supplied by air and directed by wireless could operate for long periods in the heart of enemy territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He led the first Chindit expedition into Burma in February 1943, which Mountbatten thought proved trained troops superior to the Japanese in the jungle, and became almost overnight a national hero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Churchill met him in London in August 1943 and took him to the Quebec conference, where the Allies agreed to a far larger long-range penetration force under his command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Promoted major-general, he built Special Force largely from a broken-up British division, but his insistence that hope of victory lay in his force alone, and a manner often bordering on rudeness, made enemies of those trying to help him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 24 March 1944, less than 3 weeks after his brigades had been flown into Burma, the American B-25 bomber carrying him crashed in the hills west of Imphal, and all on board were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that August that there had been a man of genius who might well have become also a man of destiny, whose spirit lived on in the long-range penetration groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official history is harsher, finding his first plan inflexible and warning that his views risked being over-exploited, and an American air history called his first expedition without important accomplishments though acclaimed for its daring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India's Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 243–44, 324–28, 399, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 391, 404, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-21.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 424, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-22.html. [Open access]","Lee Bowen, “Allied Offensive in Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 493–94, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 37, 212–13, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 August 1944, vol. 402, col. 1462, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 17, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Wavell keep choosing him?","a":"Because Wavell had seen in Palestine in 1939 that he might lead unorthodox enterprises, and picked him to work with the Ethiopian Patriots in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His Gideon Force helped clear the way for the Emperor's return in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the War Office offered him again in 1942, Wavell, who recognised his excellent if unorthodox qualities, accepted and sent him to reconnoitre upper Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill take him to Quebec?","a":"Because the first Chindit expedition had made him a national hero and Churchill was impressed by his ideas for beating the Japanese with columns landed behind their lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Quebec the Allies decided on a much larger force under his command, and the Americans promised a special air unit to fly and supply it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his reputation still argued over?","a":"Because admirers and critics both had evidence. Churchill called him a man of genius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official history criticised his planning and feared his methods would be over-exploited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also records that his manner made enemies of those trying to help him, though his drive got Special Force ready in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Chindit_leaders_Burma_1944_IWM_MH_7873.jpg","title":"Wingate at Broadway, March 1944","desc":"Orde Wingate, centre, with British and American officers at the landing ground code-named Broadway in Burma, waiting for a night supply drop; Mike Calvert and Frank Merrill are among them.","credit":"British official photographer, Imperial War Museums (MH 7873), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chindit_leaders_Burma_1944_IWM_MH_7873.jpg","alt":"Seven men in jungle kit stand together at night in long grass; the bearded man in the centre wears a sun helmet and holds a rifle."},"wiki":"Orde Wingate"},
    "ww2-516": {"questions":["Britain's official history called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the turning point of the war in South-East Asia, the first time Japanese enveloping tactics failed to bring the results they expected.","A Japanese column of some 5,000 men got behind the 7th Indian Division, but the administrative troops holding Sinzweya threw back every attack in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of February 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Admin Box</b>, which the British official history calls the Battle of Ngakyedauk Pass, was fought in February 1944 in the Arakan, Burma's coastal strip on the Bay of Bengal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the centre of the second of three Allied campaigns in the Arakan, the first of which, launched in late 1942 to take the airfields on Akyab Island, ended in withdrawal by March 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That defeat came after Japanese reinforcements crossed hills thought impassable to strike the British flank, and Mountbatten wrote that it left morale at its lowest ebb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The third campaign, in 1945, won Akyab and Ramree as air and supply bases for the Fourteenth Army's advance and ended with the seaborne capture of Rangoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Indian official history judged that the Arakan victories sealed off an exposed frontier and raised Allied morale but counted for little in the overall defeat of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 4 February 1944 the Japanese 55th Division launched its Ha-Go offensive, and within a day a column of some 5,000 men under Sakurai was in the rear of the 7th Indian Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The division's administrative area at Sinzweya, held largely by administrative troops, became the box on which the Japanese attacks fell, and it was never broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Slim had ordered that units whose communications were cut should stand fast and be supplied by air, and by 8 February some 22,000 men with only 2 days' rations were being fed by transport aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 24 February the Japanese, short of food and ammunition, abandoned the offensive, which had cost XV Corps 3,506 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British and Indian official histories both call it a turning point, the first time that Japanese tactics of infiltration and encirclement had failed against well-trained British and Indian troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 136–52, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “The Far East Campaign,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/far-east-campaign. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 16, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","Oliver Leese, “Operations in Burma from 12th November, 1944, to 15th August, 1945,” second supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39195, 12 April 1951, 1957–58, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39195/supplement/1957/data.pdf. [Open access]","N. N. Madan, <i>The Arakan Operations, 1942–45</i>, ed. Bisheshwar Prasad, Official History of the Indian Armed Forces in the Second World War, 1939–45 (Calcutta: Combined Inter-Services Historical Section, India and Pakistan, 1954), xxvii–xxx, https://archive.org/details/arakanoperations1942. [Open access]","Lee Bowen, “Allied Offensive in Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 501–502, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British stand fast instead of retreating?","a":"Because Slim had decided that the chain of Japanese successes by infiltration had to be broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any unit whose communications were cut was to hold its ground and be supplied by air while reserves came up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 8 February transport aircraft were feeding some 22,000 cut-off men, and the Japanese, who had no such supply, ran short first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a fairly small battle called a turning point?","a":"Because of what it proved rather than the ground it held. Britain's official history says it was the first time Japanese enveloping tactics failed against well-trained British and Indian troops, and that it gave the army confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Indian official history says the defence of the box enabled the command to plan its future strategy in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the first Arakan campaign fail?","a":"Because the attack lacked resources and the Japanese struck back in an unexpected way. Their reinforcements crossed hills declared impassable and hit the exposed British flank, and by March 1943 the force had to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mountbatten wrote that the defeat left morale at its lowest ebb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Douglas_Dakota_air_drop_Admin_Box_1944.jpg","title":"Air supply at Sinzweya, February 1944","desc":"A dropping zone in a dry river bed near Sinzweya, photographed from a Dakota of No. 177 Wing RAF climbing away after parachuting supplies to the cut-off 7th Indian Division.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, Imperial War Museums (CI 602), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Douglas_Dakota_air_drop_Admin_Box_1944.jpg","alt":"Seen from inside an aircraft, a dry riverbed winds between wooded hills, its sand scattered with white parachutes."},"wiki":"Battle of the Admin Box"},
    "ww2-517": {"questions":["Told in Tokyo that the operations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood little chance of success, Tojo changed the subject, and Mutaguchi went on attacking as his starving army entered the monsoon.","The meeting of Allied troops at Milestone 109 on 22 June 1944 reopened the road from Kohima and ended the encirclement at the heart of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the main thrust of U-Go."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Imphal</b> was fought from March to July 1944 around the plain of Imphal in Manipur, where IV Corps held the main Allied base on India's frontier with Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the main thrust of the Japanese offensive called U-Go, planned by Renya Mutaguchi of the 15th Army to forestall a British invasion of Burma by seizing the plain and its supply bases, and to cut the air route to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The British official history traces Japanese confidence partly to the first Chindit expedition of 1943, which showed their commanders that troops could cross the hills along the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mutaguchi's plan relied on destroying IV Corps quickly and living on captured stores, since his own supply lines over the mountains could not sustain a long battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mountbatten's commanders chose to fight on the plain, where for once the Japanese too had precarious communications, and in March the 33rd Division cut off the 17th Indian Division while the 15th cut the road to Kohima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cut off by road from early April, the garrison lived by air: between 18 April and 30 June aircraft flew in some 18,000 tonnes (20,000 short tons) of stores and 12,600 reinforcements and took out over 10,000 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Tokyo broadcast admitted that the enemy received food by air while Japanese soldiers fought on a handful of barley or grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mutaguchi kept attacking long after the offensive had failed, and when Tojo was told that the Imphal operations stood little chance of success he stopped the speaker and changed the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 June British and Indian troops met at Milestone 109 on the road from Kohima, and in July the starving remnants of the 15th Army withdrew through the monsoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official history puts the 15th Army's losses at Imphal and Kohima at 53,505 of 84,280 men, 30,502 of them dead or missing, the National Army Museum gives 53,000 dead and missing of 85,000, and British losses were just under 16,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Army Museum, “Battles of Imphal and Kohima,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/battle-imphal. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 73, 354, 372, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India's Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 328–29, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","Lee Bowen, “Allied Offensive in Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 508–10, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]","George J. Giffard, “Operations in Burma and North-East India from 16th November, 1943 to 22nd June, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39171, 19 March 1951, 1360, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39171/supplement/1360/data.pdf. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 45, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 August 1944, vol. 402, col. 1462, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/02/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese attack Imphal at all?","a":"Because they wanted to forestall a British invasion of Burma by seizing the main British base on the frontier and its supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Holding Imphal would also have let them interrupt the air route to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official history adds that the first Chindit raid had convinced their commanders that the frontier hills could be crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did air supply decide the outcome?","a":"Because the Japanese had cut every road into the plain, yet IV Corps could still be fed and reinforced by aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between April and June transport aircraft brought in some 18,000 tonnes (20,000 short tons) of stores and took out over 10,000 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mutaguchi's men, who had counted on captured supplies, starved instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the Japanese losses given differently?","a":"Because sources count them in different ways and periods. The official history gives 53,505 losses from a 15th Army of 84,280 at Imphal and Kohima, 30,502 of them dead or missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The National Army Museum gives 53,000 dead and missing from 85,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both show that more than half the army was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/A_British_Lee_tank_crosses_a_river_north_of_Imphal_to_meet_the_Japanese_advance_in_Burma%2C_1944._IND3468.jpg","title":"A Lee tank north of Imphal, 1944","desc":"An M3 Lee tank crosses a river north of Imphal to meet the Japanese advance during the battle of 1944.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums (IND 3468), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_British_Lee_tank_crosses_a_river_north_of_Imphal_to_meet_the_Japanese_advance_in_Burma,_1944._IND3468.jpg","alt":"A tall, boxy tank with a gun in its hull and crewmen in its turret drives through a shallow river, trailing dust, below a bare hillside."},"wiki":"Battle of Imphal"},
    "ww2-518": {"questions":["With the water pipeline cut in April 1944, the defenders in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived for some days on about 280 millilitres (half a pint) of water a man while aircraft dropped supplies.","Starving and short of ammunition, Kotoku Sato began to withdraw his division from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without permission at the end of May 1944, then refused Mutaguchi's order to stop."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kohima</b> was fought from April to June 1944 for the ridge on the road between Dimapur and Imphal, which the Japanese 31st Division tried to cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kohima stood on the summit of a pass at about 1,430 metres (4,700 feet), the only practical route for a road from the Assam valley to Manipur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British planners expected no more than one regiment to reach it across the roadless hills, but Lieutenant-General Kotoku Sato's division attacked with two regiments and pressed the assault for 14 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The garrison of about 2,500 under Colonel Hugh Richards was built round the 4th Battalion of the Royal West Kent Regiment and the Assam Regiment, with many administrative troops and men from a convalescent camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The siege began on the night of 4 to 5 April, and with the pipeline cut the water ration fell for some days to about 280 millilitres (half a pint) a man while aircraft dropped water and ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The wounded lay in the open until a dressing station was dug, and by 9 April it held some 200 patients, while in one sector only the width of a tennis court separated the two sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relief came on 18 April, when the 2nd British Division broke through from the west, and the official history credits the garrison with a small but very vital part in the Japanese defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Weeks of close fighting followed, the Japanese were not cleared from Kohima until 2 June, and on 22 June the road to Imphal was reopened at Milestone 109.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the end of May Sato, starving and short of ammunition, withdrew without permission, and when ordered to stop he answered that an army which had sent him no supplies could not demand obedience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Giffard called the holding of Kohima the turning point of the campaign, and the National Army Museum counts about 4,000 British casualties there, roughly a quarter of the official 16,700 for Imphal and Kohima together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 298–306, 354, 362, 372, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “Battles of Imphal and Kohima,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/battle-imphal. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 45, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","George J. Giffard, “Operations in Burma and North-East India from 16th November, 1943 to 22nd June, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39171, 19 March 1951, 1359–60, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39171/supplement/1359/data.pdf. [Open access]","Lee Bowen, “Allied Offensive in Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Pacific: Guadalcanal to Saipan, August 1942 to July 1944</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1950), 508, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/IV/AAF-IV-15.html. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 5, <i>Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: HMSO, 1966), 269–71, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-5. [Open access]","George J. Giffard, “Operations in Assam and Burma from 23rd June, 1944 to 12th November, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39187, 2 April 1951, 1711, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39187/supplement/1711/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a small hill town matter so much?","a":"Because Kohima sat on the only practical road between the Assam valley and the Imphal plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Holding it kept that road from being blocked for good and protected the base at Dimapur and the railway behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Had the ridge been lost, the official history says, reopening the road to Imphal would have been much harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the defenders so heavily outnumbered?","a":"Because British planners had misjudged the threat. They expected no more than one regiment to reach Kohima through the roadless hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instead Sato's division attacked with two regiments against a scratch garrison of about 2,500, many of them non-combatants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Sato withdraw against orders?","a":"Because his division was starving and short of ammunition, and Mutaguchi had refused his requests to pull back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of May he withdrew anyway, and when told to stop he replied that the 15th Army's failure to send supplies freed him from any obligation to obey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/IND_003698_Garrison_Hill_Kohima.jpg","title":"Garrison Hill, Kohima, 1944","desc":"The battlefield of Garrison Hill, the key to the British defences at Kohima, after the fighting.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums (IND 3698), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IND_003698_Garrison_Hill_Kohima.jpg","alt":"A hillside of bare, shell-stripped tree trunks above churned earth littered with broken branches and debris."},"wiki":"Battle of Kohima"},
    "ww2-519": {"questions":["Wounded at Gallipoli and later Governor-General of Australia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took over Burma Corps in March 1942 and brought it out of Burma on the long retreat to India.","In 1945 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> used the obvious advance on Mandalay as cover for a thrust at Meiktila, a plan Leese called excellent and triumphantly executed."],"abstract":"<b>William Slim</b> was the British general who commanded the Fourteenth Army in Burma through the defeat of the Japanese offensive of 1944 and the reconquest of the country in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commissioned in 1914, he was wounded at Gallipoli, won the Military Cross in Mesopotamia and in 1918 took a permanent commission in the Indian Army, where his career between the wars was slow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early in the Second World War he commanded a brigade in the conquest of Abyssinia and then the 10th Indian Division in Iraq, Syria and Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1942 he took command of Burma Corps and brought it out to India, and Wavell named him among the good and resolute commanders who kept the enemy's superior numbers at bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the war he was Chief of the Imperial General Staff from 1948 to 1952 and Governor-General of Australia from 1953 to 1960, and he was made a viscount in 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His reputation rests on the rebuilding of an army whose morale, in Mountbatten's words, had reached its lowest ebb after the defeats of 1942 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mountbatten wrote that Slim set out to build a new morale on the foundations laid by Giffard, and the National Army Museum stresses his insistence on jungle training and more aggressive tactics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His rule was that any unit whose communications were cut should stand fast and be supplied by air while reserves came up to destroy the enemy in its rear, the method that won in the Arakan and at Imphal in 1944, after which the Viceroy knighted him at Imphal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1945 he used the obvious advance on Mandalay as cover for a thrust at Meiktila that cut the Japanese armies off from their base, a plan Leese called excellent and triumphantly executed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mountbatten later called him the finest general the Second World War produced, a judgement the National Army Museum quotes while crediting the victory also to the courage and endurance of his troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Army Museum, “William Slim: The Soldiers' Soldier,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/william-slim. [Open access]","Archibald P. Wavell, “Operations in Burma from 15th December, 1941, to 20th May, 1942,” with the reports of T. J. Hutton and Harold Alexander, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38228, 11 March 1948, 1674, 1698, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38228/page/1674/data.pdf. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 16–17, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 150–51, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 263–64, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","Oliver Leese, “Operations in Burma from 12th November, 1944, to 15th August, 1945,” second supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39195, 12 April 1951, 1957, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39195/supplement/1957/data.pdf. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “Battles of Imphal and Kohima,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/battle-imphal. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the army's morale his first problem?","a":"Because it had known nothing but defeat. The retreat of 1942 and the failure in the Arakan in 1943 left morale, Mountbatten wrote, at its lowest ebb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Slim set out to build a new morale, insisting on hard jungle training and more aggressive tactics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first proof that it had worked came in the Arakan in February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his stand-fast rule work in Burma?","a":"Because his troops could be supplied by air and the Japanese could not. Earlier, cut-off units had retreated and been destroyed piecemeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Slim's rule they held their positions while reserves came up behind the Japanese, and in the Arakan the attackers ran out of food and ammunition first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the thrust at Meiktila decide the 1945 campaign?","a":"Because Meiktila lay on the main Japanese line of supply. While the advance on Mandalay drew all eyes, a corps crossed the Irrawaddy further south and struck at the town, cutting the Japanese armies off from their base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Leese wrote that the enemy was so mauled there that he could never again fight as an army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Field_Marshal_Sir_William_Slim%2C_General_Officer_Commanding_Fourteenth_Army_in_Burma%2C_5_March_1945._SE3310.jpg","title":"Slim at Fourteenth Army headquarters, March 1945","desc":"Slim, then a lieutenant-general commanding the Fourteenth Army, holds binoculars outside his headquarters in Burma on 5 March 1945.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums (SE 3310), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Field_Marshal_Sir_William_Slim,_General_Officer_Commanding_Fourteenth_Army_in_Burma,_5_March_1945._SE3310.jpg","alt":"A stocky officer in a slouch hat and shirt-sleeve uniform stands holding binoculars in front of a bullet-pocked wall with an arched doorway."},"wiki":"William Slim, 1st Viscount Slim"},
    "ww2-520": {"questions":["The crossing of the Irrawaddy at Nyaungu in February 1945, called the longest opposed river crossing of the war, opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that broke the Japanese hold on central Burma.","Fort Dufferin held out until 20 March 1945, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the Japanese so dearly that, Leese wrote, Kimura could never again regroup his forces to fight as an army."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Meiktila and Mandalay</b> was the decisive battle of the Allied reconquest of Burma, fought on the dry plain of central Burma in February and March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Japanese defeat at Imphal the Fourteenth Army crossed the Chindwin and advanced through the monsoon, hoping to bring the Japanese to battle on the Shwebo plain north of Mandalay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When they fell back behind the Irrawaddy, Slim recast his plan so that XXXIII Corps would close on Mandalay while IV Corps moved in secret down the Gangaw valley to cross the river near Pakokku and strike at Meiktila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountbatten's report explains that the enemy was to stay unaware of the thrust as long as possible, and to read any movement in that valley as a feint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official history calls the crossing at Nyaungu, begun on 14 February where the river was over 1,800 metres (2,000 yards) wide, the longest opposed river crossing attempted in any theatre of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The 17th Indian Division and the 255th Indian Tank Brigade then drove east and had taken Meiktila by 4 March, cutting the main line of communication of the Japanese armies to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese threw every unit they could find against the town, and the fight to hold it lasted through March while XXXIII Corps closed in on Mandalay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fort Dufferin, Mandalay's walled citadel, held out under bombing and point-blank artillery fire until 20 March, when its last defenders were found to have gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Holding Meiktila cost IV Corps 835 killed and 3,174 wounded, the battle for Mandalay cost XXXIII Corps 1,472 killed and 4,933 wounded, and the Fourteenth Army's losses to the end of March came to about 18,000, some 10,500 of them in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leese wrote that Kimura could never again regroup his forces to fight as an army, and when Rangoon fell in early May the leading corps was only some 50 kilometres (30 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 263–64, 271–72, 300–301, 311–12, 397, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","“The Liberation of Burma,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 246–48, 251, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-8.html. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “Battles of Imphal and Kohima,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/battle-imphal. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 103–104, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","Oliver Leese, “Operations in Burma from 12th November, 1944, to 15th August, 1945,” second supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 39195, 12 April 1951, 1957, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/39195/supplement/1957/data.pdf. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “William Slim: The Soldiers' Soldier,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/william-slim. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Meiktila a better target than Mandalay?","a":"Because Meiktila was the hub of the Japanese supply lines in central Burma, with airfields close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taking it cut the main line of communication of the Japanese armies fighting to the north, while Mandalay, the famous city, drew the attention of both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Japanese not see the thrust coming?","a":"Because Slim made sure they looked the other way. The obvious advances on Mandalay provided cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountbatten's report says the move down the Gangaw valley was to stay hidden as long as possible, and any sign of it was meant to look like a feint for the Mandalay attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the victory lead so quickly to Rangoon?","a":"Because the Japanese armies were too badly broken to stand again. Leese wrote that Kimura could never again regroup his forces to fight as an army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mechanised columns supplied by air pushed south, and Rangoon was reoccupied without a fight on 3 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/SE_003071_Shermans_driving_on_Meiktila.jpg","title":"The advance on Meiktila, March 1945","desc":"Tanks and lorries on the road from the Irrawaddy bridgehead at Nyaungu towards Meiktila; the original caption names a brigade that was in fact fighting at Mandalay.","credit":"British Army official photographer, Imperial War Museums (SE 3071), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SE_003071_Shermans_driving_on_Meiktila.jpg","alt":"A column of tracked armoured vehicles carrying soldiers, lorries and a motorcyclist moves along a dusty road between trees."},"wiki":"Battle of Meiktila and Mandalay"},
    "ww2-571": {"questions":["Two generals sent by the armed forces high command came to Herrlingen on 14 October 1944, and in their presence <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took poison rather than face a charge over the July plot.","Churchill told the Commons in January 1942 that Britain faced a great general in Cyrenaica, and the day after Tobruk fell that June Hitler made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a field marshal."],"abstract":"<b>Erwin Rommel</b> was the German general who commanded the German and then the German-Italian forces in North Africa from 1941 to 1943, and who died in 1944 by poison the regime supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in Heidenheim in Württemberg on 15 November 1891, the son of a teacher, he joined the Württemberg army in 1910 and won the Pour le Mérite on the Italian front in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His book <i>Infanterie greift an</i>, published in 1937, sold some 400,000 copies before 1945, and in 1938 and 1939 he commanded the escort and then the headquarters of Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After leading the 7th Panzer Division in France in 1940, he was given the German Afrika Korps in Libya in February 1941, chosen by Hitler over the objections of the army command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Churchill told the House of Commons in January 1942 that the British faced 'a very daring and skilful opponent' and 'a great General', and Hitler made him a field marshal on 22 June 1942, the day after Tobruk fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In April 1941, ignoring protests from his Italian superior, General Gariboldi, that a further advance was contrary to orders, he drove the British out of Cyrenaica and surrounded Tobruk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Franz Halder, the army's chief of staff, wrote that Rommel sent no clear reports and made senseless demands, and he sent General Paulus to 'head off this soldier gone stark mad'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the defeat at El Alamein he retreated against Hitler's order to hold, led a last thrust through the Kasserine Pass in Tunisia in February 1943, and left Africa for good in March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In France in the summer of 1944, Alfred Jodl testified at Nuremberg, Rommel asked Hitler outright how he saw the war developing and was told it was no part of his duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Accused of involvement in the plot of 20 July 1944, he took poison at Herrlingen on 14 October in front of two generals, and his son wrote that he was promised his family's safety and a state funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Manfred Wichmann, “Erwin Rommel 1891–1944,” LeMO Biografie, Deutsches Historisches Museum, Berlin, 30 June 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/erwin-rommel. [in German] [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 2, 25, 35–41, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-2.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 8, 153–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-8.html. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 6, entries of 23 April and 11 May 1941, trans. Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, statement on the war situation, <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cc. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II, Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chap. XXIII, 438–58, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-23.html. [Open access]","B. H. Liddell Hart, ed., <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, trans. Paul Findlay (London: Collins, 1953), 503–05, https://archive.org/details/ROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 5 June 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-05-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the German army's chief of staff distrust him?","a":"Rommel had not climbed the General Staff ladder and had been picked by Hitler rather than by the army command, the British official history notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Halder's diary complains that he sent no clear report, rushed about between scattered units wasting his strength, and made demands that air transport could not meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Halder sent General Paulus to North Africa to restrain him, and Paulus ordered that the failed attack on Tobruk was not to be renewed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Paulus reported back on 11 May 1941, Halder recorded his verdict that Rommel had overstepped his orders and created a situation that German supply could not sustain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he allowed to die by poison rather than be tried?","a":"His son's account, written after the war, says the two generals told Rommel that if he took the poison nothing would be done to his family or his staff, and that he would have a state funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rommel told his son that it was very much in the regime's interest to keep the affair out of public view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His death was reported to the family as a brain seizure on the way to a conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The German Historical Museum's biography records that a great state ceremony was held in Ulm on 18 October before his burial at Herrlingen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British take him so seriously?","a":"In April 1941, against Italian protests that he was exceeding his orders, he pushed on and drove the British out of Cyrenaica in under a fortnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By January 1942 Churchill was telling the House of Commons that the British faced a very daring and skilful opponent and, across the havoc of war, a great general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After Tobruk fell in June 1942, Hitler made him a field marshal the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-784-0238-06A%2C_Nordafrika%2C_Erwin_Rommel_mit_Fernglas.jpg","title":"Rommel in North Africa, 1942","desc":"A German propaganda company photograph of Rommel watching through binoculars in the desert.","credit":"Moosmüller, Bundesarchiv Bild 101I-784-0238-06A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-784-0238-06A,_Nordafrika,_Erwin_Rommel_mit_Fernglas.jpg","alt":"A German officer in a peaked cap leaning against a low wall in the desert and looking through binoculars."},"wiki":"Erwin Rommel"},
    "ww2-572": {"questions":["Destroyers and fast minelayers running in on moonless nights carried Polish and British troops into Tobruk during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that most Australians could leave as their government insisted.","Ships carried 34,113 men and nearly 34,000 tons of stores into the port during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted from 11 April to 10 December 1941 and cost 34 vessels."],"abstract":"The <b>Siege of Tobruk</b> was the 8-month investment of the Libyan port by German and Italian forces in 1941, during which a garrison led by the 9th Australian Division held the fortress and was supplied only by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Rommel drove the British out of Cyrenaica in April, Wavell decided that Tobruk must be held to keep its large stocks of supplies, its harbour and its water out of the enemy's hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The defenders used the Italian works, a double ring of concrete posts 13 to 14 kilometres (8 or 9 miles) from the harbour, though Wavell judged their perimeter of some 43 kilometres (27 miles) very long for the force available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the 36,000 people inside in April, a third were base troops, Libyan refugees and prisoners, and the generals in command resolved from the start that the defence would be aggressive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rommel had assumed that the ships in the harbour had come to take the garrison off, and by 14 April he was reporting to the army high command a stubborn resistance that blocked his advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In the Easter battle of 13 and 14 April German tanks broke through the wire but were caught by artillery and tanks, and withdrew with sixteen of 38 lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Through the summer the garrison lived with dust, glare and swarms of flies, and dysentery and desert sores spread where men had little chance to wash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Through the summer the Australian government, on General Blamey's advice, insisted that its exhausted division be relieved, and Churchill told it that he regretted the decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In three operations between August and October destroyers and fast minelayers, running only on dark nights, replaced most Australians with the Polish Carpathian Brigade and the British 70th Division, though the fast minelayer Latona was sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the siege ended on 10 December, ships had carried in 34,113 men and 33,946 tons of stores and taken out 47,280 people, at a cost of 34 warships and merchant ships lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), chap. 2, 35–41, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-2.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), 24–26, 78, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","Archibald Wavell, “Operations in the Middle East from 7th February, 1941 to 15th July, 1941,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37638, 3 July 1946, 3429–30, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37638/data.pdf. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 4, “At Bay – The Easter Battle,” 111–58, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417162. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 8, “Last Counter-attack and a Controversial Relief,” 305–83, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417166. [Open access]","Allan S. Walker, <i>Middle East and Far East</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 5, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), chap. 11, “Medical Conditions in the Western Desert and Tobruk,” https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417523. [Open access]","Franz Halder, <i>The Private War Journal of Generaloberst Franz Halder</i>, vol. 6, entries of 14 April 1941, trans. Office of Chief of Counsel for War Crimes, Office of Military Government for Germany (1947), https://archive.org/details/HalderWarJournal. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Wavell decide to hold Tobruk at all?","a":"Wavell wrote in his despatch that it was essential to keep the large reserves of supplies there and to stop the enemy using the port and its water for a further advance into Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He accepted that the Italian perimeter, some 43 kilometres (27 miles) long, was very extended for the force he had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official history adds that the old works were far enough out to keep the enemy's guns away from the harbour, which was the garrison's only line of supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the garrison relieved in the middle of the siege?","a":"General Blamey advised the Australian government that its division, worn out after months in the line, had to be relieved, and successive Australian governments held to that against Churchill's pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told them he regretted their decision, and the British official history notes that the naval commanders had been strongly opposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relief was done by fast warships on moonless nights, and when the minelayer Latona was sunk Admiral Cunningham stopped it, leaving the 2/13th Battalion behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did dysentery and sores spread inside the fortress?","a":"The Australian medical history records that flies were so numerous and persistent that they were very hard to keep out of food and drink.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Native labourers, inhabitants and prisoners of war inside the perimeter were all possible carriers of dysentery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Desert sores appeared in proportion to the lack of washing facilities, especially after several months, and in Tobruk infected tissue often came with fever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Australian_2-13th_Inf_Bn_at_Tobruk_%28AWM_photo_020780%29.jpg","title":"An Australian patrol at Tobruk, 1941","desc":"A patrol from the Australian 2/13th Infantry Battalion near Tobruk on 8 September 1941.","credit":"Australian War Memorial 020780, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_2-13th_Inf_Bn_at_Tobruk_(AWM_photo_020780).jpg","alt":"Australian soldiers in shorts and helmets moving across open, stony desert ground with rifles."},"wiki":"Siege of Tobruk"},
    "ww2-573": {"questions":["Rommel's armoured raid towards the Egyptian frontier on 24 November 1941 failed to break <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and by mid-December the British advance had relieved the Tobruk garrison and pushed westward.","German and Italian losses of about 38,000 men against some 17,700 British, most of the Axis total listed as missing, were the British official estimate for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the winter of 1941."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Crusader</b> was the British Eighth Army's winter offensive in Libya in 1941, which aimed to destroy the German and Italian armour in Cyrenaica and relieve the besieged garrison of Tobruk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Auchinleck, the commander-in-chief in the Middle East, gave Alan Cunningham the task in September and planned on some 450 to 500 British tanks against about 250 German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German record put the British at about 700 tanks, against roughly 360 serviceable German tanks and 150 weaker Italian ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The offensive opened on 18 November and took the enemy by surprise, since his forces were moving into position for their own assault on Tobruk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To the troops inside Tobruk, the planned link-up with their sortie force at Ed Duda was the main point of the first phase, and the early reports puzzled them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Heavy armoured fighting round Sidi Rezegh destroyed much of the British armour, and Cunningham warned that going on might mean the annihilation of the tank force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Auchinleck ordered the attack to continue, judged that Cunningham was thinking of defence rather than offence, and replaced him on 26 November with Neil Ritchie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rommel's raid towards the frontier on 24 November failed to break the offensive, and with about 100 serviceable tanks left he had to raise the siege, which ended on 10 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British pushed on to Benghazi, and the frontier garrisons gave up at Bardia on 2 January and at Halfaya on 17 January 1942, by which time the offensive was spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The official history estimates about 38,300 German and Italian casualties, 29,900 of them missing, against about 17,700 British, and Churchill told the Commons that the enemy had been hurled backward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chaps. 1–2, 1–72, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chap. 3, 73–102, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","Claude Auchinleck, “Operations in the Middle East from 1st November 1941 to 15th August 1942,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38177, 15 January 1948, 312, 331–39, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38177/data.pdf. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 10, “Ed Duda,” 418–513, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417191. [Open access]","Alfred Toppe et al., <i>Desert Warfare: German Experiences in World War II</i>, trans. H. Heitman (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1991), 38–41, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA244832. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, statement on the war situation, <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cc. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Auchinleck replace his army commander in mid-battle?","a":"After the heavy tank losses round Sidi Rezegh, Cunningham told Auchinleck that continuing might mean the annihilation of the British tank force and so endanger Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Auchinleck visited him on 23 November and ordered the offensive to go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Back in Cairo he decided that Cunningham was unduly influenced by the threat of an enemy counterstroke and was thinking of defence rather than offence, and he chose his own deputy chief of staff, Neil Ritchie, who took over on 26 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rommel's raid to the frontier not save him?","a":"The German account says Rommel overestimated his success at Sidi Rezegh and drove for the frontier on 24 November, returning to the Tobruk front on 28 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In his absence the British had broken through the ring round Tobruk, and the Afrika Korps, down to about 100 serviceable tanks, could not restore the position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Auchinleck's despatch adds that the thrust caused confusion in the rear areas but inflicted little material damage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the British victory incomplete?","a":"The British official history says the British cleared Cyrenaica only after a long and costly struggle, so that by the time they reached its western end their blow was spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Supply was the problem: leaking 4-gallon tins and long lorry hauls from railhead meant only the barest petrol needs were met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German record notes that two convoys reached Tripoli in December after, by its account, 3 months in which no supply ship had reached Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Crusader_and_Panzer_IV_tanks_1941.jpg","title":"A Crusader tank passes a German tank, 24 November 1941","desc":"A British Crusader tank drives past an abandoned German Panzer IV during the November fighting.","credit":"Australian armed forces, Australian War Memorial 010720, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crusader_and_Panzer_IV_tanks_1941.jpg","alt":"A British tank moving across flat desert past a larger, abandoned German tank in the foreground."},"wiki":"Operation Crusader"},
    "ww2-574": {"questions":["Swinging round the southern end of a British line of mined defended localities on 26 May 1942, Rommel opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with some 560 German and Italian tanks.","The British lost heavily attacking the Axis armour in the so-called Cauldron on 5 June 1942, and they lost the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the tank fighting of 11 to 13 June."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Gazala</b> was the German and Italian offensive in eastern Libya in May and June 1942 that broke the British Eighth Army's line between Gazala and Bir Hacheim and opened the way to Tobruk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the spring the British had built a defensive system of mined positions between Gazala on the coast, Tobruk, Bir el Gubi and Bir Hacheim, while preparing an offensive of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their armour had about 850 tanks, among them 167 of the new American Grants, whose 75-millimetre gun could fight German tanks at unheard-of ranges, with more on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rommel's mobile force had about 332 German and 228 Italian tanks, and the Axis plan, the British later learned from captured papers, was to take Tobruk by 30 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German planning tied the offensive to Malta: after taking Tobruk Rommel was to pause while German and Italian forces seized the island, so that his supplies could flow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> On 26 May the Axis mobile divisions swung round the open southern end of the line, but the plan miscarried and by the evening of 27 May they were split and in danger of encirclement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rommel regrouped in a pocket inside the British minefields, the so-called Cauldron, and a British attack on it on 5 June failed with great loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Bir Hacheim the 1st Free French Brigade held out under heavy bombing until the night of 10 June, when about 2,700 of its original 3,600 men broke out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the tank fighting of 11 to 13 June the British armour was defeated, and Ritchie decided to pull his divisions back from the Gazala position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexander later summed up the result: the enemy had defeated the Eighth Army at Gazala and Tobruk and driven it back to El Alamein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chap. 9, 197–222, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chap. 10, 223–52, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","Claude Auchinleck, “Operations in the Middle East from 1st November 1941 to 15th August 1942,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38177, 15 January 1948, 324–26, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38177/data.pdf. [Open access]","B. H. Liddell Hart, ed., <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, trans. Paul Findlay (London: Collins, 1953), 217–20, https://archive.org/details/ROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]","Alfred Toppe et al., <i>Desert Warfare: German Experiences in World War II</i>, trans. H. Heitman (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1991), 44–46, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA244832. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II, Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chap. I, 7–9, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-1.html. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August 1942 to 13th May 1943,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 839, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Free French defence of Bir Hacheim matter?","a":"The British official history says the defence lengthened and complicated the enemy's temporary supply route round the southern end of the line and cost him many casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also gave the British time to recover from their defeat in the Cauldron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the Free French it was the first time in the Middle East that they had fought the Germans and Italians as a complete formation of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rommel himself wrote that the French had shown great courage there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rommel's opening sweep nearly fail?","a":"The German study says the two Italian infantry corps were too weak to pin the British in the north, and the mobile divisions spread out as they turned, which made the defence easier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the evening of 27 May the attacking force had split into three groups in danger of being encircled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rommel was cut off from most of his radio stations until 29 May, and supplies had to be routed round Bir Hacheim with heavy losses of vehicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Malta bound up with the battle?","a":"The American official history says Rommel's success and the capture of Malta were treated as interdependent, because aircraft and submarines from the island cut the flow of supplies from Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The plan was for Rommel to take Tobruk and push to Mersa Matruh, then pause while German and Italian forces seized the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the victory the German study records that Rommel won the argument to press on instead, and the attack on Malta was postponed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/British_25-pounder_field_guns_in_action_on_the_night_of_2_June_1942_during_the_Battle_of_Gazala_in_Libya._E12789.jpg","title":"British guns at night, 2 June 1942","desc":"British 25-pounder field guns firing on the night of 2 June 1942 during the Battle of Gazala.","credit":"William George Vanderson, No. 1 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum E 12789, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_25-pounder_field_guns_in_action_on_the_night_of_2_June_1942_during_the_Battle_of_Gazala_in_Libya._E12789.jpg","alt":"A field gun firing in the dark, its muzzle flash lighting the crew and the desert around it."},"wiki":"Battle of Gazala"},
    "ww2-575": {"questions":["Churchill heard the news in Roosevelt's room at the White House, and after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the President took newly issued Sherman tanks back from his own troops to send to Egypt.","A Commons motion of no confidence in the central direction of the war, tabled after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in June 1942, was defeated on 2 July by 475 votes to 25."],"abstract":"The <b>Axis capture of Tobruk</b> on 21 June 1942 ended a one-day assault in which Rommel's German and Italian forces took the Libyan port and most of its garrison after their victory at Gazala.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since February it had been known that the British did not mean to accept a second siege, and the official history concludes that the fortress was not properly prepared for one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ritchie put four infantry brigade groups and some tanks inside under Major-General Klopper, commander of the 2nd South African Division, and kept the rest of the army outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German planning had treated Tobruk as the first step: once it fell, Rommel was meant to pause while the Axis seized Malta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> With the Desert Air Force's fighters out of range, nearly 400 tons of bombs were dropped on a small sector of the eastern defences when the attack began early on 20 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> German tanks reached the harbour that evening, and at dawn on 21 June Klopper, his transport lost, judged that further resistance would cost terrible casualties for little gain and surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The official history puts the prisoners at about 33,000, about a third of all South Africa's forces in the field, while the German study gives the garrison as 25,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rommel wrote that the road was lined with some ten thousand prisoners when he met Klopper at the Hotel Tobruk, and Hitler made him a field marshal the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill heard the news at the White House, and he later told the Commons that Roosevelt took newly issued Sherman tanks back from American troops to send to Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the Commons the debate on a motion of no confidence in the central direction of the war ended on 2 July with its defeat by 475 votes to 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chaps. 11–12, 253–78, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","Claude Auchinleck, “Operations in the Middle East from 1st November 1941 to 15th August 1942,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38177, 15 January 1948, 326–27, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38177/data.pdf. [Open access]","“Central Direction of the War,” debate, <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 2 July 1942, vol. 381, cc. 527–611, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jul/02/central-direction-of-the-war. [Open access]","B. H. Liddell Hart, ed., <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, trans. Paul Findlay (London: Collins, 1953), 230–32, https://archive.org/details/ROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]","Alfred Toppe et al., <i>Desert Warfare: German Experiences in World War II</i>, trans. H. Heitman (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1991), 45–46, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA244832. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II, Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chap. I, 7–9, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-1.html. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Erwin Rommel 1891–1944,” LeMO Biografie, Deutsches Historisches Museum, Berlin, 30 June 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/erwin-rommel. [in German] [Open access]","Winston Churchill, speech in the debate on the Address, <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 11 November 1942, vol. 385, cc. 8–56, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/nov/11/debate-on-the-address. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Tobruk fall in a day when it had held for months in 1941?","a":"The British official history says it had been known since February that there was no intention of accepting a second siege, so the place was not properly prepared even for a determined assault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither Auchinleck nor Ritchie grasped how badly the army had been beaten at Gazala, and the 2nd South African Division's staff lacked the experience to use the forces they had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the British fighters out of range, the attackers dropped nearly 400 tons of bombs on a small sector of the defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fall shake Parliament so badly?","a":"It came straight after a run of defeats, and the official history notes it was among the reasons a motion of no confidence in the central direction of the war was tabled on 25 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the debate Aneurin Bevan contrasted the holding of Sebastopol for months with the collapse of Tobruk in 26 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The motion was defeated on 2 July by 475 votes to 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did victory at Tobruk change Axis plans?","a":"Axis planning had made the capture of Tobruk the moment for Rommel to pause while Malta was taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German study records that Rommel instead believed he had a chance that would never recur to push on to the Nile, and with the support of the German high command he got his way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The attack on Malta was postponed, and the air forces stayed with the pursuit into Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B21459%2C_Bei_Tobruk%2C_britische_Kriegsgefangene.jpg","title":"Prisoners near Tobruk, June 1942","desc":"A German propaganda photograph, released on 1 July 1942, of British Commonwealth prisoners near Tobruk waiting to be moved after the battle.","credit":"Lutz Koch, Bundesarchiv Bild 183-B21459, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B21459,_Bei_Tobruk,_britische_Kriegsgefangene.jpg","alt":"A large group of soldiers sitting and standing on open desert ground under guard."},"wiki":"Axis capture of Tobruk"},
    "ww2-576": {"questions":["Alexander later called 2 July 1942 the critical day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the Eighth Army defeated Rommel's most desperate efforts to break through.","Writing home on 18 July 1942 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rommel called it militarily the most difficult period he had ever been through and feared that his front would crack."],"abstract":"The <b>First Battle of El Alamein</b> was the month of fighting in July 1942 in which the British Eighth Army, under Auchinleck's personal command, halted Rommel's advance into Egypt about 100 kilometres (60 miles) from Alexandria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The position's strength, Alexander explained, was that its southern flank rested on the Qattara Depression, a basin of quicksands and salt marshes impassable even to a loaded camel, so it could not be turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 25 June Auchinleck judged the danger to Egypt so great that he took command of the army himself, and he chose to fall back on El Alamein rather than be pinned at Mersa Matruh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rommel arrived on 30 June with exhausted troops and, by the German account, only about fifty serviceable tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Behind the line the British prepared for the worst, building defences round the Pyramids, flooding country south of Alexandria and planning to move headquarters out of Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Rommel's attack on 1 July was beaten off at the Alamein fortifications, and on 2 July he called off his armour after the 90th Light Division again failed to advance under artillery and machine-gun fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander later called 2 July the critical day on which the Eighth Army defeated the enemy's most desperate efforts to break through, gaining time for fresh divisions and better tanks to arrive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 10 July Australian and South African troops attacked near the coast at Tell el Eisa and threw the enemy onto the defensive, but two attacks on Ruweisat Ridge and El Mreir later in the month failed with heavy loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rommel wrote to his wife on 18 July that it was militarily the most difficult period he had ever been through, and that the front would crack if it went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> July ended in stalemate, with both sides sowing belts of mines between the sea and the depression, and no new British offensive was possible before mid-September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chap. 12, 277–98, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chap. 14, 331–60, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","Claude Auchinleck, “Operations in the Middle East from 1st November 1941 to 15th August 1942,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38177, 15 January 1948, 328–29, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38177/data.pdf. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August 1942 to 13th May 1943,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 839–41, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 12, “At El Alamein Under Auchinleck,” 542–602, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417193. [Open access]","Alfred Toppe et al., <i>Desert Warfare: German Experiences in World War II</i>, trans. H. Heitman (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1991), 46, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA244832. [Open access]","B. H. Liddell Hart, ed., <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, trans. Paul Findlay (London: Collins, 1953), 247–58, https://archive.org/details/ROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Auchinleck fall back to El Alamein instead of Mersa Matruh?","a":"Auchinleck wrote that the army was so weak in tanks and field artillery that it could probably not hold Matruh, and might be enveloped and destroyed piecemeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was determined above all to keep the Eighth Army together as a mobile force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> El Alamein offered by far the strongest position in the Western Desert, with both flanks on impassable obstacles, and falling back lengthened the enemy's supply lines while shortening his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Rommel not break through in July?","a":"The German study says he reached the Alamein position with thoroughly exhausted troops and only about fifty serviceable tanks, and found it better fortified than anything he had met before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official history adds that his columns were bombed many times a day and scattered by attacks at night, and that fuel and ammunition were running short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His supply traffic dwindled so far that, the German study says, a new offensive was out of the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British July attacks not win the battle?","a":"Auchinleck wrote that his armour was too weak to keep up the momentum of the attack and carry it to a decisive end, though the army had recovered the initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official history records that two attacks on the enemy's centre on the Ruweisat Ridge failed with heavy loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander judged that the July attacks had at least made the enemy disperse his forces and gained time to improve the British defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/AWM_024517_2-8th_Field_Regiment_in_action_at_El_Alamein_12_July_1942.JPG","title":"Australian guns at El Alamein, 12 July 1942","desc":"Guns of the Australian 2/8th Field Regiment firing near El Alamein on 12 July 1942.","credit":"Australian War Memorial 024517, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AWM_024517_2-8th_Field_Regiment_in_action_at_El_Alamein_12_July_1942.JPG","alt":"A field gun crew working their gun on flat desert ground, with a cloud of smoke and dust beside them."},"wiki":"First Battle of El Alamein"},
    "ww2-577": {"questions":["At Lüneburg on 4 May 1945 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> signed the instrument by which all German forces in Holland, north-west Germany and Denmark surrendered to the 21st Army Group.","Telling his staff that if the army could not stay at El Alamein alive it would stay there dead, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised to hit Rommel 'for six right out of Africa'."],"abstract":"<b>Bernard Montgomery</b> was the British general who commanded the Eighth Army from August 1942 to the end of 1943, winning the Second Battle of El Alamein, and who later commanded the Allied ground forces in the Normandy landings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the summer of 1942 he was named to command a British task force in the planned landings in French North Africa, but was moved to a more urgent post before that operation began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Churchill decided in August 1942 to change the command in the Middle East, General Gott was chosen for the Eighth Army, and only after Gott was killed on 7 August was Montgomery sent instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexander, the new commander-in-chief, had known him in France and in Southern Command and wrote that he was an inspiring leader and an outstanding trainer of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Montgomery took command on 13 August 1942 and backed the principle, which Alexander had already laid down, that there would be no further withdrawal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Addressing his headquarters, the British official history records, he said that if the army could not stay at El Alamein alive it would stay there dead, and that it would hit Rommel 'for six right out of Africa'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He beat off Rommel's attack at Alam el Halfa at the turn of August and September, and would not open his own offensive until everything was ready, attacking on 23 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British official history judged him 'resilient but resolute' at El Alamein, changing his plan when the first assault faltered and never showing the anxiety he probably felt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His own account, based on his diary, claimed that the Eighth Army went from El Alamein to halfway up Italy without losing a battle or ever withdrawing a yard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His command of the ground forces in France ended on 1 September 1944, he pressed Eisenhower that autumn for one concentrated thrust into Germany, and on 4 May 1945 he took the surrender of the German forces in north-west Germany, Holland and Denmark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), chaps. 15–16, 361–93, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), chap. 3, 53–80, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","Bernard Montgomery, <i>El Alamein to the River Sangro</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1948), foreword, 3–5, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.82003. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August 1942 to 13th May 1943,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 840, 842, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II, Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chap. III, 32–59, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-3.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II, European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), chap. 4, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-4.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II, European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), chap. 14, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-14.html. [Open access]","“Instrument of Surrender of All German Armed Forces in Holland, in Northwest Germany Including All Islands, and in Denmark,” 4 May 1945, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/gs10.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was he given the Eighth Army at all?","a":"Churchill and the Chief of the Imperial General Staff, Alan Brooke, decided in August 1942 that a drastic change in the Middle East command was needed, and at first chose General Gott for the Eighth Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brooke's diary shows he thought Montgomery the better choice and that Auchinleck agreed, but he did not press it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Gott was killed on 7 August, Brooke recommended Montgomery, even though it meant a second change of British commander for the North African landings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he put so much weight on morale?","a":"Alexander found the troops puzzled by retreats they had not understood, and wrote that a soldier forced to retreat through no fault of his own loses confidence in the higher command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Montgomery wrote that when he arrived the army's determination was undermined by plans for further withdrawals and that the 'atmosphere' was wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official history describes how he toured his whole army to see and be seen, with an effect it calls electric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he quarrel with Eisenhower in 1944?","a":"After the breakout from Normandy supplies were scarce, and Montgomery insisted that Allied resources were not enough for two full-blooded attacks and that a compromise would only prolong the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He asked Eisenhower to put everything behind one thrust towards Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Eisenhower, who had taken over the direction of the ground battle on 1 September 1944, refused what he called a pencil-like thrust into Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Lieutenant_General_Bernard_Montgomery%2C_commanding_the_British_Eighth_Army_in_North_Africa%2C_in_the_turret_of_his_Grant_command_tank_at_El_Alamein%2C_5_November_1942._E18980.jpg","title":"Montgomery at El Alamein, 5 November 1942","desc":"Montgomery watches the start of the German retreat from the turret of his Grant command tank.","credit":"Capt. G. Keating, No. 1 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum E 18980, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lieutenant_General_Bernard_Montgomery,_commanding_the_British_Eighth_Army_in_North_Africa,_in_the_turret_of_his_Grant_command_tank_at_El_Alamein,_5_November_1942._E18980.jpg","alt":"A British officer in a black beret standing in the turret of a tank, holding binoculars."},"wiki":"Bernard Montgomery"},
    "ww2-578": {"questions":["The German commander Georg Stumme died of a heart attack under fire on the first morning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Rommel was sent back from sick leave to take over.","Because the infantry needed moonlight to lift mines, Alexander and Montgomery set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to open on 23 October 1942, the eve of the full moon."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Battle of El Alamein</b> was the 12-day battle of October and November 1942 in which the British Eighth Army under Montgomery broke the German-Italian Panzer Army in Egypt and drove it into retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The date was set by the moon, since the infantry needed moonlight to lift mines, and Alexander also had to fit the battle to the Allied landings planned in French North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Montgomery had refused Churchill's pressure for a September attack, arguing that troops not yet fully equipped and trained would probably fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 23 October the Eighth Army had about 195,000 men and 1,029 tanks fit for action, against roughly 104,000 Germans and Italians with 496 tanks, the British official history estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Axis held a shield of minefields of unprecedented density, and with the sea on one flank and the Qattara Depression on the other there was no way round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The break-in, codenamed Lightfoot, opened at twenty to ten on the night of 23 October with 456 guns firing on the main front, and the German commander, Georg Stumme, died of a heart attack under fire next morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Montgomery's own account says his plan was to destroy the defending infantry by a methodical 'crumbling' while his armour held off the enemy's tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rommel, back from Germany at Hitler's personal request on the evening of 25 October, later wrote that the northern sector became a mill grinding everything sent into it to dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Lightfoot lost momentum Montgomery changed his plan, and after the break-out, Supercharge, began on 2 November, Hitler ordered Rommel to show his troops 'no other road than to victory or death'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 4 November the line had broken and Rommel withdrew against that order, while the Eighth Army had lost about 13,560 men killed, wounded and missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), chaps. 1–2, 24–52, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), chap. 3, 53–80, and app. 3, 475–76, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August 1942 to 13th May 1943,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 848–49, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 14, “Launching the Battle,” 639–76, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417195. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 15, “The Dog Fight,” 677–754, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417196. [Open access]","Bernard Montgomery, <i>El Alamein to the River Sangro</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1948), 12–13, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.82003. [Open access]","B. H. Liddell Hart, ed., <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, trans. Paul Findlay (London: Collins, 1953), 328–35, https://archive.org/details/ROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Erwin Rommel 1891–1944,” LeMO Biografie, Deutsches Historisches Museum, Berlin, 30 June 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/erwin-rommel. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the battle open by moonlight?","a":"Alexander wrote that the plan needed a series of night infantry attacks to clear gaps in the minefields for the armour, and that the infantry needed good moonlight to lift the mines quickly and accurately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Full moon fell on 24 October, so he and Montgomery chose 23 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official history adds that Rommel foresaw a moonlight attack and ordered his troops redeployed in greater depth by 20 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British win where earlier offensives had failed?","a":"The British official history says their numerical advantage had never been so complete, in fighters, tanks, guns, armoured cars and supplies, with the new Sherman tank as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between August and late October some 41,000 men and over a thousand tanks were sent up to the Eighth Army, Alexander wrote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The official history adds that the Desert Air Force's superiority kept British morale high and wore down the enemy's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rommel retreat against Hitler's order?","a":"The British official history says Rommel, caught between the order and his losses, first tried a compromise, holding some formations in place and pulling the Afrika Korps back a short way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the morning of 4 November he asked permission to withdraw to Fuka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German Historical Museum's biography records that after the British breakthrough he began the retreat against Hitler's order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/El_Alamein_barrage_general_view.jpg","title":"The barrage at El Alamein, 23 October 1942","desc":"The British night artillery barrage that opened the battle, with infantry carriers and ambulances waiting to move up.","credit":"Sgt. Gladstone, No. 1 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum E 18465, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Alamein_barrage_general_view.jpg","alt":"Vehicles silhouetted against a night sky lit by the flashes of many guns firing at once."},"wiki":"Second Battle of El Alamein","places":["pl:q153376"]},
    "ww2-579": {"questions":["Australian doctors noted that on days of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when hot wind was added to the sun's heat, newly arrived men suffered heat exhaustion, and they warned against cutting the water ration.","Wavell's reception party took almost an hour to find him on the Tobruk airfield on 8 April 1941 because a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was filling the air with swirling sand and dust."],"abstract":"The <b>khamsin</b> is the hot, dry wind that blows out of the Sahara across Egypt, filling the air with sand, and its Arabic name, meaning ‘fifty’, comes from the roughly 50 days between March and May in which it blows at intervals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Libya such sandstorms were called ghibli, and German officers recorded that they came roughly every 4 weeks in all seasons and usually lasted 3 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Visibility could fall below 3 metres (10 feet), so that finding the way became impossible and ground and air operations alike came to a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 8 April 1941 a khamsin was blowing so fiercely at Tobruk that Wavell's reception party took almost an hour to find him on the airfield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> 2 days later Australian infantry moving out to the perimeter could not see the ground they were to defend, and the wind refilled their trenches as fast as they dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The wind was one hardship among many, for the German study reports midday temperatures of about 60 °C (140 °F) in the hottest months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Australian doctors found that on khamsin days, when hot wind was added to the sun's heat, newly arrived men suffered heat exhaustion, and they warned against cutting the water ration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water was short everywhere, and after several days of ghibli in May 1941 Rommel wrote that the British in Tobruk were down to half a litre a day and that he hoped to cut it further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Flies were so persistent that they could hardly be kept out of food and drink, and dysentery and desert sores spread where men had no chance to wash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fuel suffered too: leaking 18-litre (4-gallon) tins could cost a British convoy up to 40 per cent of its petrol, while German water columns had to carry everything in 20-litre cans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Khamsin,” in <i>Encyclopædia Britannica</i>, 11th ed., vol. 15 (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1911), Wikisource, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Khamsin. [Open access]","Allan S. Walker, <i>Clinical Problems of War</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 5, vol. 1 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1952), chap. 29, “Environmental Conditions,” https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417365. [Open access]","Allan S. Walker, <i>Middle East and Far East</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 5, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), chap. 11, “Medical Conditions in the Western Desert and Tobruk,” https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417523. [Open access]","Barton Maughan, <i>Tobruk and El Alamein</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1966), chap. 4, “At Bay – The Easter Battle,” 111–58, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417162. [Open access]","Alfred Toppe et al., <i>Desert Warfare: German Experiences in World War II</i>, trans. H. Heitman (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1991), 7–8, 12–13, 23–24, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA244832. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb (September 1941 to September 1942)</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1960), 91, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","B. H. Liddell Hart, ed., <i>The Rommel Papers</i>, trans. Paul Findlay (London: Collins, 1953), 133–34, https://archive.org/details/ROMMELPAPERS. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a sandstorm stop a battle?","a":"The German study says the storms cut visibility to less than 3 metres, so that no one could find his way and ground and air operations came to a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Tobruk in April 1941 the khamsin left artillery officers unable to watch the approaches to the perimeter, and reports that the enemy was coming forward under cover of the dust raised the tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was water a weapon in the desert war?","a":"Rommel wrote in May 1941 that the British in Tobruk were getting only half a litre a day and that he hoped to cut their ration still further with his dive-bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Australian doctors warned that thirst is a physiological warning that cannot be disciplined past a certain point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German study adds that the brackish water held up to a gram of salt in a litre, and that German columns had to carry it in 20-litre cans because they had no tank lorries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did newly arrived troops suffer most?","a":"The German study describes a hand-picked parachute brigade sent to El Alamein in July 1942, more than half of whose men were soon sick from heat, poor food and brackish water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It concludes that units should not be sent to the desert in summer, when the heat and flies were worst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Australian doctors recorded heat exhaustion among recently arrived men on khamsin days, especially where food and water arrangements were poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/A_soldier_battling_his_way_through_a_sandstorm_in_the_Western_Desert_during_1942._CBM1358.jpg","title":"A sandstorm in the Western Desert, 1942","desc":"A soldier battling his way to his tent through a desert sandstorm, photographed by Cecil Beaton for the Ministry of Information.","credit":"Cecil Beaton, Imperial War Museum CBM 1358, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_soldier_battling_his_way_through_a_sandstorm_in_the_Western_Desert_during_1942._CBM1358.jpg","alt":"A lone figure leaning into the wind in a haze of blowing sand, with a tent barely visible."},"wiki":"Khamsin"},
    "ww2-580": {"questions":["Admiral Darlan's order of 10 November 1942 to stop the fighting brought General Noguès to Fedala to negotiate with Patton, ending French resistance to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Morocco after 3 days.","German aircraft began landing at El Aouina near Tunis on 9 November 1942, the day after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put Allied troops ashore near Casablanca, Oran and Algiers."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Torch</b> was the Anglo-American invasion of French North Africa on 8 November 1942, with landings near Casablanca in Morocco and at Oran and Algiers in Algeria, territories then ruled by Vichy France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Combined Chiefs of Staff gave the operation its code name on 25 July 1942, once a heavy landing across the Channel that year had been ruled out for want of landing craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill later told the Commons that the decision was to hold the enemy on the French shore and strike at his southern flank through North Africa, and that orders went out at the end of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 30 July Roosevelt told his chiefs very definitely that, as commander-in-chief, he had decided Torch would be undertaken at the earliest possible date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eisenhower took the title of Commander in Chief, Allied Force, over three task forces, and Patton's Western Task Force, sailing for Morocco, was eventually cut to 33,843 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The French resisted at Casablanca and Oran, while Algiers was surrendered to Ryder's Eastern Assault Force by nightfall on the first day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Late on 10 November Admiral Darlan ordered a halt to the fighting in Pétain's name, and on 11 November General Noguès came to Fedala to negotiate an armistice with Patton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American official history counts 1,469 Allied battle casualties in Morocco and Algeria from 8 to 11 November, 530 of them killed, and says French losses in Morocco were much heavier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany reacted at once: German aircraft began landing at El Aouina near Tunis on 9 November, and by the next day the occupation of Tunisia was under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Egypt, Alexander had timed the attack at El Alamein to fit the plan, for the Allies meant to clear the whole north shore of Africa from both ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II, Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chap. III, 32–59, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-3.html. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II, Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), chap. IX, 171–84, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-9.html. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff and Edwin M. Snell, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1941–1942</i>, United States Army in World War II, The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. 12, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WD-Strategic1/USA-WD-Strategic1-12.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Destruction of the Axis Forces in Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1966), chaps. 6–7, 137–92, https://archive.org/details/med-middle-east-vol-4. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), chap. 3, 66–67, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-3.html. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August 1942 to 13th May 1943,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 849, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, speech in the debate on the Address, <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 11 November 1942, vol. 385, cc. 8–56, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/nov/11/debate-on-the-address. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why invade North Africa rather than France in 1942?","a":"Churchill told the Commons that there would not be enough landing craft for a heavy landing across the Channel in the good weather of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The British and American staffs therefore agreed to hold the enemy on the French shore and strike at his southern flank through North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Roosevelt settled the matter on 30 July, announcing that he had decided Torch would go ahead at the earliest possible date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans reach Tunisia so quickly?","a":"The British official history says that after an ultimatum had encouraged Vichy to acquiesce, German aircraft began landing at El Aouina on 9 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The French commander in Tunisia, General Barré, did not oppose the landings but withdrew his troops westward into the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 15 November there were some 81 German fighters and 28 dive-bombers in Tunisia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fighting in Morocco stop after 3 days?","a":"Late on 10 November General Noguès learned that Admiral Darlan had ordered an end to the useless fighting in Marshal Pétain's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> French officers reached the American lines under white flags early on 11 November, and the cease-fire came only minutes before a full attack on Casablanca was due to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Algiers the city had already been surrendered on the first evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/U.S._landing_barges_speed_shoreward_during_landing_operations%2C_Fadala%2C_French_Morocco_%2827294151155%29.jpg","title":"Landing craft off Fedala, November 1942","desc":"United States landing craft head for the shore at Fedala, about 24 kilometres north of Casablanca, during the Torch landings.","credit":"U.S. Navy, National Museum of the U.S. Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._landing_barges_speed_shoreward_during_landing_operations,_Fadala,_French_Morocco_(27294151155).jpg","alt":"Soldiers crowded in a small landing craft moving across choppy water towards a distant shore."},"wiki":"Operation Torch"},
    "ww2-521": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in September 1942 that 140,000 new volunteers had joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 2 months, proof, he claimed, that Congress could not sway its loyalty.","Officer numbers grew tenfold, from about 1,500 to 15,000, as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dropped old limits on commissioning educated Indians and on recruiting outside the so-called martial races."],"sources":["Bisheshwar Prasad, ed., <i>India and the War, 1939–45</i>, Official History of the Indian Armed Forces in the Second World War (New Delhi: Combined Inter-Services Historical Section, India and Pakistan, 1966), 254–60, https://archive.org/details/india-and-the-war. [Open access]","Gary Sheffield, review of <i>The Indian Army in the Two World Wars</i>, ed. Kaushik Roy, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=36276. [Open access]","Sumit Guha, review of <i>The Indian Army and the End of the Raj</i>, by Daniel Marston, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45021. [Open access]","Anirudh Deshpande, review of <i>India’s War: World War II and the Making of Modern South Asia</i>, by Srinath Raghavan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, October 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47272. [Open access]","Rohit Wanchoo, review of <i>India at War: The Subcontinent and the Second World War</i>, by Yasmin Khan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, January 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45610. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 383 (10 September 1942), “India (Situation),” cols. 302–10, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/sep/10/india-situation. [Open access]","Ryan W. Booth, review of <i>The 1945 Burma Campaign and the Transformation of the British Indian Army</i>, by Raymond Callahan and Daniel Marston, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2022, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=57018. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the army stay voluntary even as it grew so large?","a":"Recruitment rested on volunteers from the start, and Churchill told the Commons in 1942 that there was no compulsory service in India; men joined for pay, food and family reasons, which the social histories of the war stress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the old martial-race system break down?","a":"Expansion needed far more men, and more technically educated ones, than the favoured communities could supply, so the army recruited outside them and commissioned Indian officers on a scale never planned before 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do figures for Indian prisoners who changed sides differ?","a":"The evidence is fragmentary and contested: one review of recent work reports that perhaps more than half of some fifty thousand prisoners joined the Indian National Army, another that the great majority kept their oath to the Crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>British Indian Army</b> was the army of the Raj, a long-service force of Indian soldiers under largely British officers whose first tasks were internal security and the North-West Frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It recruited from a few communities the British called martial races, and the Indian official history admits that before 1939 neither nationalist opinion nor British leaders wanted a large Indian army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Service stayed voluntary throughout the war, and the army grew to almost 900,000 men by the end of 1941, over 1.5 million by the end of 1942 and 1.83 million a year later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its officers multiplied from about 1,500 in 1939 to about 15,000 in 1945, as emergency commissions brought in far more Indians than the slow pre-war Indianisation had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In all about 2.5 million men enlisted in the army, a figure a review of the standard modern study notes was exceeded by only a few countries in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The expansion drew more and more men from outside the old martial classes, and recruits joined for wages, food and family reasons at least as often as for any cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In September 1942, at the height of the Quit India rising, Churchill told the Commons that more than 140,000 volunteers had come forward in 2 months, as proof that Congress could not sway the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Indian divisions fought in East Africa, the Middle East, North Africa and Italy, and an army rebuilt after the defeats in Malaya and Burma, and speeding the Indianisation of its officers, won the Burma campaign of 1944–45.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The official history counts about 70,000 men taken prisoner in Malaya and puts the army’s total casualties at 175,000, of whom about 20,000 were killed in action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Accounts differ on how many Indian prisoners of the Japanese changed sides, from a minority to more than half, and by 1945 doubts about the army’s loyalty were pushing Britain towards concessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/INDIAN_TROOPS_IN_BURMA%2C_1944.jpg","title":"Indian infantry on the Arakan front, 1944","desc":"A section of the 2nd Battalion, 7th Rajput Regiment about to go on patrol in Burma.","credit":"No. 9 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museum IND 2917, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INDIAN_TROOPS_IN_BURMA%2C_1944.jpg","alt":"Smiling Indian soldiers in steel helmets and webbing sit on the ground with rifles and a light machine gun."},"wiki":"British Indian Army"},
    "ww2-522": {"questions":["Australian officials complained that the procedures of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were unintelligible to them, and the Indian clearing house for Empire war supplies in the East was wound up in 1943.","Paired with a Central Provision Office that gathered the armies’ demands, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to share out orders for rifles, guns and stores among India, Australia and New Zealand."],"sources":["Bisheshwar Prasad, ed., <i>India and the War, 1939–45</i>, Official History of the Indian Armed Forces in the Second World War (New Delhi: Combined Inter-Services Historical Section, India and Pakistan, 1966), 262–68, https://archive.org/details/india-and-the-war. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 368 (6 February 1941), “Eastern Group Conference,” col. 1055, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/feb/06/eastern-group-conference. [Open access]","S. J. Butlin, <i>War Economy, 1939–1942</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, Series 4, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1955), chap. 9, “The Drive for Greater Production,” 333–35, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070647. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 13, 372–73, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-13.html. [Open access]","Rohit Wanchoo, review of <i>India at War: The Subcontinent and the Second World War</i>, by Yasmin Khan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, January 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45610. [Open access]","Anirudh Deshpande, review of <i>India’s War: World War II and the Making of Modern South Asia</i>, by Srinath Raghavan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, October 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47272. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the supply council based in India?","a":"India was meant to be the hub: the conference proposed a Central Provision Office in India, stocks for the council were to be held there, and India was becoming the base for the armies in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the council achieve so little?","a":"Needs were hard to forecast, specifications and terms differed between countries, members bought from one another directly, and the Group’s whole share of Empire munitions stayed under two per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did India end the war as Britain’s creditor?","a":"India paid in rupees for supplies bought on Britain’s behalf and exported war goods without matching imports, so it built up sterling balances of more than 1,300 crore rupees by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Eastern Group Supply Council</b> was a wartime body set up in India to pool the production of munitions and military stores among Britain’s territories east of Suez.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the Eastern Group Conference that met in New Delhi in October 1940, to which Australia sent a delegation at Britain’s instigation and the Netherlands East Indies an observer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In February 1941 the Secretary of State for India announced that the governments had accepted its two main proposals, a Central Provision Office in India to coordinate military demands and a council to arrange supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain named Sir Archibald Carter, lately Permanent Secretary to the Admiralty, as its representative and chairman, and the two bodies began work in July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Australian official history found the machinery slow to gather momentum, its forecasts unreliable and its Indian procedures hard for outsiders to follow, and the Indian official history records that it was wound up in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The council was one strand of a far larger effort that, in the Indian official history’s account, turned India into the supply base and arsenal for the armies fighting in Asia and the Middle East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Indian factories made rifles, shells, parachutes, uniforms and machine tools on a scale unknown before 1939, and 3 years into the war India was producing more war supplies than Australia, New Zealand and South Africa combined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet the Eastern Group as a whole, mainly Australia, New Zealand and India, never supplied as much as two per cent of the British Empire’s munitions in any year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> India met in rupees the cost of supplies bought on Britain’s behalf, so its credits in London, the sterling balances, passed 1,300 crore rupees by 1945, while the flood of new currency drove prices up at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New ventures such as the Hindustan Aircraft Company prospered, but a review of the standard modern survey stresses that traders profited from inflation and hoarding while the poor suffered terribly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Indian_workers_at_Hindustan_Aircraft_Factory_in_Bangalore_1944.jpg","title":"War production in Bangalore, 1944","desc":"Workers check newly built auxiliary fuel tanks for aircraft at the Hindustan Aircraft factory.","credit":"US Signal Corps photographer, Imperial War Museum, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_workers_at_Hindustan_Aircraft_Factory_in_Bangalore_1944.jpg","alt":"Rows of large torpedo-shaped metal fuel tanks fill a factory yard while workers bend over them to inspect them."},"wiki":"Eastern Group Supply Council"},
    "ww2-523": {"questions":["Grain dealers shut their shops and fled during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in December 1942, so the Bengal government requisitioned the city’s rice stocks and sold them through controlled shops.","Twenty-seven bombers and about 100 fighters struck the docks on 5 December 1943, the heaviest raid of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, leaving some five hundred civilian casualties behind."],"sources":["Denis Richards and Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. 14, “The Long Road Back to Burma,” 295–300, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RAF-III/UK-RAF-III-14.html. [Open access]","Famine Inquiry Commission, <i>Report on Bengal</i> (New Delhi: Manager of Publications, 1945), 36–37, 83, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.26318. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 120–21, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India’s Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 126, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","Rohit Wanchoo, review of <i>India at War: The Subcontinent and the Second World War</i>, by Yasmin Khan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, January 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45610. [Open access]","Denis Richards and Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), chap. 15, “Arakan, Kohima, and Imphal,” 312–13, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RAF-III/UK-RAF-III-15.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a few small raids cause such disruption?","a":"Their effect was on people more than buildings: thousands fled, cleaners and shopkeepers among them, so grain shops closed and rubbish piled up, and the government had to requisition food stocks within a week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the size of the exodus differ between accounts?","a":"No one counted it: the RAF history gives a round figure of one and a half million fleeing before dawn, while the famine commission, working from Bengal’s records, describes an evacuation that started small and grew over several days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the raids matter for the famine of 1943?","a":"Calcutta’s market dominated Bengal’s rice trade, and the commission found that the closure of shops after the raids forced the government to requisition stocks and then to buy rice on a larger scale in January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>bombing of Calcutta</b> was a series of Japanese air raids on the capital of Bengal, the main port and base behind the Burma front, from December 1942 to December 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The danger had been clear since April 1942, when Japanese warships sank nineteen merchant ships off India’s east coast in a few hours and sailings from Calcutta stopped until the port was almost at a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Through 1942 the city waited for air attack, its Hurricanes flying from the Red Road on the Maidan in full view of the crowds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first raids came by night on 20, 22, 23, 24 and 28 December 1942, when a few bombers did little material damage beyond slightly damaging an oil plant at Budge-Budge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Night fighters with interception radar arrived in mid-January 1943 and shot down five raiders in 2 nights, after which the attacks stopped for the rest of the winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The heaviest raid came by day on 5 December 1943, when 27 bombers with about 100 fighters eluded the defence, bombed the docks twice and caused some 500 civilian casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The RAF’s official history claims that one and a half million people fled before dawn after the first raid, while the famine commission describes an exodus that began small on 22 December and peaked on 24 and 25 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many grain shops closed as their keepers left, so on 27 December the Bengal government requisitioned stocks in the city and sold them through controlled shops and approved markets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commission later judged that the danger of raids upsetting the Calcutta market had been a reason to set up official grain buying months earlier, and the December emergency pushed the government into wider rice procurement in January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the dock raid only a tenth of the labour force stayed at work, though the official history judged that the raids had little effect on military preparations in a city a social history describes as crowded with soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Royal_Air_Force_Operations_in_the_Far_East%2C_1941-1945._CI1893.jpg","title":"A fighter on the Maidan, Calcutta","desc":"A Spitfire leaves its dispersal on the Maidan to take off from the Red Road, the city-centre airstrip built to defend Calcutta from Japanese raids.","credit":"Flying Officer Rider, Royal Air Force official photographer, Imperial War Museum CI 1893, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Air_Force_Operations_in_the_Far_East%2C_1941-1945._CI1893.jpg","alt":"A single-engined fighter aircraft stands on a tree-lined road in a city park, with a man walking beside its wing."},"wiki":"Bombing of Calcutta","places":["city:1159151617"]},
    "ww2-524": {"questions":["Rice prices five or six times their level of early 1942 turned a short harvest into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which an official inquiry reckoned killed some 1.5 million people in Bengal.","Cut off from Burmese rice and stripped of boats by a policy meant to slow a Japanese invasion, the coastal delta suffered among the worst of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the year after."],"sources":["Famine Inquiry Commission, <i>Report on Bengal</i> (New Delhi: Manager of Publications, 1945), 1–4, 25–27, 102–10, 120, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.26318. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 392 (12 October 1943), “India (Food Situation),” cols. 712–16, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/oct/12/india-food-situation. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 393 (4 November 1943), “India (Food Situation),” cols. 886–970, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/nov/04/india-food-situation. [Open access]","Bisheshwar Prasad, ed., <i>India and the War, 1939–45</i>, Official History of the Indian Armed Forces in the Second World War (New Delhi: Combined Inter-Services Historical Section, India and Pakistan, 1966), 267, https://archive.org/details/india-and-the-war. [Open access]","William Logan, review of <i>Churchill’s Secret War: The British Empire and the Ravaging of India during World War II</i>, by Madhusree Mukerjee, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2013, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=37898. [Open access]","Dietmar Rothermund, review of <i>Churchill’s Secret War: The British Empire and the Ravaging of India during World War II</i>, by Madhusree Mukerjee, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, September 2010, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=31289. [in German] [Open access]","Rohit Wanchoo, review of <i>India at War: The Subcontinent and the Second World War</i>, by Yasmin Khan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, January 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45610. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a short harvest become a famine?","a":"Prices did the killing: with Burmese rice cut off and controls failing, rice rose to five or six times its early-1942 price, beyond the reach of the poor, and later critics blamed speculation more than the harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll still disputed?","a":"Registration broke down as village officials fled or died and people died on the roads, so the commission’s 1.5 million rests on incomplete records, and later estimates reach two to three million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain not ship grain sooner?","a":"Amery told the Commons that help from Britain was entirely a matter of shipping, judged against other Allied needs, while critics argue that Churchill’s government refused grain the viceroy urgently requested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Bengal famine of 1943</b> was the worst famine of the Second World War in British India, killing well over a million people in the province that lay behind the Allied front in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its background was poverty, for the famine commission found that much of Bengal lived on the margin of subsistence, with no margin of safety in either health or wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fall of Burma in 1942 cut off imports of rice, the main harvest at the end of 1942 was short after a cyclone in Midnapore, and the price of rice rose to five or six times its level of early 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mortality soared from July 1943, and the commission estimated some 1.5 million deaths above normal in 1943 and the first half of 1944, many from smallpox and other epidemics among people weakened by hunger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later estimates run from two to three million, while the commission rejected a contemporary survey’s figure of 3.5 million for 1943 alone as statistically unsound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was a famine in a theatre of war, for Bengal was the base for the Burma front and the commission judged that the war’s effects were felt there more than anywhere in India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the denial policy of 1942 the army removed surplus rice and large boats from the coastal delta for fear of invasion, which, the commission found, hampered the movement of grain and hit the fishermen hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In London the Secretary of State for India, Leo Amery, told the Commons in October 1943 that British help was entirely a question of shipping, weighed against the Allies’ other urgent needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In November he reported some 8,000 deaths in Calcutta alone between mid-August and mid-October, as the new viceroy, Lord Wavell, sent troops to carry and distribute food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics have blamed London’s refusal of grain, or a long colonial policy of extraction, as much as local failures, and one recent history counts the famine’s dead among the war dead, like those of Stalingrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Dead_or_dying_children_on_a_Calcutta_street_%28the_Statesman_22_August_1943%29.jpg","title":"Calcutta, August 1943","desc":"Dead or dying children on a Calcutta street, a photograph published by the Statesman on 22 August 1943.","credit":"The Statesman, Calcutta, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dead_or_dying_children_on_a_Calcutta_street_%28the_Statesman_22_August_1943%29.jpg","alt":"A woman kneels on a pavement beside two emaciated children lying motionless on the ground."},"wiki":"Bengal famine of 1943"},
    "ww2-525": {"questions":["A shortfall of about 3 weeks’ rice and prices five or six times normal were the two basic causes of the Bengal famine named by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its 1945 report.","Abandoning controls in March 1943 was a mistake, concluded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which also condemned the Bengal government’s ‘No Shortage’ propaganda and its failure to ration Calcutta."],"sources":["Famine Inquiry Commission, <i>Report on Bengal</i> (New Delhi: Manager of Publications, 1945), iii–iv, 25–27, 102–06, https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.26318. [Open access]","Famine Inquiry Commission, <i>Final Report</i> (Madras: Superintendent, Government Press, 1945), vii, https://archive.org/details/dli.csl.1130. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 392 (12 October 1943), “India (Food Situation),” cols. 712–16, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/oct/12/india-food-situation. [Open access]","Rohit Wanchoo, review of <i>India at War: The Subcontinent and the Second World War</i>, by Yasmin Khan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, January 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45610. [Open access]","Dietmar Rothermund, review of <i>Churchill’s Secret War: The British Empire and the Ravaging of India during World War II</i>, by Madhusree Mukerjee, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, September 2010, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=31289. [in German] [Open access]","Bisheshwar Prasad, ed., <i>India and the War, 1939–45</i>, Official History of the Indian Armed Forces in the Second World War (New Delhi: Combined Inter-Services Historical Section, India and Pakistan, 1966), 267, https://archive.org/details/india-and-the-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the commission sit in camera?","a":"Its chairman said feelings over the famine ran so high that witnesses must be free to speak fully, and that public hearings risked political or communal controversy that would disturb the all-India food plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it blame the Bengal government most?","a":"Other provinces handled their food problems with success, and the commission concluded that Bengal could largely have prevented the famine by bold, resolute and well-conceived measures at the right time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why have later writers disputed its findings?","a":"It stressed an absolute shortfall of rice, while later economists argued that speculation and failures of government caused the famine, and others that London’s refusal of grain mattered more than local errors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Famine Inquiry Commission</b> was the body the Government of India appointed in 1944 to report on the causes of the food shortage and epidemics of 1943, above all in Bengal, and on how to prevent their return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Set up under an ordinance and chaired by Sir John Woodhead, it had four other members, three Indians and the physician W. R. Aykroyd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An inquiry had been demanded in the Commons in October 1943, when a member asked for one once the crisis was over and Amery promised only to consider it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commission first met in New Delhi on 18 July 1944, heard 130 witnesses in Calcutta and more in the districts, and sat in camera so that witnesses could speak freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its report on Bengal was signed on 10 April 1945 and published in May, and a final report on food, nutrition and farming across India followed later that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It found two basic causes of the famine, a real shortfall of rice in 1943 of about 3 weeks’ supply and a rise in prices to five or six times their level of early 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It held that famine in the form it took could have been prevented by resolute action, and blamed the Bengal government for abandoning controls in March 1943, its ‘No Shortage’ propaganda and its failure to ration Calcutta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Government of India shared the blame for its late plan to move grain between provinces, while the war’s burdens, the denial policy among them, were weighed as real but not decisive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A recent social history of India’s war argues that the government tried to blunt the report’s impact by publishing it to coincide with the victory celebrations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later economists disputed its stress on a shortfall, arguing that speculation and government failure rather than bad harvests caused the famine, while other critics put the main blame on London’s refusal to send grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/Destitute_mother_and_child_Bengal_famine_1943.jpg","title":"A destitute mother and child, Calcutta","desc":"From Bengal Speaks, a collection of famine photographs published in Calcutta in 1944.","credit":"Kalyani Bhattacharyee, ed., Bengal Speaks (1944), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destitute_mother_and_child_Bengal_famine_1943.jpg","alt":"A thin woman sits on a street with a small, frail child standing beside her and a few tins and scraps of food on the ground."},"wiki":"Famine Inquiry Commission"},
    "ww2-526": {"questions":["Railway stations, telegraph lines and police posts across Bihar were wrecked in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the government arrested the Congress leaders on the morning of 9 August 1942.","Amery reported 846 dead and 2,024 wounded in the suppression of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, admitting that crowds had been machine-gunned from the air on five occasions in August and September 1942."],"sources":["Government of India, <i>Congress Responsibility for the Disturbances, 1942–43</i> (New Delhi: Manager of Publications, 1943), 15, 22–23, 89, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.35918. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 383 (10 September 1942), “India (Situation),” cols. 302–10, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/sep/10/india-situation. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 383 (8 October 1942), “Disturbances (Casualties),” cols. 1341–42, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/oct/08/disturbances-casualties. [Open access]","Sumit Guha, review of <i>The Indian Army and the End of the Raj</i>, by Daniel Marston, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45021. [Open access]","Anirudh Deshpande, review of <i>India’s War: World War II and the Making of Modern South Asia</i>, by Srinath Raghavan, H-Asia, H-Net Reviews, October 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47272. [Open access]","The Under Secretary of State (Welles) to President Roosevelt, 11 August 1942, enclosing a message from Chiang Kai-shek, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1942</i>, vol. 1, <i>General; the British Commonwealth; the Far East</i>, doc. 602, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v01/d602. [Open access]","The Officer in Charge at New Delhi (Merrell) to the Secretary of State, 10 August 1942, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1942</i>, vol. 1, <i>General; the British Commonwealth; the Far East</i>, doc. 600, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v01/d600. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Congress launch the campaign in August 1942?","a":"Gandhi had decided on a final struggle for complete freedom with no room left for negotiation, and he told the Bombay meeting that every Indian should act as a free person from that moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the British able to crush it so quickly?","a":"They arrested the whole leadership at once and used troops, police and aircraft against the crowds, while the army stayed loyal and recruiting even rose during the rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the casualty figures differ so widely?","a":"Each side counted for its own purposes: the government reported 846 killed by October, while an underground Congress bulletin it reprinted claimed hundreds killed and thousands wounded within the first weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Quit India Movement</b> was the campaign the Indian National Congress launched in August 1942 to force an immediate British withdrawal from India, and the rising that followed the arrest of its leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The All-India Congress Committee passed the resolution at Bombay on 8 August, and Gandhi told the meeting that every Indian should act as if already free: ‘We shall do or die.’<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government arrested Gandhi and the Congress leaders the next morning, and from 11 August crowds in Bihar, the eastern United Provinces, Bombay and Madras attacked railways, telegraphs and police stations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government’s own account, published in 1943 to prove Congress guilty, stressed the technical skill of the sabotage, and it reprinted an underground bulletin that claimed 700 dead and 7,000 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Troops and police put the rising down within weeks, firing on crowds and on five occasions machine-gunning them from the air, and by October the official count was 846 dead and 2,024 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The movement came at the worst moment of the war in Asia for Britain, with Burma lost, Indian Army morale low and the Japanese on the frontier of Assam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons that Congress represented neither India nor the Hindu masses, and suggested that Japanese fifth-column work might have aided its sabotage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He cited more than 140,000 new army volunteers in 2 months as proof that Congress could not sway the army, which noted with satisfaction that even new Indian units fired on rioters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Abroad, Chiang Kai-shek warned Roosevelt that the arrests were a great setback to the Allied cause, while the American mission in Delhi expected the movement to go underground and turn more violent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its legacy is still argued over, and a recent survey reads its results as mixed, warning against seeing them through either nationalist or anti-nationalist blinkers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Discussing_the_%22Quit_India%22_movement_with_Nehru.jpg","title":"Gandhi and Nehru, 1942","desc":"Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru discuss the Quit India campaign in 1942.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Discussing_the_%22Quit_India%22_movement_with_Nehru.jpg","alt":"Two men sit cross-legged on a mat; one in a white cap leans towards the other, a bare-chested older man reading papers."},"wiki":"Quit India Movement"},
    "ww2-527": {"questions":["Brought to Tokyo by submarine and aircraft in 1943, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took command of the Indian National Army and headed a Provisional Government of Free India from Singapore.","Two Indian inquiries accepted that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in an air crash in 1945, but a third in 2005 rejected it, and the government in turn rejected that finding."],"sources":["Office of Strategic Services, Research and Analysis Branch, <i>The Rise and Fall of the Indian National Army</i>, Current Intelligence Study no. 24, R&A 3142S (8 June 1945), 1–4, CIA Reading Room document 0000709795, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-0000709795. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India’s Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 432, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","Jaideep Prabhu, review of <i>Subhas Chandra Bose in Nazi Germany: Politics, Intelligence, and Propaganda 1941–43</i>, by Romain Hayes, H-War, H-Net Reviews, April 2012, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=35513. [Open access]","Leah Renold, review of <i>Women at War: Subhas Chandra Bose and the Rani of Jhansi Regiment</i>, by Vera Hildebrand, H-War, H-Net Reviews, October 2019, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52575. [Open access]","Lok Sabha, Ministry of Home Affairs, answer to unstarred question no. 5462, “Death of Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose,” 6 September 2011, https://archive.org/details/eparlib.nic.in.606682. [Open access]","The Commissioner in India (Merrell) to the Secretary of State, 28 February 1946, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 5, <i>The British Commonwealth, Western and Central Europe</i>, doc. 46, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v05/d46. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Decisive Battles</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 446, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Bose turn to the Axis?","a":"He held that compromise with Britain was hopeless and that independence could come only by force, so Britain’s enemies were his natural allies, whatever he thought of their regimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany never declare for Indian independence?","a":"Ribbentrop said Germany would not promise what it could not carry out, and Hitler told Bose that a declaration was useless until the military situation allowed it to be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his death still disputed?","a":"The evidence is indirect: inquiries in 1956 and 1974 accepted the crash and that his ashes lie in a Tokyo temple, while the 2005 commission held that he did not die in the crash, a finding the government rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Subhas Chandra Bose</b> was a Bengali nationalist who led the Indian National Congress before the war and then sought India’s freedom in alliance with the Axis, at the head of the Japanese-sponsored Indian National Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was elected Congress president in 1938 and again in 1939, but a clash with Gandhi and Nehru, who distrusted his radicalism, forced him to resign, and he founded the Forward Bloc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had long argued that independence would come only by force, and through the 1930s he courted Mussolini and German officials while still denouncing Nazi racial policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Escaping from India in 1941, he reached Berlin, where he won a Free India Centre, a radio station and leave to recruit Indian prisoners, but never the Axis declaration for Indian independence he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler told him that such a declaration was useless until the military situation allowed it to be enforced, and Bose, the record suggests, privately called Hitler raving mad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese brought him by submarine and air to Tokyo in May 1943, and in Singapore he became head of a Provisional Government of Free India and commander-in-chief of the INA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He recruited among Indian prisoners and the plantation workers of Malaya, raised a women’s unit, the Rani of Jhansi Regiment, and pressed Japan to invade India, where its planners hoped his army might spark a revolt in Bengal and Bihar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> American intelligence called him an excellent potential puppet for Japan, yet recorded his difficulties with Japanese commanders and his disputes over propaganda and over Indian property in Burma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was reported killed in an air crash in 1945, and two Indian inquiries accepted the crash, while a third in 2005 rejected it, a finding the government refused to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many Indians would not believe he was dead, and his birthday in January 1946 brought disturbances in Bombay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101III-Alber-064-31A%2C_Subhas_Chandra_Bose_bei_Heinrich_Himmler.jpg","title":"Bose with Himmler, 1942","desc":"Subhas Chandra Bose, right, in conversation with Heinrich Himmler, left, and SS officers at a German field headquarters in the summer of 1942.","credit":"Kurt Alber, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101III-Alber-064-31A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101III-Alber-064-31A,_Subhas_Chandra_Bose_bei_Heinrich_Himmler.jpg","alt":"A group of men in uniform talk outdoors among trees; a man in glasses and a grey SS uniform gestures at the left, and a man in glasses and a dark coat stands at the right."},"wiki":"Subhas Chandra Bose"},
    "ww2-528": {"questions":["Three regiments of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the Chindwin in March 1944 for the much-heralded March on Delhi, and came back from Imphal sick, hungry and demoralised.","The British put the number enrolled in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 43,000, of whom only 20,000 had served in the Indian Army, while a modern study puts its peak at 40,000."],"sources":["Office of Strategic Services, Research and Analysis Branch, <i>The Rise and Fall of the Indian National Army</i>, Current Intelligence Study no. 24, R&A 3142S (8 June 1945), 1–4, CIA Reading Room document 0000709795, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-0000709795. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 416 (3 December 1945), written answers, “Indian National Army,” cols. 2049–50W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written-answers/1945/dec/03/indian-national-army. [Open access]","Takuma Melber, conference report, “Indians in War and Revolution in the Asia-Pacific Region,” Australian National University, 20–21 February 2017, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, September 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50517. [Open access]","Sumit Guha, review of <i>The Indian Army and the End of the Raj</i>, by Daniel Marston, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45021. [Open access]","Gary Sheffield, review of <i>The Indian Army in the Two World Wars</i>, ed. Kaushik Roy, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=36276. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 2, <i>India’s Most Dangerous Hour</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1958), 383, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-2. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 371–72, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did captured Indian soldiers join it?","a":"Motives were mixed: enlistment offered escape from bad camps and a hope of going home, many believed they would help free India, and others joined under pressure or threats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Japanese make so little use of it in battle?","a":"They often regarded its men with suspicion, gave them inferior equipment and used them for propaganda, reconnaissance and screening rather than as regular fighting units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it matter more after the war than during it?","a":"Its military effect was small, but the trials of its officers in 1945 turned it into a political issue that proved dangerous for the government and the Indian Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Indian National Army</b> was a force of Indian prisoners of war and Indian civilians in South-East Asia, raised under Japanese sponsorship to fight for India’s independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Japanese intelligence began forming it among the Indian troops captured in Malaya as soon as Singapore fell on 15 February 1942, and a conference at Bangkok launched it formally in June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A quarrel between its first leader and the Japanese-backed Rash Behari Bose led to its temporary disbandment, and Subhas Chandra Bose revived it after reaching Singapore in mid-1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 70,000 Indian soldiers were in Japanese hands, and men joined to escape the prison camps, from belief in Indian freedom or under pressure, while plantation workers in Malaya were recruited in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its size is variously given, for the British put the number enrolled at about 43,000, of whom only 20,000 had served in the Indian Army, while a standard modern study puts its peak at 40,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Three INA regiments crossed the Chindwin in March 1944 for the Japanese offensive on Imphal, the much-heralded March on Delhi, and came back sick, hungry and demoralised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese used its units mainly for propaganda, reconnaissance and screening, gave them inferior equipment, and seldom trusted them as regular fighting formations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Burma fighting of 1945 its men surrendered on a large scale, as at Magwe in April, where most of the INA in a mixed force gave themselves up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> American intelligence judged in June 1945 that it had been an ineffective device, since British-Indian troops proved willing to fight it, and a modern study agrees it had little effect on the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yet it exposed the British belief in the inherent loyalty of Indian soldiers as a fantasy, and the trials of its officers after the war proved politically dangerous for the Raj.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Surrendered_Indian_National_Army_troops_at_Mount_Popa.jpg","title":"INA soldiers after surrender, 1945","desc":"Indian National Army troops who surrendered at Mount Popa in Burma, around April 1945.","credit":"Soldiers of the British Fourteenth Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surrendered_Indian_National_Army_troops_at_Mount_Popa.jpg","alt":"A group of soldiers in forage caps and khaki sit on sandy ground under palm trees, looking at the camera."},"wiki":"Indian National Army"},
    "ww2-529": {"questions":["Students marching in Calcutta in November 1945 against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> clashed with police for 4 days, while strikes halted all public transport in the city.","Held in the Red Fort at Delhi from November 1945, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put officers of a Japanese-sponsored army in the dock for waging war, murder and brutality to prisoners."],"sources":["Nripendra Nath Mitra, ed., <i>The Indian Annual Register, July–December 1945</i>, vol. 2 (repr., New Delhi: Gian Publishing House), 25, 291–92, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.45420. [Open access]","Durlab Singh, ed., <i>Formation and Growth of the Indian National Army (Azad Hind Fauj)</i> (Lahore: Hero Publications, 1946), publishers’ note and charge sheet, https://archive.org/details/1946azadhindfauj. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 416 (3 December 1945), written answers, “Indian National Army,” cols. 2049–50W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written-answers/1945/dec/03/indian-national-army. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 416 (3 December 1945), written answers, “Disturbances (Madras and Calcutta),” cols. 2050–52W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written-answers/1945/dec/03/disturbances-madras-and-calcutta. [Open access]","<i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 419 (15 February 1946), “Calcutta (Riots),” cols. 665–66, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1946/feb/15/calcutta-riots. [Open access]","Nripendra Nath Mitra, ed., <i>The Indian Annual Register, January–June 1946</i>, vol. 1 (Calcutta: Annual Register Office, 1946), 28–29, 281–82, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.85515. [Open access]","Sumit Guha, review of <i>The Indian Army and the End of the Raj</i>, by Daniel Marston, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45021. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the first three officers freed after being convicted?","a":"The law allowed only death or transportation for life for waging war, but the government had announced that it would punish only gross brutality, so the commander-in-chief confirmed the verdicts and remitted the sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Captain Rashid’s case cause riots?","a":"His sentence was reduced to 7 years rather than remitted, because he was found guilty of beating prisoners, and students in Calcutta protested that he was not released as the first three had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the trials worry the government?","a":"Public protest, strikes and rioting followed them, and a modern study concludes that they proved politically dangerous for both the government and the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>INA trials</b> were the courts martial that the Government of India held from November 1945 against officers of the Japanese-sponsored Indian National Army, the first of them in the Red Fort at Delhi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Policy announced in December 1945 was to try only men accused of serious crimes beyond waging war against the King, such as murder or brutality to prisoners, an estimated 20 to 50 of some 43,000 people involved with the INA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first trial opened on 5 November 1945 against three officers of the Indian Army, Captain Shah Nawaz Khan, Captain P. K. Sahgal and Lieutenant Gurbakhsh Singh Dhillon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three were charged under the Indian Penal Code with waging war against the King Emperor in Malaya and Burma between September 1942 and April 1945, Dhillon with murder, and the other two with abetment of murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bhulabhai Desai led the defence, Nehru had demanded their release or at least mild treatment, and a Lahore publisher of the day called the trial the issue that had gripped India more than any other in 15 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Students protesting against the trial clashed with police at Madura on 6 November and in Calcutta from 21 to 24 November, where police fired 14 times and strikes stopped all public transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The court found all three guilty of waging war, which carried a mandatory sentence of death or transportation for life, and sentenced them to transportation for life and cashiering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 January 1946 the commander-in-chief in India confirmed the findings but remitted the sentences of transportation, and the three officers were freed and given public receptions in Delhi and Lahore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Captain Abdul Rashid’s life sentence for brutality to prisoners was commuted only to 7 years, Muslim students in Calcutta protested in February 1946, and the riots left 38 civilians dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A modern study of the army at the end of the Raj judges that the trials proved politically dangerous for both the government and the Indian Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Netaji_Subhas_Chandra_Bose_and_Members_of_the_Azad_Hind_Fauj.jpg","title":"Bose and officers of the INA","desc":"Subhas Chandra Bose with INA officers, among them Shah Nawaz Khan, P. K. Sahgal and G. S. Dhillon, later the accused of the first Red Fort trial.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Netaji_Subhas_Chandra_Bose_and_Members_of_the_Azad_Hind_Fauj.jpg","alt":"Eleven men and one woman in military uniform stand in two rows on the steps of a building."},"wiki":"Indian National Army trials"},
    "ww2-530": {"questions":["With a constitution approved by an assembly the Japanese had appointed, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent Ba Maw to the Greater East Asia Conference in Tokyo in November 1943.","District officials of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent their days finding labourers for the Japanese army, a British survey of 1944 reported, and were slapped in public for any hesitation."],"sources":["W. H. Payton, comp., <i>Burma during the Japanese Occupation</i>, vol. 2 (Simla: Government of India Press, 1944), 10–14, 30–31, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.206262. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 33–34, 335–37, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","Michael Barnhart, review of <i>The Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere: When Total Empire Met Total War</i>, by Jeremy A. Yellen, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, December 2019, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=54609. [Open access]","Lee Eysturlid, review of <i>The Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere: When Total Empire Met Total War</i>, by Jeremy A. Yellen, H-Empire, H-Net Reviews, April 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=55164. [Open access]","Office of Strategic Services, Research and Analysis Branch, <i>The Rise and Fall of the Indian National Army</i>, Current Intelligence Study no. 24, R&A 3142S (8 June 1945), 1–4, CIA Reading Room document 0000709795, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-0000709795. [Open access]","The Consul at Rangoon (Abbey) to the Secretary of State, 22 April 1946, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 1, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v08/d1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan grant Burma independence in 1943?","a":"With the war going badly, Japan began treating the states of its sphere as nominally independent under its guidance, and Burmese nationalists used the grant to build an army and a state of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did British observers call the independence a sham?","a":"Ba Maw was proclaimed without any election, the assembly that approved the constitution was a Japanese creation, and district officials answered to local Japanese commanders rather than to Rangoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Burma National Army turn on the Japanese?","a":"Its leaders were in contact with an anti-Japanese organisation and British agents from 1944, and with the British advancing in 1945 they rose about 27 March, before the British were ready.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>State of Burma</b> was the government that Japan set up in occupied Burma on 1 August 1943, with Ba Maw as its head of state, the Adipadi, holding full sovereign powers on paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed a Japanese military administration and a Burmese executive administration set up in 1942 and 1943, and its constitution was approved that morning by an assembly that was the Japanese-appointed Independence Preparatory Commission under another name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same afternoon Ba Maw read out a declaration of war on Britain and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its leaders, Thakin Nu as foreign minister among them, tried to use Japan’s promises to lay the foundations of independence, from a Burmese army to a national bank, and Ba Maw attended the Greater East Asia Conference in Tokyo in November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its army, given the name Burma National Army in August 1943 under Aung San, was an army that, one study notes, Japan’s sphere had done much to create, and it would dominate Burma after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Real power stayed with the Japanese army, and a survey compiled for the exiled British government of Burma in 1944 found district officials spending their time finding labour for the Japanese and being slapped for any hesitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cattle, food, clothing and livestock were requisitioned and labour was levied, and the same survey reported Ba Maw personally unpopular and his single party beginning to fall apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That survey was written by the regime’s enemies, while modern scholarship treats Ba Maw and his colleagues as patriotic collaborators who used the occupation for national ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Relations with Burma’s large Indian minority were tense, and American intelligence noted Burmese anger that the Japanese let Indians keep control of their property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In March 1945 the Burma National Army, some 8,000 strong, rose against the Japanese, and the war left the country, in an American consul’s words, completely devastated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Ba_Maw_1943.png","title":"Ba Maw, 1943","desc":"Ba Maw, head of state of Japanese-occupied Burma, in a Japanese photograph of 1943.","credit":"Unknown photographer, from a 1943 Japanese photograph album in the National Diet Library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ba_Maw_1943.png","alt":"Portrait of a smiling middle-aged man wearing a white Burmese headdress and a white jacket."},"wiki":"State of Burma","places":["city:1159151477"]},
    "ww2-531": {"questions":["Wearing a uniform like a Japanese major-general's, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met Slim at Meiktila in May 1945 and claimed to command the army of a provisional Burmese government.","Gunmen in army uniforms drove into the Secretariat grounds in Rangoon on 19 July 1947 and killed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along with five other members of Burma's Executive Council."],"abstract":"<b>Aung San</b> was the Burmese nationalist who commanded the army raised in Burma under the Japanese occupation and then turned it against the Japanese in the last months of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That army carried several names between its founding as the Burma Independence Army in early 1942 and its renaming as the Patriot Burmese Forces in July 1945, after which it was absorbed into the regular Burmese army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aung San also sat on the executive council of the Anti-Fascist Organisation, an underground nationalist movement whose military wing his army became.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From late 1943 the British clandestine service Force 136 tried to reach the movement, and on 1 January 1945 an agent reported that the army, some 8,000 strong, was preparing to fight the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rose against them about 27 March 1945, as British and Indian forces were driving south towards Rangoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Backing the rising was a political gamble, and Mountbatten reminded the Chiefs of Staff that Aung San was known to be guilty of treason through his collaboration with the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He argued all the same that refusing to let the Burmese fight the common enemy would cause trouble with liberal opinion in Britain and America, and that suppressing the movement by force would tie down troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Aung San met General Slim at Meiktila on 16 May 1945, wearing a uniform like a Japanese major-general's, he presented himself as the commander of a provisional government's army, and Slim told him that his army had no legal status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1947, after long talks with Aung San and other members of Burma's Executive Council, the British government agreed that a Constituent Assembly would be elected that April by more than six million voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 19 July 1947 gunmen in army uniforms killed Aung San and five other members of the Executive Council in Rangoon, and U Saw, whom two surviving members suspected and in whose house a large store of weapons was found, was arrested the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 33, 333–38, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 50–52, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 144, 174, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","Clement Attlee, statement on the United Kingdom and Burma agreement, House of Commons, 28 January 1947, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 432, cols. 777–82, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1947/jan/28/united-kingdom-and-burma-agreement. [Open access]","“Burma (Assassinations),” House of Commons, 21 July 1947, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 440, cols. 865–67, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1947/jul/21/burma-assassinations. [Open access]","“The Consul General at Rangoon (Packer) to the Secretary of State,” 19 July 1947, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1947</i>, vol. 6, <i>The Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1972), doc. 41, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1947v06/d41. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Douglas) to the Secretary of State,” 22 July 1947, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1947</i>, vol. 6, <i>The Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1972), doc. 42, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1947v06/d42. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British back a man they regarded as a collaborator?","a":"Because his army was going to rise whatever they did, and Mountbatten judged that refusing to let the Burmese fight the common enemy would anger liberal opinion at home and in America, while crushing the rising would tie down troops he needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why had Aung San's army first served under the Japanese?","a":"It was raised under the Japanese occupation in early 1942 and later became the military wing of the Anti-Fascist Organisation, a nationalist movement; when he met Slim in 1945 Aung San said he answered to a provisional government of Burma, not to any foreign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his murder not halt Burma's road to independence?","a":"Thakin Nu agreed at once to form a new Executive Council, and Attlee told the Commons that the killings would in no way deflect the government from its settled policy towards Burma's aspirations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Aung_San_%281915-1947%29_door_Klaas_van_Buiten_%28Anefo%29.jpg","title":"Aung San in January 1947","desc":"Aung San photographed by the Dutch agency Anefo on 22 January 1947, the month of the London talks that set a date for Burma's Constituent Assembly.","credit":"Klaas van Buiten / Anefo, Nationaal Archief, CC0 1.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aung_San_(1915-1947)_door_Klaas_van_Buiten_(Anefo).jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of Aung San in a dark overcoat among other men."},"wiki":"Aung San"},
    "ww2-532": {"questions":["American agents who reached Bangkok in 1945 found that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was less a resistance movement than a conspiracy of the regent, the police chief and ministers.","Guerrillas of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, trained by the Office of Strategic Services and Force 136, were never called into action before Thailand disavowed its declaration of war in August 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Free Thai Movement</b> was the network of Thais at home and abroad who worked with the Allies against Japan while their own government was Japan's ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thailand signed a treaty of alliance with Japan at Bangkok on 21 December 1941, and on 25 January 1942 it declared war on Britain and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington chose to ignore the declaration, kept recognising the Thai minister in Washington, Seni Pramoj, and treated Thailand as enemy-occupied territory, while Britain accepted that it was at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first agents the American Office of Strategic Services sent in were Thai students recruited in the United States and infiltrated from China, and their results were poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Contact with the regent, Pridi Banomyong, came only at the end of 1944, when two agents dropped into the north reached Bangkok and confirmed that he himself headed the underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What the Americans found was less a resistance movement than a patriotic conspiracy inside the state, embracing the regent, the chief of police, several ministers and senior officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By January 1945 the State Department saw Thailand as an occupied country whose collaborationist administration had given way to one largely controlled by Pridi, who wanted his army to fight beside the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The guerrillas trained by the Americans and by Britain's Force 136 were never called into action, but the Japanese suspected the Thais and reinforced their garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 16 August 1945, the day after Japan's surrender, Thailand disavowed its declaration of war and repudiated its agreements with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Seni Pramoj returned to become prime minister, and the state of war with Britain did not formally end until 1 January 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“The Department of State to the British Embassy,” aide-mémoire, 19 January 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 1, <i>General; the British Commonwealth; the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1960), doc. 803, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v01/d803. [Open access]","“The Department of State to the British Embassy,” aide-mémoire, 7 February 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 1, <i>General; the British Commonwealth; the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1960), doc. 807, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v01/d807. [Open access]","“Memorandum Prepared in the Division of Southwest Pacific Affairs,” 13 January 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 922, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d922. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Chief of the Division of Southeast Asian Affairs (Moffat),” 16 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 949, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d949. [Open access]","United States Office of Strategic Services, <i>War Report of the OSS</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Overseas Targets</i> (New York: Walker, 1976), 407–12, https://archive.org/details/warreportofossof02unit. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 289–92, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States never declare war on Thailand?","a":"Washington judged it more practical not to answer the declaration, kept dealing with the Thai minister in Washington, and treated the country as enemy-occupied territory; by 1945 the State Department regarded Thailand as an occupied country rather than an enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Thai underground unlike resistance movements in Europe?","a":"Because it was run from inside the government: the regent, the police chief, ministers and senior officers belonged to it, so the state's own intelligence services became the American network in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans favour Pridi's government?","a":"They saw Pridi as an opponent of Japan from the first who was aiding Allied intelligence and wanted Thai troops to fight beside the Allies, and they feared that stripping Thailand of territory would look like a continuation of European imperialism in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Pridi_Banomyong_in_Uniform_Photo_1946.png","title":"Pridi Banomyong","desc":"Pridi Banomyong, regent of Thailand during the war and the secret head of its underground, in civil service uniform in 1946.","credit":"Thai government, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pridi_Banomyong_in_Uniform_Photo_1946.png","alt":"A formal portrait of a man in a white civil service uniform with decorations."},"wiki":"Free Thai Movement"},
    "ww2-533": {"questions":["Emperor Bao Dai was placed at the head of a Vietnam declared independent of France after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> swept away the Vichy administration in March 1945.","Some French columns fought their way into China during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Tokyo tribunal later judged a war of aggression against the Republic of France."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese coup d'état in French Indochina</b> was the seizure of the colony on 9 March 1945, when Japanese forces disarmed the French army and police and ended the rule of the Vichy administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Since 1940 the Japanese had stationed troops in Indochina while leaving its French government in place, and that government had collaborated openly with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After its reverses in the Pacific the French administration grew indifferent, and by February 1945 Imperial General Headquarters feared that it would support an American landing on the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Supreme War Council therefore decided to demand that all French troops and armed police be placed under Japanese command, together with the means of communication and transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At six in the evening of 9 March the Japanese ambassador presented the demand to Governor-General Jean Decoux and gave him 2 hours to accept it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Decoux replied that he could accept only if an American landing took place, and he was arrested as Japanese troops attacked French garrisons across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was fighting at Hanoi, Hue, Lang Son and elsewhere, French soldiers were in some places killed after surrendering, and Japan held the country by 14 March, though some French columns escaped into China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The French provisional government had organised a resistance in the colony, and on 12 March its ambassador in Washington asked for help, which was soon authorised as far as it could be spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japan then set up Emperor Bao Dai at the head of a Vietnam declared independent of France, and Cambodia also declared its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The coup ended French authority before the Allies arrived, and an American study later judged that it gave the Viet Minh a new political opening, which the movement used to set up its own administration at Hanoi when Japan surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VII, “The Pacific War,” 992–93, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 229–30, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 290, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","“The French Ambassador (Bonnet) to the Secretary of State,” 12 March 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 173, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d173. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Secretary of State (Dunn),” 19 March 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 177, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d177. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Post Surrender Tasks: Section E of the Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1969), 286–87, https://archive.org/details/postsurrendertasks. [Open access]","United States Department of Defense, <i>United States–Vietnam Relations, 1945–1967</i>, pt. I, sec. I.B.1, “The Character and Power of the Viet Minh,” B-26–B-27, National Archives, https://www.archives.gov/research/pentagon-papers. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan overthrow an administration that had cooperated with it?","a":"By early 1945 Japanese headquarters believed that the French in Indochina would back an American landing, so it decided to put their troops and police under Japanese command and acted when Decoux refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Tokyo tribunal call the coup a war of aggression?","a":"Japan's council had declared that the two countries would not be considered at war, but the tribunal found that disarming French forces by ultimatum and attack was itself the waging of aggressive war against France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the coup matter for Vietnam's future?","a":"It destroyed French authority months before the war ended, put a nominally independent Vietnamese government in place under Bao Dai, and gave the Viet Minh a new political opening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Prise_de_Hanoi_9_mars_1945.jpg","title":"Japanese troops in Hanoi","desc":"Japanese soldiers marching through a street, captioned on Commons as Hanoi after the coup of 9 March 1945.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prise_de_Hanoi_9_mars_1945.jpg","alt":"A column of helmeted soldiers with rifles and a Rising Sun flag marches down a street watched by civilians."},"wiki":"Japanese coup d'état in French Indochina"},
    "ww2-534": {"questions":["Hồ Chí Minh's declaration of independence in September 1945 blamed French and Japanese rule for more than two million deaths in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> earlier that year.","Peasants forced to grow jute for Japan instead of rice were among the victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which reached its height in Tonkin in March and April 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Vietnamese famine of 1944–1945</b> was the mass starvation that struck northern and north-central Vietnam under Japanese occupation and French colonial rule, reaching its height in March and April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of the dead run from French figures of 600,000 to 700,000 to official Vietnamese figures of one to two million, and the total is still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure of two million was first put forward by Hồ Chí Minh and repeated for decades with little evidence, and a joint Vietnamese and Japanese study of the 1990s could not settle a final number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A recent economic history puts the toll at about a million and treats typhoons that struck the coast as the immediate cause of the shortfall in food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It argues that the Japanese occupiers, the French administration and the Americans directing attacks on transport could each have limited the famine, but under the pressure of war none chose to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The heaviest burden on peasants was the compulsory sale of rice to the authorities at a fixed price far below the market's, and the quotas took between half and four-fifths of the real harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peasants were also made to uproot rice for jute and other crops Japan needed, rice and maize were burned as fuel when bombing cut coal supplies, and the dikes were neglected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enough rice existed in the south to avert the disaster, yet bombing hampered transport northwards and rice exports to other parts of Japan's empire went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In January 1945 the new French head of Tonkin did not cut the Japanese-imposed rice levy, and on 9 March the Japanese swept away the French administration altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Viet Minh broke open rice stores and made the famine central to its case against French and Japanese rule, and Hồ Chí Minh's declaration of independence in September 1945 blamed them for more than two million deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey Gunn, “The Great Vietnamese Famine of 1944–45 Revisited,” <i>Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 9, no. 5 (2011): e5, https://doi.org/10.1017/S1557466011010850. [Open access]","Ken MacLean, “History Reformatted: Vietnam’s Great Famine (1944–45) in Archival Form,” <i>Southeast Asian Studies</i> 5, no. 2 (2016): 187–218, https://doi.org/10.20495/seas.5.2_187. [Open access]","Gregg Huff, “Causes and Consequences of the Great Vietnam Famine, 1944–5,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 72, no. 1 (2019): 286–316, abstract, https://ehs.org.uk/article/causes-and-consequences-of-the-great-vietnam-famine-1944-5. [Paywalled]","Van Nguyen-Marshall, review of <i>Rice Wars in Colonial Vietnam: The Great Famine and the Viet Minh Road to Power</i>, by Geoffrey C. Gunn, <i>Pacific Affairs</i> 89, no. 3 (2016): 714–16, https://pacificaffairs.ubc.ca/book-reviews/rice-wars-in-colonial-vietnam-the-great-famine-and-the-viet-minh-road-to-power-by-geoffrey-c-gunn/. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 229–30, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","United States Department of Defense, <i>United States–Vietnam Relations, 1945–1967</i>, pt. I, sec. I.B.1, “The Character and Power of the Viet Minh,” B-34, National Archives, https://www.archives.gov/research/pentagon-papers. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do estimates of the dead differ so widely?","a":"The records are uneven: French officials counted 600,000 to 700,000, the Vietnamese state adopted Hồ Chí Minh's two million with little supporting evidence, and a joint Vietnamese and Japanese study in the 1990s could not fix a total.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a shortage of food become a famine?","a":"Compulsory rice sales took much of the harvest, land was turned over to jute and other crops for Japan, the dikes were neglected, and rice from the south did not reach the north while exports to the Japanese empire went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the famine help the Viet Minh?","a":"It seized rice stocks and blamed the French and Japanese together, so the famine gave it relief to offer, a common enemy to name and people to organise and recruit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Famine_in_Vietnam%2C_1945.jpg","title":"Famine in Vietnam, 1945","desc":"A crowd of destitute people sitting in a city street during the famine of 1945, photographed by Võ An Ninh.","credit":"Võ An Ninh, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Famine_in_Vietnam,_1945.jpg","alt":"A street packed with ragged people sitting and lying on the ground in front of colonial buildings."},"wiki":"Vietnamese famine of 1944–1945"},
    "ww2-535": {"questions":["American intelligence officers worked with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the only effective resistance inside Vietnam, and in August 1945 its forces took power in Hanoi.","Seizing rice stocks during the famine of 1944–1945 brought recruits to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ordered a general uprising on 13 August 1945 as Japan collapsed."],"abstract":"The <b>Việt Minh</b>, short for the League for the Independence of Vietnam, was the national front founded in May 1941 under the leadership of the Indochinese Communist Party to resist both Japanese and French rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was set up at a meeting in southern China convened by Nguyen Ai Quoc, the man later known as Hồ Chí Minh, who became its general secretary while most of its key posts went to party members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first permanent bases were built in 1942 and 1943 in the mountains north of Hanoi, and on 22 December 1944 it formed the first unit of its Liberation Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of 1944 it claimed 500,000 members, a figure from the movement itself, and a British official history credits it with some 10,000 men under arms by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officers of the American Office of Strategic Services dealt with it as the only effective resistance organisation inside Vietnam, for intelligence and for help in rescuing downed pilots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese overthrow of the French administration on 9 March 1945 opened new political room, and in April the Communist leadership ordered a shadow government to be formed throughout the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the famine of 1944–1945 the Việt Minh led resistance to forced rice sales and seized rice stocks, which brought it support and recruits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One economic history treats the famine as instrumental in the revolution that followed, though a reviewer of another study judged the evidence linking the two to be thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 13 August 1945, as Japan collapsed, the Việt Minh ordered a general uprising, and on 19 August its forces took power in Hanoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Emperor Bao Dai abdicated, a Vietnamese republic was proclaimed, and the Việt Minh took the Japanese surrender in the north while it organised resistance to the British and French in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of Defense, <i>United States–Vietnam Relations, 1945–1967</i>, pt. I, sec. I.B.1, “The Character and Power of the Viet Minh,” B-18–B-19, B-26–B-27, National Archives, https://www.archives.gov/research/pentagon-papers. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 290–91, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Post Surrender Tasks: Section E of the Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1969), 286–87, https://archive.org/details/postsurrendertasks. [Open access]","Geoffrey Gunn, “The Great Vietnamese Famine of 1944–45 Revisited,” <i>Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 9, no. 5 (2011): e5, https://doi.org/10.1017/S1557466011010850. [Open access]","Van Nguyen-Marshall, review of <i>Rice Wars in Colonial Vietnam: The Great Famine and the Viet Minh Road to Power</i>, by Geoffrey C. Gunn, <i>Pacific Affairs</i> 89, no. 3 (2016): 714–16, https://pacificaffairs.ubc.ca/book-reviews/rice-wars-in-colonial-vietnam-the-great-famine-and-the-viet-minh-road-to-power-by-geoffrey-c-gunn/. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Reconquest of Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1965), 229–30, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-4. [Open access]","Gregg Huff, “Causes and Consequences of the Great Vietnam Famine, 1944–5,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 72, no. 1 (2019): 286–316, abstract, https://ehs.org.uk/article/causes-and-consequences-of-the-great-vietnam-famine-1944-5. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Americans work with a Communist-led movement?","a":"Because during the war the Office of Strategic Services found it the only effective resistance organisation inside Vietnam, able to supply intelligence and to help rescue downed Allied pilots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the Việt Minh take power so quickly in August 1945?","a":"The Japanese coup had already destroyed French authority, its village committees had become the real government in many areas, and it moved to disarm the Japanese and take power before Allied forces arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the movement's own figures treated with caution?","a":"Much of the record of its wartime strength comes from Communist histories, which an American study judged to magnify its importance, and its membership claims were its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Ho_Chi_Minh_%28third_from_left_standing%29_and_the_OSS_in_1945.jpg","title":"Hồ Chí Minh and the Deer Team","desc":"Hồ Chí Minh, third from left standing, and Võ Nguyên Giáp with members of the American OSS Deer Team in 1945.","credit":"U.S. Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ho_Chi_Minh_(third_from_left_standing)_and_the_OSS_in_1945.jpg","alt":"A group of American soldiers and Vietnamese men posing in a jungle clearing, some standing and some kneeling."},"wiki":"Viet Minh"},
    "ww2-536": {"questions":["Disturbed by the forced labourers dying around them, a battalion of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Blitar in East Java rose against the Japanese on 14 February 1945.","The day after independence was proclaimed, the Japanese ordered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disbanded, but many of its units in Java kept their weapons and clashed with them."],"abstract":"The <b>Defenders of the Homeland</b>, known by the initials PETA, was the volunteer army that the Japanese raised in occupied Java, trained by Japanese instructors but officered by Indonesians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was formally created on 3 October 1943 by an ordinance of the Japanese 16th Army, which occupied Java and Madura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its battalions were formed from the end of 1943, roughly one to each district, and to keep them harmless they were not linked to one another but answered separately to local Japanese commands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A British official history notes that the Japanese at first issued arms only during drill and watched the officers lest they begin to think of themselves as an independent army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its peak in the middle of 1945 the force numbered about 120,000 armed men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nationalist leaders, Sukarno among them, used their influence over the choice of officers and their speeches to the units to turn the force against imperialism in general, not only against the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 14 February 1945 a battalion at Blitar in East Java, angered partly by the deaths of forced labourers working near it, rose against the Japanese and was persuaded to surrender after a few days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the 55 men later brought before a Japanese military court in Jakarta, six were sentenced to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 18 August 1945, the day after independence was proclaimed, the Japanese ordered the force disbanded, but in many parts of Java its units kept their arms and clashed with the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became the backbone of the Indonesian Republic's army, which met the returning Allies with Japanese weapons, while the Dutch insisted that the nationalist leaders were collaborators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 308–10, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","George McTurnan Kahin, <i>Nationalism and Revolution in Indonesia</i> (Ithaca, NY: Cornell University Press, 1952; repr. Cornell Southeast Asia Program, 2003), 109, 113–14, 135–37, https://archive.org/details/george-mc-turnan-kahin-1952-2003-nationalism-revolution-in-indonesia. [Open access]","Nugroho Notosusanto, “The Revolt of a PETA-Battalion in Blitar, February 14, 1945,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 1 (1969): 111–23, https://www.asj.upd.edu.ph/mediabox/archive/ASJ-07-01-1969/notosusanto-revolt%20peta-battalion%20blitar%20february%2014%201945.pdf. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Post Surrender Tasks: Section E of the Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1969), 289–90, https://archive.org/details/postsurrendertasks. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Acheson),” 10 October 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 853, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d853. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Japanese arm Indonesians?","a":"They wanted a local force to help defend Java against an Allied invasion, but they kept its battalions apart from one another and at first issued arms only for drill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a Japanese-trained army turn against Japan?","a":"Nationalist leaders shaped the choice of its officers and taught its men to oppose imperialism in general, and some soldiers were outraged by the treatment of the forced labourers they saw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Dutch distrust the Republic's army?","a":"The Dutch government held that the nationalist leaders were Japanese collaborators whose movement kept Japanese influence alive, and the Republic's forces were built largely from Japanese-trained units carrying Japanese weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Inauguration_of_PETA_troops_in_Jakarta%2C_Djawa_Baroe%2C_Vol._2%2C_Iss._2_%281944-01-15%29%2C_p18.jpg","title":"PETA troops in Jakarta","desc":"The inauguration of PETA troops in Jakarta, from the Japanese-sponsored magazine <i>Djawa Baroe</i> of 15 January 1944.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inauguration_of_PETA_troops_in_Jakarta,_Djawa_Baroe,_Vol._2,_Iss._2_(1944-01-15),_p18.jpg","alt":"Uniformed recruits march behind banners along a road lined with rows of soldiers in caps."},"wiki":"Defenders of the Homeland"},
    "ww2-537": {"questions":["Spain hastened to tell Washington that a courtesy cable to President Laurel implied no recognition of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, proclaimed in occupied Manila in October 1943.","Manuel Roxas held office in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while passing information on its workings to the guerrillas, and in 1946 he became president of an independent Philippines."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Philippine Republic</b> was the state that declared its independence in Manila on 14 October 1943 under Japanese occupation, with José P. Laurel as its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 31 May 1943 Japan's leaders had approved a plan whose main point was to hasten the independence of the Philippines and Burma, and the new republic at once concluded an alliance with Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution gave executive power to a president elected by a National Assembly, in which every provincial governor and city mayor sat alongside one elected delegate from each province and city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several clauses tied it to the conflict, freezing the rules for elections during the Greater East Asia War and promising a new constitution within a year of the war's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States called it a puppet government set up by Japan, and when Spain's foreign minister answered a cable from Laurel, Madrid hurried to assure Washington that its reply implied no recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> An agent sent by the exiled Commonwealth government reached Manila in 1943 to learn how the regime worked from Manuel Roxas, who served in it while keeping in touch with guerrilla leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As American forces returned, Laurel's cabinet moved to Baguio on 22 December 1944, taking with it officials such as Roxas, the speaker Benigno Aquino and the foreign minister Claro Recto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After liberation some 3,800 to 4,000 suspected collaborators were held, but an order of September 1945 allowed bail, and former guerrillas complained that collaborators had gained most from liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Laurel, taken to Japan and arrested there after the surrender, was handed over for trial at the request of President Roxas in July 1946 and later released on bail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The collaboration question divided the politics of the new republic, not least because Roxas, president of the new republic from 1946, had himself held office under Laurel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["1943 Constitution of the Republic of the Philippines, preamble, arts. 2–3, 11–12, The LawPhil Project, Arellano Law Foundation, https://lawphil.net/consti/cons1943.html. [Open access]","Foreign Histories Division, Headquarters United States Army Japan, <i>Japanese Monograph No. 45: History of Imperial General Headquarters, Army Section</i>, rev. ed. (Tokyo: Headquarters United States Army Japan, n.d.), 148, 366, 369, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/Monos/JM-45/index.html. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Note From the Spanish Government Regarding Message of Spanish Foreign Minister to the Philippine Puppet Government,” <i>Department of State Bulletin</i> 9, no. 229 (13 November 1943), https://archive.org/details/sim_department-of-state-bulletin_1943-11-13_9_229. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, 1994), 283, 300–301, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurTheCampaigns. [Open access]","“The Consul General at Manila (Steintorf) to the Secretary of State,” 5 September 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 911, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d911. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in the Philippines (McNutt) to the Secretary of State,” 17 July 1946, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1971), doc. 685, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v08/d685. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan grant the Philippines independence in 1943?","a":"As the war turned against it, Japan's leaders decided in May 1943 to hasten independence for the Philippines and Burma, and the new republic was bound to Japan by an alliance signed that autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the United States refuse to recognise it?","a":"Washington regarded it as a puppet government set up by Japan, so even a courtesy telegram from Spain's foreign minister to Laurel drew a formal Spanish assurance that no recognition was meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did collaboration become a bitter issue after the war?","a":"Thousands of suspects were jailed but those who could pay were bailed, former guerrillas felt cheated, and leading figures such as Roxas had served in Laurel's government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Laurel_inauguration%2C_1943.jpg","title":"Laurel's inauguration","desc":"The inauguration of José P. Laurel as president of the Second Philippine Republic on 14 October 1943.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurel_inauguration,_1943.jpg","alt":"A large crowd gathered before a building draped with flags for an outdoor ceremony."},"wiki":"Second Philippine Republic"},
    "ww2-538": {"questions":["At parades held across Malaya on 1 December 1945 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed in its weapons for gratuities, though its framework and the Communist Party behind it survived.","Malay attacks on Chinese followed when guerrillas of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seized police stations in the weeks between Japan's surrender and the return of British administration."],"abstract":"The <b>Malayan Peoples' Anti-Japanese Army</b> was the guerrilla force, controlled by the Malayan Communist Party and made up mainly of young Chinese, that resisted the Japanese occupation of Malaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in the weeks before the fall of Singapore, when Britain's Special Operations Executive trained a party of young Chinese communists, about 150 or 165 by different counts, and slipped them into the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Survivors of Dalforce, a Chinese irregular force raised from all classes and political opinions to help defend Singapore, joined them along with British stay-behind parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A political twin, the Malayan People's Anti-Japanese Union, organised civilian support, and the guerrillas enjoyed wide backing among the Chinese population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British lost contact with the guerrillas after the conquest, and once it was restored Force 136 supplied them with arms and ammunition by air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In early 1945 Mountbatten won approval to keep arming this predominantly left-wing movement, as he did the Anti-Fascist Organisation in Burma, despite warnings about internal security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Estimates of its strength in 1945 run from more than 3,000 in Mountbatten's report to some 7,000 in a later official history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British returned without a fight, and in places the guerrillas seized police stations and took the law into their own hands, provoking Malay attacks on Chinese that killed about 400 people in Johore in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At parades held on 1 December 1945 the guerrillas handed in their arms for gratuities, and by December some 6,000 men had been disbanded and 5,500 weapons surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The organisation's framework and the Communist Party's leadership survived disbandment, and a British official history judged that they caused much trouble to colonial rule in later years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 279–80, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Post Surrender Tasks: Section E of the Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1969), 300–302, https://archive.org/details/postsurrendertasks. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South-East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1951), 144, https://archive.org/details/reportsupremealliedcom1943-5. [Open access]","Hayashi Hirofumi, “British War Crimes Trials of Japanese,” <i>Nature-People-Society: Science and the Humanities</i>, no. 31 (Kanto Gakuin University, July 2001), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng08.htm. [Open access]","Hayashi Hirofumi, “Massacre of Chinese in Singapore and Its Coverage in Postwar Japan,” in <i>New Perspectives on the Japanese Occupation in Malaya and Singapore</i>, ed. Akashi Yoji and Yoshimura Mako (Singapore: NUS Press, 2008), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng18.htm. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 364, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British arm a communist guerrilla army?","a":"It was the main resistance movement in Malaya and could help an invasion, so Mountbatten secured the Chiefs of Staff's approval in 1945 to go on arming it despite warnings that it was left-wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were most of the guerrillas Chinese?","a":"The resistance grew out of Chinese volunteers who had fought for Singapore and then suffered Japanese reprisals, while the Japanese used a police force of Malays and Sikhs against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did disbandment not end the problem?","a":"The guerrillas handed in thousands of weapons, but the organisation's framework and the Communist Party's central leadership stayed in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/The_British_Reoccupation_of_Malaya_SE5878.jpg","title":"Disbandment at Johore Bahru","desc":"The 4th Regiment of the Malayan Peoples' Anti-Japanese Army marching past at its disbandment ceremony at Johore Bahru in 1945.","credit":"Sergeant W. A. Morris, No. 9 Army Film and Photographic Unit, Imperial War Museums SE 5878, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_British_Reoccupation_of_Malaya_SE5878.jpg","alt":"A column of uniformed guerrillas with rifles marching past officers taking the salute."},"wiki":"Malayan Peoples' Anti-Japanese Army"},
    "ww2-539": {"questions":["A Japanese garrison commander's diary counted 5,000 victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while a secret army record of May 1942 listed 11,110 missing from bombing and the purge.","Staff officer Tsuji Masanobu escaped trial for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Singapore, although a British court in 1947 sent a Kempeitai officer to prison for life."],"abstract":"The <b>Sook Ching</b>, a Chinese name meaning ‘purge through purification’, was the Japanese army's screening and mass killing of Chinese men in Singapore after the British surrender in February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 25th Army's military government had laid down a harsh policy towards the Chinese of Malaya and Singapore in late December 1941, weeks before its troops reached the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 18 February the army commander, Yamashita Tomoyuki, ordered the new garrison commander to carry out mopping-up operations, and the plan was drawn up with the military police, the Kempeitai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese men aged 18 to 50 were ordered to screening centres, and those judged anti-Japanese were taken by lorry to the coast or other isolated places and machine-gunned or bayoneted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The screening in the city was carried out mainly by the Kempeitai between 21 and 23 February, and in the suburbs by the Imperial Guards Division at the end of the month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The number killed is unsettled: the garrison commander's diary gives 5,000, a secret army record of May 1942 counted 11,110 missing from bombing and the purge, and some Japanese textbooks speak of tens of thousands across Singapore and Malaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese defenders later blamed the resistance of the Chinese volunteers of Dalforce, but that force was small and poorly armed, and the purge had been planned before the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many Dalforce survivors were caught and executed, and Mountbatten's report later recalled that the Chinese who fought to the last for Singapore had suffered retaliation and atrocities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal listed Singapore in February and March 1942 among the war's massacres, and a British court in Singapore in 1947 sent a Kempeitai officer involved to prison for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The staff officer Tsuji Masanobu, held to be at the centre of the killings of Chinese in Singapore and Malaya, escaped trial with help from the Chinese Nationalists and American intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Hayashi Hirofumi, “Massacre of Chinese in Singapore and Its Coverage in Postwar Japan,” in <i>New Perspectives on the Japanese Occupation in Malaya and Singapore</i>, ed. Akashi Yoji and Yoshimura Mako (Singapore: NUS Press, 2008), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng18.htm. [Open access]","Hayashi Hirofumi, “British War Crimes Trials of Japanese,” <i>Nature-People-Society: Science and the Humanities</i>, no. 31 (Kanto Gakuin University, July 2001), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng08.htm. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, 1 November 1948, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities),” 1032–33, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 279, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Post Surrender Tasks: Section E of the Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1969), 300, https://archive.org/details/postsurrendertasks. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Loss of Singapore</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 364, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Singapore's Chinese singled out?","a":"Japanese officers who had fought in China regarded the overseas Chinese as anti-Japanese from the start, and the 25th Army had settled on a harsh policy towards them before it reached Singapore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll still disputed?","a":"The Japanese records give 5,000, or 11,110 missing on unclear grounds, a Japanese Justice Ministry official later claimed only about 800, and evidence put before the trials had been manipulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did few of those responsible face trial?","a":"Tsuji escaped with help from the Chinese Nationalists and American intelligence, and the British courts prosecuted only some of the commanders and Kempeitai officers involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/Sook_Ching_Centre_site.JPG","title":"A Sook Ching screening centre","desc":"A memorial plaque in Singapore marking the site of one of the temporary registration centres used to screen Chinese men in February 1942.","credit":"Terence Ong, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sook_Ching_Centre_site.JPG","alt":"A metal plaque with text in several languages mounted beside a street."},"wiki":"Sook Ching"},
    "ww2-540": {"questions":["Sukarno read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outside his own house in Jakarta, and Indonesian staff of the Domei news agency broadcast it across the archipelago behind locked doors.","Told nothing by the Dutch, Mountbatten protested to Field Marshal Terauchi that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 17 August 1945 violated the surrender agreement signed at Rangoon."],"abstract":"The <b>Proclamation of Indonesian Independence</b> was the declaration read by Sukarno outside his house in Jakarta on the morning of 17 August 1945, 2 days after Japan's surrender, announcing an independent Indonesian republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had promised the East Indies independence at some future date in September 1944, and in July 1945 Tokyo decided that it should come as soon as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 11 August Field Marshal Terauchi summoned Sukarno and other nationalist leaders to Saigon and told them to form a committee to prepare for independence, which was to be proclaimed in September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When word of the surrender came, youth leaders who wanted a clean break with Japan kidnapped Sukarno and Hatta on 16 August and took them to a PETA garrison at Rengasdengklok to demand an immediate declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The text was drafted that night in the house of a Japanese naval officer, Vice-Admiral Maeda, and after the reading Indonesian staff of the Domei news agency broadcast it across the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese, bound by the surrender to act as agents of the Allies, tore down the posted announcements and the next day ordered PETA and other armed Indonesian groups to disband.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Instead many Japanese units handed over their arms, and by the time a British warship reached Batavia in mid-September the Republic had had a month to organise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mountbatten, told nothing of this by the Dutch, protested to Terauchi that the Republic violated the surrender agreement, while the Dutch government called its leaders collaborators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By October the British accepted that the Indonesians controlled Java and confined their troops to a few key points, and at the end of the month the Dutch offered internal self-government rather than independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Republic's prime minister, Sutan Sjahrir, appealed to President Truman against the British landing of Dutch troops, and after 4 years of fighting and talks the Netherlands transferred sovereignty on 27 December 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1969), 308–11, https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","George McTurnan Kahin, <i>Nationalism and Revolution in Indonesia</i> (Ithaca, NY: Cornell University Press, 1952; repr. Cornell Southeast Asia Program, 2003), 134–37, 231, https://archive.org/details/george-mc-turnan-kahin-1952-2003-nationalism-revolution-in-indonesia. [Open access]","Vice-Admiral the Earl Mountbatten of Burma, <i>Post Surrender Tasks: Section E of the Report to the Combined Chiefs of Staff by the Supreme Allied Commander, South East Asia, 1943–1945</i> (London: HMSO, 1969), 289–90, https://archive.org/details/postsurrendertasks. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Acheson),” 10 October 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 853, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d853. [Open access]","“Mr. Soetan Sjahrir to President Truman,” 25 December 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 872, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d872. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Chief of the Division of Southeast Asian Affairs (Moffat),” 18 October 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 855, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d855. [Open access]","“Memorandum of Conversation, by the Chief of the Division of Southeast Asian Affairs (Moffat),” 8 November 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), doc. 858, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d858. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sukarno and Hatta hesitate to declare independence?","a":"They doubted at first that Japan had really surrendered, believed the underground groups could not yet overthrow the Japanese, and feared a useless blood bath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the British caught unprepared in Java?","a":"Mountbatten had taken over the Netherlands East Indies without intelligence reports, and the Dutch had given him no reason to expect anything beyond rounding up the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Dutch at first refuse to accept the Republic?","a":"They regarded its leaders as collaborators whose movement kept Japanese influence alive, and they offered self-government within the Kingdom rather than independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/SukarnoBacaProklamasi.jpg","title":"Sukarno reads the proclamation","desc":"Sukarno reading the proclamation of independence in Jakarta on 17 August 1945, photographed by Frans Mendur.","credit":"Frans Mendur, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SukarnoBacaProklamasi.jpg","alt":"A man in a white suit stands at a microphone reading from a sheet of paper, with others standing behind him."},"wiki":"Proclamation of Indonesian Independence"},
    "ww2-591": {"questions":["Churchill admitted to the Commons that the Allies had cut it very fine indeed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after German counter-attacks in mid-September nearly broke the beachhead south of Salerno.","Baytown across the Strait of Messina, Slapstick at Taranto and the main assault south of Salerno were the three landings that made up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in September 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Allied invasion of Italy</b> was the series of landings on the Italian mainland in September 1943 that opened the Allied campaign there after the conquest of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began at 4:30 AM on 3 September, when the British Eighth Army crossed the Strait of Messina into Calabria in Operation Baytown against almost no resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same day an Italian envoy signed a short armistice at an Allied camp in Sicily, and when it was announced on 8 September the Germans moved at once to disarm their former allies and occupy the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The next day some 3,600 British airborne troops landed unopposed at the naval base of Taranto in Operation Slapstick, by prior agreement with the Italians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The main blow, Operation Avalanche, fell on the beaches south of Salerno, chosen because it was the farthest north the Allies could strike without losing fighter cover from Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> About 450 ships brought Mark Clark's Fifth Army, made up of equal numbers of American and British divisions, into the gulf in the early hours of 9 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German armoured divisions counter-attacked from 12 September, and on the evening of the 13th the line where the Sele and Calore rivers meet came so close to breaking that Clark had plans drawn up to take a corps off the beaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Naval gunfire, air attacks and the slow approach of the Eighth Army from the south persuaded the German commander, General von Vietinghoff, to ask on 16 September for leave to break off the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fight for the beachhead cost about 3,500 American and 5,500 British casualties against German losses of roughly 3,500, and Churchill admitted to the Commons that the Allies had cut it very fine indeed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The 82nd Airborne Division entered Naples on 1 October, the British held Foggia and its airfields by the same date, and the advance to the Volturno had cost more than 12,000 British and American casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Kenneth V. Smith, <i>Naples-Foggia</i>, CMH Pub 72-17, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), https://archive.org/details/Naples-Foggia. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Salerno to Cassino</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1993), 25–26, 52–53, 115–16, 133–35, 144, 165–70, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Salerno/index.html. [Open access]","“Armistice with Italy; September 3, 1943,” The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/italy01.asp. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2885–86, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War Situation,” House of Commons Debates, 21 September 1943, vol. 392, cc69–170, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/sep/21/war-situation. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 494, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-15.html. [Open access]","“Resistenza,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/resistenza_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies land at Salerno rather than nearer Rome?","a":"Because Salerno was the farthest north they could go and still cover the landing with single-engined fighters flying from Sicily, where the beaches lay barely within range. Churchill told the Commons that even there the fighters had only about a quarter of an hour over the battle area, and Alexander called fighter cover the decisive factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Salerno beachhead nearly lost?","a":"Because the Germans, having disarmed the Italian army, could turn armoured divisions on a landing that the Sele river split in two, and on 13 September their attacks came so close to breaking the line that Clark had plans drawn up to evacuate a corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans break off the battle?","a":"Because, in General von Vietinghoff's own report, attacks carried out with spirit had failed to reach their objectives owing to fire from naval guns and low-flying aircraft and the slow but steady approach of the Eighth Army, so on 16 September he asked to withdraw to defensive positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/HM_LST-2_Salerno_9_September_1943.jpg","title":"A tank landing ship at Salerno, 9 September 1943","desc":"HM LST-2 unloads American naval construction troops and British soldiers on the first day of the Salerno landings.","credit":"Royal Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HM_LST-2_Salerno_9_September_1943.jpg","alt":"A large landing ship with its bow doors open at the edge of a beach, with men and vehicles moving across the sand."},"wiki":"Allied invasion of Italy"},
    "ww2-592": {"questions":["Trying to cross the Rapido in January 1944, the American 36th Division lost 1,681 men in 2 days in the first round of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the road to Rome.","Kesselring called the defences a string of pearls, and only in May 1944, after three failed attacks, did the Allies win the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and open the Liri valley towards Rome."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Monte Cassino</b> was a series of four Allied offensives between January and May 1944 against the German Gustav Line, anchored on the massif that blocked the Liri valley, the quickest route to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Germans had made the line a zone of mutually supporting firing positions, a string of pearls in Kesselring's phrase, watched over from the heights crowned by the Benedictine abbey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first offensive failed in January, when the American 36th Division lost 1,681 men in 48 hours trying to cross the Rapido river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two more failed after the bombing of the abbey in February and of Cassino town in March, when more than 1,000 tons of bombs fell in 3.5 hours and still the New Zealand and Indian troops could not break through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the time Alexander halted the third battle on 23 March, some 600,000 shells had been fired, the New Zealanders had lost 1,316 men and the Indians 3,000, and the town and the mountain were still German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The fourth battle opened an hour before midnight on 11 May, when 1,660 Allied guns fired from Cassino to the sea, and 2 hours later the Polish 2nd Corps attacked the heights around the abbey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Poles were thrown back with heavy losses, but the French broke through in the mountains to the south, and by 17 May British and Polish pincers had made the German position untenable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That night Kesselring ordered the 1st Parachute Division out, and Allied codebreakers had deciphered the order within minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 10:30 AM the Poles raised their red and white flag over the ruins of the monastery, as British troops finished clearing the town below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons on 6 June that Polish, British Empire, French and American forces had all broken the German lines in front of them, and the last battle, from 11 May to the capture of Rome, cost the Allies over 43,000 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Martin Blumenson, <i>Salerno to Cassino</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1993), 312, 346, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Salerno/index.html. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 17, 43–77, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","Clayton D. Laurie, <i>Rome-Arno</i>, CMH Pub 72-20, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), https://archive.org/details/Rome-Arno. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 366–68, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-10.html. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2908, 2924, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War and International Situation,” House of Commons Debates, 22 February 1944, vol. 397, cc686–88, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","“Montecassino,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/montecassino/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “Liberation of Rome: Landings in France,” House of Commons Debates, 6 June 1944, vol. 400, cc1207–11, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jun/06/liberation-of-rome-landings-in-france. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Cassino so hard to take?","a":"Because the Gustav Line was a deep belt of mutually supporting positions built round the stone houses of the region, and the heights above the town gave the Germans almost perfect observation of the plain. Alexander noted that the Italian General Staff had used the mountain as its example of an impregnable position, and in February he told Churchill the fighting there surpassed all his previous experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did bombing the town in March not open the way?","a":"Because the infantry moved in too slowly behind the bombs, giving the defenders time to climb out of cellars, dugouts and a tunnel and fight from the ruins, while rain turned the craters into ponds that stopped the tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fourth battle succeed where the others failed?","a":"Because it was a combined offensive by two armies along the whole front, opened by 1,660 guns, and French and British gains on either side of the mountain left the paratroopers on Monte Cassino in danger of being cut off, so Kesselring ordered them out on 17 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Infantry_of_the_East_Surrey_Regiment_enter_the_ruins_of_Cassino%2C_Italy%2C_18_May_1944._NA14989.jpg","title":"Entering the ruins of Cassino, 18 May 1944","desc":"Infantry of the East Surrey Regiment move into the destroyed town on the day the battle ended.","credit":"Sgt Bowman, No 2 Army Film & Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Infantry_of_the_East_Surrey_Regiment_enter_the_ruins_of_Cassino,_Italy,_18_May_1944._NA14989.jpg","alt":"British soldiers with rifles walking in single file past heaps of rubble and shattered walls."},"wiki":"Battle of Monte Cassino"},
    "ww2-593": {"questions":["On 15 February 1944 Allied bombers dropped almost 600 tons of high explosive on the Benedictine abbey of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose ruins German paratroopers then turned into a strongpoint.","Sacked by the Lombards and later by the Saracens, the abbey of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had its archive carried to safety before Allied bombers reduced it to rubble in February 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Monte Cassino</b> is a rocky hill about 516 metres (1,690 feet) high above the town of Cassino, between Rome and Naples, crowned by the Benedictine abbey that Saint Benedict founded around 529 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gregory the Great's <i>Dialogues</i> tell how the saint smashed the idol in an old temple of Apollo on the summit and built oratories to Saint Martin and Saint John in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Benedict wrote his Rule and was buried there, and Gregory records his prophecy that the house would be destroyed, fulfilled when the Lombards sacked it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rebuilt in 717 and again after the Saracens wrecked it in the 9th century, the abbey flourished under Abbot Desiderius in the 11th century and was largely rebuilt from 1512, its basilica being consecrated in 1727.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Declared a national monument in 1866, it holds a library and archive of some 2,000 manuscripts and 40,000 parchments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the winter of 1943 to 1944 it stood above the German Gustav Line, and at German officers' suggestion most monks and many treasures went to Rome in German trucks, leaving the abbot, a few monks and about 150 civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Allies had spared it during the American attacks, but believing that the Germans were using it they gave way to the New Zealand Corps commander, Bernard Freyberg, who insisted on its destruction, and on 15 February 1944 bombers dropped almost 600 tons of explosive on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In an interview filmed by the Germans for propaganda the abbot said hundreds of innocent people had been killed, and a civilian who reached the American lines reported that no Germans had been inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German troops then occupied the ruins, which Alexander conceded were probably more valuable to them than the intact building, and held them until Kesselring withdrew his paratroopers in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The official American air history calls German use of the abbey one of the most controversial questions of the war, and the monastery was rebuilt with help from the Italian government and private American donors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Gregory the Great, <i>Dialogues</i> 2.8 and 2.17, trans. “P. W.” (1608), ed. Edmund G. Gardner (1911), Internet Medieval Sourcebook, Fordham University, https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/g1-benedict1.asp. [Open access]","“Montecassino,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/montecassino/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Salerno to Cassino</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1993), 398–99, 410–16, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Salerno/index.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 363–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-10.html. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2908, 2914, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879. [Open access]","Clayton D. Laurie, <i>Rome-Arno</i>, CMH Pub 72-20, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), https://archive.org/details/Rome-Arno. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 77, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Benedict's abbey stand on a mountaintop?","a":"Because the site was strong and hard to reach, as Alexander noted when he wrote that Benedict and the Italian General Staff had both chosen it for that reason, and because, as Gregory tells it, the summit held a pagan temple whose worshippers Benedict set out to convert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Allies bomb the abbey?","a":"Because their commanders believed it was part of the German defences, and Freyberg, whose New Zealand Corps was to storm the heights, insisted that its destruction was a military necessity, overriding Clark's objections, until Wilson ordered the air forces to destroy it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombing fail to help the attack?","a":"Because the ground troops could not exploit it, and the Germans occupied the rubble, which made better defensive positions than the building; the American army historian concluded that it achieved nothing beyond destruction, indignation, sorrow and regret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/The_ruined_monastery_at_Cassino%2C_Italy%2C_19_May_1944._NA15141.jpg","title":"The ruined monastery, 19 May 1944","desc":"The shell of the abbey of Monte Cassino the day after its capture by the Polish 2nd Corps, with the one wall that survived the February bombing.","credit":"Sgt McConville, No 2 Army Film & Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_ruined_monastery_at_Cassino,_Italy,_19_May_1944._NA15141.jpg","alt":"A broken stone wall with empty window openings standing above heaps of rubble on a hilltop."},"wiki":"Monte Cassino"},
    "ww2-594": {"questions":["A German railway gun the troops called Anzio Annie shelled the beachhead from the Alban Hills during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Churchill had pressed for to outflank the Gustav Line.","John Lucas was replaced by Lucian Truscott in February 1944 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after German counter-attacks down the Albano road tried to drive VI Corps into the sea."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Anzio</b> was the 4-month struggle that followed the Allied amphibious landing, Operation Shingle, at Anzio and Nettuno on the coast south of Rome on 22 January 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meant to outflank the Gustav Line and speed the capture of Rome, it depended on landing ships that Churchill persuaded Roosevelt to keep in the Mediterranean a few weeks longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The landing achieved almost complete surprise, and by midnight more than 36,000 men and 3,200 vehicles were ashore for the loss of 13 killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The corps commander, John P. Lucas, erred on the side of caution, and the Germans, at first surprised, brought in reserves from as far away as France and Yugoslavia and sealed off the beachhead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 12 February an estimated 120,000 Germans surrounded a perimeter of about 56 kilometres (35 miles) held by 96,401 Allied soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The main German counter-offensive began on 16 February down the Albano road and in 5 days cost the attackers 5,389 men and the Allies some 3,496.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lucian Truscott replaced Lucas on 23 February, and a second German offensive, begun on 29 February against the Cisterna sector, was over by 4 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For 3 months the beachhead resembled a trench front of the First World War, with most casualties caused by shelling and bombing, including fire from a German railway gun the troops called Anzio Annie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The breakout began on 23 May and met troops from the south on 25 May, but Mark Clark sent most of his strength north towards Rome, and the thrust towards Valmontone was too weak to stop the German Tenth Army escaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> VI Corps suffered over 29,200 combat casualties against an estimated 27,500 German, and while Churchill claimed on 6 June that the landing had borne good fruit, the US Army's campaign history judges that it failed in its immediate aims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Clayton D. Laurie, <i>Anzio</i>, CMH Pub 72-19, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), https://archive.org/details/Anzio. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Salerno to Cassino</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1993), 294–98, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Salerno/index.html. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 163–65, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2914, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War and International Situation,” House of Commons Debates, 22 February 1944, vol. 397, cc686–88, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","“Anzio,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/anzio/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “Liberation of Rome: Landings in France,” House of Commons Debates, 6 June 1944, vol. 400, cc1207–11, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jun/06/liberation-of-rome-landings-in-france. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies land at Anzio?","a":"Because the Gustav Line could not be broken from the front, and a landing behind it was meant to outflank it, restore movement to the campaign and speed the capture of Rome. It was possible only because Churchill won Roosevelt's approval to keep the landing ships in the Mediterranean for a few more weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the beachhead not break out at once?","a":"Because Lucas built up his force instead of striking for the almost undefended Alban Hills, while the Germans moved reserves from France, northern Italy and Yugoslavia so fast that by 12 February about 120,000 men ringed the perimeter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the breakout still argued over?","a":"Because after the link-up on 25 May Clark sent most of his strength towards Rome, and the force he left heading for Valmontone proved insufficient, so most of the German Tenth Army escaped north to fight again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/A_Sherman_tank_of_23rd_Armoured_Brigade_coming_ashore_from_a_landing_craft_at_Anzio%2C_Italy%2C_22_January_1944._NA11035.jpg","title":"Coming ashore at Anzio, 22 January 1944","desc":"A Sherman tank of the British 23rd Armoured Brigade leaves a landing craft on the first day of the landings.","credit":"Sgt Dawson, No 2 Army Film & Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Sherman_tank_of_23rd_Armoured_Brigade_coming_ashore_from_a_landing_craft_at_Anzio,_Italy,_22_January_1944._NA11035.jpg","alt":"A tank driving down the ramp of a landing craft into shallow water near a beach."},"wiki":"Battle of Anzio"},
    "ww2-595": {"questions":["Mark Clark turned his breakout from Anzio away from Valmontone in late May 1944 to win the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for his Fifth Army, a choice some historians say let German troops escape.","Hitler forbade the demolition of the Tiber bridges and the Germans withdrew without a fight, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 4 June 1944 left the city's monuments standing 2 days before D-Day."],"abstract":"The <b>liberation of Rome</b> was the entry of the American Fifth Army into the Italian capital on 4 and 5 June 1944, the first Axis capital to fall to the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German commanders had long debated whether to defend the city or give it up, and Hitler forbade the destruction of its historic bridges over the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The occupation had brought reprisals such as the shooting of 335 people in the Ardeatine Caves on 24 March 1944, ten for every German policeman killed in a partisan bomb attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the May offensive broke the Gustav Line, Alexander meant the troops breaking out of Anzio to drive on Valmontone and trap the German Tenth Army, but Mark Clark, wanting Americans to liberate Rome, turned most of them north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some historians argue that this let much of the German army escape, though Alexander's despatch records the change without complaint, quoting Clark's order for a new attack along the most direct route to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 3 June the German high command ordered Kesselring to withdraw beyond the Tiber, treating Rome as an open city in what the American army historian calls an anticipatory surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leaflets on the morning of 4 June told Romans to stay calm, and that day mobile American columns pushed through the suburbs to seize the Tiber bridges intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crowds filled the streets on 5 June to greet the main body of the Fifth Army, though the first troops had passed through in darkness and near silence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That evening Roosevelt called it one up and two to go while warning against inflating its military importance, and Churchill told the Commons on 6 June that Alexander's prime object had never been the liberation of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Since 11 May the drive had cost the Fifth Army 17,931 American casualties, and the Normandy landings 2 days after the city fell soon overshadowed it, to the detriment, Alexander complained, of exploiting his victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 202–16, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","Clayton D. Laurie, <i>Rome-Arno</i>, CMH Pub 72-20, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), https://archive.org/details/Rome-Arno. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” 5 June 1944, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-2. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2927–29, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 9–10, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","Amedeo Osti Guerrazzi, “Fosse Ardeatine, Roma, 24.03.1944,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=2127. [in Italian] [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “Liberation of Rome: Landings in France,” House of Commons Debates, 6 June 1944, vol. 400, cc1207–11, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jun/06/liberation-of-rome-landings-in-france. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome escape the destruction of Naples?","a":"Because the Germans chose to withdraw rather than fight in the streets. Hitler had forbidden the destruction of the Tiber bridges and declared that Rome, as a place of culture, must not become a scene of combat, though Roosevelt insisted that no thanks were due to the Germans for sparing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Clark head for Rome instead of Valmontone?","a":"Because he believed the trap at Valmontone could not be closed against the German troops massed there, feared the Caesar Line would stop VI Corps, and wanted Americans to liberate Rome, so he turned most of the corps north without consulting Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the liberation matter less than it seemed?","a":"Because the German armies south of Rome were not destroyed, and 2 days later the Normandy landings overshadowed Italy; Roosevelt himself warned that it would be unwise to inflate the military importance of taking the city, and Churchill said destroying the enemy army had been the single aim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Entry_of_Allied_Troops_Into_Rome%2C_5_June_1944_TR1840.jpg","title":"Allied troops in Rome, June 1944","desc":"Allied soldiers climb the steps of Saint Peter's during the entry into Rome.","credit":"Capt Tanner, War Office official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entry_of_Allied_Troops_Into_Rome,_5_June_1944_TR1840.jpg","alt":"Soldiers in uniform walking up a broad flight of stone steps towards a large church facade."},"wiki":"Liberation of Rome"},
    "ww2-596": {"questions":["Hitler had the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> renamed in June 1944, fearing that the Allies would trumpet the breaching of so grand a name, and Kesselring called it the Green Line instead.","Operation Olive opened the Eighth Army's assault on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 25 August 1944, but Rimini fell only on 21 September and the offensive stalled short of Bologna."],"abstract":"The <b>Gothic Line</b> was the German defensive zone across the Northern Apennines, running some 290 kilometres (180 miles) from near Massa on the Ligurian coast to Pesaro on the Adriatic and guarding the Po plain and the industrial north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rommel had begun reconnoitring it in August 1943, but serious work started only in the spring of 1944, when the Todt Organisation set several thousand Italian civilians to building it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In June 1944 Hitler, fearing the Allies would make propaganda out of breaking a line with so grand a name, had it renamed, and Kesselring called it the Green Line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not due to be finished until December, and inspections in July and August found its mines and anti-tank works far from complete, even north of Florence and on the Adriatic flank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Germans emptied a dead zone 20 kilometres (12 miles) deep in front of it, and behind it Kesselring's orders against the partisans fell on civilians too, as at Sant'Anna di Stazzema, where 392 people were killed on 12 August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Allies attacked after losing seven divisions, three American and four French, to the invasion of southern France, and the Eighth Army opened Operation Olive on the Adriatic flank on 25 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polish and Canadian troops broke into the line near Pesaro by 30 August, but German reserves plugged the breach, and Rimini fell only on 21 September after an advance of about 50 kilometres (30 miles) in 26 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the centre the American Fifth Army took the Il Giogo pass between 12 and 18 September, outflanking the Futa pass for more than 2,730 casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between 10 September and 26 October its II Corps lost over 15,000 men and the Eighth Army nearly 14,000, and the offensive halted short of Bologna as winter set in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander wrote that the line was meant to be held to the last to preserve the rich lands of northern Italy, and the Allies broke out into the Po valley only in April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 242, 299–302, 465–66, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","Dwight D. Oland, <i>North Apennines</i>, CMH Pub 72-34, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1996), https://archive.org/details/NorthApennines-nsia. [Open access]","Harold Alexander, “The Allied Armies in Italy from 3rd September, 1943, to 12th December, 1944,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38937, 12 June 1950, 2930, 2940, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38937/supplement/2879. [Open access]","Paolo Pezzino, “Sant’Anna di Stazzema 12.08.1944,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=4908. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Il progetto,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=9. [in Italian] [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 10–11, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","Clayton D. Laurie, <i>Rome-Arno</i>, CMH Pub 72-20, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1994), https://archive.org/details/Rome-Arno. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans build a line in the Northern Apennines?","a":"Because there the mountains swing across the peninsula and form what Alexander called a first-class strategic obstacle in front of the Po plain, and he wrote that the line was meant to be held to the last to preserve the rich lands of northern Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler change its name?","a":"Because he reasoned that if the Allies broke through they would seize on the more pretentious, historic name to magnify their victory, so he ordered it changed and Kesselring renamed it the Green Line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the autumn offensive stall?","a":"Because the invasion of southern France had taken seven divisions, German reserves sealed each breach, and rain, rivers and ridges wore the attackers down until II Corps and the Eighth Army had lost nearly 30,000 men in about 7 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/46/Fortifications_of_the_Gothic_line_in_the_Apennines_used_against_Fifth_Army%2C_November%2C_1944._%2849699159142%29.jpg","title":"Gothic Line fortifications, November 1944","desc":"German defensive works in the Apennines used against the American Fifth Army.","credit":"US Army Signal Corps, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fortifications_of_the_Gothic_line_in_the_Apennines_used_against_Fifth_Army,_November,_1944._(49699159142).jpg","alt":"A fortified position dug into a hillside in mountainous country."},"wiki":"Gothic Line"},
    "ww2-597": {"questions":["Some 130,000 men had joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the late summer of 1944, but winter and German counter-measures cut its armed bands to about 50,000 by the spring of 1945.","Ravenna in December 1944 and Milan in April 1945 had already been freed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before Allied troops arrived, after Allied headquarters called for a general rising in the north."],"abstract":"The <b>Italian resistance movement</b> was the armed and civil opposition to the German occupation and to Mussolini's Italian Social Republic that grew up, mainly in central and northern Italy, after the armistice of 8 September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was led by the Committee of National Liberation, which joined the revived anti-fascist parties and monarchist currents, and its formations ranged from the communist Garibaldi Brigades to the Action Party's Justice and Freedom brigades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of its strength came from young men called up by the Social Republic who chose the partisan brigades instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Partisans fought in the mountains and the cities through sabotage, attacks, guerrilla warfare, strikes and a clandestine press, and briefly held territory as partisan republics in the Ossola and at Alba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their numbers rose and fell with the front, from an estimated 130,000 in late summer 1944 to about 50,000 in spring 1945, sustained by some 2,400 tons of Allied supplies and about 60 Allied mission teams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1944 Keitel gave Kesselring command of the fight against the partisans, by then a serious menace to German forces, and Kesselring's orders promised protection to commanders who exceeded the usual restraint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The violence fell on partisans and civilians alike, from fifteen political prisoners shot in Milan in August 1944 to the more than 5,000 episodes of killing of unarmed people recorded across Italy by a national survey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From late 1944 partisans fought more openly beside the Allies, and at Ravenna, freed by a partisan rising, the 900-strong Communist-led 28th Garibaldi Brigade joined the Eighth Army's ranks in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In April 1945 Allied headquarters called for a general uprising, and town after town in the north, Milan among them, was in partisan hands before the Allied armies arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The movement was split between moderates who wanted to restore the order that preceded fascism and a left that hoped to refound the state, but it marked a sharp break with fascism and helped found the republic of 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“Resistenza,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/resistenza_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 402–3, 458, 505, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 10–11, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","“Il progetto,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=9. [in Italian] [Open access]","Thomas A. Popa, <i>Po Valley</i>, CMH Pub 72-33, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), https://archive.org/details/PoValley. [Open access]","Giovanni Scirocco and Luigi Borgomaneri, “Piazzale Loreto, Milano, 10.08.1944,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=1623. [in Italian] [Open access]","Emilio Gentile, “Mussolini, Benito,” in <i>Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani</i>, vol. 77 (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2012), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/benito-mussolini_(Dizionario-Biografico)/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many young men join the partisans?","a":"Because the Social Republic called up their age groups, and many chose the partisan brigades rather than serve it; numbers swelled further in the late summer of 1944, when an ill-founded hope of early liberation drew an estimated 130,000 men to the bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the partisan bands shrink in the winter of 1944 to 1945?","a":"Because the Allied offensive bogged down short of the Po, and discouragement and vigorous German counter-action reduced the bands to a hard core of about 50,000 by the spring, ready to expand if the Germans retreated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the resistance also described as a civil war?","a":"Because it fought not only the German occupier but also the Italians of the Social Republic who collaborated with it, and in that sense, as the Italian historical dictionary puts it, it also took on the nature of a civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/An_Italian_partisan_in_Florence%2C_14_August_1944._TR2282.jpg","title":"A partisan in Florence, 14 August 1944","desc":"An Italian partisan in Florence, where partisans exchanged fire with German snipers left behind after the German withdrawal.","credit":"Capt Tanner, War Office official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Italian_partisan_in_Florence,_14_August_1944._TR2282.jpg","alt":"A man in civilian clothes holding a rifle in a city street."},"wiki":"Italian resistance movement"},
    "ww2-598": {"questions":["Black Brigades of fascist party members were raised in 1944 to hunt partisans during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which Italians killed Italians across the German-occupied north.","Extraordinary assize courts set up in April 1945 tried Italians accused of collaborating with the German invader after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until the amnesty of June 1946 cut their work short."],"abstract":"The <b>Italian Civil War</b> is the name given to the fighting between Italians in German-occupied Italy from September 1943 to April 1945, when the resistance fought Mussolini's Italian Social Republic as well as the German occupiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mussolini announced the Social Republic in a broadcast from Munich on 18 September 1943, giving its refounded Republican Fascist Party the leading role in a state whose German ally acted as if its authorities did not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It raised a National Republican Guard for policing, special tribunals for the traitors of 25 July and, in 1944, the Black Brigades, armed units of party members created to fight the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Verona in January 1944 it tried the fascist leaders who had voted against Mussolini, and five of them, Galeazzo Ciano among them, were shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Repressing the resistance absorbed much of its activity, and its militias killed on their own account, as when the Guard and the Muti Legion shot fifteen political prisoners in Milan's Piazzale Loreto on 10 August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The resistance was above all a war of liberation from the German invader, but because it also fought Italians who collaborated with the occupier, it took on the nature of a civil war as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even Mussolini complained to Kesselring of German atrocities so revolting that they drove peaceful Italians to the partisans, as Kesselring's order of 24 September 1944 records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Memory of the violence stayed divided for decades, and the national survey of massacres notes that many local memories blamed the partisans as well as the killers for provoking reprisals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the war ended in April 1945 the government created extraordinary assize courts, with jurors drawn from lists compiled by the liberation committees, to try Italians accused of collaborating with the invader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their work was cut back by the amnesty of 22 June 1946, and the purge of fascist officials, first conceived as a radical renewal of public life, dwindled to minimal proportions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["“Resistenza,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/resistenza_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Repubblica sociale italiana,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/repubblica-sociale-italiana_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Brigate nere,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/brigate-nere/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Giovanni Scirocco and Luigi Borgomaneri, “Piazzale Loreto, Milano, 10.08.1944,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=1623. [in Italian] [Open access]","“La memoria,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=131. [in Italian] [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 11, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","“Corti d’Assise Straordinarie,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=139. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Epurazione,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/epurazione/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this fighting called a civil war?","a":"Because the resistance, though above all a war of liberation from the German invader, also fought the Italians of the Social Republic who collaborated with the occupier, so Italians fought and killed Italians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Social Republic create the Black Brigades?","a":"Because repressing the resistance absorbed much of its activity, and in 1944 it raised armed units from the members of its own Republican Fascist Party to fight the partisans alongside the National Republican Guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the reckoning with fascism after 1945 fall short?","a":"Because the special courts that tried collaborators worked only until the end of 1947 and were curtailed by the amnesty of June 1946, while the purge of officials was gradually scaled down to minimal proportions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Alessandro_Pavolini_e_Vincenzo_Costa_passano_in_rassegna_gli_squadristi_delle_Brigate_bere_della_Resega_estate_1944.jpg","title":"Black Brigades in Milan, 1944","desc":"The fascist party secretary Alessandro Pavolini and Vincenzo Costa review men of the Resega Black Brigade.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alessandro_Pavolini_e_Vincenzo_Costa_passano_in_rassegna_gli_squadristi_delle_Brigate_bere_della_Resega_estate_1944.jpg","alt":"Two men in dark uniforms walking past a line of armed militiamen standing to attention."},"wiki":"Italian Civil War"},
    "ww2-599": {"questions":["Partisans of the Red Star brigade had their bases in the farms south of Bologna that SS troops swept in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an operation that killed people in 115 separate places.","Files on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and other German crimes lay shelved in a Rome office from 1960 to 1994, and only in 2007 did a court at La Spezia convict ten former SS men."],"abstract":"The <b>Marzabotto massacre</b> is the usual name for the killing of some 770 people by German troops between 29 September and 5 October 1944 on and around Monte Sole, in the hills south of Bologna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was one of more than 5,000 episodes in a national survey of killings of unarmed civilians and partisans by German units and the forces of the Italian Social Republic between 1943 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The survey links that violence to orders that legitimised it, to campaigns against partisans and opponents, to round-ups for forced labour and to clearing ground near defensive lines and lines of retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In June 1944 Kesselring ordered partisans fought with the utmost severity and promised to protect any commander who exceeded the usual restraint, and in July he ordered villages that fired on troops burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A British court at Venice sentenced him to death in 1947 for inciting such reprisals, a sentence commuted to life imprisonment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Monte Sole had become part of the front in the Allied autumn offensive against the Gothic Line, and the Red Star partisan brigade of Mario Musolesi, known as Lupo, was based in its farms and hamlets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Troops of the 16th SS Panzergrenadier Division, above all Walter Reder's reconnaissance battalion, swept the area in an operation planned at divisional level to destroy the conditions that let the partisans exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People were killed in 115 separate places in the communes of Marzabotto, Grizzana and Monzuno, most of them women and children, 115 of them aged eleven or younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The national survey therefore prefers to speak of the Monte Sole massacre, since this was no single punitive raid on the town of Marzabotto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reder was sentenced to life by a military court in Bologna in 1951, and in 2007 a court at La Spezia sentenced ten former SS men to life, after files on such crimes had lain shelved in Rome from 1960 until 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Paolo Pezzino, “Monte Sole (scheda generale),” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=5705. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Marzabotto,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/marzabotto/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Il progetto,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=9. [in Italian] [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 10–11, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","“Tribunali militari,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=137. [in Italian] [Open access]","Dwight D. Oland, <i>North Apennines</i>, CMH Pub 72-34, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1996), https://archive.org/details/NorthApennines-nsia. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many of the victims women and children?","a":"Because the operation was aimed not at beating the partisan brigade in battle but at destroying the human and physical surroundings that sustained it, so the troops killed whoever they found in the farms and hamlets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German commanders feel free to act this way?","a":"Because Kesselring's orders of June and July 1944 promised protection to commanders who exceeded the usual restraint and ordered men shot and villages burned in reprisal, and the prosecution at his trial argued that such orders and the atrocities that followed were cause and effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did most trials come so late?","a":"Because after a first round of trials that ended in the early 1950s, 695 investigation files on Nazi and fascist crimes were improperly shelved in 1960 at the military prosecutor's office in Rome and found only in 1994, so most later defendants were tried in their absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Rovine_di_un%27abitazione%2C_Montesole.07-05-2024.jpg","title":"Ruins on Monte Sole","desc":"The remains of a house at Caprara di Sopra on Monte Sole, destroyed in the Second World War.","credit":"Protein.tree, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rovine_di_un%27abitazione,_Montesole.07-05-2024.jpg","alt":"Broken stone walls of a ruined farmhouse among trees on a hillside."},"wiki":"Marzabotto massacre"},
    "ww2-600": {"questions":["Fascist militiamen had shot fifteen political prisoners in Milan's Piazzale Loreto in August 1944, the square where bodies hung by the feet after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in April 1945.","Who ordered the immediate killing and who fired the shots are still disputed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which came near Lake Como on 28 April 1945 as partisan risings swept northern Italy."],"abstract":"The <b>death of Benito Mussolini</b> came on 28 April 1945, when Italian partisans shot the former dictator and his companion Claretta Petacci at Giulino di Mezzegra, near Lake Como, a day after capturing him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since 1943 he had headed the Italian Social Republic, a fascist state in the German-occupied north that stayed subordinate to its ally and ended with the insurrection of April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As the Allies broke into the Po valley, Allied headquarters called for a general uprising, and the resistance freed the great northern cities, in some cases before the Allied armies arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After trying in vain to negotiate a surrender with the resistance, Mussolini fled north, and on 27 April partisans caught him hidden in a German lorry bound for the Valtellina, dressed as a German soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One Italian reference work says he was shot on the orders of the Committee of National Liberation, but who wanted the immediate killing, how it was done and who actually fired remain disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 29 April his body, Petacci's and those of other executed fascist leaders were taken to Piazzale Loreto in Milan, abused by the crowd and hung by the feet from a petrol station.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In that square on 10 August 1944 fascist militiamen had shot fifteen political prisoners in a German reprisal and left them on show as a warning, a killing for which an SS captain, Theo Saevecke, was later sentenced to life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> American troops sent across Lake Garda on 30 April to look for Mussolini found the report false and soon learned that partisans had executed him 2 days before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The historical dictionary calls the display a symbol of the end of fascism, while his entry in the national biographical dictionary describes the crowd applauding as the bodies hung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After various wanderings, partly meant to stop them becoming an object of cult, his remains were returned to his family in 1957 and buried at Predappio, where he was born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Emilio Gentile, “Mussolini, Benito,” in <i>Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani</i>, vol. 77 (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2012), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/benito-mussolini_(Dizionario-Biografico)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Mussolini, Benito,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/benito-mussolini_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Thomas A. Popa, <i>Po Valley</i>, CMH Pub 72-33, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II (Washington, DC: U.S. Army Center of Military History, 1992), https://archive.org/details/PoValley. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 505, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","Giovanni Scirocco and Luigi Borgomaneri, “Piazzale Loreto, Milano, 10.08.1944,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=1623. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Repubblica sociale italiana,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/repubblica-sociale-italiana_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Resistenza,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/resistenza_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Tribunali militari,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=137. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Mussolini near Lake Como when he was caught?","a":"Because after failing to negotiate a surrender with the resistance he fled north towards the Valtellina, hidden in a German lorry and dressed as a German soldier, while partisan risings were taking over the towns of Lombardy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his death still argued over?","a":"Because accounts of his last days differ, and who wanted him killed at once, how the killing was done and who actually fired are still matters of rival hypotheses and interpretations, even though one reference work attributes the order to the Committee of National Liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the bodies displayed at Piazzale Loreto?","a":"Because the display was meant as a symbol of the end of fascism, in a square where fascist militiamen had shot fifteen political prisoners in August 1944 and left their bodies on show as a warning to the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Biglietto_di_Mussolini_a_Dongo.jpg","title":"Mussolini's note from Dongo, 27 April 1945","desc":"A handwritten note Mussolini wrote after his arrest at Dongo, saying the 52nd Garibaldi Brigade had captured him and treated him correctly.","credit":"Benito Mussolini, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biglietto_di_Mussolini_a_Dongo.jpg","alt":"A small sheet of paper with a few lines of handwriting in ink and a signature."},"wiki":"Death of Benito Mussolini"},
    "ww2-501": {"questions":["On the Burma–Siam railway the Asian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outnumbered Allied prisoners of war about three to one, and the Tokyo tribunal found that at least 60,000 of them died.","Indonesian textbooks made the Japanese word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, meaning ‘manual labourers’, the leading symbol of the occupation, glossing it as ‘forced labourers’ or ‘soldiers of labour’."],"abstract":"<b>Rōmusha</b>, a Japanese word for ‘manual labourers’, was the name given to the local people whom Japan recruited, at first as volunteers and increasingly by force, to work for its war effort in occupied South East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese planners expected Java, home to some seventy million people, to supply most of this labour, and the word came to mean above all the forced labourers taken from Java.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Quotas were passed down through village headmen, labour bureaus and companies, and under the general mobilisation each family had to provide at least one person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 200,000 and 300,000 labourers were sent from Java to other occupied territories, more than 40 per cent of them to Sumatra, where many were put to work on the railway to Pekanbaru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They also died at sea: when the Junior Maru was sunk off Sumatra in September 1944 it was carrying large numbers of conscripted Indonesian labourers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known case is the Burma–Siam railway, built between July 1942 and October 1943, on which Asian labourers outnumbered the Allied prisoners of war by roughly three to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counts differ: the Tokyo tribunal found about 150,000 such labourers on the railway, of whom at least 60,000 died, while the Australian War Memorial gives some 200,000 with perhaps 75,000 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among them were 92,000 Burmese and 78,204 people from Malaya, mostly Malays and Tamils, and about 41 per cent of the 70,000 Malayans sent in 1943 died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal judged their treatment, if anything, worse than that of the prisoners, and recruitment turned to deception and coercion once deserters carried home word of the conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Indonesian history textbooks of the 1980s and 1990s the word became the leading symbol of the occupation, glossed as forced labourers, soldiers of labour or even heroes of labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Takuma Melber, “The Labour Recruitment of Local Inhabitants as <i>Rōmusha</i> in Japanese-Occupied South East Asia,” <i>International Review of Social History</i> 61, no. S24 (2016): 165–85, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0020859016000390. [Open access]","Masanori Sato, “A Study on ‘Romusha’ in History Textbooks in Indonesia,” <i>Japanese Journal of Southeast Asian Studies</i> 32, no. 4 (1995): 495–522, abstract, https://doi.org/10.20495/tak.32.4_495. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chapter VIII, \"Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)\" (Tokyo, 1948), 1054, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, \"Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – The Burma–Thailand Railway,\" online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan/burmathai. [Open access]","Fikrul Hanif Sufyan, Zusneli Zubir, Refisrul and Rita Novita, “<i>Dai Nippon</i> Forced Labor Politics, 1943–1945: <i>Romusha</i> Rendezvous at Muaro Sijunjung Lane, Sijunjung Regency,” <i>Paramita: Historical Studies Journal</i> 35, no. 1 (2025): 97–101, https://doi.org/10.15294/paramita.v35i1.12789. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chapter VIII, \"Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)\" (Tokyo, 1948), 1069–70, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan turn to forced labour in South East Asia?","a":"Because from 1942 its war situation and economy worsened, and it treated the region as a source of war resources, the labour of its people included, for railways, fortifications and production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the death tolls so uncertain?","a":"Because records were incomplete and different bodies counted differently: the tribunal and the Australian War Memorial give different totals for the railway alone, and estimates for those taken from Java are wider still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did recruitment become more coercive over time?","a":"Because deserters returning to their villages told of the real conditions on the railway, and the administration in Malaya answered with deception and force, which headmen tolerated or supported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Bij_de_intocht_in_Bantam_troffen_de_Nederlandse_troepen_een_monument_aan%2C_dat_wa%2C_Bestanddeelnr_2596.jpg","title":"A monument to the rōmusha in Banten, 1948","desc":"Dutch troops entering Banten in western Java on 23 December 1948 found this monument, raised by the Indonesian Republican authorities to the rōmusha who had died there.","credit":"Hesselman / Dienst voor Legercontacten, Nationaal Archief, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bij_de_intocht_in_Bantam_troffen_de_Nederlandse_troepen_een_monument_aan%2C_dat_wa%2C_Bestanddeelnr_2596.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a white stone memorial on a low stepped platform with a flagpole, standing in overgrown ground below wooded hills."},"wiki":"Rōmusha"},
    "ww2-502": {"questions":["In May 1945 the mines of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sank more Japanese shipping than submarines for the first time, and by August barely 30 ships a fortnight passed Shimonoseki.","Flying from Tinian, the 313th Bombardment Wing laid 12,053 mines in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a campaign the army air forces had taken on only at the navy’s urging."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Starvation</b> was the American aerial mining campaign of 1945 against the ports and shipping lanes of Japan, flown by the B-29s of the 313th Bombardment Wing from Tinian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The navy had pressed for it since 1944, arguing that the blockade already funnelled Japanese shipping through the Inland Sea and that its spout, the Shimonoseki Strait, could be plugged with mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army Air Forces took the job on grudgingly, unwilling to divert bombers from industrial targets, and LeMay’s plan of January 1945 put Shimonoseki at the head of the target list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first missions, coded Starvation I and II, sowed acoustic and magnetic mines in the strait on the nights of 27 and 30 March, as the invasion of Okinawa was about to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 14 August the wing had flown 1,528 sorties and laid 12,053 mines, the heaviest aerial mining campaign ever waged, for the loss of 16 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Japanese officers said after the war that the strait was closed about 15 days in every month from March onwards, and that the Inland Sea and then the main ports on the Sea of Japan had to be abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In May mines for the first time sank more shipping than submarines did, and in the first half of August only 30 ships passed Shimonoseki, about 7 per cent of the March traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Counts of the damage differ: the Strategic Bombing Survey credited mines with 9.3 per cent of all Japanese shipping lost in the war, and with half of everything sunk or damaged after April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its transport study put the toll at over 670,000 tons sunk or disabled, while the joint Army–Navy assessors credited mines laid by army aircraft with 257 ships of about 580,000 tons actually sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With coal stranded at the ports and the remaining ships carrying only food from the mainland, the transport study judged that an earlier, heavier campaign would have cut effective shipping nearly to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), chap. 21, \"The All-Out B-29 Attack,\" 661–65, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-21.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), chap. 21, \"The All-Out B-29 Attack,\" 665–74, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-21.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 11, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Transportation Division, <i>The War Against Japanese Transportation, 1941–1945</i> (Washington, DC, May 1947), chap. 1, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/waragainstjapane54unit. [Open access]","Joint Army-Navy Assessment Committee, <i>Japanese Naval and Merchant Shipping Losses During World War II by All Causes</i> (Washington, DC, February 1947), table II, vii, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Japan/IJN/JANAC-Losses/JANAC-Losses-2.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, CMH Pub 13-2), chap. XIX, 595–96, 613–14, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Shimonoseki Strait the first target?","a":"Because by March 1945 the loss of the southern convoy routes had driven the bulk of Japanese shipping through the Inland Sea and out by this one narrow passage, and mining it would also block reinforcements for Okinawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Army Air Forces hesitate to mine?","a":"Because their weapon and doctrine were built for attacks on industry, they believed Japan’s air power had to be destroyed first, and they feared an early mining programme would disrupt bombing that was still finding its feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for ships lost to mines differ?","a":"Because the sources count different things: the transport study counted ships sunk or disabled, the assessment committee only ships sunk, and the Survey’s 9.3 per cent is a share of losses over the whole war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/9th_Bombardment_Group_Aerial_Mining.jpg","title":"A B-29 drops aerial mines","desc":"A B-29 of the 313th Bombardment Wing’s 9th Bombardment Group releases two parachute-retarded Mark 26 magnetic mines, apparently on a training run.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9th_Bombardment_Group_Aerial_Mining.jpg","alt":"A four-engined bomber in flight over the sea, with two cylindrical mines falling far beneath it on small parachutes."},"wiki":"Operation Starvation"},
    "ww2-503": {"questions":["At the White House on 18 June 1945 Truman approved the Kyushu landing of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but kept back approval of the invasion of the Tokyo Plain for later.","American intelligence saw Japanese troops on southern Kyushu rise from about 80,000 to 206,000 by late July 1945, threatening the first stage of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> planned for November."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Downfall</b> was the American plan for the invasion of Japan in 1945–46, made up of Olympic, a landing on southern Kyushu, and Coronet, a landing on Honshu aimed at the Tokyo Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 29 March 1945 the Joint Chiefs of Staff set tentative dates of 1 December 1945 for Olympic and 1 March 1946 for Coronet, and on 25 May they ordered Olympic for 1 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Krueger’s Sixth Army was to land three corps on the east and west coasts of southern Kyushu, about 815,000 men in all, to seize airfields and Kagoshima Bay as bases for the second blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coronet was to use troops redeployed from Europe, and the plans foresaw Australian, Canadian, British and French divisions joining in its later stages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Potsdam the Combined Chiefs named the invasion the supreme operation of the war against Japan and set 15 November 1946 as the planning date for the end of organised Japanese resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> At the White House on 18 June Marshall argued that the first 30 days on Kyushu should cost no more than Luzon’s 31,000 casualties, and Truman approved Olympic while holding back the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Marshall then put the defenders at eight divisions or about 350,000 men, but by late July MacArthur’s intelligence staff reckoned southern Kyushu alone had grown from some 80,000 troops in April to 206,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan’s plan for the homeland battle, Ketsu-Go, was ordered on 8 April 1945 and put its weight on Kyushu and on the Kanto Plain around Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its generals hoped that landings would let them inflict casualties high enough to win a negotiated peace, and at the surrender the home islands held more than 9,000 aircraft ready for suicide attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan surrendered in August before either landing, and the Strategic Bombing Survey judged in 1946, from Japanese leaders’ testimony, that it would probably have done so before 1 November even had no invasion been planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, CMH Pub 13-3), chap. XIII, \"Downfall: The Plan for the Invasion of Japan,\" 395–99, 410, 416–17, 426, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurTheCampaigns. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1966; repr. Center of Military History, CMH Pub 13-2), chap. XIX, \"Homeland Defense Strategy,\" 601–2, 666, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","A. J. McFarland, secretary of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, minutes of meeting held at the White House, 18 June 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 1, Office of the Historian, document 598, 903–10, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d598. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), 10, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Benis M. Frank and Henry I. Shaw Jr., <i>Victory and Occupation</i>, History of U.S. Marine Corps Operations in World War II, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Historical Branch, G-3 Division, Headquarters, U.S. Marine Corps, 1968), part III, chap. 1, \"Future Operations,\" 403–8, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USMC/V/USMC-V-III-1.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Japan’s Struggle to End the War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 12–13, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/JapansStruggle/index.html. [Open access]","The Combined Chiefs of Staff to President Truman and Prime Minister Churchill, report, 24 July 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 2, Office of the Historian, document 1381, 1462–73, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv02/d1381. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the planners choose Kyushu for the first landing?","a":"Because its airfields and the great harbour of Kagoshima Bay could become bases for the blow at Honshu, and the Chiefs saw a lodgement there as essential both to tightening the blockade and to invading the Tokyo Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japan build up its forces on southern Kyushu?","a":"Because its commanders correctly judged southern Kyushu a likely target and hoped that heavy American losses on the beaches would bring better terms than unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Truman not approve the whole invasion in June?","a":"The minutes show him approving the Kyushu operation and leaving a later decision open; he noted that the next step would depend greatly on what the Soviet Union agreed to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Operation_Downfall_-_Map.jpg","title":"The plan for the invasion of Japan","desc":"A United States Military Academy campaign map of Operation Downfall, showing the planned Olympic landings on southern Kyushu and the Coronet landings near Tokyo.","credit":"Department of History, United States Military Academy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Operation_Downfall_-_Map.jpg","alt":"A map of the western Pacific from Japan to the Philippines, with arrows and boxes marking the planned Olympic landing on Kyushu, the Coronet landing near Tokyo and the forces assigned to each."},"wiki":"Operation Downfall"},
    "ww2-504": {"questions":["A Japanese director of the Bank for International Settlements asked its Swedish economic adviser, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to open a channel to the Americans like the one used for northern Italy.","Long before he led the International Monetary Fund from 1956, the economist <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met two Japanese officials at Basel who hoped to end the war in July 1945."],"abstract":"<b>Per Jacobsson</b> was a Swedish economist, born at Tanum in 1894, who spent most of his career in international finance and in July 1945 carried one of Japan’s peace feelers to the Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After 8 years in the League of Nations secretariat he became head of the Monetary and Economic Department of the Bank for International Settlements in Basel in 1931, writing the bank’s annual reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From December 1956 until his death in May 1963 he was chairman and managing director of the International Monetary Fund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1945 one of the bank’s directors was Kojiro Kitamura of the Yokohama Specie Bank, and in July Kitamura asked Jacobsson to put him in touch with American representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kitamura knew of the secret talks through which the Office of Strategic Services had arranged the surrender of the German forces in northern Italy, and he wanted a contact of the same kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was one of several such approaches: Japanese officials had sounded out the Swedish minister in Tokyo and a German contact in Bern that spring, and Washington told its envoys not to talk with Japanese who showed no authority to speak for their government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between 10 and 13 July Jacobsson met Kitamura and a second Japanese official of the bank, Yoshimura, who claimed to act with the minister in Bern and named the foreign and navy ministers among their peace group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They seemed to accept unconditional surrender but pressed two points, the preservation of the emperor and a return to the constitution of 1889, and Jacobsson was brought to Wiesbaden on 15 July to put them to Allen Dulles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dulles replied that he had no comment on dynastic or constitutional questions and that prompt unconditional surrender was the only way to save anything, while the OSS suspected the approach had begun locally rather than in Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In public the Japanese government denied ever proposing peace through any channel, and in private its leaders were pinning their hopes on Soviet mediation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Per Jacobsson Foundation, \"Per Jacobsson’s Biography,\" https://www.perjacobsson.org/bio.htm. [Open access]","Charles S. Cheston, acting director of the Office of Strategic Services, to the Secretary of State, memorandum, 13 July 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 360, 488–89, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d360. [Open access]","William J. Donovan, director of the Office of Strategic Services, to the Secretary of State, memorandum, 16 July 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 361, 489–91, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d361. [Open access]","William J. Donovan, director of the Office of Strategic Services, to the Secretary of State, memorandum, 12 May 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 349, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d349. [Open access]","The Ambassador in Portugal (Baruch) to the Secretary of State, airgram, 30 May 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 355, 484–85, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d355. [Open access]","The Acting Secretary of State (Grew) to the Ambassador in China (Hurley), telegram, 30 May 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 354, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d354. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Japan’s Struggle to End the War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 7, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/JapansStruggle/index.html. [Open access]","The Minister in Sweden (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, telegram, 7 April 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 344, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d344. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a Japanese banker turn to Jacobsson?","a":"Because both worked at the Bank for International Settlements in Basel, Jacobsson was a neutral Swede, and the Japanese hoped to reach the OSS men in Switzerland who had arranged the German surrender in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans treat the approach so cautiously?","a":"Because Washington had ordered its representatives not to deal with Japanese lacking authority, and the OSS believed this approach had been started locally rather than on instructions from Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the feeler come to nothing?","a":"The documents show no authorised voice behind it: Dulles would only repeat the demand for unconditional surrender, Tokyo publicly denied any peace proposal, and its leaders were waiting on Moscow instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Per_Jacobsson_Anefo.jpg","title":"Per Jacobsson in 1962","desc":"Per Jacobsson photographed in November 1962, when he was managing director of the International Monetary Fund.","credit":"Unknown photographer, ANEFO, Nationaal Archief, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Per_Jacobsson_Anefo.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white portrait of an elderly, smiling man with swept-back hair and heavy dark-rimmed glasses, in a dark suit."},"wiki":"Per Jacobsson","places":["city:1159135709"]},
    "ww2-505": {"questions":["Japan’s note of 10 August 1945 accepted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the understanding that it did not prejudice the prerogatives of the emperor as a sovereign ruler.","An American draft of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> allowed for a constitutional monarchy under the present dynasty, but the text published in July 1945 said nothing about the emperor at all."],"abstract":"The <b>Potsdam Declaration</b> was the proclamation issued on 26 July 1945 in the names of the American president, the president of Nationalist China and the British prime minister, setting out the terms on which Japan was to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It warned that fighting on would mean the destruction of Japan’s armed forces and the devastation of its homeland, and it ended by calling for the unconditional surrender of all Japanese armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its terms included removing those who had misled Japan into world conquest, occupying points in Japan, limiting Japanese sovereignty to the four main islands and minor islands the Allies chose, and letting disarmed soldiers go home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It said the Japanese would not be enslaved as a race or destroyed as a nation, but that war criminals would meet stern justice and freedom of speech, religion and thought would be established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet Union, still at peace with Japan, did not sign it, and joined the declaration only when it declared war on 8 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The text said nothing about the emperor: an American draft had allowed a constitutional monarchy under the present dynasty, and on 18 July the Joint Chiefs warned that the phrase could be misread in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Tokyo nobody in the inner cabinet objected to ending the war, but while the prime minister and the foreign and navy ministers would accept, the war minister and both chiefs of staff thought the terms too dishonourable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tokyo radio reported that Prime Minister Suzuki Kantarō had told the press Japan would ignore the ultimatum, and the government did ignore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the bombing of Hiroshima and the Soviet entry into the war, Japan accepted on 10 August on the understanding that the declaration did not prejudice the emperor’s prerogatives as a sovereign ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Allied reply of 11 August placed the emperor’s authority under the Supreme Commander and left Japan’s final form of government to the freely expressed will of its people, and Japan accepted on 14 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Proclamation Defining Terms for Japanese Surrender, July 26, 1945,\" in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","The Joint Chiefs of Staff to President Truman, memorandum, 18 July 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 2, Office of the Historian, document 1239, 1268–69, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv02/d1239. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Part 1: Military Defeat and Efforts to Reform the Constitution,\" <i>Birth of the Constitution of Japan</i>, outline, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/outline/01outline.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Japan’s Struggle to End the War</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 8–9, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/JapansStruggle/index.html. [Open access]","\"Soviet Declaration of War on Japan,\" 8 August 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s4.asp. [Open access]","The Secretary of State (Byrnes) to the Swiss Chargé (Grässli), note, 11 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 412, 631–32, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d412. [Open access]","Winston S. Churchill to President Truman, letter, 25 July 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 2, Office of the Historian, document 1249, 1279–80, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv02/d1249. [Open access]","The Swiss Chargé (Grässli) to the Secretary of State, note, 10 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 406, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d406. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the declaration leave out the emperor?","a":"The sources here show the warning rather than the final decision: the Joint Chiefs feared that a clause on a monarchy under the present dynasty would be read in Japan either as a threat to depose the emperor or as a promise to keep emperor worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Tokyo not accept the terms at once?","a":"Because the inner cabinet split: the prime minister, the foreign minister and the navy minister would accept, while the war minister and the two chiefs of staff held out over the emperor, war crimes trials and occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the reply of 11 August satisfy Japan in the end?","a":"It did not grant the emperor’s prerogatives, but it said that Japan’s final form of government would rest on the freely expressed will of its people, and at the conference of 14 August the emperor said he found the American answer acceptable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Kantar%C5%8D_Suzuki_Cabinet_19450407.jpg","title":"The Suzuki cabinet, April 1945","desc":"Prime Minister Suzuki Kantarō, centre front, with his cabinet on its first day, 7 April 1945; this was the government that received the Potsdam terms.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kantar%C5%8D_Suzuki_Cabinet_19450407.jpg","alt":"A formal black-and-white group photograph of some fifteen men in dark suits and uniforms, standing in rows on a staircase."},"wiki":"Potsdam Declaration","places":["city:1159140675"]},
    "ww2-506": {"questions":["Restated in Washington on 24 December 1941, the strategy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that Germany was still the prime enemy and that the defeat of Japan must follow hers.","Admiral Stark’s Plan Dog memorandum of November 1940 began the strategy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the ABC-1 staff report wrote down in early 1941."],"abstract":"<b>Europe first</b> was the Anglo-American decision that, in a war with both Germany and Japan, the main effort would go against Germany while the Pacific was held with lesser forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its starting point was Admiral Harold Stark’s Plan Dog memorandum of November 1940, which the American army’s history calls perhaps the most important single document in the strategy of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American and British staff talks of early 1941 wrote it into their report, ABC-1: Germany was to be defeated first, and the Atlantic and European area was to be the decisive theatre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Pearl Harbor the British chiefs restated it in Washington on 24 December 1941, holding that Germany was still the prime enemy and that her defeat was the key to victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once Germany was beaten, the paper argued, the defeat of Japan must follow, so only the minimum force needed to guard vital interests should be diverted elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Much of the American public saw it the other way round at first: in <i>Fortune</i>’s survey of February 1942, 47 per cent wanted the main effort aimed at Japan and 16 per cent wanted everything turned on Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only about a quarter chose to concentrate on Hitler while holding Japan in check, and on the Pacific coast fewer than one in six did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Opinion moved during the year, and by June 1942 Gallup found half of Americans naming Germany the chief enemy, a quarter Japan, and most of the rest calling the two equal threats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roosevelt made the global case in his fireside chat of 23 February 1942, asking listeners to spread a world map before them and rejecting a turtle policy of keeping the forces close to home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even inside the armed forces the doctrine bent, since in late 1942 American planners reworded it to leave room for pressure in the Pacific, and a year after Pearl Harbor the army had as many troops there as in Britain and North Africa together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. III, \"Europe Versus the Pacific,\" 81–88, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-3.html. [Open access]","British Chiefs of Staff, memorandum WW-1 (U.S. Revised), 24 December 1941, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, Office of the Historian, document 114, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d114. [Open access]","Office of Public Opinion Research, comp., \"Gallup and Fortune Polls,\" <i>Public Opinion Quarterly</i> 6, no. 2 (Summer 1942): 310–11, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/sim_public-opinion-quarterly_summer-1942_6_2. [Open access]","Office of Public Opinion Research, comp., \"Gallup and Fortune Polls,\" <i>Public Opinion Quarterly</i> 6, no. 3 (Fall 1942): 486–87, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/sim_public-opinion-quarterly_fall-1942_6_3. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Fireside Chat,\" 23 February 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-6. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff and Edwin M. Snell, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1941–1942</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. XVI, \"Strategic Inventory: December 1942,\" 353, 359, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WD-Strategic1/USA-WD-Strategic1-16.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XVIII, \"The Pacific in Grand Strategy,\" 377, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-18.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allied staffs put Germany before Japan?","a":"Because they judged Germany the predominant member of the Axis and the Atlantic and European area the decisive theatre, and reasoned that Japan’s defeat must follow once Germany had fallen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did many Americans want the main effort against Japan?","a":"The polls do not give reasons, but they show the feeling was strongest on the Pacific coast, where nearly eight in ten of those asked by <i>Fortune</i> in February 1942 wanted the main effort aimed at Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Pacific receive more forces than the doctrine implied?","a":"Because commitments in the Pacific that the War Department had not planned for kept arriving, and planners in late 1942 reworded the strategy so that the Pacific would not be held on a purely defensive footing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/FDR-Map-1942.jpg","title":"Roosevelt’s map broadcast","desc":"Roosevelt points to a map of the world during his fireside chat of 23 February 1942, for which listeners were asked to have their own maps in front of them.","credit":"New York World-Telegram and the Sun, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FDR-Map-1942.jpg","alt":"A man at a desk with radio microphones, pointing at a large map of the world on the wall beside him."},"wiki":"Europe first"},
    "ww2-507": {"questions":["In April 1941 Matsuoka wrote to Churchill of Japan’s great racial aim of bringing about the world envisaged in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a peace without conquest or exploitation.","The Tokyo tribunal held that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a phrase ascribed to the legendary emperor Jimmu, became a symbol of world domination through military force by the 1930s."],"abstract":"<b>Hakkō ichiu</b>, ‘the eight corners of the world under one roof’, was the slogan with which wartime Japan described its mission of bringing the world, beginning with Asia, together under the rule of its emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was traced to a rescript ascribed to Jimmu, the legendary first emperor, at the reputed founding of the empire, where the Tokyo tribunal judged it had meant no more than a universal principle of humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Meiji Restoration it was tied to the dynasty again as a rallying point for patriotism, together with Kōdō, the Imperial Way of loyalty that was said to lead to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 1920s and 1930s advocates of expansion invoked it again and again, and the tribunal concluded that it became a symbol of world domination through military force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 1 August 1940 the cabinet’s statement of fundamental national policy named its lofty spirit as the basis of a new order in Greater East Asia, built on Japan, Manchukuo and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Japan’s leaders spoke of it in the language of race: in April 1941 Foreign Minister Matsuoka wrote to Churchill of Japan’s great racial aim of bringing about the world envisaged in Hakko-Ichiu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He called it universal peace without conquest or exploitation, and told the Diet in January 1941 that Europeans and Americans who objected had simply failed to understand the idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American commentators read such claims as race doctrine, and one writer in 1943 argued that Japan had copied its racism from Nazi Germany, claiming to be the superior race and casting Britain and America as its scapegoats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same writer warned that Japan’s appeal to the coloured peoples against white rule would outlast its defeat unless Americans and Britons faced their own race prejudice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Race weighed on American planning too: at the White House on 18 June 1945 Truman asked whether an invasion by white men would unite the Japanese more closely, and Stimson thought it would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chapter IV, \"The Military Domination of Japan and Preparation for War\" (Tokyo, 1948), 84–86, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-4.html. [Open access]","Japanese Government, \"Statement by the Japanese Government, August 1, 1940,\" in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Japan, 1931–1941</i>, vol. 2, Office of the Historian, document 66, 108–10, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d66. [Open access]","Yosuke Matsuoka to Winston Churchill, 22 April 1941, transmitted by the Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Secretary of State, 27 April 1941, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1941</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Far East</i>, Office of the Historian, document 795, 969, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941v04/d795. [Open access]","Yosuke Matsuoka, statement before the Japanese Diet, 29 January 1941, enclosed in the Ambassador in Japan (Grew) to the Secretary of State, 10 February 1941, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Japan, 1931–1941</i>, vol. 2, Office of the Historian, document 220, 306–7, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1931-41v02/d220. [Open access]","Saul K. Padover, \"Japanese Race Propaganda,\" <i>Public Opinion Quarterly</i> 7, no. 2 (Summer 1943): 191–204, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/sim_public-opinion-quarterly_summer-1943_7_2. [Open access]","A. J. McFarland, secretary of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, minutes of meeting held at the White House, 18 June 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 1, Office of the Historian, document 598, 908–9, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv01/d598. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Tokyo tribunal give the slogan so much attention?","a":"Because it judged that a phrase which had once meant a universal principle of humanity had become, in the hands of expansionists, a symbol of world domination by military force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japanese leaders insist that the West misunderstood it?","a":"Matsuoka presented it as a peace with no conquest, oppression or exploitation, and held that Western objections to Japan’s plans for East Asia came from a failure to grasp the idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did race matter to American planners in 1945?","a":"The minutes show Truman and Stimson expecting that an invasion by white men would draw the Japanese more closely together, a judgement they weighed alongside the military case for landing on Kyushu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Founding_Ceremony_of_the_Hakko-Ichiu_Monument.JPG","title":"The Hakkō ichiu monument, 1940","desc":"The completion ceremony of the Hakkō ichiu monument at Miyazaki on 25 November 1940, in the year Japan celebrated the 2,600th anniversary of its legendary founding.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Founding_Ceremony_of_the_Hakko-Ichiu_Monument.JPG","alt":"A tall stone tower lit at night, with flags on either side and a large crowd gathered at its foot for a ceremony."},"wiki":"Hakkō ichiu"},
    "ww2-508": {"questions":["Unlike the selective arrests in Hawaii, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> removed whole families from California, Oregon and Washington to ten camps from Manzanar to Arkansas.","The Civil Liberties Act of 1988 apologised for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and paid $20,000 to each survivor, after a commission blamed race prejudice and war hysteria."],"abstract":"The <b>internment of Japanese Americans</b> was the forced removal of people of Japanese ancestry, citizens and immigrants alike, from the American West Coast in 1942 and their confinement in camps for most of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt’s Executive Order 9066 of 19 February 1942 let the secretary of war and his commanders exclude any or all persons from military areas, and though it named no group it was applied to the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counts differ with who is included: the army’s history says more than 110,000, the congressional commission about 120,000 and the National Archives about 122,000, nearly 70,000 of them American citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were held first in assembly centres at racetracks and fairgrounds and then in ten relocation centres in remote parts of the West and Arkansas, behind barbed wire and under military guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ban on returning to the coast was lifted in December 1944, the month the Supreme Court upheld the exclusion in <i>Korematsu</i>, and the last camp, Tule Lake, closed on 20 March 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> It was one of the war’s many internments of civilians, but no mass removal was ordered against Americans of German or Italian descent, and action against other enemy aliens was individual and selective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Hawaii, where nearly 158,000 people of Japanese ancestry made up more than a third of the population, there was no similar mass removal, only the arrest and internment of selected individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army’s own official history found little contemporary evidence that military necessity required a mass evacuation, and recorded that searches had uncovered no sign of planned sabotage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1982 a congressional commission reported that no documented act of espionage or sabotage had been committed by a Japanese American on the West Coast, and named race prejudice, war hysteria and failed political leadership as causes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Civil Liberties Act of 1988 acknowledged the fundamental injustice of the removal, apologised on behalf of the nation and paid $20,000 to each eligible survivor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Commission on Wartime Relocation and Internment of Civilians, <i>Personal Justice Denied</i>, part 1 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1982), summary, 2–4, 18, National Archives, https://www.archives.gov/files/research/japanese-americans/justice-denied/summary.pdf. [Open access]","Commission on Wartime Relocation and Internment of Civilians, <i>Personal Justice Denied</i>, part 1 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1982), chap. 11, \"Hawaii,\" 261–62, National Archives, https://www.archives.gov/files/research/japanese-americans/justice-denied/chapter-11.pdf. [Open access]","National Archives, \"Executive Order 9066: Resulting in Japanese-American Incarceration (1942),\" Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, \"Executive Order 9066—Authorizing the Secretary of War to Prescribe Military Areas,\" 19 February 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-9066-authorizing-the-secretary-war-prescribe-military-areas. [Open access]","Stetson Conn, Rose C. Engelman and Byron Fairchild, <i>Guarding the United States and Its Outposts</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Western Hemisphere (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1964), chap. V, \"Japanese Evacuation From the West Coast,\" 137, 147–49, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WH-Guard/USA-WH-Guard-5.html. [Open access]","<i>Korematsu v. United States</i>, 323 U.S. 214 (1944), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/323/214. [Open access]","Civil Liberties Act of 1988, Public Law 100-383, 102 Stat. 903 (10 August 1988), govinfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-102/pdf/STATUTE-102-Pg903.pdf. [Open access]","War Relocation Authority, <i>WRA: A Story of Human Conservation</i> (Washington, DC: United States Department of the Interior, 1946), introduction, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/wrastoryofhumanc00unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Japanese Americans removed when German and Italian Americans were not?","a":"The commission traced the decision to a long history of anti-Japanese agitation on the West Coast, to fear after Pearl Harbor and to failures of political leadership, and found no military analysis that justified singling them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was there no mass removal in Hawaii?","a":"The army’s history notes the contrast without settling it: Hawaii had a far larger Japanese population, more than a third of the islands’ people, much closer to the fighting, yet only selected individuals were interned there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Supreme Court uphold the exclusion?","a":"In <i>Korematsu</i> the majority treated the case as one about an exclusion order alone and accepted it as a wartime measure, while Justice Murphy dissented from what he called the legalization of racism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Manzanar_Relocation_Center%2C_Manzanar%2C_California._Street_scene_of_barrack_homes..._-_NARA_-_538128_-_restored.png","title":"Barracks at Manzanar","desc":"A street of barrack homes at the Manzanar relocation centre in California once a windstorm had passed, photographed for the War Relocation Authority by Dorothea Lange.","credit":"Dorothea Lange, War Relocation Authority, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manzanar_Relocation_Center%2C_Manzanar%2C_California._Street_scene_of_barrack_homes..._-_NARA_-_538128_-_restored.png","alt":"Rows of low wooden barracks along a broad, bare street beneath an American flag, with snow-capped mountains behind."},"wiki":"Internment of Japanese Americans"},
    "ww2-509": {"questions":["In June 1944 the Hawaiian 100th Infantry Battalion was absorbed into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Japanese American unit that went on to fight in Italy and France.","Reviewing the Nisei soldiers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Washington in July 1946, Truman told them they had fought not only the enemy but prejudice."],"abstract":"The <b>442nd Infantry Regiment</b> was the core of the 442nd Regimental Combat Team, a United States Army unit of Japanese Americans, most of them second-generation Nisei, that fought in Italy and France in 1944 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Pearl Harbor the army stopped taking Japanese Americans, but in early 1943 it reopened service to Nisei volunteers and formed the combat team, which trained at Camp Shelby in Mississippi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its volunteers came from Hawaii and the mainland, many of them directly from the relocation centres where their families were still held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In Italy on 15 June 1944 it absorbed the 100th Infantry Battalion, a Nisei unit raised from the Hawaii National Guard that had been in action since Salerno in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It fought north of Rome that summer and across the Arno, moved to France in the autumn, and returned to Italy in March 1945, attacking over the Apuan Alps in April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In the Vosges in October 1944 it helped take Bruyères and then fought for days through forest to reach a battalion of the 141st Infantry cut off on a hill, the so-called Lost Battalion, on 30 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The army’s history records that the regiment came out of the battle at little more than half its authorised strength, and the commission that studied the internment counted 800 casualties in a single week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In seven major campaigns the team took 9,486 casualties, including 600 killed, among the more than 18,000 men who served in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its honours have grown since the war: the commission listed one Medal of Honor in 1982, Clinton presented 22 to Asian American veterans after a review in 2000, and a law of 2010 credited the team and the 100th Battalion with 21.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Truman reviewed the regiment in Washington on 15 July 1946, he told its men that they had fought not only the enemy but prejudice, and had won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Commission on Wartime Relocation and Internment of Civilians, <i>Personal Justice Denied</i>, part 1 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1982), chap. 10, \"Military Service,\" 253–59, National Archives, https://www.archives.gov/files/research/japanese-americans/justice-denied/chapter-10.pdf. [Open access]","An Act to Grant the Congressional Gold Medal, Collectively, to the 100th Infantry Battalion and the 442nd Regimental Combat Team, Public Law 111-254, 124 Stat. 2637 (5 October 2010), govinfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-111publ254/pdf/PLAW-111publ254.pdf. [Open access]","Jeffrey J. Clarke and Robert Ross Smith, <i>Riviera to the Rhine</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1993), chap. XVIII, \"The Forests of the Meurthe,\" 329–32, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Riviera/USA-E-Riviera-18.html. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1977), chap. XXVI, \"Breakthrough on the Eighth Army Front,\" 460–61, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/USA-MTO-Cassino-26.html. [Open access]","William J. Clinton, \"Remarks on Presenting the Congressional Medal of Honor to Asian-American Heroes of World War II,\" 21 June 2000, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-presenting-the-congressional-medal-honor-asian-american-heroes-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, \"Remarks Upon Presenting a Citation to a Nisei Regiment,\" 15 July 1946, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-upon-presenting-citation-nisei-regiment. [Open access]","National Archives, \"Executive Order 9066: Resulting in Japanese-American Incarceration (1942),\" Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the army reopen service to Nisei in 1943?","a":"The 100th Battalion’s training record and petitions from prominent civilians and officers helped persuade Roosevelt and the War Department, according to the findings Congress adopted in 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Lost Battalion rescue cost so much?","a":"The 442nd had to fight through dense forest against counterattacks supported by armour, approaching from several directions over several days, and it emerged at little more than half strength with no replacements in sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the number of its Medals of Honor change?","a":"Because a later review of Asian American combat records found that some soldiers had been passed over for the highest award, so awards made in 2000 raised the count from the single medal listed in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/442nd_Infantry_receives_7th_Presidential_Unit_Citation_1946-07-15_1.jpg","title":"Truman reviews the 442nd, 1946","desc":"President Truman fastens a Presidential Unit Citation streamer to the colours of the 442nd Infantry in Washington on 15 July 1946.","credit":"United States Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:442nd_Infantry_receives_7th_Presidential_Unit_Citation_1946-07-15_1.jpg","alt":"A man in a suit attaching a streamer to a regimental flag held by helmeted soldiers, with officers and spectators standing behind on a lawn."},"wiki":"442nd Infantry Regiment"},
    "ww2-510": {"questions":["An executive order of 12 March 1942 made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> both commander of the United States Fleet and Chief of Naval Operations, and the president’s principal naval adviser.","Accepting that Germany came first, Admiral <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still had the planners drop the label offensive-defensive for the Pacific so that pressure on Japan would never slacken."],"abstract":"Fleet Admiral <b>Ernest King</b> commanded the United States Fleet from December 1941 and was also Chief of Naval Operations from March 1942, the American navy’s senior officer for the rest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An executive order of 12 March 1942 joined the two posts in his hands, named him the president’s principal naval adviser and gave him more control over the navy’s bureaus than any earlier chief had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sat with Marshall and Arnold on the Joint Chiefs of Staff and, with the British chiefs, on the Combined Chiefs, which shared Allied forces between the theatres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His official reports divided the Pacific war into four stages, from a pure defensive through two mixed phases to a full offensive, and dated the first real offensive to Guadalcanal in August 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By February 1944, he reported, the Japanese had been cleared from the Aleutians and ejected from the Gilberts and the western Marshalls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The strategy he served under had been restated at Washington in December 1941: Germany remained the prime enemy, and only the minimum force needed to guard vital interests elsewhere was to be diverted from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> King accepted the priority of Europe, but in late 1942 he objected when planners labelled the Pacific an ‘offensive-defensive’ theatre, and the wording was changed so that pressure on Japan would not slacken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Casablanca on 14 January 1943 Marshall proposed sharing Allied resources 70 per cent to the Atlantic and 30 per cent to the Pacific, and King estimated that only 15 per cent was then engaged against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pressed by the British not to weaken the main effort, he answered that the Combined Chiefs should decide only the balance between the efforts against Germany and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In practice the balance was closer than the doctrine: at the end of 1942 about 464,000 American soldiers faced Japan and 378,000 Germany, and only in 1943 did Germany’s figure pull ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. X, \"The U.S. and Japanese High Commands,\" 226–34, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-10.html. [Open access]","Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), chap. XVIII, \"The Pacific in Grand Strategy,\" 377–84, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-18.html. [Open access]","Combined Chiefs of Staff, minutes of meeting, 14 January 1943, 10:30 a.m., in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, Office of the Historian, document 336, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d336. [Open access]","British Chiefs of Staff, memorandum WW-1 (U.S. Revised), 24 December 1941, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, Office of the Historian, document 114, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d114. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff and Edwin M. Snell, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1941–1942</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), chap. XVI, \"Strategic Inventory: December 1942,\" 353, 359, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WD-Strategic1/USA-WD-Strategic1-16.html. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), chap. XVII, \"Strategic Inventory: December 1943,\" 391–92, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WD-Strategic2/USA-WD-Strategic2-17.html. [Open access]","Ernest J. King, <i>U.S. Navy at War, 1941–1945: Official Reports to the Secretary of the Navy</i>, first report, issued 23 April 1944 (Washington, DC: United States Navy Department, 1946), 38–39, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/USNatWar/USN-King-1.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did King object to calling the Pacific an offensive-defensive theatre?","a":"Because he wanted no doubt that the United States meant to keep steady pressure on Japan, so that it could not consolidate its conquests, and he thought stronger forces would be needed there than had been allotted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British resist a fixed percentage for the Pacific?","a":"Because any fixed share might weaken the main effort against Germany, which the Washington agreement of December 1941 had made the cardinal principle of Allied strategy, and they were also worried about a Burma offensive and India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were more American troops facing Japan than Germany at the end of 1942?","a":"Because successive commitments in the Pacific and the Mediterranean, which the War Department had not planned for, pulled forces away from the build-up in Britain; by December 1942 the Pacific build-up exceeded the original plans by about 150,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/FADM_Ernest_J._King.jpg","title":"Fleet Admiral Ernest King","desc":"Ernest King in about 1945, by then Commander in Chief of the United States Fleet and Chief of Naval Operations.","credit":"United States Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FADM_Ernest_J._King.jpg","alt":"A colour portrait of a naval officer in a dark double-breasted uniform and peaked cap, with rows of ribbons and broad gold stripes on his sleeves."},"wiki":"Ernest J. King"},
    "ww2-561": {"questions":["<i>The Times</i> called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most mortifying blow to sea power in home waters since the 17th century, yet the German Naval Staff judged it a strategic defeat.","Six Swordfish torpedo-bombers were shot down attacking the ships of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> off Calais, and a secret inquiry later examined why Coastal Command had not spotted them sooner."],"abstract":"The <b>Channel Dash</b> was the run of the German battleships Scharnhorst and Gneisenau and the heavy cruiser Prinz Eugen from Brest, through the Strait of Dover in daylight, to German ports on 11 to 13 February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The battleships had sheltered at Brest since March 1941, joined by Prinz Eugen in May, on the flank of Britain's main convoy route, free to break out at any time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To keep them damaged the RAF flew 3,299 bomber sorties and dropped 4,000 tons of bombs on them, losing 247 men and 43 aircraft, as Churchill told the Commons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler, fearing a British invasion of Norway, wanted every heavy ship there and, against his navy commander's advice, ordered the Brest squadron home by a surprise break through the Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He accepted that leaving after dark meant passing Dover in daylight, so the ships were given swept channels, a spring tide and sixteen fighters overhead throughout the hours of light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Admiralty had predicted on 2 February that the squadron would come up the Channel, but the ships were not identified until late the next morning off Le Touquet, 12 hours after sailing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two night patrols by Coastal Command aircraft had failed with broken radar, and the jamming of the coastal radar stations that morning was not at first recognised for what it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attacks that followed were piecemeal: all six Swordfish led by Lieutenant-Commander Esmonde were shot down, and no torpedo from destroyers, motor torpedo-boats or Beauforts, nor any bomb from 242 bombers, hit the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only mines laid by Bomber Command did damage, and the belief that the squadron had passed unscathed under the noses of the navy and air force caused a wave of indignation and a secret inquiry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The German Naval Staff summed up the outcome as a tactical victory but a strategic defeat, and the British official histories agree, since the threat from Brest to the Atlantic convoys was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 148–59, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-6.html. [Open access]","Dennis Richards, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Fight at Odds</i> (London: HMSO, 1953), 362–75, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RAF-I/UK-RAF-I-12.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “German Warships’ Escape: Inquiry,” <i>Hansard</i>, 17 February 1942, vol. 377, cols. 1658–74, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/feb/17/german-warships-escape-inquiry. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Channel Naval Action (Committee’s Report),” <i>Hansard</i>, 4 March 1942, vol. 378, col. 642, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/mar/04/channel-naval-action-committees-report. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 232–33, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Decision/USA-EF-Decision-11.html. [Open access]","Karl Dönitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i> (Washington, DC: Office of Naval Intelligence, Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 19–20, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans choose the Channel rather than the long route north of Scotland?","a":"Because the Channel run took under 24 hours, part of it in darkness, under an umbrella of fighters the whole way, while in the open Atlantic the ships risked being found by British reconnaissance and slowed by torpedo attack, as the Bismarck had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler also wanted the squadron in home waters to guard Norway against an invasion he expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dönitz later called the passage an unusually hazardous venture that could succeed only by surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the British attacks so ineffective?","a":"Because the ships were at sea for 12 hours before anyone recognised them, and by then the striking forces had to be thrown in piecemeal, in bad weather, without the fighter escorts arranged for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some crews took off knowing only that they were looking for enemy ships, since warning of the likely date had not been passed down the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill call the escape beneficial to Britain?","a":"Because it removed a squadron that had threatened the convoy routes for 10 months and ended the costly bombing of Brest, so the bombers could be turned on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both battleships had been damaged by mines on the way, Gneisenau was then disabled by a bombing raid in Kiel before the month was out, and the German Naval Staff itself judged the result a strategic defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Unternehmen_Cerberus.jpg","title":"The route of the Channel Dash","desc":"Map 15 of the British official naval history, tracing the escape of Scharnhorst, Gneisenau and Prinz Eugen up the Channel from Brest on 12 and 13 February 1942.","credit":"S. W. Roskill, The War at Sea 1939–1945, HMSO, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unternehmen_Cerberus.jpg","alt":"A map of the English Channel and the southern North Sea with the track of the German squadron running from Brest past Dover to the German coast."},"wiki":"Channel Dash"},
    "ww2-562": {"questions":["Admiral Tovey called the order to scatter <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> premature and its results disastrous, after only 11 of its merchantmen and two rescue ships reached Russian ports.","With 22 American merchantmen among its ships, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sailed from Iceland on 27 June 1942, and its fate led Britain to suspend the Arctic convoys for July and August."],"abstract":"<b>Convoy PQ 17</b> was an Allied convoy bound for Archangel that sailed from Iceland on 27 June 1942 and was ordered to scatter in the Barents Sea on 4 July, with the loss of most of its ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Accounts put its merchant ships at anything from 33 to 37, depending on what they count, 22 of them American, and more warships escorted or covered it than there were merchant ships to protect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four cruisers, two of them American, gave close cover and a battle fleet distant cover, the first time substantial American forces had served under British orders in such an operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commander of the Home Fleet, Admiral Tovey, had argued that the season was wrong, the convoy too large and its escort too weak, and was shocked to learn that the Admiralty might order it to scatter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Germans planned a combined air, U-boat and surface attack called Rösselsprung with Tirpitz, Hipper and Scheer, but Hitler forbade the heavy ships to engage until the British carrier had been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On the evening of 4 July, knowing that Tirpitz had reached Altenfjord and with air patrols broken off, the First Sea Lord ordered the cruisers to withdraw and the convoy to disperse, then to scatter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tirpitz sailed only the next day and was recalled within hours, because aircraft and U-boats were already destroying the scattered ships one by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only 11 merchantmen and two rescue ships reached Russian ports, and 3,350 vehicles, 430 tanks and 210 aircraft went to the bottom, while the attackers lost five of 202 aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tovey wrote in his despatch that the order to scatter had been premature and its results disastrous, and the British official history judges that the threat from Tirpitz never justified it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain then suspended the convoys for July and August over violent protests from Stalin, and Russian historians still differ over whether it was a tragic accident or an escort withdrawn for reasons hard to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 133–45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-5.html. [Open access]","Dennis Richards and Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Fight Avails</i> (London: HMSO, 1954), 77–79, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RAF-II/UK-RAF-II-5.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 1 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 179–92, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke and Magna E. Bauer, <i>Moscow to Stalingrad: Decision in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1987), 426–30, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-EF-Decision/USA-EF-Decision-20.html. [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 564–65, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]","Sergey M. Monin, “The Second World War: Myths about Lend-Lease,” <i>People and Texts: Historical Almanac</i>, no. 12 (2019), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vtoraya-mirovaya-voyna-mify-o-lend-lize. [in Russian] [Open access]","M. N. Suprun, “The Significance of the Northern Convoys in the Organisation of Military-Economic Cooperation between the USSR and the Allies, 1941–1945,” <i>Administrative Consulting</i>, no. 2 (2005), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/znachenie-severnyh-konvoev-v-organizatsii-voenno-ekonomicheskogo-sotrudnichestva-sssr-i-soyuznikov-v-1941-1945-gg. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Admiralty order the convoy to scatter?","a":"Because by the evening of 4 July it knew that Tirpitz had joined Scheer at Altenfjord, its air patrols had broken down, and the Naval Staff judged that surface ships could overwhelm cruisers, escort and convoy together, a greater danger than aircraft and U-boats against dispersed ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official history argues that the Germans were never likely to risk Tirpitz against an escort armed with torpedoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did scattering prove so costly?","a":"Because merchant ships and escorts in close order defend one another, while a convoy that loses cohesion leaves each ship to whatever enemy finds it next, as Tovey had warned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German aircraft hunted the merchantmen almost at leisure, and Raeder recalled Tirpitz once aircraft and U-boats were doing the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the disaster strain the alliance?","a":"Because the lost cargo had been promised to a hard-pressed Soviet Union, and Britain then stopped the convoys for 2 months, which Stalin protested violently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rumours in America meanwhile accused the Royal Navy of abandoning the merchant ships, and a Russian historian still calls the withdrawal of the escort hard to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/Convoy_PQ_17_sailing_in_Hvalfjord.jpg","title":"Ships of PQ 17 at Hvalfjord","desc":"Escorts and merchant ships at Hvalfjord in Iceland before Convoy PQ 17 sailed; behind the destroyer Icarus lies the Soviet tanker Azerbaijan.","credit":"Lt C. J. Ware, Royal Navy official photographer, Imperial War Museums A 8953, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Convoy_PQ_17_sailing_in_Hvalfjord.jpg","alt":"A grey destroyer in the foreground of a fjord with a tanker and other merchant ships at anchor behind it beneath bare hills."},"wiki":"Convoy PQ 17"},
    "ww2-563": {"questions":["After the Argentia meeting of August 1941 the corvettes and destroyers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed to an American admiral's command while the United States was still neutral.","Hastily trained crews in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> escorted slow convoys from Sydney in Nova Scotia, and convoy SC 42 lost about 15 ships to a wolf pack in September 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Newfoundland Escort Force</b> was the Royal Canadian Navy's first ocean escort command, formed at Saint John's in June 1941 to escort convoys across the western Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Admiralty decided on 20 May 1941, as the U-boats pushed westward, to base an escort force there, and Ottawa hastened to offer to run it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the first time the main Canadian naval fighting force was concentrated under Canadian command, that of Commodore L. W. Murray, on a task tied directly to the defence of Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Roosevelt and Churchill met at Argentia in August 1941, the still neutral United States joined in convoy escort, and the force passed to an American admiral's overall command with little consultation of Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ships, many of them new corvettes, took slow convoys from Sydney in Nova Scotia to a mid-ocean meeting point south of Iceland, and early in 1942 it numbered fourteen destroyers and about forty corvettes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The navy grew faster than it could train: experienced men were moved to newly commissioned ships, crews had almost no time in harbour, and convoy SC 42 lost about 15 ships to a wolf pack in September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By November 1942 Canada supplied 48 per cent of the North Atlantic escorts and Britain 50 per cent, the United States 2 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heavy losses in convoys with Canadian escorts that winter led the Admiralty to take three Canadian groups off the North Atlantic run between February and April 1943 for training, which hurt in Ottawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Atlantic Convoy Conference in Washington in March 1943 then gave Canada its own theatre, and from 30 April Rear-Admiral Murray commanded the protection of convoys north of New York and west of 47° W.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The navy behind the force grew from about 3,600 people and 13 ships in 1939 to some 93,000 and 939 vessels in 1945, and the semi-official American naval history concedes that British and Canadian forces sank more U-boats than American ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["C. P. Stacey, <i>Arms, Men and Governments: The War Policies of Canada, 1939–1945</i> (Ottawa: Queen's Printer, 1970), 310–15, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/AMG_e.pdf. [Open access]","W. A. B. Douglas, “The RCN and RCAF in the Battle of the Atlantic,” in <i>The Second World War as a National Experience</i>, ed. Sidney Aster (Ottawa: Canadian Committee for the History of the Second World War, Department of National Defence, 1981), 159–70, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/Natl_Exp/NatlExp-12.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 91, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-4.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 1 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 71, 90–91, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 357–58, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-14.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 361–62, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Canada want to run the force itself?","a":"Because it was the first chance to use the main Canadian naval force concentrated under Canadian command, on a task tied to the defence of Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ottawa also feared that Anglo-American planning would shut it out of Newfoundland, and the Admiralty, with no destroyers to spare, could hardly refuse the offer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Canadian escorts suffer so badly in 1941 and 1942?","a":"Because the navy expanded faster than it could train and equip itself: corvettes went to sea with untried crews, experienced men were moved on to new ships, and by late 1942 Canadian ships lagged behind the Royal Navy in radar and other equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An escort as thin as SC 42's one destroyer and three corvettes could not hold off a pack of up to twelve U-boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Canada get its own command in 1943?","a":"Because by late 1942 it supplied nearly half the North Atlantic escorts while the United States supplied 2 per cent, and Admiral Nelles pressed for a conference to settle who controlled them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Americans chose to withdraw from the northern convoys, Britain and Canada took complete charge of them, with Canada controlling the area west of 47° W.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/HMCS_Snowberry_%28K166%29_IWM_FL_5516.jpg","title":"A Canadian Flower-class corvette, 1943","desc":"An aerial photograph of the Royal Canadian Navy corvette HMCS Snowberry in May 1943; ships of this class made up much of Canada's convoy escort force.","credit":"Royal Navy official photographer, Imperial War Museums FL 5516, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HMCS_Snowberry_(K166)_IWM_FL_5516.jpg","alt":"A small grey warship with a single funnel and mast seen from above, under way on a dark sea."},"wiki":"Newfoundland Escort Force","places":["city:1159151071"]},
    "ww2-564": {"questions":["Admiral Stark argued in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that if Britain won decisively against Germany the United States could win everywhere, but if she lost it might win nowhere.","The logic of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the Atlantic the decisive theatre, which is why the Allies at Casablanca made the defeat of the U-boat a first charge on their resources."],"abstract":"The <b>Plan Dog memo</b> was the study of American strategy that the chief of naval operations, Admiral Stark, drafted on 4 November 1940 and revised on 12 November for the President.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It took its name from paragraph D, 'Dog' in military speech, which set out the course for war: a strong offensive in the Atlantic beside Britain and a defensive in the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central point was that American security depended on Britain: if Britain won decisively against Germany, Stark wrote, the United States could win everywhere, but if she lost it might not win anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He doubted that blockade and bombing could defeat Germany, judged that large American land and air forces would have to go to Europe or Africa, and urged that war with Japan be avoided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army planners endorsed it, Churchill found it strategically sound, and the American official history calls it perhaps the most important single document of the war's strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its logic carried into the staff talks of early 1941, known as ABC-1, which named Germany the main enemy and the Atlantic the decisive theatre, which the staffs confirmed on 31 December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every part of that strategy rested on the sea, since the armies meant to defeat Germany could reach Europe and Africa only across the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Early in 1942 the First Lord of the Admiralty warned that if Britain lost the war at sea it would lose the war, and aircraft for the convoys competed with the bombing of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Casablanca in January 1943 the Combined Chiefs of Staff made the defeat of the U-boat a first charge on the resources of the United Nations, and the shortage of escorts had already limited the build-up in North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Only after the U-boats were beaten could men and material cross almost unhindered for Sicily and Normandy, and an American naval history judges that without it they could never have reached the theatre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Morton, <i>Strategy and Command: The First Two Years</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1962), 67–91, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Strategy/Strategy-3.html. [Open access]","Memorandum by the United States and British Chiefs of Staff, “American-British Grand Strategy,” ABC-4/CS-1, WW-1 (Final), 31 December 1941, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, document 115, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d115. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 77–78, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-3.html. [Open access]","Combined Chiefs of Staff, minutes of meeting, 18 January 1943, 5 p.m., in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, document 355, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d355. [Open access]","Memorandum by the Combined Chiefs of Staff, “Conduct of the War in 1943,” C.C.S. 155/1, 19 January 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, document 408, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d408. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 361, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 468–69, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stark put the Atlantic before the Pacific?","a":"Because he judged American security to depend on Britain's survival and Britain unable to defeat Germany alone, while an all-out war against Japan would draw ships and supplies away from the Atlantic and cut aid to Britain without serving the main American aims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the strategy make the Atlantic so important?","a":"Because the forces meant to defeat Germany had to cross it, which is why the Anglo-American staffs counted the maintenance of essential communications among the essential features of their strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the U-boats were beaten, men and material for Sicily and Normandy crossed almost unhindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the U-boat come first at Casablanca?","a":"Because the measures against the submarine menace were described as the security for all other operations: Sir Alan Brooke summed up the agreed strategy as beginning with them and only then concentrating on the defeat of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In late 1942 the shortage of convoy escorts had already limited what could be sent to North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Admiral_King_and_Admiral_Pound_at_Casablanca_Conference%2C_Morocco%2C_1943_%2824656877531%29.jpg","title":"Admirals King and Pound at Casablanca, 1943","desc":"Admiral Ernest J. King of the United States Navy and Admiral of the Fleet Sir Dudley Pound at the Casablanca Conference in January 1943, where the defeat of the U-boat was made a first charge on Allied resources.","credit":"Office of War Information, National Museum of the U.S. Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Admiral_King_and_Admiral_Pound_at_Casablanca_Conference,_Morocco,_1943_(24656877531).jpg","alt":"Two senior naval officers in uniform standing side by side outdoors."},"wiki":"Plan Dog memo"},
    "ww2-565": {"questions":["Lord Woolton told the Lords that nothing imported bulked so large as wheat, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> baked from wheatmeal flour would free ships for moving troops.","Ministers chose the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1942 for shipping reasons alone, saving at least 400,000 tons of imported grain a year, though few Britons had bought wheatmeal voluntarily."],"abstract":"The <b>National Loaf</b> was the wheatmeal bread that Britain made compulsory in March 1942, when millers were barred from making white flour and had to keep 85 per cent of the wheat grain in their flour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain's dry-cargo imports had averaged 55 million tons a year in peacetime, while its total imports in 1941 came to only 30.5 million tons, 14.7 million of them food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ministry of Food had promoted a voluntary National Wheatmeal Loaf since 1941, but despite advertising on a national scale it made little headway with the public or the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ministry raised the extraction rate only from 73 to 76 per cent that year, judging that with meat, milk, cheese and jam already short it was a bad moment to take away white bread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Opponents also warned that more of the grain in flour meant less wheat offal to feed pigs, poultry and dairy cows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pearl Harbor changed the sums: shipments fell, the services claimed more ships, and in January 1942 imports for the quarter were not expected to exceed 5¾ million tons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ministry of Food warned that unless 200,000 tons more wheat arrived before April, bread would have to be rationed or the extraction rate raised, and the War Cabinet chose the higher rate on 4 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lord Woolton told the Lords that nothing imported bulked so large as wheat and that the change would free ships for the movement of troops, though he would not say how much tonnage it saved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The official historians found no sign that anything but shipping weighed with ministers, and they put the net saving at not less than 400,000 tons of imported grain a year, the greatest single economy open to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As the North African landings took more ships the flour was also diluted with other grains, and British imports in 1942 fell to a third below their level of 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["R. J. Hammond, <i>Food</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Growth of Policy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1951), 167–69, 260–61, 267–68, https://archive.org/details/food-volume1. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 420–22, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-14.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 266–67, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-10.html. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, “Food Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 11 March 1942, vol. 122, cols. 227–37, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1942/mar/11/food-situation. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “White Flour,” <i>Hansard</i>, 17 March 1942, vol. 378, col. 1334, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/mar/17/white-flour. [Open access]","C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 5, 51, https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 217–18, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-8.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was wheat the obvious place to save shipping?","a":"Because of all the foods Britain imported nothing bulked so large as wheat, and a higher extraction rate let the same wheat make more flour without extra ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raising the rate was by far the greatest single relief to the shipping position open to the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why had the change been put off for so long?","a":"Because the public preferred white bread, the millers and the trade disliked wheatmeal, and the agricultural departments feared the loss of feed for livestock, so in 1941 ministers judged it a bad moment to force the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even in March 1942 deliveries of white flour were restricted to stop a rush to buy it before it disappeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the official histories call it a shipping measure rather than a health measure?","a":"Because although the Minister of Health argued that high-extraction flour was better for people, he said he would not have pressed for the change on those grounds alone, and the records show that only shipping decided it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/London_Carries_On-_Shopping_in_Wartime_London%2C_1942_D6567.jpg","title":"Queueing for bread in London, 1942","desc":"Civilians and men and women of the forces queue for bread and rolls at the bakery counter of a large London department store in 1942, the year the National Loaf became compulsory.","credit":"Imperial War Museums (D 6567), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Carries_On-_Shopping_in_Wartime_London,_1942_D6567.jpg","alt":"A crowded food hall with an ornate ceiling, where a queue of shoppers, some in uniform, waits at a bread counter."},"wiki":"National Loaf"},
    "ww2-566": {"questions":["About 1,800 Italian prisoners were aboard the troopship sunk by U-156 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only about 450 of them survived by Dönitz's own figures.","The order Dönitz issued after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was judged at Nuremberg undoubtedly ambiguous, though not proof that he had deliberately ordered shipwrecked survivors killed."],"abstract":"The <b>Laconia incident</b> was the sinking of the British troopship Laconia by U-156 in the South Atlantic on 12 September 1942, and the rescue, the bombing and the order forbidding rescues that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ship was carrying about 1,800 Italian prisoners of war besides its crew and passengers, and the U-boat's commander, Hartenstein, reported the sinking and asked for orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dönitz sent other U-boats to help, Vichy France was asked to send warships from Dakar, and Hartenstein broadcast in English that he would not attack any ship that came to assist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The U-boats took survivors aboard and towed lifeboats, but on 16 September an American Liberator bombed U-156 while it towed four boats under a Red Cross flag, and one lifeboat capsized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By Dönitz's figures about 800 of the 811 British aboard were saved but only about 450 of the 1,800 Italians, and survivors were later handed over to French warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 17 September Dönitz ordered that no attempt of any kind be made to rescue the crews of ships sunk, a rule later known as the Laconia order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Nuremberg the tribunal found the order undoubtedly ambiguous and deserving the strongest censure, but held that the evidence did not establish that Dönitz had deliberately ordered survivors killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It did find that he had ordered the rescue provisions of the 1936 submarine protocol not to be carried out, and one flotilla commander, Möhle, told crews to kill survivors, though few if any did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war at sea was paid for above all by merchant seamen: the British Merchant Navy alone lost 30,248 men to enemy action, and no total for all the Allied crews could be computed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Submarines sank 2,828 Allied and neutral ships, and though better life-saving gear meant a smaller share of the shipwrecked died in 1942, far more ships went down that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 209–10, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/UK-RN-II-8.html. [Open access]","Testimony of Karl Dönitz, 9 May 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947–49), 281–87, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-09-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Doenitz,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947–49), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 57–58, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Cross-examination of Karl Dönitz, 10 May 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947–49), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-10-46.asp. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, pt. 2, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 304–6, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-2. [Open access]","C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 171–72, https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Dönitz stop the rescue after it had begun?","a":"Because the bombing of U-156 showed him that a U-boat crowded with survivors and towing lifeboats could not dive or defend itself, and the naval staff had passed on Hitler's wish that no submarine be risked in rescue work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He later told the tribunal that he had at first refused to throw the survivors back into the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Laconia order judged so harshly?","a":"Because the 1936 protocol required a submarine that could not rescue a ship's crew to let the ship pass, so an order forbidding rescue broke it even if no one was meant to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its wording, which called rescue contrary to the most primitive laws of warfare, was ambiguous enough for one flotilla commander to read it as licence to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Dönitz not sentenced for his conduct of the submarine war?","a":"Because the tribunal took account of a British order of May 1940 to sink all ships at sight in the Skagerrak and of Admiral Nimitz's statement that the United States had waged unrestricted submarine warfare in the Pacific from its first day in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/U-156_37-35_Laconia_1942_09_15.jpg","title":"Laconia survivors between two U-boats","desc":"Survivors of the Laconia being ferried between U-156 in the foreground and U-507 behind it on 15 September 1942, the day before the bombing.","credit":"Leopold Schuhmacher, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U-156_37-35_Laconia_1942_09_15.jpg","alt":"The deck of a surfaced submarine in the foreground and a second submarine further off across calm open sea."},"wiki":"Laconia incident"},
    "ww2-567": {"questions":["From July 1940 any cargo bound for a European port without a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was liable to seizure by British patrols, along with a ship lacking its own.","Revived from the Letters of Assurance of 1916, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let Britain ration European neutrals' imports by refusing cargoes intended for re-export."],"abstract":"A <b>navicert</b> was a British certificate, a kind of commercial passport, issued in a neutral country to show that a cargo bound for Europe was not contraband and could pass the Allied blockade without delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system came into force on 1 December 1939, revived from the Letters of Assurance, code-named navicerts, that Britain and the United States had worked out in March 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American Secretary of State, Cordell Hull, had himself asked for such a scheme on 4 September 1939, since it spared shippers the delay and cost of detention at British contraband-control ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Washington still took no official position on it, warning that it must never cut the normal volume of genuine neutral exports or discriminate against American exporters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For Britain it brought advance knowledge of shipments and a way to stop a cargo simply by refusing a navicert, which neutrals sometimes denounced as dictating to them on their own soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Once German armies held the coast from the North Cape to the Pyrenees, the Minister of Economic Warfare announced on 30 July 1940 that every cargo for a European port would need a navicert or be liable to seizure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same day the Minister of Shipping announced ship's warrants, without which neutral owners would lose ready access to the bunkers, docks, repairs and insurance under British control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Navicerts were now granted only for what a neutral needed for its own consumption and not for re-export, and the United States acquiesced without giving official approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neutral ships mattered for Britain's own supply too, since about 24 million of the 55 million tons it imported in a year of peace came in foreign ships, and it chartered neutral tonnage at high rates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany answered by sinking neutral ships without warning, and the Nuremberg tribunal held that sinking neutrals found in its declared operational zones broke the 1936 submarine protocol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["W. N. Medlicott, <i>The Economic Blockade</i>, vol. 1, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), 94–96, 422, 436–37, https://archive.org/details/economic-blockade-v-1. [Open access]","C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 51, 58–59, https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]","Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Secretary of State (Grady), 2 January 1940, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940</i>, vol. 2, <i>General and Europe</i>, document 1, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d1. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Contraband Control (Navicerts),” <i>Hansard</i>, 30 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 1130–31, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/30/contraband-control-navicerts. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Neutral Shipping (Government Measures),” <i>Hansard</i>, 30 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 1163–65, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/30/neutral-shipping-government-measures. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Doenitz,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947–49), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddoeni.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States welcome navicerts at first?","a":"Because they spared American exporters the delay, expense and danger of detention at British contraband bases, which is why Hull himself asked for the scheme in September 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Washington still set conditions, insisting that the system must not be used to discriminate against American trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain make the system compulsory in 1940?","a":"Because with Germany holding the western coast of Europe Britain had to control all shipping crossing the Atlantic, and diverting every ship to contraband bases in British waters or West Africa would have been a grave burden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Checking cargoes at the port of loading did the work instead, and ship's warrants gave neutral owners a strong reason to comply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did neutral shipping matter to Britain's own supply?","a":"Because foreign ships had carried nearly half of Britain's peacetime imports, and Germany sank neutral ships without warning from October 1939 to frighten their owners away from British charters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal later held that sinking neutrals in declared zones violated the 1936 protocol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/The_Royal_Navy_during_the_Second_World_War_A6080.jpg","title":"A contraband-control boarding party","desc":"A whaler from HMS Sheffield with an armed boarding party is lowered to inspect a merchant ship, whose papers would be checked to make sure it was not supplying the enemy.","credit":"Imperial War Museums (A 6080), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Royal_Navy_during_the_Second_World_War_A6080.jpg","alt":"A ship's boat full of sailors being lowered on davits down the grey side of a warship towards the sea."},"wiki":"Navicert"},
    "ww2-568": {"questions":["Once American yards were building ships faster than U-boats could sink them in 1943, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Dönitz had pursued could no longer be won.","Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a ship returning from Africa counted as much as a loaded one bound for Britain, so U-boats moved wherever sinking came cheapest."],"abstract":"The <b>tonnage war</b> was Dönitz's name for the U-boat campaign as a race to sink Allied merchant ships faster than they could be built, so that shipping as a whole, not any route or cargo, was the target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the U-boat command's war diary on 31 December 1942 he called it the submariners' main task and probably their decisive contribution, to be fought wherever the greatest successes cost the smallest losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under this idea a ship returning from Africa counted as much as one loaded for Britain, so whenever the North Atlantic grew costly he sent his boats to easier waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1942 it came close to working, for Allied losses reached 1,664 ships of 7,790,697 tons while just over 7 million tons were built, and British imports fell a third below their 1939 level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dönitz ended that year with 212 boats operational out of 393, and in March 1943 his U-boats sank 108 ships of 627,377 tons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Then the balance turned: by his own account aircraft and radar let the escorts find U-boats far off, and losses rose from 13 per cent of the boats at sea to between 30 and 50 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After more than 30 U-boats were sunk in the first 3 weeks of May 1943, Dönitz broke off the battle against the North Atlantic convoys on 24 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> American yards meanwhile pushed their programme for 1943 to 20 million deadweight tons, and the American and British naval histories date the moment new building overtook sinkings for good to December 1942 or to July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the last quarter of 1943 more than two U-boats were sunk for every merchant ship, and of the 1,162 U-boats Germany built in the war it lost 784.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Axis submarines still sank 2,828 ships in all, yet the American naval history concludes that the tonnage war failed completely, since the flow of men and supplies across the Atlantic was never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), epigraph, 11, 59, 245, 361, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Karl Dönitz, <i>The Conduct of the War at Sea: An Essay</i> (Washington, DC: Office of Naval Intelligence, Navy Department, 15 January 1946), 22–23, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/Germany/KM/WarAtSea/index.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 217–18, 356–57, 377–78, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-RN-II/index.html. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Offensive</i>, pt. 2, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1961), 304–5, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-3-part-2. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Seekrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/seekrieg. [in German] [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 428–30, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-14.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the tonnage war fail?","a":"Because from the spring of 1943 aircraft and radar found the U-boats before they could attack, and losses rose to between 30 and 50 per cent of the boats at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the same time American yards were turning out ships faster than the U-boats could sink them, so the race at the heart of the strategy was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the official histories date the turning point differently?","a":"Because they measure different curves: the American naval history says new Allied construction passed merchant losses in December 1942, while the British official history dates the moment when building crossed sinkings for good to July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the concept counted among Dönitz's blunders?","a":"Because by chasing tonnage wherever it was cheapest he treated an empty ship like a loaded one and moved his boats away from the convoy routes whenever they grew dangerous, which an American naval history calls perhaps his greatest strategic blunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany had counted on the U-boats to stop the build-up of American forces in Britain, and in that they failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f8/U-826_at_Loch_Eriboll_Scotland_after_surrender_1945_IWM_A_28531.jpg","title":"A surrendered U-boat at Loch Eriboll, 1945","desc":"A British naval armed guard watches a German rating carry torpedo parts from the surrendered U 826 at Loch Eriboll in Scotland, as the U-boat fleet began to surrender to the Royal Navy.","credit":"Imperial War Museums (A 28531), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U-826_at_Loch_Eriboll_Scotland_after_surrender_1945_IWM_A_28531.jpg","alt":"Sailors on the deck of a surfaced submarine in a loch, one carrying a long metal part while an armed guard stands by."},"wiki":"Tonnage war"},
    "ww2-569": {"questions":["Because every advance in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stretched supplies along a single coast road, the front swung back and forth across Cyrenaica for 2 years.","Alexander called the final phase of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first wholly successful campaign against the Axis, won by British, American and French forces fighting together."],"abstract":"The <b>North African campaign</b> was the war fought along the southern shore of the Mediterranean, from Egypt to Morocco, between the Axis and the British Empire and its allies, whom the Americans joined in November 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy declared war on 11 June 1940 and invaded Egypt from Libya on 13 September, while Britain held Egypt to guard the Suez Canal and its oil supplies from the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In December the Western Desert Force of 36,000 men struck back, destroying eight Italian divisions within a month, and by 7 February 1941 the count stood at nine divisions and 130,000 prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler then sent a blocking force that grew into the German Africa Corps under Rommel, whose first troops landed at Tripoli on 11 February 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The front swung back and forth across Cyrenaica for 2 years, because every advance stretched supplies along one coast road, about 1,085 kilometres (674 miles) from Tripoli to Benghazi alone, and fuel ran short at crucial moments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rommel retook Cyrenaica in the spring of 1941, the British relieved Tobruk in December after Operation Crusader, and in June 1942 Tobruk fell with a garrison Churchill put at about 25,000 men in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Axis advance stopped at El Alamein, about 100 kilometres (60 miles) from Alexandria, and Montgomery's Eighth Army broke through there on 4 November 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 8 November more than 100,000 American and British troops landed in Morocco and Algeria behind the Axis, and the number of escorted convoys rather than the ports then set the pace of their build-up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Caught between two armies, the Axis forces in Tunisia surrendered on 12 and 13 May 1943, and the Allies took about a quarter of a million prisoners, 275,000 by the American official count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Alexander called it the first wholly successful campaign against the Axis, and he credited the victory to a close collaboration of British, American and French forces that he judged a war-winning factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["National Army Museum, “The Struggle for North Africa, 1940–43,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/struggle-north-africa-1940-43. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Early Successes against Italy (to May 1941)</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1954), 277–98, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-I/UK-Med-I-15.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 9–15, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-1.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Afrikafeldzug,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/afrikafeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Central Direction of the War,” <i>Hansard</i>, 2 July 1942, vol. 381, cols. 527–611, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jul/02/central-direction-of-the-war. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 664–66, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-34.html. [Open access]","H. R. Alexander, Viscount Alexander of Tunis, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August, 1942 to 13th May, 1943,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 839–87, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 468–69, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the front swing back and forth so far?","a":"Because both sides depended on long supply lines along a single coast road, and the further either advanced the harder it was to keep its forces supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fuel ran short at crucial moments, so rapid advances were often followed by equally rapid retreats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany send troops to Africa at all?","a":"Because the Italian defeat threatened the loss of Libya and of Italy's whole colonial empire, and Hitler feared that a weakened Italy and a stronger British position in the Mediterranean could open a new front in southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At first he sent only a blocking force to hold Tripolitania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Axis collapse so completely in Tunisia?","a":"Because its armies were caught between the Eighth Army in the east and the Allied forces from the west, short of fuel and ammunition, and Hitler and Mussolini refused to prepare an evacuation for fear of what it would do to morale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Alexander reported that only 663 men escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/The_British_Army_in_North_Africa_1942_E16132.jpg","title":"Crusader tanks on patrol in the Western Desert","desc":"Crusader tanks and other vehicles out on patrol in the Western Desert on 26 August 1942.","credit":"Imperial War Museums (E 16132), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_British_Army_in_North_Africa_1942_E16132.jpg","alt":"Tanks and lorries moving across flat open desert under a pale sky, raising dust."},"wiki":"North African campaign"},
    "ww2-570": {"questions":["Equipped largely on theory, with tank engines lacking proper filters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still outfought British armour whose tanks and infantry fought almost separate battles.","The army high command sent Paulus to Libya in April 1941 to restrain the commander of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whom Halder called a soldier gone stark mad."],"abstract":"The <b>Afrika Korps</b>, in German the Deutsches Afrikakorps, was the German army corps sent to Libya in 1941 to save Italy's North African colony, and it fought in the desert until the Axis surrender in Tunisia in May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the Italian collapse Hitler ordered a blocking force on 11 January 1941, built from units of the 3rd Panzer Division as the 5th Light Division, and in February he added the 15th Panzer Division to make up the corps under Rommel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither division had trained for the desert, and their equipment was planned largely on theory, so tank engines lacked proper air and oil filters and the water needed was overestimated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet the 5th Light Division was formidable, with about 150 tanks, strong anti-tank units with a few 88-millimetre guns and a battle drill learned in mechanised war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first combat troops reached Tripoli in mid-February, and although told to act cautiously Rommel attacked at the end of March and drove the British back to the Egyptian frontier, leaving Tobruk besieged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The army high command sent General Paulus to rein him in, and its chief of staff, Halder, wrote that Paulus was perhaps the only man who could head off this soldier gone stark mad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For months its tactics outclassed a British army whose armour and infantry fought almost separate battles, and the Panzer III tank was its main strike weapon in 1941 and 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In January 1942 Churchill told the Commons that Britain faced a very daring and skilful opponent and, across the havoc of war, a great general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The corps became part of the German and Italian Panzer Army Africa, which was stopped at El Alamein in 1942 and had to give up Libya at the end of January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There the corps, under General Cramer, went into captivity when about a quarter of 1 million Axis soldiers surrendered on 12 and 13 May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 12–15, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-1.html. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair et al., <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Germans Come to the Help of Their Ally (1941)</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1956), 153, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Med-II/UK-Med-2-8.html. [Open access]","National Army Museum, “The Struggle for North Africa, 1940–43,” https://www.nam.ac.uk/explore/struggle-north-africa-1940-43. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Afrikafeldzug,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/afrikafeldzug-1941. [in German] [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “War Situation,” <i>Hansard</i>, 27 January 1942, vol. 377, cols. 569–690, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/jan/27/war-situation. [Open access]","George F. Howe, <i>Northwest Africa: Seizing the Initiative in the West</i>, United States Army in World War II: Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 645–46, 664–66, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-NWA/USA-MTO-NWA-34.html. [Open access]","H. R. Alexander, Viscount Alexander of Tunis, “The African Campaign from El Alamein to Tunis, from 10th August, 1942 to 13th May, 1943,” supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38196, 5 February 1948, 839–87, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38196/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany send a corps to Africa in 1941?","a":"Because the Italian defeat threatened the loss of Libya and of Italy's colonial empire, and Hitler feared that a British victory could open a new front in southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The force began as a blocking detachment meant only to hold Tripolitania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the corps do so well against the British at first?","a":"Because its units had practical experience of mechanised warfare, tested equipment and a clear, uniform doctrine, so they had only to adapt to the desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British, by contrast, struggled to make their armour and infantry work together, and their armour often fell victim to coordinated German attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German high command try to restrain Rommel?","a":"Because it had few resources to spare for Africa while it prepared the invasion of the Soviet Union, and it wanted the desert kept from becoming an embarrassment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Paulus was sent to assess the position and to make Rommel understand that little further help would come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-424-0258-32%2C_Tripolis%2C_Ankunft_DAK%2C_Rommel.jpg","title":"Rommel greets Italian officers at Tripoli, 1941","desc":"The arrival of the first units of the Afrika Korps at Tripoli in February 1941: Rommel, second from right, greets Italian officers, among them General Italo Gariboldi, the Italian commander-in-chief in North Africa.","credit":"Sturm, Bundesarchiv Bild 101I-424-0258-32, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-424-0258-32,_Tripolis,_Ankunft_DAK,_Rommel.jpg","alt":"A group of German and Italian officers in uniform shaking hands and talking in the open air."},"wiki":"Afrika Korps"},
    "ww2-601": {"questions":["Molotov protested in March 1945 that the Bern contacts which led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were negotiations carried on behind the back of the state bearing the war's main burden.","The American official history calls the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the pattern for the piecemeal German surrenders to the British and Americans that followed in early May 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Surrender at Caserta</b> was the capitulation of the German forces in Italy, signed at Allied Force Headquarters at Caserta on 29 April 1945 and in force from noon on 2 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two officers, Lieutenant Colonel von Schweinitz for the army group commander Heinrich von Vietinghoff and Major Wenner for the SS general Karl Wolff, signed an instrument making the capitulation unconditional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The territory surrendered covered all of Italy except Venezia Giulia east of the Isonzo river, together with western and parts of central Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Soviet general, Aleksey Kislenko, attended the final talks as an observer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fighting in Italy ended days before the general German surrender, and the American official history calls the Italian capitulation the pattern for a series of piecemeal surrenders to the British and Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Secret contacts began in February 1945, when Swiss intelligence officers put go-betweens acting for Wolff in touch with Allen Dulles of the American Office of Strategic Services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March Wolff came to Switzerland himself, and as proof of good faith his side handed over the captured Italian resistance leader Parri, which Washington reported to Moscow on 11 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Refused a part in the talks, the Soviet government protested through Molotov that they were going on behind the back of the state bearing the main burden of the war against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 20 April the Combined Chiefs of Staff ordered the contacts broken off, but envoys with written powers from Vietinghoff then reached Switzerland, and on 26 April they were allowed to come to Caserta on condition that there be no bargaining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kesselring, who relieved Vietinghoff for treasonable contact with the Allies before finally approving the cease-fire, later testified at Nuremberg that he had himself joined Wolff in negotiating through Switzerland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1977), 513–29, 543–44, https://archive.org/details/CassinoToTheAlps. [Open access]","“The Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman) to the Secretary of State,” Moscow, 23 March 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission; Austria; Germany</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1968), document 528, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d528. [Open access]","“Mr. Alexander C. Kirk, Political Adviser on the Staff of the Supreme Allied Commander, Mediterranean Theater, to the Acting Secretary of State,” Caserta, 27 April 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission; Austria; Germany</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1968), document 553, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d553. [Open access]","“Mr. Alexander C. Kirk, Political Adviser on the Staff of the Supreme Allied Commander, Mediterranean Theater, to the Acting Secretary of State,” Caserta, 29 April 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission; Austria; Germany</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1968), document 558, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d558. [Open access]","Testimony of Albert Kesselring, 13 March 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 9 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/03-13-46.asp. [Open access]","“The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet Union (Harriman),” Washington, 11 March 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission; Austria; Germany</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1968), document 515, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d515. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet government object to the contacts in Switzerland?","a":"Because it had asked for its own officers to take part and been refused, and Molotov wrote that talks with German commanders were going on behind the back of the state that was bearing the main burden of the war against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the surrender stop at the Isonzo river?","a":"Because the German high command had placed the land east of the Isonzo under a different army group, Army Group E, so the Trieste area was not covered, and the instrument signed at Caserta excluded Venezia Giulia east of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the surrender nearly undone after it was signed?","a":"Because back at Bolzano Kesselring relieved Vietinghoff for treasonable contact with the Allies and two army commanders refused at first to pass on the cease-fire, so it held only once Kesselring finally gave his approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Surrender at Caserta","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/Caserta_surrender_-_Resa_di_Caserta_-_final_meeting_-_29_April_1945_%281_of_4%29.jpg","title":"Final meeting at Caserta, 29 April 1945","desc":"German and Allied delegations at the final meeting in the palace of Caserta at which the surrender was signed.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caserta_surrender_-_Resa_di_Caserta_-_final_meeting_-_29_April_1945_(1_of_4).jpg","alt":"Men in military uniform and civilian suits standing around a long table in a room with a large map on the wall."}},
    "ww2-602": {"questions":["Churchill told the opening session of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that getting Italy out of the war was the great prize, while Roosevelt shrank from sending large armies into Italy.","Seven divisions were to be held ready to leave the Mediterranean from November under a compromise struck at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between British hopes for Italy and American plans for France."],"abstract":"The <b>Trident Conference</b> was the meeting of Roosevelt, Churchill and the Combined Chiefs of Staff held in Washington from 12 to 25 May 1943, the largest gathering of senior British and American officials of the war so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill crossed on the liner Queen Mary with a party of about a hundred, and the Combined Chiefs met almost daily while the two leaders joined them six times at the White House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history calls it the second Washington conference, while the American documentary edition counts it as the third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the opening session Churchill called the elimination of Italy the great prize in the Mediterranean, while Roosevelt said he had always shrunk from putting large armies into Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The talks also covered the Pacific, Burma and China and the U-boat war, and the Combined Chiefs received the American plan for a combined bomber offensive against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The heart of the Mediterranean strategy debate was whether operations after the conquest of Sicily would prepare the way for the invasion of France or starve it of men and ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British chiefs argued that knocking Italy out would draw German forces from the Soviet front and ease a later landing, whereas Marshall feared a commitment that would absorb Allied strength through most of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The compromise set a target date of 1 May 1944 for a landing from Britain with 29 divisions and let Eisenhower plan to eliminate Italy, but four American and three British divisions were to be ready to leave the Mediterranean from 1 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British official historian judged that events proved Marshall right, since the speed and stubbornness of the German reaction in Italy kept the argument over resources alive for a full year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the Cairo conference of November 1943 the British chiefs were again asking to adjust the Trident decisions, warning against treating a fixed invasion date as the pivot of all strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 126–45, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WD-Strategic2/USA-WD-Strategic2-6.html. [Open access]","Michael Howard, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 4, <i>August 1942–September 1943</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1972), 409–11, 431–33, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-4. [Open access]","“Meeting of the Combined Chiefs of Staff with Roosevelt and Churchill,” White House, 12 May 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington and Quebec, 1943</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1970), 24–32, document 29, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943/d29. [Open access]","“Resolutions by the Combined Chiefs of Staff: C.C.S. 237/1, European Operations,” Washington, 20 May 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington and Quebec, 1943</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1970), 281–82, document 97, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943/d97. [Open access]","John Ehrman, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 5, <i>August 1943–September 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1956), 6–7, 109–10, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-5. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The CBO Plan,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 348–68, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-11.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British want to go on fighting in the Mediterranean after Sicily?","a":"Because, as Churchill and the British chiefs argued, eliminating Italy would draw German forces away from the Soviet front and from the Balkans, and so ease the task of a later landing in northern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Marshall resist large operations in Italy?","a":"Because he feared that landing ground forces there would create a vacuum that swallowed the troops needed in Britain, committing the Allies to a Mediterranean policy through most of 1944 and delaying the defeat of both Germany and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the argument go on after the conference?","a":"Because the Germans reinforced Italy quickly and fought stubbornly, which kept Allied forces tied down there, and by November 1943 the British chiefs were asking to adjust the decisions taken in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Washington Conference (1943)","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Trident_Conference_-_DPLA_-_5a73335741da646a8a9d1e2c771b7765_%28page_1%29.jpg","title":"Trident Conference papers, May 1943","desc":"Title page of the bound papers and minutes of the Trident Conference, printed for the Combined Chiefs of Staff in 1943.","credit":"Office of the Combined Chiefs of Staff, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trident_Conference_-_DPLA_-_5a73335741da646a8a9d1e2c771b7765_(page_1).jpg","alt":"A typed title page reading Trident Conference, May 1943, Papers and Minutes of Meetings, with a declassification stamp."}},
    "ww2-603": {"questions":["Doolittle reckoned that winter weather favoured the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over bases in eastern England by two or three to one for high-level bombing of southern Germany.","The American official history answers Kesselring's case for defending all of Italy by pointing to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had already opened the southern Reich to Allied air attack."],"abstract":"The <b>Foggia airfields</b> were the cluster of airfields around the town of Foggia in south-eastern Italy, which the British Eighth Army occupied, abandoned by the Germans, on 27 September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American aviation engineers began building heavy bomber fields there in late October, laying runways of steel plank or paving up to about 1,800 metres (6,000 feet) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A fuel pipeline from the port of Manfredonia to Foggia, working by 25 November, could move about 600,000 litres (160,000 US gallons) of aviation petrol each week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 1 November 1943 the new American Fifteenth Air Force was activated under Doolittle to fly the strategic bombing offensive from Italian bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Doolittle argued that for high-level bombing in winter the weather favoured Foggia over the bases in eastern England by two or three to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Airfields in south-eastern Italy from which the Combined Bomber Offensive could be intensified were one of the arguments for invading the Italian mainland at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From there heavy bombers could reach the Balkans, Czechoslovakia, Austria and southern and eastern Germany, with the oil of Ploesti in Romania and the aircraft works of Wiener Neustadt in range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Strategic Bombing Survey found that renewed attacks on the Ploesti refineries from April 1944, together with the mining of the Danube, materially reduced Romanian oil deliveries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In its assessment of the campaign, the American official history uses the Foggia fields to rebut Kesselring's case for defending the whole peninsula, noting that they had already opened all of Italy and the southern Reich to air attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same history weighs the campaign's gains against some 312,000 Allied casualties between September 1943 and May 1945, and 434,646 German casualties by the German army's own count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Albert F. Simpson, “The Invasion of Italy,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 488–545, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-15.html. [Open access]","Albert F. Simpson, “The Fifteenth Air Force,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 546–66, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-16.html. [Open access]","Michael Howard, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 4, <i>August 1942–September 1943</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1972), 410–11, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-4. [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1977), 539–46, https://archive.org/details/CassinoToTheAlps. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 8, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies want airfields in south-eastern Italy?","a":"Because bombers based there could intensify the Combined Bomber Offensive and reach targets that were hard to hit from England, from the Balkans and Austria to southern and eastern Germany, including the oil of Ploesti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a new bomber force based in Italy rather than England?","a":"Because its commanders argued that winter weather over eastern Italy was better for high-level bombing than over eastern England, with fewer storms and less icing on the routes to southern Germany and the Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the official history doubt Kesselring's defence of the peninsula?","a":"Because one of his arguments was that giving up Italy would open southern Germany to air attack, and the Allies' Foggia fields had already opened all of Italy and the southern Reich to bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/15thafmap-foggia-italy.jpg","title":"Fifteenth Air Force bases around Foggia","desc":"A US Army Air Forces map of the Fifteenth Air Force's fighter and bomber bases around Foggia and Cerignola in south-eastern Italy.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:15thafmap-foggia-italy.jpg","alt":"A black and white map of the Adriatic coast of south-eastern Italy marking about two dozen airfields and the groups based at each."},"wiki":"Foggia Airfield Complex"},
    "ww2-604": {"questions":["Roosevelt pleaded in 1939 for civilians to be spared from the air, yet the RAF's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Germany grew from 16,000 tons of bombs in 1940 to 676,000 tons in 1944.","By the Bombing Survey's estimate, Allied <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed at least 305,000 people in Germany, a figure it called a minimum, and wounded some 780,000 more."],"abstract":"<b>Strategic bombing</b> is air attack on an enemy's industry, communications and cities, meant to wreck its war economy and the will of its people rather than to support armies on the battlefield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between the wars the American Air Corps Tactical School claimed a decisive, independent role for long-range bombing, ideas drawn from Billy Mitchell that had become an article of faith in the Air Corps by 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 1 September 1939 President Roosevelt appealed to the warring governments never to bomb civilian populations or unfortified cities from the air, warning that otherwise hundreds of thousands of innocent people would die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British air plans of the 1930s aimed at specific military targets, but in the war the RAF came to prefer night and area bombing and the Americans daylight precision attack, differences the American official history calls ones of method rather than principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In all, the Allied air forces in Europe dropped almost 2,700,000 tons of bombs and lost 79,265 American and 79,281 British airmen, by the Survey's preliminary figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The weight of the offensive came late, for the RAF dropped 16,000 tons in 1940 and 46,000 in 1941, against 676,000 in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In February 1942 the British Air Staff directed Bomber Command to focus on the morale of German civilians, and above all of industrial workers, with built-up areas as its aiming points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Survey estimated that air attack killed at least 305,000 people in Germany and wounded 780,000, a figure it called a minimum, and it judged the official German count too low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Britain the Bishop of Chichester told the House of Lords in February 1944 that obliterating a whole town because parts of it held military and industrial targets abandoned any fair balance between means and purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Survey concluded that Allied air power had been decisive in western Europe and had brought the German economy close to collapse, though it conceded that hindsight suggested it might have been used differently or better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “An Appeal to Great Britain, France, Italy, Germany, and Poland to Refrain from Air Bombing of Civilians,” 1 September 1939, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/appeal-great-britain-france-italy-germany-and-poland-refrain-from-air-bombing-civilians. [Open access]","James Lea Cate and Wesley Frank Craven, “The Army Air Arm between Two Wars, 1919–39,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 33–35, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-2.html. [Open access]","E. Kathleen Williams, “Air War, 1939–41,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 100, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-3.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 1, 3, 15–16, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>Preparation</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 322–24, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-1. [Open access]","House of Lords, “Bombing Policy,” <i>Parliamentary Debates</i>, 5th ser., vol. 130 (9 February 1944), cols 737–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1944/feb/09/bombing-policy. [Open access]","Randall Wakelam, “Bomber Harris and Precision Bombing – No Oxymoron Here,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 2–3, https://jmss.org/article/view/58013. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did British and American bombing methods differ?","a":"Because British experience had led the RAF to prefer night and area bombing, while the American air forces stressed daylight precision attack, although the American official history treats these as differences of method rather than of principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombing offensive reach its full weight so late?","a":"Because the forces were small at first: the RAF dropped only 16,000 tons of bombs in 1940 and 46,000 in 1941, against 676,000 in 1944, and in 1942 replacements for the young Eighth Air Force were diverted to North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How many people did the bombing kill in Germany?","a":"The Strategic Bombing Survey estimated at least 305,000 killed and 780,000 wounded, and called its own figure a minimum, after checking official German statistics against city records and finding them too low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Strategic bombing","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/B-17_raid_bombs_through_Frankfurt%2C_Germany_during_WWII.jpg","title":"B-17s bombing Frankfurt, 1944","desc":"American B-17 Flying Fortresses release their bombs over Frankfurt during a daylight raid in 1944.","credit":"Unknown author, United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B-17_raid_bombs_through_Frankfurt,_Germany_during_WWII.jpg","alt":"Three four-engined bombers in flight with long strings of bombs falling beneath them."}},
    "ww2-605": {"questions":["The official history counts 55,888 men of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed in action or on active service, 47,268 of them on operations, most of them aircrew.","On 3 nights in July and August 1943 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck Hamburg in what the Bombing Survey called perhaps the most devastating attack on a single city of the war."],"abstract":"<b>RAF Bomber Command</b> was the Royal Air Force command that controlled Britain's home-based bomber force, created when separate Bomber and Fighter Commands were set up in July 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first commander-in-chief, Sir John Steel, took office on 14 July 1936, and Sir Arthur Harris led it from 22 February 1942 for more than 3 years, to the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Driven to fly by night in 1940 to avoid prohibitive losses in daylight, its crews found that they often could not find their targets in the dark, and the command came to prefer night and area bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its bombing grew slowly, from 16,000 tons in 1940 and 46,000 in 1941 to 676,000 in 1944, and on 30 May 1942 it mounted its first raid of a thousand aircraft, against Cologne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An operational research section of scientists working beside its staff was set up in 1941 to get the most bombs on target for each aircraft lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> From August 1942 it worked beside the American Eighth Air Force, whose bomber commander Ira Eaker agreed to attend Harris's daily operations conference and to co-ordinate targets with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its night attacks on cities reached a peak in the Hamburg raids of July and August 1943, after which, the Bombing Survey found, it went on to destroy one major German urban centre after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method was attacked at home, notably by the Bishop of Chichester, who asked the House of Lords in February 1944 how bombers aiming blind could hit anything more than a great space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The official historians count 55,888 of its men killed in action or on active service, 47,268 of them on operations, a loss borne mostly by a relatively small force of aircrew from across the Commonwealth and allied nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They also cite a calculation that the British strategic air offensive absorbed on average 7 per cent of the manpower effort of the fighting services during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>Preparation</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 82–83, 494–95, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-1. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 286–87, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Alfred Goldberg, “Establishment of the Eighth Air Force in the United Kingdom,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 613–22, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-17.html. [Open access]","House of Lords, “Bombing Policy,” <i>Parliamentary Debates</i>, 5th ser., vol. 130 (9 February 1944), cols 737–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1944/feb/09/bombing-policy. [Open access]","Randall Wakelam, “Bomber Harris and Precision Bombing – No Oxymoron Here,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 3–6, https://jmss.org/article/view/58013. [Open access]","E. Kathleen Williams, “Air War, 1939–41,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 100, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Bomber Command turn to bombing by night?","a":"Because in 1940 night attacks were its only way of avoiding prohibitive losses in daylight, even though its crews then found that they often could not locate their targets in the dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the command set up an operational research section?","a":"Because its leaders wanted scientists working alongside the staff to measure its problems and find solutions, with a mandate to get the greatest weight of bombs on target for every aircraft lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Bomber Command's loss counted across many nations?","a":"Because of the 55,888 men killed, 38,792 were serving in the Royal Air Force, 9,913 in the Royal Canadian Air Force, 4,037 in the Royal Australian Air Force and 1,676 in the Royal New Zealand Air Force, with 928 Poles among the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"RAF Bomber Command","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/A_graphic_line-up_of_all_the_personnel_required_to_keep_one_Avro_Lancaster_of_RAF_Bomber_Command_flying_on_operations%2C_taken_at_Scampton%2C_Lincolnshire%2C_11_June_1942._CH15362.jpg","title":"Keeping one Lancaster flying, Scampton, 1942","desc":"An RAF photograph lining up all the people and equipment needed to keep one Avro Lancaster of Bomber Command on operations, taken at Scampton on 11 June 1942.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_graphic_line-up_of_all_the_personnel_required_to_keep_one_Avro_Lancaster_of_RAF_Bomber_Command_flying_on_operations,_taken_at_Scampton,_Lincolnshire,_11_June_1942._CH15362.jpg","alt":"Rows of airmen, a fuel tanker, a tractor and bomb trolleys arranged in front of a four-engined bomber on an airfield."}},
    "ww2-606": {"questions":["Portal minuted the day after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was issued that its aiming points were to be the built-up areas, not the dockyards or aircraft factories named in its lists.","The arrival of the Gee radio navigation aid, which the Air Staff called a revolutionary advance, was the stated reason for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that sent Bomber Command back to full effort."],"abstract":"The <b>Area Bombing Directive</b> was the Air Ministry instruction of 14 February 1942 that told RAF Bomber Command to make the morale of the German civilian population, and in particular of industrial workers, the primary object of its operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Signed by Air Vice-Marshal N. H. Bottomley, Deputy Chief of the Air Staff, it reached Bomber Command 8 days before Arthur Harris took command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended the conservation of the force, justifying full effort by the arrival of a radio navigation aid, codenamed TR 1335 and known as Gee, which the Air Staff called a revolutionary advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its lists named industrial areas within Gee's range, chiefly in the Ruhr with Essen as the first target, and added Berlin for harassing raids meant to maintain the fear of attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next day the Chief of the Air Staff, Sir Charles Portal, minuted that the aiming points were to be the built-up areas, not the dockyards or aircraft factories mentioned in the lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It followed months in which night bombing had proved so inaccurate that selective, precise attack at night was judged impracticable, experience that led the RAF to prefer night and area bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In March 1942 Lord Cherwell argued to Churchill that if half the bombs of 10,000 bombers fell on the built-up areas of 58 German towns, most of their people would lose their homes, which he believed would break their spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official historians note that the directive was not superseded until February 1943, and that it still left room for attacks on particular industries and for a return to precision bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the years that followed the RAF went on, in the words of the Bombing Survey, to destroy one major German urban centre after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Critics such as the Bishop of Chichester, speaking in the Lords in February 1944, called the result a policy of obliteration, while the government replied that the offensive was planned to end the war at the earliest possible moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>Preparation</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 178–80, 322–24, 331–32, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-1. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Annexes and Appendices</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 143–46, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-4. [Open access]","Randall Wakelam, “Bomber Harris and Precision Bombing – No Oxymoron Here,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 3–5, https://jmss.org/article/view/58013. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","House of Lords, “Bombing Policy,” <i>Parliamentary Debates</i>, 5th ser., vol. 130 (9 February 1944), cols 737–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1944/feb/09/bombing-policy. [Open access]","E. Kathleen Williams, “Air War, 1939–41,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 100, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the directive aim at morale rather than at precise targets?","a":"Because night bombing in 1941 had proved so inaccurate that selective, precise attack at night was judged impracticable, and those problems helped produce a directive aimed at the population of whole industrial areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the arrival of Gee matter so much?","a":"Because the Air Staff expected the device to let crews concentrate on a target as never before, but thought the Germans would counter it within about 6 months, so the directive demanded maximum effort while it lasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Portal add a minute to the directive?","a":"Because its target lists mentioned each town's industries, and he wanted it understood that the aiming points were the built-up areas themselves, not the dockyards or aircraft factories named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Area bombing directive","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Aerial_photograph_taken_during_RAF_Bomber_Command%27s_raid_on_the_North_German_port_of_Lubeck_on_the_night_of_28-29_March_1942._C2364.jpg","title":"Lübeck burning, 28–29 March 1942","desc":"A vertical photograph taken during Bomber Command's night raid on Lübeck, with incendiary fires burning in the old town.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photograph_taken_during_RAF_Bomber_Command's_raid_on_the_North_German_port_of_Lubeck_on_the_night_of_28-29_March_1942._C2364.jpg","alt":"A night aerial photograph of city streets partly hidden by bright streaks of fire and light."}},
    "ww2-607": {"questions":["In Mesopotamia in the 1920s the squadron commander <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fitted bomb racks to Vickers Vernon transports and trained their crews to bomb precisely and by night.","Portal wrote to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in January 1945 that only the end of the war would show which of them had been right about the attack on German oil."],"abstract":"<b>Arthur Harris</b> was the British air officer who commanded RAF Bomber Command from February 1942 to the end of the war in Europe, and became the figure most associated with the bombing of German cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born in Cheltenham in 1892, he spent his youth in Rhodesia, returned to England in 1915 and flew in the First World War, ending it as a major commanding a squadron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Mesopotamia in the 1920s he turned the Vickers Vernon transports of 45 Squadron into bombers and trained their crews in precision and night bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Commanding 5 Group from September 1939, he saw how often crews failed to find their targets, and in 1940 Sir Edgar Ludlow-Hewitt praised his exceptionally alert, creative and enterprising mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took over at High Wycombe on 22 February 1942, 8 days after the directive that made German civilian morale the primary target, and for more than 3 years he drove the area offensive against German cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 30 May 1942 Bomber Command sent its first raid of a thousand aircraft against Cologne, and after Hamburg in 1943 it destroyed one major German urban centre after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Of Berlin he spoke of bombing until the heart of Nazi Germany ceased to beat, words the Bishop of Chichester quoted to the House of Lords in February 1944 when he condemned area bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He worked closely with the American bomber commander Ira Eaker, who from 1942 attended Harris's daily operations conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the winter of 1944 to 1945 he rejected the Air Staff's plan to concentrate on oil as another panacea and asked Portal whether he should remain in command, but Portal kept him, writing on 20 January 1945 that only the end of the war would show who had been right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 28 March 1945 Churchill minuted that bombing German cities simply to increase the terror should be reviewed, which the official historians judge unfair after 4 years in which he had encouraged area attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aksel Schreiner Brakestad, “Arthur Travers Harris,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, last updated 24 February 2025, https://snl.no/Arthur_Travers_Harris. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>Preparation</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 323, 494–95, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-1. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 85–87, 92–93, 112, 286, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","Randall Wakelam, “Bomber Harris and Precision Bombing – No Oxymoron Here,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 4–6, https://jmss.org/article/view/58013. [Open access]","Alfred Goldberg, “Establishment of the Eighth Air Force in the United Kingdom,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 622, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-17.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","House of Lords, “Bombing Policy,” <i>Parliamentary Debates</i>, 5th ser., vol. 130 (9 February 1944), cols 737–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1944/feb/09/bombing-policy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Harris oppose the plan to bomb German oil?","a":"Because he believed the economic experts always overstated their case for such panaceas, as with ball-bearings, and he preferred to finish the general area offensive against German cities that he thought was largely complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Portal not replace him in January 1945?","a":"Because Harris had greater prestige than any other commander in the Royal Air Force, and the official historians judge that removing him at that stage would have been a remedy worse than the disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some historians question his image as an enemy of precision bombing?","a":"Because in the 1920s he had trained his own squadrons in precision and night bombing, his superiors praised his technical imagination, and his command used scientific operational research to improve accuracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Arthur Harris","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Air_Chief_Marshal_Sir_Arthur_Harris.jpg","title":"Sir Arthur Harris at High Wycombe, 1944","desc":"Air Chief Marshal Sir Arthur Harris at his desk at Bomber Command headquarters, High Wycombe, on 24 April 1944.","credit":"Fg Off Stannus, Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Air_Chief_Marshal_Sir_Arthur_Harris.jpg","alt":"A man in RAF uniform seated at a desk, holding a cigarette and looking at the camera."}},
    "ww2-608": {"questions":["Churchill called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a very serious paper requiring Portal's most urgent attention, after it showed how few night bombers reached the Ruhr.","By showing that the first problem of night bombing was navigation, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of August 1941 rendered Bomber Command a service second to none, its official historians judged."],"abstract":"The <b>Butt Report</b> was a study of the accuracy of Britain's night bombing, dated 18 August 1941, in which D. M. Butt of the War Cabinet secretariat analysed photographs taken by Bomber Command's aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was undertaken on the initiative of Lord Cherwell, one of several people outside the Air Ministry who doubted how accurate the bombing was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Butt examined some 633 photographs, given in the report as 650, taken during raids between 2 June and 25 July 1941, together with operational summaries and other records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He concluded that of the aircraft recorded as having attacked their targets, only one in three had got within 8 kilometres (5 miles) of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over the Ruhr the figure fell to one in ten and on moonless nights to one in fifteen, and of all sorties despatched only one in five came that close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the time the RAF's whole bombing effort was small, 46,000 tons in 1941 against 676,000 in 1944, and it had turned to night operations in 1940 to escape heavy losses by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Senior officers at Bomber Command objected that the weather had been unusually bad and the sample too small, but their objections did not blunt the report's impact in high places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill called it a very serious paper requiring Portal's most urgent attention, and Portal agreed that improving night bombing was perhaps the greatest operational problem the air force faced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official historians judge that by showing the first problem to be navigation it rendered Bomber Command a service second to none, hastening scientific aids and new tactics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also made plain that precise attack on chosen targets at night was impracticable, which helped push British bombing towards the area attacks of the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>Preparation</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 178–80, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-1. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Annexes and Appendices</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 205–06, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-4. [Open access]","Randall Wakelam, “Bomber Harris and Precision Bombing – No Oxymoron Here,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 3–4, https://jmss.org/article/view/58013. [Open access]","E. Kathleen Williams, “Air War, 1939–41,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 100, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-3.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Bomber Command's officers dispute the report?","a":"Because they argued that the weather in June and July had been unusually bad, that photographs of one sortie in ten were too small a sample, and that squadron commanders gave the cameras to the crews they trusted least.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the report matter so much despite its flaws?","a":"Because the figures were striking enough to convince Churchill and Portal that the problem was urgent, and they showed that bombers which could not find a target within 8 kilometres (5 miles) could hardly aim at a factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the report recommend?","a":"It recommended checking its results against day photographs and keeping continuous statistical records of night photographs, with a trained statistician and clerical staff to maintain them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Butt Report","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/A_vertical_aerial_photograph_taken_during_a_raid_on_Berlin_on_the_night_of_2-3_September_1941._The_broad_wavy_lines_are_the_tracks_of_German_searchlights_and_anti-aircraft_fire._C2056.jpg","title":"Night photograph over Berlin, September 1941","desc":"A photograph taken by a bomber during a night raid on Berlin on 2–3 September 1941, of the kind the Butt Report analysed; the wavy lines are searchlights and anti-aircraft fire.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_vertical_aerial_photograph_taken_during_a_raid_on_Berlin_on_the_night_of_2-3_September_1941._The_broad_wavy_lines_are_the_tracks_of_German_searchlights_and_anti-aircraft_fire._C2056.jpg","alt":"A night aerial photograph of city streets crossed by bright wavy streaks of light."}},
    "ww2-609": {"questions":["German fighter strength became the intermediate objective of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in June 1943, because the growth of the enemy's fighter force threatened the bombers themselves.","Marshall told the American chiefs in May 1943 that without the air bombardment of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an invasion across the Channel would be a visionary matter."],"abstract":"The <b>Combined Bomber Offensive</b> was the joint British and American bombing campaign against Germany from bases in the United Kingdom, set out in a directive the Combined Chiefs of Staff approved at Casablanca on 21 January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That directive gave both bomber forces one primary object: the progressive destruction and dislocation of the German military, industrial and economic system and the undermining of German morale until the capacity for armed resistance was fatally weakened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first list of priorities ran from submarine construction yards through the aircraft industry, transport and oil to other war industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Casablanca the Eighth Air Force's commander, Ira Eaker, defended daylight bombing against pressure, apparently led by Churchill, to move the Americans to night operations, arguing that day and night attacks together could keep the enemy under pressure around the clock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A plan worked out under Eaker and approved by the Combined Chiefs in May 1943, during the Trident conference, named 76 targets in six industrial systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Pointblank directive of 10 June 1943 gave first priority to German fighter strength as an intermediate objective, because the growth of the enemy's fighter force threatened to leave the bombers unable to carry out their tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same directive left Bomber Command to pursue its main aim, the general disorganisation of German industry, asking only that its attacks complement the Americans' as far as practicable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Marshall told the American chiefs during the Trident conference that without the air bombardment of the coming year, a cross-Channel invasion would be a visionary matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official history of grand strategy observes that through 1943 the combination of the two air forces was still far from complete in practice, while American daylight losses rose in the summer and autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Strategic Bombing Survey later traced the air forces' objective to Casablanca and concluded that Allied air power had been decisive in the war in western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Memorandum by the Combined Chiefs of Staff: C.C.S. 166/1/D, The Bomber Offensive from the United Kingdom,” Casablanca, 21 January 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1958), document 412, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d412. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The Casablanca Directive,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 274–307, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-9.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The CBO Plan,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 348–68, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-11.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Annexes and Appendices</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 158–59, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-4. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 5, 15–16, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Maurice Matloff, <i>Strategic Planning for Coalition Warfare, 1943–1944</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 130–31, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-WD-Strategic2/USA-WD-Strategic2-6.html. [Open access]","John Ehrman, <i>Grand Strategy</i>, vol. 5, <i>August 1943–September 1944</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1956), 6–7, https://archive.org/details/grand-strategy-vol-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did German fighters become the first priority in June 1943?","a":"Because the British night offensive and the American day raids had forced Germany to build up its fighter forces in the west, and unless that growth was checked the bombers might be unable to carry out any of their tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Eaker refuse to switch the Americans to night bombing?","a":"Because his losses by day had been lower than the RAF's by night, his crews were neither trained nor equipped for night flying, and he estimated that daylight bombing was about five times as accurate, so the two methods could complement each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the offensive only loosely combined?","a":"Because Bomber Command kept its own main aim of disorganising German industry and was asked only to complement the Americans where practicable, and the British official history notes that the combination was still far from complete in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Combined Bomber Offensive","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/1943_CCS_166_1_D.jpg","title":"The Casablanca directive, 21 January 1943","desc":"The printed text of C.C.S. 166/1/D, the Combined Chiefs of Staff directive that launched the Combined Bomber Offensive.","credit":"Combined Chiefs of Staff, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1943_CCS_166_1_D.jpg","alt":"Two facing pages of typed text headed Combined Chiefs of Staff and The Bomber Offensive from the United Kingdom."}},
    "ww2-610": {"questions":["Ira Eaker set up the bomber headquarters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a hurriedly evacuated girls' school at High Wycombe, about 50 kilometres (30 miles) west of London.","After the second attack on Schweinfurt in October 1943 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> halted unescorted deep raids into Germany, and in December Eaker was moved to the Mediterranean."],"abstract":"The <b>Eighth Air Force</b> was the United States Army Air Forces command that carried the American daylight bombing offensive against Germany from bases in England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its headquarters was activated at Savannah Army Air Base in Georgia on 28 January 1942, and its VIII Bomber Command at Langley Field, Virginia, on 1 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ira Eaker reached England with six other officers on 20 February 1942 and set up his bomber headquarters in a hurriedly evacuated girls' school at High Wycombe, about 50 kilometres (30 miles) west of London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first heavy bomber mission was flown on 17 August 1942 by twelve B-17s against the railway yards and workshops of Rouen-Sotteville in France, with Eaker in the lead bomber of the second flight and RAF Spitfires as escort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its growth was soon slowed when replacements were diverted to support the invasion of North Africa, and the Pointblank directive of June 1943 made German fighter strength its first objective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Its leaders held to daylight precision bombing, and at Casablanca in January 1943 Eaker argued that his losses by day were lower than the RAF's by night and that his bombsight gave about five times the accuracy of the best night bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eaker had agreed with Harris to attend Bomber Command's daily operations conference and to co-ordinate targets and press releases, the start of a close working partnership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deep raids without fighter escort in 1943 proved costly, and within a week of the second attack on Schweinfurt on 14 October the Eighth halted unescorted deep penetrations into Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In December 1943 Arnold and Spaatz moved Eaker to command the air forces in the Mediterranean and gave the Eighth to Doolittle, a change Eaker called heartbreaking just before the climax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spaatz returned to England to command the new United States Strategic Air Forces in Europe, which controlled the operations of both the Eighth and the Italian-based Fifteenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alfred Goldberg, “Establishment of the Eighth Air Force in the United Kingdom,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 613–22, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-17.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Rouen-Sotteville No. 1, 17 August 1942,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Plans and Early Operations, January 1939 to August 1942</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 655–63, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/I/AAF-I-18.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The Casablanca Directive,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 300–302, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-9.html. [Open access]","Alfred Goldberg and Albert F. Simpson, “Final Reorganization,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 733–56, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-22.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i>, 30 September 1945 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1946), 3, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Lee Lacy, “Command Decision: Leadership Lessons from the Strategic Air War Against Germany,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 16, no. 4 (2016): 108–10, https://jmss.org/article/view/58159. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Annexes and Appendices</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 158–59, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-4. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Eighth Air Force insist on bombing by day?","a":"Because Eaker argued that its losses by day had been lower than the RAF's at night, that its crews were not trained for night flying, and that daylight let its bombers hit small targets such as single factories that could not be found in the dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Eighth stop deep raids in October 1943?","a":"Because unescorted missions deep into Germany, above all the second attack on Schweinfurt, had cost so many bombers and crews that the commanders concluded air superiority was needed before daylight bombing could go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Eaker replaced in December 1943?","a":"Because Arnold and Spaatz wanted a commander experienced in dealing with the British for the new Mediterranean air command, so Eaker went there and Doolittle took over the Eighth under Spaatz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Eighth Air Force","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Boeing_B-17F-25-BO_Fortress_42-24577_Hells_Angels.jpg","title":"The B-17 Hell's Angels of the 303rd Bomb Group","desc":"Air and ground crew on the B-17F Hell's Angels of the 303rd Bombardment Group, an Eighth Air Force bomber flown from Molesworth in England.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_B-17F-25-BO_Fortress_42-24577_Hells_Angels.jpg","alt":"About two dozen airmen sitting on and around the nose of a four-engined bomber with Hells Angels painted on its side."}},
    "ww2-611": {"questions":["Prewar boasts of 'pickle barrel' accuracy for American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> faded over Europe, where only about a fifth of the bombs aimed at such targets landed near the aiming point.","At Casablanca in January 1943 Ira Eaker defended daylight <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against pressure to join the night offensive, claiming it was about five times as accurate as the best night bombing."],"abstract":"<b>Precision bombing</b>, the doctrine of the American air forces, called for destroying carefully chosen targets such as single factories by daylight attack from high altitude, while the British bombed by night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the war the Americans had trained a few crews to a very high standard on bombing ranges, giving rise to boasts of 'pin point' and 'pickle barrel' bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Royal Air Force had abandoned daylight raids on German industry in 1940 because of heavy losses, then failed to find such targets at night and turned to raids on cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the first American heavy bombers struck the Rouen-Sotteville marshalling yard in August 1942, many in the Royal Air Force were politely sceptical that the method would work over Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A joint directive of September 1942 gave night bombing to the Royal Air Force and day bombing to the Americans, so that the offensive against the Axis would be continuous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under pressure, apparently led by the Prime Minister, to join the night campaign, the American commander Ira Eaker argued in January 1943 that day bombing was about five times as accurate and that the two methods together would press the enemy around the clock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history judged that the Americans held to their theory more rigidly than Bomber Command, which had moved from day to night and from precision to area attack as experience dictated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Australian official history notes that the breaching of the Ruhr dams in May 1943 undercut British calls to abandon area bombing for the American theory of daylight precision attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In practice the Bombing Survey found that only about a fifth of the bombs aimed at precision targets fell within about 300 metres (1,000 feet) of the aiming point, and a critic in the Commons doubted the claimed accuracy altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Washington asked in February 1945 whether a new directive allowed indiscriminate attacks on cities, Carl Spaatz replied that his forces were attacking transport facilities inside cities, not the cities themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur B. Ferguson, “The Daylight Bombing Experiment,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 211–13, 225–26, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-7.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “The Casablanca Directive,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 300–02, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-9.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 2–4, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 38–39, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","John E. Fagg, “The Climax of Strategic Operations,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 724–25, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-20.html. [Open access]","John Herington, “The Radar Bombing Offensive, 1943,” in <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), 495, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417797. [Open access]","<i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons Debates, 6 March 1945, vol. 408, cols. 1898–1901, “Air Estimates, 1945,” https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/mar/06/air-estimates-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Royal Air Force bomb at night rather than by day?","a":"Because its daylight raids on German industrial targets in 1940 were abandoned when losses proved unbearably heavy, and when it then failed to find such targets often enough at night it turned to attacks on German cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans refuse to switch their bombers to night operations?","a":"Because Eaker argued that their crews were neither equipped nor trained for night work, that converting them would cause long delays, and that only day bombers could find and hit small targets such as individual factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did real accuracy fall so far short of the prewar claims?","a":"Because cloud, smoke screens and industrial haze hid targets, enemy fighters forced the bombers into tight defensive formations, and anti-aircraft fire kept bombing runs short, so most of each bomb pattern spilled onto neighbouring ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Precision bombing","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/DDayMuseumNordenBombsight1.jpg","title":"Norden bombsight","desc":"A Norden bombsight, the instrument American bombardiers used for daylight precision bombing, on display at the National WWII Museum in New Orleans.","credit":"Infrogmation of New Orleans, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DDayMuseumNordenBombsight1.jpg","alt":"A bulky black optical instrument with dials and knobs mounted on a wooden stand inside a glass display case, with a printed label in front."}},
    "ww2-612": {"questions":["Bomber Command first used Window, a countermeasure that threw German radar into confusion, on the opening night of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1943, losing only twelve aircraft.","Speer later said he had warned Hitler that more raids like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of July 1943 might bring a rapid end to the war, so alarmed were German officials."],"abstract":"The <b>bombing of Hamburg</b> in the Second World War reached its climax in the summer of 1943, when British night raids and American day raids over 10 days burned out much of Germany's second-largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its shipyards built about 45 per cent of Germany's U-boats, and its closeness to the sea made it easy to find on the new H2S radar, though since January 1942 Bomber Command had lost 5.4 per cent of its aircraft on each raid there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The memorial at the ruined church of Saint Nikolai, whose tower the bombers used as a landmark, sets the raids beside the German bombs that fell first on Guernica, Wieluń, Warsaw, Rotterdam and Coventry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The attack, which the Allied files named Operation Gomorrah, opened on the night of 24 July 1943, the same night that Bomber Command first used Window, a countermeasure that confused German radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only twelve bombers failed to return that night, as searchlights raked the sky aimlessly and the German night fighter controllers lost track of the stream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> American Flying Fortresses bombed the U-boat yards by day on 25 and 26 July, and on the night of 27 July a force of 787 British bombers returned to the burning city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tens of thousands of small fires then merged into a firestorm of typhoon-like intensity, a German report said, and the worst destruction fell on the residential districts east of the Alster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Further raids on 29 July and 2 August completed the devastation at a cost to Bomber Command of 86 aircraft from 3,095 sorties, and nearly a million people fled the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dead are counted at about 34,000 by the Saint Nikolai memorial and about 43,000 in the figure the British official historians took from the American survey, which also quoted German estimates of 60,000 to 100,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Speer told interrogators that he had warned Hitler a rapid end of the war might follow if such attacks continued, though no later city raid had the same shock effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 151–56, 236, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 2–3, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Förderkreis Mahnmal St. Nikolai, “Geschichte,” Mahnmal St. Nikolai, Hamburg, https://www.mahnmal-st-nikolai.de/geschichte/. [in German] [Open access]","Förderkreis Mahnmal St. Nikolai, “80 Jahre ‘Operation Gomorrha’,” Mahnmal St. Nikolai, Hamburg, 2023, https://www.mahnmal-st-nikolai.de/80-jahre-operation-gomorrha/. [in German] [Open access]","John Herington, “Hamburg – The Last Raids on Italy – The Struggle against Night Fighters,” in <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), 587–88, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417800. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Pointblank,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 677, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-20.html. [Open access]","Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 6–7, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were British losses so low on the first night?","a":"Because it was the first operation in which Window was used, and it threw the German defences into confusion: searchlights wandered aimlessly, guns were hampered and fighter controllers could not direct their pilots, so only twelve of the bombers were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the firestorm kill so many people?","a":"Because tens of thousands of separate fires merged into one storm of typhoon-like force, and in such storms most deaths came not from burns but from carbon monoxide poisoning in cellars and shelters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the death tolls for Hamburg differ so widely?","a":"Because many records were destroyed and counting was difficult, so the official German figure of about 43,000 was doubted at the time, German estimates quoted by the American survey ran from 60,000 to 100,000, and the city's memorial now gives about 34,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bombing of Hamburg in World War II","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Royal_Air_Force_Bomber_Command%2C_1942-1945._CL3400.jpg","title":"Burnt-out Hamburg after the firestorm","desc":"Rows of gutted houses in Hamburg-Eilbek, burnt out in the firestorm of 27–28 July 1943, photographed from the air after the war.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Air_Force_Bomber_Command,_1942-1945._CL3400.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of streets lined with roofless, burnt-out apartment blocks, with a park of trees beyond them."}},
    "ww2-613": {"questions":["In 43 major attacks during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of spring 1943, Bomber Command lost 872 aircraft while devastating Wuppertal, Düsseldorf, Duisburg and the Krupp works at Essen.","Speer set up a special staff to repair the damage of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1943, and Hitler agreed that the region's heavy industry was the one base that could not be evacuated."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Ruhr</b> was the name given to Bomber Command's night offensive from March to July 1943, which centred on the Ruhr, the largest region of heavy industry and coal-mining in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its cities had long been hard to hit, since Essen had no prominent landmark and could hardly be told apart from the rest of a built-up valley usually covered by industrial haze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During 1942 a tenth of Bomber Command's whole effort had gone against Essen with very little success, and the Krupp works at its centre were virtually untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What changed in 1943 was Oboe, a British blind-bombing aid steered by beams from ground stations, which was accurate but reached only about 320 kilometres (200 miles) and was kept for fast Mosquitoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The battle opened on the night of 5 March 1943, when Oboe Mosquitoes marked Essen for a force of 442 bombers, of which fourteen failed to return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 43 major attacks up to the raid on Aachen on 13 July, Bomber Command despatched some 18,506 sorties and lost 872 aircraft, about 4.7 per cent, with over 2,000 more damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the most destructive raids fell on the Barmen district of Wuppertal on the night of 29 May, when about 2,450 people were killed and 118,000 left homeless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American bombers made their own first large daylight penetration of the Ruhr on 22 June, shutting the synthetic rubber plant at Hüls for a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speer took charge of repairs, set up a special Ruhr staff and brought workers from the Atlantic Wall, while Hitler agreed that the Ruhr was the one base of German industry that could not be evacuated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet the British official historians judged the damage serious but in no sense crippling, the American survey found that production in bombed cities recovered within weeks, and Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that output kept rising until the autumn of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 108–10, 236, 257–58, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Denis Richards and Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Fight Avails</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 283–91, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]","John Herington, “The Radar Bombing Offensive, 1943,” in <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), 479–80, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417797. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 2–3, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Pointblank,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 671, 690–91, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-20.html. [Open access]","Testimony of Albert Speer, 20 June 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, Nuremberg, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had Essen escaped serious damage before 1943?","a":"Because it had no prominent landmark, lay some distance from the Rhine and merged into the almost continuous built-up area of the Ruhr valley under a haze of industrial smoke, so night crews rarely found it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Oboe not guide every raid on Germany?","a":"Because it worked on beams sent from ground stations in England, which limited it to about 320 kilometres (200 miles), and the British, short of sets and afraid the enemy would learn its secret, kept it for their fast Mosquitoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the battle less decisive than it seemed at the time?","a":"Because British estimates overrated the importance of the Krupp works, which by 1943 mattered more for development than for volume, and because production in bombed cities recovered within weeks while German armament output as a whole kept rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of the Ruhr","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/57/Essen%2C_Royal_Air_Force_Bomber_Command%2C_1942-1945_CL2383.jpg","title":"The wrecked Krupp works at Essen","desc":"RAF officers inspect an unfinished gun barrel in a wrecked workshop of the Krupp armaments works at Essen in 1945.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Essen,_Royal_Air_Force_Bomber_Command,_1942-1945_CL2383.jpg","alt":"A huge gun barrel lies across a factory floor strewn with debris under a twisted steel roof frame, with a man standing beside it."}},
    "ww2-614": {"questions":["Special bombs designed by Barnes Wallis had to be released from exactly 18 metres (60 feet) during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the low-level night attack that breached the Möhne and Eder dams.","Over half of the roughly 1,200 people drowned or missing after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were forced labourers from the East, swept away when the breached Möhne dam flooded the Ruhr valley."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Chastise</b> was the code name of the Royal Air Force raid of 16–17 May 1943 in which 617 Squadron attacked dams above the Ruhr valley at low level with special bombs, a raid popularly known as the Dam Busters raid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Möhne dam controlled the level of the river Ruhr and held over 130 million tons of water, which supplied pumping stations and power plants in the most industrialised region of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its masonry wall was about 34 metres (112 feet) thick at the base, too strong for any ordinary bomb, so the weapon came from the engineer Barnes Wallis, who had great difficulty winning official belief in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each bomb had to be released from exactly 18 metres (60 feet) at a set speed, so the crews trained for 6 weeks in low flying over lakes and dams in England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nineteen Lancasters set out in three waves against the Möhne, the Eder and the Sorpe, with smaller dams held as reserve targets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Led by Guy Gibson, who circled the Möhne to draw the defenders' fire from the aircraft attacking after him, the first wave breached it and then the Eder, while the earth-walled Sorpe held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eight of the nineteen Lancasters failed to return, and Gibson's Victoria Cross was announced in the <i>London Gazette</i> of 28 May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 1,200 people were reported drowned or missing in the floods, and over half of them were forced labourers from the East who had been held in a labour camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Neheim alone, a witness who helped recover the bodies recalled, 712 of the 859 dead were foreign workers, and an exhibition in Arnsberg has since commemorated the women of the Möhnewiesen camp who died that night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official historians judged the effect on the German war economy local, temporary and largely agricultural, though Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that 50,000 workers were taken from the Atlantic Wall to repair the dams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 168–79, 290–91, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Denis Richards and Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Fight Avails</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 291–95, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]","John Herington, “The Radar Bombing Offensive, 1943,” in <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), 492–95, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417797. [Open access]","“Air Ministry, 28th May, 1943,” supplement to <i>The London Gazette</i>, no. 36030, 28 May 1943, 2361, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/36030/supplement/2361. [Open access]","“Möhne Dam Fragment,” object record, Royal Air Force Museum, London, https://collections.rafmuseum.org.uk/collection/object/object-158247. [Open access]","Bezirksregierung Arnsberg, “Die vergessenen Opfer der Möhne-Katastrophe,” press release, 2018, https://www.bra.nrw.de/presse/die-vergessenen-opfer-der-moehne-katastrophe. [in German] [Open access]","Werner Brähler, “Die Bombardierung der Möhnetalsperre 1943,” eyewitness account, February 2010, LeMO Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/werner-braehler-die-bombardierung-der-moehnetalsperre-1943. [in German] [Open access]","Testimony of Albert Speer, 21 June 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, Nuremberg, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bombs have to be released from such a low height?","a":"Because a bomb dropped from too high tended to break up on hitting the water, one dropped too soon spent its force before reaching the wall, and one dropped too late could strike the dam too hard, so it had to fall from exactly 18 metres (60 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Sorpe dam survive?","a":"Because it was built differently, with long earthen banks on either side of a concrete core, no suitable bomb existed for it, and most of the aircraft sent against it were lost or turned back, so only two attacked and the wall held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many forced labourers die in the flood?","a":"Because their camp lay in the path of the water and the warnings failed: the police at Neheim received the alarm, but most people were in air-raid shelters and could not be told in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Chastise","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Mohne_Dam_Breached.jpg","title":"The breached Möhne dam, 17 May 1943","desc":"The Möhne dam the morning after the raid, photographed by Flying Officer Jerry Fray from a reconnaissance Spitfire, with water pouring through the breach.","credit":"Jerry Fray, Royal Air Force, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohne_Dam_Breached.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a long masonry dam with a wide gap in its centre, water flooding through into the valley below, and barrage balloons over the lake."}},
    "ww2-615": {"questions":["Sixty American heavy bombers, sixteen per cent of those despatched, were shot down during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a double strike on a fighter factory and Germany's main centre of bearing production.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> damaged their bearing plants in August 1943, the Germans turned to substitute bearings and supplies from Sweden and Switzerland, and were never seriously short of them."],"abstract":"The <b>Schweinfurt–Regensburg mission</b> of 17 August 1943 was a double raid by the United States Eighth Air Force on the ball-bearing plants at Schweinfurt and the Messerschmitt aircraft complex at Regensburg, the two most critical targets on its list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It fell on the first anniversary of American heavy-bomber operations from Britain, and its 376 Flying Fortresses made up the largest force the Eighth had yet sent out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plants around Schweinfurt then produced about half of Germany's anti-friction bearings, which Allied planners assumed every branch of war production could not do without.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Regensburg force, carrying long-range fuel tanks, was to bomb and fly on to bases in North Africa, while the Schweinfurt force was to return to England along its outward route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bad weather held the Schweinfurt force back by 3.5 hours, so the two formations crossed Germany separately and both met almost continuous fighter attack, pressed home with the utmost intensity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sixty bombers were shot down, 36 from the Regensburg force and 24 from the Schweinfurt force, which was 16 per cent of those despatched and 19 per cent of those that attacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Regensburg the bombs damaged every important building in the plant, while at Schweinfurt they cut output in the ball department of one works from 140 tons in July to 50 in September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Germans answered by finding substitute bearings and buying more from Sweden and Switzerland, and at no time during the war were they seriously short of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British had planned to follow the raid with a night attack by Bomber Command, but it was not ordered, and Harris argued that Schweinfurt was too small and too well defended to find at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official historians saw in the losses the probable fate of any unescorted force sent so deep, and the Bombing Survey found no evidence that the attacks on bearings had any measurable effect on essential war production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur B. Ferguson, “Pointblank,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 681–86, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-20.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 4–5, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 39–40, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Big Week,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 44, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","“Track Chart of Bombing Mission to Schweinfurt,” 17 August 1943, 306th Bombardment Group Operations, Records of the Army Air Forces, Record Group 18, National Archives at College Park, National Archives Identifier 596346, DocsTeach, https://docsteach.org/documents/document/track-chart-of-bombing-mission-to-schweinfurt. [Open access]","Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 24–25, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Schweinfurt and Regensburg attacked on the same day?","a":"Because they were the two most critical targets on the Eighth Air Force's list: Schweinfurt made about half of Germany's bearings, and Regensburg was one of the great Messerschmitt fighter complexes, so a simultaneous blow was meant to strike both before their defences could react.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Regensburg force fly on to North Africa?","a":"Because its long-range tanks let it continue south instead of fighting its way home, and the move seems to have surprised the Luftwaffe, which probably spared it even heavier losses than if it had returned to England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the damage to the bearing plants not cripple German production?","a":"Because the Germans switched to simpler or more plentiful types of bearing, bought finished stock from Sweden and Switzerland, and drew on large stocks already in the pipeline, so planners had overrated how vulnerable the industry was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Schweinfurt–Regensburg mission","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/B-17F_formation_over_Schweinfurt%2C_Germany%2C_August_17%2C_1943.jpg","title":"B-17s over Schweinfurt, 17 August 1943","desc":"Flying Fortresses of the Eighth Air Force over Schweinfurt during the raid of 17 August 1943, with smoke rising from the target below.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B-17F_formation_over_Schweinfurt,_Germany,_August_17,_1943.jpg","alt":"Four-engined bombers fly in formation high above a patchwork of fields and a town partly hidden by smoke."}},
    "ww2-616": {"questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended a week in which 148 American bombers were lost, the Eighth Air Force made no further deep penetrations into Germany in clear weather for the rest of 1943.","Germany's bearing industry suffered its heaviest damage of the war in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, yet it was dispersed so successfully that output had recovered by the autumn of 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Schweinfurt raid</b> of 14 October 1943 was an attack by the United States Eighth Air Force on the ball-bearing plants at Schweinfurt that cost it sixty Flying Fortresses and their crews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came at the end of a week in which the Eighth had sent four unescorted missions deep into Germany, and in those 6 days it lost 148 bombers and crews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American doctrine held that heavily armed bombers in close formation could defend themselves, and a 1940 Air Corps study had concluded that no fighter could escort bombers to their full radius and still fight on equal terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Thunderbolt escort turned back near Aachen, about 390 kilometres (240 miles) from the English coast, and German fighters attacked from there to the target and back to the Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Single-engine fighters closed in with cannon while twin-engine fighters fired rockets into the formations from about 900 metres (1,000 yards), breaking them up so that cripples could be finished off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The 228 bombers that reached Schweinfurt still bombed accurately, and the raid did more damage to the bearing industry than any other of the sixteen attacks made on it during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German records later showed 38 fighters destroyed in combat that day against American claims of 186, while 17 of the returning bombers were badly damaged and 121 more needed repair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Eighth made no more deep penetrations into Germany in clear weather for the rest of the year, because for the time being it had lost air superiority over Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next day Göring ordered the industry dispersed and its stocks pooled under a special commissioner, airframe makers removed four-fifths of the bearings from their designs, and output was back to its pre-raid level by the autumn of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The British official historians wrote that the Eighth's mix of resolution and rigidity nearly brought it to tragic defeat, but that the result was the downfall of the Luftwaffe once long-range escort fighters arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur B. Ferguson, “Pointblank,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 695–705, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-20.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 4–5, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 38–40, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","“AAF Aircraft of World War II,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), 216–19, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-6.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Big Week,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 44, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 25–27, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did losses mount once the Thunderbolts turned back?","a":"Because the escort's range ended near Aachen, and beyond it the Luftwaffe used rocket-firing twin-engine fighters to break up one formation at a time while single-engine fighters refuelled and returned, finishing off damaged bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Eighth not return to Schweinfurt for 4 months?","a":"Because the raid had shown that deep daylight penetrations without escort cost more than could be borne, and because commanders wrongly believed the plants had been decisively destroyed, which gave the Germans time to disperse them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the long-range fighter the answer to the crisis?","a":"Because the rocket-firing German fighters were clumsy and vulnerable to other fighters, so escorts that could go all the way to the target, as the P-51 eventually could, would protect the bombers and destroy the defenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Second Schweinfurt raid","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Schweinfurt_in_flames%2C_14_October_1943.jpg","title":"Schweinfurt burning, 14 October 1943","desc":"Smoke billows from Schweinfurt as a B-17 turns away during the raid of 14 October 1943.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schweinfurt_in_flames,_14_October_1943.jpg","alt":"A great column of smoke rises from a town in a valley, seen from above, with the wing and tail of a bomber at the right edge."}},
    "ww2-617": {"questions":["In March 1944 American bombers flew all the way to Berlin escorted by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a sight that Göring told interrogators he had never thought possible.","The Bombing Survey called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in some respects the most important addition to Allied air power in the European war, because it let daylight bombers strike deep into Germany again."],"abstract":"The <b>P-51 Mustang</b> was a single-engine fighter built by North American Aviation which, given extra fuel tanks, became the escort that took American bombers deep into Germany in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> North American designed it in 1940 for the British as a substitute for the P-40, and the Royal Air Force first flew it on tactical reconnaissance with its Army Co-operation Command, as over Dieppe in August 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Royal Air Force soon rated it the best American fighter yet to reach Britain, and in 1942 an American attaché urged fitting it with the Merlin engine to make a high-altitude fighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By November 1942 the Rolls-Royce engine was being tested in the aircraft and about 2,200 had been ordered, though the change of engine held up production through the following winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American airmen had long doubted that any fighter could match a bomber's range and still fight on equal terms, and their official history called the neglect of the P-51 close to their costliest mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first P-51 group in Britain flew its first escort on 5 December 1943, and on 13 December Mustangs helped take a force of 710 bombers to Kiel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With extra wing tanks it could escort bombers to about 1,370 kilometres (850 miles) from base, a then unheard-of range, and in March 1944 Mustangs went with the bombers all the way to Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Escort pilots were then ordered to hunt German fighters at every opportunity rather than stay beside the bombers, German fighter losses mounted sharply, and on a raid to Berlin in February 1945 Mustangs kept the Luftwaffe away altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Bombing Survey called the Mustang in some respects the most important addition to Allied air power in the European war, and Göring said the sight of American fighters over Berlin was something he had never thought possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official historians judged that the American victory in air combat, won above all by long-range fighters, made Big Week one of the most decisive battles of the air war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“AAF Aircraft of World War II,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), 216–19, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-6.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Winter Bombing,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 10–12, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-1.html. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Big Week,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 48–52, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 5–7, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 88, 190, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 131, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","John E. Fagg, “The Climax of Strategic Operations,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 725, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-20.html. [Open access]","Denis Richards and Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Fight Avails</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 143–44, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had American airmen thought a long-range escort impossible?","a":"Because a formal opinion of 1940 held that no fighter could be designed to escort bombers to their full radius and still fight enemy interceptors on equal terms, and that such an escort would have to be as large and expensive as the bomber itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Merlin engine matter so much?","a":"Because the original Mustang had been built for short-range ground support, and fitting the Rolls-Royce Merlin turned an excellent airframe into a high-altitude fighter that the British compared favourably with the Spitfire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Luftwaffe suffer so badly once Mustangs arrived in numbers?","a":"Because escorts were freed to hunt German fighters instead of only shielding the bombers, so the Germans lost not just aircraft but experienced pilots, and by the spring of 1944 their opposition had ceased to be effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"North American P-51 Mustang","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/%22Bottisham_Four%22_-_Four_P-51_Mustangs_fighters_in_flight.jpg","title":"P-51 Mustangs of the 361st Fighter Group","desc":"Four P-51 Mustangs of the 375th Fighter Squadron, 361st Fighter Group, in flight from RAF Bottisham on 26 July 1944.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Bottisham_Four%22_-_Four_P-51_Mustangs_fighters_in_flight.jpg","alt":"Four silver single-engine fighters with yellow noses fly in echelon above white clouds, the nearest carrying two drop tanks."}},
    "ww2-618": {"questions":["Code-named Argument by its planners, the offensive later called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began on 20 February 1944 after months of waiting for clear skies over Germany's fighter factories.","German aircraft acceptances were higher in March 1944 than in January despite <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because dispersed factories recovered quickly even as the Luftwaffe lost pilots in the air battles."],"abstract":"<b>Big Week</b> was the name later given to 6 days of coordinated Allied attacks on the German aircraft industry from 20 to 25 February 1944, an operation planned since November 1943 under the code name Argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Eighth Air Force from Britain and the Fifteenth from Italy were to strike airframe and final assembly plants by day, while Bomber Command timed its night attacks on cities to match them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Planners chose airframes because they expected that bombing them would show up more quickly in German front-line fighter strength than an attack on engine factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Weather had forced the operation to be postponed again and again since December, and the American commanders were ready to accept the loss of as many as 200 heavy bombers in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became possible because long-range escort fighters, above all the Mustang, could now go with the bombers deep into Germany without prohibitive losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Over 3,300 bombers of the Eighth and more than 500 of the Fifteenth attacked the main targets and dropped almost 10,000 tons of bombs, about as much as the Eighth had dropped in its entire first year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Americans lost 226 heavy bombers, about 6 per cent, while Bomber Command lost 157 aircraft in five night raids, 78 of them on the night of 19 February against Leipzig.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bombing damaged or destroyed three-quarters of the buildings in plants that made 90 per cent of German aircraft, but the industry was dispersed and its machine tools largely salvaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1944 the German air force accepted 39,807 aircraft of all types, more than in any earlier year, and Speer said aircraft output did not peak until September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The air battles mattered more than the factories, and the British official historians judged that the American victory in combat made Big Week one of the most decisive battles of the war in the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur B. Ferguson, “Big Week,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 30–45, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 5–7, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 88, 205, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 131, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Pointblank,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 704–05, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-20.html. [Open access]","Testimony of Albert Speer, 20 June 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, Nuremberg, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the planners concentrate on airframe plants?","a":"Because they had consistently assumed that bombing airframe manufacture would be reflected more quickly in the enemy's front-line fighter strength than an attack on the factories that made aircraft engines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German aircraft production recover so quickly?","a":"Because machine tools survived far better than factory buildings, the airframe industry could be dispersed into rough shelters in woods, and a special staff under Speer's ministry took over production and mobilised unused capacity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Big Week matter if production recovered?","a":"Because the 6 days of air fighting cost the Luftwaffe aircraft and experienced pilots it could not replace, and the forced dispersal left the industry exposed to later attacks on transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Big Week","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Consolidated_B-24_Liberator_of_the_446th_BG_%28USAAF%29_in_flight_over_the_Gothaer_Waggonfabrik%2C_Gotha%2C_Germany%2C_24_February_1944_%28342-FH-3A21390-50679AC%29.jpg","title":"A B-24 over Gotha, 24 February 1944","desc":"A B-24 Liberator of the 446th Bombardment Group over the Gotha aircraft works on 24 February 1944, during Big Week.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Consolidated_B-24_Liberator_of_the_446th_BG_(USAAF)_in_flight_over_the_Gothaer_Waggonfabrik,_Gotha,_Germany,_24_February_1944_(342-FH-3A21390-50679AC).jpg","alt":"A four-engined bomber seen from above flies over a snowy landscape scattered with bomb bursts, with smoke rising from a factory below."}},
    "ww2-619": {"questions":["American bombers joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the first time on 4 March 1944, escorted by long-range Mustangs over a capital the Luftwaffe seemed willing to defend at high cost.","About 48,000 tons of bombs had fallen during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by August 1944, causing very great but not catastrophic destruction, much of it in the suburbs rather than the centre."],"abstract":"The <b>bombing of Berlin</b> by British and American air forces ran through most of the war, but its heaviest phase was Bomber Command's winter campaign of 1943–44, known as the Battle of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before that campaign the capital had been attacked only incidentally, and when H2S radar was first used against it in March 1943 the city proved so large that its operators could hardly pick out the aiming point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By August 1944 about 48,000 tons of bombs had fallen on the city, causing very great but not catastrophic destruction, much of it in the suburbs rather than the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> American bombers first reached Berlin on 4 March 1944, and 2 days later lost 69 aircraft in a fierce air battle over a capital the Luftwaffe seemed willing to defend at high cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 3 February 1945 nearly 1,000 Flying Fortresses attacked railway targets and the government district, hitting the Reich Chancellery, the Air Ministry and the Gestapo headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Harris told Churchill on 3 November 1943 that Bomber Command could wreck Berlin from end to end if the Americans came in on it, and it would cost Germany the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 18 November 1943 and 24 March 1944 his force flew sixteen major raids on Berlin, 9,111 sorties, mostly by Lancasters on a round trip of at least 1,850 kilometres (1,150 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Winter cloud and the city's great size scattered the bombing, and in twelve of fifteen big raids 5,166 people were killed and somewhat under a fifth of the housing made uninhabitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 35 major operations of the whole period Bomber Command lost 1,047 bombers, and a final raid on Nuremberg on 30 March 1944 cost it 94 of the 795 aircraft sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British official historians concluded that in the operational sense the Battle of Berlin was more than a failure, it was a defeat, and Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that German output kept rising until the autumn of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 190–93, 210–11, 264–67, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","John Herington, “Berlin and Other Long-distance Raids,” in <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), 635–37, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417802. [Open access]","Arthur B. Ferguson, “Big Week,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 47–52, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","John E. Fagg, “The Climax of Strategic Operations,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 724–25, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-20.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 54–55, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 12–13, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-3. [Open access]","Testimony of Albert Speer, 20 June 1946, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, Nuremberg, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Battle of Berlin achieve less than the attack on Hamburg?","a":"Because Berlin lay at long range beyond the reach of the British navigation aids, the German night fighters were growing ever more effective, and cloud and the city's vast size spread the bombing too thinly for concentrated devastation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Bomber Command fight the battle almost alone?","a":"Because the American Eighth Air Force was neither equipped nor trained for night operations, and until long-range Mustangs arrived in March 1944 it could not sustain deep daylight raids, so its first attack on Berlin came near the battle's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans bomb Berlin so heavily in February 1945?","a":"Because the capital's railways were thought to be carrying German forces to the Eastern Front, a heavy blow on the eve of the Yalta conference might reassure the Soviet Union, and its leaders hoped to demoralise the Nazi government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bombing of Berlin in World War II","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1978-085-28%2C_Berlin%2C_Ausgebombte_nach_britischen_Luftangriff.jpg","title":"Bombed-out Berliners, August 1943","desc":"Berliners who have lost their homes sit with their salvaged belongings in the street after a British air raid in August 1943.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1978-085-28, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1978-085-28,_Berlin,_Ausgebombte_nach_britischen_Luftangriff.jpg","alt":"Two women sit on piles of bedding and furniture by a wooden fence next to rubble in a tree-lined street."}},
    "ww2-620": {"questions":["Churchill minuted on 28 March 1945 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remained a serious query against the conduct of Allied bombing, before withdrawing the minute for a more cautious version.","A historians' commission found no evidence for the long-told story that Allied fighters strafed fleeing refugees during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of the most propagandised air attacks of the war."],"abstract":"The <b>bombing of Dresden</b> was the series of British and American air raids of 13 to 15 February 1945 that destroyed the centre of the Saxon capital and killed up to 25,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dresden, with a pre-war population of over 600,000 swollen by refugees from the east, had escaped serious bombing until 1945 and was a junction for troop trains bound for the Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The idea went back to an Air Staff proposal of August 1944 for a blow of catastrophic force on a single big town hitherto relatively undamaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Malta in January 1945 the air leaders ranked Berlin, Leipzig, Dresden and associated cities second only to oil, where heavy attack would confuse the civilian evacuation from the east and hamper reinforcements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the night of 13 February just over eight hundred Bomber Command aircraft attacked, and their high-explosive and incendiary bombs raised a firestorm that destroyed about 80,000 homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Over 300 American bombers followed on 14 February and more than 200 on 15 February, bombing the marshalling yards blind through smoke that still rose to some 4,600 metres (15,000 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A press report of 17 February that the Allies had adopted deliberate terror bombing was read out in the Commons on 6 March by the member for Ipswich, Mr Stokes, who asked whether terror bombing was now British policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Harris remained unmoved, calling Dresden a centre of government and munitions and a key transport city, but on 28 March Churchill minuted that its destruction remained a serious query against Allied bombing, before withdrawing the minute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The raids were used intensively by propaganda and estimates have ranged from 20,000 to 500,000 dead, but a historians' commission appointed by the city found in 2010 that up to 25,000 were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same commission found no evidence for the long-popular story of a bloodbath among refugees strafed by Allied fighters, and noted that the dead included soldiers, refugees, forced labourers, prisoners and prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 54–55, 108–09, 112–14, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","John E. Fagg, “The Climax of Strategic Operations,” in <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i>, ed. Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 723–26, 730–31, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-20.html. [Open access]","Dresdner Historikerkommission, <i>Abschlussbericht der Historikerkommission zu den Luftangriffen auf Dresden zwischen dem 13. und 15. Februar 1945</i>, ed. Matthias Neutzner (Dresden: Landeshauptstadt Dresden, 2010), 49, 67–70, 80, https://www.dresden.de/media/pdf/infoblaetter/Historikerkommission_Dresden1945_Abschlussbericht_V1_14a.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Dresdner Historikerkommission, <i>Erklärung der Dresdner Historikerkommission zur Ermittlung der Opferzahlen der Luftangriffe auf die Stadt Dresden am 13./14. Februar 1945</i> (Dresden: Landeshauptstadt Dresden, 2008), https://www.dresden.de/media/pdf/presseamt/Erklaerung_Historikerkommission.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Bombardierung von Dresden,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2026, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/dresden. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons Debates, 6 March 1945, vol. 408, cols. 1898–1901, “Air Estimates, 1945,” https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/mar/06/air-estimates-1945. [Open access]","Hilary St. George Saunders, <i>Royal Air Force 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Fight Is Won</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 269–71, https://archive.org/details/royal-air-force-1939-1945-vol-3. [Open access]","John Herington, “Bomber Command and the Transportation Plan: Spring 1945,” in <i>Air Power over Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1963), 419–21, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417675. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Dresden attacked in February 1945?","a":"Because the Allied air leaders had ranked it with Berlin and Leipzig as a city where heavy attack would hamper German reinforcements for the Eastern Front, and it was a rail junction full of refugees, though no evidence shows that the Soviets asked for Dresden by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why have estimates of the dead varied so widely?","a":"Because the raids were exploited by propaganda and surrounded by myth, and while the authorities counted about 25,000 dead in 1945, an undocumented figure of 35,000 was set in 1946 and far higher numbers circulated for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill withdraw his minute about Dresden?","a":"Because it overlooked that he himself had urged attacks on the eastern cities in January 1945 and had been told Dresden was among the targets, and that the raid had come in February, when the war's end looked much less certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bombing of Dresden","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1994-041-07%2C_Dresden%2C_zerst%C3%B6rtes_Stadtzentrum.jpg","title":"The ruined centre of Dresden, 1945","desc":"The destroyed centre of Dresden in 1945, looking across the Neumarkt and the ruins of the Frauenkirche towards the Elbe and the Neustadt.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1994-041-07, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1994-041-07,_Dresden,_zerstörtes_Stadtzentrum.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a city of roofless, gutted buildings and rubble-filled streets, with domed and towered buildings in the distance."}},
    "ww2-621": {"questions":["2 years after hearing Dietrich Bonhoeffer describe the German opposition at Sigtuna in Sweden, the churchman <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became Britain’s best-known public critic of bombing whole German cities.","Warning the Lords in 1944 that old towns such as Dresden were almost certainly on Bomber Command’s list, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> insisted that his challenge implied no criticism of the aircrews."],"abstract":"<b>George Bell</b> was the Church of England’s Bishop of Chichester through the Second World War and the best-known churchman in Britain to challenge the bombing of German cities in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Active in the ecumenical movement between the wars, he first met the German pastor Dietrich Bonhoeffer at a church meeting in Geneva in August 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two worked together to give the Confessing Church, the German Protestants who resisted Nazi control of their church, a place in the ecumenical movement, most famously at Fanø in Denmark in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1942, while visiting the Swedish churches for the Ministry of Information, Bell met Bonhoeffer at Sigtuna and heard of a German opposition that meant to remove Hitler and seek peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He tried without success to win British support for those circles, and he reminded the Lords that his public opposition to Hitler and the Nazis went back to 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 9 February 1944 he asked the Lords to hear the government’s bombing policy, arguing that the obliteration of whole cities such as Hamburg and Berlin by area bombing was not a justifiable act of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He accepted attacks on industrial and military targets, warned that old towns such as Dresden were almost certainly on Bomber Command’s list, and said that no criticism of the aircrews was intended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Replying for the government, Lord Cranborne denied that the Royal Air Force made terror raids and argued that great centres of production and communication were military targets in a total war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument returned in March 1945, when the Labour member Richard Stokes asked the Commons whether terror bombing was now policy, and a junior air minister answered that it was absolutely not so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later that month Churchill himself minuted that bombing cities simply to increase the terror should be reviewed, withdrew the minute when Portal objected, and signed a milder redraft on 1 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Bombing Policy,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Lords Debates, 9 February 1944, vol. 130, cc. 737–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1944/feb/09/bombing-policy. [Open access]","“Air Estimates, 1945,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons Debates, 6 March 1945, vol. 408, cc. 1847–930, at cc. 1899–1901, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/mar/06/air-estimates-1945. [Open access]","“Air Training Corps,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons Debates, 6 March 1945, vol. 408, cc. 1930–92, at cc. 1989–90, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/mar/06/air-training-corps. [Open access]","Dietmar Süß, “Erinnerungen an den Luftkrieg in Deutschland und Großbritannien,” Dossier Nationalsozialismus, Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/themen/nationalsozialismus-zweiter-weltkrieg/dossier-nationalsozialismus/39584/erinnerungen-an-den-luftkrieg-in-deutschland-und-grossbritannien/. [in German] [Open access]","Dan D. Cruickshank, “‘To Our Earthly View Dietrich Is Dead’: George Bell’s Eulogy for Dietrich Bonhoeffer,” <i>Studies in Church History</i> 59 (2023): 446–70, at 447 and 458, https://doi.org/10.1017/stc.2023.19. [Open access]","“Education: Bombing Germany Again,” <i>Finest Hour</i> 127, International Churchill Society, https://winstonchurchill.org/publications/finest-hour/finest-hour-127/education-bombing-germany-again/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Bell object to area bombing but not to all bombing?","a":"Because he drew a line between attacks on military and industrial targets, which he accepted as legitimate, and the destruction of whole cities, residential districts and all, which he called obliteration and said was not a justifiable act of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the government answer him in the Lords?","a":"Lord Cranborne denied that the Royal Air Force had ever made pure terror raids and argued that in a total war the great centres of administration, production and communication were military targets in themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Churchill’s minute of March 1945 matter to this debate?","a":"Because the Prime Minister himself questioned bombing cities simply to increase the terror, although he withdrew the wording when Portal objected, and the redraft he signed on 1 April left the word terror out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"George Bell (bishop)","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Rev._G._K._A._Bell_%28Dean_of_Canterbury%29_LCCN2014695579.jpg","title":"George Bell as Dean of Canterbury","desc":"George Bell photographed for the Bain News Service while Dean of Canterbury, before he became Bishop of Chichester.","credit":"Bain News Service, public domain (no known restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rev._G._K._A._Bell_(Dean_of_Canterbury)_LCCN2014695579.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of a young clergyman in a cassock and clerical collar against a dark background."}},
    "ww2-622": {"questions":["Strips of metallised paper code-named Window, dropped over Hamburg in July 1943, threw the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into chaos by blinding the radars on which its interceptions depended.","Run from Zeist in the occupied Netherlands, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost its border belt of searchlights in the spring of 1942, when Hitler moved them to defend the larger cities."],"abstract":"The <b>Kammhuber Line</b> was the German night-fighter defence that Josef Kammhuber built across the western approaches to the Reich, run from Zeist in the occupied Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began as a belt of searchlights along the German–Dutch border, where fighters hunted bombers caught in the beams, until Hitler moved the searchlights to the big cities in spring 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its place grew the Himmelbett method, in which each ground station watched its own box of sky with one Freya and two Würzburg-Riese radars, one tracking the bomber and the other the station’s own fighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A controller plotted both and talked the fighter onto the target by radio, while airborne radar, ordered with top urgency only in July 1941, closed the last 3 or 4 kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British losses rose to 5.6 per cent in summer 1942, a rate that threatened the whole bomber offensive if it lasted, and by early 1943 the ground organisation employed about 40,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its weakness was that each station could guide only one interception at a time, so Bomber Command flew in a compact bomber stream that crossed few boxes and swamped the guns, searchlights and fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British held back Window, strips of metallised paper that confused German radar, for more than a year for fear the Germans would copy it, while 858 bombers were lost between April and mid-July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Window was first dropped over Hamburg on the night of 24 to 25 July 1943 it threw the system into chaos, leaving night-fighter crews to hunt on their own, and the Americans adopted it that autumn as Chaff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Kammhuber had to hand over his command on 15 September 1943, freer hunting inside the stream replaced the fixed boxes, and jamming later blinded the Freya radars too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Air defence, which the Luftwaffe also controlled, swallowed ever more guns, up by nearly a third in Germany and the West between early 1943 and mid-1944, and the oil plant at Leuna had the heaviest flak in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["W. H. Lutgert and R. de Winter, “Nederland en de Duitse Nachtjacht: Van jager tot prooi (deel 2),” <i>Militaire Spectator</i> 164, no. 1 (1995): 5–17, at 5–6, 10–12 and 14, https://militairespectator.nl/sites/default/files/bestanden/uitgaven/1995/1995-0005-01-0008.PDF. [in Dutch] [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour, Part 4</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 141–48 and 295, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory, Part 5</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 126 and 150, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 9, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Luftwaffe,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftwaffe. [in German] [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), ch. 20, “Pointblank,” 694–95, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-20.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bomber stream defeat the box system?","a":"Because each Himmelbett station could guide only one fighter onto one bomber at a time, so a compact stream that crossed only a few boxes swamped them while the fighters in neighbouring boxes stayed idle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British wait so long to use Window?","a":"Because they feared that the Germans would copy it against British night fighters, although by late 1942 an intelligence report judged it certain that the Germans already understood the principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What replaced the fixed boxes after Window?","a":"Freer methods in which fighters hunted on their own inside the bomber stream or over the target, while Kammhuber himself had to hand over his command in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kammhuber Line","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/German_W%C3%BCrzburg-Riese_radar%2C_in_1944_%28342-FH-3A20074-26729AC%29.jpg","title":"Würzburg-Riese radar, 1944","desc":"A German Würzburg-Riese radar of the kind that tracked bombers and night fighters in each box of the Kammhuber Line.","credit":"U.S. Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Würzburg-Riese_radar,_in_1944_(342-FH-3A20074-26729AC).jpg","alt":"A large dish-shaped radar antenna standing on bare, sandy ground beside a small hut, with trees behind."}},
    "ww2-623": {"questions":["Between February 1943 and May 1944 the fighter force committed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the West nearly doubled, while the German fighter strength on the Eastern Front did not grow.","Göring forbade his general of fighters to report that American escorts reached Liège, a sign of how badly the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was led as the bombers pushed deeper into Germany."],"abstract":"The <b>Defence of the Reich</b> was the Luftwaffe’s campaign to protect Germany itself against the British and American bomber offensives, fought with day and night fighters and with the anti-aircraft guns and civil air protection it also controlled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> German planning had not expected it, for the air force was built as an arm of the blitzkrieg, and in 1941 its chief of staff said he would not know what to do with more than 360 fighters a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler and Göring refused for years to believe the evidence of the air offensive being prepared against them, and accepted it only when it was too late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As the bombing grew, fighters were pulled back from other fronts, so that between February 1943 and May 1944 the fighter force in the Reich and the West nearly doubled while that in the East did not grow at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Göring even forbade his general of fighters, Adolf Galland, to report that American escorts were reaching as far as Liège, because he would not believe that it was possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The turning point came early in 1944, when the American Mustang and Thunderbolt escorts were ordered to leave the bombers and engage German fighters at every opportunity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German fighter losses claimed by the Allies were 1,115 in January, 1,118 in February and 1,217 in March, and with the aircraft went experienced pilots who could not be replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the spring of 1944 the Luftwaffe’s opposition had ceased to be effective, and on D-Day it had only 80 operational aircraft in France to meet the invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Single-engine fighter acceptances still climbed to 3,031 in September 1944, but only a minority of the new aircraft ever appeared in combat, and the oil offensive soon left the force short of fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the Me 262 jet fighter changed little, since the Luftwaffe lacked the well-trained, experienced pilots to exploit it, and about 430,000 members of its units died before the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 2 and 6–8, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 22, “Mission Accomplished,” 792–93, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 9, “The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” 288, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour, Part 4</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 141–48 and 295, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, proceedings of 21 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Luftwaffe,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftwaffe. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did German fighter production rise while the Luftwaffe lost the battle?","a":"Because the dispersed factories kept turning out aircraft, but the best pilots were dead or crippled and could not be replaced, only a minority of the new aircraft reached combat, and the oil attacks soon cut fuel for training and operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the change in escort tactics matter so much?","a":"Because the Mustangs and Thunderbolts were no longer tied to protecting the bombers but told to engage German fighters at every opportunity, which drove German losses past a thousand a month early in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Eastern Front feel the defence of Germany?","a":"Because fighters were pulled back to defend the Reich, so the fighter force in Germany and the West nearly doubled between February 1943 and May 1944 while that in the East did not grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Defence of the Reich","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/German_Focke-Wulf_Fw_190A_is_shot_down_near_Kassel%2C_Germany%2C_in_1944_%28342-FH-3A20376-51315AC%29.jpg","title":"Fw 190 shot down near Kassel, 1944","desc":"Gun-camera frames from an American fighter show a German Focke-Wulf Fw 190 being shot down near Kassel in 1944.","credit":"U.S. Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Focke-Wulf_Fw_190A_is_shot_down_near_Kassel,_Germany,_in_1944_(342-FH-3A20376-51315AC).jpg","alt":"Four grainy gun-camera frames in a row showing a single-engine fighter against clouds, ending in a burst of smoke."}},
    "ww2-624": {"questions":["After the February raids of 1944 aircraft production passed from the Luftwaffe to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and acceptances in March were higher than in January despite the bombing.","Prisoners supplied by the SS and Hungarian Jews were put to work for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Speer widened into an armaments staff for all weapons in August 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Jägerstab</b>, or ‘Fighter Staff’, was the emergency body set up by a decree of Albert Speer on 1 March 1944 to restore German fighter production after the heavy American raids of February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> According to the prosecution at Erhard Milch’s trial, Milch conceived it, he and Speer were its joint chiefs, and Karl Saur acted as its chief of staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It drew together experts from industry and from the ministries of labour, transport, power and raw materials, and it met almost daily, first at the Air Ministry in Berlin and later at Tempelhof airfield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tasks were the quick repair of bombed plants, the dispersal of the aircraft industry into small units, and the building of underground factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its labour came from Sauckel’s deportations from the occupied countries and from the SS, which supplied concentration-camp prisoners and Hungarian Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Until then aircraft had been procured by the Luftwaffe rather than Speer’s ministry, and the industry, spread across the Reich with air attack in mind, was inefficient and had much spare capacity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the last week of February 1944 the escorted bombers dropped 3,636 tons on aircraft plants, damaging three-quarters of the buildings in plants that made 90 per cent of Germany’s aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Machine tools survived far better than buildings, and acceptances were higher in March than in January and kept rising through the summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The staff did so well that Speer widened it into an armaments staff for all weapons on 1 August 1944, and single-engine fighter acceptances reached 3,031 that September, at the cost of bombers and transports for the Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Speer testified that aircraft output peaked in September 1944, but the scattered plants depended on transport that the bombers then wrecked, and quality and spare parts may have been sacrificed for numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Milch Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office), opening statement and argument of the prosecution on the Jaegerstab, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-II/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-II.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 6–7, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 2, “Big Week,” 44–46 and 60, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 9, “The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” 288, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, proceedings of 20 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour, Part 4</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 295, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did German aircraft output rise after the February raids?","a":"Because the industry had much spare capacity, machine tools survived better than buildings, and the new staff dispersed production into small, hidden units and pushed repairs with overriding priority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did dispersal later turn against the Germans?","a":"Because scattered plants depended on moving parts between them, so when the bombers turned on transport in the autumn of 1944 the dispersed industry was left extremely vulnerable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Who did the work the staff organised?","a":"Workers deported from the occupied countries through Sauckel’s office and prisoners supplied by the SS, including concentration-camp inmates and Hungarian Jews, as the prosecution showed at Milch’s trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jägerstab"},
    "ww2-625": {"questions":["Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, begun on 12 May 1944, cost Germany 90 per cent of its fuel and the war as far as production went.","Arthur Harris dismissed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as another panacea in letters to Portal in December 1944, even as German explosives output fell with the loss of synthetic nitrogen."],"abstract":"The <b>oil campaign</b> was the Allied bombing of Germany’s fuel supply from May 1944, aimed above all at the synthetic-oil plants, its only source of aviation spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German oil was tight throughout the war, which is why Germany secured Romania’s oilfields, and the Ploesti fields and Hungary’s together gave about a quarter of its liquid fuel in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As early as January 1941 the British Chiefs of Staff had called oil the most vulnerable target in the German war machine, but the night bombers of the time could not find such targets often enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Eighth Air Force opened the campaign on 12 May 1944, and Speer later said that this raid made real what had been a nightmare to him for more than 2 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Synthetic output fell from 316,000 tons a month to 107,000 tons in June and 17,000 tons in September, and aviation spirit from those plants from 175,000 tons in April to 5,000 tons in September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Nuremberg Speer testified that the attacks from 12 May 1944 cost Germany 90 per cent of its fuel and meant the loss of the war as far as production was concerned, since new tanks and jets were useless without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The damage spread further, for the same plants made most of Germany’s synthetic nitrogen and methanol, so explosives output fell and shells were filled out with rock salt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pilot training was cut for lack of fuel, and in the Ardennes offensive of December 1944 many armoured units were lost when they ran out of petrol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Harris of Bomber Command still doubted it, writing to Portal in December 1944 that the economic experts had always overstated their case for such panaceas, and Portal replied that he was profoundly disappointed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The official American air history called the campaign decisive within less than a year, while the bombing survey added that it should have begun sooner and that perhaps 3 per cent of bombs on three plants hit damageable targets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 3 and 8–10, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 22, “Mission Accomplished,” 794–96, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 9, “The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” 287, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, proceedings of 20 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory, Part 5</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 85–87, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","Sven Panthöfer, “Rumänien als Verbündeter des Deutschen Reiches,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/rumaenien-als-verbuendeter-des-deutschen-reiches. [in German] [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Annexes and Appendices</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), appendix 9, 188–92, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-4. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the synthetic plants such a good target?","a":"Because they were the only source of German aviation spirit, they were large complexes that could not easily be broken up and dispersed, and they also made most of Germany’s synthetic nitrogen and methanol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the oil attacks affect explosives as well as fuel?","a":"Because the synthetic-oil plants were the largest producers of nitrogen and methanol, so bombing them cut the raw materials for explosives and forced the Germans to fill shells with rock salt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Harris resist the oil plan?","a":"Because he believed the Ministry of Economic Warfare had always overstated its case for single target systems, and he expected the Germans to find other sources of fuel, though he told Portal he carried out decisions once made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Oil campaign of World War II","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Bombing_of_Concordia_Vega_oil_refinery_in_Ploe%C8%99ti_by_USAAF_B-24s%2C_31_May_1944_%E2%80%94_restored.jpg","title":"B-24s over Ploești, 31 May 1944","desc":"Fifteenth Air Force B-24 Liberators fly over the burning Concordia Vega oil refinery at Ploești in Romania on 31 May 1944.","credit":"Richard R. Ganczak, U.S. Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bombing_of_Concordia_Vega_oil_refinery_in_Ploești_by_USAAF_B-24s,_31_May_1944_—_restored.jpg","alt":"Four-engined bombers flying in formation above thick columns of black smoke rising from the ground."}},
    "ww2-626": {"questions":["Though the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drilled millions in air-raid protection, German shelters held only about eight million people by 1945, and casualties among those in basements were heavy.","People’s gas masks and cots pumped with fresh air were part of the preparations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which proved useless against the firestorms of the area raids."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichsluftschutzbund</b>, or ‘Reich Air Protection League’, was the mass civil-defence organisation founded at the end of April 1933 under Göring’s aviation ministry to train volunteer air-raid wardens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law of 1935 made air-raid protection a duty for much of the population, and the league grew to more than 13.5 million members by 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its wardens led air-protection groups in blocks of houses, and it ran drills, posters and courses under the slogan ‘air protection is necessary’ to prepare people for a coming war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas attack was the great fear, so there was a people’s gas mask for adults, a protective jacket for small children and a cot pumped with fresh air for babies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against area bombing these measures proved useless, for sandbags and hand pumps could do nothing against firestorms or the lack of oxygen that followed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The concrete bunkers Germany built were excellent, one in Hamburg holding up to 60,000 people, but by the end of the war there was shelter space for only about eight million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most people sheltered in basements, where casualties were heavy, and after raids no systematic attempt was made to recover all the bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Official German statistics counted 250,253 people killed by air attack between January 1943 and January 1945, including foreigners and soldiers in the cities attacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American bombing survey judged that too low and gave its own minimum of 305,000 dead and 780,000 wounded for the whole war, while a 2013 history of the bombing war put German deaths at about 353,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The toll has long been argued over in Germany, where military historians spent decades refuting legends about victim numbers, and even Dresden, once said to have lost far more, is put at up to 25,000 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Der Luftschutz,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/luftschutz. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 1 and 14–15, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 22, “Mission Accomplished,” 801, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]","Review of <i>The Bombing War: Europe 1939–1945</i>, by Richard Overy, <i>Times Higher Education</i>, 19 September 2013, https://www.timeshighereducation.com/books/the-bombing-war-europe-1939-1945-by-richard-overy/2007343.article. [Open access]","Dietmar Süß, “Erinnerungen an den Luftkrieg in Deutschland und Großbritannien,” Dossier Nationalsozialismus, Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/themen/nationalsozialismus-zweiter-weltkrieg/dossier-nationalsozialismus/39584/erinnerungen-an-den-luftkrieg-in-deutschland-und-grossbritannien/. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Bombardierung von Dresden 1945,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/bombardierung-von-dresden-1945. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did German civil defence fail against the big raids?","a":"Because it had been designed against small, isolated attacks, and in the great fire raids no fire-fighting equipment was of much use, while sandbags and hand pumps could do nothing against firestorms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are there several figures for German bombing deaths?","a":"Because the official German statistics covered only part of the war and were judged too low, the American survey gave its own minimum of 305,000, and later historians have reached other totals such as about 353,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the excellent bunkers not enough?","a":"Because there were too few of them, with room for only about eight million people at the end of the war, so most people sheltered in basements, where casualties were heavy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reichsluftschutzbund","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Ausstellung_mit_Plakaten%2C_Gasmasken%2C_Hausmodellen_u.a._in_Bezug_auf_den_Luftschutz_im_2._Weltkrieg_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Wertheim_S-N_70_G_4091.jpg","title":"Air-raid protection exhibition","desc":"An exhibition on air-raid protection in Wertheim with posters, gas masks and model houses, from the Second World War.","credit":"Fotosammlung Wehnert, Landesarchiv Baden-Württemberg, Staatsarchiv Wertheim S-N 70 G 4091, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ausstellung_mit_Plakaten,_Gasmasken,_Hausmodellen_u.a._in_Bezug_auf_den_Luftschutz_im_2._Weltkrieg_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Wertheim_S-N_70_G_4091.jpg","alt":"A room with display boards of posters, small model houses on a table, a life-size figure cut-out and a model bomb."}},
    "ww2-627": {"questions":["After studying Germany, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned to Japan at Truman’s request in August 1945 and judged that Japan would have surrendered before 1946 even without the atomic bombs.","Set up on 3 November 1944 under the insurance executive Franklin D’Olier, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced more than 200 reports on what the bombing of Germany had achieved."],"abstract":"The <b>United States Strategic Bombing Survey</b> was the body that the American Secretary of War set up on 3 November 1944, on a directive from President Roosevelt, to study what the bombing of Germany had achieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was meant as an impartial study to guide the air war against Japan and the future of American air power, and its chairman was the insurance executive Franklin D’Olier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its establishment provided for 300 civilians, 350 officers and 500 enlisted men, and its teams followed close behind the armies into Germany in April 1945 to seize records before they were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They inspected several hundred plants and cities and interrogated thousands of Germans, nearly all the surviving leaders among them, finding records in caves, barns, a hen house and coffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1945 President Truman asked it for a similar study of Japan, and its Pacific summary judged that Japan would have surrendered before the end of 1945 even without the atomic bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its 200-odd European reports were candid about failures such as the misdirected attack on ball bearings and low accuracy, and it found that the February 1944 raids had damaged three-quarters of the buildings in the main aircraft plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It concluded that the city raids before the autumn of 1944 had not substantially affected German war production, putting the loss of total output to area bombing at 9 per cent in 1943 and 17 per cent in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It gave minimum figures of 305,000 German dead and 780,000 wounded from air attack, and it judged the attacks on oil and transport decisive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its final verdict was that Allied air power had been decisive in western Europe, and the official American air history thought its tone did not do justice to the work of the Royal Air Force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British official historians judged that it and the smaller British survey did not in the main conflict, but that neither paid enough attention to the winning of air superiority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), i, 3–4 and 15–16, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), iii and 26, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 22, “Mission Accomplished,” 789–92, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Annexes and Appendices</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 47–49, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-4. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 2, “Big Week,” 44, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-2.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour, Part 4</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 252, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the survey set up before the war ended?","a":"Because its teams had to follow close behind the armies to seize German records and interview officials before vital evidence was lost, and its findings were wanted for the air war against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some airmen dislike its reports?","a":"Because it dismissed several difficult and costly raids as unnecessary or ineffective, criticised accuracy and target choice, and took a deprecating tone towards the Royal Air Force’s contribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did it conclude about the city raids?","a":"That before the autumn of 1944 they did not substantially affect German war production, which recovered within weeks, although they lowered German morale and left a lasting lesson in the horror of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"United States Strategic Bombing Survey","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Effects_of_strategic_bombing_on_German_morale.jpg","title":"Survey diagram of bombing and morale","desc":"A diagram from the United States Strategic Bombing Survey's morale reports showing how bombing bore on German will to resist.","credit":"US military, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Effects_of_strategic_bombing_on_German_morale.jpg","alt":"An illustration of five falling bombs labelled killed, wounded, evacuated, deprived of utilities and homes destroyed, above a block labelled will to resist."}},
    "ww2-628": {"questions":["With only about one in four bomber crewmen expected to finish a tour unscathed, the fear of being classed as showing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped keep aircrew flying.","The waverer letter of September 1941 laid down that a man with medical symptoms must be treated as ill and that only the fit could show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the rules."],"abstract":"<b>Lack of moral fibre</b>, usually shortened to LMF, was the Royal Air Force’s label for aircrew who refused to fly on operations without a medical reason, meant to deter such refusals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The term was first used at a meeting on 21 March 1940 about the growing number of airmen refusing operations, and rules went out to all commands the following month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A revised instruction of 28 September 1941, known as the ‘waverer letter’, said that a man with medical symptoms must be treated as a medical case and only a fit man could be found lacking in moral fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was never a medical diagnosis, and the line between LMF and nervous illness was never settled, leaving medical officers caught between senior commanders and the service’s own psychiatric consultants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rumour made it loom large, with tales of men publicly stripped of their wings and rank, although historians who studied the Air Ministry’s figures put the share of bomber aircrew classed as LMF at under one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The pressure behind it came from the losses, for a bomber crewman’s chance of finishing a tour of operations unscathed was about one in four, and a first tour was usually 30 operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 1 April and 14 July 1943 Bomber Command lost 858 aircraft, and in October the Americans lost 60 of the 229 bombers that attacked Schweinfurt in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the end of the war 55,888 men had been killed serving in Bomber Command, 47,268 of them on operations, most of them from its relatively small force of aircrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American bombing survey counted 79,281 British and 79,265 American airmen lost in air action over Europe, though it called the British figures preliminary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One veteran recalled two men of his Lancaster crew at Mildenhall announcing over the intercom in February 1944 that they had destroyed their maps and would not fly, and both were charged with LMF.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Dan Ellin, “A ‘Lack of Moral Fibre’ in Royal Air Force Bomber Command and Popular Culture,” <i>British Journal for Military History</i> 6, no. 3 (2020): 42–65, at 42, 45, 51 and 55, https://doi.org/10.25602/GOLD.bjmh.v6i3.1425. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory, Part 5</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 286–87, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 1, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Bernard Dye, “They Refused to Fly,” veteran’s account, International Bomber Command Centre Digital Archive, University of Lincoln, https://ibccdigitalarchive.lincoln.ac.uk/collections/document/17746. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour, Part 4</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 144, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 22, “Mission Accomplished,” 800, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was LMF kept apart from medical illness?","a":"Because the Air Ministry wanted it to be an executive and disciplinary matter, so the waverer letter said that any man showing medical symptoms must be treated as a medical case, though the boundary was never resolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the label matter so much to aircrew?","a":"Because losses were so heavy that only about one in four could expect to finish a tour unscathed, and the stigma of LMF, spread by rumours of public humiliation, was meant to keep men flying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How many Bomber Command men died?","a":"Some 55,888 were killed in action or on active service, 47,268 of them on operations, and most of them came from the relatively small force of aircrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lack of Moral Fibre","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/550_Squadron_Lancaster_and_crew_IWM_CH_17852.jpg","title":"A 550 Squadron Lancaster crew","desc":"The crew of Lancaster ED905 of No. 550 Squadron, which flew more than a hundred operations, pose on their aircraft.","credit":"Cpl W. H. Teal, Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:550_Squadron_Lancaster_and_crew_IWM_CH_17852.jpg","alt":"Seven airmen sitting and standing on the nose of a bomber painted with rows of bomb symbols and the words Press on regardless."}},
    "ww2-629": {"questions":["Churchill told the Commons in July 1944 that each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> launched against Britain had so far killed almost exactly one person, 2,752 deaths from 2,754 launches.","Launched from ramps the Organisation Todt built in northern France, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flew at nearly 600 kilometres an hour (370 miles per hour) with about a tonne of explosive."],"abstract":"The <b>V-1 flying bomb</b> was a pilotless, jet-propelled aircraft carrying a warhead, which Germany fired at London and later at Antwerp from June 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Developed by the engineers Robert Lusser and Fritz Gosslau and first tested at Peenemünde in December 1942, it was 7.73 metres (25 feet) long and weighed 2,200 kilograms (4,850 pounds), almost half of it explosive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It flew at nearly 600 kilometres an hour (370 miles per hour) with a range of 370 kilometres (230 miles), launched first from ramps the Organisation Todt built in northern France and later from aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nazi propaganda had promised since 1943 to answer the bombing of German cities with weapons of retaliation, and the V stood for Vergeltung, the German word for ‘retaliation’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Allied bombing of the launching sites in France probably delayed the attack on Britain by 3 or 4 months, and from August 1944 prisoners of the Mittelbau-Dora camp built the bombs in underground tunnels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The first ten were launched early on 13 June 1944, a week after the Normandy landings, and the first to reach England came down near Gravesend at 4:18 AM.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the morning of Sunday 18 June one struck the chapel at Wellington Barracks near Buckingham Palace, killing 58 civilians and 63 servicemen, the worst toll from a single missile so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 6 July Churchill told the Commons that 2,754 flying bombs had been launched and that they had killed almost exactly one person per bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British pressed for every countermeasure, Eisenhower kept attacks on the sites as the bombers’ first priority, and the assault from France ended as the armies overran the launching area, though air-launched bombs and attacks from Holland went on until March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In all, flying bombs killed 6,184 civilians in the United Kingdom and seriously injured 17,981, about nine-tenths of them in the London region, while at Antwerp the Allied guns soon shot down more than 60 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), ch. 24, “The Flying Bomb: Part Two,” 370–78 and 389–97, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-24.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), appendix L, “Civilian Casualties Caused in the United Kingdom by Bombing and by Various Forms of Long-Range Bombardment,” 528, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-L.html. [Open access]","Johannes Leicht, “Die ‘Wunderwaffen’ V1 und V2,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/die-wunderwaffen-v1-und-v2. [in German] [Open access]","“Prime Minister’s Review,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons Debates, 6 July 1944, vol. 401, cc. 1322–39, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jul/06/prime-ministers-review. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 22, “Mission Accomplished,” 799–800, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 9, “The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” 288, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","“Tunnel Complex,” KZ-Gedenkstätte Mittelbau-Dora, Stiftung Gedenkstätten Buchenwald und Mittelbau-Dora, https://www.dora.de/en/geschichte/historischer-ort/stollen. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory, Part 5</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 48, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans call it a weapon of retaliation?","a":"Because Nazi propaganda had promised since 1943 to repay the bombing of German cities with new weapons, to keep up civilian morale and the fighting spirit of the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many fail to reach their targets?","a":"Because many crashed from technical faults and fighters, guns and balloons shot down a growing share, so that only about a quarter of all those fired reached their targets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How many people did the flying bombs kill in Britain?","a":"About 6,184 civilians were killed and 17,981 seriously injured, roughly nine-tenths of them in the London Civil Defence Region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"V-1 flying bomb","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/German_V-1_flying_bomb_in_flight_over_Europe%2C_in_1944-1945_%28342-FH-3A15839-66778AC%29.jpg","title":"V-1 flying bomb in flight","desc":"A V-1 flying bomb photographed in flight over wooded country from an American B-17 bomber in 1944–45.","credit":"U.S. Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_V-1_flying_bomb_in_flight_over_Europe,_in_1944-1945_(342-FH-3A15839-66778AC).jpg","alt":"An aerial view of fields and a dark wood, with a small pale winged missile visible over the trees."}},
    "ww2-630": {"questions":["Prisoners of the Mittelbau-Dora camp built nearly 6,000 examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in tunnels near Nordhausen, and more than 12,000 of them died doing so.","Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost almost as much as a fighter aircraft, calling the weapon very expensive and its effect negligible."],"abstract":"The <b>V-2 rocket</b> was the liquid-fuelled ballistic missile that German engineers under Wernher von Braun developed at Peenemünde as the Aggregat 4, or A4, and that Germany fired at Allied cities from September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first successful launch, on 3 October 1942, flew 190 kilometres (120 miles) on a burn of 58 seconds, and Hitler backed the programme after von Braun reported to him in July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carrying a warhead of 1,000 kilograms (2,200 pounds), it reached more than 5,000 kilometres an hour (3,100 miles per hour) and a height of 90 kilometres (56 miles), with a range of 400 kilometres (250 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It fell faster than sound and came without warning, and Churchill told the Commons that no reliable public warning could be given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After 597 British bombers struck Peenemünde on the night of 17 August 1943, production moved into tunnels near Nordhausen, where prisoners of the Mittelbau-Dora camp, at first sleeping underground, built nearly 6,000 rockets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The first rocket to strike Britain fell at Chiswick at 6:40 PM on 8 September 1944, killing three people, and a second landed near Epping 16 seconds later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rocket joined the flying bomb against London that September, but the government said nothing in public until 10 November, when Churchill confirmed the attacks after the German high command had announced them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The last of 1,115 rockets to fall on Britain or close offshore came down at Orpington on 27 March 1945, hours after one had killed 134 people in a block of flats at Stepney.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rockets killed 2,754 civilians in the United Kingdom and seriously injured 6,523, and estimates of all those killed by them, mostly in London and Antwerp, run from 8,000 to 12,000, while more than 12,000 prisoners died building them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that the rockets were very expensive and their effect negligible, since for the cost of one rocket Germany could almost have built a fighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), ch. 25, “The Long-Range Rocket,” 406–07 and 420, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-25.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), appendix L, “Civilian Casualties Caused in the United Kingdom by Bombing and by Various Forms of Long-Range Bombardment,” 528, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-L.html. [Open access]","Johannes Leicht, “Die ‘Wunderwaffen’ V1 und V2,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/die-wunderwaffen-v1-und-v2. [in German] [Open access]","“German Long-Range Rockets,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons Debates, 10 November 1944, vol. 404, cc. 1653–54, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/nov/10/german-long-range-rockets. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, proceedings of 21 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 9, “The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” 288, HyperWar Foundation, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","“Tunnel Complex,” KZ-Gedenkstätte Mittelbau-Dora, Stiftung Gedenkstätten Buchenwald und Mittelbau-Dora, https://www.dora.de/en/geschichte/historischer-ort/stollen. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour, Part 4</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 158–59, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the rocket not be intercepted or warned of?","a":"Because it climbed to about 90 kilometres (56 miles) and fell faster than sound, so it arrived without warning and no reliable public alert could be given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government keep silent at first?","a":"Because any announcement might have given the enemy useful information, so ministers said nothing until the German high command had announced the attacks itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Speer call the rocket a mistake?","a":"Because each one was very expensive to make while its effect was negligible, and the army and air force agreed that the cost of one rocket would almost have built a fighter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"V-2 rocket","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1978-Anh.026-01%2C_Peenem%C3%BCnde%2C_V2_beim_Start.jpg","title":"A4 rocket launch at Peenemünde, 1943","desc":"An A4 rocket, later called the V-2, lifting off from its test stand at Peenemünde in 1943.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1978-Anh.026-01, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1978-Anh.026-01,_Peenemünde,_V2_beim_Start.jpg","alt":"A tall black-and-white striped rocket rising in a cloud of smoke beside a launch gantry and crane."}},
    "ww2-631": {"questions":["On a conservative estimate at least 20,000 of the roughly 60,000 people deported to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died, many of them after working on rockets in the underground Mittelwerk.","10 days after the Royal Air Force bombed Peenemünde, 107 prisoners from Buchenwald arrived to found <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the camp whose inmates would assemble Germany's V-2 rocket."],"abstract":"<b>Mittelbau-Dora</b> was the SS concentration camp near Nordhausen in the Harz Mountains whose prisoners were forced to build, and then to work in, the underground Mittelwerk factory where Germany assembled its V-2 rockets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rocket production had begun at Peenemünde on the Baltic, where by 1943 some three thousand mostly East European forced labourers were building the factory and 1,300 concentration-camp prisoners had been brought in to staff it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the Royal Air Force bombed Peenemünde in August 1943, manufacture was moved underground, and the American air force's official history judges that the raid probably hastened, if it did not cause, that dispersal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 28 August 1943 the first 107 prisoners arrived at the Kohnstein hill with their SS guards to found a new subcamp of Buchenwald, and by Christmas there were more than 10,500 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of hundreds of subcamps placed beside factories and tunnel sites to work prisoners for the war effort, and in October 1944 it became the main camp of its own concentration camp, Mittelbau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> With no barracks yet built, the SS housed the prisoners inside the tunnels, in chambers fitted with four-tiered wooden bunks and with oil barrels sawn in half for latrines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hunger, thirst, cold and heavy construction work killed nearly 2,900 of them between October 1943 and March 1944, and about 3,000 more who were dying were shipped to Majdanek and Bergen-Belsen, where hardly any survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rocket assembly began in January 1944, and by March 1945 nearly 6,000 of the weapons had been made there by about 2,000 civilian technicians and some 10,000 prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the roughly 60,000 people deported to the Mittelbau camps, at least 20,000 died on the memorial's conservative estimate, which cannot be made exact because so many deaths went unrecorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Smithsonian ascribes about half of those deaths to the V-2 programme, and the German Historical Museum sets more than 12,000 prisoners who died making it against 8,000 to 12,000 people killed by its attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“‘Buried Alive’,” Mittelbau-Dora Concentration Camp Memorial, Buchenwald and Mittelbau-Dora Memorials Foundation, https://www.dora.de/en/geschichte/chronologie/konzentrationslager/lebendig-begraben. [Open access]","“The Final Tally,” Mittelbau-Dora Concentration Camp Memorial, Buchenwald and Mittelbau-Dora Memorials Foundation, https://www.dora.de/en/geschichte/chronologie/konzentrationslager/bilanz. [Open access]","“V-2 Missile,” collection object A19600342000, National Air and Space Museum, Smithsonian Institution, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/missile-surface-surface-v-2-a-4/nasm_A19600342000. [Open access]","Johannes Leicht, “Die ‘Wunderwaffen’ V1 und V2,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2023, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/die-wunderwaffen-v1-und-v2. [in German] [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 540, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-15.html. [Open access]","“Forced Labor: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/forced-labor-in-depth. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was V-2 production moved into tunnels in the Harz?","a":"Because the Royal Air Force bombed the rocket centre at Peenemünde in August 1943, after which manufacture was shifted to the underground Mittelwerk near Nordhausen, and the American air force's official history judges that the raid probably hastened, if it did not cause, that dispersal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many prisoners die in the first winter?","a":"Because finishing the underground plant came before building a barrack camp, so the SS kept the prisoners in the tunnels themselves, where they suffered hunger, thirst, cold and heavy labour, and nearly 2,900 died between October 1943 and March 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the death toll not be stated exactly?","a":"Because only about 12,000 deaths were registered in the SS files, while unregistered killings, the transports of the dying to Lublin and Bergen-Belsen and the death marches of April 1945 went uncounted, so the memorial gives at least 20,000 as a conservative estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mittelbau-Dora concentration camp","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Mittelbau_Dora.jpg","title":"Mittelwerk tunnel entrance, 1945","desc":"American servicemen at a camouflaged tunnel entrance of the underground Mittelwerk near Nordhausen after its capture.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mittelbau_Dora.jpg","alt":"A group of uniformed men standing in front of a tunnel mouth under timber scaffolding and camouflage netting."}},
    "ww2-632": {"questions":["Fighter-bombers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flew over the American tank columns breaking out of Normandy in July 1944, talking to them by radio from above.","Under the Allied Expeditionary Air Force, the American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Royal Air Force's Second Tactical Air Force shared an advanced headquarters at Uxbridge for the invasion."],"abstract":"The <b>Ninth Air Force</b> was the American tactical air force of the campaign in north-west Europe, built in England in 1943–44 to support the invasion of Normandy and the armies that then fought their way into Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had served in the Middle East, but when its headquarters moved to England in autumn 1943 it inherited little more than its name, its commander Lewis H. Brereton and four headquarters staffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 7.5 months it became what the official history calls the most powerful single tactical air force on any of the world's fronts, manned on D-Day by more than 170,000 troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its striking power lay in eighteen fighter groups, thirteen of them flying the P-47, beside groups of medium and light bombers that attacked bridges, rail centres and airfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the Royal Air Force's Second Tactical Air Force it came under the Allied Expeditionary Air Force, and from May 1944 the two were coordinated at an advanced headquarters at Uxbridge, over Brereton's protests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its medium bombers and fighter-bombers flew support against gun positions, tank laagers, headquarters and defended positions in front of the armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 11 June the German Seventh Army's commander wrote that the Luftwaffe had to be committed in strength against the unbearable enemy air superiority if his supplies were to move by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In August Field Marshal von Kluge reported from Avranches that the enemy's air superiority smothered almost all German movement and that losses in men and equipment were extraordinary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the breakout of late July its IX Tactical Air Command, under Elwood R. Quesada, kept fighter-bombers over each armoured column, with an air force radio in the column to talk to the pilots, a practice kept up until the Siegfried Line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British official history concluded that no single factor did so much to help the Allied armies win in Normandy as the sustained air attack, against which the German air force could neither hit back nor defend itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 107–13, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-5.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 239, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-8.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 126, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-7.html. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 65, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/65/data.pdf. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 411, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 490, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What made a tactical air force different from the bombers attacking German cities?","a":"Because its task was the battle itself: its fighter-bombers and medium bombers struck bridges, rail centres, airfields and troops in support of the invasion and the armies ashore, rather than the industries of the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German commanders in Normandy complain so bitterly about the air?","a":"Because Allied air superiority smothered almost all their movements, as von Kluge reported from Avranches, and supply by day became so hard that the Seventh Army's commander begged for the Luftwaffe to be committed in strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did aircraft protect the tank columns breaking out in July 1944?","a":"Because the IX Tactical Air Command kept fighter-bombers over each armoured column, and at Quesada's suggestion an air support party with an air force radio rode in the column so tanks and pilots could talk to each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ninth Air Force","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/9th_Air_Force_Havocs_with_Pathfinder_Radar.jpg","title":"Ninth Air Force Havocs bombing","desc":"A-20 Havoc light bombers of the Ninth Air Force, flying from bases in France, release their bombs above the clouds.","credit":"Office of Scientific Research and Development, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9th_Air_Force_Havocs_with_Pathfinder_Radar.jpg","alt":"Two twin-engined bombers with white star markings flying above a layer of cloud while bombs fall beneath them."}},
    "ww2-633": {"questions":["Questioned after the war, Alfred Jodl told American investigators that the Allies' winning of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had altogether decided the war against Germany.","Eisenhower postponed D-Day by a day in June 1944 because bad weather threatened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on which the whole invasion plan had been made to depend."],"abstract":"<b>Air supremacy</b> is control of the air so complete that the enemy's air force is no longer a serious threat, and how the Allies won it over Germany was the subject of the Strategic Bombing Survey's summary report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Allies did not plan to win the war from the air alone: their strategy turned on invading the Continent in spring 1944, and their plans called for gaining air superiority before that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The staff that drew up the first invasion plan named a reduction of the German fighter force as the condition that, above all others, would dictate the date of the landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was won in the first months of 1944, when long-range P-51 and P-47 escorts were ordered to seek out German fighters, and the Germans' claimed fighter losses rose above 1,100 a month from January to March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By spring the Survey judged that Luftwaffe opposition had ceased to be effective, and German air generals said they had only 80 operational aircraft in France on D-Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When bad weather on 4 June threatened the air plan, Eisenhower postponed the landing for a day, remembering that it had been accepted as feasible only if Allied air superiority could be brought to bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The troop carriers that took three airborne divisions to Normandy lost no aircraft to enemy fighters, and by late June the Allies held unrestricted command of sea and air over the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The British official historian judged that no single factor did so much for the Allied victory in Normandy as the sustained air attack that this command of the air made possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Survey concluded that Allied air power was decisive in the war in western Europe, a deliberately regional verdict, and the American air force's official history called air supremacy its most significant achievement because it made the invasions possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several German leaders agreed when questioned after the war, and Alfred Jodl said that the winning of air superiority had decided the war altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), 1–7, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 786–92, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-22.html. [Open access]","Historical Sub-Section, Office of Secretary, General Staff, Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, <i>History of COSSAC (Chief of Staff to Supreme Allied Commander), 1943–1944</i> (May 1944), 29, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/COSSAC/index.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 169, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-9.html. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 58, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/58/data.pdf. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 490, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the invasion planners care so much about German fighters?","a":"Because the first invasion plan made a reduction of the German fighter force the requirement that above all others would dictate the date of the landing, and Eisenhower later treated air superiority as the condition on which the operation had been judged feasible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the Luftwaffe beaten in early 1944?","a":"Because long-range P-51 and P-47 escorts were ordered to engage German fighters at every opportunity, so fighter and pilot losses climbed above 1,100 claimed aircraft a month and by spring the Luftwaffe's opposition had ceased to be effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Survey's verdict name only western Europe?","a":"Because the Survey studied the Anglo-American air war and was not allowed into the Soviet zone, so its conclusion that Allied air power was decisive was a judgement about the war in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Air supremacy","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/P-51_Mustangs_of_the_325th_Fighter_Group,_15th_Air_Force_escort_a_B-17_Flying_Fortress.jpg","title":"Mustangs escorting a Flying Fortress, 1944","desc":"P-51 Mustangs of the 325th Fighter Group fly alongside a B-17 bomber, the kind of long-range escort that beat the Luftwaffe in 1944.","credit":"United States Army Air Forces, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P-51_Mustangs_of_the_325th_Fighter_Group,_15th_Air_Force_escort_a_B-17_Flying_Fortress.jpg","alt":"Four single-engined fighters with chequered tails flying beside the nose of a four-engined bomber above clouds."}},
    "ww2-634": {"questions":["Marshall wrote in 1942 that the landings in North Africa were of necessity a substitution for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and not simply a postponement of the attack across the Channel.","Planners in May 1943 conceded that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its form as a ten-division assault was a logistic impossibility, so a smaller cross-Channel invasion was planned for 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Roundup</b> was the Allied plan for a full-scale invasion of north-western France, first drafted by British planners in 1941 and adopted in April 1942 as the main Anglo-American effort against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The British planners' first version assumed a Germany already giving up hope of victory and withdrawing, and proposed landings either side of Le Havre by six and a half infantry and six armoured divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In April 1942 the Marshall Memorandum turned it into a plan for spring 1943, with 48 divisions and 5,800 combat aircraft landing between Étretat and Cap Gris Nez.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> George C. Marshall and Harry Hopkins took the plan to London that month and quickly won British agreement, with a much smaller emergency landing, Sledgehammer, kept ready for 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In June the White House announced after Molotov's visit that full understanding had been reached on the urgent tasks of creating a second front in Europe in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In July 1942 Roosevelt chose to land in North Africa instead, and Marshall wrote that this operation was of necessity a substitution for Roundup and not a postponement of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In August Stalin protested that a second front in 1942 had been decided during Molotov's visit to London, and that the Soviet command had built its summer and autumn plans on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Casablanca in January 1943 the Allies accepted that a full-scale attack across the Channel could not take place before 1944, and appointed a chief of staff to plan it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By May even Marshall agreed that Roundup as a ten-division assault was a logistic impossibility for spring 1944, and the American chiefs began to speak of a smaller, glorified Sledgehammer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That month Churchill and Roosevelt agreed at Washington on an invasion of France in spring 1944, the operation that the new planning staff would prepare under the name Overlord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 7–8, 16–22, 31–32, 65, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","“Press Release Issued by the White House, June 11, 1942,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1961), 593–94, doc. 483, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d483. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin to Winston Churchill and W. Averell Harriman, aide-mémoire, 13 August 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1961), 621–22, doc. 516, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d516. [Open access]","Historical Sub-Section, Office of Secretary, General Staff, Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, <i>History of COSSAC (Chief of Staff to Supreme Allied Commander), 1943–1944</i> (May 1944), 1, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/COSSAC/index.html. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 1, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-1.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the plan laid aside in July 1942?","a":"Because Roosevelt decided to invade North Africa that year, landing craft were too few even for the smaller landing of 1942, and the British War Cabinet had ruled that there should be no substantial landing in France unless the Allies meant to stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin protest so angrily in August 1942?","a":"Because the communiqués after Molotov's visits had spoken of a second front in Europe in 1942, and Stalin said the Soviet command had planned its summer and autumn operations on that expectation, so its cancellation was a moral blow to Soviet opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Roundup lead to Overlord?","a":"Because after Casablanca a combined staff was set up to plan the cross-Channel attack for 1944, and once Roundup as a ten-division assault was judged impossible, a smaller invasion for spring 1944 was agreed in May 1943 and planned as Overlord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Roundup (1942)"},
    "ww2-635": {"questions":["Stalin told Roosevelt and Churchill at Tehran that until a commander was appointed, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not be considered really in progress as an operation.","The assault phase was code-named Neptune, so the landings of 6 June 1944 formed only the opening of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the larger plan to win a lodgement in north-west Europe."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Overlord</b> was the code name for the Allied plan and campaign to invade north-west Europe in 1944, whose aim was a lodgement on the Continent from which further offensives could be launched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its planning was the work of COSSAC, the Anglo-American staff of Lieutenant-General F. E. Morgan, whose plan the conference at Washington in May 1943 tied to a target date of 1 May 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Morgan's planners rejected the Pas-de-Calais, the most strongly defended stretch of the French coast, and chose the beaches near Caen, from which Cherbourg and the Brittany ports could be seized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Combined Chiefs of Staff, with Roosevelt and Churchill, approved the outline plan at the Quebec Conference in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Tehran in November Stalin asked who would command it, and said that until a commander was appointed the operation could not be considered really in progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Eisenhower, named supreme commander in December, thought the three-division assault of the plan too weak and too narrow, and in January 1944 he and his commanders agreed to widen it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Finding landing craft for five assault divisions meant putting the target date back to the end of May or early June and weakening, then postponing, the landing planned in southern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Overlord was to unfold in two phases, a landing in Normandy followed by the capture of Cherbourg and the enlargement of the area to the Brittany and Loire ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first or assault phase was given a code name of its own, Neptune, which is why the landings of 6 June are a part of Overlord rather than the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the end of June nearly 1 million Allied soldiers had landed in France, and when the plan's 90 days ran out in early September, eight new airfields in France had already been abandoned as too far behind the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Historical Sub-Section, Office of Secretary, General Staff, Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, <i>History of COSSAC (Chief of Staff to Supreme Allied Commander), 1943–1944</i> (May 1944), 27–30, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/COSSAC/index.html. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 69, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 1–3, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-1.html. [Open access]","Bohlen Minutes, 29 November 1943, 4 p.m., in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers: The Conferences at Cairo and Tehran, 1943</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1961), 535–38, doc. 366, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943CairoTehran/d366. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 111–20, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-6.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 62–63, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 82, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/82/data.pdf. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the beaches near Caen chosen rather than the Pas-de-Calais?","a":"Because the Pas-de-Calais was the most strongly defended part of the coast, with poor exits from its beaches, while the Caen sector was lightly defended, sheltered from the prevailing winds and close to Cherbourg and the Brittany ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin care who would command the invasion?","a":"Because he took the appointment of one man responsible for preparing and carrying out the operation as the sign that it was real, and said until then it could not be considered really in progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the invasion postponed from May to June 1944?","a":"Because Eisenhower and his commanders widened the assault to five divisions, and the extra landing craft could be found only by delaying the target date and the landing in southern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Overlord","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Overlord_Plan_-_Combined_Bomber_Offensive_and_German_depositions_6_June_1944.jpg","title":"The Overlord plan, June 1944","desc":"A West Point map of the Overlord plan, the bomber offensive and the German dispositions in north-western Europe on 6 June 1944.","credit":"United States Military Academy, Department of History, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Overlord_Plan_-_Combined_Bomber_Offensive_and_German_depositions_6_June_1944.jpg","alt":"A coloured map of France, the Low Countries and Germany with arrows from southern England to Normandy and red boxes marking German armies."}},
    "ww2-636": {"questions":["Dummy landing craft in south-eastern ports and an army group said to be under Patton belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the plan that kept the German Fifteenth Army waiting at Calais.","Because of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Hitler took the Normandy landing for a diversion and held his Fifteenth Army in the Pas-de-Calais well into July 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Fortitude</b> was the Allied cover and deception plan for the invasion of Normandy, designed to make the German command believe that the main landing would come later and somewhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave effect, for Overlord, to a wider deception plan called Bodyguard, which misrepresented Allied strategy in Europe in order to induce the Germans to make faulty dispositions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fiction was that the campaign would open with an attack on southern Norway from Scottish ports, while the main blow would fall on the Pas-de-Calais about the third week of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fictitious Fourth Army was conjured up in Scotland chiefly from contrived wireless traffic, while in south-east England dummy craft and false signals made real formations look like an army poised to cross to Calais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed the deception threats of 1943, code-named Cockade, which had been meant to pin German forces in the west but had failed to shake Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Real shipping was also massed in the Thames estuary and at Dover, and the bombers struck two targets outside Normandy for every one inside it so as not to point to the true beaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The plan played on what the Germans already believed, for Hitler and his generals expected the main assault at the narrowest point of the Channel, where the Atlantic Wall was strongest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even after 6 June Hitler took the Normandy landing for a feint, and the Allies used the Germans' high regard for Patton to persuade them that an army group under him remained in England for a second landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German Fifteenth Army therefore stayed on the Channel coast into July, and the divisions that finally left the Pas-de-Calais were harried by air attack on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The British official history judged the Germans' failure to see through the bluff an expensive mistake, explained partly by poor intelligence and partly by Hitler's belief that he could foresee what the Allies would do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 103–4, 127–28, 489–90, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 351, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 13, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-1.html. [Open access]","Historical Sub-Section, Office of Secretary, General Staff, Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, <i>History of COSSAC (Chief of Staff to Supreme Allied Commander), 1943–1944</i> (May 1944), 13, 27, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/COSSAC/index.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 143, 168, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-6.html. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 63, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/63/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the deception point at the Pas-de-Calais?","a":"Because the Germans already expected the main assault there, at the shortest sea crossing on the direct road to the Ruhr, so the plan only had to confirm a belief that Hitler and his generals held from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How could an army that did not exist be made convincing?","a":"Because the Germans had little air reconnaissance over Britain, so wireless traffic, dummy craft and real formations in the south-east were enough to fill their maps with an imaginary order of battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the deception achieve after D-Day?","a":"Because the Germans believed a second landing was coming, the Fifteenth Army stayed on the Channel coast into July instead of reinforcing Normandy, an expensive mistake in the British official history's judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Fortitude","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/DecoyLCT.jpg","title":"Dummy landing craft, 1944","desc":"Dummy landing craft moored as decoys in a south-eastern English harbour before D-Day.","credit":"War Office official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DecoyLCT.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of three landing craft moored in a line along a narrow tidal channel between mudflats."}},
    "ww2-637": {"questions":["Rommel laid five or six million mines and covered beaches with stakes and steel obstacles to strengthen the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the months before the Allied landings of 1944.","Rundstedt later called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an enormous bluff, and on 6 June 1944 its beach defences in Normandy proved no decisive hindrance to the Allied landings."],"abstract":"The <b>Atlantic Wall</b> was the German system of coastal fortifications built from 1942 to 1944 along the occupied coast of western Europe against an Allied invasion from the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building it was the work of Organisation Todt, the construction organisation that had built the West Wall, but until 1942 most of its labour went into bombproof U-boat pens on the Atlantic coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Dieppe raid of August 1942, Hitler told his commanders in September that only concrete could withstand Allied bombs and shells, and called for 15,000 strongpoints held by 300,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wanted the work finished by 1 May 1943, when the builders hoped to have about 40 per cent done, so open beaches, lowest on the list after U-boat bases and harbours, were left least defended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strongest works stood where the Germans expected the main blow, on the Pas-de-Calais at the narrowest crossing of the Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Erwin Rommel, sent in November 1943 to inspect the coastal defences and then commanding Army Group B on the Channel coast, believed the battle would be decided at the water's edge within 48 hours of a landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He laid five or six million mines by D-Day, against the fifty million he thought the minimum, and planted stakes, hedgehogs and other obstacles between the tide marks to wreck landing craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Labour was scarce, so some infantry battalions spent 3 days a week on construction instead of training, and in Normandy French labour was used on the beach obstacles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Allied bombers dropped some 23,000 tons on coastal batteries in the 2 months before D-Day, most of it on batteries outside the assault area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 6 June the wall proved no decisive hindrance anywhere: the British official history calls its failure a fiasco, Rundstedt later dismissed it as an enormous bluff, and Eisenhower wrote that Rommel had pinned his faith on it with fatal misjudgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 131–38, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 249–52, 264, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 120, 193, 268, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","[Charles H. Taylor], <i>Omaha Beachhead, 6 June–13 June 1944</i>, American Forces in Action (Washington, DC: Historical Division, War Department, 1945), 11, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-A-Omaha/USA-A-Omaha-2.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 47, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/47/data.pdf. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 26, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-2.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler put his faith in concrete?","a":"Because he believed that only concrete could withstand the weight of Allied bombs and shells, and that massive works, like the West Wall in 1938, would also deter the enemy by their very existence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the coast of Normandy weaker than that of Calais?","a":"Because building followed a strict order of priority, with U-boat bases and harbours first and open beaches last, and the strongest works went where the Germans expected the main assault, at the narrowest point of the Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Rommel want everything at the water's edge?","a":"Because he believed the Allies' command of the air would stop German armour reaching the coast in time, so the battle had to be won on the beaches within the first 48 hours of a landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atlantic Wall","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Atlantic_Wall_beach_obstructions_in_Normandy_in_June_1944.jpg","title":"Beach obstacles in Normandy, 1944","desc":"Mined anti-landing obstacles off the Normandy coast in early June 1944, with pillboxes on the shore behind.","credit":"United States Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlantic_Wall_beach_obstructions_in_Normandy_in_June_1944.jpg","alt":"Rows of slanting posts sticking out of shallow water off a low coastline."}},
    "ww2-638": {"questions":["Some 156,000 Allied soldiers were in France by the evening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, more than 130,000 of them brought ashore from the sea onto five beaches.","German commanders, expecting an invasion at high tide near a good harbour, were surprised when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came soon after low tide on open beaches in rough seas."],"abstract":"The <b>Normandy landings</b> of 6 June 1944 were the seaborne and airborne assault that opened Operation Overlord, putting Allied armies ashore on five beaches, Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eisenhower had chosen 5 June, with the two following days acceptable, but on the morning of 4 June the forecasts were so bad that he postponed the landing by 24 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At 4:00 AM on 5 June, trusting a forecast of a short break in the weather, he took what he called the final and irrevocable decision to land the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rough weather helped surprise, for the Germans thought a crossing impossible in such seas and expected a landing at high tide near a good harbour, not soon after low tide on open beaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 1,200 warships and 7,500 aircraft supported more than 3,100 landing craft, while three airborne divisions were dropped in the night in what was then the greatest airlift of assault troops ever attempted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Over 130,000 men came ashore from the sea and more than 23,000 by air, so that some 156,000 Allied soldiers were in France by the end of the first day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Utah the American 4th Division lost only 197 men, but at Omaha the 1st Division met a full German division that Allied intelligence had missed, and for some hours the whole operation faced its gravest crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German air force hardly appeared, with only 80 operational aircraft in France by its own generals' account, and the Allies lost 127 aircraft in more than 14,000 sorties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The day's losses are not exactly known: the American official history doubts that accurate figures will ever exist, while giving 2,374 for V Corps at Omaha and roughly 1,250 for each American airborne division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 12 June about 330,000 Allied soldiers had joined the beachheads into one front about 100 kilometres (60 miles) long and 30 kilometres (20 miles) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 19, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-2.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 193–94, 223, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 285–86, 300, 319–20, 330, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","<i>Utah Beach to Cherbourg, 6–27 June 1944</i>, American Forces in Action (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1947), 55, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-A-Utah/USA-A-Utah-3.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 58, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/58/data.pdf. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 167–69, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-9.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), 7, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bad weather help the Allies?","a":"Because the Germans had concluded that no crossing was possible while the seas ran so high, and with their radar weakened by air attack they were unprepared when the landings came in a short break in the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Omaha go so much worse than Utah?","a":"Because the 1st Division landed against the one stretch of coast with a strong cordon defence and a full German division whose presence Allied intelligence had missed, while at Utah the 4th Division lost only 197 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is there no exact figure for the day's losses?","a":"Because units counted their casualties in different ways and revised them later, so the American official history doubts accurate figures by unit will ever be available and gives the various counts instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Normandy landings","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/D-day_-_British_Forces_during_the_Invasion_of_Normandy_6_June_1944_B5111.jpg","title":"Sword Beach, 6 June 1944","desc":"The view from a tank landing craft during the first assault on Queen Red beach, Sword area, about 8 a.m. on D-Day.","credit":"J. Mapham, No. 5 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D-day_-_British_Forces_during_the_Invasion_of_Normandy_6_June_1944_B5111.jpg","alt":"Tanks and beach obstacles in shallow water seen from the rail of a landing craft, with smoke rising from buildings on the shore."}},
    "ww2-639": {"questions":["The German 352nd Division, whose move to the coast Allied intelligence had missed, met the 1st and 29th Divisions at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the morning of the Normandy landings.","American destroyers came in almost to the sand at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to shell pillboxes and strongpoints that had survived the bombing of D-Day morning."],"abstract":"<b>Omaha Beach</b> was the code name for the stretch of the Normandy coast between Vierville-sur-Mer and Colleville-sur-Mer where the American V Corps landed on 6 June 1944, and where the D-Day assault met its greatest difficulties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The usable beach ran for about 6,400 metres (7,000 yards) in a very slight crescent, backed by bluffs that merged into cliffs at either end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At low tide a gently sloping flat of firm sand about 270 metres (300 yards) wide lay open to fire, and the Germans had studded it with obstacles, using French labour on some of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first waves were the 116th Infantry of the 29th Division in the west and the 16th Infantry of the 1st Division in the east, timed to touch down at 6:30 AM.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 34,250 men were landed there on D-Day, more than on any other beach, while at Utah to the west the 4th Division lost only 197 men all day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The heavy bombers, bombing through overcast and anxious not to hit their own troops, dropped their main loads between a few hundred metres and about 5 kilometres (3 miles) inland, so the beach was not softened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the rough sea only 5 of the 32 amphibious tanks launched in the eastern sector reached the shore, and at least ten landing craft carrying infantry were swamped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The troops also met a full German division, the 352nd, whose move to the coast Allied intelligence had missed, and at noon the German corps announced that the landing had been smashed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Destroyers closed almost onto the beach to fire on pillboxes and strongpoints, and by nightfall the troops had pushed at most about 2.4 kilometres (1.5 miles) inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> V Corps put its losses for the day at 2,374, though the American official history warns that accurate figures by unit will probably never exist, and by 13 June its casualties had reached 5,846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["[Charles H. Taylor], <i>Omaha Beachhead, 6 June–13 June 1944</i>, American Forces in Action (Washington, DC: Historical Division, War Department, 1945), 11, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-A-Omaha/USA-A-Omaha-2.html. [Open access]","[Charles H. Taylor], <i>Omaha Beachhead, 6 June–13 June 1944</i>, American Forces in Action (Washington, DC: Historical Division, War Department, 1945), 37–42, 163, https://archive.org/details/OmahaBeachhead. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 305, 319–20, 330, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 23, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-2.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 192, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-7.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 223, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Invasion of France and Germany, 1944–1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II, vol. 11 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1957), 142–43, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-xi-the-invasion-of-france-and-germany-1944-1945. [Open access]","<i>Utah Beach to Cherbourg, 6–27 June 1944</i>, American Forces in Action (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1947), 55, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-A-Utah/USA-A-Utah-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bombing fail to help the troops?","a":"Because the heavy bombers had to bomb through overcast and delayed their release to avoid hitting the landing craft, so their main loads fell from a few hundred yards to 3 miles inland and left the beach defences unsoftened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Omaha so much harder than Utah?","a":"Because Omaha's troops landed in worse surf among mined obstacles and against a full German division that had reinforced the coast, while the 4th Division at Utah lost only 197 men all day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll at Omaha uncertain?","a":"Because units counted and revised their losses in different ways, so the official history gives V Corps' figure of 2,374 for the day while warning that accurate figures by unit will probably never be available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Omaha Beach","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg","title":"Into the Jaws of Death, Omaha Beach","desc":"Troops of the 1st Division wade ashore from a Coast Guard landing craft at Omaha Beach on the morning of 6 June 1944.","credit":"Robert F. Sargent, United States Coast Guard, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg","alt":"Soldiers wading through surf towards a beach under low cloud, seen from inside a landing craft with its ramp down."}},
    "ww2-640": {"questions":["The scattered parachute drops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left many British soldiers in flooded ground by the Dives, yet the battery at Merville was silenced before the seaborne landings began.","Aircraft of the Royal Air Force's 38 and 46 Groups flew the British 6th Airborne Division to Normandy in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while American troop carriers took two divisions to the Cotentin."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Tonga</b> was the night landing of the British 6th Airborne Division east of the River Orne at the start of 6 June 1944, one of the three airborne operations that opened the invasion of Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The airborne plan dropped two American divisions, the 82nd and 101st, behind the beaches of the Cotentin, while the British division was to secure the eastern flank of the whole landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its tasks were to seize and hold the bridges over the Orne and the Caen Canal, to cut the bridges over the Dives by which German troops could reach the flank, and to silence a battery at Merville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aircraft and gliders of the Royal Air Force's 38 and 46 Groups flew the troops from airfields south of Oxford, and the evening lift of the 6th Air Landing Brigade was called Operation Mallard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Paratroops and glider troops were to take important points inland while the seaborne armies landed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Soon after midnight six Horsa gliders brought a coup de main party of the Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire Light Infantry down beside the two bridges at Bénouville and Ranville, which were taken intact within 15 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parachute drops went far worse, many sticks scattering or falling into flooded ground by the Dives, and the division landed amid a regiment of the 21st Panzer Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eisenhower's report says the troops were dropped precisely in the appointed area, but the British official history estimates that no more than sixty per cent of the 4,800 men landed could join the early fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, by daylight every primary task had been done: the Orne bridges were held, four bridges over the Dives and its tributary had been blown and the Merville guns had been put out of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only seven of 264 parachute aircraft and 22 of 98 gliders were missing, and by evening the seaborne 3rd Division had reached the Orne bridges and made contact with the airborne troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 138–39, 148–56, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” Fourth Supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 58, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/58/data.pdf. [Open access]","Gordon A. Harrison, <i>Cross-Channel Attack</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1951), 185, 332, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub741CrossChannelAttack. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Report by the Supreme Commander to the Combined Chiefs of Staff on the Operations in Europe of the Allied Expeditionary Force, 6 June 1944 to 8 May 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 22, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/SCAEF-Report/AEF-Report-2.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Invasion of France and Germany, 1944–1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II, vol. 11 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1957), 181, 186–87, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-xi-the-invasion-of-france-and-germany-1944-1945. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why land airborne troops east of the Orne at all?","a":"Because the bridges over the Orne and the Caen Canal, the crossings of the Dives and the battery at Merville controlled the eastern flank of the whole landing, and holding them shielded the seaborne troops from German counter-attack on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the parachute drops so scattered?","a":"Because dust and smoke from a bomber attack on Merville and cloud obscured the dropping zones, pathfinders were misplaced, and many sticks landed far off or in flooded ground beside the Dives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the reports of the landing disagree?","a":"Because Eisenhower's report says the troops came down precisely where intended, while the British official history found that many landed in the wrong places and perhaps sixty per cent at most could join the first actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Tonga","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Operation_Overlord_(the_Normandy_Landings)-_D-day_6_June_1944_B5233.jpg","title":"Gliders by the Caen Canal, June 1944","desc":"Horsa gliders that crash-landed on the bank of the Caen Canal, bringing men of the Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire Light Infantry on D-Day.","credit":"Christie, No. 5 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Operation_Overlord_(the_Normandy_Landings)-_D-day_6_June_1944_B5233.jpg","alt":"Two broken gliders with striped wings lying in a grassy field beside a line of tall trees and houses."}},
    "ww2-641": {"questions":["A gale lasting from 19 to 22 June 1944 wrecked the American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> off Omaha Beach, while the British one at Arromanches survived and served through the following winter.","Churchill's minute of May 1942 asking for piers that would float up and down with the tide led to each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the Allies built off Arromanches and Saint-Laurent."],"abstract":"A <b>Mulberry harbour</b> was one of the two prefabricated ports that the Allies towed across the English Channel and assembled off the Normandy beaches in June 1944, so that the invasion could be supplied before a French port was taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The idea went back to a minute of May 1942 in which Churchill asked for piers that would float up and down with the tide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In October 1943 the projects received their code names, Mulberry A for the American harbour off Saint-Laurent-sur-Mer at Omaha Beach and Mulberry B for the British one at Arromanches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each harbour was enclosed by sunken blockships and by concrete caissons called Phoenixes, the largest about 18 metres (60 feet) tall and weighing 6,000 tons, while floating steel Bombardons farther out were meant to break the swell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside, floating steel roadways known as Whales ran out to Lobnitz pierheads that rose and fell with the tide, so that coasters and tank landing ships could unload at any state of the tide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first vehicles drove ashore over the pierheads on 16 June, but 3 days later an unforecast north-easterly gale began, the worst June storm in the Channel for over 40 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Off Omaha, drifting craft smashed into the piers, all 24 Bombardons broke loose, and by 21 June only eight of the 32 Phoenixes that had been sited were still effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Within days the Allied commanders decided not to rebuild Mulberry A, to strengthen its blockship breakwater instead, and to send the parts that could be salvaged to finish the British harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Partly sheltered by the Calvados reef, Mulberry B survived, and for more than 3 months it averaged 6,765 tons a day, about 48 per cent of all the tonnage landed for the British forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its value was disputed, since one British officer rated the harbours at only 15 per cent of the landings' success, and the American official history concludes that the planners had underrated what open beaches could handle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 1, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 271–82, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Logistics1/USA-E-Logistics1-7.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Invasion of France and Germany, 1944–1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 11 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1957), 24–27, 178–79, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-xi-the-invasion-of-france-and-germany-1944-1945. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 88–89, 272–73, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 1, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1953), 406–15, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Logistics1/USA-E-Logistics1-10.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 173–75, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-10.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 145, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-6.html. [Open access]","Bertram H. Ramsay, “The Assault Phase of the Normandy Landings,” despatch of 16 October 1944, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 38110, 30 October 1947, 5120, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/38110/supplement/5120/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the invasion need harbours of its own?","a":"Because the Allies expected to land over open beaches before any French port could be captured and repaired, and supplies had to keep coming in whatever the weather. The harbours were planned to last 90 days, until deepwater ports such as Cherbourg were working, and later studies extended that to the winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the American harbour abandoned after the storm?","a":"Because the gale of 19 to 22 June left it a near total loss: its Bombardons broke loose, most of its Phoenixes failed, and drifting craft wrecked its piers. Admiral Kirk advised against rebuilding it, the commanders agreed, and its surviving parts went to complete the British harbour at Arromanches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British harbour survive when the American one did not?","a":"Because the Calvados reef and the capes to the east broke the main force of the waves at Arromanches, and its breakwater had been laid more tightly. It was later reinforced with salvaged American units and its caissons were filled with sand, which helped it serve through the winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mulberry harbours","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/MulberryB_-_Piers.jpg","title":"Mulberry B at Arromanches from the air","desc":"The piers, pierheads and breakwater of the British Mulberry harbour off Arromanches, photographed by the RAF in October 1944.","credit":"Royal Air Force official photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MulberryB_-_Piers.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of three long floating roadways running out from the shore to a line of pierheads, with ships moored behind a breakwater."}},
    "ww2-642": {"questions":["Some 200 British tanks were lost on the first day of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an attack whose purpose, a breakthrough or a battle to hold German armour away from Cobra, was argued over afterwards.","Canadian troops of Operation Atlantic fought beside the three British armoured divisions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1944, losing 441 men killed in 4 days south of Caen."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Goodwood</b> was the British Second Army's armoured offensive east of Caen, launched on 18 July 1944, in which the three armoured divisions of 8th Corps attacked from the small bridgehead east of the Orne towards the Bourguébus ridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It grew out of the stalled American advance in the Cotentin, and the British blow was timed to come a few days before the American offensive, Cobra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Montgomery's written instruction of 15 July gave its object as engaging the German armour and writing it down, while gaining a firm bridgehead over the Orne through Caen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The air commander's despatch called its opening bombardment the heaviest and most concentrated air attack in support of ground forces ever attempted, with 1,676 heavy and 343 medium and light bombers dropping 7,700 US tons of bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many prisoners taken in the shattered forward positions stayed deaf for a day, but beyond them the tanks ran into a screen of anti-tank guns that intelligence had not reported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> By the evening of the first day the three attacking corps had lost about 1,500 men and some 200 tanks, and congestion around the village of Cagny had slowed the armoured advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heavy rain and a thunderstorm on 20 July turned the ground to mud, and by the next day the offensive was over, having gained up to about 11 kilometres (7 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Canadian units attacking alongside in Operation Atlantic suffered 1,965 casualties in the 4 days, 441 of them killed or dead of wounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eisenhower, who had read Montgomery's messages as promising a breakthrough, judged the result a failure, and the gap between the claims made to the press and the modest gains fed a lasting dispute about what the operation had been for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The German commanders saw it differently, since it drew still more armour east of the Orne, and on 21 July von Kluge told Hitler that the air attacks had convinced him that Rommel's gloomy view of the battle was right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 330–52, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 166–79, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-7.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 187–90, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-10.html. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 188–90, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-10.html. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 64, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/64/data.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 207–08, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British attack where the Germans were strongest?","a":"Because Montgomery meant to hold the German armour on the eastern flank, away from the soft point Bradley had chosen for the American breakout, so whether Goodwood gained much ground or little it served his purpose if it engaged and damaged that armour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Eisenhower think Goodwood had failed?","a":"Because Montgomery's messages before the battle could be read as promising a breakthrough, the airmen had given it the heaviest support of the campaign, and he did not see Montgomery's narrower written instruction to Dempsey. When the attack stopped after modest gains, he judged it disappointing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the armoured advance slow down on the first day?","a":"Because beyond the bombed forward positions the tanks met an anti-tank screen that had not been reported, the small bombs used against the German guns did little to silence them, and armour of two divisions became tangled round Cagny while thousands of vehicles crowded the Orne crossings behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Goodwood","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Sherman_tanks_carrying_infantry_wait_to_advance_at_the_start_of_Operation_%27Goodwood%27%2C_Normandy%2C_18_July_1944._B7511.jpg","title":"Shermans waiting at the start of Goodwood","desc":"British Sherman tanks carrying infantry wait to advance at the start of Operation Goodwood on 18 July 1944.","credit":"Sgt Laing, No. 5 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sherman_tanks_carrying_infantry_wait_to_advance_at_the_start_of_Operation_%27Goodwood%27,_Normandy,_18_July_1944._B7511.jpg","alt":"Several Sherman tanks in a field, the nearest crowded with infantrymen sitting on its hull, and a soldier standing in front of it."}},
    "ww2-643": {"questions":["Heavy bombers struck the northern edge of the city on 7 July 1944, a turning point in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that wrecked its university and caused hundreds of French civilian casualties.","Operation Epsom crossed the Odon to Hill 112 in late June during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Germans fought as the defence of their gateway to Paris."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle for Caen</b> was the struggle of the British and Canadian armies through June and July 1944 to take the Norman city of Caen, which the invasion plan had listed among the objectives for D-Day itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Caen was a road centre that opened the way east to the Seine and south-east to the Paris–Orléans gap, and the open plain beyond it suited tanks and airfields, so armour, air and supply staffs all wanted it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Germans, who saw Caen as the gateway to Paris, massed their reserves to hold it, and the British were stopped short of the city in the first week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 7 June the 12th SS Panzer Division threw back a Canadian advance near Authie, and a number of Canadians who had surrendered were afterwards murdered by the SS troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British operation Epsom, begun west of the city on 25 June, crossed the Odon and reached Hill 112, and it spoiled the last German attempt to break the Allied front with fresh armour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For Montgomery the battle also served to pull German armour onto the British front while the Americans pushed towards Cherbourg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the evening of 7 July, 467 heavy bombers of Bomber Command dropped 2,562 tons of bombs on the northern edge of the city, wrecking much of it, the university included, and causing between 300 and 400 French civilian casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> British and Canadian infantry attacked the next morning, and by 9 July they had cleared the city north of the Orne, though craters and rubble choked the streets and the bridges were blocked or under fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The operation, Charnwood, cost the attacking corps about 3,500 casualties and some 80 tanks, while the southern suburbs across the river stayed in German hands until the next offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The slow pace fed charges in Britain and America that Montgomery was too cautious, and the American official history calls the failure to take Caen by 1 July the greatest single disappointment of the invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 275–86, 309–16, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 4, 13–14, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-1.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 173, 181–84, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-10.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 132–33, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-6.html. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 64, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/64/data.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 207, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-7.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 158–72, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did both sides care so much about Caen?","a":"Because it was a road centre leading to the Seine and to the Paris–Orléans gap, and the flat plain beyond it suited tanks and airfields. The Germans saw it as the gateway to Paris and massed their reserves to defend it, so it became the hinge of the whole front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombing of 7 July help less than hoped?","a":"Because the craters and rubble it left choked the narrow streets and slowed the armour that was meant to exploit it, while the heaviest damage fell on the city and its people. The infantry still had to fight village by village before reaching the centre on 9 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Montgomery accused of caution?","a":"Because a city meant to fall on D-Day still held out a month later, and the ground gained looked small. His own aim, to draw German armour onto the British front while the Americans advanced in the west, was not widely understood outside his headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle for Caen","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/British_Sherman_tanks_and_a_6-pdr_anti-tank_gun_in_the_centre_of_Caen%2C_Normandy%2C_10_July_1944._B6924.jpg","title":"British troops in the centre of Caen, 1944","desc":"British Sherman tanks and a 6-pounder anti-tank gun in the centre of Caen on 10 July 1944, the day after the northern city was cleared.","credit":"Sgt Christie, No. 5 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Sherman_tanks_and_a_6-pdr_anti-tank_gun_in_the_centre_of_Caen,_Normandy,_10_July_1944._B6924.jpg","alt":"A street of damaged houses with a Sherman tank and soldiers in the middle distance and an anti-tank gun and its crew in the foreground, a church tower behind."}},
    "ww2-644": {"questions":["Coutances fell on 28 July 1944 and Avranches 3 days later after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke the German line along the Périers–Saint-Lô road in Normandy.","Of the 28,000 Germans the American First Army took prisoner in July 1944, 20,000 were captured in the last 6 days, after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had broken the front."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Cobra</b> was the American offensive west of Saint-Lô that broke through the German front in Normandy at the end of July 1944 and opened the way for the Allied breakout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bradley's First Army staff had drawn it up by 10 July as a blow by 7th Corps on a narrow front, to be followed by an armoured thrust deep into the German rear towards Coutances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was to open with an intense air bombardment of a rectangle about 5.5 by 2.2 kilometres (6,000 by 2,400 yards) just south of the Périers–Saint-Lô road, after which three infantry divisions would attack across the devastated ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bad weather halted the bombardment on 24 July after part of the bomber force had already struck, and the attack went in the next day after more than 1,500 heavy bombers had dropped over 3,300 tons of bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Some of those bombs fell short on American lines, killing 111 soldiers, General Lesley McNair among them, and wounding 490, and the 2 days of bombing cost nearly 900 American casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On the German side the shock was severe, for Army Group B reported bomb carpets of hitherto unknown dimensions, and communications in the Panzer Lehr Division's sector were destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first day's ground gains were small, but the defence had been broken, and on 28 July the First Army took Coutances, which completed the operation in its basic details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 31 July American armour had reached Avranches at the base of the Cotentin peninsula, and of the 28,000 German prisoners the First Army took in July, 20,000 were captured in the last 6 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 25 July the Canadians also attacked down the Falaise road in Operation Spring, and with about 1,500 casualties it was the Canadian Army's costliest day of the war apart from Dieppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That attack met seven German armoured divisions massed against the British front, and the air commander's despatch held that the carpet bombing had started the breakthrough that decided the battle of Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 197–200, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-11.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 380–81, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 228–46, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-12.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 230–37, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-8.html. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 323–31, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-17.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 183–94, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-8.html. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 65, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/65/data.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the attack made on such a narrow front?","a":"Because Bradley meant to concentrate the whole weight of the bombers and of 7th Corps on one small rectangle, break the defence there, and then pass armour through the gap into the German rear towards Coutances, instead of pushing slowly forward through the hedgerows along the whole line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did American bombs fall on American troops?","a":"Because clouds forced the bombers to change height in flight, smoke and dust from the first bombs hid the target line, some formations misread their landmarks, and one lead pilot released early. Bombs from 35 heavy and 42 medium bombers landed inside American lines on 25 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Canadians attack on the same day?","a":"Because Montgomery wanted the Germans to keep building up their strength on the Caen front rather than against the Americans. Operation Spring met very strong German armoured forces and was stopped with heavy losses, but those divisions stayed in the east while Cobra broke through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Cobra","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Saint_Lo_and_Vicinity_-_Operation_Cobra.jpg","title":"Map of Operation Cobra, 25–29 July 1944","desc":"A United States Army map of the Cobra operation west of Saint-Lô, showing the American advance towards Coutances.","credit":"U.S. Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Lo_and_Vicinity_-_Operation_Cobra.jpg","alt":"A coloured relief map of the country around Saint-Lô and Coutances with blue arrows and shading for the American advance and red lines for German positions."}},
    "ww2-645": {"questions":["Bradley's decision to halt the American advance just south of Argentan on 13 August 1944 left open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from which many Germans later escaped eastwards.","A British survey counted 344 armoured vehicles and 2,447 lorries and cars left inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the fighting in Normandy ended in August 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Falaise pocket</b> was the area between Falaise and Argentan in Normandy where the German Seventh Army and Panzer Group Eberbach were almost encircled by the Allied armies in August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It formed after Hitler ordered a counter-attack through Mortain towards Avranches, launched on the night of 6 August, which he called a unique, never recurring opportunity for a complete reversal of the situation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack failed, and while American forces swung north from Le Mans, the First Canadian Army drove south down the Falaise road behind a bombardment of 5,210 tons on the night of 7 to 8 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 13 August Bradley halted the American 15th Corps just south of Argentan, leaving a gap of less than 40 kilometres (25 miles) to the Canadians, who were still north of Falaise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The halt later became the subject of considerable dispute, and Bradley seemed afterwards to consider it a mistake while placing the responsibility on Montgomery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Allied fighter-bombers struck the columns crowding the narrowing gap, but the heavy bombers were kept out because the Ninth Air Force judged that large Allied casualties would be a practical certainty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Even so, misdirected air attacks on 18 August hit British and Canadian troops advancing north of the pocket, and the 51st Division suffered most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the evening of 19 August Polish and American troops met at Chambois, while Canadians held on at Saint-Lambert-sur-Dives and the Poles fought for the heights near Coudehard, and the gap was finally closed on 21 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later estimates of the Germans who escaped ranged from 20,000 to 40,000, few of them combat troops, while about 50,000 were captured and some 10,000 dead were found on the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A British survey of part of the area afterwards counted 344 armoured vehicles, 2,447 lorries and cars and 252 guns, and reported that the stench of dead horses forced its teams to hurry past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 206–07, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-11.html. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 506–13, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-26.html. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 528–58, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-27.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 260–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-10.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 444–48, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","Trafford Leigh-Mallory, “Air Operations by the Allied Expeditionary Air Force in N.W. Europe from November 15th, 1943 to September 30th, 1944,” despatch, supplement to the <i>London Gazette</i>, no. 37838, 2 January 1947, 65, https://www.thegazette.co.uk/London/issue/37838/supplement/65/data.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 252–54, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-8.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler's counter-attack make the encirclement possible?","a":"Because he sent his armoured divisions west through Mortain towards Avranches, deeper into the trap, just as American forces were swinging round from the south and the Canadians were pushing down from the north. When the attack failed, those divisions were caught between the two jaws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the gap not closed sooner?","a":"Because Bradley halted the Americans south of Argentan on 13 August, the army group boundary was not moved, and the inexperienced Canadian and Polish armoured divisions advanced slowly against fierce resistance. Contact came only on 19 August and the gap was not fully closed until 21 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the heavy bombers kept out of the pocket?","a":"Because the fronts were fluid and the bomb lines kept shifting, and the Ninth Air Force advised that bombing the pocket would almost certainly cause large British and American casualties. The targets were left mostly to fighter-bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Falaise pocket","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Three_French_boys_looking_at_a_knocked-out_German_Panther_tank_in_the_Falaise_pocket%2C_Normandy%2C_25_August_1944._B9665.jpg","title":"A wrecked Panther in the Falaise pocket","desc":"Three French boys look at a knocked-out German Panther tank in the Falaise pocket on 25 August 1944.","credit":"Capt Knight, No. 5 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_French_boys_looking_at_a_knocked-out_German_Panther_tank_in_the_Falaise_pocket,_Normandy,_25_August_1944._B9665.jpg","alt":"Three boys seen from behind standing in a village street in front of a large abandoned tank with a long gun."}},
    "ww2-646": {"questions":["Captain Dronne's tanks reached the Hôtel de Ville at 9:22 PM on 24 August 1944, the eve of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> being completed by Leclerc's division.","De Gaulle told the crowd at the Hôtel de Ville that the city had freed itself during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a claim that left little room for the Allied armies."],"abstract":"The <b>Liberation of Paris</b> was the freeing of the French capital from German occupation in August 1944, begun by a Resistance rising and completed by French and American troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eisenhower had meant to bypass the city, partly to put off the burden of feeding it, while promising that Leclerc's French 2nd Armoured Division would take part in its liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strikes spread through the public services from 10 August, and on 18 August Colonel Rol-Tanguy, commanding the Resistance forces of the Paris region, ordered a general mobilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From 19 August Resistance groups seized police stations, town halls and ministries, and that evening the Swedish consul general Raoul Nordling arranged a truce with the German commander, General Dietrich von Choltitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler ordered on 20 August that the battle for Paris be fought regardless of the city's destruction, but Choltitz kept to his understanding, while Resistance envoys hurried to beg the Allies to come at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 22 August Eisenhower changed his plan, and Bradley ordered Leclerc's division to march on the capital with the American 4th Infantry Division beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At 9:22 PM on 24 August Captain Dronne's tanks reached the Hôtel de Ville, and next day French troops stormed the Hôtel Meurice and Choltitz surrendered at the Prefecture of Police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> De Gaulle arrived that afternoon and told the crowd at the Hôtel de Ville that Paris had liberated itself with the help of the armies of France, and in London Churchill likened the rising to the famous days of the Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Order of the Liberation counts more than 1,500 of the city's fighters killed, 582 of them civilians, against 3,200 Germans killed and 12,800 captured, while Leclerc's division lost 71 killed on the road to Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within days Allied aircraft were landing food from England, while de Gaulle's parade on 26 August, held although the American corps commander had ordered Leclerc's troops to stay out of it, showed that liberation was also a contest for authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), 590–621, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-29.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 239–43, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-13.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Battle of Normandy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 454–57, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-1. [Open access]","“Paris,” Communes Compagnon de la Libération, Musée de l’Ordre de la Libération, https://www.ordredelaliberation.fr/fr/les-compagnons/les-communes/paris. [in French] [Open access]","“Philippe Leclerc de Hauteclocque,” Compagnons de la Libération, Musée de l’Ordre de la Libération, https://www.ordredelaliberation.fr/fr/compagnons/philippe-leclerc-de-hauteclocque. [in French] [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 28 September 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 403, col. 495, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 568, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-16.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Eisenhower first plan to bypass Paris?","a":"Because taking the city would add nothing immediate to the pursuit of the German armies, and once liberated its people would have to be fed from Allied supplies that were already stretched. He intended to pinch it out and enter when it was a sound military proposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Choltitz agree to a truce instead of crushing the rising?","a":"Because his garrison was small and scattered, he wanted to keep order and avoid street fighting that would kill civilians and wreck the city, and he hoped the rival Resistance groups would fall out among themselves. He later admitted that he lacked the strength as well as the will to suppress it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it matter who entered Paris first?","a":"Because the liberation of the capital was a test of who would govern France. A French division leading the way, and de Gaulle taking possession of the government ministries and speaking of a city freed by its own people, strengthened his provisional government's claim to authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Liberation of Paris","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Crowds_of_French_patriots_line_the_Champs_Elysees-edit2.jpg","title":"The Champs-Élysées, 26 August 1944","desc":"Crowds line the Champs-Élysées as French armour and half-tracks pass after the liberation of Paris, 26 August 1944.","credit":"Jack Downey, U.S. Office of War Information, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowds_of_French_patriots_line_the_Champs_Elysees-edit2.jpg","alt":"A colour photograph of armoured vehicles driving down a wide avenue lined with dense crowds, the Arc de Triomphe in the background and a banner reading Vive de Gaulle."}},
    "ww2-647": {"questions":["Churchill argued until early August 1944 against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the landing in Provence long planned under the code name Anvil, preferring to use the forces in Italy.","Marseille's peacetime capacity of 20,000 tons a day made it the great prize for supplying the armies that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put ashore in southern France in August 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Dragoon</b> was the Allied invasion of southern France on 15 August 1944, in which American and French forces landed on the coast of Provence east of Toulon, around Saint-Tropez and Saint-Raphaël.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Planned for many months under the code name Anvil, it was meant to seize Toulon and Marseille as supply ports and to drive north up the Rhône valley in support of the landings in Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Tehran in late 1943 the Soviet delegation proposed such an invasion, to the surprise of both Western allies, while the British long preferred to use the Mediterranean forces in Italy or farther east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Churchill made no effort to hide his distaste for it, and until 10 August his attitude was, in the words of the American naval historian, anything but Anvil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Combined Chiefs of Staff ordered it on 2 July after an appeal from Roosevelt, with Eisenhower arguing that France was the decisive theatre and that more divisions were available than the northern ports could supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Before dawn American and British paratroopers dropped near Le Muy to block German movement into the assault area, and by the end of the first day every beachhead was secure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> French divisions under General de Lattre de Tassigny took Toulon and Marseille, both of which surrendered on 28 August, in less than half the time the planners had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The beaches handled 280,000 tons of supplies in the first month, and by mid-September more troops and matériel were landing through the two ports than over the beaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the German Army Group G retreated up the Rhône, the Allies pursued it past Lyon to meet Patton's Third Army, and the American official history counts its losses in prisoners and cut-off garrisons at 131,250 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in September that the landing had led with inconceivable rapidity to the capture of both ports and that its army had taken over 80,000 prisoners before passing under Eisenhower's command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Jeffrey J. Clarke and Robert Ross Smith, <i>Riviera to the Rhine</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1993), 8–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Riviera/USA-E-Riviera-1.html. [Open access]","Jeffrey J. Clarke and Robert Ross Smith, <i>Riviera to the Rhine</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1993), 171–97, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Riviera/USA-E-Riviera-10.html. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Invasion of France and Germany, 1944–1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II 11 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1957), 222–27, 289–90, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-xi-the-invasion-of-france-and-germany-1944-1945. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 218–19, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-12.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 413–29, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-12.html. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 28 September 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 403, col. 473, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]","Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 2, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 116–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Logistics2/USA-E-Logistics2-4.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Americans insist on landing in southern France?","a":"Because they saw it as part of the attack on Germany through France, drawing German forces away from Normandy and above all opening Marseille, a port big enough to supply many more divisions than the northern ports could receive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Churchill oppose the operation for so long?","a":"Because he wanted the Mediterranean forces used in Italy and beyond, towards Trieste and the Ljubljana Gap, where he thought they could achieve more. He pressed Eisenhower and the American chiefs hard to drop the plan, and gave way only at the last moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did French troops take Toulon and Marseille?","a":"Because General Patch insisted that the French army should capture the two ports. That part of the plan, and the large share of French warships in the invasion, made the people of Provence feel that this was a French campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Dragoon","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/80-G-K-2112_Invasion_of_Southern_France%2C_August_1944.jpg","title":"Landing craft at Fréjus, August 1944","desc":"Tank landing craft and smaller landing craft unload supplies on the beach at Fréjus on 20 August 1944, five days after the Dragoon landings.","credit":"United States Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:80-G-K-2112_Invasion_of_Southern_France,_August_1944.jpg","alt":"A colour photograph of several grey landing craft beached side by side, a lorry on the sand and a coastal town and hills behind."}},
    "ww2-648": {"questions":["Hitler demanded a fanatical defence of the old coronation city of the emperors during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first fight for a major German city in the west.","American infantry of two divisions met on a hill on 16 October 1944 to close the ring in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 5 days before the garrison surrendered."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Aachen</b> was the American encirclement and capture of the German city of Aachen in October 1944, the first major German city that the Western Allies threatened with capture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In less than 2 weeks after crossing the Seine the Allies had advanced more than 320 kilometres (200 miles) to the German border, and by mid-September they had crossed the frontier in places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There the pursuit lost its momentum at the West Wall, Germany's frontier fortifications, chiefly because the armies had outrun their supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aachen, with fewer than 20,000 of its 165,000 pre-war inhabitants still at home, mattered to Hitler beyond its military value as the city where 32 emperors and kings had been anointed between 813 and 1531.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two American corps attacked on 2 October to encircle it, and Hitler's call for a last-ditch fight drew in German reinforcements from other parts of the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 October three Americans under a white flag delivered an ultimatum giving the garrison 24 hours to surrender, and its commander rejected it under last-stand orders from Hitler himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After heavy air and artillery bombardment, house-to-house fighting began inside the city on 14 October, and on 16 October men of the 30th and 1st Divisions met on a hill to close the ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The garrison commander, Colonel Wilck, surrendered in an air-raid bunker on 21 October, using two captured American soldiers to arrange it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two American divisions listed more than 11,000 prisoners, the 30th Division had lost some 3,000 men since 2 October, and the Germans admitted 5,100 casualties of all types.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the small areas taken near Aachen the Americans set up their first military government in Germany, appointing burgomasters and reviving German administration at town and village level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 244, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-14.html. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Siegfried Line Campaign</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 281–306, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Siegfried/USA-E-Siegfried-12.html. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Siegfried Line Campaign</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 307–20, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Siegfried/USA-E-Siegfried-13.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 304–06, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-17.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 595, 610, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-17.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis and A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 161–62, 421, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allied advance stop at the German border?","a":"Because the armies had raced more than 320 kilometres from the Seine in less than 2 weeks and outrun their supply lines, while the Germans manned the West Wall fortifications along their frontier. The pursuit lost its momentum there, and a winter of hard fighting followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler insist on holding Aachen to the last?","a":"Because it was the first major German city threatened with capture and carried a heritage precious to Nazi ideology as the coronation city of the medieval emperors. Losing it without a fight would have been a blow to prestige as well as to the defence of the West Wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the battle matter beyond its own ground?","a":"Because the Germans, though they failed to prevent the encirclement, imposed a costly delay on the American advance towards the Rhine, feeding in units piecemeal to stave off each crisis. Taking the city also opened the first area of Germany placed under Allied military government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of Aachen","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/SC_195638_-_Aided_by_the_armored_force%2C_Yank_infantry_moves_forward_to_engage_the_enemy_in_Aachen%2C_Germany._15_October%2C_1944._%2852598831960%29.jpg","title":"American infantry and tanks in Aachen","desc":"Infantry of the US 1st Division advance with armoured support through a street in Aachen on 15 October 1944.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SC_195638_-_Aided_by_the_armored_force,_Yank_infantry_moves_forward_to_engage_the_enemy_in_Aachen,_Germany._15_October,_1944._(52598831960).jpg","alt":"A rubble-strewn city street with two tanks and several helmeted soldiers moving forward between damaged apartment buildings."}},
    "ww2-649": {"questions":["Only 2,398 men came back across the lower Rhine on the night of 25 September 1944, of about 9,000 who had fought at Arnhem in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Netherlands.","American paratroopers crossed the Waal in canvas assault boats on 20 September 1944 to seize the Nijmegen bridge for the British tanks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> heading north."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Market Garden</b> was the Allied attempt in September 1944 to seize a chain of bridges in the Netherlands with airborne troops and to push a British armoured corps along them across the lower Rhine at Arnhem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Montgomery proposed it to Eisenhower on 10 September as an enlarged version of a cancelled smaller plan, and Eisenhower agreed, hoping for a bridgehead over the Rhine and a use for an airborne army idle since the summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Market, three and a half airborne divisions were to drop near Grave, Nijmegen and Arnhem to open a corridor more than 80 kilometres (50 miles) long northward from Eindhoven, while in Garden the 30th Corps drove up a single road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the largest airborne operation of the war, and on the first day, 17 September, 1,546 aircraft and 478 gliders carried about half the airborne strength, losing 35 aircraft and 13 gliders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the same time Montgomery hoped that the First Canadian Army could open the port of Antwerp and clear the Channel ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> At Arnhem the British 1st Airborne Division ran into the II SS Panzer Corps, two depleted panzer divisions refitting north-east of the town, which Model at once ordered into action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only a small force under Colonel Frost reached the north end of the Arnhem road bridge, while at Nijmegen American paratroopers crossed the Waal in canvas boats and took the Waal bridge on 20 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ground column's progress was, in Montgomery's words, depressingly slow, and on the night of 25 September the survivors at Arnhem were withdrawn across the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of about 9,000 men who had fought on the north bank only 2,398 came back, and the airborne phase cost 11,850 casualties in all, with an estimated 1,480 more in the 30th Corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American official history calls the operation a failure by the merciless logic of war, though it won a salient towards Germany, while Churchill told the Commons that the sacrifice at Arnhem had not been in vain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Siegfried Line Campaign</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 119–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Siegfried/USA-E-Siegfried-6.html. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Siegfried Line Campaign</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 174–99, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Siegfried/USA-E-Siegfried-8.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 279–81, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-16.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis and A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 45–47, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), 601–03, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-17.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 307–10, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-13.html. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 28 September 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 403, cols. 472–73, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/sep/28/war-and-international-situation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Eisenhower approve such a risky plan?","a":"Because the pursuit across France and Belgium was running out of supplies, and a quick bridgehead over the Rhine before the Germans could reorganise seemed worth the gamble. He also wanted to use the First Allied Airborne Army, which had been waiting for action since July and August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the paratroopers at Arnhem fare so badly?","a":"Because two SS panzer divisions refitting near Arnhem were thrown against them at once, only a small force reached the bridge, and the ground column, held up at Nijmegen and slowed on its single road, never broke through to them. Bad weather also delayed the Polish brigade sent to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the operation called a failure when it gained ground?","a":"Because its real purpose, a bridgehead over the lower Rhine at Arnhem, was not achieved and the British airborne division was largely destroyed. It did win the bridges at Nijmegen and a corridor into the Netherlands, which Churchill presented as a sacrifice not made in vain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Market Garden","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/A_fleet_of_Allied_aircraft_flies_overhead_as_paratroopers_of_the_Allied_Airborne_Command_float_groundward_in_the_invasion_of_the_Netherlands%2C_still_another_step_towards_the_liberation_of_Europe_HD-SN-99-02724.jpg","title":"Airborne landings in the Netherlands, 1944","desc":"Allied transport aircraft fly over the Netherlands as paratroopers descend on the first day of Operation Market Garden, 17 September 1944.","credit":"Tischler, Air Transport Command, U.S. Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_fleet_of_Allied_aircraft_flies_overhead_as_paratroopers_of_the_Allied_Airborne_Command_float_groundward_in_the_invasion_of_the_Netherlands,_still_another_step_towards_the_liberation_of_Europe_HD-SN-99-02724.jpg","alt":"An aerial photograph of a long line of aircraft crossing a hazy sky above flat Dutch farmland."}},
    "ww2-650": {"questions":["The Canadian official historian called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a Cinderella operation, long given more lip-service than priority by the Allied high command in the autumn of 1944.","Some 86,000 men of the German Fifteenth Army escaped across the West Scheldt in September 1944, before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cleared both banks of the estuary that autumn."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Scheldt</b> was the campaign of the First Canadian Army, with British and Polish formations under its command, from September to November 1944 to clear the Germans from both banks of the Scheldt estuary and open the port of Antwerp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> British armour had taken Antwerp with its wharves and docks intact on 4 September, but the Germans held both banks of the river's course of about 100 kilometres (60 miles) to the sea, so no ship could reach the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 22 September the German Fifteenth Army had ferried more than 86,000 men, 600 guns and 6,000 vehicles across the West Scheldt, in what the American official history calls a little Dunkirk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The port mattered because it lay only about 105 kilometres (65 miles) from the American depots at Liège, while Cherbourg was more than 640 kilometres (400 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet the fight to open it was long a Cinderella operation, given more lip-service than priority, until Eisenhower warned Montgomery in October that unless Antwerp was opened by mid-November all Allied operations would come to a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> South of the estuary the Canadians fought for the Breskens pocket across flooded polders and canals, in what one battalion's war diary called some of the fiercest fighting since D-Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bomber Command breached the sea dykes of Walcheren in October, flooding much of the island, and on 1 November British commandos landed through the gap at Westkapelle while other troops attacked at Flushing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German commander on the island, General Daser, surrendered at Middelburg on 6 November, and by 8 November the approaches to Antwerp were free of the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1 October to 8 November the First Canadian Army took 41,043 prisoners and suffered 12,873 casualties, almost exactly half of them Canadians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After 267 mines had been swept from the channel the first convoy entered Antwerp on 28 November, and by mid-December the port handled 19,000 tons a day, about 48 per cent of American tonnage landed on the Continent outside Marseille.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 357–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-15.html. [Open access]","C. P. Stacey, <i>The Victory Campaign: The Operations in North-West Europe, 1944–1945</i>, Official History of the Canadian Army in the Second World War 3 (Ottawa: Queen’s Printer, 1960), 392–425, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/Canada/CA/Victory/Victory-16.html. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Siegfried Line Campaign</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 207–30, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Siegfried/USA-E-Siegfried-9.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 295–96, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-16.html. [Open access]","Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 2, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 48–49, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Logistics2/USA-E-Logistics2-3.html. [Open access]","Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 2, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1959), 110, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Logistics2/USA-E-Logistics2-4.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Antwerp useless when it was captured?","a":"Because the port lies far up the Scheldt, and the Germans held both banks of the estuary and the island of Walcheren at its mouth. Until those banks were cleared and the channel swept of mines, no ship could reach the intact docks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the battle take so long?","a":"Because it was fought across low, flooded country of polders, dykes and canals that favoured the defence, against a determined enemy, and for weeks it lacked the priority and resources that the high command gave to other operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Eisenhower call Antwerp the first priority?","a":"Because the Allied advance had outrun its supply lines, and Antwerp lay close to the front, far nearer than Cherbourg. He warned that unless it was opened by the middle of November all Allied operations would come to a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of the Scheldt","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Alligator_amphibious_vehicles_passing_Terrepin_amphibious_vehicles_%28to_the_left%29_during_the_Battle_of_the_Scheldt_-_October_13%2C_1944.jpg","title":"Amphibious vehicles near Terneuzen, 1944","desc":"A column of Alligator amphibious vehicles passes Terrapin amphibians near Terneuzen during the Battle of the Scheldt, 13 October 1944.","credit":"Donald I. Grant, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alligator_amphibious_vehicles_passing_Terrepin_amphibious_vehicles_(to_the_left)_during_the_Battle_of_the_Scheldt_-_October_13,_1944.jpg","alt":"Tracked amphibious vehicles with soldiers aboard moving along a muddy track on the bank of a wide estuary."}},
    "ww2-651": {"questions":["Most of the truck companies on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were African American units, hauling fuel, food and ammunition from Normandy towards armies racing for the German border in 1944.","Starting on 25 August 1944, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried 412,193 tons of supplies across France in 81 days, until rail transfer points at Paris made it unnecessary."],"abstract":"The <b>Red Ball Express</b> was the emergency truck route the United States Army ran across France from 25 August to 16 November 1944, carrying supplies from Normandy to the armies driving towards Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Named from railway slang for a fast through freight, it ran round the clock on one-way highways reserved for its trucks, and Eisenhower recalled that every vehicle ran at least 20 hours a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The rules set a top speed of about 40 kilometres (25 miles) an hour, and convoys drove at night with full headlights because the German air force had all but vanished west of the Seine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 29 August, its best day, 132 truck companies with 5,958 vehicles delivered 12,342 tons, a record never equalled afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most companies on the route were African American, about 73 per cent of the truck companies in the theatre's Motor Transport Service were Black, and the National WWII Museum puts the share of Black drivers at three quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The route was needed because the pursuit after the breakout outran a supply plan built for a slower advance, which expected the Brittany ports and Le Havre to be working before the armies passed the Seine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Allied bombing had wrecked the French railways that might have carried the load, so supplies landed in Normandy had to go forward by road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In practice control was loose: convoys broke up, drivers ignored the speed limit, armies diverted loads, and turnaround time rose by 30 per cent in 10 days as trucks hunted for dumps further east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 81 days the express carried 412,193 tons, but trucks could not replace a working deep-water port, and Antwerp, captured on 4 September, stayed useless to the Allies for 83 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It ceased operations on 16 November, the day the Normandy beaches closed, after truck-to-rail transfer points around Paris had taken over most long-distance hauling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 1, <i>May 1941–September 1944</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (1953; repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1995), 558–68, https://archive.org/details/logisticalsuppor11rupp. [Open access]","Roland G. Ruppenthal, <i>Logistical Support of the Armies</i>, vol. 2, <i>September 1944–May 1945</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (1959; repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1995), 134–39, https://archive.org/details/logisticalsuppor02rupp. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 256–59, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-14.html. [Open access]","Ulysses Lee, <i>The Employment of Negro Troops</i>, United States Army in World War II: Special Studies (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1966), 632–34, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-4-1. [Open access]","Tyler Bamford, ““Keep ’em Rolling”: 82 Days on the Red Ball Express,” The National WWII Museum, 1 February 2021, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/red-ball-express. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 349, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 338, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies need a truck route across France at all?","a":"Because the pursuit after the breakout outran a supply plan built for a slower advance, and the French railways that could have carried the load had been wrecked by Allied bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the route work less smoothly than its plan promised?","a":"Because it was improvised within days, with too few military police to keep the roads reserved, so convoys broke up, speed limits were ignored and loads were diverted far beyond the official end of the route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Red Ball Express stop in November 1944?","a":"Because rail transfer points at Paris took over most long-distance hauling, and early in November the rule became that no more supplies would go beyond Paris by truck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Red Ball Express","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/MP_on_Red_Ball_route.jpg","title":"Red Ball Highway sign, France, 1944","desc":"A US Army military policeman beside a Red Ball Highway sign showing the day's tonnage target and urging drivers to keep 'em rolling.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MP_on_Red_Ball_route.jpg","alt":"A helmeted soldier points beside a large roadside sign reading Red Ball Highway, Stay on the Ball, Keep 'em Rolling, with a tonnage scale."}},
    "ww2-652": {"questions":["Figures given to Eisenhower put Allied losses in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 76,780, roughly equal to the lowest German estimate of German losses, but only the Allies could replace theirs.","Low cloud that grounded Allied aircraft and complete surprise helped more than 200,000 German troops open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 16 December 1944 against barely 80,000 Americans."],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Bulge</b> is the American name for the German offensive in the Ardennes that began on 16 December 1944, known to the Germans as Operation Watch on the Rhine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler first announced it to a small circle at his headquarters in East Prussia on 16 September 1944: a counter-attack out of the Ardennes with Antwerp as its objective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taking Antwerp, the port through which the Allies were supplied, was meant to split the British from the Americans and make the Western powers ready for peace, so that Germany could turn its strength against the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eisenhower later wrote that for a time he had only three divisions on some 120 kilometres (75 miles) of front there, a risk he and Bradley chose to run rather than halt their attacks elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> More than 200,000 German soldiers with about 600 tanks struck barely 80,000 Americans between Monschau and Echternach, achieving complete surprise while bad weather kept Allied aircraft on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Resistance on the Elsenborn ridge denied the Germans the northern roads, the defenders of Saint-Vith held up their timetable by nearly a week, and surrounded Bastogne refused to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By 27 December the Germans had gone over to the defensive on every sector, and by 28 January 1945 the bulge was erased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 600,000 American troops took part, making it the largest battle the United States Army has ever fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Estimates of German losses ran from 81,834 to 103,900, while figures given to Eisenhower put the casualties of the American and British divisions engaged at 76,780, so on the lowest German estimate the two sides lost roughly the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The difference, of which the Germans were well aware, was that the Allies could replace their losses in men and materiel and Germany could not, and the British official history calls the offensive ill-conceived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Hugh M. Cole, <i>The Ardennes: Battle of the Bulge</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 1–2, 16–18, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Ardennes/USA-E-Ardennes-1.html. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 52–53, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-2.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 396–97, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-20.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 355, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","“Die Ardennenoffensive 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ardennenoffensive-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Edward G. Lengel, “The Malmedy Massacre,” The National WWII Museum, 14 January 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/malmedy-massacre. [Open access]","Keith Huxen, “The Battle of the Bulge,” The National WWII Museum, 18 December 2019, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/battle-of-the-bulge. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 371, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler aim the offensive at Antwerp?","a":"Because Antwerp was the port through which the Allied armies were supplied, and taking it was meant to split the British from the Americans and push the Western powers towards peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the offensive fail despite its surprise?","a":"Because the northern attack moved slowly, the southern one never crossed the Meuse, and fresh American troops together with German supply shortages ended its momentum within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a roughly equal loss a defeat for Germany?","a":"Because the Allies could replace their losses in men and materiel and Germany could not, as the Germans themselves well knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Battle of the Bulge","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/American_soldiers_of_the_289th_Infantry_Regiment_march_along_the_snow-covered_road_on_their_way_to_cut_off_the_Saint_Vith-Houffalize_road_in_Belgium._-_NARA_-_531244.gif","title":"American infantry in the Ardennes, January 1945","desc":"Men of the 289th Infantry Regiment march along a snow-covered forest road to cut the Saint-Vith–Houffalize road on 24 January 1945.","credit":"U.S. Army Signal Corps (111-SC-199406), public domain, via the National Archives and Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_soldiers_of_the_289th_Infantry_Regiment_march_along_the_snow-covered_road_on_their_way_to_cut_off_the_Saint_Vith-Houffalize_road_in_Belgium._-_NARA_-_531244.gif","alt":"A file of helmeted soldiers in winter coats walks along a snowy track between tall snow-laden fir trees."}},
    "ww2-653": {"questions":["In all, 74 SS men, Joachim Peiper and Sepp Dietrich among them, went on trial at Dachau in 1946 for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and other killings along Peiper's line of march.","Yank, the Army weekly, printed survivors' accounts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in January 1945, while the bodies of the murdered prisoners still lay in ground held by the Germans."],"abstract":"The <b>Malmedy massacre</b> was the killing of American prisoners of war by men of Kampfgruppe Peiper, the spearhead of the 1st SS Panzer Division, near Malmedy in Belgium on 17 December 1944, the second day of the Ardennes offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between noon and 1:00 PM the column ran into a convoy of Battery B of the 285th Field Artillery Observation Battalion moving south from Malmedy and shot it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Germans captured 113 men and herded them into a field, where at a signal they were shot down, while passing tanks and half-tracks also fired into the group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> SS men then walked among the wounded shooting those who moved; a few survived by feigning death, and the museum's accounts give 84 killed against the official history's count of at least 86.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Survivors reached American lines that afternoon, the Inspector General's office took statements within hours, and in January 1945 Yank, the Army weekly, printed them while the bodies still lay in ground held by the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The killing at the crossroads was not isolated, for the official history finds that by 20 December Peiper's command had murdered about 350 American prisoners and at least 100 Belgian civilians at twelve places along its line of march.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 16 May 1946 a United States military court at Dachau opened the trial of 74 men, among them Peiper and Sepp Dietrich, commander of the Sixth SS Panzer Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 16 July all were convicted, Peiper and 45 others were sentenced to death, and Dietrich received life imprisonment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The convicted men's claims that interrogators had extracted their statements by abuse were taken up in Germany and the United States, and in 1949 a Senate subcommittee investigated them, with Senator Joseph McCarthy playing a leading part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The allegations were found to be largely fabricated, yet every death sentence was commuted, and Peiper and Dietrich were released from Landsberg prison in 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Hugh M. Cole, <i>The Ardennes: Battle of the Bulge</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), 261–64, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Ardennes/USA-E-Ardennes-11.html. [Open access]","Edward G. Lengel, “The Malmedy Massacre,” The National WWII Museum, 14 January 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/malmedy-massacre. [Open access]","Kim Guise, “Justice After the 1944 Malmedy Massacre,” The National WWII Museum, 16 July 2021, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/justice-after-1944-malmedy-massacre. [Open access]","“War Crimes Trials: Malmedy,” film, Munich 164, 16 May 1946, RG-60.2288, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://collections.ushmm.org/search/catalog/irn1001408. [Open access]","“War Crimes Trials: Malmedy, Sentencing,” film, Munich 298, 16 July 1946, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://collections.ushmm.org/search/catalog/irn1001437. [Open access]","Keith Huxen, “The Battle of the Bulge,” The National WWII Museum, 18 December 2019, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/battle-of-the-bulge. [Open access]","Ed Cunningham, “Massacre at Malmedy,” <i>Yank, the Army Weekly</i> 3, no. 32 (26 January 1945), https://archive.org/details/1945-01-26YankMagazine. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the killing near Malmedy become known so quickly?","a":"Because survivors who had feigned death escaped and reached American lines that afternoon, and the rumour that prisoners were being killed spread to the forward divisions by the same evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the official history treat it as more than a single atrocity?","a":"Because Peiper's command killed prisoners at twelve places by 20 December, about 350 Americans and at least 100 Belgian civilians, and some of his subordinates accepted the killing of prisoners as a command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was nobody executed for the massacre?","a":"Because claims that the confessions had been extracted by abuse won support in Germany and the United States, and successive reviews commuted the death sentences, the last of them by 1951, although the abuse claims were found largely fabricated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Malmedy massacre","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Malmedyprocessen.jpg","title":"The Malmedy trial at Dachau, July 1946","desc":"Defendants wearing numbered cards, Joachim Peiper among them, listen to the defence's closing statement on 11 July 1946.","credit":"Unknown U.S. Army photographer (111-SC-249309), public domain, via the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum and Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malmedyprocessen.jpg","alt":"Rows of seated men in a courtroom, many with large numbered cards hanging from their necks."}},
    "ww2-654": {"questions":["On the afternoon of 5 June 1944, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scribbled a note accepting full blame should the landings in Normandy fail, hours after deciding to launch the invasion.","At Reims in May 1945, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused a German attempt at a partial surrender in the west and insisted on the unconditional surrender of all German forces together."],"abstract":"<b>Dwight D. Eisenhower</b> was the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States\">American</span> general who, as Supreme Commander of the Allied Expeditionary Force, directed the invasion of north-west Europe in 1944 and the campaigns against Germany that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States_Military_Academy\">West Point</span> graduate of 1915, he spent the First World War training troops at home and rose through staff posts between the wars, including years under Douglas MacArthur in the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Called to the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States_Department_of_War\">War Department</span> after Pearl Harbor, he arrived in England in June 1942 to command the American forces in the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"European_theatre_of_World_War_II\">European theatre</span>, and that November he commanded the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Allies_of_World_War_II\">Allied</span> landings in North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As Allied commander in the Mediterranean he went on to direct the operations in Tunisia, Sicily and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Franklin_D._Roosevelt\">Roosevelt</span> chose him for the cross-Channel command in December 1943 because he wanted George Marshall to stay in Washington, remarking that he could not sleep at night with his <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Chief_of_Staff_of_the_United_States_Army\">chief of staff</span> out of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The appointment was announced on Christmas Eve 1943, after Stalin had pressed at Tehran to know who would carry responsibility for the invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before dawn on 5 June 1944, against uncertain weather forecasts, he decided to launch Overlord, and that afternoon he wrote a note accepting full blame should the landings fail; next morning some 150,000 Allied troops came ashore in Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> From 1 September 1944 he took control of field operations himself and kept pressure on the enemy along a broad front, refusing Montgomery's demand for one thrust under a single commander with first call on supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He told Montgomery that any pencil-like thrust into Germany would meet certain destruction while the enemy still had reserves, and by the end of March 1945 he commanded almost four and a half million men in ninety divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Reims he saw the German offer of a partial surrender in the west as an attempt to split the alliance and insisted on an immediate unconditional surrender of all forces, which Jodl signed early on 7 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 23, 30–32, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-1.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, “Biographical Sketches,” in <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 7–8, 15, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-Bio.html. [Open access]","“General Dwight D. Eisenhower’s Order of the Day (1944),” Milestone Documents, National Archives and Records Administration, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/general-eisenhowers-order-of-the-day. [Open access]","“Army Years,” Dwight D. Eisenhower Presidential Library, Museum and Boyhood Home, National Archives and Records Administration, https://www.eisenhowerlibrary.gov/eisenhowers/army-years. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 94–95, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 321–22, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-15.html. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 335–36, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2 July 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was an American given command of the invasion?","a":"Because by 1943 it was clear that the United States would furnish most of the troops, under the rule that command went to the nation supplying the majority, and Roosevelt preferred to keep Marshall in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Eisenhower refuse Montgomery's single thrust?","a":"Because he believed a pencil-like thrust into Germany would meet certain destruction while the enemy still had reserves, and that without Antwerp working the Allies could not maintain a force deep inside Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he insist on one total surrender at Reims?","a":"Because he saw the German attempt at partial surrenders in the west as an effort to break up the alliance, so he demanded unconditional surrender on all fronts, allowing 48 hours to pass the order to the troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dwight D. Eisenhower","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Eisenhower_d-day.jpg","title":"Eisenhower with paratroopers, 5 June 1944","desc":"Eisenhower talks with men of the 502nd Parachute Infantry Regiment, 101st Airborne Division, at Greenham Common before they took off for Normandy.","credit":"Unknown U.S. Army photographer, public domain, via the Library of Congress and Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eisenhower_d-day.jpg","alt":"A general in a peaked cap talks to a group of paratroopers with blackened faces and full kit."}},
    "ww2-655": {"questions":["Montgomery objected that Eisenhower's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would spread the Allies' supplies so thin that nowhere would they be strong enough to win decisive results before winter.","At Malta in early 1945 the Combined Chiefs of Staff accepted Eisenhower's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once he promised to cross the Rhine in the north without waiting to close the whole river."],"abstract":"The <b>broad front strategy</b> was General Eisenhower's plan for the advance into Germany in 1944–45: to keep pressure on the enemy north and south of the Ardennes and close up to the Rhine along its length, rather than stake everything on one thrust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from pre-invasion planning that favoured a main axis north of the Ardennes towards the Ruhr and a second through Metz, to keep the Germans guessing and stretch their forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Montgomery urged instead, from August 1944, that the mass of the Allied armies advance northwards under one commander, clear the coast as far as Antwerp and drive into the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his own account, one full-blooded thrust across the Rhine would win quickly, while a broad front would spread supplies so thin that nowhere would the Allies be strong enough for decisive results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eisenhower replied that no reallocation of resources could sustain a thrust to Berlin and that the Allies had to cross the Rhine on a wide front and seize both the Ruhr and the Saar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Brussels on 10 September he refused to consider what he called a pencil-like thrust, which he believed would meet certain destruction while the enemy still had reserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British official history thinks he exaggerated the difference, since he did give the northern advance priority, though not under one commander and not to the exclusion of the southern advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To many British observers the Ardennes offensive showed the danger of the policy, while American commanders believed that opinion at home demanded a major thrust south of the Ardennes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Malta the Combined Chiefs of Staff accepted his plan in February 1945 after he promised to seize the Rhine crossings in the north without waiting to close the river throughout its length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Montgomery argued after the war that full priority for the north would have ended the campaign sooner, but the official history doubts that faster progress could have been sustained without an equally fast improvement in supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 94–95, 348–49, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 253–55, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-14.html. [Open access]","Bernard Law Montgomery, <i>Normandy to the Baltic</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1947), 118–20, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.183410. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 335–36, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]","“Report of the Combined Chiefs of Staff to President Roosevelt and Prime Minister Churchill,” C.C.S. 776/3, 9 February 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers: The Conferences at Malta and Yalta, 1945</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1955), document 419, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Malta/d419. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 409–14, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-21.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Eisenhower want pressure along the whole front?","a":"Because his planners held that a single advance north of the Ardennes would lead to a head-on collision on a narrow front, while threats through more than one gap would keep the Germans guessing and stretch their forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Montgomery oppose it?","a":"Because he thought the Allies' supplies could sustain only one full-blooded thrust, and that spreading them along the front would give the Germans time to recover and drag the fighting into a long winter campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did national politics enter the dispute?","a":"Because many British observers saw the Ardennes offensive as proof of the policy's danger, while American commanders believed opinion at home demanded a major American thrust south of the Ardennes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/%28Photograph_of_Lt._General_Omar_N._Bradley%2C_Air_Chief_Marshal_Sir_Arthur_Tedder%2C_General_Dwight_D._Eisenhower%2C_Field_Marshal_Sir_Bernard_Montgomery%2C_and_Lt._General_William_H._Simps_-_DPLA_-_36cf49455997568f9d5f3cd8c27e5966.jpg","title":"Bradley, Tedder, Eisenhower, Montgomery, Simpson","desc":"The Allied commanders on the steps of Ninth Army headquarters at Maastricht after a council.","credit":"U.S. Army photograph, public domain, via the Dwight D. Eisenhower Presidential Library, DPLA and Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Photograph_of_Lt._General_Omar_N._Bradley,_Air_Chief_Marshal_Sir_Arthur_Tedder,_General_Dwight_D._Eisenhower,_Field_Marshal_Sir_Bernard_Montgomery,_and_Lt._General_William_H._Simps_-_DPLA_-_36cf49455997568f9d5f3cd8c27e5966.jpg","alt":"Five senior officers stand talking on the stone steps of a brick building, soldiers in helmets behind them."},"wiki":"Broad front versus narrow front controversy in World War II"},
    "ww2-656": {"questions":["A smoke screen along 32 kilometres of front hid the build-up for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for which the British stockpiled 60,000 tons of ammunition before crossing near Rees in March 1945.","Operation Varsity dropped two airborne divisions east of the Rhine on 24 March 1945 in support of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the assault that Eisenhower watched from a church tower beside Simpson."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Plunder</b> was the assault crossing of the Rhine north of the Ruhr by Montgomery's 21st Army Group, with the British Second Army and the US Ninth Army attacking side by side, on the night of 23–24 March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By then the river had already been crossed further south, at the Remagen railway bridge seized intact on 7 March and by Patton's Third Army at Oppenheim on 22 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A SHAEF directive of 8 March fixed the date for the 24th, only 2 weeks after the battle of the Rhineland ended, with the mission of a bridgehead from which to isolate the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Ninth Army's commander had wanted a surprise crossing early in March, but Montgomery kept to the long-prepared joint assault, in which the American part near Rheinberg was called Operation Flashpoint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The build-up rivalled D-Day: by 23 March the British had stockpiled 60,000 tons of ammunition, and a smoke screen hid the preparations along 32 kilometres (20 miles) of front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> British troops crossed near Rees at 9:00 PM on 23 March, commandos entered Wesel after a heavy raid by Bomber Command, and two American divisions crossed south of Wesel early on the 24th at little cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reversing the usual order, the airborne divisions of Operation Varsity dropped the next morning close behind the German front, 21,680 men carried by 1,696 transport aircraft and 1,348 gliders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The US official history doubts the drop was needed, since the 17th Airborne Division lost 159 killed, 522 wounded and 840 missing on the first day for objectives ground troops could probably have taken more cheaply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eisenhower watched the American bombardment from a church tower beside Simpson, with 29 divisions and seven brigades under Montgomery's command that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The bridgehead stayed cramped until the British broke out on 28 March, and on 30 March the Ninth Army's armour crossed the Lippe and drove east as the northern arm of the encirclement of the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 297–318, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-14.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 427–31, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-21.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 282–83, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Mark T. Calhoun, “General William H. Simpson’s Ninth US Army and the Crossing of the Rhine,” The National WWII Museum, 28 March 2023, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/general-william-h-simpsons-ninth-us-army-and-crossing-rhine. [Open access]","Ninth United States Army, <i>Conquer: The Story of Ninth Army, 1944–1945</i> (Washington, DC: Infantry Journal Press, 1947), 190–91, 211, https://archive.org/details/ConquerTheStoryOfNinthArmy. [Open access]","Bernard Law Montgomery, <i>Normandy to the Baltic</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1947), 197–203, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.183410. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 424–26, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Ninth Army not cross the Rhine earlier in March?","a":"Because Montgomery chose to keep to the plan, months in preparation, for a simultaneous British and American assault, and withheld permission for an early surprise crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the US official history question Operation Varsity?","a":"Because the German defenders were weak, the airborne objectives could probably have been taken by ground troops with fewer casualties, and the drop gave the bridgehead no more depth than the infantry won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the breakout take most of a week?","a":"Because muddy, roadless ground around the American bridgeheads and stronger resistance in the north slowed the advance, and the British kept the Wesel crossings until 31 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Plunder","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Crossing_the_Rhine_in_Buffaloes_at_Wesel.jpg","title":"Crossing the Rhine at Wesel, 24 March 1945","desc":"Men of the 1st Cheshire Regiment on the bank of the Rhine at Wesel with Buffalo amphibious vehicles, a wrecked bridge behind them.","credit":"British Army photographer (IWM BU 2336), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crossing_the_Rhine_in_Buffaloes_at_Wesel.jpg","alt":"Soldiers with rifles and packs gather on a muddy riverbank beside amphibious vehicles, a broken steel bridge spanning the river behind."}},
    "ww2-657": {"questions":["About 317,000 Germans surrendered when resistance in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended on 18 April 1945, more prisoners than the Soviet forces had taken at Stalingrad.","Rather than fight block by block through Germany's industrial heart, Eisenhower chose encirclement, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that resulted was cleared by First and Ninth Army troops in April 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Ruhr pocket</b> was the encirclement of the German Army Group B in Germany's Ruhr industrial region by the US First and Ninth Armies, closed on 1 April 1945 and destroyed by 18 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seizing the Ruhr had been part of the Allied plan since before D-Day, and Eisenhower preferred a double envelopment to a frontal assault because it would sever the industrial region from the rest of Germany and destroy one of the enemy's last major forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The First Army broke out of the Remagen bridgehead on 25 March and drove east, while the Ninth Army, once over the Rhine near Wesel, struck east along the northern edge of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their spearheads met at Lippstadt early in the afternoon of Easter Sunday, 1 April, trapping Field Marshal Walter Model's army group, all of the Fifth Panzer Army and most of the Fifteenth in a pocket about 50 by 120 kilometres (30 by 75 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Model refused to surrender while he could pin down Allied forces, though there is evidence that he did not pass on orders for widespread destruction of plants and communications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A German Twelfth Army, hastily formed east of the Elbe, was sent west with the hopeless task of opening exits for the trapped forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the pocket the Americans freed thousands of forced labourers and, at Hemer, 23,302 prisoners of war, most of them Russians, living in appalling conditions of filth, disease and hunger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Resistance ended on 18 April, and some 317,000 Germans were captured, more than the Soviet forces had taken at Stalingrad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ninth Army's share of the battle cost it about 2,400 casualties, and the First Army's losses were probably about three times as high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Model, who had said that a field marshal does not become a prisoner, shot himself in a forest north of Düsseldorf on 21 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 359–72, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-16.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 436–40, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-22.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 324, 355, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “Death in the West: The Battle of the Ruhr Pocket,” The National WWII Museum, 16 April 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/ruhr-pocket-offensive. [Open access]","Mark T. Calhoun, “General William H. Simpson’s Ninth US Army and the Crossing of the Rhine,” The National WWII Museum, 28 March 2023, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/general-william-h-simpsons-ninth-us-army-and-crossing-rhine. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 429–32, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Eisenhower encircle the Ruhr rather than attack it directly?","a":"Because a frontal battle through its cities, factories and millions of civilians threatened enormous casualties, so the plan was a pincer of two armies meeting behind the German army group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Model not surrender at once?","a":"Because he wanted to pin down Allied forces that might be used elsewhere, and he held that a field marshal does not become a prisoner; when resistance collapsed he dispersed his staff and shot himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the pocket matter beyond the prisoners?","a":"Because the Ruhr was one of the last heavy industrial centres left to the Reich, and losing it with its army group also drew a newly formed German army into a hopeless relief attempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ruhr pocket","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/WWII%2C_Europe%2C_Germany%2C_%22German_POWs_-_captured_by_American_Airborne_troops_in_the_Ruhr%22_-_NARA_-_195461.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-WWII%2C_Europe%2C_Germany%2C_%22German_POWs_-_captured_by_American_Airborne_troops_in_the_Ruhr%22_-_NARA_-_195461.tif.jpg","title":"German prisoners in the Ruhr, 1945","desc":"A field packed with German soldiers captured by American airborne troops in the Ruhr.","credit":"U.S. Army photograph (NARA 195461), public domain, via the National Archives and Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WWII,_Europe,_Germany,_%22German_POWs_-_captured_by_American_Airborne_troops_in_the_Ruhr%22_-_NARA_-_195461.tif","alt":"Thousands of men in uniform sit and stand crowded together in a field beside a village road."}},
    "ww2-658": {"questions":["Rumours of a National Redoubt in the Alps, fed by Goebbels and never real, weighed on Eisenhower's planning for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the spring of 1945.","Ninth Army troops reached the Elbe near Magdeburg on 11 April 1945 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then held on Bradley's orders to wait for the Red Army instead of pushing on to Berlin."],"abstract":"The <b>Western Allied invasion of Germany</b> was the advance of American, British, Canadian and French armies across the Rhine and into the heart of the Reich from late March 1945 until the German surrenders in early May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end of March almost four and a half million Allied soldiers in ninety divisions faced a German army in the west that intelligence rated at the equivalent of only 26 full divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 28 March Eisenhower moved the main effort from Montgomery's 21st Army Group in the north to Bradley's 12th Army Group in the centre, which was to drive towards Leipzig and Dresden and meet the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Berlin had ceased to be the objective, for Soviet troops on the Oder stood about 50 kilometres (30 miles) from it while his own were 440 kilometres (275 miles) away, and Eisenhower judged a race for the city worse than unwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Churchill protested, but the American Chiefs of Staff backed their commander, and Stalin replied that Berlin had lost its strategic significance even as his armies redeployed to take it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Rumours of a last-ditch National Redoubt in the Alps, fed by Goebbels's propaganda, also played a part, although the redoubt did not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The US Ninth Army reached the Elbe near Magdeburg on 11 April, and next day its troops entered Tangermünde, about 85 kilometres (53 miles) from Berlin, but Bradley held it on the river to await the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the north the British took Bremen and Hamburg, entered Lübeck on 2 May and reached the Baltic at Wismar only hours before Russian tanks appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 4 May the German forces facing Montgomery in north-west Germany surrendered at Lüneburg Heath, to take effect the next morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Early on 7 May Jodl signed the unconditional surrender of all German forces at Eisenhower's headquarters in Reims, and it was repeated at Berlin-Karlshorst just after midnight on 8–9 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 322–23, 338–42, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-15.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 451–53, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-23.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 324, 353, 357, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 468–69, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 433, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]","Bernard Law Montgomery, <i>Normandy to the Baltic</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1947), 218–20, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.183410. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Eisenhower not make Berlin his objective?","a":"Because the Red Army stood on the Oder about 50 kilometres from the city while his nearest troops were some 440 kilometres away, any ground taken beyond the Elbe would have to be handed to the Soviets, and supplying a strong force that far from the Rhine would have tied down units along the rest of the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British object to the change of plan?","a":"Because Churchill held that the fall of Berlin would be the supreme signal of defeat to the German people, and feared what a Soviet claim to have taken it would mean for the future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the British dash to Lübeck matter?","a":"Because it sealed off the Jutland peninsula, and British airborne troops reached Wismar on the Baltic only a few hours before Russian tanks arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Western Allied invasion of Germany","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Advance_through_Germany_-_5-18_April_1945.jpg","title":"The advance to the Elbe, 5–18 April 1945","desc":"A US Military Academy campaign map of the reduction of the Ruhr pocket and the Allied advance to the Elbe and Mulde rivers.","credit":"Department of History, United States Military Academy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Advance_through_Germany_-_5-18_April_1945.jpg","alt":"A coloured map of Germany with blue arrows for the Allied armies pushing east to the Elbe and red lines for the German positions."}},
    "ww2-659": {"questions":["President Truman announced on 27 April 1945 that the enemy had been cut in two, 2 days after the meeting of armies remembered as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a river in Saxony.","Soviet memory turned the handshakes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into an oath never to allow another war, a symbol the Cold War reshaped and later anniversaries revived."],"abstract":"<b>Elbe Day</b>, 25 April 1945, was the day American and Soviet troops first met in the middle of Germany, on and near the river Elbe around Torgau in Saxony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The US First Army had been told to halt on the Mulde and allowed only small patrols across it, but several roving units went far beyond the limit of 8 kilometres (5 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At 11:30 AM a patrol of the 69th Infantry Division under Lieutenant Albert Kotzebue met a lone Soviet horseman at Leckwitz, then crossed the Elbe near Strehla in a sailboat to meet men of the 175th Rifle Regiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At 4:00 PM Lieutenant William Robertson, who had set out only to look for prisoners, climbed across the wrecked bridge at Torgau and met a Soviet soldier on its girders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because Kotzebue had sent back the wrong map coordinates, Robertson's meeting, the second of the day, was the one confirmed first and the one that went into history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the same day Soviet spearheads met north-west of Potsdam and closed the ring round Berlin, and Eisenhower afterwards named the units at Torgau as his 69th Division and the Red Army's 58th Guards Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Divisional commanders met formally opposite Torgau on 26 April, and the American, British and Soviet governments announced the junction together at 6:00 PM on 27 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> President Truman's statement declared the enemy cut in two and presented the meeting as proof that nations able to fight together across distance and language could also work together for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the Germans it meant that each of their remaining forces had to fight its own battle, as prisoners taken since the beginning of April passed a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Soviet and Russian memory the handshakes became the 'oath of the Elbe', a promise never to allow the horrors of another war, a symbol later bent to Cold War propaganda and revived in joint statements by Russian and American leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 448–56, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-19.html. [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 452–53, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-23.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 328, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Statement by the President Announcing the Junction of Anglo-American and Soviet Forces in Germany,” 27 April 1945, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-announcing-the-junction-anglo-american-and-soviet-forces-germany. [Open access]","Александр Борисович Ручкин, “Восток встречается с Западом: встреча советских и американских войск на реке Эльба в воспоминаниях участников,” <i>Знание. Понимание. Умение</i>, no. 2 (2020), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/vostok-vstrechaetsya-s-zapadom-vstrecha-sovetskih-i-amerikanskih-voysk-na-reke-elba-v-vospominaniyah-uchastnikov. [in Russian] [Open access]","Dwight D. Eisenhower, <i>Crusade in Europe</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1948), 448, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.226615. [Open access]","Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 482, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first meeting not get the credit?","a":"Because Lieutenant Kotzebue reported the wrong map coordinates, so his meeting could not be confirmed, while Robertson brought four Soviet soldiers back to his own headquarters as living proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did American patrols go so far beyond their limit?","a":"Because everyone expected the Russians at any moment and wanted to meet them first, and orders to contact the Russians or scout the area were read broadly enough to justify pressing on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the meeting matter militarily?","a":"Because it cut the German forces in two, leaving each part to fight its own battle, when well over a million prisoners had already been taken since the start of April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Elbe Day","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/ElbeDay1945_%28NARA_ww2-121%29.jpg","title":"Robertson and Silvashko, April 1945","desc":"Lieutenant William Robertson and Lieutenant Alexander Silvashko of the Red Army embrace before an East Meets West sign, a photograph probably posed shortly after the meeting.","credit":"Pfc. William E. Poulson, U.S. Army (111-SC-205228), public domain, via the National Archives and Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ElbeDay1945_(NARA_ww2-121).jpg","alt":"An American soldier in a knitted cap and a Soviet soldier with a medal on his tunic stand arm in arm, smiling, in front of a flag and a sign."}},
    "ww2-660": {"questions":["From June 1944 Germany fought a full <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with about 2.2 million men facing 6 million Soviet troops in the east and 58 divisions stationed in the west.","Hitler's Ardennes gamble of December 1944 aimed to escape his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by forcing the Western powers to make peace, freeing his armies to turn on the Red Army."],"abstract":"A <b>two-front war</b> is a war fought against enemies on two separate fronts at once, and from June 1944 Germany faced one in full, with the Western Allies in France and the Red Army in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the Germans the second front existed before it was a reality, because the threat of invasion divided their armed forces and split their command between the army high command, which ran the Eastern Front, and the armed forces high command, which ran every other theatre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Officers in the east joked that 53 per cent of the army fought in Russia while the other 47 per cent sat in western Europe waiting for an invasion that never came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In April 1944 Jodl answered that, of 131 divisions in the other theatres, only 41 were equipped well enough for the Eastern Front and 32 of those were already engaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That spring German strength in the east fell to 2,242,649 men against more than 6 million Soviet troops, while 58 German divisions were stationed in the west, most of them near Calais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The long, brutal campaign against the Soviet Union had crippled the German army even before the Allies forced their way ashore in Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the German Historical Museum's count over 3.8 million Germans died on the Eastern Front, far more than two-thirds of all German soldiers killed in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The west still mattered, and Hitler's Ardennes offensive of December 1944 used divisions partly withdrawn from the east, in the hope of making the Western powers ready for peace so that he could turn on the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When it failed the Sixth Panzer Army was ordered to the Russian front, and by late March 1945 the German divisions in the west amounted to only about 26 complete ones against Eisenhower's ninety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The British official history adds that the broad Allied front forced a matching extension of the German front, far more than the Germans could defend for long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Earl F. Ziemke, <i>Stalingrad to Berlin: The German Defeat in the East</i>, Army Historical Series (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 309–11, https://archive.org/details/StalingradToBerlinTheGermanDefeatInTheEast. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Ostfront,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ostfront. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Landung in der Normandie 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2 July 2021, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/landung-in-der-normandie-1944. [in German] [Open access]","“Die Ardennenoffensive 1944,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/ardennenoffensive-1944. [in German] [Open access]","Forrest C. Pogue, <i>The Supreme Command</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954), 396, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Supreme/USA-E-Supreme-20.html. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 7–9, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-1.html. [Open access]","L. F. Ellis with A. E. Warhurst, <i>Victory in the West</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Defeat of Germany</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1968), 355, https://archive.org/details/victory-west-vol-2. [Open access]","Charles B. MacDonald, <i>The Last Offensive</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1973), 338, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Last/USA-E-Last-15.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the threat of invasion weigh on Germany before it came?","a":"Because divisions had to be held in the west against a landing, so both the forces and the command were divided between the Eastern Front and the other theatres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the east cost Germany so much more?","a":"Because the war there was fought on a far larger scale, and over 3.8 million German soldiers died on the Eastern Front, far more than two-thirds of all German soldiers killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the west still matter to the outcome?","a":"Because Hitler spent his last reserves in the Ardennes hoping to knock the Western powers out of the war, and the broad Allied front stretched the German line beyond what it could hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Two-front war","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Second_world_war_europe_1943-1945_map_en.png","title":"Europe, 1943–1945","desc":"A map of the Allied advances from east and west that shrank the area under German control between 1943 and May 1945.","credit":"San Jose, relabelled into English by Jarry1250, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_world_war_europe_1943-1945_map_en.png","alt":"A map of Europe shaded in reds for areas taken by the Allies at successive dates, with arrows converging on a shrinking blue area of German control."}},
    "ww2-661": {"questions":["Rule across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran from Polish provinces annexed outright to a Denmark that kept its own government until a military emergency was declared in August 1943.","The Nuremberg tribunal found that at least five million people were deported to Germany as forced labourers from the countries of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the war."],"abstract":"<b>German-occupied Europe</b> is the name for the countries and territories that Nazi Germany conquered and held during the Second World War, from Poland in 1939 to Greece, Yugoslavia and the western Soviet Union in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German policy varied from country to country, ranging from direct and brutal occupation to reliance on collaborating governments such as the one at Vichy, which ruled the unoccupied south of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In Poland part of the conquered land was annexed to the Reich as the Reichsgaue Wartheland and Danzig-West Prussia, while the rest became the General Government under Hans Frank, where real power lay with Himmler's SS and police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Denmark was at first only supervised, keeping its own cabinet of the main parties so that an appearance of sovereignty was preserved, until a state of military emergency was declared on 29 August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greece was carved into German, Italian and Bulgarian zones, while in the Netherlands a civilian Reich commissioner governed through the Dutch civil service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the differences lay a common aim, for the Nuremberg tribunal found that property across the occupied countries was systematically plundered to enlarge Germany's resources at the expense of the rest of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler boasted on 9 November 1941 that the territory working for Germany held more than 250 million people and that the very last man would be harnessed to the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribunal found that at least five million people were deported to Germany to serve its industry and agriculture, and that hostages were taken in very large numbers in all the occupied countries and shot as suited German purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The harshest rule fell on the East, where the Polish population was robbed and terrorised and the occupiers imposed the death penalty even for minor offences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the West the occupiers relied far more on local officials, and in the Netherlands the cooperation of Dutch administrators and policemen helped make the deportation of the Jews so thorough that fewer than a quarter survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “France,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das besetzte Polen,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-polen. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in Dänemark,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-daenemark. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Greece,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/greece. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Netherlands,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-netherlands. [Open access]","Martin Krechting, “Das Vichy-Regime,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vichy-regime. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Seyss-Inquart,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judseyss.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did German rule take such different forms in different countries?","a":"Because Germany adapted its rule to its aims in each place: parts of Poland were annexed outright, Denmark was at first merely supervised behind a show of sovereignty, and the Netherlands received a civilian Reich commissioner who governed through Dutch officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the occupations have in common?","a":"Exploitation. The Nuremberg tribunal found that property in every occupied country was plundered to enlarge Germany's resources, that at least five million people were deported to work in Germany, and that hostages were taken and shot everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did occupation in the East differ from the West?","a":"Because the Nazis regarded the Slavic peoples as inferior: in Poland the occupiers robbed and terrorised the population and punished even minor offences with death, while in the West they relied on cooperation from local administrations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"German-occupied Europe","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Axis_Occupation_of_Europe_%281942%29.svg","title":"Axis-occupied Europe in 1942","desc":"A map of Europe in 1942 showing Germany, the territories it occupied and the states allied to it.","credit":"TRAJAN 117, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axis_Occupation_of_Europe_(1942).svg","alt":"A map of Europe with Germany in dark green, occupied territories from France to Russia in pale green and allied states in grey."}},
    "ww2-662": {"questions":["A decree of 17 July 1941 divided the occupied Soviet lands into units, each called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which were split in turn into general regions and district areas.","As head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the occupied Netherlands, Arthur Seyss-Inquart decreed compulsory labour service in 1942 and called the Hague rules on occupation obsolete."],"abstract":"A <b>Reichskommissariat</b> was a German civil administration headed by a Reich commissioner, the form of rule Nazi Germany gave to occupied countries whose people it meant to fold into a Greater Germanic Reich after victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first was set up for Norway on 24 April 1940, when Hitler named the Essen Gauleiter Josef Terboven Reich commissioner while the conquest was still under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Netherlands followed in May 1940 under the Austrian Nazi Arthur Seyss-Inquart, whose task was to Nazify some 8.8 million Dutch, regarded by the Nazis as a kindred Germanic people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Netherlands the commissioner's German staff, which included many Austrian-born Nazis, sat above the native civil service and supervised it rather than replacing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the East the same title was used on a far larger scale, for a decree of 17 July 1941 divided the occupied Soviet lands under Alfred Rosenberg's new ministry into Reich commissariats, each split into general regions and district areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The decree made each Reich commissar answer to Rosenberg alone, yet it reserved separate powers to the army, to Göring's Four Year Plan and to Himmler's SS and police, so no commissar ever ruled unchallenged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rosenberg sometimes objected to the excesses and atrocities of his subordinates in the East, notably those of Erich Koch, but the excesses continued and Rosenberg stayed in office to the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Netherlands, the Nuremberg tribunal found, Seyss-Inquart used terror against all opposition, took part in the shooting of hostages and ran the economy for maximum German benefit, calling the Hague rules obsolete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He decreed compulsory labour service in 1942, and the tribunal put the number sent from the Netherlands to work in the Reich at over 500,000, very few of them true volunteers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Norway Terboven ruled as a dictator whose repression stirred hatred of the occupiers, and his only native support came from Vidkun Quisling's party, which formed a government dependent on the Germans in February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in den Niederlanden,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-den-niederlanden. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in Norwegen,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-norwegen. [in German] [Open access]","Adolf Hitler, “Decree of the Fuehrer concerning the administration of the newly-occupied Eastern territories,” 17 July 1941, Document 1997-PS, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1997-ps.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Seyss-Inquart,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judseyss.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Rosenberg,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judrosen.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Netherlands,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-netherlands. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany give Norway and the Netherlands civilian commissioners rather than generals?","a":"Because the Nazis saw the Norwegians and the Dutch as Germanic peoples to be Nazified and taken into a Greater Germanic Reich after victory, a political task they gave to party men such as Terboven and Seyss-Inquart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a Reich commissar in the East never all-powerful?","a":"Because the decree that created the eastern commissariats reserved separate powers to the army, to Göring's Four Year Plan and to Himmler's SS and police, alongside the commissar's duty to obey Rosenberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did a commissioner use the native administration?","a":"In the Netherlands Seyss-Inquart's German staff supervised the Dutch civil service rather than replacing it, and Dutch courts and police were pressed into carrying out his policies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reichskommissariat"},
    "ww2-663": {"questions":["Converted at an imposed rate of 20 francs to the mark, the German demand for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to 400 million francs a day, about twice the real value.","Raised to 500 million francs a day after the Germans entered the southern zone in November 1942, France's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the largest burden on its state budget."],"abstract":"<b>Occupation costs</b> were the payments an occupied country was made to hand over for the upkeep of the army occupying it, and in German-occupied Europe they became one of the main channels through which wealth was drained to the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that excessive occupation costs, together with forced credits on clearing accounts that were accounts in name only, meant the occupied countries themselves paid for the goods sent to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> France is the clearest case, for Article 18 of the armistice of June 1940 obliged the French government to bear the costs of maintaining the German troops on its soil without naming any sum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 8 August 1940 the German armistice delegation demanded daily instalments of at least 20 million marks, backdated to 25 June and payable 10 days in advance, with billeting charged separately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Converted at an imposed rate of 20 francs to the mark, about twice the real parity, that came to 400 million francs a day, and French negotiators protested that it bore no relation to a normal army of occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The sum was cut to 300 million francs a day in May 1941 and raised to 500 million after the Germans occupied the southern zone in November 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians of the occupation economy note that German officials knew the levies far exceeded the real costs of occupation, and that the money was spent among other things on purchases on the black market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In France the payments became the largest burden on the state budget, which had no matching tax revenue, while the French economy was turned almost entirely to German needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In occupied Belgium and the Netherlands, by contrast, the one-way clearing system mattered even more than occupation costs as a means of plunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Nuremberg in January 1946 the French prosecution presented the payments as an exorbitant tribute, one part of a system for seizing France's means of payment alongside clearing and the outright levy of gold and currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["“Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,” in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","German Armistice Delegation for Economy, note on occupation costs for General Huntziger, 8 August 1940, Document 1741-PS, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1741-ps.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, transcript of 22 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Gerthoffer, 3,172–3,177, Nuremberg Trials Project, Harvard Law School Library, https://nuremberg.law.harvard.edu/transcripts/7?seq=3197. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in Frankreich,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","Kim Oosterlinck, “L’impact de l’économie d’occupation sur le marché de l’art,” lecture text, Deutsches Zentrum Kulturgutverluste, https://www.kulturgutverluste.de/sites/default/files/2023-05/Vortrag-Kim-Oosterlinck.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could France not refuse the German figure?","a":"Because the armistice obliged it to pay for the occupation troops without fixing a sum, and when French negotiators protested in August 1940 the German delegation refused to discuss either the amount or the difference between occupation troops and troops preparing to fight Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the exchange rate matter so much?","a":"Because the occupier pegged the mark at 20 francs when its real value was nearer 10, so the daily 20 million marks became 400 million francs and every German purchase in France cost the Germans about half its worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the payments a form of plunder rather than a fee?","a":"Because they far exceeded what the occupation really cost, as German officials knew, and the surplus bought French goods, including on the black market, so France in effect paid for what was taken from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-664": {"questions":["A general census of Frenchmen born between 1912 and 1921 in February 1943 prepared the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which soon called up three age groups for labour on German demand.","After the voluntary exchange of French workers for prisoners of war failed in 1942, Vichy turned to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and many young men escaped into the Resistance."],"abstract":"The <b>Service du travail obligatoire</b> was the compulsory labour service introduced in occupied France by the Vichy government's law and decree of 16 February 1943, under which young Frenchmen could be sent to work in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It applied to every man born between 1 January 1920 and 31 December 1922, and a general census of men born from 1912 to 1921 was held in the same month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the last stage of a campaign by Fritz Sauckel, Germany's plenipotentiary for labour, who in 1942 obtained Pierre Laval's promise to send 350,000 workers to Germany, 150,000 of them metal workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first drive, launched in June 1942 with Laval's call on French workers to serve German industry, relied on volunteers and on an exchange of workers for prisoners of war, but it produced too few men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A law of 4 September 1942 then made every Frenchman between 18 and 50 who worked fewer than 30 hours a week register at his town hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sauckel told the Central Planning Board on 16 February 1943 that he and his staff had persuaded Laval, after difficult discussions, to introduce the compulsory labour law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that across the occupied West the search for volunteers had been backed by withdrawn ration cards, dismissals, denial of unemployment benefit and police threats against families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It quoted Sauckel's own remark of March 1944 that out of five million foreign workers who had arrived in Germany, not even 200,000 had come voluntarily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In France the policy of collaboration was meant to secure Vichy a privileged place in a Nazi-dominated Europe, but forced labour recruitment instead drove large numbers of young people into the Resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Similar measures followed elsewhere, and in the Netherlands Seyss-Inquart formally decreed compulsory labour service in 1942 and used the Security Police to stop men evading it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 5, proceedings of 18 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Herzog, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-18-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Martin Krechting, “Das Vichy-Regime,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vichy-regime. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in Frankreich,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Pierre Laval 1883–1945,” <i>LeMO-Biografien</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/pierre-laval. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Sauckel,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsauck.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Seyss-Inquart,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judseyss.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Vichy move from volunteers to compulsion?","a":"Because the exchange of workers for prisoners of war and the propaganda for volunteers failed to meet Sauckel's quotas, so the Germans pressed Vichy into laws of September 1942 and February 1943 that made labour service compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Sauckel claim the law as his own work?","a":"Because he told the Central Planning Board that he and his staff had persuaded Laval to introduce it, which gave Germany a legal means of recruiting three French age groups with the help of the French government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the law help the Resistance?","a":"Forced recruitment of labour, along with the persecution of the Jews and growing German terror, drove large numbers of young French people to join the Resistance rather than go to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Service du travail obligatoire","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/Europe%2C_r%C3%A9veille-toi_Appel_du_Gauleiter_Sauckel_aux_ouvriers_fran%C3%A7ais%2C_2008.20.jpg","title":"Sauckel's appeal to French workers","desc":"A poster of 1942–1943 carrying Fritz Sauckel's appeal to French workers to labour for Germany.","credit":"Musée de la Libération de Paris – musée Jean Moulin, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe,_réveille-toi_Appel_du_Gauleiter_Sauckel_aux_ouvriers_français,_2008.20.jpg","alt":"A large printed poster headed 'Europe, réveille-toi!' above dense columns of French text."}},
    "ww2-665": {"questions":["Out of five million foreign workers in Germany not even 200,000 had come voluntarily, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told a planning meeting in March 1944, a remark later quoted against him at Nuremberg.","Gauleiter of Thuringia since 1927, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hanged at Nuremberg on 16 October 1946 for organising slave labour across the occupied countries."],"abstract":"<b>Fritz Sauckel</b> was a Nazi Party leader from Thuringia who, as Germany's plenipotentiary general for labour from March 1942, directed the deportation of millions of people from occupied Europe to work in the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born at Hassfurt in Lower Franconia in 1894, he went to sea as a youth and spent the First World War as a prisoner of the French, who captured his freighter in the Channel in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the Nazi Party in 1923, became Gauleiter of Thuringia in 1927 and from 1933 governed the state as Reich governor, making it a model region of the party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 21 March 1942 Hitler appointed him plenipotentiary general for the utilisation of labour, formally inside Göring's Four Year Plan, with authority over all available manpower, including foreign workers and prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From September 1942 he could appoint his own commissioners in the occupied territories, who with the backing of the police filled the quotas he set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Nuremberg tribunal found that at least five million people were deported to Germany, and quoted Sauckel's remark of March 1944 that not even 200,000 of them had come voluntarily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In France he reached an agreement with Pierre Laval in 1942 on recruiting French workers and pressed Vichy step by step, boasting in February 1943 that he had persuaded Laval to introduce compulsory labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One of his regulations ordered that workers be fed, sheltered and treated so as to exploit them to the highest possible extent at the lowest conceivable expenditure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At his trial he called the recorded figure of 200,000 an impossible error and blamed other agencies for the excesses, but the tribunal found he had overall responsibility and had supported ruthless methods to fill his quotas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Convicted of war crimes and crimes against humanity on 1 October 1946, he was sentenced to death and hanged at Nuremberg on 16 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andreas Mix, “Fritz Sauckel (1894–1946),” <i>LeMO-Biografien</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 29 November 2024, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/fritz-sauckel. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Fritz Sauckel,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/fritz-sauckel. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Sauckel,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsauck.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 5, proceedings of 18 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Herzog, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-18-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 14, proceedings of 29 May 1946, testimony of Fritz Sauckel, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-29-46.asp. [Open access]","Martin Krechting, “Das Vichy-Regime,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vichy-regime. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany need a plenipotentiary for labour in 1942?","a":"Because the war had drained the German economy of men, and Hitler gave Sauckel control of all available manpower, foreign workers and prisoners of war included, to fill the gap from the occupied territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tribunal reject his defence?","a":"Because although he blamed other agencies for the excesses, his own regulations gave him charge of recruiting, transporting and allocating the workers, and he knew of the ruthless methods and backed them to meet his quotas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does his remark of March 1944 matter?","a":"Because it was his own admission that real volunteers were the exception: of five million foreign workers in Germany, he said, not even 200,000 had come voluntarily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fritz Sauckel","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Fritz_Sauckel.jpg","title":"Fritz Sauckel at Nuremberg","desc":"Fritz Sauckel in the dock of the International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg, where he was sentenced to death.","credit":"United States government photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fritz_Sauckel.jpg","alt":"A bald man with a small moustache in a dark suit, with a helmeted guard standing behind him."}},
    "ww2-666": {"questions":["The Nuremberg tribunal found that German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of food and raw materials in occupied Europe was out of all proportion to local resources and caused famine and inflation.","Research on occupied Greece found that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there was less centrally organised than once thought and usually took the form of disorganised plundering by soldiers."],"abstract":"A <b>requisition</b> is the demand by an occupying army for goods or services from the inhabitants of the territory it holds, and under the Hague Regulations of 1907 it was allowed only for the needs of the army of occupation and in proportion to the country's resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same rules forbade pillage outright and required that goods taken be paid for in cash where possible or acknowledged by a receipt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In occupied Europe the Germans requisitioned on a scale that broke both limits, sending farm produce, raw materials, machine tools, transport and even foreign securities to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that these demands were out of all proportion to the resources of the occupied countries and resulted in famine, inflation and an active black market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It quoted Göring's assurance that if there was to be famine anywhere, it would in no case be in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In France the occupiers paid with notes of their own credit institute and with requisition vouchers over which the French had no control, so their purchasing power could be enlarged at will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A German economic official wrote in April 1942 that restrictions on French food, clothing, shoes and heating had long been more rigorous than those in the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Netherlands, one of Europe's largest food exporters before the war, saw part of its produce sent to Germany from the start of the occupation, though Dutch rations stayed adequate until September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Greece the British government blamed the famine of 1941–1942 on the pillage and extortion of the invading armies in the spring of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later research on Greece has qualified that picture, finding that requisitions there were less pervasive and less centrally organised than once thought and usually took the form of disorganised plundering by soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex: Regulations, The Hague, 18 October 1907, arts. 47 and 52, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Stathis N. Kalyvas, review of <i>Famine and Death in Occupied Greece, 1941–1944</i>, by Violetta Hionidou, <i>The Historian</i>, 653–54, https://stathiskalyvas.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/hionidou.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant) to the Secretary of State, January 17, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d725. [Open access]","Laura S. Bleker, Susanne R. de Rooij, Rebecca C. Painter, Anita C. J. Ravelli, and Tessa J. Roseboom, “Cohort Profile: The Dutch Famine Birth Cohort (DFBC) – A Prospective Birth Cohort Study in the Netherlands,” <i>BMJ Open</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): e042078, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2020-042078. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, transcript of 22 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Gerthoffer, 3,172–3,177, Nuremberg Trials Project, Harvard Law School Library, https://nuremberg.law.harvard.edu/transcripts/7?seq=3197. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in den Niederlanden,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-den-niederlanden. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What limits did international law set on requisition?","a":"The Hague Regulations of 1907 allowed it only for the needs of the occupying army, in proportion to the country's resources, paid for in cash where possible or acknowledged by receipt, and they forbade pillage outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German requisitioning cause hunger?","a":"Because the Nuremberg tribunal found that food and other goods were taken out of all proportion to what the occupied countries could spare, producing famine, inflation and a black market, on the principle that Germany must never go hungry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Was requisitioning always centrally planned?","a":"Not everywhere: research on Greece has found that requisitions were less pervasive and less centrally organised than once believed and often took the form of disorganised plundering by soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-667": {"questions":["Official rations fell to between 400 and 800 calories a day during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended only after the German forces in the western Netherlands capitulated in May 1945.","At Nuremberg Seyss-Inquart called the charge that his transport embargo caused the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most serious against him, and blamed the Dutch railway strike for it."],"abstract":"The <b>Dutch famine of 1944–1945</b>, known in the Netherlands as the Hunger Winter, struck the German-occupied west of the country in the last months of the war, only months before its liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Food had been rationed throughout the occupation, but daily rations stayed adequate until September 1944, when they suddenly dropped below 1,600 calories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 17 September 1944 the Dutch government in exile called a railway strike to support the Allied advance, and the German occupier answered with an abrupt ban on food transport to the west of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ban was partly lifted in November to allow transport by water, but an early and exceptionally harsh winter froze the canals, and famine set in across Amsterdam, Rotterdam and The Hague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 4.5 million people were exposed, and average intake in the western provinces fell to around 700 calories for children and just over 500 for adolescents and adults in January to March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Older studies put the famine's deaths anywhere between 10,000 and tens of thousands, while a recent analysis of national death records estimated about 23,000 excess deaths in the six largest western cities between September 1944 and July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Nuremberg Arthur Seyss-Inquart, Reich commissioner for the occupied Netherlands, called the charge that his transport embargo caused the famine the most serious against him, blamed the Dutch strike for the emergency and said he had ordered shipping restored in mid-October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also claimed that the Dutch government had corrected its own figure of 50,000 deaths from starvation to 25,000, a statement made by a defendant in his own defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under cross-examination he conceded that his warning of starvation after the railway strike could be seen as a threat, though he described it as a forecast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The famine ended with liberation in early May 1945, when rations rose above 1,000 calories within a week through airdrops and Red Cross feeding, although the last hunger deaths were reported in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Laura S. Bleker, Susanne R. de Rooij, Rebecca C. Painter, Anita C. J. Ravelli, and Tessa J. Roseboom, “Cohort Profile: The Dutch Famine Birth Cohort (DFBC) – A Prospective Birth Cohort Study in the Netherlands,” <i>BMJ Open</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): e042078, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2020-042078. [Open access]","Daniel Ramirez and Steven A. Haas, “Windows of Vulnerability: Consequences of Exposure Timing during the Dutch Hunger Winter,” <i>Population and Development Review</i> 48 (2022): 959–89, https://doi.org/10.1111/padr.12513. [Open access]","Ingrid J. J. de Zwarte, Peter Ekamper, and L. H. Lumey, “Infant and Child Mortality in the Netherlands 1935–47 and Changes Related to the Dutch Famine of 1944–45: A Population-Based Analysis,” <i>Population Studies</i> 78, no. 3 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1080/00324728.2023.2243913. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 15, proceedings of 11 June 1946, testimony of Arthur Seyss-Inquart, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-11-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in den Niederlanden,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-den-niederlanden. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 12 June 1946, cross-examination of Arthur Seyss-Inquart, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-12-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Netherlands,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-netherlands. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was only the west of the Netherlands struck?","a":"Because the south had already been liberated, and after the railway strike of September 1944 the occupier banned food transport to the west, while the frozen waterways of an early, harsh winter cut off the big cities from supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll uncertain?","a":"Because older studies gave figures from 10,000 to tens of thousands, registration broke down during the liberation battles, and recent work on death records estimates about 23,000 excess deaths in the six largest western cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How should Seyss-Inquart's account at Nuremberg be read?","a":"As a defendant's case for himself: he blamed the Dutch strike and the confiscation of ships for the emergency, though under cross-examination he admitted his warning of starvation could be seen as a threat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dutch famine of 1944–1945","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Twee_deelnemers_aan_de_hongertochten_tijdens_de_hongerwinter.jpg","title":"A hunger journey, 1944–1945","desc":"Two women on a hunger journey into the countryside in search of food during the Dutch famine.","credit":"Unknown photographer / Anefo, Nationaal Archief, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Twee_deelnemers_aan_de_hongertochten_tijdens_de_hongerwinter.jpg","alt":"Two women in dark coats on a flat open road, one pushing a pram loaded with bundles and baskets."}},
    "ww2-668": {"questions":["Swedish ships carrying Canadian wheat through the British blockade from 1942 brought relief during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, supervised by a commission under the International Red Cross.","Britain blamed the pillage of the invading armies for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in January 1942, yet agreed that its own blockade of the occupied country had to be relaxed."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Famine of Greece</b> was the mass starvation that followed the German, Italian and Bulgarian occupation of the country in 1941, one of the last famines in Europe and one of the deadliest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the invasion of April 1941 the three occupiers divided Greece into zones, with Germany holding Salonika, the surroundings of Athens and western Crete, Bulgaria western Thrace, and Italy most of the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By December 1941 an American diplomat back from Athens reported unparalleled suffering there and said that mass starvation was now a fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The worst mortality struck Athens, Piraeus and Salonika in the winter of 1941–1942, but research has shown that famine came at different times in different places throughout the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is estimated to have killed close to 5 percent of the population, and most of its victims died of starvation itself rather than of epidemics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Responsibility was argued over at the time, and in January 1942 the British government, whose blockade covered occupied Greece, blamed the pillage and extortion of the invading armies and said Germany had done practically nothing to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same memorandum conceded that some relaxation of the blockade was now imperative and approved a single shipment of 8,000 tons of wheat under the International Red Cross, while insisting that feeding occupied countries remained the enemy's duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In March 1942 London and Washington backed a larger plan for Swedish ships to carry wheat from Canada and the United States, with a Swedish committee supervising its distribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relief under the Swedish-Swiss Red Cross commission began in the summer of 1942 and gave a significant ration to everyone in the capital, though the black market went on operating to the end of the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The memory of the famine fixed by about 1950 blamed the Axis, mostly the Germans, and dwelt on Athens in 1941–1942, leaving out the black market and famine prostitution, which historians have only recently begun to examine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Violetta Hionidou, “‘Remembering’ the Early 1940s Greek Famine: Fiction and Collective Memory, 1950–2019,” <i>Contemporary European History</i> 34 (2025): 466–79, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960777324000341. [Open access]","Stathis N. Kalyvas, review of <i>Famine and Death in Occupied Greece, 1941–1944</i>, by Violetta Hionidou, <i>The Historian</i>, 653–54, https://stathiskalyvas.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/hionidou.pdf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant), December 3, 1941,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d717. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Ambassador in the United Kingdom (Winant) to the Secretary of State, January 17, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d725. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Under Secretary of State (Welles) to President Roosevelt, March 11, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d743. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Greece,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/greece. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain at first refuse to let food through to Greece?","a":"Because its blockade policy held that occupiers must feed the countries they had overrun, and London feared that an exception for Greece would bring renewed appeals from other occupied allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the famine remembered mainly as an Athenian disaster?","a":"Because the worst mortality hit Athens and Piraeus in 1941–1942 and the memory fixed by 1950 concentrated there, although research shows famine struck other places at other times throughout the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What changed in the summer of 1942?","a":"Swedish ships allowed through the blockade began bringing wheat, and the Swedish-Swiss Red Cross commission gave a significant ration to everyone in the capital, extended later to other towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/FamineGr%C3%A8ce1941-1944.png","title":"Births and deaths in occupied Greece","desc":"Monthly births and deaths in Athens and in Macedonia from 1940 to 1945, drawn by a Commons contributor after Mark Mazower.","credit":"Aeleftherios, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FamineGrèce1941-1944.png","alt":"Two line charts with French labels in which the deaths line in Athens leaps to about 7,000 a month in early 1942."},"wiki":"Great Famine (Greece)"},
    "ww2-669": {"questions":["Hostages taken from a list supplied by Vichy's interior minister were shot in October 1941 on the orders of the head of the German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after an officer was killed at Nantes.","Placed under the German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the armistice of 1940, northern France and the Atlantic coast were governed through French officials bound to carry out German regulations."],"abstract":"The <b>Military Administration in France</b> was the German army's government of occupied France from 1940 to 1944, headed by a military commander, the Militärbefehlshaber, who resided in Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the armistice of June 1940 the German Reich exercised all the rights of an occupying power in the occupied zone, and the French government undertook to have its own officials carry out German regulations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The zone covered the northern half of France with its industrial regions and the whole Atlantic coast to the Spanish border, while the departments of Nord and Pas-de-Calais came under the military commander in Belgium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its command staff led the occupying troops and its administrative staff supervised the French administration, so that about 1,200 officials and officers were enough to govern the zone and secure deliveries for the German war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Otto von Stülpnagel held the post from October 1940 until early 1942 and was succeeded by his cousin, Carl-Heinrich von Stülpnagel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As attacks on German soldiers grew in 1941, the administration built up what the French prosecution at Nuremberg called a 'hostages code', a set of rules for seizing and shooting French hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the military commandant of Nantes was killed in October 1941, Stülpnagel had the Vichy interior minister designate the most dangerous Communist prisoners, and hostages from the list of 60 names were shot at Châteaubriant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Keitel had ordered on 1 October 1941 that military commanders always hold hostages ready to execute when German soldiers were attacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yet Otto von Stülpnagel and his largely conservative staff refused Hitler's and Keitel's demands for mass shootings, the quarrel ended in his resignation, and from May 1942 the shooting of hostages became the responsibility of the SS police leader Carl Oberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The administration was also the channel for German demands for labour, as when its economic staff told Vichy in August 1942 that decrees to supply workers for Germany must now be issued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Das deutsche Besatzungsregime in Frankreich,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “France,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","“Armistice Agreement between the German High Command of the Armed Forces and French Plenipotentiaries, Compiègne, June 22, 1940,” in <i>Documents on German Foreign Policy 1918–1945</i>, Series D, vol. 9 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1956), 671–76, Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/frgearm.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 6, proceedings of 24 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Dubost, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-24-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 5, proceedings of 18 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Herzog, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-18-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Keitel,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judkeite.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How could so small a German staff govern half of France?","a":"Because the armistice bound the French administration to carry out German regulations, so a staff of about 1,200 officials and officers only had to supervise French officials rather than replace them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the hostage policy pass to the SS in 1942?","a":"Because Otto von Stülpnagel and his staff, though they had hostages shot, refused the mass shootings Hitler and Keitel demanded; after his resignation the policy was given to the SS police leader Carl Oberg from May 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What part did Vichy play in the shootings at Châteaubriant?","a":"Its interior minister supplied, at Stülpnagel's demand, a list of 60 Communist prisoners held at Châteaubriant from whom the hostages to be shot were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"German military administration in occupied France during World War II","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Avis_du_Milit%C3%A4rbefehlshaber_en_France_-_General_der_Infanterie_von_St%C3%BClpnagel_-_concernant_le_Parti_communiste_fran%C3%A7ais%2C_1994.250.jpg","title":"A notice of the military commander, 1941","desc":"Otto von Stülpnagel's notice of 14 August 1941 threatening death by German court martial for Communist activity in France.","credit":"Musée de la Libération de Paris – musée Jean Moulin, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avis_du_Militärbefehlshaber_en_France_-_General_der_Infanterie_von_Stülpnagel_-_concernant_le_Parti_communiste_français,_1994.250.jpg","alt":"A cream poster headed AVIS beneath an eagle and swastika, with bold French text signed Von Stülpnagel."}},
    "ww2-670": {"questions":["Fritz Sauckel boasted that he had persuaded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after difficult discussions, to introduce compulsory labour in France, under which three age groups were soon called up.","Shot in Paris for treason on 15 October 1945, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had engineered the vote of July 1940 that gave Marshal Pétain full powers over the French state."],"abstract":"<b>Pierre Laval</b> was a French politician who, as deputy and then head of the Vichy government, became the figure most closely identified with France's collaboration with Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born at Châteldon in 1883, the son of a café owner, he began as a Socialist lawyer who defended trade unionists, moved steadily to the right and served twice as prime minister in the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the defeat of 1940 he engineered the vote of 10 July that gave Marshal Pétain full powers, became Pétain's deputy, and was dismissed and placed under house arrest in December 1940 for pressing too hard for rapprochement with Hitler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In April 1942 Pétain recalled him as head of government under German pressure, and Laval cultivated close relations with Otto Abetz, the German Foreign Office representative in Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whereas Pétain pursued a more limited collaboration, Laval followed an anti-British and pro-German course in political, economic and military affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In June 1942 he called on French workers to make themselves available to German war industry and declared that he hoped for a German victory against Bolshevism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had told American diplomats privately in April and June 1942 that he believed in, and would prefer, a German victory, fearing the spread of Communism, and in August Sumner Welles cited his public declaration as proof that Vichy was openly assisting Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Laval promised Fritz Sauckel to send 350,000 workers to Germany and, after what Sauckel called difficult discussions, introduced the compulsory labour law of February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the summer of 1942 French police and officials began deporting foreign and French Jews from the southern zone, though in 1943 Laval refused to strip French Jews of their citizenship to make deportation easier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the liberation he was tried for treason and sentenced to death, and on 15 October 1945 he was shot in Paris, hours after a failed attempt to poison himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Manfred Wichmann, “Pierre Laval 1883–1945,” <i>LeMO-Biografien</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/pierre-laval. [in German] [Open access]","Martin Krechting, “Das Vichy-Regime,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vichy-regime. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “France,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 5, proceedings of 18 January 1946, presentation of the French prosecution by M. Herzog, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-18-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), August 11, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d192. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Consul General at Algiers (Cole) to the Secretary of State, April 18, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d271. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Chargé in France (Tuck) to the Secretary of State, June 13, 1942,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d189. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans want Laval back in power in 1942?","a":"Because Hitler hoped Laval would bring greater support from the Vichy regime, and Laval, unlike Pétain, followed a pro-German course in politics, the economy and military affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his speech of June 1942 matter abroad?","a":"Because his declared hope for a German victory was taken in Washington as proof that Vichy was openly assisting Germany, and Sumner Welles told the French ambassador so in August 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Laval serve German labour demands?","a":"He promised Sauckel 350,000 workers for Germany and, when volunteers fell short, introduced the compulsory labour law of February 1943 under German pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pierre Laval","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Pierre_Laval_and_Ren%C3%A9_Bousquet_1943.jpg","title":"Laval and Bousquet at Vichy, 1943","desc":"Pierre Laval and the Vichy police chief René Bousquet inspect the regime's guard of honour in 1943.","credit":"Anonymous photographer (Keystone-France), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Laval_and_René_Bousquet_1943.jpg","alt":"Two men in suits walk past a line of helmeted guards saluting in a city street."}},
    "ww2-671": {"questions":["Seven Jewish hostages were shot against a cemetery wall at Rillieux-la-Pape in June 1944 by the armed wing of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a crime punished by a life sentence 50 years later.","An armed Franc-Garde, raised in June 1943 to fight the maquis, made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Vichy's main paramilitary weapon against the rural guerrilla bands of the French resistance."],"abstract":"The <b>Milice</b> was the paramilitary force that the Vichy regime created on 30 January 1943 to fight what it called the enemy within, and Joseph Darnand led it from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the Service d'ordre légionnaire, the armed elite that Darnand had organised from December 1941 inside Marshal Pétain's veterans' movement, the Légion française des combattants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On paper it was to be the chief instrument of France's moral, intellectual and social recovery, but under Pierre Laval it became a police force serving the Nazis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With more than 30,000 men at its height, it tracked résistants, Jews, communists and freemasons, and its armed wing, the Franc-Garde, created in June 1943, led the fight against the maquis, the rural guerrilla bands of the resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darnand, a decorated soldier of the First World War who joined the Waffen-SS in 1942, became Vichy's chief of public order in December 1943 and carried the Milice into the occupied zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the last 6 months of the regime the Milice turned to murder, abducting and killing the former minister Jean Zay on 20 June 1944 and Georges Mandel, handed over to it from prison, on 7 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When résistants shot Philippe Henriot, a Milice leader who was Vichy's minister of propaganda and its best-known voice on the radio, its answer came the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 29 June 1944 Paul Touvier, head of the Milice intelligence service in Lyon, had the Franc-Garde shoot seven Jewish hostages against the cemetery wall at Rillieux-la-Pape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The provisional government disbanded the Milice after the liberation, and Darnand was convicted of treason and executed in October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Touvier escaped, was pardoned in 1971 and hid for years with help from churchmen, until his arrest in 1989 led to a life sentence in 1994 as the first Frenchman convicted of crimes against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“La Milice,” in <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i> (Paris: Éditions Larousse), https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/divers/la_Milice/133196. [in French] [Open access]","“Joseph Darnand,” in <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i> (Paris: Éditions Larousse), https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/personnage/Joseph_Darnand/115703. [in French] [Open access]","“Paul Touvier,” in <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i> (Paris: Éditions Larousse), https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/personnage/Paul_Touvier/180712. [in French] [Open access]","“Philippe Henriot,” in <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i> (Paris: Éditions Larousse), https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/personnage/Philippe_Henriot/123635. [in French] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “France,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","“Georges Mandel,” in <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i> (Paris: Éditions Larousse), https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/personnage/Georges_Mandel/131463. [in French] [Open access]","“Jean Zay,” in <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i> (Paris: Éditions Larousse), https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/personnage/Jean_Zay/150518. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a Vichy veterans' movement produce an armed police?","a":"Because Darnand had already built the Service d'ordre légionnaire as the armed elite of the Légion française des combattants, and the Milice was created out of it in January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Laval it was turned from a movement of national recovery into a police force hunting the regime's enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Jews shot at Rillieux-la-Pape in June 1944?","a":"Because résistants had killed Philippe Henriot, Vichy's minister of propaganda and a leader of the Milice, the day before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paul Touvier, who ran the Milice intelligence service in Lyon, had seven Jewish hostages shot in reprisal by the Franc-Garde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Touvier's trial come only in 1994?","a":"Because he escaped after the war, received a presidential pardon in 1971 and then hid in monasteries when it became public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> New complaints of crimes against humanity led to his arrest in 1989, and a court near Versailles sentenced him to life in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Milice","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Loi_du_30_janvier_1943_portant_cr%C3%A9ation_de_la_Milice_fran%C3%A7aise_1_-_Archives_Nationales_-_A-1853.jpg","title":"Statutes of the Milice, January 1943","desc":"The first page of the statutes annexed to the Vichy law of 30 January 1943 that created the Milice française, held by the Archives nationales.","credit":"État français, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loi_du_30_janvier_1943_portant_cr%C3%A9ation_de_la_Milice_fran%C3%A7aise_1_-_Archives_Nationales_-_A-1853.jpg","alt":"A typed page headed 'Statuts de la Milice française' with the law number and the date 30 January 1943, listing its first articles."}},
    "ww2-672": {"questions":["A German ultimatum demanding death sentences for saboteurs was refused on 29 August 1943, ending the cooperation policy of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Danish crews scuttled part of their fleet.","More than 30,000 people were interned and 46 executed in the legal purge that followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended with the German capitulation in May 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Occupation of Denmark</b> began on 9 April 1940, when German troops invaded in the same operation as the attack on Norway and took the country almost without resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Germany promised the Danes political independence in return for a guarantee from their government that German troops in the country would be safe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As a result the king, the parliament, the courts, the police and the local councils carried on, and the two states kept dealing through their foreign ministries as if both were free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until 1943 German rule was comparatively mild, partly because the Germans counted the Danes as fellow Aryans, and the Danish authorities imposed none of the anti-Jewish measures seen elsewhere in western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It ended on 5 May 1945, the day after the German capitulation was announced over the BBC, and the purge that followed interned more than 30,000 people and executed 46.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Danes called the arrangement the policy of negotiation and cooperation, and its aim, in the prime minister's words of 9 April 1940, was to spare the country the consequences of a state of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both sides gained, since Germany held the country at little cost while Danish democracy survived longer than anywhere else under German rule, though historians still dispute whether to call it collaboration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the summer of 1943 sabotage and strikes spread through provincial towns, Odense and Esbjerg above all, and Hitler had his plenipotentiary Werner Best present an ultimatum demanding curfews, special courts and death sentences for saboteurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The parties and the government refused, and on 29 August 1943 the cabinet, parliament and king stopped working while the German army disarmed the Danish forces at a cost of 23 Danish and 5 German soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crews scuttled part of the navy or sailed it to Sweden, though most ships fell to the Germans, and for the rest of the war the heads of the ministries kept up the contact with the occupier without ministers above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Niels Wium Olesen, “Forhandlings- og samarbejdspolitikken under besættelsen, 1940-1945,” <i>Danmarkshistorien</i>, Aarhus Universitet, https://danmarkshistorien.lex.dk/Forhandlings-_og_samarbejdspolitikken_under_bes%C3%A6ttelsen,_1940-1945. [in Danish] [Open access]","Niels Wium Olesen, “Augustoprøret 29. august 1943,” <i>Danmarkshistorien</i>, Aarhus Universitet, https://danmarkshistorien.lex.dk/Augustopr%C3%B8ret_29._august_1943. [in Danish] [Open access]","Niels-Birger Danielsen, “Werner Best,” in <i>Den Store Danske</i>, Lex, https://lex.dk/Werner_Best. [in Danish] [Open access]","Ulf Jørgen Christensen, “Besættelsen,” in <i>Den Store Danske</i>, Lex, https://lex.dk/Bes%C3%A6ttelsen. [in Danish] [Open access]","Peter Yding Brunbech, “Retsopgøret efter besættelsen, 1945-1955,” <i>Danmarkshistorien</i>, Aarhus Universitet, https://danmarkshistorien.lex.dk/Retsopg%C3%B8ret_efter_bes%C3%A6ttelsen,_1945-1955. [in Danish] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Denmark,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/denmark. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Invasion of Denmark and Norway,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddenma.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany leave the Danish government in place?","a":"Because it wanted to occupy the country as cheaply as possible: if the Danes ran their own affairs, Germany needed few men to govern and guard it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A peaceful occupation of a democracy also looked good to neutral opinion, in the United States above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the cooperation break down in August 1943?","a":"Because sabotage and strikes rose sharply that summer as Germany suffered defeats, and Hitler answered with an ultimatum the Danish parties would not accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rather than impose death sentences for saboteurs themselves, the government, parliament and king stopped working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the policy still argued about?","a":"Because it protected Danish lives and institutions while also giving Germany what it wanted at low cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some historians call it collaboration in a neutral sense, while others reply that the word cannot shed the condemnation it carries from Vichy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Denmark in World War II","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Det_tyske_hovedkvarter_p%C3%A5_d%27Angleterre_med_en_sporvogn_foran._%285712185915%29.jpg","title":"German headquarters in Copenhagen","desc":"The Hotel d'Angleterre in Copenhagen, used as a German headquarters during the occupation, with a tram passing in front, around 1943.","credit":"Nationalmuseet – National Museum of Denmark, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Det_tyske_hovedkvarter_p%C3%A5_d%27Angleterre_med_en_sporvogn_foran._(5712185915).jpg","alt":"A Copenhagen square with a tram and pedestrians in front of a large hotel flying a swastika flag from its roof."}},
    "ww2-673": {"questions":["At a ceremony in Akershus Castle on 1 February 1942, Reichskommissar Terboven installed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as minister-president, though the German occupation authority kept a veto over his laws.","Shot at Akershus Fortress in October 1945 after his conviction for high treason, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had led the fascist Nasjonal Samling since founding it in 1933."],"abstract":"<b>Vidkun Quisling</b> was a Norwegian army officer and fascist politician who led Norway as minister-president under German occupation and was executed for treason in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After years of relief work in Russia with Fridtjof Nansen and a spell as defence minister from 1931 to 1933, he founded the party Nasjonal Samling in 1933, which never won a seat in parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In December 1939 the German navy's chief brought him to Hitler, after years of contact with Rosenberg's party foreign office, and his claim that Britain would seize Norwegian bases helped turn German attention north; the Nuremberg tribunal noted that planning began a few months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Germany invaded on 9 April 1940 he went on the national radio, declared himself prime minister and called off the mobilisation, but within days Hitler dropped him because the coup was stiffening Norwegian resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coup made his name a byword, for British newspapers and then the BBC began to use 'quisling' to mean a traitor, and the word spread across the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From September 1940 the Reichskommissar Josef Terboven banned every other party and ruled through ministers drawn mostly from Nasjonal Samling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 1 February 1942, at a ceremony in Akershus Castle, Terboven installed Quisling as minister-president with the powers that had belonged to the king and parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quisling hoped the step would lead to real independence and a peace with Germany, but the German supervision continued and Terboven could in practice veto his laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His government shared the blame for the occupation's repression: from autumn 1942 Norwegian police helped arrest the country's Jews, of whom about 770 were deported and only some 34 survived, and from 1943 Quisling confirmed death sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arrested on 9 May 1945, he was sentenced to death for high treason on 10 September and shot on 24 October, the first act of a purge that investigated 92,805 cases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Hans Fredrik Dahl and Magne Skodvin, “Vidkun Quisling,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Vidkun_Quisling. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Torgeir Ekerholt Sæveraas and Kjetil Braut Simonsen, “Nasjonal Samling,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Nasjonal_Samling. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Magne Skodvin and Torgeir Ekerholt Sæveraas, “Statsakten,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Statsakten. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Johs. Andenæs, Frode Sulland and Tore Pryser, “Landssvikoppgjøret,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/landssvikoppgj%C3%B8ret. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Alfred Rosenberg, “The Political Preparation of the Norway Action,” Document 004-PS, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/004-ps.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Invasion of Denmark and Norway,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/juddenma.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Raeder,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judraede.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Norway,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/norway. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler drop Quisling only days after his coup?","a":"Because the self-proclaimed government provoked Norwegians into resistance and so complicated the German military operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 15 April 1940 Quisling had to hand power to an administrative council installed by the German envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did becoming minister-president in 1942 give him so little power?","a":"Because Hitler changed his mind after the Allied commando raids of late 1941 and kept the German supervising administration in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Terboven could in practice veto Quisling's laws, and German plans for a peace treaty with his government were rejected outright by September 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he meet Hitler in December 1939?","a":"Because the navy's chief, Erich Raeder, wanted Hitler to look north and saw in Quisling a way to do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quisling argued that Norway's neutrality was a sham and that Britain would gain bases there, and Rosenberg's office, which had long been in contact with him, backed the meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vidkun Quisling","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/06M_P833S1D1358_0010_1_VIDKUN_QUISLING_%28Norwegian_Fascist_politician_WW2_Nazi_collaborator_1887-1945%29_Adolf_Hitler_Norw._new_Prime_Minister_Quisling_Reichskanzlei_Berlin_1942-02-13_Martin_Bormann_-_BAnQ_Biblioth..._Qu%C3%A9bec_PDM_cropped.jpg","title":"Quisling and Hitler, February 1942","desc":"Vidkun Quisling shakes hands with Adolf Hitler at the Reich Chancellery in Berlin on 13 February 1942, soon after becoming minister-president.","credit":"Unknown press photographer, Bibliothèque et Archives nationales du Québec, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:06M_P833S1D1358_0010_1_VIDKUN_QUISLING_(Norwegian_Fascist_politician_WW2_Nazi_collaborator_1887-1945)_Adolf_Hitler_Norw._new_Prime_Minister_Quisling_Reichskanzlei_Berlin_1942-02-13_Martin_Bormann_-_BAnQ_Biblioth..._Qu%C3%A9bec_PDM_cropped.jpg","alt":"Two men in uniform shaking hands, one heavy-set and seen from the side, the other with a moustache, with a third man behind them."}},
    "ww2-674": {"questions":["Belgium was given a German military government in 1940, but the Netherlands received the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a civilian regime under SS influence that treated the Dutch as a Germanic brother nation.","Over half a million workers were sent from the Netherlands to the Reich by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose chief, the Nuremberg tribunal accepted, largely prevented the army's scorched-earth policy."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichskommissariat Niederlande</b> was the German civilian government of the occupied Netherlands, headed from 18 May 1940 by the Austrian Nazi Arthur Seyss-Inquart as Reich commissioner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was installed under SS auspices, and its staff, many of them Austrian Nazis, supervised a Dutch civil service whose senior officials had been told by their exiled government to stay at their posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its leaders regarded the Dutch as a Germanic brother nation and tried to win them over to Nazism, an aim they applied far less to the Belgians and not at all to the French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seyss-Inquart ruled by decree, used hostage shootings and police courts against opposition, plundered Dutch property and from 1942 conscripted labour, sending over 500,000 people to work in the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrest of several hundred young Jews provoked a general strike on 25 February 1941, which the occupier crushed, and about three-quarters of the Jews in the Netherlands were murdered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Belgium, conquered in the same campaign, was governed differently, by a German military administration under General Alexander von Falkenhausen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That administration supervised rather than governed, leaving most of the work to the Belgian authorities and shadowing each Belgian office with a German one so that it needed few men of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Berlin judged its security policy too lax, and in July 1944 Hitler replaced the military government with a civilian one under a Reich commissioner, Josef Grohé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The share of Jews murdered was far higher in the Netherlands than in Belgium or France, and the Anne Frank House counts the SS-dominated civilian regime among the reasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Nuremberg the tribunal accepted that he had largely prevented the army's scorched-earth plans, but it found him a knowing participant in war crimes and sentenced him to death by hanging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Seyss-Inquart,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judseyss.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Netherlands,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-netherlands. [Open access]","Anne Frank House, “The Netherlands: The Greatest Number of Jewish Victims in Western Europe,” https://www.annefrank.org/en/anne-frank/go-in-depth/netherlands-greatest-number-jewish-victims-western-europe/. [Open access]","Dimitri Roden, “Administration militaire allemande (Militärverwaltung),” <i>Belgium WWII</i>, CegeSoma and State Archives in Belgium, https://www.belgiumwwii.be/belgique-en-guerre/articles/administration-militaire-allemande-militarverwaltung.html. [in French] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Belgium,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/belgium. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Sentences,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsent.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Netherlands get a civilian regime and Belgium a military one?","a":"Because the Nazis counted the Dutch as a Germanic brother nation to be won over to Nazism, and gave the task to a civil administration under strong SS influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Belgium was put under a military administration whose main aim was order and the use of its economy for the German war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Dutch civil service keep working?","a":"Because the exiled governments of the Netherlands and Belgium instructed their top civil servants to stay and work with the occupier in the interests of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Netherlands they were supervised by German officials, many of them Austrian Nazis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tribunal reject Seyss-Inquart's defence?","a":"Because although some crimes were ordered from Berlin or committed by the army and SS, he governed the territory and knew what was being done under his authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal accepted that he had resisted some extreme measures, but called him a knowing and voluntary participant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reichskommissariat Niederlande","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Arthur_Sey%C3%9F-Inquart%2C_Alexander_von_Falkenhausen%2C_Friedrich_Christiansen%2C_Den_Haag%2C_1940.jpg","title":"Seyss-Inquart in The Hague, May 1940","desc":"Arthur Seyss-Inquart leaves a building in The Hague on 29 May 1940 with General Alexander von Falkenhausen, military governor of Belgium, and General Friedrich Christiansen.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Haags Gemeentearchief, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Sey%C3%9F-Inquart,_Alexander_von_Falkenhausen,_Friedrich_Christiansen,_Den_Haag,_1940.jpg","alt":"A man in a party uniform walks down carpeted steps ahead of a group of German officers, with a helmeted soldier standing guard."}},
    "ww2-675": {"questions":["Baltic, Volhynian and Bessarabian Germans were settled in the homes of deported Poles in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Greiser vowed that not a centimetre of land would belong to a Pole again.","The killing centre at Chełmno was set up in autumn 1941 to murder the Jews of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the annexed Polish province that the Nazis meant to make wholly German."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichsgau Wartheland</b> was the region of western Poland that Germany annexed outright after its conquest in 1939, unlike the General Government further east, which was occupied but not incorporated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A decree of Hitler of 8 October 1939 incorporated the province of Poznań with parts of those of Łódź and Pomerania, first as Reichsgau Posen and then under the name Wartheland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1939 it held about five million people, among them some 4.2 million Poles, about 400,000 Jews and 325,000 ethnic Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its governor and Nazi party leader was Arthur Greiser, who answered directly to Hitler, while the SS and police were commanded by Wilhelm Koppe, who reported to Himmler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two men set out to Germanise the territory by deporting, imprisoning and killing Poles and Jews, and Chełmno, the first camp built to murder Jews in gas chambers, was set up there in 1941 for the Jews of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Deportations began within weeks, as Poles were turned out of their homes in Poznań and Łódź and sent east into the General Government, often with only hand luggage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ethnic Germans from the Baltic states, Volhynia, Bessarabia and Bukovina were then brought in and settled on Polish farms and in Polish flats, and across the annexed lands more than 500,000 arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greiser's proclamation of May 1941 promised that never again would a centimetre of the conquered land belong to a Pole, who might work only as a hireling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poles who were not entered on the list of ethnic Germans lost their rights to property, schools, associations and the law's protection, and Łódź, renamed Litzmannstadt, held the largest ghetto in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the war Poland's Supreme National Tribunal tried Greiser in June and July 1946, found him guilty, and had him hanged in public on 21 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations War Crimes Commission, “Trial of Gauleiter Artur Greiser,” <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 13 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 70–104, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-13/Law-Reports_Vol-13.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Chełmno,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/chelmno. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The RuSHA Case</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1950), 125–128, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lodz,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lodz. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Wartheland treated differently from the General Government?","a":"Because it was annexed to the Reich to become German land, whereas the General Government was kept as an occupied territory and a dumping ground for those expelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the annexed lands the aim was to remove Poles and Jews and settle Germans in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where did the settlers come from?","a":"Mostly from German minorities further east, moved under treaties or persuaded by promises, from the Baltic states, eastern Poland, Bessarabia and Bukovina, and given the farms and homes of deported Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many waited months in transit camps before being settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a Polish court try Greiser rather than an international one?","a":"Because his crimes were committed on Polish territory against Polish citizens, and Poland's Supreme National Tribunal judged them in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court treated them as part of the wider Nazi conspiracy and sentenced him to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reichsgau Wartheland","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J09394%2C_Lodz%2C_Millionster_Umsiedler_im_Wartheland.jpg","title":"Settlers welcomed in the Wartheland, 1944","desc":"A ceremony in Łódź in March 1944 marking the arrival of the millionth ethnic German resettler in the Wartheland, attended by Arthur Greiser.","credit":"Hoffmann, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-J09394, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J09394,_Lodz,_Millionster_Umsiedler_im_Wartheland.jpg","alt":"SS and party officials in uniform stand in a hall listening to a man in a dark suit speaking at a lectern with microphones."}},
    "ww2-676": {"questions":["Sorted into four groups by the Deutsche Volksliste, nearly three million Poles had been registered by 1944 in a programme of forced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run by Himmler's offices.","Neurath proposed in August 1940 that most Czechs be assimilated by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while the intelligentsia and others who might resist were expelled from the Protectorate."],"abstract":"<b>Germanisation</b> means making people or a territory German, and in the older sense it meant teaching a population the German language and German law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nazi Germany used the word for something else, and Himmler wrote in July 1942 that the task in the East was to ensure that only people of purely Germanic blood lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler appointed him Reich Commissioner for the Strengthening of Germanism on 7 October 1939, and four offices under him carried the programme out, from planning settlement to examining people by race, while Heydrich's security police carried out the expulsions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its means were expulsion and resettlement, the selection of those judged racially valuable, forced labour and the kidnapping of children, first in annexed Poland and then in Slovenia, Alsace, Lorraine and Luxembourg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Czech lands the Reich protector proposed in August 1940 that most Czechs be assimilated while the intelligentsia and others likely to resist were expelled, and Heydrich later pursued the same aim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In annexed Poland the main tool was the Deutsche Volksliste, the German people's list, which sorted inhabitants into four groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Groups one and two, people counted as ethnic Germans, became citizens automatically, while groups three and four, chiefly people of German stock said to have become Polish, received a citizenship that could be revoked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those left off the list in the annexed lands were classed as stateless, and in the Wartheland they lost their rights to property, free choice of work and most education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Registration was often forced, by threats to send people to concentration camps or to take their children, and by January 1944 nearly three million Poles had been entered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the General Government's Zamość region in 1942 and 1943 the SS removed some 100,000 Poles, and children judged suitable were shipped to the Reich to be raised as Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The RuSHA Case</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1950), 88–100, 120–128, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Von Neurath,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judneur.asp. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “Trial of Gauleiter Artur Greiser,” <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 13 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 72–73, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-13/Law-Reports_Vol-13.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4, proceedings of 20 December 1945 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/12-20-45.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lidice,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lidice. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How did Nazi Germanisation differ from the older kind?","a":"The older kind taught a population German language and law, so that anyone could in principle become German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Himmler rejected that: Nazi Germanisation meant selecting people by supposed blood, keeping those judged Germanic and removing the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why sort Poles into four groups?","a":"Because the regime wanted to keep anyone it judged of German stock or racially valuable while expelling or subordinating the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The group decided a person's citizenship, from automatic for groups one and two to revocable for groups three and four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did people register if they did not wish to?","a":"Because refusal could be punished: the RuSHA tribunal found that German authorities threatened to take children or send people to concentration camps, and sometimes registered people without their consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Wartheland those left off the list lost basic rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Germanisation","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/German_Police_%28Nazi_Germany_WW2%29_Supreme_Headq._Allied_Expedit._Force_%28SHAEF%29_intelligence_handbook_April_1945._Personal_documents_155_Ausweis_der_deutschen_Volksliste_%28Green_certificate_of_registration_for_Racial_Germans%29_No_copyright.jpg","title":"A Deutsche Volksliste certificate","desc":"The cover and inside of a green registration certificate of the Deutsche Volksliste, reproduced in an Allied intelligence handbook of April 1945.","credit":"Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Police_(Nazi_Germany_WW2)_Supreme_Headq._Allied_Expedit._Force_(SHAEF)_intelligence_handbook_April_1945._Personal_documents_155_Ausweis_der_deutschen_Volksliste_(Green_certificate_of_registration_for_Racial_Germans)_No_copyright.jpg","alt":"A green identity booklet titled 'Ausweis der Deutschen Volksliste' with an eagle on the cover, shown open beside its stamped inner pages."}},
    "ww2-677": {"questions":["An American tribunal in 1948 acquitted the officials of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> society of kidnapping children, although it had placed children seized in the East with German families.","Polish children judged racially valuable were taken to the Reich, renamed and passed off as German orphans, and some reached adoptive families through the SS society <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the war."],"abstract":"<b>Lebensborn</b>, meaning ‘fount of life’, was a society created by the SS in late 1935 to raise the birth rate of children the Nazis considered racially valuable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ran discreet maternity homes where unmarried mothers who could prove their Aryan descent could give birth, and it offered financial help and arranged adoptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Himmler oversaw it closely, as part of his wish that SS men should have large families and that racially approved pregnancies should not end in abortion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the war it opened homes in occupied countries and took charge of children fathered by Germans on local women whose ancestry passed its tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also helped place children taken from eastern and southeastern Europe with German families, who were told the children were war orphans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The seizure of children was a policy, not an accident, and in October 1943 Himmler said Germany would take children of good blood from other nations, kidnapping them if necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In annexed Poland children in orphanages and foster homes were examined by SS race experts, and those chosen were to be described as German orphans from the regained eastern territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum estimates that at least 20,000 Polish children were taken from their families and sent to the Reich to be Germanised, many from the Zamość region, and nine children of Lidice were chosen in the same way in 1942 and given German names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When an American military tribunal at Nuremberg judged the SS race offices in 1948, it convicted officials who organised the kidnappings but acquitted the four Lebensborn officials of kidnapping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It found that only a small share of the kidnapped children ever reached Lebensborn, which it called a welfare institution, but the Holocaust Museum's history describes the society as complicit in the kidnapping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Lebensborn Program,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensborn-program. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The RuSHA Case</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1950), 100–108, 162–163, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “Trial of Gauleiter Artur Greiser,” <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 13 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 72, 87, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-13/Law-Reports_Vol-13.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lidice,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lidice. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Reinhard Heydrich 1904–1942,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/reinhard-heydrich. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the SS want children from other nations at all?","a":"Because Nazi racial thinking held that good blood could be found among other peoples and should be taken for Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Himmler said in 1943 that such children would be won for Germany, by robbing or stealing them if need be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were kidnapped children called German orphans?","a":"Because the decree on Polish orphanages ordered that the phrase 'Polish children suitable for Germanisation' must not reach the public, so the children were described as German orphans from the eastern territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Wartheland they were also given German names and cut off from their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the 1948 tribunal acquit Lebensborn's officials?","a":"Because it judged that the prosecution had not proved Lebensborn's part in the kidnapping programme with enough certainty, and that most children in its care were orphans of ethnic Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later accounts, such as the Holocaust Museum's, describe the society as complicit all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lebensborn","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1973-010-11%2C_Schwester_in_einem_Lebensbornheim.jpg","title":"A Lebensborn home, 1943","desc":"A nurse with prams outside a Lebensborn home under an SS flag, from an SS publication of 1943.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1973-010-11, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1973-010-11,_Schwester_in_einem_Lebensbornheim.jpg","alt":"A nurse in white stands beside a row of covered prams on a terrace outside a half-timbered house, with a large SS flag flying above."}},
    "ww2-678": {"questions":["Hitler judged Reich Protector <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> too lenient towards Czech resistance and in September 1941 sent Reinhard Heydrich to Prague to take over his duties.","Sentenced to 15 years at Nuremberg for his part in aggression and his rule of Bohemia and Moravia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was released early from Spandau because of illness."],"abstract":"<b>Konstantin von Neurath</b> was a German diplomat who served as foreign minister from 1932 to 1938 and then as the first Reich Protector of Bohemia and Moravia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As foreign minister under Papen, Schleicher and Hitler he advised on leaving the League of Nations, on rearmament and on the reoccupation of the Rhineland in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He attended the meeting of November 1937 at which Hitler set out his plans for war, resigned the ministry in February 1938, and still stayed in the government as a minister without portfolio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appointed Reich Protector on 18 March 1939, he answered to Hitler alone and could overrule the nominally autonomous Czech government at will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Nuremberg he was convicted on all four counts and sentenced to 15 years, and he was released from Spandau in 1954 because of illness and died in 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His administration abolished the free press, political parties and trade unions, introduced Nazi anti-Jewish laws and harnessed Czech industry to German war production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When war broke out about 8,000 prominent Czechs were taken into custody, and after student demonstrations that autumn the universities were closed, 1,200 students imprisoned and nine leaders shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The proclamation announcing this bore his signature, though he told the tribunal he had not been informed beforehand, and he did intervene for the release of some prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1940 he sent Berlin a memorandum proposing that most Czechs be assimilated while the intelligentsia and others who might resist were expelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1941 Hitler told him he was not harsh enough and sent Reinhard Heydrich to take over his duties, and Neurath went on leave on 27 September, though his resignation was formally accepted only in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Von Neurath,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judneur.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Konstantin Freiherr von Neurath 1873–1956,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/konstantin-neurath. [in German] [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “‘Reichsprotektorat Böhmen und Mähren’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/reichsprotektorat-boehmen-und-maehren. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Sentences,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judsent.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Czechoslovakia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/czechoslovakia. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Reinhard Heydrich 1904–1942,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/reinhard-heydrich. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Neurath held responsible for repression carried out by the police?","a":"Because he was the chief German official in the Protectorate and knew that war crimes were being committed under his authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He argued that the Security Police answered to his state secretary Karl Hermann Frank and to Himmler, but the tribunal did not accept that this cleared him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler replace him with Heydrich?","a":"Because Czech resistance was growing and Hitler judged that Neurath was not dealing with it harshly enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neurath tried to talk Hitler out of sending Heydrich, failed, offered to resign and went on leave instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he receive a prison sentence rather than death?","a":"Because the tribunal weighed in mitigation that he had intervened for the release of many arrested Czechs and students and had opposed Heydrich's appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was sentenced to 15 years' imprisonment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Konstantin von Neurath","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-L15757%2C_Prag%2C_Hochschulkonferenz%2C_v._Neurath.jpg","title":"Neurath in Prague, December 1940","desc":"Reich Protector Konstantin von Neurath, right, at a conference of university directors in Prague in December 1940.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-L15757, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-L15757,_Prag,_Hochschulkonferenz,_v._Neurath.jpg","alt":"A group of men in suits and uniforms talking in a room, with an older grey-haired man in uniform seen from the side in the foreground."}},
    "ww2-679": {"questions":["Head of the Reich Security Main Office, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chaired the Wannsee Conference of January 1942 to coordinate the murder of the Jews of Europe.","Dismissed from the navy in 1931, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built the SS security service for Himmler and went on to command the Gestapo and the Einsatzgruppen."],"abstract":"<b>Reinhard Heydrich</b> was the SS officer who headed the Nazi security apparatus and was one of the chief organisers of the murder of Europe's Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forced out of the navy in 1931 after a court of honour, he was taken on by Himmler to build the party's security service, the SD, and by 1936 he also commanded the Gestapo and the criminal police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In August 1939 he ordered the faked Polish attack on the radio station at Gleiwitz that served as a pretext for war, and from September he led the Reich Security Main Office, whose Einsatzgruppen murdered Polish elites and later Soviet Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In July 1941 Göring authorised him to prepare a total solution of the Jewish question in Europe, and at the Wannsee Conference of 20 January 1942 he set out the plan to the ministries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His minutes there list the Jews of every European country, eleven million in all, as the target of the final solution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In September 1941 Hitler made him acting Reich Protector of Bohemia and Moravia, because he judged Neurath too soft on the growing Czech resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heydrich declared martial law on arrival, and in October and November special courts sentenced 342 people to death while 1,289 more were handed to the Gestapo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among the first executed were leaders of the military resistance, and on 24 November he opened the Theresienstadt ghetto to concentrate and deport the Czech Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> He then courted Czech industrial workers and farmers with German wages and benefits, and within 6 months acts of sabotage had fallen by 73 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confident that the country was pacified, he drove through Prague in an open car, and Czech parachutists sent from Britain attacked him on 27 May 1942; he died of his wounds on 4 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Reinhard Heydrich: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/reinhard-heydrich-in-depth. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Reinhard Heydrich 1904–1942,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/reinhard-heydrich. [in German] [Open access]","“Minutes of the Wannsee Conference, 20 January 1942,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/wannsee.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “‘Reichsprotektorat Böhmen und Mähren’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/reichsprotektorat-boehmen-und-maehren. [in German] [Open access]","Vojenský historický ústav Praha, “Dnes si připomínáme 84 let od seskoku výsadkové skupiny Anthropoid,” https://www.vhu.cz/dnes-si-pripominame-84-let-od-seskoku-vysadkove-skupiny-anthropoid/. [in Czech] [Open access]","Vojenský historický ústav Praha, “Atentát 80: Oduznání Mnichova a atentát na Reinharda Heydricha na křižovatkách československých a velmocenských zájmů,” https://www.vhu.cz/atentat-80-oduznani-mnichova-a-atentat-na-reinharda-heydricha-na-krizovatkach-ceskoslovenskych-a-velmocenskych-zajmu/. [in Czech] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4, proceedings of 20 December 1945 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/12-20-45.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Theresienstadt,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/theresienstadt. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Heydrich mix terror with better wages in the Protectorate?","a":"Because Czech industry and farming mattered to the German war effort, so after a narrow wave of terror against real and suspected resistance leaders he offered workers and farmers German wages and benefits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sabotage fell sharply within 6 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Czech exile leadership decide to kill him?","a":"Because his first weeks in Prague brought executions of leaders of the home military resistance, and the Czech intelligence chiefs in London wanted retribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British Special Operations Executive backed the plan for its own reasons, seeing a chance to damage the Nazi security apparatus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was he so central to the Holocaust?","a":"Because his Reich Security Main Office directed the Einsatzgruppen and the deportations, and Göring charged him in 1941 with planning a total solution of the Jewish question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Wannsee he coordinated the ministries behind that plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reinhard Heydrich","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1969-054-16%2C_Reinhard_Heydrich.jpg","title":"Reinhard Heydrich","desc":"A portrait of Reinhard Heydrich in SS uniform, by Heinrich Hoffmann.","credit":"Heinrich Hoffmann, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1969-054-16, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1969-054-16,_Reinhard_Heydrich.jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders portrait of a fair-haired man in a grey SS uniform with collar insignia and medal ribbons."}},
    "ww2-680": {"questions":["Betrayed by a fellow parachutist, seven parachutists hid in the Church of Saints Cyril and Methodius in Prague after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and fought their last battle against SS troops there on 18 June 1942.","Czech military intelligence kept preparing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after President Beneš warned in October 1941 that killing Heydrich would bring savage reprisals at home."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Anthropoid</b> was the plan to assassinate Reinhard Heydrich, the acting Reich Protector of Bohemia and Moravia, carried out in Prague on 27 May 1942 by Czechoslovak soldiers sent from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The decision was taken in London at the turn of September and October 1941 by František Moravec, head of the exiled Czechoslovak army's intelligence, and the British Special Operations Executive was told on 3 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jozef Gabčík and Karel Svoboda were chosen first, but Svoboda was injured in training and Jan Kubiš took his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Halifax bomber of the RAF's 138 Squadron dropped Gabčík and Kubiš near the village of Nehvizdy, close to Čelákovice, in the early hours of 29 December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With help from other parachutists they fired on Heydrich's open car on his usual route to the airport and threw a grenade, whose splinters caused an infection of which he died on 4 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> For the Czech intelligence chiefs the motive was retribution for Heydrich's executions of resistance leaders, while the British saw a chance to strike at the whole Nazi security apparatus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Beneš warned Moravec in October 1941 that the plan risked savage reprisals, but the intelligence service kept preparing it in secret and told him the parachutists had another task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Martial law followed from 27 May to 3 July 1942, and the reprisals included the destruction of Lidice, where 173 men and boys were shot, and of Ležáky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Betrayed by the parachutist Karel Čurda, seven parachutists hiding in the Church of Saints Cyril and Methodius fought their last battle against 757 SS troops on 18 June and were all killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In August 1942 Britain declared it would not be bound by the frontier changes of 1938, a step the attack and the reprisals helped to bring about, though it stopped short of repudiating Munich outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Vojenský historický ústav Praha, “Dnes si připomínáme 84 let od seskoku výsadkové skupiny Anthropoid,” https://www.vhu.cz/dnes-si-pripominame-84-let-od-seskoku-vysadkove-skupiny-anthropoid/. [in Czech] [Open access]","Vojenský historický ústav Praha, “Atentát 80: Oduznání Mnichova a atentát na Reinharda Heydricha na křižovatkách československých a velmocenských zájmů,” https://www.vhu.cz/atentat-80-oduznani-mnichova-a-atentat-na-reinharda-heydricha-na-krizovatkach-ceskoslovenskych-a-velmocenskych-zajmu/. [in Czech] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Reinhard Heydrich: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/reinhard-heydrich-in-depth. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “‘Reichsprotektorat Böhmen und Mähren’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/aussenpolitik/reichsprotektorat-boehmen-und-maehren. [in German] [Open access]","Vojenský historický ústav Praha, “Nová zjištění ohledně posledního boje parašutistů v kryptě kostela v Resslově ulici,” https://www.vhu.cz/nova-zjisteni-ohledne-posledniho-boje-parasutistu-v-krypte-kostela-v-resslove-ulici/. [in Czech] [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Reinhard Heydrich 1904–1942,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/reinhard-heydrich. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lidice,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lidice. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British back a Czech plan to kill one man?","a":"Because the Special Operations Executive saw that Heydrich would be hard to replace and that his death would weaken the Nazi security apparatus it fought across occupied Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its chief of operations said as much in an internal paper of 31 May 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was President Beneš kept in the dark?","a":"Because he had warned that the attack was too risky without agreement from home and would bring brutal reprisals, which in effect stopped it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The intelligence chiefs, distrusting civilian secrecy, carried on and gave him a different account of the parachutists' task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How were the parachutists found in the church?","a":"Through betrayal: Karel Čurda, another parachutist, gave them away, and German forces surrounded the church in Resslova Street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seven men held out against 757 SS troops, who could attack only in groups of about twenty, in a long and bitter fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Assassination of Reinhard Heydrich","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1972-039-44%2C_Heydrich-Attentat.jpg","title":"Heydrich's car after the attack","desc":"Heydrich's Mercedes on the road in Prague after the attack of 27 May 1942, its side torn by the grenade.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1972-039-44, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1972-039-44,_Heydrich-Attentat.jpg","alt":"An open-topped car with its folded roof at the back stands at the roadside, its rear door and wheel arch blown open and debris on the ground."}},
    "ww2-681": {"questions":["Nine children were picked out for Germanisation and about eighty others murdered, probably at Chełmno, after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that razed a mining village near Kladno in June 1942.","A letter found by investigators gave Hitler his pretext for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 10 June 1942, although no real evidence tied the village to Heydrich's assassins."],"abstract":"The <b>Lidice massacre</b> was the destruction of the Czech mining village of Lidice, about 20 kilometres (12 miles) from Prague, by German police and SS men on 10 June 1942, in reprisal for the assassination of Reinhard Heydrich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler ordered the village annihilated on 9 June, the day of Heydrich's state funeral, because a letter found early in the investigation had named it, though no real evidence linked its people to the assassins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kladno Gestapo suspected the Horák family, whose son served in the Czechoslovak army in Britain, but a search of the village found no weapons, transmitter or compromising material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Surrounding Lidice in the night, the Germans separated the men and boys aged fifteen and over from the women and younger children and shot 173 of them at a farmstead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They then burned the houses, blew them up and destroyed the church and even the cemetery, so that by 1943 only an empty space remained, closed by notices forbidding entry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The women and children were taken to Kladno, and most of the 203 women were deported to Ravensbrück concentration camp, where 53 of them died before the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Łódź officials of the SS Race and Settlement Main Office screened the children, chose nine for Germanisation and adoption, and the eighty or so others were murdered, probably by gas at Chełmno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In all 340 people of Lidice were killed, 143 women and 17 children came home, and a monument of 82 bronze children, completed in 2000, now looks over the valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By September 1942 a movement called Lidice Shall Live, begun in Britain by the doctor and MP Barnett Stross, set out to keep the name on the map, and places around the world took it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Nuremberg the Soviet prosecution read a Czechoslovak report on the village, and the tribunal's judgment named Lidice as an example of the organised use of terror to destroy all opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lidice: The Annihilation of a Czech Town,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lidice. [Open access]","Lidice Memorial, “History of the Village Lidice,” https://www.lidice-memorial.cz/en/memorial/memorial-and-reverent-area/history-of-the-village-lidice/. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Lidice Memorial, “War Children’s Victims Monument,” https://www.lidice-memorial.cz/en/memorial/war-childrens-victims-monument/. [Open access]","Imke Hansen, review of <i>Topographies of Suffering: Buchenwald, Babi Yar, Lidice</i>, by Jessica Rapson, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, April 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=46665. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 8, proceedings of 18 February 1946 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-18-46.asp. [Open access]","Lidice Memorial, “Sir Barnett Stross,” https://www.lidice-memorial.cz/en/exhibitions-and-events/on-tour/sir-barnett-stross/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Lidice chosen for the reprisal?","a":"Because a letter found in the first days of the investigation named the village, and the Kladno Gestapo linked it to the Horák family, whose son was a soldier in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A search found nothing, but the Germans wanted an act of vengeance and singled out Lidice anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the children of Lidice?","a":"Most were sent to Łódź, where SS racial examiners chose nine for Germanisation and adoption by German families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About eighty others were murdered, probably in gas vans at Chełmno, and only 17 children came home after a 2-year search.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans publicise what they had done?","a":"Because the destruction was meant as a warning: they filmed it and German radio reported it, promising to erase the name from the map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan failed, for Lidice became an international symbol of Nazi brutality and the film was shown at Nuremberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lidice massacre","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-020-26A%2C_Lidice%2C_Ort_nach_Zerst%C3%B6rung.jpg","title":"Lidice after its destruction, June 1942","desc":"The ruins of Lidice in June 1942, after German police and SS men had burned and demolished the village.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1993-020-26A, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-020-26A,_Lidice,_Ort_nach_Zerst%C3%B6rung.jpg","alt":"A field of rubble with a few gutted buildings and trees, men in uniform standing among the ruins."}},
    "ww2-682": {"questions":["General Böhme ordered a hundred hostages shot for every German soldier killed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his report of October 1941 listed 2,300 hostages shot at Kragujevac.","Security Police used a gas van to murder the Jewish women and children of the Semlin camp in spring 1942, leaving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with virtually no Jews alive by summer."],"abstract":"The <b>Territory of the Military Commander in Serbia</b> was the part of Serbia that Germany placed under military occupation after Yugoslavia surrendered in April 1941, governed from Belgrade, with the Banat and its ethnic German minority occupied beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler defined German interests in the Balkans narrowly, as the supply routes to the air bases in Greece and Crete, shipping on the Danube and the copper-producing area of north-eastern Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A commissarial administration under Milan Aćimović was formed on 30 April 1941, and when it could not restore order it was replaced on 29 August by a government under the former general Milan Nedić.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nedić's government had a gendarmerie and a political police, and beside them the occupiers raised a Border Guard, a State Guard and the Serbian Volunteer Corps of Dimitrije Ljotić.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A postwar study for the United States Army called Nedić a Pétain-like figure and found the SS and police agencies answering to Himmler a constant irritation to the military commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By late summer 1941 an uprising led by the communist partisans and by Draža Mihailović's Chetniks had inflicted serious casualties on German troops and police in Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Keitel's order of 16 September 1941 spoke of fifty or a hundred lives for each German, and General Franz Böhme, given executive power in Serbia, ordered a hundred hostages shot for every German soldier killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Böhme's report of 31 October 1941 listed 2,300 hostages shot at Kragujevac and 1,700 at Kraljevo, and by German count 20,149 people were executed in Serbia between September 1941 and February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under cover of the hostage order the army shot virtually all male Jews in Serbia, about 8,000 people, among them 2,100 Jews and Roma taken from the camps at Šabac and Belgrade in October 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between March and May 1942 a gas van killed about 6,280 people, mostly Jewish women and children from the Semlin camp, and by that summer virtually no Jews remained alive in Serbia outside hiding or the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 13–20, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “Trial of Wilhelm List and Others,” <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 38–42, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","Yves Tomic, “Massacres in Dismembered Yugoslavia, 1941–1945,” <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 June 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/massacres-in-dismembered-yugoslavia-1941-1945/. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11, <i>The Hostage Case</i> (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1950), 802–5, prosecution opening statement, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany rule Serbia directly when Italy took most of the Balkans?","a":"Because Hitler wanted the occupation left mainly to the Italians and kept for Germany only what mattered to the war: supply routes, Danube shipping and the copper of north-eastern Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Serbia was also seen as a special problem, given its people's hostility to Germany and their former dominance in Yugoslavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did a hostage order become a means of murdering Jews?","a":"Because the order to shoot a hundred hostages for every German killed let the army choose whom to shoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Male Jews were among those arrested as hostages, and German units used the order to shoot virtually all of them in the autumn of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans set up a Serbian government at all?","a":"Because puppet regimes and native security forces lightened the administrative burden and reduced the number of German troops needed to keep order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Aćimović administration had failed to master the uprising, so Nedić replaced it in August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Territory of the Military Commander in Serbia","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Milan_Nedi%C4%87%2C_predsednik_kvislin%C5%A1ke_vlade%2C_govori_trupama_uo%C4%8Di_borbe_protiv_partizana.jpg","title":"Milan Nedić addresses his troops","desc":"Milan Nedić, head of the puppet Serbian government, speaks to troops before an operation against the partisans.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milan_Nedi%C4%87,_predsednik_kvislin%C5%A1ke_vlade,_govori_trupama_uo%C4%8Di_borbe_protiv_partizana.jpg","alt":"A man in a dark suit on a low platform facing ranks of helmeted soldiers in front of a building."}},
    "ww2-683": {"questions":["The memorial site's incomplete list of victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names 83,145 people, while the Holocaust Memorial Museum estimates between 77,000 and 99,000 dead.","About 600 men tried to break out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 22 April 1945 as Ustaša guards murdered the last prisoners before the partisans arrived."],"abstract":"The <b>Jasenovac concentration camp</b> was a complex of camps on the Sava river, about 100 kilometres (60 miles) south of Zagreb, run from August 1941 to April 1945 by the Ustaša regime of the Independent State of Croatia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The site was chosen for its railway line, its flat ground that was easy to watch and hard to attack, and the brickworks, sawmill and chain factory of the Bačić family, which gave prisoners work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first two camps, Krapje and Bročice, were broken up in November 1941 by mass killings, and the survivors were moved to Camp III, the brickworks called Ciglana, which from then on worked as a death camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stara Gradiška, a holding centre for political prisoners, became a camp for women in the winter of 1942, and the whole complex was guarded by Croatian political police and the Ustaša militia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners were starved and tortured and were killed by hanging, with knives, mallets and axes, or by shooting, and the main killing ground lay across the river at Donja Gradina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The number of dead is contested and politicised, and estimates have ranged from a few thousand to 700,000 or even a million, shaped by postwar ideological and ethnic agendas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum estimates that the regime murdered between 77,000 and 99,000 people there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By its count they were 45,000 to 52,000 Serbs, 12,000 to 20,000 Jews, 15,000 to 20,000 Roma and 5,000 to 12,000 Croats and Muslims who opposed the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jasenovac Memorial Site's list, built from a Yugoslav inventory of 1964 and hundreds of other sources and still incomplete, names 83,145 victims, among them 20,101 children under fourteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The memorial site judges that the true number will probably never be known but that Serbs were the largest group, and on 22 April 1945 about 600 men tried to break out as the guards killed the last prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jasenovac,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jasenovac. [Open access]","Spomen-područje Jasenovac, “Poimenični popis žrtava,” Jasenovac Memorial Site, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=6284. [in Croatian] [Open access]","Spomen-područje Jasenovac, “Srbi u KL Jasenovac,” Jasenovac Memorial Site, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=9593. [in Croatian] [Open access]","Spomen-područje Jasenovac, “Otpor u logoru,” Jasenovac Memorial Site, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=9680. [in Croatian] [Open access]","Yves Tomic, “Massacres in Dismembered Yugoslavia, 1941–1945,” <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 June 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/massacres-in-dismembered-yugoslavia-1941-1945/. [Open access]","Spomen-područje Jasenovac, “Jasenovački sustav logora,” Jasenovac Memorial Site, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=5345. [in Croatian] [Open access]","Spomen-područje Jasenovac, “Logor smrti,” Jasenovac Memorial Site, https://www.jusp-jasenovac.hr/Default.aspx?sid=9459. [in Croatian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do estimates of the dead differ so widely?","a":"Because many documents were destroyed, those that survived were long closed to independent scholars, and postwar writing was shaped by ideological and ethnic agendas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Figures have ranged from a few thousand to a million, while the museum's estimate rests on census records and archives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Serbs the largest group among the victims?","a":"Because the Ustaša state set out to settle what it called the Serbian question by expulsion, forced conversion and killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Serbs were deported to Jasenovac from its founding, and after the Kozara offensive of summer 1942 tens of thousands of Serbian peasants were brought there and most were killed at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happened to the Jews held at Jasenovac?","a":"Croatian authorities deported Jews there from 1941 to 1943 and shot many at the nearby killing sites of Granik and Gradina, sparing some with useful skills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1942 and May 1943 most surviving Croatian Jews, including many at Jasenovac, were handed to the Germans and sent to Auschwitz-Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jasenovac concentration camp","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Jasenovac_prisoners_enter_the_camp.jpg","title":"Prisoners arriving at Jasenovac","desc":"Ustaša guards confiscate the belongings of newly arrived prisoners at the Jasenovac camp.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jasenovac_prisoners_enter_the_camp.jpg","alt":"Uniformed guards searching kneeling and standing civilians, with open suitcases and clothes spread on the ground."}},
    "ww2-684": {"questions":["More than 40,000 Jews of Salonika were deported to Auschwitz-Birkenau between March and August 1943 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when the last Germans left in November 1944.","General Tsolakoglou, who had surrendered the Army of Epirus, headed a collaborationist government during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that raised police and security units but no national army."],"abstract":"The <b>Axis occupation of Greece</b> began when German and Italian forces, supported by Bulgarian and Hungarian units, attacked on 6 April 1941, and it lasted until the last German units left on 2 November 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany kept Salonika and western Macedonia, the area round Athens, western Crete and the islands near Turkey, Italy took most of the mainland and many islands, and Bulgaria took western Thrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Bulgarians felt they were recovering land lost in 1913 and ruled harshly from the outset, although the final status of western Thrace was to be settled only at the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A collaborationist government was formed under General Tsolakoglou, who had surrendered the Army of Epirus on 20 April 1941, and it raised police and security units but no national army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With imports cut off and production falling, official food in the Athens area was estimated at 183 calories a person a day in November 1941, until Red Cross relief began to arrive in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The occupiers' differing policies decided the fate of nearly 72,000 Jews, for Italian officials generally ignored German demands and thousands of Jews fled into the Italian zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Salonika, home to the largest community, the Germans registered 9,000 Jewish men for forced labour in Liberty Square in July 1942 and demanded a ransom, partly raised by selling the Jewish cemetery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between March and August 1943 they deported more than 40,000 Jews from Salonika to Auschwitz-Birkenau, where most were murdered on arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bulgarian forces handed about 4,200 Jews of Thrace to the Germans in March 1943 for murder at Treblinka, and after Italy's surrender Germany deported the Jews of Athens, Corfu and Rhodes, so that nearly 60,000 Greek Jews died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German orders of 1943 called for hostages from every class of the population and the destruction of nearby villages after attacks, and the Hostages trial found many reprisals in Greece aimed at people unconnected with the deed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Greece,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/greece. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Salonika,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/salonika. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 13–18, 64–65, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]","League of Nations, Economic, Financial and Transit Department, <i>Food, Famine and Relief, 1940–1946</i> (Geneva: League of Nations, 1946), 38, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.201861. [Open access]","Nadège Ragaru, “Contrasting Destinies: The Plight of Bulgarian Jews and the Jews in Bulgarian-occupied Greek and Yugoslav Territories during World War Two,” <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 15 March 2017, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/contrasting-destinies-the-plight-of-bulgarian-jews-and-the-jews-in-bulgarian-occupied-greek-and-yugoslav-territories-during-world-war-two/. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “Trial of Wilhelm List and Others,” <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 46–48, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fate of Greek Jews depend on the zone they lived in?","a":"Because the three occupiers pursued different policies: Italian officials generally ignored German demands and even protected Jews, while the Germans deported the Jews of Salonika in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only after Italy surrendered did Germany extend the deportations to the former Italian zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Athens go hungry so quickly?","a":"Because Greece normally imported about 40 per cent of its wheat, and the occupation cut off imports while domestic production fell sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rationing broke down early, and the city population received perhaps 600 to 800 calories a day until relief arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Bulgaria rule its zone harshly from the start?","a":"Because Bulgarians believed they were recovering territory lost to Greece in the Second Balkan War of 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unlike southern Dobrudja, however, western Thrace had no treaty behind it, and its final status was to be decided at the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Axis occupation of Greece","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-168-0894-21A%2C_Griechenland%2C_Saloniki%2C_Erfassung_von_Juden.jpg","title":"Liberty Square, Salonika, July 1942","desc":"Jewish men assembled in Liberty Square in German-occupied Salonika to be registered for forced labour in July 1942.","credit":"Dick, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-168-0894-21A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-168-0894-21A,_Griechenland,_Saloniki,_Erfassung_von_Juden.jpg","alt":"Thousands of men standing in rows across a large city square, seen from above, with buildings behind."}},
    "ww2-685": {"questions":["Joseph Darnand, Philippe Henriot and Marcel Déat, leading figures of French <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, entered the Vichy government only in December 1943 and under German pressure.","Visceral anti-communism recurs in the memoirs of French, Spanish and Italian volunteers whose <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led them into German service, while Nordic SS recruits stood closer to Nazism itself."],"abstract":"<b>Collaborationism</b> is the term historians use for collaboration with Nazi Germany chosen out of conviction or ideological agreement, as distinct from the cooperation of an occupied state out of necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1968 the historian Stanley Hoffmann set this intentional, individual form against state collaboration, voluntary or forced, which aimed to keep public order and economic life going, as the Vichy government did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Collaboration by governments and citizens was a fundamental part of the working of German-occupied Europe, and unlike open ideological commitment to Nazism it was by no means a marginal phenomenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Berlin preferred to deal with the traditional elites as long as they kept order, and turned to the extremists only as those elites drew back once the war against the Soviet Union multiplied its demands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In France the collaborationists Joseph Darnand, Philippe Henriot and Marcel Déat entered the government only in December 1943, under German pressure, yet the boundaries were porous and many ultra-collaborationists believed they were patriots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Motives varied widely, and the Holocaust Memorial Museum names antisemitism, nationalism, ethnic hatred, anti-communism and opportunism among the reasons people in occupied nations worked with the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Volunteers from France, Spain, Italy and Greece wrote again and again of a visceral anti-communism, while Nordic recruits to the SS were politically closer to the Nazis from the outset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thousands of Danes, Swedes and Swiss who joined the SS saw themselves as the vanguard of a Germanic empire that would end liberal democracy, and many were educated, successful men rather than misfits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the East men also joined German units to build national states of their own, to escape starving in prisoner-of-war camps or for power and plunder, and some simply sought adventure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A study of the western Pyrenees found the same people acting as collaborationists at some moments and as resisters at others, which shows how hard the categories are to apply to lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Barbara Lambauer, “Collaboration in Europe, 1939–1945,” <i>Encyclopédie d’histoire numérique de l’Europe</i>, Sorbonne Université, https://ehne.fr/en/encyclopedia/themes/wars-and-memories/occupybe-occupied/collaboration-in-europe-1939-1945. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Collaboration,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/collaboration. [Open access]","Jeff Rutherford, review of <i>The Waffen-SS: A European History</i>, ed. Jochen Böhler and Robert Gerwarth, H-German, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50223. [Open access]","Richard Byers, review of <i>Building a Nazi Europe: The SS’s Germanic Volunteers</i>, by Martin R. Gutmann, H-War, H-Net Reviews, August 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52066. [Open access]","John W. Weigel, review of <i>Living with the Enemy: German Occupation, Collaboration and Justice in the Western Pyrenees, 1940–1948</i>, by Sandra Ott, H-War, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50482. [Open access]","Lisa Kirschenbaum, review of <i>Ghosts of War: Nazi Occupation and Its Aftermath in Soviet Belarus</i>, by Franziska Exeler, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, September 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=60637. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Berlin prefer traditional elites to fascist enthusiasts?","a":"Because economic, military and security needs came before ideology, and established politicians and officials could keep order and supply the war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only when those elites withdrew their support under heavier German demands did the extremist collaborators come forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the line between state collaboration and collaborationism hard to draw?","a":"Because the forms overlapped, and many ultra-collaborationists saw themselves as acting for their country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the local level people could behave as collaborators at one moment and as resisters at another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What drew men from the occupied East into German service?","a":"Anti-communism after Stalinist rule mattered, and many believed they were helping to build their own national states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Survival counted too: for prisoners of war a security unit meant escape from starvation, and civilians saw service as a way to feed their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Le_fasciste_fran%C3%A7ais_Jacques_Doriot_descendant_les_Champs-Elys%C3%A9es%2C_%C3%A0_Paris%2C_lors_d%E2%80%99une_manifestation_du_Parti_populaire_fran%C3%A7ais%2C_ao%C3%BBt_1943.jpg","title":"Jacques Doriot on the Champs-Élysées, 1943","desc":"The French fascist leader Jacques Doriot salutes from a car during a rally of his Parti populaire français in Paris in August 1943.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain in France, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_fasciste_fran%C3%A7ais_Jacques_Doriot_descendant_les_Champs-Elys%C3%A9es,_%C3%A0_Paris,_lors_d%E2%80%99une_manifestation_du_Parti_populaire_fran%C3%A7ais,_ao%C3%BBt_1943.jpg","alt":"A man in glasses standing in an open car with his arm raised, the Arc de Triomphe behind him and saluting supporters around."},"wiki":"Wartime collaboration"},
    "ww2-686": {"questions":["Local self-defence groups of 1941 were purged and reorganised by the SS into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a native police that helped German units murder Jews across the occupied Soviet Union.","For a Soviet prisoner of war, joining the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could mean escape from starvation, though other men enlisted for plunder, out of antisemitism or under forced conscription."],"abstract":"The <b>Schutzmannschaft</b> was the native auxiliary police that the German SS and police raised in the occupied Soviet territories from Lithuanians, Latvians, Estonians, Belarusians, Ukrainians and others, serving locally and in battalions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its roots lay in the self-defence groups that sprang up in the summer of 1941, which the SS and police purged and reorganised into auxiliaries who helped massacre hundreds of thousands of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some 300,000 Soviet citizens served in the German police during the war, and the auxiliary police grew fast in 1942 as the threat from the partisans increased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Men joined for power, plunder or privileges, out of antisemitism or resentment of Soviet rule, or because they were conscripted by force, and for prisoners of war service was an escape from starvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly all the Lithuanian police battalions took part in the Holocaust in 1941, and Ukrainian auxiliaries helped the SS and police murder 23,600 Jews at Kamenets-Podolsk in August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Schutzmannschaft Battalion 118, formed in the Kyiv region in the spring of 1942, had a core of Ukrainian nationalists from Bukovyna and filled its ranks with Soviet prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roughly 500 men served in Belarus until July 1944, where German collective punishment of partisan areas levelled 627 villages and murdered their inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The battalion is remembered above all for destroying the village of Khatyn and murdering its 186 civilian residents in March 1943, but Khatyn was only one of the villages it destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its men burned houses and people in villages such as Chmelevichi and Zarech'e, and at Osovy they gathered the men in the local school and burned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet Belarusian courts tried former members after the war, and Canadian court records from the case against Volodymyr Katriuk corroborate much of their testimony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Per Anders Rudling, “Terror and Local Collaboration in Occupied Belarus: The Case of the Schutzmannschaft Battalion 118. I. Background,” <i>Historical Yearbook</i> 8 (2011): 195–214, https://www.historicalyearbook.ro/wp-content/volumes/hy_2011/14-rudling.pdf. [Open access]","Per Anders Rudling, “Terror and Local Collaboration in Occupied Belarus: The Case of the Schutzmannschaft Battalion 118. II. War Criminality,” <i>Historical Yearbook</i> 9 (2012): 99–121, https://historicalyearbook.ro/wp-content/volumes/hy_2012/09-rudling.pdf. [Open access]","Jeff Rutherford, review of <i>The Waffen-SS: A European History</i>, ed. Jochen Böhler and Robert Gerwarth, H-German, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50223. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Collaboration,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/collaboration. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Lisa Kirschenbaum, review of <i>Ghosts of War: Nazi Occupation and Its Aftermath in Soviet Belarus</i>, by Franziska Exeler, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, September 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=60637. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans come to rely on local police auxiliaries?","a":"Because the failure to defeat the Soviet Union quickly left them short of men, and racial principle gave way to need as the partisan threat grew in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reorganised local units became reliable auxiliaries of the German civil, military and police authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were whole villages burned in Belarus?","a":"Because German policy against the partisans made little distinction between partisans, Jews and Roma and punished entire communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burned villages were treated as partisan villages and were not to be rebuilt, and anyone found in them was to be shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Khatyn remembered when so many other villages are not?","a":"Because Khatyn became a symbol of Belarusian and Soviet wartime suffering and an integral part of post-Soviet historical culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court records show that Battalion 118 destroyed several other villages, among them Chmelevichi, Zarech'e and Osovy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Schutzmannschaft","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_121-1500%2C_Ukraine%2C_Ordnungspolizei%2C_Rayonposten_Sarig.jpg","title":"A Ukrainian Schutzmannschaft post, 1942","desc":"German police officers and local auxiliaries outside the Ukrainian Schutzmannschaft district post at Sarig in December 1942.","credit":"Scherer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 121-1500, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_121-1500,_Ukraine,_Ordnungspolizei,_Rayonposten_Sarig.jpg","alt":"Four men in winter uniforms and a dog by a wooden fence under a sign in German and Ukrainian reading Ukr. Schutzmannschaft."}},
    "ww2-687": {"questions":["Only about 2,000 men fought their way out of the encirclement near Cherkassy, and the remnant of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later retreated from Budapest to the American lines in Austria.","Norwegian volunteers serving in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to swear their oath to Adolf Hitler rather than to Norway, which angered some of them, and many died on the Eastern Front."],"abstract":"The <b>SS Division Wiking</b> was a Waffen-SS division formed in 1941 that placed volunteers from northern and western Europe beside German soldiers, and it fought on the Eastern Front from the first days of the invasion of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Waffen-SS, the armed SS that Himmler persuaded Hitler to allow in late 1939, grew from four divisions to more than twenty and about half 1 million men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal put it at about 580,000 men in 40 divisions by the end of the war, reckoned that about a third of its recruits were conscripts, and declared the SS, its armed branch included, a criminal organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Believing that northern Europeans had especially pure blood, the SS recruited nearly fifty thousand Germanic volunteers from the Netherlands, Flanders, Denmark, Norway, Sweden and elsewhere as building blocks of a future Germanic Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As losses mounted after 1941 the racial ideal gave way to need, and the SS went on to raise Baltic, Ukrainian, Bosnian Muslim and other units, many of them by conscription.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Wiking was assembled at Heuberg in Württemberg in February and March 1941 from the regiments Nordland, Westland, with its Dutch volunteers, and Germania, some 19,375 men in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It entered the war 3 days after the invasion began, reached Rostov in 1941, drove towards the oil fields of the Caucasus in 1942 and fought at Kursk in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Encircled near Cherkassy, only about 2,000 of its men fought their way out, and at the end of the war the remnant retreated from Budapest to the American lines in Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of some 3,600 Norwegians who reached the front, more than 700 were killed, while about 7,000 Danes are thought to have enlisted for German war service and some 3,300 were imprisoned after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians now agree that all the foreign units were involved in war crimes, from front-line divisions such as Wiking to security formations in the East, and many Nordic volunteers fought out of conviction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Torgeir Ekerholt Sæveraas, Tore Dyrhaug and Magne Skodvin, “Frontkjempere,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/frontkjempere. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Jeff Rutherford, review of <i>The Waffen-SS: A European History</i>, ed. Jochen Böhler and Robert Gerwarth, H-German, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50223. [Open access]","Richard Byers, review of <i>Building a Nazi Europe: The SS’s Germanic Volunteers</i>, by Martin R. Gutmann, H-War, H-Net Reviews, August 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52066. [Open access]","Aage Trommer, “Frikorps Danmark,” in <i>Den Store Danske</i>, Lex, https://lex.dk/Frikorps_Danmark. [in Danish] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Waffen SS,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/waffen-ss. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Accused Organisations,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judorg.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the SS want Dutch and Scandinavian recruits?","a":"Because SS ideology held that men from northern Europe had a particularly pure blood lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Himmler also hoped to centralise power in a Greater German Reich, and the Germanic volunteers were meant to be its building blocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did men from neutral countries volunteer for the SS?","a":"Because many shared the SS's anti-democratic vision of a Germanic Europe and saw National Socialism as a third way between liberal democracy and Stalinist communism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most were not outcasts but educated men, some with good records in their own armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How were the volunteers treated after the war?","a":"Norwegian front fighters were convicted as traitors, usually with prison and the loss of civil rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Denmark about 3,300 were jailed for 2 to 4 years, and across Europe courts punished the act of collaboration more than the crimes committed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"5th SS Panzer Division Wiking","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101III-Altstadt-055-12%2C_Russland%2C_SS-Division_Wiking_beim_Vormarsch.jpg","title":"SS Division Wiking advancing, June 1942","desc":"Infantry and a tank of the SS Division Wiking advance across the steppe in southern Russia in June 1942.","credit":"Altstadt, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101III-Altstadt-055-12, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101III-Altstadt-055-12,_Russland,_SS-Division_Wiking_beim_Vormarsch.jpg","alt":"Soldiers walking through tall grass on open flat land beside a tank under a pale sky."}},
    "ww2-688": {"questions":["Draža Mihailović's Chetniks followed a cautious strategy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in occupied Serbia, gathering their forces in expectation of an Allied landing in the Balkans.","Historians warn that speaking of a France of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without dating it is meaningless, since opinion under the occupation shifted as the war went on."],"abstract":"<b>Attentisme</b>, from the French for ‘waiting’, is the wait-and-see stance that much of the population of occupied Europe is said to have taken, keeping clear of both resistance and collaboration until the outcome of the war was plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In France the popular account holds that most people waited cautiously, somewhere between support for Vichy and accommodation with the occupier, until the turn of the war at the end of 1942 drew them towards the likely victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That account owes much to the film <i>Le Chagrin et la Pitié</i>, a documentary about the resistance that audiences received as a film about attentisme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians meeting at Besançon in 2012 set beside the word a family of finer terms, among them accommodation, constrained adaptation, avoidance, double thinking and ambivalence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They warned that speaking of an attentiste France without dates makes no sense, because opinion changed as the occupation went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The occupation pressed these choices on ordinary people, for the occupiers requisitioned resources and labour, and propaganda went to great lengths to hide the food shortages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Studies of everyday life in Vichy France describe a grey zone in which, as one contributor put it, it was not all resistance or all collaboration, and people feared starvation, bombing and violence from both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study of the western Pyrenees found people answering the occupation with collaboration, opportunism, indifference or resistance, often posing as obedient while quietly breaking the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word was used of armed movements too, and in Serbia Draža Mihailović's Chetniks followed a cautious, attentiste strategy, gathering their strength for an Allied landing in the Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Critics of the stereotype note that letters of denunciation in France numbered between 300,000 and 500,000, not millions, and that some 300,000 to 330,000 people held resistance fighters' cards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“Les comportements collectifs en France et dans l’Europe allemande, 1940–1945: Un colloque du CH2R tenu à Besançon, 25–27 septembre 2012,” Association des professeurs d’histoire et de géographie, https://www.aphg.fr/IMG/pdf/120927-comportements-collectifs-1940-1945.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Joëlle Alazard, “Les comportements des populations dans l’Europe occupée par l’Allemagne nazie,” notes on a round table with Antoine Prost, Barbara Lambauer and Claire Andrieu, Rendez-vous de l’histoire de Blois, October 2015, SNES-FSU, https://www.snes.edu/IMG/pdf/populations_ds_europe_occupee_par_l_all_nazie.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Benedetta Carnaghi, review of <i>Vichy France and Everyday Life: Confronting the Challenges of Wartime, 1939–1945</i>, ed. Lindsey Dodd and David Lees, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=53631. [Open access]","John W. Weigel, review of <i>Living with the Enemy: German Occupation, Collaboration and Justice in the Western Pyrenees, 1940–1948</i>, by Sandra Ott, H-War, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50482. [Open access]","Yves Tomic, “Massacres in Dismembered Yugoslavia, 1941–1945,” <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 June 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/massacres-in-dismembered-yugoslavia-1941-1945/. [Open access]","Frédéric Dessberg, “Occupation Regimes and Logic,” <i>Encyclopédie d’histoire numérique de l’Europe</i>, Sorbonne Université, https://ehne.fr/en/encyclopedia/themes/wars-and-memories/occupybe-occupied/occupation-regimes-and-logic. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 19–20, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do historians distrust the picture of a waiting France?","a":"Because it flattens a society whose attitudes changed over 4 years, and talking of an attentiste France without dates makes no sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians have proposed finer words, such as accommodation, avoidance and ambivalence, to describe what people actually did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many ordinary people keep their heads down?","a":"Because daily life was dominated by shortages and fear: people feared starvation, bombing and violence from both the Germans and the resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The occupiers requisitioned resources and men, and survival often meant outward obedience combined with quiet law-breaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Chetniks choose to wait?","a":"Because they planned to gather their forces and rise when the Allies landed in the Balkans, limiting themselves after 1941 mainly to sabotage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mihailović was also appalled by the German execution of Serb hostages in reprisal for guerrilla attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Attentisme","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Novembre_1942_-_%C3%A9coliers_carte_de_rationnement_textile.jpg","title":"Ration points for prisoners, Vichy, 1942","desc":"French schoolchildren hand their teacher a point from their textile ration cards for prisoners of war, November 1942.","credit":"Agence Trampus, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Novembre_1942_-_%C3%A9coliers_carte_de_rationnement_textile.jpg","alt":"Children crowding round a smiling teacher at her desk, holding up papers, with a blackboard behind reading 1 point de textile pour les prisonniers."}},
    "ww2-689": {"questions":["After failing to suppress the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in occupied Europe, the Germans set up a purchasing agency to buy goods for Germany on it, the Nuremberg tribunal found.","City dwellers in occupied Poland had to turn to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to survive, because official rations met perhaps half of their needs and were often not available."],"abstract":"A <b>black market</b> is illegal trade in rationed or controlled goods at prices above the official ones, and in occupied Europe it became an ordinary part of how millions of people found enough to eat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rationing worked only with an efficient administration and public support, and where either was missing, especially in getting food from farmers, a growing share of supplies went to the black market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lower the official rations fell, the stronger the incentive to supply and use illegal markets, and the more readily public opinion condoned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League of Nations judged that rationing held throughout the war in Germany, Czechoslovakia and Denmark, worked less well in Belgium, France and Norway, and broke down altogether in southern and eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In some countries the black market was thought to supply as much as a third of certain goods, and industrial workers too poor to pay its prices bore the heaviest cuts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The occupation itself fed the trade, for the Germans requisitioned goods out of all proportion to the resources of the occupied countries, bringing famine, inflation and an active black market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At first the German authorities tried to suppress it as a channel keeping local products out of their hands, and when that failed they set up a purchasing agency to buy for Germany on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In occupied Poland official rations met perhaps half of needs and were often unavailable, so city dwellers had to buy illegally to survive, while Jews were granted half the Polish ration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the ghettos widespread smuggling of food and medicine supplemented the miserable official allotments and held down the death rate, and prisoners who could turned to smuggling and illegal trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trading could cost a life, and in the western Pyrenees a black-market bicycle dealer was executed after the liberation while others who had worked openly with the Germans went only to prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations, Economic, Financial and Transit Department, <i>Food, Famine and Relief, 1940–1946</i> (Geneva: League of Nations, 1946), 2–3, 9, 39, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.201861. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Frédéric Dessberg, “Occupation Regimes and Logic,” <i>Encyclopédie d’histoire numérique de l’Europe</i>, Sorbonne Université, https://ehne.fr/en/encyclopedia/themes/wars-and-memories/occupybe-occupied/occupation-regimes-and-logic. [Open access]","John W. Weigel, review of <i>Living with the Enemy: German Occupation, Collaboration and Justice in the Western Pyrenees, 1940–1948</i>, by Sandra Ott, H-War, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50482. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Warsaw,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","Anne van Mourik, review of <i>The Atrocity of Hunger: Starvation in the Warsaw, Lodz, and Krakow Ghettos during World War II</i>, by Helene Julia Sinnreich, H-Genocide, H-Net Reviews, April 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=59599. [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 75, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did black markets grow where rations were lowest?","a":"Because as food became scarcer the incentive both to supply it illegally and to buy it rose, while fear of punishment and public disapproval weakened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once rations fell below an adequate diet, black markets were bound to expand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans stop trying to suppress the black market?","a":"Because suppression failed, and the illegal market was keeping local goods out of German hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A German purchasing agency was organised to buy on it instead, in line with Göring's insistence that if there was famine anywhere it would not be in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the poorest suffer most from rationing?","a":"Because farmers could not be strictly rationed and kept their own consumption near normal, while urban rations fell short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Industrial workers were too poor to pay black-market prices, so the greatest cuts fell on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Black market"},
    "ww2-690": {"questions":["Norwegian women accused of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with German soldiers were often interned in 1945, and those who married Germans lost their citizenship under a law that applied only to women.","Spontaneous crowds rather than any national plan carried out most punishments for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in liberated France, and at Monterfil women were killed after being shorn in August 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Horizontal collaboration</b> was the contemptuous name for sexual or romantic relationships between women of the occupied countries and German soldiers, which many of their compatriots treated as a betrayal of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Norwegians called such women tyskertøser, and estimates of how many young Norwegian women went out with a German soldier range from 30,000 to 120,000, between one in twelve and one in four of those aged fifteen to thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their relationships, not necessarily sexual, produced between 10,000 and 12,000 children in Norway, and many of the women suffered harassment and ostracism during the war itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Women's experiences of occupation varied widely: in France some forged real ties with colonial prisoners of war, while women in Wehrmacht brothels found their bodies made one more resource at the Germans' disposal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those later accused included women who had truly collaborated, women in love, prostitutes and women who had to work for the occupier, and a court in Pau heard of a shopkeeper who had found women for an SS officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> At the liberation an estimated 20,000 French women accused of horizontal collaboration had their heads shaved in public, though the figure is only an estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shearing women was a Europe-wide practice that had begun in the First World War, and it helped men humiliated by occupation to reassert their masculinity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Women were seized, paraded before a crowd, shaved and sometimes daubed with swastikas, and in more than two-thirds of cases the acts they were accused of rested on no verified facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most shavings were the work of spontaneous crowds rather than any national plan, though legal authorities were sometimes present, and at Monterfil in Brittany women were killed after being shorn on 4 August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Norway a relationship with a German was no crime, but many women were interned in 1945, and those who married Germans lost their citizenship and were deported to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Torgeir Ekerholt Sæveraas, “Tyskerjenter,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/tyskerjenter. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Assemblée nationale, “Pardon aux « femmes tondues »,” written question no. 26059 by Jean-Luc Lagleize, <i>Journal officiel</i>, 28 January 2020, and government answer, 24 March 2020, https://www.assemblee-nationale.fr/dyn/15/questions/QANR5L15QE26059.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Mouloud Boukala, review of <i>Femmes tondues, France-Libération: Coupables, amoureuses, victimes</i>, by Julie Desmarais, <i>Anthropologie et Sociétés</i> 35 (2011), https://www.anthropologie-societes.ant.ulaval.ca/sites/anthropologie-societes.ant.ulaval.ca/files/comptes-rendus/vol_35_-_desmarais_2009_-_boukala_-_cr.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Claire Miot, “Liberating and Being Liberated in Nineteenth and Twentieth-Century Europe,” <i>Encyclopédie d’histoire numérique de l’Europe</i>, Sorbonne Université, https://ehne.fr/en/encyclopedia/themes/wars-and-memories/occupybe-occupied/liberating-and-being-liberated-in-nineteenth-and-twentieth-century-europe. [Open access]","Benedetta Carnaghi, review of <i>Vichy France and Everyday Life: Confronting the Challenges of Wartime, 1939–1945</i>, ed. Lindsey Dodd and David Lees, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=53631. [Open access]","John W. Weigel, review of <i>Living with the Enemy: German Occupation, Collaboration and Justice in the Western Pyrenees, 1940–1948</i>, by Sandra Ott, H-War, H-Net Reviews, March 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50482. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the punishment target women's hair?","a":"Because the shaving was a gendered punishment aimed at a symbol of femininity, staged as a purification of the signs of the German presence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also let men humiliated by the occupation reassert themselves after liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has France declined to apologise to the shorn women?","a":"Because, the government answered in 2020, the situations were too diverse for a single explanation, and the shavings were mainly the work of spontaneous crowds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some legal authorities took part, but there was no national government plan to inflict the punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Norwegian wives of Germans lose their citizenship?","a":"Because a government decree changed the citizenship law so that a woman lost it the moment she married a citizen of an enemy state, and the change was made retroactive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It applied only to women: the 28 Norwegian men who married German women faced no such penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Horizontal collaboration","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/This_girl_pays_the_penalty_for_having_had_personal_relations_with_the_Germans._Here%2C_in_the_Montelimar_area%2C_France..._-_NARA_-_531211.jpg","title":"A woman shorn near Montélimar, August 1944","desc":"French civilians shave the head of a young woman accused of relations with Germans in the Montélimar area in August 1944.","credit":"Smith, US Army photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:This_girl_pays_the_penalty_for_having_had_personal_relations_with_the_Germans._Here,_in_the_Montelimar_area,_France..._-_NARA_-_531211.jpg","alt":"A young woman in a flowered dress sitting on a bench while two men cut her hair, onlookers standing behind."}},
    "ww2-691": {"questions":["Women in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw a venereal-disease doctor several times a week, because the army's purpose in regulating them was to keep its soldiers fit to fight.","Soviet propaganda pictured every woman in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the occupied east as dragged there by force, yet some used them to survive and neighbours called it betrayal."],"abstract":"<b>Wehrmacht brothels</b> were the brothels set up and regulated for German servicemen in the Reich and across occupied Europe and one part of the sexual exploitation that came with the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Legal brothels had opened in Nazi Germany for officers in 1934 and for soldiers in 1936, and on 9 September 1939 the interior minister Wilhelm Frick decreed that they be organised throughout the Reich for the men going to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aim was to keep soldiers fit to fight, so the women saw a venereal-disease doctor several times a week, while every visitor was checked by a unit doctor and entered in a register.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In occupied France the early years were a time when German troops enjoyed tourism, shopping and extracting sexual favours from French women, and the brothels were places of leisure for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Men and women remembered the houses very differently, the soldiers keeping a rather positive memory while the women found their bodies turned into yet another French resource placed at the Germans' disposal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the occupied Soviet Union, permanent brothels for the troops were organised by the security police and the SD from 1942, once the war in the east was plainly going to be long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet wartime propaganda pictured every woman in these houses as a victim dragged there by force, but commission files and partisan reports show that some women used their bodies to survive and received material benefits in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their neighbours did not see legal prostitution in the terms of the propaganda, and treated it as betrayal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The brothels existed beside rape, which a recent collection on mass violence in occupied Europe counts among the secondary crimes, with looting and the seizure of property, that vast numbers of German soldiers in the east committed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Women were not recognised as victims at the Soviet public trials or at Nuremberg and received no compensation, and even a careful 2018 study of occupied Russia mentions the brothels only in passing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Nadezhda S. Nizhnik, “Prinuzhdenie k prostitutsii – prestuplenie natsistov protiv mirnogo naseleniya okkupirovannykh territoriy v gody Velikoy Otechestvennoy voyny,” <i>Vestnik Ural’skogo yuridicheskogo instituta MVD Rossii</i>, no. 2 (2025): 32–41, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/prinuzhdenie-k-prostitutsii-prestuplenie-natsistov-protiv-mirnogo-naseleniya-okkupirovannyh-territoriy-v-gody-velikoy. [in Russian] [Open access]","Ksenia Sak, “Women’s Daily Life in the Occupation: Sexual Violence in Documents and Soviet Propaganda,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 601–16, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2024.2.898. [Open access]","Benedetta Carnaghi, review of <i>Vichy France and Everyday Life: Confronting the Challenges of Wartime, 1939–1945</i>, ed. Lindsey Dodd and David Lees, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=53631. [Open access]","Maris Rowe-McCulloch, review of <i>Soviet Russians under Nazi Occupation: Fragile Loyalties in World War II</i>, by Johannes D. Enstad, H-Russia, H-Net Reviews, July 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=53673. [Open access]","Mark Montesclaros, review of <i>Mass Violence in Nazi-Occupied Europe</i>, ed. Alex J. Kay and David Stahel, H-War, H-Net Reviews, August 2019, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=53625. [Open access]","Perry Biddiscombe, review of <i>German Soldiers and the Occupation of France, 1940–1944</i>, by Julia S. Torrie, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2022, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=54180. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the German army regulate brothels rather than ban them?","a":"Because its concern was the fighting strength of its men, so it controlled venereal disease with compulsory medical checks for the women and a register of every visitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Soviet propaganda an unreliable guide to these houses?","a":"Because it showed every woman as a victim taken by force, while files of the investigating commission and partisan reports show some women using their bodies to survive in return for material benefits, and neighbours who called it betrayal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the women go uncompensated after the war?","a":"Because neither the Soviet public trials nor the Nuremberg tribunal recognised them as victims, and once the war ended the subject lost its use for propaganda and dropped out of memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101II-MW-1019-03%2C_Frankreich%2C_Brest%2C_Soldatenbordell.jpg","title":"Soldiers' brothel in Brest, 1940","desc":"German servicemen leave a brothel in occupied Brest whose door notice sets its opening hours for soldiers.","credit":"Dietrich, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101II-MW-1019-03, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101II-MW-1019-03,_Frankreich,_Brest,_Soldatenbordell.jpg","alt":"Two German soldiers at the doorway of a building whose wall is painted with a notice giving opening hours from 10 to 21 o'clock."},"wiki":"German military brothels in World War II"},
    "ww2-692": {"questions":["Protestant villagers around Le Chambon-sur-Lignon forged ration cards for many of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> they sheltered between 1940 and 1944, guiding some across the border to Switzerland.","After liberation, rescuers in hundreds of cases refused to hand back the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to their families, and in the Netherlands a state committee became guardian of most surviving orphans."],"abstract":"The <b>hidden children</b> were the Jewish children who survived the Holocaust in German-occupied Europe by being concealed, either physically or behind a false identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their survival stands against the scale of the killing, in which as many as 1.5 million Jewish children were murdered or died at the hands of the Nazis and their collaborators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Children were not singled out because they were children but because of the group they were said to belong to, and with the elderly they had the lowest chance of surviving the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The great majority of Jews never went into hiding, because it meant leaving relatives behind, risking savage punishment and finding someone willing to take them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The occupiers and their collaborators punished rescuers harshly and paid informers, and in Germany from March 1943 the Gestapo used Jewish informers who betrayed as many as 2,000 people in hiding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Children who could pass as non-Jews lived openly on forged papers, while those who could not were kept in cellars, attics, barns and chicken coops, silent for hours on end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In France the Children's Aid Society and other Jewish groups hid children in their homes and smuggled some to Switzerland, while convents in Poland, Belgian Catholics and Muslim families in Albania and Yugoslavia took others in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the plateau around Le Chambon-sur-Lignon, Protestant villagers sheltered about 3,000 to 3,500 Jews between December 1940 and September 1944, forging identity and ration cards and guiding some to Switzerland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hiding could fail through a slip of the tongue, a bad document or a quarrel, as when two Dutch sisters aged eight and six were denounced in 1944 after a quarrel between their rescuers and were murdered at Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Liberation brought its own trials, for in hundreds of cases rescuers would not give children back, and in the Netherlands more than half of the 4,000 to 6,000 surviving Jewish children were made wards of a state committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Children during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/children-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","“Life in Shadows: Hidden Children and the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/life-in-shadows-hidden-children-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]","“Hidden Children: Hardships,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hidden-children-hardships. [Open access]","“Hidden Children: Daily Life,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hidden-children-daily-life. [Open access]","“Hidden Children: Quest for Family,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hidden-children-quest-for-family. [Open access]","“Le Chambon-sur-Lignon,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/le-chambon-sur-lignon. [Open access]","“Jewish Aid and Rescue,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-aid-and-rescue. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so few Jewish families send their children into hiding?","a":"Because hiding meant parting from relatives, finding a rescuer willing to take the risk and facing severe punishment if caught, and many hoped the danger would pass before it came to that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were some children hidden in cellars rather than living openly?","a":"Because only a child who could pass as a non-Jew, speak the local language and carry convincing false papers could live in the open; the rest had to be physically concealed from neighbours and police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For them reading, play and creative work had to fill seemingly endless hours of pain and boredom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the end of the war not end the children's ordeal?","a":"Because many had no surviving family, some rescuers refused to give them up, and infants placed in hiding remembered no other parents, so reclaiming them could mean yet another painful separation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Hidden children during the Holocaust","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Achterhuis_-_boekenkast.jpg","title":"The bookcase hiding the Secret Annex","desc":"A reconstruction of the movable bookcase that hid the entrance to the Amsterdam annex where Anne Frank and her family lived in hiding from 1942 to 1944.","credit":"Ymnes, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achterhuis_-_boekenkast.jpg","alt":"A wooden bookcase filled with box files standing in a narrow landing beside a doorway, under a map on a patterned wall."}},
    "ww2-693": {"questions":["Hundreds of secondary-school pupils were among those shot in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the local German commandant had warned would have a catastrophic effect on a loyal town.","A memorial park opened on the site in 1953 commemorates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the German reprisal policy for occupied Serbia reached its most notorious single killing."],"abstract":"The <b>Kragujevac massacre</b> was the shooting of thousands of people from the Serbian town of Kragujevac and its villages by German troops on 19–21 October 1941, the best-known of the reprisal killings of occupied Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It followed a rising in the summer of 1941 led by communist Partisans and Serb nationalist Chetniks, which cost the German forces serious casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 16 September an order of the German high command declared that a human life counted for practically nothing in such countries and that 50 to 100 Communists should generally die for each German soldier killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Serbia the ratio became fixed at 100 civilians shot for every German killed and 50 for every German wounded, and it was applied in hundreds of reprisals that autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The order served as a pretext to shoot virtually all the male Jews of Serbia, about 8,000, together with thousands of Communists, nationalists and Roma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Kragujevac the occasion was the loss of about ten Germans killed and more than twenty wounded in fighting with insurgents on the road to Gornji Milanovac in mid-October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Troops arrested male Jews and Communists on 18 October, shot more than 400 men in nearby villages the next day, and on 20 October rounded up men aged 16 to 60 from the streets, squares, shops and homes of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The local German commandant warned in writing that shooting partly or entirely innocent townspeople would have a catastrophic effect, but the executions went ahead on the morning of 21 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German report gave 2,300 hostages shot in Kragujevac, while the memorial today counts 2,794 dead over 3 days, among them 261 secondary-school pupils, 40 children, at least 200 Roma and 39 Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the Hostages Trial in Nuremberg in February 1948 the judges held that hostages might be shot only as a last resort under strict conditions, and sentenced Field Marshal List to life imprisonment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 766, 803, 980–83, 1249–50, 1317–19, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]","Luke Gramith, review of <i>Serbia under the Swastika: A World War II Occupation</i>, by Alexander Victor Prusin, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52012. [Open access]","“Kragujevačka tragedija,” Spomen-park Kragujevački oktobar, https://www.spomenpark.rs/kragujevacka-tragedija/. [in Serbian] [Open access]","“Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","Thomas Jander, “Partisanenkrieg in Jugoslawien,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/partisanenkrieg-in-jugoslawien. [in German] [Open access]","Spomen-park Kragujevački oktobar, home page, https://www.spomenpark.rs/. [in Serbian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans set a fixed ratio of deaths?","a":"Because the high command believed that only unusual severity would deter a centrally directed uprising, and it ordered from the start that dozens of lives be taken for each German soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Serbia's Jews among the first shot?","a":"Because the occupiers used the reprisal order as a pretext, and the Holocaust in Serbia unfolded inside the anti-guerrilla shootings, with collaborators offering Jews as the first hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some German officers object to the killings?","a":"Because, as the commandant at Kragujevac wrote, shooting loyal townspeople would drive their relatives to revenge and would lose the Germans the people who had welcomed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kragujevac massacre","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/German_Soldiers_arresting_in_1941_people_in_Kragujevac.jpg","title":"Men led to execution, Kragujevac, 1941","desc":"German soldiers escort men from Kragujevac and the surrounding villages to be shot in October 1941.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, courtesy of the Museum of the Revolution of the Peoples of Yugoslavia). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Soldiers_arresting_in_1941_people_in_Kragujevac.jpg","alt":"A column of civilian men in coats and caps walking along a street, escorted by armed men, several of them in German uniform."}},
    "ww2-694": {"questions":["Amnesties freed the Alsatian conscripts convicted at Bordeaux in 1953 for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, opening a rift between the village and Paris that lasted for decades.","A German inquiry closed in January 1945 declared the reprisals of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> justified for military reasons, although no resistance fighters were in the village that day."],"abstract":"The <b>Oradour-sur-Glane massacre</b> was the killing of 642 or 643 people, almost the whole population of a village near Limoges, by a company of the Waffen-SS division Das Reich on 10 June 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> German repression in France had already brought the shooting of hostages, which passed to the SS and police command in May 1942, but it never reached the scale of the killing in Serbia, Greece or the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Allied landings of 6 June the resistance struck harder, and the division, sent north towards Normandy from Montauban, was first ordered to crush it around Tulle and Limoges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its commander, Heinz Lammerding, had served as chief of staff to the head of anti-partisan warfare in the occupied Soviet Union, and on 9 June his men hanged 99 men in Tulle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Oradour about 150 soldiers rounded up the inhabitants and refugees, shot the men in barns and sheds, killed the women and children in the burning church, and then looted and burned the village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Oradour lay in no maquis zone, and the village's memorial centre calls the massacre a methodical act of terror against an unarmed population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That evening the officers ordered silence and spread the story that the resistance was to blame, and a German war diary of 14 June claimed that explosives stored in the church had killed the women and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A German military inquiry closed in January 1945 with the finding that the reprisals appeared justified for military reasons, a phrase the French prosecutor read out at Nuremberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who gave the order is still disputed, and at Bordeaux in 1953 only 21 men, no officer among them, were tried, most of them Alsatians forcibly drafted into the SS who were amnestied within days of the verdict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ruins were classified as a historic monument in 1946 and kept as they stood, and Oradour became France's national symbol of suffering under the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“Oradour-sur-Glane,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/oradour-sur-glane. [Open access]","Andrea Erkenbrecher, “Oradour-sur-Glane 1944,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/oradour-sur-glane-1944. [in German] [Open access]","“Le récit du massacre d’Oradour-sur-Glane,” Centre de la mémoire d’Oradour, Département de la Haute-Vienne, https://www.visitlimousin.com/centre-de-la-memoire-doradour-sur-glane/le-centre-de-la-memoire-doradour/le-recit-du-massacre-doradour-sur-glane/. [in French] [Open access]","“L’histoire du village martyr d’Oradour-sur-Glane,” Centre de la mémoire d’Oradour, Département de la Haute-Vienne, https://www.visitlimousin.com/centre-de-la-memoire-doradour-sur-glane/le-centre-de-la-memoire-doradour/lhistoire-du-village-martyr-doradour-sur-glane/. [in French] [Open access]","“Téléfilm Oradour sur TF1 : la fiction n’est pas l’histoire,” Centre de la mémoire d’Oradour, Département de la Haute-Vienne, https://www.visitlimousin.com/centre-de-la-memoire-doradour-sur-glane/actualites-du-centre-de-la-memoire-doradour-sur-glane/telefilm-oradour-sur-tf1-au-dela-de-la-fiction-decouvrez-la-veritable-histoire-et-visitez-le-site-de-memoire/. [in French] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, proceedings of 31 January 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-31-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Besatzungsregime in Frankreich,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a German phrase from 1945 quoted in the record of Nuremberg?","a":"Because the army's own inquiry ended by finding the reprisals justified for military reasons, and the French prosecutor read it out to show that the German army regarded the crime as fully justified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Bordeaux verdicts cause anger in Oradour?","a":"Because the sentences were thought too mild, and days later parliament amnestied the convicted Alsatians, who had been forcibly drafted into the SS, in the name of national unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the ruins still standing?","a":"Because the state took them over in 1946, listed them as a historic monument and has preserved them as they were left, while a new village was built beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Oradour-sur-Glane massacre","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Oradour-sur-Glane-Streets-1290.jpg","title":"The ruined main street of Oradour","desc":"The main street of Oradour-sur-Glane near the church, its burned houses preserved as they were left in June 1944.","credit":"Dennis Nilsson, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oradour-sur-Glane-Streets-1290.jpg","alt":"A street lined with roofless stone house ruins under a large tree, with a low wall in the foreground and tram wires overhead."}},
    "ww2-695": {"questions":["Because of a clerical error, 335 men died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than the 330 demanded by the German order of ten Italians for each policeman killed.","Herbert Kappler received life imprisonment in 1948 for organising the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but escaped in 1977 from a military hospital in Rome to West Germany."],"abstract":"The <b>Ardeatine massacre</b> was the shooting of 335 men in caves on the Via Ardeatina outside Rome on 24 March 1944, ordered by the German command as a reprisal for a partisan attack the day before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 23 March a bomb laid by a partisan unit of the Rome GAP in Via Rasella killed 32 or 33 men of a German police unit marching through the city, and two civilians died too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The order came from Hitler's headquarters, through Field Marshal Kesselring, to shoot ten Italians for every German killed within 24 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The task passed to Lieutenant-Colonel Herbert Kappler, head of the SD, the German security service, in Rome, who drew up lists from the prisoners of Via Tasso and Regina Coeli and took others from the Italian police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only four of the people chosen had anything to do with the bomb, and the dead included a boy of fourteen, a man of seventy, partisans, officers, carabinieri and dozens of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They were driven to a tufa quarry, made to kneel in groups of five and shot in the back of the head, and when it was over engineers blew up the entrance to hide the bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A clerical error in the lists meant that 335 were killed instead of the 330 the ratio demanded, and the bodies could only be dug up and identified after Rome was liberated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> British military courts sentenced Generals von Mackensen and Maelzer and then Kesselring to death for the killing, but every sentence was commuted to life imprisonment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An Italian military court gave Kappler life in 1948 and acquitted his subordinates for obeying orders, and in 1977 he escaped from a military hospital in Rome to West Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His captain Erich Priebke, found in Argentina in 1994 and extradited, was finally sentenced to life in 1998, and the hunt for evidence against him uncovered hundreds of improperly shelved war-crimes files.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Amedeo Osti Guerrazzi, “Fosse Ardeatine, Roma, 24.03.1944,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&id_strage=2127. [in Italian] [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 8 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1949), 1–2, 9–10, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-8/Law-Reports_Vol-8.pdf. [Open access]","Gabriele Maselli, “Ardeatine, Fosse,” in <i>Enciclopedia Italiana</i>, II Appendice (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 1948), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/fosse-ardeatine_%28Enciclopedia-Italiana%29/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Kappler, Herbert,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/herbert-kappler/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Tribunali militari,” Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, Istituto nazionale per la storia del movimento di liberazione in Italia and Associazione nazionale partigiani d’Italia, https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=137. [in Italian] [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 784, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]","“Priebke, Erich,” in <i>Enciclopedie on line</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/erich-priebke/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were people with no link to the bomb killed?","a":"Because the order demanded ten Italians for each German within a day, there were not enough prisoners under sentence of death, and the security police filled the number with political prisoners, Jews and people the Italian police handed over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the generals escape execution?","a":"Because the confirming authorities commuted the death sentences passed on von Mackensen, Maelzer and Kesselring to life imprisonment, a decision taken amid much criticism of the Kesselring verdict in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Italian trials for the massacre resume in the 1990s?","a":"Because the search for evidence against Priebke in 1994 turned up 695 investigation files on wartime massacres that had been improperly shelved in 1960, which opened a second season of trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ardeatine massacre","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-312-0983-03%2C_Rom%2C_Festnahme_von_Zivilisten.jpg","title":"Round-up after Via Rasella, March 1944","desc":"German and Italian soldiers hold civilians against the railings of Palazzo Barberini after the partisan attack in Via Rasella on 23 March 1944.","credit":"Koch, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-312-0983-03, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-312-0983-03,_Rom,_Festnahme_von_Zivilisten.jpg","alt":"A long line of men with their hands raised stand facing the railings of a building while helmeted soldiers with rifles guard them on the street."}},
    "ww2-696": {"questions":["Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski became Himmler's plenipotentiary for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a campaign in which surrounded villages were screened for forced labourers and the rest left to the commander.","A German official in Minsk counted 4,500 enemy dead but only 492 rifles taken in one operation of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1943, and concluded that many of the dead were peasants."],"abstract":"<b>Bandenbekämpfung</b>, ‘the fight against bands’, was the name Nazi Germany gave to its war against partisans in occupied Europe, above all in the Soviet Union and Yugoslavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the summer of 1942 the word 'partisan' was no longer to be used, for psychological reasons, and German orders spoke instead of bands and bandits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Partisans in the occupied Soviet Union grew from about 30,000 in January 1942 to about 200,000 by the summer of 1943, and they did serious damage to German railways and supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler's directives called for their complete extermination, and Himmler made the SS and police general Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski his plenipotentiary for the fight against bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bach-Zelewski told the Nuremberg tribunal that the operations were carried out mainly by Wehrmacht formations, with SS and police units and convicted criminals of the Dirlewanger unit among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A typical operation surrounded a village, screened its people, picked out those fit for forced labour and left the fate of the rest to the commander on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Captured fighters had little hope, for in November 1942 one panzer army ordered that every bandit, uniformed or not, be shot unless he had surrendered of his own free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Civilians were caught between partisans who used violence to get supplies and a German strategy of burning whole villages to the ground, and one German report of 1943 counted 4,500 enemy dead but only 492 rifles taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German success reports for 1943 and 1944 listed about 150,000 bandits killed, while the true toll of the partisan war ran into hundreds of thousands of partisans, civilians and soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The methods travelled west with officers who had learned them in the east, and in Yugoslavia the civilian toll rose sharply after the SS took over the hunt for bands in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Thomas Jander, “Partisanenkrieg im Osten,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/partisanenkrieg-im-osten. [in German] [Open access]","Thomas Jander, “Partisanenkrieg in Jugoslawien,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/partisanenkrieg-in-jugoslawien. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, proceedings of 7 January 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 88–89, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]","Lisa Kirschenbaum, review of <i>Ghosts of War: Nazi Occupation and Its Aftermath in Soviet Belarus</i>, by Franziska Exeler, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, September 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=60637. [Open access]","“Oradour-sur-Glane,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/oradour-sur-glane. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans stop calling their enemies partisans?","a":"Because from the summer of 1942 the word was banned for psychological reasons, and calling armed opponents bandits denied them the standing of soldiers, so even uniformed fighters could be shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many civilians die in these operations?","a":"Because Bach-Zelewski admitted there were no clear rules on who counted as a partisan, lower commanders acted on their own, and an order barred courts from punishing soldiers for crimes against civilians in partisan areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the terror fail to end the partisan war?","a":"Because brutality drove more people to the partisans rather than deterring them, and in Yugoslavia it deepened hatred of the occupiers instead of frightening the population into obedience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bandenbekämpfung","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101III-Niquille-067-24%2C_Russland%2C_Festnahme_von_Partisanen.jpg","title":"Waffen-SS on a partisan sweep, 1943","desc":"Waffen-SS soldiers searching for partisans in the central Soviet Union in July 1943 come upon a small boy.","credit":"Niquille, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101III-Niquille-067-24, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101III-Niquille-067-24,_Russland,_Festnahme_von_Partisanen.jpg","alt":"Four soldiers in camouflage smocks and helmets, two with submachine guns, stand around a small boy sitting in long grass at the edge of a wood."}},
    "ww2-697": {"questions":["Race commissions sorted the settlers who arrived through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into those fit for the annexed east, those to stay in the old Reich, and special cases.","Hitler's Reichstag speech of 6 October 1939 promised to bring untenable splinters of the German nation abroad back under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a programme he entrusted to Himmler."],"abstract":"<b>Heim ins Reich</b>, ‘home into the Reich’, was the slogan under which Nazi Germany moved ethnic Germans from eastern and south-eastern Europe into conquered Polish land from 1939, emptying it of Poles and Jews to make room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a Reichstag speech of 6 October 1939 Hitler announced a new ordering of ethnic relations, in which untenable splinters of the German nation abroad would be resettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He put Himmler in charge as Reich Commissioner for the Strengthening of German Nationhood, working with the party's ethnic German liaison office and a central immigration office of the Reich Security Main Office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A confidential protocol to the German-Soviet treaty of 28 September 1939 bound Moscow not to obstruct persons of German descent who wished to leave its new territories, with the same promise for Ukrainians and Belarusians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The newcomers were meant to settle the annexed districts of Wartheland and Danzig-West Prussia, which Hitler wanted wholly Germanised within 10 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> More than 100,000 people left Volhynia and eastern Galicia by February 1940, about nine in ten Baltic Germans had gone by that spring, and some 187,000 followed from Bukovina and Bessarabia late in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On arrival race commissions sorted them, sending those rated most valuable to settle the annexed east, keeping others in the old Reich and marking a few thousand for the General Government or return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To make room, the occupiers murdered, deported or drove hundreds of thousands of Jews and Poles judged unfit for Germanisation into the General Government from November 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Estimates of the settlers vary, from more than 300,000 by 1941 in one account to more than 500,000 in another, but far too few came, and the expulsions around Zamość in 1942 and 1943 settled only about 9,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A recent history of population transfer counts these resettlements among the transfers that reshaped 20th-century Europe, and after 1945 more than two thirds of the Bukovina and Bessarabia settlers fled west again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Kolonisierung und Vertreibung in Polen,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kolonisierung-und-vertreibung-in-polen. [in German] [Open access]","Dorlis Blume, “Umsiedlung aus der Bukowina,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 11 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/aussenpolitik/umsiedlung-aus-der-bukowina. [in German] [Open access]","“German-Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty: Confidential Protocol,” 28 September 1939, in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939–1941</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/conproto.asp. [Open access]","“Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","Thomas Kühne, review of <i>Making Minorities History: Population Transfer in Twentieth-Century Europe</i>, by Matthew Frank, H-Nationalism, H-Net Reviews, April 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52226. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Besatzungsregime in Polen,” LeMO, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-polen. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the resettlement require the expulsion of Poles?","a":"Because the incoming Germans were to settle the annexed districts, and the occupiers made room for them by murdering, deporting or driving out the Jewish and Polish people who lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union agree to let these Germans go?","a":"Because a confidential protocol of September 1939 made the exchange mutual: Moscow let persons of German descent leave, and Germany did the same for Ukrainians and Belarusians in its sphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the settlement scheme fall short of its aims?","a":"Because there were far too few ethnic Germans to fill the emptied districts, few Germans of the old Reich wanted to move east, and at Zamość Polish resistance helped halt the expulsions in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Heim ins Reich","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Bundesarchiv_Bild_137-051846%2C_Polen%2C_Umsiedler%2C_Pferdewagentreck.jpg","title":"Resettlers crossing the San, 1940","desc":"A horse-drawn wagon train of ethnic German resettlers from eastern Poland crosses the bridge over the San at Przemyśl in 1940.","credit":"Boris Spahn, Bundesarchiv, Bild 137-051846, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_137-051846,_Polen,_Umsiedler,_Pferdewagentreck.jpg","alt":"Covered horse-drawn wagons and men in long coats on a snowy iron girder bridge."}},
    "ww2-698": {"questions":["Franco's decree of 12 June 1940 replaced Spanish neutrality with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, citing the spread of the war to the Mediterranean after Italy's entry into it.","An American ambassador wrote in October 1943 that Spain's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was politically dead, after Franco publicly described his country's position as neutrality for the first time."],"abstract":"<b>Non-belligerence</b> was the position claimed by several states early in the Second World War, between being at war and being neutral: they stayed out of the fighting but did not treat both sides alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neutrality had a settled meaning in law, for the Hague Convention of 1907 made a neutral's territory inviolable and required it to apply any restriction on the belligerents impartially to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy used the new term first, in September 1939, to mean legal but not political neutrality, a watchful waiting in full agreement with the plans of its German ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Turkey followed in January 1940, declaring itself not neutral but simply outside the war while keeping its commitments to Britain and France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bulgaria, Romania, Hungary and Slovakia were also called non-belligerents without saying so, and Egypt was too, though it lent its territory to the Allies as a base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The term covered such different situations that it had no single legal meaning, and acts such as letting troops pass or supplying arms openly were unlawful whatever a state called itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain adopted it on 12 June 1940, just after Italy entered the war, in a decree signed by Franco and his foreign minister citing the spread of the fighting to the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American ambassador in Madrid reported the jubilant Spanish reaction to the collapse of France a few days later and called the new stance a pro-Axis non-belligerency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By July 1943 the American ambassador was telling Franco that Spain could no longer afford dubious non-belligerency, a pro-Axis press, an unchecked Falange or the Blue Division on the Russian front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In a speech at the start of October 1943 Franco used the word neutrality for the first time, and the ambassador wrote that non-belligerency was as politically dead as its author, Serrano Suñer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Non belligeranza,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/non-belligeranza_%28Dizionario-di-Storia%29/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Riccardo Monaco, “Non belligeranza,” in <i>Enciclopedia Italiana</i>, II Appendice (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 1949), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/non-belligeranza_%28Enciclopedia-Italiana%29/. [in Italian] [Open access]","“Convention (V) Respecting the Rights and Duties of Neutral Powers and Persons in Case of War on Land,” The Hague, 18 October 1907, articles 1–5 and 9, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague05.asp. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940</i>, vol. 2, <i>General and Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1957), document 920, Alexander W. Weddell to the Secretary of State, 13 June 1940, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d920. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1940</i>, vol. 2, <i>General and Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1957), document 924, Alexander W. Weddell to the Secretary of State, 17 June 1940, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1940v02/d924. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1964), document 558, Carlton J. H. Hayes to President Roosevelt, 4 October 1943, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v02/d558. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did states prefer this label to plain neutrality?","a":"Because neutrality obliged a state to treat both sides impartially, while the new label let it stay out of the fighting and still favour one side, as Italy and Spain favoured the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the label not protect a state that helped one side?","a":"Because acts such as allowing troops to pass or supplying arms at public expense lay outside what any neutral might do, so they were unlawful whatever the state proclaimed itself to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Spain drop non-belligerency in 1943?","a":"Because from July 1943 the American ambassador pressed Franco to curb the pro-Axis press and the Falange and to bring the Blue Division home, and by October Franco was speaking of neutrality instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Non-belligerent","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-L15327%2C_Spanien%2C_Heinrich_Himmler_bei_Franco.jpg","title":"Himmler received by Franco, October 1940","desc":"Heinrich Himmler with Francisco Franco and Ramón Serrano Suñer in Madrid during the months of Spanish non-belligerency.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-L15327, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-L15327,_Spanien,_Heinrich_Himmler_bei_Franco.jpg","alt":"A group of men in uniform standing in a curtained room, with Himmler in SS uniform and glasses beside Franco in a Spanish army tunic."}},
    "ww2-699": {"questions":["Tungsten from Portugal, manganese from Spain and iron ore from Sweden were paid for by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the Reichsbank sold through Switzerland, whose franc was then the only currency convertible worldwide.","Under the Washington agreement of May 1946 Switzerland paid 250 million francs to settle Allied claims over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bought from the Reichsbank during the war."],"abstract":"<b>Nazi gold</b> is the gold that Germany's central bank, the Reichsbank, gathered and sold during the war, much of it seized from occupied countries and some taken from murdered people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold paid for iron ore from Sweden, manganese from Spain, tungsten from Portugal and chromium from Turkey, trades that needed foreign currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once continental European assets were frozen in the United States in June 1941, the Swiss franc became the only currency convertible anywhere, and Switzerland became the hub of German gold dealings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Swiss National Bank bought gold worth 1.2 billion francs from Germany and just under 2.2 billion from the United States, Britain and Canada, while Portugal bought 452 million francs' worth from the Swiss and Spain 185 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among it was victim gold: from August 1942 an SS officer named Bruno Melmer sent 76 shipments from the camps to the Reichsbank, and about 119 kilograms of those bars reached the Reichsbank's depository at the Swiss National Bank in Bern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the autumn of 1940 the Swiss bank's directors had grounds to believe that the Reichsbank held gold from invaded countries, yet the purchases went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 22 February 1944 the United States, Britain and the Soviet Union declared that they would not recognise any transfer of looted gold and would not buy gold from countries still dealing with the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sweden refused German gold from August 1944, and in March 1945 Swiss negotiators agreed to recommend an end to buying it except for prisoners of war, the Red Cross and the German legation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the Washington agreement of 25 May 1946 Switzerland paid 250 million francs in gold to settle Allied claims, while Spanish and Portuguese assets were still blocked in 1948 over looted gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A Swiss commission of experts set up in 1996 revisited the trade and reported in 2002, part of a wider reckoning with the neutrals' record in the years of the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Independent Commission of Experts Switzerland – Second World War, <i>Switzerland and Gold Transactions in the Second World War: Interim Report</i> (Bern: Independent Commission of Experts Switzerland – Second World War, 1998), https://www.uek.ch/en/publikationen1997-2000/golde.pdf. [Open access]","Independent Commission of Experts Switzerland – Second World War, “The Independent Commission of Experts in Brief,” https://www.uek.ch/en/uekinkuerze.htm. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 2, <i>General: Economic and Social Matters</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1967), document 161, circular telegram of the Acting Secretary of State, 22 February 1944, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v02/d161. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 5, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), document 596, Leland Harrison to the Secretary of State, 5 March 1945, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v05/d596. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 5, <i>The British Commonwealth, Western and Central Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1969), document 140, Randolph Paul to President Truman, 3 June 1946, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v05/d140. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1948</i>, vol. 3, <i>Western Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1974), document 631, the Secretary of State to the Embassy in Portugal, 5 February 1948, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1948v03/d631. [Open access]","Wolf Kaiser, review of <i>Bystanders, Rescuers or Perpetrators? The Neutral Countries and the Shoah</i>, ed. Corry Guttstadt et al., H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, September 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47917. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany need to sell gold at all?","a":"Because the raw materials of war it bought from neutral countries had to be paid for in foreign currency, and after June 1941 only the Swiss franc could be spent everywhere, so German gold went to Switzerland to be turned into francs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Allies threaten the neutral buyers in 1944?","a":"Because looted gold gave the Axis foreign exchange and imports, so the Allies announced that they would neither recognise its transfer nor buy gold from any country that kept trading with the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Swiss settlement of 1946 criticised later?","a":"Because the Swiss official accounts of the time ignored embarrassing questions about the gold, and the commission that reopened the subject found the bank had suspected looted origins as early as 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nazi gold","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Capture_of_Germany%27s_Gold_-_NARA_-_5757188.jpg","title":"Reichsbank gold in the Merkers mine, 1945","desc":"Sacks of Reichsbank gold and other treasure found by the US Third Army in a salt mine at Merkers in April 1945.","credit":"US Army Signal Corps, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (US National Archives 111-SC-205409). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capture_of_Germany%27s_Gold_-_NARA_-_5757188.jpg","alt":"Rows of hundreds of filled sacks on the floor of a long, low underground gallery lit by bare bulbs, with a narrow-gauge rail track beside them."}},
    "ww2-700": {"questions":["Deeply suspicious of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after their treatment in North Africa, the French won civil affairs agreements of their own, signed by Eisenhower and Koenig in August 1944.","Renamed Allied Military Government in October 1943, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed Sicily and the south back to the Italian government in February 1944 and ended in December 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Allied Military Government of Occupied Territory</b>, AMGOT for short, was the joint British and American administration set up to govern Sicily and then mainland Italy as the Allies conquered them from July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American proposal to the British in 1943 made the British general commanding the invasion force Military Governor, with a British chief, an American deputy and an Anglo-American staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An order of Allied headquarters of 1 May 1943 created it, with General Alexander as Military Governor of Sicily and Lord Rennell as its head, the Chief Civil Affairs Officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ruled indirectly through Italian prefects and mayors, with about 400 officers for Sicily, one for every 10,000 people, and its legal division wrote the proclamations and set up Allied military courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plan for Sicily became the model for each part of the mainland as it was taken, and on 24 October 1943 it was renamed simply Allied Military Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> As the front moved north, territory passed back to the Italian government, beginning on 11 February 1944 with Sicily and the south up to Bari, Potenza and Salerno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 31 December 1945 the rest of northern Italy except Venezia Giulia and the province of Udine was handed back too, and military government formally came to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Liberated allies were treated differently, for agreements with the Norwegian, Dutch and Belgian governments in exile based the commander's authority on military necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dutch agreement even declared in its preamble that the arrangements in no way affected the sovereignty of the Netherlands government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The French, very suspicious of AMGOT after their treatment in North Africa, won their own civil affairs agreements, signed on 25 August 1944 by Eisenhower and General Koenig.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["C. R. S. Harris, <i>Allied Military Administration of Italy, 1943–1945</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), 4–5, 34, 96, 122, 366, https://archive.org/details/allied-mil-admin-italy. [Open access]","Harry L. Coles and Albert K. Weinberg, <i>Civil Affairs: Soldiers Become Governors</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1964), 657–58, 714, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-3. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1964), document 295, the Department of State to the British Embassy, undated, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v02/d295. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1964), document 383, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v02/d383. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 3, <i>The British Commonwealth and Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1965), document 657, John G. Winant to the Secretary of State, 26 June 1944, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v03/d657. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 4, <i>Europe</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1968), document 704, memorandum of conversation by the Acting Secretary of State, 6 August 1945, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v04/d704. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did AMGOT rule through Italian officials?","a":"Because the Allies chose indirect rule, so a small staff of about 400 officers could supervise the prefects and mayors of Sicily rather than replace them, with one officer for every 10,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Norway, the Netherlands and Belgium not put under military government?","a":"Because they were allies with governments in exile, and agreements with them rested the commander's authority on military necessity, the Dutch text declaring that Dutch sovereignty was untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the French distrust the Allied plans for civil affairs?","a":"Because they had resented their treatment in North Africa and were very suspicious of AMGOT, so the British departed from the model agreement made with the Belgians to meet their state of mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Allied Military Government of Occupied Territories","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Italian_national_policeman_on_guard_outside_an_Allied_military_government_office%2C_Sicily%2C_1943_%2823933646594%29.jpg","title":"An AMGOT office in Sicily, 1943","desc":"An Italian carabiniere in full dress stands guard at the door of an Allied military government office in Sicily in September 1943.","credit":"Office of War Information, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (National Museum of the U.S. Navy). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_national_policeman_on_guard_outside_an_Allied_military_government_office,_Sicily,_1943_(23933646594).jpg","alt":"A policeman in a plumed hat and dress uniform holding a rifle stands in a doorway beside a tall wooden door bearing a notice headed AMGOT."}},
    "ww2-701": {"questions":["The Third Geneva Convention of 1949 granted prisoner-of-war status to members of an organised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> operating even in occupied territory, provided they met four conditions.","Backed by a largely loyal population, Filipino guerrillas formed a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Japan, and at least 118,000 of them were still in action in August 1945."],"abstract":"A <b>resistance movement</b>, in the sense the Second World War gave the phrase, was the organised opposition of people in an occupied country to the occupying enemy, both as open partisan war and in secret through sabotage, hiding people and refusing to cooperate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between 1939 and 1945 up to 230 million Europeans lived under German occupation, and resistance groups formed in almost every occupied country, often with political aims so different that some were irreconcilable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their members ranged from communists, socialists and trade unionists to churchmen and conservative officers, and their methods ran from strikes and clandestine newspapers to raids and assassinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In France the specialised networks that gathered intelligence, ran escape lines or sabotaged for a staff abroad worked apart from the movements, which tried to reach the public through their newspapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Resistance to occupation was not only European, for in the Philippines at least 118,000 guerrillas backed by a largely loyal population were still in action against the Japanese in August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The laws of war gave such fighters little protection, because the Hague regulations of 1907 extended the rights of war only to militia and volunteer corps that met four conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They had to be commanded by a person responsible for his subordinates, wear a fixed emblem recognisable at a distance, carry arms openly and obey the laws and customs of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The occupiers answered resistance with hostage shootings and collective reprisals that fell above all on civilians, and in Belarus thousands of villages were destroyed in such punitive actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain built the Special Operations Executive to support these movements, and put a Labour minister in charge of subversion because it was expected to rely mainly on the forces of the Left in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> After the war the Third Geneva Convention of 1949 named members of organised resistance movements, even in occupied territory, among those owed treatment as prisoners of war if they met the same four conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Non-Jewish Resistance,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/non-jewish-resistance. [Open access]","Alfons Adam, Axel Bangert and Martin Borkowski-Saruhan, “Europa unter deutscher Besatzung,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/europa-unter-deutscher-besatzung. [in German] [Open access]","Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]","“Resistenza,” <i>Enciclopedia on line</i>, Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana Treccani, section “Diritto internazionale,” https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/resistenza/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and its Annex: Regulations, The Hague, 18 October 1907, Annex, art. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","Geneva Convention Relative to the Treatment of Prisoners of War, 12 August 1949, art. 4, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva03.asp. [Open access]","W. J. M. Mackenzie, <i>The Special Operations Executive: Britain and the Resistance Movements in Europe</i>, vol. 1, Cabinet Office Historical Section, typescript, The National Archives, CAB 102/649, 104, https://archive.org/details/cab-102-649. [Open access]","Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), 651, 657, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-33.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did resistance fighters have so little protection in law?","a":"Because the Hague regulations of 1907 granted the rights of war only to militia and volunteer corps that had a responsible commander, wore a fixed emblem visible at a distance, carried arms openly and obeyed the laws of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only in 1949 did the Third Geneva Convention name organised resistance movements, even in occupied territory, among those owed treatment as prisoners of war, and then only on the same four conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the resistance groups of one country so often quarrel?","a":"Because they were held together only by opposition to the occupier and often had very different political aims, some of them irreconcilable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their members ran from communists and socialists to churchmen and conservative officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did civilians suffer so heavily from resistance activity?","a":"Because the occupiers answered it with hostage shootings and collective reprisals that struck the civilian population above all, and in Belarus thousands of villages were destroyed in such punitive actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Resistance movement"},
    "ww2-702": {"questions":["An American wartime manual of simple <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> advised workers to salt railway switch points, misdirect enemy convoys and refer every decision to large committees.","Rather than face another deadly bombing raid, a director of the Peugeot works at Sochaux agreed in 1943 to an inside act of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that halted its tank-turret shop."],"abstract":"<b>Sabotage</b>, the deliberate wrecking or obstruction of an enemy's transport, equipment and production, was the commonest armed form of resistance in occupied Europe, where armed groups attacked infrastructure and cut German supply lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It sat alongside unarmed forms, for civil resistance took in strikes in war factories and mines, and non-violent resisters sheltered Jews, listened to forbidden Allied radio and produced anti-Nazi newspapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In France the first clandestine sheets circulated from the summer of 1940, often handwritten or typed and ending with the instruction to copy them and pass them on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The earliest French sabotage was just as improvised, as individuals removed Wehrmacht signposts, punctured the tyres of military vehicles and cut electric and telephone cables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Organised groups went much further, and in Norway saboteurs destroyed the supply of heavy water at the Norsk Hydro plant at Vemork in late February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In July 1940 Britain set up an organisation to co-ordinate all action by way of subversion and sabotage against the enemy overseas, the Special Operations Executive, which absorbed the sabotage branch of the Secret Intelligence Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> One of its agents showed in 1943 that sabotage could save lives, by persuading a director of the Peugeot works at Sochaux, which made tank turrets for the German army, to help disable his own factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An RAF night raid in July had missed the plant by nearly a kilometre and killed some hundreds of townspeople, whereas the inside job kept the turret shop out of production for much of the rest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An American field manual of simple sabotage taught ordinary people to do damage without special tools, by salting railway switch points so that rain would short-circuit them or by giving enemy convoys wrong directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It also prescribed sabotage by deliberate inefficiency, telling people to insist on doing everything through channels, make long speeches and refer all matters to committees of never fewer than five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Non-Jewish Resistance,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/non-jewish-resistance. [Open access]","Alfons Adam, Axel Bangert and Martin Borkowski-Saruhan, “Europa unter deutscher Besatzung,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/europa-unter-deutscher-besatzung. [in German] [Open access]","Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]","Direction du patrimoine, de la mémoire et des archives, “La presse clandestine,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-10. [in French] [Open access]","W. J. M. Mackenzie, <i>The Special Operations Executive: Britain and the Resistance Movements in Europe</i>, vol. 1, Cabinet Office Historical Section, typescript, The National Archives, CAB 102/649, 103–104, https://archive.org/details/cab-102-649. [Open access]","M. R. D. Foot, <i>SOE in France: An Account of the Work of the British Special Operations Executive in France, 1940–1944</i>, History of the Second World War (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966; 2nd impression 1968), 286–88, https://archive.org/details/soe-france-foot. [Open access]","Office of Strategic Services, <i>Simple Sabotage Field Manual</i>, Strategic Services Field Manual no. 3 (Washington, DC: Office of Strategic Services, 1944), 20–22, 28–29, https://archive.org/details/OSS199420. [Open access]","The National Archives (UK), “Records of the Special Operations Executive,” series HS, administrative history, Discovery catalogue, https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/API/records/v1/details/C153. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could sabotage be kinder to civilians than bombing?","a":"Because a raid on a small target in a town was likely to miss: the RAF's attack on the Peugeot works at Sochaux in July 1943 fell nearly a kilometre away and killed some hundreds of townspeople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Workers inside the factory, by contrast, put its tank-turret shop out of production for much of the rest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a sabotage manual tell people to hold long meetings?","a":"Because the American field manual counted deliberate inefficiency as sabotage too, urging people to do everything through channels, make long speeches and refer matters to large committees, so that an occupied economy would work slowly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the first French sabotage look so amateur?","a":"Because in 1940 it came from scattered individuals with no organisation or equipment, who removed Wehrmacht signposts, punctured tyres and cut electric and telephone cables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clandestine papers began the same way, handwritten or typed and passed from hand to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Maly_sabotaz_kotwica.jpg","title":"An anchor sign painted in occupied Warsaw","desc":"The anchor of Fighting Poland, painted on the plinth of the Aviator Monument in Warsaw by members of the Grey Ranks scouts as an act of small sabotage.","credit":"Anonymous, public domain (PD-Polish), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maly_sabotaz_kotwica.jpg","alt":"An anchor-shaped emblem painted on the stone base of a monument in a city square."},"wiki":"Sabotage"},
    "ww2-703": {"questions":["Between 20,000 and 30,000 members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in combat or before firing squads in the 4 years that ended with the liberation of France in 1944.","Gathered in a National Council that first met in Paris in May 1943, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> merged its armed groups into the French Forces of the Interior early in 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>French Resistance</b> was the whole body of movements and networks that fought the German occupation of France, and the collaborating Vichy regime, from June 1940 until the Liberation of 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first acts in 1940 were spontaneous and uncoordinated, and it remained a small minority of the population until well into 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members came from every walk of life and every part of the political spectrum, from conservative nationalists and clergymen to liberal republicans, socialists and communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Networks were small specialised groups that worked for a staff abroad on intelligence, escape or sabotage, while movements such as Combat, Libération and Franc-Tireur sought to win opinion through clandestine newspapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its actions ranged from strikes and passing intelligence to the Allies to assassinations and sabotage, which grew sharply after the German attack on the Soviet Union in June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The movements were united step by step, as the three main southern movements founded the United Movements of Resistance on 27 January 1943 and the National Council of the Resistance first met in Paris on 27 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The council joined the movements of both zones to resistant parties and trade unions, and on 15 March 1944 it adopted a programme for immediate action and for reforms after the Liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its armed groups were merged as the French Forces of the Interior early in 1944, and after the Allied landings of 6 June tens of thousands of resisters fought beside the Allies or sabotaged the German rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Allied aircraft armed them on a large scale, dropping something over 20,000 packages and nearly 100,000 containers into France for the Special Operations Executive during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 20,000 and 30,000 resisters died in combat or by execution before the Liberation in September 1944, and reprisals struck civilians too, as at Oradour-sur-Glane, where the Waffen-SS murdered over 640 villagers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]","Christine Levisse-Touzé and Isabelle Rivé, “L’unification de la Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-15. [in French] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Besatzungsregime in Frankreich,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “France,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/france. [Open access]","M. R. D. Foot, <i>SOE in France: An Account of the Work of the British Special Operations Executive in France, 1940–1944</i>, History of the Second World War (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966; 2nd impression 1968), 80–81, https://archive.org/details/soe-france-foot. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Vormarsch in Frankreich,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vormarsch-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","Danielle Tartakowsky, “Le programme du CNR : élaboration et mise en application,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-2. [in French] [Open access]","“Maquis,” <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i>, https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/divers/maquis/187399. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the French Resistance grow so much after 1942?","a":"Because the war turned against Germany while the occupier and Vichy grew harsher, with labour requisitions, round-ups and deportations of Jews and the occupation of the southern zone in November 1942 driving many to join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hostage shootings and forced labour in Germany swelled its ranks in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Free France want the movements united?","a":"Because de Gaulle saw unity of action as essential both for effectiveness and for his image with the Allies, and sent delegates such as Jean Moulin to gather the leaders into common bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result was the United Movements of Resistance in January 1943 and the National Council of the Resistance in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Resistance's losses so heavy in 1944?","a":"Because after the landings resisters fought openly beside the Allies and attacked the German rear, and the Germans answered with brutal terror against fighters and civilians alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between 20,000 and 30,000 members of the Resistance died before the Liberation was complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Maquis_resistance_fighters%2C_France_slnsw.jpg","title":"French resistance fighters, 1944–45","desc":"A group of armed French resistance fighters photographed in France at the end of the occupation, from the archive of Le Courrier Australien.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (State Library of New South Wales, PXE 1397). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maquis_resistance_fighters,_France_slnsw.jpg","alt":"A line of armed fighters in mixed civilian and military clothes stands to attention with rifles at the shoulder, a young woman in a floral dress among them."},"wiki":"French Resistance"},
    "ww2-704": {"questions":["Arrested at Caluire near Lyon in June 1943 and tortured by Klaus Barbie's men, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died that July while being taken to Germany, and his ashes later went to the Panthéon.","As prefect at Chartres in June 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut his own throat rather than sign a German statement blaming African colonial troops for deaths that German bombing had caused."],"abstract":"<b>Jean Moulin</b> was a French prefect who became General de Gaulle's delegate in occupied France and did more than anyone to unite the internal Resistance before his arrest and death in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Born at Béziers in 1899, he became the youngest sub-prefect in France in 1925 and the youngest prefect in 1937, and under the air minister Pierre Cot he helped send aircraft secretly to republican Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As prefect at Chartres he refused on 17 June 1940 to sign a German statement falsely blaming African colonial troops for the deaths of civilians, and after being beaten and locked up he cut his own throat rather than give way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dismissed by Vichy in November 1940 as a prefect of the left, he made contact with the resistance movements of the southern zone and in September 1941 reached London by way of Spain and Portugal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came as the representative of three movements, asking for money, communications, arms and above all moral support, and warned that only the communists would gain if these were refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> De Gaulle sent him back as his delegate, and on the night of 1 to 2 January 1942 he was dropped into Provence with funds and radio equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He persuaded the leaders of Combat, Libération and Franc-Tireur to merge their paramilitary groups into a Secret Army under General Delestraint and, early in 1943, to unite their movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not all his judgements prevailed, for in March 1943 he argued that young men fleeing forced labour lacked the training and arms to become fighters, a view soon abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 27 May 1943 he chaired the first meeting of the National Council of the Resistance in the rue du Four in Paris, which recognised de Gaulle as the sole political leader of fighting France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Arrested at Caluire near Lyon on 21 June 1943 by Klaus Barbie's security police, he was tortured and died on about 8 July while being taken to Germany, in circumstances still uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Musée de l’Ordre de la Libération, “Jean Moulin,” <i>Les Compagnons de la Libération</i>, https://www.ordredelaliberation.fr/fr/compagnons/jean-moulin. [in French] [Open access]","“Jean Moulin,” <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i>, https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/personnage/Jean_Moulin/126779. [in French] [Open access]","M. R. D. Foot, <i>SOE in France: An Account of the Work of the British Special Operations Executive in France, 1940–1944</i>, History of the Second World War (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966; 2nd impression 1968), 180–82, https://archive.org/details/soe-france-foot. [Open access]","Christine Levisse-Touzé and Isabelle Rivé, “L’unification de la Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-15. [in French] [Open access]","Danielle Tartakowsky, “Le programme du CNR : élaboration et mise en application,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-2. [in French] [Open access]","Fabrice Grenard, “1943. Les conséquences du STO dans la création des maquis,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-9. [in French] [Open access]","Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did de Gaulle trust Moulin with the Resistance?","a":"Because Moulin was a former prefect with a commanding presence who had refused the Germans at Chartres, and in London he impressed de Gaulle as the first figure of any political standing to come out of France and join him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> De Gaulle sent him back to rally and unite the movements and to create a Secret Army separate from their political work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the National Council of the Resistance matter politically?","a":"Because it brought the movements of both zones together with parties and trade unions and, at its first meeting, recognised de Gaulle as the sole political leader of fighting France, a sign of French unity at a time when his authority was contested by the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the manner of Moulin's death uncertain?","a":"Because after Barbie's interrogation in Lyon he was moved to Paris in a desperate state, and it is not known whether this came from torture or from an attempt at suicide; he died in a train somewhere between Metz and Frankfurt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Moulin_Harcourt_1937.jpg","title":"Portrait by Studio Harcourt, about 1937","desc":"A studio portrait taken in about 1937, when he had just become the youngest prefect in France.","credit":"Studio Harcourt, public domain (PD-France), via Wikimedia Commons (RMN). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moulin_Harcourt_1937.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white studio portrait of a man with slicked-back dark hair, in a dark jacket, white shirt and tie, looking to the right."},"wiki":"Jean Moulin"},
    "ww2-705": {"questions":["Named after the dense brushwood of Corsica, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> numbered perhaps 30,000 to 40,000 fighters by the autumn of 1943, only a fraction of all who refused labour service.","Massed in large numbers on the Glières plateau, at Mont-Mouchet and in the Vercors in 1944, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were crushed when they tried to hold ground against German troops."],"abstract":"The <b>maquis</b> were the bands of resisters who lived hidden in the forests and mountains of occupied France from the end of 1942, named after the Corsican word for ‘thick brushwood’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They began as refuges for young men who would not go to work in Germany, and in December 1942 workers from Grenoble settled at a farm at Ambel that became camp number 1 of the Vercors maquis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vichy's law of 16 February 1943 created the Compulsory Labour Service, or STO, which sent every fit young man born in 1920, 1921 or 1922 to work in Germany for 2 years and turned scattered refusals into a movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Life in the camps was hard, and at first a lack of water, food and warm clothing troubled the men more than security, since the Germans took them for unimportant gangs of outlaws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The camps spread through the Alps, the Jura, the Massif Central and the forests of the south-west and centre during 1943, but barely at all in the more urban and heavily garrisoned north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The movements soon took them in hand, setting up a National Maquis Service at the end of April 1943 to turn refuges into disciplined fighting camps, and by that autumn there were roughly 30,000 to 40,000 maquisards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were only 15 to 20 per cent of the men who had refused the STO, for many preferred cover as farm workers to military discipline and its risks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Communist-led groups favoured immediate action through sabotage and ambushes, while most other movements wanted to hold their maquis back until an Allied landing and then act with the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some commanders tried to build maquis fortresses of several hundred men, and the large gatherings were crushed in pitched battles on the Glières plateau, at Mont-Mouchet and in the Vercors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When German troops took a resistance cave hospital in the Vercors on 21 July 1944, they murdered about 200 of the wounded, doctors and nurses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Fabrice Grenard, “1943. Les conséquences du STO dans la création des maquis,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-9. [in French] [Open access]","M. R. D. Foot, <i>SOE in France: An Account of the Work of the British Special Operations Executive in France, 1940–1944</i>, History of the Second World War (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966; 2nd impression 1968), 282–83, 358, https://archive.org/details/soe-france-foot. [Open access]","“Maquis,” <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i>, https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/divers/maquis/187399. [in French] [Open access]","Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Vormarsch in Frankreich,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/vormarsch-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Besatzungsregime in Frankreich,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/besatzungsregime-in-frankreich. [in German] [Open access]","Gil Emprin, “L’histoire du maquis et de la bataille des Glières,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-7. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the STO law fill the hills with young men?","a":"Because it was a French law, not a German order, that required every fit man born in 1920, 1921 or 1922 to work in Germany for 2 years, with fines and prison for refusal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many refused and hid in remote farms and chalets, which became the first camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Resistance hesitate to arm the maquis at first?","a":"Because some leaders, Jean Moulin among them in March 1943, thought the young men lacked military experience and that there were not enough arms to train them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most non-communist movements also preferred to keep the maquis back until an Allied landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the big maquis gatherings of 1944 end in disaster?","a":"Because they tried to hold fixed ground against regular German troops with too few heavy weapons, a form of fighting in which the old guerrilla methods no longer worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Glières plateau, Mont-Mouchet and the Vercors were all crushed in pitched battles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Members_of_the_Maquis_in_La_Tresorerie.jpg","title":"Maquisards at La Trésorerie, September 1944","desc":"Members of the maquis in the hamlet of La Trésorerie, near Boulogne-sur-Mer, photographed by a Canadian army photographer on 14 September 1944.","credit":"Donald I. Grant, Department of National Defence (Canada), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Library and Archives Canada, PA-166396). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Members_of_the_Maquis_in_La_Tresorerie.jpg","alt":"Six young men and a woman in berets and work clothes stand talking in a village street, some holding rifles, the woman wearing an armband and a shoulder bag."},"wiki":"Maquis (World War II)"},
    "ww2-706": {"questions":["Fifty women agents went into occupied France for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, nearly all of them through its F section, and thirteen of them never came back.","Hugh Dalton took charge of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1940, and the War Cabinet minuted that no parliamentary questions about it should appear on the order paper."],"abstract":"The <b>Special Operations Executive</b>, or SOE, was the secret British organisation formed in 1940 to promote sabotage and subversion in enemy-occupied territory and to train men and women to help local resistance groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It merged three bodies set up in 1938: Section D, the sabotage branch of the Secret Intelligence Service, the War Office research branch MI R, and Electra House, a propaganda section of the Foreign Office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its formal charter, a paper by Neville Chamberlain, was circulated on 19 July 1940 and approved by the War Cabinet on 22 July, with the Labour minister Hugh Dalton, the Minister of Economic Warfare, in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Cabinet minuted that it would be very undesirable for any parliamentary questions about it to appear on the order paper, and its headquarters moved into 64 Baker Street in London on 31 October 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its propaganda branch passed to the new Political Warfare Executive in August 1941, and it remained an organisation for planning and operations until it was disbanded in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its main effort was in occupied Europe, where its emissaries helped French resisters build their first networks, but it also worked in North Africa, the Middle East, South Asia and the Far East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> France alone received fifty of its women agents, thirteen of whom did not come back, and something over 20,000 packages and nearly 100,000 containers of stores were dropped there for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Working with the Polish general staff, it brought 310 specially trained soldiers and about 300 tonnes of supplies to occupied Poland between February 1941 and December 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In May 1942 Czech agents whom the British had parachuted in at the request of the Czechoslovak government in exile attacked Reinhard Heydrich in Prague, and the Germans retaliated against the villages of Lidice and Ležáky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From September 1942 its London group and the special operations branch of the American Office of Strategic Services were practically fused in north-western Europe, though the British kept the lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["The National Archives (UK), “Records of the Special Operations Executive,” series HS, administrative history, Discovery catalogue, https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/API/records/v1/details/C153. [Open access]","W. J. M. Mackenzie, <i>The Special Operations Executive: Britain and the Resistance Movements in Europe</i>, vol. 1, Cabinet Office Historical Section, typescript, The National Archives, CAB 102/649, 103–104, https://archive.org/details/cab-102-649. [Open access]","M. R. D. Foot, <i>SOE in France: An Account of the Work of the British Special Operations Executive in France, 1940–1944</i>, History of the Second World War (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966; 2nd impression 1968), 31, 35, 48, 80, https://archive.org/details/soe-france-foot. [Open access]","Wiesław Kaczmarczyk, “Akcje Special Operations Executive na terenie okupowanej Polski – zrzuty sprzętu i ludzi,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 27 September 2020, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/polskie-panstwo-podziem/74798,Akcje-Special-Operations-Executive-na-terenie-okupowanej-Polski-zrzuty-sprzetu-i.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Non-Jewish Resistance,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/non-jewish-resistance. [Open access]","Wyman W. Irwin, “A Special Force: Origin and Development of the Jedburgh Project in Support of Operation Overlord” (MMAS thesis, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1991), 18, Defense Technical Information Center ADA241173, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA241173. [Open access]","Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a Labour minister put in charge of SOE?","a":"Because the post balanced other appointments in the coalition, and because subversion was expected to rely mainly on the forces of the Left in Europe, so a Labour minister seemed appropriate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did SOE lose its propaganda branch in 1941?","a":"Because after a dispute with the Ministry of Information and the Foreign Office the bulk of that branch was moved to the new Political Warfare Executive, leaving SOE purely an organisation for planning and operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did SOE work so closely with the Americans?","a":"Because an agreement of September 1942 between the British and American chiefs of staff practically fused SOE's London group with the special operations branch of the Office of Strategic Services in north-western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dual control was the principle, but the British stayed in the lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/SOE_agents_with_a_bearded_partisan_in_Macedonia_during_operation_Bethesda%2C_May-December_1944.jpg","title":"Agents in Macedonia, 1944","desc":"Two British agents with a bearded partisan in Macedonia during a mission of May to December 1944.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Imperial War Museum). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SOE_agents_with_a_bearded_partisan_in_Macedonia_during_operation_Bethesda,_May-December_1944.jpg","alt":"Two smiling young men in rolled-up shirts and trousers stand either side of a bearded man in a cap with a pistol holster, in front of a wooden farm building."},"wiki":"Special Operations Executive"},
    "ww2-707": {"questions":["Truman abolished the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 1 October 1945, sending its research and analysis branch to the State Department and the rest of its work to the War Department.","About a third of the first staff of the Central Intelligence Agency had served in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the wartime agency that at its peak employed more than 13,000 people."],"abstract":"The <b>Office of Strategic Services</b>, or OSS, was the United States' intelligence and special operations agency of the Second World War and the first centralised intelligence agency in American history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of the Office of the Coordinator of Information, which Franklin Roosevelt created on 11 July 1941 under William J. Donovan, a lawyer who had won the Medal of Honor in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A military order of 13 June 1942 renamed that office, without its foreign information work, as the Office of Strategic Services, placed it under the Joint Chiefs of Staff and made Donovan its Director.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gathered intelligence openly and covertly, analysed it for other agencies and ran clandestine operations in support of military plans, through branches for research and analysis, secret intelligence, special operations and morale operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At its peak it employed more than 13,000 military personnel and civilians, about 35 per cent of them women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Truman abolished it by Executive Order 9621 of 20 September 1945, effective on 1 October, sending its research and analysis branch to the State Department and the rest of its work to the War Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a letter to Donovan that day he wrote that the government's peacetime intelligence services were being built on the foundation of what the office had mobilised in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What went to the War Department became the Strategic Services Unit, whose duties passed in 1946 to the new Central Intelligence Group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The National Security Act of 1947 then created the Central Intelligence Agency, and a third of its first personnel were OSS veterans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A foreword to the office's own war report, compiled by a historical staff in 1946 and 1947, claimed that its experience showed how essential complete intelligence was to national policy and that unorthodox warfare was now a vital part of American defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Order Establishing the Office of Strategic Services,” military order, 13 June 1942, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/order-establishing-the-office-strategic-services. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Executive Order 9621—Termination of the Office of Strategic Services and Disposition of Its Functions,” 20 September 1945, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-9621-termination-the-office-strategic-services-and-disposition-its. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Letter to General William J. Donovan on the Termination of the Office of Strategic Services,” 20 September 1945, The American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/letter-general-william-j-donovan-the-termination-the-office-strategic-services. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the Office of Strategic Services [OSS], Record Group 226,” <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/226.html. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “History of CIA,” https://www.cia.gov/legacy/cia-history/. [Open access]","Strategic Services Unit, History Project, <i>War Report, Office of Strategic Services</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: War Department, 1947), CIA Reading Room document C00168776, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-00168776. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “The Legend of Wild Bill: How Donovan Got His Nickname,” 11 November 2020, https://www.cia.gov/stories/story/the-legend-of-wild-bill-how-donovan-got-his-nickname/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the office put under the Joint Chiefs of Staff?","a":"Because after Pearl Harbor White House approval alone was no longer enough, and its operational plans needed the clearance of the military authorities, as Donovan himself had acknowledged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The order of June 1942 duly made its Director work under the direction and supervision of the Joint Chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the office broken up rather than kept after the war?","a":"Because Truman abolished it along with many other war agencies, keeping its research and analysis work in the State Department and passing the rest to the War Department for liquidation or continued use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the office seen as the forerunner of the CIA?","a":"Because its surviving branches passed through the Strategic Services Unit and the Central Intelligence Group to the agency created in 1947, and a third of that agency's first personnel had served in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Truman said as much in 1945, writing that peacetime intelligence would be built on its foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Office_of_Strategic_Services_Insignia.svg","title":"The spearhead emblem","desc":"A redrawing of the agency's gold spearhead on a black shield, the emblem its members wore.","credit":"United States Army, public domain (derivative drawing by Maxxl²), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Office_of_Strategic_Services_Insignia.svg","alt":"A gold spearhead pointing upwards on a black oval patch with a gold border."},"wiki":"Office of Strategic Services"},
    "ww2-708": {"questions":["Named after a small Scottish border town in July 1942, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> trained its teams at Milton Hall near Peterborough before the first of them dropped into France in June 1944.","Thirteen teams of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were parachuted into France in June 1944 and eighty more followed during the summer, and they lost only 21 men killed."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Jedburgh</b> was the Anglo-American plan of 1944 to parachute three-man teams of special forces behind German lines in western Europe, to co-ordinate the resistance with the Allied campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was one strand of a far larger Allied effort to supply the resistance by air, which dropped something over 20,000 packages and nearly 100,000 containers into France for the Special Operations Executive alone, where resistance networks received the parachuted men and stores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Special duty squadrons also flew supplies to occupied Poland, first over Denmark and later from bases in Tunisia and Italy, delivering about 300 tonnes to the underground there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In France the British and Americans ran the work jointly, and from 1 May 1944 their combined headquarters went by the cover name of Special Force Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most non-communist French movements wanted to hold their maquis back until a landing, and the Allies parachuted these teams in 1944 to advise the maquis, link them with London or Algiers and obtain their arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The name first appears in July 1942, when the security chief of the Special Operations Executive rejected Jumpers for Colin Gubbins's project and chose Jedburgh, a small town on the Scottish border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each team had two officers and a sergeant wireless operator, usually an Englishman, an American and a Frenchman, trained in guerrilla tactics, leadership and demolition to act as a staff for the local resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They trained at Milton Hall near Peterborough, and the Americans had to find officers with excellent French so fast that only 46 per cent passed the final tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first team, codenamed HUGH, was alerted on 3 June 1944 and dropped blind near Châteauroux in central France together with a Special Air Service party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thirteen teams were dropped into France in June 1944 and eighty more followed, and they lost only 21 dead, few by the standards of the agents who worked in plain clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["M. R. D. Foot, <i>SOE in France: An Account of the Work of the British Special Operations Executive in France, 1940–1944</i>, History of the Second World War (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966; 2nd impression 1968), 32–34, 80, https://archive.org/details/soe-france-foot. [Open access]","Wyman W. Irwin, “A Special Force: Origin and Development of the Jedburgh Project in Support of Operation Overlord” (MMAS thesis, U.S. Army Command and General Staff College, 1991), iii, 41–42, 116–17, 140–41, Defense Technical Information Center ADA241173, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA241173. [Open access]","Strategic Services Unit, History Project, <i>War Report, Office of Strategic Services</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: War Department, 1947), CIA Reading Room document C00168776, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-00168776. [Open access]","“Maquis,” <i>Encyclopédie Larousse</i>, https://www.larousse.fr/encyclopedie/divers/maquis/187399. [in French] [Open access]","Wiesław Kaczmarczyk, “Akcje Special Operations Executive na terenie okupowanej Polski – zrzuty sprzętu i ludzi,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 27 September 2020, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/polskie-panstwo-podziem/74798,Akcje-Special-Operations-Executive-na-terenie-okupowanej-Polski-zrzuty-sprzetu-i.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","Denis Mazzucchetti and Frantz Malassis, “La Résistance,” <i>Chemins de mémoire</i>, Ministère des Armées et des Anciens combattants, https://www.defense.gouv.fr/chemins-memoire/histoire-memoires/ressources-historiques/seconde-guerre-mondiale/resister/resistance-interieure-16. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the teams drop in uniform?","a":"Because their job was nearer open warfare than secret work: they were to act as a staff for the local resistance, co-ordinate it with Allied strategy and arrange arms, almost like the Special Air Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their losses, only 21 dead, were low compared with those of agents in plain clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans struggle to man the plan?","a":"Because they needed officers of superior intelligence, ready to parachute and fluent in French, at short notice, and of the recruits sent to London only 46 per cent passed the final tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the teams sent to the maquis in 1944?","a":"Because most movements other than the communists had held their maquis back until a landing, and the teams were to advise them, link them with London or Algiers, find them arms and coordinate them with the invading forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Captain_Aaron_Bank_in_France_during_WWII.jpg","title":"A team near Alès, 1944","desc":"An American officer with the French officer and French radio operator of his team and two local helpers near Alès in southern France in 1944, beside a car taken from a collaborator.","credit":"United States Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Captain_Aaron_Bank_in_France_during_WWII.jpg","alt":"Several men, most in military uniform, stand in a farmyard in front of a stone building, beside a parked car."},"wiki":"Operation Jedburgh"},
    "ww2-709": {"questions":["Lured to talks at Pruszków in March 1945, sixteen leaders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were seized by the NKVD and tried in Moscow that June for diversion behind Red Army lines.","The Home Army served as the military arm of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Government Delegate in occupied Warsaw was also a vice-premier of the cabinet in exile."],"abstract":"The <b>Polish Underground State</b> was the clandestine state that Poles built under German occupation, answerable to the government in exile, first in Paris and then in London, with couriers carrying reports between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Apart from the communists and the far right, most resistance groups saw themselves as heirs of the Second Republic and wanted to restore its statehood, so civil and military forces alike placed themselves under that government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had a parliament of its own and secret systems of social welfare and justice, and its civil offices ran a secret school system, radio stations and underground printing houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Poland the government was represented by the Delegation, the Delegatura, which among its tasks co-ordinated the work of the Home Army, the underground's military arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It even audited itself, for in the autumn of 1942 a control section was formed within the Government Delegation to check how its money was spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its end came with the Red Army, when in March 1945 a Soviet NKVD officer invited the Government Delegate, Vice-Premier Jan Stanisław Jankowski, to talks and guaranteed his personal safety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 27 and 28 March the Delegate, the last Home Army commander General Leopold Okulicki, three underground ministers and party leaders went to Pruszków for the talks and did not return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Polish ambassador in Washington warned that the invitation might be a stratagem to arrest and deport them, and in May Molotov confirmed that they had been arrested on a charge of diversion against the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sixteen of them were tried in Moscow from 18 June 1945 before the Military Collegium of the Soviet Supreme Court, accused of espionage, diversion and sabotage behind the Red Army's lines and of belonging to an illegal Home Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 21 June Okulicki was sentenced to 10 years and Jankowski to eight, and both died in Soviet prisons, Okulicki in December 1946 and Jankowski in March 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Widerstand und Selbstbehauptung in Polen,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/widerstand-und-selbstbehauptung-in-polen. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","Waldemar Grabowski, “Konspiracyjny NIK w latach 1942–1945. Struktury kontrolne Państwa Podziemnego,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/armia-krajowa/23527,Konspiracyjny-NIK-w-latach-1942-1945-Struktury-kontrolne-Panstwa-Podziemnego.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Polish Ambassador (Ciechanowski) to the Secretary of State, April 4, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945, Europe</i>, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1967), document 164, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v05/d164. [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Polish Ambassador (Ciechanowski) to the Acting Secretary of State,” May 1945, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945, Europe</i>, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1967), document 211, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v05/d211. [Open access]","Ryszard Sodel, “Proces szesnastu,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 18 June 2024, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/polskie-panstwo-podziem/107693,Proces-szesnastu.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jan Karski,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jan-karski. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did most Polish resisters accept the exile government's authority?","a":"Because, apart from the communists and the far right, they saw themselves as heirs of the Second Republic and wanted to restore its statehood, so civil and military groups alike placed themselves under the government in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the underground's leaders walk into a Soviet trap?","a":"Because an NKVD officer had guaranteed the Delegate's personal safety and even granted a request for a delegation to fly to London, before inviting the leaders to Pruszków for talks from which none returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Polish side believed the Western Allies protected it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the arrests alarm the Western governments?","a":"Because the Polish government had kept London and Washington informed since February and saw Molotov's admission of the arrests as confirming its worst fears about Soviet intentions in Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ambassador warned that any later talks with the prisoners would take place in isolation and under pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Polish Underground State"},
    "ww2-710": {"questions":["About 45,000 soldiers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only a quarter of them armed, rose against the German garrison of Warsaw on 1 August 1944 and fought on for 63 days.","Ordered by General Komorowski in November 1943, Operation Tempest sent units of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into local risings to free Polish towns before the Red Army arrived."],"abstract":"The <b>Home Army</b>, in Polish Armia Krajowa or AK, was the main underground army of occupied Poland and the military arm of the Polish Underground State, loyal to the government in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of the Service for Poland's Victory, founded in September 1939, and the Union of Armed Struggle, which General Sikorski's government set up in November 1939 to take its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 14 February 1942 a dispatch from Sikorski renamed the Union the Home Army, partly to show that Poland was still fighting at home at a time when the Soviets accused the Poles of shirking the fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most other armed groups then came under its command, among them the National Military Organisation and the Peasant Battalions, and by the summer of 1944 it had almost 400,000 sworn members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its intelligence sent London reports on German and, until June 1941, Soviet forces, and passed on information about the German V-1 and V-2 weapons, along with captured parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> For years it attacked the Germans only sporadically, training its recruits in secret while it prepared a general rising for the moment the occupier was weak enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 20 November 1943 its commander, General Tadeusz Komorowski, ordered Operation Tempest, a series of local risings to free Polish territory ahead of the advancing Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its largest action was the Warsaw Uprising of 1 August 1944, in which about 45,000 of its soldiers, only a quarter of them armed, fought some 25,000 well-equipped German troops for 63 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 11,000 of its soldiers became prisoners of war when the rising ended, Komorowski among them, and General Leopold Okulicki, a parachutist sent in from Britain, commanded it until 19 January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 2 months later Okulicki, described by the Polish government as commander of the dissolved Home Army, went to talks with the Soviets at Pruszków and did not return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Piotr Niwiński, “Powstanie Armii Krajowej,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 14 February 2021, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/armia-krajowa/51799,Powstanie-Armii-Krajowej.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","Waldemar Grabowski, “Od ZWZ do Armii Krajowej,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 14 February 2022, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/armia-krajowa/62796,Od-ZWZ-do-Armii-Krajowej.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","Włodzimierz Suleja, “‘Burza’,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 31 July 2023, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/armia-krajowa/102501,Burza.html. [in Polish] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Warsaw Uprising,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-warsaw-polish-uprising. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Widerstand und Selbstbehauptung in Polen,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/widerstand-und-selbstbehauptung-in-polen. [in German] [Open access]","United States Department of State, “The Polish Ambassador (Ciechanowski) to the Secretary of State, April 4, 1945,” <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1945, Europe</i>, vol. 5 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1967), document 164, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v05/d164. [Open access]","Wiesław Kaczmarczyk, “Akcje Special Operations Executive na terenie okupowanej Polski – zrzuty sprzętu i ludzi,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Instytut Pamięci Narodowej, 27 September 2020, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/polskie-panstwo-podziem/74798,Akcje-Special-Operations-Executive-na-terenie-okupowanej-Polski-zrzuty-sprzetu-i.html. [in Polish] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Union of Armed Struggle renamed the Home Army?","a":"Because the new name made a political point: it showed that Poland was still fighting at home, at a time when the Soviets were presenting Polish reluctance to fight on their terms as a lack of commitment against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also stressed that the underground was part of the regular Polish armed forces, not a political union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Home Army wait so long before rising?","a":"Because its plan was to attack the occupier in force only when Germany was weak enough inside and out, so until then it trained in secret and carried out only scattered attacks and sabotage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Operation Tempest plan risings ahead of the Red Army?","a":"Because the Home Army wanted to free Polish towns before Soviet troops entered them, and so to assert the claim of the government in exile, with which Moscow had broken off relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Akcja_Burza%2C_1_kompania_samborska_podczas_akcji_%28NAC_37-1442-4%29.png","title":"A company during Operation Tempest, 1944","desc":"Soldiers of a company from the Sambor area during Operation Tempest in the summer of 1944, from the National Digital Archive in Warsaw.","credit":"Mieczysław Michalski, public domain (PD-Poland), via Wikimedia Commons (Narodowe Archiwum Cyfrowe, 37-1442-4). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akcja_Burza,_1_kompania_samborska_podczas_akcji_(NAC_37-1442-4).png","alt":"Soldiers lie in the grass at the edge of a forest aiming rifles and a submachine gun, while an officer kneels behind them."},"wiki":"Home Army"},
    "ww2-711": {"questions":["Polish agents in France working for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reported U-boats leaving Bordeaux from early 1941, and by that June from Brest and Le Havre as well.","In May 1942 Washington agreed to give 12 million dollars to finance Polish resistance and the intelligence work in Europe run by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from exile in London."],"abstract":"The <b>Second Department of the Polish General Staff</b>, which its allies called the Second Bureau, was the intelligence service of the Polish armed forces, and after Poland's defeat in 1939 it went on working for the Polish government in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> According to Polish accounts its cipher bureau recruited mathematicians in September 1932, who took barely 4.5 months to break the early version of the German Enigma machine and from 1934 built copies of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a meeting in Warsaw on 24 and 25 July 1939 the Poles revealed this success to French and British cryptanalysts and agreed to give each of them a Polish-built Enigma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once the governments in exile had settled in London, the Polish service agreed to hand the British Secret Intelligence Service everything it gathered except material on Poland's internal affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From January 1941 the Polish bureau was the only channel for that material, and the British official history records that liaison caused few difficulties because the Poles already ran an extensive network with efficient wireless links.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From early 1941 Polish agents in France reported U-boat departures from Bordeaux, and by June from Brest and Le Havre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At London's request the Poles also built a network to watch German transport and depots in eastern Europe, and from May 1941 they reported eight series of German railway movements during the build-up against the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States joined in, agreeing in May 1942 to give the Polish government 12 million dollars over the coming year to finance resistance in Poland and Polish intelligence work in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1943 General Sikorski told the State Department that a standing liaison would now give the War Department the fullest measure of Polish military intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same channels carried unwelcome news, for in April 1943 Sikorski said that German claims about the graves of Polish officers near Smolensk matched what Polish intelligence had already reported to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["F. H. Hinsley, with E. E. Thomas, C. F. G. Ransom and R. C. Knight, <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 276–77, 333, 438, 457–58, 463–64, 487–93, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe</i>, document 131, the Secretary of State to the Ambassador to the Polish Government in Exile (Biddle), 21 May 1942, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d131. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 3, <i>The British Commonwealth, Eastern Europe, the Far East</i>, document 234, memorandum of conversation by the Under Secretary of State (Welles), 4 January 1943, 315, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d234. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 3, <i>The British Commonwealth, Eastern Europe, the Far East</i>, document 279, the Ambassador to the Polish Government in Exile (Biddle) to the Secretary of State, 17 April 1943, 380, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v03/d279. [Open access]","Trevor Yates, review of <i>Project Eagle: The Top-Secret OSS Operation that Sent Polish Spies behind Enemy Lines in World War II</i>, by John S. Micgiel, H-War, H-Net Reviews, February 2026, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=61301. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British find the Polish service easy to work with?","a":"Because the Poles already ran an extensive network of agents in Europe with efficient wireless channels, and they agreed to hand over everything they gathered except material on Poland's internal affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans pay for Polish intelligence work?","a":"Because Roosevelt and Sikorski agreed in May 1942 that the United States would fund resistance in Poland and Polish intelligence in Europe, and a standing liaison was then set up to pass Polish military intelligence to the War Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Polish intelligence matter in the Katyn crisis?","a":"Because when Germany announced the discovery of the graves near Smolensk in April 1943, Sikorski said the German claims matched reports he had already received through Polish intelligence channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Second Department of Polish General Staff"},
    "ww2-712": {"questions":["Women made up about 12 per cent of the fighters in the combat units of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose communist leaders also recruited peasant women through the Antifascist Front of Women.","German reports estimated that some 90,000 of the 145,000 guerrillas in the Balkans in September 1943 belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by then the largest resistance force in the region."],"abstract":"The <b>Yugoslav Partisans</b> were the communist-led resistance army that fought the Axis occupiers of Yugoslavia and their local allies from 1941 to 1945 under Josip Broz, known as Tito.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement started in Belgrade straight after the surrender of April 1941, and in August Tito moved his headquarters into the field to command its growing forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike the Chetniks, who kept mostly local units to be called up when needed, the Partisans built large mobile formations and did not hold back for fear of the reprisals the occupiers would take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those reprisals were savage, for Hitler ordered 100 hostages shot for every German killed, and in the autumn of 1941 German units used the order to shoot almost all the male Jews of Serbia along with communists, nationalists and Roma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The communist party also mobilised women on a large scale, through the Antifascist Front of Women behind the lines and in combat units, where they made up about 12 per cent of the fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the autumn of 1943 the Partisans were the strongest resistance force in the Balkans, and the Germans estimated them at some 90,000 of the 145,000 guerrillas facing them that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Tehran on 1 December 1943 the three Allied leaders agreed that the Partisans should be supported with supplies and equipment to the greatest possible extent and by commando operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that Tito had more than a quarter of a million men, a figure one member in the same debate called a considerable exaggeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Bosnia their success depended on winning over Muslims, and their units were local coalitions whose members could change sides, so that for many Muslim communities liberation felt like a military occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Victory brought reprisals of its own, and the killing by Partisans of retreating Ustaša soldiers and accompanying civilians in May 1945 remains one of the most bitterly argued memories of the war in Croatia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 20–21, 31, 50, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]","“War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 22 February 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 397, cc. 663–795, Winston Churchill’s statement at cc. 692–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","“Military Conclusions of the Tehran Conference,” Tehran, 1 December 1943, in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tehran.asp. [Open access]","“Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","Robin Lech, review of <i>Women and Yugoslav Partisans: A History of World War II Resistance</i>, by Jelena Batinić, H-War, H-Net Reviews, January 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=44700. [Open access]","Emily Greble, review of <i>Bosnian Muslims in the Second World War: A History</i>, by Marko Attila Hoare, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, December 2014, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=43002. [Open access]","Dario Brentin, conference report on “Bleiburg &amp; Beyond: Transnational Approaches towards Memory Politics and Commemorative Practices,” H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, July 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47366. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Partisans outgrow the Chetniks?","a":"Because they built mobile units not tied to one district and were not deterred by reprisals, while Chetnik policy held back to avoid them; Churchill also noted that Croats, Slovenes and many Serbs joined Tito.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Western Allies decide to back the Partisans?","a":"Because the Allied leaders at Tehran agreed to support them as fully as possible, and Churchill judged in February 1944 that they were then the only force doing effective fighting against the Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did women matter so much to the movement?","a":"Because the party won over peasant women with promises of equality and heroines from epic poetry, and its women's organisation turned their traditional work into a reserve behind the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Yugoslav Partisans"},
    "ww2-713": {"questions":["Born at Kumrovec in 1892, wounded and captured on the Russian front in 1915, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was elected general secretary of the Yugoslav Communist Party in October 1940.","After Stalin expelled Yugoslavia from the Cominform in 1948, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built his own socialist system and helped found the Non-Aligned Movement beside leaders such as Nehru and Nasser."],"abstract":"<b>Josip Broz Tito</b> was the communist who led the Yugoslav Partisans against the Axis occupation from 1941 to 1945 and then ruled Yugoslavia until his death in 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Born at Kumrovec in Croatia on 7 May 1892, he trained as a locksmith, was wounded and captured on the Russian front in 1915, and joined the Bolshevik side in Russia before returning home in 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As a communist organiser he spent the years from 1928 to 1934 in prison, took the party name Tito in 1934, took over the leadership of the party in 1937 and was elected its general secretary in 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the Axis invasion of April 1941 he formed a military committee within days, and on 27 June he set up the Partisans' headquarters with himself as supreme commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the autumn of 1941 he held a short-lived liberated area round Užice in Serbia and met the royalist commander Draža Mihailović, but the talks got no further and the two movements turned on each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His capture was itself a German aim, and on 25 May 1944 paratroopers and glider troops landed on his headquarters at Drvar, which was taken, though Tito escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The raid broke the Partisan chain of command only until he re-established himself on the island of Vis under British protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Tehran in December 1943 the Allies had agreed to supply his Partisans as fully as possible, and in February 1944 Churchill told the Commons that in Tito they had found an outstanding leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His forces were in Belgrade by October 1944, and at the end of the war he became head of state in fact and crushed his Chetnik opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1948 Stalin expelled Yugoslavia from the Cominform, and Tito went on to build his own socialist system and to help form the Non-Aligned Movement with leaders such as Nehru and Nasser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Dušan Bilandžić and Marijan Sentić, “BROZ, Josip-Tito,” in <i>Hrvatski biografski leksikon</i> (Zagreb: Leksikografski zavod Miroslav Krleža), online edition, https://hbl.lzmk.hr/clanak/broz-josip-tito. [in Croatian] [Open access]","Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 20–21, 31–32, 65–66, 70, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]","“War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 22 February 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 397, cc. 663–795, Winston Churchill’s statement at cc. 692–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","“Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","Paul Brenard Chiudza Banda, review of <i>The Search for a Cold War Legitimacy: Foreign Policy and Tito’s Yugoslavia</i>, by Robert Niebuhr, H-War, H-Net Reviews, August 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=55253. [Open access]","Svein Mønnesland, “Josip Broz Tito,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Josip_Broz_Tito. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","“Military Conclusions of the Tehran Conference,” Tehran, 1 December 1943, in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/tehran.asp. [Open access]","Luke Gramith, review of <i>Serbia under the Swastika: A World War II Occupation</i>, by Alexander Prusin, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52012. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Tito's talks with Mihailović fail?","a":"Because the two wanted different futures: the Chetnik leaders aimed to restore the old kingdom and in the autumn of 1941 turned on the Partisans, according to the Croatian biographical account, while the American study notes the talks got no further than one brief conference at Užice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans attack Drvar in May 1944?","a":"Because the area was the refuge the Partisans fell back on under pressure, and seizing Tito's headquarters was meant to strike a blow from which they would not soon recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain back Tito rather than the royal government's general?","a":"Because Churchill judged that the Partisans were doing the effective fighting, while some of Mihailović's commanders had made accommodations with the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/MARSHAL_TITO_DURING_THE_SECOND_WORLD_WAR_IN_YUGOSLAVIA%2C_MAY_1944_NA15129.jpg","title":"Tito and his ministers at Drvar, May 1944","desc":"Tito, at the right, stands with members of his National Committee outside his cave headquarters at Drvar in May 1944, the month of the German raid.","credit":"No. 2 Army Film and Photographic Unit, public domain (Crown copyright expired), via Wikimedia Commons (Imperial War Museum NA 15129). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MARSHAL_TITO_DURING_THE_SECOND_WORLD_WAR_IN_YUGOSLAVIA,_MAY_1944_NA15129.jpg","alt":"A group of men in military tunics and caps stand on a narrow path beside a rock face under a small tree, with a dog sitting at their feet."},"wiki":"Josip Broz Tito"},
    "ww2-714": {"questions":["Taking their name from a Serb organisation that had fought the Ottomans, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held back from open fighting to avoid the mass reprisals Serbia had suffered in the First World War.","Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that some commanders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had made accommodations with Italian and German troops in return for being left alone in the mountains."],"abstract":"The <b>Chetniks</b> of the Second World War were the Serb royalist guerrillas of Colonel, later General, Draža Mihailović, named after an older Serb nationalist organisation that had resisted the Ottomans and fought in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement gained strength in the early summer of 1941 and made contact with King Peter's government in exile, which named Mihailović commander of the resistance at home and then its minister of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its policy was to build up underground forces in Serbia for a rising timed with an Allied landing, because Mihailović was determined to avoid a repeat of the mass shooting of Serb hostages in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chetniks therefore kept mostly to small actions and sabotage, and many of them soon came to see Tito's Partisans as a greater threat to the monarchy than the Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The royal government insisted in December 1942 that Mihailović's units were tying down 30 to 40 Axis divisions and that reports of his collaboration were an ill-intentioned campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A civil war between the two movements opened in late 1941, after a single conference between Tito and Mihailović at Užice came to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the war went on, some Chetnik commanders made armistice arrangements with the occupiers and even assisted them, and the Germans and especially the Italians could count on Chetnik help against the Partisans, and in Bosnia there were even distinct Muslim Chetnik units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that these accommodations had left some Chetnik bands alone in mountain areas doing little or nothing against the enemy, and that Britain was now giving its aid to Tito.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1944 Mihailović made a last alliance with Serbia's collaborationist forces to forestall a communist seizure of power, but Tito's forces were in Belgrade by October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the war Mihailović was tried before a Yugoslav military court in July 1946, sentenced to death and executed on 17 July 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 20–21, 31, 52, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]","“War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 22 February 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 397, cc. 663–795, Winston Churchill’s statement at cc. 692–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe</i>, document 719, the Yugoslav Embassy to the Department of State, 16 December 1942, 833–34, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v03/d719. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 6, <i>Eastern Europe, the Soviet Union</i>, document 631, the Secretary of State to the Chargé in Yugoslavia (Shantz), 6 June 1946, 898–99, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v06/d631. [Open access]","Luke Gramith, review of <i>Serbia under the Swastika: A World War II Occupation</i>, by Alexander Prusin, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52012. [Open access]","“Axis Invasion of Yugoslavia,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/axis-invasion-of-yugoslavia. [Open access]","Emily Greble, review of <i>Bosnian Muslims in the Second World War: A History</i>, by Marko Attila Hoare, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, December 2014, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=43002. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Mihailović hold back from open fighting?","a":"Because he had been appalled by the execution of some 35,000 Serb hostages in the First World War and meant to avoid such reprisals until an Allied landing made a general rising worthwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain stop backing the Chetniks?","a":"Because Churchill concluded that some Chetnik commanders had made accommodations with the enemy and were doing little fighting, while the Partisans were doing the effective fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Chetnik record still argued over?","a":"Because Mihailović himself stayed loyal to the royal government while some of his subordinates dealt with the occupiers, and a recent study treats him evenhandedly without hiding that his actions often served German interests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chetniks"},
    "ww2-715": {"questions":["Churchill complained in February 1944 that within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Greeks were killing Greeks with ammunition the Allies had sent them for killing Germans.","Divided between ELAS under the communist-led EAM and the EDES of Colonel Zervas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw its largest army disbanded under the Varkiza Agreement of February 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Greek Resistance</b> was the opposition to the German, Italian and Bulgarian occupation of Greece from 1941 to 1944, and like the Yugoslav resistance it divided between groups loyal to the royal government in exile and groups led or strongly influenced by communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The largest force was ELAS, the Greek People's Liberation Army, which operated across most of the country under EAM, the National Liberation Front, a coalition of left-wing parties with a hard core of communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main rival was EDES of Colonel Zervas, which held the mountains of Epirus and kept in touch with the government in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The guerrillas undertook nothing of importance in 1941, but on 25 November 1942 a small force overpowered the Italian guards and blew up the Gorgopotamos bridge on the vital Athens to Salonika railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Supplied by British liaison officers, EDES grew to about 7,000 men by July 1943, and ELAS under Colonel Sarafis to an estimated 12,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two fought each other as well as the occupier, and Churchill told the Commons in February 1944 that Greeks were killing Greeks with ammunition sent to them to kill Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Occupation reprisals were savage, and at the village of Domenikon the Italian occupiers executed sixteen local people for every Italian lost to the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thousands of Greek Jews survived by hiding or by joining the partisans, though by the German withdrawal in the autumn of 1944 nearly 60,000 Greek Jews had died in the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fighting that broke out after liberation was ended for a time by the Varkiza Agreement of 12 February 1945, which provided for ELAS to be demobilised and an amnesty for political crimes committed since 3 December 1944, though within a month EAM's leaders complained that the government was breaking its terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The communist party behind EAM became the only one in the Balkans that failed to take power, and its armed struggle collapsed in 1948 and 1949 when its foreign allies cut off their support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Department of the Army, <i>German Antiguerrilla Operations in the Balkans (1941–1944)</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-243, German Report Series (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1954), 27–32, 38–39, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-243. [Open access]","“War and International Situation,” House of Commons debate, 22 February 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 397, cc. 663–795, Winston Churchill’s statement at cc. 692–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/feb/22/war-and-internationa-situation. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Near East and Africa</i>, document 63, Agreement between the Greek Government and the Central Committee of EAM, Varkiza, 12 February 1945, 109–13, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v08/d63. [Open access]","“Greece,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/greece. [Open access]","Paschalis Pechlivanis, review of <i>Under Stalin’s Shadow: A Global History of Greek Communism</i>, by Nikos A. Marantzidis, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, October 2023, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=59292. [Open access]","Richard J. B. Bosworth, review of <i>Nel cantiere della memoria: Fascismo, Resistenza, Shoah, Foibe</i>, by Filippo Focardi, H-Italy, H-Net Reviews, March 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=56355. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Near East and Africa</i>, document 69, the Ambassador in Greece (MacVeagh) to the Secretary of State, Athens, 10 March 1945, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v08/d69. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Greek resistance split?","a":"Because, as in Yugoslavia, it divided along political lines between groups loyal to the royal government in exile and groups led or influenced by communists, and the rivals came into open conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Gorgopotamos sabotage worry the Germans?","a":"Because it halted supplies on the vital Athens to Salonika line until repairs were made and forced German troops to take over guarding a long stretch of railway in the Italian zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the communist bid for power in Greece fail?","a":"Because the Greek party never won power as its Balkan neighbours did, and its armed struggle collapsed once fraternal communist states cut off their support in 1948 and 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Greek resistance"},
    "ww2-716": {"questions":["Freed from confinement in August 1943, Luigi Longo went to Milan to take command of the communist <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the party's own formations in the partisan war.","Inspired by the Communist Party, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fought beside the Action Party's Justice and Freedom brigades in the Italian resistance directed by the Committee of National Liberation."],"abstract":"The <b>Garibaldi Brigades</b> were the partisan formations inspired and organised by the Italian Communist Party in the armed resistance to the German occupation and Mussolini's Social Republic that followed the armistice of September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They fought beside other political formations such as the Justice and Freedom brigades of the Action Party, within a resistance led by the Committee of National Liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the partisans' strength came from young men called up by the Social Republic who chose to join the bands instead, along with militants and leaders of the anti-fascist parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their chief organisers were the communist leaders Luigi Longo and Pietro Secchia, both freed from fascist imprisonment or confinement in August 1943, and Longo went to Milan to take command of the brigades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Longo also served with Ferruccio Parri of the Action Party as deputy commander of the Volunteer Freedom Corps, under the battle name Italo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Secchia tried to channel the spontaneity of the bands into a single movement, so that the party's influence grew in step with its ability to organise the war of liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Valsesia the communist Vincenzo Moscatelli took to the mountains with 22 other men and by the liberation commanded, as political commissar of a Garibaldi grouping, several brigades of some thousands of partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Julian March the communist resisters were caught between their own party and the Slovene and Croatian communists, whose forces gained the upper hand there in 1943 and 1944 and aimed to annex Italian territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When a partisan rising drove the Germans from Ravenna in December 1944, the 900-strong, communist-led 28th Garibaldi Brigade took a place in the Eighth Army's ranks and was supplied like a regular unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It held a quiet sector through the winter, and in the April 1945 offensive it was still attached to the British 56th Division on the Adriatic flank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Resistenza,” in <i>Dizionario di Storia</i> (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2010), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/resistenza_(Dizionario-di-Storia)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Giuseppe Sircana, “LONGO, Luigi,” in <i>Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani</i>, vol. 65 (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2005), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/luigi-longo_(Dizionario-Biografico)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Marco Albeltaro, “SECCHIA, Pietro,” in <i>Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani</i>, vol. 91 (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2018), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/pietro-secchia_(Dizionario-Biografico)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Marco Albeltaro, “MOSCATELLI, Vincenzo,” in <i>Dizionario Biografico degli Italiani</i>, vol. 77 (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2012), https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/vincenzo-moscatelli_(Dizionario-Biografico)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Ernest F. Fisher Jr., <i>Cassino to the Alps</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1989), 401–2, 449–50, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-MTO-Cassino/index.html. [Open access]","Karlo Ruzicic-Kessler, review of <i>“Si soffre ma si tace”: Luigi Frausin, Natale Kolarič; Comunisti e resistenti</i>, by Patrick Karlsen and Luca G. Manenti, H-Italy, H-Net Reviews, December 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=57213. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the brigades so closely tied to the Communist Party?","a":"Because the party's own leaders organised them: Longo commanded them from Milan and Secchia built a structure that drew the bands into one movement, so the party's influence grew with the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the 28th Garibaldi Brigade matter to the Allies?","a":"Because it was the first major, well-organised partisan unit to appear on the Allied front, and after Ravenna it held part of the Eighth Army's line, supplied through army channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the communists of the Julian March in a difficult position?","a":"Because Tito's forces gained the upper hand there in 1943 and 1944 and aimed to annex Italian territory, and the Italian party first accepted this before arguing for an Italian view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Brigate Garibaldi"},
    "ww2-717": {"questions":["Some 1,100 teachers were arrested in 1942 after resisting a Nazi-controlled teachers' union, one of many stands by the largely unarmed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Quisling's regime.","Through 'the King's No' of 1940, King Haakon VII in London became the foremost symbol of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which opposed the occupation regime set up that September."],"abstract":"The <b>Norwegian resistance movement</b>, often called the home front, was the civil and military struggle against the occupation regime that Hitler's Germany set up in Norway on 25 September 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was above all an unarmed struggle of attitude against attempts by Vidkun Quisling's party, Nasjonal Samling, to nazify society, and King Haakon VII in London became its chief symbol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In May 1941 a joint letter from 43 organisations with about 700,000 members protested to Reichskommissar Terboven, who answered by deposing their leaders and drove the open protest underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1942 some 1,100 teachers were arrested after resisting a new Nazi-controlled teachers' union, and over 90 per cent of the clergy of the Church of Norway laid down their offices, while that autumn the underground helped about 1,000 Jews escape to Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its military arm, Milorg, was founded around the turn of March and April 1941 and recognised by the government in London that November as part of the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> SOE found Norwegian leaders, in exile and at home, reluctant to risk sabotage that would bring reprisals or wreck the railways and roads they would need after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Norwegians also agreed a division of labour with the British Secret Intelligence Service, collecting military intelligence while the British watched shipping for the Admiralty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sabotage came mostly in the last year, when Milorg and the Norwegian-British Kompani Linge destroyed labour-office registers, oil depots and railway lines, nearly 1,000 men cutting lines on over 40 stretches in one night in March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the German surrender in May 1945 Milorg, renamed the Home Forces, could muster nearly 40,000 armed men and women, more than twice the regular Allied and Norwegian troops who arrived weeks later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Norway was one of the peripheries of the western resistance, which, a recent comparative history concludes, would have been powerless without Anglo-American aid, while the Allied secret services would have been blind without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Frode Færøy, “motstandskampen i Norge,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/motstandskampen_i_Norge. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Frode Færøy, “Milorg,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Milorg. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Ole Kristian Grimnes, “Norge under andre verdenskrig,” in <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Norge_under_andre_verdenskrig. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley, with E. E. Thomas, C. F. G. Ransom and R. C. Knight, <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 277, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Fred R. Coventry, review of <i>Special Operations in Norway: SOE and Resistance in World War II</i>, by Ian Herrington, H-War, H-Net Reviews, January 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=54122. [Open access]","Robert Weldon Whalen, review of <i>The Resistance in Western Europe, 1940–1945</i>, by Olivier Wieviorka, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=54845. [Open access]","“Norway,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/norway. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Norwegian resistance mostly unarmed?","a":"Because its first aim was to resist the nazification of society, and civilian leaders feared that armed action would bring a far harsher German policy, as the destruction of Telavåg in 1942 showed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did SOE find Norway a difficult partner?","a":"Because Norwegian authorities did not want sabotage that would bring reprisals on civilians or damage the infrastructure needed after the war, and Norway was a low priority for Allied weapons and transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Milorg matter so much at the liberation?","a":"Because no large Allied force was ready for Norway, so its nearly 40,000 Home Forces had to secure order, infrastructure and the civil takeover until regular troops arrived weeks later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Milorg_District_12_%28D12%29_on_parade_in_1945.jpg","title":"Milorg on parade in Oslo, 1945","desc":"Men of Milorg's District 12 march past in front of the Royal Palace in Oslo after the liberation of 1945.","credit":"Anders Beer Wilse, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Norwegian Museum of Cultural History). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milorg_District_12_(D12)_on_parade_in_1945.jpg","alt":"Long ranks of uniformed men march across a parade ground past two flagpoles flying flags with Nordic crosses, watched by a few figures in the foreground."},"wiki":"Norwegian resistance movement"},
    "ww2-718": {"questions":["The uprisings at Treblinka and Sobibór in 1943 and the Sonderkommando revolt at Auschwitz-Birkenau in 1944 were acts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inside the killing centres themselves.","The secret Oneg Shabbat archive of the Warsaw ghetto, which documented Jewish life and then the mass murder, was a form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that used no weapons."],"abstract":"<b>Jewish resistance</b> under Nazi rule was the opposition, collective and individual, that Jews offered to persecution and murder, from armed revolt in ghettos and camps to rescue, refusal and the deliberate preservation of Jewish life and memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 1941 and 1943 underground movements grew up in about 100 ghettos in German-occupied eastern Europe, aiming to stage uprisings, break out and join partisan units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their members knew that revolts would not stop the Germans and that only a handful of fighters would escape, yet in 1943 Jews rose in the ghettos of Warsaw, Vilna, Białystok and other towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners also rose at the killing centres of Treblinka in August 1943 and Sobibór in October 1943, and in October 1944 men of the Jewish Sonderkommando at Auschwitz-Birkenau mutinied against the SS.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In many countries resistance took the form of aid and rescue, and Jews also fought in the national resistance movements of France, Belgium, Italy, Poland, Yugoslavia, Greece and Slovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Much resistance used no weapons, and the term spiritual resistance covers clandestine schools, secret prayer and underground archives kept up against Nazi attempts to dehumanise the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Warsaw ghetto the historian Emanuel Ringelblum's secret Oneg Shabbat group collected documents on Jewish life and, from 1942, on the mass murder, passing copies to the Polish underground so that its reports reached the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armed organisation came late, and the Jewish Combat Organization was founded in Warsaw on 28 July 1942, while the great deportation to Treblinka was under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Help from outside was limited, for the Polish government in London set a policy of only limited aid to the ghetto fighters, and some Home Army units in the east treated Jewish fugitives as enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Postwar assumptions of Jewish passivity framed much of the later argument, and studies of resistance answered them by documenting its many forms, survival itself among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Jewish Resistance,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-resistance. [Open access]","“Spiritual Resistance in the Ghettos,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/spiritual-resistance-in-the-ghettos. [Open access]","Anna Majchrowska, “Emanuel Ringelblum – Optimist Who Believed in the Human Being,” Jewish Historical Institute, 2020, https://www.jhi.pl/en/articles/emanuel-ringelblum-optimist-who-believed-in-the-human-being,3612. [Open access]","“Cywia Lubetkin (1914–1978),” <i>People of the Ghetto</i>, Warsaw Ghetto Museum, https://1943.pl/en/ludzie-getta/cywia-lubetkin-1914-1978/. [Open access]","Kevin Hall, review of <i>Resistance: Jews and Christians Who Defied the Nazi Terror</i>, by Nechama Tec, H-USA, H-Net Reviews, January 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=41844. [Open access]","Jadwiga Biskupska, review of <i>The Polish Underground and the Jews, 1939–1945</i>, by Joshua D. Zimmerman, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50741. [Open access]","“Resistance,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, The Wiener Holocaust Library, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/resistance-responses-collaboration/resistance/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did ghetto fighters rise when they knew they could not win?","a":"Because most ghetto inhabitants had already been deported, and they chose to fight for Jewish honour and to avenge the murdered, knowing that resistance could not save those unable to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is unarmed defiance counted as resistance?","a":"Because keeping schools, prayer and archives alive was a conscious refusal of Nazi efforts to dehumanise the Jews and to erase them from human memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so little help reach Jewish fighters from outside?","a":"Because the Polish government set a policy of only limited aid to the Warsaw fighters, and decisions to help or harm Jews were often made by individuals at low levels of command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jewish resistance in German-occupied Europe"},
    "ww2-719": {"questions":["Jürgen Stroop's report on crushing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, titled 'The Warsaw Ghetto Is No More', was quoted by the Nuremberg tribunal for its count of 56,065 Jews.","The Jewish Combat Organization's command bunker at 18 Miła Street fell on 8 May 1943, during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its commander Mordecai Anielewicz is thought to have died there."],"abstract":"The <b>Warsaw Ghetto Uprising</b> was the armed revolt of the Jews remaining in the Warsaw ghetto, which began on 19 April 1943, the eve of Passover, when German SS and police entered to deport its surviving inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It was the largest uprising by Jews during the war and the first significant urban revolt against German occupation in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 265,000 Jews had been deported from the ghetto to Treblinka in the great deportation of 1942, and by early 1943 some 70,000 to 80,000 remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two fighting organisations had formed, the left-wing Jewish Combat Organization and the smaller but better-armed Jewish Military Union of the right, which agreed on sectors of defence without a joint command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An armed clash in January 1943 and the refusal of many residents to report made the Germans suspend that round of deportations, and the ghetto began building bunkers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> When about 2,000 soldiers and police under Jürgen Stroop entered on 19 April, some 700 young fighters armed mainly with pistols and grenades drove them back and twice set a tank on fire with petrol bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stroop answered by burning the ghetto block by block, and his own report describes Jews leaping from burning buildings and smoke candles lowered into the sewers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 8 May the Germans took the Combat Organization's command bunker at 18 Miła Street, where its commander Mordecai Anielewicz and many fighters are thought to have taken their own lives, and survivors escaped through the sewers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stroop ended the operation on 16 May by blowing up the Warsaw synagogue and reported a proved total of 56,065 Jews caught or killed, a figure from his illustrated report that the Nuremberg tribunal quoted in its judgment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Polish government in London set a policy of only limited help, and the Home Army offered training, at least one delivery of weapons and a few unsuccessful attempts at assistance from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Warsaw Ghetto Uprising,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw-ghetto-uprising. [Open access]","Jürgen Stroop, “The Warsaw Ghetto Is No More,” report of 16 May 1943, Document 1061-PS, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1061-ps.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” 1 October 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","“Cywia Lubetkin (1914–1978),” <i>People of the Ghetto</i>, Warsaw Ghetto Museum, https://1943.pl/en/ludzie-getta/cywia-lubetkin-1914-1978/. [Open access]","Przemysław Batorski, “A Handful of Doomed in a Hopeless Fight: The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising,” Jewish Historical Institute, https://www.jhi.pl/en/articles/a-handful-of-doomed-in-a-hopeless-fight-the-warsaw-ghetto-uprising,5169. [Open access]","Jadwiga Biskupska, review of <i>The Polish Underground and the Jews, 1939–1945</i>, by Joshua D. Zimmerman, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50741. [Open access]","“The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, The Wiener Holocaust Library, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/the-camps/the-warsaw-ghetto-a-case-study/the-warsaw-uprising/. [Open access]","“Resistance,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, The Wiener Holocaust Library, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/resistance-responses-collaboration/resistance/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fighters rise on 19 April 1943?","a":"Because the SS and police entered that day to deport the remaining Jews, and after the January clash the Combat Organization had warned residents to hide and prepared to resist a final deportation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans burn the ghetto down?","a":"Because fighters and civilians hid in a network of bunkers and sewers, and Stroop decided to destroy the whole area by setting every block on fire to force them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Home Army give so little help?","a":"Because its commander first judged arming the ghetto militarily inadvisable and the government in London set a policy of limited aid, far below what the fighters asked for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Stroop_Report_-_Warsaw_Ghetto_Uprising_-_26552.jpg","title":"Zamenhofa Street burning, 1943","desc":"A housing block on Zamenhofa Street burns during the suppression of the revolt, in a photograph taken for Stroop's own report.","credit":"Photographer unknown (Stroop Report), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stroop_Report_-_Warsaw_Ghetto_Uprising_-_26552.jpg","alt":"A tall corner building with flames pouring from its windows and smoke filling the street, a lone soldier standing in the road below."},"wiki":"Warsaw Ghetto Uprising"},
    "ww2-720": {"questions":["The family camp of Tuvia Bielski in the Naliboki Forest, which grew to about 1,200 people, was the largest group of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and took in any Jew who reached it.","Soviet war memory celebrated the partisans of Belorussia as heroes of every nationality, leaving out for decades what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among them had escaped in the ghettos."],"abstract":"<b>Jewish partisans</b> were Jews who fought the Germans and their allies as irregular guerrillas, in all-Jewish units or within wider partisan movements, above all from bases in the forests of eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As many as 20,000 Jews fought in the forests of eastern Europe, despite little support and often open antisemitic hostility from the surrounding population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most came from ghettos and labour camps, from which thousands escaped, at Novogródek even by a tunnel, and Jews from Minsk alone formed seven partisan fighting units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Life in the forest meant constant moves, raids on farms for food and winters in shelters of logs and branches, with the ever-present danger that local informers would betray a camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the west Jews also joined partisan groups, from the Armée Juive founded in Toulouse in January 1942 to communist units in France and Belgium that attacked German personnel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In western Belorussia, western Ukraine and eastern Poland family camps sheltered civilians who repaired weapons, made clothing and cooked for the fighters, and as many as 10,000 Jews survived by taking refuge with Jewish partisan units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest was the group led by Tuvia Bielski and his brothers in the Naliboki Forest, which took in any Jew and grew to about 1,200 people by the summer of 1944, with a hospital, classes and workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the same forest Shalom Zorin's Soviet partisan detachment 106 accepted women and teenagers unable to fight and supplied food to combat units, while antisemitism and sexism kept many Jews out of other units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some Polish Home Army units around Białystok and Nowogródek regarded Jews as enemies, killing and robbing fugitives and refugee groups, including some who tried to join them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Soviet commemoration made the partisan war a story of heroes of every nationality, so that the Jewishness of many partisans and the genocide they had escaped were long left out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["“Jewish Partisans,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-partisans. [Open access]","“Armed Jewish Resistance: Partisans,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/armed-jewish-resistance-partisans. [Open access]","Andrej Kotljarchuk, review of <i>Pioneers and Partisans: An Oral History of Nazi Genocide in Belorussia</i>, by Anika Walke, H-Russia, H-Net Reviews, April 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=48117. [Open access]","Jadwiga Biskupska, review of <i>The Polish Underground and the Jews, 1939–1945</i>, by Joshua D. Zimmerman, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50741. [Open access]","“Tuvia Bielski,” Jewish Partisan Educational Foundation, https://www.jewishpartisans.org/partisans/tuvia-bielski. [Open access]","“Resistance,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, The Wiener Holocaust Library, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/resistance-responses-collaboration/resistance/. [Open access]","“Jack: Bielski Brothers,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, The Wiener Holocaust Library, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/jacks-testimony/jack-bielski-brothers/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Jewish partisans find so little local support?","a":"Because antisemitism was widespread in the east and villagers feared severe punishment for helping, so partisans could seldom count on aid and lived in fear of informers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the family camps matter?","a":"Because they let civilians who could not fight survive by working for the fighters, and as many as 10,000 Jews came through the war in this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Jewish partisan story muted after the war?","a":"Because Soviet memory turned the war into a story of heroes of all nationalities and neglected the Holocaust, leaving Jewish survivors at once part and not part of official commemoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jewish partisans"},
    "ww2-721": {"questions":["In 1944 members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the camp's Home Army branch set up a joint Military Council, though they judged an open revolt against the SS too unequal to begin.","Prisoner groups of many nations, mostly on the political left, merged in 1943 into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an underground body that smuggled news, photographs and evidence of the camp's crimes to the outside."],"abstract":"<b>Kampfgruppe Auschwitz</b>, the Auschwitz Combat Group, was an international underground organisation formed by prisoners inside the Auschwitz concentration camp in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the camp's first years organised resistance had been mostly the work of Polish political prisoners, among them Witold Pilecki, who let himself be caught in a Warsaw street roundup in September 1940 in order to reach the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the prisoner population changed, Jews, Austrians and Germans, Czechs, French, Russians and Yugoslavs formed groups of their own at the turn of 1942 and 1943, most of them on the political left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of these joined Polish leftists and socialists in 1943 to create the Combat Group, while a separate camp branch of the Home Army took its orders from the Polish government-in-exile in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such networks smuggled information and photographs out of the camp, told prisoners how the war was going and secretly found medicine, food and clothing for those who needed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1944 the Combat Group and the camp Home Army set up a joint Auschwitz Military Council to prepare an uprising, and the Polish underground outside was ready to join the fight against the garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The camp underground never gave the order, judging the fight too unequal and seeing no practical way to shelter tens of thousands of escaped prisoners in the country around the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Escape was the other road out, and of 928 prisoners known to have tried it, 196 succeeded, two Jews who left in July 1944 doing so with the help of the camp resistance and the local underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one armed rising came on 7 October 1944, when Jewish prisoners of the Sonderkommando set fire to a crematorium, about 250 of them died fighting, and four women who had smuggled them explosives were later hanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Historians of camp resistance stress that even small acts of defiance were open only to some prisoners, depending on the camp and on a prisoner's place in its hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“Organized Resistance,” Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/resistance/organized-reistance. [Open access]","“Prisoner Mutinies,” Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/resistance/prisoner-mutinies. [Open access]","“Escapes and Reports,” Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/resistance/escapes-and-reports. [Open access]","“Resistance,” Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/resistance/. [Open access]","“Auschwitz,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/auschwitz. [Open access]","“Killing Center Revolts,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/killing-center-revolts. [Open access]","Tanja Schönborn, review of the conference <i>Widerstand im Konzentrationslager: Formen, Voraussetzungen, Möglichkeiten und Verarbeitung</i>, Regensburg, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, November 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45566. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the camp underground never launch its planned uprising?","a":"Because it judged the fight too unequal, and in a mass escape there would have been no practical way to shelter tens of thousands of prisoners in the area around Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did resistance in Auschwitz change after 1942?","a":"Because the prisoner population changed: at first resisters were mostly Polish political prisoners, but Jews and prisoners of other nations arrived, formed their own groups and in 1943 joined Polish leftists in the Combat Group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Polish groups best placed to reach the outside world?","a":"Because Auschwitz lay in occupied Poland, so Polish networks could keep in touch with resistance outside, and local civilians often helped escapers to get away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Auschwitz Combat Group"},
    "ww2-722": {"questions":["Planned in 3 weeks by the Red Army lieutenant Alexander Pechersky, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let some 300 prisoners break through wire and a minefield, though only about 50 of them survived the war.","Weeks after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of October 1943, the killing centre where at least 167,000 people had been murdered was dismantled by prisoners brought from Treblinka, who were then shot."],"abstract":"The <b>Sobibor uprising</b> was the revolt of Jewish prisoners at the Sobibor killing centre in German-occupied Poland on 14 October 1943, in which some 300 prisoners broke out of the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sobibor had been built in the spring of 1942 for the sole purpose of murdering Jews, the second killing centre of Operation Reinhard, and by mid-October 1943 its staff had killed at least 167,000 people there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the transports dwindled in 1943 and the prisoners learned that Belzec had been dismantled and its last prisoners killed, they feared the same end and formed a resistance group in the late spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polish Jews led by Leon Feldhendler made little headway until Jewish Red Army prisoners of war arrived from Minsk in September, and within 3 weeks Lieutenant Alexander Pechersky had worked out a plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners lured SS men one by one into the workshops and killed them, eleven in all including the deputy commandant Johann Niemann, before the alarm was raised at evening roll call.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Under fire and unable to reach the main gate, around 300 of the roughly 600 prisoners broke through the wire and the minefield, while all those left behind were shot by the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At least 100 were caught in the manhunt that followed, many others were betrayed or killed by civilians or partisans, and only about 50 survived the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Treblinka had risen first, on 2 August 1943, when prisoners seized weapons from the armoury but were discovered too early and stormed the main gate under machine-gun fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> More than 300 escaped there too, though two-thirds were hunted down, and both camps were then dismantled by Jewish prisoners who were shot when the work was done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Treblinka revolt also had a political afterlife in Poland, where a later version giving gentile Poles and the Home Army the leading part has been shown to be false.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Sobibor Uprising,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/sobibor-uprising. [Open access]","“Sobibor,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/sobibor. [Open access]","“Killing Center Revolts,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/killing-center-revolts. [Open access]","“Treblinka,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/treblinka. [Open access]","Darren L. Johnson, review of <i>From “Euthanasia” to Sobibor: An SS Officer's Photo Collection</i>, ed. Martin Cüppers, Anne Lepper and Jürgen Matthäus, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=59584. [Open access]","Joanna Sliwa, review of <i>Treblinka 43: Bunt w fabryce śmierci</i>, by Michał Wójcik, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2019, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=54698. [Open access]","“Jewish Resistance,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-resistance. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the prisoners revolt in the autumn of 1943?","a":"Because the transports were dwindling and they had learned that Belzec had been dismantled and its prisoners killed, so they expected to be murdered when Sobibor closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Soviet prisoners of war so important to the plan?","a":"Because the Polish Jews' secret committee lacked military experience, and the Red Army men from Minsk brought the training to plan the killing of the SS staff and the breakout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few of the escapers survive?","a":"Because SS, police and army units hunted them down, and many who got away were betrayed to the Germans or killed by Polish civilians or partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sobibor uprising"},
    "ww2-723": {"questions":["Communists, Social Democrats, churchmen, students and army officers all took part in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though very few of its groups ever protested openly against the murder of the Jews.","The coup attempt of 20 July 1944 was the boldest act of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its failure brought thousands of arrests and trials before the People's Court in Berlin."],"abstract":"<b>German resistance to Nazism</b> was the opposition mounted by Germans against the National Socialist regime from inside its own state between 1933 and 1945, by people of very different backgrounds and beliefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most Germans welcomed the new rulers and only a minority resisted, while the regime used torture, prison and concentration camps against every opponent it found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest opposition came from the workers' movement, though Communists and Social Democrats never formed a common front and the police crushed their organisations by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its members met in secret, ran courier services and passed on leaflets, and after 1939 they tried to sabotage the arms industry and to help the persecuted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchmen resisted too, and the Catholic bishop of Münster, Clemens August von Galen, denounced the killing of the sick and disabled as murder in a sermon of 3 August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The forms ranged from refusing to obey regulations to attempts on Hitler's life, and the carpenter Georg Elser came close early with a bomb in the Munich Bürgerbräukeller on 8 November 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler left the hall minutes before it exploded, and Elser, arrested at Constance as he tried to reach Switzerland, was murdered in Dachau on 9 April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Very few groups openly protested against the genocide of the Jews, and the Munich students of the White Rose were among the exceptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Officers and conservative civilians around Ludwig Beck and Carl Goerdeler planned a coup in which Hitler's death would signal a general revolt, and Claus von Stauffenberg set off a bomb at Rastenburg on 20 July 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hitler survived and the coup failed, and in the weeks that followed thousands of opponents were arrested, many of them condemned by the People's Court and executed at Plötzensee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“Resistance against National Socialism,” Topics 1, German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/1-resistance-against-national-socialism. [Open access]","“Resistance from the Workers' Movement,” Topics 4, German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/4-resistance-from-the-workers-movement. [Open access]","“Georg Elser and the Assassination Attempt of November 8, 1939,” Topics 7, German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/7-georg-elser-and-the-assassination-attempt-of-november-8-1939. [Open access]","“German Resistance to Hitler,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-resistance-to-hitler. [Open access]","“Resistance inside Germany,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/resistance-inside-germany. [Open access]","“Widerstand im Zweiten Weltkrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg. [in German] [Open access]","“White Rose,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/white-rose. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the workers' movement fail to stop Hitler?","a":"Because Communists and Social Democrats could not bridge their differences, so no common front formed, and after 1933 the Security Police crushed the left-wing organisations by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Georg Elser's bomb miss Hitler?","a":"Because Hitler left the assembly hall of the Bürgerbräukeller unexpectedly, only minutes before the explosion on 8 November 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was resistance inside Germany so small?","a":"Because most Germans welcomed the regime, and the police with its many informers made opposition so dangerous that only a minority risked it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"German resistance to Nazism"},
    "ww2-724": {"questions":["Arrested on 18 February 1943 while scattering the sixth leaflet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Munich University, Hans and Sophie Scholl were executed 4 days later with Christoph Probst.","Written largely by the philosophy professor Kurt Huber, the last leaflet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> urged the students of Munich to rise against Hitler after the defeat at Stalingrad."],"abstract":"The <b>White Rose</b> was a resistance group of students at the University of Munich, formed around Hans Scholl and Alexander Schmorell in 1942, that wrote and spread leaflets against the Nazi regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its core included Sophie Scholl, Christoph Probst and Willi Graf, and the philosophy professor Kurt Huber, with whom the students discussed the foundations of the new political order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many of them had heard of the mass murder of Polish Jews, and Hans Scholl, serving as a soldier on the eastern front, had seen Jewish forced labourers mistreated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 27 June and 12 July 1942 Scholl and Schmorell wrote the first four leaflets in the Schmorell family home, about 100 copies of each, sent mostly to educated readers in and around Munich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second named the murder of the Jews in Poland as the most terrible crime against human dignity, which makes it one of the few known documents of the German resistance to denounce it in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The fifth leaflet of January 1943, addressed to all Germans, ran to between 6,000 and 9,000 copies and was carried by friends to Ulm, Stuttgart, Saarbrücken and other cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the defeat at Stalingrad the group painted slogans such as 'Down with Hitler' on Munich walls at night and issued a sixth leaflet, largely written by Huber, calling on the students to rise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 18 February 1943 a university caretaker saw Hans and Sophie Scholl leaving copies at the university and betrayed them to the Gestapo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The People's Court under Roland Freisler sentenced them and Probst to death on 22 February, and all three were executed the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Graf, Schmorell and Huber were condemned on 19 April 1943 and executed, and ten more opponents linked to the Munich and Hamburg circles were later murdered or driven to their deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“White Rose,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/white-rose. [Open access]","“The White Rose,” Topics 15, German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/15-the-white-rose. [Open access]","“Flugblätter,” Weiße Rose Stiftung, https://www.weisse-rose-stiftung.de/widerstandsgruppe-weisse-rose/flugblaetter/. [in German] [Open access]","“Verbreitung der Flugblätter,” Weiße Rose Stiftung, https://www.weisse-rose-stiftung.de/widerstandsgruppe-weisse-rose/verbreitung-der-flugblaetter/. [in German] [Open access]","“Die Weiße Rose,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/widerstand-im-zweiten-weltkrieg/die-weisse-rose. [in German] [Open access]","“Resistance inside Germany,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/resistance-inside-germany. [Open access]","“German Resistance to Hitler,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-resistance-to-hitler. [Open access]","“Resistance by Young People,” Topics 13, German Resistance Memorial Center, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/13-resistance-by-young-people. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the White Rose unusual among young opponents of the regime?","a":"Because most youth opposition, such as the Edelweiss Pirates and the Swing Kids, resisted through its way of life and seldom fought the regime directly, while the White Rose's resistance was openly political.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the fifth leaflet different from the first four?","a":"Because it spoke in plain political language to the whole population, ran to thousands of copies in several cities, and its title was meant to suggest that a large resistance movement already existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the White Rose limit its aims?","a":"Because the students knew that only military force could end Nazi rule, so they aimed at a renewal from within of the wounded German spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"White Rose"},
    "ww2-725": {"questions":["The social worker Irena Sendler headed the children's section of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the autumn of 1943, smuggling several hundred children out of the Warsaw ghetto under new names.","Born of a committee that Zofia Kossak-Szczucka helped to found in September 1942, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had branches in Warsaw, Kraków and Lwów and paid Jews in hiding a monthly benefit."],"abstract":"<b>Żegota</b> was the code name of the Council to Aid Jews, an underground rescue organisation of Poles and Jews that worked in German-occupied Poland from 4 December 1942 to January 1945 with the backing of the Polish government-in-exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a committee founded in Warsaw on 27 September 1942 by Zofia Kossak-Szczucka and Wanda Krahelska-Filipowicz, after the deportation of Warsaw's Jews to Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kossak-Szczucka, a Catholic writer who made no secret of her antisemitic views, had issued a protest declaring that whoever stays silent in the face of murder becomes the murderer's accomplice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Council that replaced the committee was led by party representatives, the socialist Julian Grobelny as chairman and Leon Feiner of the Jewish Bund as his deputy, and it acted under the Government Delegation, the exile government's arm in Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Branches were set up in Kraków and Lwów as well as Warsaw, with sections for housing, children, legal matters and fieldwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its main work was forgery and hiding, for Żegota supplied tens of thousands of Polish Jews with false identity papers and delivered money, food and medical help to thousands in its care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Irena Sendler, head of its children's section from the autumn of 1943, helped smuggle several hundred Jewish children out of the Warsaw ghetto and recorded their real names in code so their families could find them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Poland's Institute of National Remembrance calls it the only official state organisation helping Jews in Europe, and the <i>Encyclopaedia Judaica</i> the only Polish clandestine body devoted to their rescue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It worked amid mixed attitudes, for rescue was not the dominant behaviour of the Polish population and units of the underground ranged from aiding Jews to robbing and killing them, so individual choices mattered more than orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As many as 20,000 Jews lived in hiding in and around Warsaw with the help of Polish civilians, and in 1963 Yad Vashem invited the co-founder Władysław Bartoszewski to plant an olive tree in Żegota's honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“The Council for Aid to Jews: Żegota,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/gallery/the-council-for-aid-to-jews-zegota. [Open access]","“Rescue,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/rescue. [Open access]","“The Council to Aid Jews ‘Żegota’,” Przystanek Historia, Institute of National Remembrance, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/english-content/62064,The-Council-to-Aid-Jews-Zegota.html. [Open access]","Jadwiga Biskupska, review of <i>The Polish Underground and the Jews, 1939–1945</i>, by Joshua D. Zimmerman, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=50741. [Open access]","“Righteous Among the Nations,” <i>Encyclopaedia Judaica</i>, 2nd ed., Jewish Virtual Library, https://www.jewishvirtuallibrary.org/jsource/judaica/ejud_0002_0017_0_16756.html. [Open access]","“Irena Sendler (1910–2008),” In Memoriam, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://www.ushmm.org/information/press/in-memoriam/irena-sendler-1910-2008. [Open access]","Joanna Sliwa, review of <i>The Light of Days: The Untold Story of Women Resistance Fighters in Hitler's Ghettos</i>, by Judy Batalion, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=57110. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Council replace the original committee?","a":"Because the Government Delegation apparently wanted more control over a body acting in its name, so social activists gave way to representatives of political parties, Jewish parties among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were forged papers so central to its work?","a":"Because a Jew trying to pass as a non-Jew needed convincing documents, and acquiring good forgeries could mean the difference between life and death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could Żegota help only some of those in danger?","a":"Because the underground could barely protect itself: its leaders, such as the Government Delegate Jan Piekałkiewicz and General Grot-Rowecki, were themselves arrested and murdered in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Tablica_%C5%BBegota_ul._%C5%BBurawia_24.JPG","title":"A plaque in Żurawia Street, Warsaw","desc":"A memorial plaque at 24 Żurawia Street in Warsaw marks the address of the council's wartime secretariat.","credit":"Adrian Grycuk, CC BY-SA 3.0 PL, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablica_%C5%BBegota_ul._%C5%BBurawia_24.JPG","alt":"A pale stone plaque with a carved Polish inscription fixed to the wall of a building."},"wiki":"Żegota"},
    "ww2-726": {"questions":["Only about 470 people were caught during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and all but 51 of them survived Theresienstadt because Danish officials kept asking the Germans about their fate.","A warning read out at the New Year service in Copenhagen's synagogue set off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that German police found few Jews when their roundup began on 1 October 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>rescue of the Danish Jews</b> was the flight of most of Denmark's Jews across the Øresund to neutral Sweden in October 1943, organised by Danes to forestall a German roundup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany had occupied Denmark in April 1940 but left its government in charge of domestic affairs, and until 1943 the country adopted no antisemitic measures such as marking Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strikes and sabotage grew after Allied victories, and on 29 August 1943 the government resigned rather than let German military courts try saboteurs, after which martial law was declared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In September the SS general Werner Best, the German civilian administrator, proposed using martial law to deport the Jews and Hitler approved, though Best then had second thoughts about the political cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 28 September the German shipping attaché Georg Ferdinand Duckwitz warned the Social Democrat leader Hans Hedtoft, who passed the news to the resistance and the Jewish community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The warning was read out the next day at the New Year service in the Copenhagen synagogue, and Jews scattered into homes, hospitals and churches while the Danish police largely refused to help the German hunt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the roundup began on the night of 1 October the police found few Jews, and fishermen went on to ferry some 7,200 Jews and about 700 non-Jewish relatives to Sweden, which had announced it would take them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The short crossing, a small community near the coast, a light occupation and German officials who made little effort to stop the escapes all helped, so conditions were far easier than in Poland or the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 470 Jews were still caught and sent to Theresienstadt, visited by Danish officials in June 1944, and all but 51 survived, largely because Danish officials kept pressing the Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A Danish historian has since questioned the heroic national story, arguing that the low priority of persecution in Denmark explains more than the rescue does and that the Jewish community itself spread the warning and paid for much of the flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“Rescue in Denmark,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/rescue-in-denmark. [Open access]","“Denmark,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/denmark. [Open access]","“Danes Help Jews Escape to Neutral Sweden,” <i>History Unfolded</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://newspapers.ushmm.org/events/danes-help-jews-escape-to-neutral-sweden. [Open access]","“Redningen av de danske jødene 1943,” <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/Redningen_av_de_danske_j%C3%B8dene_1943. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","“Righteous among the Nations,” Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, Yad Vashem, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/Microsoft%20Word%20-%205829.pdf. [Open access]","“Righteous Among the Nations,” <i>Encyclopaedia Judaica</i>, 2nd ed., Jewish Virtual Library, https://www.jewishvirtuallibrary.org/jsource/judaica/ejud_0002_0017_0_16756.html. [Open access]","“Theresienstadt,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/theresienstadt. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans move against Denmark's Jews only in 1943?","a":"Because until August 1943 the Danish government ran domestic affairs and had adopted no antisemitic measures, and its resignation and the martial law that followed removed that protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many of Denmark's Jews escape?","a":"Because they were warned in time, most lived close to a short sea crossing to Sweden, which agreed to take them, and the German authorities did little to stop them, though some helpers paid dearly, like Henry Thomsen, who died in Neuengamme for ferrying Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did most of those deported survive?","a":"Because Danish officials kept pressing the Germans about the people sent to Theresienstadt, and all but 51 of about 470 deportees lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rescue of the Danish Jews"},
    "ww2-727": {"questions":["All 117 inhabitants of the Dutch village of Nieuwlande, where every household hid a Jewish family or person, were named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Yad Vashem's commission in Jerusalem.","First-hand testimony from those rescued is required before Yad Vashem confers the title of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which by 2024 had been given to 28,707 people from 51 countries."],"abstract":"<b>Righteous Among the Nations</b> is the title that Yad Vashem, Israel's Holocaust memorial, gives to non-Jews who risked their lives to save Jews during the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name comes from Jewish tradition, from a Talmudic phrase holding that the righteous among the nations of the world have a place in the world to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law passed by the Knesset in 1953 created Yad Vashem and charged it with honouring the 'high-minded Gentiles who risked their lives to save Jews', without defining the term exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A commission chaired by Moshe Landau, a judge of Israel's Supreme Court, set the criteria, and an avenue where trees were planted for rescuers was opened in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those recognised receive a medal and a certificate of honour, their names are inscribed on the walls of honour in the Garden of the Righteous, and the award may be made after death, as it was for the Dutch businessman Jan Zwartendijk in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> To qualify, a rescuer must have been actively involved in saving Jews, must have risked life or liberty, and must have acted to save them rather than for money, conversion or adoption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commission asks for first-hand testimony from those rescued or for firm documents, and it weighs hard cases, such as diplomats protected by immunity, a rescuer who held antisemitic views, or Oskar Schindler, accused of misconduct before he planted his tree in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rescuers came from every background, from consuls issuing visas to a Haarlem watchmaker, Corrie ten Boom, who hid Jews in her home, and whole communities have been honoured, among them all 117 inhabitants of the Dutch village of Nieuwlande.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Organisations were remembered too, and in 1963 Yad Vashem invited Władysław Bartoszewski to plant an olive tree for Żegota, the Polish council that aided Jews in hiding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1 January 2024 the title had been awarded to 28,707 people from 51 countries, though how many Jews were saved by non-Jews in all remains unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“Righteous Among the Nations,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/righteous-among-the-nations. [Open access]","“Righteous among the Nations,” Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, Yad Vashem, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/Microsoft%20Word%20-%205829.pdf. [Open access]","“Righteous Among the Nations,” <i>Encyclopaedia Judaica</i>, 2nd ed., Jewish Virtual Library, https://www.jewishvirtuallibrary.org/jsource/judaica/ejud_0002_0017_0_16756.html. [Open access]","“Rescue,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/rescue. [Open access]","“The Council for Aid to Jews: Żegota,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/gallery/the-council-for-aid-to-jews-zegota. [Open access]","“Oskar Schindler,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/oskar-schindler. [Open access]","“Corrie ten Boom,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/corrie-ten-boom. [Open access]","“Jan Zwartendijk,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jan-zwartendijk. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the title leave out some people who helped Jews?","a":"Because the original motive must have been to save them, and help given for money, to convert Jews to Christianity or to adopt a child does not qualify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were rescuing diplomats a difficult case for the commission?","a":"Because men such as Aristides de Sousa Mendes and Sugihara had immunity and faced less danger, yet they disobeyed their governments and lost jobs or reputations, so the commission chose to honour them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the commission insist on testimony?","a":"Because the law never defined the term exactly, so the commission must establish both the rescuer's deeds and motives from survivors' accounts and corroborating documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/134583_avenue_of_the_righteous_among_the_nations_yad_vas_PikiWiki_Israel.jpg","title":"The Avenue of the Righteous, Yad Vashem","desc":"The avenue at Yad Vashem in Jerusalem, opened in 1962, where trees were planted beside signs naming individual rescuers and their countries.","credit":"Baruch Niv, Pikiwiki Israel, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:134583_avenue_of_the_righteous_among_the_nations_yad_vas_PikiWiki_Israel.jpg","alt":"Trees shading a sandy path in bright sun, with small markers at their feet and picnic benches to one side."},"wiki":"Righteous Among the Nations"},
    "ww2-728": {"questions":["Because circumcision marked Jewish men, the Jewish underground chose young women as its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who passed as Poles to smuggle arms and messages through the ghetto walls.","Some 40 to 50 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the news of mass murder from ghetto to ghetto after 1941, and only a handful of these young women survived the war."],"abstract":"The <b>kashariyot</b> were the couriers, nearly all of them young women, who travelled on illegal missions for the Jewish resistance in German-occupied Poland, Lithuania and parts of Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name comes from the Hebrew word kesher, ‘connection’, because they were the link that joined the walled ghettos to one another and to the world outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Using false papers, they carried underground newspapers, letters, money, forged identity cards, medicine, weapons and sometimes other Jews in and out of the ghettos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Most were already leaders in a Jewish youth movement or party, such as Dror, Ha-Shomer ha-Za'ir or the Bund, and most were in their late teens or early twenties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 40 to 50 women served the ghetto undergrounds as couriers, and only a handful of them survived the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The underground chose women on purpose, since circumcision marked Jewish men, and a young woman in the street drew less suspicion and was less likely to be seized for forced labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fluent Polish without a Yiddish accent mattered as much as looks, and many couriers had learned it in Polish state schools, to which Jewish families more often sent daughters than sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their work changed with German policy, from sustaining the ghettos to warning them of the mass killings, then smuggling arms for revolt and finally helping Jews to escape and hide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Women resisted inside the camps as well, and at Auschwitz Jewish women prisoners smuggled explosives from an armaments factory for the Sonderkommando revolt of October 1944 and were hanged for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their story long stood in the shadow of the armed fighters, and recent work has stressed how their youth movements and networks shaped what they could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Lenore J. Weitzman, “Kashariyot (Couriers) in the Jewish Resistance During the Holocaust,” <i>Shalvi/Hyman Encyclopedia of Jewish Women</i>, 27 February 2009, Jewish Women's Archive, https://jwa.org/encyclopedia/article/kashariyot-couriers-in-jewish-resistance-during-holocaust. [Open access]","“The Kashariyot (Couriers),” <i>The Holocaust: The Facts</i>, holocaust.com.au, https://holocaust.com.au/the-facts/resistance/the-kashariyot-couriers/. [Open access]","Joanna Sliwa, review of <i>The Light of Days: The Untold Story of Women Resistance Fighters in Hitler's Ghettos</i>, by Judy Batalion, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, November 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=57110. [Open access]","“Killing Center Revolts,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/killing-center-revolts. [Open access]","“Prisoner Mutinies,” Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/resistance/prisoner-mutinies. [Open access]","“Jewish Resistance against the Nazis during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-resistance-against-the-nazis-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were most of the couriers women?","a":"Because circumcision identified Jewish men, young men in the street were stopped more often and risked seizure for forced labour, so the underground deliberately recruited women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the couriers' mission change after June 1941?","a":"Because the mass shootings that followed the invasion of the Soviet Union showed the underground that the Germans meant to kill all Jews, so warning the ghettos became the most urgent task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the couriers' warnings often not believed?","a":"Because the Germans used secrecy and deception, telling families they were being resettled, and it was hard for anyone to accept that they would be killed for no reason at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-729": {"questions":["Prisoners seized under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wore jackets marked NN in camps such as Natzweiler-Struthof, and nearly 5,000 of the 7,000 people arrested under it were taken in France.","Keitel wrote that lasting intimidation required either death or a prisoner's silent disappearance, the reasoning behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Nuremberg tribunal later judged a war crime."],"abstract":"<b>Nacht und Nebel</b>, ‘Night and Fog’, was the code name of a decree of 7 December 1941, issued by Hitler and signed by Field Marshal Wilhelm Keitel, under which people accused of resisting the occupation were made to vanish without trace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was aimed at civilians in the occupied territories whose acts were held to endanger German forces, as sabotage grew after the invasion of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Military courts in the occupied countries were to try such cases only where a death sentence was passed and could be pronounced within 8 days of arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Everyone else was to be taken secretly to Germany and tried by special courts, and German offices were to answer every enquiry by saying that the state of the proceedings allowed no further information.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Keitel's covering letter of 12 December 1941 gave the purpose, that efficient and enduring intimidation could be achieved only by death or by measures that left the relatives and the population ignorant of the prisoner's fate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The decree was applied chiefly in occupied western Europe, and about 7,000 people were arrested under it, nearly 5,000 of them in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Acquitted or released prisoners were often handed straight to concentration camps such as Gross-Rosen and Natzweiler-Struthof, where they wore jackets marked N.N. and died at a very high rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An SS order of June 1943 banned such prisoners from writing, receiving mail or parcels, or being spoken of, so that relatives would be kept in uncertainty, and families were not told even of a death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree of 30 July 1944 widened the system, treating every violent act by a non-German in the occupied territories as terrorism to be handed to the Security Police and SD.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal judged the decree a war crime and found Keitel guilty on all four counts, and the later trial of Reich justice officials found the whole scheme illegal and cruel and held that some defendants had knowingly carried it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Night and Fog Decree,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/night-and-fog-decree. [Open access]","“Natzweiler-Struthof,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/natzweiler-struthof. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 7 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 870–73, document L-90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag07unit. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Justice Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), judgment, “Crimes under the Night and Fog Decree,” https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-III/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-III.pdf. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 578–80, document 1932-PS, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag04unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Keitel,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judkeite.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Night and Fog prisoners hidden even from their families?","a":"Because Keitel held that intimidation worked only through death or through measures that left relatives and the population ignorant of the prisoner's fate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hitler bypass the military courts in the occupied countries?","a":"Because he thought military justice too cumbersome and often too lenient to deter resistance, and wanted immediate and lasting intimidation of the population instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the decree used mostly in the west?","a":"Because it targeted the resistance in occupied western Europe, in Belgium, France, Luxembourg, Norway, Denmark and the Netherlands, where sabotage grew after mid-1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nacht und Nebel"},
    "ww2-730": {"questions":["Heroic Resistance films such as <i>La Libération de Paris</i> had run their course by the end of 1946, which suggests that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the myth of a wholly resistant France, held the screen only briefly.","A near-twin of the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, spelled with a t, was used in 1948 by a former resister to attack what he called the exploitation of the true Resistance by political parties."],"abstract":"<b>Résistancialisme</b> is the name given in 1987 by the historian Henry Rousso to the myth of a France that had been unanimously resistant during the German occupation of 1940 to 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An older word spelled with a t had served Vichy's defenders first, and in 1948 the abbé Desgranges, himself a former resister, attacked 'résistantialisme' as the exploitation of the true Resistance by political parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rousso traced the memory of Vichy through phases, from the mourning and purges of 1944 to 1954 to a long repression in which a Gaullist memory of the Resistance revived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On this reading the myth was strongest under de Gaulle's Fifth Republic from 1958 to 1969, when a fraternal liberation of France was celebrated and collaboration left out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The years 1971 to 1974 then broke the mirror as the repressed past returned and the myths ended, and from 1974 an obsessive phase followed, marked by the reawakening of Jewish memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The idea has been contested, and one historian of the Resistance argues that the French never really believed in a unanimously resistant France, the Resistance having been a minority without being marginal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The glorification was short-lived even on screen, since the cycle of heroic films that began with <i>La Libération de Paris</i> was exhausted by the end of 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The purges that Vichy's defenders denounced were also smaller than they claimed, with fewer than 9,000 summary executions before, during and after the Liberation in the 76 departments surveyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Critics of Rousso's book called it a faithful reflection of the dominant scholarship in the wake of Robert Paxton, and others questioned how a nation's collective memory could be defined at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later work has also set the experience of ordinary people under Vichy against the grand narratives of resistance, collaboration, heroism and guilt that dominated the argument over memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Laurent Douzou, “Le mythe du résistantialisme et la réception de <i>La France de Vichy</i> de Robert Paxton,” in “Occupation (France): Mémoires et débats,” <i>Encyclopædia Universalis</i>, https://www.universalis.fr/encyclopedie/occupation-france-memoires-et-debats/5-le-mythe-du-resistantialisme-et-la-reception-de-la-france-de-vichy-de-robert-paxton/. [in French] [Paywalled]","Laurent Douzou, “La mémoire de Vichy: de la Libération à la fin des années 1960,” in “Occupation (France): Mémoires et débats,” <i>Encyclopædia Universalis</i>, https://www.universalis.fr/encyclopedie/occupation-france-memoires-et-debats/2-la-memoire-de-vichy-de-la-liberation-a-la-fin-des-annees-1960/. [in French] [Paywalled]","Hervé Coutau-Bégarie, review of <i>Le syndrome de Vichy, 1944–1987</i>, by Henry Rousso, <i>Politique étrangère</i> 53, no. 3 (1988): 784, https://www.persee.fr/doc/polit_0032-342x_1988_num_53_3_3805_t1_0784_0000_2. [in French] [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Rioux, review of <i>L'épuration, 1943–1953</i>, by Herbert R. Lottman, <i>Annales</i> 43, no. 3 (1988): 703–4, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ahess_0395-2649_1988_num_43_3_283512_t1_0703_0000_002. [in French] [Open access]","Tanja Schönborn, review of the conference <i>Widerstand im Konzentrationslager: Formen, Voraussetzungen, Möglichkeiten und Verarbeitung</i>, Regensburg, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, November 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=45566. [in German] [Open access]","Marie-Claire Lavabre, “Du poids et du choix du passé: Lecture critique du ‘syndrome de Vichy’,” <i>Cahiers de l'Institut d'histoire du temps présent</i> 18 (1991): 177–85, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ihtp_0769-4504_1991_num_18_1_2200. [in French] [Open access]","Benedetta Carnaghi, review of <i>Vichy France and Everyday Life: Confronting the Challenges of Wartime, 1939–1945</i>, ed. Lindsey Dodd and David Lees, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2020, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=53631. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do two spellings of the word exist?","a":"Because the form with a t was a polemical term of 1948 aimed at the Resistance by Vichy's defenders, while the form with a c names the later historians' idea of a myth of unanimous resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the idea of the myth been questioned?","a":"Because the Resistance was a minority but not marginal, the heroic films faded by the end of 1946, and the French arguably never believed they had all resisted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the memory of Vichy change after 1971?","a":"Because, in the scheme set out in 1987, the years 1971 to 1974 broke the mirror: the repressed past returned and the myths ended, and an obsessive phase began in 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Résistancialisme"},
    "ww2-731": {"questions":["Called in Hebrew the Shoah, ‘catastrophe’, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed about 2.7 million Jews in five killing centres in occupied Poland, roughly a million of them at Auschwitz alone.","On 17 December 1942 the Allies publicly condemned what is now called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, declaring that Germany was carrying out Hitler's oft-repeated intention to exterminate the Jewish people in Europe."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust</b> was the systematic, state-sponsored persecution and murder of six million European Jews by Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators between 1933 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Hebrew it is called the Shoah, ‘the catastrophe’, a word in use even before the mass killing began in 1941, and in Yiddish the khurbn, ‘the destruction’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Persecution began inside Germany in 1933 with a boycott of Jewish businesses and laws that pushed Jews out of the civil service and the professions and, in 1935, out of citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Conquest from 1939 brought millions more Jews under German rule, and in occupied Poland they were marked with armbands, robbed, put to forced labour and shut into ghettos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most research holds that before late 1941 the aim was removal rather than murder, with schemes for a reservation near Lublin or on Madagascar, but during 1941 the leadership resolved to kill every Jew within its reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The killing took two main forms, mass shootings in more than 1,500 cities, towns and villages of occupied eastern Europe and poison gas, above all in five killing centres in occupied Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 2.7 million Jews were murdered in those centres, around one million of them at Auschwitz, while the rest were shot or died of hunger, disease and brutality in ghettos and camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 17 December 1942 the Allied governments declared in London, Moscow and Washington that the Germans were carrying into effect Hitler's intention to exterminate the Jewish people in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their statement followed a note of 10 December in which the Polish government in exile set out reports of the walling of the Warsaw ghetto and of deportations to Treblinka, Bełżec and Sobibór.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Nuremberg in 1946 the International Military Tribunal found the persecution proved in the greatest detail and cited Eichmann's own estimate that six million Jews had been killed, four million of them in the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Introduction to the Holocaust: What Was the Holocaust?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/introduction-to-the-holocaust. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “How Many People Did the Nazis Murder?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/documenting-numbers-of-victims-of-the-holocaust-and-nazi-persecution. [Open access]","David Engel, “Holocaust: An Overview,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/11. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","House of Commons, “United Nations Declaration,” <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 385, 17 December 1942, cols. 2082–87, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/dec/17/united-nations-declaration. [Open access]","Republic of Poland, Ministry of Foreign Affairs, <i>The Mass Extermination of Jews in German Occupied Poland: Note Addressed to the Governments of the United Nations on December 10th, 1942, and Other Documents</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1942), 3–9, https://archive.org/details/TheMassExterminationOfJewsInGermanOccupiedPoland. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2024), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “The Number of Victims,” <i>Auschwitz and Shoah</i>, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-and-shoah/the-number-of-victims. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Nazi Regime in Germany,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judnazi.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do historians date the Holocaust from 1933 when the mass killing began in 1941?","a":"Because the persecution that ended in murder started as soon as the Nazis took power. A state boycott of Jewish businesses in April 1933 was followed by laws driving Jews from the civil service and the professions, and in 1935 the Nuremberg Laws took away their citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The museum in Washington therefore dates the Holocaust 1933 to 1945, while the deliberate murder of all Jews, the 'Final Solution', ran from 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before late 1941, most research holds, Nazi planners still imagined removing Jews to a reservation near Lublin or on Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do we know that six million Jews were killed?","a":"Because the figure is built from many kinds of evidence that agree. The museum in Washington calculates it from surviving German reports and records, from population studies before and after the war, from records kept by Jews themselves and from the reports of resistance groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The transports to the killing centres are among the best-documented parts, which is why their toll of about 2.7 million is known with some precision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Nuremberg the tribunal cited Eichmann's own estimate of six million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the Allies know during the war what was happening?","a":"Yes, in outline. On 10 December 1942 the Polish government in exile sent the Allied governments a note describing the sealing of the Warsaw ghetto and the deportation of its people to Treblinka, Bełżec and Sobibór, where, it feared, all had been killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A week later Anthony Eden read to the House of Commons a joint declaration, issued in London, Moscow and Washington, that Germany was carrying out Hitler's intention to exterminate the Jews of Europe, and promised retribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust"},
    "ww2-732": {"questions":["Founded in Lublin in 1944 to gather survivors' testimony, the Central Jewish Historical Commission was an early institution of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, becoming the Jewish Historical Institute in 1947.","Because no written order from Hitler to murder all Jews has ever been found, much of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has argued over how and when the decision for mass murder was taken."],"abstract":"<b>Holocaust historiography</b>, the writing of the history of the Holocaust, began while the killing was still going on, among the victims themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Warsaw Ghetto the historian Emanuel Ringelblum's secret archive, Oyneg Shabes, gathered testimony, essays and everyday objects, and from 1942 its aim was to document the destruction of Polish Jewry and send the evidence abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several hundred diaries survive from ghettos and hiding places, and even from the crematoria of Auschwitz-Birkenau, where three Jewish prisoners buried their notes in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1944 the Central Committee of Polish Jews set up a Central Jewish Historical Commission in Lublin to collect survivors' accounts and evidence for the prosecution of German war criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1946 it held about 2,000 personal accounts and thousands of documents, and in 1947 it became the Jewish Historical Institute in Warsaw, keeper of the recovered Ringelblum Archive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The German paper trail formed the basis of the case against the Nazi leaders at Nuremberg, and the trial documents and survivors' testimony became the foundation of the historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word Holocaust itself came into common use in the United States only in the 1960s, after the trial of Adolf Eichmann, while in Hebrew the event had been called the Shoah since before the mass killing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much argument has turned on how the decision for mass murder was taken, because no written order from Hitler for the murder of all Jews has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some historians hold that the leadership decided in Berlin and passed the order down by word of mouth, though they differ over whether victory, frustration in the Soviet campaign or the American entry into the war was the trigger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Others stress that local initiatives in the occupied east and self-made pressures such as food shortages and fear of epidemics drove the radicalisation, in a regime whose officials worked towards what they took to be the Führer's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Kassow, “Ringelblum, Emanuel,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/998. [Open access]","Alexandra Garbarini, “Holocaust: Holocaust Diaries,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/226. [Open access]","Emanuel Ringelblum Jewish Historical Institute, “History,” https://www.jhi.pl/en/about-the-institute/history. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “How Many People Did the Nazis Murder?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/documenting-numbers-of-victims-of-the-holocaust-and-nazi-persecution. [Open access]","David Engel, “Holocaust: An Overview,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/11. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2024), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Jews in the ghettos risk their lives to write things down?","a":"Because they wanted the record to outlive them. A discovered diary could cost the writer his life, and Avraham Tory in the Kovno ghetto wrote that he had overcome the fear of death that came with every page.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ringelblum's archive first hoped to shape postwar Jewish life, and after the mass killing began it set out to document the destruction and get the evidence abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the historical commission founded so soon after liberation?","a":"Because survivors' memories and the evidence lying in the ruins had to be gathered at once. The commission set up in Lublin in 1944 aimed to collect survivors' accounts and to provide proof for the prosecution of German war criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within 2 years it held about 2,000 personal accounts, dozens of diaries, books found in the ghetto ruins and the Ringelblum Archive itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is there still debate about how the Final Solution was decided?","a":"Because no explicit written order for it has been found, and in all likelihood none was ever issued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians therefore reconstruct the decision from other evidence, and some date it to the summer of 1941, others to the autumn or to the American entry into the war in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Others point to local initiatives and to shortages and fears of epidemics that pushed policy step by step towards murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Holocaust studies"},
    "ww2-733": {"questions":["At Kishinev in April 1903 a mob roused by a ritual-murder charge killed 49 Jews, a pogrom that made the city a symbol of the violent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of tsarist Russia.","The stab-in-the-back legend, blaming Jews and communists for Germany's defeat in 1918, gave postwar <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a new myth, though German Jews had served loyally in the war."],"abstract":"<b>Antisemitism</b>, hostility to Jews as a group, took a new political form in 19th-century Europe, as one state after another granted Jews equality under the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Emancipation let Jews enter the professions, the civil service and politics, and some who felt left behind by rapid economic change accused them of displacing others and blamed them for the ills of modern society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Germany, where the constitution of 1871 completed the emancipation of about 512,000 Jews, the journalist Wilhelm Marr argued in a pamphlet of 1879 that Jewish influence was a matter of race, not religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> More than 250,000 Germans signed a petition against Jewish equality in 1880 and 1881, and antisemitic parties appeared in Germany and then in Austria-Hungary, where a party founded in 1883 won 17 seats in the Hungarian parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Russian Empire waves of riots called pogroms struck Jewish towns from 1881, and at Kishinev in April 1903 a mob roused by a ritual-murder charge killed 49 Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The First World War gave antisemites new myths, above all the stab-in-the-back legend that Jews and communists had betrayed Germany and Austria to defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The charge was false, since German Jews had served loyally and out of proportion to their numbers, but the defeated military leadership spread it to escape blame for its own policies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The revolution in Russia and the brief communist regimes in Bavaria and Hungary fed the myth of a Jewish Bolshevism, and in Germany a single antisemitic league soon claimed more than 200,000 members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The new states of eastern Europe turned on their Jewish minorities, and in September 1920 Hungary became the first country in interwar Europe to set quotas for Jews in higher education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armies on several sides of the Russian Civil War carried out pogroms in Ukraine whose victims have never been counted, and in Germany the Nazi party's programme of 1920 declared that no Jew could be a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Antisemitism in History: The Era of Nationalism, 1800–1918,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism-in-history-the-era-of-nationalism-1800-1918. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Antisemitism in History: World War I,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism-in-history-world-war-i. [Open access]","Leon Volovici, “Antisemitic Parties and Movements,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/3. [Open access]","John Klier, “Pogroms,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/260. [Open access]","Wolf Moskovich, “Kishinev,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/366. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Antisemitismus,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Kaiserreich (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/kaiserreich/antisemitismus. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Antisemitismus,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Weimarer Republik (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/antisemitismus. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Nazi Regime in Germany,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judnazi.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did hostility to Jews grow just as they gained equal rights?","a":"Because emancipation brought Jews into fields that had been closed to them. Once they could own land, enter the civil service and serve as officers, some who felt left behind by rapid change came to believe that Jews were taking places that belonged to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wider voting rights and mass schooling then let antisemitic parties, and mainstream parties too, use antisemitic rhetoric to win votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Germany the stock-market crash of 1873 was also blamed on Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What was new about the antisemitism of the 1870s and 1880s?","a":"It claimed to rest on race rather than religion. Marr's pamphlet of 1879 grounded hostility to Jews in race, and propaganda began to treat difference as a fixed, inherited inferiority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It also became organised politics, with the first antisemitic parties in Germany around 1880 and an International Anti-Jewish Congress at Dresden in 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Such movements set out to limit or revoke the rights Jews had gained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the First World War make antisemitism worse?","a":"Because defeat seemed to the losing nations to need an explanation, and Jews were made the scapegoat. The stab-in-the-back legend blamed Jews and communists for the defeat, and it was believed although it was entirely untrue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Germany leaflets claimed from the autumn of 1918 that Jews had dodged the front and profited from the war, while a Jewish veterans' league founded in 1919 tried, mostly in vain, to answer them with lists of Jewish soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Antisemitism"},
    "ww2-734": {"questions":["Göring's conference after the November pogrom of 1938 pushed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> further, fining German Jews one billion Reichsmarks and barring them from running shops and businesses.","Nazi <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1938 made Jews register property worth over 5,000 Reichsmarks, stamped a 'J' in their passports and gave them the extra names Israel and Sara."],"abstract":"Nazi Germany's <b>anti-Jewish legislation</b> was the mass of laws, decrees and regulations by which the regime stripped Germany's Jews of their rights and their livelihoods between 1933 and the outbreak of war in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 400 such measures were issued, many as national laws and many more on the initiative of state, regional and city officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party programme of 1920 had already declared that no Jew could be a member of the nation, and once in power the party made persecution official state policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 1 April 1933 stormtroopers and SS men stood outside Jewish shops, surgeries and law offices for a nationwide boycott, which was called off within days because it won little public support and alarmed foreign opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regime turned instead to law, and the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service of 7 April 1933 allowed 'non-Aryan' officials to be dismissed, the first form of the so-called Aryan paragraph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Further laws that spring set a quota for Jewish pupils and students and cut Jews out of medicine and law, and the Nuremberg Laws of September 1935 took away their citizenship and forbade marriage with non-Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1938 Jews had to register all property worth more than 5,000 Reichsmarks, and those whose first names were not held to be Jewish had to add Israel or Sara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the pogrom of 9 and 10 November 1938 Göring told a meeting of ministers that the problem was mainly an economic one, and German Jews were fined one billion Reichsmarks and barred from running shops and businesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Further decrees shut Jews out of schools, universities, cinemas, theatres and sports grounds, and forced them to hand over their gold, silver and jewels to the state without compensation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal at Nuremberg found that by the autumn of 1938 Nazi policy aimed at the complete exclusion of Jews from German life, and the pressure was meant to drive them abroad, though few countries would take many refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Antisemitic Legislation 1933–1939,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/law-for-the-restoration-of-the-professional-civil-service. [Open access]","Burkhard Asmuss, “Geschäftsboykott 1933,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung/antisemitismus. [in German] [Open access]","Oliver Schweinoch, “Novemberpogrome 1938,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2021), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung-und-verfolgung/novemberpogrom-1938. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Nazi Regime in Germany,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judnazi.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), document 1816-PS, stenographic report of the meeting on the Jewish question under Göring, 12 November 1938, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1816-ps.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime switch from boycotts to laws so early?","a":"Because the boycott of 1 April 1933 did not have the effect the Nazis wanted. Most Germans responded coolly, some showed solidarity with Jewish neighbours, and sharp reactions abroad threatened German trade and prestige, so it was called off after a day and declared over 3 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The leadership then began to settle the so-called Jewish question by law, starting with the civil service law of 7 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the civil service law have such a bland name?","a":"Because it was meant to look unremarkable. Its supporters claimed that the Weimar civil service had been filled with political appointees and that the law would restore professionalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In fact its third article let the state remove Jews, and its fourth removed officials who could not be counted on to defend the Nazi state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exemptions for war veterans, insisted on by President Hindenburg, were dropped after his death in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the measures of November 1938 focus on money?","a":"Because the leadership treated the next step as an economic one. At the meeting of 12 November 1938 Göring complained that smashing Jewish shops harmed the German economy, refused to let insurers' payments reach Jews, and settled on a collective fine of one billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The tribunal at Nuremberg noted that the fine and the seizure of Jewish assets came when rearmament had put the treasury in difficulties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Anti-Jewish laws"},
    "ww2-735": {"questions":["With about 3.3 million in Poland and 3.0 million in the Soviet Union on the eve of the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was concentrated overwhelmingly in the east of the continent.","Speaking Yiddish in great numbers and living in shtetls across the east, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost two of every three of its members within little more than a decade of 1933."],"abstract":"<b>European Jewry</b>, the Jewish communities of Europe, had lived on the continent for more than 2,000 years, and in 1933 it numbered about 9.5 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were 1.7 per cent of Europe's population but more than 60 per cent of the world's Jews, who were then estimated at 15.3 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most lived in eastern Europe, with about three million in Poland, 2.5 million in the European part of the Soviet Union and 756,000 in Romania, while Germany had about 525,000 and Hungary 445,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The west had smaller communities, about 300,000 in Britain, 250,000 in France and 156,000 in the Netherlands, and Greece, with about 73,000, had the largest in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Communities differed widely in work, religious practice and integration into national life, and in the east many Jews lived in shtetls, market towns where they were set apart from peasant neighbours by religion, occupation, language and culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On the eve of the Second World War Poland had about 3.3 million Jews and the Soviet Union about 3.0 million, and after the Soviet annexations of 1939 and 1940 the Soviet figure reached 5.1 to 5.2 million by June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polish Jews, counted at 3.1 million in 1931, were nearly a tenth of Poland's people and the largest Jewish community in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yiddish was the everyday language of much of this world, with approximately 13 million speakers on the eve of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the 1930s hostility grew, from quotas and segregated 'ghetto benches' in Polish universities to Polish parties that demanded the Jews' mass emigration and a Romanian league that sought to strip them of their political rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Within little more than a decade most of Europe was conquered, occupied or annexed by Nazi Germany and its partners, and two out of every three European Jews were dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Population of Europe in 1933: Population Data by Country,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-population-of-europe-in-1933-population-data-by-country. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Life in Europe before the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-life-in-europe-before-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Mark Tolts, “Population and Migration: Population since World War I,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/19. [Open access]","Gershon Bacon, “Poland: Poland from 1795 to 1939,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/16. [Open access]","Samuel Kassow, “Shtetl,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/27. [Open access]","Dovid Katz, “Language: Yiddish,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/235. [Open access]","Leon Volovici, “Antisemitic Parties and Movements,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did most of Europe's Jews live in the east?","a":"Because the great centres of Jewish settlement lay in the lands of old Poland and the Russian Empire. In 1933 about 5.5 million of Europe's 9.5 million Jews lived in Poland and the Soviet Union alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shtetl grew out of the private market towns of the old Polish nobility, and in the 19th century Jews also moved in large numbers into towns in Ukraine, Hungary, Bessarabia and Bucovina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Poland's Jewish population grow more slowly between the wars?","a":"Partly because Jews married later. Polish Jews numbered 2.8 million in 1921 and 3.1 million in 1931, but growth was slowing, among other reasons because the average age at marriage had risen among Jews compared with Christians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jews had also reached the modern pattern of low death rates and low birth rates earlier than their neighbours, with longer life expectancy than the population as a whole in 1931 and 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the number for the Soviet Union jump between 1939 and 1941?","a":"Because the Soviet Union took in new territory with large Jewish populations. After eastern Poland, the Baltic states, Bessarabia and northern Bucovina were incorporated in 1939 and 1940, the Soviet Jewish population rose from about 3.0 million to 5.1 to 5.2 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That figure included perhaps 200,000 to 300,000 refugees who had fled the German-occupied part of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"History of the Jews in Europe"},
    "ww2-736": {"questions":["From late 1939 decrees in the General Government forced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the age of ten to wear a white armband with a blue Star of David and registered men for forced labour.","About 400,000 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were among the 1.4 million Polish citizens deported to the Soviet interior in 1939–1941, a harsh exile that proved to be their best chance of survival."],"abstract":"<b>Polish Jews</b>, the largest Jewish community in Europe, were split between two occupiers in September 1939, when Germany invaded Poland on the 1st and the Soviet Union attacked from the east on the 17th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly two million came under German rule, while as many as 1.3 million lived in the lands the Soviet Union annexed, joined there by about 350,000 refugees from the German zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Soviet rule Jewish businesses and larger homes were nationalised, and about 400,000 Jews were among the 1.4 million Polish citizens deported to the Soviet interior, where perhaps a third of the deportees died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harsh as it was, that deportation turned out to offer Polish Jews their best chance of survival, for conditions under German rule were far more severe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany annexed western Poland, Łódź included, outright and made the rest, with Warsaw, Kraków and Lublin, into the General Government under Hans Frank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 21 September 1939 Heydrich ordered the Einsatzgruppen to concentrate Jews in cities on railway lines and to set up councils of Jewish elders to carry out German orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1939 SS and police units murdered some 60,000 Polish politicians, priests, teachers and doctors as well as Jews, and in Łódź the four main synagogues were burned and dynamited in November 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Between December 1939 and March 1940, 200,000 of the 600,000 Jews of the annexed lands were driven into the General Government, many towards a planned reservation around Nisko near Lublin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Decrees of late 1939 blocked Jewish bank accounts, made every Jew over ten wear a white armband with a blue Star of David, and made men aged 14 to 60 register for forced labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jewish schools were closed and property seized, and from 1940 Jews were shut into ghettos, yet some two million Polish Jews lived under Nazi rule for more than 2 years before the mass killing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["David Engel, “Poland: Poland since 1939,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/18. [Open access]","David Engel, “Holocaust: An Overview,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/11. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “‘Give Me Your Children’: Voices from the Lodz Ghetto,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/give-me-your-children-voices-from-the-lodz-ghetto. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Warsaw,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2024), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), document 3363-PS, Heydrich to the chiefs of the Einsatzgruppen, 21 September 1939, 97–101, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Robert Moses Shapiro, “Łódź,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/472. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was deportation to the Soviet interior a better fate than staying?","a":"Because it took Jews beyond the reach of the German killing that began in 1941. The Soviet authorities deported about 400,000 Jews among 1.4 million Polish citizens, and conditions were so severe that perhaps a third died of cold, hunger or disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No one could foresee it at the time, but this exile gave Polish Jews their best chance of survival, since conditions under German rule were far worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans set up Jewish councils?","a":"Because they wanted Jews to carry out German orders themselves. Heydrich's letter of 21 September 1939 ordered a Council of Jewish Elders in each community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 28 November 1939 Frank ordered all Jewish communities in the General Government to form councils, and although the order spoke of elections, the members were in fact appointed by the Germans and answered for carrying out their instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In only a few ghettos did the councils win their people's trust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the Germans plan mass murder in Poland from the start?","a":"Most research says not yet. In 1939 and 1940 their plans centred on removing Jews to a remote reservation, near Lublin or later on Madagascar, rather than on killing them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The persecution of those years, with its terror, plunder, forced labour, badges and ghettos, was lethal for many, but whether it was a planned prelude to the killing is debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The killing of Polish Jews in the death camps began in March 1942 with the Jews of Lublin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"History of the Jews in Poland"},
    "ww2-737": {"questions":["Foreshadowed in Heydrich's express letter of 21 September 1939, the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appeared at Piotrków that October, though no central order ever required one in every town.","Named after a word first used for a Venetian Jewish quarter of 1516, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were emptied from 1942 in what the Germans called liquidation, their people shot or deported."],"abstract":"<b>Nazi ghettos</b> were districts of cities and towns in which Nazi Germany and its allies forced Jews to live apart from non-Jews during the Second World War, and more than 1,300 of them were set up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all lay in occupied Poland, the Baltic states and the occupied Soviet Union, and there were none in western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word was old, going back to a Jewish quarter created by the Venetian authorities in 1516, but the Nazi ghettos bore little resemblance to their forerunners in purpose or in conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Warsaw's packed more than 350,000 people into fewer than 70 streets behind high walls, while most others were closed by barbed wire or wooden fences and some in the Lublin district were left open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jews there were made to register, wear a badge and work as forced labourers, and a Jewish council appointed by the Germans had to carry out their orders and, in the end, help with the deportations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first Nazi order to mention ghettos was Reinhard Heydrich's express letter of 21 September 1939, which told the Einsatzgruppen to gather the Jews of occupied Poland into cities on railway lines as a step towards a secret ultimate goal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest ghetto followed in October 1939 at Piotrków, yet no central order to confine all Jews was ever issued, and local German officials decided where and when to make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ghettos of 1939 and 1940 were meant as holding places before some further removal, at a time when plans looked to a Jewish reservation near Lublin or on Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Those set up in the occupied Soviet Union from June 1941, and the short-lived ghettos of Hungary in 1944, were holding pens for Jews whose murder had already been decided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1942 the ghettos were emptied in what the Germans called liquidation, and the killing centres were deliberately placed near the large ghettos of occupied Poland or on easy railway routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Ghettos,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ghettos. [Open access]","David Silberklang, “Ghettos: Establishment of Ghettos,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/223. [Open access]","Lea Preiss, “Ghettos: Life in Ghettos,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/224. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Ghettos,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/ghettos. [in German] [Open access]","Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: United States Government Printing Office, 1946), document 3363-PS, Heydrich to the chiefs of the Einsatzgruppen, 21 September 1939, 97–101, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Introduction to the Holocaust: What Was the Holocaust?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/introduction-to-the-holocaust. [Open access]","David Engel, “Holocaust: An Overview,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/11. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans create ghettos before they had decided on mass murder?","a":"Because in 1939 and 1940 the plan was to remove the Jews rather than kill them, and ghettos were meant as holding places until that removal. Heydrich's letter of 21 September 1939 ordered Polish Jews gathered into cities on railway lines as a step towards a secret ultimate goal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The schemes of those years looked to a Jewish reservation near Lublin or on Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Local officials also made ghettos to steal Jewish property, seize housing, control the black market or, as they claimed, contain epidemics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Were all the ghettos walled like Warsaw's?","a":"No. A solid wall was found in only a few places, including Warsaw, Kraków, Tarnów and Kremenets, while most ghettos in eastern Europe were closed with barbed wire or wooden fences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some, called open ghettos, had no fence at all, as in parts of the Lublin district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some lasted for years and others for only weeks or days, and the conditions in them ranged from the deadly, tightly sealed ghettos of Warsaw and Łódź to the far looser open ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the ghettos change once the killing began?","a":"They became holding pens for the murder that followed. Ghettos made in the occupied Soviet Union from June 1941 were from the start part of the plan to kill their inmates, and those in Hungary in 1944 lasted only 2 to 6 weeks before deportation to Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1942 the Germans emptied the older ghettos in operations they called liquidation, sending most people to killing centres sited near the big ghettos or on easy railway routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Jewish ghettos established by Nazi Germany"},
    "ww2-738": {"questions":["Adam Czerniaków, head of the Jewish council of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, took his own life on 23 July 1942 rather than pass on the German order for mass deportation to Treblinka.","Hidden in caches found in 1946 and 1950, the Oyneg Shabes archive recorded life in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where some 83,000 people died of hunger and disease by mid-1942."],"abstract":"The <b>Warsaw Ghetto</b> was the largest of the Nazi ghettos, the district of occupied Warsaw in which the Germans confined the city's Jews from November 1940 until its destruction in the spring of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the war Warsaw had more than 350,000 Jews, about 30 per cent of its people and the largest Jewish community in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To create the ghetto the Germans forced 113,000 Poles and 138,000 Jews from their homes, and on 16 November 1940 they sealed it behind a wall about 3 metres (10 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With Jews brought in from nearby towns its population rose above 400,000, crowded on average 7.2 to a room in about 3.4 square kilometres (1.3 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Jewish council under the engineer Adam Czerniaków had to run it and carry out German orders, and its taxes and forced-labour levies made it the target of bitter resentment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> German food allotments were far too small to sustain life, and between 1940 and mid-1942 some 83,000 people died of starvation and disease in spite of large-scale smuggling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From November 1940 an underground archive called Oyneg Shabes, led by the historian Emanuel Ringelblum, collected everything from diaries to tram tickets as a record of ghetto life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 22 July 1942 the mass deportations began, and by 21 September about 265,000 Jews had been sent to the killing centre at Treblinka while some 35,000 were killed inside the ghetto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Czerniaków refused to pass on the deportation order and took his own life on 23 July, writing that he was being asked to take part in the murder of the children of his people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When the SS came to clear the rest of the ghetto on 19 April 1943 its Jews fought back, and the illustrated report of the SS commander Jürgen Stroop bore the title 'The Jewish Ghetto in Warsaw no longer exists'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Warsaw,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Deportations to and from the Warsaw Ghetto,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/deportations-to-and-from-the-warsaw-ghetto. [Open access]","Antony Polonsky, “Warsaw,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/487. [Open access]","Susanne Härtel, “Ghetto Warschau,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/ghetto-warschau. [in German] [Open access]","Republic of Poland, Ministry of Foreign Affairs, <i>The Mass Extermination of Jews in German Occupied Poland: Note Addressed to the Governments of the United Nations on December 10th, 1942, and Other Documents</i> (London: Hutchinson, 1942), 4–9, https://archive.org/details/TheMassExterminationOfJewsInGermanOccupiedPoland. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Samuel Kassow, “Czerniaków, Adam,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/2216. [Open access]","Samuel Kassow, “Ringelblum, Emanuel,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/998. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many people die in the ghetto before any deportations?","a":"Because the Germans deliberately gave it too little food. In 1941 the average inmate lived on about 1,125 calories a day, and the German ration for Jews was set at just 181.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Polish government's 1942 note reported about a pound of bread per person a week, prices ten times higher than outside the walls, and an annual death rate of 13 per cent in the winter of 1941–1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smuggling kept the toll from rising still further, but some 83,000 people died before the deportations began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Jewish council so resented?","a":"Because the Germans used it to carry out their orders, and its own choices bore hardest on the poor. Its taxes fell heavily on the poorest people, wealthy Jews could buy exemption from forced labour, and corruption was widespread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Germans understood that by working through the council they could turn Jewish anger away from themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Czerniaków nonetheless refused to hand over the deportees in July 1942 and killed himself rather than do so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the ghetto's people keep a secret archive?","a":"Because they meant to leave a record for the future. Emanuel Ringelblum founded Oyneg Shabes in November 1940, and its members gathered refugee accounts, essays, diaries and everyday objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Once the mass killing began, the aim became to document the destruction of Polish Jewry and send the evidence abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Two of its caches were dug up after the war, in 1946 and 1950, and they remain an invaluable source on life in the ghetto and German policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Warsaw_Ghetto_wall_1941.jpg","title":"At the ghetto wall, February 1941","desc":"Jews pass in front of the ghetto wall at 8 Tłomackie Street in Warsaw in February 1941, in a colour photograph taken by a German soldier.","credit":"Wehrmacht soldier, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Jewish Historical Institute, Warsaw). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warsaw_Ghetto_wall_1941.jpg","alt":"People in winter coats walk along a pavement beside a tall brick wall in a city street in 1941."},"wiki":"Warsaw Ghetto"},
    "ww2-739": {"questions":["The 'Eldest of the Jews' gambled that useful workers would be spared, and by 1943 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had more than 70,000 people labouring in 93 workshops for the German economy.","The last ghetto left in occupied Poland, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was liquidated in August 1944, when more than 65,000 of its people were deported to Auschwitz-Birkenau."],"abstract":"The <b>Łódź Ghetto</b> was the Jewish quarter that the Germans created in the Polish city of Łódź, which they renamed Litzmannstadt, and it lasted from 1940 until August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Łódź, the centre of Poland's textile industry, had the country's second-largest Jewish community and was annexed to Germany in 1939 as part of the Warthegau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ghetto was announced on 8 February 1940 in the poor districts of Bałuty and the Old Town and sealed on 30 April, about 4 square kilometres (1.5 square miles) of mostly wooden houses without sewers or running water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Official records counted 160,320 Jews there in June 1940, and deportations from Vienna, Prague, Luxembourg, Germany and the small towns nearby brought about 200,000 people into it in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the most completely sealed of all the ghettos, with little chance to smuggle food or to hide outside, and only a handful of Jews are known to have hidden on the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Germans made Mordechai Chaim Rumkowski 'Eldest of the Jews', the only channel between them and the ghetto, and the ghetto's own money carried his portrait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He gambled that the Germans would not kill useful workers, and by 1943 more than 70,000 people, 85 per cent of the ghetto, worked in 93 workshops making uniforms and other goods for German firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rations fell to about 600 calories a day by the summer of 1942, and hunger, typhus and tuberculosis killed more than 43,000 people, more than a fifth of all who passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1942 about 70,000 Jews and about 4,300 Roma were sent to be gassed at Chełmno, and in September Rumkowski begged parents to give up their children to the deportation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In August 1944 the last ghetto in occupied Poland was liquidated and more than 65,000 people were sent to Auschwitz, Rumkowski among them, and the Red Army found 877 Jews alive there in January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lodz,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lodz. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “‘Give Me Your Children’: Voices from the Lodz Ghetto,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/give-me-your-children-voices-from-the-lodz-ghetto. [Open access]","Robert Moses Shapiro, “Łódź,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/472. [Open access]","Robert Moses Shapiro, “Rumkowski, Khayim Mordkhe,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/2218. [Open access]","Susanne Härtel, “Ghetto Lodz,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/ghetto-lodz. [in German] [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Ghettos,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/ghettos. [in German] [Open access]","Lea Preiss, “Ghettos: Life in Ghettos,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/224. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Łódź Ghetto last longer than the others?","a":"Because its workshops made it valuable to the Germans. Rumkowski built it into a labour complex, and by 1943 more than 70,000 people worked in 93 factories, one of the largest and most profitable complexes in the German economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the deportations of 1942, which took mainly people who could not work, the ghetto came to resemble a forced-labour camp, and that gave it a time-limited right to exist in German eyes until the order to clear it in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the people of Łódź not smuggle food as Warsaw's did?","a":"Because the ghetto was sealed far more tightly. Unlike the walled ghettos with gates and gaps, Łódź was hermetically closed and strictly patrolled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> There were few chances to smuggle, escape or keep contact with the outside, and the ghetto's own money was worthless beyond the fence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet hunger and disease killed people at similar rates in sealed Łódź and in Warsaw, where smuggling flourished, which shows how much depended on German policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"How is Rumkowski judged?","a":"Very differently. Some have argued that he was a power-hungry man who collaborated with the Germans, and in 1942 he sought to remove the old, the sick and thousands of children under ten so that the rest might be saved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Others point out that the workshops kept 68,500 Jews alive until August 1944, when the Red Army was only about 110 kilometres (70 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He died at Auschwitz with most of those he had ruled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-133-0703-28%2C_Polen%2C_Ghetto_Litzmannstadt%2C_Br%C3%BCcke.jpg","title":"A footbridge over Hohensteiner Strasse, 1941","desc":"A wooden footbridge carries ghetto inmates over Hohensteiner Strasse, today Zgierska Street, a road that ran through the ghetto but was kept outside it.","credit":"Zermin, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-133-0703-28, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-133-0703-28,_Polen,_Ghetto_Litzmannstadt,_Br%C3%BCcke.jpg","alt":"A crowded wooden footbridge spans a street fenced off with barbed wire, with another footbridge visible in the distance."},"wiki":"Łódź Ghetto"},
    "ww2-740": {"questions":["Claiming that Jews were its carriers, German officials used the fear of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to justify the ghettos, first declaring the future Warsaw Ghetto a restricted epidemic area in November 1939.","In the Lwów ghetto the microbiologist Ludwik Fleck devised a way to make a vaccine against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the urine of patients sick with the louse-borne disease."],"abstract":"Epidemic <b>typhus</b> is a disease caused by the bacterium <i>Rickettsia prowazekii</i> and carried by the human body louse, which multiplies in conditions of filth, overcrowding and cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After an incubation of about 2 weeks it brings high fever, headache, a spreading rash and, in the worst cases, delirium and death within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Known since ancient times, it was endemic in the countryside of eastern Europe until the 1950s and flourishes wherever people live crowded, hungry and without sanitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had killed some five million people after the First World War, and German fear of it fed the old antisemitic idea of Jews as carriers of disease, which Nazi ideology turned into the claim that Jews were the disease itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1941 the chief health officer of occupied Poland told an audience of doctors that the Jews in the ghetto must either be starved to death or shot, and he was applauded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Disease was a pretext for the ghettos themselves, and the district that became the Warsaw Ghetto was first declared a restricted epidemic area that German soldiers were forbidden to enter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside, the Germans deliberately left the ghettos short of housing, food and medical care, and in Łódź, where rations sank to about 600 calories a day, typhus and diarrhoea spread among the starving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Warsaw an epidemic grew from a handful of cases in 1940 to about 3,500 new cases in October 1941, and one recent analysis puts its dead at between 16,000 and 22,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then, just as winter should have spread it faster, the epidemic collapsed, and studies credit the delousing, health teaching and diagnosis organised in secret by the ghetto's own doctors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Lwów ghetto the microbiologist Ludwik Fleck devised a way to make a typhus vaccine from patients' urine, and in Vilna the Germans themselves sent thousands of doses to stop an outbreak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Lewi Stone, Daihai He, Stephan Lehnstaedt and Yael Artzy-Randrup, “Extraordinary Curtailment of Massive Typhus Epidemic in the Warsaw Ghetto,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 30 (2020): eabc0927, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7455495/. [Open access]","Hermann Feldmeier, “Typhus in the Warsaw Ghetto 1940–1942: How an Epidemic Increased Exponentially, Was Abated and Eventually Disappeared,” <i>One Health &amp; Implementation Research</i> 1 (2021): 51–55, https://doi.org/10.20517/ohir.2021.03. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Ghettos,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ghettos. [Open access]","Susanne Härtel, “Ghetto Warschau,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum, 2015), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/ghetto-warschau. [in German] [Open access]","Lea Preiss, “Ghettos: Life in Ghettos,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/224. [Open access]","Robert Moses Shapiro, “Łódź,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/472. [Open access]","Ilana Löwy, “Fleck, Ludwik,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i> (New York: YIVO Institute for Jewish Research, 2010), https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/2226. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did typhus spread so fast in the ghettos?","a":"Because the ghettos gave its louse everything it needed. The body louse multiplies under poor hygiene, filth, overcrowding and cold, which were exactly the ghetto's conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Warsaw some 445,000 people were shut into 3.4 square kilometres, and German rations began at about 200 calories a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Starvation weakened people first, and the epidemic followed hard on its heels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans talk so much about epidemics?","a":"Because fear of disease gave them a pretext. German thinking about hygiene was shaped by the antisemitic idea that Jews carried disease, and the fear of typhus was used to justify sealing Jews into ghettos and later destroying them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In November 1939 part of Warsaw's old town, where mostly Jews lived, was declared an epidemic area closed to German soldiers, the first step towards the ghetto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ghetto wall was later called the epidemic wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the Warsaw epidemic brought to a halt?","a":"By the ghetto's own people, working in secret. The number of new cases peaked in October 1941 and then fell almost as fast as it had risen, reaching none at all by July 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recent studies credit better diagnosis, health education and measures against body and head lice, organised without the knowledge of the Germans, and the work of the ghetto doctors and the much-criticised ghetto leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-134-0782-35%2C_Polen%2C_Ghetto_Warschau%2C_Fleckfieber-Warnung.jpg","title":"A quarantine notice in the Warsaw Ghetto, May 1941","desc":"A boy looks out through the round window of a door beneath a German and Polish notice forbidding anyone to enter or leave a house struck by the disease.","credit":"Ludwig Knobloch, Bundesarchiv, Bild 101I-134-0782-35, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-134-0782-35,_Polen,_Ghetto_Warschau,_Fleckfieber-Warnung.jpg","alt":"A boy's face peers through a round glass pane in a wooden door, under a printed sign in German and Polish."},"wiki":"Typhus"},
    "ww2-741": {"questions":["In Łódź the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Rumkowski pursued rescue through labour, while in Warsaw its chairman took his own life the day after the great deportations began.","Running food supply, work, medical care and a Jewish police inside the ghettos, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still divides opinion because its members faced impossible moral dilemmas under German orders."],"abstract":"A <b>Judenrat</b>, or ‘Jewish council’, was the body of Jewish officials that the German occupiers set up in a community or ghetto to see that Nazi orders and regulations were carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reinhard Heydrich ordered them in occupied Poland on 21 September 1939, calling for a Council of Jewish Elders of up to 24 men drawn from the remaining influential personalities and rabbis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each council was to be made fully responsible for carrying out every instruction, warned of the severest measures for any sabotage, and told to take a census of the Jews in its area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The model was not new, for Eichmann and his colleagues had already worked through the Jewish community in Vienna, a system his office praised for shifting most of the work onto the Jews themselves, and it suited the occupiers that the Jewish officials, not the Germans, drew the anger of the ghetto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 400 councils were set up in occupied Poland alone, for communities of anything from 500 people to almost 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the large ghettos of Łódź, Warsaw, Kraków and Lwów they ran food supply, work, medical care, schooling and a Jewish police, almost all of it subject to German approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some chairmen complied in the hope of saving part of the population, and in Łódź Mordechai Chaim Rumkowski pursued 'rescue through labour' and urged the residents to report for deportation, in September 1942 even calling on them to give up their young children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Others refused, and in Warsaw, where the council had to assemble 6,000 people a day for the transports, its chairman Adam Czerniaków took his own life on 23 July 1942, the day after the deportations began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jewish police who enforced the councils' orders were already hated inside the ghettos, a reputation born of the community's own experience rather than of later judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their role remains controversial, and the Holocaust Memorial Museum stresses both the impossible moral dilemmas their members faced and the services they provided under brutal conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Councils (Judenraete),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-councils-judenraete. [Open access]","European Holocaust Research Infrastructure, “Jewish Administrations,” <i>EHRI Online Course in Holocaust Studies</i>, Ghettos under Nazi Rule, https://training.ehri-project.eu/node/11. [Open access]","<i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 97–99, document 3363-PS, Heydrich to the chiefs of the Einsatzgruppen, 21 September 1939, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Ghettos,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/ghettos. [in German] [Open access]","Jadwiga Biskupska, review of <i>Warsaw Ghetto Police: The Jewish Order Service during the Nazi Occupation</i>, by Katarzyna Person, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, August 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=56798. [Open access]","District Court of Jerusalem, judgment in the trial of Adolf Eichmann, part 9, para. 76, “The Madagascar Plan,” Nizkor Project text, https://phdn.org/archives/www.ess.uwe.ac.uk/genocide/Eichmanni.htm. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Final Solution: Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/final-solution-overview. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans want Jewish councils at all?","a":"Because it made the administration of the ghettos easier: Heydrich's order made each council fully responsible for carrying out every instruction, and Jewish officials, not Germans, then drew the anger of the ghetto population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some council chairmen urge cooperation?","a":"Because they believed that complying, and above all making the ghetto useful to the German economy through work, would ensure that at least part of the population survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the ghetto police so widely hated?","a":"Because they conscripted people for labour, beat and threatened ghetto Jews, escorted them to imprisonment and deportation and demanded bribes, so their reputation was made inside the ghetto itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/46/Kovno_Judenrat_1943.jpg","title":"The Kovno ghetto council, 1943","desc":"A meeting of the Jewish council of the Kovno ghetto in Lithuania in 1943, with its chairman Elchanan Elkes in the middle.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain (PD-anon-70-EU), via Wikimedia Commons (United States Holocaust Memorial Museum). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kovno_Judenrat_1943.jpg","alt":"Five men in suits sit side by side behind a long table covered with papers and microphones, photographed in black and white."},"wiki":"Judenrat"},
    "ww2-742": {"questions":["Because British sea power controlled the routes to the Indian Ocean, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was abandoned in September 1940 once the German air offensive against Britain had failed.","After the failure of the Nisko reservation of 1939, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imagined Europe's Jews isolated as forced labourers in an SS police state off the coast of Africa."],"abstract":"The <b>Madagascar Plan</b> was the scheme, taken up by the Nazi leadership in the summer of 1940, to deport the Jews of Europe to Madagascar, a French island colony off the south-east coast of Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea was far older, for European antisemites had been suggesting it for decades, and in 1937 the Polish government sent a commission to the island to study the settlement of Jews there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An officer of Eichmann's office later told the Nuremberg tribunal that policy until 1940 had been planned emigration, followed by concentration in ghettos and, from early 1942, by planned extermination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The first territorial scheme was the Nisko and Lublin Plan of autumn 1939, under which Eichmann sent transports of Jews from Vienna, Moravská Ostrava and Katowice to a marshy site on the San.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those transports were soon stopped and the camp at Nisko was closed in April 1940, officially because of 'technical difficulties'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The defeat of France made Madagascar seem within reach, and on 24 June 1940 Heydrich wrote to Ribbentrop proposing it as a 'territorial final solution' for all the Jews under German rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eichmann's office drew up a detailed plan that summer, received by the Foreign Ministry in mid-August, to deport four million Jews within 4 years to a 'police state' supervised by the SS.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Jerusalem court that later tried Eichmann found that the deportees would have lived in complete isolation, at forced labour such as draining swamps and building roads, paid for with their own confiscated property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Everything depended on defeating Britain, whose fleet controlled the sea routes, and when the air offensive against Britain failed all work on the plan was stopped in September 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It mattered because it kept the planners thinking of deportation out of Europe, though in the end the deportations went not to an island but to the extermination camps in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Madagascar Plan,” Yad Vashem, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/Microsoft%20Word%20-%206635.pdf. [Open access]","Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Nisko and Lublin Plan,” Yad Vashem, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/Microsoft%20Word%20-%205965.pdf. [Open access]","Terezín Initiative Institute, “The Territorial Solution to the Jewish Question,” holocaust.cz, https://holocaust.cz/en/history/final-solution/general-2/the-territorial-solution-to-the-jewish-question/. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Madagaskar-Plan,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/madagaskar-plan. [in German] [Open access]","District Court of Jerusalem, judgment in the trial of Adolf Eichmann, part 9, para. 76, “The Madagascar Plan,” Nizkor Project text, https://phdn.org/archives/www.ess.uwe.ac.uk/genocide/Eichmanni.htm. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 3 January 1946, 355–58, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Final Solution: Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/final-solution-overview. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fall of France revive the idea?","a":"Because Madagascar was a French colony, and the armistice seemed to offer the chance of obtaining the island from France in the peace treaty that Germany expected to draw up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the plan never carried out?","a":"Because it depended on defeating Britain, whose navy controlled the sea routes, and once the air offensive against Britain failed in 1940 the work on the plan was stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was it no humane alternative to murder?","a":"Because the written plan foresaw an SS police state, forced labour and complete isolation for four million people, which the German museum's account calls in effect a giant ghetto and an island of death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Madagascar Plan"},
    "ww2-743": {"questions":["At Nuremberg Otto Ohlendorf testified that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been ordered orally at Pretzsch, before the invasion, to kill the Jews and Soviet commissars in their areas.","First used in Austria and the Sudetenland in 1938, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed the army into Poland in 1939 and shot thousands of Jews and members of the Polish elite."],"abstract":"The <b>Einsatzgruppen</b> were mobile units of the Security Police and the SD, the SS intelligence service, that followed the German army into invaded countries, and they are best known for shooting Jews in the occupied Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Special units of this kind were first used in Austria and the Sudetenland in 1938 and in Czechoslovakia in 1939, and in Poland in 1939 they shot thousands of Jews and tens of thousands of the Polish elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their formal tasks were to identify and neutralise enemies of German rule, seize important sites, prevent sabotage and recruit collaborators and informers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the invasion of the Soviet Union four groups, lettered A to D, were attached to the three army groups and to the 11th Army, with only about 3,000 men among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their commanders were often lawyers with doctorates, and one of them, Otto Ohlendorf, led Einsatzgruppe D from June 1941 to June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ohlendorf told the Nuremberg tribunal that the commanders had been ordered orally before the invasion, at a conference at Pretzsch, that the Jews and Soviet political commissars in their areas were to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the pretext of military necessity they organised the shooting of more than half a million people in the first 9 months of the war, the great majority of them Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not act alone, for Waffen-SS and Order Police units, the Wehrmacht, Romanian forces and local auxiliaries helped them to identify, guard and kill their victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their own reports went to Berlin and became evidence after the war, as when Sonderkommando 4a reported 33,771 Jews shot in Kyiv on 29 and 30 September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shooting remained the preferred method in the occupied Soviet territories even after gas vans were added, and at least 1.5 million and possibly more than 2 million Jews died there in shootings or gas vans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Einsatzgruppen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/einsatzgruppen. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/de/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 3 January 1946, 315–22, testimony of Otto Ohlendorf, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 7 January 1946, 463–64, 476–78, report of Einsatzgruppe A to 15 October 1941 (L-180) and testimony of Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Einsatzgruppen Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 18, 43, 145–49, indictment, opening statement and Operational Situation Report USSR No. 106, 7 October 1941, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV.pdf. [Open access]","Gerhard Altmann, “Einsatzgruppen,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 11 April 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/einsatzgruppen. [in German] [Open access]","Yad Vashem, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Chapter 4: Mogilev and the Final Solution,” lesson plan, https://www.yadvashem.org/education/educational-materials/lesson-plans/mogilev/final-solution.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could so few men kill so many people?","a":"Because they had help: Waffen-SS and Order Police battalions, Wehrmacht units, Romanian forces and local auxiliaries supplied the manpower, and local helpers identified victims as well as killing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Himmler order gas vans for the groups?","a":"Because the shootings were costly and troubled the shooters, and Ohlendorf said Himmler wanted the men, mostly married, spared from aiming at women and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the army have to be told of their task?","a":"Because the killings took place in the army's operational areas, so commanders were instructed to support the groups, and the report of Einsatzgruppe A called its cooperation with the army generally good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Einsatzgruppen"},
    "ww2-744": {"questions":["More than half the victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were Jews deported from Hungary as aliens who could not prove citizenship and then marched from Kolomyia into German-held Ukraine.","Coming weeks after the shootings widened to women and children, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marked the turn to destroying entire Jewish communities in the occupied Soviet territories."],"abstract":"The <b>Kamianets-Podilskyi massacre</b> was the shooting of 23,600 Jews near Kamianets-Podilskyi, Kamenets-Podolsk in Russian, a town in German-occupied western Ukraine, on 26–28 August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the time it was the largest massacre of Jews that the Nazis had carried out anywhere in German-occupied Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than half of the victims were Jews deported from Hungary, Germany's ally, and the rest were local Jews, about two-thirds of the town's Jewish community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was carried out by detachments of the Einsatzgruppen and troops under Friedrich Jeckeln, the Higher SS and Police Leader for the south, helped by locally recruited auxiliaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure of 23,600 comes from Jeckeln's own report, and the Holocaust Memorial Museum calls the action the first large-scale mass murder in pursuit of the Final Solution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The road to it began in Budapest, where after Hungary entered the war in June 1941 the agency for foreign nationals decided to deport 'alien' Jews, most of them Polish and Russian Jews but also refugees from western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hungarian Jews who could not prove their citizenship were taken as well, and whole communities of Transcarpathia were deported, crossing the border at about 1,000 people a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end of August some 18,000 Jews had been handed over to the SS, and from Kolomyia they were marched, often still in families, to Kamianets-Podilskyi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The massacre came weeks after the shootings had widened from Jewish men of military age to women and children, the escalation that turned the killing into the destruction of whole communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the following summer a German field police officer stationed there could boast in a letter to his general that of its 24,000 Jews only a disappearing percentage was left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Kamenets-Podolsk,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kamenets-podolsk. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Massacre at Kamenets-Podolsk,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> timeline, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline-event/massacre-at-kamenets-podolsk. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/de/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Kamenets-Podolski,” Yad Vashem, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/Microsoft%20Word%20-%206423.pdf. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Einsatzgruppen Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 425–26, letter of a field police officer, 21 June 1942 (NO-5655), quoted in the judgment, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV.pdf. [Open access]","Yad Vashem, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Chapter 4: Mogilev and the Final Solution,” lesson plan, https://www.yadvashem.org/education/educational-materials/lesson-plans/mogilev/final-solution.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Jews from Hungary killed in Ukraine?","a":"Because the Hungarian authorities deported 'alien' Jews, and many Hungarian Jews who could not prove citizenship, across the border and handed them over to the Germans, who marched them to the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the massacre seen as a turning point?","a":"Because it killed whole families on an unprecedented scale weeks after the shootings widened from men to women and children, and it is called the first large-scale mass murder in pursuit of the Final Solution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the death toll rest on a German report?","a":"Because the 23,600 figure is Jeckeln's own, and the Germans kept no exact count of the shootings and destroyed nearly all such reports before the war ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Friedrich_Jeckeln_Funkspruch_29._August_1941.jpg","title":"Jeckeln's radio message, 29 August 1941","desc":"The radio message of 29 August 1941 in which Friedrich Jeckeln's staff reported the action at Kamenets-Podolsk completed.","credit":"Friedrich Jeckeln, public domain (PD-GermanGov), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Jeckeln_Funkspruch_29._August_1941.jpg","alt":"A typed German signal form with a ruled grid, a stamped number, the handwritten letters KTB and a received stamp dated 30 August 1941."},"wiki":"Kamianets-Podilskyi massacre"},
    "ww2-745": {"questions":["The Germans blamed Kyiv's Jews for Soviet mines that wrecked the city centre, and used the explosions as the pretext for the massacre at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the end of September 1941.","Until the autumn of 1943 the ravine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remained a killing site for Roma, psychiatric patients and prisoners of war, and prisoners were made to burn the bodies before the Germans left."],"abstract":"<b>Babi Yar</b>, Babyn Yar in Ukrainian, is a ravine on what was then the edge of Kyiv, where on 29 and 30 September 1941 SS and police units shot 33,771 Jews, one of the largest massacres of the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> German forces entered Kyiv on 19 September, by which time only about 60,000 of the city's 160,000 Jews remained, most of them women, children, the elderly and the sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Explosions from mines left by the retreating Soviets destroyed the German headquarters and much of the city centre, and the Germans used the sabotage as the pretext for murdering the Jews who remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a meeting on 27 September attended by the town commandant, Wehrmacht officers, the Secret Field Police and Einsatzgruppe C, the SS leaders Jeckeln, Rasch and Blobel decided to kill the city's Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ravine stayed a killing site until the autumn of 1943 for Jews, Roma, psychiatric patients, Soviet prisoners of war and other civilians, some 100,000 people in all by Soviet estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Posters put up across the city by the Ukrainian militia ordered all Jews to appear at 8:00 AM on Monday 29 September, and it was announced that they were to be moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than 30,000 people came instead of the 5,000 or 6,000 the Germans had expected, believing until the moment of their execution that they were being resettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were made to hand over papers and valuables and to undress, and Sonderkommando 4a under Paul Blobel, with two detachments of Police Regiment South, shot them in small groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Einsatzgruppe C's own report admitted that even 75,000 killings of this kind could not solve what it called the Jewish problem, a sign that the shooting squads were reaching their limits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1943 prisoners were forced to dig up and burn the bodies to hide the crime, and the Soviet monument of 1976 named 100,000 victims without saying that at least a third of them were Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Kyiv and Babyn Yar,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kiev-and-babi-yar. [Open access]","Heinke Lang, “Massenmorde von Babi Jar 1941,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/massenmorde-von-babi-jar-1941. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Einsatzgruppen Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 18, 43, 145–49, indictment, opening statement and Operational Situation Report USSR No. 106, 7 October 1941, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Einsatzgruppen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/einsatzgruppen. [Open access]","Wolfgang Scheffler, “Zur Entstehungsgeschichte der ‚Endlösung‘,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i> B 43 (1982), Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/archiv/532459/zur-entstehungsgeschichte-der-endloesung/. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/de/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Gerhard Altmann, “Einsatzgruppen,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 11 April 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/einsatzgruppen. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many of Kyiv's Jews obey the posters?","a":"Because the order said they were to be moved, and the German report itself records that more than 30,000 came and believed in their resettlement until the moment they were shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the bodies dug up and burned in 1943?","a":"Because the Red Army was approaching Kyiv and the Germans wanted to hide the mass murder, so they forced prisoners from the nearby Syrets camp to exhume and burn the remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Babi Yar become a struggle over memory?","a":"Because for decades there was no memorial, and the Soviet monument of 1976 spoke of 100,000 victims without saying that at least a third were Jews killed in September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Babyn_Yar._General_view_of_the_place_of_massacres.jpg","title":"The ravine at Babyn Yar, 1943","desc":"The ravine at the edge of Kyiv where the massacres took place, photographed in 1943, with five people standing on the snow at its floor.","credit":"Unknown photographer, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons (National Museum of the History of Ukraine in the Second World War). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babyn_Yar._General_view_of_the_place_of_massacres.jpg","alt":"A deep ravine with steep, almost bare slopes, its floor covered in snow, with five small figures in winter clothes standing at the bottom."},"wiki":"Babi Yar"},
    "ww2-746": {"questions":["A week after returning from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, held by Schenckendorff for Army Group Centre's rear area, a Wehrmacht major ordered the murder of the Jews in his district.","Officers of the 691st Infantry Regiment attended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on fighting partisans in Belarus, and in October 1941 one of its companies shot the Jews of Krucha unaided."],"abstract":"The <b>Mogilev Conference</b> was a 3-day meeting on fighting partisans that opened on 24 September 1941 at Mogilev in occupied Belarus, called by General Max von Schenckendorff, commander of the rear area of Army Group Centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than sixty Wehrmacht commanders and officers attended, together with SS and police officers who had already led shootings of Jews, among them Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski and Arthur Nebe of Einsatzgruppe B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Regimental commanders and an officer from each battalion took part, and more than half of all the participants were captains or lieutenants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its slogan was 'The Jew is a partisan and the partisan is a Jew', although there was in fact little partisan organisation or activity in Belarus in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The participants discussed the 'Jewish question' as part of anti-partisan warfare and then went out to watch demonstration operations in nearby towns and villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The summary sent down to company level told soldiers that whoever fought ruthlessly and mercilessly, with complete disregard for any personal surge of emotion, acted correctly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A week after the conference a Wehrmacht major, Commichau, ordered the murder of the Jews in the area under his command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 10 October 1941 the 3rd Company of the 691st Infantry Regiment, whose regimental commander had attended, killed at least 150 Jewish men, women and children at Krucha without help from any other organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between June 1941 and May 1942 the rear area reported 80,000 'partisan' deaths against 1,094 German losses, killings justified by an essentially invented danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bach-Zelewski told the Nuremberg tribunal that anti-partisan operations were carried out mainly by Wehrmacht formations because the SS and police were too few, which is why the conference is read as a deliberate effort to bring the army's manpower to bear against the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Yad Vashem, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Chapter 4: Mogilev and the Final Solution,” lesson plan, https://www.yadvashem.org/education/educational-materials/lesson-plans/mogilev/final-solution.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Resources on the German Military and the Holocaust: Case Study, Army Group Center (Rear),” https://vault.ushmm.org/adaptivemedia/rendition/id_a4b8bce8339bfd46832d96b08553f4e9b6b70383. [Open access]","Jeff Rutherford, review of <i>Marching into Darkness: The Wehrmacht and the Holocaust in Belarus</i>, by Waitman Wade Beorn, <i>Michigan War Studies Review</i> 2014-082, https://michiganwarstudiesreview.com/2014-082.htm. [Open access]","Irina Rebrova, review of <i>Marching into Darkness: The Wehrmacht and the Holocaust in Belarus</i>, by Waitman Wade Beorn, <i>sehepunkte</i> 16, no. 5 (2016), https://www.sehepunkte.de/2016/05/28205.html. [Open access]","Waitman W. Beorn, “A Calculus of Complicity: The Wehrmacht, the Anti-Partisan War, and the Final Solution in White Russia, 1941–42,” <i>Central European History</i> 44, no. 2 (2011): 308–37, abstract at Northumbria University Research Portal, https://researchportal.northumbria.ac.uk/en/publications/a-calculus-of-complicity-the-wehrmacht-the-anti-partisan-war-and-/. [Paywalled]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 7 January 1946, 476–78, testimony of Erich von dem Bach-Zelewski, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why equate Jews with partisans when there were few partisans?","a":"Because the equation turned the murder of civilians into a military duty, which brought the Wehrmacht's prestige and above all its manpower to a killing programme the SS could not carry out alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did every unit that heard the message take part?","a":"No: at Krucha the 1st Company's commander refused to let his men join, and two soldiers asked to be relieved from the firing party, which shows how much depended on local leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it hard to say how typical these units were?","a":"Because the units studied were rear-area security and occupation troops, a minority of the Eastern Army, and survivors often could not tell soldiers from SS or police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mogilev Conference"},
    "ww2-747": {"questions":["The national investigation that reported in 2002 found that about 40 Poles carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Germans taking part in gathering and abusing the victims in the square.","The culmination of a wave of 1941 pogroms that included Radziłów and Wąsocz, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led to a trial at Łomża in May 1949 at which 21 men were accused."],"abstract":"The <b>Jedwabne pogrom</b> was the murder of the Jews of Jedwabne, a small town in the Białystok region of north-eastern Poland, by their Polish neighbours and peasants from nearby villages on 10 July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After hours of torture the Jews, with refugees from other places, were driven into a barn and burned alive, and people found hiding, small children among them, were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of the dead range from 900 to 1,600, while a partial exhumation in 2001 found two graves holding perhaps 300 to 400 and 35 to 40 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came a few weeks after the German army took the region from the Soviets and was the culmination of a wave of increasingly violent pogroms there, at Wąsocz and Radziłów among other places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German part is not fully explained, but the national investigation reported in 2002 that about 40 Poles did the killing, the Germans taking part when the Jews were gathered and abused in the square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Local violence of this kind was what the Germans wanted, for the report of Einsatzgruppe A for 1941 says that native antisemitic forces were induced to start pogroms so that the world would see the population acting first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same report admits that this proved very difficult, and a study of the summer of 1941 found pogroms in only 9 per cent of the localities of eastern Poland it examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It concluded that pogroms were most likely where non-Jews saw a Jewish threat to their political dominance, and that Jedwabne, more polarised than its neighbours, was an extreme case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the occupied east the Germans also relied on local auxiliaries to round up and shoot Jews, and many of the killers and their victims knew one another as neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A court at Łomża sentenced 11 of 21 accused men to prison and one to death in 1949, but the event was little known until a book of 2000 set off Poland's most significant public argument about its past since 1989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Rafał Żebrowski, “Anniversary of the Jedwabne Pogrom,” Jewish Historical Institute, Warsaw, https://www.jhi.pl/en/articles/anniversary-of-the-jedwabne-pogrom,3752. [Open access]","Rachel Rothstein, review of <i>Intimate Violence: Anti-Jewish Pogroms on the Eve of the Holocaust</i>, by Jeffrey S. Kopstein and Jason Wittenberg, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, July 2019, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52448. [Open access]","Piotr Wróbel, review of <i>Niechciana debata</i>, by Magdalena Nowicka-Franczak, <i>Spory o Grossa</i>, by Paweł Dobrosielski, and <i>Po Jedwabnem</i>, by Piotr Forecki, H-Poland, H-Net Reviews, October 2019, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=54626. [Open access]","Anja Hennig, “Jedwabne: Nach der Wahrheit die geistige Befreiung?,” <i>Berliner Osteuropa Info</i> 19 (2003): 56–57, https://www.oei.fu-berlin.de/media/publikationen/boi/boi_19/11_hennig.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 7 January 1946, 463–64, report of Einsatzgruppe A to 15 October 1941 (L-180), Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-07-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/de/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Einsatzgruppen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/einsatzgruppen. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans want local people to start pogroms?","a":"Because, as Einsatzgruppe A's report put it, it had to be shown to the world that the native population took the first action, and harsh measures by the Security Police were apt to stir even German circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did pogroms break out in some towns but not others?","a":"Because, the study of 1941 found, they were most likely where non-Jews saw Jews, especially Jewish nationalists, as a threat to their political dominance, rather than simply out of revenge for the Soviet occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the number of victims become so contested?","a":"Because estimates of 900 to 1,600 dead sat beside a halted exhumation that found far fewer, and critics used the gap to try to undermine the whole account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Jedwabne_Z-pomnik.jpg","title":"The memorial at Jedwabne","desc":"The memorial of 2001 at Jedwabne, on the site of the barn, with its inscription in Polish, Hebrew and Yiddish.","credit":"Fczarnowski, CC BY-SA 3.0 or GFDL, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jedwabne_Z-pomnik.jpg","alt":"A tall concrete block with inscriptions in three scripts, a dark stone slab set into one side and small stones laid along its top, standing in a field."},"wiki":"Jedwabne pogrom"},
    "ww2-748": {"questions":["Einsatzgruppen, police battalions, Wehrmacht units and local auxiliaries all took part in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the shootings that killed about a third of all the Jewish victims of the Holocaust.","German cartridge cases found beside unmarked pits in Ukraine help to locate the sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which almost one and a half million Ukrainian Jews died between 1941 and 1944."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust by bullets</b> is a name for the mass shootings in which Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators murdered Jews in the territories seized from the Soviet Union from the summer of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As many as 2 million Jews were killed in these shootings and related massacres, and shooting accounted for about a third of all the Jewish victims of the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The usual pattern was to round up a community, march it to the outskirts, force the victims to dig a pit or take them to one already dug, and shoot the men, women and children into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The killers included the Einsatzgruppen, Order Police battalions, Waffen-SS and Wehrmacht units and local auxiliaries, and Romanian forces did the same in the territory they controlled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Ukraine alone almost one and a half million Jews were killed, the great majority by firing squads and only a small minority after deportation to the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Unlike the killing centres, the shootings were often carried out in broad daylight and within sight and earshot of local people, some of whom were requisitioned to dig or fill the pits or to carry victims and materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method was costly in men, ammunition and transport, and worry about its effect on the shooters led to the gas vans, yet shooting stayed the preferred method in the occupied Soviet territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Germans kept no exact count, destroyed nearly all their reports and tried to hide the graves, and Soviet postwar accounts spoke of murdered peaceful Soviet citizens rather than of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Since 2004 a French research team, Yahad-In Unum, has gone village by village in search of eyewitnesses, who were children or teenagers at the time, and has located hundreds of execution sites and mass graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> German cartridge cases found around such graves, like the 310 recovered in 2005 at Khvativ in the Lviv region, are evidence that German units carried out the executions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/de/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Mémorial de la Shoah, “The Holocaust by Bullets,” exhibition, 20 June 2007 – 6 January 2008, https://memorialdelashoah.org/upload/minisites/ukraine/en/en_exposition1.htm. [Open access]","Mémorial de la Shoah, “Father Patrick Desbois and Yahad-In Unum,” <i>The Holocaust by Bullets</i> exhibition, https://memorialdelashoah.org/upload/minisites/ukraine/en/en_exposition5-desbois.htm. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “310 Discharged German Manufactured Bullet Casings Found at a Mass Execution Site,” Yahad-In Unum Collection, EHRI Portal, https://portal.ehri-project.eu/units/us-005578-irn36907-irn42868. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Einsatzgruppen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/einsatzgruppen. [Open access]","Yad Vashem, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Chapter 4: Mogilev and the Final Solution,” lesson plan, https://www.yadvashem.org/education/educational-materials/lesson-plans/mogilev/final-solution.html. [Open access]","Irina Rebrova, review of <i>Marching into Darkness: The Wehrmacht and the Holocaust in Belarus</i>, by Waitman Wade Beorn, <i>sehepunkte</i> 16, no. 5 (2016), https://www.sehepunkte.de/2016/05/28205.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the shootings not be kept secret?","a":"Because they were carried out near towns, often in daylight and within sight and earshot of local people, some of whom the Germans requisitioned to dig the pits or carry the victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did shooting continue after the gas vans arrived?","a":"Because the vans struggled on rough roads, needed scarce fuel and parts, killed large groups far more slowly, and the men disliked removing the bodies and cleaning them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the number of victims only an estimate?","a":"Because the Germans kept no exact count and destroyed nearly all their reports, so totals rest on the few surviving reports and on demographic studies of the Jewish population before and after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in Belarus"},
    "ww2-749": {"questions":["An SS officer told the Nuremberg tribunal that the words <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were Eichmann's disguise for biological annihilation, the stage that followed emigration and ghettos and lasted until October 1944.","Historians still debate exactly when Hitler decided on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though most place the decision in 1941, after the invasion of the Soviet Union and the first mass shootings."],"abstract":"The <b>Final Solution</b>, in full the 'final solution of the Jewish question', was the Nazi leaders' euphemism for the deliberate and systematic murder of the Jews of Europe, carried out from 1941 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It replaced the policies meant to drive Jews out of the Reich, which an officer of Eichmann's office described at Nuremberg as planned emigration until 1940 and concentration in ghettos until early 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eichmann told that officer that the words disguised the planned biological annihilation of the Jews, a stage that lasted until Himmler ordered the destruction stopped in October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its means were shooting and gassing, and SS and police murdered nearly 2,700,000 Jews in the killing centres alone, part of the six million Jews killed in the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Wannsee Conference of 20 January 1942 Heydrich gathered state secretaries and party and SS officials to coordinate a genocide already under way, in which over 500,000 Jews had been shot or gassed in Poland and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It is not known exactly when Hitler decided to murder all of Europe's Jews, but the decision was probably made in 1941, in connection with the invasion of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By August 1941 the SS and police units in the occupied Soviet Union had moved from shooting Jewish men of military age to massacring whole communities regardless of age or sex, after Himmler ordered Jewish women killed in the Pripyat marshes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 31 July 1941 Göring charged Heydrich with preparing 'a complete solution of the Jewish question in the German sphere of influence in Europe', extending his earlier task of emigration and evacuation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much recent research sees a step-by-step process of several decisions, with the murder of Soviet Jews decided in July or August 1941 and that of all European Jews dated to the autumn or, on one view, to December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An older German study placed every essential decision between March and November 1941, when Hitler believed victory over the Soviet Union was close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Final Solution: Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/final-solution-overview. [Open access]","<i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 525–26, document 710-PS, Göring to Heydrich, 31 July 1941, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag03unit. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 3 January 1946, 355–58, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Wannsee-Konferenz,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/wannsee-konferenz. [in German] [Open access]","Wolfgang Scheffler, “Zur Entstehungsgeschichte der ‚Endlösung‘,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i> B 43 (1982), Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/archiv/532459/zur-entstehungsgeschichte-der-endloesung/. [in German] [Open access]","Bogdan Musial, “The Origins of ‘Operation Reinhard’: The Decision-Making Process for the Mass Murder of the Jews in the Generalgouvernement,” trans. William Templer, Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, Yad Vashem, 1–2, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/microsoft%20word%20-%203222.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mass Shootings of Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/de/article/mass-shootings-of-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Yad Vashem, International School for Holocaust Studies, “Chapter 4: Mogilev and the Final Solution,” lesson plan, https://www.yadvashem.org/education/educational-materials/lesson-plans/mogilev/final-solution.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Nazi leaders use a euphemism?","a":"Because the words hid what was meant: Eichmann explained to his subordinate that the planned biological annihilation of the Jews was disguised by the concept and wording 'final solution'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the date of the decision still uncertain?","a":"Because it is not known exactly when Hitler decided, and the evidence points to a process of several steps across 1941 rather than one order whose date can be fixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the invasion of the Soviet Union matter here?","a":"Because within weeks the shootings there widened to whole communities, and the decisive months fell while Hitler expected a quick victory in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Final Solution"},
    "ww2-750": {"questions":["One side of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds that no single comprehensive order existed, and that the murder programme grew out of separate killing actions until the spring of 1942.","Studies that date every decisive order to 1941, when Hitler expected to defeat the Soviet Union, take the side of his aims in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over how the genocide began."],"abstract":"The <b>functionalism–intentionalism debate</b> is the argument over how the Holocaust came about: whether it followed from Hitler's long-held intention to destroy the Jews, or grew out of the workings of the Nazi state at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The intentionalist side treats antisemitism and Hitler's aims as the cause that, by one route or another, led to the murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The functionalist side looks instead at decrees, measures and officials, and at killing actions begun separately in different places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both read the same record, which shows policy moving from planned emigration before 1940 to ghettos and then, from early 1942, to planned extermination, as an officer of Eichmann's office told the Nuremberg tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The dispute is over mechanism, not fact, for Hitler's surviving wartime words reveal his will to annihilation in general terms even though no written order from him survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A leading functionalist essay of 1977 argued that there had been no single comprehensive order, and that the murder programme grew out of separate actions until the spring of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading the killing arose partly as a way out of a dead end the regime had manoeuvred itself into, when its planned deportations of Jews to the east could not be carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A German study of 1982 took the other view, that every essential decision fell between March and November 1941, when Hitler expected victory, and that once Soviet Jews were being killed the rest was a matter of timing and organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later work blurred the line, describing several decisions between the summer and December 1941 in which the initiative of regional SS leaders, such as Odilo Globocnik at Lublin, also played a part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some cultural historians have since moved beyond both schools, asking how the Nazis came to imagine a world without Jews rather than tracing chains of cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Martin Broszat, “Hitler und die Genesis der ‚Endlösung‘,” <i>Vierteljahrshefte für Zeitgeschichte</i> 25, no. 4 (1977): 739–75, at 753, 763, PDF hosted by PHDN, https://phdn.org:443/negation/irving/VfZ-1977okt-v25n4-BROSZAT-HITLER_UND_DIE_GENESIS_DER_ENDLOSUNG.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Wolfgang Scheffler, “Zur Entstehungsgeschichte der ‚Endlösung‘,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i> B 43 (1982), Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/archiv/532459/zur-entstehungsgeschichte-der-endloesung/. [in German] [Open access]","Amos Goldberg, review of <i>A World without Jews: The Nazi Imagination from Persecution to Genocide</i>, by Alon Confino, H-Judaic, H-Net Reviews, January 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=41643. [Open access]","Bogdan Musial, “The Origins of ‘Operation Reinhard’: The Decision-Making Process for the Mass Murder of the Jews in the Generalgouvernement,” trans. William Templer, Shoah Resource Center, International School for Holocaust Studies, Yad Vashem, 1–2, https://wwv.yadvashem.org/odot_pdf/microsoft%20word%20-%203222.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Final Solution: Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/final-solution-overview. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg, 1947), 3 January 1946, 355–58, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this not a debate about whether the Holocaust happened?","a":"Because both sides work from the same record of the murder and of Hitler's will to annihilation; what they dispute is how and when the decision was reached, not the crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the date of the decision matter so much?","a":"Because a decision taken in mid-1941, at the height of Hitler's hopes of victory, points to intention, while killing that grew out of failed deportation plans points to the workings of the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the old divide faded?","a":"Because later research found several decisions taken step by step in 1941, shaped by both Hitler's aims and regional initiatives, and cultural history asked different questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Functionalism–intentionalism debate"},
    "ww2-751": {"questions":["The minutes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, kept by Adolf Eichmann, counted some 11 million Jews to be included, among them the Jews of Britain, Ireland, Sweden and Switzerland.","Rather than deciding on genocide, the officials at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> discussed how to carry out a policy already settled at the top, and not one of them objected to it."],"abstract":"The <b>Wannsee Conference</b> was a meeting of senior SS, party and government officials held on 20 January 1942 in a villa at Am Grossen Wannsee 56–58 in Berlin to coordinate what the regime called the final solution of the Jewish question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was called by Reinhard Heydrich, chief of the Security Police and the SD, and the 15 men present included state secretaries of several ministries and of the General Government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heydrich opened by saying that the Reich Marshal, Hermann Göring, had appointed him to prepare the final solution, a reference to a letter of 31 July 1941 charging him to plan a complete solution of the Jewish question in the German sphere of influence in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The minutes, kept by Adolf Eichmann, counted about 11 million Jews to be included, among them those of Britain, Ireland, Sweden, Switzerland and Spain, beyond German control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their language was euphemistic, speaking of evacuation to the East and of labour columns in which a large part would fall away through natural causes, with any survivors to be treated accordingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The meeting did not decide on genocide: the officials discussed how to carry out a policy already settled at the highest level, and none of them objected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By then the killing was in full swing, for the Einsatzgruppen had shot or gassed more than 500,000 Jews, and gassing at Chełmno, which no one mentioned, had begun in December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the General Government the decision to murder all the Jews was most probably taken around the turn of September and October 1941, and its state secretary, Josef Bühler, asked that the final solution begin there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians place Hitler’s decision somewhere in 1941 and read the conference as coordination rather than decision, a means for Heydrich to bind the ministerial bureaucracy to the plan by making it complicit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The minutes survive as the 16th of 30 numbered copies, marked top secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Minutes of the Wannsee Conference, 20 January 1942, sixteenth of thirty copies, English translation, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/wannsee.asp. [Open access]","Hermann Göring to Reinhard Heydrich, 31 July 1941, document 710-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 3 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 525–26, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag03unit. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Wannsee Conference and the ‘Final Solution’,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 25 March 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/wannsee-conference-and-the-final-solution. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “‘Final Solution’: Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 8 December 2020, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/final-solution-overview. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Wannsee-Konferenz 1942,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/wannsee-konferenz. [in German] [Open access]","Peter Longerich, “The Systematic Character of the National Socialist Policy for the Extermination of the Jews,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/longsys/. [Open access]","Muzeum i Miejsce Pamięci w Bełżcu, “‘Aktion Reinhardt’: Zagłada Żydów w Generalnym Gubernatorstwie,” Państwowe Muzeum na Majdanku, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.belzec.eu/warte-uwagi/aktion-reinhardt. [in Polish] [Open access]","The Wiener Holocaust Library, “The Wannsee Conference, 1942,” in “How Did the Holocaust Happen?,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/how-and-why/how/the-wannsee-conference/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Heydrich invite civil servants as well as SS officers?","a":"Because the murder of the Jews of Europe needed the cooperation of ministries and agencies across the German sphere, and Heydrich wanted to inform them, secure their support and make plain that Hitler had put him and the Reich Security Main Office in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the count include Jews in Britain and in neutral countries?","a":"Because the plan was meant to cover the whole of Europe, so the minutes listed about 11 million Jews country by country, including those of Britain, Ireland, Sweden, Switzerland and Spain, which were out of German reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the conference not seen as the moment genocide was decided?","a":"Because the killing had already begun: the Einsatzgruppen had murdered more than 500,000 Jews, the decision for the General Government had probably been taken in the autumn of 1941, and the meeting discussed how to implement a policy settled before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Berlin-Wannsee%2C_Villa_Marlier_%281%29.jpg","title":"The villa at Am Grossen Wannsee 56–58","desc":"The lakeside villa in the Berlin district of Wannsee where Heydrich’s meeting of 20 January 1942 was held, photographed in 2023.","credit":"Palickap, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin-Wannsee,_Villa_Marlier_(1).jpg","alt":"A large pale two-storey villa with a columned entrance stands at the end of a paved path lined with hedges, tall pine trees and red tulips."},"wiki":"Wannsee Conference"},
    "ww2-752": {"questions":["Prisoner revolts at Treblinka and Sobibór in 1943 led Himmler to wind up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last act was the shooting of more than 42,000 Jews in Operation Harvest Festival.","Globocnik listed the aims of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the murder of the General Government’s Jews, the exploitation of some of their labour first, and the seizure of everything they owned."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Reinhard</b> was the code name for the German plan to murder the roughly two million Jews of the General Government, the part of occupied Poland under a German civil administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name appeared only in June 1942, when the killing was already under way, and honoured Reinhard Heydrich, head of the Reich Security Main Office, who had been fatally wounded by Czech resistance fighters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heinrich Himmler entrusted it to Odilo Globocnik, the SS and police leader of the Lublin district, on whose staff Hermann Höfle organised the transports and Georg Wippern the seizure of property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its three killing centres, Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, were run by small German staffs including 92 veterans of the T4 programme for murdering disabled people, with guards trained at Trawniki from former Soviet prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Globocnik listed its aims as the resettlement, by which he meant murder, of the Jews, the exploitation of some of their labour before death, and the seizure of their property, from clothing and currency to factories and land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The deportations began in Lublin on 16 March 1942, and the first transports, from the ghettos of Lublin and Lwów, went to Bełżec.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The liquidation of the Warsaw ghetto opened a new phase when the first transport reached Treblinka on 23 July 1942, and in August more than 300,000 Jews were murdered at Bełżec and Sobibór alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prisoner revolts at Treblinka on 2 August 1943 and at Sobibór on 14 October 1943 led Himmler to order the operation closed and the remaining Jewish prisoners of the Lublin district killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That order was carried out on 3 and 4 November 1943 as Operation Harvest Festival, in which more than 42,000 Jews were shot at Majdanek, Trawniki and Poniatowa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The museum at Bełżec puts the dead at about 440,000 there, 170,000 at Sobibór and 800,000 to 900,000 at Treblinka, while the US Holocaust Memorial Museum gives about 1.7 million Jews killed in the whole operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Operation Reinhard (Einsatz Reinhard),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 14 May 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/operation-reinhard-einsatz-reinhard. [Open access]","Muzeum i Miejsce Pamięci w Bełżcu, “‘Aktion Reinhardt’: Zagłada Żydów w Generalnym Gubernatorstwie,” Państwowe Muzeum na Majdanku, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.belzec.eu/warte-uwagi/aktion-reinhardt. [in Polish] [Open access]","Muzeum i Miejsce Pamięci w Sobiborze, “Zagłada Żydów w niemieckiej akcji ‘Reinhardt’,” exhibition text by Dariusz Libionka, Państwowe Muzeum na Majdanku, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.sobibor-memorial.eu/wystawy/zaglada-zydow-w-niemieckiej-akcji-reinhardt. [in Polish] [Open access]","Muzeum Treblinka, “Obóz Zagłady Treblinka II (1942–1943),” accessed 7 November 2026, https://muzeumtreblinka.eu/oboz-zaglady-treblinka-ii-1942-1943/. [in Polish] [Open access]","Central Commission for Investigation of German Crimes in Poland, “The Treblinka Extermination Camp,” in <i>German Crimes in Poland</i>, vol. 1 (Warsaw, 1946), 92–103, https://archive.org/details/pbc.gda.pl.Vol_01. [Open access]","Christopher R. Browning, “Evidence for the Implementation of the Final Solution,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/browning/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Killing Centers: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 15 September 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/killing-centers-an-overview. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were men from the T4 programme sent to run the camps?","a":"Because they had already gassed disabled people with carbon monoxide and knew the techniques of gassing and cremation; every commandant of a Reinhard camp came to occupied Poland by way of T4, and so did many of the German staff at Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the operation have an economic side?","a":"Because seizing Jewish property was one of its stated aims: clothing, currency, jewellery, factories, flats and land were taken, and the loot was sorted in depots in Lublin, with the victims’ shoes stored at Majdanek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Himmler end the operation in the autumn of 1943?","a":"Because the revolts at Treblinka and Sobibór made him fear further uprisings, so he ordered the murder of the Jewish forced labourers left in the Lublin district, carried out as Operation Harvest Festival in November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Einsatz_Reinhardt_Odilo_Globocnik_15_grudnia_1943.jpg","title":"A financial statement of the operation, 15 December 1943","desc":"The last page of a statement on the money and goods seized from the victims, signed by Globocnik and by his officers Rzepa and Wippern, as published in Warsaw in 1949.","credit":"Odilo Globocnik, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (from S. Piotrowski, Misja Odyla Globocnika, 1949). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einsatz_Reinhardt_Odilo_Globocnik_15_grudnia_1943.jpg","alt":"A typed German document headed with a summary table of sums in Reichsmarks, ending in a total of more than 178 million, with typed names and a handwritten signature at the foot."},"wiki":"Operation Reinhard"},
    "ww2-753": {"questions":["Chełmno, Bełżec, Sobibór, Treblinka and Auschwitz-Birkenau were each an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and about 2.7 million Jews, almost half the Holocaust’s Jewish victims, were murdered in them.","A sham railway station with signs for a waiting room and booking office greeted deportees at Treblinka, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> designed to deceive its victims until the last moment."],"abstract":"An <b>extermination camp</b>, in German Vernichtungslager, was a camp built not to hold prisoners but to kill the people brought to it, almost all of them on the day they arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nazi Germany built five such killing centres in occupied Poland, at Chełmno, Bełżec, Sobibór, Treblinka and Auschwitz-Birkenau, where about 2.7 million Jews were murdered, almost half the Jewish victims of the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Polish commission of 1946 called them camps in appearance only, since apart from small working parties forced to bury the dead and sort their belongings there were no prisoners at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That set them apart from concentration camps, places of slow death by hunger and labour, though Auschwitz combined both roles and Majdanek, once counted a sixth killing centre, is now usually classed as a concentration camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, built in the General Government for Operation Reinhard, were the purest form of the type, killing with carbon monoxide from engines in fixed gas chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Each was a small, hidden site beside a railway, and Treblinka, about 17 hectares (42 acres), was run by 30 to 40 Germans and Austrians with 100 to 120 guards recruited from Soviet prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deception was built in, for Treblinka had a sham railway station with signs for a waiting room and booking office, and its victims were driven along a fenced path to gas chambers labelled as showers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bełżec began killing in March 1942, Sobibór in May and Treblinka in July, and after the prisoner revolts of 1943 the last two were dismantled and their grounds planted over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No names were even recorded for the Jews brought to Sobibór from Poland and Belarus, so the numbers killed must be reconstructed from railway records and witnesses, and estimates differ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Bełżec museum gives about 440,000 dead there, 170,000 at Sobibór and 800,000 to 900,000 at Treblinka, while the Sobibór memorial gives 180,000 and the US Holocaust Memorial Museum about 925,000 for Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Killing Centers: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 15 September 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/killing-centers-an-overview. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Treblinka,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 3 March 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/treblinka. [Open access]","Muzeum i Miejsce Pamięci w Bełżcu, “‘Aktion Reinhardt’: Zagłada Żydów w Generalnym Gubernatorstwie,” Państwowe Muzeum na Majdanku, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.belzec.eu/warte-uwagi/aktion-reinhardt. [in Polish] [Open access]","Muzeum i Miejsce Pamięci w Sobiborze, “SS-Sonderkommando Sobibor (1942–1943),” Państwowe Muzeum na Majdanku, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.sobibor-memorial.eu/historia-obozu/ss-sonderkommando-sobibor-1942-1943. [in Polish] [Open access]","Muzeum Treblinka, “Obóz Zagłady Treblinka II (1942–1943),” accessed 7 November 2026, https://muzeumtreblinka.eu/oboz-zaglady-treblinka-ii-1942-1943/. [in Polish] [Open access]","Central Commission for Investigation of German Crimes in Poland, <i>German Crimes in Poland</i>, vol. 1 (Warsaw, 1946), 14–16, https://archive.org/details/pbc.gda.pl.Vol_01. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “The Functions,” Auschwitz II-Birkenau, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-ii/the-functions. [Open access]","Central Commission for Investigation of German Crimes in Poland, “The Treblinka Extermination Camp,” in <i>German Crimes in Poland</i>, vol. 1 (Warsaw, 1946), 92–103, https://archive.org/details/pbc.gda.pl.Vol_01. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a Polish commission in 1946 call these places camps in appearance only?","a":"Because they held no prisoners in the usual sense: the deportees were as a rule killed at once, and only small working parties were kept to bury the bodies and sort the belongings, and these too were murdered and replaced after a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the death tolls of these camps given as ranges?","a":"Because the victims were not registered: at Sobibór only transports from western Europe left name lists, so the totals are reconstructed from railway records and testimony, and the museums and the US Holocaust Memorial Museum arrive at different figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Auschwitz not the same kind of camp as Treblinka?","a":"Because Auschwitz joined a concentration camp, where registered prisoners died slowly of hunger and labour, to a centre for immediate killing, while Treblinka and Bełżec existed for direct extermination alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Extermination camp"},
    "ww2-754": {"questions":["About 700 Jews from Koło, murdered on 8 December 1941, were the first victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first fixed site where the Nazis used poison gas to murder Jews en masse.","Herbert Lange, whose unit had been murdering disabled patients with a gas van since 1939, was put in charge of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when it was set up in late 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Chełmno extermination camp</b>, Kulmhof in German, was a killing centre in the village of Chełmno nad Nerem in the Warthegau, the part of western Poland annexed to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the first fixed site where the Nazis used poison gas for the mass murder of Jews, and its first transport, about 700 Jews from nearby Koło, arrived on 7 December 1941 and was killed the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The killing was entrusted to Herbert Lange, whose SS unit had murdered more than 7,500 disabled patients in the region with a gas van since 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had two parts, a manor house in the village, where victims were told they would bathe before working in the East, and a forest clearing at Rzuchów about 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Instead of fixed gas chambers it used vans whose exhaust was piped into a sealed compartment, killing the people locked inside before they were driven to the forest to be buried and later burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1942 the regional governor, Arthur Greiser, told Himmler that the special treatment of some 100,000 Jews would soon be complete, and a memo of the Reich Security Main Office in June recorded that three vans had processed 97,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From January 1942 the main victims were the Jews of the Łódź ghetto, among them Jews deported there from Germany, Austria, Czechoslovakia and Luxembourg, as well as Roma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The SS blew up the manor and the crematoria in April 1943, then returned in the summer of 1944 to murder 7,196 people from the Łódź ghetto in ten transports before leaving for good in January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only seven Jewish men are known to have escaped, several of whom later testified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of the dead run from about 350,000, found by a Polish judge after the war, to the 152,000 accepted at a trial in Bonn in the 1960s, and the museum gives about 200,000 as most probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Chełmno (Kulmhof) Killing Center,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 16 October 2025, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/chelmno. [Open access]","Museum of the Former German Kulmhof Death Camp in Chełmno on Ner, “History of the Camp,” accessed 7 November 2026, https://chelmno-muzeum.eu/en/history-of-the-camp/. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “Das Vernichtungslager Chelmno,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/vernichtungslager-chelmno. [in German] [Open access]","Central Commission for Investigation of German Crimes in Poland, “The Extermination Camp at Chełmno,” in <i>German Crimes in Poland</i>, vol. 1 (Warsaw, 1946), 106–15, https://archive.org/details/pbc.gda.pl.Vol_01. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Gas Chambers,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 27 October 2025, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gassing-operations. [Open access]","Christopher R. Browning, “Evidence for the Implementation of the Final Solution,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/browning/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Chełmno kill with vans rather than gas chambers?","a":"Because its staff came from Herbert Lange’s unit, which had already murdered disabled patients and then Jews of the Konin district in a gas van; at Chełmno victims were loaded at the manor and gassed with exhaust before the drive to the forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the estimates of the dead differ so widely?","a":"Because few records and very few survivors remained: a Polish judge found about 350,000, a West German court in Bonn accepted 152,000, the museum gives about 200,000 as most probable, and the US Holocaust Memorial Museum gives at least 152,000 Jews and 4,300 Roma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the SS come back to Chełmno in 1944?","a":"Because Jews were still alive in the Łódź ghetto, and in June and July 1944 ten transports were murdered in huts built in the forest, after which the unit stayed on into January 1945 burning bodies to remove the evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Che%C5%82mno_%28Kulmhof%29_1942_%28Ko%C5%82o%29.jpg","title":"Deportees on the way to Chełmno, 1942","desc":"Guards and police among deportees and their bundles beside lorries and railway wagons in the Koło area in 1942, from an original in the District Museum in Konin.","credit":"Unknown SS photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (District Museum in Konin). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chełmno_(Kulmhof)_1942_(Koło).jpg","alt":"Uniformed men, one holding a rifle and one a stick, stand among civilians and piles of clothing and bundles on the ground between covered lorries and a line of railway wagons."},"wiki":"Chełmno extermination camp"},
    "ww2-755": {"questions":["Rudolf Höss swore in 1946 that 3 million people had died at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the camp's memorial and historians put the dead at about 1.1 million, around one million of them Jews.","Divided in November 1943 into a main camp, Birkenau and the industrial subcamps, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was entered by Soviet troops on 27 January 1945, who found about 8,500 prisoners there."],"abstract":"The <b>Auschwitz concentration camp</b> was the largest of the Nazi camps, set up by the SS in May 1940 in a suburb of Oświęcim, which the Germans called Auschwitz, in eastern Upper Silesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was built in prewar Polish army barracks for Polish political prisoners, with Rudolf Höss as commandant, and the first transport of Poles arrived on 14 June 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until early 1942 it was a concentration camp in the strict sense, a place of slow killing through starvation and deliberately inhuman conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the spring of 1942 it was also the largest centre for the immediate killing of Jews, a role for which, by Höss’s account, Himmler had chosen it in the summer of 1941 because it was easy to reach by rail and could be isolated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At its peak in the summer of 1944 it covered about 40 square kilometres (15 square miles) with more than 40 subcamps and held about 135,000 people, a quarter of everyone in the camp system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 22 November 1943 it was divided into three camps: Auschwitz I, the main camp; Auschwitz II at Birkenau, where most of the gas chambers stood; and Auschwitz III, the subcamps at industrial plants such as IG Farben’s Buna works at Monowitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The US Holocaust Memorial Museum and the memorial on the site both put the dead at about 1.1 million, of whom around one million were Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The memorial counts some 70,000 Poles, 21,000 Roma and Sinti and 15,000 Soviet prisoners of war among the dead, with about 12,000 people of other nationalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Höss swore in 1946 that about three million had died there, though he later put the number at 1.5 million at most, and communist Poland long upheld an official figure of 4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The SS drove about 56,000 prisoners west on death marches in January 1945, leaving the sick without water or light, and Soviet troops arriving on 27 January found about 8,500 prisoners there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Auschwitz I,” accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-i/. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “The Number of Victims,” Auschwitz and Shoah, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-and-shoah/the-number-of-victims. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Auschwitz,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 15 September 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/auschwitz. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Auschwitz: Key Dates,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 23 January 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/auschwitz-key-dates. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “Das Konzentrations- und Vernichtungslager Auschwitz,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/konzentrations-und-vernichtungslager-auschwitz. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 11, proceedings of 15 April 1946, testimony of Rudolf Höss, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-15-46.asp. [Open access]","Rudolf Höss, affidavit of 5 April 1946, document 3868-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 787–90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Robert Jan van Pelt, “The Van Pelt Report,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/vanpelt/. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 6, proceedings of 28 January 1946, testimony of Marie-Claude Vaillant-Couturier, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-28-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why, by its commandant’s account, was Auschwitz chosen for the murder of Jews?","a":"Because, Höss told the Nuremberg tribunal, Himmler said in the summer of 1941 that it was easy to reach by rail and that its large site allowed it to be isolated; the surrounding country was cleared of its inhabitants and closed to anyone without a pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Höss’s figure of three million dead not accepted?","a":"Because it cannot be reconciled with the evidence of how many people were sent there: historians estimate that about 1.1 million died, around one million of them Jews, and Höss himself later wrote that 1.5 million was the most he could account for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the camp divided into three in November 1943?","a":"Because the complex had grown too large to run as one camp, and the division into Auschwitz I, Birkenau and the industrial subcamps formalised functions that had already drifted apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Arbeit_macht_frei_sign%2C_main_gate_of_the_Auschwitz_I_concentration_camp%2C_Poland_-_20051127.jpg","title":"The gate of the main camp","desc":"The main gate of Auschwitz I, with its iron sign reading “Arbeit macht frei”, photographed in 2005; behind it stand barracks of the prewar Polish army.","credit":"Tulio Bertorini, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arbeit_macht_frei_sign,_main_gate_of_the_Auschwitz_I_concentration_camp,_Poland_-_20051127.jpg","alt":"An iron gate under a curved metal sign with the words Arbeit macht frei, with brick barrack buildings and bare trees beyond it."},"wiki":"Auschwitz concentration camp","places":["pl:q7342"]},
    "ww2-756": {"questions":["About nine in ten of all who died at Auschwitz, roughly a million people, died at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where four gas chambers and crematoria with ovens by Topf and Sons went into operation in 1943.","Nearly 3,000 Roma and Sinti still alive in their family camp at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were murdered in its gas chambers on 2 August 1944, ending the camp the SS had opened in February 1943."],"abstract":"<b>Auschwitz II-Birkenau</b> was the largest of the more than 40 camps and subcamps of the Auschwitz complex, built on the site of the village of Brzezinka, whose Polish inhabitants were expelled in April 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Work began in October 1941, on Himmler’s order, on a camp for well over 100,000 Soviet prisoners of war, and 10,000 prisoners were marched there daily to build it, most of whom died within months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 25 January 1942 Himmler decided that it would hold Jews instead, and from March it served both as a branch concentration camp and as a centre for the extermination of the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came to cover about 140 hectares (350 acres) with some 300 buildings, and a railway spur and ramp opened inside it in May 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside it the SS opened a women’s camp in August 1942, a family camp for Roma and Sinti in February 1943 and a family camp for Jews from Theresienstadt in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The killing began in two converted farmhouses, bunkers 1 and 2, which Höss said lay deep in the woods out of sight, the first in use from around 23 March 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In February 1942 Hans Kammler of the SS moved a planned large crematorium from the main camp to Birkenau, and four buildings, each with an undressing room, a gas chamber and a crematorium with ovens by J. A. Topf and Sons, went into use in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Germans reckoned that 1.6 million people a year could be killed and burned there, and Höss claimed that each new chamber could take 2,000 people at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 90 per cent of all who died at Auschwitz, approximately a million people, died at Birkenau, more than nine in ten of them Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 2 August 1944 the nearly 3,000 Roma and Sinti still in their camp were gassed, and a French witness at Nuremberg described the gassing of the Theresienstadt family camp after 6 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Auschwitz II-Birkenau,” accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-ii/. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “The Construction of the Camp,” Auschwitz II-Birkenau, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-ii/the-construction-of-the-camp. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “Das Vernichtungslager Auschwitz-Birkenau,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/vernichtungslager-auschwitz-birkenau. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Auschwitz: Key Dates,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 23 January 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/auschwitz-key-dates. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 11, proceedings of 15 April 1946, testimony of Rudolf Höss, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-15-46.asp. [Open access]","Rudolf Höss, affidavit of 5 April 1946, document 3868-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 787–90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 6, proceedings of 28 January 1946, testimony of Marie-Claude Vaillant-Couturier, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-28-46.asp. [Open access]","Robert Jan van Pelt, “The Van Pelt Report,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/vanpelt/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Birkenau first planned as a camp for prisoners of war?","a":"Because Himmler meant to hold well over 100,000 Soviet prisoners there as labour for the planned colonisation of the East; when most of those brought to build it died, the SS turned the camp to holding Jews selected for labour and to killing the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the converted farmhouses replaced by new buildings?","a":"Because the SS wanted to kill more people and burn the bodies faster, so four complexes, each with an undressing room, a gas chamber and a crematorium, were built and went into use in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did most of the victims of Auschwitz die at Birkenau rather than in the main camp?","a":"Because most of the machinery of mass murder was built there, first the two bunkers and then the four large gas chambers and crematoria, so that about 90 per cent of the dead of the whole complex died at Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Auschwitz_II_Birkenau_-_gate%2C_tracks.jpg","title":"The gatehouse and the railway spur","desc":"The brick gatehouse of Birkenau seen along the railway tracks that led through it to the unloading ramp, photographed in 2019.","credit":"Ingo Mehling, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auschwitz_II_Birkenau_-_gate,_tracks.jpg","alt":"Railway tracks run straight across a grassy plain towards a long, low brick building with a central tower and an arched entrance, with wooden watchtowers to either side."}},
    "ww2-757": {"questions":["Because only Auschwitz-Birkenau carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a large scale, far more Jews survived Auschwitz than survived all the other killing centres put together.","Sick and weak prisoners already registered at Auschwitz also faced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in camp hospitals, showers and roll calls, and those chosen were killed by phenol injection or gas."],"abstract":"The <b>Selektion</b>, German for ‘selection’, was the Nazi practice of sorting deported Jews and camp prisoners into those to be kept alive for forced labour and those to be killed, most notoriously on the railway ramps of Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The SS called the procedure Aussortierung, sorting out, and it began with transports of July 1942, regularly from 4 July, after which almost every mass transport of Jews to Auschwitz went through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Families were split as they left the trains into two columns, men and older boys in one and women and children in the other, and led past SS doctors who judged each person on sight, one survivor recalling the doctor simply pointing to the left or the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Those known to have selected on the ramps include the SS doctors Josef Mengele, Fritz Klein, Heinz Thilo and Eduard Wirths, who sometimes first asked a person’s age or trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only Auschwitz-Birkenau selected on this scale, which is why far more Jews survived it than all the other killing centres together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Age was a principal criterion, for as a rule children under 16, and from 1944 under 14, were sent to die with the old and with women who had small children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On average about one person in five was chosen for labour, so that of some 1.1 million Jews deported to Auschwitz about 200,000 were registered and about 900,000 were killed in the gas chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rudolf Höss, the commandant, described two SS doctors making spot decisions as prisoners marched past, young children always being sent to their deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A French prisoner told the Nuremberg tribunal that of 1,200 Jewish women in a transport from France only 125 entered the camp and the rest went straight to the gas chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Selection did not end at the ramp, for SS doctors also picked out sick and weak prisoners in the camp hospitals, the showers and at roll call, killing them with phenol injections or gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “The Unloading Ramps and Selections,” Auschwitz and Shoah, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-and-shoah/the-unloading-ramps-and-selections. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Selections of Jews Upon Arrival at Auschwitz,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 21 August 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/selections-of-jews-upon-arrival-at-auschwitz. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Selections of Prisoners Registered in the Auschwitz Camp Complex,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 27 August 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/selections-of-prisoners-registered-in-the-auschwitz-camp-complex. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “‘Selektion’ im Vernichtungslager Auschwitz,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 6 September 2002, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/selektion-im-vernichtungslager-auschwitz. [in German] [Open access]","Rudolf Höss, affidavit of 5 April 1946, document 3868-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 787–90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 6, proceedings of 28 January 1946, testimony of Marie-Claude Vaillant-Couturier, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-28-46.asp. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “‘Zyklon B Was Used Exclusively for Disinfection’,” Stop Denial, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/stop-denial/zyklon-b/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were children and the old sent straight to the gas chambers?","a":"Because the test was fitness for labour: as a rule children under 16, later under 14, the elderly and women with small children were classed as unfit, and Höss said young children were always killed because they could not work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Auschwitz leave more Jewish survivors than the other killing centres?","a":"Because it alone chose large numbers of new arrivals for forced labour, about 200,000 of some 1.1 million Jews deported there, while the other centres kept only very small numbers alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the SS tell those chosen for death that they were going to bathe?","a":"Because the SS wanted to murder thousands of people quickly and without complications, so the victims were told they were going to be bathed and deloused and were driven into gas chambers disguised as showers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Selection_on_the_ramp_at_Auschwitz-Birkenau%2C_1944_%28Auschwitz_Album%29_1b.jpg","title":"The ramp at Birkenau, May 1944","desc":"Jews deported from Hungary on the ramp at Birkenau in May 1944, from the SS photographs known as the Auschwitz Album, held by Yad Vashem.","credit":"Unknown SS photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Auschwitz Album, Yad Vashem). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selection_on_the_ramp_at_Auschwitz-Birkenau,_1944_(Auschwitz_Album)_1b.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd of men, women and children in coats and headscarves stands beside a long line of freight wagons, with uniformed men and prisoners in striped clothes among them and a gatehouse tower in the distance."},"wiki":"Selection (Holocaust)"},
    "ww2-758": {"questions":["At Sobibór a wooden <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of three cells was enlarged in 1942 to eight, fed with engine exhaust, and killing there took about 20 minutes.","Between 2.3 and 3 million of the six million Jews murdered in the Holocaust died in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, killed with engine exhaust or with hydrogen cyanide from Zyklon B."],"abstract":"A <b>gas chamber</b> is a sealed room or compartment into which poison gas is let in to kill the people shut inside, and Nazi Germany built about 50 of them for mass murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some were fitted into vans, others into existing rooms or purpose-built buildings, and they were often disguised as bathing rooms, sometimes with fake shower heads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first were made for the murder of disabled people, with an experimental gassing at Fort VII in Poznań in October 1939 and, from January 1940, chambers using bottled carbon monoxide at killing centres in Germany and Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 70,000 patients had been killed in them by the end of August 1941, when, after the Bishop of Münster had denounced the killings from the pulpit, Hitler had the gassings stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three gases were used: pure carbon monoxide from canisters, carbon monoxide from engine exhaust, and hydrogen cyanide released from pellets of Zyklon B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Many of the men who had run the gassings in the asylums went on to Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, where engines pumped exhaust into fixed chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Sobibór a wooden building of three chambers was enlarged in 1942 to eight cells and killing took about 20 minutes, while at Treblinka a building of three chambers was joined that autumn by one of ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Auschwitz the SS first used Zyklon B in the cellars of Block 11 in September 1941, then in a converted mortuary, two farmhouses and finally four large chambers at Birkenau, each holding about 2,000 people, with false showers made in the camp workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Rudolf Höss, its commandant, called his chambers an improvement on those of Treblinka, which he said took only 200 people each, and said that his victims were fooled into thinking they would be deloused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between 2.3 and 3 million of the six million Jews murdered in the Holocaust were killed with poison gas, and the last mass gassings took place in late April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Gas Chambers,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 27 October 2025, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gassing-operations. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Euthanasia Program and Aktion T4,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 17 March 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]","Mirjam Husemann and Oliver Schweinoch, “‘Euthanasie’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 31 August 2023, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/euthanasie. [in German] [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Gas Chambers,” Auschwitz and Shoah, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-and-shoah/gas-chambers. [Open access]","Muzeum i Miejsce Pamięci w Sobiborze, “SS-Sonderkommando Sobibor (1942–1943),” Państwowe Muzeum na Majdanku, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.sobibor-memorial.eu/historia-obozu/ss-sonderkommando-sobibor-1942-1943. [in Polish] [Open access]","Rudolf Höss, affidavit of 5 April 1946, document 3868-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 787–90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","Central Commission for Investigation of German Crimes in Poland, “The Treblinka Extermination Camp,” in <i>German Crimes in Poland</i>, vol. 1 (Warsaw, 1946), 92–103, https://archive.org/details/pbc.gda.pl.Vol_01. [Open access]","Robert Jan van Pelt, “The Van Pelt Report,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/vanpelt/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the gas chambers disguised as showers?","a":"Because the Nazis wanted to trick the victims and hide the truth from them for as long as possible; at Auschwitz people were told they had to be disinfected and bathed before going into the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did staff from the asylum killings go to the camps in occupied Poland?","a":"Because they had shown themselves reliable in the first programme of mass murder and knew how to gas people, and when the asylum gassings were stopped in August 1941 many were sent to Treblinka, Sobibór and Bełżec.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Auschwitz use Zyklon B rather than engine exhaust?","a":"Because Höss judged the carbon monoxide used at Treblinka inefficient; at Auschwitz the gas was first tried on prisoners in Block 11 in September 1941, and he described it as crystals dropped into the chamber through a small opening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Gas_chamber_in_the_main_camp_of_Auschwitz_immediately_after_liberation.jpg","title":"The gas chamber of the Auschwitz main camp, 1945","desc":"The gas chamber beside crematorium I in the Auschwitz main camp, photographed in January 1945 shortly after the liberation.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Dokumentationsarchiv des österreichischen Widerstandes). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gas_chamber_in_the_main_camp_of_Auschwitz_immediately_after_liberation.jpg","alt":"A long, empty, low-ceilinged room with stained whitewashed walls, heavy beams overhead, a puddle on the concrete floor and a doorway at the far end."},"wiki":"Gas chamber"},
    "ww2-759": {"questions":["A British court in Hamburg sentenced Bruno Tesch and Karl Weinbacher to death in 1946 for supplying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the SS knowing that it would be used to kill people.","First used to kill people at Auschwitz on 3 September 1941, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was poured on Soviet prisoners of war and sick Polish prisoners locked in the cellars of Block 11."],"abstract":"<b>Zyklon B</b> was a commercial pesticide based on hydrogen cyanide, sold in sealed tins of pellets that released the poison as a gas when exposed to air, and it became the means of murder in the gas chambers of Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the war it killed insects in homes, warehouses and ships’ holds, and a Polish pharmacists’ magazine described tins of it as early as 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the camps it was also used as intended, and from 1943 the Auschwitz complex had about ten disinfection chambers that treated prisoners’ clothing with it or with hot air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A British military court heard in 1946 that it was 99 per cent prussic acid, was made by another firm and was supplied east of the Elbe through the Hamburg agency of Tesch and Stabenow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first use to kill people at Auschwitz came on 3 September 1941, when about 600 Soviet prisoners of war and some 250 sick Polish prisoners were gassed in the cellars of Block 11.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In the large Birkenau chambers the pellets were lowered through wire-mesh columns made in the camp workshop, and the commandant, Rudolf Höss, said death took 3 to 15 minutes depending on the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Höss claimed he chose it because he judged the carbon monoxide used at Treblinka inefficient, and witnesses at the Hamburg trial said that Auschwitz received more of it than any other camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Delivery papers for Auschwitz used euphemisms such as material for the resettlement of Jews and for special treatment to hide what the gas was for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1946 the court sentenced Bruno Tesch, the firm’s owner, and his deputy Karl Weinbacher to death for supplying the gas knowing it would be used to kill Allied nationals, and both were hanged, while the gassing technician Joachim Drosihn was acquitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hydrogen cyanide kills by depriving the body of oxygen, and witnesses described death in the chambers not as humane or peaceful but as terrifying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “‘Zyklon B Was Used Exclusively for Disinfection’,” Stop Denial, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/stop-denial/zyklon-b/. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Gas Chambers,” Auschwitz and Shoah, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-and-shoah/gas-chambers. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “The Zyklon B Case: Trial of Bruno Tesch and Two Others, British Military Court, Hamburg, 1st–8th March, 1946,” in <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 1 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1947), 93–103, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-1/Law-Reports_Vol-1.pdf. [Open access]","Rudolf Höss, affidavit of 5 April 1946, document 3868-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 787–90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Gas Chambers,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 27 October 2025, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gassing-operations. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Killing Centers: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 15 September 2026, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/killing-centers-an-overview. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “Das Konzentrations- und Vernichtungslager Auschwitz,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/konzentrations-und-vernichtungslager-auschwitz. [in German] [Open access]","Robert Jan van Pelt, “The Van Pelt Report,” expert report for the defence in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books and Lipstadt</i>, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/vanpelt/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did delivery papers for Auschwitz speak of resettling Jews?","a":"Because such euphemisms hid the real purpose of the gas: documents for consignments to the camp described them as material for the resettlement of Jews or for special treatment, when the tins were meant for mass murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Hamburg firm prosecuted when it did not make the gas?","a":"Because it had the sole agency for supplying the gas east of the Elbe and arranged the orders, and the prosecution argued that knowingly supplying a state that used it to exterminate Allied civilians was a war crime under the Hague Regulations of 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Höss prefer it to carbon monoxide?","a":"Because he thought the engine exhaust used at Treblinka was not efficient, while the crystals dropped into the Auschwitz chambers killed, he said, in 3 to 15 minutes; the method had first been tried in Block 11 in September 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Label_Zyklon_B.jpg","title":"A label from a tin","desc":"A torn paper label from a tin of the pesticide, marked Giftgas, poison gas, and with the Degesch name, displayed at the Mémorial de Caen.","credit":"Loxyger, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Label_Zyklon_B.jpg","alt":"A creased, torn paper label in red, white and black, printed with skull-and-crossbones symbols and the word Giftgas, mounted against a wooden display panel."},"wiki":"Zyklon B"},
    "ww2-760": {"questions":["Kept apart from other prisoners and usually killed after a few months, members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knew more about the gassings than anyone else in the camp.","Four photographs of bodies being burned at Birkenau in 1944 were taken in secret by a member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and smuggled out by the Polish resistance."],"abstract":"The <b>Sonderkommando</b>, German for ‘special detachment’, was the name given in the killing centres to the squads of Jewish prisoners forced to work in and around the gas chambers and crematoria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word had many other uses, for the SS unit that ran Chełmno was itself a Sonderkommando, and the camp squads must not be confused with the Sonderkommandos of the Einsatzgruppen that shot Jews in the occupied Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Auschwitz-Birkenau its members met the victims in the undressing rooms, and after the SS had carried out the gassing they removed the bodies, shaved the hair, took out gold teeth and rings and burned the corpses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, which lacked the machinery of Auschwitz, the squads also had to dig up the mass graves and burn the bodies on grates made from rails, to destroy the evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Members were kept apart from other prisoners, forbidden to warn the victims and routinely killed after a few months, because they knew more about the killing than anyone and could not be allowed to survive as witnesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 October 1944, when it was clear that they too were about to be killed, the Jews of the Birkenau Sonderkommando rose up, set fire to one of the crematoria and attacked the SS men near them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some cut through the fence, but the SS surrounded and killed them, and about 250 Jews died fighting, among them the leaders Załmen Gradowski and Józef Deresiński, while three SS men were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four Jewish women who had taken explosives from the Union armaments works and passed them to the conspirators were later hanged in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Members of the Sonderkommando also left evidence behind, burying manuscripts near the Birkenau crematoria and secretly photographing the burning of bodies in open pits in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One member, the Greek prisoner Alberto Errera, is credited with four photographs smuggled out by the Polish resistance, among the very few images known to show events near the gas chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Sonderkommandos,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 17 September 2020, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/sonderkommandos. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Prisoner Mutinies,” Resistance, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/resistance/prisoner-mutinies. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Lack of Sources,” Stop Denial, accessed 7 November 2026, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/stop-denial/lack-of-sources/. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “Das Vernichtungslager Auschwitz-Birkenau,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 15 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/vernichtungslager-auschwitz-birkenau. [in German] [Open access]","Central Commission for Investigation of German Crimes in Poland, “The Extermination Camp at Chełmno,” in <i>German Crimes in Poland</i>, vol. 1 (Warsaw, 1946), 106–15, https://archive.org/details/pbc.gda.pl.Vol_01. [Open access]","Rudolf Höss, affidavit of 5 April 1946, document 3868-PS, in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 6 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 787–90, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag06unit. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Treblinka,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 3 March 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/treblinka. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were members of the Sonderkommando routinely killed?","a":"Because of all the prisoners they knew the most about the murder of the Jews, so the SS kept them apart from everyone else and could not let them survive to testify; most were killed after a few months and replaced from new transports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Birkenau Sonderkommando revolt in October 1944?","a":"Because the murder of all its members was plainly at hand, so they organised an uprising, blew up crematorium IV with its gas chamber and tried to break out; the SS shot everyone who took part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the Sonderkommando’s manuscripts matter to historians?","a":"Because they are testimony written during the war by the men closest to the gas chambers, buried near the crematoria, and with the secret photographs they are among the sources that confirm how the gas chambers worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Auschwitz_Resistance_280.jpg","title":"A photograph taken in secret, August 1944","desc":"One of the four photographs taken secretly at Birkenau in August 1944 and smuggled out of the camp, showing bodies being burned in the open when the crematoria were full.","credit":"Member of the Sonderkommando, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auschwitz_Resistance_280.jpg","alt":"Seen through a dark doorway, men stand among bodies lying on open ground while thick white smoke rises in front of a line of trees."},"wiki":"Sonderkommando"},
    "ww2-761": {"questions":["From Westerbork the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left every Tuesday from 1943, and more than 90 of them carried Jews from the Netherlands to Auschwitz-Birkenau, Sobibor, Theresienstadt and Bergen-Belsen.","Sealed freight wagons with a single bucket and no food or water made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deadly in themselves, and armed guards had orders to shoot anyone who tried to escape."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust trains</b> were the rail transports on which Germany and its allies deported Jews, and Roma too, from all over Europe to ghettos, shooting sites and, above all, the killing centres in occupied Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Germans called the deportations resettlement in the East and spoke of labour camps, but from 1942 deportation meant for most Jews a journey to a killing centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Reich Security Main Office directed the deportations, Adolf Eichmann's department IV B 4 planned the trains with the Transport Ministry, the Order Police escorted them, and the Foreign Office pressed Germany's allies to hand over their Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jews from the Reich often travelled in passenger coaches to keep up the story, but in the east most were packed into sealed freight wagons with a bucket and no food or water, and many died on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Armed guards had orders to shoot anyone who tried to escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Systematic deportation from the Reich began in October 1941, with 20,000 Jews sent to the Łódź ghetto and about 49,000 more by October 1942 to Riga, Minsk, Kovno and Raasiku, where most were shot on arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From March 1942 the SS and police deported about 1,526,000 Jews, mostly by train, to Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, and by December 1944 about 1.1 million Jews and 23,000 Roma and Sinti to Auschwitz-Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the west the trains left from transit camps, and from Westerbork in the Netherlands the first left for Auschwitz on 15 July 1942, more than 90 followed, and from 1943 they left every Tuesday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 64,000 Jews left Drancy near Paris in 62 transports, fewer than 2,000 of whom survived, and 28 trains carried 25,490 Jews and 353 Roma from the Dossin barracks at Mechelen to Auschwitz-Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between May and July 1944 nearly 440,000 Jews were deported from Hungary, most to Auschwitz-Birkenau, and of all those carried to the camps of Operation Reinhard fewer than 500 survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Deportations to Killing Centers,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/deportations-to-killing-centers. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Deportations,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/deportations. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Westerbork,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/westerbork. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Drancy,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/drancy. [Open access]","Kazerne Dossin, “Memorial,” Kazerne Dossin: Memorial, Museum and Documentation Centre on Holocaust and Human Rights, https://kazernedossin.eu/en/memorial/. [Open access]","The Wiener Holocaust Library, “Types of camps,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/the-camps/types-of-camps/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Germans call the deportations resettlement?","a":"Because they wanted to hide what the trains were for. The deportees were told they were going to labour camps in the East, and Jews from the Reich were often given passenger coaches so that Germans would believe the story, though from 1942 most deportees were being carried to killing centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the deportations need so many different agencies?","a":"Because moving millions of people across a continent was an office task as well as a police one. The Reich Security Main Office directed it, Eichmann's department planned the trains with the Transport Ministry, the Order Police escorted them and the Foreign Office bargained with Germany's allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Jews in western Europe pass through transit camps first?","a":"Because the occupiers used camps such as Westerbork, Drancy and Mechelen to hold Jews until the next transport left, and a transit camp was usually the last stop before a killing centre. From Westerbork the trains left every Tuesday from 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Umschlagplatz_loading.jpg","title":"Deportation from the Umschlagplatz, Warsaw","desc":"Jews of the Warsaw ghetto being loaded into freight wagons at the Umschlagplatz, the assembly point beside the railway, during the German occupation.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (USHMM, courtesy of the Jewish Historical Institute, Warsaw). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umschlagplatz_loading.jpg","alt":"A crowd of people with bundles stands beside a long line of wooden freight wagons while helmeted German police watch in the foreground."},"wiki":"Holocaust trains"},
    "ww2-762": {"questions":["In 1998 a memorial opened at Platform 17 of Berlin's Grunewald station, its 186 cast steel plates recording the deportation transports that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had run from Berlin.","Between the autumn of 1941 and the autumn of 1944 trains of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and other European railways carried millions of people to killing sites in occupied Poland and the Soviet Union."],"abstract":"The <b>Deutsche Reichsbahn</b> was the German state railway, and under Nazi rule it ran the trains that deported Jews from Germany and occupied Europe to the ghettos and killing centres in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Reich Security Main Office directed the deportations and the Transport Ministry drew up the timetables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The railway used freight and passenger cars, and deportees got no food or water even when a train waited for days on a siding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the autumn of 1941 and the autumn of 1944 millions were carried by rail to killing sites in occupied Poland and the Soviet Union, and the killing centres were placed near major railway lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The deportation trains were a small share of German rail traffic, but one study argues they had to be balanced against trains carrying ammunition, fuel and food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> How directly the railway's masters served the killing appears in a letter of 28 July 1942 from Albert Ganzenmüller, state secretary in the Reich Transport Ministry, to Karl Wolff, chief of Himmler's personal staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Passing on a report from his General Directorate of the Eastern Railway in Kraków, he wrote that since 22 July one train a day with 5,000 Jews had been running from Warsaw by way of Małkinia to Treblinka, and two a week with 5,000 each from Przemyśl to Bełżec.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wolff replied on 13 August, thanking him also in Himmler's name and saying he was especially pleased to learn that for a fortnight a daily train with 5,000 Jews had gone to Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shown the letters as a witness at Oswald Pohl's trial after the war, Wolff, who had denied knowing of the transports, accepted that both were genuine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For decades neither German state's railway faced this past, and only on 27 January 1998 did Deutsche Bahn open a memorial at Platform 17 of Berlin's Grunewald station, whose 186 steel plates record the date, size, origin and destination of each transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “German Railways and the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-railways-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pohl Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), testimony of Karl Wolff, 771–78, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","Deutsche Bahn AG, “The Track 17 memorial at Grunewald railway station,” Deutsche Bahn history pages, https://www.deutschebahn.com/en/group/history/topics/platform17_memorial-6929106. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Deportations to Killing Centers,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/deportations-to-killing-centers. [Open access]","Edward Westermann, review of <i>Holocaust versus Wehrmacht: How Hitler's “Final Solution” Undermined the German War Effort</i>, by Yaron Pasher, H-War, H-Net Reviews, November 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=44880. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Deportations,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/deportations. [Open access]","The Wiener Holocaust Library, “Types of camps,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/the-camps/types-of-camps/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the deportations not have happened without the railway?","a":"Because millions of people had to be moved hundreds of kilometres to killing centres in occupied Poland, and only the railways could do that. The Transport Ministry drew up the timetables, and Deutsche Bahn itself says the research literature agrees that without the Reichsbahn the deportations would not have been possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the Ganzenmüller letters matter as evidence?","a":"Because they show a senior transport official reporting the daily trains to Treblinka and Bełżec to Himmler's office in plain numbers, and Himmler's staff chief thanking him. When Karl Wolff was shown the letters after the war, he admitted they were genuine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a memorial to the deportations take until 1998?","a":"Because for decades both the West German Bundesbahn and the East German railway were unwilling to look critically at the Reichsbahn's part in the crimes. Only after the railways were merged into Deutsche Bahn did the board decide on a central memorial at Grunewald station.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Ostbahn_Krakau_shuttle_train_Warschau-Treblinka.jpg","title":"Timetable order no. 548, Kraków, 3 August 1942","desc":"Timetable order of the General Directorate of the Eastern Railway in Kraków, dated 3 August 1942, for a daily special train with resettlers from Warsaw to Treblinka from 6 August, and the empty train back.","credit":"Generaldirektion der Ostbahn, public domain (official work), via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostbahn_Krakau_shuttle_train_Warschau-Treblinka.jpg","alt":"A typewritten German railway document headed with the Kraków railway directorate and a date in August 1942, listing departure and arrival times between Warsaw, Małkinia and Treblinka."},"wiki":"Deutsche Reichsbahn"},
    "ww2-763": {"questions":["Captured almost intact by Soviet troops in July 1944, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the edge of Lublin was the first major Nazi concentration camp to be overrun by the Allies.","A Polish-Soviet commission claimed in 1944 that about 1.5 million people had died at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where research now puts the dead at about 80,000, some 60,000 of them Jews."],"abstract":"<b>Majdanek</b>, officially Konzentrationslager Lublin, was the SS camp on the south-eastern edge of Lublin that operated from October 1941 to July 1944, ordered by Heinrich Himmler in July 1941 for 25,000 to 50,000 prisoners to work in SS workshops and on building sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began as a prisoner-of-war camp of the Waffen-SS and was renamed a concentration camp in February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its first prisoners were about 2,000 Soviet prisoners of war, almost all of whom were dead by February 1942, and from December 1941 Jews from Lublin and then from transports bound for the killing centres were brought in as forced labourers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also held Polish political prisoners, villagers expelled from the Zamość region and Belarusian deportees, and stored the belongings of Jews murdered at Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the autumn of 1942 it had gas chambers using Zyklon B and carbon monoxide, in which Jews judged unfit for work and exhausted prisoners were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 3 November 1943, in Operation Harvest Festival, more than 18,000 Jews were shot in ditches behind Field V, the largest single execution in the history of the concentration camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The camp was captured almost intact on 22 to 24 July 1944, and as the first major Nazi camp to be liberated it was shown to journalists by the Soviet authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Polish-Soviet commission reported that about 1.5 million people had been killed there, a figure the Soviet prosecution put before the Nuremberg tribunal on 19 February 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later research has cut that figure sharply, and the State Museum at Majdanek now counts about 80,000 dead among some 130,000 people deported there, about 60,000 of them Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other estimates differ: the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum gives 80,000 to 110,000 dead in the main camp and 95,000 to 130,000 in the whole camp system, while the German Historical Museum gives at least 78,000 dead in all and another USHMM page at least 89,000 Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lublin/Majdanek Concentration Camp: Conditions,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lublin-majdanek-concentration-camp-conditions. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lublin/Majdanek: Key Dates,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lublin-majdanek-key-dates. [Open access]","State Museum at Majdanek, “Brief Outline,” Camp History, https://www.majdanek.eu/en/camp-history/brief-outline. [Open access]","State Museum at Majdanek, “Extermination,” Camp History, https://www.majdanek.eu/en/camp-history/extermination. [Open access]","Jenny Oertle, “Vernichtungslager Majdanek,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/vernichtungslager-majdanek. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 19 February 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-19-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Concentration Camp System: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/concentration-camp-system-in-depth. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is Majdanek no longer counted as a sixth killing centre?","a":"Because it served mainly to hold Jews spared for a time as forced labourers, alongside Polish prisoners and deported villagers, although Jews judged unfit for work were killed there by shooting or gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the SS shoot the Jews of the Lublin camps in November 1943?","a":"Because armed Jewish resistance in the Białystok and Vilna ghettos and the uprisings at Treblinka and Sobibór made the SS leadership afraid of further revolts, and it decided to kill the remaining Jews of the Lublin district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the death toll fall from 1.5 million to about 80,000?","a":"Because the 1.5 million was the claim of the Polish-Soviet commission of 1944, put before the Nuremberg tribunal by the Soviet prosecution, while the museum on the site, drawing on its latest research, now counts about 80,000 dead among some 130,000 deported. Other present-day estimates still differ, from at least 78,000 to 110,000 in the main camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Majdanek_%28June_24%2C_1944%29.jpg","title":"Majdanek from the air, 24 June 1944","desc":"A reconnaissance photograph of the camp taken a month before its liberation, with barracks being dismantled in the lower half and those still in use above.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (State Museum at Majdanek). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Majdanek_(June_24,_1944).jpg","alt":"An aerial view of rows of long wooden barracks divided by roads and fences, with timber stacked beside a road in the foreground."},"wiki":"Majdanek concentration camp"},
    "ww2-764": {"questions":["Richard Glücks headed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 1939 until 1945, years in which camps such as Buchenwald came to run dozens of satellite camps beside war factories.","Prisoners passed into the hands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the moment they entered a camp, and it answered for them until they died or were released, beyond the reach of any court."],"abstract":"The <b>Concentration Camps Inspectorate</b>, in German Inspektion der Konzentrationslager or IKL, was the SS office that ran the Nazi concentration camp system from 1934 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1934 Heinrich Himmler placed the camps holding prisoners in so-called protective custody under it and made Theodor Eicke, the commandant of Dachau, its chief, and Richard Glücks replaced Eicke in 1939 and held the post until 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1942 it became Office Group D of Oswald Pohl's SS Economic and Administrative Main Office, a move that matched Himmler's decision to use camp labour for the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The prosecutors who tried Pohl and 17 other officials of that office at Nuremberg in 1947 described its duties as the prisoners' food, clothing, housing, sanitation, medical care and discipline, and the supply of their labour to public and private employers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The SS Death's-Head Units that guarded and administered the camps came under the Inspectorate and were notorious for their cruelty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its camps stood outside German law, beyond review by any court, and while the Gestapo or the criminal police ordered each arrest, the Inspectorate held the prisoner from arrival until death or release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1939 it controlled seven large camps, Dachau, Sachsenhausen, Buchenwald, Neuengamme, Flossenbürg, Mauthausen and Ravensbrück, and in 1938 Eicke was one of the three SS leaders who inspected the site for Mauthausen beside a stone quarry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Especially in 1943 and 1944 hundreds of subcamps grew up beside factories as prisoners were put to work in the armaments industry, and Buchenwald alone had 88 by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Camp staff routinely reported murders as suicides, as shootings of prisoners trying to escape or as deaths from a weak heart, and nearly half of the more than 700,000 prisoners left in the system in January 1945 had died by the end of May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the Inspectorate's own work in the war years is hard to trace, because its wartime records have largely not survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Concentration Camp System: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/concentration-camp-system-in-depth. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Camps,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pohl Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), 202–3, 216–17, 268–69, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","Bertrand Perz, review of <i>KL: Die Geschichte der nationalsozialistischen Konzentrationslager</i>, by Nikolaus Wachsmann, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, July 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47276. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings, Case #4, The Pohl Case,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings-case-4-the-pohl-case. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mauthausen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mauthausen. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Inspectorate put under the SS economic office in 1942?","a":"Because Himmler had decided to use camp prisoners more intensively for the war effort, and placing the camps under Oswald Pohl's SS Economic and Administrative Main Office in March 1942 tied them to the supply of labour. Pohl's own defence at Nuremberg argued that the move was forced by the state of the German labour market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could no court check what happened inside the camps?","a":"Because the camps rested on an authority outside the law that Hitler granted as Führer, and from 1933 no judicial or administrative body outside the SS and police could review them. Prisoners were held without charge, indefinitely and without legal recourse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Inspectorate's wartime work hard to document?","a":"Because the Inspectorate's own records for the war years are largely missing, and the camps falsified the records they did keep, writing up murders as suicides, escape attempts or heart failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/T-Geb%C3%A4ude.jpg","title":"The T-building at Oranienburg","desc":"The so-called T-building in Oranienburg near Berlin, photographed in 2011, which once housed the camp inspectorate and is now a tax office and the seat of the Brandenburg memorials foundation.","credit":"Jumbo1435, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T-Geb%C3%A4ude.jpg","alt":"A long white three-storey building with a dark tiled roof and two wings stands behind a lawn planted with young trees under a cloudy sky."},"wiki":"Concentration Camps Inspectorate"},
    "ww2-765": {"questions":["Prisoners carrying granite up the steps of the Wiener Graben quarry at Mauthausen, where at least 90,000 died, were worked to death in what prosecutors called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the camps.","Pohl's order of April 1942 that camp working hours have no limit turned the camps to armaments work, and the American prosecution at Nuremberg tied it directly to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a programme."],"abstract":"<b>Extermination through labour</b>, in German Vernichtung durch Arbeit, was the working of concentration camp prisoners to death, and the phrase itself appears in a 1942 agreement between Himmler and the Reich Ministry of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Labour was deadly in the camps before the war, above all in the quarries that supplied SS building, and the Nuremberg judgment found that camp conditions made labour and death almost synonymous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Mauthausen in Austria the SS founded the German Earth and Stone Works in 1938 to quarry granite with prisoners, who carried the blocks up more than 180 steps out of the Wiener Graben quarry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In January 1941 Reinhard Heydrich made Mauthausen a category III camp, for prisoners judged beyond reform, and in the early war years it and its satellite Gusen had the harshest conditions and highest mortality of all the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly three-quarters of some 4,000 Spanish Republicans held at Gusen in 1940 died within a year, and of 4,400 Soviet prisoners of war brought in 1941 fewer than 500 were alive by early 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In 1942 the camps were turned towards armaments, and on 30 April Oswald Pohl reported to Himmler that the economic side now came first and ordered that there be no limit to working hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The memorandum of Himmler's meeting with the justice minister Thierack on 18 September 1942 provided for the transfer of asocial elements from prisons to the SS for extermination through work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those to be handed over included all in protective arrest, Jews, Roma, Russians, Ukrainians, Czechs, Poles serving over 3 years and Germans serving over eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Nuremberg the American prosecution presented these documents as evidence of a Nazi programme of extermination through work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From late 1943 prisoners were driven to dig underground factories at Melk, Ebensee and Sankt Georgen an der Gusen, and of some 190,000 people held in the Mauthausen system at least 90,000 died by the memorial's count, at least 95,000 by the USHMM's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 3, proceedings of 12 December 1945, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/12-12-45.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Mauthausen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mauthausen. [Open access]","Mauthausen Memorial, “The Mauthausen Concentration Camp 1938–1945,” KZ-Gedenkstätte Mauthausen, https://www.mauthausen-memorial.org/en/History/The-Mauthausen-Concentration-Camp-19381945. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Gusen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gusen. [Open access]","Bertrand Perz, review of <i>KL: Die Geschichte der nationalsozialistischen Konzentrationslager</i>, by Nikolaus Wachsmann, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, July 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47276. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the SS build camps beside quarries?","a":"Because the SS wanted stone for its building projects and meant to extract it with prisoner labour, which is why it founded the German Earth and Stone Works at Mauthausen in 1938 and sent prisoners daily to the Gusen quarries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the justice ministry hand prisoners over to the SS?","a":"Because under the agreement of 18 September 1942 the ministry gave up whole groups of prisoners to Himmler, and Jews, Poles, Roma, Russians and Ukrainians were in future to be dealt with by the SS instead of being tried by the ordinary courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did deaths rise again late in the war?","a":"Because from the end of 1943 prisoners were driven to dig underground factories under murderous conditions, and in the last months camps such as Mauthausen were swamped by evacuation transports, with overcrowding, hunger and disease killing great numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_192-269%2C_KZ_Mauthausen%2C_H%C3%A4ftlinge_im_Steinbruch.jpg","title":"Stone carriers in the Mauthausen quarry","desc":"An SS photograph of prisoners of the Mauthausen penal company carrying stone blocks up the steps out of the Wiener Graben quarry.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 192-269, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_192-269,_KZ_Mauthausen,_H%C3%A4ftlinge_im_Steinbruch.jpg","alt":"A long column of men in striped clothes climbs a steep rock stairway out of a quarry, each carrying a stone block on his back."},"wiki":"Extermination through labour"},
    "ww2-766": {"questions":["The Nuremberg tribunal found in 1948 that prisoners on the Auschwitz building site of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived and worked under the shadow of extermination, yet passed no sentence longer than 8 years.","Degesch, the firm that sold Zyklon B to the SS, was 42.5 per cent owned by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose directors on its board were cleared of knowing how the gas was used."],"abstract":"<b>IG Farben</b> was the German chemical conglomerate that from 1941 built a vast synthetic rubber and fuel plant at Monowitz, beside Auschwitz in Upper Silesia, using the forced labour of concentration camp prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Farben owned and financed the plant, which was to make buna, a synthetic rubber, and motor fuel for the armed forces, and built it to last into peacetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the first building conference, on 24 March 1941, the SS was reported to have promised 700 Auschwitz prisoners, and by October the camp was supplying 1,300 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prisoners were marched about 7 kilometres (4 miles) to the site under SS guard, and in the winter of 1941 to 1942 many who grew too weak to work were gassed at Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such use of camp labour spread through German industry, and without millions of forced labourers, prisoners of war and camp inmates the Reich could not have fought on beyond 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In 1942, at Farben's instigation, the Monowitz camp was built across the road, and from November 1943 it was the third main camp of the Auschwitz complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Monowitz grew from about 2,000 prisoners to almost 11,000 by the summer of 1944, and the Auschwitz Museum counts 1,670 murdered at the site or dead in its hospital and 11,000 sent back to Auschwitz and Birkenau, most of whom were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In July 1948 an American tribunal at Nuremberg found that the camp workers lived and worked under the shadow of extermination, and that the defendants closest to the project had taken the initiative in employing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It cleared the Farben directors on the board of Degesch, the firm that sold Zyklon B and was 42.5 per cent Farben-owned, of knowing how the gas was used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of 23 defendants 13 were convicted and 10 acquitted outright, and the sentences ran from 1.5 to 8 years, the longest going to Otto Ambros and to the site's chief engineer, Dürrfeld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Farben Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1952), 1167–68, 1180–85, 1205–7, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-VIII/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-VIII.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings, Case #6, The IG Farben Case,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings-case-6-the-ig-farben-case. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Monowitz,” Auschwitz sub-camps, Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/auschwitz-sub-camps/monowitz/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Concentration Camp System: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/concentration-camp-system-in-depth. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Forced Labor,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/forced-labor. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Zwangsarbeit,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft/zwangsarbeit. [in German] [Open access]","The Wiener Holocaust Library, “Case Study: Auschwitz-Birkenau,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/the-camps/case-study-auschwitz-birkenau/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Farben want concentration camp prisoners at all?","a":"Because free workers could not be found in the numbers the building firms needed, labour procurement was Farben's own responsibility, and Himmler's office had promised the help of the Auschwitz camp. Across Germany, forced labour had become indispensable to the war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Monowitz prisoners afraid to report sick?","a":"Because a prisoner too weak to work could be selected by the SS and sent to Birkenau to be gassed. The tribunal found that this fear deterred many from seeking medical help and drove many, especially Jews, to work until they collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Monowitz camp built beside the plant?","a":"Because Farben pressed for it; until then the prisoners had been marched to the site from the main camp about 7 kilometres away. Monowitz was physically somewhat better than the main camp, but its prisoners stayed under SS control and those unable to work were sent away to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Heinrich_Himmler%2C_IG_Farben_Auschwitz_plant%2C_July_1942.jpeg","title":"Himmler at the Auschwitz plant, July 1942","desc":"Heinrich Himmler, with an IG Farben engineer in a dark suit at his side and the Auschwitz commandant among the officers, inspecting the building site of the plant in July 1942.","credit":"SS photographer of the Auschwitz identification service, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Institute of National Remembrance, Poland). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Himmler,_IG_Farben_Auschwitz_plant,_July_1942.jpeg","alt":"A group of SS officers in caps and boots walks along a railway track on a building site beside a man in a suit and hat, with scaffolding behind them."},"wiki":"IG Farben"},
    "ww2-767": {"questions":["The tribunal that judged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Nuremberg in 1947 set out rules for research whose first is that the voluntary consent of the human subject is absolutely essential.","Sterilisation trials at Auschwitz and Ravensbrück and the twin studies of Josef Mengele formed the racial strand of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, beside military and drug testing in other camps."],"abstract":"<b>Nazi human experimentation</b> was the series of painful and often deadly medical experiments that German doctors carried out on thousands of concentration camp prisoners, without their consent, during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many German doctors had backed racial hygiene before 1933 and welcomed the regime's stress on heredity, its careers and its research money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One group of experiments served the armed forces, and at Dachau in August 1942 prisoners were immersed in cold water until their body temperature fell to 28 °C (82 °F), while others were put in pressure chambers or made to test ways of drinking seawater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second group tested vaccines and drugs against malaria, typhus, tuberculosis and other diseases in several camps, while at Ravensbrück doctors carried out bone grafts and tested sulfa drugs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third group served racial ideology, from Josef Mengele's experiments on twins at Auschwitz to sterilisation trials, mainly at Auschwitz and Ravensbrück, meant to find a cheap way of sterilising Jews, Roma and others in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Mengele, chief camp physician of Birkenau from November 1943, was a trained researcher whose Auschwitz work served other German scientists, and many of his subjects died or were killed so their bodies could be examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 9 December 1946 an American military tribunal at Nuremberg opened the trial of 23 doctors and administrators, the first of the Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its judgment found that the experiments were not the acts of individual doctors working alone but the product of policy planned at high government, military and Nazi Party levels as part of the total war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sixteen defendants were found guilty on 20 August 1947, and the seven sentenced to death were executed on 2 June 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The judgment also laid down ten principles for permissible research on human beings, later known as the Nuremberg Code, of which the first is that the voluntary consent of the human subject is absolutely essential.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Medical Experiments,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-medical-experiments. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Doctors Trial: The Medical Case of the Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-doctors-trial-the-medical-case-of-the-subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Josef Mengele,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/josef-mengele. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Medical Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office), 171, 181–82, 298–300, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-II/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-II.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]","The Wiener Holocaust Library, “Case Study: Auschwitz-Birkenau,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/the-camps/case-study-auschwitz-birkenau/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many German doctors take part?","a":"Because many had supported racial hygiene before 1933 and welcomed the regime's funding and careers, and the Nuremberg judges found the experiments were planned at high levels of government rather than being the work of lone doctors. Mengele himself was a respected researcher whose work fed that of other scientists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Nuremberg Code still important?","a":"Because it set out, for the first time in a court judgment, standards for research on people, above all the rule that the human subject must give voluntary, informed consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do scientists reject using the results?","a":"Because the experiments were done in inhumane conditions on prisoners who had not consented, and to questionable research standards. Only a few of the experts involved were ever tried or disciplined, and many carried on their careers after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nazi human experimentation"},
    "ww2-768": {"questions":["Nazi officials ordered that the property of murdered Jews be called goods originating from thefts, and prosecutors at Nuremberg valued this <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Lublin area alone at over 180 million Reichsmarks.","Elderly German Jews signed home purchase contracts for a retirement home that turned out to be Theresienstadt, one of the ruses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that took their last savings."],"abstract":"<b>Nazi plunder</b> of Jewish property was the systematic theft of everything Jews owned, from businesses and bank accounts before the war to the clothes, watches and gold teeth of those murdered in the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jews deported in the war lost what remained, for their blocked accounts were seized and their belongings auctioned or given to Germans bombed out of their homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elderly German Jews were even made to sign home purchase contracts that took their savings in return for a promised place in a retirement home, which turned out to be the Theresienstadt ghetto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the camps plunder followed murder, and a Soviet commission found in six surviving storehouses at Auschwitz, 29 others having been burned, 348,820 sets of men's clothing and 836,255 of women's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Majdanek held storerooms for the clothing and belongings taken from Jews killed at Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, and its prisoners worked in a large clothing depot in Lublin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The SS Economic and Administrative Main Office managed the proceeds, and on 26 September 1942 its official August Frank ordered cash paid into a Reichsbank account and gold from teeth, jewellery and foreign currency delivered for the bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His order said the property was in future to be called goods originating from thefts, receipt of stolen goods and hoarded goods, and that watches, fountain pens and scissors were to be repaired and sent to front-line troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Odilo Globocnik's report on Operation Reinhard divided it into four branches, of which the third was the utilisation of property, and in July 1944 Oswald Pohl sent Himmler gold wristwatches from it for commanders of technical units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that Walther Funk's Reichsbank stored gold from victims' spectacles and teeth in its vaults, and the German Historical Museum puts the valuables taken in Operation Reinhard at about 180 million Reichsmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the war, a conference of historians and restitution officials concluded in 2014, the former owners' property rights were only rarely restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pohl Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), opening statement of the prosecution, 254–58, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pohl Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), documents NO-057 and NO-2756, 715–21, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Lublin/Majdanek Concentration Camp: Conditions,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lublin-majdanek-concentration-camp-conditions. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, ““Aryanization”,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/aryanization. [Open access]","Laura Meier-Ewert, conference report on <i>Dispossession: The Plundering of German Jewry, 1933–1945 and Beyond</i>, Boston University, 9–11 November 2014, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, February 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=43436. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, vol. 7, proceedings of 19 February 1946, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/02-19-46.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Funk,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judfunk.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the SS keep such careful accounts of stolen property?","a":"Because the property was treated as income of the Reich: cash went to a Reichsbank account, gold and jewels were delivered to the bank, and useful goods were repaired and issued to troops or to the office resettling ethnic Germans. Prosecutors later used these very accounts as evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the victims' belongings stored at Majdanek and Auschwitz?","a":"Because sorting and storing clothes, shoes and valuables was part of the killing operation, so that nothing of value was lost to the Reich. Majdanek held the belongings of Jews murdered at the Reinhard camps, and Auschwitz had dozens of storehouses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was so little property returned after the war?","a":"Because restitution became caught up in postwar politics, with East and West Berlin using property questions against each other, and dealers in looted art went on trading after the war. The 2014 conference found that former owners' property rights had only rarely been restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nazi plunder"},
    "ww2-769": {"questions":["At a meeting on 12 November 1938 Göring planned state trustees to value Jewish firms as low as possible, speeding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the German economy after the November pogrom.","In the Czech lands after March 1939 the occupiers left much persecution to Czech authorities but kept for themselves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Jewish property and the theft of valuables."],"abstract":"<b>Aryanisation</b>, in German Arisierung, was the transfer of Jewish-owned businesses and property to non-Jewish owners in Nazi Germany between 1933 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In early 1933 Germany had about 100,000 Jewish-owned businesses, half of them small shops selling mostly clothing or footwear, the rest factories, workshops and professional offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a first, so-called voluntary phase, opened by the boycott of 1 April 1933, terror, propaganda and laws drove owners to sell, and by 1938 some two-thirds of these firms had closed or passed to non-Jews, often at 20 or 30 per cent of their value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pogrom of 9 to 10 November 1938 opened the second, forced phase, and a decree of 12 November, proposed by the economics minister Walther Funk, barred Jews from all business activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each remaining Jewish firm got a non-Jewish trustee to arrange its sale, whose fee, paid by the owner, was often little below the price, and part of the proceeds went to Göring's Four Year Plan office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The policy was set out at a meeting Hermann Göring chaired in the Reich Air Ministry on 12 November 1938, where he recalled the decision to aryanise the German economy and take the Jew out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He wanted state trustees to value each business and fix the owner's payment as low as possible, so that, as he put it, the state would not suffer but profit from the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany's Jews were made to pay a so-called atonement of one billion Reichsmarks, denied their insurance money and made to repair the pogrom's damage themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These measures were meant to force emigration, yet the Reich Flight Tax took much of what emigrants owned, the rest was frozen in blocked accounts, and after March 1939 the occupiers also aryanised Jewish property in the Czech lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Historians now see the theft as part of the persecution, not a by-product, and one estimate puts German Jews' assets in 1936 at about 6.4 billion Reichsmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, ““Aryanization”,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/aryanization. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Ausgrenzung und Verfolgung,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung. [in German] [Open access]","“Document 1816-PS: Stenographic Report of the Meeting on the Jewish Question under the Chairmanship of Field Marshal Goering in the Reich Air Ministry, 12 November 1938,” in Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/1816-ps.asp. [Open access]","Oliver Schweinoch, “Novemberpogrome 1938,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung-und-verfolgung/novemberpogrom-1938. [in German] [Open access]","Laura Meier-Ewert, conference report on <i>Dispossession: The Plundering of German Jewry, 1933–1945 and Beyond</i>, Boston University, 9–11 November 2014, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, February 2015, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=43436. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Funk,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judfunk.asp. [Open access]","Jens Hoppe, review of <i>The Holocaust in Bohemia and Moravia: Czech Initiatives, German Policies, Jewish Responses</i>, by Wolf Gruner, H-Judaic, H-Net Reviews, February 2023, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=57549. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Jewish owners sell for so little before 1938?","a":"Because terror, boycotts, propaganda and discriminatory laws had made it ever harder to run a business, and many owners were desperate to emigrate or to get rid of a failing firm, so they accepted 20 or 30 per cent of its value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Göring want the state to control the transfers?","a":"Because he saw the question as an economic one and wanted the gain to go to the state and the war economy. At the meeting of 12 November 1938 he had trustees fix the owners' payments as low as possible, and part of the proceeds went to his Four Year Plan office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the regime fine the Jews after the pogrom?","a":"Because it wanted the victims to bear the cost of the destruction and to be driven to emigrate. The billion-Reichsmark payment, the confiscated insurance money and the repairs at their own expense were part of the push that, with forced sales, stripped them of their livelihood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Aryanization"},
    "ww2-770": {"questions":["Armbands with the Star of David, blocked bank accounts and registration for forced labour in late 1939 were the first stages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which turned to mass murder in 1941.","Most victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were killed by gas in Chełmno, Bełżec, Sobibór, Treblinka and Auschwitz-Birkenau, the killing centres the Germans built on occupied Polish soil."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in Poland</b> was the destruction by Nazi Germany of Polish Jewry, which with about 3 million people, 9.5 per cent of the population, was the largest Jewish community in eastern Europe before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Germany and the Soviet Union divided the country in 1939, as many as 1.3 million Jews lived in the Soviet-annexed east, the rest in lands annexed to the Reich or in the Generalgouvernement, ruled by Hans Frank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under German rule Jewish bank accounts were blocked, Jews over 10 had to wear a Star of David armband from December 1939, men were registered for forced labour, and Jews from the annexed lands were expelled eastwards from November 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From October 1939 Jews were forced into ghettos, the first at Piotrków Trybunalski, and in Warsaw alone hunger and typhus killed more than 43,000 in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every community had a Judenrat, a Jewish council appointed by the Germans to carry out their orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After June 1941 the Einsatzgruppen shot Jews in almost every town of the former Polish east, killing about 150,000 by the end of October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From December 1941 the murder spread to the rest of Poland, and about 2 million Polish Jews were gassed in killing centres on Polish soil, mostly between March 1942 and October 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The three camps of Operation Reinhard alone received about 1,526,000 Jews by the USHMM's count, and the German Historical Museum puts the dead there at about 1.75 million, most of them Polish Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the Warsaw ghetto, which had held about 400,000 Jews, more than 300,000 were taken between 22 July and 3 October 1942, and by January 1944 Frank reckoned only 103,000 Jews were left in his territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The last great ghetto, Łódź, was sent to its death in August 1944, and in all almost 3 million Polish Jews, close to 90 per cent of the prewar community, perished between September 1939 and January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["David Engel, “Poland: Poland since 1939,” <i>The YIVO Encyclopedia of Jews in Eastern Europe</i>, https://encyclopedia.yivo.org/article/18. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Population of Europe in 1933: Population Data by Country,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-population-of-europe-in-1933-population-data-by-country. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Deportations to Killing Centers,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/deportations-to-killing-centers. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Völkermord,” <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pohl Case</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), opening statement of the prosecution, 253–54, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-V.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Concentration Camp System: In Depth,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/concentration-camp-system-in-depth. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Frank,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals</i>, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judfrank.asp. [Open access]","The Wiener Holocaust Library, “German occupation and alliances,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/life-in-nazi-occupied-europe/occupation-case-studies/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did deportation to the Soviet interior save some Polish Jews?","a":"Because it took them out of reach of the German killing. About 400,000 Jews were among the Polish citizens the Soviets deported in 1939 to 1941, and though perhaps a third of the deportees died, the largest group of Polish Jewish survivors, about 230,000, spent the war in the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some Jewish councils hope work would save their ghettos?","a":"Because their leaders believed the Germans might spare ghettos that proved useful to the war effort, a strategy of salvation through work. In pursuing it they often drove the ghetto population hard, which estranged them from it, and it did not stop the deportations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the figures for the Reinhard camps given as a range?","a":"Because different counts measure different things: the USHMM counts about 1,526,000 Jews deported to Bełżec, Sobibór and Treblinka, while the German Historical Museum puts the number killed there at about 1.75 million. The Nuremberg judgment held Frank part of a programme that murdered at least three million Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in Poland"},
    "ww2-771": {"questions":["About 2,650,000 Jews were left under German occupation after mobilisation, evacuation and flight, and estimates of the dead in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> range from about 1,340,000 to 2,600,000.","Otto Ohlendorf told the Nuremberg tribunal that his Einsatzgruppe D reported 90,000 people killed in its first year, one unit’s share of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that began in June 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in the Soviet Union</b> was the murder of Jews in the Soviet lands that Germany and its allies occupied after the invasion of 22 June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On figures given by the Israeli historian Yitzhak Arad, about 5,150,000 Jews lived in the Soviet Union when the invasion began, some 4,250,000 of them in regions that Germany and its allies would occupy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many were mobilised into the Red Army, evacuated or fled east before the Germans arrived, but about 2,650,000 were left under occupation, almost all of whom were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One study described in a Russian survey puts the Jews murdered on Soviet territory at about 2,600,000, of whom 1,600,000 died in the western lands annexed under the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The US Holocaust Memorial Museum’s table of losses by country, which counts Latvia and Lithuania separately, gives about 1,340,000 Jewish deaths for the Soviet Union and warns that every figure is an estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the victims were not deported to camps but shot near their homes by the four Einsatzgruppen of the Security Police and SD, helped by the Waffen-SS, the Order Police, the army, Romanian forces and local collaborators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Otto Ohlendorf, who led Einsatzgruppe D, told the Nuremberg tribunal in January 1946 that the order was to kill the Jews and the Soviet political commissars, and that his units reported 90,000 people killed in their first year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prosecutors at the later Einsatzgruppen trial reckoned that no more than 3,000 men had killed at least 1,000,000 people in about 2 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Babi Yar outside Kiev the groups’ own reports counted 33,771 Jews shot in 2 days in September 1941, and the museum puts all those killed by shooting or in gas vans on Soviet territory at between 1,500,000 and more than 2,000,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet state long played down the antisemitic character of Nazi policy, though the genocide was never wholly silenced, for Yiddish publications reported the fate of the occupied lands’ Jews fairly often.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Einsatzgruppen: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/einsatzgruppen. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Losses during the Holocaust: By Country,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-losses-during-the-holocaust-by-country. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Otto Ohlendorf, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 4, <i>United States of America v. Otto Ohlendorf et al.</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office), 38–39, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV.pdf. [Open access]","M. M. Mints, “Sovetskie evrei v gody voiny i Kholokosta (svodnyi referat),” <i>Istoriia Rossii v sovremennoi zarubezhnoi nauke</i>, no. 1 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/sovetskie-evrei-v-gody-voyny-i-holokosta-svodnyy-referat. [in Russian] [Open access]","M. M. Mints, “Sovetskie evrei v gody voiny i Kholokosta: noveishaia zarubezhnaia istoriografiia (referativnyi obzor),” <i>Sotsial’nye i gumanitarnye nauki. Otechestvennaia i zarubezhnaia literatura. Seriia 5: Istoriia</i>, no. 1 (2016), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2016-01-016-m-m-mints-sovetskie-evrei-v-gody-voyny-i-holokosta-noveyshaya-zarubezhnaya-istoriografiya-referativnyy-obzor. [in Russian] [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Persecution of the Jews,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the death tolls for Soviet Jews differ so widely?","a":"Because they count different territories and no master list exists. One estimate of about 2,600,000 includes the 1,600,000 who died in the western lands the Soviet Union annexed under the Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact, while the US museum’s table, which lists Latvia and Lithuania separately, gives about 1,340,000 and calls every figure an estimate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were so many Soviet Jews shot rather than deported?","a":"Because the killing units followed the army into the occupied lands and murdered Jews where they found them, rounding up whole communities and shooting them at pits outside their towns. The four groups had only about 3,000 men, but the Waffen-SS, the Order Police, the army, Romanian forces and local collaborators helped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German army matter to the killing squads?","a":"Because the shootings took place in its operational areas. Ohlendorf told the tribunal that the armies had no order to kill but had to be ordered to support the groups, and that without such instructions their activities would not have been possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in the Soviet Union"},
    "ww2-772": {"questions":["Because Horthy and Kállay refused German demands to deport, the main phase of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came only after March 1944, when about 770,000 Jews were still alive there.","Transit ghettos set up by Hungarian officials in brickyards and factories near railway lines, and guarded by gendarmes, were the last stage of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the trains left."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in Hungary</b> was the persecution and murder of the Jews under Hungarian rule, carried out by the Hungarian government on its own initiative and, from 1944, together with Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 825,000 Jews were under Hungarian control during the war, counting lands annexed from Czechoslovakia, Romania and Yugoslavia in 1938–41, and about 550,000 of them were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In a first stage from 1938, anti-Jewish laws pushed Jews out of economic and social life, Jewish men were forced into labour service, and between 44,000 and 63,000 Jews were killed as a result of Hungarian actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Regent Miklós Horthy and Prime Minister Miklós Kállay refused German demands to deport, so that in March 1944 between 760,000 and 780,000 Jews still lived in Hungary, the largest Jewish population left alive in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany occupied its ally on 19 March 1944, kept Horthy as regent and had him appoint the pro-German Döme Sztójay as prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Adolf Eichmann arrived with a special commando of the Security Police, and his officer Dieter Wisliceny told the Nuremberg tribunal that the concentration of Jews began zone by zone in Ruthenia and Transylvania in mid-April 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hungarian officials and gendarmes held the Jews in transit ghettos in brickyards, factories and warehouses near railway lines, and then forced them onto freight trains alongside German deportation experts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 15 May and 9 July 1944 about 437,000 Jews were deported on 147 trains, about 420,000 of them to Auschwitz-Birkenau, where some 100,000 were selected for labour and about 330,000 murdered on arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rudolf Höss, its former commandant, put the Hungarian Jews killed there that summer at 400,000, and the Auschwitz museum counts the Jews deported from Hungary as the largest single group the Security Police sent to the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Horthy ordered a halt on 7 July 1944, and when a Hungarian note in August called the deported Jews workers placed at Germany’s disposal, Washington replied that the officials responsible would answer for their fate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Holocaust in Hungary,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hungary-after-the-german-occupation. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 11 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 15 April 1946, testimony of Rudolf Höss, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-15-46.asp. [Open access]","Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, “Jews in Auschwitz,” https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/jews-in-auschwitz/. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1966), document 660, Leland Harrison to the Secretary of State, Bern, 5 August 1944, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v01/d660. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1966), document 669, the Secretary of State to Leland Harrison, Washington, 19 August 1944, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v01/d669. [Open access]","Anna Foa, “Shoah,” <i>Enciclopedia Italiana</i>, VII Appendice (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2007), Treccani, https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/shoah_(Enciclopedia-Italiana)/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the mass deportations from Hungary come so late in the war?","a":"Because Hungary was a sovereign ally until March 1944, and Horthy and Kállay refused German demands to deport its Jews, calling their fate a domestic matter. Only after the German occupation and the appointment of Sztójay did ghettoisation and deportation begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the summer of 1944 the deadliest period at Auschwitz-Birkenau?","a":"Because about 420,000 Jews from Hungary reached the camp in less than 2 months, and about three in four of them were murdered in the gas chambers on arrival. The Auschwitz museum counts them as the largest group the Security Police deported to the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hungarian officials matter so much to the deportations?","a":"Because the Germans sent only a small team. Wisliceny told the tribunal that Hungarian state secretaries made the formal request and the Hungarian police carried out the evacuation, while the museum describes mayors, gendarmes and officials running the ghettos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in Hungary"},
    "ww2-773": {"questions":["German police who ran the deportations almost alone, using exemptions later withdrawn one by one, help explain why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed about three in four Jews there.","Registration of 159,806 people as Jews in January 1941 and the yellow star from April 1942 were early steps in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Reich Commissioner Seyss-Inquart."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in the Netherlands</b> was the persecution, deportation and murder of the Jews of the occupied Netherlands, and it destroyed a far larger share of the Jewish population than the occupations of Belgium or France did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the invasion of May 1940 the country was put under a German civil administration headed by the Reich Commissioner Arthur Seyss-Inquart, which supervised the Dutch civil service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jews were barred from the civil service in 1940, made to register in January 1941, when 159,806 people did so, and made to wear a yellow star from 29 April 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Deportations ran from the summer of 1942 until the last train left the Westerbork transit camp for Auschwitz on 3 September 1944, and of some 107,000 Jews deported, mostly to Auschwitz and Sobibor, only 5,200 survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found Seyss-Inquart a knowing participant in the deportation of almost 120,000 of the 140,000 Jews of the Netherlands,.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Two historians writing for the Anne Frank House count about 104,000 Dutch Jews dead, about 74 percent, against more than 25,000 in Belgium, about 38 percent, and around 80,000 in France, about 25 percent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The US museum counts nearly 25,000 Jews deported from Belgium, where the civil administration refused to cooperate, and the Mémorial de la Shoah counts 76,000 deported from France, more than 11,000 of them children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They explain the Dutch toll above all by the German police, who ran the deportations almost alone and used deceptions such as exemptions later withdrawn one by one, moving the Jews with little open force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Belgium violent round-ups drove the remaining Jews, most of them immigrants wary of the authorities, into hiding, while in France the Vichy government stopped large-scale arrests from October 1942 after protests by the Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Organised help for people in hiding came late in the Netherlands, and the Auschwitz museum lists about 60,000 Jews from the Netherlands among those it received, against 69,000 from France and 25,000 from Belgium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Netherlands,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-netherlands. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Belgium,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/belgium. [Open access]","Pim Griffioen and Ron Zeller, “The Netherlands: The Greatest Number of Jewish Victims in Western Europe,” Anne Frank House, https://www.annefrank.org/en/anne-frank/go-in-depth/netherlands-greatest-number-jewish-victims-western-europe/. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), judgment, “Seyss-Inquart,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judseyss.asp. [Open access]","Mémorial de la Shoah, “Les Juifs de France dans la Shoah,” https://www.memorialdelashoah.org/archives-et-documentation/quest-ce-que-la-shoah/juifs-de-france-shoah.html. [in French] [Open access]","Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, “Jews in Auschwitz,” https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/jews-in-auschwitz/. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Persecution of the Jews,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a larger share of Dutch Jews die than of Belgian or French Jews?","a":"Because in the Netherlands the German police controlled the deportations almost without interference and used deception, while organised help for people in hiding came late. In Belgium violent round-ups sent Jews into hiding, and in France the Vichy government stopped large-scale arrests from October 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the figures for Dutch Jewish victims differ?","a":"Because they count different things. The usual figures are 107,000 deported and 102,000 dead, while a count of about 104,000 also includes suicides, deaths in camps inside the Netherlands and people who died in hiding or while trying to escape abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Belgian case different?","a":"Because most Jews in Belgium were foreign or stateless immigrants, mistrustful of official appeals, and the Belgian civil administration refused to cooperate. More than 25,000 went into hiding, and nearly 25,000 were deported to Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in the Netherlands"},
    "ww2-774": {"questions":["Deported on the last transport from Westerbork to Auschwitz in September 1944, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died of typhus in Bergen-Belsen in early 1945, aged fifteen, 2 years before her diary appeared.","Inspired by a Radio Orange appeal to keep wartime diaries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began in 1944 to rewrite her own as a book called <i>Het Achterhuis</i>, published by her father in 1947."],"abstract":"<b>Anne Frank</b> was a German-born Jewish girl who hid with her family from the Nazis in occupied Amsterdam for 2 years and kept a diary that is many readers’ first encounter with the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was born Annelies Marie Frank in Frankfurt am Main on 12 June 1929, the second daughter of Otto and Edith Frank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the Nazis came to power her father moved to Amsterdam and set up a branch of the Opekta company, and Anne, the last to arrive, followed in February 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the German invasion of May 1940 new laws barred Jews from parks, cinemas and non-Jewish shops, cost her father his business and sent Jewish children to separate schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When her sister Margot was called up for a so-called labour camp on 5 July 1942, the family went into hiding the next day in an annex behind the business at Prinsengracht 263, where four more people later joined them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Anne wrote about life in the annex in the diary she had been given for her thirteenth birthday, and after an appeal on Radio Orange to keep wartime diaries she began in the summer of 1944 to rewrite it as a book, <i>Het Achterhuis</i>, ‘The Annex’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Police found the eight on 4 August 1944, and why is still unknown, since no record of the raid survives and betrayal has never been proven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The eight left Westerbork, the transit camp through which more than 100,000 Jews passed, on its last transport to Auschwitz-Birkenau on 3 September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Anne and Margot were moved to Bergen-Belsen in early November 1944, and both died of typhus there in February or March 1945, leaving Otto the only survivor of the eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otto published the diary on 25 June 1947 in 3,000 copies, forensic study of the 1980s found that Anne wrote both versions between 1942 and 1944, and an English court in 2000 found doubts about its authenticity without substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Anne Frank House, “Who Was Anne Frank?,” https://www.annefrank.org/en/anne-frank/who-was-anne-frank/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Anne Frank Biography: Who Was Anne Frank?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/anne-frank-biography. [Open access]","Levke Harders and Oliver Schweinoch, “Anne Frank 1929–1945,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, Berlin, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/anne-frank. [in German] [Open access]","Anne Frank Fonds, “Anne Frank,” Basel, https://www.annefrank.ch/en/family/anne-frank. [Open access]","Anne Frank Fonds, “Publications,” Basel, https://www.annefrank.ch/en/diary/publications. [Open access]","Anne Frank House, “The Authenticity of the Diary of Anne Frank,” https://www.annefrank.org/en/anne-frank/go-in-depth/authenticity-diary-anne-frank/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Westerbork,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/westerbork. [Open access]","Anne Frank Zentrum, “Anne Frank,” Berlin, https://www.annefrank.de/anne-frank. [in German] [Open access]","Mr Justice Gray, judgment in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books Ltd and Deborah Lipstadt</i>, High Court of Justice, Queen’s Bench Division (2000), para. 13.149, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/judge/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Frank family go into hiding when they did?","a":"Because on 5 July 1942 Margot received a call-up to report for a so-called labour camp in Germany, and her parents did not believe it was about work. They went into hiding the next day in the annex Otto Frank had been preparing with his colleagues since the spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it not known who betrayed the people in the annex?","a":"Because no document about the raid of 4 August 1944 survives, so explanations rest on testimony. Otto Frank and his helpers believed they had been betrayed, but there is no concrete evidence of it, and the US museum notes a theory that police found the hiding place by chance while investigating ration fraud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the diary known to be genuine?","a":"Because it has been examined repeatedly. Hamburg handwriting experts in 1960 found all the entries identical with Anne’s hand, and the Netherlands Forensic Institute’s study of the 1980s concluded that both versions were written by her between 1942 and 1944, refuting the claims of forgery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/AnneFrankSchoolPhoto.jpg","title":"A school photograph, Amsterdam, 1940","desc":"A school photograph taken in 1940 at the 6th Montessori school on the Niersstraat in Amsterdam.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Collectie Anne Frank Stichting Amsterdam, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AnneFrankSchoolPhoto.jpg","alt":"A dark-haired girl sits at a school desk with a pen in her hand and an open exercise book, smiling at the camera."},"wiki":"Anne Frank"},
    "ww2-775": {"questions":["Which occupier held each zone shaped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since Italian officials shielded Jews until September 1943 while Bulgaria handed the Jews of Thrace to the Germans.","Deportations of about 800 Jews from Athens and nearly 2,000 each from Corfu and Rhodes in 1944 were the last stage of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after Italy’s surrender."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in Greece</b> was the murder of most of the Jews of Greece under the German, Italian and Bulgarian occupation that followed the Axis invasion of April 1941, a fate that depended largely on which power held each zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly 72,000 Jews lived in Greece in 1941, and the US museum estimates the dead at 58,800 to 65,000, more than 80 percent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany held Salonika and western Macedonia, Bulgaria held western Thrace, and Italy held most of the rest, where its officials generally ignored German demands and even protected Jews until Italy surrendered in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In March 1943 Bulgarian troops and gendarmes rounded up about 4,200 Jews in Thrace and handed them to the Germans, who sent them to Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1944 the Germans deported about 800 Jews from Athens and almost 2,000 each from Corfu and Rhodes to Auschwitz-Birkenau, and the Balkans fared no better, with about 15,000 of 17,200 Jews killed in German-occupied Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Salonika had been a great Sephardic city since Jews expelled from Spain settled there in 1492, and about 50,000 Jews lived there around 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In July 1942 Jewish men were made to report for forced labour, in December the ancient Jewish cemetery was destroyed, and from February 1943 the Jews were made to wear the yellow star and confined to ghettos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dieter Wisliceny, sent by Adolf Eichmann with Alois Brunner, told the Nuremberg tribunal that the army’s transport command supplied the trains and that about 54,000 people were deported from Salonika and Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The deportations began in March 1943, the city’s community dating the first train to 15 March, and by August more than 40,000 Jews of Salonika had been sent to Auschwitz-Birkenau, where nearly all were murdered on arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Auschwitz museum counts about 55,000 Jews from Greece among those brought to the camp, the Thessaloniki community reckons that 96 percent of its members were killed, and some who escaped fought with the partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Greece,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/greece. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Losses during the Holocaust: By Country,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-losses-during-the-holocaust-by-country. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","Central Board of Jewish Communities in Greece, “History,” Jewish Community of Thessaloniki, https://www.kis.gr/en/communities/active-communities/thessaloniki/history. [Open access]","Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, “Jews in Auschwitz,” https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/jews-in-auschwitz/. [Open access]","Anna Foa, “Shoah,” <i>Enciclopedia Italiana</i>, VII Appendice (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2007), Treccani, https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/shoah_(Enciclopedia-Italiana)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Jewish Museum of Greece, “Synagonistis: Greek Jews in the National Resistance,” digital exhibition, Athens, https://jewishmuseum.gr/en/psifiaki-ekthesi/synagonistis-greek-jews-in-the-national-resistance/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fate of Greek Jews depend on the zone they lived in?","a":"Because Germany, Italy and Bulgaria each ran their own zone with different priorities. Italian officials generally ignored German demands and protected Jews, so thousands fled into their zone, while the Bulgarians handed the Jews of Thrace to the Germans in March 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German army matter in Salonika?","a":"Because the deportations needed its trains and its consent. Wisliceny testified that the military administration raised no objection, that the transport command supplied the cars and locomotives, and that the military administration provided the food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Jews of Athens and the islands deported only in 1944?","a":"Because they had lived in the Italian zone, where Italian officials refused to carry out the killing. Only after Italy surrendered on 8 September 1943 did the Germans take over, concentrate the Jews of Athens, Corfu and Rhodes and send them to Auschwitz-Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in Greece"},
    "ww2-776": {"questions":["A census of Jewish citizens made under the racial laws of 1938 later helped the police to find their victims during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Italian Social Republic.","Italian police holding Jews at Fossoli for German transports to Auschwitz show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not only a German crime after the Social Republic’s order of December 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in Italy</b> was the persecution of Italian Jews under the Fascist racial laws from 1938 and their deportation and murder after Germany occupied the north and centre of the country in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jewish community, one of the oldest in Europe, numbered about 50,000 in 1933 and was fully integrated, and until 1938 Jews could join the Fascist Party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The racial laws of 1938 were modelled on the Nazi laws and so defined Jews by descent, and they came with a census of Jewish citizens that later became an essential tool for finding Jews to deport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fascist Italy nonetheless refused to hand over Jews from its occupation zones in France, Greece and Yugoslavia, so that thousands escaped into them between 1941 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the surrender of 8 September 1943 the Germans seized the north and centre, set up the Italian Social Republic under Mussolini and began to round up the remaining 43,000 Jews there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 16 October 1943 German forces alone arrested over 1,000 Jews in Rome and sent them to Auschwitz, from where only about fifteen came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Meina on Lake Maggiore men of the Waffen-SS division Leibstandarte Adolf Hitler murdered 16 Jews held in a hotel and hid their bodies in the lake in September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From 1 December 1943 the Social Republic made all Jews liable to arrest by Italian police, who held them in the camp at Fossoli and later Bolzano, from where the Germans deported them to Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The research of the CDEC foundation counts 8,078 Jews deported from Italy and 6,806 identified dead, about 16 percent of Italian Jews, and the US museum’s figure for deaths is 7,858.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Auschwitz museum lists about 7,500 Jews from Italy among those it received, and historians explain the low share by the short occupation, the help of non-Jewish Italians and how hard Italian Jews were to tell apart from their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Italy,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jewish Losses during the Holocaust: By Country,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-losses-during-the-holocaust-by-country. [Open access]","Anna Foa, “Shoah,” <i>Enciclopedia Italiana</i>, VII Appendice (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2007), Treccani, https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/shoah_(Enciclopedia-Italiana)/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Fondazione CDEC, “I nomi della Shoah,” Milan, https://www.cdec.it/i-nomi-della-shoah/. [in Italian] [Open access]","Memorial and Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau, “Jews in Auschwitz,” https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/jews-in-auschwitz/. [Open access]","Atlante delle stragi naziste e fasciste in Italia, “Meina, 22–23.09.1943,” https://www.straginazifasciste.it/?page_id=38&lang=it&id_strage=2437. [in Italian] [Open access]","Comunità Ebraica di Roma, “La storia della Comunità Ebraica di Roma,” https://romaebraica.it/storia/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Jews relatively safe in Italian-occupied territory until 1943?","a":"Because Italian military authorities generally refused to take part in the mass murder of Jews or to permit deportations, and the Fascist leadership was unable and unwilling to force the issue, so thousands of Jews fled into the Italian zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a smaller share of Italian Jews die than in most occupied countries?","a":"Because the occupation was short, ending in central Italy in June 1944, and because Italian Jews could hide more easily among their neighbours, who often helped them. A study cited in the Treccani encyclopedia counts about 16 percent of Italian Jews dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the census of 1938 so dangerous?","a":"Because it recorded the Jewish citizens of Italy, and in 1943 it became an essential tool for identifying them, so that the German police in Rome came to the round-up of 16 October carrying documents from the census and from the looted community archives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in Italy"},
    "ww2-777": {"questions":["Protests by the deputy speaker Dimitur Pešev, intellectuals and the Orthodox Church made Tsar Boris cancel the deportations from Sofia, setting the limit of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in May 1943.","Expelled to the provinces rather than deported, almost 20,000 Jews of Sofia survived the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which left the country’s Jewish population near its prewar level in 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Holocaust in Bulgaria</b> was the persecution of Jews by Bulgaria, an ally of Germany that deported the Jews of the Greek and Yugoslav lands it occupied but not the roughly 50,000 Jews of its own core provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From July 1940 Bulgarian laws excluded Jews from public service, restricted where they lived and worked, and banned marriage between Jews and non-Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bulgaria joined the Axis in March 1941, occupied most of Greek Thrace, Yugoslav Macedonia and Pirot in eastern Serbia, and later set up a Commissariat for Jewish Affairs to prepare deportations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the winter of 1943 its government had agreed with Adolf Eichmann’s office to deport 20,000 Jews as a first stage, about 13,000 from the occupied lands and some 8,000 from Sofia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the first half of March 1943 Bulgarian police and soldiers handed 11,343 Jews from the occupied territories to the Germans, who killed virtually all of them at Treblinka, though Dieter Wisliceny later put the number at about 8,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> As news of these deportations reached Sofia, opposition politicians, intellectuals and clergy protested, and the deputy speaker of parliament, Dimitur Pešev, persuaded Tsar Boris III to delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pešev’s resolution of 19 March 1943 against the deportations was voted down and he was forced to resign, but further protests, including the Orthodox Metropolitan’s intervention, led Boris to cancel the deportations in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Russian review of a study of the Orthodox churches notes that the Bulgarian church alone condemned the genocide officially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Almost 20,000 Jews of Sofia were instead expelled to the provinces and their men sent to forced labour, while a British offer of January 1943 to take 4,500 children from Bulgaria to Palestine failed because Bulgaria would not let them leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Denmark marked the other limit, for after German officials leaked the deportation order of late September 1943 the Danes hid their Jews and took them to Sweden, which had received over 8,000 refugees from Denmark by December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Bulgaria,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/bulgaria. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Denmark,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/denmark. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1963), document 103, aide-mémoire of the British Embassy to the Department of State, 20 January 1943, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d103. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1963), document 200, Herbert Bucknell to the Secretary of State, London, 5 December 1943, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d200. [Open access]","M. M. Mints, review of <i>Kholokost i pravoslavnaia tserkov’</i>, by M. V. Shkarovskii, <i>Sotsial’nye i gumanitarnye nauki. Otechestvennaia i zarubezhnaia literatura. Seriia 5: Istoriia</i>, no. 1 (2018), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/2018-01-014-shkarovskiy-m-v-holokost-i-pravoslavnaya-tserkov-m-veche-2016-448-s-il. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Jews of the occupied lands deported but not those of Bulgaria proper?","a":"Because the occupied territories were emptied first, in early March 1943, and the news of it set off protests in Sofia. Pešev’s intervention, the clergy and the wider public pressure delayed and then ended the planned deportations from the core provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Bulgaria’s record both a rescue and a persecution?","a":"Because the Jews of Bulgaria proper, about 50,000, survived the war, yet the same state passed anti-Jewish laws, sent 11,343 Jews from the occupied lands to their deaths, expelled almost 20,000 Jews from Sofia and confiscated most of the property they left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many Danish Jews survive?","a":"Because German officials, among them the naval attaché Georg Duckwitz, warned Danes of the plan before the final order of 28 September 1943, and the Danes alerted the Jewish community, hid Jews and carried them to Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The Holocaust in Bulgaria"},
    "ww2-778": {"questions":["Led by Heydrich and then by Kaltenbrunner, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deployed the Einsatzgruppen and hosted the Wannsee Conference of January 1942 to plan the murder of Europe’s Jews.","Eichmann’s reports went through the Gestapo chief Heinrich Müller to the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and on to Himmler, a chain Dieter Wisliceny described to the Nuremberg tribunal."],"abstract":"The <b>Reich Security Main Office</b>, or RSHA, was the headquarters of the Security Police and the SD that Heinrich Himmler founded on 27 September 1939, weeks after the invasion of Poland, and it coordinated and carried out many parts of the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the SD, the intelligence service of the Nazi Party’s SS, to the state’s Security Police of Gestapo and Criminal Police, so that it was at once a main office of the SS and an office of the Ministry of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reinhard Heydrich led it until his death in June 1942, and from January 1943 it was led by the Austrian SS and police leader Ernst Kaltenbrunner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its leaders were mostly highly educated men in their thirties and committed National Socialists, many of them already proven violent before the war and in Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its offices stood on Prinz-Albrecht-Straße and Wilhelmstraße in Berlin, where the Topography of Terror now documents these institutions and their crimes across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Within Office IV, the Gestapo, Adolf Eichmann’s section IV B 4 coordinated the deportation of Jews from western, central and southern Europe, and the RSHA also deployed the Einsatzgruppen, developed gassing methods and hosted the Wannsee Conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Nuremberg Otto Ohlendorf, who led Einsatzgruppe D for the Chief of the Security Police and SD, named the four groups’ commanders, and Dieter Wisliceny described how Eichmann’s reports passed through the Gestapo chief Heinrich Müller to Kaltenbrunner and Himmler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Prosecutors at the Einsatzgruppen trial counted no more than 3,000 men in the four groups and at least 1,000,000 people killed in about 2 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The International Military Tribunal declared the Gestapo and SD criminal organisations, citing among their crimes the persecution and extermination of the Jews and the murder of prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet the RSHA did not act alone, for the deportations needed the state railways and the Foreign Office, and the killing relied on civilian occupation authorities, the army, the Reichsbank, doctors and industrial firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Reich Security Main Office (RSHA),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/reich-security-main-office-rsha. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Perpetrators,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/perpetrators. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), judgment, “The Accused Organisations,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judorg.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Otto Ohlendorf and Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 4, <i>United States of America v. Otto Ohlendorf et al.</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office), 38–39, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-IV.pdf. [Open access]","Topography of Terror Documentation Center, “Topography of Terror: Gestapo, SS and Reich Security Main Office on Wilhelmstraße and Prinz-Albrecht-Straße,” Berlin, https://www.topographie.de/en/exhibitions/topography-of-terror. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Himmler and Heydrich create the RSHA?","a":"Because they wanted to merge the SD, a party body staffed by Nazi ideologues, with the professional Security Police into one organisation with a shared culture. Founded weeks after the invasion of Poland, it was intended for war, though the merger was never completed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg tribunal judge the Gestapo and SD together?","a":"Because from a functional point of view they were closely related groups within the Security Police and SD, which had a single command under Heydrich and later Kaltenbrunner and a single headquarters, the RSHA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the RSHA not carry out the Holocaust alone?","a":"Because mass deportation needed the cooperation of the state railways and the Foreign Office, and killing in occupied Poland and the Soviet Union needed the civilian occupation authorities and the army, which supplied the Einsatzgruppen with transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reich Security Main Office"},
    "ww2-779": {"questions":["In April 1945 Count Folke Bernadotte carried an offer from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to surrender on the western front alone, and Hitler stripped him of all his offices in response.","Taking over an SS of only 280 men in January 1929, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made it the master of the German police, the concentration camps and the murder of Europe’s Jews."],"abstract":"<b>Heinrich Himmler</b> was the Reich Leader of the SS from 1929 to 1945 and the senior Nazi official chiefly responsible for conceiving and overseeing the Final Solution, the plan to murder the Jews of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in Munich on 7 October 1900 to a conservative Catholic family, he studied agriculture after the First World War and became a fanatical völkisch, or racist-nationalist, activist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the Nazi Party in August 1923, marched in the Beer Hall Putsch, and in January 1929 took over an SS of only 280 men, which had over 52,000 men by 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took over the political police of every German state, became Chief of German Police in June 1936 and built the SS camp system from Dachau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In September 1939 he founded the Reich Security Main Office, and in October Hitler made him Reich Commissioner for the Strengthening of German Ethnic Stock, in charge of resettlement in occupied Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Einsatzgruppen formed on his orders followed the army into the Soviet Union, and in July 1941 Hitler gave him responsibility for security there, which authorised SS and police units to begin the mass murder of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otto Ohlendorf testified that Himmler visited Nikolaev in late summer 1941, repeated the killing order and told the men that the responsibility was his alone and the Führer’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a speech to SS generals at Posen on 4 October 1943 he spoke openly of exterminating the Jewish race and called it a page of glory in German history that was never to be written, and 2 days later told party leaders this people had to disappear from the face of the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In April 1945 he asked Count Folke Bernadotte to arrange a meeting with Eisenhower to surrender in the west while fighting on in the east, and Hitler stripped him of his offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Captured under a false name on 20 May 1945 and handed to the British, he killed himself with a hidden cyanide capsule on 23 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Heinrich Himmler,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/heinrich-himmler. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Heinrich Himmler 1900–1945,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, Berlin, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/heinrich-himmler. [in German] [Open access]","Document 1919-PS, speech of the Reichsführer-SS at the meeting of SS major-generals at Posen, 4 October 1943, in <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 4 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), 558–78, at 563, https://archive.org/details/naziconspiracyag04unit. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Otto Ohlendorf, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>European Advisory Commission; Austria; Germany</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1968), document 548, Herschel Johnson to the Secretary of State, Stockholm, 25 April 1945, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v03/d548. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Reich Security Main Office (RSHA),” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/reich-security-main-office-rsha. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Persecution of the Jews,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Mr Justice Gray, judgment in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books Ltd and Deborah Lipstadt</i>, High Court of Justice, Queen’s Bench Division (2000), para. 5.222, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/judge/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Himmler’s control of the police matter for the Holocaust?","a":"Because tying the German police to the SS removed police personnel, finances and operations from judicial or administrative review, and his authority came directly from Hitler, which enabled indefinite imprisonment and mass murder that the laws of the state might not have allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Himmler tell the killers that he bore the responsibility?","a":"Because, as Ohlendorf described it, he wanted to relieve the leaders and men of the Einsatzkommandos of any sense of personal guilt, telling them at Nikolaev that responsibility for carrying out the order lay with him alone and with the Führer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Himmler approach Count Bernadotte in April 1945?","a":"Because he believed Hitler was finished and that he now had the authority to act, so he asked the Swedish government to arrange a meeting with Eisenhower to capitulate in the west. Bernadotte told him that fighting on in the east would not be acceptable to the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R99621%2C_Heinrich_Himmler.jpg","title":"Portrait of the Reich Leader of the SS, 1938","desc":"An official portrait photograph of 1938 from the Bundesarchiv’s collection.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-R99621, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R99621,_Heinrich_Himmler.jpg","alt":"A man with small round glasses and a narrow moustache, in a black uniform and a peaked cap with a skull badge, looks to one side."},"wiki":"Heinrich Himmler"},
    "ww2-780": {"questions":["Seized in Argentina in May 1960 while living as Ricardo Klement, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was tried in Jerusalem the next year and hanged at midnight between 31 May and 1 June 1962.","Arriving in Budapest with a special commando in March 1944, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> directed on the ground the deportation of about 440,000 Hungarian Jews, most of them to Auschwitz."],"abstract":"<b>Adolf Eichmann</b> was the SS officer who ran the Jewish affairs section of the Reich Security Main Office and organised the deportation of more than 1.5 million Jews to ghettos, killing centres and killing sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in Solingen on 19 March 1906 and raised in Linz, he drifted between jobs, among them salesman for the Vacuum Oil Company, before joining the Austrian Nazi Party and the SS in 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After joining the SD in 1934 he watched Jewish organisations, and in 1938 he set up the Central Office for Jewish Emigration in Vienna, which forced about 128,000 Jews out of Austria in under 18 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1941 he headed section IV B 4 of the Gestapo in Berlin, prepared a presentation for the Wannsee Conference of January 1942 and relayed its plans to his deportation network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His men, among them Alois Brunner, Theodor Dannecker and Dieter Wisliceny, organised deportations from Slovakia, the Netherlands, France and Belgium in 1942, and from Greece, northern Italy and Hungary in 1943–44.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Wisliceny testified at Nuremberg that in summer 1942 Eichmann showed him a written order from Himmler and explained that ‘final solution’ disguised the planned biological annihilation of the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only in Hungary, where he arrived with a special commando in March 1944, did he direct deportations on the ground, sending about 440,000 Jews, mostly to Auschwitz, by early July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wisliceny recalled him boasting he would leap laughing into the grave with five million people on his conscience, and Eichmann said the early trains from the Netherlands had really rolled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Escaping American custody in 1946, he reached Argentina, where Israeli agents seized him in May 1960, living as Ricardo Klement, and flew him to Israel, which Argentina took to the UN Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tried in Jerusalem in 1961, he called himself a little cog who followed orders, but was convicted in December of crimes against the Jewish people and against humanity and hanged at midnight between 31 May and 1 June 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Adolf Eichmann,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-eichmann. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Eichmann Trial,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/eichmann-trial. [Open access]","Susanne Eckelmann and Mark Rüdiger, “Adolf Eichmann 1906–1962,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, Berlin, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/adolf-eichmann. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 4 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), proceedings of 3 January 1946, testimony of Dieter Wisliceny, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/01-03-46.asp. [Open access]","Pim Griffioen and Ron Zeller, “The Netherlands: The Greatest Number of Jewish Victims in Western Europe,” Anne Frank House, https://www.annefrank.org/en/anne-frank/go-in-depth/netherlands-greatest-number-jewish-victims-western-europe/. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960</i>, vol. 13, <i>Arab-Israeli Dispute; United Arab Republic; North Africa</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1992), document 155, memorandum of a conversation, Department of State, 27 June 1960, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v13/d155. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1947), judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Persecution of the Jews,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Anna Foa, “Shoah,” <i>Enciclopedia Italiana</i>, VII Appendice (Rome: Istituto della Enciclopedia Italiana, 2007), Treccani, https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/shoah_(Enciclopedia-Italiana)/. [in Italian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Eichmann’s office matter so much to the Holocaust?","a":"Because section IV B 4 coordinated the deportation of Jews from western, central and southern Europe. Eichmann planned the transports in detail with other German agencies, decided how the deportees’ property would be seized, and relied on his network of officials in occupied and allied countries to carry them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Israel seize Eichmann rather than ask for his extradition?","a":"Because Argentina had a history of denying extradition requests, so Prime Minister David Ben-Gurion decided to capture him secretly. Argentina protested to the UN Security Council that its sovereignty had been violated before the dispute was smoothed over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Eichmann’s trial so different from Nuremberg?","a":"Because the Nuremberg trials relied mainly on written documents, while the Jerusalem court put survivors at the centre. The proceedings were among the first trials widely televised, and many historians date the firm place of the Holocaust in public consciousness to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Adolf_Eichmann_at_Trial1961.jpg","title":"The defendant in Jerusalem, 1961","desc":"A photograph from the Israel Government Press Office of the trial in Jerusalem in 1961.","credit":"Israel Government Press Office, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolf_Eichmann_at_Trial1961.jpg","alt":"A balding man in heavy-rimmed glasses, a dark suit and tie, wearing headphones, speaks towards a cluster of microphones."},"wiki":"Adolf Eichmann"},
    "ww2-781": {"questions":["Postwar testimony from members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the main evidence in the argument over whether conformity or a deep antisemitism made ordinary German policemen into killers.","A unit of the Order Police, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of just under 500 men took part in deporting at least 45,000 Jews to death camps in occupied Poland."],"abstract":"<b>Reserve Police Battalion 101</b> was a unit of the German Order Police, Nazi Germany’s uniformed police, raised in Hamburg and sent to occupied Poland, where it took part in the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Order Police formed battalions of about 550 men from reservists in their thirties and younger recruits, led by veteran policemen and new Nazi officers and trained in ideology as well as police work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Battalion 101 numbered just under 500 men, most of them middle-aged and working-class, and few of them had any close ties to the Nazi Party or the SS.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1940 and 1941 its men served near Łódź, where one of them was photographed beside the sign at the entrance to the ghetto, since guarding ghettos was among the police battalions’ tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Before its work was done the battalion had helped to deport at least 45,000 Jews to the death camps and had itself shot at least 38,000 more, for the SS relied on many other German institutions to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its first mass killing came at Józefów near Biłgoraj on 13 July 1942, when Major Wilhelm Trapp told his men that they were to kill the town’s Jews but that anyone who felt unable to take part could step aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 1,500 Jews, most of them women, children and old people, were driven by lorry to a forest clearing called Winiarczykowa Góra and shot there that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the testimony its former members gave to Hamburg prosecutors survives, the battalion became the central case in the argument over what turns ordinary men into mass murderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One answer stresses conformity, the pressures of a dictatorship and the fear of seeming weak before comrades, noting that perhaps 10 to 20 per cent of the men refused or evaded the shooting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The other holds that a widespread antisemitism of the kind its author called eliminationist made the killers willing, and the two answers were argued out at the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum on 8 April 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“The Order Police,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-order-police. [Open access]","“Perpetrators,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/perpetrators. [Open access]","“Facing Police and Holocaust: Ein Vierteljahrhundert nach Christopher R. Brownings ‘Ordinary Men’,” Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/themen/holocaust/facing-police-and-holocaust/. [in German] [Open access]","“Józefów,” <i>Places and History of the Holocaust in the Lublin District</i> (New York: World Jewish Congress), https://operationreinhard.aboutholocaust.org/en/location/jozefow. [Open access]","Daniel J. Goldhagen, Christopher R. Browning and Leon Wieseltier, <i>The “Willing Executioners”/“Ordinary Men” Debate: Selections from the Symposium, April 8, 1996</i>, introduction by Michael Berenbaum (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Research Institute, 1996), https://www.ushmm.org/m/pdfs/Publication_OP_1996-01.pdf. [Open access]","“German Police in the Nazi State,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-police-in-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the men offered a choice at Józefów?","a":"On the morning of 13 July 1942 their commander, Major Wilhelm Trapp, told them what they were to do and offered to excuse anyone who felt unable to take part, and the shooting of about 1,500 Jews then went ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does this battalion matter to the question of motive?","a":"Its men were not SS or Gestapo but middle-aged, mostly working-class policemen with few close ties to the Nazi Party, yet they shot or deported tens of thousands of Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The record of their postwar interrogations let historians ask what turned such men into killers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians disagree about what drove the killers?","a":"One side points to conformity, the fear of seeming a coward and the pressures of a dictatorship, noting that a minority refused or evaded the shooting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The other rejects coercion, obedience and peer pressure and holds that antisemitism alone explains the killers’ willingness and their cruelty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reserve Police Battalion 101"},
    "ww2-782": {"questions":["Dismissed by the American minister in Bern as a rumour born of fear, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Rabbi Stephen Wise only through a British MP 3 weeks later.","Information that the industrialist Eduard Schulte carried to Switzerland after Himmler toured Auschwitz became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the warning that preceded the Allied declaration of December 1942."],"abstract":"The <b>Riegner Telegram</b> was the cable that Gerhart Riegner, the World Jewish Congress representative in Geneva, dictated at the American and British consulates in August 1942 to warn that Nazi Germany planned to murder the Jews of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its news came from Eduard Schulte, a German industrialist secretly opposed to the regime, who reached Switzerland on 29 July 1942 after learning of Heinrich Himmler’s tour of Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 8 August Riegner told the American vice-consul Howard Elting that three and a half to four million Jews were to be killed at one blow after deportation east, perhaps with prussic acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Riegner believed the campaign was still being planned for the autumn, when in fact the mass killing was already well under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His was not the first warning, for British intelligence had told Winston Churchill in September 1941 that the German police were killing every Jew they caught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The American minister in Bern, Leland Harrison, dismissed the report as a rumour born of fear, and the State Department decided not to pass it to its intended recipient, Rabbi Stephen Wise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British Foreign Office held its copy for a week, fearing embarrassment, then gave it to the MP Sidney Silverman, whose copy reached Wise on 29 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Under Secretary of State, Sumner Welles, asked Wise to keep silent while the department checked the report, and only on 24 November 1942 did he tell Wise that the evidence confirmed his deepest fears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wise told the press the next day, and Jan Karski, a courier of the Polish underground, soon brought eyewitness accounts of the Warsaw ghetto and the Izbica transit camp to London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 17 December 1942 Anthony Eden read to the House of Commons a declaration by eleven Allied governments and the French National Committee condemning a bestial policy of cold-blooded extermination, though it promised punishment, not rescue, which was never an Allied war aim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“The Riegner Telegram,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-riegner-telegram. [Open access]","“This Week in Jewish History: Riegner Telegram Alerts World of Holocaust,” World Jewish Congress, https://www.worldjewishcongress.org/en/news/this-week-in-jewish-history--riegner-telegram-alerts-world-of-holocaust-8-2-2020. [Open access]","Anthony Eden, statement on the United Nations Declaration, House of Commons, 17 December 1942, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 385, cols. 2082–87, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/dec/17/united-nations-declaration. [Open access]","“Persecution of the Jews: Allies’ Declaration,” House of Lords, 17 December 1942, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 125, cols. 607–12, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1942/dec/17/persecution-of-the-jews-allies. [Open access]","“Jan Karski,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jan-karski. [Open access]","“The United States and the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-united-states-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the State Department hold the telegram back?","a":"Its officials agreed with the minister in Bern that the report could not possibly be true, and decided not to forward it to Rabbi Wise, the man Riegner had meant it for, because of what they called the fantastic nature of the allegation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the warning reach the public?","a":"Through Wise, who had a copy from Silverman in London and waited while Sumner Welles checked it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Welles confirmed it on 24 November 1942, Wise told the press, and newspapers reported it the next day, though mostly on their inside pages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the Allied declaration of December 1942 promise?","a":"It condemned the extermination of the Jews in the strongest terms and promised that those responsible would not escape retribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It offered no rescue, and Eden warned the Commons that what could be done at that stage must inevitably be slight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Riegner telegram"},
    "ww2-783": {"questions":["A Treasury memorandum on the acquiescence of the government in the murder of the Jews led Roosevelt to set up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, headed by three cabinet secretaries.","Called ‘little and late’ by its own first director, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first and only official American response to the Holocaust, closed by Truman in 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>War Refugee Board</b> was the United States government agency set up by President Franklin D. Roosevelt’s Executive Order 9417 of 22 January 1944 for the ‘immediate rescue and relief of the Jews of Europe and other victims of enemy persecution’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was headed by the secretaries of State, War and the Treasury, drew most of its staff from the Treasury and ran until President Harry Truman closed it on 15 September 1945, the first and only official American response to the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 9 days later Washington told the British that the board showed its resolve to rescue Europe’s refugees without delay, and the effort would not be American alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Little had been done before, for at Bermuda from 19 to 30 April 1943 British and American delegates had agreed beforehand not to press each other on immigration quotas or Palestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bermuda produced only a plan to move some refugees from Spain to North Africa and a revived Intergovernmental Committee on Refugees, and was widely judged a failure, the Allies holding that victory was the answer to the genocide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The board owed its existence to Treasury officials who found that the State Department had tried to stop news of the mass murder reaching America and had stalled a relief plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their memorandum on the government’s acquiescence in the murder of the Jews went with Henry Morgenthau Jr. to Roosevelt on 16 January 1944, while Congress debated a rescue resolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once at work, the board licensed relief money in weeks rather than months and helped send about $12,000,000 overseas, and its man in Turkey helped some 8,000 refugees reach Palestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It asked Latin American governments to protect holders of their papers, even forged ones, and through Sweden it recruited Raoul Wallenberg for the Swedish legation in Budapest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first director, John Pehle, later called it little and late, though the board’s final report estimated that it had saved tens of thousands of lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“War Refugee Board: Background and Establishment,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/war-refugee-board-background-and-establishment. [Open access]","“War Refugee Board: Activities,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/war-refugee-board-activities. [Open access]","United States War Refugee Board, <i>History of the War Refugee Board, with Selected Documents, January 22, 1944 – September 15, 1945</i> (Washington, DC: War Refugee Board, 1945), https://archive.org/details/historyofwarrefu01unit. [Open access]","<i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office), 989–90, document 556, the Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom, 31 January 1944, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v01/d556. [Open access]","“The Bermuda Conference,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/resistance-responses-collaboration/responses/the-bermuda-conference/. [Open access]","“The United States and the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-united-states-and-the-holocaust. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Treasury rather than the State Department push for the board?","a":"Treasury staff found cables showing that the State Department had tried to stop news of the mass murder reaching America and had delayed a relief plan for Jews in France and Romania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Morgenthau took their memorandum, with a draft executive order, to Roosevelt in January 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Bermuda Conference judged a failure?","a":"The British and Americans agreed in advance not to push each other on immigration quotas or Palestine, so no serious rescue plan was discussed, and the conference produced only a small camp in North Africa and a revived refugee committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the board try to protect Jews in Hungary?","a":"Unable to post its own representative in Budapest while the United States was at war with Hungary, it worked through Sweden, whose foreign ministry assigned the businessman Raoul Wallenberg to its legation; Swedish protective papers shielded their holders from immediate deportation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War Refugee Board"},
    "ww2-784": {"questions":["American bombers struck the IG Farben works at Monowitz in August 1944 but not the nearby gas chambers, a contrast at the heart of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over Allied inaction.","Reports by escapees such as Rudolf Vrba and Alfred Wetzler in 1944 prompted appeals to bomb the gas chambers and the railways, starting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that continues decades later."],"abstract":"The <b>Auschwitz bombing debate</b> is the argument over whether the Allies should have bombed the gas chambers of Auschwitz-Birkenau, or the railways leading to them, in 1944 to slow the murder of Europe’s Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Explicit information about the gassing reached the Allies in the spring of 1944, much of it from the report of Rudolf Vrba and Alfred Wetzler, two Slovak Jews who had escaped from the camp that April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their account and others were printed by the War Refugee Board in Washington in November 1944, which called it a fact beyond denial that the Germans had deliberately murdered millions of civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The appeals grew as Jews from German-occupied Hungary were deported to Auschwitz, where on some days as many as 10,000 people were murdered in the gas chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jewish leaders were themselves divided, some calling for the gas chambers to be bombed and others opposing it, since no one could be sure of the result and both sides feared the deaths of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 14 August 1944 the Assistant Secretary of War, John J. McCloy, advised that a raid could be made only by diverting air support needed for decisive operations and would be of doubtful efficacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British Air Ministry took a similar stand, and the War Department held that the quickest possible victory over Germany was the best help for the victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet within a week American heavy bombers struck the IG Farben oil and rubber works at Monowitz, less than 8 kilometres (5 miles) from the gas chambers, a complex that reconnaissance aircraft had been photographing since April 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those who say the Allies should have tried argue that a raid, though it might have killed some prisoners, could have slowed the killing and in the end saved lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who doubt it point out that a raid in mid-July 1944 would not have saved the roughly 310,000 Hungarian Jews already killed at Birkenau, and that barracks near the gas chambers held 51,117 prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“The United States and the Holocaust: Why Auschwitz Was Not Bombed,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-united-states-and-the-holocaust-why-auschwitz-was-not-bombed. [Open access]","“The Issue of Bombing Auschwitz,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/informing-the-world/the-issue-of-bombing-auschwitz. [Open access]","United States War Refugee Board, <i>History of the War Refugee Board, with Selected Documents, January 22, 1944 – September 15, 1945</i> (Washington, DC: War Refugee Board, 1945), 154–55, https://archive.org/details/historyofwarrefu01unit. [Open access]","“An Aerial Reconnaissance Photograph Showing the Auschwitz III Monowitz Complex Surrounding the I.G. Farben Buna Production Plant,” photograph 91365, Collections, United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, courtesy of the National Archives and Records Administration, https://collections.ushmm.org/search/catalog/pa21499. [Open access]","War Refugee Board, <i>German Extermination Camps: Auschwitz and Birkenau</i> (Washington, DC: Executive Office of the President, War Refugee Board, November 1944), https://archive.org/details/WarRefugeeBoardAuschwitzReport. [Open access]","“Reports by Auschwitz Escapees,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/informing-the-world/reports-by-auschwitz-escapees. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the War Department refuse to bomb Auschwitz?","a":"It held that a raid could only be made by diverting air support needed for decisive operations elsewhere and would be of doubtful efficacy, and that military resources should not be used for non-military ends when the quickest victory was the best help for the victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some Jewish leaders oppose bombing the camp?","a":"Like some Allied officials, they feared the number of prisoners a raid would kill and the use German propaganda might make of it, and no one could be certain what bombing would achieve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the raid on Monowitz matter to the argument?","a":"It showed that American bombers could reach the Auschwitz area: within a week of the refusal they heavily bombed the IG Farben works less than 8 kilometres (5 miles) from the gas chambers, a complex Allied aircraft had photographed since April 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Auschwitz bombing debate"},
    "ww2-785": {"questions":["A term probably coined by the prisoners themselves, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved camp inmates on foot and in open freight cars away from the advancing Allied armies in 1944 and 1945.","More than a thousand prisoners burned alive in a barn at Gardelegen in April 1945 were victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that emptied camps such as Dora-Mittelbau before liberation."],"abstract":"The <b>death marches</b> were the forced evacuations of concentration camp prisoners over long distances, on foot and in open rail cars, that the SS carried out as Allied armies closed in on the camps in 1944 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The term was probably coined by the prisoners themselves, who came to believe that the Germans meant them all to die on the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Soviet troops overran Majdanek in July 1944, before the SS could empty it, Heinrich Himmler ordered the prisoners of the camps in the east evacuated into the interior of the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The SS wanted to keep prisoners from falling into Allied hands and testifying, to go on using them as slave labour and, in Himmler’s case, to hold Jews as hostages for a separate peace in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Guards had orders to kill anyone who could not keep up, and thousands more died of cold, hunger and exhaustion on the roads and in unheated freight trains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The largest marches left Auschwitz and Stutthof, and from 17 to 21 January 1945 about 56,000 prisoners were marched out of Auschwitz and its sub-camps towards Gliwice and Wodzisław Śląski.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Auschwitz Memorial estimates that at least 9,000, and more probably 15,000, of its prisoners died in the evacuation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Leszczyny near Rybnik on 22 January, SS guards and local police fired into a train of prisoners from Gliwice, and more than 300 bodies were gathered afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the spring prisoners were driven on from Buchenwald and Flossenbürg towards Dachau and Mauthausen and from Sachsenhausen and Neuengamme north to the Baltic, and many of those the Allies freed in Germany had survived such marches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Gardelegen on 13 April 1945 the SS and local auxiliaries burned more than a thousand prisoners from Dora-Mittelbau in a barn, and American troops found 1,016 bodies 2 days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["“Death Marches,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/death-marches. [Open access]","“The Final Evacuation and Liquidation of the Camp,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/evacuation/the-final-evacuation-and-liquidation-of-the-camp. [Open access]","“Gardelegen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gardelegen. [Open access]","“Death Marches, 1944–1945,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/how-and-why/how/death-marches-1944-1945/. [Open access]","“Liberation of Nazi Camps,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/liberation-of-nazi-camps. [Open access]","“Concentration Camps, 1942–45,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/concentration-camps-1942-45. [Open access]","“In the Wake of the Death March,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/evacuation/in-the-wake-of-death-march/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the SS march prisoners away rather than abandon them?","a":"To keep them from falling into Allied hands, where they could testify, to go on using them as forced labour and, for Himmler, to hold Jews as hostages in the hope of a separate peace in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many prisoners die on the marches?","a":"Guards had orders to shoot those who fell behind or could not climb out of the trains, and prisoners already starved faced the winter of 1944–45 on foot or in open and unheated rail cars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did Allied troops do when they found the dead?","a":"They often made local Germans view the exhumed bodies and bury them properly; at Gardelegen the American commander ordered between 200 and 300 men of the town to give each victim an individual grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Death march"},
    "ww2-786": {"questions":["British troops found thousands of unburied dead at Bergen-Belsen on 15 April 1945, one of the scenes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that exposed the full scope of Nazi crimes.","Not a primary aim of the Allied campaigns, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw troops free prisoners, give them food and medical care, and gather evidence for war crimes trials."],"abstract":"The <b>liberation of Nazi concentration camps</b> was the freeing of their surviving prisoners by Soviet, American, British and Canadian troops as the Allied armies advanced across Europe between July 1944 and May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Freeing the camps was not a primary aim of the Allied campaigns, but the troops who reached them released prisoners from their guards, gave them food and medical care and collected evidence for war crimes trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet forces reached the first major camp, Majdanek near Lublin, on the night of 22–23 July 1944 and found it almost intact, because its staff had fled without dismantling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Soviet troops went on to free Auschwitz, Stutthof, Sachsenhausen and Ravensbrück, American troops Buchenwald, Dora-Mittelbau, Flossenbürg, Dachau and Mauthausen, and British troops Neuengamme and Bergen-Belsen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of those freed were starving and sick, many had survived death marches into Germany, and for most of them liberation was only the start of a long road back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Soldiers of the Soviet 60th Army opened the gates of Auschwitz on 27 January 1945 and found about 7,000 prisoners in the main camp, Birkenau and Monowitz, with the corpses of about 600 more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the warehouses that survived they found hundreds of thousands of men’s suits, more than 800,000 women’s garments and more than 6,300 kilograms (14,000 pounds) of human hair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet field hospitals and Polish Red Cross volunteers treated more than 4,500 former prisoners there, who had to be brought back to normal eating a spoonful at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> British troops took over Bergen-Belsen on 15 April 1945 and found some 55,000 starving and sick prisoners, more than 60,000 by another count, among the bodies of about 13,000 recent dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 13,000 of them died within 3 months despite field hospitals and the burning of the lice-infested huts, and General Dwight D. Eisenhower visited a camp himself so that no one could later dismiss such reports as propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“Liberation of Nazi Camps,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/liberation-of-nazi-camps. [Open access]","“Liberation: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/liberation-an-overview. [Open access]","“Day of Liberation,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/liberation/day-of-liberation. [Open access]","“Liberation, 1944–1945,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/how-and-why/how/liberation-1944-1945/. [Open access]","“Liberation of Bergen-Belsen,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/the-camps/bergen-belsen/liberation-of-bergen-belsen/. [Open access]","“First Help,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/liberation/first-help. [Open access]","“Bergen-Belsen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/bergen-belsen. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Ebensee_concentration_camp_prisoners_1945.jpg","title":"Survivors at Ebensee, May 1945","desc":"Starved prisoners of Ebensee, a sub-camp of Mauthausen in Austria, photographed by a US Army photographer on 7 May 1945, after the camp’s liberation by American troops.","credit":"Arnold E. Samuelson, US Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (National Archives and Records Administration). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ebensee_concentration_camp_prisoners_1945.jpg","alt":"A group of severely emaciated men, starved almost to death, stand close together facing the camera."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Majdanek found almost intact?","a":"The Soviet advance was so fast that the remaining staff abandoned the camp without fully dismantling it, so Soviet officials could invite journalists to inspect the evidence of mass murder there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many die at Bergen-Belsen after liberation?","a":"The camp had been flooded with prisoners from the death marches and was swept by typhus and starvation; in the first days after liberation more than 500 people died each day, and more than 13,000 died within 3 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Eisenhower visit a camp himself?","a":"He wrote to General George C. Marshall on 15 April 1945 that he had gone deliberately, so that he could give firsthand evidence if anyone ever tried to dismiss the reports as propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-787": {"questions":["Earl Harrison’s report of August 1945 found Jewish <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held behind barbed wire, sometimes in the very camps where they had been victimised, and urged their resettlement.","The pogrom at Kielce in July 1946 killed at least 42 Jews and convinced many Polish <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that they had no future in Poland after the war."],"abstract":"<b>Holocaust survivors</b> were the Jews and others persecuted by the Nazis who outlived the camps, ghettos, hiding and flight of the war, and for most of them liberation began a long struggle to rebuild their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many had lost their families, homes and livelihoods, camp survivors were weakened by years of hunger and disease, and many had nowhere and no one to return to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Most Jewish survivors could not or would not go back to eastern Europe, where antisemitism persisted and their communities had been destroyed, and those who did often feared for their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Kielce in Poland on 4 July 1946 soldiers, police and civilians killed at least 42 Jews, and the pogrom set off a mass flight of Jews from Poland and the rest of eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Survivors searched for relatives through radio broadcasts, lists and tracing bureaus, and the tracing service moved to Arolsen in January 1946 now holds records on the fate of more than 17.5 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> More than 250,000 Jewish survivors lived as displaced persons in camps and towns in Germany, Austria and Italy between 1945 and 1952, most of them Polish Jews who had fled into the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At first many were held behind barbed wire, sometimes in the very camps where they had suffered, until Earl G. Harrison’s report to President Truman in August 1945 demanded they be treated as a group with special needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The camps filled with schools, newspapers, theatre, weddings and births, and the largest survivors’ organisation, Sh’erit ha-Pletah, Hebrew for ‘surviving remnant’, pressed for emigration, above all to Palestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain restricted entry to Palestine, turning back ships such as the <i>Exodus 1947</i> and interning would-be immigrants on Cyprus, until the State of Israel was declared on 14 May 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United States passed a Displaced Persons Act in 1948, and when the crisis ended about 136,000 Jewish displaced persons had gone to Israel, more than 80,000 to the United States and some 20,000 elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["“The Survivors,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-survivors. [Open access]","“Displaced Persons,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/displaced-persons. [Open access]","“The Harrison Report,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-harrison-report. [Open access]","“The Aftermath of the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-aftermath-of-the-holocaust. [Open access]","“The Kielce Pogrom: A Blood Libel Massacre of Holocaust Survivors,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-kielce-pogrom-a-blood-libel-massacre-of-holocaust-survivors. [Open access]","“Displaced Persons Camps,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/survival-and-legacy/survivors-and-dp-era/dp-camps/. [Open access]","“The International Tracing Service,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/survival-and-legacy/survivors-and-dp-era/the-international-tracing-service/. [Open access]","“Postwar Refugee Crisis and the Establishment of the State of Israel,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/postwar-refugee-crisis-and-the-establishment-of-the-state-of-israel. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many Jewish survivors not go home?","a":"Their communities had been destroyed and antisemitism persisted in parts of eastern Europe, so many feared to return; at Kielce in July 1946 a mob of soldiers, police and civilians killed at least 42 Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the Harrison report change?","a":"It condemned the treatment of Jewish displaced persons and urged that they be recognised as a separate group, moved to better housing under civilian control and resettled in Palestine or the United States; Truman ordered Eisenhower to inspect the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did survivors wait years in the camps?","a":"Britain restricted immigration to Palestine until Israel was founded in May 1948, and American quotas stayed tight until the Displaced Persons Act of 1948, so most survivors had few places to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Holocaust survivors"},
    "ww2-788": {"questions":["Buried in milk cans in the Warsaw ghetto, the Oyneg Shabes archive became part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that victims themselves compiled while the killing was still going on.","A London court in 2000 found that the convergence of documents, eyewitnesses and physical traces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left no fair-minded historian cause to doubt the Auschwitz gas chambers."],"abstract":"The <b>evidence and documentation for the Holocaust</b> is the body of perpetrator records, testimony, photographs, film and physical remains that shows how the Nazi regime and its collaborators murdered the Jews of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victims recorded the crime as it happened, and in the Warsaw ghetto the historian Emanuel Ringelblum organised the Oyneg Shabes archive, buried in milk cans and metal boxes and partly recovered after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Escapees from Auschwitz such as Witold Pilecki in 1943 and Rudolf Vrba and Alfred Wetzler in April 1944 wrote detailed reports that reached the Polish underground, the Allied governments and the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Although the Germans destroyed many records in 1945, the Allied armies captured millions of documents, from army and SS files to those of the Foreign Office and industrial firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even burned files could be partly rebuilt, as when the records of the Reich Security Main Office, burned in Prague, were pieced together from copies held in local Gestapo offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At the Nuremberg trial the prosecution chose to rest its case on the regime’s own words, submitting more than 100 German records of the persecution and murder of the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They included Heinrich Himmler’s speech of 4 October 1943 calling the annihilation of the Jews a glorious page of history never to be written, the Einsatzgruppen reports and the Stroop report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perpetrators testified too, and on 15 April 1946 the former Auschwitz commandant Rudolf Höss confirmed in court an affidavit describing how arrivals were selected and how the rest were killed with Zyklon B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each kind of evidence is weighed against the others, and Höss’s estimate of about 3,000,000 dead at Auschwitz has been revised by the camp’s memorial to about 1.1 million, about 1 million of them Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When a denier challenged it in a London libel trial, the judge ruled in 2000 that the convergence of documents, eyewitnesses and physical traces left no fair-minded historian serious cause to doubt the gas chambers of Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“Combating Holocaust Denial: Evidence of the Holocaust Presented at Nuremberg,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/combating-holocaust-denial-evidence-of-the-holocaust-presented-at-nuremberg. [Open access]","“Evidence from the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/evidence-from-the-holocaust. [Open access]","<i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 11, 108th day, 15 April 1946, testimony and affidavit of Rudolf Höss, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/04-15-46.asp. [Open access]","“Reports by Auschwitz Escapees,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/informing-the-world/reports-by-auschwitz-escapees. [Open access]","“Overall Numbers by Ethnicity or Category of Deportee,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/the-number-of-victims/overall-numbers-by-ethnicity-or-category-of-deportee. [Open access]","“Collecting Evidence: Oneg Shabbat,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/resistance-responses-collaboration/opposition/collecting-evidence-oneg-shabbat/. [Open access]","Mr Justice Gray, judgment in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books Limited and Lipstadt</i>, High Court of Justice, Queen’s Bench Division, 2000, paras. 13.72–13.91, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/judge/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg prosecutors rely on German documents?","a":"They wanted to convict the defendants with their own words rather than on testimony that could be called biased, and Robert Jackson argued that incredible events had to be established by credible evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How could records the Germans burned still be recovered?","a":"Copies often survived elsewhere: the Reich Security Main Office burned its files in Prague, but copies of many were collected from local Gestapo offices across Germany, and the Allies captured millions of other documents in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Höss’s figure for Auschwitz revised?","a":"A perpetrator’s estimate is one piece of evidence among many; the Auschwitz Memorial’s own study puts the dead at about 1.1 million rather than the 3,000,000 Höss gave, while his account of selection and gassing stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Evidence and documentation for the Holocaust"},
    "ww2-789": {"questions":["The Leuchter Report of 1988, written by a man without an engineering degree, became a favourite text of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though it misread how the gas chambers were used.","A London judge ruled in 2000 that David Irving had deliberately falsified history, a landmark against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the United Nations formally rejected in 2005."],"abstract":"<b>Holocaust denial</b> is the claim that the Nazi genocide of the Jews did not happen, or was far smaller than the evidence shows, usually by denying the gas chambers, and it works not by answering the evidence but by ignoring it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deniers say the Holocaust was a myth invented by the Allies, Soviet communists and Jews for their own ends, and they assert that discrediting a single fact discredits the whole history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its forms range from manipulating sources and minimising the numbers to trivialising the genocide as the kind of thing that happens in every war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is driven by antisemitism, by the wish to rehabilitate fascism and by the money made from books and donations, and its literature often wears footnotes and bibliographies to look scholarly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Nazis prepared its ground themselves, using code words such as ‘special treatment’ for killing, destroying most documents and, in Operation 1005, burning the bodies in the mass graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Early deniers included Paul Rassinier in France, whose 1964 book called the gas chambers an invention, and the Institute for Historical Review, founded in California in 1978, which publishes material and sponsors conferences denying the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its best-known claim to expertise, the Leuchter Report of 1988, came from a maker of execution equipment who was later shown to have no engineering degree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The report found less cyanide residue in the gas chamber ruins than in the delousing chambers, but the gas chambers were used for about half an hour a day against 24 hours for delousing, and their walls lie in ruins open to the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2000 the High Court in London, hearing David Irving’s libel action over a book about denial, found him an active Holocaust denier who had deliberately falsified the historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The judge held that no fair-minded historian could seriously doubt the gas chambers of Auschwitz, and in 2005 the United Nations General Assembly rejected any denial of the Holocaust, in full or in part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["“Combating Holocaust Denial: Origins of Holocaust Denial,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/combating-holocaust-denial-origins-of-holocaust-denial. [Open access]","“Holocaust Denial: Key Dates,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/holocaust-denial-key-dates. [Open access]","“Holocaust Deniers and Public Misinformation,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/holocaust-deniers-and-public-misinformation. [Open access]","“Leuchter Report,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/holocaust-denial/leuchter-report. [Open access]","“Denial Forms,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/holocaust-denial/denial-forms. [Open access]","“Holocaust and Genocide Denial after the War,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/holocaust-denial/holocaust-and-genocide-denial-after-the-war. [Open access]","Mr Justice Gray, judgment in <i>Irving v. Penguin Books Limited and Lipstadt</i>, High Court of Justice, Queen’s Bench Division, 2000, paras. 13.91, 13.95, 13.163, Holocaust Denial on Trial, Emory University, https://www.hdot.org/judge/. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, Resolution 60/7, <i>Holocaust Remembrance</i>, A/RES/60/7, adopted 1 November 2005, https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n05/487/96/pdf/n0548796.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the Leuchter Report fail?","a":"It compared cyanide residue in the gas chambers with that in the delousing chambers without allowing for how each was used, about half an hour a day for killing against 24 hours for delousing, or for walls now in ruins and open to the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its author also admitted he had no training in chemistry or toxicology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do deniers attack single facts?","a":"They claim that if one detail can be discredited the whole history falls, which lets them ignore the overwhelming mass of documents, testimony and physical evidence rather than answer it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the Irving trial establish?","a":"The court found that Irving was an active Holocaust denier who had deliberately misrepresented historical evidence for ideological reasons, and that the convergence of evidence left no fair-minded historian serious cause to doubt the gas chambers of Auschwitz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Holocaust denial"},
    "ww2-790": {"questions":["First held at United Nations headquarters on 27 January 2006, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has taken a yearly theme since 2010, among them women in 2011 and children in 2012.","The Stockholm pledge of 46 governments in January 2000 to encourage an annual day of remembrance preceded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the United Nations fixed on 27 January in 2005."],"abstract":"<b>International Holocaust Remembrance Day</b> is the annual day of commemoration on 27 January that the United Nations General Assembly designated in resolution 60/7, adopted on 1 November 2005, in memory of the victims of the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The date is the anniversary of 27 January 1945, when soldiers of the Soviet 60th Army opened the gates of Auschwitz and found about 7,000 prisoners still alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The resolution urged member states to teach the lessons of the Holocaust to help prevent future genocide, rejected any denial of the Holocaust in full or in part, and commended states that preserve the sites of the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first ceremony at United Nations headquarters in New York was held on 27 January 2006, and since 2010 each year’s commemoration has had a theme, such as women in 2011 and children in 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many countries also keep days of their own, such as Israel’s Yom Ha-Shoah, the American Days of Remembrance set up by Congress in 1979, and Hungary’s 16 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Such a day was a late development, for the Holocaust was not at first remembered as the central horror of the war, and memory of it grew differently from country to country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Soviet bloc Jews were rarely named as victims, and the monument at Babi Yar, where the Nazis had murdered 35,000 Jews, spoke only of more than 100,000 citizens of Kiev and prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the United States awareness came slowly, and even the Eichmann trial of 1961, which 87 per cent of Americans had heard of, left knowledge of the Holocaust limited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A turning point came in Stockholm in January 2000, when representatives of 46 governments pledged to promote Holocaust education, remembrance and research and to encourage an annual day of remembrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain held its first Holocaust Memorial Day on 27 January 2001, coordinated at first by the Home Office, and its trust now also remembers the victims of later genocides in Cambodia, Rwanda, Bosnia and Darfur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“International Holocaust Remembrance Day,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i> (Washington, DC: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum), https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/international-holocaust-remembrance-day. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, Resolution 60/7, <i>Holocaust Remembrance</i>, A/RES/60/7, adopted 1 November 2005, https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n05/487/96/pdf/n0548796.pdf. [Open access]","“The Stockholm Declaration,” Holocaust Memorial Day Trust, https://hmd.org.uk/what-is-holocaust-memorial-day/the-stockholm-declaration/. [Open access]","“What Is Holocaust Memorial Day?,” Holocaust Memorial Day Trust, https://www.hmd.org.uk/what-is-holocaust-memorial-day/. [Open access]","“Remembering the Holocaust,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/survival-and-legacy/legacy-and-memory/remembering-the-holocaust/. [Open access]","“Holocaust Memorial Day,” <i>The Holocaust Explained</i> (London: The Wiener Holocaust Library), https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/survival-and-legacy/legacy-and-memory/holocaust-memorial-day/. [Open access]","“Day of Liberation,” Auschwitz-Birkenau Memorial and Museum, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/liberation/day-of-liberation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was 27 January chosen?","a":"It is the anniversary of the liberation of Auschwitz-Birkenau, the largest Nazi death camp, whose gates soldiers of the Soviet 60th Army opened on 27 January 1945, finding about 7,000 prisoners alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet bloc remember the Holocaust differently?","a":"Communist governments presented the war as a shared struggle against fascism and tended not to single out Jews as victims, so memorials such as the one at Babi Yar spoke of murdered citizens in general terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does resolution 60/7 ask of member states?","a":"It urges them to develop educational programmes on the lessons of the Holocaust to help prevent genocide, rejects any denial of the Holocaust, commends states that preserve the camps and condemns religious and ethnic intolerance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"International Holocaust Remembrance Day"},
    "ww2-791": {"questions":["Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin warned in October 1943 that any German who committed such a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would be sent back to be judged where the deed was done.","The Nuremberg tribunal held that by 1939 the Hague rules were recognised by all civilised nations, so each breach by an individual was a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> punishable under international law."],"abstract":"A <b>war crime</b> is a violation of the laws or customs of war for which an individual can be held personally responsible, and the Charter of the International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg gave the term its standard definition in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Charter's list included the murder, ill-treatment or deportation to slave labour of civilians in occupied territory, the murder or ill-treatment of prisoners of war, the killing of hostages, plunder and the wanton destruction of towns not justified by military necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of those rules were older than the war, for the Hague Convention of 1907 required that the lives, family honour and private property of people in occupied territory be respected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Geneva Convention of 1929 added that prisoners of war must at all times be humanely treated and protected against violence, and it prohibited reprisals against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> War crimes stood beside two other crimes in the Charter, crimes against peace and crimes against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The Allies had promised such trials in wartime, when Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin declared in October 1943 that Germans responsible for atrocities would be sent back to be judged in the countries where they had committed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Nuremberg the defence argued that the Hague rules bound only states that had ratified them, but the tribunal held that by 1939 they were recognised by all civilised nations and that individuals who broke them were punishable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The judgment found that Germany had ruled the occupied territories by a systematic regime of violence and terror, and that the deaths of Soviet prisoners resulted from systematic plans to murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Tokyo the tribunal recorded that Japan, which had not ratified the 1929 convention, promised in January 1942 to apply it to Allied prisoners 'mutatis mutandis', meaning with the necessary changes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It then set a death rate of 4 per cent among British and American prisoners held by Germany and Italy against 27 per cent among those held by Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charter of the International Military Tribunal, 8 August 1945, art. 6, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “The Law Relating to War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” Judgment, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawre.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” Judgment, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex, The Hague, 18 October 1907, annex, art. 46, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","Convention Relative to the Treatment of Prisoners of War, Geneva, 27 July 1929, art. 2, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva02.asp. [Open access]","“Joint Four-Nation Declaration: Statement on Atrocities,” Moscow Conference, October 1943, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1001–02, 1097–98, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/international-military-tribunal-at-nuremberg. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Nuremberg tribunal apply rules Germany disputed?","a":"Because the tribunal held that the Hague Convention of 1907 had restated laws and customs of war that already existed, and that by 1939 its rules were recognised by all civilised nations, so individuals who broke them were punishable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did the 1929 Geneva Convention require for prisoners?","a":"It required that prisoners of war be humanely treated at all times and protected against violence, insults and public curiosity, and it forbade reprisals against them; the Nuremberg judgment named its articles among those the Charter's war crimes already covered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Tokyo tribunal compare death rates?","a":"Because the comparison showed the scale of the neglect: of British and American prisoners, 4 per cent died in German and Italian hands but 27 per cent in Japanese hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War crime"},
    "ww2-792": {"questions":["When deportations to Poland were halted in 1940, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became long-term holding pens, and Lackenbach in Austria grew into the largest forced-labour camp for Sinti and Roma.","Racial researchers working with the police measured and questioned families in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Roma were confined, registered and sent out to forced labour under criminal police supervision."],"abstract":"The <b>Zigeunerlager</b>, German for ‘Gypsy camps’, were the compounds into which German police and local authorities forced Roma and Sinti families from 1935, first on the edges of towns and later as forced-labour camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They grew out of an older police apparatus, for Bavaria had kept a central registry of Roma since 1899, and the Nazi state moved it to Berlin as the Reich Central Office for the Suppression of the Gypsy Nuisance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1936 Robert Ritter's racial hygiene institute measured and questioned the inmates, and it went on to write nearly 24,000 racial assessments that later served as the basis for deportations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The camps served, in the words of the Berlin memorial foundation, for concentration, registration, isolation and recruitment to forced labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many inmates went out to work by day and had to return at night, and the men were later made to work in armaments plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known camp was opened at Marzahn, an open field beside a cemetery and a sewage dump in eastern Berlin, when police arrested about 600 of the city's Roma shortly before the Olympic Games of 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After 1939 Berlin planned to deport all Roma to occupied Poland, and when deportations were suspended in 1940 the camps became long-term holding pens where hundreds died of the conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marzahn and the Austrian camps at Lackenbach and Salzburg were among the worst, and Lackenbach, opened in November 1940, became the largest forced-labour camp for Sinti and Roma in the Reich, with more than 2,000 people at its peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Local Germans complained about the camps and demanded the inmates' deportation, and the police used those complaints to press Himmler to resume deportations to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Holocaust museum calls the camps a preliminary stage on the road to genocide, and the men of Marzahn were sent to Sachsenhausen in 1938 while their families were deported to Auschwitz in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Persecution of Roma (Gypsies) in Prewar Germany, 1933–1939,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-roma-gypsies-in-prewar-germany-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Genocide of European Roma (Gypsies), 1939–1945,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/genocide-of-european-roma-gypsies-1939-1945. [Open access]","Stiftung Denkmal für die ermordeten Juden Europas, „Denkmal für die im Nationalsozialismus ermordeten Sinti und Roma Europas: Chronologie des Völkermords an den Sinti und Roma“, https://www.stiftung-denkmal.de/denkmaeler/denkmal-fuer-die-im-nationalsozialismus-ermordeten-sinti-und-roma-europas/ [in German]. [Open access]","Dokumentations- und Kulturzentrum Deutscher Sinti und Roma, „Lackenbach: Das größte Zwangsarbeitslager für Sinti und Roma im Deutschen Reich“, 30 October 2026, https://dokuzentrum.sintiundroma.de/lackenbach/ [in German]. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Genocide of the Roma,” 23 September 2024, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/genocide-roma. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, „Ausgrenzung und Verfolgung von Sinti und Roma“, <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung-und-verfolgung/ausgrenzung-und-verfolgung-von-sinti-und-roma [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Roma camps set up in German towns?","a":"Because the Nazi state treated Roma as racially alien and confined them so that they could be concentrated, registered, isolated and drafted for forced labour, often at the urging of local citizens and police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the camps grow worse after 1940?","a":"Because the regime suspended its deportations of Roma to occupied Poland, so camps meant as collection points became long-term holding pens where hundreds died of the conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the camps lead to genocide?","a":"They held people already registered and assessed by race, so the police could later deport them: the men of Marzahn went to Sachsenhausen in 1938 and their families to Auschwitz in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Bundesarchiv_Bild146-1990-104-16A%2C_K%C3%B6ln%2C_Razzia_im_Internierungslager_f%C3%BCr_Sinti_und_Roma.jpg","title":"A police raid in the Cologne camp, 1937","desc":"Criminal police officers check a woman's papers during a raid on the internment camp for Sinti and Roma at Cologne's Schwarz-Weiß-Platz in December 1937.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1990-104-16A, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild146-1990-104-16A,_K%C3%B6ln,_Razzia_im_Internierungslager_f%C3%BCr_Sinti_und_Roma.jpg","alt":"Two men in hats and overcoats, one holding a paper, stand on a dirt track beside a woman in a dark coat, with a fence and a large painted sign behind them."},"wiki":"Zigeunerlager"},
    "ww2-793": {"questions":["The night of 2 August 1944, when the SS gassed the last prisoners of the Birkenau family camp, is now the memorial day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across Europe.","In Croatia the Ustaša state destroyed nearly all its 25,000 Roma, one of many killings by Germany's allies that belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alongside the shootings in the occupied east."],"abstract":"The <b>Porajmos</b>, a Romani word meaning ‘the devouring’, is one of the names for the genocide of the Roma and Sinti of Europe by Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Persecution began in Germany in 1933 with internment in so-called Gypsy camps and forced sterilisation, and in November 1935 the Nuremberg race laws were extended to the Romani population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> During the war German units shot tens of thousands of Roma in occupied Poland, the Soviet Union and Serbia, at least 30,000 of them in the Baltic states and elsewhere in the occupied Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany's allies killed too, for the Ustaša state in Croatia destroyed virtually its entire Romani population of about 25,000, and Romania deported some 26,000 Roma to Transnistria, where thousands died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The total is unknown because the prewar population and the killings were poorly recorded, and estimates run from at least 250,000 to as many as 500,000 dead, making the Roma the largest group of victims after the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The deportations to Auschwitz followed an order of Himmler of 16 December 1942, and from February 1943 about 23,000 Roma and Sinti from much of Europe were sent to a family camp in sector BIIe of Birkenau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Families were not separated there, but hunger, overcrowding and epidemics of typhus killed most of them, and the camp physician Josef Mengele chose children and twins for experiments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the SS came to clear the camp on 16 May 1944, the prisoners barricaded themselves in with tools and pieces of wood, and the guards withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After stronger prisoners had been moved to other camps, the SS gassed those left on the night of 2 August, 2,897 by one count and about 4,300 by the Auschwitz Memorial's, a date the European Parliament made a day of remembrance in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the roughly 23,000 sent to Auschwitz about 21,000 died, and West Germany formally recognised the killing of its Roma as genocide only in March 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Genocide of European Roma (Gypsies), 1939–1945,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/genocide-of-european-roma-gypsies-1939-1945. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Genocide of the Roma,” 23 September 2024, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/genocide-roma. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Sinti and Roma in Auschwitz,” <i>History: Categories of Prisoners</i>, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/sinti-and-roma-in-auschwitz/. [Open access]","Stiftung Denkmal für die ermordeten Juden Europas, „Denkmal für die im Nationalsozialismus ermordeten Sinti und Roma Europas: Chronologie des Völkermords an den Sinti und Roma“, https://www.stiftung-denkmal.de/denkmaeler/denkmal-fuer-die-im-nationalsozialismus-ermordeten-sinti-und-roma-europas/ [in German]. [Open access]","Zentralrat Deutscher Sinti und Roma, „81. Jahrestag am 2. August 2025 – Europäischer Holocaust-Gedenktag für Sinti und Roma“, 29 July 2025, https://zentralrat.sintiundroma.de/81-jahrestag-am-2-august-2025-europaeischer-holocaust-gedenktag-fuer-sinti-und-roma/ [in German]. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, „Ausgrenzung und Verfolgung von Sinti und Roma“, <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung-und-verfolgung/ausgrenzung-und-verfolgung-von-sinti-und-roma [in German]. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Persecution of Roma (Gypsies) in Prewar Germany, 1933–1939,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-roma-gypsies-in-prewar-germany-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the death toll of the genocide uncertain?","a":"Because no one knows exactly how many Roma lived in Europe before the war, and the genocide was recognised and recorded late, with testimony and forensic evidence still emerging, so estimates run from at least 250,000 to about 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roma at Auschwitz resist on 16 May 1944?","a":"Because they had been warned that the SS meant to kill them, so they armed themselves with tools and pieces of wood and barricaded their barracks, and the guards withdrew for the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the genocide recognised so late in West Germany?","a":"Because postwar courts treated measures against Roma before 1943 as legitimate police action against criminals, closing the door to compensation until 1965, and a chancellor first called the killings genocide only in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Bundesarchiv_R_165_Bild-244-48%2C_Asperg%2C_Deportation_von_Sinti_und_Roma.jpg","title":"Deportation from Asperg, May 1940","desc":"Sinti and Roma families wait at an assembly point in Asperg, Württemberg, on 22 May 1940 before deportation to occupied Poland.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, R 165 Bild-244-48, photographer unknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_R_165_Bild-244-48,_Asperg,_Deportation_von_Sinti_und_Roma.jpg","alt":"A colour photograph of a long line of men, women and children standing against a high wall below a brick building, one man holding a guitar."},"wiki":"Romani Holocaust"},
    "ww2-794": {"questions":["From August 1942 nurses at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed patients by drug overdoses and starvation, among them forced labourers with tuberculosis whose murder led to a trial at Wiesbaden in 1945.","Reached by American troops on 26 March 1945, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held a cemetery whose rows of single graves hid mass burials made there from 1942 onwards."],"abstract":"The <b>Hadamar killing centre</b> was a state psychiatric hospital in the town of Hadamar in Hesse where, by the American Holocaust museum's count, almost 15,000 people, most of them psychiatric patients and people with disabilities, were murdered between 1941 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It served the Nazi 'euthanasia' programme, which from 1939 killed children and then adults in institutions and which, by the German Historical Museum's estimate, claimed more than 200,000 lives by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The adult killings were run from Tiergartenstraße 4 in Berlin, hence the name T4, where assessors decided from registration forms alone which patients would live and which would die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hadamar was the sixth and last of the T4 gassing centres, and from 13 January 1941 patients were killed with carbon monoxide in a gas chamber in its cellar and burned in its crematorium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Families received letters giving a false cause, time and sometimes place of death, and urns that did not hold their relatives' ashes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Over 10,000 patients had been murdered at Hadamar when the central programme was halted on 24 August 1941, weeks after the bishop of Münster had denounced the killings in a sermon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In August 1942 the staff began killing again, now by overdoses of medication, starvation and neglect, and the names of more than 4,400 victims of this second phase are known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The victims now included shell-shocked soldiers, children, Jewish children of mixed parentage and, from 1943 and 1944, forced labourers with tuberculosis, buried in mass graves behind a cemetery of fake individual graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American troops reached the town on 26 March 1945, and the evidence they gathered led to a trial before a United States military commission at Wiesbaden from 8 to 15 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the Americans could not yet try Germans for killing Germans, the charge was the murder of 476 Polish and Soviet nationals, and the commission sentenced the administrator and two male nurses to death and the chief physician to life imprisonment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hadamar Memorial Museum, “The “T4” Programme and the Hadamar Killing Centre (1941),” https://www.gedenkstaette-hadamar.de/en/history/the-t4-programme-and-the-hadamar-killing-centre-1941/. [Open access]","Hadamar Memorial Museum, ““Decentralised Euthanasia” and the Hadamar Killing Centre (1942–1945),” https://www.gedenkstaette-hadamar.de/en/history/decentralised-euthanasia-and-the-hadamar-killing-centre-1942-1945/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Hadamar Trial,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-hadamar-trial. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Hadamar,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hadamar. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “The Hadamar Trial: Trial of Alfons Klein and Six Others,” in <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 1 (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1947), 46–48, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-1/Law-Reports_Vol-1.pdf. [Open access]","Mirjam Husemann and Oliver Schweinoch, „„Euthanasie““, <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/voelkermord/euthanasie [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Hadamar staff tried only for killing foreigners?","a":"Because before Allied Control Council Law No. 10 of December 1945, American military courts could try Germans only for crimes against Allied nationals, so prosecutors charged the murder of 476 Polish and Soviet forced labourers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the killing go on after the T4 programme was halted?","a":"Because the halt of August 1941 ended only the central gassing, and from 1942 regional administrations ran their own killings in their institutions by overdoses and starvation, without central instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How were the victims' families deceived?","a":"They were sent letters with a false cause, time and sometimes place of death, and urns that did not contain their relatives' ashes, and from 1942 a cemetery of fake single graves hid mass burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Hadamar_cemetry_1945.jpg","title":"The Hadamar cemetery, April 1945","desc":"An American soldier looks over the institution's cemetery, where victims were buried in mass graves, on 5 April 1945.","credit":"Troy A. Peters, U.S. Army, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (United States Holocaust Memorial Museum). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadamar_cemetry_1945.jpg","alt":"A helmeted soldier stands on a slope looking out over a large field of bare earth marked by rows of short wooden posts, with a village and hills beyond."},"wiki":"Hadamar killing centre"},
    "ww2-795": {"questions":["In the SS marking system of 1937 and 1938, red meant a political prisoner, purple a Jehovah's Witness and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a man accused of homosexuality.","Many prisoners marked with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were set to work in the clay pits and brickworks of Sachsenhausen, among the hardest labour in the camp system."],"abstract":"The <b>pink triangle</b> was the badge that the SS made men imprisoned in Nazi concentration camps as 'homosexual' offenders wear on their uniforms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belonged to a marking system the SS introduced in 1937 and 1938, in which coloured inverted triangles showed the alleged reason for arrest: red for political prisoners, green for criminals, black for 'asocials' and purple for Jehovah's Witnesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind it lay Paragraph 175 of the criminal code, which banned sexual relations between men and which the Nazis tightened in 1935 so that any 'indecent act' between men could be punished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Police made about 100,000 arrests under the paragraph, about half ended in convictions, and most convicted men served a prison sentence and were released.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A minority, often men with several convictions, were sent to the camps, where the badge set them apart at the bottom of the prisoner hierarchy, despised by guards and fellow prisoners alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Estimates of the men held in the camps as homosexual offenders run from 5,000 to 15,000 in the American Holocaust museum's account and from 10,000 to 15,000 in the German Historical Museum's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Survivors describe them as among the most abused prisoners, given the hardest labour, shunned by others for fear of guilt by association and cut off from the networks that helped many survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Sachsenhausen many worked in the clay pits and brickworks, and an article published by the Auschwitz Memorial puts their death rate at about 55 per cent against 40 per cent for political prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Liberation in 1945 did not end their persecution, for West Germany kept the Nazi version of Paragraph 175 unchanged until 1969, and men still serving sentences under it stayed in prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany set aside the Nazi-era convictions under the paragraph only in 2002, and in 2008 a memorial to the homosexuals persecuted under National Socialism was opened in Berlin's Tiergarten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Gay Men under the Nazi Regime,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gay-men-under-the-nazi-regime. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Classification System in Nazi Concentration Camps,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/classification-system-in-nazi-concentration-camps. [Open access]","Robert Biedroń, “Nazism’s Pink Hell,” Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, first published in <i>Pro Memoria</i>, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/homosexuals-a-separate-category-of-prisoners/robert-biedron-nazisms-pink-hell/. [Open access]","Joachim Neander, “To My Comrades – from Karl,” Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, first published in <i>Pro Memoria</i>, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/homosexuals-a-separate-category-of-prisoners/joachim-neander-to-my-comrades--from-karl/. [Open access]","Stiftung Denkmal für die ermordeten Juden Europas, “Memorial to the Persecuted Homosexuals under National Socialism,” https://www.stiftung-denkmal.de/en/memorials/memorial-to-the-persecuted-homosexuals-under-national-socialism/. [Open access]","Sarah Bornhorst, „Homosexuellenverfolgung“, <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ausgrenzung/homosexuellenverfolgung [in German]. [Open access]","Gesetz zur Aufhebung nationalsozialistischer Unrechtsurteile in der Strafrechtspflege (NS-AufhG), 25 August 1998, as last amended 24 September 2009, annex, no. 26, Bundesministerium der Justiz, https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/ns-aufhg/BJNR250110998.html [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were pink-triangle prisoners so vulnerable in the camps?","a":"Because fellow prisoners shunned them for fear of guilt by association, which left them isolated and outside the networks that gave others food and clothing, and they were often given the hardest work and abused by guards and inmates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did liberation not free all of these men?","a":"Because sexual relations between men stayed illegal: West Germany kept the 1935 version of Paragraph 175 until 1969, so men serving sentences under it remained in prison, and the Nazi-era convictions were set aside only in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do estimates of the number imprisoned differ?","a":"Because they come from different reckonings, and some gay men were held under other categories and wore other badges; the American museum gives 5,000 to 15,000 and the German Historical Museum 10,000 to 15,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Gedenktafel_Homosexuelle_Opfer_Nollendorfplatz_Berlin.jpg","title":"Memorial at Nollendorfplatz, Berlin","desc":"A granite triangle and explanatory plaque on the wall of Nollendorfplatz station commemorate the homosexual victims of National Socialism.","credit":"Michael F. Mehnert, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gedenktafel_Homosexuelle_Opfer_Nollendorfplatz_Berlin.jpg","alt":"An inverted triangle of reddish granite carved with the words Totgeschlagen Totgeschwiegen, mounted on a concrete wall above a small bronze plaque."},"wiki":"Pink triangle"},
    "ww2-796": {"questions":["On 20 June 1937 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> distributed tens of thousands of copies of a secretly printed open letter across Germany, 2 years after their Watchtower Society was dissolved.","Registered at Auschwitz under the letters IBV, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept meeting and praying in the camps, and the SS often used them as servants because they would not escape."],"abstract":"The <b>Jehovah's Witnesses</b>, a Christian community founded in Pittsburgh in 1872 and known in Germany as the Bible Students, were persecuted systematically in Germany from 1933 and across Nazi-controlled Europe from 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The regime targeted them because they would not accept the authority of the state in matters of conscience, because of their international ties, and because they refused to bear arms for any earthly power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Witnesses refused to give the Hitler salute, join party organisations, let their children enter the Hitler Youth, vote in the regime's plebiscites or serve in the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bavaria banned their society soon after Hitler took office, by 1 April 1935 the Watchtower Society had been ordered dissolved, and a special Gestapo unit kept a register of everyone believed to be a Witness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In June 1937 they answered with an open letter, printed in secret in tens of thousands of copies and distributed across Germany on a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the camps they wore a purple triangle, at Auschwitz with the letters IBV for their German name, the International Bible Students Association.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some were tortured to make them sign declarations renouncing their faith, but few gave way, and in the camps they went on meeting, praying and seeking converts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because they refused to escape or fight their guards, camp officers thought them trustworthy and often used them as servants, though many also refused war work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counts of those sent to camps run from about 3,000 in the American Holocaust museum's figures to 4,200 in the Berlin memorial foundation's and nearly 10,000 in the German Historical Museum's, and at least 273 were executed for refusing military service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Estimates of the dead range from over 1,000 to at least 1,750, and a memorial to the persecuted Witnesses was opened in Berlin's Tiergarten on 24 June 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Persecution of Jehovah’s Witnesses,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-jehovahs-witnesses. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Jehovah’s Witnesses in Germany from the 1890s to the 1930s,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jehovahs-witnesses-in-germany-from-the-1890s-to-the-1930s. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Jehovah’s Witnesses,” <i>History: Categories of Prisoners</i>, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/jehovahs-witnesses/. [Open access]","Stiftung Denkmal für die ermordeten Juden Europas, “Memorial to Jehovah’s Witnesses Persecuted and Murdered under National Socialism,” https://www.stiftung-denkmal.de/en/memorials/mahnmal-fuer-die-im-nationalsozialismus-verfolgten-und-ermordeten-zeugen-jehovas/. [Open access]","German Resistance Memorial Center, “Resistance out of Christian Faith,” https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/topics/5-resistance-out-of-christian-faith. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Genocide of the Roma,” 23 September 2024, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/genocide-roma. [Open access]","Claudia Prinz and Arnulf Scriba, „Kirchen im NS-Regime“, <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/innenpolitik/kirchen-im-ns-regime [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Nazi state treat the Witnesses as enemies?","a":"Because they would not accept the state's authority in matters of conscience: they refused the Hitler salute, party membership, the plebiscites and military service, and the regime distrusted their international connections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the SS use Witnesses as servants in the camps?","a":"Because they refused to escape or to resist their guards physically, so camp officers thought them relatively trustworthy and often made them domestic servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the conflict sharpen after 1935?","a":"Because Germany reintroduced conscription in March 1935, and Witnesses who refused to be drafted or to do military work, and who kept meeting illegally, were arrested and sent to prisons and camps in growing numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Kz-gedenkst%C3%A4tte-neuengamme-gedenkhain-denkmal-zeugen-jehovas-winkel.JPG","title":"Memorial at Neuengamme","desc":"A triangular plaque in the memorial grove of the Neuengamme concentration camp memorial commemorates the Witnesses imprisoned there between 1940 and 1945.","credit":"Roland.h.bueb, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kz-gedenkst%C3%A4tte-neuengamme-gedenkhain-denkmal-zeugen-jehovas-winkel.JPG","alt":"A purple inverted triangle with a black border, lettered in German, fixed to a wall of yellow and red bricks."},"wiki":"Jehovah's Witnesses"},
    "ww2-797": {"questions":["Naming the captured generals Kachalov and Kirillov as cowards, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered that encircled units which chose to surrender be destroyed by every means, on the ground and from the air.","First printed by a Soviet military journal in September 1988, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stripped the families of surrendered Red Army soldiers of their state assistance."],"abstract":"<b>Order No. 270</b> was an order of the Soviet Supreme High Command, issued on 16 August 1941 and signed by Stalin, Molotov, Budyonny, Voroshilov, Timoshenko, Shaposhnikov and Zhukov, that made surrender to the enemy a form of desertion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It named Lieutenant General Kachalov of the 28th Army and Major General Kirillov of the 13th Rifle Corps as cowards who had gone over to the Germans, and blamed their staffs for failing to stop them or Ponedelin from surrendering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commanders and political workers who tore off their insignia, deserted to the rear or surrendered were to be treated as deserters and shot on the spot, and their families arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Encircled units were to fight to the last, and a unit that chose surrender instead was to be destroyed by every means on the ground and from the air, its men's families losing their state assistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order was to be read out in every company, battery and staff, but it was first published only in September 1988, when the Soviet military history journal printed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was issued as German encirclements took Soviet soldiers prisoner in huge numbers, and by the American Holocaust museum's figures some 5.7 million Red Army personnel fell into German hands during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 3.3 million of them, some 57 per cent, died in German captivity under policies the museum calls deliberate, against 3.6 per cent of British and American prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Auschwitz the first Soviet prisoners were shot in the gravel pits in 1941, and in September about 600 were gassed with Zyklon B in the cellars of block 11 in the largest of its first test killings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the eyes of their own leadership, a Russian military journal has written, captivity became not only shameful but criminal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Men who escaped encirclement or captivity met arbitrary repression after the order, and from June 1942 the relatives of men deemed traitors faced 5 years of exile to remote regions of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Headquarters of the Supreme High Command of the Red Army, “Order No. 270 of 16 August 1941,” trans. in <i>JPRS Report: Soviet Union, Military History Journal</i>, no. 9, September 1988, JPRS-UMJ-89-003 (Arlington, VA: Foreign Broadcast Information Service, 1989), from <i>Voyenno-istoricheskiy zhurnal</i>, no. 9 (1988): 23–32, https://archive.org/details/jprs-report_jprs-umj-89-003. [Open access]","И. В. Анохин и В. И. Субботин, «„В сухих строчках отражаются как высоты человеческого духа… так и падения…“. Судьбы советских военнопленных в трофейных немецких документах», <i>Военно-исторический журнал</i>, no. 1 (2023), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/v-suhih-strochkah-otrazhayutsya-kak-vysoty-chelovecheskogo-duha-tak-i-padeniya-sudby-sovetskih-voennoplennyh-v-trofeynyh-nemetskih [in Russian]. [Open access]","В. Я. Беккер, «Сталинизм как фактор „второй гражданской войны“ в период Великой Отечественной войны 1941–1945 годов», <i>Вестник Хакасского государственного университета им. Н. Ф. Катанова</i>, no. 14 (2015), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/stalinizm-kak-faktor-vtoroy-grazhdanskoy-voyny-v-period-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyny-1941-1945-godov [in Russian]. [Open access]","И. Б. Степанова и О. В. Соколова, «Особенности уголовно-правовой политики в годы Великой Отечественной войны», <i>Право и практика</i>, no. 4 (2024), https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/osobennosti-ugolovno-pravovoy-politiki-v-gody-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyny [in Russian]. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]","Auschwitz-Birkenau State Museum, “Soviet POWs,” <i>History: Categories of Prisoners</i>, https://www.auschwitz.org/en/history/categories-of-prisoners/soviet-pows/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the order threaten soldiers' families?","a":"Because the Supreme Command meant to deter surrender: the families of commanders who surrendered were to be arrested as families of deserters, and those of ordinary soldiers lost their state assistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Supreme Command issue the order in August 1941?","a":"Because the Red Army was being encircled and destroyed, and the order itself complains of shameful cases of surrender, naming generals who had gone over to the enemy while praising those who fought their way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the order shape the treatment of former prisoners?","a":"It cast captivity as a crime rather than a misfortune, and it opened the way to gross violations of legality and arbitrary treatment of soldiers who came out of encirclement, escaped captivity or were freed by Soviet troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Order No. 270"},
    "ww2-798": {"questions":["Between the ceasefire and the arrival of American troops in late August 1945, Japanese authorities burned records to hide evidence of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, especially those against prisoners of war.","Australian military courts tried 922 men for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against prisoners and civilians, convicting 641 and executing 137, alongside 919 accused tried by Britain."],"abstract":"<b>Japanese war crimes</b> were the violations of the laws of war committed by the armed forces of the Empire of Japan from the war in China to the surrender of August 1945, against prisoners of war, civilian internees and the peoples of occupied Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal found that torture, murder, rape and other cruelties were freely practised by the army and navy, on a scale so vast and so uniform that they must have been secretly ordered or wilfully permitted by the government or the military leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Successive governments called the fighting in China an 'incident' rather than a war, and the military authorities used that as an excuse to claim that the rules of war did not apply there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Singapore in February 1942, Chinese men were screened and taken away to be killed under a harsh policy decided by the 25th Army's headquarters, one of many massacres of civilians across occupied Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dead are hard to count, and one survey notes that estimates of 15 million Chinese and 20 million South-East Asians who died in the conflict are disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunal's list of crimes against prisoners ran from killings and death marches to forced labour in tropical heat and starvation, and on the march from Bataan some 750 Americans and 5,000 Filipinos died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It counted 4 per cent of British and American prisoners dead in German and Italian hands against 27 per cent of the 132,134 held by Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over 22,000 Australian servicemen were captured by the Japanese, and about 8,000 of them died before the war ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Tokyo seven of 28 leaders were hanged, while Britain tried 919 accused in 304 cases and confirmed 222 death sentences, and Australian courts tried 922 men, convicted 641 and executed 137.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between the ceasefire of 15 August 1945 and the landing of American advance parties on 28 August, Japanese authorities systematically destroyed archives and ordered units to burn evidence of crimes against prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1001–03, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Arnaud Doglia, “Japanese Mass Violence and Its Victims in the Fifteen Years War (1931–45),” <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 October 2011, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/japanese-mass-violence-and-its-victims-in-the-fifteen-years-war-1931-45/. [Open access]","Hayashi Hirofumi, “British War Crimes Trials of Japanese,” <i>Nature-People-Society: Science and the Humanities</i>, no. 31 (Kanto Gakuin University, July 2001), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng08.htm. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – The War Crimes Trials,” online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan/warcrimes. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – Prisoners of the Japanese,” online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan. [Open access]","Edward Drea, “Introduction,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 10, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 32–33, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Hayashi Hirofumi, “Massacre of Chinese in Singapore and Its Coverage in Postwar Japan,” in <i>New Perspectives on the Japanese Occupation in Malaya and Singapore</i>, ed. Akashi Yoji and Yoshimura Mako (Singapore: NUS Press, 2008), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng18.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japanese commanders claim the laws of war did not apply in China?","a":"Because successive governments refused to call the fighting a war and called it an 'incident', and the military authorities used that as an excuse to treat the rules of war as not applying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do fewer records survive than for Nazi crimes?","a":"Because between the ceasefire of 15 August 1945 and the arrival of American advance parties on 28 August, Japanese authorities systematically destroyed archives and ordered units to burn evidence, while in Germany Allied teams were seizing records as the Reich fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many more prisoners die in Japanese hands?","a":"The tribunal pointed to killings, death marches, forced labour in tropical heat and a lack of food, housing and medicine that caused thousands of deaths from disease, so that 27 per cent of British and American prisoners died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Japanese war crimes"},
    "ww2-799": {"questions":["Unmarked and so attacked by Allied submarines like any other vessel, the Montevideo Maru was a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose sinking in July 1942 killed about 1,000 Australian prisoners and civilians.","Japan blamed conditions aboard every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its shortage of shipping, but the Tokyo tribunal held that a state unable to move prisoners lawfully had no right to move them."],"abstract":"A <b>hell ship</b> was one of the cargo and passenger vessels in which Japan moved Allied prisoners of war and conscripted labourers by sea around its empire, packed into holds and coal bunkers in conditions that killed many on the voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal described wooden platforms built in the holds about 0.9 metres (3 feet) apart, with a space of about 1.8 metres (6 feet) square for every 15 prisoners, who had to sit cross-legged for the whole voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Buckets lowered on ropes served as latrines, food was served uncooked, water was short, and the prisoners, many already sick with dysentery, were kept below decks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Australian official history adds that the ships usually carried 500 to 1,500 prisoners, that the holds were dark and poorly ventilated, and that voyages that took 10 days in 1942 took up to 70 by 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Japan defended the practice by its shortage of shipping, a plea the tribunal rejected because a state unable to move prisoners under the laws of war was not entitled to move them at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The ships were unmarked, so Allied submarines and aircraft attacked them like any other Japanese vessel, and thousands of prisoners died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The worst Australian loss came on 1 July 1942, when the American submarine Sturgeon torpedoed the Montevideo Maru, carrying prisoners from Rabaul, and an estimated 845 soldiers and up to 208 civilians died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Lisbon Maru sank in October 1942 with British prisoners from Hong Kong, guards fastened down the hatches, and survivors of the Oryoku Maru in December 1944 were shot in the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American codebreakers read Japanese messages on such sinkings, one reporting that a transport torpedoed in September 1944 had carried 950 prisoners, of whom 431 remained missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over a thousand Australians died in such sinkings, and of the more than 30,000 prisoners shipped to Japan, 3,526 of the Americans transported, about 10 per cent, had died by the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1067–71, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Montevideo Maru – Sinking of the Montevideo Maru, 1 July 1942,” https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/montevideo_maru. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – Prisoners of the Japanese,” online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 32–33, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Robert Hanyok, “Wartime COMINT Records in the National Archives about Japanese War Crimes in the Asia and Pacific Theaters, 1978–1997,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 120, 135, 139, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 25, “Camps in Borneo, Japan and Elsewhere,” 611–15, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070117. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many prisoners killed by Allied attacks?","a":"Because the prison ships were unmarked, Allied forces could not tell them from other Japanese ships, and packing prisoners below decks made escape from a sinking ship almost impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japan's War Ministry order better treatment at sea?","a":"Because prisoners were arriving too sick or weak to work; an order of December 1942 aimed to deliver them fit for labour, and the tribunal found that such measures were meant to preserve their labour, not to obey the laws of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Allied commanders know little about who was aboard?","a":"Because intercepted Japanese messages mentioned prisoners only obliquely and were often released weeks or months late, and a bulletin on the convoy of the Arisan Maru said nothing of the prisoners aboard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/MV_Montevideo_Maru.jpg","title":"The Montevideo Maru, about 1941","desc":"The passenger ship that was carrying Australian prisoners and civilians from Rabaul when an American submarine sank it on 1 July 1942.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (Australian War Memorial 303640). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MV_Montevideo_Maru.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a long dark-hulled passenger liner with a single funnel and two masts, steaming on a choppy sea."},"wiki":"Hell ship"},
    "ww2-800": {"questions":["Officers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Manchuria sent prisoners to Unit 731 without any trial, recording them in their files as 'special consignments' to be killed in experiments.","In February 1942 men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> screened Singapore's Chinese men aged 18 to 50 at mass screening centres, picking out those to be taken away and killed."],"abstract":"The <b>Kempeitai</b> was the military police of the Imperial Japanese Army, a branch under a provost marshal general responsible to the Minister of War, whose headquarters dealt with discipline and the control of thought in the armed forces, security and counter-espionage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Japan it answered to the War Ministry for military duties, to the Home Ministry when it helped the civil police and to the Justice Ministry for legal work, and its tasks were discipline, security, conscription and crime among soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In occupied areas it took on far more, from travel permits and the arrest of fifth columnists to requisitioning food, recruiting local labour and running local espionage networks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American wartime handbook believed it had 315 officers and over 6,000 men in 1937, and its men wore a white armband bearing the two characters of its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The War Ministry administered it and ran its training school, and the Tokyo tribunal inferred that its conduct reflected ministry policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunal found the Kempeitai the most active of Japan's forces in torture, using the water treatment, burning, electric shocks, suspension and flogging, and local police forces it organised in occupied territories did the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Singapore its men carried out most of the urban screening of Chinese men aged 18 to 50 between 21 and 23 February 1942, in a purge planned with the Kempeitai commander Oishi Masayuki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Manchuria its officers sent Chinese, Manchurian and Soviet prisoners to Unit 731 to be killed in experiments, recording them as 'special consignments' so that no court proceedings were needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When more subjects were wanted, it also rounded up civilians at random in the villages and cities of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American archival survey notes that most Japanese crimes were committed by regular army troops, but that the Kempeitai gained notoriety for abetting and taking part in crimes in China and Malaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United States War Department, <i>Handbook on Japanese Military Forces</i>, TM-E 30-480 (Washington, DC, 1 October 1944), chap. VI, “Japanese Military Police,” 83–84, https://archive.org/details/TME30-480. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1057–58, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Hayashi Hirofumi, “Massacre of Chinese in Singapore and Its Coverage in Postwar Japan,” in <i>New Perspectives on the Japanese Occupation in Malaya and Singapore</i>, ed. Akashi Yoji and Yoshimura Mako (Singapore: NUS Press, 2008), author’s English text, http://hayashihirofumi.g1.xrea.com/eng18.htm. [Open access]","<i>Materials on the Trial of Former Servicemen of the Japanese Army Charged with Manufacturing and Employing Bacteriological Weapons</i> (Moscow: Foreign Languages Publishing House, 1950), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/MaterialsOnTheTrialOfFormerServicemen. [Open access]","Arnaud Doglia, “Japanese Mass Violence and Its Victims in the Fifteen Years War (1931–45),” <i>Mass Violence &amp; Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 October 2011, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/japanese-mass-violence-and-its-victims-in-the-fifteen-years-war-1931-45/. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 37, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Michael Petersen, “The Intelligence that Wasn’t: CIA Name Files, the U.S. Army, and Intelligence Gathering in Occupied Japan,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 224, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Tokyo tribunal blame the War Ministry for Kempeitai torture?","a":"Because the War Ministry administered the Kempeitai and ran its training school in Japan, so the tribunal judged it a reasonable inference that the conduct of the military police reflected ministry policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Kempeitai so powerful in occupied territory?","a":"Because in occupied areas it added duties over the local population to its police work, from travel permits and arresting suspected fifth columnists to requisitioning food, recruiting labour and running espionage networks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What part did it play in the Singapore massacre?","a":"Its commander Oishi Masayuki helped plan the purge of Chinese men with the army's staff, and Kempeitai personnel carried out most of the screening in the city between 21 and 23 February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/De_Kempeitai-zaak_voor_de_Krijgsraad_te_Batavia%2C_Bestanddeelnr_902-2466.jpg","title":"A military police trial in Batavia, 1947","desc":"Japanese defendants before a Dutch court-martial in Batavia on 1 July 1947, in a case against members of the army's military police.","credit":"Anefo, Nationaal Archief, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Kempeitai-zaak_voor_de_Krijgsraad_te_Batavia,_Bestanddeelnr_902-2466.jpg","alt":"A courtroom with men seated at long tables covered in papers and microphones, a row of defendants on the left and helmeted guards standing behind them."},"wiki":"Kempeitai"},
    "ww2-821": {"questions":["A survey made for the United States in 1945 judged that the German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed incompletely mobilised until the defeat at Moscow, with many factories working a single shift.","Turning out munitions worth 61.3 billion dollars in 1944 while consumers still spent more than before, the American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made almost 45 per cent of all combat munitions that year."],"abstract":"A <b>war economy</b> is a national economy reorganised so that labour, raw materials, factories and money go first to the armed forces, with civilian production cut back to what is left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Second World War this reorganisation created the home front, because the work of civilians in factories, fields and offices became as necessary to the fighting as the soldiers it equipped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Governments took powers to direct workers, allocate scarce materials, fix prices and wages and ration food and clothing, so that markets no longer decided what was made or who received it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trade passed into state hands as well, and by July 1940 the British Ministry of Food was buying nearly 90 per cent of the country's imports of human and animal food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the United States war-related production rose from about 2 per cent of gross national product to 40 per cent in 1943, and unemployment fell to 1.2 per cent in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The belligerents did not mobilise at the same speed, and an American survey of 1945 judged that German industry stayed incompletely mobilised until the defeat before Moscow, much of it working a single shift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain went further, and by July 1941 its Ministry of Labour reckoned that about 49 per cent of the occupied population was employed on government work of one kind or another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To hold down spending it rationed clothing by points from June 1941 and canned meat, fish and beans by points from December 1941, after rejecting a single ration covering all expenditure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> American output grew so fast that the War Production Board reported munitions worth 61.3 billion dollars in 1944 and reckoned that the United States made almost 45 per cent of the world's combat munitions that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet American consumers spent almost 7 billion dollars more on goods and services in 1944 than in 1943, so a war economy could press on civilians very unevenly from one country to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 11, “Manpower,” 281–314, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-11.html. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]","War Production Board, <i>War Production in 1944: Report of the Chairman of the War Production Board</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 7–22, GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-P32_4800-9e92e30522a253276362395c30ab5450/html/GOVPUB-P32_4800-9e92e30522a253276362395c30ab5450.htm. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","The National Archives, “The Home Front in the Second World War,” education resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/home-front-second-world-war/. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 12, “The Cost of Increasing Effort,” 315–46, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-12.html. [Open access]","Robert Boothby, speech on the Ministry of Food, House of Commons, 18 July 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 363, cols. 447–543, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/18/ministry-of-food. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did governments not leave war production to the market?","a":"Because firms would not stop making consumer goods of their own accord, for fear of losing customers to rivals, so the American government created agencies that bought equipment and closely directed how it was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain likewise counted its workers and budgeted them between the services and industry, because the shortage of people was the tightest limit on what it could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Germany slow to mobilise its economy fully?","a":"Because, according to the American survey of 1945, Germany had expected to win by short lightning campaigns and did not foresee the need for full economic mobilisation until the defeat at Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of its industry therefore kept a single shift and drew in few women until later in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How could American civilians spend more in the middle of a war?","a":"Because wages rose about 65 per cent over the war while price controls held inflation down, so many workers gained real income even as cars and other goods disappeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The War Production Board noted that consumers spent almost 7 billion dollars more in 1944 than in 1943, though higher prices meant they did not actually buy much more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War economy"},
    "ww2-822": {"questions":["Britain's National Service Act of December 1941, which bound everyone aged 18 to 60 to some form of national service, carried the logic of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> further than most belligerents went.","An American survey of 1945 concluded that Germany turned to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> late, its industry incompletely mobilised until the defeat at Moscow and few German women drawn into its factories."],"abstract":"<b>Total war</b> is war in which a state tries to harness its entire population and all its material resources to the fighting, treating civilian labour, industry and morale as weapons of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its marks in the Second World War included the compulsory direction of civilians into war work as well as into the forces, and the bombing of the enemy's factories, transport and cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Britain, Winston Churchill told the Commons on 2 December 1941 that the crisis of man-power and woman-power was at hand and would dominate the year 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The new National Service Act that followed in December bound everyone aged 18 to 60 to some form of national service and made unmarried women and childless widows aged 20 to 30 liable to call-up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Total war reached the citizen's purse too, as Britain rationed clothes by points from June 1941 and the United States raised its number of federal taxpayers from 4 million in 1939 to 43 million in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In Germany the idea came late, and an American survey of 1945 concluded that German industry was incompletely mobilised until the defeat at Moscow and that relatively few German women were drawn into factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the catastrophe of Stalingrad, Joseph Goebbels proclaimed total war on 18 February 1943 before about 3,000 selected listeners in the Berlin Sportpalast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His speech announced the closing of bars, night clubs, luxury restaurants and luxury shops, and it ended with ten questions, among them whether the audience wanted a war more total and radical than they could yet imagine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A month earlier Hitler had ordered the mobilisation of all resources, making men aged 16 to 65 and women aged 17 to 45 liable to be called on for the defence of the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany filled much of its labour gap with foreign and forced workers, and after July 1944 Goebbels, as plenipotentiary for total war, closed businesses not needed for the war and imposed working weeks of over 70 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Joseph Goebbels, “Nation, Rise Up, and Let the Storm Break Loose,” speech in the Berlin Sportpalast, 18 February 1943, trans. Randall Bytwerk, German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/goeb36.htm. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Totaler Krieg,” LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/totaler-krieg.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 11, “Manpower,” 281–314, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-11.html. [Open access]","H. M. D. Parker, <i>Manpower: A Study of War-time Policy and Administration</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 163, 286–87, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/manpower-parker. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, speech moving the motion on maximum national effort, House of Commons, 2 December 1941, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 376, cols. 1009–95, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/02/maximum-national-effort. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 12, “The Cost of Increasing Effort,” 315–46, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-12.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels proclaim total war only in 1943?","a":"Because the end of the battle of Stalingrad had made the seriousness of the war plain, and Goebbels wanted both to rouse popular enthusiasm and to win greater powers over the war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American survey of 1945 judged that Germany had not foreseen the need for full economic mobilisation until its defeat at Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain let conscripted women choose their war work?","a":"Because ministers, the Prime Minister among them, at first opposed conscripting women at all, fearing that many would be unsuited to service life and that servicemen would protest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The act passed only with safeguards: it covered unmarried women aged 20 to 30, only volunteers went to combatant duties, and each woman could choose the services, industry or civil defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did Germany fill the gap in its workforce?","a":"Largely with foreign and forced labour, recruited by the plenipotentiary for labour deployment, Fritz Sauckel, while posters promised that total war would be the shortest war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American survey found that relatively few German women were drawn into industry, fewer than in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Total war"},
    "ww2-823": {"questions":["On the night of 7 September 1940 some local commanders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rang church bells to call out their men, wrongly suggesting that German parachutists were already landing.","Ordered to stand down on 1 November 1944 after manning rocket batteries and anti-aircraft guns near their homes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was formally disbanded on 31 December 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Home Guard</b> was Britain's unpaid, part-time citizen defence force of the Second World War, raised in May 1940 as the Local Defence Volunteers when a German invasion seemed possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The decision to raise it was taken on 12 May 1940, the Secretary of State for War appealed for volunteers by broadcast on 14 May, and more than 250,000 men had come forward by 20 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first tasks were observation, delaying small enemy parties landed from the air and guarding vulnerable points, so that the army's trained divisions need not be broken up for local guard duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many early volunteers had no uniform beyond a khaki armband stencilled with the letters LDV, worn over their own clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 23 July 1940 Anthony Eden announced that the force, by then more than 1,300,000 strong, would take the title Home Guard, and the change took effect on 31 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Organised to fight near their own homes, its units were meant to turn villages, parishes and townships into a network of defended places that would hamper troops landed from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It never fought an invasion, and on the night of 7 September 1940 some of its local commanders rang church bells to call out their men, wrongly suggesting that German parachutists were already descending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From January 1942 the Minister of Labour could direct men to enrol in it, and about 946,000 such directions had been issued by September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Members also manned rocket batteries from 1941 and heavy anti-aircraft guns near their homes from 1942, until compulsory training and parades ended in September 1944 and the force stood down on 1 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was formally disbanded on 31 December 1945, when the Secretary of State for War called it “a citizen force without parallel in our long history.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Edward Grigg, statement on the Local Defence Volunteers, House of Commons, 22 May 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 361, cols. 238–76, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/may/22/local-defence-volunteers. [Open access]","Anthony Eden, answers on the Local Defence Volunteers and the Home Guard, House of Commons, 23 July 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 363, cols. 575–80, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/23/local-defence-volunteers-the-home-guard. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. 14, “The Invasion Risk: The Crisis and After,” 219–23, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-14.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: HMSO, 1957), chap. 21, “The Watch on the Base,” 321–24, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-21.html. [Open access]","J. J. Lawson, statement on the disbandment of the Home Guard, House of Commons, 12 December 1945, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 417, cols. 418–20, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/dec/12/home-guard-disbandment. [Open access]","James Grigg, answer on the Home Guard, House of Commons, 31 October 1944, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 404, cols. 605–6, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/oct/31/home-guard. [Open access]","The National Archives, “The Home Front in the Second World War,” education resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/home-front-second-world-war/. [Open access]","H. M. D. Parker, <i>Manpower: A Study of War-time Policy and Administration</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1957), appendix I, 164–65, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/manpower-parker. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the force raised in such a hurry in May 1940?","a":"Because the German campaign in the west raised the fear of an invasion of Britain, and ministers wanted local men who could watch for and delay small parties landed by parachute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Using volunteers also freed the army's trained divisions from scattered guard duties, so that they could be kept together for training and battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did ringing church bells in September 1940 cause alarm?","a":"Because bells were the signal that parachutists had landed, and when the code-word Cromwell went out on 7 September some commanders called out their men by ringing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The next morning General Brooke ordered that bells be rung only by a Home Guard member who had himself seen at least 25 parachutists descending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Home Guard take over anti-aircraft work later in the war?","a":"Because regular gunners were being withdrawn for the field army preparing to invade Europe, so the defence of Britain was left increasingly to the Home Guard and other second-line forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Members manned rocket batteries from 1941 and heavy guns from 1942, each expected to report for duty on one night in eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Home Guard (United Kingdom)"},
    "ww2-824": {"questions":["Launched on 1 December 1941 with canned meat, fish and beans, the points scheme became the most popular part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, letting shoppers choose how to spend their allowance.","Although bread and restaurant meals stayed off the ration throughout the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covered bacon, butter, sugar, meat, clothes, sweets and many tinned foods by the middle of 1942."],"abstract":"<b>Rationing in the United Kingdom</b> was the system by which the British government limited and shared out scarce food, clothing and other goods, many of which had to come from abroad, during the Second World War and for years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every man, woman and child received a ration book, and households registered with chosen shopkeepers, who were supplied according to the customers registered with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ration books had been prepared before the war, and in November 1939 opposition members protested that the rationing of bacon and butter would not begin until at least the middle of December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the end bacon, butter and sugar were rationed from 8 January 1940 and meat from 11 March 1940, the meat ration being fixed by price at first at one shilling and tenpence worth a head each week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By July 1940 the Ministry of Food was buying nearly 90 per cent of the country's food imports, and its spokesman called rationing “a rigid, arithmetical and somewhat inhuman way of allocating the food of individuals.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its most popular device was the points scheme, which began on 1 December 1941 with canned meat, fish and beans and let shoppers spend an allowance of points on whichever covered foods they chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Clothing had been rationed by points from June 1941, sweets and chocolate followed in the middle of 1942, and the basic petrol ration for private motorists was abolished in March 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bread was never rationed during the war, and meals in canteens and restaurants stayed off the ration, although restaurant meals were limited in courses and to a maximum charge of five shillings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rationing outlasted the fighting, for a mild bread ration was introduced in 1946, and in February 1954 the government announced that ending meat and bacon rationing would be the final step in dismantling the wartime system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Butter, margarine and cooking fat came off the ration on 8 May 1954 and meat on 4 July 1954, 14 years after rationing had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["R. J. Hammond, <i>Food</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Growth of Policy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1951), 114–20, 199–205, 284, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/food-volume1. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 12, “The Cost of Increasing Effort,” 315–46, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-12.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. 17, “The Civilian Economy,” 491–514, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-17.html. [Open access]","Robert Boothby, speech on the Ministry of Food, House of Commons, 18 July 1940, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 363, cols. 447–543, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/18/ministry-of-food. [Open access]","Charles Hill, answer on meat and bacon derationing, House of Commons, 17 February 1954, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 523, cols. 1974–75, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1954/feb/17/meat-and-bacon-derationing. [Open access]","Derick Heathcoat Amory, answer on prices, House of Commons, 21 February 1955, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 537, cols. 864–66, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1955/feb/21/prices. [Open access]","The National Archives, “The Home Front in the Second World War,” education resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/home-front-second-world-war/. [Open access]","A. V. Alexander, speech moving a motion on food supply, House of Commons, 8 November 1939, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 353, cols. 269–380, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/nov/08/food-supply. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did food rationing start only in January 1940?","a":"Because the machinery fell behind: the national registration records were handed over late, the ration books took until late October to write up and up to a fortnight to deliver, and shoppers then had to register with retailers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ministry of Food therefore put rationing off until after Christmas, despite protests in Parliament that it was overdue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did officials choose points rather than group rationing?","a":"Because points were flexible: shoppers needed no new registrations and could choose among many foods, while the points price of each could be raised or lowered to match supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Group rationing would have required six new registrations, favoured larger shops and meant far more regimentation of retailers and consumers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was bread kept off the ration during the war?","a":"Because officials had long treated an unlimited supply of bread as the foundation of food policy, and a Minister of Food called bread rationing “the last resort of a starving nation”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When it was debated in 1942 the issue was waste rather than hunger, and the Ministry of Food rejected it, so bread was rationed only in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rationing in the United Kingdom"},
    "ww2-825": {"questions":["Lady Denman resigned as head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in February 1945 because its members were left out of the post-war grants given to the women's services and civil defence.","Sanctioned in the spring of 1939 so that recruits could be trained before war began, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had grown to 52,000 members by September 1942 as farms lost men to the forces."],"abstract":"The <b>Women's Land Army</b> was the British organisation that recruited women to work on farms during the Second World War, replacing men called into the forces while the country tried to grow more of its own food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It had been sanctioned in the spring of 1939 so that women could be enrolled and trained before war broke out, and it became farming's one continuous source of extra labour in 1940 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most members lived in farmers' homes or cottages and were employed by individual farmers at the ordinary weekly wage of farm workers, a wage a minister admitted in 1945 was small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They wore uniform and belonged to a force called an army, but they were not under the discipline of the women's auxiliary services and were not employed by the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many recruits had been typists, clerks or shop assistants, and the work was heavy for women new to it, part of a wider upheaval that took thousands of women into the services, factories and farms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its strength grew by about 1,500 a month to more than 19,000 by August 1941 and reached 52,000 by September 1942, after women were made liable to direction into war work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1941 the Ministry of Labour had already listed the Land Army among the vital war work to which women able to move away from home could be sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recruitment was stopped in 1943, yet the official historian reckoned that its members did work equal to about 64,000 men, and on 31 January 1945 it still had 64,551 members in England and Wales and 7,972 in Scotland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In February 1945 Lady Denman, its leader since its foundation, resigned because members had been left out of the post-war grants offered to the women's auxiliary services and civil defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The force was kept on after the war and still had 33,236 members in March 1946, but plans for peacetime farming assumed it would be disbanded and replaced by more machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Keith A. H. Murray, <i>Agriculture</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1955), 59, 82, 126, 159, 205, 223, 352, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/agriculture-murray. [Open access]","H. M. D. Parker, <i>Manpower: A Study of War-time Policy and Administration</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1957), 274, 295, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/manpower-parker. [Open access]","R. S. Hudson, written answer on the Women's Land Army, House of Commons, 8 February 1945, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 407, cols. 2250–51W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1945/feb/08/womens-land-army. [Open access]","R. S. Hudson, statement on Lady Denman's resignation, House of Commons, 20 February 1945, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 408, cols. 633–34, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/feb/20/womens-land-army. [Open access]","J. Henderson Stewart and Tom Williams, debate on the Women's Land Army, House of Commons, 14 February 1945, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 408, cols. 352–62, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/feb/14/womens-land-army. [Open access]","The National Archives, “The Home Front in the Second World War,” education resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/home-front-second-world-war/. [Open access]","Tom Williams, answers on the Women's Land Army, House of Commons, 1 April 1946, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 421, cols. 821–23, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1946/apr/01/womens-land-army. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did British farms need women so quickly in 1939?","a":"Because the call-up of the Territorial Army and of young men who had registered for service threatened to take farm workers away before the harvest and the autumn ploughing, and the Land Army was the only help in sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the same time farms were asked to grow more food, since much of what Britain ate had come from abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Land Army members feel slighted compared with the women's services?","a":"Because they wore uniform and were called an army, yet most were employed by individual farmers at farm wages and were treated as civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The government argued that their conditions of recruitment and service differed from the auxiliary services, and on that ground left them out of the grants that led Lady Denman to resign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did recruitment speed up in 1942?","a":"Because in January 1942 the government announced a new manpower policy that made women liable to be directed into war work such as the women's services or industry, and many chose the Land Army instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ministry of Labour had already counted the Land Army as vital war work for women free to move away from home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Women's Land Army"},
    "ww2-826": {"questions":["Tyres from January 1942, sugar from May and gasoline nationwide from December were all rationed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Roosevelt had armed with the Emergency Price Control Act.","Abolished in May 1947 after handing its sugar, rent and price work to other departments, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had fixed ceiling prices and rationed scarce goods throughout the American war."],"abstract":"The <b>Office of Price Administration</b> was the United States federal agency that fixed maximum prices and rents and rationed scarce goods during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It replaced a weaker Office of Price Administration and Civilian Supply, set up in April 1941, when it was established by executive order on 28 August 1941, and it gained its main powers from the Emergency Price Control Act of 30 January 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Signing that act, Franklin Roosevelt said that nothing could better serve the enemy than inflation, and that effective price control would ensure that wartime sacrifices were equitably distributed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its General Maximum Price Regulation tried to hold most prices at their March 1942 level, and from April 1942 to June 1946 inflation ran at an annual rate of only 3.5 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rationing began with tyres on 5 January 1942, gasoline followed on the East Coast in May and across the nation in December, and coffee was rationed for 8 months from November 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Sugar was the first food rationed for every household, with deliveries to consumers forbidden from 28 April 1942 except against stamps from War Ration Book One, issued at registrations held from 4 to 7 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> School officials helped run the registrations, and the agency's 60,000 employees relied on some 200,000 volunteers and more than 5,600 local War Price and Rationing Boards across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From March 1943 processed foods and then meats, fats and oils were rationed by points, with the agency fixing each item's point value and families spending coupons on any mix of covered goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Even so, the War Production Board found that consumers spent almost 7 billion dollars more on goods and services in 1944 than in 1943, though higher prices meant they bought little more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Black markets for meat, tyres and gasoline boomed, but most Americans supported the system, and the agency was abolished on 29 May 1947 after its last functions passed to other departments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the Office of Price Administration [OPA],” in <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, record group 188, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/188.html. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]","Tally D. Fugate, “Rationing,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry.php?entry=RA016. [Open access]","Office of Price Administration, <i>Sugar Rationing Regulations: Rationing Order No. 3</i> (Washington, DC: Office of Price Administration, 1942), sections 1407.41, 1407.61–62, GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-PR32_4200-570be911bc5cf22ab825d973fd5a4661/html/GOVPUB-PR32_4200-570be911bc5cf22ab825d973fd5a4661.htm. [Open access]","War Production Board, <i>War Production in 1944: Report of the Chairman of the War Production Board</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 7–22, GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-P32_4800-9e92e30522a253276362395c30ab5450/html/GOVPUB-P32_4800-9e92e30522a253276362395c30ab5450.htm. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Statement on Signing the Emergency Price Control Act,” 30 January 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-signing-the-emergency-price-control-act. [Open access]","Oliver E. Rooker, “Supplying the Civilians: A Photo Essay of World War II Ration Stamps,” <i>The Chronicles of Oklahoma</i> 69, no. 4 (Winter 1991–92): 430–32, https://gateway.okhistory.org/ark:/67531/metadc2031671/m2/1/high_res_d/1991-v69-n04_a05.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States set up price controls at all?","a":"Because the inflation of the First World War had raised the cost of that war and cut the living standards of millions, and planners resolved not to repeat it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Roosevelt argued that inflation would serve the enemy and that price control would spread the sacrifices of war fairly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was sugar among the first foods rationed?","a":"Because about two-thirds of American sugar had been imported from the Philippines, Hawaii and Cuba, and the war cut off the Philippines, reduced shipments from Hawaii and curtailed imports from Cuba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Demand rose at the same moment for alcohol, synthetic rubber and explosives, so every household was registered for War Ration Book One in May 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could price control not stop inflation on its own?","a":"Because, as Roosevelt warned, controlling prices also needed adequate taxes, a broad savings programme, sound production and effective rationing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the strictest controls ended, prices leapt: inflation ran at an annual rate of 28 per cent in the 6 months after June 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Office of Price Administration"},
    "ww2-827": {"questions":["Dividing steel, copper and aluminium among claimant agencies each quarter, the Controlled Materials Plan announced in November 1942 became the core of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its direction of industry.","Planners of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> warned in February 1942 that the services wanted 102 billion dollars of war production for 1943 from an economy producing perhaps 120 billion dollars."],"abstract":"The <b>War Production Board</b> was the United States agency that directed the country's war procurement and industrial production during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Franklin Roosevelt created it by Executive Order 9024 on 16 January 1942, replacing the Supply Priorities and Allocations Board and, 8 days later, the Office of Production Management.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its chairman was to set the policies of all federal agencies on purchasing, contracting, the conversion of factories, requisitioning and plant expansion, and to issue whatever directives he judged necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first chairman was Donald Nelson, a former Sears, Roebuck executive, who was followed by Julius Krug from 1944 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The board ran twelve regional offices and about 120 field offices, and a related Smaller War Plants Corporation helped firms with fewer than 500 employees win war contracts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its central problem was balancing the needs of civilians, especially the war workers who kept the economy running, against the demands of the armed services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its planners warned in February 1942 that the services' estimates for 1943 called for 102 billion dollars of war production in an economy producing perhaps 120 billion dollars of goods and services in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In late 1942 Nelson won this feasibility dispute by persuading the military to scale back its demands, and the board introduced the Controlled Materials Plan, which shared out steel, copper and aluminium among claimant agencies each quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The board never fully controlled the services, however, and from May 1943 a new Office of War Mobilization under James Byrnes became the dominant body in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It still reported munitions worth 61.3 billion dollars in 1944, including 96,359 aircraft, and it was abolished on 3 November 1945, when its remaining duties passed to the Civilian Production Administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the War Production Board [WPB],” in <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, record group 179, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/179.html. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Executive Order 9024—Establishing the War Production Board in the Executive Office of the President and Defining Its Functions and Duties,” 16 January 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-9024-establishing-the-war-production-board-the-executive-office-the. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]","Tally D. Fugate, “War Production Board,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry.php?entry=WA021. [Open access]","War Production Board, <i>War Production in 1944: Report of the Chairman of the War Production Board</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 7–22, GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-P32_4800-9e92e30522a253276362395c30ab5450/html/GOVPUB-P32_4800-9e92e30522a253276362395c30ab5450.htm. [Open access]","Civilian Production Administration, <i>Minutes of the Planning Committee of the War Production Board, February 20, 1942, to April 1, 1943</i>, Historical Reports on War Administration, Documentary Publication No. 5 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 5–7, GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-P32_4800-ee05c03719ccce420c1c35cea189e760/html/GOVPUB-P32_4800-ee05c03719ccce420c1c35cea189e760.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the board created soon after Pearl Harbor?","a":"Because the declarations of war meant the American economy had to be turned fully to war production, and officials saw an effective administrative machine as the first step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The order creating the board cites the states of war declared by Congress on 8 and 11 December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Controlled Materials Plan rest on just three metals?","a":"Because controlling all production directly was too complex, so the board chose steel, copper and aluminium, the most important critical metals, as common denominators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its Requirements Committee estimated each quarter's supply and divided it among the claimant agencies, which curbed quarrels between the services and civilian agencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the board's planners doubt the military's targets?","a":"Because the 1943 estimates asked for war production almost as large as the whole economy's output, and studies showed that only 65 per cent of the ammunition objective could be met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nelson argued that all-out production would harm America's ability to keep producing for war after 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War Production Board"},
    "ww2-828": {"questions":["A 1943 poster of a woman flexing her arm hung in Westinghouse plants for only 2 weeks and was linked with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when it re-emerged as a feminist icon in the 1980s.","Norman Rockwell painted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a rivet gun before an American flag for a 1943 cover of <i>The Saturday Evening Post</i>, celebrating women in wartime factories."],"abstract":"<b>Rosie the Riveter</b> was the fictional factory worker who became the emblem of the American women who took industrial jobs during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name came from a song of 1942 about a patriotic woman on the assembly line, and in 1943 Norman Rockwell painted his own Rosie, holding a rivet gun before an American flag, for <i>The Saturday Evening Post</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The War Manpower Commission was charged with recruiting women into war work, and its campaigns had to change many men's and women's attitudes to women working outside the home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> About two million women worked in war industries, half of them in aircraft production, though most of the almost 19 million women employed by 1945 still held clerical, service and other traditional jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The committee policing fair employment reported that Black women's work in industry multiplied between 1942 and 1944, when thousands of them assembled communications equipment for the forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Women who entered the labour force during the war were more often in factories than other women, 37 per cent of them working as operatives or craft workers in 1944, and one working woman in six was in a war industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Women's Bureau survey in ten war production areas found that about 75 per cent of the women employed there in wartime expected to stay in the labour force after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most were nonetheless laid off as war orders fell and servicemen came home, and by 1947 about a quarter of American women worked outside the home, close to the 1940 share and well below the wartime peak of 36 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The poster most often called Rosie today, J. Howard Miller's <i>We Can Do It!</i>, was made for Westinghouse and hung inside its plants for only 2 weeks in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It reached the National Archives in 1944 among more than 1,500 posters offered by the War Production Board, and only in the early 1980s did it re-emerge as a feminist icon carrying Rosie's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Park Service, “Who Was Rosie the Riveter?,” Rosie the Riveter WWII Home Front National Historical Park, https://www.nps.gov/rori/learn/historyculture/who-was-rosie-the-riveter.htm. [Open access]","Claudia Goldin, “The Role of World War II in the Rise of Women's Work,” NBER Working Paper 3203 (Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research, 1989), https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w3203/w3203.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “Research Starters: Women in World War II,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/students-teachers/student-resources/research-starters/research-starters-women-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Women's Bureau, <i>Women Workers in Ten War Production Areas and Their Postwar Employment Plans</i>, Bulletin No. 209 (Washington, DC: US Department of Labor, 1946), https://njdigitalhighway.org/sites/default/files/lessons/ww_ii_industrial_production/pdf/women_workers_in_ten_war_production_areas.pdf. [Open access]","National Archives Foundation, “We Can Do It!,” DocsTeach, poster 179-WP-1563, Records of the War Production Board, record group 179, National Archives at College Park, https://docsteach.org/document/we-can-do-it/. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Women in the Work Force during World War II,” educator resource, https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/wwii-women.html. [Open access]","Committee on Fair Employment Practice, <i>Final Report, June 28, 1946</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-PR32_400-b5497620099b9756252b6142d521effd/html/GOVPUB-PR32_400-b5497620099b9756252b6142d521effd.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the government have to persuade women to take war jobs?","a":"Because with millions of men in the forces factories needed new workers, and both men's and women's own ideas about women working outside the home stood in the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The extensive wartime propaganda aimed at drawing women into the labour force is one reason many later credited the war with changing women's work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did most wartime women workers lose their jobs?","a":"Because orders for war materials fell sharply and millions of returning servicemen wanted work, so nearly all were laid off even though a majority wanted to stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1947 the share of women working outside the home was back near its level of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Did the war permanently change American women's work?","a":"Less than the symbol suggests: more than half the women who joined the labour force in the 1940s did so after the war, and almost half of those who entered during it had left by 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most historians, the National WWII Museum notes, doubt that wartime work by itself spurred the later women's movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rosie the Riveter"},
    "ww2-829": {"questions":["The first presidential directive on race since Reconstruction, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set up a Committee on Fair Employment Practice to investigate complaints against defence employers and unions.","Every new defence contract signed under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to bind the contractor not to discriminate against any worker, though only one Black worker in 20 had been in defence industry."],"abstract":"<b>Executive Order 8802</b> was the order signed by Franklin Roosevelt on 25 June 1941 that banned discrimination in the employment of workers in defence industries and in government because of race, creed, colour or national origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the first presidential directive on race since Reconstruction, the period that followed the American Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As rearmament created millions of jobs, African Americans who moved to northern and western cities in search of defence work were often met with violence and discrimination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1941 only one Black worker in 20 was employed in defence industry, though every tenth American was Black, and two-thirds of the Black labour force lived in southern states holding 13.5 per cent of defence contracts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A. Philip Randolph of the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters, with Walter White and others, threatened a mass march on Washington, and Roosevelt issued the order in exchange for calling it off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The order required government training programmes to be run without discrimination and every new defence contract to oblige the contractor not to discriminate against any worker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also set up a Committee on Fair Employment Practice to investigate complaints, a body moved between agencies until a stronger committee replaced it under Executive Order 9346 in May 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The committee's final report claimed that it had settled nearly 5,000 cases by negotiation in 5 years, including 40 strikes caused by racial differences, and that Black workers filed 80 per cent of its complaints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Black employment in war industry rose steadily, and by 1945 the share of Black Americans holding war jobs, about 8 per cent, approached their share of the population of about 10 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most historians have nonetheless judged the committee a weak body, especially in the South, and Congress ended it on 28 June 1946 through a war agencies appropriation act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, “Executive Order 8802: Prohibition of Discrimination in the Defense Industry (1941),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-8802. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Executive Order 8802—Reaffirming Policy of Full Participation in the Defense Program by All Persons, Regardless of Race, Creed, Color, or National Origin, and Directing Certain Action in Furtherance of Said Policy,” 25 June 1941, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-8802-reaffirming-policy-full-participation-the-defense-program-all-persons. [Open access]","Committee on Fair Employment Practice, <i>Final Report, June 28, 1946</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-PR32_400-b5497620099b9756252b6142d521effd/html/GOVPUB-PR32_400-b5497620099b9756252b6142d521effd.htm. [Open access]","Henry Louis Gates Jr., “What Was Black America's Double War?,” <i>The African Americans: Many Rivers to Cross</i>, PBS, https://www.pbs.org/wnet/african-americans-many-rivers-to-cross/history/what-was-black-americas-double-war/. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the Committee on Fair Employment Practice [FEPC],” in <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, record group 228, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/228.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Roosevelt agree to issue the order?","a":"Because Randolph and other Black leaders refused to call off their march unless he acted, and Randolph warned that he could bring ten, twenty or fifty thousand marchers to the White House lawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Facing a public relations disaster on the eve of war, the president signed the order and the march was cancelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the order speak of national unity rather than only of fairness?","a":"Because its preamble argued that the democratic way of life could be defended only with the help and support of all groups, and that barring needed workers on grounds of race harmed workers' morale and national unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The committee later echoed that wording when it described the order's purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the committee often judged to have been weak?","a":"Because the first committee had only part-time members and a small staff, and its public hearings provoked hostility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Historians have called it powerless and ineffectual, especially in the South, and Congress refused to make it permanent, ending it in June 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Executive Order 8802"},
    "ww2-830": {"questions":["Begun in the <i>Pittsburgh Courier</i> in February 1942, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread to clubs, war bond drives and beauty contests while the FBI investigated the Black press for sedition.","A study of the <i>Pittsburgh Courier</i> found that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fading by July 1942 and virtually dead by October, even as its readers refused to soft-pedal their demands."],"abstract":"The <b>Double V campaign</b> was a drive launched in 1942 by the <i>Pittsburgh Courier</i>, the largest Black newspaper in the United States, for victory over the Axis abroad and over racial discrimination at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It grew from a letter by James G. Thompson, a 26-year-old Black worker at the Cessna aircraft plant in Wichita, Kansas, which the paper printed on 31 January 1942 under the title “Should I Sacrifice to Live ‘Half-American’?”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thompson proposed that Black Americans adopt a double V, the first for victory over enemies from without and the second for victory over enemies from within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Courier</i> printed Double V drawings on 7 February 1942, launched the campaign on its front page a week later, and claimed 200,000 Double V members by mid-July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The slogan spread through Black community life in Double V clubs, war bond drives, baseball games, gardens and beauty contests, and other Black newspapers took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Behind it lay grievances the Black press had long aired, for the Marines, Coast Guard and air corps would not accept Black men and the Navy took them only as messmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed the success of A. Philip Randolph's threatened march on Washington, which had won Executive Order 8802 against discrimination in defence industries in June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The FBI investigated Black newspapers in 1942 and recommended indicting some editors for sedition, but Roosevelt refused, and a <i>Courier</i> journalist later said the pressure only sold more papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of the <i>Courier</i>'s pages found the campaign declining by July 1942 and virtually dead by October, perhaps because Black officers and war workers were now making gains the paper wanted to protect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a poll published on 24 October 1942, 88.7 per cent of its readers rejected the idea that Black Americans should soft-pedal their demands, and the insignia stayed in the paper until September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Pat Washburn, “The <i>Pittsburgh Courier</i>'s Double V Campaign in 1942,” paper presented to the History Division, Association for Education in Journalism, East Lansing, MI, August 1981, ERIC ED205956, https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED205956.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Should I Sacrifice to Live ‘Half-American’?,” Experiencing History: Holocaust Sources in Context, https://perspectives.ushmm.org/item/should-i-sacrifice-to-live-half-american. [Open access]","Euell A. Dixon, “The Double V Campaign (1942–1945),” BlackPast.org, 2 July 2020, https://www.blackpast.org/african-american-history/events-african-american-history/the-double-v-campaign-1942-1945/. [Open access]","Henry Louis Gates Jr., “What Was Black America's Double War?,” <i>The African Americans: Many Rivers to Cross</i>, PBS, https://www.pbs.org/wnet/african-americans-many-rivers-to-cross/history/what-was-black-americas-double-war/. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Executive Order 8802: Prohibition of Discrimination in the Defense Industry (1941),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-8802. [Open access]","Christopher J. Tassava, “The American Economy during World War II,” EH.Net Encyclopedia, ed. Robert Whaples, 10 February 2008, https://eh.net/encyclopedia/the-american-economy-during-world-war-ii/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Courier stress its patriotism so heavily?","a":"Because it wanted to convince wary white readers that it was not urging Black Americans to be unpatriotic, and it restated the slogan as a two-pronged fight for freedom by people who were Americans too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also sent an American flag with every subscription and urged readers to buy war bonds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the campaign fade within a year?","a":"Because, one study suggests, wartime prosperity made middle-class Black Americans want to protect new gains, as Black men were commissioned for the first time and Black workers were hired in large numbers at defence plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1945 about 8 per cent of war jobs were held by Black workers, close to their share of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Falling circulation was not the cause, for the paper's circulation rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did federal officials watch the campaign closely?","a":"Because some white Americans saw it as a danger to the war effort, and FBI director J. Edgar Hoover branded support for it seditious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bureau recommended indicting Black editors, but Roosevelt refused, and about 1.2 million Black men and women served in uniform during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Double V campaign"},
    "ww2-811": {"questions":["Praised after the Greek and Turkish exchanges as a humane cure for quarrels over minorities, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was written into treaties by almost every state east of the Rhine by 1946.","A confidential protocol of September 1939 arranged a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between the Reich and the Soviet Union, sending people of German descent west and Ukrainians and Belarusians east."],"abstract":"A <b>population transfer</b> is the organised removal of a whole population, usually an ethnic or national minority, from one territory to another, whether by agreement between states or by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the first half of the 20th century it was widely seen as a constructive way to prevent conflict and build stable nation-states, and the Greek and Turkish exchanges of the 1920s were held up as a success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 1913 and 1946 almost every state in continental Europe east of the Rhine signed at least one transfer agreement, democracies as well as dictatorships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The states rarely asked the people concerned, and historians of ethnic cleansing stress that the humane image hid lost homelands, destroyed communities and violence that often ran out of control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg charter of 1945 listed the deportation of civilians both as a war crime and as a crime against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> A confidential protocol to the German-Soviet treaty of 28 September 1939 let people of German descent in Soviet-held lands leave for Germany, with Ukrainians and Belarusians moving the other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under such agreements about 187,000 ethnic Germans were brought from Bukovina and Bessarabia in late 1940, sorted by race commissions and mostly settled in annexed Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To make room, the Germans drove hundreds of thousands of Poles from the annexed lands into the General Government and settled more than 500,000 ethnic Germans in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Soviet Union deported whole peoples it accused of collaboration, among them about 1.2 million Soviet Germans from 1941 and nearly 478,000 Chechens and Ingush in 1944, of whom 30 to 50 per cent died on different estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Potsdam in 1945 Britain, the United States and the Soviet Union accepted that the Germans left in Poland, Czechoslovakia and Hungary would have to be transferred, asking only that it be orderly and humane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thomas Kühne, review of <i>Making Minorities History: Population Transfer in Twentieth-Century Europe</i>, by Matthew Frank, H-Nationalism, H-Net Reviews, April 2018, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=52226. [Open access]","“German-Soviet Boundary and Friendship Treaty: Confidential Protocol,” 28 September 1939, in <i>Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939–1941</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/conproto.asp. [Open access]","Dorlis Blume, “Umsiedlung aus der Bukowina,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 11 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/aussenpolitik/umsiedlung-aus-der-bukowina. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","Aurélie Campana, “The Soviet Massive Deportations: A Chronology,” <i>Mass Violence and Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 5 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-soviet-massive-deportations-a-chronology/. [Open access]","“Protocol of the Proceedings of the Berlin Conference,” 1 August 1945, in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did governments think moving whole peoples was a good idea?","a":"Because ethnic minorities were seen as a threat to domestic and international peace, and transferring them was presented as a way to protect them and secure nation-states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The apparent success of the Greek and Turkish exchanges gave the idea prestige that lasted to the end of the 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Nazi Germany bring ethnic Germans into annexed Poland?","a":"Because the regime meant to Germanise the annexed lands, so hundreds of thousands of Poles were driven out into the General Government and German settlers from Bukovina, Bessarabia and elsewhere were moved in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Settlers were sorted by race commissions, and those judged most valuable were sent east to the annexed territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did whole Soviet peoples pay for the acts of a few?","a":"Because the Soviet government treated entire nationalities as collectively guilty of collaboration, from the Volga Germans in 1941 to the Chechens, Ingush, Balkars and Crimean Tatars in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Women, children and the elderly made up most of several deported groups, and some peoples, such as the Meskhetian Turks, lived where German troops had never arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Population transfer"},
    "ww2-812": {"questions":["A United States tribunal ruled in 1948 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a besieged city was not a crime, wishing the law were otherwise but bound to apply it as it stood.","Banned as a method of warfare only in 1977, the deliberate <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made a war crime by the Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court in 1998."],"abstract":"The <b>starvation of civilians</b> as a method of warfare means deliberately depriving a population of food by siege, blockade, requisition or the destruction of crops and water supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the Second World War it was not a crime, and an American military tribunal at Nuremberg held in 1948 that a besieging commander might try to reduce a city by starvation and drive back civilians who tried to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that ground the tribunal acquitted Field Marshal Wilhelm von Leeb of the charge over Leningrad, saying it had to administer the law as it found it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Starvation of civilians became a prohibited method of warfare only on 8 June 1977, when the first Additional Protocol to the Geneva Conventions also banned destroying food supplies to deny them to a population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court, adopted in 1998, then listed intentionally starving civilians, including wilfully impeding relief supplies, as a war crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Germany built hunger into its war on the Soviet Union, and in June 1941 Alfred Rosenberg declared that there was no reason to feed the Russian people from the surplus of the south, calling this a harsh necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once the land was occupied, the Nuremberg tribunal found, food was seized on a large scale with complete disregard of the needs of the inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet prisoners of war were the first victims of that policy, often given at most 700 calories a day, and more than three million of them died in German custody, mostly of malnutrition and exposure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler called off the storming of Leningrad and ordered the city besieged so as to starve its people, and Germany's national history museum puts the deaths there by the end of January 1944 at 800,000 to a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The harm outlasted the war, since babies born in the besieged city in 1942 weighed 500 to 600 grams (1.1 to 1.3 pounds) less than normal and boys starved there around puberty later died more often of heart disease and stroke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nuernberg Military Tribunals, <i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11, “The High Command Case” (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1950), 562–63, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Treatment of Soviet POWs: Starvation, Disease, and Shootings, June 1941–January 1942,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-treatment-of-soviet-pows-starvation-disease-and-shootings-june-1941january-1942. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]","Pär Sparén et al., “Long Term Mortality after Severe Starvation during the Siege of Leningrad: Prospective Cohort Study,” <i>BMJ</i> 328, no. 7430 (2004): 11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC313894/. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Protocols Additional to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949</i> (Geneva: ICRC), Protocol I, art. 54, https://www.icrc.org/en/doc/assets/files/other/icrc_002_0321.pdf. [Open access]","International Criminal Court, <i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i> (The Hague: International Criminal Court, 2021), art. 8(2)(b)(xxv), https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was von Leeb acquitted over the starvation of Leningrad?","a":"Because the law of war as the tribunal found it allowed a besieging commander to cut a city off from every source of food and even to drive back civilians who tried to escape. The judges said plainly that they might wish the law were otherwise, but they held that they had to apply it as it stood, so no criminality attached to that charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German planners accept that Soviet civilians would go hungry?","a":"Because the produce of southern Russia and the Caucasus was meant to feed Germany, and Rosenberg said openly that Germany had no obligation to feed the Russian people from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The occupiers then confiscated food with no regard for the inhabitants, and at Leningrad Hitler chose a siege meant to starve the city rather than a costly assault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What changed in the law after the war?","a":"The protocol of 1977 to the Geneva Conventions stated for the first time that starving civilians as a method of warfare is prohibited, and it banned destroying crops, livestock and water supplies to deny them to a population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Rome Statute later made intentionally starving civilians, including blocking relief, a war crime that the International Criminal Court can try.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Neither rule existed when Leningrad was besieged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Starvation (crime)"},
    "ww2-813": {"questions":["Left out of the Nuremberg charter but named in the law for later trials in occupied Germany, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now appears in the Rome Statute as rape, sexual slavery and enforced prostitution.","Estimates of how many women Japan's forces held in comfort stations, a central part of the record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Asia, range among researchers from about 20,000 to 410,000."],"abstract":"<b>Wartime sexual violence</b> is rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution and other sexual abuse committed in the course of armed conflict, crimes now named in the statute of the International Criminal Court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its record from the Second World War is uneven, because the warring states used the image of the abused woman in propaganda to incite hatred of the enemy and then largely dropped the subject once peace came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Nuremberg charter of 1945 did not name rape among war crimes or crimes against humanity, and women were not recognised as victims at Nuremberg or in the Soviet public trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Control Council Law No. 10, the basis for later trials in occupied Germany, did list rape as a crime against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Rome Statute of 1998 names rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy and enforced sterilisation as crimes against humanity when part of a widespread or systematic attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The Tokyo tribunal found that approximately 20,000 cases of rape occurred in Nanking during the first month of the Japanese occupation, and that many women were killed afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan's forces also set up comfort stations across Asia, and in 1993 the Japanese government acknowledged that the military had been directly or indirectly involved and that many women were recruited against their will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No survey has established how many women were held in them, and the researchers' estimates collected by the Asian Women's Fund range from about 20,000 to 410,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the occupied Soviet lands, Soviet investigators of German crimes recorded rape and forced work in brothels, though the line between coercion and bargains for survival was often hard to draw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As the war in Europe ended in 1945, rape and robbery were everyday events in Germany's eastern regions, in the account of Germany's national history museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]","Allied Control Council, “Control Council Law No. 10: Punishment of Persons Guilty of War Crimes, Crimes against Peace and against Humanity,” 20 December 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imt10.asp. [Open access]","Yohei Kono, “Statement by Chief Cabinet Secretary Yohei Kono on the Result of the Study on the Issue of ‘Comfort Women’,” 4 August 1993, reproduced by the Asian Women's Fund, https://www.awf.or.jp/e6/statement-02.html. [Open access]","Asian Women's Fund, “Number of Comfort Stations and Comfort Women,” <i>Digital Museum: The Comfort Women Issue and the Asian Women's Fund</i>, https://www.awf.or.jp/e1/facts-07.html. [Open access]","Ksenia Sak, “Women’s Daily Life in the Occupation: Sexual Violence in Documents and Soviet Propaganda,” <i>Quaestio Rossica</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 601–16, https://doi.org/10.15826/qr.2024.2.898. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Das Kriegsende 1945,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/kriegsende-1945. [in German] [Open access]","International Criminal Court, <i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i> (The Hague: International Criminal Court, 2021), arts. 7–8, https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the wartime record of sexual violence so patchy?","a":"Because the subject served the warring states mainly as propaganda against the enemy, and once the war ended it lost that use and was left out of collective memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Nuremberg charter did not name rape, and women were not treated as victims there or in the Soviet trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the number of comfort women still unknown?","a":"Because no documents give comprehensive figures, so every total is an estimate built from the number of soldiers overseas, an assumed ratio of soldiers to women and a rate of replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Different assumptions give answers from about 20,000 to 410,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What did Japan's government admit in 1993?","a":"That comfort stations were run in wide areas for long periods at the request of the military, which was directly or indirectly involved in setting them up and moving the women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It said that many women, a large part of them from Korea, were recruited against their will, and it offered apologies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Wartime sexual violence"},
    "ww2-814": {"questions":["Index cards made in Paris by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded each work of art it seized, numbering it by the collection it came from, so that 'R' marked a Rothschild picture.","The agency's own reports said the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had emptied 69,619 Jewish homes in the West of their furniture, 38,000 of them in Paris, filling almost 27,000 railway cars."],"abstract":"The <b>Einsatzstab Reichsleiter Rosenberg</b>, the Reichsleiter Rosenberg Taskforce, was the Nazi Party agency under Alfred Rosenberg that seized libraries, archives, works of art and household goods across occupied Europe and shipped them to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of Hitler's decree of 29 January 1940 ordering Rosenberg to prepare the library of the 'Hohe Schule', a Nazi centre of ideological research to be founded after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What began as a research library became a programme of seizure, and a decree of 1 March 1942 let Rosenberg search libraries, archives, lodges and cultural institutions and take cultural treasures owned by Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its staff worked in France, Belgium, the Netherlands, Luxembourg, Norway and Denmark in the west, and in the occupied Soviet lands, Greece, Hungary and Yugoslavia in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of what it took from France was stored in Bavarian castles such as Neuschwanstein, where American officers found it in 1945 beside the agency's own carefully kept files.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In France its staff traced Jewish collections through police address lists, Jewish handbooks and the order books of shipping firms, until the seizures covered the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each seized work was entered on an index card made in Paris and numbered by the collection it came from, with 'R' for Rothschild, and the cards survive in the United States National Archives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its pictorial art staff reported 29 large shipments to the Reich between March 1941 and July 1944, 137 freight cars carrying 4,174 cases of art works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the furniture programme begun in December 1941, 69,619 Jewish homes in the West were plundered, 38,000 of them in Paris, and moving the goods took 26,984 railway cars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal rejected the claim that the seizures were meant to protect the art, found Rosenberg guilty on all four counts, and he was hanged on 16 October 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Office of United States Chief of Counsel for Prosecution of Axis Criminality, <i>Nazi Conspiracy and Aggression</i>, vol. 1, chap. 14, “The Plunder of Art Treasures” (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/chap_14.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Rosenberg,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judrosen.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Alfred Rosenberg,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/alfred-rosenberg. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Einsatzstab Reichsleiter Rosenberg (ERR) Records,” <i>Art Provenance and Claims Records: Descriptive List of Key Records</i> (College Park, MD: NARA, 2008), https://www.archives.gov/research/holocaust/art/key-series-descriptions/key-series-descriptions-13.html. [Open access]","American Commission for the Protection and Salvage of Artistic and Historic Monuments in War Areas, <i>Report of the American Commission for the Protection and Salvage of Artistic and Historic Monuments in War Areas</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 136–38, https://archive.org/details/report-compressed. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How did a library project turn into the plunder of art?","a":"Hitler's first order of January 1940 asked Rosenberg only to prepare research and a library for a Nazi institute planned for after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The project soon grew into the seizure of cultural treasures, and the decree of March 1942 explicitly allowed Rosenberg to take treasures owned by Jews or whose owners could not be clearly established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Göring also claimed works brought to Paris for his own collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg judges reject the claim that the art was being protected?","a":"Because the German orders themselves spoke of confiscation for the benefit of the Reich, and the director of the Dresden picture gallery wrote of enriching German collections from Polish ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribunal treated the seizures as plunder of public and private property, a war crime under its charter, and convicted Rosenberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the agency's own index cards still matter?","a":"Because each card describes a single seized work with its measurements and an inventory number naming the collection it was taken from, and some record its earlier ownership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States National Archives keeps the card file among the records it lists for tracing the provenance of art and for claims by owners and heirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reichsleiter Rosenberg Taskforce"},
    "ww2-815": {"questions":["On 3 May 1942 German bombers gutted the medieval centre of Exeter and damaged its cathedral in the most devastating raid of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against England's historic cities.","The Luftwaffe lost some forty aircraft in the fourteen main operations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which aimed about 1,500 tons of bombs at lightly defended towns of historic and artistic interest."],"abstract":"The <b>Baedeker Blitz</b> was a series of German air raids on English cities in the spring of 1942, aimed at places chosen largely for their beauty and history and their lack of strong defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler ordered it after Bomber Command set fire to the old Hanseatic city of Lübeck on 28 March 1942, and on 14 April he sanctioned raids of a retaliatory nature where they would most affect civilian life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both sides soon called them the Baedeker raids, and they began on the night of 23 April with an attack on Exeter that largely missed the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bath, Norwich and York followed within days, and at York clusters of incendiary bombs fell north and south of the Minster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 May the bombers returned to Exeter, gutting much of its medieval centre and seriously damaging the cathedral, and Canterbury's old quarter burned at the end of the month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The raids were one part of a far wider destruction of cultural heritage, and the Nuremberg charter made the wanton destruction of cities, towns or villages without military necessity a war crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that museums, palaces and libraries in the occupied Soviet lands were systematically looted, with rare books and art taken from Peterhof, Tsarskoye Selo and Pavlovsk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Poland the Germans set out to destroy the nation's culture, toppling monuments such as the Grunwald memorial in Kraków, and after the Warsaw Uprising Himmler ordered the city destroyed, leaving 90 per cent of its museums, libraries, churches and monuments in ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Allied bombing and shelling destroyed the abbey of Montecassino in February 1944, although its archives and the Naples art stored there had already been taken to the Vatican.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the war the Hague Convention of 14 May 1954 set rules for protecting cultural property in armed conflict, and the Geneva protocol of 1977 forbade acts of hostility against historic monuments, works of art and places of worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty's Stationery Office, 1957), chap. 20, 304–7, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-20.html. [Open access]","Martin Borkowski-Saruhan, “Der Warschauer Aufstand 1944,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 31 July 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/warschauer-aufstand-1944. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","American Commission for the Protection and Salvage of Artistic and Historic Monuments in War Areas, <i>Report of the American Commission for the Protection and Salvage of Artistic and Historic Monuments in War Areas</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 66–68, https://archive.org/details/report-compressed. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Protocols Additional to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949</i> (Geneva: ICRC), Protocol I, art. 53, https://www.icrc.org/en/doc/assets/files/other/icrc_002_0321.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hitler order raids on historic English towns?","a":"Because the burning of Lübeck by Bomber Command caused much resentment in Germany and deeply impressed him, and he abandoned his policy of not provoking heavier British attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His order of 14 April called frankly for terror attacks of a retaliatory nature on targets where they would most affect civilian life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the raids on Bath and Exeter succeed when others failed?","a":"Because the targets were chosen for being easy: Bath had no balloon barrage or heavy guns and lay in a hollow that worsened blast, and Exeter's medieval core of narrow streets was thought more liable to fire than almost anywhere in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later raids on Norwich, Hull, Poole and Grimsby were misled by decoy fires or bad weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the destruction of Montecassino judged a great loss?","a":"Because the abbey had been a seat of religious and educational life for more than 1,400 years, and its church, library and picture gallery were reduced to rubble in the bombardments of February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American commission noted that the loss would have been far greater had the archives and the art stored there not been moved to the Vatican beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Baedeker Blitz"},
    "ww2-816": {"questions":["The Allies found between 10 and 12 million displaced persons in liberated Europe in 1945, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped to send millions of them home within months.","Funded close to half by the United States and led first by Herbert Lehman, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed its work to the International Refugee Organization in 1947 when its money ran out."],"abstract":"The <b>United Nations Relief and Rehabilitation Administration</b>, or UNRRA, was created at a conference of 44 nations at the White House on 9 November 1943 to bring relief to liberated countries and to repatriate and care for refugees under Allied control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States paid close to half its budget, and three Americans led it in turn, the first being Herbert Lehman, a former governor of New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Europe it worked under the authority of the Allied supreme headquarters, and in 1945 it helped send millions of refugees home and ran hundreds of camps for displaced persons in Germany, Italy and Austria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It set up a Central Tracing Bureau to help survivors find their relatives and oversaw the work of 23 voluntary welfare agencies, among them the Joint Distribution Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its funds ran out, and in 1947 the International Refugee Organization took over its tasks, inheriting the care of 643,000 displaced persons in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The size of its task came from the war's uprooting of civilians, as at least 1.5 million Polish citizens alone were deported to German territory for forced labour between 1939 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Soviet Union moved peoples of its own, deporting about 1.2 million Soviet Germans to Siberia and Central Asia between August 1941 and June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the Warsaw Uprising of 1944 the Germans sent about 60,000 of the city's people to concentration camps and about 90,000 to forced labour in the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When the Allies liberated Germany and the lands it had occupied, they found between 10 and 12 million displaced persons, of whom six million had gone home by September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 12 million more Germans and German-speakers were forced from their homes in eastern and central Europe at and after the war's end, some 7.9 million of them taken in by the western zones of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “United Nations Relief and Rehabilitation Administration,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/united-nations-relief-and-rehabilitation-administration. [Open access]","Arolsen Archives, “Background Information on Displaced Persons Documents,” <i>e-Guide</i>, https://eguide.arolsen-archives.org/en/additional-resources/background-information-on-displaced-persons-documents/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Displaced Persons,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/displaced-persons. [Open access]","Walter Ziegler, “Refugees and Expellees,” <i>Historisches Lexikon Bayerns</i>, 6 September 2011, English version 9 March 2020, https://www.historisches-lexikon-bayerns.de/Lexikon/EN:Refugees_and_Expellees. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]","Aurélie Campana, “The Soviet Massive Deportations: A Chronology,” <i>Mass Violence and Résistance</i>, Sciences Po, 5 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-soviet-massive-deportations-a-chronology/. [Open access]","Martin Borkowski-Saruhan, “Der Warschauer Aufstand 1944,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 31 July 2025, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/warschauer-aufstand-1944. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new international agency needed in 1943?","a":"Because the Allies expected to take control of huge numbers of refugees and of countries ruined by occupation, and UNRRA was set up both to aid people fleeing Axis aggression and to give economic help to liberated nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Germany was liberated the Allies found between 10 and 12 million people who were no longer in their countries of origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did millions of displaced persons go home so quickly in 1945?","a":"Because many returned on their own as soon as they were free, and the Allies and UNRRA organised repatriation on a large scale, so that six million had gone home by September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Soviet Union insisted that its citizens be repatriated quickly and without exception, since it needed workers after its wartime losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some displaced persons stay in the camps for years?","a":"Because many could not or would not go back, and most Jewish survivors were unable or unwilling to return to eastern Europe, where antisemitism persisted and their communities had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than 250,000 Jewish displaced persons lived in camps and urban centres in Germany, Austria and Italy between 1945 and 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"United Nations Relief and Rehabilitation Administration"},
    "ww2-817": {"questions":["The United States Strategic Bombing Survey found that 9 months of air attack on Japan caused about 806,000 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, including some 330,000 deaths, probably more than Japan's combat casualties.","Poland lost about 5.6 million people in the war against some 240,000 military dead, so that its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outnumbered its fallen soldiers by more than twenty to one."],"abstract":"<b>Civilian casualties</b> are the people killed or wounded in war who do not belong to the armed forces, and the Geneva protocol of 1977 laid down that such civilians must never be the object of attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the Second World War civilians died in greater numbers than soldiers, and the National WWII Museum estimates about 45 million civilian deaths against 15 million battle deaths, while warning that estimates vary widely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burden fell hardest on occupied and fought-over lands, so that on the same estimates Poland lost about 5.6 million people against 240,000 military dead, and China perhaps 20 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The largest deliberate killing was the Holocaust, in which six million Jews were murdered, while about 1.8 million non-Jewish Poles and more than 310,000 Serb civilians in Croatia were also killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many totals rest on demography rather than counts of bodies, and Soviet demographers put the country's wartime excess deaths, soldiers and civilians together, at 26.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Hunger killed on a vast scale, and in besieged Leningrad alone between 800,000 and a million inhabitants died by January 1944, on the figure of Germany's national history museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Massacre was another cause, and the Tokyo tribunal found that over 200,000 civilians and prisoners were murdered in and around Nanking in the first 6 weeks of the Japanese occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Bombing killed fewer but reached deep into every combatant's homeland, and the United States Strategic Bombing Survey put German deaths from air attack at a minimum of 305,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same survey reckoned that 9 months of air attack on Japan, the atomic bombs included, caused about 806,000 civilian casualties, about 330,000 of them deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It judged that these casualties probably exceeded Japan's combat casualties for the whole war, which the Japanese estimated at about 780,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["National WWII Museum, “Research Starters: Worldwide Deaths in World War II,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/students-teachers/student-resources/research-starters/research-starters-worldwide-deaths-world-war. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Documenting Numbers of Victims of the Holocaust and Nazi Persecution,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/documenting-numbers-of-victims-of-the-holocaust-and-nazi-persecution. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Die Belagerung von Leningrad,” <i>LeMO</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 19 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/belagerung-von-leningrad. [in German] [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Counting Soviet Deaths in the Great Patriotic War: Comment,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 55, no. 6 (2003): 939–44, postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/eas03a_postprint.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Protocols Additional to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949</i> (Geneva: ICRC), Protocol I, arts. 50–51, https://www.icrc.org/en/doc/assets/files/other/icrc_002_0321.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did more civilians than soldiers die in this war?","a":"Because so much of the killing happened away from the battlefield, in occupied countries and besieged cities: six million Jews were murdered in the Holocaust, and hundreds of thousands starved in Leningrad alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bombing also reached homes far behind the fronts, killing at least 305,000 people in Germany and about 330,000 in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the totals for civilian deaths so uncertain?","a":"Because there were often no records of individual deaths, so totals are estimated from censuses and demographic data, and the museum's own table warns that civilian deaths in China alone might exceed 50 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet war deaths, for example, are calculated as excess deaths above what peacetime mortality would have produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombing survey revise Germany's official figure upward?","a":"Because when it checked the German official statistics of air raid casualties against the records of individual cities that had been attacked, it found them too low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its revised estimate of 305,000 killed and 780,000 wounded was itself described as a minimum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Civilian casualty"},
    "ww2-818": {"questions":["Stalin announced about seven million Soviet war dead in 1946, but an audit of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> commissioned under Gorbachev put the true figure at 26 to 27 million.","Where no record of individual deaths survives, war totals such as the Soviet Union's 26.6 million are calculated as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> above the deaths normal peacetime conditions would have produced."],"abstract":"<b>Excess mortality</b> is the number of deaths in a period beyond those that would have been expected under normal conditions, and it is the usual way of estimating war deaths where individual deaths were never recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method subtracts the deaths that peacetime mortality would have caused from the total fall in a population, assuming that no one killed by the war would have died anyway in the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes it deliberately conservative, a lower limit on the dead, although the possible error for the Soviet case has been calculated at about 1.9 million rather than many times that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other totals rest on documents, and the six million Jews murdered in the Holocaust are calculated from German records together with prewar and postwar demographic data, since no single document accounts for every death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Totals for the whole war remain estimates that vary widely, and one museum's table gives China 20 million dead while noting that its civilian deaths alone might exceed 50 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Soviet dead show how far a figure could be bent, for in March 1946 Stalin put the country's losses at about seven million, a number conveniently just below Germany's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Khrushchev later raised it to 20 million, another round number, and only under Gorbachev was the national statistical agency asked for a full audit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That audit put the dead at 26 to 27 million, about one in eight of the prewar population, a figure broadly accepted ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2017 a Russian legislator endorsed a new estimate of 42 million at a parliamentary hearing, but critics have found that it has no sound demographic basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One Russian demographer argues that such inflated totals ignore censuses and the limit set by the number of men of military age, while holding that the official military count understated deaths in German captivity at least twofold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Harrison, “Counting Soviet Deaths in the Great Patriotic War: Comment,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 55, no. 6 (2003): 939–44, postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/eas03a_postprint.pdf. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Counting the Soviet Union’s War Dead: Still 26–27 Million,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 71, no. 6 (2019): 1036–47, accepted manuscript, https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/98747/. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, “Interview to ‘Pravda’ Correspondent Concerning Mr. Winston Churchill’s Speech at Fulton,” March 1946, in <i>J. V. Stalin on Post-War International Relations</i> (London: Soviet News, 1947), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1946/03/x01.htm. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Documenting Numbers of Victims of the Holocaust and Nazi Persecution,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/documenting-numbers-of-victims-of-the-holocaust-and-nazi-persecution. [Open access]","National WWII Museum, “Research Starters: Worldwide Deaths in World War II,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/students-teachers/student-resources/research-starters/research-starters-worldwide-deaths-world-war. [Open access]","Sergei Maksudov, “Otsenka voennykh poter’: poiski istiny, nevezhestvo ili predvziatost’?” <i>Demograficheskoe obozrenie</i> 4, no. 3 (2017): 141–52, https://demreview.hse.ru/article/download/7320/8192. [in Russian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is excess mortality only a lower limit on war deaths?","a":"Because it counts only deaths above what peacetime would have brought, so anyone killed by the war who would in any case have died in those years is not counted as a war death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the Soviet Union the possible shortfall from this has been calculated at roughly 1.9 million, not the 16 million total of normal deaths once suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin announce such a low figure?","a":"Because he did not wish to disclose the real scale of Soviet losses, and seven million was a round number conveniently just below Germany's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so, he used it to argue that Soviet losses were several times those of Britain and the United States together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the figure of 42 million rejected?","a":"Because it set aside the censuses and the records of births and deaths in favour of a single planning estimate of the population found in an archive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When it is placed beside the Soviet demographic accounts it is found to be without foundation, and the audited figure of 26 to 27 million stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Excess mortality"},
    "ww2-819": {"questions":["The Nuremberg tribunal declined to treat Nazi persecution inside Germany before 1939 as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it found no proven link between those acts and the war.","Acquitted of conspiracy, the newspaper publisher Julius Streicher was nonetheless convicted at Nuremberg of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for inciting the murder of Jews while killings in the East went on."],"abstract":"<b>Crimes against humanity</b> are grave acts such as murder, extermination, enslavement, deportation and persecution committed against a civilian population, punishable under international law whether or not the law of the country where they happen permits them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The category was first written into law in the charter of the International Military Tribunal, which France, the Soviet Union, Britain and the United States signed on 8 August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its article 6 listed inhumane acts against any civilian population before or during the war, and persecutions on political, racial or religious grounds connected with another crime the tribunal could try.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Control Council Law No. 10 of 20 December 1945, the basis of later trials in occupied Germany, added imprisonment, torture and rape to the list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Rome Statute of 1998 now defines them as such acts committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack on a civilian population, a definition that does not mention war at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> At Nuremberg the tribunal accepted that political opponents had been murdered and Jews persecuted inside Germany before the war, often in organised and systematic ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet it held that acts before the war counted as crimes against humanity only if they were connected with aggression or war crimes, and it found that link unproven for the years before 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the outbreak of war, by contrast, it ruled that the inhumane acts charged were all committed in connection with aggressive war and so were crimes against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under that heading it judged the deportation of whole populations to Germany for slave labour and the extermination of the Jews of occupied Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Julius Streicher, publisher of the antisemitic weekly <i>Der Stürmer</i>, was acquitted of conspiracy and convicted on this count alone, for inciting murder while Jews in the East were being killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Law Relating to War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawre.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: Streicher,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judstrei.asp. [Open access]","Allied Control Council, “Control Council Law No. 10: Punishment of Persons Guilty of War Crimes, Crimes against Peace and against Humanity,” 20 December 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imt10.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/international-military-tribunal-at-nuremberg. [Open access]","International Criminal Court, <i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i> (The Hague: International Criminal Court, 2021), art. 7, https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new crime needed beside war crimes?","a":"Because the war crimes in the Nuremberg charter were violations of the laws or customs of war, such as the ill-treatment of civilians in occupied territory or of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crimes against humanity reached persecutions and inhumane acts against any civilian population, and the charter made them punishable whether or not they broke the domestic law of the country where they were committed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tribunal leave the crimes before 1939 unpunished under this heading?","a":"Because the charter tied crimes against humanity to the other crimes the tribunal could try, aggression and war crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judges called the pre-war persecution revolting and horrible, but held that its connection with those crimes had not been satisfactorily proved, so they could not declare it a crime against humanity in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How has the definition changed since 1945?","a":"The law for the later trials in occupied Germany added imprisonment, torture and rape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Rome Statute keeps murder, extermination, enslavement, deportation and persecution, adds sexual violence, enforced disappearance and apartheid, and requires a widespread or systematic attack on civilians, with no link to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Crimes against humanity"},
    "ww2-820": {"questions":["The Nuremberg indictment of 1945 accused the defendants of deliberate and systematic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against Jews, Poles and Gypsies, though the word did not yet name a crime in law.","Adopted by the United Nations on 9 December 1948, the convention against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> requires proof of intent to destroy a national, ethnic, racial or religious group in whole or in part."],"abstract":"<b>Genocide</b> is the crime of committing acts such as killing or causing serious harm with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnic, racial or religious group as such.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word was coined by the Polish-Jewish lawyer Raphael Lemkin in his 1944 book <i>Axis Rule in Occupied Europe</i>, from the Greek 'genos', meaning race or tribe, and the Latin ending for killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lemkin meant by it not only mass killing but a coordinated plan of actions aimed at the essential foundations of a group's life, striking individuals because they belonged to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations adopted the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide on 9 December 1948, and the Rome Statute of 1998 repeats its definition word for word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Besides killing, the convention lists inflicting conditions of life calculated to destroy a group, preventing births and forcibly transferring its children, and courts have set a high bar for proving the intent to destroy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The word entered a legal document in the Nuremberg indictment of 1945, which charged deliberate and systematic genocide against Jews, Poles, Gypsies and others in occupied territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was not yet a crime in law, however, and the verdict covered such acts only as crimes committed in connection with an aggressive war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal found that plans to exterminate the Jews of all Europe were made in the summer of 1941, and that the Einsatzgruppen were given the task of killing the Jews behind the German armies in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In all, Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators murdered six million Jews, the genocide now called the Holocaust, and at least 250,000 and possibly 500,000 Roma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In occupied Poland the Germans also set out to destroy the Polish nation and its culture, shooting thousands of its educated class and taking at least 20,000 children for Germanisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Coining a Word and Championing a Cause: The Story of Raphael Lemkin,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/coining-a-word-and-championing-a-cause-the-story-of-raphael-lemkin. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Introduction to the Definition of Genocide,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/introduction-to-the-definition-of-genocide. [Open access]","United Nations, “Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide,” 9 December 1948, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/genocide.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Indictment: Count Three, War Crimes,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 1 (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/count3.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","International Criminal Court, <i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i> (The Hague: International Criminal Court, 2021), art. 6, https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Documenting Numbers of Victims of the Holocaust and Nazi Persecution,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/documenting-numbers-of-victims-of-the-holocaust-and-nazi-persecution. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Polish Victims,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/polish-victims. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Lemkin think a new word was needed?","a":"Because he had been troubled since his student days by the destruction of the Armenians in the First World War and believed international law should protect groups from being destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His efforts in the 1930s failed, and his book of 1944 documented Nazi rule in occupied Europe and named the old practice in its modern form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the legal definition depend so much on intent?","a":"Because the convention defines genocide as certain acts committed with intent to destroy a group as such, not by the number killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Courts infer that intent from the scale of the killing, its systematic targeting of one group and the repetition of destructive acts, and they require it to be the only reasonable inference from the facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was no one convicted of genocide at Nuremberg?","a":"Because genocide was not yet a crime in law in 1945, even though Lemkin had the word placed in the indictment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The judges dealt with the murder of the Jews as war crimes and crimes against humanity, which they could try only in connection with the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Genocide"},
    "ww2-861": {"questions":["A threefold rise in German arms output under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> showed, the Strategic Bombing Survey argued, how undermobilised industry had been before 1942 rather than how efficient dictatorship was.","Responsible from August 1944 even for aircraft, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled its factories with foreign workers whom Fritz Sauckel conscripted by force to meet Speer's demands."],"abstract":"The <b>Reich Ministry of Armaments and War Production</b> was the German ministry that, under Albert Speer, directed most of Germany's arms production in the second half of the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It began as Fritz Todt's Reich Ministry for Armaments and Munitions, which Speer took over on 15 February 1942 after Todt was killed in an air crash, and took its final name on 2 September 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that he found little organisation in place, because Hitler had ranked army armament below the air force in autumn 1941 and Todt began building up his staff only in January 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its authority grew from army weapons to naval armaments, civilian production and, from 1 August 1944, aircraft, and Speer dominated the Central Planning Board that allocated raw materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speer ran it through committees and rings of industrialists, a system of self-administration that he agreed at Nuremberg was closed to other authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Under the ministry armament output rose about threefold, but the Strategic Bombing Survey read this as a sign of how undermobilised German industry had been before 1942, not of a dictatorship's efficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rise came more from better industrial efficiency than from general mobilisation, for much of German industry stayed on a single shift and relatively few women were drawn into factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aircraft came under the ministry only after the raids of early 1944, when a special Fighter Staff dispersed production into small units that bombers found hard to hit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its output depended heavily on forced labourers and concentration-camp prisoners, whom Fritz Sauckel supplied by force to meet the numbers Speer demanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The tribunal convicted Speer of war crimes and crimes against humanity for his part in this slave-labour programme and sentenced him to 20 years in prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “Speer” (Nuremberg, 1 October 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judspeer.asp. [Open access]","Albert Speer, testimony, 19 June 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1948), 431–32, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-19-46.asp. [Open access]","Albert Speer, cross-examination by Robert Servatius, 20 June 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1948), 506–7, Avalon Project, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), “The German War Economy” and “The Attack on German Aircraft Plants,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Gabriel Eikenberg, “Albert Speer 1905–1981,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/albert-speer. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Albert Speer,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/albert-speer. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Speer have to build the ministry almost from scratch?","a":"Because, as he told the tribunal, Todt had neglected armament, and in autumn 1941 Hitler, expecting victory in Russia, had ordered army armament to take second place to the air force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That order was not rescinded until January 1942, so Todt had only begun to build an organisation when he died, just as winter losses in Russia made more weapons urgent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did output rise so fast after 1942?","a":"Because there was so much slack to take up: the Strategic Bombing Survey found that German industry had been incompletely mobilised until the defeat at Moscow, with arms production in 1940 and 1941 generally below Britain's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Speer rationalised production and removed the worst inefficiencies in the earlier controls, which the survey judged more important than any general mobilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Speer convicted though he did not run the labour programme?","a":"Because the tribunal found that he sent Sauckel estimates of the workers his industries needed knowing they would be foreign labourers under compulsion, and attended the conferences that extended the programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In mitigation it noted that his blocked industries kept many workers in their homes, and that late in the war he told Hitler the war was lost and tried to prevent senseless destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reich Ministry of Armaments and War Production"},
    "ww2-862": {"questions":["Announcing closed luxury restaurants and a broad duty for women to work, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 18 February 1943 was also meant to win Goebbels more power over the war economy.","The total mobilisation demanded in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came late, for much of German industry still worked a single shift and drew in fewer women than in the First World War."],"abstract":"The <b>Sportpalast speech</b> was the address in which Joseph Goebbels, the Nazi propaganda minister, called for total war before a carefully selected audience in the Berlin Sportpalast on 18 February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It came weeks after the surrender at Stalingrad, and Goebbels meant it both to rouse popular enthusiasm for the war and to persuade Hitler to give him greater powers over the war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 3,000 chosen listeners, most of them sent by the party, cheered the broadcast speech, which ended with ten questions, among them whether they wanted total war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Goebbels announced that luxury restaurants and shops had been closed, that hundreds of thousands of military exemptions had been cancelled, and that women not yet working now had a broad duty to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A month earlier Hitler had ordered the total mobilisation of people and resources, under which men aged 16 to 65 and women aged 17 to 45 could be called up for the defence of the Reich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The speech exposed how far Germany was from the total effort it described, for its industry had been incompletely mobilised until the defeat at Moscow and much of it still worked a single shift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Relatively few German women, fewer than in the First World War, were drawn into industry, and the average working week stayed below British standards throughout the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Albert Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that the reorganisation planned after Stalingrad to free German labour was never carried out, because the committee of Lammers, Keitel and Bormann lacked the technical knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The labour gap was filled instead by recruiting foreign and forced workers, whom Fritz Sauckel obtained by compulsion to meet the armament industry's demands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Goebbels became plenipotentiary for total war only on 25 July 1944, after which businesses not vital to the war were closed and working weeks of over 70 hours imposed, too late to matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Joseph Goebbels, “Nation, Rise Up, and Let the Storm Break Loose,” speech in the Berlin Sportpalast, 18 February 1943, trans. Randall Bytwerk, German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, from <i>Der steile Aufstieg</i> (Munich: Zentralverlag der NSDAP, 1944), 167–204, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/goeb36.htm. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der ‘Totale Krieg’,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/totaler-krieg.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), “The German War Economy,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Albert Speer, testimony, 20 June 1946, in <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16 (Nuremberg: International Military Tribunal, 1948), 478–79, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","Susanne Eckelmann, “Joseph Goebbels 1897–1945,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/joseph-goebbels. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “Speer” (Nuremberg, 1 October 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judspeer.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels give the speech?","a":"Because after Stalingrad the seriousness of the war was evident to everyone, and he wanted both to build popular enthusiasm and to convince Hitler to give him greater powers in running the war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He framed his demands as freeing soldiers for the front and workers for the armaments industry, even at the cost of the standard of social life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the mobilisation it demanded fall short?","a":"Because the plan to free German labour was left to a committee of Lammers, Keitel and Bormann, which, Speer said, lacked the technical knowledge to carry it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Women stayed underused throughout the war, much industry stayed on one shift, and the regime turned instead to foreign and forced labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the audience react so enthusiastically?","a":"Because it was hand-picked: most of those present had been sent by the party, and Goebbels himself called it the politically best-trained audience in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The audience reactions printed in the published text are sometimes stronger than the recording of the speech justifies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sportpalast speech"},
    "ww2-863": {"questions":["American intelligence in March 1949 read the removal of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the State Planning Commission as a release from routine work, though it began his fall in the Leningrad affair.","Before his execution in 1950, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> published an account of the war economy claiming that Soviet industrial output had fallen 2.1 times between June and November 1941."],"abstract":"<b>Nikolai Voznesensky</b> was the Soviet economist and official who headed Gosplan, the State Planning Commission, from January 1938 and directed economic planning through the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Born near Tula in 1903, he rose under the patronage of the Leningrad party chief Andrei Zhdanov to become first deputy prime minister, a Politburo member and a member of Stalin's war cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gosplan was never the all-powerful hand of Western imagination, but Stalin used its planners as agents above the ministries' battles for resources who could give him a truthful picture of the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Voznesensky introduced ways to monitor and manage the most acute shortages, realigned industrial prices with costs and drew up detailed long-term plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first task, reviving an economy that had stagnated since 1937, was still unfinished when Germany invaded in June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By his own later account, territory holding about 40 per cent of the population and producing 63 per cent of Soviet coal and 58 per cent of its steel was in enemy hands by November 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He wrote that gross industrial output fell 2.1 times between June and November 1941, as evacuated plants stopped at their old sites before they could restart in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war exposed how inadequate the pre-war plans for a war economy had been, and for a while the planners lost control, so that war production soared while the civilian sector came close to collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Victory at Stalingrad and Allied aid made it possible to restore economic balance in 1943 and 1944, by which time Voznesensky had become one of Stalin's favourites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lost Gosplan in March 1949 and was executed in 1950, and in 1956 Khrushchev named him among the innocent victims of the fabricated Leningrad affair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Harrison, “Voznesensky, Nikolai A.,” in <i>Encyclopedia of Russian History</i>, ed. James R. Millar (New York: Macmillan Reference, 2003), author’s postprint, University of Warwick, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/voznesensky2003.pdf. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Gosplan,” in <i>Dizionario del comunismo nel XX secolo</i>, ed. Silvio Pons and Robert Service, vol. 1 (Turin: Einaudi, 2007), 338–40, English postprint, University of Warwick, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/gosplan2007.pdf. [Open access]","N. A. Voznesensky, <i>Soviet Economy during the Second World War</i> (New York: International Publishers, 1949), 37–43, https://archive.org/details/economy_202103. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “Molotov-Mikoyan Shift,” <i>Weekly Summary</i> no. 42, 18 March 1949, CIA-RDP78-01617A002100210001-4, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp78-01617a002100210001-4. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “Politburo Maneuvers,” <i>Weekly Summary</i> no. 45, 8 April 1949, CIA-RDP78-01617A002100240001-1, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp78-01617a002100240001-1. [Open access]","Nikita Khrushchev, speech to the 20th Congress of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union, 24–25 February 1956, from <i>Congressional Record</i>, 84th Congress, 2nd session, vol. 102, part 7 (4 June 1956), 9389–9403, Internet Modern History Sourcebook, Fordham University, https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/mod/1956khrushchev-secret1.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin need Gosplan at all?","a":"Because in a command economy every minister was judged by results and so had a reason to hide the truth, and Stalin needed agents above those battles who would tell him how the economy really stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To keep the planners loyal he purged Gosplan in 1930, 1937 and 1949, but spared them from being judged by plan fulfilment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet economy come so close to collapse?","a":"Because the invasion took away regions that had held about 40 per cent of the population and most of the coal, iron and steel, while hundreds of factories were in transit to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pre-war plans for a war economy proved inadequate, and as war output soared the civilian economy that fed and housed the workers neared collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did American intelligence misread his fall?","a":"Because in March 1949 it saw only a reshuffle, describing the relief of Molotov, Mikoyan and Voznesensky from operational duties as freeing them for wider policy work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A few weeks later it noticed that Voznesensky had apparently been dropped from the Politburo too, and linked this to Zhdanov's death and Malenkov's rise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nikolai Voznesensky"},
    "ww2-864": {"questions":["Begun as German armies overran the western regions in 1941, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grafted huge factories onto remote rural places in the Urals and Siberia, where many stayed after the war.","In late 1941 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a trough in which moved plants had stopped at their old sites but had not yet restarted in the east, and output fell sharply."],"abstract":"The <b>evacuation of Soviet industry</b> was the removal by rail of factories, machinery and workers from the western Soviet Union to the Volga, the Urals, Siberia and Central Asia after the German invasion of June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 3 July 1941 Stalin ordered in a radio broadcast that all rolling stock be evacuated in any retreat and that valuable property that could not be withdrawn be destroyed without fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nikolai Voznesensky, the head of Gosplan, later wrote that more than 1,360 large, mainly war, plants were moved east in some 3 months of 1941, 455 of them to the Urals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The move shifted the geographical centre of the war economy hundreds of kilometres to the east, and huge evacuated factories were grafted onto remote rural localities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It mattered for the outcome, since despite the speed of the German advance a large part of the arms industry had been brought to safety in the Urals and Siberia during 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The price was a deep trough, for in the last 2 months of 1941 the evacuated plants had stopped at their old sites but had not yet restarted at their new ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Voznesensky put the fall in gross industrial output between June and November 1941 at 2.1 times, and some trains carrying defence plants east were looted on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American intelligence report of 1947, drawn from an informant, described Factory No. 183 at Nizhny Tagil, a railway-wagon works before 1941, absorbing machinery from Kharkov and Leningrad and building T-34 tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A later report on the same Ural railway-wagon plant, Uralvagonzavod, said that its production of T-34 tanks ended by 1948 as it returned to building wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yet despite some return westward after the war, the new war economy of the Urals and Siberia was kept in existence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["N. A. Voznesensky, <i>Soviet Economy during the Second World War</i> (New York: International Publishers, 1949), 37–43, https://archive.org/details/economy_202103. [Open access]","John Barber and Mark Harrison, “Patriotic War, 1941 to 1945,” in <i>The Cambridge History of Russia</i>, vol. 3, <i>The Twentieth Century</i>, ed. Ronald Grigor Suny (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2006), 217–42, authors’ postprint, University of Warwick, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/patrioticwar2006.pdf. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The USSR and Total War: Why Didn’t the Soviet Economy Collapse in 1942?,” in <i>A World at Total War: Global Conflict and the Politics of Destruction, 1939–1945</i>, ed. Roger Chickering, Stig Förster and Bernd Greiner (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2005), 137–56, author’s postprint, University of Warwick, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/totalwar2005.pdf. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, radio broadcast, 3 July 1941, Stalin Archive, Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1941/07/03.htm. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “Industries in the Urals: Sverdlovsk, Nizhni Tagil, Kushva, Kamensk Uralsk, Sinarskaya, etc.,” intelligence report, 20 May 1947, CIA-RDP82-00457R000600100010-7, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00457r000600100010-7. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Agency, “Tank Production of the Ural Railroad Car Plant near Nizhniy Tagil,” information report, 4 December 1952, CIA-RDP82-00457R015100050010-5, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00457r015100050010-5. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Der Überfall auf die Sowjetunion,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/sowjetunion.html. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Soviet output collapse in late 1941?","a":"Because evacuated plants had stopped working in the west but were not yet running in the east, while the regions lost by November 1941 had produced most of the country's coal, iron and steel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Voznesensky reported that between August and November 1941, 303 ammunition works ceased production through occupation or evacuation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1942 the output of food, fuels and metals had fallen by half or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the factories restart so far from home?","a":"Because, by Voznesensky's account, the plants were shipped to industrial sites already under construction or to working enterprises, which sped the restart, and capital construction in the east rose sharply in the second half of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Urals over 400 of the 455 evacuated enterprises were running again by the end of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work was chaotic rather than planned in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the evidence on single plants come partly from American intelligence?","a":"Because after the war American intelligence questioned informants about plants in the Urals, while Voznesensky's published account gives only totals of enterprises for each region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such reports were marked as unevaluated information, so their details, such as how many tanks a plant built, need caution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Evacuation in the Soviet Union"},
    "ww2-865": {"questions":["Neville Chamberlain told the Commons in April 1939 that a new <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would take over the Royal Ordnance Factories, while the Admiralty and the Air Ministry kept their own production.","Unable to wring more tonnage from the Ministry of Shipping, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended the first year of war nearly 2 million tons short of the iron ore it had budgeted for."],"abstract":"The <b>Ministry of Supply</b> was the British government department created on the eve of the Second World War to provide munitions for the army and to control most of the raw materials that the forces and civilians needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 20 April 1939 Neville Chamberlain told the Commons that a bill would set it up under a minister sitting in the Cabinet, a decision that followed the German occupation of Prague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first tasks were army supply, the stores that the War Office already bought for other departments, and the reserves of essential metals and other raw materials, and it took over the Royal Ordnance Factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came into existence on 1 August 1939, but the Admiralty and the Air Ministry kept their own design and production, so it never became the single supply department that Clement Attlee had urged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leslie Burgin was its first minister, followed by Herbert Morrison in May 1940, Andrew Duncan that October, Lord Beaverbrook from June 1941 to February 1942, and Duncan again until July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Through its Raw Materials Department it oversaw the controls on materials, some statutory and others, for less critical goods such as rubber, asbestos and silk, run voluntarily by trade associations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pre-war plans relied on licensing rather than on sharing out every material among users, so control began cautiously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shipping soon proved the hardest limit, for the ministry asked for 7 million tons of imported iron ore in the first year, and for the first 3 months ore arrived at little more than half that rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the end of the first year it was nearly 2 million tons short of the ore it had budgeted for, and timber imports often ran at less than half of what it required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In January 1941 Ernest Bevin explained the slow start by saying that the ministry had first had to use the limited capacity it found while the Royal Ordnance Factories were greatly expanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["House of Commons, “Ministry of Supply,” oral answers, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 20 April 1939, vol. 346, cols. 496–99, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/apr/20/ministry-of-supply. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. II, “Between the Wars,” 56–57, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-2.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. IV, “Cash and Carry,” 128–29, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-4.html. [Open access]","M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. III, “From Peace to War, October 1938 to June 1940,” 89–90, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-3.html. [Open access]","M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. IV, “From Dunkirk to Pearl Harbor,” notes 33–34, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-4.html. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Ministry of Supply Bill,” committee, clause 1, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 19 June 1939, vol. 348, cols. 1835–56, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/jun/19/clause-1-establishment-of-ministry-of. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Production, Supply and Man-Power,” debate, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 21 January 1941, vol. 368, cols. 81–150, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/jan/21/production-supply-and-man-power. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the navy and the air force keep their own supply?","a":"Because Chamberlain told the Commons that their supply arrangements were working extremely well and that interfering with them might reduce output rather than raise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bill was nonetheless framed so that a ministry of supply in the full sense could be set up later, which is what Attlee pressed for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The question of whether one ministry should supply all three services had been argued for years and was settled only after Prague, in favour of a ministry for the army alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the bill reached committee in June 1939, one supporter noted that the new minister would take over supplies worth about £100 million a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the ministry short of iron ore in the first winter?","a":"Because the requisitioned ships were concentrated on a wheat crisis, and ore imports ran at little more than half the planned rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only in February 1940, when stocks had fallen below the needs of the trade and works were closing, could more tramp steamers be sent to Narvik, North Africa, Sierra Leone and Newfoundland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Timber was sacrificed to wheat and iron, and its imports never recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did munitions output rise slowly at first?","a":"Because, as Bevin told the Commons in 1941, the ministry had to begin with the limited capacity that existed while civilian life carried on, and new factories needed building materials and labour before they could produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The raw materials controls were also designed to be light, working through licences rather than full allocation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ministry of Supply"},
    "ww2-866": {"questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ordinances of October 1940 told Japanese banks to invest as the government directed and required companies to conserve funds for the war effort.","Although the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had given Tokyo sweeping economic powers since 1938, early victories delayed Japan's total economic mobilisation until after the defeats of late 1942."],"abstract":"The <b>National Mobilization Law</b> was the Japanese statute of 1938 that let the government take far-reaching steps to prepare the country for war without first submitting them to the Diet for approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the army's 5-year plans for war industries, whose consideration had been deferred when fighting broke out in China in July 1937, until the mobilisation plan was reinstated early in 1938 as an annual measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American ambassador Joseph Grew reported in March 1938 that the political parties were using obstructive tactics on the bill in committee, even after pledging to support the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In May he wrote that the government had given the Diet a formal assurance that the law would not be applied during the hostilities in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Grew counted it among laws far-reaching in their effect on Japan's political and social structure, passed while business resented the growing state control of industry and commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Ordinances under the law followed, and in October 1940 decrees made banks invest as the government directed and required companies to conserve funds for the law's purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Planning Board drew up yearly plans to mobilise material and human resources, until falling shipping capacity forced cuts and most of its work passed to the new Munitions Ministry in November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even so, the Strategic Bombing Survey found that early victories delayed total economic mobilisation until after the defeats of late 1942, with national product barely rising from 1940 to 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The share of national product spent directly on the war then rose from 23 per cent in 1941 to 52 per cent in 1944, by which time Japan could force no more of its economy into the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Behind every plan lay a dependence on imports, for Japan had almost no oil or bauxite of its own and lost most of its coal and iron-ore imports as its shipping was sunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. IV, “The Military Domination of Japan and Preparations for War” (Tokyo, 1948), 182–83, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-4.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VII, “The Pacific War” (Tokyo, 1948), 851–52, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “4-19 Total National Mobilization,” <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description19.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 13–15, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Joseph C. Grew to the Secretary of State, telegram, Tokyo, 8 March 1938, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 795, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v04/d795. [Open access]","Joseph C. Grew to the Secretary of State, despatch no. 2912, Tokyo, 2 May 1938, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Far East</i>, 596–98, doc. 796, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v04/d796. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the political parties resist the bill?","a":"Because, as Grew reported, the parties were increasingly conscious that their opposition to the revival of bureaucratic power was futile, and business was intensely unhappy at the trend towards state control of industry and commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The friction over the bill came to a head in early March 1938 before the leading parties again pledged their support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did full mobilisation still come so late?","a":"Because the success of the first campaigns made total mobilisation seem unnecessary, and the Survey judged the slow growth to 1942 a matter of inadequate planning rather than of the economy's limits, since national product rose from 41 billion yen in 1942 to 50 billion in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Falling shipping capacity soon forced the planners to cut their resource plans back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the government act without the Diet?","a":"Because the law itself made it possible for the government to take far-reaching steps for war without first submitting them to the Diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Imperial ordinances issued under it, such as those of October 1940 on bank investment and company funds, extended state control over finance by decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"State General Mobilization Law"},
    "ww2-867": {"questions":["In 9 months of 1943 Allied <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and related methods took 1,160 tons of Portuguese wolfram off the market, the American minister in Lisbon reported.","When <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could no longer keep Portuguese wolfram from Germany, the Allies pressed the old Anglo-Portuguese alliance and won a total ban on its export in June 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Preclusive purchasing</b> was the buying of strategic goods in neutral countries chiefly to keep them out of enemy hands, a method of economic warfare that the Allies used against the Axis in the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain and France had disagreed before the war over how to share its cost, and in December 1939 they agreed to divide the burden of preemptive purchases in the ratio of three to two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chief targets were materials the Axis lacked, above all wolfram, the ore of tungsten, from Spain and Portugal, while American officials weighed similar buying of chrome in Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Allies were short of such materials too, for the Japanese conquests of 1942 removed sources of tungsten and chromite as well as most of the world's rubber and much of its tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1943 Washington planned to use phosphate rock and food from French North Africa to obtain extra wolfram, zinc and tin from the Iberian Peninsula beyond its regular purchases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The American minister in Lisbon reported in January 1944 that in 9 months the Allies had taken 1,160 tons of Portuguese wolfram off the market, besides 400 to 500 tons bought in Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He doubted the feat could be repeated and argued that only a complete embargo, won by invoking the old Anglo-Portuguese alliance, could cut Germany off from Portugal's wolfram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spain suspended all wolfram exports in February 1944 while it prepared counter-proposals, which were expected to limit its sales to Germany to the level of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 5 June 1944 Portugal undertook to ban wolfram exports entirely and to stop production, which the State Department said would deprive the enemy of a vital war material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The minister judged that the appeal to the alliance had finally carried the day, helped by a surprise approach from Brazil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. VII, 181–82, 189, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-7.html. [Open access]","M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. V, 211, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-5.html. [Open access]","Cordell Hull to Carlton J. H. Hayes, no. 323, Washington, 16 March 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i>, 19–20, doc. 11, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d11. [Open access]","R. Henry Norweb to the Secretary of State, telegram, Lisbon, 10 January 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 4, <i>Europe</i>, 84–86, doc. 88, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d88. [Open access]","Carlton J. H. Hayes to the Secretary of State, telegram, Madrid, 5 February 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 4, <i>Europe</i>, doc. 336, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d336. [Open access]","Edward R. Stettinius Jr. to R. Henry Norweb, telegram, Washington, 8 June 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 4, <i>Europe</i>, doc. 146, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d146. [Open access]","Cordell Hull to Laurence A. Steinhardt, telegram, Washington, 4 June 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Near East and Africa</i>, doc. 1201, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v04/d1201. [Open access]","R. Henry Norweb to the Secretary of State, telegram, Lisbon, 9 June 1944, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1944</i>, vol. 4, <i>Europe</i>, 131–32, doc. 147, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1944v04/d147. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why buy materials the Allies did not need for themselves?","a":"Because the aim was to damage the Axis: American planners spoke openly of increasing the preemptive effect of Allied activity in the Iberian Peninsula by buying strategic and preclusive materials beyond their own needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every ton bought or immobilised was a ton Germany could not have, and the State Department called wolfram a vital war material whose loss could shorten the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was buying no longer enough by 1944?","a":"Because the minister in Lisbon judged that the unorthodox methods of 1943 could not be repeated, partly because some of the success came when the Germans had run out of funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He saw no economic benefit the Allies could offer Salazar that would make him cut exports, and so urged a full embargo backed by the alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Portugal finally agree to an embargo?","a":"Because, in the minister's view, the invocation of the Anglo-Portuguese Alliance carried the day, while Brazil's appeal, as the daughter nation whose soldiers were about to fight, took Salazar by surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The State Department announced the ban on 6 June 1944, saying it should prove a factor in shortening the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Preclusive purchasing"},
    "ww2-868": {"questions":["Completed on 14 August 1943, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its companion line for refined products together moved more than 350 million barrels to the East Coast before victory in Europe.","Harold Ickes had warned by 1940 that submarines would cut tanker traffic, but steel for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was refused three times before approval came in June 1942."],"abstract":"The <b>Big Inch</b> was the crude-oil pipeline, 61 centimetres (24 inches) across, laid during the Second World War from East Texas to the north-eastern United States beside a narrower line for refined products called the Little Big Inch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harold Ickes, the Secretary of the Interior, had realised by 1940 that German submarines would make it impossible to ship oil to the north-east by tanker in wartime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The danger was real, for between December 1941 and June 1942 the Allies lost nearly 1.5 million gross tons of tankers, at times one 15,000-ton tanker a day, most of them to submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ickes's requests for steel were refused twice in 1941 and again after Pearl Harbor, until the War Production Board approved the first section on 10 June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Built by War Emergency Pipelines but owned by the government's Defense Plant Corporation, the line ran some 2,020 kilometres (1,254 miles) from Longview in Texas to Phoenixville in Pennsylvania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In December 1942 Roosevelt set up the Petroleum Administration for War under Ickes to coordinate oil policy and provide adequate supplies of petroleum for the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Big Inch was completed on 14 August 1943 and the Little Big Inch on 2 March 1944, at a cost for the two of $146 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together they carried more than 350 million barrels of crude oil and refined products to the East Coast before the war in Europe ended in May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain too lived on oil carried by sea, and by the early summer of 1941 its stocks had fallen to a danger level of 4.5 million tons until American tankers came to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Axis had no such plenty, for Germany's oil supply was tight throughout the war and Japan depended almost wholly on oil imported from the United States or the Dutch East Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jerrell Dean Palmer and John G. Johnson, “Big Inch and Little Big Inch,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/big-inch-and-little-big-inch. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, Executive Order 9276, “Establishing the Petroleum Administration for War and Defining Its Functions and Duties,” 2 December 1942, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-9276-establishing-the-petroleum-administration-for-war-and-defining-its. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. XIV, “Outline of Shipping,” 413, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-14.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), “The Attack on Oil,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 13–14, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), chap. X, “War Transport,” 256, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-10.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why build a pipeline when tankers already carried the oil?","a":"Because tankers on the coastal route were exposed to submarines, as Ickes had foreseen by 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the first half of 1942 U-boats off the American coast sank tankers at times at the rate of one large ship a day, with no coastal convoys or blackout to protect them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A pipeline overland was safe from that attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the pipeline take so long to approve?","a":"Because steel was scarce and needed elsewhere: the Federal Allocation Board refused Ickes's request in September and again in November 1941, and the War Production Board refused it once more after Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only on 10 June 1942 did the board approve the first section, and the second leg on 26 October 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government take such control of oil?","a":"Because, in Roosevelt's words, policy on petroleum had to be coordinated and centralised to provide adequate supplies for the successful prosecution of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order made the Secretary of the Interior the Petroleum Administrator, responsible to the President for plans covering the whole American oil industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Big Inch"},
    "ww2-869": {"questions":["A wrong turn at Târgoviște sent the leading bombers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> towards Bucharest, so that most of the force reached Ploiești only after its defences had been alerted.","Costing 54 bombers and 532 airmen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed perhaps 42 per cent of Ploiești's refining capacity, but idle plant and quick repairs soon made good the loss."],"abstract":"<b>Operation Tidal Wave</b> was the low-level attack by American B-24 Liberator bombers on the oil refineries around Ploiești in Romania on 1 August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allied planners estimated that crude oil gave Germany two-thirds of its petroleum and that 60 per cent of that crude came from Ploiești, or roughly a third of all its liquid fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan, devised by Colonel Jacob Smart, broke with the air force's doctrine of high-altitude daylight precision bombing by sending the bombers in at treetop height in the hope of surprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Five groups, 177 aircraft carrying 1,725 Americans and one Briton, flew some 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) from Benghazi in Libya under Brigadier General Uzal Ent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cloud broke up the formation, and the leading group mistook Târgoviște for its final turning point and flew towards Bucharest, alerting the defences before most groups reached their targets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some groups went in at 30 to 90 metres (100 to 300 feet) through flak, fires, balloon cables and fighters, and five airmen were awarded the Medal of Honor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American official history counted 54 aircraft lost and 532 airmen dead, captured, missing or interned, while the National WWII Museum gives 52 shot down and 310 airmen killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 42 per cent of the refining capacity was thought destroyed, but the Germans brought idle units into use and repaired the rest, and within weeks the complex refined more than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Strategic Bombing Survey later found that Ploiești and the Hungarian fields together gave about a quarter of German liquid fuel in 1943, and that the August raid had only limited effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The refineries were left alone until April 1944, when bombers from Italy began a campaign that dropped 13,469 tons of bombs for 350 heavy bombers lost and left them ruined before Soviet troops arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Albert F. Simpson, “The Conquest of Sicily,” in Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Europe: Torch to Pointblank, August 1942 to December 1943</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1949), 477–83, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/II/AAF-II-14.html. [Open access]","“The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” in Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chap. 9, 280–97, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), “The Attack on Oil,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Jason Dawsey, “Over the Cauldron: Ploesti and the American Air War in Romania,” The National WWII Museum, 11 August 2019, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/over-cauldron-ploesti-american-air-war-romania. [Open access]","John G. Bunnell, “Knockout Blow? The Army Air Force’s Operations against Ploesti and Balikpapan” (master’s thesis, School of Advanced Air and Space Studies, Air University, Maxwell Air Force Base, 2005), Defense Technical Information Center ADA477018, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA477018. [Open access]","Robert J. Modrovsky, “1 August 1943 – Today’s Target Is Ploesti: A Departure from Doctrine” (research report, Air War College, Air University, Maxwell Air Force Base, 1999), Defense Technical Information Center ADA395189, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA395189. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bombers attack at such a low height?","a":"Because the planners hoped that surprise and minimum altitude would let them hit the key installations in each of Ploiești's nine major refineries accurately before the defences could react.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a deliberate break with the doctrine of high-altitude daylight precision bombing that the American air arm had built over 20 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The crews practised for days on a full-scale outline of the targets laid out in the Libyan desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the raid fail to cut German oil supplies for long?","a":"Because errors of execution left many targets untouched, many bombs failed to explode, and no follow-up attacks had been planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Germans brought idle refining units into use and repaired damaged plant quickly, and within weeks the complex was refining more oil than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The heavy losses also left the Ninth Air Force, for practical purposes, finished as a bomber command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attacks of 1944 succeed where the first raid had not?","a":"Because they were sustained: the official history concludes that the key to the destruction of Ploiești lay in the unbroken character of the offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bases in southern Italy halved the distance, and long-range fighters could escort the bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mining of the Danube cut deliveries further, and the Soviet occupation of Romania in August 1944 ended the supply altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operation Tidal Wave","places":["city:1159135863"]},
    "ww2-870": {"questions":["Patented by a German chemist in 1913 and honoured with a Nobel Prize in 1931, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns coal into oil by forcing hydrogen into it under heat and high pressure.","Leuna near Merseburg, the first plant to work the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a commercial scale, was bombed 22 times in 1944 and 1945 and averaged 9 per cent of its capacity."],"abstract":"The <b>Bergius process</b> is a way of turning coal into liquid fuel by exposing it to hydrogen gas under high pressure, developed by the German chemist Friedrich Bergius in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bergius filed his first patent on the high-pressure hydrogenation of oils in May 1913, and that summer his assistant turned 80 per cent of an artificial coal into gases and liquids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Industrial apparatus was developed at his Mannheim-Rheinau works between 1915 and 1925, and I.G. Farben then improved the catalysts and ran the process in its own factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bergius shared the 1931 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for contributions to the invention and development of chemical high-pressure methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> I.G. Farben's Leuna works near Merseburg was the world's first commercial-scale hydrogenation plant and the first to run on coal, treating local brown coal at 230 atmospheres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the Second World War the synthetic plants were Germany's chief source of oil and its only source of aviation petrol, making an average of 316,000 tons a month before the bombing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Allied investigators found that the hydrogenation plants made an overwhelming share of German aviation fuel, while the rival Fischer–Tropsch method was barely being extended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Leuna alone made slightly more than 600,000 tons of liquid products a year in 1943 and early 1944, about 40 per cent of it base petrol for aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When American bombers struck the synthetic plants in May 1944, Albert Speer said the attack made real a nightmare of 2 years, and by September synthetic output was down to 17,000 tons a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same raids cut into synthetic rubber, which came from four plants because Germany had entered the war with rubber stocks for only 2 or 3 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Friedrich Bergius, “Chemical Reactions under High Pressure,” Nobel Lecture, 21 May 1932, in <i>Nobel Lectures, Chemistry 1922–1941</i> (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 1966), https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/bergius-lecture.pdf. [Open access]","Nobel Prize Outreach, “Friedrich Bergius: Facts,” The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1931, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1931/bergius/facts/. [Open access]","R. Holroyd and W. F. Faragher, “Hydrogenation,” in R. Holroyd, ed., <i>Report on Investigations by Fuels and Lubricants Teams at the I. G. Farbenindustrie A. G. Works at Leuna</i>, CIOS Target No. 30/402 (London: Ministry of Fuel and Power, 24 September 1945), Fischer-Tropsch Archive, 18–37, http://www.fischer-tropsch.org/primary_documents/gvt_reports/MofFP/ig_farb_at_leuna/ig_hydrogenation.htm. [Open access]","R. Holroyd, “German Oil Production,” in R. Holroyd, ed., <i>Report on Investigations by Fuels and Lubricants Teams at the I. G. Farbenindustrie A. G. Works at Leuna</i>, CIOS Target No. 30/402 (London: Ministry of Fuel and Power, 24 September 1945), Fischer-Tropsch Archive, 133, http://www.fischer-tropsch.org/primary_documents/gvt_reports/MofFP/ig_farb_at_leuna/ig_oil_production.htm. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), “The Attack on Oil” and “Further Dividends from the Oil Attack,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","“The Strategic Bomber Strikes Ahead,” in Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chap. 9, 280–86, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-9.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the process need such high pressure?","a":"Because, as Bergius explained in his Nobel lecture, high pressure raised the concentration of hydrogen and so sped up its addition to the oils and coal as they broke down under heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without enough hydrogen, cracking heavy oils left coke and poor fuel, which was the defect he set out to cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method exposes coal to hydrogen under high pressure to form hydrocarbons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany rely on coal hydrogenation for aircraft fuel?","a":"Because the synthetic plants were its only source of aviation petrol, with natural crude coming mainly from Romania and Hungary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Leuna drew its brown coal from open workings owned by I.G. Farben next to the plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most Romanian and Hungarian oil went straight to the armed forces in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could bombing keep Leuna out of action?","a":"Because the synthetic plants were vast, complex works that could not be broken up and dispersed, so each repair was met by a fresh attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A surprise raid in July 1944 coked up Leuna's coal converters, which had no safe emergency draining, and the plant had to switch to brown coal tar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over a year, 6,552 bomber sorties dropped 18,328 tons of bombs on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bergius process"},
    "ww2-841": {"questions":["Opencast coal mines in Wales were the subject that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suggested to Graham Sutherland in 1943, after he had already drawn a series of limestone quarries.","In 1946 a third of the pictures gathered by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went to the Imperial War Museum and the rest to galleries across Britain and the Commonwealth."],"abstract":"The <b>War Artists' Advisory Committee</b> was the body of the British Ministry of Information that chose artists and commissioned pictures to record the Second World War, and it sat from November 1939 to December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A minister told the Commons in February 1940 that it had been formed to draw up a list of artists able to compile an artistic record of the war, like the one made in 1914–18 and kept in the Imperial War Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It advised the service departments on which artists to employ, and on copyright, exhibition and the publication of reproductions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chaired by Kenneth Clark, it was more structured than the official picture collecting of the First World War, which had employed painters such as Paul Nash and William Orpen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over 300 artists were commissioned, among them John Piper, Graham Sutherland, Henry Moore, Paul Nash and Stanley Spencer at home, and Anthony Gross, Edward Bawden and Edward Ardizzone overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It often proposed the subject too, and in the summer of 1943 it suggested opencast coal mines to Sutherland, who drew them in Wales after a series of limestone quarries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Other commissions sent painters abroad, as when Robert Medley, then an air-raid warden, was given 3 months in France to record the landings of troops for the British Expeditionary Force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Asked in May 1940 whether Scottish artists would get the same facilities as English ones, the Minister of Information replied that no distinction was made by residence or membership of any body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Clark also wrote on art for the people and prefaced books of reproductions such as <i>War Pictures by British Artists</i>, though several war artists disliked his tastes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pictures were shown in London and on tours at home and abroad, and in 1946 a third of the collection went to the Imperial War Museum and the rest to galleries across the United Kingdom and the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Tate, “War Artists,” Art Term, Tate, https://www.tate.org.uk/art/art-terms/w/war-artists. [Open access]","Jonathan Black, “To Hell with Materialism,” paper abstract, <i>Art Writers in Britain: Kenneth Clark</i>, Tate research project, https://www.tate.org.uk/about-us/projects/art-writers-britain/kenneth-clark/abstracts. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Artists' Advisory Committee,” written answer by Sir Edward Grigg, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 7 February 1940, vol. 357, cols. 235–36W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1940/feb/07/artists-advisory-committee. [Open access]","House of Commons, “War Artists (Facilities),” written answer by Alfred Duff Cooper, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 28 May 1940, vol. 361, cols. 429–30W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1940/may/28/war-artists-facilities. [Open access]","Tate Archive, Graham Sutherland, “Sketchbook 8,” 1943, TGA 812/8, catalogue description, https://www.tate.org.uk/art/archive/items/tga-812-8/sutherland-sketchbook-8. [Open access]","Tate Archive, “The Papers of Robert Medley,” TGA 894, collection description, https://www.tate.org.uk/art/archive/tga-894/the-papers-of-robert-medley. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Ministry of Information want artists at all?","a":"Because it wanted an artistic record of this war like the one made in 1914–18 and kept in the Imperial War Museum, and the committee existed to list the artists able to make it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the committee choose subjects as well as artists?","a":"Because it advised the service departments on which artist should be employed for which purpose, so it could steer painters towards scenes it wanted recorded, as it did when it suggested opencast mines to Sutherland in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some war artists criticise the chairman?","a":"Because Clark promoted favoured movements, such as the Euston Road School and the painters later called New Romantics, and artists such as Eric Kennington were highly critical of his views on state-sponsored art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War Artists' Advisory Committee"},
    "ww2-842": {"questions":["Under the Geneva Convention of 1929, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> captured while following an army was to be treated as a prisoner of war if he carried that army's certificate.","Killed by a Japanese machine gunner on Ie Shima on 18 April 1945, Ernie Pyle was the American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whom President Truman praised as the ordinary soldier's spokesman."],"abstract":"A <b>war correspondent</b> is a journalist who reports a war from the field, and in the Second World War the term covered radio commentators, artists and photographers as well as reporters, all working under military rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Canadian regulations of 1942 distinguished correspondents recognised as eligible from those accredited to a force in the field, who wore an officer's uniform without badges of rank and carried no weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Accredited correspondents were subject to military law and censorship, and were promised in return frank treatment and every proper facility, provided they wrote nothing of value to the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Geneva Convention of 1929 gave them a status of their own, for newspaper correspondents who followed an army and fell into enemy hands were to be treated as prisoners of war if they held its certificate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Access was a political decision, and in 1944 the British government refused to accredit a correspondent of the Communist <i>Daily Worker</i> to any of the three services preparing to go overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Women reporters found accreditation harder still, and in July 1944 the War Office counted 18 British, 10 Dominion and 22 American women correspondents accredited to Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> British women were not given full accreditation and, the War Office admitted, their facilities in theatres of war did not include covering the operations of front-line troops, a restriction that members of the Commons attacked repeatedly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the Eastern Front, the British Foreign Secretary told the Commons in January 1945 that as far as he knew no foreign correspondent was permanently accredited to the Soviet armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Correspondents also died, and on 18 April 1945 the American columnist Ernie Pyle was killed by a Japanese machine gunner on Ie Shima while riding to the front with a regimental commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> President Truman declared that no man in the war had told the story of the American fighting man so well, and called Pyle the spokesman of the ordinary American in arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Convention Relative to the Treatment of Prisoners of War, Geneva, 27 July 1929, art. 81, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva02.asp. [Open access]","Canada, Department of National Defence, <i>Regulations for War Correspondents: Canadian Army (Home Forces)</i> (Ottawa: King's Printer, 1942), 3–11, https://archive.org/details/31761116517319. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Women War Correspondents,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 27 July 1944, vol. 402, cols. 894–95, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jul/27/women-war-correspondents. [Open access]","House of Commons, “War Correspondents, Normandy,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 1 August 1944, vol. 402, cols. 1155–56, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/01/war-correspondents-normandy. [Open access]","House of Commons, ““Daily Worker” (War Correspondent),” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 2 March 1944, vol. 397, cols. 1571–72, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/mar/02/daily-worker-war-correspondent. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Russia (Foreign War Correspondents),” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 17 January 1945, vol. 407, col. 145, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/jan/17/russia-foreign-war-correspondents. [Open access]","Roy E. Appleman, James M. Burns, Russell A. Gugeler and John Stevens, <i>Okinawa: The Last Battle</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), chap. 7, 162, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Okinawa/USA-P-Okinawa-7.html. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Statement by the President on the Death of Ernie Pyle,” 18 April 1945, American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-the-death-ernie-pyle. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did correspondents wear uniform but carry no weapons?","a":"Because they went with an army without belonging to it: the Canadian rules of 1942 gave them an officer's uniform without rank and forbade weapons, and the Geneva Convention protected such followers as prisoners of war only if they carried the army's certificate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could a correspondent be refused accreditation?","a":"Because accreditation was granted by the armed forces, not claimed as a right: Canada's rules reserved the right to refuse any application without giving a reason, and in 1944 the British government refused the <i>Daily Worker</i> a correspondent with any of the three services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did women correspondents protest in 1944?","a":"Because the War Office would not give British women full accreditation, and their facilities in a theatre of war did not include covering front-line troops, even though 18 British women had been accredited to Supreme Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War correspondent"},
    "ww2-843": {"questions":["Capsules drawn from a glass bowl in Washington on 29 October 1940 fixed the order in which men registered under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be called for a year's training.","In August 1941 the House of Representatives voted by 203 to 202 to extend the service of men drafted under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beyond their original single year."],"abstract":"The <b>Selective Training and Service Act of 1940</b> was the law that brought compulsory military service to the United States in peacetime, approved by Franklin Roosevelt on 16 September 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its opening section declared that in a free society the obligations and privileges of military training and service should be shared generally under a fair and just system of selective compulsory service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every man between the ages of 21 and 36 had to register, and men of 18 to 36 were to be allowed to volunteer for induction regardless of race or colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those inducted were not to be employed beyond the Western Hemisphere except in American territories and possessions such as the Philippines, a limit shared with the resolution of August 1940 that called up the National Guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bill had been introduced in June 1940 by Senator Edward Burke and Representative James Wadsworth, and it passed the Senate by 47 votes to 25 and the House by 232 to 124.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On registration day, 16 October 1940, more than 16 million men registered, and in each of some 6,500 local areas a board of their neighbours gave every man a number at random.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 29 October capsules holding numbers up to 7,836 were drawn from a glass bowl in Washington and read out on the radio, and only the first tenth drawn could be called that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The act forbade discrimination on account of race or colour in selection, but Black leaders feared that its clause inducting men only when acceptable to the services and when housing was ready would be used to limit their call-up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Men conscientiously opposed to all military service could be assigned instead to work of national importance under civilian direction, though a separate register for objectors was struck from the bill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The single year of service was extended in August 1941, after General George Marshall warned Congress what release would do to the Army, by a House vote of 203 to 202.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Selective Training and Service Act of 1940</i>, Pub. L. No. 76-783, 54 Stat. 885 (16 September 1940), secs. 1–3, GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-54/pdf/STATUTE-54-Pg885.pdf. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Proclamation 2425—Selective Service Registration,” 16 September 1940, American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/proclamation-2425-selective-service-registration. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Radio Address for the Drawing Under the Selective Service Act of 1940,” 29 October 1940, American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/radio-address-for-the-drawing-under-the-selective-service-act-1940. [Open access]","Marvin A. Kreidberg and Merton G. Henry, <i>History of Military Mobilization in the United States Army, 1775–1945</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-212 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, 1955), 578–80, 592–93, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-212. [Open access]","Ulysses Lee, <i>The Employment of Negro Troops</i>, United States Army in World War II: Special Studies (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1966), 73–74, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-4-1. [Open access]","Neal M. Wherry, <i>Conscientious Objection</i>, Special Monograph No. 11, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Selective Service System, 1950), 164–65, https://archive.org/details/conscientiousobj01wher. [Open access]","Melvin Gingerich, <i>Service for Peace: A History of Mennonite Civilian Public Service</i> (Akron, PA: Mennonite Central Committee, 1949), 73–74, https://archive.org/details/serviceforpeaceh00ging. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the call-up decided by a lottery?","a":"Because the act demanded a fair and just system of selection, so each local board numbered its registrants at random and a national drawing fixed the order, with only the first tenth liable to be called that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Black leaders uneasy about the act?","a":"Because although it banned discrimination by race or colour, it let the services induct men only when they were acceptable and when shelter and hospitals were ready, which could be used to limit Black inductions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the 1941 extension matter so much?","a":"Because without it the National Guard, the draftees and the reserve officers would all have gone home after one year, which General Marshall told Congress would undo the new Army; the House passed it by a single vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Selective Training and Service Act of 1940"},
    "ww2-844": {"questions":["More than 1.5 million man-days in state mental hospitals were worked by objectors in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some of whom smuggled in cameras to expose conditions on the wards.","The 36 men chosen for a semi-starvation study at the University of Minnesota in November 1944 were volunteers from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the alternative service of American conscientious objectors."],"abstract":"<b>Civilian Public Service</b> was the programme under which American conscientious objectors who refused both combatant and noncombatant military service did work of national importance under civilian direction from 1941 to 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It rested on the Selective Training and Service Act of 1940, and an executive order of 6 February 1941 let the Director of Selective Service establish such work and decide which agencies would direct it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The historic peace churches, the Mennonites, the Church of the Brethren and the Friends, sponsored most of the camps and ran their camp life, while federal agencies supervised the work itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first draftees reached a Friends camp near Baltimore on 15 May 1941, and the Mennonite camp at Grottoes in Virginia and a Brethren camp in Indiana received their first men a week later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In all 151 camps were set up, and about 12,000 men served in the programme, out of some 43,000 who registered as objectors during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The men received no pay or family allowances from the government, and the churches paid for their camps, partly so that they could direct the religious and educational life of their own young men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1942 the whole cost of the Friends' share of the programme, which then covered about 1,600 men, was met by voluntary gifts of $363,383.90, of which Friends gave $216,147.14.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From May 1941 to March 1947 the objectors put in 5,931,632 man-days of project work, most of it forestry and soil conservation, but over 1.5 million of those days in state mental hospitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hospital units, which began in 1942, handled violent patients by non-violent methods, and some men smuggled in cameras to expose conditions that <i>Life</i> magazine published in May 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Others volunteered as human guinea pigs for medical research, among them 36 men in a semi-starvation study at the University of Minnesota from November 1944, meant to help in feeding the starving of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Executive Order 8675—Authorizing the Director of Selective Service To Establish or Designate Work of National Importance Under Civilian Direction,” 6 February 1941, American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-8675-authorizing-the-director-selective-service-establish-or-designate. [Open access]","Neal M. Wherry, <i>Conscientious Objection</i>, Special Monograph No. 11, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Selective Service System, 1950), 164–65, 173, 209–10, 269–70, 309, https://archive.org/details/conscientiousobj01wher. [Open access]","Melvin Gingerich, <i>Service for Peace: A History of Mennonite Civilian Public Service</i> (Akron, PA: Mennonite Central Committee, 1949), 59–60, 73–74, https://archive.org/details/serviceforpeaceh00ging. [Open access]","American Friends Service Committee, <i>1942 Annual Report</i> (Philadelphia: American Friends Service Committee, 1943), 21–22, https://archive.org/details/1942annualreport00amer. [Open access]","Kali Martin, “Conscientious Objectors and Civilian Public Service in World War II,” National WWII Museum, 16 October 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/conscientious-objectors-civilian-public-service. [Open access]","Neal M. Wherry, <i>Conscientious Objection</i>, Special Monograph No. 11, vol. 2, <i>Appendices A–G</i> (Washington, DC: Selective Service System, 1950), appendix G, item 119, “List of 151 Civilian Public Service Camps,” https://archive.org/details/conscientiousobj02wher. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the churches pay for the camps?","a":"Because federal money was not available for camps the churches would run, and their leaders wanted to direct the religious, educational and recreational life of their own men, which they could do only partly in government camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did some objectors go to prison instead?","a":"Because about 6,000 objectors refused to cooperate with Selective Service, more than two-thirds of them Jehovah's Witnesses, and those convicted nearly always went to prison, with a median sentence of 2 to 3 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did objectors work in mental hospitals?","a":"Because the hospitals were short of staff in wartime, and from 1942 units of objectors worked in them, where the Friends reported that they handled violent patients without violence and won praise from medical directors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Civilian Public Service"},
    "ww2-845": {"questions":["Baldur von Schirach, ordered by Hitler in the autumn of 1940 to run the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, told the Nuremberg tribunal that preserving children's lives mattered more than their schooling.","Flag-raising, roll-calls and Hitler Youth drill filled the days in the camps of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where German children of ten to fourteen were housed class by class."],"abstract":"The <b>Kinderlandverschickung</b>, a word meaning ‘sending children to the countryside’, was the Nazi programme that moved German city children away from air raids during the Second World War, and by its end about 2.5 million boys and girls had been evacuated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name revived an older practice of sending sickly town children for holidays in the country, which let propaganda present wartime evacuation as health trips and avoid admitting that air defence had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler ordered the scheme for Berlin and Hamburg at the end of September 1940 and made the former Reich Youth Leader Baldur von Schirach responsible for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Children of 10 to 14 went class by class to some 9,000 camps run by the Hitler Youth, younger children were placed with foster families, and mothers with infants were also sent away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exact numbers are uncertain, but one estimate is about 850,000 children in the camps, as many again in foster families and half a million infants with their mothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Nuremberg in May 1946 Schirach testified that Hitler had told him education was now secondary and that the main thing was to preserve life and the nervous energy of the young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Camp life was nonetheless strictly regulated, with flag-raising, roll-calls, sport and harvest work under the Hitler Youth, and it kept children for months or even years away from their parents' influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Hamburg boy sent with his class to Dresden in 1940 later remembered that their elderly teacher preferred reading aloud to teaching, and that they learned almost nothing from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians describe several functions at once, namely shelter from the bombing, holidays in safer regions, relief for parents, the state's grip on children and their militarisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Memories differ widely, with many former evacuees recalling comradeship and adventure while others remember homesickness, poor food, drill and the domineering Hitler Youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bernhard Struck, “Kinderlandverschickung (KLV),” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/alltagsleben/kinderlandverschickung.html [in German]. [Open access]","Felix Bellaire, “Kinderlandverschickung,” <i>Historisches Lexikon Bayerns</i>, 8 February 2021, https://www.historisches-lexikon-bayerns.de/Lexikon/Kinderlandverschickung [in German]. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 14 (Nuremberg, 1948), proceedings of 23 May 1946, testimony of Baldur von Schirach, 411–12, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/05-23-46.asp. [Open access]","Fritz Schleede, “Die Kinderlandverschickung 1940,” witness account written August 2002, LeMO Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/fritz-schleede-die-kinderlandverschickung-1940 [in German]. [Open access]","Edith Stampe, “Kinderlandverschickung,” witness account written April 2001, LeMO Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/edith-stampe-kinderlandverschickung [in German]. [Open access]","Gisela Miller-Kipp, review of <i>Kriegskinder: Kriegskindheiten in Düsseldorf 1939–1945</i>, ed. Bastian Fleermann and Benedikt Mauer, H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, May 2016, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=47017 [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime avoid calling it an evacuation?","a":"Because an evacuation would have admitted that air-raid protection might fail and that the Luftwaffe could not stop British bombing, so the old name for health holidays was used instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Hitler Youth so closely involved?","a":"Because from 1940 it had organisational responsibility for the scheme and ran the camps for ten- to 14-year-olds, which let the regime shape children away from home and school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do memories of the camps differ so much?","a":"Because experiences depended on the leaders, the lodging, the food and the work, so some recall comradeship and adventure while others recall homesick letters, hunger and harsh or bullying camp leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Evacuations of children in Germany during World War II"},
    "ww2-846": {"questions":["An amendment for equal pay for women teachers, carried by one vote in March 1944, was struck out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after Churchill made the clause a question of confidence.","Setting the school-leaving age at fifteen and promising sixteen when practicable, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> received the Royal Assent on 3 August 1944, before the war in Europe had ended."],"abstract":"The <b>Education Act 1944</b> was the wartime law that reorganised state schooling in England and Wales, recasting the whole law of education and replacing the Board of Education with a Minister charged to promote the education of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It organised public education in three progressive stages, primary, secondary and further education, and made local authorities responsible for making efficient education available through all of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Compulsory schooling was to run from five to fifteen, and the Minister was to raise the upper limit to sixteen as soon as that became practicable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fees were abolished in schools maintained by local authorities, and secondary education was to give all children over 11 equivalent opportunities to use their aptitudes, not an academic training for a select few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Church schools could choose to become controlled or aided schools, and every county and voluntary school was to begin the day with a collective act of worship and to give religious instruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> R. A. Butler, President of the Board of Education, had asked the Commons in July 1943 to welcome a White Paper on educational reconstruction, and on 19 January 1944 he called the bill the first of the government's measures of social reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He told the Commons that evacuation and other war experiences had shown how many homes needed help, and that the war would slow reform because teachers and building labour were short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 28 March 1944 MPs carried, by a single vote and against the government, an amendment moved by Mrs Cazalet Keir that teachers' pay should not differ between men and women solely on grounds of sex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill made the clause a question of confidence, and on 30 March the Commons voted by 425 to 23 against keeping the clause as amended, which took equal pay out of the bill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bill received the Royal Assent on 3 August 1944, and its main provisions on schools were set to come into force on 1 April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Education Act 1944</i>, 7 & 8 Geo. 6, c. 31, s. 7, “Stages and purposes of statutory system of education,” as enacted, legislation.gov.uk, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo6/7-8/31/section/7/enacted. [Open access]","<i>Education Act 1944</i>, 7 & 8 Geo. 6, c. 31, s. 35, “Compulsory school age,” as enacted, legislation.gov.uk, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo6/7-8/31/section/35/enacted. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Education Bill,” second reading moved by R. A. Butler, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 19 January 1944, vol. 396, cols. 207–322, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/jan/19/education-bill. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Clause 82.—(Remuneration of teachers.),” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 28 March 1944, vol. 398, cols. 1355–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/mar/28/clause-82-remuneration-of-teachers. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Clause 82.—(Remuneration of teachers.),” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 30 March 1944, vol. 398, cols. 1578–656, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/mar/30/clause-82-remuneration-of-teachers. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Educational Reconstruction,” motion moved by R. A. Butler, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 29 July 1943, vol. 391, cols. 1825–928, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/jul/29/educational-reconstruction. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Royal Assent,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 3 August 1944, vol. 402, col. 1648, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/03/royal-assent. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Butler mention evacuation in the debate?","a":"Because evacuation had shown how many homes needed help, and he presented the bill as a way to strengthen family life and children's physical, moral and religious development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the reforms not start at once?","a":"Because many teachers were past retiring age or were married women helping in wartime, and building labour and materials would be needed for bombed and replanned areas after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the equal-pay vote reversed?","a":"Because the government treated the amended clause as a question of confidence, and on 30 March 1944 the Commons voted by 425 to 23 against keeping the clause as amended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Education Act 1944"},
    "ww2-847": {"questions":["Empty casualty beds kept by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the Blitz were opened to the chronic sick, and about 4,000 old Londoners had been moved to country hospitals by December 1940.","Salaried doctors enrolled into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 1938, ranging from house officers to specialists, staffed the hospitals that the Ministry of Health had reserved for air-raid casualties."],"abstract":"The <b>Emergency Medical Service</b> was the state scheme through which Britain's Ministry of Health organised hospitals, doctors and nurses to treat air-raid casualties in the Second World War, joining voluntary and local-authority hospitals into one national system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Planning was driven by fear of bombing, and in April 1939 a Cabinet committee set a target of 300,000 beds for air-raid casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A review for the Cabinet in March 1939 had found only about 80,000 beds in England and Wales that could be used for the prolonged treatment of casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gap was to be closed by sending patients home, crowding wards, upgrading poor institutions and building hutted annexes, and the London region was divided into ten sectors so that casualties could be moved outwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1938 it was decided to enrol a corps of salaried doctors, from house officers to specialists, and standard rates of pay were arranged for doctors, nurses and auxiliaries working full time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The expected mass casualties did not come in 1939, and by October the Minister of Health was answering questions about mental patients sent home to free beds, whom he put at no more than 200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In December specialists who had waited for months at country stations were allowed to take other hospital work whenever the service did not need them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Once the Blitz began, empty casualty beds were also used for the sick, and by early December 1940 about 4,000 old and infirm Londoners had been moved to emergency hospital beds in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scheme exposed how uneven the pre-war hospitals were, for of about 700 general voluntary hospitals over 500 had fewer than 100 beds, and many had no specialist staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1941 the Minister of Health told the Commons that the government aimed, after the war, to ensure by a comprehensive hospital service that appropriate treatment would be readily available to every person in need of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), chap. 5, “Preparations: The Emergency Medical Service,” 54–86, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-Social/UK-Civil-Social-5.html. [Open access]","Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), chap. 22, “Hospitals in Demand,” 443–52, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-Social/UK-Civil-Social-22.html. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Emergency Medical Services,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 13 September 1939, vol. 351, cols. 628–29, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/sep/13/emergency-medical-services. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Emergency Hospital Scheme,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 12 October 1939, vol. 352, cols. 490–94, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/oct/12/emergency-hospital-scheme. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Emergency Medical Service,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 7 December 1939, vol. 355, col. 798, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/dec/07/emergency-medical-service. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Hospital Service (Government Proposals),” statement by Ernest Brown, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 9 October 1941, vol. 374, cols. 1116–20, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/oct/09/hospital-service-government-proposals. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many beds reserved before the war?","a":"Because Air Staff estimates of bombing were turned into enormous casualty figures, which led a Cabinet committee in April 1939 to set a target of 300,000 beds when only about 80,000 suitable beds existed in England and Wales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did MPs question the scheme in its first months?","a":"Because beds were held empty for casualties who did not come, while mental patients were sent home and specialists waited idle at country stations until the Minister let them take other work in December 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the scheme matter for post-war plans?","a":"Because it exposed the weaknesses of the pre-war hospitals, and in October 1941 the government promised a comprehensive hospital service after the war, built on partnership between local authorities and voluntary hospitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Emergency medical services"},
    "ww2-848": {"questions":["Expecting only 300,000 permanent homes within 2 years of victory in Europe, Parliament voted £150,000,000 in 1944 for the factory-built <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a temporary house meant to last 10 years.","Aluminium recovered from damaged aircraft went into one version of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the temporary British house whose average factory cost the Minister of Works put at £690 in 1946."],"abstract":"A <b>prefab</b> was one of the factory-made temporary bungalows that the British government planned in 1944 for families made homeless by bombing or left waiting by the halt to house-building during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scale of the damage explains the plan, for the official history estimated that 222,000 houses in the United Kingdom were destroyed or damaged beyond repair and about 3,745,000 different houses damaged in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The raids of 1940–1941 alone made more than two million people in the United Kingdom homeless for a time, and about one person in six in the London region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most damaged houses were only patched, and by 1941 over 1,100,000 had been made wind and weatherproof, often at a cost of only a few pounds each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In August 1944 the Minister of Health told the Commons that no more than 300,000 permanent houses could be built or building by the end of the second year after the war in Europe, because the building trades would be far smaller than before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The government's bill for temporary housing carried a financial resolution of £150,000,000, and its houses were costed on the basis of an average life of 10 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first design, the Portal bungalow, was a pressed-steel house of about 57 square metres (616 square feet) with a living room, kitchen, two bedrooms, bathroom, separate lavatory and outdoor shed, estimated at about £600 each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ministers argued that 100,000 such bungalows would need only 8,000 to 10,000 building workers, where the same number of ordinary houses usually took about 100,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other types followed, among them the Arcon and Uni-Seco houses and aluminium houses partly made from metal recovered from damaged aircraft, and in 1946 the average factory cost was put at about £690.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Act stayed in force after the war, and in October 1945 the new Minister of Health, Aneurin Bevan, confirmed that land used for temporary bungalows could later be put to other approved purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), chap. 14, “The Challenge of London's Homeless,” 255–300, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-Social/UK-Civil-Social-14.html. [Open access]","Richard M. Titmuss, <i>Problems of Social Policy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1950), chap. 16, “Arithmetic of Stress,” 328–30, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-Social/UK-Civil-Social-16.html. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Housing (Temporary Accommodation) Bill,” second reading moved by Henry Willink, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 1 August 1944, vol. 402, cols. 1251–312, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1944/aug/01/housing-temporary-accommo-dation-bill. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Aluminium Houses,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 3 December 1945, vol. 416, col. 1905, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/dec/03/aluminium-houses. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Temporary Houses (Cost),” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 16 April 1946, vol. 421, cols. 2479–80, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1946/apr/16/temporary-houses-cost. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Arcon and Uni-Seco Houses,” <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 16 July 1946, vol. 425, cols. 1023–24, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1946/jul/16/arcon-and-uni-seco-houses. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Housing (Temporary Accommodation) Act, 1944,” written answer by Aneurin Bevan, <i>Hansard</i>, HC Deb 25 October 1945, vol. 414, col. 2313W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1945/oct/25/housing-temporary-accommodation-act-1944. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why build temporary houses instead of permanent ones?","a":"Because the building labour force was expected to be far below its pre-war size, the government judged that it could not build enough permanent houses for some years, and a factory-made bungalow needed far fewer building workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the need for housing so great?","a":"Because bombing destroyed or damaged beyond repair about 222,000 houses, damaged millions more, and made more than two million people homeless for a time in the raids of 1940–1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did critics doubt the Portal bungalow?","a":"Because MPs questioned its cost, its siting and its planned life of 10 years, and one argued that its much-praised fittings could be put into other kinds of house just as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Prefabs in the United Kingdom"},
    "ww2-849": {"questions":["Seventeen African Americans shot dead by police, and not one white person, was the record of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Walter White and Thurgood Marshall denounced for the NAACP that July.","Overcrowded housing, the Sojourner Truth project dispute and a strike against Black promotions at Packard all fed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose rioters Roosevelt ordered to disperse on 21 June."],"abstract":"The <b>1943 Detroit race riot</b> was an outbreak of racial violence in Detroit, Michigan, that began on 20 June 1943 and left 34 people dead, 25 of them African Americans and 9 white, before federal troops restored order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> War production had drawn about half a million newcomers to the Detroit area between 1940 and 1943, many of them white Southerners, and Black workers competed with them for jobs and for scarce housing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Black Detroiters were crowded into a few districts such as Paradise Valley and Black Bottom, and in 1942 white crowds harassed and attacked the first Black families moving into the Sojourner Truth housing project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tension ran through the war plants as well, and early in June 1943 more than 25,000 workers at the Packard plant stopped work in protest at the promotion of three Black workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That summer serious disorders also struck Los Angeles, Beaumont and Harlem, and soldiers and sailors took part in the riots in Detroit and Los Angeles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The violence began among crowds on Belle Isle, the city's island park, on Sunday 20 June, and rumours that spread overnight on both sides turned it into rioting, looting and beatings across the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 21 June, at the request of the governor of Michigan, President Franklin Roosevelt issued a proclamation ordering the rioters to disperse so that federal forces could be used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The NAACP's investigators Walter White and Thurgood Marshall accused the Detroit police, who had only forty Black officers among 3,600, of one-sided enforcement, noting that 17 of the African Americans killed were shot by police while no white person was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army's use of white soldiers only for riot duty angered Black units, and eighty Black soldiers at a nearby air base protested to the President that they too should have been called.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A fact-finding committee appointed by the governor largely ignored the social inequalities behind the riot, while Mayor Edward Jeffries set up an Interracial Committee to address discrimination and racial tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Proclamation 2588—Directing Detroit Race Rioters to Disperse,” 21 June 1943, American Presidency Project, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/proclamation-2588-directing-detroit-race-rioters-disperse. [Open access]","Detroit Historical Society, “Race Riot, 1943,” Encyclopedia of Detroit, https://detroithistorical.org/learn/encyclopedia-of-detroit/race-riot-1943. [Open access]","Walter White and Thurgood Marshall, <i>What Caused the Detroit Riot? An Analysis</i> (New York: National Association for the Advancement of Colored People, July 1943), https://archive.org/details/whatcauseddetroi00whit. [Open access]","Ulysses Lee, <i>The Employment of Negro Troops</i>, United States Army in World War II: Special Studies (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1966), 376–77, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-4-1. [Open access]","Earl Brown, <i>Why Race Riots? Lessons from Detroit</i>, Public Affairs Pamphlet No. 87 (New York: Public Affairs Committee, 1944), 1–2, 6, https://archive.org/details/WhyRaceRiots. [Open access]","Johanna Russ, “The 1943 Detroit Race Riot,” Walter P. Reuther Library, Wayne State University, https://reuther.wayne.edu/node/8738. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why had tension built up in Detroit?","a":"Because war production brought about half a million newcomers, many of them white Southerners, into a city short of housing, and Black residents met hostility over jobs, promotions and the Sojourner Truth housing project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the NAACP blame the police?","a":"Because, its investigators found, police searched and beat Black residents while only reasoning with white mobs, and 17 of the African Americans killed were shot by police while no white person was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a presidential proclamation needed?","a":"Because federal troops could be used against domestic violence only after the governor applied and the President ordered the rioters to disperse, and Detroit's police commissioner later blamed the delay on not knowing a proclamation was required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"1943 Detroit race riot","places":["city:1159151245"]},
    "ww2-850": {"questions":["Broadcast from London and scattered as Allied leaflets, the 1941 sermons of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the Catholic bishop of Münster famous abroad as the 'Lion of Münster'.","Outspoken against the killing of the disabled yet silent about Jews and Poles, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> backed the war against Bolshevism in 1941 and was made a cardinal in February 1946."],"abstract":"<b>Clemens August von Galen</b> was the Catholic bishop of Münster from 1933 until 1946, whose sermons of 1941 against the murder of disabled patients made him the best-known church critic of the Nazi regime during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Born into an aristocratic Catholic family in 1878 and ordained in 1904, he was a parish priest in Berlin and Münster, chosen as bishop partly for his pastoral reputation, and was regarded as a nationalist and a conservative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He nevertheless opposed Nazi attacks on the Church, its associations and its schools from 1934, and helped to spread the papal encyclical <i>Mit brennender Sorge</i> of 1937, which condemned the regime's church and racial policies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the summer of 1941 he preached three sermons attacking the methods of the Gestapo, the seizure of monasteries and the killing of patients in psychiatric clinics and homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the sermon of 3 August he said that patients classed as unproductive members of the national community were being taken from Westphalian hospitals to be killed, and that he had reported this to the state prosecutor as murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The sermons were copied and passed from hand to hand across Germany, broadcast from London and dropped as leaflets by Allied aircraft, and abroad he became known as the 'Lion of Münster'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nazi leaders wanted to silence him but feared public unrest in wartime, and Goebbels prevailed in leaving him alone, with a reckoning postponed until after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Public knowledge of the killings and protests by clergy, Galen's among them, contributed to Hitler's order in late August 1941 to halt the gassing programme, although the murder of patients continued in other forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His protest had limits, for he remained silent about the fate of Jews and Poles, and in September 1941 he wrote a letter endorsing the war against what he called the Jewish-Bolshevik rulers in Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pope Pius XII made him a cardinal in February 1946, shortly before his death in Münster the following month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["German Resistance Memorial Center, “Clemens August Graf von Galen,” biography, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/biographies/index_of_persons/biographie/view-bio/clemens-august-graf-von-galen/. [Open access]","Manfred Wichmann, “Clemens August Graf von Galen 1878–1946,” LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 14 September 2014, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/clemens-august-graf-von-galen [in German]. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Euthanasia Program and Aktion T4,” Holocaust Encyclopedia, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]","Clemens August von Galen, sermon of 3 August 1941, excerpt, with government correspondence of 12–13 August 1941, German History in Documents and Images, German Historical Institute, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/excerpt-from-bishop-von-galen-s-sermon-august-3-1941-and-government-correspondence-regarding-the-sermon-and-the-charges-raised-against-galen-as-a-result-august-12-13-1941. [Open access]","Martin Menke, review of <i>German Catholicism at War, 1939–1945</i>, by Thomas Brodie, H-War, H-Net Reviews, May 2021, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=56454. [Open access]","Michael Hirschfeld, conference report, “Zwischen Seelsorge und Politik: Katholische Bischöfe im Nationalsozialismus,” H-Soz-u-Kult, H-Net Reviews, February 2017, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=49127 [in German]. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime not arrest him?","a":"Because he was popular among Catholics, and the regime feared unrest and weakened loyalty in wartime, so Goebbels's view prevailed and any reckoning was put off until after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did he report the killings to the police?","a":"Because the Penal Code treated deliberate killing as murder and made it an offence to know of a planned crime against life without reporting it, so he wrote to the prosecutor and the police chief on 28 July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his record still debated?","a":"Because he defended the Church and disabled patients against the regime, yet stayed silent about the persecution of Jews and Poles and supported the war against Bolshevism in the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Clemens August Graf von Galen"},
    "ww2-851": {"questions":["Passed on 28 December 1945, an American act let each foreign <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a serviceman enter outside the quotas, though a race bar remained until Congress amended it in 1947.","Ottawa paid the passage and printed a booklet of advice for every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> landing at Pier 21 in Halifax, where war bride clubs then helped the newcomers settle across Canada."],"abstract":"A <b>war bride</b> was a woman who married a serviceman from another country while he was stationed abroad in wartime and then emigrated to join him, a movement the Second World War produced in tens of thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most Canadian troops spent about 3 years in England before they saw action, and in 1940 alone 1,222 British women married Canadian soldiers, a figure that swelled by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Canadian Department of National Defence counted about 48,000 such marriages and 22,000 births between 1942 and 1946, about 93 per cent of the marriages to British women and most of the rest to Dutch and Belgian women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American servicemen married wherever they were based, and in Australia more than 10,000 marriages and 2,000 engagements between Australian women and United States servicemen took place in about 2.5 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Armies treated these marriages warily, and in December 1944 the Canadian Army told officers to refuse consent where they saw no basis for a happy marriage and to impose a 4-month wait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In January 1942 the Canadian War Committee agreed to pay minimum-cost ocean and rail passage for the wives and children of servicemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between April 1942 and March 1948 Canada brought in 43,464 war brides and 20,995 children, three-quarters of them after the war, most landing at Pier 21 in Halifax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government published a booklet called <i>Welcome to War Brides</i>, and war bride clubs across the country helped the newcomers adapt to unfamiliar customs and surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shipping was short after the war, and while Canada hoped in December 1945 to finish moving its servicemen's wives by August 1946, British wives of American soldiers waited on the American authorities, losing their allowances once their husbands were demobilised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The United States act of 28 December 1945 admitted such spouses outside the immigration quotas if otherwise admissible, and because race bars still applied, an amendment of July 1947 forbade refusing a spouse on grounds of race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jan Raska, “Movement of War Brides and Their Children through Pier 21,” Canadian Museum of Immigration at Pier 21, updated 22 June 2022, https://pier21.ca/research/immigration-history/movement-war-brides-and-children-pier-21. [Open access]","Parks Canada, “War Brides National Historic Event,” Directory of Federal Heritage Designations, https://www.pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_nhs_eng.aspx?id=1766. [Open access]","HistoricPlaces.ca, “Pier 21 and Canada’s War Brides,” Canadian Register of Historic Places, https://historicplaces.ca/en/pages/36_pier_21.aspx. [Open access]","<i>An Act to Expedite the Admission to the United States of Alien Spouses and Alien Minor Children of Citizen Members of the United States Armed Forces</i>, Public Law 271, 79th Cong., 1st sess., <i>U.S. Statutes at Large</i> 59 (28 December 1945): 659, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-59/pdf/STATUTE-59-Pg659.pdf. [Open access]","<i>An Act to Amend the Act Approved December 28, 1945</i>, Public Law 213, 80th Cong., 1st sess., <i>U.S. Statutes at Large</i> 61 (22 July 1947): 401, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-61/pdf/STATUTE-61-Pg401.pdf. [Open access]","Australian News and Information Bureau, “Melbourne, Vic. C. 1945. Four Wives of American Servicemen Gather with Their Children in the Botanical Gardens,” photograph P00561.033, Australian War Memorial, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C301265. [Open access]","Hector McNeil, oral answer, “Allied Forces (British Wives),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 15 October 1945, vol. 414, cols. 670–72, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/oct/15/allied-forces-british-wives. [Open access]","Alfred Barnes, written answer, “Canadian Servicemen (Wives, Transport),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 11 December 1945, vol. 417, cols. 363–64W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1945/dec/11/canadian-servicemenwives-transport. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many Canadian soldiers marry in Britain?","a":"Because most Canadian troops were stationed in England for about 3 years before they saw any active duty, which left time for courtship; in 1940 alone 1,222 British women married Canadian soldiers, and the number grew sharply by 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did army officers try to slow the marriages down?","a":"Because Canadian Military Headquarters complained of too many irresponsible marriages in Britain, and a directive of 1944 warned that marriage across nationality and religion risked unhappiness, imposing a 4-month wait before a wedding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the American act need amending in 1947?","a":"Because the 1945 act admitted spouses only if they were otherwise admissible under the immigration laws, which still excluded people by race; the 1947 amendment said a spouse should not be considered inadmissible because of race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War bride"},
    "ww2-852": {"questions":["Sunk by a German submarine on 2 July 1940, the Arandora Star was carrying Italians and Germans, each an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in British eyes, to internment camps in Canada.","Given the words 'Refugee from Nazi oppression' on their papers in 1939, exempted refugees escaped the label of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the law gave nationals of an enemy state."],"abstract":"An <b>enemy alien</b> was a national of a state at war with the country where he or she lived, and in Britain the Crown held a prerogative power to intern such people for as long as the war lasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most Germans and Austrians in Britain in 1939 were refugees from Nazism, and over 100 tribunals sorted those aged 16 to 70 into categories: A interned, B restricted and C free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the end of October 1939 the tribunals had heard 13,031 cases, interning 186 people and exempting 9,656 from both internment and the special restrictions on enemy aliens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those judged to be genuine refugees had 'Refugee from Nazi oppression' endorsed on their registration certificates, and the term 'enemy alien' was kept off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1940 the Commons was told that 415 enemy aliens had been interned when war broke out and 554 more on the tribunals' advice, while about 60,000 remained at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After the German victories in France and the Low Countries came mass arrests regardless of religion or politics, and when Italy entered the war in June, Italian men were interned too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By late August 1940 about 13,000 Germans and Austrians of category C had been interned, and 2,358 internees of category A and 4,206 of categories B and C had been shipped overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 2 July 1940 a German submarine sank the Arandora Star off Ireland while it carried 1,213 internees to Canada, and 470 of the Italians and 143 of the Germans aboard were among more than 800 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Dunera then took 2,542 men to Australia, 2,036 of them anti-Nazi refugees, most of them Jewish, in conditions later called inhumane, and many were held at Hay in New South Wales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A peer complained in August that families went weeks without news of men taken at seven in the morning, and as feeling turned releases began, while by October about 85 per cent of those still held were on the Isle of Man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sir John Anderson, oral answer, “Aliens (Tribunals),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 2 November 1939, vol. 352, cols. 2094–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/nov/02/aliens-tribunals. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Aliens (Detention),” <i>Hansard</i>, 1 February 1940, vol. 356, cols. 1270–71, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/feb/01/aliens-detention. [Open access]","Sir John Anderson, written answers, “Internees,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 22 August 1940, vol. 364, cols. 1476–80W, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/written_answers/1940/aug/22/internees. [Open access]","Herbert Morrison, oral answers, “Internees,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 17 October 1940, vol. 365, cols. 801–7, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/oct/17/internees. [Open access]","The National Archives, “The Loss of SS Arandora Star,” Stories from the Collection, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/stories/the-loss-of-ss-arandora-star/. [Open access]","National Library of Australia, “Dunera,” Digital Classroom: Internment, World War 2 (1939–45), https://www.library.gov.au/learn/digital-classroom/internment-world-war-2-1939-45/dunera. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Lords, “Internment of Aliens,” <i>Hansard</i>, 6 August 1940, vol. 117, cols. 107–39, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1940/aug/06/internment-of-aliens. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Jewish refugees from Germany interned as enemies?","a":"Because their nationality was German or Austrian, and after Germany's victories in France and the Low Countries panic set in and the government ordered the mass arrest of enemy aliens regardless of religion, politics or personal history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the sinking of the Arandora Star change opinion?","a":"Because more than half of those aboard died, and the inquiry under Lord Snell found that the Italians had been chosen from lists of Fascist party members without any tribunal hearing, some of them deported in error.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were many internees released in late 1940?","a":"Because a White Paper set out categories eligible for release, such as the old, the infirm and active opponents of Nazism, and by mid-October 1940 the release of about 5,200 internees had been authorised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Enemy alien"},
    "ww2-853": {"questions":["Proposed in a British note of January 1943, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> judged every rescue plan by whether it would delay the war effort and whether it could be carried out in wartime.","Keeping its recommendations secret, the Anglo-American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of April 1943 proposed moving about 20,000 refugees from Spain to North Africa and enlarging the committee founded at Évian."],"abstract":"The <b>Bermuda Conference</b> was a meeting of British and American delegates held on the island in April 1943 to discuss the refugees who had escaped from Nazi-occupied Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It came out of a refugee crisis that had begun before the war, and its proposals were meant to pass to the Intergovernmental Committee on Refugees, the body organised at the Évian meeting of 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain proposed the talks in a note of 20 January 1943, warning that refugees were crowding into neutral countries whose capacity to absorb them was approaching its limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same note argued that the problem could not be treated as wholly Jewish and feared that Germany might switch from extermination to extrusion, flooding other countries with refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both governments limited their delegates beforehand, the Americans forbidding commitments on shipping, money or new agencies and the British keeping Palestine off the agenda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Press reports before it opened said it would deal only with refugees already in Spain, Portugal and Switzerland, not with rescuing people still under Nazi rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The joint communiqué of 28 April said every proposal had faced two tests, whether it would delay the war effort and whether it could be carried out under war conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The recommendations were kept confidential, and a public statement in May explained that diverting Allied shipping was ruled out and that no negotiations with Hitler could be undertaken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In private the Secretary of State listed the main proposals as moving about 5,000 people from Bulgaria through Turkey to Palestine and about 20,000 refugees from Spain to North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American Jewish organisations denounced the outcome, one newspaper advertisement calling Bermuda a 'cruel mockery', and the conference had opened on the day the Warsaw ghetto uprising began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["British Embassy to the Department of State, aide-mémoire, “Refugees from Nazi-occupied Territory,” 20 January 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i>, ed. William M. Franklin and E. R. Perkins (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1963), doc. 103, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d103. [Open access]","Consul General at Hamilton to the Secretary of State, telegram transmitting the joint communiqué of the Bermuda Conference, 28 April 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i>, ed. William M. Franklin and E. R. Perkins (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1963), doc. 137, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d137. [Open access]","Cordell Hull to Franklin D. Roosevelt, 7 May 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i>, ed. William M. Franklin and E. R. Perkins (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1963), doc. 141, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d141. [Open access]","Department of State, statement issued to the press, 19 May 1943, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1943</i>, vol. 1, <i>General</i>, ed. William M. Franklin and E. R. Perkins (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1963), doc. 148, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1943v01/d148. [Open access]","<i>American Experience</i>, “The Bermuda Conference,” <i>America and the Holocaust</i>, PBS, https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/americanexperience/features/holocaust-bermuda/. [Open access]","Jewish Telegraphic Agency, “Bermuda Conference Will Deal with Refugee Problem, Not with Rescuing Jews in Nazi Lands,” 15 April 1943, https://www.jta.org/archive/bermuda-conference-will-deal-with-refugee-problem-not-with-rescuing-jews-in-nazi-lands. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the conference held in Bermuda?","a":"Because wartime regulations restricted access to the island, which kept the deliberations out of the public eye; even so, the delegates later made clear to the press that most proposals had been rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the delegates reject most proposals?","a":"Because they measured every proposal against two tests, the war effort and practicability in wartime, and agreed that diverting Allied shipping to move or care for refugees raised almost insuperable military difficulties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain want the problem treated as more than a Jewish one?","a":"Because its note argued that there were many non-Jewish refugees and much suffering in Allied countries, so a marked preference for removing Jews might bring Allied criticism and stir antisemitism where many foreign Jews arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bermuda Conference"},
    "ww2-854": {"questions":["The first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> landed at Harwich on 2 December 1938 with about 200 children, some of them from a Berlin Jewish orphanage destroyed during Kristallnacht.","Ended by the outbreak of war in September 1939, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had brought nearly 10,000 children to Britain, most of whom never saw their murdered parents again."],"abstract":"The <b>Kindertransport</b>, German for ‘children's transport’, was the informal name for the rescue that brought nearly 10,000 mostly Jewish children under 17 from Nazi Germany and the lands it had annexed to Britain in 1938 and 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began after Kristallnacht, the nationwide anti-Jewish riot of 9 to 10 November 1938, and on 21 November the Prime Minister told the Commons of the urgency of the problem of people anxious to leave Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That evening, late in a Commons debate on persecuted minorities, the Home Secretary, Sir Samuel Hoare, said the Home Office would give visas to children whose maintenance was guaranteed by private funds or individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The children entered on temporary travel documents, and a bond of £50 had to be posted for each child so that none would become a burden on the public purse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The government facilitated the scheme but did not organise it, a task left to the Movement for the Care of Children from Germany, later the Refugee Children's Movement, and at least 175 local committees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Hoare's concession covered only unaccompanied children, and the parents left behind stayed subject to the same strict immigration rules that members had begged him to relax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He spoke of the terrible dilemma of Jewish parents choosing between sending their children into the unknown and keeping them in the conditions now imposed on them in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first transport landed at Harwich on 2 December 1938 with about 200 children, some from a Berlin Jewish orphanage destroyed in the pogrom, and by early July 1939 more than 80 transports had brought about 7,750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The outbreak of war ended the scheme, the last transport reaching Harwich on 1 September 1939, while a parallel effort led by Nicholas Winton and others brought 669 children from Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1943 the Refugee Children's Movement, by then mainly dependent on government grants, still cared for some 8,000 children, and most never saw their parents again because their families were murdered in the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Kindertransports to the United Kingdom, 1938–1939,” Holocaust Encyclopedia, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kindertransport-1938-40. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “First Kindertransport Arrives in Great Britain,” Holocaust Encyclopedia, timeline of events, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/timeline-event/first-kindertransport-arrives. [Open access]","Sir Samuel Hoare, speech in the debate “Racial, Religious and Political Minorities,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 21 November 1938, vol. 341, cols. 1428–83, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/nov/21/racial-religious-and-political-minorities. [Open access]","Jennifer Craig-Norton, “Revisiting the Origins of the Kindertransport on Its 80th Anniversary,” History of Parliament, 21 November 2018, https://historyofparliament.com/2018/11/21/kindertransport-80-anniversary/. [Open access]","Isabel Wollaston, “The Kindertransport – 75 Years On,” Perspective, University of Birmingham, https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/research/perspective/refugees-wollaston. [Open access]","Kindertransport Association, “Rising to the Moment,” Historical Timeline, https://kindertransport.org/history/historical-timeline/rising-to-the-moment/. [Open access]","Neville Chamberlain, statement, “Refugees (Government Proposals),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 21 November 1938, vol. 341, cols. 1313–17, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1938/nov/21/refugees-government-proposals. [Open access]","Earl of Munster, speech moving the second reading of the Guardianship (Refugee Children) Bill, <i>Hansard</i>, House of Lords, 7 December 1943, vol. 130, cols. 105–9, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1943/dec/07/guardianship-refugee-children-bill-hl. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were children admitted when adults were not?","a":"Because the Home Secretary believed children could be dealt with in large numbers if responsible bodies and individuals sponsored them, while adult cases would still be checked one by one, and he stressed the plan would do no harm to the British population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a £50 bond required for each child?","a":"Because the children were admitted on condition that their stay would be temporary and that they would not be a burden on the public purse, the bond being meant to assure their eventual resettlement elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the transports stop in 1939?","a":"Because the outbreak of war in September 1939 ended them; the Refugee Children's Movement was already winding down for lack of money, and a final transport from Prague was cancelled at the last moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kindertransport"},
    "ww2-855": {"questions":["Selling over half a million copies, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> won 86 per cent approval in a Gallup poll, yet 97 Labour members voted against the government's response to it in February 1943.","Assuming children's allowances, a comprehensive health service and the avoidance of mass unemployment, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called the war a revolutionary moment that demanded revolutions, not patching."],"abstract":"The <b>Beveridge Report</b>, published as <i>Social Insurance and Allied Services</i> at the start of December 1942, was a plan by Sir William Beveridge for a unified system of social security in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew from a survey of the existing schemes announced by the Minister without Portfolio in June 1941, and ministers decided that it would be signed by Beveridge alone, with his civil servants acting only as advisers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It proposed one national scheme with a flat rate of contribution and a flat rate of subsistence benefit, run by a new Ministry of Social Security alongside national assistance and voluntary insurance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan rested on three assumptions: children's allowances, comprehensive health and rehabilitation services available to all, and the avoidance of mass unemployment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beveridge called want only one of five giants on the road of reconstruction, the others being disease, ignorance, squalor and idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The report tied its ambitions to the war itself, declaring that a revolutionary moment in the world's history was a time for revolutions, not for patching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Interest ran high before it appeared, and in November 1942 one member asked in the Commons whether the government agreed that the report took the country half-way to Moscow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It sold over half a million copies, and a Gallup poll soon after publication found 86 per cent of the public in favour, including over 70 per cent of employers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A minister reported the greatest interest among the troops, and in the Lords in February 1943 the name 'Beveridge' was called a symbol of hope at home and a synonym for social progress abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Ministers would not commit themselves, saying in January only that no conclusions had been reached and in March that a fuller statement could not yet be dated, and 97 Labour members voted against the government after the Commons debate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Beveridge, <i>Social Insurance and Allied Services</i>, Cmd. 6404 (London: HMSO, 1942), summary and paras. 300–309, Internet Modern History Sourcebook, Fordham University, https://fordham.edu/Halsall/mod/1942beveridge.asp. [Open access]","<i>Social Insurance and Allied Services: The Beveridge Report in Brief</i> (London: HMSO, 1943), 2–3, Oxford Brookes University RADAR, https://radar.brookes.ac.uk/radar/file/57edbe39-4e51-438b-b982-451ae456ed9e/1/DH-5-2-3.pdf. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Social Welfare (Beveridge Report),” <i>Hansard</i>, 17 November 1942, vol. 385, cols. 187–88, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1942/nov/17/social-welfare-beveridge-report. [Open access]","Sir William Jowitt, oral answer, “Beveridge Report,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 19 January 1943, vol. 386, cols. 36–37, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/jan/19/beveridge-report. [Open access]","United Kingdom, House of Commons, “Social Insurance and Allied Services,” <i>Hansard</i>, 16 February 1943, vol. 386, cols. 1596–694, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/feb/16/social-insurance-and-allied-services. [Open access]","Richard Paul Chapman, “The Development of Policy on Family Allowances and National Insurance in the United Kingdom, 1942–1946” (PhD thesis, London School of Economics and Political Science, 1991), LSE Research Online, https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/132144/1/U048581.pdf. [Open access]","Clement Attlee, oral answer, “Beveridge Report (Government’s Attitude),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 2 March 1943, vol. 387, cols. 469–70, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1943/mar/02/beveridge-report-governments-attitude. [Open access]","Lord Nathan, speech opening the debate “The Beveridge Report,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Lords, 24 February 1943, vol. 126, cols. 230–306, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1943/feb/24/the-beveridge-report. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the report speak of five giants?","a":"Because Beveridge treated social insurance as one part only of a wider policy of social progress: insurance could attack want, but disease, ignorance, squalor and idleness had to be tackled by other means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Beveridge alone responsible for the report?","a":"Because ministers decided in January 1942 that questions of high policy would arise, so the departmental representatives on his committee became his advisers and the report was to be signed by him alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Commons debate of 1943 matter politically?","a":"Because the government seemed reluctant to accept the plan and 97 Labour members voted against it; but for a rousing closing speech the motion might have been defeated and the coalition broken up, and Churchill soon made post-war social reform the theme of a broadcast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Beveridge Report"},
    "ww2-856": {"questions":["Through blockade, navicerts and pre-emptive purchases of strategic goods in Spain, Portugal and Turkey, Allied <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> aimed to starve German industry of the materials it needed.","When Allied bombers cut German synthetic fuel output from 316,000 tons a month to 17,000 by September 1944, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at last struck hard at the enemy's war production."],"abstract":"<b>Economic warfare</b> is the use of economic means to weaken an enemy's capacity to fight, by cutting off its trade, buying up what it needs and destroying what it produces, and in the Second World War it began on the first day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain had agreed before the war that a single ministry should conduct it, and the Ministry of Economic Warfare was set up on 3 September 1939, its first task the blockade of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In November 1939, as a reprisal, Britain decided to stop all exports from Germany, a step that in the previous war had taken some 6 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The blockade was enforced on neutrals by blacklisting and certification, and from January 1940 a state trading company made pre-emptive purchases of strategic goods, notably in Turkey, Spain and Portugal, to keep them from Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In July 1940 the minister defined the aim as denying the enemy, so far as possible, all goods and services that might aid his war effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The blockade leaked, and in March 1940 a member complained that loopholes in neutral trade were coming to light a tablespoonful at a time, despite assurances that it was virtually watertight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Germany had expected it and planned to secure food and oil by conquest, and once its armies held the coast from the North Cape to the Pyrenees, Britain extended navicerts to all seaborne goods bound for any European port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Germany waged its own economic war with submarines, and by one count the Battle of the Atlantic cost about 30,000 Allied seamen and 14.5 million gross tons of shipping against 28,000 German submarine crew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strategic bombing became the Allies' heaviest weapon, and after attacks on synthetic fuel plants began in May 1944 their output fell from 316,000 tons a month to 17,000 tons in September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Economic warfare proved a complement to fighting rather than a substitute for it, because its effects depended on how far the enemy could find substitutes and disperse and defend production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Harrison, “World War II: Economic Warfare,” lecture 10, EC224 War and Economy in the Twentieth Century, University of Warwick, 2018–19, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/war_and_economy/10_ww2_economic_warfare.pdf. [Open access]","US National Archives and Records Administration, “RG 84: Great Britain,” Holocaust-Era Assets finding aid, https://www.archives.gov/research/holocaust/finding-aid/civilian/rg-84-great-britian.html. [Open access]","Hugh Dalton, oral answers, “Economic Warfare,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 2 July 1940, vol. 362, cols. 656–57, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/02/economic-warfare. [Open access]","Hugh Dalton, statement, “Contraband Control (Navicerts),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 30 July 1940, vol. 363, cols. 1130–31, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jul/30/contraband-control-navicerts. [Open access]","Viscount Cecil of Chelwood, speech, “The War: Blockade of Germany,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Lords, 22 November 1939, vol. 114, cols. 1874–78, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1939/nov/22/the-war-blockade-of-germany. [Open access]","Colonel Baldwin-Webb, speech in the adjournment debate “Economic Warfare,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 18 March 1940, vol. 358, cols. 1773–80, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/mar/18/economic-warfare. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), “The German War Economy,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the blockade hurt Germany less than expected?","a":"Because Hitler expected it and planned to win security in food and oil through conquest, while trade through neutral countries left loopholes that critics said were admitted only a little at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain buy goods it did not need?","a":"Because pre-emptive purchases of strategic goods in neutral countries such as Turkey, Spain and Portugal kept those goods from falling into German hands, a practice that was greatly extended from the summer of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the attack on oil matter so much?","a":"Because German oil supply was tight throughout the war and controlled military operations; the synthetic plants were the only source of aviation fuel, and by late June 1944 Speer reported losses of it of up to 90 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Economic warfare"},
    "ww2-857": {"questions":["Warned in 1936 that one raid on Coventry might wreck the scheme, ministers kept each new <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside the motor industry, saying that moving it would defeat the object.","Paid £75 for each engine as a management fee, the firm running a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked a plant that the government owned outright, as the Air Ministry confirmed to the Commons in 1939."],"abstract":"A <b>shadow factory</b> was a plant built at British government expense in the years before and during the Second World War and run by an established firm, often a motor manufacturer, to add capacity for aircraft and aero-engines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The principles of a 'shadow' armament industry were set out by a small advisory group of industrialists appointed in December 1933 to assess the potential of industry for making arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Air Ministry launched projects with the firms in 1936, and in June that year it confirmed that one contract would go to Rootes Securities, whose group of motor firms would lend its staff to engine production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In February 1937 the government named the firms of the engine group, Austin, Bristol, Daimler, Rootes, Rover and Standard, with Austin and Rootes also building airframes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The firms earned management fees, £75 for each engine and £200 or £225 for each airframe once production began, while the state kept full ownership of the factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The factories formed part of the insurance layer of British policy, which combined appeasement with limited rearmament for deterrence and with preparations in case both failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They were first conceived as reserve war potential, but as the air programmes grew they had to be counted as additions to peacetime capacity, and still more capacity had to be laid down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In November 1936 a member warned that four of the firms might be destroyed by a single raid on Coventry, and the minister replied that building away from the centre of the motor industry would defeat the object of the scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By February 1939 about £12 million had been spent, and seven new factories were complete or nearly so besides the six of the engine group, under managers including Lord Nuffield and de Havilland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even so, money remained tight, and in May 1939 the extension of a propeller shadow factory was approved only after spending in dollars on its machinery had been drastically cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 2, “Early Rearmament, 1934–1938,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-2.html. [Open access]","Sir Philip Sassoon, oral answers, “Contract (Messrs. Rootes Securities, Limited),” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 17 June 1936, vol. 313, cols. 978–79, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1936/jun/17/contract-messrs-rootes-securities-limited. [Open access]","Sir Philip Sassoon, oral answer, “Air Armaments Programme,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 5 November 1936, vol. 317, cols. 248–49, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1936/nov/05/air-armaments-programme. [Open access]","Sir Thomas Inskip, reply, “Shadow Factories,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 17 February 1937, vol. 320, cols. 1178–80, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1937/feb/17/shadow-factories. [Open access]","Harold Balfour, oral answer, “Aircraft Factories,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Commons, 20 February 1939, vol. 344, cols. 35–36, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1939/feb/20/aircraft-factories. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “World War II: Economic Mobilization,” lecture 8, EC224 War and Economy in the Twentieth Century, University of Warwick, 2018–19, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/war_and_economy/08_ww2_mobilization.pdf. [Open access]","M. M. Postan, <i>British War Production</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1952), chap. 3, “From Peace to War, October 1938 to June 1940,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarProduction/UK-Civil-WarProduction-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were motor firms chosen to run the factories?","a":"Because the scheme was founded on the existing motor industry, whose manufacturing experience and technical staff could be enlisted for aero-engine production; Rootes, for example, controlled a group of well-known car firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the factories called shadows?","a":"Because they were first conceived as contributions to war potential, reserve capacity standing behind the regular aircraft industry, though as the air programmes grew they had to be counted as additions to peacetime capacity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain build them before the war began?","a":"Because British policy layered appeasement, limited rearmament for deterrence and insurance against both failing, and the shadow factories were part of that insurance, alongside study of the shortages of the Great War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"British shadow factories"},
    "ww2-858": {"questions":["Leaked to the Chicago Tribune and printed as 'F.D.R.'s War Plans' on 4 December 1941, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had projected an army of almost 8.8 million men.","Raising the sights for American aircraft output far beyond earlier schedules, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of September 1941 put heavier emphasis on the big bombers of the air forces."],"abstract":"The <b>Victory Program</b> was the estimate of the munitions and forces the United States would need to defeat its potential enemies, drawn up by the Army and Navy in the summer of 1941, months before the country entered the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 9 July 1941 President Roosevelt asked the secretaries of war and the navy to determine the ultimate production requirements for defeating all potential enemies, and on 30 August he added that Lend-Lease must be included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wanted the answer by 10 September, because the demands of supplying Britain under Lend-Lease had already thrown the army's own procurement into disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army's share was written by Major Albert Wedemeyer, whose plan became one part of the overall Army and Navy production estimate known collectively as the Victory Program.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American defence spending had been insignificant in proportion to national income as late as 1940, so this was a plan for mobilising an economy that had barely begun to rearm, yet by 1944 its output was nearly twice the 1938 level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Submitted in early September, Wedemeyer's estimate called for ground and air forces totalling 8,795,658 men, with a projected 215 divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For industry the Victory Program of September 1941 raised the sights for aircraft production far beyond anything yet scheduled, with heavier emphasis on the big bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its details leaked on 4 December 1941, when the isolationist Chicago Tribune published them under the headline 'F.D.R.'s War Plans', compromising the war plan on which they rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army reached a peak of 8,157,386 men in March 1945, very close to the 1941 estimate, although it never fielded more than 90 divisions instead of the 215 projected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By mid-1942 war contracts exceeded the value of the gross national product of 1941, and the aircraft programme alone eventually cost almost $45 billion of a munitions programme of $183 billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Charles E. Kirkpatrick, <i>An Unknown Future and a Doubtful Present: Writing the Victory Plan of 1941</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1992), chap. 2, “The Requirement,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-Victory/USA-Victory-2.html. [Open access]","Charles E. Kirkpatrick, <i>An Unknown Future and a Doubtful Present: Writing the Victory Plan of 1941</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1992), chap. 5, “Assessments,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-Victory/USA-Victory-5.html. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Resource Mobilization for World War II: The U.S.A., U.K., U.S.S.R., and Germany, 1938–1945,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 41, no. 2 (1988): 171–92, author’s postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/ehr88postprint.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), chap. 9, “Expansion of Aircraft Production,” 299–330, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-9.html. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s copy, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), chap. 10, “The Production Record,” 331–61, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-10.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Roosevelt order the estimate in 1941?","a":"Because Lend-Lease and the supply of Britain had disrupted the army's procurement, and the president wanted to know the ultimate production needed to defeat all potential enemies, including what Lend-Lease itself would require.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the army field fewer divisions than planned?","a":"Because the plan used an unrealistic figure for the men needed to support each division in the field, so the army could produce nowhere near 215 divisions and reached a peak of 90 in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the leak cause an uproar?","a":"Because the Chicago Tribune and other isolationist papers printed the plan's goal of 10 million armed men and a land drive against Germany by July 1943, exposing the war plan days before Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Victory Program"},
    "ww2-859": {"questions":["Built by Ford near Ypsilanti to an Albert Kahn design more than a mile long, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> plant applied the assembly lines of the car industry to the B-24 Liberator.","In 1944 the factory at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced more airframe weight than any other American plant, its peak monthly output equalling half that of the entire German airframe industry."],"abstract":"<b>Willow Run</b> was the bomber plant that the Ford Motor Company built near Ypsilanti, Michigan, from 1941 to mass-produce the B-24 Liberator, a four-engine heavy bomber designed by Consolidated Aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The government financed the plant, having chosen Ford in the autumn of 1940 to join Consolidated and Douglas in a pool of manufacturers producing the B-24.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Designed with the architect Albert Kahn, its main building was more than a mile long, with parallel assembly lines and an L shape that kept it out of neighbouring Wayne County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The site lay about 56 kilometres (35 miles) from Detroit, and at its peak the plant employed more than 42,000 people, about 35 per cent of them women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ford meant to apply the moving assembly line of the motor industry to aircraft, breaking the bomber down into 11 major assemblies and 69 subassemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Production started slowly, because the government's constant design changes clashed with Ford's fixed tooling, and only 56 aircraft were finished in 1942, so critics called the plant 'Will it Run'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In September 1943 the Materiel Command even suggested that the government take over the management of the plant, an idea dropped in favour of strong pressure from the War Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Output then climbed to a peak of 428 B-24s in August 1944, and Ford delivered 6,791 complete bombers between September 1942 and June 1945, plus 1,893 sets of parts for assembly elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1944 Willow Run produced about 42,000 tonnes (92.6 million pounds) of airframes, the largest total of any American plant, and its peak monthly output by weight was half that of the whole German airframe industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Detroit, where 91 per cent of the army's helmets were stamped and Chrysler built half the nation's tanks, came to be called the Arsenal of Democracy, and Willow Run became its emblem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), chap. 9, “Expansion of Aircraft Production,” 299–330, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-9.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), chap. 10, “The Production Record,” 331–61, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-10.html. [Open access]","The Henry Ford, “Willow Run Bomber Plant,” Digital Collections, https://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/sets/detail/willow-run-bomber-plant?AssetId=THF251440. [Open access]","Detroit Historical Society, “Willow Run,” Encyclopedia of Detroit, https://detroithistorical.org/learn/encyclopedia-of-detroit/willow-run. [Open access]","Detroit Historical Society, “Arsenal of Democracy,” Encyclopedia of Detroit, https://detroithistorical.org/learn/encyclopedia-of-detroit/arsenal-democracy. [Open access]","Brian Casserly, review of <i>Planning the Home Front: Building Bombers and Communities at Willow Run</i>, by Sarah Jo Peterson, <i>Michigan War Studies Review</i> 2014-032, https://michiganwarstudiesreview.com/2014-032.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Willow Run start so slowly?","a":"Because Ford's mass-production methods depended on a fixed design, and the government's constant changes to the B-24 forced costly retooling; Ford also needed more help from the aircraft industry than it had first expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a car maker asked to build bombers?","a":"Because heavy bomber production on the scale planned was too large for the original makers alone, so in the autumn of 1940 the Air Corps formed a pool in which Ford and Douglas joined Consolidated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the plant so hard to staff?","a":"Because it stood about 56 kilometres (35 miles) from Detroit with no highway or streetcar link and little housing, so Ford turned to underused Detroit labour, white women and Black men and women, while the government built dormitories and family housing nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Willow Run"},
    "ww2-860": {"questions":["Detroit, whose firms produced a quarter of everything the Allies used, came to be known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the name Roosevelt had given American industry in December 1940.","Promised in a fireside chat of 1940, the role of the United States as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on firmer ground once Lend-Lease removed Britain's need to pay in dollars in March 1941."],"abstract":"The <b>Arsenal of Democracy</b> was the phrase Franklin Roosevelt used in a radio broadcast on 29 December 1940 for the role he wanted American industry to play, arming the nations fighting the Axis while the United States stayed out of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He opened by saying that this was not a fireside chat on war, then argued that if Britain fell the Axis powers would control Europe, Asia, Africa and the high seas and could turn their strength against the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British, he said, asked for the implements of war, the planes, tanks, guns and freighters, and there was no demand for sending an American expeditionary force abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appealed to owners, managers and workers to produce munitions swiftly and without stint, declaring that 'we must be the great arsenal of democracy' in an emergency as serious as war itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phrase came to stand for the vast manufacturing capacity of the United States, and Detroit, whose firms produced a quarter of everything used by the Allies, was known by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Neutrality law let Britain buy arms only for cash, and with its money nearly gone, Congress passed the Lend-Lease Act 2 months after the broadcast, removing the dollar constraint and at first authorising $7 billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That December the union leader Walter Reuther proposed building 500 fighters a day in idle car plants, but in January 1941 the Office of Production Management chose not to curtail car production for 6 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The motor industry later stopped making civilian vehicles and turned to jeeps, tanks, bombers and ammunition, with 91 per cent of the army's helmets stamped in Detroit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ford's Willow Run plant, often taken as the embodiment of the arsenal of democracy, eventually built a B-24 bomber every 63 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The country made almost 300,000 military aircraft from July 1940 to August 1945, and by a 1946 estimate its munitions output rose from $1.5 billion in 1940 to $42 billion in 1944, against $17 billion for Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” radio address, 29 December 1940, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-9. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), chap. 9, “Expansion of Aircraft Production,” 299–330, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-9.html. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “World War II: Economic Mobilization,” lecture 8, EC224 War and Economy in the Twentieth Century, University of Warwick, 2018–19, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/war_and_economy/08_ww2_mobilization.pdf. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Resource Mobilization for World War II: The U.S.A., U.K., U.S.S.R., and Germany, 1938–1945,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 41, no. 2 (1988): 171–92, author’s postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/ehr88postprint.pdf. [Open access]","Detroit Historical Society, “Arsenal of Democracy,” Encyclopedia of Detroit, https://detroithistorical.org/learn/encyclopedia-of-detroit/arsenal-democracy. [Open access]","The Henry Ford, “Willow Run Bomber Plant,” Digital Collections, https://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/sets/detail/willow-run-bomber-plant?AssetId=THF251440. [Open access]","Charles E. Kirkpatrick, <i>An Unknown Future and a Doubtful Present: Writing the Victory Plan of 1941</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1992), chap. 2, “The Requirement,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-Victory/USA-Victory-2.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), chap. 10, “The Production Record,” 331–61, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-10.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Roosevelt say the broadcast was not about war?","a":"Because he argued that the surest way to keep the United States out of war was to help the nations defending themselves against the Axis now rather than wait to be attacked later, and he insisted no expeditionary force was being sought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain need Lend-Lease?","a":"Because American neutrality law allowed sales only for cash with the buyer responsible for shipping, so Britain had to export as well as rearm to earn dollars; the Lend-Lease Act of March 1941 removed that constraint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was car production not stopped at once?","a":"Because in January 1941 the Office of Production Management decided there should be no curtailment of car output in the first half of the year, and the large firms obtained new plants and machine tools instead of converting idle ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Arsenal of Democracy"},
    "ww2-871": {"questions":["Prepared since 1937, German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of fat, meat, butter, milk, cheese, sugar and jam began on 1 September 1939, and its shortfalls were later met by taking food from occupied Europe.","Britain's wartime planners counted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alongside taxation and saving as a way of curbing civilian spending, since controlled prices alone would only have produced empty shops and queues."],"abstract":"<b>Rationing</b> is the sharing out of scarce goods by a fixed allowance for each person rather than by price, and in the Second World War every major belligerent applied it to food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain's official history of its war economy counted it as one of three ways, with taxation and saving, of holding down civilian spending once the supply of consumer goods had been cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Without it, the same history argued, controlled prices would only have produced shortages in the shops that favoured people with the leisure to stand in queues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany had prepared its scheme since 1937, and from 1 September 1939 fat, meat, butter, milk, cheese, sugar and jam could be bought only with ration cards, with bread and eggs following on 25 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet Union, poorer and invaded, held its economy together through central planning, rationing and a procurement system that ensured farmers could not withhold food from the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Roosevelt told Americans in April 1942 that every scarce essential commodity must be rationed, one of seven points for holding down the cost of living, when tyres, petrol and sugar were already rationed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Britain, heavily dependent on imported food, expanded its home production of calories without any major breakdown of supply, and its official historian judged that the average diet was maintained and even improved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Germany the American bombing survey found food strictly rationed but nutritionally adequate throughout the war, with about the same calories as the British diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The difference came from occupied Europe, for the Nuremberg tribunal found that very large amounts of food were shipped to Germany from occupied Poland, and the campaign in the east aimed at the greatest possible quantity of food and oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Japan, which rationed food from April 1941 and imported about a fifth of its calories, saw average intake fall from about 2,000 calories a day before the war to about 1,680 by the summer of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["R. J. Hammond, <i>Food</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Growth of Policy</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1951), 114–15, 202–3, 369–70, https://archive.org/details/food-volume1. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 325–28, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-12.html. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Alltagsleben,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/alltagsleben. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s preprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, Judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” 28 April 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did governments ration food instead of letting prices rise?","a":"Because high prices and empty shops would both have shared out scarce food unfairly, the first by the size of a family's purse and the second by favouring people with the leisure to queue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain's planners treated rationing, taxation and saving together as the means of holding down the spending that rising wartime incomes made possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German civilians eat about as well as the British until late in the war?","a":"Because Germany took food from the lands it occupied: the Nuremberg tribunal found that large amounts were shipped from occupied Poland, and the campaign in the east was meant to deliver as much food and oil as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The German museum's account adds that the occupied territories were ruthlessly exploited to feed Germans and that death by starvation in eastern Europe was deliberately brought about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet food system hold where Russia's had collapsed in the First World War?","a":"Because the Soviet economy was held together by coercion, leadership, national feeling, central planning and rationing, and by a procurement system that stopped farmers withholding food from the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its size, its near self-sufficiency before the war and Allied aid also helped, though size alone had not saved Russia in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rationing"},
    "ww2-872": {"questions":["Selling each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to households rather than to banks both paid for the war and drained off spending power that full employment had created when shops had little left to sell.","Redeemable at any time for its price plus interest, the American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1941 to 1945 answered the losses of earlier buyers forced to sell Liberty Bonds on the open market."],"abstract":"A <b>war bond</b> is a loan from the public to a government at war, sold in small denominations so that ordinary savers can lend, and bought partly as an investment and partly as a patriotic act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Selling bonds to households both raised money for the war and soaked up spending power that full employment created when there was little in the shops to buy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In April 1941 Britain's chancellor put the inflationary gap at about £500 million and expected savings campaigns such as War Weapons Weeks to raise £200 million to £300 million more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Weekly sales of single savings certificates, he reported, had risen from 261,000 early in the campaign to 1,129,000 in early 1941, evidence that saving had reached the wage earner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Britain paid a rising share of its war spending out of revenue, from 39 per cent in 1940 to 55 per cent in 1944, and controlled the loan markets to borrow the rest at low and steady interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Roosevelt told Americans in April 1942, with war spending at about 100 million dollars a day, that putting more billions into war bonds was one of seven points for holding down prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The American Treasury had sold small savings bonds since 1935, redeemable at any time for their price plus interest, because many small buyers of Liberty Bonds had lost money selling them on the market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 1 May 1941 it launched the Series E bond, sold as the Defense Bond in 1941 and the War Bond from 1942 to 1945, promoted by volunteer bankers, publishers, business leaders and Hollywood entertainers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A recent study credits the bonds with funding slightly more than half of the American war effort, though buyers seem to have thought more of patriotism and their families at the front than of inflation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany took another path, paying for much of what it took from western Europe through credits in the occupied countries' own banks, to be settled only after a German victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Department of the Treasury, “The Beginning of the Savings Bonds Program,” TreasuryDirect, https://www.treasurydirect.gov/research-center/history-of-savings-bond/beginnings-of-savings-bond/. [Open access]","U.S. Department of the Treasury, “The Volunteer Program and Series E Savings Bonds,” TreasuryDirect, https://www.treasurydirect.gov/research-center/history-of-savings-bond/volunteer-program/. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 324–28, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-12.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 500–2, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-17.html. [Open access]","Nicholas Sambaluk, review of <i>The World War II Bond Campaign</i>, by Lawrence R. Samuel, H-War, H-Net Reviews, June 2026, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=61864. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Industrie und Wirtschaft,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft. [in German] [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Fireside Chat,” 28 April 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/fireside-chat-5. [Open access]","Kingsley Wood, “Savings and Taxation in 1941–42,” <i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 370 (7 April 1941), cols. 1322–25, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/apr/07/savings-and-taxation-in-1941-42. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did governments want ordinary people, not only banks, to buy war bonds?","a":"Because borrowing that could not be covered by taxes and savings was almost certain to be partly inflationary, while saving by households took money that would otherwise have chased scarce goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain's planners saw voluntary saving as a buffer between the state's needs and people's varied circumstances, and the American Treasury hoped bonds would curb inflation and build a habit of saving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were American savings bonds made impossible to sell on the market?","a":"Because many small buyers of Liberty Bonds in the First World War had lost money when personal circumstances forced them to sell on the market before maturity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The savings bond instead paid fixed interest and could be redeemed after a short holding period for its purchase price plus the interest it had earned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Britain not simply tax away the whole inflationary gap?","a":"Because indirect taxes high enough would have fallen heavily on near-necessities, while direct taxes beyond a certain point might drive people to sell capital or weaken the will to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wartime budgets therefore never raised income tax above its 1941 level, and voluntary savings had to fill much of the gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War bond"},
    "ww2-873": {"questions":["General Marshall threatened to give up his post unless the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chaired in Washington by Harry Hopkins, answered to the Combined Chiefs of Staff rather than to Roosevelt and Churchill directly.","China's foreign minister protested in June 1942 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cutting to nothing for July the monthly munitions allotment agreed in May, because air transport from India had failed."],"abstract":"The <b>Combined Munitions Assignments Board</b>, usually called the Munitions Assignments Board, was the Anglo-American body that divided the weapons produced by the United States and Britain among the Allied forces from 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was one of three combined boards announced by Roosevelt and Churchill on 26 January 1942, beside a Combined Raw Materials Board and a Combined Shipping Adjustment Board.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their joint statement declared that the entire munitions resources of the two countries would be deemed to be in a common pool, about which the fullest information would be interchanged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Committees under the Combined Chiefs of Staff in Washington and London, each with a civilian chairman, were to advise on assignments by strategic need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Combined Food Board and a Combined Production and Resources Board followed in June 1942, filling the gap left between raw materials and finished weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its form was settled at the White House on 14 January 1942, when General Marshall said he could not remain Chief of Staff unless it answered to the Combined Chiefs rather than to the two leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Harry Hopkins backed Marshall and became chairman in Washington, and the charter of 4 February 1942 made the board responsible for assigning finished war material to Britain, the United States and the other Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About forty claimants were split into a British group of the Empire and European Allies and an American group of Latin America and China, while Soviet supplies were settled separately in protocols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Smaller allies stood at the edge of the system, and in June 1942 China's foreign minister protested to Hopkins that a monthly assignment of munitions agreed in May was to be cut to nothing for July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As American output grew to four times Britain's by mid-1943 the balance tilted ever further towards Washington, and by the end of the war the common pool was little more than a figure of speech, though pooling had still made the Allied effort far more integrated than the Axis's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Statement on Raw Materials, Munition Assignments, and Shipping Adjustment Boards,” 26 January 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-raw-materials-munition-assignments-and-shipping-adjustment-boards. [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 247–54, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 390–97, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-13.html. [Open access]","Soong, Chinese Minister for Foreign Affairs, to Harry Hopkins, 25 June 1942, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conferences at Washington, 1941–1942, and Casablanca, 1943</i>, doc. 306, Office of the Historian, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1941-43/d306. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s preprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","Robert W. Coakley and Richard M. Leighton, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1943–1945</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 628–30, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1943-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Marshall insist the board answer to the military chiefs?","a":"Because he believed that no military organisation could take responsibility for operations if the supplies essential to them were not under its control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proposal he opposed would have made the board answer directly to Roosevelt and Churchill, with Hopkins and Beaverbrook as its two chairmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the claimants divided into a British and an American group?","a":"Because endless confusion would have arisen if each of some forty claimants could submit requirements in both capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most Empire and European allies used British-type weapons and fought beside British forces, while the Latin American republics and China fell naturally into the American group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the combined boards matter for the Allied economies?","a":"Because the division of British manpower between the forces and industry depended on knowing what weapons would come from the Washington pool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More broadly, the United States shared its capital-intensive resources with Britain and the Soviet Union, making the Allied war effort an integrated whole, while each Axis power relied on its own colonial sphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Combined Munitions Assignments Board"},
    "ww2-874": {"questions":["Admiral Emory Land, already building merchant ships as chairman of the Maritime Commission, became a shipping czar as well when he was made head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1942.","British requests for American cargo ships went to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which under a June 1942 agreement let the Army load freighters but reclaimed each one after a single outward voyage."],"abstract":"The <b>War Shipping Administration</b> was the American agency that controlled the country's merchant ships in the Second World War, established by Executive Order 9054 on 7 February 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was given control of the operation, purchase, charter, requisition and use of all ocean vessels under American flag or control, except the combatant ships and transports of the armed forces and coastal and inland shipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ships formed a pool that the administrator allocated to the Army, the Navy, other agencies and the governments of the United Nations, complying with strategic military requirements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also represented the United States in dealing with the British Ministry of War Transport, under an agreement of January 1942 that the two countries' shipping would in principle be pooled through Combined Shipping Adjustment Boards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The agency trained merchant crews as well as operating ships, and it was abolished on 1 September 1946, when the Maritime Administration took over its work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The American chiefs of staff wanted no civilian interference, but the President, persuaded by British arguments, put the country's merchant ships under this civilian authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The order made Admiral Emory S. Land, already chairman of the Maritime Commission that built the ships, the War Shipping Administrator responsible directly to the President, a 'shipping czar' as well as a 'ship construction czar'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Disputes over loading and over the length of allocations continued until an agreement of 13 June 1942 left cargo loading to the Army but returned each freighter to the agency's control after a single outward voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> British officials complained that the War Department resented its powers and made them largely ineffective, and at the Casablanca conference the American chiefs still refused to sit at the same table as its representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In practice the combined boards never became a single allied authority, and control of shipping stayed national, with Britain asking the War Shipping Administration for help in negotiations that were often hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Executive Order 9054—Establishing a War Shipping Administration in the Executive Office of the President and Defining Its Functions and Duties,” 7 February 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-9054-establishing-war-shipping-administration-the-executive-office-the. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Statement on Raw Materials, Munition Assignments, and Shipping Adjustment Boards,” 26 January 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-raw-materials-munition-assignments-and-shipping-adjustment-boards. [Open access]","Richard M. Leighton and Robert W. Coakley, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1940–1943</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 215–19, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1940-1943. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 419–29, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-14.html. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the War Shipping Administration [WSA],” Record Group 248, <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/248.html. [Open access]","C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i> (London: HMSO, 1955), 287–89, 330, https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army resist a single shipping authority run by the Navy?","a":"Because it wanted to keep its own transport fleet and ports of embarkation, and feared that a cabinet-level shipping chief might challenge the influence of the Secretary of War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Navy had even proposed that it should clear all Army requests for shipping, which the Army rejected out of hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the agency refuse long-term block allocations to the services?","a":"Because handing ships to the Army or Navy for long periods violated the pooling principle on which it worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The order that created it made its vessels a pool to be allocated among the Army, the Navy, other agencies and the Allied governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Anglo-American shipping never fully pooled?","a":"Because the combined boards did not become an international authority examining all Allied shipping, and each country kept control of its own ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Americans had to build a shipping administration almost from scratch, while the British were preoccupied with their own import programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War Shipping Administration"},
    "ww2-875": {"questions":["Chaired by the Federal Security Administrator, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> absorbed the United States Employment Service in 1942 and recruited and trained workers for war industries until its abolition in September 1945.","As the draft drained aircraft plants of young men, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recruited labour in a market where employers turned increasingly to women and Black workers."],"abstract":"The <b>War Manpower Commission</b> was the American agency created by Executive Order 9139 on 18 April 1942 to secure the most effective mobilisation and use of the nation's manpower for the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chaired by the Federal Security Administrator, it brought together the War, Navy, Agriculture and Labor departments, the War Production Board, the Selective Service System and the Civil Service Commission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chairman was to estimate the manpower needs of industry, review the claims of the forces, agriculture and civilian work, and direct government agencies on how the available labour should be allocated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It recruited and trained workers for war and essential civilian industries, took over the United States Employment Service in September 1942 and for a year had the Selective Service System under its control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was abolished on 19 September 1945, and most of its functions passed to the Department of Labor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The labour force of the American aircraft industry grew about twentyfold between January 1940 and December 1943, to almost 2.1 million, and as the draft took younger men the plants turned to women and Black Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The share of white married women aged 35 to 44 in the labour force rose from 13.8 per cent in 1940 to 25.3 per cent in 1950, though almost half of the women who entered work during the war had left by 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain resorted to compulsion, and Churchill told the Commons on 2 December 1941 that unmarried women aged 20 to 30 could be called up and given a choice between the auxiliary forces, civil defence and specified industrial work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its official history judged that this demanded the service of men and women on a scale that Britain's totalitarian enemies never dared ask of their own people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Germany only 300,000 more women were in work in September 1944 than in the summer of 1939, as the regime relied on forced labourers and prisoners of war, and the bombing survey found relatively few German women drawn into industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Executive Order 9139—Establishing the War Manpower Commission in the Executive Office of the President and Transferring and Coordinating Certain Functions to Facilitate the Mobilization and Utilization of Manpower,” 18 April 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/executive-order-9139-establishing-the-war-manpower-commission-the-executive-office-the. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the War Manpower Commission [WMC],” Record Group 211, <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/211.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), 344–46, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-10.html. [Open access]","Claudia Goldin, “The Role of World War II in the Rise of Women’s Work,” NBER Working Paper 3203 (Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research, 1989), 1–2, 9–10, https://doi.org/10.3386/w3203. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 312–14, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-11.html. [Open access]","Winston Churchill, “Maximum National Effort,” <i>Parliamentary Debates, Commons</i>, 5th ser., vol. 376 (2 December 1941), cols. 1009–95, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1941/dec/02/maximum-national-effort. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Alltagsleben,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/alltagsleben. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States create a manpower agency in 1942?","a":"Because the order creating it aimed to assure the most effective mobilisation and use of the nation's manpower, which meant weighing the claims of the forces, farms, industry and government against one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recruitment, training and the employment service had been scattered across several agencies, which it absorbed during 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did aircraft plants need so many new kinds of workers?","a":"Because the industry's labour force grew from perhaps 100,000 to almost 2.1 million in 4 years, while the draft took younger men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Assembly-line methods let the plants employ unskilled and semi-skilled workers, and women and Black Americans filled much of the gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few German women enter war work?","a":"Because the regime, fearing unrest and falling morale, avoided general labour obligations for women in the first war years and relied instead on forced labourers and prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The bombing survey found relatively few German women drawn into industry, fewer than in the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"War Manpower Commission"},
    "ww2-876": {"questions":["Albert Speer admitted at Nuremberg that as chief of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> he had relied on compulsory service to staff its building sites in the occupied countries, such as the Atlantic Wall.","The highest death rate in Alderney's camps came in late 1942 among eastern European labourers employed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to fortify the Channel Island for the German war effort."],"abstract":"The <b>Organisation Todt</b> was Nazi Germany's military construction organisation, formed in May 1938 when Hitler gave Fritz Todt, the builder of the motorways, the task of building the West Wall fortifications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from building administrations, private firms and labour service units into the most important wartime organisation outside the Wehrmacht and the SS, though no law or decree ever established it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the occupied countries it built U-boat bunkers on the French coast from 1940 and, from December 1941, the Atlantic Wall, along with roads, factories, bridges and the launch sites for the V-weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Todt's death in February 1942 Albert Speer took over both the organisation and Todt's armaments ministry, and from 1943 it repaired bomb damage and built underground armament and fuel plants inside Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of 1944 it had about 1,360,000 workers, of whom only about 60,000 were Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At first it relied largely on volunteers recruited in western Europe, but from 1943 forced labourers and prisoners of war worked its sites in the harshest conditions, within a system of forced labour in which many died of ill-treatment, disease and starvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal found that Speer admitted relying on compulsory service to staff it, and that the deportation of foreign workers had been planned as an integral part of the German war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That economy employed more than 7.5 million foreign workers by late 1944, about a fifth of all those employed and half of them on farms, and could not have continued the war from 1942 without them and the prisoners and camp inmates beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On Alderney, where the organisation first ran four labour camps for workers of about 30 nationalities, the highest death rate came in late 1942 among the eastern Europeans it employed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A British review of 2024 put the likely deaths on the island at between 641 and 1,027, rejecting claims of many thousands, and found eastern Europeans treated far worse than western workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Thoms, “Die ‘Organisation Todt’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 17 September 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/ns-organisationen/organisation-todt. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, Judgment, “Speer,” 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judspeer.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, Judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Zwangsarbeit,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 August 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft/zwangsarbeit. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Forced Labor: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/forced-labor-an-overview. [Open access]","Alderney Expert Review Panel, <i>The Lord Pickles Alderney Expert Review</i> (London: Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office, May 2024), 4, 9–11, https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6656f459dc15efdddf1a84bb/Lord_Pickles-Alderney_expert_review.pdf. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Industrie und Wirtschaft,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the organisation turn to forced labour?","a":"Because its huge building programmes in the occupied countries needed far more workers than volunteers from western Europe could supply, and from 1943 forced labourers and prisoners of war were put to work on its sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speer admitted relying on compulsory service to keep it staffed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany depend so heavily on foreign workers?","a":"Because the war drew millions of German men into the forces, and the regime filled the shortage by conscripting foreign workers rather than mobilising German women on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Without millions of forced labourers, prisoners of war and camp inmates, the German museum's account concludes, the war could not have continued from 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Alderney review revise the island's death toll?","a":"Because claims of many thousands of deaths, and of a 'mini-Auschwitz' on the island, were not supported by the evidence the panel examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It put the likely toll at between 641 and 1,027, well above the 389 counted after liberation, and its sponsor warned that exaggerating numbers is also a form of distortion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Organisation Todt"},
    "ww2-877": {"questions":["Plants of the de Wendel concern in Lorraine were allotted to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on terms that judges at Nuremberg later held to violate the Hague Conventions.","Paul Pleiger, a dominant figure in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from its founding, also ran the corporation set up in 1941 to exploit the mines of Nikopol, Krivoi Rog and the Donets Basin."],"abstract":"The <b>Reichswerke Hermann Göring</b> were a German state-owned combine founded in 1937 to mine and smelt iron ore, which grew in the war into Europe's largest enterprise by absorbing foreign concerns by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Up to a million people worked for it, among them tens of thousands of forced labourers and concentration camp prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its driving force was Paul Pleiger, on its managing board from 1937 and a dominant figure in its mining and steel companies until the war's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Göring, as Plenipotentiary for the Four Year Plan, was in theory and in practice the economic dictator of the Reich, and his deputy in the plan chaired the combine's supervisory boards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It served the aim of a self-sufficient continental economy under German leadership, in which plunder of conquered lands, above all in the Soviet Union, was to make good Germany's lack of resources for a long war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It received property seized in occupied Poland through the Main Trustee Office East and plants of the de Wendel concern in Lorraine, on terms the Nuremberg judges held to breach the Hague Conventions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In August 1941 Pleiger was given charge of the Berg- und Hüttenwerksgesellschaft Ost, a corporation created to exploit the occupied Soviet territories, which concentrated on the manganese of Nikopol, the iron of Krivoi Rog and the coal and ore of the Donets Basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Göring's 'Green Folder' for the east, the Nuremberg tribunal found, contemplated abandoning industry in regions short of food and diverting food from the surplus regions to German needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the West exploitation kept a pretence of payment, but goods sent to Germany were in effect paid for by the occupied countries themselves, through excessive occupation costs or credits on clearing accounts that were accounts in name only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Occupation drew 30 to 40 per cent of the national product of France, the Netherlands and Norway, far more than the poor agrarian east yielded, as the industry of France, the Low Countries, Poland and Czechoslovakia was harnessed to Germany's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, “Industrie und Wirtschaft,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft. [in German] [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 14, <i>The Ministries Case</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office), 728–32, 736–44, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XIV/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XIV.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, Judgment, “Goering,” 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judgoeri.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, Judgment, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s preprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany seize foreign industry rather than simply buy from it?","a":"Because the regime and German industry expected conquest to supply the raw materials and food for a self-sufficient large-area economy under German control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Poland Göring laid down that enterprises not required for the bare existence of the population must be transferred to Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did occupied France yield more than the occupied Soviet Union?","a":"Because exploitation worked best in rich, industrialised economies, and France gave Germany as much food as all of the occupied Soviet Union and more industrial materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wherever the Axis conquered poorer, agrarian lands, incomes fell and extracting resources grew harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the occupied countries end up paying for their own exports?","a":"Because Germany paid for the goods with money raised from the occupied countries themselves, through excessive occupation costs or forced loans recorded as credits on clearing accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In most of the occupied East even this pretence was dropped, and economic exploitation became deliberate plunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reichswerke Hermann Göring"},
    "ww2-878": {"questions":["Kaiser's Richmond yards brought <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to shipbuilding by preassembling whole sections of ships, finishing them in two-thirds of the usual time at a quarter of the cost.","Slow to see the advantages of standardised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, German industry kept craft methods early in the war while American factories outbuilt it in aircraft more than twice over by 1944."],"abstract":"<b>Mass production</b> is the making of standardised goods in great quantities by breaking work into simple repeated steps, with interchangeable parts, specialised machine tools and moving assembly lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before 1939 American aircraft were built largely by hand, assembled from the floor up by highly skilled mechanics, because orders were small and designs changed constantly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1940 manufacturers adopted the methods of the motor industry, and by 1944 moving assembly lines turned out aircraft at rates measured in hours rather than days or weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ford's Willow Run plant for the B-24 bomber struggled at first to adapt car methods to airframes, but in 1944 and 1945 it became the prime example of mass-produced aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In shipbuilding, Kaiser's Richmond yards preassembled whole sections of ships and finished them in two-thirds of the time and at a quarter of the cost of other yards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In January 1942 Roosevelt set goals of 60,000 aircraft and 45,000 tanks for that year, so that Allied superiority in munitions would be overwhelming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the years of most intense fighting, 1942 to 1944, Allied factories outproduced the Axis by at least five to two in rifles and combat aircraft, three to one in guns and machine guns, and five to one in tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American aircraft output rose from 2,141 in 1939 to 96,318 in 1944, against 39,807 for Germany and 28,180 for Japan, whose monthly output peaked at 2,572 in September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> German industry, rooted in a craft tradition and slow to see the advantages of standardised mass production, raised its armaments index from 100 to 322 between early 1942 and July 1944 under Albert Speer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bombing survey judged that rise a sign of earlier undermobilisation, and an American planner's estimate put American munitions output in 1944 at 42 billion dollars against 17 billion for Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), 331–33, 349–50, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-10.html. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Historic Richmond Shipyards,” Rosie the Riveter WWII Home Front National Historical Park, https://www.nps.gov/rori/learn/historyculture/historic-richmond-shipyards.htm. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s preprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Industrie und Wirtschaft,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “State of the Union Address,” 6 January 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/state-the-union-address-1. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Resource Mobilization for World War II: The U.S.A., U.K., U.S.S.R., and Germany, 1938–1945,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 41, no. 2 (1988): 171–92, author’s postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ehr88postprint.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did mass production let America use unskilled workers?","a":"Because assembly lines and precision tools broke skilled work into specialised tasks, so that large pools of unskilled labour could be employed who could never have been trained in time for the old methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the shipyards narrow job classifications let a newcomer advance from trainee to journeyman within months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the Allies win by quantity rather than quality?","a":"Because the Allies knew they could not make better soldiers, guns, ships or aircraft than the Germans or Japanese, but they could make more of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Until the atomic bomb was available there was no alternative to quantity, and in the west the Axis could only be beaten by an immense numerical advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German output rise so steeply after 1942?","a":"Because Speer's ministry restructured and rationalised production, raising the armaments index more than threefold by July 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bombing survey judged that the rise reflected how undermobilised German industry had been, rather than the efficiency of dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mass production"},
    "ww2-879": {"questions":["By 1944 the Allied great powers' combined <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was about 3.3 times that of the Axis, up from twice it in 1941, as American output neared double its prewar level.","Only on the eastern front was the war closely balanced, because the Soviet Union's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was roughly equal to Germany's before the invasion despite its much larger population."],"abstract":"<b>Gross domestic product</b>, or GDP, is the value of the final goods and services produced in an economy over a period, the standard measure of a country's total output.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Figures in real terms are adjusted to remove the effect of inflation, so that output in different years can be compared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparing countries is harder, because each economy's product must be valued in a common set of prices, and estimates for the war years are general ratios rather than exact measurements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For a state at war, GDP and population set the upper limit on the production and personnel it could put into the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1945 the American government itself proposed the share of national income devoted to the war as the best financial measure of each ally's effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Within the frontiers of 1938 the Allies and their empires had more than 1,000 billion dollars of real product against about 750 billion for the Axis, an advantage of 1.4 to 1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Counting the great powers as they were aligned in 1942, Allied GDP was 2.4 times the Axis figure before the war, fell to twice it in 1941 and reached 3.3 times by 1944, as American output rose to nearly double its 1938 level and its industry reached four times British production by mid-1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Only on the eastern front was the balance close, since Soviet GDP was roughly equal to Germany's before the war, fell well behind in 1942 and regained only a narrow lead from 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan's economy had only about a tenth of the American one's potential, enough for a short war, and by 1946 an American planner was arguing that the Allies won by producing vastly more munitions than the Axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German industry as a whole grew little, its index rising only from 100 to 106 between 1938 and 1942 while armaments rose to 320, and the bombing survey found German arms production in 1940 and 1941 generally below Britain's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis, “Gross Domestic Product,” Learning Center, https://www.bea.gov/resources/learning-center/what-to-know-gdp. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s preprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Resource Mobilization for World War II: The U.S.A., U.K., U.S.S.R., and Germany, 1938–1945,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 41, no. 2 (1988): 171–92, author’s postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ehr88postprint.pdf. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Industrie und Wirtschaft,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","W. K. Hancock and M. M. Gowing, <i>British War Economy</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Civil Series (London: HMSO, 1949), 368–70, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Civil-WarEcon/UK-Civil-WarEcon-13.html. [Open access]","Robert W. Coakley and Richard M. Leighton, <i>Global Logistics and Strategy, 1943–1945</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War Department (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 628–30, https://archive.org/details/GlobalLogisticsAndStrategy1943-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a larger GDP matter more than a larger population?","a":"Because a higher output per head brought the commercial, transport and administrative strengths that let an economy mobilise without breaking down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poor countries under massive attack tended to disintegrate, as China did after 1937, so a rich coalition held a double advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the balance swing against the Allies in 1941?","a":"Because the Axis economies expanded while the resources of defeated France became available to Germany, and Soviet output began to fall under German attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1942 American mobilisation, Soviet recovery and Italy's defeat turned the ratio steadily in the Allies' favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did people at the time see production as decisive?","a":"Because an American planner's balance sheet of 1946 showed the Allies overcoming an initial disadvantage to produce vastly more munitions than the Axis, and he concluded that this was what won the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japan, with about a tenth of America's economic potential, could support only a short war or one of limited liabilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Gross domestic product"},
    "ww2-880": {"questions":["After 1942 the struggle turned into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which rising losses and force levels made Allied output and population count for more than Axis fighting quality.","Expecting short campaigns, the German war economy of 1939 lacked the resources for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and after the United States entered the war its arms race could no longer be won."],"abstract":"<b>Attrition warfare</b> is a strategy of wearing an enemy down through continuous losses of men and materiel until it can no longer fight, rather than seeking a quick decision by manoeuvre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a war of attrition the opposing forces grind each other down with rising force levels and rising losses, and superior fighting qualities count for less than greater output and population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It favours the side able to take risks, absorb the cost of its mistakes and replace what it loses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For that reason Prussia and later Germany, surrounded by states that outweighed them in manpower and resources, sought short, sharp wars won by decisive battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word 'Blitzkrieg' itself first circulated in the 1930s to describe a rapid victory, as the opposite of the long war of attrition that had ended in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> German planning in 1939 assumed short campaigns because the Reich lacked the resources for drawn-out wars of attrition, and once the United States entered the war the arms race could no longer be won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt aimed from January 1942 at a superiority in munitions so overwhelming that the Axis could never catch up, and Japan, with about a tenth of America's economic potential, could sustain only a short war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In one widely held view the war fell into two periods, with military qualities winning the Axis its victories until about 1942 and economic weight deciding the war of attrition that followed, as an American planner had argued in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The economic case is not the whole story, since German and Japanese fighting power forced the Allies to build up a vast numerical superiority first, and the Red Army beat Germany with a smaller margin than the western Allies needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bombing added to the attrition, and the American survey concluded that by early 1945 Germany's economy was disintegrating and its armies would have had to stop any effective fighting within a few months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Harrison, “The Economics of World War II: An Overview,” in <i>The Economics of World War II: Six Great Powers in International Comparison</i>, ed. Mark Harrison (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1998), 1–42, author’s preprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ww2overview1998.pdf. [Open access]","Robert Citino, “The War Hitler Won: The Battle for Europe, 1939–1941,” <i>Journal of Military and Strategic Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2011): 5–6, https://jmss.org/article/view/58008. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, “Industrie und Wirtschaft,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 13 May 2015, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Resource Mobilization for World War II: The U.S.A., U.K., U.S.S.R., and Germany, 1938–1945,” <i>Economic History Review</i> 41, no. 2 (1988): 171–92, author’s postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/soc/economics/staff/mharrison/public/ehr88postprint.pdf. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “State of the Union Address,” 6 January 1942, The American Presidency Project, ed. Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/state-the-union-address-1. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Axis win so much early on despite smaller economies?","a":"Because from 1939 to 1941 Germany and Japan had superior fighting power, surprise, speed and skill in concentrating their forces, which let them defeat economically stronger enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their leaders saw these military qualities as a substitute for productive power and a way to neutralise the enemy's advantage in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the Soviet Union win with a smaller margin than the western Allies?","a":"Because the Red Army displayed growing fighting power of its own, so it could beat Germany with a smaller quantitative edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the west, by contrast, the Axis could only be beaten by an immense numerical advantage, which the British and Americans built up in 1942 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Germany's leaders prefer short wars?","a":"Because Germany, like Prussia before it, was outweighed by its neighbours in manpower and resources and could not expect to win long wars of attrition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German museum's account adds that in 1939 the Reich lacked the resources for drawn-out wars, which is why its campaigns were planned to be short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Attrition warfare"},
    "ww2-881": {"questions":["Roosevelt praised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in November 1944 as a unique experiment in teamwork, and its Committee on Medical Research had backed penicillin, DDT and blood fractionation.","The Radiation Laboratory at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology was the largest single activity of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and of the research committee that Vannevar Bush had chaired from 1940."],"abstract":"The <b>Office of Scientific Research and Development</b> was the American agency that organised civilian scientists to improve weapons and medicine in the Second World War, created by Executive Order 8807 on 28 June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the National Defense Research Committee, set up on 27 June 1940 under Vannevar Bush of the Carnegie Institution of Washington, with the presidents of Harvard and the Massachusetts Institute of Technology among its members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea was to reverse the usual order, letting scientists tell the services what had become possible instead of waiting for officers to ask for things they did not know existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rather than build government laboratories, it signed contracts with universities and firms, and the largest, the Radiation Laboratory at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, was founded because no existing laboratory could take on microwave radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In their five busiest years the two bodies contracted for more than 500 million dollars of work, against the 10 million first requested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its weapons work included the M-9 gun director, the SCR-584 radar and the proximity fuze, which together helped to shoot down the flying bombs aimed at London in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bush credited its Committee on Medical Research with the development of penicillin and DDT, with blood fractionation and with standardising the treatment of malaria with atabrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its uranium section, inherited from an advisory committee of 1939, carried the first American work towards an atomic bomb until the Army's Manhattan Engineer District took over its contracts on 31 March 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Army's ordnance history judged that enlisting civilian scientists spared the United States the lack of co-ordination that cost Germany the fruits of its science, though relations were not always cordial, and the committee twice refused Air Corps requests to take on jet propulsion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In November 1944 Roosevelt called the office a unique experiment in teamwork, Bush proposed that the partnership continue in peacetime, and the office was abolished with effect from 31 December 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Irvin Stewart, <i>Organizing Scientific Research for War: The Administrative History of the Office of Scientific Research and Development</i>, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 7–8, 23–26, 35–36, https://archive.org/details/organizingscient00stew. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the Office of Scientific Research and Development [OSRD],” Record Group 227, <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/227.html. [Open access]","Vannevar Bush, <i>Science, the Endless Frontier: A Report to the President</i> (Washington, DC, July 1945; repr. Alexandria, VA: National Science Foundation, 2020), with Franklin D. Roosevelt’s letter of 17 November 1944, https://nsf-gov-resources.nsf.gov/2023-04/EndlessFrontier75th_w.pdf. [Open access]","Richard C. Tolman, foreword to <i>New Weapons for Air Warfare: Fire-Control Equipment, Proximity Fuzes, and Guided Missiles</i>, ed. Joseph C. Boyce, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), ix–xi, https://archive.org/details/NewWeaponsForAirWarfareOSRDBoyce. [Open access]","Constance McLaughlin Green, Harry C. Thomson and Peter C. Roots, <i>The Ordnance Department: Planning Munitions for War</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 218, 227–28, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-9. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), ch. 7, “The Quest for Better Weapons,” 246–47, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the office contract its work out instead of building its own laboratories?","a":"Because the committee that preceded it was authorised from the start to make contracts with individuals, educational institutions and industrial firms, where the scientists it needed already worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its surveys found no government or commercial laboratory equipped for microwave radar, so that work went to the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army's ordnance history adds that handing long-range research to civilian scientists spared the United States the confusion that hurt German research after 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did spending grow so far beyond the first estimate?","a":"Because after Pearl Harbor the emphasis shifted from research to development, and turning laboratory ideas into equipment for the services cost far more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The committee asked for 10 million dollars in 1940 and received about 6.5 million for its first year, yet over its five busiest years it and the office contracted for more than 500 million dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Roosevelt ask Bush for a report in 1944?","a":"Because he wanted the lessons of the wartime experiment used in peace: his letter of 17 November 1944 asked how the office's information, techniques and research experience could improve national health, create new enterprises and jobs and raise the standard of living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bush answered in July 1945 that only government could undertake secret, expensive military research, and that civilian scientists should keep a share of it in peacetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Office of Scientific Research and Development"},
    "ww2-882": {"questions":["The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as a United States Navy coinage, while the British spoke of radiolocation and RDF for the same pulse-and-echo way of locating distant objects.","German U-boats carried the Metox receiver from late 1942 to detect Allied metre-wave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> transmissions, until 10-centimetre sets that Metox could not hear arrived early in 1943."],"abstract":"<b>Radar</b> is the technique of detecting distant objects by sending out pulses of radio waves and timing their echoes, which return within millionths of a second, to find a target's bearing and range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nikola Tesla foresaw the principle in 1900 and Christian Hülsmeyer patented a crude form of it in 1904, but practical sets were developed in secret in several countries at once during the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It went by many names, among them the British radiolocation and RDF, until the United States Navy's coinage radar, from radio, detection and ranging, became standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long-wave sets with huge aerials, such as Britain's Chain Home towers, gave early warning of aircraft, while microwave sets, which in the United States had to start almost from scratch, needed far smaller aerials and could pick up far smaller objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The official history of the American air forces called its use for navigation and target identification the most significant advance of the war years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Its most famous American failure came at Opana on Oahu on 7 December 1941, where an Army SCR-270 set picked up a large flight of aircraft about 212 kilometres (132 miles) away at 7:02 AM.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The officer at the information centre, believing them to be American bombers expected from the mainland, told the operators to forget it, and the Japanese attack began at 7.55.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At sea the contest became a race of measure and countermeasure, and from about October 1942 U-boats carried the Metox receiver, which detected Allied metre-wave radar transmissions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Allies answered about February 1943 with 10-centimetre radar, which Metox could not detect, and the Germans later counted airborne microwave radar the principal factor in the defeat of their submarines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vannevar Bush's report of July 1945 warned that the new eyes radar had given the fighting forces could be blinded by countermeasures, and noted that many of its leaders had studied the atomic nucleus before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Dulany Terrett, <i>The Signal Corps: The Emergency (To December 1941)</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1956), 40–41, 194–96, https://archive.org/details/SignalCorpsTheEmergency. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1956), 205, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]","George Raynor Thompson, Dixie R. Harris, Pauline M. Oakes and Dulany Terrett, <i>The Signal Corps: The Test (December 1941 to July 1943)</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 3–5, https://archive.org/details/TheSignalCorpsTheTest. [Open access]","Irvin Stewart, <i>Organizing Scientific Research for War: The Administrative History of the Office of Scientific Research and Development</i>, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 23–25, https://archive.org/details/organizingscient00stew. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, Operations Evaluation Group Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, Navy Department, 1946), 40–41, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II, vol. 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 52–53, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), ch. 6, “AAF Aircraft of World War II,” 193–94, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-6.html. [Open access]","Vannevar Bush, <i>Science, the Endless Frontier: A Report to the President</i> (Washington, DC, July 1945; repr. Alexandria, VA: National Science Foundation, 2020), https://nsf-gov-resources.nsf.gov/2023-04/EndlessFrontier75th_w.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the warning from Opana ignored?","a":"Because the duty officer had heard that a flight of B-17 bombers was due from the mainland that morning, and he assumed the echoes were those bombers or other American aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The radar crew kept tracking the flight until it was lost among echoes from the surrounding mountains about 32 kilometres (20 miles) out, and the attack began minutes later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did 10-centimetre radar take the U-boats by surprise?","a":"Because Metox was designed for metre waves and could not pick up the shorter transmissions, made possible by the British magnetron of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Suspecting other causes, the Germans tried new receivers, and only in September 1943, months after the Luftwaffe had captured a 10-centimetre bombing radar, did the U-boat command recognise what was finding its boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By then the new sets were flying in Allied antisubmarine aircraft on both sides of the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Bush call the U-boat war a battle of techniques?","a":"Because every new detecting device quickly drew countermeasures that could blind it, and he judged that the Allied margin of success against the U-boat had been dangerously small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From this he argued that the United States could not again rely on allies to hold off an enemy while it caught up, and that military research must continue in peacetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Radar"},
    "ww2-883": {"questions":["German Metox receivers could not hear the 10-centimetre radar that the British <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had made practical, and Allied air attacks on U-boats rose sharply once it arrived in 1943.","A team at the University of Birmingham developed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an electronic tube producing microwaves with enough force to put precise radar into night fighters and antisubmarine aircraft."],"abstract":"The <b>cavity magnetron</b> is an electronic vacuum tube that generates powerful microwaves, and in the Second World War it became the heart of the transmitter in Allied microwave radar, with the klystron serving the receiver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earlier magnetrons, including a split-plate tube studied in Japan in 1928 and a 10-centimetre tube made by Westinghouse in 1931 and 1932, had produced very short waves but very little power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A research team at the University of Birmingham developed a multicavity magnetron that produced microwaves with force, and an American naval study dates the British strapped magnetron to the spring of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Short waves needed only short aerials, so the tube made it possible to put precise radar into night fighters and antisubmarine aircraft, where the long aerials of metre-wave sets were out of the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Waves under 50 centimetres also gave greater accuracy and definition, were harder to jam and saved weight and space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A model of the tube crossed the Atlantic with the British mission led by Sir Henry Tizard, which reached the United States late in August 1940, one of several British secrets the Americans received in 1940 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bell Telephone Laboratories made the first American version in the closing months of 1940, while the National Defense Research Committee founded the Radiation Laboratory at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology to develop microwave radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The laboratory had a 10-centimetre airborne interception set working early in 1941, and by July 1942 American antisubmarine aircraft were flying with 10-centimetre radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Metox receivers of the U-boats could not detect the new wavelength, and the number of air attacks on U-boats rose sharply after its arrival in early 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One American naval history repeated the description of the magnetron as the most valuable cargo ever brought to American shores, while the Army's Signal Corps history dismissed that phrase as written in an unguarded moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Dulany Terrett, <i>The Signal Corps: The Emergency (To December 1941)</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1956), 192–97, https://archive.org/details/SignalCorpsTheEmergency. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II, vol. 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 52–53, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, Operations Evaluation Group Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, Navy Department, 1946), 40–41, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","George Raynor Thompson, Dixie R. Harris, Pauline M. Oakes and Dulany Terrett, <i>The Signal Corps: The Test (December 1941 to July 1943)</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1957), 86–87, https://archive.org/details/TheSignalCorpsTheTest. [Open access]","Irvin Stewart, <i>Organizing Scientific Research for War: The Administrative History of the Office of Scientific Research and Development</i>, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 23–25, https://archive.org/details/organizingscient00stew. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), ch. 7, “The Quest for Better Weapons,” 238–39, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-7.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain hand over such a secret in 1940?","a":"Because it needed American factories and laboratories: the Signal Corps history describes the haste and deep anxiety with which the Tizard Mission was sent to obtain mass production of the tube in the United States and to set American research organisations working on its uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both countries held some secrets back, but the American air forces' history judged the exchange notable for its large measure of success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did short waves matter so much in aircraft?","a":"Because a radar's aerial grows with its wavelength: the British airborne sets of 1940 worked on waves of a metre and a half and needed long external aerials, while a fighter needed a small aerial and a narrow beam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 10-centimetre SCR-520 used a compact dish-like transmitter, though early models weighed about 270 kilograms (600 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Signal Corps history sceptical of the praise?","a":"Because both nations already had long-wave radar, and longer waves stayed better for some tasks: the 11-metre Chain Home stations detected the German rockets of 1944 and 1945 better than microwave sets could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The naval history, by contrast, reported that the Germans themselves considered airborne microwave radar the principal factor in the defeat of the U-boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cavity magnetron"},
    "ww2-884": {"questions":["Between 19 and 31 May 1944 the destroyer escort USS England sank five Japanese submarines, using the ahead-throwing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as her primary weapon northeast of the Admiralty Islands.","British figures show the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> succeeding in 7.5 per cent of attacks in 1943 but in over 20 per cent by late 1944, a rise put down to training rather than new equipment."],"abstract":"<b>Hedgehog</b> was a British anti-submarine weapon, a multi-spigot mortar that threw 24 contact-fused bombs ahead of an escort ship to land in a ring centred some 230 metres (250 yards) in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It answered a basic flaw of the depth charge, for an escort's echo-ranging gear lost contact with a submerged submarine as the ship ran in over it, leaving a blind interval before charges could be dropped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thrown while contact still held, its bombs cut that blind time from 45 to 90 seconds to 15 to 20, and because they burst only on striking the hull they searched every depth, which mattered when the gear could not measure depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Admiralty revived an earlier idea for such a mortar, and by the end of 1941 Hedgehog was being manufactured, though it never displaced the depth charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States Navy adopted it in 1942, and from March that year its charges were filled with Torpex, an explosive about 1.7 times as powerful as TNT for its volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its early results disappointed, partly because a miss gave none of the shock of a depth-charge pattern, and British figures put its success at 7.5 per cent of attacks in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With training the rate rose above 20 per cent by late 1944, more than three times that of depth charges in the same incidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the convoy battles of May 1943, when their U-boats suffered grave losses, the Germans became aware and apprehensive of new weapons such as Hedgehog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its most famous success came in the Pacific, where the destroyer escort USS England, using it as her main weapon, sank five Japanese submarines and shared in a sixth between 19 and 31 May 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American naval history judged it more successful in the Pacific than in the Atlantic, perhaps because of better weather, and Vannevar Bush called the whole struggle against the submarine a battle of scientific techniques won by a dangerously small margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, Operations Evaluation Group Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Naval Operations, Navy Department, 1946), 21–22, 30–31, 52–53, 123–25, https://archive.org/details/antisubmarinewar51ster. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Defensive</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1954), 479–80, https://archive.org/details/worldat-sea-1939-1945-vol-1. [Open access]","S. W. Roskill, <i>The War at Sea, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Period of Balance</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1956), 375, https://archive.org/details/war-at-sea-1939-1945-vol-2. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Battle of the Atlantic, September 1939–May 1943</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II, vol. 1 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 211–12, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-i-the-battle-of-the-atlantic-september-1939-may-1943. [Open access]","Samuel Eliot Morison, <i>The Atlantic Battle Won, May 1943–May 1945</i>, History of United States Naval Operations in World War II, vol. 10 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1956), 52, https://archive.org/details/husno-volume-x-the-atlantic-battle-won-may-1943-may-1945. [Open access]","Vannevar Bush, <i>Science, the Endless Frontier: A Report to the President</i> (Washington, DC, July 1945; repr. Alexandria, VA: National Science Foundation, 2020), https://nsf-gov-resources.nsf.gov/2023-04/EndlessFrontier75th_w.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why throw the charges ahead of the ship at all?","a":"Because sound contact with a submerged submarine was always lost some 90 to 140 metres (100 to 150 yards) from it, as it passed under the beam, so a depth charge rolled off the stern was partly a guess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Firing ahead while the target was still held meant the ship could aim, and a miss left no disturbed water to spoil the search for the submarine afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Hedgehog not replace the depth charge?","a":"Because the depth charge kept improving: the streamlined, fast-sinking Mark-9 of summer 1943 could be set as deep as 180 metres (600 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A large depth-charge explosion could also shake a U-boat badly when it missed, which contact-fused bombs could not do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hedgehog's weight and severe recoil kept it off smaller craft, which were given the rocket-thrown Mousetrap instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did its success rate climb so steeply?","a":"Because crews learned to use it: the analysts noted no radical change in the sound gear or in German evasive tactics over the period, and ascribed the rise largely to training.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early theoretical predictions had also been too optimistic, and after a year of use the predictions and the results came to agree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Hedgehog (weapon)"},
    "ww2-885": {"questions":["Flying in low by night with incendiaries on 9 and 10 March 1945, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bombers of XXI Bomber Command burned out about 41 square kilometres (15.8 square miles) of Tokyo.","The Enola Gay, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that dropped the atomic bomb on Hiroshima on 6 August 1945, flew as the weather reconnaissance aircraft for the Nagasaki mission 3 days later."],"abstract":"The <b>B-29 Superfortress</b> was a four-engined American heavy bomber built by Boeing, the largest bombardment plane used by any belligerent in the Second World War and the first bomber to house its crew in pressurised compartments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Air Corps issued its requirement in February 1940, contracts for experimental models followed on 6 September 1940, and in what was called the three-billion-dollar gamble 1,644 were ordered in September 1942, before any had been flight-tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One airframe took as much material by weight as eleven P-51 fighters, new factories rose at Wichita, Renton, Marietta and Omaha, and 3,763 had been accepted by the end of August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carried up to about 9,100 kilograms (20,000 pounds) of bombs internally and a crew of eleven, with guns in remote-controlled turrets aimed by a central fire-control computer that General Electric rushed into production by early 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its Wright R-3350 engines stayed troublesome for 3 years, and some aircraft carried an extra wing-shaped aerial for the Eagle bombing radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> From June 1944 B-29s struck Japan from bases in China, but the American bombing survey later judged that the results did not warrant the fuel and supplies those bases consumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the Marianas, Curtis LeMay changed tactics in March 1945, sending the bombers in by night with incendiaries at between about 1,500 and 2,800 metres (4,900 and 9,200 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tokyo raid of 9 to 10 March burned out about 41 square kilometres (15.8 square miles), and the official casualty count listed 83,793 dead and 40,918 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 6 August 1945 the B-29 Enola Gay dropped the first atomic bomb used in combat on Hiroshima, and 3 days later Bockscar dropped the second on Nagasaki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> B-29s dropped 147,000 of the 160,800 tons of bombs that fell on the home islands, and radar stations on Iwo Jima helped bring many a damaged bomber home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), ch. 6, “AAF Aircraft of World War II,” 207–10, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-6.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Pacific: Matterhorn to Nagasaki, June 1944 to August 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), ch. 20, “Urban Area Attacks,” 612–17, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/V/AAF-V-20.html. [Open access]","National Air and Space Museum, “Boeing B-29 Superfortress <i>Enola Gay</i>,” collection object A19500100000, Smithsonian Institution, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/boeing-b-29-superfortress-enola-gay/nasm_A19500100000. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC, 1 July 1946), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/PTO-Summary.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>The Effects of Atomic Bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki</i> (Washington, DC, 1946), ch. 2, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/AtomicEffects/AtomicEffects-2.html. [Open access]","Joseph C. Boyce, ed., <i>New Weapons for Air Warfare: Fire-Control Equipment, Proximity Fuzes, and Guided Missiles</i>, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), 47–48, https://archive.org/details/NewWeaponsForAirWarfareOSRDBoyce. [Open access]","George Raynor Thompson and Dixie R. Harris, <i>The Signal Corps: The Outcome (Mid-1943 Through 1945)</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1991), 237, 451–52, https://archive.org/details/TheSignalCorpsTheOutcome. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was it ordered before it had been tested?","a":"Because the air forces' leaders, Arnold and Lovett, chose to gamble rather than lose years waiting for test results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official history notes that the risk lay less in money, of which wartime brought plenty, than in the allocation of scarce materials and manufacturing plant, and that the gamble produced a remarkable combat record within 4 years of the first experimental contract.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Electric, for its part, froze the fire-control computer's design early to meet the production line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did LeMay send the bombers in low and by night?","a":"Because high-altitude bombing over Japan had disappointed: strong winds at height made aiming difficult, and the incendiary clusters fell grossly inaccurately from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Flying low promised greater accuracy, longer engine life and bomb loads that rose to about 6 tons a plane, and the Japanese defences were caught unprepared for the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the bombing survey criticise the China bases?","a":"Because their supplies had to be flown in from India, so the bombers had supplies for only a few strikes a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their roughly 800 tons on the home islands between June 1944 and January 1945 lacked the weight and accuracy to matter, and the survey thought the fuel would have done more for tactical and antishipping operations in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Boeing B-29 Superfortress"},
    "ww2-886": {"questions":["Two Junkers Jumo 004 engines that lasted only about 10 hours on average for lack of heat-resistant alloys were one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took so long to reach German fighter squadrons.","Hitler's 1944 order to make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a fighter-bomber was long blamed for its delay, though engine troubles now seem the main cause and the order diverted some 30 per cent of airframes."],"abstract":"The <b>Messerschmitt Me 262</b> was a German fighter and the world's first operational jet fighter, powered by two Junkers Jumo 004 turbojets and armed with four 30-mm cannon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A jet engine drives an aircraft forward by the reaction of the gases it expels, an old principle that gas-turbine and aircraft design were only ready to exploit in the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first jet-powered flight, by a Heinkel 178, came in Germany on 27 August 1939, 4 days before the invasion of Poland, and Frank Whittle's British engine first flew on 15 May 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States lagged, building Whittle's design at General Electric, and its first jet, the Bell XP-59A, flew on 18 October 1942, but no American jet fighter fought in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain's Gloster Meteor reached an RAF squadron in 1944 and was used against the V-1 flying bombs that summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The Me 262, conceived in 1938, first flew on a piston engine on 18 April 1941 and under jet power on 19 July 1942, and it was about 190 kilometres (120 miles) an hour faster than the P-51 Mustang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Jumo 004 engines lacked heat-resistant alloys and lasted only about 10 hours on average, although some 6,000 were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Messerschmitt completed 1,443 of the fighters but only about 300 saw combat, though in January 1945 Allied air commanders were alarmed enough to make jet production a first-priority target alongside oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German witnesses told the bombing survey that Hitler's 1944 order to turn it into a fighter-bomber had delayed it, but the Smithsonian judges that slow engine development was the real cause and that the order diverted about 30 per cent of airframes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Albert Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that once the fuel plants were bombed the new tanks and jet planes were of no use, and Allied command of the air kept the fighter from ever reaching its potential.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Air and Space Museum, “Messerschmitt Me 262 A-1a <i>Schwalbe</i> (Swallow),” collection object A19600328000, Smithsonian Institution, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/messerschmitt-me-262-1a-schwalbe-swallow/nasm_A19600328000. [Open access]","Leslie E. Neville and Nathaniel F. Silsbee, <i>Jet Propulsion Progress: The Development of Aircraft Gas Turbines</i> (New York: McGraw-Hill, 1948), 5–13, https://archive.org/details/jetpropulsionpro00nevi. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 6, <i>Men and Planes</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), ch. 7, “The Quest for Better Weapons,” 245–49, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/VI/AAF-VI-7.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), ch. 20, 716–17, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/III/AAF-III-20.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 20 June 1946, testimony of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-20-46.asp. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), ch. 24, “The Flying Bomb: Part Two,” 383–84, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-24.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so few of the jets that were built ever fight?","a":"Because most were lost before they could: the Smithsonian records that of 1,443 completed only about 300 saw combat, the rest destroyed in training accidents or by Allied bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their airfields were under constant attack, and in the last days the remaining units flew from makeshift strips along the autobahns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fuel was the other limit, for Speer said that after May 1944 attacks on the fuel plants cost Germany 90 per cent of its fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the engines take so long to perfect?","a":"Because Germany lacked the high-quality, heat-resistant lightweight alloys a turbine needs, so the Jumo 004 had to run at a low maximum speed and wore out after about 10 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its jet-engine programme, planned to reach large-scale production by the end of 1942, ran at least 2 years late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Smithsonian concludes that this slow engine work, not Hitler's bomber order, was the main reason the fighter came late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Allies so worried by a few hundred jets?","a":"Because the jets were far faster than any Allied piston fighter, and American planners believed in January 1945 that the Germans had already built about 700 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bombing survey thought that had they come 6 months earlier with good pilots they would not have changed the course of the war, but would have sharply raised the losses of the attacking bombers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Messerschmitt Me 262"},
    "ww2-887": {"questions":["After Tobruk fell in June 1942 Roosevelt promised 300 of the new <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tanks to the Middle East, and they first went into battle at El Alamein that October.","In Normandy ordnance units welded hedgerow-cutting prongs devised by Sergeant Curtis Culin onto the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, letting American tanks burst through the banks before Operation Cobra."],"abstract":"The <b>M4 Sherman</b> was the main American medium tank of the Second World War, armed at first with a 75-mm gun in a turret that could turn through a full 360 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It replaced the hurriedly designed M3 Grant, whose main gun sat in a sponson at the side, and the first Shermans came off the lines in July 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end of that year five models were in production, differing mainly in their engines, and the diesel M4A2 went in large numbers to the Soviet Union and Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American production of medium tanks ended in July 1945 after some 57,000 had been built, and later Shermans carried a high-velocity 76-mm gun or a 105-mm howitzer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Tobruk fell in June 1942 Roosevelt promised 300 Shermans to the Middle East, and at El Alamein that October the British praised it as the tank that ended a long reign of German superiority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A few months later in Tunisia it proved outgunned and outarmoured by the Tiger, and in Italy and France by the Panther, whose high-velocity gun it could not match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its strengths were reliability, mobility and a gyrostabiliser with power traverse that allowed quicker fire, so American crews relied on numbers and flanking attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Normandy, ordnance units welded steel prongs devised by Sergeant Curtis Culin onto Shermans so that they could force their way through the hedgerows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cost was heavy, for one tank battalion of the 2nd Armored Division lost 70 per cent of its tanks between 26 July and 12 August 1944, and Bradley wrote that the willingness to expend Shermans offered little comfort to their crews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ordnance Department's history blames years of resistance to a heavier tank, and in the Ardennes Patton, knowing the Panther's superiority, put the few up-armoured Jumbo Shermans in the lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Constance McLaughlin Green, Harry C. Thomson and Peter C. Roots, <i>The Ordnance Department: Planning Munitions for War</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 279–86, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-9. [Open access]","Harry C. Thomson and Lida Mayo, <i>The Ordnance Department: Procurement and Supply</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 252–54, 263–64, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-10. [Open access]","Lida Mayo, <i>The Ordnance Department: On Beachhead and Battlefront</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 28–29, 322–23, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-11. [Open access]","I. S. O. Playfair, F. C. Flynn, C. J. C. Molony and T. P. Gleave, <i>The Mediterranean and Middle East</i>, vol. 3, <i>British Fortunes Reach Their Lowest Ebb</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1960), 278–79, https://archive.org/details/mediterranean-middle-east-vol-3. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), ch. 11, 204–6, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-11.html. [Open access]","Hugh M. Cole, <i>The Lorraine Campaign</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1950), ch. 14, 602–3, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Lorraine/USA-E-Lorraine-14.html. [Open access]","Hugh M. Cole, <i>The Ardennes: Battle of the Bulge</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1965), ch. 21, 524, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Ardennes/USA-E-Ardennes-21.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army keep the Sherman when better German tanks appeared?","a":"Because the Army Ground Forces opposed any new vehicle it did not judge essential and insisted that new tanks be fully tested before going overseas, while regulations capped a tank at 30 tons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ordnance Department urged a better-armed successor from August 1943, but the heavy M26 Pershing reached the front only in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meanwhile crews fought Panthers with flanking moves and, later, the 76-mm gun with scarce tungsten-cored ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Normandy hedgerows defeat ordinary tanks?","a":"Because a tank climbing an earth bank tipped upward, exposing its thin belly and pointing its gun at the sky, and clearing gaps with dozers and explosives was slow, so tanks kept to the roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Culin's prongs pinned the tank down while it smashed a hole, and Bradley had them made in quantity from steel salvaged from German beach obstacles, keeping them secret until Cobra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Lorraine tank battles not settle the argument?","a":"Because the fighting was lopsided: American tanks always outnumbered their opponents, many German crews were untrained, and the panzers fought with no air cover and little artillery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The official historian also warned that the testimonials Patton and others gave for the M4 were meant to build public confidence, not to evaluate the tank critically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"M4 Sherman"},
    "ww2-888": {"questions":["A Springfield Armory engineer took no royalty for designing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the gas-operated rifle that the armory turned out at 500 a day by December 1940 and more than 3.5 million times by 1945.","In the close hedgerow fighting of Normandy, American troops armed with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a weapon more effective at long range, were somewhat at a disadvantage against an enemy met at short distances."],"abstract":"The <b>M1 Garand</b> was the standard rifle of the United States Army in the Second World War, a gas-operated, air-cooled self-loading weapon fed by clips of eight rounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Weighing about 4.3 kilograms (9.5 pounds), it fired once for each squeeze of the trigger without the bolt being worked by hand, more than twice as fast as the bolt-action Springfield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its designer, John C. Garand, joined Springfield Armory in October 1919 with orders to design such a rifle, and it was standardised as Rifle, Semiautomatic, M1 on 9 January 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army had first proposed a semi-automatic rifle in 1900, and one congressman complained in 1937 that the war had been over nearly 20 years and the Army had only 80 semi-automatics in service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Garand took no royalty and received nothing beyond his armory salary, though a bill to grant him 100,000 dollars was introduced in Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Mass production proved harder than expected, slowed by a public controversy over the rival Johnson rifle from 1938, the year in which only some 2,000 had been made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By December 1940 Springfield was turning out 500 a day and aiming for 1,000, and a popular magazine called it the deadliest firearm ever invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Springfield produced 3,519,471 by the end of the war and Winchester 513,880, while the standard German rifles stayed bolt-operated Mausers loaded by five-round clips, supplemented by the semi-automatic Gewehr 41 and Model 43.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the hedgerow fighting of Normandy, though, troops armed with the M1, a weapon more effective at long range, were at a disadvantage at close quarters, where submachine guns were in short supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Marine Corps and the Navy had adopted it by 1940, more than six million were eventually made, and the M14 replaced it by 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Constance McLaughlin Green, Harry C. Thomson and Peter C. Roots, <i>The Ordnance Department: Planning Munitions for War</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 59, 175–77, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-9. [Open access]","Harry C. Thomson and Lida Mayo, <i>The Ordnance Department: Procurement and Supply</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1960), 160–63, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-10. [Open access]","National Park Service, “John C. Garand,” Springfield Armory National Historic Site, last updated 25 March 2023, https://www.nps.gov/spar/learn/historyculture/john-c-garand.htm. [Open access]","War Department, <i>Handbook on German Military Forces</i>, TM-E 30-451 (Washington, DC, 15 March 1945), chap. 7, sec. 2, https://archive.org/details/TME-30-4511945. [Open access]","War Department, <i>Basic Field Manual: U.S. Rifle, Caliber .30, M1</i>, FM 23-5 (Washington, DC, 20 July 1940, with changes of 1941), sec. 1, https://archive.org/details/fm-23-5-caliber-.-30-m-1. [Open access]","Edwin Teale, “He Invented the World’s Deadliest Rifle,” <i>Popular Science</i> 137, no. 6 (December 1940): 68–71, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_JScDAAAAMBAJ. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1961), ch. 3, 41, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-E-Breakout/USA-E-Breakout-3.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did adopting the rifle take so long?","a":"Because between the wars the Ordnance Department felt no great urgency and wanted the rifle as nearly perfect as possible before standardising it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rival designs were tested through the 1920s, MacArthur ordered work moved from a .276 to a .30 calibre rifle, and troop trials sent the rifles back for redesign after many stoppages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even after 1936 small design changes went on while tooling and manufacture proceeded at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was mass production so hard to start?","a":"Because Springfield Armory had lived hand to mouth since the First World War, with machine tools 20 or 30 years old and some older than the American Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress gave 1.8 million dollars for retooling in 1938, while the Johnson rifle controversy raged in Congress and the press and critics doubted the Garand could be mass-produced at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 2 years later the armory was expanding and producing hundreds a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the German infantry rifle compare?","a":"The standard German rifles and carbines remained bolt-operated Mauser Model 98 types fed by five-round clips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The War Department's handbook of 1945 noted a German trend towards more semi-automatic and fully automatic weapons, including the gas-operated Gewehr 41 and its successor, the Model 43, but the bolt-action rifle remained standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The M1's eight-round clip gave each American rifleman a larger volume of accurate fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"M1 Garand"},
    "ww2-889": {"questions":["Kept to firing over water for 18 months lest a dud betray its secret, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was released for land warfare and rushed into the Battle of the Bulge in December 1944.","Guns on the English coast firing shells fitted with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and laid with the help of SCR-584 radar, brought down a growing share of the V-1 flying bombs in 1944."],"abstract":"A <b>proximity fuze</b> sets off a shell, bomb or rocket when it comes close to its target rather than on impact or after a set time, and the wartime American version carried a miniature radio transmitter and receiver in the nose of the projectile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its signal was reflected back from the target, and the interference between outgoing and returning waves, the Doppler frequency, triggered the firing circuit once it grew strong enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Work began under the Office of Scientific Research and Development in August 1940, and in spring 1941 the Navy took charge of fuzes for spinning shells while the Army took those for bombs, rockets and mortars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> British physicists had thought a fuze for shells virtually impossible, and the hardest task was making valves and batteries rugged enough to survive the shock of firing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its code name VT, often taken to mean variable time, meant nothing at all, and the fuze was one of the best-kept secrets of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The cruiser USS Helena first fired it in combat against a Japanese aircraft on 5 January 1943, and for 18 months it was kept to firing over water, where no dud could fall into enemy hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the summer of 1944 guns moved to the English coast fired it at the V-1 flying bombs, and with new American radar and predictors it vastly increased their success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vannevar Bush wrote that three new devices finally defeated the V-1 attack on London, and the official history of the work names them as a gun director, the SCR-584 radar and this fuze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Released for ground warfare in October 1944, it was rushed into the Battle of the Bulge from 18 December, after stocks were saved from a threatened depot, and some 3,000 were issued to two American corps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bursting above the ground, it robbed foxholes and trenches of much of their protection, while fuzes for bombs, slower to develop, were first used against Iwo Jima in February 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Joseph C. Boyce, ed., <i>New Weapons for Air Warfare: Fire-Control Equipment, Proximity Fuzes, and Guided Missiles</i>, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1947), ix–xi, 104–5, https://archive.org/details/NewWeaponsForAirWarfareOSRDBoyce. [Open access]","Constance McLaughlin Green, Harry C. Thomson and Peter C. Roots, <i>The Ordnance Department: Planning Munitions for War</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1955), 363–66, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-9. [Open access]","Lida Mayo, <i>The Ordnance Department: On Beachhead and Battlefront</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1968), 308, https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-11. [Open access]","Wilbur S. Hinman Jr. and Cledo Brunetti, “Radio Proximity Fuze Design,” <i>Journal of Research of the National Bureau of Standards</i> 37, no. 1 (July 1946): 1–2, https://archive.org/details/jresv37n1p1. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), ch. 24, “The Flying Bomb: Part Two,” 383–84, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-24.html. [Open access]","Vannevar Bush, <i>Science, the Endless Frontier: A Report to the President</i> (Washington, DC, July 1945; repr. Alexandria, VA: National Science Foundation, 2020), https://nsf-gov-resources.nsf.gov/2023-04/EndlessFrontier75th_w.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a fuze for shells so hard to build?","a":"Because a complete radio sending and receiving set had to fit into the nose of a shell and survive the shock of firing, and building valves rugged enough for that was first thought naive in both Britain and America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shell's spin helped: a glass phial of electrolyte broke on firing and the spin spread the fluid through the battery, whereas bombs and rockets needed tiny wind-driven generators instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was it kept off land battlefields until late 1944?","a":"Because a dud landing on enemy ground could be recovered and copied, so its use was limited to the Navy and to a few anti-aircraft batteries in England until the Combined Chiefs of Staff released it for ground warfare in October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany had a radio fuze for rockets in production by the end of the war, but none saw service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did bursts above the ground matter so much against troops?","a":"Because a shell exploding at the right height showered fragments down into foxholes and trenches, which time-fuzed fire could rarely manage in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some military leaders said the fuze made moving troops in the open impossible, and Eisenhower's headquarters reported that unseen fire at all hours had severely upset the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Proximity fuze"},
    "ww2-890": {"questions":["A junior SS officer from 1940, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled to the Nordhausen area about fifteen times to visit the Mittelwerk and, NASA states, took part in decisions on the use of slave labour.","Surrendering to the Americans in 1945, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was taken to Fort Bliss in Texas under Project Paperclip and later directed the building of the Saturn V Moon rocket."],"abstract":"<b>Wernher von Braun</b> was the engineer who led the technical work of the German army's rocket programme and later built missiles and space rockets for the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in 1912 and inspired by Hermann Oberth's writing on space travel, he went to work for the army on liquid-fuel rockets in 1932 and gained a doctorate for that research on 27 July 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army's first small rockets, the A-1 and A-2, were tested at Kummersdorf south of Berlin, and two A-2s flew from Borkum in December 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In April 1937 the rocket group of about 90 men moved to a secret research centre at Peenemünde on the Baltic coast, where von Braun was technical director throughout and Walter Dornberger the military head until 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their A-4 missile, later called the V-2, was first launched successfully in October 1942 and fired at London, Paris and Antwerp from September 1944, the first to hit Britain falling on 8 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> He joined the Nazi Party in 1937 and became a junior SS officer in 1940, and in July 1943 he reported on the missile to Hitler, who then backed the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the RAF bombed Peenemünde on the night of 17 to 18 August 1943, production moved into tunnels near Nordhausen, where the first 107 concentration camp prisoners arrived on 28 August to build the Mittelwerk factory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Smithsonian estimates that at least 10,000 camp workers died making the missile, which itself killed about 2,700 people in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> NASA states that von Braun made about fifteen trips to the Mittelwerk area, knew of its terrible conditions and took part in decisions on slave labour, and its history office notes that scholars are still reassessing his role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Albert Speer told the Nuremberg tribunal that a rocket cost almost as much as a fighter, and in 1945 von Braun surrendered to the Americans, who took him to the United States under Project Paperclip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Aeronautics and Space Administration, “Wernher von Braun,” NASA, last updated 6 February 2024, https://www.nasa.gov/people/wernher-von-braun/. [Open access]","NASA History Office, “Sputnik Biographies: Wernher von Braun (1912–1977),” https://history.nasa.gov/sputnik/braun.html. [Open access]","National Air and Space Museum, “V-2 Missile,” collection object A19600342000, Smithsonian Institution, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/missile-surface-surface-v-2-a-4/nasm_A19600342000. [Open access]","Johannes Leicht, “Die ‘Wunderwaffen’ V1 und V2,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, 9 November 2023, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/die-wunderwaffen-v1-und-v2 [in German]. [Open access]","Mittelbau-Dora Concentration Camp Memorial, “‘Buried Alive’,” Buchenwald and Mittelbau-Dora Memorials Foundation, https://www.dora.de/en/geschichte/chronologie/konzentrationslager/lebendig-begraben. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC, 30 September 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), ch. 25, “The Long-Range Rocket,” 405–6, 418–19, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-25.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 16, proceedings of 21 June 1946, cross-examination of Albert Speer, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/06-21-46.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was missile production moved underground?","a":"Because the RAF raid on Peenemünde on the night of 17 to 18 August 1943 showed how exposed the site was, and the Nazi leadership decided to build the missile in a new underground plant near Nordhausen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 10 days after the raid the SS brought the first prisoners to the Kohnstein hill to dig out the Mittelwerk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American bombing survey later judged the attacks on Peenemünde ineffective, because the V-2 had already moved underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many of the prisoners die?","a":"Because until January 1944 the SS housed them in the tunnels themselves, and many, most of them Russians, Poles and French, did not survive the months of tunnel building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They slept on wooden bunks in the cold, used halved oil drums as latrines and had neither warm clothing nor enough food, and the Deutsches Historisches Museum estimates that about a third of all the prisoners died before the camp was cleared in April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is his wartime record still argued over?","a":"Because the celebrated pioneer of American spaceflight was also an SS officer who helped manage a weapon built by slave labour, and NASA's history office says scholars are still reassessing his role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Smithsonian records that Dornberger and the factory's chief engineer, Arthur Rudolph, decided to exploit camp labour, and that von Braun knew of these decisions and was increasingly involved in managing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> NASA adds that he knew of the terrible conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Wernher von Braun"},
    "ww2-891": {"questions":["On the night of 17 August 1943, 597 British bombers directed by a master bomber attacked the rocket station at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, killing more than 700 people in its settlement and labour camp.","After the raid on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in August 1943, V-2 production moved to tunnels in the Harz mountains, where concentration camp prisoners were forced to assemble the rockets."],"abstract":"<b>Peenemünde</b> was the German weapons-research complex on the northern tip of the Baltic island of Usedom, where the army developed the rocket later called the V-2 and the air force tested the flying bomb later called the V-1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plans for a station there were made in 1936, and the air force shared the cost in return for its own test station, Peenemünde West, beside the army's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The army's rocket group of about 90 men moved there in April 1937, with Wernher von Braun as technical director and Walter Dornberger as military head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1942 Peenemünde East employed about 5,000 people, while some 3,000 mostly East European forced labourers were building a production plant to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 3 October 1942 an A-4 rocket flew its intended course for the first time, and on 24 December the first flying bomb was launched from a ramp there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> British intelligence had heard of the station in the Oslo report of 1939, but only in the spring of 1943 did agents' reports and air photographs convince London that long-range rockets were being tested there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the night of 17 August 1943 Bomber Command sent 597 heavy bombers against three aiming points, under a master bomber who directed the attack by radio telephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forty aircraft failed to return, and more than 700 people died in the settlement and a labour camp, among them the propulsion expert Walter Thiel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The raid spared the essential plant but drove rocket production into tunnels in the Harz mountains, where concentration camp prisoners were forced to build V-2s, and the official historians judged that all the attacks may have delayed the V-2 offensive by about 2 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Prisoners worked at Peenemünde too, in the Karlshagen I camp that held 1,500 men from May 1943 to April 1945, and in 1945 von Braun arranged the surrender of 500 of his rocket scientists to the Americans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Basil Collier, <i>The Defence of the United Kingdom</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), chap. 22, “The Threat from Long-Range Weapons (1939–1944),” 331–44, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/UN/UK/UK-Defence-UK/UK-DefenseOfUK-22.html. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 158–60, 283–86, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","Johannes Leicht, “Die ‘Wunderwaffen’ V1 und V2,” <i>Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/die-wunderwaffen-v1-und-v2. [in German] [Open access]","Historisch-Technisches Museum Peenemünde, “Die Denkmal-Landschaft,” https://museum-peenemuende.de/das-museum/die-denkmal-landschaft/. [in German] [Open access]","National Air and Space Museum, “Missile, Surface-to-Surface, V-2 (A-4),” object record A19600342000, https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/missile-surface-surface-v-2-v-2/nasm_A19600342000. [Open access]","KZ-Gedenkstätte Mittelbau-Dora, “Tunnel Complex,” Stiftung Gedenkstätten Buchenwald und Mittelbau-Dora, https://www.dora.de/en/geschichte/historischer-ort/stollen. [Open access]","NASA History Office, “Wernher von Braun (1912–1977),” Sputnik Biographies, https://history.nasa.gov/sputnik/braun.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the army and the air force share the site?","a":"Because the air force agreed to pay part of the cost of the new station in return for setting up its own experimental station beside the army's and sharing the facilities both needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its airfield at Peenemünde West became one of the largest in Germany and tested remote-controlled weapons, rocket aircraft and the flying bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did British planners hesitate before bombing it?","a":"Because the Ministry of Economic Warfare warned in May 1943 that an attack would delay production only if the rocket's development was not yet complete, and that it would warn the enemy and might fatally handicap the gathering of further intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the effect of the raid hard to measure?","a":"Because Dornberger's estimate of a delay of 4 to 6 weeks was a personal view with no detailed reasons behind it, and the rocket still had serious defects to correct whatever happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the raid certainly did was push manufacturing into the underground Mittelwerk plant in the Harz and testing to another site in Poland, which itself caused delay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Peenemünde"},
    "ww2-892": {"questions":["Blood transfusion, penicillin, specialist surgical units and evacuation by air all helped <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Second World War to cut the share of wounded soldiers who died after reaching hospital.","DDT powder that stopped a typhus epidemic in Naples in the winter of 1943–1944 was among the wartime advances in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that came out of Allied research programmes."],"abstract":"<b>Military medicine</b> is the medical care of armed forces, and in the Second World War it fought disease as much as wounds, since in some theatres sickness put far more men in hospital than the enemy did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Burma the sickness rate of the British and Indian forces was 1,850 admissions per 1,000 men a year in 1942, and in 1943 and 1944 more men were admitted to hospital than the force contained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Pacific war cut off quinine in December 1941, American forces relied on the synthetic drug quinacrine while chemists screened compounds for better antimalarials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Burma the daily suppressive dose against malaria was made a matter of discipline, and responsibility for malaria control was placed on the combatant officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The insecticide DDT, adopted as a louse powder in 1943, stopped a typhus epidemic in Naples in the winter of 1943–1944, which its developers called the first time such an epidemic had been halted at its peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For the wounded, an American review judged that the outstanding surgical advance was resuscitation with blood and plasma in forward areas together with early operation, and put the control of infection second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the First World War between 8 and 11 of every 100 wounded American soldiers who reached forward hospitals alive died there, and in the Second the figure fell to 4.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British medical history credited the control of haemorrhage, shock and sepsis to early skilled treatment, specialist units, blood transfusion and penicillin, which the United States could not supply for a single patient in 1941 but had in quantity by D-Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Air transport changed evacuation, and in north-west Europe more than 100,000 men were flown from front-line units to base hospitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Burma in 1944 about 48 of every 1,000 men were wounded and fewer than 3 of those died of their wounds, yet in the peak week sick admissions outnumbered battle casualties by more than nine to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 4, <i>North-West Europe</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), xi, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-4. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 5, <i>Burma</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1966), ix–x, 612–13, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-5. [Open access]","Douglas B. Kendrick, <i>Blood Program in World War II</i>, Medical Department, United States Army (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1964), xiii, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA286756. [Open access]","George A. Carden Jr., “Introduction,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 2, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 665–66, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX2.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","H. L. Haller and Stanley J. Cristol, “The Development of New Insecticides,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 2, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 621–24, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX2.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","Chester S. Keefer, “Penicillin: A Wartime Achievement,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 2, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 717–21, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX2.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","John S. Lockwood, “Introduction,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 91–93, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did combat officers, not doctors, enforce antimalarial tablets in Burma?","a":"Because the daily suppressive dose was treated as a matter of discipline rather than choice, and responsibility for controlling malaria was placed squarely on the combatant officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The British medical history contrasts the havoc malaria caused among the Japanese with its much smaller effect on Allied troops, which it puts down to these preventive measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did fewer of the wounded die than in the First World War?","a":"Because, in the view of the American blood programme's history, the death rate from combat wounds falls as resuscitation becomes quicker and more complete, with whole blood and plasma playing major roles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British history adds early surgery near the front, specialist units, a better understanding of shock and penicillin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did losing quinine matter so much?","a":"Because American forces faced a long war in some of the most malaria-ridden regions in the world and quinine had been their only reliable drug against the disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They had to rely on quinacrine while thousands of compounds were tested on birds, monkeys and finally men in search of something better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Military medicine"},
    "ww2-893": {"questions":["Corn steep liquor and a mould found on a cantaloupe at a laboratory in Peoria helped American firms mass-produce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after Florey and Heatley brought the Oxford work across the Atlantic.","Trials on infected war wounds at Bushnell General Hospital in Utah in 1943 persuaded the American army that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a drug of the highest military importance."],"abstract":"<b>Penicillin</b> is an antibiotic made by moulds of the genus Penicillium, and Alexander Fleming of <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"St_Mary's_Hospital,_London\">Saint Mary's Hospital</span> in London described its effect on bacteria after a chance observation in 1928, publishing in 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fleming could not purify the unstable compound, and it stayed a curiosity until Howard Florey's team at <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"University_of_Oxford\">Oxford</span>, among them Ernst Chain and Norman Heatley, set out to extract it in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their tests on infected mice were published in August 1940, and their first patient, an Oxford policeman, improved strikingly in February 1941 before the tiny supply ran out and he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With British firms committed to other war work, Florey and Heatley reached the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"United_States_Department_of_Agriculture\">Department of Agriculture</span>'s laboratory at Peoria, Illinois, in July 1941, where corn steep liquor raised yields more than tenfold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A strain of Penicillium found on a cantaloupe collected in Peoria gave far higher yields in submerged culture, and in 1945 Fleming, Chain and Florey shared the Nobel Prize for the discovery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> In the summer of 1941 the United States had not enough penicillin to treat a single patient, and by June 1942 only enough for ten had been made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trials on infected war wounds at Bushnell General Hospital in Utah from April 1943 convinced the American armed forces of its military value, and the War Production Board took charge of production in July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between June 1943 and June 1945 monthly output rose 2,500 times and the cost fell from 200 to 6 dollars per million units, so that by D-Day there was enough for the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Allies_of_World_War_II\">Allied</span> forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> British doctors first used it on battle casualties in the field in 1942 in the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Mediterranean_and_Middle_East_theatre_of_World_War_II\">Middle East</span>, with the very limited amounts then available, before its extensive use in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An American review ranked the control of wound infection second only to the treatment of shock, and the British army in north-west Europe appointed an adviser in penicillin and chemotherapy with a mobile laboratory to control its use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Gaynes, “The Discovery of Penicillin—New Insights After More Than 75 Years of Clinical Use,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 23, no. 5 (2017): 849–53, https://doi.org/10.3201/eid2305.161556. [Open access]","Alexander Fleming, “Penicillin,” Nobel Lecture, 11 December 1945, in <i>Nobel Lectures, Physiology or Medicine 1942–1962</i> (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 1964), 83–84, https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/fleming-lecture.pdf. [Open access]","Kenneth B. Raper, “Research in the Development of Penicillin,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 2, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 723–25, 737, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX2.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","Chester S. Keefer, “Penicillin: A Wartime Achievement,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 2, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 717–21, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX2.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","John Winslow Hirshfeld, “The Application of Penicillin to Surgical Problems,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 123–24, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","John S. Lockwood, “Introduction,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 91–93, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 2, <i>Hong Kong, Malaya, Iceland and the Faroes, Libya 1942–1943, North-West Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), 263, 364, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-2. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 4, <i>North-West Europe</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 86–87, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-4. [Open access]","Nobel Prize Outreach, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1945,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1945/summary/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was mass production developed in the United States rather than Britain?","a":"Because British drug companies were tied up with other war commitments, so Florey and Heatley took the mould to the United States in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Committee on Medical Research then brought in commercial firms, and research at Peoria on strains, culture media and extraction was passed straight to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the early supply rationed so tightly?","a":"Because there was so little that it had to go to the cases that would teach the armed services most, chosen by a committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From June 1942 until February 1943 there was enough to study only 100 patients, and no individual doctor or patient could buy it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did patients need injections every 2 or 3 hours?","a":"Because the kidneys excreted penicillin very quickly, so frequent injections were needed to keep enough of it in the body fluids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Early tests also showed that it did not reach pus in long-standing abscesses or joints, which had to be injected directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Penicillin"},
    "ww2-894": {"questions":["The Barcelona service of the Spanish Civil War first proved that stored civilian blood could support <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for wounded men at the front, a lesson Britain applied from 1939.","Of the more than 13 million donations the American Red Cross collected for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 1941 to 1945, blood from Black donors was at first refused and later kept segregated."],"abstract":"<b>Blood transfusion</b> is the transfer of blood or its components from a donor into a patient, and in the Second World War it became central to resuscitating wounded men in shock from loss of blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Spanish Civil War had first shown that stored civilian blood could reach the wounded at the front, the Barcelona service collecting more than 9,000 litres between 1936 and 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain entered the war with a separate army transfusion service already planned, and its Army Blood Supply Depot at Bristol supplied the British forces overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whole blood kept only a few weeks, so the Allies also used plasma, the fluid part of the blood, which keeps longer and suits a patient of any blood group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Blood for Britain project in New York, supervised from September 1940 by the surgeon Charles Drew, shipped over 5,000 litres of plasma, and the American Red Cross then collected 13,326,242 donations by September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plasma brought down severe wound shock, but it carried no red cells, and experience in North Africa after November 1942 showed that it could not replace whole blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A citrate and dextrose preservative that kept blood for at least 3 weeks made it possible to fly whole blood from the United States to Europe from August 1944 and to the Pacific from November 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British field transfusion units worked beside casualty clearing stations, and in Libya a transfusion unit with two surgical units made an efficient advanced surgical centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the day after D-Day about 230 litres (400 pints) of blood a day crossed the Channel, and of 144,649 wounded in the British-led 21st Army Group about 12 per cent were transfused in forward units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Red Cross at first refused blood from Black donors and from January 1942 kept it separate, a policy Drew condemned because no scientific evidence showed any difference between the blood of different races.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Douglas B. Kendrick, <i>Blood Program in World War II</i>, Medical Department, United States Army (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1964), xiii, 11, 14–16, 130, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA286756. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 4, <i>North-West Europe</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1962), 87–89, 204–05, 546–47, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-4. [Open access]","F. A. E. Crew, <i>The Army Medical Services: Campaigns</i>, vol. 2, <i>Hong Kong, Malaya, Iceland and the Faroes, Libya 1942–1943, North-West Africa</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Medical Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1957), 263, 364, https://archive.org/details/army-medical-services-campaigns-vol-2. [Open access]","Edwin J. Cohn, “The History of Plasma Fractionation,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 364–66, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, “Becoming ‘the Father of the Blood Bank,’ 1938–1941,” <i>Charles R. Drew</i>, Profiles in Science, https://profiles.nlm.nih.gov/spotlight/bg/feature/blood. [Open access]","Joseph T. Wearn, “Introduction,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 331–33, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","John S. Lockwood, “Introduction,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 91–93, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","Charles A. Janeway and J. L. Oncley, “Blood Substitutes,” in <i>Advances in Military Medicine, Made by American Investigators Working under the Sponsorship of the Committee on Medical Research</i>, vol. 1, Science in World War II (Boston: Little, Brown, 1948), 444–45, https://archive.org/details/32430610RX1.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could plasma not fully replace whole blood?","a":"Because plasma restores the volume of the blood but carries no red cells, so a patient given plasma or albumin may recover from shock and still suffer from acute anaemia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the war went on, giving these solutions to the wounded exposed a lack of haemoglobin that only transfused red cells could correct, and whole blood was used more and more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was blood not flown to Europe before August 1944?","a":"Because when a workable plan to fly blood overseas was put forward in November 1943, the Surgeon General thought it both impractical and unnecessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The casualties of the first weeks in Normandy showed that relying on blood collected locally was unrealistic, and the same plan was then put into effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Blood for Britain project matter later?","a":"Because it showed how large-scale collection had to work, above all that blood and plasma stay safe only when handled in a completely closed system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Drew standardised its procedures, equipment and records, and its methods became the model for the Red Cross's national blood donor service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Blood transfusion"},
    "ww2-895": {"questions":["Begun when radar scientists studied fighter interception at Biggin Hill before the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew into sections attached to every major command of the three British services.","Defined as a scientific method of giving executives a quantitative basis for decisions, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the United States Navy in April 1942 with an anti-submarine group under Philip Morse."],"abstract":"<b>Operations research</b>, called operational research in Britain, is the use of scientific method to give commanders a quantitative basis for decisions about their operations, and it first became a recognised activity in the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its roots lay in British air defence, when radar scientists joined RAF officers at Biggin Hill from 1936 to study how radar warnings could be turned into fighter interceptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scientists from the Bawdsey radar station stayed with Fighter Command at Stanmore when war broke out, and in June 1941 their group became the RAF's first operational research section.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The physicist Patrick Blackett led groups at Anti-Aircraft Command, at Coastal Command and, from December 1941, at the Admiralty, and his memorandum 'Scientists at the Operational Level' helped define the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1945 more than 1,000 people did such work in Britain, mostly physicists, engineers and mathematicians, though biologists and statisticians were recruited too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known study concerned Coastal Command's aerial depth charges, set to explode at about 15 metres (50 feet) although in half of all attacks the U-boat was still on the surface or partly visible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Analysis showed that a setting of about 7.5 metres (25 feet) would at least triple the chance of success, and within months of the change air attacks became more than twice as effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Admiralty studies of Atlantic convoys from 1941 to 1943 showed that larger convoys with the same number of escorts lost a smaller share of their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The head of Bomber Command's section, B. G. Dickins, told an Air Ministry conference in April 1943 that the radar-jamming foil called Window might save one in three of the bombers then being lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United States Navy's Anti-Submarine Warfare Operations Research Group, formed under the physicist Philip Morse on 1 April 1942, had 86 scientists by 1945 evaluating equipment, action reports and tactics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charles R. Shrader, <i>History of Operations Research in the United States Army</i>, vol. 1, <i>1942–62</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Deputy Under Secretary of the Army for Operations Research, 2006), 9–21, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA468757. [Open access]","Philip M. Morse and George E. Kimball, <i>Methods of Operations Research</i>, rev. ed. (Cambridge, MA: Technology Press of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology; New York: John Wiley, 1951), 1, 56–57, https://archive.org/details/methodsofoperati030158mbp. [Open access]","Charles M. Sternhell and Alan M. Thorndike, <i>Antisubmarine Warfare in World War II</i>, OEG Report No. 51 (Washington, DC: Operations Evaluation Group, Navy Department, 1946), foreword, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USN/rep/ASW-51/index.html. [Open access]","INFORMS, “Patrick M. S. Blackett,” Biographical Profiles, History of O.R. Excellence, https://www.informs.org/Explore/History-of-O.R.-Excellence/Biographical-Profiles/Blackett-Patrick-M.-S. [Open access]","“Patrick M. S. Blackett – Biographical,” from <i>Nobel Lectures, Physics 1942–1962</i> (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 1964), NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1948/blackett/biographical/. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 2, <i>Endeavour</i> (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1961), 146, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-2. [Open access]","INFORMS, “Philip M. Morse,” Biographical Profiles, History of O.R. Excellence, https://www.informs.org/Explore/History-of-O.R.-Excellence/Biographical-Profiles/Morse-Philip-M. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the scientists mostly stay civilians?","a":"Because Blackett and other leading figures argued that the work needed people of the highest intellectual ability, and that civilians dealt more easily with all ranks and were not distracted by routine staff duties or a military career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Members of overseas sections were given honorary commissions so that their status would be clear if they were captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did larger convoys lose fewer ships?","a":"Because with the number of escorts held constant the loss rate of merchant ships fell as convoys grew, and larger convoys also cut the number of crossings by a third and the number of U-boat attacks by almost as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seeing that this would work needed some knowledge of how ships behaved in port and in convoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans honour Blackett and Morse after the war?","a":"Because Blackett's groups at Coastal Command and the Admiralty had studied how to defeat the U-boats, and in 1946 he received the American Medal for Merit for that work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Morse, who had organised the navy's anti-submarine group and estimated U-boat numbers from merchant ship losses, received the same award.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Operations research"},
    "ww2-896": {"questions":["Because the Enigma machine flattened letter frequencies, wartime <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> relied instead on captured keys, operators' mistakes and guessed plain text tested on electromechanical machines.","American naval <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke into the Japanese fleet code JN 25 early in 1942, and by mid-April Hawaii was turning intercepted messages into translations within 6 hours."],"abstract":"<b>Cryptanalysis</b> is the breaking of codes and ciphers without the key, and in the Second World War it became an effort on an industrial scale in which both sides read part of their enemies' secret radio traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Older ciphers could be attacked by counting how often each letter appeared, but machines such as the German Enigma scrambled the letters so that every cipher letter turned up about equally often.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Machine ciphers were therefore attacked through captured material, operators' mistakes and cribs, or guessed stretches of plain text, with machines built to test thousands of possible settings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British Government Code and Cypher School broke its first wartime Enigma messages in January 1940, and from June 1941 its most secret product went to commanders under the prefix Ultra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Pacific, American naval cryptanalysts broke into the Japanese navy's main operational code, JN 25, early in 1942, and by mid-April messages were being decrypted and translated in Hawaii within 6 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Codebreaking worked in both directions, and the German navy's own service, the B-Dienst, read the Allied Naval Cypher No. 3 used for Atlantic convoy communications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American study counted at least 39 cases from January to June 1943 in which U-boat signals showed that Allied naval communications had been compromised, nearly all involving that cipher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After Naval Cypher No. 5 replaced it in June 1943, only two more confirmed cases were recorded that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German navy, convinced that Enigma could not be broken, explained its mounting U-boat losses by Allied radar and guarded its ciphers against capture rather than against codebreaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much had been read stayed secret for decades, and a study written for the United States Army War College in 1980 noted that the Allied reading of German traffic had become generally known only in the previous few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Jennifer Wilcox, <i>Solving the Enigma: History of the Cryptanalytic Bombe</i>, rev. ed. (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2006), 1–18, https://archive.org/details/solving_enigma-nsa. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 140, 346, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 3, <i>German Naval Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-024, chap. 4, “Compromise of Naval Ciphers #3 and #5,” 35–36, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-024/SRH024-4.html. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 3, <i>German Naval Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-024, chap. 7, “German Fears of the Compromise of Their Naval Cipher,” 83–84, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-024/SRH024-7.html. [Open access]","Frederick D. Parker, <i>A Priceless Advantage: U.S. Navy Communications Intelligence and the Battles of Coral Sea, Midway, and the Aleutians</i> (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1993), part one, “The Battle of the Coral Sea,” HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/Magic/COMINT-CoralSea/PartOne.html. [Open access]","Jerry C. Russell, <i>Ultra and the Campaign Against the U-boats in World War II</i>, SRH-142 (Carlisle Barracks, PA: US Army War College, 1980), chaps. 1–2, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-142/index.html. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Enigma,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/enigma/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did counting letters fail against cipher machines?","a":"Because every keystroke moved the machine's rotors, changing the substitution from one letter to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cipher letters therefore appeared about equally often, destroying the frequency counts that had broken earlier systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Germans not suspect that Enigma was being read?","a":"Because their high command assured Dönitz that the machine could not be broken and offered radar, direction finding or a traitor instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British also disguised Ultra by flying reconnaissance aircraft over targets before attacking, and German countermeasures in 1943 and 1944 guarded only against capture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did suspicion of the convoy cipher grow in early 1943?","a":"Because the highly successful U-boat attack on convoy ON 166 in February 1943, when patrol lines were moved at the last minute, made American analysts suspect that Allied signals were being read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By May the compromise of Naval Cypher No. 3 had been established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cryptanalysis"},
    "ww2-897": {"questions":["Adopted by the German navy in 1926, the army in 1929 and the air force in 1934, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered settings that the British official history put at 150 million million million.","In February 1942 the German navy added a fourth wheel to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried by its U-boats, leaving British codebreakers unable to read submarine traffic for most of the year."],"abstract":"The <b>Enigma machine</b> was an electromechanical cipher machine, invented by the German engineer Arthur Scherbius after the First World War and sold commercially in the 1920s before the German armed forces adopted it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The navy took it up in 1926, the army in 1929 and the air force in 1934, and each then added changes such as plugs between keyboard and wheels, new wheels and more frequent changes of setting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Housed in a wooden box about 30 centimetres (1 foot) square, it sent the current from each key through a plugboard, three wheels and a reflector and back to light a lamp, and the wheels stepped so that the substitution changed with every letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the reflector returned the current by a different route, no letter could be enciphered as itself, a property the British codebreakers found invaluable for placing guessed text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With the wheel order, ring settings and about 140 million million possible plug pairings, the British official history reckoned that anyone without the day's setting faced about 150 million million million possibilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> British codebreakers had made little headway against such machines before the war, but the Poles had broken Enigma in the 1930s and in July 1939 handed their methods and replicas to the French and British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weaknesses lay as much in use as in design, for operators chose guessable message settings and the air force was lax about procedure, while the navy enforced its rules strictly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The navy also had eight wheels to choose from, and in February 1942 a fourth wheel on the U-boat machines blinded the codebreakers for most of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Captured material repeatedly supplied what they lacked, as when U-110 was boarded in May 1941 with its Enigma, wheels and key lists intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German naval commanders, sure the cipher was secure, blamed their losses on Allied radar and guarded against the physical capture of their ciphers rather than cryptanalysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 487–91, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Jennifer Wilcox, <i>Solving the Enigma: History of the Cryptanalytic Bombe</i>, rev. ed. (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2006), 1–18, https://archive.org/details/solving_enigma-nsa. [Open access]","A. P. Mahon, “The History of Hut Eight, 1939–1945,” The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/2, 3, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/hut8/hut8-000.htm. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Enigma,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/enigma/. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 3, <i>German Naval Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-024, chap. 7, “German Fears of the Compromise of Their Naval Cipher,” 83–84, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-024/SRH024-7.html. [Open access]","C. H. O’D. Alexander, “Cryptographic History of Work on the German Naval Enigma,” c. 1945, The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/1, 1–5, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/gne/gne-000.htm. [Open access]","Jerry C. Russell, <i>Ultra and the Campaign Against the U-boats in World War II</i>, SRH-142 (Carlisle Barracks, PA: US Army War College, 1980), chaps. 1–2, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-142/index.html. [Open access]","GCHQ, “Bletchley Park &amp; WWII,” https://www.gchq.gov.uk/section/history/bletchley-park-and-wwii. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the plugboard turn out to help the codebreakers?","a":"Because a plug joining one letter to another automatically joined the second back to the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Gordon Welchman built that reciprocity into a diagonal board for the British Bombe, which cut the possible wheel settings from thousands to a handful that could be tested by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did operators' habits matter so much?","a":"Because each clerk chose his own three-letter message settings, and some picked keyboard runs such as QWE, names, or settings that spelt out words such as HITLER across two messages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The air force and to a lesser degree the army were so sure of the machine that they grew lax about procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the navy not replace the machine outright?","a":"Because the U-boats' machines had to keep working with those of the rest of the navy, so Dönitz could only add a thin fourth wheel that could be set to act like a three-wheel machine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German analysts also saw physical capture rather than codebreaking as the danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Enigma machine"},
    "ww2-898": {"questions":["By D-Day the workforce at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was decrypting almost 5,000 German Enigma messages a day and sending them to Allied commanders by teleprinter within hours.","The Government Code and Cypher School moved from London to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the outbreak of war in 1939, after a rehearsal during the Munich crisis that had not gone well."],"abstract":"<b>Bletchley Park</b> is a Victorian manor and estate at Bletchley in England that served as the wartime home of the Government Code and Cypher School, the British codebreaking agency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The decision to move the school out of London on the outbreak of war was taken before 1939, and a rehearsal of the move during the Munich crisis of 1938 was judged by the Admiralty not to have worked well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mathematicians recruited from Cambridge, among them Alan Turing and Gordon Welchman, reported there in early September 1939 and were briefed on the Polish work against Enigma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The organisation began with about 150 staff and grew to nearly 10,000 in its wider network, and by 1945 three-quarters of them were women, six in ten of whom were in uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its sections worked in numbered wooden huts, such as Hut 8 for the naval Enigma, and from 1940 electromechanical Bombes, run mainly by members of the Women's Royal Naval Service, searched for the daily Enigma settings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its intelligence went out under the cover name Ultra, and from August 1941 special liaison units at headquarters overseas deciphered the signals and enforced the rules for handling them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the time of the Normandy landings its workforce of about 7,000 was decrypting almost 5,000 Enigma messages a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For 8 weeks from May to July 1944 the park intercepted German traffic on site to save time, and decoded messages reached Allied commanders by teleprinter within 2.5 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Partnership with the United States and the Commonwealth gave the agency a global reach, and in January 1944 it also became Britain's national technical authority for information security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The agency, by then called GCHQ, left Bletchley Park in 1946 and moved by way of the London suburbs to Cheltenham, while the wartime work stayed covered by the Official Secrets Act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 25–26, 570–71, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Jennifer Wilcox, <i>Solving the Enigma: History of the Cryptanalytic Bombe</i>, rev. ed. (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2006), 8–12, https://archive.org/details/solving_enigma-nsa. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Who Were the Codebreakers?,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/who-were-the-codebreakers/. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Bletchley Park and D-Day,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/bletchley-park-and-d-day/. [Open access]","GCHQ, “Bletchley Park &amp; WWII,” https://www.gchq.gov.uk/section/history/bletchley-park-and-wwii. [Open access]","GCHQ, “1950s Onwards,” https://www.gchq.gov.uk/section/history/1950s-onwards. [Open access]","A. P. Mahon, “The History of Hut Eight, 1939–1945,” The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/2, 1, 14, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/hut8/hut8-000.htm. [Open access]","J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Alan Mathison Turing,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, 2003, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Turing/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the move from London planned so carefully?","a":"Because the service ministries and the school had agreed that it would be wise to move to Bletchley as soon as war broke out, and the trial move during the Munich crisis had shown problems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After it the Admiralty even wanted its naval section to go elsewhere, before being persuaded otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Bletchley Park employ so many women?","a":"Because as codebreaking became more mechanised and the volume of intercepted messages grew, many more staff were needed than the universities could supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A large share were recruited from the women's services, the WRNS, the ATS and the WAAF, and by 1945 women made up three-quarters of the staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were special liaison units needed overseas?","a":"Because Ultra could not travel through ordinary channels, so each unit deciphered every signal on its link, circulated it within the headquarters and supervised the security precautions for handling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recipients were told the source only once this system was in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Bletchley Park"},
    "ww2-899": {"questions":["In a paper of 1936, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> described an imaginary machine following simple instructions on an endless tape, an idea later seen as a theoretical model of the computer.","Convicted in 1952 over a relationship with a man and given hormone treatment instead of prison, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was granted a posthumous royal pardon in 2013."],"abstract":"<b>Alan Turing</b> was a British mathematician, born in London on 23 June 1912, whose 1936 paper 'On Computable Numbers' gave a definition of computation and is regarded as the founding work of modern computer science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Fellow of King's College, Cambridge, from 1935, he studied at Princeton and returned to Britain in 1938, when he was recruited to help the Government Code and Cypher School.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From September 1939 he worked full-time at Bletchley Park, first in Dilly Knox's Enigma research section, and he became the best known of the new recruits who transformed British codebreaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Building on Polish work and an existing interest in machine computation, he designed a machine that tested settings against guessed plain text rather than message indicators, which with Gordon Welchman's diagonal board became the British Bombe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first Bombes were delivered in 1940, and over 200 were eventually built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Turing took up the German naval Enigma because, as he put it, no one else was doing anything about it and he could have it to himself, and he became the first head of Hut 8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the end of 1939 he had worked out the naval indicator system, and he devised the statistical method called Banburismus, which reduced the number of wheel orders the Bombes had to run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was in the United States from November 1942 to March 1943, and after the war he designed the ACE computer at the National Physical Laboratory before moving to Manchester in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1952 he was convicted over a sexual relationship with a man and, to avoid prison, accepted hormone treatment, after which his security clearance was withdrawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He died of cyanide poisoning at his home in Wilmslow on 7 June 1954, which an inquest ruled to be suicide, and he was granted a posthumous royal pardon in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Hodges, “Alan Turing,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, first published 2002, substantive revision 30 September 2013, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/turing/. [Open access]","J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Alan Mathison Turing,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, 2003, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Turing/. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “10 Things to Know about Alan Turing,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/10-things-to-know-about-alan-turing/. [Open access]","A. P. Mahon, “The History of Hut Eight, 1939–1945,” The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/2, 1, 14, 20, transcribed at http://www.ellsbury.com/hut8/hut8-000.htm. [Open access]","Jennifer Wilcox, <i>Solving the Enigma: History of the Cryptanalytic Bombe</i>, rev. ed. (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2006), 7–10, https://archive.org/details/solving_enigma-nsa. [Open access]","GCHQ, “Alan Turing,” 11 July 2019, https://www.gchq.gov.uk/information/alan-turing. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 494, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Enigma,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/enigma/. [Open access]","GCHQ, “Bletchley Park &amp; WWII,” https://www.gchq.gov.uk/section/history/bletchley-park-and-wwii. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Turing's machine not rely on message indicators?","a":"Because the Polish Bomba had searched for matches in the indicators, and the Germans had already changed how indicators were used once and could do so again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A machine working from assumed plain text did not depend on them and simply ruled out every setting that could not produce it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was his war work so little recognised in his lifetime?","a":"Because his wartime achievements remained totally secret, so his post-war ideas on computing, which led the field in 1946, were little appreciated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was forbidden by the Official Secrets Act even to talk about the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was his clearance withdrawn after his conviction?","a":"Because security officers now regarded a man with complete knowledge of GCHQ's work as a security risk, and even a holiday in Greece in 1953 alarmed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At his trial GCHQ's head of cryptanalysis, Hugh Alexander, had been allowed to speak for him and called him a national asset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Alan Turing"},
    "ww2-900": {"questions":["To hide the existence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the British usually flew a reconnaissance aircraft over a target before attacking it, so that the Germans would blame the aircraft rather than Enigma.","Special liaison units at Allied headquarters deciphered and guarded every signal carrying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the intelligence from broken high-grade enemy ciphers that only indoctrinated officers could see."],"abstract":"<b>Ultra</b> was the cover name the British gave from June 1941 to intelligence obtained by breaking high-grade enemy ciphers, above all the German Enigma, and the word came to stand for that whole source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From January 1940 the Admiralty had sent such intelligence to selected flag officers in messages prefixed 'Hydro', and in June 1941, the first month in which the naval Enigma was read currently, it replaced that prefix with 'Ultra'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ultra signals went out in a one-time pad cipher that gave total protection, under strict new rules governing who could handle them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Overseas commands received them through special liaison units, linked to Cairo from August 1941, which deciphered every signal on the link, circulated it within the headquarters and supervised its security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only recipients indoctrinated as to the source could see the material, and as the partnership with the United States grew, small groups of American personnel were brought into Bletchley Park itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> To keep the source hidden, the British took no action based on Ultra alone without first giving the Germans a plausible explanation, most often a reconnaissance aircraft flown over the target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German navy blamed its rising U-boat losses on Allied radar and guarded its ciphers against capture rather than codebreaking, so its suspicions were largely deflected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By D-Day the codebreakers at Bletchley Park were decrypting almost 5,000 Enigma messages a day, and during the landings decoded messages reached Allied commanders within 2.5 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The source stayed secret long after the war, and a study written for the United States Army War College in 1980 noted that it had become generally known only in the previous few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first published accounts also carried errors, and the official history rejected the claim that Coventry was left undefended in November 1940 to protect Ultra, showing that countermeasures were prepared and put into effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), 140, 346, 536, 570–71, https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Jennifer Wilcox, <i>Solving the Enigma: History of the Cryptanalytic Bombe</i>, rev. ed. (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2006), 16, https://archive.org/details/solving_enigma-nsa. [Open access]","Jerry C. Russell, <i>Ultra and the Campaign Against the U-boats in World War II</i>, SRH-142 (Carlisle Barracks, PA: US Army War College, 1980), chaps. 1–2, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-142/index.html. [Open access]","National Security Agency, <i>Battle of the Atlantic</i>, vol. 3, <i>German Naval Communications Intelligence</i>, SRH-024, chap. 7, “German Fears of the Compromise of Their Naval Cipher,” 83–84, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/ETO/Ultra/SRH-024/SRH024-7.html. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Bletchley Park and D-Day,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/bletchley-park-and-d-day/. [Open access]","GCHQ, “Bletchley Park &amp; WWII,” https://www.gchq.gov.uk/section/history/bletchley-park-and-wwii. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Who Were the Codebreakers?,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/who-were-the-codebreakers/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the British fly reconnaissance aircraft before acting on Ultra?","a":"Because when they then attacked, the Germans would believe their forces had been spotted from the air rather than given away by Enigma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dönitz still suspected his ciphers, but German headquarters assured him the machine could not be broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German suspicions not lead to a new cipher system?","a":"Because the German navy's countermeasures were aimed at the physical capture of its ciphers, not at cryptanalysis, and its losses were officially explained by Allied radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The U-boat machine also had to keep working with the rest of the navy, so it could only be modified, not replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Coventry story doubted?","a":"Because the official history found that Coventry was not identified as the target until about 3 pm on 14 November 1940, that the name was not spelt out in an intercepted signal, and that extensive countermeasures were prepared and put into effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ultra (cryptography)"},
    "ww2-801": {"questions":["An order captured from the naval defence force told troops to gather Filipinos in one place before killing them, a document that the Tokyo tribunal cited in judging the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1945.","Hanged in February 1946 after an American commission found the killings too widespread to be unknown to him, General Yamashita was tried above all for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried out by troops he commanded."],"abstract":"The <b>Manila massacre</b> was the killing of Filipino and other civilians by Japanese forces in Manila in February 1945, while American and Filipino troops fought to retake the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When it became clear that Manila would be lost, the Tokyo tribunal found, massacres were committed all over the city, together with rape and arson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Yamashita Tomoyuki had planned no defence of Manila, but its defence fell to a naval force of nearly 16,000 men under Admiral Iwabuchi, who would not leave until his naval missions were done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A captured file of that force's orders told troops to gather Filipinos together before killing them so as to save ammunition and labour, and a brigade order of 13 February said that everyone on the battlefield who was not Japanese was to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the German Club, people sheltering from shellfire were trapped behind a burning barricade and shot or bayoneted as they ran, and in Fort Santiago American troops found most of the male hostages dead in the dungeons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An estimated 100,000 Filipino civilians died during the month of fighting, which ended on 3 March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One assessment of the records says they were massacred by retreating Japanese troops or killed by the American bombardment, and both happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> General MacArthur forbade bombing the walled Intramuros district from the air because it would kill thousands of civilians, but his commanders used a week of heavy artillery instead, and the official history doubts that this saved any lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American military commission in Manila heard 286 witnesses and found the crimes so widespread that Yamashita must have permitted or ordered them, while his defence blamed naval troops who ignored his order to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was not charged with ordering the killings but with failing to control his troops, the Supreme Court refused to intervene over two dissents, and he was hanged on 23 February 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1036–41, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), chap. 16, “Manila: The Last Resistance,” 294, 298–99, 307, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-16.html. [Open access]","Robert Ross Smith, <i>Triumph in the Philippines</i>, United States Army in World War II: The War in the Pacific (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1963), chap. 13, “Manila: The Defenders and the Defenses,” 240–42, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Triumph/USA-P-Triumph-13.html. [Open access]","<i>In re Yamashita</i>, 327 U.S. 1 (1946), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/327/1. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, “Case No. 21: Trial of General Tomoyuki Yamashita,” in <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 4 (London: His Majesty's Stationery Office, 1948), 1–5, 34–35, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-4/Law-Reports_Vol-4.pdf. [Open access]","Greg Bradsher, “The Exploitation of Captured and Seized Japanese Records Relating to War Crimes, 1942–1945,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 154–55, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 32, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the killing happen in a city the Japanese army had meant to give up?","a":"Because the naval troops left in Manila under Admiral Iwabuchi put their naval orders first and would not withdraw, and captured orders from that force and from an army brigade told men to kill Filipinos and everyone on the battlefield who was not Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the 100,000 dead not all be blamed on the massacres?","a":"Because the city was also destroyed by a month of street fighting; the records point both to killings by Japanese troops and to deaths under the American bombardment, including the artillery fired into Intramuros once MacArthur had ruled out bombing from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Yamashita condemned for killings he was not shown to have ordered?","a":"Because the charge was that he had failed in his duty as commander to control his troops and so permitted the atrocities; the commission found them too widespread to be unknown to him, and the Supreme Court, over two dissents, would not overturn the trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Manila massacre","places":["city:1159151525"]},
    "ww2-802": {"questions":["Meant, the Tokyo tribunal judged, to stop an Allied landing from freeing them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took starving prisoners from an airfield camp on the coast to the village of Ranau.","Remembered every 15 August at a park on the old camp site, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are called by the Australian War Memorial the greatest single atrocity committed against Australians in war."],"abstract":"The <b>Sandakan death marches</b> were the forced marches of Australian and British prisoners of war from Sandakan, on the north-east coast of British North Borneo, to Ranau in the interior in 1945, which almost none of them survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The prisoners had been shipped from Singapore from July 1942, beginning with about 1,500 men of B Force, to build an airfield on a site chosen before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their rice was cut through 1944 and stopped in January 1945, though there seems to have been plenty in the camp, and in March starvation and withheld medicine killed 317 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fearing an Allied landing, the Japanese began moving the prisoners west in late January, and the Tokyo tribunal judged that the purpose of the marches was to prevent their liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The track crossed swamp, jungle and mountains on the slopes of Mount Kinabalu, and men already sick with malaria, dysentery and beriberi were made to carry food and ammunition for their guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A first group of 470 left in batches from 29 January, and a second of more than 500 left at the end of May as the camp was burned, and at each night's halt those who could not go on were shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the second group only 142 Australians and 61 British reached Ranau 4 weeks later, and there the survivors were starved, worked to death or shot, the last of them about 1 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The men too ill to march had been left at Sandakan, and by 15 August 1945 all of them had died or been killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six Australians who escaped survived, out of a total of prisoners that the Tokyo tribunal put at more than 2,000, the official history at some 2,500 and both the medical history and the memorial park on the camp site at about 2,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The camp commandant, Captain Hoshijima, and the officer commanding on the march were hanged after trials on Labuan, among 922 men tried in Australian war crimes courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1046–48, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Lionel Wigmore, <i>The Japanese Thrust</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 1, vol. 4 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1957), chap. 25, “Camps in Borneo, Japan and Elsewhere,” 597, 600–604, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/RCDIG1070117. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – Sandakan,” online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan/sandakan. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – The War Crimes Trials,” online exhibition, https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan/warcrimes. [Open access]","Sabah Tourism Board, “Sandakan Memorial Park,” https://www.sabahtourism.com/destination/sandakan-memorial-park/. [Open access]","Allan S. Walker, <i>Middle East and Far East</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 5, vol. 2 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1953), chap. 27, “Other Prison Camps in the Far East,” 647–48, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417561. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the prisoners moved at all in 1945?","a":"Because the Japanese feared an Allied landing on Borneo, and the Tokyo tribunal concluded that the marches were meant to keep the prisoners from being liberated; the Australians landed on Tarakan on 1 May, and the second march began at the end of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so few reach Ranau alive?","a":"Because men already starving and sick were driven over swamp and mountain tracks on tiny rations, made to carry their guards' loads, and shot where they could not go on, so that only about 200 of the second group arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the totals given differently?","a":"Because nearly every prisoner died and the camp records were burned, so the count rests on later reconstruction: the Tokyo tribunal spoke of more than 2,000 prisoners, the army's official history of some 2,500, and the medical history and the memorial park of about 2,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sandakan death marches"},
    "ww2-803": {"questions":["Shooting about 300 villagers at Distomo in June 1944 was plain calculated murder, judges ruled in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though they allowed that hostages might lawfully be shot as a last resort.","Judges in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote in 1948 that an international agreement on hostages was badly needed, and the Geneva Convention of 1949 answered by prohibiting the taking of hostages outright."],"abstract":"The <b>Hostages Trial</b> was the seventh of the twelve American trials held at Nuremberg after the main war crimes tribunal, against twelve German generals indicted in May 1947 for their conduct in south-eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were charged with the murder of hundreds of thousands of civilians in Greece, Yugoslavia and Albania in reprisal for attacks on German troops, under orders to kill 100 'hostages' for each German soldier killed and 50 for each one wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One defendant killed himself and another was too ill for trial, and on 19 February 1948 the tribunal convicted eight and acquitted two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wilhelm List and Walter Kuntze were sentenced to life imprisonment and the rest to 7 to 20 years, but most were released in 1951 and 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The main tribunal had already condemned the practice, quoting an order of 16 September 1941 by Wilhelm Keitel that spoke of fifty or a hundred lives in the occupied Soviet Union for each German life taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In Serbia in October 1941, the court found, 2,300 people were shot at Kragujevac and 1,700 at Kraljevo, and a report of December counted 11,164 shot of the 31,338 due under the hundred-to-one order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Greece on 10 June 1944 troops of one regiment shot about 300 men, women and children at Distomo, which the court called plain calculated murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet the judges held that an occupier might take hostages and, as a last resort after a public warning and a court-martial finding, even shoot them, a rule resting on a theory of collective responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hague regulations of 1907 forbade only general penalties on a population for acts of individuals for which it could not be held jointly and severally responsible, and the court noted that no treaty outlawed the killing of hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Geneva Convention on civilians of August 1949 closed that gap, stating that the taking of hostages is prohibited and listing it among the grave breaches of the convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11, “The Hostage Case,” Opinion and Judgment of Military Tribunal V (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), 1230, 1248–53, 1275–76, 1307–8, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings, Case #7: The Hostage Case,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings-case-7-the-hostage-case. [Open access]","Harvard Law School Library, “NMT Case 7: The Hostage Case,” Nuremberg Trials Project, https://nuremberg.law.harvard.edu/nmt_7_intro. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Murder and Ill-Treatment of Civilian Population” (Nuremberg, 1 October 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","<i>Geneva Convention Relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War</i>, 12 August 1949, arts. 34, 147, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva07.asp. [Open access]","<i>Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex: Regulations Concerning the Laws and Customs of War on Land</i>, The Hague, 18 October 1907, art. 50, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the court not simply declare all hostage killing illegal?","a":"Because it found no treaty that forbade it, saw the practice as resting on a theory of collective responsibility, and so held that hostages might be shot as a last resort, but only after a public warning, a court-martial finding and a number no greater than the offence it was meant to deter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the killings in Serbia still murder under that rule?","a":"Because the court's conditions were never met: people were shot in fixed ratios of a hundred for one, without trial and often far from where the attack had happened, and the court held that such reprisals without judicial finding were themselves unlawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the law change in 1949?","a":"Because the trial had shown that existing treaties left the question open, and the Geneva Convention on civilians answered it by stating that the taking of hostages is prohibited and by making it a grave breach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Hostages Trial"},
    "ww2-804": {"questions":["The Admiralty complained in 1942 that the Air Staff expected to win the war not by defeating the enemy's forces but only by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the German population and destroying its industry.","Aimed at the homes of industrial workers rather than at their factories, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left some 7,500,000 Germans homeless by 1945, yet the bombing survey found that they went on working."],"abstract":"<b>Dehousing</b> was the British wartime word for destroying the homes of a city's people by bombing, in the belief that this would cripple war production and break morale, and in 1942 the Admiralty complained that the Air Staff hoped to win the war only by dehousing the German population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Air Staff paper of September 1941 said that the ultimate aim of attacking a town was to break the morale of its people by making it physically uninhabitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A directive of 14 February 1942 made attacks on the morale of the enemy civil population, and in particular of the industrial workers, the main task of Bomber Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 30 March 1942 Lord Cherwell sent Churchill a minute arguing that each ton of bombs on a built-up area made 100 to 200 people homeless, so that the bombers due by mid-1943 could turn a third of the German population out of house and home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Losing one's house, the minute claimed from raids on Hull and elsewhere, hurt morale more than losing friends or even relatives, while Sir Henry Tizard found important fallacies in its figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In late July 1943 raids on Hamburg raised a firestorm that a German report called beyond all human imagination, and 74 per cent of its closely built residential districts were heavily damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> German estimates put Hamburg's dead at 60,000 to 100,000, and the American bombing survey reckoned that air attack killed at least 305,000 people in Germany and left some 7,500,000 homeless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the House of Lords in February 1944 the Bishop of Chichester called the policy obliteration, and the government replied that the RAF had never indulged in pure terror raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The survey found that German morale sagged but that people kept working efficiently as long as the means of production remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Churchill wrote on 28 March 1945 that the bombing of German cities simply for the sake of increasing the terror should be reviewed, though he had backed the area offensive for 4 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 1, <i>Preparation</i> (London: Her Majesty's Stationery Office, 1961), 331–34, 372, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-1. [Open access]","Charles Webster and Noble Frankland, <i>The Strategic Air Offensive against Germany, 1939–1945</i>, vol. 3, <i>Victory</i> (London: Her Majesty's Stationery Office, 1961), 112, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/strat-air-off-germ-vol-3. [Open access]","John Herington, <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), chap. 12, “Experiments in Bombing Technique,” 298–99, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417791. [Open access]","John Herington, <i>Air War against Germany and Italy, 1939–1943</i>, Australia in the War of 1939–1945, series 3, vol. 3 (Canberra: Australian War Memorial, 1954), chap. 22, “Hamburg – The Last Raids on Italy – The Struggle against Night Fighters,” 587–88, https://www.awm.gov.au/collection/C1417800. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1945), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/AAF/USSBS/ETO-Summary.html. [Open access]","“Bombing Policy,” <i>Hansard</i>, House of Lords, 5th ser., vol. 130 (9 February 1944), cols. 737–55, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1944/feb/09/bombing-policy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did planners aim at homes rather than factories?","a":"Because the RAF could not locate single factories at night often enough, and an air staff paper argued that making a town uninhabitable would break its people's morale; Cherwell's minute added that losing a home hurt morale more than losing friends or relatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the policy not break Germany as expected?","a":"Because, the American survey found, Germans under police control kept working efficiently while the means of production lasted, and slack in the German economy let it absorb much of the damage, though some 7,500,000 people were made homeless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the policy attacked in Britain during the war?","a":"Because critics such as the Bishop of Chichester argued that destroying whole towns because parts held military targets was obliteration rather than a justifiable act of war, and by March 1945 Churchill himself wanted bombing for the sake of terror reviewed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Dehousing"},
    "ww2-805": {"questions":["Pointing to belligerents that had never ratified them, German defendants argued that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not bind them, but the Nuremberg tribunal held their rules recognised by all civilised nations.","A preamble leaving soldiers and civilians under the laws of humanity and the dictates of the public conscience wherever the written rules ran out was part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1899 and 1907."],"abstract":"The <b>Hague Conventions</b> of 1899 and 1907 were treaties drawn up at peace conferences in The Hague that codified the laws and customs of war on land, and their rules were the main written law of land war when the Second World War began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 1907 convention, which replaced that of 29 July 1899 between the powers that ratified it, carried regulations on who counted as a lawful combatant, how prisoners were to be treated and what an occupying army might do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They forbade killing an enemy who had surrendered, declaring that no quarter would be given and bombarding undefended towns, and required an occupier to respect family honour, lives and private property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For cases the regulations did not cover, the preamble left inhabitants and belligerents under the principles of the law of nations, the laws of humanity and the dictates of the public conscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet the rules applied only between contracting powers and only if every belligerent was a party, and the convention named no crime, set no penalty and created no court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Both weaknesses mattered after 1939, since several belligerents had not ratified the 1907 convention, among them Italy and Bulgaria, though the Soviet Union had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal answered that by 1939 the rules were recognised by all civilised nations and declaratory of the laws and customs of war, so it did not need to rule on the participation clause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American army's own manual of 1940 still said that soldiers would not be punished for such offences committed on the orders of their government or commanders, a rule it changed in November 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> German orders broke the code openly, as when Hitler ordered on 18 October 1942 that enemy commandos, even uniformed soldiers trying to surrender, be slaughtered to the last man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tokyo tribunal held that the 1907 convention had formally embodied the customary law that prisoners are in the power of the government that captures them, which must protect them from mistreatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex: Regulations Concerning the Laws and Customs of War on Land</i>, The Hague, 18 October 1907, preamble, arts. 2–4, annex arts. 1, 23, 25, 46, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","<i>Convention (II) with Respect to the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex</i>, The Hague, 29 July 1899, preamble, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/hague02.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “The Law Relating to War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity” (Nuremberg, 1 October 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawre.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “The Law of the Charter” (Nuremberg, 1 October 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawch.asp. [Open access]","<i>Trials of War Criminals before the Nuernberg Military Tribunals under Control Council Law No. 10</i>, vol. 11, “The High Command Case,” Judgment of Military Tribunal V-A (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1950), 525–26, 531–33, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI/2011525364_NT_war-criminals_Vol-XI.pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. II, “The Law” (Tokyo, 1948), 27–29, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-2.html. [Open access]","United States War Department, <i>Rules of Land Warfare</i>, Basic Field Manual FM 27-10 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1940), 1, 86–87, with Changes no. 1 (15 November 1944), Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/Fm27-10. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Germany claim the conventions did not bind it?","a":"Because the 1907 convention applied only between contracting powers and only if every belligerent was a party, and several belligerents, among them Italy and Bulgaria, had never ratified it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Nuremberg tribunal reject that defence?","a":"Because it held that by 1939 the rules were recognised by all civilised nations and were declaratory of the existing laws and customs of war, so they bound Germany whatever the treaty's participation clause said.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could men be tried for breaking rules that named no crime?","a":"Because, the tribunal said, military courts had long tried and punished individuals for violating the rules of land warfare laid down by the convention, although it set no sentence and created no court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Hague Conventions of 1899 and 1907"},
    "ww2-806": {"questions":["Because the Soviet Union had never joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, German officials claimed that its captured soldiers had no protection, and about 3.3 million of 5.7 million died.","Banning reprisals against captives and naming neutral protecting powers to oversee their treatment, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set the rules under which 3.6 per cent of British and American prisoners in German hands died."],"abstract":"The <b>Geneva Convention on Prisoners of War</b>, signed on 27 July 1929, was the treaty that governed the treatment of captured soldiers in the Second World War, largely replacing the shorter rules of the Hague regulations of 1907 between its parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It required that prisoners be humanely treated at all times and protected against violence, insults and public curiosity, and it banned reprisals against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neutral protecting powers were to oversee its working, and a central information agency, which the International Committee of the Red Cross could propose, was to pass news of prisoners to their home countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stayed in force between the belligerents that were parties to it even when an enemy was not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan signed it but never ratified it, and the Soviet Union was not a party at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Of the 231,000 British and American prisoners held by Germany about 8,300, or 3.6 per cent, died in German custody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of some 5.7 million Soviet soldiers captured, about 3.3 million were dead by the end of the war, a toll that German officials partly excused by the Soviet absence from the convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Admiral Canaris protested in September 1941 that general international law still forbade killing or injuring helpless prisoners, but Keitel noted that this was the destruction of an ideology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan promised in January 1942 to apply the convention 'mutatis mutandis', with the necessary changes, while its war ministry held that it had no binding power on Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 27 per cent of the 132,000 American and British prisoners in Japanese hands died, and the conventions of 1949 replaced the treaty of 1929 with rules that bind parties even towards an enemy that accepts and applies them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Convention Relative to the Treatment of Prisoners of War</i>, Geneva, 27 July 1929, preamble, arts. 2, 79, 82, 86, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva02.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Murder and Ill-Treatment of Prisoners of War” (Nuremberg, 1 October 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","<i>Geneva Convention Relative to the Treatment of Prisoners of War</i>, 12 August 1949, arts. 2, 134, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva03.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VIII, “Conventional War Crimes (Atrocities)” (Tokyo, 1948), 1096–99, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-8.html. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 32–33, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","United States War Department, <i>Rules of Land Warfare</i>, Basic Field Manual FM 27-10 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1940), 1, Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/Fm27-10. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did British and American prisoners in Germany mostly survive?","a":"Because German policy towards prisoners from Britain and the United States, countries the Nazis regarded as racial equals, differed sharply from its treatment of Soviet prisoners, and only about 3.6 per cent of the 231,000 British and American prisoners it held died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the convention no shield for Soviet prisoners?","a":"Because the Soviet Union was not a party, and German leaders used that to justify a deliberate policy of killing and starvation, overruling Canaris's protest that general international law protected helpless prisoners anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japan promise to apply a treaty it had not ratified?","a":"Because its foreign minister feared for the several hundred thousand Japanese living in Allied countries, so Japan offered to apply the convention with the necessary changes while insisting it was not bound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Geneva Convention on Prisoners of War"},
    "ww2-807": {"questions":["Local reports that claimed 20,000 Tatar deserters where only 479 are now recognised helped justify the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the State Defence Committee ordered on 11 May 1944.","Ukraine's parliament recognised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as genocide in November 2015 and made 18 May, the day the trains began to leave in 1944, a day of remembrance."],"abstract":"The <b>deportation of the Crimean Tatars</b> was the removal by the Soviet state in May 1944 of the whole Crimean Tatar people, a Muslim, Turkic-speaking nation native to Crimea, to Central Asia and other regions of the USSR.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It belonged to a series of wartime deportations that sent over two million Soviet citizens of minority nationalities, among them Volga Germans, Chechens, Ingush, Kalmyks and Balkars, into exile as special settlers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 10 May 1944 Lavrenti Beria, head of the NKVD, proposed the operation to Stalin, and the next day the State Defence Committee ordered it, accusing the Tatars of collective collaboration with the German occupiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The charge rested on reports by local officials and partisan leaders that exaggerated the part of Tatar self-defence units formed by the Germans and claimed 20,000 Tatar deserters where 479 are recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some Crimean Tatars had fought against Soviet partisans, but the whole nation was punished, and most Tatar men then serving in the Red Army were sent to labour camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From 18 May NKVD troops gave families 20 to 30 minutes to pack and loaded them into cattle trains, and within 3 days 180,014 people, mostly women, children and old people, had been deported, most of them to Uzbekistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The death toll is disputed: an NKVD document records 44,887 deaths among the exiles from Crimea in 1944 and 1945, while a census by the Crimean Tatar movement claimed that 46.2 per cent died within 18 months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crimea was stripped of Tatar place names, books and monuments, and in 1946 its autonomous republic was reduced to an ordinary region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of 5 September 1967 lifted the charge of collective collaboration, but the Tatars had no right to return to Crimea until 1989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In November 2015 Ukraine's parliament recognised the deportation as genocide, and in 2016 the European Parliament condemned restrictions on its annual commemoration in Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aurélie Campana, “Sürgün: The Crimean Tatars' Deportation and Exile,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 16 June 2008, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/surgun-the-crimean-tatars-deportation-and-exile/. [Open access]","Aurélie Campana, “The Soviet Massive Deportations: A Chronology,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 5 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-soviet-massive-deportations-a-chronology/. [Open access]","Nicolas Werth, “Mass Crimes under Stalin, 1930–1953,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 14 March 2008, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/mass-crimes-under-stalin-1930-1953/. [Open access]","Mykhailo Zhdan and Arkadii Zhukovsky, “Tatars,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies; first published in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 5, 1993), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CT%5CA%5CTatars.htm. [Open access]","“Crimean Tatars,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, updated 2014), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CC%5CR%5CCrimeanTatars.htm. [Open access]","European Parliament, “Resolution of 4 February 2016 on the Human Rights Situation in Crimea, in Particular of the Crimean Tatars (2016/2556(RSP)),” <i>Official Journal of the European Union</i> C 35 (31 January 2018), EUR-Lex, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:52016IP0043. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a whole people punished for what some of its members had done?","a":"Because Soviet policy in the war declared entire nationalities enemies of the regime, and the decree of 11 May 1944 blamed the Tatars collectively, relying on reports that inflated their collaboration and their desertion from the Red Army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the number of dead so hard to fix?","a":"Because Soviet records are incomplete and lump all exiles from Crimea together, while the Crimean Tatar movement's own census gave a far higher share; estimates range from about a fifth of the deportees to nearly half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the deportation still matter politically?","a":"Because the Tatars could not go home until 1989, Ukraine has called the deportation genocide, and the European Parliament has condemned limits placed on its annual commemoration in Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Deportation of the Crimean Tatars"},
    "ww2-808": {"questions":["Bodies left in the prisons of Lwów by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were displayed by the Germans, who falsely blamed the Jews and so helped set off a pogrom on 1 July 1941.","Ordered on 23 June 1941 to shoot prisoners accused of counter-revolutionary crimes rather than let them fall into German hands, guards carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the annexed eastern lands."],"abstract":"The <b>NKVD prisoner massacres</b> were the killings of thousands of prisoners by the Soviet security police in the lands the Soviet Union had annexed in 1939 and 1940, in the days after Germany invaded on 22 June 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After 1939 the NKVD took over 145 prisons in the annexed Polish territories and packed them far beyond capacity with people it called counter-revolutionaries, most of them Poles until 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When war came, the railways were told to carry 45,483 prisoners east from the Ukrainian, Belarusian and Lithuanian republics in 1,537 wagons, a plan that could not be carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the evening of 23 June Lavrenti Beria ordered the NKVD of Belarus to evacuate its prisons at once, and that night its western districts were told to shoot those accused of counter-revolutionary crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Prisoners were shot in cellars and courtyards or driven east on foot in columns where stragglers were killed, and in some towns guards fled and later came back to finish the killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Berezwecz the NKVD dug pits in the prison yard and shot prisoners in the cellars while loud music covered the noise of the shots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Łuck on 23 June prisoners were sorted by nationality into two courtyards before guards opened fire on them from the walls and towers, a survivor recalled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Lwów estimates of the dead run from 2,000 to 3,000 in the American Holocaust museum's account to about 4,000 in a Polish one, and most of the victims there were recently arrested Ukrainian nationalists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the Germans arrived on 30 June they displayed the bodies and falsely blamed the Jews, stirring a pogrom in which several hundred to several thousand Jews were murdered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The total is unknown because NKVD records are incomplete and often false, and one Polish estimate puts the dead at 20,000 to 24,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Lwów Pogrom of July 1, 1941,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-lwow-pogrom-of-july-1-1941. [Open access]","Ewa Siemaszko, “Według planu: ‘Rozładowanie’ więzień po sowiecku,” in <i>Lato 1941: polski dramat</i>, special supplement to <i>Rzeczpospolita</i>, 22–23 June 2011, Institute of National Remembrance, Przystanek Historia, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/download/166/77689/22062011Lato1941.pdf, 2–3. [in Polish] [Open access]","Ewa Siemaszko, “Dzieło NKWD we Lwowie: Masakry w piwnicach, celach i na dziedzińcu,” in <i>Lato 1941: polski dramat</i>, special supplement to <i>Rzeczpospolita</i>, 22–23 June 2011, Institute of National Remembrance, Przystanek Historia, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/download/166/77689/22062011Lato1941.pdf, 4. [in Polish] [Open access]","Sławomir Kalbarczyk, “Tysiąc ofiar z Berezwecza,” in <i>Lato 1941: polski dramat</i>, special supplement to <i>Rzeczpospolita</i>, 22–23 June 2011, Institute of National Remembrance, Przystanek Historia, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/download/166/77689/22062011Lato1941.pdf, 4. [in Polish] [Open access]","Jean-Yves Potel, “Chronology of Mass Violence in Poland, 1918–1948,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 10 May 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/chronology-of-mass-violence-in-poland-1918-1948/. [Open access]","Rafał Guz, “Masakra w Łucku: Rozmowa z ocalałym więźniem Władysławem Siemaszką,” in <i>Lato 1941: polski dramat</i>, special supplement to <i>Rzeczpospolita</i>, 22–23 June 2011, Institute of National Remembrance, Przystanek Historia, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/download/166/77689/22062011Lato1941.pdf, 8. [in Polish] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the NKVD kill prisoners instead of releasing them?","a":"Because the Soviet authorities would not let people they counted as counter-revolutionaries fall into German hands, and when there were no wagons to move them east, orders from Moscow to shoot whole categories of prisoners settled their fate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the victims hard to count?","a":"Because NKVD records left out some of the places where people were held and are often contradictory or false, so even for Lwów the estimates run from 2,000 to about 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the massacres lead to violence against Jews?","a":"Because the Germans and Ukrainian nationalist activists who entered Lwów displayed the bodies and blamed Jews for the killings, drawing on the conspiracy theory that equated Jews with communism, although Soviet authorities had done them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"NKVD prisoner massacres"},
    "ww2-809": {"questions":["A term that entered common use during the Bosnian war of 1992, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now applied to the expulsions and massacres by which wartime regimes emptied whole regions of a people.","Unlike genocide, whose aim is to destroy a group, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> aims to drive a people off a territory, though the wartime deportations show how often the two shade into each other."],"abstract":"<b>Ethnic cleansing</b> is the forcible removal of a population defined by ethnicity or religion from a territory, by violence, terror or expulsion, so that another group can hold the land alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A United Nations commission on the former Yugoslavia defined it as rendering an area ethnically homogeneous by using force or intimidation to remove persons of given groups, and called it contrary to international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The phrase entered common use during the war in Bosnia in 1992 and has since been applied retrospectively to earlier events, many of them in the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a distinct crime in international law, and its aim of removing a people differs from the intent to destroy one that defines genocide, though in practice the two can shade into each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A short definition calls it the murder or forced relocation of one ethnic group by another so that the latter becomes dominant or alone in an area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The war gave cover to it on a vast scale: Germany deported about 450,000 Poles and Jews from its annexed western Polish lands by 1941, and around Zamość 300 villages were emptied between November 1941 and August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Independent State of Croatia, where 1.9 million Serbs made up about 30 per cent of the people, the Ustaša regime sought to settle the Serbian question by expulsion, forced conversion and extermination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Serbian Četnik commanders in turn reported destroying Muslim villages in early 1943, in operations whose victims are estimated at 10,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Soviet Union deported over two million citizens of punished nationalities, among them Volga Germans, Chechens and Crimean Tatars, and in 1943 Ukrainian nationalists set out to cleanse Volhynia of its Poles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the war's end Poland and Czechoslovakia expelled their German populations, over 11.5 million people in all, a removal one historian treats chiefly as ethnic cleansing given an international stamp at Potsdam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Norman Naimark, “Ethnic Cleansing,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 4 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/ethnic-cleansing/. [Open access]","Yves Tomić, “Massacres in Dismembered Yugoslavia, 1941–1945,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 7 June 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/massacres-in-dismembered-yugoslavia-1941-1945/. [Open access]","Nicolas Werth, “Mass Crimes under Stalin, 1930–1953,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 14 March 2008, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/mass-crimes-under-stalin-1930-1953/. [Open access]","Jean-Yves Potel, “Chronology of Mass Violence in Poland, 1918–1948,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 10 May 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/chronology-of-mass-violence-in-poland-1918-1948/. [Open access]","Wiener Holocaust Library, “Ethnic Cleansing,” glossary, <i>The Holocaust Explained</i>, https://www.theholocaustexplained.org/ethnic-cleansing/. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, <i>Final Report of the Commission of Experts Established Pursuant to Security Council Resolution 780 (1992)</i>, S/1994/674 (27 May 1994), paras. 129–30, International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia, https://www.icty.org/x/file/About/OTP/un_commission_of_experts_report1994_en.pdf. [Open access]","Aurélie Campana, “The Soviet Massive Deportations: A Chronology,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 5 November 2007, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/the-soviet-massive-deportations-a-chronology/. [Open access]","Aurélie Campana, “Sürgün: The Crimean Tatars' Deportation and Exile,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 16 June 2008, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/surgun-the-crimean-tatars-deportation-and-exile/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is ethnic cleansing not simply called genocide?","a":"Because its purpose is the forced removal of a people from a territory rather than the destruction of the group, so tribunals have usually treated its acts as crimes against humanity such as deportation, although expulsion can turn into mass murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Second World War make ethnic cleansing so common?","a":"Because war gave regimes both the means and the excuse to move or kill whole populations, from German resettlement in Poland to the Ustaša war on Croatia's Serbs and Soviet deportations of punished nationalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a 1990s word used for events of the 1940s?","a":"Because the term, popularised during the Bosnian war, gave a name to a pattern historians recognised in earlier removals, such as the wartime deportations and the post-war expulsion of Germans from Poland and Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ethnic cleansing"},
    "ww2-810": {"questions":["On Sunday 11 July 1943 nationalist units attacked 99 localities at once, the bloodiest day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Poland has marked with a national day of remembrance since 2017.","Polish estimates of 80,000 to 100,000 dead and Ukrainian estimates of 40,000 to 60,000 divide historians of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as does the question of whether to call them genocide."],"abstract":"The <b>massacres of Poles in Volhynia and Eastern Galicia</b> were the killings of Polish civilians in 1943–1945 by the Bandera faction of the Organisation of Ukrainian Nationalists and its military wing, the Ukrainian Insurgent Army, in the German-occupied south-east of interwar Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The perpetrators' documents called the planned extermination of the Poles an 'anti-Polish operation', carried out in lands they regarded as indigenously Ukrainian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Interwar Polish governments had pressed forced assimilation on the region's Ukrainians, and the Soviet deportations and the German murder of the Jews showed the nationalists how a population could be removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first mass killing came on 9 February 1943, when a UPA company killed at least 155 Poles at the Parośla colony, and in March several thousand Ukrainian policemen in German service deserted to the UPA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In June 1943 the UPA commander in Volhynia, Dmytro Klyachkivsky, gave oral orders to murder the Poles of whole districts, though no written order survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On Sunday 11 July 1943 UPA units, joined by mobilised peasants, attacked 99 localities at once, many while Poles were at church, and at least 10,000 Poles were killed that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Conservative Polish estimates put the Polish dead at 80,000 to 100,000 for 1943–1945, while Ukrainian historians estimate 40,000 to 60,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polish retaliation also killed Ukrainian civilians, 9,000 to 13,000 by Polish estimates and 17,000 to 24,000 by Ukrainian ones, though Polish historians reject equating it with planned extermination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polish prosecutors investigate the massacres as genocide, while some Ukrainian historians describe a second Polish-Ukrainian war or a peasant revolt, and a 1993 Ukrainian encyclopedia presented the UPA as protecting Ukrainians from German and Soviet repression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Since 2017 Poland has marked 11 July as a day of remembrance for the victims, who included Jews, Armenians and Czechs as well as Poles, and for the Ukrainians who refused to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mariusz Zajączkowski, “Polish-Ukrainian Historical Disputes over the Volhynian Massacres,” <i>Volhynia Massacre</i>, Institute of National Remembrance, https://volhyniamassacre.eu/zw2/controversies/183,Polish-Ukrainian-Historical-Disputes-over-the-Volhynian-Massacres.html. [Open access]","Mirosław Szumiło, “Dmytro Klyachkivsky ‘Klym Savur’: The Main Perpetrator of the Volhynian Genocide,” <i>Przystanek Historia</i>, Institute of National Remembrance, https://przystanekhistoria.pl/pa2/tematy/english-content/77551,Dmytro-Klyachkivsky-quotKlym-Savurquot-the-main-perpetrator-of-the-Volhynian-gen.html. [Open access]","Institute of National Remembrance, “Criminal Investigations,” <i>Volhynia Massacre</i>, https://volhyniamassacre.eu/zw2/investigations/182,Criminal-Investigations.html. [Open access]","Institute of National Remembrance, Lublin Branch, National Education Office, <i>The Neighbors' Blood: Genocide in Volhynia and Galicia, 1943–1945</i>, exhibition catalogue, https://volhyniamassacre.eu/download/99/45985/TheNeighborsBloodGenocideinVolhyniaandGalicia1943-1945exhibitionpreparedbytheLub.pdf. [Open access]","Jean-Yves Potel, “Chronology of Mass Violence in Poland, 1918–1948,” <i>Mass Violence and Resistance</i>, Sciences Po, 10 May 2010, https://www.sciencespo.fr/mass-violence-war-massacre-resistance/en/news/chronology-of-mass-violence-in-poland-1918-1948/. [Open access]","Petro Sodol, “Ukrainian Insurgent Army,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i> (Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies; first published in <i>Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, vol. 5, 1993), https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/display.asp?linkpath=pages%5CU%5CK%5CUkrainianInsurgentArmy.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ukrainian nationalists turn on their Polish neighbours in 1943?","a":"Because the OUN-B wanted lands it saw as indigenously Ukrainian cleared of Poles before the war ended, and the example of Soviet deportations and German genocide against the Jews suggested that a population could be removed by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Polish and Ukrainian figures differ so widely?","a":"Because each side counts from its own records and its own definitions; Polish estimates of Polish dead run to 80,000 to 100,000, Ukrainian ones to 40,000 to 60,000, and each side gives higher figures for its own losses from the other's retaliation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the massacres still divide Poland and Ukraine?","a":"Because Poland treats them as genocide and commemorates them each 11 July, while in western Ukraine the UPA commander held most responsible is honoured with monuments and street names as a fighter for independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Massacres of Poles in Volhynia and Eastern Galicia"},
    "ww2-831": {"questions":["Poles and Soviet citizens among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of wartime Germany had to wear a 'P' or 'OST' badge on their clothes and were barred from cinemas and swimming baths.","Of five million <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who had come to Germany, Fritz Sauckel admitted on 1 March 1944, not even 200,000 had come voluntarily, a remark the Nuremberg tribunal later quoted."],"sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Zwangsarbeit,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft/zwangsarbeit. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Forced Labor: An Overview,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/forced-labor-an-overview. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity: Slave Labour Policy” (Nuremberg, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Arbeitserziehungslager im Deutschen Reich,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/industrie-und-wirtschaft/arbeitserziehungslager-im-deutschen-reich. [in German] [Open access]","Friedrich Didier, <i>Europa arbeitet in Deutschland: Sauckel mobilisiert die Leistungsreserven</i> (Munich: Zentralverlag der NSDAP, 1943), trans. Randall Bytwerk as “Europe is Working for Germany,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/sauckel.htm. [Open access]","“Ein Briefwechsel, der zu denken gibt,” <i>Der Hoheitsträger</i> 6, no. 3 (1943): 23–24, trans. Randall Bytwerk as “A Thought-Provoking Exchange of Letters,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/russian.htm. [Open access]","Reichsfrauenführung, <i>Nachrichtendienst der Reichsfrauenführung, Sonderdienst</i> 10, no. 16 (September 1941), trans. Randall Bytwerk as “Advice to Women's Leaders,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/rff-sonderdienst.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany come to depend on foreign workers?","a":"Because after the failure before Moscow in late 1941 the war economy could not continue without them, and without forced labourers, prisoners of war and camp inmates Germany could not have gone on fighting from 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their work also spared German women from mass mobilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were some foreign workers treated far worse than others?","a":"Because treatment followed Nazi racial doctrine: workers from northern and western Europe received rations close to those of Germans, while Poles and Soviet workers got less food, lower pay and harsher discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many forced labourers died of ill-treatment, disease and starvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the party try to stop Germans befriending them?","a":"Because the regime needed foreign labour but regarded most of these workers as racially inferior, so it demanded separation and constant education against contact or marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Even suspected sexual relations could bring execution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Fremdarbeiter</b>, ‘foreign workers’, was the Nazi term for the millions of civilians from occupied and allied Europe who worked in wartime Germany, most of them recruited or deported by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over a million Poles were already working in Germany by the summer of 1940, and after the failure before Moscow at the end of 1941 foreign labour became indispensable to the German war economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 21 March 1942 Fritz Sauckel was made Plenipotentiary-General for the Utilisation of Labour, and the Nuremberg tribunal found that at least 5 million people were deported to work in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sauckel himself said in 1944 that of five million workers who had arrived, not even 200,000 came voluntarily, while a mass-edition book of 1943 presented them as contented volunteers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the end of 1944 more than 7.5 million foreign workers, a third of them women, laboured in almost every branch of the economy, alongside prisoners of war and concentration camp inmates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their labour let the regime keep German living standards relatively high until the end of the war and long avoid the mass employment of German women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most lived in some 30,000 camps, and the lower a group stood in Nazi racial doctrine the worse its food, pay and treatment, so that Western Europeans ate roughly as Germans did while many Eastern workers went hungry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poles and Soviet workers had to wear a 'P' or 'OST' badge, were barred from cinemas and baths, and faced harsh punishment, even execution, for suspected sexual relations with Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Party organisations worked to keep Germans apart from them, and a party magazine of 1943 told a soldier that his family might treat their Belarusian maid decently but must not share a table with her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Workers who refused work or fled could be sent by the Gestapo to labour education camps, whose number grew from eight in 1940 to about 200, and through which at least half a million people passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","wiki":"Forced labour under German rule during World War II"},
    "ww2-832": {"questions":["Withheld from any mother who could not prove Aryan descent, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been given to three million German women by 1939 and was presented at state Mother's Day ceremonies from 1942.","While the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> praised motherhood as a woman's battlefield, an American survey found that fewer German women were drawn into war industry than in the First World War."],"sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Mutterkreuz,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/innenpolitik/mutterkreuz. [in German] [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Frauenpolitik,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/innenpolitik/frauen. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Women in the Third Reich,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/women-in-the-third-reich. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 2, https://archive.org/details/101634748.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","Joseph Goebbels, “Nun, Volk steh auf, und Sturm brich los! Rede im Berliner Sportpalast,” in <i>Der steile Aufstieg</i> (Munich: Zentralverlag der NSDAP, 1944), 167–204, trans. Randall Bytwerk as “Nation, Rise Up, and Let the Storm Break Loose,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/goeb36.htm. [Open access]","Reichsfrauenführung, <i>Nachrichtendienst der Reichsfrauenführung, Sonderdienst</i> 10, no. 16 (September 1941), trans. Randall Bytwerk as “Advice to Women's Leaders,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/rff-sonderdienst.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the cross modelled on a military order?","a":"Because the regime wanted motherhood honoured as a service to the nation equal to a soldier's, and Hitler called it a woman's battlefield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Nazi thinking a larger, racially 'purer' population would strengthen Germany's armed forces and supply settlers for conquered land in eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could a mother be refused the cross?","a":"Because it went only to Reich German mothers who could prove Aryan descent and whose children were judged hereditarily healthy, and it could be taken away again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The policy that rewarded some births tried to prevent others, through marriage bans and the forced sterilisation of hundreds of thousands of women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war strain the regime's picture of women?","a":"Because the need for labour forced the state to push women into work through the Duty Year, labour service and auxiliary posts in the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Party newsletters admitted that working women carried a double burden, and in 1943 Goebbels demanded that women without jobs join war production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Cross of Honour of the German Mother</b>, known as the Mutterkreuz, was a decoration that the Nazi Party instituted in 1938 for mothers of large families, meant to do for mothers what the Iron Cross did for soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came in three grades by the number of children, bronze for four, silver for six and gold for eight, and was worn on a blue and white ribbon at the neck on formal occasions only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler, in whose name it was awarded, called motherhood a woman's battlefield, and three million women had already received the cross in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of several incentives, with marriage loans, child allowances, tax relief and harsher punishment of abortion, by which the regime pressed 'racially pure' women to bear as many children as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only Reich German mothers who could prove Aryan descent and whose children counted as hereditarily healthy were eligible, and a cross could be withdrawn, so the honour also marked out the families the regime persecuted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From 1942 the cross was presented at state Mother's Day ceremonies, part of a cult of the mother that the war did not interrupt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ideal of the mother at home sat uneasily with the war, which pushed women into work through the compulsory Duty Year and labour service and brought about 500,000 into the armed forces as auxiliaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even so, an American survey after the war found that relatively few German women, fewer than in the First World War, had been drawn into industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Stalingrad Goebbels publicly demanded that women without jobs, those from the better circles included, take up war work, and warned that a doctor's note would not excuse them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A newsletter for the party's women's leaders admitted in 1941 that many women now carried a double burden, a new job on top of their household and their children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","wiki":"Cross of Honour of the German Mother"},
    "ww2-833": {"questions":["Jews in wartime Germany received reduced allowances on their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and could buy food only at set hours and in certain shops, leaving many households short of the barest essentials.","German officials threatened to withhold a child's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> if parents kept the child in a bombed city, a threat carried out once whole schools were evacuated from the autumn of 1943."],"sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Chronik 1939,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/jahreschronik/1939. [in German] [Open access]","Gaupropagandaamt Oberdonau, “Front der Heimat,” no. 2 (Linz, c. October 1939), trans. Randall Bytwerk as “The Home Front,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/heimat.htm. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Exponat: Lebensmittelkarte, 1939/40,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/bestand/objekt/exponat-lebensmittelkarte-193940. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, <i>Judgment</i>, “War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity” (Nuremberg, 1946), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judwarcr.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “German Jews during the Holocaust,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/german-jews-during-the-holocaust. [Open access]","Joseph Goebbels, “Nun, Volk steh auf, und Sturm brich los! Rede im Berliner Sportpalast,” in <i>Der steile Aufstieg</i> (Munich: Zentralverlag der NSDAP, 1944), 167–204, trans. Randall Bytwerk as “Nation, Rise Up, and Let the Storm Break Loose,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/goeb36.htm. [Open access]","Reichsfrauenführung, <i>Nachrichtendienst der Reichsfrauenführung, Sonderdienst</i> 10, no. 16 (September 1941), trans. Randall Bytwerk as “Advice to Women's Leaders,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/rff-sonderdienst.htm. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Civilian Defense Division, <i>Final Report</i>, 2nd ed. (Washington, DC, 1947), 178, https://archive.org/details/14021750R.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany issue ration cards from the first weeks of the war?","a":"Because food and other goods had to be shared out under wartime shortage, and regulations allocating food on ration cards came into force in September 1939, with clothing cards following in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each card covered a kind of food for a fixed period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did German rations hold up better than those of occupied countries?","a":"Because occupied Europe was plundered to feed Germany: Göring wanted any famine to fall anywhere but on Germany, and the Nuremberg tribunal found that German requisitions caused famine, inflation and black markets in the occupied countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the card a weapon against Jews in Germany?","a":"Because Jews received reduced rations once general rationing began, and further decrees limited when and where they could buy food, leaving many households short of basic essentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>ration card</b>, in German Lebensmittelkarte, was the sheet of coupons on which Germans drew their allowance of food in the Second World War, each card covering a kind of food, such as meat, for a fixed period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> New food regulations providing for the allocation of food on ration cards took effect in Germany in September 1939, weeks after the invasion of Poland, and clothing cards followed in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within weeks a party flyer for members in Linz was warning of the dangers of scavenging in the countryside for food and explaining the rationing of clothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The card was also an instrument of persecution, for Jews in Germany received reduced rations and could shop only at set hours and in certain stores, so their households often lacked the most basic essentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Officials also threatened to withhold food cards from children whose parents kept them in bombed cities, and the American bombing survey found that the threat was carried out from the autumn of 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The regime met shortage by feeding Germany at the expense of occupied Europe, and in August 1942 Göring told occupation officials that it made no difference to him if their people starved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If there was to be famine anywhere, he had said, it would in no case be in Germany, and the Nuremberg tribunal found that German requisitions caused famine, inflation and black markets across the occupied countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Very large amounts of food were shipped to Germany from the Government-General in occupied Poland, where people starved, and plans for the Soviet Union accepted that many millions might starve to feed the German armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shortage still showed at home, and in February 1943 Goebbels admitted that luxury shops had nothing to sell except to customers who paid in butter or eggs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A party newsletter of 1941 told women's leaders to explain the daily difficulties of shopping by the distances supplies travelled and the number of people to be fed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","wiki":"Rationing"},
    "ww2-834": {"questions":["Lessons were meant to go on at the gun sites, with teachers sent along, but a former <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remembered them as a farce, broken off whenever a siren sounded however far away.","In January 1944 a Saxon schoolboy began service as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Humboldthain flak tower in Berlin, where pupils born in 1927 had already served since September 1943."],"sources":["Wolfgang Herchner, “Luftwaffenhelfer,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, July 2002, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/wolfgang-herchner-luftwaffenhelfer. [in German] [Open access]","Helmut Becker-Floris, “Mein Leben als Luftwaffenhelfer 1944–1945,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, November 2005, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/helmut-becker-floris-mein-leben-als-luftwaffenhelfer-1944-1945. [in German] [Open access]","Hans Mendgen, “Als Luftwaffenhelfer in Salzgitter 1943/44,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/hans-mendgen-als-luftwaffenhelfer-in-salzgitter-194344. [in German] [Open access]","Wolfgang Waldhauer, “Als Luftwaffenhelfer 1944 in Berlin und bei Leuna,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Zeitzeugen, Deutsches Historisches Museum, April 2001, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/zeitzeugen/dr-wolfgang-waldhauer-als-luftwaffenhelfer-1944-in-berlin-und-bei-leuna. [in German] [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Der ‘Totale Krieg’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/totaler-krieg.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (European War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 15, https://archive.org/details/101634748.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were schoolboys put on the anti-aircraft guns?","a":"Because the regime wanted the boys to replace the flak soldiers and free them for the front, as one helper called up at 15 remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was part of a total mobilisation in which even Hitler Youth members under 18 were drafted into the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did their schooling suffer?","a":"Because teachers came to the batteries only a few days a week, lessons were held in the barracks, and every distant siren gave the boys a reason to run to the guns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another helper said lessons went on only as a formality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the helpers feel proud rather than afraid?","a":"Because they had been prepared for years by the Hitler Youth, with war auxiliary service such as clearing rubble after raids, and firing the heavy guns let them feel they were fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some looked down on the classmates who had been left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"A <b>Luftwaffenhelfer</b>, ‘air force helper’, was one of the German schoolboys called up in the later years of the war to serve the anti-aircraft guns of the home flak, in place of soldiers needed at the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole school classes went together, aged about 15 to 17, and one Hamburg pupil remembered his headmaster sending off the eleventh classes on 4 January 1944 with appeals to unconditional obedience to the Führer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their schooling was supposed to continue, with teachers sent to the batteries, but former helpers recalled the lessons as a mere formality or a farce, broken off whenever a siren sounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lived in wooden barracks with three-tier bunks, trained on the 8.8 cm gun, and served beside older flak men and Soviet prisoners who carried the ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scheme belonged to a wider combing of German society for manpower, in which Hitler Youth members under 18 were drafted into the Wehrmacht straight from training camps from August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The boys defended industry and cities under attack, and one battery guarding the Hermann Göring works at Salzgitter saw a decoy site of flares and automatic guns draw the bombers onto empty ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Saxon pupil entered the Humboldthain flak tower in Berlin on 14 January 1944, where helpers born in 1927 had served since September and had lived through the heavy night raids of November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One former helper felt safer at his gun during raids than in a shelter at home on leave, and recalled the pride of being able to take an active part in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By late 1944 a teenage helper could command a gun crew of helpers, Volkssturm men and foreign prisoners, and one battery was moved to the Eastern Front in 1945 to fire on tanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The raids they fought against killed at least 305,000 people in Germany and left some 7.5 million homeless, by the estimate of the American bombing survey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","wiki":"Luftwaffenhelfer"},
    "ww2-835": {"questions":["The 1944 Soviet edict that created the title of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also recognised only registered marriages and turned divorce into a public court case costing hundreds or even thousands of roubles.","Created after the Russian republic's birth rate had fallen three- to fourfold and nearly a third of babies in its rear regions died in 1942, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crowned a wartime drive for births."],"sources":["Presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the USSR, “Increased Aid for Mothers and Children and Changes in the Divorce Laws,” edict of 8 July 1944, <i>Vedomosti</i>, no. 37 (1944), trans. in <i>The American Review on the Soviet Union</i> 6, no. 1 (November 1944): 69–76, in <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/love-and-romance-in-war/love-and-romance-in-war-texts/aid-for-mothers-and-children/. [Open access]","Supreme Soviet of the USSR, “Procedure for Application of the Decree of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the USSR of July 8, 1944, to Children whose Parents did not Enter into Registered Marriage,” 14 March 1945, <i>Vedomosti</i>, no. 15 (1945), trans. in <i>The American Review on the Soviet Union</i> 7, no. 2 (February 1946): 66, in <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/love-and-romance-in-war/love-and-romance-in-war-texts/benefits-for-illegitimate-children/. [Open access]","James von Geldern, “Love and Romance in War,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/love-and-romance-in-war/. [Open access]","Mie Nakachi, “Analiz pronatalistskoi semeinoi politiki v SSSR v 1940-kh–1960-kh godakh,” <i>Demograficheskoe obozrenie</i> 9, no. 1 (2022): 34–55, CyberLeninka, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/analiz-pronatalistskoy-semeynoy-politiki-v-sssr-v-1940-h-1960-h-godah. [in Russian] [Open access]","N. V. Chernysheva, “Mery demograficheskoi politiki SSSR v gody Velikoi Otechestvennoi voiny,” <i>Narodonaselenie</i> 28, no. 3 (2025): 86–99, CyberLeninka, https://cyberleninka.ru/article/n/mery-demograficheskoy-politiki-sssr-v-gody-velikoy-otechestvennoy-voyny. [in Russian] [Open access]","Kristen Edwards, “Wartime Evacuation of Soviet Civilians, 1941–1943,” <i>Seventeen Moments in Soviet History</i>, Michigan State University, https://soviethistory.msu.edu/1943/wartime-evacuation/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet state reward large families in the middle of the war?","a":"Because the war was destroying the population: the Soviet Union lost 27 million people or more by some estimates, and the edict was meant to replace the dead and speed up population growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Births had collapsed and infant deaths soared in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Evacuees in the rear often fell ill and died of malnutrition and exposure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the edict make divorce harder?","a":"Because it was meant to consolidate the family as well as raise births, so only registered marriages carried rights, and divorce required a court hearing, a notice in the local newspaper and fees that could reach 2,000 roubles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Divorce rates then stayed low until the procedure was eased in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were unmarried mothers given state money instead of a claim on the father?","a":"Because Khrushchev argued that paternity suits and maintenance payments discouraged men from having children, and proposed that the state pay single mothers instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The edict abolished the mother's right to establish fatherhood in court and gave her a monthly allowance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Mother Heroine</b> was the highest Soviet honour for motherhood, a title created by an edict of the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet on 8 July 1944 for women who had borne and reared ten children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same edict created the Order of Glory of Motherhood for seven to nine children and the Motherhood Medal for five or six, grading motherhood as military valour was graded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An award took effect only when the youngest child reached the age of one and the mother's other children were living, though children killed or missing at the front still counted towards family allowances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The edict paid allowances from the third child instead of the seventh, gave unmarried mothers a monthly sum and lengthened maternity leave for women workers from 63 to 77 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also doubled extra food rations for pregnant and nursing women and ordered nurseries wherever many women were employed, for the mobilisation of men had put women at the head of farms and factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Behind it lay a demographic catastrophe, for the birth rate of the Russian republic fell three- to fourfold to a low in 1943, and in 1942 almost one baby in three in its rear regions died before its first birthday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The war also loosened family life, as couples met knowing they might never see each other again and illegitimate births rose so steeply that the state had to support unmarried mothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The edict, first proposed by Nikita Khrushchev, recognised only registered marriages, ended an unmarried mother's right to sue the father for support, and made divorce a public court case with heavy fees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It widened a 1941 tax on single and childless citizens to those with one or two children, so that small families helped to pay for large ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In March 1945 children of unregistered couples born before the edict were granted inheritance and servicemen's family pensions if their father had died, a concession to the wartime unions that the law no longer recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","wiki":"Mother Heroine"},
    "ww2-836": {"questions":["One Japanese informant told American surveyors that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was 'a dragon's head with a snake's body', a movement that never won real popular support and faded away in 1945.","In May 1942 the newly merged Great Japan Women's Association, which soon enrolled 19 million women, joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alongside the neighbourhood groups that handled wartime rationing."],"sources":["National Diet Library, “Taisei Yokusankai (Imperial Rule Assistance Association),” <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, sec. 4-12, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description12.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “The Yokusan (Imperial Rule Assistance) System,” <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, sec. 4-15, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description15.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, chap. VII, “The Pacific War” (Tokyo, 1948), 852–53, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-7.html. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, Morale Division, <i>The Effects of Strategic Bombing on Japanese Morale</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), 108–10, https://archive.org/details/effectsofstrateg00unit. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), 20–21, https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","E. Taylor Atkins, review of <i>Gas Mask Nation: Visualizing Civil Air Defense in Wartime Japan</i>, by Gennifer Weisenfeld, H-Diplo, H-Net Reviews, October 2024, https://www.h-net.org/reviews/showrev.php?id=60762. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the association created in 1940?","a":"Because the army had forced the abolition of the political parties and some new organisation had to fill the gap, rallying the nation for an all-out effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Privy councillors also worried how the people would bear the hardships that the Tripartite Pact and American sanctions would bring, and Konoe's answer was the association.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it never become a party like the Nazis?","a":"Because it depended on government money, had no large membership or single irreplaceable leader, and its directors changed with each cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its political activities were banned in February 1941, leaving it an administrative body under the Home Ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did its propaganda reach people only weakly?","a":"Because the neighbourhood leaders who were meant to pass it on were already overwhelmed by rationing and other practical duties, and hardly listened to the propaganda themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hunger, exhausting work and rising black market prices weighed more heavily on daily life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Rule Assistance Association</b>, or Taisei Yokusankai, was the national organisation founded in October 1940 under Prime Minister Konoe Fumimaro, after the political parties were abolished, to rally the Japanese people behind the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The prime minister led it by virtue of his office, with branches in every prefecture and locality and cooperation councils meant to enlist the entire population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its articles were drawn so that it would spread into every district, city and even every home, and the Tokyo war crimes tribunal found it designed to turn Japan into a one-party state on European totalitarian lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet it never became a mass party like the Nazi or Fascist ones, for it lived on government funds, its leaders kept changing, and in February 1941 its political activities were banned and it became an administrative arm of the Home Ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One informant called it 'a dragon's head with a snake's body', and after Tojo Hideki's fall in July 1944 it decayed until it died away in the spring of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It reached daily life through the neighbourhood associations of eight to ten families, revived in 1938 or 1939 for rationing and used from 1943 as the last link in its propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Women's groups were merged into the Great Japan Women's Association, which joined it in May 1942 and enrolled 19 million members within a year, every adult woman except the young and unmarried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> These bodies carried the weight of a home front where food was rationed from April 1941 and the average diet fell from about 2,000 calories a day before Pearl Harbor to about 1,680 by the summer of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Civil air defence had been drilled since a first exercise in Osaka in 1928 that mobilised nearly two million people, yet the incendiary raids of 1945 simply overwhelmed Japan's civilian defence organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> American surveyors found that neighbourhood leaders, swamped with directives on food and supplies, passed on little propaganda and almost no candid opinion back up the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","wiki":"Imperial Rule Assistance Association"},
    "ww2-837": {"questions":["Every week until 1942 a 5-minute film made by Britain's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given free showing time in the country's cinemas, after which a monthly 15-minute film took its place.","Home Intelligence reports on public morale, daily at first and weekly from 1941, told officials of Britain's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> how their posters, films and campaigns were being received."],"sources":["The National Archives, “Second World War Propaganda Posters,” https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/explore-by-time-period/second-world-war/second-world-war-propaganda-posters/. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Working with Second World War Posters as Sources,” teaching resource, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/second-world-war-posters/. [Open access]","Cyril Radcliffe, “Information and Propaganda,” unpublished account of the Ministry of Information, The National Archives, CAB 102/373, chap. “A Ministry of Information,” 93–96, MOI Digital, https://moidigital.ac.uk/papers/unpublished-thesis-by-cyril-radcliffe-information-and-propaganda-cab-102-373/idm140328484617472/. [Open access]","Cyril Radcliffe, “Information and Propaganda,” unpublished account of the Ministry of Information, The National Archives, CAB 102/373, chap. “Films,” 63–67, MOI Digital, https://moidigital.ac.uk/papers/unpublished-thesis-by-cyril-radcliffe-information-and-propaganda-cab-102-373/idm140328474778096/. [Open access]","MOI Digital, “Home Intelligence Reports,” <i>A History of the Ministry of Information, 1939–46</i>, Institute of English Studies, University of London, https://moidigital.ac.uk/reports/home-intelligence-reports/. [Open access]","MOI Digital, “Wartime Social Survey,” <i>A History of the Ministry of Information, 1939–46</i>, Institute of English Studies, University of London, https://moidigital.ac.uk/reports/wartime-social-survey/. [Open access]","“Allied Communication to the Public during the Second World War,” MOI Digital blog, posted by Hirving, 26 November 2019, https://moidigital.ac.uk/blog/allied-communication/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the ministry make documentaries rather than feature films?","a":"Because after an early venture into financing a commercial feature film drew criticism from a parliamentary committee, the minister accepted that public money should not normally fund such films.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Documentary, a fairly new technique, also suited the British idea of fitting propaganda, and the ministry's output grew every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the ministry watch public morale so closely?","a":"Because officials needed to know how their messages were received and to change course if the response was poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Home Intelligence produced daily summaries of morale early in the war and weekly reports from 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Radcliffe warn against a powerful Ministry of Information?","a":"Because he thought a government in wartime too readily confuses its convenience with the public interest, and a ministry able to impose official views on the free press would undermine the free opinion the country claimed to defend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also saw film and posters as useful for practical campaigns such as salvage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Ministry of Information</b> was the British government department responsible for official publicity and propaganda at home and abroad during the Second World War, set up in 1939 and wound up in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It had to be built in 1939 without any existing structure, by planners who expected heavy air attack to wreck the ordinary press and were unsure whether they were replacing the free publicity system or adding to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its posters urged the British public to grow food, save salvage and join the forces, and a poster of 1941 called on the women of Britain to come into the factories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It warned against gossip in the 'Careless talk costs lives' campaign, with slogans such as 'Keep mum' and 'There's often a listener – silence is safety'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Films Division made documentaries, with a 5-minute film given free showing time in the country's cinemas every week until 1942, then a monthly 15-minute film, while mobile projectors toured the regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its Home Intelligence section wrote daily summaries of public morale early in the war and weekly ones from 1941, for propaganda that was to be trusted had largely to rest on truth and on knowing how messages were received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From 1941 its Wartime Social Survey questioned the public for other departments on matters from food and salvage to the call-up of women, and on the ministry's own films.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Alliances needed careful handling, and its 1942 exhibition of Soviet posters, Comrades in Arms, promoted the Soviet war effort while avoiding praise of Stalin's leadership or of communist doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cyril Radcliffe, who worked at the ministry from 1939 to 1945, argued that in a democracy it should seek efficiency but not power, and must never turn free opinion into government opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Campaigns to make people eat potatoes or burn less coal were harmless, he wrote, but a ministry strong enough to impose the government's views on a free press would have been disastrous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","wiki":"Ministry of Information (United Kingdom)"},
    "ww2-838": {"questions":["Asking American editors to hold back troop movements and weather forecasts, the voluntary code issued by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also kept the atomic bomb out of the press until Hiroshima.","Byron Price, the director, held that no one who did not dislike censorship should exercise it, and on 15 August 1945 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stopped censoring at once and gave its staff 30 days' notice."],"sources":["Byron Price, <i>A Report on the Office of Censorship</i>, Historical Reports on War Administration, Office of Censorship, Series 1 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1945), 1–5, 15, 33–35, https://archive.org/details/Reportontheofficeofcensorship1945. [Open access]","Patrick S. Washburn, “The Office of Censorship's Attempt to Control Press Coverage of the Atomic Bomb during World War II” (paper presented at the annual meeting of the Association for Education in Journalism and Mass Communication, Portland, OR, July 1988), ERIC ED295201, https://archive.org/details/ERIC_ED295201. [Open access]","United States Office of Censorship, <i>Code of Wartime Practices for American Broadcasters</i>, edition of 15 June 1942 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1942), https://archive.org/details/OWICode1942-06-15. [Open access]","United States Office of Censorship, <i>U.S. Postal Censorship Regulations</i>, edition of 13 April 1942 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1942), https://archive.org/details/USPostalCensorshipReg. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, statement on censorship, 16 December 1941, and Executive Order 8985, 19 December 1941, in Robert E. Summers, comp., <i>Wartime Censorship of Press and Radio</i>, The Reference Shelf 15, no. 8 (New York: H. W. Wilson, 1942), 95–96, https://archive.org/details/wartimecensorshi010852mbp. [Open access]","Cyril Radcliffe, “Information and Propaganda,” unpublished account of the Ministry of Information, The National Archives, CAB 102/373, chap. “Censorship,” 31–34, MOI Digital, https://moidigital.ac.uk/papers/unpublished-thesis-by-cyril-radcliffe-information-and-propaganda-cab-102-373/idm140328476943344/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was censorship of the American press left voluntary?","a":"Because Price believed that in a free country press censorship ought to be voluntary in principle, and that in practice a voluntary system could work as well as a compulsory one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The press largely kept to the code, mainly because it enthusiastically supported the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did radio stations stop playing record requests?","a":"Because an enemy agent could ask a station to dedicate a song to someone at a set time and so pass a signal abroad, and street interviews could let an agent seize the microphone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The code also asked broadcasters to keep back weather data that could help the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the office close so quickly after the war?","a":"Because Price held that censorship should exist solely as an instrument of war, and on 27 June 1945 he submitted the recommendation on which its ending was based.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 15 August 1945 the President ordered censoring to stop at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Office of Censorship</b> was the United States agency set up by Franklin D. Roosevelt's executive order of 19 December 1941 to censor communications crossing the country's borders and to supervise the voluntary self-censorship of press and radio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roosevelt chose Byron Price, executive news editor of the Associated Press, as Director of Censorship, answerable directly to the President.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its staff grew from a single room and borrowed clerks to a peak of 14,462 in February 1943, most of them in postal censorship, which handled well over a million letters a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Postal regulations of April 1942 forbade letters abroad to reveal sailing times more precisely than within a week, weather beyond what the local press printed, or anything about military communications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Price wrote that everything the censor does is contrary to the fundamentals of liberty, and that no one who did not dislike censorship should ever be allowed to exercise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For press and radio the office issued a Code of Wartime Practices, asking editors and broadcasters to keep back weather reports and troop movements unless an appropriate official released them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Broadcasters also dropped record requests and street interviews, lest an enemy agent use a dedication or an open microphone to send a hidden message.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The voluntary system kept the atomic bomb project out of the news for 3 years, the office sending confidential notes to editors whenever stories on heavy water or atom smashing appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Price judged voluntary censorship as effective as the compulsory systems he saw in Britain and Canada, though a senior British official recalled that home press censorship there was voluntary too, backed only by the threat of prosecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 15 August 1945 the President ended voluntary censorship and all censorship of international communications, and the office was formally abolished on 15 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","wiki":"Office of Censorship"},
    "ww2-839": {"questions":["Lacking short wave, the cheap <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at first kept many Germans out of reach of the BBC's German Service, whose broadcasts opened with the first notes of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony.","Between 1933 and 1941 the share of German households owning a wireless rose from 25 to 65 per cent, helped by the mass-produced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Goebbels launched in August 1933."],"sources":["Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Volksempfänger,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/alltagsleben/volksempfaenger. [in German] [Open access]","Joseph Goebbels, “Der Rundfunk als achte Großmacht,” speech of 18 August 1933, in <i>Signale der neuen Zeit</i> (Munich: Zentralverlag der NSDAP, 1938), 197–207, trans. Randall Bytwerk as “The Radio as the Eighth Great Power,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/goeb56.htm. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Rundfunkpropaganda für das Ausland im Zweiten Weltkrieg,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/zweiter-weltkrieg/kriegsverlauf/rundfunkpropaganda-fuer-das-ausland-im-zweiten-weltkrieg. [in German] [Open access]","Gaupropagandaamt Oberdonau, “Front der Heimat,” no. 2 (Linz, c. October 1939), trans. Randall Bytwerk as “The Home Front,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/heimat.htm. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Joseph Goebbels,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","Eugen Hadamovsky, “Die lebende Brücke: Vom Wesen der Funkwartarbeit,” in <i>Dein Rundfunk</i> (Munich: Zentralverlag der NSDAP, 1934), 22–26, English translation “The Living Bridge: On the Nature of Radio Warden Activity,” German Propaganda Archive, Calvin University, https://research.calvin.edu/german-propaganda-archive/hada3.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime want a cheap radio in every home?","a":"Because Goebbels regarded radio as the most effective means of influencing the masses, and a standard, mass-produced set at a low price would let every household hear the Führer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called radio the eighth great power, the medium that would shape the 20th century as the press had shaped the 19th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many Germans still hear enemy broadcasts?","a":"Because although the cheapest sets lacked short wave, the BBC's German Service won listeners through its credibility, admitting British setbacks, and Germans listened often to fill the gaps in their own news.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regime's harsh penalties show how seriously it took the danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were even party members forbidden to listen to foreign stations?","a":"Because a party flyer argued that no one was immune to repeated enemy lies and that anyone who listened would want to talk about it, spreading the effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 1939 decree threatened listeners with prison and, at worst, death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"The <b>Volksempfänger</b>, or ‘people's receiver’, was the cheap standard wireless set that Nazi Germany introduced at the Berlin radio exhibition in August 1933, its model name VE 301 recalling 30 January, the day of the Nazi takeover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Developed at the instigation of the propaganda ministry, it was built to one design by every radio maker in the Reich and sold for 76 Reichsmarks, against 200 to 400 for a better set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goebbels, who pioneered radio as a tool of propaganda, opened the exhibition by calling radio the 'eighth great power' and launching a campaign to double German listenership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Listeners rose from 4.3 million to 5.5 million in the first 15 months of Nazi rule, by the party radio chief's count, and the share of households with a set rose from 25 to 65 per cent between 1933 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its slogan was 'All Germany hears the Führer with the people's receiver', and request concerts, entertainment and, in wartime, army reports kept listeners from tiring of propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The sets were cheap partly because they could not receive short wave, which at first kept many Germans from hearing foreign propaganda broadcasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even so, the German Service of the BBC, opening with a drumbeat of the first notes of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony, became the most heard enemy station in Germany, largely because it did not hide British setbacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree published on 7 September 1939 made deliberately listening to foreign stations, or spreading what they said, punishable at worst by death, and in 1943 courts sentenced eleven people to death for such radio crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A party flyer of autumn 1939 told members that the law made no exception for them, since no one was unaffected by what he heard and anyone who listened wanted to tell someone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many offenders had been denounced by relatives or neighbours, and a campaign of 1941–42 to hang warning labels on the dials of some 16 million sets was dropped after it provoked resentment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","wiki":"Volksempfänger"},
    "ww2-840": {"questions":["Army researchers found in 1943 that films of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> series taught soldiers the facts of the war well but had no measurable effect on their motivation to serve.","Prelude to War, the first film of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> series, won an Academy Award as the best documentary of 1942, though its release to cinemas was held up for 6 months."],"sources":["Barry E. Cardwell, “Film and Motivation: The ‘Why We Fight’ Series,” individual study project, 7 January 1991, 58–66, DTIC ADA237991, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA237991. [Open access]","Carl I. Hovland, Arthur A. Lumsdaine and Fred D. Sheffield, <i>Experiments on Mass Communication</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II 3 (Princeton, NJ: Princeton University Press, 1949), 21–22, 64–65, https://archive.org/details/world-war-ii-3-experiments. [Open access]","Cyril Radcliffe, “Information and Propaganda,” unpublished account of the Ministry of Information, The National Archives, CAB 102/373, chap. “Films,” 63–67, MOI Digital, https://moidigital.ac.uk/papers/unpublished-thesis-by-cyril-radcliffe-information-and-propaganda-cab-102-373/idm140328474778096/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Joseph Goebbels,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Film ‘Kolberg’,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg/kunst-und-kultur/film-kolberg. [in German] [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, “Filmpolitik,” <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/kunst-und-kultur/filmpolitik. [in German] [Open access]","“Allied Communication to the Public during the Second World War,” MOI Digital blog, posted by Hirving, 26 November 2019, https://moidigital.ac.uk/blog/allied-communication/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army turn to a Hollywood director?","a":"Because General Marshall found officers' lectures on the war so mediocre that he ordered a series of films instead, and Capra, a highly successful director, was given the mission of telling soldiers why they fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The films aimed to change attitudes rather than simply convey facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Capra use the enemy's own films?","a":"Because he believed that letting soldiers hear Axis leaders proclaim their master-race claims would show them why they were in uniform, so the films were built from enemy footage set against a free world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the films change motivation so little?","a":"Because, the researchers suggested, the men's earlier indoctrination as civilians, conflicting motives, the limits of a single 50-minute film and the need for time to sink in all weakened the effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Factual learning, by contrast, was large and lasting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"abstract":"<b>Why We Fight</b> was a series of seven films made for the United States Army under the Hollywood director Frank Capra to explain to soldiers the causes of the war and the principles for which they were fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In February 1942 the Army's Chief of Staff, General George C. Marshall, gave Capra the task, having found officers' lectures to the men on what they were fighting for unsatisfactory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The films of about 50 minutes each, from Prelude to War in 1942 to War Comes to America in 1944, traced the war from the rise of fascism and Japan's seizure of Manchuria in 1931 to American mobilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Capra decided to let the enemy prove his own case, cutting Axis footage into the films so that soldiers heard their enemies proclaim their aims in their own words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every soldier had to see the series before going overseas, with the viewing marked on his record, and Prelude to War won an Academy Award as the best documentary of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Army researchers who tested the films on soldiers in 1943 found that they taught facts well but changed broader opinions little and had no effect on the men's motivation to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prelude to War was finished in October 1942, but its release to cinemas was delayed 6 months because the Office of War Information, which also sponsored films promoting racial equality, found it biased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In Britain, where Churchill had the series shown to the public, the Ministry of Information made its own documentaries and counted Target for Tonight and Desert Victory as its outstanding successes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Germany Goebbels controlled a film industry wholly state-owned by 1941, which made more than 1,200 feature films under the Nazis, many of them escapist entertainment meant to relieve wartime strain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His costliest film, Kolberg, which cost 8.5 million Reichsmarks, had its premiere on 30 January 1945 as a call to hold out, too late to have its intended effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","wiki":"Why We Fight"},
    "ww2-961": {"questions":["Closed to historians for decades and opened for research only in 2007, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold more than 30 million documents on the fates of people persecuted by the Nazis.","A Holocaust survivor called the collection of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a paper monument, since its records hold information on about 17.5 million victims and survivors of National Socialism."],"abstract":"The <b>Arolsen Archives</b>, in the German town of Bad Arolsen, hold the world's most comprehensive archive on the victims and survivors of National Socialism, with information on about 17.5 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 30 million documents record incarceration, forced labour and the Allied care of displaced persons after the war, and the original collections have belonged to UNESCO's Memory of the World since 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The institution grew out of wartime efforts to trace the missing: a Tracing Bureau was formed at the British Red Cross in 1943, and a Central Tracing Bureau under Allied supreme headquarters followed on 15 February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the war the relief agency UNRRA ran a Central Tracing Bureau to help camp survivors find living relatives, while radio broadcasts and newspapers carried lists of survivors and their whereabouts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bureau moved to Bad Arolsen, a central point among the four occupation zones, and in 1948 it was renamed the International Tracing Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For many decades its main work was clarifying the fates of individuals and searching for the missing, and it still answers inquiries about some 20,000 victims of Nazi persecution every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the 1950s it was run under an international commission by the International Committee of the Red Cross, which gave up its management in 2012, when the German Federal Archives became its partner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The archive was opened for research only in 2007, and an 11-nation commission, from Belgium and Israel to Poland and the United States, still oversees it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its digital copy, held in America by the Holocaust Memorial Museum, runs to more than 200 million images, yet it lacks the names of many people murdered on arrival at the camps or killed on death marches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The survivor Thomas Buergenthal called the whole collection a paper monument, and besides persecution it documents forced labour, displaced persons and migration after 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Arolsen Archives, “Profile,” https://arolsen-archives.org/en/about-us/profile/. [Open access]","Arolsen Archives, “Archive,” https://arolsen-archives.org/en/archive/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “International Tracing Service Digital Archive,” https://www.ushmm.org/remember/resources-holocaust-survivors-victims/international-tracing-service. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Displaced Persons,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/displaced-persons. [Open access]","European Holocaust Research Infrastructure, “Arolsen Archives: International Center on Nazi Persecution,” EHRI Portal, https://portal.ehri-project.eu/institutions/de-002409. [Open access]","Carolin Piorun, “Archives of the International Tracing Service,” in <i>ESE Archives Guide: A Web Archive Guide to East and Southeast European Archives</i>, Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München, 19 April 2018, https://www.ese-archives.geschichte.uni-muenchen.de/?p=636. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the tracing service placed at Bad Arolsen?","a":"Because the town lay at a central point among the four zones into which occupied Germany had been divided, and the Allied tracing bureau moved there before it was renamed the International Tracing Service in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Allies had begun the search for missing persons during the war itself, through a bureau at the British Red Cross and then one under their supreme headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are some victims missing from its records?","a":"Because people murdered on arrival at the camps and killing centres were often never registered, and those who died on death marches may appear only in earlier documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The records are also fuller for people deported from Western Europe than for those living in Eastern Europe, and they come from very few ghettos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the archive still receive inquiries so long after the war?","a":"Because clarifying fates and searching for missing persons was its core task for decades, and families still ask about some 20,000 victims every year, often after a long search.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its online archive was built so that people throughout the world can look for information about their loved ones, and it keeps growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Arolsen Archives"},
    "ww2-962": {"questions":["Stalin put Soviet losses at about seven million in 1946, but later demographic work on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised the Soviet toll to some 26 to 27 million.","Chinese deaths are among the least certain <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, put at about 20 million in one table but possibly above 50 million for civilians alone."],"abstract":"<b>World War II casualties</b> were the dead and wounded of the war of 1939 to 1945, and one widely used table gives about 15 million battle deaths, 25 million battle wounded and 45 million civilian deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same table warns that estimates vary widely, and that civilian deaths in China alone may well have been more than 50 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Civilians far outnumbered soldiers among the dead, and they included six million Jews murdered by Nazi Germany and its allies and collaborators, besides millions of Soviet prisoners of war, Poles, Roma and disabled people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The heaviest national losses fell on the Soviet Union and China, followed by Germany, Poland, Japan and Yugoslavia, while Britain and the United States each lost fewer than half a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deaths went on after the fighting, and estimates of the Germans who died in the flight and expulsions from Eastern Europe range from 400,000 to two million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Soviet losses show how hard the counting is: in March 1946 Stalin put them at about seven million, killed in the fighting, under occupation and by deportation to German servitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Demographers later rebuilt the figure from the population itself, comparing the 196.7 million people of mid-1941 with the 159.5 million still living on the same territory in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After subtracting the deaths that peacetime would have brought, they arrived at about 26.6 million excess deaths, an estimate widely accepted for a quarter of a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A newer Russian estimate of 42 million has been judged to lack substantial foundations, leaving 26 to 27 million as the best estimate of the Soviet war dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Countless families did not know who had died: in post-war Germany one person in four was searching for a relative or being sought, and the German Red Cross still holds about 1.3 million unresolved cases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["The National WWII Museum, “Research Starters: Worldwide Deaths in World War II,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/students-teachers/student-resources/research-starters/research-starters-worldwide-deaths-world-war. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Documenting Numbers of Victims of the Holocaust and Nazi Persecution,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/documenting-numbers-of-victims-of-the-holocaust-and-nazi-persecution. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Counting the Soviet Union’s War Dead: Still 26–27 Million,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 71, no. 6 (2019): 1036–47, accepted manuscript, https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/98747/. [Open access]","Mark Harrison, “Counting Soviet Deaths in the Great Patriotic War: Comment,” <i>Europe-Asia Studies</i> 55, no. 6 (2003): 939–44, postprint, https://warwick.ac.uk/markharrison/public/eas03a_postprint.pdf. [Open access]","J. V. Stalin, “Interview to ‘Pravda’ Correspondent Concerning Mr. Winston Churchill’s Speech at Fulton,” March 1946, in <i>J. V. Stalin on Post-War International Relations</i> (London: Soviet News, 1947), Marxists Internet Archive, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1946/03/x01.htm. [Open access]","Anne Baghdady, Regina Haunhorst and Markus Würz, “Flucht und Vertreibung,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/alltag/flucht-und-vertreibung.html. [in German] [Open access]","Anne Baghdady and Markus Würz, “Suchdienst,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/alltag/suchdienst.html. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do totals of the war's dead vary so widely?","a":"Because records are incomplete and estimates come from many different sources, so that the museum's own table warns that worldwide figures vary widely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even for the Holocaust there is no single document recording every death, and totals are built from Nazi records, demographic studies before and after the war, and testimony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are Soviet deaths estimated from the population rather than counted?","a":"Because no complete count of the dead exists, so demographers compare the population before and after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of 194 million people on Soviet territory in 1941 who did not emigrate, only 159.5 million were alive in 1945; removing the 11.9 million who would have died anyway leaves 22.6 million excess deaths among them, before war babies are added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were so many people still missing after the war?","a":"Because the war tore families apart: soldiers vanished, relatives were separated in flight, and children were left without parents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Church charities and the German Red Cross built a joint card index of millions of cases, and the Red Cross has since clarified the fates of nine million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"World War II casualties"},
    "ww2-963": {"questions":["Newspapers in Berlin and the Soviet zone coined the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1945 and 1946, praising volunteers when the women clearing rubble were mostly unemployed and directed by labour offices.","In the 1980s campaigners for older women's pensions declared a whole generation to be <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, crediting them with laying the ground for West Germany's economic miracle."],"abstract":"The <b>Trümmerfrauen</b>, or ‘rubble women’, were the German women who helped clear the ruins of bombed cities after the Second World War, and they became a symbol of the will to rebuild.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The task was vast: about 2.25 million homes had been destroyed, Cologne was 70 per cent ruined, and some 400 million cubic metres of rubble lay across Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Women knocked old mortar off bricks so they could be used again, earning a wage and higher food rations while many husbands and sons were dead, wounded or still prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Clearing had begun during the air war itself, using craftsmen, soldiers, civilian forced labourers, prisoners of war and concentration camp inmates, which marked it as punishment work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After 1945 the occupiers and German towns first set former Nazi party members and German prisoners of war to clear rubble, but most of it was moved by professional firms with heavy machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Historians now think women's share was clearly secondary: the American and French zones refused to use them, and the British zone employed only a very small number between 1945 and 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only in Berlin and the Soviet zone did many women, most of them unemployed and registered with labour offices that could withdraw ration cards, take part in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There, in 1945 and 1946, newspapers coined the word 'Trümmerfrau' and praised volunteers, a campaign meant to win women for work most people disliked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> East Germany made the figure a model of the new socialist woman, while West Berlin raised a memorial to its rubble women in 1955 and the wider republic took up the image only in the 1980s, in a pension debate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That revised picture stung women who had done the work, and surviving Trümmerfrauen in Berlin told reporters in 2015 that they had sorted and cleaned bricks while the men came back only slowly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Leonie Treber, “Mythos ‘Trümmerfrau’: deutsch-deutsche Erinnerungen,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i>, 70 Jahre Kriegsende, Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, 8 April 2015, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/204282/mythos-truemmerfrau-deutsch-deutsche-erinnerungen/. [in German] [Open access]","Leonie Treber, <i>Mythos Trümmerfrauen: Von der Trümmerbeseitigung in der Kriegs- und Nachkriegszeit und der Entstehung eines deutschen Erinnerungsortes</i>, Schriftenreihe 1655 (Bonn: Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, 2015), excerpt, https://www.bpb.de/system/files/dokument_pdf/Textauszug_Mythos%20Tr%C3%BCmmerfrauen.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Anne Baghdady and Markus Würz, “Leben in Trümmern,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/alltag/leben-in-truemmern.html. [in German] [Open access]","Universität Duisburg-Essen, “Mythos Trümmerfrauen,” press release, 30 October 2014, https://www.uni-due.de/de/presse/meldung.php?id=8709. [in German] [Open access]","Nicole Kramer, review of <i>Mythos Trümmerfrauen</i>, by Leonie Treber, <i>WerkstattGeschichte</i>, no. 73 (2016): 131–33, https://werkstattgeschichte.de/wp-content/uploads/2017/05/WG73_131-133_KRAMER_TRUEMMERFRAU.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Derek Scally, “Berlin’s Rubble Women Did Much of the Post-War Work,” <i>Irish Times</i>, 9 May 2015, https://www.irishtimes.com/news/world/europe/berlin-s-rubble-women-did-much-of-the-post-war-work-1.2205344. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did women clear rubble in Berlin but rarely in the western zones?","a":"Because the American and French occupiers rejected using women for the work, and the British used only a few between 1945 and 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Berlin and the Soviet zone men and women of working age had to register with labour offices, and the unemployed, mostly women there, could be sent to clear rubble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did newspapers praise the rubble women so loudly?","a":"Because city governments needed unemployed people to clear rubble, and the work was disliked: it was heavy, dirty and associated with punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Papers in Berlin and the Soviet zone therefore presented the women as willing volunteers, and their reports gave the word 'Trümmerfrau' its stereotypes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the image spread across West Germany only in the 1980s?","a":"Because campaigners for women born before 1921, who were left out of the 'baby year' pension rule of 1986, adopted the name for a whole generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Popular women's history of the same decade also presented the rubble women as heroines of reconstruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Trümmerfrau"},
    "ww2-964": {"questions":["The Soviet Union and its allies refused to join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because most of the people in its care were Eastern Europeans who declined, for political reasons, to return.","When the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed in 1952 about 100,000 refugees were still living in camps in West Germany and Austria, and the new UN High Commissioner took over their protection."],"abstract":"The <b>International Refugee Organization</b> was a temporary specialised agency of the United Nations, set up in 1946 to care for Europeans made homeless by the Second World War and to repatriate or resettle them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was formally established in December 1946 by a resolution of the UN General Assembly, had its headquarters in Geneva, and was funded by the 18 countries that ratified its constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1 July 1947 it took over the work of the relief agency UNRRA, including the camps where most displaced persons lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its services ran from care in the camps and vocational training to preparation for emigration and an extensive tracing service for lost relatives, and it also took on the legal protection of refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 1.6 million people came under its protection, among them some 200,000 Ukrainians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the war's end about 10.8 million displaced persons were in Germany alone, Soviet prisoners of war, forced labourers, refugees and freed camp inmates, and those from Western Europe went home without difficulty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many from Eastern Europe refused to go, Poles fearing their country's new rulers and Soviet forced labourers fearing punishment as collaborators, and early in 1946 the UN decided that return must be voluntary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Soviet Union and its allies therefore refused to join an agency that cared mainly for such people, and very few were repatriated after 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Resettlement became the main task, and countries such as Australia, Canada and the United States took refugees under special laws, the American Displaced Persons Act of 1948 admitting 200,000 over 2 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The organisation was dissolved on 15 February 1952 after resettling about a million people, about 100,000 refugees were still in camps in West Germany and Austria, and the UN High Commissioner for Refugees took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Treaty Collection, “Annex X – International Refugee Organization (IRO) – to the Convention on the Privileges and Immunities of the Specialized Agencies,” <i>Multilateral Treaties Deposited with the Secretary-General</i>, chap. III, no. 2.10, https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/MTDSG/Volume%20I/Chapter%20III/III-2-10.en.xml. [Open access]","“International Refugee Organization,” <i>Internet Encyclopedia of Ukraine</i>, Canadian Institute of Ukrainian Studies, https://www.encyclopediaofukraine.com/pages%5CI%5CN%5CInternationalRefugeeOrganization.htm. [Open access]","European Holocaust Research Infrastructure, “International Refugee Organization,” EHRI Portal, https://portal.ehri-project.eu/authorities/ehri_cb-530. [Open access]","Markus Würz, “Displaced Persons,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/befreiung-und-besatzung/displaced-persons.html. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Displaced Persons,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/displaced-persons. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Statement by the President upon Signing the Displaced Persons Act,” 25 June 1948, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-upon-signing-the-displaced-persons-act. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many displaced persons refuse to go home?","a":"Because many Poles feared the new rulers of their country, and Soviet and Ukrainian forced labourers were afraid of being treated as collaborators and punished at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Soviet Union insisted on return, even by force, until the United Nations decided early in 1946 that repatriation could only be voluntary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union stay out of the organisation?","a":"Because the refugees it cared for were mostly Eastern Europeans living in the British, American and French zones who refused to return to their countries for political reasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Supporting their resettlement abroad ran against the Soviet demand that its citizens be brought home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Truman call the 1948 American law discriminatory?","a":"Because it admitted only displaced persons who had reached Germany, Austria or Italy by 22 December 1945, a date that he said shut out more than 90 per cent of the Jewish displaced persons still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He signed it anyway, so that the 200,000 people it did admit would not be penalised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"International Refugee Organization"},
    "ww2-965": {"questions":["Some 28,000 German residents of Brno were marched to the Austrian border in 1945 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose death toll is put at between 400,000 and two million.","West Germany's equalisation of burdens law of 1952 compensated victims of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some eight million of whom were living in the Federal Republic by 1950."],"abstract":"The <b>flight and expulsion of Germans</b> at the end of the Second World War forced between twelve and fourteen million ethnic Germans out of Eastern Europe, or kept those who had already fled from going home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hundreds of thousands fled the eastern German territories in the winter of 1944–45 ahead of the advancing Red Army, and violent expulsions of German minorities began while the war was still being fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The removals had been planned long before: by mid-1942 the British government accepted the principle of transferring German minorities, and at Yalta the Big Three agreed that Poland must gain territory in the north and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Potsdam the three powers recognised that Germans remaining in Poland, Czechoslovakia and Hungary would have to be transferred to Germany, and agreed that this should be done in an orderly and humane manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> How many people died is still unclear, and estimates range from 400,000 to as many as two million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the 'wild expulsions' of 1945 troops, police and militia drove Germans out, and at Brno some 28,000 people were rounded up and marched to the Austrian border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About a quarter of a million Germans were held in camps in Czechoslovakia alone, often in atrocious conditions, before being pushed west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Potsdam asked the expelling governments to pause until the Control Council had worked out how to share the arrivals among the zones, yet the 'organised expulsions' of 1946 and 1947 were still chaotic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The census of October 1946 counted 9.6 million refugees, and in 1950 about eight million lived in West Germany and four million in East Germany, which officially called them 'resettlers'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> West Germany's equalisation of burdens law, in force from September 1952, compensated losses from war and expulsion, and by the end of 1993 it had paid out 123 billion Deutschmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“Protocol of the Proceedings,” Potsdam Conference, 1 August 1945, sec. XII, “Orderly Transfer of German Populations,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","Anne Baghdady, Regina Haunhorst and Markus Würz, “Flucht und Vertreibung,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/alltag/flucht-und-vertreibung.html. [in German] [Open access]","Richard J. Evans, “The Other Horror,” review of <i>Orderly and Humane: The Expulsion of the Germans after the Second World War</i>, by R. M. Douglas, <i>New Republic</i>, 25 June 2012, https://newrepublic.com/article/102925/orderly-humane-expulsion-germans-richard-evans. [Open access]","Marion Frantzioch-Immenkeppel, “Die Vertriebenen in der Bundesrepublik Deutschland: Flucht, Vertreibung, Aufnahme und Integration,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i>, no. 28 (1996), Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/archiv/537753/die-vertriebenen-in-der-bundesrepublik-deutschland-flucht-vertreibung-aufnahme-und-integration/. [in German] [Open access]","Alexander Weidle, “Der Umgang mit ‘Flucht und Vertreibung’ in der ehemaligen DDR,” <i>Einsichten und Perspektiven</i>, Themenheft 1/21, <i>Flucht und Vertreibung</i> (Munich: Bayerische Landeszentrale für politische Bildungsarbeit, 2021), 32, https://www.blz.bayern.de/data/pdf/themenheft-flucht-und-vertreibung-1021-1605-44.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","“Protocol of Proceedings of Crimea Conference,” 11 February 1945, sec. VII, “Poland,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","Anne Baghdady and Markus Würz, “Potsdamer Konferenz,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/befreiung-und-besatzung/potsdamer-konferenz.html. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Potsdam insist that the transfers be orderly and humane?","a":"Because the expulsions already under way were brutal: by midsummer 1945 more than 5,000 Germans a day reached Berlin from Czechoslovakia, many dead or dying, and Western observers were shocked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The formula sanctioned further transfers on that condition, and in doing so it also served to justify the partly violent expulsions that had already begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Poland's frontier move so far to the west?","a":"Because Stalin kept the eastern Polish lands he had annexed in 1939, so Poland was compensated with German territory up to the Oder and Neisse rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Potsdam the Western powers accepted the Oder–Neisse line provisionally, leaving the final frontier to a peace treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the expellees not destabilise West Germany?","a":"Because they were absorbed with state help, from the equalisation of burdens law to housing and social insurance, while the economic miracle raised their living standards within about a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a charter proclaimed at Stuttgart in August 1950 their organisations renounced revenge and retaliation and declared for a united Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Flight and expulsion of Germans (1944–1950)"},
    "ww2-966": {"questions":["After reading the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Truman ordered Eisenhower to move Jewish survivors out of guarded camps, and separate Jewish centres with larger rations followed within weeks.","A call to admit 100,000 Jewish survivors to Palestine made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a source of open friction between Washington and Attlee's government in late 1945."],"abstract":"The <b>Harrison Report</b> was an account of the conditions facing Jewish and other displaced persons in Germany and Austria, written by the American lawyer Earl G. Harrison in August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allied armies had sent more than 7 million displaced persons home by the end of July 1945, but many Jewish survivors of the Holocaust could not or would not return to their former homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harrison, a former commissioner of immigration and the American representative on the Intergovernmental Committee on Refugees, was asked by the State Department to study their needs, and Truman approved the mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With Joseph Schwartz of the American Jewish Joint Distribution Committee he visited 30 camps and found many survivors behind barbed wire under armed guard, some in the very camps where they had suffered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote that the Allies appeared to be treating the Jews as the Nazis had treated them, except that they did not exterminate them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Harrison urged that Jewish survivors be treated as a distinct group with greater needs, housed in unguarded centres under civilian control, and resettled quickly, most of them in Palestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Truman sent the report to Eisenhower, telling him to requisition houses from the German population and get survivors out of the camps, and adding that he was asking Britain to open Palestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under Eisenhower, Jewish-only centres were created, rations rose from 2,200 to 2,500 calories a day, and an adviser on Jewish affairs was appointed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attlee's government rejected the call to admit 100,000 Jews to Palestine, yet the Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry it set up recommended exactly that in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The report also moved Truman to give displaced persons preference within existing quotas, and Harrison's campaigning helped win the special law of 1948 that opened the United States to more of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “The Harrison Report,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-harrison-report. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Earl G. Harrison: Biography,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/earl-g-harrison-biography. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Letter to General Eisenhower Concerning Conditions Facing Displaced Persons in Germany,” 31 August 1945, released 29 September 1945, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/letter-general-eisenhower-concerning-conditions-facing-displaced-persons-germany. [Open access]","Anglo-American Committee of Inquiry, <i>Report of the Anglo-American Committee of Enquiry Regarding the Problems of European Jewry and Palestine</i> (Lausanne, 1946), chap. 1, “Recommendations and Comments,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/angch01.asp. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Statement by the President upon Signing the Displaced Persons Act,” 25 June 1948, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-the-president-upon-signing-the-displaced-persons-act. [Open access]","Markus Würz, “Displaced Persons,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/befreiung-und-besatzung/displaced-persons.html. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were survivors still in camps months after liberation?","a":"Because the military authorities' first priority was sending displaced persons home quickly, and they housed them in assembly centres separated by nationality, treated as temporary until repatriation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many from Eastern Europe would not go back, and many Jewish survivors had no ability or desire to return to their prewar homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Harrison want Jews treated as a separate group?","a":"Because Jewish displaced persons had suffered far more and for far longer than others, so their physical and mental condition was worse and their needs greater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He therefore asked that they be moved into civilian housing or unguarded centres under civilian control, with regular inspections to make sure their needs were met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the report strain relations with Britain?","a":"Because Truman forwarded it to Attlee and asked that 100,000 Jewish survivors be allowed into Palestine, which Britain governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attlee warned of grievous harm to relations if Washington pressed the case publicly, and the dispute became open when the report and Truman's message were released in late 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Harrison Report"},
    "ww2-967": {"questions":["Some 850 delegates from fifty nations, with their staff and more than 2,500 journalists, made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> perhaps the largest international gathering held until then.","Summoned by the Big Three at Yalta, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> adopted the Charter unanimously at the San Francisco Opera House on 25 June 1945, and delegates signed it the next day."],"abstract":"The <b>United Nations Conference on International Organization</b>, also called the San Francisco Conference, met from 25 April to 26 June 1945 and agreed the Charter of the United Nations and the Statute of the International Court of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea went back to the Moscow Conference of October 1943, where four powers declared the need for a general international organisation based on the sovereign equality of all peace-loving states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Dumbarton Oaks in Washington in 1944, American, British, Soviet and Chinese representatives drafted proposals for a General Assembly and a Security Council, which became the conference's working document.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Yalta in February 1945 Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin agreed to summon a United Nations conference in the United States for 25 April, and settled the voting formula for the Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only states that had declared war on Germany and Japan and subscribed to the Declaration by United Nations were invited, and the conference itself added four more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Delegates of fifty nations attended, about 850 of them, and with advisers, staff and secretariat the total reached 3,500, besides more than 2,500 press and radio representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work was divided among four commissions and twelve technical committees, more than five thousand documents were considered, and every part of the Charter needed a two-thirds majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Charter was adopted unanimously on 25 June 1945 in the San Francisco Opera House and signed the next day, and Poland, absent because its government was not yet recognised, signed later as the 51st original member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Closing the conference, Truman told the delegates that if the world had had such a Charter a few years earlier, with the will to use it, millions now dead would be alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Charter came into force on 24 October 1945, once the five permanent members of the Security Council and a majority of the other signatories had ratified it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations, “1945: The San Francisco Conference,” History of the United Nations, https://www.un.org/en/about-us/history-of-the-un/san-francisco-conference. [Open access]","United Nations, “Preparatory Years: UN Charter History,” History of the United Nations, https://www.un.org/en/about-us/history-of-the-un/preparatory-years. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “The United Nations, 1945,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/un. [Open access]","“Protocol of Proceedings of Crimea Conference,” 11 February 1945, sec. I, “World Organization,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","“Joint Four-Nation Declaration,” Moscow Conference, October 1943, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Address in San Francisco at the Closing Session of the United Nations Conference,” 26 June 1945, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/address-san-francisco-the-closing-session-the-united-nations-conference. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were only some states invited to San Francisco?","a":"Because invitations went to states that had declared war on Germany and Japan and subscribed to the Declaration by United Nations by March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conference itself then invited Byelorussia, Ukraine, newly liberated Denmark and Argentina, bringing the number of nations to fifty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ukraine and Byelorussia get seats of their own?","a":"Because Roosevelt agreed with Stalin before the conference that the two Soviet republics should be members of the General Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt reserved the right to seek two extra votes for the United States as well, but never used it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the United Nations not exist once the Charter was signed?","a":"Because many countries had to have the Charter approved by their parliaments first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came into force only when China, France, Britain, the Soviet Union, the United States and a majority of the other signatories had ratified it, and the American Senate approved it on 28 July 1945 by 89 votes to 2.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"United Nations Conference on International Organization"},
    "ww2-968": {"questions":["Under the reparations terms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each occupier took from its own zone, while the Soviet Union also received a share of surplus industrial equipment from the western zones.","Placing German lands east of the Oder and western Neisse under Polish administration, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left Poland's final western frontier to wait for a future peace settlement."],"abstract":"The <b>Potsdam Agreement</b> was the set of decisions reached by the Soviet Union, the United States and Britain at their conference near Berlin from 17 July to 2 August 1945, recorded in a protocol dated 1 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The protocol was signed by Stalin, Truman and Clement Attlee, who replaced Churchill as British prime minister while the conference was sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It set up a Council of Foreign Ministers to prepare peace treaties with Italy, Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary and Finland, and later a settlement for Germany once a German government fit for the purpose existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Supreme authority in Germany was to be held by the four commanders-in-chief, each in his own zone and jointly, through the Control Council, in matters affecting Germany as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its aims for Germany were demilitarisation, denazification, democratisation, the breaking up of cartels and decentralisation, together with the arrest and trial of war criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> During the occupation Germany was to be treated as a single economic unit, with common policies on matters from industry and finance to reparations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Yalta the Soviets had pressed for heavy reparations, but Truman and his Secretary of State, James Byrnes, wanting to avoid a repeat of Versailles, won the rule that each occupier would take reparations from its own zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Soviet Union was also to receive 15 per cent of surplus industrial equipment from the western zones in exchange for food and raw materials, and a further 10 per cent without payment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pending a peace settlement, German territory east of the Oder and western Neisse rivers was placed under Polish administration, and the city of Königsberg was promised to the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> France accepted the decisions on 7 August 1945 with reservations, while the transfer of German populations was to be carried out in an orderly and humane manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “The Potsdam Conference, 1945,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/potsdam-conf. [Open access]","Anne Baghdady and Markus Würz, “Potsdamer Konferenz,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/befreiung-und-besatzung/potsdamer-konferenz.html. [in German] [Open access]","“Demontagen,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/doppelte-staatsgruendung/demontagen.html. [in German] [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Radio Report to the American People on the Potsdam Conference,” 9 August 1945, The American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/radio-report-the-american-people-the-potsdam-conference. [Open access]","“Protocol of Proceedings of Crimea Conference,” 11 February 1945, sec. V, “Reparation,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","“Protocol of the Proceedings of the Berlin Conference,” Berlin, 1 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 2, doc. 1383, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv02/d1383. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were reparations to be taken in equipment rather than money?","a":"Because Truman said the United States would not again make the mistake of exacting reparations in money and then lending Germany the money to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> This time they were to be paid in physical assets not needed for Germany's peacetime subsistence, and their first purpose was to remove everything with which it could prepare another war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did each power take reparations from its own zone?","a":"Because Truman and Byrnes wanted to soften the treatment of Germany and avoid the situation created by the Treaty of Versailles, whose heavy reparations many experts blamed for weakening the German economy and helping the Nazis rise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each occupier was therefore allowed to dismantle plants only in its own zone, with the Soviet Union taking an extra share from the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the agreement fail to keep Germany together?","a":"Because the powers wanted different things: the Soviet Union and France sought quick and extensive reparations, while Britain and the United States wanted to steady the German economy so that they did not have to feed the population themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The national German government it foresaw was postponed indefinitely, and the Control Council ran the country instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Potsdam Agreement"},
    "ww2-969": {"questions":["An American directive of April 1945 told Eisenhower that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would be held not for the purpose of liberation but as a defeated enemy nation.","When the Soviet delegate walked out of the Control Council in March 1948, four-power rule over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke down, and two German states soon followed."],"abstract":"<b>Allied-occupied Germany</b> was the country as governed by the four victorious powers, the United States, the Soviet Union, Britain and France, after the unconditional surrender of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Yalta in February 1945 the Big Three agreed that France should have a zone carved out of the British and American ones, and that a Control Council should run Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> By the Berlin Declaration of 5 June 1945 the four governments assumed supreme authority over Germany, including all the powers of its government, and reserved the right to fix its boundaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each commander-in-chief ruled his own zone, while the four acted jointly through the Control Council in matters affecting Germany as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Berlin, deep inside the Soviet zone, was likewise split into four sectors run jointly by an Allied Kommandatura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> An American directive of April 1945 told Eisenhower that Germany would be occupied not for the purpose of liberation but as a defeated enemy nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Control Council first met on 30 July 1945, repealed Nazi laws and prepared demilitarisation, denazification and democratisation, but its decisions had to be unanimous and the four powers increasingly acted alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain and the United States merged their zones economically into the 'Bizone' at the start of 1947 after the Soviet Union rejected a joint administration of all four, and France joined only in April 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Soviet delegate left the Control Council on 20 March 1948, and on 24 June Soviet forces blockaded land and water access to the western sectors of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The airlift that supplied the city until the blockade was lifted in May 1949 sealed the division of Germany and of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Declaration Regarding the Defeat of Germany and the Assumption of Supreme Authority by Allied Powers,” 5 June 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger01.asp. [Open access]","US Joint Chiefs of Staff, “Directive to Commander-in-Chief of United States Forces of Occupation Regarding the Military Government of Germany,” April 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger02.asp. [Open access]","“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","Markus Würz, “Alliierte Besatzung,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/befreiung-und-besatzung/alliierte-besatzung.html. [in German] [Open access]","Markus Würz, “Scheitern der Viermächte-Regierung,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/doppelte-staatsgruendung/scheitern-der-viermaechte-regierung.html. [in German] [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","“Protocol of Proceedings of Crimea Conference,” 11 February 1945, sec. III–IV, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the occupiers take over all government in Germany?","a":"Because, as the Berlin Declaration put it, there was no central government or authority in Germany able to keep order, run the country and carry out the victors' requirements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The four governments therefore assumed supreme authority, including all the powers of the German government and of every state and local authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did four-power government break down?","a":"Because the Control Council could act only unanimously, and the occupiers increasingly went their own ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the Soviet Union and the West came to see each other as ideological blocs, joint rule failed: the Soviet Union rejected a common economic administration, the British and American zones merged, and in 1948 the Soviet representative walked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Berlin become the first crisis of the Cold War?","a":"Because the city lay far inside Soviet-controlled eastern Germany while its western sectors were held by the United States, Britain and France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the Soviets blockaded those sectors in June 1948, the Western powers answered by flying food and fuel in from their bases in western Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Allied-occupied Germany"},
    "ww2-970": {"questions":["In the Soviet zone <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was tied to a socialist remaking of society, and some 520,000 people lost their posts by 1949, many replaced by communists.","Ordered at Potsdam in 1945, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meant removing every more than nominal Nazi party member from public office and from positions of responsibility in important firms."],"abstract":"<b>Denazification</b> was the Allied effort after 1945 to rid German public life, law and society of National Socialism, as agreed at the Potsdam Conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The occupiers banned the Nazi party and its organisations, repealed Nazi laws and removed everyday traces of the regime, from street names to uniforms and medals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Potsdam the powers agreed that all party members who had been more than nominal participants, and everyone hostile to Allied purposes, should be removed from public office and responsible posts in important firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American directive of April 1945 defined such people as those who had held party office, taken part in Nazi crimes, been avowed believers or given substantial support to the party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Allies also interned officials and suspects, about 120,000 in the American zone, 90,000 in the British and 21,500 in the French, while some 189,000 people were held in the ten Soviet special camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the American zone the Law for Liberation from National Socialism and Militarism, proclaimed at Munich on 5 March 1946, handed the work to German tribunals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its registration covered about 13.4 million Germans over 18, among them some 3.67 million former members of the party or its organisations, and the tribunals placed people in five classes from major offenders to the exonerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal had declared the Leadership Corps, the Gestapo and SD and the SS criminal organisations, which shaped who counted as compromised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Control Council tried to make procedures uniform with Directive 38 in October 1946, and by 1950 more than 950,000 tribunal cases had been heard in the American zone alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the Americans ended the process on 31 March 1948 many cases against the more serious offenders were still open and many escaped punishment, while Britain and France put rebuilding first and the Soviets used the purge to install communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andreas Grau, Regina Haunhorst and Markus Würz, “Entnazifizierung,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/entnazifizierung-und-antifaschismus/entnazifizierung.html. [in German] [Open access]","Markus Würz, “Internierungs- und Speziallager,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/entnazifizierung-und-antifaschismus/internierungs-und-speziallager.html. [in German] [Open access]","“The Berlin (Potsdam) Conference, July 17–August 2, 1945: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” sec. III, “Germany,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","US Joint Chiefs of Staff, “Directive to Commander-in-Chief of United States Forces of Occupation Regarding the Military Government of Germany,” April 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/ger02.asp. [Open access]","The United States Political Adviser for Germany (Murphy) to the Secretary of State, despatch no. 2330, Berlin, 7 March 1946, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 5, <i>The British Commonwealth, Western and Central Europe</i>, doc. 438, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v05/d438. [Open access]","German Historical Institute Washington, “Vorbereitung der Spruchkammerverfahren in Nürnberg (1. Februar 1947),” <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/de/die-besatzungszeit-und-die-entstehung-zweier-staaten-1945-1961/vorbereitung-der-spruchkammerverfahren-in-nuernberg-1-februar-1947. [in German] [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Accused Organizations,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judorg.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Americans hand denazification to German tribunals?","a":"Because a policy board in the American military government aimed to place as much responsibility as possible on the Germans themselves, and the resulting law was approved and proclaimed at Munich in March 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tribunals were to be staffed by people without political taint, ideally qualified as judges or senior officials, who reported their findings to the military government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the tribunals struggle to find staff?","a":"Because Nazi involvement was common among lawyers and officials, the very people best qualified to sit on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The process also grew uneven from place to place and fell into disrepute, which made it harder still to fill the posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did denazification differ so much from zone to zone?","a":"Because each occupier pursued its own aims: the Americans classified and judged strictly, the British and French gave priority to rebuilding administration and the economy, and the Soviets linked it to a socialist transformation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Soviet special camps the secret police also held people who had been denounced and political opponents, and over a third of the prisoners died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Denazification"},
    "ww2-971": {"questions":["Under article 8 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a defendant who had acted on the orders of his government or a superior was not freed from responsibility, though punishment might be mitigated.","Article 9 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> allowed the Nuremberg judges to declare whole organisations criminal when they convicted a member, which later made membership itself punishable."],"abstract":"The <b>London Charter</b> was the charter of the International Military Tribunal, annexed to an agreement signed in London on 8 August 1945 by the United States, France, Britain and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The agreement set up the tribunal to try war criminals whose offences had no particular geographical location, while leaving others to be sent back to the countries where they had committed their crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That division followed the Moscow Declaration of 1943, which promised to return German criminals to the scenes of their deeds and to punish the major ones by joint decision of the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The charter gave the tribunal four members, one from each signatory, each with an alternate, and the power to impose death or any other punishment it judged just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It defined three crimes, crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and included participation in a common plan or conspiracy to commit crimes against peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The charter swept away two familiar defences: article 7 made official position, even as head of state, no ground for freedom from responsibility, and article 8 allowed obedience to orders only in mitigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Article 9 let the tribunal declare a group or organisation criminal when it convicted one of its members, so that membership itself could later be punished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American chief negotiator, Robert Jackson, had told Truman in June that the Allies could punish the leaders without a hearing, but that undiscriminating executions would violate pledges repeatedly given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the signing he said that for the first time four powerful nations had agreed on individual responsibility for attacking the international peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He conceded that the procedure followed continental rather than American practice and that the trials risked looking like victors' vengeance, but argued that there was no other way to judge such crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Agreement for the Prosecution and Punishment of the Major War Criminals of the European Axis” (London Agreement), 8 August 1945, in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtchart.asp. [Open access]","“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]","“Statement on Atrocities,” Moscow Conference, October 1943, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/moscow.asp. [Open access]","Robert H. Jackson, “Report to the President on Atrocities and War Crimes,” 7 June 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imt_jack01.asp. [Open access]","Robert H. Jackson, “Statement by Justice Jackson on War Trials Agreement,” 12 August 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imt_jack02.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/international-military-tribunal-at-nuremberg. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the charter follow mostly European rather than American procedure?","a":"Because Jackson accepted that the continental system was the one the Germans themselves had used and understood, and that a trial of Europeans in Europe for crimes committed in Europe could reasonably follow it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He insisted that the essentials of a fair trial had still been assured, and the charter set out rights for the defendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the charter matter beyond Nuremberg?","a":"Because its definitions, Jackson said, imposed liability on war-making statesmen of all countries alike, not only on Germans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was also the first time that four nations with such different legal systems had tried to knit their ideas of criminal procedure into one cooperative trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a trial chosen instead of summary punishment?","a":"Because Jackson argued that executions or punishments without definite findings of guilt, fairly arrived at, would not sit easily on the American conscience or be remembered by later generations with pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A trial would also set out the record of the crimes, which he thought the world needed to see presented patiently and fairly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-972": {"questions":["Defendants at Nuremberg argued that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke the rule against punishing acts that were not crimes when done, since no statute had made aggressive war a crime.","The judges answered objections to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by pointing to the Kellogg–Briand Pact of 1928, which bound 63 nations, Germany included, to renounce war as an instrument of policy."],"abstract":"<b>Crimes against peace</b> were defined in the charter of the International Military Tribunal in 1945 as planning, preparing, initiating or waging a war of aggression or a war breaking treaties, or conspiring to do so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They stood first among the three crimes the tribunal could try, ahead of war crimes and crimes against humanity, and carried individual responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Unlike war crimes, which rested on existing custom and conventions, crimes against peace had never before been defined as punishable offences in international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The drafters argued that the new charge rested on pre-war treaties and declarations that had condemned wars of aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The indictment, signed by prosecutors from the four powers, charged the defendants with crimes against peace and with a common plan or conspiracy to commit them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The defence argued that punishing acts that were not crimes when committed was abhorrent to civilised law, and that no statute had defined aggressive war or fixed a penalty for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribunal replied that the rule against such punishment was a principle of justice, not a limit on sovereignty, and that the defendants must have known they were defying international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It pointed to the Kellogg–Briand Pact of 27 August 1928, by which the parties condemned recourse to war and renounced it as an instrument of national policy, and which bound 63 nations in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It called the initiation of a war of aggression the supreme international crime, differing from other war crimes only in that it contains within itself the accumulated evil of the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The idea survives as the crime of aggression in the Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court, a leader's manifest violation of the Charter of the United Nations by armed force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” art. 6, in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Law of the Charter,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawch.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Nazi Regime in Germany; The Common Plan or Conspiracy and Aggressive War,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judnazi.asp. [Open access]","“General Treaty for Renunciation of War as an Instrument of National Policy” (Kellogg–Briand Pact), 27 August 1928, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/kbpact.asp. [Open access]","Robert H. Jackson, François de Menthon, Hartley Shawcross and R. A. Rudenko, “Indictment,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/count.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “How Were the Crimes Defined?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/how-were-the-crimes-defined. [Open access]","International Criminal Court, <i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i> (The Hague: International Criminal Court, 2021), art. 8 bis, https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could individuals be punished for a state's war?","a":"Because, the tribunal held, crimes against international law are committed by men, not by abstract entities, and only by punishing individuals who commit them can the law be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The charter itself made individual responsibility attach to each of the crimes it listed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the judges treat aggression as the gravest crime?","a":"Because war is essentially an evil thing whose consequences reach the whole world, not only the states at war, and starting such a war therefore contains within itself the accumulated evil of all the other crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The judgment called it, for that reason, the supreme international crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the charge accused of being retroactive?","a":"Because international law before 1945 had condemned aggressive war but had never made it a punishable offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The defence said that no court, statute or penalty had existed for it, and the tribunal answered that the defendants, who knew the treaties Germany had signed, could not claim it would be unjust to punish them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Crime of aggression"},
    "ww2-973": {"questions":["The UN General Assembly affirmed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unanimously in December 1946, 2 months after the judgment at Nuremberg had sentenced twelve defendants to death.","Drawn from the charter and judgment of 1945 and 1946, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold that a head of state who commits a crime under international law is not thereby relieved of responsibility."],"abstract":"The <b>Nuremberg principles</b> are seven principles of international law drawn from the charter of the International Military Tribunal and from the tribunal's judgment, formulated by the UN International Law Commission in 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly first affirmed the principles in resolution 95 (I) of 11 December 1946, adopted on American initiative after the judgment of 1 October 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1947 it directed the newly created commission to formulate them, and the commission decided that, since they were already affirmed, its task was merely to state them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Principle I makes anyone who commits a crime under international law responsible and liable to punishment, echoing the judgment's words that such crimes are committed by men, not by abstract entities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Principle II adds that the absence of a penalty in a country's own law does not free the person from responsibility under international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Principle III denies immunity to a head of state or responsible official, building on article 7 of the charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Principle IV rejects the defence of superior orders provided a moral choice was in fact possible, a test taken from the judgment, while dropping the charter's mention of mitigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Principle V gives anyone charged the right to a fair trial on the facts and law, Principle VI lists crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and Principle VII makes complicity a crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Assembly never formally adopted the formulation, merely inviting governments to comment in 1950, and a draft code of offences built on it was shelved in 1954.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their substance lives on in the Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court, which makes official capacity irrelevant and allows a defence of orders only in narrow cases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Law Commission, “Principles of International Law Recognized in the Charter of the Nürnberg Tribunal and in the Judgment of the Tribunal,” 1950, in <i>Yearbook of the International Law Commission, 1950</i>, vol. 2, para. 97, https://legal.un.org/ilc/texts/instruments/english/draft_articles/7_1_1950.pdf. [Open access]","International Law Commission, “Formulation of the Nürnberg Principles,” Summaries of the Work of the International Law Commission, https://legal.un.org/ilc/summaries/7_1.shtml. [Open access]","Antonio Cassese, “Affirmation of the Principles of International Law Recognized by the Charter of the Nürnberg Tribunal: General Assembly Resolution 95 (I),” United Nations Audiovisual Library of International Law, 2009, https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/ga_95-I/ga_95-I.html. [Open access]","“Charter of the International Military Tribunal,” in <i>Nuremberg Trial Proceedings</i>, vol. 1, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtconst.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Law of the Charter,” <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i> (Nuremberg, 1947), Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawch.asp. [Open access]","International Criminal Court, <i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i> (The Hague: International Criminal Court, 2021), arts. 27 and 33, https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the commission not judge whether the principles were valid law?","a":"Because the General Assembly had already affirmed them unanimously in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commission therefore concluded that its task was not to express any appreciation of them as principles of international law but merely to formulate them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the commission add the idea of a moral choice to superior orders?","a":"Because the Nuremberg judgment had said the true test was not the existence of an order but whether moral choice was in fact possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commission took that test into its principle and left the question of lighter punishment to the competent court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it matter that domestic law cannot excuse these crimes?","a":"Because the judgment held that the very essence of the charter was that individuals have international duties transcending the obedience owed to their own state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same idea already appeared in the charter's definition of crimes against humanity, which covered such acts whether or not they broke the domestic law of the country where they were committed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nuremberg principles"},
    "ww2-974": {"questions":["In the Einsatzgruppen case, one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, all 22 men tried for mass shootings in the occupied Soviet Union were convicted and fourteen were sentenced to death.","Brought under Control Council Law Number 10 with Telford Taylor as chief prosecutor, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in 24 death sentences, though clemency later shortened many prison terms."],"abstract":"The <b>Subsequent Nuremberg trials</b> were twelve proceedings held by American military tribunals at Nuremberg after the trial of the major war criminals, against leading industrialists, military figures, SS perpetrators and others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their legal basis was Control Council Law No. 10 of 20 December 1945, which gave occupied Germany a uniform basis for prosecuting war criminals other than those tried by the International Military Tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The law listed crimes against peace, war crimes, crimes against humanity and membership in organisations declared criminal, and it added imprisonment, torture and rape to the acts counted as crimes against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American ordinance of 18 October 1946 created the tribunals, each of three or more members who had practised for at least 5 years in the highest courts of an American state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> General Telford Taylor was appointed chief prosecutor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the first tribunal had already established that these acts were crimes, the later trials set out to decide the guilt of second-tier Nazis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The defendants included doctors, lawyers, industrialists, SS and police leaders, soldiers, ministers, officials and diplomats, and the trials continued until 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Medical Case, the first of them, defendants charged over experiments on prisoners included Karl Brandt, the regime's commissioner for public health, who was convicted and executed in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the Einsatzgruppen case 24 men were indicted for murdering between 723,661 and one million people, and all 22 who were tried were convicted, fourteen of them sentenced to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In all 185 people were indicted and 177 tried, giving 24 death sentences, 20 life terms and 98 other prison sentences against 35 acquittals, though many terms were later shortened by clemency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Subsequent Nuremberg Proceedings, Case #9: The Einsatzgruppen Case,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/subsequent-nuremberg-proceedings-case-9-the-einsatzgruppen-case. [Open access]","Allied Control Council, “Control Council Law No. 10: Punishment of Persons Guilty of War Crimes, Crimes against Peace and against Humanity,” 20 December 1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imt10.asp. [Open access]","Office of Military Government for Germany (US), “Ordinance No. 7: Organization and Powers of Certain Military Tribunals,” 18 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imt07.asp. [Open access]","Anne Baghdady and Markus Würz, “Nürnberger Prozesse,” <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Stiftung Haus der Geschichte der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, https://www.hdg.de/lemo/kapitel/nachkriegsjahre/entnazifizierung-und-antifaschismus/nuernberger-prozesse.html. [in German] [Open access]","Harvard Law School Library, “The Medical Case: Introduction,” Nuremberg Trials Project, https://nuremberg.law.harvard.edu/nmt_1_intro. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the later trials held by Americans alone?","a":"Because Control Council Law No. 10 let each occupying power try war criminals in its own zone, and the United States set up its own military tribunals under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ordinance allowed joint trials with other zone commanders, but the twelve proceedings at Nuremberg were American.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did these trials focus on professions such as doctors and industrialists?","a":"Because the first tribunal had already established that war crimes, aggression and crimes against humanity were crimes, so the later trials could examine the guilt of the second tier of people who carried them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That second tier included the doctors, lawyers, industrialists and officials whose work had made the crimes possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were many sentences shorter than those handed down?","a":"Because the reviewing authorities later commuted many of them to shorter terms as an act of clemency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, the Einsatzgruppen judges had sentenced fourteen of 22 defendants to death for mass killings in the occupied Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Subsequent Nuremberg trials"},
    "ww2-975": {"questions":["Seven men convicted by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them the former prime minister Tojo, were hanged at Sugamo Prison in Tokyo on 23 December 1948.","Unlike at Nuremberg, only men charged with crimes against peace could be tried by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which set up court in the old War Ministry building at Ichigaya."],"abstract":"The <b>International Military Tribunal for the Far East</b>, often called the Tokyo Trial, was the court the Allies set up to try the leaders of Japan after the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Potsdam Declaration of July 1945 had warned that stern justice would be meted out to all war criminals, including those who had been cruel to prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> General Douglas MacArthur established the tribunal by a special proclamation on 19 January 1946 and approved its charter, which closely followed the one used at Nuremberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charter allowed between six and eleven judges, and the bench was enlarged to eleven so that India and the Philippines could each name one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Like the Nuremberg charter, it held that neither official position nor superior orders could by themselves free an accused from responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunal sat in the former War Ministry building at Ichigaya in Tokyo, and the prosecution opened its case on 3 May 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In all, 28 men, most of them senior officers and officials, were charged with crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity for the years from 1928 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two accused died during the trial and a third was found unfit, and over 2 years the court admitted 4,336 exhibits and heard 419 witnesses in a transcript of 48,412 pages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All 25 remaining defendants were convicted, all but one of crimes against peace, and seven were sentenced to death, sixteen to life imprisonment and two to shorter terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The seven, Tojo among them, were hanged at Sugamo Prison on 23 December 1948, and the absence of the emperor from the dock ran through many of the judges' separate opinions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["The National WWII Museum, “The Tokyo War Crimes Trial,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/topics/tokyo-war-crimes-trial. [Open access]","Yuma Totani, “International Military Tribunals at Tokyo, 1946–1949: Individual Responsibility for War Crimes,” in <i>Historical Origins of International Criminal Law</i>, vol. 2, ed. Morten Bergsmo, Cheah Wui Ling and Yi Ping, FICHL Publication Series 21 (Brussels: Torkel Opsahl Academic EPublisher, 2014), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/925003/pdf. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, part A, chap. 1, “Establishment and Proceedings of the Tribunal” (Tokyo, 1948), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-1.html. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment</i>, part C, chap. 10, “Verdicts” (Tokyo, 1948), HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-10.html. [Open access]","“Charter of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East,” 19 January 1946, annex A-5 to the <i>Judgment</i>, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-A5.html. [Open access]","“The Potsdam Declaration,” 26 July 1945, annex A-1 to the <i>Judgment</i> of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East, HyperWar, http://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/PTO/IMTFE/IMTFE-A1.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did crimes against peace dominate the Tokyo Trial?","a":"Because a decision taken at the highest level among the Allies made the tribunal a venue to try major war criminals principally for crimes against peace and only secondarily for atrocities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only individuals charged with crimes against peace could be prosecuted before it, so no lesser category of charge was heard in Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was evidence of atrocities hard to gather?","a":"Because the prosecution had to give priority to crimes against peace and was chronically short of staff and resources for investigating war crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese officials and military units had also tried to destroy physical and documentary evidence of atrocities before Allied investigators arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the trial take more than 2 years?","a":"Because the court admitted a vast mass of documents and testimony, filling 48,412 pages of transcript, and both the facts and the meaning of each incident were disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The judges also complained that counsel and witnesses tended to be prolix and irrelevant, and they introduced rules such as written depositions to save time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"International Military Tribunal for the Far East"},
    "ww2-976": {"questions":["A Croatian priest in Rome, Krunoslav Draganović, ran one of the best known <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and American army intelligence used it to move informants out of Europe.","Sent along one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the name Klaus Altmann, the former Gestapo chief of Lyon reached South America in 1951 and lived in Bolivia for decades."],"abstract":"<b>Ratlines</b> were the escape routes by which Nazi war criminals and their collaborators fled Europe after the Second World War, mostly through Italy to South America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1947 an American official, Vincent La Vista, reported to the State Department that the Red Cross was issuing passports without screening and that a group of priests was helping former Nazis emigrate illegally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bishop Alois Hudal, rector of a German college in Rome, was accused from 1949 of hiding and helping Germans, and the Treblinka commandant Franz Stangl later said Hudal had given him a Red Cross passport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best known route ran from Austria to Italy and relied on Krunoslav Draganović, a Croatian priest at a seminary in Rome who helped Croatian nationalists escape with Red Cross passports and South American visas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Draganović helped numerous Croatian war criminals reach South America through the College of Saint Jerome in Rome, and later worked as a spy for the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> American army intelligence in Austria used this 'rat line' for several years to evacuate defectors and informants from the Soviet bloc, paying Draganović to take them on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1951 it sent Klaus Barbie, the former head of the Gestapo in Lyon and its informant since 1947, down the line under the name Klaus Altmann, and he left for South America in March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Adolf Eichmann also fled to Argentina with the help of Catholic Church officials and lived there under aliases such as Ricardo Klement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stangl lived openly in Brazil from 1951 until his arrest in 1967, and the great majority of Nazi offenders were never punished at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Bolivia expelled Barbie to France in 1983, a Justice Department report admitted the American role, and the United States expressed its regret for delaying justice in Lyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Matteo Sanfilippo, “Ratlines and Unholy Trinities: A Review-Essay on (Recent) Literature Concerning Nazi and Collaborators Smuggling Operations out of Italy,” Stiftung für Sozialgeschichte des 20. Jahrhunderts, https://stiftung-sozialgeschichte.de/index.php/en/beitraege/87-zeitschrift-archiv/94-sozial-geschichte-extra/95-beitraege/182-ratlines-and-unholy-trinities. [Open access]","Allan A. Ryan Jr., <i>Klaus Barbie and the United States Government: A Report to the Attorney General of the United States</i> (Washington, DC: US Department of Justice, Criminal Division, August 1983), 135–51, https://www.justice.gov/sites/default/files/criminal-hrsp/legacy/2011/02/04/08-02-83barbie-rpt.pdf. [Open access]","Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, <i>Final Report to the United States Congress</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration, April 2007), https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/reports/final-report-2007.pdf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Evading Justice,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/evading-justice. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Adolf Eichmann,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-eichmann. [Open access]","“Behind the Headlines: A Report That Almost Didn’t Make It,” Jewish Telegraphic Agency, 23 September 1983, https://jta.org/archive/behind-the-headlines-a-report-that-almost-didnt-make-it. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did American intelligence help a wanted man escape?","a":"Because Barbie had worked as an informant for the Counter Intelligence Corps since 1947, and the corps feared that if he were caught he would be extradited to France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rat line, already used to move defectors out of Europe, offered a ready-made way to get him beyond reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were Red Cross papers so useful to fugitives?","a":"Because Red Cross passports let people without valid documents travel, and in 1947 an American report found the Red Cross issuing them without screening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With such papers and South American visas, fugitives could sail from Italian ports such as Genoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the Vatican's role and the size of the escapes still disputed?","a":"Because much of the early writing repeated older accounts without new evidence, and clergy in Rome told one investigator that Hudal was a small fish who tried to seem more powerful than he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hudal and Draganović also competed with each other and distrusted the Vatican leadership, which complicates any simple story of a single Church network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ratlines (World War II)"},
    "ww2-977": {"questions":["Eleven of the 30 defendants found guilty in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were sentenced to death, among them Irma Grese, and were hanged at Hameln in December 1945.","Because many of the camp's staff had earlier served at Auschwitz, charges about Auschwitz were heard in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as well, though Auschwitz lay outside every Allied zone."],"abstract":"The <b>Belsen trial</b> was the trial of Josef Kramer and 44 others by a British military court at Lüneburg from 17 September to 17 November 1945, for crimes at the Bergen-Belsen and Auschwitz camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> British troops had liberated Bergen-Belsen on 15 April 1945, and their reports from the camp shaped much of British understanding of Nazi atrocities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a British rather than an Allied trial, held under a Royal Warrant of June 1945 that let army courts try violations of the laws and usages of war committed since 2 September 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The warrant covered war crimes but not crimes against humanity, which had no accepted definition in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 45 accused, 24 were men and 21 women, 35 were SS personnel and ten were prisoner functionaries known as kapos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The prosecution charged both individual offences and knowing participation in a common plan to run a system of ill-treatment and murder in the camps, and it relied unusually heavily on documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The defence argued that camp crimes were not war crimes, that many victims were not Allied nationals and that the camps were lawful under German law, and many pleaded superior orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 6 hours of deliberation the court convicted 30 defendants, acquitted 14 and sentenced eleven to death, including Kramer, the doctor Fritz Klein and the guard Irma Grese, who were hanged at Hameln on 13 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A newsreel shown to Germans in November presented Kramer as the beast of Belsen and said the verdict could not erase the camp's suffering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Parliament the government explained that no one had been charged specifically with murder, and survivors and the French, Soviet and Polish governments found the sentences too lenient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Bergen-Belsen Trial,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/bergen-belsen-trial. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Bergen-Belsen,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/bergen-belsen. [Open access]","United Nations War Crimes Commission, <i>Law Reports of Trials of War Criminals</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Belsen Trial</i> (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1947), case no. 10, 1–2, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/Law-Reports_Vol-2/Law-Reports_Vol-2.pdf. [Open access]","“Royal Warrant: Regulations for the Trial of War Criminals,” 14 June 1945, Army Order 81/1945, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/imtroyal.asp. [Open access]","German Historical Institute Washington, “Report on the First Belsen Trial (November 1945),” <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/occupation-and-the-emergence-of-two-states-1945-1961/report-on-the-first-belsen-trial-november-1945.pdf. [Open access]","“Belsen Trial,” House of Commons, 3 December 1945, <i>Hansard</i>, 5th ser., vol. 416, cols. 1909–10, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/dec/03/belsen-trial. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Auschwitz crimes tried in a British court in Germany?","a":"Because Auschwitz was not in any Allied occupation zone, so none of the four powers could hold a trial there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many Belsen staff had served at Auschwitz, and most survivors had been at Belsen for under 3 months, so adding Auschwitz widened the witnesses and crimes the court could hear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the trial charge a common plan as well as individual acts?","a":"Because the prosecution argued that the accused had all knowingly taken part in operating a system of ill-treatment and murder, so each could answer for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Royal Warrant allowed evidence of a unit's collective responsibility to be used against each member of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did many people think the verdicts too lenient?","a":"Because fourteen defendants were acquitted and some found guilty received short prison terms, which upset Jewish and survivor communities and the French, Soviet and Polish governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When a member of Parliament asked how many convicted of murder had escaped death, the government replied that none had been charged with murder at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Belsen trials"},
    "ww2-978": {"questions":["Because the exile government had made membership of Quisling's party a crime, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached tens of thousands of ordinary members of Nasjonal Samling as well as torturers.","Critics of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that the decree of December 1944 on which it rested really punished acts after the fact, and that profiteers escaped more lightly than politicians."],"abstract":"The <b>legal purge in Norway</b>, known in Norwegian as the landssvikoppgjøret, was the judicial reckoning after the Second World War with Norwegians who had helped the enemy during the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached every kind and degree of treason, from political leaders, torturers, informers and murderers to rank-and-file members of Quisling's party, Nasjonal Samling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exile government in London had made it a crime to join or remain in the party, which from 1940 had been the only legal party and formed the collaborating government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its decrees allowed the death penalty after the war and fines or loss of civic trust for membership, and the treason decree of 15 December 1944 gathered the rules together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 200 camps held suspects after the liberation, some of them for years, and Norway ended with one of the highest numbers of convicted collaborators in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In all 92,805 cases were investigated, and 46,085 people, 30,002 men and 16,083 women, were punished, while 37,150 cases were dropped for lack of evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thirty Norwegians were sentenced to death and 25 executed, most of them members of the Norwegian or German security police guilty of torture and murder, but only three politicians, among them Quisling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The purge also reached the economy, the civil service and organisations, and the writer Knut Hamsun was ordered by a court to pay 425,000 kroner in damages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Recent research has found that 38 Norwegian writers were investigated for treason, more than twice as many as once thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prison terms were shortened over time, and the last four men serving life were freed in 1957, while critics questioned whether the 1944 decree was retroactive and why profiteers fared better than politicians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Johs. Andenæs, “Landssvikoppgjøret,” <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/landssvikoppgj%C3%B8ret. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Johs. Andenæs, “Landssviklovgivningen,” <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/landssviklovgivningen. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Trond Risto, “Landssvikleirene,” <i>Store norske leksikon</i>, https://snl.no/landssvikleirene. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","Arkivverket, “Tema: Landssvikoppgjøret,” <i>Arkivmagasinet</i>, no. 3 (2009), https://www.nasjonalarkivet.no/content/uploads/2025/12/Arkivmagasinet-3_09-Landssvikoppgjoret.pdf. [in Norwegian] [Open access]","OsloMet, “Langt flere norske forfattere ble etterforsket i landssvikoppgjøret enn vi har trodd,” research news, https://www.oslomet.no/forskning/forskningsnyheter/forfattere-landssvikoppgjoret. [in Norwegian] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the purge reach so many ordinary party members?","a":"Because the exile government had made membership of Nasjonal Samling itself a crime, so every member could be prosecuted after the occupation ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Membership of Nazi organisations counted automatically as punishable in Norway, which is a main reason why so many were convicted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the death penalty restored for the purge?","a":"Because the exile government decided in 1941 that death could be imposed after the war for crimes against the state, and in 1942 it extended the penalty with murderers and torturers particularly in mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the 25 people executed, 22 were connected with the Norwegian or German security police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the purge been criticised?","a":"Because it was argued that the treason decree of December 1944 in reality punished acts retroactively, and that making the purge so wide was unwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Critics also said it was lopsided, since many who profited economically from the Germans were not party members and escaped more lightly than the political collaborators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Legal purge in Norway after World War II"},
    "ww2-979": {"questions":["Some 100,000 French men and women were found guilty of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the Liberation and lost rights from voting to working as teachers, lawyers or journalists.","Courts called civic chambers judged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose penalty of national degradation was cut back by the amnesty law of 5 January 1951 for about 80 per cent of those condemned."],"abstract":"<b>Indignité nationale</b>, ‘national unworthiness’, was a crime created in liberated France by an ordinance of 26 August 1944 to punish French people who had served the Vichy regime and its collaboration with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It struck anyone who after 16 June 1940 had given direct or indirect aid to Germany or its allies, or had harmed the unity of the nation or the liberty or equality of the French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lawyers in the Resistance had seen the need for a new crime as early as 1942, because the penal code covered dealings with the enemy but not voluntary help given to a French government that had chosen to collaborate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Typical grounds were membership of a pro-collaboration party, office in the Vichy government or its administration, or racist or fascist propaganda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ordinance was retroactive, punishing acts within a strictly bounded period, and it was codified by a second ordinance of 26 December 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Special courts called civic chambers, set up beside the courts of justice that tried graver collaboration, judged the crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its penalty, national degradation, stripped the condemned of a whole block of rights, from voting and bearing arms to working as a civil servant, lawyer, teacher, journalist or banker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later laws added a 10 per cent tax surcharge and loss of war damages, and about 10 per cent of the roughly 100,000 people condemned received national degradation for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such harshness turned some Vichy supporters into apparent victims of the Liberation, and an amnesty law of 5 January 1951 covered about 80 per cent of those condemned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians have argued that the scale of both the judicial and the administrative purge was underestimated by official figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jean-Jacques Urvoas, <i>Rapport d’information sur l’indignité nationale</i>, no. 2677, Commission des lois, Assemblée nationale, 25 March 2015, https://www.assemblee-nationale.fr/14/pdf/rap-info/i2677.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Sébastien Le Gal, “Réflexions sur le crime d’indignité nationale et la peine de dégradation nationale,” consultation for the Commission des lois of the Assemblée nationale, February 2015, Université Jean Moulin Lyon 3, https://hd.univ-lyon3.fr/medias/fichier/le-gal-rapport_1463675033301-pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Anne Simonin, “Rétablir l’indignité nationale? Une perspective historique,” Fondation Jean-Jaurès, https://www.jean-jaures.org/publication/retablir-lindignite-nationale-une-perspective-historique/. [in French] [Open access]","Henry Rousso, “L’épuration en France: une histoire inachevée,” <i>Vingtième Siècle, revue d’histoire</i>, no. 33 (1992): 78–105, Persée, https://www.persee.fr/doc/xxs_0294-1759_1992_num_33_1_2491. [in French] [Open access]","François Rouquet, “L’épuration administrative en France après la libération: une analyse statistique et géographique,” <i>Vingtième Siècle, revue d’histoire</i>, no. 33 (1992): 106–17, Persée, https://www.persee.fr/doc/xxs_0294-1759_1992_num_33_1_2492. [in French] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did France need a new crime after the Liberation?","a":"Because the penal code of 1940 could punish dealings with the enemy, but not the help that French people had given to their own government, Vichy, after it chose to collaborate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Resistance lawyers had to choose between stretching the old texts and inventing a new crime, and they chose a new one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was national degradation such a heavy penalty?","a":"Because it imposed an indivisible block of lost rights, dismissals, forfeitures and incapacities, and it could add confiscation of present and future property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those tried in their absence received it at its maximum, for life, with a ban from French territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the crime amnestied so soon?","a":"Because its harshness had, in one historian's words quoted by parliament, turned the Vichyists if not into martyrs then into victims of the Liberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The amnesty of January 1951 covered about 80 per cent of the condemned and cut the remaining incapacities to eight, for at most 20 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Indignité nationale"},
    "ww2-980": {"questions":["Alongside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, West Germany agreed a protocol with the Claims Conference, and 450 million Deutschmarks were paid through Israel for Jews living elsewhere.","Opposed by many in Adenauer's coalition and by much of the West German public, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed the Bundestag in 1953 only with the votes of the Social Democrats."],"abstract":"The <b>Luxembourg Agreement</b> was the treaty signed in Luxembourg on 10 September 1952 by which West Germany undertook to pay compensation to the State of Israel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its preamble recalled the unspeakable crimes committed against the Jewish people under National Socialism and the heavy burden Israel had taken on in resettling uprooted and destitute Jewish refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed Adenauer's declaration in the Bundestag on 27 September 1951 that the Federal Republic would, within its capacity, make good the material damage caused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West Germany was to pay Israel 3,000 million Deutschmarks in annual instalments, used to buy goods and services for settling and rehabilitating Jewish refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A further 450 million Deutschmarks was paid through Israel for the Conference on Jewish Material Claims against Germany, founded in 1951 by 23 Jewish organisations to represent Jews outside Israel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The agreement was bitterly contested on both sides: Israel's opposition at first vehemently rejected German 'blood money', and Arab states threatened to stop trading with West Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Critics in Germany argued that Israel had no right to reparations because it had not existed under Nazi rule, and in August 1952 only 11 per cent of West Germans approved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Adenauer pushed it through despite opposition in his cabinet and coalition, and it passed the Bundestag in 1953 only with the votes of the Social Democratic opposition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deliveries continued even during the Sinai war of 1956, which Israelis noted as proof of German reliability, and by 1966 deliveries worth 3,450 million marks had helped resettle about 500,000 Jewish refugees in Israel, including 60 ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Separate protocols with the Claims Conference led to German laws compensating individual Jewish victims, under which Germany has paid more than 90 billion dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Agreement between the State of Israel and the Federal Republic of Germany,” signed at Luxembourg, 10 September 1952, <i>United Nations Treaty Series</i>, vol. 162, no. 2137, https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%20162/volume-162-I-2137-English.pdf. [Open access]","German Historical Institute Washington, “Communiqué Regarding Restitution for Israel and the Jews (September 10, 1952),” <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/occupation-and-the-emergence-of-two-states-1945-1961/communique-regarding-restitution-for-israel-and-the-jews-september-10-1952. [Open access]","German Historical Institute Washington, “Chancellor Konrad Adenauer on the Federal Republic’s Attitude towards the Jews (September 27, 1951),” <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/occupation-and-the-emergence-of-two-states-1945-1961/chancellor-konrad-adenauer-on-the-federal-republic-s-attitude-towards-the-jews-september-27-1951. [Open access]","Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, “Wege zur diplomatischen Anerkennung,” <i>Informationen zur politischen Bildung</i>, Deutsch-Israelische Beziehungen, 2015, https://www.bpb.de/izpb/204306/wege-zur-diplomatischen-anerkennung?p=1. [in German] [Open access]","Conference on Jewish Material Claims Against Germany, “History,” https://www.claimscon.org/about/history/. [Open access]","“Germany Makes Final Report on Israel Reparations of $861,250,000,” Jewish Telegraphic Agency, 30 March 1966, https://www.jta.org/archive/germany-makes-final-report-on-israel-reparations-of-861250000. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the money paid mostly in goods?","a":"Because the agreement required the sum to be used to buy commodities and services for expanding the settlement and rehabilitation of Jewish refugees in Israel, with capital goods given particular weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1966 the deliveries had included 60 ships and other equipment, and a large share had paid for oil shipped to Israel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Adenauer press on despite opposition?","a":"Because he held to the agreement for both practical political and moral reasons, even though the coalition government was severely strained by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His commitment also stood against the background of East Germany's refusal to offer any restitution at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did many Israelis oppose taking German money?","a":"Because the opposition in Israel at first vehemently rejected accepting 'blood money' from Germany so soon after the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Prime Minister Ben-Gurion nevertheless backed the agreement, which was economically indispensable for a young state absorbing so many refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Reparations Agreement between Israel and the Federal Republic of Germany"},
    "ww2-941": {"questions":["In 1956 survivors' associations formed Nihon Hidankyo under the motto 'No more <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>', and in 2024 the movement received the Nobel Peace Prize for its witness testimony.","Japanese law counts among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> those who entered a zone about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) from the hypocentre within 2 weeks, and even children then in an exposed mother's womb."],"abstract":"<b>Hibakusha</b> is the Japanese name for the survivors of the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two bombs killed an estimated 120,000 inhabitants of the cities, and a comparable number died of burns and radiation injuries in the months and years that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fate of those who survived was long concealed and ignored, and the Japanese government took its first official relief measures only in 1957, 12 years after the bombings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The law grants a health handbook to people directly exposed in or near either city, to those who entered a zone about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) from the hypocentre within 2 weeks, to others exposed to radiation, and to those then in the womb of such a mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Since April 2022 Hiroshima has also issued handbooks to people recognised as exposed to the radioactive 'black rain' in the same circumstances as the plaintiffs who won a lawsuit for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The number of recognised survivors rose from about 200,000 in 1957 to a peak of about 372,000 in 1980, and had fallen to about 320,000 by 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Survivors have been studied since the Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission began work in 1947, above all for late effects such as excess cancers that became evident only years after exposure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Studies of the children of survivors since the late 1940s have so far found no evidence of clinical or subclinical effects inherited from their parents' exposure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1956 local survivors' associations, together with victims of nuclear tests in the Pacific, formed Nihon Hidankyo, whose motto became 'No more Hibakusha'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its witness testimony helped build an international norm against using nuclear weapons, often called the nuclear taboo, and in 2024 it received the Nobel Peace Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Norwegian Nobel Committee, “The Nobel Peace Prize 2024,” press release, Oslo, 11 October 2024, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/2024/press-release/. [Open access]","Nobel Prize Outreach, “Nihon Hidankyo – Facts – 2024,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/2024/nihon-hidankyo/facts/. [Open access]","Itsuzo Shigematsu, “Atomic Bomb Survivors Relief Law,” in <i>Effects of Ionizing Radiation: Atomic Bomb Survivors and Their Children (1945–1995)</i>, ed. Leif E. Peterson and Seymour Abrahamson (Washington, DC: Joseph Henry Press, 1998), https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/5805/chapter/24. [Open access]","City of Hiroshima, “Application for Atomic Bomb Survivor’s Handbook from Outside of Japan,” updated 16 February 2025, https://www.city.hiroshima.lg.jp/site/english/3342.html. [Open access]","Radiation Effects Research Foundation, “Late Effects on Survivors,” https://www.rerf.or.jp/en/programs/roadmap_e/health_effects-en/late-en/. [Open access]","Radiation Effects Research Foundation, “Genetic Effects of Radiation in the Offspring of Atomic-Bomb Survivors,” https://www.rerf.or.jp/en/programs/roadmap_e/health_effects-en/geneefx-en/. [Open access]","Frank W. Putnam, “The Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission in Retrospect,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 10 (1998), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC33857/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did state medical care for the survivors begin only in 1957?","a":"Because the fate of the survivors was long concealed and ignored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government did not even know how many survivors there were until the first postwar national census in 1950, and the Atomic Bomb Survivors Medical Care Law followed in March 1957, with examinations and treatment beginning that April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the law include people who were not in the city at the moment of the explosion?","a":"Because the law covers more than the blast itself: it includes those who entered the zone near the hypocentre within 2 weeks and others exposed to radiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hiroshima's criteria name, for example, people who took part in relief work for survivors or handled the bodies of the dead up to 20 August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Nihon Hidankyo receive the Nobel Peace Prize?","a":"Because, the Norwegian Nobel Committee said, the survivors' personal testimony helped create a powerful international norm stigmatising the use of nuclear weapons as morally unacceptable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organisation has provided thousands of witness accounts and sent annual delegations to the United Nations and to peace conferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Hibakusha"},
    "ww2-942": {"questions":["Survivors in Hiroshima often resented that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> examined them in its laboratory of Quonset huts on Hijiyama Hill without offering the treatment they hoped for.","Registering babies born to atomic bomb survivors from March 1948, a genetic study run by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> eventually followed some 70,000 children in the two bombed cities."],"abstract":"The <b>Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission</b>, known as the ABCC, was the American research body that studied the survivors of Hiroshima and Nagasaki and their children from 1947 to 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a directive signed by President Truman on 26 November 1946, which asked the National Research Council of the National Academy of Sciences to develop the programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Funding came from the newly founded Atomic Energy Commission, and the work was a joint enterprise of the United States and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first permanent laboratory, a group of Quonset huts on Hijiyama Hill overlooking Hiroshima, was built in 1950, by which time the staff exceeded 1,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1975 the commission was dissolved and replaced by the Radiation Effects Research Foundation, which continued the same studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Acute radiation syndrome struck people who had received high doses, with vomiting within hours and then diarrhoea, falling blood counts, bleeding and hair loss over the following days and weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At high doses death could follow within 10 to 20 days from damage to the intestines, or within 1 or 2 months, mostly from failure of the bone marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first delayed effect to appear was leukaemia, an excess of which Japanese physicians noticed among survivors in the late 1940s and which ABCC surveys then followed through the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A genetic study registered babies born to survivors from March 1948, recording pregnancy outcomes, sex ratios and later health, and grew to some 70,000 children in the two cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many survivors complained that the commission only examined them and gave no treatment, a charge one former staff member called not always true while accepting that those examined hoped to be treated as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Frank W. Putnam, “The Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission in Retrospect,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 10 (1998), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC33857/. [Open access]","Radiation Effects Research Foundation, “Acute Radiation Syndrome,” https://www.rerf.or.jp/en/programs/roadmap_e/health_effects-en/early-en/early_01_en/. [Open access]","Dale L. Preston, “A Historical Review of Leukemia Risks in Atomic Bomb Survivors,” in <i>Effects of Ionizing Radiation: Atomic Bomb Survivors and Their Children (1945–1995)</i>, ed. Leif E. Peterson and Seymour Abrahamson (Washington, DC: Joseph Henry Press, 1998), https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/5805/chapter/9. [Open access]","James V. Neel, “Studies on the Genetic Effects of the Atomic Bombs: Past, Present, and Future,” in <i>Effects of Ionizing Radiation: Atomic Bomb Survivors and Their Children (1945–1995)</i>, ed. Leif E. Peterson and Seymour Abrahamson (Washington, DC: Joseph Henry Press, 1998), https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/5805/chapter/14. [Open access]","Radiation Effects Research Foundation, “History Forum 4: Interview with a Former ABCC Staff Member,” <i>RERF Update</i>, https://www.rerf.or.jp/uploads/2017/09/historyforum04e.pdf. [Open access]","Itsuzo Shigematsu, “Atomic Bomb Survivors Relief Law,” in <i>Effects of Ionizing Radiation: Atomic Bomb Survivors and Their Children (1945–1995)</i>, ed. Leif E. Peterson and Seymour Abrahamson (Washington, DC: Joseph Henry Press, 1998), https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/5805/chapter/24. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did survivors resent the commission?","a":"Because people said the ABCC only studied survivors and provided no treatment, and a former staff member accepted that those who went through examinations hoped to be treated as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> State medical care for survivors began only under the medical care law of 1957, 12 years after the bombings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did doctors pay such attention to hair loss?","a":"Because severe hair loss was one of the principal signs of acute radiation syndrome, and its frequency rises sharply with dose above about 1 gray.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Survivors' reports of it were later used to test estimates of the doses they had received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was leukaemia the first long-term effect to be noticed?","a":"Because the excess of leukaemia appeared within a few years of the bombings and seemed to peak between 5 and 10 years after exposure, earliest of all among those exposed as children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japanese physicians in Hiroshima and Nagasaki raised the alarm in the late 1940s, and the documents founding the commission already mentioned the need to look for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Atomic Bomb Casualty Commission"},
    "ww2-943": {"questions":["Promised at Yalta in return for southern Sakhalin and the Kurile Islands, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began hours after Molotov told Japan's ambassador that Moscow would be at war from 9 August.","Soviet figures for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> count about 84,000 Japanese killed and 594,000 taken prisoner, many of whom were held on Soviet territory for years after the war."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet invasion of Manchuria</b> was the offensive that the Red Army launched against Japan's Kwantung Army in north-east China shortly after midnight on 9 August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Yalta Conference in February 1945 Stalin had agreed to enter the war against Japan 2 or 3 months after Germany's surrender, in return for southern Sakhalin, the Kurile Islands and rights in Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviet Union was still bound by the neutrality pact it had signed with Japan in April 1941, but it had denounced the pact on 5 April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 8 August Molotov told the Japanese ambassador that Japan's rejection of the Allied demand for surrender had removed any basis for Soviet mediation, and that the two countries would be at war from the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 1.5 million Soviet troops then advanced along several axes on a front of more than 4,400 kilometres (2,700 miles), from the deserts of Inner Mongolia to the Sea of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Japanese sources put the Japanese troops in Manchuria itself at about 713,700 men, and the forces facing the Soviets also included Manchukuoan and Inner Mongolian troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet columns broke through quickly, and organised Japanese resistance had largely ceased by 18 August, when Japanese forces began laying down their arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet figures give Japanese losses of about 84,000 killed and 594,000 taken prisoner, against about 8,000 Soviet dead and 24,000 wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the roughly 900,000 Japanese servicemen estimated to have surrendered to Soviet forces, only 436,000 had been repatriated by July 1948, and Soviet authorities gave no information about the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American survey of 1946 nevertheless judged that Japan would have surrendered before the end of that year even if the Soviet Union had not entered the war and no atomic bomb had been dropped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["David M. Glantz, <i>August Storm: The Soviet 1945 Strategic Offensive in Manchuria</i>, Leavenworth Papers 7 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1983), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA144378. [Open access]","“Soviet Declaration of War on Japan,” 8 August 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s4.asp. [Open access]","“Yalta (Crimea) Conference: Agreement Regarding Japan,” 11 February 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","“Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Pact,” 13 April 1941, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s1.asp. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union attack only in August 1945?","a":"Because at Yalta Stalin had agreed to enter the war 2 or 3 months after Germany surrendered, on conditions that included southern Sakhalin, the Kurile Islands and rights in Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soviet leaders also wished to cement their country's postwar position in the Far East, and planning for the final campaign followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why had Japan hoped for help from Moscow instead?","a":"Because the pact of 1941 bound each state to stay neutral if the other was attacked, and Japan had scrupulously observed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tokyo had even asked the Soviet Union to mediate, a proposal that Molotov's declaration of war said had lost all basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many prisoners take years to return home?","a":"Because Soviet authorities gave no information about the Japanese troops they held, and of some 900,000 servicemen who surrendered to them only 436,000 had come home by July 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Kurile Islands, for example, disarmed Japanese troops were formed into labour battalions and moved to Siberia or northern Sakhalin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Soviet invasion of Manchuria"},
    "ww2-944": {"questions":["Stalin's request that led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was granted by Truman on 17 August 1945, though he refused Stalin northern Hokkaido and asked for American air base rights in the chain.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, disarmed Japanese garrisons from Shumushu to Etorofu were formed into labour battalions and shipped to Siberia or to northern Sakhalin."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet invasion of the Kuril Islands</b> was the occupation of the island chain between Kamchatka and Hokkaido by Soviet forces in the last weeks of August 1945, after Japan had announced its surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Yalta in February 1945 the Allied leaders had agreed that the Kurile Islands would be handed over to the Soviet Union once it entered the war against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan had occupied the Kuriles between 1875 and 1880, and in 1945 it defended them with divisions and brigades of its Fifth Area Army in northern, central and southern sectors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet planners had counted the Kuriles, with southern Sakhalin, among the prizes of their final campaign against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Truman sent Stalin the text of General Order No. 1, Stalin asked on 16 August that all the Kuril Islands be added to the Soviet surrender zone, citing the Yalta decision, together with the northern half of Hokkaido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Truman agreed about the Kuriles on 17 August, but he refused Hokkaido and asked for American air base rights on one of the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stalin answered on 22 August that such demands were usually made of a conquered state, and that neither he nor his colleagues understood what had prompted this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The only fighting came in the north, where Soviet troops landed on Shumushu Island under cover of a bombardment, and a ceasefire was agreed on board a Soviet warship on 23 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soviet forces then landed on Matsuwa, Etorofu and Uruppu between 25 and 31 August, and the disarmed garrisons were formed into labour battalions and moved to Siberia or northern Sakhalin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the peace treaty signed at San Francisco in 1951 Japan renounced all claim to the Kurile Islands, but the Soviet Union refused to sign that treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","“Yalta (Crimea) Conference: Agreement Regarding Japan,” 11 February 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin to Harry S. Truman, 16 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 450, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d450. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman to Joseph Stalin, 17 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 452, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d452. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin to Harry S. Truman, 22 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 470, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d470. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Japan, Signed at San Francisco, on 8 September 1951,” <i>United Nations Treaty Series</i> 136, no. 1832 (1952), https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%20136/volume-136-I-1832-English.pdf. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>August Storm: The Soviet 1945 Strategic Offensive in Manchuria</i>, Leavenworth Papers 7 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1983), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA144378. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/japan-reconstruction. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Stalin claim the islands?","a":"Because the agreement reached at Yalta stated that the Kurile Islands would be handed over to the Soviet Union in return for its entry into the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his message of 16 August Stalin asked for all of them on exactly that ground, as islands which had to come into Soviet possession under the decisions of the three powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin also ask for half of Hokkaido?","a":"Because, he wrote, Russian public opinion would be seriously offended if Soviet troops had no occupation zone in Japan proper, since Japanese troops had occupied the whole Soviet Far East in 1919 to 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Truman replied that Japanese forces on all the home islands, Hokkaido included, would surrender to General MacArthur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the treaty of 1951 not settle the question?","a":"Because although Japan renounced all right, title and claim to the Kurile Islands, the treaty did not say to whom they passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Soviet Union, together with Poland and Czechoslovakia, refused to sign it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Soviet invasion of the Kuril Islands"},
    "ww2-945": {"questions":["On 5 April 1945 Moscow told Japan's ambassador it meant to abrogate the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it had lost its significance, though the treaty was not due to expire until April 1946.","Still trusting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1941, Japan's leaders asked Moscow to mediate an end to the war in the summer of 1945, only for the Soviet Union to declare war on 8 August."],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet–Japanese Neutrality Pact</b> was a treaty signed in Moscow on 13 April 1941 by Vyacheslav Molotov for the Soviet Union and Yōsuke Matsuoka for Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each side undertook to respect the other's territorial integrity and to remain neutral if the other became the object of hostilities by one or more third powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pact was valid for 5 years and would be prolonged automatically unless one party denounced it a year before it expired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave the Soviet Union a measure of security in the east, and Japan observed it scrupulously, which allowed Soviet troops to be moved west against the German attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 5 April 1945 Moscow told the Japanese ambassador that it intended to abrogate the pact because it had lost its significance, although the treaty was not due to expire until April 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Japan's leaders still placed their hopes in Moscow, and from May 1945 they sought its intercession as a mediator to end the war, but the talks made no progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 8 August Molotov told the ambassador that Japan's rejection of the Allied demand for surrender had removed any basis for mediation, and the Soviet Union entered the war the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American survey of 1946, drawing on the testimony of surviving Japanese leaders, concluded that Japan would have surrendered before the end of 1945 even without the atomic bombs or the Soviet entry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Scholars still debate how far the bombs were needed to force the surrender, and one influential argument of 1965 held that they were used mainly to strengthen the American hand against the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Others reply that Truman believed the bomb necessary to make military leaders who were determined to fight to the death accept unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["“Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Pact,” 13 April 1941, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s1.asp. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, part 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","“Soviet Declaration of War on Japan,” 8 August 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/s4.asp. [Open access]","David M. Glantz, <i>August Storm: The Soviet 1945 Strategic Offensive in Manchuria</i>, Leavenworth Papers 7 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: Combat Studies Institute, 1983), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA144378. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1946), https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Atomic Diplomacy,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/atomic. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan keep looking to Moscow after the pact was renounced?","a":"Because the treaty still had a year to run and Japan had kept to it scrupulously, even letting Soviet troops move west against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From May 1945 Tokyo therefore tried to use the Soviet Union as a mediator to end the war, though the talks made no progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Molotov say mediation had lost its basis?","a":"Because, his declaration ran, Japan had rejected the demand of the United States, Britain and China of 26 July for unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Soviet Government said it had accepted the Allies' proposal to join the war in order to shorten it and reduce the number of victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the American survey of 1946 matter to the debate?","a":"Because it concluded, from the testimony of surviving Japanese leaders, that Japan would have surrendered before the end of 1945 even if no bomb had been dropped and the Soviet Union had not entered the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Scholars still disagree about how far the bombs were needed to end the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Soviet–Japanese Neutrality Pact"},
    "ww2-946": {"questions":["Five atomic bomb survivors who sued the Japanese state in 1955 brought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, arguing that by waiving their claims against the United States in 1951 Japan owed them compensation.","Ending with the judges' regret at 'the political poverty', the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told the Diet and the Cabinet that relief for the victims of the bombs was their duty rather than the court's."],"abstract":"The <b>Shimoda case</b> was a lawsuit brought against the Japanese state by five survivors of the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, which ended in a judgment of the Tokyo District Court on 7 December 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plaintiffs were residents of the two cities who had lost members of their families or been injured themselves, and they began their action in 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They argued that the bombings had been illegal under international law, and that Japan, by waiving its citizens' claims against the United States in the peace treaty of 1951, had made itself liable to pay them damages instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court held that the bombing of the two cities was an illegal act of hostility, because it amounted to the indiscriminate aerial bombardment of undefended cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also judged that the pain caused by the bomb was severer than that of poisons and poisonous gases, and so contrary to the principle that unnecessary pain must not be given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Yet the court dismissed the claim, ruling that individuals had no right to damages under international law unless a treaty specifically gave them one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It added that the state ought to take sufficient relief measures for the victims, but that this was a duty of the Diet and the Cabinet rather than of the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 'We cannot see this suit without regretting the political poverty,' the judges wrote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Japanese lawyers' association pointed out in 2013 that the court's reasoning had the same framework as a later advisory opinion of the International Court of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That opinion, requested by the United Nations General Assembly and delivered on 8 July 1996, found that the threat or use of nuclear weapons would generally be contrary to the rules of international law applicable in armed conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["International Crimes Database, “Shimoda et al. v. the State,” T.M.C. Asser Instituut, https://www.internationalcrimesdatabase.org/Case/53/Shimoda-et-al/. [Open access]","Tokyo District Court, <i>Shimoda et al. v. The State</i>, judgment of 7 December 1963, trans. in <i>Japanese Annual of International Law</i> 8 (1964), reproduced by Trident Ploughshares, https://tridentploughshares.org/?p=2403. [Open access]","Richard A. Falk, “The Shimoda Case: A Legal Appraisal of the Atomic Attacks upon Hiroshima and Nagasaki,” <i>American Journal of International Law</i> 59, no. 4 (1965): 759–93, https://doi.org/10.2307/2197093. [Paywalled]","Japan Association of Lawyers Against Nuclear Arms, “Statement on the Humanitarian Impact of Nuclear Weapons Use and the Illegality of Atomic Bombing Today,” 8 December 2013, https://www.hankaku-j.org/data/jalana/131208_english.html. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Legality of the Threat or Use of Nuclear Weapons,” advisory opinion of 8 July 1996, case 95, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/95. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the survivors sue Japan rather than the United States?","a":"Because in Article 19a of the peace treaty of 1951 Japan had waived its nationals' claims against the United States and its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plaintiffs argued that this waiver, by taking away their claims, obliged the Japanese state to pay them damages itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the court find the bombings illegal?","a":"Because the power of the bombs caused casualties without distinguishing combatants from non-combatants, so their use amounted to an indiscriminate bombardment of undefended cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court also held that the pain the bomb caused was severer than that of poison gas, which broke the principle that unnecessary pain must not be given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the survivors lose all the same?","a":"Because the court ruled that individuals had no rights under international law unless a treaty specifically provided them, so they could not claim damages under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relief for the victims, it said, was a duty for the legislature and the executive rather than for the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ryuichi Shimoda et al. v. The State"},
    "ww2-947": {"questions":["Citing 'a new and most cruel bomb', Hirohito asked his subjects in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to end the war by 'enduring the unendurable and suffering what is insufferable'.","Rebel officers who searched the imperial palace on the night of 14 August 1945 were hunting for the recordings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the government had announced for noon."],"abstract":"The <b>Jewel Voice Broadcast</b> was the radio address of 15 August 1945 in which Emperor Hirohito told the Japanese people that Japan would accept the Allied terms, the first time an emperor had ever addressed the nation by radio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its text was an imperial rescript issued on 14 August, which the emperor recorded that same day for broadcast at noon on the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tokyo radio station announced on the evening of the 14th that there would be an important broadcast at noon, and many people expected the emperor to urge even greater efforts for victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Instead he said that he had ordered his government to tell the United States, Britain, China and the Soviet Union that his empire accepted their joint declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rescript never used the word 'surrender', and the emperor delivered it in formal, florid Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It admitted that the war situation had developed 'not necessarily to Japan's advantage', and it gave as one reason that the enemy had begun to employ 'a new and most cruel bomb'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To continue fighting, it declared, would lead not only to the collapse and obliteration of the Japanese nation but to the total extinction of human civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emperor asked his people to pave the way for peace for all generations to come by 'enduring the unendurable and suffering what is insufferable'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also said that the structure of the imperial state had been safeguarded, the national polity that Japan had tried to preserve when it accepted the Potsdam Declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> During the night before the broadcast, rebel officers searched the palace in vain for the recordings in an attempt to stop it from going on the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“Imperial Rescript, August 14, 1945,” <i>Nippon Times</i>, 15 August 1945, in “The World and Japan” Database, National Graduate Institute for Policy Studies and University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/docs/19450814.O1E.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Imperial Rescript of the Termination of the War, August 14, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/017shoshi.html. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “The Jewel Voice Broadcast,” National Museum of Nuclear Science and History, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/?p=16362. [Open access]","“Hirohito’s ‘Jewel Voice Broadcast’,” <i>Air &amp; Space Forces Magazine</i>, August 2012, https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/0812keeperfile/. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, part 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Popular Sovereignty and the Emperor System,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/ronten/01ronten.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were so many listeners taken by surprise?","a":"Because most Japanese had no idea what to expect, and the emperor had never before used the microphone to speak to his people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With the Soviet Union now in the war, many assumed he would call on them to make even greater efforts for victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rescript mention the atomic bomb?","a":"Because it gave the bomb as a reason to stop: its power to do damage was incalculable, it was taking many innocent lives, and fighting on would end in the obliteration of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emperor referred to 'a new and most cruel bomb' as a reason for surrender, without ever using that word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the emperor say the imperial state had been safeguarded?","a":"Because Japan had offered to accept the Potsdam Declaration only on the understanding that it did not prejudice the prerogatives of the emperor, so as to preserve the national polity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the rescript he stated that this structure of the imperial state had been maintained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Hirohito surrender broadcast"},
    "ww2-948": {"questions":["Searching the Imperial Household Ministry for the emperor's recorded rescript, the officers behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> failed to stop it being broadcast at noon on 15 August 1945.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collapsed on the morning of 15 August 1945, its leaders Hatanaka and Shiizaki killed themselves in the palace plaza, and the government banned all news of it."],"abstract":"The <b>Kyūjō incident</b> was the failed attempt by a group of army officers, on the night of 14 to 15 August 1945, to seize the imperial palace in Tokyo and stop Japan's surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its leaders were Major Kenji Hatanaka and Lieutenant Colonel Jirō Shiizaki, who hoped to touch off a general army uprising against the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earlier, officers who still wished to fight on had proposed to the army minister, General Anami, a coup by the army leaders to take over the government, but the chief of the army general staff, Umezu, refused to approve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The emperor had meanwhile recorded the rescript ending the war, which was to be broadcast to the nation at noon on 15 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In it he announced that Japan accepted the provisions of the joint declaration of the Allied powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When Lieutenant General Takeshi Mori, commander of the 1st Imperial Guards Division, refused to join the plot, Hatanaka shot him dead, and the plotters issued a forged division order under Mori's seal to surround the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The guards sealed the palace grounds, and rebels ransacked the Imperial Household Ministry for the recordings, but officials refused to say where they were and the Lord Privy Seal, Kido, escaped by hiding in a shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> General Tanaka of the Eastern District Army took direct command of the guards division, told its regiments to disregard the false order and by eight in the morning had restored order in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hatanaka and Shiizaki killed themselves in the palace plaza that afternoon, and the government banned all news of the uprising from publication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> War Minister Anami, who had not joined the plot, had already killed himself at his official residence early that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, part 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","“Hirohito’s ‘Jewel Voice Broadcast’,” <i>Air &amp; Space Forces Magazine</i>, August 2012, https://www.airandspaceforces.com/article/0812keeperfile/. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Imperial Rescript of the Termination of the War, August 14, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/017shoshi.html. [Open access]","“Imperial Rescript, August 14, 1945,” <i>Nippon Times</i>, 15 August 1945, in “The World and Japan” Database, National Graduate Institute for Policy Studies and University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/docs/19450814.O1E.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rebels hunt for the recordings?","a":"Because the emperor's rescript was due to be broadcast at noon on 15 August, and the conspirators had to seize the recordings to stop it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A popular account says that loyal officials smuggled the record to safety in a laundry basket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the plot collapse so quickly?","a":"Because the army leadership refused to support it: Umezu had already rejected a coup, and the Eastern District Army command was entirely unwilling to back the uprising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its commander took control of the guards division and ordered every regiment to ignore the forged order, and order was restored by eight in the morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Japanese public learn so little about it at the time?","a":"Because the government rigidly banned all news of the uprising from publication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A War Ministry communiqué announced General Anami's suicide the next day but said nothing about the circumstances surrounding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kyūjō incident"},
    "ww2-949": {"questions":["Handed to General Kawabe's delegation in Manila on 20 August 1945, the text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> placed the emperor's authority to rule under the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers.","Besides MacArthur as Supreme Commander, nine Allied nations signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Tokyo Bay, from the United States and China to the Netherlands and New Zealand."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese Instrument of Surrender</b> was the document signed in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945 that formally completed the surrender of Japan to the Allied powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it the signatories, acting on behalf of the emperor, the government and Imperial General Headquarters, accepted the Potsdam Declaration of 26 July 1945, to which the Soviet Union had later adhered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They proclaimed the unconditional surrender of Imperial General Headquarters and of all Japanese armed forces and all forces under Japanese control, wherever they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The document ordered the immediate release of all Allied prisoners of war and civilian internees, and made the authority of the emperor and the government to rule subject to the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last clause repeated the Allied reply to Japan's offer in August to accept the Potsdam terms only if the emperor's prerogatives were left untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The text had been handed over in Manila on 20 August to a Japanese delegation led by Lieutenant General Kawabe, together with General Order No. 1 and a proclamation for the emperor to issue after the signing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the deck of the battleship USS Missouri, Foreign Minister Mamoru Shigemitsu signed for the emperor and the government, and General Yoshijirō Umezu, chief of the army general staff, for Imperial General Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> General MacArthur then accepted the surrender as Supreme Commander, followed by representatives of the United States, China, Britain, the Soviet Union, Australia, Canada, France, the Netherlands and New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An imperial rescript of the same day ordered the signing, and a proclamation in the emperor's name commanded all his people to cease hostilities and lay down their arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Immediately afterwards MacArthur issued his first general order to the Japanese, which repeated the requirements outlined to the emissaries at Manila 2 weeks before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Instrument of Surrender,” 2 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j4.asp. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Imperial Rescript on the Signing of the Instrument of Surrender, September 2, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/021shoshi.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Popular Sovereignty and the Emperor System,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/ronten/01ronten.html. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","Battleship Missouri Memorial, “The Surrender Ceremony Aboard USS Missouri,” https://ussmissouri.org/learn-the-history/surrender. [Open access]","“Emperor Hirohito’s Receipt of the Surrender: Proclamation,” 2 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j5.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did two Japanese sign the document?","a":"Because the surrender was made on behalf of both the emperor and government and of Imperial General Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shigemitsu, the foreign minister, signed for the emperor and the government, and Umezu, chief of the army general staff, signed for the armed forces' headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Japanese envoys in Manila ask for more time?","a":"Because they needed 10 days to ensure control of the Japanese armed forces and avoid regrettable incidents when the occupation forces arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The request was granted, and the main landings were postponed from 25 to 28 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the document make the emperor subject to the Supreme Commander?","a":"Because Japan had offered to accept the Potsdam terms on the understanding that the emperor's prerogatives as a sovereign ruler would not be touched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Allies replied that his authority to rule would be subject to the Supreme Commander, and the instrument wrote that answer into its final clause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Japanese Instrument of Surrender"},
    "ww2-950": {"questions":["Flying Admiral Nimitz's pennant, the Iowa-class battleship <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chosen as the scene of Japan's formal surrender, and she is now preserved as a museum ship at Pearl Harbor.","President Truman told Americans that their thoughts were centred on the battleship <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that night, just as 4 years earlier the world's fears had centred on Pearl Harbor."],"abstract":"<b>USS Missouri</b> is the American battleship on whose deck Japan signed the Instrument of Surrender in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Iowa-class battleship built at the Brooklyn Navy Yard and launched in 1944, she was the youngest of the four ships of her class, after Iowa, New Jersey and Wisconsin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She served with the American fast carrier force in the last months of the war, and was slightly damaged when a swarm of kamikaze aircraft attacked it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Belonging to the Third Fleet and flying the pennant of Admiral Chester Nimitz, she was chosen as the scene of the formal surrender ceremony, set for nine in the morning on 2 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After serving in three wars she was decommissioned in 1992, and she has been a museum ship at Pearl Harbor since opening as a memorial in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The ceremony began at 0902 with a brief speech by General MacArthur calling for justice, tolerance and rebuilding, and it lasted only 23 minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreign Minister Mamoru Shigemitsu signed first for the emperor and the government, and General Yoshijirō Umezu, chief of the army general staff, for Imperial General Headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> MacArthur signed as Supreme Commander with six pens, two of which he gave to the former prisoners of war General Jonathan Wainwright and General Arthur Percival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Representatives of the United States, China, Britain, the Soviet Union, Australia, Canada, France, the Netherlands and New Zealand signed after him, and at 0925 MacArthur closed the proceedings and the war was officially over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speaking that evening, President Truman said the thoughts and hopes of all America were centred on the battleship Missouri, and recalled that 4 years earlier the world's fears had centred on Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Battleship Missouri Memorial, “The Surrender Ceremony Aboard USS Missouri,” https://ussmissouri.org/learn-the-history/surrender. [Open access]","Battleship Missouri Memorial, “USS Missouri (BB-63) and the Iowa Class Battleship,” https://ussmissouri.org/history/the-ship. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Radio Address to the American People after the Signing of the Terms of Unconditional Surrender by Japan,” 1 September 1945, Public Papers of the Presidents, Harry S. Truman Library and Museum, https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/122/radio-address-american-people-after-signing-terms-unconditional-surrender. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","“Instrument of Surrender,” 2 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j4.asp. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Imperial Rescript on the Signing of the Instrument of Surrender, September 2, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/021shoshi.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, part 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did MacArthur hand pens to Wainwright and Percival?","a":"Because both men had been prisoners of the Japanese: Wainwright had defended Corregidor until its fall in May 1942, and Percival had just been released from a prison camp in Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> MacArthur signed with six pens and gave two of them to these former prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Truman speak of Pearl Harbor on the night of the surrender?","a":"Because, he said, 4 years earlier the thoughts and fears of the whole civilised world had been centred on another piece of American soil, Pearl Harbor, and now they were centred on the Missouri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 'We shall not forget Pearl Harbor,' he told his listeners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Japanese delegates bring a document from the emperor?","a":"Because an imperial rescript of 2 September ordered the signing of the instrument on the emperor's behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A specially prepared copy, signed by the emperor and countersigned by the prime minister and the whole cabinet, was handed to the Supreme Commander aboard the Missouri before the signing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"USS Missouri (BB-63)"},
    "ww2-951": {"questions":["Stalin asked on 16 August 1945 that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be changed to give his forces all the Kuril Islands and the northern half of Hokkaido, and Truman granted him only the islands.","Drafted in haste in the War Department in Washington, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew the line between the Soviet and American surrender zones in Korea at the 38th parallel."],"abstract":"<b>General Order No. 1</b> was the order, approved by President Truman on 15 August 1945, that told Japanese commanders across Asia and the Pacific to which Allied commander each of their forces must surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was drafted in haste in the War Department, and its key first paragraph, naming the commands that would accept the surrenders, was written in a few minutes by an army planner, Colonel Bonesteel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese forces in China outside Manchuria, in Formosa and in French Indochina north of the 16th parallel were to surrender to Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those in Manchuria, in Korea north of the 38th parallel and in Karafuto were to surrender to the commander of the Soviet forces in the Far East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South-East Asia from Burma to the Netherlands Indies went to Mountbatten or the Australian commander, the Pacific islands to the United States Pacific Fleet, and Japan, southern Korea and the Philippines to MacArthur's army command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Truman sent the text to Stalin, who on 16 August asked that all the Kuril Islands and the northern half of Hokkaido be added to the Soviet zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Truman accepted the change for the Kuriles but refused Hokkaido, replying that the Japanese forces on all the home islands would surrender to MacArthur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> MacArthur ruled that surrenders in other theatres could be signed only after the main instrument had been signed in Tokyo, and Japanese forces overseas then surrendered to the commanders the order named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Japanese commanders in the Philippines surrendered to American officers at Baguio on 3 September, and those in southern Korea to the American commander at Seoul on 9 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Singapore on 12 September General Seishirō Itagaki signed the surrender of the Southern Regions forces to Mountbatten, in compliance with General Order No. 1 and the Tokyo instrument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","James F. Schnabel, <i>Policy and Direction: The First Year</i>, United States Army in the Korean War (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1972), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA353574. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin to Harry S. Truman, 16 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 450, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d450. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman to Joseph Stalin, 17 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 452, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d452. [Open access]","“Instrument of Surrender of Japanese Forces in the Southern Regions,” 12 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j3.asp. [Open access]","“Instrument of Surrender of Japanese Forces in the Philippine Islands,” 3 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j6.asp. [Open access]","“Formal Surrender by the Senior Japanese Ground, Sea, Air and Auxiliary Forces Commands within Korea South of 38 North Latitude,” 9 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j1.asp. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, part 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Korea divided at the 38th parallel?","a":"Because the planner wanted a surrender line as far north as he thought the Soviets would accept, knowing their troops could reach the south of Korea before any American force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The parallel cut the country roughly in half and placed Seoul and a nearby prisoner-of-war camp in the American zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Mountbatten's responsibilities suddenly grow so large?","a":"Because the order handed his South-East Asia Command a large part of the South-West Pacific, enlarging his area from about 2.6 million to 36 million square kilometres (1 million to 14 million square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It held more than 128 million people and nearly three-quarters of a million Japanese, of whom 630,000 were armed troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the overseas surrenders wait until September?","a":"Because MacArthur insisted that no surrender document in another theatre could be signed until the main instrument had been signed in Tokyo on 2 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After that, Japanese forces overseas surrendered formally to the Allied commanders designated for each area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"General Order No. 1"},
    "ww2-952": {"questions":["Land reform for tenant farmers, an attack on the zaibatsu and a constitution renouncing war all came in the first phase of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, before the 'reverse course' of 1947 and 1948.","The Korean War made Japan the chief supply depot for United Nations forces in the last years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended after 49 nations signed a peace treaty at San Francisco."],"abstract":"The <b>Occupation of Japan</b> was the period from the surrender of 1945 to 1952 during which Allied forces under General Douglas MacArthur controlled the defeated country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Although Britain, the Soviet Union and China had an advisory role, MacArthur as Supreme Commander had the final authority, and in practice the occupation was essentially an American operation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Truman had told Stalin in August 1945 that MacArthur would use Allied token forces, Soviet ones included, only so far as he judged necessary to carry out the surrender terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The first occupation troops landed near Tokyo at the end of August, after Japanese envoys in Manila had asked for 10 days to prepare for their arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> MacArthur's command also reached into Korea, where his proclamation of 7 September 1945 established American military control south of the 38th parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> American policy chose indirect control, under which the Supreme Commander exercised his authority through the machinery of the Japanese government, including the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first phase, roughly until 1947, punished and reformed Japan: war crimes trials were held, the army was dismantled and former officers were barred from political leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land reform aimed to help tenant farmers, MacArthur tried to break up the great business conglomerates called zaibatsu, and a new constitution of 1947 made the emperor a figurehead and renounced war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From late 1947 fears of economic crisis and communism brought a 'reverse course' that put economic recovery first, and the Korean War of 1950 made Japan the main supply depot for United Nations forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In all, 49 nations signed the peace treaty at San Francisco in September 1951, which recognised the full sovereignty of the Japanese people and came into force on 28 April 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/japan-reconstruction. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “U.S. Initial Post-Surrender Policy for Japan,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/022shoshi.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Basic Initial Post-Surrender Directive to Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, November 1, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/036shoshi.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Japan, Signed at San Francisco, on 8 September 1951,” <i>United Nations Treaty Series</i> 136, no. 1832 (1952), https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%20136/volume-136-I-1832-English.pdf. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","James F. Schnabel, <i>Policy and Direction: The First Year</i>, United States Army in the Korean War (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1972), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA353574. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman to Joseph Stalin, 17 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 452, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d452. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Americans rule through the Japanese government?","a":"Because American policy, revised after the Potsdam Declaration, adopted indirect control, in which the Supreme Commander would exercise his authority through the Japanese government and its agencies, including the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The basic directive of November 1945 repeated that MacArthur would act through the emperor or the government, keeping direct action as a last resort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did occupation policy change around 1948?","a":"Because an economic crisis in Japan and fears about the spread of communism led American officials to reconsider their policies, a shift sometimes called the 'reverse course'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reviving the Japanese economy took centre stage, since a weak economy was thought likely to strengthen the domestic communist movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union refuse to sign the peace treaty?","a":"Because, together with Poland and Czechoslovakia, it objected to the promise forced on Japan to support the Republic of China and not to deal with the People's Republic of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The treaty nonetheless came into force for the states that ratified it on 28 April 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Occupation of Japan"},
    "ww2-953": {"questions":["An eleven-nation Far Eastern Commission in Washington and a four-member Allied Council in Tokyo were meant to guide the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but in practice MacArthur had the final word.","Ten special staff sections for economic, scientific and other civil matters were set up on 2 October 1945 in the General Headquarters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Tokyo."],"abstract":"The <b>Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers</b>, known as SCAP, was the title of General Douglas MacArthur as head of the occupation of Japan, and the name was also used for the headquarters he led.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> MacArthur was designated Supreme Commander on 14 August 1945 under an agreement among the United States, Britain, China and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His instructions told him that from the moment of surrender the authority of the emperor and the Japanese government to rule the state would be subject to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was charged with making the overall arrangements for the surrender, and he accepted it on behalf of the Allies aboard USS Missouri on 2 September 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A message of 6 September, approved by Truman, gave him supreme authority and stated that relations with Japan rested on unconditional surrender rather than on any contract.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> General Headquarters, SCAP, was set up on 2 October 1945 with general and special staff sections, and ten special sections were created that day to handle civil affairs such as the economy and science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In principle policy was Allied: an eleven-nation Far Eastern Commission in Washington set its principles, and an Allied Council for Japan met in Tokyo every 2 weeks to advise the Supreme Commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In practice MacArthur had the final authority to make all decisions, and Britain, the Soviet Union and China had only an advisory role.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The basic directive of November 1945 let him take any steps he thought proper, except that the future of the emperor system required the prior consent of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Truman had insisted from the start that Japanese forces on all the home islands would surrender to MacArthur, refusing Stalin's request for a Soviet zone in Hokkaido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Message to General of the Army Douglas MacArthur concerning the Authority of the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, September 6, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/023shoshi.html. [Open access]","“Instrument of Surrender,” 2 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j4.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/japan-reconstruction. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Basic Initial Post-Surrender Directive to Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, November 1, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/036shoshi.html. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman to Joseph Stalin, 17 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 452, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d452. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Supreme Commander's authority called supreme?","a":"Because his orders said that the authority of the emperor and government would be subject to him and that he would command all Allied forces enforcing the surrender in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The message of 6 September added that he might use force if necessary, since the basis of relations was unconditional surrender and not a contract.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Allied bodies have so little influence?","a":"Because the occupation was essentially an American operation, and the policies of the Far Eastern Commission reached MacArthur only through the American Joint Chiefs of Staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Allied Council in Tokyo could consult and advise, but MacArthur had the final authority over all decisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did MacArthur need Washington's consent on the emperor?","a":"Because the basic directive of November 1945 gave him supreme authority in everything except the continued existence of the emperor system, which required the prior consent of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also made clear that he would exercise his powers through the emperor or the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers"},
    "ww2-954": {"questions":["At Hirohito's insistence the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began by quoting the Five Clauses of the Meiji Emperor's Charter Oath of 1868, and General MacArthur praised it on the day it appeared.","Because its Japanese text renounced the arcane word 'akitsumikami', readers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Japanese were left less sure than readers of the English what the emperor had given up."],"abstract":"The <b>Humanity Declaration</b> is the name commonly given to the imperial rescript of 1 January 1946 in which Emperor Hirohito declared that he was not a living god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened by quoting the Five Clauses of the Charter Oath proclaimed by the Meiji Emperor in 1868, which were included at Hirohito's own insistence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The rescript then called for old abuses to be discarded and the popular will to be given full play, so that a new and peaceful Japan could be built out of the devastation of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In its sixth paragraph it stated that the ties between the emperor and his people did not depend on legends and myths, nor on the false conception that the emperor was divine and the Japanese superior to other races and fated to rule the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Japanese text used the arcane word 'akitsumikami' for what the official English translation called ‘divinity’, so the Japanese version left unclear exactly what the emperor was renouncing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> 2 weeks earlier the occupation authorities had issued the Shinto Directive, ordering Shinto rites, myths and symbols out of all institutions of the state, and their reports counted the renunciation of divinity beside it among the reforms of the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the day the rescript appeared, General MacArthur praised the emperor as a leader bringing democracy to the people of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later that month MacArthur told Washington he had found no evidence that the emperor had committed war crimes, and warned that charging him would throw Japan into confusion and require a longer and larger occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American diplomat in Tokyo reported that the rescript was favourably received by most Allied officials and most Japanese, and set it alongside the Meiji Charter Oath as a foundation of Japanese policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The constitution adopted the following year went further and reduced the emperor to a figurehead without political control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, “Emperor, Imperial Rescript Denying His Divinity (Professing His Humanity),” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/03/056shoshi.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Telegram, MacArthur to Eisenhower, concerning Exemption of the Emperor from War Criminals, January 25, 1946,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/03/064shoshi.html. [Open access]","Tomohiro Kanke, “Reshaping Emperor Hirohito’s Persona: A Study of Fragmented Arguments in Multiple Texts,” ISSA Proceedings 2006, <i>Rozenberg Quarterly</i>, https://rozenbergquarterly.com/?p=17453. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/japan-reconstruction. [Open access]","Ōhara Yasuo, “Shintō Shirei (The Shinto Directive),” in <i>Encyclopedia of Shinto</i>, Kokugakuin University Digital Museum, https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/?id=8852. [Open access]","Max W. Bishop to the Secretary of State, 4 February 1946, with enclosure “Official Translation of Imperial Rescript, January 1, 1946,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 8, <i>The Far East</i>, doc. 138, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v08/d138. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the rescript begin with the Charter Oath of 1868?","a":"Because Hirohito insisted that the Five Clauses of his grandfather's oath be written into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prime Minister Shidehara's commentary presented the oath as the founding principle of Japanese democracy, and an American diplomat in Tokyo wrote that the rescript took its place beside the oath as a foundation of government policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did MacArthur want the emperor kept on the throne?","a":"Because he reported in January 1946 that he had found no evidence of the emperor committing war crimes, and believed that charging him would cause confusion and demand a longer occupation with more troops and officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had already praised the New Year rescript as a sign of the emperor leading his people towards democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it unclear how much the emperor actually renounced?","a":"Because the Japanese text used 'akitsumikami', an arcane prewar term, where the English said 'divinity', and gave no plain explanation of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A newspaper of the day described the prewar emperor only as a 'mysterious existence', which is hard to read as a clear denial of divinity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Humanity Declaration"},
    "ww2-955": {"questions":["Malaria and hunger killed more than 150 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held on Rempang and Galang islands in the Riau Archipelago while they waited for ships to take them home.","About 100,000 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were kept back as labour in Burma, Malaya, Siam and the Netherlands Indies, and the last of them did not reach Japan until late 1947."],"abstract":"<b>Japanese Surrendered Personnel</b>, or JSP, was the status given to the Japanese forces who capitulated in South-East Asia in 1945, in place of the status of prisoners of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classification was meant to leave the men's maintenance and discipline the responsibility of their own officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Japan surrendered there were over five million Japanese, Formosan and Korean servicemen and civilians outside Japan, and about 740,000 of them were in the enlarged South-East Asia Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Itagaki signed the surrender of the Southern Regions forces to Mountbatten at Singapore on 12 September 1945, one of the formal surrenders made overseas to designated Allied commanders after the ceremony in Tokyo Bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some Japanese troops were at first left armed in parts of Indochina, Siam, Java and Sumatra to help keep order, and the rest were concentrated near ports to await shipping home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Many were sent to Rempang and Galang in the Riau Archipelago, where hunger, malnutrition and malaria killed more than 150 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the American zone of responsibility repatriation went faster, and between August 1945 and July 1948 the occupation demobilised about 6.4 million Japanese servicemen in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lack of shipping threatened to keep the JSP in South-East Asia for 3 years, until the Americans agreed in April 1946 to lend Liberty ships to clear them by the end of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British nonetheless kept about 100,000 of them as labour in Burma, Malaya, Siam and the Netherlands Indies, and MacArthur's headquarters pressed in vain for their return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The last labourers went home in monthly lifts from June to December 1947, after some of the men had spent 2.5 years in South-East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","Eriko Ogihara-Schuck and Cecilia Gaspar, “The Long Surrender: The Experience of Japanese Soldiers Interned in Rempang after the War,” <i>BiblioAsia</i> 22, no. 3 (October–December 2026), National Library Board, Singapore, https://isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg/339/11d54ac8-9e5b-46d4-b71d-0c9f60c96b3b/BA-Oct-Dec2026-Rempang.pdf. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","“Instrument of Surrender of Japanese Forces in the Southern Regions,” 12 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j3.asp. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 2, part 2, <i>Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the men not called prisoners of war?","a":"Because the British wanted the forces that capitulated to remain the responsibility of their own officers for maintenance and discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Burma the Allied land command also said it could not take on the extra burden of feeding them, so they were to stay on their own supply system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it take so long to send them home?","a":"Because the Japanese merchant shipping recovered after the surrender was far too small, and only part of what South-East Asia Command received could make the ocean voyage to Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was reckoned that repatriation would take 5 years, and then three, until extra American Liberty ships were lent in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were some still in South-East Asia in 1947?","a":"Because the British Chiefs of Staff agreed to keep about 103,000 of them as labour in Burma, Malaya, Siam and the Netherlands Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> MacArthur's headquarters asked for their return by the end of 1946, but some 80,000 were still in Malaya and Burma when that year ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Japanese Surrendered Personnel"},
    "ww2-956": {"questions":["American truce teams under General Marshall persuaded the Chinese Communists to let Japanese in their areas join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran through a port held by the Nationalists.","A cholera outbreak in August 1946 held ships in quarantine during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the hardest part of bringing more than six million Japanese home from Asia after the surrender."],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese repatriation from Huludao</b> was the shipping home of Japanese soldiers and civilians stranded in Manchuria after the war, through the port of Huludao, then usually written Hulutao in English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It formed part of a wider movement in which more than six million Japanese were repatriated from overseas after the surrender, many of them returning without enough clothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Repatriation from Manchuria proved the most difficult of all, because the region was divided between Chinese Nationalist and Communist forces, and the Communists did little to cooperate until the autumn of 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American estimates in May 1946 put the Japanese in Manchuria at 600,000 prisoners of war and 700,000 civilians, with perhaps 200,000 more, mostly soldiers, removed to Soviet territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the Soviet Union restricting the use of Dairen, Huludao was the best available port in Manchuria, and all Japanese repatriates from the region were routed through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Sailings began on 7 May 1946, and after a halting start the rate was raised from 3,000 people a day to 7,500 a day by July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cholera outbreak in August held ships in quarantine, but by the end of September more than 10,000 people were leaving every day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American truce teams under General Marshall obtained a Communist agreement that Japanese in Communist-held areas would also be sent home through the Nationalist-held port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 10 September 1946 some 765,105 Japanese had been repatriated from Manchuria, and by the end of October the region was cleared except for about 68,000 people in Communist areas or kept as technicians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smaller sailings continued, and an American intelligence report considered the repatriation from Nationalist-held Manchuria complete after the last groups left Mukden for Huludao in October 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]","Robert L. Smyth to the Secretary of State, 22 May 1946, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 10, <i>The Far East: China</i>, doc. 615, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v10/d615. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman Library and Museum, “Port of Hulutao, Manchuria,” photograph, accession no. 2009-688, Edwin W. Pauley Papers, https://trumanlibrary.gov/photograph-records/2009-688. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Group, “Political Information: Repatriation of Japanese, Manchuria,” information report, November 1946, CIA-RDP82-00457R000100320007-2, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00457r000100320007-2. [Open access]","Central Intelligence Group, “Economic Information: Completion of Repatriation of Japanese from Nationalist-Held Manchuria,” information report, November 1947, CIA-RDP82-00457R001100090005-9, https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00457r001100090005-9. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the repatriation go through Huludao?","a":"Because the Soviet Union restricted the use of Dairen, which left Huludao as the best available port in Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was also under the control of the Nationalist forces, through whom the repatriation was organised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Manchuria the hardest place to clear?","a":"Because Nationalists and Communists were fighting a civil war there, and the Communists did little to help until the autumn of 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some Nationalist officials also feared that if they evacuated all the Japanese in their zone, the Communists would be left holding trained Japanese, military personnel among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did cholera slow the sailings?","a":"Because every ship arriving from a port with cholera was held in quarantine until public health officials cleared it, which made it hard to keep enough shipping in service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The threat eased only with the cold weather of the autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Japanese repatriation from Huludao"},
    "ww2-957": {"questions":["Japanese forces north of 38 degrees surrendered to the Soviets and those to the south to General Hodge at Seoul, beginning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that produced two rival states by 1948.","Promised freedom 'in due course' at Cairo in 1943, the former Japanese colony instead faced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as elections held only in the south in May 1948 created a separate republic."],"abstract":"The <b>division of Korea</b> was the splitting of the peninsula at the 38th parallel in 1945 into a Soviet zone in the north and an American zone in the south, which hardened into two rival states by 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korea had been a Japanese colony, and in their declaration at Cairo in 1943 the Allies had promised that in due course it would become free and independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In August 1945 an American planner chose the 38th parallel as the line between the Soviet and American surrender zones, because it cut Korea roughly in half and left Seoul in American hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Order No. 1 then directed Japanese forces in Korea north of 38 degrees to surrender to the Soviet commander, and those south of it to the American commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Stalin raised no objection to the line in his reply of 16 August, although he asked for changes elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The new boundary, about 300 kilometres (190 miles) across, cut more than 75 streams, 12 rivers and six north–south railway lines, with no regard for political or geographical divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American zone of about 96,000 square kilometres (37,000 square miles) held some 21 million people, and the Soviet zone of about 124,000 square kilometres (48,000 square miles) about 9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Seoul on 9 September 1945 the Japanese commanders and the governor-general surrendered southern Korea to General John R. Hodge, commander of the American forces there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Talks between the two occupiers stalled, and after elections held only in the south in May 1948 the Republic of Korea was proclaimed on 15 August, and a rival government formed in the north on 9 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two powers had meant the division to be temporary, for overseeing the removal of Japanese forces, and in the peace treaty of 1951 Japan recognised the independence of Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["James F. Schnabel, <i>Policy and Direction: The First Year</i>, United States Army in the Korean War (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1972), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA353574. [Open access]","Joseph Stalin to Harry S. Truman, 16 August 1945, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1945</i>, vol. 6, <i>The British Commonwealth, the Far East</i>, doc. 450, Office of the Historian, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v06/d450. [Open access]","“Formal Surrender by the Senior Japanese Ground, Sea, Air and Auxiliary Forces Commands within Korea South of 38 North Latitude,” 9 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j1.asp. [Open access]","“Certification of Receipt by the Acting Governor General of Korea,” 9 September 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/j2.asp. [Open access]","“Cairo Communiqué,” released 1 December 1943, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/cairo.asp. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Japan, Signed at San Francisco, on 8 September 1951,” <i>United Nations Treaty Series</i> 136, no. 1832 (1952), https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%20136/volume-136-I-1832-English.pdf. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Korean War and Japan’s Recovery,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the line drawn at the 38th parallel?","a":"Because the planner wanted a surrender line as far north as he thought the Soviets would accept, knowing their troops could reach southern Korea before any Americans arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parallel cut the country roughly through the middle and put Seoul and a nearby prisoner-of-war camp in the American zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union accept the American line?","a":"Because Stalin's reply to General Order No. 1 said nothing specifically about the 38th parallel and raised no objection to its substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His requests concerned the Kuril Islands and Hokkaido instead, and his message settled the surrender zones in Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a temporary line become a lasting border?","a":"Because the American and Soviet commission meant to plan a united government reached only stalemate, and the Soviet Union refused to take part in a United Nations commission on Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elections were then held only in the south, and each half formed its own government in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Division of Korea"},
    "ww2-958": {"questions":["Negotiated largely by John Foster Dulles, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was signed by 49 nations at a conference of 52, but rejected by the Soviet Union, Poland and Czechoslovakia.","On the day that Dean Acheson signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the United States, he and Prime Minister Yoshida Shigeru also signed a separate security treaty between their two countries."],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of San Francisco</b> was the peace treaty with Japan signed on 8 September 1951, which ended the state of war between Japan and most of the Allied powers and brought the American occupation to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Negotiated mainly by John Foster Dulles in 1950 and 1951, it was signed by 49 of the 52 nations at the conference, while the Soviet Union, Poland and Czechoslovakia refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first article declared the state of war terminated and recognised the full sovereignty of the Japanese people over Japan and its territorial waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came into force on 28 April 1952 for the first group of states that had ratified it, the United States and Britain among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It excused Japan from reparations for the war, and on the same day the American secretary of state, Dean Acheson, and the Japanese prime minister signed a separate security treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its territorial articles dismantled an empire whose expansion had begun when Japan occupied the Kuriles, the Bonins and the Ryukyus between 1875 and 1880.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Japan recognised the independence of Korea and renounced all claim to it, and it also renounced Formosa and the Pescadores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It renounced the Kurile Islands and the part of Sakhalin gained in 1905, both promised to the Soviet Union at Yalta, as well as its Pacific mandate and its claims to the Spratly and Paracel Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> These terms carried out the promise of the Cairo Declaration of 1943 that Japan would lose the Pacific islands seized since 1914 and be expelled from all territories taken by violence and greed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Potsdam Declaration had already limited Japanese sovereignty to Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, Shikoku and such minor islands as the Allies determined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Treaty of Peace with Japan, Signed at San Francisco, on 8 September 1951,” <i>United Nations Treaty Series</i> 136, no. 1832 (1952), https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%20136/volume-136-I-1832-English.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/japan-reconstruction. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Korean War and Japan’s Recovery,” Milestones in the History of US Foreign Relations, US Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","“Cairo Communiqué,” released 1 December 1943, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/cairo.asp. [Open access]","“Proclamation Defining Terms for Japanese Surrender,” Potsdam, 26 July 1945, in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy: Basic Documents, 1941–49</i>, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","“Yalta (Crimea) Conference: Agreement Regarding Japan,” 11 February 1945, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/yalta.asp. [Open access]","S. Woodburn Kirby et al., <i>The War Against Japan</i>, vol. 5, <i>The Surrender of Japan</i>, History of the Second World War, United Kingdom Military Series (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1969), https://archive.org/details/war-against-japan-vol-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union refuse to sign?","a":"Because, together with Poland and Czechoslovakia, it objected to the promise forced on Japan by American politicians to support the Republic of China and not to deal with the People's Republic of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most nations allied with the Soviet Union stayed outside the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the terms so lenient towards Japan?","a":"Because by 1950 the United States saw the spread of communism in Asia as the real threat, and a rearmed Japan no longer alarmed its officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty excused Japan from reparations, while the United States kept its bases in Okinawa and elsewhere and promised a security pact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japan lose so much territory?","a":"Because the Allies had promised at Cairo in 1943 to strip Japan of the Pacific islands it had seized since 1914 and of every territory taken by violence, and at Potsdam they limited its sovereignty to the home islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The treaty turned those promises into renunciations of Korea, Formosa, the Kuriles and southern Sakhalin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Treaty of San Francisco"},
    "ww2-959": {"questions":["Unidentified ashes brought back from the former Soviet republics, the Philippines and Iwo Jima are laid to rest at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Japan's welfare ministry in a government memorial service.","A gilt bronze urn given by the Shōwa Emperor, holding remains that symbolise all who died in the war, rests in a ceramic coffin at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Tokyo."],"abstract":"<b>Chidorigafuchi National Cemetery</b> is the cemetery in Tokyo established by the Japanese government in 1959 to house the remains of the many unknown Japanese who died overseas during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the remains were recovered by government missions that began in 1953, and others were brought home after the war by returning military units and by individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The remains lie in underground ossuaries, and a ceramic coffin holds a gilt bronze urn, a gift of the Shōwa Emperor, containing remains that symbolise all who died in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare places there the ashes of unidentified dead brought home by government missions from places such as the former Soviet republics, the Philippines and Iwo Jima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2001, for example, the ashes of 1,042 people were added, bringing to 348,406 the number of unidentified Japanese whose ashes were housed at the cemetery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese government counts approximately 3.1 million of its people as having lost their lives in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They include those who fell on the battlefields, those killed by the atomic bombings, the air raids on Japanese cities and the ground battles on Okinawa, and those who died after the war in distant foreign lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Occupation records of 1948 verified the deaths of about 1.4 million servicemen not reported dead before the surrender, while nearly 77,000 more were still carried as missing overseas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fate of many who had surrendered to Soviet forces was still unknown in 1949, since the Soviet authorities gave no information about the Japanese troops they held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the national memorial ceremony held every 15 August, Japanese prime ministers have promised never to forget the large number of war dead whose remains have still not been recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ministry of the Environment, Government of Japan, “Chidorigafuchi National Cemetery: Outline,” https://www.env.go.jp/garden/chidorigafuchi/english/index.html. [Open access]","“State Holds Memorial Service for Unidentified War Dead,” <i>Japan Times</i>, 29 May 2001, https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2001/05/29/national/state-holds-memorial-service-for-unidentified-war-dead. [Open access]","Sanae Takaichi, “Address by Prime Minister Takaichi at the Eighty-First National Memorial Ceremony for the War Dead,” 15 August 2026, provisional translation, Prime Minister’s Office of Japan, https://japan.kantei.go.jp/105/speech/202608/15shikiji.html. [Open access]","Shinzo Abe, “Address by Prime Minister Abe at the Seventy-Fourth Memorial Ceremony for the War Dead,” 15 August 2019, Prime Minister’s Office of Japan, https://japan.kantei.go.jp/98_abe/statement/201908/_00005.html. [Open access]","General Headquarters, Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur</i>, vol. 1, supplement, <i>MacArthur in Japan: The Occupation: Military Phase</i> (Washington, DC: US Army Center of Military History, 1966), https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurMacarthurInJapan. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the remains in the cemetery unidentified?","a":"Because the cemetery was established for the many unknown Japanese who died overseas, whose remains could not be returned to particular families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When missions do establish identities, the remains go home instead: in 2001 the ashes of 22 people were returned to their families after the government verified who they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the government still search for remains abroad?","a":"Because, as successive prime ministers have said at the August memorial ceremony, a large number of the war dead have still not been recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The government calls it the responsibility of the nation to spare no effort in returning them to their hometowns as soon as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the numbers of the dead so uncertain after the war?","a":"Because many deaths had not been reported before the surrender and had to be verified afterwards, and tens of thousands of men were still listed as missing in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Soviet authorities gave no information about the Japanese troops in their hands, so their dead and missing could not be counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chidorigafuchi National Cemetery"},
    "ww2-960": {"questions":["Proclaiming Sunday, 2 September 1945, as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a radio address that evening, President Truman called it the day of formal surrender by Japan.","Australian governments gazetted a public holiday for Victory in the Pacific in August 1945, though Britain, the United States and New Zealand preferred the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the occasion."],"abstract":"<b>Victory over Japan Day</b>, or VJ Day, is the day marking the surrender of Japan in 1945, observed on 15 August in Britain and on 2 September in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan accepted the Allied terms on 14 August, and the emperor's rescript announcing that his empire accepted the Allied declaration was broadcast to the Japanese people at noon on 15 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As soon as the news was announced on 14 August celebrations erupted across the United States, and in New York's Times Square sailors climbed lampposts to unfurl flags under falling ticker tape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain declared the following day, 15 August, its official VJ Day, and Americans joined in that day's celebrations too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Australia governments gazetted a public holiday as VP Day, for Victory in the Pacific, and most newspapers reported it under that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Britain, the United States and New Zealand preferred VJ Day, and the claim that the Australian day was first called VJ and quietly renamed later is not true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The formal surrender was signed aboard USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay on 2 September, and the ceremony ended at 0925, when the war was officially over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That evening President Truman proclaimed Sunday, 2 September 1945, to be V-J Day, the day of formal surrender by Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He asked Americans to set it aside as a day of renewed consecration to the principles which, he said, had made them the strongest nation on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Japan 15 August is the day of the National Memorial Ceremony for the War Dead, at which the prime minister honours the roughly 3.1 million Japanese who lost their lives in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Harry S. Truman, “Radio Address to the American People after the Signing of the Terms of Unconditional Surrender by Japan,” 1 September 1945, Public Papers of the Presidents, Harry S. Truman Library and Museum, https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/122/radio-address-american-people-after-signing-terms-unconditional-surrender. [Open access]","National WWII Museum, “V-J Day,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/v-j-day. [Open access]","Royal British Legion, “Remembering VJ Day,” https://www.britishlegion.org.uk/get-involved/remembrance/remembrance-events/vj-day. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Victory in the Pacific (VP) Day,” last updated 16 April 2025, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/vp_day. [Open access]","Sanae Takaichi, “Address by Prime Minister Takaichi at the Eighty-First National Memorial Ceremony for the War Dead,” 15 August 2026, provisional translation, Prime Minister’s Office of Japan, https://japan.kantei.go.jp/105/speech/202608/15shikiji.html. [Open access]","“Imperial Rescript, August 14, 1945,” <i>Nippon Times</i>, 15 August 1945, in “The World and Japan” Database, National Graduate Institute for Policy Studies and University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/docs/19450814.O1E.html. [Open access]","Battleship Missouri Memorial, “The Surrender Ceremony Aboard USS Missouri,” https://ussmissouri.org/learn-the-history/surrender. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Imperial Rescript of the Termination of the War, August 14, 1945,” Birth of the Constitution of Japan, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/shiryo/01/017shoshi.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is VJ Day marked on two different dates?","a":"Because Japan's acceptance of the surrender terms was announced on 14 August, and Britain declared the next day, 15 August, its official VJ Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States instead fixed its official day on 2 September, when the formal surrender documents were signed aboard USS Missouri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do Australians often speak of VP Day?","a":"Because in August 1945 Australian governments gazetted the public holiday as VP Day, for Victory in the Pacific, and most newspapers reported it that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Australian War Memorial notes that the governments of Britain, the United States and New Zealand preferred VJ Day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does Japan mark 15 August so differently?","a":"Because it was the day the emperor's recorded rescript ending the war was broadcast to the nation at noon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Japan now holds its National Memorial Ceremony for the War Dead on that date, mourning those who fell in battle, in the air raids and atomic bombings, and abroad after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Victory over Japan Day"},
    "ww2-981": {"questions":["Weizsäcker's 1985 speech warned that the German effort called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could never mean finishing with the past, for all Germans, guilty or not, must accept it.","The Frankfurt Auschwitz trial of 1963 to 1965, which Fritz Bauer pressed as self-enlightenment for West German society, is a landmark of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Federal Republic."],"abstract":"<b>Vergangenheitsbewältigung</b>, a German word usually translated as ‘coming to terms with the past’, names the long and contested public effort in Germany to confront the crimes of National Socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As early as the late 1950s the philosopher Theodor Adorno warned that talk of working through the past had taken on a revisionist sense, the wish to draw a line under it and wipe it from memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two German states answered differently, for East Germany drew its identity from an official antifascism and celebrated 8 May as the day of liberation from fascism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the West more than 900 proceedings were brought against Nazi-era defendants after 1949, yet most ended in acquittal or light sentences, and former Nazi officials made up most of the judges for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many West Germans long called 8 May 1945 a day of defeat and collapse, stressing their own suffering and rarely mentioning the victims of the occupation and the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A turning point came in Frankfurt, where the Hessian attorney general Fritz Bauer, a jurist from a Jewish family who had survived the war in exile, initiated the Auschwitz trial held from December 1963 to August 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bauer saw trials of Nazi perpetrators as a means of self-enlightenment and self-cleansing for West German society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 8 May 1985 President Richard von Weizsäcker told the Bundestag that the day had liberated all Germans from the inhumanity and tyranny of the National Socialist regime, and could not be separated from 30 January 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He added that all Germans, guilty or not, must accept the past, while insisting that coming to terms with it, in the sense of being done with it, was not possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe, opened in the centre of Berlin on 10 May 2005, and President Steinmeier's reminder in 2025 that most Germans stayed loyal to the regime to the last day show the effort continuing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Peter Dudek, “„Vergangenheitsbewältigung“: Zur Problematik eines umstrittenen Begriffs,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i>, no. 1–2 (1992), Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/archiv/535899/vergangenheitsbewaeltigung-zur-problematik-eines-umstrittenen-begriffs/. [in German] [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “War Crimes Trials,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/war-crimes-trials. [Open access]","Fritz Bauer Institut, “Fritz Bauer (1903–1968),” https://www.fritz-bauer-institut.de/en/fritz-bauer. [Open access]","Richard von Weizsäcker, “Speech during the Ceremony Commemorating the 40th Anniversary of the End of War in Europe and of National-Socialist Tyranny,” Bundestag, Bonn, 8 May 1985, official translation, transcribed by American Rhetoric, https://www.americanrhetoric.com/speeches/richardvonweizsacker40thanniversaryendofwareurope.htm. [Open access]","Arolsen Archives, “Defeat, Liberation or Victory? Perspectives on May 8, 1945,” 19 May 2025, https://arolsen-archives.org/en/news/defeat-liberation-or-victory/. [Open access]","Foundation Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe, “Memorial to the Murdered Jews of Europe,” https://www.stiftung-denkmal.de/en/memorials/memorial-to-the-murdered-jews-of-europe/. [Open access]","Frank-Walter Steinmeier, “We Are All Children of 8 May,” speech, Berlin, 8 May 2025, Office of the Federal President, https://www.bundespraesident.de/SharedDocs/Reden/EN/Frank-Walter-Steinmeier/Reden/2025/250508-End-WWII.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Adorno distrust talk of working through the past?","a":"Because he suspected, by the end of the 1950s, that the phrase had taken on a revisionist meaning in public use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it he heard the wish to draw a line under what had happened and, if possible, to wipe it from memory altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Frankfurt Auschwitz trial matter so much?","a":"Because it came when most West German proceedings against Nazi-era defendants ended in acquittal or light sentences and former Nazi officials still made up most of the judges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fritz Bauer, who initiated it, treated such trials as a means of self-enlightenment and self-cleansing for West German society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Weizsäcker's 1985 speech seen as a landmark?","a":"Because he was the first German president to say that 8 May was a day of liberation, where many had spoken of defeat and collapse, and the speech won widespread praise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He tied the end of the war to the start of the tyranny in 1933 and told Germans that all of them must accept the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vergangenheitsbewältigung"},
    "ww2-982": {"questions":["A Socialist prime minister's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, marking 50 years since the surrender, set the standard of apology that Japan's leaders followed for nearly 2 decades until 2013.","Abe's statement of 2015 echoed the 'deep remorse and heartfelt apology' set out in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but said that postwar generations should not be predestined to apologise."],"abstract":"The <b>Murayama Statement</b> was the declaration issued by Prime Minister Tomiichi Murayama on 15 August 1995, the 50th anniversary of the end of the war, in which Japan's government apologised for its colonial rule and aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It said that Japan, following a mistaken national policy, had advanced along the road to war and caused tremendous damage and suffering to the people of many countries, particularly those of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Murayama, head of the Japan Socialist Party, led a coalition government from June 1994 to January 1996, and his statement is seen as Japan's main expression of remorse for its wartime and colonial past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It followed a statement by Chief Cabinet Secretary Yōhei Kōno in August 1993, which admitted the military's involvement in the wartime comfort stations and offered apologies and remorse to the women who suffered in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Murayama's statement was approved in advance by the cabinet, which gave it unprecedented weight for a prime minister's words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was made in a country deeply divided over the war, where liberals wanted atonement for militarism and colonialism while conservatives insisted that the conflicts had been waged in self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Murayama spoke forthrightly of aggression rather than the usual 'acts of aggression', and stated his apology in a direct and unambiguous Japanese expression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statement also mourned victims at home as well as abroad and recalled that Japan was the only country to have suffered atomic bombing, pledging it to work for nuclear disarmament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It set a standard followed by every prime minister for nearly 2 decades, until Shinzō Abe stopped apologising in 2013 amid complaints in his party that it hurt national pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Abe's own statement of 2015 repeated that Japan had expressed deep remorse and heartfelt apology, but added that generations born after the war should not be predestined to apologise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Tomiichi Murayama, “Statement by Prime Minister Tomiichi Murayama on the 50th Anniversary of the End of the War,” 15 August 1995, Prime Minister of Japan and His Cabinet, https://japan.kantei.go.jp/aniv.html. [Open access]","Cameron W. Barr, “Japan Makes Clear Apology,” <i>Christian Science Monitor</i>, 16 August 1995, https://www.csmonitor.com/1995/0816/16062.html. [Open access]","Yōhei Kōno, “Statement by Chief Cabinet Secretary Yohei Kono on the Result of the Study on the Issue of ‘Comfort Women’,” 4 August 1993, Digital Museum: The Comfort Women Issue and the Asian Women’s Fund, https://awf.or.jp/e6/statement-02.html. [Open access]","Shinzō Abe, “Statement by Prime Minister Shinzo Abe,” 14 August 2015, Prime Minister of Japan and His Cabinet, https://japan.kantei.go.jp/97_abe/statement/201508/0814statement.html. [Open access]","Associated Press, “Former Japanese PM Murayama, Known for Apology over Wartime Aggression, Dies at 101,” 17 October 2025, via OPB, https://www.opb.org/article/2025/10/17/former-japanese-prime-minister-murayama-dies-at-101/. [Open access]","Reuters, “Japan’s Past Apologies over WWII, Colonial History, ‘Comfort Women’,” <i>Japan Times</i>, 14 August 2015, https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2015/08/14/national/history/japans-past-apologies-wwii-colonial-history-comfort-women/. [Paywalled]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the cabinet's approval matter so much?","a":"Because a statement approved in advance by the whole cabinet carried far more weight than a prime minister's personal remarks, and the Monitor's correspondent judged that such words would have been unthinkable a decade earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became the standard that later prime ministers followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the wording watched so closely?","a":"Because Japanese opinion was split between those who wanted atonement for militarism and colonialism and those who held that the wars were fought in self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Murayama said aggression rather than 'acts of aggression', and used a direct expression for his apology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did later leaders move away from it?","a":"Because nationalist politicians, including members of Abe's Liberal Democratic Party, said that repeated apologies interfered with Japan's national pride, and Abe stopped apologising in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His 2015 statement still cited Japan's earlier remorse but said that the postwar generations should not be predestined to apologise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Murayama Statement"},
    "ww2-983": {"questions":["Paul Hoffman left the presidency of Studebaker to head the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Paris office worked with the Europeans to divide Marshall aid among 16 countries.","Every counterpart fund project financed with the local currency matching a Marshall Plan grant needed the approval of the administrator of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Washington before it could proceed."],"abstract":"The <b>Economic Cooperation Administration</b> was the United States government agency created by the Economic Cooperation Act of 3 April 1948 to administer the European Recovery Program, better known as the Marshall Plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The programme grew out of Secretary of State George Marshall's speech at Harvard on 5 June 1947, which called for a comprehensive effort to rebuild a Europe ravaged by war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over 4 years Congress appropriated about $13.3 billion for European recovery, which supplied the capital and materials the Europeans needed to rebuild their economies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The aid went to 16 countries, all in Western Europe, since the Soviet Union and its East European satellites stayed out of the plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agency was abolished by the Mutual Security Act of October 1951, and its functions passed to the Mutual Security Agency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Congress made it a separate agency so that it could escape regulations that would impede flexibility, and also because the Republican Congress distrusted a State Department run by a Democratic administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its first administrator, from 1948 to 1950, was Paul G. Hoffman, president of the Studebaker Corporation, who was both a Republican and a businessman as congressional leaders required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A regional office of 600 people in Paris, headed by Averell Harriman, coordinated the national programmes and worked with the European-run Organisation for European Economic Co-operation, which recommended how the aid should be divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Grants made up more than 90 per cent of the programme, and each country had to match them with its own currency in a counterpart fund whose projects the administrator had to approve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hoffman later recalled that the first line of the Harriman report, which he wrote, was 'Only the Europeans can save Europe', and that he pressed the recipients hard to cut the barriers to trade among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Marshall Plan, 1948,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/marshall-plan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Administrators of the Economic Cooperation Administration,” https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/principalofficers/administrator-economic-cooperation-administration. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of U.S. Foreign Assistance Agencies, 1948–1961 [Record Group 469],” Guide to Federal Records, 469.2, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/469.html. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Marshall Plan (1948),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/marshall-plan. [Open access]","Paul G. Hoffman, oral history interview by Philip C. Brooks, 25 October 1964, Harry S. Truman Library, https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/oral-histories/hoffmanp. [Open access]","Congressional Research Service, <i>The Marshall Plan: Design, Accomplishments, and Relevance to the Present</i>, CRS Report 97-62 (Washington, DC, 6 January 1997), 9–12, https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/97-62.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a new agency created instead of using the State Department?","a":"Because a separate agency could be exempted from many government regulations that would have slowed a programme of this size, and because the Republican Congress distrusted a State Department headed by a Democratic administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Congress still required the administrator to work closely with the Secretary of State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were counterpart funds part of the scheme?","a":"Because each country had to match every dollar of grant aid with a dollar's worth of its own currency, which went into a fund for roads, power plants, housing and similar projects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each project needed the administrator's approval, and Britain simply used its fund to reduce debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the aid go only to Western Europe?","a":"Because Soviet concern over American economic domination of its East European satellites, and Stalin's unwillingness to open his society to Westerners, doomed their participation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Office of the Historian also judges it unlikely that Congress would have funded the plan so generously if aid had gone to Communist states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Economic Cooperation Administration"},
    "ww2-984": {"questions":["Running from the Bay of Lübeck to the Czechoslovak frontier, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as the boundary between the Soviet zone and the Western zones of occupied Germany.","A study published in 2017 counted 327 people, from a baby of 6 months to someone of 81, killed by the East German regime on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1949 and 1989."],"abstract":"The <b>inner German border</b> was the frontier between East and West Germany, running for about 1,400 kilometres (870 miles) from the Baltic coast at the Bay of Lübeck to the Czechoslovak frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It began as the line between the Soviet occupation zone and the Western zones of defeated Germany, and after the founding of the German Democratic Republic on 7 October 1949 it became the dividing line between the two German states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For its first years it was still permeable, and film of the time shows guards helping people over the line and children waving across to the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 26 May 1952, the day the Western powers signed the Germany Treaty with West Germany in Bonn, East Germany's Council of Ministers decreed a strict border regime, carried out by the Ministry for State Security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A prohibited zone 5 kilometres (3 miles) deep was laid along the whole border, with a ploughed control strip 10 metres (33 feet) wide that was partly mined and a protective strip 500 metres (1,640 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> People judged politically unreliable were forced out of their villages near the border, and by the end of 1961 about 12,000 had been resettled away from the prohibited zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The border was built up until 1989 into a deadly barrier, with by one count 302 watchtowers, 55,000 spring-gun devices installed between 1971 and 1984, and roughly 1.3 to 1.4 million mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A study by the Free University of Berlin published in 2017 counted 327 men, women and children from both German states who died because of the border regime, aged from 6 months to 81 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That figure was criticised soon after publication, partly because it also counted deaths from before the Berlin Wall was built in August 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The border opened on the night of 9 to 10 November 1989, and museums at the former crossing between Duderstadt and Worbis and at Schlagsdorf now tell its history from 1945 to 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["German Historical Institute Washington, “Inner-German Border near Heldra (Hesse) (1952),” <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, vol. 8, <i>Occupation and the Emergence of Two States (1945–1961)</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/occupation-and-the-emergence-of-two-states-1945-1961/inner-german-border-near-heldra-hesse-1952. [Open access]","Birgit Neumann-Becker, “Gedenkrede für die Opfer des Grenzregimes,” Hötensleben, 26 May 2017, Landesbeauftragte zur Aufarbeitung der SED-Diktatur Sachsen-Anhalt, https://aufarbeitung.sachsen-anhalt.de/fileadmin/Bibliothek/Politik_und_Verwaltung/Stasi-Unterlagen/LStU_PDF/Gedenkrede_fuer_die_Opfer_des_Grenzregimes-2017-05-26.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Stiftung Gedenkstätten Sachsen-Anhalt, “Gedenkveranstaltung zum 73. Jahrestag der Schließung der innerdeutschen Grenze,” press release 65/2025, 15 May 2025, https://stgs.sachsen-anhalt.de/fileadmin/Bibliothek/STGS/03_Presse/Pressemitteilungen/Pressemitteilungen_2025/PM_65_2025_GDT_Gedenkveranstaltung_26.05.25.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Politische Memoriale e.V., “Border House Schlagsdorf,” Mecklenburg-Vorpommern tourism board, https://www.off-to-mv.com/destinations/a-grenzhus-schlagsdorf. [Open access]","Freie Universität Berlin, “Study: A Total of 327 Men, Women, and Children from East and West Germany Alike Fell Victim to the East German Border Regime,” press release, 26 June 2017, https://www.fu-berlin.de/en/presse/informationen/wissenschaft/2017/201706/opfer-ddr-grenzregime.html. [Open access]","Wissenschaftliche Dienste des Deutschen Bundestages, <i>Todesopfer des SED-Regimes</i>, WD 1 - 3000 - 015/21 (Berlin, 2021), 5, https://www.bundestag.de/resource/blob/855618/WD-1-015-21-pdf.pdf. [in German] [Open access]","Grenzlandmuseum Eichsfeld, “Herzlich willkommen im Grenzlandmuseum Eichsfeld,” https://www.grenzlandmuseum.de/. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the border sealed in May 1952?","a":"Because the Western powers had rejected the Stalin Note of 1952, and on the very day they signed the Germany Treaty with West Germany the East German Council of Ministers decreed a strict border regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The measures were carried out by the Ministry for State Security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were people forced out of the border villages?","a":"Because the regime wanted no one it thought politically unreliable living inside the prohibited zone, and the language of the orders spoke of enemies and vermin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of 1961 about 12,000 people had been resettled from the zone to the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the number of the dead still argued about?","a":"Because counts depend on which cases are included, and the Free University study of 2017 checked 1,492 suspected cases before arriving at 327 deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Critics objected that it also counted deaths from before the building of the Berlin Wall in 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Inner German border"},
    "ww2-985": {"questions":["The paper NSC-68, drafted under Paul Nitze in 1950, turned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a call for a massive military build-up stretching from the industrial centres to the whole world.","John Foster Dulles argued in the 1952 campaign that American policy should seek the rollback of Soviet power rather than mere <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the liberation of Eastern Europe."],"abstract":"<b>Containment</b> was the policy, formulated by the American diplomat George F. Kennan, of blocking the expansion of Soviet influence, and it became the basic United States strategy of the Cold War from 1947 to 1989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in the 'Long Telegram' that Kennan sent from Moscow as chargé d'affaires on 22 February 1946, which traced the Kremlin's neurotic view of world affairs to a traditional and instinctive Russian sense of insecurity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soviet power, he wrote, was impervious to the logic of reason but highly sensitive to the logic of force, and it usually withdrew when it met strong resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In July 1947 the journal <i>Foreign Affairs</i> published his anonymous 'X' article, which called for a long-term, patient but firm and vigilant containment of Russian expansive tendencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He predicted that such a policy would promote tendencies leading to either the break-up or the gradual mellowing of Soviet power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was first applied in 1947, when Britain said it could no longer aid the Greek government in its civil war and Truman told Congress on 12 March that the United States must support free peoples resisting subjugation by armed minorities or outside pressures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Truman asked Congress for $400 million of aid to Greece and Turkey for the period ending in June 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kennan, the first director of the State Department's Policy Planning Staff from 1947 to 1949, saw the Soviet threat as mainly political and favoured economic aid such as the Marshall Plan, which was applied only to Western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> His successor Paul Nitze saw it in military terms, and the paper NSC-68 of April 1950 called for a massive build-up of American forces and extended containment to the whole world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The columnist Walter Lippmann attacked the idea for failing to separate vital from peripheral interests, while John Foster Dulles called in 1952 for the 'rollback' of Soviet power instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Kennan and Containment, 1947,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/kennan. [Open access]","George F. Kennan, “The Chargé in the Soviet Union (Kennan) to the Secretary of State,” Moscow, 22 February 1946, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1946</i>, vol. 6, <i>Eastern Europe; the Soviet Union</i>, document 475, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1946v06/d475. [Open access]","X [George F. Kennan], “The Sources of Soviet Conduct,” <i>Foreign Affairs</i> 25, no. 4 (July 1947), https://www.foreignaffairs.com/russian-federation/george-kennan-sources-soviet-conduct. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “The Truman Doctrine, 1947,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/truman-doctrine. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Address before a Joint Session of Congress,” 12 March 1947, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/trudoc.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “NSC-68, 1950,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/NSC68. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Directors of Policy Planning,” https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/principalofficers/director-policy-planning. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Marshall Plan, 1948,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/marshall-plan. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Kennan think firmness would work?","a":"Because he judged that Soviet power did not take unnecessary risks or work to fixed plans, and that it was highly sensitive to the logic of force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If an adversary had enough force and made clear its readiness to use it, the Kremlin could easily withdraw, and usually did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Kennan and Nitze disagree about containment?","a":"Because Kennan saw the Soviet threat as primarily political and preferred economic aid and psychological warfare, while Nitze read 'counter-force' as military power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nitze's view won in 1950, when NSC-68 called for a drastic expansion of the military budget.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did NSC-68 call for a military build-up?","a":"Because its authors, writing after the Communist victory in China and the first Soviet atomic test, expected the Soviet threat to grow as Moscow added weapons, including nuclear ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They concluded that the best answer was to respond in kind with a massive build-up of American forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Containment"},
    "ww2-986": {"questions":["France's ambassador called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of May 1945 an 'inexcusable tragedy' in 2005, the first official French acknowledgement of killings Algeria counts at 45,000 dead.","The air and naval bombardment of villages around Sétif and the executions by militias at Guelma made up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a turning point for Algerian nationalism after the Second World War."],"abstract":"The <b>Sétif and Guelma massacre</b> was the killing of thousands of Algerians by the French army, police and settler militias in the Constantine region of eastern Algeria from 8 May 1945, the day Germany's surrender was celebrated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> During the war the moderate nationalist Ferhat Abbas, a pharmacist from Sétif, had issued a Manifesto in February 1943 arguing that Algeria, through the service of its soldiers, stood with the Allies against Nazism, and claiming an Algerian state federated with France once peace returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His movement, the Friends of the Manifesto and of Liberty, spread ideas that matched the Atlantic Charter's promise that peoples would be free to choose their own government after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 8 May nationalists joined the victory parades to demand the release of their leader Messali Hadj, and at Sétif a policeman shot dead a 22-year-old demonstrator, Bouzid Saal, who refused to drop an Algerian flag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The riots and revenge attacks that followed killed 102 Europeans in the region between Sétif and Kherrata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The army replied with heavy weapons, aircraft and the navy, waging a real war against civilians around Sétif, while at Guelma settler militias chose, arrested and executed nationalists into late June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No exact count exists, for the French civil authorities gave an official figure of about 1,500 dead, historians' estimates run from 5,000 to 20,000, and the Algerian state's figure is 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The killings transformed the young anti-colonial movement, and the war of independence that broke out 9 years later ended with Algeria's independence in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> France's first official acknowledgement came on 27 February 2005, when its ambassador Hubert Colin de Verdière called the massacres an 'inexcusable tragedy' at Sétif.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Even the name still divides the two countries, since Algerians long spoke of a colonialist genocide and the French of the troubles of the North Constantinois, and in 2025 French deputies proposed recognising the massacres as crimes of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jean-Pierre Peyroulou, “Le cas de Sétif-Kherrata-Guelma (mai 1945),” <i>Violence de masse et Résistance – Réseau de recherche</i>, Sciences Po, 21 March 2008, copy at https://le-souvenir-francais.fr/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Le-cas-de-Setif-Kherrata-Guelma-Mai-1945-_-Sciences-Po-Violence-de-masse-et-Resistance-Reseau-de-recherche.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Charles-Robert Ageron, “Les troubles du nord-constantinois en mai 1945: Une tentative insurrectionnelle?,” <i>Vingtième Siècle: Revue d’histoire</i>, no. 4 (1984): 23–38, https://doi.org/10.3406/xxs.1984.1714. [in French] [Open access]","Charles-Robert Ageron, “Mai 1945 en Algérie: Enjeu de mémoire et histoire,” <i>Matériaux pour l’histoire de notre temps</i>, no. 39–40 (1995): 52–56, https://doi.org/10.3406/mat.1995.402764. [in French] [Open access]","Sylvie Thénault, “Guelma, un massacre dans l’Algérie coloniale,” review of <i>Guelma, 1945</i>, by Jean-Pierre Peyroulou, <i>La Vie des idées</i>, 26 March 2009, https://laviedesidees.fr/IMG/pdf/20090326_thenault.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Assemblée nationale, <i>Proposition de résolution visant à reconnaître les massacres du 8 mai 1945 à Sétif, Guelma et Kherrata, en Algérie, comme crimes d’État</i>, no. 1776 rectifié, 9 September 2025, https://www.assemblee-nationale.fr/dyn/17/textes/l17b1776_proposition-resolution.pdf. [in French] [Open access]","Agence France-Presse, “Algeria Remembers Mass Killings under French Rule,” JusticeInfo.net, 8 May 2021, https://www.justiceinfo.net/en/76982-algeria-remembers-mass-killings-under-french-rule.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the war raise Algerian nationalists' hopes?","a":"Because Algerian soldiers fought for the Allies, and Ferhat Abbas's Manifesto of 1943 argued that this placed Algeria on the Allied side against Nazism and earned it a state of its own federated with France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His supporters read the Atlantic Charter's promise of self-determination as applying to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the death toll still disputed?","a":"Because precise records are lacking, some still classified or destroyed after the events, including by the burning of bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The French civil authorities' official figure was about 1,500, while officials, parties and historians gave figures from 5,000 upwards, and the Algerian state settled on 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is Guelma treated separately from Sétif?","a":"Because at Guelma there was no riot that provoked a disproportionate response, as at Sétif.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Instead settlers organised in militias, with the backing of local officials, chose, arrested and executed men engaged in the nationalist cause, and the killing went on into late June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sétif and Guelma massacre"},
    "ww2-987": {"questions":["Once the American steamer <i>President Warfield</i>, which served off Normandy in 1944, the vessel renamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was bought by the Hagana to carry Jewish refugees to Palestine.","UN committee members in Palestine described to their colleagues the towing into Haifa of the immigrant ship <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose passengers Britain later forced ashore at Hamburg."],"abstract":"The <b>SS Exodus</b>, sailing as <i>Exodus 1947</i>, was a ship that tried in July 1947 to carry more than 4,500 Jewish Holocaust survivors and displaced persons from France to British-ruled Palestine without permission to land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Built as the American steamer <i>President Warfield</i>, it had served in the Normandy invasion in June 1944 before the Hagana, an underground Jewish military organisation, bought it to carry Jews who sought to enter Palestine illegally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain had restricted Jewish immigration since its White Paper of 1939, which allowed some 75,000 immigrants over the following 5 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1945 and 1948 the British captured more than 50,000 Jewish refugees at sea and sent them to detention camps on the island of Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The passengers of the Exodus had come from the displaced persons camps of Germany, and Hamburg's memorial plaque records that they were refused entry on the ground that their presence would destabilise British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> On 18 July 1947 British destroyers intercepted the ship before it reached Palestine's waters, and in the struggle a Jewish crew member and two passengers were killed and dozens wounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Members of the United Nations Special Committee on Palestine, then in the country, gave their colleagues an eyewitness account of the ship being brought into Haifa under British naval escort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The British put the passengers on three transports back to France, where they refused to land at Port-de-Bouc and held a hunger strike, and then to Hamburg, where soldiers removed them by force and took them to camps near Lübeck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The worldwide outcry embarrassed Britain and helped sway international opinion towards a Jewish state, which the UN General Assembly's partition resolution of 29 November 1947 then proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> David Ben-Gurion proclaimed the State of Israel on 14 May 1948, and President Truman recognised it the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Exodus 1947,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/exodus-1947. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Postwar Refugee Crisis and the Establishment of the State of Israel,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/postwar-refugee-crisis-and-the-establishment-of-the-state-of-israel. [Open access]","British Government, “British White Paper of 1939,” statement of policy on Palestine, 1939, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/brwh1939.asp. [Open access]","United Nations Special Committee on Palestine, <i>Report to the General Assembly</i>, A/364, 3 September 1947, chap. 2, United Nations Information System on the Question of Palestine, https://www.un.org/unispal/document/auto-insert-179435/. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, Resolution 181 (II), “Future Government of Palestine,” 29 November 1947, United Nations Information System on the Question of Palestine, https://www.un.org/unispal/document/auto-insert-185393/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Creation of Israel, 1948,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/creation-israel. [Open access]","Gedenkstätten in Hamburg, “Memorial Plaque for the ‘Exodus’ Emigrant Ship,” https://gedenkstaetten-in-hamburg.de/en/memorials/show/memorial-plaque-for-the-exodus-emigrant-ship. [Open access]","KZ-Gedenkstätte Neuengamme, “Ein Flüchtlingsschiff wird zum Symbol / A Refugee Ship Becomes a Symbol,” exhibition panel 27, https://www.lernwerkstatt-neuengamme.de/medien/pdf/Tafel_27_Exodus.pdf. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Britain send the passengers back to Europe instead of to Cyprus?","a":"Because it was attempting to make an example of the Exodus 1947, towing it to Haifa and loading the passengers onto three navy transports that returned to Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the French would not force them ashore at Port-de-Bouc, the British took them on to Hamburg in their own zone of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the episode help the case for a Jewish state?","a":"Because the sight of Holocaust survivors forced back to Germany attracted worldwide publicity, embarrassed the British government and raised sympathy for Europe's Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Hamburg memorial describes an international outcry that accelerated the foundation of Israel, and UN committee members had seen the ship brought into Haifa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many survivors try to reach Palestine illegally?","a":"Because legal entry was capped, first by the White Paper of 1939 and then by British refusal to allow open immigration after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain feared that unlimited Jewish immigration would damage its relations with the Arabs and its interests in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"SS Exodus"},
    "ww2-988": {"questions":["A Red Cross draft protecting civilians, adopted at Tokyo in 1934, never reached its conference before war broke out, and its ideas finally became law in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1949.","The Red Cross commentary of 1958 says the conference that drafted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dominated by a common horror of the evils caused by the recent world war."],"abstract":"The <b>Fourth Geneva Convention</b>, formally the Geneva Convention relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, was signed on 12 August 1949 as the first of the Geneva treaties devoted to civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Second World War broke out, civilians had no effective protection under any treaty, since the Hague Regulations of 1907 dealt with them only under enemy occupation and assumed that fighting would stay close to the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A draft convention for civilians adopted by the International Red Cross Conference in Tokyo in 1934 was meant to go to a diplomatic conference in early 1940, which the outbreak of war made impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By wartime agreement some 160,000 interned enemy civilians were treated like prisoners of war, but people in occupied countries had no such safeguard and millions were deported, taken hostage or sent to concentration camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The atrocities committed against the populations of occupied Europe accounted for much of that war's high civilian death toll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The International Committee of the Red Cross took up the work of revision in 1945, and the conference that sat in Geneva for almost 4 months in 1949 was dominated by a common horror of the evils of the recent war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The convention forbids individual or mass forcible transfers and deportations of protected persons from occupied territory, whatever the motive, and bans collective penalties, reprisals and the taking of hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered into force on 21 October 1950, 6 months after Switzerland and Yugoslavia ratified it, and with the other three conventions of 1949 it protects people who take no part in the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal had already held that war crimes against civilians were covered by the Hague Convention of 1907, and that individuals who committed them could be punished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Some historians now argue that the protections it contains were shaped as much by the efforts of non-state bodies between the wars as by the atrocities of the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jean S. Pictet, ed., <i>The Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949: Commentary</i>, vol. 4, <i>Geneva Convention Relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War</i> (Geneva: International Committee of the Red Cross, 1958), 3–9, Library of Congress, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/llmlp/GC_1949-IV/GC_1949-IV.pdf. [Open access]","<i>Geneva Convention Relative to the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War</i>, 12 August 1949, arts. 33, 34 and 49, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva07.asp. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, “The Geneva Conventions and Their Commentaries,” https://www.icrc.org/en/law-and-policy/geneva-conventions-and-their-commentaries. [Open access]","<i>Convention (IV) Respecting the Laws and Customs of War on Land and Its Annex: Regulations</i>, The Hague, 18 October 1907, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/hague04.asp. [Open access]","Sarina Landefeld, “Inter-War Interactions in the Development of the Protection of Civilians: A Historical Perspective,” <i>Legal Studies</i> 45 (2025): 319–36, https://doi.org/10.1017/lst.2025.10080. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “The Law Relating to War Crimes and Crimes against Humanity,” judgment, Nuremberg, 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judlawre.asp. [Open access]","Ardi Imseis, “On the Fourth Geneva Convention and the Occupied Palestinian Territory,” <i>Harvard International Law Journal</i> 44, no. 1 (2003), https://law.queensu.ca/sites/default/files/files/Professors/On%20the%20GCIV%20and%20the%20OPT.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did civilians lack protection when the war began?","a":"Because the only rules, the Hague Regulations of 1907, covered civilians only in occupied territory and were written for wars fought close to the front, not for total war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tokyo Draft of 1934, which would have protected people in occupied countries, had never been adopted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it ban deportation regardless of motive?","a":"Because millions of civilians in occupied countries had been deported, taken as hostages or interned in concentration camps during the war, and hundreds of thousands died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article 49 therefore prohibits individual or mass forcible transfers and deportations from occupied territory, regardless of their motive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some scholars question the usual story of its origins?","a":"Because the standard account presents it as humanitarian progress led by the Red Cross in response to the atrocities of the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recent research argues that its protection regime was shaped earlier by several non-state bodies working between the wars, whose limits also explain some of its weaknesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fourth Geneva Convention"},
    "ww2-989": {"questions":["Raphael Lemkin, who lost 49 relatives in the Holocaust, campaigned for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until his death in 1959, though the United States waited until 1988 to ratify it.","Drafters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck out cultural genocide but kept the forcible transfer of children from one group to another as a punishable act."],"abstract":"The <b>Genocide Convention</b>, formally the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide, was adopted by the United Nations General Assembly on 9 December 1948 and entered into force on 12 January 1951.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the first human rights treaty adopted by the General Assembly, and the United Nations presents it as the international community's commitment to 'never again'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its article II defines genocide as any of five kinds of act, killing members of the group among them, committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnical, racial or religious group as such.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word had been coined by the Polish-Jewish lawyer Raphael Lemkin and first printed in his 1944 book <i>Axis Rule in Occupied Europe</i>, which analysed Nazi policies of persecution and mass murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Nuremberg tribunal used the related idea of crimes against humanity instead, and 'genocide' does not appear in its final judgment of 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Because the tribunal judged only crimes linked to the war, Cuba, Panama and India pressed the General Assembly to declare genocide a crime in peacetime too, and resolution 96 of 11 December 1946 called it a crime under international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lemkin, who learned at Nuremberg that 49 members of his family had been killed, campaigned for the convention and its ratification until his death in poverty in 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The drafters left out cultural genocide and the forcing of groups from their homes, while keeping the forcible transfer of children, and recognised only national courts and a future international tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States, which signed on 11 December 1948, did not ratify until 25 November 1988, and the treaty's register now lists 154 states as parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The International Court of Justice treats its principles as part of customary law binding on every state, and in 2007 it found that the killings at Srebrenica in July 1995 were genocide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["William A. Schabas, “Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide,” introductory note, United Nations Audiovisual Library of International Law, https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/cppcg/cppcg.html. [Open access]","<i>Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide</i>, 9 December 1948, art. II, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/genocide.asp. [Open access]","United Nations Office on Genocide Prevention and the Responsibility to Protect, “Genocide,” https://www.un.org/en/genocide-prevention/definition. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Coining a Word and Championing a Cause: The Story of Raphael Lemkin,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/coining-a-word-and-championing-a-cause-the-story-of-raphael-lemkin. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “What Is Genocide?,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/what-is-genocide. [Open access]","United Nations Treaty Collection, “Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide,” status, chap. IV.1, https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?src=TREATY&mtdsg_no=IV-1&chapter=4. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, <i>Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide (Bosnia and Herzegovina v. Serbia and Montenegro)</i>, case overview, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/91. [Open access]","United Nations, “International Day of Commemoration and Dignity of the Victims of the Crime of Genocide and of the Prevention of This Crime,” https://www.un.org/en/observances/genocide-prevention-day. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a convention needed after the Nuremberg trial?","a":"Because the Nuremberg tribunal had confined crimes against humanity to acts committed after the outbreak of war in September 1939, leaving peacetime attacks on groups unpunished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lemkin and the delegations that followed him wanted genocide recognised as a crime whether committed in war or in peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 'ethnic cleansing' not counted as genocide under it?","a":"Because the drafters voted down, by a large margin, an amendment that would have made it a crime to force members of a group to abandon their homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Courts have since relied on those debates to keep 'ethnic cleansing' outside the definition, which requires intent to destroy the group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it bind states that never ratified it?","a":"Because the International Court of Justice has repeatedly said that the convention's principles are part of general customary international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every state is therefore bound by the rule that genocide is a crime, and the court has also called its prohibition a peremptory norm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Genocide Convention"},
    "ww2-990": {"questions":["When Congress refused to ratify the Havana charter for an International Trade Organization, the provisional <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the main framework for tariff cuts for 47 years.","The wartime promise of the Atlantic Charter of equal access to the world's trade found its postwar form in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose first article demanded most-favoured-nation treatment."],"abstract":"The <b>General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade</b> was the treaty signed in Geneva on 30 October 1947 by 23 countries that set the rules for much of world trade from 1948 until the World Trade Organization replaced it in 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots lay in the war, for the fourth point of the Atlantic Charter of August 1941 promised all states, victor or vanquished, access on equal terms to the trade and raw materials of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Article VII of their lend-lease agreement of February 1942, Britain undertook in return for American aid to work towards eliminating all forms of discriminatory treatment in international commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American policymakers had drawn the lesson that the high tariffs, competitive devaluations and discriminatory trading blocs of the 1930s destabilised the world without curing the Depression, a policy symbolised by the Smoot-Hawley tariff of 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Bretton Woods conference of July 1944 created the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank, but agreement on trade proved harder, not least over the Commonwealth's system of imperial preference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Fifteen countries began talks in December 1945 to cut tariffs, and the first round produced 45,000 tariff concessions affecting about a fifth of the world's trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agreement was designed as an interim code until an International Trade Organization was created, and its first article bound members to give one another most-favoured-nation treatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The charter of that organisation was agreed at the Havana conference in March 1948, but the most serious opposition came in the United States Congress, and in 1950 the American government gave up seeking its ratification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 'provisional' agreement therefore remained, for 47 years, the framework within which tariffs were cut through a series of negotiating rounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last of them, the Uruguay Round, produced the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995, the biggest reform of world trade since the agreement was created at the end of the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["World Trade Organization, “The GATT Years: From Havana to Marrakesh,” Understanding the WTO: Basics, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/whatis_e/tif_e/fact4_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Bretton Woods-GATT, 1941–1947,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/bretton-woods. [Open access]","<i>The General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT 1947)</i>, Geneva, 30 October 1947, art. I, World Trade Organization, https://www.wto.org/english/docs_e/legal_e/gatt47_01_e.htm. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt and Winston S. Churchill, “The Atlantic Charter,” 14 August 1941, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/wwii/atlantic.asp. [Open access]","United Nations Conference on Trade and Employment, <i>Final Act and Related Documents</i>, Havana, 21 November 1947 – 24 March 1948 (Lake Success, NY: Interim Commission for the International Trade Organization, 1948), World Trade Organization, https://www.wto.org/english/docs_e/legal_e/havana_e.pdf. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Uruguay Round,” Understanding the WTO: Basics, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/whatis_e/tif_e/fact5_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “Protectionism in the Interwar Period,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did wartime planners care so much about trade?","a":"Because they believed that free trade promoted peace as well as prosperity, and the 1930s seemed to prove it, when high tariffs, devaluations and trading blocs destabilised the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Smoot-Hawley tariff of 1930 became a symbol of those beggar-thy-neighbour policies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the agreement only meant to be temporary?","a":"Because it was intended to protect the value of the tariff cuts already negotiated until the much broader International Trade Organization came into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signatories accepted some of the draft charter's rules provisionally, while allowing for the possibility that the organisation might never be created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did imperial preference delay agreement?","a":"Because American officials such as Cordell Hull wanted the Commonwealth's lower internal tariffs abolished, while many British and Commonwealth officials wanted to keep them until the United States cut its own high Smoot-Hawley rates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took more than 4 years of negotiation to settle this and other issues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade"},
    "ww2-991": {"questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried out the Beveridge Report's attack on Want, one of five giants on the road of reconstruction, and came fully into force in July 1948.","A huge central office in Newcastle was built to hold the records of every person insured under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which replaced Britain's separate prewar schemes of health and unemployment insurance."],"abstract":"The <b>National Insurance Act 1946</b> was the British law that created a single compulsory scheme of social insurance, paying unemployment and sickness benefit, maternity benefit, retirement pensions, widows' benefits, a guardian's allowance and a death grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It put into effect the central proposal of the Beveridge Report of 1942, which called social insurance an attack upon Want, one of five giants on the road of reconstruction alongside Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The wartime coalition published its own proposals in a White Paper, 'Social Insurance', in September 1944, and a little over a week later it created a Ministry of National Insurance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Labour's election victory in July 1945 the new minister was James Griffiths, a former miner and president of the South Wales miners' union who had helped lead the Commons revolt over Beveridge in February 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Griffiths presented the bill on 20 December 1945, and it also repealed the old separate schemes of unemployment insurance, health insurance and contributory pensions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The war had changed the politics of welfare, and one account holds that the unions' greater bargaining power and the entry of Labour ministers into government were what made universal benefits for children possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Commons debate of February 1946 members argued over whether rates such as 26 shillings a week for a widow or a retired woman were enough to live on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Act also provided for regular reports by the Government Actuary on the finances of the National Insurance Fund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The complete scheme came into force on the 'appointed day' in July 1948, by which time hundreds of regulations had been made and a huge central office set up in Newcastle to hold the records of everyone insured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its benefits were based on Beveridge's rates, but by 1948 fewer people accepted them as a subsistence income, and many still had to apply for means-tested national assistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>National Insurance Act 1946</i>, 9 & 10 Geo. 6, c. 67, legislation.gov.uk, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo6/9-10/67/contents/enacted. [Open access]","House of Commons, “National Insurance Bill,” bill presented, <i>Hansard</i>, 20 December 1945, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1945/dec/20/national-insurance-bill. [Open access]","House of Commons, “National Insurance Bill,” debate, <i>Hansard</i>, 11 February 1946, vol. 419, cols. 37–106, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1946/feb/11/national-insurance-bill. [Open access]","Richard Paul Chapman, “The Development of Policy on Family Allowances and National Insurance in the United Kingdom, 1942–1946” (thesis, London School of Economics and Political Science, 1991), https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/132144/1/U048581.pdf. [Open access]","William Beveridge, <i>Social Insurance and Allied Services</i>, Cmd. 6404 (London: HMSO, 1942), excerpt, Internet Modern History Sourcebook, Fordham University, https://fordham.edu/Halsall/mod/1942beveridge.asp. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Actuarial Report,” <i>Hansard</i>, 6 July 1948, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1948/jul/06/actuarial-report. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the Act linked so closely to the Beveridge Report?","a":"Because it carried out the report's plan for social insurance, which Beveridge presented as an attack on Want within a wider programme against Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The coalition's White Paper of 1944 had already accepted its broad conclusions, and Labour completed the work after 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it take until 1948 to start the scheme?","a":"Because the Ministry of National Insurance was new and at first had no staff, buildings or procedures, so a thousand new offices had to be created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than six thousand units of the old approved societies that paid sickness benefit also had to be wound up and their work absorbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did many people still need means-tested help after 1948?","a":"Because the leading insurance benefits remained lower than national assistance rates for many claimants, so about 800,000 people previously on assistance stayed on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beveridge's rates had been based on prewar patterns of spending, and inflation had eroded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"National Insurance Act 1946"},
    "ww2-993": {"questions":["In 1947 veterans made up almost half of all college admissions in the United States, most of them studying on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Congress had passed in 1944.","Local control written into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the insistence of Mississippi's John Rankin let officials steer many Black veterans away from colleges and home loans."],"abstract":"The <b>GI Bill</b>, formally the Servicemen's Readjustment Act of 1944, was the American law that gave returning Second World War veterans money for college or training, unemployment pay and guaranteed loans for homes, farms and businesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> While the war was still being fought, the Department of Labor estimated that 15 million servicemen and women could be unemployed after it, and planners feared a postwar depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fate of First World War veterans, who had received little more than a $60 allowance and a train ticket home and had marched on Washington in 1932 for their bonuses, added to the urgency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Legion designed its main features, its former national commander Harry Colmery drafted the first version, and a Legion publicist named it 'the GI Bill of Rights'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress nearly deadlocked over paying unemployed veterans $20 a week, but Roosevelt signed the bill on 22 June 1944, just days after the invasion of Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the peak year of 1947 veterans made up 49 per cent of college admissions, and by the time the original programme ended on 25 July 1956, 7.8 million of the 16 million veterans had used its education or training benefits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> By 1994, a congressional resolution recorded, the home loan programmes it began had guaranteed more than 14 million loans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The law's language was race-neutral, but John Rankin of Mississippi, a staunch segregationist who chaired the committee that drafted it, ensured that local officials controlled the distribution of benefits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many Black veterans in the South were steered into vocational schools or denied benefits outright, and in the summer of 1947 only two of some 3,000 VA home loans in Mississippi went to Black veterans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One estimate holds that 28 per cent of white veterans went to college on the bill against 12 per cent of Black veterans, and in 2021 members of Congress proposed restoring benefits to the families of those denied them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, “Servicemen’s Readjustment Act (1944),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/servicemens-readjustment-act. [Open access]","United States Department of Veterans Affairs, “Born of Controversy: The GI Bill of Rights,” https://www.va.gov/opa/publications/celebrate/gi-bill.pdf. [Open access]","<i>Servicemen’s Readjustment Act of 1944</i>, Public Law 78-346, 58 Stat. 284 (22 June 1944), GovInfo, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-58/pdf/STATUTE-58-Pg284.pdf. [Open access]","Franklin D. Roosevelt, “Statement on Signing the G.I. Bill,” 22 June 1944, American Presidency Project, University of California, Santa Barbara, https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/statement-signing-the-gi-bill. [Open access]","Joseph Thompson, “GI Bill Opened Doors to College for Many Vets, but Politicians Created a Separate One for Blacks,” <i>The Conversation</i>, November 2019, https://theconversation.com/gi-bill-opened-doors-to-college-for-many-vets-but-politicians-created-a-separate-one-for-blacks-126394. [Open access]","Shannon Luders-Manuel, “The Inequality Hidden within the Race-Neutral G.I. Bill,” <i>JSTOR Daily</i>, 18 September 2017, https://daily.jstor.org/the-inequality-hidden-within-the-race-neutral-g-i-bill. [Open access]","Aaron Morrison, “Not All WWII Veterans Given Full GI Bill Benefits: New Effort to Address,” Associated Press, 11 November 2021, via NBC Washington, https://www.nbcwashington.com/news/national-international/not-all-wwii-veterans-given-full-gi-bill-benefits-new-effort-address/2879117/. [Open access]","United States Senate, “The 50th Anniversary of the Servicemen’s Readjustment Act of 1944,” S.J. Res. 202, <i>Congressional Record</i> 140, no. 80 (22 June 1994), https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-1994-06-22/html/CREC-1994-06-22-pt1-PgS62.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did planners fear the return of the soldiers?","a":"Because the Department of Labor expected some 15 million returning servicemen and women to be unemployed, and planners feared a new depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The memory of First World War veterans, sent home with $60 and a train ticket and later driven from Washington in 1932, made Congress anxious to do better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Black veterans gain so much less from it?","a":"Because John Rankin, who chaired the drafting committee, made sure that local officials controlled the benefits, and in the South many of those officials were white segregationists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They pushed Black veterans into vocational training instead of colleges, or denied benefits and home loans altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it remembered as such an important law?","a":"Because it put higher education within reach of millions of veterans, about 2.3 million of whom attended colleges and universities within 7 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its loan guarantees helped make home ownership, once out of reach for most Americans, a common part of postwar life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"G.I. Bill"},
    "ww2-994": {"questions":["A wall of 4,048 gold stars at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Washington recalls the more than 400,000 Americans who died in the war.","Critics sued to stop the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, arguing that its site damaged the historic Mall, until Congress in 2001 ordered it built at the Rainbow Pool regardless."],"abstract":"The <b>National World War II Memorial</b> on the National Mall in Washington honours the 16 million Americans who served in the armed forces during the war and the millions more who supported it at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plaza around the restored Rainbow Pool is ringed by 56 granite columns for the states, the District of Columbia and the territories of the time, with two pavilions recording the war's theatres and campaigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Freedom Wall of 4,048 gold stars, the wartime symbol of a family's sacrifice, recalls the more than 400,000 Americans who died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Representative Marcy Kaptur of Ohio first introduced legislation for the memorial on 10 December 1987, and President Clinton signed the law authorising it on 25 May 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American Battle Monuments Commission was made responsible for it, and Friedrich St. Florian, an Austrian-born architect teaching in Rhode Island, won the design competition in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The choice of site proved bitterly contested, for the Rainbow Pool, approved in 1995, lay on the Mall's central axis between the Lincoln Memorial and the Washington Monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 2000 a federal advisory council found that the design had serious and unresolved adverse effects on the historic character of the Mall, and civic groups, including a veterans' coalition, sued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Congress answered in 2001 with a law ordering the memorial to be built expeditiously at the Rainbow Pool site, and construction ran from September 2001 to April 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It opened to the public on 29 April 2004 and was dedicated on 29 May before about 150,000 people, many of them veterans of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> President George W. Bush received it on behalf of the American people as a tribute open and expansive like America, and the Park Service presents it as a monument to the Greatest Generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["National Park Service, “History & Culture,” World War II Memorial, https://www.nps.gov/wwii/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “World War II Memorial,” https://www.nps.gov/wwii/index.htm. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Timeline,” National World War II Memorial Registry, https://wwiiregistry.abmc.gov/timeline/. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Memorial Dedication,” National World War II Memorial Registry, https://wwiiregistry.abmc.gov/memorial-dedication/. [Open access]","National Mall Coalition, “WWII Memorial in the Media: The Controversy,” https://www.nationalmallcoalition.org/wwii-memorial-in-the-media-the-controversy/. [Open access]","National Mall Coalition, “Photo Gallery: History of National World War II Memorial,” August 2017, https://www.nationalmallcoalition.org/2017/08/photo-gallery-history-of-national-world-war-ii-memorial. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Design,” National World War II Memorial Registry, https://wwiiregistry.abmc.gov/design/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the memorial's site so controversial?","a":"Because the Rainbow Pool lay on the Mall's main axis between the Lincoln Memorial and the Washington Monument, one of the most sensitive places in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A federal advisory council found in 2000 that the design harmed the historic character of the Mall, and several groups went to court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Congress intervene in 2001?","a":"Because a lawsuit and a procedural question about the planning commission's approval were delaying construction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Congress passed a law directing that the memorial be built expeditiously at the Rainbow Pool site, consistent with the approvals already given, and President Bush signed it in May 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Freedom Wall carry gold stars?","a":"Because during the war a gold star was the symbol of a family's sacrifice, and the stars on the wall commemorate the more than 400,000 Americans who gave their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Park Service describes the wall as a reminder of the supreme sacrifice that made victory possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"World War II Memorial"},
    "ww2-995": {"questions":["The Park Service presents the National World War II Memorial in Washington as a tribute to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a label critics say bathes the war years in a golden haze.","By 2026 only about 30,600 of the 16.4 million Americans who served in the war survived, as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> celebrated in books and films passed from living memory."],"abstract":"The <b>Greatest Generation</b> is the name popularised by the NBC journalist Tom Brokaw for the Americans who grew up in the Great Depression and fought or worked through the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His book <i>The Greatest Generation</i> of 1998, built from some fifty sketches of veterans and their families, became an instant bestseller and spent more than 80 weeks on the <i>New York Times</i> list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belonged to a wider wave of celebration that included Studs Terkel's ironically titled oral history <i>The Good War</i> of 1984, Stephen Ambrose's books and Steven Spielberg's <i>Saving Private Ryan</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name has entered official memory, for the National Park Service describes the National World War II Memorial as a tribute to the Greatest Generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the memorial's dedication in 2004 the chairman of the American Battle Monuments Commission greeted his audience as his fellow members of the greatest generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Critics have argued that the label bathes the men and women of that era in a golden haze, and that America's memory of a 'last good war' bears too little resemblance to the lived experience of those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One reviewer in 2000 called the book a largely unironic appreciation, under the headline 'The Sappiest Generation'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army's wartime surveys of more than half 1 million soldiers, now published online, have been welcomed as a blow against the sanitised version of the war that such books promoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scholars describe the comfortable assumptions of 'the good war' and 'the greatest generation' as myths that have prompted a lengthening list of conflicting narratives and gainsayers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The generation itself is passing, for by 2026 only about 30,600 of the 16.4 million Americans who served in the war were still alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Sean Elder, “The Sappiest Generation,” <i>Salon</i>, 31 July 2000, https://www.salon.com/2000/07/31/generation/. [Open access]","Richard Parker, “David Nasaw’s Unsparing Tour of America’s World War II and Its Aftermath,” <i>The Nation</i>, 16 December 2025, https://www.thenation.com/article/society/world-war-fascism-democracy-military/. [Open access]","Samuel Goldman, “The Myth of the Greatest Generation,” <i>The Week</i>, 23 December 2021, https://theweek.com/feature/opinion/1008390/how-great-was-the-greatest-generation. [Open access]","Michael Snape, “Introduction,” in <i>God and Uncle Sam: Religion and America’s Armed Forces in World War II</i>, Cambridge Core, published online 2 March 2024, https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/97625C5B06F8C62E4AFBD1F49D66E3B7. [Paywalled]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” World War II Memorial, https://www.nps.gov/wwii/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Memorial Dedication,” National World War II Memorial Registry, https://wwiiregistry.abmc.gov/memorial-dedication/. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “WWII Veteran Statistics,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/wwii-veteran-statistics. [Open access]","Virginia Tech, <i>The American Soldier in World War II</i>, https://americansoldierww2.org/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Brokaw's book strike such a chord?","a":"Because it offered a straightforward appreciation of the men and women who survived the Depression and fought the war, told through dozens of personal sketches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It arrived amid a wider celebration of the war in books and films, from Ambrose's histories to <i>Saving Private Ryan</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do historians call the idea a myth?","a":"Because, they argue, the golden image bears a weak resemblance to the lived experience of the war years and passes over the war's human costs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wartime army surveys, in which soldiers said what they actually thought, are cited as a corrective to that sanitised picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why has the phrase become part of official remembrance?","a":"Because it gave a ready name to the generation honoured by the National World War II Memorial, which the Park Service presents as a tribute to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speakers at the memorial's dedication in 2004 used it to address the veterans in the crowd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Greatest Generation"},
    "ww2-996": {"questions":["Jeremy Isaacs won approval for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> series within a day by warning Thames that the BBC might otherwise make its own history of the Second World War first.","Hitler's secretary Traudl Junge and Albert Speer were among the witnesses interviewed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> series, a television history made with the help of the Imperial War Museum."],"abstract":"The <b>World at War</b> is a British documentary series of 26 one-hour episodes on the Second World War, made by Thames Television for ITV and first broadcast on Wednesday 31 October 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the idea of the producer Jeremy Isaacs, who proposed it in the autumn of 1970, and it was made in co-operation with the Imperial War Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Over 4 years a team working in 18 countries searched 3 million feet of archive film, much of it unseen since the war, and shot a further million feet of interviews and location film.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The historian Noble Frankland was responsible for checking its historical accuracy, and Carl Davis wrote the score.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Laurence Olivier narrated it, although after the first episode word came that he would do no more, and he had to be won back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its interviews were central, ranging from Hitler's secretary Traudl Junge, Albert Speer and Karl Dönitz to ordinary soldiers and civilians caught up in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The episodes moved from the rise of Nazi Germany through the campaigns and the home fronts to an episode on genocide and a last one called 'Remember'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thames made it after the BBC's success with <i>The Great War</i> in 1964, and Isaacs won approval within 24 hours by warning that the BBC might get there first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It cost about £1 million, won awards including an International Emmy, was repeated on BBC2 in 1994 and 2000, and had a companion book by Mark Arnold-Forster published in 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> 50 years after its first showing it was still being streamed, and one critic writing in 2023 called it the greatest documentary series ever made for television.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Museum of Broadcast Communications, “World at War, The,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Television</i>, https://www.museum.tv/tv-encyclopedia-19/world-at-war-the. [Open access]","Sinclair McKay, “The World at War Is the Greatest Documentary Series Ever Made,” <i>The Spectator Australia</i>, 18 October 2023, https://www.spectator.com.au/?p=10792261. [Paywalled]","Military History Matters, “Military Classics: Jeremy Isaacs’ The World at War (1973),” 12 November 2010, https://www.military-history.org/feature/world-war-2/the-world-at-war-review.htm. [Open access]","Berkeley Art Museum and Pacific Film Archive, catalogue record for <i>The World at War</i>, no. 11, “Red Star: Soviet Union, 1941–1943” (Thames Television, 1973), https://bampfa.org/node/187493. [Open access]","Mark Arnold-Forster, <i>The World at War</i> (London: Collins, 1973), Internet Archive, https://archive.org/details/worldatwar0000arno. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum Library, catalogue record for <i>The World at War</i> (Thames; FremantleMedia; History Channel), https://collections.ushmm.org/search/catalog/bib128921. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Thames commit to such an expensive series?","a":"Because the BBC had shown with <i>The Great War</i> in 1964 how successful a long television history could be, and ITV was under pressure from its regulators to make serious programmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Isaacs warned that the BBC might move first, and the project was approved within a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the interviews so valuable?","a":"Because they recorded witnesses from every level of the war while many were still alive, from Traudl Junge, Speer and Dönitz to soldiers and civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only a small part of the material gathered was used, and the unused interviews later supplied further programmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the series still matter as a source of memory?","a":"Because it shaped how a generation of viewers understood the war, combining archive film with eyewitness testimony across 26 episodes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has never gone out of circulation, and was still being streamed half a century after it was first shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"The World at War"},
    "ww2-997": {"questions":["Poland's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 2018 threatened up to 3 years in prison for accusing the Polish nation of complicity in Nazi crimes, until an outcry abroad forced a retreat.","Russia's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 2014 against the 'rehabilitation of Nazism' has been used to prosecute people who said Stalin shared responsibility for the outbreak of the war."],"abstract":"A <b>memory law</b> is a law that prescribes an official interpretation of past events, and in the narrow sense one that punishes certain kinds of speech about history, such as denying the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a broader sense the term also covers constitutional preambles, parliamentary resolutions and days of commemoration that set out a state's view of its own past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hard core of such laws grew from the Holocaust denial laws passed in West Germany, Israel and France, and was later extended to other subjects such as the Armenian genocide and colonialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> France's Gayssot Act of 1990 made it a crime to contest the existence of crimes against humanity as defined by the International Military Tribunal at Nuremberg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2008 the European Union required its members to punish publicly condoning, denying or grossly trivialising the crimes defined in the charter of that tribunal when aimed at a group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Laws about the Second World War have also been used to defend national myths, and scholars argue that many reinforce popular narratives of national martyrdom, glory and innocence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2018 Poland made it a crime, punishable by up to 3 years in prison, to accuse the Polish nation or state of complicity in Nazi crimes, provoking an outcry in Israel and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> 5 months later the government moved to make the offence a civil rather than a criminal matter, calling the change a correction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia's criminal code has banned the 'rehabilitation of Nazism' since 2014, and the article has been used against people who said that Stalin's Soviet Union shared responsibility for the outbreak of the war through its pact with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since the full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, scholars have pointed to Russia's mobilisation around the mythology of the Great Patriotic War as the most extreme case of the past put to political use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Anna Wójcik and Paula Rhein-Fischer, “Introduction to the Special Section ‘Memory Laws and the Rule of Law’,” <i>European Constitutional Law Review</i> 19, no. 4 (2023): 591–601, https://doi.org/10.1017/S1574019624000014. [Open access]","European Observatory on Memories, “Memory Laws in Europe: What Common Horizons Are We Journeying Towards?,” <i>Observing Memories</i>, 11 March 2022, https://europeanmemories.net/magazine/?p=1275. [Open access]","Council of the European Union, <i>Council Framework Decision 2008/913/JHA of 28 November 2008 on Combating Certain Forms and Expressions of Racism and Xenophobia by Means of Criminal Law</i>, art. 1, EUR-Lex, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex:32008F0913. [Open access]","BBC News, “Poland Holocaust Law: Government U-Turn on Jail Threat,” 27 June 2018, https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-44627129. [Open access]","Asser Institute, “[Interview] Andrii Nekoliak: ‘The Politics of Memory Laws Can Be Intense’,” https://asser.nl/news/interview-andrii-nekoliak-the-politics-of-memory-laws-can-be-intense. [Open access]","Mischa Gabowitsch, review of <i>Memory Laws, Memory Wars: The Politics of the Past in Europe and Russia</i>, by Nikolay Koposov, <i>Laboratorium: Russian Review of Social Research</i> 11, no. 1 (2019): 186–90, https://soclabo.org/index.php/laboratorium/article/download/867/2244/6708. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do critics see memory laws as a danger to free speech?","a":"Because by prescribing a state-approved interpretation of history they limit the rights and freedoms of individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In states sliding away from the rule of law they can reinforce simplified myths of national martyrdom and innocence and polarise society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Poland's law of 2018 cause such an outcry?","a":"Because Israel and the United States, two of Poland's key allies, saw it as an attempt to deny historical truth and to muzzle testimony and research about the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within months the government moved to turn the offence from a criminal into a civil one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the Russian law described as a misuse of the war?","a":"Because the ban on rehabilitating Nazism has been used to prosecute those who point to the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, and later those who condemned Russia's war on Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Scholars link this to the state's mobilisation of the mythology of the Great Patriotic War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Memory law"},
    "ww2-998": {"questions":["Accusations of 'apologetic tendencies' in writing about the Nazi era, printed in <i>Die Zeit</i> in July 1986, opened a second front in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among West German intellectuals.","Fought in the feuilletons of 1986 and 1987, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned on whether the Holocaust was singular or one in a long series of modern state atrocities."],"abstract":"The <b>Historikerstreit</b>, German for ‘historians' quarrel’, was a public dispute in West Germany in 1986 and 1987 over how to interpret the Nazi past, and above all whether the Holocaust was unique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began on 6 June 1986 with an article in the <i>Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung</i> by the historian Ernst Nolte entitled 'The Past That Will Not Pass'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nolte asked whether the Gulag was not more original than Auschwitz, and whether the Bolsheviks' 'class murder' was not the logical and factual precondition of the Nazis' 'race murder'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In <i>Die Zeit</i> on 11 July 1986 the philosopher Jürgen Habermas accused Nolte, the historian Andreas Hillgruber and Michael Stürmer, an adviser to Chancellor Kohl, of apologetic tendencies in writing recent history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hillgruber had published a short book that year setting the destruction of Europe's Jews beside the collapse of the eastern front and the expulsion of Germans from the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The quarrel was fought in the culture pages of the national press for months and drew in many leading scholars and journalists, all of them men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belonged to a wider struggle for political and cultural hegemony, after Kohl had used the politics of history, as in the controversial visit with Ronald Reagan to the military cemetery at Bitburg, where SS men lay, in May 1985.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Critics condemned Nolte and his supporters for trying to relativise the Nazi crimes and above all the 'final solution', and Habermas is generally seen as having successfully resisted that relativisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The dispute also revived old complaints about too much or too little coming to terms with the past, and gave a political edge to calls to 'historicise' the Nazi era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Habermas hoped that Germans would develop a constitutional patriotism instead of a conventional national identity, and 40 years on the quarrel is still invoked in arguments over German memory culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Jürgen Habermas, “A Kind of Settlement of Damages: The Apologetic Tendencies in German History Writing” (11 July 1986), with abstract, <i>German History Intersections</i>, German Historical Institute Washington, https://germanhistory-intersections.org/en/knowledge-and-education/ghis:document-151. [Open access]","Andrew I. Port, “Holocaust Scholarship and Politics in the Public Sphere: Reexamining the Causes, Consequences, and Controversy of the Historikerstreit and the Goldhagen Debate,” <i>Central European History</i> 50, no. 3 (2017): 375–403, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0008938917000826. [Open access]","Michael Brenner, “Rückblende 1986: Der »Historikerstreit«,” <i>Jüdische Allgemeine</i>, 13 August 2013, https://www.juedische-allgemeine.de/politik/1986-der-historikerstreit. [in German] [Open access]","Peter Dudek, “„Vergangenheitsbewältigung“: Zur Problematik eines umstrittenen Begriffs,” <i>Aus Politik und Zeitgeschichte</i>, no. 1–2 (1992), Bundeszentrale für politische Bildung, https://www.bpb.de/shop/zeitschriften/apuz/archiv/535899/vergangenheitsbewaeltigung-zur-problematik-eines-umstrittenen-begriffs/. [in German] [Open access]","Matheo Malik, “En Allemagne, la mémoire ne protège plus la démocratie,” <i>Le Grand Continent</i>, 5 June 2026, https://legrandcontinent.eu/fr/2026/06/05/allemagne-memoire-democratie/. [in French] [Open access]","Meron Mendel, “Über den Verfassungspatriotismus hinaus: Für die Pluralisierung der Erinnerungskultur 40 Jahre nach dem Historikerstreit,” <i>Blätter für deutsche und internationale Politik</i>, no. 6 (2026), https://www.meronmendel.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/mend2606.pdf. [in German] [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Nolte's article cause such outrage?","a":"Because it questioned the singularity of the Nazi genocide, suggesting that the Nazis' crimes had a model and a cause in the Bolsheviks' earlier 'class murder'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics read this as an attempt to relativise the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the dispute about politics as well as scholarship?","a":"Because it was part of a struggle for cultural hegemony in West Germany, in which Chancellor Kohl, with Stürmer as an adviser, sought to use history to overcome what he called a moral crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bitburg visit of 1985 had already shown how charged the politics of the past had become.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the quarrel still discussed today?","a":"Because it shaped how Germans think about the singularity of the Holocaust and about their relationship to the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> 40 years on, writers ask whether the constitutional patriotism Habermas hoped for still serves a more diverse society's memory of the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Historikerstreit"},
    "ww2-999": {"questions":["Known by the code name Oneg Shabbat after its organisers' secret Saturday meetings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered diaries, posters and interview reports inside the Warsaw ghetto.","Workmen preparing a building site in Warsaw on 1 December 1950 dug up two milk cans holding the second part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, buried in February 1943."],"abstract":"The <b>Ringelblum Archive</b>, the Underground Archive of the Warsaw Ghetto, is a collection of documents gathered in secret by the Oneg Shabbat group to record the fate of Polish Jews under German occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The historian Emanuel Ringelblum began it as a chronicle in October 1939, and after the ghetto was sealed in November 1940 it became an organised underground operation with several dozen contributors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its code name, Oneg Shabbat, meaning ‘joy of the Sabbath’, came from the group's secret Saturday meetings, held under the cover of the Jewish Social Self-Help organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The group collected official records, newspapers, posters, food stamps, letters and diaries, wrote reports from interviews and surveys, and tried to give a complete and objective picture of events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When news of the mass murder of Jews reached Warsaw in 1942 it turned to documenting the destruction of whole communities, and its reports reached the West through Polish and Jewish organisations that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The teacher Izrael Lichtensztajn and two former pupils buried the first part in ten tin boxes beneath a school at 68 Nowolipki Street on 3 August 1942, during the great deportations to Treblinka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second part went into two milk cans in February 1943, and a third was buried on 18 April 1943, the day before the ghetto uprising began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the archive's staff were murdered, Ringelblum and his family among them, but two survivors led searchers to the first cache, which was recovered on 18 September 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Builders preparing a housing estate found the milk cans on 1 December 1950, while the third part has never been found despite later searches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Listed on UNESCO's Memory of the World register in 1999, its roughly 25,000 pages, compiled by people who nearly all perished, are regarded as the most important source for the history of Polish Jews in the Holocaust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “One of the Ten Metal Boxes in Which Portions of the Ringelblum Oneg Shabbat Archives Were Hidden,” photograph record with event history, https://collections.ushmm.org/search/catalog/pa1147069. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Warsaw,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","Anna Majchrowska, “68th Anniversary of Discovering the Second Part of the Ringelblum Archive,” Jewish Historical Institute, Warsaw, https://www.jhi.pl/en/articles/68th-anniversary-of-discovering-the-second-part-of-the-ringelblum-archive,404. [Open access]","Jewish Historical Institute, “About the Ringelblum Archive,” https://www.jhi.pl/en/research/the-ringelblum-archive-and-the-oneg-shabbat-group/about-the-ringelblum-archive. [Open access]","UNESCO, “Warsaw Ghetto Archives (Emanuel Ringelblum Archives),” Memory of the World Register, 1999, https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/warsaw-ghetto-archives-emanuel-ringelblum-archives. [Open access]","Yad Vashem, “One of Ten Metal Containers in Which the ‘Oneg Shabbat’ Archive Was Hidden,” Artifacts Collection, https://yadvashem.org/artifacts/museum/oneg-shabbat-archive. [Open access]","About Holocaust, “What Was the Ringelblum Archive and How Was It Found Hidden in Milk Cans after the War?,” https://aboutholocaust.org/en/facts/what-was-the-ringelblum-archive-and-how-was-it-found-hidden-in-milk-cans-after-the-war. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Oneg Shabbat work in such secrecy?","a":"Because documenting German crimes was dangerous, and the inhabitants of the ghetto themselves did not know of the group's work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It met on Saturdays and used the Jewish Social Self-Help, a welfare body the Germans tolerated, as its cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the group change its purpose in 1942?","a":"Because when the first news of the mass murder of Jews reached Warsaw, a broad study of Jewish life in Poland gave way to recording the destruction of communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It passed copies to the Polish underground, and its reports on the Holocaust reached the West in 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the documents buried in different containers?","a":"Because the archivists feared that the ghetto would be destroyed, so they hid the collection in stages as deportations and the uprising approached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The airtight milk cans of 1943 kept their contents in better condition than the tin boxes found in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ringelblum Archive"},
    "ww2-1000": {"questions":["Work on an international criminal court, requested by the UN in 1948 and stalled through the Cold War, finally produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which entered into force in 2002.","The preamble of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recalls that millions of children, women and men were victims of unimaginable atrocities during the 20th century."],"abstract":"The <b>Rome Statute</b> is the treaty, adopted in Rome on 17 July 1998, that created the International Criminal Court, the world's first permanent international criminal court, with its headquarters in The Hague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It traces its lineage to the Second World War, for it was in the wake of that war's atrocities that international criminal justice had its foundational period, with the military tribunals at Nuremberg and Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those tribunals were set up by the victors, and at Nuremberg each of the four Allied powers supplied one judge and a team of prosecutors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When the General Assembly adopted the Genocide Convention in 1948 it also directed that work begin on a statute for an international criminal court, work that led half a century later to the Rome Statute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The International Law Commission produced drafts in 1951 and 1953, but the divisions of the Cold War left the project in stalemate for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Only after the Cold War, amid the atrocities in the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda, did states return to it, and Secretary-General Kofi Annan recalled that many had thought the horrors of the Second World War could never happen again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The conference adopted the statute by 120 votes to 7, with 21 abstentions, and it entered into force on 1 July 2002 after its 60th ratification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its preamble recalls that millions of children, women and men had been victims of unimaginable atrocities during the century, and it gives the court jurisdiction over genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike the temporary tribunals, the court is complementary to national courts, which have the first duty to investigate and prosecute international crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The treaty's register lists 137 signatories and 125 states parties, and the court presents itself as a court of last resort that seeks to complement, not replace, national justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Giuseppe Nesi, “Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court,” introductory note, United Nations Audiovisual Library of International Law, https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/rsicc/rsicc.html. [Open access]","<i>Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court</i>, Rome, 17 July 1998, preamble and art. 5, International Criminal Court, https://www.icc-cpi.int/sites/default/files/RS-Eng.pdf. [Open access]","United Nations Treaty Collection, “Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court,” status, chap. XVIII.10, https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?src=TREATY&mtdsg_no=XVIII-10&chapter=18. [Open access]","International Criminal Court, “The Court,” https://www.icc-cpi.int/about/the-court. [Open access]","United Nations, “Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court: Overview,” https://legal.un.org/icc/general/overview.htm. [Open access]","William A. Schabas, “Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide,” introductory note, United Nations Audiovisual Library of International Law, https://legal.un.org/avl/ha/cppcg/cppcg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, United States Department of State, “The Nuremberg Trial and the Tokyo War Crimes Trials (1945–1948),” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nuremberg. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did it take 50 years after Nuremberg to create the court?","a":"Because although the General Assembly asked for a draft statute in 1948 and the International Law Commission produced drafts in 1951 and 1953, the rivalries of the Cold War blocked further progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only the atrocities in the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda in the 1990s revived the project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a permanent court thought better than tribunals set up case by case?","a":"Because creating an ad hoc tribunal for each conflict raises the question of selective justice, and many crimes against humanity since 1945 had gone unpunished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A permanent court could operate more consistently, and the likelihood of prosecution could itself act as a deterrent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the court defer to national courts?","a":"Because of the principle of complementarity, under which it is first and foremost the duty of states to investigate and prosecute international crimes at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court describes itself as a court of last resort that complements national courts instead of replacing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Rome Statute"},
    "ww2-992": {"questions":["Hugh Dalton told the Commons in October 1946 that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Home Civil Service would henceforth be abolished, though dismissed wives gained no right to return.","In the United States the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against married teachers and office workers was virtually abandoned in the 1950s, once limiting the supply of labour grew too costly."],"abstract":"A <b>marriage bar</b> was a rule that barred married women from certain jobs, so that a single woman had to leave on marrying and a married woman could not be hired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the United States such bars were adopted by firms and school boards from about 1900 to 1950, and at their height they affected more than half of all office workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Britain married women were shut out of established posts in the Civil Service, even though the Sex Disqualification (Removal) Act of 1919 said that no one should be disqualified by sex or marriage from a public function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Ireland a ban introduced in 1932 required women primary teachers who qualified after that date to retire on marriage, and it lasted until 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Such bars rested on the belief that a man should earn enough to keep his family and that married women ought not to compete with him for work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Second World War shook them, for married women were needed again, and in Victoria many returned to teaching as temporary staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain's Education Act of 1944 provided that no woman could be disqualified from teaching in a state school, or dismissed, by reason only of marriage, and the BBC dropped its bar the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 15 October 1946 the Chancellor, Hugh Dalton, announced that the marriage bar in the Home Civil Service would henceforth be abolished, though without reinstating women already forced out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attitudes changed more slowly than rules, for a Treasury document of 1947 still called married women a perfect nuisance and many women expected to leave work when they married.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the United States the bars were virtually abandoned in the 1950s when the cost of limiting the labour supply rose sharply, while Victoria's teachers won their own act only in 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Vicky Iglikowski, “‘A Perfect Nuisance’: The History of Women in the Civil Service,” <i>History of Government</i> blog, The National Archives, 26 May 2015, https://history.blog.gov.uk/2015/05/26/a-perfect-nuisance-the-history-of-women-in-the-civil-service. [Open access]","<i>Education Act 1944</i>, 7 & 8 Geo. 6, c. 31, s. 24, legislation.gov.uk, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Geo6/7-8/31/section/24/enacted. [Open access]","House of Commons, “Civil Service (Marriage Bar Abolition),” <i>Hansard</i>, 15 October 1946, vol. 427, cols. 794–96, https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1946/oct/15/civil-service-marriage-bar-abolition. [Open access]","Judith Harford and Jennifer Redmond, “‘I Am Amazed at How Easily We Accepted It’: The Marriage Ban, Teaching and Ideologies of Womanhood in Post-Independence Ireland,” <i>Gender and Education</i> (2019), https://doi.org/10.1080/09540253.2019.1680807, author copy at https://mural.maynoothuniversity.ie/id/eprint/12975/1/JR_I%20am%20amazed.pdf. [Open access]","Donna Dwyer, “Justice at Last? The Temporary Teachers’ Club and the Teaching Service (Married Women) Act 1956,” <i>Labour History</i> (2006), Victoria University Research Repository, https://vuir.vu.edu.au/2965/. [Open access]","Claudia Goldin, “Marriage Bars: Discrimination against Married Women Workers, 1920’s to 1950’s,” NBER Working Paper 2747 (Cambridge, MA: National Bureau of Economic Research, 1988), https://www.nber.org/papers/w2747. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were married women barred from these jobs?","a":"Because of the belief that men's wages should support a family, which made it seem inappropriate for married women to hold paid work when men were unemployed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the United States the bars also suited firms that promoted from within and paid salaries by length of service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war help to end the bars?","a":"Because wartime labour shortages brought married women back into work, as when married temporary teachers returned to Victorian schools in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Britain the bars for teachers and at the BBC fell in 1944 and in the Home Civil Service in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did abolition not bring equality at once?","a":"Because officials and women alike kept old assumptions, and a Treasury paper of 1947 still described married women as a perfect nuisance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 1946 decision also gave no right of return to women who had already been made to resign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Marriage bar"},
    "ww2-921": {"questions":["More than a third of the Australians held by the Japanese died, while only 242 of the 8,591 Australians taken as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Europe did not come home.","The Red Cross's Central Agency in Geneva, which tried to trace every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for anxious families, held 36 million index cards by June 1947."],"abstract":"A <b>prisoner of war</b> is a member of the armed forces held captive by an enemy power, and in the Second World War millions of soldiers, sailors and airmen passed into captivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Geneva Convention of 1929 required that prisoners be humanely treated and protected against violence, insults and public curiosity, and it forbade reprisals against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It allowed captors to put able prisoners to work according to their rank, though officers could not be compelled to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Red Cross delegates inspected the camps, which ranged from a few dozen men to tens of thousands, and the Red Cross's Central Agency in Geneva gathered 36 million index cards on prisoners and the missing by June 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mass escapes embarrassed the captors, yet many prisoners felt they had done their duty and resented the punishments that followed every attempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> What happened to a prisoner depended above all on who held him, and the differences were stark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the 231,000 British and American prisoners held by the Germans, about 8,300, or 3.6 per cent, died in German custody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of some 5.7 million Soviet soldiers who fell into German hands, about 3.3 million, roughly 57 per cent, were dead by the end of the war, and German officials excused this by pointing out that the Soviet Union had not signed the Geneva Convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Tokyo trial found that as many as 27 per cent of the 132,000 American and British prisoners held by Japan died, and more than one in three of the 22,000 Australians it captured, about 8,000, did not survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Australians held by the Germans and Italians fared far better, for 242 of the 8,591 captured in Europe died, while the 77 American nurses taken in the Philippines all survived until liberation in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Nazi Persecution of Soviet Prisoners of War,” <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-persecution-of-soviet-prisoners-of-war. [Open access]","Daqing Yang, “Documentary Evidence and the Studies of Japanese War Crimes: An Interim Assessment,” in Edward Drea et al., <i>Researching Japanese War Crimes Records: Introductory Essays</i> (Washington, DC: National Archives and Records Administration for the Nazi War Crimes and Japanese Imperial Government Records Interagency Working Group, 2006), 31–32, https://www.archives.gov/files/iwg/japanese-war-crimes/introductory-essays.pdf. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Stolen Years: Australian Prisoners of War – Prisoners of the Japanese,” https://www.awm.gov.au/visit/exhibitions/stolenyears/ww2/japan. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Australian Prisoners of War: Second World War – Prisoners in Europe,” https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/pow/ww2. [Open access]","“Convention between the United States of America and Other Powers, Relating to Prisoners of War; July 27, 1929,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva02.asp. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Report of the International Committee of the Red Cross on Its Activities during the Second World War (September 1, 1939 – June 30, 1947)</i>, vol. 1, <i>General Activities</i> (Geneva: ICRC, 1948), 231–32, https://archive.org/details/ReportOfTheInternationalCommitteeOfTheRedCrossOnItsActivitiesDuringTheSecondWorl. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Report of the International Committee of the Red Cross on Its Activities during the Second World War (September 1, 1939 – June 30, 1947)</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Central Agency for Prisoners of War</i> (Geneva: ICRC, 1948), https://archive.org/details/ReportOfTheInternationalCommitteeOfTheRedCrossOnItsActivitiesDuringTheSecondWorl. [Open access]","Kimberly Guise, “Nurse POWs: Angels of Bataan and Corregidor,” The National WWII Museum, 5 May 2021, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/nurse-pows-bataan-and-corregidor. [Open access]","Dianne Rutherford, “An Officer’s First Duty,” <i>Wartime</i> 52, Australian War Memorial, https://www.awm.gov.au/wartime/52/rutherford_officer_first_duty. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so many Soviet prisoners die in German hands?","a":"Because the Nazis treated Soviet prisoners as racial enemies, and second only to the Jews they were the largest group of victims of Nazi racial policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German officials excused their ill treatment and murder by pointing out that the Soviet Union had not signed the Geneva Convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were prisoners of the Japanese so likely to die?","a":"Because they suffered their captors' indifference and brutality, and many were forced to work on projects such as the Thai–Burma railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over a thousand Australian prisoners died when Allied submarines torpedoed the unmarked ships carrying them around the Japanese empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Red Cross keep so many index cards?","a":"Because families often had no news of men missing after battles and no way of knowing whether they were prisoners, wounded or dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Central Agency gathered official lists and enquiries, and its index grew from seven million cards in the First World War to 36 million by June 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Prisoner of war"},
    "ww2-922": {"questions":["Labour detachments of the German <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> numbered VIII B sent prisoners down Silesian mines, conditions against which delegates of the International Committee of the Red Cross protested.","Run by the Luftwaffe for captured Allied airmen, the camp called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Luft III became famous for the mass breakout known as the Great Escape."],"abstract":"A <b>Stalag</b> was a German prisoner-of-war camp for enlisted men in the Second World War, a word that American Red Cross bulletins of the time explained as 'base camp'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Camps for officers were called Oflags, and the Luftwaffe ran its own camps for captured airmen, such as Stalag Luft I and Stalag Luft III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany identified its camps only by such names followed by a Roman numeral or letter, and for reasons of military security it would not tell the International Committee of the Red Cross exactly where they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A camp could hold anything from a few dozen men to tens of thousands, and the largest were real towns with streets, police and cleaning services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prisoners of many nations passed through them, among them Australians captured in Greece, Crete and North Africa who were moved to camps in Germany, Austria and Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Geneva Convention of 1929 a captor could put able prisoners to work according to their rank, but officers could not be compelled to work, so the men of a Stalag laboured while those of an Oflag did not have to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Most prisoners of a Stalag were in fact spread across labour detachments, or Kommandos, on farms and in factories and mines, linked to their base camp by camp newspapers and visits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Red Cross delegates protested at what they found, such as men of Stalag VIII B housed at a pithead near Gleiwitz amid noxious gases, and men of Stalag 344 at Lamsdorf doing heavy work 10 and 11 hours a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Food, clothing and medicines came through the Red Cross, which in 1944 shipped food packages from Geneva to meet the urgent needs of American prisoners at Stalag III B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prisoners were taught that it was their duty to try to escape, and the mass escape from Stalag Luft III became famous as 'the Great Escape'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Report of the International Committee of the Red Cross on Its Activities during the Second World War (September 1, 1939 – June 30, 1947)</i>, vol. 1, <i>General Activities</i> (Geneva: ICRC, 1948), 231–32, 305–6, 328–30, https://archive.org/details/ReportOfTheInternationalCommitteeOfTheRedCrossOnItsActivitiesDuringTheSecondWorl. [Open access]","American National Red Cross, <i>Prisoners of War Bulletin</i> 1, no. 5 (October 1943), https://archive.org/details/PrisonersOfWarBulletinVol.15November1943. [Open access]","American National Red Cross, <i>Prisoners of War Bulletin</i> 2, no. 10 (October 1944), https://archive.org/details/PrisonersOfWarBulletinVol.210October1944. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Australian Prisoners of War: Second World War – Prisoners in Europe,” https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/encyclopedia/pow/ww2. [Open access]","Dianne Rutherford, “An Officer’s First Duty,” <i>Wartime</i> 52, Australian War Memorial, https://www.awm.gov.au/wartime/52/rutherford_officer_first_duty. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Great Escapes: Remarkable Second World War Captives,” exhibition, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/whats-on/exhibitions/great-escapes/. [Open access]","“Convention between the United States of America and Other Powers, Relating to Prisoners of War; July 27, 1929,” Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/geneva02.asp. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did enlisted men work while officers did not?","a":"Because the Geneva Convention of 1929 allowed a captor to use the labour of able prisoners according to their rank and aptitude, but excepted officers, who were to be given suitable work only if they asked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Non-commissioned officers could be required to do only supervisory work unless they asked otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the camps known only by code names?","a":"Because the belligerents, for reasons of military security, gave the Red Cross's Central Agency only coded addresses such as 'Oflag' or 'Stalag' with a number or letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The committee had to delete any mention of a camp's location from its delegates' reports before passing them to governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did not every prisoner welcome escape attempts?","a":"Because many felt they had already risked their lives and done their duty, and every prisoner suffered when the guards responded to escapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Germans increased security and parades and removed privileges such as mail and the life-saving Red Cross parcels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Stalag"},
    "ww2-923": {"questions":["Led to Spain by the Comet Line, evading airmen were among those the British agency <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was created to bring home from occupied Europe.","Some 1,750,000 cloth escape maps, many printed by the board-game maker Waddington, were produced for the British agency <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to help servicemen evade capture."],"abstract":"<b>MI9</b>, Military Intelligence Section 9, was the secret British agency set up in December 1939 to help servicemen evade capture in enemy territory and to help prisoners of war escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its aims were to get trained men back, to tie down enemy manpower on guard duties, to bring evaders home, to gather information, to deny information to the enemy and to keep up the morale of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lectured servicemen on how to evade capture and what to do if taken, stressing that it was everyone's duty to evade capture or, failing that, to try to escape and rejoin their unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also designed equipment for the purpose, such as maps, compasses and survival kits, most of it the work of Clayton Hutton, who called the map 'the escaper's most important accessory'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1942 an American delegation studied its work, and the Americans then set up a similar organisation, MIS-X, which worked with MI9 in supplying equipment to servicemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its maps were printed on silk, or later on rayon and tissue paper, because they could be hidden in small places and survive wear and immersion in water, and the British produced about 1,750,000 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many were printed by John Waddington, the maker of Monopoly and other board games, before a military survey unit took over the printing in August 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> MI9 also devised codes, and prisoners hid messages in their letters home, which reached the agency and carried intelligence out of the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its escape reports include that of Airey Neave, who on 5 January 1942 escaped from Colditz Castle in German uniform and became the first British officer to get away from it successfully.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Evaders also owed their freedom to civilian escape lines in occupied Europe, such as the Comet Line, founded in Brussels by Andrée de Jongh and financed by the British, which led Allied servicemen to Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Dianne Rutherford, “Second World War Escape and Evasion Maps,” Australian War Memorial, 8 July 2015, https://www.awm.gov.au/articles/blog/second-world-war-escape-and-evasion-maps. [Open access]","Dianne Rutherford, “An Officer’s First Duty,” <i>Wartime</i> 52, Australian War Memorial, https://www.awm.gov.au/wartime/52/rutherford_officer_first_duty. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Great Escapes: Remarkable Second World War Captives,” exhibition, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/whats-on/exhibitions/great-escapes/. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Escaping Colditz,” https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/explore-the-collection/explore-by-time-period/second-world-war/escaping-colditz/. [Open access]","Australian War Memorial, “Escape Map,” REL38439, Memorial Box 4, https://www.awm.gov.au/learn/memorial-boxes/4/object-list/escape-map. [Open access]","Veterans Affairs Canada, “Help from the Comet Line,” video and transcript, https://www.veterans.gc.ca/en/news-and-media/video-gallery/help-comet-line. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did MI9 want prisoners to keep trying to escape?","a":"Because an escaper brought back a trained serviceman and, just as importantly, forced the enemy to keep extra men on guard duties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mass escapes were particularly embarrassing for the Germans, though every prisoner suffered when security was tightened and privileges were removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were escape maps printed on cloth?","a":"Because silk, and later rayon, could be hidden in small places and would survive wear and tear and immersion in water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Maps on cloth could be easily concealed and still be used after rough treatment in enemy territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did prisoners send intelligence home to MI9?","a":"Because MI9 had devised codes in the early years of the war, prisoners could write coded messages inside ordinary letters to their families, which were passed on to the agency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its escape reports, now in the National Archives, record escapes such as Airey Neave's from Colditz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"MI9"},
    "ww2-924": {"questions":["Led by Colonel Oveta Culp Hobby, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent its members to North Africa, Europe, the Southwest Pacific and Asia as clerks, telephone operators, mechanics and weather observers.","Formed in November 1944, the 6888th Central Postal Directory Battalion of Black and Latina women of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cleared a backlog of 17 million pieces of mail for troops overseas."],"abstract":"The <b>Women's Army Corps</b> was the women's branch of the United States Army in the Second World War, created on 1 July 1943, when the president signed the law converting the Women's Army Auxiliary Corps into part of the Army itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its forerunner, the auxiliary corps, had been set up by a law of 15 May 1942 that grew out of a bill introduced by Congresswoman Edith Nourse Rogers of Massachusetts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The auxiliaries served with the Army rather than in it, with lower pay than men of comparable rank and without the overseas pay, insurance and protection as prisoners of war that soldiers received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oveta Culp Hobby, a Texas newspaper editor, was sworn in as its first director on 16 May 1942 and led the Corps as a colonel after the conversion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first trainees reached Fort Des Moines, Iowa, on 20 July 1942, and the recruiting goal of 25,000 for the first year was passed so quickly that the ceiling was raised to 150,000 in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> More than 150,000 women served in the Corps during the war, and it reached a peak strength of about 100,000, some 40,000 of them with the Army Air Forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A recruiting booklet of 1944 boasted that its members did 239 Army jobs, from switchboards and weather reports to laboratory tests and maps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They served in North Africa, the Mediterranean, Europe, the Southwest Pacific, China, India, Burma and the Middle East, the first unit reaching Eisenhower's headquarters in Algiers on 27 January 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Black women served in segregated units, and in November 1944 the 6888th Central Postal Directory Battalion of Black and Latina women was formed, which cleared a backlog of 17 million pieces of mail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A slander campaign in 1943 hurt recruiting badly, and when the war ended members of the Corps were released on an interim score of 44 points, married women at their own request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Army Center of Military History, <i>The Women’s Army Corps: A Commemoration of World War II Service</i>, CMH Pub 72-15 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1993), https://archive.org/details/TheWomensArmyCorps. [Open access]","Mattie E. Treadwell, <i>The Women’s Army Corps</i>, United States Army in World War II: Special Studies (1954; repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1991), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-8. [Open access]","War Department, <i>A Book of Facts about the WAC, Women’s Army Corps</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1944), https://archive.org/details/ABookOfFactsAboutTheWac. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Women’s Army Corps (WAC),” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/womens-army-corps-wac-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Army Heritage Center Foundation, “Supporting the Nation: The Women’s Army Auxiliary Corps &amp; Women’s Army Corps,” https://www.armyheritage.org/soldier-stories-information/supporting-the-nation-the-womens-army-auxiliary-corps-womens-army-corps/. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, “Object 56: Life Magazine Story on the WAACs,” <i>VA History</i>, https://department.va.gov/history/object-56-waac/. [Open access]","Major John C. Sparrow, <i>History of Personnel Demobilization in the United States Army</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-210 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, July 1952), 314–15, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-210. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army want women in uniform at all?","a":"Because General Marshall believed the two-front war would cause a manpower shortage, and the Army could not afford the time and money to train men in skills such as typing and switchboard work that many women already had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hobby argued that every woman doing such work would free a man for combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the auxiliary corps turned into the Women's Army Corps?","a":"Because the auxiliaries lacked the pay, benefits and legal protection of soldiers, which limited their service overseas, and Army leaders asked to make the corps part of the Army itself rather than merely serving with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The law making that change was signed on 1 July 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did recruiting fall in 1943?","a":"Because a sudden backlash against women in the armed forces swept the country, with rumours about the morals of the auxiliaries spread by servicemen and in the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A sample of soldiers' letters mentioning the corps found that 84 per cent were unfavourable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Women's Army Corps"},
    "ww2-925": {"questions":["When Corregidor fell in May 1942, 66 women of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became prisoners of the Japanese, and all survived to be freed from Santo Tomas in 1945.","A law of June 1944 finally gave members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> temporary commissions with the full pay and privileges of Army officers of their grade."],"abstract":"The <b>Army Nurse Corps</b> is the nursing branch of the United States Army Medical Department, made a permanent corps by the Army Reorganization Act of 2 February 1901 after contract nurses had served in the Spanish–American War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For decades its members held an uncertain status, with relative rank from 1920 but without the full pay and privileges of officers of comparable grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There were fewer than 7,000 Army nurses on active duty when the United States entered the Second World War, and the Corps expanded to a peak of more than 57,000 in August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Florence Blanchfield, its chief from 1943 to 1947, it remained a corps of women only, and when the Women's Army Corps was formed its members were the first women other than nurses to serve in the Army's ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A law of 22 June 1944 gave Army nurses temporary commissions with full pay and privileges, and the Army–Navy Nurses Act of 1947 made their commissioned rank permanent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nurses followed the troops ashore, arriving at Anzio 5 days after the landings of January 1944 and in Normandy 4 days after D-Day, and serving in field hospitals as far forward as the Battle of the Bulge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Corregidor fell in May 1942, 66 Army nurses became prisoners of the Japanese, and with the Navy nurses taken in the Philippines all 77 survived until liberation in February 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sixteen Army nurses died by enemy action during the war, among them six killed when a suicide aircraft struck the hospital ship Comfort off Okinawa on 28 April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Black nurses were admitted only in small numbers, served in segregated hospitals and were often assigned to care for German prisoners of war, which troubled many of them deeply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In January 1945 the Secretary of War even asked the president to draft nurses, but by May enough had volunteered to meet the expected needs of the war against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Army Center of Military History, <i>Highlights in the History of the Army Nurse Corps</i> (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1981), DTIC ADA232470, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA232470. [Open access]","Mary M. Roberts, <i>The Army Nurse Corps, Yesterday and Today</i> ([Washington, DC]: U.S. Army Nurse Corps, 1957), https://archive.org/details/TheArmyNurseCorpsYesterdayAndToday. [Open access]","Kimberly Guise, “Nurse POWs: Angels of Bataan and Corregidor,” The National WWII Museum, 5 May 2021, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/nurse-pows-bataan-and-corregidor. [Open access]","U.S. Army Center of Military History, <i>The Women’s Army Corps: A Commemoration of World War II Service</i>, CMH Pub 72-15 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1993), https://archive.org/details/TheWomensArmyCorps. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Celebrate Nurses Week with the Military Nurse,” <i>The Unwritten Record</i>, 11 May 2021, https://unwritten-record.blogs.archives.gov/2021/05/11/celebrate-nurses-week-with-the-military-nurse/. [Open access]","Army Heritage Center Foundation, “World War II: Army Nurse Corps,” https://www.armyheritage.org/soldier-stories-information/world-war-ii-army-nurse-corps/. [Open access]","Military Women’s Memorial, <i>And Still, They Served: Black Servicewomen in World War II</i>, lesson plan (2021), https://womensmemorial.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/And-Still-They-Served-Lesson-Plan2.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Army nurses given commissions in 1944?","a":"Because their relative rank had never carried the full rights of officers, such as base pay equal to that of officers of comparable grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Public Law 350 of 22 June 1944 granted temporary commissions with full pay and privileges for the duration of the emergency and 6 months beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the nurses held in the Philippines all survive?","a":"Because their leaders, Chief Nurse Maude Davison and her deputy Josie Nesbit, kept them working to a regular duty schedule caring for the internees, which gave them purpose and discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were held with civilians at Santo Tomas and Los Baños until American forces freed them in February 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Black nurses resent their assignments?","a":"Because they were often posted to segregated hospitals or to camps treating German prisoners of war rather than wounded Americans, and lived apart from white nurses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Many Black nurses were refused entry to the nurse corps at all, though no rule formally limited their numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"United States Army Nurse Corps"},
    "ww2-926": {"questions":["Nearly 24,000 men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with no grave but the sea are named on the Second World War extension of the Tower Hill Memorial in London.","From May 1941 the Essential Work Order gave seamen of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> continuous employment, a reserve pool between ships and pay while waiting for a posting."],"abstract":"The <b>Merchant Navy</b> is the name for Britain's civilian ships and the officers and men who crew them, which in the Second World War served as the supply service behind the Royal Navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ships carried troops and supplies to the armies, raw materials to the munitions factories and food and fuel to Britain itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In wartime it numbered roughly 185,000 seamen, about 40,000 of them Indian, Chinese and of other nationalities, the many Indian seamen being known as lascars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the war employment at sea was precarious, men moved in and out of the service freely, and until 1941 the law did little to stop seamen leaving the sea for jobs ashore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From May 1941 the Essential Work Order changed that, giving seamen continuous employment, a reserve pool through which about 60 per cent of them passed, and pay while on leave or waiting for a ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The losses were heavy from the start: out of a serving population that cannot have averaged more than 145,000 British seamen, nearly 6,000 were killed by enemy action in 1940 and over 7,000 in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By one count 4,786 merchant ships were lost and 32,000 sailors killed in the war, and the deaths registered by the Registrar General list more than 50,000 merchant seamen who died during and just after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those with no grave but the sea are named on the Tower Hill Memorial in London, whose Second World War extension, designed by Sir Edward Maufe, commemorates almost 24,000 people, while a memorial at Halifax remembers some 2,200 Canadian merchant seamen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From 1941 serving seamen were entered in a new Central Register of Seamen, and a Roll of Honour lists those who died in service or were declared missing, presumed dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Merchant seamen received the same campaign stars as the armed forces, including the Atlantic Star for service between 3 September 1939 and 8 May 1945, and their deaths were recorded in official returns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["C. B. A. Behrens, <i>Merchant Shipping and the Demands of War</i>, History of the Second World War: United Kingdom Civil Series (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1955), https://archive.org/details/merchant-shipping-demands-of-war. [Open access]","Veterans Affairs Canada, “Tower Hill Memorial,” https://www.veterans.gc.ca/en/remembrance/memorials/overseas/tower-hill-memorial. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “Research Guide C10: The Merchant Navy: World War Two,” https://www.rmg.co.uk/collections/research-guides/research-guide-c10-merchant-navy-world-war-two. [Open access]","Royal Museums Greenwich, “VE Day 2020: Searching Second World War Merchant Navy Records,” 8 May 2020, https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/maritime-history/library-archive/ve-day-2020-searching-second-world-war-merchant-navy. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Merchant Navy Seamen in Service since 1918,” research guide, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/merchant-seamen-serving-since-1918/. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Merchant Navy Campaign Medal Records 1939–1945,” research guide, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/help-with-your-research/research-guides/merchant-seamens-campaign-medal-records-1939-1945/. [Open access]","Veterans Affairs Canada, “Merchant Navy Memorial,” National Inventory of Canadian Military Memorials, https://veterans.gc.ca/en/remembrance/memorials/canada/merchant-navy-memorial. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Essential Work Order such a change for seamen?","a":"Because before it employment at sea was precarious, and nothing stopped men leaving for safer jobs ashore unless they were of military age and liable to be called up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order gave continuous employment, held men in a reserve pool between ships and paid them while on leave or waiting for a posting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are so many merchant seamen named on a memorial instead of a grave?","a":"Because most were lost at sea when their ships were sunk, so they have no grave but the sea, and the Tower Hill Memorial's Second World War extension names almost 24,000 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their deaths, including those 'missing supposed killed', were recorded in the official returns now held at Greenwich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did so many merchant seamen die?","a":"Because men from sunk ships were often lost at sea, like the engineer of the SS Fort Longueuil recorded in 1943 as 'missing supposed killed, drowned or died of exposure'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Out of perhaps 145,000 serving British seamen, nearly 6,000 were killed by enemy action in 1940 and over 7,000 in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Merchant Navy (United Kingdom)"},
    "ww2-927": {"questions":["From June 1943 a report had to be filed within 48 hours whenever an American aircraft and its crew were declared destroyed or <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by their commanders.","Nearly 72,000 Americans of the Second World War are still listed as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or otherwise unaccounted for, and their names stand on walls at overseas cemeteries."],"abstract":"<b>Missing in action</b> is the casualty status given to a serviceman whose fate after an action is unknown, and in the American Army of 1941 it stood with killed and wounded as one of the three facts a casualty report had to record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A man reported missing might be a prisoner, wounded, sick or dead, and the Red Cross's Central Agency in Geneva worked to trace such men for their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its card index, which had held seven million cards at the end of the First World War, contained 36 million by the end of June 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A year after the fall of France in 1940 its French section still had 40,000 names of missing men whose families had heard nothing and who appeared on no German list of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Commonwealth dead with no known grave are named on memorials such as Runnymede, which records 20,450 airmen, and Groesbeek, for soldiers who fell between the Seine and the end of the war in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> For American air crews the War Department required, from 11 June 1943, a Missing Air Crew Report within 48 hours of an aircraft being declared destroyed or missing, recording the last known circumstances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 16,605 such case files survive, created so that later information could be matched against them to settle the fate of the men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Graves Registration units buried the dead with one identification tag on the body and the other on the grave marker, and the postwar search identified nearly 97 per cent of the recovered dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every American missing or lost at sea is named on Walls of the Missing at an overseas cemetery or memorial, such as the more than 1,700 names at Margraten, and a rosette is added when one is found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly 72,000 Americans remain unaccounted for from the Second World War, and the search for them, using archives and DNA from relatives, still goes on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, <i>Missing Air Crew Reports (MACRs) of the U.S. Army Air Forces, 1942–1947</i>, microfiche publication M1380, descriptive pamphlet (Washington, DC: NARA, 2005), https://www.archives.gov/files/research/microfilm/m1380.pdf. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “Defense POW/MIA Accounting Agency,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/topics/defense-powmia-accounting-agency. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Graves Registration Service in World War II,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/graves-registration-service-world-war-ii. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Identifying Our Missing: February 2017 Identifications,” 6 March 2017, https://www.abmc.gov/news-events/news/identifying-our-missing-february-2017-identifications/. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Five Things You May Not Know about Netherlands American Cemetery,” 18 December 2023, https://www.abmc.gov/news-events/news/five-things-you-may-not-know-about-netherlands-american-cemetery/. [Open access]","Veterans Affairs Canada, “Runnymede Memorial,” https://www.veterans.gc.ca/en/remembrance/memorials/overseas/runnymede-memorial. [Open access]","Veterans Affairs Canada, “Groesbeek Memorial,” https://www.veterans.gc.ca/en/remembrance/memorials/overseas/groesbeek-memorial. [Open access]","International Committee of the Red Cross, <i>Report of the International Committee of the Red Cross on Its Activities during the Second World War (September 1, 1939 – June 30, 1947)</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Central Agency for Prisoners of War</i> (Geneva: ICRC, 1948), https://archive.org/details/ReportOfTheInternationalCommitteeOfTheRedCrossOnItsActivitiesDuringTheSecondWorl. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the air forces need a special report for missing crews?","a":"Because a study of how the fate of airmen missing in the First World War had been determined found the methods and sources inadequate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new report recorded the last known circumstances of a lost crew so that information gathered later could be matched against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could families wait so long for news?","a":"Because many missing men were not shown on the official lists, so relatives had no means of knowing whether they were prisoners, wounded, sick or dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Red Cross's Central Agency questioned prisoners from the same units to trace men missing after big engagements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the return of the dead bring no closure to some families?","a":"Because their servicemen remained missing in action, either never recovered or among the unknowns who could not be identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their names stay on the Walls of the Missing even after they are found, with a rosette to show that they are no longer missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Missing in action"},
    "ww2-928": {"questions":["Maintained by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Netherlands American Cemetery at Margraten holds some 8,300 American dead and names more than 1,700 missing on its Wall of the Missing.","Families who chose to leave their dead overseas after 1947 entrusted the graves to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which keeps American military cemeteries abroad in perpetuity."],"abstract":"The <b>American Battle Monuments Commission</b> is the agency of the United States government that builds and cares for American military cemeteries and memorials abroad, set up by a law that President Warren G. Harding signed on 4 March 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first chairman, General John J. Pershing, gave it the motto 'Time will not dim the glory of their deeds', and it first built eight cemeteries and eleven monuments for the Americans of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Second World War it grew to include fourteen cemeteries for that war, designed in the same spirit, and today it cares for 24 cemeteries and 25 monuments, memorials and markers around the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also built the World War II Memorial and the Korean War Veterans Memorial on the National Mall in Washington, which are now in the care of the National Park Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the war the Graves Registration Service collected, identified and buried the American dead in temporary cemeteries, keeping isolated graves to a minimum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Return of the Dead Program the next of kin could choose burial in a permanent American military cemetery overseas, return home for burial in a national or private cemetery, or burial in a foreign homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than 60 per cent of families chose to bring their dead home, and of some 280,000 identified American dead of the war, 171,000 were returned to the United States at their families' request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first war dead from Europe reached the United States on 26 October 1947, while those who stayed abroad rest in cemeteries maintained in perpetuity by the commission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Netherlands American Cemetery at Margraten, begun as a temporary cemetery in November 1944, now holds about 8,300 service members and names more than 1,700 missing on its Wall of the Missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Local people began adopting its graves in 1945, and a small bronze rosette beside a name on the wall marks a missing man whose remains have since been found and identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["American Battle Monuments Commission, “Honoring the Service, Achievements, and Sacrifices of U.S. Armed Forces for 90 Years,” https://www.abmc.gov/news-events/news/honoring-service-achievements-and-sacrifices-us-armed-forces-90-years/. [Open access]","American Battle Monuments Commission, “Five Things You May Not Know about Netherlands American Cemetery,” 18 December 2023, https://www.abmc.gov/news-events/news/five-things-you-may-not-know-about-netherlands-american-cemetery/. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, National Cemetery Administration, <i>America’s World War II Burial Program</i>, World War II 75th Commemorative Series (Washington, DC, 2020), https://www.cem.va.gov/docs/wcag/history/WWII-Burial-Program-America.pdf. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Graves Registration Service in World War II,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/graves-registration-service-world-war-ii. [Open access]","National Park Service, “World War II Burials in Gettysburg National Cemetery,” https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/world-war-ii-burials-in-gettysburg-national-cemetery.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, “Object 6: 1948 Repatriation Album of Alaska World War II Dead,” <i>VA History</i>, https://department.va.gov/history/100-objects/object-6-repatriation-album/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do so many American war dead lie overseas?","a":"Because after the war the next of kin were given the choice, and the dead of families who did not bring them home were left in permanent overseas cemeteries maintained in perpetuity by the commission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than 60 per cent chose instead to bring their dead home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the commission set up after the First World War?","a":"Because families were offered a choice between bringing their dead home and burying them among their comrades in tended government cemeteries overseas, and those cemeteries needed a permanent body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The law of 4 March 1923 created it, and it went on to supervise eight cemeteries and eleven monuments from that war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a rosette appear beside some names on the Wall of the Missing?","a":"Because the walls list service members whose remains could not be recovered or identified, and when such a person is later found, a small bronze rosette is placed by the name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It shows that he is no longer considered missing in action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"American Battle Monuments Commission"},
    "ww2-929": {"questions":["Inaugurated by the Post Office Department in June 1942, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> service carried more than a billion letters between home and the forces overseas before it ended in November 1945.","Because about 1,600 letters fitted on one roll of film, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut the weight of military mail by some 98 per cent and freed space on crowded transports."],"abstract":"<b>V-mail</b>, short for 'Victory mail', was an American wartime postal service in which letters to and from members of the armed forces overseas were photographed onto microfilm, flown out and printed again for the reader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was modelled on Britain's Airgraph system, and the Post Office Department inaugurated it on 15 June 1942 in cooperation with the War and Navy Departments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The writer used a special lightweight form that was both letter and envelope, wrote in dark ink, dark pencil or type, and could enclose nothing with the message.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Members of the forces could post it free by writing 'Free' on the envelope, while civilians paid 3 cents, or 6 cents for airmail to the V-mail station.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than a billion V-mail letters were delivered before the service ended on 1 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The point of the system was to cut the weight and bulk of military mail, so that crowded ships and aircraft could carry other cargo instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 1,600 letters fitted on a roll of 16-millimetre film 30 metres (100 feet) long, which with its carton weighed about 200 grams (7 ounces) against about 17 kilograms (37 pounds) for the same number of ordinary letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Censors read every letter before it was filmed, and because the sheet was to be photographed, sensitive words had to be blacked out rather than cut out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the station of destination the film was printed as small photographs about 10 by 13 centimetres (4 by 5 inches), and the originals were kept until every copy was known to have been made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mail was treated as a lifeline for morale, and the files of the Army Postal Service, set up in 1942, survive at the National Archives beside the V-mails kept in collections such as the Library of Congress's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Historian, United States Postal Service, “V-Mail” (Washington, DC: United States Postal Service, July 2008), https://about.usps.com/who/profile/history/pdf/v-mail.pdf. [Open access]","War Department, <i>FM 12-105: Adjutant General’s Field Manual, Army Postal Service</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 7 May 1943), 53–54, https://archive.org/details/Fm12-105. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “Mail Call: V-mail,” 7 December 2019, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/mail-call-v-mail. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “Mail Call: Letters from the Archives,” 7 March 2018, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/mail-call-letters-archives. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Correspondence,” <i>From the Home Front and the Front Lines</i>, exhibition, Veterans History Project, https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/homefront/correspondence.html. [Open access]","Norfolk Record Office, “United States Army Air Force (USAAF) 2nd Air Division Memorial Library Archive,” online catalogue, https://nrocatalogue.norfolk.gov.uk/index.php/united-states-army-air-force-usaaf-2nd-air-division-memorial-library-archive. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Records of the Adjutant General’s Office, 1917– (Record Group 407),” <i>Guide to Federal Records in the National Archives of the United States</i>, 407.6.1, https://www.archives.gov/research/guide-fed-records/groups/407.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the letters microfilmed instead of sent as they were?","a":"Because the service existed to reduce the weight and bulk of military mail and so free space for vital cargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Army's manual reckoned that photographing the letters cut their weight by about 98 per cent, a roll of 1,600 letters weighing 7 ounces against 37 pounds for the same letters in ordinary form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were writers told to use dark ink and to enclose nothing?","a":"Because every sheet was to be photographed, and faint or small writing did not reproduce well on the reduced copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The form was a single combined letter and envelope that went through the filming machine, so nothing could be enclosed with the message.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the government care so much about soldiers' mail?","a":"Because officials believed that frequent contact with home strengthened fortitude and made loneliness endurable, as the 1942 report to the Postmaster General put it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For those serving far away a letter was a lifeline, and waiting for the mail was a constant preoccupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"V-mail"},
    "ww2-930": {"questions":["Enlisted men needed 85 points under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when releases began in May 1945, while women of the Women's Army Corps needed only 44.","Veterans overseas resented the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because newly arrived fathers could outscore men who had fought for years but had no children."],"abstract":"The <b>Adjusted Service Rating Score</b>, which soldiers called the point system, was the method by which the United States Army decided who would be sent home first after the defeat of Germany in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> General George Marshall, who had seen the disorderly demobilisation that followed the First World War, set planners to work on it in July 1943, and the system was announced in September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A soldier earned one point for each month in the Army and another for each month overseas, five for each campaign, each decoration for merit or valour and each Purple Heart, and twelve for each dependent child, up to three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scores counted as of 12 May 1945, and an interim score of 85 points was fixed for enlisted men of the ground, air and service forces, with separate critical scores to be worked out for each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system had been designed for men and gave particular weight to combat service and children, so a separate score of 44 points was set for the Women's Army Corps at its director's request.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In theory the scheme was fair, but in practice it was an administrative tangle, and veterans overseas resented the points that newly arrived fathers won for their children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After Japan's surrender demobilisation sped up, and by 19 September 1945 more than 15,200 men were leaving the Army every day, a rate Truman promised would keep rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ships of every kind joined the sealift called Operation Magic Carpet, which began on 6 September 1945 and brought millions of Americans home over the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The required score fell from 85 to 50 by the end of 1945, but an announced slowdown in January 1946 set off soldiers' protests around the world, the largest in Manila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Army ended the point system in June 1946 in favour of discharging soldiers after 2 years' service, and when demobilisation officially ended on 30 June 1947 the Army had shrunk from 8 million soldiers to 684,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Major John C. Sparrow, <i>History of Personnel Demobilization in the United States Army</i>, Department of the Army Pamphlet 20-210 (Washington, DC: Department of the Army, July 1952), 314–15, https://archive.org/details/PAM20-210. [Open access]","Tyler Bamford, “The Points Were All That Mattered: The US Army’s Demobilization after World War II,” The National WWII Museum, 27 August 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/points-system-us-armys-demobilization. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “‘Home Alive by ’45’: Operation Magic Carpet,” 2 October 2020, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/operation-magic-carpet-1945. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Statement by the President Concerning Demobilization of the Armed Forces,” 19 September 1945, <i>Public Papers of the Presidents</i>, Harry S. Truman Library, https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/138/statement-president-concerning-demobilization-armed-forces. [Open access]","Mattie E. Treadwell, <i>The Women’s Army Corps</i>, United States Army in World War II: Special Studies (1954; repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1991), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-8. [Open access]","Kevin Phillip Anastas, “Demobilization and Democratizing Discipline: The Doolittle Board and the Post World War II Response to Criticism of the United States Army” (MA thesis, Duke University, 1983), DTIC ADA128437, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA128437. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army not simply send whole divisions home?","a":"Because after the First World War divisions had gone home as complete units, and by 1945 the older divisions held many soldiers who had arrived as replacements only in the last months of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Army needed objective criteria to release men fairly, or it risked protests from restless soldiers with no enemy left to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were children worth so many points?","a":"Because the scheme gave twelve points for each dependent child, up to three, alongside points for service, campaigns and decorations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It gave particular weight to combat service and to children, which is why the Women's Army Corps needed a separate score.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did soldiers protest in January 1946?","a":"Because an official press release announced a slowdown in demobilisation, and soldiers already bored and frustrated took to the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thousands demonstrated in London, Paris, Germany and the Philippines, demanding to go home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Adjusted Service Rating Score"},
    "ww2-931": {"questions":["Under the doctrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the invasion fleet was to be shattered by mass suicide attacks at sea and the defenders were to make their decisive stand on the beaches.","To meet an expected landing on Kyushu, almost 8,000 aircraft had been hoarded by the end of June 1945 for the decisive battle planned under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Japan's high command."],"abstract":"<b>Ketsu-Go</b>, the ‘decisive operation’, was the Japanese plan of 1945 for the final defence of the home islands against an Allied invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Imperial General Headquarters issued it to the field commands on 8 April 1945 as Army Order No. 1299, dividing the defence into seven operations from the Kuriles and Hokkaido to Kyushu and Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The main effort was to fall on the Kanto plain around Tokyo and on Kyushu, where emergency preparations were to be finished by early June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its doctrine was to destroy the invasion fleet at sea with mass special attacks, the suicide tactics called 'tokko', and then to make the decisive stand on the beaches rather than inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 23 June a National Volunteer Service Act ordered volunteer units to be formed in every prefecture, to labour for the army and, if need be, to fight beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Americans' own plan, Downfall, aimed to land on southern Kyushu on 1 November 1945 and on the Kanto plain early in 1946, and a costly invasion of the home islands seemed likely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the end of June almost 8,000 aircraft, mostly for special attack, had been hoarded for the decisive battle, while some 325 airstrips were being rushed to completion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Decoded Japanese messages showed the build-up on Kyushu outrunning every forecast, with eleven combat divisions confirmed there and some 534,000 troops estimated by 2 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some historians have used that intelligence to argue that by early August American planners were so concerned that they were reconsidering their invasion plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the surrender Japan still had some 2,000,000 troops and over 9,000 aircraft in the home islands, a force that Ketsu-Go never used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (1966; facsimile repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1994), chaps. 18–19, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","Douglas J. MacEachin, <i>The Final Months of the War with Japan: Signals Intelligence, U.S. Invasion Planning, and the A-Bomb Decision</i> (Washington, DC: Center for the Study of Intelligence, Central Intelligence Agency, 1998), https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/The-Final-Months-of-the-War-With-Japan-Monograph.pdf. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1 July 1946), 26, https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “The War Enters Its Final Phase, 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/war_1945.htm. [Open access]","Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: The Campaigns of MacArthur in the Pacific</i>, vol. 1 (1966; facsimile repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1994), 395–98, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurTheCampaigns. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Ketsu-Go concentrate on Kyushu and the Kanto plain?","a":"Because those were the areas where an American landing was expected, and the plan placed the emphasis on the Kanto area and on Kyushu, with Kyushu to be ready first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The priority given to Kyushu drained almost all the material strength of the military, so it was likely to be the only area where a decisive battle could be supported at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the plan insist on fighting at the beaches?","a":"Because experience in the Philippines and on Okinawa had shown that Japanese forces could not match American firepower once it was ashore, so the doctrine called for the defenders to make their decisive stand on the beach and coastal plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mass special attacks were to destroy as many landing craft as possible before they arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Kyushu build-up alarm American planners?","a":"Because intercepted communications showed far more Japanese divisions arriving than the estimates presented to Truman in June had allowed for, so the predicted cut-off of reinforcements had not happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 2 August the estimate of military manpower on Kyushu had reached 534,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-932": {"questions":["Badly wounded by army rebels in the uprising of 26 February 1936, the former grand chamberlain <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was saved by his wife, who shielded him with her body.","At the cabinet meeting of 13 August 1945 Prime Minister <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that the Allied reply gave a dim hope in the dark of preserving the emperor."],"abstract":"<b>Kantarō Suzuki</b> was a Japanese admiral and statesman, born in Osaka on 18 January 1868, who was prime minister in the last months of the Second World War and presided over Japan's decision to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A graduate of the Naval Academy in 1887, he fought in the war with China and in the Battle of the Japan Sea against Russia, and rose to command the Combined Fleet and, in 1925, to head the Naval General Staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1929 he became grand chamberlain, a post of advice to the throne, and on 26 February 1936 he was badly wounded by army rebels who saw him as a moderate influence around the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His life was saved by his wife, who shielded his body after he was shot, and he afterwards held no post but membership of the Privy Council, becoming its president late in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His cabinet resigned after accepting the Potsdam Declaration, and he died on 17 April 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 April 1945 the Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal, Marquis Kido, installed the 77-year-old admiral as prime minister, believing that he alone had the conviction and courage to stand up to the military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also had the full trust of the emperor, while his moderate record gave the extremists few concrete charges to level against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In public he talked tough, and when journalists asked about the Potsdam Declaration he apparently said that his government could only ignore it, using the word 'mokusatsu'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Japanese government's apparent rejection of the declaration was followed by the atomic bombings and the Soviet entry into the war, which together led to Japan's acceptance on 14 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He presided at the imperial conference of 9 to 10 August, and on 13 August, working quietly with the peace party, he told the cabinet that the Allied reply gave a 'dim hope in the dark' of preserving the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, “SUZUKI Kantaro,” <i>Portraits of Modern Japanese Historical Figures</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/portrait/e/datas/113. [Open access]","Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (1966; facsimile repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1994), 672–73, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1 July 1946), 26, https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Outline, Part 1: Military Defeat and Efforts to Reform the Constitution,” <i>Birth of the Constitution of Japan</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/outline/01outline.html. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Potsdam and the Final Decision to Bomb (Potsdam, Germany, July 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/potsdam_decision.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was an old admiral chosen to lead Japan in 1945?","a":"Because Marquis Kido believed that Suzuki alone had the deep conviction and personal courage to stand up to the military and end the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had the emperor's full trust, and his moderate record gave extremists few concrete charges to level against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why had army rebels tried to kill him in 1936?","a":"Because, as grand chamberlain, he was seen as one of the moderate influences around the throne that the uprising of 26 February meant to eliminate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was also believed to have helped secure acceptance of the London naval treaty against the Navy General Staff and opposed military interference in politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did his answer to the Potsdam Declaration matter so much?","a":"Because his reported statement that the government could only ignore the declaration, and would fight to the bitter end, was read abroad as a rejection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Japanese government did ignore the declaration until the atomic bombings and the Soviet entry into the war forced its acceptance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Kantarō Suzuki"},
    "ww2-933": {"questions":["Split three against three over the Potsdam terms on the night of 9 August 1945, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was deadlocked until Emperor Hirohito himself broke the tie in favour of surrender.","Meeting without their secretaries in May 1945, the six members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> agreed to open talks with the Soviet Union to keep it out of the war."],"abstract":"The <b>Supreme Council for the Direction of the War</b>, the 'Big Six', was the small inner council that directed Japan's war policy from August 1944 until the surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was formed on 5 August 1944 under Prime Minister Kuniaki Koiso, replacing the older Liaison Conference between Imperial General Headquarters and the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its six members were the prime minister, the foreign, army and navy ministers, and the chiefs of the army and navy general staffs, so that the cabinet and the high command sat together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Koiso hoped it would bring full coordination of civil and military policy, but it failed to do so, and he found he still could not obtain all the military information he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its importance came later, when it became the mechanism through which the question of surrender was eventually resolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1945, under Prime Minister Kantarō Suzuki, the six began meeting without their secretaries and agreed not to disclose what was said even to their subordinates, so that they could speak frankly about ending the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At meetings on 11, 12 and 14 May they agreed on early talks with the Soviet Union, aimed first of all at keeping it out of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the Potsdam Declaration the council split evenly, the prime minister, foreign minister and navy minister ready to accept, the army minister and the two chiefs of staff holding out for further conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hard-liners wanted several conditions, among them no occupation and disarmament by Japan itself, and no peace could be made without the consent of the military leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At the imperial conference on the night of 9 to 10 August the emperor himself broke the tie, and Japan offered to surrender on one condition concerning his position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (1966; facsimile repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1994), 670, 683, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1 July 1946), 26, https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Japan Surrenders, August 10–15, 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/surrender.htm. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Outline, Part 1: Military Defeat and Efforts to Reform the Constitution,” <i>Birth of the Constitution of Japan</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/outline/01outline.html. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Potsdam and the Final Decision to Bomb (Potsdam, Germany, July 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/potsdam_decision.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the secretaries shut out of the May meetings?","a":"Because the members came to believe that only restricted meetings would let the leaders of the government and the high command discuss ending the war frankly, and they agreed not to reveal what was said even to their immediate subordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The principle of restricted discussion was then kept up to the final surrender decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why could the council not agree on the Potsdam terms?","a":"Because it was split three against three: the prime minister, the foreign minister and the navy minister were prepared to accept, while the army minister and the two chiefs of staff wanted mitigating conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The split persisted exactly as before even after Hiroshima and the Soviet entry into the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the emperor's intervention matter so much?","a":"Because the vote was tied, and for the first time in a generation the emperor stepped forward from his normally ceremonial role to break it and order Japan to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The prime minister had used the fear of further atomic attacks to bring the emperor directly into the discussions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-934": {"questions":["Summoned at last to see Molotov on 8 August 1945, the Japanese ambassador <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was handed not an answer to Tokyo's peace approach but the Soviet declaration of war.","Intercepted and decoded by the Americans, the cables of the ambassador <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> warned that if Tokyo dithered over peace terms all Japan would be reduced to ashes."],"abstract":"<b>Naotake Satō</b> was a Japanese diplomat, born in Osaka on 30 October 1882, who as ambassador in Moscow carried Japan's attempt in 1945 to have the Soviet Union mediate an end to the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the Foreign Ministry in 1905 and served as envoy to Poland, as ambassador to Belgium and to France, and as foreign minister in 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1933 he walked out of the League of Nations with Yōsuke Matsuoka and others in protest at the resolution refusing to recognise Manchukuo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He became ambassador to the Soviet Union in 1942, and near the end of the war he urged the foreign minister, Shigenori Tōgō, to end it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the war he sat in the House of Councillors, chaired it from 1949 and worked for Japan's entry into the United Nations, and he died on 18 December 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In April 1945 Satō was told bluntly that Moscow meant to abrogate the Soviet–Japanese neutrality pact, which it said had 'lost its significance'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From May Japan's inner war council sought Soviet help, and in July Tōgō instructed him to tell the Soviet foreign minister, Molotov, of the emperor's private wish to send Prince Konoe to Moscow as a special envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Satō doubted the whole approach, holding back instructions he thought certain to be rejected and warning that without concrete proposals there was no point in seeing the Soviets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Americans read these cables through their interception of Japanese diplomatic messages, including his warning that if the government and the military dithered all Japan would be reduced to ashes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Molotov at last received him on the evening of 8 August, Moscow time, it was to hand him the Soviet declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, “SATO Naotake,” <i>Portraits of Modern Japanese Historical Figures</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/portrait/e/datas/413. [Open access]","Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (1966; facsimile repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1994), chap. 20, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1 July 1946), 26, https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Japan Surrenders, August 10–15, 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/surrender.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan hope the Soviet Union would mediate?","a":"Because the Soviet Union was not yet at war with Japan, and Japan's leaders hoped that talks might keep it out of the war and win its help in ending the war on terms better than unconditional surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tokyo did not know that the Soviets had already promised their allies to declare war on Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Satō so pessimistic about the approach?","a":"Because he judged that circumstances made a Soviet rejection a practical certainty, and he told Tokyo there was no point in meeting Molotov unless it had a concrete plan for ending the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He thought it unthinkable that Russia would ignore the Potsdam proclamation and then talk with a Japanese special envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How did the Americans know about the mediation attempt?","a":"Because the United States had long been intercepting and decoding Japanese diplomatic messages, and so knew that Japan was trying to use the Soviet Union as an intermediary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cables between Tōgō and Satō appeared in the 'Magic' diplomatic summaries prepared for American officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Naotake Satō"},
    "ww2-935": {"questions":["Without an interpreter, Truman told Stalin at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1945 that America had a new weapon of unusual destructive force, and Stalin showed little interest.","The ultimatum threatening Japan with prompt and utter destruction was issued on 26 July 1945 during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the United States, Britain and China, though not the Soviet Union."],"abstract":"The <b>Potsdam Conference</b> was the last wartime meeting of the Big Three, held at Potsdam near Berlin from 17 July to 2 August 1945 between Joseph Stalin, Harry Truman and Winston Churchill, replaced on 26 July by Clement Attlee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main business was defeated Germany, confirmed as a disarmed and demilitarised country under four zones of Allied occupation, with each occupying power to take reparations largely from its own zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The leaders also set up a Council of Foreign Ministers to prepare the peace treaties and agreed in principle that Königsberg and the area around it should pass to the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poland received a large area of German territory, and the conference declared that transfers of German populations should be carried out in an orderly and humane manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American diplomatic record of the conference includes Japanese peace feelers through the Soviet Union and the use of atomic weapons against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Japan was on the agenda too, since Stalin had promised to join the war against it by 15 August, and Truman learned at Potsdam that the first atomic test had succeeded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 24 July, without an interpreter, Truman told Stalin of a 'new weapon of unusual destructive force', and Stalin, whose intelligence services had known of the project since 1941, simply hoped it would be put to good use against the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 26 July the United States, Britain and China issued the Potsdam Declaration, calling on Japan to proclaim the unconditional surrender of its armed forces or face 'prompt and utter destruction', and the Soviet Union did not sign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tokyo meanwhile pressed its ambassador in Moscow to see the Soviet foreign minister before he left for Potsdam, and the timing of the conference interfered with a plan to send Prince Konoe there as a special envoy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The declaration said nothing about the emperor, and after the Japanese prime minister answered that his government would ignore it, Hiroshima was bombed on 6 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, “The Potsdam Conference, 1945,” <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/potsdam-conf. [Open access]","Avalon Project, Yale Law School, “Potsdam Conference: Protocol of the Proceedings, August 1, 1945,” in <i>A Decade of American Foreign Policy, 1941–1949</i>, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/decade17.asp. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Potsdam and the Final Decision to Bomb (Potsdam, Germany, July 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/potsdam_decision.htm. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","National Diet Library, “Potsdam Declaration: Proclamation Defining Terms for Japanese Surrender, Issued at Potsdam, July 26, 1945,” <i>Birth of the Constitution of Japan</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/constitution/e/etc/c06.html. [Open access]","Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, <i>Reports of General MacArthur: Japanese Operations in the Southwest Pacific Area</i>, vol. 2, pt. 2 (1966; facsimile repr., Washington, DC: Center of Military History, 1994), chap. 20, https://archive.org/details/ReportsOfGeneralMacarthurJapaneseOperationsPt2. [Open access]","United States Strategic Bombing Survey, <i>Summary Report (Pacific War)</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1 July 1946), 26, https://archive.org/details/summaryreportpac00unit. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, The Conference of Berlin (The Potsdam Conference), 1945</i>, vol. 2 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1960), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945Berlinv02. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Soviet Union not sign the Potsdam Declaration?","a":"Because it had not yet declared war on Japan, so the declaration was issued by the United States, Britain and China alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stalin had promised to enter the war against Japan by 15 August, but after the atomic test Truman and his advisers were no longer sure they wanted that help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Stalin show so little surprise at Truman's news?","a":"Because Soviet intelligence had been receiving information about the American atomic bomb programme since the autumn of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Truman mentioned a new weapon of unusual destructive force, Stalin replied only that he hoped it would be put to good use against the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the declaration's silence on the emperor matter?","a":"Because it left the emperor's status unclear, while Japanese leaders still hoped for a negotiated peace that would protect the imperial house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prime Minister Suzuki's reported answer that his government would ignore the declaration was all that Truman and his colleagues saw of Japan's response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Potsdam Conference"},
    "ww2-936": {"questions":["At its meeting of 31 May 1945 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> agreed that the best target would be a vital war plant employing many workers and closely surrounded by their houses.","The four physicists of the Scientific Panel, Oppenheimer among them, told the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in June 1945 that they could propose no technical demonstration likely to end the war."],"abstract":"The <b>Interim Committee</b> was a high-level advisory group formed in early May 1945 by the Secretary of War, Henry L. Stimson, with President Truman's approval, to advise on the wartime use of atomic weapons and on postwar atomic policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was called 'interim' because it was meant to last only until a more permanent body for nuclear affairs was set up, and it met eight times from 9 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stimson chaired it, and its members included Ralph Bard of the Navy, the science administrators Vannevar Bush, James Conant and Karl Compton, and James Byrnes as Truman's representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Scientific Panel of four physicists, Arthur Compton, Ernest Lawrence, Robert Oppenheimer and Enrico Fermi, advised it, and Generals Marshall and Groves attended without being members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of its work looked beyond the war, to secrecy, postwar controls and whether the Soviet Union should be told about the project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At its meeting of 31 May the committee discussed the bomb's use against Japan and agreed that the most desirable target would be a vital war plant employing many workers and closely surrounded by workers' houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A demonstration to persuade Japan to surrender was raised and rejected, for fear that the bomb might be a dud, that prisoners of war might be moved into the area or that the aircraft might be shot down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 6 June Stimson told Truman that the committee recommended using the bomb as soon as possible and without warning, and on 16 June the Scientific Panel wrote that it could propose no technical demonstration likely to end the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not every member agreed: on 27 June Bard wrote that Japan should be given 2 or 3 days' warning and that its government might be looking for a way to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The directive of 25 July, approved by Truman and issued through Stimson and Marshall, then ordered the first bomb dropped on Hiroshima, Kokura, Niigata or Nagasaki as soon after 3 August as the weather allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Debate over How to Use the Bomb (Washington, D.C., Late Spring 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/debate.htm. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “The Interim Committee,” Nuclear Museum, 5 June 2014, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/history/interim-committee/. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","A. H. Compton, E. O. Lawrence, J. R. Oppenheimer and E. Fermi, “Recommendations on the Immediate Use of Nuclear Weapons,” 16 June 1945, Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/manhattan-project/interim-committee.html. [Open access]","Ralph A. Bard, “Memorandum on the Use of S-1 Bomb,” 27 June 1945, Harrison-Bundy Files, RG 77, National Archives, Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/manhattan-project/bard-memo.html. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Potsdam and the Final Decision to Bomb (Potsdam, Germany, July 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/potsdam_decision.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the committee reject a demonstration of the bomb?","a":"Because its members feared that a demonstration might fail if the bomb proved a dud, that the Japanese might move American prisoners of war into the area, or that the aircraft might be shot down, and that the shock value of the weapon would be lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Scientific Panel agreed that it could propose no technical demonstration likely to bring an end to the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the target described as a war plant surrounded by houses?","a":"Because the members wanted the greatest psychological effect on Japan, and agreed that the most desirable target would be a vital war plant employing many workers and closely surrounded by their homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The idea of such a 'dual target' was part of the case for dropping the bomb without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Ralph Bard break with the other members?","a":"Because he felt that Japan should have 2 or 3 days' warning before the bomb was used, as befitted what he called a great humanitarian nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also suspected that the Japanese government was looking for a way to surrender, and proposed that emissaries meet Japanese representatives after the Potsdam conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Interim Committee"},
    "ww2-937": {"questions":["Wanting a city untouched by earlier raids so that the bomb's effects could be measured, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked the air force to spare its chosen targets from conventional bombing.","Kyoto headed the list drawn up by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in May 1945 until the Secretary of War, Henry Stimson, objected to bombing Japan's former capital."],"abstract":"The <b>Target Committee</b> was a group of military officers and scientists set up by General Leslie Groves in the spring of 1945 to recommend how and where the atomic bomb should be used against Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its first meeting, on 27 April 1945, discussed bombing techniques, target criteria and mission requirements, and Hiroshima was already named as the largest untouched target not on the air force's priority list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At its second meeting, on 10 and 11 May in Robert Oppenheimer's office at Los Alamos, it also worked out the heights at which the bombs should burst and agreed that the attack should be made by visual bombing if at all possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those present included Oppenheimer, John von Neumann and the British physicist William Penney, who worked on the damage to be expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its recommendations fed into the work of the Interim Committee, the civilian body that advised the Secretary of War on the use of the bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The committee looked for important targets in an urban area more than about 5 kilometres (3 miles) across, likely to be damaged effectively by blast and unlikely to have been attacked by August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A first target relatively untouched by earlier raids would let the effect of a single bomb be measured, so cities on its list, Kyoto, Hiroshima, Yokohama and Kokura among them, were to be spared conventional bombing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Psychological effect counted heavily, since members wanted the greatest impact on Japan and a first use spectacular enough for the weapon's importance to be recognised internationally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 28 May the committee agreed that the bomb should be aimed at the centre of a city rather than at a pinpoint industrial target, and Kyoto, the former capital, was later dropped after the Secretary of War, Henry Stimson, objected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The order of 25 July named Hiroshima, Kokura, Niigata and Nagasaki, the last added only at the end of July, and the choice on the day depended on the weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["J. A. Derry and N. F. Ramsey, memorandum to Major General L. R. Groves, “Summary of Target Committee Meetings on 10 and 11 May 1945,” 12 May 1945, Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/manhattan-project/targeting.html. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Potsdam and the Final Decision to Bomb (Potsdam, Germany, July 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/potsdam_decision.htm. [Open access]","Thos. T. Handy, Acting Chief of Staff, directive to General Carl Spaatz, 25 July 1945, Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/manhattan-project/spaatz-memo.html. [Open access]","Manhattan Engineer District, <i>The Atomic Bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</i> (1946), “The Selection of the Target,” Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/med/med_chp5.html. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “The Interim Committee,” Nuclear Museum, 5 June 2014, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/history/interim-committee/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the committee want a city untouched by earlier bombing?","a":"Because the effect of a single atomic bomb could only be judged on a target relatively untouched by previous raids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The committee therefore agreed that the cities on its list should be exempt from the army air force's bombing, which was then laying waste to Japan's cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Kyoto on the list at all?","a":"Because it was a large urban industrial area of about a million people, and the committee thought that people in an intellectual centre were more apt to appreciate the significance of the weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stimson objected, citing its long history as a cultural centre and former capital of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the committee insist on visual bombing?","a":"Because members wanted the bomb placed accurately, and agreed to wait for a good forecast over one of three alternative targets rather than drop it by radar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Weather records showed that good bombing days were rare over Japan, so the plans were built around using whatever good weather came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-938": {"questions":["With its uranium-235 in two pieces each smaller than a critical mass, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fired one into the other with a chemical explosive to start its chain reaction.","Carried to Tinian aboard the cruiser Indianapolis in July 1945, the bomb nicknamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a yield later estimated at about 15 kilotons of TNT."],"abstract":"<b>Little Boy</b> was the code name of the uranium atomic bomb that the United States dropped on Hiroshima on 6 August 1945, the first nuclear weapon used in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a gun-type weapon, in which the uranium-235 was divided into two pieces, each smaller than a critical mass, and a chemical explosive drove one into the other to make a supercritical mass and start a chain reaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was about 3 metres (10 feet) long and 71 centimetres (28 inches) across, and it weighed about 4,400 kilograms (9,700 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Planners found no safe place near the base to jettison it in an emergency, since water leaking into the bomb could set off a nuclear reaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its main parts reached the island of Tinian aboard the cruiser USS Indianapolis on 26 July, and the final nuclear components followed by air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An order of 2 August named Hiroshima the primary target for 6 August, and that morning the B-29 Enola Gay, piloted by Colonel Paul Tibbets, released the bomb over the city at about 8:15 AM.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It exploded 43 seconds later, about 580 metres (1,900 feet) above a parade ground where soldiers were doing their morning exercises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its yield was later put at about 15 kilotons of TNT, although estimates have ranged from 6 to 23 kilotons and the Hiroshima museum gives 16.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heat, blast and the firestorm destroyed nearly every structure within 1.6 kilometres (1 mile) of ground zero, and some 70,000 people probably died from the initial effects, a toll that passed 100,000 by the end of 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tokyo learned what had happened only from Washington's announcement 16 hours later, in which Truman warned that more attacks would follow if Japan did not surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “The Atomic Bombing of Hiroshima (Hiroshima, Japan, August 6, 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/hiroshima.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Potsdam and the Final Decision to Bomb (Potsdam, Germany, July 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/potsdam_decision.htm. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Nagasaki City, “About the Atomic Bomb,” <i>Nagasaki Peace</i>, https://nagasakipeace.jp/en/search/record/about.html. [Open access]","Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum, “5-2-1 The Atomic Bombs Dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki,” permanent exhibition, https://hpmmuseum.jp/modules/exhibition/index.php?action=ItemView&item_id=64&lang=eng. [Open access]","Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum, “5-1-3 The Atomic Bombing of Hiroshima,” permanent exhibition, https://hpmmuseum.jp/modules/exhibition/index.php?action=ItemView&item_id=38&lang=eng. [Open access]","John Malik, <i>The Yields of the Hiroshima and Nagasaki Nuclear Explosions</i>, LA-8819 (Los Alamos, NM: Los Alamos National Laboratory, September 1985), Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/pdfs/00313791.pdf. [Open access]","J. A. Derry and N. F. Ramsey, memorandum to Major General L. R. Groves, “Summary of Target Committee Meetings on 10 and 11 May 1945,” 12 May 1945, Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/manhattan-project/targeting.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Little Boy called a gun-type bomb?","a":"Because it worked like a gun: a chemical explosive drove one piece of uranium-235 into another, so that together they formed a supercritical mass and a chain reaction began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each piece on its own was smaller than a critical mass and could not explode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do estimates of its yield differ?","a":"Because, unlike the Nagasaki explosion, the Hiroshima explosion left no fireball or radiochemical data, so its yield had to be worked out from its blast, heat and radiation effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Those methods point to about 15 kilotons, while earlier estimates had ranged from 6 to 23.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Japan's high command not know at once what had happened?","a":"Because radio and telegraph links with Hiroshima suddenly stopped at 8.16 a.m., while the command knew that no large raid had taken place and that no great stores of explosives were in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A staff officer sent by plane saw the cloud over the city from nearly 160 kilometres (100 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Little Boy"},
    "ww2-939": {"questions":["Turned away from Kokura by smoke and haze, the crew of Bock's Car released <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the Urakami valley, midway between two great Mitsubishi works.","Squeezing a core of plutonium-239 into a supercritical mass with surrounding explosives, the bomb called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the second and last nuclear weapon used in war."],"abstract":"<b>Fat Man</b> was the code name of the plutonium atomic bomb that the United States dropped on Nagasaki on 9 August 1945, the second and last nuclear weapon used in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was an implosion weapon, in which a core of plutonium-239, divided into parts each smaller than a critical mass, was surrounded by explosive charges that squeezed it into a single dense, supercritical mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its round shape followed from that design: it was about 3.25 metres (10.7 feet) long and 1.52 metres (60 inches) across, and it weighed about 4.5 tonnes (10,000 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its yield of about 21 kilotons of TNT is well established, roughly 40 per cent more than the Hiroshima bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Further bombs were to follow as they became ready, and a third plutonium weapon was expected to be ready after 17 or 18 August before Truman halted atomic attacks during the surrender talks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The B-29 Bock's Car, commanded by Major Charles Sweeney, took off from Tinian early on 9 August for its primary target, the arsenal city of Kokura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smoke and haze hid Kokura through three bombing runs, so, short of fuel and under anti-aircraft fire, the crew turned to the secondary target, Nagasaki, and dropped the bomb at 11:02 AM through a brief break in the cloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It burst high over the Urakami valley, almost midway between the two great Mitsubishi works, so the hills shielded much of the city's residential and commercial heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British mission to Japan reckoned that about 1.5 square miles, nearly 4 square kilometres, were destroyed and nearly 40,000 people killed, out of just over 260,000 then in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The American best estimate was some 40,000 dead at first, approaching 70,000 by January 1946, and on the day after the attack Japan offered to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “The Atomic Bombing of Nagasaki (Nagasaki, Japan, August 9, 1945),” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/nagasaki.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Japan Surrenders, August 10–15, 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/surrender.htm. [Open access]","William Burr, ed., “The Atomic Bomb and the End of World War II,” National Security Archive Briefing Book 716, 4 August 2020, https://nsarchive.gwu.edu/briefing-book/nuclear-vault/2020-08-04/atomic-bomb-end-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Nagasaki City, “About the Atomic Bomb,” <i>Nagasaki Peace</i>, https://nagasakipeace.jp/en/search/record/about.html. [Open access]","Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum, “5-2-1 The Atomic Bombs Dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki,” permanent exhibition, https://hpmmuseum.jp/modules/exhibition/index.php?action=ItemView&item_id=64&lang=eng. [Open access]","John Malik, <i>The Yields of the Hiroshima and Nagasaki Nuclear Explosions</i>, LA-8819 (Los Alamos, NM: Los Alamos National Laboratory, September 1985), Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/pdfs/00313791.pdf. [Open access]","Manhattan Engineer District, <i>The Atomic Bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki</i> (1946), “The Attacks,” Atomic Archive, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/med/med_chp7.html. [Open access]","British Mission to Japan, <i>The Effects of the Atomic Bombs at Hiroshima and Nagasaki</i> (London: His Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1946), https://archive.org/details/460101-effects-of-atomic-bombs-hiroshima-nagasaki. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the bomb fall on Nagasaki rather than Kokura?","a":"Because the primary target, Kokura, was hidden by smoke and haze, and three bombing runs failed to give a sight of the aiming point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With fighters and anti-aircraft fire rising and fuel running short, the crew turned to the secondary target, Nagasaki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Nagasaki suffer less than Hiroshima from a stronger bomb?","a":"Because the hills of Nagasaki, the city's layout and the bomb's detonation over an industrial area shielded parts of it from the blast, heat and radiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hills enclosing the target area restricted the spread of the blast, so a smaller area was destroyed even though the explosion was more powerful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the plutonium bomb built as an implosion weapon?","a":"Because the plutonium core had to be squeezed into a single dense, supercritical mass by surrounding explosive charges, rather than brought together like the two pieces of uranium in the Hiroshima bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The method gave the bomb its round, heavy shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fat Man"},
    "ww2-940": {"questions":["Blown by the wind, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 6 August 1945 fell mostly around Koi and Takasu, west of Hiroshima's hypocentre, rather than on the hypocentre itself.","In July 2021 Japan's government chose not to appeal further against residents who had been exposed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outside the zone it had designated for relief."],"abstract":"<b>Black rain</b> was the dark, sticky rain that fell over parts of Hiroshima and Nagasaki shortly after the atomic bombings of August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is thought to have formed in the air currents that rose from the heat of the bombs and of the fires that followed, and it took its colour from their soot and ash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After each explosion the fireball rose high into the atmosphere, and as its material cooled some of it fell with the rain, although most was probably scattered widely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the rain was radioactive, and residual radiation could harm even people who had not been directly exposed to the bomb itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Hiroshima injured and thirsty people fleeing the fires drank the rain as it poured onto them, and it left black streaks on walls and spots on clothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because of the wind it fell not on the hypocentres but mainly to the west of Hiroshima's, around Koi and Takasu, and to the east of Nagasaki's, around Nishiyama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many more people met it in Hiroshima than in Nagasaki: in surveys between 1949 and 1961, 20 per cent of Hiroshima subjects said they had been caught in the rain, against 2.6 per cent in Nagasaki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Early surveys found residual radiation only in localised areas, and the highest estimated doses from fallout were 0.006 to 0.02 gray in Hiroshima and 0.12 to 0.24 gray in Nagasaki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Studies of survivors have found no consistent evidence that the rain was generally radioactive at a considerable level or caused lasting harm, while warning that their data are limited and uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan's government gave relief to people inside a designated area of heavy rain, but residents outside it, where the rain also fell, went to court and won, and in July 2021 the government decided not to appeal further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum, “2-2-6 Black Rain,” permanent exhibition, https://hpmmuseum.jp/modules/exhibition/index.php?action=ItemView&item_id=113&lang=eng. [Open access]","Radiation Effects Research Foundation, “Frequently Asked Questions,” https://www.rerf.or.jp/en/faq/. [Open access]","K. Ozasa, R. Sakata, H. M. Cullings and E. J. Grant, “Association of Acute Radiation Syndrome and Rain after the Bombings in Atomic Bomb Survivors,” <i>Radiation Research</i> 185 (2016): 604–15, https://www.rerf.or.jp/library/rr_e/rr1501full.pdf. [Open access]","Ritsu Sakata, Eric J. Grant, Kyoji Furukawa, Munechika Misumi, Harry Cullings, Kotaro Ozasa and Roy E. Shore, “Long-Term Effects of the Rain Exposure Shortly after the Atomic Bombings in Hiroshima and Nagasaki,” <i>Radiation Research</i> 182 (2014): 599–606, https://www.rerf.or.jp/library/rr_e/rr1407full.pdf. [Open access]","Ken Inoue, Noriyuki Kawano, Nobuo Takeichi, Yoshihiro Noso and Masaharu Hoshi, “A Combined Scientific and Public Health Approach Is Needed to Evaluate the Health Impacts of Internal Exposure to Radiation,” <i>The Lancet Regional Health: Western Pacific</i> 15 (2021): 100296, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8524187/. [Open access]","Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum, “5-2-5 Radiation,” permanent exhibition, https://hpmmuseum.jp/modules/exhibition/index.php?action=ItemView&item_id=39&lang=eng. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the rain black?","a":"Because it was thought to have formed in the air currents rising from the heat of the bombs and the fires that consumed much of the cities, and it was mixed with their soot and ash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The term describes the rain's colour and the soot it carried, which also contained radioactive material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the rain miss the hypocentres?","a":"Because the wind carried the rising material away, so the rain fell mainly in the west of Hiroshima, around Koi and Takasu, and in the east of Nagasaki, around Nishiyama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early measurements found localised residual radiation in those same districts, which were thought to have received fallout in the rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did black rain end up in the courts?","a":"Because relief went only to people inside the government's designated area of heavy rain, while testimony and studies by Hiroshima City suggested the rain fell over an area about six times larger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Residents of the wider area sued, and in July 2021 the government chose not to appeal the rulings in their favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Black rain"},
    "ww2-901": {"questions":["The electromechanical Bomba, built by a Warsaw radio firm to run through more than 17,000 rotor positions of the German cipher machine, was designed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the war.","Evacuated through Romania in September 1939, the codebreaker <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked at the secret Cadix station in Vichy France before reaching Britain by way of Spain in 1943."],"abstract":"<b>Marian Rejewski</b> was a Polish mathematician and cryptologist, born in Bromberg, now Bydgoszcz, on 16 August 1905, and one of the team that reconstructed the German military Enigma cipher machine before the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He attended a secret cryptology course that the Polish General Staff's Cipher Bureau ran for mathematics students at Poznań in 1929, and from 1930 he worked at the bureau's Poznań branch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 1 September 1932 he began full-time work at the Cipher Bureau in Warsaw, where two other young mathematicians, Jerzy Różycki and Henryk Zygalski, joined him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commercial Enigma the Poles had bought was useless to them, because the German military had changed the machine too much, so their first task was to work out the wiring of its rotors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rejewski attacked the problem with the mathematics of permutations, filled in the unknowns with German documents sold to the French officer Gustave Bertrand, and broke the cipher in December 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Even after the Germans changed their procedure in September 1936, the three mathematicians could read about three-quarters of the Enigma messages passed to them by January 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rejewski designed the Bomba, a machine built by the AVA radio company that worked through more than 17,000 possible positions, while Zygalski devised perforated sheets that compared message indicators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Germans added two new rotors at the end of 1938, the Bomba would have needed sixty linked Enigmas instead of six, more than the Polish system could handle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 25 and 26 July 1939, with invasion looming, the Poles showed French and British cryptologists their replica Enigmas and their machine at Pyry near Warsaw, at a time when Britain's codebreakers held out little hope of mastering the cipher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rejewski escaped through Romania to France, worked at the secret Cadix station in Vichy France, reached Britain in August 1943 and served in Polish radio intelligence there, and went home to Poland in 1946, dying on 13 February 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["J. J. O’Connor and E. F. Robertson, “Marian Adam Rejewski,” MacTutor History of Mathematics Archive, University of St Andrews, https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Rejewski/. [Open access]","Museum of the Second World War in Gdańsk, “Enigma and Marian Rejewski,” 20 March 2020, https://www.muzeum1939.pl/en/news/enigma-and-marian-rejewski-m2wsonline-11228. [Open access]","Jennifer Wilcox, <i>Solving the Enigma: History of the Cryptanalytic Bombe</i>, rev. ed. (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 2006), https://archive.org/details/solving_enigma-nsa. [Open access]","Bletchley Park Trust, “Enigma,” https://bletchleypark.org.uk/our-story/enigma/. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Gill Bennett, “What’s the Context? Polish Cryptologists Reveal They Have Cracked the Enigma Code, 26 July 1939,” <i>History of Government</i> blog, UK Government, 26 July 2019, https://history.blog.gov.uk/2019/07/26/whats-the-context-polish-cryptologists-reveal-they-have-cracked-the-enigma-code-26-july-1939/. [Open access]","Roman Murawski, “Marian Rejewski,” Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, Adam Mickiewicz University, Poznań, https://wmi.amu.edu.pl/en/faculty/history/biographical-sketches/marian-rejewski. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the Poles not simply use a commercial Enigma?","a":"Because the commercial version they bought in 1928 used four rotors and had no plugboard, and the German military had made too many changes to the machine for it to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Poles therefore had to rebuild the military machine on paper, and Rejewski did this by applying the theory of permutations, an old branch of mathematics, to intercepted messages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German documents obtained by French intelligence filled in values his equations could not reach alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It took him several months, and by December 1932 the cipher had been broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Poles share their secret in July 1939?","a":"Because the threat of German invasion was looming over them, and the Germans had already added two new rotors that pushed the problem beyond what Polish equipment could handle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Until then Poland had never disclosed the break, even to its French partners, and the visitors were astonished to see replica Enigmas and a machine that could find the settings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Bletchley Park, Dilly Knox realised that the Polish achievement shortened the British attack on Enigma by at least a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The British official history says that before this gift Britain's codebreakers held out little hope of mastering Enigma even in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Poles had broken it as early as 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was Rejewski's part unknown for so long?","a":"Because after the war he returned to communist-controlled Poland, where he had to keep secret his work for the pre-war Polish intelligence service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Britain he had been kept away from Enigma, and after being demobbed in November 1946 he went home to his family in Bydgoszcz and took a job in sales rather than return to university mathematics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He did not reveal his role until 1967, when he wrote memoirs and deposited them with the Military Historical Institute, and his achievements became public only towards the end of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Marian Rejewski"},
    "ww2-902": {"questions":["Eight of the ten machines were dismantled after the war, and the secret of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was kept until captioned photographs were released to the Public Record Office in 1975.","Working from early February 1944, the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped Bletchley Park read messages between Hitler and his generals enciphered on the teleprinter system the British called Tunny."],"abstract":"The <b>Colossus computer</b> was a series of British electronic machines built during the Second World War with a single purpose: to help decipher the Lorenz-enciphered messages, codenamed Tunny, sent between Hitler and his generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tunny was a teleprinter cipher of the German High Command, more complex than Enigma, and its British attack was led at Bletchley Park by the mathematician Max Newman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The engineer Tommy Flowers of the Post Office Research Station at Dollis Hill, who had worked on the earlier Heath Robinson machine, proposed a far bigger machine using thousands of valves, the vacuum tubes of electronic telephone exchanges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Flowers spent 11 months designing and building the first Colossus at Dollis Hill, and after a functional test it was delivered to Bletchley Park around the turn of 1944 and was working in early February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is said to have held about 1,500 to 1,600 valves, and its electronic circuits counted, did binary arithmetic and Boolean logic, and could branch on conditions set by switches and plug panels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The cipher text was fed in on punched paper tape, which the machine read at 5,000 characters a second, and Colossus worked out the settings of the Lorenz machine's chi-wheels far faster than the codebreakers could by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An improved Mark 2 with about 2,400 valves followed in the summer of 1944, just in time for D-Day, and by the German surrender ten machines were at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of the war 550 people, helped by the ten machines, had deciphered 63 million characters of high-grade German communications, intelligence widely held to have shortened the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Churchill ordered the machines destroyed and kept secret, though GCHQ kept two of them, so Colossus was left out of the history of computing for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Captioned photographs were released in October 1975, the full report on Tunny only in 2000, and Colossus is now seen as the first computer that was at once digital, programmable and electronic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["GCHQ, “Tommy Flowers,” https://www.gchq.gov.uk/person/tommy-flowers. [Open access]","The National Museum of Computing, “Colossus,” https://www.tnmoc.org/colossus. [Open access]","I. J. Good, D. Michie and G. Timms, <i>General Report on Tunny, with Emphasis on Statistical Methods</i> (Bletchley Park, 1945), The National Archives, Kew, HW 25/4 and HW 25/5, digital facsimile at AlanTuring.net, https://www.alanturing.net/tunny_report/. [Open access]","B. Randell, <i>The COLOSSUS</i>, Technical Report Series 90 (Newcastle upon Tyne: University of Newcastle upon Tyne Computing Laboratory, 1976), http://www.cs.ncl.ac.uk/publications/trs/papers/90.pdf. [Open access]","Lori Cameron, “Thomas H. Flowers: The Hidden Story of the Bletchley Park Engineer Who Designed the Code-Breaking Colossus,” IEEE Computer Society, 9 August 2018, https://publications.computer.org/annals/2018/08/09/thomas-flowers-code-breaker-wwii-colossus-machines/. [Open access]","Computer History Museum, “Colossus: Breaking the Code,” <i>Revolution: The First 2000 Years of Computing</i>, https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/birth-of-the-computer/4/82. [Open access]","IEEE History Center, “Milestones: Code-breaking at Bletchley Park during World War II, 1939–1945,” Engineering and Technology History Wiki, https://ethw.org/Milestones:Code-breaking_at_Bletchley_Park_during_World_War_II,_1939-1945. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Colossus built for Tunny rather than Enigma?","a":"Because the German army had asked the Lorenz company for a more complex teleprinter cipher, and by the war's end Tunny carried messages from Hitler and the High Command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enigma was attacked with the electromechanical Bombe, while the electronic Colossus was designed by Tommy Flowers for the Lorenz traffic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Journalists still sometimes give Colossus to Alan Turing or say that it targeted Enigma, which the record does not support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Flowers think valves would work?","a":"Because before the war he had foreseen electronic telephone exchanges in which valves would replace electromechanical switches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When he joined the work on Tunny he used that insight to design a large valve-based machine, and he led the team that built it at Dollis Hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first Colossus was built and assembled there under his close supervision, with much of the production and wiring done by women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was delivered to Bletchley Park and working in early February 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Flowers get so little credit for so long?","a":"Because the use of computers in codebreaking stayed highly sensitive, so he had retired before his part in modern computing could be told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Churchill acknowledged that the machines had shortened the war but ordered them destroyed and kept secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only in 1975, after 32 years of official silence, did the government release photographs of Colossus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Recognition came late, with an honorary doctorate in 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Colossus computer"},
    "ww2-903": {"questions":["Reports by Japan's ambassador in Berlin on Hitler's plans and the Atlantic Wall reached the Allies through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after American codebreakers had solved the machine cipher they called Purple.","Decrypted cables between Tokyo and its Washington embassy, circulated under the codename <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1941, showed not only what Japan's diplomats were saying but what they were thinking."],"abstract":"<b>Magic</b> was the American codename for the intelligence the United States obtained by intercepting, decrypting and translating secret Japanese diplomatic messages during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was highly classified and closely guarded, and it went to only a few of the highest-level American officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its main source was the Type B machine that Japan introduced on its high-level diplomatic circuits in February 1939, which American cryptologists called Purple and issued only to the more important embassies, including Washington, London, Rome and Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For 18 months the Army's Signal Intelligence Service under William Friedman struggled with it, until on 20 September 1940 the statistician Genevieve Grotjan found a pattern in the cipher texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A week later Frank Rowlett handed Friedman two complete deciphered messages, and the team reconstructed the machine without ever having seen one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Through 1941 Magic gave Washington the instructions Tokyo sent to its ambassador during the secret talks with Secretary of State Cordell Hull, which ran from February until the attack on Pearl Harbor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It gave no warning of that attack, because Japan's Foreign Ministry was not told the military's plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its richest source later in the war was Ōshima Hiroshi, the ambassador in Berlin and an army general, whose detailed reports of talks with Hitler and other German leaders were sent to Tokyo in Purple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His message intercepted on 10 November 1943, describing a tour of the Atlantic Wall with the positions and strength of German divisions, was used by Eisenhower's staff in planning the invasion of Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The reports were first given the codeword Magic and then Ultra, when the United States and Britain standardised their terms, and Ōshima died in 1975 without knowing how much he had told the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Frederick D. Parker, <i>Pearl Harbor Revisited: United States Navy Communications Intelligence, 1924–1941</i>, United States Cryptologic History, series 4, vol. 6 (Fort Meade, MD: Center for Cryptologic History, National Security Agency, 1994), https://archive.org/details/pearl_harbor_revisited-nsa. [Open access]","United States Department of Defense, <i>The “Magic” Background of Pearl Harbor</i>, vol. 1, <i>February 14, 1941 – May 12, 1941</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1977), preface, https://archive.org/details/MagicBackgroundOfPearlHarborVolumeIFebruary141941May121941. [Open access]","Michael E. Bigelow, “SIS Makes Breakthrough Against Japanese Code (20 SEP 1940),” US Army Intelligence Center of Excellence, DVIDS, 22 September 2023, https://www.dvidshub.net/news/454045/sis-makes-breakthrough-against-japanese-code-20-sep-1940. [Open access]","Lori S. Stewart, “MAGIC Contributes to D-Day Success (10 NOV 1943),” US Army Intelligence Center of Excellence, DVIDS, 8 November 2024, https://www.dvidshub.net/news/484976/magic-contributes-d-day-success-10-nov-1943. [Open access]","National Security Agency, “Baron Oshima’s Contributions,” <i>Cryptologic Almanac</i>, September 1998, released 2010, https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/nsa/cryptoalmanac/baron_oshimas_contributions.pdf. [Open access]","George C. Marshall Foundation, “Marshall, PURPLE and Pearl Harbor,” 5 December 2014, https://www.marshallfoundation.org/?p=1315. [Open access]","Joint Committee on the Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack, <i>Investigation of the Pearl Harbor Attack: Report of the Joint Committee</i>, 79th Congress, 2nd session, Senate Document 244 (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1946), https://archive.org/details/investigationofp00unit. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Magic not warn of Pearl Harbor?","a":"Because Purple carried the Foreign Ministry's traffic, and Japan's Foreign Ministry was not privy to Japanese military planning, so no warning of the attack came from this source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What Magic did give was the diplomatic side: Tokyo's instructions to its Washington embassy during the Hull–Nomura talks of 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The congressional inquiry found that this material showed not only what Japan and its ambassadors were saying but literally what they were thinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> George Marshall, warned that a blow was coming, assumed it would fall on the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was a Japanese ambassador so useful for the war in Europe?","a":"Because Ōshima, a general and an early supporter of the Nazi Party, had good access to Hitler and senior German officials and reported regularly and in detail to Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His messages filled a gap, since the Allies had broken the German military code but not the German diplomatic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In September 1944 Marshall wrote that Ōshima's reports were the main basis of American information about Hitler's intentions in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were also the United States' only authentic information about fighting on the Russo-German front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Purple take so long to break?","a":"Because the new Type B machine was far more complex than the Type A machine before it, which Japanese embassies still used when theirs needed repair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rowlett's team spent 6 months with pencil and paper to work out how one small group of letters was enciphered, and a device built by Leo Rosen then helped reveal fragments of messages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only after Grotjan spotted repeated sequences in September 1940 did the larger group give way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Friedman's team rebuilt the machine without ever seeing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Magic (cryptography)"},
    "ww2-904": {"questions":["At Iwo Jima six of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked round the clock and sent more than 800 messages without an error, according to the act of Congress that honoured them.","Kept secret until 1968 in case it was needed again, the work of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was honoured with Congressional Gold Medals for the original 29 men in 2001."],"abstract":"The <b>Navajo code talkers</b> were Navajo, or Diné, men recruited by the United States Marine Corps in the Second World War to send secret messages in a code built on their own language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Navajo was chosen because it was a complex and mostly unwritten language that few outsiders could speak, and Choctaw soldiers had already sent messages in their language in France in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The idea came from Philip Johnston, a missionary's son raised on the Navajo reservation, and on 28 February 1942 four Navajos showed General Clayton Vogel at Camp Elliott near San Diego how quickly they could pass typical combat messages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first 29 recruits arrived for training in May 1942 and designed a code of 211 terms, using words such as 'humming bird' for a fighter plane and 'chicken hawk' for a dive bomber, with a Navajo word for each letter of the alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> To stop repeated letters giving it away, the alphabet grew to 44 terms and the vocabulary to 411, and the whole code was kept in memory rather than on paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> About 400 Navajos served as code talkers, working in pairs by radio and telephone in every major Marine operation in the Pacific from Guadalcanal in 1942 to Okinawa in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Iwo Jima six of them worked round the clock and passed more than 800 messages without an error, and their signal officer said the Marines would never have taken the island without them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many had been punished at boarding school for speaking their language, and code talkers sometimes needed Marine bodyguards because they could be mistaken for Japanese soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The code was never broken, and it stayed secret until 1968 in case it was needed again, so the men could not even tell their families what they had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recognition came slowly: a Navajo Code Talkers Day in 1982, then a law of December 2000 that awarded gold medals to the original 29 and silver medals to the rest, presented in 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Central Intelligence Agency, “Navajo Code Talkers and the Unbreakable Code,” https://www.cia.gov/stories/story/navajo-code-talkers-and-the-unbreakable-code/. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Code Talkers,” last reviewed 4 October 2016, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/military/code-talkers. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Memorandum Regarding the Enlistment of Navajo Indians,” Teaching with Documents, https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/code-talkers. [Open access]","<i>Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2001</i>, Public Law 106-554, appendix D, title XI, sec. 1101, “Honoring the Navajo Code Talkers,” 114 Stat. 2763A-311 (21 December 2000), https://archive.org/details/plaw106publ554. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “American Indian Code Talkers,” https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/american-indian-code-talkers. [Open access]","“The Navajo Code Talkers,” reprinted from <i>The Wall Street Journal</i>, <i>Cryptolog</i> 6, no. 6 (June 1979), National Security Agency, declassified 2012, https://archive.org/details/cryptolog_54. [Open access]","Jennifer Davis, “Navajo Code Talkers Day,” <i>In Custodia Legis</i>, Law Library of Congress, 14 August 2020, https://blogs.loc.gov/law/2020/08/navajo-code-talkers-day/. [Open access]","Martha Davidson, “Secret Warriors,” <i>American Indian</i> 3, no. 2 (Spring 2002), National Museum of the American Indian, https://www.americanindianmagazine.org/sites/default/files/2018-01/NMAI-SPRING-2002.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a spoken language harder to break than a cipher?","a":"Because Navajo was mostly unwritten and very few non-Navajos could speak it; in 1942 only 28 other Americans were thought to know it well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Marines then disguised it further, giving everyday Navajo words military meanings, such as birds for aircraft and fish for ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Navy intelligence officers spent 3 weeks trying to decipher one test message and failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A test message that took 2.5 minutes in Navajo code could take hours by other means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were some officers doubtful at first?","a":"Because Germany and Japan had sent students to study Native American languages after the First World War, and some feared a code based on one would be cracked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One colonel argued that speed mattered little, since urgent messages in battle were often sent uncoded, and he disliked orders being relayed in a language officers could not understand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Marine Corps went ahead anyway, approving a pilot group of 29 recruits who met strict tests in both English and Navajo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The program later grew to hundreds of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did their recognition come so late?","a":"Because the military kept the code classified until 1968, wanting it ready for future wars, and the code talkers were told to keep their work secret even from their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only after declassification did their part in the Pacific campaigns become widely known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Navajo Code Talkers Day was first celebrated in 1982, 14 years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In July 2001 President George W. Bush presented gold medals to the four surviving members of the original group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-905": {"questions":["Many soldiers of the American <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from art schools and advertising agencies, and painters such as Ellsworth Kelly inflated dummy artillery for it close to the front line.","In its last and largest operation, near the Rhine in March 1945, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> impersonated two complete divisions to draw German units away from the real crossing of the Ninth Army."],"abstract":"The <b>Ghost Army</b> was the popular name of two secret United States Army units, the 23rd Headquarters Special Troops and the 3133rd Signal Service Company, which carried out battlefield deception in Europe during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 23rd was activated at Camp Forrest, Tennessee, on 20 January 1944 at the suggestion of Ralph Ingersoll, who had worked with the British on Operation Fortitude, the larger plan to make the Germans expect the invasion at the Pas-de-Calais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had about 1,100 men in four parts: a camouflage battalion that inflated rubber tanks, guns and aircraft, a radio company that sent false traffic, a sound company with loudspeakers on half-tracks, and a company of combat engineers to guard them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many were recruited from art schools, advertising agencies and communications firms, and the painter Ellsworth Kelly and the fashion designer Bill Blass were among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its radio operators could even copy the personal Morse style, or 'fist', of the operators of a unit that had moved away, so the enemy would think it was still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The deceptions began in Normandy in June 1944, and the 23rd completed 21 full-scale operations, often on or near the front line, until March 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sewed on counterfeit patches, painted false markings on vehicles and set up phony headquarters with fake generals, so that it could impersonate units far larger than itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In its final and largest operation, Viersen, near the Rhine in March 1945, it posed as two complete divisions to draw German units away from the Ninth Army's real crossing, and the army's commander praised its careful planning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three of its soldiers were killed and dozens wounded, and an Army analysis later judged that rarely had so few men had so great an influence on a major campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The units stayed classified until 1996, and Congress awarded them a Congressional Gold Medal by a law of 2022, presented at the Capitol in March 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Ghost Army Congressional Gold Medal Act</i>, Public Law 117-85, 136 Stat. 11 (1 February 2022), https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ85/html/PLAW-117publ85.htm. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, “The Ghost Army: Canvas and Camouflage,” 9 May 2024, https://www.nationalww2museum.org/war/articles/ghost-army-canvas-and-camouflage. [Open access]","The Ghost Army Legacy Project, “23rd Headquarters Special Troops,” https://ghostarmy.org/about/history/23rd-Headquarters-Special-Troops/. [Open access]","Associated Press, “WWII ‘Ghost Army’ Members, Whose Mission Had Been Secret, Honored with Congressional Gold Medal,” <i>PBS NewsHour</i>, 21 March 2024, https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-live-wwii-ghost-army-whose-mission-had-been-secret-honored-with-congressional-gold-medal. [Open access]","“Video, Slide Show: The Ghost Army in Action,” <i>Princeton Alumni Weekly</i>, https://paw.princeton.edu/article/video-slide-show-ghost-army-action. [Open access]","Christopher Klein, “The Top-Secret WWII Unit That Fooled the Nazis,” History.com, 3 March 2022, updated 31 January 2025, https://www.history.com/articles/ghost-army-world-war-ii. [Open access]","Mindy Weisberger, “‘Ghost Army’ in WWII Used Inflatable Tanks to Fool the Nazis and Win the War,” <i>Live Science</i>, 2 September 2020, https://livescience.com/wwii-ghost-army.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a deception unit need artists?","a":"Because its main weapons were illusions: rubber tanks, guns and aircraft had to look real to German aerial reconnaissance and distant observers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many of the camouflage soldiers had trained at schools such as Cooper Union and the Pratt Institute and worked in advertising or comic illustration before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Artists painted the decoys with details authentic enough to deceive, and units also stencilled false markings on jeeps to match the division they were copying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One veteran, an art student drafted from Pratt, described the work as putting on a big act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why combine sight, sound and radio?","a":"Because the Germans gathered most of their intelligence from intercepted communications, so visual decoys alone could accomplish only so much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The radio company generated bogus traffic, while the sound company played recordings of troop movement and bridge building that could be heard about 24 kilometres (15 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Operation Brest in August 1944 was the first time the unit used visual, radio and sonic deception together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Combined, the illusions let 1,100 men project the strength of two divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the unit's story so long unknown?","a":"Because its activities remained highly classified for more than 40 years after the war and received little recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its veterans could not discuss what they had done until declassified files broke the silence in 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A documentary of 2013 and a book of 2015 helped bring the story to light, and families worked for almost a decade for the medal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> President Biden signed the law in 2022, and three surviving members attended the ceremony in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ghost Army"},
    "ww2-906": {"questions":["A senior officer of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> known as A-54 supplied the Czech and British secret services with German military intelligence from 1936 into the first years of the war.","Under cover of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Hans Oster and Hans von Dohnanyi plotted against Hitler and passed off a group of threatened Berlin Jews as spies so they could reach Switzerland."],"abstract":"The <b>Abwehr</b> was the German military intelligence agency, the service that gathered secret information abroad and protected the German armed forces against foreign spies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1935 it was the counter-intelligence department of the Reich War Ministry, and by 1938 it had become the Office for Foreign Affairs and Counter-intelligence in the high command of the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its head from 1935 was Rear Admiral Wilhelm Canaris, a naval officer who had welcomed the Nazis' rise in 1933 in the hope of revising the Treaty of Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An officer who joined in 1938 told the Nuremberg tribunal that it then had three Abwehr divisions and a foreign division, the first of which collected information and was called the Secret Information Service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its traffic was itself a target, since the British codebreakers broke the hand cipher of its main group in December 1940 and read its signals, later sent by machine, until the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Abwehr was also the home of one of the main circles of German military resistance to Hitler, and one of its officers later named Canaris as that group's spiritual leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its central department was run by Hans Oster, who wanted to overthrow the regime as early as 1938 and in 1940 warned the Dutch military attaché in Berlin of the coming invasion of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A high-ranking officer known as A-54 passed intelligence to the Czech and British services from 1936, and the British official history calls him the sole Abwehr officer who worked directly with Allied intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1942 Hans von Dohnanyi saved a group of Berlin Jews threatened with deportation by passing them off as Abwehr spies so they could travel to Switzerland, and Canaris covered for the plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dohnanyi was arrested in April 1943, Canaris was removed in February 1944 as some of its members were moved to the SS security service, and both men were murdered with Oster at Flossenbürg on 9 April 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","Testimony of Erwin Lahousen, 30 November 1945, <i>Trial of the Major War Criminals before the International Military Tribunal</i>, vol. 2, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/11-30-45.asp. [Open access]","German Resistance Memorial Center, “Wilhelm Canaris,” biography, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/biographies/index_of_persons/biographie/view-bio/wilhelm-canaris/. [Open access]","German Resistance Memorial Center, “Hans Oster,” biography, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/biographies/index_of_persons/biographie/view-bio/hans-oster/. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal, “Judgment: The Accused Organizations,” 30 September – 1 October 1946, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/judorg.asp. [Open access]","German Resistance Memorial Center, “Hans von Dohnanyi,” biography, https://www.gdw-berlin.de/en/recess/biographies/index_of_persons/biographie/view-bio/hans-von-dohnanyi/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did British codebreakers read Abwehr signals?","a":"Because its messages showed what German intelligence was doing, including its agents abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first Abwehr messages decrypted were intercepted by MI5's Radio Security Service after a tip-off from a double agent and broken at Bletchley Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In December 1940 the codebreakers broke the hand cipher of the main Abwehr group, and they kept reading its traffic, which later moved to a machine cipher, until the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A naval section used the decrypts to break up a German weather-reporting scheme in Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did resisters gather in a Nazi intelligence office?","a":"Because Canaris let opposition members in counter-intelligence act freely from 1938, though he took no active part in their plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oster was the focal point of that group and had key duties in every coup plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1939 he had Dohnanyi transferred to the office precisely so that he could prepare a coup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> An Abwehr officer told the Nuremberg tribunal that a small group of chiefs had been deeply influenced by Canaris's clear inner stance against the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were transferred Abwehr members excluded from the SD's guilt?","a":"Because the Nuremberg prosecution had expressly excluded members of the Abwehr who were transferred to the SD, the SS security service, from its charge against that organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tribunal therefore excluded them too when it declared the SD criminal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By then the Abwehr's leaders had fallen: Oster was forced out in 1943 and Canaris removed in 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both were murdered on Hitler's orders at Flossenbürg weeks before the war ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Abwehr"},
    "ww2-907": {"questions":["Before the Normandy landings the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fed the German secret service false reports from agents such as Garbo, helping convince it that the main blow would fall at Calais.","Intercepted and decrypted German intelligence signals helped MI5 catch the Abwehr's agents in Britain, and the best of them were then run by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the rest of the war."],"abstract":"The <b>Double-Cross System</b> was the British operation of the Second World War in which captured German agents were turned and used as double agents against the German military intelligence service, the Abwehr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its name was a pun on the Roman numerals XX of the Twenty Committee that coordinated it, which was chaired by the Oxford don J. C. Masterman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Decrypted German signals helped MI5 and MI6 identify and arrest Abwehr agents in Britain, and MI5's section B1A then chose some of them to keep writing to the Abwehr under British control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The double agents had two purposes: to identify other German spies and reveal Abwehr plots, and to deceive the German command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masterman wrote a report on the work of B1A and its special agents in 1945, which was kept closed until 1999, though his own book on the system was published in 1972.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The most famous agent was Juan Pujol García, codenamed Garbo, a Spaniard who offered himself to the Germans after the British embassy turned him away and sent them invented reports from Lisbon from April 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> MI5 accepted him in the spring of 1942, brought him to London and, with his case officer Tomás Harris, built a network of 27 fictitious spies, all of whom the Germans paid for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From January 1944 his reports were crucial to Operation Fortitude, which aimed to persuade the Germans that the main invasion would come at the Pas de Calais rather than in Normandy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He even warned of the landings hours in advance, though the Germans did not answer until after they had begun, and he went on insisting that Normandy was a diversion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hitler awarded him the Iron Cross 6 weeks after D-Day, and Britain gave him a secret MBE in December 1944, so that he was honoured by both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Robert Cowden, “A Pioneering Experiment: OSS Double-Agent Operations in World War II,” <i>Studies in Intelligence</i> 58, no. 2 (June 2014), Central Intelligence Agency, https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/OSS-Double-Agent-Operations.pdf. [Open access]","English Heritage, “Juan Pujol Garcia (1912–1988),” Blue Plaques, https://english-heritage.org.uk/visit/blue-plaques/juan-pujol-garcia. [Open access]","“Mr J C Masterman’s Report on the Operation of B1A in Connection with Special Agents during the Second World War, the Double Cross System,” 1945, The National Archives, Kew, KV 4/5, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C3539650/. [Open access]","F. H. Hinsley et al., <i>British Intelligence in the Second World War: Its Influence on Strategy and Operations</i>, vol. 1 (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1979), https://archive.org/details/HarryHinsleyBritishIntelligenceInTheSecondWorldWar. [Open access]","David B. Kneafsey, <i>The Combatant Commander and Effective Operational HUMINT: Lessons from the Double Cross System of World War II and the CJ2X of Operation Joint Guard</i>, paper, Joint Military Operations Department (Newport, RI: Naval War College, 2002), Defense Technical Information Center ADA420278, https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA420278. [Open access]","Richard Armstrong, “The Liar That Helped Win D-Day,” <i>The Engines of Our Ingenuity</i>, episode 3133, University of Houston, https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/3133. [Open access]","“PUJOL GARCIA: Juan. Second World War Double Agent Codenamed GARBO. A Summary of the Case Written by the Case Officer,” 1945, The National Archives, Kew, KV 2/41, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C3539622/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why tell the Germans the truth about D-Day?","a":"Because Garbo had won German trust by often feeding them true information, but too late for them to act on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On D-Day his warning was bait: the plan was to follow it a few days later with a report that a larger invasion was still to come, so that German reserves would be held back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because he had tipped them off about the date but insisted Normandy was a diversion, the Germans kept two armoured and 19 infantry divisions waiting at Calais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Americans learn double-cross work from Britain?","a":"Because the British double-cross programme, launched after the outbreak of war, had neutralised German intelligence in Britain, and British officials wanted a single American counter-intelligence office to work with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The OSS created its counter-espionage branch, X-2, in 1943, and the British trained it to handle double agents before the invasion of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They remained wary that American novices might expose the double-cross system or the Ultra secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do some doubt that the system alone explains its success?","a":"Because critics have argued that its results owed as much to the Abwehr's weaknesses and internal politics as to British skill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The system also relied on codebreaking: reading Abwehr signals was what let the British find and control its agents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first Abwehr messages decrypted had come from a tip-off by a double agent, and Bletchley Park read the service's traffic from December 1940 to the end of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Double-Cross System"},
    "ww2-908": {"questions":["When Donald Maclean and Guy Burgess fled to Moscow in May 1951, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began to unravel, and suspicion soon fell on Kim Philby in Washington.","Anthony Blunt, who worked for MI5 from 1940 to 1945 and later looked after the royal pictures, confessed in 1964 to having been one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spying for the Soviet Union."],"abstract":"The <b>Cambridge Five</b> were Kim Philby, Guy Burgess, Donald Maclean, Anthony Blunt and John Cairncross, graduates of Cambridge University recruited by Soviet intelligence in the 1930s, the most infamous spy ring of the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Calling them a ring is misleading, since although they were connected and sometimes conspired together they took separate paths and largely worked independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They worked across the British state between 1935 and 1952: Blunt for MI5, Maclean for the Foreign Office, Burgess and Philby in the secret services and the Foreign Office, and Cairncross in several departments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the Second World War and after, they passed highly classified information to the Soviet Union, and by the early 1950s they had penetrated all the major institutions of British state security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Blunt worked for MI5 from 1940 to 1945 and passed significant quantities of secret intelligence to the Soviets, while Cairncross worked on decrypted messages at Bletchley Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Philby, recruited in 1934 and in the Secret Intelligence Service from 1940, told Soviet intelligence in 1944 that British codebreakers had turned their attention to Soviet ciphers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was that codebreaking, the joint American and British effort called Venona, that by the spring of 1951 identified Maclean as the agent who had passed embassy cables to Moscow from Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Warned by Philby, who had misread a telegram about when Maclean would be questioned, Maclean and Burgess left England just before midnight on 25 May 1951.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their flight threw suspicion on Philby, who was recalled from Washington, and led to investigations of Blunt and Cairncross, while the Foreign Office faced hard questions about why colleagues had said nothing of the two men's behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Philby made a partial confession in Beirut in January 1963 and then defected to the Soviet Union, Blunt confessed in April 1964 in return for immunity, and Cairncross admitted in 1964 that he had been recruited in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mark Dunton, “Confessions from the Cambridge Five: A File Release from MI5,” The National Archives, 14 January 2025, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/blogs/the-collection-blog/confessions-from-the-cambridge-five. [Open access]","Berenice Burnett, “How a Poorly Drafted Telegram Spelled the Decline and Fall of the Cambridge Five,” King’s College London, 19 April 2021, https://www.kcl.ac.uk/how-a-poorly-drafted-telegram-spelled-the-decline-and-fall-of-the-cambridge-five. [Open access]","Christopher J. Murphy and Dan Lomas, “Revealed: The Panic That Followed the Defection of the Cambridge Spies,” <i>The Conversation</i>, 23 October 2015, https://theconversation.com/revealed-the-panic-that-followed-the-defection-of-the-cambridge-spies-49623. [Open access]","Robert Louis Benson and Michael Warner, eds., <i>Venona: Soviet Espionage and the American Response, 1939–1957</i> (Washington, DC: National Security Agency and Central Intelligence Agency, 1996), https://archive.org/details/Venona. [Open access]","“John CAIRNCROSS: British,” Security Service personal file, The National Archives, Kew, KV 2/4691, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C19404927/. [Open access]","“Anthony BLUNT: British,” Security Service personal file, The National Archives, Kew, KV 2/4705, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C19404941/. [Open access]","“Harold Adrian Russell (Kim) PHILBY: British,” Security Service personal file, The National Archives, Kew, KV 2/4737, catalogue entry, https://beta.nationalarchives.gov.uk/catalogue/id/C19404973/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were Cambridge graduates so useful to Moscow?","a":"Because they went into the heart of the British state: between them they served in MI5, the secret services, the Foreign Office, the Treasury and the wartime codebreaking school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cairncross alone served in the Foreign Office, the Treasury, the codebreakers' school at Bletchley, the Secret Intelligence Service and the Ministry of Supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Blunt passed significant quantities of secret intelligence while working for MI5 from 1940 to 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the early 1950s the five had penetrated all the major institutions of British state security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did a telegram matter so much in 1951?","a":"Because Philby, in Washington, knew of the leak investigation but had only snippets of news from London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A poorly drafted telegram on 16 May made him think Maclean would be questioned soon after 23 May, when in fact the interview was being planned for June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He and Burgess had already set the escape in motion, so the two men fled on 25 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Venona decrypts had narrowed the suspects to Maclean by the beginning of May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the defections cause panic in Whitehall?","a":"Because colleagues and friends had known about the two men's erratic behaviour, yet very little of it had been reported officially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Telling tales on a friend was considered bad taste in the Foreign Service, and one ambassador later regretted not voicing his doubts about Burgess earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Foreign Office's response included harsh measures against homosexual staff that lasted for 4 decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Burgess's defection also meant that Philby came under suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cambridge Five"},
    "ww2-909": {"questions":["When the economist Alexander Sachs read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Roosevelt in October 1939, the President said that this required action and set up an Advisory Committee on Uranium.","Noting that Germany had stopped selling uranium from the Czech mines it had taken over, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hinted that Hitler's scientists were already at work on fission."],"abstract":"The <b>Einstein–Szilard letter</b> was the letter of August 1939, signed by Albert Einstein, that warned President Franklin D. Roosevelt that research on uranium chain reactions might make 'extremely powerful bombs' possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was drafted chiefly by the Hungarian émigré physicist Leo Szilard, who with Eugene Wigner and Edward Teller feared that German scientists might win the race to build such a weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Szilard first thought of asking Einstein to warn Belgium, whose Congo held the most important uranium ore, but decided to go straight to the White House through the economist Alexander Sachs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Szilard and Teller took the draft to Einstein, who was on holiday on Long Island, on 2 August, and sources disagree on whether he rewrote it or simply signed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The letter said a chain reaction was almost certain soon, that a single bomb carried by boat might destroy a whole port, and that Germany had stopped selling uranium from the Czech mines it had seized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because war broke out in Europe, Sachs waited to present the case in person, and he did not see Roosevelt until 11 October 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He read aloud his own covering letter, and Roosevelt, remarking that what Sachs wanted was to see that the Nazis did not blow the country up, said that this required action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The President appointed an Advisory Committee on Uranium under Lyman Briggs of the Bureau of Standards, which first met on 21 October and reported on 1 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Roosevelt replied to Einstein on 19 October, though Szilard and his colleagues had read the long delay as a sign that he did not take the danger seriously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The official history counts the approval of uranium research in October 1939 as only the first of many decisions that led to the Manhattan Project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Einstein’s Letter, 1939,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1939-1942/einstein_letter.htm. [Open access]","Albert Einstein to Franklin D. Roosevelt, 2 August 1939, transcribed at AtomicArchive.com, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/beginnings/einstein.html. [Open access]","Richard G. Hewlett and Oscar E. Anderson Jr., <i>The New World, 1939–1946</i>, A History of the United States Atomic Energy Commission, vol. 1 (University Park: Pennsylvania State University Press, 1962), https://archive.org/details/the-new-world-1939-1946-hewlett-anderson-1962. [Open access]","Vincent C. Jones, <i>Manhattan: The Army and the Atomic Bomb</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1985), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-10. [Open access]","F. G. Gosling, <i>The Manhattan Project: Making the Atomic Bomb</i>, DOE/MA-0001 (Washington, DC: History Division, Department of Energy, 1999), https://archive.org/details/de-99001330-the-manhattan-project-making-the-atomic-bomb-1999. [Open access]","Henry DeWolf Smyth, <i>Atomic Energy for Military Purposes: The Official Report on the Development of the Atomic Bomb under the Auspices of the United States Government, 1940–1945</i> (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1945), https://archive.org/details/atomicenergyform00smytrich. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “Leo Szilard,” Nuclear Museum, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/profile/leo-szilard/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Einstein's name needed at all?","a":"Because Sachs believed that a scientist more eminent than Szilard was needed to counter the doubtful impression already left by physicists at Columbia, and Einstein was the obvious name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Szilard had become a close friend of Einstein in Berlin in the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The text was mainly Szilard's work, drafted in Sachs's office, and Einstein signed a version on 2 August 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Einstein was later invited to the first meeting of the uranium committee but did not attend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the letter mention the Belgian Congo?","a":"Because the physicists worried about uranium ore falling into German hands, and the letter said that the United States had only poor ores while the most important source was in the Belgian Congo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany had already closed Czechoslovakia's uranium exports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The letter asked Roosevelt to name someone to keep government departments informed and to secure a supply of ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the letter take 2 months to reach Roosevelt?","a":"Because war broke out in Europe before Sachs could deliver it, and he wanted to present the case in person rather than leave a paper on the President's desk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt was preoccupied with the international crisis and the fight to repeal the arms embargo, so Sachs waited until early October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The delay worried Szilard, who took it as a sign that the danger was not being taken seriously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once convinced, Roosevelt acted within days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Einstein–Szilard letter"},
    "ww2-910": {"questions":["By the end of the war the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had employed about 130,000 people at its peak and spent some $2.2 billion, a labour force comparable to the American car industry.","Although it kept its secrets from Germany and Japan, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was penetrated by Soviet spies at Los Alamos and other sites, speeding the Soviet bomb."],"abstract":"The <b>Manhattan Project</b> was the huge, top-secret American mobilisation of scientists, engineers, technicians and soldiers that produced the first atomic weapons during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It began as a race to get the bomb before Nazi Germany, and it grew out of the uranium research that Roosevelt approved in 1939 through an Advisory Committee on Uranium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1942 the Army Corps of Engineers took over the building of the plants, and the Manhattan Engineer District was set up by general order on 13 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 17 September the Army put Colonel Leslie Groves, who had built the Pentagon, in charge of the whole effort, and within 2 days he had secured a production site in Tennessee and a higher priority rating for the project's scarce materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 28 December 1942 Roosevelt approved what became a government investment of more than $2 billion in full-scale plants to make bomb fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its three main sites were Oak Ridge in Tennessee, which separated uranium, Hanford in Washington, which made plutonium in reactors, and Los Alamos in New Mexico, which designed the bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reactors rested on Enrico Fermi's experimental pile in Chicago, which achieved the first self-sustaining chain reaction on 2 December 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The workforce reached nearly 129,000 in June 1944 at the height of construction, and by the end of the war about $2.2 billion had been spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its security kept the secret from Germany and Japan, but Soviet spies penetrated Los Alamos and other sites and sent back information that helped speed the Soviet bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> After the Trinity test of 16 July 1945 the bombs fell on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and Americans were astounded to learn of a secret enterprise as large as their car industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Groves and the MED, 1942,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1942/groves_med.htm. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “The Manhattan Project and the Second World War, 1939–1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/retrospect.htm. [Open access]","F. G. Gosling, <i>The Manhattan Project: Making the Atomic Bomb</i>, DOE/MA-0001 (Washington, DC: History Division, Department of Energy, 1999), https://archive.org/details/de-99001330-the-manhattan-project-making-the-atomic-bomb-1999. [Open access]","Vincent C. Jones, <i>Manhattan: The Army and the Atomic Bomb</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1985), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-10. [Open access]","Richard G. Hewlett and Oscar E. Anderson Jr., <i>The New World, 1939–1946</i>, A History of the United States Atomic Energy Commission, vol. 1 (University Park: Pennsylvania State University Press, 1962), https://archive.org/details/the-new-world-1939-1946-hewlett-anderson-1962. [Open access]","Henry DeWolf Smyth, <i>Atomic Energy for Military Purposes: The Official Report on the Development of the Atomic Bomb under the Auspices of the United States Government, 1940–1945</i> (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1945), https://archive.org/details/atomicenergyform00smytrich. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History and Culture,” Manhattan Project National Historical Park, https://www.nps.gov/mapr/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of Legacy Management, “Manhattan Project Background Information and Preservation Work,” https://www.energy.gov/lm/manhattan-project-background-information-and-preservation-work. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Espionage and the Manhattan Project, 1940–1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1942-1945/espionage.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Army take over a scientists' project?","a":"Because by 1942 the work had moved from research to building enormous production plants, and the Corps of Engineers had the construction skills and could keep it secret.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Army control the effort changed from one led by research scientists to one in which they supported a construction enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Groves took over in September 1942 he quickly bought the Tennessee site that his predecessor had hesitated over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He later moved the district's headquarters from New York to Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were there several plants making different fuels?","a":"Because no one knew which method of producing fissionable material would work, so the project pursued more than one at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roosevelt's approval in December 1942 covered full-scale gaseous diffusion and plutonium plants as well as a compromise electromagnetic plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Uranium was separated at Oak Ridge and plutonium was made in reactors at Hanford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Fermi's Chicago pile had shown on 2 December 1942 that a chain reaction could be sustained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the project matter so much to the Allies?","a":"Because it began as a race against Nazi Germany, and the thought of an atomic bomb in the hands of one of the world's most murderous regimes was chilling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war was the deadliest in history, with perhaps 60 million dead worldwide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki were among the last acts of the war, and the department's history calls the Manhattan Project inexplicable outside the context of that war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also ushered in the nuclear age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Manhattan Project"},
    "ww2-911": {"questions":["Having named the first nuclear test 'Trinity' after poems by John Donne, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> watched it from a control bunker in the New Mexico desert on 16 July 1945.","In 1954 the Atomic Energy Commission revoked the security clearance of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a decision the Department of Energy vacated in 2022 as the product of a flawed process."],"abstract":"The American theoretical physicist<b> J. Robert Oppenheimer</b>, born in New York on 22 April 1904, directed the Los Alamos laboratory of the Manhattan Project and is often called the father of the atomic bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Harvard he studied at the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge and took his doctorate under Max Born at Göttingen at the age of 22, where he helped develop the new quantum theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then taught at both the University of California, Berkeley, and the California Institute of Technology, founding an American school of theoretical physics and writing early papers on what are now called black holes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1942 he led a group of theorists studying bomb design at Berkeley and argued for one isolated laboratory where scientists could freely exchange ideas, a proposal Groves accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Groves and his committee soon chose him, at 38, to head the laboratory, despite worries about his inexperience as an administrator, his left-wing sympathies and his lack of a Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> As scientific director at Los Alamos, beside a military post commander, he persuaded leading physicists to join and kept the laboratory as much an academic community as possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He worked well with Groves despite their very different temperaments, and their partnership of mutual respect was a major factor in the project's success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He named the first test 'Trinity' after poems by John Donne and watched it from a control bunker on 16 July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After the war he chaired the General Advisory Committee of the Atomic Energy Commission and opposed building the hydrogen bomb, and in June 1954 the commission revoked his security clearance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The commission gave him its Enrico Fermi Award in 1963, he died in Princeton on 18 February 1967, and in 2022 the Department of Energy vacated the 1954 decision as the product of a flawed process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Atomic Heritage Foundation, “J. Robert Oppenheimer,” Nuclear Museum, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/profile/j-robert-oppenheimer/. [Open access]","“J. Robert Oppenheimer,” Biographies, AtomicArchive.com, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/biographies/oppenheimer.html. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, “Secretary Granholm Statement on DOE Order Vacating 1954 Atomic Energy Commission Decision In the Matter of J. Robert Oppenheimer,” 16 December 2022, https://www.energy.gov/articles/secretary-granholm-statement-doe-order-vacating-1954-atomic-energy-commission-decision. [Open access]","Jennifer M. Granholm, Secretarial Order, “Vacating 1954 Atomic Energy Commission Decision: In the Matter of J. Robert Oppenheimer,” US Department of Energy, 16 December 2022, https://www.energy.gov/sites/default/files/2022-12/S1%20Oppenheimer%20Order%2012.12.22%20signed%20by%20S1%2012-16-2022.pdf. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Establishing Los Alamos, 1942–1943,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1942-1945/establishing_los_alamos.htm. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “The Trinity Test, July 16, 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/trinity.htm. [Open access]","F. G. Gosling, <i>The Manhattan Project: Making the Atomic Bomb</i>, DOE/MA-0001 (Washington, DC: History Division, Department of Energy, 1999), https://archive.org/details/de-99001330-the-manhattan-project-making-the-atomic-bomb-1999. [Open access]","Vincent C. Jones, <i>Manhattan: The Army and the Atomic Bomb</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1985), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-10. [Open access]","Richard G. Hewlett and Oscar E. Anderson Jr., <i>The New World, 1939–1946</i>, A History of the United States Atomic Energy Commission, vol. 1 (University Park: Pennsylvania State University Press, 1962), https://archive.org/details/the-new-world-1939-1946-hewlett-anderson-1962. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Oppenheimer an unexpected choice?","a":"Because he had little managerial experience and some troubling past links with Communist causes, though Groves recognised his scientific brilliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The department's history adds that he had no Nobel Prize while several of the scientists he would lead had one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was only 38 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> He proved an excellent director, and within 3 years of his selection the United States had used two atomic bombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Oppenheimer want Los Alamos isolated but open inside?","a":"Because he believed theoretical and experimental work needed one central place run by ordinary scientific standards, to ensure accuracy and speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He suggested that the laboratory work in secret in a remote area while allowing free exchange of ideas among its staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Groves agreed, and they settled on a boys' school on a mesa in the Jemez Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Oppenheimer also feared that a military chain of command suited scientific decisions badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was his clearance revoked in 1954?","a":"Because the commission concluded that there were fundamental defects in his character, though it never claimed that he had mishandled secrets or questioned his loyalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its security board had found no evidence of disloyalty but cited his past associations and his opposition to the hydrogen bomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2022 the Department of Energy said the process had violated the commission's own rules and showed bias and unfairness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By then he had perhaps had more access to nuclear secrets than anyone else in government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"J. Robert Oppenheimer"},
    "ww2-912": {"questions":["A coded telegram telling the Secretary of War at Potsdam that a patient had been 'operated on this morning' reported the success of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the American delegation.","Families in the Tularosa Basin who say fallout from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> caused cancers and other illnesses in their communities have not been covered by the federal compensation law for radiation exposure."],"abstract":"The <b>Trinity test</b> was the first explosion of a nuclear weapon, a plutonium bomb fired by the Manhattan Project in the New Mexico desert on 16 July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A test seemed vital for the plutonium bomb, both to confirm its new implosion design and to gather data on nuclear explosions in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Oppenheimer named it 'Trinity' after poems by John Donne, and Kenneth Bainbridge, responsible for the test since March 1944, led the division that prepared it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The site was a remote corner of the Alamogordo Bombing Range called the Jornada del Muerto, ‘Journey of Death’, about 340 kilometres (210 miles) south of Los Alamos and only 32 kilometres (20 miles) from the nearest homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Observers sheltered almost 10 kilometres (6 miles) away, and Groves and Oppenheimer watched from different places in case one of them was killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After rain delayed it, the device exploded at 5:30 AM, vaporising the steel tower, turning the sand around its base to glass and raising the mushroom cloud that became a symbol of the atomic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first reactions were surprise, joy and relief, and Oppenheimer later said it brought to mind the legend of Prometheus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A coded telegram reached the Secretary of War, Henry Stimson, at Potsdam that day, and Los Alamos scientists soon put the blast at 15,000 to 20,000 tons of TNT, more than most had predicted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Planners had worried about fallout over nearby towns, and the Army was ready to evacuate a few civilians from their homes if radiation levels rose too high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> People of the Tularosa Basin downwind of the site say fallout damaged their health for generations, but the compensation law of 1990 does not cover them, and the site became a National Historic Landmark in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “The Trinity Test, July 16, 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/trinity.htm. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Safety and the Trinity Test, July 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/trinity_safety.htm. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Evaluations of Trinity, July 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/trinity_evaluations.htm. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “Trinity Test – 1945,” Nuclear Museum, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/history/trinity-test/. [Open access]","F. G. Gosling, <i>The Manhattan Project: Making the Atomic Bomb</i>, DOE/MA-0001 (Washington, DC: History Division, Department of Energy, 1999), https://archive.org/details/de-99001330-the-manhattan-project-making-the-atomic-bomb-1999. [Open access]","Vincent C. Jones, <i>Manhattan: The Army and the Atomic Bomb</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1985), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-10. [Open access]","Richard G. Hewlett and Oscar E. Anderson Jr., <i>The New World, 1939–1946</i>, A History of the United States Atomic Energy Commission, vol. 1 (University Park: Pennsylvania State University Press, 1962), https://archive.org/details/the-new-world-1939-1946-hewlett-anderson-1962. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History and Culture,” Manhattan Project National Historical Park, https://www.nps.gov/mapr/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the plutonium bomb tested but not the uranium one?","a":"Because the plutonium weapon used a new implosion design, and a test seemed vital to confirm it and to gather data on nuclear explosions in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several plutonium bombs were in the pipeline and would be available in the following weeks, so one could be spared for a test.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oppenheimer put Kenneth Bainbridge in charge of Project Trinity to oversee the firing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Groves's assistant remarked afterwards that the war was over, and Groves replied that it would be after two bombs on Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the test matter at Potsdam?","a":"Because Truman had arrived at Potsdam the day before to discuss the end of the war with Japan, and the news changed the calculations behind the talks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stimson told Truman and his Secretary of State as soon as the coded telegram arrived, and told Churchill the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Groves's longer report followed on 21 July, describing glass shattered 200 kilometres (125 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Truman, the department's history says, went back to the table with a new card in his hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were planners worried about radiation?","a":"Because plutonium in the device would fission into radioactive products, and neutrons striking the ground would make some elements radioactive, all to be lifted into a cloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As the test drew near, planners realised that fallout over local towns posed a real hazard, and Groves feared legal blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Army agents mapped everyone within about 64 kilometres (40 miles) and an offsite monitoring system was set up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Downwind families still say that the fallout harmed their communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Trinity (nuclear test)"},
    "ww2-913": {"questions":["When American troops reached the Black Forest in April 1945 they found that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had worked in a small underground cave, a textile factory wing and an old brewery.","Ten scientists of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Werner Heisenberg, were interned at Farm Hall in England, where their recorded disbelief at the news of Hiroshima showed how far behind they had been."],"abstract":"The <b>Uranverein</b>, or ‘uranium club’, was the secret German nuclear research programme of the Second World War, begun in April 1939 only months after Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann had discovered fission in Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew from a secret conference in Berlin, after which the education ministry started formal uranium research and banned uranium exports, and the War Office backed the work once Paul Harteck warned it of an explosive of unsurpassable power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1939 Kurt Diebner of the War Office gathered Hahn, Hans Geiger, Werner Heisenberg and others, who agreed to study reactor design, the separation of uranium isotopes and fast-neutron fission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Conquest brought it Belgian uranium, a French cyclotron and the Norsk Hydro plant in Norway, the world's largest producer of the heavy water needed to slow neutrons in a reactor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heisenberg, who became director of the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute for Physics in Berlin in 1941, emerged as the scientific leader of the effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In January 1941 a mistaken measurement by Walther Bothe led German scientists to rule out graphite, leaving only scarce heavy water as a moderator for a reactor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By mid-1941 they had grown pessimistic about any weapon, and as the war turned against Germany the state put its resources into more immediate needs, while Allied raids struck the Norwegian heavy-water plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the Americans' Alsos mission reached Haigerloch and Hechingen in April 1945, it found the programme's laboratories on a ludicrously small scale and its reactor still short of a chain reaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten of its scientists were then interned at Farm Hall in England from July 1945 to January 1946, and their secretly recorded talk after Hiroshima showed how far from a bomb they had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether some, Heisenberg above all, held back deliberately, as they later claimed, or were excusing their failure is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Atomic Rivals and the ALSOS Mission, 1938–1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1942-1945/rivals.htm. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “German Atomic Bomb Project,” Nuclear Museum, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/history/german-atomic-bomb-project/. [Open access]","Vincent C. Jones, <i>Manhattan: The Army and the Atomic Bomb</i>, United States Army in World War II (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1985), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub11-10. [Open access]","Richard G. Hewlett and Oscar E. Anderson Jr., <i>The New World, 1939–1946</i>, A History of the United States Atomic Energy Commission, vol. 1 (University Park: Pennsylvania State University Press, 1962), https://archive.org/details/the-new-world-1939-1946-hewlett-anderson-1962. [Open access]","F. G. Gosling, <i>The Manhattan Project: Making the Atomic Bomb</i>, DOE/MA-0001 (Washington, DC: History Division, Department of Energy, 1999), https://archive.org/details/de-99001330-the-manhattan-project-making-the-atomic-bomb-1999. [Open access]","“Werner Heisenberg – Biographical,” from <i>Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941</i> (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 1965), NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1932/heisenberg/biographical/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Allies fear a German bomb?","a":"Because fission had been discovered in Berlin, and German science and industry looked capable of the massive effort a bomb required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the Manhattan Project's scientists were refugees who had studied under German teachers before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Groves said they had to assume Germany's best scientists were working on a bomb with full state support until they knew otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only reports from Strasbourg in late 1944 suggested the Germans were no further on than the Americans had been at the end of 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the German project stall?","a":"Because a mistaken measurement led its scientists to reject graphite, so a reactor depended on scarce heavy water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The isotope separation method they chose first was inefficient, and they judged the others too costly and uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The project was disorganised, and one interned scientist later said each group called the others unimportant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Energy Department's history adds that the government's attempts to forge a coherent strategy met with little success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the scientists interned at Farm Hall?","a":"Because the Allies wanted to know what Germany's leading nuclear scientists knew, and their talk in a bugged house in England was secretly recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some, such as Heisenberg and Diebner, had led the work, while others, such as Hahn and Max von Laue, were found not to have been involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Heisenberg's disbelief at the news of Hiroshima convinced the Allies that the German effort had never been close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Heisenberg returned to Germany in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"German nuclear program during World War II"},
    "ww2-914": {"questions":["Proposing that the new weapon be shown to the United Nations 'on the desert or a barren island', the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> urged a demonstration instead of a surprise attack on a Japanese city.","Some sentences of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written at the Metallurgical Laboratory and rejected by the Interim Committee, were later blacked out in ink even in the original copy."],"abstract":"The <b>Franck Report</b> was a report of 11 June 1945 by a committee of scientists at the Manhattan Project's Metallurgical Laboratory in Chicago, which argued against using the atomic bomb on Japan without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its committee was chaired by the German-born physicist James Franck, and its other members were Donald Hughes, J. J. Nickson, Eugene Rabinowitch, Glenn Seaborg, J. C. Stearns and Leo Szilard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It arose after Arthur Compton briefed the Chicago scientists on 2 June about the conclusions of the Interim Committee, the body advising the government on the bomb, and the laboratory decided to offer a second opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rabinowitch drafted it, and he later wrote that its stress on not using the bomb against Japan was due to Franck and Szilard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The report argued that nuclear bombs could not stay an American secret for more than a few years, and that without international control an arms race was certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It warned that the United States, with its people and industry crowded into a few great cities, would be at a disadvantage in such a race against nations spread over wide areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An early, unannounced attack on Japan would therefore cost the country support throughout the world, it said, so the weapon should first be shown to representatives of the United Nations on a desert or a barren island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Franck took it to Washington on 12 June, but the Scientific Panel of Oppenheimer, Fermi, Compton and Lawrence concluded that no technical demonstration would make Japan surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 21 June the Interim Committee agreed with the panel that the bomb should be used as soon as possible, without warning, against a war plant surrounded by other buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Szilard then circulated a petition to President Truman, signed by 68 Chicago scientists, that never reached him, and some sentences of the report were later blacked out in ink even in the original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Committee on Political and Social Problems, Metallurgical Laboratory, University of Chicago, “Report of the Committee on Political and Social Problems,” 11 June 1945, US National Archives, Record Group 77, transcribed at AtomicArchive.com, https://www.atomicarchive.com/resources/documents/manhattan-project/franck-report.html. [Open access]","Gene Dannen, “The Franck Report, June 11, 1945,” <i>Atomic Bomb: Decision</i>, Dannen.com, http://www.dannen.com/decision/franck.html. [Open access]","US Department of Energy, Office of History and Heritage Resources, “Debate over How to Use the Bomb, Late Spring 1945,” <i>The Manhattan Project: An Interactive History</i>, OSTI.GOV, https://www.osti.gov/opennet/manhattan-project-history/Events/1945/debate.htm. [Open access]","Richard G. Hewlett and Oscar E. Anderson Jr., <i>The New World, 1939–1946</i>, A History of the United States Atomic Energy Commission, vol. 1 (University Park: Pennsylvania State University Press, 1962), https://archive.org/details/the-new-world-1939-1946-hewlett-anderson-1962. [Open access]","F. G. Gosling, <i>The Manhattan Project: Making the Atomic Bomb</i>, DOE/MA-0001 (Washington, DC: History Division, Department of Energy, 1999), https://archive.org/details/de-99001330-the-manhattan-project-making-the-atomic-bomb-1999. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “Leo Szilard,” Nuclear Museum, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/profile/leo-szilard/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Chicago scientists think secrecy would not protect America?","a":"Because the scientific facts behind the bomb were well known to scientists of other countries, so the weapon could not remain a secret for more than a few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The report judged it futile to avoid an arms race by hiding the science or cornering the supply of raw materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within 10 years, it warned, other countries might have bombs, each able to destroy an urban area of more than 26 square kilometres (10 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The only hope for safety was international control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the report propose a demonstration?","a":"Because it believed that showing the weapon in an uninhabited area would create better conditions for an international agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> America could then say to the world that it had a weapon it did not use and was ready to renounce, the report imagined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A demonstration would not rule out using the bomb later against Japan with the support of other nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decision, it insisted, should not be left to military tacticians alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the report fail to change the decision?","a":"Because the Scientific Panel, which was supposed to link scientists and policymakers, disagreed and concluded that no technical test would convince Japan to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Compton had passed an unsigned copy to the War Department, adding that a demonstration might prolong the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Interim Committee then agreed on use without warning on 21 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Szilard's later petition was strongly opposed by Groves and never reached the President.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Franck Report"},
    "ww2-915": {"questions":["Pointing to penicillin, radar and the battle against the U-boat, the report <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> urged the American government to fund university science in peacetime through a new foundation.","5 years of argument and a presidential veto followed the report <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before Congress created the National Science Foundation in 1950 to support basic research."],"abstract":"<b>Science, the Endless Frontier</b> was the report that Vannevar Bush, director of the wartime Office of Scientific Research and Development, presented to President Truman on 5 July 1945 on how the United States should support science after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It answered a letter of 17 November 1944 in which Roosevelt asked how the lessons of applying science to wartime problems could be carried over into peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roosevelt asked four questions: how to make known the war's contributions to science, how to continue the war against disease, how government could aid research, and how to find and train scientific talent in American youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bush wrote the report himself but based it on the work of expert committees, whose full reports he attached as appendices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had also arranged the request partly to counter a rival plan by Senator Harley Kilgore for a broad, politically controlled science agency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The report opened with the war: penicillin had saved countless wounded men, radar had helped win the war against Germany, and the battle against the U-boat had been one of scientific techniques with a dangerously small margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central claim was that basic research, done in the purest realms of science, is the pacemaker of technological progress, and that a nation depending on others for new knowledge would be weak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It proposed a National Research Foundation to support the best basic research in colleges and universities, run in the traditional way Bush knew best.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A bill was introduced on the day the report was released, but Truman vetoed a version in 1947, objecting that the agency would be divorced from control by the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Congress created the National Science Foundation in 1950, while wartime science had shown how much a large, coordinated effort between science, government and industry could achieve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Vannevar Bush, <i>Science, the Endless Frontier: A Report to the President</i> (Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office, 1945), https://archive.org/details/scienceendlessfr00unit_0. [Open access]","US National Science Foundation, “History,” About NSF, https://www.nsf.gov/about/history/vbush1945.htm. [Open access]","France A. Córdova, “Celebrating NSF’s 70th Birthday,” foreword to Vannevar Bush, <i>Science, the Endless Frontier</i>, 75th anniversary edition (Alexandria, VA: National Science Foundation, 2020), https://nsf-gov-resources.nsf.gov/2023-04/EndlessFrontier75th_w.pdf. [Open access]","George T. Mazuzan, <i>The National Science Foundation: A Brief History</i>, NSF 88-16 (Arlington, VA: National Science Foundation, 1994), https://www.nsf.gov/about/history/nsf50/nsf8816.jsp. [Open access]","Harry S. Truman, “Memorandum of Disapproval of the National Science Foundation Bill,” 6 August 1947, <i>Public Papers of the Presidents</i>, Harry S. Truman Library, https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/library/public-papers/169/memorandum-disapproval-national-science-foundation-bill. [Open access]","Atomic Heritage Foundation, “German Atomic Bomb Project,” Nuclear Museum, https://ahf.nuclearmuseum.org/ahf/history/german-atomic-bomb-project/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Bush want the government to fund basic research?","a":"Because, he wrote, new products and processes rest on new principles developed by research in the purest realms of science, so basic research is the pacemaker of technological progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 19th century American ingenuity could build on European discoveries, but now a nation that depended on others for basic knowledge would be slow in industry and weak in trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the war, universities had relied on endowments and companies with vested interests, and federal support had been minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His report sought to change that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the war shape the report so strongly?","a":"Because Bush drew his lessons from directing the Office of Scientific Research and Development, whose secret coordination of research Roosevelt called a unique experiment in teamwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The report pointed to penicillin, radar and the U-boat war, and warned that the country could not again rely on allies to hold off an enemy while it caught up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It therefore asked for more military research in peacetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coordinated American bomb effort had shown what such teamwork could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did it take until 1950 to create the foundation?","a":"Because the Kilgore and Magnuson bills differed on patents, the geographical spread of funds, the social sciences, basic against applied research and control of the agency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Truman vetoed the 1947 bill because it would hand vital national policies and large public funds to essentially private citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He said he still wanted a science foundation and regretted the veto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A compromise finally passed in 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "ww2-916": {"questions":["On Kiska in August 1943, jittery American and Canadian troops found no Japanese on the island, yet suffered dead and wounded from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the fog.","On the night of 11 July 1943 Allied guns off Sicily shot down 23 of 144 transport planes carrying American paratroopers, a disaster of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> caused partly by poor coordination."],"abstract":"<b>Friendly fire</b>, which military writers also call amicicide or fratricide, is the killing or wounding of one's own or allied troops by mistake in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In the Second World War it came from every arm: aircraft bombing their own lines, artillery and small arms, and warships firing on friendly ships and aircraft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One study counted 53 incidents in which American ships were hit by Allied fire during the war, killing 186 and wounding 438, most of them in 1945 and in amphibious operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The submarine Seawolf, attacked by an American escort that mistook her for an enemy, was lost with 83 lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Total casualties from friendly fire in modern war are probably small, perhaps under 2 percent of all, but single incidents could inflict serious casualties, wreck local operations and badly shake the confidence of combat troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the night of 11 July 1943, Allied naval and ground fire hit transport planes carrying American paratroopers to Sicily, and 23 of the 144 aircraft never returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 504th Parachute Combat Team suffered 229 casualties that night, including 81 dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Kiska in August 1943, troops expecting fierce resistance found the Japanese had already left, and the only guns fired were those of friend against friend by mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the opening of Operation Cobra on 25 July 1944, bombs from 35 heavy and 42 medium bombers fell inside American lines, killing General Lesley McNair, head of the Army Ground Forces, and over 100 soldiers by differing counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Experience of it was common: in a survey of April 1945, half or more of veteran infantrymen in Italy said they had several times been bombed or strafed by their own planes or shelled by their own artillery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Charles R. Shrader, <i>Amicicide: The Problem of Friendly Fire in Modern War</i>, Combat Studies Institute Research Survey 1 (Fort Leavenworth, KS: US Army Command and General Staff College, 1982), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA211713. [Open access]","Martin Blumenson, <i>Breakout and Pursuit</i>, United States Army in World War II: The European Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1961), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub751BreakoutAndPursuit. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1965), https://archive.org/details/SicilyAndTheSurrenderOfItaly. [Open access]","Wesley Frank Craven and James Lea Cate, eds., <i>The Army Air Forces in World War II</i>, vol. 3, <i>Europe: Argument to V-E Day, January 1944 to May 1945</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951; repr. Washington, DC: Office of Air Force History, 1983), https://archive.org/details/Vol3EuropeArgumentToVEDay. [Open access]","Eleanor D. Gauker and Christopher G. Blood, <i>Amicicide at Sea: Friendly Fire Incidents during World War II Naval Operations</i> (San Diego: Naval Health Research Center, 1993), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA278291. [Open access]","George L. MacGarrigle, <i>Aleutian Islands</i>, The U.S. Army Campaigns of World War II, CMH Pub 72-6 (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1992), https://archive.org/details/AleutianIslands. [Open access]","Samuel A. Stouffer et al., <i>The American Soldier: Combat and Its Aftermath</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II, vol. 2 (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1949), https://archive.org/details/studies-in-social-psychology-in-world-war-ii-2-the-american-soldier-combat-and-its-aftermath. [Open access]","Lars R. H. Orloff, “Analysis of Fratricide in United States Naval Surface and Submarine Forces in the Second World War” (MS thesis, Naval Postgraduate School, 1999), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA374561. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did so much friendly fire come from the air?","a":"Because bombing close to friendly troops needed precise coordination, and human error caused far more incidents than mechanical failures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before Cobra, clouds forced bombardiers to recompute their aim in flight, and dust and smoke hid the markers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One lead bombardier released visually after his bombsight failed, another misread landmarks, and a third pilot ordered bombs away too soon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The air force's history notes that such gross errors had been anticipated by both air and ground commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the Sicily airborne disaster happen?","a":"Because the transports' route took them over Allied warships and the assault beaches, where gunners opened fire on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Paratroop commander Matthew Ridgway had repeatedly sought assurances that the Navy would clear an air corridor, fearing exactly this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some troops on the ground also fired on the paratroopers, as not every unit had the same password.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly a sixth of the aircraft were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the Kiska casualties so embarrassing?","a":"Because the Allies had attacked an empty island: the Japanese garrison of 5,183 men had slipped away almost 3 weeks earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Expecting the enemy on the high ground, nervous troops fired on one another in fog, and the dead and wounded ashore came partly from that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A later Army study used Kiska to show the danger of uncontrolled firing by green troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A destroyer that struck a mine added to the losses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Friendly fire"},
    "ww2-917": {"questions":["Army psychiatrists in Italy credited the lower breakdown rates of veteran divisions partly to the protective power of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built as men lived and fought together.","Sending replacements forward one by one as strangers, rather than in whole units, was criticised for undermining the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that held American rifle companies together."],"abstract":"<b>Unit cohesion</b> is the bond of loyalty, trust and mutual dependence among the members of a military unit, especially the small group of men who fight side by side, that holds them together under the stress of battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wartime psychiatrists quickly learned how strongly morale, leadership and group cohesiveness shaped the behaviour of individual soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American surveys found that soldiers attached little importance to patriotism or war aims, and that a deep but tacit belief in the cause mattered less day to day than the solidarity of the immediate combat group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among the incentives enlisted veterans named, solidarity with the group came second only to the wish to get the job finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scout wounded at Salerno said men in his outfit grew close-knit and depended on each other, and that loyalty to them was the main thing that kept a man from going haywire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Asked what helped when the going was tough, most infantry veterans in both the Pacific and the Mediterranean said that thinking they could not let the other men down helped them a lot, second only to prayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychiatrists in Italy credited part of the lower breakdown rates in veteran units to the protective power of cohesiveness built through repeated danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American system of sending replacements forward one by one, passed from depot to depot without officers they knew, was blamed for damaging their morale before they ever met their new comrades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Company commanders often overestimated their own men's pride in their company, which showed how hard such bonds were to measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Studies of the German army after the war stressed the primary group as the key to its tenacity, though later historians argued that Nazi ideology also drove many German soldiers, above all on the Eastern Front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel A. Stouffer et al., <i>The American Soldier: Combat and Its Aftermath</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II, vol. 2 (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1949), https://archive.org/details/studies-in-social-psychology-in-world-war-ii-2-the-american-soldier-combat-and-its-aftermath. [Open access]","Samuel A. Stouffer et al., <i>The American Soldier: Adjustment during Army Life</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II, vol. 1 (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1949), https://archive.org/details/studies-in-social-psychology-ww2-1. [Open access]","Albert J. Glass, ed., <i>Neuropsychiatry in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Overseas Theaters</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1973), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA295201. [Open access]","Albert J. Glass and Robert J. Bernucci, eds., <i>Neuropsychiatry in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Zone of Interior</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1966), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA295202. [Open access]","Robert R. Palmer, Bell I. Wiley and William R. Keast, <i>The Procurement and Training of Ground Combat Troops</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Army Ground Forces (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), https://archive.org/details/TheProcurementAndTrainingOfGroundCombatTroops. [Open access]","Stephen G. Fritz, “‘We Are Trying ... to Change the Face of the World’: Ideology and Motivation in the Wehrmacht on the Eastern Front; The View from Below,” <i>Journal of Military History</i> 60, no. 4 (1996): 683–710, https://www.jstor.org/stable/2944661. [Paywalled]","Alexander A. Cox, <i>Unit Cohesion and Morale in Combat: Survival in a Culturally and Racially Heterogeneous Environment</i>, monograph (Fort Leavenworth, KS: School of Advanced Military Studies, US Army Command and General Staff College, 1995), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA309830. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did ideals matter so little in soldiers' own words?","a":"Because combat veterans felt that talk of idealistic purposes from civilians or rear areas insulted what they had been through, and a taboo against flag-waving talk applied even within the group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The more typical frame of mind was a tacit conviction that their side was right and the war necessary, after which the soldier concerned himself with his job and with staying alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In that world, the researchers concluded, heroism entered mainly through the close solidarity of the immediate combat group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were replacements at a disadvantage?","a":"Because they were shipped without unit organisation or strong command and passed mechanically from one agency to another, often for months, before joining a unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In that time they grew physically soft, their discipline slackened and their training was undone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Italy in 1945, men who had joined their outfits as replacements were less likely than original members to condemn a soldier who went absent from the front for letting the other fellows down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also rated their training lower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did historians question the primary-group explanation for Germany?","a":"Because the post-war study of German prisoners had stressed comradeship and the needs met by the small group, and had found Nazi convictions to be a slight factor except among a hard core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later historians argued that cohesion stemmed from organisation and training, or that ideology shaped the German soldier even in combat, especially in Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Studies of the Japanese American 442nd Regimental Combat Team have also used it as an example of strong cohesion and morale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Unit cohesion"},
    "ww2-918": {"questions":["On 3 August 1943 General Patton slapped a soldier at a Sicilian evacuation hospital who said he could not take it, an early public clash over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the American army.","Long-serving infantrymen in Italy who broke down after months of good service, the 'old sergeant syndrome', convinced psychiatrists that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could strike any man in time."],"abstract":"<b>Combat stress reaction</b> is the breakdown of a soldier's ability to function under the strain of battle, known in the Second World War as combat exhaustion or battle fatigue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the First World War it was called shell shock and blamed on brain damage from exploding shells, but Second World War doctors found that most men who blamed blast had suffered psychic rather than physical injury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American army had screened its recruits hard, rejecting five to six times as many on psychiatric grounds as in 1917 and 1918, yet psychiatric disorders were two to three times as common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When large numbers of psychiatric casualties appeared in Tunisia, they were labelled 'psychoneurosis', seen as weaklings who had slipped through screening, and often sent to hospitals far in the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In March 1943 the army adopted the simple term 'exhaustion', which matched how most front-line soldiers looked after days of fighting with little food or sleep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The army relearned the lesson of the First World War that brief treatment close to the front, with rest, sleep and food, worked best, the principles later summed up as proximity, immediacy and expectancy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Treated near their units, men kept their ties to their comrades and expected to go back, while evacuation to distant hospitals implied failure and fixed their symptoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Italy the 'old sergeant syndrome', the collapse of men who had fought well for months, seemed to show that every man has his breaking point, while a system that replaced casualties one by one left infantrymen no end in sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 3 August 1943 General George Patton slapped a soldier diagnosed with an anxiety state at an evacuation hospital in Sicily, and the scandal became part of the story of the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Up to half of American military discharges in the war were said to result from combat exhaustion, and the army film <i>Let There Be Light</i> of 1946 followed veterans through a 10-week course of treatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Albert J. Glass, ed., <i>Neuropsychiatry in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Overseas Theaters</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1973), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA295201. [Open access]","Albert J. Glass and Robert J. Bernucci, eds., <i>Neuropsychiatry in World War II</i>, vol. 1, <i>Zone of Interior</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1966), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA295202. [Open access]","Samuel A. Stouffer et al., <i>The American Soldier: Combat and Its Aftermath</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II, vol. 2 (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1949), https://archive.org/details/studies-in-social-psychology-in-world-war-ii-2-the-american-soldier-combat-and-its-aftermath. [Open access]","Albert N. Garland and Howard McGaw Smyth, <i>Sicily and the Surrender of Italy</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Mediterranean Theater of Operations (Washington, DC: Center of Military History, United States Army, 1965), https://archive.org/details/SicilyAndTheSurrenderOfItaly. [Open access]","US Department of Veterans Affairs, National Center for PTSD, “History of PTSD in Veterans: Civil War to DSM-5,” https://www.ptsd.va.gov/understand/what/history_ptsd.asp. [Open access]","John Huston, dir., <i>Let There Be Light</i> (US Army Signal Corps, 1946), National Archives and Records Administration, 111-M-1241, https://archive.org/details/gov.archives.arc.35924.b. [Open access]","Robert R. Palmer, Bell I. Wiley and William R. Keast, <i>The Procurement and Training of Ground Combat Troops</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Army Ground Forces (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), https://archive.org/details/TheProcurementAndTrainingOfGroundCombatTroops. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the army rename the condition 'exhaustion'?","a":"Because 'psychoneurosis' implied inner mental weakness, and soldiers read the unfamiliar word simply as 'psycho', so casualties were not accepted as products of battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 'Exhaustion' described how most psychiatric casualties, and indeed most combat soldiers, actually looked after days of fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A theatre directive in Italy later ordered that no other diagnosis be used in the combat zone, and psychiatric casualties became almost respectable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change helped men return to duty without carrying the stigma of a mental illness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did screening recruits fail to prevent breakdowns?","a":"Because screening could not predict how men would react to the hazards and deprivations of combat itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rejections for psychiatric reasons were five to six times higher than in the First World War, yet psychiatric disorders were two to three times as frequent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Psychiatrists came to see that repeated exposure to combat produced crippling anxiety even in normal people, which supported a policy of rotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lack of any end to combat weighed on infantrymen, many of whom hoped only for a wound that would not kill them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did Patton's slapping matter?","a":"Because it set a famous general against medical officers who treated combat breakdown as an illness, and Patton suspected the men of malingering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The soldier he struck had been diagnosed with a moderately severe anxiety state, and Patton then sent his commanders a memorandum complaining of soldiers going to hospital on the pretext of nerves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The National Center for PTSD notes that Patton did not believe battle fatigue was real.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The incidents caused a scandal that for some eclipsed his army's achievements in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Combat stress reaction"},
    "ww2-919": {"questions":["Tried in a court-martial lasting under 2 hours in the Hürtgen Forest in November 1944, the replacement <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had handed a cook a written confession that he would run away again.","Buried among executed murderers and rapists in Plot E of the Oise-Aisne cemetery, the deserter <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reburied in Detroit in 1987 beside his wife Antoinette."],"abstract":"<b>Eddie Slovik</b> was an American private who became the only United States soldier executed for desertion in the Second World War, and the first since the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A young man from near Detroit with a prison record, he had been classed as unfit for service until the army lowered its standards and drafted him in January 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He reached France in August 1944 as a replacement for the 28th Infantry Division, and under shellfire on his first approach to the front he hid and fell in with a Canadian unit for 6 weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Back with his regiment in October, he asked whether leaving again would count as desertion, walked off, and the next day handed a cook a written confession promising to run away again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He refused offers to drop the charges if he took back his statement and agreed to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 11 November 1944, during the grim fighting in the Hürtgen Forest, a court of nine staff officers convicted him in a trial that lasted 100 minutes and sentenced him to be shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobody on the court expected the sentence to be carried out, since some 21,000 American servicemen faced desertion charges in the war and every other death sentence was commuted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eisenhower confirmed it on 23 December, during the Battle of the Bulge, as a warning to others, and a squad of twelve riflemen shot Slovik in eastern France on 31 January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army kept the execution secret, and Slovik was buried among executed murderers and rapists in Plot E of the Oise-Aisne American Cemetery until his remains were returned to Detroit in 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of his judges later regretted the verdict after seeing panic and flight in the Bulge, and opinion remains divided on whether Slovik was a coward or a man unfairly made an example of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Benedict B. Kimmelman, “The Example of Private Slovik,” <i>American Heritage</i> 38, no. 6 (September/October 1987), https://americanheritage.com/node/55767. [Open access]","Allen Pusey, “January 31, 1945: Pvt. Eddie Slovik Executed,” <i>ABA Journal</i>, 1 January 2013, https://abajournal.com/magazine/article/january_31_1945_pvt._eddie_slovik_executed. [Open access]","HISTORY.com Editors, “The Execution of Eddie Slovik Is Authorized,” This Day in History, 23 December, History.com, https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/december-23/the-execution-of-eddie-slovik-is-authorized. [Open access]","Allen Frazier, “Death by Firing Squad: Eddie Slovik Became the Only U.S. Soldier Executed for Desertion in WWII,” Military.com, 5 December 2025, https://www.military.com/daily-news/investigations-and-features/2025/12/05/death-firing-squad-eddie-slovik-became-only-us-soldier-executed-desertion-wwii.html. [Open access]","Samuel A. Stouffer et al., <i>The American Soldier: Combat and Its Aftermath</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II, vol. 2 (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1949), https://archive.org/details/studies-in-social-psychology-in-world-war-ii-2-the-american-soldier-combat-and-its-aftermath. [Open access]","Robert R. Palmer, Bell I. Wiley and William R. Keast, <i>The Procurement and Training of Ground Combat Troops</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Army Ground Forces (Washington, DC: Historical Division, Department of the Army, 1948), https://archive.org/details/TheProcurementAndTrainingOfGroundCombatTroops. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Slovik singled out among so many deserters?","a":"Because he handed the army everything it needed: a signed confession, a refusal to take it back, and a statement that he would desert again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His case reached Eisenhower at the worst moment, during the Battle of the Bulge, when heavy casualties were expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court itself had expected him to be imprisoned rather than shot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No other American soldier was executed for desertion in the war, although 48 others had also been sentenced to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did soldiers' attitudes to deserters vary?","a":"Because men's views depended on how long they had fought and whether they had come over with their outfit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In a survey in Italy in April 1945, a large share of privates thought a man who went absent from the front after long service probably could not help it, though many still had no use for anyone who let the other fellows down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Replacements, sent forward as strangers after long journeys through depots, were often less settled in their units than original members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did one of the judges change his mind?","a":"Because in the Battle of the Bulge he saw officers and enlisted men flee without orders and specialists who had never fought thrust into the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to believe the court of staff officers, none of whom had commanded troops in combat, had judged too simply and too quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slovik's last words to the priest suggested that it was the randomness of death in battle he could not take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army never publicised the execution meant to deter others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Eddie Slovik"},
    "ww2-920": {"questions":["Biscuits, a small can of meat or cheese, dextrose tablets, chewing gum and four cigarettes went into each meal box of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an American field ration of the Second World War.","Infantrymen crossing the Rhine and the Roer in 1945 carried the compact <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in preference to the bulky C ration, though many complained of its taste and lost weight on it."],"abstract":"The <b>K-ration</b> was an American individual combat ration of the Second World War, packed as three meal units, labelled breakfast, dinner and supper, each small enough to fit into a pocket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began in June 1941, when the Chief of Infantry asked the army's Subsistence Research Laboratory for a ration that would fit the pockets of paratroopers' uniforms, about 15 by 6 by 2.5 centimetres (6 by 2.5 by 1 inches) and no heavier than 340 grams (three-quarters of a pound).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its prototype held pemmican biscuits, a chocolate bar, a meat preparation and a lemon or orange drink powder, and it was named Field Ration, Type K, so that any troops could use it, and adopted in 1942 after field tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A typical meal box held biscuits and crackers, dextrose tablets, a can of meat, meat and egg or processed cheese, chewing gum and four cigarettes, with coffee and other extras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its makers sacrificed taste to the limits of space and weight, and although revisions improved it, it stayed less palatable than the improved C ration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was meant for assaults and fast movement: on D-Day each American soldier came ashore carrying one D and one K ration, and the first build-up supplies were 40 percent K rations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Soldiers widely disliked its biscuits, dextrose tablets and synthetic lemon drink, and one quartermaster reported using the lemon powder to scrub floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Italy a medical officer reported that men living on C or K rations fell 400 to 1,800 calories a day short in cold weather and hard work, and men lost weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet it remained popular because it was easy to carry, and in the 1945 crossings of the Roer and the Rhine most soldiers carried K rations rather than the bulkier C ration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Army psychiatrists noted that soldiers lost their appetite after a few days of K-rations, and a wartime study judged that many would remember the mud and the K-rations long after the danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Erna Risch, <i>The Quartermaster Corps: Organization, Supply, and Services</i>, vol. 1, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1953), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-12. [Open access]","William F. Ross and Charles F. Romanus, <i>The Quartermaster Corps: Operations in the War against Germany</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1965), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-15. [Open access]","Alvin P. Stauffer, <i>The Quartermaster Corps: Operations in the War against Japan</i>, United States Army in World War II: The Technical Services (Washington, DC: Office of the Chief of Military History, Department of the Army, 1956), https://archive.org/details/CMHPub10-14. [Open access]","C. A. Elvehjem, <i>Nutritive Value of Army Rations</i>, project report, Committee on Food Research, Quartermaster Food and Container Institute for the Armed Forces (Chicago, 1947), National Library of Medicine, https://archive.org/details/101714261.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","<i>Conference on Survival Rations at QMC Subsistence Research and Development Laboratory on 20 Aug. 1945</i> (Chicago: Quartermaster Food and Container Institute for the Armed Forces, 1945), National Library of Medicine, https://archive.org/details/23810710R.nlm.nih.gov. [Open access]","Samuel A. Stouffer et al., <i>The American Soldier: Combat and Its Aftermath</i>, Studies in Social Psychology in World War II, vol. 2 (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1949), https://archive.org/details/studies-in-social-psychology-in-world-war-ii-2-the-american-soldier-combat-and-its-aftermath. [Open access]","Albert J. Glass, ed., <i>Neuropsychiatry in World War II</i>, vol. 2, <i>Overseas Theaters</i> (Washington, DC: Office of the Surgeon General, Department of the Army, 1973), https://archive.org/details/DTIC_ADA295201. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the K-ration designed so small?","a":"Because it began as a request in June 1941 for a ration that paratroopers could carry in their uniform pockets, with fixed limits on size and weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those limits, more than taste, shaped what went into it, so palatability was sacrificed to space and weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army soon agreed it suited all troops, not only parachutists, and dropped any limit on its use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Pacific it was shipped in blocks of combat rations for invasions, alongside C and ten-in-one rations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did soldiers complain about it?","a":"Because its biscuits, dextrose tablets and synthetic lemon crystals were widely disliked, and many men did not eat all of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Medical officers in Italy found that the C and K rations did not replace the energy men spent fighting in the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Air crews testing a K-ration breakfast in 1945 also found it low in acceptability and complained about the fruit bar and the smell of the canned meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Psychiatrists linked poor eating to the physical exhaustion of men in combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did nutrition scientists keep studying it after the war?","a":"Because doubts remained about whether the field rations could keep men healthy over long periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In tests reported in 1947, monkeys fed on K rations lost weight, and researchers concluded that the ration did not contain enough of two B vitamins for growing animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Similar studies were then run on the C and ten-in-one rations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> During the war itself the army had already dropped the K-ration from the ten-in-one ration because of its unpopularity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"K-ration"}
  };
  (window.CARD_EXTRA_IN = window.CARD_EXTRA_IN || []).push({ CARD_EXTRA: CARD_EXTRA });
})();
